A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,19,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 19 ChatGPT boosts self-efficacy for task resolution (H2) Figure 2 illustrates participant self-efficacy for creating useful and original ideas in the experimental task. Participant assessments of self-efficacy for task resolution without ChatGPT indicated no significant differences between the control group and experimental group, F(1,142) = 1.35, p = .247, η2p = .01. However, participant assessments of self-efficacy for task resolution with ChatGPT showed moderately higher self-efficacy in comparison to the control group, which did not use ChatGPT, F(1,142) = 16.28, p < .001, η2p = .10. Furthermore, participants in the experimental condition were asked to assess their self-efficacy for task resolution with and without ChatGPT. The within-subject differences were very strong, F(1,75) = 17.57, p < .001, η2p = .19, indicating that individual participants were more confident when solving the task with ChatGPT than without it. Interestingly, prior experience of ChatGPT was unrelated to self-efficacy for task resolution, F(1,75) = 1.51, p = .222, η2p = .00. Figure 2 Self-efficacy to resolve the problem-solving task without and with ChatGPT",173
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,19,"Interestingly, prior experience of ChatGPT was unrelated to self-efficacy for task resolution, F(1,75) = 1.51, p = .222, η2p = .00. Figure 2 Self-efficacy to resolve the problem-solving task without and with ChatGPT",33
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,20,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 20 From an exploratory perspective, it is important to note that self-efficacy and the quality, elaboration, and originality of the solutions on PIT (hereafter labeled as actual problem- solving performance; see variables 5, 6 and 7 in Table 3) had only a very small correlation in the control group (median r = .11) and a negligible one in the experimental group (median rno-ChatGPT = .01, median rwith-ChatGPT = .04). However, higher self-efficacy was associated with higher bias indices (i.e., greater overestimation) of one’s performance in both the control group (median r = .49) and experimental group (median rno-ChatGPT = .23, median rwith-ChatGPT = .26). To conclude, the findings suggest that ChatGPT boosts self-efficacy, but the benefits of higher self-efficacy for actual performance may be limited. ChatGPT has no influence on the absolute accuracy of self-evaluations (H3) Interestingly, there were no differences between the control and experimental group in self- evaluation accuracy, nor in self-assessment of quality, F(1,142) = 0.16, p = .687, η2p = .00, or originality, F(1,142) = 1.08, p = .301, η2p = .01.",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,20,"Interestingly, there were no differences between the control and experimental group in self- evaluation accuracy, nor in self-assessment of quality, F(1,142) = 0.16, p = .687, η2p = .00, or originality, F(1,142) = 1.08, p = .301, η2p = .01. Prior ChatGPT experience had no relationship with self-assessment accuracy of quality, F(1,142) = 0.02, p = .892, η2p = .00, or originality, F(1,142) = 2.32, p = .130, η2p = .02. However, an exploratory examination of the correlation matrix in Table 3 offers one important insight. With the use of ChatGPT, there is no correlation between self-evaluation judgments and quality (r = .02) or originality (r = -.03) of solutions on PIT, suggesting that participants at the group level were unable to calibrate their judgments according to their performance. However, it is important to keep in mind that absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. Further exploration of the correlations in Table 3 also shows that the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT is moderately associated with higher bias index (i.e. overestimation) of performance (median r = .24). This may prove",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,20,"evidence is not evidence of absence. Further exploration of the correlations in Table 3 also shows that the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT is moderately associated with higher bias index (i.e. overestimation) of performance (median r = .24). This may prove crucial because perceived usefulness of ChatGPT does not correlate with actual problem-solving performance (median r = -.01). In other words, the more useful the participants found ChatGPT, the more they overestimated the quality and originality of their answers. But as suggested above, these results are exploratory and they require further investigation.",91
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,21,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 21 Figure 3 Perceived task interest, difficulty, and mental effort invested in task resolution ChatGPT use does not make the task resolution more interesting (H4) Another rather surprising finding is that participants in both conditions considered the task resolution similarly interesting. As illustrated in Figure 3, there were no significant differences between the control and experimental groups in task interest, F(1,142) = 0.27, p = .602, η2p = .00. Moreover, there was no relationship between prior ChatGPT experience and perceived task interest, F(1,142) = 0.22, p = .642, η2p = .00. Task resolution is easier with ChatGPT (H5) However, as further illustrated in Figure 3, task resolution with ChatGPT was perceived as moderately easier than task resolution without it, F(1,142) = 12.16, p = <.001, η2p = .08. Overall, prior ChatGPT experience was unrelated to perceived difficulty, F(1,142) = 2.62, p = .108, η2p = .02.",150
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,22,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 22 However, the exploration of correlations in Table 3 found moderate negative correlation between perceived difficulty and prior ChatGPT experience when working with ChatGPT, r = -.24, indicating that the task resolution was perceived easier with greater ChatGPT experience. Again, this finding is exploratory and requires further testing. Task resolution with ChatGPT requires less mental effort (H6) And finally, Figure 3 shows that task resolution with ChatGPT required moderately less mental effort than task resolution without, F(1,142) = 12.07, p < .001, η2p = .08. Prior ChatGPT experience was unrelated to invested mental effort, F(1,142) = 0.09, p = .760, η2p = .00. Discussion The goal of the present study was to investigate creative problem-solving performance in university students with and without ChatGPT assistance. The Product Improvement Task employed in the present study was a complex ill-defined problem-solving task in which participants were asked to create three product improvements that were both original and useful for achieving two overarching objectives (Puente-Díaz et al., 2021). In line with expectations (Noy & Zhang; 2023, March 2; Yilmaz &",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,22,"ill-defined problem-solving task in which participants were asked to create three product improvements that were both original and useful for achieving two overarching objectives (Puente-Díaz et al., 2021). In line with expectations (Noy & Zhang; 2023, March 2; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023), the present study found that students who used ChatGPT created solutions that were more original, elaborated and better reflected the task goals. Task resolution was perceived as easier (Sawyer, 2018) and requiring less mental effort (Iku-Silan et al., 2023). Moreover, students reported higher task self-efficacy compared to those not using ChatGPT (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023). Surprisingly, ChatGPT assistance did not make task resolution more interesting (contrasting the findings of Guo et al., 2023; Liu et al., 2022). Furthermore, the effect of ChatGPT on self- evaluation accuracy was unclear. Although there were no differences in the absolute accuracy of self-evaluations between control and experimental condition, the negligible correlation between self-evaluation judgments and performance with the assistance of ChatGPT suggests that participants at the group level had serious problems calibrating when using ChatGPT. In this context, it is notable that",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,22,"self-evaluations between control and experimental condition, the negligible correlation between self-evaluation judgments and performance with the assistance of ChatGPT suggests that participants at the group level had serious problems calibrating when using ChatGPT. In this context, it is notable that the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT was moderately associated with self-evaluation accuracy of performance and so could serve as a heuristic cue for self- evaluation of the answers (see Ackerman, 2019).",70
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,23,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 23 Quality, Elaboration, and Originality of Solutions Co-Created with ChatGPT The present study found that the solutions of participants using ChatGPT were of higher quality (i.e., they more closely reflected the general objectives of the task). Moreover, these solutions were better elaborated and more original than the solutions of peers working on their own. Interestingly, these findings contradict the views of Dwiwedi et al. (2023) and Peters et al. (2023). In their position papers, they claim that ChatGPT could “kill creativity”, that ChatGPT solutions “lack originality” and that ChatGPT “just did not bear a chance against human creativity”. Although ChatGPT can produce solutions that are not original, it may be important to keep in mind that the goal of generative AI should not be to replace users but to help users develop their own ideas. This is corroborated by Hitsuwari et al. (2023), who found that AI-generated haikus – only when further elaborated by human creators – were rated the most beautiful. Based on the present study, it may be possible to propose several explanations of",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,23,"is corroborated by Hitsuwari et al. (2023), who found that AI-generated haikus – only when further elaborated by human creators – were rated the most beautiful. Based on the present study, it may be possible to propose several explanations of why ChatGPT improved all three dimensions: quality, elaboration, and originality (Todd et al., 2019). As suggested by Woo et al. (2023), ChatGPT may enhance divergent thinking processes by exposing students to a wide range of possible solutions. This exposure then encourages students to consider a larger pool of solutions, allowing them to select more promising prototypical solutions (Mumford et al., 1991; 2019). Furthermore, ChatGPT can provide support to students in the iterative development of their ideas, investigating these ideas in a greater detail, enabling them to explore the problem space in more depth, and thus develop more elaborate solutions. Finally, interacting with ChatGPT may spark novel combinations of concepts, contributing to more original final solutions. As will be discussed further, this may be especially true of students with greater prior experience of ChatGPT. The Role of Experience in Using ChatGPT",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,23,"interacting with ChatGPT may spark novel combinations of concepts, contributing to more original final solutions. As will be discussed further, this may be especially true of students with greater prior experience of ChatGPT. The Role of Experience in Using ChatGPT to Solve Problems Zamfirescu-Pereira et al. (2023) in their analysis identified difficulties non-expert users had in prompting chatbot to provide solutions that could help them to achieve their assigned goals. Participants with no prior experience did not know how to initiate interaction with the chatbot and their prompts were too vague to guide the conversation. The subsequent frustration led them to abandon the task prematurely, and they were ineffective at seeking help. Moreover, they did not systematically test their approach and although they all eventually reached their goals (with the help of the interviewer), they were unable to",138
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,24,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 24 accurately evaluate the quality of their prompts or explain why their prompts worked. In other words, these findings suggest that novice participants lack the metacognitive skills of monitoring and regulation (Smy et al., 2016). In light of these findings, one can see why in the present study prior experience was moderately associated with the quality, elaboration, and originality of the solutions created with ChatGPT and why participants with greater prior experience thought task resolution was easier. Therefore, future research should target the temporal aspects of the problem-solving process in participants with different levels of experience, in addition to measuring the outcomes. It is necessary to explore how participants with different levels of expertise approach prompt engineering and how the use of different kinds of prompts affects performance on ill-defined problem-solving tasks (Dang et al., 2022, September 3). The Use of ChatGPT Boosts Self-efficacy In academic settings, students who possess strong self-efficacy in a particular subject tend to approach complex assignments with confidence and overcome problems because they perceive the task as manageable and within their",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,24,"September 3). The Use of ChatGPT Boosts Self-efficacy In academic settings, students who possess strong self-efficacy in a particular subject tend to approach complex assignments with confidence and overcome problems because they perceive the task as manageable and within their capabilities. As a result, they perform better (Zimmerman, 2000). The present study found that self-efficacy for task resolution with ChatGPT was considerably higher than self-efficacy for task resolution without ChatGPT assistance. Participants with higher self-efficacy for resolving the task with ChatGPT assistance thought task resolution was both easier and more interesting and that ChatGPT was more useful. This finding suggests that people with greater confidence in their abilities to solve the task using ChatGPT considered ChatGPT more useful. However, it is important to note that there was no association between higher self- efficacy and actual task performance. The higher self-efficacy was on the other hand associated with overestimation of one’s performance. In other words, although ChatGPT strongly enhanced self-efficacy, students with higher self-efficacy did not perform better, although they believed that they did. This could be explained by Vancouver &",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,24,"was on the other hand associated with overestimation of one’s performance. In other words, although ChatGPT strongly enhanced self-efficacy, students with higher self-efficacy did not perform better, although they believed that they did. This could be explained by Vancouver & Kendall’s (2006) findings that higher levels of self-efficacy were associated with better performance when aggregated for the group of participants, but that higher self-efficacy at the within- subject level was associated with a negative performance. This means that if individuals believe they will perform better on some tasks than on others, that belief may in fact hinder their performance on tasks they believe they will excel at. Since ChatGPT is a novel technology that enhances self-efficacy at both the between- and within-subject levels, it may",125
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,25,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 25 be that this boost in self-efficacy has yet to be fully taken into account when performing a task and self-evaluating the outcomes. In other words, the boost in self-efficacy from ChatGPT might be making people overconfident in their abilities, which could negatively impact their performance. Accuracy of Metacognitive Monitoring with ChatGPT Although the hybrid human-AI regulation theory (Molenaar, 2022a; 2022b) highlights the importance of metacognitive monitoring in human-AI collaboration, there was no study conducting research on effects of generative AI on accuracy of metacognitive monitoring (Joksimovic et al., 2023). Self-evaluation plays a crucial role in student learning and problem-solving (Brown & Harris, 2013). It requires students to actively monitor their own problem-solving process and, by taking responsibility for assessing their progress, students become actively engaged in evaluating their understanding and making any necessary adjustments. When students self-evaluate their own work, they are encouraged to reflect on the overall quality of their work as well on the strategies they have employed (Panadero et al., 2017). However, Zamfirescu-Pereira et al. (2023) found that novice users are generally",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,25,"students self-evaluate their own work, they are encouraged to reflect on the overall quality of their work as well on the strategies they have employed (Panadero et al., 2017). However, Zamfirescu-Pereira et al. (2023) found that novice users are generally inaccurate at metacognitive monitoring when working with a chatbot. The present study’s findings are therefore rather ambiguous. Firstly, there was no difference in the absolute accuracy of self-evaluations between the groups of participants. In absolute terms, participants in both groups were similarly accurate when evaluating quality and similarly inaccurate when evaluating originality. However, the negligible correlation between self- evaluation judgments and performance in experimental group may suggest that aggregated data may hide the fact that ChatGPT use may have a different impact on different participants. Previous studies have shown that participants can be broken down into those who are skilled and aware, those who are unskilled but aware, those who are skilled but underestimate their performance, and those who are unskilled and overestimate their performance (Dörrenbächer-Ulrich & Perels, 2023; Urban & Urban, 2021). In other words, this finding may be",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,25,"and aware, those who are unskilled but aware, those who are skilled but underestimate their performance, and those who are unskilled and overestimate their performance (Dörrenbächer-Ulrich & Perels, 2023; Urban & Urban, 2021). In other words, this finding may be related to the clustered nature of self-evaluative skills. Perceived Usefulness of ChatGPT as a Heuristic Cue in Metacognitive Monitoring",59
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,26,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 26 When individuals metacognitively monitor their performance, self-evaluation judgments rely on various heuristic cues rooted in self-perceptions, task characteristics and metacognitive experiences (Ackerman, 2019; Hoch et al., 2023). The question is which heuristic cues were participants using ChatGPT relying on in the situations where their self-evaluation judgments did not correlate with performance. The explorations in the present study offer two suggestions. First, the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT was moderately associated with overestimation of the performance. The more useful the participants found ChatGPT, the more they overestimated the quality and originality of their answers. Second, perceived task difficulty was negatively associated with self-evaluation accuracy. That means that participants who thought the task easier also overestimated their performance more. This finding is crucial because students using ChatGPT thought the task was moderately less difficult than students working on their own. In the other words, ChatGPT use decreased the perceived difficulty, but with a lower difficulty, students may have felt that their solutions were better and therefore invested less mental effort in the task resolution. The existing research suggests",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,26,"their own. In the other words, ChatGPT use decreased the perceived difficulty, but with a lower difficulty, students may have felt that their solutions were better and therefore invested less mental effort in the task resolution. The existing research suggests that subjective effort appraisals reflect more than just the effort invested in the task, as they are influenced by factors unrelated to the tasks themselves (Scheiter et al., 2020). That could mean that in their effort appraisals learners also make use of cues that are invalid in terms of effort expenditure. Students using ChatGPT reported moderately less effort required to successfully complete the task. However, lower effort was associated with higher overestimation of one’s performance. These findings suggest that students using ChatGPT in problem-solving may have difficulty using valid heuristic cues in self-evaluation because task resolution with ChatGPT is seen as easier and requiring less mental effort. Limitations and Future Directions The limitations of the present study mainly relate to its experimental nature. The study used a complex ill-defined problem-solving task (product improvement task) that is often employed in creative",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,26,"as easier and requiring less mental effort. Limitations and Future Directions The limitations of the present study mainly relate to its experimental nature. The study used a complex ill-defined problem-solving task (product improvement task) that is often employed in creative problem-solving research to explore maximum achievable performance in individuals (Torrance, 2008; K. Urban & Urban, 2023). The task is not contextualized in classroom practice and so is ideal for experimental designs because participants have no prior beliefs, experiences, or motivations that might hinder performance. On the other hand, the findings may have lower ecological validity, as is often the case with laboratory experiments. Future quasi-experimental research should therefore target problem-solving of ill-defined",112
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,27,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 27 tasks in real classroom conditions (such as writing essays for their academic course; see Jonassen, 2011). This becomes particularly important since the participants in the current study did not consider the resolved task particularly interesting. The weak, albeit non- significant, correlation between perceived interest and problem-solving performance implies that altering students' perception of task interest may influence their problem-solving behavior. Introducing tasks that are either more or less interesting to students could bring new insights into students’ classroom motivation. Moreover, future studies need to employ diverse samples of participants, as the sample in the present study was composed of university students. To comprehensively understand the impact of ChatGPT across different age groups and backgrounds, future research endeavors could involve the inclusion of participants from diverse educational levels, study subjects and possibly cultural contexts. This expansion would provide valuable insights into potential variations in the utilization and effectiveness of ChatGPT, thereby enhancing the generalizability of the presented findings. Future studies should also focus specifically on the temporal aspects of a problem- solving process in addition to",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,27,"would provide valuable insights into potential variations in the utilization and effectiveness of ChatGPT, thereby enhancing the generalizability of the presented findings. Future studies should also focus specifically on the temporal aspects of a problem- solving process in addition to measuring outcomes, exploring the trace data and various on- task indices (such as monitoring judgments during problem-solving; Winne, 2010). For example, it is worth noting that the students using ChatGPT took longer to solve the tasks despite them reporting lower difficulty and less mental effort. Furthermore, the metacognitive experiences in the present study were measured using a single judgment. There may be other, more accurate measuring tools (such as the enjoyment subscale from the Intrinsic Motivation Inventory that measures on-task interest; Ryan et al., 1983) or qualitative research methods, including in-depth interviews and think-aloud protocols. This qualitative exploration will offer a nuanced understanding of how metacognitive strategies unfold during hybrid human- AI problem-solving, contributing to a richer and more comprehensive analysis of student interactions with ChatGPT. The future interventional studies may employ a strategy instruction training to foster accurate self-evaluation",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,27,"a nuanced understanding of how metacognitive strategies unfold during hybrid human- AI problem-solving, contributing to a richer and more comprehensive analysis of student interactions with ChatGPT. The future interventional studies may employ a strategy instruction training to foster accurate self-evaluation skills when using ChatGPT. These may prove crucial in enhancing students’ ability to critically evaluate and validate information generated by ChatGPT, ensuring a more informed and discerning use of the AI tool within educational settings. Finally, the future studies should address the potential long-term effects of using ChatGPT, by extending the research scope to encompass longitudinal investigations. By tracking the progress and experiences of students over an extended period, the studies should aim to gain insights into the sustained impact of ChatGPT on learning outcomes,",125
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,28,"CHATGPT AND CREATIVE PROBLEM-SOLVING 28 metacognitive development, and any evolving challenges or benefits that may emerge over time. These future research directions are pivotal in providing a holistic understanding of the implications of integrating ChatGPT into educational contexts. Conclusions Dellermann et al. (2019) argued that human-AI hybrid intelligence is characterized as the capacity to accomplish complex objectives by blending human and artificial intelligence, resulting in outcomes superior to those that can be achieved independently and allowing continuous improvement as they learn from one another. In an idealized scenario, humans and AI share agency (Molenaar, 2022a; 2022b), each providing a unique competence and together achieving optimal results (Järvelä et al., 2023; Rafner et al., 2021). The present study showed that student co-creation with ChatGPT considerably improved creative problem- solving performance together with their on-task self-efficacy. The task resolution was considered easier and requiring less mental effort. In the learning context, this translates into the ideal conditions for effective learning (Pass & Van Merriënboer, 1994). Yet the present study also identified potential pedagogical problems that educators and students will face: they will",180
A005,A005.pdf,10.1016/j.compedu.2024.105031,28,"and requiring less mental effort. In the learning context, this translates into the ideal conditions for effective learning (Pass & Van Merriënboer, 1994). Yet the present study also identified potential pedagogical problems that educators and students will face: they will have to learn to deliberately use valid monitoring cues during task resolution and evaluation, and be aware that ChatGPT’s usefulness and perceived ease of task resolution does not automatically result in more useful and original solutions (Baars et al., 2020). In other words, ChatGPT may enhance divergent thinking skills, but require greater use of metacognitive skills. This will be especially important for students choosing to use ChatGPT for their actual learning assignments (Guo, 2022). These findings may prove essential for future advancements of hybrid human-AI regulation theory (Molenaar, 2022a; 2022b), as integrating metacognitive prompts or various types of feedback (Kuklick et al., 2023) may prove crucial for production of problem-solving outcomes that are bot",154
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,1,"DOI: 10.1002/tl.20624 O R I G I N A L A R T I C L E Generative AI detection in higher education assessments Cesare Giulio Ardito Department of Mathematics, School of Natural Sciences, Faculty of Science and Engineering, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK Correspondence Cesare Giulio Ardito, Department of Mathematics, School of Natural Sciences, Faculty of Science and Engineering, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK. Email: cesaregiulio.ardito@manchester.ac.uk Abstract This chapter presents a critical analysis of genera- tive AI (GenAI) detection tools in higher education assessments. The rapid advancement and widespread adoption of GenAI, particularly in education, necessi- tates a reevaluation of traditional academic integrity mechanisms. I explore the effectiveness, vulnerabil- ities, and ethical implications of AI detection tools in the context of preserving academic integrity. My analysis synthesizes insights from various case stud- ies, newspaper articles, and student testimonies to scrutinize the practical and philosophical challenges associated with AI detection. I argue that reliance on detection mechanisms is misaligned with the educa- tional landscape, where AI plays an increasing role. I advocate for a strategic shift toward robust assess- ment",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,1,"scrutinize the practical and philosophical challenges associated with AI detection. I argue that reliance on detection mechanisms is misaligned with the educa- tional landscape, where AI plays an increasing role. I advocate for a strategic shift toward robust assess- ment methods and educational policies that embrace GenAI usage while ensuring academic integrity and authenticity in assessments. INTRODUCTION In today’s educational landscape, educators need to identify and regulate AI usage by students. The ability of AI to perform a variety of tasks, from generating text to solving problems, makes it a tempting tool for academic dishonesty. This usage undermines the foundation of academic integrity, as the authenticity of student work comes into question. Though the issue has many similarities with the issue of essay mills, the wide availability of a range of advanced AI tools, many for free and even able to pass several advanced exams (OpenAI, 2023), poses a problem of scale where most students today report using such a tool (Freeman, 2024). Educators naturally seek methods to control and guide AI usage This is an open access article under",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,1,"to pass several advanced exams (OpenAI, 2023), poses a problem of scale where most students today report using such a tool (Freeman, 2024). Educators naturally seek methods to control and guide AI usage This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. © 2024 The Author(s). New Directions for Teaching and Learning published by Wiley Periodicals LLC. New Dir Teach Learn. 2025;2025:11–28. wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/tl 11",86
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,2,"12 GENERATIVE AI DETECTION IN HIGHER EDUCATION ASSESSMENTS in the classroom, and a working system to enable AI usage detection would be the most immediate, intuitive, and easy-to-implement solution to ensure academic integrity. The most basic form of detection of AI-generated content consists of simple human insight, when someone believes that the text they are reading has been generated, com- pletely or in part, by AI. In some cases, the evidence is overwhelming: for instance, I once received a reference letter ending with the words “regenerate response,” which appeared at the end of every ChatGPT response generated before September 2023. However, in the absence of unambiguous signs, studies show that humans are overconﬁdent in their ability to detect AI-generated content (Clark et al., 2021; Jakesch et al., 2023; Köbis & Mossink, 2021), in both general and specialized contexts (Gao et al., 2023). In higher edu- cation, therefore, educators would be wise not to rely on their own ability to differentiate AI-generated text from human-written text. The rapid mass availability and enhanced capabilities of GenAI tools, starting with Chat- GPT’s release",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,2,"al., 2023). In higher edu- cation, therefore, educators would be wise not to rely on their own ability to differentiate AI-generated text from human-written text. The rapid mass availability and enhanced capabilities of GenAI tools, starting with Chat- GPT’s release to the public in November 2022, saw the market respond with a multitude of machine-aided detectors: text-classiﬁer tools that output the probability that AI generated a given textual input. The underlying idea is simple and appealing: the user pastes text into a box, and the detector runs several statistical evaluations, such as checking the sequence of words (tokens) against the probabilities (weights) of particular AI models, to determine whether the text was generated by AI. Such detectors require no technical expertise and are easily accessible to the public. In early 2023, many competing solutions entered the market, from established com- panies such as Turnitin and focused new start-ups such as Copyleaks, GPTZero, Sapling, ZeroGPT, and many others. OpenAI released another text-classiﬁer tool, but later withdrew it due to its low rate of accuracy, choosing to focus on detection only of",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,2,"panies such as Turnitin and focused new start-ups such as Copyleaks, GPTZero, Sapling, ZeroGPT, and many others. OpenAI released another text-classiﬁer tool, but later withdrew it due to its low rate of accuracy, choosing to focus on detection only of AI-generated audio and visual content in the future (Kirchner et al., 2023). In general, AI detection in academic settings has emerged as a highly controversial topic. This paper’s primary aim is to advocate for the development of robust assessment methods in the era of GenAI, rather than prescribe speciﬁc solutions. I critically examine the via- bility and implications of employing AI detection tools in the realm of academic integrity. Given the rapid advancement and widespread availability of GenAI, particularly in the con- text of higher education, an evaluation of the effectiveness and appropriateness of these detection methods is urgently needed. My analysis delves into the broader considerations of AI detection, questioning its place in future academic settings. I examine the accuracy, vulnerabilities, and ethical dimensions of AI detection tools, assessing the implications of incorporating such technology in an academic setting.",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,2,"My analysis delves into the broader considerations of AI detection, questioning its place in future academic settings. I examine the accuracy, vulnerabilities, and ethical dimensions of AI detection tools, assessing the implications of incorporating such technology in an academic setting. First, I undertake a comprehensive examination of the role and efﬁcacy of AI detec- tion tools within the context of academic integrity, anchoring my analysis in a variety of case studies, newspaper articles, and student testimonies. I then examine AI detec- tion from a broader perspective, highlighting fundamental issues beyond the current shortcomings of the technology, and reﬂecting on whether it has a place in the future in academic contexts where AI usage is integrated and part of the learning objectives. Finally, I argue in favor of developing an alternative framework to teach critical and ethical usage of GenAI to students while safeguarding academic integrity and the authenticity of assessments. PITFALLS IN DETECTING GenAI OUTPUT AI detection tools are based on a variety of statistical approaches and show varying performances. In this section, I highlight common issues and potential pitfalls,",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,2,"students while safeguarding academic integrity and the authenticity of assessments. PITFALLS IN DETECTING GenAI OUTPUT AI detection tools are based on a variety of statistical approaches and show varying performances. In this section, I highlight common issues and potential pitfalls, as the case for using AI detectors would undeniably be stronger if the technology were more reliable. 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",96
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,3,"NEW DIRECTIONS FOR TEACHING AND LEARNING 13 For readers interested in more technical details, I recommend the survey paper by J. Wu et al. (2023). Vulnerabilities AI detectors can be vulnerable to several attacks. Perhaps unsurprisingly, researchers have found that paraphrasing attacks (consisting of the replacement of some words in the text with synonyms) can bypass several detectors (Krishna et al., 2023; Weber-Wulff et al., 2023). Paraphrasing tools are widespread, with some speciﬁcally tailored to automatically deceive detectors (CharlyAI, 2024; StealthGPT, n.d.). Moreover, simple prompting strategies such as asking for a certain style or guiding the AI model toward one can also lower the sensi- tivity of detectors (Fishchuk, 2023; Fishchuk & Braun, 2023). An example of such a prompt is “Include personal reﬂections, use a mix of long and short sentences, employ rhetorical questions to engage the reader, maintain a conversational tone in parts, and play around with the paragraph structure to create a dynamic and engaging piece of writing” (Fishchuk & Braun, 2023, p. 6). Another strategy is asking the model to generate a new answer, adding further",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,3,"a conversational tone in parts, and play around with the paragraph structure to create a dynamic and engaging piece of writing” (Fishchuk & Braun, 2023, p. 6). Another strategy is asking the model to generate a new answer, adding further requirements. Parameter tweaking, such as increasing the temperature or the top p score, adjusts how an AI model generates text and leads to more variety in phrasing, reducing detection (Fishchuk & Braun, 2023). Text generated under altered parameters is underrepresented in detectors’ AI-generated training sets, which shows that detectors focus on particular incar- nations of a model, and their performance can degrade when any signiﬁcant deviation from the model’s default setup is introduced. Further, several commercial services, such as Undetectable AI (https://undetectable. ai), allow users to input their text and check it against multiple AI detectors, enabling a trial-and-error process that, through paraphrasing, rewriting, or other means, ends up producing seemingly human-written text. Other services, still powered by AI, allow paraphrasing text to make it impervious to detection. An example of such a service is Humanizer Pro, available in OpenAl’s",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,3,"that, through paraphrasing, rewriting, or other means, ends up producing seemingly human-written text. Other services, still powered by AI, allow paraphrasing text to make it impervious to detection. An example of such a service is Humanizer Pro, available in OpenAl’s GPT store (CharlyAI, 2024). More generally, any vulnerability in AI detection mechanisms automatically undermines their reliability and effectiveness. Indeed, the core of AI technology is automation: devel- opers of GenAI can take advantage of a detection vulnerability as soon as it is found, and release an updated model that produces text less likely to be identiﬁed as AI-generated. For instance, developers could easily program a version of GenAI that follows style guide- lines similar to the ones found above in the sample user-written prompt from Fishchuk and Braun (2023). In the context of academic integrity, the existence of online networks dedicated to facil- itating academic malpractice is well documented (Monteiro et al., 2018; Parks et al., 2018), so a vulnerability could be shared and widely exploited in a matter of days. The combina- tion of this rapid dissemination with commercial",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,3,"dedicated to facil- itating academic malpractice is well documented (Monteiro et al., 2018; Parks et al., 2018), so a vulnerability could be shared and widely exploited in a matter of days. The combina- tion of this rapid dissemination with commercial incentives to identify such weaknesses makes reliance on AI detection tools for maintaining academic integrity a fundamentally ﬂawed strategy. Future-proof detection Demonstrating that any sentence can be part of the output of a large language model (LLM), the name given to the technology behind modern GenAI, is straightforward, as shown in Figure 1. 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",132
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,4,"14 GENERATIVE AI DETECTION IN HIGHER EDUCATION ASSESSMENTS F I G U R E 1 Conversation with ChatGPT using GPT-3.5 Turbo (default parameters) on the OpenAI playground. Source: Ardito and ChatGPT (2023). Getting ChatGPT to repeat text is less trivial than it may appear at ﬁrst glance, as LLMs generate tokens one at a time, with no further insight or planning (Nardo, 2023)—in other words, the model is writing those words, not copying and pasting the whole string. To put it simplistically, the current limited effectiveness of AI detectors results from how LLMs operate: they use their training dataset to produce statistically likely sentences (J. Wu et al., 2023). However, as LLMs advance in complexity and capability, their ability to use more complex syntactic structures and the information in their training datasets increases, so the scope of their text generation grows. Over time, these models might attain the pro- ﬁciency to generate any form of text, in any style, effectively mimicking the entirety of human writing output. The datasets used to test the efﬁcacy of AI detection tools could expand",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,4,"grows. Over time, these models might attain the pro- ﬁciency to generate any form of text, in any style, effectively mimicking the entirety of human writing output. The datasets used to test the efﬁcacy of AI detection tools could expand to encompass all conceivable text, making the very concept of detection paradoxical, similar to a search in Jorge Luis Borges’s (1962) “Library of Babel.” Such a scenario is still far off, but the speed of improvements is itself concerning: for instance, GPT-4 has a richer prose than GPT-3.5 (Fishchuk, 2023), and many detectors perform signiﬁcantly worse on the former. These trends will likely continue, so distinguishing between AI and human authorship becomes a perpetual cat-and-mouse game, with detection always a step behind. Consequently, relying on AI detection to maintain academic integrity is doomed to become increasingly untenable as time passes. Instead, educational systems and assess- ment methods that do not depend on the ﬂawed premise of accurately distinguishing AI-generated content from human work must be designed. This shift is not just a practical necessity but a strategic adaptation to the",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,4,"Instead, educational systems and assess- ment methods that do not depend on the ﬂawed premise of accurately distinguishing AI-generated content from human work must be designed. This shift is not just a practical necessity but a strategic adaptation to the rapidly evolving landscape of AI capabilities, 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",85
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,5,"NEW DIRECTIONS FOR TEACHING AND LEARNING 15 ensuring that educational integrity is upheld through more robust and future-proof methods. Bias and non-English speakers Even if AI detectors were highly accurate in identifying content generated by current LLMs, false positives may still not be uniformly distributed across text samples but instead dis- proportionately affect certain subgroups of human writers. Recently, researchers from Stanford University reported that AI detectors were more likely to incorrectly ﬂag papers from non-native English speakers than from native speakers (Liang et al., 2023). To my knowledge, the only other study on the subject appeared on Turnitin’s blog (Adamson, 2023) and began with a critique of Liang et al.’s (2023) small sample of 91 works, each less than 150 words. Turnitin reported that its AI detector showed no such bias when tested on a larger number of longer texts (note that the Stanford study did not use Turnitin’s detec- tor). However, the ﬁnding of bias against non-native English speakers has been recently replicated by larger studies (Perkins et al., 2024). However, bias against non-native English speakers would not",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,5,"texts (note that the Stanford study did not use Turnitin’s detec- tor). However, the ﬁnding of bias against non-native English speakers has been recently replicated by larger studies (Perkins et al., 2024). However, bias against non-native English speakers would not be surprising from an intu- itive point of view, as several detectors employ a perplexity metric. An LLM can be thought of as a Markov chain, whose probabilities (weights) come from training on a big corpus of human-written text, so a model is more likely to write “common” sentences—that is, those that follow patterns with widespread usage. In simple terms, perplexity measures how sur- prising a given sentence is. Writers who are not native English speakers are likely to adhere closely to standard syntax in their prose, and hence exhibit lower perplexity scores, which trigger a higher rate of false positives with AI detectors. An extreme example of this dynamic appeared in news reports in July 2023, after several detectors ﬂagged the Constitution of the United States as AI-generated (Edwards, 2023). The Constitution has an extremely low per- plexity score",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,5,"positives with AI detectors. An extreme example of this dynamic appeared in news reports in July 2023, after several detectors ﬂagged the Constitution of the United States as AI-generated (Edwards, 2023). The Constitution has an extremely low per- plexity score because it appears in every AI model’s training data and writers reproduce it identically in most instances when citing it. Perplexity is not the only metric, and some detectors use alternative measurements, often combined with machine learning (Desaire et al., 2023). However, the example of perplexity provides a cautionary tale: an overall low false positivity rate is, on its own, not enough to promote the widespread usage of an AI detector. Indeed, the statistical nature of detection methods may cause disproportionate false positivity rates among certain popu- lation subgroups, based on characteristics of their prose. Strong, robust statistical analyses of such tools should be performed before these tools can be accepted as reliable, but given the rapid advancements in GenAI, a tool is likely to become obsolete before undergoing close scrutiny. Dataset pollution Assessments of detector accuracy rely on comparing",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,5,"such tools should be performed before these tools can be accepted as reliable, but given the rapid advancements in GenAI, a tool is likely to become obsolete before undergoing close scrutiny. Dataset pollution Assessments of detector accuracy rely on comparing a dataset of AI-generated essays with another of human-generated essays. The latter normally comprises essays, papers, or arti- cles written before the release of ChatGPT or even GPT-2, to avoid AI-written or cocreated content (Turnitin, n.d.-a). Despite the recency of GenAI, researchers widely accept that any datasets containing post-2022 text are unreliable sources of authentic human text (Heikkilä, 2022). This pollution has implications for the future reliability of GenAI models, which will train on content increasingly ﬁlled with AI-generated pieces. It also affects detection. Sourcing authentic human-written datasets will become harder, and retraining detectors on content 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",175
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,5,"from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",35
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,6,"16 GENERATIVE AI DETECTION IN HIGHER EDUCATION ASSESSMENTS that they authenticate as human-written could amplify existing biases and inaccuracies. Further, language evolves with time, so detectors trained only on old pre-2023 text may start misclassifying human-written text as AI-generated simply because it is anachronistic. Once more, these issues highlight that AI detection can at most be a stop-gap short-term solution, and that more fundamental changes are necessary to safeguard future academic integrity. Watermarking An interesting, alternative approach to enable detection is watermarking. This practice consists of artiﬁcially embedding a hidden “signature” in text generated by an AI model, which can later be detected. An elementary example follows: dictionary words are divided into two sets (red and blue), and the model chooses words alternating between the two. For sufﬁciently long paragraphs, a human author is extremely unlikely to pick words respecting the alternating sequence, enabling detection of the AI-generated text. Although detectors would still be vulnerable to paraphrasing attacks, researchers have proposed more sophis- ticated approaches that are more resistant to standard attacks. Nevertheless, authors of a recent paper claim they",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,6,"the alternating sequence, enabling detection of the AI-generated text. Although detectors would still be vulnerable to paraphrasing attacks, researchers have proposed more sophis- ticated approaches that are more resistant to standard attacks. Nevertheless, authors of a recent paper claim they have proved that no watermark is immune to obfuscation through paraphrasing (Zhang et al., 2023). Another issue is that current models are not watermarked, and they are already avail- able in a multitude of solutions. Further, not all of them can be withdrawn, since some models have been leaked online (Vincent, 2023). If watermarking becomes a mainstream solution for AI detection, perhaps even enforced by regulation, there would be a clear business incentive in having non-watermarked models available for a variety of applica- tions (certainly including contract cheating). In other words, the cat is out of the bag, and watermarking, feasible or not, is not an advisable path forward. DETECTORS ARE NOT USEFUL Current AI detectors possess several vulnerabilities, and future detectors will likely also have signiﬁcant technological shortcomings. In this section, I argue that any approach that incorporates AI",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,6,"feasible or not, is not an advisable path forward. DETECTORS ARE NOT USEFUL Current AI detectors possess several vulnerabilities, and future detectors will likely also have signiﬁcant technological shortcomings. In this section, I argue that any approach that incorporates AI detection comes with a broader set of problems. Detectors are misaligned AI detection and traditional technological aids differ crucially from academic malpractice. Plagiarism detectors such as Turnitin Similarity (Turnitin, n.d.-b) have proven effective in identifying nonoriginal text and tracing its sources, facilitating the detection of plagiarism in academic assignments. The user of such tools wants to check that the text has not been copied from another source, and the tool is perfectly aligned with this goal; further, the user receives a report and the means to independently verify its accuracy, through links to the alleged plagiarized sources. AI detectors operate on a fundamentally different principle. They assess the degree to which text aligns with the most common outputs of supported LLMs and do not provide any means to independently verify their assessment. Moreover, the primary concern for the user is",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,6,"on a fundamentally different principle. They assess the degree to which text aligns with the most common outputs of supported LLMs and do not provide any means to independently verify their assessment. Moreover, the primary concern for the user is discerning the student’s original contribution, which does not necessarily correlate with the portion of text that has likely been generated by AI. This discrepancy becomes 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",104
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,7,"NEW DIRECTIONS FOR TEACHING AND LEARNING 17 especially problematic when considering legitimate uses of AI in academic work, such as reviewing, paraphrasing, enhancing expression, or improving accessibility. Consider two hypothetical students: Alice, who writes an original draft essay and then uses an LLM for reﬁnement, implementing many of the suggestions, and Bob, who gen- erates an essay with ChatGPT a few minutes before his deadline, changing a few words, restructuring a few sentences, and then submitting. Alice’s work would be more likely to receive a high score on a detector since the last form of the work comes directly from an LLM. Bob’s work, on the other hand, is less likely to be similar to AI output. Hence, the detector scores would not accurately reﬂect the extent of their individual contributions or the presence of malpractice. Further, for the majority of courses, Alice would still have satisﬁed the learning objec- tives. If writing skills were assessed, she arguably would still have learned how to express her viewpoints in a professional, coherent, and formal way, and she could replicate said performance",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,7,"of courses, Alice would still have satisﬁed the learning objec- tives. If writing skills were assessed, she arguably would still have learned how to express her viewpoints in a professional, coherent, and formal way, and she could replicate said performance in any future instance, such as in the employment sector. Indeed, GenAI is here to stay—we live in a world where LLMs can help us with a variety of tasks. The world without LLMs is gone, and it will not come back. Detectors are not falsiﬁable Validating the results of AI detectors brings an inherent challenge. When a detector indi- cates that a substantial portion of a text is AI-generated, this claim is not veriﬁable, as the detector provides no evidence other than the results of its assessment. Nor can such proof exist. As previously shown, an LLM can be made to output any given text, so even determining the used prompts through reverse prompt engineering cannot prove anything. The wider availability and aggressive marketing of detection tools has led to increased usage by academics and teachers. Moreover, the integration",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,7,"made to output any given text, so even determining the used prompts through reverse prompt engineering cannot prove anything. The wider availability and aggressive marketing of detection tools has led to increased usage by academics and teachers. Moreover, the integration of AI detection into main- stream antiplagiarism tools such as Turnitin has further complicated the landscape, drawing a false parallel between two very different tools. The resultant academic environ- ment, where students and academics are left to interpret “AI scores” without a coherent policy framework, has led to inconsistent and unjust outcomes. In April 2023, a student was accused of malpractice due to misdetection and was able to prove her innocence only thanks to the autosaving drafts functionality of Google Docs (Klee, 2023). Another student, accused similarly, had to access expensive legal advice and consultancy from a linguistics expert to have the case dropped (Sokol, 2023). A simple search on social media—Instagram, Reddit, TikTok—reveals thousands of reports of similar cases across all ages and levels (Gorichanaz, 2023). In August 2023, the Washington Post published a guide for students to defend",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,7,"have the case dropped (Sokol, 2023). A simple search on social media—Instagram, Reddit, TikTok—reveals thousands of reports of similar cases across all ages and levels (Gorichanaz, 2023). In August 2023, the Washington Post published a guide for students to defend themselves from wrongful accusations of malpractice due to misdetection (Fowler, 2023). Two strategies commonly appear in advice to students. The ﬁrst is to keep drafts of their work, ideally in an online repository that can certify dates, so they can show the evolution of the work if they are accused of malpractice. The second is to independently check their scores on AI detectors before submitting an assignment, through the use of several com- mercial services that claim to replicate the performance of mainstream AI detectors (Malik et al., 2023). In my opinion, the latter option is, predictably, full of problems related to pri- vacy and integrity, as these tools and their privacy policies are often opaque and unreliable. Imagine a student uploads a human-written essay to one of these portals, and an essay mill gets possession of it and sells",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,7,"related to pri- vacy and integrity, as these tools and their privacy policies are often opaque and unreliable. Imagine a student uploads a human-written essay to one of these portals, and an essay mill gets possession of it and sells the work to a third party, which makes it publicly available on the net… later leading to a hard-to-dismiss plagiarism accusation against the student validated through Turnitin’s plagiarism detector! It is not inconceivable. 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",112
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,8,"18 GENERATIVE AI DETECTION IN HIGHER EDUCATION ASSESSMENTS In general, academic malpractice investigations induce severe anxiety in students, due to the formality of the processes and the severe consequences that can result (Gullifer & Tyson, 2010; Hughes & McCabe, 2006). A wrongful malpractice accusation can be likened to the famous opening of Kafka’s (1925/2001) The Trial, where Josef K. faces inexplicable, unfalsiﬁable accusations and has no way to prove himself innocent while thrust into a daunting and formalized process of scrutiny. This situation can be deeply unsettling and contribute to a sense of helplessness and anxiety. Existing challenges to student mental health further compound the situation (Brown, 2018; Emmerton et al., 2024). The contemporary academic environment, characterized by high demands, competitive pressures, and the challenges of balancing academic and personal life, already places a signiﬁcant mental burden on students (Brown, 2018). The added stressor of possible wrongful accusations of AI-assisted malpractice, especially with the increasing deployment of imperfect AI detection tools, risks exacerbating these mental health challenges. Moreover, repeated occurrences of false malpractice accusations could erode trust between students and",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,8,"2018). The added stressor of possible wrongful accusations of AI-assisted malpractice, especially with the increasing deployment of imperfect AI detection tools, risks exacerbating these mental health challenges. Moreover, repeated occurrences of false malpractice accusations could erode trust between students and educational institutions, with far-reaching implications. Even a detector with a seemingly low false positive rate of 1% poses a dramatic threat of wrongful accusations when considering the volume of essays a student writes throughout their academic career. The number of written submissions, including coursework, projects, reports, and dissertations, varies by discipline, but often reaches up to 70. Assuming a uni- form false positive rate of 1% across all submissions, a quick calculation reveals that an entirely honest student who completes 70 essays faces a 50.5% chance of being erroneously ﬂagged for AI use at least once. Extrapolating this rate to the scale of a typical educational institution, such a detection system could lead to thousands of unfounded allegations of academic misconduct each year, placing an undue and unfair burden on students and causing a climate of anxiety and unfair scrutiny",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,8,"the scale of a typical educational institution, such a detection system could lead to thousands of unfounded allegations of academic misconduct each year, placing an undue and unfair burden on students and causing a climate of anxiety and unfair scrutiny similar to the one produced by proctoring technology during the COVID-19 pandemic (Harwell, 2022). Detectors are incompatible with good usage After an initial period of stop-gap measures during which students were simply told to not use GenAI, recognition of the need to integrate and teach responsible use has grown. UK universities in the Russell Group, for example, have acknowledged the signiﬁcant beneﬁts of this technology in various sectors and are actively working to promote AI literacy among students and staff. The Russell Group (2023) has also adopted principles on GenAI use: the third reads “Universities will adapt teaching and assessment to incorporate the ethical use of generative AI and support equal access” (p. 1) and the fourth reads “Universities will ensure academic rigor and integrity is upheld” (p. 1). Any policy that employs AI detection is fundamentally incompatible with teaching",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,8,"to incorporate the ethical use of generative AI and support equal access” (p. 1) and the fourth reads “Universities will ensure academic rigor and integrity is upheld” (p. 1). Any policy that employs AI detection is fundamentally incompatible with teaching eth- ical and critical usage of LLMs. Detectors, by their design, are unable to differentiate good from bad usage. Naturally, not every use of AI should be legitimized, and there exists a spectrum of AI usage in academic settings (see Figure 2), with some applications constitut- ing malpractice and others representing legitimate and compliant uses that can enrich the learning experience, which may vary depending on the course and learning objectives. The indiscriminate nature of AI detectors fails to recognize this nuance, treating all AI-assisted work with the same level of suspicion, effectively becoming useless as soon as any use of GenAI is allowed. The result of any detection is to stiﬂe the educational use of a promising new technology. Students, aware that any AI-assisted work could be ﬂagged, may shy away from experi- menting with and learning from these",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,8,"of GenAI is allowed. The result of any detection is to stiﬂe the educational use of a promising new technology. Students, aware that any AI-assisted work could be ﬂagged, may shy away from experi- menting with and learning from these tools, even in contexts where their use is encouraged or could offer signiﬁcant educational beneﬁts. I have participated in several focus groups 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",101
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,9,"NEW DIRECTIONS FOR TEACHING AND LEARNING 19 F I G U R E 2 A scale of possible uses of generative AI in academic settings. on the subject of GenAI, and the most common concern expressed by students was that they would expose themselves to malpractice accusations by using it. Similar reports have recently appeared (Gorichanaz, 2023; Litvinaite, 2023; Liu & Bridgeman, 2023). Accessibility technology deserves a separate mention. The visual and audial capabilities of models such as GPT-4 make GenAI a huge asset for inclusivity and accessibility. For instance, GenAI can easily create captions for images; read text out loud from pictures (including handwritten ones); translate text, pictures, and videos into different languages; and reword concepts in simpler language (Glazko et al., 2023). Again, the indiscriminate nature of AI detection could constitute a signiﬁcant barrier for users of accessibility solutions (Center for Teaching Innovation, n.d.). If AI detection achieves greater reliability, it might align with policies enforcing a com- plete ban on GenAI use. However, as soon as institutions encourage, tolerate, or teach the use of GenAI, a fundamental",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,9,"solutions (Center for Teaching Innovation, n.d.). If AI detection achieves greater reliability, it might align with policies enforcing a com- plete ban on GenAI use. However, as soon as institutions encourage, tolerate, or teach the use of GenAI, a fundamental incompatibility with AI detection methods becomes evident, as these tools do not distinguish between permissible and impermissible uses of AI. The crux of the issue is not merely in the technological limitations of AI detectors but in their philosophical misalignment with the necessities of education. As universities and colleges increasingly and correctly advocate for incorporating GenAI into their curricula, and teach about critical, ethical, balanced, and informed usage, the indiscriminate nature of AI detection stands in contrast, pushing in the opposite direction. The association between AI and malpractice Research has shown that correct usage of GenAI signiﬁcantly improves student produc- tivity (Fauzi et al., 2023). In many educational systems, instructors evaluate how a student performs relative to their peers, with grades adjusted to maintain consistency across different years, classes, and student groups. This form of evaluation suggests that an essay",180
A007,A007.pdf,10.1002/tl.20624,9,"tivity (Fauzi et al., 2023). In many educational systems, instructors evaluate how a student performs relative to their peers, with grades adjusted to maintain consistency across different years, classes, and student groups. This form of evaluation suggests that an essay that merited a 70% grade in 2019 might secure only a 60% in 2024 if the widespread use of GenAI among students leads to an overall improvement in the quality of submitted work (R. Wu & Yu, 2024). Under such a scaling system, students are therefore implicitly incentivized to utilize GenAI tools. This incentive exists even for those who might not be otherwise inclined to use such technology. The growing demand for AI skills in the job market further com- plicates this picture. As industries and professions increasingly integrate AI into their 15360768, 2025, 182, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/tl.20624 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [29/10/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",171
