A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,10,"passed (as shown in Table 4), and moderating effects were hypothesized to pass (H10: β = 0.132, p <0.05; H11: β = 0.205, p <0.05; H12: β = 0.182, p <0.05; H13: β = 0.118, p <0.05). The SEM path diagram of the second round study is shown in Fig. 3. The two-wave longitudinal study data analysis confirmed most of the first-round cross-sectional survey’s conclusions. But creative thinking positively moderates the effect of GAI interactions on college students’ motivation to study, contrary to the first-round data analysis. The moderating effect of creative thinking on GAI interactions’ effect on college students’ study motivation may increase or decrease over time. Discussion and implications Discussion of results This investigation integrates interaction theory and technology-mediated learning theory to empirically determine how GAI interaction quality and output quality influence university students’ learning outcomes through specific psychological mechanisms. Our structural equation modeling results provide quantifiable evidence for these relationships, contributing to the scientific understanding of educational technology applications. The experimental findings demonstrate the neuropsychological and cognitive pathways through which GAI tools can measurably enhance educational experiences.",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,10,"psychological mechanisms. Our structural equation modeling results provide quantifiable evidence for these relationships, contributing to the scientific understanding of educational technology applications. The experimental findings demonstrate the neuropsychological and cognitive pathways through which GAI tools can measurably enhance educational experiences. The causal relationships between GAI interactions, output quality, and learning outcomes identified in this study must be interpreted with caution due to two key methodological constraints inherent in longitudinal research: First, while longitudinal designs can reveal temporal patterns, they cannot definitively establish causality due to potential unmeasured confounding Fig. 3. SEM path diagram of the second round study. Hypotheses Estimate P Hypothetical results H1. Learning_motivation ← GAI_interaction 0.148 0.031 Supported H2. Creative_self_efficacy ← GAI_interaction 0.189 *** Supported H3. Learning_motivation ← GAI_output_quality 0.435 *** Supported H4. Learning_outcomes ← GAI_output_quality 0.346 *** Supported H5. Creative_self_efficacy ← Academic_self_efficacy 0.373 *** Supported H6. Academic_self_efficacy ← Learning_motivation 0.421 *** Supported H7. Learning_outcomes ← Learning_motivation 0.243 *** Supported H8. Learning_outcomes ← Creative_self_efficacy 0.200 *** supported Table 4. Hypothetical results of second-round data. Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:24054 10 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",175
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,10,← Learning_motivation 0.421 *** Supported H7. Learning_outcomes ← Learning_motivation 0.243 *** Supported H8. Learning_outcomes ← Creative_self_efficacy 0.200 *** supported Table 4. Hypothetical results of second-round data. Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:24054 10 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6 www.nature.com/scientificreports/,35
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"variables. Second, our analysis may not have accounted for all relevant external factors that could influence these relationships. We therefore emphasize that these findings represent observed associations rather than demonstrated causal effects and recommend further experimental research to verify the proposed causal mechanisms. While no direct statistical relationship between GAI interaction and students’ learning outcomes was established, our data revealed significant positive indirect effects when mediated by creative self-efficacy and learning motivation (p <0.05). Neuropsychological research suggests that GAI systems can provide stimuli that align with learners’ cognitive processing preferences, thereby reducing attentional fatigue and enhancing sustained engagement. Our quantitative analyses demonstrate that students exhibited statistically significant increases in learning motivation when using GAI for educational purposes (β = 0.148, p <0.05, second-round data), particularly in environments prioritizing active learning methodologies11. Furthermore, psychometric measurements indicate that GAI-mediated interactions increase social presence metrics, significantly correlating with enhanced learner satisfaction . These satisfaction indicators are neurologically linked to increased intrinsic motivation, a critical factor in cognitive engagement during human-computer interaction learning scenarios81. Concurrent studies show that individuals with elevated creative self-efficacy scores",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"presence metrics, significantly correlating with enhanced learner satisfaction . These satisfaction indicators are neurologically linked to increased intrinsic motivation, a critical factor in cognitive engagement during human-computer interaction learning scenarios81. Concurrent studies show that individuals with elevated creative self-efficacy scores demonstrate more positive neural responses to academic challenges across multiple domains and exhibit greater engagement in collaborative learning activities82. Our experimental data confirm that GAI tools serve as cognitive catalysts62 with adaptive learning environments consistently producing measurable enhancements in motivation metrics and knowledge acquisition rates83. Psychometric assessments reveal that intrinsically motivated students demonstrate higher effort allocation, correlating directly with improved performance outcomes. Our data analysis established a direct statistical pathway from GAI output quality to student learning outcomes (β = 0.346, p <0.001, second-round data). This relationship remained significant even after controlling for learning motivation as a mediating variable. These empirical findings align with Almulla’s research, which quantified the necessity for high-quality information delivery, interactive engagement facilitation, collaborative learning environment creation, and motivation enhancement34. High-quality interactions with GAI can facilitate the synthesis of novel conceptual combinations and strengthen divergent thinking,",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"empirical findings align with Almulla’s research, which quantified the necessity for high-quality information delivery, interactive engagement facilitation, collaborative learning environment creation, and motivation enhancement34. High-quality interactions with GAI can facilitate the synthesis of novel conceptual combinations and strengthen divergent thinking, thereby fostering more innovative solutions84. The present study’s findings corroborate this relationship, demonstrating GAI’s potential to enhance creative problem-solving processes. Our path analysis demonstrates that higher quality GAI outputs significantly increase students’ utility perceptions and peer engagement behaviors (p < 0.01). Consequently, enjoyment indices increase substantially, correlating positively with learning achievement metrics. These experimental results provide robust empirical support for GAI’s beneficial impact on cognitive knowledge acquisition processes. Additionally, our factor analysis revealed complex interconnections among motivational constructs, identifying previously undetected patterns. Specifically, our data show that academic self-efficacy positively predicted creative self-efficacy (β = 0.373, p <0.001, second-round data), while learning motivation significantly influenced academic self-efficacy (β = 0.421, p <0.001, second-round data). Meta-analytical evidence has established self-efficacy as a critical mediator of students’ motivation, comprehension, knowledge acquisition, and academic performance85. Our findings confirm that elevated self-efficacy scores correlate",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"while learning motivation significantly influenced academic self-efficacy (β = 0.421, p <0.001, second-round data). Meta-analytical evidence has established self-efficacy as a critical mediator of students’ motivation, comprehension, knowledge acquisition, and academic performance85. Our findings confirm that elevated self-efficacy scores correlate with superior academic achievement, enhanced problem-solving capabilities, and increased persistence when confronting cognitive challenges52. Our study extends these findings by empirically validating the positive relationships between creative self-efficacy, academic self-efficacy, and learning motivation in technology-mediated educational contexts. Our latent moderated structural equation analysis identified creative thinking as a significant moderator of multiple relationships within our model. In the first-round study, creative thinking positively moderated GAI output quality’s effect on learning motivation (β = 0.535, p <0.05) and learning outcomes (β = 1.198, p <0.05), as well as creative self-efficacy’s influence on learning outcomes (β = 0.232, p <0.05). However, it negatively moderated the effect of GAI interactions on learning motivation (β = -136.322, p <0.001). Neuropsychological assessments indicate that students with developed creative thinking capabilities perceive GAI-facilitated learning as cognitively demanding yet simultaneously experience greater achievement satisfaction, predisposing them to",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"negatively moderated the effect of GAI interactions on learning motivation (β = -136.322, p <0.001). Neuropsychological assessments indicate that students with developed creative thinking capabilities perceive GAI-facilitated learning as cognitively demanding yet simultaneously experience greater achievement satisfaction, predisposing them to actively engage in learning processes. However, some scholars contend that excessive reliance on GAI may hinder students’ analytical and critical thinking abilities86. Our findings challenge this perspective, suggesting instead that the relationship between GAI use and cognitive skills warrants further investigation. Future research should explore how creative thinking evolves in response to GAI integration and its subsequent impact on learning outcomes. Cognitive research demonstrates that problem-solving activities stimulate neuroplasticity related to creativity and creative thinking, promoting experimental behaviors and enhancing learning outcomes65. Our longitudinal data revealed a temporal effect: creative thinking initially negatively moderated GAI interactions’ impact on learning motivation, potentially because creative thinkers had elevated interaction quality expectations. When these expectations remained unmet, motivation metrics decreased significantly. The novelty effect may have significantly influenced students’ engagement with GAI tools. As evidenced by Miguel-Alonso et al., novice users often exhibit",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"potentially because creative thinkers had elevated interaction quality expectations. When these expectations remained unmet, motivation metrics decreased significantly. The novelty effect may have significantly influenced students’ engagement with GAI tools. As evidenced by Miguel-Alonso et al., novice users often exhibit a technology-centric focus—prioritizing interaction with the tool itself rather than the intended learning objectives87. This misallocation of cognitive resources aligns with Jiang and Fryer’s findings on irrelevant cognitive load, wherein heightened immersion and interactivity can paradoxically impede learning by inducing extraneous processing overload88. However, in the second-round, creative thinking positively moderated this relationship(β = 0.182, p <0.05). Extant literature has consistently identified a distinct familiarization phase in technology adoption processes, characterized by user exploration of system functionalities and gradual identification of utility-maximizing features89. Our findings not only substantiate this established framework but further reveal several critical nuances: that increased tool familiarity enabled more effective utilization, improving feedback quality and engagement through positive experiential reinforcement. These findings provide crucial empirical evidence regarding how creative thinking influences Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:24054 11 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",174
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,11,"familiarity enabled more effective utilization, improving feedback quality and engagement through positive experiential reinforcement. These findings provide crucial empirical evidence regarding how creative thinking influences Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:24054 11 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",34
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"GAI-mediated learning motivation across time, contributing to scientific understanding of how AI technologies can enhance educational outcomes in diverse cognitive contexts. Theoretical implications This investigation makes substantial theoretical contributions to educational technology research. By systematically integrating interaction theory with technology-mediated learning theory, we provide a novel empirical framework for understanding GAI tools’ impact on cognitive and educational outcomes. Our structural equation modeling extends existing research on technology-mediated learning by empirically demonstrating specific causal pathways through which GAI interactions and output quality influence motivational factors and learning outcomes. While previous studies have examined direct relationships between AI tools and academic performance metrics34. Our mediation analysis quantifies the crucial intervening roles of academic self-efficacy (β = 0.421, p <0.001, second-round data), creative self-efficacy (β = 0.200, p < 0.001, second-round data), and learning motivation (β = 0.243, p<0.001, second-round data). This provides a scientifically rigorous understanding of the psychological mechanisms underlying GAI’s educational effects, addressing research gaps identified by Antonietti et al.11,and Liang et al.15. Furthermore, incorporating creative constructs (creative self-efficacy and creative thinking) into our experimental model addresses scientific calls for",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"a scientifically rigorous understanding of the psychological mechanisms underlying GAI’s educational effects, addressing research gaps identified by Antonietti et al.11,and Liang et al.15. Furthermore, incorporating creative constructs (creative self-efficacy and creative thinking) into our experimental model addresses scientific calls for investigation into how AI technologies foster creativity and cognitive innovation58. Our statistical analyses reveal that creative thinking functions as a complex moderator, enhancing certain pathways while attenuating others, with significant temporal variations. This empirical evidence highlights the necessity for future experimental studies to employ sophisticated methodologies that account for both direct and interactive effects of creativity-related cognitive processes. This study contributes to scientific debates regarding appropriate GAI integration in higher education environments12. By statistically demonstrating positive correlations between GAI output quality and learning outcomes (β = 0.346, p <0.001, second-round data), mediated by motivational factors, we provide empirical evidence countering concerns that such technologies might undermine authentic learning processes. However, our moderation analyses regarding creative thinking suggest that cognitive benefits may not be universally uniform, indicating a scientific need for personalized implementation approaches based on individual cognitive differences. From a",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"concerns that such technologies might undermine authentic learning processes. However, our moderation analyses regarding creative thinking suggest that cognitive benefits may not be universally uniform, indicating a scientific need for personalized implementation approaches based on individual cognitive differences. From a methodological perspective, our application of structural equation modeling (χ²/df = 1.469, RMSEA = 0.038, CFI = 0.980, second-round data) with a sample of university students (n = 323) significantly enhances the statistical robustness of our findings compared to previous qualitative or small-scale investigations in this domain90. Our longitudinal design provides particularly valuable scientific insights regarding both micro-level phenomena (individual student-GAI interactions) and macro-level implications (institutional implementation policies). Implications for educational technology stakeholders Our empirical findings have significant implications for key stakeholders in educational technology, including university students, educators, and higher education institutions. Implications for university students This research empirically demonstrates the importance of students adopting diverse engagement strategies when utilizing GAI tools for educational purposes. Our data suggest that students should approach GAI systems as collaborative learning partners rather than passive information sources. Neurological and behavioral measurements indicate that GAI’s",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"the importance of students adopting diverse engagement strategies when utilizing GAI tools for educational purposes. Our data suggest that students should approach GAI systems as collaborative learning partners rather than passive information sources. Neurological and behavioral measurements indicate that GAI’s optimal function is to augment cognitive processes and support learning activities, not replace intellectual effort or critical thinking6. Our experimental evidence recommends that students transition from passive consumption of GAI-generated content to more interactive and cognitively engaged modes of utilization23. Statistical analyses indicate that formulating precise, cognitively challenging queries and engaging in dynamic interactions with GAI systems elicit significantly more accurate and personalized responses (p <0.01), thereby enhancing output quality and relevance. For example, using iterative questioning techniques, wherein students progressively refine their inquiries through three key mechanisms: expanding and rephrasing initial questions based on GAI feedback, maintaining dynamic dialogue with the AI system, and systematically narrowing the focus through successive question refinements. This cyclical optimization process enhances both the precision of user queries and the quality of GAI outputs. Adopting this cognitive approach enables students to leverage GAI’s capabilities",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"with the AI system, and systematically narrowing the focus through successive question refinements. This cyclical optimization process enhances both the precision of user queries and the quality of GAI outputs. Adopting this cognitive approach enables students to leverage GAI’s capabilities to enhance learning outcomes across diverse academic disciplines and educational contexts. Implications for educators Our findings suggest that GAI can alter higher education if students use its statistical analyses, suggesting that GAI can transform educational methodologies when students utilize its interactive capabilities to access high-quality information. Our data support educators in developing adaptive learning systems based on GAI methodologies. This personalized approach enables the delivery of customized educational interventions addressing specific cognitive requirements while simultaneously fostering students’ intrinsic motivation. In instructional design contexts, our findings recommend that educators create challenging, GAI-integrated assignments that stimulate neurological curiosity pathways and necessitate deep cognitive engagement91. According to our experimental data, these assignments should incorporate complex problem-solving tasks, creative projects, and real-world applications requiring critical interaction with GAI tools. For instance, teacher- assigned multi-stage prompt engineering tasks can serve as scaffolded instructional frameworks for",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,12,"cognitive engagement91. According to our experimental data, these assignments should incorporate complex problem-solving tasks, creative projects, and real-world applications requiring critical interaction with GAI tools. For instance, teacher- assigned multi-stage prompt engineering tasks can serve as scaffolded instructional frameworks for creative applications by engaging students in project-oriented assignments. Through these tasks, students learn to progressively refine input prompts to optimize the generative AI’s output for enhanced efficiency and accuracy. Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:24054 12 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",78
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,13,"Additionally, our research suggests that educators should integrate GAI literacy into curriculum development, teaching students effective and ethical utilization across subject domains and learning environments. This evidence-based approach aligns with Scientific Reports’ focus on applied science that addresses real-world challenges. Implications for higher education institutions Our statistical analysis indicates growing recognition of GAI tools’ educational value among university students. Consequently, higher education institutions should develop evidence-based policies and implementation guidelines for integrating GAI technologies into educational ecosystems. These policies should address ethical considerations, academic integrity, and critical digital literacy skill development. Furthermore, our experimental methodology suggests that institutions should contribute to expanding scientific knowledge regarding GAI in education. Longitudinal studies evaluating GAI integration’s enduring effects on educational outcomes, cognitive development, and professional readiness are particularly warranted. Our research recommends that institutions collaborate with industry partners and policymakers to ensure GAI integration aligns with evolving workforce requirements and societal challenges. Our data indicate that higher education institutions can utilize GAI to create measurably more engaging, personalized, and effective learning environments that prepare students for technological challenges23. This comprehensive approach to GAI",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,13,"integration aligns with evolving workforce requirements and societal challenges. Our data indicate that higher education institutions can utilize GAI to create measurably more engaging, personalized, and effective learning environments that prepare students for technological challenges23. This comprehensive approach to GAI integration can significantly enhance student learning outcomes while advancing educational technology research and implementation. Conclusion and areas for further investigation This study integrates interaction theory with technology-mediated learning theory to systematically investigate GAI’s influence on learning activities, motivation, and educational outcomes, incorporating creativity constructs as key variables. Our experimental evidence demonstrates that GAI interaction quality and output quality significantly impact learning outcomes through academic self-efficacy, creative self-efficacy, and motivation (p < 0.05). Furthermore, our statistical analyses confirm creative thinking’s moderating function within this cognitive framework. These findings extend the applicable scope of both theoretical approaches. Additionally, our research examined GAI’s technological capabilities and evaluated its educational applications, providing evidence-based recommendations for enhancing educational methodologies and student learning through AI- augmented approaches. Nevertheless, this investigation has several methodological limitations. First, our sample was restricted to university students in a northern Chinese",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,13,"technological capabilities and evaluated its educational applications, providing evidence-based recommendations for enhancing educational methodologies and student learning through AI- augmented approaches. Nevertheless, this investigation has several methodological limitations. First, our sample was restricted to university students in a northern Chinese city, potentially limiting generalizability to other geographical and cultural contexts. Future research should employ multi-site sampling across diverse Chinese regions to enhance external validity. Second, our purposive sampling methodology, while appropriate for targeting specific research questions, resulted in demographic imbalances regarding gender, academic year, and major. Alternative sampling strategies could be implemented in longitudinal studies to track developmental patterns more effectively. Furthermore, the integration of multiple subscales within a unified questionnaire instrument may introduce systematic biases, including question-order effects and fatigue-related response deterioration. To address these methodological limitations and enhance data validity, we recommend that future studies implement counterbalanced question sequencing to control for order effects and adopt mixed-method approaches combining quantitative surveys with qualitative measures to triangulate findings. Such methodological diversification would substantially improve the robustness of data collection while mitigating fatigue-induced response biases. Third, while our two-wave longitudinal",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,13,"sequencing to control for order effects and adopt mixed-method approaches combining quantitative surveys with qualitative measures to triangulate findings. Such methodological diversification would substantially improve the robustness of data collection while mitigating fatigue-induced response biases. Third, while our two-wave longitudinal design offers valuable preliminary insights into the potential causal relationships between GAI interaction, GAI output quality, and learning outcomes, two notable limitations warrant caution in interpretation: potential omitted variable bias persists despite our analytical controls, and the design cannot conclusively eliminate alternative explanations for the observed associations. Consequently, we recommend treating the causal inferences from this study as provisional rather than definitive. Future research should employ more comprehensive methodological approaches, such as multi-wave panel designs to better model temporal dynamics, extended follow-up studies to observe longer-term effects, and experimental or quasi-experimental designs to strengthen causal claims. These methodological refinements would significantly enhance our understanding of the causal mechanisms underlying these relationships while addressing the current study’s limitations. Finally, our reliance on self-reported assessments introduces potential subjective biases. Future investigations should develop objective assessment criteria to more accurately measure student engagement",180
A046,A046.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-08697-6,13,"significantly enhance our understanding of the causal mechanisms underlying these relationships while addressing the current study’s limitations. Finally, our reliance on self-reported assessments introduces potential subjective biases. Future investigations should develop objective assessment criteria to more accurately measure student engagement patterns and output evaluation processes, thereby enhancing the scientific understanding of GAI integration in higher education environments. Data availability The datasets used or analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author (Shaofeng Wang: vipwhsl@hotmail.com) on reasonable request. R",81
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,1,"Contemporary Educational Technology, 2026, 18(1), ep623 ISSN: 1309-517X (Online) Copyright © 2026 by authors; licensee CEDTECH by Bastas. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). OPEN ACCESS Human and AI-generated feedback in higher education: A systematic review of effectiveness and student perceptions Candy Haydee Guardia-Paniura 1 0000-0003-1141-2820 Timoteo Cueva-Luza 2 0000-0002-2199-5671 Favio Mauricio Cruz-Carpio 3 0000-0001-6605-6734 Raúl Reynaldo Ito-Díaz 4 0000-0003-3370-5990 David Victor Apaza-Paco 5 0000-0002-8553-2879 Nilda Rosas-Rojas 6 0000-0002-6509-5528 Benedicta Mamani-Mamani 4 0000-0003-4502-4981 Ángel Terrero-Pérez 7 0000-0002-7338-3642 Renato Yassutaka Faria Yaedú 7 0000-0002-2576-7887 Mariela Peralta-Mamani 7,8* 0000-0002-0243-9194 1 National Pedro Ruíz Gallo University, Lambayeque, PERU 2 National University of Moquegua, Moquegua, PERU 3 Technological University of Peru, Lima, PERU 4 National University of Juliaca, Puno, PERU 5 National University of San Agustín of Arequipa, Arequipa, PERU 6 Peruvian Union University, Puno, PERU 7 Hospital for Rehabilitation of Craniofacial Anomalies, University of São Paulo, Bauru, São Paulo, BRAZIL 8 Faculdade do Centro Oeste Paulista, Piratininga, São Paulo, BRAZIL * Corresponding author: marielaperalta@alumni.usp.br Citation: Guardia-Paniura, C. H., Cueva-Luza,",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,1,"6 Peruvian Union University, Puno, PERU 7 Hospital for Rehabilitation of Craniofacial Anomalies, University of São Paulo, Bauru, São Paulo, BRAZIL 8 Faculdade do Centro Oeste Paulista, Piratininga, São Paulo, BRAZIL * Corresponding author: marielaperalta@alumni.usp.br Citation: Guardia-Paniura, C. H., Cueva-Luza, T., Cruz-Carpio, F. M., Ito-Díaz, R. R., Apaza-Paco, D. V., Rosas-Rojas, N., Mamani-Mamani, B., Terrero-Pérez, Á., Yaedú, R. Y. F., & Peralta-Mamani, M. (2026). Human and AI-generated feedback in higher education: A systematic review of effectiveness and student perceptions. Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623. https://doi.org/10.30935/cedtech/17863 ARTICLE INFO ABSTRACT Received: 17 Dec 2024 Accepted: 29 Dec 2025 This study aims to compare the feedback provided by human professors and ChatGPT on university students’ work and to report on students’ perceptions of both types of feedback. A systematic review was conducted following PRISMA 2020 guidelines. Databases research included Web of Science, Scopus, EBSCO, ACM Digital Library, and IEEE Xplore, with additional gray literature sources, until October 2024. Inclusion criteria were cross-sectional studies evaluating university students’ work, comparing feedback from ChatGPT with human professors. Review Article",173
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,1,"EBSCO, ACM Digital Library, and IEEE Xplore, with additional gray literature sources, until October 2024. Inclusion criteria were cross-sectional studies evaluating university students’ work, comparing feedback from ChatGPT with human professors. Review Article",33
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,2,"Guardia-Paniura et al. 2 / 18 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 Data extraction was performed using a standardized form, and risk of bias was assessed with the Joanna Briggs Institute critical appraisal tool. A narrative synthesis of the results was made. PROSPERO registration number: CRD42024566691. This review included 8 studies with 461 students. ChatGPT feedback was detailed and rapid, while human feedback was valued for its personalization and emotional support. Students appreciated the detailed and immediate nature of ChatGPT feedback but noted its lack of emotional nuance and context-specific guidance. Human feedback was preferred for addressing individual learning needs and providing affective support. A combination of both types of feedback to maximize benefits. ChatGPT can assist human teachers by providing detailed and timely feedback to university students. However, human supervision is essential to ensure feedback is nuanced and contextually appropriate. A hybrid approach can optimize the learning experience in higher education. Further research is necessary to explore AI applications in educational settings and understand their impact on learning outcomes. Keywords: higher education, ChatGPT, feedback, systematic review INTRODUCTION Feedback is any",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,2,"A hybrid approach can optimize the learning experience in higher education. Further research is necessary to explore AI applications in educational settings and understand their impact on learning outcomes. Keywords: higher education, ChatGPT, feedback, systematic review INTRODUCTION Feedback is any information given to a student after their response to inform them about their performance. Educational feedback is an effective approach to enhance student learning. However, it can be labor-intensive, which motivates the use of automated feedback tools (Bauer et al., 2023; Dai et al., 2023a). Providing feedback to higher education students is an essential skill for teachers and significantly influences the learning process. The development of writing skills in university students is crucial for their academic and professional success. Constructive feedback from teachers plays a fundamental role by offering ideas and recommendations to improve students’ writing abilities. This contributes to a deeper understanding and enhances the ability to communicate effectively, having a significant impact on the professionalization of higher education (Al-Bashir et al., 2016; AlGhamdi, 2024). Providing individualized feedback for the student becomes challenging, as teachers are often overwhelmed in",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,2,"a deeper understanding and enhances the ability to communicate effectively, having a significant impact on the professionalization of higher education (Al-Bashir et al., 2016; AlGhamdi, 2024). Providing individualized feedback for the student becomes challenging, as teachers are often overwhelmed in large classes of students. Thus, these challenges have led to looking for innovative solutions, such as automated feedback using artificial intelligence (AI) (Al-Bashir et al., 2016; AlGhamdi, 2024). ChatGPT is an intelligent AI-developed chatbot that was launched in November 2022. It has multiple applications and the ability to generate various forms of text, answer questions, and provide translations (Xiao & Zhi, 2023). AI is a powerful data analysis tool that enhances the quality of feedback, which can boost productivity. Tools like ChatGPT can be useful for this purpose, providing individualized and timely feedback. However, they are limited in terms of quality, authenticity, and emotional intelligence. People may have a negative perception of these tools (Tong et al., 2021). In recent years, research on the use of AI in education has expanded considerably. Nevertheless, few studies have directly compared feedback generated",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,2,"quality, authenticity, and emotional intelligence. People may have a negative perception of these tools (Tong et al., 2021). In recent years, research on the use of AI in education has expanded considerably. Nevertheless, few studies have directly compared feedback generated by ChatGPT with that provided by human professors, particularly regarding students’ perceptions, emotional responses, and learning outcomes. Understanding these differences is essential for guiding evidence-based pedagogical practices and ensuring the ethical and effective integration of AI in higher education. This gap in the literature highlights the need for a systematic synthesis that examines the advantages and limitations of AI- and human-generated feedback, providing insights into how both can be combined to enhance the teaching-learning process. Therefore, this systematic review aims to compile and critically analyze studies comparing feedback generated by ChatGPT with that provided by human professors in higher education. The review was guided by the following research question: Among higher education students, how does feedback generated by ChatGPT compare with that provided by human professors in terms of effectiveness, quality, and students’ perceptions? MATERIALS AND METHODS Protocol and Registration",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,2,"The review was guided by the following research question: Among higher education students, how does feedback generated by ChatGPT compare with that provided by human professors in terms of effectiveness, quality, and students’ perceptions? MATERIALS AND METHODS Protocol and Registration This systematic review followed the methodological framework proposed by the PRISMA 2020 statement (Page et al., 2021) to ensure transparent and standardized reporting. The study protocol was prospectively",68
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,3,"Contemporary Educational Technology, 2026 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 3 / 18 registered in the PROSPERO database (international prospective register of systematic reviews) under the identification number CRD42024566691 (www.crd.york.ac.uk/PROSPERO) Eligibility Criteria Inclusion criteria The systematic review included studies that met the following PECO strategy: (1) participants: university students, (2) exposure: feedback from ChatGPT, (3) control: feedback from human professors, and (4) outcome: effectiveness of the feedback and student perceptions of the feedback. All cross-sectional studies that evaluated the work of university students comparing feedback from ChatGPT with feedback from human professors were included. Both cross-sectional and short-term longitudinal designs were eligible if they involved an evaluation of university students’ academic work comparing ChatGPT- and human-generated feedback. Exclusion criteria Studies that included postgraduate students or university professors, or that used ChatGPT for purposes other than generating feedback on university work, were excluded. Additionally, reviews and letters to the editor were excluded. Exposure and Control The exposure in this systematic review was feedback provided by ChatGPT, an AI chatbot. The control was feedback given by human professors. This comparison aimed to evaluate",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,3,"excluded. Additionally, reviews and letters to the editor were excluded. Exposure and Control The exposure in this systematic review was feedback provided by ChatGPT, an AI chatbot. The control was feedback given by human professors. This comparison aimed to evaluate the effectiveness and perceptions of both feedback sources in the context of higher education. Information sources and Search The search strategy included the following electronic bibliographic databases: Web of Science, Scopus, EBSCO, ACM Digital Library, and IEEE Xplore. Additionally, gray literature was searched using the Brazilian digital library of theses and dissertations, OpenGrey, ProQuest, and Google Scholar (first 100 records). The search terms related to “higher education,” “ChatGPT,” and “feedback,” and were combined using Boolean operators “OR” and “AND.” No restrictions were applied to the year of publication or language initially. Additionally, a manual search was conducted to identify eligible studies. Studies published up to October 2024 were included in the systematic review. The search strategy was adapted for each database (see Appendix A). All collected records were imported into EndNote Web (www.myendnoteweb.com), where duplicates were removed. Study Selection All",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,3,"studies. Studies published up to October 2024 were included in the systematic review. The search strategy was adapted for each database (see Appendix A). All collected records were imported into EndNote Web (www.myendnoteweb.com), where duplicates were removed. Study Selection All records were imported into Rayyan software for the initial phase of study selection. In this phase, two reviewers (ÁT-P and MP-M) independently screened the titles and abstracts. During the second phase, the full texts of potentially eligible studies were reviewed to confirm whether they met the eligibility criteria. Any discrepancies between the reviewers were resolved by consensus. Data Extraction and Data Items Data extraction was performed independently by two reviewers (ÁT-P and MP-M) using a standardized form in Microsoft Excel. If there was any discrepancy, it was resolved by consensus. The extracted data included: first author and year of publication, geographic region, total number of participants, participant demographics (age and sex), type of work evaluated, details of the feedback provided (ChatGPT and human professors), study variables and results (Appendix B, Appendix C, Appendix D, and Appendix E).",177
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,3,"geographic region, total number of participants, participant demographics (age and sex), type of work evaluated, details of the feedback provided (ChatGPT and human professors), study variables and results (Appendix B, Appendix C, Appendix D, and Appendix E).",37
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,4,"Guardia-Paniura et al. 4 / 18 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 Risk of Bias in Individual Studies The risk of bias in individual studies was assessed using the “Joanna Briggs Institute critical appraisal checklist for analytical cross-sectional studies” from the Joanna Briggs Institute (Moola et al., 2020). This tool consists of eight items that evaluate various criteria of the studies, including the clear definition of inclusion criteria, detailed description of the subjects and the study setting, the validity and reliability of exposure measurement, the use of objective standard criteria for condition measurement, identification of confounding factors and strategies to manage them, the validity and reliability of outcome measurement, and the adequacy of the statistical analysis used. The possible responses to each item were: “yes,” “no,” “unclear,” or “not applicable,” as appropriate. Two reviewers independently assessed each study, with discrepancies resolved by consensus. Summary Measures Effectiveness was measured using various metrics such as the intraclass coefficient, absolute values, and percentages. Student perceptions were assessed through surveys and Likert scale ratings, capturing measures of satisfaction and perceived quality of the feedback. Synthesis",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,4,"by consensus. Summary Measures Effectiveness was measured using various metrics such as the intraclass coefficient, absolute values, and percentages. Student perceptions were assessed through surveys and Likert scale ratings, capturing measures of satisfaction and perceived quality of the feedback. Synthesis of Results A narrative synthesis of the results was structured around the comparison of feedback from ChatGPT and human professors, including student perceptions. The data synthesis focused on identifying common themes and differences in feedback effectiveness and student perceptions across the included studies. RESULTS Study Selection A total of five databases and gray literature were searched, yielding 288 records. After excluding duplicates, 246 records were included for the title and abstract screening phase. Of these, 210 studies were excluded as they did not meet the eligibility criteria. Out of the remaining 36 studies, 16 were deemed ineligible for this review. Therefore, 20 studies proceeded to the full-text review phase. Thus, 12 studies were excluded for various reasons. Two studies were excluded from being reviews (Bauer et al., 2023; Cowling et al., 2023), one study was excluded for using ChatGPT in",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,4,"Therefore, 20 studies proceeded to the full-text review phase. Thus, 12 studies were excluded for various reasons. Two studies were excluded from being reviews (Bauer et al., 2023; Cowling et al., 2023), one study was excluded for using ChatGPT in the supervision of postgraduate student investigations (Dai et al., 2023b), and one study focused on high school teachers’ perception of ChatGPT use (El Sayary, 2023). Another article dealt with ChatGPT feedback without comparing it to human feedback (Yan, 2024). Seven studies were excluded because ChatGPT was used to assist in various tasks: a study on student perception using ChatGPT for Java programming (Haindl & Weinberger, 2024), a study on using ChatGPT to create a founding team within an entrepreneurship course (Hammoda, 2024), a study on using ChatGPT to facilitate the development of educational experiences in Roblox (Hoo & Lee, 2023), a study on the effectiveness of ChatGPT as a tool for developing English learning skills (Muniandy & Selvanathan, 2024), a study on using ChatGPT for learning (benefits, barriers, and possible solutions) (Ngo, 2023), a study about RECaP-GPT, which integrates human",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,4,"a study on the effectiveness of ChatGPT as a tool for developing English learning skills (Muniandy & Selvanathan, 2024), a study on using ChatGPT for learning (benefits, barriers, and possible solutions) (Ngo, 2023), a study about RECaP-GPT, which integrates human action and uses ChatGPT-4 as a feedback teaching support tool (Ossa & Willatt, 2023), and a study on using AI for a comprehensive review of existing film courses and AI- recommended courses (Yang et al., 2023). Thus, this systematic review included 8 studies (AlGhamdi, 2024; Escalante et al., 2023; Guo & Wang, 2023; Ivanovic, 2023; Jukiewicz, 2024; Lu et al., 2024; Tossell et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024) (Figure 1). Study Characteristics The included studies were published between 2022 and 2024, as ChatGPT was launched in November 2022. One study was from Saudi Arabia (AlGhamdi, 2024), two studies were from the USA (Escalante et al., 2023; Tossell et al., 2024), three studies were from China (Guo & Wang, 2023; Lu et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024), one study was from Montenegro (Ivanovic, 2022), and one study was from",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,4,"from the USA (Escalante et al., 2023; Tossell et al., 2024), three studies were from China (Guo & Wang, 2023; Lu et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024), one study was from Montenegro (Ivanovic, 2022), and one study was from Poland (Jukiewicz, 2024). In total, 461 higher education students were included, 202 were men and 150 women, aged 18 to 36 years. Two studies did not report the gender and age of participants (Jukiewicz, 2024; Wang et al., 2024).",79
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,5,"Contemporary Educational Technology, 2026 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 5 / 18 The evaluated work included essay writing as part of semester assignments in various fields and writing assignments in English courses. The analyzed variables included clarity, usefulness, preference, quality, organization, educational value, and confidence in the evaluation. All studies compared feedback from human instructors with feedback from ChatGPT. Two studies used ChatGPT-4 (Escalante et al., 2023; Tossell et al., 2024), four studies used ChatGPT-3.5 (Ivanovic, 2023; Jukiewicz, 2024; Lu et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024), and two studies did not specify the version used (AlGhamdi, 2024; Guo & Wang, 2023). Most of the studies evaluated student assignments over a period of 6 weeks (AlGhamdi, 2024; Escalante et al., 2023; Lu et al., 2024), while others evaluated work from a single class session (Guo & Wang, 2023), 8 weeks (Wang et al., 2024), two months (Ivanovic, 2023; Tossell et al., 2024), and 15 weeks (Jukiewicz, 2024) (Appendix B, Appendix C, Appendix D, and Appendix E). Risk of Bias Within Studies The quality assessments of the individual studies are listed",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,5,"(Wang et al., 2024), two months (Ivanovic, 2023; Tossell et al., 2024), and 15 weeks (Jukiewicz, 2024) (Appendix B, Appendix C, Appendix D, and Appendix E). Risk of Bias Within Studies The quality assessments of the individual studies are listed in Table 1. This evaluation is based on the Joanna Briggs Institute critical appraisal tool for analytical cross-sectional studies. The eight included studies exhibit various levels of bias risk. Six studies present a low risk of bias due to their rigorous designs, clear inclusion criteria, and standardized evaluations (AlGhamdi, 2024; Escalante et al., 2023; Guo & Wang, 2023; Ivanović, 2022; Lu et al., 2024; Tossell et al., 2024). However, these studies have limitations that may affect the generalizability of the results. AlGhamdi (2024) included only male students. Tossell et al. (2024) used a small, homogeneous sample of USAFA cadets. Escalante et al. (2023) had self-selected participants and a homogeneous sample. Guo and Wang (2023) worked with a limited sample of five teachers and self-selected participants. Ivanović (2022) faced limitations due to the limited variability in the sample and the possible",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,5,"et al. (2023) had self-selected participants and a homogeneous sample. Guo and Wang (2023) worked with a limited sample of five teachers and self-selected participants. Ivanović (2022) faced limitations due to the limited variability in the sample and the possible influence of human evaluators. Lu et al. (2024) presented a limitation in the homogeneity of the sample of Chinese students and the possible influence of the feedback sequence. Figure 1. Flowchart of study selection for qualitative syntheses (Authors’ own elaboration, based on PRISMA 2020 guidelines)",85
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,6,"Guardia-Paniura et al. 6 / 18 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 On the other hand, the studies by Jukiewicz (2024) and Wang et al. (2024) present a moderate risk of bias due to the lack of detailed information about the age and gender of the participants. Although both studies used blind designs and standardized evaluations, the homogeneity of their samples limits the generalizability and representativeness of the results (Figure 2). Table 1. JBI critical appraisal checklist for analytical cross sectional studies Questions AlGhamdi (2024) Escalante et al. (2023) Guo and Wang (2023) Ivanovic (2023) Jukiewicz (2024) Lu et al. (2024) Tossell et al. (2024) Wang et al. (2024) 1. Were the criteria for inclusion in the sample clearly defined? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 2. Were the study subjects and the setting described in detail? Yes Yes Yes Yes No Yes Yes No 3. Was the exposure measured in a valid and reliable way? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 4. Were objective, standard criteria used for measurement of the condition? Yes Yes Yes Yes",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,6,"Yes Yes No Yes Yes No 3. Was the exposure measured in a valid and reliable way? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 4. Were objective, standard criteria used for measurement of the condition? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 5. Were confounding factors identified? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 6. Were strategies to deal with confounding factors stated? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 7. Were the outcomes measured in a valid and reliable way? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes 8. Was appropriate statistical analysis used? Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Yes Figure 2. Risk of bias graph (Authors’ own elaboration using the Joanna Briggs Institute critical appraisal tool)",124
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,7,"Contemporary Educational Technology, 2026 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 7 / 18 Results of Individual Studies The study conducted by AlGhamdi (2024) found that feedback generated by ChatGPT had emotional, psychological, and educational impacts on first-year computing students. Responses to the feedback ranged from positive emotions, such as motivation and enthusiasm, to negative ones, such as frustration and confusion. Regarding quality and usefulness, some students appreciated the detailed improvements provided by ChatGPT, while others criticized its lack of consistency and personalization. In terms of development and progress, many students acknowledged improvements in their writing skills due to regular and detailed feedback, although some noted the lack of personalization compared to human feedback. In summary, the study highlights the potential of ChatGPT to provide useful and timely feedback but emphasizes the need to complement it with human comments to more effectively address the emotional and educational needs of students. The study by Escalante et al. (2023) indicates that there were no significant differences between the feedback generated by ChatGPT-4 and human instructors. Approximately the same number of students preferred AI-generated feedback",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,7,"address the emotional and educational needs of students. The study by Escalante et al. (2023) indicates that there were no significant differences between the feedback generated by ChatGPT-4 and human instructors. Approximately the same number of students preferred AI-generated feedback and human feedback. Some characteristics of AI feedback include clarity and specificity, while human feedback is valued for its affective benefits and direct interaction. The results suggest that AI-generated feedback can be incorporated into student essay evaluations without negatively affecting learning outcomes, and they recommend a mixed approach that combines the strengths of both types of feedback. The results of the study by Guo and Wang (2023) showed that ChatGPT generated longer, more detailed, and specific feedback compared to human instructors, who focused on issues related to content and language. Additionally, ChatGPT provided more balanced comments. Instructors expressed both positive and negative perceptions, noting that ChatGPT can complement their own feedback. However, human supervision and adjustment are necessary to maximize its effectiveness in developing writing skills. The study by Ivanovic (2023) compared feedback from human instructors and ChatGPT, finding good",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,7,"and negative perceptions, noting that ChatGPT can complement their own feedback. However, human supervision and adjustment are necessary to maximize its effectiveness in developing writing skills. The study by Ivanovic (2023) compared feedback from human instructors and ChatGPT, finding good consistency and reliability (intraclass correlation coefficient [ICC] of 0.8). ChatGPT can evaluate work in less than 30 seconds and provide detailed analysis similar to human instructors, objectively based on training data. It may be more lenient with minor errors, often giving slightly higher grades. However, it is not capable of capturing emotional and cultural nuances and has difficulty detecting inconsistencies in lengthy texts. On the other hand, human instructors take about 10 to 30 minutes to perform evaluations, providing detailed and individualized analysis. However, they can be more critical and may be influenced by personal biases and fatigue, potentially resulting in lower grades. ChatGPT can serve as an evaluation assistant, offering immediate and meaningful feedback, thereby reducing the workload. The study by Jukiewicz (2024) found a strong positive correlation between grades given by ChatGPT and human instructors, with an insignificant",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,7,"in lower grades. ChatGPT can serve as an evaluation assistant, offering immediate and meaningful feedback, thereby reducing the workload. The study by Jukiewicz (2024) found a strong positive correlation between grades given by ChatGPT and human instructors, with an insignificant difference between the two. ChatGPT-generated grades were slightly lower than those given by human instructors, as ChatGPT appeared to be stricter regarding programming assignment standards and more adept at detecting code errors. Human instructors tended to give higher grades to work that wasn’t perfect, provided the code functioned and met the task requirements. This study evaluated assignments in a Programming course within the cognitive science program, using Python programming tasks. Lu et al. (2024) evaluated the differences in feedback provided by ChatGPT and human instructors for academic writing tasks in Chinese. They found moderate to good consistency between the scores given by human instructors and ChatGPT (ICC = 0.6 and ICC = 0.75, respectively). ChatGPT provided more general and extensive evaluations, while human instructors offered specific explanations and solutions. Human instructor feedback was more frequently implemented by students (80.2%) compared",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,7,"given by human instructors and ChatGPT (ICC = 0.6 and ICC = 0.75, respectively). ChatGPT provided more general and extensive evaluations, while human instructors offered specific explanations and solutions. Human instructor feedback was more frequently implemented by students (80.2%) compared to ChatGPT feedback (59.9%). The integration of ChatGPT in evaluations promoted a deeper understanding and independent thinking in student revisions, significantly improving their academic writing. The study by Tossell et al. (2024) indicates that ChatGPT did not simplify students’ writing tasks but changed how they perceive and approach assignments given by instructors, thereby improving their learning. Initially, students viewed ChatGPT as a fraudulent tool requiring human supervision, technical competence, and calibrated trust. After using it, students recognized it as a valuable learning tool, perceiving it as more ethical and benevolent. Despite this, they showed low comfort in taking responsibility for tasks completed",142
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,8,"Guardia-Paniura et al. 8 / 18 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 with ChatGPT’s assistance due to ethical concerns and a lack of confidence in the accuracy of its results. Students preferred to be evaluated by both ChatGPT and the instructor, rather than by ChatGPT alone. The study by Wang et al. (2024) evaluated ChatGPT’s ability to provide feedback on university students’ arguments and found that ChatGPT demonstrated high accuracy (91.8%) in evaluating quantitative points such as claims, evidence, and refutations, although its recall rate was 63.2%. ChatGPT’s accuracy decreased with longer arguments and was influenced by the use of discourse markers. It provides more extensive, rapid, and text-based feedback, relying on data, but struggles to deliver affective feedback that is effective for students. In contrast, feedback from human instructors is more focused and based on experience. DISCUSSION This systematic review analyzed eight studies comparing feedback generated by ChatGPT with that provided by human professors in higher education. The findings show that AI-generated feedback offers distinctive advantages in clarity, comprehensiveness, and immediacy. ChatGPT provides structured and extensive comments that allow students",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,8,"analyzed eight studies comparing feedback generated by ChatGPT with that provided by human professors in higher education. The findings show that AI-generated feedback offers distinctive advantages in clarity, comprehensiveness, and immediacy. ChatGPT provides structured and extensive comments that allow students to promptly identify areas for improvement (Guo & Wang, 2023; Ivanovic, 2023). Its ability to analyze and produce feedback within seconds substantially reduces turnaround time compared to human instructors, who may require several minutes to review a single essay (Jukiewicz, 2024). This immediacy promotes iterative learning cycles, enabling students to apply feedback quickly and observe their progress in real time. Such efficiency supports self-regulated learning, a cornerstone of higher education, by empowering students to monitor and adjust their performance independently (Escalante et al., 2023). Additionally, the consistency and objectivity of AI-based feedback minimize discrepancies often observed among human evaluators, particularly in large classes where instructors face heavy workloads (Messer et al., 2024). These aspects collectively position ChatGPT as a potentially transformative tool for formative assessment, enhancing accessibility and timeliness in feedback delivery. However, these technological strengths coexist with clear pedagogical",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,8,"in large classes where instructors face heavy workloads (Messer et al., 2024). These aspects collectively position ChatGPT as a potentially transformative tool for formative assessment, enhancing accessibility and timeliness in feedback delivery. However, these technological strengths coexist with clear pedagogical limitations. Despite providing detailed and objective analyses, ChatGPT lacks the emotional intelligence and empathic communication that are fundamental to effective human feedback. Several studies reported that AI-generated comments, although comprehensive, sometimes fail to acknowledge students’ affective needs or learning struggles, producing an impersonal experience that may reduce motivation (AlGhamdi, 2024; Wang et al., 2024). For learners with lower language proficiency, excessive or poorly contextualized feedback can even increase anxiety and hinder comprehension (Guo & Wang, 2023). This suggests that feedback is not merely a cognitive tool but also a social and emotional exchange that shapes students’ confidence, self-efficacy, and persistence. Without empathy or encouragement, feedback, no matter how precise, risks becoming mechanistic and detached from the human dimensions of learning. Human professors, in contrast, provide feedback enriched by context, experience, and emotional resonance. Teachers often tailor their comments to students’",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,8,"Without empathy or encouragement, feedback, no matter how precise, risks becoming mechanistic and detached from the human dimensions of learning. Human professors, in contrast, provide feedback enriched by context, experience, and emotional resonance. Teachers often tailor their comments to students’ backgrounds and personalities, fostering trust and engagement. Their affective and motivational phrasing, acknowledging effort, improvement, and potential helps learners internalize constructive criticism and transform it into self-growth (Lu et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024). Furthermore, human feedback can interpret subtleties of expression, creativity, and intent that AI systems cannot yet capture reliably. These qualitative dimensions of pedagogy are essential to the development of higher-order skills such as critical thinking, reflection, and ethical reasoning. Nevertheless, human feedback is limited by time, subjectivity, and variability between evaluators, which may compromise consistency and scalability in large academic environments. The complementary nature of AI and human feedback emerges as one of the most significant findings of this review. Students perceive ChatGPT not as a substitute for human professors but as a collaborative tool that can reinforce and extend traditional feedback (Tossell et al.,",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,8,"AI and human feedback emerges as one of the most significant findings of this review. Students perceive ChatGPT not as a substitute for human professors but as a collaborative tool that can reinforce and extend traditional feedback (Tossell et al., 2024). When used under professor supervision, AI can handle repetitive or technical aspects, such as grammar, coherence, or structure–while professors focus on higher-order elements like argument quality, originality, and conceptual depth (Escalante et al., 2023; Ivanovic, 2023). This division of cognitive labor aligns with contemporary educational theories that advocate human-machine symbiosis, where technology enhances rather than replaces pedagogy. Integrating both forms of feedback can increase fairness and timeliness while preserving the affective and contextual richness of professor-student relationships. However, such integration requires thoughtful pedagogical",124
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,9,"Contemporary Educational Technology, 2026 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 9 / 18 design to prevent overreliance on automated systems and ensure that human judgment remains central in evaluating complex and creative work. In practical terms, implementing a hybrid feedback model requires defining clear pedagogical roles for each agent. AI systems such as ChatGPT could be primarily employed for formative feedback, offering immediate, grammar or structure, related suggestions that support iterative learning. The human professors would remain responsible for summative evaluations and for addressing interpretative, ethical, and affective dimensions of student work. This division not only preserves academic integrity and emotional depth but also leverages AI’s scalability to alleviate workload pressures in large classes. To ensure effective integration, institutions should promote structured frameworks that combine automation with human supervision, establishing guidelines for transparency, verification, and student agency. The implications of these findings extend beyond classroom practice to institutional and ethical domains. Universities must establish clear policies regulating the use of AI tools, ensuring transparency, academic integrity, and data protection (Chan & Hu, 2023). Educators need training not only in how to",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,9,"findings extend beyond classroom practice to institutional and ethical domains. Universities must establish clear policies regulating the use of AI tools, ensuring transparency, academic integrity, and data protection (Chan & Hu, 2023). Educators need training not only in how to use ChatGPT effectively but also in how to critically evaluate its output, recognizing potential biases and limitations in language generation. Ethical literacy in AI should become an integral component of teacher education programs, fostering awareness of issues such as fairness, accountability, and human oversight. At the same time, the academic community should resist the tendency to delegate all evaluative functions to algorithms, as this could diminish students’ intellectual autonomy and critical reasoning abilities. Feedback should remain a dialogic process an exchange of meaning and reflection, rather than a one-way transmission of corrective information. Future research should adopt longitudinal and mixed-method approaches to examine the long-term cognitive, emotional, and behavioral impacts of AI-assisted feedback on student learning outcomes. Understanding how sustained exposure to AI feedback influences academic identity, motivation, and metacognitive awareness is essential for developing robust and sustainable pedagogical frameworks.",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,9,"approaches to examine the long-term cognitive, emotional, and behavioral impacts of AI-assisted feedback on student learning outcomes. Understanding how sustained exposure to AI feedback influences academic identity, motivation, and metacognitive awareness is essential for developing robust and sustainable pedagogical frameworks. Comparative studies across disciplines are also needed to investigate how feedback practices vary between technical and humanistic contexts. From a broader pedagogical perspective, the integration of AI tools such as ChatGPT challenges educators to redefine the very purpose of feedback, shifting it from evaluation to dialogue, and from correction to student autonomy. From a broader pedagogical perspective, the integration of AI tools such as ChatGPT challenges educators to redefine the very purpose of feedback, shifting it from evaluation to dialogue, and from correction to student autonomy. It is also important to consider that demographic and contextual variables, such as academic discipline, cultural background, and prior exposure to AI tools, may influence how students perceive and respond to different feedback sources. Future studies should explore these factors to better understand cross- disciplinary and cross-cultural differences in the reception and effectiveness of",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,9,"discipline, cultural background, and prior exposure to AI tools, may influence how students perceive and respond to different feedback sources. Future studies should explore these factors to better understand cross- disciplinary and cross-cultural differences in the reception and effectiveness of AI-assisted feedback. This systematic review has several limitations that must be considered. The included studies exhibit heterogeneity in design, sample size, and educational context, which may affect the generalizability of the findings. The rapid development of AI technologies such as ChatGPT also poses a methodological challenge, as research outcomes may quickly become outdated. Moreover, the emotional and contextual nuances that characterize human feedback are inherently difficult to quantify, complicating comparisons with AI-generated responses. Although most of the included studies were cross-sectional, some adopted short-term longitudinal or quasi-longitudinal designs, assessing feedback effectiveness or student perceptions over several weeks. Future research should therefore employ long-term longitudinal and mixed-method approaches to better integrate temporal, contextual, and affective dimensions of AI-generated feedback and its influence on learning outcomes. Additionally, AI-generated feedback can occasionally produce inaccurate or contextually inappropriate suggestions, which may mislead students or",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,9,"therefore employ long-term longitudinal and mixed-method approaches to better integrate temporal, contextual, and affective dimensions of AI-generated feedback and its influence on learning outcomes. Additionally, AI-generated feedback can occasionally produce inaccurate or contextually inappropriate suggestions, which may mislead students or distort their understanding of concepts. Its responses can vary across iterations, raising concerns about consistency and reproducibility. These challenges highlight the need for continuous human oversight and validation mechanisms when integrating AI into academic feedback systems.",76
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,10,"Guardia-Paniura et al. 10 / 18 Contemporary Educational Technology, 18(1), ep623 Beyond methodological aspects, it is also crucial to examine the ethical implications and potential biases inherent in AI tools, ensuring that their implementation complements, rather than replaces, the human elements essential to education. Overreliance on automated systems risks diminishing the irreplaceable pedagogical and mentoring roles of human professors, potentially leading to depersonalized learning environments. In addition, the use of AI for summative assessments raises questions of fairness, transparency, and accountability, as algorithms may misinterpret student intent or context. Data privacy and informed consent also represent major ethical concerns, given that AI models often rely on extensive datasets that could include sensitive educational information. Therefore, institutional policies must ensure data protection, academic integrity, and human oversight at every stage of AI use. While these technologies can enhance efficiency and accessibility, maintaining students’ intellectual autonomy and preventing dependency on automated systems remain critical goals. Recognizing and managing the ethical challenges associated with AI use in education is fundamental. Thus, the findings indicate that technologies like ChatGPT should be regarded as complements,",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,10,"maintaining students’ intellectual autonomy and preventing dependency on automated systems remain critical goals. Recognizing and managing the ethical challenges associated with AI use in education is fundamental. Thus, the findings indicate that technologies like ChatGPT should be regarded as complements, not replacements, to the human dimensions of education. A balanced combination of AI-driven efficiency and human empathy can cultivate more personalized, reflective, and ethically grounded learning environments in higher education. CONCLUSION ChatGPT provides effective and timely feedback, enhancing learning through detailed and rapid responses. However, its lack of emotional nuance and specific guidance suggests it cannot completely replace human feedback in higher education. Integrating feedback from ChatGPT with that from human professors can optimize the learning experience. A hybrid approach that combines both forms of feedback may be the most effective strategy for improving educational outcomes in higher education. Students value the speed and detail of ChatGPT’s feedback but prefer the personalization and empathy of feedback from human professors. From a practical standpoint, ChatGPT can be integrated as a formative tool to support professors in large classes, provide immediate feedback,",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,10,"value the speed and detail of ChatGPT’s feedback but prefer the personalization and empathy of feedback from human professors. From a practical standpoint, ChatGPT can be integrated as a formative tool to support professors in large classes, provide immediate feedback, and promote student self-reflection. Nevertheless, its use should follow clear pedagogical objectives and ethical guidelines to maintain human-centered education. Future studies should investigate the long-term effects of AI-assisted feedback, its impact on students’ motivation and autonomy, and its applicability across different disciplines and learning contexts. Combining AI efficiency with human empathy can create more inclusive, responsive, and ethically grounded learning environments in higher education. Author contributions: CHG-P: conceptualization, writing – review & editing; TC-L: formal analysis, writing – original draft; FMC-C: methodology, writing – original draft, writing – review & editing; RRI-D: data curation, writing – original draft; DVA-P: data curation, writing – original draft; NR-R: data curation, writing – original draft; BM-M: conceptualization, writing – review & editing; ÁT-P: methodology, writing – review & editing; RYFY: data curation, writing – review & editing; MP-M: methodology, writing – review &",180
A047,A047.pdf,10.30935/cedtech/17863,10,"curation, writing – original draft; NR-R: data curation, writing – original draft; BM-M: conceptualization, writing – review & editing; ÁT-P: methodology, writing – review & editing; RYFY: data curation, writing – review & editing; MP-M: methodology, writing – review & editing. All authors approved the final version of the article. Funding: This article was supported by the Coordenação de Aperfeiçoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior–Brasil (CAPES)–“Bolsista CAPES/BRASIL” finance code number 88887.967267/2024-00. Ethics declaration: This study is a systematic review and did not involve the collection of primary data from human participants. Therefore, ethical approval from a Research Ethics Committee and informed consent from participants were not required. AI statement: During the preparation of this manuscript, the authors used ChatGPT (OpenAI) to assist with reviewing the clarity and readability of the text. All content was critically reviewed and edited by the authors, who take full responsibility for the final version of the manuscript. Declaration of interest: The authors declared no competing interest. Data availability: Data generated or analyzed during",168
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,1,"Volume 23, 2024 Accepting Editor Peter Blakey │ Received: June 29, 2024 │ Revised: August 18, September 4, 2024 │ Accepted: September 9, 2024. Cite as: Zogheib, S., & Zogheib, B. (2024). Understanding university students’ adoption of ChatGPT: Insights from TAM, SDT, and beyond. Journal of Information Technology Education: Research, 23, Article 25. https://doi.org/10.28945/5377 (CC BY-NC 4.0) This article is licensed to you under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License. When you copy and redistribute this paper in full or in part, you need to provide proper attribution to it to ensure that others can later locate this work (and to ensure that others do not accuse you of plagiarism). You may (and we encour- age you to) adapt, remix, transform, and build upon the material for any non-commercial purposes. This license does not permit you to use this material for commercial purposes. UNDERSTANDING UNIVERSITY STUDENTS’ ADOPTION OF CHATGPT: INSIGHTS FROM TAM, SDT, AND BEYOND Salah Zogheib* King Abdulaziz University, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia salahzogheib@hotmail.com Bashar Zogheib American University of Kuwait, Salmiya, Kuwait bzogheib@auk.edu.kw ABSTRACT Aim/Purpose The aim of this",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,1,"material for commercial purposes. UNDERSTANDING UNIVERSITY STUDENTS’ ADOPTION OF CHATGPT: INSIGHTS FROM TAM, SDT, AND BEYOND Salah Zogheib* King Abdulaziz University, Jeddah, Saudi Arabia salahzogheib@hotmail.com Bashar Zogheib American University of Kuwait, Salmiya, Kuwait bzogheib@auk.edu.kw ABSTRACT Aim/Purpose The aim of this study is to explore the factors that influence higher education students’ adoption of ChatGPT by incorporating constructs from the Technol- ogy Acceptance Model (TAM) and Self-Determination Theory (SDT) with trust, social influence, and personal innovativeness. Background Even though the use of ChatGPT has become more popular among university students, there is no clear evidence about the reasons that would make them adopt or abstain from using such a tool. Methodology The study utilized a survey that was answered by 150 university students regis- tered in the faculty of engineering at a public university. The survey was devel- oped by Google Forms and focused on how useful and easy they think ChatGPT is, their motivations, trust, social influence, innovativeness, and their readiness to use it. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS 26 and Smart- PLS4, with the latter being particularly useful",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,1,"Forms and focused on how useful and easy they think ChatGPT is, their motivations, trust, social influence, innovativeness, and their readiness to use it. Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS 26 and Smart- PLS4, with the latter being particularly useful due to the study’s complex model and adherence to sample size criteria. Contribution This research provides fresh insights into how students perceive and start using modern AI tools like ChatGPT. It also helps educators and policymakers under- stand how to integrate AI technologies into education better to make learning more effective. Findings The study reveals that students are more likely to adopt ChatGPT if they per- ceive it as useful and easy to use. External motivation and social influence signif- icantly impact students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT, while trust also",131
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,2,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 2 plays a crucial role. Intrinsic motivation, however, does not significantly affect behavioral intention. The strongest predictor of actual use is behavioral inten- tion, indicating that students who intend to use ChatGPT are highly likely to do so. Personal innovativeness is another significant factor influencing both behav- ioral intention and actual use. Recommendations for Practitioners Educators and policymakers should focus on enhancing the perceived useful- ness, ease of use, trust, social influence, and innovativeness related to ChatGPT to increase its adoption in educational settings. Recommendations for Researchers Future research should explore additional psychological and contextual factors that may influence the adoption of ChatGPT and other similar technologies among students. Impact on Society Understanding the factors that influence the adoption of ChatGPT can help in developing strategies to integrate such tools effectively in education, potentially improving learning outcomes and digital literacy among students. Future Research Further studies should examine the long-term effects of ChatGPT usage on students’ learning outcomes and investigate the adoption patterns in different educational contexts and disciplines. Keywords ChatGPT, higher education, TAM,",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,2,"improving learning outcomes and digital literacy among students. Future Research Further studies should examine the long-term effects of ChatGPT usage on students’ learning outcomes and investigate the adoption patterns in different educational contexts and disciplines. Keywords ChatGPT, higher education, TAM, motivational drivers INTRODUCTION In an era where technology is becoming an essential part of educational curricula worldwide, incor- porating artificial intelligence (AI) tools into educational systems offers new opportunities for en- hancing learning processes. ChatGPT has emerged as a notable example among the various AI tools available, providing users with extensive support and rapid responses. It facilitates engaging interac- tions on various subjects (Dwivedi et al., 2023). ChatGPT is particularly favored for the natural and intuitive experience it offers learners (Baidoo-anu & Ansah, 2023). Its versatility is evident, as it is used by business owners, educators, teachers, and university students alike for diverse tasks such as writing assignments, developing websites, managing social media platforms, and marketing (Wang et al., 2023). Among its various applications, one of the most common is assisting students in improving their language skills, especially in producing",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,2,"alike for diverse tasks such as writing assignments, developing websites, managing social media platforms, and marketing (Wang et al., 2023). Among its various applications, one of the most common is assisting students in improving their language skills, especially in producing gram- matically correct sentences (Kung et al., 2023). Despite ChatGPT’s growing popularity, there needs to be more evidence of the factors influencing its acceptance and use. Specifically, there needs to be more understanding of the motivational drivers among higher education students. Previous research has applied models like the Technology Ac- ceptance Model (TAM) and Self-Determination Theory (SDT) to explore technology use in educa- tion (e.g., Ryan & Deci, 2000; B. Zogheib & Daniela, 2022; S. Zogheib, 2024). TAM typically focuses on perceived usefulness and ease of use, while SDT examines intrinsic and extrinsic motivations. However, integrating these theories to study AI tools like ChatGPT in education remains an ongoing process, as ChatGPT is one of the newest technologies. Additional constructs, such as trust (Dickson et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2011), social influence (Graf-Vlachy et al., 2018), and personal innovativeness",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,2,"AI tools like ChatGPT in education remains an ongoing process, as ChatGPT is one of the newest technologies. Additional constructs, such as trust (Dickson et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2011), social influence (Graf-Vlachy et al., 2018), and personal innovativeness (Ofosu-Ampong et al., 2023), may also play significant roles in technology adoption. This study aims to address these gaps by developing a comprehensive model that integrates TAM and SDT with trust, social influence, and personal innovativeness constructs. By examining university students’ intentions to use ChatGPT and their actual use, this research seeks to contribute valuable insights into the burgeoning literature on AI in education. Understanding these dynamics is crucial as",110
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,3,"Zogheib & Zogheib 3 AI continues to reshape educational landscapes, providing timely and critical insights into the factors affecting AI technology adoption in higher education settings. In conclusion, this paper explores the intersection of technology acceptance and motivation theories within the context of AI and education. It addresses the literature gap by examining the variables in- fluencing ChatGPT adoption among university students, offering a comprehensive view of AI tool adoption in education and potentially guiding more effective and widespread use of AI technologies like ChatGPT. LITERATURE REVIEW The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) tools like ChatGPT into educational systems has sparked much discussion about how these technologies might transform traditional teaching methods. This literature review explores the use of ChatGPT across various educational contexts, highlighting its strengths and limitations and examining the key factors that influence its adoption. In the realm of mathematics education, research by Frieder et al. (2023) shows that while ChatGPT handles simple questions quite well, it struggles with more complex problems that require advanced mathematical thinking. Such findings highlight a significant limitation of ChatGPT: it still",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,3,"realm of mathematics education, research by Frieder et al. (2023) shows that while ChatGPT handles simple questions quite well, it struggles with more complex problems that require advanced mathematical thinking. Such findings highlight a significant limitation of ChatGPT: it still needs to be capable of addressing all types of mathematical challenges. Educators need to be aware of these limi- tations to make the most out of this tool. Further research should investigate how factors like per- ceived usefulness and ease of use, central to the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), influence students’ acceptance of ChatGPT in mathematics education. Additional studies by Pardos and Bhandari (2023) and Shakarian et al. (2023) reveal that while ChatGPT can generate basic algebra prompts, more is needed to match the effectiveness of prompts created by human teachers. There is potential for improvement through algorithmic enhancements, which could make ChatGPT more useful in educational settings. These findings suggest that alt- hough ChatGPT shows promise, significant improvements are needed to make it a more effective educational tool. When we look at broader applications of ChatGPT, the reception is",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,3,"could make ChatGPT more useful in educational settings. These findings suggest that alt- hough ChatGPT shows promise, significant improvements are needed to make it a more effective educational tool. When we look at broader applications of ChatGPT, the reception is mixed. Wardat et al. (2023) found that early adopters are cautiously optimistic about AI tools in education. However, studies in other fields, such as those by Lehnert (2023) in physics and Nov et al. (2023) in medicine, point out that ChatGPT still has limitations in handling specialized tasks. This underscores the need for ongo- ing improvements to make the tool more effective. The urgency of innovation is clear, and it is cru- cial for the future success of ChatGPT in education. Social influence also plays a crucial role in technology adoption. Research by Graf-Vlachy et al. (2018) and Prasarry et al. (2023) shows that social influence can significantly impact how new technologies are accepted. The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) by Venkatesh et al. (2003) highlights that social influence is powerful during the early stages of",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,3,"al. (2023) shows that social influence can significantly impact how new technologies are accepted. The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) by Venkatesh et al. (2003) highlights that social influence is powerful during the early stages of technology adoption. In educational settings, the opinions of influential figures like teachers and administrators can significantly affect whether students and faculty are willing to adopt new technologies. Research by Lee et al. (2013) supports this, showing that the social environment can encourage or deter technology use. Understanding these dynamics is crucial for designing effective strategies to implement AI tools like ChatGPT. Trust is another crucial factor in adopting new technologies. Mayer et al. (1995) argue that trust helps reduce perceived risks, especially in education, where concerns about data privacy and AI accuracy are significant. For educators and students to trust and effectively use ChatGPT, the tool must be transparent and demonstrate its value. This emphasis on trust underscores the need for transparency and reliability in the development and use of AI tools like ChatGPT.",174
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,3,"and effectively use ChatGPT, the tool must be transparent and demonstrate its value. This emphasis on trust underscores the need for transparency and reliability in the development and use of AI tools like ChatGPT.",34
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,4,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 4 Personal innovativeness, or the willingness to embrace new technologies, is also essential. Research by He and Zhu (2017) and Ofosu-Ampong et al. (2023) shows that people with high personal inno- vativeness are often the first to adopt new technologies. Fostering a culture of innovation can speed up the adoption of tools like ChatGPT in educational settings. The Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), introduced by Davis (1989), provides a framework for understanding how people adopt and use technology. TAM suggests that perceived ease of use (PEU) and perceived usefulness (PU) are crucial factors in technology acceptance. Studies by Rasi- mah et al. (2011) and Šumak et al. (2011) support TAM’s claims, showing that PU and PEU strongly influence user attitudes and intentions. Recent research has expanded TAM to include additional fac- tors, such as social influences, offering a more comprehensive view of technology adoption (Burhan- Horasanlı, 2022; Luo et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023; B. Zogheib & Daniela, 2022). Building on the above insights, this study aims to integrate TAM with concepts like trust,",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,4,"influences, offering a more comprehensive view of technology adoption (Burhan- Horasanlı, 2022; Luo et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023; B. Zogheib & Daniela, 2022). Building on the above insights, this study aims to integrate TAM with concepts like trust, social influ- ence, and personal innovativeness to understand better the factors affecting the adoption of ChatGPT among university students. By doing so, it hopes to contribute valuable insights to the growing field of AI in education, offering a nuanced understanding of how innovative educational technologies are adopted. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK This section delves into the theoretical frameworks that help explain the adoption and effective utili- zation of ChatGPT in educational settings. The integration of ChatGPT, as explored in various stud- ies, raises significant questions about the factors influencing its acceptance and effectiveness. This study draws upon established information systems and psychology models, notably the TAM and SDT, supplemented by insights into trust, social influence, personal innovativeness, gender, and ex- perience. TECHNOLOGY ACCEPTANCE MODEL (TAM) TAM was developed by Davis (1989), and it suggests that P U and P EOU are significant",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,4,"models, notably the TAM and SDT, supplemented by insights into trust, social influence, personal innovativeness, gender, and ex- perience. TECHNOLOGY ACCEPTANCE MODEL (TAM) TAM was developed by Davis (1989), and it suggests that P U and P EOU are significant indicators and determinants of users’ acceptance and usage of technological tools. TAM is especially convenient in the context of ChatGPT acceptance among university students, as the perceived utility of ChatGPT in handling complex problems and its ease of use might be essential to determine users’ willingness to use ChatGPT. Derived hypotheses include: H1: Perceived usefulness (P U) of ChatGPT will have a significant influence on students’ be- havioral intention to use (B I) ChatGPT. H2: Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) of ChatGPT will have a significant influence on stu- dents’ behavioral intention to use (B I) ChatGPT. H3: Behavioral intention (B I) to use ChatGPT will significantly influence ChatGPT actual use (A U). H4: Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) will positively influence the perceived usefulness (PU) of ChatGPT. SELF-DETERMINATION THEORY (SDT) Articulated by Ryan and Deci (2013), SDT",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,4,"intention (B I) to use ChatGPT will significantly influence ChatGPT actual use (A U). H4: Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) will positively influence the perceived usefulness (PU) of ChatGPT. SELF-DETERMINATION THEORY (SDT) Articulated by Ryan and Deci (2013), SDT distinguishes between intrinsic motivations, driven by the inherent satisfaction derived from an activity, and extrinsic motivations, influenced by external re- wards. In educational settings, understanding these motivational drivers is essential for designing AI tools that support academic tasks and align with the students’ desire for independence, competence, and connection with others (Berkowitz et al., 2017). Derived hypotheses include: H5: Intrinsic motivation (I M) related to ChatGPT will have a significant influence on stu- dents’ behavioral intention (B I) to use ChatGPT",121
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,5,"Zogheib & Zogheib 5 H6: Extrinsic motivation (E M) related to ChatGPT will have a significant influence on stu- dents’ behavioral intention (B I) to use ChatGPT. TRUST, SOCIAL INFLUENCE, AND PERSONAL INNOVATIVENESS As explained earlier, these additional constructs enrich our understanding of technology adoption. Derived are the following hypotheses: H7: Trust (TR) in ChatGPT will have a significant influence on students’ behavioral intention to use (B I) ChatGPT. H8: Social influence (S INF) will have a significant influence on students’ behavioral intention to use (B I) ChatGPT. H9: Personal innovativeness (P INVT) in IT will have a significant influence on students’ be- havioral intention to use (B I) ChatGPT. H10: Personal innovativeness (P INVT) in IT will have a significant influence on students’ ac- tual use (A U) of ChatGPT. The above hypotheses provide a robust framework that will help investigate the factors influencing university students’ intentions to use ChatGPT and their actual use. They offer a comprehensive ap- proach to understanding how technological, motivational, and behavioral factors influence the adop- tion of AI-driven tools in higher education. The",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,5,"help investigate the factors influencing university students’ intentions to use ChatGPT and their actual use. They offer a comprehensive ap- proach to understanding how technological, motivational, and behavioral factors influence the adop- tion of AI-driven tools in higher education. The subsequent empirical validation of these hypotheses will utilize structured questionnaires and statistical methods like structural equation modeling to test the relationships proposed. Figure 1 shows the theoretical framework adopted in this study, the possible relationship among the various variables, their possible influence on behavioral intentions, and the actual use of ChatGPT for educational purposes. This model mainly depicts the possible influence of perceived ease of use, perceived usefulness, trust, personal innovativeness, social impact, and intrinsic and extrinsic motiva- tion on behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. It also depicts the possible influence of behavioral in- tention and personal innovativeness on the actual use of ChatGPT. Finally, the figure shows the pos- sible impact of perceived ease of use on perceived usefulness. Such possible relationships and predic- tions will be examined thoroughly in the Findings section. Figure 1. Suggested model",178
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,5,"actual use of ChatGPT. Finally, the figure shows the pos- sible impact of perceived ease of use on perceived usefulness. Such possible relationships and predic- tions will be examined thoroughly in the Findings section. Figure 1. Suggested model",38
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,6,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 6 METHODOLOGY SUBJECTS The study involved 150 university students from a Middle Eastern university. Of these, 90% were from the Faculty of Engineering and 10% from the Faculty of Science. Due to the small number of female participants (5%), gender was not considered in the analysis. The choice to focus on students in engineering and science was deliberate, as these fields are typically more engaged with new tech- nologies. This focus helps understand how students in these technology-oriented disciplines use and view ChatGPT in their academic work. SAMPLE Given the time constraints and limited access to students from other faculties, a convenience sam- pling method was used. The survey was created using Google Forms and shared through social me- dia apps to reach students who were easily accessible and willing to participate. This approach en- sured that we could efficiently gather data from a relevant group of students. INSTRUMENT A customized survey tool was created, integrating elements from the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self-Determination Theory (SDT), and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,6,"en- sured that we could efficiently gather data from a relevant group of students. INSTRUMENT A customized survey tool was created, integrating elements from the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self-Determination Theory (SDT), and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Tech- nology (UTAUT). Table 1 provides detailed information about the items included in the survey. We administered the survey a few weeks before final exams to avoid potential interference with students’ academic responsibilities. Ethical standards were rigorously followed, including informing partici- pants about the study’s objectives, ensuring their consent, and maintaining confidentiality of their re- sponses. Table 1. Survey items Factor Item Reference Perceived Usefulness “ChatGPT would improve my learning performance.” Foroughi et al. (2023) “ChatGPT would increase my productivity in college.” Foroughi et al. (2023) Perceived Ease of Use “I find ChatGPT easy to use.” Biloš and Budimir (2024) “It’s easy to become skillful at using ChatGPT.” Biloš and Budimir (2024) Trust “I believe that ChatGPT is trustworthy.” Formulated for this study “I am confident that the developer of ChatGPT will not exploit my personal information."" Formulated for",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,6,"(2024) “It’s easy to become skillful at using ChatGPT.” Biloš and Budimir (2024) Trust “I believe that ChatGPT is trustworthy.” Formulated for this study “I am confident that the developer of ChatGPT will not exploit my personal information."" Formulated for this study Social Influ- ence “People who influence my behavior think that I should use ChatGPT.” UTAUT (Venkatesh et al., 2003) “My family and friends frequently use ChatGPT services.” UTAUT (Venkatesh et al., 2003) Intrinsic Motivation “I use ChatGPT because I enjoy the learning process it facilitates.” Formulated for this study “Exploring ChatGPT’s capabilities is fun and satisfying.” Formulated for this study",102
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,7,"Zogheib & Zogheib 7 Factor Item Reference Extrinsic Motivation “I use ChatGPT to complete assignments more efficiently.” Formulated for this study “Achieving higher grades is a key reason I use ChatGPT.” Formulated for this study Personal Innovativeness “If I heard about a new information technol- ogy, I would look for ways to experiment with it.” Karaiskos et al. (2009); Soliman (2012) “Among my peers, I am usually the first to try out new information technologies.” Karaiskos et al. (2009); Soliman (2012) Behavioral Intention to Use “I intend to use ChatGPT in the future.” Formulated for this study “I have a strong intention to use ChatGPT reg- ularly.” Formulated for this study Actual Use “I use ChatGPT on a regular basis.” Formulated for this study “I use ChatGPT for a variety of tasks, such as information seeking, learning, and content cre- ation.” Formulated for this study “I rely on ChatGPT as an essential tool for my daily academic and personal activities.” Formulated for this study RESULTS SPSS 26 and Smart-PLS 4 were used in this paper due to their potential to",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,7,"ation.” Formulated for this study “I rely on ChatGPT as an essential tool for my daily academic and personal activities.” Formulated for this study RESULTS SPSS 26 and Smart-PLS 4 were used in this paper due to their potential to handle complex statistical models. The Smart PLS4 software platform is essential to our research purpose, especially since the sample size meets the recommended criterion. It stipulates that it should surpass tenfold the magni- tude of the largest cluster of indicators within the model. SPSS was also used to determine the signifi- cant correlations among the variables. Ensuring the model’s reliability requires examining composite reliability, convergent validity, and dis- criminant validity (Barclay et al., 1995). More importantly, the composite reliability coefficients for all constructs, as shown in Table 2, were above 0.82 and less than 0.95. As indicated by Nunnally and Bernstein (1994), such values ensure the reliability of the measures adopted in this study. Table 2. Composite reliability Composite reliability Perceived Usefulness (P U) 0.85 Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) 0.83 Behavioral intention (B I) 0.87 Trust (TR) 0.84",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,7,"Bernstein (1994), such values ensure the reliability of the measures adopted in this study. Table 2. Composite reliability Composite reliability Perceived Usefulness (P U) 0.85 Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) 0.83 Behavioral intention (B I) 0.87 Trust (TR) 0.84 Social Influence (S INF) 0.83 Personal Innovativeness (P INV) 0.85 Intrinsic Motivation (I M) 0.89 Extrinsic Motivation (E M) 0.90 Actual Use (A U) 0.90",65
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,8,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 8 Table 3 shows that the average variance extracted values for each construct are above 0.5, indicating that the convergent validity was accomplished and met the standards. This also indicates that there is a strong correlation among the various items within each construct. Likewise, the research methodology, which utilized the Fornell-Larcker criterion (Fornell & Larcker, 1981) to establish discriminant validity, guaranteed that every construct was measured separately. Table 3 demonstrates that each construct’s square root of the AVE was more significant than its connection with other constructs, confirming the measures’ uniqueness. Table 3. Average variance extracted (AVE) Perceived Usefulness (P U) 0.81 Perceived Ease of Use (P EOU) 0.78 Behavioral intention (B I) 0.65 Trust (TR) 0.78 Social Influence (S INF) 0.85 Personal Innovativeness (P INV) 0.58 Intrinsic Motivation (I M) 0.65 Extrinsic Motivation (E M) 0.73 Actual Use (A U) 0.81 The positive correlation between intrinsic motivation (I M) and personal innovativeness (P INV) (0.316** at the 1% level) suggests that students who are intrinsically motivated are more likely to be innovative in",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,8,"(E M) 0.73 Actual Use (A U) 0.81 The positive correlation between intrinsic motivation (I M) and personal innovativeness (P INV) (0.316** at the 1% level) suggests that students who are intrinsically motivated are more likely to be innovative in their use of ChatGPT, indicating that those driven by personal satisfaction and curiosity are more inclined to explore and adopt new technologies. The significant correlation between extrin- sic motivation (E M) and social influence (S INF) (0.219** at the 1% level) reveals that students who are motivated by external rewards are more influenced by social pressure when adopting ChatGPT. This implies that academic or social incentives substantially influence technology adoption. The nega- tive correlation between personal innovativeness (P INV) and social influence (S INF) (0.300** at the 1% level) shows that more innovative students tend to be less swayed by social norms. This suggests that early adopters of technology are less influenced by peer pressure and more driven by their inter- ests. Interestingly, the actual use of ChatGPT (A U) exhibits a strong positive correlation with behavioral intention (B I),",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,8,"social norms. This suggests that early adopters of technology are less influenced by peer pressure and more driven by their inter- ests. Interestingly, the actual use of ChatGPT (A U) exhibits a strong positive correlation with behavioral intention (B I), indicating that students who intend to use ChatGPT are likely to follow through. This emphasizes the importance of fostering positive behavioral intentions to increase actual usage. Stu- dents’ tendency to use ChatGPT increases when they perceive it as valuable and easy to use. This aligns with the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), reinforcing the need to highlight the tool’s benefits and usability to enhance adoption. Finally, all the variables except for intrinsic motivation significantly correlate with behavioral inten- tion (B I) to use ChatGPT. These findings provide valuable insights into the complex interplay of motivational, perceptual, and behavioral factors influencing students’ adoption and use of AI-driven tools in higher education. Table 4. Correlation coefficients I M E M P INV S INF TR P U P EOU B I A U I M 1 E M 0.078 1 P INV 0.316**",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,8,adoption and use of AI-driven tools in higher education. Table 4. Correlation coefficients I M E M P INV S INF TR P U P EOU B I A U I M 1 E M 0.078 1 P INV 0.316** 0.006 1 S INF -0.245** 0.219** -0.189** 1,48
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,9,"Zogheib & Zogheib 9 I M E M P INV S INF TR P U P EOU B I A U TR 0.047 -0.001 -0.262** 0.123 1 P U 0.217** 0.480** -0.133 0.300** 0.149 1 P EOU 0.175* 0.352** 0.060 0.151 0.032 0.441** 1 B I -0.139 0.495** -0.365** 0.465** 0.324** 0.556** 0.298** 1 A U -0.047 0.298** 0.060 0.350** 0.121 0.255** 0.241** 0.642** 1 ** Significance at the 0.01 level (2-tailed) * Significance at the 0.05 level (2-tailed) The study hypotheses testing reveals that eight out of ten factors significantly influence university students’ behavioral intention to use and actual use of ChatGPT (Table 5). External motivators strongly drive students’ intentions to use ChatGPT, and those who use it will likely use it for real. However, internal motivation and perceived ease of use do not significantly impact users’ intention to use ChatGPT. Students’ tendency to use ChatGPT increases when they perceive it as applicable and find it easy to use. Furthermore, students are more likely to utilize ChatGPT if they trust it, are more receptive to new technology, and are",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,9,"use ChatGPT. Students’ tendency to use ChatGPT increases when they perceive it as applicable and find it easy to use. Furthermore, students are more likely to utilize ChatGPT if they trust it, are more receptive to new technology, and are inspired by their friends. These findings demonstrate the vital roles of social influence, perceived utility, trust, and willingness to try new things in motivating pupils. Table 5. Hypotheses testing results Model’s coefficients β T statistics P values H1: P U-> B I 0.186 2.425 0.015 H2: P EOU -> B I 0.045 0.483 0.629 H3: B I -> A U 0.680 18.90 0.000 H4: P EOU-> P U 0.430 6.140 0.000 H5: E M -> B I 0.351 4.509 0.000 H6: I M-> B I -0.120 1.261 0.207 H7: TR -> B I 0.238 4.420 0.000 H8: S INF -> B I 0.220 2.807 0.005 H9: P INV -> B I -0.234 3.340 0.001 H10: P INV -> A U 0.349 5.479 0.000 Figure 2 visually represents the SEM model, illustrating the R2 value, significant path coefficients, and p-values. This",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,9,"B I 0.220 2.807 0.005 H9: P INV -> B I -0.234 3.340 0.001 H10: P INV -> A U 0.349 5.479 0.000 Figure 2 visually represents the SEM model, illustrating the R2 value, significant path coefficients, and p-values. This graphical aid enhances the interpretation of the model’s complex relationships and un- derscores the critical findings derived from the analysis. DISCUSSION This study delves into the factors affecting university students’ behavioral intention and use of ChatGPT. The findings offer a rich context for understanding how different variables impact behav- ioral intentions and the actual use of this technology in educational settings.",101
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,10,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 10 Figure 2. Significant R2 value, significant path coefficients, and p-values One of the significant findings was that perceived usefulness predicted behavioral intention to use ChatGPT, whereas perceived ease of use did not. A possible explanation for this finding is that the utility of ChatGPT might overshadow its ease of use when students decide to use it. This makes sense as students seem willing to overcome all the obstacles that might prevent them from using ChatGPT, especially when they know the value they will get from it. This result highlights that while ease of use is essential, the practical benefits of the technology are crucial in driving its adoption. Fu- ture research could examine why perceived ease of use is less influential in this context and explore whether this pattern persists across different technologies or educational settings. Another significant finding is that extrinsic motivation affects users’ behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. This finding suggests that university students think of the external benefits they can get from using ChatGPT as the ultimate reward they seek",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,10,"or educational settings. Another significant finding is that extrinsic motivation affects users’ behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. This finding suggests that university students think of the external benefits they can get from using ChatGPT as the ultimate reward they seek at the expense of intrinsic motivation, which is significant in this case. At this stage of their lives, students may focus more on the tangible benefits they will get from using a new technology. If this technology means improved grades or other meas- urable outcomes, students are more motivated to use it. This aligns with existing research indicating that tangible rewards are a significant factor in technology adoption (Fagan et al., 2016; Khaliq et al., 2023; Tokan & Imakulata, 2019). Educators and developers should thus emphasize these practical benefits to align with students’ priorities. The role of trust in determining acceptance was found to be a significant predictor of students’ be- havioral intention to use ChatGPT. This reveals that students are more willing to use technologies they see as secure and reliable. In educational contexts where data privacy and accuracy",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,10,"acceptance was found to be a significant predictor of students’ be- havioral intention to use ChatGPT. This reveals that students are more willing to use technologies they see as secure and reliable. In educational contexts where data privacy and accuracy are para- mount, ensuring high security and transparency standards is crucial. Technology developers must pri- oritize these aspects to foster trust and promote adoption. It is intriguing to find out that personal innovativeness significantly influences ChatGPT use but not the behavioral intention to use it. The more innovative individuals are, the more likely they are to adopt new technologies; this does not seem to apply to individuals’ intention to use ChatGPT. One",112
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,11,"Zogheib & Zogheib 11 possible explanation could be users’ willingness to use ChatGPT lies in its utility and trustworthiness. However, individuals with higher levels of personal innovativeness were more likely to engage with ChatGPT once they started using it. This suggests that while personal innovativeness may not di- rectly impact the intention to adopt, it fosters continued use among those who overcome initial res- ervations. Further research could explore the characteristics of AI tools that appeal to innovative us- ers and address their concerns to improve adoption rates. Longitudinal studies also provide insights into how attitudes towards AI-driven tools evolve. The study also identified non-significant results that warrant further exploration. For instance, per- ceived ease of use did not significantly influence behavioral intention. This finding invites investiga- tion into why ease of use is less impactful than perceived usefulness. Are other factors at play over- shadowing ease of use, or is it a characteristic specific to ChatGPT? Addressing these questions could uncover more profound insights into technology adoption dynamics. The theoretical implications of these findings suggest an extension of TAM",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,11,"other factors at play over- shadowing ease of use, or is it a characteristic specific to ChatGPT? Addressing these questions could uncover more profound insights into technology adoption dynamics. The theoretical implications of these findings suggest an extension of TAM and SDT theories. The emphasis on perceived usefulness and extrinsic motivation highlights a need to reevaluate TAM’s fo- cus on ease of use and SDT’s consideration of external rewards. These theories may need adaptation to fit the context of AI tools in education. Practical implications for educators, policymakers, and technology developers include a focus on demonstrating practical benefits and ensuring robust security. Educators should emphasize how AI tools like ChatGPT can enhance learning outcomes. Policymakers and developers should prioritize transparency and data security to build user trust and promote technology adoption. Comparing these findings with studies conducted in various educational contexts could offer a broader perspective on their applicability. For instance, research in different regions or educational levels might reveal whether the observed trends hold universally or if they vary significantly. Acknowledging the study’s limitations adds transparency. The sample was",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,11,"contexts could offer a broader perspective on their applicability. For instance, research in different regions or educational levels might reveal whether the observed trends hold universally or if they vary significantly. Acknowledging the study’s limitations adds transparency. The sample was limited to specific univer- sity students, which may not fully represent the broader population. Additionally, the cross-sectional design provides a snapshot rather than longitudinal data. Future research should include a more di- verse sample and track attitude changes over time. Expanding the survey scope could also enhance understanding of the adoption factors for AI tools. In conclusion, this study contributes to understanding AI adoption in education by providing empiri- cal evidence on the various factors that influence university students’ use of ChatGPT. Our findings emphasize that perceived usefulness significantly influences students’ intention to use ChatGPT, un- derscoring the priority of functional benefits over ease of use in educational technology adoption. Additionally, extrinsic motivation highlights the need for educational tools to align with students’ im- mediate, tangible rewards, often prioritized over intrinsic motivations during their academic journey. Statistical analysis revealed that",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,11,"functional benefits over ease of use in educational technology adoption. Additionally, extrinsic motivation highlights the need for educational tools to align with students’ im- mediate, tangible rewards, often prioritized over intrinsic motivations during their academic journey. Statistical analysis revealed that perceived usefulness (β = 0.45, p < 0.01) and extrinsic motivation (β = 0.39, p < 0.01) were strong predictors of behavioral intention, while perceived ease of use did not show a significant impact (p > 0.05). These results suggest that the practical benefits and external re- wards associated with ChatGPT are more influential than its usability. The study also contributes to the theoretical understanding of technology adoption by suggesting that the TAM model’s emphasis on ease of use may need reconsideration in the context of AI tools in education. Instead, perceived usefulness appears to be more critical. Similarly, our findings suggest that SDT’s focus on intrinsic motivation might be less relevant for technologies like ChatGPT, where extrinsic rewards are more significant drivers. However, this study has a few limitations. The sample was primarily limited to male university stu- dents.",180
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,11,"findings suggest that SDT’s focus on intrinsic motivation might be less relevant for technologies like ChatGPT, where extrinsic rewards are more significant drivers. However, this study has a few limitations. The sample was primarily limited to male university stu- dents. Future research could adopt a more diverse participant pool that includes students from vari- ous universities and that has both male and female participants. Finally, this research paper examined a comprehensive set of factors that proved to be at the core of explaining the acceptance and use of ChatGPT. Additional unexplored variables, such as institutional support or pedagogical approaches,",99
A048,A048.pdf,10.28945/5377,12,"Understanding University Students’ Adoption of ChatGPT 12 can be added to future models exploring technology acceptance and the utilization of AI tools in ed- ucation. By addressing these issues, a better judgment can be reached about the design and imple- mentation of AI-driven educational intervent",45
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,1,"OPEN Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 1 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z The role of generative artificial intelligence as a catalyst for telecollaborative learning: insights from engineering Toochi Priscilia Farinola & Ildeniz Ozverir The emergence of generative AI has sparked debates recently. These discussions cut across its efficacy in developing students’ skills and improving learning outcomes. On the other hand, access to technology remains a major challenge, particularly in integrating telecollaborative learning into education. While ChatGPT and telecollaboration each show promise in educational contexts, no empirical research has tested whether they interact synergistically. This study employed a 2 × 2 factorial experimental design to examine their combination. Eighty engineering sophomores were randomly assigned to four conditions: (1) neither intervention (control), (2) ChatGPT only, (3) telecollaboration only, or (4) both ChatGPT and telecollaboration. All participants completed writing and website design projects over a 14-week course. A convergent mixed-methods design combined quantitative pretest-posttest measures with qualitative interviews (N = 20). Results revealed synergistic effects: Condition 4 students demonstrated a 11.90-point writing gain (18% improvement), substantially exceeding the sum of the individual intervention gains (5.33 +",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,1,"course. A convergent mixed-methods design combined quantitative pretest-posttest measures with qualitative interviews (N = 20). Results revealed synergistic effects: Condition 4 students demonstrated a 11.90-point writing gain (18% improvement), substantially exceeding the sum of the individual intervention gains (5.33 + 2.76 = 8.09, predicted). Two- way ANOVA confirmed a significant interaction effect, F(1,76) = 21.02, p<.001, η2p = .119, indicating that ChatGPT’s benefits are amplified within telecollaborative contexts. Qualitative themes revealed mechanisms and implementation risks. These findings suggest that careful integration of AI within collaborative frameworks produces emergent benefits not predictable from isolated intervention effects. The study provides implications for pedagogy in international education contexts. Keywords Generative AI, Telecollaboration, Artificial intelligence, Technology, Teaching and learning, Education Integrating educational resources such as artificial intelligence (AI) into diverse learning environments poses challenges. Research acknowledges that using technology and digital resources for teaching and learning may require ensuring equitable access and addressing varied tech proficiency among learners and educators1,2. Adapting to rapid technological advancements and sustaining practical pedagogical approaches can hinder integration3. A lot must be considered before integrating technology and digital resources",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,1,"and learning may require ensuring equitable access and addressing varied tech proficiency among learners and educators1,2. Adapting to rapid technological advancements and sustaining practical pedagogical approaches can hinder integration3. A lot must be considered before integrating technology and digital resources in the classroom. The same applies to new digital technologies like augmented reality, virtual reality, and artificial intelligence. Recent and prior scientific studies maintain that regardless of their potential, adequate measures must be considered before implementing them in the classroom4,5. A lot must be considered to implement digital and technological resources in teaching and learning. While generative AI holds significant potential in education, it also presents several challenges and concerns. For instance, generative AI models can inadvertently perpetuate or amplify biases, leading to unfair or discriminatory outcomes6. Excessive dependence on AI tools may diminish critical thinking skills and human interaction7. Students relying solely on AI-generated content may miss out on developing research and analytical skills8. Generative AI models may struggle to adapt to diverse learning styles and preferences5. Others admit that AI-generated content might not effectively cater to individualized learning",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,1,"Students relying solely on AI-generated content may miss out on developing research and analytical skills8. Generative AI models may struggle to adapt to diverse learning styles and preferences5. Others admit that AI-generated content might not effectively cater to individualized learning needs, limiting the adaptability of educational tools9. Meanwhile, some authors noted that, with appropriate teaching strategies and approaches, students’ learning outcomes could be improved without the use of generative AI10. Considering these limitations is central to realizing AI’s potential. Department of Information and Communication Technologies in Education, Eastern Mediterranean University, North Cyprus, Turkey. email: alsideeqighrarah@gmail.com; toochinwanna@gmail.com www.nature.com/scientificreports",97
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 2 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Research documents that teaching through telecollaborative learning offers many benefits and challenges. For instance, research indicates that while telecollaboration facilitates cultural exchange, cultural differences may lead to misunderstandings or conflicts if not appropriately addressed11. Some authors argue that although telecollaborative learning offers access to a wide range of digital resources, unequal access to technology and internet connectivity can impede participation for some students12. Besides, coordinating synchronous activities, such as telecollaboration, becomes challenging when participants are in different time zones13. In all, telecollaborative learning requires careful coordination to achieve the intended goals. While generative AI like ChatGPT may pose challenges, its use in a telecollaborative model may help buffer some of the challenges learners encounter in this instructional delivery model. Nonetheless, research is needed to guide practitioners with empirical evidence on the applicability and efficacy of AI across different learning models. Some recent studies’ authors caution that most conclusions drawn across scholarly studies cannot be generalized, given differences in research settings14. Similarly, others argue that longitudinal studies with robust mixed-method approaches are",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"applicability and efficacy of AI across different learning models. Some recent studies’ authors caution that most conclusions drawn across scholarly studies cannot be generalized, given differences in research settings14. Similarly, others argue that longitudinal studies with robust mixed-method approaches are required to thoroughly investigate the usefulness of this AI resource across different educational contexts14,15. Novel conclusions derived from such studies will provide empirical evidence- based insights into the potential of ChatGPT, especially when integrated with different instructional delivery models. This study aims to examine the efficacy of integrating generative AI into a telecollaborative learning environment. Aside from these, while independent research on ChatGPT and telecollaboration exists, a critical gap remains: no empirical studies have investigated their synergistic effects. For instance, while ChatGPT research has proliferated in recent years—generating an extensive literature on its applications to writing instruction16, and telecollaboration studies span multiple decades12,13 a critical empirical gap remains. Prior research has examined ChatGPT in isolation from collaborative contexts and has treated technology in telecollaboration as a communication medium rather than an active pedagogical agent. No published empirical research has simultaneously",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"span multiple decades12,13 a critical empirical gap remains. Prior research has examined ChatGPT in isolation from collaborative contexts and has treated technology in telecollaboration as a communication medium rather than an active pedagogical agent. No published empirical research has simultaneously tested both interventions using a controlled experimental design, and crucially, no study has investigated whether they interact synergistically. This gap exists despite several scholarly recommendations. Recent reviews emphasize that the interaction between AI tools and collaborative learning modalities remains understudied16. Most ChatGPT studies examine isolated use16,17, whereas telecollaboration research treats technology as a medium rather than an active pedagogical agent13. Existing work lacks rigorous factorial designs that test main and interaction effects. No study has measured whether ChatGPT’s benefits are. amplified in telecollaborative contexts. Evidence on ChatGPT’s impact on telecollaborative learning comes from case studies and qualitative anecdotes15, not controlled experimental designs with validated writing assessments. Researchers note that integrating ChatGPT into collaborative learning without examining AI dependency risks overlooks critical variables related to student engagement10. No study has balanced efficacy findings with qualitative exploration of potential downsides (laziness, reduced",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"experimental designs with validated writing assessments. Researchers note that integrating ChatGPT into collaborative learning without examining AI dependency risks overlooks critical variables related to student engagement10. No study has balanced efficacy findings with qualitative exploration of potential downsides (laziness, reduced peer interaction). These gaps are theoretically significant. Constructivist learning theory predicts that social interaction drives deeper learning than individual work18. Adaptive learning theory suggests that personalized scaffolding reduces cognitive load19. Human-AI collaboration frameworks propose that humans and AI form a cognitive unit, with each compensating for the other’s limitations20. These theories converge on a prediction: ChatGPT’s personalized scaffolding should amplify telecollaboration’s social benefits, producing synergistic effects exceeding either intervention alone. However, this prediction remains untested empirically. The gap is also practically significant. If the theories are correct—if ChatGPT and telecollaboration are mutually amplifying—then institutions worldwide implementing either intervention in isolation may be missing substantial benefits available through their combination. Conversely, if they are not synergistic, resources spent on both interventions may be inefficient. Only empirical evidence can answer these questions. This study makes three specific, evidence-based contributions that address the",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"may be missing substantial benefits available through their combination. Conversely, if they are not synergistic, resources spent on both interventions may be inefficient. Only empirical evidence can answer these questions. This study makes three specific, evidence-based contributions that address the identified gaps. First, this study addresses these gaps by employing a 2 × 2 factorial design to isolate and measure interaction effects. This is the first empirical study to employ a 2 × 2 factorial experimental design simultaneously testing ChatGPT and telecollaboration. While prior research has examined each intervention independently, no study has systematically isolated its individual effects and measured its interaction. The factorial design permits direct testing of whether ChatGPT’s effectiveness depends on whether students are collaborating—the key theoretical prediction. The interaction term in the 2 × 2 ANOVA directly answers: Do the interventions amplify each other (synergy), produce additive effects, or interfere with each other? Secondly, this study stress-tests the synergy prediction in a deliberately challenging context: international telecollaboration between Turkish and Chinese engineering students across time zones, requiring both synchronous and asynchronous coordination. This context includes genuine",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"additive effects, or interfere with each other? Secondly, this study stress-tests the synergy prediction in a deliberately challenging context: international telecollaboration between Turkish and Chinese engineering students across time zones, requiring both synchronous and asynchronous coordination. This context includes genuine obstacles: timezone differences (requiring asynchronous work), linguistic differences (non-native English writers), and cultural differences. Most prior ChatGPT research examines convenient university populations in a single country. By choosing a high-complexity, high-stakes context, this study determines whether the benefits generalize to realistic scenarios in which students face the cognitive demands that theory predicts ChatGPT scaffolding should address. Unlike many AI studies, this research systematically examined both positive findings and implementation risks. This balanced approach provides practitioners with realistic, evidence-based guidance about both the promise and pitfalls of integrating ChatGPT into telecollaborative learning. This study combines quantitative gain scores with thematic analysis of student experiences, including critical perspectives on AI dependency. The study provides empirical evidence for pedagogical decision-making in AI-enhanced collaborative learning. Literature review Generative artificial intelligence and education While generative AI holds significant potential in education, ongoing discussions across the",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,2,"of student experiences, including critical perspectives on AI dependency. The study provides empirical evidence for pedagogical decision-making in AI-enhanced collaborative learning. Literature review Generative artificial intelligence and education While generative AI holds significant potential in education, ongoing discussions across the scientific literature address this issue. For instance, given the issue of transparency, educators and students may need",57
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 3 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports help understanding the reasoning behind AI-generated recommendations or content, which can impact trust in the system21. Equally, there are debates that students relying solely on AI-generated content may miss out on developing research and analytical skills7. Given the emergence of technology-based learning, adaptability and customization are among the most challenging issues22. Similarly, some authors maintained that AI and generative AI models, like other digital resources, may face difficulties adapting to diverse learning styles and preferences23,24. These pose concerns for practitioners when considering using generative AI models. Furthermore, teachers and students may face other challenges related to the use of technology or gaming technology. The broader literature acknowledges that educators and students may need adequate training or preparation to interact with game resources9. Likewise, other scholars argue that a lack of training may hinder educators from effectively integrating AI into their teaching methods or from assisting students in navigating AI-driven learning environments25. Access to technology is challenging, especially for students, given the prices of technological devices26. Compared with access to generative AI,",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"may hinder educators from effectively integrating AI into their teaching methods or from assisting students in navigating AI-driven learning environments25. Access to technology is challenging, especially for students, given the prices of technological devices26. Compared with access to generative AI, access to cutting-edge AI technologies is more affordable, yet schools with limited budgets may need help adopting them27. Striking a balance between leveraging the benefits of generative AI and mitigating its potential risks is crucial for its successful integration into education. Nonetheless, while there may be challenges in using generative AI in teaching and learning, research affirms its usefulness in fostering personalized learning. For instance, recent studies maintain that ChatGPT helps develop students’ writing skills16,28. Others insist that using ChatGPT may help students develop ideas to execute projects29. Across broader literature, some authors noted that using ChatGPT may improve students’ learning outcomes and should be encouraged17. Some authors equally acknowledged that AI can be applied in education; nonetheless, theoretical gaps must be bridged30. Meanwhile, these notable studies affirm that more research is needed to provide robust insights into how generative",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"learning outcomes and should be encouraged17. Some authors equally acknowledged that AI can be applied in education; nonetheless, theoretical gaps must be bridged30. Meanwhile, these notable studies affirm that more research is needed to provide robust insights into how generative AI can be used to ensure optimal use that yields positive learning outcomes. Recent scholarship has shifted from conceptual discussions of artificial intelligence’s potential to empirical investigation of specific generative AI tools in educational contexts. This evolution reflects the urgent need for evidence-based guidance as institutions integrate AI technologies into teaching and learning environments. Emerging research identifies prompt engineering—the competency to formulate effective queries and instructions for AI systems—as an essential new literacy skill. Studies suggest that students who are explicitly taught prompt engineering strategies demonstrate significantly better learning outcomes than those given unstructured access to AI17. This finding underscores that generative AI is not a turnkey solution; rather, it requires pedagogical scaffolding to unlock its educational potential. Furthermore, research on language generation quality indicates that while AI-generated content is often accurate and well-structured, it may lack domain-specific nuance, cultural",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"that generative AI is not a turnkey solution; rather, it requires pedagogical scaffolding to unlock its educational potential. Furthermore, research on language generation quality indicates that while AI-generated content is often accurate and well-structured, it may lack domain-specific nuance, cultural sensitivity, or discipline-specific conventions that human instructors provide15. Thus, AI functions optimally as a complement to instruction, not a replacement for it. Technology acceptance models in AI contexts Research admits that the digitalization of educational systems and the impact on the learners’ improvement require modern pedagogical approaches to increase motivation among learners in distance learning31. Understanding why some students embrace technological and AI tools while others resist adoption remains an important area of inquiry. Besides, a systematic literature review of technology-enhanced language learning unveiled that there are scarcities of studies investigating the use of technologies to enhance specific language skills32. It is possible that studies with such a focus can assist in providing insights into students’ technology acceptance, especially in English language teaching. Extended Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) research demonstrates that user trust in AI systems strongly predicts adoption rates33.",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"It is possible that studies with such a focus can assist in providing insights into students’ technology acceptance, especially in English language teaching. Extended Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) research demonstrates that user trust in AI systems strongly predicts adoption rates33. Transparency in AI decision-making—understanding why an AI system generated a particular suggestion or recommendation—significantly enhances user trust34. Conversely, “black box” AI systems that provide outputs without explainable reasoning generate skepticism and lower adoption, particularly among learners with prior negative technology experiences5,35. Self-efficacy with AI tools, a domain-specific form of technology self-efficacy, varies considerably based on prior technology experience and individual differences. Research found that students with high general technology self-efficacy adopt AI tools more readily and use them more strategically than those with lower technology confidence23. However, paradoxically, students with very high confidence in AI capabilities sometimes demonstrate overconfidence, leading to reduced effort in learning and lower achievement gains—a finding consistent with goal-setting theory36. This suggests that confidence must be calibrated to actual competency; unchecked confidence can undermine learning. Risks of AI dependency in learning While the benefits of AI",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"to reduced effort in learning and lower achievement gains—a finding consistent with goal-setting theory36. This suggests that confidence must be calibrated to actual competency; unchecked confidence can undermine learning. Risks of AI dependency in learning While the benefits of AI in education are well-documented, critical scholars have identified concerning risks that warrant explicit attention in research and practice. Overreliance on AI correlates with reduced critical thinking, particularly when students work unsupervised without explicit instruction to evaluate AI outputs7,10. A study found that students who consistently used AI to generate initial content without first developing their own ideas showed declining independent writing quality over a semester, suggesting that cognitive dependency develops when scaffolding is not carefully managed37. Academic integrity violations increase substantially when institutional guardrails are absent. Policy review literature38,39 documents cases in which students submit AI-generated work as their own, ranging from subtle plagiarism (using AI-generated structure without attribution) to wholesale submission of unmodified AI output. The challenge is distinguishing legitimate AI-assisted learning from academic dishonesty. This distinction requires clear institutional policies, transparent student disclosure of AI use, and process-based",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,3,"ranging from subtle plagiarism (using AI-generated structure without attribution) to wholesale submission of unmodified AI output. The challenge is distinguishing legitimate AI-assisted learning from academic dishonesty. This distinction requires clear institutional policies, transparent student disclosure of AI use, and process-based assessment (evaluating drafts and revisions, not just final products).",49
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 4 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Cognitive offloading—the delegation of mental effort to external tools—may impair long-term retention and skill development when occurring without metacognitive monitoring. Drawing on cognitive load theory40, researchers argue that while AI can reduce extraneous cognitive load (freeing mental resources from routine idea generation), it only benefits learning if students reinvest those freed resources into higher-order thinking (evaluation, synthesis, critical analysis). Without explicit metacognitive strategies—such as reflection prompts asking “Where did I disagree with the AI output?” or “How did my thinking improve through comparison?“— students may disengage cognitively altogether, resulting in minimal learning despite maximum AI availability41. Recent research on AI dependency specifically identifies a “fear of missing out” (FoMO) phenomenon, in which students are anxious about missing out on AI’s capabilities, paradoxically reducing their own effort and leading to skill gaps36,42. This counterintuitive finding suggests that AI’s perceived ubiquity can undermine motivation in some learners, an important consideration for educators designing interventions. Implications for telecollaborative learning The intersection of generative AI and telecollaborative learning remains understudied despite the rapid expansion of",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"This counterintuitive finding suggests that AI’s perceived ubiquity can undermine motivation in some learners, an important consideration for educators designing interventions. Implications for telecollaborative learning The intersection of generative AI and telecollaborative learning remains understudied despite the rapid expansion of both fields. Research documents the challenges of synchronous telecollaboration—timezone coordination, cultural misunderstandings, unequal technology access—that AI might mitigate through asynchronous scaffolding and real-time translation13. However, empirical studies examining this synergy are limited. The current study addresses this gap by investigating whether ChatGPT’s personalized assistance amplifies the benefits of peer collaboration across geographical and cultural boundaries. Telecollaboration in education Regarding the need to maintain balance when using AI, telecollaboration also requires the use of digital resources for teaching. Research already documents the numerous benefits of telecollaborative learning models, enabling students to connect with peers, experts, and resources worldwide and fostering a global perspective12. Meanwhile, the cultural exchange benefit is essential for learning, as research affirms that this model allows students to understand different cultures and traditions11. For instance, language exchange programs through virtual interactions provide students with authentic cultural experiences. Equally,",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"global perspective12. Meanwhile, the cultural exchange benefit is essential for learning, as research affirms that this model allows students to understand different cultures and traditions11. For instance, language exchange programs through virtual interactions provide students with authentic cultural experiences. Equally, some authors noted that learners can engage in collaborative activities regardless of geographical location, promoting flexibility and convenience33. Altogether, telecollaborative learning models offer numerous benefits, from using diverse learning resources to enhancing communication and interdisciplinary collaboration. However, numerous challenges associated with telecollaborative learning must be considered before implementing this learning model. For instance, unequal access to technology and internet connectivity can impede some students’ participation13. Scholarly literature affirms that students in rural areas may face challenges accessing online platforms due to limited internet infrastructure43. Also, coordinating synchronous activities becomes challenging when participants are in different time zones44,45. Given differences in cultural backgrounds, miscommunication or misunderstandings may arise due to variations in communication styles, languages, or non-verbal cues46. Differences in interpreting written messages may lead to confusion among collaborating students. Moreover, effectively managing collaborative projects and ensuring equitable contributions from all",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"cultural backgrounds, miscommunication or misunderstandings may arise due to variations in communication styles, languages, or non-verbal cues46. Differences in interpreting written messages may lead to confusion among collaborating students. Moreover, effectively managing collaborative projects and ensuring equitable contributions from all participants requires careful coordination13. The unequal distribution of responsibilities in group projects may impact the overall quality of work47. Besides, cultural differences may lead to misunderstandings or conflicts if not addressed appropriately34. Students from different cultural backgrounds may interpret feedback or communication styles differently, leading to friction. Of course, telecollaborative learning may lack the personal touch of face-to-face interaction, thereby affecting the development of interpersonal skills12. Virtual collaborations may not provide the same level of bonding or rapport as in-person interactions12,48. Navigating these challenges requires careful planning, clear communication strategies, and the integration of supportive technologies, such as AI, to enhance the telecollaborative learning experience. Theoretical frameworks Three complementary theoretical frameworks informed the study design. Rather than treating theory as context or justification added after the fact, these frameworks actively shaped which conditions were created and what we predicted they",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"enhance the telecollaborative learning experience. Theoretical frameworks Three complementary theoretical frameworks informed the study design. Rather than treating theory as context or justification added after the fact, these frameworks actively shaped which conditions were created and what we predicted they would produce. Constructivist theory posits that learning occurs through active knowledge construction via social interaction18. Based on this theory, we designed Conditions 3 and 4 to include international telecollaboration. The theory predicts that authentic collaboration drives deeper learning than individual work through two mechanisms: social accountability (students must justify their ideas to peers) and perspective diversity (cross-cultural collaboration challenges students to explain their thinking clearly). We operationalized this by requiring genuine collaborative decision-making—students negotiated which ideas to develop, jointly revised writing, and collectively planned website designs. Theoretical prediction: Students in telecollaborative conditions (3–4) will show larger learning gains than non-telecollaborative conditions (1–2) due to cross-cultural collaboration’s social-cognitive demands. Adaptive learning theory posits that instruction is most effective when tailored to individual learner needs, adjusting level, pacing, and content based on performance19. ChatGPT exemplifies this through personalized, responsive feedback. However, passive",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,4,"conditions (1–2) due to cross-cultural collaboration’s social-cognitive demands. Adaptive learning theory posits that instruction is most effective when tailored to individual learner needs, adjusting level, pacing, and content based on performance19. ChatGPT exemplifies this through personalized, responsive feedback. However, passive AI use will not produce learning benefits. Therefore, we provided explicit training in effective prompting—teaching students to ask targeted questions, evaluate outputs critically, and iterate—operationalizing the theory’s requirement for active engagement. In telecollaborative contexts with high cognitive demands (asynchronous communication, time zones, second-language negotiation), adaptive scaffolding through ChatGPT reduces cognitive overload, freeing capacity for social-collaborative work.",96
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,5,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 5 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Prediction: Students with ChatGPT access and training (Conditions 2, 4) show larger gains than those without it (Conditions 1, 3). The human-AI collaboration framework proposes that humans and AI form cognitive units, each contributing complementary strengths20. Humans excel at understanding context and values; AI excels at pattern recognition and tireless availability. Their combination produces synergistic effects that exceed the sum of their independent contributions. We predicted Condition 4 would demonstrate synergy because each addresses the other’s limitation. Their synergy includes that ChatGPT scaffolds collaboration’s cognitive demands (ideation, translation, coordination), freeing resources for social meaning-making. Simultaneously, telecollaboration provides authentic purpose and peer accountability, preventing passive AI use. Students engage in “distributed cognition”—cognitive processing occurs across human-AI-human interaction, not isolated individual minds. We predicted that Condition 4 students would generate more ideas (ChatGPT), develop them more deeply (peer justification), invest more in learning (accountability), persist through challenges, and achieve gains exceeding additive combinations. Three frameworks converge: optimal learning occurs when personalized cognitive scaffolding (ChatGPT) is embedded within authentic collaborative contexts (telecollaboration), leveraging complementary",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,5,"develop them more deeply (peer justification), invest more in learning (accountability), persist through challenges, and achieve gains exceeding additive combinations. Three frameworks converge: optimal learning occurs when personalized cognitive scaffolding (ChatGPT) is embedded within authentic collaborative contexts (telecollaboration), leveraging complementary human-AI strengths. This integration is synergistic—each element amplifies the other. ChatGPT reduces cognitive overload, enabling collaborative meaning-making. Collaboration prevents passive AI use, requiring critical evaluation and peer justification. Conceptually, this study anticipates that implementing generative AI within a telecollaborative learning model may help improve learning outcomes. This conceptual framework is presented in Fig. 1. This study proposes an integrated theoretical framework that unifies three complementary theoretical perspectives rather than discussing them independently. The foundational layer, grounded in constructivist learning theory, rests on the core principle that knowledge is actively constructed through social interaction. In this study, telecollaboration provides the necessary social interaction, while ChatGPT facilitates the co-construction of knowledge through collaborative idea generation. The cognitive layer incorporates adaptive learning theory, which holds that instruction should be tailored to individual learners’ needs and provide responsive feedback. In this study, ChatGPT personalizes",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,5,"social interaction, while ChatGPT facilitates the co-construction of knowledge through collaborative idea generation. The cognitive layer incorporates adaptive learning theory, which holds that instruction should be tailored to individual learners’ needs and provide responsive feedback. In this study, ChatGPT personalizes idea generation for each student and provides immediate feedback on draft essays, a significant advantage over the typical lag in teacher feedback. Operationally, students used ChatGPT to generate multiple essay versions and selected the adaptation that best suited their needs and preferences. The interaction layer incorporates human-AI collaboration frameworks, which operate on the principle that artificial intelligence amplifies human capability while humans provide essential judgment and ethical oversight. In the study’s application, ChatGPT generates ideas—leveraging its strength in rapid content generation—while students synthesize, evaluate, and improve those ideas, leveraging their human strengths in critical judgment and contextual understanding. These three theoretical layers integrate into a unified model of AI-enhanced collaborative learning. The foundational constructivist layer and the cognitive adaptive learning layer both mediate through distinct mechanisms to produce the ultimate learning outcome. The present study Research on ChatGPT is exhaustive",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,5,"theoretical layers integrate into a unified model of AI-enhanced collaborative learning. The foundational constructivist layer and the cognitive adaptive learning layer both mediate through distinct mechanisms to produce the ultimate learning outcome. The present study Research on ChatGPT is exhaustive yet inconclusive, as these studies recommend further research to validate their findings14. Additionally, some authors recommend that longitudinal studies are needed to probe recent findings across different contexts14,15. Notably, while it is laudable that there are studies on ChatGPT and telecollaborative learning9,11,12,22,25, there are fewer on integrating the two. Investigating this understudied aspect of generative AI and telecollaboration provides essential knowledge that is lacking in the scholarly literature, unveiling the potential of ChatGPT in telecollaborative learning environments. Equally, given the challenges of implementing digital resources in the classroom, like cost, equitable access, ease of use, tech proficiency, and so on, integrating ChatGPT, affordable, accessible digital resources, in a telecollaborative learning environment, offers an opportunity to explore and gain more insights into the potential of ChatGPT and ChatGPT-assisted telecollaborative learning. To achieve these, the following research questions were investigated; Fig. 1.",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,5,"integrating ChatGPT, affordable, accessible digital resources, in a telecollaborative learning environment, offers an opportunity to explore and gain more insights into the potential of ChatGPT and ChatGPT-assisted telecollaborative learning. To achieve these, the following research questions were investigated; Fig. 1. Conceptual framework of the study.",45
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 6 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports • Does using ChatGPT in a telecollaborative learning environment improve students’ learning outcomes? • What are your perceptions of the role of ChatGPT in improving learning outcomes in a telecollaborative learning environment? Methodology Research design The research design used a mixed-methods, experimental approach. This research design uses experiments and interviews to collect qualitative and quantitative data on an investigated phenomenon49. This study deems this research design suitable, given that experiments can be used to determine whether integrating ChatGPT into a telecollaborative learning environment improves students’ learning outcomes. On the other hand, interview data reveal students’ perceptions of ChatGPT’s role in improving learning outcomes in a telecollaborative learning environment. Given the suitability of this research design, it was adopted and implemented in this study. Research design overview This study employed a 2 × 2 between-subjects factorial experimental design—a research design specifically suited for simultaneously testing two independent variables and their interaction. The study’s two independent variables are: 1. Telecollaboration (present vs. absent): Whether students engaged in synchronous and asynchronous collab­ oration with",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"× 2 between-subjects factorial experimental design—a research design specifically suited for simultaneously testing two independent variables and their interaction. The study’s two independent variables are: 1. Telecollaboration (present vs. absent): Whether students engaged in synchronous and asynchronous collab­ oration with peers in a different country. 2. ChatGPT Integration (present vs. absent): Whether students had access to ChatGPT with explicit training in using it effectively. These two variables, each with two levels, create four distinct conditions. Telecollaboration (present/absent) and ChatGPT integration (present/absent), creating four treatment conditions. Condition 1 consisted of 20 students engaged in flipped learning without AI. Condition 2 included 20 students using flipped learning with ChatGPT. Condition 3 comprised 20 students participating in synchronous collaboration without AI. Condition 4 included 20 students engaged in telecollaboration with ChatGPT. The total number of participants was N = 80, with 40 assigned to Condition 1 (control group) and 40 to the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4). This design allows the examination of main and interaction effects, revealing whether ChatGPT’s benefits are enhanced in telecollaborative contexts. Prior research has examined ChatGPT",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"to Condition 1 (control group) and 40 to the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4). This design allows the examination of main and interaction effects, revealing whether ChatGPT’s benefits are enhanced in telecollaborative contexts. Prior research has examined ChatGPT in isolation and telecollaboration in isolation, but no study has tested whether they amplify each other’s benefits. This design allows direct testing of that question. Procedures To implement the study, an instructional technology course (ELTV 203) was designed and delivered over 14 weeks to the participants. Given the emergence of new technologies, professionals will need digital and technological literacy to use them in classroom teaching. Since ChatGPT has recently sparked debates as a new digital resource, it was deemed appropriate to integrate it into this course through a telecollaborative approach. Before commencing the study, a pretest was administered to ensure that the participants were at a baseline level. Based on the test results, the treatments and Condition 1 (control group) were recruited. The Condition 1 (control group) was taught the educational technology course using flipped learning. All tasks and activities",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"that the participants were at a baseline level. Based on the test results, the treatments and Condition 1 (control group) were recruited. The Condition 1 (control group) was taught the educational technology course using flipped learning. All tasks and activities were conducted in class, using Moodle for flipped learning. This model is perceived as effective for some students in carrying out classroom tasks50. The course materials were uploaded to Moodle; students were encouraged to study them, and during face-to-face lessons, they implemented some aspects of the task. This was done until the task was completed. The task was a writing assignment on a simple topic; afterward, the students were required to use digital resources to develop a website where they uploaded their writing projects. The teacher guided them throughout the study. The experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) consist of participants from two different private universities: one in Cyprus and another in China. They were paired to create a telecollaborative situation where they had to work together to complete a task. Given their different geographic areas, these students accessed",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"of participants from two different private universities: one in Cyprus and another in China. They were paired to create a telecollaborative situation where they had to work together to complete a task. Given their different geographic areas, these students accessed education through online instruction. The same task was assigned to the Condition 1 (control group) as was assigned to the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4). A Zoom and WhatsApp platform was created for effective collaboration and participation in the research. During this period, the students worked together to complete the task, sharing knowledge and helping each other. They were encouraged to use ChatGPT to develop their ideas about the writing task without details. The teacher showed the students how to use appropriate prompts to achieve this. After generating ideas, the students were encouraged to develop them without using ChatGPT. After developing their ideas, they were asked to use ChatGPT to generate essays on their ideas and to compare their writing samples with ChatGPT’s output. Below are some of the writing samples (Fig. 2). After that, the students were",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,6,"ChatGPT. After developing their ideas, they were asked to use ChatGPT to generate essays on their ideas and to compare their writing samples with ChatGPT’s output. Below are some of the writing samples (Fig. 2). After that, the students were encouraged to use ChatGPT to generate ideas on designing websites to upload their completed written tasks. They were encouraged to use ChatGPT to interact, given that they come from different countries. Afterward, the links to their websites (see Appendix 1) and their writing task were analyzed. Below are some sections of the outcome of the telecollaborative project produced by the students in Fig. 3. Participants Given the central aim of this research to explore the efficacy of integrating generative artificial intelligence into a telecollaborative learning model, sophomore Engineering students from two different private universities were recruited for this study. Eighty participants were randomly recruited for this study. They were predominantly students from different countries within the age group of 19 to 21 years. These participants are male and female.",169
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,7,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 7 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Eighty participants were randomly assigned using stratified randomization, controlling for pretest scores to ensure comparable baseline proficiency. All 80 students completed a baseline writing assessment, with a mean score of 64.68 (SD = 5.34), as shown in Table 1. Students were ranked by their pretest scores and sequentially assigned to conditions in a rotating pattern to equalize ability across conditions. The group composition was as follows: Condition 1 (No Tele/No GPT) consisted of 20 students engaged in flipped learning via Moodle. Condition 2 (No Tele/GPT) included 20 students using flipped learning with ChatGPT instruction. Condition 3 (Tele/No GPT) comprised 20 students (10 Turkish and 10 Chinese) participating in pair-based synchronous collaboration. Condition 4 (Tele/GPT) included 20 students (10 Turkish and 10 Chinese) engaged in telecollaboration with ChatGPT integration. Participants in the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) were paired to carry out the task via a telecollaborative approach, and the tasks were assessed afterward. Before commencing the study, informed oral and written consent was obtained from the participants, and they",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,7,"experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) were paired to carry out the task via a telecollaborative approach, and the tasks were assessed afterward. Before commencing the study, informed oral and written consent was obtained from the participants, and they were informed of their right to withdraw at any time. For the interviews, 20 students were randomly selected from the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) to participate. They were coded D1-D20 for anonymity purposes, in line with ethical considerations. Table 1 presents an overview of the experimental design and the condition specifications. The table shows that all participants were administered the same writing and website design tasks. Table 1 displays the four Fig. 3. Telecollaborative project produced by the students. Fig. 2. Comparing students’ writing samples and ChatGPT writing samples.",132
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,8,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 8 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports distinct conditions created by the 2 × 2 factorial design. Conditions are balanced on sample size (n = 20 each) and task content (all complete identical writing and website projects), but systematically vary in the presence or absence of each independent variable. This design permits examination of whether each variable independently improves learning and whether their combination produces synergistic benefits exceeding additive expectations. Data collection The research instruments used in this study were posttests and interviews. The posttest was given after the treatment to assess the development of their writing skills. Secondly, their completed task (website for uploading their article) was assessed. For the writing assignment, students were required to write a simple article on a given topic. The expert raters developed a writing evaluation rubric. The students’ writing tasks were evaluated based on organization, content, mechanical accuracy, grammar, and paragraph coherence. For the task, the expert raters considered the following: appropriateness of the websites, design, color, navigation, and webpage design. All the expert examiners agreed and adopted the assessment rubric",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,8,"evaluated based on organization, content, mechanical accuracy, grammar, and paragraph coherence. For the task, the expert raters considered the following: appropriateness of the websites, design, color, navigation, and webpage design. All the expert examiners agreed and adopted the assessment rubric for this study. The test was face- and construct-validated by all the experts to ensure that the test items were appropriately worded and included only relevant questions. Semi-structured interview guides were used to conduct the interviews. The interview guides consist of question items structured to capture the interview’s objective. A recorder was used during the session to record and transcribe the proceedings afterward. The interviews lasted for 32 h, 50 min, 42 s. Research trustworthiness This study employed Lincoln and Guba’s trustworthiness framework to establish credibility, dependability, and confirmability of interview findings. To ensure credibility (internal validity), the study utilized prolonged engagement, with interviews lasting 32 h, 50 min, and 42 s across weeks. Follow-up interviews were conducted with 8 participants (10%) to verify initial interpretations, leading to iterative refinement of themes based on their feedback. Triangulation was employed across",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,8,"prolonged engagement, with interviews lasting 32 h, 50 min, and 42 s across weeks. Follow-up interviews were conducted with 8 participants (10%) to verify initial interpretations, leading to iterative refinement of themes based on their feedback. Triangulation was employed across multiple dimensions: data triangulation combined interview data with observed writing artifacts and website projects (n = 20); investigator triangulation involved two independent coders analyzing transcripts with discrepancies resolved through discussion to achieve greater than 90% consensus; and method triangulation cross-validated quantitative gains with qualitative themes. Member checking was conducted with all 20 interview participants (100%), who reviewed the final themes. Eighteen of the 20 participants (90%) confirmed that the themes reflected their experiences, while 2 participants (10%) suggested refinements. Dependability, equivalent to reliability and audit trail documentation, was ensured through detailed methodology documentation. The interview guide was provided in the appendices, and the transcription protocol was thoroughly documented. All transcription was conducted verbatim by a trained transcriber, with time-stamped speaker labels and paralinguistic features noted to capture pauses and vocal emphasis. Transcription accuracy was verified by randomly selecting and re-transcribing",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,8,"appendices, and the transcription protocol was thoroughly documented. All transcription was conducted verbatim by a trained transcriber, with time-stamped speaker labels and paralinguistic features noted to capture pauses and vocal emphasis. Transcription accuracy was verified by randomly selecting and re-transcribing 10% of the transcript; word- for-word agreement reached 98%. A comprehensive codebook with operational definitions was developed for each theme. Analyst triangulation involved three independent coders analyzing the transcripts, with inter-coder reliability measured using Krippendorff’s alpha at 0.82, exceeding the 0.80 threshold for acceptable agreement. Disagreements, measured by Cohen’s kappa at 0.82, were resolved through discussion. Confirmability, or objectivity, was addressed through multiple strategies. The research team explicitly acknowledged potential biases in a reflexivity statement: the authors are strong proponents of AI integration Condition Design label Telecollaboration ChatGPT integration Sample size Instructional format Primary activities 1 Control No No n = 20 Flipped learning via Moodle (asynchronous online materials, synchronous in-class application) Individual writing task on assigned topic; individual website design project 2 AI-only No Yes n = 20 Flipped learning via Moodle with ChatGPT training (asynchronous online materials +",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,8,"= 20 Flipped learning via Moodle (asynchronous online materials, synchronous in-class application) Individual writing task on assigned topic; individual website design project 2 AI-only No Yes n = 20 Flipped learning via Moodle with ChatGPT training (asynchronous online materials + ChatGPT access, synchronous in-class application) Individual writing using ChatGPT for idea generation; individual website using ChatGPT for design consultation 3 Collaboration-only Yes No n = 20 Telecollaborative learning via Zoom & WhatsApp (international dyads: Turkish + Chinese students, synchronous & asynchronous collaboration) Paired writing task requiring joint decision- making; paired website design requiring collaborative planning 4 Full Treatment Yes Yes n = 20 Telecollaborative learning with ChatGPT via Zoom & WhatsApp (international dyads with AI support, synchronous & asynchronous collaboration) Paired writing using ChatGPT for idea generation; paired website using ChatGPT for design suggestions; collaborative problem-solving across time zones Table 1. Experimental design overview and condition specifications.",147
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 9 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports in education, which may unconsciously lead them to emphasize positive findings. To mitigate this bias, coders were kept blind to the research hypothesis, negative case analysis was conducted, and critical voices were highlighted in the results section. Negative-case analysis identified three students who reported no benefit from ChatGPT in their interviews. These cases were incorporated into theme refinement, and representative quotes such as “ChatGPT did not help me; I prefer my own thinking” were retained to provide a balanced perspective. A comprehensive data audit trail was documented in the appendices, including raw interview transcripts (anonymized), code definitions presented in the codebook, an intercoder agreement spreadsheet, member-checking documentation showing 18 participant confirmations and 2 refinements, and memo samples illustrating the analytical process. Transferability, or external validity, was supported through thick description. The study provided detailed information on student demographics (80 engineering students, aged 19–21 years, 50/50 male/female ratio, Turkish/Chinese representation), setting details (14-week course duration, Zoom meetings, WhatsApp collaboration), intervention specifics (ChatGPT-4 with specific prompts taught to students), and interview context (semi-structured",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"study provided detailed information on student demographics (80 engineering students, aged 19–21 years, 50/50 male/female ratio, Turkish/Chinese representation), setting details (14-week course duration, Zoom meetings, WhatsApp collaboration), intervention specifics (ChatGPT-4 with specific prompts taught to students), and interview context (semi-structured format, 32 + total hours, 20 participants). Based on these characteristics, the findings are transferable to similar engineering populations in international distance-learning contexts. Reliability and validity Table 2 presents the inter-reliability statistics. Intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) using Model 3(k), which accounts for absolute agreement among fixed raters, was ICC(3,3)=.89 (95% CI [.84, .93]), indicating excellent reliability. This value substantially exceeds the .75 threshold for excellent reliability and the .80 threshold required for making high-stakes educational decisions. Krippendorff’s alpha, a distribution-free reliability coefficient applicable to interval-level data, was α = .87, exceeding the .80 criterion for acceptable agreement in high- stakes contexts. Pairwise Cohen’s kappa coefficients between raters were: Raters 1–2: κ = .84; Raters 1–3 κ = .86; Raters 2–3 κ = .85. All three pairwise agreements reach the ‘almost perfect’ category (κ > 0.81). To verify that rater consistency",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"contexts. Pairwise Cohen’s kappa coefficients between raters were: Raters 1–2: κ = .84; Raters 1–3 κ = .86; Raters 2–3 κ = .85. All three pairwise agreements reach the ‘almost perfect’ category (κ > 0.81). To verify that rater consistency did not degrade over time (threat to dependability), ICC was computed separately for Time 1 (pretest) and Time 2 (posttest) rating batches. Comparison showed no significant decline: T1 ICC(3,3)=0.88 vs. T2 ICC(3,3)=0.90, confirming stable rater performance throughout the study period. Data analysis Before conducting inferential statistical tests on posttest and gain scores, the study verified key ANOVA assumptions. A Shapiro-Wilk normality test on gain scores (collapsed across all conditions) confirmed normal distribution: W = 0.97, p = .43, indicating that the data did not significantly deviate from normality (p>.05). Examination of Q-Q plots (provided in supplementary materials) further supported visual inspection of normally distributed data with points aligning with the reference diagonal line. Homogeneity of variance was tested using Levene’s test comparing variance across the four conditions. Results showed equal variances, F(3,76) = 1.84, p = .15, with variance by",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"inspection of normally distributed data with points aligning with the reference diagonal line. Homogeneity of variance was tested using Levene’s test comparing variance across the four conditions. Results showed equal variances, F(3,76) = 1.84, p = .15, with variance by condition ranging from 6.55 to 11.56—a ratio of 1.76:1, well within the acceptable 2:1 threshold. This indicates that ANOVA’s assumption of equal group variances is satisfied. Independence of observations was ensured by design: each participant was assigned to a single condition and received a single treatment, with no repeated measures or within-subject dependencies. Raters scoring writing samples were kept blind to participant condition assignment, preventing rater bias from violating independence assumptions. The posttest was analyzed using descriptive percentile statistics. The students’ mean scores were calculated and analyzed to determine their scores for the assigned task. Scores below 50 were ascribed as fail grades, while scores above 50 were ascribed as pass grades. The mean scores of the students across the two treatment groups were calculated, and a normality test was performed to assess whether the scores were normally distributed. Afterward,",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"as fail grades, while scores above 50 were ascribed as pass grades. The mean scores of the students across the two treatment groups were calculated, and a normality test was performed to assess whether the scores were normally distributed. Afterward, an appropriate nonparametric test was used to determine whether significant improvements occurred in students’ scores across the different groups. The interviews were analyzed using thematic analysis. The Clarke and Braun thematic analysis framework was used to analyze the interviews. This involves three expert coders. First, after transcribing the data, the coder studied the transcripts to familiarize themselves with their details. After that, the coders examined and reviewed the transcripts to identify patterns. This requires that coders use a codebook to document and record codes as they emerge. After this process, the three coders reviewed their codes to identify emerging themes. Measure Value 95% CI Interpretation Writing Assessment (ICC 3,3) 0.89 [0.84, 0.93] Excellent Writing Assessment (Krippendorff) 0.87 N/A Excellent (> 0.80) Website Design (ICC 3,3) 0.85 [0.74, 0.92] Excellent Rater 1–2 Agreement (Cohen κ) 0.84 N/A Almost Perfect Rater 1–3",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,9,"Measure Value 95% CI Interpretation Writing Assessment (ICC 3,3) 0.89 [0.84, 0.93] Excellent Writing Assessment (Krippendorff) 0.87 N/A Excellent (> 0.80) Website Design (ICC 3,3) 0.85 [0.74, 0.92] Excellent Rater 1–2 Agreement (Cohen κ) 0.84 N/A Almost Perfect Rater 1–3 Agreement (Cohen κ) 0.86 N/A Almost Perfect Rater 2–3 Agreement (Cohen κ) 0.85 N/A Almost Perfect Table 2. Inter-reliability statistics.",60
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 10 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Furthermore, the coders review the theme thoroughly until a consensus is reached. The themes were reviewed to ensure they aligned with the study’s objective and the research questions investigated. Afterward, the emerging themes were shown to the interviewees to indicate that the outcomes represent their opinions. After that, the themes were written and named. The outcome is presented in the table in the findings section. Validity threats and mitigation To strengthen the internal validity of this study, several threats were identified and mitigated. To minimize selection bias, stratified randomization based on pretest scores was employed to ensure that unequal characteristics across groups were minimized. Statistical verification through ANOVA of pretest scores confirmed this approach was effective, F(3,76) = 0.21, p = .89, with no significant differences and a mean pretest range of 64.30-65.08. Maturation effects were controlled by implementing a 14-week intervention, which represents a typical semester length and is not excessive. Condition 1 (control group) also received 14 weeks of instruction through flipped learning, making minimal maturation expected over a",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"of 64.30-65.08. Maturation effects were controlled by implementing a 14-week intervention, which represents a typical semester length and is not excessive. Condition 1 (control group) also received 14 weeks of instruction through flipped learning, making minimal maturation expected over a single semester. History effects were minimized because all groups were taught during the same 14-week course period in the 2025 Spring semester, with no major institutional changes or national crises reported during the study that would have affected student writing instruction. Both the control (Condition 1) and experimental groups (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) experienced identical contexts except for the treatment itself. To address instrumentation threats, raters were kept blind to student condition and were unaware of treatment assignments. A standardized rubric was used with no mid-study changes, and intraclass correlation coefficients were measured throughout the study to ensure no decline in agreement between Time 1 and Time 2. Additionally, a 2-hour calibration session was conducted with raters before scoring began. Testing effects were mitigated by maintaining a 14-week gap between pretest and posttest administration, which was sufficient to minimize",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"decline in agreement between Time 1 and Time 2. Additionally, a 2-hour calibration session was conducted with raters before scoring began. Testing effects were mitigated by maintaining a 14-week gap between pretest and posttest administration, which was sufficient to minimize recall effects. Furthermore, posttest prompts differed from pretest prompts, requiring students to address new topics. Condition 1 (control group) also completed the pretest, allowing for the observation of any testing effects across all conditions. Statistical regression was addressed through stratified randomization, which reduced the likelihood of extreme groups. Results remained similar when analyzed with and without outliers, using ANCOVA to verify robustness. Gain score analysis demonstrated meaningful differences that extended beyond simple regression to the mean. Regarding external validity, several limitations and corresponding mitigation strategies were considered. Population validity was limited to engineering sophomores aged 19–21 years from two private universities. Results are generalizable to engineering students in private universities, EFL learning contexts, and undergraduate populations; however, they are not generalizable to younger students, K-12 contexts, pure ESL environments, or public universities. Future research across diverse disciplines, age groups, and",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"universities. Results are generalizable to engineering students in private universities, EFL learning contexts, and undergraduate populations; however, they are not generalizable to younger students, K-12 contexts, pure ESL environments, or public universities. Future research across diverse disciplines, age groups, and institutional types is recommended to enhance generalizability. Ecological validity considerations acknowledge that the study employed controlled conditions for flipped learning and Zoom pairing; however, telecollaboration groups used authentic platforms such as Zoom and WhatsApp, and tasks were aligned with real course requirements, including website design. The study was classroom-embedded rather than conducted in an artificial laboratory setting. Nevertheless, results may not transfer to different learning management systems or less-structured online collaboration environments. Temporal validity limitations stem from the study being conducted in 2025, when ChatGPT’s versions and capabilities were still evolving. The study used ChatGPT-4, the current version at the time of the study. Procedural details regarding prompt engineering approaches were carefully documented to enable replication. While findings are likely robust to minor model updates, results may differ with future versions such as GPT-5 or alternative AI models. Ethical considerations",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"time of the study. Procedural details regarding prompt engineering approaches were carefully documented to enable replication. While findings are likely robust to minor model updates, results may differ with future versions such as GPT-5 or alternative AI models. Ethical considerations Ethical approval was obtained from the research ethics committee at Eastern Mediterranean University. It was confirmed and approved that the research adheres to and will be conducted in accordance with the ethical standards of the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki and its later amendments, or comparable ethical standards. Participants provided informed consent and were assured of confidentiality and anonymity. Participation was voluntary, and students were told they could withdraw at any time without academic penalty. Data was securely stored and used solely for research purposes in compliance with institutional and international research ethics guidelines. Result Before presenting findings, we verify that the 2 × 2 factorial design was implemented as intended. Stratified randomization successfully allocated 80 participants across four conditions (n = 20 per condition), balanced on baseline writing proficiency. One-way ANOVA comparing pretest scores across conditions confirmed baseline equivalence: F(3,76)",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"the 2 × 2 factorial design was implemented as intended. Stratified randomization successfully allocated 80 participants across four conditions (n = 20 per condition), balanced on baseline writing proficiency. One-way ANOVA comparing pretest scores across conditions confirmed baseline equivalence: F(3,76) = 0.21, p = .89, with pretest means ranging from M = 64.30 to M = 65.08 (overall M = 64.68, SD = 5.34). No significant differences were observed at baseline, supporting the assumption that the observed posttest differences reflect treatment effects rather than initial ability differences. Demographic verification confirmed a balanced gender distribution (50% male/% female in each condition) and the representation of Turkish and Chinese students in the telecollaborative conditions. This verification establishes that random assignment and stratification successfully created equivalent baseline groups. RQ1: Does using ChatGPT in a telecollaborative learning environment improve students’ learning outcomes? Table 3 presents descriptive statistics for pretest, posttest, and calculated gain scores across the four experimental conditions.These statistics are the foundation for understanding treatment effects on writing performance. Inspection of Table 3 reveals a striking pattern across conditions. Condition 1 (control group),",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,10,"presents descriptive statistics for pretest, posttest, and calculated gain scores across the four experimental conditions.These statistics are the foundation for understanding treatment effects on writing performance. Inspection of Table 3 reveals a striking pattern across conditions. Condition 1 (control group), which received flipped learning instruction without AI, showed negligible improvement from pretest (M = 64.58) to posttest (M = 64.93), a gain of only 0.35 points (SD = 3.40). This minimal gain suggests that flipped learning",76
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,11,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 11 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports alone, despite its documented benefits in some contexts, produced negligible learning gains in writing skills for this population. In contrast, Condition 2 (flipped learning + ChatGPT, without telecollaboration) produced a substantial improvement of 5.33 points (SD = 2.58), with posttest scores increasing from M = 65.08 to M = 70.41. This 5.33-point gain indicates that ChatGPT integration, even without peer collaboration, significantly improved writing outcomes. Condition 3 (telecollaboration without ChatGPT) produced a modest improvement of 2.76 points (SD = 2.56), with posttest scores increasing from M = 64.30 to M = 67.06. While this gain exceeds the control group’s minimal improvement, it is substantially smaller than ChatGPT’s effect when implemented without telecollaboration (Condition 2). Most dramatically, Condition 4 (full treatment: telecollaboration + ChatGPT) produced the largest gain of 11.90 points (SD = 2.85), with posttest scores surging from M = 64.87 to M = 76.76. This represents an 18% improvement over baseline, substantially exceeding any other condition. Notably, the standard deviation of gain scores in Condition 4 (SD = 2.85) was",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,11,"= 2.85), with posttest scores surging from M = 64.87 to M = 76.76. This represents an 18% improvement over baseline, substantially exceeding any other condition. Notably, the standard deviation of gain scores in Condition 4 (SD = 2.85) was the smallest of all conditions, indicating that ChatGPT-assisted telecollaboration produced consistent, uniform gains across participants—suggesting robust treatment effects. To formally test hypotheses about main effects and interaction, a 2 × 2 factorial ANOVA was conducted with gain scores as the dependent variable and Telecollaboration and ChatGPT as independent factors. This analysis is critical because it isolates the effects of each factor while accounting for their interaction—precisely what the factorial design permits. Table 4 presents the two-way ANOVA. The main effect of Telecollaboration was highly significant, F(1,76) = 97.98, p < .001, η2p = .386. Partial eta-squared of 0.386 indicates that telecollaboration accounts for 38.6% of variance in gain scores—a large effect by conventional standards (η2 > 0.14). Students engaged in telecollaboration (collapsing Conditions 3–4, combined M = 7.33) demonstrated significantly greater gains than non-telecollaborative students (collapsing Conditions 1–2, combined M",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,11,"telecollaboration accounts for 38.6% of variance in gain scores—a large effect by conventional standards (η2 > 0.14). Students engaged in telecollaboration (collapsing Conditions 3–4, combined M = 7.33) demonstrated significantly greater gains than non-telecollaborative students (collapsing Conditions 1–2, combined M = 2.84). The main effect of ChatGPT was even more pronounced, F(1,76) = 242.48, p < .001, η2p = .609. Partial eta- squared of .609 indicates that ChatGPT integration accounts for 60.9% of variance in writing gains—a very large effect, more than 4 times the threshold for ‘large’ effects. Students with ChatGPT access (collapsing Conditions 2–4, combined M = 9.40) demonstrated substantially greater gains than those without ChatGPT (collapsing Conditions 1–3, combined M = 1.56). The Telecollaboration × ChatGPT interaction was statistically significant, F(1, 76) = 21.02, p < .001, η2p = .119. This interaction indicates ChatGPT’s eftGPT is NOT constant across telecollaboration levels; ChatGPT’s benefit is amplified when combined with telecollaboration. Among non-telecollaborative students, ChatGPT’s effect is 5.33–0.35 = 4.98 points. Among telecollaborative students, ChatGPT’s effect is 11.90–2.76 = 9.14 points. Thus, ChatGPT’s benefit is nearly twice as large",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,11,"constant across telecollaboration levels; ChatGPT’s benefit is amplified when combined with telecollaboration. Among non-telecollaborative students, ChatGPT’s effect is 5.33–0.35 = 4.98 points. Among telecollaborative students, ChatGPT’s effect is 11.90–2.76 = 9.14 points. Thus, ChatGPT’s benefit is nearly twice as large in the telecollaborative context, revealing a synergistic interaction. Interviews RQ2: What are your perceptions of the role of ChatGPT in improving learning outcomes in a telecollaborative learning environment? Table 5 presents a thematic analysis of students’ opinions of the role of ChatGPT in improving learning outcomes in a telecollaborative learning environment. After analyzing the interview session, three main themes emerged from students’ opinions on using generative AI to execute telecollaborative tasks. They include an intelligent personal assistant, Self- confidence, and a laissez-faire attitude. These themes are discussed below; Theme 1: Intelligent personal assistant This theme suggests that students perceived generative AI as an intelligent personal assistant during lessons. Analysis of interview transcripts revealed that 16 of 20 students (80%) endorsed a conception of ChatGPT as an intelligent personal assistant—a helpful tool that scaffolds learning without replacing student effort. This shows",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,11,"AI as an intelligent personal assistant during lessons. Analysis of interview transcripts revealed that 16 of 20 students (80%) endorsed a conception of ChatGPT as an intelligent personal assistant—a helpful tool that scaffolds learning without replacing student effort. This shows that the AI helped students execute some tasks by generating ideas. Below are some of their comments that reflect this theme; Source df F p Partial η2 Telecollaboration 1,76 97.98 < 0.001 0.386 ChatGPT 1,76 242.48 < 0.001 0.609 Tele × GPT 1,76 21.02 < 0.001 0.119 Table 4. Two-Way ANOVA: main effects and interaction. Condition N Pretest M Posttest M Gain M Gain SD 95% CI 1: Control 20 64.58 64.93 0.35 3.40 [-1.35, 2.05] 2: AI Only 20 65.08 70.41 5.33 2.58 [4.04, 6.62] 3: Collab Only 20 64.30 67.06 2.76 2.56 [1.48, 4.04] 4: Full Treatment 20 64.87 76.76 11.90 2.85 [10.51, 13.29] Table 3. Descriptive statistics for pretest, posttest, and calculated gain scores across the four experimental conditions",162
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,12,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 12 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports ChatGPT was like a “handyman,” useful for generating ideas for the task… it was helpful in producing a good project. Before discussing it with my partner, I used ChatGPT to get ideas about the project. İt served as an innovative, helpful tool for answering all my questions. The project we received came out perfectly because we used this digital resource. I called it “smart digital assistance” because it helped me know what to do in writing and designing websites. My partner said the same thing. Sometimes, when discussing something with my partner and not understanding something, I quickly seek assistance with ChatGPT. It was always there to help me gain clarity when I wanted. This is good for all students. This theme aligns with Vygotsky’s (1978) zone of proximal development and scaffolding theory. ChatGPT functions as a ‘more capable other,’ providing responsive support calibrated to students’ specific needs. Some of their comments indicate that generative artificial intelligence was helpful to the student as it was perceived as intelligent personal assistance for",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,12,"scaffolding theory. ChatGPT functions as a ‘more capable other,’ providing responsive support calibrated to students’ specific needs. Some of their comments indicate that generative artificial intelligence was helpful to the student as it was perceived as intelligent personal assistance for teaching and learning. Theme 2: Self-confidence Fourteen of 20 interviewed students (70%) reported that access to ChatGPT increased their confidence in tackling writing and design tasks. This theme suggests that students reported that generative AI boosted their self-confidence during the telecollaborative project. The participants noted that self-confidence played a vital role in the outcome of the projects assigned to them. These were some of their comments that suggest this theme. Knowing that ChatGPT has answers to questions about the project boosts my confidence and spurs me to perform the task assigned excellently. My partner was always curious about how to execute the task, but with ChatGPT, I was confident we could complete it successfully. This thought became a reality because I used ChatGPT to generate ideas for my projects, which turned out well. Another commented; Learning to write is complex,",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,12,"execute the task, but with ChatGPT, I was confident we could complete it successfully. This thought became a reality because I used ChatGPT to generate ideas for my projects, which turned out well. Another commented; Learning to write is complex, and the teacher helped us as well…I did very well using ChatGPT to generate ideas about the paragraphs. Then, I was confident that I could design the website using ChatGPT.I received some helpful ideas, and my confidence grew knowing that, with ChatGPT, I could do some things independently without asking the teacher for guidance or assistance. This aligns with Bandura’s (1997) social cognitive theory and self-efficacy. Students’ confidence grew knowing a helpful tool was available, representing productive self-efficacy (not dependent, but capable). These were some of the participants’ responses that reflect this theme. Theme 3: Laissez-Faire attitude Twelve of 20 interviewed students (60%) reported observing concerning patterns of reduced engagement and effort among some peers who had access to ChatGPT. This theme emerged as the most critical finding regarding implementation challenges. Here are some of their comments indicating this theme;",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,12,"interviewed students (60%) reported observing concerning patterns of reduced engagement and effort among some peers who had access to ChatGPT. This theme emerged as the most critical finding regarding implementation challenges. Here are some of their comments indicating this theme; When we organize meetings via videoconferencing, students do not take them seriously because they think they can get by with ChatGPT. However, these meetings are crucial. We were assigned tasks, and we needed to study the materials to complete them, but some students did not take it seriously. When I asked why they behaved this way, they said that with ChatGPT, we do not need to study the materials. I do not believe that ChatGPT is just a personal assistant to guide the students. We should rely on it sparingly. The students do not care about studying the materials sent by the instructor because they believe that ChatGPT can do the task. Sometimes, they do not even listen to the teacher while she teaches. ChatGPT is helpful, but we must rely on something other than it to avoid becoming lazy.",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,12,"by the instructor because they believe that ChatGPT can do the task. Sometimes, they do not even listen to the teacher while she teaches. ChatGPT is helpful, but we must rely on something other than it to avoid becoming lazy. Another stated; I once asked my partner why he did not study the project material, and he replied that he could always use ChatGPT to find out how to do the project. He always told me not to bother scheduling days for our meetings; he said it was not necessary because we could use ChatGPT to handle the project. He only Themes Codes F(%) Intelligent personal assistant Assist, helper, smart helper, guide, assisted 16(80) Self-confidence Confidence, more enlightened, 14(70) Laissez-Faire attitude Lazy, laziness, less interested, do not care 12(60) Table 5. Thematic analysis of students’ opinions of the role of ChatGPT in improving learning outcomes in a telecollaborative learning environment skills.",151
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 13 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports attended some meetings that I scheduled. When I asked why he never attended the meetings, he replied, “I have the ChatGPT.” Theme 3 reports represent observations of peer behavior, not universal attitudes. The fact that 60% observed this pattern in peers, while fewer exhibited it themselves, suggests negative outcomes may be concentrated among learners with preexisting risk factors. Some of the participants’ comments reflected the themes generated from the interviews. The findings are discussed in the next section. Discussion The following results emerged from the findings derived above. In this study of 80 engineering sophomores in an EFL context, combining ChatGPT with telecollaboration significantly improved writing outcomes, as measured using a standardized rubric. The effect was large and consistent (Condition 4: M gain = 11.90 points, SD = 2.85), but generalizability to other populations, disciplines, and contexts remains an open empirical question. Equally, these findings align with the suppositions of some studies that ChatGPT can help develop students’ writing skills16, 28. Furthermore, comparing the outcomes of the telecollaborative projects assigned to",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"to other populations, disciplines, and contexts remains an open empirical question. Equally, these findings align with the suppositions of some studies that ChatGPT can help develop students’ writing skills16, 28. Furthermore, comparing the outcomes of the telecollaborative projects assigned to the two groups, this study found that the experimental conditions (Conditions 2, 3, and 4) significantly outperformed Condition 1 (control group). These results affirm the findings of similar studies that using ChatGPT may assist students in developing ideas to execute projects29. Notably, there was a significant difference in students’ learning outcomes, corroborating the conclusions of some authors that using ChatGPT may improve learning outcomes and should be encouraged17. Meanwhile, these are indicators of ChatGPT’s potential when applied in the classroom; our study found that condition 1 (the control group) achieved notable improvements in the outcomes of their telecollaborative projects, despite not using ChatGPT for their classwork. These suggestions suggest that students can perform well without using ChatGPT, as a recent study indicates10. Nonetheless, their writing skills did not improve significantly compared to the other group. Already, some authors have suggested",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"not using ChatGPT for their classwork. These suggestions suggest that students can perform well without using ChatGPT, as a recent study indicates10. Nonetheless, their writing skills did not improve significantly compared to the other group. Already, some authors have suggested that one of the challenges of telecollaboration is access to technology13. At least, this study found that using digital resources like ChatGPT may be affordable and accessible to all students, thus making it ideal for a telecollaborative learning approach. The three qualitative themes that emerged from student interviews do not represent isolated phenomena; rather, they reflect interconnected dimensions of how students cognitively and affectively engage with AI- assisted collaborative learning. Understanding these connections reveals the mechanisms through which the synergistic interaction between telecollaboration and ChatGPT produces substantially larger gains than either intervention alone. Students characterized ChatGPT as “like a handyman useful in generating ideas,” describing a metacognitive experience enabling externalization of problem-solving processes, responsive feedback, and iterative idea refinement. Consistent with established literature showing ChatGPT provides personalized assistance28, 36, this function aligns precisely with Vygotsky’s zone of proximal development (ZPD),",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"useful in generating ideas,” describing a metacognitive experience enabling externalization of problem-solving processes, responsive feedback, and iterative idea refinement. Consistent with established literature showing ChatGPT provides personalized assistance28, 36, this function aligns precisely with Vygotsky’s zone of proximal development (ZPD), wherein a “more capable other” provides calibrated support. ChatGPT’s adaptive explanations created personalized learning experiences, as acknowledged by research40, 41 that neither traditional flipped instruction nor peer collaboration alone could replicate. Condition 2 students demonstrated a 5.33-point gain, substantially exceeding the control’s 0.35-point gain, confirming that personalized AI scaffolding alone produced meaningful improvement. Condition 4 produced the largest gains (11.90 points, 18% improvement) by combining cognitive scaffolding with telecollaboration. This synergy reveals a critical insight: telecollaboration amplified ChatGPT scaffolding’s effectiveness by creating authentic social contexts for applying learned concepts, as acknowledged by research29. Turkish and Chinese engineering students engaged in “distributed cognition,” leveraging ChatGPT’s suggestions while negotiating meaning with international peers through multiple iterations. This synergistic effect far exceeded the additive prediction (8.09 points). 70% of interviewed students reported ChatGPT access increased confidence in writing and design tasks—a fundamental shift",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"“distributed cognition,” leveraging ChatGPT’s suggestions while negotiating meaning with international peers through multiple iterations. This synergistic effect far exceeded the additive prediction (8.09 points). 70% of interviewed students reported ChatGPT access increased confidence in writing and design tasks—a fundamental shift in self-efficacy. For engineering students, particularly Chinese students writing in English, ChatGPT reduced performance anxiety through on-demand expert guidance. Students developed “productive self-efficacy”—realistic confidence grounded in actual capability. Condition 4 participants achieved the highest posttest scores (M = 76.76) and the lowest gain variability (SD = 2.85), indicating remarkably consistent improvements across heterogeneous students, as noted in the research5, 15, 24. This combination of cognitive scaffolding and affective support created accessible pathways to improvement regardless of baseline ability or prior confidence, contrasting sharply with Condition 1’s larger standard deviation (SD = 3.40). While 60% of students observed concerning engagement patterns among some peers, these observations clustered among subsets, suggesting negative effects depend on learner characteristics and pedagogical structuring. Students who developed productive self-efficacy viewed ChatGPT as a learning scaffold and showed less vulnerability to dependency. Condition 4’s telecollaborative context required",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,13,"among some peers, these observations clustered among subsets, suggesting negative effects depend on learner characteristics and pedagogical structuring. Students who developed productive self-efficacy viewed ChatGPT as a learning scaffold and showed less vulnerability to dependency. Condition 4’s telecollaborative context required active participation and cross- cultural negotiation, creating accountability structures that mitigated AI dependency. Concerns emerged primarily in discussions of unsupervised access, demonstrating that pedagogical structure—requiring active collaboration, establishing clear learning objectives, and embedding AI within accountability frameworks— moderates whether integration produces productive scaffolding or counterproductive dependency. This study demonstrates empirically what theoretical frameworks predict: AI tools and collaborative learning are mutually amplifying, not merely additive. The significant interaction effect (F = 21.02, p<.001, η2p=.119) reflects a genuine pedagogical phenomenon wherein each intervention’s strengths compensate for the other’s limitations. Telecollaboration creates authentic social contexts and accountability structures essential for meaningful learning, as suggested by research12, but generates substantial cognitive demands—managing group dynamics, negotiating across linguistic and cultural differences, and coordinating asynchronous/synchronous",160
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 14 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports communication across time zones. These demands overwhelm students lacking adequate scaffolding, potentially reducing engagement, as noted in studies on AI2. Conversely, ChatGPT provides personalized cognitive scaffolding and on-demand explanations but lacks the social context, authentic audience, and collaborative accountability that drive sustained motivation47. The synergy emerges when ChatGPT scaffolds collaboration’s cognitive demands, freeing resources for social-emotional work, while the authentic collaborative context makes ChatGPT’s assistance feel purposeful rather than abstract. This integration aligns with three complementary theoretical frameworks. From a constructivist perspective (Piaget, von Glasersfeld), Condition 4 students actively constructed understanding by leveraging ChatGPT while negotiating meaning through collaborative interaction. From an adaptive learning perspective19, ChatGPT’s adaptive explanations combined with telecollaboration’s responsive interaction created a doubly adaptive environment. From a human-AI collaboration framework, students and AI functioned as a cognitive unit through distributed cognition, with each compensating for the other’s limitations. Regarding heterogeneity, Condition 4’s SD of 2.85 indicates consistent gains despite individual variation (8–15 points). Exploratory analysis suggests that students with lower baseline writing proficiency benefited more from ChatGPT scaffolding,",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"through distributed cognition, with each compensating for the other’s limitations. Regarding heterogeneity, Condition 4’s SD of 2.85 indicates consistent gains despite individual variation (8–15 points). Exploratory analysis suggests that students with lower baseline writing proficiency benefited more from ChatGPT scaffolding, aligning with Vygotsky’s ZPD: scaffolding benefits those at the edge of their capabilities, not those well within them. The interaction effect (d = 3.85) substantially exceeds published effect sizes for either intervention alone. Meta-analytic reviews report telecollaboration d = 0.65–0.85 and AI tutoring d = 0.52–0.71. Even Condition 2 (d = 1.52) exceeded typical AI tutoring effects, reflecting ChatGPT’s flexibility over earlier domain-specific systems. These findings establish that synergistic combinations generate emergent benefits that are unpredictable from the independent effects of the components. Conclusions The study aims to explore the use of ChatGPT in a telecollaborative learning environment to improve student learning outcomes and to ascertain students’ perceptions of ChatGPT’s impact on learning outcomes. Based on the results above, the following conclusions were made: The study summarises that ChatGPT, in a telecollaborative learning environment, improves students’ learning outcomes. This conclusion",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"student learning outcomes and to ascertain students’ perceptions of ChatGPT’s impact on learning outcomes. Based on the results above, the following conclusions were made: The study summarises that ChatGPT, in a telecollaborative learning environment, improves students’ learning outcomes. This conclusion is based on the significant improvement in students’ writing skills. Equally, the outcome of the telecollaborative projects was positively remarked. This study fills three critical gaps. It is the first to employ a 2 × 2 factorial design examining ChatGPT and telecollaboration simultaneously, revealing that the combination produces a 11.90-point gain (vs. 0.35 for neither condition). By pairing Turkish and Chinese students across time zones, it tests ChatGPT’s utility in high-complexity telecollaborative scenarios, demonstrating both efficacy and risks. Unlike cheerleading studies, it surfaced student concerns (laissez-faire attitudes) alongside benefits, providing practitioners with realistic guidance for implementation. Furthermore, the study summarises that students perceived using ChatGPT in their learning from different perspectives. First, ChatGPT was seen as a valuable personal assistant for learning. However, this may result in self-confidence, which the study fears may lead to an I- know-it-all attitude. The",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"that students perceived using ChatGPT in their learning from different perspectives. First, ChatGPT was seen as a valuable personal assistant for learning. However, this may result in self-confidence, which the study fears may lead to an I- know-it-all attitude. The study argues that this may be the reason behind students’ laissez-faire attitude towards lessons. From all indications, using ChatGPT may lead to student laziness and lower lesson attendance. This must be carefully managed. When properly structured with explicit training, collaborative accountability, and authentic international contexts, ChatGPT integrated within telecollaborative learning demonstrated substantial improvements in engineering students’ writing outcomes. Whether these benefits generalize to other disciplines, student populations, and implementation contexts is a question for future research. Implication These findings, derived from engineering education in an EFL context, suggest implications for how engineering educators might prepare students to use AI in future professional collaborative contexts. The mechanisms identified—ChatGPT as a cognitive scaffold, collaborative accountability structures, integration of AI within human collaboration—may have broader applicability. However, disciplinary differences and different professional fields may require adaptations. The engineering students in this study were",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"future professional collaborative contexts. The mechanisms identified—ChatGPT as a cognitive scaffold, collaborative accountability structures, integration of AI within human collaboration—may have broader applicability. However, disciplinary differences and different professional fields may require adaptations. The engineering students in this study were not merely acquiring isolated writing skills; they were preparing for professional contexts wherein they will collaborate internationally and must leverage AI tools for productivity. The results suggest that the most effective preparation is not practice using AI tools in isolation but rather integrating them into authentic collaborative workflows. Students who learned to use ChatGPT as a thinking partner within collaborative problem-solving developed not just improved writing skills but also productive habits of AI integration—approaches to AI tool use that treat it as a cognitive assistant within human-centered collaboration rather than as a substitute for human effort or expertise. These findings have potential relevance for professional development and AI literacy across sectors. Teacher preparation and student engagement Teachers require systematic preparation to integrate ChatGPT into telecollaborative learning models effectively. Training should address using ChatGPT as a teaching aid, managing discussions involving",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,14,"have potential relevance for professional development and AI literacy across sectors. Teacher preparation and student engagement Teachers require systematic preparation to integrate ChatGPT into telecollaborative learning models effectively. Training should address using ChatGPT as a teaching aid, managing discussions involving ChatGPT, and discerning when to deploy AI rather than human intervention. Understanding how students interact with ChatGPT and its impact on engagement in collaborative environments is essential for informing future teaching practices. This study demonstrates that structured pedagogical design, not technology alone, determines whether AI integration succeeds.",87
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 15 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Navigating the ethical landscape The substantial learning benefits must be situated within careful ethical analysis. Some students observed concerning laissez-faire attitudes in peers using ChatGPT without a pedagogical structure, raising fundamental questions about authentic learning. The critical distinction is “student-learned versus student-bypassed,” not “AI-generated versus student- generated.” Traditional academic integrity frameworks predate the understanding of AI as fundamentally different from human assistance. ChatGPT operates through statistical pattern-matching, occupying an epistemically ambiguous space. In this study, students using ChatGPT within structured tasks requiring original problem-solving engaged in authentic epistemic work—ChatGPT assisted thinking rather than replacing it. The characterization as “a handyman useful in generating ideas” reflects ideation assistance rather than outsourcing thought. Students bypassing substantive thinking engaged in epistemic fraud, regardless of technical novelty. In condition 4, ChatGPT integrated within structured telecollaborative contexts produced consistent gains (low SD) with no reported dependency patterns—suggesting structured integration mitigated risks. Conversely, unsupervised use creates bypassing risks for learning. Therefore, ethical evaluation must be pedagogy-focused: ethical integration requires deliberate design, maintaining epistemic integrity, and preserving authentic",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"contexts produced consistent gains (low SD) with no reported dependency patterns—suggesting structured integration mitigated risks. Conversely, unsupervised use creates bypassing risks for learning. Therefore, ethical evaluation must be pedagogy-focused: ethical integration requires deliberate design, maintaining epistemic integrity, and preserving authentic engagement. Pedagogical implications for learning design and student agency Traditional educational theory emphasizes student autonomy as paramount. However, empirical evidence indicates that the most profound learning occurred with collaborative AI support (Condition 4), rather than independent AI support (Condition 2). This challenges the assumption that “more independence is better,” suggesting that human collaboration informed by AI scaffolding creates optimal conditions. The reframing shifts from “independent learning with AI assistance” to “leveraging AI within collaborative learning.” Rather than universally distributing ChatGPT, institutions should structure AI integration around collaborative tasks where individual use is embedded within group accountability. Students draft ideas individually using ChatGPT, then justify and refine them through peer discussion, preserving scaffolding while maintaining social-epistemic benefits. Assessment design is critical. Traditional individual assessments cannot distinguish between student and AI-assisted thinking. However, collaborative assessments—group projects, peer discussion, live presentations— create natural",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"using ChatGPT, then justify and refine them through peer discussion, preserving scaffolding while maintaining social-epistemic benefits. Assessment design is critical. Traditional individual assessments cannot distinguish between student and AI-assisted thinking. However, collaborative assessments—group projects, peer discussion, live presentations— create natural accountability structures requiring authentic engagement. Condition 4’s success suggests assessment redesign offers the most promising path toward maintaining academic integrity while leveraging AI’s legitimate benefits. Equity, access, and long-term outcomes Substantial benefits risk exacerbating educational inequities if AI access remains restricted to privileged populations. Critically, lower-performing students benefited most from ChatGPT, making equitable access both a justice and effectiveness concern. Institutions must ensure universal access through institutional licensing, open-source alternatives, or free-tier integration. Long-term questions remain: Do benefits generalize to younger students with developing metacognitive capacities? Do students develop independent problem-solving skills or become dependent? Does confidence translate to resilience without support? Early exposure to powerful AI tools may shape future learning: students may delegate challenging tasks to AI or use it as a thinking partner within collaborative contexts. These trajectories depend critically on pedagogical framing—whether AI is presented as",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"without support? Early exposure to powerful AI tools may shape future learning: students may delegate challenging tasks to AI or use it as a thinking partner within collaborative contexts. These trajectories depend critically on pedagogical framing—whether AI is presented as a substitute for thinking or as a tool enhancing thinking. Longitudinal research is essential. Limitations and scope This study’s findings are robust within its specific context but should not be overgeneralized beyond the study sample and setting. Explicit acknowledgment of limitations is essential for the appropriate interpretation and application of results. For instance, the study comprised 80 engineering sophomores (ages 19–21) from two private universities. The sample represents a narrow disciplinary context (engineering), specific institution types (private universities), and specific developmental stage (second-year undergraduates). Findings are most directly applicable to Engineering education and similar STEM disciplines with writing-intensive coursework. Similarly, findings may apply to only English as a Foreign Language (EFL) context, where students write in a non-native language, and to undergraduate populations with basic metacognitive development. Findings may not generalize to younger students (K-12, early college) who are still",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"findings may apply to only English as a Foreign Language (EFL) context, where students write in a non-native language, and to undergraduate populations with basic metacognitive development. Findings may not generalize to younger students (K-12, early college) who are still developing metacognitive awareness and self-regulation. Findings may not generalize to other academic disciplines (humanities, social sciences) with different writing demands and assessment structures. The study was conducted over a single 14-week semester (Spring 2025). While this represents a typical academic term, it does not address the long-term retention of writing improvements, the long-term effects on student independence, or the sustainability of confidence gains. Longitudinal research spanning multiple years is needed to address these questions. Aside from temporal limitations, the deliberate choice of a high-complexity context was a strength (stress-testing the intervention) but also a limitation, as results may not apply to lower- complexity telecollaborative scenarios. Despite these limitations, this study provides experimental evidence that, in engineering education at private universities with appropriate instructional support, ChatGPT and telecollaboration can interact synergistically to improve writing outcomes. The mechanisms identified (cognitive scaffolding, confidence",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,15,"to lower- complexity telecollaborative scenarios. Despite these limitations, this study provides experimental evidence that, in engineering education at private universities with appropriate instructional support, ChatGPT and telecollaboration can interact synergistically to improve writing outcomes. The mechanisms identified (cognitive scaffolding, confidence building, distributed cognition) likely apply broadly, but empirical confirmation across diverse contexts is needed.",54
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,16,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:29076 16 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z www.nature.com/scientificreports Suggestions for future studies While the conclusions of this study are insightful, Follow-up studies could explore the long- term effects of using ChatGPT in telecollaborative learning. This could involve tracking students’ progress over an extended period to assess the sustainability of any improvements in learning outcomes. Comparative studies could examine the effectiveness of ChatGPT alongside other AI tools and traditional teaching methods in telecollaborative settings. This could help identify ChatGPT’s unique contributions and limitations in improving learning outcomes. Research could focus on developing pedagogical strategies for effectively integrating ChatGPT into telecollaborative and other learning models. This could involve exploring how teachers can best leverage ChatGPT to support various instructional goals and learning objectives. Our study focused on students’ experiences; future research could investigate students’ and teachers’ experiences with ChatGPT in telecollaborative learning. This could include assessing user satisfaction, identifying usability issues, and understanding factors influencing engagement with ChatGPT. Research could explore the generalizability of findings across different educational contexts, student populations, and subject areas. This could involve replicating the study in",180
A050,A050.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-59225-z,16,"learning. This could include assessing user satisfaction, identifying usability issues, and understanding factors influencing engagement with ChatGPT. Research could explore the generalizability of findings across different educational contexts, student populations, and subject areas. This could involve replicating the study in diverse settings to assess the robustness of the results. Altogether, future research could build on the initial study’s findings to better understand the potential benefits and challenges of using ChatGPT in telecollaborative learning and to inform best practices for its implementation. Data availability The author affirms that data supporting this study’s findings are available upon reasonable request. Received: 18 February 2026; Ac",102
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,1,"Academic Editor: Carlos Hervás-Gómez Received: 29 May 2025 Revised: 19 June 2025 Accepted: 24 June 2025 Published: 27 June 2025 Citation: Mutanga, M.B.; Msane, J.; Mndaweni, T.N.; Hlongwane, B.B.; Ngcobo, N.Z. Exploring the Impact of LLM Prompting on Students’ Learning. Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31. https://doi.org/10.3390/ higheredu4030031 Copyright: © 2025 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license (https://creativecommons.org/ licenses/by/4.0/). Article Exploring the Impact of LLM Prompting on Students’ Learning Murimo Bethel Mutanga * , Jotham Msane, Thaddeus Ndumiso Mndaweni, Bongokuhle Brightman Hlongwane and Neliswa Ziyanda Ngcobo Department of Information and Communication Technology, Mangosuthu University of Technology, Durban 4026, South Africa; msane@mut.ac.za (J.M.); 22116979@live.mut.ac.za (T.N.M.); 22109577@live.mut.ac.za (B.B.H.); 22117916@live.mut.ac.za (N.Z.N.) * Correspondence: mutangamb@mut.ac.za Abstract Integrating large language models (LLMs) into higher education, particularly in program- ming education, reshapes how students interact with learning materials and develop coding skills. However, while the general utility of LLMs like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude has been acknowledged, a critical gap exists in understanding how",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,1,"higher education, particularly in program- ming education, reshapes how students interact with learning materials and develop coding skills. However, while the general utility of LLMs like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude has been acknowledged, a critical gap exists in understanding how specific prompt- ing strategies influence student learning outcomes. This issue is significant in the context of programming education, where problem-solving, critical thinking, and conceptual under- standing are essential yet complex cognitive skills. Although prior research has classified prompting behaviors, it has largely failed to assess their impact on actual learning. To address this gap, we explored how IT students employ various prompting strategies when engaging with LLMs during programming tasks. A mixed-methods approach was adopted, primarily qualitative and supported by basic quantitative analysis, to examine 842 prompts generated by 140 students across four core software development modules. The results revealed five dominant prompting strategies, which varied significantly in how they facil- itated learning. Our findings suggest that prompting strategies significantly shape how students interact with LLMs and influence the depth of their learning. Keywords: large language models (LLMs); prompt engineering;",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,1,"five dominant prompting strategies, which varied significantly in how they facil- itated learning. Our findings suggest that prompting strategies significantly shape how students interact with LLMs and influence the depth of their learning. Keywords: large language models (LLMs); prompt engineering; programming education; ChatGPT; student learning behavior; artificial intelligence in education 1. Introduction In recent years, intelligent systems capable of understanding and generating human- like content have taken center stage, driving a wave of transformation across different fields. These advanced models are not only revolutionizing how we interact with computers but also redefining creativity, problem-solving, and automation. From generating text, images, and videos to engaging in natural conversations, translating languages, and even debugging complex code, their capabilities mark a significant shift in the digital landscape. As these systems continue to evolve, they are unlocking groundbreaking possibilities, reshaping industries, and pushing the boundaries of what machines can achieve. According to [1], the most widely used large language models (LLMs) today include ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Mistral, and Llama, with ChatGPT remaining the most dominant overall. However, ref. [2], found that students in the",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,1,"boundaries of what machines can achieve. According to [1], the most widely used large language models (LLMs) today include ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Mistral, and Llama, with ChatGPT remaining the most dominant overall. However, ref. [2], found that students in the programming field also frequently use tools like Chegg and Bing Chat (now known as Microsoft Copilot). These two platforms, while not consistently ranked among the top general-purpose LLMs, are often favored for their academic focus and support in educational contexts. Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 https://doi.org/10.3390/higheredu4030031",87
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,2,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 2 of 11 LLMs have significantly simplified various aspects of modern life, including reducing complexity in the field of programming [3]. For instance, a study by [4] found that LLMs have encouraged some students to become active questioners by frequently initiating interactions with the tool and seeking information to deepen their understanding. In contrast, other students preferred a more passive approach, engaging minimally and choosing instead to observe and process information are referred to as silent listeners. While the study provides valuable insight into how students interact with AI agents, it does not evaluate the effectiveness of these interactions on learning outcomes. For many IT students, AI tools serve as active tutors, offering instant explanations of programming concepts and code snippets. Additionally, this immediate feedback loop enables students to debug errors much faster, understand complicated syntax, and complete tasks efficiently. LLM also provide support to students who may struggle with language barriers or lack access to an actual tutor [5]. Despite this convenience, it may come with a cost, in which students may start",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,2,"complicated syntax, and complete tasks efficiently. LLM also provide support to students who may struggle with language barriers or lack access to an actual tutor [5]. Despite this convenience, it may come with a cost, in which students may start to rely too much on the tool, utilizing it for completing tasks without fully engaging in the learning process [6–8]. This over-reliance would become a hindrance to developing core problem-solving skills such as debugging logic errors and the comprehension of algorithm design on their own [6]. The accessibility of LLMs has contributed to a rise in academic dishonesty, as students are increasingly using AI-generated content to complete assignments and exams, often presenting it as their own work [9]. However, ref. [10] found that integrating LLM tools into students’ collaborative programming improved their computational thinking and reduced cognitive load. In contrast, ref. [11] observed that over-reliance on LLM tools for tasks requiring independent cognitive effort has significantly impacted academic performance, resulting in only surface-level understanding for some students. The growing dependence on LLMs among IT students is influenced by several factors.",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,2,"ref. [11] observed that over-reliance on LLM tools for tasks requiring independent cognitive effort has significantly impacted academic performance, resulting in only surface-level understanding for some students. The growing dependence on LLMs among IT students is influenced by several factors. One major contributor is the increasing complexity of programming tasks, as assignments become more intricate, students often struggle to develop solutions independently. In such cases, LLM tools become an appealing alternative, offering quick code generation, debugging support, and step-by-step assistance, which helps manage these challenges efficiently [12]. Additionally, ref. [13] emphasizes that many students face conceptual gaps in their understanding of programming. To bridge these gaps, they often turn to LLMs for explanations and ready-made solutions. While these tools can support learning, excessive reliance may lead to superficial understanding, as students might use AI-generated code without fully understanding the underlying logic or principles they are expected to master. It is thus crucial for students to understand the best ways of using LLM tools. A key aspect of effectively using LLM tools is prompting. Good prompting techniques often result in the",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,2,"logic or principles they are expected to master. It is thus crucial for students to understand the best ways of using LLM tools. A key aspect of effectively using LLM tools is prompting. Good prompting techniques often result in the desired output from LLM tools. Prompting first emerged in natural language processing (NLP) tasks in 2018 with a single dataset and was further refined in 2021 with multiple datasets. By 2022, text-to-code and code-to-code techniques were introduced and have continued to evolve. The text- to-code approach involves describing programming problems in natural language, while the code-to-code strategy uses partial code snippets or hints to help ChatGPT generate complete solutions. Students’ prompting strategies play a crucial role in how they use LLMs for program- ming tasks. Well-structured prompts lead to more accurate responses, making learning and solving coding problems easier for students. As LLMs become more common in education, understanding how to prompt them is essential for maximizing their benefits. There has been extensive research examining how students formulate prompts when interacting with",172
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,2,"students. As LLMs become more common in education, understanding how to prompt them is essential for maximizing their benefits. There has been extensive research examining how students formulate prompts when interacting with",32
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,3,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 3 of 11 large language models, aiming to identify strategies that optimise response quality and support deeper learning. The work reported in [14] presented a qualitative analysis of students’ prompts, identifying three distinct prompting strategies. Moreover, their research also uncovered ad- ditional prompting techniques that students use when interacting with ChatGPT. One such strategy is Single Copy & Paste Prompting, where a student copies a question from the prob- lem statement and pastes it directly into the ChatGPT prompt field without modification. The student then adopts the response generated by ChatGPT and submits it to the Moodle quiz without any further revisions. Another strategy is Single Reformulated Prompting, in which a student rephrases the question either partially or entirely in their own words before prompting ChatGPT. The student then uses the AI-generated response and submits it to the Moodle quiz without making any changes. Similarly, Multiple-Question Prompting occurs when a student asks ChatGPT multiple questions before selecting and using the response provided by the model. While existing studies have begun to explore how students",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,3,"submits it to the Moodle quiz without making any changes. Similarly, Multiple-Question Prompting occurs when a student asks ChatGPT multiple questions before selecting and using the response provided by the model. While existing studies have begun to explore how students interact with LLMs during programming tasks and have categorized various prompting strategies, such as copy-paste, reformulation, and iterative prompting there remains a limited understanding of how these strategies influence learning and skill development. Much of the current literature is descriptive, focusing on how students use these tools, rather than evaluative, assessing whether different prompting behaviors result in meaningful improvements in programming comprehension, problem-solving ability, or academic performance. Consequently, there is a need for deeper, outcome-focused research that examines whether and how prompting strategies used by IT students with LLM tools directly impact their learning. This study, therefore, seeks to investigate the prompting techniques IT students employ when using LLMs and evaluate the possible impact on their learning. 2. Materials and Methods 2.1. Research Design This study followed a qualitative research design with minor elements of quantitative analysis. The qualitative approach",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,3,"the prompting techniques IT students employ when using LLMs and evaluate the possible impact on their learning. 2. Materials and Methods 2.1. Research Design This study followed a qualitative research design with minor elements of quantitative analysis. The qualitative approach was best suited because it allowed us to explore how students interacted with Large Language Models when completing programming tasks. Our focus was on understanding the students’ intentions, thought processes, and learning behavior through their prompts. Although we counted the number of prompts to identify common patterns, the main goal was to examine the nature and depth of these prompts. To do this effectively, we needed a qualitative lens that could help us interpret not just what students asked, but why they asked it. This mixed approach of using both qualitative and quantitative elements was inspired by three similar research papers. Ref. [3] explored the use of LLMs in programming tasks by combining qualitative analysis of how students interacted with the tools and quantitative analysis of prompt frequency and task perfor- mance, which demonstrated how both methods can be used",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,3,"papers. Ref. [3] explored the use of LLMs in programming tasks by combining qualitative analysis of how students interacted with the tools and quantitative analysis of prompt frequency and task perfor- mance, which demonstrated how both methods can be used together to gain a complete understanding of learning behavior. The work in [4] adopted a similar approach by catego- rizing different types of student-generated prompts and applying statistical methods such as cluster analysis to interpret behavioral patterns, while also using qualitative techniques to explore student engagement types. Similarly, ref. [14] used a mixed-methods design, where they assessed the impact of student-generated prompts on ChatGPT performance through performance metrics and thematic analysis, offering deeper insights into prompt- ing behaviors. These studies supported our decision to adopt a mixed research approach, as they show how combining both qualitative and quantitative techniques can provide richer, more comprehensive findings when studying how students interact with AI tools in educational settings.",156
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,4,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 4 of 11 2.2. Context and Participants The investigation was conducted at a University of Technology in South Africa, within the Diploma in Software Development programme. All students registered for the diploma constitute the study’s target population. For practical and curricular relevance, we narrowed the accessible population to those taking the four core software-development modules that span the three programme years. All the students in the study had prior knowledge of LLM tools and had used them in similar tasks. Using a census approach, we invited every student enrolled in those modules to obtain complete responses from 140 out of 160 students across the three cohorts. This strategy maximised coverage while preserving validity, because the data was collected during scheduled laboratory sessions that all students were already required to attend. The multi-year sample provides het- erogeneity in prior programming experience and exposure to AI-assisted coding tools, thereby strengthening the transferability of findings. No demographic identifiers beyond year-of-study were collected. 2.3. Task Design and Data Collection Procedure Data generation took place during laboratory sessions scheduled",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,4,"het- erogeneity in prior programming experience and exposure to AI-assisted coding tools, thereby strengthening the transferability of findings. No demographic identifiers beyond year-of-study were collected. 2.3. Task Design and Data Collection Procedure Data generation took place during laboratory sessions scheduled in each module’s normal timetable. The session was not strictly supervised so as to give the students a relaxed atmosphere to complete the tasks. At the start of the session, lecturers issued a brief orientation covering study goals and the requirement that students operate under their own LLM credentials. All 140 participants signed into their personal ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude accounts (whichever they had already used for coursework). This ensured that every prompt could be traced back to an individual session record in the LLM provider’s history log. Each student then tackled at least three authentic software-development tasks, selected by the course lecturers to mirror weekly coursework. Students were free to query the LLM as often as needed; no time limits were imposed on individual tasks within the overall session window. All prompts and immediate LLM responses were later recorded",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,4,"the course lecturers to mirror weekly coursework. Students were free to query the LLM as often as needed; no time limits were imposed on individual tasks within the overall session window. All prompts and immediate LLM responses were later recorded and exported to CSV for analysis. 2.4. Data Preparation Raw CSV files were first de-identified (replacement of e-mail addresses and platform IDs with random hashes) and merged into a single corpus (N = 842 prompts). System messages, greeting phrases and automatically appended code comments were removed with a custom Python 3.12.0 script. Each prompt was then sentence-segmented to enable coding of multi-question requests at their most granular meaning unit. The cleaned dataset, together with a lookup table linking prompts to the corresponding task, was produced for further analysis. 2.5. Data Analysis The data analysis process followed a mixed-methods approach, combining descriptive quantitative techniques with qualitative thematic coding. To ensure the validity of our results, we used the typical workflow proposed in [15]. The aim was to identify patterns in prompting behaviour and interpret how these patterns reflected student learning strategies.",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,4,"descriptive quantitative techniques with qualitative thematic coding. To ensure the validity of our results, we used the typical workflow proposed in [15]. The aim was to identify patterns in prompting behaviour and interpret how these patterns reflected student learning strategies. Quantitatively, all 842 prompts were first categorized based on surface features such as prompt length, frequency per student, and LLM used. Descriptive statistics were calculated to determine the relative prevalence of different prompting styles. This included computing the percentage distribution of prompt types, which informed the dominant strategies reported in Section 3. Prompts were clustered into overarching categories representing prompting strategies. Each prompt was reviewed independently by two researchers to assign initial codes reflecting the prompt’s purpose and cognitive demand. Discrepancies",121
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,5,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 5 of 11 were discussed and resolved through consensus. Inter-rater reliability was quantified using Cohen’s κ, which yielded a value of 0.78, indicating substantial agreement between the two coders. This measure of reliability, proposed by [16] has been used and adopted in numerous studies [17,18]. To enhance analytical rigor, the researchers employed constant comparison meth- ods to refine categories and ensure mutual exclusivity among the strategies. Bloom’s Taxonomy [19] was used as an interpretive lens to assess the cognitive complexity of each prompt category, while elements of constructivist learning theory were applied to evaluate the extent of student engagement and conceptual growth. This combination of quantitative prevalence analysis and qualitative interpretive coding allowed for a better understanding of what students asked and how and why they interacted with LLMs during programming tasks. 3. Results This study investigated the prompting strategies used by Information Technology students when working with LLMs to complete programming-related tasks. Students were asked to complete tasks across core programming modules as part of their coursework. While the task content varied, the",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,5,"This study investigated the prompting strategies used by Information Technology students when working with LLMs to complete programming-related tasks. Students were asked to complete tasks across core programming modules as part of their coursework. While the task content varied, the prompts provided valuable insights into how students engaged with the AI tool. From this, we identified five dominant prompting strategies that influenced student learning, as shown in Figure 1. Figure 1. Distribution of Prompting Strategies Used by Students. 3.1. Direct Instruction Prompts This was the most frequently used strategy, accounting for approximately 60.2% of all student prompts. Many students used prompts that asked LLM tools to define concepts or explain programming ideas. For instance, one student asked, “What is supervised learning and how does it work?”, while another typed, “Explain the difference between classification and regression in machine learning”. This kind of prompting is similar to what students might do when reading a textbook or asking a direct question in class. Across the dataset, similar patterns appeared in prompts related to databases and programming tools. Examples included, “What is a",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,5,"kind of prompting is similar to what students might do when reading a textbook or asking a direct question in class. Across the dataset, similar patterns appeared in prompts related to databases and programming tools. Examples included, “What is a relational database?” or “How do I connect to SQL in Visual Studio?” These types of prompts suggest that students were primarily aiming to understand key terms or foundational ideas before attempting the actual task. This strategy proved very popular with students. Most responses from the LLM were simple and easy to follow, which helped students get started. However, in many",100
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,6,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 6 of 11 cases, students did not ask follow-up questions or attempt to apply what they had learned unless the task explicitly required it. This limited their opportunity to explore the topic more deeply. Therefore, while this type of prompt helped students obtain quick and relevant in- formation, relying on it alone may not support deep or independent learning—especially when students do not push themselves to explore further. From a Bloom’s Taxonomy perspective, these prompts reflect lower-order thinking skills such as “remembering” and “understanding”. There was limited evidence of constructivist learning, as students relied on the tool for direct answers rather than building knowledge through active exploration. Similarly, there were few signs of self-directed learning, since students seldom followed up or reflected beyond the initial response. 3.2. Step by Step Guidance Prompts This strategy made up 34.3% of the student prompts and reflected a more structured and deliberate approach. Some students broke their task into clear steps. One example involved a student who broke the task into clear steps, first asking what supervised learning",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,6,"made up 34.3% of the student prompts and reflected a more structured and deliberate approach. Some students broke their task into clear steps. One example involved a student who broke the task into clear steps, first asking what supervised learning is, then identifying beginner-friendly algorithms, and finally requesting a simple implementation in Python. A similar pattern continued in prompts such as, “How do I connect to a database in C#?”, followed by “How do I insert data using a button click?”, and later, “How do I display the inserted data in a DataGridView?” These prompts showed that the student was progressing from understanding the tools, to implementing functionality, and then verifying output all in a logical order. The use of step-by-step guidance prompts indicates that students were not merely seeking final answers but were instead using ChatGPT to support a structured learning process. By breaking down complex programming problems into smaller, manageable steps—such as starting with a conceptual query (“What is supervised learning?”) and progressing toward practical implementation (“Show me a minimal Python example”)— students were able to guide the",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,6,"structured learning process. By breaking down complex programming problems into smaller, manageable steps—such as starting with a conceptual query (“What is supervised learning?”) and progressing toward practical implementation (“Show me a minimal Python example”)— students were able to guide the LLM interaction in a way that mirrored instructional scaffolding. This sequencing allowed them to build understanding incrementally, reducing cognitive overload and making it easier to process and apply new information. The prompt structure also appeared to encourage deeper engagement, as students refined their understanding by comparing outputs, testing solutions, and adjusting their queries accordingly. In this way, the LLM functioned as a responsive learning tool, offering just-in-time feedback that supported students in moving from basic comprehension to application and analysis. While this approach aligns well with self-regulated learning and constructivist principles, it also highlights a potential risk: students may become overly reliant on predefined prompt structures, which could hinder their ability to tackle more complex, less familiar problems independently. For educators, this underscores the importance of teaching students not only how to use AI tools effectively but also how to",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,6,"overly reliant on predefined prompt structures, which could hinder their ability to tackle more complex, less familiar problems independently. For educators, this underscores the importance of teaching students not only how to use AI tools effectively but also how to critically evaluate and adapt their own learning strategies over time. 3.3. Code Centric Prompts This prompt style accounted for 4.9% of the data and was characterized by a strong focus on code generation. Some students jumped straight into implementation. Prompts included “Generate a classification model in Python using scikit-learn” and “Build a supervised learning model with Python code.” Others submitted prompts like “Give me the full code to connect C# to SQL and add data” and “Build a CRUD app in Windows Forms.” These students typically pasted the code into their development environment without much explanation or reflection.",138
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,7,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 7 of 11 The frequent use of direct code-generation prompts—such as “Write a Python func- tion for. . .” or “Generate code to solve. . .”—suggests that some students were primarily focused on completing tasks rather than understanding the underlying principles. This pattern, especially common among novice programmers, points to a surface approach to learning, where the primary goal is task performance rather than conceptual mastery. Within the framework of Bloom’s Taxonomy, this strategy remains largely at the “remem- bering” and “applying” levels, with limited evidence of progression to “analyzing” or “evaluating”, which require deeper engagement with logic, structure, and reasoning. Stu- dents often accepted and submitted the first code output generated, rarely modifying or testing alternative solutions—an indication of minimal reflection or critical assessment. From a constructivist perspective, this approach underutilizes the learner’s active role in meaning-making and problem-solving. Constructivist learning emphasizes the con- struction of knowledge through experience, interaction, and reflection; however, these direct prompts suggest a transactional model of learning, where the LLM is treated as a source of ready-made solutions",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,7,"active role in meaning-making and problem-solving. Constructivist learning emphasizes the con- struction of knowledge through experience, interaction, and reflection; however, these direct prompts suggest a transactional model of learning, where the LLM is treated as a source of ready-made solutions rather than a tool for exploration or conceptual growth. While these prompts can be helpful in time-constrained scenarios, they risk undermin- ing the development of higher-order thinking skills and independent problem-solving capabilities. Thus, educators should encourage students to move beyond passive code consumption and engage in iterative processes—debugging, evaluating alternatives, and justifying choices—to support deeper learning and knowledge transfer. 3.4. Reflective Prompts Only 0.2% of prompts were reflective, showing curiosity and a desire to understand reasoning or compare alternatives. Some students submitted prompts like, “Why is su- pervised learning better than unsupervised in some cases?” and “What are the pros and cons of using decision trees?” Others asked, “What are the different ways to connect a database in C#?” or “Which database type is best for a small inventory system?”. The use of troubleshooting prompts—such as requests for help with",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,7,"and cons of using decision trees?” Others asked, “What are the different ways to connect a database in C#?” or “Which database type is best for a small inventory system?”. The use of troubleshooting prompts—such as requests for help with specific error messages or unexpected output—demonstrates a more reflective and diagnostic engagement with the LLM. These prompts typically occurred after students had already attempted a solution, indicating a shift from code generation to problem analysis. Within Bloom’s Taxonomy, this behaviour reflects movement into the “analyzing” and “evaluating” stages, where learners are required to identify the causes of problems, interpret system feedback, and make informed decisions about revisions. Unlike direct code-generation prompts, which often bypass critical reasoning, troubleshooting prompts necessitate a deeper cognitive investment. From a constructivist learning perspective, this interaction embodies the prin- ciple that knowledge is actively constructed through experience, reflection, and iterative problem-solving. When students used ChatGPT to examine errors and explore alternative explanations, they engaged in an authentic learning process that mirrors expert behaviour. However, it is noteworthy—and concerning—that only a small percentage of prompts fell into",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,7,"experience, reflection, and iterative problem-solving. When students used ChatGPT to examine errors and explore alternative explanations, they engaged in an authentic learning process that mirrors expert behaviour. However, it is noteworthy—and concerning—that only a small percentage of prompts fell into this category. Given the nature of software development as a discipline grounded in debugging, problem decomposition, and iterative refinement, this limited use of trou- bleshooting strategies suggests a gap in how students are approaching computational thinking. The low frequency of these higher-order prompts raises pedagogical questions about students’ readiness to engage with the core practices of the discipline. Furthermore, the effectiveness of such prompts was highly dependent on how specifically and con- textually they were framed. Vague queries often returned unhelpful responses, whereas well-structured prompts—accompanied by relevant code snippets or error logs—enabled more meaningful feedback. This indicates that while LLMs can support higher-order think- ing, their value is maximized when students approach them as tools for hypothesis testing",158
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,8,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 8 of 11 and refinement rather than as simple troubleshooting shortcuts. Encouraging learners to articulate their debugging rationale not only enhances metacognitive awareness but also promotes the development of transferable skills essential for independent programming beyond AI-assisted contexts. 3.5. Debugging Prompts Debugging accounted for 0.5% of the prompts, where students used the LLM to identify and resolve errors in their code. Examples included prompts such as, “Why am I getting a value error in this Python model?” and “How do I fix this scikit-learn import error?” Others asked, “Why won’t my SQL query insert data?” or “How do I fix a null exception in my C# form?”. This strategy reflected active problem-solving and trial-and- error learning. While it wasn’t always clear whether students fully understood the fixes, the prompts showed persistence and a willingness to engage with the debugging process. Prompts that asked for explanations—such as “Explain what this code does” or “Why is this algorithm better?”—reflected a more conceptual engagement with the LLM and demonstrated an intent to understand rather than simply produce",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,8,"to engage with the debugging process. Prompts that asked for explanations—such as “Explain what this code does” or “Why is this algorithm better?”—reflected a more conceptual engagement with the LLM and demonstrated an intent to understand rather than simply produce or fix code. These prompts align with the “understanding”, “analyzing”, and in some cases, “evaluating” levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy, as they require the learner to interpret, compare, and make judgments about code logic and design choices. From a constructivist learning perspective, such prompts indicate a shift from procedural knowledge to conceptual understanding. When students actively sought to clarify how or why a certain approach worked, they were engaging in reflective learning—constructing meaning through dialogue and integrating new information with prior knowledge. These interactions are pedagogically valuable, as they promote not only comprehension but also critical thinking and abstraction, which are essential for long-term skill development in software engineering. However, similar to troubleshooting prompts, explanation-seeking prompts were relatively rare. This limited use is particularly concerning in the context of software development, a discipline that depends heavily on understanding how and why",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,8,"essential for long-term skill development in software engineering. However, similar to troubleshooting prompts, explanation-seeking prompts were relatively rare. This limited use is particularly concerning in the context of software development, a discipline that depends heavily on understanding how and why code behaves as it does. The low frequency of these conceptual prompts may signal a tendency among students to prioritise task completion over deep learning—potentially reinforced by the immediate utility and responsiveness of LLMs. Furthermore, the quality of these explanatory prompts varied significantly. Students who provided context—such as in- cluding code snippets or posing comparisons between algorithms—received richer, more accurate explanations. In contrast, vague or overly general questions yielded less infor- mative responses. This suggests that while LLMs have the capacity to foster conceptual engagement, their effectiveness is mediated by the student’s ability to formulate thoughtful, specific queries. Supporting students in asking better questions—ones that elicit deeper understanding—should therefore be a key instructional goal. Doing so not only enhances metacognitive awareness but also cultivates the analytical mindset required for professional software development. 4. Discussion The present study set out to",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,8,"in asking better questions—ones that elicit deeper understanding—should therefore be a key instructional goal. Doing so not only enhances metacognitive awareness but also cultivates the analytical mindset required for professional software development. 4. Discussion The present study set out to determine whether students’ interactions with a LLM demonstrably supported learning in an undergraduate software-development context. The prompt log tells a consistent story: most exchanges sat at Bloom’s “remembering” and “applying” levels, with students requesting ready-made code or syntax explanations and seldom progressing to the “analyzing”, “evaluating”, or “creating” stages that typify expert programming practice [20]. Less than 12% of all prompts fell into the higher-order categories of troubleshooting or reflective explanation, mirroring patterns of surface engagement reported in similar AI-assisted coding studies [21]. In constructivist terms, learners treated",128
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,9,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 9 of 11 the model as an answer dispenser rather than a partner in knowledge construction, thereby bypassing the productive struggle that underpins conceptual change [22,23]. This limited cognitive reach is unsurprising when one considers the design of the prompts themselves. Straightforward requests such as “Write a Python function that sorts a list” require little cognitive elaboration; the LLM supplies a working snippet, and the task appears complete. Cognitive-load theory predicts that such minimal-guidance tasks reduce germane load—learning-relevant effort—while preserving extraneous load, because students do not need to organise or integrate new information [24,25]. The result, as our qualitative inspection confirmed, is code that “works” but a mental model that remains opaque. Without deliberate opportunities to interrogate, debug, or adapt that output, students accumulate what [26] call surface knowledge—rapidly gained, rapidly lost. From a constructivist perspective, the scarcity of explanation-seeking and troubleshoot- ing prompts is particularly troubling. Knowledge in software engineering is built through cycles of hypothesis, test, and revision [27]. When only a small minority of learners ask “Why is this algorithm preferable?”",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,9,"constructivist perspective, the scarcity of explanation-seeking and troubleshoot- ing prompts is particularly troubling. Knowledge in software engineering is built through cycles of hypothesis, test, and revision [27]. When only a small minority of learners ask “Why is this algorithm preferable?” or “Explain the cause of this stack-trace”, opportunities to connect new code to prior mental schemata are forfeited. Consequently, the interaction pattern we observed cannot be said to exhibit learning in the strong sense of conceptual growth or transferable problem-solving skill [28]. We therefore make the following two recommendations to lecturers: 4.1. Re-Design Assessment Questions Instructors can push students beyond procedural recall by structuring tasks that require higher-order engagement. Questions that ask students to critique alternative im- plementations, interpret error logs, or refactor inefficient code oblige them to operate at Bloom’s “analyzing” and “evaluating” levels. Empirical work in computer-science educa- tion shows that when prompts embed such metacognitive triggers, learners spend more time reasoning and produce higher-quality solutions [29]. Rubrics should therefore allocate explicit marks for explanation, justification, and iteration, not merely for a runnable script. 4.2. Teach Prompt Engineering",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,9,"tion shows that when prompts embed such metacognitive triggers, learners spend more time reasoning and produce higher-quality solutions [29]. Rubrics should therefore allocate explicit marks for explanation, justification, and iteration, not merely for a runnable script. 4.2. Teach Prompt Engineering as a Literacy Prompting is itself a learnable skill [30]. Short workshops can demonstrate how to (a) provide sufficient context, (b) decompose complex problems, and (c) request stepwise reasoning or code annotations. When students use targeted prompts—e.g., “Given this traceback, describe two possible causes and suggest fixes with time-complexity estimates”—the LLM supplies feedback that naturally scaffolds inquiry, aligning with the fading-scaffold model of cognitive apprenticeship [31]. Early evidence indicates that explicit instruction in prompt design increases the proportion of explanation-seeking interactions by up to 30% and correlates with gains on post-test debugging tasks [32]. Together, these interventions reposition the LLM from an automated code generator to a catalytic tutor—one that supports learners in constructing, evaluating, and extending their own understanding. Future research should test the long-term impact of prompt-literacy curricula and higher-order assessment designs on students’ ability to transfer debugging",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,9,"from an automated code generator to a catalytic tutor—one that supports learners in constructing, evaluating, and extending their own understanding. Future research should test the long-term impact of prompt-literacy curricula and higher-order assessment designs on students’ ability to transfer debugging and design skills to unfamiliar, LLM-free environments. 5. Conclusions By exploring how IT students prompted LLMs during programming tasks, this study uncovered five core strategies that shaped their learning experiences. These ranged from seeking simple explanations to building complex solutions through structured and iterative dialogue. Each approach offered insight into how students used AI tools to support or sometimes short-circuit their understanding.",102
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,10,"Trends High. Educ. 2025, 4, 31 10 of 11 The analysis showed that while all five strategies were used by students, they varied in depth and impact. Direct Instruction helped with quick concept recall but rarely went beyond surface-level understanding. Step-by-Step Guidance encouraged deeper thinking and gradual skill-building. Code-Centric prompts offered speed but carried the risk of passive learning. Debugging Prompts highlighted problem-solving and resilience, while Reflective Prompts reflected critical thinking and evaluation. What becomes clear is that prompting is not just a technical skill it’s a learning habit. Students who approached LLMs tools with purpose, reflection, and a desire to understand are likely to get more out of the experience. These insights suggest that teaching students how to prompt more effectively through guided practice, modeling, or classroom discussions could strengthen their learning outcomes. In short, the way students engage with AI matters. By fostering better prompting habits, educators can help students become more independent, reflective, and capable learners in an AI-augmented educational environment. Author Contributions: Conceptualization, T.N.M. and M.B.M.; methodology, B.B.H.; software, N.Z.N.; validation, J.M. and M.B.M.; formal analysis,",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,10,"with AI matters. By fostering better prompting habits, educators can help students become more independent, reflective, and capable learners in an AI-augmented educational environment. Author Contributions: Conceptualization, T.N.M. and M.B.M.; methodology, B.B.H.; software, N.Z.N.; validation, J.M. and M.B.M.; formal analysis, T.N.M. and M.B.M.; investigation, B.B.H.; resources, J.M.; data curation, N.Z.N.; writing—original draft preparation, J.M. and B.B.H.; writing— review and editing, T.N.M. and J.M.; visualization, B.B.H. and M.B.M.; supervision, M.B.M.; project administration, M.B.M.; funding acquisition, M.B.M. All authors have read and agreed to the pub- lished version of the manuscript. Funding: This research was funded by the Research Directorate at Mangosuthu University of Technology. The APC was funded by the Research Directorate at Mangosuthu University. Institutional Review Board Statement: The study was approved by the Ethics Committee of Mangosuthu University of Technology (Ethical Clearance Number: RD1/15/2023, approved on 15 January 2023). Informed Consent Statement: Informed consent was obtained from all subjects involved in the study. The consent form for participation was distributed to all participants and signed. Data Availability Statement: Data are not publicly available as they are protected under the",180
A051,A051.pdf,10.3390/higheredu4030031,10,Informed Consent Statement: Informed consent was obtained from all subjects involved in the study. The consent form for participation was distributed to all participants and signed. Data Availability Statement: Data are not publicly available as they are protected under the ethics guidelines. Conflicts of Interest: The authors declare no conflicts of interest.,52
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,1,"Frontiers in Psychology 01 frontiersin.org Enhancing academic writing skills and motivation: assessing the efficacy of ChatGPT in AI-assisted language learning for EFL students Cuiping Song 1* and Yanping Song 2 1School of Foreign Studies, North Minzu University, Yinchuan, Ningxia, China, 2School of Public Administration, Central South University, Changsha, Hunan, China Introduction: This mixed-methods study evaluates the impact of AI-assisted language learning on Chinese English as a Foreign Language (EFL) students’ writing skills and writing motivation. As artificial intelligence (AI) becomes more prevalent in educational settings, understanding its effects on language learning outcomes is crucial. Methods: The study employs a comprehensive approach, combining quantitative and qualitative methods. The quantitative phase utilizes a pre-test and post- test design to assess writing skills. Fifty EFL students, matched for proficiency, are randomly assigned to experimental (AI-assisted instruction via ChatGPT) or control (traditional instruction) groups. Writing samples are evaluated using established scoring rubrics. Concurrently, semi-structured interviews are conducted with a subset of participants to explore writing motivation and experiences with AI-assisted learning. Results: Quantitative analysis reveals significant improvements in both writing skills and motivation among",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,1,"Writing samples are evaluated using established scoring rubrics. Concurrently, semi-structured interviews are conducted with a subset of participants to explore writing motivation and experiences with AI-assisted learning. Results: Quantitative analysis reveals significant improvements in both writing skills and motivation among students who received AI-assisted instruction compared to the control group. The experimental group demonstrates enhanced proficiency in various aspects of writing, including organization, coherence, grammar, and vocabulary. Qualitative findings showcase diverse perspectives, ranging from recognition of AI’s innovative instructional role and its positive influence on writing skills and motivation to concerns about contextual accuracy and over-reliance. Participants also reflect on the long- term impact and sustainability of AI-assisted instruction, emphasizing the need for ongoing development and adaptation of AI tools. Discussion: The nuanced findings offer a comprehensive understanding of AI’s transformative potential in education. These insights have practical implications for practitioners and researchers, emphasizing the benefits, challenges, and the evolving nature of AI’s role in language instruction. KEYWORDS AI-assisted language learning, writing skills, writing motivation, EFL students, mixed methods study, ChatGPT 1 Introduction Academic writing holds a pivotal role in",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,1,"practitioners and researchers, emphasizing the benefits, challenges, and the evolving nature of AI’s role in language instruction. KEYWORDS AI-assisted language learning, writing skills, writing motivation, EFL students, mixed methods study, ChatGPT 1 Introduction Academic writing holds a pivotal role in the language development of English language learners, necessitating proficiency in diverse areas such as writing organization, coherence, grammar, and vocabulary (Campbell, 2019). Proficient writing skills empower learners to effectively communicate their ideas, articulate thoughts clearly, and achieve academic excellence OPEN ACCESS EDITED BY Valentin Riemer, University of Ulm, Germany REVIEWED BY Bin Zou, Xi’an Jiaotong-Liverpool University, China Musa Nushi, Shahid Beheshti University, Iran *CORRESPONDENCE Cuiping Song songcp2023@163.com RECEIVED 18 July 2023 ACCEPTED 28 November 2023 PUBLISHED 15 December 2023 CORRECTED 22 July 2026 CITATION Song C and Song Y (2023) Enhancing academic writing skills and motivation: assessing the efficacy of ChatGPT in AI-assisted language learning for EFL students. Front. Psychol. 14:1260843. doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 COPYRIGHT © 2023 Song and Song. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,1,"ChatGPT in AI-assisted language learning for EFL students. Front. Psychol. 14:1260843. doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 COPYRIGHT © 2023 Song and Song. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 15 December 2023 DOI 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843",95
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 02 frontiersin.org across various professional domains (Yoon, 2011). However, the process of monitoring and providing insightful feedback on student writing poses challenges in terms of time, effort, and subjectivity (Yu and Lee, 2014; Lim and Phua, 2019). Moreover, English language learners often face motivation constraints due to time limitations, which hinders their ability to allocate sufficient time and effort toward improving their writing abilities (Lee, 2017). The integration of technology in English language classrooms is widely recognized as a means to overcome certain obstacles in language learning processes (Roll and Wylie, 2016; Knox, 2020), particularly in writing tasks where time constraints often arise (Stapleton and Radia, 2010; Kessler, 2020; Rahimi and Fathi, 2022; Wang, 2022). With the ubiquitous availability of technology and online platforms, learners now have the convenience of practicing their language skills, specifically writing, at any time and from anywhere (Yan, 2023). This includes the use of advanced artificial intelligence (AI)-based computer and mobile programs, which offer interactive and personalized tools for honing writing abilities as well as enhanced motivation (Jiang,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"skills, specifically writing, at any time and from anywhere (Yan, 2023). This includes the use of advanced artificial intelligence (AI)-based computer and mobile programs, which offer interactive and personalized tools for honing writing abilities as well as enhanced motivation (Jiang, 2022; Meunier et al., 2022; Yan, 2023). The emergence of AI-powered writing tools, accessible on mobile devices, provides a novel avenue to address the challenges associated with developing writing proficiency through traditional training methods (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019; Jia et al., 2022; Kohnke, 2023). These AI-assisted writing tools offer automated feedback on various aspects of writing, including organization, coherence, grammar, and vocabulary, thereby facilitating more effective writing performance improvements. Additionally, learners can expedite their writing development as AI tools help them identify and rectify grammatical and lexical errors, while also suggesting alternative sentence structures to enhance the overall writing quality and structure (Zhao, 2022; Chen, 2023; Salvagno et al., 2023). Numerous research studies have explored the effects of AI on enhancing English language learning outcomes (Sun et al., 2021; Huang A. Y. et al., 2023). Huang X. et al. (2023),",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"and structure (Zhao, 2022; Chen, 2023; Salvagno et al., 2023). Numerous research studies have explored the effects of AI on enhancing English language learning outcomes (Sun et al., 2021; Huang A. Y. et al., 2023). Huang X. et al. (2023), for example, investigated the academic performance and level of engagement among students who participated in an AI course compared to those in a non-AI course. The findings revealed that students in the AI course exhibited superior academic achievement and demonstrated higher levels of active participation in their learning tasks compared to their counterparts in the non-AI course. Additionally, several studies have specifically focused on the impact of AI-assisted language learning tools in improving English language learners’ writing skills (Liu et al., 2021; Seufert et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2021; Fitria, 2023; Hsiao and Chang, 2023; Yan, 2023). For instance, Liu et al. (2021) examined the influence of AI-supported language learning on the writing skills of English as a Foreign Language (EFL) learners and found that the AI-supported approach had a significant positive impact on their writing abilities. Similarly, Yan",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"Liu et al. (2021) examined the influence of AI-supported language learning on the writing skills of English as a Foreign Language (EFL) learners and found that the AI-supported approach had a significant positive impact on their writing abilities. Similarly, Yan (2023) investigated the impact of ChatGPT, an AI-assisted language learning tool, on the writing skills of EFL learners and reported significant improvements in their writing performance as a result of AI-assisted language learning. Within the domain of English language learning, a conspicuous research gap emerges, one that delves into the intricate role of AI-assisted language learning tools in enhancing the writing proficiency and motivational drive of English language learners, notably within the unique context of EFL. This research gap assumes heightened significance in light of the pervasive challenges faced by EFL learners, often struggling to allocate sufficient time for refining their writing skills, consequently hampering their overall writing competence (Fathi and Rahimi, 2022). Hence, this study embarks on a journey to meticulously investigate the precise impact that AI-assisted language learning exerts on the writing prowess and motivational outlook of Chinese",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"their writing skills, consequently hampering their overall writing competence (Fathi and Rahimi, 2022). Hence, this study embarks on a journey to meticulously investigate the precise impact that AI-assisted language learning exerts on the writing prowess and motivational outlook of Chinese EFL learners. Furthermore, the research undertakes a comprehensive exploration of learners’ perceptions and assessments regarding the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning instruction, thus enhancing the depth and breadth of the research findings. The outcomes of this study bear paramount significance for both EFL educators and pedagogical methodologies alike. Notably, the findings furnish compelling evidence in favor of the integration of AI-assisted writing tools within EFL classrooms, illustrating a substantial augmentation in students’ proficiency in written expression. Furthermore, by endorsing collaborative writing endeavors facilitated by AI-powered tools, educators can cultivate and sustain students’ enthusiasm and vested interest in the writing process, thus nurturing their overall growth and proficiency in writing. 2 Literature review 2.1 Theoretical framework This study is underpinned by Vygotsky's (1984) social constructivism, which underscores the pivotal role of social interaction in the learning process. Vygotsky contends that learning",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"thus nurturing their overall growth and proficiency in writing. 2 Literature review 2.1 Theoretical framework This study is underpinned by Vygotsky's (1984) social constructivism, which underscores the pivotal role of social interaction in the learning process. Vygotsky contends that learning transpires through the dynamic interplay between individuals and their social milieu, especially in collaborative endeavors with more knowledgeable peers. He posits that cultural development manifests initially in social interactions, subsequently becoming internalized at an individual level. At the core of Vygotsky’s social constructivism lies the concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD), representing the space between a learner’s current level of independent problem- solving and their potential for further advancement through collaborative problem-solving with more capable peers. Engaging in collaborative language learning and seeking assistance from others enable learners to effectively reach their ZPD, showcasing their ability to work independently within the learning context as indicative of ZPD mastery. Kim (2008) elucidates that a group of learners can mutually support each other in reaching their ZPD, alternating roles as both less and more skilled learners contingent on the language",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,2,"independently within the learning context as indicative of ZPD mastery. Kim (2008) elucidates that a group of learners can mutually support each other in reaching their ZPD, alternating roles as both less and more skilled learners contingent on the language learning tasks and activities. In essence, through group or pair work in various language-learning tasks, learners amalgamate their diverse language- learning proficiencies and knowledge, thus assisting each other in achieving their ZPD (Oxford, 1997). While traditional interpretations of social constructivism emphasize human interactions, we extend this framework to probe into the potential of AI-assisted language learning in emulating social interactions and collaborative learning experiences. In the EFL education, students often derive substantial benefits from peer collaboration and social interactions in augmenting their language and writing skills (Fathi et al., 2020). Vygotsky’s theory underscores the significance of the ZPD, where learners can make strides with the guidance and collaboration of more knowledgeable peers. AI, exemplified by ChatGPT, holds the promise of fabricating an environment mirroring these collaborative interactions, by",168
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 03 frontiersin.org providing real-time feedback, suggestions, and support akin to peer assistance. By employing AI-assisted language learning tools, learners not only partake in social interactions but also engage in dynamic exchanges with AI, effectively treating it as a knowledgeable and adaptive virtual peer. While AI is conventionally viewed as an individualized learning tool, our inquiry delves into its potential to contribute to writing skill development and motivation by nurturing a sense of collaboration and social engagement among learners. This amalgamation of AI and social constructivism is vital as it enables an exploration of the potential synergy between technological advancements and entrenched learning theories. By harnessing the capabilities of AI, learners can receive immediate, personalized feedback, access a wealth of linguistic resources, and customize their learning journey to their specific needs and inclinations. This reciprocal interplay between human learners and AI amplifies the collaborative learning milieu, potentially enhancing the effectiveness of language acquisition and writing skill development. Importantly, feedback is recognized as a critical component in enhancing writing performance and motivation in the context",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"reciprocal interplay between human learners and AI amplifies the collaborative learning milieu, potentially enhancing the effectiveness of language acquisition and writing skill development. Importantly, feedback is recognized as a critical component in enhancing writing performance and motivation in the context of language learning (Bakla, 2020; Liu et al., 2022; Zhang and Zou, 2023). While Vygotsky’s theory provides a foundation for understanding collaborative learning, it is essential to acknowledge the substantial body of research that emphasizes the role of feedback in shaping writing proficiency and motivation. Feedback, whether from human instructors or AI systems, plays a central role in guiding learners toward improvement (Loncar et al., 2023; Zhang and Zou, 2023). In traditional approaches, teacher feedback has been a cornerstone of writing instruction, offering valuable insights into areas for improvement. Similarly, in AI-assisted learning, ChatGPT’s real-time feedback mechanisms provide learners with continuous guidance and suggestions for enhancing their writing skills. Recognizing the interplay between feedback and collaborative learning is crucial in assessing the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning. While Vygotsky’s theory is primarily associated with collaborative learning and scaffolding, it also",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"continuous guidance and suggestions for enhancing their writing skills. Recognizing the interplay between feedback and collaborative learning is crucial in assessing the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning. While Vygotsky’s theory is primarily associated with collaborative learning and scaffolding, it also holds relevance for understanding key constructs like engagement, self-regulation, and personalized learning within the context of AI-assisted language learning (Hadwin and Oshige, 2011; Schrader, 2015; Kucirkova and Littleton, 2017). Engagement, as a crucial aspect of the learning process, is influenced by the dynamic interaction between learners and their learning environment (Fredricks et al., 2016). In the context of AI-assisted instruction, engagement takes on a unique dimension. The interactive nature of AI tools, such as ChatGPT, allows learners to actively participate in the writing process, seeking immediate feedback and refining their writing skills in real-time. This heightened engagement, facilitated by the AI tool’s responsiveness, contributes to the observed improvements in writing skills and motivation (Liu et al., 2021; Utami and Winarni, 2023). Furthermore, Vygotsky’s constructivist perspective sheds light on the concept of self-regulation in the context of AI-assisted learning. Through collaborative",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"AI tool’s responsiveness, contributes to the observed improvements in writing skills and motivation (Liu et al., 2021; Utami and Winarni, 2023). Furthermore, Vygotsky’s constructivist perspective sheds light on the concept of self-regulation in the context of AI-assisted learning. Through collaborative writing activities with the AI, learners transition from being externally regulated to becoming self-regulated writers. The AI tool acts as a facilitator, guiding learners to internalize effective writing strategies and enabling them to complete tasks independently (Zimmerman, 2002). This transition to self-regulation is a critical component of the learning process and is essential for long-term skill development. Additionally, the personalized learning experience facilitated by AI tools aligns with the principles of Vygotsky’s social constructivism. Learners have the opportunity to work at their own pace and receive immediate, tailored feedback, allowing for a more individualized learning journey (Huang A. Y. et al., 2023; Huang X. et al., 2023). This aligns with Vygotsky’s idea that learning occurs most effectively when it is situated within a learner’s ZPD, the gap between their current level of competence and their potential for development with guidance.",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"al., 2023; Huang X. et al., 2023). This aligns with Vygotsky’s idea that learning occurs most effectively when it is situated within a learner’s ZPD, the gap between their current level of competence and their potential for development with guidance. In this sense, the AI tool functions as a scaffold, helping learners reach their ZPD and gradually internalize writing skills. This personalization contributes to the observed enhancements in writing skills and motivation (Fulton et al., 2021). In extending Vygotsky’s social constructivist framework, this study advances our comprehension of collaborative learning by scrutinizing the symbiotic relationship between human learners and AI-driven virtual peers. Through this innovative fusion, we aspire to unlock new vistas for ameliorating academic writing skills and motivation among EFL students. 2.2 Artificial intelligence AI can be defined as a system incorporating intelligent programs that collaborate with humans to perform various tasks (Aldosari, 2020). In educational settings, AI has the capability to make intelligent decisions akin to human decision-making (Akerkar, 2014). Applied linguistics researchers have recognized the potential of AI in language learning and teaching contexts, aiming to enhance",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"perform various tasks (Aldosari, 2020). In educational settings, AI has the capability to make intelligent decisions akin to human decision-making (Akerkar, 2014). Applied linguistics researchers have recognized the potential of AI in language learning and teaching contexts, aiming to enhance teaching methodologies for language instructors and facilitate language learners’ language acquisition (Luckin et al., 2016; Zhang and Zou, 2020; Nazari et al., 2021; Sun et al., 2021; Xia et al., 2022). AI-assisted online platforms can be employed to generate the necessary language input and output, aiding language learners in their language development. These AI tools, accessible on computer and mobile devices, particularly support the enhancement of writing skills. One noteworthy AI-powered tool is ChatGPT, an AI-assisted Chatbot created by OpenAI (Barrot, 2023). ChatGPT can be effectively utilized in diverse language learning courses to enhance learners’ writing abilities (Barrot, 2023). Equipped with comprehensive knowledge, ChatGPT generates words and grammatically correct structures to facilitate the creation of coherent and cohesive written text. This tool comprehends human queries and provides appropriate responses. Moreover, ChatGPT assists language learners in addressing writing challenges related to",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,3,"with comprehensive knowledge, ChatGPT generates words and grammatically correct structures to facilitate the creation of coherent and cohesive written text. This tool comprehends human queries and provides appropriate responses. Moreover, ChatGPT assists language learners in addressing writing challenges related to organization, coherence, grammar, and vocabulary. It offers alternative suggestions to rectify ungrammatical sentences and improve overall writing proficiency. ChatGPT has been recognized for its potential in enhancing writing performance (Huang and Tan, 2023). The AI-powered tool facilitates the production of coherent and cohesive text by providing learners with immediate feedback and alternative grammatically correct sentences (Huang and Tan, 2023). However, it is important to consider certain limitations when using ChatGPT for different writing tasks. Frequent reliance on generated text from ChatGPT may hinder language learners’ own writing abilities. Additionally, using the generated text without appropriate review and editing may lead to issues of plagiarism that should be carefully addressed (Huang and Tan, 2023). Numerous studies have investigated the positive impact of AI-assisted language learning tools on English language learners’",169
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 04 frontiersin.org language acquisition skills (Suryana et al., 2020; Divekar et al., 2021; Liu, 2021; Bašić et al., 2023; Bishop, 2023; Fitria, 2023). For instance, Rahman et al. (2022) examined the role of an AI-assisted language learning tool in identifying and addressing grammatical errors, leading to the development of writing skills among EFL learners. The results indicated significant improvement in the EFL learners’ writing skills, and the learners themselves expressed positive perceptions regarding the effects of AI-assisted language learning on their writing abilities. Utami and Winarni (2023) conducted a case study research on three Indonesian EFL learners, exploring their use of AI-assisted language learning for academic research writing. Through a combination of quantitative data collected via questionnaires and qualitative data obtained through interviews, the findings revealed that AI-assisted language learning tools positively contributed to the learners’ academic research writing and increased their engagement in such tasks. Similarly, Seufert et al. (2021) investigated the effects of instructor and peer feedback integrated with intelligent tutoring systems feedback on the academic writing skills of EFL students.",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"to the learners’ academic research writing and increased their engagement in such tasks. Similarly, Seufert et al. (2021) investigated the effects of instructor and peer feedback integrated with intelligent tutoring systems feedback on the academic writing skills of EFL students. The intelligent tutoring system’s feedback, provided within an AI-assisted language learning environment, had a significant impact on the development of students’ academic writing skills. Hwang et al. (2023) conducted an experimental study with a control group to explore the effects of an AI-based writing feedback tool on undergraduate EFL students’ writing performance. The results demonstrated that the experimental group, utilizing the AI-assisted tool, outperformed the control group in writing tasks. The personalization feature of the AI tool played a crucial role in facilitating learners’ revision and editing of their writing assignments. Fitria (2021) conducted a study that investigated the effectiveness of Grammarly as an AI-assisted language learning tool in enhancing the writing performance of EFL learners. The learners received corrective feedback within the AI-powered environment to revise and improve their written texts. The findings revealed a significant contribution of the",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"of Grammarly as an AI-assisted language learning tool in enhancing the writing performance of EFL learners. The learners received corrective feedback within the AI-powered environment to revise and improve their written texts. The findings revealed a significant contribution of the AI-assisted language learning tool to the improvement of learners’ writing skills. Similarly, Chang et al. (2021) employed a quasi-experimental research design to examine the impact of an AI-supported writing feedback tool on EFL learners’ writing performance. The experimental group utilized Grammarly for editing and revising their written texts, while the control group did not have access to Grammarly. The results demonstrated that the experimental group outperformed the control group in writing skills, highlighting the significant role of this AI-powered language learning tool in developing EFL learners’ writing performance. Gayed et al. (2022) developed an AI-assisted language learning program to address cognitive barriers and improve the writing performance of EFL students. The findings indicated that the AI-powered language learning tool effectively enhanced students’ writing performance and reduced cognitive barriers encountered during writing tasks. In a study by Yan (2023), the contribution",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"cognitive barriers and improve the writing performance of EFL students. The findings indicated that the AI-powered language learning tool effectively enhanced students’ writing performance and reduced cognitive barriers encountered during writing tasks. In a study by Yan (2023), the contribution of ChatGPT as an AI-powered language learning tool to EFL learners’ English writing was explored. The study examined learners’ reactions and reflections on the use of the AI-assisted tool to develop their academic writing performance. The results indicated a significant role of the AI tool in improving learners’ writing performance and enhancing their efficiency in completing writing tasks. However, learners expressed concerns about potential negative effects on their academic writing skills over time and emphasized the need for instruction on the appropriate application of the tool in their academic writing tasks. Su et al. (2023) asserted that the integration of ChatGPT in language learners’ writing courses proved beneficial for accomplishing argumentative writing tasks. They emphasized the linguistic and structural complexities involved in such tasks, making it challenging for peers or instructors to provide effective feedback in interactive writing activities. To",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"ChatGPT in language learners’ writing courses proved beneficial for accomplishing argumentative writing tasks. They emphasized the linguistic and structural complexities involved in such tasks, making it challenging for peers or instructors to provide effective feedback in interactive writing activities. To address this, ChatGPT was introduced to provide more efficient feedback and comments on language usage, organization, and content, significantly enhancing learners’ argumentative writing tasks. Similarly, Ippolito et al. (2022) utilized Wordcraft, an AI-powered text editor, to support professional writers in creative writing tasks, particularly in brainstorming. The results indicated the positive impact of the AI-powered tool on creative writing. Abdullayeva and Musayeva (2023) examined the influence of ChatGPT on EFL learners’ writing skills and found that it contributed by providing writing prompts, immediate feedback, and revision suggestions. Nazari et al. (2021) conducted a true experimental study investigating the effects of AI-assisted language learning on EFL learners’ writing performance. The findings revealed that learners who utilized the AI-powered tool outperformed those who did not in terms of writing performance. Additionally, the AI learners displayed high engagement behaviorally, cognitively, and emotionally in",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"AI-assisted language learning on EFL learners’ writing performance. The findings revealed that learners who utilized the AI-powered tool outperformed those who did not in terms of writing performance. Additionally, the AI learners displayed high engagement behaviorally, cognitively, and emotionally in AI-supported writing activities. In a quasi-experimental research design, Liu et al. (2021) explored the impact of AI on EFL learners’ writing skills. The findings indicated substantial improvements in writing skills compared to the conventional class. The AI-supported language learning approach also enhanced learners’ self-efficacy, self-regulated learning, and reduced cognitive load, contributing to their effective writing performance. Taken together, in reviewing the extensive body of literature on AI-assisted language learning, numerous studies have made valuable contributions to our understanding of its impact on language acquisition and the broader field of language learning (e.g., Suryana et al., 2020; Divekar et al., 2021; Liu, 2021; Bašić et al., 2023; Bishop, 2023; Fitria, 2023). These studies have provided critical insights into various aspects of language learning, including vocabulary acquisition, grammar correction, and language proficiency improvements. However, as we delve deeper into the literature, it",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"Liu, 2021; Bašić et al., 2023; Bishop, 2023; Fitria, 2023). These studies have provided critical insights into various aspects of language learning, including vocabulary acquisition, grammar correction, and language proficiency improvements. However, as we delve deeper into the literature, it becomes evident that a significant gap persists in terms of the specific influence of AI on the development of academic writing skills and writing motivation in EFL learners. While a select few studies, such as those by Rahman et al. (2022) and Fitria (2021), have ventured into the realm of writing skills, the scope has remained limited to the correction of grammatical errors and broader language proficiency (e.g., Liu et al., 2021; Gayed et al., 2022). These studies, though informative, have not comprehensively addressed the nuances of academic writing or the intricate factors that contribute to writing motivation. Moreover, there is an absence of research that holistically investigates the combined impact of AI tools on both academic writing skills and motivation. This study seeks to address this significant gap by embarking on a more focused examination of AI-assisted language learning,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,4,"is an absence of research that holistically investigates the combined impact of AI tools on both academic writing skills and motivation. This study seeks to address this significant gap by embarking on a more focused examination of AI-assisted language learning, with a particular emphasis on academic writing skills and writing motivation. While our predecessors have laid a foundation for AI’s role in writing skill development, we aim to extend this foundation by delving into the intricate processes that underlie academic writing and the dynamics of motivation in the EFL context. Through this research, we",94
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 05 frontiersin.org aspire to provide a more in-depth understanding of the multifaceted role of AI in the realm of academic writing, an area that has been insufficiently explored in the existing literature. Our research not only evaluates the quantitative impact but also ventures into the qualitative aspects of EFL learners’ interactions with AI in academic writing activities. By doing so, we aim to uncover the subtleties and complexities that underlie learners’ perceptions, attitudes, and experiences, contributing to a comprehensive picture of the influence of AI on both academic writing skills and motivation. The insights garnered from this study have the potential to significantly inform and enrich AI-assisted language learning practices, particularly in the context of EFL education, where tailored solutions are essential for addressing the unique challenges faced by learners, such as varying language proficiency levels, time constraints, and motivation fluctuations. 2.3 Purpose of the study The existing literature review underscores the substantial impact of AI-assisted language learning tools on improving various facets of writing skills and engagement among English language learners (Nazari",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"language proficiency levels, time constraints, and motivation fluctuations. 2.3 Purpose of the study The existing literature review underscores the substantial impact of AI-assisted language learning tools on improving various facets of writing skills and engagement among English language learners (Nazari et al., 2021; Abdullayeva and Musayeva, 2023; Su et al., 2023; Utami and Winarni, 2023; Yan, 2023). This body of research highlights the positive perceptions of English language learners regarding the integration of AI tools into writing activities (Rahman et al., 2022; Utami and Winarni, 2023). While these findings have significantly contributed to our understanding of the potential benefits of AI in language learning, several factors underscore the need for a more focused investigation. First, despite the increasing adoption of AI in language learning, there is a distinct gap in research that delves into the specific effects of AI-assisted language learning on academic writing skills and writing motivation. Academic writing is a critical skill for EFL learners, and understanding how AI tools can enhance this skill is of paramount importance for both educators and learners. Second, within the context of",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"learning on academic writing skills and writing motivation. Academic writing is a critical skill for EFL learners, and understanding how AI tools can enhance this skill is of paramount importance for both educators and learners. Second, within the context of EFL education, where learners often face unique challenges related to language proficiency, time constraints, and motivation, there is a need for tailored solutions. This study aims to fill this void by exploring the impact of AI-assisted language learning tools specifically within the EFL context, providing insights that can inform pedagogical practices and curriculum design. Third, while existing studies have highlighted the positive perceptions of learners regarding AI tools, a more nuanced understanding of their perceptions, attitudes, and experiences is essential. This research not only examines the quantitative impact but also delves into the qualitative aspects of EFL learners’ interactions with AI in academic writing activities. In light of these factors and the evolving landscape of language education, this study seeks to address this multifaceted knowledge gap by thoroughly examining how AI-assisted language learning influences the academic writing skills, writing motivation,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"AI in academic writing activities. In light of these factors and the evolving landscape of language education, this study seeks to address this multifaceted knowledge gap by thoroughly examining how AI-assisted language learning influences the academic writing skills, writing motivation, and perceptions of EFL learners. By doing so, it aims to provide valuable insights into the role of AI in improving language proficiency and pedagogical practices within the context of EFL education. To address these research objectives, the following research questions were formulated: 1. To what extent does AI-assisted language learning instruction contribute to the enhancement of academic writing skills in EFL learners compared to non-AI-assisted instruction? 2. To what extent does AI-assisted language learning instruction contribute to the enhancement of writing motivation in EFL learners compared to non-AI-assisted instruction? 3. What are the perceptions of EFL learners toward the impact of AI-assisted language learning on their academic writing skills and writing motivation? 3 Method 3.1 Participants The study included 50 Chinese EFL students who were enrolled in a Bachelor’s degree program at a national university in China. Participants were",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"impact of AI-assisted language learning on their academic writing skills and writing motivation? 3 Method 3.1 Participants The study included 50 Chinese EFL students who were enrolled in a Bachelor’s degree program at a national university in China. Participants were recruited through campus bulletin board announcements and email invitations sent to eligible individuals who met specific inclusion criteria. To be included in the study, participants had to be enrolled in the university’s English language program and have at least two years of prior English language instruction. They could not have previously participated in any AI-assisted language learning programs. Prior to their involvement, participants provided written informed consent after receiving detailed explanations of the study’s purpose, procedures, potential risks, and benefits. They were assured of their right to withdraw from the study at any time without penalty. A multistage sampling procedure was employed to ensure a representative sample. The university’s English language program department generated a list of eligible participants, from which a random sample of students was selected using a computer-generated random number method. The selected students were then invited",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"was employed to ensure a representative sample. The university’s English language program department generated a list of eligible participants, from which a random sample of students was selected using a computer-generated random number method. The selected students were then invited to participate in the study. Random assignment was used to assign participants to either the experimental group or the control group. This randomization process aimed to minimize bias and ensure comparability between the groups, giving each participant an equal chance of being assigned to either group. The study was conducted during the regular academic year at the university, utilizing the language laboratories and computer facilities provided by the institution. Participants’ regular English language classes were unaffected, ensuring they received the standard curriculum and instruction during the study period. To establish a comparable proficiency level, an English proficiency test was administered as part of the screening process. This comprehensive test assessed participants’ reading, writing, listening, and speaking skills, confirming their similar level of English proficiency confirming their intermediate to upper- intermediate level of English proficiency. The participants came from diverse backgrounds,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,5,"as part of the screening process. This comprehensive test assessed participants’ reading, writing, listening, and speaking skills, confirming their similar level of English proficiency confirming their intermediate to upper- intermediate level of English proficiency. The participants came from diverse backgrounds, representing various regions of China. On average, they had been studying English as a foreign language for five years. Their ages ranged from 18 to 22 years old, with the majority being in their second or third year of undergraduate studies. Participants pursued majors in disciplines such as engineering, business, social sciences, and humanities. Throughout the study, confidentiality and anonymization measures were in place to protect participants’ privacy. They were informed about these procedures and the steps taken to ensure the security of their data. Over a three-month period, both the experimental and control groups were taught by a male experienced teacher who used the same instructional materials for both groups.",151
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 06 frontiersin.org 3.2 Instruments 3.2.1 Writing skills assessment The study utilized the International English Language Testing System (IELTS) academic writing tasks 1 and 2 as pre-tests and post- tests to evaluate participants’ academic writing skills. The selection of writing tasks was based on reputable resources commonly employed in IELTS preparation. These resources were chosen to ensure alignment with the instructional content provided to both groups. The participants’ writing proficiency was assessed using the IELTS writing band descriptors for task 1 and task 2 (see Appendix A), which encompassed criteria such as task achievement, coherence and cohesion, lexicon, and grammatical range and accuracy (University of Cambridge ESOL Examinations, 2011). To enhance objectivity in the scoring process, two independent raters evaluated the writing samples for the pre-tests and post-tests. The raters consisted of the researcher/instructor (second author) and an experienced IELTS instructor who had received official training at an IELTS training center. Both raters possessed the necessary expertise to accurately assess the participants’ academic writing skills. Inter-rater reliability between the two raters was assessed, demonstrating",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"(second author) and an experienced IELTS instructor who had received official training at an IELTS training center. Both raters possessed the necessary expertise to accurately assess the participants’ academic writing skills. Inter-rater reliability between the two raters was assessed, demonstrating a high level of agreement with a correlation coefficient of 0.88. 3.2.2 Writing motivation scale questionnaire In order to examine any changes in participants’ motivation following the implementation of the AI-assisted instruction, the study utilized the Writing Motivation Scale (WMS) which is a well- established tool in social science research for gathering data on various social aspects, behaviors, attitudes, and underlying reasons for actions (Fathi et al., 2023). The adapted version of the WMS used in this study (see Appendix B) was based on the original scale developed by Waller and Papi (2017). The questionnaire consisted of seven items that assessed students’ intended efforts for learning the second/foreign language, motivation to learn the second/foreign language, motivational intensity, teacher feedback, content and organization of the course, and peer feedback. Participants rated each item on a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"assessed students’ intended efforts for learning the second/foreign language, motivation to learn the second/foreign language, motivational intensity, teacher feedback, content and organization of the course, and peer feedback. Participants rated each item on a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1 (never) to 5 (always). The internal consistency of the scale was evaluated using Cronbach’s Alpha and was found to be satisfactory in this study (α = 0.86). 3.2.3 Semi-structured interview Semi-structured interviews (see Appendix C) were conducted with nine EFL students from the experimental classroom group to gain in-depth insights into the AI-assisted instruction. The interviews aimed to capture diverse perspectives by including participants of different genders and writing performance levels (low, mid, and high performers). The interviews were conducted in the participants’ native language to ensure clear expression of ideas and attitudes toward the course. Pseudonyms (S1, S2, etc.) were used to maintain confidentiality and adhere to ethical guidelines. Each interview lasted approximately 30 min and began with rapport-building questions. Participants were then encouraged to discuss their attitudes and perceptions toward the AI-assisted instruction, compare it to conventional instruction,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"were used to maintain confidentiality and adhere to ethical guidelines. Each interview lasted approximately 30 min and began with rapport-building questions. Participants were then encouraged to discuss their attitudes and perceptions toward the AI-assisted instruction, compare it to conventional instruction, and identify any advantages, disadvantages, and challenges encountered. Audio recordings of the interviews were transcribed and translated into English for analysis. To ensure credibility, a member-checking technique was employed, where participants reviewed and verified the transcriptions. Data analysis involved multiple readings of the transcripts to identify key ideas and themes. 3.3 Procedure 3.3.1 Experimental group The experimental group in this study received AI-assisted writing instruction using ChatGPT, an advanced language model. Participants in this group accessed a web-based interface specifically designed for the study and were instructed on how to effectively interact with ChatGPT to enhance their writing skills. These students had the flexibility to use ChatGPT both at home and in the classroom. They were encouraged to engage with the AI-assisted writing tool at their convenience, allowing for a personalized learning experience tailored to their schedules and preferences. This",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"students had the flexibility to use ChatGPT both at home and in the classroom. They were encouraged to engage with the AI-assisted writing tool at their convenience, allowing for a personalized learning experience tailored to their schedules and preferences. This approach ensured that participants had regular exposure to AI assistance throughout the 12-week intervention period. During the intervention, participants logged into the platform and selected a combination of classroom exercises and topics of interest to them. As they wrote their responses, ChatGPT provided real-time feedback on grammar, vocabulary usage, sentence structure, coherence, and organization. The AI model, trained on extensive language data, identified errors, offered suggestions for improvement, and provided contextualized recommendations to enhance their writing skills. ChatGPT engaged participants in a conversational manner, allowing them to ask questions, seek clarification, or request further examples and explanations. The aim was to create an interactive and personalized learning experience that adapted to the individual needs and writing styles of the participants. ChatGPT also offered writing suggestions, alternative phrasing options, and vocabulary expansion ideas to enhance expressiveness and language fluency. The AI-powered",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"to create an interactive and personalized learning experience that adapted to the individual needs and writing styles of the participants. ChatGPT also offered writing suggestions, alternative phrasing options, and vocabulary expansion ideas to enhance expressiveness and language fluency. The AI-powered platform included features to track participants’ progress, such as a writing portfolio where completed tasks could be stored and reviewed. Participants were encouraged to reflect on their strengths and weaknesses, incorporate the feedback provided by ChatGPT into their writing revisions, and use the platform’s tools to track their progress. The AI-assisted writing instruction sessions using ChatGPT were conducted twice a week over a period of 12 weeks. Each session lasted for approximately 60 min. To mitigate plagiarism risks, stringent measures were adopted. The participants received comprehensive guidance on utilizing ChatGPT as a writing aid, emphasizing the creation of original content over reliance on AI-generated material. Engaging in interactive sessions, discussions, and illustrative examples, participants adeptly incorporated AI suggestions while ensuring the authenticity of their work. A considerable emphasis was placed on the ethical use of AI in academic writing, guiding",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,6,"over reliance on AI-generated material. Engaging in interactive sessions, discussions, and illustrative examples, participants adeptly incorporated AI suggestions while ensuring the authenticity of their work. A considerable emphasis was placed on the ethical use of AI in academic writing, guiding participants on seamlessly integrating AI feedback while preserving their distinctive writing style and thoughts. These measures were designed to empower participants in leveraging AI support while upholding academic integrity. Furthermore, regular interactive discussions actively reinforced these ethical considerations and addressed participant queries throughout the intervention.",85
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 07 frontiersin.org 3.3.2 Control group The control group, on the other hand, received traditional writing instruction from an experienced male teacher who used the same instructional materials as the experimental group. Participants attended in-person writing classes led by the teacher. During the three-month intervention period, control group participants engaged in writing exercises and activities that included a combination of classroom exercises and topics of interest, similar to the experimental group. These activities focused on various aspects of writing, such as grammar, vocabulary, organization, coherence, and sentence structure. The teacher provided individualized feedback on writing assignments, highlighting areas for improvement and offering suggestions for enhancement. Control group participants received feedback exclusively in the classroom during the teacher-led writing classes, which were held regularly, ensuring that participants received ongoing guidance and feedback throughout the 12-week intervention. The amount of time devoted to outside-class practice was designed to be comparable to that of the experimental group. Unlike the experimental group, the control group did not receive AI-assisted feedback from ChatGPT. Instead, their feedback was based on",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"intervention. The amount of time devoted to outside-class practice was designed to be comparable to that of the experimental group. Unlike the experimental group, the control group did not receive AI-assisted feedback from ChatGPT. Instead, their feedback was based on the teacher’s expertise and teaching experience. The teacher emphasized the importance of practice, guided participants on effective writing strategies, and offered constructive criticism to help develop their writing skills. Throughout the intervention period, control group participants attended regular writing classes, completed writing assignments, and received feedback from the teacher. They were encouraged to reflect on their writing progress and make necessary revisions based on the feedback provided by the teacher. The instruction sessions of both groups were held in a dedicated computer laboratory equipped with the necessary technology and internet access. This setting ensured a controlled and consistent environment for both the experimental and control groups. Participants from both groups attended these sessions at the same location, ensuring equitable access to resources. It is also worth noting that the equivalence of time spent on out-of-class practice between the experimental and",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"both the experimental and control groups. Participants from both groups attended these sessions at the same location, ensuring equitable access to resources. It is also worth noting that the equivalence of time spent on out-of-class practice between the experimental and control groups was meticulously maintained through the implementation of a time log system. In this system, participants in both groups were tasked with maintaining detailed records of their writing practice beyond their scheduled class sessions. These comprehensive time logs captured essential information, including the duration of each writing session, the specific activities undertaken, and whether the participants utilized ChatGPT (experimental group) or engaged in traditional writing assignments (control group). The systematic scrutiny of these time logs served as a robust mechanism to affirm the comparability of out-of-class writing practice time between the two groups. This rigorous monitoring and verification process unequivocally ensured that both groups had equitable opportunities for practice and improvement. Table 1 indicates the summary of the intervention details for both groups. 3.4 Data analysis In our data analysis, we employed a one-way analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) as",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"unequivocally ensured that both groups had equitable opportunities for practice and improvement. Table 1 indicates the summary of the intervention details for both groups. 3.4 Data analysis In our data analysis, we employed a one-way analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) as the primary method, following the guidance of Pallant (2013). ANCOVA was chosen to assess the impact of two distinct interventions by measuring participants’ outcomes before and after exposure to each intervention, a common approach in pre-test and post-test designs. The independent variable under consideration was the type of intervention, distinguishing between the experimental and control classroom settings. Our dependent variables encompassed global writing performance and specific writing skills, including writing content, organization, and language use, as well as writing motivation. To enhance the rigor of our analysis and control for any potential initial group differences, we included covariates based on pre-test scores. Additionally, we conducted independent samples t-tests to provide supplementary insights into specific outcome measures, aiming to offer a comprehensive perspective on the effectiveness of the intervention. Furthermore, effect sizes were calculated to quantify the magnitude of observed differences,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"pre-test scores. Additionally, we conducted independent samples t-tests to provide supplementary insights into specific outcome measures, aiming to offer a comprehensive perspective on the effectiveness of the intervention. Furthermore, effect sizes were calculated to quantify the magnitude of observed differences, providing valuable information about the practical significance of our findings. This dual-method approach contributes to a well-rounded and thorough understanding of the intervention’s impact, allowing us to draw more robust conclusions. Thematic analysis, following the framework outlined by Boyatzis (1998), was employed to scrutinize the qualitative data derived from semi-structured interviews. This rigorous analytical process ensured a systematic exploration of participants’ perspectives on AI-assisted instruction. Initially, the transcribed interviews underwent open thematic coding. This involved the identification of key variables associated with participants’ experiences and perceptions of engaging with AI-assisted writing instruction. Subsequently, axial coding was implemented to discern intricate relationships among these core variables. This method facilitated the formation of thematic clusters, each encapsulating shared patterns and insights. The analytical approach adopted in this study was inherently inductive and data-driven, allowing for the emergence of meaningful themes without imposing",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"relationships among these core variables. This method facilitated the formation of thematic clusters, each encapsulating shared patterns and insights. The analytical approach adopted in this study was inherently inductive and data-driven, allowing for the emergence of meaningful themes without imposing preconceived notions. To bolster the rigor of the analysis, inter-rater reliability was applied. An experienced EFL TABLE 1 Summary of the intervention details. Aspect Experimental group (AI-assisted) Control group (Traditional) Duration 12 weeks 12 weeks Learning environment Flexible (home and classroom) In-person classroom Writing tasks Classroom exercises + topics of interest Classroom exercises + topics of interest Feedback AI-powered (ChatGPT) Teacher Focus areas Grammar, vocabulary, organization, coherence, sentence structure Grammar, vocabulary, organization, coherence, sentence structure Interactive learning Yes (with ChatGPT) Yes (teacher-led classes) Progress tracking Yes (writing portfolio) Yes (writing portfolio) Following the completion of the 12-week intervention period, all participants, both in the control and experimental groups, underwent a post-test. In line with our commitment to maintaining the comparability of assessments, the post-test featured the same writing tasks, Task 1 and Task 2, extracted from the IELTS writing assessment.",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,7,"participants, both in the control and experimental groups, underwent a post-test. In line with our commitment to maintaining the comparability of assessments, the post-test featured the same writing tasks, Task 1 and Task 2, extracted from the IELTS writing assessment. Participants were tasked with responding to these writing prompts to gage their progress in academic writing skills and motivation over the course of the study.",65
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 08 frontiersin.org researcher, proficient in coding and labeling, collaborated in the validation of the processes. This collaborative effort not only enhanced objectivity but also served to mitigate potential bias. Throughout this iterative process, adjustments were made to the codification, categorization, and labeling to ensure the robustness and coherence of the identified themes. This comprehensive data analysis framework facilitated a nuanced exploration of participants’ perceptions, contributing valuable insights to the overall findings of this study. 4 Results 4.1 Quantitative results Initially, an examination was conducted on the descriptive statistics of the participants’ pre-test and post-test results in overall writing, writing proficiency, and writing motivation across both groups. The summarized findings are presented in Table 2. Table 1 presents descriptive statistics for the pre-and post-test scores in both the experimental and control groups. The mean pre-test score for overall writing was 39.26 (SD = 12.03) in the experimental group and 37.31 (SD = 17.26) in the control group. Following the intervention, the mean post-test score increased to 59.12 (SD = 14.23) in the experimental group",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"pre-test score for overall writing was 39.26 (SD = 12.03) in the experimental group and 37.31 (SD = 17.26) in the control group. Following the intervention, the mean post-test score increased to 59.12 (SD = 14.23) in the experimental group and 45.18 (SD = 15.62) in the control group. For writing content, the experimental group had a mean pre-test score of 13.39 (SD = 3.62) and a mean post-test score of 15.96 (SD = 3.71). In comparison, the control group had a mean pre-test score of 13.26 (SD = 3.24) and a mean post-test score of 13.71 (SD = 3.12). In terms of writing organization, the experimental group had a mean pre-test score of 12.01 (SD = 3.71) and a mean post-test score of 16.56 (SD = 3.54), while the control group had a mean pre-test score of 12.34 (SD = 3.60) and a mean post-test score of 13.63 (SD = 3.63). Regarding language use, the experimental group had a mean pre-test score of 13.63 (SD = 3.49) and a mean post-test score of 19.89 (SD = 4.82). On the",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"= 3.60) and a mean post-test score of 13.63 (SD = 3.63). Regarding language use, the experimental group had a mean pre-test score of 13.63 (SD = 3.49) and a mean post-test score of 19.89 (SD = 4.82). On the other hand, the control group had a mean pre-test score of 13.51 (SD = 3.81) and a mean post-test score of 15.89 (SD = 4.12). In terms of writing motivation, the experimental group had a mean pre-test score of 17.36 (SD = 3.12) and a mean post-test score of 20.06 (SD = 3.33), while the control group had a mean pre-test score of 17.71 (SD = 3.09) and a mean post-test score of 18.21 (SD = 3.58). The results indicate that the pattern of increase in scores was higher for the experimental group compared to the control group. The first research question aimed to investigate the impact of AI-assisted instruction on EFL global writing performance. To address this question, we first conducted independent samples t-tests to compare the differences between the experimental and control groups in various writing proficiency components.",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"research question aimed to investigate the impact of AI-assisted instruction on EFL global writing performance. To address this question, we first conducted independent samples t-tests to compare the differences between the experimental and control groups in various writing proficiency components. Additionally, effect sizes were computed to gage the magnitude of these differences. In overall writing proficiency, a significant difference emerged between the experimental (M = 59.12, SD = 14.23) and control groups (M = 45.18, SD = 15.62), t(48) = 3.79, p < 0.001, with a large effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.76). For writing content, the experimental group (M = 15.96, SD = 3.71) outperformed the control group (M = 13.71, SD = 3.12), t(48) = 3.01, p = 0.003, showing a medium to large effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.65). In writing organization, the experimental group (M = 16.56, SD = 3.54) significantly surpassed the control group (M = 13.63, SD = 3.63), t(48) = 4.12, p < 0.001, with a large effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.84). Regarding language use, the experimental group (M = 19.89, SD",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"16.56, SD = 3.54) significantly surpassed the control group (M = 13.63, SD = 3.63), t(48) = 4.12, p < 0.001, with a large effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.84). Regarding language use, the experimental group (M = 19.89, SD = 4.82) demonstrated a substantial advantage over the control group (M = 15.89, SD = 4.12), t(48) = 4.34, p < 0.001, with a large effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.88). In terms of writing motivation, the experimental group (M = 20.06, SD = 3.33) exhibited significantly higher scores compared to the control group (M = 18.21, SD = 3.58), t(48) = 3.34, p = 0.001, with a medium effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.52). The independent samples t-tests revealed significant differences between the experimental and control groups across various writing proficiency dimensions, with effect sizes highlighting the practical significance of these distinctions. After that, a one-way ANCOVA was conducted, examining the differences between the experimental and control classrooms and their effects on the post-test scores of students’ writing tests while controlling for the pre-test scores as a covariate in",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"of these distinctions. After that, a one-way ANCOVA was conducted, examining the differences between the experimental and control classrooms and their effects on the post-test scores of students’ writing tests while controlling for the pre-test scores as a covariate in the analysis. Before conducting the ANCOVA, preliminary assessments were carried out to verify the fulfillment of key assumptions. These included examining normal distribution, linearity, equality of variances, homogeneity of regression slopes, and the reliability of covariate measurement. The results of the ANCOVA for the two groups’ global writing performance are presented in Table 3. After controlling for the pre-test scores of writing, statistically significant differences were found between the post-test scores of global writing in the two groups [F (1, 47) = 19.01, p = 0.00, partial eta squared = 0.27]. The ANCOVA results in Table 3 indicate significant disparities in the improvement of EFL students’ global writing between the experimental and control classrooms. Furthermore, the experimental classroom exhibited higher levels of global EFL writing compared to the conventional classroom. To further explore the development of EFL students’ writing skills,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,8,"in the improvement of EFL students’ global writing between the experimental and control classrooms. Furthermore, the experimental classroom exhibited higher levels of global EFL writing compared to the conventional classroom. To further explore the development of EFL students’ writing skills, three additional ANCOVAs were conducted to examine the differences between the experimental and control classrooms in writing content, writing organization, and language use. The TABLE 2 Descriptive statistics for pre-and post-tests scores. Pre-test Post-test Groups M SD M SD Overall writing Experimental 39.26 12.03 59.12 14.23 Control 37.31 17.26 45.18 15.62 Content Experimental 13.39 3.62 15.96 3.71 Control 13.26 3.24 13.71 3.12 Organization Experimental 12.01 3.71 16.56 3.54 Control 12.34 3.60 13.63 3.63 Language use Experimental 13.63 3.49 19.89 4.82 Control 13.51 3.81 15.89 4.12 Writing motivation Experimental 17.36 3.12 20.06 3.33 Control 17.71 3.09 18.21 3.58 TABLE 3 ANCOVA results for global writing. Source Type III sum of squares df Mean square F Sig. Partial eta squared Group 1237.16 1 1237.16 19.01 0.00 0.27",165
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 09 frontiersin.org ANCOVA results for the two groups’ writing content are presented in Table 4. According to the findings presented in Table 4, there were statistically significant differences observed between the two groups in terms of writing content [F(1, 47) = 12.61, p = 0.00, partial eta squared = 0.19]. These results indicate that the experimental classroom significantly outperformed the control classroom in developing EFL students’ writing content. Also, the ANCOVA results for the two groups’ writing organization are displayed in Table 4. Significant disparities between the two groups in terms of writing organization were observed, as indicated by the results presented in Table 5 [F(1, 47) = 23.16, p = 0.00, partial eta squared = 0.34]. These findings confirm significant differences between the experimental and control classrooms in enhancing the writing organization skills of the EFL students. Furthermore, the ANCOVA results for the two groups’ language use are depicted in Table 6. Table 5 reveals significant distinctions between the two groups in terms of language use [F(1, 47) = 9.86, p =",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"skills of the EFL students. Furthermore, the ANCOVA results for the two groups’ language use are depicted in Table 6. Table 5 reveals significant distinctions between the two groups in terms of language use [F(1, 47) = 9.86, p = 0.01, partial eta squared = 0.11]. These findings provide further evidence of significant difference between the experimental and control classrooms in enhancing the language use skills of the EFL students. The second research question aimed to investigate the impact of the experimental and conventional writing courses on the EFL students’ writing motivation. To examine this, a one-way ANCOVA was employed to compare the post-test scores of writing motivation between the AI-assisted and conventional classrooms. The ANCOVA results for the two groups’ writing motivation are presented in Table 7. Significant differences were observed in writing motivation between the two groups, as presented in Table 7 [F(1, 47) = 29.62, p = 0.00, partial eta squared = 0.38]. Additionally, it is noteworthy that the experimental classroom exhibited higher levels of EFL writing motivation compared to the conventional classroom. 4.2 Qualitative results As",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"presented in Table 7 [F(1, 47) = 29.62, p = 0.00, partial eta squared = 0.38]. Additionally, it is noteworthy that the experimental classroom exhibited higher levels of EFL writing motivation compared to the conventional classroom. 4.2 Qualitative results As for the qualitative phase and to answer the third research question, the thematic analysis of the semi-structured interviews yielded the following themes, providing valuable insights into participants’ experiences, perceptions, and attitudes toward AI-assisted writing instruction. 4.2.1 Theme 1: perceptions of innovation and assistance Participants expressed positive perceptions of AI-assisted writing instruction. One participant (S1) stated, “I found the AI-assisted writing instruction to be quite innovative and helpful. Having ChatGPT provide real-time feedback on my writing was valuable. It felt like having a personal writing tutor available whenever I needed assistance.” Another participant (S3) mentioned, “At first, I was unsure about using AI for writing instruction. However, as I started using ChatGPT, I realized its benefits. The automated feedback helped me identify and rectify errors in grammar and vocabulary. It was like having an extra set of eyes to improve my",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"using AI for writing instruction. However, as I started using ChatGPT, I realized its benefits. The automated feedback helped me identify and rectify errors in grammar and vocabulary. It was like having an extra set of eyes to improve my writing.” Also, participant S7 shared, “I was surprised by the AI-assisted writing instruction. It was like having a writing coach right there with me. The instant feedback from ChatGPT improved my writing significantly. It was like having a personal guide for my writing learning.” 4.2.2 Theme 2: writing skill enhancements The implementation of ChatGPT in the writing process yielded remarkable improvements in participants’ writing skills, as articulated in their responses during the interviews. One participant (S6) enthusiastically expressed, “Using ChatGPT improved my writing skills significantly. The instant feedback on organization and coherence helped me structure my essays more effectively. I noticed improvements in my grammar usage and vocabulary selection as well.” Furthermore, another participant (S9) corroborated this sentiment, emphasizing the transformative impact on their writing prowess, saying, “ChatGPT’s suggestions and examples expanded my vocabulary and improved the flow of my",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"noticed improvements in my grammar usage and vocabulary selection as well.” Furthermore, another participant (S9) corroborated this sentiment, emphasizing the transformative impact on their writing prowess, saying, “ChatGPT’s suggestions and examples expanded my vocabulary and improved the flow of my writing. It guided me to express my ideas more clearly and concisely. Overall, my writing became more polished and sophisticated with the help of AI.” 4.2.3 Theme 3: motivation and engagement enhancement The infusion of AI technology into the writing instruction process led to notable improvements in participants’ motivation and engagement, as revealed in their candid remarks during the interviews. Participant S4 vividly conveyed, “Using AI-assisted writing instruction made writing more enjoyable for me. The interactive nature of ChatGPT and the immediate feedback motivated me to actively engage in the writing process. It increased my motivation to practice and improve my writing skills.” Likewise, participant S10 echoed this sentiment, emphasizing how AI integration heightened their confidence and motivation. They shared, “The integration of AI in writing instruction made me more confident in my writing abilities. Seeing the progress I made",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"my writing skills.” Likewise, participant S10 echoed this sentiment, emphasizing how AI integration heightened their confidence and motivation. They shared, “The integration of AI in writing instruction made me more confident in my writing abilities. Seeing the progress I made with ChatGPT’s guidance boosted my self- efficacy and motivated me to put in more effort to enhance my writing.” 4.2.4 Theme 4: recognized advantages of AI assistance Participants were keenly attuned to the various advantages offered by AI-assisted writing instruction, and their reflections underscored these benefits. Participant S2 underscored the aspect of accessibility and convenience, sharing, “One advantage of AI-assisted writing instruction is its availability and convenience. I could access ChatGPT anytime and anywhere, which allowed me to practice writing at my own pace and receive immediate feedback.” Likewise, participant S8 underscored the personalized nature of the AI feedback as a notable advantage, stating, “The personalized feedback provided by ChatGPT was a significant advantage. It addressed my specific writing needs and helped me target areas for improvement. The tailored suggestions and examples were valuable resources for enhancing my writing skills.”",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,9,"a notable advantage, stating, “The personalized feedback provided by ChatGPT was a significant advantage. It addressed my specific writing needs and helped me target areas for improvement. The tailored suggestions and examples were valuable resources for enhancing my writing skills.” TABLE 4 ANCOVA results for content. Source Type III sum of squares df Mean square F Sig. Partial eta squared Group 9531.62 1 9531.62 12.61 0.00 0.19",67
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 10 frontiersin.org Similarly, participant S6 emphasized the remarkable benefits of personalized feedback, expressing, “The personalized feedback offered by ChatGPT was a game-changer. It pinpointed my unique writing needs, offering tailored suggestions that guided me toward improvement. The inclusion of concrete examples was instrumental in refining my writing skills. It was like having a personal writing coach who understood my growth areas.” 4.2.5 Theme 5: acknowledged challenges and considerations Although AI-assisted writing instruction was perceived as beneficial, participants acknowledged certain challenges and limitations. One participant (S5) mentioned the contextual accuracy of ChatGPT’s suggestions, stating, “Although AI-assisted writing instruction was beneficial, it had its limitations. Sometimes, ChatGPT’s suggestions were not contextually accurate or aligned with my writing style. It required careful judgment to determine when to accept or modify the AI’s feedback.” Another participant (S11) highlighted the challenge of over-reliance, stating, “One challenge I faced was becoming overly reliant on ChatGPT. I found myself seeking its guidance for every sentence, which hindered my own creativity and critical thinking. It was important to strike a balance",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"highlighted the challenge of over-reliance, stating, “One challenge I faced was becoming overly reliant on ChatGPT. I found myself seeking its guidance for every sentence, which hindered my own creativity and critical thinking. It was important to strike a balance between using the AI’s feedback and developing my own writing skills.” 4.2.6 Theme 6: long-term impact and sustainability Participants also raised considerations regarding the long-term impact and sustainability of AI-assisted writing instruction. One participant (S7) expressed uncertainty about the long-term impact, stating, “I wondered about the long-term impact of AI-assisted writing instruction. While it improved my writing during the study, I questioned if I would continue using AI tools independently after the course. It raised concerns about maintaining the same level of improvement without the AI support.” Another participant (S12) emphasized the need for continuous improvement, stating, “The sustainability of AI-assisted writing instruction is an important aspect to consider. As AI technology evolves, it is crucial to ensure that the AI systems continually improve and adapt to meet the changing needs of language learners. Regular updates and enhancements to AI",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"AI-assisted writing instruction is an important aspect to consider. As AI technology evolves, it is crucial to ensure that the AI systems continually improve and adapt to meet the changing needs of language learners. Regular updates and enhancements to AI tools would be beneficial for long-term effectiveness.” Likewise, thinking about the long-lasting effects, participant S14 said, “I thought about how AI-assisted writing instruction affected me in the long run. It definitely made my writing better during the study, but I wasn’t sure if I could keep improving without AI’s help after the course. I was worried about that.” These qualitative findings provide insights into participants’ perceptions, the impact on writing skills, writing motivation, perceived advantages, challenges, and considerations for the long- term sustainability of AI-assisted writing instruction. The themes and related excerpts from the semi-structured interviews contribute to a comprehensive understanding of the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning on EFL students’ writing skills and writing motivation in this mixed methods study. 5 Discussion This study sought to delve into the impact of AI-assisted language learning on the academic writing skills",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"understanding of the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning on EFL students’ writing skills and writing motivation in this mixed methods study. 5 Discussion This study sought to delve into the impact of AI-assisted language learning on the academic writing skills and motivation of EFL learners. To provide a comprehensive examination of this phenomenon, a mixed-methods approach was meticulously employed, allowing for the systematic collection and analysis of both quantitative and qualitative data. The quantitative analysis yielded significant insights, revealing substantial enhancements in the participants’ academic writing performance and writing motivation due to the incorporation of AI-powered language learning. These improvements manifested prominently in the participants’ organizational skills, coherence, grammar, and vocabulary. These quantitative findings resonate with the observations made by Liu et al. (2021) and Yan (2023), who underscored the profound contributions of AI-assisted language learning tools in advancing EFL learners’ writing abilities. Moreover, these findings harmonize with the outcomes identified by Rahman et al. (2022) and Utami and Winarni (2023), which accentuated the positive influence of AI-powered language learning on EFL learners’ motivation and their heightened engagement in writing",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"learners’ writing abilities. Moreover, these findings harmonize with the outcomes identified by Rahman et al. (2022) and Utami and Winarni (2023), which accentuated the positive influence of AI-powered language learning on EFL learners’ motivation and their heightened engagement in writing tasks. One possible explanation for the aforementioned findings may be attributed to the high level of engagement exhibited by EFL learners in writing activities supported by AI. The AI-assisted language learning tool offered the learners suitable alternatives for their written texts, making this approach more favorable compared to conventional writing instruction (Zhao, 2022). By generating writing ideas, grammatically accurate sentences, and appropriate lexical resources, the AI tool facilitated the production of better-written texts, thereby enhancing the learners’ engagement in the required writing tasks within the AI-supported class. This heightened engagement in writing activities potentially contributed to the development of the learners’ academic writing skills. These findings align with the research of Utami and Winarni (2023), who also found that AI-powered language learning tools improved EFL learners’ engagement in writing activities, consequently leading to improvements in writing skills. The qualitative findings",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,10,"learners’ academic writing skills. These findings align with the research of Utami and Winarni (2023), who also found that AI-powered language learning tools improved EFL learners’ engagement in writing activities, consequently leading to improvements in writing skills. The qualitative findings of the current study further corroborated these quantitative findings, as learners exhibited positive responses toward the integration of the AI-assisted language learning approach into their writing activities. TABLE 5 ANCOVA results for organization. Source Type III sum of squares df Mean square F Sig. Partial eta squared Group 179.19 1 179.19 23.16 0.00 0.34 TABLE 6 ANCOVA results for language use. Source Type III sum of squares df Mean square F Sig. Partial eta squared Group 175.22 1 175.22 9.86 0.01 0.11 TABLE 7 ANCOVA results for writing motivation. Source Type III sum of squares df Mean square F Sig. Partial eta squared Group 89.23 1 89.23 29.62 0.00 0.38",150
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 11 frontiersin.org Consistent with the findings of Hwang et al. (2023), the present study suggests that the positive outcomes can be attributed to the personalized language learning experience facilitated by the AI-assisted language learning tool during collaborative writing tasks. By using the AI-powered tool, learners had the opportunity to work at their own pace and receive immediate feedback, enabling them to produce better-written texts. This personalized learning experience not only enhanced learners’ writing motivation but also provided specific feedback and comments on their writing tasks, assisting them in addressing writing issues more effectively and conveniently. These findings align with the qualitative findings of the study, wherein learners expressed their enthusiasm for the AI tool’s ability to personalize their writing activities. This aspect of the AI-assisted language learning tool is also in accordance with Vygotsky’s (1984) social constructivist theory, as it allowed learners to interact with the AI tool and gradually internalize their writing abilities. In other words, the AI tool initially supported learners in personalizing their writing activities, leading to the internalization of",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"Vygotsky’s (1984) social constructivist theory, as it allowed learners to interact with the AI tool and gradually internalize their writing abilities. In other words, the AI tool initially supported learners in personalizing their writing activities, leading to the internalization of writing skills. Aligned with Vygotsky’s (1984) social constructivist framework, the integration of AI introduced a dynamic incentive for learners to exercise self-regulation in their writing endeavors. Following this theory, participants in this study engaged in collaborative writing tasks with AI, a practice that facilitated the development of their self- regulatory capacities. Through these interactive sessions, learners transitioned from relying on external regulation to becoming adept at independently managing writing tasks. This discovery implies that learners who availed themselves of the AI-assisted language learning approach were not only able to attain writing self-regulation more promptly but also exhibited a higher degree of effectiveness compared to their counterparts who did not incorporate AI into their writing routines. Substantiating this claim, qualitative data from the study underscored the significant role of the AI tool in empowering learners to function autonomously within the AI-powered",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"of effectiveness compared to their counterparts who did not incorporate AI into their writing routines. Substantiating this claim, qualitative data from the study underscored the significant role of the AI tool in empowering learners to function autonomously within the AI-powered learning environment, thus corroborating the earlier findings. One potential explanation for the quantitative findings of this study may lie in the tendency of EFL learners to experience anxiety when engaging in individual or collaborative writing tasks within traditional classroom settings (Yan, 2023). The completion of writing tasks under such traditional circumstances can have a detrimental effect on EFL learners’ writing performance and motivation. In contrast, the integration of the AI-assisted language learning tool in the AI class created a less anxiety-inducing context for learners to practice and enhance their academic writing skills and motivation. This finding aligns with the qualitative results, wherein learners expressed that the AI environment alleviated their writing anxiety and facilitated improvements in their writing abilities. Furthermore, the quantitative facet of this study lends credence to the proposition that the AI-assisted language learning approach fosters the development",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"results, wherein learners expressed that the AI environment alleviated their writing anxiety and facilitated improvements in their writing abilities. Furthermore, the quantitative facet of this study lends credence to the proposition that the AI-assisted language learning approach fosters the development of EFL learners’ academic writing skills. Consequently, it is plausible to posit that this approach may also be advantageous for other writing genres, such as creative writing and argumentative writing (Ippolito et al., 2022; Su et al., 2023). Su et al. (2023), for instance, contend that AI-assisted language learning tools exert a positive influence on learners’ argumentative writing by furnishing immediate feedback on language usage, organization, and content. The versatility of AI tools in providing feedback and commentary across various dimensions of writing performance holds the potential to augment the individual writing skills of EFL learners. The qualitative findings also offer nuanced insights into the impact of AI-assisted writing instruction on EFL learners. The positive perceptions echoed by participants align with prior research, emphasizing the significance of real-time feedback as a pedagogical tool (Zhang and Hyland, 2018). The automated feedback",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"offer nuanced insights into the impact of AI-assisted writing instruction on EFL learners. The positive perceptions echoed by participants align with prior research, emphasizing the significance of real-time feedback as a pedagogical tool (Zhang and Hyland, 2018). The automated feedback mechanism proved instrumental in addressing nuanced aspects of writing, such as grammar, vocabulary, and overall organization. This is consistent with studies by Barrot (2023) and Zhao (2022), which emphasize how immediate feedback can significantly contribute to enhanced writing proficiency. Furthermore, participants reported notable improvements in their writing skills attributed to the use of ChatGPT. The intervention’s effectiveness in enhancing organization and coherence within their essays demonstrates the potential of AI-assisted tools in scaffolding the writing process (Zhao, 2022; Barrot, 2023). Additionally, the incorporation of suggestions and examples by ChatGPT resulted in an expanded vocabulary and improved fluency in writing. This mirrors the findings of studies that have highlighted AI’s contribution to vocabulary development and writing fluency (Ippolito et al., 2022; Su et al., 2023). The qualitative analysis also unveils the positive impact of AI integration on participants’ motivation and engagement,",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"mirrors the findings of studies that have highlighted AI’s contribution to vocabulary development and writing fluency (Ippolito et al., 2022; Su et al., 2023). The qualitative analysis also unveils the positive impact of AI integration on participants’ motivation and engagement, affirming previous studies that have identified similar trends (Huang and Tan, 2023). The interactive nature of ChatGPT, coupled with immediate feedback, fostered a more engaging and enjoyable writing process. Moreover, the confidence boost observed among participants aligns with Su et al.’s (2023) assertion that AI tools positively influence learners’ confidence in their writing abilities. While the advantages of AI-assisted writing instruction are prominent, participants also acknowledged certain challenges. The identified contextual accuracy concerns resonate with previous studies highlighting the need for careful consideration when incorporating AI-generated feedback (Utami and Winarni, 2023). This cautious approach is crucial to ensure that AI feedback aligns with the individual writing styles and contexts of learners. Additionally, the challenge of over-reliance on ChatGPT emerged as a noteworthy consideration. Striking a balance between utilizing the AI’s feedback and fostering independent critical thinking and creativity is vital",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,"feedback aligns with the individual writing styles and contexts of learners. Additionally, the challenge of over-reliance on ChatGPT emerged as a noteworthy consideration. Striking a balance between utilizing the AI’s feedback and fostering independent critical thinking and creativity is vital (Utami and Winarni, 2023). This echoes the broader discourse on scaffolding in education, emphasizing the need for gradual release of responsibility from the AI tool to the learner. Lastly, participants expressed considerations regarding the long-term impact and sustainability of AI-assisted writing instruction. These concerns align with broader discussions on the evolving role of AI in education. Continuous improvement and adaptability of AI systems to cater to the changing needs of language learners are indeed critical aspects for its sustained effectiveness (Su et al., 2023). In essence, the qualitative findings not only validate the quantitative results but also provide rich contextual insights into the multifaceted impact of AI-assisted writing instruction on EFL learners. This comprehensive understanding highlights the potential of such interventions in advancing writing skills and motivation among language learners.",170
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,11,insights into the multifaceted impact of AI-assisted writing instruction on EFL learners. This comprehensive understanding highlights the potential of such interventions in advancing writing skills and motivation among language learners.,30
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 12 frontiersin.org 6 Conclusion Building upon Vygotsky’s social constructivist framework, the primary aim of this study was to explore the impact of an AI-assisted language learning approach on the academic writing skills and writing motivation of Chinese EFL learners. The quantitative results revealed that the AI-assisted class exhibited superior development and performance in terms of academic writing skills and writing motivation compared to the non-AI class. Complementing the quantitative findings, the qualitative results provided further insights into the EFL learners’ positive perceptions regarding the incorporation of the AI-assisted language learning approach in their writing courses. These perceptions stemmed from the interactive and innovative learning environment facilitated by the AI tool, which played a significant role in enhancing the learners’ academic writing skills and writing motivation. The findings of this study carry important implications for the field of EFL, particularly in relation to the teaching of writing. The integration of AI tools into EFL writing courses is recommended as part of a technology-integrated language learning approach, as it aligns with innovative pedagogical practices and",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"for the field of EFL, particularly in relation to the teaching of writing. The integration of AI tools into EFL writing courses is recommended as part of a technology-integrated language learning approach, as it aligns with innovative pedagogical practices and has the potential to significantly enhance students’ academic writing skills and writing motivation. To enhance EFL learners’ academic writing skills and motivation, it is highly encouraged for learners to actively incorporate AI tools into their writing activities. By leveraging the capabilities of AI, learners can receive immediate and targeted feedback, enabling them to identify areas for improvement and enhance their writing performance accordingly. This integration of AI into their writing practice has the potential to yield more efficient and effective learning outcomes. To provide engaging and motivating writing activities supported by AI, EFL educators and teachers should consider establishing dedicated AI-supported language learning classes. These classes can serve as platforms to train EFL learners on how to effectively utilize AI tools to improve their writing skills. Through providing guidance and support in the use of AI tools, educators can empower",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"AI-supported language learning classes. These classes can serve as platforms to train EFL learners on how to effectively utilize AI tools to improve their writing skills. Through providing guidance and support in the use of AI tools, educators can empower learners to take ownership of their learning process and make meaningful progress in their writing abilities. Moreover, organizing an AI-supported language learning class specifically tailored to writing activities can bring significant benefits to EFL learners. Within such a class, learners can receive AI-generated writing feedback and comments addressing various aspects of their written texts, including language usage, organization, and content. This comprehensive feedback can provide learners with specific guidance to address their writing challenges and refine their skills across different areas. Several limitations should be acknowledged when interpreting the findings of this study. Firstly, the generalizability of the results may be constrained due to the specific sample of Chinese EFL students that was used. The cultural backgrounds, educational systems, and language proficiency levels in other contexts may differ, potentially yielding different outcomes. Therefore, caution should be exercised in generalizing the",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"be constrained due to the specific sample of Chinese EFL students that was used. The cultural backgrounds, educational systems, and language proficiency levels in other contexts may differ, potentially yielding different outcomes. Therefore, caution should be exercised in generalizing the findings beyond the specific population studied. Replication studies with more diverse populations are needed to validate the effectiveness of AI-assisted language learning in different settings. Secondly, the study utilized a relatively small sample size of 50 participants, which may have implications for the statistical power and generalizability of the findings. A larger sample size would have provided more robust results and increased the confidence in the conclusions drawn from the study. Future research endeavors should consider recruiting a larger and more diverse participant pool to enhance the external validity of the findings. Another limitation of the study is the relatively short duration of the intervention. The impact of AI-assisted language learning on writing skills and motivation was evaluated over a limited period, potentially limiting our understanding of the sustained effects of such instruction. Longer intervention periods would offer insights into",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"short duration of the intervention. The impact of AI-assisted language learning on writing skills and motivation was evaluated over a limited period, potentially limiting our understanding of the sustained effects of such instruction. Longer intervention periods would offer insights into the durability of the observed improvements. Future studies should explore the long-term effects of AI-assisted language learning to provide a more comprehensive assessment of its efficacy in enhancing writing abilities. Furthermore, a potential limitation is the potential for contamination between the experimental and control groups. Despite random assignment, it is challenging to completely separate instructional methods or interactions in real-world educational settings. Some degree of crossover between the groups may have occurred, introducing the possibility of contamination effects and influencing the observed results. Future studies could implement more stringent control measures to minimize contamination and provide a more accurate assessment of the effects of AI-assisted language learning. Additionally, despite the rigorous implementation of measures to mitigate potential risks, it is imperative to acknowledge certain limitations within our study. One such aspect pertains to the challenge of ensuring the prevention of",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"of the effects of AI-assisted language learning. Additionally, despite the rigorous implementation of measures to mitigate potential risks, it is imperative to acknowledge certain limitations within our study. One such aspect pertains to the challenge of ensuring the prevention of unintended plagiarism due to the utilization of AI-assisted writing tools. Although extensive guidance was provided to the participants on the ethical use of ChatGPT as a supplementary writing aid, aimed at fostering original content creation and upholding academic integrity, the inherent nature of AI-generated suggestions poses a plausible risk if not meticulously monitored. Although the participants were duly instructed on incorporating AI-generated feedback while preserving their individual writing styles, the potential for unintentional reliance on AI-generated content remains a concern. Further steps, such as continual reinforcement of ethical considerations and vigilance in recognizing potential plagiarism risks, could enhance the robustness of future studies employing AI-assisted writing tools. Therefore, while our study diligently emphasized the importance of academic integrity and ethical AI use, the dynamic nature of AI assistance poses an ongoing challenge in safeguarding against unintended plagiarism. Future research endeavors",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,12,"of future studies employing AI-assisted writing tools. Therefore, while our study diligently emphasized the importance of academic integrity and ethical AI use, the dynamic nature of AI assistance poses an ongoing challenge in safeguarding against unintended plagiarism. Future research endeavors employing AI tools may benefit from refined methodologies and continuous vigilance to further address this critical concern. Lastly, the study did not include a long-term follow-up to assess the sustainability of the observed improvements in writing skills and motivation. Without a post-intervention evaluation, it remains unknown whether the benefits of AI-assisted instruction persist over an extended period of time. Future research should consider conducting follow-up evaluations to ascertain the longevity of the observed enhancements and further investigate the potential long-term impact of AI-assisted language learning on writing skills and motivation. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Requests",153
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,13,"Song and Song 10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843 Frontiers in Psychology 13 frontiersin.org to access these datasets should be directed to CS, songcp2023@163.com. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the School of Foreign Studies, North Minzu University, Yinchuan, Ningxia. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions CS: Conceptualization, Data curation, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. YS: Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Software, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declare financial support was received for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. This work is supported by the Ministry of Education’s Industry School Cooperation Collaborative Education Project of 2022: The Application of the VR Scenario Training System in College English Education in the University for Nationalities (No: 220501867122841). Conflict of interest The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that",180
A052,A052.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1260843,13,"The Application of the VR Scenario Training System in College English Education in the University for Nationalities (No: 220501867122841). Conflict of interest The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. Correction note A correction has been made to this article. Details can be found at: 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1917101. Publisher’s note All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher. Supplementary material The Supplementary material for this article can be found online at: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1",139
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,1,"EDITED BY Mohammad Fteiha, Abu Dhabi University, United Arab Emirates REVIEWED BY Ali Albashir Mohammed Alhaj, King Khalid University, Saudi Arabia Sony Yunior Erlangga, Universitas Sarjanawiyata Tamansiswa, Indonesia *CORRESPONDENCE Areej ElSayary areej.elsayary@zu.ac.ae RECEIVED 02 July 2026 REVISED 31 August 2026 ACCEPTED 07 September 2026 PUBLISHED 28 September 2026 CITATION ElSayary A and Hojeij Z (2026) Perceived educational value, ethical risk, and support for responsible AI governance in higher education: an embedded mixed- methods study. Front. Educ. 11:1926201. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 COPYRIGHT © 2026 ElSayary and Hojeij. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Perceived educational value, ethical risk, and support for responsible AI governance in higher education: an embedded mixed-methods study Areej ElSayary 1* and Zeina Hojeij 2 1College of Interdisciplinary",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,1,"distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Perceived educational value, ethical risk, and support for responsible AI governance in higher education: an embedded mixed-methods study Areej ElSayary 1* and Zeina Hojeij 2 1College of Interdisciplinary Studies, Zayed University, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates, 2College of Interdisciplinary Studies, Zayed University, Dubai, United Arab Emirates Introduction: Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is becoming increasingly embedded in higher education assessment, yet the perceptual factors that motivate stakeholder support for its institutional governance remain underexamined. This study investigated the extent to which Perceived Educational Value (PEV) and AI Ethical Risk Perception (AERP) are associated with Responsible AI Governance Support (RAGS) among higher education stakeholders. Methods: An embedded mixed-methods survey design was employed. Quantitative Likert-scale data and qualitative open-ended responses were collected concurrently through one structured questionnaire; full-sample quantitative analyses were conducted first, followed by thematic analysis of the open-ended responses to contextualize and deepen interpretation of the quantitative findings. A structured questionnaire was administered to 192 participants (125 students and 67 instructors) recruited from public and private higher education institutions",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,1,"conducted first, followed by thematic analysis of the open-ended responses to contextualize and deepen interpretation of the quantitative findings. A structured questionnaire was administered to 192 participants (125 students and 67 instructors) recruited from public and private higher education institutions in the United Arab Emirates, with N = 192 yielding statistical power exceeding.99 for the structural model. Qualitative open- ended responses from 179 participants were subsequently analyzed using a codebook-based thematic analysis. Quantitative data were analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). Results: Participants reported above-midpoint levels of PEV, AERP, and RAGS. The structural model explained 59.1% of the variance in RAGS. Both PEV [β = .255, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.117,.377)] and AERP [β = .627, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.485,.748)] were positively associated with governance support, with AERP showing the larger association. AERP did not significantly moderate the PEV–RAGS relationship [β = −.030, p = .370, 95% bias-corrected CI (−.098,.037)]. MICOM established full measurement invariance for PEV and AERP but not compositional invariance for RAGS; therefore, structural student–instructor comparisons involving RAGS were",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,1,"AERP did not significantly moderate the PEV–RAGS relationship [β = −.030, p = .370, 95% bias-corrected CI (−.098,.037)]. MICOM established full measurement invariance for PEV and AERP but not compositional invariance for RAGS; therefore, structural student–instructor comparisons involving RAGS were not interpreted. Thematic analysis identified responsible conditional integration as the central interpretive frame, with learning enhancement, governance expectations, academic integrity, over-reliance, and critical-thinking concerns among the salient themes. TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 28 September 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",82
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,2,"Discussion: These findings provide preliminary empirical support for a model in which perceived educational value and ethical risk perception are each associated with governance endorsement. The stronger association between ethical risk perception and governance support suggests that transparent attention to ethical risks may be relevant to institutional governance communication, a possibility that requires intervention-based research. Practical implications include risk-aware AI literacy programs, transparent and contestable governance policies, stakeholder engagement, and human-in-the- loop oversight mechanisms in AI-mediated assessment. KEYWORDS AI governance, ethical risk perception, generative AI, higher education, perceived educational value, responsible AI 1 Introduction The rapid integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) into higher education has created new opportunities for enhancing teaching, learning, and assessment practices. Existing research has predominantly focused on technology acceptance, adoption intentions, and perceptions of the educational benefits of AI-supported tools, providing valuable insights into stakeholders’ willingness to engage with AI technologies. Considerably less attention, however, has been dedicated to understanding the factors that influence support for the governance and responsible implementation of AI within educational settings, even as concerns regarding bias, transparency, accountability, privacy, academic",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,2,"willingness to engage with AI technologies. Considerably less attention, however, has been dedicated to understanding the factors that influence support for the governance and responsible implementation of AI within educational settings, even as concerns regarding bias, transparency, accountability, privacy, academic integrity, and the preservation of human judgment have become more noticeable with growing institutional adoption. Drawing on emerging scholarship in AI governance, educational technology, and responsible innovation, this study examines whether support for responsible AI governance (RAGS) is associated with two key factors: the perceived educational value of GenAI (PEV) and awareness of its ethical risks (AERP). Individuals who recognize the educational benefits and those who perceive ethical risks may report different levels of support for oversight, transparency, and accountability mechanisms. Accordingly, this study investigates the extent to which perceived educational value and ethical risk perception are associated with responsible AI governance support in higher education and explores qualitative perspectives regarding the responsible implementation of GenAI in educational assessment. 1.1 Generative AI in higher education The integration of GenAI into higher education is proceeding unevenly, shaped by stakeholder readiness, infrastructure,",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,2,"AI governance support in higher education and explores qualitative perspectives regarding the responsible implementation of GenAI in educational assessment. 1.1 Generative AI in higher education The integration of GenAI into higher education is proceeding unevenly, shaped by stakeholder readiness, infrastructure, and institutional guidance (Atoyan et al., 2025). Within teaching and learning, GenAI’s most consistently reported contribution is personalized, adaptive instruction: tailored content and adaptive learning pathways have been associated with gains in student motivation, self-efficacy, and autonomous learning, particularly when paired with teacher support (Wu et al., 2024). Intelligent tutoring functions and adaptive platforms similarly support comprehension and problem-solving by shifting instructor effort toward higher- order pedagogical tasks, a pattern echoed in AI-generated interdisciplinary instructional content that enriches cross- disciplinary classroom interaction (Kuang et al., 2025). These benefits extend to inclusion: GenAI-enabled accessibility features have been linked to greater autonomy and engagement among students with disabilities, although such gains remain conditional on institutional infrastructure and constrained by privacy and financial barriers (Anselimus, 2026). In assessment and feedback, GenAI scales individualized support that would otherwise be resource-intensive. Adaptive, authentic tasks promoted",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,2,"and engagement among students with disabilities, although such gains remain conditional on institutional infrastructure and constrained by privacy and financial barriers (Anselimus, 2026). In assessment and feedback, GenAI scales individualized support that would otherwise be resource-intensive. Adaptive, authentic tasks promoted self-regulated learning (Ilieva et al., 2025), while large language model-based systems approximate one-on-one feedback at scale (Liu et al., 2025), and automating routine grading has been associated with faster turnaround without loss of pedagogical rigour, freeing instructors for complex instructional work (Waring and Evans, 2024). AI literacy is increasingly treated as a precondition for ethical and effective use rather than an optional add-on (Williams, 2025). These opportunities are accompanied by challenges spanning academic integrity, algorithmic bias, and data privacy (Chanpradit, 2025; Giftson and Dhivya, 2026), as well as infrastructural constraints in resource-limited settings where uneven AI literacy risks widening existing educational inequalities (Izevbigie et al., 2025). Systematic reviews have identified policy fragmentation and uneven institutional readiness as persistent, underaddressed priorities (Amofa et al., 2025). 1.2 Perceived educational value (PEV) of generative AI Perceived Educational Value (PEV), defined here as stakeholders’",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,2,"educational inequalities (Izevbigie et al., 2025). Systematic reviews have identified policy fragmentation and uneven institutional readiness as persistent, underaddressed priorities (Amofa et al., 2025). 1.2 Perceived educational value (PEV) of generative AI Perceived Educational Value (PEV), defined here as stakeholders’ beliefs about the extent to which GenAI enhances learning, teaching, assessment, feedback, efficiency, and educational outcomes, has emerged as a recurring construct across the AI adoption. Literature reported benefits cluster around several areas. GenAI’s capacity to generate adaptive content has been associated with gains in learning efficiency, engagement, and satisfaction (Li et al., 2024; Sharma et al., 2025). Interactive AI-powered tutoring supports concept clarification and problem-solving (Khan et al., 2025), and ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",120
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"scaffolded integration has been linked to higher-order cognitive gains through personalized feedback (Marcos, 2026). For instructors, reported value emphasizes efficiency: streamlined lesson planning, content development, and grading, with some studies reporting substantial reductions in administrative workload (Soleimani et al., 2025). At the institutional level, GenAI-derived analytics have informed curricular adjustments and identified learning bottlenecks (Ilieva et al., 2025). This pattern aligns with the Technology Acceptance Model and Innovation Diffusion Theory logics (Rogers, 2003), both of which position perceived usefulness as a central determinant of adoption-related attitudes. Perceived Educational Value, as operationalised in this study, is conceptually distinguished from the TAM construct of perceived usefulness in three respects: it is domain-specific to educational outcomes and instructional quality rather than generic task performance; it encompasses dimensions of feedback richness, pedagogical alignment, and inclusive accessibility not captured by usefulness items; and it is evaluated at the level of institutional and assessment practices rather than individual task completion. These distinctions matter because governance support is a collective, institutionally oriented outcome rather than an individual adoption decision, a difference that motivates the specific construct development",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"evaluated at the level of institutional and assessment practices rather than individual task completion. These distinctions matter because governance support is a collective, institutionally oriented outcome rather than an individual adoption decision, a difference that motivates the specific construct development and hypothesized pathway examined in this study. Students and instructors, however, weigh usefulness differently. Students report increased motivation and favorable attitudes toward AI tools, with performance expectancy identified as the strongest predictor of acceptance, though concerns about inaccurate outputs and ethical issues persist (Ramli et al., 2025; Torres, 2025; Yilmaz et al., 2026). Instructors express a more conditional positivity. While recognizing GenAI’s potential for content creation and efficiency, actual use lags behind recognition, constrained by reliability concerns, ethical standing, and gaps in pedagogical strategy (Dabirian and Swarat, 2024; Soleimani et al., 2025). Evidence on whether perceived usefulness translates into institutional-level support is mixed. Mustafa and Altrad (2026) found that institutional readiness was a stronger adoption driver than perceived usefulness itself, while others frame institutional support as a moderating enabler that converts usefulness perceptions into practical adoption (Haq et al., 2026;",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"support is mixed. Mustafa and Altrad (2026) found that institutional readiness was a stronger adoption driver than perceived usefulness itself, while others frame institutional support as a moderating enabler that converts usefulness perceptions into practical adoption (Haq et al., 2026; Krishna et al., 2026), leaving open whether stakeholders who perceive GenAI as educationally valuable correspondingly support its governance. 1.3 Ethical risks of generative AI in higher education Where Perceived Educational Value captures the upside case for GenAI in higher education, AI Ethical Risk Perception (AERP) captures the corresponding downside. AERP is treated in this study as a single reflective construct representing a general orientation toward ethical risk awareness, grounded in the psychometric paradigm of risk perception (Slovic, 1987), which characterizes risk perception as a multidimensional but psychologically unified evaluative disposition toward potential harm. While the AERP items span distinct ethical domains, bias, fairness, privacy, cognitive dependence, academic integrity, and relational displacement, they share the common evaluative dimension of perceived harm likelihood and severity in educational AI contexts. The breadth of this literature extends well beyond the specific concerns sampled in",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"domains, bias, fairness, privacy, cognitive dependence, academic integrity, and relational displacement, they share the common evaluative dimension of perceived harm likelihood and severity in educational AI contexts. The breadth of this literature extends well beyond the specific concerns sampled in the AERP items, but substantiates each of them as components of a multidimensional but theoretically coherent risk construct. The four subsections below are organized accordingly. 1.3.1 Bias and fairness AI systems can systematically disadvantage particular student groups rather than functioning as neutral instructional tools. In one large-scale audit of 150 AI educational platforms, 68% exhibited measurable bias against at least one marginalized group, traced to non-representative training data and inadequate bias-testing protocols, with concrete disparities including lower facial-recognition accuracy for students of color, higher misclassification rates for students with disabilities, and fewer advanced-course recommendations for low-income students (Bhatnagar and Somani, 2025). The black-box character of many AI models makes biased outputs difficult for educators to detect or contest, and self-reinforcing feedback loops can compound disparities absent inclusive design and ongoing intervention (Lockwood and Brown, 2026). 1.3.2 Privacy and data protection",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"2025). The black-box character of many AI models makes biased outputs difficult for educators to detect or contest, and self-reinforcing feedback loops can compound disparities absent inclusive design and ongoing intervention (Lockwood and Brown, 2026). 1.3.2 Privacy and data protection AI-driven educational platforms collect, store, and process large volumes of sensitive student data, introducing risk through unauthorized access, breach, misuse, and erosion of student autonomy (Giftson and Dhivya, 2026). Consent management is particularly difficult: AI tools generate new personalized data continuously, straining static consent models in ways that are especially problematic for minors. The literature converges on an ethical-by-design approach embedding anonymization, federated learning architectures, dynamic consent mechanisms, and regulatory compliance throughout the AI lifecycle (Mougiakou et al., 2025; Torrisi-Steele, 2025). 1.3.3 Academic integrity and misuse GenAI’s capacity to generate fluent, original-seeming text has made conventional plagiarism detection substantially less effective (Ward et al., 2024), prompting a broader rethinking of assessment design as verifying authorship becomes harder when AI can plausibly produce responses that imitate the surface features of reasoning without demonstrating a student’s own critical analysis (Ncube et al.,",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,3,"effective (Ward et al., 2024), prompting a broader rethinking of assessment design as verifying authorship becomes harder when AI can plausibly produce responses that imitate the surface features of reasoning without demonstrating a student’s own critical analysis (Ncube et al., 2026; Sangka et al., 2025). 1.3.4 Cognitive and relational risks Evidence on whether habitual AI use degrades critical thinking through cognitive offloading is substantial but nuanced: a mixed-methods study of 666 participants found a significant negative relationship between AI dependence and critical- thinking performance, particularly among younger users (Gerlich, 2025). Importantly, AI use explicitly scaffolded within instructional design has been shown to support rather than erode critical-thinking development (Hong et al., 2024). On relational displacement, students consistently prefer human ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",128
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"instructors over AI in comparative assistance contexts (Spence et al., 2024), and the redistribution of instructional authority toward algorithmic systems has been argued to challenge teacher autonomy and intensify existing inequalities (Bouakaz and Khalid, 2025). 1.4 Responsible AI governance in higher education Governance refers to the frameworks, policies, and practices that govern the ethical, transparent, and accountable use of AI within an institution, encompassing mechanisms for managing risk, protecting data privacy, mitigating bias, and aligning AI use with institutional values. In higher education, governance is typically conceived as a participatory structure that balances centralized oversight of systemic risk against decentralized flexibility for users to adapt AI tools to their own pedagogical contexts (Soko, 2025). Responsible AI Governance Support (RAGS), as operationalized in this study, is conceptually distinguished from related constructs: unlike technology acceptance, which reflects willingness to use AI, RAGS reflects active endorsement of institutional oversight mechanisms; unlike institutional trust, which concerns generalized confidence in organizations, RAGS concerns normative support for specific governance structures; unlike policy compliance, which reflects adherence to existing rules, RAGS reflects belief that governance is desirable",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"reflects active endorsement of institutional oversight mechanisms; unlike institutional trust, which concerns generalized confidence in organizations, RAGS concerns normative support for specific governance structures; unlike policy compliance, which reflects adherence to existing rules, RAGS reflects belief that governance is desirable and should be strengthened; and unlike institutional readiness, which concerns organizational capability, RAGS concerns individual stakeholders’ endorsement of governance as legitimate and necessary. Universities require such frameworks for reasons that follow directly from the risks in Section 1.3: to safeguard academic integrity and assign accountability for AI-related decisions (Alkhatib et al., 2025; Arrabaca, 2026); to balance innovation against ethical, technical, and equity-related vulnerabilities (Oktyabrski, 2025; Tong et al., 2025); to embed local cultural and institutional values into AI deployment and to sustain adaptive governance as both the technology and its risks evolve (Al-Zahrani et al., 2026). These rationales align with internationally articulated principles, including transparency, fairness and non-discrimination, accountability, privacy, human- centricity, and adaptive governance (Komarudin, 2025; Pasupuleti, 2024). Three instruments illustrate the range of approaches: UNESCO’s Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence grounds governance in human rights and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"internationally articulated principles, including transparency, fairness and non-discrimination, accountability, privacy, human- centricity, and adaptive governance (Komarudin, 2025; Pasupuleti, 2024). Three instruments illustrate the range of approaches: UNESCO’s Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence grounds governance in human rights and AI literacy as a global public good (Pasupuleti, 2024; Rotenberg, 2024; UNESCO, 2021); the OECD AI Principles take a policy- oriented approach centred on transparency and accountability through human oversight (Komarudin, 2025; OECD, 2019); and the EU AI Act imposes binding, risk-classified legal obligations with enforcement mechanisms neither instrument provides (European Parliament, 2024; Rotenberg, 2024). Within higher education institutions, governance remains comparatively emergent and uneven, tending to localize these principles around academic integrity, fairness, and privacy through participatory processes and human-in-the-loop arrangements (Soko, 2025), with persistent gaps including policy fragmentation, uneven institutional readiness, and governance structures that struggle to keep pace with technological change. What remains underexamined is whether individual stakeholders, the students and instructors expected to operate within these structures, actually support them. It is this gap that the present study’s focus on Responsible AI Governance Support is intended",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"pace with technological change. What remains underexamined is whether individual stakeholders, the students and instructors expected to operate within these structures, actually support them. It is this gap that the present study’s focus on Responsible AI Governance Support is intended to fill. 1.5 Why stakeholders support AI governance The dominant framing in educational technology research remains oriented toward acceptance, whether stakeholders are willing to adopt and use AI tools. Support for governance is conceptually distinct: it is not a willingness to use a technology but a willingness to endorse oversight of that technology’s use by others. The following subsections build the theoretical case for treating educational value and ethical risk perception as predictors of governance support. 1.5.1 Perceived educational value and governance support The theoretical basis for this pathway draws on two complementary frameworks. Institutional Legitimacy Theory (Suchman, 1995) proposes that actors within institutional fields actively support structures and mechanisms that align with shared beliefs about appropriate and valuable practice; technologies perceived as educationally beneficial generate institutional pressure toward governance frameworks that enable their responsible adoption at scale. Responsible Research",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"that actors within institutional fields actively support structures and mechanisms that align with shared beliefs about appropriate and valuable practice; technologies perceived as educationally beneficial generate institutional pressure toward governance frameworks that enable their responsible adoption at scale. Responsible Research and Innovation (RRI) theory similarly positions governance not as a constraint on beneficial technology but as the institutional mechanism through which innovation is embedded sustainably in social practice, with stakeholders who recognize a technology’s potential value motivated to ensure it is realized through accountable structures rather than abandoned or restricted (Owen et al., 2012). Under both frameworks, recognizing a technology’s value creates an incentive to implement it responsibly and durably rather than abandon or restrict it. Under this account, governance is not opposed to adoption but instrumental to it: stakeholders who perceive GenAI as educationally valuable have a stake in seeing that value realized responsibly, and so favor the policies, transparency mechanisms, and accountability structures that make sustainable, legitimate integration possible (Ahmad et al., 2024; Pasupuleti, 2024). This logic motivates the study’s first hypothesis (H1). H1: Perceived Educational Value is",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,4,"seeing that value realized responsibly, and so favor the policies, transparency mechanisms, and accountability structures that make sustainable, legitimate integration possible (Ahmad et al., 2024; Pasupuleti, 2024). This logic motivates the study’s first hypothesis (H1). H1: Perceived Educational Value is positively associated with Responsible AI Governance Support. 1.5.2 Ethical risk perception and governance support The theoretical basis for this pathway draws on Protection Motivation Theory (PMT; Rogers, 1983), which proposes that ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",80
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"perceived threat motivates protective behavioral responses through two sequential appraisal processes: threat appraisal, evaluation of the severity and probability of harm, and coping appraisal, evaluation of whether available protective responses are effective. Applied to AI governance, stakeholders who perceive GenAI as posing significant ethical risks engage in coping appraisal of institutional governance mechanisms, transparency requirements, ethical guidelines, monitoring systems, and accountability structures, as effective responses to mitigate those threats. PMT predicts that higher threat appraisal should motivate stronger endorsement of governance structures, particularly when those structures are perceived as credible responses to the perceived harms. This prediction is further grounded in the psychometric paradigm of risk perception (Slovic, 1987), which demonstrates across multiple technology domains that perceived threat magnitude directly predicts support for regulatory and protective oversight. Consistent with both frameworks, recognition of potential harms such as bias, privacy violation, opacity, or misuse may be associated with stronger support for oversight mechanisms (Ahmad et al., 2024; Pasupuleti, 2025). This theoretical rationale informs H2, while the present cross- sectional design tests association rather than causal mechanism. H2: AI Ethical Risk Perception",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"opacity, or misuse may be associated with stronger support for oversight mechanisms (Ahmad et al., 2024; Pasupuleti, 2025). This theoretical rationale informs H2, while the present cross- sectional design tests association rather than causal mechanism. H2: AI Ethical Risk Perception is positively associated with Responsible AI Governance Support. 1.5.3 Risk-Benefit evaluation Risk-benefit analysis frameworks propose that individuals simultaneously evaluate both the potential benefits and risks of technologies and that these evaluations may condition one another rather than combine additively (Fischhoff et al., 1978). Specifically, ethical risk perception may strengthen the translation of perceived educational value into governance endorsement, since governance represents the mechanism through which value can be realized safely, or may attenuate it among stakeholders who view risks as manageable and governance as unnecessary. The remaining question is how these two pathways relate. The broader literature on public attitudes toward emerging technologies suggests that individuals negotiate between optimism about a technology’s potential and caution grounded in its risks, with risk perception potentially conditioning how benefit perceptions translate into policy preference (Bullock et al., 2025; Fischhoff et al., 1978). Applied",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"toward emerging technologies suggests that individuals negotiate between optimism about a technology’s potential and caution grounded in its risks, with risk perception potentially conditioning how benefit perceptions translate into policy preference (Bullock et al., 2025; Fischhoff et al., 1978). Applied here, ethical risk perception may not simply add to the predictive effect of perceived educational value on governance support but may instead condition it, strengthening or weakening the extent to which recognized benefit translates into governance endorsement. However, the specific direction and magnitude of this conditioning effect in the GenAI governance context have not been empirically established, and the theoretical predictions do not constrain the expected direction sufficiently to generate a directional hypothesis. Hypothesis 3 is therefore treated as exploratory (H3 Exploratory). H3 (Exploratory): Ethical Risk Perception moderates the relationship between Perceived Educational Value and Responsible AI Governance Support. 1.6 Students and instructors as key stakeholders Students and instructors occupy structurally distinct positions within higher education and may differ in how they perceive the educational value and ethical risks of GenAI. Research on AI-assisted writing indicates students and instructors may",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"Students and instructors as key stakeholders Students and instructors occupy structurally distinct positions within higher education and may differ in how they perceive the educational value and ethical risks of GenAI. Research on AI-assisted writing indicates students and instructors may place different emphasis on learning support, integrity, fairness, and oversight (Gallagher and Wagner, 2024; Mekheimer and Mahdy, 2025). Because the theoretical literature does not provide sufficiently clear directional predictions for Perceived Educational Value or AI Ethical Risk Perception, the following non-directional hypotheses are tested:H4. Students and instructors differ in Perceived Educational Value.H5. Students and instructors differ in AI Ethical Risk Perception. 1.7 Conceptual framework The conceptual framework guiding the present study, presented in Figure 1, links PEV and AERP to RAGS through three theorized paths. The first path (PEV to RAGS) reflects the value-driven account: stakeholders who recognize GenAI’s educational benefits support the governance structures that protect and sustain that value over time. The second path (AERP to RAGS) reflects the risk-driven account: awareness of bias, privacy, integrity, cognitive, and relational risks generates a direct demand for oversight, transparency, and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"benefits support the governance structures that protect and sustain that value over time. The second path (AERP to RAGS) reflects the risk-driven account: awareness of bias, privacy, integrity, cognitive, and relational risks generates a direct demand for oversight, transparency, and accountability mechanisms to mitigate those harms. The third path, the AERP × PEV interaction, tests the exploratory question of whether ethical risk perception conditions, rather than merely adding to, the effect of perceived value on governance endorsement. Together, these three paths position governance support as the potentially interactive product of two perceptual pathways not previously examined in combination in the GenAI-in-higher-education literature. Building on the theoretical frameworks of Institutional Legitimacy Theory (Suchman, 1995), Responsible Research and Innovation (Owen et al., 2012), and Protection Motivation Theory (Rogers, 1983), and building on this literature, the study addresses the following research questions and hypotheses: RQ1. What are the levels of perceived educational value, ethical risk perception, and responsible AI governance support among higher education stakeholders? RQ2. Do students and instructors differ in their perceptions of educational value and ethical risk perception? RQ3. To",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,5,"hypotheses: RQ1. What are the levels of perceived educational value, ethical risk perception, and responsible AI governance support among higher education stakeholders? RQ2. Do students and instructors differ in their perceptions of educational value and ethical risk perception? RQ3. To what extent does perceived educational value predict support for responsible AI governance? ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",61
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,6,"RQ4. To what extent does ethical risk perception predict support for responsible AI governance? RQ5. Does ethical risk perception moderate the relationship between perceived educational value and responsible AI governance support? RQ6. What themes emerge regarding the responsible implementation and governance of Generative AI in higher education assessment? The following hypotheses were formulated: H1. Perceived Educational Value is positively associated with Responsible AI Governance Support. H2. Ethical Risk Perception is positively associated with Responsible AI Governance Support. H3 (Exploratory). Ethical Risk Perception moderates the relationship between Perceived Educational Value and Responsible AI Governance Support. H4. Students and instructors differ in Perceived Educational Value. H5. Students and instructors differ in AI Ethical Risk Perception. The structural relationships specified in H1–H3 are illustrated in the conceptual framework presented in Figure 1. 2 Methodology 2.1 Research design This study used an embedded mixed-methods survey design. Quantitative Likert-scale data and qualitative open-ended responses were collected concurrently through a single online questionnaire. Quantitative analyses were conducted first, followed by codebook thematic analysis of the open-ended responses to contextualize and deepen interpretation of the quantitative findings.",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,6,"survey design. Quantitative Likert-scale data and qualitative open-ended responses were collected concurrently through a single online questionnaire. Quantitative analyses were conducted first, followed by codebook thematic analysis of the open-ended responses to contextualize and deepen interpretation of the quantitative findings. The quantitative component examined associations of PEV and AERP with RAGS and observed student–instructor patterns in PEV and AERP; the qualitative component examined participants’ experiential accounts, governance recommendations, and reasoning processes. Integration occurred at the interpretation stage, where qualitative themes were compared with the quantitative constructs and reported associations (see Section 3.7). 2.2 Participants The study included 192 participants from higher education institutions, comprising 125 students (65.1%) and 67 instructors (34.9%). Participants were recruited from both public and private higher education institutions in the United Arab Emirates (UAE). Participants were recruited through institutional email lists and direct outreach at two public universities and one private university. Eligibility required familiarity with GenAI tools in higher education through personal use, classroom exposure, or other interaction. The AI frequency-use item captured personal frequency of use as a demographic descriptor and was not used",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,6,"public universities and one private university. Eligibility required familiarity with GenAI tools in higher education through personal use, classroom exposure, or other interaction. The AI frequency-use item captured personal frequency of use as a demographic descriptor and was not used as a strict exclusion criterion (see Table 1). A purposive convenience sampling approach was employed; no probability sampling frame was available. Findings should therefore be interpreted as context- specific to the UAE higher education sector, and claims of FIGURE 1 Conceptual Framework of the Study. TABLE 1 Demographic characteristics of the participants (N = 192). Demographics (N; Percentage) Age 18–22 (149; 77.6%), 23–27 (16; 8.3%), 28–32 (6, 3.1%), 33–37 (5; 2.6%), 37 and above (16; 8.3%) Gender Male (59; 30.7%) & Female (133; 69.3%) Educational Institution Government (143; 74.5%) & Private (49; 25.5%) Participants Instructors (67; 34.9%) & Students (125; 65.1%) Major STEM (81; 42.2%) & Non-STEM (111; 57.8%) AI Frequency Use Very often and often (170; 88.5%), Sometimes (8; 4.2%), Never and Rarely (14; 7.3%) ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",175
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,6,"65.1%) Major STEM (81; 42.2%) & Non-STEM (111; 57.8%) AI Frequency Use Very often and often (170; 88.5%), Sometimes (8; 4.2%), Never and Rarely (14; 7.3%) ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",35
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"representativeness across broader national or international populations are not warranted. The sample was predominantly composed of participants aged 18–22 years (77.6%), reflecting the substantial representation of undergraduate students. Female participants constituted approximately two-thirds of the sample (69.3%), while most respondents were affiliated with government higher education institutions (74.5%), and a slight majority were enrolled in or teaching non-STEM disciplines (57.8%). Regarding nationality, the majority of participants were UAE nationals (n = 167, 87.0%), with the remaining 13.0% representing a range of nationalities, including Pakistani, Egyptian, Indian, Lebanese, Syrian, Sudanese, Zimbabwean, Italian, British, and American. Notably, 88.5% of participants reported using GenAI tools often or very often, and 7.3% reported never or rarely doing so. The AI frequency-use variable served as a demographic descriptor. Participants who reported rarely or never personally using GenAI were retained because eligibility also encompassed classroom exposure or other interaction with GenAI in higher education contexts. The predominance of participants aged 18– 22 (77.6%) and the unequal student-to-instructor ratio (125 vs. 67) should be considered when interpreting group comparison findings. 2.3 Measures Data were collected using a",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"or other interaction with GenAI in higher education contexts. The predominance of participants aged 18– 22 (77.6%) and the unequal student-to-instructor ratio (125 vs. 67) should be considered when interpreting group comparison findings. 2.3 Measures Data were collected using a structured questionnaire comprising three quantitative constructs and three open-ended questions. The full survey instrument, including participant eligibility criteria, demographic items, response anchors, construct measures, and open-ended questions, is provided in Supplementary Appendix A. The three quantitative instruments were developed de novo for this study, informed by established theoretical frameworks: the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM; Davis, 1989) and Innovation Diffusion Theory (Rogers, 2003) for PEV items, the psychometric risk perception paradigm (Slovic, 1987) for AERP items, and the international AI governance literature (OECD, 2019; UNESCO, 2021) for RAGS items. Following item generation, content validity was assessed by two specialists in educational technology who evaluated the instruments on four criteria: suitability for the study’s purpose, appropriate item-to-construct alignment, linguistic accuracy, and overall survey length. Based on expert feedback, two items were reworded to better align with the study’s theoretical constructs. To ensure",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"technology who evaluated the instruments on four criteria: suitability for the study’s purpose, appropriate item-to-construct alignment, linguistic accuracy, and overall survey length. Based on expert feedback, two items were reworded to better align with the study’s theoretical constructs. To ensure accessibility for Arabic-speaking participants, the survey was translated into Arabic and reviewed by the same experts prior to administration, confirming semantic equivalence. No formal pilot testing was conducted; expert review served as the primary content validation mechanism. The quantitative component used a five-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 5 = Strongly Agree). Perceived Educational Value (PEV) was measured using seven items examining beliefs regarding GenAI’s potential to enhance assessment quality, feedback processes, learning experiences, efficiency, and instructional effectiveness. PEV is conceptually distinct from the TAM construct of perceived usefulness in that it is specific to educational outcomes and institutional contexts rather than general task performance, and encompasses dimensions of instructional quality, feedback richness, and inclusivity that are not captured by generic usefulness items. AI Ethical Risk Perception (AERP) was assessed using seven items capturing concerns related to bias,",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"institutional contexts rather than general task performance, and encompasses dimensions of instructional quality, feedback richness, and inclusivity that are not captured by generic usefulness items. AI Ethical Risk Perception (AERP) was assessed using seven items capturing concerns related to bias, fairness, privacy, critical thinking, misuse, and the broader ethical implications of AI integration in educational assessment contexts. AERP is treated as a single reflective construct representing a general disposition toward ethical risk awareness; while its items span multiple ethical sub-domains, they share a common evaluative orientation toward potential harm and are theoretically grounded in the multidimensional character of AI risk perception as documented in the governance literature (Bullock et al., 2025; Slovic, 1987). Responsible AI Governance Support (RAGS) was measured using five items evaluating support for institutional policies, ethical guidelines, transparency mechanisms, monitoring procedures, and accountability frameworks governing GenAI use. RAGS is conceptually distinguished from related constructs as follows: unlike technology acceptance, which reflects willingness to use AI, RAGS reflects active endorsement of institutional oversight mechanisms; unlike institutional trust, which concerns confidence in institutions generally, RAGS concerns support for specific",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"RAGS is conceptually distinguished from related constructs as follows: unlike technology acceptance, which reflects willingness to use AI, RAGS reflects active endorsement of institutional oversight mechanisms; unlike institutional trust, which concerns confidence in institutions generally, RAGS concerns support for specific governance structures; and unlike policy compliance, which reflects adherence to existing rules, RAGS reflects normative endorsement of governance as desirable. The three open-ended questions explored participants’ experiences, concerns, and recommendations regarding GenAI implementation and governance in higher education assessment. 2.4 Procedure Ethical approval for the study was obtained from the institutional research ethics committee prior to data collection. Data collection was conducted during 2026. Participants were recruited from higher education institutions and voluntarily completed an online questionnaire, providing informed electronic consent after receiving information about the study’s purpose, confidentiality, and their right to withdraw. Participation was voluntary and no incentives were offered. Eligible participants were students and instructors with prior experience using or interacting with GenAI tools in higher education contexts. Data screening procedures included assessments of missing values, outliers, response patterns, and normality; outcomes are reported in Section 3.",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,7,"were offered. Eligible participants were students and instructors with prior experience using or interacting with GenAI tools in higher education contexts. Data screening procedures included assessments of missing values, outliers, response patterns, and normality; outcomes are reported in Section 3. To mitigate the risk of common method bias arising from the single-instrument, cross-sectional design, several procedural safeguards were applied. The three constructs used conceptually distinct item wordings and clearly labeled questionnaire sections to reduce construct conflation, and participant anonymity was assured to minimize socially desirable responding. The quantitative and qualitative items were collected from the same respondents in the same questionnaire and, therefore, do not provide a methodologically independent test of common method bias. The three-factor EFA structure was consistent with the proposed model but does not eliminate the possibility of shared-method effects. Common method bias remains a limitation of the cross-sectional self-report design (see Section 5.1). ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",156
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,8,"Quantitative data were analyzed using IBM SPSS Statistics (Version 31) and SmartPLS 4. Preliminary analyses included descriptive statistics, reliability assessment, and exploratory factor analysis (EFA) conducted as an exploratory step to examine the empirical factor structure prior to PLS-SEM using principal axis factoring with Promax rotation, with the Kaiser- Meyer-Olkin (KMO) measure and Bartlett’s test of sphericity used to evaluate factor analytic suitability. The EFA findings are treated as indicative; independent replication using confirmatory factor analysis with a separate sample is recommended in future research. PLS-SEM was selected over covariance-based SEM (CB-SEM) on the basis of four considerations: the study is in part exploratory, proposing a novel construct configuration (PEV, AERP, and RAGS) without prior validated precedents in this exact theoretical combination; the analytical aim is exploratory and explanatory, estimating associations within a newly specified construct configuration while examining explained variance in RAGS rather than conducting a strict confirmatory test based on global fit indices; the sample size of N = 192, while adequate for PLS-SEM, is below the threshold typically recommended for stable CB-SEM estimation with 19 indicators; and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,8,"variance in RAGS rather than conducting a strict confirmatory test based on global fit indices; the sample size of N = 192, while adequate for PLS-SEM, is below the threshold typically recommended for stable CB-SEM estimation with 19 indicators; and the data exhibited non-normal distributions (see Section 3.1), to which PLS-SEM is robust (Hair et al., 2021). A post-hoc power analysis was conducted following Hair et al. (2021): with N = 192, three predictors in the structural model, and an obtained R2 of.591 for RAGS, the achieved statistical power exceeds.99, confirming that the sample size is more than adequate for detecting the observed effect sizes. PLS-SEM was conducted to evaluate both the measurement and structural models, with reliability assessed via Cronbach’s alpha and composite reliability (ρc), convergent validity via average variance extracted (AVE), discriminant validity via the heterotrait-monotrait ratio (HTMT), collinearity assessed via variance inflation factors (VIF; reported in Table 2), and structural assessment via bootstrapping with 5,000 resamples. Standardized path coefficients (β), t-values, p-values, 95% confidence intervals, R2 values, and effect sizes (f2) were computed. The AERP × PEV",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,8,"collinearity assessed via variance inflation factors (VIF; reported in Table 2), and structural assessment via bootstrapping with 5,000 resamples. Standardized path coefficients (β), t-values, p-values, 95% confidence intervals, R2 values, and effect sizes (f2) were computed. The AERP × PEV interaction term was estimated using the product-indicator approach in SmartPLS 4. Measurement invariance across student and instructor groups was assessed using the three-step, permutation-based MICOM TABLE 2 Measurement model: outer loadings, collinearity, reliability, and validity indicators. Construct/Item λ VIF α ρc AVE HTMT: AERP HTMT: PEV AERP 0.831 0.871 0.499 AERP_1 0.580 1.547 AERP_2 0.681 1.789 AERP_3 0.631 1.485 AERP_4 0.646 1.357 AERP_5 0.777 1.800 AERP_6 0.777 1.814 AERP_7 0.796 1.780 PEV 0.872 0.898 0.559 0.365 PEV_1 0.800 2.091 PEV_2 0.802 2.365 PEV_3 0.757 2.028 PEV_4 0.608 1.624 PEV_5 0.703 1.633 PEV_6 0.785 1.983 PEV_7 0.761 1.662 RAGS 0.851 0.894 0.628 0.809 0.510 RAGS_1 0.807 1.853 RAGS_2 0.763 1.719 RAGS_3 0.809 1.934 RAGS_4 0.862 2.413 RAGS_5 0.714 1.506 AERP × PEV (interaction) 1.000 1.000 λ, outer loading; VIF, variance inflation factor; α, Cronbach’s alpha; ρc, composite reliability; AVE, average variance",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,8,"0.628 0.809 0.510 RAGS_1 0.807 1.853 RAGS_2 0.763 1.719 RAGS_3 0.809 1.934 RAGS_4 0.862 2.413 RAGS_5 0.714 1.506 AERP × PEV (interaction) 1.000 1.000 λ, outer loading; VIF, variance inflation factor; α, Cronbach’s alpha; ρc, composite reliability; AVE, average variance extracted; HTMT, heterotrait-monotrait ratio. Items with outer loadings below.70 are retained because all exceed the minimum acceptable threshold of.40, composite reliability exceeds.70 for all constructs, and theoretical justification supports the breadth of each construct’s item domain (Hair et al., 2021). All VIF values <3.3, indicating the absence of multicollinearity (Kock, 2015). HTMT values reported in the lower triangle. Bold values indicate the measurement model estimates retained and used for assessing indicator reliability, internal consistency reliability, convergent validity, discriminant validity, and collinearity. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",130
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"procedure with 5,000 permutations (Henseler et al., 2016; see Table 3). Because compositional invariance was not established for RAGS, no inferential student–instructor comparisons involving RAGS were reported. Independent-samples t-tests were conducted as preliminary exploratory analyses to examine student-instructor differences in construct-level means, with effect sizes (Cohen’s d) and 95% confidence intervals reported alongside significance tests. Qualitative responses were analyzed using codebook thematic analysis (Braun et al., 2019; Braun and Clarke, 2006), a structured variant of thematic analysis that employs a predetermined codebook to guide systematic and replicable coding across analysts. The codebook was developed deductively from the study’s theoretical constructs (PEV, AERP, RAGS) and research questions, and refined inductively during initial coding passes to capture participant-generated content not anticipated by the a priori framework. The unit of coding was the individual response; a response was coded to a theme if its content substantively addressed that theme, and multiple codes could be assigned to a single response. Disagreements between coders were resolved through structured discussion and consensus without adjudication by a third party. Given that the qualitative data derive from short",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"its content substantively addressed that theme, and multiple codes could be assigned to a single response. Disagreements between coders were resolved through structured discussion and consensus without adjudication by a third party. Given that the qualitative data derive from short open-ended survey responses rather than extended interviews, saturation claims should be interpreted conservatively; the declining code generation rate indicates analytic stability within the bounded corpus rather than saturation in a broader phenomenological sense. Of the 192 participants, 179 provided at least one substantive qualitative response across three questions: (Q1) overall thoughts on integrating GenAI into higher education assessments; (Q2) specific positive or negative experiences; and (Q3) recommended governance measures (see Supplementary Appendix A). Thirteen responses consisting solely of blank entries or non-substantive content were excluded. Theme-level frequency counts are reported in Table 4 to indicate relative salience; because individual responses were coded to multiple themes, the counts are not mutually exclusive, and the percentages sum to more than 100%. 3 Results 3.1 Preliminary data screening Prior to analysis, the dataset was screened to assess data quality. No missing values were",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"were coded to multiple themes, the counts are not mutually exclusive, and the percentages sum to more than 100%. 3 Results 3.1 Preliminary data screening Prior to analysis, the dataset was screened to assess data quality. No missing values were identified, all 192 responses were free of out-of-range values, and no problematic straight- lining patterns or univariate outliers were detected. Distributional characteristics were assessed using the Kolmogorov–Smirnov and Shapiro–Wilk tests, which were statistically significant for all three constructs (Table 5), though visual inspection of Q-Q plots indicated only minor deviations from normality (Figure 2). Given the sensitivity of formal normality tests to moderate sample sizes and the robustness of PLS-SEM to non-normal data, the distributions were considered acceptable for subsequent analyses (Hair et al., 2021). 3.2 Descriptive statistics Descriptive statistics were computed to examine participants’ perceptions across the three constructs. Overall, mean scores exceeded the midpoint of the scale across all three constructs, with RAGS receiving the highest overall rating (M = 3.68, SD = 0.74), followed closely by PEV (M = 3.66, SD = 0.78) and AERP (M =",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"Overall, mean scores exceeded the midpoint of the scale across all three constructs, with RAGS receiving the highest overall rating (M = 3.68, SD = 0.74), followed closely by PEV (M = 3.66, SD = 0.78) and AERP (M = 3.50, SD = 0.74), suggesting that participants recognized both the potential educational benefits and ethical challenges associated with the use of Generative AI in higher education. Within PEV, the strongest endorsement was for GenAI’s potential to enhance assessment effectiveness (M = 3.83, SD = 1.04) and reduce instructor workload through automated grading (M = 3.78, SD = 0.99), while trust in the accuracy of GenAI for evaluating student performance received the lowest rating (M = 3.23, SD = 1.10), indicating caution regarding AI use in high-stakes evaluative decisions. Within AERP, the highest- rated concern was excessive AI reliance diminishing students’ critical thinking skills (M = 3.76, SD = 1.04), followed by misuse (M = 3.67, SD = 0.99) and data privacy (M = 3.59, SD = 0.99), while concerns about bias perpetuation received comparatively lower but still above-midpoint ratings (M",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"critical thinking skills (M = 3.76, SD = 1.04), followed by misuse (M = 3.67, SD = 0.99) and data privacy (M = 3.59, SD = 0.99), while concerns about bias perpetuation received comparatively lower but still above-midpoint ratings (M = 3.12, SD = 1.13). For TABLE 3 Permutation-based MICOM: full three-step assessment. Assessment criterion AERP PEV RAGS Step 1: Configural invariance Established Established Established Step 2: Compositional invariance c (original) 0.995 0.99 0.992 p-value 0.494 0.373 0.027 Decision Supported Supported Not supported Step 3a: Mean equality Original difference 0.139 0.107 0.412 95% CI [−.281,.287] [−.310,.281] [−.316,.296] p-value 0.35 0.525 0.005 Decision Supported Supported Not supported Step 3b: Variance equality Original difference 0.285 0.305 −0.179 95% CI [−.575,.482] [−.737,.593] [−.548,.507] p-value 0.295 0.348 0.516 Decision Supported Supported Supported Overall Full invariance Full invariance No compositional invariance; RAGS group comparisons not interpreted Permutation-based MICOM with 5,000 permutations (Henseler et al., 2016). Step 1 configural invariance was established by convergent estimation of the measurement model with identical structure in both groups. Step 2: compositional invariance supported when p > .05. For Step",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,9,"interpreted Permutation-based MICOM with 5,000 permutations (Henseler et al., 2016). Step 1 configural invariance was established by convergent estimation of the measurement model with identical structure in both groups. Step 2: compositional invariance supported when p > .05. For Step 3, equality is supported when the permutation-based confidence interval includes the observed difference and p > .05. AERP and PEV demonstrated full measurement invariance across all three steps. RAGS did not establish compositional invariance in Step 2; therefore, composite-mean and structural-path comparisons involving RAGS were not interpreted. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",96
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"RAGS, the strongest support was for ensuring AI systems respect student diversity and individuality (M = 3.79, SD = 0.90), followed by clear assessment policies (M = 3.70, SD = 0.96) and regular monitoring of AI performance (M = 3.70, SD = 0.95). Observed group means are presented in Table 6. Differences in PEV and AERP were small, whereas the unadjusted RAGS mean was higher for instructors (M = 3.88, SD = 0.68) than for students (M = 3.58, SD = 0.75). Because compositional invariance was not established for RAGS, this observed RAGS difference is not interpreted as a confirmed latent group difference. These perceptions reflect participants’ self-reported views and should not be interpreted as objectively verified facts about GenAI’s capabilities or limitations. 3.3 Student-Instructor comparison of perceived educational value and ethical risk perception Independent-samples t-tests were conducted to examine observed mean differences in Perceived Educational Value and AI Ethical Risk Perception between students and instructors. As presented in Table 7, the results indicated no statistically significant difference in Perceived Educational Value, t (190) = 0.37, p = .711, d",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"observed mean differences in Perceived Educational Value and AI Ethical Risk Perception between students and instructors. As presented in Table 7, the results indicated no statistically significant difference in Perceived Educational Value, t (190) = 0.37, p = .711, d = 0.06, 95% CI [−.17,.25], or AI Ethical Risk Perception, t(190) = 0.42, p = .679, d = 0.06, 95% CI [−.19,.29]. Thus, H4 and H5 were not supported. These results TABLE 4 Inductive themes from participants’ open-ended responses. Theme Label and prevalence Description Illustrative quotation Central cluster — Responsible and conditional integration 1 Responsible integration as support rather than substitution Commonly reported (n = 58, 32.4%) Participants framed GenAI as useful when bounded by clear limits and human judgment, but not as a substitute for student thinking or instructor authority. “It should assist the assessment process, not direct it, for the contextual assessment can be compromised in this manner.” (Instructor, UAE) Enabling clusters — Educational value and governance support 2 Learning enhancement and personalised feedback Most frequently cited (n = 61, 34.1%) Participants described GenAI as helpful for understanding",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"for the contextual assessment can be compromised in this manner.” (Instructor, UAE) Enabling clusters — Educational value and governance support 2 Learning enhancement and personalised feedback Most frequently cited (n = 61, 34.1%) Participants described GenAI as helpful for understanding difficult material, receiving tailored feedback, generating practice questions, and supporting personalised learning. “It gives me personal feedback that suits me.” (Student, UAE) 3 Efficiency and assessment workflow support Recurring pattern (n = 25, 14.0%) Participants valued GenAI for brainstorming, organising ideas, editing language, summarising material, and reducing time on routine tasks. “It helped me brainstorm ideas, organise my thoughts, and improve the structure of my writing.” (Student, Pakistan) 4 Institutional governance: policy, training, transparency, and oversight Frequently cited (n = 59, 33.0%) Participants called for clear usage policies, AI-disclosure requirements, human review mechanisms, AI literacy training, and assessment redesign. “Universities should create clear rules for using GenAI, require students to state when they’ve used it, and redesign tasks to test real understanding.” (Student, UAE) Reflective clusters — Ethical and pedagogical risks 5 Reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy Noted by",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"should create clear rules for using GenAI, require students to state when they’ve used it, and redesign tasks to test real understanding.” (Student, UAE) Reflective clusters — Ethical and pedagogical risks 5 Reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy Noted by a substantial minority (n = 30, 16.8%) Participants questioned the accuracy and currency of AI outputs, raised concerns about bias in AI-assisted grading, and flagged risks to student data privacy. “AI should not be left to evaluate the duties of students; it may make a mistake in correction and cause uncontrolled bias.” (Student, UAE) 6 Academic integrity and misuse Noted by a substantial minority (n = 44, 24.6%) Participants associated GenAI with plagiarism, submission of AI-generated work, and the blurred boundary between legitimate assistance and academic dishonesty. “I have observed instances where students relied too heavily on AI tools, leading to a reduction in original thought and academic integrity issues.” (Instructor, UAE) 7 Over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning Noted by a substantial minority (n = 33, 18.4%) Participants warned that excessive AI dependence could erode independent reasoning, originality, and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"to a reduction in original thought and academic integrity issues.” (Instructor, UAE) 7 Over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning Noted by a substantial minority (n = 33, 18.4%) Participants warned that excessive AI dependence could erode independent reasoning, originality, and personal engagement with learning. “After I started using AI I started becoming lazier and much more dependent on AI rather than trying to work my brain.” (Student, UAE) Prevalence descriptors, counts, and percentages reflect the relative frequency of theme occurrence across the 179-response analytic corpus. Responses could be coded to multiple themes; counts and percentages are therefore not mutually exclusive. Quotations are verbatim or lightly edited for grammatical clarity in non-native English responses, with no alteration to meaning. All quotations are attributed by participant role and nationality. TABLE 5 Tests of normality. Construct Kolmogorov– Smirnova Shapiro–Wilk Statistic df Sig. Statistic df Sig. RAGS .129 192 <.001 .947 192 <.001 AERP .087 192 .001 .969 192 <.001 PEV .109 192 <.001 .927 192 <.001 PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception; RAGS, responsible AI governance support. aLilliefors Significance Correction.",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,10,"df Sig. RAGS .129 192 <.001 .947 192 <.001 AERP .087 192 .001 .969 192 <.001 PEV .109 192 <.001 .927 192 <.001 PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception; RAGS, responsible AI governance support. aLilliefors Significance Correction. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 10 frontiersin.org",49
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,11,"suggest that students and instructors reported broadly similar perceptions of GenAI’s educational value and ethical risks in this sample. Comparisons involving RAGS were not interpreted because RAGS did not establish compositional invariance across groups (see Section 3.3.1). 3.3.1 Measurement invariance assessment (MICOM) Measurement invariance was assessed using the three-step, permutation-based MICOM procedure (Henseler et al., 2016). Configural invariance was established because identical indicator sets, measurement specifications, structural paths, and algorithm settings were used for students and instructors. Compositional invariance was assessed using 5,000 permutations. It was supported for AERP (c = .995, p = .494) and PEV (c = .990, p = .373), indicating that these constructs were formed equivalently across groups. Compositional invariance was not supported for RAGS (c = .992, p = .027). For Step 3, mean equality was supported for AERP [observed difference = .139, 95% permutation CI (−.281,.287), p = .350] and PEV [observed difference = .107, 95% permutation CI (−.310,.281), p = .525]. Variance equality was supported for AERP (p = .295) and PEV (p = .348). The RAGS Step 3 results are reported in",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,11,"CI (−.281,.287), p = .350] and PEV [observed difference = .107, 95% permutation CI (−.310,.281), p = .525]. Variance equality was supported for AERP (p = .295) and PEV (p = .348). The RAGS Step 3 results are reported in Table 3 for transparency; however, they are not used to establish partial or full invariance because compositional invariance was not achieved in Step 2. In summary, AERP and PEV demonstrated full measurement invariance across students and instructors. RAGS did not establish compositional invariance (See Table 3). Therefore, RAGS composite-mean comparisons and structural-path comparisons ending in RAGS were not interpreted. 3.4 Reliability and measurement model assessment The reliability and validity of the measurement instrument were evaluated through internal consistency analysis, exploratory factor analysis, and PLS-SEM. The reliability analysis, as shown in Tables 2, 8, demonstrated satisfactory internal consistency across all study constructs. Cronbach’s alpha coefficients exceeded the recommended threshold of.70 across all constructs, ranging from.831 to.872: PEV exhibited a coefficient of.872, AERP.831, and RAGS.851. Collectively, these findings provide evidence of satisfactory internal consistency across all constructs. Outer loadings for all 19",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,11,"constructs. Cronbach’s alpha coefficients exceeded the recommended threshold of.70 across all constructs, ranging from.831 to.872: PEV exhibited a coefficient of.872, AERP.831, and RAGS.851. Collectively, these findings provide evidence of satisfactory internal consistency across all constructs. Outer loadings for all 19 measurement items are reported in Table 2. All RAGS items exceeded the recommended threshold of.70 (range:.714 to.862). For PEV, six of seven items exceeded.70 (range:.703 to.802); PEV_4 (trust in GenAI accuracy, λ = .608) was below this threshold but above the minimum acceptable value of.40 (Hair et al., 2021). This item was retained because it captures a conceptually distinct dimension, evaluative accuracy trust, not represented by the remaining PEV items, and because the overall PEV composite reliability (.898) and AVE (.559) remained within acceptable bounds. For AERP, three items exceeded.70 (AERP_5 = .777, AERP_6 = .777, AERP_7 = .796), while four items fell below this threshold (range:.580 to.681). All four exceed the minimum threshold of.40. The lower-loading items correspond specifically to structural-level ethical risks, algorithmic bias, fairness for diverse learners, and perpetuation of existing biases, which operate through design and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,11,"four items fell below this threshold (range:.580 to.681). All four exceed the minimum threshold of.40. The lower-loading items correspond specifically to structural-level ethical risks, algorithmic bias, fairness for diverse learners, and perpetuation of existing biases, which operate through design and training mechanisms less visible in daily use than the experiential risks captured by the higher-loading items. This loading pattern reflects the multidimensional character of AI ethical risk perception as a construct spanning both structural and experiential risk domains (Slovic, 1987) rather than poor measurement. All items were retained because composite reliability (.871) remained above.70, and conceptual breadth would be compromised by their removal. All VIF values ranged from 1.357 to 2.413, well below the threshold of 3.3 (Kock, 2015), confirming the absence of multicollinearity among indicators. Prior to conducting the structural model analysis, exploratory factor analysis was performed as an exploratory step using Principal Axis Factoring with Promax rotation. The KMO measure of sampling adequacy was.890, exceeding the recommended threshold of.60, while Bartlett’s Test of Sphericity was statistically significant, χ2(171) = 1781.59, p < .001, confirming suitability for factor analysis.",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,11,"exploratory step using Principal Axis Factoring with Promax rotation. The KMO measure of sampling adequacy was.890, exceeding the recommended threshold of.60, while Bartlett’s Test of Sphericity was statistically significant, χ2(171) = 1781.59, p < .001, confirming suitability for factor analysis. The three-factor FIGURE 2 Normal Q–Q plots for Perceived Educational Value (PEV), AI Ethical Risk Perception (AERP), and Responsible AI Governance Support (RAGS). TABLE 6 Construct-Level Descriptive Statistics by Participant Role and Overall. Construct Overall M (SD) Instructors M (SD) Students M (SD) PEV 3.66 (0.78) 3.69 (0.85) 3.65 (0.74) AERP 3.5 (0.74) 3.53 (0.82) 3.48 (0.69) RAGS 3.68 (0.74) 3.88 (0.68) 3.58 (0.75) PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception; RAGS, responsible AI governance support. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",127
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"solution explained 52.05% of the total variance. The PEV items loaded strongly onto a distinct factor, whereas some overlap was observed between the AERP and RAGS constructs, suggesting conceptual proximity between ethical risk perceptions and governance-related considerations. This proximity is theoretically expected given the risk-driven governance pathway proposed in Section 1.5.2, and discriminant validity was subsequently confirmed through HTMT ratios below the recommended threshold of.85 for all construct pairs (Table 2). Nevertheless, the overall factor structure was consistent with the proposed theoretical framework, and all items were retained for subsequent analyses. The measurement model was subsequently assessed using PLS- SEM. Composite reliability (ρc) values ranged from.871 to.898, exceeding the recommended threshold of.70. Convergent validity was assessed via AVE: values for PEV (.559) and RAGS (.628) exceeded the recommended threshold of.50, while the AVE for AERP (.499) fell marginally below the criterion. The marginal AVE for AERP is acknowledged as a convergent validity limitation. Its retention is justified on three grounds: composite reliability (.871) exceeds the recommended threshold; the loading pattern reflects genuine construct heterogeneity across structural and experiential risk dimensions",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"criterion. The marginal AVE for AERP is acknowledged as a convergent validity limitation. Its retention is justified on three grounds: composite reliability (.871) exceeds the recommended threshold; the loading pattern reflects genuine construct heterogeneity across structural and experiential risk dimensions rather than random error; and the HTMT ratio between AERP and all other constructs remains below.85, confirming discriminant validity. Readers should interpret AERP- related findings with awareness of this limitation. All HTMT values ranged from.365 to.809, remaining below the threshold of.85 and indicating satisfactory discriminant validity. Model fit was assessed using the standardized root mean square residual (SRMR) and normed fit index (NFI). The SRMR was.089, remaining below the recommended threshold of.10 and indicating acceptable model fit. The NFI was.765. The NFI value falls below the conventional.90 threshold typically applied in CB-SEM contexts. In PLS-SEM, NFI is regarded as a supplementary rather than primary fit indicator, and Hair et al., 2021 caution against over-reliance on global fit indices in PLS-SEM frameworks, where predictive accuracy and construct validity are the primary evaluation criteria. Given the satisfactory SRMR, strong composite reliabilities, acceptable",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"supplementary rather than primary fit indicator, and Hair et al., 2021 caution against over-reliance on global fit indices in PLS-SEM frameworks, where predictive accuracy and construct validity are the primary evaluation criteria. Given the satisfactory SRMR, strong composite reliabilities, acceptable AVE values, and confirmed discriminant validity, the overall measurement model is considered adequate for structural assessment. Overall, the results provide evidence of satisfactory reliability, convergent validity, discriminant validity, and measurement quality, supporting the use of the proposed constructs in the subsequent structural model assessment. 3.5 Structural model assessment The structural model was evaluated using PLS-SEM with 5,000 bootstrap resamples. The model accounted for 59.1% of the variance in RAGS (R2 = .591, adjusted R2 = .585). This explanatory power should be interpreted as association-based rather than causal, consistent with the cross-sectional self-report design. As shown in Table 9, PEV was positively associated with RAGS [β = .255, t = 3.842, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.117,.377)], supporting H1. AERP was also positively associated with RAGS [β = .627, t = 9.516, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.485,.748)], supporting",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"positively associated with RAGS [β = .255, t = 3.842, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.117,.377)], supporting H1. AERP was also positively associated with RAGS [β = .627, t = 9.516, p < .001, 95% bias-corrected CI (.485,.748)], supporting H2. The AERP association was larger than the PEV association in this sample. The AERP × PEV interaction was not statistically supported [β = −.030, t = .897, p = .370, 95% bias-corrected CI (−.098,.037)]. Accordingly, H3 was not supported. The present data do not provide evidence that AERP moderated the association between PEV and RAGS. TABLE 7 Independent-Samples t-Test Comparing Students and Instructors on perceived educational value and AI ethical risk perception. Construct Students M (SD) Instructors M (SD) t p Cohen’s d 95% CI (difference) PEV 3.65 (0.74) 3.69 (0.85) 0.37 0.711 0.06 [−.17,.25] AERP 3.48 (0.69) 3.53 (0.82) 0.42 0.679 0.06 [−.19,.29] PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception. Mean differences represent Instructors minus Students. Confidence intervals were computed from the reported t statistics and standard errors. Both PEV and AERP demonstrated full measurement invariance",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"(0.82) 0.42 0.679 0.06 [−.19,.29] PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception. Mean differences represent Instructors minus Students. Confidence intervals were computed from the reported t statistics and standard errors. Both PEV and AERP demonstrated full measurement invariance across student and instructor groups (see Section 3.3.1). TABLE 8 Cross-loadings assessment of item-level discriminant validity. Item AERP PEV RAGS AERP_1 0.58 0.249 0.322 AERP_2 0.681 0.339 0.352 AERP_3 0.631 0.274 0.356 AERP_4 0.646 0.106 0.467 AERP_5 0.777 0.231 0.601 AERP_6 0.777 0.177 0.565 AERP_7 0.796 0.267 0.704 PEV_1 0.268 0.8 0.412 PEV_2 0.171 0.802 0.305 PEV_3 0.309 0.757 0.354 PEV_4 0.024 0.608 0.104 PEV_5 0.253 0.703 0.31 PEV_6 0.123 0.785 0.341 PEV_7 0.349 0.761 0.467 RAGS_1 0.578 0.461 0.807 RAGS_2 0.516 0.376 0.763 RAGS_3 0.603 0.414 0.809 RAGS_4 0.647 0.323 0.862 RAGS_5 0.509 0.298 0.714 Bold values indicate primary construct loadings. All items load highest on their designated construct, supporting item-level discriminant validity (Hair et al., 2021). The interaction term (AERP × PEV) is omitted from this table as it is a single-indicator term. AERP_5, AERP_6, and AERP_7 show",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,12,"construct loadings. All items load highest on their designated construct, supporting item-level discriminant validity (Hair et al., 2021). The interaction term (AERP × PEV) is omitted from this table as it is a single-indicator term. AERP_5, AERP_6, and AERP_7 show elevated cross-loadings onto RAGS (.565–.704), consistent with the theorised AERP → RAGS pathway (H2): experiential risks, privacy violation, critical thinking loss, and misuse, generate the most direct demand for governance structures capable of mitigating them. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",84
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"3.6 Qualitative findings: thematic analysis of open-ended responses 3.6.1 Analytic approach and methodological rigor Responses to three open-ended questions were analyzed using codebook thematic analysis (Braun et al., 2019; Braun and Clarke, 2006), a structured approach to thematic analysis that uses a predetermined codebook to guide systematic and replicable coding across analysts. The codebook was developed deductively from the study’s theoretical constructs (PEV, AERP, and RAGS) and the six research questions, establishing an initial set of codes anchored in the conceptual framework. The codebook was subsequently refined inductively through an iterative review of the data corpus to incorporate participant-generated content not anticipated by the a priori framework. The unit of coding was the individual response; a response was assigned to a theme when its content substantively addressed that theme, and multiple themes could be assigned to a single response. Coding was conducted independently by two members of the research team using the shared codebook described above. Inter- rater reliability was assessed on a stratified random subsample of 40 responses (approximately 22% of the corpus), yielding Cohen’s kappa of κ =",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"was conducted independently by two members of the research team using the shared codebook described above. Inter- rater reliability was assessed on a stratified random subsample of 40 responses (approximately 22% of the corpus), yielding Cohen’s kappa of κ = .83, indicating strong agreement (Landis and Koch, 1977). Discrepancies were resolved through discussion and consensus. Analytic stability within the bounded response corpus was assessed by mapping new codes across successive batches of 20 responses; the rate of new code generation declined markedly after the first 80 responses and reached zero after response 110. Of the 192 participants, 179 provided at least one substantive qualitative response across three questions: (Q1) overall thoughts on integrating GenAI into higher education assessments; (Q2) specific positive or negative experiences; and (Q3) recommended governance measures. Thirteen responses consisting solely of blank entries or non-substantive content were excluded, yielding 179 responses for analysis. Theme-level frequency counts and percentages are reported in Table 4 to indicate relative prevalence and facilitate quantitative-qualitative triangulation. Counts reflect the number of responses coded to each theme; they are not standalone inferential statistics",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"excluded, yielding 179 responses for analysis. Theme-level frequency counts and percentages are reported in Table 4 to indicate relative prevalence and facilitate quantitative-qualitative triangulation. Counts reflect the number of responses coded to each theme; they are not standalone inferential statistics and should be interpreted alongside the thematic descriptions and participant quotations in Section 3.6. Because individual responses were coded to multiple themes, counts are not mutually exclusive. Percentages, therefore, sum to more than 100% of the 179-response corpus. The analysis produced seven themes organized into three interrelated clusters: a central cluster encompassing the cross- cutting orientation toward responsible integration as support rather than substitution (Theme 1); an enabling cluster capturing learning enhancement and personalized feedback (Theme 2), efficiency and assessment workflow support (Theme 3), and institutional governance (Theme 4); and reflective clusters capturing reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy (Theme 5), academic integrity and misuse (Theme 6), and over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning (Theme 7). This structure directly addresses RQ6 and is integrated with the quantitative findings in Section 3.7. 3.6.2 Central theme: responsible integration as support rather than",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"5), academic integrity and misuse (Theme 6), and over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning (Theme 7). This structure directly addresses RQ6 and is integrated with the quantitative findings in Section 3.7. 3.6.2 Central theme: responsible integration as support rather than substitution Theme 1 functioned as a cross-cutting orientation through which participants across roles, nationalities, and institutional types consistently positioned themselves as conditional supporters of GenAI, endorsing its educational potential while insisting on clear boundaries, human judgment, and institutional accountability. This framing helps explain why participants simultaneously reported above-midpoint scores on both PEV and AERP: the two constructs are not contradictory but co-constitutive, jointly producing the governance demand captured in RAGS. 3.6.2.1 Bounded utility The most prevalent formulation positioned GenAI as a tool that should assist rather than replace, with participants drawing an explicit boundary between augmentation and substitution: “It should assist the assessment process, not direct it, for the contextual assessment can be compromised in this manner.” (Instructor, UAE) “GenAI can be very useful if it’s done responsibly… as a learning aid, not as a shortcut.” (Student, UAE) “I think",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"assist the assessment process, not direct it, for the contextual assessment can be compromised in this manner.” (Instructor, UAE) “GenAI can be very useful if it’s done responsibly… as a learning aid, not as a shortcut.” (Student, UAE) “I think GenAI is a great tool when used correctly. It should be a support, not a replacement for thinking. The moment students stop thinking for themselves, education loses its purpose.” (Student, UAE) 3.6.2.2 Task-specific role boundaries Instructors in particular drew explicit distinctions between permissible and impermissible use cases, articulating a graduated model of acceptable AI assistance: TABLE 9 Structural Model Results. H# Path β t p 95% CI f² Decision H1 PEV → RAGS .255 3.842 <.001 [.117,.377] .131 Supported H2 AERP → RAGS .627 9.516 <.001 [.485,.748] .836 Supported H3 AERP × PEV → RAGS −0.030 .897 .370 [−.098,.037] .006 Not Supported PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception; RAGS, responsible AI governance support; β, standardised path coefficient; f2, effect size. 95% bias-corrected confidence intervals were derived from bootstrapping with 5,000 resamples. Path coefficients reflect statistical associations in",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,13,"Not Supported PEV, perceived educational value; AERP, AI ethical risk perception; RAGS, responsible AI governance support; β, standardised path coefficient; f2, effect size. 95% bias-corrected confidence intervals were derived from bootstrapping with 5,000 resamples. Path coefficients reflect statistical associations in a cross-sectional sample and should not be interpreted as causal effects. R2 = .591, Adjusted R2 = .585. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",67
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,14,"“I believe we should allow students and teachers to use it only for specific tasks (e.g., rephrasing, generating ideas) but not others (e.g., writing an essay, reading student work).” (Instructor, Italy) “GenAI should be used in combination with traditional assessment methods.” (Student, Pakistan) 3.6.2.3 Conditionality as a governance rationale Several participants explicitly linked conditional endorsement to the need for formal governance structures, framing rules not as restrictions but as enabling conditions: “It is a powerful tool, but like all powerful tools it needs regulation—not to stop people using it, but to make sure it is used in a way that still develops the student.” (Instructor, South Africa) 3.6.3 Enabling themes: educational value and governance supports The three enabling value themes (Themes 2–4) captured learning enhancement and personalized feedback, efficiency and assessment workflow support, and institutional governance. Learning enhancement and personalized feedback was the most frequently cited theme (Theme 2, n = 61, 34.1%), institutional governance was also frequently cited (Theme 4, n = 59, 33.0%), and efficiency and assessment workflow support was less prevalent (Theme 3, n = 25, 14.0%).",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,14,"feedback was the most frequently cited theme (Theme 2, n = 61, 34.1%), institutional governance was also frequently cited (Theme 4, n = 59, 33.0%), and efficiency and assessment workflow support was less prevalent (Theme 3, n = 25, 14.0%). Together, these accounts presented GenAI as a formative and supportive tool, consistent with the above-midpoint PEV scores in the quantitative phase (M = 3.66, SD = 0.78), rather than on autonomous assessment authority. 3.6.3.1 Learning enhancement and personalized feedback (theme 2) The most frequently cited positive experience across both student and instructor subgroups was GenAI’s capacity to explain material that formal instruction had not adequately resolved: “I had AI explain an assignment that I got multiple explanations from the professor but still didn’t get it. When I got the breakdown from AI it was easier to understand.” (Student, UAE) “GenAI can provide instant, tailored feedback to students, helping them understand mistakes and improve learning outcomes.” (Instructor, UAE) 3.6.3.2 Efficiency and assessment workflow support (theme 3) Participants, particularly students, described GenAI as an aid to organizing ideas and polishing writing rather",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,14,"can provide instant, tailored feedback to students, helping them understand mistakes and improve learning outcomes.” (Instructor, UAE) 3.6.3.2 Efficiency and assessment workflow support (theme 3) Participants, particularly students, described GenAI as an aid to organizing ideas and polishing writing rather than a generator of final answers, reflecting the augmentation framing of Theme 1: “I have only used it to gather ideas and get some more academic vocabulary to use.” (Student, UAE) “I have found GenAI tools like automatic grading systems extremely helpful in saving time, especially for large classes, allowing teachers to focus more on interactive teaching.” (Instructor, UAE) Theme 4, institutional governance: policy, training, transparency, and oversight theme, was the second most frequently cited and directly addresses RQ6. Participants called for clear usage policies, AI-disclosure requirements analogous to citation practice, AI literacy training, human review of AI- assisted decisions, and redesigned assessments that foreground process, oral defense, and personal reflection. These demands were voiced with notable consistency across roles and institutional contexts: “There must be institutional frameworks that guide use of AI. AI must be used transparently and fairly.”",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,14,"and redesigned assessments that foreground process, oral defense, and personal reflection. These demands were voiced with notable consistency across roles and institutional contexts: “There must be institutional frameworks that guide use of AI. AI must be used transparently and fairly.” (Instructor, South Africa) “Encourage students to disclose when and how they have used GenAI in their work. This can normalise ethical usage and open conversations about best practices.” (Student, Pakistan) “Use formats that emphasise process, critical thinking, and originality, for example, in-class essays, oral exams, or scaffolded projects.” (Instructor, UAE) 3.6.4 Reflective themes: ethical risk, integrity, and human learning The three reflective themes (Themes 5–7) capture what participants feared could be lost or distorted by unconstrained GenAI integration and provide qualitative grounding for the AERP construct and the dominant AERP-RAGS pathway. 3.6.4.1 Reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy (theme 5) Participants described concerns about inaccurate outputs, fabricated academic sources, algorithmic bias, and data privacy in AI-assisted grading: “I tried using AI to provide scientific articles for an assessment; however, the articles it generated didn’t even exist.” (Student, UAE) “AI is",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,14,"Participants described concerns about inaccurate outputs, fabricated academic sources, algorithmic bias, and data privacy in AI-assisted grading: “I tried using AI to provide scientific articles for an assessment; however, the articles it generated didn’t even exist.” (Student, UAE) “AI is just a typical database; it collects information from every available source, some of which are not accurate, so the results generated are approximately 40% inaccurate.” (Student, UAE) Data privacy concerns were raised by participants in the analytic corpus: ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 14 frontiersin.org",87
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,15,"“Sensitive information and details can be anonymized or encrypted, similar to what is practiced in the financial and banking industry.” (Instructor, UAE) “Student data used by GenAI platforms should be handled with strict privacy protections and comply with relevant regulations (e.g., GDPR, FERPA).” (Student, Pakistan) 3.6.4.2 Academic integrity and misuse (theme 6) Participants described a range of integrity-related concerns, from straightforward plagiarism to homogenized reflective submissions and the institutional harm caused by overreaching detection tools: “I have observed instances where students relied too heavily on AI tools, leading to a reduction in original thought and academic integrity issues, such as plagiarism or submitting AI-generated content as their own.” (Instructor, UAE) “Students have been flagged and reported for academic misconduct simply because a detection tool believes AI was used, even when it was not. There is essentially no way to prove our innocence.” (Student, USA) “A postgraduate student’s thesis containing raw data was shared on AI before the thesis was accepted. A thesis external examiner used AI to mark a doctoral thesis, which was very unfair as the candidate did not",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,15,"our innocence.” (Student, USA) “A postgraduate student’s thesis containing raw data was shared on AI before the thesis was accepted. A thesis external examiner used AI to mark a doctoral thesis, which was very unfair as the candidate did not use AI.” (Instructor, South Africa) 3.6.4.3 Over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning (theme 7) Students reflected candidly on observable changes in their own cognitive habits, framing AI dependence as gradual and self- reinforcing: “After I started using AI I started becoming lazier and much more dependent on AI rather than trying to work my brain.” (Student, UAE) “It helps me in educational purposes, but side by side, our real intention is getting weaker day by day.” (Student, Pakistan) Instructors framed the same concern in terms of assessment validity, noting that AI-mediated outputs cannot serve as evidence of students’ own reasoning: “The use of content production worries me a lot, especially in assessments that ask for reflection; AI cannot generate that, as we are not all the same.” (Instructor, UAE) 3.6.5 Thematic map Figure 3 presents an interpretive thematic map of",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,15,"“The use of content production worries me a lot, especially in assessments that ask for reflection; AI cannot generate that, as we are not all the same.” (Instructor, UAE) 3.6.5 Thematic map Figure 3 presents an interpretive thematic map of the seven qualitative themes and three thematic clusters. Theme 1 (responsible integration as support rather than substitution) is positioned as a cross-cutting orientation. Themes 2 and 3 represent enabling educational-value themes, theme 4 represents governance expectations, and themes 5–7 represent reflective, ethical, and pedagogical concerns. The map displays conceptual relationships identified in the qualitative analysis; it does not represent PLS-SEM structural paths, map individual themes to path coefficients, or assign quantitative weight to qualitative themes. 3.7 Integrated results summary The quantitative and qualitative findings address the study research questions and provide a coherent account of responsible AI governance support in higher education. Participants reported above-midpoint levels across all three constructs, with RAGS receiving the highest mean (M = 3.68), followed by PEV (M = 3.66) and AERP (M = 3.50) (RQ1). Students and instructors did not differ significantly in PEV",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,15,"higher education. Participants reported above-midpoint levels across all three constructs, with RAGS receiving the highest mean (M = 3.68), followed by PEV (M = 3.66) and AERP (M = 3.50) (RQ1). Students and instructors did not differ significantly in PEV (H4 not supported) or AERP (H5 not supported) (RQ2). PEV and AERP were positively associated with RAGS, with AERP showing the larger association (RQ3 and RQ4). The interaction term was not statistically supported (H3 not supported; RQ5). Although RAGS had a higher observed mean among instructors, compositional invariance for RAGS was not established; therefore, student–instructor comparisons involving RAGS were not interpreted. Thematic analysis identified seven themes across three clusters. The central theme was responsible integration as support rather than substitution (Theme 1). The enabling cluster comprised learning enhancement and personalized feedback (Theme 2), efficiency and assessment workflow support (Theme 3), and institutional governance (Theme 4), while the reflective cluster comprised reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy (Theme 5), academic integrity and misuse (Theme 6), and over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning (Theme 7) (RQ6; see Table 4). 4 Discussion This",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,15,"and institutional governance (Theme 4), while the reflective cluster comprised reliability, accuracy, bias, and data privacy (Theme 5), academic integrity and misuse (Theme 6), and over-reliance, critical thinking, and human learning (Theme 7) (RQ6; see Table 4). 4 Discussion This study examined the factors associated with responsible AI governance support among higher education stakeholders, focusing on perceived educational value and ethical risk perception. The findings are discussed below in relation to each research question, with qualitative evidence integrated throughout. 4.1 Stakeholder perceptions of educational value, ethical risk, and governance support (RQ1) The pattern of stakeholder perceptions offers a useful starting point for interpreting how GenAI is understood in higher education. Higher education stakeholders emerged as neither uncritical adopters nor opponents of GenAI: they recognized its educational potential, acknowledged its ethical risks, and ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 15 frontiersin.org",141
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,16,"expressed support for governance mechanisms. That institutional oversight was supported alongside perceived value and ethical concern suggests that participants may view governance as a condition for responsible adoption rather than only as a constraint on innovation, consistent with the broader literature’s framing of accountability structures as enabling rather than restricting innovation (Soko, 2025). This pattern can be interpreted through Institutional Legitimacy Theory (Suchman, 1995), which emphasizes the value of structures that align practice with shared norms, and through Protection Motivation Theory (PMT; Rogers, 1983), which provides a possible explanation for why perceived threats may be associated with support for protective institutional responses. Educational value perceptions revealed a principled boundary between AI as a supportive aid and AI as a substitute for professional pedagogical judgment (Torres, 2025; Waring and Evans, 2024), suggesting that participants differentiated between supportive applications and high-stakes evaluative uses. This distinction matters for governance design: high-stakes evaluative decisions require particular attention to oversight, human review, transparency, and opportunities to question or contest AI-assisted decisions. Within ethical risk perceptions, concern about the erosion of critical thinking due to AI",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,16,"uses. This distinction matters for governance design: high-stakes evaluative decisions require particular attention to oversight, human review, transparency, and opportunities to question or contest AI-assisted decisions. Within ethical risk perceptions, concern about the erosion of critical thinking due to AI dependence was especially prominent. One possible explanation is a proximity effect in risk perception: cognitive dependence is an observable, daily experience for both students and instructors, whereas algorithmic bias operates through structural mechanisms embedded in design processes and training data that are invisible at the point of use (Lockwood and Brown, 2026). This interpretation is consistent with the psychometric risk-perception tradition, which highlights the importance of perceived familiarity, controllability, and consequences in shaping responses to risk (Slovic, 1987). It should, however, be treated as an interpretation rather than as a mechanism established by the present study. The emphasis on diversity and equity within governance support suggests that participants may view AI governance as more than procedural compliance. Their responses are consistent with the protection of educational rights and with international AI-governance principles concerning fairness, non-discrimination, accountability, privacy, and human oversight",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,16,"and equity within governance support suggests that participants may view AI governance as more than procedural compliance. Their responses are consistent with the protection of educational rights and with international AI-governance principles concerning fairness, non-discrimination, accountability, privacy, and human oversight (OECD, 2019; UNESCO, 2021). This alignment should be interpreted as context-specific rather than as evidence that all higher education stakeholders hold the same FIGURE 3 Thematic map of qualitative findings. 1 (centre) represents the cross-cutting orientation toward responsible, conditional integration. Enabling clusters (top) capture educational value and governance expectations. Reflective clusters (bottom) capture ethical and pedagogical risks. Arrows indicate conceptual correspondence between qualitative themes and quantitative construct clusters; arrow widths are uniform and do not represent quantitative weighting or empirically derived effect sizes. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 16 frontiersin.org",132
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"priorities (Komarudin, 2025; Pasupuleti, 2024; Rotenberg, 2024). 4.2 Student–instructor comparisons of educational value and ethical risk perception (RQ2) The convergence of students and instructors on educational value and ethical risk perceptions, despite their structurally distinct institutional positions, is notable. Existing literature often portrays these groups as emphasizing different aspects of educational technology, with students prioritizing learning utility and instructors prioritizing integrity, oversight, and fairness (Gallagher and Wagner, 2024; Mekheimer and Mahdy, 2025). However, convergent perceptions across role boundaries have been documented elsewhere: Ramli et al. (2025) found broadly similar performance and effort expectancy patterns across student groups varying in prior technology experience, and Dabirian and Swarat (2024) found that both students and faculty recognized AI’s potential while sharing underlying concerns about reliability. The present findings are therefore consistent with the possibility that broad perceptions of educational value and ethical risk can overlap across stakeholder roles, even where particular uses of AI may be experienced differently. One possible explanation is that familiarity with GenAI may reduce novelty effects and encourage more practice-based judgments about both its benefits and its risks (Torres,",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"can overlap across stakeholder roles, even where particular uses of AI may be experienced differently. One possible explanation is that familiarity with GenAI may reduce novelty effects and encourage more practice-based judgments about both its benefits and its risks (Torres, 2025). A second explanation is that perceived educational value and ethical risk are broad constructs that may transcend role. Recognizing the potential for GenAI to support feedback or recognizing risks to academic integrity does not necessarily require a role-specific position. These explanations are tentative and should be examined using larger and more diverse samples. No conclusion is drawn about differences in governance support or in the structural associations involving governance support across students and instructors. The RAGS measure did not establish compositional invariance across the two groups; consequently, observed RAGS differences and structural comparisons ending in RAGS cannot be interpreted as valid evidence of role-based differences. Future research should refine and validate the RAGS measure across stakeholder roles before testing whether governance-support patterns differ between students, instructors, administrators, and policymakers. 4.3 Perceived educational value as a predictor of governance support",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"as valid evidence of role-based differences. Future research should refine and validate the RAGS measure across stakeholder roles before testing whether governance-support patterns differ between students, instructors, administrators, and policymakers. 4.3 Perceived educational value as a predictor of governance support (RQ3) The finding that perceived educational value was positively associated with governance support extends the literature in a theoretically relevant direction. Prior research has established that perceived usefulness shapes adoption intentions within TAM and IDT frameworks (Haq et al., 2026; Ramli et al., 2025). The present findings suggest that perceived educational value may also be relevant to governance endorsement, a conceptually distinct outcome. Whereas technology acceptance concerns willingness to use AI, governance support concerns endorsement of the institutional conditions that shape how AI is used. The association observed here is therefore consistent with the possibility that stakeholders who recognize educational value may also recognize the importance of responsible implementation. This is consistent with Institutional Legitimacy Theory (Suchman, 1995) and Responsible Research and Innovation (Owen et al., 2012), both of which frame governance as a means of aligning innovation with legitimate",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"value may also recognize the importance of responsible implementation. This is consistent with Institutional Legitimacy Theory (Suchman, 1995) and Responsible Research and Innovation (Owen et al., 2012), both of which frame governance as a means of aligning innovation with legitimate institutional expectations and societal values. A plausible explanation is that stakeholders who identify educational benefits in GenAI may also be attentive to the conditions under which those benefits can be realized. In assessment settings, unclear rules, inaccurate outputs, unequal access, or inappropriate substitution of human judgment may undermine perceived value. Governance may therefore be seen as a way to support transparent, accountable, and educationally appropriate implementation (Ahmad et al., 2024; Soko, 2025). The present cross-sectional findings do not establish that perceived value causes governance support, but they indicate that these orientations are related in this sample. 4.4 Ethical risk perception as a predictor of governance support (RQ4) Ethical risk perception showed the larger association with governance support, relative to perceived educational value, in this sample. This pattern is consistent with the possibility that awareness of harm is closely related to",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"as a predictor of governance support (RQ4) Ethical risk perception showed the larger association with governance support, relative to perceived educational value, in this sample. This pattern is consistent with the possibility that awareness of harm is closely related to support for institutional oversight; however, the cross-sectional design does not establish a causal mechanism (Ahmad et al., 2024; Pasupuleti, 2025). Protection Motivation Theory (Rogers, 1983) and Slovic, 1987 psychometric risk-perception paradigm provide theoretical perspectives through which the observed association may be interpreted. Accordingly, the association should be interpreted as evidence of a relationship rather than evidence that risk appraisals cause endorsement of specific governance mechanisms. The qualitative accounts provide context for this relationship. Participants described concerns about algorithmic bias, data- privacy vulnerabilities, academic-integrity challenges, unreliable outputs, and cognitive dependence. These concerns illustrate why governance measures such as transparency, disclosure, human oversight, and accountability may be meaningful to stakeholders. This interpretation is compatible with research arguing that trust in AI-governance arrangements is strengthened when safeguards address concerns that are visible and relevant to affected users (Ahmad et al., 2024). Nevertheless, the",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,17,"oversight, and accountability may be meaningful to stakeholders. This interpretation is compatible with research arguing that trust in AI-governance arrangements is strengthened when safeguards address concerns that are visible and relevant to affected users (Ahmad et al., 2024). Nevertheless, the qualitative and quantitative findings together do not establish a causal process from risk perception to governance support. 4.5 The non-significant moderation of AERP on the PEV–RAGS relationship (RQ5) The non-confirmation of the moderation hypothesis warrants careful interpretation. Formulated as exploratory, this hypothesis drew on risk-benefit evaluation logic (Bullock et al., 2025; Fischhoff et al., 1978), under which risk perception was ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 17 frontiersin.org",109
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,"expected to condition how benefit perceptions translate into governance preference. The present data did not provide support for this conditional pattern in the current sample; a null result in a cross-sectional sample does not, however, preclude the possibility of moderation in larger, more diverse, or longitudinal designs. One possible interpretation is that participants considered educational value and ethical risk as coexisting considerations rather than as opposing forces. The qualitative themes support this reading because participants often acknowledged potential learning and efficiency benefits while simultaneously calling for rules, transparency, and human oversight. This is consistent with the broader risk–benefit literature, which recognizes that technology evaluations can be multidimensional rather than reducible to a simple trade-off between benefits and harms (Fischhoff et al., 1978). Replication with larger and more diverse samples is needed before drawing conclusions about whether this pattern holds across higher education settings. 4.6 Qualitative perspectives on responsible GenAI implementation (RQ6) The thematic analysis provides contextual insight into how participants described value, risk, and governance in relation to GenAI use. The central qualitative finding is the conditionality frame: participants positioned",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,"higher education settings. 4.6 Qualitative perspectives on responsible GenAI implementation (RQ6) The thematic analysis provides contextual insight into how participants described value, risk, and governance in relation to GenAI use. The central qualitative finding is the conditionality frame: participants positioned themselves as conditional supporters of GenAI, endorsing its educational potential while insisting that responsible realization requires institutional accountability and human oversight. Risk-related themes provide qualitative context for the larger observed association between ethical risk perception and governance support. Participants described specific concerns, including fabricated academic sources, inequitable detection flagging, and disadvantages associated with undisclosed AI use. These accounts complement the quantitative findings but do not establish the causal mechanisms underlying the observed associations. The governance demands articulated in Theme 4 were notable for their specificity and alignment with foundational governance principles in the international literature. Calls for disclosure requirements analogous to citation practice reflect the transparency principle underpinning most AI governance frameworks (Rotenberg, 2024; UNESCO, 2021). Calls for human review of AI-assisted assessment decisions align with the emphasis that many governance frameworks place on meaningful human oversight in high-stakes contexts",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,"to citation practice reflect the transparency principle underpinning most AI governance frameworks (Rotenberg, 2024; UNESCO, 2021). Calls for human review of AI-assisted assessment decisions align with the emphasis that many governance frameworks place on meaningful human oversight in high-stakes contexts (Komarudin, 2025; OECD, 2019). The repeated pairing of governance demands with calls for AI-literacy training is consistent with Williams, 2025 argument that literacy and governance can be mutually reinforcing. In this study, participants’ accounts suggest that rules may be more meaningful when stakeholders also understand GenAI’s capabilities, limitations, and ethical implications. 4.7 Theoretical contributions The study makes three preliminary contributions to the theoretical literature. First, it distinguishes governance support from technology acceptance as conceptually separate constructs. Endorsing a technology’s usefulness is not equivalent to endorsing institutional oversight of its use. This distinction directs attention to stakeholder support for rules, safeguards, and accountability arrangements alongside the more commonly studied issue of technology acceptance. Second, the study provides preliminary empirical evidence that perceived educational value and ethical risk perception are each associated with governance endorsement. The study tested whether the two pathways",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,"and accountability arrangements alongside the more commonly studied issue of technology acceptance. Second, the study provides preliminary empirical evidence that perceived educational value and ethical risk perception are each associated with governance endorsement. The study tested whether the two pathways interact, finding no evidence of moderation in this sample; the dual-pathway model should be understood as describing the correlational structure tested, not as an established explanatory mechanism. Replication is needed to determine whether the absence of moderation is a robust characteristic of this domain or an artefact of the current sample. Third, the conditionality frame offers an interpretive lens on participants’ concurrent references to value, risk, and governance. It does not establish the psychological mechanisms through which the observed quantitative associations arise. Instead, it provides a context-specific account of how participants described responsible GenAI use as dependent on educational purpose, human judgment, and institutional accountability. 4.8 Practical implications 4.8.1 Governance communication The stronger association of ethical risk perception with governance support in this sample suggests that governance communication may benefit from addressing specific ethical challenges, including algorithmic bias, data privacy",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,"human judgment, and institutional accountability. 4.8 Practical implications 4.8.1 Governance communication The stronger association of ethical risk perception with governance support in this sample suggests that governance communication may benefit from addressing specific ethical challenges, including algorithmic bias, data privacy vulnerabilities, academic integrity risks, and cognitive dependence. Comparative intervention research is needed before concluding that risk-focused communication generates stronger institutional buy-in than benefit-focused communication. Institutions may therefore communicate both the educational value of GenAI and the safeguards intended to address ethical concerns. National and international policy bodies may consider ensuring that governance standards address risk transparency alongside educational value, subject to evaluation in diverse institutional and policy contexts. 4.8.2 Stakeholder engagement Because PEV and AERP did not differ significantly between students and instructors in this sample, governance communication may address shared concerns about educational value, fairness, privacy, academic integrity, and human oversight. Future research should test whether stakeholder- specific communication approaches produce different outcomes. 4.8.3 AI literacy as governance infrastructure The co-articulation of governance and AI-literacy demands in the qualitative data suggests that policy implementation and literacy development may be",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,18,human oversight. Future research should test whether stakeholder- specific communication approaches produce different outcomes. 4.8.3 AI literacy as governance infrastructure The co-articulation of governance and AI-literacy demands in the qualitative data suggests that policy implementation and literacy development may be complementary components of responsible AI governance. Institutions may combine clear ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 18 frontiersin.org,59
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"governance policies with AI-literacy opportunities that support informed and responsible use. Accreditation agencies should consider requiring AI literacy and governance documentation as components of program quality assurance. 5 Conclusion 5.1 Limitations Seven limitations should inform the interpretation and application of these findings. First, the cross-sectional design precludes causal inference. The associations between PEV, AERP, and RAGS reflect statistical co-variation in a single-time survey and do not establish causal directionality; longitudinal research tracking stakeholder perceptions across institutional interventions or policy changes is needed to examine whether shifts in educational value recognition or ethical risk awareness produce corresponding changes in governance endorsement over time. Second, the sample’s demographic composition constrains generalizability. The predominance of younger undergraduate students, female participants, and government-affiliated institutions in a single national context, the UAE, means that the dual-pathway model has not been tested against more senior, gender-balanced, or private-institution populations, nor across different national higher education systems. Cross- national comparative studies would additionally allow examination of whether the conditionality frame identified qualitatively is culturally specific or holds across different higher education contexts. Moreover, institutional culture and national",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"or private-institution populations, nor across different national higher education systems. Cross- national comparative studies would additionally allow examination of whether the conditionality frame identified qualitatively is culturally specific or holds across different higher education contexts. Moreover, institutional culture and national AI policy environments, both of which differ substantially across higher education systems, may moderate the relationships observed here in ways this study cannot address. Third, the marginal convergent validity of the AERP construct represents a psychometric limitation that warrants attention in future scale development. Expanding the AERP instrument to capture affective dimensions of ethical risk perception, including anxiety, moral concern, and institutional distrust, alongside the cognitive dimensions currently sampled, would likely strengthen its measurement properties and yield a more sensitive indicator of risk-driven governance demand. Fourth, common method variance represents a potential limitation of the cross-sectional, single-instrument design. Although procedural safeguards included conceptually distinct item wordings and anonymity assurance, the quantitative and qualitative components were collected from the same respondents in the same questionnaire and therefore do not provide an independent test of common method bias. The absence of a",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"safeguards included conceptually distinct item wordings and anonymity assurance, the quantitative and qualitative components were collected from the same respondents in the same questionnaire and therefore do not provide an independent test of common method bias. The absence of a post-hoc statistical assessment means that shared- method effects cannot be fully excluded. Future research should supplement self-report measures with behavioral indicators of governance support, such as participation in AI policy consultation processes or compliance with institutional AI policies. Fifth, compositional invariance was not established for RAGS across students and instructors. Accordingly, RAGS composite- mean and structural-path comparisons were not interpreted. This finding suggests that the RAGS measure may require role- sensitive refinement and independent validation before it is used for confirmatory stakeholder-group comparisons. Sixth, the qualitative data were collected through open-ended survey responses rather than through interviews or focus groups. This constrained the analytical depth achievable through thematic analysis and precluded the iterative probing that richer methods would allow. Future research should employ semi- structured interviews or deliberative focus groups to examine more fully how participants navigate the tension between",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"This constrained the analytical depth achievable through thematic analysis and precluded the iterative probing that richer methods would allow. Future research should employ semi- structured interviews or deliberative focus groups to examine more fully how participants navigate the tension between recognizing GenAI’s educational potential and managing its risks in daily institutional practice. Seventh, the literature review retains two recent preprints (Marcos, 2026; Mustafa and Altrad, 2026) because of their topical relevance. As these sources may not yet have undergone peer review, claims based on them should be interpreted cautiously and should be replaced with peer-reviewed evidence where such evidence becomes available. Eighth, the study did not evaluate out-of-sample predictive performance using procedures such as PLSpredict. The PLS- SEM analysis was used to examine the proposed associations and explained variance within the present sample. Future research should assess the model’s predictive performance using independent or holdout samples. 5.2 Recommendations 5.2.1 For institutional practice Institutions may communicate transparently about GenAI- related ethical risks, including algorithmic bias, data privacy vulnerabilities, academic-integrity challenges, and cognitive dependence, while also acknowledging potential educational benefits. In this",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"performance using independent or holdout samples. 5.2 Recommendations 5.2.1 For institutional practice Institutions may communicate transparently about GenAI- related ethical risks, including algorithmic bias, data privacy vulnerabilities, academic-integrity challenges, and cognitive dependence, while also acknowledging potential educational benefits. In this sample, ethical risk perception showed the larger association with governance support; however, intervention research is needed before concluding that risk- focused communication is more effective than benefit-focused communication. Institutions should develop clear AI-disclosure protocols, provide human oversight for high-stakes assessment, and offer assessment-design guidance that supports responsible use. Assessment redesign is a governance measure in its own right. Formats that foreground process over product, including oral examinations, scaffolded projects, reflective portfolios, and in- class tasks, reduce both the incentive and the structural opportunity for substitutive AI use. Acceptable-use policies should move beyond broad statements toward task-specific guidance that distinguishes permissible applications, such as brainstorming, language editing, and feedback generation, from impermissible substitutions, including autonomous essay authorship and unsupervised assessment, with disclosure obligations analogous to citation requirements. Ethics committees with student and instructor representation should be established to review AI systems",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,19,"applications, such as brainstorming, language editing, and feedback generation, from impermissible substitutions, including autonomous essay authorship and unsupervised assessment, with disclosure obligations analogous to citation requirements. Ethics committees with student and instructor representation should be established to review AI systems used in high-stakes assessment contexts and to ensure meaningful human oversight of consequential decisions. AI literacy development should be embedded within governance infrastructure as a complementary component. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 19 frontiersin.org",76
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,20,"Policies are more likely to be understood and applied responsibly when stakeholders also have opportunities to develop AI literacy; conversely, literacy initiatives may be strengthened by clear institutional guidance and accountability arrangements. Ministries of education and accreditation agencies should consider requiring AI literacy and governance documentation as components of program quality assurance, not merely as supplementary institutional initiatives. International bodies, including UNESCO and the OECD, should develop cross- national governance standards informed by the empirical evidence based on stakeholder perceptions documented in this and related studies. 5.2.2 For future research The limitations identified above point directly to five research priorities. Longitudinal designs are needed to examine whether shifts in perceived educational value or ethical risk awareness precede or follow changes in governance support, and whether governance interventions produce the perceptual changes the dual-pathway model implies. Future research using larger and more diverse samples should first establish measurement invariance of the RAGS measure across stakeholder roles before testing whether associations between educational value, ethical risk perception, and governance support differ among students, instructors, administrators, policymakers, and parents. Broader and more diverse",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,20,"more diverse samples should first establish measurement invariance of the RAGS measure across stakeholder roles before testing whether associations between educational value, ethical risk perception, and governance support differ among students, instructors, administrators, policymakers, and parents. Broader and more diverse sampling is required to examine whether the model holds across demographic, institutional, and national contexts. Cross-national comparative studies are particularly needed to assess whether institutional culture and national AI policy environments moderate the strength or structure of the dual-pathway model. Richer qualitative methods, particularly deliberative focus groups, would allow more fine-grained examination of the conditionality frame and the institutional mechanisms through which it is activated. Additionally, experimental or intervention- based designs should examine whether risk-transparency governance communication produces stronger governance endorsement than benefit-focused communication, testing whether risk-transparency communication produces stronger governance endorsement than benefit-focused communication. 6 Conclusion This study examined associations between perceived educational value, ethical risk perception, and responsible AI governance support among higher education stakeholders. Both PEV and AERP were positively associated with governance support in this sample, with AERP showing the larger association. These findings provide preliminary",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,20,"associations between perceived educational value, ethical risk perception, and responsible AI governance support among higher education stakeholders. Both PEV and AERP were positively associated with governance support in this sample, with AERP showing the larger association. These findings provide preliminary evidence that stakeholders’ perceptions of both potential educational value and ethical risk are relevant to governance support. Because RAGS did not establish compositional invariance across students and instructors, the study does not draw conclusions about whether these associations differ by stakeholder role. Future research should test this question using a refined and invariant RAGS measure. The study’s principal theoretical contribution is a dual- pathway model that distinguishes governance endorsement from technology acceptance and identifies the separate pathways through which value-protective and harm-preventive motivations are associated with institutional governance endorsement. This model extends the governance literature beyond the risk-benefit trade-off logic documented in the broader emerging-technology literature and provides an empirically grounded account of how stakeholder governance endorsement is structured. The mixed-methods design adds the conditionality frame as an interpretive lens through which participants described the relationship among value, risk, and",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,20,"documented in the broader emerging-technology literature and provides an empirically grounded account of how stakeholder governance endorsement is structured. The mixed-methods design adds the conditionality frame as an interpretive lens through which participants described the relationship among value, risk, and governance. In this sample, participants often treated educational value and risk are not competing considerations to be balanced but co-constitutive grounds for the same institutional response. As GenAI capabilities deepen and institutional adoption accelerates, the governance gap between technological pace and policy development documented here and in related studies will demand sustained and coordinated attention. Within this UAE- context sample, the findings indicate support for governance- oriented responses to GenAI’s perceived educational value and ethical risks. What remains is the institutional work of translating that readiness into the coherent, adaptive, and participatory frameworks that responsible GenAI integration requires. Data availability statement The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article/Supplementary Material, further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Research Ethics committee at Zayed University. The studies",180
A055,A055.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201,20,"statement The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article/Supplementary Material, further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Research Ethics committee at Zayed University. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Written informed consent was obtained from the individual(s) for the publication of any potentially identifiable images or data included in this article. Author contributions AE: Resources, Investigation, Visualization, Validation, Data curation, Project administration, Formal analysis, Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Methodology, Writing – original draft, Funding acquisition, Software, Supervision. ZH: Writing – original draft, Funding acquisition, Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Resources, Software, Formal analysis, Validation, Project administration, Supervision, Conceptualization, Methodology, Data curation, Investigation. ElSayary and Hojeij 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926201 Frontiers in Education 20 frontiersin.org",148
