A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,17,"Page 17 of 23 Shahzad et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2024) 21:46 In our meditation analysis, we examined three mediators: perceived ease of use (PE), usefulness (PU), and intelligence (PI). We established a significant mediation effect in the relation among CGPTAW and CGPTAI, as indicated by values (β = 0.179, 0.036, 0.044), (t = 3.981, 2.582, 2.402) and (p < 0.05) supporting hypotheses H2c, H3c, and H4c. In terms of moderation, the results reveal that perceived trust (PT) significantly moder­ ates the association among perceived ease of use, usefulness, and intelligence with values (β = 0.152, 0.081, 0.133, t = 2.551, 2.292, 2.314, and p < 0.05). Thus, H5a, H5b, and H5c have all been validated. Further, R2 values show how effectively the independent vari­ able may define the Dependent variable. This research model’s R2 values are as follows: PE = 12.7%, PU = 34.0%, PI = 9.3% and CGPTAI = 71%. Figure 2 shows all of the route coefficient values. Furthermore, Figs. 3, 4 and 5 show the moderating effect of perceived trust between",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,17,"values are as follows: PE = 12.7%, PU = 34.0%, PI = 9.3% and CGPTAI = 71%. Figure 2 shows all of the route coefficient values. Furthermore, Figs. 3, 4 and 5 show the moderating effect of perceived trust between PE, PU, PI and ChatGPT awareness. These figures show that after high ChatGPT aware­ ness strengthens the connections between PE, PU, and PI under high perceived trust. Discussion The study’s main aim was to assess ChatGPT awareness and its adoption intentions among Chinese university students using the TAM model. Perceived ease of use, useful­ ness, and intelligence significantly mediate the association between ChatGPT awareness and adoption intention of ChatGPT. Further, perceived trust significantly moderates the relationship between ChatGPT awareness and perceived ease of use, usefulness, and intelligence. To fulfill this purpose, a conceptual framework was developed to assess ChatGPT awareness with the help of TAM constructs (PE, PU, and PI) and its effect on ChatGPT adoption intentions in the context of Chinese university students. We statisti­ cally verified the proposed conceptual model on Chinese university students who used the OpenAI",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,17,"with the help of TAM constructs (PE, PU, and PI) and its effect on ChatGPT adoption intentions in the context of Chinese university students. We statisti­ cally verified the proposed conceptual model on Chinese university students who used the OpenAI ChatGPT application for academic activities. Furthermore, it examines the moderation effect of PT among CGPTAW, PE, PU, and PI towards the CGPTAI. To get Fig. 3 Moderating graph of PT between PE and ChatGPTAW",74
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,18,"Page 18 of 23 Shahzad et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2024) 21:46 the appropriate results, data were collected from 320 university students who use Chat­ GPT as an application for academic purposes. We used a survey-based questionnaire technique to collect feedback on using the ChatGPT application and distributed it to the intended audience. The results of the study are divided into five main sections: First, the results predict that ChatGPT awareness positively affects ChatGPT adoption intentions. OpenAI’s ChatGPT application has been recognized as a significant tool for improving students’ academic learning. Given their increased awareness of this technol­ ogy, students are likely to agree on the practical application of ChatGPT. To support the above argument, a study by (Ali et al., 2023) predicted the impact of ChatGPT awareness among research scholars and students for their learning purposes; results revealed that Fig. 5 Moderating graph of PT between PI and ChatGPTAW Fig. 4 Moderating graph of PT between PU and ChatGPTAW",165
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,19,"Page 19 of 23 Shahzad et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2024) 21:46 the awareness of ChatGPT application among research scholars and students would help to boost their knowledge, skill-set and are more willing to adopt the ChatGPT applica­ tion for enhancement of their self-learning capabilities. Due to the emerging usage of the ChatGPT application, there is still a dearth of research available on the actual intention of adopting the Open AI tool. Second, the study examined the mediation effect of PE among the CGPTAW and CGPTAI. The findings reveal that the mediating role of PE in terms of CGPTAW and CGPTAI is less explored in prior studies (Maheshwari, 2023; Saif et al., 2023) as ear­ lier studies focused on PE in different technological adoption contexts while the current study has focused on CGPTAW among students. Additionally, the students feel more comfortable and at ease with using ChatGPT technology, resulting in more willingness to adopt the ChatGPT application. Third, the current study has examined the mediating role of PU in the relation of CGP­ TAW and",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,19,"Additionally, the students feel more comfortable and at ease with using ChatGPT technology, resulting in more willingness to adopt the ChatGPT application. Third, the current study has examined the mediating role of PU in the relation of CGP­ TAW and CGPTAI. This finding is consistent with prior literature (Niu & Mvondo, 2024) as users feel more productive results and enhance their performance and actual results due to the application of ChatGPT in their professional lives. Similarly, when students experience the benefits of using ChatGPT for tasks like project and assignment comple­ tion, report writing assistance, and software development, it consequently enhances stu­ dents’ intention to adopt the ChatGPT. Fourthly, the mediation effect of perceived intelligence among ChatGPT awareness and ChatGPT adoption intentions has been discussed in the present Chinese context. The findings reveal that all Chinese university students find ChatGPT more intelligent and intellectual in getting academic assistance than they are willing to adopt and use ChatGPT. This study’s results align with a prior study (Ali et al., 2023), which explains that college students find the ChatGPT application highly intellectual",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,19,"more intelligent and intellectual in getting academic assistance than they are willing to adopt and use ChatGPT. This study’s results align with a prior study (Ali et al., 2023), which explains that college students find the ChatGPT application highly intellectual and intelligent due to prompt results of given technical queries with minimum error rate. Consequently, stu­ dents become more aware and willing to adopt the application for educational purposes. Lastly, the moderating role of PT is determined by the association among CGPTAW, PE, PU, PI, and CGPTAI. However, the result reveals that the perceived trust signifi­ cantly moderates the relationship mentioned above. The current study has filled this gap by using perceived trust as a moderator, which has not been discussed in prior liter­ ature. However, perceived trust has been extensively studied in the prior literature on technology adoption, consumer behavior, and consumer psychology studies (Bilquise et al., 2023; Hyun Baek & Kim, 2023). However, it is predicted that the role of perceived trust as moderator in terms of ChatGPT adoption is suggested as the students’ perceived trustworthiness in the",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,19,"behavior, and consumer psychology studies (Bilquise et al., 2023; Hyun Baek & Kim, 2023). However, it is predicted that the role of perceived trust as moderator in terms of ChatGPT adoption is suggested as the students’ perceived trustworthiness in the usage and performance of ChatGPT then it significantly enhances the chances to adopt the ChatGPT. The present study results theoretically and practi­ cally contribute to information technology, technology management and consumer psy­ chology literature. Conclusion The comprehensive investigation into the features of prompting the adoption inten­ tion and subsequent utilization of ChatGPT, an AI-driven chat system, sheds light on pivotal determinants crucial for academia. This extensive analysis has delineated the multifaceted aspects shaping users’ inclinations toward adopting ChatGPT technology, offering valuable insights into strategic avenues for enhancing awareness, acceptance,",129
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,20,"Page 20 of 23 Shahzad et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2024) 21:46 and utilization of the TAM model. The present research has five different outcomes: Firstly, it confirmed that CGPTAW positively impacts CGPTAI. Second, PE has a sig­ nificant mediating role in the association between CGPTAW and CGPTAI. Third, PU has a mediating role in the relationship between CGPTAW and CGPTAI. Fourth, PI has a significant mediating association between CGPTAW and CGPTAI. Lastly, PT signifi­ cantly moderates the relationship among PE, PU, PI, and CGPTAW. These findings offer actionable strategies and insights for technology developers, organizations, and aca­ demia to enhance the acceptance, utilization, and further development of AI-driven chat systems like ChatGPT. By addressing these determinants, stakeholders can significantly influence user perceptions and intentions, fostering greater adoption and utilization of this innovative ChatGPT technology. Theoretical implications and practical contribution The present study provides several theoretical contributions that help understand the ChatGPT awareness and adoption intentions regarding PE, PU, and PI among Chinese university students. First, according to our best available knowledge, it is the",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,20,"Theoretical implications and practical contribution The present study provides several theoretical contributions that help understand the ChatGPT awareness and adoption intentions regarding PE, PU, and PI among Chinese university students. First, according to our best available knowledge, it is the only study that has discussed ChatGPT awareness and its adoption intentions in terms of (PE, PE, and PI) among Chinese students. Multiple studies are available that have tried to discuss ChatGPT adoption in multiple contexts but have not discussed the relation between PE, PU, and PI with CGPTAW. Second, the direct effect of CGPTAW on CGPTAI has not been discussed in prior studies (Maheshwari, 2023; Strzelecki, 2023); thus, this study examined the awareness of ChatGPT as a novel technology and its effect on students’ ultimate intention to adopt the technology. Third, integrating ChatGPT awareness with TAM (technology acceptance model) constructs is considered a novel contribution to the technology management literature and academic sector. Fourth, applying the TAM model with its constructs within the context of Chinese university students is a valuable contribution to support the study’s authenticity and credibility for",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,20,"is considered a novel contribution to the technology management literature and academic sector. Fourth, applying the TAM model with its constructs within the context of Chinese university students is a valuable contribution to support the study’s authenticity and credibility for future researchers. Notably, perceived ease of use (PE), usefulness (PU), and intelligence (PI) were critical drivers of students’ attitudes and behaviors toward ChatGPT. Finally, this study contrib­ utes to the development of existing conceptual models produced for various Open AI applications by providing significant proof of parameters within the setting of this new technology. The current research presents practical insights that can help educational institutions integrate ChatGPT into academic settings. The favorable effect of PE on CGPTAI shows the importance of accessible interfaces and receptive tools. Nonetheless, when integrat­ ing ChatGPT into the academies, educational institutions must examine the implica­ tions for examinations. While students will inevitably use ChatGPT in their academic activities, considerable consideration should be made to its incorporation into the valua­ tion phase. Developing the evaluations to support responsible ChatGPT use while main­ taining students’ critical thinking",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,20,"tions for examinations. While students will inevitably use ChatGPT in their academic activities, considerable consideration should be made to its incorporation into the valua­ tion phase. Developing the evaluations to support responsible ChatGPT use while main­ taining students’ critical thinking skills and uniqueness in writing assessment is vital. Instructors can help in this area by offering valid rules and guidelines on utilizing AI tools appropriately and ethically during evaluations. Professors can support the effective use of ChatGPT in academic evaluations to confirm that the students know the antici­ pated aim and limitations.",92
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,21,"Page 21 of 23 Shahzad et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2024) 21:46 Limitations and future directions The present research has certain limitations and needs recommendations for future researchers. Firstly, this study was limited to a single environment in China’s Beijing uni­ versities. It used a small sample size, thus restricting the generalizability of the result to different settings or in various nations. Secondly, researchers only collected data from university students; future research should look into other educational contexts and use larger sample sizes to improve external validity. Thirdly, future researchers could look at comparative studies among other nations or student associates (e.g., college vs. univer­ sity) to inspect the possible differences in CGPTAI and usage. Fourthly, students’ opin­ ions and adoption intentions may have been influenced by their lack of knowledge of ChatGPT in various ways. Students who are unfamiliar with this application may trust largely on first insight or previous beliefs to shape their attitudes and intentions. As a result, the constrained familiarity shown in our study is a snapshot of their initial experi­",180
A072,A072.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00478-x,21,"ways. Students who are unfamiliar with this application may trust largely on first insight or previous beliefs to shape their attitudes and intentions. As a result, the constrained familiarity shown in our study is a snapshot of their initial experi­ ences. Finally, social, ethical, and socioeconomic aspects can affect students’ beliefs and behaviors regarding ChatGPT, and examining these dissimilarities can help provide fur­ ther comprehensive knowledge of the domain. Acknowledgements The authors thank the editors and anonymous reviewers for their valuable feedback to improve the quality of this work. Author contributions All authors contributed equally to this work. Funding This work received financial support from the National Natural Science Foundation of China under grant number 72074014. Data availability Data will be made available on request. Declarations Conflict of interest The authors declare no conflict of interest. Received: 19 February 2024 / Ac",142
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,1,"1 “ChatGPT Seems Too Good to be True”: College Students’ Use and Perceptions of Generative AI (Version of December 12, 2023) Clare Baek1, Tamara Tate1, Mark Warschauer1 1: University of California, Irvine Abstract: This survey study investigates ChatGPT’s usage and perceptions among U.S. college students (N=1001), exploring its relationship with societal structures and values. Most participants reported using ChatGPT at least monthly for general tasks and writing, while its use for computer programming was minimal. Non-native English speakers utilized ChatGPT more frequently for writing, consistent with its perceived role as a leveling tool in academic language challenges. Natural Language Processing and thematic analysis revealed five main student concerns about using ChatGPT: ethical implications, skepticism towards AI, quality of ChatGPT’s output, potential loss of learning and job opportunities, and accessibility. While some students expressed no concerns, citing confidence in their appropriate use of ChatGPT, others hesitated to use it for writing, valuing their unique writing process and voice. Computer science majors expressed concerns related to job displacement due to the advent of generative AI. Students from low-income backgrounds highlighted constraints in",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,1,"use of ChatGPT, others hesitated to use it for writing, valuing their unique writing process and voice. Computer science majors expressed concerns related to job displacement due to the advent of generative AI. Students from low-income backgrounds highlighted constraints in resources as a barrier to effectively utilizing ChatGPT. Sentiment analysis revealed that students from higher-income groups generally held less negative views on ChatGPT compared to their lower-income counterparts. Our research underscores how technology can both empower and marginalize within educational settings; we advocate for equitable integration of AI in academic environments. Keywords: generative AI, artificial intelligence, equity, higher education, educational technology",101
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,2,"2 Introduction The launch of ChatGPT in November 2022 marked a disruptive moment across multiple domains, including education. ChatGPT reached one million users within just five days of its launch, a milestone unprecedented in comparison to other fast-growing platforms such as Facebook, Twitter, or Instagram (Firat, 2023). As a generative AI that produces new data from user inputs, ChatGPT’s advanced capabilities surpass those of traditional AI systems, which primarily analyze existing data (Dwivedi et al., 2023). In the education sector, responses to ChatGPT have varied greatly, ranging from enthusiastic adoption to complete prohibition (see, e.g., discussion in Fütterer et al., 2023). While AI’s prominence in society might seem a recent phenomenon, its existence and continual evolution span several decades. The term “artificial intelligence” has been in use since 1955 (Cope et al., 2021), and discussions about AI’s role in education date back decades (e.g., Khan, 1977; see discussion in Doroudi, 2022). In more recent years, AI research in education, particularly in areas like machine learning, intelligent tutoring systems, and predictive analysis, has seen rapid growth (Baek & Doleck, 2020). The",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,2,"date back decades (e.g., Khan, 1977; see discussion in Doroudi, 2022). In more recent years, AI research in education, particularly in areas like machine learning, intelligent tutoring systems, and predictive analysis, has seen rapid growth (Baek & Doleck, 2020). The surge of generative AI use heralds a new phase for AI in education. Inevitably, AI will continue to be a part of the evolving society, including in educational settings. As such, it is essential to move beyond a dichotomous discussion about the use of generative AI in education (Draxler et al., 2023), and rather to discuss how to effectively, ethically, and equitably integrate AI into educational settings. Generative AI offers powerful affordances for education, including providing immediate and personalized feedback to students, automating laborious tasks, aiding in problem-solving activities such as debugging code, and facilitating brainstorming on diverse topics (Cotton et al., 2023; Firat, 2023; Smolansky et al., 2023). ChatGPT can support the writing process of those",157
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,3,"3 who may need extra help, such as non-native English speakers and those with language disabilities (Cotton et al., 2023; Tate et al., 2023). Also, ChatGPT can be an efficient tool to support CS learning for underrepresented students, potentially influencing their success and career paths in CS (Amoozadeh et al., 2023). On the other hand, there are limitations with ChatGPT, including potential data biases and inaccuracies. Using ChatGPT for academic and professional work raises concerns about students’ over-reliance on ChatGPT, which could lead to diminished creativity and instances of plagiarism (Draxler et al, 2023). Understanding these challenges and opportunities is crucial for guiding higher education stakeholders in integrating generative AI to support students’ ethical and effective usage (Cotton et al., 2023). Revealing how college students utilize and perceive ChatGPT in their academic and professional endeavors is vital for integrating generative AI into higher education. The adoption of new technologies is shaped by user perceptions and usage patterns (Venkatesh & Bala, 2008). By identifying students’ concerns with new technologies like ChatGPT, we can pinpoint factors that discourage or hinder their use",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,3,"AI into higher education. The adoption of new technologies is shaped by user perceptions and usage patterns (Venkatesh & Bala, 2008). By identifying students’ concerns with new technologies like ChatGPT, we can pinpoint factors that discourage or hinder their use in learning contexts (Hsu & Lin, 2022; Wingo et al., 2017). Identification of reasons that hinder certain students from using the new technology can help educators address emerging digital equity issues and suggest strategies to bridge potential gaps in generative AI utilization. Theoretical Framework Our research is grounded in a critical theory of technology, which posits that technology is deeply intertwined with societal structures (Feenberg, 1991) and that it is neither inherently good nor bad, nor neutral (Kranzberg, 1986). This critical perspective recognizes the complex and intricate relationship between technology and the social, political, and economic systems in",138
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,4,"4 which it is situated. In the context of generative AI, particularly ChatGPT, we view that such technologies are embedded within societal dynamics and power relations. We posit that technology, such as ChatGPT, is not merely an instrument for academic tasks but part of a broader ecological system that includes cultural, social, and institutional factors (Warschauer 1998, 2004). This perspective guides us to examine how students’ interactions with ChatGPT are influenced by and reflective of their social and educational environments. We draw a parallel between the emergence of generative AI and prior transformative technologies for print (e.g., Eisenstein, 1980) and digital (e.g., Warschauer, Electronic Literacies) literacy, both of which similarly helped reshape notions of writing and authorship. Our study delves into how these changes manifest in the educational sphere, especially considering existing unequal power relationships and access disparities within society. Our investigation focuses on the interplay between students’ ecological contexts and their use of and attitudes toward ChatGPT. We aim to provide an understanding of how college students interact with and perceive ChatGPT. By acknowledging the complex interplay of technology,",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,4,"Our investigation focuses on the interplay between students’ ecological contexts and their use of and attitudes toward ChatGPT. We aim to provide an understanding of how college students interact with and perceive ChatGPT. By acknowledging the complex interplay of technology, society, and education, our study seeks to contribute nuanced insights into the evolving landscape of learning in the digital age. Our research is guided by the following questions: RQ1: How frequently do students use ChatGPT for general tasks, writing, and computer programming? RQ2: What are the common concerns among students regarding the use of ChatGPT in their course and professional work? RQ3: Does sentiment toward ChatGPT use vary based on student characteristics?",112
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,5,"5 Related Work Since the diffusion of generative AI to the public has been very recent, studies on the use and perspective of generative AI among students are just starting to emerge. Existing studies present an array of findings on the frequency of generative AI use. Amoozadeh et al. (2023) investigated how generative AI influences performance in CS courses across two universities in US and India, finding that most students have been using generative AI. Conversely, Petricini et al. (2023) reported infrequent use of ChatGPT by both faculty and students at a US university, despite their knowledge that ChatGPT will inevitably be integrated into higher education. Bin- Nashwan et al. (2023) examined ChatGPT’s use by scholars, noting those who reported higher academic integrity had lower usage of ChatGPT in their work. The findings of Bin-Nashwan et al. (2023) imply that the concerns on ethics of using ChatGPT hinder the use of ChatGPT. The perceived usefulness of ChatGPT among college students also shows variation. In Amoozadeh et al.’s (2023) study, about half of the students expressed distrust in the quality of",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,5,"the concerns on ethics of using ChatGPT hinder the use of ChatGPT. The perceived usefulness of ChatGPT among college students also shows variation. In Amoozadeh et al.’s (2023) study, about half of the students expressed distrust in the quality of generative AI outputs, with only a quarter of the students finding it helpful. Petricini et al. (2023) found a consensus among faculty and students that ChatGPT’s use violates their institutional policies, yet they acknowledged generative AI’s value in education. Smolansky et al. (2023) found differing opinions between educators and students regarding generative AI’s impact on online essay assessments across two universities in the US and Australia. Educators favored ChatGPT integration, while students expressed mixed feelings due to concerns about diminished creativity and originality in writing. Ngo (2023) reported favorable views from students at a Vietnamese university, with the perceived advantages of using ChatGPT including saving time, easier idea generation in writing, and personalized feedback",154
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,6,"6 Students have concerns and skepticism about using generative AI. A major concern is academic integrity, with perceptions that using ChatGPT could be regarded as cheating by students’ institutions and instructors (Draxler et al., 2023; Firat, 2023; Petricini et al., 2023). Another prevalent concern is that reliance on ChatGPT might erode students’ creativity and critical thinking skills (Draxler et al., 2023; Smolansky et al., 2023). Concerns about the accuracy and reliability of ChatGPT outputs are prevalent as well (Amoozadeh et al., 2023; Ngo, 2023). Shoufan’s (2023) study with computer engineering students in China revealed students found ChatGPT to be helpful for study and work, but also expressed concerns about inconsistent accuracy. Concerns about jobs being replaced by artificial intelligence in the future were also prevalent (Firat, 2023; Shoufan, 2023). Our study extends the existing literature with a nationally representative survey of US college students on their use and concerns about ChatGPT. First, we aim to contribute to the literature by uncovering the frequency of ChatGPT usage among college students at this point in time, particularly for writing and computer programming,",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,6,"of US college students on their use and concerns about ChatGPT. First, we aim to contribute to the literature by uncovering the frequency of ChatGPT usage among college students at this point in time, particularly for writing and computer programming, to better understand how this new technology is being adopted in US higher education. Second, by identifying the types of concerns students have about ChatGPT, we seek to inform institutions and stakeholders on how to support the ethical and effective integration of this new technology. Third, we explore the sentiment of students’ concerns across different demographic characteristics. Analyzing these sentiments offers insights into users’ perceptions of new technology adoption (e.g., Kwarteng et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2022) and potential equity issues sprouting in the use of generative AI in education.",131
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,7,"7 Methods A total of 1091 participants completed a survey through Prolific, a research panel service identified as providing high data quality in terms of attention, comprehension, honesty, and reliability in online behavioral research (Peer et al., 2022). Prolific has been successfully used as a subject pool across diverse areas of research (e.g., Jung, 2020; Marder et al., 2020; Stanovic & Toplak, 2019). To recruit our sample, we set specific screening criteria on Prolific: current education level (enrollment in an undergraduate degree program or technical/community college), current student status, and current residence within the US. We dropped 90 participants who failed to correctly answer two attention check questions, resulting in a final sample of 1001. We paid each participant according to Prolific’s recommended rate of $3.02 for a 15-minute survey. Data collection took place from August 2023 to September 2023. Each participant completed the survey using an anonymized link delivered through Prolific. This study complied with the procedure for self-exemption of the IRB of the University of California, Irvine. Participants answered a series of multiple-choice questions on their use of",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,7,"Each participant completed the survey using an anonymized link delivered through Prolific. This study complied with the procedure for self-exemption of the IRB of the University of California, Irvine. Participants answered a series of multiple-choice questions on their use of ChatGPT, followed by one open-ended question and a demographic questionnaire. We employed three 7- point Likert-scale questions to gauge the frequency of ChatGPT use in general (“I use ChatGPT”), writing (“I use ChatGPT for writing”), and computer programming (“I use ChatGPT in my computer programming”), with options ranging from never to several times a day. We analyzed the ChatGPT usage questions using descriptive statistics in Stata, focusing on the percentage frequency of each type of use. We analyzed the open-ended responses using two different approaches–Natural Language Processing and thematic analysis. The open-ended question prompted participants to express their concerns about using ChatGPT in their course or professional work in two to three",152
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,8,"8 sentences (“What concerns you about using ChatGPT in your course or professional work?”). Using both the NLP and qualitative approaches draws on the strengths of each approach, the results of the thematic analysis provide deeper insights (e.g., revealing nuances) into the data science outputs, such as keyword frequencies (Skeen et al., 2022). For NLP, we used the “tidytext” package in R Studio to conduct frequency analysis for unigrams and bigrams. The data cleaning process involved removing punctuation, numbers, symbols, and English stopwords. Given the specific focus on concerns about ChatGPT in the open-ended response, we also removed words directly related to concerns and ChatGPT (“concern,” “concerns,” “concerned,” “worry,” “worried,” and “chatgpt”) to yield more meaningful results. We note that for the bigram analysis, we only removed instances where all of the words in the bigram are stopwords. In addition, we generated a word cloud for unigrams using the “wordcloud” package in R, where the size of each word represents its frequency (Imran et al., 2020). We conducted a sentiment analysis of the open-ended responses. We used the “sentimentanalysis” package",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,8,"we generated a word cloud for unigrams using the “wordcloud” package in R, where the size of each word represents its frequency (Imran et al., 2020). We conducted a sentiment analysis of the open-ended responses. We used the “sentimentanalysis” package in R Studio to generate a sentiment score between -1 (most negative) and 1 (most positive), based on dictionary-based semantic annotations (Aguilar & Baek, 2020). We then compared the sentiment scores across various student characteristics, including native English speaker status, type of institution, income level, gender, and major. We conducted a thematic analysis using an inductive approach, following the phases of thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2006). The first author began by iteratively reading and coding the data, identifying noteworthy features in the open-ended responses. After several iterations of generating initial codes, the other authors were consulted to refine these codes and identify emerging themes. Once themes were established, the authors collectively reviewed and refined them to ensure alignment with the coded examples and the overall dataset. Each theme",169
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,9,"9 was then defined and named. Finally, the first author re-examined the entire dataset to assign themes to each open-ended response. Results The majority of the participants reported to be enrolled in 4-year universities (n=713), while the remainder reported to be enrolled in community colleges or technical universities (n=288). We aggregated majors into STEM and non-STEM groups, with math, computer science, engineering, and physical sciences coded as STEM, and humanities, social sciences, and arts/performing arts as non-STEM. There was about an equal number of STEM (n=487) versus non-STEM majors (n=496). Most participants reported English as their first language (n=945), whereas 38 participants indicated that English is not their first language. The demographic characteristics of the participants are presented in Table 1. Table 1. Demographic characteristics (N=1001) N Percentage Gender Male 414 41.4% Female 569 56.8% Other 18 1.8% Age 18-23 514 51.4% 24-29 232 23.2% 30-35 122 12.25 36-41 65 6.5% 42-47 34 3.4%",154
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,10,"10 48-53 21 2.1% Above 54 13 1.3% Race Hispanic/Latinx 130 13.0% American Indian/Alaskan Native 3 0.3% Asian 108 10.8% Black/African American 117 11.7% White 554 55.3% Two or more races 65 6.5% Regarding institutional policy on the use of ChatGPT, only 6.8% of the participants reported that their institution allows its use (Figure 1). Most participants were either unsure of their institution’s ChatGPT policy or reported that their institution does not have a specific policy. Among those who selected the ‘other’ option for their institution’s ChatGPT policy, the majority indicated that the policy varies by department or professor. About one-third of the participants stated that their institution does not allow ChatGPT use. Figure 1. Institution’s ChatGPT policy",118
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,11,"11 Research Question 1: How frequently do students use ChatGPT for general tasks, writing, and computer programming? Our results indicate a notable trend towards the adoption of ChatGPT in US colleges, marking a pivotal moment in the diffusion of this technology in higher education settings (see Table 2). Our findings are consistent with contemporary studies, showing that most students are using ChatGPT (e.g., Amoozadeh et al., 2023). The majority of students reported using ChatGPT for general purposes (“I use ChatGPT”), suggesting that they find ChatGPT to be a useful tool for general tasks. Almost half of the participants (46.8%) reported using ChatGPT on a monthly basis, and about one-third of the participants (33.1%) indicated that they use ChatGPT for writing on a monthly basis. However, the majority of students (66.8%) reported never using ChatGPT for computer programming, which might be attributed to the relative frequency of writing for students across the disciplines, while computer programming is primarily limited to STEM courses. Indeed, a higher percentage of STEM majors (33.47%) reported monthly use of ChatGPT for computer programming compared to non-STEM",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,11,"attributed to the relative frequency of writing for students across the disciplines, while computer programming is primarily limited to STEM courses. Indeed, a higher percentage of STEM majors (33.47%) reported monthly use of ChatGPT for computer programming compared to non-STEM majors (32.26%), and the daily use of ChatGPT for computer programming was twice as prevalent among STEM students (6.98%) than among those in non-STEM fields (2.82%). For writing, about twice as many STEM majors reported using ChatGPT for writing at least monthly (45.17%) than those in non-STEM majors (22.58%). Other contributing factors to low usage for computer programming could include a lack of awareness of ChatGPT’s capabilities in programming support or perceived inadequacies in the AI’s programming support. The relatively lower frequency of use for academic tasks compared to general use may reflect a concern that integrating ChatGPT into academic tasks may have academic integrity implications. Furthermore, the low frequency of daily use for specific academic activities, such",158
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,12,"12 as writing and programming, may signal underlying anxiety about the effectiveness, appropriateness, or ethical implications of using AI in these academic domains (Draxler et al., 2023; Firat, 2023), but also reflects the frequency of those activities in general across the curriculum. Table 2. Use of ChatGPT frequency General use For writing For programming Never 22.7% 46.8% 66.0% Monthly (once to several times a month) 46.8% 33.1% 12.2% Weekly (once to several times a week) 21.8% 15.2% 10.0% Daily (once to several times a day) 8.8% 5.0% 4.7% The notably higher daily usage of ChatGPT for writing among non-native English speakers (13.2%) compared to native speakers (4.65%) suggests that non-native speakers find substantial value in ChatGPT as a tool that supports academic writing (see Table 3). This trend is further underscored by weekly usage statistics, where non-native speakers reported nearly double the usage rate (26.3%) compared to native speakers (14.6%). The higher usage of ChatGPT by non-native English speakers may reflect the potential of ChatGPT to serve as a resource that gives immediate feedback for grammar correction and sentence structure",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,12,"double the usage rate (26.3%) compared to native speakers (14.6%). The higher usage of ChatGPT by non-native English speakers may reflect the potential of ChatGPT to serve as a resource that gives immediate feedback for grammar correction and sentence structure improvement (Smolansky et al., 2023), which are common challenges faced by non-native speakers.",53
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,13,"13 Table 3. Use of ChatGPT for native speakers versus non-native speakers Frequency use for writing Native English Speakers Non-Native English Speakers Never 47.7% 34.2% Monthly (once to several times a month) 33.1% 26.3% Weekly (once to several times a week) 14.6% 26.3% Daily (once to several times a day) 4.6% 13.2% Indeed, the open-ended responses from non-native English speakers capture how ChatGPT has been used as a tool to support the process of writing, such as checking grammar and enhancing their writing process. One student shared that they use ChatGPT during college assignments to “create an idea or structure in order to be clear with the writing. It can also be used to speed up the process of finishing assignments.” Another student mentioned using ChatGPT to ensure their sentences are grammatically correct, “I use it to make sure my sentences are grammatical or to check if what I am saying is ok.” This student’s use of ChatGPT to confirm that their writing is “ok” implies a concern about the accuracy of their English expression. In this case, ChatGPT scaffolds",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,13,"sentences are grammatical or to check if what I am saying is ok.” This student’s use of ChatGPT to confirm that their writing is “ok” implies a concern about the accuracy of their English expression. In this case, ChatGPT scaffolds the student’s writing by, checking students’ grammar and word selections, and perhaps also fostering their confidence as a writer (Calvaler et al., 2016; Potter & Fuller, 2008). Yet, another student who identified themselves as a non-native English speaker shared their concern about using ChatGPT due to the fear of being accused of cheating by the AI checker that their institution uses. Their concerns and hesitation in using ChatGPT, stemming from fear of institutional consequences, reflect how new technology interacts with the sociocultural context of particular institutions (Kranzberg, 1986; Warschauer,",129
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,14,"14 1998). This situation engenders a power dynamic between institutions and students, influencing how students use technology for learning. Personally, I have too much on the line for school and would be too afraid to even use ChatGPT for ideas out of fear they may be plagiarized off a person on the internet and I miss that when double checking. Our school also requires every submission to go through some AI checker to make sure you didn’t utilize anything like ChatGPT anyway. Many people get accused even when they didn’t, so it doesn’t seem worth the risk to use it. Research Question 2: What are the common concerns among students regarding the use of ChatGPT in their course and professional work? The analysis of frequently occurring words regarding the use of ChatGPT for academic and professional work, as shown in Figure 2 and Figure 3, reveals insights into students’ perceptions and apprehensions. First, the prominence of words like “plagiarism,” “cheating,” “accused of” and “in trouble” indicate a concern about academic integrity in the era of AI- assisted writing. This suggests",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,14,"Figure 2 and Figure 3, reveals insights into students’ perceptions and apprehensions. First, the prominence of words like “plagiarism,” “cheating,” “accused of” and “in trouble” indicate a concern about academic integrity in the era of AI- assisted writing. This suggests that students are aware of the potential for misuse of ChatGPT in academic settings. The recurrence of these words indicates an ethical dilemma posed by AI technologies in education. Also, it raises concerns about the evolving definition of originality and authorship in academic work and how institutions need to adapt their policies and guidelines to address these emerging changes and challenges. Thus, this new technology is introducing new forms of what it means to produce academic products and creating new power dynamics similar to how the development of print literacy introduced new forms of literacy practices and power structures (Street, 1993).",141
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,15,"15 The prominence of the words “wrong,” “accurate,” and “incorrect” emphasize students’ skepticism about the quality of information produced by ChatGPT and, more broadly, their concerns about the reliability and accuracy of AI-generated content. Also, these concerns suggest a critical engagement by students with the outputs of AI, where they are not merely passive recipients but active evaluators of the technology’s capabilities and limitations. Indeed, existing studies have found that students expressed concerns about the quality and accuracy of ChatGPT output (e.g., Amoozadeh et al., 2023). The prevalence of the words “rely,” “rely on” and “reliant on” in students’ responses points to concerns about the potential over-reliance on ChatGPT. This implies a fear of dependency on AI for academic and professional work, which could lead to a diminishing of knowledge and skills. These concerns of students align with the previous findings in the literature that students are concerned about losing their creativity and critical thinking skills through relying on ChatGPT (e.g., Draxler et al., 2023; Smolansky et al., 2023). It underscores the need for a balanced approach to integrating AI",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,15,"findings in the literature that students are concerned about losing their creativity and critical thinking skills through relying on ChatGPT (e.g., Draxler et al., 2023; Smolansky et al., 2023). It underscores the need for a balanced approach to integrating AI into educational practices, ensuring that ChatGPT serves as a supporting tool rather than a replacement for independent learning and intellectual development. The frequent occurrence of words such as “learning,” “skills,” “knowledge,” and “to write” reveals a consciousness among students of the impact of technology on the learning process. Students are concerned and aware of how generative AI such as ChatGPT influences the process of learning and knowledge obtaining process. Further, the prevalence of the word “job” highlights students’ concerns about the implications of AI on future professional opportunities and the job market. This could reflect anxieties about the role of AI in automating tasks and the potential impact on employability and skill requirements in professional settings. Further, the concerns about future employment signal",163
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,17,"17 Figure 3. Frequently occurring bigrams Words in Figure 4, such as “fear,” “caught,” and “accused,” indicate a fear of repercussions for using ChatGPT. This prevalent fear reveals a tension between the potential benefits of AI assistance and the academic rules and norms. This fear could be rooted in the lack of clear guidelines and policies regarding the use of AI in academic settings, leaving students uncertain about the legitimacy and acceptability of such tools in their academic work.",79
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,18,"18 Figure 4. Word cloud of the frequently occurring unigrams According to the thematic analysis of the open-ended responses, the most prevalent concerns about using ChatGPT pertained to ethics, quality, and opportunities. Other concerns included accessibility and skepticism regarding ChatGPT being too good to be true. Some participants indicated they had no concerns about using ChatGPT. We provide descriptions of each theme below in Table 4.",66
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,19,"19 Table 4. Themes and description Theme Description Ethics ● Unintentionally or intentionally violating rules ● Plagiarism, cheating ● Unfair advantage to people who use/abuse it Quality ● Misinformation ● Inaccuracy ● Lacking authenticity ● Bias Opportunities ● Losing opportunities to learn and grow ● Losing jobs No concern ● None ● ChatGPT can only help ● Knowing how to use ChatGPT correctly, so no concerns Skepticism ● Disbelief and fear about ChatGPT ● Data privacy concerns Accessibility ● Cost ● WIFI access ● Not knowing how to use it effectively Ethics Concerns related to ethics centered around two main issues: the risk of plagiarism or cheating, and the potential for gaining an unfair academic advantage. Students expressed apprehension about unintentional plagiarism, fearing that reliance on ChatGPT could inadvertently compromise the originality of their work. This concern is heightened by institutional policies against AI-assisted work, with students wary of potential academic penalties, including accusations of plagiarism, which could arise from the perceived influence of ChatGPT on their writing style.",169
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,20,"20 I would be hesitant to use it for certain tasks because I would be afraid that it would cause an increased instance in accidental plagiarism. I would especially be afraid to use it for a literature class or something similar… Moreover, there is an underlying anxiety about the fairness of using ChatGPT. Some students worried about being unfairly penalized for using AI as a writing aid, “I know that it isn't necessarily our own work, but when I use it to help me rephrase something or make my work sound a bit better, I feel it's unfair to completely count against that.” Others expressed the ethical dilemma of ChatGPT potentially giving certain students an undue advantage, particularly in situations where AI is used to complete assignments with minimal personal effort, “It will unfairly give an advantage to people who otherwise wouldn't have had an advantage when it comes to grades, and passing their courses…”. The anxiety about unintentional plagiarism and fear of punitive measures from institutions underscore a conflict between traditional academic values and the disruptive nature of AI",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,20,"otherwise wouldn't have had an advantage when it comes to grades, and passing their courses…”. The anxiety about unintentional plagiarism and fear of punitive measures from institutions underscore a conflict between traditional academic values and the disruptive nature of AI technology like ChatGPT. This tension reflects a complex societal struggle to redefine ethics in the face of emerging technologies (Farran et al., 2015), revealing the need for a reevaluation of academic guidelines and policies in the generative AI era as well as establishing more clear AI policy for institutions (Chan, 2023). Quality The quality concerns about ChatGPT revolve around issues of authenticity, accuracy, and bias, reflecting students’ perceptions of AI’s role in academic writing. Students’ fear of losing their own voice in writing when using ChatGPT shows a critical aspect of AI in education: the potential erosion of individual writing and creativity (Smolansky et al., 2023). The willingness of",149
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,21,"21 a student to endure the challenges of writing rather than rely on generative AI reflect their values in their own creativity. This concern was particularly pronounced among those in fields where personal voice and creativity are more central (non-STEM majors). Students’ resistance to letting AI impact their writing style implies their commitment to maintaining the authenticity and integrity of personal expression. My only worry about using ChatGPT is losing my own voice in my writing. I hate writing and have a hard time starting but I'd rather suffer through than hand such personal work over to an AI. The concerns about the accuracy of information produced by ChatGPT highlight students’ critical evaluation of generative AI’s reliability in academic contexts. Students’ concerns about the potential for ChatGPT to provide inaccurate information reveal awareness of the limitations of AI in contexts where precision and factual correctness are essential, “accuracy matters in my field, and I'm not always confident in the accuracy of information provided by ChatGPT.” This anxiety reaches beyond just the factual correctness of AI output but also its contextual",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,21,"where precision and factual correctness are essential, “accuracy matters in my field, and I'm not always confident in the accuracy of information provided by ChatGPT.” This anxiety reaches beyond just the factual correctness of AI output but also its contextual appropriateness and depth; students seem to worry that generative AI cannot achieve the nuanced communication that comes from human analysis and expression. Furthermore, students’ concern about inherent bias in ChatGPT’s output due to its training on large datasets reflects their awareness of the limitations and potential ethical implications of AI. The data used to train AI can produce discriminatory results and be harmful (Gašević et al., 2023; Akgun & Greenhow, 2021), and students are well aware of these risks of AI. Students recognize that generative AI is not neutral and that it can perpetuate existing societal biases, leading to outputs that may be discriminatory or offensive.",146
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,22,"22 ChatGPT is trained on a massive dataset of text and code, which means that it can inherit the biases that exist in that data. This could lead to ChatGPT generating text that is discriminatory or offensive. Students’ concerns about quality collectively imply a critical stance among students toward the integration of AI in academic writing. These concerns reveal that students are aware of the potential pitfalls of relying too heavily on AI for tasks that require personal insight, critical thinking, and an understanding of complex social and ethical contexts. Opportunities The concerns regarding opportunities center on issues of the learning process and the future of job markets. These concerns reveal a complex interplay between AI technology and personal development as learners and humans. The concerns that reliance on ChatGPT could hinder the development of critical thinking skills reflect students’ views that learning is a “personal journey.” Students said that a learning process that is “too comfortable” can hinder their academic growth, correctly interpreting current research about productive struggle. Their worries about becoming overly reliant on ChatGPT for answers imply",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,22,"views that learning is a “personal journey.” Students said that a learning process that is “too comfortable” can hinder their academic growth, correctly interpreting current research about productive struggle. Their worries about becoming overly reliant on ChatGPT for answers imply an anxiety that the values of independent thought and inquiry in education could diminish in the future. Indeed, previous studies have shown that students value the deep engagement of the learning process that involves active self-reflection, discussion, and application (e.g., Lumpkin et al., 2015). Active, critical, and meaningful learning processes lead to higher engagement and better learning of the relevant content knowledge (Cho et al., 2017; Vos et al., 2011). Students also expressed concerns about their peers becoming overly dependent on ChatGPT, and thus potentially not engaging deeply with the learning material. The concerns about the peers reflect two dimensions. First, students are concerned about how ChatGPT can",148
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,23,"23 impact the educational context broadly, beyond impacting themselves but also others. Second, students’ concerns about their peers becoming over reliant on ChatGPT may also stem from the concerns of unfairness, as stated in the ethics section above. Students can be concerned that their peers, by using ChatGPT frequently, would receive an unfair academic advantage over them. I've seen classmates become overly dependent on ChatGPT, and it worries me that they're not truly learning the material. Students in computer science-related fields expressed concerns about job loss due to the advancement of generative AI. For example, an AI developer voiced concerns about their job security with the increasing sophistication of AI systems like ChatGPT: As an AI developer, I'm worried that my job may become obsolete as AI systems like ChatGPT become more sophisticated. Similarly, CS majors shared apprehensions about their future job prospects as ChatGPT’s capabilities improve: ChatGPT has the potential to lessen the value of entry-level programmers. As a CS major, I find this concerning. The concerns of students in computer science and AI-related fields about job security echo",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,23,"future job prospects as ChatGPT’s capabilities improve: ChatGPT has the potential to lessen the value of entry-level programmers. As a CS major, I find this concerning. The concerns of students in computer science and AI-related fields about job security echo a wider societal debate about the impact of AI and automation on employment. Fears about AI systems such as ChatGPT making certain jobs obsolete or lessening the value of entry-level positions imply a growing recognition of the disruptive potential of AI in the job market. This concern is particularly acute in fields directly impacted by AI advancements, where the rapid evolution of technology might outpace the need for human skills. Students’ concerns about their job security can lead them to leave the AI and CS fields, as studies have found that employees’ perceptions of their jobs being threatened by technology can lead to job departure (e.g.,",146
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,24,"24 Brougham & Haar, 2020). The mixed views among students in literacy fields about the threat of automation to their careers highlight a dichotomy in perceptions of AI’s capabilities. While some students fear that AI could automate aspects of their work (e.g., “I'm concerned that it could automate parts of my career as news articles have been written using ChatGPT. It could replace the need for writers”), others maintain that the unique qualities of human creativity and authenticity are irreplaceable by AI. For example, one student commented that ChatGPT cannot replace human writers due to its lack of authenticity and that everything ChatGPT produces is “soulless.” These contrasting views speak to how the impact of technology varies according to different contexts, and there are debates about the role of AI even amongst students in the same field. No Concern The perspective of students who reported no concerns about using ChatGPT provides a contrasting yet insightful angle into the discourse on AI’s role in higher education. Students who expressed no concern perceived ChatGPT not as a threat but as a beneficial",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,24,"perspective of students who reported no concerns about using ChatGPT provides a contrasting yet insightful angle into the discourse on AI’s role in higher education. Students who expressed no concern perceived ChatGPT not as a threat but as a beneficial and augmentative tool, reflecting a more optimistic and pragmatic approach to generative AI integration in education. Some students viewed ChatGPT as an enhancement to existing technologies, appreciating its efficiency in aggregating and synthesizing information from multiple sources. This view indicates an acknowledgment of the potential of AI to complement human effort, rather than replace it, in the pursuit of knowledge and efficiency. I don't have any concerns over my use. I feel chat GPT is just an enhancement on search engine technology we already have. It just makes gathering the information from multiple sources highly more efficient",137
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,25,"25 The lack of concern among some students stems from a clear understanding of AI’s capabilities and limitations. By recognizing AI as a tool that aids but does not supplant human work, these students demonstrate a balanced perspective on AI’s role in their academic and professional lives. This view reflects a broader understanding that while AI technologies like ChatGPT can enhance certain aspects of work and study, they are not at a stage where they replicate human intelligence and creativity. I honestly don't have that many concerns as I think it is a tool, not a replacement for humans. It makes my studies and job easier. I understand that some people do fear that they will lose their jobs/livelihood but I think they just don't understand it. We are so far away from actual true AI. Some students acknowledged the inevitability of technological advancement in the learning sphere and society as a whole. These students viewed that it is necessary for individuals to accept these changes. For example, one student expressed strong views on the essential nature of generative AI,",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,25,"inevitability of technological advancement in the learning sphere and society as a whole. These students viewed that it is necessary for individuals to accept these changes. For example, one student expressed strong views on the essential nature of generative AI, such that, “it is the future and all who don't adapt will be left behind.” Furthermore, some students implied their confidence in using ChatGPT in an efficient and ethical manner, that they do not have concerns because they know “how” to use ChatGPT. These students with no concerns about using ChatGPT often convey a recognition of AI’s affordances, balanced with an understanding of its limitations, and the confidence to use it correctly. And yet, the contrasting views between those concerned about using ChatGPT and those confident in its usage suggest that existing societal inequities might be extending to the use of generative AI. Students who reported confidence in using ChatGPT and leveraging its affordances might come from higher-income backgrounds, as people from higher-income backgrounds can have higher digital literacy and self-esteem compared to those from lower-",176
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,25,"of generative AI. Students who reported confidence in using ChatGPT and leveraging its affordances might come from higher-income backgrounds, as people from higher-income backgrounds can have higher digital literacy and self-esteem compared to those from lower-",36
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,26,"26 income backgrounds (e.g., Bailey et al., 2015; Wong et al., 2015). Accessibility Indeed, those students expressing accessibility concerns about ChatGPT’s cost or connection issues with older models came from the lowest family income group, between $0- $50,000. For example, one student stated that they “really don’t know how to use it.” Another student voiced their worry that “in the future it might be more expensive to use this product.” Another student highlighted potential limitations of the older version of ChatGPT: If using the 3.5 model, being unable to connect to the internet, or finetune it causes major issues. Skepticism The skepticism expressed by some students about the safety of their personal data when using ChatGPT reveals their deep mistrust of the mechanisms behind AI technologies. Some students were skeptical that ChatGPT is too convenient, “ChatGPT is too good to be true. Other students expressed concerns about unauthorized access, data misuse, and surveillance as consequences of using ChatGPT. The fear that personal data could be recorded and potentially used against them or lead to the creation of personal profiles signals",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,26,"be true. Other students expressed concerns about unauthorized access, data misuse, and surveillance as consequences of using ChatGPT. The fear that personal data could be recorded and potentially used against them or lead to the creation of personal profiles signals students’ deep mistrust of AI technology. That everythings being recorded and its being sent to the government. There's also the concern that what is said in the conversation can be used against me in whatever situation. Furthermore, the fears about potential cybersecurity threats, such as viruses or cybercrimes, indicate an awareness of the vulnerabilities inherent in digital technologies. Also, these deep mistrust and concerns picture a stark contrast to those students who expressed no concern about",116
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,27,"27 using ChatGPT, citing their knowledge of ChatGPT. Then, it is important to educate these students with a deep mistrust of new technology about AI systems, potential risks, and the measures taken to protect their data, as this awareness and literacy of AI are crucial in this era. This AI skepticism comes not only from people’s lack of knowledge of AI but also from the notion that AI technologies do not meet expectations of how effective learning should be conducted with these tools due to the gap between the users’ expectations and what the system currently offers (Chen et al., 2022). Again, the limitations of AI technologies for learning are evident, and students are well aware of them. RQ3: Does sentiment toward ChatGPT use vary based on student characteristics? There was little variation in sentiment scores across different majors, first languages, genders, and types of schools attended, as indicated by the similar average sentiment scores in these categories (see Table 5). The minimal variation in sentiment scores across different majors, first languages, genders, and types of schools, indicates a broadly",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,27,"languages, genders, and types of schools attended, as indicated by the similar average sentiment scores in these categories (see Table 5). The minimal variation in sentiment scores across different majors, first languages, genders, and types of schools, indicates a broadly consistent perception of ChatGPT across diverse student demographics. The similarity of sentiment scores across these categories suggests that factors like academic discipline, language background, gender, and school type may not significantly influence students’ overall attitudes toward ChatGPT. However, the relationship between income groups and sentiment scores introduces a socioeconomic dimension to the perception of ChatGPT (see Table 6 and Figure 5). The trend of less negative sentiment scores in higher income groups compared to lower income groups could reflect varying levels of access to and familiarity with technology like ChatGPT. Students from higher-income backgrounds might have more exposure to and comfort with using advanced technologies, potentially leading to more positive perceptions or less skepticism about AI. The gradual increase in minimum sentiment scores from lower to higher income categories (except",170
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,27,"comfort with using advanced technologies, potentially leading to more positive perceptions or less skepticism about AI. The gradual increase in minimum sentiment scores from lower to higher income categories (except",30
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,28,"28 for the extreme income categories of “no income” and “above $250,000”) suggests an incremental improvement in attitudes towards ChatGPT as income rises, possibly due to better resources, education, or technological exposure. Indeed, research has shown that students from higher income groups have more exposure to technology as well as more resources (e.g., better equipment, parent support with technology; Aguilar et al., 2020; Katz et al., 2018). In addition, lower-income students may have had, or be aware of, use of AI for policing purposes or the biases embedded in generative AI, which would contribute to an increased negative perception overall. The unequal power relationships, contextual uses, and access disparities within society again shape the diffusion and implementation of ChatGPT (Kranzeberg, 1986; Warschauer, 2004). Table 5. Summary statistics of sentiment scores N Mean SD Min Max Language Native 945 0.12 0.18 -0.58 1 Non-Native 38 0.11 0.18 -0.4 0.5 Major STEM 487 0.12 0.17 -0.58 0.67 Non-STEm 496 0.13 0.18 -0.5 1 Gender Male 414 0.12 0.18 -0.5 0.67 Female 569 0.12 0.18 -0.58 1 Other 16 0.09 0.18 -0.33 0.31",180
