A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,29,"29 Community college/tec h 288 0.12 0.17 -0.5 0.67 Table 6. Income level and sentiment scores Category Income Frequency Percent Mean SD Min Max 1 No income 10 1.02% 0.10 0.18 -0.13 0.33 2 $0-$25,000 146 14.85% 0.11 0.18 -0.58 0.6 3 $25,001- $50,000 241 24.52% 0.12 0.18 -0.5 1 4 $50,001- $75,000 190 19.33% 0.12 0.18 -0.5 0.55 5 $75,001- $100,000 149 15.16% 0.13 0.17 -0.5 0.67 6 $100,001- $125,000 99 10.07% 0.14 0.17 -0.33 0.58 7 $125,001- $175,000 83 8.44% 0.12 0.17 -0.25 0.67 8 $175,001- $200,000 19 1.93% 0.17 0.18 -0.17 0.5 9 $200,001- $225,000 12 1.22% 0.20 0.16 -0.04 0.5 10 $225,001- $250,000 9 0.92% 0.23 0.10 0.05 0.35 11 Above $250,000 25 2.54% 0.07 0.22 -0.43 0.4",122
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,30,"30 Figure 5. Scatter plot of sentiment score per income level Discussion Our analysis of student use and perceptions regarding ChatGPT use in US higher education reveals the multifaceted and complex interplay between technological advancement and social, political, and economic structures. Technology is not merely a tool but is deeply embedded in social and power structures, as evidenced by students’ concerns about ethical implications of AI use, transformation of the learning process, and future employment. Our results indicate that ChatGPT has penetrated US higher education, with over half of the students using it for general purposes and writing at least monthly. Our findings suggest that a dichotomizing approach of either using AI or completely banning it in higher education institutions is unrealistic, given ChatGPT’s widespread adoption (Petricini et al., 2023). Artificial",131
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,31,"31 intelligence is not “better” than human intelligence–it is both less and more than human intelligence–and in education, we need to draw on its strengths while recognizing its limitations (Cope et al., 2021). It is essential and timely for institutions to establish and communicate clear policies regarding the use of ChatGPT; currently most students reported uncertainty about their institution’s policy on ChatGPT, and about one-third stated that their institution bans its use. Institutions, educators, and researchers should explore ways to ethically integrate generative AI, utilizing its capabilities as a tool to enhance learning. The affordances of ChatGPT in supporting those who most need it are evident, particularly among non-native English speakers who reported using ChatGPT more frequently for writing than native speakers. Such support is crucial in academic and professional settings for those struggling with English writing (Jacob et atl., 2023; Tate et al., 2023). Our thematic analysis revealed that non-native English speakers use ChatGPT to enhance grammar and coherence and to expedite the writing process. Non-native English speakers, who may face challenges in organizing ideas and producing coherent, grammatically",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,31,"2023; Tate et al., 2023). Our thematic analysis revealed that non-native English speakers use ChatGPT to enhance grammar and coherence and to expedite the writing process. Non-native English speakers, who may face challenges in organizing ideas and producing coherent, grammatically correct text, can experience writing anxiety and low self-esteem (Kara, 2013; Medve & Takac, 2013). By aiding in organizing and editing students’ writing, ChatGPT not only improves the writing process and output but also can boost self-confidence. This support in the writing process is beneficial not only for non-native English speakers but also for individuals with language or learning disabilities (Tate et al., 2023). However, concerns remain, as a non-native English speaker shared the concern that they are “too afraid” to even use ChatGPT because of the “fear” that the AI detection software their institution uses may detect the use of generative AI. A primary concern among participants was the fear of being accused of cheating for using generative AI, especially relevant for non-native speakers who are much more likely to be",172
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,31,"of generative AI. A primary concern among participants was the fear of being accused of cheating for using generative AI, especially relevant for non-native speakers who are much more likely to be",32
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,32,"32 falsely identified as producing AI-generated text (Liang et al., 2023). Our analysis shows students’ prevalent fear of accusations and being caught, as highlighted in the frequently occurring unigrams, bigrams, and themes. Various concerns revolve around being flagged by AI detection software used by institutions, which deters students from using ChatGPT. This reinforces the need for clear guidelines on the ethical use of generative AI. Institutions should address challenges such as the misuse of generative AI while allowing students who need it most to benefit from generative AI (Cotton et al., 2023). Most critical, instructors need to clearly and explicitly delineate what types of AI are allowed in what assignments, and in what ways. For example, instructors could require students to disclose generative AI use and limit the use of generative AI to brainstorming and editing, or certain types of assignments. Further, students’ fear of unintentional cheating highlights the importance of providing training in ethical AI use and AI literacy (Petricini et al., 2023). Prior research indicates students’ desire for AI training even before the launch of ChatGPT (Sit et",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,32,"assignments. Further, students’ fear of unintentional cheating highlights the importance of providing training in ethical AI use and AI literacy (Petricini et al., 2023). Prior research indicates students’ desire for AI training even before the launch of ChatGPT (Sit et al., 2020; Wood et al., 2021). There are AI literacy frameworks that can be adopted to train students and instructors. For example, Warschauer et al. (2023) developed an AI literacy framework that includes five elements–understand, access, prompt, corroborate, and incorporate–that can guide students and instructors on AI to support learning. Students’ concerns about the quality of the data from ChatGPT underscore two key points. First, generative AI has its weaknesses, and therefore, it is not “better” than humans, as discussed above. For example, students noted instances of inaccurate results from ChatGPT, affirming that while generative AI can be a valuable tool managed by humans, it cannot replace them. Second, students are well aware of generative AI’s limitations, such as inaccuracies and biases in data. Students’ reluctance to use ChatGPT, stemming from concerns about accuracy",174
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,32,"tool managed by humans, it cannot replace them. Second, students are well aware of generative AI’s limitations, such as inaccuracies and biases in data. Students’ reluctance to use ChatGPT, stemming from concerns about accuracy",34
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,33,"33 and losing their writing voice, shows their value for retaining intellectual contribution and authenticity. This perspective suggests that college students can be responsible users of new educational technologies, valuing their authentic learning process. With proper training, students can utilize the affordances of this new technology tool to promote their learning and become managers of the tool instead of passive users. Students in computer science-related fields expressed concerns about job insecurity, highlighting two common types of concerns: insecurity about job continuity and overall future job existence (Nam, 2019). The impact of generative AI on employment is still uncertain, potentially leading to job displacement, creation, or shifting roles (Budwar et al., 2023). It is evident, however, that this new AI technology will significantly affect jobs. Thus, stakeholders need to consider how these changes, especially in computer science fields, may unfold and prepare students accordingly. Doing so will mitigate the job insecurities of students in CS fields. For example, adding a curriculum that integrates AI as a tool can be beneficial, as future CS jobs will most likely increasingly involve generative AI.",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,33,"prepare students accordingly. Doing so will mitigate the job insecurities of students in CS fields. For example, adding a curriculum that integrates AI as a tool can be beneficial, as future CS jobs will most likely increasingly involve generative AI. Some students who reported no concerns about using ChatGPT demonstrated confidence in using it appropriately. For example, a student mentioned using ChatGPT for only ideation and that they know “how” to use it. This contrasts with students who expressed fear; those confident in their ability to use new technology were not deterred from using it. Moreover, some students recognized that ChatGPT cannot replace humans, viewing it as a tool to be utilized efficiently. Overall, there is a wide range of student perceptions on the use of generative AI, the level of anxiety, and the confidence in using it. The accessibility concerns of students from the lowest income groups, who also expressed more negative sentiments about using ChatGPT, suggest that income inequality is",162
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,34,"34 extending to generative AI use, perpetuating the digital divide. Issues such as WiFi problems with the free version of ChatGPT and concerns about future costs of using ChatGPT reflect the broader accessibility challenges these students face with technology. These difficulties often stem from their K-12 experiences, where low-income families typically have limited access to the internet, devices, and digital skills (Aguilar et al., 2020). Our findings indicate that while new technologies can reduce inequality, they also have the potential to make the “rich get richer” and exacerbate the digital gap (Warschauer et al., 2023). Conclusion Our study underscores the intricate and multifaceted role of ChatGPT in US higher education. Our findings affirm that technology, specifically ChatGPT, is deeply intertwined with social structures, transcending its function as a mere academic tool. ChatGPT’s impact on learning processes, ethical considerations, and the job market is reflective of its interaction with cultural, social, and institutional factors, resonating with Warschauer’s (1998, 2004) ecological system perspective. Students’ interactions with ChatGPT are not merely shaped by these societal, cultural, and institutional factors but also reflective of",180
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,34,"job market is reflective of its interaction with cultural, social, and institutional factors, resonating with Warschauer’s (1998, 2004) ecological system perspective. Students’ interactions with ChatGPT are not merely shaped by these societal, cultural, and institutional factors but also reflective of them. The diverse utility of ChatGPT among different student demographics, including its role as a support for non-native English speakers, emphasizes the technology’s potential as an important educational aid within this complex ecosystem. The concerns related to ethics and job displacement parallel the historical transformative impacts of print and digital literacy technologies on writing and authorship. Generative AI, like its predecessors, is reshaping notions of learning and authorship in contemporary education. Moreover, our findings on the digital divide reveal the unequal power relationships and access disparities. Addressing this divide is pivotal to prevent exacerbating existing inequalities in",137
A074,A074.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100294,35,"35 educational access and quality, ensuring that generative AI tools like ChatGPT are integrated equitably. The integration of generative AI in education should be conducted in a thoughtful manner, acknowledging the benefits as well as its limits while examining its impact on existing societal structures. To navigate these challenges, educational institutions should develop and enforce policies and guidelines on the use of generative AI that are context-specific. Complementing this with targeted training for both students and educators is timely and crucial to promote AI literacy and ethical AI use. Doing so is one of the essential steps in ensuring that this revolutionary technology enriches the educational landscape by complementing human creativity and learning while addressing the inherent challenges posed by such disruptive technologies. Acknowledgment This study was supported by the National Science Foundation under Grant Number 23152984.",137
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,1,"LONG PAPER Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w 1 Introduction The continuous evolution of digital technologies has had a profound impact on education, prompting institutions and educators to adopt increasingly sophisticated tools for teaching and learning. Among these, Information and Com­ munication Technologies (ICTs) have played a foundational role, enabling new forms of content delivery, interaction, and student support. More recently, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has emerged as one of the most transformative forces in this landscape. While AI has long been present in various domains (including agriculture [1], healthcare [2], law [3], transportation [4], and education [5, 6]) its recent advance­ ments, particularly in generative capabilities, have intro­ duced new paradigms that redefine how humans interact with technology. Of particular relevance today is the subset of Generative AI (GenAI), which refers to systems capable of producing Miguel Á. Conde mconde@usal.es Rocío García-Pascual rocio.garcia@unileon.es Francisco J. Rodríguez-Sedano fjrods@unileon.es Jesús-Ángel Román-Gallego zjarg@usal.es 1 Department of Computer Science and Automatic, Escuela Politécnica Superior de Zamora, Universidad de Salamanca, Avda. Cardenal Cisneros, 34, 49022 Zamora, Spain 2 Department of Psychology, Sociology,",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,1,"Á. Conde mconde@usal.es Rocío García-Pascual rocio.garcia@unileon.es Francisco J. Rodríguez-Sedano fjrods@unileon.es Jesús-Ángel Román-Gallego zjarg@usal.es 1 Department of Computer Science and Automatic, Escuela Politécnica Superior de Zamora, Universidad de Salamanca, Avda. Cardenal Cisneros, 34, 49022 Zamora, Spain 2 Department of Psychology, Sociology, and Philosophy, Faculty of Education, Universidad de León, Campus de Vegazana S/N, León, Spain 3 Department of Electric, Systems and Automatics Engineering, Robotics Group, Universidad de León, Campus de Vegazana S/N, 24071 León, Spain Abstract This study investigates how university students engage with generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), specifically Chat­ GPT, when completing knowledge-based academic tasks across six courses and two institutions. By comparing perfor­ mance and perceptions in engineering and non-engineering subjects, the study examines whether students can use GenAI effectively without prior training and to what extent such tools meaningfully support learning. The work also explores how these findings may inform future research on accessible and inclusive learning design. A multi-method design was employed with 254 undergraduate and graduate students assigned to experimental groups (allowed to use ChatGPT) or control groups (restricted to traditional, non-GenAI resources). Quantitative analyses included",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,1,"may inform future research on accessible and inclusive learning design. A multi-method design was employed with 254 undergraduate and graduate students assigned to experimental groups (allowed to use ChatGPT) or control groups (restricted to traditional, non-GenAI resources). Quantitative analyses included descriptive statistics, a general linear model, and non-parametric comparisons, complemented by a topic-based analysis of open-ended survey responses addressing students’ perceptions, usage patterns, and desired functionalities. Students in the experimental groups generally obtained higher scores, with significant improvements in several subjects (e.g., computer systems administration, informatics, childhood disorders). A weak but significant positive correlation emerged between iterative engagement with ChatGPT (edits) and academic performance. Qualitative analysis showed that students valued ChatGPT for fast informa­ tion access, clarification of concepts, and organizational support, while also expressing concerns about inaccuracies, over­ reliance, and limitations of free versions. GenAI can enhance student performance when used actively and reflectively, although its effectiveness varies by disciplinary context. The findings highlight the need for explicit AI-literacy instruction to ensure critical and responsible use. While the study does not directly address disability or accessibility outcomes, the qualitative",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,1,"when used actively and reflectively, although its effectiveness varies by disciplinary context. The findings highlight the need for explicit AI-literacy instruction to ensure critical and responsible use. While the study does not directly address disability or accessibility outcomes, the qualitative patterns suggest potential intersections with inclusive and multimodal learning design, pointing to promising avenues for future research. Received: 30 September 2025 / Accepted: 4 February 2026 / Published online: 10 March 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Expanding the lens: multi-institutional evidence on student use of ChatGPT in higher education Miguel Á. Conde1 · Rocío García-Pascual2 · Francisco J. Rodríguez-Sedano3 · Jesús-Ángel Román-Gallego1 1 3",104
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 original content in response to user prompts by leveraging large datasets and neural architectures [7, 8]. A key mile­ stone in the dissemination of GenAI was the launch of ChatGPT in November 2022, a chatbot based on Large Lan­ guage Models trained on massive corpora to perform natu­ ral language tasks such as text generation, summarization, and question answering [9–11]. Since then, other models and tools (such as Bard, Copilot, and various multimodal systems) have followed, capable of generating not only text but also images, code, video, audio, and 3D representations [9, 12]. These developments have triggered both optimism and concern in the educational community [13]. Some schol­ ars frame the arrival of GenAI as a “calculator moment” for education [14], suggesting that pedagogical goals and methods must adapt to a fundamentally new technologi­ cal reality. Others, however, warn of potential risks such as academic dishonesty, reduced cognitive engagement, and an erosion of critical thinking [15]. Nonetheless, the wide­ spread accessibility of these tools (often freely available) makes it imperative for students",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"new technologi­ cal reality. Others, however, warn of potential risks such as academic dishonesty, reduced cognitive engagement, and an erosion of critical thinking [15]. Nonetheless, the wide­ spread accessibility of these tools (often freely available) makes it imperative for students to learn not only how to use them effectively but also how to critically assess the outputs they generate [12]. Within educational research, multiple frameworks have been proposed to classify the roles of AI and GenAI in learning contexts. For instance, Hwang et al. [6] identify several functional roles: intelligent tutors, tutees, learn­ ing partners, and policy advisors. In parallel, Ouyang [16] presents pedagogical paradigms that position the learner as a recipient (AI-directed), collaborator (AI-supported), or leader (AI-empowered). ChatGPT and similar tools can occupy multiple positions within these frameworks, func­ tioning both as interactive tutors and as partners that support problem-solving, creativity, and reflection. Applications of GenAI in education include personalized feedback, automated grading, intelligent tutoring, adaptive learning, and virtual assistance [17]. These tools can also facilitate translation, content generation, and even code debugging, thereby broadening the range of educational",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"problem-solving, creativity, and reflection. Applications of GenAI in education include personalized feedback, automated grading, intelligent tutoring, adaptive learning, and virtual assistance [17]. These tools can also facilitate translation, content generation, and even code debugging, thereby broadening the range of educational activities that can be enhanced or transformed through AI [12]. At the same time, they raise important questions about digital literacy, algorithmic transparency, and the need for curriculum adaptations that must be addressed by the edu­ cational institutions [18]. Despite the growing interest in this area, research is still limited regarding how students use GenAI in discipline- specific tasks, particularly in STEM and engineering fields, where the nature of knowledge and cognitive demands dif­ fer substantially from those in writing or humanities-based subjects. At the same time, this interest has been accompa­ nied by the rapid development of platforms and infrastruc­ tures that facilitate the deployment of GenAI, expanding its accessibility in educational contexts [19]. However, acces­ sibility must be understood not only in terms of technologi­ cal availability, but also in relation to diverse learner needs. AI is increasingly",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"infrastruc­ tures that facilitate the deployment of GenAI, expanding its accessibility in educational contexts [19]. However, acces­ sibility must be understood not only in terms of technologi­ cal availability, but also in relation to diverse learner needs. AI is increasingly used to support disabled users in educa­ tional activities [20], in daily life settings [21, 22] and even for diagnostic purposes [23]. Yet fully leveraging the poten­ tial of these tools for students with disabilities requires care­ ful attention to privacy, ethical concerns, and user agency [24]. Although GenAI could, in principle, contribute to reducing barriers, current tools are not inherently designed with accessibility as a core principle, and often lack adapta­ tions tailored to users with specific needs. As highlighted in recent literature, meaningful accessibility emerges not from the technology alone but from the design of learning activities and the pedagogical guidance provided by educa­ tors [25]. This paper aims to address both issues by presenting a case study on how undergraduate engineering students utilize ChatGPT to answer knowledge-based questions in their domain. The goal is to explore whether",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"the pedagogical guidance provided by educa­ tors [25]. This paper aims to address both issues by presenting a case study on how undergraduate engineering students utilize ChatGPT to answer knowledge-based questions in their domain. The goal is to explore whether students are able to apply GenAI effectively and meaningfully in tech­ nical problem-solving, or whether specific instruction is needed to foster responsible and productive usage. Further­ more, understanding how students naturally engage with GenAI (and whether such engagement leads to measurable improvements in performance) provides an empirical foun­ dation for future work on inclusive and accessible learn­ ing design. Insights into typical usage patterns can inform the development of AI-supported activities not only for the general student population but also for learners with diverse needs, who may benefit from more adaptive, personalized, and barrier-reducing instructional approaches. The remainder of this article is structured as follows: Sect. 2 provides a description of related works about the application of GenAI in engineering education; Sect. 3 pres­ ents the methodology and context of the case study; Sect. 4 reports the results obtained; Sect.",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,2,"article is structured as follows: Sect. 2 provides a description of related works about the application of GenAI in engineering education; Sect. 3 pres­ ents the methodology and context of the case study; Sect. 4 reports the results obtained; Sect. 5 discusses the implica­ tions of the findings; Sect. 6 outlines the conclusions and directions for future research. 2 Related works The integration of GenAI into engineering education has rapidly intensified, with several reviews mapping its emerg­ ing roles. Liu, Wang and Wang [26] identified seven main application domains (including virtual laboratories, tutoring systems, and automated evaluation) while Liu et al. [27] showed a sharp rise in AI-related engineering education publications after 2020. From a curricular perspective, Yelamarthi et al. [28] reported that GenAI supports content automation, 1 3 48 Page 2 of 14",134
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 simulation-based learning, and accreditation processes across electrical, mechanical, and computer engineering programs, though ethical and faculty-training challenges remain. Hao et al. [29], in turn, emphasized the importance of context-sensitive course design when integrating AI modules, particularly given student concerns about bias and tool dependency. Assessment practices have also been influenced by GenAI. Stankovski et al. [30] found that allowing Chat­ GPT in PLC (Programmable Logic Controller)-related evaluations improved student performance, although learn­ ers stressed the need for critical verification of AI outputs. Mustapha et al. [31] showed that while tools like ChatGPT and Bard assist with documentation, design, and formative assessment, their limitations in precision and engineering reasoning require cautious adoption. Performance analyses within specific engineering domains further highlight these constraints. Zhong et al. [32] reported that although GPT-4 performs well on basic conceptual tasks in Digital Logic Design and Signal Pro­ cessing, all evaluated models struggle with higher-order analytical problems. Complementing this perspective, Álvarez Ariza et al. [33] noted in their scoping review that existing GenAI initiatives are mainly student-centered and",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"basic conceptual tasks in Digital Logic Design and Signal Pro­ cessing, all evaluated models struggle with higher-order analytical problems. Complementing this perspective, Álvarez Ariza et al. [33] noted in their scoping review that existing GenAI initiatives are mainly student-centered and cognitively focused, teacher-oriented frameworks or socio- affective learning dimensions. Student perception studies, such as those by Vidalis et al. [34] report low adoption rates despite recognized benefits in information access, writing support, and conceptual clarifi­ cation. Students also pointed to reliability issues, insufficient engineering specificity, and the lack of visual or interactive outputs - concerns that reflect current technological limita­ tions for discipline-specific learning. Beyond performance and learning outcomes, several studies have highlighted the importance of GenAI for enhancing accessibility and inclusion in educational con­ texts [20, 35–37]. GenAI tools can function as linguistic mediators, accessible interfaces, or assistive technologies for students with diverse needs, supporting tasks such as writing, reading, research, and self-organization [38, 39]. Their effective integration, however, requires not only tech­ nological availability but also pedagogical expertise and an understanding of students’ specific disabilities and support needs.",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"for students with diverse needs, supporting tasks such as writing, reading, research, and self-organization [38, 39]. Their effective integration, however, requires not only tech­ nological availability but also pedagogical expertise and an understanding of students’ specific disabilities and support needs. In this regard, frameworks such as Universal Design for Learning (UDL) offer valuable guidance for structuring inclusive, flexible, and learner-centered GenAI-enhanced environments [37, 40, 41]. An emerging question concerns whether GenAI can actively improve the academic performance of students with disabilities in STEM disciplines. Although some studies have examined how disabled students use and perceive GenAI tools [39, 38], evidence on the impact of these technologies on discipline-specific learning outcomes remains scarce. Addressing this question requires first understanding how GenAI supports knowledge acquisition among general student populations across multiple institu­ tions (a foundational step before adapting such interven­ tions to disability-specific contexts). The present study contributes to this gap by extending previous research that was limited to a single university and a relatively small sample [42]. Here, we analyze stu­ dent use of ChatGPT across several undergraduate courses from different",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"disability-specific contexts). The present study contributes to this gap by extending previous research that was limited to a single university and a relatively small sample [42]. Here, we analyze stu­ dent use of ChatGPT across several undergraduate courses from different universities, including both engineering and non-engineering subjects. This broader and multi-institu­ tional perspective provides an essential empirical basis for future work on the inclusive design of AI-supported learn­ ing activities, shedding light on how GenAI is appropriated by learners in varied cognitive and institutional contexts and ultimately informing more scalable, equitable, and evidence-based strategies for integrating AI into higher education. 3 Materials and methods This study employed a multi-method experimental design aimed at comparing students’ performance when complet­ ing a knowledge-based task with GenAI support versus completing the same task without it. To this end, partici­ pants were assigned to either an experimental group or a control group. Both groups received the same set of knowl­ edge questions under equivalent conditions but differed in the resources they were allowed to use. Students in the experimental group were permitted to use",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"either an experimental group or a control group. Both groups received the same set of knowl­ edge questions under equivalent conditions but differed in the resources they were allowed to use. Students in the experimental group were permitted to use ChatGPT or other GenAI tools freely during the activity, whereas students in the control group were instructed not to use any GenAI system and instead rely on traditional learning resources (e.g., lecture notes, textbooks, personal notes) as well as any online or digital materials that did not involve GenAI. No specific training in the use of GenAI was provided, and fewer than 7% of participants reported having any prior instruction, reinforcing the naturalistic and untrained char­ acter of their use of these tools. To provide full transparency regarding the experimental setup, the following subsections detail the academic con­ text, sample composition, methodological procedures and the obtained results. 3.1 Materials To understand the scope of the experiments, it is first neces­ sary to describe the academic context in which they were conducted. The initial implementation took place at Uni­ versity of",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,3,"composition, methodological procedures and the obtained results. 3.1 Materials To understand the scope of the experiments, it is first neces­ sary to describe the academic context in which they were conducted. The initial implementation took place at Uni­ versity of Salamanca during the 2023/2024 academic year, 1 3 Page 3 of 14 48",53
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,4,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 aim of the course is to train future teachers in the early detection of these difficulties within the school context, and in the design or collaboration in inter­ vention and educational adaptation strategies that promote inclusion and support learning. ● Human Neuropsychology (HN). A mandatory sub­ ject within the Master’s Degree in Research in Psy­ chology and Educational Sciences at the University of León. It is taught during the second semester. The course addresses the study of basic psychological processes (such as attention, memory, perception, and executive functions) and their neural bases. It distinguishes the main functional systems of the brain and identifies psychological processes, their impairments, and their neuropsychological under­ pinnings. Students acquire foundational knowledge of neuropsychology, enabling them to understand both the normal functioning and the dysfunction of these processes. Furthermore, the course introduces students to research lines focused on the prevention, diagnosis, and intervention of neuropsychological disorders and deficits, offering specialized training oriented toward both clinical and research practice. ● Computer Animation (CA). A third-year subject in the Computer",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,4,"Furthermore, the course introduces students to research lines focused on the prevention, diagnosis, and intervention of neuropsychological disorders and deficits, offering specialized training oriented toward both clinical and research practice. ● Computer Animation (CA). A third-year subject in the Computer Engineering program. This course introduces students to general principles of design, as well as techniques for modeling and three-dimen­ sional animation of objects. Its main learning objec­ tive is to enable students to manage all stages of an audiovisual production project in realistic settings. The course content is structured into three main modules: Introduction, Fundamentals of Animation, and Animation Techniques. 3.2 Participants As commented, this study builds upon a previous investiga­ tion [42], which involved 88 students across three engineer­ ing courses. In the current iteration, the sample has been expanded to include a total of 254 students (170 male and 84 female) across two institutions. Of these participants, 133 students belonged to the experimental group and 121 to the control group, as detailed in Table 1. Of these, 219 students come from engineering programs, allowing for a broader, multi-institutional",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,4,"and 84 female) across two institutions. Of these participants, 133 students belonged to the experimental group and 121 to the control group, as detailed in Table 1. Of these, 219 students come from engineering programs, allowing for a broader, multi-institutional and interdisciplinary analysis that includes, for the first time, participants from non-engi­ neering backgrounds. Table 1 summarizes the composition of the sample, dis­ aggregated by gender, academic subject, institution, and group assignment (experimental or control). Participants were initially assigned to groups using a systematic sam­ pling procedure. However, a very small number of students involving three undergraduate courses [42]. Based on the promising results obtained, the experiment was replicated in the same courses in 2024/2025 and extended to additional courses from a different Spanish University, the University of León, to evaluate the scalability and generalizability of the findings. The courses involved are: ● University of Salamanca: ● Computer Systems Administration (CSA). A fourth- year course in the Bachelor’s Degree in Computer Science. This course guides students through the workflows, data management, security, and optimi­ zation processes in both public and",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,4,"involved are: ● University of Salamanca: ● Computer Systems Administration (CSA). A fourth- year course in the Bachelor’s Degree in Computer Science. This course guides students through the workflows, data management, security, and optimi­ zation processes in both public and private digital environments. With a strong theoretical and prac­ tical component, students are expected to apply concepts through hands-on activities to consolidate learning. The course fosters skills for analyzing organizational IT needs and designing appropriate information systems. Participation in the experi­ ment was optional and did not affect the final course grade. ● Service-Oriented Architectures (SOA). A fourth- year course in the Computer Science program, this subject introduces service-based computing mod­ els in which clients and providers interact through services. Students learn to analyze, design, and deploy service-oriented solutions following engi­ neering standards and methodologies. Enterprise applications and Business Intelligence tools are also explored. As in CSA, the experiment was optional and ungraded. ● Informatics (INF). A first-year course shared by the Bachelor’s Degrees in Mechanical Engineering and Agri-Food Engineering. The course provides a foundation in computer science topics such",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,4,"tools are also explored. As in CSA, the experiment was optional and ungraded. ● Informatics (INF). A first-year course shared by the Bachelor’s Degrees in Mechanical Engineering and Agri-Food Engineering. The course provides a foundation in computer science topics such as hard­ ware architecture, operating systems, programming languages, and data structures. The experimental activity was optional but offered up to 10 bonus points (out of 100) toward the final grade, provided that students successfully passed the compulsory course components. ● University of León: ● Childhood Disorders (CD). A second - year course of the Bachelor’s Degree in Primary Education delivered during the first semester. It focuses on the understanding of the main psychopathological disorders that may arise during the Primary Educa­ tion stage, including learning disorders, neurodevel­ opmental disorders, and behavioral disorders. The 1 3 48 Page 4 of 14",140
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,5,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 the same set of knowledge-based questions, using the same scoring rubric and assessment criteria regardless of their assigned condition. The selected knowledge questions correspond directly to core learning objectives in each course, ensuring content validity for evaluating student achievement in the targeted domains. The use of identical instruments for both groups was intentional, as the aim of the study was to compare students’ perfor­ mance on a standard academic task when GenAI tools were available versus when they were not. Keeping the test structure unchanged allowed us to isolate the ef­ fect of access to GenAI instead of introducing variabil­ ity through different versions of the assessment. Addi­ tional data were collected to better understand students’ interaction with the tools (for instance, the number of prompts submitted by those using ChatGPT). The com­ plete set of knowledge questions is shown in Table 2. Some of these questions were previously used in [42], while others were specifically designed to align with the content of the newly included subjects. ● Qualitative Component. Students from",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,5,"com­ plete set of knowledge questions is shown in Table 2. Some of these questions were previously used in [42], while others were specifically designed to align with the content of the newly included subjects. ● Qualitative Component. Students from both groups completed open-ended questions aimed at assessing their perceptions of ChatGPT’s usefulness, reliabil­ ity, and limitations. Additionally, information about did not follow the instructions associated with their assigned condition during the activity - for example, some students in the control group used GenAI tools, while some in the experimental group chose not to use them. These were not voluntary changes of group membership, but rather mis­ matches between the assigned condition and the behavior actually displayed when completing the task. To maintain analytical consistency and avoid misclassification bias, these few cases were reclassified according to the condition they effectively followed. This correction affected only a minimal subset of the sample and ensured that group labels accurately reflected the conditions under which students completed the activity. It should be noted that there is an imbalance between experimental and control groups",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,5,"followed. This correction affected only a minimal subset of the sample and ensured that group labels accurately reflected the conditions under which students completed the activity. It should be noted that there is an imbalance between experimental and control groups in some subjects. This was largely due to the elective nature of the activity and tim­ ing issues, as certain courses were nearing completion when the study was conducted. Additionally, the data reflect a well-documented gender gap in engineering education, with female students representing only about 24% of the engi­ neering sample - consistent with prior research [43]. This gender disparity is particularly pronounced in computer science courses (17–24% female participation), while other engineering disciplines in the study report a more balanced figure of approximately 29% female students. In contrast, the two education-related subjects included in the study had a higher proportion of female participants. Participation in the study was voluntary. All students provided informed consent, allowing the research team to access and analyze their data. All data were anonymized to protect participant privacy, and students retained the right to",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,5,"higher proportion of female participants. Participation in the study was voluntary. All students provided informed consent, allowing the research team to access and analyze their data. All data were anonymized to protect participant privacy, and students retained the right to withdraw at any time. Participants were informed that there were no associated risks or compensation for participation. 3.3 Methods This study follows a multi-method approach [44], combin­ ing quantitative and qualitative data to explore the use of ChatGPT in academic tasks. ● Quantitative Component. Students were divided into ex­ perimental and control groups. Both groups completed Table 1 Sample distribution by university and subjects University of Salamanca University of León INF SOA CSA CD HN CA Totals N 109 41 39 30 16 19 254 Exp group 57 21 23 16 8 8 133 Male 36 15 19 6 2 6 84 Female 21 6 4 10 6 2 49 Cont group 52 20 16 14 8 11 121 Male 41 16 13 6 1 9 86 Female 11 4 3 8 7 2 35 Table 2 Knowledge questions per",180
