A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,6,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 highest completion ratio in HN (88.88%) and the lowest in CD (22.90%). These differences, likely due to course struc­ ture or timing of the activity, should be considered when interpreting the generalizability of the results. To gain a general understanding of the effects of GenAI across multiple courses, a two-way analysis of variance was performed using the general linear model (GLM) procedure. The model included two between-subject factors—Group (experimental versus control) and Subject (the six involved courses)—as well as their interaction. The results revealed statistically significant main effects for both the Group fac­ tor (F(1242) = 27.217, p < 0.001) and the Subject factor (F(5,242) = 14.492, p < 0.001), indicating that both the use of AI and the specific course context significantly influenced student performance. Importantly, the interaction between Group and Subject was also significant (F(5242) = 8.920, p < 0.001), suggesting that the impact of AI was not uniform across disciplines. The model accounted for approximately 48.3% of the variance in students’ grades (R² = 0.483, Adjusted R² = 0.460).",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,6,"Subject was also significant (F(5242) = 8.920, p < 0.001), suggesting that the impact of AI was not uniform across disciplines. The model accounted for approximately 48.3% of the variance in students’ grades (R² = 0.483, Adjusted R² = 0.460). However, it is important to note that assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances were not met in several groups, as verified through preliminary diagnostics. Given these violations, the robustness of the GLM should be interpreted with caution. To strengthen the validity of the conclusions, additional follow-up analyses were conducted individually for each subject, using appropriate statistical methods based on the distribution characteristics of each dataset. To further illustrate the interaction between subject area and experimental condition, an interaction plot was generated (see Fig. 1). The figure shows a clear pattern in which, for most subjects, the experimental group (students who used ChatGPT) obtained higher average grades than the control group. Notable exceptions include two subjects with relatively small sample sizes, one from a master’s pro­ gram (HN) and another from a computer animation course (CA), where the difference between",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,6,"who used ChatGPT) obtained higher average grades than the control group. Notable exceptions include two subjects with relatively small sample sizes, one from a master’s pro­ gram (HN) and another from a computer animation course (CA), where the difference between groups was minimal or slightly reversed. These deviations likely reflect the instabil­ ity associated with small samples rather than a systematic effect. Overall, the visual pattern reinforces the statistical evi­ dence: although AI-assisted learning tends to be associated with higher academic performance across most disciplinary contexts, its effectiveness may vary depending on subject characteristics and sample composition. To further explore the relationship between group con­ dition and student performance within each academic con­ text, a series of pairwise comparisons were conducted. Depending on the distributional characteristics of the data in each subject, either parametric (Student’s t test) or non- parametric (Mann–Whitney U test) methods were applied to students’ usage patterns of ChatGPT was collected us­ ing the instrument developed by Amo-Filvà et al. [45]. 3.4 Results 3.4.1 Quantitative results To present the findings, it is essential to begin by exploring",180
