A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,10,"Likewise, the posi­ tive correlation between edits and academic performance mirrors students’ descriptions of iterative prompt refine­ ment as a key strategy for obtaining clearer or more use­ ful responses. Conversely, concerns about inaccuracies and the risk of overreliance resonate with the non-significant effects observed in courses where either task demands were not well aligned with GenAI support or students lacked suf­ ficient AI-literacy skills. Together, these patterns illustrate how students’ experiences with GenAI help explain the variability found in quantitative outcomes. 4.3 Implications for learning, accessibility, and inclusive design The findings of this study provide several implications for understanding how GenAI shapes students’ learning pro­ cesses and how such tools may support more inclusive and accessible educational practices. From a learning perspec­ tive, the results reinforce the growing body of evidence suggesting that GenAI can act as an effective scaffold for understanding and completing academic tasks. Students’ perceptions of fast access to information, clarification of concepts, and time savings align with mechanisms previ­ ously identified in studies where GenAI enhanced perfor­ mance in factual or concept-based assessments [48, 29].",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,10,"scaffold for understanding and completing academic tasks. Students’ perceptions of fast access to information, clarification of concepts, and time savings align with mechanisms previ­ ously identified in studies where GenAI enhanced perfor­ mance in factual or concept-based assessments [48, 29]. These mechanisms are also reflected in the positive cor­ relation between iterative prompt refinement (edits) and academic performance, underscoring that active and reflec­ tive engagement with AI (rather than passive use) is key to obtaining meaningful learning benefits. Beyond individual performance, the qualitative themes highlight several dimensions that intersect with principles of UDL and accessibility-oriented educational design. non-parametric tests, which confirmed significant differ­ ences in CSA, INF, and CD, but not in SOA, HN, or CA. These differences highlight the importance of course- specific factors, such as the nature of the task, the lin­ guistic demands, or the opportunity to leverage ChatGPT effectively. Complementary analyses revealed no significant perfor­ mance differences between institutions or between engi­ neering and non-engineering subjects, suggesting that the benefits of GenAI are not restricted to specific disciplinary cultures. This aligns with findings by Mustapha et",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,10,"leverage ChatGPT effectively. Complementary analyses revealed no significant perfor­ mance differences between institutions or between engi­ neering and non-engineering subjects, suggesting that the benefits of GenAI are not restricted to specific disciplinary cultures. This aligns with findings by Mustapha et al. [31], who highlight the broad applicability of AI tools across diverse program types. A particularly meaningful finding concerns the relation­ ship between performance and iterative use of ChatGPT. While the number of prompts showed no significant corre­ lation with grades, the number of edits was positively corre­ lated with performance across the full sample. This suggests that students who refine their inputs (engaging in a more active, reflective interaction) tend to benefit more from the tool. Similar observations were reported by Stankovski et al. [30] and García-Peñalvo et al. [47], who observed that students became more proficient over time in interacting with AI tools, leading to improved performance. Despite these strengths, several methodological limita­ tions must be acknowledged. Some subjects, particularly CA and HN, had small sample sizes due to the timing of the study. The assumption violations in",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,10,"in interacting with AI tools, leading to improved performance. Despite these strengths, several methodological limita­ tions must be acknowledged. Some subjects, particularly CA and HN, had small sample sizes due to the timing of the study. The assumption violations in the GLM reduce robustness, and differences across subjects and institutions introduce contextual variability. These limitations caution against overgeneralizing the results and point to the need for balanced sampling and longitudinal designs in future research. 4.2 Qualitative findings The qualitative data provide valuable insight into students’ perceptions of ChatGPT and help contextualize the quantita­ tive outcomes. Three prominent themes emerged regarding positive aspects: fast access to information, time savings, and support for doubt resolution. These themes reflect a widespread perception of ChatGPT as a tool that increases efficiency and scaffolds understanding, consistent with find­ ings reported in recent GenAI educational research [29, 34]. Students also described ChatGPT as a “personal assis­ tant” particularly highlighting its usefulness for summariz­ ing, structuring, or clarifying ideas. This aligns with broader evidence showing that GenAI can enhance learning by pro­ viding rapid feedback, personalized explanations,",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,10,"[29, 34]. Students also described ChatGPT as a “personal assis­ tant” particularly highlighting its usefulness for summariz­ ing, structuring, or clarifying ideas. This aligns with broader evidence showing that GenAI can enhance learning by pro­ viding rapid feedback, personalized explanations, and cog­ nitive scaffolding [34, 48]. Negative perceptions centered on inaccuracies, the risk of overreliance, and reduced cognitive effort, issues commonly 1 3 48 Page 10 of 14",68
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,11,"Universal Access in the Information Society (2026) 25:48 implementing naturalistic experiments in real academic settings. Third, the study relied on a single set of knowledge ques­ tions per subject. This choice enabled a direct comparison between traditional and open-AI conditions, but it also restricts the range of cognitive processes assessed. Tasks involving problem-solving, multi-step reasoning, or criti­ cal evaluation may yield different patterns of AI-supported performance and should be incorporated in future research. Fourth, the qualitative data capture students’ percep­ tions at one point in time, during a period in which GenAI tools are rapidly evolving. As functionalities expand, users’ expectations and behaviors may change accordingly, mean­ ing that future cohorts could engage with AI in substantially different ways. Finally, although the study was not designed to evalu­ ate accessibility or disability-related outcomes, the qualita­ tive themes suggest potential intersections between GenAI and inclusive or multimodal learning design. Future work should explicitly examine these possibilities by including diverse learner profiles and by testing structured AI-sup­ ported interventions aligned with accessibility frameworks such as UDL. Longitudinal studies, larger and more balanced",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,11,"between GenAI and inclusive or multimodal learning design. Future work should explicitly examine these possibilities by including diverse learner profiles and by testing structured AI-sup­ ported interventions aligned with accessibility frameworks such as UDL. Longitudinal studies, larger and more balanced sam­ ples, and tasks targeting a wider set of competencies will be essential to deepen understanding of how GenAI can be responsibly and effectively integrated into higher education. 5 Conclusions This study provides multi-institutional evidence that GenAI tools such as ChatGPT can support students’ learning pro­ cesses and, in several subjects, improve performance, particularly when used in an active and iterative manner. Students frequently described the tool as helpful for clarify­ ing concepts, organizing ideas, and accessing information efficiently, and these perceptions complement the quantita­ tive finding that iterative prompt refinement is associated with higher scores. Together, these results suggest that the educational value of GenAI depends not simply on access to the tool but on how learners engage with it. However, students also expressed concerns regarding inaccuracies, outdated responses, and the risk of overreli­ ance, highlighting the need for",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,11,"that the educational value of GenAI depends not simply on access to the tool but on how learners engage with it. However, students also expressed concerns regarding inaccuracies, outdated responses, and the risk of overreli­ ance, highlighting the need for explicit guidance on criti­ cal evaluation and responsible use. These findings reinforce current discussions on AI literacy and point to the impor­ tance of pedagogical integration rather than unstructured adoption. Although this study did not focus on accessibility or disability-related outcomes, some qualitative patterns (such as students’ demand for multimodal explanations or Students’ requests for multimodal capabilities (such as image generation, audio explanations, or visual summa­ ries) closely mirror the UDL principle of providing multiple means of representation, which is particularly relevant for learners who benefit from alternative modalities of informa­ tion processing [49]. Similarly, student calls for personal­ ized interactions and adaptive responses reflect the need for flexible support mechanisms, a core tenet of inclusive learn­ ing frameworks [50]. Although this study does not directly evaluate popula­ tions with disabilities, its findings offer insights into how GenAI may reduce",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,11,"interactions and adaptive responses reflect the need for flexible support mechanisms, a core tenet of inclusive learn­ ing frameworks [50]. Although this study does not directly evaluate popula­ tions with disabilities, its findings offer insights into how GenAI may reduce cognitive barriers associated with information search, comprehension, and task organiza­ tion. Recent research has highlighted the potential of AI tools to support disabled learners in navigating academic content, organizing tasks, or accessing alternative formats [20, 35–37]. The themes identified in this experiment data (especially those related to clarity, scaffolding, multimodal­ ity, and structured support) suggest that GenAI could be lev­ eraged to complement accessibility practices within higher education, provided that issues of accuracy, user training, and ethical considerations are carefully addressed. At the same time, the concerns raised by students (e.g., inaccuracies, risk of overreliance, uneven familiarity with AI tools) point to potential equity challenges. Without targeted AI-literacy training, learners with lower digital confidence (or those requiring specific cognitive or linguistic supports) may be disadvantaged or overwhelmed by unfiltered AI outputs. This reinforces current discussions emphasizing the need for explicit",180
A076,A076.pdf,10.1007/s10209-026-01315-w,11,"point to potential equity challenges. Without targeted AI-literacy training, learners with lower digital confidence (or those requiring specific cognitive or linguistic supports) may be disadvantaged or overwhelmed by unfiltered AI outputs. This reinforces current discussions emphasizing the need for explicit pedagogical guidance when integrating AI into learning environments [51, 12]. From an inclusion standpoint, such guidance is essential to ensure that GenAI acts as an enabler rather than a source of new access barriers [52] something that is aligned with the Safe AI in Education Manifesto [53]. 4.4 Limitations and future research Despite providing multi-institutional evidence on students’ use of GenAI, several limitations should be considered when interpreting the findings. First, sample sizes varied substantially across subjects, with particularly low partici­ pation in CA and HN. Although non-parametric tests were applied when statistical assumptions were violated, these imbalances introduce uncertainty and limit the strength of between-subject comparisons. Second, participation in the activity was voluntary, and a small number of students did not follow the instructions regarding tool use, requiring reclassification during data cleaning. While this adjustment affected only a minimal",180
