A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y Exploring student and teacher perspectives on ChatGPT’s impact in higher education Katarína Žáková1 · Diana Urbano2 · Ricardo Cruz-Correia3 · José Luis Guzmán4 · Jakub Matišák1 Received: 16 January 2024 / Accepted: 11 November 2024 / Published online: 29 November 2024 © The Author(s) 2024 Abstract Understanding how students interact with AI bots is a ﬁrst step towards integrating them into instructional design. In this report, the results of a survey conducted in three European higher education institutions, and in the context of four different areas are presented. Among other things, they reveal for what purposes students use ChatGPT, whether they trust and feel satisﬁed with the interaction, how they perceive ChatGPT as a tool to support learning, and if they intend to use it in the future. The study compares results across groups by analyzing data obtained from convenience samples, which include participants of three European countries, with diverse backgrounds, varying technology and science-related ﬁelds, as well as academic program levels. Students’ opinions regarding the utilization of ChatGPT in assessments are also",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,1,"groups by analyzing data obtained from convenience samples, which include participants of three European countries, with diverse backgrounds, varying technology and science-related ﬁelds, as well as academic program levels. Students’ opinions regarding the utilization of ChatGPT in assessments are also documented, along with their perspectives on the potential future applications of these AI tools. The authors, teaching different subjects at different levels of higher education programs, describe their views on integrating ChatGPT and similar AI bots into instructional design. Keywords ChatGPT · AI chatbots · Instructional design · Educational technology B Katarína Žáková katarina.zakova@stuba.sk Ricardo Cruz-Correia rcorreia@med.up.pt José Luis Guzmán joseluis.guzman@ual.es https://w3.ual.es/personal/joguzman/ 1 Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Information Technology, Slovak University of Technology in Bratislava, Ilkoviˇcova 3, Bratislava 84104, Slovakia 2 LAETA-INEGI, Faculty of Engineering (FEUP), University of Porto, Rua Dr. Roberto Frias, Porto 4200-465, Portugal 3 CINTESIS, Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto, Al. Prof. Hernâni Monteiro, Porto 4200 - 319, Portugal 4 Centro Mixto CIESOL, ceiA3, University of Almería, Ctra. Sacramento s/n, Almería 04120, Spain 123",169
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,2,"650 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 1 Introduction The recent access to Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI) chatbots that can deal with huge amounts of data, surpassing by a large amount the human brain processing capabilities for storing, computing, and retrieving information, will most certainly have profound impacts on how we learn, communicate, and work. So big are the foreseen implica- tions, that a large number of individuals have asked for a halt in the development and deployment of AI bots, in order to predict the consequences and regulate their use. In some cases, countries are even taking measures to limit its access (Khatsenkova, 2023). One of the areas identiﬁed for signiﬁcant changes is teaching and learning. When discussing AI chatbots and Higher Education, several key concepts have been consid- ered such as, integration in instructional design (Zhou, 2023; Aithal & Aithal, 2023), pedagogical strategies to better incorporate them (Kiryakova & Angelova, 2023), stu- dent engagement (Sadegh-Zadeh et al., 2023), assessment, accessibility, fraud and academic integrity (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023). There are many suggestions that plan- ning and modiﬁcation of assessment",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,2,"Aithal, 2023), pedagogical strategies to better incorporate them (Kiryakova & Angelova, 2023), stu- dent engagement (Sadegh-Zadeh et al., 2023), assessment, accessibility, fraud and academic integrity (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023). There are many suggestions that plan- ning and modiﬁcation of assessment of competencies acquired by students and trainees should be considered a priority (Kooli, 2023; Lo, 2023; Rudolph et al., 2023). As a language model that has been trained with large amounts of data from different areas of knowledge, ChatGPT is able to give information on many areas such as: science and engineering, computer science, arts, medicine, humanities, law, economics and ﬁnance, etc. Blending the large language model with a computational engine such as Wolfram Alpha (Wolfram Research, Inc., 2023) can certainly have a greater impact on science and engineering related areas. Neural symbolic techniques have already been used to solve mathematical problems such as proving mathematical theorems or ﬁnding the roots of polynomials. It has also been able to simulate complex physical systems, as for instance the behavior of particles in quantum mechanics. One of the biggest challenges for AI",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,2,"mathematical problems such as proving mathematical theorems or ﬁnding the roots of polynomials. It has also been able to simulate complex physical systems, as for instance the behavior of particles in quantum mechanics. One of the biggest challenges for AI systems development is the lack of data to train neural symbolic models. Another is the ability to understand and reason abstract concepts and mapping those concepts into mathematical models. In view of the very fast developments of AI systems, it will not come as a surprise that these limitations can be quickly overcome in the future. Thirty years ago, books and classes were the main source of learning. More recently, books have been somewhat replaced by videos. There is a profusion of information on theinternetthatanyonecanusetolearn,toperformassignments,tocode,ortohelpwith thesis writing. But that requires assembling information, processing it, and making the necessary adjustments to tackle the speciﬁcity of each problem. Moreover, the origin of the information is most of the times explicitly given and there is the awareness that crediting ideas, facts and knowledge is an ethical necessity (Thorp, 2023). Neither books, videos, not",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,2,"tackle the speciﬁcity of each problem. Moreover, the origin of the information is most of the times explicitly given and there is the awareness that crediting ideas, facts and knowledge is an ethical necessity (Thorp, 2023). Neither books, videos, not information retrieved from the internet allow for pro- viding a personalized learning experience. On the contrary, with AI bots users can supposedly learn at their own pace, engage in “conversations” where information is tailored to their needs, and ask a very large number of questions to clarify reason- ing, which is not possible to do with the means described above and very difﬁcult to effectively realize in face-to-face class with the teacher and a large number of students. 123",119
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 651 Many researchers consider one-to-one tutoring as the holy grail of teaching and learning(Kirchner&Hendrick,2020).Meta-analysisstudies,suchastheonedescribed in Kulik and Fletcher (2016); Ma et al. (2014), report signiﬁcant performance gains when students use intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) to provide individual instruction. However, both face-to-face as well as intelligent tutoring systems can be difﬁcult to develop and implement across different domains (Kochmar et al., 2022), mainly due to economic reasons. In the meta-analysis (Wu & Yu, 2023), the main conclusion about the effects of AI chatbots in students learning outcomes is that higher education students are the ones beneﬁting the most and the gains are more visible if the pedagogical interventions have a duration of less than 10 weeks. It would also be very important to understand differences in these ﬁndings across areas of knowledge. Although AI bots can be very useful to learn, many concerns have been raised about them. Review papers such (Rudolph et al., 2023; Sok & Heng, 2023) describe strengths and limitations of ChatGPT. There, the authors analyze several published works that deal with",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,3,"can be very useful to learn, many concerns have been raised about them. Review papers such (Rudolph et al., 2023; Sok & Heng, 2023) describe strengths and limitations of ChatGPT. There, the authors analyze several published works that deal with experimenting with ChatGPT in various contexts. To attain the learning outcomes deﬁned for each speciﬁc area and level of knowl- edge, the design of the instruction should takes into account, among other aspects, prior knowledge, the processes students engage in learning, their preferences, attitudes, how they perform academic assignments, etc. (Stefaniak, 2021). A recent report (Hadi Mogavi et al., 2024) explores early users’ perceptions and experiences of ChatGPT in education by performing a qualitative analyzes of texts obtained from different social media platforms. The main conclusions are that higher education is the setting where ChatGPT is more used, as compared to K12 or practical skills learning. Moreover, the most frequently discussed topics are efﬁciency, produc- tivity, and ethics. Another result is that user’s have mixed opinions on the use of AI in educational settings, referring both advantages and disadvantages. The",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,3,"to K12 or practical skills learning. Moreover, the most frequently discussed topics are efﬁciency, produc- tivity, and ethics. Another result is that user’s have mixed opinions on the use of AI in educational settings, referring both advantages and disadvantages. The authors would like to highlight that, since the design of the study reported in this work, a signiﬁcant number of publications on the integration of artiﬁcial intelligence (AI) in education have emerged. Using data from the PubMed database alone, an analysisrevealedasigniﬁcantevolutioninthenumberofarticlespublishedonthistopic from the mid-20th century to the present. In the 1960s, only one article on education and AI was recorded. This number began to slowly increase in the following decades, with notable growth starting in the 1980s. In 1984, there was a single published article, but by 1989 this number had grown to 21. During the 1990s, interest continued to rise, peaking at 37 articles in 1995. The early 21st century marked a turning point, with a substantial increase in the number of publications. From 49 articles in 2003 and 2004, the number jumped to 95 in 2006 and continued",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,3,"rise, peaking at 37 articles in 1995. The early 21st century marked a turning point, with a substantial increase in the number of publications. From 49 articles in 2003 and 2004, the number jumped to 95 in 2006 and continued to grow almost annually. By 2010, there were 104 articles, and this growth accelerated even further in the last decade. In 2019, 166 articles were published, and this number rose to 183 in 2020. In recent years, the growth rate has been even more impressive (Fig. 1). In 2021, 254 articles were published, followed by 378 in 2022 and 412 in 2023. For 2024, based on the publication rate up to August, it is estimated that approximately 790 articles will be published throughout the year, representing an exponential increase in interest and research on the application of AI in education. 123",141
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,4,"652 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 1965 1984 1985 1986 1987 1988 1989 1990 1991 1992 1993 1994 1995 1996 1997 1998 1999 2000 2001 2002 2003 2004 2005 2006 2007 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 0 200 400 600 800 Year Number of articles Fig. 1 Publications per year indexed on PubMed on education and artiﬁcial intelligence This increase is not limited to the ﬁeld of health, although it is one of the most notable areas where AI has been applied. In medical education, for example, AI has been used to personalize teaching, improve medical simulations, and analyze large volumes of clinical data for educational purposes. Recent studies highlight the use of intelligent tutoring systems that adapt content according to students’ progress and individual needs, promoting more effective and efﬁcient learning. Beyond health, AI is being integrated into engineering, social sciences, arts, and humanities curricula. In engineering, AI facilitates the development of practical skills through simulations and virtual environments. In social sciences, the analysis of large",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,4,"more effective and efﬁcient learning. Beyond health, AI is being integrated into engineering, social sciences, arts, and humanities curricula. In engineering, AI facilitates the development of practical skills through simulations and virtual environments. In social sciences, the analysis of large data sets with AI techniques is enabling new forms of research and understanding of complex social phenomena. In areas such as arts and humanities, AI is being used to create new forms of expression and critical analysis. The growing body of literature suggests that AI has the potential to revolutionize education across multiple domains, providing opportunities for personalized learning, access to advanced resources, and the creation of new teaching methodologies. This outlook indicates a continuous trend of research and application of AI in education, with the expectation that new technological developments will continue to expandtheboundariesofwhatispossibleineducationalterms.Educationalinstitutions and educators must stay abreast of these innovations, adapting their practices and curricula to incorporate these powerful tools, thus better preparing students for an increasingly digital and interconnected future. Although there is a steep rise in interest and exploration of AI chatbots, they are still",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,4,"of these innovations, adapting their practices and curricula to incorporate these powerful tools, thus better preparing students for an increasingly digital and interconnected future. Although there is a steep rise in interest and exploration of AI chatbots, they are still a relatively new technology and therefore it is not possible to provide precise and universal guidelines for their use in education. The ﬁeld is constantly evolving and 123",68
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 653 cannot be completely closed. At the local level, each institution and ultimately instruc- tor should reﬂect on how to integrate these resources in their teaching environment. The pedagogical strategies should be guided, not only by the teachers’ perspectives, but also on how students react, and use them. The current exploratory study can be viewed as an initial effort to incorporate AI chatbots within the instructional framework of specialized science and technology programs in higher education. It addresses some of the issues, outlined above, related to the deployment of AI chatbots in speciﬁc setting. In particular the research aims to collect data on students’ current utilization of ChatGPT in educational context. This includes assessing their level of trust in its information, evaluating its user- friendliness, and gauging their perception of its usefulness and value as a learning tool. Additionally, it examines their intentions regarding future usage. Furthermore, the study evaluates perceptions of its utilization on assessment, alongside analyzing participants’ perspectives on its prospective usage. Given the possibility to survey students from varied backgrounds, different",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,5,"as a learning tool. Additionally, it examines their intentions regarding future usage. Furthermore, the study evaluates perceptions of its utilization on assessment, alongside analyzing participants’ perspectives on its prospective usage. Given the possibility to survey students from varied backgrounds, different levels of their academic programs, and three different European cultures, another objective of this research is to compare the results across these diverse groups. Additionally, the paper presents the authors’ viewpoints on integrating AI chatbots into teaching practices, considering the relevant concepts arouse by using AI in HE, their own experiences with the AI bots, and the ﬁndings uncovered by the data from the surveys. The insights derived from this research will inform the development of AI chatbot-driven pedagogical strategies that may enhance student engagement and motivation, in each speciﬁc learning context. To achieve its primary objectives, the study utilizes a convenience sampling method, wherein a survey about ChatGPT was distributed among convenience groups. The groups included students enrolled in various courses, managed by the authors, at different stages of technology and science programs of three distinct HE European institutions.",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,5,"a convenience sampling method, wherein a survey about ChatGPT was distributed among convenience groups. The groups included students enrolled in various courses, managed by the authors, at different stages of technology and science programs of three distinct HE European institutions. The survey comprises numerous questions, with some based on existing literature (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh et al., 2003; Indrawati & Putri, 2018; Camilleri & Camilleri, 2022; Urbano et al., 2020) and others crafted by the authors. It features a mix of Likert- scale and open-ended questions. The analysis of the former type is segmented into descriptive statistics, group comparisons, and factor analysis. The latter is employed to reduce the number of variables, conﬁrm underlying latent variables, and obtain the respective factors scores (DiStefano et al., 2009) that can be compared across the different groups of students. The open-ended questions aimed to gather students’ views on using AI for learning and assessment, as well as their thoughts on future AI applications. The responses were examined by identifying shared characteristics and variations among different student groups. The paper is outlined as follows. Section",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,5,"gather students’ views on using AI for learning and assessment, as well as their thoughts on future AI applications. The responses were examined by identifying shared characteristics and variations among different student groups. The paper is outlined as follows. Section 2 lists beneﬁts and limitations of ChatGPT in education, as provided by ChatGPT, followed by authors’ comments on that infor- mation. Section 3 describes the study, including the participating students, the survey, the methodology for analyzing and presenting the results. Section 4 is devoted to the results. In Section 5, the authors describe their plans to use ChatGPT in their teaching 123",102
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,6,"654 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 activities pertaining to different areas of knowledge. Section 6 presents comments, discussion and outlook. 2 Beneﬁts and limitations of AI bots in education This section analyses the advantages and limitations of using AI bots in education as indicated by ChatGPT (OpenAI, 2023), and comments from the authors about them are provided. 2.1 Benefits According to Ma et al. (2014), an Intelligent Tutoring System (ITS) presents the infor- mation to be learned, asks questions, proposes assignments, provides feedback and prompts, with the goal of helping each student acquire the desired knowledge, be motivated, increase the meta-cognitive abilities, and help in becoming an independent learner. The ITS computes inferences by analyzing student interaction with the system (test scores and time spent on a given task, navigation, etc.), and constructs a multidi- mensional model of the student cognitive state. That model is then used to adjust and adapt all the activities involved in tutoring as described above. The idea that AI bots can function as a ITS is expressed in the answer given by ChatGPT (OpenAI,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,6,"student cognitive state. That model is then used to adjust and adapt all the activities involved in tutoring as described above. The idea that AI bots can function as a ITS is expressed in the answer given by ChatGPT (OpenAI, 2023) when prompted about the beneﬁts of its usage in education, as transcribed in the ﬁrst two paragraphs below. ‘Personalized Learning: ChatGPT can provide personalized learning experiences for students by analyzing their learning style, interests, and previous academic perfor- mance. This can help students to stay engaged and motivated in their studies.’ ‘Immediate Feedback: ChatGPT can provide instant feedback to students, helping them to identify areas of strength and weakness. This can help students to improve their academic performance and make more informed decisions about their learning.’ Another beneﬁt described by ChatGPT is: ‘Consistent Quality: ChatGPT can provide consistent quality in terms of teaching and feedback, reducing the variability in quality that can occur with human teachers. This can help to ensure that all students receive a high-quality learning experience, regardless of their location or socioeconomic status.’ Also referred as",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,6,"in terms of teaching and feedback, reducing the variability in quality that can occur with human teachers. This can help to ensure that all students receive a high-quality learning experience, regardless of their location or socioeconomic status.’ Also referred as advantages were ‘24/7 availability, cost effective way to deliver education in under served areas, and improved accessibility for students with disabil- ities’. 2.2 Limitations and challenges As to the limitations and challenges ChatGPT reports technical problems such as ‘internet connectivity and software glitches that can disrupt the learning process’, ‘lack of human interaction’ that is essential to ‘develop social skills, crucial for the personal andprofessional growth’, ‘potential for bias’, duetolimitations of datausedto train the large language models (LLM), inability to deal with complex and specialized 123",126
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 655 subjects that require ‘practical skills, and hands-on experience’, ‘privacy and security of students’ data’, ‘high development costs’, the need for ‘redesigning instruction and assessment’. 2.3 Comments It is difﬁcult to disagree with the information provided by ChatGPT concerning the advantages and limitation of its usage in education. However, the statements need conﬁrmation that can only be obtained with concrete studies where students use such AI bots, in the framework of different disciplines, of different social and economic environments, levels of knowledge, and taking into account all the different factors that are known to inﬂuence learning (Chaudhary & Singh, 2022). The idea that ChatGPT can provide ‘consistent high quality of teaching and feed- back’, independent of human teachers raises some questions, because it implies that it would be able to do that, regardless of how is being used. Authors’ experience in interacting with ChatGPT reveals that even if more powerful versions like GPT4 give more accurate information, and is able to solve problems in subjects like Physics and Mathematics, novices will have difﬁculties in",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,7,"how is being used. Authors’ experience in interacting with ChatGPT reveals that even if more powerful versions like GPT4 give more accurate information, and is able to solve problems in subjects like Physics and Mathematics, novices will have difﬁculties in prompting to get accurate, to the point, understandable and consistent information. If the basic knowledge is not sound, they might not be able to assess the relevance of the information obtained, and guide the AI tutor in order to build a correct understanding of the situations presented. Moreover, the text delivered may contain ideas that are wrong or biased due to the kind of data training the models. Critical thinking and solid base knowledge is crucial for interpretation (Plebani, 2023). It is expected that students will increasingly use AI for learning and completing academic tasks. Therefore, it is crucial to teach them how to ask precise questions, provide clear instructions, and interpret results. In classroom settings with numer- ous students, employing the Socratic method-a proven technique for fostering critical thinking (Zare & Mukundan, 2015)– can be challenging, also because of",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,7,"teach them how to ask precise questions, provide clear instructions, and interpret results. In classroom settings with numer- ous students, employing the Socratic method-a proven technique for fostering critical thinking (Zare & Mukundan, 2015)– can be challenging, also because of time lim- itations. Therefore, the idea that AI chatbots could be leverage to engage students in a Socratic type of interaction is appealing. However, without proper training and guidance there’s a risk that misconceptions could be reinforced rather than overcome, specially for undergraduates whose critical thinking skills my be less developed. A very important limitation referred in the ChatGPT feedback, has to do with student assessment. In fact, this seems to be an universal apprehension (Fyfe, 2023; Hadi Mogavi et al.,2024). Traditional homeworkassignments might not beappropriate anymore since plagiarism is now very difﬁcult if not impossible to detect. The user is anonymously replaced as the author of how ideas are explained and conveyed, although no references are given concerning the origin of the information, which of course, raises ethical concerns. Moreover, fairness in assessment can be called out, because not",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,7,"is anonymously replaced as the author of how ideas are explained and conveyed, although no references are given concerning the origin of the information, which of course, raises ethical concerns. Moreover, fairness in assessment can be called out, because not all students might be utilizing these tools. Formative assessment activities might be a possibility to cir- cumvent this problem, as well as to opt for face-to-face written and oral summative assessments. 123",72
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,8,"656 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 There are advantages and limitations that seem contradictory. On the one hand, an AI bot teaching assistant is more efﬁcient if it can retrieve data about the user to assess ‘learning style, interests, and previous academic performance’. But what about data privacy? Therefore, the problem of how to keep the information private has to be addressed (Glorin, 2023). Many suggestions on how to use these tools to learn can be made, but only the knowledge obtained from students and teachers feedback, and from controlled studies with randomized groups (Zhang et al., 2022; Clark et al., 2012), will be able to assess the real impact of AI bots in learning. Such studies can provide important information about how ChatGPT and similar technologies can be integrated in the instructional design in order to maximize learning, for each different area of knowledge. In areas related to science and engineering, experimentation might still not replace- able. Therefore, investing more on experimental training could be an important way to develop knowledge and insight of how relationships between physical",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,8,"for each different area of knowledge. In areas related to science and engineering, experimentation might still not replace- able. Therefore, investing more on experimental training could be an important way to develop knowledge and insight of how relationships between physical quantities that describe concepts and phenomena of the real world are mapped into mathematical models. AI bots can assist in experimental activities, by helping planning an experi- ment, deﬁning the important data to be gathered, the relevant measurement systems to obtain the data, and how to analyze it. From the perspective of teachers are researchers, AI bots can serve as powerful aids, streamlining tasks such as content creation for classes, plan personalized learning sequences, write proofread texts, translate, grade, organize a course, or a set of courses in a speciﬁc ﬁeld, proposing meaningful problems, lab assignments, etc (Noy & Zhang, 2023; Nazaretsky et al., 2022; Hadi Mogavi et al., 2024). For Higher Education instructors that lecture courses with large number of students, supervise master or doctoral degrees students, and do research, this serves as a mean to increase productivity. In",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,8,"2023; Nazaretsky et al., 2022; Hadi Mogavi et al., 2024). For Higher Education instructors that lecture courses with large number of students, supervise master or doctoral degrees students, and do research, this serves as a mean to increase productivity. In this case, the interpretation and evaluation of the information is a lesser problem since, in principle, there is a deeper understanding and ability for critical judgment. A possible concern is that AI bots comply with one basic law of nature that is a common features of human beings: taking the path of least effort. Relying on AI for various learning activities might lead to more superﬁcial knowledge, prevent acquiring independence of thought, and the development of learning habits that foster connecting ideas, analytical reasoning, critical thinking and openness to conﬂicting ideas. Similar apprehensions are reported in Hadi Mogavi et al. (2024), where a large scale analysis of texts written in social media platforms was conducted. Ultimately, the main goal of deploying AI chatbots for student training is to meet all the expected advantages that a personalized digital assistant can have",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,8,"(2024), where a large scale analysis of texts written in social media platforms was conducted. Ultimately, the main goal of deploying AI chatbots for student training is to meet all the expected advantages that a personalized digital assistant can have to promote meaningful learning, and, at the same time, avoid the most relevant concerns such as data privacy, biases, and lack of ethical concerns. Educational institutions should be oriented in deﬁning the relevant competences young generations have to acquire in order to face the future challenges, and establish- ing how these competences are assessed, to avoid being overtaken by rapidly changing circumstances (Stokel-Walker, 2023; Lund & Wang, 2023). Discussing guidelines and setting the rules (Kooli, 2023) on how to use AI bots responsibly and effectively is of major importance. 123",130
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 657 3 Description of the study As previously mentioned, this study primarily seeks to investigate how students uti- lize AI conversational tool ChatGPT during the initial phase of its widespread and unrestricted implementation. It also aims to inquire about their perspectives and appre- hensions regarding the use of AI in higher education settings and its potential future applications. Furthermore, with data available from groups of three European coun- tries and at different stages of their programs, another goal is to compare students’ feedback across those groups. To achieve these objectives, in May of 2023, a survey was carried out with students from three European countries who were enrolled in various scientiﬁc and techno- logical higher education programs at different points in their educational journey. A convenience sample was utilized, consisting of students taking courses taught by the authors at three distinct European higher education institutions. This section, describes the sample, the questionnaire used, the analysis performed and how the results are presented. 3.1 The sample Table 1 provides a summary of the higher education",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,9,"taught by the authors at three distinct European higher education institutions. This section, describes the sample, the questionnaire used, the analysis performed and how the results are presented. 3.1 The sample Table 1 provides a summary of the higher education institutions and the programs in which the surveyed students are enrolled. Slovak participants (SK) are from the Slovak University of Technology in Bratislava, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Information Technology. They were 189 in total mostly in the 3th year of the Applied Informatics bachelor study program (170). Four more students are from the Applied Informatics master study program and another 15 students study Mechatronics in either bachelor or master programs. Students from the Faculty of Engineering of the University of Porto in Portugal (PT1) are enrolled in three different engineering programs: Civil , Environmental, and Electrotechnical and Computers (Electr. & Comp.). The study was conducted in the context of the ﬁrst Physics course of the syllabus of each program. A total of 166 students completed the questionnaire. As to the Medicine students from the University of Porto (PT2) they",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,9,"& Comp.). The study was conducted in the context of the ﬁrst Physics course of the syllabus of each program. A total of 166 students completed the questionnaire. As to the Medicine students from the University of Porto (PT2) they are all post graduate, 11 in their master studies and 11 in the Health Data Science PhD program. Spanish students (SP) who participated in the study were enrolled in industrial engi- neering at the University of Almería. The majority specialized in industrial electronics Table 1 Institutions and programs Country University Faculty Program Slovakia Slovak University of Tech- nology in Bratislava Electrical Engineering and Information Technology Applied Informatics, Automotive Mechatronics Portugal University of Porto Engineering Medicine Environmental Civil Electr. & Comp. Medicine Spain University of Almería Superior Engineering School Industrial Electronics and Automatics Mechanical and Electrical Engineering 123",137
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,10,"658 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 and automatics, while some others pursued mechanical and electrical engineering. Overall, all students fell under the industrial engineering umbrella. The study took place during the fourth year in a course that typically has about 50 students, with 42 participating in this case. 3.2 The questionnaire The questionnaire is composed of 5-point Likert scale items along with open-ended questions. Items from 1 to 10 in Tables 6 and 7 in Appendix A where crafted by the authors. The ﬁrst three aimed at determining whether and when the participants had previously interacted with ChatGPT, as well as the extent of their interest in the AI bot as expressed in conversations with peers. Questions 4 to 10, inquire students that had already experience with ChatGPT about the purpose of using it, how it was used, if they are satisﬁed and trust the answers of ChatGPT and if any instructor had already asked them to use it in the context of a given course. Students who had already experienced with ChatGPT, were asked to answer the next",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,10,"they are satisﬁed and trust the answers of ChatGPT and if any instructor had already asked them to use it in the context of a given course. Students who had already experienced with ChatGPT, were asked to answer the next 31 Likert type questions, depicted in Tables 7 and 8 of Appendix A, as well as the ﬁve open ended items of Table 9. Finally, all the participants were requested to respond to the questions of Table 10 of Appendix A. Aiming at ensuring content validity of the questionnaire, most of the Likert-type items in Tables 7, 8 and 10 of the Appendix A where modiﬁed and adapted from previous published studies that explain how users accept a certain technology, intent to use it, and their subsequent usage behavior, like the ones found in references (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh et al., 2003; Indrawati & Putri, 2018; Camilleri & Camilleri, 2022; Urbano et al., 2020). 3.3 Analysis and presentation of the results To fulﬁll the main goals of the study, data analysis comprised different procedures: descriptive statistics, comparisons across the four different",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,10,"2003; Indrawati & Putri, 2018; Camilleri & Camilleri, 2022; Urbano et al., 2020). 3.3 Analysis and presentation of the results To fulﬁll the main goals of the study, data analysis comprised different procedures: descriptive statistics, comparisons across the four different groups of students des- ignated above as SK, PT1, PT2 and SP, and factor analysis (Hair et al., 2019). This last technique was employed with the aim of reducing the number of variables, by grouping the item responses in factors, whose scores (DiStefano et al., 2009) can than be analyzed and compared across groups. Tables and graphical representations are used to show the results of these analyses. With the answers to items 12 to 34 of the questionnaire, provided by 346 students who had already used ChatGPT, conﬁrmatory factor analysis (CFA) was conducted, and six latent variables were extracted, and the respective factor scores obtained. Similarly, two factors where extracted using items 40 to 46, answered by all the 419 participants. The latent variables obtained can be described as follows: Perceived Ease of Use (PEU) reﬂects the students’ perception on",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,10,"the respective factor scores obtained. Similarly, two factors where extracted using items 40 to 46, answered by all the 419 participants. The latent variables obtained can be described as follows: Perceived Ease of Use (PEU) reﬂects the students’ perception on how easy to use is ChatGPT. 123",47
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 659 Perceived Value (PV) can be viewed as a measure of how students feel ChatGPT is a good tool to support learning. Perceived Usefulness (PU) assesses students view on the usefulness of ChatGPT to increase academic performance and productivity. Enjoyment (Enj) measures students opinion on how enjoyable it is to use this AI bot. Willingness to Use (WU) assesses how students were willing to use ChatGPT in their academic tasks by the time they participated in the survey. Behavioral intention (BI) reﬂects participants intention to use ChatGPT and sim- ilar AI bots in the future. Using the answer to questions 40 to 46 from all participating students, two following two constructs were conﬁrmed: Concern about AI (CAI) factor reﬂects students concerns about the AI technol- ogy. Behavioral intention (BI1) can be viewed as a manifestation of students’ Intention to start using ChatGPT and similar AI bots in the context of their academic tasks and assignments. The technical details of this analysis can be found in Appendix B, where all the mea- sures validating the",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,11,"manifestation of students’ Intention to start using ChatGPT and similar AI bots in the context of their academic tasks and assignments. The technical details of this analysis can be found in Appendix B, where all the mea- sures validating the proposed structures are calculated and presented. Also discussed, are the limitations of the analysis. The comparisons across different groups of students were performed using the non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis test, which, unlike the ANOVA test, does not require normality of the distributions (Mishra et al., 2019). Also investigated are differences between each pair of the four groups of students The different aspects of the data analysis were conducted using software packages (IBM Corp., n.d) and Arbuckle (2019). In the next section, the results will be presented in the following way: • characterization of the participating students. • graphical representations for some items of the questionnaire, and respective com- parisons across groups of students. • graphical representations of the factor scores for PEU, Enj, PV, PU, WU and BI as well as BI1 and CAI, and respective comparisons across groups of students. •",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,11,"of the questionnaire, and respective com- parisons across groups of students. • graphical representations of the factor scores for PEU, Enj, PV, PU, WU and BI as well as BI1 and CAI, and respective comparisons across groups of students. • report of the open-ended questions, summarizing common features and stressing relevant ideas and opinions. 4 Results 4.1 Characterization of the participating students Table 2 shows the number of surveyed students from each Institution and Program, as well as the average age and percentage of female students. 123",87
