A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,20,"668 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 (H(3, 419) = 22.890, p < 0.001), and they are signiﬁcant between PT1 and SK (p = 0.001) and marginal between PT1 and PT2 (p = 0.025). 4.3 Open ended questions Table 9 illustrates the open-ended questions utilized to obtain personalized opinions from students. Not every student who previously reported having already used Chat- GPT answered to these open-ended questions. In this section, the authors present a synthesis of the gathered responses, providing some organization and interpretation. For that purpose common ideas conveyed by the students will be presented in tables or in lists. 4.3.1 If you were a teacher would you allow students to use ChatGPT during exams? Table 4 shows the percentage of respondents from each group that answered ‘yes’ or ‘no’, and the total number of responses. ‘Other’ means that students gave a more detailed answer. The engineering students from Portugal and from Spain wouldn’t allow ChatGPT during the exams, whereas Slovak and medicine Portuguese students are more posi- tive towards that notion. Future studies could explore the reasons behind",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,20,"students gave a more detailed answer. The engineering students from Portugal and from Spain wouldn’t allow ChatGPT during the exams, whereas Slovak and medicine Portuguese students are more posi- tive towards that notion. Future studies could explore the reasons behind the differing opinions on using AI chatbots in examinations. One possible explanation might be the subject area and type of knowledge being assessed. For example, students may ﬁnd it natural to use AI technology during exam assessment when programming skills are required. In contrast, when the evaluation emphasizes problem-solving that depends on speciﬁc conceptual and procedural knowledge in engineering ﬁelds, AI based assis- tance might not be viewed as positively. As to the other open-ended answers collected, one can summarize the most frequent opinions, expressed by all the groups as: • it depends on the exam. • it depends on the subject. • it depends on the context. Other opinions found across groups are • the use of ChatGPT in exams should be on par with the use of internet search engines. Table 4 Students react differently to the idea",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,20,the subject. • it depends on the context. Other opinions found across groups are • the use of ChatGPT in exams should be on par with the use of internet search engines. Table 4 Students react differently to the idea of letting ChatGPT be used in exams PT1 SK SP PT2 Yes 28.9% 65.0% 28.1% 57.1% No 62.8% 16.5% 68.8% 28.6% Other 8.30% 18.5% 3.10% 14.3% N. responses 121 157 32 21 123,73
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,21,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 669 • the basic general theoretical knowledge should be assessed without allowing for consultation, whereas in problem solving assessment, involving more complex tasks, ChatGPT should be allowed. • the assessment should be varied, with some portions allowing the use of ChatGPT and others where its use is prohibited. Here’s a transcript of some of the more representative answers. – It depends on the type and difﬁculty of the task. If the exam is more focused on knowledge students should master, probably no. But if the exam is focused on obtaining the solution for a complex set of tasks that the student has to code, than it seems the same as, for instance, the possibility for searching for solutions in stackoverﬂow. – Yes, since AI is the future and students have to adapt to the new technologies. – If we’ve been taught how to use ChatGPT during the semester, it would be helpful to have a special part in exams where we can use it. As revealed in the short texts, students are well aware",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,21,"new technologies. – If we’ve been taught how to use ChatGPT during the semester, it would be helpful to have a special part in exams where we can use it. As revealed in the short texts, students are well aware that assessment comprises different aspects, and suggest the usage of a tool such as ChatGPT only for certain types assessments. One way to interpret the negative answer given by most of PT1 students, is that being 1st year students, concepts and analytically resolution of problems still play the dominant role. This is regarded to be the more ‘theoretical’ part of the syllabuses, where they learn important tools, and solve mostly academic problems. The Applied Informatics students, on the other hand, mainly at the 3th year of their studies, have much more contact with coding and programming languages, and are already used to search the internet for coding solutions. For that purpose, ChaGPT can be a much more efﬁcient assistant. 4.3.2 What do you think about using ChatGPT or other AI chatbot in future? Students have both positive and negative views",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,21,"used to search the internet for coding solutions. For that purpose, ChaGPT can be a much more efﬁcient assistant. 4.3.2 What do you think about using ChatGPT or other AI chatbot in future? Students have both positive and negative views on the use of AI chatbots in the future. The prevailing positive contribution expressed is the possibility to increase productiv- ity by • speeding up programming • performing repetitive tasks • searching and aggregating information • planning projects • helping in decision making • essay writing • solving problems The list of negative consequences of using AI technologies such as ChatGPT in the future is shorter, with the concerns being • biased or wrong information provided • loosing or reducing the number of jobs 123",125
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,22,"670 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 • lack of regulation As expected, Slovak students of Applied Informatics were the ones seeing the main beneﬁt of the ChatGPT or AI in helping with programming. They refer the advantage in speeding up programming, as ChatGPT allows generating basic code from which building blocks can be created when creating larger applications, i.e. this tool allows reducing the amount of low level programming and writing routine repetitive code. As one student wrote nobody wants to write the same condition x times. Students also appreciate the help in debugging and tracking down programming errors that can be easily overlooked. So, students envision AI chatbots becoming essential in a programmer’s everyday routine. They understand the importance of mastering the skills required to efﬁciently interact with the large language models (LLM) powering these tools. Nonetheless, there are apprehensions that the prevalence of AI chatbots could lead to a reduced demand for programmers, potentially resulting in job displacement. Conversely, some argue that a human will always be needed, but they will be put in a position where",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,22,"are apprehensions that the prevalence of AI chatbots could lead to a reduced demand for programmers, potentially resulting in job displacement. Conversely, some argue that a human will always be needed, but they will be put in a position where they will compose answers from the AI chatbot and that is how larger projects will be built. As already mentioned, students see the second big bonus in speeding up their search for information on the Internet. There is no need to search through a large number of sources, but the information can be aggregated in one place. One student commented that ChatGPT often considers the drawbacks and limitations of a solution when explaining a problem, and this has personally broadened his horizons in the area of code security. Another student correctly noted that in the future, one can expect a battle between technology giants for primacy in the quality of AI results, similar to what has happened in the past with Internet search engines. As for writing essays, Applied Informatics students are not forced to write them very often. Rather,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,22,"battle between technology giants for primacy in the quality of AI results, similar to what has happened in the past with Internet search engines. As for writing essays, Applied Informatics students are not forced to write them very often. Rather, they express concern in this area that the use of AI chatbots can have a signiﬁcant impact on students’ vocabulary and therefore they would not recommend the use of these tools especially for high school students. Spanish students of Industrial Engineering also consider ChatGPT as useful tool that can be helpful in understanding new assignments and solving problems. However, they also think that today it’s not full developed for some tasks in university grades, specially in engineering. In the future AIs will become more and more useful and smarter. Portuguese Engineering students seem not to have a very concrete opinion on how AI could be used in the future. In fact, many of those expressing their opinion, either wrote it is a good idea or it is good to use it. Many other responses reveal that they are thinking in",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,22,"on how AI could be used in the future. In fact, many of those expressing their opinion, either wrote it is a good idea or it is good to use it. Many other responses reveal that they are thinking in the context of academic tasks, much more than in the broader context of utilization of AI in the working place. Other only state that it has advantages, but also disadvantages. Conveyed in some of the texts is the idea that AI serves to retrieve information and on that regards it can save a lot of time both to students and teachers. Some answers reveal awareness of the beneﬁts of AI tools to assist in more repetitive tasks, or to provide client support, and help in decision making. 123",128
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,23,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 671 As to the Portuguese medical students, from the masters program in Medicine and the PhD program in Health Data Science, they have shown to be thinking more on ChatGPT as a tool to increasing productivity, specially in research and professional environments. They believe on the inevitability of future usage of AI. They also pointed out that it is very helpful for non-English speaking researchers to communicate their ﬁndings more efﬁciently and increase the impact of their work. One of the students also referred that the sooner this tools are introduced in the education system the better prepared future generation will be. 4.3.3 Describe the most interesting experience with ChatGPT or other AI chatbot (positive or negative). Since students are majoring different areas, the positive and negative experiences they report show common, but also different perspectives. Common positive experiences are listed below • programming and debugging was facilitated. • accurate information was obtained quickly. • it helped in problem solving. • it was able to provide explanations for difﬁcult concepts. • in general topics,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,23,"different perspectives. Common positive experiences are listed below • programming and debugging was facilitated. • accurate information was obtained quickly. • it helped in problem solving. • it was able to provide explanations for difﬁcult concepts. • in general topics, it made valuable suggestions concerning movies, cooking, diet, physical activity, as well as helping in music creation and writing a theater play. As to negative experiences, the most frequently referred were • inaccuraciesandlimitationsinChatGPT’sresponses,particularlyintechnicalareas like mathematics. • occasional difﬁculties in obtaining information promptly due to high demand for the service, resulting in long waiting times. • ChatGPT sometimes struggled to maintain context during conversations or failed to provide satisfactory responses to speciﬁc queries, leading to frustration for users. Students of Applied informatics like the fact that they are able to prepare better understandable code. They were surprised at the speed and simplicity with which ChatGPT can generate code for anything they ask it to do. And they also like that it can explain the code line by line, evaluate their own code, give feedback on it, and maybe suggest a way",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,23,"simplicity with which ChatGPT can generate code for anything they ask it to do. And they also like that it can explain the code line by line, evaluate their own code, give feedback on it, and maybe suggest a way to write the same thing more efﬁciently or differently. ChatGPT can help to understand a complex assignment, explain it better and to tell what technology to use. Portuguese Engineering students highlighted positive experiences with ChatGPT, noting that it offered explanations that clariﬁed exercises within the context of Calculus or Numerical Analysis. They also mentioned requesting the answer to a conceptual multiple-choice question, where the bot elaborated on why a particular choice was correct while delineating why the others were incorrect. Students from Medical school, being at level of masters or PhD were more inclined to refer content creation, editing and brainstorm interactions to provide ideas as main positive aspects of their interaction with ChatGPT. In concrete they’ve point out that it 123",162
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,24,"672 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 helps in preparing texts, writing coherent reports, writing abstracts with a ﬁxed number of words, and even writing cover letter for job applications in the area of technology. Here’s a short list of speciﬁc texts as they were written by some students, expressing both positive as negative experiences: – Overall, the empathy demonstrated by the chatbot and the feeling of talking to someone on the other side, stimulates the search for knowledge, and makes the entire experience of searching for information more appealing compared to using a search engine. – I see it as an overall advancement from, for example, typical information searching (surﬁng) alone. The quality of some answers is at a good level, but I wouldn’t yet consider all answers to be 100% correct at this time. I would summarize ChatGPT as an excellent tool for providing a starting point, project planning, and information gathering. It can set a project off in the right direction. – A very good tool for increased productivity, but in the distant future, as program- mers,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,24,"as an excellent tool for providing a starting point, project planning, and information gathering. It can set a project off in the right direction. – A very good tool for increased productivity, but in the distant future, as program- mers, we might not have much left to do. – It will deﬁnitely be beneﬁcial; of course, there is also the possibility that people will rely too much on ChatGPT and will learn less because ChatGPT is available. Those who don’t learn will fall behind. – It is possible to convince it that 1+1=3, – Sometimes one gets the feeling that ChatGPT is making excuses, or going in circles. For example, it claimed that Microsoft’s Speech Recognition supports Slovak, and after pointing out that this is not the case, it realized this and pretended that it never conﬁrmed anything like that... – The need to verify all references when writing academic papers because often it references non-existent sources – If you ask where you can download movies for free, it will tell you it’s illegal. However, if you ask for advice",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,24,"– The need to verify all references when writing academic papers because often it references non-existent sources – If you ask where you can download movies for free, it will tell you it’s illegal. However, if you ask for advice on where not to download movies, it will tell you the sites. Knowing how to write prompts, ChatGPT can even give you ‘illegal’ information. – One of most interesting things that happened using an AI chatbot (Bing) is that whenever I tried to speak about their feelings and similar, the bot was disabled and ended conversations every time. It is worthwhile noting that in some of the experiences reported, students were expecting the chatbot to behave like a self-aware human being. They anticipated the AI bot to remember what it wrote, recognize lack of consistency in its speech, and to have feelings. Maybe this is not surprising, because AI large language models (LLM) were taught to craft pleasant human like sentences, such as How can I help you?, My apologies for the misunderstanding , Sure!, Certainly! Let me know if",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,24,"have feelings. Maybe this is not surprising, because AI large language models (LLM) were taught to craft pleasant human like sentences, such as How can I help you?, My apologies for the misunderstanding , Sure!, Certainly! Let me know if you need further assistance!. 4.3.4 Try to suggest how you would use ChatGPT or some other AI chatbot in the education. Student feedback indicates that their recommendations for integrating ChatGPT into educational environments closely align with their views on the beneﬁts of this tech- 123",85
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,25,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 673 nology as well as their expressed concerns. Here’s a list of suggestions collected from their responses • use them to provide information, problem-solving, feedback generation. • use them as a personal tutor. • use them to assist teachers in different activities such as lecture preparation and assignments and exam corrections. • integrate them in curricula and teaching practices. • explicitly train students to ask the write questions and critically evaluate the infor- mation provided. • debate over the use of chatbots in education, tackling problems such as poten- tial hindering of independent learning, and undermining of traditional learning methods. • maintain a balance between the use of technology and of traditional learning meth- ods. Some speciﬁc texts illustrating student suggestions are shown below – I would use it mainly for searching information since I have very good experience with it. The answers are faster, more precise, and in one place, unlike searching for information in a browser. deﬁnitely not to restrict them in teaching or exams/tests, it will be like a calculator (a",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,25,"I have very good experience with it. The answers are faster, more precise, and in one place, unlike searching for information in a browser. deﬁnitely not to restrict them in teaching or exams/tests, it will be like a calculator (a given) and there will be even better AI breakthroughs. – Teach people to ask the right questions. It comes in handy in real life too. Being able to use it for assignments at home and possibly searching for information online instead of ﬂipping through textbooks. Or to help in integrating disabled children. Assist teachers in teaching and creating tests. Or like Khan Academy and Duolingo, where AI will be a personal teacher for the student. – as a Socratic teacher – To create/solve new problems – Use it in a subject where the user has already some knowledge – AI bots can assist students with doubts in speciﬁc tasks, providing relevant infor- mation and step-by-step guidance. – For example, it can help teachers make their classes more dynamic and practical. Students should use it as a guiding tool when solving",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,25,"bots can assist students with doubts in speciﬁc tasks, providing relevant infor- mation and step-by-step guidance. – For example, it can help teachers make their classes more dynamic and practical. Students should use it as a guiding tool when solving a problem. – Summarizeinformation,createdescriptivetables,explaindifferencesbetweencon- cepts. – Personally, I don’t think it’s a suitable tool for use in education because I see many cases where the student just lets the tasks/code be generated and doesn’t further explore its meaning or try to learn from it, which, in my opinion, is a pity. From the students’ responses, we can derive several generalizations regarding the potential use of ChatGPT or other AI chatbots in education. Students suggest using AI chatbots for accessing information quickly, accurately, and conveniently, particu- larly for tasks such as searching, problem-solving, and generating feedback. There is a consensus among students on the potential for AI chatbots to serve as personal tutors, providing tailored support and guidance to individual learners. Students envision AI chatbots assisting teachers in various aspects of instruction, including lecture prepa- ration, assignment creation, exam corrections, and",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,25,"students on the potential for AI chatbots to serve as personal tutors, providing tailored support and guidance to individual learners. Students envision AI chatbots assisting teachers in various aspects of instruction, including lecture prepa- ration, assignment creation, exam corrections, and classroom activities. They also 123",45
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,26,"674 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 propose integrating AI chatbots into curricula and teaching practices. Also referred is the importance of training to ask the right questions and to critically evaluate the infor- mation provided by AI chatbots, promoting deeper understanding and independent learning. Some students raise concerns about the potential drawbacks of AI chatbots in education. They advocate for maintaining a balance between technology use and traditional learning approaches. Students suggest using AI chatbots to create and solve new problems, as well as for understanding exercise solutions, clarifying doubts, and providing resolution suggestions. AI chatbots are seen as tools to make classes more dynamic and practical, guiding students through problem-solving processes and aid- ing in summarizing information or explaining concepts. Some participants express reservations about overreliance on AI chatbots, cautioning against passive acceptance of generated tasks without further exploration or learning. 4.3.5 Did you try to use some other AI chatbot similar to ChatGPT? Table 5 provides information on the percentage of other chatbots mentioned by par- ticipants in each group, as well as the percentage of students",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,26,"exploration or learning. 4.3.5 Did you try to use some other AI chatbot similar to ChatGPT? Table 5 provides information on the percentage of other chatbots mentioned by par- ticipants in each group, as well as the percentage of students who indicated not having used any chatbot other than ChatGPT. First-year students primarily used ChatGPT, while a larger percentage of partici- pants at higher levels of their training also experimented with other AI chatbots. This variation in chatbot usage indicates differences among students at different stages of their educational journey. Also referred by some students were the applications Jasper and Midjourney. Clearly, by the time the survey took place, ChatGPT was the most popular AI chatbot. 5 Integrating ChatGPT in instruction The authors of this article are active in several areas of scientiﬁc knowledge and teach various subjects at their universities: physics, web technologies, automation, and health informatics. In the context of their subjects, they report their own experience with ChatGPT or similar AI chatbots and try to propose some ways to use these tools in teaching. Table 5 Students’",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,26,"universities: physics, web technologies, automation, and health informatics. In the context of their subjects, they report their own experience with ChatGPT or similar AI chatbots and try to propose some ways to use these tools in teaching. Table 5 Students’ reported use of other AI chatbots ChatGPT GitHub Copilot Bing Chat YouChat SK 56.1% 22.1% 18.8% 2.8% PT1 73.5% 6.1% 5.3% 1.5% PT2 50.0% 9.1% 36.4% SP 50.0 23.5 26.5 123",71
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,27,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 675 5.1 Web technologies (Slovak University of Technology in Bratislava) The subjects Web technologies I and Web technologies II are taught in the 3rd year of bachelor study in the Applied Informatics study program. The ﬁrst course is devoted to client-side programming using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The second course focuses on server-side programming using PHP (Python) and MySQL databases. Stu- dents who enrol in these courses usually already have sufﬁcient programming skills in other programming languages. During both courses, students have to complete several individual assignments. Except for that, their knowledge is tested by small programming tasks that have to be completed in the computer lab within a limited time slot (approx. 80 minutes). At least one task is devoted to basic control structures to verify that students are able to solve this type of assignment independently without the help of classmates. In the past, this was a standard procedure which had the advantage that the task could be formulated very precisely. While solving it, students could always surf the Internet as they",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,27,"type of assignment independently without the help of classmates. In the past, this was a standard procedure which had the advantage that the task could be formulated very precisely. While solving it, students could always surf the Internet as they will use this kind of programming in practice. Students very often used Stack Overﬂow (stackoverﬂow.com) or other similar discussion forums where they could ﬁnd partial solutions to their problems. Never happened that everything was solved there. This year, however, ChatGPT came along, which we, as teachers, were not prepared for. Since the students were allowed to use the Internet for solving tasks, they submitted the entire problem to ChatGPT to solve. The precisely worded task was solved so the students could immediately hand it to the teacher for checking. At this point, we realized we have a problem. So, how do we proceed further? Following students’ answers from the questionnaire, it is evident that ChatGPT has no problem solving tasks that can be easily algorithmized. Therefore, it is necessary to ﬁnd a way to use this ability and not look",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,27,"do we proceed further? Following students’ answers from the questionnaire, it is evident that ChatGPT has no problem solving tasks that can be easily algorithmized. Therefore, it is necessary to ﬁnd a way to use this ability and not look at it as an obstacle. ChatGPT will become an integral part of a programmer’s job, and we should prepare students for it. They should be able to deﬁne and formulate a problem to solve. This means that after explaining the theory and principles, assignments should be deﬁned more vaguely without specifying the exact sequence of steps to be solved. Programming the loops and conditions themselves will also be handled by ChatGPT. However, the student should be able to say which part of the code is supposed to do what. ChatGPT can also design the structure of the program itself, but the programmer should be able to assess whether the generated code is appropriate for the overall solution of the project. Perhaps for this purpose, it would be useful to give students problems using graph- ical sketches that clarify what needs",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,27,"the programmer should be able to assess whether the generated code is appropriate for the overall solution of the project. Perhaps for this purpose, it would be useful to give students problems using graph- ical sketches that clarify what needs to be done, but the problem itself would not be formulated verbally. It means it would not be possible to copy the text into ChatGPT and get the solution right away, but it would be necessary ﬁrst to clarify the process of how we want to solve the problem and formulate the individual tasks. It is likely that in the short future ChatGPT will also be able to process images, but there is no guarantee that it will interpret them correctly, i.e. it will need to be guided and controlled. This will be a task for students to master. In addition to help in writing program code, ChatGPT also brings other beneﬁts to programmers. 123",155
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,28,"676 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 First of all, ChatGPT, with its vast technical knowledge, could provide a second opinion on complicated problems and bring new ideas which were not considered in previous discussions. It’s been trained in a diverse range of programming languages, frameworks, libraries, and concepts, which means that it can assist with a wide variety of programming tasks and answer questions across different technologies. So, it can propose solutions using technologies that have not been considered before, along with the procedure to implement. Secondly, quick code examples or code snippets can be especially helpful when pro- grammers are looking for syntax or implementation ideas. Also, problem-solving and debugging the issues with ChatGPT, can help identify potential causes more quickly and guide programmers through the debugging process. Structuring the answers with code snippets also helps in learning those new approaches. Additionally, it can help with algorithm and data structure optimizing or selecting appropriate data structures. It can offer insights and suggestions to help programmers make informed decisions. Considering code quality, it can be used for documentation generation",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,28,"those new approaches. Additionally, it can help with algorithm and data structure optimizing or selecting appropriate data structures. It can offer insights and suggestions to help programmers make informed decisions. Considering code quality, it can be used for documentation generation and also as an improvement for standardized generic code writing, which is time-consuming. Even in the case of not providing a full-scale solution, it can guide the programmer in the right direction. All of these beneﬁts make work easier, and time of the task can be shortened. The students have already ﬁgured this out and it is also evident from the results of the questionnaire. Therefore, it would be appropriate if this area was also supported in teaching. Finally, let’s try to summarize suggestions that might be useful in teaching pro- gramming courses: • not to prohibit students from using ChatGPT during their programming, • to let students formulate and algorithmize the tasks to be programmed since it allows them to specify the requirement for ChatGPT better, • enable students to reduce manual work with ChatGPT and leave the more",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,28,"ChatGPT during their programming, • to let students formulate and algorithmize the tasks to be programmed since it allows them to specify the requirement for ChatGPT better, • enable students to reduce manual work with ChatGPT and leave the more creative work to them, • to support them in debugging their code via ChatGPT. 5.2 Undergraduate physics (Faculty of Engineering, University of Porto) The suggestions for using ChatGPT in undergraduate Physics teaching/learning aim to develop some of the skills referred to in the learning outcomes stated by the European and American accreditation agencies for engineering programs (European Network for Accreditation of Engineering Education, 2021; Accreditation Board for Engineering and Technology, Inc, 2021), namely, to develop the ability to communicate effectively and to develop critical thinking capability. Other goals are to increase motivation and to expose students to a technology that most certainly will be relevant in their future. Undergraduate Physics courses, common to many engineering programs and cover- ing Classical Mechanics, Electromagnetism, Thermodynamics and Waves, introduce some of the most fundamental concepts and universal language for all experimental- based scientiﬁc",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,28,"most certainly will be relevant in their future. Undergraduate Physics courses, common to many engineering programs and cover- ing Classical Mechanics, Electromagnetism, Thermodynamics and Waves, introduce some of the most fundamental concepts and universal language for all experimental- based scientiﬁc disciplines. 123",42
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,29,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 677 There is evidence, and our own experience supports it, that students have difﬁculties in mastering the concepts, even if they are reasonably skillful in problem-solving (Crouch & Mazur, 2001). This fact is visible in the way they answer conceptual multiple-choice questions addressing, for instance, Newton’s laws of motion or basic direct current circuits concepts. In fact, most 1st year students are non-newtonian or pre-newtonian as far as their understanding of the motion of macroscopic objects with non-relativistic speeds at the surface of the earth (Pérez-Lemonche et al., 2019). Since there is a big probability that students are going to use chat-bots in their study- ing, it is advisable that teachers experience it in order to assess the tool. Knowing how the bot performs in certain Physics knowledge, will help instructors devise strategies that can guide the trainees in the best way to explore the tool and, most importantly, train them to have a critical-thinking spirit. To that end, ChatGPT versions 3.5 and 4, were tested in different contexts: request the right choice in",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,29,"can guide the trainees in the best way to explore the tool and, most importantly, train them to have a critical-thinking spirit. To that end, ChatGPT versions 3.5 and 4, were tested in different contexts: request the right choice in conceptual multiple choice questions; request formulation of thought-provoking questions related to physics concepts, in particular to Newton’s laws of motion scenarios; convert some of the questions provided to multiple choice questions and answer them; getting an answer for a situation that describes a given graphical representation by text. One can summarize the feedback of ChatGPT in the following way: • the answer to the same multiple choice question was different on different days, and sometimes the explanations were contradictory and inconsistent, revealing similar misconceptions shown by students. • appropriate prompting can lead to the correct answers with good explanations. • wrong results are given for simple calculations. • ChatGPT4seemstohaveabetterperformancefromthepointofviewofconceptual explanations and calculations. • there is information that is implied in the description of given scenarios that is not well interpreted, thus requiring different rounds of prompting. Raising more concern might",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,29,"wrong results are given for simple calculations. • ChatGPT4seemstohaveabetterperformancefromthepointofviewofconceptual explanations and calculations. • there is information that is implied in the description of given scenarios that is not well interpreted, thus requiring different rounds of prompting. Raising more concern might be the fact that novice students will have difﬁculties in identifying inconsistent and wrong information. Instead of correcting misconceptions, this could contribute to deepening them. Our task as instructors is to avoid leaving students completely on their own in experiencing these tools. Therefore, they should be trained in utilizing the technology to explore its many advantages, some of which are closely aligned with the referred learning outcomes. As theresults of thesurveyindicate, alargepercentageof students don’t rephrasethe questions very often. Through ChatGPT, instructors can promote clear and objective writing, stressing the importance of using the appropriate vocabulary, and engagement in conversations, which may lead to a better understanding of the physics situations and concepts involved. At the same time, it trains written communication. Students have shown a somewhat cautious opinion about the quality of the infor- mation retrieved, and the number of",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,29,"may lead to a better understanding of the physics situations and concepts involved. At the same time, it trains written communication. Students have shown a somewhat cautious opinion about the quality of the infor- mation retrieved, and the number of those who always trust the information is very small. This might be a good starting point to help students develop critical thinking abilities by exploring different alternatives to convince themselves of the correctness of the responses to their prompts. In the end, students should feel that they are the ones guiding the AI chatbot to give a scientiﬁcally sound explanation or result. 123",103
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,30,"678 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 Since this is relatively recent tool, its integration in the instructional design of a subject that deals with the most basic concepts and relationships that govern nature’s behavior, has to be done with caution. Below, the ﬁrst steps towards using the chabots in the context of undergraduate Physics teaching/learning for engineers are outlined. • Brieﬂy explain what AI chats bots are, in which algorithms they are based, and what can be the advantages and disadvantages of using them as learning assistants. • Give some tips on how they can use the bots to organize their study. • Promote the use of ChatGPT in class and train students in prompting. • Promote discussions around the information obtained, and help students analyze it in the framework of the laws of Physics. • In the context of formative assessment, in problem solving, as well as in conceptual questions tests, ask students to use ChatGPT, and to report all the steps performed, including all the prompts, the answers obtained, and how they interpret them. • Give feedback",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,30,"of formative assessment, in problem solving, as well as in conceptual questions tests, ask students to use ChatGPT, and to report all the steps performed, including all the prompts, the answers obtained, and how they interpret them. • Give feedback about the way they are utilizing the Chatbots. • Inform about the possible harms of AI usage in general, and why preventing academic and scientiﬁc fraud is important. • ChatGPT and other chatbots should not be allowed in individual face-to-face sum- mative assessment, i.e., tests and exams. Veriﬁcation that competences are being acquired cannot be left to chance. The actions proposed aim at training students how to think when confronted with situations that require an analyzes purely based on concepts and relationships between physical quantities that describe the phenomena at hand. By doing so, another impor- tant purpose can be achieved, to instill respect for nature. Only by respecting the laws of nature can engineers be able to fulﬁll their goals of conceiving, designing, building and operating systems, devices and structures that contribute to the well being of the populations.",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,30,"achieved, to instill respect for nature. Only by respecting the laws of nature can engineers be able to fulﬁll their goals of conceiving, designing, building and operating systems, devices and structures that contribute to the well being of the populations. 5.3 Courses of the medicine program and (Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto) and of the master in medical informatics Three key activities are planned in the fourth-year course DECIDES III, of the Medicine program, covering the topics Decision, Data, and Digital Health. The ﬁrst activity employs ChatGPT as an assistant to explain medical data science concepts in simple terms. This method not only makes complex topics more accessible but also frees up instructor time for more advanced discussions. The objective is to build a strong foundational knowledge in medical data science while boosting student engagement. For the second activity, a specialized ChatGPT assistant will tackle complex issues. The assistant can provide nuanced insights, supporting evidence, and even play devil’s advocate to stimulate discussion. The educational aim is to cultivate critical thinking, problem-solving skills, and a nuanced understanding of ethical",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,30,"a specialized ChatGPT assistant will tackle complex issues. The assistant can provide nuanced insights, supporting evidence, and even play devil’s advocate to stimulate discussion. The educational aim is to cultivate critical thinking, problem-solving skills, and a nuanced understanding of ethical and practical consid- erations in digital health. 123",48
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,31,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 679 Lastly, the course introduces clinical narratives and patient simulators. Simulators offer a risk-free environment for practicing skills, while narratives can be used to explore decision-making processes and ethical dilemmas in patient care. Together, these tools aim to offer hands-on experience and bolster the practical understanding of patient care, making students better prepared for real-world medical situations. For the ﬁrst-year course of the master in Medical Informatics in Health Information Systems and Electronic Health Records, multiple activities are planned. ChatGPT will act as an assistant in project planning, providing frameworks, check- lists, and real-world examples. This aims to impart project management skills crucial for the successful development and implementation of health information systems, thereby increasing the likelihood of project success and reducing system inefﬁcien- cies. Students will engage in coding exercises facilitated by ChatGPT, aimed to improve their programming skills essential in medical informatics. This personalized learning experience allows students to work at their own pace, ensuring a deeper understanding of the coding principles and practices. Additionally, several practical exercises are planned to further",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,31,"aimed to improve their programming skills essential in medical informatics. This personalized learning experience allows students to work at their own pace, ensuring a deeper understanding of the coding principles and practices. Additionally, several practical exercises are planned to further enhance the learning experience in medical informatics: • Data Privacy Challenge: Students can be tasked with identifying potential vulnera- bilities in a mock EHR (Electronic Health Record) system and proposing solutions to improve data privacy. • API Integration Exercise: Students can work on connecting a health monitoring device to an existing EHR system using APIs (Application Programming Inter- faces), thereby gaining hands-on experience in system interoperability. • Machine Learning Mini-Project: A simpliﬁed predictive modeling task can be given, where students would need to predict patient outcomes based on a given set of features. Students will use Python and scikit-learn to build and evaluate their models. • Workﬂow Optimization: Students can be given a case study of a healthcare facility with certain inefﬁciencies. They would then map out the workﬂow and propose improvements that can be facilitated by medical informatics. These",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,31,"build and evaluate their models. • Workﬂow Optimization: Students can be given a case study of a healthcare facility with certain inefﬁciencies. They would then map out the workﬂow and propose improvements that can be facilitated by medical informatics. These exercises are designed to not only improve technical skills but also to culti- vate a mindset of problem-solving and practical application in the realm of medical informatics. To improve test-taking and content retention, ChatGPT will facilitate mock exams with real-time, instant feedback. This iterative learning approach aims to bolster long-term retention and understanding, thereby making students more effective and conﬁdent learners. Furthermore, we intend to introduce students to the concept of ‘prompt engineering’, a technique that teaches them how to articulate questions or prompts that result in more targeted and useful responses from the AI. This not only aids in their academic pursuits but also empowers them to customize their learning experience. The blend of traditional exam preparations with modern AI-facilitated learning methods introduces a dynamic, personalized learning environment where students can continually adapt and focus on areas that need",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,31,"their academic pursuits but also empowers them to customize their learning experience. The blend of traditional exam preparations with modern AI-facilitated learning methods introduces a dynamic, personalized learning environment where students can continually adapt and focus on areas that need improvement. 123",42
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,32,"680 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 Additional activities will focus on exploring workﬂows and information ﬂows within healthcare systems. Practical exercises will simulate real-world scenarios, enabling students to understand bottlenecks and inefﬁciencies, with the aim of improv- ing system design and patient care. Finally, students will prepare for technical questions likely to appear in professional interviews by leveraging ChatGPT to simulate a wide range of possible questions and scenarios. This not onlyaims toenhancetheir employabilitybyfamiliarizingthemwith potential interview questions but also aids them in articulating their knowledge and skills effectively. Importantly, the course aims to equip students with the proﬁciency to work with advanced tools like ChatGPT, a skill increasingly valued in the job market. As artiﬁcial intelligence and machine learning continue to penetrate various aspects of healthcare and medical research, the ability to interact and extract meaningful insights from these systems will be a strong asset. Hence, students will not only leave the course well-prepared for interviews but also well-equipped for a future job market that demands adeptness in working with intelligent systems like ChatGPT. 5.4 Industrial control techniques (University",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,32,"systems will be a strong asset. Hence, students will not only leave the course well-prepared for interviews but also well-equipped for a future job market that demands adeptness in working with intelligent systems like ChatGPT. 5.4 Industrial control techniques (University of Almería, Spain) Most of the students participating in this study belong to the subject Industrial control techniques, which is taught in the 4th year of the Industrial Engineering degree in Electronics and Automatics at the University of Almería. The main objective of this subject is to combine and apply the knowledge acquired during the ﬁrst three years of the bachelor degree on modelling and process control to solve industrial control problems. Thus, students must use concepts related to signal processing, linearization, control design, simulation, coding, reporting results, and oral presentations. Theoret- ical lectures are more oriented to understand how to combine and design the different control algorithms to solve an industrial problem. Lab sessions are focused on coding (in blocks with Simulink and in code with Matlab) their own simulator of a real sys- tem and the control system",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,32,"to combine and design the different control algorithms to solve an industrial problem. Lab sessions are focused on coding (in blocks with Simulink and in code with Matlab) their own simulator of a real sys- tem and the control system architecture developed to solve several given problems. On the other hand, at the beginning of the course, an industrial problem based on a simulator using experimental data (every year the benchmark problem is changed with a different process such as an industrial boiler, a wind mill, a greenhouse, an industrial refrigeration system, a solar collector ﬁeld, etc.) is provided to the students to be studied and solved as a case study along the semester. The students are organized in couples, and they have to propose a step-by-step solution from the system modeling to the ﬁnal control algorithm. These benchmark problems are evaluated as internal competition among the different groups of students. During the last year, students have used ChatGPT as support for some of the activ- ities in the course, namely the following: • to recall mathematical concepts related to",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,32,"are evaluated as internal competition among the different groups of students. During the last year, students have used ChatGPT as support for some of the activ- ities in the course, namely the following: • to recall mathematical concepts related to signal processing, such as the use of the Laplace transform or Z transform, • to look for examples of ﬁrst-principles modeling and linearization of nonlinear systems, • to summarize the PID tuning rules that will be used for control design purposes, 123",82
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,33,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 681 • to get tips on Matlab coding and examples on the implementation of control algo- rithms, • to obtain ideas on how to solve the proposed benchmark problem based on similar industrial control problems. However, the students’ experience using ChatGPT was very isolated, and they only used it to obtain particular ideas in very speciﬁc moments along the academic course. After discussing the use of AI chatbots with students to make them understand about the potential of these tools, and based on the lecturer’s experience, we deﬁned a list of very interesting uses that can be exploited in the future to contribute in the learning activities, which are summarized in the following: • First, many students still think that the use of ChatGPT is completely forbidden. Therefore, the use of ChatGPT as a learning support tool by the students will be included in the subject program as a positive action. • Provide suggestions to use ChatGPT to summarize and remember the basic mod- elling and control concepts learned during the ﬁrst three years",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,33,"learning support tool by the students will be included in the subject program as a positive action. • Provide suggestions to use ChatGPT to summarize and remember the basic mod- elling and control concepts learned during the ﬁrst three years of the bachelor. Now, some students have used it to ask for speciﬁc topics, but it could be used to create a summary of the content with questions and exercises to revise the most important concepts to be used in the subject. • Most engineering students have limited knowledge of coding and typically are required to code in Matlab. Thus, to contribute in improving this aspect, Python- based implementations will be required, where ChatGPT will be used to look for examples and required libraries. • Look for control algorithm combinations available in industry such as PID con- trollers with antiwindup, with feedforward compensator, with gain-scheduling capabilities, with decouplers, etc. • Use AI chatbots as a support to write reports and prepare presentations to improve writing and synthesis skills. However, there are some pending issues that AI chatbots need to solve",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,33,"with feedforward compensator, with gain-scheduling capabilities, with decouplers, etc. • Use AI chatbots as a support to write reports and prepare presentations to improve writing and synthesis skills. However, there are some pending issues that AI chatbots need to solve about block programming and block diagrams. In engineering, and especially in control engineer- ing, block diagrams are used frequently for design purposes, which can be translated directly into block coding using software environments like Simulink. In this sense, the current AI chatbots like ChatGPT do not provide this type of functionality, which would be a really powerful tool for future educational assistant activities. 6 Discussion and outlook This study examines the reactions of students in science and technology programs from three European countries to using ChatGPT and similar AI chatbots for learning support. The research compares the reaction and perspectives of student groups from different countries, programs, and academic stages, including ﬁrst-year engineering students (PT1), third-year Applied Informatics students from Slovakia (SK), fourth- year Spanish students in industrial electronics and automation (SP), and Portuguese 123",175
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,33,"of student groups from different countries, programs, and academic stages, including ﬁrst-year engineering students (PT1), third-year Applied Informatics students from Slovakia (SK), fourth- year Spanish students in industrial electronics and automation (SP), and Portuguese 123",35
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,34,"682 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 graduate students in Health Data Science masters or doctoral programs. Additionally, the study explores teachers’ perspectives on integrating AI chatbots into the instruc- tional settings of courses taught to the student groups mentioned earlier. The conducted research was carried out on a sample of more than 400 respondents from three different countries. Although this sample is sufﬁciently big enough, we point out some limitations of the present study. It uses convenience samples, which strongly limits generalization. Also, compar- isons across groups have to be taken cautiously mainly due to unbalanced numbers of respondents in each group. Due to the fact that it took place during the initial phase of widespread and unrestricted implementation of AI chatbots, parts of the survey, crafted by the authors, lack scrutiny. On the positive side, most of the items included are adopted and based on established models that can be found in the literature, pro- viding its content validity. Another limitation is that it conveys the perspectives of a small group of educators concerning integration of ChatGPT and",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,34,"items included are adopted and based on established models that can be found in the literature, pro- viding its content validity. Another limitation is that it conveys the perspectives of a small group of educators concerning integration of ChatGPT and AI chatbots in gen- eral, in different settings, all belonging to science and technology subjects in Higher Education. Nevertheless, the conclusion and perspectives presented can be compared to other similar and very recent works, some new insights, prospects for future works and implications can be a value for many instructors and researchers. The key ﬁndings from the study on students’ use of ChatGPT can be summarized as follows: • Most students began using ChatGPT in 2023, speciﬁcally within the four months preceding the survey. • Usage frequency varied, with most students using it several times a week. First- year engineering students from Portugal reported the lowest usage. • Over half of the students utilized ChatGPT primarily for searching information, programming, debugging code, and rephrasing text. • The quality of information retrieved from ChatGPT was generally met with satis- faction; however,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,34,"students from Portugal reported the lowest usage. • Over half of the students utilized ChatGPT primarily for searching information, programming, debugging code, and rephrasing text. • The quality of information retrieved from ChatGPT was generally met with satis- faction; however, its trustworthiness was less favorably viewed, particularly among the Portuguese ﬁrst-year students. • Despite not showing heavily using it for academic tasks (WU) at the time of the survey, students who had already experienced with the tool, found ChatGPT valuable for supporting learning (PV), easy to use (PEU), useful as an academic assistant (PU), enjoyable (Enj), and showed a positive intention to use it in the future (BI1). • When accounting for students who had not used ChatGPT at the time of the survey, the results indicate that they hold a neutral stance on the potential future use of ChatGPT (BI1). Also, they express not being very concerned regarding its application in academic settings. The comparisons between groups reveal that students generally exhibit similar responses across the board. However, most of the observed differences occurred between groups PT1 and SK,",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,34,"(BI1). Also, they express not being very concerned regarding its application in academic settings. The comparisons between groups reveal that students generally exhibit similar responses across the board. However, most of the observed differences occurred between groups PT1 and SK, between PT1 and PT2. Notably, concerning their inten- tions to use ChatGPT in the future, these differences were signiﬁcant between PT1 and all the other groups. 123",67
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,35,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 683 Several reasons for the observed differences in student responses to using Chat- GPT might be suggested. First-year students are often still adjusting to the demands of higher education, including the volume of content and a new learning pace, and have not yet identiﬁed their most effective learning strategies. This adjustment period may make it challenging for them to integrate AI chatbots like ChatGPT into their learn- ing activities, especially without encouragement from their instructors. Additionally, the subject matter also plays a role in the differing perceptions. For example, ﬁrst- year engineering students, who often face rigorous math and physics courses, view ChatGPT as unreliable in these areas. Conversely, students more involved in coding, debugging activities, and dealing with medical data base system, like those in the SK and PT2 groups, ﬁnd ChatGPT beneﬁcial for enhancing efﬁciency. Analysis of open-ended questions indicates that at the time of the survey, students were not frequently using other chatbots besides ChatGPT. The responses also indicate that students have a keen understanding of various facets of academic assessment.",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,35,"for enhancing efﬁciency. Analysis of open-ended questions indicates that at the time of the survey, students were not frequently using other chatbots besides ChatGPT. The responses also indicate that students have a keen understanding of various facets of academic assessment. In fact, some students recommend that different types of content and knowledge should be assessed in distinct ways. Speciﬁcally, they propose that assessments of theoretical knowledge should not permit interactions with chatbots (or any other forms of assistance). Conversely, they suggest that for more complex tasks like problem-solving and project development, the use of an assistant tool like ChatGPT should be permitted. The gathered views and concerns about using ChatGPT in educational settings are aligned with the ones expressed in studies such as Ayanwale and Ndlovu (2024); Ibrahim et al. (2023); Hadi Mogavi et al. (2024). ChatGPT is viewed as good tool to increase productivity, mainly by searching and aggregating information, coding and debugging. In the present study, which surveyed students from various science and technology ﬁelds, the research identiﬁed differing reactions, particularly regarding the quality of responses in the",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,35,"good tool to increase productivity, mainly by searching and aggregating information, coding and debugging. In the present study, which surveyed students from various science and technology ﬁelds, the research identiﬁed differing reactions, particularly regarding the quality of responses in the context of rigorous scientiﬁc subjects, such as those found in the initial years of engineering programs. In Hadi Mogavi et al. (2024) early adopters’ utilization and perspectives about ChatGPT in education were investigated by analyzing text from different social media platforms. In Higher Education, most of the feedback described appears to be from researchers, instructors or PhD students. They mainly refer as main advantages the support in content creation and editing, content summarizing, and collaborative data analysis. This is in line with the feedback PT2 master and PhD students provided. Such users are evidently more able to better scrutiny the information retrieved, and more precisely guide the chatbot to meet their needs. From the present work it seems clear that the perspectives change according to the level of experience and the domain of the learner. From the point of view",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,35,"information retrieved, and more precisely guide the chatbot to meet their needs. From the present work it seems clear that the perspectives change according to the level of experience and the domain of the learner. From the point of view of the teachers, common concerns about integrating chatbots in instruction are similar to those found in the literature (Rudolph et al., 2023; Fyfe, 2023; Kooli, 2023). Two of them seem to be more prevalent: academic integrity and quality of learning. In particular, there is a broad concern that chatbots will replace students as authors of the academic assignments, without proper acknowledgment. On the other hand, relying on AI, may contribute to superﬁcial knowledge and impair the development of abilities such as analytical reasoning and critical thinking. 123",127
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,36,"684 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 A shared perspective is that AI technology is here to stay and that its use in academic settings will become more prevalent. Prohibiting its use seems unrealistic and could be more harmful than advantageous. The recommendations presented in this study aim to integrate AI chatbots into educational activities, helping students develop skills such as problem-solving in engineering and physics, evaluating solutions, formulating and algorithmizing tasks, debugging code, identifying vulnerabilities, optimizing workﬂows, recalling and sum- marizing complex concepts, and brainstorming ideas for benchmark problems. To optimize the use of AI, it is crucial that teachers familiarize themselves with AI tech- nology, experimenting with it to better understand its capabilities and limitations in relation to student assignments. Additionally, creating rubrics and offering clear grad- ing and feedback for AI-assisted activities can help guide students and enhance AI’s role as a learning tool. To address concerns regarding academic integrity and fairness, AI-based activities should be primarily used in formative assessments. While previous studies have recognized both the beneﬁts and drawbacks of using ChatGPT in education, this",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,36,"enhance AI’s role as a learning tool. To address concerns regarding academic integrity and fairness, AI-based activities should be primarily used in formative assessments. While previous studies have recognized both the beneﬁts and drawbacks of using ChatGPT in education, this study introduces a novel insight: the perceived advantages and challenges of AI chatbot usage vary signiﬁcantly depending on the subject matter and the stage of the learning process. This distinction reveals that there is no one-size- ﬁts-all approach to incorporating AI into education, as strategies must be adapted to the speciﬁc learning context. As a result, this study not only identiﬁes the need for customized AI integration strategies but also offers practical, subject-speciﬁc recommendations that can serve as a guide for instructors in higher education. This tailored approach represents a new perspective by highlighting how AI can be effectively integrated across different disciplines and educational levels. While it’s true that ChatGPT has its limitations, such as training on a data set that becomes outdated over time, it’s essential to recognize the transformative potential of AI in education and beyond. From",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,36,"across different disciplines and educational levels. While it’s true that ChatGPT has its limitations, such as training on a data set that becomes outdated over time, it’s essential to recognize the transformative potential of AI in education and beyond. From a developer’s standpoint, the current freely available model may seem outdated; however, its foundational capabilities can still offer value in educational and programming contexts. But it’s equally important to stay vigilant about the data limitations and steer clear of implementing deprecated libraries or obsolete coding techniques. In the realm of education, ChatGPT’s inability to browse the internet in real-time (in the time of questionnaire conducting) could be seen as a limitation. However, it can also be viewed as an opportunity to teach students critical thinking and research skills, as the AI can guide but not spoon-feed information. With OpenAI’s introduction of a premium, internet-connected version of ChatGPT, this limitation is being addressed, paving the way for a more interactive and updated educational experience. Even though ChatGPT can’t cite real-time resources, the information it provides serves as a strong foundational base",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,36,"of a premium, internet-connected version of ChatGPT, this limitation is being addressed, paving the way for a more interactive and updated educational experience. Even though ChatGPT can’t cite real-time resources, the information it provides serves as a strong foundational base for learners and developers alike. What it lacks in up-to-the-minute data, it makes up for in breadth and depth of knowledge, presenting an overview of a wide array of topics that can be reﬁned through further research. Moving forward, the key to maximizing the utility of AI chatbots like ChatGPT lies in the synergy between machine learning models and human oversight. Educators and 123",104
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,37,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:649–692 685 Table 6 Frequency of use and purpose of usage of ChaGPT Number Question 1 When did you use ChatGPT for the ﬁrst time? 2 Do you use ChatGPT regularly? 3 I discuss with my colleagues about ChatGPT programmers should use AI as a complementary tool, rather than a replacement for traditional teaching and development methods. In this symbiotic relationship, AI can handle routine queries and offer basic guidance, freeing up human experts to focus on more complex and nuanced tasks. As OpenAI continues to update and reﬁne models like ChatGPT, we can expect even more enhanced features and up-to-date information, further bridging the gaps that currently exist. The future is bright, and the potential for AI to revolutionize both programming and education is immense. But like any powerful tool, its effectiveness will be determined by how well we understand its capabilities and limitations, and how thoughtfully we integrate it into our workﬂows. In parallel, fostering critical thinking is vital for both assimilation of knowledge, nurturing independence in learning, and conﬁdence in the ability",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,37,"determined by how well we understand its capabilities and limitations, and how thoughtfully we integrate it into our workﬂows. In parallel, fostering critical thinking is vital for both assimilation of knowledge, nurturing independence in learning, and conﬁdence in the ability to assess and ana- lyze the information. As learners navigate the landscape of new technologies, it is important that they neither fear them nor blindly trust and use them. Instead, they ought to be educated in trusting their own judgment, foresee consequences of misuse, and understand how to use this particular technology in a productive way, without compromising ethical standard values. Appendix A: The questionnaire The ﬁrst part of the survey is shown in Table 6, and was answered by all students. The second part of the survey consisted of questions from 4 to 34 and was answered by 346 students who already had used ChatGPT. The items of Table 7 were crafted by the authors to assess the level of trust and satisfaction with ChatGPT interaction. Items of Table 8 are inspired in the works of references (Davis, 1989;",180
A080,A080.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13184-y,37,"students who already had used ChatGPT. The items of Table 7 were crafted by the authors to assess the level of trust and satisfaction with ChatGPT interaction. Items of Table 8 are inspired in the works of references (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh et al., 2003; Indrawati & Putri, 2018; Camilleri & Camilleri, 2022; Urbano et al., 2020) A third part of the questionnaire is composed of ﬁve open-ended questions. The fourth part of the survey comprised seven question, answered by all students Finally students were asked about their age and sex. Items 12 to 34, as well as items 40 to 46 are measured in a 5 point Likert scale, where 5 corresponds to “Totally agree”, 4 to “Agree”, 3 to “Neutral opinion”, 2 to “Disagree “, and 1 to “Totally disagree”. Appendix B: Measurement model and factor scores A conﬁrmatory factor analysis was performed to assess whether items 12 to 34 from Tables 7 and 8, which were answered by students already familiar with ChatGPT, are 123",167
