A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"Yan et al. 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286 Frontiers in Psychology 13 frontiersin.org Because the two-stage interaction terms are products of standardised scores and are not themselves standardised, their f2 values are com­ puted from the change in R2 when each term is omitted and cannot be recovered from their path coefficients alone. Predictive relevance was assessed with PLSpredict (k = 10 folds, 10 repetitions). Q2predict was 0.214 for GenAI feedback literacy and 0.344 for critical thinking, both above zero, indicating that the model has out-of-sample predictive relevance for both endogenous constructs. The standardised root mean square residual (SRMR) of the estimated model was 0.073, below the 0.08 threshold (Henseler et al., 2016). 5 Discussion This study set out to test a moderated mediation model linking generative artificial intelligence use to university students’ self- reported critical thinking, with GenAI feedback literacy as the mecha­ nism and reflective thinking as a boundary condition. Three findings stand out. First, GenAI use was positively related to critical thinking, both directly when modelled alone and through GenAI feedback lit­ eracy when both were entered together. Second, the inclusion",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"nism and reflective thinking as a boundary condition. Three findings stand out. First, GenAI use was positively related to critical thinking, both directly when modelled alone and through GenAI feedback lit­ eracy when both were entered together. Second, the inclusion of feed­ back literacy reduced the direct effect to non-significance and accounted for nearly three-quarters of the total effect, a pattern con­ sistent with feedback literacy as a principal mechanism. Third, the model was conditional on reflective thinking: at low reflective think­ ing the link was direct and the indirect path was not significant, whereas at high reflective thinking the indirect path through feedback literacy strengthened and the direct path disappeared. Read together, these results are consistent with a dual-route interpretation of the inconsistent existing evidence on GenAI and critical thinking, in which the structure of the association is sensitive to learner disposi­ tion. Each finding warrants discussion. Before turning to each finding, one interpretive boundary should be made explicit. Critical thinking was measured with the critical ana­ lytical skills sub-scale of the Chinese Critical Thinking Scale, on which students",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"to learner disposi­ tion. Each finding warrants discussion. Before turning to each finding, one interpretive boundary should be made explicit. Critical thinking was measured with the critical ana­ lytical skills sub-scale of the Chinese Critical Thinking Scale, on which students rate their own habitual analytical behaviour. The outcome therefore represents self-reported critical analytical skills, not critical- thinking performance demonstrated on a task, a test or an expert- rated piece of work. Self-reports of this kind capture students’ perceptions of how they approach problems rather than the observed quality of their reasoning, and they share a method with the self- reported predictors. Accordingly, where we write that GenAI use, feedback literacy or reflective thinking “predicts” critical thinking, the claim is that these variables are associated with higher self-rated ana­ lytical skill in a cross-sectional model, not that they produce better critical-thinking performance. This boundary applies to every finding discussed below. 5.1 The link between GenAI use and critical thinking The positive total association between GenAI use and critical thinking (β = 0.257) supports H1 and is consistent with a growing body",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"performance. This boundary applies to every finding discussed below. 5.1 The link between GenAI use and critical thinking The positive total association between GenAI use and critical thinking (β = 0.257) supports H1 and is consistent with a growing body of evidence that GenAI can scaffold higher-order cognition rather than simply substitute for it (Balart et al., 2026; Hwang et al., 2025; Vargas-Murillo et al., 2026; Wu et al., 2026). The result is con­ sistent with the extended-mind argument that external resources can become functional components of the cognitive system when they participate substantively in information processing (Clark and Chalmers, 1998), and with cognitive load theory’s expectation that absorbing routine sub-tasks frees working-memory resources for analysis and inference (Evans et al., 2024; Sweller, 2024). In the present sample, students who reported higher GenAI use across course learn­ ing, research, daily life and career-preparation tasks also reported stronger critical analytical capacity. At the same time, the direct effect became non-significant once GenAI feedback literacy entered the model. This pattern is congruent with concerns about cognitive offloading and “metacognitive laziness” (Fan et",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"life and career-preparation tasks also reported stronger critical analytical capacity. At the same time, the direct effect became non-significant once GenAI feedback literacy entered the model. This pattern is congruent with concerns about cognitive offloading and “metacognitive laziness” (Fan et al., 2025; Gerlich, 2025; Risko and Gilbert, 2016; Tian and Zhang, 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). Self-reported GenAI use was not, on its own, associated with higher critical analytical skills once feedback literacy was taken into account; what appears to matter is what learn­ ers do with the content the model produces. This dual reading—a positive total effect coexisting with a near-disappearing direct effect— provides a tractable framework for resolving the apparent contradic­ tions in recent reviews (Balart et al., 2026; Vargas-Murillo et al., 2026): bivariate or aggregate analyses detect a positive average association, but they obscure the process that may underlie it. Studies that report only zero-order correlations between AI use and critical thinking therefore risk under-explaining the relationship. 5.2 The mediating role of GenAI feedback literacy Support for H2, H3 and H4 places GenAI feedback literacy at the",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"may underlie it. Studies that report only zero-order correlations between AI use and critical thinking therefore risk under-explaining the relationship. 5.2 The mediating role of GenAI feedback literacy Support for H2, H3 and H4 places GenAI feedback literacy at the centre of the model. GenAI use predicted feedback literacy (β = 0.438), feedback literacy predicted critical thinking (β = 0.421), and the indi­ rect effect was substantial (β = 0.185; VAF = 71.98%). This finding extends Carless and Boud’s (2018) feedback-literacy framework to the GenAI context and complements the recent emphasis on AI-specific feedback engagement (Jin et al., 2025; Liu and Deris, 2025; Zhan and Yan, 2026; Zhan et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2026). Three implications are worth highlighting. First, the result points to a candidate cognitive mechanism con­ necting technology use to higher-order thinking, subject to the design caveats noted above. The technology itself does not determine cogni­ tive outcomes; effects are channelled through the cognitive processes that interaction elicits (Yan et al., 2024). What GenAI provides, by virtue of its multi-turn, prompt-and-respond architecture, is an unusually high-frequency",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"caveats noted above. The technology itself does not determine cogni­ tive outcomes; effects are channelled through the cognitive processes that interaction elicits (Yan et al., 2024). What GenAI provides, by virtue of its multi-turn, prompt-and-respond architecture, is an unusually high-frequency feedback environment. Whether learners use that environment to strengthen analysis depends on whether they can comprehend, judge and enact the feedback they receive. Feedback literacy, in this sense, may be the converter that turns interaction fre­ quency into cognitive depth. Second, the four dimensions of the GenAI-SFLS suggest that domain-specific feedback literacy may differ from generic feedback literacy in important ways. The behavioural dimension includes feed­ back-prompting skills—an explicitly co-constructive capacity that is unique to AI-mediated feedback—whilst the ethical dimension rec­ ognises that critical engagement with GenAI requires judgement about provenance, accuracy and appropriate use (Knoth et al., 2024; Tang et al., 2025; Walton et al., 2026). The strong loading of the behav­ ioural dimension (0.929) underscores the centrality of active prompt­ ing and enactment in GenAI feedback literacy. As GenAI agents become more multimodal and tool-augmented, the construct may",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,13,"et al., 2025; Walton et al., 2026). The strong loading of the behav­ ioural dimension (0.929) underscores the centrality of active prompt­ ing and enactment in GenAI feedback literacy. As GenAI agents become more multimodal and tool-augmented, the construct may need to expand further to cover, for example, evaluation of AI-generated visualisations and tool outputs.",55
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"Yan et al. 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286 Frontiers in Psychology 14 frontiersin.org Third, the near-disappearance of the direct effect once feedback literacy is added has implications for intervention, provided the medi­ ational ordering is confirmed in longitudinal or experimental work. If it is, encouraging students to use GenAI more without scaffolding their feedback engagement would be unlikely to produce robust cog­ nitive returns (Tepgec et al., 2025). The result supports calls to treat AI feedback literacy as an explicit pedagogical target rather than an emer­ gent by-product of access (Jin et al., 2025; Zhan et al., 2025). 5.3 The moderating role of reflective thinking and a dual-route interpretation The model exhibited a two-pathway structure conditional on reflective thinking. At low reflective thinking (M − 1 SD), GenAI use was directly associated with critical thinking (β = 0.177, p = 0.008) but the indirect effect was non-significant; at high reflective thinking (M + 1 SD), the direct association disappeared whilst the indirect effect through feedback literacy was strongest (β = 0.106, p = 0.001). The interaction between reflective thinking and GenAI use on critical",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"effect was non-significant; at high reflective thinking (M + 1 SD), the direct association disappeared whilst the indirect effect through feedback literacy was strongest (β = 0.106, p = 0.001). The interaction between reflective thinking and GenAI use on critical thinking was negative (β = −0.125), and the interaction between reflective thinking and feedback literacy was positive (β = 0.069). The index of moderated mediation was positive and, because the first-stage path is uniformly positive across bootstrap samples, its significance follows from the interaction coefficient, whose confidence interval excluded zero; H5a, H5b and H6 are therefore supported. These patterns are consistent with a dual-route interpretation, which we advance as a theoretically informed reading of the results rather than as a demonstrated cognitive mechanism. On this reading, less reflective learners show a small, direct association between GenAI use and critical analytical skills that does not pass through systematic feedback engagement; this would be consistent with the modest scaf­ folding that even relatively passive use can provide, such as organisa­ tion, examples and paraphrase (Tian and Zhang, 2025). More reflective learners, by",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"skills that does not pass through systematic feedback engagement; this would be consistent with the modest scaf­ folding that even relatively passive use can provide, such as organisa­ tion, examples and paraphrase (Tian and Zhang, 2025). More reflective learners, by contrast, may treat GenAI outputs as material to be inspected, compared and revised, prompting repeatedly, judging the quality of responses and reworking their own reasoning in light of them; on this account, feedback literacy is the pathway through which their GenAI use relates to critical analytical skills (Chan and Lee, 2021; Walton et al., 2026; Xiao and Liu, 2025). The negative interaction on the direct path does not indicate that GenAI “hurts” critical thinking amongst highly reflective students; statistically, it is consistent with a redistribution of the association into the feedback-literacy pathway, although the model does not test this redistribution directly. We stress that the present study did not observe processing depth directly: no measure of shallow versus deep engagement, and no behavioural trace of how students actually interacted with GenAI, was collected. The terms “shallow” and “deep” therefore describe",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"directly. We stress that the present study did not observe processing depth directly: no measure of shallow versus deep engagement, and no behavioural trace of how students actually interacted with GenAI, was collected. The terms “shallow” and “deep” therefore describe the theoretical mecha­ nism with which the moderation pattern is consistent, not a mecha­ nism the data demonstrate. Read in this cautious way, the dual-route interpretation still helps to reconcile the apparently contradictory empirical literature. The cognitive-offloading view may describe less reflective learners, whose GenAI use is, on this interpretation, less tied to systematic feedback engagement (Gerlich, 2025; Tian and Zhang, 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). The cognitive-scaffolding view may describe more reflective learners, whose GenAI use is more strongly associated with an iterative loop of comprehension, evaluation and revision (Hwang et al., 2025; Nasr et al., 2025; Raitskaya and Tikhonova, 2025). The pattern is also compat­ ible with Fan et al.’s (2025) experimental finding that AI-supported learners performed comparably to expert-supported learners on essay outcomes but exhibited fewer self-regulatory and metacognitive moves: similar surface-level performance may be reached via",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"Tikhonova, 2025). The pattern is also compat­ ible with Fan et al.’s (2025) experimental finding that AI-supported learners performed comparably to expert-supported learners on essay outcomes but exhibited fewer self-regulatory and metacognitive moves: similar surface-level performance may be reached via routes of different cognitive depth. Whether only one of those routes builds critical thinking durably is a question for longitudinal and process- tracing designs, not one the present data can answer. The same tech­ nology, on this reading, can be a substitute or a partner depending on the metacognitive stance the learner brings to it. 5.4 Theoretical implications The study contributes to the literature in three theoretically sub­ stantive ways. First, by integrating extended-mind, cognitive-load and feedback-literacy frameworks, it offers a process-level account of how GenAI use may relate to critical thinking, moving beyond the bivariate “GenAI helps/hurts” debate to a moderated mediation specification consistent with calls for mechanism-focused research in educational technology (Yan et al., 2024). Second, by identifying GenAI feedback literacy as a statistically strong mediator in a cross-sectional model, with VAF approaching the conventional threshold for full",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"a moderated mediation specification consistent with calls for mechanism-focused research in educational technology (Yan et al., 2024). Second, by identifying GenAI feedback literacy as a statistically strong mediator in a cross-sectional model, with VAF approaching the conventional threshold for full mediation, it positions feedback literacy as a plausible principal cognitive inter­ face between AI exposure and higher-order thinking, and extends Carless and Boud’s (2018) framework to AI-mediated environments (Jin et al., 2025; Nieminen and Carless, 2023; Zhan and Yan, 2026). Third, by modelling reflective thinking as a moderator of both the direct and indirect paths, the study identifies a metacognitive bound­ ary condition that may help explain why studies that pool learners produce inconsistent estimates. The dual-route interpretation—a pre­ dominantly direct path at low reflectivity and an indirect, feedback- literacy-driven path at high reflectivity—offers a parsimonious and testable frame for future research and a way of reading findings that have so far appeared contradictory. 5.5 Practical and pedagogical implications The findings suggest implications for higher-education policy and practise, which we offer as hypotheses for intervention research rather than as established",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"future research and a way of reading findings that have so far appeared contradictory. 5.5 Practical and pedagogical implications The findings suggest implications for higher-education policy and practise, which we offer as hypotheses for intervention research rather than as established prescriptions, given the cross-sectional, self-report design. We highlight four. First, GenAI integration policies that focus on usage volume or access alone are unlikely, on the present evidence, to be sufficient for gains in critical thinking. Universities should pair access with explicit feedback-literacy curricula that teach students to comprehend, judge and act on AI-generated feedback, including through prompt-engineering instruction, source-checking exercises and structured comparison of multiple AI outputs (Knoth et al., 2024; Liu and Deris, 2025). Second, instructional design should privilege tasks that require iterative engagement with GenAI rather than one- shot retrieval—for example, multi-stage assignments in which stu­ dents draft, prompt for feedback, evaluate the response, revise their own work and document the reasoning behind their revisions. Such designs map directly onto the comprehension–evaluation–enaction structure of feedback literacy found to mediate the association with critical thinking in this study, and",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,14,"prompt for feedback, evaluate the response, revise their own work and document the reasoning behind their revisions. Such designs map directly onto the comprehension–evaluation–enaction structure of feedback literacy found to mediate the association with critical thinking in this study, and discourage the metacognitive short­ cuts that can occur when AI is used as a one-shot answer machine (Fan et al., 2025). Third, because the indirect path strengthens with reflective think­ ing, institutions should embed reflective practises—learning journals, structured debriefs, metacognitive prompts and explicit “think- before-AI” protocols—into AI-augmented coursework, especially in",90
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"Yan et al. 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286 Frontiers in Psychology 15 frontiersin.org the early years when students are still establishing habits of AI use (Merkebu et al., 2024; Xiao and Liu, 2025). Fourth, the ethical dimen­ sion of GenAI feedback literacy deserves explicit curricular attention; teaching students to interrogate GenAI outputs for accuracy, bias, hal­ lucination and appropriate attribution converts ethical engagement into a cognitive resource rather than a compliance issue (Walton et al., 2026). Taken together, these implications imply a reorientation of AI-in-education policy from access and adoption metrics towards measurable feedback-literacy and reflection outcomes, consistent with recent calls in the field (Yan et al., 2024; Zhan et al., 2025). 5.6 Limitations and directions for future research Several limitations bound the study’s claims. First, the cross-sec­ tional design precludes causal inference. All variables were measured concurrently, so the mediation and moderated-mediation estimates describe patterns of association that are consistent with the hypothe­ sised ordering but cannot establish it; reverse or reciprocal orderings (for example, students with stronger analytical skills making heavier or more sophisticated use of GenAI, or feedback literacy shaping use",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"describe patterns of association that are consistent with the hypothe­ sised ordering but cannot establish it; reverse or reciprocal orderings (for example, students with stronger analytical skills making heavier or more sophisticated use of GenAI, or feedback literacy shaping use rather than the reverse) are equally compatible with the data. Although PLS-SEM with bootstrapped indirect effects and theoretically moti­ vated path direction is well suited to estimating complex models, the findings should be replicated using longitudinal panel data or ran­ domised intervention designs that track how GenAI use, feedback literacy and reflective thinking co-evolve over time. Second, the sample is drawn from Chinese universities and is skewed female (69.6%); cross-cultural and gender-balanced replications are needed to assess generalisability across educational systems with different assessment cultures and AI-policy environments. Third, all measures are self-reported, and the outcome in particular represents self- reported critical analytical skills rather than demonstrated critical- thinking performance. Although the study’s common-method-bias diagnostics did not flag a serious problem, behavioural and perfor­ mance-based measures of critical thinking and feedback engagement (e.g., analyses of think-aloud protocols, log data from",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"reported critical analytical skills rather than demonstrated critical- thinking performance. Although the study’s common-method-bias diagnostics did not flag a serious problem, behavioural and perfor­ mance-based measures of critical thinking and feedback engagement (e.g., analyses of think-aloud protocols, log data from AI interactions, expert ratings of student work) would strengthen inference (Fan et al., 2025). Fourth, critical thinking was operationalised through the ana­ lytical-skills sub-scale of a Chinese instrument; future studies could examine the model with broader operationalisations covering infer­ ence, evaluation, explanation and self-regulation (Facione, 2011) and across disciplines, since GenAI’s affordances and risks may differ between, say, the humanities and STEM. Relatedly, the dual-route interpretation in this study rests on the pattern of moderation, not on direct observation of cognitive processing; the study did not measure whether students engaged with GenAI outputs shallowly or deeply, and process-tracing methods such as interaction logs, think-aloud protocols or eye-tracking are needed to test the proposed routes directly. Fifth, the incremental fit indices of the item-level measure­ ment model fell below conventional thresholds, as is common with long instruments such as the 39-item",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"as interaction logs, think-aloud protocols or eye-tracking are needed to test the proposed routes directly. Fifth, the incremental fit indices of the item-level measure­ ment model fell below conventional thresholds, as is common with long instruments such as the 39-item GenAI-SFLS (Kenny and McCoach, 2003; Shi et al., 2019); future work could refine the GenAI- SFLS for shorter administration without losing dimensional coverage. Finally, GenAI tools are evolving rapidly, and the empirical model captures a snapshot of usage patterns at a given moment; tracking the moderated mediation structure across model generations and modali­ ties (text, voice, multimodal agents) is an important agenda for the field. 6 Conclusion Generative AI is rapidly becoming part of the cognitive infrastruc­ ture of higher education, and a settled empirical answer to whether its use is associated with stronger or weaker critical thinking has remained elusive. The present study reframes the question. In a cross-sectional sample of Chinese undergraduates, GenAI use was positively associated with self-reported critical analytical skills, but the association was largely indirect: it was carried by students’ capacity to interpret, judge and",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"elusive. The present study reframes the question. In a cross-sectional sample of Chinese undergraduates, GenAI use was positively associated with self-reported critical analytical skills, but the association was largely indirect: it was carried by students’ capacity to interpret, judge and act on AI-generated feedback, and its structure depended on students’ reflective stance. At low reflective thinking the association was direct and modest; at high reflective thinking it ran through feedback literacy, yield­ ing a stronger indirect association. If these associations reflect the hypothesised causal ordering, a question that longitudinal and experi­ mental designs must settle, the cognitive dividend of GenAI is unlikely to come from access alone, and universities that pair AI integration with explicit feedback-literacy training and reflective pedagogical practises may be better placed to convert ubiquitous AI use into the analytical capacity that higher education is meant to cultivate. The moderated mediation model presented here offers a tractable framework for the next wave of empirical work on GenAI and higher-order cognition. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by",180
A085,A085.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1939286,15,"cultivate. The moderated mediation model presented here offers a tractable framework for the next wave of empirical work on GenAI and higher-order cognition. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the Research Ethics Committee of Minzu Normal University of Xingyi. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institu­ tional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions ZY: Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition, Resources, Formal analysis, Software, Writing – original draft, Methodology, Visualization, Validation, Conceptualization, Investigation, Data cura­ tion. ZF: Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Methodology, Formal analysis, Investigation. WZ: Conceptualization, Funding acquisition, Resources, Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Methodology, Supervision, Validation. Funding The author(s) declared that financial support was received for this work and/or its publication. This work was supported by the General Project of the 2024 Annual Philosophy and",180
