A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,1,"EDITED BY Murat Dogan Sahin, Anadolu University, Türkiye REVIEWED BY Qijun Song, Guangdong University of Education, China Yenni Sri Utami, Universitas Pembangunan Nasional Veteran Yogyakarta, Indonesia *CORRESPONDENCE Tianyue Niu t24095@fzu.edu.cn RECEIVED 02 July 2026 REVISED 01 August 2026 ACCEPTED 13 August 2026 PUBLISHED 28 August 2026 CITATION He Y, Niu T and Ashari ZM (2026) Exploring the impact of multimodal generative artificial intelligence on CT dispositions in inquiry-based academic writing: a quasi-experimental analysis in higher education. Front. Educ. 11:1926173. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 COPYRIGHT © 2026 He, Niu and Ashari. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Exploring the impact of multimodal generative artificial intelligence on CT dispositions in inquiry-based academic writing: a quasi-experimental analysis in higher education Yijie He 1, Tianyue",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,1,"practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Exploring the impact of multimodal generative artificial intelligence on CT dispositions in inquiry-based academic writing: a quasi-experimental analysis in higher education Yijie He 1, Tianyue Niu 2* and Zakiah Mohamad Ashari 1 1Faculty of Educational Sciences and Technology, Universiti Teknologi Malaysia, Johor, Malaysia, 2Xiamen Academy of Arts and Design, Fuzhou University, Xiamen, China Introduction: Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) offers new possibilities for scaffolding inquiry-based writing (IBW). However, its impact on critical thinking (CT) dispositions remains empirically underexplored, particularly regarding how the modality of GenAI interaction shapes this impact. Grounded in the Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning, this study examines whether multimodal GenAI, which simultaneously processes and generates text, images, and visual representations, cultivates CT dispositions and improves academic writing quality more effectively than text-based GenAI or no GenAI support. Methods: A quasi-experimental pretest-posttest design with three conditions (multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI control) was implemented with 165 undergraduate students over an eight-week IBW intervention. One-way ANCOVAs were conducted to compare condition effects on",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,1,"or no GenAI support. Methods: A quasi-experimental pretest-posttest design with three conditions (multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI control) was implemented with 165 undergraduate students over an eight-week IBW intervention. One-way ANCOVAs were conducted to compare condition effects on posttest outcomes, controlling for pretest scores. Results: Analyses revealed significant main effects of condition on both CT dispositions and academic writing quality. The multimodal GenAI group showed the greatest improvement on both outcomes, followed by the text- based GenAI group, with the no GenAI control group showing the smallest gains. Subdimensional analyses indicated that the multimodal advantage was concentrated in openness and analytical thinking within CT dispositions, and in evidence use and analytical depth within writing quality. Discussion: These findings extend the Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning into GenAI-assisted learning contexts and demonstrate that the modality of GenAI interaction is a consequential design variable for cultivating CT dispositions through IBW. Practical implications for educators and instructional designers are discussed. KEYWORDS academic writing quality, cognitive theory of multimedia learning, CT dispositions, genAI, higher education, inquiry-based writing, multimodal learning, quasi- experimental design",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,1,"a consequential design variable for cultivating CT dispositions through IBW. Practical implications for educators and instructional designers are discussed. KEYWORDS academic writing quality, cognitive theory of multimedia learning, CT dispositions, genAI, higher education, inquiry-based writing, multimodal learning, quasi- experimental design TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 28 August 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",54
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"1 Introduction Inquiry-based academic writing in higher education is not only a tool for presenting and organizing knowledge, but also a generative cognitive process through which students question hypotheses, evaluate evidence, construct arguments, and develop their academic identity as scholars (Chanpradit, 2025; Luo et al., 2025b). Writing plays a central role in both the learning and assessment methods of higher education students, a function that is even more pronounced in inquiry-based learning environments. Some inquiry-based writing (IBW) instruction helps students participate in pre-writing preparation, such as generating ideas, discovering and defining writing topics, exploring information from different sources, and articulating their thinking by translating understanding into real-world contexts, thus positioning academic writing as a means and outcome of in-depth inquiry (Wale and Bogale, 2021). However, IBW demands a high level of cognition and motivation, and without well-designed scaffolded instruction, the argumentation in academic writing remains superficial (Wale and Bogale, 2021). pointed out that in academic writing projects, many students merely summarize data rather than critically examine it; they simply present arguments rather than evaluate their supporting evidence. A major factor",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"argumentation in academic writing remains superficial (Wale and Bogale, 2021). pointed out that in academic writing projects, many students merely summarize data rather than critically examine it; they simply present arguments rather than evaluate their supporting evidence. A major factor is that students lack sufficient CT skills to support the content of their academic writing. Central to the promise of IBW is its potential to cultivate CT dispositions. CT, as a core higher-order cognitive ability for students, emphasizes the thinking skills individuals possess when comprehensively analyzing and evaluating information to form judgments and decisions (Loyens et al., 2023). An important category of CT is CT disposition, which refers to sustained intrinsic motivation that drives individuals to engage in and value CT (Facione, 2000). Unlike CT skills, which concern whether students can perform specific reasoning operations, CT dispositions concern whether students habitually choose to engage in careful, reflective, evidence-based inquiry, making them particularly consequential for the quality of IBW and for students’ development as lifelong intellectual agents (Dunlap and Grabinger, 2003; Luo et al., 2026). The emergence of generative artificial intelligence",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"habitually choose to engage in careful, reflective, evidence-based inquiry, making them particularly consequential for the quality of IBW and for students’ development as lifelong intellectual agents (Dunlap and Grabinger, 2003; Luo et al., 2026). The emergence of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) offers new possibilities for constructing inquiry-based academic writing and may have a profound impact on students’ CT skills (Niu et al., 2025). Numerous empirical studies demonstrate that GenAI can enhance learning and cultivate CT skills: guiding students to seek suggestions for writing errors or to propose opposing viewpoints from GenAI creates conditions that encourage reflective, multi-perspective thinking: a hallmark of excellent CT (Hikmawati and Mohammad, 2025). However, a key concern is that blind reliance on generative AI may weaken rather than enhance students’ CT abilities. The rapid and authoritative provision of answers by GenAI can mislead people into believing its output is objective and coherent, thus overlooking inherent biases in AI-generated content and fostering a passive information acquisition approach. This contradicts the truth-seeking and analytical skills that IBW aims to cultivate. Therefore, whether and how GenAI can influence CT",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"its output is objective and coherent, thus overlooking inherent biases in AI-generated content and fostering a passive information acquisition approach. This contradicts the truth-seeking and analytical skills that IBW aims to cultivate. Therefore, whether and how GenAI can influence CT dispositions in IBW contexts largely depends on its presentation design (Bozkurt et al., 2024; Larson et al., 2024). Regarding presentation methods, a particularly important but under-explored dimension is the role of modality. Most current research on GenAI in academic writing focuses on text-based conversational AI, which operates entirely within the linguistic channel of cognitive processing. Multimodal GenAI, capable of simultaneously processing and generating text, images, audio, and video, is theoretically a radically different intervention, and its impact on CT dispositions may differ significantly from text- only GenAI (Bozkurt et al., 2024). Cognitive theories of multimedia learning positize that humans possess independent information processing channels for linguistic and visual information, and meaningful learning involves selecting, organizing, and integrating information across both channels (Smith et al., 2025). This suggests that multimodal GenAI can engage students more deeply and richly in the inquiry",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"independent information processing channels for linguistic and visual information, and meaningful learning involves selecting, organizing, and integrating information across both channels (Smith et al., 2025). This suggests that multimodal GenAI can engage students more deeply and richly in the inquiry process by simultaneously activating both channels. Beyond cognitive effects, multimodal interaction can also have a motivating effect: richer, more diverse, and visually appealing AI interactions, by simultaneously presenting multiple perspectives and modes of evidence, can foster a stronger thirst for knowledge and an open mind; while more personalized and visually responsive feedback can enhance students’ confidence in CT (Mayer and Moreno, 2005). Students’ ability to use GenAI plays an important role in cultivating CT. Higher GenAI proficiency encourages learners to question, analyze and improve AI-generated content, suggesting that more interactive and responsive AI models may have a more significant biased effect (Sutrisno et al., 2024). While multimodal GenAI holds theoretical potential in cultivating CT skills in IBW, sufficient empirical research is currently lacking to directly compare the impact of multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI on students’ CT skills.",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"effect (Sutrisno et al., 2024). While multimodal GenAI holds theoretical potential in cultivating CT skills in IBW, sufficient empirical research is currently lacking to directly compare the impact of multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI on students’ CT skills. To fill this research gap, this study employs a quasi- experimental pretest-posttest design with three conditions to examine how multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI differentially influence higher education students’ CT dispositions and academic writing quality in IBW courses. This study addresses two research questions: RQ1: How do these three instructional conditions differ in their impact on students’ CT dispositions? RQ2: How do these three instructional conditions differ in their impact on academic writing quality? 2 Literature review 2.1 Inquiry-based academic writing and CT dispositions Inquiry-based learning (IBL) positions students as active inquirers, encouraging them to ask questions, gather evidence, and construct arguments, rather than passively receiving information (Luo et al., 2026; Pedaste et al., 2015). When applied to academic writing, IBL transforms the writing process from knowledge transmission to knowledge transformation, requiring students to identify problems, evaluate conflicting",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,2,"evidence, and construct arguments, rather than passively receiving information (Luo et al., 2026; Pedaste et al., 2015). When applied to academic writing, IBL transforms the writing process from knowledge transmission to knowledge transformation, requiring students to identify problems, evaluate conflicting viewpoints, and synthesize evidence into coherent arguments He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",56
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"(Lin et al., 2023; Luo et al., 2025). This process places certain demands on students’ higher-order cognitive abilities, particularly CT dispositions. CT encompasses both skills and dispositions. CT skills refer to the cognitive operations an individual performs when analyzing, evaluating, and constructing arguments, such as reasoning, explanation, and description (Facione, 2000). In contrast, CT disposition refers to the sustained intrinsic motivation that drives an individual to engage in and value CT, including habits such as the pursuit of truth, open-mindedness, analytical skills, and a thirst for knowledge (Facione et al., 1995). Skills focus on whether a student possesses CT abilities, while propensity focuses on whether a student habitually chooses to engage in CT. This distinction is crucial for IBW, because even students with sufficient reasoning abilities may produce superficial arguments if they lack the inclination to question assumptions, seek counter-evidence, or assess the strength of their own arguments (Mercier and Sperber, 2011). Research indicates that well-designed IBW instruction can cultivate CT skills (Wale and Bogale, 2021). found that IBW tasks encourage students to move beyond superficial generalization and engage more",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"or assess the strength of their own arguments (Mercier and Sperber, 2011). Research indicates that well-designed IBW instruction can cultivate CT skills (Wale and Bogale, 2021). found that IBW tasks encourage students to move beyond superficial generalization and engage more deeply in the analysis of evidence. However, research also suggests that without appropriate scaffolding, many students fail to make this transition, resulting in arguments lacking analytical depth (Sinha et al., 2021). This indicates that cultivating CT through IBW is not a natural process and requires further exploration of the quality and nature of instructional support. 2.2 Application of AI in educational writing The integration of AI tools into the field of educational writing has attracted widespread research attention. A growing body of empirical research shows that AI can support the writing process in several ways. First, AI can assist in brainstorming and pre- writing preparation, helping students brainstorm, explore different perspectives, and organize their thoughts before drafting (Lim et al., 2023). Second, AI can provide real-time feedback on drafts, identifying structural flaws, logical inconsistencies, and areas where arguments need further",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"brainstorming and pre- writing preparation, helping students brainstorm, explore different perspectives, and organize their thoughts before drafting (Lim et al., 2023). Second, AI can provide real-time feedback on drafts, identifying structural flaws, logical inconsistencies, and areas where arguments need further elaboration (Hikmawati and Mohammad, 2025). Third, AI can act as a dialogue partner, enabling students to test their own arguments with AI-generated counterarguments, thereby stimulating deeper thinking and perspective-taking (Bozkurt et al., 2024). However, the relationship between GenAI use and CT is not straightforward. A central concern is that GenAI may undermine the very cognitive processes that IBW aims to develop. When GenAI provides fluent, well-structured responses to student queries, students may accept these outputs uncritically, bypassing the effortful processes of evidence evaluation and argument construction that build CT dispositions (Larson et al., 2024). This risk is amplified by the authority effect: GenAI outputs are often perceived as objective and authoritative, which can discourage the sceptical questioning that characterises strong CT (Kasneci et al., 2023). Empirical findings are mixed. Some studies report that GenAI- assisted writing improves the quality of",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"authority effect: GenAI outputs are often perceived as objective and authoritative, which can discourage the sceptical questioning that characterises strong CT (Kasneci et al., 2023). Empirical findings are mixed. Some studies report that GenAI- assisted writing improves the quality of student argumentation (Hikmawati and Mohammad, 2025), while others find that students who rely heavily on GenAI produce arguments that are well-formatted but analytically shallow (Luo et al., 2024). These divergent findings suggest that the impact of GenAI on CT dispositions may depend on how the tool is designed and integrated into the learning process. 2.3 Multimodal GenAI and cognitive engagement The role of modality in GenAI’s impact on learning is a dimension that has not yet been fully explored. Currently, most research on GenAI in academic writing focuses on text-based conversational AI, which operates entirely within the language channel. However, the rapid development of multimodal GenAI systems capable of simultaneously processing and generating text, images, audio, and video introduces a radically different mode of interaction, potentially stimulating learners’ cognitive processes in unique ways (Tan et al., 2025). The Cognitive Theory",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"the rapid development of multimodal GenAI systems capable of simultaneously processing and generating text, images, audio, and video introduces a radically different mode of interaction, potentially stimulating learners’ cognitive processes in unique ways (Tan et al., 2025). The Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning (CTML) provides a theoretical basis for understanding how multimodal presentation affects learning (Mayer, 2024). CTML posits that humans possess two separate channels for processing verbal and visual information, that each channel has limited capacity, and that meaningful learning occurs when learners actively select, organise, and integrate information across both channels. When applied to GenAI-assisted writing, CTML suggests that multimodal GenAI, which can present evidence through images, diagrams, and visual representations alongside text, may engage students in deeper cognitive processing than text-only GenAI. By activating both the verbal and visual channels, multimodal GenAI may help students build richer mental models of the problems they are investigating, which in turn may support more nuanced evaluation and argumentation. Beyond cognitive effects, multimodal interaction may also influence motivation and engagement (Mayer and Moreno, 2005). found that multimedia presentations can reduce cognitive",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"mental models of the problems they are investigating, which in turn may support more nuanced evaluation and argumentation. Beyond cognitive effects, multimodal interaction may also influence motivation and engagement (Mayer and Moreno, 2005). found that multimedia presentations can reduce cognitive overload while increasing learner interest, provided that the visual and verbal elements are well-integrated and relevant to the learning task. In the context of IBW, multimodal GenAI that presents diverse forms of evidence, such as statistical visualisations, illustrative images, and textual explanations, may foster greater intellectual curiosity and open-mindedness, two core components of CT dispositions (Facione, 2000). However, the richer the information environment, the greater the risk of cognitive overload or distraction if the multimodal elements are not well-aligned with the inquiry task (Sweller et al., 2019). Whether multimodal GenAI enhances or hinders CT dispositions is therefore an empirical question that depends on the specific design of the intervention. 2.4 Research gap and theoretical framing The preceding review highlighted three shortcomings that this study aims to address. First, while existing research has explored the impact of GenAI on writing quality",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,3,"that depends on the specific design of the intervention. 2.4 Research gap and theoretical framing The preceding review highlighted three shortcomings that this study aims to address. First, while existing research has explored the impact of GenAI on writing quality and CT skills, empirical studies on its influence on CT tendencies are scarce. Second, most current research focuses on text-based GenAI, while the unique He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",73
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,4,"cognitive and motivational effects of multimodal GenAI are rarely examined. Third, no research has directly compared the effects of multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI on CT tendencies in the same exploratory writing context, thus failing to distinguish the influence of modal factors from the influence of GenAI itself. To address these shortcomings, this study employs a three-group quasi-experimental design, supplemented by qualitative thematic analysis. The quantitative part compares the changes in CT tendencies and academic writing quality under the three conditions (multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI). 3 Methodology 3.1 Research design This study adopted a quasi-experimental pretest-posttest design with three non-equivalent groups. Three intact class sections were assigned to three experimental conditions: (1) multimodal GenAI, (2) text-based GenAI, and (3) no GenAI control. All three groups received the same IBW instruction and completed the same writing tasks; the only systematic difference was the availability and modality of the GenAI tool. Pretest and posttest measures of CT dispositions, cognitive engagement, and academic writing quality were collected to assess within-group change and between-group differences. 3.2 Participants and context",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,4,"tasks; the only systematic difference was the availability and modality of the GenAI tool. Pretest and posttest measures of CT dispositions, cognitive engagement, and academic writing quality were collected to assess within-group change and between-group differences. 3.2 Participants and context We recruited 165 first-year undergraduate students from a university in southern China. All first-year students are required to take the compulsory English 1 Course, which includes an IBW component, requiring students to conduct independent research and write argumentative essays. Three full classes were assigned to three experimental groups, each taught by the same instructor using the same course. Eligibility criteria required participants to have no prior formal training in GenAI-assisted writing and to be enrolled in the course for the first time. Prior to data collection, an a priori power analysis was conducted using G*Power 3.1 to determine the minimum sample size required to detect a moderate effect size (Cohen’s f = 0.25) with sufficient statistical power (1 −β = 0.80) at a significance level of α = 0.05, based on a one-way ANOVA for three-group comparisons. This calculation indicated",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,4,"sample size required to detect a moderate effect size (Cohen’s f = 0.25) with sufficient statistical power (1 −β = 0.80) at a significance level of α = 0.05, based on a one-way ANOVA for three-group comparisons. This calculation indicated a minimum of 53 participants per group (159 in total). Although the primary analyses employed ANCOVA rather than ANOVA, ANCOVA typically provides equal or greater statistical power when the covariate is correlated with the dependent variable, making the ANOVA-based estimate conservative. The actual sample of 165 participants (55 per group) exceeded this minimum requirement. 3.3 Experimental conditions These three experimental conditions share a single GenAI Agent platform from Qwen, as shown in Figure 1, aiming to isolate the influence of the GenAI model on CT. Students in each experimental group interacted using a large language model corresponding to one of three conditions. Condition 1: Multimodal GenAI Group. Students in this group used a multimodal GenAI tool (Qwen3-VL) capable of processing and generating text, images, charts, and visualizations. During IBW, students interacted with the tool through textual prompts and received feedback",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,4,"three conditions. Condition 1: Multimodal GenAI Group. Students in this group used a multimodal GenAI tool (Qwen3-VL) capable of processing and generating text, images, charts, and visualizations. During IBW, students interacted with the tool through textual prompts and received feedback integrating textual explanations, visual evidence, concept maps, and illustrations. The tool was configured to support inquiry-based interactions, such as presenting multiple perspectives on a topic, visualizing argument structures, and generating visual comparisons of competing viewpoints. Condition 2: Text-Based GenAI Group. Students in this group used a text-only GenAI tool (Qwen3) with equivalent conversational capabilities but without visualization generation or processing capabilities. The tool provided the same types of intellectual support, including brainstorming, feedback, and rebuttals, but entirely through verbal channels. This condition, as an active control, aims to distinguish the impact of multimodal presentation from the impact of GenAI interaction itself. Condition 3: No GenAI Control Group. Students in this condition completed the same IBW task and did not have access to any GenAI tools. They had access to regular resources, including library databases, search engines, and teacher feedback. This",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,4,"itself. Condition 3: No GenAI Control Group. Students in this condition completed the same IBW task and did not have access to any GenAI tools. They had access to regular resources, including library databases, search engines, and teacher feedback. This condition served as the baseline. In all three conditions, students received the same IBW instruction, the same task prompts, and the same teaching duration. The following covariates were collected at baseline: (1) pretest scores on CT dispositions and writing quality (serving as covariates in the primary analyses), (2) prior GenAI experience (frequency and proficiency of use), and (3) demographic variables (age, gender, major). FIGURE 1 Qwen platform example. He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",117
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"3.4 Instruments 3.4.1 CT dispositions This study used the Chinese version of the CT Disposition Inventory (CTDI-CV) to assess students’ CT levels before and after the intervention (Yang and Niu, 2026). The original inventory included seven dimensions: truth-seeking, openness, analytical thinking, systematic thinking, confidence in CT, curiosity, and cognitive maturity. A 6-point Likert scale was used for scoring (1 = strongly disagree, 6 = strongly agree), with higher scores indicating a stronger tendency towards CT. To improve the applicability of the instrument to the present research context, the research team adopted a shortened and domain-adapted version of the CTDI-CV following the instrument adaptation procedure established by (Yang and Niu, 2026) and subsequently replicated by (Liu et al., 2026). In both prior studies, which investigated GenAI-supported interventions in Chinese undergraduate contexts, the original seven-dimension structure was refined through expert review and focus group discussion, resulting in the retention of five dimensions most closely aligned with the cognitive demands of GenAI-assisted inquiry tasks, including openness, analytical thinking, systematic thinking, confidence in CT, and cognitive maturity, were retained. Truth-seeking and curiosity were excluded",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"focus group discussion, resulting in the retention of five dimensions most closely aligned with the cognitive demands of GenAI-assisted inquiry tasks, including openness, analytical thinking, systematic thinking, confidence in CT, and cognitive maturity, were retained. Truth-seeking and curiosity were excluded based on both theoretical and empirical considerations. From a theoretical perspective, the truth-seeking dimension, defined as a habitual desire for objectivity and evidence-driven decision-making (Facione, 2000), is relevant in open-ended contexts where learners must independently determine which knowledge claims to pursue. However, in structured IBW tasks, the discriminant validity of this construct is significantly reduced because the sources of evidence and the argumentative framework are already provided as part of the instructional design (Ip et al., 2000; Tiwari et al., 2003) Similarly, the curiosity dimension, whcih is defined as the desire for knowledge regarding learning opportunities, conceptually overlaps with intrinsic motivation and task engagement (Litman, 2008). In intervention studies involving GenAI tools, curiosity are considered susceptible to confounding effects arising from the novelty and responsiveness inherent in the AI interaction itself (Haber, 2023). From an empirical perspective, this dimensional reduction",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"motivation and task engagement (Litman, 2008). In intervention studies involving GenAI tools, curiosity are considered susceptible to confounding effects arising from the novelty and responsiveness inherent in the AI interaction itself (Haber, 2023). From an empirical perspective, this dimensional reduction is consistent with the adaptation procedure established by Yang and Niu, 2026 and replicated by Liu et al., 2026, both of which investigated GenAI-supported interventions in Chinese undergraduate contexts and arrived at the same five-dimension structure through expert review and focus group discussion. Recognizing that reducing the number of items might affect the instrument’s psychometric properties, a three-stage pilot validation procedure was conducted with a separate sample of 60 undergraduate student prior to the main data collection. Participants for the pilot study were recruited from the same university as the main study subjects, specifically through school research laboratories. After undergoing a one-hour course experience under laboratory conditions that mirrored the setup of the main study, the pilot participants completed a revised version of the CTDI-CV. In the first stage, content validity was evaluated through expert review. Five experts in educational",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"a one-hour course experience under laboratory conditions that mirrored the setup of the main study, the pilot participants completed a revised version of the CTDI-CV. In the first stage, content validity was evaluated through expert review. Five experts in educational psychology and CT research independently rated the relevance and representativeness of each item on a 4-point scale. Items rated 3 or 4 were considered content-valid. The revised CTDI-CV achieved an overall Content Validity Index (CVI) of 0.87, indicating good content validity. In the second stage, an exploratory factor analysis (EFA) was conducted on the pilot data to examine whether the hypothesised five-factor structure was empirically supported. Principal axis factoring was used, as the five dimensions of CT dispositions were expected to be correlated. The Kaiser-Meyer- Olkin (KMO) measure of sampling adequacy was 0.72, and Bartlett’s test of sphericity was statistically significant (χ2 = 212.48, p < .001), indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. Five factors with eigenvalues greater than 1.0 were extracted, collectively accounting for 68.4% of the total variance. All 15 items loaded on their intended",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"significant (χ2 = 212.48, p < .001), indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. Five factors with eigenvalues greater than 1.0 were extracted, collectively accounting for 68.4% of the total variance. All 15 items loaded on their intended factors with primary loadings ranging from 0.56 to 0.79, and inter-factor correlations ranging from 0.28 to 0.53, indicating that the dimensions were related but distinguishable, providing preliminary evidence of construct validity for the adapted instrument. In the third stage, reliability was assessed. Reliability analysis revealed an overall Cronbach’s α coefficient was 0.81, and the Cronbach’s α coefficients for each dimension ranged from 0.70 to 0.79. Although subdimension alphas were slightly lower than those of the full-length original due to the reduced number of items per subscale (three items each), they remained within the acceptable range for short-scale instruments in exploratory educational research (Adamson and Prion, 2013) and were comparable to the values reported by Yang and Niu, 2026; α = 0.61–0.74) and Liu et al., 2026; α = 0.64–0.78). The present study’s subdimension alphas (0.70–0.79) were in fact somewhat higher",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"educational research (Adamson and Prion, 2013) and were comparable to the values reported by Yang and Niu, 2026; α = 0.61–0.74) and Liu et al., 2026; α = 0.64–0.78). The present study’s subdimension alphas (0.70–0.79) were in fact somewhat higher than those in both precedent studies, likely reflecting the closer alignment of the selected items with the IBW context. 3.4.2 Academic writing quality Academic writing quality. Academic writing quality was assessed using an analytic scoring rubric adapted from Stapleton’ 2001 framework for evaluating CT in argumentative writing (Stapleton, 2001). The original framework assesses writing across five dimensions: argument strength, evidence support, recognition of opposing viewpoints, refutation quality, and avoidance of logical fallacies. To improve the applicability of the rubric in this research context, the research team revised the original framework through a process combining expert review and pilot testing. Given the inquiry-based nature of the writing tasks in this study, the research team reorganised the assessment dimensions into four categories most relevant to the learning objectives: (1) argument structure, assessing whether the essay presents a clear thesis, logically organised sub-arguments,",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,5,"the inquiry-based nature of the writing tasks in this study, the research team reorganised the assessment dimensions into four categories most relevant to the learning objectives: (1) argument structure, assessing whether the essay presents a clear thesis, logically organised sub-arguments, and a coherent overall progression; (2) evidence use, assessing whether claims are supported by relevant, sufficient, and appropriately cited evidence drawn from multiple sources; (3) analytical depth, assessing whether the essay moves beyond surface-level description to offer critical evaluation, comparison of competing He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",91
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"perspectives, and nuanced interpretation; and (4) coherence and integration, assessing whether ideas are connected through explicit logical transitions and whether the essay achieves a unified line of reasoning from introduction to conclusion. Each dimension was scored on a 5-point scale (1 = very poor, 5 = excellent), with detailed descriptors developed for each score level to ensure consistency across raters. The maximum possible score was 20 points. All writing samples were scored independently by two trained raters who were blind to the experimental condition. Prior to the main scoring, a calibration session was conducted in which both raters jointly scored a subset of 10 essays and discussed any discrepancies to establish a shared understanding of the scoring criteria. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC). The overall ICC was 0.85, and the ICCs for each dimension ranged from 0.78 to 0.89, indicating good to excellent inter-rater agreement (Koo and Li, 2016). In cases where the two raters’ scores on a given dimension differed by more than one point, a third rater was consulted and the final score was",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"0.78 to 0.89, indicating good to excellent inter-rater agreement (Koo and Li, 2016). In cases where the two raters’ scores on a given dimension differed by more than one point, a third rater was consulted and the final score was determined by majority agreement. 3.5 Procedure The study proceeded in three stages over eight weeks: Stage 1: Pretest (Week 1). All participants across the three conditions completed the revised CTDI-CV and produced a baseline writing sample on a standardised inquiry topic under timed conditions (60 min). A brief background questionnaire was also administered, covering demographic information and self-reported academic writing experience (most recent writing course grade). The pretest writing samples were collected, anonymised, and stored for subsequent blind scoring. Stage 2: Intervention (Weeks 2–7). Over the course of six weeks, students in all three groups participated in an IBW task based on given data and materials as part of their regular coursework. The IBW instruction followed a consistent structure across all conditions: each week, students received a new inquiry prompt, conducted independent research, developed an evidence-based argument, and submitted a",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"based on given data and materials as part of their regular coursework. The IBW instruction followed a consistent structure across all conditions: each week, students received a new inquiry prompt, conducted independent research, developed an evidence-based argument, and submitted a written draft. The instructor provided the same instructional guidance, feedback schedule, and task prompts across all three sections. The conditions differed only in the GenAI tool available during the inquiry and writing process. Students in Condition 1 used their assigned multimodal tool to interact with text, images, and visual representations throughout the inquiry and writing process. Students in Condition 2 used their assigned text-only tool for the same purposes. Students in Condition 3 relied on conventional resources, including library databases, search engines, and instructor feedback. To prevent cross- contamination between conditions, students in the control group were instructed not to use GenAI tools for course-related writing tasks during the intervention period, and compliance was monitored through periodic self-report checks. To ensure that any observed differences between the two GenAI conditions could be attributed to modality rather than to differences in",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"use GenAI tools for course-related writing tasks during the intervention period, and compliance was monitored through periodic self-report checks. To ensure that any observed differences between the two GenAI conditions could be attributed to modality rather than to differences in the quality or quantity of AI assistance, several design controls were implemented. Both tools were built on models from the same Qwen family (Qwen3-VL and Qwen3), sharing equivalent conversational capabilities, knowledge bases, and response quality. Both were configured with identical system prompts that specified the same intellectual support functions (presenting multiple perspectives, generating counterarguments, and providing feedback on argument structure) and differed only in whether the output could include visual elements. Interaction frequency and duration were not restricted in either condition. To ensure treatment fidelity, the research team implemented two monitoring measures. First, tool usage logs were collected for Conditions 1 and 2, recording the frequency, duration, and type of GenAI interactions. Second, the instructor completed a brief fidelity checklist after each session, confirming that the same instructional content and procedures were delivered across all three sections. Stage 3: Posttest",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"1 and 2, recording the frequency, duration, and type of GenAI interactions. Second, the instructor completed a brief fidelity checklist after each session, confirming that the same instructional content and procedures were delivered across all three sections. Stage 3: Posttest (Week 8). All participants completed the CTDI-CV again and produced a final writing sample on a comparable inquiry topic under the same timed conditions as the pretest (60 min). To minimise topic effects, the pretest and posttest inquiry topics were matched in difficulty, scope, and disciplinary relevance, and the order of topics was counterbalanced across conditions. All writing samples were anonymised, randomised, and scored by two trained raters blind to both condition and time point. The scoring procedure followed the calibration and reliability protocols described in Section 3.4. 3.6 Data analysis The data analysis proceeded in three steps. Step 1: Preliminary checks. Baseline equivalence across the three groups was examined using one-way ANOVA on pretest CTDI-CV scores, pretest writing quality scores, and key demographic variables (age, prior GenAI experience). Chi- square tests were used for categorical variables (gender, major). No",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"Preliminary checks. Baseline equivalence across the three groups was examined using one-way ANOVA on pretest CTDI-CV scores, pretest writing quality scores, and key demographic variables (age, prior GenAI experience). Chi- square tests were used for categorical variables (gender, major). No significant baseline differences were detected on any variable; had any been found, the relevant variable would have been included. Assumptions of ANCOVA were checked prior to the main analysis, including normality of residuals (Shapiro– Wilk test), homogeneity of variance (Levene’s test), and homogeneity of regression slopes (testing the interaction between the covariate and the independent variable). In the event of violated assumptions, non-parametric alternatives (Quade’s rank ANCOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test with post hoc Dunn’s test) would have been employed. Step 2: Group differences in CT dispositions (RQ1). A one- way ANCOVA was conducted to compare posttest CTDI-CV scores across the three conditions, with pretest CTDI-CV scores as the covariate. This analysis tested whether the three instructional conditions produced significantly different changes in CT dispositions after controlling for baseline differences. If the omnibus F-test is significant, post hoc pairwise comparisons with",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,6,"three conditions, with pretest CTDI-CV scores as the covariate. This analysis tested whether the three instructional conditions produced significantly different changes in CT dispositions after controlling for baseline differences. If the omnibus F-test is significant, post hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction were conducted to identify which specific conditions differed. Effect sizes were reported as partial η2, with η2 = 0.01, 0.06, and 0.14 interpreted as small, medium, and large effects, respectively (Cohen, 1988). In addition to the overall CTDI-CV score, the same ANCOVA procedure was applied to each of the five subdimensions (openness, analytical thinking, systematic He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",106
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"thinking, confidence in CT, and cognitive maturity) to explore whether the treatment effect was uniform across dimensions or concentrated in specific aspects of CT. Step 3: Group differences in academic writing quality (RQ2). The same ANCOVA procedure was applied to posttest academic writing quality scores, with pretest writing quality scores as the covariate. The overall writing quality score (out of 20) served as the primary outcome. As the omnibus test was significant, the same three pairwise contrasts were tested with Bonferroni correction. To explore differential effects across writing dimensions, supplementary ANCOVAs were conducted on each of the four rubric subdimensions (argument structure, evidence use, analytical depth, coherence and integration) separately. This analysis aimed to reveal whether the treatment effect was driven by improvement across all dimensions or concentrated in specific aspects of writing quality. All analyses were conducted using SPSS (version 28). Statistical significance was set at α = 0.05 for all tests. For the subdimensional analyses, a Bonferroni-adjusted alpha level was applied to control for familywise error rate (α = 0.05/5 = 0.01 for CTDI-CV subdimensions; α = 0.05/4",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"(version 28). Statistical significance was set at α = 0.05 for all tests. For the subdimensional analyses, a Bonferroni-adjusted alpha level was applied to control for familywise error rate (α = 0.05/5 = 0.01 for CTDI-CV subdimensions; α = 0.05/4 = 0.0125 for writing quality subdimensions). 4 Results 4.1 Participants and demographic characteristics A total of 165 undergraduate students participated in the study, with 55 students in each of the three conditions. Table 1 presents the demographic characteristics and control variables across the three groups. The sample was predominantly female (57.0%), with a mean age of 20.46 years (SD = 0.94). Participants reported moderate levels of prior GenAI experience (M = 2.96, SD = 1.06, on a 5-point scale). The distribution of demographic characteristics appeared comparable across the three conditions. 4.2 Preliminary checks 4.2.1 Baseline equivalence To verify that the three groups were comparable prior to the intervention, one-way ANOVAs were conducted on all continuous baseline variables, and a chi-square test was conducted for gender. As shown in Table 2, no significant differences were found across the three conditions on",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"three groups were comparable prior to the intervention, one-way ANOVAs were conducted on all continuous baseline variables, and a chi-square test was conducted for gender. As shown in Table 2, no significant differences were found across the three conditions on any baseline variable, confirming that the groups were statistically equivalent at pretest. Because baseline equivalence was established on all variables, only the pretest score on the respective dependent variable was included as the covariate in each primary ANCOVA, consistent with the planned analysis. Prior GenAI experience, age, and gender were not included as additional covariates because they showed no significant baseline imbalance and their inclusion would reduce statistical power without improving the precision of the estimates. 4.2.2 Assumption checks Prior to the main analyses, the assumptions of ANCOVA were examined. Normality of residuals was assessed using the Shapiro– Wilk test; no significant departures from normality were detected for either dependent variable. Levene’s test indicated that the assumption of homogeneity of variance was met for both posttest CT scores [F(2, 162) = 0.31, p = .734] and posttest WQ scores [F(2,",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"no significant departures from normality were detected for either dependent variable. Levene’s test indicated that the assumption of homogeneity of variance was met for both posttest CT scores [F(2, 162) = 0.31, p = .734] and posttest WQ scores [F(2, 162) = 1.18, p = .310]. The homogeneity of regression slopes assumption was tested by examining the interaction between the covariate and the grouping variable; no significant interactions were found [CT: F(2, 159) = 0.42, p = .658; WQ: F(2, 159) = 0.89, p = .413]. All assumptions were satisfied, and the planned ANCOVA analyses were conducted. 4.2.3 Comparative GenAI usage To examine whether differences existed in the volume of AI interactions between the two generative AI (GenAI) conditions, the researchers analyzed tool usage logs collected during the six- week intervention. Three usage metrics were extracted for each student: the total number of interaction sessions initiated, the cumulative duration of interactions (in minutes), and the total TABLE 1 Participant demographic characteristics and control variables by condition. Variable Multimodal GenAI (n = 55) Text- based GenAI (n = 55) No GenAI",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"total number of interaction sessions initiated, the cumulative duration of interactions (in minutes), and the total TABLE 1 Participant demographic characteristics and control variables by condition. Variable Multimodal GenAI (n = 55) Text- based GenAI (n = 55) No GenAI Control (n = 55) Age, M (SD) 20.49 (0.96) 20.35 (0.90) 20.55 (0.95) Gender, n (%) Male 22 (40.0%) 25 (45.5%) 24 (43.6%) Female 33 (60.0%) 30 (54.5%) 31 (56.4%) Prior GenAI experience (1– 5), M (SD) 2.96 (1.06) 2.93 (1.10) 3.00 (1.04) Pretest CT Total (15–90), M (SD) 55.06 (6.78) 53.21 (5.94) 54.27 (7.28) Pretest WQ Total (4–20), M (SD) 11.15 (1.92) 10.89 (1.68) 10.30 (2.09) TABLE 2 Baseline equivalence tests. Variable Test Statistic p Interpretation Pretest CT Total ANOVA F(2, 162) = 1.05 0.352 No significant difference Pretest WQ Total ANOVA F(2, 162) = 2.86 0.06 No significant difference Age ANOVA F(2, 162) = 0.81 0.446 No significant difference Prior GenAI experience ANOVA F(2, 162) = 0.12 0.89 No significant difference Gender Chi- square χ²(2) = 0.67 0.715 No significant difference He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,7,"difference Age ANOVA F(2, 162) = 0.81 0.446 No significant difference Prior GenAI experience ANOVA F(2, 162) = 0.12 0.89 No significant difference Gender Chi- square χ²(2) = 0.67 0.715 No significant difference He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",42
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,8,"number of prompts submitted. Independent-samples t-tests (two- tailed) were used to compare the performance of the two GenAI student groups across these metrics. As shown in Table 3, no significant differences were found between the multimodal GenAI group and the text-based GenAI group regarding these three usage metrics, and all effect sizes were small. 4.3 RQ1: effects on CT dispositions 4.3.1 Overall CTDI-CV scores A one-way ANCOVA was conducted to compare posttest CT disposition scores across the three conditions, with pretest CTDI- CV total scores as the covariate. The results, as shwon in Table 4, revealed a significant main effect of condition on posttest CT dispositions, F(2, 161) = 44.52, p < .001, partial η2 = .356. The effect size was large, indicating that the instructional condition accounted for approximately 35.6% of the variance in posttest CT scores after controlling for pretest differences. Post hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction revealed that all three contrasts were statistically significant (Table 5). The multimodal GenAI group showed the greatest improvement in CT dispositions, significantly outperforming both the text-based GenAI group (d =",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,8,"for pretest differences. Post hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction revealed that all three contrasts were statistically significant (Table 5). The multimodal GenAI group showed the greatest improvement in CT dispositions, significantly outperforming both the text-based GenAI group (d = 0.34) and the no GenAI control group (d = 0.68). The text-based GenAI group also significantly outperformed the control group (d = 0.39). 4.3.2 Subdimensional analysis To examine whether the treatment effect was uniform across the five dimensions of CT dispositions, separate ANCOVAs were conducted for each subdimension, with the corresponding pretest subdimension score as the covariate. A Bonferroni- adjusted significance threshold of α = .01 (0.05/5) was applied to control for familywise error. The results are presented in Table 6. The treatment effect was strongest for openness (η2p = .242) and analytical thinking (η2p = .164), both of which reached significance at the adjusted threshold. For openness, the multimodal group scored substantially higher than the control group, with the text-based group falling in between. A similar pattern was observed for analytical thinking. The effects on systematic thinking and CT",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,8,"reached significance at the adjusted threshold. For openness, the multimodal group scored substantially higher than the control group, with the text-based group falling in between. A similar pattern was observed for analytical thinking. The effects on systematic thinking and CT confidence were moderate and reached significance at the conventional α = .05 level but did not survive the Bonferroni correction. The effect on cognitive maturity was small and not significant at the adjusted threshold. 4.4 RQ2: effects on academic writing quality 4.4.1 Overall writing quality scores A one-way ANCOVA was conducted to compare posttest academic writing quality scores across the three conditions, with pretest writing quality total scores as the covariate. The results revealed a significant main effect of condition, F(2, 161) = 41.12, p < .001, partial η2 = .314, indicating that the instructional condition accounted for approximately 31.4% of the variance in posttest writing quality scores after controlling for pretest performance. Table 7 presents the descriptive statistics and adjusted posttest means by condition. Post hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction revealed that all three contrasts were statistically significant",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,8,"of the variance in posttest writing quality scores after controlling for pretest performance. Table 7 presents the descriptive statistics and adjusted posttest means by condition. Post hoc pairwise comparisons with Bonferroni correction revealed that all three contrasts were statistically significant (Table 8). The multimodal GenAI group produced the highest-quality writing at posttest, significantly outperforming both the control group (d = 0.72) and the text-based GenAI group (d = 0.46). The TABLE 3 Comparative GenAI usage statistics between the two GenAI conditions. Metric Multimodal GenAI M (SD) Text-based GenAI M (SD) t p Cohen’s d Interaction sessions (count) 18.40 (5.21) 19.13 (5.78) −0.67 0.503 0.13 Total duration (minutes) 412.55 (96.83) 425.18 (101.27) −0.66 0.513 0.13 Prompts submitted (count) 119.82 (28.41) 124.36 (31.05) −0.79 0.434 0.15 TABLE 4 Presents the descriptive statistics and adjusted posttest means by condition. Condition Pretest M (SD) Posttest M (SD) Adjusted Posttest M Mean Gain 95% CI Multimodal GenAI 55.06 (6.78) 62.12 (7.29) 61.29 7.06 [60.52, 62.06] Text-based GenAI 53.21 (5.94) 57.82 (6.69) 58.73 4.61 [57.96, 59.50] No GenAI Control 54.27 (7.28) 56.18 (6.91) 56.09 1.91 [55.32,",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,8,"Posttest M (SD) Adjusted Posttest M Mean Gain 95% CI Multimodal GenAI 55.06 (6.78) 62.12 (7.29) 61.29 7.06 [60.52, 62.06] Text-based GenAI 53.21 (5.94) 57.82 (6.69) 58.73 4.61 [57.96, 59.50] No GenAI Control 54.27 (7.28) 56.18 (6.91) 56.09 1.91 [55.32, 56.86] TABLE 5 Post hoc pairwise comparisons for CT dispositions. Contrast p (Bonferroni) Cohen’s d Interpretation Multimodal vs. Control <.001 0.68 Medium-to-large effect Text-based vs. Control <.001 0.39 Small-to-medium effect Multimodal vs. Text-based <.001 0.34 Small-to-medium effect He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",86
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,9,"text-based GenAI group also significantly outperformed the control group (d = 0.38). Notably, the modality effect (multimodal vs. text-based) was larger for writing quality (d = 0.46) than for CT dispositions (d = 0.34), suggesting that the visual processing channel provided by multimodal GenAI may translate more directly into improvements in the written product. 4.4.2 Subdimensional analysis Separate ANCOVAs were conducted for each of the four writing quality subdimensions, with the corresponding pretest subdimension score as the covariate. A Bonferroni-adjusted significance threshold of α = .0125 (0.05/4) was applied. The results are presented in Table 9. All four subdimensions reached significance at the Bonferroni-adjusted threshold. However, the effect sizes varied substantially across dimensions, revealing a differential pattern that is theoretically informative. The modality effect was strongest for evidence use (η2p = .193) and analytical depth (η2p = .181), where the multimodal group showed a clear advantage over the text-based group. In contrast, the modality effect was weaker for argument structure (η2p = .103) and coherence (η2p = .118), where the two GenAI groups performed more similarly. 5 Discussion 5.1 Overview",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,9,"multimodal group showed a clear advantage over the text-based group. In contrast, the modality effect was weaker for argument structure (η2p = .103) and coherence (η2p = .118), where the two GenAI groups performed more similarly. 5 Discussion 5.1 Overview of findings This study examined how multimodal GenAI, text-based GenAI, and no GenAI differentially influence higher education students’ CT dispositions and academic writing quality in IBW courses. The results revealed a consistent pattern across both outcome measures: the multimodal GenAI group showed the greatest improvement, followed by the text-based GenAI group, with the no GenAI control group showing the smallest gains. All pairwise contrasts were statistically significant, and the effect sizes ranged from small-to-medium (text-based vs. control) to medium-to-large (multimodal vs. control). 5.2 The effect of GenAI on CT dispositions Both GenAI groups outperformed the control group on posttest CT dispositions, suggesting that GenAI-assisted IBW was associated with greater gains in CT dispositions more effectively than conventional instruction alone. This finding is consistent with prior research suggesting that GenAI can serve as a dialogic partner in educational settings, offering students",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,9,"dispositions, suggesting that GenAI-assisted IBW was associated with greater gains in CT dispositions more effectively than conventional instruction alone. This finding is consistent with prior research suggesting that GenAI can serve as a dialogic partner in educational settings, offering students opportunities to encounter alternative perspectives, test their arguments against counterarguments, and receive immediate feedback on the logical structure of their reasoning (Hikmawati and Mohammad, 2025). These dialogic interactions may, over the course of a sustained intervention, cultivate evaluative habits TABLE 6 Subdimensional ANCOVA results for CT dispositions. Dimension F(2, 161) p η2p Multimodal M [95% CI] Text-based M [95% CI] Control M [95% CI] Significance Openness 25.65 <.001 0.242 12.90 [12.40, 13.40] 12.20 [11.70, 12.70] 10.40 [9.90, 10.90] *** Analytical Thinking 15.8 <.001 0.164 12.50 [12.15, 12.85] 11.60 [11.25, 11.95] 11.10 [10.75, 11.45] *** Systematic Thinking 7.1 0.001 0.081 12.70 [11.93, 13.47] 11.00 [10.23, 11.77] 10.80 [10.03, 11.57] ** CT Confidence 5.08 0.007 0.059 11.80 [11.39, 12.21] 11.10 [10.69, 11.51] 10.90 [10.49, 11.31] ** Cognitive Maturity 3.3 0.039 0.039 12.30 [12.08, 12.52] 12.10 [11.88, 12.32] 11.90 [11.68, 12.12] **p <",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,9,"[11.93, 13.47] 11.00 [10.23, 11.77] 10.80 [10.03, 11.57] ** CT Confidence 5.08 0.007 0.059 11.80 [11.39, 12.21] 11.10 [10.69, 11.51] 10.90 [10.49, 11.31] ** Cognitive Maturity 3.3 0.039 0.039 12.30 [12.08, 12.52] 12.10 [11.88, 12.32] 11.90 [11.68, 12.12] **p < .01, ***p < .001. TABLE 7 Academic writing quality by condition. Condition Pretest M (SD) Posttest M (SD) Adjusted Posttest M Mean Gain 95% CI Multimodal GenAI 11.15 (1.92) 13.98 (2.19) 13.61 2.83 [13.32, 13.90] Text-based GenAI 10.89 (1.68) 12.71 (2.00) 12.6 1.82 [12.31, 12.89] No GenAI Control 10.30 (2.09) 11.22 (2.38) 11.71 0.92 [11.42, 12.00] TABLE 8 Post hoc pairwise comparisons for academic writing quality. Contrast p (Bonferroni) Cohen’s d Interpretation Multimodal vs. Control <.001 0.72 Medium-to-large effect Text-based vs. Control <.001 0.38 Small-to-medium effect Multimodal vs. Text-based <.001 0.46 Medium effect He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",142
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"that strengthen dispositions such as open-mindedness, although the present study did not directly measure the dialogic process itself. The multimodal GenAI group significantly outperformed the text-based GenAI group (d = 0.34), suggesting that the modality of GenAI interaction may was associated with differential gains in CT dispositions. This modality effect was not uniform across subdimensions: it was strongest for openness and analytical thinking, moderate for systematic thinking and CT confidence, and negligible for cognitive maturity. Openness, defined as the tendency to consider diverse perspectives and tolerate intellectual ambiguity, may be particularly responsive to multimodal GenAI. One possible explanation, consistent with CTML, is that visual representations make the plurality of evidence more concrete and perceptually salient. When students encounter multiple perspectives through visual comparisons and diagrams, they may find the diversity of viewpoints harder to ignore than when it is conveyed through text alone. 5.3 The effect of GenAI on academic writing quality The results for academic writing quality paralleled those for CT dispositions: multimodal GenAI produced the strongest gains, followed by text-based GenAI, with the control group showing the smallest",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"through text alone. 5.3 The effect of GenAI on academic writing quality The results for academic writing quality paralleled those for CT dispositions: multimodal GenAI produced the strongest gains, followed by text-based GenAI, with the control group showing the smallest improvement. The modality effect was particularly instructive at the subdimensional level. The multimodal advantage was strongest for evidence use and analytical depth, and weaker for argument structure and coherence. This differential pattern aligns with the predictions of the Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning. Evidence use and analytical depth are writing dimensions that may benefit from the ability to perceive, compare, and integrate diverse forms of evidence, as these processes are theoretically supported by the availability of visual representations alongside text. An important observation is that the modality effect was larger for writing quality (d = 0.46 for multimodal vs. text-based) than for CT dispositions (d = 0.34). One possible interpretation is that multimodal GenAI may have a more immediate impact on task performance (writing quality) than on dispositional change (CT orientations), although this inference should be treated with caution given",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"than for CT dispositions (d = 0.34). One possible interpretation is that multimodal GenAI may have a more immediate impact on task performance (writing quality) than on dispositional change (CT orientations), although this inference should be treated with caution given that the present design involved only a single posttest and did not track the temporal trajectory of change. This pattern is nevertheless consistent with the distinction between skills and dispositions in the CT literature: skills can improve rapidly in response to task-specific support, whereas dispositions, as trait-like orientations, change more slowly and require sustained exposure to supportive environments (Facione, 2000). 5.4 Implications This study makes three theoretical contributions. First, it provides preliminary empirical evidence that the dual-channel processing principles identified by CTML may be applicable to GenAI-assisted education. The findings are consistent with the prediction that multimodal presentation facilitates deeper cognitive engagement, and they suggest that these principles may extend to interactive, AI-mediated learning environments. However, this extension remains tentative, as the present study did not directly measure channel-specific cognitive activity; future research incorporating process-level measures such as eye-tracking or",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"cognitive engagement, and they suggest that these principles may extend to interactive, AI-mediated learning environments. However, this extension remains tentative, as the present study did not directly measure channel-specific cognitive activity; future research incorporating process-level measures such as eye-tracking or think-aloud protocols would be needed to confirm the CTML mechanisms underlying the observed effects. Second, this study contributes to the CT literature by providing evidence that CT dispositions are malleable through instructional intervention, and that the modality of the learning environment is associated with differential responsiveness across specific dimensions of disposition. Third, this study contributes to the emerging literature on GenAI in education by providing evidence that the design of the GenAI tool is associated with differential learning outcomes. The findings of this study also have several practical implications for educators and instructional designers. Educators should consider configuring multimodal GenAI tools to present multiple perspectives visually, generate argument structure diagrams, and create comparative visualisations of evidence, rather than simply providing text-based answers to student queries. For instructional designers developing GenAI-assisted learning environments, the differential subdimensional effects identified in this study",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"tools to present multiple perspectives visually, generate argument structure diagrams, and create comparative visualisations of evidence, rather than simply providing text-based answers to student queries. For instructional designers developing GenAI-assisted learning environments, the differential subdimensional effects identified in this study provide specific design guidance. If the goal is to improve argument structure and coherence, text-based GenAI feedback may be sufficient. If the goal is to deepen evidence use and analytical reasoning, multimodal features should be incorporated. 5.5 Limitations and future research Several limitations of this study should be acknowledged. First, since experimental conditions were assigned at the class level rather than the individual level, there may be differences in TABLE 9 Subdimensional ANCOVA results for academic writing quality. Dimension F(2, 161) p η2p Multimodal M [95% CI] Text-based M [95% CI] Control M [95% CI] Significance Argument Structure 9.28 <.001 0.103 3.80 [3.54, 4.06] 3.50 [3.24, 3.76] 3.00 [2.74, 3.26] *** Evidence Use 19.25 <.001 0.193 3.60 [3.39, 3.81] 3.00 [2.79, 3.21] 2.70 [2.49, 2.91] *** Analytical Depth 17.83 <.001 0.181 3.10 [2.96, 3.24] 2.80 [2.66, 2.94] 2.50 [2.36, 2.64]",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,10,"0.103 3.80 [3.54, 4.06] 3.50 [3.24, 3.76] 3.00 [2.74, 3.26] *** Evidence Use 19.25 <.001 0.193 3.60 [3.39, 3.81] 3.00 [2.79, 3.21] 2.70 [2.49, 2.91] *** Analytical Depth 17.83 <.001 0.181 3.10 [2.96, 3.24] 2.80 [2.66, 2.94] 2.50 [2.36, 2.64] *** Coherence & Integration 10.73 <.001 0.118 3.50 [3.34, 3.66] 3.40 [3.24, 3.56] 3.00 [2.84, 3.16] *** *** indicates statistical significance at p < .05. He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173 Frontiers in Education 10 frontiersin.org",74
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,11,"class-level characteristics, such as pre-existing peer interaction patterns or the quality of teacher-student rapport, that were not captured by baseline measures. The current study design does not allow for the statistical modeling of these class effects because each experimental condition comprises only a single intact class; this precludes the use of multilevel modeling to disentangle individual-level and class-level variance, potentially introducing error into the results. Future research should consider, where feasible, randomly assigning individual students to different groups or assigning multiple classes to each experimental condition to enable multilevel analysis, thereby enhancing both internal and external validity. Second, the intervention period of six weeks, while sufficient to detect significant effects on most outcome measures, may have been too short to produce measurable change on the deeper dimensions of CT dispositions. Future research should examine whether longer exposure to multimodal GenAI produces larger effects on these slow-developing dimensions, and whether the effects observed in this study persist beyond the intervention period. Third, this study measured CT dispositions through self- report, which is subject to social desirability bias and may not fully",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,11,"produces larger effects on these slow-developing dimensions, and whether the effects observed in this study persist beyond the intervention period. Third, this study measured CT dispositions through self- report, which is subject to social desirability bias and may not fully capture the behavioural changes associated with dispositional growth. Future studies could supplement self- report measures with behavioural indicators of CT, such as the quality of questions students ask, the frequency with which they challenge AI-generated content, or the complexity of the alternative perspectives they consider. Fourth, the study did not directly examine the cognitive processes through which multimodal GenAI influences CT dispositions. The CTML-based interpretation offered in this discussion, while theoretically grounded, remains inferential. Future research should incorporate process-level measures, such as think-aloud protocols, eye-tracking, or interaction log analysis, to trace the cognitive mechanisms that mediate the relationship between multimodal GenAI use and dispositional change. Fifth, the findings may be context-dependent. The study was conducted in a specific disciplinary context, with a specific student population, using a specific multimodal GenAI tool. The extent to which the results generalise to other",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,11,"GenAI use and dispositional change. Fifth, the findings may be context-dependent. The study was conducted in a specific disciplinary context, with a specific student population, using a specific multimodal GenAI tool. The extent to which the results generalise to other disciplines, student populations, cultural contexts, and GenAI platforms remains an empirical question. Cross-cultural and cross- disciplinary replications are needed to establish the boundary conditions of the findings. Although both sets of GenAI experimental conditions employed the same series of models and utilized identical system prompts and intelligent assistance features, the unrestricted nature of the interaction process means that the existence of unmeasured qualitative differences between the conditions cannot be entirely ruled out. For instance, multimodal responses inherently possess higher information density than text-only responses; this implies that, even with identical interaction durations, there may be differences in the actual volume of information processed. 6 Conclusion This study examined the impact of multimodal GenAI on CT dispositions and academic writing quality in an IBW context in higher education. The results demonstrated that multimodal GenAI produced the greatest improvements in both CT",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,11,"of information processed. 6 Conclusion This study examined the impact of multimodal GenAI on CT dispositions and academic writing quality in an IBW context in higher education. The results demonstrated that multimodal GenAI produced the greatest improvements in both CT dispositions and academic writing quality, followed by text- based GenAI, with the control group showing the smallest gains. The modality effect was not uniform across outcome dimensions: for CT, the multimodal advantage was concentrated in openness and analytical thinking; for writing quality, it was concentrated in evidence use and analytical depth. Cognitive theories of multimedia learning offer a potential cognitive perspective on these patterns of difference, suggesting that the added value of multimodal GenAI may have a relationship with its activation of the visual processing channel. The study contributes to the literature by extending CTML into GenAI-assisted learning contexts, by providing evidence that CT dispositions are differentially responsive to different modalities of instructional support, and by demonstrating that the design of GenAI tools matters as much as their availability. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this",180
A086,A086.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173,11,"providing evidence that CT dispositions are differentially responsive to different modalities of instructional support, and by demonstrating that the design of GenAI tools matters as much as their availability. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Fuzhou University Ethics Committee. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions YH: Software, Methodology, Writing – original draft, Formal analysis, Investigation, Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Data curation. TN: Resources, Supervision, Writing – review & editing, Project administration, Methodology. ZA: Software, Validation, Formal analysis, Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declared that financial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Conflict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. He et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1926173",180
