A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,1,"RESEARCH AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 https://doi.org/10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w past 30 years. Among the various A.I. tools and software, one of the most controversial and widely discussed is Chat­ GPT, a Large Language Model (LLM) chatbot launched by OpenAI in 2022. According to OpenAI, the promises of ChatGPT include writing, brainstorming, editing, and exploring ideas with its users, doing summary, automating repetitive tasks for them and allowing users to learn new things. The mechanisms that enable it to deliver the promises are manifold. The three mechanisms most relevant to the current context are (1) a diverse set of training data from a wide range of texts from books, articles, websites, and other written sources, for example, Wikipedia [39]; (2) contextual understanding that take the order of words and their meanings into the process and generate a coherent and contextually relevant response and (3) fine-tuning that utilizes the training method of Rein­ forcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF). Given ChatGPT’s capacities to generate informative, contextually relevant and personalized responses, it is unsurprising that it has caused a significant sensation and uncertainties in edu­",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,1,"(3) fine-tuning that utilizes the training method of Rein­ forcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF). Given ChatGPT’s capacities to generate informative, contextually relevant and personalized responses, it is unsurprising that it has caused a significant sensation and uncertainties in edu­ cation. These uncertainties are demonstrated by the initial 1 Introduction Artificial Intelligence in Education (AIEd) is currently one of the most debated topics in all areas of education research [19, 60, 67]. A.I. is considered the new form of teaching and learning innovativeness. Application of A.I. tools in higher education, including personalized learning software, intel­ ligent tutoring systems (ITS), virtual learning environment, etc., has gradually emerged in teaching and learning in the Lorraine Ka Chung Yeung lorraine@hkbu.edu.hk Daisy Pui Lun Chow puilunchow@cuhk.edu.hk Pak Hang Wong pakhangwong@hkbu.edu.hk Sam Shun Shun Lau samlau@hkbu.edu.hk 1 Hong Kong Baptist University, Hong Kong, China 2 Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China Abstract Generative artificial intelligence like ChatGPT paints a promising picture of enhancing students’ learning in higher edu­ cation. However, little is known regarding whether the university students actually adopt ChatGPT for enhancement",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,1,"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China Abstract Generative artificial intelligence like ChatGPT paints a promising picture of enhancing students’ learning in higher edu­ cation. However, little is known regarding whether the university students actually adopt ChatGPT for enhancement or misuse it for disburdenment. This study employed a qualitative descriptive research approach to investigate if there is the problem of misuse through an examination of the adoption journey of 20 university students in Hong Kong. Our findings show only a few interviewees did not use ChatGPT for disburdenment while the majority allow the cognitive artifact to liberate them from burdens of learning. Moreover, reports from misusers also show more pronounced automation bias and perception of AI agency than non-misusers. Among the misusers who are aware of the problem of AI hallucination, some exhibit a paradoxical approach of adoption behavior: they continue to rely on ChatGPT despite noticing that the new burdens of cross-checking its output may be induced. Such a decision calls into question the reasons for continuous reli­ ance on ChatGPT. Our finding suggests that this may be",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,1,"they continue to rely on ChatGPT despite noticing that the new burdens of cross-checking its output may be induced. Such a decision calls into question the reasons for continuous reli­ ance on ChatGPT. Our finding suggests that this may be associated with the students’ perception of ChatGPT as agentic, a factor known to positively shape users’ affect-based trust in AI. This study illuminates how actual cases of misuse and problematic adoption behavior unfold, offering useful directions for education of sensible, responsible and effective use of AI in the higher education context. Keywords ChatGPT · Automation bias · Trust in AI · Misuse · AI agency · AI literacy Received: 24 May 2025 / Accepted: 23 October 2025 / Published online: 1 December 2025 © The Author(s) 2025, corrected publication 2025 In ChatGPT they trust: a study of students’ perceptions and misuse of ChatGPT in higher education Lorraine Ka Chung Yeung1 · Daisy Pui Lun Chow2 · Pak Hang Wong1 · Sam Shun Shun Lau1 1 3",166
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 hesitation of including Generative AI into the educational setting among the universities in Hong Kong, for example, followed by the subsequent acceptance of generative AI in the form of a regulated approach as part of higher education [12, 33]. Scholars have examined the extensive range of concerns and challenges that ChatGPT and similar AI tools introduce to the educational landscape [36]. Notably, there is the emer­ gence of “technology-education tension” [11]. This tension is characterized, on one end, by educators’ apprehensions regarding the potential adverse effects of ChatGPT on stu­ dent learning. These apprehensions stem from the AI tool’s capacity to afford academic dishonesty, thereby bringing disruptive techno-moral changes related to academic integ­ rity. This is also the case in Hong Kong, where plagiarism, fabrication of data, and the unauthorized use of ChatGPT for assignment became the major concerns [33]. There is also a growing concern that students may increasingly outsource their assignments to ChatGPT. By allowing ChatGPT to replace them in performing cognitive tasks, such as writing, summarizing, and calculations, etc. students may fail",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"became the major concerns [33]. There is also a growing concern that students may increasingly outsource their assignments to ChatGPT. By allowing ChatGPT to replace them in performing cognitive tasks, such as writing, summarizing, and calculations, etc. students may fail to attain the expected learning outcomes. The legitimacy of educators’ concerns is underscored by the assertion that the pervasive use of large language models poses a risk of diminishing user’ own writing and critical thinking skills [30]. This concern is validated by findings from two recent studies. One is conducted in United King­ dom on a diverse sample comprised by 669 participants across different age groups, which found a “significant negative correlation between the frequent use of AI tools (cognitive offloading) and critical thinking abilities” [24]. The other study is conducted in Turkey in which nearly a thousand students in high school participated, which found that those who had access to an AI tool that mimicked a standard GPT interface in learning mathematics performed worse when the AI tool was taken away, when compared to students who never had access",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"high school participated, which found that those who had access to an AI tool that mimicked a standard GPT interface in learning mathematics performed worse when the AI tool was taken away, when compared to students who never had access to the AI tool [5]. On the other end of the tension is the belief that it is of utmost importance for teachers to educate students to use generative AI technologies responsibly and appropriately in learning, as these technologies become increasingly inte­ grated in society. Kasneci et al. [36] argue that fully leverag­ ing large language models (LLMs) in education necessitates enhancing students’ competencies and literacies in under­ standing the technology, including knowledge of the AI’s capacities, limitations, and the risk of false information. Additionally, it requires equipping students with strategies for fact-checking and strengthening their critical thinking. To better evaluate the impact of LLM on students’ learning process and outcomes, Heersmink [30] advocates for robust longitudinal empirical studies. These studies should inves­ tigate how students employ LLMs in their educational tasks and assess any resultant effects on their cognitive",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"evaluate the impact of LLM on students’ learning process and outcomes, Heersmink [30] advocates for robust longitudinal empirical studies. These studies should inves­ tigate how students employ LLMs in their educational tasks and assess any resultant effects on their cognitive skills and critical thinking ability. Currently, such empirical evidence is very limited. Concurrently, an attempt to investigate why and how students employ LLMs, and more specifically, to identify irresponsible uses of ChatGPT among students and to understand the factors promoting (or moderating) their misuse can offer insight into developing effective educa­ tional strategies that promote responsible and appropriate use of LLMs, thereby enhancing students’ AI literacy. There is a growing body of research on students’ perception, inten­ tion to adopt and use of ChatGPT in learning [1, 22, 46, 59]. There are also a few studies on stake holders’ perceptions or willingness of using ChatGPT to cheat [26, 42, 45]. How­ ever, a notable research gap still remains when it comes to examining actual cases in which university students misuse ChatGPT. With the recent launches of DeepSeek [17]—also a Large",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"or willingness of using ChatGPT to cheat [26, 42, 45]. How­ ever, a notable research gap still remains when it comes to examining actual cases in which university students misuse ChatGPT. With the recent launches of DeepSeek [17]—also a Large Reasoning Model that is said to be better than Chat­ GPT in reasoning—as well as OpenAI’s Deep Research [52], the needs to understand university students’ misuse of generative AI for academic purposes become even more pressing. This study aims to investigate students’ perceptions and utilization of ChatGPT for academic purposes, with a focus on the problem of misuse, including potential infringements on academic integrity. As such, this study serves as an ini­ tial effort to address both ends of the technology-education tension. Consequently, we address the following research questions: 1. What are university students’ utilizations and percep­ tions of ChatGPT for academic purposes? 2. To what extent are students using ChatGPT in ways that validate the educators’ concerns and apprehensions indicated in the technology-education tension? 3. What (technology-related) factors are associated with the misuse of ChatGPT (if any) by students?",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,2,"for academic purposes? 2. To what extent are students using ChatGPT in ways that validate the educators’ concerns and apprehensions indicated in the technology-education tension? 3. What (technology-related) factors are associated with the misuse of ChatGPT (if any) by students? 2 Literature review and clarifications of concepts 2.1 Misuse To address the research questions, we first construct a work­ ing categorization scheme that delineate different types of proper use and misuse of ChatGPT among students in higher education. For this purpose, we provide a general overview of the promises ChatGPT can offer to students’ learning, from which we derive specific types of proper use and misuses. As a working categorization scheme, it remains subject to further elaboration and revisions. 1 3 11 Page 2 of 20",126
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 Language learning, whether written or spoken, neces­ sitates extensive input, practice, and feedback. However, various constraints such as limited class time, classroom settings, teachers’ workload, students’ reticence and nega­ tive attitudes towards seeking assistance, and individual differences in language proficiency and personality traits exacerbate the challenges inherent in acquiring linguistic competence. ChatGPT is believed to be able to address this gap. One proposed application of ChatGPT is its utilization as a conversational partner within the context of task-ori­ ented dialogue [10, 15, 20, 74]. It is suggested that such conversational simulations, whether in written or oral form, serve as valuable linguistic resources for students, comple­ menting traditional grammar and vocabulary exercises. Fur­ thermore, students can gain a better understanding of the prescriptive usage of the target language through real-time feedback, which involves identifying errors highlighted by the AI, reading the explanations for these errors, and reflect­ ing on their mistakes. ChatGPT’s ability to comprehend complex relationships between ideas and make judgments or provide responses according to specific frameworks and criteria constitutes a significant distinction between",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"by the AI, reading the explanations for these errors, and reflect­ ing on their mistakes. ChatGPT’s ability to comprehend complex relationships between ideas and make judgments or provide responses according to specific frameworks and criteria constitutes a significant distinction between AI software and search engines like Google. Furthermore, ChatGPT exhibits con­ tinuity in the prompt-and-feedback chain, allowing students to continue conversations previously initiated and request additional information or perform further tasks. It is posited that this continuous interactivity, coupled with high order “cognitive” capabilities, enables students to enhance their critical and creative thinking skills. A commonly suggested application of ChatGPT is to utilize it as a debate partner. For instance, students can prompt ChatGPT to critique their arguments in assignments and pose thought-provoking questions to encourage deeper reflection on the topics under discussion [57]. Through these virtual “debates,” students can develop the ability to analyze and evaluate issues from multiple perspectives and practice defending a standpoint with reasoned arguments [2]. Another influential change to students’ learning antici­ pated with proper use of ChatGPT is its potential to facili­ tate personalized",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"develop the ability to analyze and evaluate issues from multiple perspectives and practice defending a standpoint with reasoned arguments [2]. Another influential change to students’ learning antici­ pated with proper use of ChatGPT is its potential to facili­ tate personalized learning and adaptive learning pathways [4, 51]. Students can input their specific learning require­ ments, desired difficulty levels, and preferences for types of learning materials, enabling ChatGPT to function as a personalized tutor. Instructional materials and guidance can be adjusted based on students’ progress and performance. ChatGPT is not only capable of generating human-like, real- time responses but is also adaptive. By synthesizing Big Data and existing research on learning, and by analyzing students’ performance data, ChatGPT can predict students’ responses and success rates in real-time learning tasks. This capability allows ChatGPT to create and adjust instructional content and materials tailored to individual students’ prog­ ress, needs, and abilities, thereby achieving the goal of “personalized adaptive learning (PAL)” [55]. According to Rusal [57], PAL, facilitated by ChatGPT, embodies the prin­ ciples of constructivist theory in the learning process. Con­ structivist",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"tailored to individual students’ prog­ ress, needs, and abilities, thereby achieving the goal of “personalized adaptive learning (PAL)” [55]. According to Rusal [57], PAL, facilitated by ChatGPT, embodies the prin­ ciples of constructivist theory in the learning process. Con­ structivist learning theory posits that knowledge is actively constructed by learners building connection between new information with their existing knowledge base. ChatGPT supports this process by providing a range of learning activities, thought-provoking questions, and personalized responses that stimulate cognitive engagement and facilitate the construction of new knowledge. Stapleton and Stefaniak [66] point out that these scaffolded learning experiences are crucial for helping students grasp new concepts. Based on the promises and potential benefits of utiliz­ ing ChatGPT, we have identified key points regarding its use. In what follows, uses of ChatGPT that align with these key points are considered proper, whereas those that do not are deemed misuse in an academic setting. The definition of “misuse” of generative AI adopted in this paper is based on a normative pedagogical standpoint rooted in active learn­ ing principles [76–78]. Central to the",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"whereas those that do not are deemed misuse in an academic setting. The definition of “misuse” of generative AI adopted in this paper is based on a normative pedagogical standpoint rooted in active learn­ ing principles [76–78]. Central to the idea of active learning is the requirement for students to engage in learning activi­ ties and reflect on their actions. Rather than merely memo­ rization and adapting information, students are expected to construct knowledge and acquire skills through analysis, synthesizing and evaluation. Accordingly, the “misuse” of generative AI, including ChatGPT, is defined as any use of these tools which does not involve—or, indeed, replace— higher-order thinking or the cognitive effort needed for acquiring the skills and knowledge, prioritize task comple­ tion over engagement. In what follows, we will illustrate the concepts of “mis­ use” with respect to the aforementioned benefits of utilizing ChatGPT in higher education context. We do not intend it to be an exhaustive list of proper use and misuses. Firstly, in terms of language learning, ChatGPT can function as either a practice partner or a tutor. As",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,3,"of utilizing ChatGPT in higher education context. We do not intend it to be an exhaustive list of proper use and misuses. Firstly, in terms of language learning, ChatGPT can function as either a practice partner or a tutor. As a practice partner, ChatGPT provides scenarios and responses tailored to students’ answers and progress. Learning happens when students engage in virtual conversation and exchange, facil­ itating learning through virtual conversation and exchange. As a tutor, ChatGPT’s primary role is to point out errors and provide real-time feedback, guiding students through revision and improvement. Learning occurs when students recognize their mistakes and understand the underlying rea­ sons. Improvement in language proficiency happens when students comprehend the feedback and can make the neces­ sary corrections. Therefore, if students use ChatGPT solely for proofreading or rewriting without seeking or under­ standing the explanations provided in the feedback, it is considered misuse. 1 3 Page 3 of 20 11",155
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 2.2 Over-trust, automation bias, over-reliance Misuses of AI are associated with factors related to human- AI interaction, among which over-trust in AI has recently drawn growing attention [25, 34]. The rise of conversational AI like ChatGPT-4 has changed the dynamics of human- computer interaction in the way that the AI may no longer be a mere tool or medium but a distinct communication “part­ ner” [28, 37]. ChatGPT is a conversational, communicative, relational entity. As such, users may regard it as a social actor “with which they develop relationship” [54]. These changing dynamics highlight and revitalize the importance of the research on trust [25, 37]. Trust is a fluid concept, whose meaning is highly con­ text-dependent. In Human-Computer Interaction studies, “trust” is defined as “the willingness of a party to be vulner­ able to the actions of another party based on the expectation that the other will perform a particular action important to the trustor” [37, 48]. Alternatively, it is “the attitude that an agent will help achieve an individual’s goals in situations characterized by",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"actions of another party based on the expectation that the other will perform a particular action important to the trustor” [37, 48]. Alternatively, it is “the attitude that an agent will help achieve an individual’s goals in situations characterized by uncertainty and vulnerability” [40, 54]. In relation to AI/automation technology adoption behavior, trust is a major predictor of whether users will rely on AI’s advice or output [38]. While trust is an attitude, reliance is a behavioral outcome associated with trust [37]. Human-AI trust can be examined in terms of multiple dimensions: reli­ ability; technical competence; understandability, faith, and personal attachment [41]. We further distinguish reliability, technical competence, understandability as the (perceived) properties of the AI, while faith and personal attachment are affectively-charged attitudes that the trustor has towards the AI. The above descriptions show that trust is understood as a kind of willingness, expectation, or attitude, and it is asso­ ciated with behavior (reliance). In philosophy, the concept of “trust” as an attitude humans have towards each other is similarly difficult to define, encompassing multiple dimen­ sions and theoretical",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"a kind of willingness, expectation, or attitude, and it is asso­ ciated with behavior (reliance). In philosophy, the concept of “trust” as an attitude humans have towards each other is similarly difficult to define, encompassing multiple dimen­ sions and theoretical approaches [61, 63]. Three prominent philosophical accounts of trust are the rational account, the affective account, and the normative account. Following these accounts, we can further distinguish different kinds of willingness, expectation and attitude in question. As we will show below, doing so equips us with finer conceptual tools that guide our data analysis. On the rational account of trust, trust is a matter of rationality. It is “the consideration a rational actor applies in deciding whether to place a bet [on the trustee]” [13]. This consideration typically involves weighing up pros and cons on the part of the rational actor when placing the bet [61]. The willingness to place the bet is based on the trus­ tor’s rational evaluation of the trustee’s competence. This Secondly, with regard to developing critical thinking and problem-solving skills, ChatGPT can be utilized to",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"actor when placing the bet [61]. The willingness to place the bet is based on the trus­ tor’s rational evaluation of the trustee’s competence. This Secondly, with regard to developing critical thinking and problem-solving skills, ChatGPT can be utilized to validate arguments and opinions presented by students. Engaging in “conversations” with ChatGPT, such as debates, question­ ing, and commenting, prompts students to evaluate reason­ ing and reconsider topics from diverse perspectives. Rather than seeking direct answers from ChatGPT, students should address counterarguments and critiques by clarifying ques­ tions and related concepts, refining their arguments, and conducting further research. This process serves to enhance students’ higher cognitive skills. Consequently, prompting ChatGPT to generate ideas and formulate arguments for specific projects or essay topics constitutes misuse. Third, with regard to personalized learning, ChatGPT can aid knowledge acquisition by providing an overview of theories or concepts and suggesting further study sources. Students can begin by asking ChatGPT to summarize a par­ ticular theory and recommend key literature or foundational texts in the domain. This offers learners an entry point into understanding the theory",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"theories or concepts and suggesting further study sources. Students can begin by asking ChatGPT to summarize a par­ ticular theory and recommend key literature or foundational texts in the domain. This offers learners an entry point into understanding the theory or concept. Moreover, students can leverage ChatGPT’s searching and organizing capabili­ ties to broaden their studies with interdisciplinary knowl­ edge. With these initial insights, students are expected to verify the information by consulting the referenced sources provided by ChatGPT. This verification process is crucial not only for ensuring the accuracy and reliability of the information but also as part of active engagement with the material, which is essential for active learning. Dynamic assessments are an integral part of meaningful personalized learning. Students can prompt ChatGPT to design questions or engage in a “discussion” to consolidate their knowledge. Learning is achieved when students review the suggestions provided by ChatGPT, verify the generated content by con­ sulting original sources, and confirm their understanding through assessments. However, if students merely read the content generated by ChatGPT without verifying its cred­ ibility and consulting",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,4,"when students review the suggestions provided by ChatGPT, verify the generated content by con­ sulting original sources, and confirm their understanding through assessments. However, if students merely read the content generated by ChatGPT without verifying its cred­ ibility and consulting the original sources, it constitutes misuse. The most crucial factor in the proper use of ChatGPT for learning and enhancing cognitive skills lies in students’ active engagement and reflection. The absence of this engagement is evident when students use ChatGPT to cheat and generate content for their assignments, effectively act­ ing as ghostwriters. While the ability to provide real-time and personalized feedback is a significant advantage for learning, it also presents a double-edged sword. On one hand, it is a key factor in the potential benefits of ChatGPT in learning. On the other hand, it tempts students to take shortcuts and outsource the cognitive effort required for assignments, which are intended to be an essential part of the learning process, to machines. 1 3 11 Page 4 of 20",169
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 questionable “decisions” from the machines without con­ sidering further corroborative or contradictory information; users may even dismiss information from other existing sources [64]. A cognitive bias is a systematic error arising from human cognitive system, which involves the fast, automated think­ ing System 1, driven by affects and intuitions and the slow, deliberative thinking System 2 [35]. Cognitive biases are often characterized by the fast-thinking system’s inability to process information exceeding its capacity or its intui­ tive, automatic output unchecked by System 2. Some biases are also affect- or desire-driven. For example, confirmation bias refers to the tendency that “information is searched for, interpreted, and remembered in such a way that it system­ atically impedes the possibility that the hypothesis could be rejected—that is, it fosters the immunity of the hypothesis” [53]. It is explained by the limited capacity for one to pro­ cess information and/or the desire to uphold one’s exist­ ing belief(s). To our best knowledge, there is no decisive characterization determining the feature of automation bias. We also note that a constitutive",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"by the limited capacity for one to pro­ cess information and/or the desire to uphold one’s exist­ ing belief(s). To our best knowledge, there is no decisive characterization determining the feature of automation bias. We also note that a constitutive feature of automation bias is similar to confirmation bias, in that one exhibits com­ placency when making judgements. Therefore, we adopt the most inclusive understanding of automation bias that accommodate the most possibilities. Accordingly, where over-trust is understood as “a poorly calibrated trust exceeding system capabilities” [37] and refers primarily to an erroneous factual judgment of the technology’s features or competency, we consider it as a failed case in the domain of rational/cognitive trust. In this paper, we follow Gilkson and Woolley [25] and term it “cognitive over-trust”. Cases of cognitive over-trust can be identified from the users’ descriptive statements about their perceptions and adoption behavior of the AI. When over- trust cannot be explained by the trustor’s erroneous factual judgement of the technology’s features or competency, we classify theses as cases of “affective over-trust”. Although this classification is admittedly",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"about their perceptions and adoption behavior of the AI. When over- trust cannot be explained by the trustor’s erroneous factual judgement of the technology’s features or competency, we classify theses as cases of “affective over-trust”. Although this classification is admittedly crude, it serves our discus­ sion purposes and is not intended to be a rigid one. Both cognitive and affective over-trust can lead to over­ reliance [25, 34, 37]. Consider the case of over-reliance in which a New York lawyer included references in a filing to precedent cases that did not actually exist, provided by ChatGPT. The lawyer’s affiliated law firm explained, “we made a good faith mistake in failing to believe that a piece of technology could be making up cases out of whole cloth” [70]. This over-trust is primarily a result of an erroneous evaluation of ChatGPT’s technical features and capabilities, a factual error. We consider it a paradigm case of overreli­ ance resulting from cognitive over-trust. Kaas [34] instances an experiment showing that even if participants first wit­ nessed a robot misleading them to the wrong room",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"features and capabilities, a factual error. We consider it a paradigm case of overreli­ ance resulting from cognitive over-trust. Kaas [34] instances an experiment showing that even if participants first wit­ nessed a robot misleading them to the wrong room in an evaluation is a factual judgment on the trustee’s compe­ tence, which can be articulated in a descriptive statement. Next, the affective account asserts that trust is a matter of the trustor’s optimistic attitude in placing confidence and belief in the goodwill of the trustee [61, 63]. The optimis­ tic attitude is affectively charged, typically involving posi­ tive affect towards the trustee, whose motivation to perform the entrusted action is believed to stem from their goodwill towards the trustor. While the rational and affective accounts imply predictive expectations of the trustee’s competency or goodwill, respectively, the normative account states that trust is the ascription of an obligation to another, making the expectation normatively laden [61]. On these philosophical accounts, trust can imply a behav­ ioral dimension. For example, the rational account states that if one acts in a way",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"that trust is the ascription of an obligation to another, making the expectation normatively laden [61]. On these philosophical accounts, trust can imply a behav­ ioral dimension. For example, the rational account states that if one acts in a way that relies on the trustee and thereby exposes oneself to risks, then one places trust in the trustee [61]. On this definition, trust conceptually implies the action of reliance. In this study, we adopt the rational and affective accounts of trust in philosophy, with some adaptations. In addition to the condition of the trustor’s belief in the trustee’s good­ will in the affective account, we include any perceived posi­ tive properties of AI that form affective trust documented in Human-AI interaction studies. In doing so, we align this study with Glikson and Woolley et al. [25], in which human- AI trust is classified into cognitive trust and affect-based trust. The former “involves rational evaluation of the trustee and situational features” and the latter is influenced by non- rational factors such as emotions and moods [25]. Taken together, “cognitive trust” in",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"trust is classified into cognitive trust and affect-based trust. The former “involves rational evaluation of the trustee and situational features” and the latter is influenced by non- rational factors such as emotions and moods [25]. Taken together, “cognitive trust” in AI refers to cases in which an actor is willing to place a bet on an AI to achieve a goal for the actor in situations typically characterized by uncertainty and vulnerability, and the willingness is based on rational evaluations of the AI’s competency, typically alongside a cost and benefit analysis of placing the bet. “Affective trust” refers to cases in which an actor is willing to place a bet in the aforesaid manner, but the willingness is influenced by factors such as certain positive properties of the AI but not primarily based on rational evaluations of the trustee’s com­ petency that characterize cognitive trust. Stipulating different kinds of “trust” allows us to address effectively the problem of over-trust and its associated mis­ uses or “inappropriate overreliance” on a technology [25]. Users exhibit over-trust in technology when they blindly rely",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,5,"petency that characterize cognitive trust. Stipulating different kinds of “trust” allows us to address effectively the problem of over-trust and its associated mis­ uses or “inappropriate overreliance” on a technology [25]. Users exhibit over-trust in technology when they blindly rely on systems that do not warrant such trust [37, 40]. This attitude of over-trust in automated decisions is at the core of the cognitive bias known as “automation bias”. Automa­ tion bias refers to the thoughtless acceptance of decisions or recommendations made by the system [64]. It is character­ ized by the tendency to over-trust and over-rely on machines that are allegedly neutral, that is, users follow wrong or 1 3 Page 5 of 20 11",116
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 or an object as having human qualities, such as feelings” [25]. Anthropomorphism is a long-standing phenomenon in human-computer interaction. The well-documented “Eliza effect,” for example, refers to “people’s tendency to believe that a chatbot is thinking and understanding like a person” [50]. It occurs when users attribute intrinsic human-like qualities and abilities to computer systems, which likely exceed the system’s capabilities. The perception of agency in ChatGPT, as Pan et al. [54] suggests, arises from two features of conversational AI agents, namely their autonomy and subjective language. Autonomy refers to AI system’s appearance of being “inde­ pendent, capable of making its own decisions, and has control over its own behaviors,” while subjective language includes the use of first-person pronouns and other commu­ nicative means indicating personal opinions and perspec­ tives [54]. They found that the two features positively affect human’s trust of AI in a way consistent with findings show­ ing that people liked erroneous robotic AI more. For exam­ ple, participants’ trust and liking of AI chatbots were higher for autonomous AI that used",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"two features positively affect human’s trust of AI in a way consistent with findings show­ ing that people liked erroneous robotic AI more. For exam­ ple, participants’ trust and liking of AI chatbots were higher for autonomous AI that used subjective language, when compared to autonomous AI that uses objective language. However, the groups did not differ in their evaluations of the chat quality. The results showed that human’s perception of AI as agentic “served as an important cue for impression formation, particularly in terms of perceived trustworthi­ ness and liking” [54]. Since human trust in the AI chatbots can be formed independently of chat qualities, that is, the competency and capabilities of the AI, we can consider it as a case of “affective trust”. If affective trust in AI is possible, the same is likely to be true for over-trust. The features identified by Pan et al. [54] are certainly useful in understanding users’ perception of agency in AI systems; however, they lack nuance in capturing the grada­ tions in perceived agency. In this respect, we refer to the",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"over-trust. The features identified by Pan et al. [54] are certainly useful in understanding users’ perception of agency in AI systems; however, they lack nuance in capturing the grada­ tions in perceived agency. In this respect, we refer to the multidimensional account proposed by Dung [18], which distinguishes five dimensions of (artificial) agency: (i) goal- directedness, (ii) autonomy, (iii) efficacy, (iv) planning, and (v) intentionality. Goal-directedness involves an entity having preferences and acting to achieve them. Dung (2024) distinguishes between minimal and higher levels of goal-directedness. A high level implies the entity exercises goal-directed control, which denotes the degree of control an entity has over the choice of action to realize the preference. Accordingly, an entity with goal-directed control selects actions based on expected outcomes or utility from a range of possibilities [18]. Autonomy is the ability to function without external intervention. A higher level of autonomy entails the capacity to learn from the environment rather than relying solely on pre-programmed knowledge. This flexibility enables more evacuation scenario, they still followed the robot’s subse­ quent instructions, demonstrating “a potentially dangerous",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"external intervention. A higher level of autonomy entails the capacity to learn from the environment rather than relying solely on pre-programmed knowledge. This flexibility enables more evacuation scenario, they still followed the robot’s subse­ quent instructions, demonstrating “a potentially dangerous level of over-trust” in the machines [34]. This over-trust does not seem to be explained by erroneous factual judge­ ment of the robot’s competency. We consider it as a case involving affective over-trust. An instance of high affective over-trust might include the faith dimension of trust in the AI Siri, typically exemplified in users’ statements such as “I believe advice from Siri even when I don’t know for certain that it is correct” and ”when Siri gives unusual advice, I am for certain that it is correct” [41]. 2.3 Perceptions of AI agency As already noted, human trust in AI is not merely a cognitive construct but can also be affected-based. In fact, research on affect-based trust reveals more complicated AI adoption behavior. Some studies have found that people experience more positive emotions toward cheating and even erroneous robotic AI,",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"is not merely a cognitive construct but can also be affected-based. In fact, research on affect-based trust reveals more complicated AI adoption behavior. Some studies have found that people experience more positive emotions toward cheating and even erroneous robotic AI, “sometimes even more than they like the one that does not make any mistakes” [25]. This highlights the growing need to understand what and how the features of AI shape affect-based trust in humans. In the case of virtual AI, the positive emotions users have towards AIs are linked to the AI’s human-likeness, personalization, responsiveness, interactive abilities, positive feedback during on-going con­ versations, and social praise for the users, etc [25]. Known factors for forming affect-based trust in embedded AI like ChatGPT also include the reputation of the technology and the reputation of the organization associated with it [25, 31]. In other words, affect-based trust in AI interacts with both technology-related and non-technological, contingent factors. More recent research on human interaction with conver­ sational AI has shown the trend of examining both cogni­ tive and affective trust in AI simultaneously.",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,6,"In other words, affect-based trust in AI interacts with both technology-related and non-technological, contingent factors. More recent research on human interaction with conver­ sational AI has shown the trend of examining both cogni­ tive and affective trust in AI simultaneously. In this context, “trust is not viewed solely as evaluations of the performance and competence of machines” but rather as user’s subjec­ tive perceptions and experiences of the properties of the AI, including its “sincerity, candidness, and trustworthi­ ness within the context of human-AI interaction” [54]. This subjective perception is also closely associated with the affect-based attitude of liking, i.e. the users’ positive feel­ ings toward the AI [54]. A crucial factor contributing to human’s subjective perception of AI is the level of agency the AI appears to have [54]. Research has shown that it is not uncommon for “humans may perceive AI to have some levels of agency in their own behaviors such as the thinking and acting capaci­ ties that they appear to have during Human-AI interac­ tion” [44]. The tendency to perceive AI as agentic can be",180
