A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 Borgmann’s conception is relevant to our study, which attends to the possibility that the different interpretations of “perceived usefulness” of ChatGPT may represent either proper or improper utilizations. The need to differentiate these interpretations of “per­ ceived usefulness” arises from instances in which a student claims that ChatGPT is useful for it “helps elaborate points “without [my] wasting time thinking of them” [1, p.13628]. The case suggests that “perceived usefulness” of ChatGPT does not lie in enhancement but in how it liberates the stu­ dent from certain burdens of learning. By adopting Borg­ mann’s framework, we can distinguish between cases in which a student’ perceived usefulness of ChatGPT primarily consists in disburdenment versus enhancement. When students engage ChatGPT to complete their assignments primarily for the disburdenment, without this disburdenment clearly integrating with other forms of enhancement in learning, 1 it is likely to divert the students from intended learning outcomes. It is a strong indicator of misuse. It is important to note that in this context, “disbur­ denment” is viewed negatively. 4 Research design We",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"enhancement in learning, 1 it is likely to divert the students from intended learning outcomes. It is a strong indicator of misuse. It is important to note that in this context, “disbur­ denment” is viewed negatively. 4 Research design We employed a qualitative description research approach to investigate university students’ adoption journey the Chat­ GPT in learning and the effects on their learning attitudes, habits, and behaviors. A qualitative description approach, which has been widely used to examine experiences in the use of educational technology [72], is deemed appropri­ ate for gaining a deeper understanding of the participants’ perspectives and lived experience regarding a specific phenomenon [14, 49]. Rather than sending out survey with open-ended questions, we conducted one-on-one, semi-structured interviews. It allows us to probe deeper into individual cases of misuse and track the interviewees’ subsequent attitudinal or behavioral adjustment, as we can examine how the interviewees actually described ChatGPT and narrated their changes throughout the adoption journey. To ensure complete and transparent reporting and improve rigor, and credibility, the Standards for Reporting Qualita­ tive Research (SRQR) checklist was",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"adjustment, as we can examine how the interviewees actually described ChatGPT and narrated their changes throughout the adoption journey. To ensure complete and transparent reporting and improve rigor, and credibility, the Standards for Reporting Qualita­ tive Research (SRQR) checklist was used in the study [8]. 4.1 Participants and context Participants eligible for inclusion were full-time university students in Hong Kong, China, during the 2023–2024 aca­ demic year who are aware of the utilization of ChatGPT in 1 We acknowledge that sometimes disburdenment may lead to other­ forms of enhancement in learning, for example, by freeing up some cognitive loads for more demanding tasks. [65] adaptive and responsive behaviors from the entity in the environment it is situated [18]. Efficacy refers to the ability to affect the physical world without mediation or intervention by others, whereas plan­ ning involves the capability to act for long-term goals, pri­ oritizing broader objectives over immediate reactions [18]. Finally, intentionality denotes the capacity to act for spe­ cific reasons, such as beliefs or preferences. When an entity generates responses that appear reasoned or purposeful, it",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"to act for long-term goals, pri­ oritizing broader objectives over immediate reactions [18]. Finally, intentionality denotes the capacity to act for spe­ cific reasons, such as beliefs or preferences. When an entity generates responses that appear reasoned or purposeful, it is more likely to be viewed as intentional [18]. Given our research focuses on students’ perception of the agency of ChatGPT in their interaction with it, rather than their long-term engagement, (iv) planning is not directly relevant. In addition, we consider (iii) efficacy could be reformulated to account for students’ use of ChatGPT for their academic needs, which does not necessarily involve affecting the physical world. Based on the various dimen­ sions offered by Dung, we propose a perception of agency in ChatGPT when the students attribute to it high-level of goal-directedness, autonomy, or intentionality. 3 Theoretical framework 3.1 Technology acceptance model (TAM) In the field of technology adoption studies, a well-docu­ mented technology-related factor prompting users’ adoption behavior is the time-saving feature, which includes the per­ ceived usefulness and perceived ease of using the technol­ ogy [62]. These factors,",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"acceptance model (TAM) In the field of technology adoption studies, a well-docu­ mented technology-related factor prompting users’ adoption behavior is the time-saving feature, which includes the per­ ceived usefulness and perceived ease of using the technol­ ogy [62]. These factors, which account for the technology adoption behavior, are at the core of the prominent Tech­ nology Adoption Model (TAM) [16], and are applicable to the education context [9, 46]. In TAM, perceived usefulness refers to the extent a person believes the technology can enhance their performance in a task, in which “usefulness” is understood as “capable of being used advantageously” [16]. In the context of education, the advantages encompass any reinforcement for good performance, say, better grades. Perceived ease of use refers to the extent to which a person believes that using the technology would be free from dif­ ficulty or great effort [16]. 3.2 Borgmann’s theory of device as disburdenment Technology philosopher Albert Borgmann’s characteriza­ tion of modern technology, which he refers to as a “device,” provides a conceptual framework for distinguishing two possible interpretations of “perceived usefulness”. In",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,7,"or great effort [16]. 3.2 Borgmann’s theory of device as disburdenment Technology philosopher Albert Borgmann’s characteriza­ tion of modern technology, which he refers to as a “device,” provides a conceptual framework for distinguishing two possible interpretations of “perceived usefulness”. In Borgmann’s theory, a device is disburdening for it deliv­ ers commodities and services to users instantaneously and ubiquitously, making them safe and easy to obtain [6]. 1 3 Page 7 of 20 11",73
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,8,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 allow the participants to explain their perceptions or expe­ riences with ChatGPT in detail. Probing questions were used as appropriate. The guide was validated by an expert on qualitative research to ensure that the questions align with the study aims. Each participant who completed the interview was compensated HK$100. The interviews were audio-recorded (Table 1). 4.3 Data analysis To ensure robust data analysis, data were analyzed con­ currently with data collection. Sampling was discontin­ ued upon reaching data saturation, defined as the point at which no new patterns emerged from additional interview data [27]. A sample size of 9–17 participants is generally sufficient to achieve saturation in qualitative studies with homogenous populations and narrowly defined objectives [32]. In this study, data saturation was achieved by the twen­ tieth interview. The audio recordings were transcribed verbatim by the research assistant and then compared to recordings by the first and the fourth authors to ensure the accuracy of the transcripts. We analyzed the data using the six-phase the­ matic analysis framework outlined by Braun and Clarke [7].",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,8,by the research assistant and then compared to recordings by the first and the fourth authors to ensure the accuracy of the transcripts. We analyzed the data using the six-phase the­ matic analysis framework outlined by Braun and Clarke [7]. This framework is widely adopted in qualitative research and is particularly suitable for exploratory studies like the present one. It facilitated the emergence of themes related students’ perceptions and factors associated with their learning or had first-hand experience using ChatGPT for academic purposes. This is the first academic year in which the public universities in Hong Kong had switched from prohibiting to “embracing” ChatGPT in teaching and learn­ ing [33]. This study adopted maximum variation purposeful sampling to understand the phenomenon of using ChatGPT by capturing a wider range of cases and perspectives from a range of academic disciplines across different universi­ ties possible [68]. Invitations with the researchers’ contact details and the aim of the study were widely distributed via emails and social media to university students. Eventually 20 undergraduate students from 9 different disciplines in 6 different universities,180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,8,"different universi­ ties possible [68]. Invitations with the researchers’ contact details and the aim of the study were widely distributed via emails and social media to university students. Eventually 20 undergraduate students from 9 different disciplines in 6 different universities and tertiary institutions in Hong Kong participated in the interviews. 4.2 Data collection Interviews were conducted via Zoom in October and November 2023. To ensure consistency, a trained research assistant was appointed in conducting all interviews. Stu­ dents provided their gender, course of study, study year, institution, and confirmed their experience in using Chat­ GPT in their learning. Individual interviews, conducted in Chinese, lasted on average 60 min and were guided by a semi-structured interview guide developed by the research team (Appendix A) based on an extensive literature review. The guide included general engagement questions, an explanation of the study aims, and open-ended questions to Table 1 Information of interviewees Category Sub-category Percentage (%) Count (n) Gender Male 50 10 Female 50 10 Non-binary 0 0 Field of study Arts 10 2 Business 5 1 Communication 10 2 Creative arts",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,8,"aims, and open-ended questions to Table 1 Information of interviewees Category Sub-category Percentage (%) Count (n) Gender Male 50 10 Female 50 10 Non-binary 0 0 Field of study Arts 10 2 Business 5 1 Communication 10 2 Creative arts 5 1 Education 5 1 Health care 20 4 Health science 5 1 Science 20 4 Social sciences 20 4 Year of study Junior year 1–2 25 5 Senior year 3 or above 75 15 Usage of ChatGPT for academic purposes Habitual 70 14 Occasional 25 5 Never 5 1 Versions of ChatGPT in use Paid version (limited usage) 5 1 University-controlled version with quota limit 5 1 Free (downloaded or through POE) 30 6 An unspecified version that allowed unlimited usage 30 6 Others (the interviewee did not specify) 30 (5% non-user) 6 (among which 1 is a non-user) 1 3 11 Page 8 of 20",147
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,9,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 and dependability, the research team held regular meetings to reach consensus on theme development and definitions. Representative quotes were selected to guide the coding processes, and themes were refined iteratively as new inter­ pretations emerged. Documentation of the progression from raw data to coded data was systematically maintained. Each researcher independently coded one to two subsets of the transcripts, with two coders assigned to each theme. For example, data concerning students’ use patterns of Chat­ GPT included in the first theme (see Table 2) were coded by the first and second authors; data related to students’ trust in ChatGPT (see Table 2) were coded by the second and fourth authors; data indicating students’ perception of Chat­ GPT as agentic were coded by the first and third authors. To ensure inter-coder reliability, coded results were shared among the responsible authors for checking, with refine­ ments tracked on individual coding notes. All final results were collated by the first author, and reviewed by the whole team to reach consensus. Discussions were held to resolve discrepancies, ensuring consistency",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,9,"among the responsible authors for checking, with refine­ ments tracked on individual coding notes. All final results were collated by the first author, and reviewed by the whole team to reach consensus. Discussions were held to resolve discrepancies, ensuring consistency in coding and minimiz­ ing potential bias in data interpretation. Confirmability was supported through the use of reflexive notes, recorded in utilizations of ChatGPT for academic purposes. In the first phase of familiarization, all authors read and re-read the interview transcripts to identify prominent patterns. In the second phase, initial codes related to students’ perceptions of ChatGPT were systematically generated. Given that our codes involved fluid or technical concepts such as “trust” and “AI agency”, we turned to philosophical frameworks for refined conceptualization and precise instantiation, as discussed in our literature review. In the third phase, codes are collated into potential themes that reflected the prom­ inent patterns in the data. The fourth and the fifth phases involved discussion sessions among all authors to review the themes and reach a consensus on defining and nam­ ing the extracted themes and",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,9,"potential themes that reflected the prom­ inent patterns in the data. The fourth and the fifth phases involved discussion sessions among all authors to review the themes and reach a consensus on defining and nam­ ing the extracted themes and sub-themes respectively. In the final phase of producing the report, the selected themes were woven into a comprehensive narrative, incorporating illustrative quotes from representative interviewees. The quotes are translated into English by the first and second authors. The interviewees were assigned number and their identities remain anonymous. Throughout the process, measures were taken to ensure credibility, dependability and confirmability. For credibility Table 2 Example of theme, definition, codes and representative quotes Theme Definition Codes Representative quotes Students’ tendency to misuse Chat­ GPT for academic purposes This theme captures students’ adoption journey and actual cases of using ChatGPT for different academic purposes, including help in language, thinking, problem-solving, personalized learning, and cheating. It reflects the two distinct utilizations and interpretations of “perceived usefulness”: enhancement and disburdenment. Proof-reading “ChatGPT can correct the grammatical mistakes in the whole work for me.” (3) Brainstorming",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,9,"purposes, including help in language, thinking, problem-solving, personalized learning, and cheating. It reflects the two distinct utilizations and interpretations of “perceived usefulness”: enhancement and disburdenment. Proof-reading “ChatGPT can correct the grammatical mistakes in the whole work for me.” (3) Brainstorming “I use ChatGPT when, for example we have to come up with a proposal for an event but we cannot think of anything. Then I might use it for brainstorming.” (9) Providing ideas “I habitually turn to ChatGPT to see what ideas it can think of for a topic when doing an assignment.” (7) Searching information “When there was something that I didn’t understand in an assignment, ChatGPT could really provide me with concrete information”. (4) Completing assignment “I just threw the question to ChatGPT and asked it to help me write an essay of 2000 words.” (13) Cheating “I polished ChatGPT’s writing hard to ensure that it won’t be detected as AI writing.” (6) Enhancement “I used ChatGPT for translation and I asked it for explanations of the translation.” (15) Disburdenment “I just let ChatGPT do all the assignments",180
