A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 (as we have stipulated in Sect. 3) rather than enhancement. What’s more, ChatGPT made the answers of assignments easier to obtain for Interviewee 2, whose adoption journey is illustrative of how adoption for the sake of disburdenment slips into misuse as well: “I first adopted ChatGPT simply because I had to ful­ fill a requirement by taking a course whose content was so unfamiliar to me that I did not even know how to google for relevant reference. So I tried the assign­ ment out on ChatGPT. I found it useful. Eventually I used it to complete all assignments of the course.” (2). Results also show that the interviewees habitually turn to ChatGPT for language assistance such as proof-reading and language polishing. They said “ChatGPT can cor­ rect the grammatical mistakes in the whole work for me” (3), “I asked ChatGPT to proof-read the essay” (6), or “I asked ChatGPT can you help me rewrite in a professional way” (18). The language assistance also includes getting ChatGPT “to generate a script for my presentation” (8)",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"for me” (3), “I asked ChatGPT to proof-read the essay” (6), or “I asked ChatGPT can you help me rewrite in a professional way” (18). The language assistance also includes getting ChatGPT “to generate a script for my presentation” (8) by giving ChatGPT 1-paged information and some points under time pressure. These instances did not clearly demon­ strate the students’ engagement in the process of language learning. In addition to help in addition to language assistance, students also turn to ChatGPT for assistance in organizing ideas, synthesizing information, and providing answers for their assessments. Phrases such as “giving me ideas and directions” (19), “telling me the answers” (13), “to help me think” (14), “to help me summarize” (10) and “to help me organize” (7) frequently appear in interviewees’ reports. Rather than engaging in lecture notes, topic-related read­ ings and conducting their own literature searches, they seek quick and easy answers from ChatGPT. Interviewee 17 recounted “I asked ChatGPT “what is probability” and “can you explain [with] a simple example” because “some­ times the explanations on the lecture notes are relatively",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"their own literature searches, they seek quick and easy answers from ChatGPT. Interviewee 17 recounted “I asked ChatGPT “what is probability” and “can you explain [with] a simple example” because “some­ times the explanations on the lecture notes are relatively complicated.” In other words, turning to ChatGPT for a simpler explanation liberated 17 from the burden of mak­ ing cognitive effort in comprehending the more complicated example on lecture notes. They also seek disburdenment by outsourcing the pro­ cesses of understanding questions, concepts and their interrelations, as well as synthesizing ideas, and structur­ ing arguments to ChatGPT. For example, interviewee 5 noted that getting ChatGPT to help synthesize information “is different from slowly searching for useful information on Google, which is like digging needles from the sea.” Interviewee 13 said, “as I don’t understand the question [assigned in a class discussion], I just pass the question to ChatGPT and get it do it for me.” shared documents to capture analytic decisions made dur­ ing moments of uncertainty. These notes were discussed in debriefing meetings following initial independent coding. 4.4 Ethical",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"discussion], I just pass the question to ChatGPT and get it do it for me.” shared documents to capture analytic decisions made dur­ ing moments of uncertainty. These notes were discussed in debriefing meetings following initial independent coding. 4.4 Ethical considerations This study was reviewed and approved by the university’s Research Ethics Committee and was carried out accord­ ing to the Declaration of Helsinki. Informed consent was obtained at the beginning of the interview. Participants were informed that participation was optional and that they were free to withdraw from the interview at any time and were encouraged to ask questions. The data were anonymized and kept confidential. 5 Results 5.1 Theme 1 Students’ misusing ChatGPT Our study found that five interviewees indicated they do not use ChatGPT to write or complete their assignments. They are not hindered by any technical barriers from access­ ing ChatGPT. In fact, none of the 20 interviewees reported encountering any technical obstacles. All of them expressed that they could access ChatGPT without constraints if they wanted to. Instead, the reasons for not using included con­",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"barriers from access­ ing ChatGPT. In fact, none of the 20 interviewees reported encountering any technical obstacles. All of them expressed that they could access ChatGPT without constraints if they wanted to. Instead, the reasons for not using included con­ cerns about questionable output, worries about becoming dependent on it, and a preference for valuing the process of thinking. Despite having similar concerns to these five interviewees, the other interviewees adopt ChatGPT not for enhancement but for disburdenment. Cases of misuses were identified from 15 out of the 20 interviewees, which involved the five different versions of ChatGPT listed in Table 1. There is no sign indicating that their pattern of misuse is linked to a particular version of ChatGPT they adopted. In any event, the phenomena of misuse validate the educators’ concerns and apprehensions in technology- education tension to a significant extent. The misusers explained their adoption of ChatGPT based on its perceived usefulness. In this respect, our data aligns the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). As an example, Interviewee 2 remarked that “Homework is […] nothing more than a",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,10,"a significant extent. The misusers explained their adoption of ChatGPT based on its perceived usefulness. In this respect, our data aligns the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). As an example, Interviewee 2 remarked that “Homework is […] nothing more than a task you must complete in a short time. After that you don’t care about that knowledge anymore. In such case, ChatGPT is really helpful.” The remark indicates prioritiz­ ing task completion over engagement, a strong indicator of misuse. Note also that the perceived usefulness primarily refers to the time-saving feature of ChatGPT, which makes possible the instantaneous delivery of the commodities, i.e. answers to the homework for interviewee 2. It is a case showing that the perceived usefulness lies in disburdenment 1 3 11 Page 10 of 20",128
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 the information I input before, it was expected to be able to answer questions related to that persona.” (8). The case validates one of the major concerns voiced in Huang et al. [33]. The interviewee explained that it was not easy to recruit the targeted interviewees for the research. Conducting interviewee with ChatGPT alleviates the bur­ dens associated with presumably time- and effort-consum­ ing interviews required for authentic data collection in the coursework. This disburdenment from academic responsi­ bilities severely undermines the educational purpose of the coursework. 5.2 Theme 2 Misusers’ trust in ChatGPT To identify cases where students show automation bias of varying degrees, including cognitive over-trust or affec­ tive over-trust, we examined their statements of which they described the technical features or competencies of Chat­ GPT and their perceptions of its reliability. We found that none of the non-misusers (Interviewees 1, 9, 11, 15 and 20) showed automation bias (cognitive or affective over-trust). Meanwhile, most students, including the misusers, were well-aware of the issue of AI hallucination and showed limited trust ChatGPT’s answers.",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"found that none of the non-misusers (Interviewees 1, 9, 11, 15 and 20) showed automation bias (cognitive or affective over-trust). Meanwhile, most students, including the misusers, were well-aware of the issue of AI hallucination and showed limited trust ChatGPT’s answers. Statements like “Chat­ GPT makes things up” (15), “ChatGPT make mistakes” (3) and “I don’t trust it a lot” (8) frequently appeared in our data, which involve participants who adopt the paid ver­ sion, free version and the two unspecified versions of Chat­ GPT respectively. They are aware of the need to fact-check ChatGPT’s answers, cautioning that “You have to fact-check the references ChatGPT provide” (7). These quotes show that their evaluation of ChatGPT’s competency is a ratio­ nal, accurate one—regardless of which version they adopt. Nevertheless, 4 out of the misusers (Interviewees 4, 12, 13, 17) showed different degrees or kinds of automation bias. They use ChatGPT through university-controlled environ­ ment with usage quota (4), the free downloadable ChatGPT (12, 13) and an unspecified version that allows unlimited usage (17) respectively. As will be shown below, there is no",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"or kinds of automation bias. They use ChatGPT through university-controlled environ­ ment with usage quota (4), the free downloadable ChatGPT (12, 13) and an unspecified version that allows unlimited usage (17) respectively. As will be shown below, there is no clear indication that participants’ over-trust in ChatGPT varied depending on the version they used. Among the four misusers, interviewee 13 showed the strongest automation bias, whose trust in a free download­ able ChatGPT primarily based on the belief that “ChatGPT is an AI” and the perceived responsiveness of ChatGPT. 13 said, “I trust ChatGPT. Because it can provide the answer immediately”. 13’s claims suggest a case of both cogni­ tive and affective over-trust. The latter quote is about a perceived positive feature of AI that forms affective trust [25], a factor other than rational evaluations. This is coupled with Interviewee 13’s tendency to interpret information in These behaviors are primarily driven by the desire for instantaneous commodities or services that are easy and safe to obtain, in order to save time or cognitive effort. At the same time, they did",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"13’s tendency to interpret information in These behaviors are primarily driven by the desire for instantaneous commodities or services that are easy and safe to obtain, in order to save time or cognitive effort. At the same time, they did not indicate how this disburdenment is integrated with other forms of enhancement. A notable quote is from Interviewee 13, who added that with the AI, “I became lazy and am not willing to think anymore”. Interviewee 16, who admitted having the problem of over­ reliance on ChatGPT, likewise explained that “sometimes I didn’t even start to think, and I just asked ChatGPT what to do”. As for time-saving, Interviewee 3 said that with ChatGPT, “I don’t have to select the answers […] It is time- saving and more convenient”. Interviewee 4 said that “for courses that I don’t have any background knowledge, then I need to research and think of a decent answer, which takes a lot of time. But if I outsource this to AI, it can produce a good answer in just 30 seconds.” Interviewee 17 likewise said",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"background knowledge, then I need to research and think of a decent answer, which takes a lot of time. But if I outsource this to AI, it can produce a good answer in just 30 seconds.” Interviewee 17 likewise said further that in asking ChatGPT for simple example, “I don’t have to, or I could save time to check the lecture notes for [the relatively more complicated] answers.” The most evident misuse of ChatGPT include cases in which students “simply copy” ChatGPT’s answers (12), sometimes without verification “if the answers do not look too wrong” (2) and got ChatGPT to “complete (all) the assignment(s) for me” (2, 6, 13). For some, it is particu­ larly true for “non-major courses, like those common core courses” or “courses that I am not interested in” (6) or “courses that I do not care too much for the grades” (2). In these cases, adopting ChatGPT enabled them to evade the burden of doing unwanted or unchosen assignments. Oth­ ers did so to conceal their incompetency, which freed them from the burden of seeking self-improvement",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,11,"too much for the grades” (2). In these cases, adopting ChatGPT enabled them to evade the burden of doing unwanted or unchosen assignments. Oth­ ers did so to conceal their incompetency, which freed them from the burden of seeking self-improvement and overcom­ ing barriers. For example, interviewee 7 admitted that “my English is not good. I can include ChatGPT’s sentences in my paper as elaboration […] that makes my paper longer”. It is more or less the same with Interviewee 19, who did not manage to write to meet the word requirement and eventu­ ally got “ChatGPT to help me think of other directions to complete the essay”. Last but not least, there is a peculiar case of misuse involving the exploitation of ChatGPT’s adaptive character­ istics to fabricate data: “I just input information about that particular group, the background and background of the characters, for example, age, income, profession, habits, where they live. After inputting the information, I told ChatGPT the characteristics of the role I wanted it to play and that I was going to conduct an interview",180
