A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 Table 1 respectively. There is no crucial indicator showing that the perception of AI agency varies with specific ver­ sions of ChatGPT in use. Among the 12 students, only 1 (Interviewee 9) is a non-misuser, and none of the other non- misusers demonstrated a strong perception of agency. In contrast, all misusers exhibited perceptions of AI as highly agentic, with the exception of Interviewee 2. For many, ChatGPT is characterized by high levels of goal-directedness, autonomy and intentionality. The goal- directedness of ChatGPT emerged as a recurring theme in the interviews, with students noting its ability to effectively produce, organize, and synthesize ideas and complete vari­ ous tasks that require elaborated means. They view ChatGPT as an entity with “its own ideas” (12), is capable of “sorting points from Big Data” and “help me select the answers in one go” (3), “integrate a wide range of opinions” (8), or “to check if there’s any inconsistencies in my paper” (6). Additionally, ChatGPT is often recognized for its auton­ omy and adaptability, with students noting its capacity",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"the answers in one go” (3), “integrate a wide range of opinions” (8), or “to check if there’s any inconsistencies in my paper” (6). Additionally, ChatGPT is often recognized for its auton­ omy and adaptability, with students noting its capacity to function independently and generate outputs from specific inputs. For example, 12 described that “[Then] the ideas that ChatGPT has obtained on-line may appear, or there comes ChatGPT’s own ideas”, while 16 said that “language-wise it can encapsulate the ideas I have in mind.” Finally, students often describe ChatGPT as if it pos­ sesses intentionality, using phrases like ‘thinking’, ‘trying its best’, ‘deciding’ or ‘debating’. Their perceptions of AI agency are reflected in their anthropomorphist expressions regarding ChatGPT. Interviewee 13, who displayed stron­ gest automation bias, spoke as if ChatGPT embodied all the three dimensions. Interviewee 13 stated that It summaries things for me, it decides for me, it can tell me which question is simpler. It told me that because the Little Red Riding Hood is a well-known fairy tale, it is high in education-value. Seven more others students",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"summaries things for me, it decides for me, it can tell me which question is simpler. It told me that because the Little Red Riding Hood is a well-known fairy tale, it is high in education-value. Seven more others students spoke as if ChatGPT possessed all the three dimensions. Below are two representative quotes: “At the beginning even if it did not know the answers, it would still do its best to talk nonsense, offering you something. Recently I have the feeling that that it messes up less things. Or if it does not know, it shuts up faster.” (5). “Sometimes I don’t even know what I am asking, but I can feel that the AI is trying its best to answer me […] it sees what you need, meaning that it understands what you mean, then it can express what I want to say in a pinpoint, condensed way.” (12). a way that reaffirms its trustworthiness. 13 recounted that “Once I realize that what ChatGPT told me was incorrect. So, I thought I mistakenly trusted it. But the assignment",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"what I want to say in a pinpoint, condensed way.” (12). a way that reaffirms its trustworthiness. 13 recounted that “Once I realize that what ChatGPT told me was incorrect. So, I thought I mistakenly trusted it. But the assignment with ChatGPT’s incorrect answer ended up getting an A-. So, I think that ChatGPT was right after all.” There is, however, a borderline case of cognitive over- trust that also involves the adoption of a free downloadable version of ChatGPT. Interviewee 12 asserted “ChatGPT is far more trustworthy than Wikipedia, because [unlike the content on Wikipedia], ChatGPT’s messages could not have been written by anybody.” This statement raises questions about the evaluation of ChatGPT’s reliability in compari­ son to Wikipedia, suggesting a lack of awareness that Chat­ GPT’s training data includes contributions from Wikipedia, which is open to input from anyone. (In saying so, we do not mean that it explains the problem of AI hallucination.) Another case of cognitive over-trust involves Inter­ viewee 4, who uses the version provided by the university that comes with limited quotas. Interviewee 4",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"anyone. (In saying so, we do not mean that it explains the problem of AI hallucination.) Another case of cognitive over-trust involves Inter­ viewee 4, who uses the version provided by the university that comes with limited quotas. Interviewee 4 had no doubts about the accuracy of ChatGPT’s answers, repeatedly asserting that “its answers are very precise and accurate”. It suggests a case of cognitive over-trust in ChatGPT’s com­ petency or technical feature. If trust in AI can also be built based on the reputation of the organizations associated with it [25], then Interviewee 4’s trust in ChatGPT might also be linked to the fact that 4 accesses it through the university 4 is affiliated. However, no explicit clues from the data can be identified in support of this speculation. Lastly, in asserting that that “ChatGPT gave me fake references” and that “I ask ChatGPT first, regardless its answers are correct or not”, Interviewee 17’s over-trust in an unspecified version that allows unlimited usage of Chat­ GPT is affective rather than cognitive. The willingness to place a bet on ChatGPT",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,"and that “I ask ChatGPT first, regardless its answers are correct or not”, Interviewee 17’s over-trust in an unspecified version that allows unlimited usage of Chat­ GPT is affective rather than cognitive. The willingness to place a bet on ChatGPT despite its known incompetency indicates that it is likely influenced by factors other than the rational evaluations that characterize cognitive trust. The case approximates the faith dimension of trust [41]. 5.3 Theme 3: Misusers’ perception of ChatGPT as highly agentic Based on Dung’s multidimensional account of artificial agency, when students perceive ChatGPT as possessing two dimensions of agency— high levels of goal-directedness, autonomy, or intentionality—they develop a strong percep­ tion of its agency. The results show that 12 students (Inter­ viewees 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16, and 17) have a strong perception of agency in ChatGPT. The cases involved all the 5 versions of ChatGPT listed in Table 1. The group who did not show perception of ChatGPT as highly agentic include students who reported adopting the free download­ able version and the two",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,12,in ChatGPT. The cases involved all the 5 versions of ChatGPT listed in Table 1. The group who did not show perception of ChatGPT as highly agentic include students who reported adopting the free download­ able version and the two unspecified versions indicated in 1 3 11 Page 12 of 20,51
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 disburdenment. As indicated above, most students adopted ChatGPT for disburdenment initially. Paradoxically, the continued reliance imposed the new burdens, such as fact-checking ChatGPT’s answers with multiple sources, discerning the relevant answers from the irrelevant ones, deciphering ChatGPT’s incomprehensible answers, and mulling over ChatGPT’s mistakes etc. To elaborate in the words of TAM: despite that the hand-on experience with ChatGPT reduces their perceived ease of use and/or useful­ ness over time, it does not discourage them from relying on it. Cases of paradoxical continued reliance was identified in 11 out of the 15 misusers (2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 12, 13, 14, 16, 17 and 19). The misuser Interviewee 4 did not exhibit the paradoxi­ cal adoption. It can be explained by the interviewee’s cogni­ tive trust in ChatGPT and consequently interviewee 4 did not see the need of fact-checking. Here are some illustrative quotes of paradoxical continued reliance: “I google [ChatGPT’s answers] before using them […] then I check lecture notes and see if the lecture notes contain ChatGPT’s answers. But the real conve­ nience",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"the need of fact-checking. Here are some illustrative quotes of paradoxical continued reliance: “I google [ChatGPT’s answers] before using them […] then I check lecture notes and see if the lecture notes contain ChatGPT’s answers. But the real conve­ nience lies in the fact that I don’t have to [fact-]check with lecture notes for ChatGPT’s answers. But even­ tually when using ChatGPT, I check lecture notes for confirmation.” (3). “Yes, it [using ChatGPT] is indeed more time-con­ suming. It is because sometimes you have to check ChatGPT’s answers on Google. Then it could happen that you cannot find them on Google […]. Then you have to check the answers with more platforms, like the library system, or several other sources to con­ firm if what ChatGPT told you is true or not. So, after all, confirming ChatGPT’s answer takes longer than searching for the answer myself.” (19). As for interviewee 13, who showed the strongest automa­ tion bias and expressed a reluctance to think as result of relying on ChatGPT, said that “Sometimes I don’t under­ stand ChatGPT’s answers. But",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"than searching for the answer myself.” (19). As for interviewee 13, who showed the strongest automa­ tion bias and expressed a reluctance to think as result of relying on ChatGPT, said that “Sometimes I don’t under­ stand ChatGPT’s answers. But I keep asking […] and that made me think.” The cases are reminiscent of the revenge effect of tech­ nology, which is described as “whenever we try to take advantage of some new technology, we may discover that it induces behavior which appears to cancel out the very reasons for using it” [69]. One of the revenge effects of tech­ nology is dubbed “a repeating effect,” which occurs when some devices and systems that are supposed to free time for other things result in users doing the same thing more often rather than giving free time to do other things [69]. As shown in the above cases, turning to ChatGPT for information or answers appears to result in students spending more time Expressions such as “ChatGPT tried its best” or “it made its utmost effort” suggest that interviewees may perceive",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"[69]. As shown in the above cases, turning to ChatGPT for information or answers appears to result in students spending more time Expressions such as “ChatGPT tried its best” or “it made its utmost effort” suggest that interviewees may perceive the AI as having goodwill, which is at the core of the affec­ tive account of trust. Other examples of anthropomorphistic expressions include “ChatGPT often rejects its own answer or changes its stance” and “Sometimes I debate with Chat­ GPT” (17) and “I get ChatGPT to think with me on how to perfect the work.”(14) These expressions suggest that students who use them likely perceive ChatGPT as highly agentic. Conversely, students who do not exhibit the ten­ dency of perceiving ChatGPT as highly agentic, such as Interviewee 11, commonly describe ChatGPT using non- anthropomorphistic expressions like “it generates”; “its output” (rather than “its ideas”) but tend to reserve terms like “thinking” and “understanding” for humans. 6 Discussion The results showing misuses validate the educator’s con­ cerns and apprehension of incorporating generative AI in higher education. The desire for disburdenment was",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"(rather than “its ideas”) but tend to reserve terms like “thinking” and “understanding” for humans. 6 Discussion The results showing misuses validate the educator’s con­ cerns and apprehension of incorporating generative AI in higher education. The desire for disburdenment was prev­ alent among the participants, which likely reflected the systematic academic pressures of obtaining higher grades and the academic qualification considered necessary in this highly competitive society. Our results are also largely consistent with the literature on how over-trust in AI may prompt inappropriate use and how perceptions of AI as agen­ tic interact with trust in AI. Despite the fact that 75% of our participants are from senior year and only 25% of them are from junior year, which may bias our results towards users who have more academic experience, there is no clear sign indicating that the participants’ level of technology accept­ ability and trust pattern vary with their academic levels. The only student (interviewee 1) who refused to adopt ChatGPT in learning due to the problem of AI hallucination is a senior year student. Regardless of their",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"level of technology accept­ ability and trust pattern vary with their academic levels. The only student (interviewee 1) who refused to adopt ChatGPT in learning due to the problem of AI hallucination is a senior year student. Regardless of their academic levels, interview­ ees generally explained that they adopt ChatGPT primarily due to its perceived usefulness, alongside their curiosity or their university’s open policy regarding the use of genera­ tive AI. Next, our data showed that only 4 (out of the 15) misusers exhibited different levels of cognitive and/or affec­ tive over-trust. Among them, 1 (interviewee 4) is junior year student and 3 (interviewees 12, 13, 17) are senior year students. The distribution is 25% and 75%, which reflected the exact distribution of the participants. Meanwhile, most of the other misusers could identify the mistakes ChatGPT made and its limitations. The data simultaneously reveal a subtler yet paradoxi­ cal pattern among those students in their adoption behav­ ior: most of them continue to rely on ChatGPT, even if to varying degrees this defeats their original purpose of 1 3 Page",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,13,"The data simultaneously reveal a subtler yet paradoxi­ cal pattern among those students in their adoption behav­ ior: most of them continue to rely on ChatGPT, even if to varying degrees this defeats their original purpose of 1 3 Page 13 of 20 11",44
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 17 explained further that “It gives me a sense of security in doing so, or I know that if I have a question, I can ask.” 6.3 Positive values in ChatGPT's mistakes More intriguingly, Interviewee 17 added that “even when it gave me wrong answers […] I can follow its wrong answers, adjust and see if any new things might come out.” It appears to us that 17 justified continued reliance by placing posi­ tive values on ChatGPT’s mistakes. This is also the case with a few other interviewees. Interviewee 16 said that rec­ ognizing ChatGPT’s mistakes “deepened my interest in the topic.” Interviewee 2 said that “the process [of fact-check­ ing ChatGPT’s answers] can give you stronger initiative to search and to fact-check ChatGPT’s answers.” Interviewee 2 added that doing so is “a motivator for me to read and learn more.” Interviewee 5 likewise explained that “I think that no matter what sources you turn to, there is room for mistake […] even if ChatGPT makes mistakes, and I dis­ cover its mistakes, then",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"for me to read and learn more.” Interviewee 5 likewise explained that “I think that no matter what sources you turn to, there is room for mistake […] even if ChatGPT makes mistakes, and I dis­ cover its mistakes, then it is also a way to help me learn.” Our speculation is that the interviewees adopted the cop­ ing mechanisms of post-factum rationalization as a reac­ tion to cognitive dissonance. The phenomenon of cognitive dissonance in technology adoption has only recently been included as a technology adoption factor [43, 47, 58]. Cog­ nitive dissonance in technology adoption occurs when, dur­ ing the adoption journey, the performance of the technology falls short of meeting the initial expectations, leading to negative disconfirmation and consequently dissatisfaction or frustration on the part of the users [21, 47] The users then experience cognitive dissonance, a psychological discom­ fort characterized by conflicting beliefs, attitudes and/or actions related to the adoption of the technology. When cog­ nitive dissonance occurs, the users change their beliefs and/ or behavior to alleviate cognitive dissonance through cop­ ing mechanism [23, 29].",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"psychological discom­ fort characterized by conflicting beliefs, attitudes and/or actions related to the adoption of the technology. When cog­ nitive dissonance occurs, the users change their beliefs and/ or behavior to alleviate cognitive dissonance through cop­ ing mechanism [23, 29]. There are two well-documented coping mechanisms. First, users discontinue use, resulting in overall dissatisfaction with the technology. Second, users may enter “an affective state that reduces the perceived dis­ crepancy between expectation and performance, thus poten­ tially lead to satisfaction” [47]. The second mechanism further involves users’ change of attitude, consonant infor­ mation-seeking and behavioral change. Change of attitude can take the form of post-factum justification of the technol­ ogy adoption or rationalization of the technology’s perfor­ mance, aiming to maintain the integrity of their beliefs and outcomes [47]. The consonant information-seeking mode operates when individual selectively search for reaffirm­ ing information about the belief through different channels, while behavioral adjustment involves “the withdrawal of the behavior causing the dissonance” [29, 47]. and effort in fact-checking and/or thinking. An interesting question arises is, why continued reliance? A further look into",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"ing information about the belief through different channels, while behavioral adjustment involves “the withdrawal of the behavior causing the dissonance” [29, 47]. and effort in fact-checking and/or thinking. An interesting question arises is, why continued reliance? A further look into the data showed that there are a few explanations for the apparently paradoxical approach to continued reliance. 6.1 A modified cost-benefit analysis Some students explained their continued reliance on with ChatGPT through a modified cost-benefit analysis. After weighing the perceived costs and burdens against the ben­ efits, they still found using ChatGPT to be cost-effective. Their continued reliance on ChatGPT can be explained by their attempted rational evaluation of the pros and cons of utilizing ChatGPT. Alternatively, they engage with Chat­ GPT more selectively, using it only for tasks where it excels to avoid complications and additional burdens. For example, Interviewee 14 eventually uses ChatGPT more for creative projects than for academic papers. To Interviewee 14, engaging ChatGPT for creative tasks is helpful, even if ChatGPT’s ideas cannot be directly adopted. The process resembles having a “conversation” or brainstorming with",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"Interviewee 14 eventually uses ChatGPT more for creative projects than for academic papers. To Interviewee 14, engaging ChatGPT for creative tasks is helpful, even if ChatGPT’s ideas cannot be directly adopted. The process resembles having a “conversation” or brainstorming with someone, which can stimulate thoughts and facilitate the generation of creative ideas. This aligns with our categori­ zation of proper use (the second category). Similar adjust­ ments in the subsequent adoption behavior, resulting from the recognition of ChatGPT’s limitations, are also observed in Interviewee 19, who has reduced their use and employs ChatGPT more selectively. We refer to these cases of “quali­ fied cognitive/rational trust” in ChatGPT, in which an actor is willing to—despite its perceived limitations—place a bet on the AI to achieve a goal (old or new) for the actor, and the willingness is based on a modified evaluation of the AI’s competency, typically alongside a re-evaluations of the costs and benefits. 6.2 Psychological values In contrast, some students offered “non-rationalist” expla­ nations—psychologically there are practical values of con­ tinued reliance on AI rather than seeking help from",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,14,"evaluation of the AI’s competency, typically alongside a re-evaluations of the costs and benefits. 6.2 Psychological values In contrast, some students offered “non-rationalist” expla­ nations—psychologically there are practical values of con­ tinued reliance on AI rather than seeking help from peers or teacher for help. Interviewee 16 said that, with ChatGPT, 16 does not have to overcome the language barrier encountered when communicating with foreign teachers. 16 explained that “Because I am in control. That is, I typed the ques­ tion. ChatGPT can usually provide the answers I want. When asking a teacher, the teacher may not understand me because of my inadequate English proficiency.” This case illustrates the allure of hassle-free interaction with AI in which users prefers the less burdensome interaction with AI than human interaction [75]. And as already noted in Theme 2, Interviewee 17’s continued reliance is for the sake of psy­ chological comfort but not for correct answers. Interviewee 1 3 11 Page 14 of 20",161
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,15,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 guiding it to provide answers for your assignment…in way that the answers are what you wanted.” Interviewee simi­ larly 14 said that “I spend more time to chat with it more” to get ChatGPT “to give finer-grained answers” by asking it more questions “to perfect its answers”, making the content “more aligned with your direction.” These cases approximate Sherry Turkle’s more nuanced interpretation of the Eliza effect, according to which users of the chatbot are willing to “change their modes of interaction so as to “help” the bot produce comprehensible responses” [50]. It suggests that the bot user’s “deep willingness to see machines as intelligence [.] their own desires to breathe life into a machine,” which is believed to have some practical value for the users [50]. For the students in the current study, it may reveal their will to believe in a technological panacea in completing their assignments. To our best knowledge, no studies on ChatGPT have addressed this phenomenon. So, we dub it “the ChatGPT effect” after the manner of Turkle’s interpretation of",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,15,"may reveal their will to believe in a technological panacea in completing their assignments. To our best knowledge, no studies on ChatGPT have addressed this phenomenon. So, we dub it “the ChatGPT effect” after the manner of Turkle’s interpretation of the Eliza effect (Table 3). 7 Implications of the study The contribution of this study to the existing research are multifaceted. Theoretically, this study proposed a categori­ zation scheme that delineates the proper and improper use of ChatGPT guided by active learning principles and by means of the philosophical concept of “disburdenment”. It Our data shows that in cases (except Interviewee 2, whose initial attitude towards ChatGPT is neutral) where paradoxical continued reliance occurs, the students’ ini­ tiation expectations towards ChatGPT are positive. Their positive expectations were captured in their responses to questions one to three in the interview guide (see Appendix A). Alongside the result indicating that they were aware of the problem of AI hallucination, most of the cases fulfill the condition of negative disconfirmation preceding cognitive dissonance. The statements made by Interviewees 5, 16, and 17 above",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,15,"(see Appendix A). Alongside the result indicating that they were aware of the problem of AI hallucination, most of the cases fulfill the condition of negative disconfirmation preceding cognitive dissonance. The statements made by Interviewees 5, 16, and 17 above might represent a post-factum justification of the technology adoption, a cognitive adjustment that serves as a coping mechanism: by placing positive values on ChatG­ PT’s mistakes, the interviewees justified the continued reli­ ance “by strengthening the positive attitude towards [the] technology and seeking positive information about the tech­ nology” [47]. No cues suggesting attitudinal or behavior adjustments as coping mechanisms can be found in Inter­ viewee 3 and 6. Lastly, while examining the students’ paradoxical con­ tinued reliance, we also observed a notable subsequent behavioral adjustment. Three interviewees (12, 14 and 16) willingly took up the new burden of putting more effort to provide detailed prompts to “guide” or “help” ChatGPT produce better answers. These interviewees expressed as if they already had some answers they wanted, they still sought ChatGPT’s responses. For example, Interviewee 12 said that “You got to",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,15,"more effort to provide detailed prompts to “guide” or “help” ChatGPT produce better answers. These interviewees expressed as if they already had some answers they wanted, they still sought ChatGPT’s responses. For example, Interviewee 12 said that “You got to know how to ask ChatGPT questions, No. H1: Misuser? (15/20) H2: Automation bias? (4/20) H3: Perceived AI as highly agentic? (12/20) Paradoxical continued reliance? (11/20) Non-rationalist explanations (E) or adjustment (A) for the continued reli­ ance? (6/11) 1 No No No No 2 Yes No No Yes + E 3 Yes No Yes Yes 4 Yes Yes, cognitive Yes No 5 Yes No Yes Yes + E 6 Yes No Yes Yes 7 Yes No Partial Yes 8 Yes No Yes No 9 No No Yes No 10 Yes No Yes No 11 No No No No 12 Yes Yes (borderline), cognitive Yes Yes + A (the ChatGPT effect) 13 Yes Yes, cognitive and affective Yes Yes 14 Yes No Yes Yes + A (the ChatGPT effect) 15 No No No No 16 Yes No Yes Yes + E and",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,15,"(borderline), cognitive Yes Yes + A (the ChatGPT effect) 13 Yes Yes, cognitive and affective Yes Yes 14 Yes No Yes Yes + A (the ChatGPT effect) 15 No No No No 16 Yes No Yes Yes + E and A (the ChatGPT effect) 17 Yes Yes, affective Yes Yes + E and A 18 Yes No No No 19 Yes No Partial Yes 20 No No No No Table 3 Is a summary of how misuse, automation bias, per­ ceived AI agency and Paradoxical continued reliance interact 1 3 Page 15 of 20 11",95
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 features of AI tools or interacted with automation bias, cog­ nitive and affective over-trust, and the perceptions of AI as agentic. Pertinent educational institutions can address this by designing courses that enhance students’ AI literacy. This can be done by raising students’ awareness of their adoption behaviors and the possible factors shaping them. For example, students should be informed about the clear distinction between adopting AI for disburdenment (in the negative sense) and for enhancement. Training in meta­ cognition skills to reflect on their decision-making process when entrusting an LLM a specific academic task and the resulting adoption behaviors is also crucial. What’s more, students need to be sensitive to the features of the LLM that may invite questionable attribution of agentic properties or unwarranted trust in the AI chatbot. Students need to be informed about the dynamics of human-AI chatbot interac­ tion and communication, and more broadly, the nature of technology-human relationship. These factors can shape their psychology and potentially undermine their human agency when collaborating with AI. 8 Conclusion, limitations and suggested future",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"about the dynamics of human-AI chatbot interac­ tion and communication, and more broadly, the nature of technology-human relationship. These factors can shape their psychology and potentially undermine their human agency when collaborating with AI. 8 Conclusion, limitations and suggested future research This study examines how the university students’ percep­ tions of ChatGPT interact with their adoption behavior, continued reliance and subsequent attitudinal and behav­ ioral adjustment when engaging with ChatGPT in academic tasks. Our findings revealed how the perceived disburdening feature of ChatGPT prompts misuse. They highlighted that automation bias and the perception of AI agency are more prevalent among misusers, some of whom also exhibited the paradoxical continued reliance, compared to non-misusers. Of the two factors, the interaction between misuse and per­ ception of AI agency is more significant than the interaction between misuse and automation bias. This is also evident in numerous cases of paradoxical continued reliance, which are simultaneously associated with non-rationalist explana­ tions for the continued reliance or behavioral adjustment such as the ChatGPT effect. Our data showed that in five out of the six cases",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"also evident in numerous cases of paradoxical continued reliance, which are simultaneously associated with non-rationalist explana­ tions for the continued reliance or behavioral adjustment such as the ChatGPT effect. Our data showed that in five out of the six cases (5, 12, 14, 16, and 17), where the non- rationalist explanations or subsequent behavioral adjust­ ments for continued reliance occurred, the interviewees spoke as if ChatGPT is highly agentic. This suggests that perceiving ChatGPT as agentic positively shapes a kind of affective (over) trust in, or liking of, ChatGPT. This finding is consistent with the existing literature in human-AI inter­ action. The current study contributes to this line of research by revealing the interaction among the factors being exam­ ined unfolds in the context of using it for learning in higher education. contributes to unify the numerous proposals and ideas for the effective use and inappropriate use of AI in the context of higher education. Notably, there is a significant lack of guidelines on “proper” or “good” use of AI in study, par­ ticularly in the university study. Suggestions for",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"for the effective use and inappropriate use of AI in the context of higher education. Notably, there is a significant lack of guidelines on “proper” or “good” use of AI in study, par­ ticularly in the university study. Suggestions for effective use of AI in learning are also too generic with a few Do’s and/or Don’t’s, making it challenging for both teachers and students to distinguish between appropriate and inappropri­ ate use cases [12]. Therefore, practically, our categorization scheme can function as a guideline, informing all stakehold­ ers about what amount to proper and improper use of Chat­ GPT in learning. Next, this study incorporated philosophical theories and conceptualizations into the empirical studies of Human-AI interaction. The theoretical contribution is twofold. Firstly, the integration of philosophical accounts of trust into the conceptual framework adopted by existing empirical stud­ ies of trust in AI offers a more nuanced characterizations of “cognitive trust” and “affective trust” in AI and interpreta­ tion of automation bias. This nuanced interpretation allows us to identify paradigm case of over-trust and cases show­ ing different levels or",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"trust in AI offers a more nuanced characterizations of “cognitive trust” and “affective trust” in AI and interpreta­ tion of automation bias. This nuanced interpretation allows us to identify paradigm case of over-trust and cases show­ ing different levels or kinds of over-trust. This serves as a reference for any future empirical studies that see the need to consider different levels or types of over-trust in AI in understanding AI adoption behaviors and overreliance. Sec­ ondly, while the existing characterization of AI agency in terms of the AI’s autonomy and the AI’s subjective language provides an instructive understanding of users’ perception of AI agency, it remains static in that it leaves little room for a differentiation of an AI being perceived as having a thresh­ old or a more full-fledged agency. Additionally, there is a call for “further differentiating AI agency” [54]. This study responded by drawing on a multi-dimensional, philosophi­ cal theorization of “AI agency” that captures the gradations in perceived agency in AI. However, we reformulated cer­ tain dimensions and discerned the relevant dimensions to be adopted for",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,"agency” [54]. This study responded by drawing on a multi-dimensional, philosophi­ cal theorization of “AI agency” that captures the gradations in perceived agency in AI. However, we reformulated cer­ tain dimensions and discerned the relevant dimensions to be adopted for studies that focus on users’ perceptions of AI, and the dimensions that may be required for studies that focus on users’ longer-term engagement with AI. This the­ oretical contribution opens up directions for finer-grained classification of the users’ perceptions of AI agency, which we anticipate will become increasingly significant, espe­ cially in view of the recent launching of the arguably more advanced AI tools including DeepSeek and Deep Research. In addition, our study aims to contribute by addressing the second end of the Technology Education Tension. The findings have the practical implication of offering initial directions on how to educate students—especially those in the same cultural context or similar learning environment— to use generative AI sensibly. Considering students’ spe­ cific adoption behaviors—some of which are undesirable or questionable—can be influenced by the disburdening 1 3 11 Page 16 of 20",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,16,students—especially those in the same cultural context or similar learning environment— to use generative AI sensibly. Considering students’ spe­ cific adoption behaviors—some of which are undesirable or questionable—can be influenced by the disburdening 1 3 11 Page 16 of 20,40
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"AI and Ethics (2026) 6:11 in the university typically do not use the version provided by university due to its quota limitation, and that they have strong preference for using the versions that allows unlim­ ited, constraint-free usage, or are free of charge. Their reports square with the fact that participants consistently described their usage as unrestricted and they can access ChatGPT whenever needed. Alternatively, they use their preferred versions and the university-controlled one simul­ taneously. Therefore, we consider that the specific version of ChatGPT-3 used (e.g. university-controlled or not) is unlikely to have significantly influenced the results related to their utilization or perceptions of the AI. Next, our themes did not address factors that are not technology-related, such as factors included in the extended TAM or those in “Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT2), which encompass contextual fac­ tors, ideology and user’s personal characteristics or motiva­ tion [73]. We also acknowledge that how people use an AI tool is “steered by dominated values of the social context in which the tool emerges” [71], their university’s AI",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"contextual fac­ tors, ideology and user’s personal characteristics or motiva­ tion [73]. We also acknowledge that how people use an AI tool is “steered by dominated values of the social context in which the tool emerges” [71], their university’s AI policy, as well as the users’ technological knowledge of the AI tool, self-confidence, etc [25]. As mentioned above, our research samples are specific. It might be worth-mentioning that a somewhat peculiar contextual-factor prompting the adop­ tion of ChatGPT among our research samples is that most of them are required to use English, their second-language, in learning. It might not be uncommon for the local students to perceive it as a burden in learning. As an interviewee remarked, ChatGPT empowers local students who are aca­ demically disadvantaged primarily due to their poor Eng­ lish proficiency. Regarding the possible adoption of coping mechanisms to cognitive dissonance, which might have led to paradoxical continued reliance on ChatGPT, the students’ initial expectation towards ChatGPT leading to the negative disconfirmation might also be shaped by contingent factors, including the reputation of the institutions associated with",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"mechanisms to cognitive dissonance, which might have led to paradoxical continued reliance on ChatGPT, the students’ initial expectation towards ChatGPT leading to the negative disconfirmation might also be shaped by contingent factors, including the reputation of the institutions associated with the AI [3, 25]. In the current case, it might be the students’ university that embraces ChatGPT. The possible cognitive dissonance might also interact with their pre-existing beliefs about technology in general, such as the optimistic view that technology is supposed to serve human goods, as expressed by some of our interviewees. To encapsulate, this study examined how students mis­ use ChatGPT in learning and the factors associated with the misuses. We also identified cases of paradoxical, con­ tinued, questionable reliance on ChatGPT. Our discussion attempted to offer an understanding for this apparently para­ doxical reliance. Our findings underscore the need for future studies on the seemingly mixed messages about the benefits of adopting AI tools in learning and the necessary cautions regarding their drawbacks. Ensuring that education poli­ cies and curriculum designs foster a balanced environment While our study",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"need for future studies on the seemingly mixed messages about the benefits of adopting AI tools in learning and the necessary cautions regarding their drawbacks. Ensuring that education poli­ cies and curriculum designs foster a balanced environment While our study offers valuable insights into university students’ utilization and perceptions of ChatGPT, this study acknowledges certain limitations. First, the generalizabil­ ity of the findings to other cultures may be limited due to our specific research sample, which consists of Chinese university students studying in Hong Kong. Nevertheless, we believe that our study provides a valuable foundation for future research in the similar contexts and offers a point of comparison for university students in other learning environments. Second, the findings may be subject to social desirability bias as participants might not answer honestly about their use of ChatGPT. In spite that the data was collected after the local public universities have embraced ChatGPT using a regulated approach, its use could still be associated with academic dishonesty. A related limitation due to the data collection period is that our study focused on ChatGPT-3,",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"data was collected after the local public universities have embraced ChatGPT using a regulated approach, its use could still be associated with academic dishonesty. A related limitation due to the data collection period is that our study focused on ChatGPT-3, while the more capable ChatGPT-5 is already launched. Students’ adoption and perceptions may have changed since then. A longitudinal follow-up study on the participants would therefore offer valuable insights, though it is beyond the scope of the current paper. Nevertheless, it is worth not­ ing that several of our interviewees expressed that if Chat­ GPT becomes more capable, they will rely on it more. For example, “If one day ChatGPT becomes very capable, then I might also become lazy and over-reliant on it” (7). Some students did not outsource to ChatGPT assignment that they take seriously, but this may change: “if in future I don’t have to fact check what it says, or I submit a question with details to it and it can generate an B or B + essay.” (6). Assuming other factors remain constant, it is reasonable",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"“if in future I don’t have to fact check what it says, or I submit a question with details to it and it can generate an B or B + essay.” (6). Assuming other factors remain constant, it is reasonable to expect that the problem of misuse and over-reliance will exacerbate. The rapidly advancing competency of ChatGPT may also foster stronger cognitive trust or reinforce the perception of AI as highly agentic in the users, thereby providing the con­ dition of affective trust. Having said so, we recommend that future studies adopt a longitudinal design and focus on more recent versions of ChatGPT to yield more robust findings. Another limitation of our study lies in the presence of a few cases where the specific version of ChatGPT-3 used by participants was not clearly identified. The problem stemmed from instances where interviewees either did not mention the version during the interview or were unable to provide a clear response. While some may question whether differences in versions may influence our findings, our analysis of cases with identifiable versions revealed no sub­",180
A088,A088.pdf,10.1007/s43681-025-00855-w,17,"interviewees either did not mention the version during the interview or were unable to provide a clear response. While some may question whether differences in versions may influence our findings, our analysis of cases with identifiable versions revealed no sub­ stantial variation in the patterns of misuse, trust, and their perception of AI agency, as we have indicated throughout Sect. 5. Meanwhile, we believe that most of the students were likely using the free versions or those without quota or usage limitation. This belief is based on the report from some interviewees who mentioned that they and their peers 1 3 Page 17 of 20 11",106
