A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,1,"RESEARCH Open Access International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w Understanding student perspectives on generative AI chatbots: a human-centred mixed-methods study in higher education Stephen Colbran1, Meena Jha2* and Caitlin Schiavone3 *Correspondence: Meena Jha m.jha@cqu.edu.au 1Central Queensland University, Brisbane, Australia 2Central Queensland University, Sydney, Australia 3Central Queensland University, Melbourne, Australia © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,1,"Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Abstract This study addresses the educational challenges of understanding how generative AI chatbots can equitably and effectively enhance student learning in higher education. Chatbot system analytics data and a mixed methods survey (n = 121) was used to examine four research questions related to the use of chatbots (academic value, frequency of use, and attitudes), technology experience and AI literacy (self-reported digital proficiency, device usage and AI familiarity), barriers to use of chatbots (ethical concerns, UX issues and policy ambiguity), and general user experience (clarity, relevance, accuracy, satisfaction and likelihood of recommendation). Descriptive statistics, one-sample t-tests, and ANOVAs revealed generally positive attitudes toward chatbots and perceived gains in knowledge and understanding, together with robust support for academic-integrity requirements. Usage analytics confirmed 24/7",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,1,"user experience (clarity, relevance, accuracy, satisfaction and likelihood of recommendation). Descriptive statistics, one-sample t-tests, and ANOVAs revealed generally positive attitudes toward chatbots and perceived gains in knowledge and understanding, together with robust support for academic-integrity requirements. Usage analytics confirmed 24/7 usage requirements, with 36.8% of interactions occurring after hours. Users reported clear, relevant, largely accurate responses and an easy interface but overall satisfaction was mixed. The study recommends human-centred design (e.g., opt-in launch, clearer links to official resources), explicit AI policies and labels for assessment, staff and student training (including prompt literacy, and potential use cases), subject-specific knowledge bases, and continuous monitoring for errors and edge cases, and subsequent update of system instructions and knowledge bases. Study findings highlight conditions under which chatbots can equitably complement human instruction and methods for chatbot engagement across different student cohorts. Keywords Generative AI chatbots, Higher education conversational agents, Chatbot design, Chatbot student experience Introduction The integration of Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in higher education has accelerated rapidly, bringing with it a new suite of digital tools designed to enhance teaching and learning (Murdan",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,1,"chatbots, Higher education conversational agents, Chatbot design, Chatbot student experience Introduction The integration of Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in higher education has accelerated rapidly, bringing with it a new suite of digital tools designed to enhance teaching and learning (Murdan & Halkhoree, 2024). Among the most prominent and contentious of these innovations are educational chatbots (Kuhail et al., 2023), AI- powered conversational agents (Córdova-Esparza, 2025) embedded in digital learning",69
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,2,"Page 2 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 environments to assist students in navigating course materials (Srinivasan et al., 2024), understanding assessment requirements (Tsivitanidou & Ioannou, 2020), or accessing academic resources (Davar et al., 2025). As universities trial and scale these tools (Ait Baha et al., 2024; Neumann et al., 2021; Veras et al., 2023) understanding how students engage (Abbas et al., 2022) with and respond to them becomes increasingly important for ensuring inclusive, ethical, and effective implementation. While educational chatbots promise benefits such as 24/7 support (Atif et al., 2021), scalable guidance (Athavale et al., 2025), and personalised feedback (Cunningham- Nelson et al., 2019), their adoption and reception among students are far from uniform (Pillai et al., 2024). Students bring diverse expectations, skills, and concerns to their encounters with AI (Koh et al., 2024; Birenbaum, 2023). For some, chatbots offer a valu­ able complement to traditional instruction and help them stay organised and clarify con­ tent (Huang et al., 2022). For others, they appear redundant, unreliable, or even ethically problematic",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,2,"(Koh et al., 2024; Birenbaum, 2023). For some, chatbots offer a valu­ able complement to traditional instruction and help them stay organised and clarify con­ tent (Huang et al., 2022). For others, they appear redundant, unreliable, or even ethically problematic (Erkılıç & Cifci, 2025; Kooli, 2023). Despite the growing presence of chatbots in educational settings, limited empirical research explores how students experience and interpret these tools in real-world con­ texts. Existing studies tend to address chatbot design (Kuhail et al., 2023), technical per­ formance (Deng & Yu, 2023), or theoretical affordances (Kakhki et al., 2024) but overlook how students themselves make sense of chatbot interactions. If AI-enhanced learning environments are to be equitable and effective, they must be informed by student expe­ rience across multiple dimensions, not just technological uptake, but also trust, compre­ hension, values, and expectations. To date, empirical research has paid limited attention to this complexity, often focusing on technological performance rather than the nuanced realities of student experience (Moradi-Kelayeh, 2025). This study responds to that gap by exploring student perspectives on AI chatbot use in a",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,2,"empirical research has paid limited attention to this complexity, often focusing on technological performance rather than the nuanced realities of student experience (Moradi-Kelayeh, 2025). This study responds to that gap by exploring student perspectives on AI chatbot use in a university setting, guided by the recognition that students are not passive recipients of educational technology but active agents who interpret, accept, reject, or repurpose tools in ways that reflect their values, literacies, and learning needs. The aim of this study is to understand student perspectives on the use of educational AI chatbots, drawing on self-reported data from real-world deployment. This study pref­ erences student voice to inform more inclusive and responsive GenAI implementation strategies in higher education. The study is guided by the following four research ques­ tions (RQs): RQ1: How do students perceive the usefulness and academic value of chatbots in their units1? RQ2: How does prior technological experience and AI literacy influence students’ willingness and confidence to use chatbots? RQ3: What are the key reasons students choose not to use chatbots, and how do these reflect concerns about",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,2,"chatbots in their units1? RQ2: How does prior technological experience and AI literacy influence students’ willingness and confidence to use chatbots? RQ3: What are the key reasons students choose not to use chatbots, and how do these reflect concerns about ethics, usability, or instructional clarity? RQ4: How do students evaluate their overall user experience with chatbots, including satisfaction, responsiveness, and interface quality? The remainder of the paper is structured as follows: Sect. “Background: educational chatbots in context” provides the background, focusing on educational chatbots. 1 In an Australian context, a unit of study is a single, discrete component of a course, usually of 12 weeks duration. It is the equivalent to a subject or a module that contributes to the overall degree.",122
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,3,"Page 3 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 Section “Design of the chatbot” outlines the design of the chatbot. Section “Method” details the method including research design and ethical considerations. Sec­ tion “Results” presents the results, followed by a discussion in Sect. “Discussion”. Sec­ tion “Limitations” addresses the study limitations, and Sect. “Conclusion and key recommendations” concludes with key recommendations. Background: educational chatbots in context Educational chatbots and conversational agents powered by natural language processing (NLP) have gained substantial attention for their potential to deliver scalable, person­ alised, and immediate support to learners in an asynchronous mode. These tools can simulate human-like interaction, automate responses to frequently asked questions, scaffold learning tasks, and serve as guides in both academic and administrative set­ tings (Winkler & Söllner, 2018; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). As educational institutions experiment with AI to improve student outcomes and streamline operations (Vashishth et al., 2025), chatbots have become a key technology in the evolving digital education landscape (Gwebu, 2025; Rojas, 2024). Recent scholarship has highlighted the potential of",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,3,"As educational institutions experiment with AI to improve student outcomes and streamline operations (Vashishth et al., 2025), chatbots have become a key technology in the evolving digital education landscape (Gwebu, 2025; Rojas, 2024). Recent scholarship has highlighted the potential of chatbots to support personalised learning, extend academic support outside formal hours (Atif et al., 2021), and promote self-directed study (Holmes et al., 2019; Schei et al., 2024). AI-based conversational agents are increasingly deployed within learning management systems (LMSs) to offer unit-specific guidance, answer common student queries, and reduce instructional load (Qazi et al., 2024). Chatbots originated in the mid-twentieth century with rule-based systems such as ELIZA invented by J. Weizenbaum designed to mimic human conversation using scripted responses. With the advent of machine learning and large language models (LLMs), modern chatbots such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT, Google’s Bard, and institution- specific bots (Chukhlomin, 2024) have achieved a level of fluency and contextual respon­ siveness once considered implausible (Gholami et al., 2025). In the educational context, chatbots are now being deployed in two broad forms: general-purpose AI assistants such as ChatGPT used",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,3,"bots (Chukhlomin, 2024) have achieved a level of fluency and contextual respon­ siveness once considered implausible (Gholami et al., 2025). In the educational context, chatbots are now being deployed in two broad forms: general-purpose AI assistants such as ChatGPT used independently by students (Laato et al., 2023), and integrated course- specific bots embedded into learning platforms such as Moodle or Canvas chatbots tai­ lored to unit content (Nunes, 2023). Educational chatbots are being designed to perform a range of pedagogical and administrative tasks, including answering queries about unit content, due dates, and assessment formats; providing links to learning resources and explaining difficult concepts; simulating quizzes or revision dialogues; and assisting with navigation of LMS interfaces or institutional policies (Holmes et al., 2019; Kuhail et al., 2023). Institutions have begun to recognise the utility of these bots for large student cohorts, particularly in online and blended delivery modes (Pillai et al., 2024). Chatbot deploy­ ment is often aligned with goals such as increasing student satisfaction, reducing staff email volume, and enhancing responsiveness in asynchronous learning environments (Alemdag, 2025). Literature offers several",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,3,"particularly in online and blended delivery modes (Pillai et al., 2024). Chatbot deploy­ ment is often aligned with goals such as increasing student satisfaction, reducing staff email volume, and enhancing responsiveness in asynchronous learning environments (Alemdag, 2025). Literature offers several findings on the benefits of educational chat­ bots, particularly when aligned with instructional goals. They provide low-stakes envi­ ronments where students can ask repetitive or basic questions without fear of judgement supporting learners who may be hesitant to engage in class or contact teaching staff (Atif et al., 2021; Kuhail et al., 2023). For international and distance education students, 24/7 availability can be especially valuable (Abbas et al., 2022).",109
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,4,"Page 4 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 In some studies, chatbot use has been linked to improved learning outcomes, higher student engagement, and increased student satisfaction (Fryer et al., 2017; Winkler & Söllner, 2018). Chatbots also offer accessibility advantages for students with neurodi­ verse profiles (Velazquez-Solis et al., 2025) or those requiring repeated explanations of concepts (Gupta & Chen, 2022). Moreover, chatbots are seen as useful transition tools for students navigating the complexity of higher education systems for the first time (Labadze et al., 2023). Despite their promise, chatbot deployment in education is not without its challenges. A recurring critique relates to the superficiality of chatbot support with concerns that AI-generated responses may lack the depth needed for complex academic inquiry (Obi­ wuru, 2024; Schmidt et al., 2025). Chatbots may produce hallucinations (Jančařík & Dušek, 2024), particularly when not fine-tuned to specific unit content or institutional policies. This has implications for academic integrity and misinformation (Walczak, & Cellary, 2025). These design limitations can lead to frustration and disengagement, espe­ cially",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,4,"produce hallucinations (Jančařík & Dušek, 2024), particularly when not fine-tuned to specific unit content or institutional policies. This has implications for academic integrity and misinformation (Walczak, & Cellary, 2025). These design limitations can lead to frustration and disengagement, espe­ cially when expectations about chatbot intelligence or helpfulness are unmet (Kerlyl et al., 2006). Design of the chatbot The “Jordan Chatbot” as shown in Fig. 1 was designed as a human-centred, pedagogical support agent grounded in constructivist and socio-cultural learning theory. Built on the GPT-4o large language model, it integrates three core components: (1) system instructions defining the chatbot’s identity, personality, inclusions, and exclu­ sions; (2) a curated knowledge base containing disciplinary and institutional materials; and (3) an embedded interface within the LMS to enable seamless student access. By sit­ uating the chatbot within authentic disciplinary contexts (e.g., Australian criminal law), the design aligns with situated learning theory (Lave & Wenger, 1991), which empha­ sises contextualised, practice-based learning. Its structured scaffolding and supportive feedback echo Vygotsky’s (1978) concept of the zone of proximal development, position­ ing the chatbot as a mediating",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,4,"the design aligns with situated learning theory (Lave & Wenger, 1991), which empha­ sises contextualised, practice-based learning. Its structured scaffolding and supportive feedback echo Vygotsky’s (1978) concept of the zone of proximal development, position­ ing the chatbot as a mediating tool that extends but does not replace teacher guidance. The inclusion of transparent policies, ethical parameters, and user control reflects human-centred design principles (Norman & Draper, 1986) and aligns with emerging frameworks for responsible AI in education (Holmes et al., 2019; Zawacki-Richter et al., Fig. 1 Design of the Jordan Chatbot",91
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,5,"Page 5 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 2019). Moreover, by offering immediate, personalised feedback, the chatbot supports self-regulated learning processes (Zimmerman, 2002), encouraging reflection, auton­ omy, and metacognitive engagement. Collectively, these design features demonstrate how pedagogically aligned GenAI systems can enhance student learning, trust, and accessibility when informed by robust educational theory. Jordan Chatbot, was developed using the GPT-4o large language model and deployed as a unit-specific conversational agent within the learning management system. It was selected over general-purpose AI chatbots because it allowed the integration of curated unit materials, configurable system instructions, and compliance with institutional pri­ vacy and governance requirements. The chatbot was configured using system prompts that defined its pedagogical role, behavioural boundaries, and response limitations, including restrictions on answering assessment-related questions. A curated knowledge base consisting of unit learning materials and relevant institutional resources was incor­ porated to ground responses in unit content and reduce the likelihood of inaccurate outputs. This configuration enabled the chatbot to function as a controlled academic support tool tailored to the",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,5,"of unit learning materials and relevant institutional resources was incor­ porated to ground responses in unit content and reduce the likelihood of inaccurate outputs. This configuration enabled the chatbot to function as a controlled academic support tool tailored to the needs of each unit while maintaining appropriate educa­ tional and ethical safeguards. Method Research design This study employed a convergent mixed-methods design (Creswell and Plano Clark, 2023) to investigate how university students perceive, use, and evaluate GenAI chat­ bots embedded within LMS. The mixed-methods approach was chosen to integrate the breadth of quantitative evidence with the depth of qualitative insights, offering a com­ prehensive understanding of students’ engagement and concerns. This design aligns with established practices in educational technology research that seek to triangulate behavioural data, user perceptions, and contextual influences (Johnson & Onwuegbuzie, 2004; Green, 2007). An LLM, GPT-5, OpenAI was used solely to enhance linguistic clarity during manu­ script preparation, with all conceptual and analytical work conducted by the authors. The LLM’s contribution is documented here to ensure methodological transparency. The study was conducted at a regional Australian",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,5,"OpenAI was used solely to enhance linguistic clarity during manu­ script preparation, with all conceptual and analytical work conducted by the authors. The LLM’s contribution is documented here to ensure methodological transparency. The study was conducted at a regional Australian university during Term 1, 2025, as part of a broader institutional initiative exploring the integration of GenAI into teach­ ing and learning. A regional university supports diverse student cohorts and provides an important setting for examining how emerging educational technologies function in authentic learning environments. Regional universities also deliver a significant propor­ tion of their units through online and blended modes, making scalable digital support tools such as AI chatbots particularly relevant. Conducting the study in this context therefore allows the research to explore how chatbot technologies can support flexible learning and complement teaching in environments where students may rely heavily on asynchronous academic support. Participants and context The study was conducted at a regional Australian university during Term 1, 2025, as part of a broader AI-enhanced learning initiative. Chatbots were deployed across nine under­ graduate business and law units.",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,5,"heavily on asynchronous academic support. Participants and context The study was conducted at a regional Australian university during Term 1, 2025, as part of a broader AI-enhanced learning initiative. Chatbots were deployed across nine under­ graduate business and law units. Participants (n = 121) represented diverse age, gender,",48
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,6,"Page 6 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 and linguistic backgrounds, providing a robust cross-section of students engaging with AI in real-world learning environments. The chatbots were implemented in nine under­ graduate units within the fields of business and law. These disciplines were selected because they involve substantial problem-solving, case analysis, and interpretation of policy and regulatory frameworks, making them well suited to chatbot-supported guid­ ance and clarification of complex concepts. These units were part of the institutional AI-enhanced learning initiative and had teaching teams willing to participate in the pilot deployment of the chatbot. While other disciplinary areas were considered for future expansion, the initial implementation focused on business and law to allow controlled testing of the chatbot within units where structured content, clear knowledge bases, and frequent student queries made the tool particularly relevant. Instruments and data collection A structured online survey was developed in Qualtrics to capture both closed- and open-ended responses aligned with the four research questions. The instrument drew on prior validated measures of technology adoption,",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,6,"made the tool particularly relevant. Instruments and data collection A structured online survey was developed in Qualtrics to capture both closed- and open-ended responses aligned with the four research questions. The instrument drew on prior validated measures of technology adoption, AI literacy, and user experience (Gavis, 1989; Kooli, 2023; Pillai et al., 2024). Items were refined through expert review and pilot testing to ensure clarity and face validity. To do so, the survey instrument underwent a structured expert review process to ensure alignment with the study’s focus on GenAI integration in higher education. The expert panel comprised three special­ ists. Two members were senior academics in educational technology, each with over 20 years of research experience in AI-supported learning, digital pedagogy, and cur­ riculum innovation. Experts were provided with the survey items and asked to assess them for conceptual alignment with the study’s research questions, clarity of wording, relevance to real-world higher education contexts, and coverage of ethical, usability, and pedagogical considerations associated with AI chatbot use. They provided feedback and item-level ratings of relevance and clarity. Based on this",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,6,"with the study’s research questions, clarity of wording, relevance to real-world higher education contexts, and coverage of ethical, usability, and pedagogical considerations associated with AI chatbot use. They provided feedback and item-level ratings of relevance and clarity. Based on this feedback, several items were revised for clarity, redundant items were removed, and additional items were added to better capture student perspectives on ethics, AI literacy, and user experience. This pro­ cess strengthened the face and content validity of the instrument and ensured that it accurately reflects student experiences and concerns regarding the use of educational AI chatbots. Ethical clearance was obtained (HREC #0000025529), and informed consent was secured prior to participation. All students in each of the nine units were provided access to the unit specific chatbot via the learning management system. Students were recruited to complete the anonymous and voluntary survey instrument by an email invite and subsequent reminder. The email included details of the study, its aims, esti­ mated time commitment, and other ethics requirements. The survey items were structured to directly operationalise the four research ques­ tions",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,6,"voluntary survey instrument by an email invite and subsequent reminder. The email included details of the study, its aims, esti­ mated time commitment, and other ethics requirements. The survey items were structured to directly operationalise the four research ques­ tions guiding the study. Closed-ended items measuring perceptions of usefulness and academic value corresponded to RQ1, while items assessing prior technological expe­ rience and AI familiarity addressed RQ2. Questions exploring reasons for non-use, including concerns related to ethics, usability, and instructional clarity, were linked to RQ3. Finally, items examining user satisfaction, response clarity, and interface experi­ ence addressed RQ4. Examples of these survey items are illustrated in the Results sec­ tion, where the sub-questions corresponding to RQ1 and RQ2 are presented in Figs. 3",123
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,7,"Page 7 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 and 4 respectively, while representative items related to RQ3 and RQ4 are reported in the accompanying text. Data analysis Quantitative data were analysed using SPSS v29 following Field (2018). SPSS was selected because it provides well-established statistical procedures commonly used in educational and social science research, including descriptive statistics, one-sample t-tests, and ANOVA analyses, which were appropriate for examining group differences and patterns in the survey data. Descriptive statistics summarised central tendencies, while one-sample t-tests and ANOVAs tested group differences, with significance set at p < .05. Effect sizes (Cohen’s d) were calculated to gauge practical significance. Qualitative responses were exam­ ined using reflexive thematic analysis (Braun and Clarke, 2020). Coding proceeded inductively through six iterative phases: familiarisation, initial coding, theme genera­ tion, review, definition, and synthesis. To enhance trustworthiness, three participating researchers reviewed codes and themes, followed by peer debriefing and audit trails doc­ umenting analytic decisions. Integration and transparency Quantitative and qualitative findings were merged at the interpretation phase to identify",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,7,"review, definition, and synthesis. To enhance trustworthiness, three participating researchers reviewed codes and themes, followed by peer debriefing and audit trails doc­ umenting analytic decisions. Integration and transparency Quantitative and qualitative findings were merged at the interpretation phase to identify convergences and divergences between measured attitudes and expressed experiences. This methodological triangulation enhances both validity and transparency (Creswell and Plano Clark, 2023). Reflexivity was maintained through analytic memos, ensuring interpretations were grounded in the data rather than researcher assumptions. Results Demographics and usage patterns The cohort of 121 students participated in the survey was diverse in age, with the major­ ity aged between 18 and 25 years (52.9%), followed by participants in the 31–35 (14.0%) and over 45 (10.7%) age brackets. Gender representation was mixed, with 68.6% identi­ fying as female, 29.8% as male, and 1.6% preferring not to disclose. Participants reported a wide range of cultural backgrounds, with the largest group identifying as Oceanian (25.6%), followed by South-East Asian (14.0%) and North-West European (9.1%). Nota­ bly, 5.8% identified as Aboriginal, and 13.2% selected “prefer not to say” for cultural",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,7,"Participants reported a wide range of cultural backgrounds, with the largest group identifying as Oceanian (25.6%), followed by South-East Asian (14.0%) and North-West European (9.1%). Nota­ bly, 5.8% identified as Aboriginal, and 13.2% selected “prefer not to say” for cultural background. In terms of student type, 80.2% were domestic and 19.8% were interna­ tional. Regarding language proficiency, 62.8% identified as native English speakers, while 23.1% reported advanced proficiency and 12.4% identified as having intermediate profi­ ciency. This demographic mix highlights the linguistic and cultural diversity of the sam­ ple and provides an important context for interpreting students’ perceptions of chatbot use in higher education. The 24-h time-of-day usage pattern for chatbot interactions shown in Fig. 2 reveals several key trends in student engagement. A significant majority of interactions (63.2%) occurred during standard working hours (9 AM to 5 PM). A notable spike occurs at 5 PM, possibly coinciding with students transitioning from structured study or work to independent review. Evening hours (6 PM to 10 PM) show sustained interaction levels, indicating continued academic activity after formal hours. Usage then tapers",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,7,"notable spike occurs at 5 PM, possibly coinciding with students transitioning from structured study or work to independent review. Evening hours (6 PM to 10 PM) show sustained interaction levels, indicating continued academic activity after formal hours. Usage then tapers off into the",43
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,8,"Page 8 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 late night and early morning, with minimal engagement between 2 and 6 AM. This pat­ tern suggests the need for around-the-clock chatbot availability to support diverse study habits and schedules. However, a notable 36.8% of interactions happened after hours, suggesting the value of the chatbot as a source of academic support beyond traditional availability times. This after-hours usage highlights students’ need for flexible, on-demand assistance, being particularly useful for those studying part-time, balancing other commitments, or in dif­ ferent time zones. RQ1: How do students perceive the usefulness and academic value of chatbots in their units? One-sample t-Tests indicated that respondents generally had a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots in education (p < .001, n = 120). Respondents were concerned about how AI-chatbots will impact students’ learning in the future (p = .011, n = 119). Respon­ dents thought that the chatbots they used, made them more effective as a learner (p = .003, n = 120) and agreed that the",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,8,"how AI-chatbots will impact students’ learning in the future (p = .011, n = 119). Respon­ dents thought that the chatbots they used, made them more effective as a learner (p = .003, n = 120) and agreed that the chatbots they used improved their knowledge and understanding (p < .001, n = 120). Respondents disagreed that chatbots generated bet­ ter results than they could produce on their own (p < .001, n = 120). Respondents agreed that using chatbots to complete assignments and exams is a breach of academic integrity (p < .001, n = 120) and disagreed that using chatbots should be prohibited in educational settings (p = .017, n = 120). Respondents also agreed that ‘my teacher(s) or university has rules or guidelines on the responsible use of chatbots’ (p < .001, n = 120). With respect to gender, ANOVA revealed significant differences existed between group means on the issue of having a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots in education (F = 3.44, Sig = .035, df = 2). Tukey’s HSD (Honestly Significant Difference) pairwise",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,8,"to gender, ANOVA revealed significant differences existed between group means on the issue of having a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots in education (F = 3.44, Sig = .035, df = 2). Tukey’s HSD (Honestly Significant Difference) pairwise comparisons revealed that males were more likely to agree than females. Differ­ ences existed in group means on the question of whether ‘the chatbots I use improve my study grades’ (F = 4.31, Sig = .016, df = 2). Fig. 2 24-h usage patterns",84
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,9,"Page 9 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 With respect to English language proficiency an ANOVA revealed significant differ­ ences existed between group means on the issue of whether the use of chatbots was common amongst fellow students (F = 3.88, Sig = .011, df = 3). Tukey’s HSD pairwise comparisons revealed that intermediate and advanced English language proficiency respondents were more likely to agree. In terms of having a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots (F = 3.38, Sig = .021, df = 3). Comparing those who used the chatbot with those that did not. Respondents were grouped based on their chatbot usage (“Yes” for users and “No” for non-users). Responses were measured on a 5-point Likert scale (3 is neutral), and means were com­ pared across groups using a one-way ANOVA, with the results appearing in Fig. 3. Users of chatbots perceived higher usage among their peers (mean = 2.57) compared to non-users (mean = 3.04), (p = .015, df = 117) indicating a perception of chatbot use",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,9,"a one-way ANOVA, with the results appearing in Fig. 3. Users of chatbots perceived higher usage among their peers (mean = 2.57) compared to non-users (mean = 3.04), (p = .015, df = 117) indicating a perception of chatbot use across the cohort. Users had significantly more positive attitudes toward chatbot use in education (mean = 2.00) than non-users (mean = 2.78) (p < .001, df = 117) indicating very strong statistical evidence that there is a significant difference between the means of the groups being compared. Users agreed to a greater extent than non-users that the chatbots improved their knowledge and understanding (p < .001, df = 117), (mean = 2.15 users; and mean = 2.82 for non-users). Chatbot users suggested that using chatbots does not go against the purpose of education, answering the question: “Using chatbots goes against the purpose of education” (p = .037, df = 117). Mean = 3.38 for chatbot users and 2.95 for non-users). RQ2: How does prior technological experience and AI literacy influence students’ willingness and confidence to use chatbots? A dominant majority",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,9,"the purpose of education” (p = .037, df = 117). Mean = 3.38 for chatbot users and 2.95 for non-users). RQ2: How does prior technological experience and AI literacy influence students’ willingness and confidence to use chatbots? A dominant majority (78%) reported using laptops as their primary device, followed dis­ tantly by desktop computers (15.3%). Very few participants relied on tablets (2.5%) or smartphones (3.4%) for educational access, indicating a clear preference for larger, more versatile computing devices when engaging with learning platforms. This may reflect the need for devices that support multitasking, extended typing, and access to complex applications, key features often associated with effective online learning. Technological Fig. 3 Comparison of users and non-users’ usefulness and academic value",120
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,10,"Page 10 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 literacy was also reported to be high, with 80.5% of participants rating themselves as having either “High” or “Very High” proficiency, suggesting that most respondents are confident users of digital tools. While general technological literacy is strong, familiarity with AI is somewhat lower. Only 15.3% rated their AI understanding as “Excellent” and just over half (52.5%) described it as “Good”. A notable 29.7% rated their understanding as merely “Fair”, and 2.5% acknowledged a “Poor” grasp. This indicates a gap between general technical con­ fidence relate to AI literacy. Before engaging with the provided chatbot, only 19.5% had used chatbots or virtual assistants “Frequently” or “Very Frequently”, while a combined 72% reported using them only “Occasionally” or less. This suggests that the provided chatbot may be one of the first substantive chatbot experiences for many students, potentially serving as a catalyst for expanding AI familiarity and digital engagement in academic settings. Gender effects were also evident in the results. With respect to gender, ANOVA",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,10,"may be one of the first substantive chatbot experiences for many students, potentially serving as a catalyst for expanding AI familiarity and digital engagement in academic settings. Gender effects were also evident in the results. With respect to gender, ANOVA revealed significant differences existed between group means on how respondents rated their understanding of AI technology (F = 3.87, Sig = .024, df = 2). Tukey’s HSD pairwise comparisons revealed that females reported having a better understanding than males. With respect, how often chatbots or virtual assistants (e.g., Siri, Alexa, ChatGPT) were used before studying the unit (F = 5.91, Sig = .004, df = 2). Tukey’s HSD pairwise compari­ sons revealed that females had a greater exposure to these technologies than males. Several ANOVAS were used to compare users with non-users of the chatbot as against questions related to technological literacy and AI usage with only one significant result as shown in Fig. 4. Users of chatbots (mean = 2.93) had a higher prior usage of chatbots or virtual assistants (e.g. Siri, Alexa, ChatGPT) than non-users (mean = 3.47)",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,10,"technological literacy and AI usage with only one significant result as shown in Fig. 4. Users of chatbots (mean = 2.93) had a higher prior usage of chatbots or virtual assistants (e.g. Siri, Alexa, ChatGPT) than non-users (mean = 3.47) Sig = .006, df = 117). It appears that AI literacy rather than technological experience is associated with a student’s willingness and confidence to use chatbots. It is possible that training students in using chatbots may encourage an uptake in their usage. Fig. 4 Users and non-user technological and AI usage",91
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,11,"Page 11 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 RQ3: What are the key reasons students choose not to use chatbots, and how do these reflect concerns about ethics, usability, or instructional clarity? There were five statistically significant reasons why respondents chose not to use the chatbots. Lack of training (p = .04, n = 72), chatbots do not provide consistently trusted, accurate, safe or helpful responses (p < .001, n = 72), “I prefer to ask teaching staff rather than a chatbot” (p < .001, n = 71), “I did not want to be accused of breaching academic integrity” (p < .001,n = 72), and “I have no interest in chatbots” (p < .001, n = 72). The ques­ tion “I oppose the use of chatbots” resulted in a neutral insignificant response. Age effects were also evident in the results. With respect to age an ANOVA revealed significant differences existed between group means on the issue of potentially being accused of breaching academic integrity (F = 2.59, Sig = .026, df",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,11,"response. Age effects were also evident in the results. With respect to age an ANOVA revealed significant differences existed between group means on the issue of potentially being accused of breaching academic integrity (F = 2.59, Sig = .026, df = 6). Tukey’s HSD pair­ wise comparisons revealed that the age groups 26–30, 31–35 and over 45 were more concerned about, this issue than the groups 18–20, 21–25 and 41–45. Open-ended comments from students who did not use the chatbot were analysed using reflexive thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2020). Seven themes emerged (see Fig. 5). Usability was the biggest theme (23% of responses) with many students disliking the intrusive pop-up design. For example, “The pop-up was annoying which made me dislike it so much that I refused to use it”. “I found the chatbot very annoying. Everything I logged in; it came up and had to keep closing it.” “I just didn’t need to and found it annoy­ ing that it kept popping up every time I opened the middle site instead of just opening it when I would",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,11,"in; it came up and had to keep closing it.” “I just didn’t need to and found it annoy­ ing that it kept popping up every time I opened the middle site instead of just opening it when I would need it”. Non-users saw the chatbot as intrusive, annoying and popped up too often, indicating a poor user experience. A preference for humans or traditional search engines over chatbots (18% of responses) can be seen in comments such as “I prefer to go to direct sources via search engines. It’s my preference”. “I don’t think to use them. I rely on my own skills to research, write and study.” “I haven’t had the urge to use chatbot and would rather still seek Teacher support.” “I prefer to ask the teaching staff rather than a chatbot”. Non-users preferred teachers, facilitators and to a lesser extent search engines instead of the chatbot. There was also a thread of self-sufficiency and perceived chatbot irrelevance. A lack of usefulness and need (18% of responses) was apparent in comments such as “I didn’t know how",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,11,to a lesser extent search engines instead of the chatbot. There was also a thread of self-sufficiency and perceived chatbot irrelevance. A lack of usefulness and need (18% of responses) was apparent in comments such as “I didn’t know how it could help so I just didn’t use it.” “Did not feel the need to use it.” “I didn’t feel the need to try it”. Some non-users were confident enough not to use the Fig. 5 Non-user themes,78
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,12,"Page 12 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 chatbot. Interestingly there was a subgroup who saw the potential of chatbots but did not perceive a personal need. This is indicative in comments such as “I think it was a wonderful idea and fully acknowledge the benefits… but I had an amazing teacher … so I didn’t feel that it was needed.” “I have no aversion to using them, and in fact find them useful … but I did not use the chat bot … as the Moodle page was easy to navigate.” Some non-users expressed concerns about AI (14% of responses), including accuracy and aspects of integrity. For example, “I do not like AI, I have heard too many instances where it generates incorrect information and fake references.” “I didn’t want to be accused of breaching academic integrity.” “I do have concerns with students being allowed to use generative AI… unfair advantage.” These types of responses show mistrust, ethical con­ cerns and a fear of academic misconduct. A few non-users were",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,12,"to be accused of breaching academic integrity.” “I do have concerns with students being allowed to use generative AI… unfair advantage.” These types of responses show mistrust, ethical con­ cerns and a fear of academic misconduct. A few non-users were not aware of the chatbot or had knowledge gaps around usage of the chatbot (8% of responses). For example, “Didn’t know chatbot existed, unclear what it was for.” Some non-users saw a conflict between time pressure and competing priorities (6% of responses) which played out in not spending time exploring the chatbot. RQ4: How do students evaluate their overall user experience with chatbots, including satisfaction, responsiveness, and interface quality? One-sample t-Tests indicated that chatbot users had a significant response to the fol­ lowing questions. Users thought that the chatbots were easy to use r (p < .001, n = 37), that the chatbot responses were clear and understandable (p < .001, n = 37), that chat­ bot responses were relevant to their questions or needs (p < .001, n = 38). Information provided by the chatbot was reported as accurate",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,12,"that the chatbot responses were clear and understandable (p < .001, n = 37), that chat­ bot responses were relevant to their questions or needs (p < .001, n = 38). Information provided by the chatbot was reported as accurate (p < .001, n = 38). Users reported that the chatbot seemed to understand and answer their questions well (p < .001, n = 38), that the chatbot personalised the interaction based on user needs and requirements (p = .026, n = 38). Users reported occasional errors or problems while using the chatbot (p < .003, n = 38). The responses are classified into 6 themes as depicted in Fig. 6. Helpfulness for infor­ mation and navigation was the biggest theme (24% of responses) with many students finding the chatbots helpful for retrieving course information, and in navigating Moodle Fig. 6 What users liked about the chatbots",147
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,13,"Page 13 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 and their general study. “Helped to find information that was located randomly across the units Moodle.” “It helped me stay on track when navigating study because it provided an immediate source of information rather than needing to spend significant time being caught in roadblocks.” “I asked specific questions regarding my class as well as questions regarding my unit and terminology. I liked that it was tailored to the unit and answered any questions I had.” Availability and accessibility accounted for 21% of responses. Users valued that the chatbot was always present, quick, and accessible. “Whenever I open my study tab it shows automatically so I like that.” “Easy access to information.” “Fast response.” Subject specific inquiries and learning support were the subject of 18% of responses. This is related to subject relevance, ability to generate quizzes, assistance with study and examination preparation and simplification of concepts to assist with understanding. “It breaks down a difficult topic into a table / graph for understanding.”",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,13,"18% of responses. This is related to subject relevance, ability to generate quizzes, assistance with study and examination preparation and simplification of concepts to assist with understanding. “It breaks down a difficult topic into a table / graph for understanding.” “It was good that it was specific to criminal law content and would only stay within the scope of that knowl­ edge.” “Good, if I wanted a very brief refresher on a topic.” Interaction and tone (12% of responses) related to the chatbot’s perceived friendliness, clarity and conversational style “What I liked most about the chatbot was how easy it was to talk to and how helpful it was when I needed answers.” Neutral, negative or limited use responses accounted for a further 12% of responses. Example responses included “I didn’t use the chat bot.” Some users did not use it, others thought it was slow and below their expectations. Users were asked how the chatbots could be improved. The responses are classified into the 6 themes depicted in Fig. 7. Pop-up and user experience issues accounted for 22% of",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,13,"it, others thought it was slow and below their expectations. Users were asked how the chatbots could be improved. The responses are classified into the 6 themes depicted in Fig. 7. Pop-up and user experience issues accounted for 22% of responses. “Don’t allow it to pop up automatically every time. I would prefer it to be a button I could click if I wanted to use it.” Suggestions for improvement (22% of responses) covered issues such as “offer­ ing more links to official university resources”, “adding voice interaction”, “a mobile App version”, “more information on how to use it”, “faster process”, and “provide a chat history.” One user suggested “If it could be more like our teachers…”. Fig. 7 Suggestions for improvement",122
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,14,"Page 14 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 Accuracy and reliability concerns represented 19% of responses. “It gave incorrect due dates or mark release dates and sometimes completely different information…” “More accurate responses…..” Neutral or minimal responses were provided by 17% of respondents. A total of 14% of respondents provided positive feedback. “I don’t think any improve­ ment need in the chatbot.” “I think it is currently effective & am sure it will get more sophisticated with time.” “Nothing that I can think of, I used it a couple of times for specific unit related questions and is already a useful tool for students.” Negative or removal feed­ back arose with 6% of respondents. “I didn’t use it enough to answer this question.” “I only used this chatbot once to get clarification…”. Discussion Our empirical study is unique in its focus on how students experienced and interpreted chatbots in a real-world higher educational context. While chatbot design (Kuhail et al., 2023) and technical performance (Deng & Yu, 2023) are clearly relevant,",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,14,"clarification…”. Discussion Our empirical study is unique in its focus on how students experienced and interpreted chatbots in a real-world higher educational context. While chatbot design (Kuhail et al., 2023) and technical performance (Deng & Yu, 2023) are clearly relevant, our study examined the diverse motivations, barriers, and perceptions that shape student engage­ ment. Student experience (Moradi-Kelayeh, 2025) is highly relevant to chatbot usage and uptake. Students are active agents who interpret, accept, reject, or repurpose tools in ways that reflect their values, literacies, and learning needs. Gender, age and English language proficiency also have nuanced effects on uptake, usage and perceptions of chat­ bots. The four research questions posed in this study provide a useful lens against which to explore the results. RQ1: How do students perceive the usefulness and academic value of chatbots in their units? Respondents had a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots in education whether they used the chatbot or otherwise, despite some concern about how chatbots will impact on student learning in the future. These concerns were not sufficient to suggest that chatbots",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,14,"a positive attitude towards the use of chatbots in education whether they used the chatbot or otherwise, despite some concern about how chatbots will impact on student learning in the future. These concerns were not sufficient to suggest that chatbots should be banned. There was agreement that chatbots improved knowl­ edge and understanding but were not seen as producing better results than students could produce on their own. This finding is somewhat surprising as student ability spans a bell curve of abilities, it being possible that chatbots would outperform those at the lower portions of these ability curves. Students respect academic integrity and Univer­ sity rules and guidelines for the responsible use of chatbots that were in place. Females had a more positive attitude towards the use of chatbots than males, though the reverse was true of whether chatbots could improve study grades. English language proficiency produced interesting outcomes. The student population using chatbots included international students whose first language was not English. In many cases intermediate level English language speakers had a more favourable view of chatbots concerning issues",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,14,"improve study grades. English language proficiency produced interesting outcomes. The student population using chatbots included international students whose first language was not English. In many cases intermediate level English language speakers had a more favourable view of chatbots concerning issues such as usage amongst peers, having a positive view of chatbots, improving their knowledge and understanding and chatbots producing better results than users could produce on their own, leading to better grades. These results suggest that intermediate English language users make relatively more use of chatbots to support their studies than more advanced language speakers. These findings are consistent with previous research reporting generally positive stu­ dent attitudes towards educational chatbots and AI-supported learning tools (Kuhail et",117
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,15,"Page 15 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 al., 2023; Pillai et al., 2024). Similar to earlier studies, students in this study viewed chat­ bots primarily as supplementary tools that support learning rather than replacements for human instruction. However, the results also highlight a nuanced perspective in which students recognise the potential benefits of AI assistance while maintaining strong sup­ port for academic integrity guidelines. This finding reinforces earlier observations that responsible AI use in education depends on clear institutional policies and pedagogical framing (Kooli, 2023). RQ2: How does prior technological experience and AI literacy influence students’ willingness and confidence to use chatbots? Respondents predominantly use laptops to access chatbots with next to no usage reported for smaller mobile devices. While respondents reported high technological lit­ eracy, the results were much lower for specific AI literacy. For many respondents the chatbots used in this study presented their first experiences with chatbot technology or perhaps a reason to avoid its use. Females had a greater exposure to prior use of chatbots than",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,15,"lower for specific AI literacy. For many respondents the chatbots used in this study presented their first experiences with chatbot technology or perhaps a reason to avoid its use. Females had a greater exposure to prior use of chatbots than males. Comparing users and non-users confirmed that chatbot users had a greater prior exposure to chatbots than non-users. This suggests that the gap between users and non-users may be closed through training and exposure to chatbot technologies. There were no effects related to overall technological literacy or ratings of understanding of AI technology. These results support previous studies suggesting that prior exposure to AI tools influ­ ences students’ willingness to adopt educational technologies (Jha & Atif, 2025; Laato et al., 2023; Schei et al., 2024). While general technological literacy among students was high, familiarity with AI systems was comparatively lower, indicating that confidence with digital tools does not necessarily translate into AI literacy. This finding highlights the importance of targeted AI literacy training in higher education to support effective and responsible use of emerging technologies. RQ3: What are the key",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,15,"indicating that confidence with digital tools does not necessarily translate into AI literacy. This finding highlights the importance of targeted AI literacy training in higher education to support effective and responsible use of emerging technologies. RQ3: What are the key reasons students choose not to use chatbots, and how do these reflect concerns about ethics, usability, or instructional clarity? Students in this study reported not using chatbots because of lack of training, chatbots do not provide consistently trusted, accurate, safe or helpful responses, they preferred to ask teaching staff rather than a chatbot, they did not want to be accused of breaching academic integrity or had no interest in chatbots They were ambivalent to the question “I oppose the use of chatbots”. Some age groups were more concerned about academic integrity than others. Clearly training would enable students to better understand the affordances offered by chatbots to improve higher education. The inclusion of starter prompts within the chat­ bot interface, while often used by students, seemly did not provide sufficient support to encourage greater uptake. The question of trust and",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,15,"understand the affordances offered by chatbots to improve higher education. The inclusion of starter prompts within the chat­ bot interface, while often used by students, seemly did not provide sufficient support to encourage greater uptake. The question of trust and accuracy is interesting. The chatbots used in this study were specifically trained on unit content, relevant legislation, policies etc. which tend to reduce hallucinations. The chatbots were continually monitored and any edge cases— situations of adverse or incorrect responses—were addressed by whether altering the system instructions or supplementary knowledge base, or both. Chatbots are not a replacement for individual student teacher interaction, rather they are a supplementary",107
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,16,"Page 16 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 tool, especially for out-of-hour inquiries and simple tasks. More complex human inter­ actions should be retained as part of higher education. The main ethical issue is related to potential breaches of academic integrity which carry harmful consequences for students, about which they are continually warned. The chatbots provided instructions on how to avoid breaches and would not answer any questions related to assessment. The general fear of academic integrity breaches may have encouraged students to avoid the chatbot altogether—even though there was in fact little to no risk. Having no interest in chatbots is a valid position, but one which is fraught with potential impacts on future employment prospects in a world transition to an AI economy. The reasons students provided for not using the chatbot also align with concerns reported in earlier studies on educational AI systems. Previous research has identified issues such as trust, perceived reliability, and ethical concerns as key barriers to adop­ tion of chatbot (Kooli, 2023; Walczak &",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,16,"using the chatbot also align with concerns reported in earlier studies on educational AI systems. Previous research has identified issues such as trust, perceived reliability, and ethical concerns as key barriers to adop­ tion of chatbot (Kooli, 2023; Walczak & Cellary, 2025). In this study, concerns about aca­ demic integrity and the perceived reliability of AI-generated responses were particularly influential. These findings reinforce the need for clear communication regarding appro­ priate AI use and the role of chatbots as supportive rather than authoritative learning tools. RQ4: How do students evaluate their overall user experience with chatbots, including satisfaction, responsiveness, and interface quality? There is no doubt the chatbots were easy to use and produced clear and understandable outputs relevant to student questions and needs. The information was largely accurate, personalised to user needs and requirements. Qualitative responses indicated that sev­ eral factors contributed to driving the use of the chatbots. This included their easy avail­ ability, the help they provide in navigating units and the learning management system, unit-specific content and their friendly tone. The chatbots presented users with useful",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,16,"eral factors contributed to driving the use of the chatbots. This included their easy avail­ ability, the help they provide in navigating units and the learning management system, unit-specific content and their friendly tone. The chatbots presented users with useful learning aids such as quizzes, definitions and simplified explanations of unit content. There were barriers in use of the chatbots including occasional slowness, accuracy con­ cerns and a lack of student engagement. This was not to say that the chatbots could not be improved. In summary, the pop-up design was the largest annoyance to users. There were some accuracy and reliability concerns and requests for better integration of reli­ able valid links, chat history, more teacher-like responses and greater academic depth in chatbot responses. These findings are broadly consistent with previous research demonstrating that stu­ dents value chatbots for their accessibility, rapid responses, and ability to provide imme­ diate clarification of unit-related questions (Kuhail et al., 2023; Winkler & Söllner, 2018). At the same time, concerns about accuracy, interface design, and occasional system limitations mirror challenges identified in earlier chatbot",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,16,"accessibility, rapid responses, and ability to provide imme­ diate clarification of unit-related questions (Kuhail et al., 2023; Winkler & Söllner, 2018). At the same time, concerns about accuracy, interface design, and occasional system limitations mirror challenges identified in earlier chatbot studies. Together, these results suggest that while conversational AI can enhance student support services (Atif et al., 2021), careful attention to user interface design, response reliability, and system integra­ tion remains essential for successful implementation. Human centered design Human centered design encourages student engagement and uptake of chatbots. The chatbots used in this study had a human name and a human facial icon. They were also",106
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,17,"Page 17 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 designed to have an engaging personality. Chatbots should be designed to avoid user training and should be simple to use to enhance engagement. It is noted however that training may encourage uptake. Starter prompts can be displayed to help orientate users in the chatbot capabilities. Practical implementation Institutions need to develop clear policies on the use of AI, particularly in relation to assessment and academic integrity, as well as policies on deployment and usage. Aca­ demics must make it clear which assessments may use AI and which may not. The extent of AI use in assessment must also be unambiguous. Chatbots need to be specifically trained to avoid breaches of these guidelines to educate students on the policies and to avoid breaches. Care must be taken to avoid academic integrity issues and ensure that student critical thinking is maintained and enhanced. The chatbot can be instructed not to answer assessment questions or simply be turned off during assessment exercises. The chatbot can also",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,17,"must be taken to avoid academic integrity issues and ensure that student critical thinking is maintained and enhanced. The chatbot can be instructed not to answer assessment questions or simply be turned off during assessment exercises. The chatbot can also inform students about academic integrity when it receives a problem­ atic prompt addressing an assessment question. University knowledge bases should be separate from Generative AI training sets to preserve university intellectual property, to ensure chatbots are referencing current information tailor made to each unit in which they are deployed. Copyright rights need to be respected where academics refer to readings produced by other copyright own­ ers. Often university libraries are subject to database contracts that prohibit the use of database content in AI knowledge bases. Chatbots should be deployed on the LMS by developers’ not academic staff. This is to ensure consistency in deployment and that appropriate standards are maintained. This study contributes to the growing body of research on GenAI and educational chatbots in higher education by examining student experiences within a real-world deployment across multiple undergraduate units. While",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,17,"ensure consistency in deployment and that appropriate standards are maintained. This study contributes to the growing body of research on GenAI and educational chatbots in higher education by examining student experiences within a real-world deployment across multiple undergraduate units. While previous studies have primar­ ily focused on technical performance or theoretical potential of educational chatbots, this research provides empirical insights into how students perceive, adopt, and evaluate chatbot-supported learning in authentic unit environments. By combining system usage analytics with mixed-methods survey data, the study highlights the importance of AI literacy, user interface design, and institutional guidance in shaping student engagement with AI tools. The findings therefore extend existing literature by emphasising the role of human-centred design and clear pedagogical framing in supporting the responsible integration of GenAI technologies in higher education. Limitations While the study provides valuable insights, some limitations must be acknowledged: The sample size (n = 121) limits generalisability to the broader student population. While the system analytics data is accurate, the survey data is self-reported and may be influenced by recall bias or social desirability. The study",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,17,"must be acknowledged: The sample size (n = 121) limits generalisability to the broader student population. While the system analytics data is accurate, the survey data is self-reported and may be influenced by recall bias or social desirability. The study does not include longitudinal data, so changes in perception over time could not be tracked. The chatbot technolo­ gies were relatively new affecting usage rates and experience. Training was not provided for students on how to use the chatbot, apart from a limited set of starter prompts. This study did not report on the types and themes associated with student prompts. This will be the subject of a follow-up paper. Despite these limitations, the research design was",117
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,18,"Page 18 of 21 Colbran et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:28 fit-for-purpose and allowed for a detailed exploration of students’ real-time experiences with educational chatbots. Future research could build on the present study by examining chatbot use across a broader range of institutions and disciplinary contexts to improve the generalisability of the findings. Longitudinal studies could also explore how student perceptions and patterns of chatbot engagement evolve as AI technologies become more familiar within higher education environments. Further research could analyse the types of prompts students use when interacting with chatbots and investigate how these interactions influence learning outcomes, critical thinking, and academic integrity practices. Such studies would contribute to a deeper understanding of how GenAI tools can be effec­ tively and responsibly integrated into higher education teaching and learning. Conclusion and key recommendations The primary objective of this study was to understand student perspectives on the use of GenAI chatbots in higher education, with particular attention to their perceived aca­ demic value, patterns of use, barriers to adoption, and overall user experience. The",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,18,"The primary objective of this study was to understand student perspectives on the use of GenAI chatbots in higher education, with particular attention to their perceived aca­ demic value, patterns of use, barriers to adoption, and overall user experience. The find­ ings indicate that this objective has been achieved through the analysis of both survey responses and chatbot usage data. The results provide empirical insights into how stu­ dents engage with chatbot technologies in real educational contexts, highlighting gen­ erally positive perceptions of their usefulness alongside important concerns related to usability, trust, and academic integrity. By addressing these dimensions, the study offers a clearer understanding of the conditions under which AI chatbots can effectively com­ plement teaching and learning in higher education environments. This study provides useful insights into how university students perceive and use AI educational chatbots. The study highlights the importance of chatbot design, and their practical implementation. It is also important to realise that the chatbots implemented and evaluated in this study were designed as unit specific support agents providing both administrative and unit specific content. Trained",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,18,"highlights the importance of chatbot design, and their practical implementation. It is also important to realise that the chatbots implemented and evaluated in this study were designed as unit specific support agents providing both administrative and unit specific content. Trained chatbots should be developed on an individual unit basis and included in the set of tools used by students to study their discipline. Academics should be trained in how chatbots are constructed, how to create appropriate knowledge bases, and taught prompt engineering to ensure that both they and their students are well versed in AI applications in their respective disciplines. Chatbots need to be thoughtfully designed using human centered design to ensure usability and student engagement. Practice ready graduates require knowledge on how to use AI assisted technologies such as generative AI chatbots to increase their productivity in the workforce, but not to replace their criti­ cal thinking, thought and communication skills. Acknowledgements The authors gratefully acknowledge the support of their respective institutions for providing time, which made the study possible. The authors have utilised artificial intelligence tools to support",180
A092,A092.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00605-w,18,"but not to replace their criti­ cal thinking, thought and communication skills. Acknowledgements The authors gratefully acknowledge the support of their respective institutions for providing time, which made the study possible. The authors have utilised artificial intelligence tools to support clarity and improve the overall readability of this manuscript. Author contributions Stephen Colbran: Supervision, Conceptualization, Data analysis, Writing-Reviewing and Editing, Validation, Investigation. Meena Jha: Writing-Original draft preparation, Validation, Software, Writing-Reviewing and Editing. Caitlin Schiavone: Data curation, Data analysis, Software, Visualization, Validation, Investigation, Writing-Reviewing and Editing. Funding Authors received fundings from the host School to conduct this research.",97
