A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,1,"EDITED BY Mark Zarb, Robert Gordon University, United Kingdom REVIEWED BY Eduarda Ferreira, New University of Lisbon, Portugal Ramazan Ahmadi, Bamyan University, Afghanistan *CORRESPONDENCE Miguel Alexander Quintanilla Villegas miguel.quintanilla@uees.edu.sv RECEIVED 08 June 2026 REVISED 29 June 2026 ACCEPTED 30 June 2026 PUBLISHED 27 July 2026 CITATION Quintanilla Villegas MA and Pineda Rivas EE (2026) Motivations, consequences, and concerns about the use of artificial intelligence in higher education: empirical evidence. Front. Educ. 11:1903769. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Motivations, consequences, and concerns about the use of artificial intelligence in higher education: empirical evidence Miguel Alexander Quintanilla Villegas* and Elia Elizabeth Pineda Rivas Docente Investigador Asociado al Centro de Investigacion",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,1,"is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Motivations, consequences, and concerns about the use of artificial intelligence in higher education: empirical evidence Miguel Alexander Quintanilla Villegas* and Elia Elizabeth Pineda Rivas Docente Investigador Asociado al Centro de Investigacion Salud y Sociedad de la Universidad Evangélica de El Salvador, San Salvador, El Salvador Introduction: The article analyzes the motivations, consequences, and concerns regarding the use of artificial intelligence (AI) among university students, with the aim of determining its influence on teaching-learning processes mediated in higher education. Methodology: A quantitative analysis of the variables was conducted through the establishment of CHAID (Chi-square Automatic Interaction Detection) relationships among the motivations, consequences, and concerns perceived in students’ educational dynamics. The analysis incorporated exogenous variables (academic workload, time pressure, sensitivity to rewards, and sensitivity to quality), endogenous variables (use of ChatGPT, procrastination, memory loss, and academic performance), and a set of sociodemographic factors (sex, age, employment status, academic cycle, marital status, previous educational sector, and parenthood). An intentional sampling was applied to 268 students from universities in El Salvador. Results: The nine decision",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,1,"loss, and academic performance), and a set of sociodemographic factors (sex, age, employment status, academic cycle, marital status, previous educational sector, and parenthood). An intentional sampling was applied to 268 students from universities in El Salvador. Results: The nine decision trees generated reveal heterogeneous yet consistent patterns. The most robust models show that academic cycle and perceived academic workload are the most relevant initial predictors, leading to pathways where difficulties in retaining information and intensive use of ChatGPT emerge as compensatory mechanisms. Discussion: The findings open a problematic field concerning the diverse socioeducational processes and adjustments influencing higher education, confirming trends documented in previous international and regional studies. Conclusions: The results highlight the urgent need to strengthen digital competencies, establish clear ethical guidelines, and promote a pedagogically oriented use of these technologies in Salvadoran higher education. KEYWORDS artificial intelligence, CHAID models, decision trees, higher education, student, universities 1 Introduction At present, the scientific community is engaged in an ongoing discussion about the drivers, consequences, and concerns surrounding Artificial Intelligence (AI) in education. This has become a critical point of analysis",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,1,"models, decision trees, higher education, student, universities 1 Introduction At present, the scientific community is engaged in an ongoing discussion about the drivers, consequences, and concerns surrounding Artificial Intelligence (AI) in education. This has become a critical point of analysis as AI integration into teaching– learning processes continues to deepen, potentially leading to a substantive alignment TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 27 July 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",70
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"between technological innovation and curriculum development and, consequently, the consolidation of new forms of learning. In this context, several studies suggest that AI can enhance students’ multiple intelligences. At the same time, they note a lack of evidence sufficient to establish clear trends regarding the implications of AI use, which makes it difficult to assess its effects on higher-education training processes. This situation calls for rethinking, on the one hand, the motivations that drive its use —understood as an adaptation to digital environments that increasingly penetrate educational settings—and, on the other hand, how individuals conceptualize learning, which appears to be shifting toward reliance on AI rather than the development of the knowing subject (Grubaugh et al., 2023; Monge Vera et al., 2024; Montúfar Flores et al., 2022; Rodríguez Gil et al., 2024). When examining the gaps this may generate, Villegas and Rivas (2025), drawing on evidence from multiple studies, show that AI use in education tends to reflect two simultaneous dynamics: first, the consolidation of multiple intelligences, which not only strengthens the learning experience (Mora and Rizzo, 2023; Castro Cedeño",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"and Rivas (2025), drawing on evidence from multiple studies, show that AI use in education tends to reflect two simultaneous dynamics: first, the consolidation of multiple intelligences, which not only strengthens the learning experience (Mora and Rizzo, 2023; Castro Cedeño and Vega Intriago, 2022); and second, the recognition that AI use may be associated with procrastination, privacy risks, and academic stress (Abbas et al., 2024; Jo, 2024; Sanabria-Navarro et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024). Collectively, this literature reflects an international trend toward identifying additional emerging factors that enable a broader understanding of AI use, its consequences, and associated concerns. Other international studies grounded in empirical evidence suggest a causal ordering of AI use in higher education associated with academic workload and time pressure for assignment submission—variables that are positively associated with commercial platforms such as ChatGPT (Abbas et al., 2024). This indicates that, within a stimulus-rich educational environment, contextual demands may condition AI use as a means of meeting established requirements. Regarding factors such as self-directed learning, knowledge, and personalization, Jo’s (2024) study on ChatGPT use indicates a positive",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"2024). This indicates that, within a stimulus-rich educational environment, contextual demands may condition AI use as a means of meeting established requirements. Regarding factors such as self-directed learning, knowledge, and personalization, Jo’s (2024) study on ChatGPT use indicates a positive effect on learning, supporting improved ways of applying and acquiring knowledge. However, the study also identifies privacy and technophobia as salient concerns, as they consistently shape the formation of positive attitudes toward the tool. Additional studies show that self-efficacy, ease of use, and perceived usefulness can foster dependence on AI. This allows for identifying relationships with ethical issues such as plagiarism, as well as potential erosion of creativity—vulnerabilities that appear especially pronounced among students with lower academic performance—along with detrimental effects on critical and creative skills (Zhang et al., 2024; Shahzad et al., 2024). Along these lines, the studies by Mahapatra (2024) and Chan (2023) are particularly relevant. The former reports that using this AI significantly improves academic writing skills and highlights its potential for integration into educational contexts, while also raising concerns related to dependency and unethical use of",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"Mahapatra (2024) and Chan (2023) are particularly relevant. The former reports that using this AI significantly improves academic writing skills and highlights its potential for integration into educational contexts, while also raising concerns related to dependency and unethical use of the tool. In Latin America, García Sánchez (2023), Solano-Barliza et al. (2024), and Zukerfeld et al. (2023) further examine students’ perceptions of ChatGPT. In Mexico, García Sánchez (2023) reports that only three out of ten students use it for academic activities and that most do not perceive improvements in research skills or sufficient teacher preparation. In Colombia’s Caribbean region, Solano-Barliza et al. (2024) identify positive perceptions regarding its usefulness for understanding complex topics, while warning about risks of dependence and the weakening of critical thinking. Finally, Zukerfeld et al. (2023) analyze labor-market impacts and find that, although ChatGPT increases productivity, users do not perceive an immediate risk of full automation of human work (Tramallino and Marize Zeni, 2024). In El Salvador, available data reflect limited access; however, existing research has identified perceptions regarding its use (Briñis-Zambrano, 2024) and explored ethical",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"users do not perceive an immediate risk of full automation of human work (Tramallino and Marize Zeni, 2024). In El Salvador, available data reflect limited access; however, existing research has identified perceptions regarding its use (Briñis-Zambrano, 2024) and explored ethical issues accompanying its implementation (Deleon et al., 2024). These dynamics are mediated by individuals’ purchasing power for commercial AI tools and by the benefits that higher-education institutions may provide to facilitate access to such technologies. This assessment is supported by both studies, which indicate that faculty and students possess only basic knowledge of tools such as ChatGPT and Copilot and that, although they recognize their educational potential, concerns persist regarding academic ethics and the need for training. These studies also suggest significant associations between AI use and academic output, albeit with ethical implications that affect the educational process. Taken together, this body of research indicates that AI use in higher education is driven primarily by curiosity rather than by planned instructional processes and that, although adoption remains incipient, there is broad acceptance of its future potential (Braga et al., 2024;",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"this body of research indicates that AI use in higher education is driven primarily by curiosity rather than by planned instructional processes and that, although adoption remains incipient, there is broad acceptance of its future potential (Braga et al., 2024; Kuhail et al., 2023; Riedel et al., 2023). Nonetheless, findings are varied and fragmented, which prevents the formulation of uniform conclusions about AI’s effects on teaching and learning processes. Recent empirical evidence reinforces this interpretation. Ahmadi and Danishyar (2025) found that AI use among Bamyan University students remains moderate but is associated with improved access to educational resources, faster task completion, academic writing, analytical skills, and independent learning. However, their study also identifies concerns related to technological dependence, reduced creativity, academic dishonesty, lack of formal training, limited infrastructure, and absence of ethical guidelines. Together, these recent studies suggest that AI adoption in higher education should be understood as a multidimensional phenomenon involving academic motivation, instrumental use, perceived cognitive and ethical concerns, institutional preparedness, and regulatory clarity. This is especially relevant for underrepresented contexts such as El Salvador, where empirical evidence",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"adoption in higher education should be understood as a multidimensional phenomenon involving academic motivation, instrumental use, perceived cognitive and ethical concerns, institutional preparedness, and regulatory clarity. This is especially relevant for underrepresented contexts such as El Salvador, where empirical evidence remains limited and institutional AI policies are still emerging. From an ethical perspective, Ahmadi et al. (2026) found that AI use among faculty members at Bamyan University is still mainly limited to basic research activities, while concerns about legal uncertainty, plagiarism, lack of transparency, and privacy risks negatively affect responsible adoption. Their findings highlight that institutional regulations, ethical frameworks, and digital literacy are essential conditions for the responsible use of AI in academic contexts. The authors agree that the use of AI in higher education yields heterogeneous outcomes depending on context, yet ethical concerns emerge as a point of consensus. This underscores the urgency of establishing clear regulations and strengthening the competencies required for appropriate, context-sensitive use of these technologies in educational settings. Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",174
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,2,"This underscores the urgency of establishing clear regulations and strengthening the competencies required for appropriate, context-sensitive use of these technologies in educational settings. Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",34
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"This study aims to develop a comprehensive understanding of how and why university students use artificial intelligence— particularly ChatGPT—and what implications this use has for their teaching–learning processes. First, it seeks to describe how academic workload and students’ current academic cycle influence the frequency and type of tool use, with particular attention to perceived cognitive difficulties—such as a sense of reduced information retention—that may mediate this relationship. At the same time, the study examines how academic performance, expressed through CUM, relates to the frequency of ChatGPT use and self-reported levels of dependence on the tool, linking these elements to perceptions of possible memory loss or deterioration of specific cognitive capacities. This perspective positions AI use not only as instrumental support but also as a potential source of cognitive concern. The analysis also incorporates age as a key dimension, with the aim of identifying whether there are meaningful differences in the use of ChatGPT for conceptual learning by age range, and how these differences intersect with academic overload and the self- directed learning approaches developed by each group. The goal is",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"aim of identifying whether there are meaningful differences in the use of ChatGPT for conceptual learning by age range, and how these differences intersect with academic overload and the self- directed learning approaches developed by each group. The goal is to determine whether certain student profiles—given their life stage and academic context—rely on AI more intensively or engage with it more critically. In addition, students’ employment status is considered a central factor for understanding reliance on ChatGPT for exam preparation and for managing academic deadlines. From this perspective, the study seeks to determine whether combining work and study promotes use patterns associated with time management or, conversely, relates to forms of procrastination that undermine the timely completion of assignments and assessments. Finally, these issues are analyzed in a context in which institutional regulations and ethics training regarding AI use appear limited. Accordingly, the research not only describes practices and perceptions but also provides evidence to inform debate about the role universities should play in supporting critical engagement, regulation, and pedagogical guidance for the use of AI tools in higher education.",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"limited. Accordingly, the research not only describes practices and perceptions but also provides evidence to inform debate about the role universities should play in supporting critical engagement, regulation, and pedagogical guidance for the use of AI tools in higher education. 2 Methods This study used a quantitative, cross-sectional, exploratory design. Inference was conducted using a decision-tree approach through the exhaustive CHAID algorithm (Chi-square Automatic Interaction Detection), which performs successive splits by comparing categories using adjusted chi-square tests and selecting the predictor with the greatest statistical discriminative power. Four models were estimated to identify sociodemographic and academic predictors that, with statistically significant evidence (p < 0.001), explain variability in AI use, procrastination, academic performance, academic overload, and perceived memory loss, producing hierarchical structures that are interpretable and consistent with the international literature (Chien-Liang and Ching-Lung, 2019; Gomes and Almeida, 2017; Díaz-Martínez et al., 2021). Because CHAID is an exploratory segmentation technique, the resulting decision trees were interpreted as models of statistical association rather than causal or predictive models. Following the recommendations of Zhang and Singer (2010) and Chou and Pham (2013),",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"et al., 2021). Because CHAID is an exploratory segmentation technique, the resulting decision trees were interpreted as models of statistical association rather than causal or predictive models. Following the recommendations of Zhang and Singer (2010) and Chou and Pham (2013), the algorithm was configured to identify the most statistically relevant partitions while preserving sufficiently large terminal nodes for reliable interpretation. Consequently, the resulting trees should be interpreted as identifying patterns of association within the analyzed sample rather than establishing causal relationships or producing generalizable predictive models. To achieve these aims, the study employed scales assessing ChatGPT use (Abbas et al., 2024), workload (Peterson et al. 1995), time pressure (Dapkus, 1985), and procrastination (Choi and Moran, 2009). These instruments were adapted into Spanish and validated by expert review (Bardin, 1996). Instead of testing causal hypotheses, the study formulated a set of exploratory research expectations derived from previous empirical literature. Specifically, the study examined whether academic workload, academic progression, age, employment status, and academic performance were statistically associated with different patterns of ChatGPT use and self-reported perceptions regarding dependence, procrastination, and memory",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"exploratory research expectations derived from previous empirical literature. Specifically, the study examined whether academic workload, academic progression, age, employment status, and academic performance were statistically associated with different patterns of ChatGPT use and self-reported perceptions regarding dependence, procrastination, and memory difficulties. Because of the exploratory and cross-sectional nature of the study, no causal relationships were assumed. The instrument included exogenous and endogenous variables, as follows: 1. Exogenous variables 1.1 Workload, measured on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). 1.2 Time pressure, reward sensitivity, and quality sensitivity, measured on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). 2. Endogenous variables 2.1 ChatGPT use, measured on a 6-point frequency scale (1 = never to 6 = always). 2.2 Procrastination and perceived memory loss, measured on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). For decision-tree construction, sociodemographic variables included sex, marital status, type of high school attended (institutional origin), academic cycle (year/term), grade point average expressed as the Coefficient of Units of Merit (CUM), employment",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"= strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). For decision-tree construction, sociodemographic variables included sex, marital status, type of high school attended (institutional origin), academic cycle (year/term), grade point average expressed as the Coefficient of Units of Merit (CUM), employment status, having children, age range, and CUM range. A total of 268 university students from seven private institutions participated using convenience sampling. The survey was administered via QuestionPro, and data were processed in SPSS v.23. Internal consistency was acceptable, internal consistency of the complete instrument was satisfactory (Adjusted Cronbach’s α = 0.824), indicating good reliability for the scales included in the questionnaire. Although the study relied on a convenience sample drawn exclusively from private higher education institutions, participants represent diverse academic programs, stages of university progression, and personal circumstances, providing sufficient variability for the exploratory objectives pursued through the CHAID segmentation models. As shown in Table 1, the sample presents a balanced distribution by sex, with a slight predominance of female participants (54.1%). Most respondents were single (84.3%), reflecting the demographic profile commonly observed among university populations in the country.",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,3,"segmentation models. As shown in Table 1, the sample presents a balanced distribution by sex, with a slight predominance of female participants (54.1%). Most respondents were single (84.3%), reflecting the demographic profile commonly observed among university populations in the country. Participants came from both public (51.9%) and private Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",59
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,4,"(47.8%) secondary education institutions, providing representation from the two principal educational sectors prior to university enrollment. Regarding academic characteristics, more than half of the participants were enrolled in bachelor’s degree programs (54.9%), while engineering students represented approximately one-third of the sample (35.8%). Students were distributed across different stages of their academic trajectory, although higher participation was observed among those enrolled in the second (34.3%), fourth (17.2%), eighth (14.2%), and tenth (17.9%) academic cycles, indicating greater representation of students in intermediate and advanced stages of their university education. The sample also reflects heterogeneous personal circumstances that may influence patterns of AI use. Although most participants were not employed (57.1%), a substantial proportion reported combining work and university studies (42.9%). Likewise, the vast majority did not have children (88.1%), suggesting that academic demands constituted the principal responsibility for most respondents during the period in which the information was collected. Overall, these characteristics provide a diverse exploratory sample suitable for examining associations between sociodemographic conditions, academic characteristics, and patterns of generative artificial intelligence use. 3 Results In the Salvadoran context, the decision trees",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,4,"period in which the information was collected. Overall, these characteristics provide a diverse exploratory sample suitable for examining associations between sociodemographic conditions, academic characteristics, and patterns of generative artificial intelligence use. 3 Results In the Salvadoran context, the decision trees identified consistent—though not uniform—patterns in the motivations, consequences, and concerns associated with the use of AI tools, particularly ChatGPT. Despite the country’s technological constraints—economic, institutional, and connectivity-related— the data show that students are not detached from these platforms and that their interaction with AI is mediated by perceived academic workload, study strategies, and emerging cognitive tensions. The models are presented below in descending order of explanatory power. 3.1 Model 1. Academic cycle–academic workload–memory problems–ChatGPT use Among the exploratory models estimated, this tree presented the highest discriminatory capacity within the analyzed sample, integrating motivational, cognitive, and instrumental dimensions of AI use across three hierarchical levels, with χ2 statistics of 2683.119 (df = 9), 1058.311 (df = 9), and 340.385 (df = 8), all p < 0.001. The root node shows the distribution by academic cycle, with even cycles 2, 4, 8,",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,4,"across three hierarchical levels, with χ2 statistics of 2683.119 (df = 9), 1058.311 (df = 9), and 340.385 (df = 8), all p < 0.001. The root node shows the distribution by academic cycle, with even cycles 2, 4, 8, and 10 predominating, indicating a sample concentrated in intermediate and advanced stages of the curriculum. The first split (see Table 2) was driven by agreement with the statement, “My academic workload is too heavy.” Students who disagreed or reported intermediate positions accounted for 73.1% of the total sample (n = 196), whereas the remaining 26.9% (n = 72) reported being somewhat or strongly in agreement with having an excessive workload. TABLE 1 Sociodemographic characteristics of the participants. Variable Category n % Sex Female 145 54.1 Male 123 45.9 Marital status Single 226 84.3 Married 19 7.1 Cohabiting 20 7.5 Other 3 1.1 High school sector Public 139 51.9 Private 128 47.8 No response 1 0.4 Degree program Bachelor’s degree 147 54.9 Engineering 96 35.8 Other 23 8.6 Professional doctorate 2 0.7 Currently employed Yes 115 42.9 No 153 57.1 Children",180
