A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"Within the group reporting lower perceived workload (Node 1), the next discriminating variable was agreement with the statement, “Lately, I cannot retain much in my mind.” Students who reported memory difficulties comprised nearly half of this node (Node 3; 48.9%, n = 131). Within this subgroup, ChatGPT use emerged as the variable that best differentiated participants according to the CHAID segmentation procedure. Node 5 concentrated students who used the tool a few times, almost always, or always. This node was characterized by a higher representation of the intermediate cycles described above, suggesting that AI adoption is particularly salient at stages when coursework becomes more complex but students have already developed some familiarity with the technology. Overall, the segmentation pattern suggests that perceived academic workload and self-reported memory difficulties jointly differentiate academic-cycle profiles in which more frequent ChatGPT use was observed. Overall, the observed pattern suggests that students who simultaneously reported higher academic workload and perceived memory difficulties also tended to report more frequent ChatGPT use within the analyzed sample. 3.2 Model 2. CUM–ChatGPT use and dependence–perceived memory loss This model",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"Overall, the observed pattern suggests that students who simultaneously reported higher academic workload and perceived memory difficulties also tended to report more frequent ChatGPT use within the analyzed sample. 3.2 Model 2. CUM–ChatGPT use and dependence–perceived memory loss This model is particularly relevant because it explores the association between academic performance, ChatGPT use, and self-reported cognitive perceptions. Although the statistical strength is more modest (χ2 = 162.025; 83.474; 25.171; all p < 0.001), the root node separates students with a CUM below 7 (22.0%, n = 54) from those with a CUM above 8 (78.0%, n = 213), indicating a sample clearly concentrated in higher academic performance. The first split is driven by the use of ChatGPT for academic activities (see Table 3). Node 1 groups students who reported using the tool a few times, almost never, sometimes, or always (79.4%, n = 212), whereas Node 2 includes those who reported never using it or using it almost always (20.6%, n = 55), illustrating two markedly different profiles of access and appropriation. Within the group with greater exposure to AI",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"(79.4%, n = 212), whereas Node 2 includes those who reported never using it or using it almost always (20.6%, n = 55), illustrating two markedly different profiles of access and appropriation. Within the group with greater exposure to AI (Node 1), the next split is self-reported dependence on ChatGPT. Students who indicated that they never, almost never, or almost always depend on the tool (Node 3) account for 59.9% of the total sample and show a high proportion of high CUM scores (≥ 8). This node then splits by perceived agreement with the statement, “I feel that I am losing my memory.” Those who endorsed agreement, strong disagreement, or intermediate positions (Node 5) maintained high rates of elevated CUM (84.4% with ≥ 8), indicating that high academic performance coexisted with subjective concerns regarding perceived memory difficulties. The observed segmentation pattern is consistent with previous empirical evidence suggesting that students with higher academic achievement may also report more frequent AI use and greater concern regarding perceived memory difficulties. However, these findings represent statistical associations observed in the present sample rather",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"pattern is consistent with previous empirical evidence suggesting that students with higher academic achievement may also report more frequent AI use and greater concern regarding perceived memory difficulties. However, these findings represent statistical associations observed in the present sample rather than causal relationships. 3.3 Model 3. Age range–academic overload–ChatGPT use for conceptual learning This model introduces a generational dimension as a moderator of the relationship between academic overload and AI use for learning (see Table 4). The root node shows that 70.1% of participants were 17–25 years old (n = 188) and 29.9% were 25 years or older (n = 80). The first split is driven by agreement with the statement, “I feel overwhelmed because of my studies.” Node 1 groups those who reported either disagreement or strong agreement, Node 2 comprises those who selected “somewhat agree/strongly disagree,” and Node 3 includes those who reported agreement. Within Node 2, the decisive variable is the use of ChatGPT to learn concepts (42.5% of the total sample). Students who reported using it sometimes, almost always, or always (Node 4) were predominantly 17–25",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"3 includes those who reported agreement. Within Node 2, the decisive variable is the use of ChatGPT to learn concepts (42.5% of the total sample). Students who reported using it sometimes, almost always, or always (Node 4) were predominantly 17–25 years old (66.7%), whereas the low or non-use group (Node 5) included a comparatively higher proportion of students aged 25 or older (40.4%). The observed pattern suggests that among students who report feeling overwhelmed by their studies, those aged 17–25 are more likely to use ChatGPT to learn course concepts than older students. This pattern may be associated with age-related differences in the adoption of digital learning resources, although the cross-sectional design does not allow the TABLE 3 CUM–ChatGPT use and dependence–perceived memory loss. Segment Rule (path) n Sample % CUM < 7% (n) CUM > 8% (n) Node 2 Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {Never, Almost always} 55 20.6 20.0 (11) 80.0 (44) Node 4 Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {A few times, Almost never, Sometimes, Always} and Q21—Depend on ChatGPT for studying ∈ {A few times,",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,5,"Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {Never, Almost always} 55 20.6 20.0 (11) 80.0 (44) Node 4 Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {A few times, Almost never, Sometimes, Always} and Q21—Depend on ChatGPT for studying ∈ {A few times, Sometimes} 52 19.5 25.0 (13) 75.0 (39) Node 5 Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {A few times, Almost never, Sometimes, Always} and Q21—Depend on ChatGPT for studying ∈ {Never, Almost never, Almost always} and Q24 —“I am losing my memory” ∈ {Somewhat agree, Strongly disagree, Neither agree nor disagree} 107 40.1 15.9 (17) 84.1 (90) Node 6 Q21—Use ChatGPT for academic activities ∈ {A few times, Almost never, Sometimes, Always} and Q21—Depend on ChatGPT for studying ∈ {Never, Almost never, Almost always} and Q24 —“I am losing my memory” ∈ {Strongly agree, Somewhat disagree} 53 19.9 24.5 (13) 75.5 (40) Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",152
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,6,"identification of the underlying mechanisms explaining these associations. 3.4 Model 4. Employment status–ChatGPT use for exams–difficulty meeting deadlines This tree captures the interaction among employment, strategic AI use for assessments, and time management. At the root node, 42.9% of participants reported being employed, whereas 57.1% were not. The first split is driven by how frequently students use ChatGPT to prepare for exams. Node 1 groups the categories “a few times,” “sometimes,” and “almost always,” accounting for 62.3% of the sample (see Table 5). Within Node 2, the next split is driven by agreement with the statement, “I find it difficult to submit my assignments and projects within the deadlines.” In Node 3 (neither agree nor disagree; strongly agree; strongly disagree), the distribution of employed and non-employed students is relatively balanced, whereas in Node 4 (somewhat disagree; somewhat agree) a higher proportion of students are not employed and simultaneously report difficulties meeting deadlines. These findings suggest that paid employment alone is not the factor most closely associated with difficulties meeting academic deadlines. Instead, such difficulty appears concentrated among students who use",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,6,"proportion of students are not employed and simultaneously report difficulties meeting deadlines. These findings suggest that paid employment alone is not the factor most closely associated with difficulties meeting academic deadlines. Instead, such difficulty appears concentrated among students who use ChatGPT less for exam preparation and who are not working, pointing to other factors related to study organization and self-regulation in the use of technology. Overall, the observed segmentation suggests that difficulties meeting academic deadlines were more frequently reported among students with limited use of ChatGPT for exam preparation, regardless of employment status. This association should be interpreted within the exploratory scope of the present study. 4 Discussion The scientific community continues to debate the motivations, consequences, and concerns arising from AI use in higher education. The evidence indicates that the rapid advancement of these technologies has introduced a critical inflection point in the relationship between technological innovation and curricular development, particularly as AI becomes embedded in teaching– learning processes and new forms of cognitive and academic interaction are consolidated. This context reinforces the need to understand not only potential",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,6,"inflection point in the relationship between technological innovation and curricular development, particularly as AI becomes embedded in teaching– learning processes and new forms of cognitive and academic interaction are consolidated. This context reinforces the need to understand not only potential pedagogical benefits but also the factors that shape AI use and the risks associated with it. Prior research agrees that AI can enhance multiple intelligences and facilitate learning processes; however, it also emphasizes that evidence remains insufficient to establish conclusive trends regarding AI’s implications in higher education. This requires reconsidering the motivations driving adoption. As Monge Vera et al. (2024) and Rodríguez Gil et al. (2024) note, the use of tools such as ChatGPT appears to reflect adaptation to increasingly digital environments rather than formal curricular planning, progressively shifting learning toward the digital tool itself rather than toward the development of the knowing subject. Within this international framework, the CHAID models generated in the present study provide empirical evidence from the Salvadoran context that aligns with several trends described in the literature. The Academic cycle–Academic workload– Memory problems–ChatGPT use model",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,6,"of the knowing subject. Within this international framework, the CHAID models generated in the present study provide empirical evidence from the Salvadoran context that aligns with several trends described in the literature. The Academic cycle–Academic workload– Memory problems–ChatGPT use model suggests that academic TABLE 4 Age range–academic overload–ChatGPT use for conceptual learning. Segment Rule (path) Sample % (n) 17–25% (n) ≥25% (n) Node 1 Q22—“I feel overwhelmed because of my studies” ∈ {Neither agree nor disagree, Strongly agree} 33.6 (90) 71.1 (64) 28.9 (26) Node 3 Q22—“I feel overwhelmed because of my studies” = {Somewhat agree} 23.9 (64) 81.2 (52) 18.8 (12) Node 4 Q22—“I feel overwhelmed because of my studies” ∈ {Somewhat disagree, Strongly disagree} and Q21— Use ChatGPT to learn concepts ∈ {Sometimes, Always, Almost always} 21.3 (57) 66.7 (38) 33.3 (19) Node 5 Q22—“I feel overwhelmed because of my studies” ∈ {Somewhat disagree, Strongly disagree} and Q21— Use ChatGPT to learn concepts ∈ {Almost never, Never, A few times} 21.3 (57) 59.6 (34) 40.4 (23) TABLE 5 Employment status–ChatGPT use for exams–difficulty meeting deadlines. Segment Rule (path)",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,6,"of my studies” ∈ {Somewhat disagree, Strongly disagree} and Q21— Use ChatGPT to learn concepts ∈ {Almost never, Never, A few times} 21.3 (57) 59.6 (34) 40.4 (23) TABLE 5 Employment status–ChatGPT use for exams–difficulty meeting deadlines. Segment Rule (path) Sample % (n) Yes % (n) No % (n) Node 1 Q21—Use ChatGPT to prepare for exams/tests ∈ {A few times, Sometimes, Almost always} 37.7 (101) 34.7 (35) 65.3 (66) Node 3 Q21—Use ChatGPT to prepare for exams/tests ∈ {Almost never, Never, Always} and Q23—“I find it difficult to submit assignments/projects on time” ∈ {Neither agree nor disagree, Strongly agree, Strongly disagree} 36.6 (98) 49.0 (48) 51.0 (50) Node 4 Q21—Use ChatGPT to prepare for exams/tests ∈ {Almost never, Never, Always} and Q23—“I find it difficult to submit assignments/projects on time” ∈ {Somewhat disagree, Somewhat agree} 25.7 (69) 46.4 (32) 53.6 (37) Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",153
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"progression, together with increasing coursework demands, is associated with higher perceived overload and more frequent ChatGPT use. Similarly, the CUM–ChatGPT use and dependence– Perceived memory loss model examines an emerging dimension in the literature: the relationship between academic performance, intensive AI use, and concern about cognitive effects. Recent studies (Jo, 2024; Zhang et al., 2024) indicate that ease of use and perceived usefulness increase the likelihood of dependence on these tools, which may negatively affect critical or analytical skills. These findings are consistent with previous international evidence and contribute additional exploratory evidence from El Salvador. Although these data do not support causal claims, these findings provide additional exploratory evidence that functional dependence on AI introduces new cognitive tensions for students, including those with high academic achievement. The Age range–Academic overload–ChatGPT use for conceptual learning model, in turn, highlights the generational dimension of the phenomenon. International studies suggest that younger students tend to integrate AI into self-directed learning more naturally, particularly when facing time constraints or comprehension difficulties (Jo, 2024). In line with this, the CHAID results show that students aged",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"dimension of the phenomenon. International studies suggest that younger students tend to integrate AI into self-directed learning more naturally, particularly when facing time constraints or comprehension difficulties (Jo, 2024). In line with this, the CHAID results show that students aged 17–25 are most likely to use ChatGPT to learn academic concepts, especially when they perceive overload. This pattern may reflect differences in learning preferences and technology adoption among younger students. The Employment status–ChatGPT use for exams–Difficulty meeting deadlines model adds another important nuance. While prior work may reflect that AI can improve academic and professional efficiency (Chan, 2023; Mahapatra, 2024), it also indicates that insufficiently regulated dependence may contribute to procrastinative behaviors or difficulties with time management (Abbas et al., 2024; Shahzad et al., 2024). The present results show that students with lower AI use—regardless of whether they work—report greater difficulty meeting deadlines. This exploratory pattern is compatible with the interpretation that some students incorporate AI as an adaptive academic resource under demanding learning conditions. At the international level, Villegas and Rivas (2025) argue that AI may strengthen multiple intelligences,",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"difficulty meeting deadlines. This exploratory pattern is compatible with the interpretation that some students incorporate AI as an adaptive academic resource under demanding learning conditions. At the international level, Villegas and Rivas (2025) argue that AI may strengthen multiple intelligences, while also being associated with risks such as procrastination, academic stress, and privacy threats. Findings from this sample of Salvadoran university students reflect this same complexity: ChatGPT use appears as a strategy to cope with overload and perceived cognitive difficulties, yet it also introduces concerns related to dependence, privacy, and perceived weakening of internal cognitive skills. In Latin America, studies such as García Sánchez (2023), Solano-Barliza et al. (2024), and Zukerfeld et al. (2023) depict a heterogeneous landscape in which ChatGPT use remains incipient, fragmented, and largely driven by curiosity or necessity. El Salvador appears to be in a similar position: although access is limited (Briñis-Zambrano, 2024), there is basic familiarity with AI tools and a perception that they can improve learning; however, ethical concerns, limited training, and cognitive tensions persist—patterns that are consistent with the CHAID models reported here.",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"position: although access is limited (Briñis-Zambrano, 2024), there is basic familiarity with AI tools and a perception that they can improve learning; however, ethical concerns, limited training, and cognitive tensions persist—patterns that are consistent with the CHAID models reported here. Overall, the evidence suggests that AI use in Salvadoran higher education—similar to international trends—is heterogeneous and motivated more by situational factors (workload, time pressure, perceived cognitive difficulties) than by planned institutional training processes. The findings also reveal ethical and cognitive tensions that converge with an emerging scientific consensus: AI adoption requires regulation, faculty–student training, and clear ethical frameworks to guide responsible use within the educational process. Based on the models developed, this study provides a nuanced picture of how and why university students rely on artificial intelligence—particularly ChatGPT—and what tensions emerge within AI-mediated teaching–learning processes. With respect to academic workload and academic cycle, the models show that progression through the program is associated with an increasing perception of overload and, in parallel, with more frequent and strategic use of ChatGPT. The tool appears to function as a compensatory resource to",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"and academic cycle, the models show that progression through the program is associated with an increasing perception of overload and, in parallel, with more frequent and strategic use of ChatGPT. The tool appears to function as a compensatory resource to manage both the volume of assignments and perceived cognitive difficulties, especially the sense of inadequate information retention. In more advanced cycles—and in contexts combining high academic demands, reduced peer support, or low motivation— AI shifts from being a matter of curiosity to becoming instrumental support used to study complex content, organize work, and reduce uncertainty around assessments. When examining academic performance (CUM), frequency of use, and dependence on ChatGPT, an ambivalent pattern emerges. On the one hand, higher-performing students tend to report more routine use of the tool in academic activities, suggesting that, in some cases, AI is functionally integrated as a complement to studying. On the other hand, within this same group, a subset reports concern about potential memory loss or diminished retention capacity, particularly when high dependence on ChatGPT is perceived. Although the data do not allow causal",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"as a complement to studying. On the other hand, within this same group, a subset reports concern about potential memory loss or diminished retention capacity, particularly when high dependence on ChatGPT is perceived. Although the data do not allow causal inference, they make a clear tension visible: the same tool that helps sustain or improve performance also raises suspicions about its medium-term cognitive effects, echoing international debates about intensive use of automated systems. Age-range differences reinforce this contextualized interpretation. Models that combine age, perceived overload, and ChatGPT use for conceptual learning suggest that younger students—and those who feel most overwhelmed—use AI more frequently to understand concepts that were unclear in class. This pattern may indicate that ChatGPT appears to function as an extension of self-directed learning resources, particularly for students with less university experience who face high levels of academic demand. However, self-learning styles are not homogeneous: profiles that use the tool selectively and critically coexist with others who integrate it more dependently, partially substituting deep reading, synthesis, and reflection. Employment status adds another layer of complexity. Models linking employment,",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,7,"demand. However, self-learning styles are not homogeneous: profiles that use the tool selectively and critically coexist with others who integrate it more dependently, partially substituting deep reading, synthesis, and reflection. Employment status adds another layer of complexity. Models linking employment, ChatGPT use for exams, and deadline compliance show that students who combine work and study tend to use AI as a time-management strategy, particularly to prepare for assessments and organize tasks during high-demand periods. In some nodes, this use is associated with greater difficulty meeting deadlines, suggesting procrastination patterns in which ChatGPT becomes both a quick-fix tool and a Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",110
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"potential facilitator of delay by providing immediate answers that postpone autonomous work. In other cases, however, AI use appears to support better organization, reinforcing the notion that what matters is not the tool per se but how it is integrated into study routines. Taken together, the findings indicate that motivations to use ChatGPT range from curiosity to the need for immediate support under academic overload and the pursuit of efficiency, while perceived consequences lie on a continuum from improved performance and understanding to concerns about memory loss, dependence, and procrastination. Ethical and regulatory concerns, in turn, do not emerge as decisive factors shaping student behavior, suggesting the absence—or weak influence—of clear institutional policies on AI use. This regulatory gap strengthens the need for higher education institutions to play an active role in fostering critical engagement, establishing explicit guidelines, and creating spaces for reflection on the cognitive, ethical, and pedagogical implications of AI, so that its role in teaching–learning processes no longer depends solely on individual decisions made under academic pressure. Based on the models developed, the analysis shows that those",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"spaces for reflection on the cognitive, ethical, and pedagogical implications of AI, so that its role in teaching–learning processes no longer depends solely on individual decisions made under academic pressure. Based on the models developed, the analysis shows that those with the greatest explanatory power are the ones that simultaneously integrate academic trajectory variables (cycle and performance), psychosocial conditions (motivation, support, perceived overload), and patterns of ChatGPT use. These models indicate that as students progress through their programs and face increasing demands, they tend to intensify AI use as an instrumental resource to manage assignment volume, prepare for exams, or clarify content not understood in class. This pattern may position ChatGPT as a compensatory mechanism in response to academic complexity, particularly among profiles that combine high workload, lower peer support, or greater difficulty meeting deadlines. Consistent with regional studies, the use of these tools does not appear to emerge from systematic training processes, but rather from individual decisions aimed at alleviating immediate academic pressure. Accordingly, the trees that link performance (CUM) and dependence on ChatGPT suggest a nuanced pattern: higher-performing",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"use of these tools does not appear to emerge from systematic training processes, but rather from individual decisions aimed at alleviating immediate academic pressure. Accordingly, the trees that link performance (CUM) and dependence on ChatGPT suggest a nuanced pattern: higher-performing students typically report more frequent AI use for academic activities, yet a subset of this same group expresses concern about potential effects on memory and retention. This coexistence appears consistent with tensions characteristic of environments in which AI is adopted without clear pedagogical guidance, aligning with international literature documenting ethical and cognitive doubts associated with sustained use of automated tools. The models also delineate specific profiles—such as younger students who more often rely on ChatGPT to learn concepts that were unclear in class; working students who use AI as a strategy to reconcile employment and study; or groups with lower motivation and less peer support who adopt a more instrumental use—yet differences across nodes are not sufficiently homogeneous to support categorical claims. The observed variability aligns with research indicating that AI’s impact in higher education depends strongly on institutional context,",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"motivation and less peer support who adopt a more instrumental use—yet differences across nodes are not sufficiently homogeneous to support categorical claims. The observed variability aligns with research indicating that AI’s impact in higher education depends strongly on institutional context, prior technological capital, and the training opportunities accompanying adoption. Overall, the four models suggest that AI use among Salvadoran university students reflects early-stage adoption driven primarily by curiosity, operational need under academic workload, and performance anxiety, rather than by instructor- guided appropriation. Although consistent patterns emerge— such as increased AI use in the presence of academic overload, difficulties organizing study, or lower levels of support—the findings remain sufficiently dispersed to rule out a single narrative about AI’s effects on learning. Nevertheless, taken together, the four exploratory models consistently point toward: the urgent need to develop ethical guidelines, clear regulatory frameworks, and explicit pedagogical strategies that support responsible and critical AI use, reduce access gaps, and strengthen the competencies needed to integrate these tools without undermining cognitive autonomy. Finally, the ethical and regulatory dimension of AI use did not yield a",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"and explicit pedagogical strategies that support responsible and critical AI use, reduce access gaps, and strengthen the competencies needed to integrate these tools without undermining cognitive autonomy. Finally, the ethical and regulatory dimension of AI use did not yield a robust predictive model, indicating that institutional regulations—policies, rules, or specific AI guidelines—are not functioning as decisive factors in student practices. This finding may reflect both the absence of such instruments at the institutional level and limited dissemination through systematic training processes. In light of the evidence, this omission reinforces the need for higher education institutions not only to adopt regulatory frameworks but also to translate them into concrete learning experiences in which the academic, cognitive, and ethical implications of generative AI use are discussed openly. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement Ethical approval was not required for the studies involving humans because the study followed ethical procedures to ensure data anonymity. It collected participants’ perceptions on several related issues, and the data",180
A094,A094.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769,8,"available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement Ethical approval was not required for the studies involving humans because the study followed ethical procedures to ensure data anonymity. It collected participants’ perceptions on several related issues, and the data cannot be linked to individual identities. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Written informed consent was obtained from the individual(s) for the publication of any potentially identifiable images or data included in this article. Author contributions MQ: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Funding acquisition, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Software, Supervision, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. EP: Formal analysis, Methodology, Validation, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. Quintanilla Villegas and Pineda Rivas 10.3389/feduc.2026.1903769 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",146
