A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,1,"EDITED BY Elizabeth Wood, University of Notre Dame, United States REVIEWED BY Gila Kurtz, Holon Institute of Technology, Israel Ross English, University of Southampton, United Kingdom *CORRESPONDENCE Elizabeth Yuriev elizabeth.yuriev@monash.edu Jae Pyun jae.pyun@monash.edu †These authors share ﬁrst authorship ‡These authors share last authorship RECEIVED 27 February 2026 REVISED 31 May 2026 ACCEPTED 08 June 2026 PUBLISHED 24 June 2026 CITATION Lo S, Lea T, Mavromoustakos A, Joseph B, Yii M, Exintaris B, Karunaratne N, Pearson DL, Ritchie T, Yuriev E and Pyun J (2026) Co-creating custom GPTs: an autoethnographic study of undergraduate students as partners in generative AI innovation. Front. Educ. 11:1818781. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Lo, Lea, Mavromoustakos, Joseph, Yii, Exintaris, Karunaratne, Pearson, Ritchie, Yuriev and Pyun. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,1,"reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Co-creating custom GPTs: an autoethnographic study of undergraduate students as partners in generative AI innovation Samuel Lo 1†, Talyssa Lea 1†, Alexandros Mavromoustakos 1, Ben Joseph 1, Megan Yii 1, Betty Exintaris 1, Nilushi Karunaratne 1, Danielle L. Pearson 2, Tom Ritchie 2, Elizabeth Yuriev 1*‡ and Jae Pyun 1*‡ 1Faculty of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences, Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences Education, Monash University, Parkville, Victoria, Australia, 2Department of Chemistry, University of Warwick, Coventry, United Kingdom This study explores how undergraduate students co-created and evaluated custom Generative AI (GenAI) tools within a students-as-partners (SaP) framework. Undergraduate participants, within a Summer Scholarship program and in a placement coursework unit, collaborated with academic staff to design, test, and reﬁne custom Generative Pre-trained Transformer chatbots (cGPTs), aligned with pedagogical goals. Using autoethnographic methodology and thematic analysis of student-authored",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,1,"Undergraduate participants, within a Summer Scholarship program and in a placement coursework unit, collaborated with academic staff to design, test, and reﬁne custom Generative Pre-trained Transformer chatbots (cGPTs), aligned with pedagogical goals. Using autoethnographic methodology and thematic analysis of student-authored reﬂections, we examine how students experienced this process as a form of partnership-driven learning. An inductive analysis of summer scholarship autoethnographic logs reveals insights into the iterative, unpredictable, and creative nature of working with GenAI. Abductive analysis of placement reﬂections, guided by SaP principles, highlights themes of agency, reciprocity, reﬂexivity, identity shift, authenticity, adaptability, and technology-pedagogy nexus. Synthesising both perspectives, we argue that co-creation with GenAI can extend the SaP paradigm to include GenAI tools. We also reﬂect on the ethical and pedagogical implications of GenAI in higher education and advocate for student voice as central to responsible innovation. By embedding partnership pedagogy into GenAI co-creation, this study offers a model for more inclusive, critical, and transformative engagement with emerging technologies in university settings. KEYWORDS co-creation, custom GPTs, generative AI, reﬂective practice, students-as-partners, undergraduate education 1 Introduction The rapid emergence",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,1,"embedding partnership pedagogy into GenAI co-creation, this study offers a model for more inclusive, critical, and transformative engagement with emerging technologies in university settings. KEYWORDS co-creation, custom GPTs, generative AI, reﬂective practice, students-as-partners, undergraduate education 1 Introduction The rapid emergence of generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GenAI) tools is transforming higher education worldwide (Belkina et al., 2025). Since the public release of advanced AI chatbots, such as ChatGPT in late 2022, universities have grappled with both the opportunities and challenges these tools pose for teaching and learning (Jensen et al., 2025). While GenAI systems can provide personalised learning support, quick TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 24 June 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",112
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"feedback, and novel ways to engage students, signiﬁcant concerns and issues exist when it comes to academic integrity, assessment design, and the preparedness of educators and students to use GenAI ethically, critically, and effectively (Matjola, 2025). Early discourse around GenAI in higher education frequently positioned students either as potential cheaters or as victims of these technologies, reﬂecting fears of GenAI undermining learning or facilitating plagiarism (Jensen et al., 2025). However, more recent scholarship increasingly argues that GenAI should be treated as a catalyst for much-needed pedagogical innovation particularly in assessment and feedback, rather than merely resisting or only mitigating the risks (Corbin et al., 2025b; Zhan et al., 2025). Within this shift, there is growing recognition that students themselves need to be actively involved in this conversation, shifting from being seen as passive users (or risks) to becoming partners in co-creating and harnessing AI for learning (Pallant et al., 2025). In parallel with the AI revolution, higher education has witnessed an expansion of students-as-partners (SaP) ideas and initiatives that reposition students as co-creators of knowledge, curriculum, and educational innovation (Cook-Sather",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"co-creating and harnessing AI for learning (Pallant et al., 2025). In parallel with the AI revolution, higher education has witnessed an expansion of students-as-partners (SaP) ideas and initiatives that reposition students as co-creators of knowledge, curriculum, and educational innovation (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Godbold et al., 2022; Matthews, 2017; Matthews et al., 2018; Williams and Ingle, 2025). SaP approaches position students and staff working together as partners in a collaborative, reciprocal process, rather than maintaining traditional hierarchical roles. This partnership ethos aligns well with the pedagogical implications of GenAI: rather than instructors alone determining how GenAI tools should be used (or banned) in courses, students can contribute their insights and creativity to co-designing GenAI applications that enhance their learning. The present study sits at the intersection of these trends. At Monash University, we engaged undergraduate science students through a Summer Scholarship program (see Methods/Participants) and an Advanced Professional Experience placement unit (BPS3072) (see Methods/ Participants) in co-creation of custom Generative Pre-trained Transformers (cGPTs) tailored to speciﬁc units (BPS3072 - Advanced Professional Experience [Online], 2025; Summer and Winter Vacation Research Scholarship",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"Scholarship program (see Methods/Participants) and an Advanced Professional Experience placement unit (BPS3072) (see Methods/ Participants) in co-creation of custom Generative Pre-trained Transformers (cGPTs) tailored to speciﬁc units (BPS3072 - Advanced Professional Experience [Online], 2025; Summer and Winter Vacation Research Scholarship Program [Online], 2026). In ﬁve distinct subjects (physiology, physical chemistry, organic chemistry, scientiﬁc inquiry, and polymer science), small teams of students partnered with academics to design, tailor, test, and optimise GPT-based chatbots specialised for the content and learning outcomes of each unit, or parts thereof. This initiative raises important questions about how GenAI can be woven into curriculum innovation through genuine student–staff partnership. Our study is informed by a dual theoretical lens. First, we draw on students-as-partners pedagogy to frame the collaboration between students and academics in designing GenAI tools. Second, we employ an autoethnographic methodology, drawing on student-authored reﬂections to capture participants’ experiences and meaning-making throughout the project. Using these two lenses, the study seeks to illuminate not only the practical outcomes of co-creating custom GenAI tools, but also the lived experience and meaning-making of the students involved. In",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"to capture participants’ experiences and meaning-making throughout the project. Using these two lenses, the study seeks to illuminate not only the practical outcomes of co-creating custom GenAI tools, but also the lived experience and meaning-making of the students involved. In what follows, we provide a brief overview of relevant literature on GenAI in higher education, the students- as-partners paradigm, the use of autoethnography to capture student voice and reﬂexivity, and emerging work on co-creation of curriculum and AI tools with students. This review will establish the context and rationale for our inquiry, leading to the research questions guiding this study. 1.1 Generative AI in higher education: opportunities and concerns In recent years there has been an explosion of interest in GenAI within higher education research and practice. GenAI models, especially large language models (LLMs) like GPTs are being increasingly integrated into educational settings, prompting both enthusiasm and caution. On the positive side, studies highlight that GenAI tools can provide immediate academic support, personalised learning experiences, and skill development opportunities for students and time-saving assistance for educators (Labadze et al., 2023).",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"into educational settings, prompting both enthusiasm and caution. On the positive side, studies highlight that GenAI tools can provide immediate academic support, personalised learning experiences, and skill development opportunities for students and time-saving assistance for educators (Labadze et al., 2023). Early adopters have experimented with AI to generate quiz questions, explain complex concepts in new ways, or act as virtual tutors, reporting improved student engagement in some cases (Batista et al., 2024). At the same time, signiﬁcant concerns temper the enthusiasm around GenAI in academia. Issues of reliability and accuracy of GenAI-generated content and the ethical ramiﬁcations and environmental concerns of GenAI use have been widely noted (Slattery et al., 2024). University instructors initially responded to the sudden availability of tools like ChatGPT with a range of strategies from outright bans and stricter exam proctoring to the use of controversial AI-detection software. But such measures have proven short-term or ineffective. A consensus is emerging that instead of trying to shield higher education from GenAI, educators should reshape assessment and teaching practices in light of AI’s capabilities (Corbin et al., 2025a).",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"AI-detection software. But such measures have proven short-term or ineffective. A consensus is emerging that instead of trying to shield higher education from GenAI, educators should reshape assessment and teaching practices in light of AI’s capabilities (Corbin et al., 2025a). Thus, the literature portrays GenAI as a double-edged sword: a disruptive force that could threaten academic integrity and quality if misused, but also a powerful tool to enhance education if used thoughtfully. Importantly, recent analyses have noted that early narratives about AI in higher education tended to marginalise the student perspective, often casting students as plagiarists-in- waiting or as passive recipients of GenAI impacts. Jensen et al. (2025) found that initial commentaries largely omitted student agency, prompting calls for greater involvement of students in shaping how GenAI is implemented in education (Jensen et al., 2025). This gap in the discourse provides a segue into the value of partnership approaches in the context of AI: rather than top- down decisions, engaging students as partners could ensure GenAI is integrated in pedagogically sound and student- centred ways. 1.2 Students-as-partners: values, principles, and",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,2,"provides a segue into the value of partnership approaches in the context of AI: rather than top- down decisions, engaging students as partners could ensure GenAI is integrated in pedagogically sound and student- centred ways. 1.2 Students-as-partners: values, principles, and challenges Engaging students as partners in higher education is a pedagogical approach that has gained a substantial momentum over the past decade. At its core, SaP is deﬁned as a “collaborative, reciprocal process through which all participants have the opportunity to contribute equally, although not necessarily in the same ways, to curricular or pedagogical Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",103
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"conceptualization, decision-making, implementation, investigation, or analysis” (Cook-Sather et al., 2014). In contrast to traditional teacher-student relationships, a partnership model treats students not as passive consumers and customers and positions them as co-producers and co-creators of knowledge (Streeting and Wise, 2009). The core values of SaP include respect, reciprocity, and shared responsibility (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Healey et al., 2014; Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017). It emphasises student agency and ownership (Cook-Sather et al., 2014). Beneﬁts of SaP include awareness outcomes, such as reﬂexivity and developing metacognitive awareness and a stronger sense of identity. A robust body of literature documents SaP initiatives. Numerous studies report that involving students in curriculum design, teaching, research, or assessment can enhance student engagement, motivation, and ownership of learning (Mercer- Mapstone et al., 2017). Students often develop greater metacognitive skills and conﬁdence, and academics report enriched perspectives on teaching as a result of the dialogue with students as peers. Partnership can also contribute to a sense of belonging and empowerment for students, particularly when their voices are genuinely heard and acted upon in educational decision-making. Alongside the",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"on teaching as a result of the dialogue with students as peers. Partnership can also contribute to a sense of belonging and empowerment for students, particularly when their voices are genuinely heard and acted upon in educational decision-making. Alongside the beneﬁts of partnership, tensions and conﬂicts can also emerge when attempting it in practice (Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017). Godbold et al. (2022) found that as power dynamics shifted during curriculum co-creation, students experienced both internal conﬂict (questioning their own role) and interpersonal conﬂict (negotiating roles with peers and the instructor). Such conﬂict, the authors argue, is not necessarily negative. It can be a genuine part of learning and change, if properly acknowledged and navigated (Godbold et al., 2022). In summary, the students- as-partners approach offers a powerful framework for curriculum development. It is highly pertinent when considering partnerships in emerging areas like AI tool development, where technical expertise, pedagogical goals, and power relations intersect. 1.3 Autoethnography as a lens for student voice and reﬂexivity In educational research, autoethnography has emerged as a valuable method for centring personal experience and reﬂective",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"areas like AI tool development, where technical expertise, pedagogical goals, and power relations intersect. 1.3 Autoethnography as a lens for student voice and reﬂexivity In educational research, autoethnography has emerged as a valuable method for centring personal experience and reﬂective narrative as a source of insight (Gannon, 2020). Autoethnography is a qualitative research approach where the researcher’s own lived experience is systematically analysed within its educational or institutional context. As a reﬂective practice, autoethnography can enhance critical reﬂection and transformative learning for the individual(s) involved. More generally, collaborative autoethnography has been shown to activate critical reﬂection among participants (Chang et al., 2012). Although our study does not claim to be a full collaborative autoethnography across all participants, collaborative autoethnography provides a useful methodological reference point because of its emphasis on dialogic reﬂection, shared meaning-making, and the systematic interpretation of lived experience. Sharing and collectively interrogating personal narratives can surface the nuanced dimensions. For instance, Dollinger et al. (2023) engaged student partners and faculty in a collective autoethnographic research project, thereby “going under the hood” of the partnership process itself to",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"experience. Sharing and collectively interrogating personal narratives can surface the nuanced dimensions. For instance, Dollinger et al. (2023) engaged student partners and faculty in a collective autoethnographic research project, thereby “going under the hood” of the partnership process itself to examine power, identity, and learning in that collaboration. Such approaches align with calls for more reﬂective research methodologies in higher education, ones that do not treat students merely as subjects of research but as co-narrators of the educational story (Dollinger et al., 2023). In the present study, autoethnographic reﬂection offers an appropriate means to capture how student co-creators experienced, interpreted, and made sense of designing and evaluating GenAI tools. By inviting students to document their experiences, emotions, and evolving thinking, and by treating these reﬂections as qualitative data, we foreground student perspectives on the co- creation of custom GPTs. This method provides a channel for student partners’ voice and agency in evaluating the pedagogical experiment, in line with our SaP approach. It also facilitates a high degree of reﬂexivity whereby students can critically examine their own assumptions, expectations, and learning",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"method provides a channel for student partners’ voice and agency in evaluating the pedagogical experiment, in line with our SaP approach. It also facilitates a high degree of reﬂexivity whereby students can critically examine their own assumptions, expectations, and learning throughout the project. The use of autoethnography thus helps answer recent calls (Jensen et al., 2025) for deeper inquiry into not just what educational innovations achieve, but how they are experienced by participants at a human level. 1.4 Co-creating curriculum and AI tools with students The concept of students and academics co-creating curriculum content has steadily gained traction, supported by the broader SaP literature described above. Co-creation in this context can range from partnering with students to redesign a single course module, to involving students in developing program-level curriculum or open educational resources. Research indicates that when students co-create aspects of the curriculum, it can lead to materials and learning activities that are more relevant, engaging, and accessible to their peers (Godbold et al., 2022). Students bring unique insights into the learner experience, which can inform the design of explanations,",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"of the curriculum, it can lead to materials and learning activities that are more relevant, engaging, and accessible to their peers (Godbold et al., 2022). Students bring unique insights into the learner experience, which can inform the design of explanations, examples, and supports that educators might not have considered. For example, Bovill (2019) outlines a co-creation typology wherein students might help devise course content, assessment tasks, or learning resources, often leading to increased motivation and deeper learning for those involved in the process (and sometimes for their classmates as well) (Bovill, 2019). Co-creation of curriculum has been documented across various disciplines from co-designing science lab activities to co-authoring study guides, typically yielding positive outcomes such as improved student conﬁdence, enhanced teacher–student relationships, and a shift toward more learner-centred pedagogy (Godbold et al., 2022). Research on student co-creation of AI tools particularly with cohorts without formal computer science training remains nascent and is only beginning to emerge (Madhav, 2025). The integration of student partnership into technology development often referred to as participatory design or co-design, has been tried in related domains,",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,3,"particularly with cohorts without formal computer science training remains nascent and is only beginning to emerge (Madhav, 2025). The integration of student partnership into technology development often referred to as participatory design or co-design, has been tried in related domains, offering a glimpse of both the promise and challenges of such collaborations. For example, Durall Gazulla et al. (2023) reported on a project that collaboratively designed a chatbot to support self-regulated learning in higher Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",83
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"education. Using an action research framework, the design team included both students and instructors who worked together through workshops, questionnaires, and iterative prototyping to develop the chatbot’s features. This approach ensured that student users’ needs and perspectives were embedded in the tool from the ground up, arguably making the chatbot more pedagogically appropriate and user-friendly. The study highlighted several challenges in the co-design process, such as the need to bridge gaps in technical understanding between stakeholders, to make tacit knowledge explicit, and to build trust so that decision- making was truly democratic (Durall Gazulla et al., 2023). Complementing this emerging work, recent studies further strengthen the rationale for positioning learners as co-designers of AI-enabled educational ecosystems where ethical governance, relational dynamics, and assessment integrity are at stake (Chang et al., 2025). By centring students this work illustrates how co-design can move beyond usability toward value-driven design principles that address privacy, trust, and power in AI- mediated learning relationships. Williams and Ingle (2025) similarly operationalise SaP in the AI era through assessment co-creation, where student–staff partners collaboratively evaluated ChatGPT outputs against",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"move beyond usability toward value-driven design principles that address privacy, trust, and power in AI- mediated learning relationships. Williams and Ingle (2025) similarly operationalise SaP in the AI era through assessment co-creation, where student–staff partners collaboratively evaluated ChatGPT outputs against co-designed criteria; their ﬁndings highlight both the pervasiveness of GenAI’s implications for assessment and the distinctive value students bring in surfacing equity, authenticity, and alignment concerns that may be under-recognised in staff-only redesign processes (Williams and Ingle, 2025). Together, these studies reinforce the scholarly case that participatory approaches are not an “add-on” to GenAI integration but a methodological and ethical necessity supporting more trustworthy, context-sensitive, and pedagogically coherent AI innovations across diverse educational settings. However, co-design does not mean that all forms of expertise are identical or interchangeable. The trustworthiness of the design process depends on combining students’ legitimate learner expertise with academics’ disciplinary and pedagogical knowledge. This aligns with Lave and Wenger’s notion of legitimate peripheral participation, whereby newcomers participate meaningfully in an authentic practice, while developing fuller participation through guided engagement with more experienced members of the community",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"expertise with academics’ disciplinary and pedagogical knowledge. This aligns with Lave and Wenger’s notion of legitimate peripheral participation, whereby newcomers participate meaningfully in an authentic practice, while developing fuller participation through guided engagement with more experienced members of the community (Lave and Wenger, 1991). This framing also aligns with principles of cognitive apprenticeship, in which expert educators scaffold participation through modelling, coaching, and feedback to ensure that learners’ contributions develop within established disciplinary and pedagogical standards (Collins et al., 2018). In an era of rapid AI evolution, involving students in creating or customising AI tools, such as in our case, unit-speciﬁc GPTs, may also serve a secondary beneﬁt: it builds students’ capacity in digital literacies and critical understanding of AI. Rather than being passive consumers of EdTech, students become active shapers. Such experience can demystify AI and empower them to use such tools more critically and effectively in the future. It is worth re-iterating that co-creative endeavours in the AI domain remain nascent, and there is scant literature directly examining students co-developing AI within their coursework. Our study addresses this",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"use such tools more critically and effectively in the future. It is worth re-iterating that co-creative endeavours in the AI domain remain nascent, and there is scant literature directly examining students co-developing AI within their coursework. Our study addresses this gap by exploring a novel scenario: students partnered with academics to create custom AI chatbots tailored to their own units. By drawing on principles from SaP and documenting the process autoethnographically, we aim to contribute knowledge about the pedagogical value and practicalities of co-creating AI tools for learning. This investigation also responds to recent calls for partnership approaches to be applied to digital innovation in higher education, ensuring that student voices guide the ethical and effective integration of GenAI into the curriculum. 1.5 Study rationale and research questions The above literature review highlights several key gaps and opportunities at the intersection of GenAI and student partnership. GenAI is poised to reshape how higher education operates, yet much of the early integration has been top-down, with limited input from students beyond use-case feedback. Meanwhile, the SaP literature underscores the beneﬁts of",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"intersection of GenAI and student partnership. GenAI is poised to reshape how higher education operates, yet much of the early integration has been top-down, with limited input from students beyond use-case feedback. Meanwhile, the SaP literature underscores the beneﬁts of engaging students in educational design and decision making, but has only begun to extend these principles into technology co-creation. Additionally, while autoethnographic and other qualitative studies have elevated student voices in various contexts, in-depth accounts of students’ experiences co-creating AI tools as part of their learning are lacking, if not completely absent. In light of these gaps, the present study is designed to investigate what happens when undergraduate students and university educators partner to develop custom AI (GPT) applications for their courses/units, in a way that is collaborative and empowering for students. The dual theoretical lens of SaP and autoethnography allows us to examine both the process (partnership dynamics, co-creation challenges) and the experience (student voice, identity, and learning) of this innovative pedagogical endeavour. The aim of this study was to illustrate a model SaP innovation in curriculum development, one",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"allows us to examine both the process (partnership dynamics, co-creation challenges) and the experience (student voice, identity, and learning) of this innovative pedagogical endeavour. The aim of this study was to illustrate a model SaP innovation in curriculum development, one that could be applied not only to AI tools but to a range of educational technologies and practices. Ultimately, the study seeks to contribute to a more nuanced understanding of how student-staff partnerships can shape the future of teaching and learning in the AI era. To guide this inquiry, we pose the following research questions: • RQ1 (the mechanics of partnership): How do students and academics engage in the co-creation of custom GPT-based learning tools, and what does this collaborative process look like in practice? • RQ2 (partnership’s impact on participants): What beneﬁts, challenges, and learning outcomes do participants perceive from co-creating AI tools for speciﬁc undergraduate science courses/units? • RQ3 (reﬂective, meaning-making dimension): How does an autoethnographic, reﬂective approach capture the student partners’ voice and development throughout the co- creation project, and in what ways does it enrich our",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,4,"from co-creating AI tools for speciﬁc undergraduate science courses/units? • RQ3 (reﬂective, meaning-making dimension): How does an autoethnographic, reﬂective approach capture the student partners’ voice and development throughout the co- creation project, and in what ways does it enrich our understanding of the partnership experience? 2 Methods 2.1 Project context and setting This study was conducted at Monash University, a large research-intensive institution in Australia. The project centred Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",77
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"on co-creating seven custom GPTs for six undergraduate units spanning diverse disciplines within the Bachelor of Pharmaceutical Science program: physiology (1 and 2), physical chemistry 2, medicinal chemistry 1, scientiﬁc inquiry, and polymer science (Knowledge and Socratic). Each target unit represents a distinct domain, providing a rich context to explore AI integration in learning and teaching. The initiative was grounded in a students-as-partners framework, intentionally positioning undergraduate students as co-creators rather than research subjects or purely as consumers of educational innovation (Dollinger et al., 2023). In line with SaP principles, students were actively involved as collaborators or colleagues in the design process, working alongside academics to develop educational AI tools. This partnership approach ensured that the project drew on both academic expertise and student lived experience in shaping the custom GPTs, aligning with Monash’s strategic emphasis on inclusive, innovative teaching. 2.2 Participants Participants in the co-creation project included both undergraduate student partners and academic staff partners. In total, the project engaged ﬁve undergraduate students and ﬁve academics (unit coordinators and content experts) across six units. Students were recruited through two",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"2.2 Participants Participants in the co-creation project included both undergraduate student partners and academic staff partners. In total, the project engaged ﬁve undergraduate students and ﬁve academics (unit coordinators and content experts) across six units. Students were recruited through two avenues designed to incorporate both voluntary research involvement and formal coursework integration. First, two students (TL and SL) were selected via Monash’s Summer Scholarship Programme, a competitive research internship scheme that invites undergraduates to work on faculty projects over the summer break. Second, four students (SL, AM, BJ, and MY) participated through an advanced placement unit, BPS3072 (Advanced Professional Experience), which required them to complete a supervised project in their discipline for academic credit (BPS3072 - Advanced Professional Experience [Online], 2025; Summer and Winter Vacation Research Scholarship Program [Online], 2026). The participants came from a mix of relevant majors (Medicinal Chemistry, Formulation, Drug Discovery Biology) and brought varied experiences, yet all shared an interest in improving learning through technology. Notably, none of the students had extensive prior research experience or had previously served as student partners in faculty projects, reﬂecting",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"Chemistry, Formulation, Drug Discovery Biology) and brought varied experiences, yet all shared an interest in improving learning through technology. Notably, none of the students had extensive prior research experience or had previously served as student partners in faculty projects, reﬂecting the project’s aim to empower novices as co-creators (Dollinger et al., 2023). Academic participants (EY, JP, NK, BE) were involved based on their role in the target units and interest in pedagogical use of AI. 2.3 Ethical considerations The study was approved by the Monash University Human Research Ethics Committee (project IDs 42410, 44572). All participants (students and staff) provided informed consent to contribute to both the co-creation activities and the research evaluation. Student partners were briefed on research ethics and submitted reﬂective reports, as part of the BPS3072 unit requirements. To protect participants’ privacy and encourage honest self-reﬂection, all personal reﬂections were de-identiﬁed in the analysis. Given the dual role of student participants as co- creators and subjects of study (through their reﬂections), particular care was taken to maintain a collegial, non- hierarchical environment and to emphasise that there",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"personal reﬂections were de-identiﬁed in the analysis. Given the dual role of student participants as co- creators and subjects of study (through their reﬂections), particular care was taken to maintain a collegial, non- hierarchical environment and to emphasise that there were no “right or wrong” answers in their reﬂections. This helped mitigate power imbalances and potential coercion, reinforcing that participation in the research component was voluntary and could be withdrawn without academic penalty. The academic participants in this study were not involved in the summative assessment of students in the context of the BPS3072 unit. 2.4 Design and co-creation process 2.4.1 Approach The design of the custom GPTs followed a participatory co- design process underpinned by the SaP paradigm and a collaborative autoethnography methodological framework. This approach was particularly appropriate for our aims, as it allowed the student and staff co-creators to situate themselves in the research process, repositioning from traditional roles (e.g., students as data sources, academics as investigators) to co-creators of cGPTs and autoethnographic data, through reﬂective narrative. The SaP model informed all stages of the design: students",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"co-creators to situate themselves in the research process, repositioning from traditional roles (e.g., students as data sources, academics as investigators) to co-creators of cGPTs and autoethnographic data, through reﬂective narrative. The SaP model informed all stages of the design: students and academics engaged as equal partners in decision-making and creative input, adhering to partnership principles of shared responsibility, reciprocity, and mutual learning. The team cultivated an open, collegial dynamic in which student voices were valued in conceptualising the cGPT tools’ features and content. 2.4.2 Process The development of the cGPTs (Table 1) was an iterative, collaborative process involving multiple phases of planning, building, and reﬁnement. At the outset, the full team convened to establish common goals and understandings. Some cGPT purposes were identiﬁed by academics, while others were suggested by student partners based on their learning experience (i.e., the two Polymer cGPTs: Knowledge and Socratic). For each cGPT, the pedagogical needs and scope of the chatbot were ﬁrst identiﬁed. For example, it was determined that the Physiology 1 cGPT should serve as a virtual tutor to support students in writing",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,5,"Polymer cGPTs: Knowledge and Socratic). For each cGPT, the pedagogical needs and scope of the chatbot were ﬁrst identiﬁed. For example, it was determined that the Physiology 1 cGPT should serve as a virtual tutor to support students in writing about complex physiological concepts (broad), while the medicinal chemistry cGPT would help students with problems and explanations for a speciﬁc reaction mechanism (focused). Multiple cGPTs adopted a Socratic approach to achieve pedagogical goals (Paul and Elder, 2006). This approach aimed to examine student thinking and allow knowledge gap identiﬁcation. This was based on a Socratic questioning framework involving systematic questioning of fundamental concepts and problems (Piro and Anderson, 2015). Following this, Socratic cGPTs were designed to respond to student queries with questions instead of answers. These questions investigated users’ background knowledge, assumptions, and implications of existing knowledge. After deﬁning objectives, the partners co-created the content and structure of the GPTs. Academics contributed unit materials (e.g., learning materials, open-source textbooks, Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",168
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,6,"existing question banks) and domain expertise, ensuring the cGPTs’ knowledge base and responses would be accurate and curriculum-aligned. Students contributed by curating and simplifying content for AI response optimisation, authoring example prompts and questions from a student perspective and anticipating how their peers might interact with the AI and editing it to adopt it for each cGPT’s purposes. While students worked together on all cGPTs, they adopted several roles, based on their expertise, interest, and preferences: content curator, builder, tester, evaluator and data analyst. Content curators assembled cGPT knowledge bases and created evaluation questions, builders wrote and edited system prompts (detailed instructions for cGPT), testers generated and reviewed cGPT responses, evaluators scored responses to evaluation questions and data analysts analysed and presented evaluation scores. For different cGPTs, various combinations of academics and student partners formed unit/course cGPT teams, depending on required expertise and speciﬁc academics’ roles in the overall project. Together, each team crafted a system prompt for the cGPT (for instance, setting the AI to use appropriate technical terminology or to provide Socratic questioning, as an instructor would). The",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,6,"required expertise and speciﬁc academics’ roles in the overall project. Together, each team crafted a system prompt for the cGPT (for instance, setting the AI to use appropriate technical terminology or to provide Socratic questioning, as an instructor would). The technical implementation leveraged a “Create GPT” function within an OpenAI Plus account, consisting of four distinct construction phases: • Creation - Writing the cGPT system prompt, and ﬁle selection for knowledge bases. • Reﬁnement - Iterative cGPT testing with prompts, system prompt editing and modifying knowledge base ﬁles based on identiﬁed areas of improvement. Student reﬂections known as SPAR (explained below) were written in this phase. • Consolidation - Deciding on ﬁnal system prompts and knowledge base ﬁles. If applicable, different cGPTs and ﬁle versions were merged to create the ﬁnal cGPT. • Evaluation - Assessment of responses generated from ten evaluation questions. Evaluation questions were curated to be representative of cGPTs’ expected knowledge ﬁeld. The overall process from construction to implementation, lasted two to six weeks. The exact duration depended on the cGPT’s topic, students’ prior experience with constructing",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,6,"ten evaluation questions. Evaluation questions were curated to be representative of cGPTs’ expected knowledge ﬁeld. The overall process from construction to implementation, lasted two to six weeks. The exact duration depended on the cGPT’s topic, students’ prior experience with constructing cGPTs, and availability of assisting resources such as system prompt templates. Established common goals from the beginning of cGPT development were translated into design principles. Design principles focused on clear, accurate and consistent responses that communicate at an appropriate level and beneﬁt students’ knowledge. These principles shaped cGPT construction by informing students’ interpretation and appraisal of cGPT responses during Reﬁnement. Throughout the summer and placement projects, students met on a regular basis (weekly - twice weekly) to test the cGPTs and reﬁne them. In these sessions, students would demo the cGPTs’ responses to sample questions or tasks and evaluate the output for correctness, clarity, and pedagogical usefulness. Issues like factual errors or confusing explanations were noted and used to adjust the prompts or training data. Notes from each session were written up as autoethnographic reﬂections known as Stimulus (Prompt sent",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,6,"output for correctness, clarity, and pedagogical usefulness. Issues like factual errors or confusing explanations were noted and used to adjust the prompts or training data. Notes from each session were written up as autoethnographic reﬂections known as Stimulus (Prompt sent to GPT), Processing (GPT’s response), Analysis (Evaluation of GPT response based on design principles) and Reﬂection (Next steps based on GPT response) (SPAR). Students used SPAR reﬂections to inform system prompt adjustment and assess the responses’ alignment with objectives from both student and educator perspectives. This iterative design cycle or “SPARing” continued until each unit’s custom GPT achieved satisfactory accuracy and instruction adherence levels. Subsequent evaluation scored twenty generated responses to each evaluation question based on accuracy, concision and relevance. Scores from two evaluators were discussed and agreed upon to create a ﬁnal average score for each evaluation question. This aimed to understand how the cGPT will respond to users’ queries when released. TABLE 1 Co-created custom GPTs from the project. cGPT Unit Purpose Link to unit handbook entry Polymer Knowledge BPS3071: Nanotechnology and polymer science in drug delivery Aid",180
