A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,7,"Student-academic teams met regularly (weekly or biweekly) to discuss cGPT construction issues and plan for improvements. The co-creation process was highly collaborative, with students often taking creative leads (e.g., proposing novel use-cases for the GPT in their unit) and academics providing guidance to keep the development aligned with intended learning outcomes. Importantly, all design decisions were made jointly; for instance, the team jointly decided how to handle the AI’s tone and limitations. By the end of the development phase, seven distinct yet consistent student-staff-designed cGPT tools had been created, each tailored to its unit’s content and learning activities. This process not only produced educational AI tools for pharmaceutical science undergraduate students but also served as a vehicle for student partners’ professional skill development (in AI literacy, teamwork, and project management) and scholarly insight into co-creative pedagogy. Documentation of the design steps, decisions, and iterative changes was maintained to inform later reﬂection and analysis. The project’s main activities, student and academic roles, and data/artefacts generated are summarised in Table 2. The pace of GenAI development posed a unique challenge for students.",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,7,"steps, decisions, and iterative changes was maintained to inform later reﬂection and analysis. The project’s main activities, student and academic roles, and data/artefacts generated are summarised in Table 2. The pace of GenAI development posed a unique challenge for students. ChatGPT’s Study mode (released 29/07/2025) and the GPT-5 model (07/08/2025) were released during the construction process. Study mode introduced guiding questions to standard ChatGPT responses, and GPT-5 changed how cGPTs responded. Students consequently adapted the evaluation by including GPT-5 responses and comparing those with the previous model, GPT-4o. This kept evaluation applicable to new ChatGPT updates, and extended understanding of how cGPTs will respond to users to the latest model. In the spirit of legitimate peripheral participation, student partners made informed contributions through prompt design, testing, learner-oriented interpretation, and evaluation, while academic partners supported truth-oriented design by verifying disciplinary accuracy, alignment with unit learning outcomes, and pedagogical appropriateness (Lave and Wenger, 1991). 2.5 Data collection Data collection focused on capturing the experiences, reﬂections, and interactions of the student and staff co-creators throughout the project. Given the ethnographic approach, the data",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,7,"alignment with unit learning outcomes, and pedagogical appropriateness (Lave and Wenger, 1991). 2.5 Data collection Data collection focused on capturing the experiences, reﬂections, and interactions of the student and staff co-creators throughout the project. Given the ethnographic approach, the data sources were largely qualitative, originating from the participants’ own narratives and discussions. Speciﬁcally, we collected data from two main sources: Summer Scholarship and Research Placement components (Figure 1). We use the term collaborative autoethnography speciﬁcally to describe the Summer Scholarship component, in which student co-creators generated SPAR reﬂections during the iterative development of the cGPTs and inductively analysed these reﬂections. The placement students generated structured reﬂective reports, guided by Gibbs’ reﬂective cycle (Gibbs, 1988), and this component is therefore described as reﬂective practice (Figure 1). Across both datasets, we treated reﬂection not as transparent evidence of experience but as situated, interpretive accounts requiring reﬂexive analysis and validation. 2.5.1 Autoethnographic logs Summer project student co-creators maintained personal logs documenting their experiences on a regular basis in a series of autoethnographic reﬂections. These reﬂections on cGPT construction comprised of four parts: •",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,7,"interpretive accounts requiring reﬂexive analysis and validation. 2.5.1 Autoethnographic logs Summer project student co-creators maintained personal logs documenting their experiences on a regular basis in a series of autoethnographic reﬂections. These reﬂections on cGPT construction comprised of four parts: • Stimulus (Prompt sent to GPT) • Processing (GPT’s response) • Analysis (Evaluation of GPT response based on design principles) • Reﬂection (Next steps based on GPT response) In these autoethnographic SPAR reﬂections, students recorded their observations as they progressed through the cGPT design process; for example, noting challenges faced in designing the cGPT and insights gained about their learning. The logs encouraged deep reﬂection and were unguided in format, allowing students to freely express both successes and frustrations. The use of ongoing self-reﬂective logs is common in collaborative autoethnography to facilitate real-time, authentic capture of participants’ internal dialogues. To reduce risks associated with collaborative autoethnography (i.e., groupthink, retrospective smoothing, and afﬁrmative self-reporting), SPAR reﬂections were written individually prior to peer discussion, TABLE 2 cGPT design and co-creation process: activities, roles, and artefacts. Project Phase Main activities Student role Academic role",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,7,"risks associated with collaborative autoethnography (i.e., groupthink, retrospective smoothing, and afﬁrmative self-reporting), SPAR reﬂections were written individually prior to peer discussion, TABLE 2 cGPT design and co-creation process: activities, roles, and artefacts. Project Phase Main activities Student role Academic role Data/artefacts generated cGPT: Initial build Deﬁne cGPT purpose, draft system prompt, compile knowledge base Propose learner needs, draft prompts Align with learning outcomes, verify scope Initial prompts, unit materials cGPT: Reﬁnement Test responses, identify errors, revise instructions Prompt testing, SPAR reﬂections, response evaluation Disciplinary and pedagogical feedback SPAR logs, revised prompts, meeting notes cGPT: Consolidation Merge versions and ﬁnalise design Compare versions, select effective system prompts Verify curriculum alignment Final system prompts, knowledge-base ﬁles cGPT: Evaluation Benchmark cGPT responses Design evaluation questions/tasks, generate responses, evaluate and score outputs Moderate criteria and interpretation Evaluation questions/tasks, response scores, scoring notes, reﬂective reports Data analysis Analysis of reﬂective artefacts Inductive analysis of SPAR reﬂective logs Abductive analysis of reﬂective reports – Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",167
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,8,"raw student-authored reﬂections were preserved, interpretation was negotiated, and explicit reﬂexive attention was paid to power relations. 2.5.2 Written reﬂective reports In the placement project, more structured written reﬂections were solicited at the end of the project. Student co-creators were asked to compose brief narrative reﬂections. These reﬂections (ranging ∼500–1000 words each) allowed participants to synthesise their experiences with some hindsight and were guided by a Gibbs reﬂective cycle (Gibbs, 1988): (i) Describe the experience. (ii) Analyse how you feel about the experience of doing research and the skills that you learned. (iii) Theorise about why the experience of doing research turned out like it did. (iv) Actions: What would you do differently if you were doing it again? Together, these data sources provided an account of students’ co-creation experience. The data collection (particularly the SPARing logs) was ongoing and iterative, occurring in parallel with the design process; this enabled participants to reﬂect in real time on unfolding events. All raw data were stored securely and anonymised for analysis, with student authors given the option to member-check the interpretation of",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,8,"iterative, occurring in parallel with the design process; this enabled participants to reﬂect in real time on unfolding events. All raw data were stored securely and anonymised for analysis, with student authors given the option to member-check the interpretation of their reﬂections to ensure accuracy and comfort before inclusion in the dataset. Although the principal data corpus consisted of student- authored reﬂective logs and reports, the data analysis (see below) was informed by multiple project artefacts: SPAR records (including iterative changes to system prompts and knowledge- base ﬁles), cGPT system and evaluation prompts, knowledge- base materials, generated cGPT responses, evaluation scores, and meeting discussions. These artefacts provided contextual triangulation by linking students’ reﬂective claims to observable design decisions and iterative changes in the cGPTs. 2.6 Data analysis We adapted a reﬂexive thematic analysis approach to interpret the qualitative data (Braun and Clarke, 2021). Reﬂexive thematic analysis is a qualitative analytic method that involves identifying patterns or themes across narrative data, while explicitly incorporating the researchers’ reﬂexivity into the process. Analytic responsibility differed across the two datasets. Summer students’ SPAR reﬂections",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,8,"and Clarke, 2021). Reﬂexive thematic analysis is a qualitative analytic method that involves identifying patterns or themes across narrative data, while explicitly incorporating the researchers’ reﬂexivity into the process. Analytic responsibility differed across the two datasets. Summer students’ SPAR reﬂections were focused on students’ experience of building cGPTs. To construct the themes associated with this experience, student co-creators analysed them inductively, in the spirit of autoethnographic research, with academic authors supporting reﬂexive discussion and ﬁndings integration. The placement students’ reﬂections were focused on their collaborative experiences, and were analysed by academic researchers using an abductive approach (Thompson, 2022), to construct themes that are grounded in the data (inductive coding) but also informed by existing literature on SaP (deductive coding). Based on extant literature, we have prepared a preliminary thematic codebook to capture SaP values and principles (Table 3) and used it to deductively code the students’ reﬂections. The inductive coding focused on speciﬁc FIGURE 1 Methodological overview of the data streams and synthesis process. The ﬁgure illustrates the distinction between the two reﬂective data streams analysed in this study. The",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,8,"it to deductively code the students’ reﬂections. The inductive coding focused on speciﬁc FIGURE 1 Methodological overview of the data streams and synthesis process. The ﬁgure illustrates the distinction between the two reﬂective data streams analysed in this study. The Summer Scholarship component was positioned as collaborative autoethnography: Summer Scholarship students and authors TL and SL generated Stimulus, Processing, Analysis, and Reﬂections (SPAR) during cGPT development and analysed these reﬂections inductively. The Research Placement component was positioned as reﬂective practice: research placement student authors BJ, AM, MY and SL generated structured reﬂective reports as part of the placement, which were analysed by academic staff authors EY and BE. The two data streams were then brought together through synthesis of ﬁndings and shared meaning making across the complementary methodological approaches. Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",138
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,9,"student experiences in the context of AI tool creation, tailored to their self-identiﬁed educational needs. The analysis proceeded in an iterative six-phase process following Braun and Clarke’s guidelines (Braun and Clarke, 2021): (i) data familiarisation, (ii) generation of initial codes, (iii) construction of themes (domain summaries), (iv) review and reﬁnement of themes, (v) deﬁning and naming of themes, (vi) writing up the analysis. Thematic analysis of placement reﬂections was conducted by two researchers (EY & BE), followed by discussions to check the interpretation of the data and to interrogate how assumptions might be inﬂuencing the coding and theme development. Negotiated agreement was reached before the ﬁnal theme structures were produced. Trustworthiness was built into the entire project, ensuring that the collected data was meaningful and reliable and ﬁndings are credible, transferable, dependable, and conﬁrmable. Continuous engagement between academics and students ensured credibility of thematic analyses by gaining deep perspectives into students’ experiences. Thick descriptions of the project’s context and methods supported transferability. The detailed methods section documenting each step of cGPT creation, student reﬂection and thematic analysis safeguarded dependability. Member",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,9,"ensured credibility of thematic analyses by gaining deep perspectives into students’ experiences. Thick descriptions of the project’s context and methods supported transferability. The detailed methods section documenting each step of cGPT creation, student reﬂection and thematic analysis safeguarded dependability. Member checking of student reﬂections and peer debrieﬁng during abductive analysis of placement reﬂections bolstered conﬁrmability of ﬁndings. Together, these four components strengthened the trustworthiness of this project’s ﬁndings, following qualitative research conventions (Ahmed, 2024; Nowell et al., 2017). Overall, the reﬂexive thematic analysis yielded two sets of themes that encapsulate the co-creation experience. In line with our reﬂexive approach adapted from Braun and Clarke, we present these themes not as an objective truth but as a co- constructed interpretation of the patterns in the shared narratives, shaped by the interdisciplinary lenses and dialogues within our team. This analytic approach was optimal for our study’s aims, as it allowed us to systematically distil insights (e.g., recurring challenges and beneﬁts of the SaP model in practice) while honouring the subjective and relational nature of the data. 3 Results 3.1 Insights from the",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,9,"optimal for our study’s aims, as it allowed us to systematically distil insights (e.g., recurring challenges and beneﬁts of the SaP model in practice) while honouring the subjective and relational nature of the data. 3 Results 3.1 Insights from the summer scholarship reﬂections: building custom GPTs as a learning experience The students’ thematic analysis revealed a range of inductive themes. During their experience, they navigated complexity using iterative design, showed ﬂexibility and evolving evaluative judgment, and recognised the serendipity, innovation and limits of GPTs’ unpredictability. Students noted the tension between creativity and repetition, and how external resources and interactions shaped GPT improvement, and therefore future interactions. Each theme is detailed below with representative quotes. 3.1.1 Navigating complexity through iterative design Students’ engagement with the custom GPT project unfolded as a deeply iterative process marked by continuous cycles of testing, evaluation, revision, and reﬂection. The project evolved through creation, reﬁnement, and consolidation stages, each demanding new forms of thinking and adaptability. Initially, the focus was on generating a workable set of instructions and understanding how prompts elicited responses from the model.",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,9,"revision, and reﬂection. The project evolved through creation, reﬁnement, and consolidation stages, each demanding new forms of thinking and adaptability. Initially, the focus was on generating a workable set of instructions and understanding how prompts elicited responses from the model. Even seemingly minor changes, such as rewording a sentence, could signiﬁcantly inﬂuence how the GPT responded, though these effects were often unpredictable: “Edits in wording, uploaded documents, addition and deletion of sentences and paragraphs could either clearly affect how the GPT responded, or have no apparent effect”. This iterative loop reﬂected the nature of AI development itself, where outcomes were neither linear nor fully controllable, and required sustained critical evaluation of evolving outputs. TABLE 3 Thematic codebook of SaP values and principles. SaP value or principle Operational deﬁnition Canonical SaP anchor (references) Typical indicators in text Reciprocity Mutual inﬂuence (learning with/from staff and peers; two-way feedback) Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017; Cook-Sather et al., 2014 (Ch 1); Healey et al., 2014 “feedback helped…”, “negotiation and agreement…”, “calibration meetings…” Agency & ownership Initiative (students enact choice, drive tasks, shape decisions) Cook-Sather et",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,9,"with/from staff and peers; two-way feedback) Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017; Cook-Sather et al., 2014 (Ch 1); Healey et al., 2014 “feedback helped…”, “negotiation and agreement…”, “calibration meetings…” Agency & ownership Initiative (students enact choice, drive tasks, shape decisions) Cook-Sather et al., 2014 (Ch 5); Healey et al., 2014; Godbold et al., 2022 “decided to…”, “took responsibility…”, “led…”, proactive feedback-seeking Identity shift From student to partner/designer (repositioning, conﬁdence changes) Matthews, 2017; Cook-Sather et al., 2014 (Ch 5) from “felt anxious” to “conﬁdent”, “more like a researcher/leader” Reﬂexivity Metacognitive stance (interrogating assumptions; learning how to learn) Matthews, 2017; Healey et al., 2014 “I realise now…”, “in hindsight…”, “reﬂective journalling” Negotiating power Role/authority calibration (leadership, mentoring peers, conﬂict) Cook-Sather et al., 2014 (Ch 7); Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017; Matthews, 2017; Godbold et al., 2022 “teaching collaborators…”, “balancing views…”, “calibration” Authenticity Real-world purpose & responsibility (time- pressures, deliverables, publication-style outputs) Healey et al., 2014 constraints; deadlines; “publication-style reporting” Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",163
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"3.1.2 Flexibility and evolving evaluative judgment Students’ reﬂections revealed a shift from rigid adherence to pre-deﬁned design principles towards a more nuanced understanding of response quality. As they encountered the diversity of GPT outputs, they learned that effective evaluation was not merely about compliance with principles but also about contextual appropriateness and user utility. Responses that technically “failed” a principle could still be valued if they offered unexpected insights: “This diversity thus necessitated an evolving, nuanced view in response evaluation”. This evolving evaluative stance mirrored the complexities of authentic AI deployment, where context-speciﬁc judgment outweighs strict metric adherence. 3.1.3 Serendipity, innovation and limits of GPT unpredictability Students’ reﬂections frequently referenced the randomness and volatility of GPT behaviour. Despite careful changes to instructions and source materials, there was no guarantee they would be deployed effectively or consistently. This unpredictability was particularly visible during the consolidation phase, where even with identical instructions and materials, GPTs often diverged in how they structured responses, “struggling to keep consistency”. Unexpected inclusions sparked creativity and prompted new directions. Additional subheadings such as “Thought Exercise” or “Prompting",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"visible during the consolidation phase, where even with identical instructions and materials, GPTs often diverged in how they structured responses, “struggling to keep consistency”. Unexpected inclusions sparked creativity and prompted new directions. Additional subheadings such as “Thought Exercise” or “Prompting Questions” suggested alternative ways to structure knowledge and communicate purpose: “these alternative structures… can promote ﬂexibility and creativity for different ways to communicate a GPT’s intended purpose”. However, GPTs consistently incorporated broad ideas mentioned in the instructions, such as the Goldilocks Help scaffold which was incorporated into the knowledge base of the GPTs. Students interpreted this as evidence of both the power and constraint of generative AI: it could internalise high-level patterns but remained resistant to precise control: “The only consistent relationship between GPT materials and instructions, and GPT responses, is if a broad idea is included, then it will likely appear in a response”. This required students to adopt an experimental mindset, accepting that variability was an inherent feature of working with large language models rather than a ﬂaw to be eliminated. 3.1.4 Structure, reuse, and the tension between",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"likely appear in a response”. This required students to adopt an experimental mindset, accepting that variability was an inherent feature of working with large language models rather than a ﬂaw to be eliminated. 3.1.4 Structure, reuse, and the tension between creativity and repetition Students noticed the “structure repetition”, conserved in GPT outputs. As development progressed, responses increasingly reused the same organisational structures and question patterns, even when the prompts changed. While this suggested the model’s capacity to stabilise around effective forms, it also raised concerns about overﬁtting and reduced adaptability. Students reﬂected on the tension between consistency and creativity: while reuse could enhance reliability, it risked constraining the GPT’s responsiveness to novel contexts. 3.1.5 External resources and interaction as a driver of improvement Students also examined how the structure and scale of external data shaped GPT performance. Experiments with different formats of the Socratic Question Database (PDF, Word, Excel) revealed that the model could draw on these resources regardless of format, although performance improved as testing continued and as the database grew. These observations prompted critical reﬂections on the design",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"formats of the Socratic Question Database (PDF, Word, Excel) revealed that the model could draw on these resources regardless of format, although performance improved as testing continued and as the database grew. These observations prompted critical reﬂections on the design decisions underlying data integration in custom GPTs. A key inductive insight was the recognition that continued interaction enhances instruction changes’ effectiveness. Students observed that as they continued to engage with the GPT, they anticipated possible outcomes of speciﬁc language choices better, resulting in improved adherence to design principles and use of relevant structures: “the more times the cGPT is used, the more consistently it may adhere to the instructions… ultimately resulting in a better custom GPT experience for users”. This conclusion led them to conceptualise GPT development not as a one-time conﬁguration task but as an ongoing dialogue, where iterative interaction contributes to shaping and reﬁning the model’s capabilities over time. Taken together, the inductive themes constructed from the Summer Scholarship SPAR reﬂections portray students’ engagement with GPT development as an iterative, unpredictable, and co-creative process. They learned to navigate",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"interaction contributes to shaping and reﬁning the model’s capabilities over time. Taken together, the inductive themes constructed from the Summer Scholarship SPAR reﬂections portray students’ engagement with GPT development as an iterative, unpredictable, and co-creative process. They learned to navigate complexity through cycles of experimentation, to embrace unpredictability as an inherent feature of generative AI, and to balance structure with ﬂexibility. Unexpected model behaviours prompted creative rethinking, while repeated interactions revealed how students learned GPTs’ “language”. The students’ growing awareness of trade-offs, between inputs and ﬂexibility, evaluation criteria and contextual appropriateness, structure and creativity, demonstrates a sophisticated engagement with both the technical and pedagogical dimensions of custom GPT development. 3.2 Insights from placement reﬂective reports: building cGPTs as a co-creation process The thematic analysis of students’ reﬂective reports revealed a series of interrelated themes aligned with key SaP principles (Table 3). Students described profound shifts in their learning experience around agency and ownership, reciprocity and negotiation, identity, reﬂexivity, adaptability arising from authenticity, and the integration of technical and pedagogical domains. Below we detail each theme, illustrating them with representative quotes",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,10,"3). Students described profound shifts in their learning experience around agency and ownership, reciprocity and negotiation, identity, reﬂexivity, adaptability arising from authenticity, and the integration of technical and pedagogical domains. Below we detail each theme, illustrating them with representative quotes from across the four reﬂective reports. 3.2.1 Agency and ownership All students reported an enhanced sense of agency and ownership over their learning during the co-creation of cGPTs. In contrast to conventional coursework, where tasks and outcomes are largely pre-deﬁned by instructors, the open-ended nature of the empowered students to take initiative and make decisions in the project. One student explicitly contrasted the Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 10 frontiersin.org",112
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"placement with ordinary classes: “Unlike coursework, where outcomes are predeﬁned, this project required problem-solving and independence”. Another noted that the project “brought upon an open-ended solution that challenged my way of thinking… [r]equiring me to adapt, self-direct, and rely on iterative learning”, ultimately teaching them “to be comfortable with the unknown”. In taking on real responsibility for designing and evaluating the cGPTs, students became agents of their own learning process, consistent with the core SaP tenet that students in partnerships are active contributors. Students also described pride and conﬁdence in the ownership they assumed. One student, who stepped into a team leader role, recounted feeling “proud of delivering the evaluation and data analysis at a rate faster than I expected to do” despite the project’s pressures. This student was responsible for key outputs and even mentored the peers, reﬂecting a high level of ownership over project outcomes. Across the reﬂections, students consistently conveyed that they were not just following instructions; they were actively steering the project, making decisions, and owning both successes and setbacks. Such experiences echo prior ﬁndings that",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"high level of ownership over project outcomes. Across the reﬂections, students consistently conveyed that they were not just following instructions; they were actively steering the project, making decisions, and owning both successes and setbacks. Such experiences echo prior ﬁndings that SaP initiatives increase students’ motivation and ownership for learning (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Godbold et al., 2022; Healey et al., 2014). 3.2.2 Reciprocity and negotiation Consistent with the partnership principle of reciprocity (the idea that all participants contribute and beneﬁt as equals), students highlighted the importance of two-way communication, feedback, and negotiation throughout the project. Working in a team of peers and with supervisors required ongoing dialogue to share ideas, resolve differences, and co-create outcomes. One student explained that collaboration entailed continuous give- and-take, saying “working alongside my peers, I learned the importance of negotiation and agreement, especially when our evaluations differed”. Another noted that the “collaborative element forced me to develop interpersonal skills: without effective communication and alignment, the evaluation data could not have been meaningfully interpreted”. The quality of their results depended on open dialogue and negotiation among",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"evaluations differed”. Another noted that the “collaborative element forced me to develop interpersonal skills: without effective communication and alignment, the evaluation data could not have been meaningfully interpreted”. The quality of their results depended on open dialogue and negotiation among partners, and this emphasis on collegial exchange mirrors SaP literature (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Healey et al., 2014; Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017) observing that successful partnerships require reciprocity, negotiation, and shared responsibility. Reciprocity was evident not only among student teammates but also in student-supervisor interactions. Students valued constructive feedback from the academic mentors and learned to actively seek it, indicating a shift from a one-directional learning model to a mutual learning relationship. One student shared that “receiving constructive feedback…became central to this process, guiding me on how to improve my approach during the testing phase”. Another student realised that asking for feedback proactively would have beneﬁted everyone, noting that “proactive engagement [with feedback] accelerated not just my improvement, but the whole team’s efﬁciency”. The student, who had taken on a leadership role, candidly described an imbalance in the collaboration: “at",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"for feedback proactively would have beneﬁted everyone, noting that “proactive engagement [with feedback] accelerated not just my improvement, but the whole team’s efﬁciency”. The student, who had taken on a leadership role, candidly described an imbalance in the collaboration: “at times it seemed as though I was teaching them as if they were under my supervision, rather than working together as students”. This student later acknowledged the need for more equitable interaction, suggesting that “more regular communication… and giving more guidance to collaborators on unfamiliar tasks” would improve the partnership dynamics in future projects. Such reﬂections show a developing awareness of shared responsibility and the need to continually negotiate roles. Even when one student had more expertise, the aim became to bring others along as partners rather than subordinates. Students emphasised how a supportive but trustful supervisory environment enabled them to “take initiative while maintaining high standards”. This suggests that the academic mentors treated the students as genuine partners, providing guidance while allowing students to drive the work and making space for sharing the (equal) realities of partnership. In summary,",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"to “take initiative while maintaining high standards”. This suggests that the academic mentors treated the students as genuine partners, providing guidance while allowing students to drive the work and making space for sharing the (equal) realities of partnership. In summary, students came to appreciate that effective partnership relies on ongoing negotiation, dialogue, and an equitable exchange of expertise which are hallmarks of reciprocity in SaP contexts (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Healey et al., 2014; Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017). 3.2.3 Identity shift Engaging as partners in this project also prompted a notable shift in students’ academic and professional identities. Students described increased conﬁdence, agency in their ﬁeld, and more clarity in their future direction as a result of the experience. For example, one student recalled that at the start they “felt uncertain about [their] ability to work in an area that combined pharmaceutical science with AI”, but by the end, “I felt conﬁdent and motivated, seeing how my scientiﬁc background could combine with emerging technologies gave me a clear sense of purpose”. Another student similarly noted that the initial apprehension “wasn’t",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"combined pharmaceutical science with AI”, but by the end, “I felt conﬁdent and motivated, seeing how my scientiﬁc background could combine with emerging technologies gave me a clear sense of purpose”. Another student similarly noted that the initial apprehension “wasn’t a barrier; it was a way of teaching me to be comfortable with the unknown”. Gaining comfort with ambiguity and conﬁdence in tackling new challenges marked a substantial identity shift for these students, from viewing themselves as novices to feeling like capable innovators in their domain (Lazarus et al., 2021). Several students also began to identify as emerging professionals or experts, not just students fulﬁlling an assignment. One student’s leadership role essentially positioned them in a quasi-teaching capacity of “teaching [peers]… rather than working together as students”. This blurred the line between student and teacher, illustrating how partnership can challenge traditional identities (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017; Matthews et al., 2018). By stepping into a mentor role, this student experienced a shift in seeing their own capabilities and responsibilities. Likewise, by the conclusion of the project, other students had",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,11,"traditional identities (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017; Matthews et al., 2018). By stepping into a mentor role, this student experienced a shift in seeing their own capabilities and responsibilities. Likewise, by the conclusion of the project, other students had begun to self-identify as a researcher, describing the placement as “transformative”, saying it “conﬁrmed that I am capable of handling technical and analytical challenges” and that by reﬁning skills they “can continue to develop as a researcher and professional” beyond the placement. Such narratives underscore a positive shift in identity from student to conﬁdent practitioner. By co-creating an educational AI tool, students came to see themselves as co-creators of knowledge and legitimate members of a scholarly community, rather than just consumers of education. This outcome resonates with broader ﬁndings that partnership experiences can positively shift students’ identity from (passive) learners to active partners (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017). Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",158
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"3.2.4 Reﬂexivity The experience also fostered deep reﬂexivity, as students engaged in critical self-reﬂection and metacognition about their learning. Reﬂection was built into the project, and participants recognised the value of this practice for their growth. One student explicitly highlighted that “reﬂective practice itself is a skill”, one they intended to carry forward “well beyond this placement”. Another said that if they were to repeat the project, they would “begin a reﬂective journal from the ﬁrst day, documenting challenges and feelings in real time”. Recognising the importance of deliberate reﬂection indicates that students were actively developing the capacity to examine their own assumptions, strategies, and emotional responses as the project unfolded. Students used their reﬂections to identify speciﬁc areas for improvement in a very candid way, demonstrating high levels of self-awareness. For instance, one student realised in hindsight that a lack of early planning had hindered the process. This student noted that if they had anticipated major changes (such as new AI model releases) and “planned the evaluation work in advance, the entire process would have been much smoother”, a",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"lack of early planning had hindered the process. This student noted that if they had anticipated major changes (such as new AI model releases) and “planned the evaluation work in advance, the entire process would have been much smoother”, a lesson that taught them “the importance of stronger foresight and more thorough planning in future projects”. Another student reﬂected on their own approach and admitted that they “often waited for [feedback] instead of asking targeted questions early on”, recognising that being more proactive “could have accelerated my progress”. Such insights show students adopting a reﬂexive stance: they not only recount what happened but analyse why things happened as they did and how they might approach a similar challenge differently next time. This level of metacognitive engagement “thinking about one’s own thinking” is a hallmark of high-impact learning experiences (Flavell, 1979). By the end of the placement, students were not only better at doing research; they were better at reﬂecting on their research practice, which is likely to beneﬁt their future studies and careers. This resonates with the proposition that genuine",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"By the end of the placement, students were not only better at doing research; they were better at reﬂecting on their research practice, which is likely to beneﬁt their future studies and careers. This resonates with the proposition that genuine partnerships “nurture power-sharing relationships through dialogue and reﬂection” (Matthews, 2017). 3.2.5 Adaptability arising from authenticity Prominent themes in the reﬂections were the authenticity of the project and the hand-in-hand adaptability of the students. Students repeatedly contrasted this real research experience with the more scripted nature of typical undergraduate coursework. The cGPT project was grounded in a genuine, novel problem; integrating AI into science education with no predetermined “right answers”, which enhanced the experience with a sense of purpose that students found motivating. As one student put it, “the placement combined practice-based learning with real responsibility”. Working with fast-evolving GenAI technology meant this project was inherently dynamic. Students had to continuously adjust their approaches and creatively engage with a project that had no pre-set outcome. All students described instances where ﬂexibility and resilience were crucial. In one case, the team’s initial",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"meant this project was inherently dynamic. Students had to continuously adjust their approaches and creatively engage with a project that had no pre-set outcome. All students described instances where ﬂexibility and resilience were crucial. In one case, the team’s initial work was upended by an external change: “Open Ai had removed their various models… releasing one super model, causing our previous work to be redundant… We had to quickly think on our feet”. Another student explicitly identiﬁed adaptability as a key skill they developed, noting that “as AI models evolved, I had to learn to adjust my approach quickly”, and described going from feeling anxious about constant changes to ultimately “seeing adaptability and clear communication as essential” for their future career. These experiences illustrate how partnership learning is process- oriented and unpredictable rather than outcomes-driven, making learning tasks more authentic and meaningful to students. Consequently, students learned to respond constructively to unexpected developments which is an invaluable outcome of working on a cutting-edge project. Because the project carried real stakes (e.g., producing useful GPT chatbots, delivering presentations to peers and",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"and meaningful to students. Consequently, students learned to respond constructively to unexpected developments which is an invaluable outcome of working on a cutting-edge project. Because the project carried real stakes (e.g., producing useful GPT chatbots, delivering presentations to peers and academics, and contributing to an ongoing research initiative), students felt they were doing something consequential. One student highlighted that the clearly deﬁned, novel objective of “testing cGPTs in a specialised scientiﬁc domain … created an authentic sense of purpose” for the team. They knew their work could inform actual teaching practices, not just earn a grade. Alongside the project’s compressed timeline and novel tasks, this created a need for adaptability. The project’s timeline was inﬂuenced by external events (such as OpenAI model updates and hard deadlines for deliverables), which made it “unrealistic” to follow a tidy predetermined plan. This unpredictability is a hallmark of authentic research, and it pushed students to be resourceful. One student described their personal growth in terms of new technical competencies over the course of the project: initially feeling uncertain about using data analysis tools, but",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"is a hallmark of authentic research, and it pushed students to be resourceful. One student described their personal growth in terms of new technical competencies over the course of the project: initially feeling uncertain about using data analysis tools, but “as I grew comfortable with Prism, Excel, and document preparation, this anxiety shifted towards conﬁdence”. In the ﬁnal reﬂections, students frequently mentioned change, evolution, and adaptation as deﬁning features of the placement. They credited the experience with helping them build a more adaptable mindset and resilience: “I have gained conﬁdence and developed a more adaptable mindset”. Overall, through the authenticity of co-creating a technology for actual educational use, students were deeply engaged and adapted quickly to new challenges, tools, and information. This reinforces ﬁndings that engagement through partnership thrives when projects feel meaningful and real to learners (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Healey et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017). Their ability to adapt became a hallmark of their learning, a skill that will likely beneﬁt them in both academic and workplace contexts. 3.2.6 Technical-pedagogical integration Finally, a unique theme was constructed around",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,12,"2014; Healey et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017). Their ability to adapt became a hallmark of their learning, a skill that will likely beneﬁt them in both academic and workplace contexts. 3.2.6 Technical-pedagogical integration Finally, a unique theme was constructed around the integration of technical work with pedagogical goals. By design, the project required students to bridge their scientiﬁc domain knowledge, educational objectives, and AI technology. This integration was evident as students talked about developing the cGPTs to meet learning needs in their discipline. One student described the project as “designing and evaluating custom GPTs tailored for [a unit] under the topic Polymers”, explaining that “the placement aimed to integrate AI into teaching by building two distinct models: a Socratic GPT that encourages critical thinking and a Knowledge GPT that provides direct answers”. Here we see the students deliberately crafting AI tools with Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",151
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"pedagogical intent, not just customising GPTs for its own sake. Students found motivation in seeing their scientiﬁc expertise meet emerging technology and recognised the value of uniting content knowledge with technical innovation to enhance learning. Students also took on tasks that required them to translate between technical and educational contexts. For instance, the team had to generate and analyse benchmark questions to evaluate the cGPTs’ performance on relevant unit content. The student team leader described how they developed data collection templates and conducted statistical analyses on the results, resulting in “two custom GPTs with 400 collected responses and 800 individual scores… indicating the performance of the custom GPTs on different benchmark questions”. This effort required understanding both the technical output of the AI and the pedagogical standards by which the answers were judged. Similarly, another student noted that the contributions were “not only technical but also organisational”, including responsibility for “ensuring consistency across evaluators and developing reporting formats suitable for a publication-style assessment”, thus integrating the technical results into a form that aligns with academic assessment and scholarship. All students",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"were “not only technical but also organisational”, including responsibility for “ensuring consistency across evaluators and developing reporting formats suitable for a publication-style assessment”, thus integrating the technical results into a form that aligns with academic assessment and scholarship. All students demonstrated this dual focus: they had to develop new technical skills (prompt engineering, data analysis, use of AI platforms) and simultaneously consider educational effectiveness (was the cGPT giving correct answers and useful feedback? aligned with the curriculum?). Navigating this intersection was challenging but rewarding, it taught them to “connect scientiﬁc practice with technological innovation”, a competency that students learned to appreciate as essential for their career. Collectively, these themes illustrate a rich and transformative learning experience for the students as partners in the cGPT project. Through agency, reciprocity, identity shifts, reﬂexivity, authenticity, adaptability, and cross-domain integration, the undergraduate partners not only contributed to an educational innovation but also underwent signiﬁcant personal and professional growth. The results demonstrate how engaging students as co-creators in teaching and learning initiatives can empower them with heightened ownership, collaborative skills, and a more nuanced understanding",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"only contributed to an educational innovation but also underwent signiﬁcant personal and professional growth. The results demonstrate how engaging students as co-creators in teaching and learning initiatives can empower them with heightened ownership, collaborative skills, and a more nuanced understanding of both technology and pedagogy. In the following Discussion section, we review these ﬁndings in light of the SaP theory and their implications for higher education practice. 4 Discussion This study draws on results from two stages of the project: summer scholarship and placement. The former set of reﬂections focused more on technological aspects of cGPT development, while the latter on the experience and personal growth. These two sets of results allow the analysis of co-creation of AI tools through a partnership lens, extending the SaP theory in the context of generative AI, and the implications for SaP research and practice. 4.1 Co-creation of generative AI tools through a partnership lens The two strands of results together paint a cohesive picture of how student co-creation of generative AI tools embodies core principles of SaP pedagogy (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Mercer-",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"practice. 4.1 Co-creation of generative AI tools through a partnership lens The two strands of results together paint a cohesive picture of how student co-creation of generative AI tools embodies core principles of SaP pedagogy (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Mercer- Mapstone et al., 2017). In both contexts, students stepped into active co-creator roles, experiencing a shift from traditional learner positions to those of genuine collaborators or contributors to educational practice, on an equal footing with educators in shaping the learning process (Healey et al., 2014). The reﬂections revealed how agency and reciprocity come to the forefront when students are entrusted as partners. The inductive themes of navigating complexity, unpredictability, serendipity, creativity, and adaptability in the cGPT projects illustrate a dynamic, dialogic learning process (Durall Gazulla et al., 2023). Rather than passively receiving knowledge, students engaged in cyclical design and problem solving, mirroring the process- focused approach that characterises partnership-based learning. Taken together, these ﬁndings suggest that when students co- create educational tools with GenAI, the technology itself becomes a catalyst for engagement, requiring students to negotiate ideas, take initiative, and",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"solving, mirroring the process- focused approach that characterises partnership-based learning. Taken together, these ﬁndings suggest that when students co- create educational tools with GenAI, the technology itself becomes a catalyst for engagement, requiring students to negotiate ideas, take initiative, and respond ﬂexibly to emerging challenges, thus reinforcing key SaP values of shared responsibility and mutual learning. These observations underscore that co-creation with generative AI is not a straightforward “engineering” task in which ﬁxed inputs deterministically produce stable outputs. Rather, it is a dynamic interactional process in which unanticipated model behaviours, serendipitous, volatile, and sometimes resistant to precise control become pedagogically consequential. In our study, students did not simply “debug” a tool toward compliance with pre-deﬁned principles; they learned to interpret variability, to weigh contextual appropriateness against formal criteria, and to recognise that productive innovation can emerge from apparent “failures” when outputs generate new ways of structuring knowledge or prompting learner thinking. Framed this way, unpredictability is not only a technical constraint but also a design condition that requires iterative judgement, reﬂective experimentation, and a willingness to recalibrate what “quality” means",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"outputs generate new ways of structuring knowledge or prompting learner thinking. Framed this way, unpredictability is not only a technical constraint but also a design condition that requires iterative judgement, reﬂective experimentation, and a willingness to recalibrate what “quality” means in situated educational use. 4.2 Autoethnography as a means of fostering and strengthening collaborative partnerships In this study, students’ reﬂections served as a ﬁrst-person narrative of navigating creation of cGPTs or engaging in partnerships. Students’ documenting and analysing their own experiences positioned them as interrogators of their learning, rather than just as subjects of it. Therefore, the use of student- authored autoethnographic reﬂection aligned well with SaP principles (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2017). The reﬂections provided an insight into the students’ evolving sense of agency, moments of struggle and breakthrough (e.g., debugging a prompt or negotiating a role on placement), and their strategies for bridging technical and pedagogical thinking. Treating these accounts as data is consistent with SaP’s emphasis on student voice. In fact, previous work has shown that amplifying student voices can foster reﬂective practice and",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,13,"role on placement), and their strategies for bridging technical and pedagogical thinking. Treating these accounts as data is consistent with SaP’s emphasis on student voice. In fact, previous work has shown that amplifying student voices can foster reﬂective practice and “encourage the embrace of a partnership approach” (Cook-Sather, 2017; Healey et al., 2014). Our ﬁndings support this: the act of reﬂection not only captured outcomes of partnership Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",76
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"experiences but also deepened those experiences by prompting students to be intentionally reﬂexive. This reﬂexivity is itself a valued outcome in partnerships, as partners critically examine their roles, assumptions, and growth. Moreover, using student reﬂections as research data is a form of collaborative meaning- making, reinforcing reciprocity (Chang et al., 2012; Dollinger et al., 2023). Students contributed authentic accounts; researchers (including, in some cases, the students themselves) collaboratively interpreted them. Such an approach blurs the line between researcher and participant, embodying the SaP ideal of shared inquiry. To summarise, the method of student- authored reﬂection is not just a data collection strategy. It is an actual practice of partnership itself. It operationalises respect for student agency, values their lived expertise, and produces knowledge with students rather than about them. This complementarity between method and theoretical orientation strengthens the credibility and authenticity of our study. 4.3 Extending students-as-partners theory in the context of generative AI An important contribution of this study is theoretical: it extends existing SaP frameworks to account not only for student-educator co-creation, but also for student co-creation with",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"authenticity of our study. 4.3 Extending students-as-partners theory in the context of generative AI An important contribution of this study is theoretical: it extends existing SaP frameworks to account not only for student-educator co-creation, but also for student co-creation with AI. SaP models conceptualise partnership as a human relationship (typically between students and faculty) grounded in values of reciprocity and trust (Cook-Sather et al., 2014; Healey et al., 2014; Matthews, 2017). And this study demonstrated how co-creation of generative AI tools reﬂects such values. However, our ﬁndings also indicate that these values are also compatible with student-AI co-creation as well. For example, the unpredictability of cGPT outputs, while initially a technical challenge, became pedagogically valuable as students learned to analyse them critically, reﬂecting an essential skill required in working with generative AI that has previously been reported (Exintaris et al., 2023; Yuriev et al., 2025). They also adapted and iterated, in response. This again echoes the process-oriented nature of SaP, in that participants focus on the process of construction (in this case, of cGPTs), rather than on a perfect end",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"2023; Yuriev et al., 2025). They also adapted and iterated, in response. This again echoes the process-oriented nature of SaP, in that participants focus on the process of construction (in this case, of cGPTs), rather than on a perfect end product. In a “partnership” between humans and technology, the GenAI partner is a tool, not a person. However, the hallmarks of a traditional partnership are also present. Namely, students exercised agency in guiding the AI and reciprocity in learning from its feedback. The interplay often resembled a dialog: students prompted the AI, the AI’s (sometimes) unexpected responses provoked new ideas, and students iteratively reﬁned their approach. We propose that SaP frameworks can be expanded to recognise such triadic partnerships, where technology serves as a mediating partner in the learning process. This does not imply equating AI with a human partner, such as another student or an educator. But it does mean that SaP theory could account for how non-human “partners” like AI can facilitate or augment partnerships. Notably, students in our study experienced identity shifts, when co-creating with AI, eventually",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"such as another student or an educator. But it does mean that SaP theory could account for how non-human “partners” like AI can facilitate or augment partnerships. Notably, students in our study experienced identity shifts, when co-creating with AI, eventually viewing themselves not just as students but also as innovators or co-educators. This identity shift parallels what occurs in student-educator staff partnerships, where learners develop a sense of authority in the academic community (Cook- Sather, 2017). By incorporating GenAI into partnership models, we acknowledge the evolving landscape of co-creation: one that is increasingly digital, where human creativity and machine intelligence intersect. Caveat: We do not argue that GenAI is a partner in the same ontological or ethical sense as students or educators. It lacks agency, intentionality, or moral responsibility. We therefore use “partner” as a metaphorical and analytic term to describe how the tool mediated the partnership process: students prompted, interpreted, evaluated, resisted, and redesigned in response to AI outputs. In this sense, GenAI did not function as an agentic collaborator (although in other contexts it could), but as a",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"describe how the tool mediated the partnership process: students prompted, interpreted, evaluated, resisted, and redesigned in response to AI outputs. In this sense, GenAI did not function as an agentic collaborator (although in other contexts it could), but as a mediating artefact that made student decision-making visible. The gain of this framing is that it captures the iterative, dialogic nature of co-design with generative AI. Its limitation is that it may obscure the asymmetry between human partners and computational tools if not carefully qualiﬁed. To summarise, our study suggests that students-as-partners pedagogy can include student-AI collaborations, provided the hallmark values of respect, shared decision-making, and reﬂection remain central. This theoretical extension invites educators to re-imagine SaP not just as a human relational practice, but as a broader framework for co-creative learning ecosystems that include advanced tools. 4.4 Educational implications The insights from this research carry several important implications for higher education practice, particularly in implementing partnership pedagogy in the context of GenAI- impacted learning: • Embracing co-creation with GenAI in curriculum: Educators should consider structuring projects and assignments that position",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"insights from this research carry several important implications for higher education practice, particularly in implementing partnership pedagogy in the context of GenAI- impacted learning: • Embracing co-creation with GenAI in curriculum: Educators should consider structuring projects and assignments that position students as co-creators with GenAI tools. As our results show, such experiences can cultivate student agency and innovation. By guiding students to use GenAI productively and ethically (e.g., building cGPTs for a unit), educators can tap into students’ insight into their own learning experience. These activities should not be framed as AI tinkering for its own sake, but as opportunities for students to take the lead in solving authentic problems with guidance, reﬂecting the mutual learning ethos of SaP. • Scaffolding for unpredictability, adaptability, and critical thinking: Incorporating GenAI into learning partnerships means embracing a degree of uncertainty since GenAI is, by its very nature, non-deterministic or probabilistic. In our study, this unpredictability became a fertile ground for learning, prompting students to iterate and adapt, or as was coined by Cook-Sather “embracing unknowingness as a mindset” (Cook-Sather, 2025). However, there",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,14,"is, by its very nature, non-deterministic or probabilistic. In our study, this unpredictability became a fertile ground for learning, prompting students to iterate and adapt, or as was coined by Cook-Sather “embracing unknowingness as a mindset” (Cook-Sather, 2025). However, there are two possible negative factors to consider: while some students may be uncomfortable about uncertainty, others may not be aware of it and thus not question GenAI outputs critically. To address both of these concerns, educators should provide scaffolding, including for Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 14 frontiersin.org",90
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"example: critique triggers, clear strategies for iterative testing, checkpoints for reﬂection, and discussions about coping with “failure” or unexpected outcomes (Pea, 2004). • Integrating technical and pedagogical training: A notable theme across both datasets was the integration of technical and pedagogical thinking. Effective partnership projects with GenAI should support students to develop skills in two domains: they must understand the technology (how to prompt, program, or evaluate the GenAI outputs) and the pedagogy (how this tool can serve learning goals). Importantly, the technical work reported by students was consistently inseparable from pedagogical intention. Rather than treating cGPT development as an isolated exercise in prompt optimisation, students framed their design choices around learning purposes for examples differentiating a Socratic model intended to scaffold critical thinking from a Knowledge model intended to provide direct explanatory support. This illustrates that the project’s value was not conﬁned to producing functional tools; it lay in cultivating students’ capacity to align system behaviours with disciplinary learning outcomes and assessment expectations. In this sense, the ability to integrate cutting- edge technology with pedagogical objectives stands out as",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"was not conﬁned to producing functional tools; it lay in cultivating students’ capacity to align system behaviours with disciplinary learning outcomes and assessment expectations. In this sense, the ability to integrate cutting- edge technology with pedagogical objectives stands out as a key outcome of the project: students learned to operate at the nexus of domain expertise and emergent educational tools, developing a form of applied, context- sensitive competence that is increasingly central to modern higher education innovation. • Fostering authenticity and shared ownership: Our study underscored the importance of authenticity in partnership learning. Students thrived when developing GenAI tools with genuine use-cases, since these tasks felt, and were indeed real. Authentic tasks signal to students that their work has meaning beyond a grade, engendering a sense of ownership. Educators should design or offer projects that have real-world relevance or tangible outcomes. 4.5 Research implications This exploratory study opens several avenues for future research at the intersection of student partnerships and GenAI: • Frameworks for student-AI partnerships: Multiple frameworks have been developed recently addressing issues of GenAI in higher education, dealing",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"outcomes. 4.5 Research implications This exploratory study opens several avenues for future research at the intersection of student partnerships and GenAI: • Frameworks for student-AI partnerships: Multiple frameworks have been developed recently addressing issues of GenAI in higher education, dealing with AI literacy [e.g., (Pretorius and de Caux, 2025; Young et al., 2024)], AI ethics [e.g., (Wynants et al., 2025)], and academic integrity [e.g., (Corbin et al., 2025b)]. However, there is a need to further theorise what partnership means when an AI tool is involved. Future scholarship could develop or reﬁne conceptual frameworks that differentiate between human-human partnerships and human-AI co-creation. For example, researchers might examine how values like reciprocity or trust are negotiated in cases where one “partner” (the AI) lacks agency or intention. Building on our ﬁndings, new models could draw from ﬁelds like human-computer interaction to articulate the pedagogical dynamics of triadic partnerships (student-educator-GenAI) (Ahn and Clegg, 2018). • Longitudinal impact on students: While our study noted immediate shifts in student identity and agency, longer- term research could investigate how sustained partnership experiences with GenAI affect students",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"the pedagogical dynamics of triadic partnerships (student-educator-GenAI) (Ahn and Clegg, 2018). • Longitudinal impact on students: While our study noted immediate shifts in student identity and agency, longer- term research could investigate how sustained partnership experiences with GenAI affect students over time. Do students who frequently co-create with GenAI develop greater self-efﬁcacy in problem solving or a stronger sense of belonging in academic communities? Longitudinal studies or post-course interviews could assess whether partnership with GenAI tools contributes to outcomes such as improved creative thinking, adaptability, or even career trajectories in digital education ﬁelds. • Power dynamics: A critical lens is important for future SaP research. Partnerships are not automatically egalitarian; issues of power dynamics always lurk below the surface. Introducing GenAI into the mix raises new questions: Who has access to and familiarity with these tools? Does working with GenAI empower all students equally, or might it reinforce certain hierarchies (e.g., privileging those with more technical experience)? Studies focusing on diverse student populations and contexts (including those with limited technology access) can ensure that the evolution of SaP with GenAI",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"empower all students equally, or might it reinforce certain hierarchies (e.g., privileging those with more technical experience)? Studies focusing on diverse student populations and contexts (including those with limited technology access) can ensure that the evolution of SaP with GenAI remains inclusive. Additionally, qualitative work (such as collaborative autoethnographies) could probe how students and educators negotiate authority and expertise when an AI is part of the team. For example, who is seen as the “expert”, when answers can come from either a human or a machine? • Student voice and ethical considerations: While students’ concerns about GenAI have been acknowledged in literature (Yuriev et al., 2025), most of the GenAI ethics literature to this point has reﬂected educators’ perspective. We advocate for continued use of student voice in researching these issues. Future inquiries might employ participatory or student-led research designs to examine the ethical dimensions of GenAI in education, from a student perspective. Partnership-based research, where students help frame the questions and interpret the impact of AI on their learning, will be vital to address these complex issues, using methods",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,15,"to examine the ethical dimensions of GenAI in education, from a student perspective. Partnership-based research, where students help frame the questions and interpret the impact of AI on their learning, will be vital to address these complex issues, using methods like reﬂective journals, collaborative inquiry groups, or student- designed research projects. 4.6 Limitations Several limitations of this study should be acknowledged. First, the dataset comprised a relatively small number of student participants. While their accounts were rich in detail and insight, they represent a self-selected, high-performing cohort likely predisposed to reﬂective and independent learning. Consequently, the strong themes reported here – particularly agency, conﬁdence, identity shift, and adaptability – may partly reﬂect the characteristics of students who were willing and able Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 15 frontiersin.org",130
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"to participate in an intensive GenAI co-creation project. As such, the ﬁndings may not generalise to broader undergraduate populations, particularly those with less experience in research, autonomy, or technical design. However, the self-selected nature of the participant group should be understood not only as a limitation of the present study, but also as a recognised challenge in SaP practice more broadly (Bovill, 2020; Mercer- Mapstone et al., 2017). SaP and co-creation initiatives are often enacted through small-scale, extracurricular, or project-based opportunities, which may disproportionately attract students who are already conﬁdent, engaged, socially connected, or academically successful. This pattern has led scholars to caution that partnership work can inadvertently reproduce the involvement of the “usual suspects” unless recruitment, access, scaffolding, and curriculum integration are deliberately designed with equity in mind (Mercer-Mapstone et al., 2021). In broader or less self-selecting cohorts, activities similar to the ones described by us may generate additional tensions, including uncertainty, uneven participation, lower conﬁdence with open- ended design work, variable GenAI literacy, and greater need for structured academic support. Thus, our ﬁndings are best read as a",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"similar to the ones described by us may generate additional tensions, including uncertainty, uneven participation, lower conﬁdence with open- ended design work, variable GenAI literacy, and greater need for structured academic support. Thus, our ﬁndings are best read as a situated proof-of-concept for SaP-based GenAI co-creation, rather than evidence that such partnerships will operate identically across all student populations. Second, the dual role of students as both participants and reﬂectors may have shaped the reﬂections toward more constructive or afﬁrming narratives. While this aligns with the SaP tenet of authentic student voice, it may have introduced a degree of positive bias. Future research might incorporate external interviews, observational data, or triangulation with peer or staff perspectives to broaden the interpretive lens. Third, the study focused on English-speaking STEM units at a single institution (Monash University). Institutional culture, disciplinary norms, and language ﬂuency may all have inﬂuenced how students engaged with GenAI and partnership practices. A wider cross-institutional or multilingual/ international study might yield additional insights. 4.7 Conclusions This study examined how undergraduate students co-created and evaluated custom generative AI tools",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"language ﬂuency may all have inﬂuenced how students engaged with GenAI and partnership practices. A wider cross-institutional or multilingual/ international study might yield additional insights. 4.7 Conclusions This study examined how undergraduate students co-created and evaluated custom generative AI tools (cGPTs) through structured partnership projects, using a combination of students- as-partners (SaP) theoretical framing and abductive thematic analysis. Across both the Summer Scholarship and placement contexts, students demonstrated rich engagement with technical and pedagogical dimensions of GenAI development, revealing a dynamic learning process shaped by iteration, unpredictability, and critical reﬂection. Their experiences afﬁrmed key SaP values of agency, reciprocity, reﬂexivity, and authenticity, while also extending them into a new terrain: triadic partnerships involving students, educators, and GenAI tools. By positioning students as partners not only in teaching and learning but in GenAI design, this study contributes a novel theoretical insight: that GenAI, when framed and scaffolded appropriately, can act as a catalyst for partnership, rather than a threat to student agency. Through guided co-creation and reﬂective analysis, students developed technical ﬂuency, ethical awareness, and a sense of ownership over educational",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"that GenAI, when framed and scaffolded appropriately, can act as a catalyst for partnership, rather than a threat to student agency. Through guided co-creation and reﬂective analysis, students developed technical ﬂuency, ethical awareness, and a sense of ownership over educational innovation. These ﬁndings underscore the pedagogical potential of integrating GenAI within a partnership-based framework, one that does not simply teach about GenAI, but involves students in shaping how GenAI is used. As higher education continues to grapple with the opportunities and risks of GenAI, this study offers a hopeful model: one in which students are not passive recipients of technology, but active contributors to its development and meaning. Embedding partnership pedagogy into the design and evaluation of educational AI tools may be key to ensuring its ethical, inclusive, and transformative use in the future. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Monash University Human Research Ethics Committee Project ID 42410, 44572. The studies were conducted in",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Monash University Human Research Ethics Committee Project ID 42410, 44572. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions SL: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Software, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. TL: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Software, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. AM: Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Software. BJ: Investigation, Software, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Formal analysis, Data curation, Methodology. MY: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Data curation, Methodology, Software. BE: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Supervision, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. NK: Formal analysis, Investigation, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing,",180
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,16,"– original draft, Writing – review & editing, Data curation, Methodology, Software. BE: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Supervision, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. NK: Formal analysis, Investigation, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Conceptualization, Supervision. DP: Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition, Project administration, Resources. TR: Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition, Project administration, Resources. EY: Funding acquisition, Project administration, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Supervision, Validation, Visualization. JP: Funding acquisition, Project administration, Resources, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Conceptualization, Investigation, Software, Supervision, Data curation, Methodology. Lo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781 Frontiers in Education 16 frontiersin.org",127
A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,17,"Funding The author(s) declared that ﬁnancial support was received for this work and/or its publication. This project was partially funded by the Monash–Warwick Alliance Education Activation Fund and the Monash University Faculty of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences Education Innovation Grant. Acknowledgments We thank Professor Erica Sloan and Dr Daniel Priebbenow from Monash University Faculty of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences for their leadership and support in BPS3072, including guidance on unit requirements and coordination of placements and associated projects. We also acknowledge Dr Roland Chung and Dr Kristian Kempe for their disciplinary expertise, contribution of unit-speciﬁc cGPT content, and careful review of materials to ensure accuracy and alignment with the learning goals of their respective units. We are grateful to the Monash University Summer Vacation Scholarship Program for supporting the student summer projects that contributed to the development and reﬁnement of the custom GPT resources reported in this manuscript. We also acknowledge the Monash–Warwick Education Activation Fund and the Monash University Faculty of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences Education Innovation Grant for providing partial funding that enabled this work. Conﬂict of interest",180
