A095,A095.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1818781,17,"for plagiarism. The tool used was OpenAI ChatGPT (model: GPT-5.1; provider/source: OpenAI). Publisher’s note All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their afﬁliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher. Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside ﬁgures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artiﬁcial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify any issues, please contact us.Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside ﬁgures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artiﬁcial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever pos",155
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,1,"In GenAI we trust: An investigation of university students’ reliance on and resistance to generative AI in language learning Feifei Wang1 , Nuoen Li2 , Alan C. K. Cheung3* and Gary K. W. Wong1 “No AI is an island” (Johnson & Vera, 2019). Artificial intelligence (AI) does not work in isolation from people; rather, generative AI (GenAI) is set to become integral to society (Lorenz et al., 2023), showing the potential to transform how human learning is deliv- ered, cultivated, and evaluated (Yan et al., 2024). The integration of GenAI in learning has initiated a paradigmatic shift in human-AI relationships from human-AI stimulus– response to human-AI collaboration (Schelble et al., 2024). In human-AI collaboration for learning, GenAI’s role is transitioning from merely a tool to a partner (Glikson & Abstract Advances in generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) have the potential to transform learning. As research increasingly calls for transforming the human-GenAI relation- ship from mere tool use to a collaborative partnership, there remains a notable gap in exploring an important factor influencing this relationship: trust in GenAI, being crucial amidst growing",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,1,"transform learning. As research increasingly calls for transforming the human-GenAI relation- ship from mere tool use to a collaborative partnership, there remains a notable gap in exploring an important factor influencing this relationship: trust in GenAI, being crucial amidst growing concerns about disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI in learner- GenAI interaction primarily via language. This study seeks to address the gap by inves- tigating university students’ trust in GenAI, its related factors of reliance and resistance, and boundary conditions in using GenAI for language learning. The research utilized an explanatory sequential mixed-methods design. In Study 1, a proposed conceptual model within the stimulus-organism-response framework was quantitatively tested through structural equation modeling using a survey. Data were collected from 682 university students who used GenAI in language learning. Study 2 further confirmed the results through 40 qualitative interviews and refined the conceptual model by offering a deeper understanding of the relationships. The findings indicate that trust in GenAI is a significant factor in university students’ use of GenAI for language learning, with its influence on behavioral intention to use mediated",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,1,"the conceptual model by offering a deeper understanding of the relationships. The findings indicate that trust in GenAI is a significant factor in university students’ use of GenAI for language learning, with its influence on behavioral intention to use mediated through reliance and resist- ance. Moreover, forms of the five factors are identified: trust in GenAI, reliance, resist- ance, perceived risk, and actual use. Additionally, two boundary conditions are identi- fied: perceived risk between trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance, and self-directed learning between behavioral intention to use and actual use. These findings provide theoretical, research, and practical implications regarding the appropriate use of GenAI in education. Keywords: Generative artificial intelligence, Trust, Reliance, Resistance, Education Open Access © The Author(s) 2025. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,1,"and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the mate- rial. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://​ creat​iveco​mmons.​org/​licen​ses/​by/4.​0/. RESEARCH ARTICLE Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9 International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education *Correspondence: alancheung@cuhk.edu.hk 1 Centre for Information Technology in Education, Faculty of Education, The University of Hong Kong, Pokfulam, Hong Kong, China 2 Department of Curriculum and Instruction, Faculty of Education, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shatin, Hong Kong, China 3 Department of Educational Administration and Policy,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,1,"in Education, Faculty of Education, The University of Hong Kong, Pokfulam, Hong Kong, China 2 Department of Curriculum and Instruction, Faculty of Education, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shatin, Hong Kong, China 3 Department of Educational Administration and Policy, Faculty of Education, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shatin, Hong Kong, China",53
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,2,"Page 2 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Woolley, 2020; Li et al., 2024; Yan et al., 2024). Learners are also expected to either infer knowledge in new contexts created by GenAI or co-create new knowledge with GenAI (Wang et al., 2024b; Yan et al., 2024). The new collaborative relationship requires learn- ers to develop appropriate trust in GenAI to ensure its appropriate use throughout the learning process, given that trust is a central component in human-AI interaction (Choi et al., 2023; Choung et al., 2023; Jacovi et al., 2021). The importance of understanding learners’ trust in GenAI should not be overstated in the current era of GenAI, especially when a growing body of research is highlighting students’ disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI in education (e.g., Klingbeil et al., 2024; Meyer et al., 2023; Shahzad et al., 2024). Scholars have indicated that students’ disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI, which are associated with their inappropriate levels of trust and trust-related factors of reli- ance and resistance, may bring harmful consequences (Dwivedi et",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,2,"al., 2023; Shahzad et al., 2024). Scholars have indicated that students’ disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI, which are associated with their inappropriate levels of trust and trust-related factors of reli- ance and resistance, may bring harmful consequences (Dwivedi et al., 2023; Jacovi et al., 2021). For one thing, when students disuse GenAI, showing distrust and resist- ance to this powerful technology, they are likely to miss out on its benefits, such as personalized support, diverse learning resources, and timely feedback in the widen- ing digital divide (Yan et al., 2024). For another, when students use GenAI, with their trust in GenAI exceeding GenAI’s trustworthiness, this may lead to misuse or abuse, resulting in a failure to monitor GenAI or biased decision-making (Jacovi et al., 2021). Common examples include the spread of misinformation in GenAI-generated content and the use of GenAI to cheat on exams by generating answers to questions (Dwivedi et al., 2023). When students misuse or abuse GenAI, they show over-reliance on it (Küper & Krämer, 2024). The over-reliance may lead students to overlook GenAI’s flaws, thereby raising",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,2,"GenAI to cheat on exams by generating answers to questions (Dwivedi et al., 2023). When students misuse or abuse GenAI, they show over-reliance on it (Küper & Krämer, 2024). The over-reliance may lead students to overlook GenAI’s flaws, thereby raising ethical concerns such as transparency, plagiarism, and privacy (Zhai et al., 2024). These harmful consequences impair effective learning, erode aca- demic integrity, diminish achievement motivation, undermine cognitive abilities such as problem-solving and critical thinking, and damage the credibility of educational institutions (Abbas et al., 2024; Higgs & Stornaiuolo, 2024; Meyer et al., 2023). Current research on trust in GenAI has been mostly documented in fields other than education, such as computer science (e.g., Schelble et al., 2024), public policy (e.g., Kreps et al., 2023), and management (e.g., Glikson & Woolley, 2020). While the research indicates that trust in GenAI influences individuals’ reliance on or resistance to GenAI’s responses (Klingbeil et al., 2024), existing research in the field of education is limited, primarily focusing on teachers’ trust in AI in relation to their acceptance of AI in teaching (e.g., Choi et",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,2,"individuals’ reliance on or resistance to GenAI’s responses (Klingbeil et al., 2024), existing research in the field of education is limited, primarily focusing on teachers’ trust in AI in relation to their acceptance of AI in teaching (e.g., Choi et al., 2023; Nazaretsky et al., 2022). Despite the increasing use of GenAI among university students, who are crucial stakeholders in integrating GenAI into learning, very limited empirical research has focused on factors that drive their GenAI use (Strzelecki, 2023). Less is known about university students’ trust in GenAI in learning. As more and more theoretical discussions, interviews, com- mentaries, and reviews are calling for research on students’ trust in GenAI (Higgs & Stornaiuolo, 2024; Hınız, 2024; Kelly et al., 2023; Law, 2024; Zhai et al., 2024), few empirical studies have investigated university students’ trust in GenAI in learning. Such an investigation of university students’ trust in GenAI is crucial for relevant stakeholders such as educators, researchers, policymakers, and educational institu- tions to understand students’ reliance and resistance that lead to disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI, shedding light on the",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,2,"investigation of university students’ trust in GenAI is crucial for relevant stakeholders such as educators, researchers, policymakers, and educational institu- tions to understand students’ reliance and resistance that lead to disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI, shedding light on the development of effective strategies to integrate",46
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,3,"Page 3 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 GenAI into teaching, learning, and policymaking (Abbas et al., 2024; Meyer et al., 2023). Therefore, in response to the important needs, the study contributes to existing literature from three aspects. First, to the best of our knowledge, this study is the first empirical study in education that aims to explore the relationship between uni- versity students’ trust in GenAI and its related factors of reliance and resistance. We focus on the use of GenAI in language learning, as language learning is a major area significantly affected by large language models like ChatGPT (Hınız, 2024), and this area stands at an inflection point calling for further research to ensure ethical, valid, and reliable use of GenAI (Pack & Maloney, 2023). Investigation in this highly influ- enced area at an inflection point may provide insights into other areas of education. Second, this study makes theoretical contributions by constructing and consolidating a refined conceptual model of learners’ trust in GenAI. This model is grounded in the stimulus-organism-response framework,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,3,"area at an inflection point may provide insights into other areas of education. Second, this study makes theoretical contributions by constructing and consolidating a refined conceptual model of learners’ trust in GenAI. This model is grounded in the stimulus-organism-response framework, including the stimulus factor (i.e., trust in GenAI), the organism factors (i.e., reliance and resistance), the response factors (i.e., behavioral intention to use, self-directed learning, and actual use), and boundary conditions (i.e., perceived risk and self-directed learning). Third, this study addresses the need to deepen understanding of psychological and cognitive factors underlying university students’ behavioral intention to use and actual use of GenAI in learning (Kelly et al., 2023; Law, 2024). Based on the aforementioned academic discourse and the identification of the three salient gaps in both theory and practice from the literature review, this study seeks to address the following three research questions (RQs). RQ1: Based on the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) framework, to what extent does the stimulus factor (i.e., trust in GenAI) relate to the organism factors (i.e., reliance and resistance) among university students who use GenAI for language",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,3,"following three research questions (RQs). RQ1: Based on the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) framework, to what extent does the stimulus factor (i.e., trust in GenAI) relate to the organism factors (i.e., reliance and resistance) among university students who use GenAI for language learning? RQ2: To what extent do the organism factors in the SOR framework (i.e., reliance and resistance) relate to the response factors (i.e., behavioral intention to use, self- directed learning, and actual use) among university students who use GenAI for language learning? RQ3: What additional attributes describe the stimulus, organism, and response factors of the SOR framework, and the circumscribed relationships defined within RQ1 and RQ2? Literature review Use of GenAI in language learning Studies on the use of GenAI in language learning can mainly be classified into two categories regarding productivity and psychological aspects (Law, 2024). The first cat- egory relates to productivity gains that have been produced by GenAI. Some stud- ies have employed descriptive statistics and experimental methods to examine the impact of productivity gains from GenAI use on text generation, grammatical error",176
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,3,"egory relates to productivity gains that have been produced by GenAI. Some stud- ies have employed descriptive statistics and experimental methods to examine the impact of productivity gains from GenAI use on text generation, grammatical error",36
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,4,"Page 4 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 correction, and sentence and paragraph refinement (e.g., Guo et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2024b). This category mainly indicated that GenAI was able to facilitate content generation, grading, and language enhancements, thereby improving productivity in language learning. Studies in the second category have explored factors influencing the use of GenAI in language learning from the perspectives of learners. These studies indicated that mul- tiple factors related to learners affected GenAI use. For instance, learners’ behavioral intention to use and motivation concurrently predicted their actual use of GenAI (Zheng et al., 2024). Positive attitudes toward the usefulness of GenAI predicted actual use of GenAI (Liu & Ma, 2023). However, these studies have insufficiently explored psychologi- cal or cognitive factors that may influence learners’ use of GenAI (Kelly et al., 2023). Continuing in the second category, only a few studies have explored the notewor- thy psychological factors of learners, i.e., trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI, that affect language learners’ use of GenAI, along with",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,4,"(Kelly et al., 2023). Continuing in the second category, only a few studies have explored the notewor- thy psychological factors of learners, i.e., trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI, that affect language learners’ use of GenAI, along with relevant cognitive factors. Trust in AI is particularly important for emerging technologies like GenAI in light of wide- spread concerns about data security and accuracy (Ma, 2024). Trust in GenAI is associ- ated with reliance and resistance, which in turn influence their acceptance and actual use (Chavoshi & Hamidi, 2019). The limited existing studies have highlighted concerns about blind trust, over-reliance, and complete resistance to GenAI, in relation to the negative impact on cognitive abilities such as critical thinking, problem-solving, and creativity (e.g., Higgs & Stornaiuolo, 2024; Law, 2024; Santiago Jr et al., 2023). While the limited existing studies have enhanced our understanding of the potential influence of the three important psychological factors: trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI, much less attention has been paid to how the three factors interact with each other. Trust in GenAI in",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,4,"enhanced our understanding of the potential influence of the three important psychological factors: trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI, much less attention has been paid to how the three factors interact with each other. Trust in GenAI in language learning The nature of trust in AI The concept of trust is crucial across many domains involving interaction between humans and other parties, whether the parties are other individuals, technologies, or automated systems. Much of the earlier research focused on the importance of inter- personal trust in facilitating communication and cooperation among individuals (e.g., Driscoll, 1978; Shapiro, 1987). According to Mayer et al. (1995), interpersonal trust typically involves evaluations of other individuals’ abilities, benevolence (i.e., the extent to which the individual is believed to have positive intentions toward the trustor), and integrity (i.e., the extent to which the individual adheres to principles acceptable by the trustor). Next, with the advent of the Internet, research on trust expanded from a focus on interpersonal trust to encompass trust in technology (e.g., Benbasat & Wang, 2005; Venkatesh et al., 2012). Research on",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,4,"adheres to principles acceptable by the trustor). Next, with the advent of the Internet, research on trust expanded from a focus on interpersonal trust to encompass trust in technology (e.g., Benbasat & Wang, 2005; Venkatesh et al., 2012). Research on trust in technology emphasizes the importance of evaluating technology’s functionality rather than abilities, helpfulness rather than benev- olence, and reliability rather than integrity when determining the acceptance and use of technology (Mcknight et al., 2011). In a similar vein, research on trust in automated systems has extended further, driven by advancements in automated systems that were capable of actively selecting data, transforming information, and making decisions (Lee & See, 2004). The studies indicate that user trust in automated systems is a dynamic construct influenced by factors related to systems (e.g., reliability and transparency),",133
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,5,"Page 5 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 users, and the context in which the interaction occurs (e.g., Balfe et al., 2018; Merritt et al., 2013). Overall, the concept of trust has continuously evolved, with different foci from interpersonal trust to trust in technology, and subsequently to trust in automated systems. The increasing cognitive complexity of technology explains a growing interest in trust in AI. Trust tends to be less salient in well-structured and stable environments, where the emphasis on order and stability reduces transactional uncertainty (Poppo et al., 2016). However, as technology has recently advanced to dynamic and self-adapting AI that can be applied in different learning contexts, learning environments become increasingly complex and uncertain (Wu et al., 2024). Therefore, trust in AI is critical in helping learners cope with the cognitive complexity and uncertainty in diverse learning environments (Choung et al., 2023; Lee & See, 2004). Trust in AI is conceptualized as an individual’s expectancy of AI’s technically com- petent role performance in the interests of the individual, especially in tasks",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,5,"complexity and uncertainty in diverse learning environments (Choung et al., 2023; Lee & See, 2004). Trust in AI is conceptualized as an individual’s expectancy of AI’s technically com- petent role performance in the interests of the individual, especially in tasks where AI’s decision-making is important to the individual (Barber, 1983; Mayer et al., 1995; Sol- berg et al., 2022). The definition highlights the key elements of trust in AI, including (1) the belief that AI will help achieve the individual’s goals, especially in important and uncertain contexts, (2) the individual’s willingness to be vulnerable by delegating partial responsibility for actions to AI, and (3) the individual’s acceptance of potential risks or consequences resulting from the delegation of responsibility (Choung et al., 2023; Glik- son & Woolley, 2020; Lee & See, 2004). The aforementioned definition distinguishes trust in AI from the related yet distinct concepts that may also influence use behavior of AI: awareness, familiarity, reliance/ resistance, and confidence. First, awareness of AI is an individual’s recognition or under- standing of AI’s existence, its potential applications in specific tasks, and the",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,5,"related yet distinct concepts that may also influence use behavior of AI: awareness, familiarity, reliance/ resistance, and confidence. First, awareness of AI is an individual’s recognition or under- standing of AI’s existence, its potential applications in specific tasks, and the associated ethical and legal concerns (Scantamburlo et al., 2025). Individuals may have awareness of AI without necessarily trusting it, in which vulnerability is minimal or absent. Sec- ond, familiarity with AI refers to an individual’s accumulated experience or knowledge gained through repeated interactions with AI (Arce-Urriza et al., 2025). Individuals who are familiar with AI do not necessarily trust AI, depending on the satisfaction of interac- tions with AI (Komiak & Benbasat, 2006). Third, reliance on or resistance to AI reflects an individual’s positive or negative evaluations or attitudes, and willingness or reluctance to adopt AI (Klingbeil et al., 2024; van Dongen & van Maanen, 2013). Reliance on and resistance to AI, as attitudes, can be shaped by trust in AI as a belief (Cheng et al., 2022). Fourth, both confidence and trust in AI involve expectations that may lead",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,5,"van Dongen & van Maanen, 2013). Reliance on and resistance to AI, as attitudes, can be shaped by trust in AI as a belief (Cheng et al., 2022). Fourth, both confidence and trust in AI involve expectations that may lead to disap- pointment, but trust differs from confidence in that trust requires prior engagement and involves recognizing and accepting potential risks (Mayer et al., 1995). An individual can have confidence in AI without necessarily trusting it through prior engagement with AI. Differentiating trust in AI from the similar concepts further clarifies its nature. The relationship between trust in AI and AI capability in defining the appropriate use of AI Inappropriate trust in AI, which is associated with disuse, misuse, and abuse, partly depends on how well trust in AI aligns with AI capabilities (Lee & See, 2004). As Fig. 1 shows, supporting appropriate trust is important for the appropriate use of AI. Trust can be calibrated when an individual’s trust in AI matches AI’s objective capabilities.",166
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,6,"Page 6 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Overtrust represents poor calibration in which trust exceeds AI capabilities, and dis- trust reflects that trust falls short of AI capabilities. To calibrate appropriate trust related to the appropriate use of AI, when trust is too high, trust dampening strategies can be employed, such as demonstrating technology failures and providing likelihood alerts (de Visser et al., 2020). When trust is too low, trust repair strategies can be applied, such as clearly stating the level of user control and demonstrating AI proficiency (Israelsen & Ahmed, 2019). When trust in AI matches AI capabilities, AI can be used appropriately. In educational settings, the appropriate use of AI occurs when individuals actively engage with AI, critically evaluate AI-generated outputs, maintain ethical awareness, transparently acknowledge the use of AI, and preserve their own agency during inter- actions with AI (Symeou et al., 2025). Conversely, the appropriateness of AI use can be compromised by disuse, misuse, and abuse (Jacovi et al., 2021). Disuse refers to avoid- ance of AI capabilities,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,6,"and preserve their own agency during inter- actions with AI (Symeou et al., 2025). Conversely, the appropriateness of AI use can be compromised by disuse, misuse, and abuse (Jacovi et al., 2021). Disuse refers to avoid- ance of AI capabilities, even when its capabilities may support learning. For example, students who lack trust in GenAI may be less motivated to use it, even when supporting resources are available, as they focus on its limitations and hazards (Zhang et al., 2024a). Misuse refers to unintended or inappropriate use due to misunderstanding, a lack of rel- evant expertise, or insufficient critical judgment, potentially resulting in decision biases (Parasuraman & Riley, 1997). For example, learners might use ChatGPT in writing with- out verifying accuracy, unintentionally reinforcing incorrect writing usage (Dergaa et al., 2023). Abuse involves intentionally exploiting AI capabilities for personal benefits or unfair advantages, typically resulting in ethical violations (Kendall & Teixeira da Silva, 2023). For example, language learners might deliberately submit GenAI-generated essays as their original work, consciously committing plagiarism (Meyer et al., 2023). Disuse, misuse, and abuse of AI, all",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,6,"unfair advantages, typically resulting in ethical violations (Kendall & Teixeira da Silva, 2023). For example, language learners might deliberately submit GenAI-generated essays as their original work, consciously committing plagiarism (Meyer et al., 2023). Disuse, misuse, and abuse of AI, all associated with trust in AI, have become important concerns impacting the appropriate use of AI in education. Fig. 1 The Relationship Between Trust in AI and AI Capability in Defining the Appropriate Use of AI (Adapted from de Visser et al., 2020 and Lee & See, 2004)",87
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,7,"Page 7 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Theoretical framework The overarching theoretical framework is the stimulus-organism-response (SOR) frame- work, as extant studies have demonstrated its predictive power regarding university students’ reactions to emerging technologies (e.g., Bai, 2025; Zhao et al., 2020). Addi- tionally, we adopt theoretical constructs from Trust Calibration Theory (Lee & See, 2004; Parasuraman & Riley, 1997) and the Theory of Reasoned Action (Ajzen & Fish- bein, 1980; Fishbein & Ajzen, 1975) to establish the relationships among constructs. The SOR framework suggests that various aspects of triggers (i.e., the stimulus) evoke inter- mediary psychological or cognitive states within individuals (i.e., the organism), which in turn influence their behavioral responses (i.e., the response) to the triggers (Mehra- bian & Russell, 1974). First, the stimulus component refers to factors that affect individuals’ internal states (Mehrabian & Russell, 1974). The traditional SOR framework primarily emphasizes external stimuli influencing individuals, but recent research indicates that stimuli can also be internal, such as brand trust (Gong et al., 2023) and health anxiety (Goyal et al.,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,7,"internal states (Mehrabian & Russell, 1974). The traditional SOR framework primarily emphasizes external stimuli influencing individuals, but recent research indicates that stimuli can also be internal, such as brand trust (Gong et al., 2023) and health anxiety (Goyal et al., 2021), particularly in contexts involving interaction with AI technology (Vafaei-Zadeh et al., 2024). In GenAI-assisted language learning, trust in GenAI impacts learners’ con- fidence and subsequent decision to use the technology, while a lack of trust leads to skepticism and reduces the likelihood of use (Shahzad et al., 2024). Thus, trust in GenAI acts as a stimulus that influences learners’ internal experiences. Second, the organism component, including intermediary psychological or cognitive states, mediates the relationship between the stimulus and the response (Mehrabian & Russell, 1974). This study considers reliance on and resistance to GenAI as organism fac- tors, based on Trust Calibration Theory that suggests that trust influences reliance on and resistance to automation (Lee & See, 2004; Parasuraman & Riley, 1997). The rela- tionships have also been theoretically and empirically supported in other studies (e.g., Cheng et al., 2022;",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,7,"Trust Calibration Theory that suggests that trust influences reliance on and resistance to automation (Lee & See, 2004; Parasuraman & Riley, 1997). The rela- tionships have also been theoretically and empirically supported in other studies (e.g., Cheng et al., 2022; Klingbeil et al., 2024; Mayer et al., 1995; Solberg et al., 2022). Third, the response component refers to the outcome, the reaction, or the final action (Mehrabian & Russell, 1974). The study includes behavioral intention to use, actual use, and self-directed learning as response factors. Empirical evidence indicates that in the final phase of the SOR framework, self-directed learning, defined as learners’ ability to actively manage or guide their own learning behaviors, influences the relationship between behavioral intention to use and actual use (Esiyok et al., 2024; Lai et al., 2022). Finally, the proposed factors in the SOR components have been supported by the The- ory of Reasoned Action (Ajzen & Fishbein, 1980; Fishbein & Ajzen, 1975), which sug- gests that behaviors (i.e., actual use in this study) are driven by intentions (i.e., behavioral intention to use), and intentions are",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,7,"been supported by the The- ory of Reasoned Action (Ajzen & Fishbein, 1980; Fishbein & Ajzen, 1975), which sug- gests that behaviors (i.e., actual use in this study) are driven by intentions (i.e., behavioral intention to use), and intentions are influenced by attitudes (i.e., reliance and resistance). Attitudes in turn are based on beliefs (i.e., trust in GenAI). Conceptual model development and hypotheses for Study 1 Stimulus: trust in GenAI As Fig. 2 shows, trust in AI is a major predictor that affects behavioral intention to use AI (Choi et al., 2023). Particularly with GenAI, trust is considered crucial because it inherently involves uncertainty and risks (Law, 2024). This characteristic of GenAI critically affects",114
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,8,"Page 8 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 learners’ trust, which may result in a lower acceptance rate (Higgs & Stornaiuolo, 2024). Possible reasons include the imperfect algorithms of GenAI, ethical dilemmas (e.g., trans- parency, equality, and beneficence), and disruptive assessment (Yan et al., 2024). Trust in GenAI may mitigate the adverse impact of uncertainty and risks, potentially influencing behavioral intention to use GenAI. Trust in GenAI influences whether individuals rely on GenAI advice or resist it (Kling- beil et al., 2024). The emergence of GenAI and its increasing accessibility have shifted the dynamics of human-AI interaction, gradually transforming machines from mere tools to partners (C. Wang et al., 2024a, 2024c), and highlighting the importance of trust in GenAI. When used appropriately, GenAI has considerable potential to improve learning, so fos- tering appropriate trust is vital (Klingbeil et al., 2024). Excessive trust might lead to over- reliance, causing learners to blindly rely on GenAI, even when such trust is not warranted (Li & Little, 2023). Conversely, a lack of trust might result in",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,8,"trust is vital (Klingbeil et al., 2024). Excessive trust might lead to over- reliance, causing learners to blindly rely on GenAI, even when such trust is not warranted (Li & Little, 2023). Conversely, a lack of trust might result in resistance, leading to low rates of adoption or complete rejection of beneficial technology (Orbaiz & Arce-Urriza, 2024). Therefore, we hypothesize: H1 Trust in GenAI positively influences behavioral intention to use. H2 Trust in GenAI positively influences reliance. H3 Trust in GenAI negatively influences resistance. Fig. 2 The Proposed Conceptual Model",90
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,9,"Page 9 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Organism: reliance on and resistance to GenAI Reliance on or resistance to GenAI reflects individuals’ positive or negative evaluations toward GenAI (Klingbeil et al., 2024; van Dongen & van Maanen, 2013). When indi- viduals opt for GenAI judgments, they tend to show reliance on GenAI, and when they prefer their own judgments, they tend to display resistance to GenAI. Individuals’ evalu- ations when choosing between GenAI and their own judgments are influenced by fac- tors such as overconfidence, the quality of GenAI outputs, and the specific domains of judgments. Typically, individuals favor GenAI in domains that require concrete accuracy standards, and they prefer their own judgments in domains based on personal experi- ence (Mahmud et al., 2022). Regarding reliance on GenAI, it has been found that reliance positively influences learners’ adoption of GenAI. The more reliance learners have on AI, the more likely they are to use it in learning (Seo et al., 2021). Furthermore, reliance on GenAI is a crucial intermediary between trust in",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,9,"that reliance positively influences learners’ adoption of GenAI. The more reliance learners have on AI, the more likely they are to use it in learning (Seo et al., 2021). Furthermore, reliance on GenAI is a crucial intermediary between trust in GenAI and learners’ adoption of GenAI (Chavoshi & Hamidi, 2019). Students with higher levels of trust in GenAI are more likely to rely on it, increasing their behavioral intention to use and actual use in practical learning activities (Shahzad et al., 2024). As for resistance to GenAI, it has been suggested that resistance negatively affects learners’ use of GenAI, and potentially influences the link between trust in GenAI and adoption of GenAI (Higgs & Stornaiuolo, 2024). Learners with lower levels of trust in GenAI are more likely to resist it, decreasing their behavioral intention to use and actual use (Nazaretsky et al., 2022). Therefore, we hypothesize: H4 Reliance positively influences behavioral intention to use. H5 Resistance negatively influences behavioral intention to use. H6 Trust in GenAI positively influences behavioral intention to use via reliance and resistance. H7 Trust in GenAI",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,9,"et al., 2022). Therefore, we hypothesize: H4 Reliance positively influences behavioral intention to use. H5 Resistance negatively influences behavioral intention to use. H6 Trust in GenAI positively influences behavioral intention to use via reliance and resistance. H7 Trust in GenAI positively influences actual use via behavioral intention to use, via reli- ance and behavioral intention to use, and via resistance and behavioral intention to use. Response: behavioral intention to use, self‑directed learning, and actual use Behavioral intention to use refers to an individual’s subjective probability of using a particular technology (Venkatesh et al., 2012). In GenAI-assisted language learning, behavioral intention to use means the degree to which learners intend to use GenAI in language learning. It is a significant predictor of actual use (Liu & Ma, 2023), which in",129
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,10,"Page 10 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 this study refers to students’ practical implementation of GenAI in language learning. Language learners’ actual practices of using GenAI are based on their behavioral inten- tion to use GenAI for language learning purposes (Zheng et al., 2024). Self-directed learning has been reconceptualized to include three aspects: learners’ personal attributes (e.g., moral, emotional, and intellectual management), autonomous process (i.e., including learner autonomy over the learning process), and learning con- text (i.e., the environmental factors) that influence how learners use GenAI to learn lan- guage (Li et al., 2024; Song & Hill, 2007). Students with higher levels of self-directed learning have more positive personal attributes (e.g., attitudes and self-efficacy) and cognitive strategies, affecting their engagement and actual use of GenAI in learning (Li et al., 2024). Furthermore, self-directed learning can potentially influence the relation- ship between behavioral intention to use and actual use (Lai et al., 2022). Students with higher levels of self-directed learning are more likely to convert their behavioral inten- tion to use into actual",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,10,"self-directed learning can potentially influence the relation- ship between behavioral intention to use and actual use (Lai et al., 2022). Students with higher levels of self-directed learning are more likely to convert their behavioral inten- tion to use into actual use. Therefore, we hypothesize: H8 Behavioral intention to use positively influences actual use. H9 Self-directed learning positively influences actual use. H10 The relationship between behavioral intention to use and actual use is moderated by self- directed learning. Figure 2 displays the conceptual model based on the developed hypotheses. Method This study employed a mixed-methods research design grounded in pragmatic philoso- phy, which is most appropriate given the nature of the RQs. Such a research design is not confined by a single research philosophy or paradigmatic assumptions, but instead offers inclusive, pluralistic, and complementary approaches that provide the best chance of obtaining useful answers by combining deduction (i.e., testing theories and hypotheses), induction (i.e., discovering patterns and rich meanings), and abduction (i.e., uncover- ing the best set of explanations for understandings results) within a pragmatic rationale (Johnson & Onwuegbuzie, 2004; Venkatesh",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,10,"obtaining useful answers by combining deduction (i.e., testing theories and hypotheses), induction (i.e., discovering patterns and rich meanings), and abduction (i.e., uncover- ing the best set of explanations for understandings results) within a pragmatic rationale (Johnson & Onwuegbuzie, 2004; Venkatesh et al., 2016). Specifically, this study followed an explanatory sequential mixed-methods design (Creswell & Plano Clark, 2017), where a quantitative phase was initially performed, followed by a subsequent qualitative phase to understand and extend the quantitative results in depth. This design is preferred in AI-related educational studies for identifying mechanisms that may remain obscured by purely quantitative approaches and for an in-depth exploration of findings from quan- titative results through qualitative data analyses (Liu et al., 2024). RQ1 and RQ2 were",121
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,11,"Page 11 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 addressed through a survey in Study 1, and the results were further explored through a subsequent explanatory qualitative analysis in Study 2. Building upon the survey results, Study 2 addressed RQ3 with semi-structured interviews and a thematic analysis, quali- tatively exploring the relationships posited within the theoretical framework (Fraenkel et al., 2018). The procedure for this explanatory sequential mixed-methods design is dis- played in Table 1. Participants Study 1 adopted a purposive sampling strategy to recruit participants with an online sur- vey. This sampling strategy ensures that participants are most likely to provide appropri- ate and useful information, maximizing the efficient use of limited research resources (Campbell et al., 2020). This study recruited eligible participants based on two criteria. First, they were students from Chinese universities. Second, they had previously used GenAI in language learning. Between June 2024 and August 2024, university students who self-identified as being from Chinese universities and had used GenAI in language learning were invited to participate. An anonymous link from",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,11,"universities. Second, they had previously used GenAI in language learning. Between June 2024 and August 2024, university students who self-identified as being from Chinese universities and had used GenAI in language learning were invited to participate. An anonymous link from Wenjuanxing, i.e., a widely used online survey platform in China, was distributed via social media tools such as WeChat, QQ, and Weibo. Of the 700 survey responses initially collected, 682 were valid and included in analyses after applying the two criteria. There were no missing data in the responses, as the submission was set on the online platform to be possible only after all items had been completed. Demographic information of the participants is shown in Table 2. Table 1 The Procedure for the Explanatory Sequential Mixed-Methods Design (Adapted from Ivankova et al., 2006) Research question (RQ) Phase Procedure Product RQ1 and RQ2 (Quantita- tive) Quantitative data collec- tion Cross-sectional online survey (N = 682) Purposive sampling Numeric data Quantitative data analysis Preliminary analyses Structural equation modeling AMOS 29.0 and IBM SPSS Statistics 26.0 Descriptive statistics Measurement model and structural",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,11,"RQ1 and RQ2 (Quantita- tive) Quantitative data collec- tion Cross-sectional online survey (N = 682) Purposive sampling Numeric data Quantitative data analysis Preliminary analyses Structural equation modeling AMOS 29.0 and IBM SPSS Statistics 26.0 Descriptive statistics Measurement model and structural model Mediation analysis, mod- eration analysis, and path analysis Bridging quantitative and qualitative phases Developing interview questions Interview guide RQ3 (Qualitative) Qualitative data collection Individual semi-structured interviews (N = 40) Purposive sampling: Quota sampling and maximal variation sampling Text data: Interview tran- scripts and memos Audio recordings Qualitative data analysis Thematic analysis Within-case and across- case comparisons of themes NVivo 14 Keywords, codes, and themes Similar and different themes Cross-thematic matrix Integrating quantitative and qualitative results Interpreting and explain- ing quantitative and qualitative results The refined conceptual model Theoretical, research, and practical implications",133
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,12,"Page 12 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 For Study 2, to develop an in-depth exploration of the results from Study 1, we adopted two purposive sampling strategies to select eligible participants from those in Study 1 who agreed to participate in Study 2 (Creswell & Guetterman, 2019). First, we employed quota sampling to ensure representation of key demographic categories (e.g., gender, age, and education level). Specifically, we aligned the quotas with the distributions in Study 1. Within each category, we calculated participants’ summed mean scores and category means from survey responses, and then selected partici- pants from each category whose mean scores fell within one standard error of the mean. This ensured that we selected participants who closely represented the cate- gory characteristics in each subgroup. Second, we used maximal variation sampling, in which we added participants with diverse characteristics within the categories. This approach allowed us to identify various dimensions of the categories and pre- serve multiple perspectives within the same categories. Altogether, as Table 2 dis- plays, the two strategies",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,12,"in which we added participants with diverse characteristics within the categories. This approach allowed us to identify various dimensions of the categories and pre- serve multiple perspectives within the same categories. Altogether, as Table 2 dis- plays, the two strategies generated a sample of 40 eligible participants, thereby trying to preserve multiple viewpoints while maintaining the demographic proportions from Study 1. Table 2 Demographic Information of Participants (Study 1: N = 682; Study 2: N = 40) Characteristic Category Frequency (Study 1) Percentage (Study 1) Frequency (Study 2) Percentage (Study 2) Gender Male 297 43.55% 19 47.50% Female 385 56.45% 21 52.50% Age Below 16 4 0.59% 1 2.50% 17–20 336 49.27% 21 52.50% 21–24 285 41.79% 15 37.50% 25–28 50 7.33% 2 5.00% Above 29 7 1.03% 1 2.50% Education level Undergraduate 587 86.07% 29 72.50% Postgraduate 95 13.93% 11 27.50% Field of study Arts & humanities 192 28.15% 15 37.50% Social sciences 153 22.43% 12 30.00% Science & engineering 256 37.54% 8 20.00% Medicine 81 11.88% 5 12.50% Location of the university Eastern China 130 19.06% 12 30.00%",180
