A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,14,"Page 14 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Quantitative data collection and analysis Survey instruments To collect quantitative data, this study adapted established questionnaires to measure all latent factors with 30 items to fit the specific context of the research, as detailed in Table 3. The survey was translated following a forward and backward translation pro- cedure. In the forward translation, three bilingual translators whose native language was Chinese independently translated the survey from English to Chinese, and then discussed any discrepancies to reach a consensus. Next, two native English-speaking translators independently performed backward translations of the consensus Chinese version and provided comments for further modifications. Finally, all translators and the research team discussed and resolved any remaining discrepancies. The six-point Lik- ert scale from “strongly disagree” to “strongly agree” was used to measure participants’ responses. The even-numbered six-point scale was used to minimize risks related to deviations in responses, aiming to improve discrimination and reliability in responses from self-reported surveys (Chomeya, 2010). Pilot testing of the survey was conducted with 53 students",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,14,"measure participants’ responses. The even-numbered six-point scale was used to minimize risks related to deviations in responses, aiming to improve discrimination and reliability in responses from self-reported surveys (Chomeya, 2010). Pilot testing of the survey was conducted with 53 students from Chinese universities who had experience using GenAI in language learning. According to their feedback, a few modifications to the wording were made to enhance the clarity and comprehensiveness of the items. Quantitative data analysis Study 1 is based on relationship testing, so a quantitative analysis was conducted to test hypotheses. We analyzed the data following a two-step structural equation mod- eling (SEM) approach with AMOS 29.0 and IBM SPSS Statistics 26.0 (Collier, 2020; Kline, 2023). First, basic descriptive statistics of all measured factors were exam- ined as preliminary analyses. Specifically, we assessed the normality of the data by Table 3 (continued) Factor Item Reference Actual use 22. I use GenAI to help understand lan- guage learning materials. Lai et al. (2022) 23. I use GenAI to acquire more language knowledge. 24. I use GenAI to help express my thoughts.",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,14,3 (continued) Factor Item Reference Actual use 22. I use GenAI to help understand lan- guage learning materials. Lai et al. (2022) 23. I use GenAI to acquire more language knowledge. 24. I use GenAI to help express my thoughts. 25. I use GenAI to seek language learning strategies and tips. 26. I use GenAI to check my understand- ing. 27. I use GenAI to check my learning progress. 28. I use GenAI to expand opportunities to use language. 29. I use GenAI to sustain motivation and interest in language learning. 30. I use GenAI to ask for support and help.,101
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,15,"Page 15 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 checking skewness and kurtosis of items. Following the initial analysis, we proceeded to assess reliability of the survey by computing Cronbach’s alpha values. We then evaluated convergent validity of the factors through the standardized factor loading of each item, and the composite reliability (CR) and the average variance extracted (AVE) for each factor. We also examined discriminant validity between the factors by comparing inter-factor correlation coefficients with the square roots of AVE for the factors. Second, we used SEM with maximum likelihood parameter estimates to test the mediation effects of reliance and resistance in the relationship between uni- versity students’ trust in GenAI and behavioral intentions to use, and the moderation effect of self-directed learning in the relationship between behavioral intentions to use and actual use. The mediation effects were achieved by estimating the measure- ment model and path analysis results (Collier, 2020). The indices used to measure the overall fit of the model included the chi-square statistic (χ2), the ratio of chi-square to degrees",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,15,"use. The mediation effects were achieved by estimating the measure- ment model and path analysis results (Collier, 2020). The indices used to measure the overall fit of the model included the chi-square statistic (χ2), the ratio of chi-square to degrees of freedom (χ2/df), the comparative fit index (CFI), the incremental fit index (IFI), the Tucker-Lewis index (TLI), the normative fit index (NFI), the standardized root mean square residual (SRMR), and the root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) (Schreiber et al., 2006). According to the literature, the threshold levels for model fit indices that indicate adequacy are as follows: χ2/df < 3, CFI, IFI, TLI, and NFI > 0.9, and SRMR and RMSEA < 0.08 (Hair et al., 2010; Kline, 2023). For media- tion analyses, we used bootstrapping with 5000 bootstrap resamples to assess indirect effects within mediation models, utilizing a two-tailed test, 95% bias-corrected con- fidence interval (CI), p-values, and user-defined estimands to determine the paths of indirect effects. For moderation analyses, we adopted the matched-pairs interaction approach to test moderation in a full structural model (Collier, 2020). This",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,15,"a two-tailed test, 95% bias-corrected con- fidence interval (CI), p-values, and user-defined estimands to determine the paths of indirect effects. For moderation analyses, we adopted the matched-pairs interaction approach to test moderation in a full structural model (Collier, 2020). This approach reduces the issues associated with the full indicator interaction and is thus recom- mended for evaluating moderation involving latent factors (Collier, 2020; Marsh et al., 2004). Qualitative data collection and analysis Study 2 aims to build upon and corroborate the quantitative results of Study 1, while providing deeper insights that enhance the theoretical and empirical undertones gained from Study 1. The final results from both studies are integrated into the refined conceptual model, as presented in Fig. 5. Study design Forty semi-structured interviews, lasting between 30 and 40 min, were conducted online to uncover the rich narrative meanings underlying the model proposed in Study 1. We first reviewed the participants’ responses to the survey, and then conducted in-depth interviews using an interview guide (see Table 4) developed from the literature (Glikson & Woolley, 2020; Jacovi et al., 2021). Communication",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,15,"the model proposed in Study 1. We first reviewed the participants’ responses to the survey, and then conducted in-depth interviews using an interview guide (see Table 4) developed from the literature (Glikson & Woolley, 2020; Jacovi et al., 2021). Communication with the participants was designed to gain detailed insights into their trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI, and use of GenAI for language learning. Such intensive communications are important for com- prehending and consolidating theoretical frameworks (Xu & Zammit, 2020).",82
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,16,"Page 16 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Qualitative data analysis A thematic analysis was performed to analyze the interviews (Creswell & Guetter- man, 2019). Each interview was audio-taped, transcribed, and analyzed with NVivo 14. The analysis adheres to the guidelines proposed by Naeem et al. (2023) for conducting a thematic analysis to develop a conceptual model, including six steps: (1) transcrib- ing, familiarizing with data, and selecting quotations, (2) selecting keywords, (3) cod- ing, (4) constructing themes, (5) conceptualizing through interpreting keywords, codes, and themes, and (6) developing a conceptual model. The credibility of the results was established through separate coding by the authors, code verification by two independ- ent researchers, and collaborative discussions among all the authors and independent researchers to resolve inconsistencies and reach a consensus (Roberts et al., 2019; Xu & Zammit, 2020). Results The quantitative results Measurement model Table 5 presents the descriptive results of 30 items. The item means ranged from 2.890 to 4.776, with the standard deviation values fluctuating between 0.838 and 1.478. The skewness and",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,16,"Xu & Zammit, 2020). Results The quantitative results Measurement model Table 5 presents the descriptive results of 30 items. The item means ranged from 2.890 to 4.776, with the standard deviation values fluctuating between 0.838 and 1.478. The skewness and kurtosis indices for the items did not exceed |3.0| and |8.0|, respectively (Kline, 2023), which indicated that the normal distribution of the data was not violated. Therefore, SEM analyses were further performed. The measurement model assessed the reliability and validity of the survey, ensuring that the dataset met the requisite assumptions for conducting advanced statistical tests. Table 4 Semi-Structured Interview Guide Main questions Examples of follow-up questions 1. Can you describe your initial thoughts or attitudes about using GenAI in language learning? Are there changes in your initial thoughts or attitudes? Why or why not? 2. Do you trust GenAI in language learning? Why or why not? What helps you decide whether GenAI’s responses are trustworthy or untrustworthy in language learning? Does the trustworthiness or untrustworthiness influ- ence how much you want to use GenAI in language learning? 3. Do",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,16,"in language learning? Why or why not? What helps you decide whether GenAI’s responses are trustworthy or untrustworthy in language learning? Does the trustworthiness or untrustworthiness influ- ence how much you want to use GenAI in language learning? 3. Do you rely on GenAI in language learning? In what ways do you find yourself relying on GenAI in language learning? How, if at all, does your trust in GenAI (or lack of trust) factor into that decision? What are other factors that influence that decision? When you feel reliance on using GenAI, how do you choose to use it for language learning? 4. Have there been times when you have been reluc- tant or refused to use GenAI for language learning? Why? Are there specific reasons that lead you to trust GenAI less and potentially resist it in language learning? When you feel resistance to using GenAI, what alterna- tives do you choose to assist you in language learning? Are the alternatives working? Why or why not? 5. Do you prefer to take the initiative in learning if you want",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,16,"When you feel resistance to using GenAI, what alterna- tives do you choose to assist you in language learning? Are the alternatives working? Why or why not? 5. Do you prefer to take the initiative in learning if you want to learn something new or encounter difficulties in learning? Does this initiative have any influence on your use of GenAI in language learning? Can you give an example? 6. Have you noticed any changes in the way you learn language since you started using GenAI? If yes, what caused the changes? If no, what has prevented any noticeable change in the way you learn language?",105
