A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,19,"Page 19 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 and reliance (H2: β = 0.662, p < 0.001). Trust in GenAI negatively influenced resistance (H3: β = −0.095, p = 0.024). Reliance positively influenced behavioral intention to use (H4: β = 0.176, p = 0.002). Resistance negatively influenced behavioral intention to use (H5: β = −0.114, p = 0.002). Behavioral intention to use positively influenced actual use (H8: β = 0.149, p < 0.001). Self-directed learning positively influenced actual use (H9: β = 0.123, p = 0.006). Second, as for indirect effects, trust in GenAI produced significant positive effects on behavioral intention to use through reliance (H6: β = 0.117, p = 0.009) and resistance (H6: β = 0.011, p = 0.029). Moreover, trust in GenAI produced significant positive effects on actual use via behavioral intention to use (H7: β = 0.066, p = 0.004), Fig. 3 Results of Structural Equation Modeling Fig. 4 Moderation of Self-Directed Learning Between Behavioral Intention to Use and Actual Use. SD self-directed learning. BIU behavioral intention to use",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,19,"behavioral intention to use (H7: β = 0.066, p = 0.004), Fig. 3 Results of Structural Equation Modeling Fig. 4 Moderation of Self-Directed Learning Between Behavioral Intention to Use and Actual Use. SD self-directed learning. BIU behavioral intention to use",40
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,20,"Page 20 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 via reliance and behavioral intention to use (H7: β = 0.017, p = 0.007), and via resistance and behavioral intention to use (H7: β = 0.002, p = 0.020). Third, concerning the mod- erating effect, the interaction term (self-directed learning × behavioral intention to use) had a significant effect on actual use (H10: β = 0.118, p = 0.007). The results of modera- tion were further explored through moderation graphs. Figure 4 also showed that there was significant moderation by self-directed learning between behavioral intention to use and actual use, as indicated by the non-parallel lines on the graph. This suggests that the impact of behavioral intention to use on actual use increases in the presence of self- directed learning. The qualitative results The qualitative data reinforced and extended the quantitative results of Study 1 by offer- ing explanatory insights through the SOR framework. First, the qualitative analysis identified forms of trust in GenAI, reliance, resistance, perceived risk, and actual use. Second, the analysis provided",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,20,"data reinforced and extended the quantitative results of Study 1 by offer- ing explanatory insights through the SOR framework. First, the qualitative analysis identified forms of trust in GenAI, reliance, resistance, perceived risk, and actual use. Second, the analysis provided explanations for students’ reliance and resistance in rela- tion to their trust in GenAI. Third, the qualitative findings explored the nuanced role of perceived risk in the relationship between trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance, and the role of self-directed learning in the relationship between behavioral intention to use and actual use. The refined conceptual model is shown in Fig. 5. Stimulus: contextualizing trust in GenAI in relation to influences from GenAI, learners, and external factors According to the interview transcripts, trust in GenAI has been contextualized in rela- tion to influences from GenAI, learners, and external factors. A dynamic interplay among these three aspects shaped learners’ trust in GenAI, influencing both intrinsic Fig. 5 The Refined Conceptual Model of Learners’ Trust in GenAI",163
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,21,"Page 21 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 and extrinsic forms of trust. Intrinsic trust arose when the observable decision process of the GenAI model matched users’ priors on how this process should operate. Extrinsic trust fundamentally relied on the evaluation scheme, specifically evaluating the GenAI model’s ability to generalize in new scenarios based on its expected behavior in similar scenarios (Jacovi et al., 2021). In other words, intrinsic trust was trust in the decision process of the GenAI model, and extrinsic trust was trust in the evaluation methodology or data for the GenAI model. Intrinsic trust emerged through the interaction between learners and GenAI, consist- ing of two codes: explainability of GenAI reasoning and cognitive alignment with learn- ers’ priors. The former code, explainability of GenAI reasoning, included four subcodes: comprehensive explanatory support, real-time interaction, consistent outputs, and transparent error handling. Comprehensive explanatory support was first stated by par- ticipants as important in building trust with GenAI in language learning. For instance, Student 27 (S27) stated, “When GenAI provides accessible and clear",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,21,"support, real-time interaction, consistent outputs, and transparent error handling. Comprehensive explanatory support was first stated by par- ticipants as important in building trust with GenAI in language learning. For instance, Student 27 (S27) stated, “When GenAI provides accessible and clear built-in explana- tions for something confusing in language learning, it builds my trust. It feels like having a reliable tutor by my side, helping me through complex parts of the language.” Further- more, real-time interaction and consistent outputs were emphasized. For instance, S40 stated, “When I receive immediate responses to my queries from GenAI, I feel that I can ask for help any time…The consistent outputs seem to reassure me of its dependability, since I can predict how it will respond in different scenarios.” Interestingly, the last sub- code of intrinsic trust, transparent error handling, also helped to increase trust, as stated by 72.5% of participants. S11 stated, “I know that GenAI is imperfect, so when it clearly explains why an error occurred, it not only helps me to understand what went wrong but also teaches me how to avoid",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,21,"as stated by 72.5% of participants. S11 stated, “I know that GenAI is imperfect, so when it clearly explains why an error occurred, it not only helps me to understand what went wrong but also teaches me how to avoid similar errors in the future.” The latter code of intrinsic trust, cognitive alignment with learners’ priors, involved four subcodes: familiarity with AI technology, cognitive load management, personal- ized feedback, and contextual appropriateness. Specifically, familiarity with AI technol- ogy, whether through understanding knowledge about AI or previous usage experience, enhanced the students’ trust in GenAI for language learning. S28 stated, “I do not know much about the mechanism of GenAI, but I use it often and have tried out some useful tips along the way. This experience has made me trust it as a helpful tool for my stud- ies.” The second subcode of cognitive load management meant that the display of GenAI outputs was expected to align with students’ cognitive abilities. S11 explained, “When GenAI presents information in a way that is neither too simple nor too complex, it helps",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,21,"second subcode of cognitive load management meant that the display of GenAI outputs was expected to align with students’ cognitive abilities. S11 explained, “When GenAI presents information in a way that is neither too simple nor too complex, it helps me to absorb the information better without feeling underwhelmed or overwhelmed.” Personalized feedback, as the third subcode, was stated by participants as crucial in building their trust in GenAI because personalization implied that GenAI understood their learning styles (S1, S18, and S24), learning goals (S16 and S22), proficiency levels (S3 and S31), pace of learning (S37), and motivations in language learning (S25 and S33). Finally, the last subcode, contextual appropriateness, was also emphasized when GenAI showed cultural sensitivity according to students’ cultural norms and values (S30), spe- cific learning tasks (S10), and ethical considerations such as promoting positive values (S4 and S31). Another form, extrinsic trust, arose from the influence of external factors and GenAI itself. Among the 40 participants interviewed, nine students (i.e., 22.5%) reported",166
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,22,"Page 22 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 perceiving explicit support from their teachers or institutions for using GenAI in lan- guage learning, whereas 15 students (i.e., 37.5%) reported receiving no explicit support. The remaining 16 students (i.e., 40%) expressed uncertainty about their teachers’ or institutions’ stance toward GenAI use in language learning. Therefore, these results indi- cated mixed perceptions among participants regarding whether GenAI use for language learning was formally permitted by teachers or institutions. In this context, the partici- pants’ extrinsic trust included three codes: key informants, evidence-supported evalua- tion, and ancillary attributes. First, the opinions of key informants on GenAI reasoning, such as teachers, student peers, and policymakers, facilitated students’ extrinsic trust in GenAI. For example, S22 explained, “When teachers share how effectively GenAI can be integrated into learning and demonstrate its benefits, it reassures me of its value in learning.” Second, evidence-supported evaluation from various media sources was high- lighted. As stated by S4, “When I read studies or news articles showing how GenAI has enhanced other students’ language",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,22,"demonstrate its benefits, it reassures me of its value in learning.” Second, evidence-supported evaluation from various media sources was high- lighted. As stated by S4, “When I read studies or news articles showing how GenAI has enhanced other students’ language skills, this developed my trust in it as beneficial and well-tested.” Third, ancillary attributes of GenAI played a role in trust-building, such as a user-friendly interface (S28 and S32), perceived emotional support from GenAI in learn- ing (S12, S20, and S34), and the inclusion of anthropomorphic elements like human-like appearance (S6 and S17). Organism: reliance and resistance based on trust in GenAI Based on trust in GenAI, participants showed related reliance and resistance in language learning. On the one hand, reliance involved three forms: over-reliance, appropriate reliance, and under-reliance (Schemmer et al., 2023). On the decision-making con- tinuum from self-reliance to AI reliance in learning, over-reliance occurred when stu- dents accepted GenAI feedback without critical evaluation, leading to misuse or abuse of GenAI (Jacovi et al., 2021). For example, S1 stated, “I accept GenAI’s suggestions and corrections without taking the",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,22,"to AI reliance in learning, over-reliance occurred when stu- dents accepted GenAI feedback without critical evaluation, leading to misuse or abuse of GenAI (Jacovi et al., 2021). For example, S1 stated, “I accept GenAI’s suggestions and corrections without taking the time to understand or evaluate them because I think its language proficiency is much higher than mine.” Alternatively, appropriate reliance occurred when students critically evaluated GenAI feedback and effectively utilized it in learning, ensuring its appropriate use. S37 stated, “Instead of passively accepting GenAI feedback, I usually evaluate whether the feedback addresses my learning questions. I often revise my questions several times to obtain the answers I seek.” Finally, under-reli- ance occurred when students primarily depended on themselves for decision-making, frequently overlooking GenAI feedback, even when it was accurate and beneficial, lead- ing to misuse or abuse of GenAI. S33 stated, “When using GenAI, I check for its useful- ness, but it often appears to fall short. However, I may miss some valuable insights, as there were times when I realized parts of its feedback were correct.” To summarize, three",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,22,"stated, “When using GenAI, I check for its useful- ness, but it often appears to fall short. However, I may miss some valuable insights, as there were times when I realized parts of its feedback were correct.” To summarize, three forms of reliance were reflected: over-reliance, under-reliance, and appropriate reliance, leading to misuse, abuse, or the appropriate use. On the other hand, resistance included two forms: passive and active resistance. Resist- ance, as a generic and negative evaluation of adopting innovations, is characterized by a reluctance to make changes associated with such adoption, and/or by satisfaction with the status quo. The potential for this rejection is either temporary or long-term (Orbaiz & Arce-Urriza, 2024). Among participants, passive resistance was mainly expressed in the behaviors of persistent adherence to former learning habits, coupled with potentially",134
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,23,"Page 23 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 temporary disuse of GenAI. Active resistance involved more pronounced behaviors such as expressing negative opinions and discouraging others from using, alongside poten- tially long-term disuse of GenAI in language learning. For instance, while explaining passive resistance, S29 stated, “I stick to traditional learning habits like using textbooks and handwritten notes because they make me feel I am really learning knowledge and encourage my deep thinking, whereas using GenAI appears to provide only superfi- cial information.” When talking about active resistance, S7 emphasized, “I am skeptical about using GenAI in language learning, often telling my friends that it is ineffective and misleading. Based on what I have seen, I will not use it in the future.” Common reasons for resistance, either passive or active, included satisfaction with existing learning hab- its (S15 and S26), complexity of GenAI use (S8 and S23), and negative personal and/or observed use experiences (S14, S21, and S29). Regarding the relationships between trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance, our inter- view transcripts revealed",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,23,"with existing learning hab- its (S15 and S26), complexity of GenAI use (S8 and S23), and negative personal and/or observed use experiences (S14, S21, and S29). Regarding the relationships between trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance, our inter- view transcripts revealed an association between participants’ levels of trust in GenAI and their corresponding levels of reliance/resistance. Specifically, those who expressed high levels of trust often relied on GenAI without critical evaluation, sometimes to the point of over-reliance (e.g., S1 and S5). By contrast, those who expressed low levels of trust, often due to skepticism about GenAI’s accuracy or ethical considerations, tended to actively resist using GenAI (e.g., S13 and S36). Moreover, those who expressed cali- brated trust adopted a more balanced approach, appropriately relying on GenAI for spe- cific tasks, such as grammar checks, while maintaining their own critical evaluation (e.g., S2 and S37). However, the association between participants’ levels of trust in GenAI and their cor- responding levels of reliance/resistance was not always consistent, as a critical moderat- ing factor emerged to shape these dynamics: perceived risk. The exploratory study",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,23,"S2 and S37). However, the association between participants’ levels of trust in GenAI and their cor- responding levels of reliance/resistance was not always consistent, as a critical moderat- ing factor emerged to shape these dynamics: perceived risk. The exploratory study also identified the boundary conditions of perceived risk that either mitigated or strength- ened the influence of trust in GenAI on reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use. The perceived risk that the students highlighted was about academic integrity (e.g., plagiarism, education fairness, and evaluation appropriateness), data accuracy and secu- rity (e.g., data free from misinformation and protected against leaks), ethical concerns (e.g., perpetuation of biases against certain groups and irresponsible use of GenAI-gen- erated content), and social equity (e.g., the digital divide and accessibility barriers). The perceived risk either weakened or enhanced the impact of trust in GenAI on reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use, demonstrating its vital role as a moderating factor. Specifically, perceived risk was the boundary condition between trust in GenAI and the three factors: reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use. In relation to",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,23,"reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use, demonstrating its vital role as a moderating factor. Specifically, perceived risk was the boundary condition between trust in GenAI and the three factors: reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use. In relation to reli- ance, S11 stated, “When I have concerns about data security, my trust diminishes, mak- ing me hesitant to depend on GenAI. However, knowing that my data are well-protected restores my trust, enhancing my reliance.” As for resistance, S35 explained, “I worry about plagiarism for the difficulty teachers face in distinguishing between student-cre- ated and GenAI-generated content, which decreases my trust and increases my resist- ance. However, plagiarism detection technologies at our university boost my trust and help reduce my resistance.” In association with behavioral intention to use, S9 expressed, “The initial GenAI tool I used focused mainly on grammar and vocabulary corrections,",143
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,24,"Page 24 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 reducing my trust due to its shallow evaluations. Switching to another GenAI tool that could offer in-depth essay assessments increased my trust, making me more inclined to use it.” These showed the important role of perceived risk in shaping students’ inter- actions with GenAI, influencing how trust in GenAI affected reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use. Responses: the moderating role of self‑directed learning and forms of actual use According to the SOR framework, stimulus (i.e., trust in GenAI) and organism factors (i.e., reliance and resistance) influence the subsequent response factors (i.e., behavioral intention to use, self-directed learning, and actual use). Similar to the stimulus-organism linkage, a boundary condition, self-directed learning, existed between behavioral inten- tion to use and actual use. This meant that behavioral intention to use influenced actual use, but this influence was shaped by the students’ self-directed learning. In the process, self-directed learning showed multi-faceted dimensions: motivation, self-management, and self-monitoring. As for motivation and self-management, S32 described, “GenAI allows me to customize",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,24,"intention to use influenced actual use, but this influence was shaped by the students’ self-directed learning. In the process, self-directed learning showed multi-faceted dimensions: motivation, self-management, and self-monitoring. As for motivation and self-management, S32 described, “GenAI allows me to customize my learning path and progress. No more feeling stuck or help- less. I can shape my learning just the way I need it, taking control and moving forward in my own attempts.” As to self-monitoring, S38 expressed, “My interaction with GenAI is not merely about getting things right. It is also about ensuring everything ties together meaningfully. I feel like I am responsible for my learning journey.” Overall, students with self-directed learning were motivated to take responsibility for constructing meaning with GenAI in self-monitoring and understanding, when they had some control over their learning experiences in self-management. Self-directed learning was the boundary condition between behavioral intention to use and actual use, emerging as an autonomous and adaptive learning process. At one end, students exercised agency and took charge of their learning process while using GenAI, and at the other end,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,24,"the boundary condition between behavioral intention to use and actual use, emerging as an autonomous and adaptive learning process. At one end, students exercised agency and took charge of their learning process while using GenAI, and at the other end, they adapted dynamically to GenAI feedback with repeated usage. When stating the moderating role of self-directed learning between behavioral intention to use and actual use, S39 stated, “I value self-management. When improving my vocab- ulary, I use GenAI to practice and track progress. This approach transforms my attempts into daily habits, bridging the gap between planning and doing.” These highlighted the importance of self-directed learning for influencing the transition from behavioral inten- tion to use to actual use. Regarding the students’ actual use of GenAI in language learning, the interview tran- scripts suggested that they used GenAI for a range of language learning tasks. Specifi- cally, the participants primarily reported using GenAI to assist with academic writing (e.g., course assignments), professional writing (e.g., emails), creative writing (e.g., sto- ries and poems), brainstorming new ideas, translating texts, summarizing lengthy pas- sages,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,24,"of language learning tasks. Specifi- cally, the participants primarily reported using GenAI to assist with academic writing (e.g., course assignments), professional writing (e.g., emails), creative writing (e.g., sto- ries and poems), brainstorming new ideas, translating texts, summarizing lengthy pas- sages, proofreading, and language learning strategies. For instance, S18 remarked, “I often use ChatGPT to summarize long passages, which helps me to quickly grasp the key ideas and save time when reviewing course materials.” Several other participants noted that they frequently used GenAI for initial proofreading to identify spelling and structural errors before conducting more thorough edits themselves (e.g., S6, S24, and S38). These findings highlight GenAI’s broad utility in facilitating various aspects of the",114
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,25,"Page 25 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 language learning process, from pre-writing ideation to the refinement of final written outputs. Within the broad range of GenAI utilities in language learning, four distinct forms of actual use of GenAI emerged: disuse, misuse, abuse, and the appropriate use. A few par- ticipants demonstrated disuse after initially trying, expressing skepticism toward GenAI or choosing not to integrate it into their learning process (e.g., S7 and S29). The second form was misuse, as unintended inappropriate use of GenAI without adequate critical review or revision due to misunderstanding or a lack of expertise (e.g., S1 and S33). Con- versely, the third form, abuse, involved the intentional and inappropriate use of GenAI for personal convenience, such as fully relying on GenAI in writing, potentially under- mining the originality of the work (e.g., S5). Finally, the appropriate use was identified among students who engaged in iterative feedback with GenAI to refine grammar and style, preserving agency over their final outputs and explicitly acknowledging the use of GenAI in writing",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,25,"of the work (e.g., S5). Finally, the appropriate use was identified among students who engaged in iterative feedback with GenAI to refine grammar and style, preserving agency over their final outputs and explicitly acknowledging the use of GenAI in writing (e.g., S37). Discussion This study employs an explanatory sequential mixed-methods design grounded in the SOR framework to investigate university students’ trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI in language learning. The quantitative analysis performed structural equation modeling to examine the relationships among the stimulus, organism, and response fac- tors within the proposed conceptual model of learners’ trust in GenAI. Based on the quantitative findings, the qualitative analysis provided a deeper understanding of the relationships within the refined conceptual model. The findings collectively illustrate university students’ trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI for language learning, and boundary conditions that influence their use. Relationships between university students’ trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance in their adoption of GenAI for language learning Quantitative and qualitative results revealed that trust in GenAI (i.e., intrinsic and extrinsic trust) exerted a positive influence on",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,25,"that influence their use. Relationships between university students’ trust in GenAI and reliance/resistance in their adoption of GenAI for language learning Quantitative and qualitative results revealed that trust in GenAI (i.e., intrinsic and extrinsic trust) exerted a positive influence on reliance (i.e., over-reliance, appropriate reliance, and under-reliance), and reliance mediated the relationship between trust in GenAI and behavioral intention to use. This indicates that students with more trust in GenAI tend to rely more on GenAI, and reliance influences the extent to which trust in GenAI affects their behavioral intention to use. The finding aligns with previous research that supports a positive relationship between individuals’ trust in AI and reliance (e.g., Choung et al., 2023; Klingbeil et al., 2024). It also echoes previous findings indicating that students who trust AI are more likely to rely on AI recommendations, which in turn increases their willingness to use (Li & Little, 2023). This is probably because individu- als’ decision-making processes tend to favor fast and optimal solutions over slow and practically constrained solutions (Zhai et al., 2024). Therefore, as AI undertakes roles",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,25,"turn increases their willingness to use (Li & Little, 2023). This is probably because individu- als’ decision-making processes tend to favor fast and optimal solutions over slow and practically constrained solutions (Zhai et al., 2024). Therefore, as AI undertakes roles traditionally played by human cognitive processes, students who trust GenAI advice tend to rely on efficient cognitive shortcuts that facilitate quick and easy decision-mak- ing approaches, thereby becoming more likely to use GenAI in language learning (Semrl et al., 2023).",80
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,26,"Page 26 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Furthermore, mixed-methods results showed that trust in GenAI exerted a negative effect on resistance (i.e., passive and active resistance), with resistance as a mediator between trust in GenAI and behavioral intention to use. This indicates that students who have more trust in GenAI tend to exhibit less resistance to GenAI, and resistance affects the extent to which trust in GenAI influences behavioral intention to use. This is consist- ent with previous findings that students who have developed trust and connections with GenAI through interaction are less likely to resist its use and less reluctant to adopt it for learning in online higher education (Aldawsari & Almohish, 2024). Possible reasons for resistance include challenges in interacting with GenAI, concerns about accuracy (Elkefi et al., 2024), fear of the potential negative academic consequences such as plagiarism and undermined thinking skills (Chen & Lin, 2024), and various AI anxieties regard- ing job replacement and artificial consciousness (Li & Huang, 2020). Thus, university students who have developed trust in",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,26,"of the potential negative academic consequences such as plagiarism and undermined thinking skills (Chen & Lin, 2024), and various AI anxieties regard- ing job replacement and artificial consciousness (Li & Huang, 2020). Thus, university students who have developed trust in GenAI tend to resist using it less, which in turn increases their behavioral intention to use. The boundary condition of perceived risk for the influence of trust in GenAI on reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use The qualitative results revealed that perceived risk (i.e., academic integrity, data accu- racy and security, ethical concerns, and social equity) either mitigates or strengthens the influence of trust in GenAI on reliance, resistance, and behavioral intention to use. This finding indicates that, despite trust, students with more perceived risks associated with using GenAI tend to be more cautious, thereby placing less reliance and behavioral intention to use, and more resistance. Although research on these relationships is scant, existing studies have consistently supported perceived risk as a boundary condition, which explains why trust in GenAI may not result in reliance or behavioral intention to",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,26,"behavioral intention to use, and more resistance. Although research on these relationships is scant, existing studies have consistently supported perceived risk as a boundary condition, which explains why trust in GenAI may not result in reliance or behavioral intention to use the technology in practice (e.g., Solberg et al., 2022; Stuck et al., 2020). For people who take risks based on their trust, their trust in AI should exceed their perceived risk in different situations, thereby demonstrating behavioral intention to use (Solberg et al., 2022). The situational risk related to AI involves potential negative impacts on individ- uals’ performance (e.g., failing or losing grades in exams), financial loss, psychological distress (e.g., anxiety or sadness), and various social concerns such as damage to social image, ethical issues, privacy violations, and security threats (Stuck et al., 2020). Specifi- cally, our findings echo the previous research indicating that when students use GenAI in language learning, they have perceived risk on accuracy, transparency, privacy, and ethics, which influence the impact of their trust on GenAI on reliance and behavioral intention to use on the",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,26,"the previous research indicating that when students use GenAI in language learning, they have perceived risk on accuracy, transparency, privacy, and ethics, which influence the impact of their trust on GenAI on reliance and behavioral intention to use on the one hand (Chan & Hu, 2023), and on resistance to GenAI on the other (Wang et al., 2024a). It should also be noted that there is a tendency for a risk–ben- efit calculation, wherein students see risks but are persuaded by the potential benefits of using GenAI (Kreps et al., 2023). In other words, students tend to rely more on GenAI and resist it less not so much because they trust GenAI, but because they perceive that their anticipated benefits of using GenAI will exceed the perceived risk (Ivanov et al., 2024). Other possible reasons include the “fear of missing out,” assessments about the lack of efficient alternatives, and optimism about technological advancements (Kreps et al., 2023). Thus, despite the boundary condition of perceived risk, we should remain",168
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,27,"Page 27 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 cautious about the possibility that students may become over-reliant on GenAI in learning. The moderating role of self‑directed learning and learners’ actual use of GenAI Both quantitative and qualitative results showed a moderating role of self-directed learn- ing (i.e., motivation, self-management, and self-monitoring) between behavioral inten- tion to use and actual use. Specifically, self-directed learning was shown to moderate the effect of behavioral intention to use on actual use. This finding indicates that students with higher levels of self-directed learning are more likely to actualize their intention regarding GenAI use in language learning. This finding aligns with empirical research indicating that effective self-directed learning strategies can improve students’ actual utilization of GenAI, making them perceive the use of GenAI as beneficial for enhanc- ing their learning (Wu et al., 2024). GenAI facilitates self-directed learning by designing learning activities, regulating learning plans, and monitoring the learning process (Chiu, 2024). Meanwhile, students with high levels of self-directed learning utilize GenAI in diverse ways, primarily employing it as",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,27,"learning (Wu et al., 2024). GenAI facilitates self-directed learning by designing learning activities, regulating learning plans, and monitoring the learning process (Chiu, 2024). Meanwhile, students with high levels of self-directed learning utilize GenAI in diverse ways, primarily employing it as an interlocutor, a content generator, and a lan- guage learning resource (Dizon, 2024). Additionally, it is worth noting that self-directed learning in GenAI-assisted language learning is an autonomous and adaptive process. In the process, learners exercise autonomy to take charge of learning and flexibly adapt interaction with GenAI, by modifying queries, prompts, and modes of engagement in response to their evolving cognitive states (Li et al., 2024). Therefore, self-directed learn- ing either amplifies or diminishes the impact of behavioral intention to use on actual use as an autonomous and adaptive process. In terms of learners’ actual use of GenAI, our quantitative and qualitative results sug- gested that trust in GenAI influenced actual use. Moreover, four forms of actual use (i.e., disuse, misuse, abuse, and the appropriate use) emerged among the diverse utilities of GenAI in language learning from initial ideation",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,27,"quantitative and qualitative results sug- gested that trust in GenAI influenced actual use. Moreover, four forms of actual use (i.e., disuse, misuse, abuse, and the appropriate use) emerged among the diverse utilities of GenAI in language learning from initial ideation to the refinement of final written outputs. The findings align with prior literature that emphasizes the importance of cali- brated trust and the appropriate use of technology (de Visser et al., 2020; Parasuraman & Riley, 1997). Specifically, disuse, reflecting skepticism and avoidance behaviors, sup- ports findings from Trust Calibration Theory, which posits that low trust may prevent beneficial technology use (Lee & See, 2004), thus preventing potential educational gains (Symeou et al., 2025). Conversely, misuse and abuse, representing either unintentional or intentional inappropriate use of GenAI in learning, echo concerns raised in research about the negative consequences associated with over-reliance on GenAI and ethical violations, which may impair learners’ cognitive abilities and academic integrity (Abbas et al., 2024; Zhai et al., 2024). However, the appropriate use, characterized by active and iterative interactions with GenAI alongside ethical awareness, critical evaluation, and",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,27,"on GenAI and ethical violations, which may impair learners’ cognitive abilities and academic integrity (Abbas et al., 2024; Zhai et al., 2024). However, the appropriate use, characterized by active and iterative interactions with GenAI alongside ethical awareness, critical evaluation, and realization of agency, aligns with previous studies highlighting that calibrated trust in GenAI fosters positive learning experiences or outcomes (de Visser et al., 2020; Symeou et al., 2025). Collectively, these findings highlight that the four forms of actual use are closely related to trust in GenAI, representing a continuum of reliance ranging from high dependence on GenAI to greater learner agency, thereby underscoring the impor- tance of calibrating trust to foster the appropriate use.",114
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,28,"Page 28 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 Limitations and future research directions While this study has provided prolific findings, several limitations should be acknowl- edged to stimulate future research. First, the cross-sectional design restricts the infer- ence of causality among factors, even though the proposed model is grounded in solid theoretical foundations. Future research may consider longitudinal or experimental designs, such as randomized controlled trials, to better clarify the temporal sequences and causal relationships among the factors. This is particularly necessary as students’ trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI may change over time as they become more familiar with the technology. Conducting experimental studies to objectively assess the behavioral changes could offer insights into the dynamic changes. Second, the discipli- nary, geographic, and cultural homogeneity of our participants, drawn exclusively from the area of language learning within higher education institutions in China, may con- strain the generalizability of the findings to other disciplines, educational systems, and cultural contexts. Future research may include participants from diverse disciplinary, geographic, and cultural contexts",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,28,"from the area of language learning within higher education institutions in China, may con- strain the generalizability of the findings to other disciplines, educational systems, and cultural contexts. Future research may include participants from diverse disciplinary, geographic, and cultural contexts to enable comparative analyses and explore potential differences. Third, despite our efforts to ensure survey clarity and participant anonymity, self-report surveys have a risk of response bias, potentially influencing the interpreta- tion of the results. For example, students might have underreported their resistance to GenAI due to social desirability bias as a tendency to underreport unfavorable attitudes or behaviors. Future studies could incorporate in-depth interviews or participant obser- vations to triangulate findings obtained through indirect and implicit measures (Bergen & Labonté, 2020). Fourth, this study did not investigate variations in individual student backgrounds or differences across application contexts. Given that students’ demo- graphic characteristics (e.g., genders and education levels) and application contexts (e.g., formal and informal settings) may influence their use of GenAI in learning (Strzelecki & ElArabawy, 2024), future research may explore how these differences shape students’ trust in,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,28,"students’ demo- graphic characteristics (e.g., genders and education levels) and application contexts (e.g., formal and informal settings) may influence their use of GenAI in learning (Strzelecki & ElArabawy, 2024), future research may explore how these differences shape students’ trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI in learning. Implications for theoretical development and educational practices This study has considerable implications for theoretical development and educational practices. In theory, the study extends the theoretical foundations of trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI by developing and consolidating a refined conceptual model within the SOR framework. It reveals relationships among the factors and enhances understanding of the boundary conditions shaping university students’ actual use of GenAI in language learning, thus providing implications for further theoretical devel- opment. First, the study expands the SOR framework by incorporating psychological factors (i.e., trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI) and cognitive factors (i.e., self-directed learning and perceived risk) within the context of GenAI-assisted language learning. This expansion improves the applicability of the SOR framework in under- standing the impacts of the psychological and",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,28,"reliance on, and resistance to GenAI) and cognitive factors (i.e., self-directed learning and perceived risk) within the context of GenAI-assisted language learning. This expansion improves the applicability of the SOR framework in under- standing the impacts of the psychological and cognitive factors on university students’ use of GenAI in language learning. Future theoretical models could incorporate envi- ronmental factors (e.g., institutional training and policy guidance), technological factors (e.g., task alignment and learner-GenAI interaction), and individual factors (e.g., person- ality traits and prior experience) to offer a more comprehensive understanding of GenAI",91
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,29,"Page 29 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 use in education. Second, this study complements Trust Calibration Theory by revealing how university students’ trust in GenAI acts as a stimulus that influences reliance and resistance, which subsequently shape their behavioral intention to use and actual use. In this study, trust in GenAI is employed for the first time as an antecedent to explain reliance and resistance in GenAI-assisted language learning. Future theoretical develop- ment could explore whether reliance or resistance dynamically influences trust in GenAI as students engage with GenAI over time (Kahr et al., 2024). Furthermore, investigat- ing how varying levels of trust in GenAI might subsequently affect levels of reliance and resistance could further deepen understanding of these dynamic relationships. Third, the study adds to the discourse on self-directed learning by revealing its multi-faceted dimensions and characterizing it as an autonomous and adaptive process in GenAI- assisted language learning. Future theoretical development could examine how GenAI tools facilitate or reshape self-directed learning and investigate potential barriers, such as AI literacy (i.e.,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,29,"revealing its multi-faceted dimensions and characterizing it as an autonomous and adaptive process in GenAI- assisted language learning. Future theoretical development could examine how GenAI tools facilitate or reshape self-directed learning and investigate potential barriers, such as AI literacy (i.e., the competencies of understanding, applying, critically evaluating, and having awareness of ethical and moral considerations during use of AI) and resource accessibility (Wang et al., 2024a). The investigations will help understand factors influ- encing whether students’ behavioral intention translates into effective actual use. In educational practices, this study provides practical implications for collaborative efforts among various stakeholders to develop effective strategies for integrating GenAI into higher education. We provide recommendations for conceptualizing trust in GenAI as a complex concept where the goal is to achieve appropriate use of GenAI in educa- tion. One key finding of this study is related to the crucial role of trust in GenAI in shap- ing students’ reliance and resistance, thereby influencing their use of GenAI for learning. Fostering appropriate trust in GenAI, which underpins appropriate reliance and ulti- mately appropriate use, requires collaborative efforts.",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,29,"to the crucial role of trust in GenAI in shap- ing students’ reliance and resistance, thereby influencing their use of GenAI for learning. Fostering appropriate trust in GenAI, which underpins appropriate reliance and ulti- mately appropriate use, requires collaborative efforts. For one thing, higher education institutions and media sources should cultivate appropriate extrinsic trust by accurately communicating both the learning opportunities GenAI can offer and potential nega- tive issues, such as plagiarism, privacy concerns, and the risk of undermining critical thinking skills (Meryer et al., 2023). For example, higher education institutions should develop and implement clear guidelines regarding the responsible use of GenAI (Moor- house et al., 2023). Such transparent communication equips students with realistic expectations and informed judgment, enabling them to critically evaluate GenAI out- puts and ethically use GenAI in learning. For another, AI developers should aim to fos- ter appropriate intrinsic and extrinsic trust through human-centered GenAI designs that prioritize appropriate trust, instead of greater trust (de Visser et al., 2020). This can be achieved by simplifying GenAI algorithms and operational processes to make user inter- action",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,29,"fos- ter appropriate intrinsic and extrinsic trust through human-centered GenAI designs that prioritize appropriate trust, instead of greater trust (de Visser et al., 2020). This can be achieved by simplifying GenAI algorithms and operational processes to make user inter- action more understandable, and by carefully evaluating ancillary attributes of GenAI to ensure appropriate trust and, consequently, appropriate reliance (Lee & See, 2004). Thus, these efforts could help foster appropriate intrinsic and extrinsic trust, thereby facilitating appropriate reliance. This study also provides evidence for the moderating effects of perceived risk and self- directed learning on students’ actual use, underscoring the importance of raising stu- dents’ awareness of potential risk and improving their self-directed learning abilities when using GenAI. To raise students’ perceived risk, educators should clearly commu- nicate concerns such as privacy issues and plagiarism, guide students to use GenAI as a",141
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,30,"Page 30 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 supplementary rather than a primary learning resource, and encourage the critical eval- uation of information generated by GenAI (Symeou et al., 2025). In addition, to enhance students’ self-directed learning abilities, curriculum developers should design custom- ized GenAI-related courses that integrate GenAI into learning activities and guide stu- dents in planning, monitoring, and evaluating their learning processes. Additionally, university administrators should establish peer support systems to assist students with limited self-directed learning abilities related to GenAI, enabling them to effectively engage in GenAI-supported learning environments (Esiyok et al., 2024). This study highlights the importance of addressing disuse, misuse, and abuse of GenAI to ensure the appropriate use of GenAI in educational settings. To this end, first, higher education institutions should develop continuous multi-faceted strategies to stay informed about updates, new GenAI tools, and evolving best practices, thus ensuring the appropriate use of GenAI in education. Given that GenAI is also relatively new to educators, an important strategy includes structured professional training opportuni- ties and experience-sharing sessions",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,30,"informed about updates, new GenAI tools, and evolving best practices, thus ensuring the appropriate use of GenAI in education. Given that GenAI is also relatively new to educators, an important strategy includes structured professional training opportuni- ties and experience-sharing sessions in which colleagues with GenAI expertise can share best practices (Symeou et al., 2025). Another strategy is that higher education institu- tions should develop AI literacy programs to equip students with the knowledge and skills to critically evaluate and effectively utilize GenAI in learning (Yan et al., 2024). As suggested by theories and prior studies, AI literacy is associated with trust in GenAI, and it is essential for promoting the effective, responsible, and ethical use of GenAI in education (e.g., Shahzad et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024a; Wong et al., 2023). Thus, the training programs may focus on AI literacy, simultaneously familiarizing students with specific tasks and various tools used in different learning contexts (Wong et al., 2023). Second, educators should foster a learning environment that guides students on using GenAI with appropriate reliance, while preventing the potential for",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,30,"literacy, simultaneously familiarizing students with specific tasks and various tools used in different learning contexts (Wong et al., 2023). Second, educators should foster a learning environment that guides students on using GenAI with appropriate reliance, while preventing the potential for over-reliance that may undermine creativity, critical thinking, and problem-solving skills (Dwivedi et al., 2023; Zhai et al., 2024). For example, educators may encourage students to derive satis- faction in mastering challenging concepts independently and find a sense of accomplish- ment by rewarding students for their genuine intellectual achievements (Abbas et al., 2024). Educators may also promote students’ creativity, critical thinking, and problem- solving skills by assigning tasks, activities, or projects that cannot be completed solely by GenAI, and applying diverse criteria to evaluate students’ learning (Yan et al., 2024). Third, policymakers should develop clear policies to prevent misuse and abuse in educa- tion that ensure appropriate use. Examples of misuse and abuse, potential consequences, and monitoring measures such as plagiarism detection tools should be explicitly com- municated (Symeou et al., 2025). Through the collaborative efforts among various stake- holders, higher",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,30,"in educa- tion that ensure appropriate use. Examples of misuse and abuse, potential consequences, and monitoring measures such as plagiarism detection tools should be explicitly com- municated (Symeou et al., 2025). Through the collaborative efforts among various stake- holders, higher education institutions may foster the appropriate use of GenAI to better prepare both teachers and students for educational opportunities and challenges in the current era of GenAI. Conclusion This study provides a new perspective on understanding university students’ trust in, reliance on, and resistance to GenAI in language learning. Based on the SOR frame- work, this explanatory sequential mixed-methods study has proposed and consolidated a refined conceptual model by exploring the nuanced relationships among the factors",116
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,31,"Page 31 of 35 Wang et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:59 in stimulus (i.e., trust in GenAI), organism (i.e., reliance and resistance), and response (i.e., behavioral intention to use, self-directed learning, and actual use), and boundary conditions among the relationships. The quantitative findings contribute to the devel- opment of a proposed conceptual model that statistically explains university students’ adoption of GenAI in language learning. Subsequently, the qualitative findings reinforce and refine the conceptual model by revealing forms of trust in GenAI, reliance, resist- ance, perceived risk, and actual use, and identifying boundary conditions concerning perceived risk and self-directed learning. Results indicate the mediating roles of reliance and resistance in the relationships between trust in GenAI and behavioral intention to use, and the moderating role of self-directed learning in the relationship between behav- ioral intention to use and actual use. Moreover, forms within the five factors have been revealed, including trust in GenAI (i.e., intrinsic and extrinsic trust), reliance (i.e., over- reliance, appropriate reliance, and under-reliance), resistance (i.e., passive and active resistance), perceived risk (i.e., academic integrity,",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,31,"and actual use. Moreover, forms within the five factors have been revealed, including trust in GenAI (i.e., intrinsic and extrinsic trust), reliance (i.e., over- reliance, appropriate reliance, and under-reliance), resistance (i.e., passive and active resistance), perceived risk (i.e., academic integrity, data accuracy and security, ethical concerns, and social equity), and actual use (i.e., disuse, misuse, abuse, and appropri- ate use). Additionally, perceived risk is the boundary condition that either mitigates or strengthens the influence of trust in GenAI on reliance, resistance, and behavioral inten- tion to use. Finally, self-directed learning in GenAI-assisted language learning is shown in motivation, self-management, and self-monitoring as an autonomous and adaptive process. These findings shed light on understanding how university students’ trust in GenAI interacts with perceived risk and self-directed learning to influence their reliance and resistance, subsequently shaping their actual use of GenAI in education. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions Feifei Wang: Conceptualization, data collection, data analysis, and manuscript draft. Nuoen Li: Conceptualization, data collection, data analysis, and manuscript revision. Alan C. K. Cheung: Conceptualization, data analysis, supervision, and manuscript revision. Gary K. W.",180
A099,A099.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00547-9,31,"education. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions Feifei Wang: Conceptualization, data collection, data analysis, and manuscript draft. Nuoen Li: Conceptualization, data collection, data analysis, and manuscript revision. Alan C. K. Cheung: Conceptualization, data analysis, supervision, and manuscript revision. Gary K. W. Wong: Data analysis, interpretation of data, project administration, and manuscript revision. Funding Not applicable. Availability of data and materials The dataset used in this study is available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Receiv",87
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w Examining Faculty and Student Perceptions of Generative AI in University Courses Junghwan Kim1 · Michelle Klopfer2 · Jacob R. Grohs2 · Hoda Eldardiry3 · James Weichert3 · Larry A. Cox II4 · Dale Pike4 Accepted: 11 December 2024 / Published online: 24 January 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Abstract As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools such as ChatGPT become more capable and accessible, their use in educational settings is likely to grow. However, the academic community lacks a comprehensive understanding of the perceptions and attitudes of students and instructors toward these new tools. In the Fall 2023 semester, we surveyed 982 students and 76 faculty at a large public university in the United States, focusing on topics such as perceived ease of use, ethical concerns, the impact of GenAI on learning, and differences in responses by role, gender, and discipline. We found that students and faculty did not differ significantly in their atti- tudes toward GenAI in higher education, except regarding ease of use, hedonic moti- vation, habit, and interest in exploring new",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,1,"in responses by role, gender, and discipline. We found that students and faculty did not differ significantly in their atti- tudes toward GenAI in higher education, except regarding ease of use, hedonic moti- vation, habit, and interest in exploring new technologies. Students and instructors also used GenAI for coursework or teaching at similar rates, although regular use of these tools was still low across both groups. Among students, we found significant differences in attitudes between males in STEM majors and females in non-STEM majors. These findings underscore the importance of considering demographic and disciplinary diversity when developing policies and practices for integrating GenAI in educational contexts, as GenAI may influence learning outcomes differently across various groups of students. This study contributes to the broader understand- ing of how GenAI can be leveraged in higher education while highlighting potential areas of inequality that need to be addressed as these tools become more widely used. Keywords ChatGPT · Generative AI · Higher Education · Perception · Technology Acceptance Extended author information available on the last page of the article",177
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,1,inequality that need to be addressed as these tools become more widely used. Keywords ChatGPT · Generative AI · Higher Education · Perception · Technology Acceptance Extended author information available on the last page of the article,37
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,2,"1282 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Introduction Technology in Education Integrating technology into higher education has historically been marked by both innovation and disagreement (Waits & Demana, 2000; Foer, 2012). As new technologies emerge and become incorporated into educational practice, they often lead to shifts in pedagogical approaches and a resulting re-evalu- ation of traditional teaching and learning. While these transitions can lead to improvements in educational practices, they are not without challenges. Often, the introduction of new technological tools has sparked adversarial interactions between students and educators during the critical phase of adoption and adap- tation in educational communities. The incorporation of new technologies into education has been met with resistance. An illustrative example can be found in the reaction of the ancient Greek philosopher Socrates to written text; he feared that the written word would undermine the foundational educational practice of memorization, which he deemed essential for true learning (Foer, 2012). The incorporation of calculators into the educational framework for math and science courses further demonstrates the pattern of resistance followed by inte- gration. The use of calculating",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,2,"educational practice of memorization, which he deemed essential for true learning (Foer, 2012). The incorporation of calculators into the educational framework for math and science courses further demonstrates the pattern of resistance followed by inte- gration. The use of calculating tools, from simple slide rules to advanced graph- ing calculators, was initially viewed as a form of cheating in schools (Waits & Demana, 2000). However, educators’ perceptions of calculators transformed as the tool’s effectiveness in enhancing the learning process was recognized. Within just six years, the percentage of high school students in the United States having access to calculators in the classroom increased from 26% in 1986 to 92% in 1992 (Waits & Demana, 2000), reflecting the rapid acceptance of calculators as educational tools. The development of the internet introduced further complexi- ties into education, initially prompting concerns about academic integrity due to the ease of plagiarism. This challenge led to the development of plagiarism- detection software, a step toward using the internet’s potential for educational purposes (Warn, 2006). Over time, educators began to explore the social and creative capabilities",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,2,"integrity due to the ease of plagiarism. This challenge led to the development of plagiarism- detection software, a step toward using the internet’s potential for educational purposes (Warn, 2006). Over time, educators began to explore the social and creative capabilities of the internet, incorporating these features into pedagogi- cal practices (Grosseck, 2009). Educators’ perspective on the use of social media in education, for example, has evolved from a problematic distraction to a val- ued component of the educational experience (Cooke, 2017). Now, web-based course-management software and online courses (Dumford & Miller, 2018) are ubiquitous in higher education. These examples – from books to calculators to the internet – demonstrate the recurring pattern of initial resistance followed by acceptance of novel technolo- gies in educational settings. Each technological advancement, originally conten- tious, has been assimilated into educational practices and has become a crucial tool for teaching and learning.",147
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,3,"1283 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Generative AI in Education This historical perspective provides a valuable context for understanding the current attitudes toward the use of generative AI in higher education. The advent of genera- tive AI, particularly following the launch of ChatGPT in 2022, has generated substan- tial interest and concern within the educational community. Generative AI refers to a class of artificial intelligence that is good at generating content in various formats— text, images, code, and more—in response to user prompts (Dwivedi et al., 2023). ChatGPT, for instance, can produce human-like text, solve problems, summarize con- tent, and even assist in coding tasks. These capabilities have opened new possibilities for enhancing education but have also prompted significant debate about their appro- priate use in academic settings. Generative AI is unique compared to earlier technological tools in education because of its versatility and accessibility. Unlike calculators, which were introduced primarily as aids in mathematics and science, or the internet, which revolutionized access to informa- tion, generative AI has the potential to impact a broad range of academic activities. It",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,3,"of its versatility and accessibility. Unlike calculators, which were introduced primarily as aids in mathematics and science, or the internet, which revolutionized access to informa- tion, generative AI has the potential to impact a broad range of academic activities. It can assist students in drafting essays, developing research ideas, improving writing fluency, debugging code, and offering personalized learning experiences by providing instant feedback and guidance. This potential makes generative AI a powerful tool for fostering student autonomy, creativity, and engagement in learning (Farrelly & Baker, 2023). However, with this potential comes considerable challenges. One of the most pressing concerns in the integration of generative AI into education is its impact on assessment and academic integrity. AI-generated content raises questions about the fairness and validity of traditional assessments, which often rely on students’ ability to produce original work. As generative AI tools become more capable of completing tasks such as writing essays, developing code, solving equations, or generating creative work, educators are faced with the dilemma of how to differentiate between a student’s authentic learning and AI-gener- ated assistance. This has",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,3,"tools become more capable of completing tasks such as writing essays, developing code, solving equations, or generating creative work, educators are faced with the dilemma of how to differentiate between a student’s authentic learning and AI-gener- ated assistance. This has led to concerns about the potential for AI tools to result in aca- demic dishonesty, particularly in environments that rely heavily on written assessments or problem-solving tasks that can be completed by AI (Smolansky et al., 2023). Beyond concerns about cheating, generative AI also poses challenges related to the learning process itself. There is apprehension that students may become overly reliant on AI tools, thereby undermining the development of critical-thinking skills, prob- lem-solving skills, and creative skills that are central to higher education (Michel- Villarreal et al., 2023). For example, while generative AI can help students quickly produce a written response or code solution, there is a risk that students may bypass the deeper cognitive processes involved in learning and instead rely on the generative AI to perform tasks that they should be mastering themselves. This raises questions about the",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,3,"response or code solution, there is a risk that students may bypass the deeper cognitive processes involved in learning and instead rely on the generative AI to perform tasks that they should be mastering themselves. This raises questions about the long-term educational outcomes of integrating such powerful tools into the learning environment. In addition, recent studies highlight that while generative AI can assist with tasks such as content creation and feedback, human teachers possess irreplaceable qualities like critical thinking, emotional intelligence, and the ability to foster social-emotional competencies in students (Chan & Tsi, 2024).",94
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,4,"1284 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 In response to these challenges, universities are beginning to establish guide- lines and policies that regulate the use of generative AI in academic contexts. Some institutions have embraced generative AI tools as valuable educational resources, encouraging their use in teaching and learning while providing training on how to use generative AI ethically and responsibly (Chan, 2023). These institutions rec- ognize that generative AI can be a beneficial supplement to traditional educational practices, particularly in providing personalized support to students and enhancing their learning experiences. For example, international students who face language barriers may use generative AI tools to improve their comprehension and communi- cation skills, thereby leveling the playing field in multilingual classrooms (Farrelly & Baker, 2023). Conversely, some universities have taken a more restrictive approach, viewing generative AI as a potential threat to academic integrity and the core values of edu- cation. In these cases, policies have been implemented that limit or even ban the use of generative AI in certain types of assessments or coursework. This reflects an ongoing debate within higher",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,4,"academic integrity and the core values of edu- cation. In these cases, policies have been implemented that limit or even ban the use of generative AI in certain types of assessments or coursework. This reflects an ongoing debate within higher education about the proper role of generative AI in learning, teaching, and evaluation. Recent developments have further complicated the landscape. For instance, as generative AI continues to evolve, it is increasingly being integrated into widely used educational software, such as learning management systems (LMS) and online course platforms. Generative AI-powered tools are now capable of automating aspects of teaching, such as grading, generating feedback, and even creating lesson plans (Moorhouse et al., 2023). This has led to discussions about the role of edu- cators in the age of generative AI, where tasks traditionally performed by human instructors could potentially be automated by AI systems. While this has the poten- tial to reduce workload and improve efficiency, it also raises ethical concerns about the dehumanization of education and the diminishing role of instructors in fostering deep, meaningful learning experiences (Chan &",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,4,"automated by AI systems. While this has the poten- tial to reduce workload and improve efficiency, it also raises ethical concerns about the dehumanization of education and the diminishing role of instructors in fostering deep, meaningful learning experiences (Chan & Tsi, 2024). Moreover, the use of generative AI is not limited to formal academic contexts. Students are increasingly experimenting with generative AI tools outside of the class- room, using them to explore topics of interest, create personal projects, or prepare for future careers. This highlights the dual nature of generative AI in education: while it presents challenges within the traditional structures of teaching and assessment, it also offers opportunities for innovation and creativity in self-directed learning. Uni- versities will need to account for these informal uses of generative AI when develop- ing policies and guidelines, as students’ experiences with generative AI outside of the classroom will inevitably shape their expectations and behaviors within it. In sum, generative AI represents both a frontier of educational innovation and a significant challenge to existing pedagogical frameworks. As institutions of higher education grapple with",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,4,"AI outside of the classroom will inevitably shape their expectations and behaviors within it. In sum, generative AI represents both a frontier of educational innovation and a significant challenge to existing pedagogical frameworks. As institutions of higher education grapple with how to best incorporate generative AI tools into their cur- ricula, they must balance the potential benefits of enhanced learning, efficiency, and personalization with the need to maintain academic integrity and foster critical thinking and creativity. The current state of generative AI in education is thus one of rapid development, marked by both enthusiasm for its possibilities and concern over its implications. However, relatively little empirical work has explored the landscape of students’ and educators’ attitudes toward generative AI in education.",121
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,5,"1285 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 The Current Study Previous studies have emphasized the need for a more comprehensive exploration of the attitudes and perceptions of students and faculty regarding the integration of generative AI into higher education. For instance, a study by Hmoud et al. (2024) focused on a small cohort of students and explored the relationship between student motivation and ChatGPT use, finding that generative AI use enhanced motivation. Smolansky et al. (2023) expanded the scope of their study to include both students and faculty in an exploration of AI’s perceived effects on assessment practices; both groups were interested in including AI in assessment, but they were apprehen- sive about how ChatGPT could negatively affect creativity and academic integrity. Although faculty generally acknowledge the potential of AI tools in educational contexts, their willingness to integrate them into coursework often depends on their level of understanding and confidence with the technology (Harper, 2024). Most crucially, there is a need for investigation into how these attitudes differ across disciplines and demographic groups. In response to this need, our study aims",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,5,"often depends on their level of understanding and confidence with the technology (Harper, 2024). Most crucially, there is a need for investigation into how these attitudes differ across disciplines and demographic groups. In response to this need, our study aims to fill this gap by investigating the attitudes and behaviors of students and faculty toward generative AI in higher education. Our survey examined key topics such as perceived ease of use, ethical concerns, the impact of generative AI on learning, and intentions to use generative AI tools in coursework or teaching. Additionally, this study focused on differences by role (student vs. faculty), gender, and disci- pline (STEM vs. non-STEM). By exploring these differences, this study offers new insights into how generative AI is being integrated into higher education and high- lights potential areas of disparity that require further attention. The significance of this study lies in its potential to inform university administra- tors, policymakers, and educators as they develop policies and guidelines for the use of generative AI in educational settings. As generative AI becomes increasingly prevalent, it is crucial",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,5,"significance of this study lies in its potential to inform university administra- tors, policymakers, and educators as they develop policies and guidelines for the use of generative AI in educational settings. As generative AI becomes increasingly prevalent, it is crucial to ensure that its adoption does not exacerbate existing ine- qualities in education. For example, some student populations may face greater chal- lenges or ethical dilemmas when using generative AI tools, which could affect their learning outcomes. Understanding these nuances will help universities develop more inclusive policies that address the diverse needs of their student body while promot- ing the responsible use of generative AI. Furthermore, this study contributes to the broader field of educational technology by identifying the conditions under which generative AI can enhance learning and by drawing attention to the ethical consider- ations that must be navigated to ensure its beneficial integration into the classroom. Our research questions are designed to explore: • How do students and faculty differ in their attitudes toward the use of generative AI (e.g., ease of use, ethical concerns, intention to use,",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,5,"to ensure its beneficial integration into the classroom. Our research questions are designed to explore: • How do students and faculty differ in their attitudes toward the use of generative AI (e.g., ease of use, ethical concerns, intention to use, and impacts of AI) in coursework and teaching? • What roles do gender and academic discipline (STEM vs. non-STEM) play in shaping attitudes and behaviors related to generative AI in educational settings? • How do perceptions of generative AI’s impact on learning vary across different student populations, particularly with respect to academic performance and ethi- cal considerations?",97
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,6,"1286 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 This study makes three key contributions to the emerging research on genera- tive AI in higher education. First, it offers a novel investigation into how demo- graphic and disciplinary differences shape attitudes and behaviors toward genera- tive AI tools, focusing on gender and STEM versus non-STEM fields. This approach provides unique insights that have not been explored in prior research. Second, the study examines both student and faculty perspectives, enabling a comprehensive understanding of how generative AI is perceived across roles in higher education. Finally, the findings inform the development of inclusive policies and educational practices that address the diverse needs of university communities as they navigate the integration of generative AI into teaching and learning. Conceptual Framework To aid our understanding of the attitudes toward generative AI and its use in higher education, this study is framed in the context of the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), developed by Venkatesh and colleagues (2012, 2016). The UTAUT model provides a robust theoretical foundation for exploring the factors affecting technology use.",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,6,"this study is framed in the context of the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), developed by Venkatesh and colleagues (2012, 2016). The UTAUT model provides a robust theoretical foundation for exploring the factors affecting technology use. The model identifies seven factors that explain an individual’s intention to use technology: Performance Expectancy, Effort Expec- tancy, Social Influence, Facilitating Conditions, Hedonic Motivation, Price Value, and Habit. These factors have been widely applied in numerous studies examining the acceptance of various technologies in educational settings, providing empirical support for their relevance and utility. Applications of UTAUT in Educational Contexts The UTAUT model has been validated in several empirical studies focused on educa- tional technology adoption. For instance, Strzelecki (2023) used the UTAUT model to examine students’ acceptance of ChatGPT in higher education. Strzelecki (2023) demonstrated that Performance Expectancy (i.e., students’ belief that ChatGPT would improve their productivity and academic performance) was a strong predictor of intention to use the tool. Khlaif et al. (2024) found that performance expectancy and other factors also affected educators’ behavioral intentions to integrate genera-",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,6,"students’ belief that ChatGPT would improve their productivity and academic performance) was a strong predictor of intention to use the tool. Khlaif et al. (2024) found that performance expectancy and other factors also affected educators’ behavioral intentions to integrate genera- tive AI into their teaching practices. Similarly, Effort Expectancy (the perceived ease of use) significantly influenced students’ willingness to integrate ChatGPT into their learning practices, which was consistent with the model’s predictions. Another study by Abbad (2021) applied the UTAUT model to explore student use of e-learning systems in higher education. Their research found that Facilitat- ing Conditions—such as access to resources, training, and institutional support— played a crucial role in shaping students’ patterns of technology usage. This aligns with the UTAUT framework’s emphasis on the importance of environmental factors in facilitating technology use. In the context of mobile learning, Alyoussef (2021) employed the UTAUT model to assess student engagement with mobile learning technologies. They found that",157
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,7,"1287 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Social Influence—the effect of peers and instructors encouraging the use of tech- nology—was an important factor influencing students’ use of mobile learning. This illustrates how the UTAUT model can capture the social dynamics involved in tech- nology acceptance in educational environments. The Hedonic Motivation factor has also been shown to play a significant role in technology adoption in education. For example, a study by Nikolopoulou et al. (2021) explored the use of mobile internet in education through tablets and phones. Their research indicated that students’ enjoyment and satisfaction with these tech- nologies (i.e., Hedonic Motivation) positively influenced their sustained engagement and use. This empirical evidence suggests that motivation derived from the enter- tainment and enjoyment of technology can be a powerful driver of acceptance in educational settings. These studies collectively demonstrate the applicability of the UTAUT model to educational technologies, highlighting its relevance in contexts like the present study on generative AI. The consistent finding across studies is that UTAUT factors strongly explain individuals’ technology acceptance. These insights provide validation for the use of",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,7,"the UTAUT model to educational technologies, highlighting its relevance in contexts like the present study on generative AI. The consistent finding across studies is that UTAUT factors strongly explain individuals’ technology acceptance. These insights provide validation for the use of UTAUT in examining attitudes toward generative AI in higher education. Adaptation of UTAUT for Generative AI This study adopts a modified version of the UTAUT, developed by Strzelecki (2023, 2024) and Strzelecki et al. (2024). Strzelecki’s modification replaces the Price Value factor with Personal Innovativeness, based on Agarwal and Prasad’s (1998) conceptualization of an individual’s willingness to adopt and experiment with new technologies. This modification is particularly relevant to the study of generative AI, as many AI tools are free to use or carry minimal costs (e.g., ChatGPT’s premium subscription is around $20 per month), making Price Value less pertinent in predict- ing technology adoption in this context. Instead, Personal Innovativeness captures the propensity of individuals to be early adopters of new technologies. Relevance of UTAUT Factors in This Study In applying the UTAUT model to this study, we focus",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,7,"in predict- ing technology adoption in this context. Instead, Personal Innovativeness captures the propensity of individuals to be early adopters of new technologies. Relevance of UTAUT Factors in This Study In applying the UTAUT model to this study, we focus on six key factors: Perfor- mance Expectancy, Effort Expectancy, Social Influence, Habit, Hedonic Motivation, and Personal Innovativeness. These factors have been shown to influence technology acceptance in educational settings and are likely to shape attitudes toward genera- tive AI in higher education. By examining these factors, this study seeks to provide a comprehensive understanding of the drivers behind the adoption of generative AI among students and faculty. Moreover, the inclusion of Personal Innovativeness offers insights into how early adopters of technology may drive the integration of generative AI into academic work. Understanding the interplay of these factors is crucial as universities develop policies to encourage the responsible use of genera- tive AI while also fostering innovation and enhancing educational outcomes.",160
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,8,"1288 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Although UTAUT provides a solid framework for understanding technology acceptance and use, the specific context of generative AI in higher education involves additional complexities (Fig. 1). Education is not simply a straightfor- ward progression toward degree completion, and educational technologies must also support learning competencies (e.g., critical thinking, problem solving, and teamwork) and learning-related attitudinal constructs (e.g., self-efficacy, intrinsic motivation, engagement, and test anxiety). Therefore, this study also considers the perceived benefits of generative AI to learning and academic performance, areas only partially addressed by the Performance Expectancy factor. These fac- tors were adopted from the survey instrument developed by Amani et al. (2023) and White et al. (2024). Furthermore, the emerging nature of generative AI as an educational tool brings ethical issues to the forefront of discussions (e.g., Kim et al., 2024a, 2024b). The moral acceptability of using generative AI in coursework and how such usage aligns with university policies could influence student and faculty behavioral intentions. These considerations include both individual ethical views and university guidelines dictating permissible use in educational settings,",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,8,"The moral acceptability of using generative AI in coursework and how such usage aligns with university policies could influence student and faculty behavioral intentions. These considerations include both individual ethical views and university guidelines dictating permissible use in educational settings, which will be investigated by survey instruments that were adopted and modified from those developed by Amani et al. (2023) and White et al. (2024). The integra- tion of generative AI tools into higher education requires a nuanced analysis of student and faculty views on this complex issue. By using a modified UTAUT framework and including additional factors related to learning and ethics, this study aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of how students and faculty perceive the potential role of generative AI in higher education, providing valu- able insights into stakeholder views as generative AI in education moves from initial resistance to, potentially, acceptance and integration. Figure 1 illustrates the conceptual framework of our study. Fig. 1 Conceptual framework, combining Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) with additional factors that may affect respondents’ intention to use",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,8,"initial resistance to, potentially, acceptance and integration. Figure 1 illustrates the conceptual framework of our study. Fig. 1 Conceptual framework, combining Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) with additional factors that may affect respondents’ intention to use generative AI in university coursework",45
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,9,"1289 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Methodology Study Population This study was conducted within a large land-grant institution in the southeast- ern United States; the university offers a diverse array of academic programs from broad disciplines, including both STEM (Science, Technology, Engineer- ing, and Math) and non-STEM degree programs. The population for this study was selected from individuals who were actively engaged (e.g., enrolled in or taught at least one regular class, excluding colloquium, field study, and internship courses) in the university’s academic environment during the 2023 fall semester. This timeframe was chosen to ensure the relevance and timeliness of the data, given the rapidly evolving landscape of AI use since the launch of ChatGPT in November 2022. The study population was composed of two groups: students and faculty. By examining both student and faculty perspectives, we aim to provide a comprehensive overview of the perceptions of generative AI use in higher educa- tion. Student participants were enrolled in at least one regular course at the uni- versity during fall 2023 to ensure that the participants had recent experience with",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,9,"a comprehensive overview of the perceptions of generative AI use in higher educa- tion. Student participants were enrolled in at least one regular course at the uni- versity during fall 2023 to ensure that the participants had recent experience with the institution’s educational offerings and potentially with generative AI tools as part of their experience. Similarly, the faculty participants taught at least one course at the university during fall 2023. This group included professors and lec- turers from all colleges at the university. Survey Instrument and Data Collection The survey instrument used in this study was developed based on existing studies (e.g., Amani et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023, 2024; Strzelecki et al., 2024; White et al., 2024) that investigated individuals’ perceptions of the use of generative AI tools in learning environments. We first adopted the model proposed by Strzelecki (2023, 2024), which identifies seven factors to explain behavioral intention and use of a generative AI tool. Strzelecki’s (2023) model is grounded in the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) theoretical framework, and the survey was designed to",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,9,"2024), which identifies seven factors to explain behavioral intention and use of a generative AI tool. Strzelecki’s (2023) model is grounded in the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) theoretical framework, and the survey was designed to explore the attitudes toward generative AI use in higher education. The survey used Likert-scale categories for responses, allowing participants to express their level of agreement or disagreement with statements related to predictors identified by Strzelecki (2023) and Strzelecki et al. (2024). The specific survey items are listed in the Appendix of this article for reference. Furthermore, we adopted a survey instrument developed by Amani et al. (2023) that was used to examine university students’ and instructors’ perceptions of gen- erative AI tools, particularly focusing on individuals’ perceived impacts of gen- erative AI on the overall learning environments and eight specific learning goals: critical thinking, problem-solving, teamwork, self-efficacy, test anxiety, aca- demic performance, intrinsic motivation, and student engagement (Amani et al., 2023; White et al., 2024). Although the survey includes questions regarding how",172
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,9,"learning goals: critical thinking, problem-solving, teamwork, self-efficacy, test anxiety, aca- demic performance, intrinsic motivation, and student engagement (Amani et al., 2023; White et al., 2024). Although the survey includes questions regarding how",32
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,10,"1290 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 students and faculty utilize generative AI tools for individual classes, a course- level analysis was outside the research scope of this manuscript. Data were collected through an anonymous online survey that was distributed to faculty and students at the university through multiple outlets, such as the univer- sity’s online learning platform pop-up message, the research team’s personal e-mail list, and flyers. Survey participation was voluntary, and approval for the study was granted by the researchers’ Institutional Review Board (IRB). To encourage partici- pation, the study included an incentive in the form of a drawing for a gift card. The survey was disseminated between December 2023 and January 2024. This time- frame was purposefully selected because it was near the end of the fall 2023 semes- ter so that participants would be most able to recall how they utilized generative AI tools for their learning and teaching. A total of 982 student responses and 76 faculty responses met the study’s criteria after excluding responses that did not pass the qualifying questions (Table 1). Analyses Data",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,10,"recall how they utilized generative AI tools for their learning and teaching. A total of 982 student responses and 76 faculty responses met the study’s criteria after excluding responses that did not pass the qualifying questions (Table 1). Analyses Data Pre‑processing We categorized the majors of student respondents as either STEM (e.g., Biologi- cal Sciences, Physical Sciences, Engineering, Math and Statistics, and Computer Science) or non-STEM based on each student’s listed major. This categorization is important for understanding the disciplinary context of attitudes toward genera- tive AI. Preliminary analysis revealed significant differences in the gender ratio between STEM and non-STEM majors, which could potentially bias the outcomes. To address this, we conducted an analysis that accounted for differences across all four gender-STEM groups. This approach allowed for a more comprehensive and equitable examination of the data that better reflected the diversity of the student population. Following the methodology of Strzelecki (2023), we conducted the confirm- atory factor analysis (CFA) and calculated factor scores for seven constructs: Performance Expectancy, Effort Expectancy, Social Influence, Hedonic Motiva- tion, Habit, Personal Innovativeness, and Behavioral",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,10,"diversity of the student population. Following the methodology of Strzelecki (2023), we conducted the confirm- atory factor analysis (CFA) and calculated factor scores for seven constructs: Performance Expectancy, Effort Expectancy, Social Influence, Hedonic Motiva- tion, Habit, Personal Innovativeness, and Behavioral Intention, while Facilitat- ing Conditions factor was excluded from the final results due to relatively weak Table 1 Demographic characteristics of the survey participants STEM majors include Biological Sciences, Physical Sciences, Engineering, Math and Statistics, and Computer Science Student (n = 982) Faculty (n = 76) Gender Female 48% 51% Race/Ethnicity Non-Hispanic White 51% 68% Teaching Experience Longer than 10 years - 57% Academic Level Undergraduate 72% - Major STEM 50% -",112
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,11,"1291 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 performance, as indicated by its low Cronbach’s alpha and inadequate CFA results. These factors were derived from survey items designed to measure the theoretical constructs in the UTAUT framework, and the specific survey items associated with each factor construct are listed in the Appendix. Calculating these scores allowed for a structured analysis of the attitudes toward generative AI. Statistical Analyses A confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) was employed to obtain the factor score of the seven constructs. Cronbach’s alpha scores of the seven constructs were obtained to check the internal validity, and CFI, TLI, and RMSEA were obtained to investigate the CFA model fitness (Tables 5 and 6 in Appendix). Welch’s two- sample t-tests were used to compare factor scores between students and faculty for the seven attitudinal constructs. ANOVA and Tukey’s HSD post-hoc tests were used to compare attitudinal factor scores among the four student groups regard- ing gender (i.e., females vs. males) and majors (i.e., STEM vs. Non-STEM). This approach allowed us to identify significant differences across groups within the student population. Lastly,",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,11,"were used to compare attitudinal factor scores among the four student groups regard- ing gender (i.e., females vs. males) and majors (i.e., STEM vs. Non-STEM). This approach allowed us to identify significant differences across groups within the student population. Lastly, the Wilcoxon rank sum test allowed us to compare Likert-scale items between students and faculty. Since the data did not follow a normal distribution, this non-parametric test was useful in providing insights into survey questions on student competencies, overall learning, ethics, and frequency of AI use. Eight hundred fifty observations were used for the student subgroup analysis because of missing values regarding gender. For analysis regarding views on learning, student competencies, and the use of generative AI tools, five observations from the faculty sample were excluded because of missing values. All analyses were conducted using R Statistical Software (R Core Team, 2021). Specifically, we used the likert package (Bryer & Speerschneider, 2016), lavaan package (Rosseel, 2012), ltm package (Rizopoulos, 2006), and psych package (Revelle, 2023) to facilitate our analyses. Several limitations should be acknowledged regarding the statistical analyses. First, the",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,11,"Team, 2021). Specifically, we used the likert package (Bryer & Speerschneider, 2016), lavaan package (Rosseel, 2012), ltm package (Rizopoulos, 2006), and psych package (Revelle, 2023) to facilitate our analyses. Several limitations should be acknowledged regarding the statistical analyses. First, the faculty sample size (n = 76) is relatively small compared to the student sample (n = 982), which may introduce a potential imbalance in the data and limit the robustness of comparisons between these groups. To mitigate this, we employed statistical techniques such as Welch’s t-tests and non-parametric tests to account for differences in sample sizes, ensuring more reliable comparisons. However, the smaller faculty sample may still affect the generalizability of the findings related to instructor attitudes toward generative AI. Second, the study was conducted at a single large public university in the southeastern United States, which may limit the broader applicability of the results to other institu- tions with different student populations, educational environments, or technologi- cal policies. While the findings provide valuable insights into generative AI use in higher education, caution should be exercised when extending these conclu-",180
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,11,"broader applicability of the results to other institu- tions with different student populations, educational environments, or technologi- cal policies. While the findings provide valuable insights into generative AI use in higher education, caution should be exercised when extending these conclu- sions to different institutional contexts, such as smaller colleges, private universi- ties, or international settings.",55
A100,A100.pdf,10.1007/s10755-024-09774-w,12,"1292 Innovative Higher Education (2025) 50:1281–1313 Results In the following sections, we present our findings on (1) attitudes related to gen- erative AI tools in learning and teaching, (2) perceived impacts of generative AI tools on students’ learning, (3) views on the ethics and policy need for generative AI use in higher education, and (4) the frequency of generative AI use in learning and teaching. For these four topics, we will present (a) comparisons between faculty and students and (b) comparisons among student groups based on majors (i.e., STEM vs. Non-STEM) and gender (i.e., females vs. males). Comparing Attitudes about the Use of Generative AI Tools in Courses Table 2 illustrates the results of the two-sample t-test on the factor scores of the seven constructs between students and faculty. For three of the seven attitudes related to the use of generative AI tools in higher education, students and faculty did not differ significantly. However, students thought that generative AI tools were eas- ier to learn (Effort Expectancy; p < 0.01) and more fun to use (Hedonic Motivation; Table 2 Comparison",180
