A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1 Abstract The rapid adoption of generative artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots, particularly ChatGPT, among university students reflects significant changes in the teaching and learning process. This study examines the usage patterns, motivations, and attitudes of 974 university students who use generative chatbots in academic settings. Results indicate widespread usage, with 61.4% of students reporting the use of chatbots, mainly ChatGPT, and notable differences in adoption rates between genders. The main motivations for their use include understanding complex concepts, managing time efficiently, and improving academic performance. Although students find these tools beneficial for study time management and access to information, they also express concerns about potential dependency and the loss of writing and analytical skills. Gender differences reveal distinct usage patterns. Men tend to make small or moderately significant changes, while women prefer generating new and personal­ ized responses based on the provided texts. The most commonly reported issues are incomplete, irrelevant, or inaccurate responses. These findings suggest the need to implement educational initiatives that integrate chatbots based on language models into academic settings from",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,1,"and personal­ ized responses based on the provided texts. The most commonly reported issues are incomplete, irrelevant, or inaccurate responses. These findings suggest the need to implement educational initiatives that integrate chatbots based on language models into academic settings from an appropriate perspective. Keywords Artificial intelligence · Chatbots · ChatGPT · Higher education · Digital literacy · Student engagement Received: 6 December 2024 / Accepted: 3 June 2025 / Published online: 14 June 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Characteristics, motivations and attitudes of students using ChatGPT and other language model-based chatbots in higher education Vicente Morell-Mengual1 · Olga Fernández-García1 · Carmen Berenguer1 · Jessica Ortega-Barón2 · María Dolores Gil-Llario1 · Verónica Estruch- García1 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",126
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 1 Introduction Technological advancements and discoveries drive significant changes in various sectors of society. One such advancement has been language model-based chatbots, like ChatGPT, which have gained rapid acceptance among university students and other academic users (Mamo et al., 2024; Stöhr et al., 2024). These tools, based on deep language models, have become popular due to their ability to interact conver­ sationally and generate natural language texts on diverse topics (Yigci et al., 2024). The incorporation of chatbots into the educational field has generated both enthu­ siasm and caution among educators and institutions (Giannakos et al., 2024). The accessibility and capability of these tools to address inquiries and synthesize infor­ mation provide students with an additional resource to explore and consolidate complex concepts (Rawas, 2024). This aspect could spark interest and stimulate critical thinking and creativity (Kiryakova & Angelova, 2023). However, their use also presents significant challenges. Some studies point to a possible technological dependency, warning that constant use of these tools might reduce the ability to solve problems independently (Tlili et al., 2023).",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,2,"and creativity (Kiryakova & Angelova, 2023). However, their use also presents significant challenges. Some studies point to a possible technological dependency, warning that constant use of these tools might reduce the ability to solve problems independently (Tlili et al., 2023). Another concerning aspect is the accu­ racy of the provided information (Limna et al., 2023). Chatbots may deliver inac­ curate responses as they rely on data that is not continuously updated (Currie, 2023). Additionally, there are ethical concerns related to academic integrity and the risk of unintentional plagiarism when using automatically generated texts (Cotton et al., 2024; Espartinez, 2024). Language model-based chatbots have penetrated the academic environment at a remarkable speed (Hsu & Ching, 2023). A significant, though still undetermined, pro­ portion of students have integrated these tools into their learning process (Abdaljaleel et al., 2024; von Garrel & Mayer, 2023). Preliminary studies indicate higher adoption among men (Stöhr et al., 2024), suggesting potential gender differences in usage frequency, type of interaction, and personal motivations. However, these prelimi­ nary findings need confirmation through more extensive and representative research, as other",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,2,"2023). Preliminary studies indicate higher adoption among men (Stöhr et al., 2024), suggesting potential gender differences in usage frequency, type of interaction, and personal motivations. However, these prelimi­ nary findings need confirmation through more extensive and representative research, as other studies have not observed such differences (Acosta-Enriquez et al., 2024). Motivations for integrating language model-based chatbots into study routines are varied but still not fully clear. Among the most cited reasons are their constant avail­ ability without physical or time restrictions for obtaining information on academic content (Adams et al., 2024; Kayalı et al., 2023). The personalized nature of this inter­ action allows for responses tailored to specific needs without concerns about external judgment or social pressure (Hawanti & Zubaydulloevna, 2023). Another recurring motivation is the search for immediate solutions to reduce study time (Ansari et al., 2024) and, ultimately, improve academic outcomes (Komba, 2024; Wu & Yu, 2024). One major issue with the rapid adoption of this technology is that the educational system has not had enough time to adapt adequately (Korseberg & Elken, 2024). The emergence and popularization",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,2,"improve academic outcomes (Komba, 2024; Wu & Yu, 2024). One major issue with the rapid adoption of this technology is that the educational system has not had enough time to adapt adequately (Korseberg & Elken, 2024). The emergence and popularization of ChatGPT have outpaced educational institutions’ ability to develop plans and learning strategies to guide its effective and ethical use (Abulibdeh et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024). Consequently, students have learned to interact with this technology entirely on their own or through tutorials available on social media (Monib et al., 2024). However, most lack adequate strategies to assess the reliability of generated responses and methodological tools to verify and cross- 1 3 22258",114
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 check information with other reliable sources (Shoufan, 2023). Additionally, students often struggle with incorrect or false responses, commonly referred to as “AI hal­ lucinations”, where the chatbot generates misleading or entirely fabricated informa­ tion that appears credible (Emsley, 2023). Without sufficient critical evaluation skills, many students may unknowingly incorporate misinformation into their academic work. Another major challenge is outdated responses, as AI models rely on static training data and may not reflect the latest developments in certain fields (Casheekar et al., 2024). Many students, regardless of gender, do not have a clear understanding of the ethical boundaries associated with using these systems (Acosta-Enriquez et al., 2024). A significant proportion does not consider that using these tools could involve practices aligned with conventional plagiarism standards (Gruenhagen et al., 2024). This perception leads to near-verbatim or minimally modified use of generated con­ tent instead of treating such texts as ideas to develop original arguments (Javaid et al., 2023; Valova et al., 2024). The use of external rephrasing tools to alter generated content and reduce similarities with",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,3,"minimally modified use of generated con­ tent instead of treating such texts as ideas to develop original arguments (Javaid et al., 2023; Valova et al., 2024). The use of external rephrasing tools to alter generated content and reduce similarities with the original text has also been observed (Haensch et al., 2023). The improper use of these tools is leading to academic issues and is being penalized in some universities (Cotton et al., 2024). Among the main tasks for which language model-based chatbots are used is infor­ mation retrieval (Zhang & Yang, 2024). These systems’ ability to process and syn­ thesize large volumes of data allows quick access to detailed explanations (Adams et al., 2024). This task often serves as a starting point for automated text writing (von Garrel & Mayer, 2023). It is important to note that unauthorized use of texts gener­ ated by these means may lead to academic issues depending on each educational institution’s integrity policies (Wang et al., 2024). Other documented tasks include idea generation (Espartinez, 2024), summary creation (von Garrel & Mayer, 2023), and translation (Sahari",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,3,"texts gener­ ated by these means may lead to academic issues depending on each educational institution’s integrity policies (Wang et al., 2024). Other documented tasks include idea generation (Espartinez, 2024), summary creation (von Garrel & Mayer, 2023), and translation (Sahari et al., 2023). The widespread acceptance of generative chatbots in higher education does not imply uniform student attitudes. Early studies suggest that attitudes vary based on perceived usefulness, ease of use, social influence, and perceived risk (Abdaljaleel et al., 2024). Generally, women tend to show more negative attitudes toward using chatbots for learning and assessment, while men exhibit greater optimism (Stöhr et al., 2024). However, due to its novelty, there are not yet enough studies to ade­ quately quantify and characterize this phenomenon’s magnitude (Michel-Villarreal et al., 2023; Royer, 2024). The current scientific literature presents a fragmented view of chatbot use in education, relying more on assumptions and future forecasts than on verified facts (McGrath et al., 2024). Therefore, this study aims to analyze the frequency, motivations, and attitudes of university students regarding the use of language model-based chatbots in",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,3,"use in education, relying more on assumptions and future forecasts than on verified facts (McGrath et al., 2024). Therefore, this study aims to analyze the frequency, motivations, and attitudes of university students regarding the use of language model-based chatbots in the academic context. Additionally, gender differ­ ences will be explored to identify possible trends and provide a more comprehensive view of these tools’ educational implications. 1 3 22259",68
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 2 Method 2.1 Participants The study sample consisted of 974 university students from Spain (Table 1). The majority were women, representing 80.2% of the sample (n = 781), while 19.4% were men (n = 189), and 0.4% identified as non-binary (n = 4). Participants’ ages ranged from 17 to 62, with a predominant age group between 17 and 19 years (42.8%). Most students (95.6%) were pursuing undergraduate studies, with health sciences (50.4%) and social and legal sciences (44.8%) being the most represented academic disciplines. Regarding the type of university, 99.3% of participants were enrolled in public universities, while 0.7% attended private institutions. The predominant study mode was in-person (91.6%). Finally, 30.8% of participants balanced their studies with paid employment, while 69.2% were not employed. There are no statistically significant differences between men and women in any of the sociodemographic vari­ ables analyzed. Table 1 Sociodemographic profile of the sample (n = 974) N % Male Female X2 p Gender Male 189 19.4 Female 781 80.2 Non-binary 4 0.4 Age 17–19 416 42.8 43.9% 42.6%",180
