A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 2.2 Mesures An ad hoc questionnaire was specifically designed for this research, based on various studies on attitudes and usage of language model-based chatbots. To ensure the rel­ evance and clarity of the items, a preliminary analysis was conducted before formal data collection. The questionnaire was modified based on feedback from several pro­ fessors of developmental and educational psychology. The final version comprises 17 items with different response formats (Likert-type, dichotomous, multiple choice, and multiple selection). The survey topics covered chatbot usage, frequency of use, actions taken after receiving texts generated by these tools, and the purposes and motivations driving their use. Additionally, difficulties experienced, academic issues resulting from their use, and attitudes toward their impact on academic performance were identified. Language model-based chatbots use The survey investigates whether students have used generative AI chatbots for their university studies and, if so, which tool they use most frequently (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google Bard, among others). One item assesses the frequency of chatbot use for academic purposes using a four-point Likert scale with response options ranging",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,5,"chatbots for their university studies and, if so, which tool they use most frequently (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google Bard, among others). One item assesses the frequency of chatbot use for academic purposes using a four-point Likert scale with response options ranging from “rarely” to “always.” Another item collects information on actions taken after receiving a text generated by a chatbot, with the following five options: “I copy the generated text verbatim,” “I use another program to modify the generated text,” “I make minor changes to the generated text,” “I make very significant changes to the generated text,” and “I create a completely personal text based on the generated text.” Purposes: A multiple-choice item explores the purposes for which students use these chatbots in their academic tasks, with the following response options: “Obtain information on a topic,” “Validate or verify information,” “Generate ideas,” “Write essays and/or theoretical papers,” “Complete practical assignments,” “Create summaries,” and “Others.” Motivations: A multiple-choice item assesses what motivates students to use AI lan­ guage chatbots for academic purposes. The response options are: ""Reduce study time,""""Improve academic performance,"" ""Enhance",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,5,"ideas,” “Write essays and/or theoretical papers,” “Complete practical assignments,” “Create summaries,” and “Others.” Motivations: A multiple-choice item assesses what motivates students to use AI lan­ guage chatbots for academic purposes. The response options are: ""Reduce study time,""""Improve academic performance,"" ""Enhance understanding of complex concepts,"" ""Improve the quality of academic work,""""Enhance writing skills,"" ""Explore new learning methods,"" and ""Social pressure and influence."" Experienced Difficulties: A multiple-choice item identifies difficulties stu­ dents have faced when using chatbots: ""None,"" ""Irrelevant or incoherent responses,""""Incomplete or insufficient responses,"" ""Incorrect or false responses,"" ""Technical or connection issues,"" ""Outdated responses,"" and ""Others."" Academic Issues: Two dichotomous items assess whether any student has witnessed or experienced academic issues resulting from chatbot use in university tasks. Attitudes and Perceptions: This section explores attitudes toward the effects of gen­ erative AI chatbot use on academic performance. It includes 8 Likert-type items with five response options, ranging from 1 ""strongly disagree"" to 5 ""strongly agree."" The first block of four items assesses positive attitudes related, for example, to improved understanding of academic concepts, efficient study time 1 3 22261",178
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,5,"items with five response options, ranging from 1 ""strongly disagree"" to 5 ""strongly agree."" The first block of four items assesses positive attitudes related, for example, to improved understanding of academic concepts, efficient study time 1 3 22261",38
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 management, or development of digital skills. The second block of four items assesses negative attitudes related, among other aspects, to increased procrastina­ tion or decline in writing skills. 2.3 Procedure The sample was recruited through an online questionnaire distributed via the Lime­ Survey platform between January and May 2024. The link to the questionnaire was sent to all students enrolled in courses taught by the authors and four other collabo­ rating professors. Additional students were also contacted via social media. Thus, the sample is a non-probabilistic convenience sample. Participation was voluntary, and guidelines from the Spanish Organic Law 3/2018, of December 5, on Personal Data Protection and guarantee of digital rights were followed. At the beginning of the survey, informed consent was obtained from all participants, and they were informed about the confidentiality and anonymity of their responses. The IP addresses of the devices used were not recorded. 2.4 Statistical analyses Statistical analyses were conducted using IBM SPSS Statistics 28.0.1.1. Frequen­ cies and percentages were calculated to describe categorical variables (frequency and mode of",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,6,"anonymity of their responses. The IP addresses of the devices used were not recorded. 2.4 Statistical analyses Statistical analyses were conducted using IBM SPSS Statistics 28.0.1.1. Frequen­ cies and percentages were calculated to describe categorical variables (frequency and mode of use, purposes, motivations, and difficulties) for the total sample and by gender. Comparisons between men and women were made using the chi-square test (χ²). The magnitude of differences was estimated using Cramer’s V index: >0.25 = very strong association; >0.15 = strong association; >0.10 = moderate association; and > 0.05 = weak association (Akoglu, 2018). Student’s t-test was used to compare means between men and women for continuous variables (attitudes and perceptions). The effect size of these differences was estimated using Cohen’s d. Values below 0.20 indicate a small effect, values around 0.50 indicate a moderate effect, and values above 0.80 indicate a large effect (Fritz et al., 2012). The statistical significance level was set at p <.05 for all tests. 3 Results 3.1 Types of chatbots, frequency, and mode of use A total of 61.4% of participants reported using",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,6,"0.80 indicate a large effect (Fritz et al., 2012). The statistical significance level was set at p <.05 for all tests. 3 Results 3.1 Types of chatbots, frequency, and mode of use A total of 61.4% of participants reported using generative AI chatbots in their uni­ versity studies (Table 2), with a higher prevalence among men (69%) compared to women (59.6%). This difference was statistically significant (p =.018). ChatGPT was, by far, the most frequently used tool among students (93.8%), with no notable differ­ ences between genders. Other chatbots, such as Bing Chat (2.6%), Perplexity (1.2%), and Google Bard (0.9%), were used to a lesser extent. Regarding usage frequency, most students reported using these tools occasionally (48.9%), followed by those who used them rarely (36.3%). Although men tended to use chatbots more frequently, the gender difference did not reach statistical significance (p =.110). 1 3 22262",146
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 Additionally, the way students modified text generated by chatbots was evaluated (Table 2). A total of 44% of participants indicated that they created a new text based on the content provided by the chatbot, with a higher prevalence among women (46.4%) compared to men (35.2%). However, men demonstrated a greater tendency to make minor edits (24.8% vs. 19.1%) or use text-editing programs (4% vs. 2.2%). These differences were statistically significant (p =.012) with a small effect size (V = 0.150). 3.2 Purposes, motivations, challenges, and associated academic problems The primary purpose of using chatbots was to obtain information (79.3%) and gen­ erate ideas (61.6%). A significantly smaller proportion used these tools for summa­ rizing content (34.5%) or verifying information (30.3%). Gender-based analyses revealed notable differences in some purposes (Table 3). Men more frequently used chatbots for summarizing content (45.7%) than women (31.1%), and this difference was statistically significant (p =.003). Similarly, the use of chatbots for writing essays and assignments was significantly more common among men (22%) compared to women (12.8%; p =.003). The",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,7,"chatbots for summarizing content (45.7%) than women (31.1%), and this difference was statistically significant (p =.003). Similarly, the use of chatbots for writing essays and assignments was significantly more common among men (22%) compared to women (12.8%; p =.003). The main motivations for using generative AI chatbots were also examined (Table 3). The most commonly cited motivation was to improve understanding of complex concepts (63.6%). Less frequently reported motivations included reducing study time (36.2%), improving academic performance (31.9%), and exploring new learning methods (31.4%). Regarding gender differences, men more often reported reducing study time as their primary motivation (52% vs. 31.8% for women). This Table 2 Types of chatbots, frequency, and mode of use Total (n = 580) Men (n = 127) Woman (n = 453) X2 p V Use of Chatbots 61.4% 69% 59.6% 5.544 0.018 0.077 Chatbot Types ChatGPT 93.8% 90.6% 94.7% 8.978 0.110 0.124 Perplexity 1.2% 0% 1.5% Google Bard 0.9% 1.6% 0.7% Bing Chat 2.6% 4.7% 2% You.com 1% 2.4% 0.7% Otro 0.5% 0.8% 0.4% Frecuency of use Rarely 36.3% 31% 37.7% 6.766 0.080 0.108 Occasionally",180
