A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,8,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 difference was statistically significant (p =.001) with a small effect size (V = 0.174). Similarly, improving academic performance was significantly more frequent among men (49.6%) compared to women (26.9%), also with a small effect size (V = 0.201). The most common problem reported by students was obtaining incomplete or insufficient answers (54%; Table 4). Other less prominent issues included generat­ ing irrelevant or incoherent responses to the requested topic (36.4%) and detecting incorrect or false answers (38.3%). Only 16.6% of students reported no significant difficulties. Gender-specific data revealed some differences. Men more frequently reported obtaining irrelevant or incoherent responses (44.9%) compared to women (34%), a statistically significant difference (p =.028). Additionally, men more often cited receiving incorrect or false responses (45.7% vs. 36.2% for women), though this difference did not reach statistical significance (p =.063). Conversely, women Table 3 Purposes and motivations Total (n = 580) Men (n = 127) Woman (n = 453) X2 P V Purposes Obtaining information 79.3% 82.7% 78.4% 1.123 0.323 0.044 Verifying information 30.3% 35.4% 28.9% 1.992 0.190 0.059",180
