A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22257–22274 more often reported experiencing no difficulties with chatbot use (18.3% vs. 10.2% for men), with a significant difference (p =.031). A small proportion (7.3%) of participants observed academic problems in other students due to the misuse of chatbots. Conversely, only 2.9% admitted to having experienced academic problems directly. No significant gender differences were detected in these aspects. 3.3 Attitudes Among the most frequently reported positive attitudes, the perception that the use of chatbots can improve the ability to understand academic concepts stood out (M = 4.06), with no significant differences between men and women (p =.413). A positive attitude was also observed regarding the potential of these tools to develop relevant digital skills (M = 3.66), with men showing a significantly higher perception than women (p =.001). Another positive attitude pertained to the ability of chatbots to help manage study time more efficiently (M = 3.61), with men again expressing a more favorable attitude compared to women (p =.001). The most commonly reported negative attitude was related to the influence of chatbots in reducing",180
A101,A101.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13650-1,9,"of chatbots to help manage study time more efficiently (M = 3.61), with men again expressing a more favorable attitude compared to women (p =.001). The most commonly reported negative attitude was related to the influence of chatbots in reducing the ability to differentiate between reliable and unreliable infor­ mation (M = 3.81). This perception was significantly more prevalent among women than men (p =.004). Students also expressed some concern regarding the potential for excessive dependency on generative tools in the completion of academic tasks (M = 3.74), with a significant gender difference, as women reported greater concern (p =.001). Another notable negative attitude related to the deterioration of written expression skills (M = 3.69), with this concern again being more pronounced among women than men (p =.006). Table 5 Perceptions and attitudes Total (n = 580) Men (n = 127) Woman (n = 453) t p d Its use can improve the ability to understand academic concepts. 4.06 (0.79) 4.05 (0.89) 4.07 (0.76) − 0.219 0.413 0.024 Its use can help foster more creative thinking. 2.89 (1.12) 2.98 (1.14)",180
