A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,1,"is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Integrating ChatGPT into inclusive and sustainable E-learning: mediating roles of student empowerment and satisfaction and the moderating role of inclusivity in achieving sustainable education goals Usman Rehman* Graduate School of Venture, Hoseo University, Seoul, Republic of Korea Introduction: This study investigates how ChatGPT, a generative AI tool, can promote inclusive and sustainable e-learning by enhancing learner empowerment, satisfaction, and engagement. Methods: Drawing on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self- Determination Theory (SDT), and the Sustainable Education Framework, a structural model was developed to examine the mediating roles of student empowerment and student satisfaction, and the moderating role of inclusivity perception. Data were collected from 350 students across South Korean universities using a cross-sectional survey and analyzed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). Results: The ﬁndings indicate that perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and accessibility of ChatGPT signiﬁcantly enhance student empowerment, which in turn increases satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes. Furthermore, inclusivity perception strengthens these relationships. Discussion: The study contributes theoretically and aligns AI-enabled education with SDG-4.",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,1,"perceived ease of use, and accessibility of ChatGPT signiﬁcantly enhance student empowerment, which in turn increases satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes. Furthermore, inclusivity perception strengthens these relationships. Discussion: The study contributes theoretically and aligns AI-enabled education with SDG-4. KEYWORDS ChatGPT, generative AI, inclusive e-learning, SDG-4, student empowerment, student satisfaction, sustainable education 1 Introduction The rapid evolution of educational technologies has fundamentally transformed the landscape of higher education. E-learning, deﬁned as the systematic use of information and communication technologies (ICT) to support teaching and learning processes, has emerged as a dominant mode of knowledge delivery worldwide. The COVID-19 pandemic accelerated this digital transition, compelling institutions to adopt online and blended learning environments as the new educational norm. As a result, educators and policymakers have increasingly recognized that e-learning is not merely a substitute for traditional classroom teaching but a critical mechanism for sustainable TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 08 April 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",158
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"and inclusive education in the twenty-ﬁrst century (Dhawan, 2020; Moore and Kearsley, 2011). Despite signiﬁcant advancements in digital infrastructure, e- learning continues to face challenges of inclusivity, accessibility, and pedagogical sustainability. Several studies have noted that the rapid adoption of digital platforms often overlooks the needs of students with disabilities, limited digital literacy, or language barriers (Al-Azawei et al., 2016; Almarashdeh, 2016; UNESCO, 2017). Sustainable e-learning requires more than technology integration; it demands equitable access, adaptive content, and inclusive pedagogy that promote lifelong learning opportunities for all, as envisioned in United Nations Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG-4) (UNESCO, 2017). Educational technologies (EdTech), such as artiﬁcial intelligence (AI), gamiﬁcation, and adaptive learning systems, are increasingly recognized as enablers of inclusive and sustainable education (UNESCO, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). AI has revolutionized personalized learning by offering data-driven feedback, automated assessment, and intelligent tutoring systems (Al-Rahmi et al., 2021; Deci and Ryan, 2017). However, while AI-assisted learning environments have been explored for personalization and efﬁciency, their role in promoting accessibility, empowerment, and sustainable learning outcomes remains underexplored. Recently, the advent of generative AI models,",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"systems (Al-Rahmi et al., 2021; Deci and Ryan, 2017). However, while AI-assisted learning environments have been explored for personalization and efﬁciency, their role in promoting accessibility, empowerment, and sustainable learning outcomes remains underexplored. Recently, the advent of generative AI models, especially ChatGPT, has reshaped the educational technology discourse. ChatGPT, based on OpenAI’s transformer architecture, enables human-like interaction, real-time language generation, and contextual assistance that can adapt to diverse learner needs (Al-Fraihat et al., 2020). Preliminary evidence suggests that ChatGPT enhances learner engagement, supports writing and critical thinking, and provides multilingual assistance. Nonetheless, empirical evidence on how ChatGPT contributes to inclusivity, accessibility, and sustainable e-learning environments remains scarce, particularly in higher education contexts. From a theoretical standpoint, most prior studies have used the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (Davis, 1989) or the Uniﬁed Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) (Venkatesh et al., 2003) to explain technology adoption behavior. While these models explain the intention to use technology, they often fail to capture deeper psychological mechanisms such as student empowerment, satisfaction, and the broader sustainability outcomes derived from continued usage (Bhattacherjee,",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"et al., 2003) to explain technology adoption behavior. While these models explain the intention to use technology, they often fail to capture deeper psychological mechanisms such as student empowerment, satisfaction, and the broader sustainability outcomes derived from continued usage (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Chiu and Wang, 2008). Moreover, Self- Determination Theory (SDT) highlights that sustainable learning outcomes are not merely the result of system usability but also of intrinsic motivation and empowerment (Deci and Ryan, 2017). Thus, integrating TAM and SDT perspectives offers a holistic understanding of how perceived usefulness, ease of use, and accessibility of ChatGPT inﬂuence empowerment, satisfaction, and sustainable learning outcomes. In addition, the role of inclusivity perception remains theoretically underdeveloped in the context of AI-based learning. Inclusivity acts as a boundary condition that determines how effectively students beneﬁt from technology- enhanced environments (Burgstahler, 2015; UNESCO, 2020). While inclusivity has been discussed in e-learning accessibility literature (Al-Azawei et al., 2016; UNESCO, 2020), its moderating effect on the psychological mechanisms linking AI adoption to sustainable outcomes has rarely been tested empirically. Hence, a signiﬁcant research gap persists regarding how ChatGPT,",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"has been discussed in e-learning accessibility literature (Al-Azawei et al., 2016; UNESCO, 2020), its moderating effect on the psychological mechanisms linking AI adoption to sustainable outcomes has rarely been tested empirically. Hence, a signiﬁcant research gap persists regarding how ChatGPT, as a form of generative AI, facilitates inclusive and sustainable e-learning by empowering learners, enhancing satisfaction, and fostering long-term engagement. Few empirical studies have systematically examined these mediating and moderating mechanisms in a uniﬁed structural framework (Bao, 2020; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). Addressing this gap, the present study develops and validates a comprehensive model that integrates ChatGPT adoption constructs (perceived usefulness, ease of use, and accessibility) with empowerment, satisfaction, and inclusivity to explain sustainable education outcomes. 1.1 Signiﬁcance and uniqueness of the study The integration of artiﬁcial intelligence into higher education has become a deﬁning feature of twenty-ﬁrst-century pedagogy; however, most prior studies have concentrated on either technological adoption or pedagogical efﬁciency, neglecting the broader dimensions of inclusivity, accessibility, and sustainability that underpin equitable education systems (Bao, 2020; Dwivedi et al., 2024; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). The present study is",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"most prior studies have concentrated on either technological adoption or pedagogical efﬁciency, neglecting the broader dimensions of inclusivity, accessibility, and sustainability that underpin equitable education systems (Bao, 2020; Dwivedi et al., 2024; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). The present study is signiﬁcant because it bridges this critical gap by examining ChatGPT, a generative AI tool, not only as a means of technological innovation but also as a catalyst for inclusive and sustainable learning ecosystems. First, this research provides a multidimensional contribution by integrating three theoretical domains that are rarely combined in a single framework: the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self-Determination Theory (SDT), and the Sustainable Education paradigm. Whereas TAM elucidates behavioral intentions toward technology adoption (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh and Davis, 2000), SDT explains intrinsic motivational mechanisms such as empowerment and satisfaction that drive continuous learning behavior (Deci and Ryan, 2017). By merging these perspectives, the study explains not only why students use ChatGPT but also how such use cultivates sustainable learning outcomes consistent with SDG-4 (Quality Education) (UNESCO, 2017). Second, this study is among the earliest empirical models to examine simultaneously",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,2,"merging these perspectives, the study explains not only why students use ChatGPT but also how such use cultivates sustainable learning outcomes consistent with SDG-4 (Quality Education) (UNESCO, 2017). Second, this study is among the earliest empirical models to examine simultaneously the mediating effects of student empowerment and student satisfaction and the moderating role of inclusivity perception in the relationship between ChatGPT adoption and sustainable learning outcomes. Previous research has largely explored AI or e-learning adoption from functional or technical perspectives (Alenezi, 2022; Bao, 2020), with limited attention to how inclusive perceptions strengthen learners’ psychological engagement and translate into sustainable educational performance (Burgstahler, 2015; UNESCO, 2020). Third, the study contributes methodological novelty by employing Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) to evaluate both direct and indirect pathways among the constructs. This approach enables robust testing of complex interrelationships and conditional effects, providing a holistic understanding of how generative AI Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",156
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"supports sustainable learning in higher education contexts (Hair et al., 2022; Henseler et al., 2016). The model simultaneously incorporates technological factors (perceived usefulness, ease of use, accessibility), psychological mediators (empowerment and satisfaction), and a contextual moderator (inclusivity), offering a balanced integration of human–technology interaction and sustainability perspectives. Fourth, the study’s contextual uniqueness lies in its focus on ChatGPT, an emerging generative AI system that remains under-researched in empirical sustainability and inclusive education frameworks. While earlier studies have examined adaptive learning, gamiﬁcation, or virtual reality-based learning (Al-Emran et al., 2018; Teo, 2009), generative conversational AI represents a new frontier that enables real-time personalized assistance, multilingual feedback, and cognitive scaffolding for diverse learners. By empirically investigating ChatGPT within a higher education setting, this study provides pioneering insights into how AI can reduce educational inequality, enhance accessibility for marginalized or language-diverse students, and promote long-term sustainable educational transformation. Finally, this research holds strong practical and policy relevance. For educators, the model identiﬁes pathways through which AI-driven tools foster learner empowerment and satisfaction, guiding the design of inclusive digital learning strategies. For policymakers, the",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"promote long-term sustainable educational transformation. Finally, this research holds strong practical and policy relevance. For educators, the model identiﬁes pathways through which AI-driven tools foster learner empowerment and satisfaction, guiding the design of inclusive digital learning strategies. For policymakers, the ﬁndings offer evidence to support the integration of generative AI within sustainable education frameworks, contributing to equitable digital transformation aligned with SDG-4 (UNESCO, 2021). For technology developers, the results inform the design of AI-based educational systems that prioritize accessibility, inclusivity, and sustainability over mere efﬁciency. In summary, the uniqueness of this study lies in its theoretical integration, empirical innovation, and practical implications, collectively positioning it as one of the earliest comprehensive efforts to explain how ChatGPT operationalizes inclusivity and sustainability principles in higher education. Moving beyond adoption-focused research, the study proposes a transformative model that aligns technological advancement with the global pursuit of quality, equitable, and sustainable education for all. 2 Theoretical framework and hypotheses development 2.1 ChatGPT adoption and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) The Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), originally proposed by Davis (1989), remains one of the most",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"global pursuit of quality, equitable, and sustainable education for all. 2 Theoretical framework and hypotheses development 2.1 ChatGPT adoption and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) The Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), originally proposed by Davis (1989), remains one of the most inﬂuential frameworks for explaining user acceptance of information systems. The model posits that perceived usefulness (PU) and perceived ease of use (PEOU) are the primary determinants of an individual’s intention to adopt and use a technology (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh and Davis, 2000). In educational research, TAM has been widely applied to explain students’ willingness to adopt e-learning platforms, learning management systems, and AI-based educational tools (Al-Emran et al., 2018; Teo, 2009; Venkatesh et al., 2003). In the context of ChatGPT, perceived usefulness refers to the extent to which students believe that using ChatGPT enhances their learning efﬁciency by providing immediate feedback, contextual explanations, and adaptive guidance (Holstein, McLaren and Aleven, 2020; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). Perceived ease of use reﬂects the degree to which learners perceive ChatGPT’s conversational interface as intuitive and effortless, thereby reducing cognitive load and facilitating interaction",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"feedback, contextual explanations, and adaptive guidance (Holstein, McLaren and Aleven, 2020; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). Perceived ease of use reﬂects the degree to which learners perceive ChatGPT’s conversational interface as intuitive and effortless, thereby reducing cognitive load and facilitating interaction (Davis, 1989; Dwivedi et al., 2024). When learners perceive ChatGPT as both useful and easy to use, they are more likely to experience a sense of empowerment, characterized by greater conﬁdence and control over their learning processes (Deci and Ryan, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). H1. Perceived usefulness of ChatGPT positively inﬂuences student empowerment. H2. Perceived ease of use of ChatGPT positively inﬂuences student empowerment. Drawing on Self-Determination Theory, perceived usefulness and ease of use contribute to student empowerment by enhancing learners’ sense of autonomy and competence. When ChatGPT is perceived as intuitive and beneﬁcial, students experience greater control over their learning activities, thereby strengthening empowerment beyond mere technology acceptance (Deci and Ryan, 2017). 2.2 Accessibility support and learner empowerment Accessibility constitutes a core pillar of sustainable and inclusive e-learning. According to UNESCO’s digital education guidelines, learning technologies must ensure equitable participation",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"activities, thereby strengthening empowerment beyond mere technology acceptance (Deci and Ryan, 2017). 2.2 Accessibility support and learner empowerment Accessibility constitutes a core pillar of sustainable and inclusive e-learning. According to UNESCO’s digital education guidelines, learning technologies must ensure equitable participation for learners regardless of physical, linguistic, or socioeconomic constraints (UNESCO, 2018, 2020). Prior studies demonstrate that accessible design features—such as captions, text-to-speech functionality, multilingual interfaces, and adaptive navigation—enhance learner engagement, self-efﬁcacy, and perceived control (Al-Azawei et al., 2016; Burgstahler, 2015). ChatGPT provides accessibility support through conversational explanations, real-time translation, and adaptive responses that accommodate diverse learning needs (Dwivedi et al., 2024; UNESCO, 2020). Such inclusive functionality enables learners to overcome language and cognitive barriers, fostering autonomy and self-conﬁdence, which are central components of empowerment (Deci and Ryan, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). H3. Accessibility support through ChatGPT positively inﬂuences student empowerment. 2.3 Student empowerment and satisfaction Student empowerment reﬂects learners’ perceptions of control, competence, and autonomy in managing their learning activities (Zimmerman, 2002). Empowered students are more intrinsically motivated and exhibit greater persistence in technology-mediated learning environments (Kanfer et al., 2017). Within",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,3,"Student empowerment and satisfaction Student empowerment reﬂects learners’ perceptions of control, competence, and autonomy in managing their learning activities (Zimmerman, 2002). Empowered students are more intrinsically motivated and exhibit greater persistence in technology-mediated learning environments (Kanfer et al., 2017). Within the framework of Self-Determination Theory, empowerment fulﬁlls the psychological needs for autonomy and Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",60
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,4,"competence, which in turn generate intrinsic motivation and satisfaction (Deci and Ryan, 2017). In ChatGPT-assisted learning contexts, empowerment arises from instant guidance, continuous feedback, and opportunities for self-regulated learning. Empirical research consistently reports a strong positive relationship between empowerment and student satisfaction in e-learning and digital learning environments (Al-Fraihat et al., 2020; Bhattacherjee, 2001). H4. Student empowerment positively inﬂuences student satisfaction. 2.4 Student satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes Student satisfaction reﬂects the degree to which technology- enhanced learning meets or exceeds learners’ expectations (Bhattacherjee, 2001). Prior ﬁndings indicate that satisfaction drives continuance intention (CI), deﬁned as the willingness to continue using a digital learning platform, which is critical for the long-term sustainability of e-learning systems (Al-Rahmi et al., 2021; Chiu and Wang, 2008). Building on Expectation– Conﬁrmation Theory, satisfaction strengthens motivation to reuse a system, while continuance intention supports sustained learning engagement and the development of long-term learning capabilities (Al-Rahmi et al., 2021; Bhattacherjee, 2001; Chiu and Wang, 2008). Evidence from recent studies on feedback modality further supports the theoretical plausibility of the empowerment–satisfaction pathway proposed in this",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,4,"supports sustained learning engagement and the development of long-term learning capabilities (Al-Rahmi et al., 2021; Bhattacherjee, 2001; Chiu and Wang, 2008). Evidence from recent studies on feedback modality further supports the theoretical plausibility of the empowerment–satisfaction pathway proposed in this research. For instance, Lo et al. (2025), in a SAGE Open study comparing teacher feedback, AI-generated feedback, and hybrid human–AI feedback, show that feedback modality signiﬁcantly inﬂuences student motivation, perceived feedback quality, and performance outcomes. In particular, hybrid feedback approaches tend to yield stronger motivational and performance beneﬁts by combining the immediacy and accessibility of AI feedback with the pedagogical judgment of instructors. These ﬁndings align conceptually with the present model, in which student empowerment and satisfaction function as key psychological mechanisms linking ChatGPT adoption beliefs to sustainable learning outcomes. H5. Student satisfaction positively inﬂuences continuance intention. H6. Continuance intention positively inﬂuences sustainable learning outcomes. 2.5 Mediating roles of empowerment and satisfaction The interaction between technological perceptions and learning outcomes often operates through motivational mediators. Studies demonstrate that perceived usefulness and ease of use affect learning satisfaction indirectly via empowerment",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,4,"positively inﬂuences sustainable learning outcomes. 2.5 Mediating roles of empowerment and satisfaction The interaction between technological perceptions and learning outcomes often operates through motivational mediators. Studies demonstrate that perceived usefulness and ease of use affect learning satisfaction indirectly via empowerment and engagement (Bhattacherjee, 2001). According to SDT, empowerment and satisfaction form a sequential path from external stimuli (system features) to internalized learning behaviors (Deci and Ryan, 2017). Accordingly, this research posits multiple mediation relationships: H7. Student empowerment mediates the relationship between perceived usefulness and student satisfaction. H8. Student empowerment mediates the relationship between perceived ease of use and student satisfaction. H9. Student satisfaction mediates the relationship between student empowerment and continuance intention. H10. Student satisfaction and continuance intentions sequentially mediate the relationship between student empowerment and sustainable learning outcomes. 2.6 Moderating role of inclusivity perception Inclusivity refers to the belief that a learning environment values and supports diversity in ability, culture, and language (Burgstahler, 2015). Inclusive design enhances belonging, motivation, and engagement (UNESCO, 2020). When learners perceive ChatGPT as inclusive providing equitable, adaptive interaction, the empowering effect of its perceived",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,4,"that a learning environment values and supports diversity in ability, culture, and language (Burgstahler, 2015). Inclusive design enhances belonging, motivation, and engagement (UNESCO, 2020). When learners perceive ChatGPT as inclusive providing equitable, adaptive interaction, the empowering effect of its perceived usefulness, ease and accessibility intensiﬁes (Dwivedi et al., 2024). Hence, inclusivity is posited to strengthen the link between ChatGPT adoption and empowerment: H11a. Inclusivity perception moderates the relationship between perceived usefulness and student empowerment, such that the relationship is stronger at higher levels of inclusivity. H11b. Inclusivity perception moderates the relationship between perceived ease of use and student empowerment, such that the relationship is stronger at higher levels of inclusivity. H11c. Inclusivity perception moderates the relationship between accessibility support and student empowerment, such that the relationship is stronger at higher levels of inclusivity. Recent evidence suggests that the psychological beneﬁts of generative AI adoption in higher education are shaped not only by system characteristics but also by broader socio-institutional conditions. For example, Lo and Chan (2025) demonstrate that students’ engagement with ChatGPT is inﬂuenced by peer norms, integrity culture, ethical",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,4,"of generative AI adoption in higher education are shaped not only by system characteristics but also by broader socio-institutional conditions. For example, Lo and Chan (2025) demonstrate that students’ engagement with ChatGPT is inﬂuenced by peer norms, integrity culture, ethical awareness, and institutional policy knowledge, which jointly condition whether perceived opportunities translate into responsible and constructive use. This perspective indicates that empowerment and satisfaction derived from AI-assisted learning do not occur in isolation but are embedded within social and policy environments that shape norms of acceptable use. In this regard, inclusivity perception in the present study can be viewed as a complementary contextual mechanism that captures learners’ perceptions of fairness, accessibility, and equitable support within AI-enabled learning environments. 2.7 Conceptual framework Drawing upon TAM, Self-Determination Theory, and the Sustainable Education Framework, the proposed conceptual model integrates direct, mediating, and moderating relationships among ChatGPT adoption factors, psychological mechanisms, and sustainability outcomes. The framework provides a holistic Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",162
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,5,"explanation of how generative AI supports inclusive, equitable, and sustainable learning ecosystems within higher education (Dwivedi et al., 2024; UNESCO, 2017). Figure 1 shows the conceptual framework of the study. 3 Research methodology 3.1 Research design This study adopts a quantitative, cross-sectional research design to empirically test the proposed structural model linking ChatGPT adoption factors (perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and accessibility support) with student empowerment, satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes, while considering the moderating role of inclusivity perception. The study follows a positivist paradigm, using a structured questionnaire as the primary data collection instrument. The quantitative approach is consistent with prior e-learning and AI- in-education studies employing Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) (Hair et al., 2022; Henseler et al., 2016). 3.2 Population and sampling The target population comprises university students enrolled in blended or fully online courses across South Korean higher education institutions, where ChatGPT and similar AI-based tools are increasingly integrated into digital learning. A purposive sampling strategy was adopted, selecting students who had used ChatGPT for academic or learning support purposes. The",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,5,"fully online courses across South Korean higher education institutions, where ChatGPT and similar AI-based tools are increasingly integrated into digital learning. A purposive sampling strategy was adopted, selecting students who had used ChatGPT for academic or learning support purposes. The survey instrument was administered in English, as all participants were proﬁcient in English and were enrolled in English-medium higher education programs. This ensured consistent interpretation of measurement items across respondents and eliminated the need for translation procedures. To mitigate potential common method variance, several procedural remedies were implemented, including ensuring respondent anonymity, emphasizing that there were no right or wrong answers, and separating measurement items across constructs within the questionnaire. Although these steps reduce the likelihood of common method bias, its complete elimination cannot be guaranteed in single-source survey research and is therefore acknowledged as a residual limitation (Podsakoff et al., 2003). Following Hair et al. (2022), a minimum sample size of 10× the maximum number of structural paths was ensured for adequate statistical power. Beyond the commonly cited 10-times rule, the sample size of 350 respondents exceeds recommended thresholds",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,5,"al., 2003). Following Hair et al. (2022), a minimum sample size of 10× the maximum number of structural paths was ensured for adequate statistical power. Beyond the commonly cited 10-times rule, the sample size of 350 respondents exceeds recommended thresholds for detecting small-to-moderate effects in structural equation models with interaction terms. Prior methodological guidance suggests that samples above 300 provide adequate statistical power for complex PLS-SEM models involving mediation and moderation, supporting the robustness of the estimated relationships in this study. The survey was conducted from January to March 2025. A total of 400 questionnaires were distributed electronically using Google Forms, yielding 350 valid responses after data cleaning (response rate: 87.5%). This sample size exceeds the threshold for PLS-SEM analysis, ensuring model stability and adequate statistical representation (Hair et al., 2022). Participants were recruited through university course mailing lists and learning management system (LMS) announcements at a higher education institution. Participation was voluntary and anonymous, and no monetary or academic incentives were provided. To be eligible for inclusion, respondents were required to have prior experience using ChatGPT for academic or",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,5,"learning management system (LMS) announcements at a higher education institution. Participation was voluntary and anonymous, and no monetary or academic incentives were provided. To be eligible for inclusion, respondents were required to have prior experience using ChatGPT for academic or learning-related purposes. Several data quality controls were applied prior to analysis. Incomplete responses, duplicate entries, and cases exhibiting straight-lining or inconsistent response patterns were removed. After data screening, a ﬁnal sample of 350 valid responses was retained. Descriptive statistics (means, standard deviations, skewness, and kurtosis) were examined for all latent constructs and indicated no severe distributional issues, supporting the suitability of PLS-SEM. 3.3 Demographic proﬁle of respondents The demographic distribution of participants is presented in Table 1. The sample represents diverse academic disciplines, gender groups, and levels of digital literacy, providing a FIGURE 1 Conceptual framework. Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",143
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,6,"balanced dataset for testing the inclusivity and accessibility dimensions of ChatGPT usage. 3.4 Instrument development The measurement instrument consisted of seven latent constructs derived from validated scales and adapted to the context of ChatGPT-based learning. All items were measured using a ﬁve-point Likert scale ranging from 1 = Strongly Disagree to 5 = Strongly Agree. Table 2 summarizes the constructs measurement sources and sample items. The complete measurement instrument, including the exact wording of all items for each construct, is provided in Supplementary Appendix A. This appendix presents all questionnaire items used in the study and is included to ensure transparency and facilitate replication. Inclusivity Perception (IP) was operationalized to capture students’ perceptions of how effectively ChatGPT supports diverse learner needs within an inclusive e-learning environment. Speciﬁcally, the construct reﬂects multiple dimensions of inclusivity, including linguistic accessibility, adaptability to different learning abilities, equitable access to learning support, and respectful interaction across diverse cultural and educational backgrounds. This operationalization ensures that inclusivity perception functions as a contextual moderator, explaining how inclusive design conditions strengthen the relationship between ChatGPT adoption factors and",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,6,"learning abilities, equitable access to learning support, and respectful interaction across diverse cultural and educational backgrounds. This operationalization ensures that inclusivity perception functions as a contextual moderator, explaining how inclusive design conditions strengthen the relationship between ChatGPT adoption factors and student empowerment. The Sustainable Learning Outcomes (SLO) construct was conceptually grounded in UNESCO’s sustainable education framework. As UNESCO publications do not provide standardized psychometric scales, the SLO items were adapted to reﬂect sustainability-oriented learning outcomes in the higher education context. The items were reviewed for TABLE 2 Measurement constructs and sample items. Construct No. of items Source(s) Sample item Perceived Usefulness (PU) 4 Davis (1989); Venkatesh and Bala (2008) “Using ChatGPT improves my learning performance.” Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU) 4 Davis (1989); Teo (2009) “Learning to interact with ChatGPT is easy for me.” Accessibility Support (AS) 4 Al-Azawei et al. (2016); UNESCO (2018) “ChatGPT provides access to information regardless of my learning barriers.” Student Empowerment (SE) 4 Zimmerman (2002); Deci and Ryan (2017) “ChatGPT enables me to take control of my own learning.” Student Satisfaction (SS) 4 Bhattacherjee (2001);",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,6,"al. (2016); UNESCO (2018) “ChatGPT provides access to information regardless of my learning barriers.” Student Empowerment (SE) 4 Zimmerman (2002); Deci and Ryan (2017) “ChatGPT enables me to take control of my own learning.” Student Satisfaction (SS) 4 Bhattacherjee (2001); Al-Fraihat et al. (2020) “Overall, I am satisﬁed with my learning experience using ChatGPT.” Continuance Intention (CI) 4 Bhattacherjee (2001); Chiu and Wang (2008) “I intend to continue using ChatGPT for future learning.” Sustainable Learning Outcomes (SLO) 4 UNESCO (2017); Teo (2009) “ChatGPT helps me develop skills for lifelong learning.” Inclusivity Perception (IP) 4 Burgstahler (2015); UNESCO (2020) “ChatGPT supports learners from diverse backgrounds and abilities.” TABLE 1 Demographic characteristics of respondents (n = 350). Variable Category Frequency Percentage (%) Gender Male 178 50.9 Female 172 49.1 Age Group 18–21 years 124 35.4 22–25 years 158 45.1 26–30 years 52 14.9 Above 30 years 16 4.6 Education Level Undergraduate 228 65.1 Graduate (Master’s) 102 29.1 Doctoral 20 5.8 Field of Study Business and Management 114 32.6 Engineering and IT 98 28.0 Social Sciences 76 21.7 Arts and Humanities 62 17.7",180
