A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,7,"clarity and relevance prior to data collection. The full wording of all SLO items is provided in Supplementary Appendix A. Measurement model results indicate satisfactory reliability and validity for the adapted scale. 3.5 Data analysis technique Data were analyzed using SmartPLS 4.0 following a two-stage analytical procedure recommended by Hair et al. (2022). Measurement model evaluation involved assessing reliability through Cronbach’s alpha, composite reliability (CR), and average variance extracted (AVE). Discriminant validity was examined using the Fornell–Larcker criterion and the heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) (Fornell and Larcker, 1981; Henseler et al., 2015). Structural model evaluation involved testing hypotheses using bootstrapping (5,000 resamples) to determine path signiﬁcance. Effect size (f2), predictive relevance (Q2), and explained variance (R2) were computed. Mediation and moderation effects were analyzed via speciﬁc indirect effects and interaction-term modeling (product indicator approach). This analytical procedure provides both explanatory and predictive validation, ensuring robustness of the proposed model linking ChatGPT adoption, empowerment, satisfaction, inclusivity, and sustainable learning outcomes. 3.6 Sample size and statistical power To strengthen the sample size justiﬁcation beyond the 10-times rule, an a priori power analysis was",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,7,"predictive validation, ensuring robustness of the proposed model linking ChatGPT adoption, empowerment, satisfaction, inclusivity, and sustainable learning outcomes. 3.6 Sample size and statistical power To strengthen the sample size justiﬁcation beyond the 10-times rule, an a priori power analysis was conducted using G*Power for linear multiple regression (ﬁxed model, R2 deviation from zero). A small effect size (f2 = 0.02; approximately β ≈ 0.10), an alpha level of 0.05, a target power of 0.80, and the maximum number of predictors entering any endogenous construct in the model (k = 7, including interaction terms) were speciﬁed. The analysis indicated a minimum required sample size of approximately 300 observations (Cohen, 1988). Given that the present study employed a sample of 350 respondents, the model is sufﬁciently powered to detect small effects, including interaction effects. The results of the power analysis are summarized in Table 3. 4 Results 4.1 Measurement model evaluation The measurement model was assessed for internal consistency reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity using SmartPLS 4.0. As shown in Table 4, all Cronbach’s alpha (α) and Composite Reliability (CR) values",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,7,"in Table 3. 4 Results 4.1 Measurement model evaluation The measurement model was assessed for internal consistency reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity using SmartPLS 4.0. As shown in Table 4, all Cronbach’s alpha (α) and Composite Reliability (CR) values exceeded the minimum threshold of 0.70, conﬁrming internal consistency (Hair et al., 2022). The Average Variance Extracted (AVE) values were above 0.50, indicating sufﬁcient convergent validity (Hair et al., 2022). Detailed item loadings and cross-loadings are reported in Supplementary Table S1. All indicators loaded highest on their respective constructs, with values exceeding 0.70, supporting indicator reliability and discriminant validity. Standardized indicator loadings together with their standard errors, 95% bootstrapped conﬁdence intervals, and exact p-values are reported in Supplementary Table S2. 4.2 Discriminant validity Discriminant validity was assessed using three complementary approaches to enhance clarity and replicability: (a) inter-construct correlations, (b) the Fornell–Larcker criterion with the square root of AVE on the diagonal (Fornell and Larcker, 1981), and (c) the heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) with bootstrapped conﬁdence intervals (HTMT inference) (Henseler et al., 2015). Discriminant validity was conﬁrmed as the square root",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,7,"the Fornell–Larcker criterion with the square root of AVE on the diagonal (Fornell and Larcker, 1981), and (c) the heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) with bootstrapped conﬁdence intervals (HTMT inference) (Henseler et al., 2015). Discriminant validity was conﬁrmed as the square root of AVE for each construct exceeded its correlations with other constructs, all HTMT values were below the recommended threshold, and none of the HTMT conﬁdence intervals included 1.00. Detailed results are presented in Tables 5–7. Discriminant validity was further conﬁrmed using the HTMT criterion with bootstrapped conﬁdence intervals, which are reported in Supplementary Table S3. As a robustness check to further conﬁrm the conceptual distinction between Accessibility Support (AS) and Inclusivity Perception (IP), an alternative structural model was estimated by specifying a direct path from IP to student empowerment. The signiﬁcance and direction of all core structural relationships remained unchanged, indicating that the study’s ﬁndings are stable across alternative model speciﬁcations. TABLE 3 A priori power analysis results (G*power). Parameter Value Statistical test Linear multiple regression: Fixed model (R2 deviation from zero) Effect size f2 = 0.02 (small; ≈ β",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,7,"indicating that the study’s ﬁndings are stable across alternative model speciﬁcations. TABLE 3 A priori power analysis results (G*power). Parameter Value Statistical test Linear multiple regression: Fixed model (R2 deviation from zero) Effect size f2 = 0.02 (small; ≈ β ≈ 0.10) α level 0.05 Target power (1−β) 0.80 Number of predictors (k) 7 (including interaction terms) Required sample size ≈ 300 Actual sample size 350 TABLE 4 Reliability and convergent validity results. Construct Cronbach’s α CR AVE Perceived Usefulness (PU) 0.873 0.910 0.717 Perceived Ease of Use (PEOU) 0.861 0.898 0.689 Accessibility Support (AS) 0.844 0.888 0.666 Student Empowerment (SE) 0.886 0.920 0.742 Student Satisfaction (SS) 0.882 0.917 0.735 Continuance Intention (CI) 0.870 0.906 0.708 Sustainable Learning Outcomes (SLO) 0.883 0.920 0.742 Inclusivity Perception (IP) 0.860 0.902 0.699 All items had standardized loadings greater than 0.70 and were statistically signiﬁcant (p < 0.001), conﬁrming indicator reliability. Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",154
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,8,"4.3 Structural model evaluation The structural model was analyzed to test the proposed direct, mediating, and moderating relationships. Collinearity diagnostics indicated that all variance inﬂation factor (VIF) values were below 3.0, suggesting no multicollinearity issues among the predictor constructs (Hair et al., 2022). In addition, common method variance (CMV) was assessed using the full collinearity variance inﬂation factor (VIF) approach proposed by Kock (2015). All full collinearity VIF values were below the conservative threshold of 3.3, indicating that CMV is unlikely to bias the estimated relationships. The detailed full collinearity VIF results are reported in Supplementary Table S4. The model demonstrated strong explanatory power. The coefﬁcients of determination (R2) were 0.63 for student empowerment (SE), 0.59 for student satisfaction (SS), 0.57 for continuance intention (CI), and 0.64 for sustainable learning outcomes (SLO). These values exceed the moderate-to- substantial thresholds suggested by Cohen (1988), conﬁrming the model’s strong predictive capability. TABLE 7 HTMT discriminant validity matrix. Construct PU PEOU AS SE SS CI SLO IP PU — PEOU 0.72 — AS 0.62 0.60 — SE 0.69 0.67 0.65 — SS 0.64",180
