A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,10,"corresponding direct effects were also signiﬁcant in the structural model (Table 8), these ﬁndings indicate partial mediation for both H7 and H8. Next, the mediating role of student satisfaction (SS) between student empowerment (SE) and continuance intention (CI) was examined (H9). As reported in Table 10, the indirect effect (SE → SS → CI) was statistically signiﬁcant, whereas the direct effect (SE → CI) was non-signiﬁcant. This pattern indicates full mediation, suggesting that the inﬂuence of student empowerment on continuance intention operates entirely through student satisfaction. Sequential mediation was further tested for the pathway SE → SS → CI → SLO (H10). As shown in Table 11, the indirect effect through student satisfaction and continuance intention was statistically signiﬁcant, while the direct effect of student empowerment on sustainable learning outcomes was non- signiﬁcant. These ﬁndings support a full sequential mediation pattern. In all mediation analyses, the sum of the direct and indirect effects equaled the total effect, satisfying standard mediation consistency criteria. 4.5 Moderation analysis The moderating role of inclusivity perception (IP) was examined using the product-indicator approach within the",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,10,"pattern. In all mediation analyses, the sum of the direct and indirect effects equaled the total effect, satisfying standard mediation consistency criteria. 4.5 Moderation analysis The moderating role of inclusivity perception (IP) was examined using the product-indicator approach within the PLS-SEM framework. Interaction terms were created to test whether inclusivity perception moderates the relationships between perceived usefulness (PU), perceived ease of use (PEOU), accessibility support (AS), and student empowerment (SE). All interaction effects were positive and statistically signiﬁcant, providing support for hypotheses H11a–H11c. To facilitate interpretation, conditional effects were examined at low (−1 SD), mean, and high (+1 SD) levels of inclusivity perception. These results indicate that the positive effect of perceived usefulness on student empowerment strengthens as inclusivity perception increases. All indicators were mean-centered prior to product-indicator creation. The results are further detailed in Tables 12–15. TABLE 12 Moderation effects of inclusivity perception. Hypothesis Interaction path β 95% CI (LL) 95% CI (UL) p-value H11a PU × IP → SE 0.114 0.04 0.19 0.003 H11b PEOU × IP → SE 0.095 0.02 0.17 0.008 H11c AS × IP →",180
