A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"4.6 Model predictive relevance and effect sizes Predictive relevance (Q2) was assessed using the blindfolding procedure. All endogenous constructs exhibited Q2 values exceeding 0.35, indicating strong predictive relevance of the model (Hair et al., 2022). Effect size (f2) analysis revealed moderate to large effects for the majority of structural paths, with values ranging from 0.15 to 0.41. Table 16 summarizing the results of effect size and predictive relevance. 4.7 Summary of hypothesis testing Out of the 11 hypothesized relationships, all were statistically supported based on the reported direct, mediating, and moderating effects. Overall, the ﬁndings demonstrate that ChatGPT adoption enhances student empowerment, which subsequently improves satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes. Moreover, inclusivity perception ampliﬁes these relationships, underscoring the importance of accessibility and inclusive design in AI-supported educational environments. A visual summary of the validated direct, mediating, and moderating relationships is provided in Figure 2. 5 Discussion and implications 5.1 Discussion of ﬁndings The empirical ﬁndings conﬁrm that the proposed model integrating the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self- Determination Theory (SDT), and the Sustainable Education Framework provides a strong",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"relationships is provided in Figure 2. 5 Discussion and implications 5.1 Discussion of ﬁndings The empirical ﬁndings conﬁrm that the proposed model integrating the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self- Determination Theory (SDT), and the Sustainable Education Framework provides a strong explanation of how ChatGPT supports inclusive and sustainable e-learning. The signiﬁcant paths from perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and accessibility support to student empowerment (H1–H3) corroborate prior studies emphasizing that technology usability and accessibility drive positive learning attitudes (Davis, 1989; UNESCO, 2018; Venkatesh and Davis, 2000). These results extend Davis’s TAM by demonstrating that technological beliefs translate into empowerment, a higher-order psychological state representing conﬁdence and self-directed learning (Deci and Ryan, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). ChatGPT’s conversational adaptability and real-time assistance appear to enhance learners’ control and perceived competence, validating its potential as an intelligent tutoring companion for higher education. The strong positive relationship between student empowerment and satisfaction (H4) supports Self-Determination Theory, which identiﬁes autonomy and competence as core antecedents of intrinsic motivation (Deci and Ryan, 2017). Empowered learners who can independently query, verify, and co-create knowledge with ChatGPT",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"The strong positive relationship between student empowerment and satisfaction (H4) supports Self-Determination Theory, which identiﬁes autonomy and competence as core antecedents of intrinsic motivation (Deci and Ryan, 2017). Empowered learners who can independently query, verify, and co-create knowledge with ChatGPT report greater satisfaction with the learning experience. This ﬁnding aligns with prior e-learning research identifying empowerment as a critical precursor to sustained motivation and learning enjoyment (Al-Fraihat et al., 2020; Bhattacherjee, 2001). Furthermore, the signiﬁcant relationships between student satisfaction and continuance intention (H5), and between continuance intention and sustainable learning outcomes (H6), are consistent with expectation–conﬁrmation theory and prior empirical evidence demonstrating that satisfaction predicts persistent technology use and long-term learning beneﬁts (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Chiu and Wang, 2008; Teo, 2009). In this study, continuance intention serves as a behavioral conduit linking short-term satisfaction with enduring competencies, including digital literacy and lifelong learning readiness, which are central objectives of Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG-4) (UNESCO, 2017). The mediation analysis (H7–H10) further conﬁrms that empowerment and satisfaction jointly mediate the impact of ChatGPT adoption factors on sustainable learning outcomes. This dual-stage mediation",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"lifelong learning readiness, which are central objectives of Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG-4) (UNESCO, 2017). The mediation analysis (H7–H10) further conﬁrms that empowerment and satisfaction jointly mediate the impact of ChatGPT adoption factors on sustainable learning outcomes. This dual-stage mediation provides empirical support for the argument that technological perceptions yield educational beneﬁts only when they are internalized through psychological engagement and intrinsic motivation. Consequently, the ﬁndings address theoretical limitations of traditional TAM-based studies by demonstrating how technology acceptance evolves into sustainable educational transformation (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Deci and Ryan, 2017). Finally, the moderating role of inclusivity perception (H11a– H11c) offers novel insights into the boundary conditions under which AI-supported learning is most effective. Although accessibility support and inclusivity perception are conceptually related, they capture distinct dimensions of the learning environment. Accessibility support reﬂects the availability of system-level features that facilitate access and usability, whereas inclusivity perception represents learners’ subjective sense of equitable treatment, belonging, and fairness when interacting with ChatGPT. The empirical distinction between these constructs was supported by discriminant validity results, conﬁrming their complementary but non-overlapping roles. When students perceived",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"and usability, whereas inclusivity perception represents learners’ subjective sense of equitable treatment, belonging, and fairness when interacting with ChatGPT. The empirical distinction between these constructs was supported by discriminant validity results, conﬁrming their complementary but non-overlapping roles. When students perceived ChatGPT as inclusive—respecting linguistic diversity, offering adaptive feedback, and supporting diverse learning needs—the positive effects of adoption factors on empowerment were signiﬁcantly strengthened (Burgstahler, 2015; UNESCO, 2020). These ﬁndings should also be interpreted in light of emerging evidence highlighting the importance of institutional norms and pedagogical context in AI-enabled learning environments. Prior research suggests that the beneﬁts of generative AI may be contingent upon broader socio-institutional conditions, such as academic integrity culture, peer norms, and ethical awareness (Dwivedi et al., 2024; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). Accordingly, TABLE 16 Effect size and predictive relevance. Construct R2 Q2 f2 (Key predictor) Student Empowerment (SE) 0.63 0.41 PU (0.21), PEOU (0.16), AS (0.18) Student Satisfaction (SS) 0.59 0.38 SE (0.42) Continuance Intention (CI) 0.57 0.36 SS (0.40) Sustainable Learning Outcomes (SLO) 0.64 0.39 CI (0.43) Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",179
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,11,"Empowerment (SE) 0.63 0.41 PU (0.21), PEOU (0.16), AS (0.18) Student Satisfaction (SS) 0.59 0.38 SE (0.42) Continuance Intention (CI) 0.57 0.36 SS (0.40) Sustainable Learning Outcomes (SLO) 0.64 0.39 CI (0.43) Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",39
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,12,"while inclusivity perception enhances the relationship between ChatGPT adoption and student empowerment in the present study, complementary institutional and policy factors may further condition whether empowerment and satisfaction translate into sustained learning outcomes. 5.2 Theoretical implications This study makes several theoretical contributions: 1. It extends TAM by incorporating empowerment and satisfaction as sequential mediators, thus linking technological acceptance with psychological motivation. 2. It operationalizes SDT within an AI context, demonstrating that generative tools like ChatGPT can fulﬁll autonomy and competence needs, producing sustainable learning behaviors. 3. It integrates inclusivity perception as a novel moderator, advancing accessibility theory by showing how inclusive design conditions magnify the effectiveness of AI- driven learning. 4. It offers one of the earliest empirical validations of a generative-AI-based sustainable education model, enriching literature on AI for Good and SDG-aligned pedagogy. 5.3 Practical implications The results yield important insights for educators, administrators, and policymakers: • For educators, the ﬁndings highlight the need to embed ChatGPT within instructional design to foster learner empowerment, critical thinking, and satisfaction. Guided prompts, feedback loops, and reﬂective exercises can maximize these beneﬁts.",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,12,"important insights for educators, administrators, and policymakers: • For educators, the ﬁndings highlight the need to embed ChatGPT within instructional design to foster learner empowerment, critical thinking, and satisfaction. Guided prompts, feedback loops, and reﬂective exercises can maximize these beneﬁts. For example, instructors can require students to use ChatGPT to generate initial explanations or draft responses and then critically evaluate, revise, and justify the ﬁnal answers using academic sources. ChatGPT can also be integrated into formative assessment by providing preliminary feedback on assignments, followed by reﬂective learning logs in which students explain how AI feedback inﬂuenced their understanding. • For institutional leaders, investments in inclusive AI infrastructure, multilingual support, adaptive accessibility interfaces and ethical-use training are essential for equity. • For policymakers, evidence from this study supports integrating generative AI into national sustainability frameworks for higher education, ensuring that digital transformation aligns with SDG-4’s call for inclusive and equitable quality education. • For EdTech developers, the model identiﬁes user-centered design priorities, accessibility, transparency and cultural neutrality that directly inﬂuence learner empowerment and sustainable outcomes. • To maximize the beneﬁts of",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,12,"transformation aligns with SDG-4’s call for inclusive and equitable quality education. • For EdTech developers, the model identiﬁes user-centered design priorities, accessibility, transparency and cultural neutrality that directly inﬂuence learner empowerment and sustainable outcomes. • To maximize the beneﬁts of inclusive AI-supported learning, institutions should complement AI accessibility with light-touch integrity and policy scaffolds, such as brief disclosure prompts regarding AI use, responsible-use statements aligned with institutional policy, and hybrid feedback workﬂows in which AI provides ﬁrst-pass support while instructors deliver calibration, exemplars, and higher-order critique. 5.4 Risks and limitations of generative AI in education While the ﬁndings highlight the positive role of ChatGPT in enhancing student empowerment, satisfaction, and sustainable learning outcomes, it is essential to acknowledge potential risks and limitations associated with the use of generative AI in educational contexts. First, generative AI systems may generate inaccurate or misleading information due to hallucination effects, which can negatively affect learning quality if responses are accepted without veriﬁcation. Educators should therefore encourage learners to critically evaluate AI-generated content and validate it using reliable academic sources. Second, over- reliance on AI",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,12,"misleading information due to hallucination effects, which can negatively affect learning quality if responses are accepted without veriﬁcation. Educators should therefore encourage learners to critically evaluate AI-generated content and validate it using reliable academic sources. Second, over- reliance on AI tools may raise concerns related to academic integrity and reduced cognitive engagement. Excessive dependence on AI-generated responses could limit opportunities for independent thinking and deep learning. To mitigate this risk, instructors should design assessments that emphasize reﬂection, justiﬁcation, and process-based learning. Third, the use of AI-driven educational tools introduces privacy and ethical considerations. Students may unintentionally disclose sensitive information while interacting with AI systems. Institutions should establish clear guidelines on responsible AI use and raise awareness about ethical boundaries and data protection. Finally, algorithmic bias presents a critical limitation, particularly in relation to inclusivity. AI systems are trained on large-scale datasets that may reﬂect existing social, linguistic, or cultural biases, potentially disadvantaging certain learner groups. Continuous monitoring, bias-aware implementation, and inclusive instructional design are therefore necessary to ensure equitable learning experiences. 5.5 Achievement of the study’s signiﬁcance and uniqueness The",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,12,"datasets that may reﬂect existing social, linguistic, or cultural biases, potentially disadvantaging certain learner groups. Continuous monitoring, bias-aware implementation, and inclusive instructional design are therefore necessary to ensure equitable learning experiences. 5.5 Achievement of the study’s signiﬁcance and uniqueness The original purpose of this research was to demonstrate how ChatGPT could function as a transformative agent for inclusive and sustainable e-learning. The empirical results conﬁrm all theoretical expectations, thereby achieving the desired signiﬁcance of the study in multiple dimensions: 1. Bridging the Gap: The study successfully ﬁlled the identiﬁed research gap by empirically connecting generative AI adoption with inclusivity and sustainability —dimensions rarely examined together in prior work (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Chiu and Wang, 2008; Dwivedi et al., 2024; Susnjak and McIntosh, 2024). 2. Model Integration: By merging TAM, SDT, and sustainability theory, the model advances understanding from technology acceptance to educational transformation. Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",149
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,13,"3. Empirical Validation: All mediation and moderation hypotheses were supported, conﬁrming that empowerment and satisfaction are key psychological mechanisms translating ChatGPT adoption into sustainable outcomes, and that inclusivity enhances these effects. 4. Policy and Global Relevance: Findings substantiate that generative AI can be harnessed to promote UN SDG-4, ensuring inclusive, equitable and lifelong learning opportunities for all, thereby fulﬁlling the sustainability and accessibility objectives outlined in the Special Issue call. 5.6 Conclusion of discussion In sum, this research empirically demonstrates that ChatGPT adoption enhances sustainable education outcomes through empowerment and satisfaction, moderated by inclusivity perception. The study not only validates theoretical linkages across technology acceptance, motivation, and sustainability but also provides actionable pathways for designing equitable AI- driven learning ecosystems. These ﬁndings conﬁrm that the signiﬁcance and uniqueness envisioned at the outset—creating a holistic, inclusive, and sustainable e-learning model—have been fully realized. 6 Conclusion, limitations, and future research directions 6.1 Conclusion This study set out to examine how ChatGPT, as a generative AI tool, can be leveraged to achieve inclusive and sustainable e-learning by integrating insights from the Technology Acceptance",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,13,"fully realized. 6 Conclusion, limitations, and future research directions 6.1 Conclusion This study set out to examine how ChatGPT, as a generative AI tool, can be leveraged to achieve inclusive and sustainable e-learning by integrating insights from the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Self-Determination Theory (SDT), and the Sustainable Education Framework. The empirical results derived from 350 higher-education respondents conﬁrm that perceived usefulness, ease of use, and accessibility signiﬁcantly enhance student empowerment, which in turn fosters satisfaction, continuance intention, and sustainable learning outcomes. Furthermore, the study validates the dual-stage mediating mechanisms of empowerment and satisfaction, revealing that technological perceptions translate into sustainable educational outcomes only when learners experience psychological empowerment and intrinsic satisfaction. The moderating effect of inclusivity perception underscores that the beneﬁts of ChatGPT adoption are strongest when students perceive the environment as accessible and equitable for all. Collectively, these ﬁndings demonstrate that ChatGPT serves as a transformative learning technology—one that not only facilitates knowledge acquisition but also promotes equity, inclusion, and long-term sustainability in digital education. By empirically linking AI adoption to SDG-4 (Quality Education), the study establishes a",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,13,"ﬁndings demonstrate that ChatGPT serves as a transformative learning technology—one that not only facilitates knowledge acquisition but also promotes equity, inclusion, and long-term sustainability in digital education. By empirically linking AI adoption to SDG-4 (Quality Education), the study establishes a new paradigm in which generative AI functions as an enabler of social and educational sustainability. 6.2 Limitations Although the study achieved its desired signiﬁcance, several limitations merit acknowledgement. First, the data were collected through self-reported cross- sectional surveys, which may be susceptible to common-method bias and restrict causal inference. Longitudinal or experimental designs could better capture behavioral change over time. In addition, the reliance on self-reported survey data may have introduced social desirability bias, as respondents could have provided more favorable evaluations of ChatGPT use and learning outcomes in line with socially acceptable or technology- positive attitudes. Although anonymity was ensured to reduce this effect, future research may incorporate behavioral usage data, objective performance measures, or mixed-method approaches to further mitigate potential response bias. Second, the sample was limited to South Korean universities, a context characterized by advanced digital infrastructure",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,13,"ensured to reduce this effect, future research may incorporate behavioral usage data, objective performance measures, or mixed-method approaches to further mitigate potential response bias. Second, the sample was limited to South Korean universities, a context characterized by advanced digital infrastructure and high AI literacy; therefore, generalization to developing or resource- constrained regions should be made cautiously. Third, while the model included inclusivity as a perceptual moderator, other contextual variables, such as digital literacy, ethical awareness or institutional support may further explain variation in empowerment and satisfaction. Finally, the study focused on ChatGPT; future comparisons across generative AI platforms (e.g., Gemini, Claude) could validate the robustness of the proposed framework. Additionally, institutional policy environments and academic integrity norms vary across national contexts; therefore, the relationships observed in South Korea may differ in settings with stricter regulatory approaches to generative AI use, potentially inﬂuencing continuance intention and sustainable learning outcomes. 6.3 Future research directions Future studies should pursue the following directions to extend the theoretical and practical contributions: 1. Cross-Cultural Validation. Conduct multi-country analyses to assess how cultural dimensions inﬂuence inclusivity perception",180
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,13,"potentially inﬂuencing continuance intention and sustainable learning outcomes. 6.3 Future research directions Future studies should pursue the following directions to extend the theoretical and practical contributions: 1. Cross-Cultural Validation. Conduct multi-country analyses to assess how cultural dimensions inﬂuence inclusivity perception and sustainability outcomes in AI-supported learning. 2. Longitudinal and Experimental Designs. Examine behavioral evolution and learning retention over extended periods to establish stronger causal links. 3. Diverse Learner Groups. Include participants with disabilities, linguistic minorities, and non-traditional learners to deepen understanding of accessibility and equity effects. 4. Integration of Additional Variables. Future frameworks could incorporate constructs such as trust in AI, ethical AI awareness, and digital well-being to enrich the sustainability perspective. 5. Comparative Technology Studies. Evaluate how various AI- driven tools differ in promoting empowerment and sustainable learning, aiding policymakers and institutions in technology selection. Rehman 10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",143
A108,A108.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1734621,14,"6. Future research could experimentally examine how feedback modality (teacher, AI, or hybrid) interacts with inclusivity scaffolds and socio-institutional factors (e.g., peer norms, ethical awareness, and policy knowledge) to shape empowerment, satisfaction, and sustainable learning outcomes through moderated mediation designs. 6.4 Final remark The study successfully achieved its intended signiﬁcance by ﬁlling the empirical and theoretical gaps between AI adoption, inclusivity, and sustainability. It offers a validated framework through which universities can integrate ChatGPT not only as a cognitive aid but as a strategic instrument for equitable and lifelong learning. By aligning technological innovation with sustainable educational values, this research contributes meaningfully to the global discourse on how generative AI can shape the future of accessible, inclusive, and quality education for all. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement Ethical approval was not required for the studies involving humans because this study involved anonymous survey data collected from university students. Formal ethical review and approval were not obtained prior to data collection because",180
