A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6 Abstract OpenAI’s generative pre-trained transformer (ChatGPT) is rapidly transforming fields such as education and becoming an integral part of our daily lives. However, its rise has sparked intense privacy debates, raising concerns about the storage of personal information and attracting global regulatory scrutiny. ChatGPT is gain­ ing popularity among undergraduate students owing to its personalized learning capabilities, leading many universities to provide guidelines and seminars on its use. Curiosity about the reasons for ChatGPT’s popularity inspired our research. Through a survey of 695 undergraduate students in South Korea, we found that “privacy fatigue”—a feeling of hopelessness and weariness regarding privacy— was associated with these students’ adoption of ChatGPT. The findings revealed that this fatigue reduces perceived privacy risks, enhances their expectations of the platform, and boosts their intention to use ChatGPT. By incorporating the con­ cepts of privacy fatigue, perceived risk, and elements from the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology, we developed a novel model to understand this phenomenon. Interestingly, despite the potential risks, the intention to use ChatGPT was",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,1,"the con­ cepts of privacy fatigue, perceived risk, and elements from the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology, we developed a novel model to understand this phenomenon. Interestingly, despite the potential risks, the intention to use ChatGPT was not significantly influenced by perceived risk. This study contributes to a bet­ ter understanding of undergraduate students’ privacy perceptions when leveraging ChatGPT in education. Keywords ChatGPT · Privacy fatigue · Educational technology · Perceived risk · Unified theory of acceptance and use of technology · South Korea Received: 9 September 2024 / Accepted: 25 November 2024 / Published online: 10 January 2025 © The Author(s) 2024 Privacy fatigue and its effects on ChatGPT acceptance among undergraduate students: is privacy dead? Jiwon Chung1 · Hun-yeong Kwon1 Hun-yeong Kwon khy0@korea.ac.kr 1 School of Cybersecurity, Korea University, Seoul 02841, Republic of Korea 1 3",141
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 1 Introduction Currently, OpenAI’s generative pre-trained transformer (ChatGPT) commands wide­ spread acclaim in various fields. It plays a significant role in cybersecurity by dis­ cerning and mitigating disinformation campaigns, identifying vulnerabilities, and formulating cybersecurity solutions (Al-Hawawreh et al., 2023). Choi et al. (2023) anticipated its potential usefulness for law school students during examinations, sug­ gesting that its performance could potentially meet the standards for earning a Juris Doctor (JD) from a selective law school, bypassing other skill-based requirements. Li et al. (2023) demonstrated its potential for colorectal surgery, showing that it can provide tailored and precise medical information, help reduce errors and complica­ tions, and ultimately help improve patient outcomes. Thus, the versatility and broad applicability of ChatGPT have garnered positive recognition in various fields. However, ChatGPT is also under scrutiny due to concerns about its potential threat to privacy. Its operation involves users inputting content after a prompt, to which ChatGPT responds, raising apprehensions about privacy infringements. According to the policy of OpenAI, data from user interactions are shared with third parties (Al-",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,2,"about its potential threat to privacy. Its operation involves users inputting content after a prompt, to which ChatGPT responds, raising apprehensions about privacy infringements. According to the policy of OpenAI, data from user interactions are shared with third parties (Al- Hawawreh et al., 2023). Moreover, ChatGPT accumulates extensive PII from users through various channels and retains all conversational data, including message con­ tent and device details (Al-Hawawreh et al., 2023). The privacy policy of ChatGPT, over 2,000 words long, states that the sharing and accumulation of PII may occur without additional user notifications, except when mandated by legislation (Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada, 2023). Moreover, these privacy concerns are exacerbated by fears about “do anything now” (DAN) attacks and research pointing to backdoor attacks during parameter tuning using ChatGPT (Wang et al., 2023). Italy suspended ChatGPT for approximately four weeks, later reinstating it after implementing verification procedures to confirm the users’ age and obtain consent from a parent or legal guardian if the users are under 13 years old (Schubert, 2023). Furthermore, the United States, Poland, and Canada",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,2,"four weeks, later reinstating it after implementing verification procedures to confirm the users’ age and obtain consent from a parent or legal guardian if the users are under 13 years old (Schubert, 2023). Furthermore, the United States, Poland, and Canada have launched investigations into OpenAI owing to concerns regarding potential privacy breaches with ChatGPT (Charlish, 2023; Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada, 2023; Zakrzewski, 2023). Privacy concerns regarding ChatGPT differ significantly from those associated with Google and Meta, which explicitly use PII for commercial purposes such as targeted advertising and profile enhancements (Fortra, 2023). For example, Google may use data from user searches to customize advertisements based on their inter­ ests, thereby refining user profiles over time. By contrast, ChatGPT mainly operates with short-term memory (Afifi-Sabet, 2024) and does not inherently require PII to function. However, temporary use of PII within sessions can occur, such as when a user inadvertently shares sensitive information during a chat. Additionally, a user may provide their email address to an integrated third-party service to receive a tran­ script of the conversation; however, this",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,2,"PII within sessions can occur, such as when a user inadvertently shares sensitive information during a chat. Additionally, a user may provide their email address to an integrated third-party service to receive a tran­ script of the conversation; however, this is optional and is not required for Chat­ GPT to function. Although ChatGPT functions efficiently without requiring personal data, these instances underscore the nuanced nature of its data-processing practices. Despite OpenAI’s policies emphasizing anonymization and legal compliance, there remains a subtle risk of exposure that can be challenging to fully mitigate, including 1 3 12322",95
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 the theoretical risk of data interception during transmission. The real issue with Chat­ GPT is that users may inadvertently share sensitive information during interactions, even without logging in or providing PII. Research (Sebastian, 2023b) has shown that users often unknowingly share personal or sensitive information when interact­ ing with chatbots. Although concerns regarding ChatGPT are evident, one notable demographic that seems to favor its use is undergraduate students (Cotton et al., 2023; Farrokhnia et al., 2023). With its ability to create personalized learning experiences and promote advanced learning outcomes through adaptive learning, ChatGPT has emerged as a potent tool for designing effective educational tasks (Farrokhnia et al., 2023). This facilitates experiential learning, which allows students to assess diverse strategies through game-based learning or student-centered approaches (Cotton et al., 2023). Owing to these advantages, various colleges and universities have taken measures to provide undergraduate students with usage guidelines for ChatGPT and have even established related special lectures (Baik, 2023; Ha, 2023; Lee, 2023). This attests to the fact that undergraduate students are among the groups",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,3,"colleges and universities have taken measures to provide undergraduate students with usage guidelines for ChatGPT and have even established related special lectures (Baik, 2023; Ha, 2023; Lee, 2023). This attests to the fact that undergraduate students are among the groups that frequently use ChatGPT. However, despite its popularity in the educational sector, ChatGPT’s exceptional performance may not be the sole reason for its widespread adoption, and concerns about privacy issues remain. Nonetheless, related information on the under­ lying reasons is limited—a topic that served as the starting point for our research. We analyzed the annual privacy protection reports released by the Personal Infor­ mation Protection Commission (2020, 2021, 2022); the survey data indicated that a high percentage of individuals demonstrated a passive attitude and took no action for redress following personal information leaks. This situation, characterized by a sense of passivity, leads to privacy fatigue, which refers to feelings of resignation and exhaustion regarding privacy issues (Choi et al., 2018). A certain degree of privacy fatigue may prevail among people, and undergraduate students are no exception to this. In this",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,3,"passivity, leads to privacy fatigue, which refers to feelings of resignation and exhaustion regarding privacy issues (Choi et al., 2018). A certain degree of privacy fatigue may prevail among people, and undergraduate students are no exception to this. In this study, we focused on whether privacy fatigue is prevalent among under­ graduate students. To the best of our knowledge, limited research has been conducted on privacy fatigue among undergraduate students who frequently use ChatGPT. Given the increasing concerns regarding privacy and potential risks associated with ChatGPT, understanding the perceived risks related to privacy is crucial. However, a noticeable gap exists in the literature regarding the relationship between this perceived risk and privacy fatigue among undergraduate students using ChatGPT as well as the influ­ ence of these factors on its use. Ultimately, we aimed to determine whether privacy fatigue influences undergraduate students’ use of ChatGPT and explore the relation­ ship between privacy fatigue and perceived risk. The contributions of this study can be summarized as follows. First, we introduce a novel approach that integrates privacy fatigue, perceived risk, and the unified",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,3,"students’ use of ChatGPT and explore the relation­ ship between privacy fatigue and perceived risk. The contributions of this study can be summarized as follows. First, we introduce a novel approach that integrates privacy fatigue, perceived risk, and the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT). Using this approach, we analyzed the relationship between specific UTAUT elements, privacy fatigue, and perceived risk. Second, our study identified the impact of privacy fatigue on the use of Chat­ GPT among undergraduate students. We propose that students experiencing privacy fatigue may be more susceptible to privacy risks, as evidenced by their increased willingness to interact with ChatGPT. This suggests a cycle where students more 1 3 12323",116
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 accepting of privacy concerns might rely more on ChatGPT, potentially increasing privacy risks. Third, our study revealed that the perceived risk associated with ChatGPT does not affect the intention to use it, contrary to our initial assumptions. This implies that students’ focus on their performance with ChatGPT outweighs any concerns about potential risks. 2 Background 2.1 Popularity of ChatGPT in South Korea’s educational sector Currently, a ChatGPT craze is sweeping through South Korean universities. Educa­ tion technology companies in the United States, such as Duolingo and Quizlet, which are popular among American high school students, have already integrated ChatGPT into their applications (Yoo, 2023). Similarly, in South Korea, there has been a move­ ment toward leveraging ChatGPT in education. At Korea University, guidelines for utilizing ChatGPT have been established to foster education that cultivates student proficiency, encouraging its use in active learning (Ha, 2023). Additionally, special lectures explaining the operating principles of and utilization strategies for ChatGPT have been held at Chung-Ang University, providing guidance on its use. Further­ more, with the introduction of",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,4,"student proficiency, encouraging its use in active learning (Ha, 2023). Additionally, special lectures explaining the operating principles of and utilization strategies for ChatGPT have been held at Chung-Ang University, providing guidance on its use. Further­ more, with the introduction of courses on research ethics and chatbots, the educa­ tional sector is witnessing a shift (Lee, 2023). Thus, in the South Korean educational context, there appears to be a prevalent voice advocating the effective utilization of ChatGPT (Baik, 2023). Therefore, to better understand the phenomenon, research on perceptions regarding ChatGPT in the educational sector would be valuable. 2.2 Digital media familiarity among South Korean students The global trend of digitalization permeates various aspects of people’s daily lives across nations, including South Korea. Known for its rapid technological advance­ ments, South Korea has swiftly adopted digital technologies in the field of education (Lee et al., 2009). This makes South Korea an interesting setting for technological awareness surveys. Given South Korea’s high student population and educational standards (Seth, 2002), investments in e-learning are regarded as highly cost-effec­ tive (Lee et al., 2009). The",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,4,"(Lee et al., 2009). This makes South Korea an interesting setting for technological awareness surveys. Given South Korea’s high student population and educational standards (Seth, 2002), investments in e-learning are regarded as highly cost-effec­ tive (Lee et al., 2009). The educational system in South Korea actively promotes online lecture learning and the use of digital media; South Korean students are thus becoming closely acquainted with digital technologies, making them ideal candidates for studies exploring perceptions regarding ChatGPT in the educational context. 1 3 12324",84
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 3 Conceptual framework and hypotheses 3.1 Conceptual framework The UTAUT is a comprehensive cause-and-effect model developed to explain and predict the acceptance and utilization of technology across various sectors. Many empirical studies have confirmed its validity and reliability (Foroughi et al., 2023; Khan et al., 2023; Li et al., 2024; Venkatesh et al., 2003). Given the popularity of ChatGPT among undergraduate students, our research sought to explore their use of ChatGPT using UTAUT as the theoretical framework. The UTAUT model comprises the following factors: performance expectancy, a user’s anticipation of the advantages or outcomes that they may attain by using this technology; effort expectancy, a user’s perception of the level of effort required to operate the technology under investigation; social influence, a user’s perceptions of the extent to which their social networks influence their technology usage; facilitat­ ing conditions, resources and support available to users for engaging with technol­ ogy; behavioral intention, a user’s willingness or inclination to adopt or utilize a specific technology; and use behavior, tangible actions of a user when interacting",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,5,"technology usage; facilitat­ ing conditions, resources and support available to users for engaging with technol­ ogy; behavioral intention, a user’s willingness or inclination to adopt or utilize a specific technology; and use behavior, tangible actions of a user when interacting with technology. In the exploration of the intended use of ChatGPT, attempts have been made to integrate traditional models with new elements (Foroughi et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023). However, to date, there has been no comprehensive research taking into account concerns about privacy and analyzing whether the intended use of ChatGPT is altered due to the abandonment or expectations thereof. To understand the impact of privacy fatigue and perceived risk among undergraduate students on their intention to use ChatGPT, we modified the UTAUT to devise a fatigue-risk-UTAUT model (Fig. 1). Based on research by Venkatesh et al. (2003), who suggested that facilitating conditions may not be significant when performance expectation and effort expec­ Fig. 1 Fatigue-risk-UTAUT model 1 3 12325",160
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 tancy are present as indicators, we removed facilitating conditions to focus more on other indicators. In fact, Venkatesh et al. (2003) also noted that facilitating conditions do not directly predict behavioral intention but instead predict actual use behavior, further supporting our decision to exclude this variable. According to the UTAUT (Venkatesh et al., 2003), performance expectancy is the strongest predictor of intention in each model and it remains significant across all measurement points. Additionally, intrinsic motivation may work through effort expectations. In other words, both performance and effort expectations positively affect behavioral intentions. This relationship has been observed in studies that inves­ tigated the acceptance attitude of ChatGPT users using the UTAUT (Duong et al., 2024; Strzelecki, 2023, as well as in research in other fields, such as LMS (Bayaga & Du Plessis, 2024). Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H1. The performance expectations of undergraduate students positively influ­ ence their behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. H2. The effort expectations of undergraduate students positively influence their behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. 3.2 Privacy fatigue",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,6,"Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H1. The performance expectations of undergraduate students positively influ­ ence their behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. H2. The effort expectations of undergraduate students positively influence their behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. 3.2 Privacy fatigue Privacy fatigue is defined as the growing difficulty in managing digital personal data and frequent consumer data breaches, which has left individuals feeling hopeless and fatigued about online privacy. It refers to a negative emotional state in which individuals become weary of making decisions related to their personal privacy and are reluctant to proactively oversee and regulate their personal privacy (Shao et al., 2022). Considering that the concept of “privacy” originated from Judge Thomas Cooley’s notion of “the right to be let alone” (Prosser, 1960), the phenomenon of privacy fatigue feels paradoxical in a personalized modern society. This phenomenon can be explained as follows: As information technology has advanced, data breaches have increased, initially sparking alarms, but soon leading to desensitization (Tang et al., 2021). Conceptually, privacy fatigue involves emotional exhaustion and cynicism, leading to a shift in how individuals",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,6,"be explained as follows: As information technology has advanced, data breaches have increased, initially sparking alarms, but soon leading to desensitization (Tang et al., 2021). Conceptually, privacy fatigue involves emotional exhaustion and cynicism, leading to a shift in how individuals approach information safeguarding. Privacy cynicism, a component of privacy fatigue, reflects a skeptical and detached attitude toward privacy protection, often perceiving it as futile and unattainable (Luzsa & Mayr, 2022). In this context, Lee et al.’s (2024) study revealed that emotional reactions to technology significantly influence user attitudes, surpassing the impact of cognitive factors such as thoughts and beliefs. The findings suggest that individuals use their emotional responses to shape their performance beliefs. Similarly, privacy fatigue, by altering emotional engagement with privacy concerns, may lead to a realloca­ tion of cognitive resources. Individuals experiencing privacy fatigue may redirect their focus from privacy concerns to other areas where they feel they would have more control or potential for positive outcomes, thereby enhancing their performance expectations in those areas. Therefore, it can be inferred that privacy fatigue affects 1 3 12326",179
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,6,"from privacy concerns to other areas where they feel they would have more control or potential for positive outcomes, thereby enhancing their performance expectations in those areas. Therefore, it can be inferred that privacy fatigue affects 1 3 12326",39
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 emotional engagement and has a significant impact on performance expectations by shifting focus and resources to areas perceived as more manageable. Considering the privacy calculus (Dinev & Hart, 2006), which balances the poten­ tial risks of sharing personal information against the perceived benefits, privacy fatigue might shift this balance. Individuals suffering from privacy fatigue might perceive the emotional costs of maintaining strict privacy protection as too high and thus prioritize other aspects of their lives or work. This shift in priorities and the reallocation of cognitive resources can lead to enhanced performance in areas where individuals feel they have more control and potential for positive outcomes, as the perceived futility of privacy pro­ tection lowers the associated emotional burden and allows for greater focus on other domains. Thus, it is reasonable to hypothesize that privacy fatigue could have a specific impact on certain populations, such as undergraduate students, who often juggle multiple responsibilities and stressors. Applying Lee et al.’s (2024) findings to the context of privacy fatigue, we can infer that emotional states play",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,7,"privacy fatigue could have a specific impact on certain populations, such as undergraduate students, who often juggle multiple responsibilities and stressors. Applying Lee et al.’s (2024) findings to the context of privacy fatigue, we can infer that emotional states play a significant role in shaping performance expectations. Therefore, we propose the following hypothesis: H3. Privacy fatigue in undergraduate students positively influences their per­ formance expectations. Prior studies (Hinds et al., 2020; Shao et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2023) have suggested that privacy fatigue can lead to a sense of disengagement and decreased motivation to protect one’s privacy. Privacy fatigue emerges when individuals feel inundated by continuous data breaches, resulting in diminished enthusiasm for protecting per­ sonal information and a sense of vulnerability to potential threats (Hinds et al., 2020). Moreover, when users experience fatigue, instead of actively pursuing methods to safeguard their privacy, they tend to withdraw, show signs of skepticism, and feel emotionally drained because of concerns about data breaches and privacy threats (Shao et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2023). This disengagement and decreased motivation can cause",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,7,"to safeguard their privacy, they tend to withdraw, show signs of skepticism, and feel emotionally drained because of concerns about data breaches and privacy threats (Shao et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2023). This disengagement and decreased motivation can cause individuals to perceive privacy risks as less significant, as they become desensitized to potential threats. Given that undergraduate students often face considerable academic and social pressure, the impact of privacy fatigue on this group may be particularly pronounced. As they juggle various responsibilities, the additional burden of managing privacy concerns may lead to a greater sense of fatigue, and consequently, a reduced percep­ tion of privacy risks. This is because their cognitive and emotional resources are already stretched thin, making it more difficult for them to actively engage with and assess privacy threats. H4. Privacy fatigue in undergraduate students negatively influences their per­ ceived risks. According to Choi et al. (2018), people who experience significant privacy fatigue are less inclined to deliberate on how and if they should share personal details, and they may consent to sharing their information without",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,7,"influences their per­ ceived risks. According to Choi et al. (2018), people who experience significant privacy fatigue are less inclined to deliberate on how and if they should share personal details, and they may consent to sharing their information without much thought. Despite the 1 3 12327",47
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,8,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 ongoing concerns raised about ChatGPT, individuals who consent to sharing their information without much consideration are likely to be indifferent to ChatGPT’s privacy issues and have a higher intention to use ChatGPT. Given that undergraduate students are often early adopters of new technologies and frequently engage with digital platforms for both academic and social purposes, their experience of privacy fatigue may similarly influence their behavior. As privacy fatigue sets in, these students may become less concerned about the privacy implica­ tions of using ChatGPT and more inclined to use it without much hesitation. H5. Privacy fatigue in undergraduate students positively influences their behav­ ioral intention to use ChatGPT. 3.3 Perceived risk Perceived risk refers to individuals’ subjective evaluation of potential negative out­ comes, encompassing performance, financial, time, psychological, and privacy risks (Han et al., 2024). The notion of perceived risk plays a pivotal role in diverse domains such as marketing, finance, and cybersecurity, because individuals often base their choices on it (Emami-Naeini et al., 2021). In cybersecurity, the concept of perceived risk is used",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,8,"2024). The notion of perceived risk plays a pivotal role in diverse domains such as marketing, finance, and cybersecurity, because individuals often base their choices on it (Emami-Naeini et al., 2021). In cybersecurity, the concept of perceived risk is used to understand how individuals perceive vulnerabilities (Debb & McClel­ lan, 2021), how marketers view cybersecurity risks (Mathur, 2018), the influence of heuristics on risk perception (Van Schaik et al., 2020), and the relationship between risk perception and precautionary behavior (Van Schaik et al., 2017). This metric is commonly used; importantly, it can serve as a significant measure for expressing the degree of risk perception of undergraduate students regarding ChatGPT. A prior study (Murtarelli et al., 2023) investigated the quality of chatbots in the fashion industry and discovered that perceived risk has a negative impact on the behavioral intention to use them. Another study (Mehrolia et al., 2023) aimed to understand customers’ perceived risk related to banking chatbots, which was found to have a negative impact on their continued use. These findings suggest that perceived risk can negatively influence the behavioral",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,8,"them. Another study (Mehrolia et al., 2023) aimed to understand customers’ perceived risk related to banking chatbots, which was found to have a negative impact on their continued use. These findings suggest that perceived risk can negatively influence the behavioral intention to use chatbots across different domains. Given that undergraduate students frequently use digital platforms for both aca­ demic and social purposes, their perceived risk regarding ChatGPT is particularly relevant. If these students perceive significant risks associated with using ChatGPT, such as privacy concerns or data breaches, this perception may negatively impact their intention to use the platform. H6. Undergraduate students’ perceived risk negatively influences their behav­ ioral intention to use ChatGPT. 1 3 12328",115
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 3.4 Social influence Social influence is conceptualized as the existence of various social norms centered on normative beliefs that shape a given behavior (Joa & Magsamen-Conrad, 2021). This influence is crucial in shaping an individual’s technology adoption and usage behavior. Peer pressure, a specific type of social influence, plays a significant role in this process. A study (Sahu, 2022) conducted with undergraduate students at Tezpur University in India, a country with a large youth population, confirmed that peer pres­ sure influences undergraduate students’ behaviors, values, and attitudes. Additionally, in Nigeria, the impact of peer pressure was found to be high among undergraduate students, significantly influencing tobacco use. Therefore, undergraduate students are influenced by peer pressure, and considering the nature of social influence, it can be inferred that they are subject to the broader effects of social influence. Given that peer pressure is a subset of social influence, it would be logical to extend these findings to other forms of social influence. The influence of others has been found to play a pivotal role in",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,9,"of social influence. Given that peer pressure is a subset of social influence, it would be logical to extend these findings to other forms of social influence. The influence of others has been found to play a pivotal role in deciding whether to use ChatGPT for educational purposes (Yilmaz et al., 2023), as evidenced by a recent study conducted at a uni­ versity in Nepal using the UTAUT2 model, which demonstrated that social influence had a significant effect on students’ behavioral intention to use ChatGPT (Bahadur et al., 2024). Bayaga and Du Plessis (2024) investigated the behavioral intentions of university staff toward the use of a learning management system (LMS) and found that social influence significantly affected the behavioral intention to adopt it. Fur­ thermore, it has been proven in various contexts (e.g., mobile learning and e-learning platforms) that social influence is crucial for determining users’ behavioral intentions regarding technology adoption (Strzelecki, 2023). Therefore, it can be inferred that social influence among undergraduate students positively influences their intention to use ChatGPT. H7. The social influence of undergraduate students positively influences",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,9,"influence is crucial for determining users’ behavioral intentions regarding technology adoption (Strzelecki, 2023). Therefore, it can be inferred that social influence among undergraduate students positively influences their intention to use ChatGPT. H7. The social influence of undergraduate students positively influences their behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. Social influence may also affect performance expectations. It plays a pivotal role in elevating users’ initial confidence in chatbots, which in turn amplifies their incli­ nation to utilize chatbots and fosters active interactions (Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023). The concept of confirmation bias, derived from the renowned four-card task (Evans, 2016) and the finding that people tend to seek information that confirms rather than refutes their pre-existing assumptions, implies that an individual’s judg­ ment influences their expectations of the outcome. Based on these theories, it was inferred that social influence affects expectations. H8. The social influence of undergraduate students positively influences their performance expectations. 1 3 12329",152
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,10,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 4 Methodology 4.1 Instrument development The instrument was developed by measuring all items on a 5-point Likert scale rang­ ing from “strongly disagree” to “strongly agree.” All items were considered reliable and sourced from various studies (Pavlou et al., 2007; Schaufeli et al., 1996; Ven­ katesh et al., 2003). They are widely regarded as exemplary reference models for measuring related attributes. We integrated the key components of our study, namely “undergraduate students” and “ChatGPT,” into these items. The items concerning performance expectation (PE), effort expectancy (EE), social influence (SI), and behavioral intention to use ChatGPT (BI) were adopted from a study by Venkatesh et al. (2003). The items related to privacy fatigue (PF) were adapted from Schaufeli et al.’s (1996) study, and those associated with perceived risk (PR) were drawn from Pavlou et al.’s (2007) study. The original items referenced from other studies were in English and were translated by a bilingual English-Korean/Korean-English transla­ tion expert and underwent notarization to enhance the reliability of the survey pro­ cess. Table 1 lists the items used",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,10,"(2007) study. The original items referenced from other studies were in English and were translated by a bilingual English-Korean/Korean-English transla­ tion expert and underwent notarization to enhance the reliability of the survey pro­ cess. Table 1 lists the items used in the study. Given the potential for skewed survey data, we employed partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) for both the pilot and main tests. This method was selected because of its nonparametric. Table 1 Items and sources Variable Item Subcode Source PE I find that using ChatGPT helps my campus life. PE1 Ven­ katesh et al. (2003) Using ChatGPT enables me to accomplish tasks more quickly. PE2 Using ChatGPT increases my productivity. PE3 EE It would be easy for me to become skillful at using ChatGPT. EE1 I find ChatGPT easy to use. EE2 Learning to operate ChatGPT is easy for me. EE3 SI People who influence my behavior think that I should use ChatGPT. SI1 People who are important to me think that I should use ChatGPT. SI2 In general, students at my university have positive views",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,10,"is easy for me. EE3 SI People who influence my behavior think that I should use ChatGPT. SI1 People who are important to me think that I should use ChatGPT. SI2 In general, students at my university have positive views on using ChatGPT. SI3 BI I predict I would use ChatGPT in the next 6 months. BI1 I intend to use ChatGPT in the next 6 months. B12 I plan to use ChatGPT in the next 6 months. BI3 PF It is tiresome for me to care about privacy while using ChatGPT (Removed after the pilot test due to a low outer loading of 0.415). PF1 Schaufe­ li et al. (1996) I have become less interested in privacy issues while using ChatGPT. PF2 I just want to use ChatGPT and not to be bothered by privacy issues. PF3 PR I am afraid that someone else may review my queries that include personal information. PR1 Pavlou et al. (2007) I think that ChatGPT has a risk to accumulate my data (Removed after the pilot test due to a low outer loading",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,10,am afraid that someone else may review my queries that include personal information. PR1 Pavlou et al. (2007) I think that ChatGPT has a risk to accumulate my data (Removed after the pilot test due to a low outer loading of 0.622). PR2 I am concerned that ChatGPT may disclose my personal data. PR3 1 3 12330,57
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 nature, which provides flexibility in model estimation with various data distribu­ tions (Hair et al., 2019). To improve the generalizability of our findings, the model was evaluated using bootstrapping techniques and employing 5000 resampling iterations. A preliminary questionnaire was presented and discussed with associates. A revised version of the questionnaire was developed based on the feedback received, specifically regarding the items that best represented the variables. The revised ver­ sion was subsequently pilot-tested in November 2023 on an online community plat­ form at Korea University involving 107 undergraduate students. This sample size aligns with the recommended range of 100−200 participants for instrument devel­ opment (Kline, 2014). Through a pilot test, we verified the reliability and validity of the structural model based on these items. The second item related to perceived risk (PR2) had an outer loading value of 0.622, and the first item related to privacy fatigue (PF1) had an outer loading value of 0.415. These values were below the rec­ ommended threshold. According to Hair et al. (2021), the outer loading value should",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,11,"outer loading value of 0.622, and the first item related to privacy fatigue (PF1) had an outer loading value of 0.415. These values were below the rec­ ommended threshold. According to Hair et al. (2021), the outer loading value should exceed 0.7 to ensure model reliability. Therefore, we removed these items. Moreover, it is recommended that the heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) value be lower than 0.85 (Franke & Sarstedt, 2019), and all our items showed values that did not exceed this threshold. Additionally, the square root of the average variance extracted (AVE) was greater than the correlation coefficient for all items. Thus, discriminant validity was verified to be high (Fornell & Larcker, 1981), ensuring that one item was not confused with another. Subsequently, we conducted the main survey. 4.2 Sample Data collection for the main survey, targeting undergraduate students in South Korea, was conducted in November 2023. It was conducted online by Macromill Embrain, a research firm, using its panel system. Initially, participants were asked several screen­ ing questions (Q1−Q8 in the Online Resource) to determine their eligibility for the study.",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,11,"Korea, was conducted in November 2023. It was conducted online by Macromill Embrain, a research firm, using its panel system. Initially, participants were asked several screen­ ing questions (Q1−Q8 in the Online Resource) to determine their eligibility for the study. Only undergraduate students who had prior experience using ChatGPT and voluntarily agreed to participate in the research were considered qualified at this ini­ tial stage, and they were subsequently presented with the main survey questions. After obtaining ethical approval, a survey was administered to 700 undergraduate students. We chose this sample size in consideration of recent studies investigating the perceptions related to ChatGPT adoption, most of which had sample sizes ranging from high-300s to mid-500s (Foroughi et al., 2023; Sallam et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023; Tiwari et al., 2024). We aimed to enhance the reliability of our survey; thus, we opted for a larger sample size than that considered in previous studies. We specifically excluded those who had only used services incorporating Chat­ GPT features, as our primary focus was on individuals with direct experience using ChatGPT. Of the 700",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,11,"opted for a larger sample size than that considered in previous studies. We specifically excluded those who had only used services incorporating Chat­ GPT features, as our primary focus was on individuals with direct experience using ChatGPT. Of the 700 participants, five were excluded, resulting in a final sample of 695 individuals for analysis. The final sample consisted of 251 male and 444 female respondents. The age group closest to the average was 22–23 years and the duration of usage closest to the average was 3–4 months. Table 2 provides an overview of the sample demographics with percentages rounded to the second decimal place. We 1 3 12331",108
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,12,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 utilized these datasets for the first stage of PLS-SEM, specifically for measurement model assessment, for reliability, validity, and discriminant validity testing. 4.3 Reliability and validity The structural model satisfied all criteria for reliability and validity. To ensure the consistency and explanatory power of the measurement items for each variable, we assessed the Cronbach’s alpha (α), composite reliability (CR), AVE, and outer load­ ings, as presented in Table 3. All relevant variables exhibited an α-value greater than 0.6, which indicates an acceptable reliability as noted in Shi et al.’s (2012) study. Furthermore, the measurement model exceeded 0.8, surpassing the recommended 0.7 threshold for CR (Taber, 2018). Similarly, the model consistently met the benchmark Table 3 Reliability and validity metrics Variable Subcode Construct reliability and validity Discriminant validity (HTMT) α CR (rho c) AVE Outer loading BI EE PE PF PR SI BI BI1 0.946 0.965 0.902 0.943 BI2 0.96 BI3 0.946 EE EE1 0.803 0.885 0.719 0.783 0.334 EE2 0.88 EE3 0.877 PE PE1 0.803 0.884 0.717 0.858 0.679 0.358 PE2 0.852 PE3 0.83 PF",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,12,BI EE PE PF PR SI BI BI1 0.946 0.965 0.902 0.943 BI2 0.96 BI3 0.946 EE EE1 0.803 0.885 0.719 0.783 0.334 EE2 0.88 EE3 0.877 PE PE1 0.803 0.884 0.717 0.858 0.679 0.358 PE2 0.852 PE3 0.83 PF PF2 0.617 0.839 0.723 0.863 0.379 0.3 0.378 PF3 0.838 PR PR1 0.866 0.937 0.882 0.937 0.077 0.107 0.075 0.486 PR3 0.941 SI SI1 0.773 0.87 0.691 0.865 0.544 0.306 0.701 0.387 0.057 SI2 0.878 SI3 0.744 Measure Item Count % Age (years) 18 − 19 84 12.09 20 − 21 189 27.19 22 − 23 231 33.24 24 − 25 122 17.55 ≥ 26 69 9.93 Duration of use (months) < 1 163 23.45 1 − 2 120 17.27 3 − 4 130 18.71 5 − 6 133 19.14 7 − 8 58 8.35 ≥ 9 91 13.09 Gender Male 251 36.12 Female 444 63.88 Table 2 Demographic profile (N = 695) 1 3 12332,156
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,13,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 set by prior research with a minimum AVE value of 0.691, exceeding the suggested 0.5 threshold (Fornell & Larcker, 1981). Additionally, we confirmed that the outer loading value of the main model exceeded 0.7 (Hair et al., 2021), satisfying this crucial criterion. 4.4 Discriminant validity To assess whether each variable was distinct from the others, thereby establishing discriminant validity, we reviewed the HTMT values and compared the square root of the AVE for each variable with its correlations with other variables. Although methods such as the Fornell–Larcker criterion, cross-loadings, and variance inflation factor (VIF) can be employed to assess discriminant validity, we chose the HTMT because of its advantages in this case. Both the Fornell–Larcker criterion and cross- loadings rely on correlations, potentially missing complex or nonlinear relationships. Furthermore, unlike the VIF, the HTMT directly evaluates the actual relationships between constructs, rendering it less vulnerable to multicollinearity. Table 3 shows that the HTMT values in our model fall below 0.85, which is the threshold considered indicative of sufficient discriminant validity (Henseler et al., 2015).",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,13,"directly evaluates the actual relationships between constructs, rendering it less vulnerable to multicollinearity. Table 3 shows that the HTMT values in our model fall below 0.85, which is the threshold considered indicative of sufficient discriminant validity (Henseler et al., 2015). As shown in Table 4, the square root of the AVE surpasses the correlation coef­ ficient for all constructs. According to Fornell and Larcker (1981), the discriminant validity of a construct is supported when the square root of the AVE exceeds its correlation with other constructs. In other words, the square root of the AVE for each construct should be greater than that of the non-diagonal elements in the corresponding row and column. Our model consistently satisfies this criterion. 4.5 Data analysis We constructed a path model using SmartPLS version 4.0.9.6 (Ringle et al., 2022). As part of the second phase of the PLS-SEM approach (i.e., structural model assess­ ment), we scrutinized the structural model by analyzing the path coefficients (β) and P-values (P) of our proposed model. According to the PLS-SEM fit metrics, the model displays a good fit.",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,13,"phase of the PLS-SEM approach (i.e., structural model assess­ ment), we scrutinized the structural model by analyzing the path coefficients (β) and P-values (P) of our proposed model. According to the PLS-SEM fit metrics, the model displays a good fit. The satu­ rated model, which represented the full model with all possible relationships between the variables, yielded a standardized root mean square residual (SRMR) of 0.06. In contrast, the estimated model, which was derived from the sample data and repre­ sented the proposed relationships between the variables, had an SRMR of 0.07. Both Table 4 Correlation matrix of constructs excluding diagonal values with the square root of AVE (in bold) BI EE PE PF PR SI BI 0.949 EE 0.291 0.848 PE 0.594 0.286 0.847 PF 0.288 0.211 0.265 0.85 PR -0.07 -0.09 -0.055 -0.359 0.939 SI 0.469 0.242 0.557 0.266 -0.048 0.831 1 3 12333",146
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,14,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 values fall below the reference benchmark of 0.08 (Hu & Bentler, 1999). The normed fit index (NFI) for the saturated model is 0.79 and 0.80 for the estimated model. It is commonly understood that an NFI value closer to one indicates a more optimal fit (Bentler & Bonett, 1980). Given that all index values align with the reference standards, we can confidently infer the relationships between the variables. Figure 2 presents a graph that illustrates the PLS-SEM model. 4.6 Ethical considerations This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board (IRB) at Korea Univer­ sity and the approval number is KUIRB-2023-0386-01. We made every effort to treat survey participants with due regard and respect. The participants were presented with an online research consent form, which explained that participation in the study was voluntary and that if they chose not to participate, they could decline at any time. Additionally, the form provided contact information for any questions regarding the ethical considerations or rights of the participants. The survey commenced only after the participants provided their",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,14,"that if they chose not to participate, they could decline at any time. Additionally, the form provided contact information for any questions regarding the ethical considerations or rights of the participants. The survey commenced only after the participants provided their informed consent. 5 Results and discussion 5.1 Hypothesis testing Setting aside assumptions regarding moderation effects, all proposed hypotheses were supported, except for H6 (Table 5). Fig. 2 PLS-SEM model. Note: *: P < 0.05, **: P < 0.01, ***: P < 0.001, N. S. (not significant): P > 0.05 1 3 12334",92
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 Performance expectancy strongly influences the behavioral intention to use Chat­ GPT (β = 0.441, P < 0.001), supporting H1. We also found that effort expectancy positively influences the behavioral intention to use ChatGPT (β = 0.101, P < 0.01), supporting H2. Additionally, the analysis revealed that privacy fatigue has a positive impact on performance expectations (β = 0.126, P < 0.001), confirming H3. Moreover, privacy fatigue negatively affects the perceived risk of using ChatGPT (β =−0.359, P < 0.001), supporting H4. We also found that privacy fatigue has a positive impact on the behavioral intention to use ChatGPT (β = 0.108, P < 0.01), supporting H5. Con­ trary to expectations, perceived risk does not significantly influence behavioral inten­ tion (P = 0.725), rejecting H6. We also discovered that social influence positively affects the behavioral intention to use ChatGPT (β = 0.171, P < 0.001), confirming H7. Finally, social influence has a substantial positive effect on performance expec­ tancy (β = 0.523, P < 0.001), supporting H8. As presented in Fig. 2, our research model",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,15,"intention to use ChatGPT (β = 0.171, P < 0.001), confirming H7. Finally, social influence has a substantial positive effect on performance expec­ tancy (β = 0.523, P < 0.001), supporting H8. As presented in Fig. 2, our research model explains 40.2% of the variance in the behavioral intention to use ChatGPT and 32.5% in the performance expectancy of ChatGPT, as reflected by the R-squared values. Both of these values are high com­ pared to those of previous studies (Alrawi et al., 2020; Foroughi et al., 2023; Jaradat & Al Rababaa, 2013; Kaba & Touré, 2014). This suggests that our proposed frame­ work adequately captures the variance in both the behavioral intention to use Chat­ GPT and performance expectancy. 5.2 Theoretical implications Since the initial release of ChatGPT on November 30, 2022 (Balu & Sriram, 2023), there has been a continuous stream of research on user technology acceptance and perceptions of ChatGPT (Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023; Duong et al., 2024; Firat, 2023; Foroughi et al., 2023; Menon & Shilpa, 2023; Sallam et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023; Tiwari et al., 2024).",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,15,"continuous stream of research on user technology acceptance and perceptions of ChatGPT (Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023; Duong et al., 2024; Firat, 2023; Foroughi et al., 2023; Menon & Shilpa, 2023; Sallam et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023; Tiwari et al., 2024). However, to our knowledge, research addressing privacy Table 5 Path coefficients and hypothesis testing Path Original sample (O) Sample mean (M) Standard deviation (STDEV) T-statistic (|O/STDEV|) P-value Hypothesis Result PE → BI 0.441 0.442 0.042 10.513 0 H1 (+) Sup­ ported EE → BI 0.101 0.102 0.034 3 0.003 H2 (+) Sup­ ported PF → PE 0.126 0.127 0.031 3.998 0 H3 (+) Sup­ ported PF → PR -0.359 -0.359 0.036 9.833 0 H4 (-) Sup­ ported PF → BI 0.108 0.108 0.034 3.187 0.001 H5 (+) Sup­ ported PR → BI 0.011 0.01 0.03 0.352 0.725 H6 (-) Not sup­ ported SI → BI 0.171 0.17 0.04 4.29 0 H7 (+) Sup­ ported SI → PE 0.523 0.524 0.03 17.688 0 H8 (+) Sup­ ported 1 3 12335",169
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 fatigue and the perceived risk related to ChatGPT has been limited. A significant research gap persists in understanding why, despite the ongoing privacy concerns raised about ChatGPT (Al-Hawawreh et al., 2023; Chowdhury et al., 2023; Hunter, 2023; Li et al., 2023; Oviedo-Trespalacios et al., 2023; Paul et al., 2023; Sebastian, 2023a; Wach et al., 2023), undergraduate students continue to use it and how privacy fatigue and perceived risks influence their usage. This study applied the UTAUT model to assess privacy fatigue and perceived risk among undergraduate students using ChatGPT and presents important theoretical and practical implications based on the results. First, it identified the role of privacy fatigue in undergraduate students’ use of ChatGPT, revealing that students overwhelmed by privacy fatigue may be more sus­ ceptible to privacy vulnerabilities. Notably, as corroborated by H5, those experienc­ ing privacy fatigue demonstrate a heightened intention to use ChatGPT. This trend suggests the existence of a feedback loop—a cycle in which one event reinforces and amplifies subsequent events (Ford, 1999). Students who are resigned due to privacy",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,16,"those experienc­ ing privacy fatigue demonstrate a heightened intention to use ChatGPT. This trend suggests the existence of a feedback loop—a cycle in which one event reinforces and amplifies subsequent events (Ford, 1999). Students who are resigned due to privacy concerns may become increasingly reliant on ChatGPT, leading to potential privacy risks. This exposure could diminish their concerns and deepen their dependence on these platforms. In alignment with H3, these students also exhibited elevated perfor­ mance expectations from ChatGPT, possibly viewing it as a solace for their privacy- related apathy. This mirrors the findings of previous studies (Kim et al., 2019; Menon & Shilpa, 2023; Schomakers et al., 2021; Xu et al., 2014) suggesting that individu­ als are willing to trade some level of privacy to benefit from innovative technolo­ gies. Moreover, according to H4, these students perceived fewer risks. Their growing indifference toward privacy safeguards diminishes their risk awareness, making them more vulnerable to privacy threats. The incident where the data analytics firm Cam­ bridge Analytica misused Facebook users’ personal information is a prime example of how low-risk awareness",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,16,"Their growing indifference toward privacy safeguards diminishes their risk awareness, making them more vulnerable to privacy threats. The incident where the data analytics firm Cam­ bridge Analytica misused Facebook users’ personal information is a prime example of how low-risk awareness can lead to privacy threats. Previous studies (Berghel, 2018; Hinds et al., 2020; Wylie, 2019) have indicated that users grant application access permissions without sufficiently understanding how their data would be used and shared, leading to inappropriate data collection. Second, contrary to the initial expectation that perceived risks would reduce the intention to use ChatGPT, we observed that these risks did not significantly affect this intention. This may be attributed to the high performance expectations associated with ChatGPT, along with its substantial social influence. Previous studies (Bayaga & Du Plessis, 2024; Chang et al., 2016; Khan et al., 2023) have noted similar pat­ terns, in which low effect sizes for a specific path can be explained by the over­ riding effect of other factors. The elevated performance expectations surrounding ChatGPT, coupled with the positive feedback undergraduate students received from their",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,16,"noted similar pat­ terns, in which low effect sizes for a specific path can be explained by the over­ riding effect of other factors. The elevated performance expectations surrounding ChatGPT, coupled with the positive feedback undergraduate students received from their peers, suggest that these factors counterbalance the influence of perceived risks on the intention to use ChatGPT. Third, we identified positive relationships between social influence, performance expectations, effort expectations, and behavioral intention to use ChatGPT. This not only sheds light on previously unexplored connections but also reaffirms the find­ ings of existing research. For undergraduate students, the expectation of performance was the most significant contributor to their intention to use ChatGPT. This relation­ ship has been observed in previous studies involving undergraduate students (Duong 1 3 12336",127
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 et al., 2024; Menon & Shilpa, 2023; Shahsavar & Choudhury, 2023). Furthermore, this expectation is largely shaped by the prevailing social dynamics among these students, which have not been fully addressed in previous studies. The finding that social influence has a significant impact on the use of ChatGPT among undergraduate students supports our initial hypothesis that peer pressure within this cohort signifi­ cantly influences ChatGPT use, highlighting its importance as a motivational factor (Abdaljaleel et al., 2023; Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023; Menon & Shilpa, 2023). Given the significant impact of social and performance expectations on the use of ChatGPT by undergraduate students, it is crucial to consider these factors when addressing privacy concerns. This study also found that user perceptions of Chat­ GPT’s ease of use positively affect its usage, which aligns with prior results (Duong et al., 2024; Menon & Shilpa, 2023). 5.3 Practical implications First, considering that privacy fatigue combined with high performance expecta­ tions for ChatGPT may amplify its usage, practical measures are needed to address students’ increased vulnerability and diminished",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,17,"results (Duong et al., 2024; Menon & Shilpa, 2023). 5.3 Practical implications First, considering that privacy fatigue combined with high performance expecta­ tions for ChatGPT may amplify its usage, practical measures are needed to address students’ increased vulnerability and diminished awareness of privacy risks due to privacy fatigue. OpenAI can consider assessing boundaries for data collection con­ cerning undergraduate students’ privacy, and ideally offer them a choice to determine the extent of data collected. The need for such measures was underscored by the Cambridge Analytica incident involving a Facebook data breach. Facebook provides users with the option to decide which applications and sites can be connected to their Facebook accounts (Browne, 2018). However, it is essential that notifications regard­ ing data collection are presented in a clear, easily understandable, and user-centric manner. Moreover, to enhance undergraduate students’ understanding of privacy risks, an effective strategy may be to embed ChatGPT’s privacy-related content in regular undergraduate courses. According to May and Luth (2013), integrating spe­ cific educational content into standard courses, rather than standalone lectures, can lead to more positive behavioral outcomes.",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,17,"risks, an effective strategy may be to embed ChatGPT’s privacy-related content in regular undergraduate courses. According to May and Luth (2013), integrating spe­ cific educational content into standard courses, rather than standalone lectures, can lead to more positive behavioral outcomes. Second, given the significant influence of social pressure on the use of ChatGPT and its performance expectations, implementing a social media campaign may effec­ tively raise awareness regarding privacy risks. Various studies (Al-Dmour et al., 2020; Momany & Alshboul, 2016; Oyza & Edwin, 2015) have demonstrated the effectiveness of social media campaigns in leveraging social influence. However, it is crucial that such campaigns do not overwhelm students with excessive information, which may easily be disregarded. Instead, presenting concise and attention-grabbing information may reduce the likelihood of privacy fatigue. 5.4 Limitations and future research Our study aimed to investigate the factors influencing the use of ChatGPT, with a specific focus on undergraduate students who are enthusiastic about this technol­ ogy. Although our study targeted this demographic, other groups such as computer programmers and graduate students could also be potentially avid users",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,17,"influencing the use of ChatGPT, with a specific focus on undergraduate students who are enthusiastic about this technol­ ogy. Although our study targeted this demographic, other groups such as computer programmers and graduate students could also be potentially avid users of ChatGPT. Future research may benefit from a broader scope encompassing these diverse groups. 1 3 12337",57
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12321–12343 Additionally, the survey was limited to South Korean undergraduate students. Fur­ thermore, the survey agency’s system was rigidly set to the binary response options of male and female under participant demographics, preventing us from asking if an individual identified as non-binary; this aspect must be addressed in future research. Future studies could also extend their reach to culturally distinct countries or include undergraduate students from multiple nations. Finally, given that the ChatGPT beta version was released less than a year before our survey, most of the respondents did not use it for an extended period. Thus, future studies should focus on users with more experience with ChatGPT, particularly as the platform matures. Acknowledgements The authors would like to express their heartfelt gratitude to everyone who made the completion and publication of this paper possible. Special thanks go to the Editor-in-Chief, the editorial team, anonymous reviewers, and language proofreaders, whose commitment and efforts were instrumental in bringing this work to fruition. Funding The authors did not receive support from any organization for the submitted work.",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,18,"Special thanks go to the Editor-in-Chief, the editorial team, anonymous reviewers, and language proofreaders, whose commitment and efforts were instrumental in bringing this work to fruition. Funding The authors did not receive support from any organization for the submitted work. Data availability Data will be made available on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval This study was approved by the IRB at Korea University (approval number KUIRB-2023-0386-01). Consent Survey participants gave their informed consent before participation. Competing interests The authors have no relevant financial or non-financial interests to disclose. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial- NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in",180
A110,A110.pdf,10.1007/s10639-024-13198-6,18,"indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://crea​tivecommons​.or",101
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) Journal of Academic Ethics (2025) 23:2087–2110 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic Tasks: Examining Disciplinary Differences Yao Qu1 · Jue Wang1 Accepted: 30 April 2025 / Published online: 10 May 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools like ChatGPT are reshaping higher educa- tion, raising concerns about academic integrity alongside potential benefits. The psycho- logical tension accompanying the decision whether to use GenAI or not for a particular task may lead to “AI guilt”—students’ moral discomfort when using GenAI for tradition- ally human tasks. This study examines the impact of AI guilt on students’ use of Chat- GPT for academic tasks, focusing on disciplinary differences between pure and applied fields. We conducted a survey at a Singaporean university, measuring AI guilt and Chat- GPT usage. Using logistic regression analysis, we found that AI guilt significantly reduces ChatGPT use for creativity-based tasks but not for routine-based tasks. The relationship between AI guilt and ChatGPT use is linear in pure fields, while applied fields show a nonlinear pattern. Specifically, the",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,1,"analysis, we found that AI guilt significantly reduces ChatGPT use for creativity-based tasks but not for routine-based tasks. The relationship between AI guilt and ChatGPT use is linear in pure fields, while applied fields show a nonlinear pattern. Specifically, the perceived risk of being detected and potential academic penalties decreases ChatGPT use in creativity-based tasks for all samples, while ration- alization tendencies increase it. Interestingly, the interaction between rationalization ten- dencies and perceived social norms reduces usage, reflecting the tension between internal justification and external pressures. Heterogeneous analysis shows that rationalization ten- dencies and perceived risk influence pure fields more, whereas social norms drive applied fields more. These results show how AI guilt varies across disciplines and affects task-level GenAI tool use, offering insights for developing tailored ethical guidelines and integration strategies in higher education. Keywords AI guilt · ChatGPT · Higher education · Disciplinary differences · Creativity- based tasks * Jue Wang wangjue@ntu.edu.sg Yao Qu yao008@e.ntu.edu.sg 1 School of Social Sciences, Nanyang Technological University, 48 Nanyang Avenue, Singapore 639818, Singapore",171
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,1,"· Higher education · Disciplinary differences · Creativity- based tasks * Jue Wang wangjue@ntu.edu.sg Yao Qu yao008@e.ntu.edu.sg 1 School of Social Sciences, Nanyang Technological University, 48 Nanyang Avenue, Singapore 639818, Singapore",31
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,2,"2088 Y. Qu, J. Wang Introduction Recent advancements in generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) are transforming higher education by introducing new tools for learning, assessment, and research, which are reshaping academia and the future job market (Adiguzel et al., 2023; Bearman et al., 2023; Chan, 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Moscardini et al., 2022). GenAI tools like ChatGPT offer significant potential to enhance learning experiences by automating complex, cognitively demanding tasks (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023; Chan & Hu, 2023; Skulmowski, 2024; Sul- livan et al., 2023). However, their rapid integration into educational settings raises ethical concerns about academic integrity, educational quality, and the evolving role of educators (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023). Specifically, when students offload complex tasks to GenAI systems, the boundaries between human and machine contributions become increasingly blurred, particularly as systems become more personalized and interaction methods more sophisticated (Draxler et al., 2023; Skulmowski, 2023). This challenges traditional notions of authorship and contribution, requiring higher education to address accountability and transparency of GenAI-augmented intellectual labour. As GenAI increasingly integrates into higher education, it prompts an urgent need to establish",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,2,"(Draxler et al., 2023; Skulmowski, 2023). This challenges traditional notions of authorship and contribution, requiring higher education to address accountability and transparency of GenAI-augmented intellectual labour. As GenAI increasingly integrates into higher education, it prompts an urgent need to establish clear ethical standards in the GenAI era (Ray, 2023; Tlili et al., 2023). The ethical implications of GenAI in academia are deeply intertwined with how stu- dents psychologically engage with these tools. This interconnection stems from the recipro- cal relationship between institutional ethical standards and students’ internal moral frame- works, which together shape their cognitive and emotional responses to GenAI integration. As GenAI tools become more prevalent in educational settings, students may experience a range of emotional and ethical challenges, including anxiety about academic integrity (Sul- livan et al., 2023), fear of over-reliance, concerns about fairness and bias in GenAI outputs (Qu & Wang, 2024), privacy risks (Skulmowski, 2024), and uncertainty about responsibil- ity and authorship in GenAI-assisted work (Draxler et al., 2023). AI guilt, introduced as a term by Chan (2024), describes the moral discomfort students experience when using",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,2,"outputs (Qu & Wang, 2024), privacy risks (Skulmowski, 2024), and uncertainty about responsibil- ity and authorship in GenAI-assisted work (Draxler et al., 2023). AI guilt, introduced as a term by Chan (2024), describes the moral discomfort students experience when using GenAI for tasks traditionally performed by humans. This conceptualization is grounded in cognitive dissonance theory, which explains how individuals experience psychological tension when their actions contradict their established beliefs or values (Festinger, 1962). Chan (2024) argues explicitly that this discomfort stems from the incongruence between students’ use of GenAI tools and internalized academic values emphasizing authenticity, individual effort, and intellectual growth. The fundamental challenge lies in reconciling GenAI’s efficiency and capabilities with traditional academic values of personal achieve- ment and intellectual ownership (Chan & Hu, 2023). Therefore, AI guilt functions as a critical psychological mechanism through which students evaluate their moral responsibility amid evolving expectations for technology use in academic settings. This cognitive-emotional response emerges when students’ use of GenAI tools conflicts with internalized academic values, creating tension that requires resolution. Empirical research on AI guilt, while theoretically promising, remains",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,2,"moral responsibility amid evolving expectations for technology use in academic settings. This cognitive-emotional response emerges when students’ use of GenAI tools conflicts with internalized academic values, creating tension that requires resolution. Empirical research on AI guilt, while theoretically promising, remains under- developed despite its potential significance. By systematically investigating AI guilt, researchers can gain valuable insights into the broader dynamics of moral responsibility in human-technology relationships. Such understanding extends beyond academic contexts to illuminate ethical negotiation patterns in all domains where automation challenges tradi- tional conceptions of human agency and accountability. Addressing these tensions requires intentional pedagogical approaches that promote both independent thinking and responsi- ble GenAI use—defined as the ethical, transparent, and accountable integration of GenAI",117
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,3,"2089 The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic… in academic work that aligns with institutional guidelines and societal norms (Bai et al., 2023; Mikalef et al., 2022). Moreover, while recent studies have identified the general phenomenon of AI guilt (Chan, 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023), there remains a significant gap in our understanding of how this guilt manifests across diverse task types and academic fields. According to cogni- tive dissonance theory, the intensity of psychological discomfort depends on the magnitude of dissonance between actions and values (Festinger, 1962). Therefore, AI guilt may affect task engagement differently depending on the cognitive demands, creative expectations, and ethical implications of various tasks (Eaton, 2023; Huang & Rust, 2018). Addition- ally, academic fields are characterized by distinct disciplinary norms—the shared expecta- tions, values, and practices that characterize academic communities within specific fields of study (Becher, 1994; Becher & Trowler, 2001; Biglan, 1973), which may also influence the nature and intensity of AI guilt experienced by students. Consequently, understanding AI guilt in relation to task types and disciplinary context clarifies",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,3,"within specific fields of study (Becher, 1994; Becher & Trowler, 2001; Biglan, 1973), which may also influence the nature and intensity of AI guilt experienced by students. Consequently, understanding AI guilt in relation to task types and disciplinary context clarifies its broader impact on stu- dents’ use of GenAI tools, reflecting the norms and culture of each field. To address the identified gap in the literature, we investigate two research questions: 1) How does AI guilt differ between pure and applied academic fields? 2) How does the dif- ference in AI guilt influence students’ use of ChatGPT for creativity-based tasks? Using a survey conducted at a Singaporean university, we investigate this issue with the aim of informing strategies to maximize the benefits of GenAI while preserving academic integ- rity. Although the study focuses on a specific institution, its findings have broader appli- cability because, as Becher (1994) points out, disciplinary cultures share norms and prac- tices that transcend institutional and national boundaries, facilitated by academic mobility, shared literature, and international collaboration. Thus, our results are likely generalizable to similar academic",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,3,"broader appli- cability because, as Becher (1994) points out, disciplinary cultures share norms and prac- tices that transcend institutional and national boundaries, facilitated by academic mobility, shared literature, and international collaboration. Thus, our results are likely generalizable to similar academic contexts within the same disciplines. This study contributes to the extant literature by integrating disciplinary differences and task-level consideration into the understanding of AI guilt. By examining the impact of AI guilt on tasks across pure and applied fields, we endeavor to reveal and analyze the com- plexity of students’ emotional and ethical experiences regarding the use of GenAI tools. This study provides a more context-specific understanding of how disciplinary norms shape the way AI guilt affects students’ task engagement and performance, which serve as a foundation for developing targeted interventions and pedagogical strategies. Literature Review and Hypotheses Distinguishing AI Guilt from Technology Anxiety The body of knowledge regarding AI’s psychological influence on educational environ- ments continues to develop. While technology anxiety has been extensively studied as a determinant in technology acceptance models (Kim et al., 2023; Meuter et al.,",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,3,"Guilt from Technology Anxiety The body of knowledge regarding AI’s psychological influence on educational environ- ments continues to develop. While technology anxiety has been extensively studied as a determinant in technology acceptance models (Kim et al., 2023; Meuter et al., 2003; Saadé & Kira, 2007), AI guilt represents a distinct psychological construct requiring separate conceptualization in educational settings. Technology anxiety stems primarily from cog- nitive uncertainty about one’s abilities, typically producing avoidance behaviours (Tsai et al., 2020). In contrast, AI guilt operates through cognitive dissonance between actions and internalized values—occurring when students can effectively use GenAI tools but rec- ognize conflicts with academic values of intellectual authenticity (Chan, 2024). This dis- tinction manifests in different behavioural patterns: anxiety leads to technology avoidance,",122
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,4,"2090 Y. Qu, J. Wang while AI guilt produces what Skulmowski (2024) terms the “AI ghostwriter effect,” where users engage with technology while concealing their dependency on it. The ethical dimensions further differentiate these responses. Technology anxiety repre- sents a self-directed concern about capability, while AI guilt incorporates broader consider- ations about academic integrity and intellectual honesty. Responsible GenAI use (Mikalef et al., 2022) requires adherence to principles of fairness, transparency, and accountabil- ity—all connected to academic integrity. AI guilt emerges when students perceive gaps between these principles and their GenAI usage, such as submitting AI-generated content without attribution. In our framework, AI guilt serves a dual function: as an adaptive ethical signal deter- ring academic misconduct and as a psychological experience students must navigate when institutional guidelines remain unclear. Our study examines this navigation process to understand when guilt reflects genuine ethical concerns versus excessive apprehension about legitimate GenAI applications. This perspective helps distinguish between beneficial GenAI augmentation (enhancing capabilities while preserving academic integrity) versus problematic automation (inappropriately outsourcing cognitive responsibilities). The following review focuses specifically on AI guilt",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,4,"reflects genuine ethical concerns versus excessive apprehension about legitimate GenAI applications. This perspective helps distinguish between beneficial GenAI augmentation (enhancing capabilities while preserving academic integrity) versus problematic automation (inappropriately outsourcing cognitive responsibilities). The following review focuses specifically on AI guilt and examines how this psycho- logical response affects students’ engagement with GenAI across various academic tasks and disciplinary contexts. Impact of AI Guilt on ChatGPT Use Across Task Types Guilt can manifest either as a temporary emotional state (situational guilt) or as a stable personality trait (chronic guilt). Situational guilt arises in response to specific perceived moral transgressions, often reflecting context-dependent emotional discomfort (Baumeister et al., 1994; Kugler & Jones, 1992). In contrast, chronic guilt denotes a persistent disposi- tion toward experiencing guilt across various situations (Tangney, 1990; Tangney & Dear- ing, 2003). Chan’s (2024) research on secondary school students found that GenAI is more readily accepted for tasks like research and idea generation, as students perceive these applications as enhancing rather than diminishing their personal contributions to academic work. In contrast, using GenAI for writing may evoke guilt, as",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,4,"found that GenAI is more readily accepted for tasks like research and idea generation, as students perceive these applications as enhancing rather than diminishing their personal contributions to academic work. In contrast, using GenAI for writing may evoke guilt, as it is perceived to undermine originality and integrity. Therefore, Chan (2024) initially conceptualized AI guilt as situ- ational, characterizing it as an emotional response rooted in ethical concerns reflecting the tension between human cognition and GenAI capabilities. Extending Chan’s work, our study examines situational AI guilt by empirically investi- gating students’ immediate psychological and moral reactions to specific academic tasks involving ChatGPT. This situational approach provides conceptual clarity by aligning closely with task-based contexts in educational settings. While Chan’s research focuses on secondary school students, our work extends beyond merely examining a different student population (undergraduates) by introducing two new analytical perspectives: disciplinary differences and task-level components. These additional dimensions offer significant theo- retical insights, as undergraduate students engage with more complex, discipline-specific tasks. Therefore, our study contributes to the literature by (1) empirically validating AI guilt in higher education",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,4,"analytical perspectives: disciplinary differences and task-level components. These additional dimensions offer significant theo- retical insights, as undergraduate students engage with more complex, discipline-specific tasks. Therefore, our study contributes to the literature by (1) empirically validating AI guilt in higher education contexts, (2) analyzing disciplinary differences in students’ expe- riences of AI guilt, and (3) conducting a task-level analysis to explore how AI guilt shapes students’ GenAI usage patterns. The consideration of task nature is crucial when examining GenAI’s impact and the ethical boundaries of its use (Qu et al., 2024). Research shows that the impact of AI on the",99
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,5,"2091 The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic… labor market is predominantly observed at the task level (Huang & Rust, 2018). Studies in management and labor have extensively explored AI’s roles in automation and augmenta- tion (Autor et al., 2003; Langer & Landers, 2021; Raisch & Krakowski, 2021), with the former referring to the complete takeover of tasks by machines, while the latter involving humans collaborating with machines to perform tasks (Raisch & Krakowski, 2021). Huang and Rust (2018) identified a hierarchical pattern in automation, where tasks requiring lower cognitive skills, such as mechanical or repetitive operations, are more vulnerable to AI replacement than those demanding complex analytical abilities or emotional intelligence. This pattern can be extended to higher education, where AI can readily automate routine tasks. Students might still be more comfortable using GenAI for routine tasks despite expe- riencing high levels of AI guilt, as their guilt is less likely to deter them from using GenAI for tasks that are repetitive and well-defined. However, for tasks requiring complex cogni- tive effort, higher AI",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,5,"for routine tasks despite expe- riencing high levels of AI guilt, as their guilt is less likely to deter them from using GenAI for tasks that are repetitive and well-defined. However, for tasks requiring complex cogni- tive effort, higher AI guilt may lead students to avoid using GenAI, as they perceive it to undermine the intellectual integrity of such work (Chan, 2024). In terms of ethical boundaries, Andersen et al., (2024) surveyed Danish universities to examine ethical perceptions of using GenAI in research tasks. The study identified two clusters with similar views on the acceptable use of GenAI. The first cluster, “GenAI as a Work Horse,” found GenAI suitable for technical tasks like coding, statistical analysis, and data pattern recognition but viewed its use in peer review and idea generation sceptically (Andersen et al., 2024). The second cluster, “GenAI as a Language Assistant Only,” was more cautious, endorsing GenAI primarily for language editing and transcription while being critical of its application in data analysis and core research activities (Andersen et al., 2024). These findings further suggest researchers are open to",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,5,"a Language Assistant Only,” was more cautious, endorsing GenAI primarily for language editing and transcription while being critical of its application in data analysis and core research activities (Andersen et al., 2024). These findings further suggest researchers are open to using GenAI for rou- tine-based and efficiency-enhancing tasks but are wary of applying it to creative or inter- pretative roles. Building on this distinction, we differentiate between creativity-based and routine- based tasks in academic settings. Creativity-based tasks involve higher-order thinking and include research, brainstorming, complex homework, writing/editing, and advanced cod- ing. These tasks typically require critical analysis, creativity, and disciplinary expertise. Routine-based tasks, which are repetitive or procedural, include fact-checking, grammar correction, paraphrasing, and translation. These tasks follow established patterns and have more predictable outcomes. Based on this categorization, we propose: Hypothesis 1a: AI guilt does not affect the use of ChatGPT in routine-based tasks. Hypothesis 1b: AI guilt reduces the use of ChatGPT in creativity-based tasks. Impact of AI Guilt on Creativity‑Based Task ChatGPT Use Across Disciplines AI guilt also varies depending on the broader disciplinary context in which",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,5,"of ChatGPT in routine-based tasks. Hypothesis 1b: AI guilt reduces the use of ChatGPT in creativity-based tasks. Impact of AI Guilt on Creativity‑Based Task ChatGPT Use Across Disciplines AI guilt also varies depending on the broader disciplinary context in which tasks are per- formed. Biglan’s (1973) seminal work categorized academic disciplines by subject mat- ter characteristics, creating a lasting framework in higher education (Bray, 2008). He divided disciplines into four clusters: hard pure, soft pure, hard applied, and soft applied, each with distinct knowledge structures and intellectual cultures. For instance, the “hard applied” cluster, which includes engineering, focuses on utility and problem-solving, while “soft pure” disciplines, such as history or sociology, emphasize holistic understanding and personal engagement. These differences in disciplinary focus and values shape how GenAI tools are perceived and the degree of AI guilt experienced by students, which can",141
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,6,"2092 Y. Qu, J. Wang be understood from the perspectives of disciplinary knowledge structures and industry alignment. Disciplinary Knowledge Structures and Paradigms The fundamental organization of knowledge differs significantly across disciplines, influencing how students engage with GenAI tools and the types of tasks they typically perform. Knowledge structure—referring to how information is organized, validated, and transmitted within a discipline’s intellec- tual framework—shapes both teaching approaches and student learning experiences. Dis- ciplines whose knowledge structure predominantly comprises creative and interpretative tasks (Becher, 1994), such as the humanities and social sciences, tend to evoke higher lev- els of AI guilt. These “soft pure” fields emphasize subjective analysis, originality, and per- sonal expression, leading students to resist GenAI tools that may undermine these core ele- ments. Their multi-paradigmatic approaches involve predominantly subjective analysis and personal contributions (Dwivedi et al., 2021), creating fundamental tensions with GenAI use. Conversely, “hard applied” fields like engineering (Biglan, 1973) feature more uni- fied paradigms and structured knowledge organization, viewing GenAI as a legitimate tool for quantitative analysis and technical problem-solving. These fields demonstrate higher technological literacy and collaboration",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,6,"with GenAI use. Conversely, “hard applied” fields like engineering (Biglan, 1973) feature more uni- fied paradigms and structured knowledge organization, viewing GenAI as a legitimate tool for quantitative analysis and technical problem-solving. These fields demonstrate higher technological literacy and collaboration patterns (Davis, 1989; Venkatesh & Davis, 2000; Walsh & Bayma, 1996), making GenAI integration more consistent with disciplinary expectations. Empirical evidence supports these distinctions, with GenAI more maturely integrated into STEM curricula while humanities and social sciences demonstrate less fre- quent GenAI use (Bond et al., 2024). This distinction aligns with our conceptualization of creativity- versus routine-based tasks, as pure fields often prioritize creativity-based tasks while applied fields frequently emphasize efficiency in more structured routine-based task environments at the undergraduate level. Disciplinary Pressures and Alignments Technology adoption patterns vary across differ- ent disciplines due to differing external and internal pressures (Becher & Trowler, 2001). Market-oriented disciplines tend to adopt new technologies more rapidly, while those less aligned with industry norms demonstrate greater resistance (Walsh & Bayma, 1996). Applied disciplines, with closer connections to industry demands (Lam et al., 2014), have",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,6,"(Becher & Trowler, 2001). Market-oriented disciplines tend to adopt new technologies more rapidly, while those less aligned with industry norms demonstrate greater resistance (Walsh & Bayma, 1996). Applied disciplines, with closer connections to industry demands (Lam et al., 2014), have greater incentives to adopt GenAI tools like ChatGPT, as these technologies align with professional practice standards. While both pure and applied disciplines navigate publica- tion requirements and research productivity metrics, they prioritize different external stake- holders—applied fields must satisfy both academic and industry expectations, while pure fields primarily respond to scholarly community standards. This difference in stakeholder orientation influences how disciplines evaluate technology adoption. As a result, applied disciplines may experience less AI guilt as they rationalize GenAI use through its profes- sional relevance, while pure disciplines may exhibit stronger concerns, perceiving GenAI use as a potential compromise of traditional scholarly values of originality and individual intellectual contribution. To summarize, the existing literature suggests that AI guilt and its impact vary across disciplines due to factors such as disciplinary knowledge structures and industry align- ment (Becher, 1994; Biglan, 1973; Walsh",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,6,"scholarly values of originality and individual intellectual contribution. To summarize, the existing literature suggests that AI guilt and its impact vary across disciplines due to factors such as disciplinary knowledge structures and industry align- ment (Becher, 1994; Biglan, 1973; Walsh & Bayma, 1996). Pure fields’ predominant focus on creativity and unified paradigms (Becher, 1994; Davis, 1989; Venkatesh & Davis, 2000) might shape this relationship differently from applied fields’ focus on",70
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,7,"2093 The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic… pragmatic, industry-oriented approaches and multi-paradigm perspectives (Biglan, 1973; Dwivedi et al., 2021; Lam et al., 2014). Hence, we propose: Hypothesis 2a: The influence of AI guilt on the use of ChatGPT for creativity-based tasks differs between students in pure and applied fields. While these perspectives provide a foundation for understanding disciplinary differ- ences in AI guilt, the specific nature of these differences remains underexplored. The relationship between AI guilt and ChatGPT use may not be linear, as scholars struggle balancing complex tensions between technological advancement and academic integrity. In pure fields, scholars must balance the pursuit of theoretical knowledge with concerns about originality and individual expression, while in applied fields, researchers negoti- ate between practical innovation needs and traditional academic values. Therefore, we hypothesize: Hypothesis 2b: The relationship between AI guilt and ChatGPT use for creativity- based tasks may exhibit non-linear patterns. Impact of Different AI Guilt on Creativity‑Based Task ChatGPT Use Chan (2024) identifies three dimensions of AI guilt: perceived laziness and authenticity, fear of judgment,",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,7,"relationship between AI guilt and ChatGPT use for creativity- based tasks may exhibit non-linear patterns. Impact of Different AI Guilt on Creativity‑Based Task ChatGPT Use Chan (2024) identifies three dimensions of AI guilt: perceived laziness and authenticity, fear of judgment, and concerns about identity and self-efficacy. These dimensions are key to understanding how students engage with GenAI tools like ChatGPT, particularly in creativity-based tasks. Perceived laziness and authenticity reflect the belief that reliance on GenAI diminishes the value and authenticity of one’s work (Chan, 2024). In pure fields such as philosophy and literature, where originality and creativity are central, students may feel that using GenAI undermines intellectual rigor, increasing AI guilt. This concern is amplified by GenAI detection tools, which primarily assess written work—the dominant form of sub- mission in pure fields—raising fears of academic dishonesty. This aligns with previous findings that perceived risk—particularly performance risk (concern over potential nega- tive outcomes of technology use)—significantly inhibits technology adoption by increasing uncertainty and potential negative consequences, thus reducing perceived ease of use and adoption intention (Featherman & Pavlou, 2003). According to",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,7,"findings that perceived risk—particularly performance risk (concern over potential nega- tive outcomes of technology use)—significantly inhibits technology adoption by increasing uncertainty and potential negative consequences, thus reducing perceived ease of use and adoption intention (Featherman & Pavlou, 2003). According to the Technology Accept- ance Model (TAM), perceived ease of use is crucial for technology adoption (Davis, 1989; Saadé & Kira, 2007; Venkatesh & Davis, 1996). The presence of GenAI detection tools in pure fields reduces this ease, as students fear difficulty integrating GenAI without detection and penalties. This lowers their intention to adopt GenAI, seeing it as a risky tool. In con- trast, applied fields like engineering and business emphasize efficiency and often involve mixed submissions that combine text with technical or project-based work. Thus, reliance on GenAI in these fields is less concerning, as it enhances productivity and aligns with industry norms. Consequently, the perceived risk of using GenAI in creativity-based tasks is likely higher in pure fields than in applied fields, where GenAI detection plays a less sig- nificant role in evaluating student performance. We propose: Hypothesis 3a:",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,7,"with industry norms. Consequently, the perceived risk of using GenAI in creativity-based tasks is likely higher in pure fields than in applied fields, where GenAI detection plays a less sig- nificant role in evaluating student performance. We propose: Hypothesis 3a: The perceived risk of being detected and potential academic penalties will have a greater influence on creativity-based task in pure fields than in applied fields.",65
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,8,"2094 Y. Qu, J. Wang The fear of judgment regarding GenAI use in academic work stems from concerns about perception by peers, supervisors, and society (Chan, 2024). This apprehension is intricately linked to social norms—defined as shared standards of acceptable behavior that guide indi- viduals’ actions within a group or society—that influence technology adoption (Dickinger et al., 2008; Sawang et al., 2014). The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Tech- nology (UTAUT) by Venkatesh et al. (2003) emphasizes the critical role of social factors in this process, defining social influence as the perceived expectations of others regarding one’s use of new technology (Venkatesh et al., 2003). This influence can manifest as peer and social pressure, potentially exacerbating feelings of guilt and discouraging the use of technologies. Empirical studies on web usage (Cheung et al., 2000) and mobile services (Kleijnen et al., 2004) corroborate the significant impact of social norms on users’ inten- tions to adopt and utilize new technologies. Different academic fields conceptualize social influence in varied ways (Graf-Vlachy et al., 2018). In social psychology, social influence is often",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,8,"et al., 2004) corroborate the significant impact of social norms on users’ inten- tions to adopt and utilize new technologies. Different academic fields conceptualize social influence in varied ways (Graf-Vlachy et al., 2018). In social psychology, social influence is often seen as a cognitive process driven by subjective perceptions (Morris et al., 2015). In contrast, applied disciplines such as business administration focus on observable behavioural patterns within social contexts (Friedkin, 2004). This distinction suggests that applied fields might be more influenced by practical demonstrations of technology use, such as the adoption of ChatGPT by peers or supervisors. Group norms, which reflect shared values and goals within a group, are strong predic- tors of technology adoption, especially for social and collaborative technologies (Bagozzi & Lee, 2002; H.-T. Tsai & Bagozzi, 2014). In applied disciplines, where collaborative work and practical problem-solving are common, group norms may exert a stronger influence on adopting ChatGPT. As these fields increasingly integrate IT in collaborative settings, GenAI tools like ChatGPT may become normalized for creativity-based tasks. Research further suggests that group norms can be more influential",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,8,"common, group norms may exert a stronger influence on adopting ChatGPT. As these fields increasingly integrate IT in collaborative settings, GenAI tools like ChatGPT may become normalized for creativity-based tasks. Research further suggests that group norms can be more influential than subjective norms in pre- dicting adoption behaviour (Graf-Vlachy et al., 2018; Tsai & Bagozzi, 2014), particularly in group-oriented or practical assessments where ChatGPT could be advantageous. There- fore, we propose: Hypothesis 3b: Social norms will have a greater influence on creativity-based task in applied fields than in pure fields. Concerns about identity and self-efficacy involve doubts about personal capability and the role of human agency in the context of GenAI’s growing capabilities (Chan, 2024). Cognitive dissonance theory offers an explanation of the conflict between students’ dis- comfort when using GenAI and their belief in the importance of personal intellectual effort (Chan, 2024; Harmon-Jones & Mills, 2019). To alleviate this discomfort, students may rationalize their GenAI use by viewing it as a tool that enhances rather than replaces their work. The justifiability of such rationalization depends on whether it aligns",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,8,"effort (Chan, 2024; Harmon-Jones & Mills, 2019). To alleviate this discomfort, students may rationalize their GenAI use by viewing it as a tool that enhances rather than replaces their work. The justifiability of such rationalization depends on whether it aligns with respon- sible GenAI use, which refers to the transparent, accountable, and ethical integration of GenAI that preserves academic integrity while supporting—not substituting for—core intellectual development. Rationalization becomes problematic when it serves to justify GenAI applications that undermine learning objectives or violate institutional guidelines, but can be appropriate when it reconciles legitimate GenAI assistance with academic values. This rationalization process aligns with the concept of internalization in technology adoption literature. In Information Systems (IS) research, internalization is not just about compliance with social norms but involves the process of integrating others’ expectations",132
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,9,"2095 The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic… into one’s own belief system (Chen et al., 2009; Hong & Tam, 2006). In fields like the humanities and social sciences, where personal intellectual contribution is central, students may find it harder to justify GenAI use, viewing it as undermining authenticity and origi- nality. This leads to higher AI guilt and a greater need for rationalization. Conversely, in applied fields such as engineering, GenAI is often seen as a practical tool that comple- ments human effort. Here, students experience less cognitive dissonance and feel less need to rationalize their GenAI use, resulting in lower AI guilt. Therefore, we propose: Hypothesis 3c: The tendency to rationalize GenAI use will have a greater influence on creativity-based task in pure fields than in applied fields. In summary, the three dimensions of AI guilt—perceived laziness and authenticity, fear of judgment, and concerns about identity and self-efficacy—will likely influence students’ use of ChatGPT for creativity-based tasks differently across pure and applied fields. Methodology This study examines how AI guilt influences students’ use",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,9,"of AI guilt—perceived laziness and authenticity, fear of judgment, and concerns about identity and self-efficacy—will likely influence students’ use of ChatGPT for creativity-based tasks differently across pure and applied fields. Methodology This study examines how AI guilt influences students’ use of GenAI tools across academic tasks, with a focus on differences between pure and applied disciplines. To gain insights into students’ experiences and perceptions, a survey was conducted among undergradu- ates at a Singaporean university. Significant disciplinary variances in GenAI adoption have been reported in an earlier study with the same dataset (Qu et al., 2024). Data Collection The research design followed a two-stage preliminary process involving a Focus Group Discussion (FGD) and a pilot study to refine the questionnaire, ensuring its relevance and clarity (Qu et al., 2024). The finalized questionnaire included a combination of closed- ended, open-ended, and Likert scale questions, gathering both quantitative and qualitative data. It was structured into two main sections: (1) Demographic Characteristics, assess- ing students’ academic level, discipline, and awareness and knowledge of GenAI; and (2) GenAI Usage, exploring the types of GenAI",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,9,"Likert scale questions, gathering both quantitative and qualitative data. It was structured into two main sections: (1) Demographic Characteristics, assess- ing students’ academic level, discipline, and awareness and knowledge of GenAI; and (2) GenAI Usage, exploring the types of GenAI used, academic tasks involving GenAI (through open-ended questions), and hypothetical scenarios (e.g., detection tool, instructor stance) that influence participants’ likelihood of using ChatGPT. The questionnaire was distributed via Google Forms between December 2023 and Feb- ruary 2024, allowing for anonymity and broad data collection, which is crucial for obtain- ing honest responses to sensitive questions regarding GenAI usage. To enhance participa- tion, an incentive of a $10 voucher was offered to the top 100 respondents. Of the 260 distributed questionnaires, we received 200 completed responses with a response rate of 76.9%. Additionally, responses from medical schools (n = 7) were excluded from the analy- sis to prevent potential bias arising from their disproportionate representation in the sample distribution. After exclusions, the final dataset comprised 193 valid responses. For participant recruitment, we employed convenience and snowball sampling meth- ods due to",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,9,"excluded from the analy- sis to prevent potential bias arising from their disproportionate representation in the sample distribution. After exclusions, the final dataset comprised 193 valid responses. For participant recruitment, we employed convenience and snowball sampling meth- ods due to feasibility constraints (Qu et al., 2024). While probability sampling would offer stronger representativeness, accessing stratified random samples across diverse academic disciplines presented significant practical barriers, including limited institutional permis- sions to access student databases across multiple colleges. The convenience sampling",80
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,10,"2096 Y. Qu, J. Wang approach allowed us to recruit participants through direct outreach in accessible academic units, while snowball sampling helped reach students in less accessible disciplines through peer referrals. To address potential bias inherent in non-probability sampling, we adopted a quota sampling approach to ensure a fair number of respondents across academic levels and disciplines. The final dataset achieved a well-balanced representation, with 47.7% from pure disciplines and 52.3% from applied disciplines. Variables Dependent Variable: Task Nature The dependent variable measures respondents’ use of ChatGPT across task types. Based on our earlier definitions of creativity-based and routine-based tasks, two researchers indepen- dently categorized responses to the question, “For what types of academic tasks do you use GenAI?” with discrepancies resolved by a third researcher. Two dummy variables, Creativ- ity_task and Routine_task, were created to indicate GenAI usage for creativity- or routine- based tasks (1 = yes, 0 = no). Figure 1 illustrates the categorization process for creativity- and routine-based tasks, along with example responses for each category. Independent Variable: AI Guilt The independent variable, AI guilt, is a",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,10,"creativity- or routine- based tasks (1 = yes, 0 = no). Figure 1 illustrates the categorization process for creativity- and routine-based tasks, along with example responses for each category. Independent Variable: AI Guilt The independent variable, AI guilt, is a composite of three dimensions: rationalization ten- dency, perceived social norms, and perceived risk. Rationalization tendency reflects stu- dents’ justification for using GenAI in academic tasks and is assessed by the question, “If the use of GenAI is permitted in universities, should there be a penalty (i.e., deduction Fig. 1 Task-based categorization of ChatGPT usage in academic settings. The figure shows the classifica- tion of student-reported ChatGPT activities into routine-based tasks (left panel) and creativity-based tasks (right panel)",117
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,11,"2097 The Impact of AI Guilt on Students’ Use of ChatGPT for Academic… of marks) if a student were to use GenAI in his/her assignment?” This measure captures rationalization tendencies by examining how students interpret ambiguous permission boundaries. Students answering “No” (coded as 1) demonstrate a rationalization pattern whereby they expand the scope of permission to eliminate consequences, potentially mini- mizing important ethical distinctions about appropriate GenAI use contexts. This aligns with cognitive dissonance and moral disengagement theory, wherein individuals resolve tension between convenient actions and established values by broadening permissible boundaries. Conversely, those answering “Yes” (coded as 0) recognize that formal permis- sion establishes baseline acceptability but does not eliminate the need for ethical judgment regarding implementation. They maintain that certain GenAI applications, even when tech- nically allowed, may still warrant scrutiny based on educational objectives and academic integrity considerations. This measure thus reveals students’ tendencies to either simplify ethical frameworks when convenient (rationalization) or maintain nuanced ethical perspec- tives that recognize the responsibilities accompanying technological permissions. Perceived social norms capture the influence of authority figures, such as professors,",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,11,"considerations. This measure thus reveals students’ tendencies to either simplify ethical frameworks when convenient (rationalization) or maintain nuanced ethical perspec- tives that recognize the responsibilities accompanying technological permissions. Perceived social norms capture the influence of authority figures, such as professors, on students’ decisions to use GenAI. This is measured by the question, “Does your professor’s stance on GenAI influence your decision to use it?” A “Yes” response (coded as 1) signi- fies greater sensitivity to social norms, while “No” (coded as 0) indicates more autono- mous decision-making. Perceived risk reflects students’ emotional discomfort or hesitancy in using GenAI, particularly in the context of potential detection. This is assessed with the question, “If GenAI detection tools were used, would you still use GenAI?” A “Yes” response (coded as 0) indicates lower perceived risk, while “No” (coded as 1) suggests higher risk aversion and ethical concern. The overall AI guilt score is calculated by summing rationalization tendency (inverted), perceived social norms, and perceived risk. A higher AI guilt score indicates greater ethical discomfort, a stricter ethical stance, and increased sensitivity to social",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,11,"aversion and ethical concern. The overall AI guilt score is calculated by summing rationalization tendency (inverted), perceived social norms, and perceived risk. A higher AI guilt score indicates greater ethical discomfort, a stricter ethical stance, and increased sensitivity to social expectations, sug- gesting more internal conflict about using GenAI. Control Variables Four control variables were included in the study: GenAI knowledge, level of study, col- lege of study, and the pure/applied field distinction. GenAI knowledge, represented by the variable AI_knowledge, captures students’ familiarity with GenAI tools, based on their response to the question, “I know how to use GenAI in my academic work” (1 = Yes, 0 = No). The variable Year refers to the student’s level of study in the university (Year 1 to Year 4), which is controlled for since familiarity with academic norms, exposure to GenAI tools, and understanding of academic integrity may vary by year. The variable Discipline represents the student’s academic discipline (e.g., 1 = Engineering, 2 = Arts and Social Sciences, 3 = Science, 4 = Business), as different disciplines have varying attitudes toward",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,11,"and understanding of academic integrity may vary by year. The variable Discipline represents the student’s academic discipline (e.g., 1 = Engineering, 2 = Arts and Social Sciences, 3 = Science, 4 = Business), as different disciplines have varying attitudes toward technology. Data Analysis We conducted logistic regression analysis using Stata 16 to investigate the relationships between AI guilt (measured by composite score, rationalization tendency, perceived social norms, and perceived risk) and task type. Separate models were estimated for each psy- chological factor. We also included interaction terms between social norms and rational- ization tendency in our models to examine how compliance-based social influence may",104
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,12,"2098 Y. Qu, J. Wang interact with internalization processes in technology adoption (Venkatesh & Morris, 2000). All variance inflation factor (VIF) values were below 2, with a mean of 1.30, suggesting no issues with multicollinearity. The Hosmer–Lemeshow goodness-of-fit tests for both the creativity-based and routine-based task models confirmed that the models were well-spec- ified and that the predictors sufficiently explained the likelihood of GenAI usage for both task types. Following Biglan’s (1973) classification, we coded academic disciplines at the college/ school level into pure (0) and applied (1) fields for heterogeneous analysis. In our sample, pure fields include disciplines from Arts and Social Sciences (e.g., psychology, sociology, literature) and theoretical Sciences (e.g., mathematics, physics), which emphasize theoreti- cal knowledge and fundamental understanding. Applied fields include Engineering and Business disciplines, as well as applied Sciences (e.g., computer science, biotechnology), which focus on practical applications and industry-relevant skills. This classification aligns with traditional undergraduate program structures where disciplinary boundaries are more clearly defined at the college level. While some disciplines within colleges may have vary- ing degrees of applied or theoretical focus,",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,12,"on practical applications and industry-relevant skills. This classification aligns with traditional undergraduate program structures where disciplinary boundaries are more clearly defined at the college level. While some disciplines within colleges may have vary- ing degrees of applied or theoretical focus, we adopted this college-level coding approach for analytical clarity and consistency with institutional structures. This broad categoriza- tion, while potentially a limitation as it may not capture nuanced variations within colleges, allows us to test systematic differences across major academic units. Results The sample has 193 respondents, with 92 (47.7%) in pure fields and 101 (52.3%) in applied fields (Table 1). Overall, ChatGPT usage for creativity-based tasks is high (79.3%, SD = 0.406), with a greater prevalence in applied fields (87.1%) compared to pure fields (70.7%). Usage for routine-based tasks is lower overall (65.3%, SD = 0.477), though it follows a simi- lar pattern, with higher adoption in applied fields (72.3%) relative to pure fields (57.6%). Regarding AI guilt-related attitudes, rationalization tendencies, reflecting ethical justi- fication for GenAI use (Schwartz, 2016), are consistent across pure (75.0%) and applied Table 1",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,12,"a simi- lar pattern, with higher adoption in applied fields (72.3%) relative to pure fields (57.6%). Regarding AI guilt-related attitudes, rationalization tendencies, reflecting ethical justi- fication for GenAI use (Schwartz, 2016), are consistent across pure (75.0%) and applied Table 1 Descriptive statistics for the full sample and stratified pure-applied subsamples Variable All sample (N = 193) Pure (N = 92) Applied (N = 101) Mean Std Dev Min (Max) Mean Std Dev Min (Max) Mean Std Dev Min (Max) Creativity_task 0.793 0.406 0(1) 0.707 0.458 0(1) 0.871 0.337 0(1) Routine_task 0.653 0.477 0(1) 0.576 0.497 0(1) 0.723 0.450 0(1) Rationalization_ten- dency 0.746 0.436 0(1) 0.75 0.435 0(1) 0.743 0.439 0(1) Perceived_social_ norm 0.591 0.493 0(1) 0.554 0.500 0(1) 0.624 0.487 0(1) Perceived_risk 0.378 0.486 0(1) 0.467 0.502 0(1) 0.297 0.459 0(1) AI_guilt 1.223 0.911 0(3) 1.272 0.891 0(3) 1.178 0.932 0(3) AI_knowledge 0.943 0.232 0(1) 0.891 0.313 0(1) 0.990 0.010 0(1) Year 2.684 1.245 1(4) 2.826 1.246 1(4) 2.554 1.237 1(4) Discipline 2.135 1.081 1(4) 2.272 0.447 2(3) 2.010 1.425 1(4)",171
