A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,16,"2102 Y. Qu, J. Wang This task-specific response demonstrates that AI guilt functions as a context-dependent moral emotion rather than a static trait, aligning with Tangney and Dearing’s (2003) theo- retical framework, which differentiates guilt from shame by emphasizing guilt as a self- conscious emotion tied to specific behaviors rather than a global self-evaluation. The dif- ferential effect across task types reveals that creativity-based tasks, which engage with core academic values of intellectual development and originality, trigger stronger moral recognition. These findings extend previous research by establishing that students’ ethical boundaries with GenAI use are systematically influenced by task complexity and perceived educational value (Andersen et al., 2024). In the stratified analysis by pure-applied fields, we found that AI guilt affects creativity- based tasks differently across disciplines (H2a), with a linear relationship in pure fields and a nonlinear pattern in applied fields (H2b). These disciplinary differences align with Andersen et al. (2024), who found that attitudes toward GenAI use vary by discipline, with researchers from technical and medical sciences, as well as quantitative social sciences and experimental natural sciences, generally",180
A111,A111.pdf,10.1007/s10805-025-09643-x,16,"applied fields (H2b). These disciplinary differences align with Andersen et al. (2024), who found that attitudes toward GenAI use vary by discipline, with researchers from technical and medical sciences, as well as quantitative social sciences and experimental natural sciences, generally more positive about using GenAI in their work. As discussed in the literature review, these differences likely reflect the distinct academic orientations of pure and applied fields, shaped by their disciplinary knowledge structures, paradigms, and the influence of disciplinary pressure and alignment. Our findings further provide more specific insights into how these differences manifest, particularly the linear and nonlinear patterns. The linear relationship in pure fields may stem from deeply internalized values around academic honesty and personal achievement. Table 5 Logistic regression results for creativity-based task engagement in pure vs. applied fields Effect Creativity-based task Pure Applied Perceived_social_norm 1.436 (1.048) 3.349** (1.458) Rationalization_tendency 2.194** (0.972) −1.785 (1.160) Perceived_social_norm * Ration- alization_tendency −2.047 (1.269) 0 (omitted) Perceived_risk −1.041* (0.579) −1.243 (0.762) AI_knowledge 3.457*** (1.148) 0 (omitted) - Discipline 4: Business −0.472 (0.895) - Discipline 3: Science −0.723 (0.629) Year (ref: Year",180
