A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,1,"EDITED BY Bilal Üstün, Nevsehir University, Türkiye REVIEWED BY Eric Best, University at Albany, United States Yaprak Alagöz Hamzaj, Anadolu University, Türkiye *CORRESPONDENCE Paula Enriquez penriqu6@asu.edu Hsin-Yu Lin hlin176@asu.edu RECEIVED 16 February 2026 REVISED 17 April 2026 ACCEPTED 08 May 2026 PUBLISHED 25 June 2026 CITATION Enriquez P, Lin H-Y and Baciu C (2026) Artiﬁcial intelligence in higher education: student use, perceived beneﬁts, and emerging risks. Front. Educ. 11:1812390. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Enriquez, Lin and Baciu. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Artiﬁcial intelligence in higher education: student use, perceived beneﬁts, and emerging risks Paula Enriquez*, Hsin-Yu Lin* and Cristina Baciu W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States Introduction: Artiﬁcial intelligence",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,1,"not comply with these terms. Artiﬁcial intelligence in higher education: student use, perceived beneﬁts, and emerging risks Paula Enriquez*, Hsin-Yu Lin* and Cristina Baciu W. P. Carey School of Business, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, United States Introduction: Artiﬁcial intelligence (AI) tools, including large language models and automated writing systems, are rapidly transforming how students engage with academic work in higher education. While institutional discussions have primarily focused on academic integrity and technological capability, comparatively less empirical research has examined students’ perspectives on how AI shapes learning, decision-making, and academic expectations. This study investigates (1) how university students use AI in academic work, (2) how they perceive its beneﬁts and risks, and (3) how institutional policy ambiguity inﬂuences their educational experiences. Methods: A convergent mixed-methods design was employed using survey responses from 467 undergraduate and graduate students at a large public research university, supplemented by student focus groups and faculty interviews. Quantitative analyses included descriptive statistics and Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models to estimate the prevalence of AI use and assess variation in perceived dependence, policy clarity, and integrity-related anxiety",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,1,"public research university, supplemented by student focus groups and faculty interviews. Quantitative analyses included descriptive statistics and Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models to estimate the prevalence of AI use and assess variation in perceived dependence, policy clarity, and integrity-related anxiety across student characteristics. Qualitative analyses were conducted to provide contextual insight into how students and faculty interpret AI use within evolving norms of teaching, learning, and assessment in higher education. Results: AI use was widespread, with most students reporting at least weekly use for studying, researching topics, and writing-related tasks. Students generally perceived AI as improving efﬁciency, accessibility, and academic support. However, many also expressed concerns regarding over-reliance, diminished critical engagement, and uncertainty about acceptable academic use. Nearly half of respondents reported an unclear understanding of institutional AI-use policies, and integrity-related anxiety was common even among students who did not report intentional misuse. Bayesian model results indicated that these concerns varied systematically by academic level and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort, suggesting important equity-related differences in how students experience AI within the same institutional environment. Discussion: These ﬁndings suggest that AI integration",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,1,"report intentional misuse. Bayesian model results indicated that these concerns varied systematically by academic level and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort, suggesting important equity-related differences in how students experience AI within the same institutional environment. Discussion: These ﬁndings suggest that AI integration in higher education is not solely a technological issue, but also a pedagogical and governance challenge. By foregrounding student perspectives and integrating quantitative and qualitative evidence, this study highlights the importance of clear policy communication, intentional assessment design, and AI literacy initiatives to support equitable and responsible AI use in higher education. KEYWORDS academic integrity anxiety, artiﬁcial intelligence, Bayesian hierarchical model, digital ethics, educational technology adoption, institutional policy ambiguity, large language model, technology mediated learning TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 25 June 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",129
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"1 Introduction Artiﬁcial intelligence (AI) tools such as large language models, automated writing assistants, and AI-based tutoring systems are increasingly integrated into higher education. As these tools become embedded in students’ academic work, universities face growing questions about how AI shapes learning, decision- making, and academic expectations. While institutional discourse has largely focused on academic integrity, assessment validity, and technological capability (Cotton et al., 2023), comparatively less empirical research has centered students’ perspectives on how AI is actually used and experienced within higher education settings. Understanding how students perceive the beneﬁts, risks, and boundaries of AI use is essential for informing institutional policies, instructional design, and student support in an era of rapid technological change. An emerging body of research suggests that AI technologies can enhance learning efﬁciency, accessibility, and personalization when used appropriately (Holmes et al., 2019; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). At the same time, scholars have raised concerns about over-reliance on automated systems, potential erosion of critical thinking, and increasing ambiguity around what constitutes legitimate academic work (Kasneci et al., 2023; Perkins, 2023). These competing perspectives underscore a",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"al., 2019). At the same time, scholars have raised concerns about over-reliance on automated systems, potential erosion of critical thinking, and increasing ambiguity around what constitutes legitimate academic work (Kasneci et al., 2023; Perkins, 2023). These competing perspectives underscore a central tension: AI tools may simultaneously support learning and introduce new cognitive, ethical, and pedagogical challenges, depending on how they are regulated and integrated into instructional contexts. Recent empirical work has begun to document how students engage with generative AI tools in academic settings, highlighting both opportunities for learning support and emerging concerns related to over-reliance, authorship, and assessment practices (e.g., Dwivedi et al., 2023; Rudolph et al., 2023). At the same time, institutional responses to AI remain fragmented, with varying levels of policy development and enforcement across institutions and disciplines. Despite growing scholarly attention, much of the existing literature remains institution- or faculty-centered, emphasizing policy development, instructional design, or technological capability (Luckin et al., 2016; Selwyn, 2022). Comparatively less empirical work foregrounds the student perspective—how students themselves use AI tools, how they interpret their beneﬁts and risks, and how",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"remains institution- or faculty-centered, emphasizing policy development, instructional design, or technological capability (Luckin et al., 2016; Selwyn, 2022). Comparatively less empirical work foregrounds the student perspective—how students themselves use AI tools, how they interpret their beneﬁts and risks, and how they navigate ethical uncertainty in the absence of consistent guidance. This omission is consequential. Students are not passive recipients of AI policy; rather, they are active agents whose behaviors, beliefs, and anxieties shape how AI is used in practice across courses and disciplines. Recent studies indicate that students frequently experience uncertainty regarding acceptable AI use, particularly when institutional or course-level policies are unclear or inconsistently communicated (Foster and Rucker, 2024). Such ambiguity may generate anxiety about unintentional misconduct, discourage responsible experimentation, and contribute to uneven adoption across student groups. Moreover, as AI tools increasingly inﬂuence how students study, write, and prepare for assessments, they may also reshape academic identities and norms of effort—issues that remain underexplored in empirical research focused on higher education contexts. The present study addresses these gaps by examining university students’ experiences with AI-enabled tools through a",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"prepare for assessments, they may also reshape academic identities and norms of effort—issues that remain underexplored in empirical research focused on higher education contexts. The present study addresses these gaps by examining university students’ experiences with AI-enabled tools through a convergent mixed-methods design that integrates survey data with qualitative insights from student focus groups and faculty interviews. Quantitative analyses combine descriptive summaries with Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models to examine patterns of AI use, perceived dependence, clarity of institutional guidance, and integrity-related anxiety across student characteristics. Qualitative analyses provide contextual depth, capturing how students and faculty interpret AI use within evolving norms of teaching, assessment, and governance in higher education. The conceptual model guiding this study is informed by frameworks in technology adoption and digital ethics, which emphasize that user behavior is shaped not only by access and perceived usefulness, but also by clarity of norms and expectations. In this context, institutional policy ambiguity is positioned as a central explanatory mechanism because it directly inﬂuences how students interpret acceptable use, manage risk, and regulate their engagement with AI tools. When",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"but also by clarity of norms and expectations. In this context, institutional policy ambiguity is positioned as a central explanatory mechanism because it directly inﬂuences how students interpret acceptable use, manage risk, and regulate their engagement with AI tools. When expectations are unclear, students must rely on individual judgment, increasing variability in behavior and the likelihood of anxiety. By foregrounding student perspectives and integrating model-based evidence with qualitative insights, this study contributes to the literature on AI in higher education in three key ways. First, it provides empirical estimates of the prevalence of AI use and associated concerns from the student viewpoint. Second, it identiﬁes institutional policy ambiguity— rather than misuse alone—as a central driver of integrity- related anxiety. Third, it highlights equity-relevant differences in how students experience AI, underscoring the need for institutional responses that move beyond one-size-ﬁts-all approaches. Together, these ﬁndings position AI integration in higher education not merely as a technological innovation, but as a pedagogical and governance challenge requiring intentional, student-informed responses. To clarify the relationships examined in this study, Figure 1 presents a conceptual model",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"Together, these ﬁndings position AI integration in higher education not merely as a technological innovation, but as a pedagogical and governance challenge requiring intentional, student-informed responses. To clarify the relationships examined in this study, Figure 1 presents a conceptual model illustrating how the availability of AI tools, patterns of student use, and institutional policy clarity interact to shape perceived beneﬁts, integrity-related anxiety, and learning implications in higher education. The model highlights policy ambiguity as a key contextual factor inﬂuencing student experiences, with these relationships further shaped by student characteristics such as academic level and ﬁnancial comfort. This framework guided both the quantitative and qualitative components of the study and provides a foundation for interpreting the results that follow. 2 Methods 2.1 Study design This study employed a convergent mixed-methods design to examine university students’ experiences with artiﬁcial intelligence (AI) in academic settings. Quantitative survey data were used to estimate the prevalence of AI use and related perceptions, while qualitative data from student focus groups Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",173
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,2,"academic settings. Quantitative survey data were used to estimate the prevalence of AI use and related perceptions, while qualitative data from student focus groups Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",33
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,3,"and faculty interviews provided contextual depth and interpretive insight. The two strands were collected and analyzed in parallel and integrated during interpretation to develop a comprehensive understanding of how students experience AI use within higher education. The study was guided by the conceptual framework shown in Figure 1, which illustrates how AI availability, student use, and institutional policy clarity interact to shape perceived beneﬁts, integrity-related anxiety, and learning implications. 2.2 Participants and institutional context Participants were undergraduate and graduate students enrolled at a large public research university in the United States. Eligibility criteria required participants to be at least 18 years old and currently enrolled in a degree-granting program. Students from all academic disciplines were eligible to participate, including both STEM and non-STEM ﬁelds. A total of 467 students completed the survey and were included in the ﬁnal analytic sample. Participants were recruited through university-afﬁliated email distributions, course-level announcements, and digital ﬂyers shared via institutional communication channels. Participation was voluntary, and no course credit or monetary compensation was provided. All study procedures were approved by the university’s Institutional Review Board",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,3,"Participants were recruited through university-afﬁliated email distributions, course-level announcements, and digital ﬂyers shared via institutional communication channels. Participation was voluntary, and no course credit or monetary compensation was provided. All study procedures were approved by the university’s Institutional Review Board (IRB). At the time of data collection, the university did not have a single centralized policy governing the use of generative AI tools. Instead, guidance regarding AI use was typically provided at the course or instructor level, resulting in variation across disciplines and classes. Students had access to widely available AI tools, including publicly accessible large language models and AI-assisted features embedded in standard software platforms (e.g., word processing and productivity tools). This decentralized approach to AI governance likely contributed to variability in expectations and shaped students’ perceptions of policy clarity. In practice, expectations regarding AI use were often deﬁned at the instructor level, leading to inconsistency across courses and contributing to student uncertainty 2.3 Data collection procedures 2.3.1 Survey instrument Quantitative data were collected using an anonymous online survey administered via Qualtrics. The survey included items assessing: ▪Frequency of",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,3,"at the instructor level, leading to inconsistency across courses and contributing to student uncertainty 2.3 Data collection procedures 2.3.1 Survey instrument Quantitative data were collected using an anonymous online survey administered via Qualtrics. The survey included items assessing: ▪Frequency of AI tool use ▪Academic purposes for AI use (e.g., studying, research, writing) ▪Perceived learning enhancement vs. dependence ▪Clarity of institutional or course-level AI-use policies ▪Concerns related to academic integrity and unintentional misconduct Most perception-based items used ﬁve-point Likert-type response scales. For selected analyses, responses were dichotomized to create binary outcomes aligned with the Bayesian modeling framework (e.g., frequent vs. infrequent use; clear vs. unclear policy understanding). Demographic measures included academic level, ﬁeld of study, enrollment modality, and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort. The survey required approximately 10–15 min to complete. Key constructs were measured using multi-item indicators. For example, perceived dependence was assessed using Likert- scale items capturing concerns about over-reliance and reduced FIGURE 1 Conceptual model illustrating how AI availability, student use, and institutional policy ambiguity interact to shape perceived beneﬁts, integrity-related anxiety, and learning and equity implications in higher education.",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,3,"assessed using Likert- scale items capturing concerns about over-reliance and reduced FIGURE 1 Conceptual model illustrating how AI availability, student use, and institutional policy ambiguity interact to shape perceived beneﬁts, integrity-related anxiety, and learning and equity implications in higher education. Relationships are moderated by academic level and ﬁnancial comfort. Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",58
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,4,"independent thinking. Policy clarity was measured through items assessing students’ understanding of institutional and course-level expectations. Integrity-related anxiety was measured using items capturing concern about unintentional misconduct. Example items included: “I am unsure when AI use is allowed in my coursework” and “I worry about being accused of misconduct when using AI tools.” 2.3.2 Qualitative data collection To complement survey ﬁndings, three student focus groups were conducted, each consisting of 6–8 participants (total n = 21). Focus groups were conducted virtually and lasted approximately 45–60 min. Discussion prompts explored students’ motivations for using AI, perceived beneﬁts and risks, experiences with policy ambiguity, and expectations for institutional guidance. In addition, four semi-structured faculty interviews were conducted with instructors involved in teaching or curriculum development across business and technology-related disciplines. Faculty interviews focused on assessment design, academic integrity concerns, and institutional responses to AI integration. All qualitative sessions were audio-recorded with participant consent and transcribed verbatim for analysis. 2.4 Quantitative data analysis Quantitative analyses proceeded in two stages. First, descriptive statistics were used to summarize AI use patterns and student perceptions across",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,4,"to AI integration. All qualitative sessions were audio-recorded with participant consent and transcribed verbatim for analysis. 2.4 Quantitative data analysis Quantitative analyses proceeded in two stages. First, descriptive statistics were used to summarize AI use patterns and student perceptions across the full sample. Frequencies and percentages are reported to provide an overview of students’ experiences. Second, Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models were estimated for selected binary outcomes, including: 1. Frequent AI use (weekly or more vs. less frequent) 2. Perceived dependence on AI 3. Unclear understanding of AI-use policies 4. Integrity-related anxiety These models were used to examine variation across student characteristics, including academic level and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort, while accounting for unobserved heterogeneity across respondents. Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression was selected for several reasons. First, the primary outcomes of interest are binary and inﬂuenced by multiple student-level characteristics, making logistic regression an appropriate modeling framework. Second, the Bayesian approach provides direct probabilistic interpretations of parameter estimates and allows for more intuitive communication of uncertainty through credible intervals, which is particularly useful for policy-relevant educational research. Third, hierarchical modeling allows",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,4,"logistic regression an appropriate modeling framework. Second, the Bayesian approach provides direct probabilistic interpretations of parameter estimates and allows for more intuitive communication of uncertainty through credible intervals, which is particularly useful for policy-relevant educational research. Third, hierarchical modeling allows for the incorporation of unobserved heterogeneity across respondents. Compared to simpler approaches such as standard logistic regression or mean comparisons, the Bayesian framework provides greater ﬂexibility in estimating subgroup differences and more stable estimates for moderate sample sizes. All models were estimated using [e.g., R (brms package) and SAS PROC MCMC]. Predictors were selected based on theoretical relevance and prior literature, including academic level and ﬁnancial comfort. Model convergence was assessed using standard diagnostic procedures, including trace plots and convergence statistics consistent with recommended Bayesian diagnostic practices (Vehtari et al., 2021). Bayesian modeling was selected to provide interpretable probability estimates and to avoid reliance on null-hypothesis signiﬁcance testing, which is less informative for policy-relevant educational research (Gelman et al., 2020). 2.5 Qualitative data analysis Qualitative data were analyzed using thematic analysis. Transcripts were reviewed iteratively by members of the research",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,4,"and to avoid reliance on null-hypothesis signiﬁcance testing, which is less informative for policy-relevant educational research (Gelman et al., 2020). 2.5 Qualitative data analysis Qualitative data were analyzed using thematic analysis. Transcripts were reviewed iteratively by members of the research team to identify recurring themes related to efﬁciency, dependence, ethical uncertainty, and institutional responsibility. Initial codes were discussed and reﬁned through team review to enhance analytic credibility and consistency. Representative quotations were selected to illustrate key themes. Integration of quantitative and qualitative ﬁndings occurred during interpretation, with qualitative insights used to contextualize and explain patterns observed in the survey and Bayesian model results. 2.6 Ethical considerations All study procedures complied with institutional ethical standards for research involving human subjects. Participation was voluntary, and informed consent was obtained electronically from survey participants and prior to qualitative sessions for focus group and interview participants. All data were de- identiﬁed prior to analysis. Participants were permitted to skip any question or withdraw from the study at any time without penalty. Data were stored securely on institutionally approved platforms accessible only to the research",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,4,"interview participants. All data were de- identiﬁed prior to analysis. Participants were permitted to skip any question or withdraw from the study at any time without penalty. Data were stored securely on institutionally approved platforms accessible only to the research team. 3 Results Results are presented in two parts. First, descriptive statistics summarize patterns of AI use and student perceptions across the full sample. Second, Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression results are used to examine variation in key binary outcomes across student characteristics, including academic level and self- reported ﬁnancial comfort. Together, these analyses provide both prevalence estimates and model-based evidence to support interpretation. 3.1 Frequency and purposes of AI use AI-enabled tools were widely adopted among university students. Of the 467 respondents, 68% reported using AI tools at least weekly for academic purposes, and 31% reported daily use. These ﬁndings indicate that AI engagement has become a regular component of students’ academic workﬂows rather than Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",164
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,5,"an occasional supplement. Sample characteristics are summarized in Table 1. As illustrated in Figure 2, daily AI use is common across the sample. With respect to purpose, AI tools were most frequently used for studying for exams, researching topics, and drafting or reﬁning written assignments, while fewer students reported using AI for problem-solving tasks such as coding or quantitative analysis. Figure 2 summarizes these academic purposes, highlighting AI’s role as a multipurpose academic support tool rather than a narrowly task-speciﬁc resource. Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression results further indicate that graduate students and students reporting greater ﬁnancial comfort have higher posterior probabilities of frequent AI use compared to undergraduates and students reporting lower ﬁnancial comfort. Qualitative ﬁndings support and contextualize these patterns. Students frequently described AI as a routine academic resource, emphasizing its role in supporting efﬁciency and managing workload, which aligns with the high prevalence of reported use in the survey data. 3.2 Perceived learning enhancement versus dependence Students’ evaluations of AI’s impact on learning reﬂect both perceived beneﬁts and persistent concerns. Overall, 72% of respondents reported that AI tools",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,5,"aligns with the high prevalence of reported use in the survey data. 3.2 Perceived learning enhancement versus dependence Students’ evaluations of AI’s impact on learning reﬂect both perceived beneﬁts and persistent concerns. Overall, 72% of respondents reported that AI tools improve efﬁciency or understanding of course material. At the same time, 46% expressed concern that frequent AI use could reduce independent thinking or deep engagement with learning. As shown in Figure 3, responses to the enhancement-vs.- dependence scale clustered around intermediate categories, indicating that students commonly view AI as simultaneously helpful and potentially problematic. While relatively few respondents characterized AI as fostering complete dependence, a substantial proportion expressed ambivalence about the long- term implications of frequent AI use. Bayesian model results indicate that students reporting daily AI use have higher posterior probabilities of reporting dependence concerns compared to less frequent users, suggesting that increased engagement with AI does not eliminate anxiety about its cognitive effects. Qualitative data provide further insight into this ambivalence. Students often described AI as beneﬁcial for efﬁciency while simultaneously expressing concern that reliance on AI may",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,5,"users, suggesting that increased engagement with AI does not eliminate anxiety about its cognitive effects. Qualitative data provide further insight into this ambivalence. Students often described AI as beneﬁcial for efﬁciency while simultaneously expressing concern that reliance on AI may reduce opportunities for independent problem-solving, illustrating how perceived beneﬁts and risks coexist in practice. 3.3 Ethical concerns and policy clarity Concerns related to academic integrity and institutional guidance emerged as a prominent theme across survey responses. Speciﬁcally, 48% of respondents reported unclear understanding of institutional or course-level expectations regarding acceptable AI use. In addition, 44% reported concern about being accused of academic misconduct, even when they did not believe they were violating policies. TABLE 1 Demographic and academic characteristics of the study sample (N = 467). Characteristic Value* Sample size 467 Undergraduate students (%) 67.7 Graduate students (%) 28.1 STEM majors (%) 24.6 Non-STEM majors (%) 75.4 Financially secure (%) 31.9 Sometimes worried about ﬁnances (%) 53.3 Often worried about ﬁnances (%) 12.0 *Percentages may not sum to 100% due to nonresponse to individual questions. FIGURE 2 Student use of",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,5,"majors (%) 24.6 Non-STEM majors (%) 75.4 Financially secure (%) 31.9 Sometimes worried about ﬁnances (%) 53.3 Often worried about ﬁnances (%) 12.0 *Percentages may not sum to 100% due to nonresponse to individual questions. FIGURE 2 Student use of AI-enabled tools for academic purposes. (A) shows the percentage of students reporting daily vs. less frequent AI use. (B) shows the academic purposes for which students report using AI-enabled tools, including studying for exams, researching topics, writing-related tasks, and problem-solving activities. Percentages in Panel B exceed 100% because respondents could select multiple purposes (N = 467). Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",105
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,6,"As summarized in Figure 4, integrity-related anxiety was common across levels of AI use, indicating that concern stems less from intentional misconduct and more from uncertainty surrounding institutional expectations. Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression results show that unclear policy understanding is strongly associated with higher posterior probabilities of integrity-related anxiety, particularly among undergraduates and students reporting lower ﬁnancial comfort. Qualitative ﬁndings help explain these patterns by illustrating how students experience policy ambiguity in practice. Participants frequently reported inconsistent expectations across courses and uncertainty about acceptable AI use, which contributed directly to feelings of anxiety about potential misconduct. 3.4 Variation across student characteristics Subgroup analyses revealed meaningful variation in AI-related experiences across student characteristics. Differences were observed by academic level and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort, with some groups reporting higher reliance on AI tools and greater concern about policy ambiguity and integrity-related consequences than others. Across Bayesian models, academic level and ﬁnancial comfort consistently emerged as predictors of AI use patterns, perceived dependence, and integrity-related anxiety. These results suggest that students’ experiences with AI are shaped not only by individual preferences, but also",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,6,"than others. Across Bayesian models, academic level and ﬁnancial comfort consistently emerged as predictors of AI use patterns, perceived dependence, and integrity-related anxiety. These results suggest that students’ experiences with AI are shaped not only by individual preferences, but also by their academic context and perceived risk within the institution. As shown in Figure 5A, graduate students and ﬁnancially secure students exhibit higher predicted probabilities of frequent AI use. Figure 5B shows substantially higher predicted probabilities of integrity-related anxiety among students reporting unclear policy guidance, particularly undergraduates. Model-based results from Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression analyses are summarized in Table 2. Qualitative evidence further supports these subgroup differences, with students reporting varying levels of conﬁdence in navigating AI use depending on their academic experience and perceived familiarity with institutional expectations. 3.5 Qualitative insights: student and faculty perspectives Qualitative ﬁndings from student focus groups provided additional context for the survey and model-based results. Students frequently described AI as a “support system” that helps clarify concepts, streamline studying, and manage workload. At the same time, participants expressed concern about becoming overly reliant on",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,6,"focus groups provided additional context for the survey and model-based results. Students frequently described AI as a “support system” that helps clarify concepts, streamline studying, and manage workload. At the same time, participants expressed concern about becoming overly reliant on AI and about unintentionally crossing boundaries of acceptable use, particularly for graded assignments. Faculty interviews echoed these concerns while emphasizing instructional and assessment-related challenges. Faculty participants described difﬁculty distinguishing between student- generated and AI-assisted work and highlighted the need to rethink assessment design rather than relying solely on detection or prohibition. Together, these qualitative insights reinforce the quantitative ﬁndings and illustrate the complex trade-offs students and instructors navigate as AI becomes embedded in academic practice. Qualitative themes and representative quotations from student focus groups and faculty interviews are summarized in Table 3. These qualitative insights help explain the quantitative ﬁnding that policy ambiguity is strongly associated with integrity-related anxiety, illustrating how students interpret and experience uncertainty in practice. FIGURE 3 Student perceptions of artiﬁcial intelligence’s impact on learning, showing the percentage of students who report perceived learning enhancement and concern",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,6,"that policy ambiguity is strongly associated with integrity-related anxiety, illustrating how students interpret and experience uncertainty in practice. FIGURE 3 Student perceptions of artiﬁcial intelligence’s impact on learning, showing the percentage of students who report perceived learning enhancement and concern about dependence (N = 467). Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",54
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,7,"4 Discussion and implications 4.1 Interpreting student experiences with AI in academic work The ﬁndings of this study indicate that AI-enabled tools have moved beyond novelty and are now embedded in students’ everyday academic practices. Descriptive results show that a substantial proportion of students use AI regularly for studying, research, and writing, conﬁrming that generative technologies are shaping how academic work is conducted rather than merely supplementing existing practices. Bayesian model results further demonstrate that patterns of AI use vary systematically by academic level and ﬁnancial comfort, suggesting that engagement with AI reﬂects students’ institutional position and perceived access rather than uniform adoption. At the same time, students’ responses reveal a persistent ambivalence toward AI use. Although most students perceive AI as enhancing efﬁciency and accessibility, nearly half express concern about over-reliance and reduced critical engagement. FIGURE 4 Percentage of students reporting unclear understanding of institutional AI-use policies and concern about unintentional academic misconduct (N = 467). FIGURE 5 Predicted probabilities from Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models. (A) shows the predicted probability of frequent AI use (weekly or more) by",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,7,students reporting unclear understanding of institutional AI-use policies and concern about unintentional academic misconduct (N = 467). FIGURE 5 Predicted probabilities from Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models. (A) shows the predicted probability of frequent AI use (weekly or more) by academic level and self-reported ﬁnancial comfort. (B) shows the predicted probability of integrity-related anxiety by institutional policy clarity and academic level. Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org,70
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,8,"Bayesian results indicate that frequent AI users have higher posterior probabilities of reporting dependence concerns, highlighting that increased use does not resolve anxiety about AI’s cognitive implications. Qualitative ﬁndings provide important explanatory context for this pattern, illustrating how students simultaneously rely on AI for efﬁciency while expressing concern about diminished opportunities for independent thinking. Together, these results suggest that students are not simply adopting AI uncritically, but are actively negotiating its role in their learning processes. This dual perception challenges narratives that frame AI as either unequivocally beneﬁcial or inherently harmful and instead suggests that student experiences with AI are characterized by tension between efﬁciency and authenticity. 4.2 Policy ambiguity as a central source of anxiety One of the most consequential ﬁndings of this study is the role of institutional policy ambiguity in shaping students’ experiences with AI. Nearly half of respondents reported unclear understanding of acceptable AI use, and a substantial proportion expressed concern about being accused of academic misconduct despite not intentionally violating policies. Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression results show a strong association between unclear policy understanding and",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,8,"of respondents reported unclear understanding of acceptable AI use, and a substantial proportion expressed concern about being accused of academic misconduct despite not intentionally violating policies. Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression results show a strong association between unclear policy understanding and integrity-related anxiety, particularly among undergraduates and students reporting lower ﬁnancial comfort. Qualitative ﬁndings reinforce and clarify this relationship by demonstrating how policy ambiguity is experienced in practice. Students frequently described inconsistent expectations across courses and uncertainty about acceptable use, indicating that anxiety arises not from deliberate misconduct, but from the need to interpret unclear or conﬂicting guidance. These ﬁndings extend prior work on academic integrity by reframing student anxiety as a function of institutional communication gaps rather than student intent or misconduct (Bretag et al., 2019). When expectations are implicit or inconsistently communicated across courses, students are required to infer boundaries independently, increasing stress and uncertainty. Importantly, these ﬁndings should be interpreted within the context of the studied institution, where AI policies were decentralized and often deﬁned at the instructor level. Similar patterns may arise in institutions with comparable governance",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,8,"boundaries independently, increasing stress and uncertainty. Importantly, these ﬁndings should be interpreted within the context of the studied institution, where AI policies were decentralized and often deﬁned at the instructor level. Similar patterns may arise in institutions with comparable governance structures, but broader generalization should be made with caution. From a higher education perspective, these results suggest that reactive approaches focused on detection or punishment are unlikely to address the root causes of integrity- related concern in the context of AI. 4.3 Equity and differential experiences with AI The results also highlight important equity-relevant differences in how students experience AI within the same institutional environment. Bayesian models indicate that students reporting lower ﬁnancial comfort have higher posterior probabilities of unclear policy understanding and integrity- related anxiety, even when accounting for academic level. These ﬁndings suggest that uncertainty surrounding AI use may disproportionately affect students with greater perceived academic risk or fewer resources. Qualitative insights provide further evidence of these differences, with some students describing greater uncertainty in navigating expectations and lower conﬁdence in interpreting TABLE 2 Posterior odds ratios (OR)",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,8,"may disproportionately affect students with greater perceived academic risk or fewer resources. Qualitative insights provide further evidence of these differences, with some students describing greater uncertainty in navigating expectations and lower conﬁdence in interpreting TABLE 2 Posterior odds ratios (OR) and 95% credible intervals (CrI) from Bayesian hierarchical logistic regression models examining student AI use and related perceptions. Outcome (binary) Predictor Posterior OR 95% CrI Frequent AI use Graduate (vs. undergraduate) 1.60 [1.20, 2.10] Financially secure (vs. often worried) 1.40 [1.10, 1.90] Perceived AI dependence Daily AI use (vs. less frequent) 1.80 [1.30, 2.40] Undergraduate (vs. graduate) 1.35 [1.00, 1.90] Unclear policy understanding Financially insecure (vs. secure) 1.70 [1.20, 2.30] Undergraduate (vs. graduate) 1.50 [1.10, 2.10] Integrity-related anxiety Unclear policy guidance 2.10 [1.60, 2.80] Undergraduate (vs. graduate) 1.45 [1.05, 2.00] OR, odds ratio; CrI, credible interval. Odds ratios greater than 1 indicate higher odds of the outcome. TABLE 3 Qualitative themes identiﬁed from student focus groups and faculty interviews, with representative illustrative quotations. Theme Description Illustrative quotation Ai as an academic support tool AI used to clarify concepts and organize",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,8,"1 indicate higher odds of the outcome. TABLE 3 Qualitative themes identiﬁed from student focus groups and faculty interviews, with representative illustrative quotations. Theme Description Illustrative quotation Ai as an academic support tool AI used to clarify concepts and organize work “It helps me understand what the question is really asking.” (Student) Ambivalence toward dependence Efﬁciency gains with concern about thinking “I worry I’m not thinking as deeply anymore.” (Student) Policy ambiguity and uncertainty Inconsistent or unclear guidance “I’m never really sure where the line is.” (Student) Fear of unintentional misconduct Anxiety about accidental rule violations “I’m more worried about getting in trouble accidentally.” (Student) Assessment and instructional challenges Difﬁculty evaluating AI- assisted work “The challenge is ﬁguring out how to assess learning.” (Faculty) Desire for institutional guidance Need for centralized expectations “Students want rules, not loopholes.” (Faculty) Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",147
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"institutional guidance. This suggests that policy ambiguity may not affect all students equally, but instead interacts with existing differences in academic experience and perceived support. Importantly, these results extend discussions of digital inequality beyond access to technology alone. Even when AI tools are widely available, unequal access to clear guidance, institutional norms, and instructional support may contribute to differential experiences and outcomes. Within the context of the studied institution, where policies were inconsistently communicated across courses, these disparities may be ampliﬁed. Institutions with more centralized or clearly articulated policies may observe different patterns. Without coordinated and transparent policies, AI integration may inadvertently widen existing disparities by privileging students who are more conﬁdent navigating ambiguous expectations. 4.4 Implications for teaching, policy, and institutional practice Taken together, the ﬁndings suggest several actionable implications for higher education institutions. First, institutions should prioritize policy clarity over prohibition. Clear, accessible guidance that articulates acceptable and unacceptable uses of AI —supported by discipline-relevant examples—can reduce integrity-related anxiety and promote responsible engagement. Second, AI integration should move beyond compliance- oriented responses toward pedagogical intentionality. Given that dependence",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"clarity over prohibition. Clear, accessible guidance that articulates acceptable and unacceptable uses of AI —supported by discipline-relevant examples—can reduce integrity-related anxiety and promote responsible engagement. Second, AI integration should move beyond compliance- oriented responses toward pedagogical intentionality. Given that dependence concerns are more prevalent among frequent users, instructional strategies should emphasize reasoning, reﬂection, and process rather than output alone. Faculty development initiatives can support assessment designs that incorporate AI transparently while still requiring critical thinking and original synthesis. Third, the ﬁndings underscore the importance of treating AI literacy as a core academic competency. Instruction that helps students evaluate AI outputs, understand ethical boundaries, and use AI responsibly can mitigate anxiety and support more equitable engagement. Such efforts may be particularly beneﬁcial for undergraduates and students reporting lower ﬁnancial comfort, for whom policy ambiguity appears most consequential. Key ﬁndings and their implications for higher education are summarized in Table 4. 4.5 Contributions and future directions By integrating descriptive evidence, Bayesian hierarchical modeling, and qualitative insights, this study makes three primary contributions to the literature on AI in higher education. First, it",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"implications for higher education are summarized in Table 4. 4.5 Contributions and future directions By integrating descriptive evidence, Bayesian hierarchical modeling, and qualitative insights, this study makes three primary contributions to the literature on AI in higher education. First, it provides model-based estimates of AI use and related concerns from the student perspective. Second, it identiﬁes institutional policy ambiguity—rather than misuse alone—as a central driver of integrity-related anxiety. Third, it highlights systematic equity-relevant differences in how students experience AI, emphasizing the need for differentiated institutional responses. These contributions should be interpreted in light of the study’s institutional context, and future research should examine whether similar patterns emerge in institutions with different policy structures and levels of AI integration. Future research should extend this work across multiple institutional contexts and examine how student perceptions evolve as AI policies and instructional practices mature. Longitudinal designs and discipline-speciﬁc analyses would further clarify how AI integration shapes learning outcomes, academic norms, and equity over time. 5 Limitations and conclusion 5.1 Limitations Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the ﬁndings of this study. First,",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"mature. Longitudinal designs and discipline-speciﬁc analyses would further clarify how AI integration shapes learning outcomes, academic norms, and equity over time. 5 Limitations and conclusion 5.1 Limitations Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the ﬁndings of this study. First, data were collected from a single large public research university, which may limit generalizability to other institutional contexts such as community colleges, private institutions, or universities with more established AI governance frameworks. However, focusing on a single institution also provides a relatively consistent policy and instructional environment, allowing clearer interpretation of how students respond to institutional guidance—or the absence thereof. In particular, the decentralized nature of AI policy at the studied institution, where expectations were often deﬁned at the course or instructor level, may have contributed to the observed patterns of policy ambiguity and student anxiety. As such, these ﬁndings are most directly applicable to institutions with similar governance structures, and caution should be exercised when generalizing to institutions with more centralized or clearly articulated AI policies. Second, the study relies primarily on self-reported survey data, which may be subject",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"are most directly applicable to institutions with similar governance structures, and caution should be exercised when generalizing to institutions with more centralized or clearly articulated AI policies. Second, the study relies primarily on self-reported survey data, which may be subject to recall bias or social desirability effects. Although anonymity was emphasized to encourage candid responses, reported AI use and integrity-related concerns may not fully reﬂect actual behaviors. The convergent mixed- methods design partially mitigates this limitation by triangulating survey ﬁndings with qualitative insights from student focus groups and faculty interviews. Nevertheless, future research could further strengthen validity by incorporating TABLE 4 Summary of Key ﬁndings and implications for higher education. Key ﬁnding Evidence from study Implication for institutions Routine AI use in academic work 68% report weekly use; 31% daily use Acknowledge AI as embedded in learning Ambivalence toward AI impact on learning 72% report efﬁciency gains; 46% dependence concerns Design assessments promoting critical engagement Policy ambiguity drives integrity anxiety 48% unclear guidance; 44% anxiety Provide clear, consistent AI-use policies Equity-related variation in AI experience Differences by academic level &",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,9,"impact on learning 72% report efﬁciency gains; 46% dependence concerns Design assessments promoting critical engagement Policy ambiguity drives integrity anxiety 48% unclear guidance; 44% anxiety Provide clear, consistent AI-use policies Equity-related variation in AI experience Differences by academic level & ﬁnancial comfort Ensure equitable access to guidance and AI literacy Enriquez et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",59
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,10,"behavioral data, such as assignment-level AI use or course-speciﬁc policy adherence. Third, the cross-sectional design captures student perceptions at a single point in time. Given the rapid evolution of AI technologies and institutional responses, students’ attitudes, behaviors, and anxieties are likely to change as policies mature and instructional practices adapt. Longitudinal research would be particularly valuable for examining how policy clarity, AI literacy initiatives, and repeated exposure to AI tools inﬂuence student experiences over time. Finally, while the qualitative component adds important contextual depth, the number of focus groups and faculty interviews was necessarily limited. Although thematic saturation was sufﬁcient to identify recurring patterns, future studies could expand qualitative inquiry across disciplines and institutions to explore how norms and expectations surrounding AI use vary by ﬁeld and instructional culture. 5.2 Conclusion Despite these limitations, this study provides timely and student-centered insight into how artiﬁcial intelligence is experienced within higher education. The ﬁndings demonstrate that AI use is widespread and often perceived as beneﬁcial for efﬁciency and accessibility, yet it is also accompanied by substantial concern related to over-reliance, reduced critical",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,10,"insight into how artiﬁcial intelligence is experienced within higher education. The ﬁndings demonstrate that AI use is widespread and often perceived as beneﬁcial for efﬁciency and accessibility, yet it is also accompanied by substantial concern related to over-reliance, reduced critical engagement, and uncertainty about acceptable use. Importantly, the results indicate that institutional policy ambiguity—rather than intentional misuse—is a central driver of integrity- related anxiety. By integrating descriptive evidence, Bayesian hierarchical modeling, and qualitative perspectives, this study reframes AI integration as a pedagogical and governance challenge rather than a purely technological issue. The combined quantitative and qualitative ﬁndings suggest that students are not seeking unrestricted use of AI, but rather clear expectations, consistent guidance, and instructional support that enable responsible engagement with emerging tools. As AI technologies continue to evolve, higher education institutions face a critical choice. Reactive approaches centered on restriction and enforcement may exacerbate anxiety and inequity, particularly for students with greater perceived academic risk. In contrast, proactive stewardship—through transparent policy communication, intentional assessment design, and the integration of AI literacy into curricula—offers an opportunity to support learning, equity,",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,10,"restriction and enforcement may exacerbate anxiety and inequity, particularly for students with greater perceived academic risk. In contrast, proactive stewardship—through transparent policy communication, intentional assessment design, and the integration of AI literacy into curricula—offers an opportunity to support learning, equity, and academic trust. While these conclusions are grounded in a single institutional context, they highlight broader challenges associated with aligning technological innovation and governance in higher education. Future work should examine how different policy environments shape student experiences and whether increased clarity and consistency can reduce integrity-related anxiety. The evidence presented here suggests that institutions that take a proactive and student-informed approach will be better positioned to harness the beneﬁts of AI while mitigating its risks in an era of rapid technological change. Data availability statement The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article/Supplementary Material, further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Arizona State University Institutional Review Board. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their",180
A114,A114.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1812390,10,"inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by Arizona State University Institutional Review Board. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions PE: Writing – original draft, Methodology, Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition, Software, Validation, Data curation, Visualization, Conceptualization. H-YL: Writing – review & editing, Funding acquisition, Software, Methodology, Writing – original draft, Data curation, Conceptualization, Visualization, Validation. CB: Writing – review & editing, Software, Data curation, Project administration. Funding The author(s) declared that ﬁnancial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Conﬂict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or ﬁnancial relationships that could be construed as a potential conﬂict of interest. Generative AI statement The author(s) declared that generative AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. Used for editing and grammar Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside ﬁgures in this article has been",180
