A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,7,"personal purposes (M = 2.76, SD = 0.98, n = 119) and academic purposes (M = 2.73, SD = 1.01, n = 119). However, the diﬀerences were not statistically signiﬁcant, with t = −0.73, p = 0.467 for personal use, and t = −0.76, p = 0.451 for educational use. These ﬁndings suggest that gender does not play a signiﬁcant role in determining the use of ChatGPT for personal or educational purposes, Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",77
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"Gasaymeh et al. 10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310 TABLE 6 Results of t-tests and descriptive statistics for use of ChatGPT for personal and academic purposes by academic level. Outcome Academic level M SD n t df Use of ChatGPT for personal purposes Undergraduate 2.77 0.97 108 −0.17 132 Postgraduate 2.81 1.12 26 Use of ChatGPT for educational purposes Undergraduate 2.74 1.02 108 −0.36 132 Postgraduate 2.82 1.07 26 as both male and female users exhibit close to “Sometimes” usage patterns. 4.4 Fourth research question: are there statistically signiﬁcant diﬀerences in college of education students’ use of ChatGPT for personal and academic purposes based on their academic level? Students’ responses to using ChatGPT for personal and academic purposes scales were compared, based on educational level, through t-tests (Table 6). The results showed no signiﬁcant diﬀerences between undergraduate and postgraduate students regarding using ChatGPT for personal and educational purposes. Postgraduate students reported slightly higher mean scores than undergraduate students for personal purposes (M = 2.81, SD = 1.12, n = 26) and academic purposes (M = 2.82, SD = 1.07, n = 26). Undergraduate students reported",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"and educational purposes. Postgraduate students reported slightly higher mean scores than undergraduate students for personal purposes (M = 2.81, SD = 1.12, n = 26) and academic purposes (M = 2.82, SD = 1.07, n = 26). Undergraduate students reported slightly lower scores for personal purposes (M = 2.77, SD = 0.97, n = 108) and academic purposes (M = 2.74, SD = 1.02, n = 108). However, the diﬀerences were not statistically signiﬁcant, with t = −0.17, p = 0.866 for personal use, and t = −0.36, p = 0.720 for educational use. These ﬁndings suggest that the academic level (undergraduate or postgraduate) does not play a signiﬁcant role in determining the use of ChatGPT for personal or educational purposes, as both groups exhibit close to “Sometimes” usage patterns. 5 Conclusion and recommendations The current study showed that Jordanian education students use ChatGPT moderately for personal and academic purposes, with personal use slightly surpassing academic use. The most common uses of ChatGPT for personal purposes among students were generating translations and brainstorming creative ideas, while academic use focused on",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"education students use ChatGPT moderately for personal and academic purposes, with personal use slightly surpassing academic use. The most common uses of ChatGPT for personal purposes among students were generating translations and brainstorming creative ideas, while academic use focused on writing prompts, summarization, and brainstorming. Furthermore, the study showed no signiﬁcant diﬀerences in usage based on gender or educational level, indicating uniform students’ use of ChatGPT regardless of gender or academic level. These ﬁndings suggest that, despite ChatGPT’s great potential, it is not yet fully integrated into their routines, likely due to limited familiarity with its features or uncertainty about its applications. ChatGPT has provided several beneﬁts, including fostering creativity, productivity, and academic growth in diverse educational contexts. However, universities should implement training programs to address these gaps and raise awareness of ChatGPT’s capabilities. These programs should guide ChatGPT’s ethical and practical use. In addition, universities should work on incorporating ChatGPT into curricula, mainly for writing courses. Integrating ChatGPT into curricula would encourage students to utilize the tool for brainstorming, drafting, and reﬁning their educational work. Importantly, faculty members are",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"and practical use. In addition, universities should work on incorporating ChatGPT into curricula, mainly for writing courses. Integrating ChatGPT into curricula would encourage students to utilize the tool for brainstorming, drafting, and reﬁning their educational work. Importantly, faculty members are integral to this process, playing a critical role in shaping the use of ChatGPT by students through mentoring their use of ChatGPT-speciﬁc academic applications and conducting periodic research that can track evolving usage patterns to inform policy adjustments and training improvements. From the cultural point of view, the Jordanian students may still rely heavily on traditional methods of learning and assessment, which could limit their exploration of emerging technologies like ChatGPT. In many educational contexts, particularly within more traditional or collectivist cultures such as Jordan, technology adoption may be inﬂuenced by societal norms, institutional expectations, and attitudes toward innovation. Cultural attitudes toward AI, concerns about academic integrity, and uncertainty about data privacy may contribute to students’ cautious adoption of such tools. The lack of clear institutional guidance or supportive peer practices might also hinder more active engagement. Fostering a constructive",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"attitudes toward AI, concerns about academic integrity, and uncertainty about data privacy may contribute to students’ cautious adoption of such tools. The lack of clear institutional guidance or supportive peer practices might also hinder more active engagement. Fostering a constructive community around AI’s ethical and practical use— where students feel safe, informed, and encouraged to experiment with these tools—could play a key role in promoting more meaningful integration in academic and personal contexts. This study provides a foundational understanding that can inform institutional policies, curriculum design, and digital literacy initiatives in Jordanian higher education. As one of the early investigations into ChatGPT use among Jordanian university students, it oﬀers insights that can guide future eﬀorts to bridge the gap between technological potential and actual usage. By highlighting the moderate level of adoption and the cultural factors that may shape students’ attitudes toward AI tools, the study underscores the need for context-sensitive strategies that promote responsible and conﬁdent use of emerging technologies. The current study represents a preliminary investigation to explore students’ use of ChatGPT for personal and educational purposes. Future",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,8,"attitudes toward AI tools, the study underscores the need for context-sensitive strategies that promote responsible and conﬁdent use of emerging technologies. The current study represents a preliminary investigation to explore students’ use of ChatGPT for personal and educational purposes. Future research should employ diﬀerent research methods and data collection tools. For instance, future studies should adopt a mixed research design that includes quantitative and qualitative data to understand students’ use of ChatGPT better. Additionally, future studies should consider collecting more information about postgraduate participants, particularly regarding their educational backgrounds and the student levels they teach. Moreover, future research should be conducted Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",106
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,9,"Gasaymeh et al. 10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310 with participants from diﬀerent academic majors and be more representative regarding gender and educational level. Moreover, future research should consider factors shaping students’ use of emerging technologies, e.g., ChatGPT. 6 Limitation This study has limitations. Some of these limitations were related to the used instrument. The questions used in the instrument were created to examine the students’ use of ChatGPT for personal and academic purposes, listing a limited number of uses. Therefore, some of ChatGPT’s uses that might have been popular among the participants might be misreported. In addition, the questionnaire did not collect the speciﬁc educational levels they teach and the academic disciplines of the postgraduate students’ bachelor’s degrees. In addition, the questionnaire used in this study did not collect information regarding participants’ prior experience with ChatGPT (e.g., duration or frequency of use). Lack of such information might limit the depth of analysis. Furthermore, most of the participants completing the questionnaire were female and undergraduate students. Such homogeneity in participants’ majors may inﬂuence the generalizability of the ﬁnding. Data availability statement The raw data supporting",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,9,"such information might limit the depth of analysis. Furthermore, most of the participants completing the questionnaire were female and undergraduate students. Such homogeneity in participants’ majors may inﬂuence the generalizability of the ﬁnding. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement Ethical review and approval was not required for the study on human participants in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. Participation in the study was voluntary and written informed consent from the participants was not required in accordance with the national legislation and the institutional requirements. Author contributions AG: Methodology, Resources, Writing – original draft. AA: Writing – review & editing. RA: Writing – review & editing, Resources. MB: Resources, Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declare that no ﬁnancial support was received for the research and/or publication of this article. Conﬂict of interest The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or ﬁnancial relationships that could be construed as a potential conﬂict",180
A115,A115.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2025.1580310,9,"ﬁnancial support was received for the research and/or publication of this article. Conﬂict of interest The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or ﬁnancial relationships that could be construed as a potential conﬂict of interest. Generative AI statement The author(s) declare that Gen AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. Generative AI was used for language editing. Publisher’s note All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their aﬃliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher.",126
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,1,"Integrating AI in higher education: factors influencing ChatGPT acceptance among Chinese university EFL students Hamzeh Moradi1* Introduction As artificial intelligence (AI) continues to advance, chatbot technology has permeated various domains of everyday life and found widespread use in multiple industries (Yu et al., 2024). The evolution of advanced AI-driven models, such as ChatGPT (developed by OpenAI), facilitated by improvements in chatbot capabilities and language processing technology, has been hailed as the dawn of a new era. AI technology, including advanced AI-driven language models like ChatGPT, has exhibited an impressive capacity to generate human-like textual output, addressing inquiries, and delivering explanations (Gilson et al., 2023). The potential applications of these advanced language models, particularly ChatGPT, extend significantly to the realm of education, wherein they show considerable promise in enhancing pedagogical meth- odologies and learning outcomes. ChatGPT holds immense promise in enhancing the Abstract This study explores how Chinese university EFL learners accept and utilise ChatGPT, employing the widely recognised UTAUT2 model. A quantitative approach was utilised, collecting data from 340 participants through an online questionnaire and analys- ing it using structural",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,1,"the Abstract This study explores how Chinese university EFL learners accept and utilise ChatGPT, employing the widely recognised UTAUT2 model. A quantitative approach was utilised, collecting data from 340 participants through an online questionnaire and analys- ing it using structural equation modelling (SEM). The results indicate that habitual behaviour considerably influences students’ behavioural intentions (BI) and actual use behaviour (UB), underscoring the importance of fostering regular engagement with ChatGPT. Performance expectancy and social influence also positively impact BI, whereas hedonic motivation, effort expectancy, and price value show non-significant effects. Interestingly, facilitating conditions exert a significant influence on actual use behaviour, despite their weak influence on BI. The study contrasts with prior research by highlighting the minimal impact of behavioural intention on actual use, suggesting that habit and supporting resources are more critical in this context. These findings offer invaluable insights for educators and policymakers aiming to incorporate AI tools into language learning curricula, emphasising the need for habitual use and clear com- munication of ChatGPT’s benefits. Keywords: ChatGPT, Chinese EFL learners, Language learning, Technology acceptance, UTAUT2 model Open Access ©",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,1,"for educators and policymakers aiming to incorporate AI tools into language learning curricula, emphasising the need for habitual use and clear com- munication of ChatGPT’s benefits. Keywords: ChatGPT, Chinese EFL learners, Language learning, Technology acceptance, UTAUT2 model Open Access © The Author(s) 2025. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the mate- rial. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://​ creat​iveco​mmons.​org/​licen​ses/​by/4.​0/. RESEARCH ARTICLE Moradi ﻿Int J Educ",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,1,"intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://​ creat​iveco​mmons.​org/​licen​ses/​by/4.​0/. RESEARCH ARTICLE Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4 International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education *Correspondence: hamzeh.m701@yahoo.com 1 Guangzhou University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, China",62
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,2,"Page 2 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 quality and effectiveness of educational practices, including personalised content, sup- porting homework tasks, and offering constructive feedback to learners (Xu et al., 2024). Incorporating ChatGPT into higher education holds the potential to transform and make far-reaching changes in teaching and learning practices by functioning as an AI- powered tool for various educational tasks (Lim et al., 2023), and this integration pre- sents transformative possibilities for pedagogical praxis. ChatGPT’s versatility allows it to operate independently or be seamlessly integrated into myriad systems and platforms used by higher education institutions. The integration of ChatGPT can enhance stu- dents’ learning experiences by generating diverse ways of articulating ideas and provid- ing immediate, personalised feedback based on data supplied by students or instructors (Hwang & Chen, 2023). Building upon the preceding discourse, there has been a discernible increase in schol- arly attention focused on the potential integration of ChatGPT in higher education contexts. This emerging focus encompasses a broad spectrum of academic disciplines, addressing both general pedagogical strategies and specific applications",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,2,"discourse, there has been a discernible increase in schol- arly attention focused on the potential integration of ChatGPT in higher education contexts. This emerging focus encompasses a broad spectrum of academic disciplines, addressing both general pedagogical strategies and specific applications in areas such as science education. The literature reflects a growing acknowledgement of how AI tech- nologies, such as ChatGPT, can transform educational practices and enhance learning outcomes across diverse fields. Scholarly investigations have identified numerous advantages of integrating ChatGPT into language learning paradigms. Its application in language education is recognised for fostering meaningful interaction, providing real-time feedback, and enabling students to engage in innovative, personalised learning processes (Xu et al., 2024; Yan, 2023). Through the simulation of authentic conversational scenarios, chatbots allow learners to experience target language use in contexts beyond the classroom (Liu et al., 2024). Furthermore, Sysoyev and Filatov (2023) discuss how chatbots leverage natural language processing (NLP) to simulate human dialogue, which not only enhances student moti- vation but also provides opportunities for practicing language skills outside traditional classroom settings. This feature is particularly beneficial, as",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,2,"and Filatov (2023) discuss how chatbots leverage natural language processing (NLP) to simulate human dialogue, which not only enhances student moti- vation but also provides opportunities for practicing language skills outside traditional classroom settings. This feature is particularly beneficial, as it helps alleviate learner anx- iety by creating a non-judgemental environment for language practice. Despite the recognised benefits of AI-powered chatbots in language learning, there is limited research on how university EFL learners perceive and use these tools for autono- mous, personalised EFL learning. This gap in research is particularly concerning, pre- senting several critical challenges for educational practitioners and institutions. Without a clear understanding of learners’ perceptions and usage patterns, educators may struggle to design effective ChatGPT-enhanced learning environments that align with students’ needs and preferences. Consequently, such misalignment may lead to ineffective technology integration, resulting in reduced learner engagement, decreased motivation, and suboptimal learning outcomes. Beyond designing effective learning environments, the absence of data on learner perceptions also has broader implications for equitable and resource-efficient integra- tion of AI technologies in education. Poorly executed technology integration efforts risk",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,2,"engagement, decreased motivation, and suboptimal learning outcomes. Beyond designing effective learning environments, the absence of data on learner perceptions also has broader implications for equitable and resource-efficient integra- tion of AI technologies in education. Poorly executed technology integration efforts risk wasting institutional resources and missing opportunities to optimise language learning outcomes. Moreover, a lack of understanding can perpetuate inequitable access to tech- nological benefits, particularly in educational contexts where AI integration is rapidly expanding.",74
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,3,"Page 3 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 Such challenges underscore the urgency of addressing this knowledge gap in order to develop pedagogical strategies that fully harness ChatGPT’s potential and ensure its suc- cessful integration into language education. The importance of aligning AI integration with student needs is a perspective shared by Chun (2019), who emphasised the dual necessity of leveraging technology and understanding student goals to advance foreign language education in the AI era. This understanding is crucial for effectively integrating chatbots into language education and enhancing support for student learning. Drawing on these identified gaps, this study explicitly poses the research question: How do Chinese university EFL learners perceive and adopt ChatGPT for autonomous and personalised language learning, and what factors influence their acceptance of it within the UTAUT2 framework? This investigation directly addresses the limited under- standing of learners’ perceptions and usage patterns, focusing on adoption factors spe- cific to this population and educational context. To effectively align AI integration with student needs, it is essential to ground investi- gations in robust",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,3,"addresses the limited under- standing of learners’ perceptions and usage patterns, focusing on adoption factors spe- cific to this population and educational context. To effectively align AI integration with student needs, it is essential to ground investi- gations in robust theoretical frameworks, such as the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology 2 (UTAUT2) of Venkatesh et al. (2012), which offers a comprehensive lens for examining technology acceptance. While UTAUT2 has been extensively employed to investigate technology acceptance, prior research using this framework has predominantly focused on conventional edu- cational technologies, such as learning management systems, e-learning, and mobile- assisted language learning platforms (Kang et al., 2015; Moorthy et al., 2019; Raza et al., 2022). However, AI-powered tools such as ChatGPT introduce unique capabilities that differ fundamentally from these more traditional technologies. In contrast to traditional educational technologies, ChatGPT brings unique affor- dances that redefine the boundaries of technology-assisted learning. Leveraging advanced NLP, ChatGPT generates human-like responses, enabling dialogic, adaptive, and personalised interactions with learners (Rezai et al., 2024; Yu & Chauhan, 2024). This capability positions ChatGPT as a",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,3,"brings unique affor- dances that redefine the boundaries of technology-assisted learning. Leveraging advanced NLP, ChatGPT generates human-like responses, enabling dialogic, adaptive, and personalised interactions with learners (Rezai et al., 2024; Yu & Chauhan, 2024). This capability positions ChatGPT as a dynamic learning assistant rather than a static tool, offering immediate, context-specific feedback, fostering authentic language prac- tice, and adapting to individual learners’ needs in real time. These features make Chat- GPT particularly relevant for language learning, where meaningful interaction is critical for developing fluency and comprehension (Li, 2024). Despite these transformative features, the current body of research largely overlooks the distinct adoption patterns associated with AI-powered tools like ChatGPT, neces- sitating further exploration. The integration of conversational AI technologies like Chat- GPT raises unique questions about performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and habit formation, given their distinct affordances and potential to redefine user-tech- nology interactions. For example, ChatGPT’s capacity to provide personalised learn- ing experiences and foster self-regulated learning may alter the relative importance of traditional UTAUT2 factors. This technological paradigm shift necessitates a method- ologically robust investigation of how established",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,3,"redefine user-tech- nology interactions. For example, ChatGPT’s capacity to provide personalised learn- ing experiences and foster self-regulated learning may alter the relative importance of traditional UTAUT2 factors. This technological paradigm shift necessitates a method- ologically robust investigation of how established theoretical constructs manifest in AI- enhanced learning environments. By addressing these unique affordances and extending established frameworks, this study contributes to both theoretical advancements and practical applications. Spe- cifically, it extends the UTAUT2 model to include ‘personal innovativeness’, a factor",80
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,4,"Page 4 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 that captures the willingness of learners to experiment with cutting-edge technolo- gies (discussed further below). Personal innovativeness is particularly relevant in this context due to its association with prior study habits, as learners with a propensity to explore innovative tools are more likely to have developed autonomous learning prac- tices that align with ChatGPT’s capabilities for self-directed, personalised language practice (Agarwal & Prasad, 1998; Huang & Hamdan, 2023). Students exhibiting higher personal innovativeness may have already developed adaptive study strategies that render them more receptive to incorporating AI tools into their existing language learning routines. Previous studies in EFL contexts have demonstrated that personal innovativeness significantly predicts technology integration in language learning (Ma, 2025), suggesting its relevance for understanding ChatGPT acceptance patterns. While UTAUT2 effectively captures external factors influencing technology adop- tion, personal innovativeness addresses the intrinsic individual differences that may be particularly salient in educational technology contexts where adoption is largely voluntary, especially among Chinese EFL learners who may leverage such innovative- ness to meet academic",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,4,"factors influencing technology adop- tion, personal innovativeness addresses the intrinsic individual differences that may be particularly salient in educational technology contexts where adoption is largely voluntary, especially among Chinese EFL learners who may leverage such innovative- ness to meet academic demands in a competitive educational environment. As Chat- GPT represents an emerging class of AI-driven educational tools, understanding how individual and contextual factors influence its acceptance is crucial for optimising its integration into educational environments. Context-specific focus is critical for understanding the practical implications of AI adoption in diverse educational settings. This study addresses a significant gap by ana- lysing Chinese university EFL learners’ engagement with AI-powered tools, focusing on how these students accept and use ChatGPT and exploring factors that influence their adoption or resistance. Understanding these interactions is crucial to integrating these technologies into education. As part of this investigation, it is vital to consider the cultural and educational demands that influence university students’ technology adoption, particularly in con- texts like China where societal and academic pressures are pronounced. Unlike K-12 learners, university students are more likely to",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,4,"this investigation, it is vital to consider the cultural and educational demands that influence university students’ technology adoption, particularly in con- texts like China where societal and academic pressures are pronounced. Unlike K-12 learners, university students are more likely to require tools for self-regulated and autonomous learning, given the increasing academic demands of higher education (Anthonysamy et al., 2020). Moreover, cultural factors, such as high regard for tech- nology in China and the societal emphasis on academic excellence (Huang et al., 2019; Li, 2012), may shape learners’ attitudes towards AI adoption differently from their counterparts in other regions. These distinctions highlight the necessity of examining this specific population to provide actionable insights that contribute to the broader field of AI in education. Building on this understanding of cultural and societal influences, it is equally important to explore the individual characteristics and contextual factors that shape AI acceptance among university students in higher education. Compared to K-12 learners, university students benefit from a more independent learning environment, fostering greater autonomy in adopting technology (Khalid et al., 2021). This inde- pendence, coupled",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,4,"and contextual factors that shape AI acceptance among university students in higher education. Compared to K-12 learners, university students benefit from a more independent learning environment, fostering greater autonomy in adopting technology (Khalid et al., 2021). This inde- pendence, coupled with enhanced critical thinking skills, enables them to evaluate AI-powered tools critically and align their use with academic objectives (Li & Salleh, 2024). These attributes highlight the relevance of university learners as a focal group for examining AI acceptance, particularly in language learning contexts.",84
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,5,"Page 5 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 While these traits are common among university students globally, Chinese EFL learn- ers represent a particularly compelling group for study due to the unique interplay of cultural, societal, and educational pressures influencing their engagement with AI tools. In China, the societal emphasis on academic excellence and widespread regard for tech- nological innovation may distinctly influence learners’ attitudes towards AI adoption. These cultural dynamics, coupled with the educational pressures faced by university students, distinguish their experiences from those of students in other regions or age groups. Examining this specific population provides actionable insights not only into the factors driving AI acceptance but also into the broader implications for integrating AI tools into diverse educational contexts. This interplay of cultural, societal, and individual factors necessitates a robust theoretical lens to understand the drivers of AI adoption, making the UTAUT2 framework particularly relevant for this study. This study is grounded in the UTAUT2 model of Venkatesh et al. (2012). In line with previous research (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Strzelecki et",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,5,"to understand the drivers of AI adoption, making the UTAUT2 framework particularly relevant for this study. This study is grounded in the UTAUT2 model of Venkatesh et al. (2012). In line with previous research (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Strzelecki et al., 2024), and in addition to the seven established constructs from the UTAUT2 framework (Venkatesh et al., 2012), we incorporated the construct of ‘personal innovativeness’, a construct originally proposed by Agarwal and Prasad (1998), capturing the willingness of individuals to experiment with emerging technologies. This construct is instrumental in elucidating how individ- ual differences may affect the adoption and application of ChatGPT. Within this theo- retical framework, behavioural intention (BI) serves as a central mediator, linking the key constructs—such as performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influ- ence—with the actual use of ChatGPT (use behaviour). This mediation role reflects the premise that individuals’ intentions are a critical precursor to their actions. By analysing BI’s mediating effects, this study aims to offer a deeper insight into how various factors collectively influence students’ actual adoption and utilisation of ChatGPT in language learning",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,5,"premise that individuals’ intentions are a critical precursor to their actions. By analysing BI’s mediating effects, this study aims to offer a deeper insight into how various factors collectively influence students’ actual adoption and utilisation of ChatGPT in language learning contexts. This study further examines these interrelations by focusing on key constructs such as performance expectancy and effort expectancy, which are founda- tional in understanding ChatGPT adoption. Drawing from extensive empirical research, this study examines the primary fac- tors affecting AI acceptance in educational contexts through the UTAUT2 framework, with particular attention to how these factors manifest in EFL contexts. At the foun- dation of AI acceptance, performance expectancy (PE) emerges as a critical predictor of behavioural intention (BI) in adopting AI technologies in language learning, where users perceive AI tools as enhancing teaching and learning outcomes (Alhadiah, 2023; Zheng et al., 2024). This construct has emerged as a primary driver in educational con- texts, with studies showing that students’ belief in AI’s ability to enhance academic per- formance significantly influences adoption (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Habibi et al., 2023).",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,5,"et al., 2024). This construct has emerged as a primary driver in educational con- texts, with studies showing that students’ belief in AI’s ability to enhance academic per- formance significantly influences adoption (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Habibi et al., 2023). Similarly, effort expectancy (EE), linked to the perceived ease of using AI tools in EFL classrooms, positively correlates with BI, particularly when learners feel confident in managing AI-assisted learning platforms (Xu & Thien, 2024). Ease of use is particu- larly relevant in language learning contexts, where it determines sustained engagement (Zhang & Pan, 2024). While individual perceptions are critical, external environmental and social influences also play a significant role in shaping AI adoption patterns. Beyond these individual perceptions, environmental and social factors play cru- cial roles in AI adoption. Social influence (SI), including peer and institutional",136
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,6,"Page 6 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 encouragement, demonstrates mixed effects in EFL contexts, often shaped by cul- tural attitudes towards AI use (Lin et al., 2022; Ragheb et al., 2022). Notably, SI plays a distinctive role in Asian educational settings, where peer and instructor attitudes significantly shape technology adoption (Habibi et al., 2023). Furthermore, facilitat- ing conditions (FC), such as institutional support for AI integration and technical infrastructure, have been shown to critically affect AI tool implementation in higher education (Zheng et al., 2024). The perceived economic value proposition, concep- tualised as price value (PV) in the UTAUT2 framework, has emerged as another sig- nificant determinant of behavioural intention, particularly in educational technology adoption where students weigh the benefits against potential monetary investments (Romero-Rodríguez et al., 2023). Equally important, and complementary to these external influences, are the per- sonal dimensions that shape AI acceptance patterns, which provide deeper insight into learners’ engagement with AI tools. Hedonic motivation (HM) emerges as a significant factor in engaging EFL learners, where interactive and enjoyable AI tools",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,6,"external influences, are the per- sonal dimensions that shape AI acceptance patterns, which provide deeper insight into learners’ engagement with AI tools. Hedonic motivation (HM) emerges as a significant factor in engaging EFL learners, where interactive and enjoyable AI tools like ChatGPT foster intrinsic motivation for language practice (Zhang & Pan, 2024). Additionally, ​ habit (H), particularly the habitual use of AI tools in language tasks, further contributes to AI acceptance (Alhadiah, 2023; Zhang & Pan, 2024). Moreo- ver, personal innovativeness (PI) significantly influences EFL learners, especially in adopting AI for tasks like writing feedback, conversational practice, and personalised learning (Huang & Hamdan, 2023). Understanding these factors is essential for exam- ining how Chinese university EFL learners perceive and adopt ChatGPT in their edu- cational practices, which this study aims to address. Incorporating these constructs enables a nuanced exploration of how various fac- tors interrelate, with behavioural intention serving as a pivotal mediator within this framework. By analysing these factors, this study endeavours to offer in-depth insights into the acceptance and practical use of ChatGPT among Chinese university EFL learners.",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,6,"how various fac- tors interrelate, with behavioural intention serving as a pivotal mediator within this framework. By analysing these factors, this study endeavours to offer in-depth insights into the acceptance and practical use of ChatGPT among Chinese university EFL learners. By exploring learners’ attitudes and practices regarding ChatGPT, the findings will enhance our understanding of how AI tools are transforming language learning beyond traditional classroom settings. This understanding will help in devel- oping strategies to better integrate AI tools into language learning curricula, thereby enhancing the overall quality of education and outcomes for language learners. This research extends beyond the Chinese context by developing a comprehensive theoretical framework for understanding AI acceptance in language learning that can be adapted to various cultural contexts. While focusing on Chinese learners, the study identifies universal factors in student acceptance of AI tools while highlighting culture-specific variations, thus providing valuable methodological insights for inves- tigating AI adoption in different educational settings. The findings of this study will contribute to the development of evidence-based frameworks for AI integration in language education, identifying potential barriers to",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,6,"highlighting culture-specific variations, thus providing valuable methodological insights for inves- tigating AI adoption in different educational settings. The findings of this study will contribute to the development of evidence-based frameworks for AI integration in language education, identifying potential barriers to the adoption of AI tools among EFL learners. These insights will inform institutional policies and support educators in designing effective AI-enhanced learning environ- ments that align with learner needs and expectations. As higher education institu- tions globally navigate the challenges of AI integration, this research offers timely guidance for optimising implementation strategies and avoiding potential pitfalls.",96
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,7,"Page 7 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 Literature review The UTAUT2 model builds on the original framework by including additional vari- ables that enhance the understanding of technology acceptance. It incorporates the four principal determinants of UTAUT—performance expectancy (perceived utility), effort expectancy (perceived ease of use), social influence (social endorsement), and facilitating conditions (infrastructural support)—along with three additional con- structs: hedonic motivation (perceived enjoyment), price value (cost–benefit analy- sis), and habit (behavioural habituation) (Venkatesh et al., 2012). The UTAUT2 is designed to explore the elements that impact technology adop- tion and usage in both organisational and personal contexts (Lavidas et al., 2024). This model serves as a robust analytical tool for investigating factors that affect the adoption of technological innovations in diverse contexts. Its application spans a wide array of educational technologies, encompassing virtual learning environments (Kumarasamy et al., 2023), mobile-based educational applications (Açıkgül & Şad, 2021), immersive virtual and augmented reality (Du & Liang, 2024), and institutional learning management systems (Qian, 2022). This broad applicability of UTAUT2 pro- vides a foundation for examining",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,7,"learning environments (Kumarasamy et al., 2023), mobile-based educational applications (Açıkgül & Şad, 2021), immersive virtual and augmented reality (Du & Liang, 2024), and institutional learning management systems (Qian, 2022). This broad applicability of UTAUT2 pro- vides a foundation for examining technology acceptance in specific fields, such as EFL education, where its constructs have yielded significant empirical insights. Empirical studies investigating technology acceptance in EFL contexts have yielded significant insights into learner behaviour and adoption patterns. For instance, research by Xu and Thien (2024) found that Chinese EFL learners’ performance expectancy significantly predicted their intention to use AI-powered language learning tools. Similarly, a study by Zheng et al. (2024) revealed that effort expec- tancy, hedonic motivation, and social influence were crucial determinants of AI tool adoption in EFL learning among Chinese university students. In the context of AI- assisted language learning, Cai et al. (2024) demonstrated that facilitating conditions and hedonic motivation significantly influenced EFL students’ continued use of AI- assisted learning platforms. They further posited that behavioural intention serves as a more robust predictor of learning effectiveness in ChatGPT-assisted language",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,7,"Cai et al. (2024) demonstrated that facilitating conditions and hedonic motivation significantly influenced EFL students’ continued use of AI- assisted learning platforms. They further posited that behavioural intention serves as a more robust predictor of learning effectiveness in ChatGPT-assisted language learning compared to performance expectancy. These empirical findings highlight the unique characteristics of EFL learners’ technology acceptance patterns, particularly in Asian educational contexts, where cultural factors and institutional support play crucial roles in technology adoption decisions. Building on these findings, our study seeks to further examine the applicability of UTAUT2 in AI-assisted EFL learning, with an emphasis on ChatGPT technology and additional determinants such as personal innovativeness. Our research hypothesises that the key determinants of the UTAUT2 framework affect Chinese university EFL students’ behavioural intention to use ChatGPT technol- ogy. Consistent with Biloš and Budimir (2024) and Strzelecki et al. (2024), we include personal innovativeness into our analytical framework. Personal innovativeness (PI)—a construct linked to individuals’ openness to new technology, initially conceptualised by (Agarwal & Prasad, 1998)—has been recognised as an important determinant in tech- nology implementation processes. Scholars suggest",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,7,"(2024), we include personal innovativeness into our analytical framework. Personal innovativeness (PI)—a construct linked to individuals’ openness to new technology, initially conceptualised by (Agarwal & Prasad, 1998)—has been recognised as an important determinant in tech- nology implementation processes. Scholars suggest that traits like PI are essential for shaping technology acceptance within the realm of information technology (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Huang & Hamdan, 2023).",64
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,8,"Page 8 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 Our study seeks to investigate the impact of the factors delineated in the UTAUT2 framework, as well as personal innovativeness, on the behavioural intention of Chi- nese EFL university students to utilise ChatGPT. Furthermore, this investigation aims to assess these students’ perceptions of ChatGPT’s effectiveness in enhancing their lan- guage acquisition processes. This study aims to guide educational strategies and pro- mote the effective implementation of AI tools in higher education by examining the key elements influencing the adoption and utilisation of ChatGPT. Hypotheses development In the UTAUT theoretical framework (Venkatesh et al., 2012), performance expectancy (PE) is defined as the user’s beliefs and perceptions about how effectively a technological system can enhance and maximise performance across various tasks. In this study, PE denotes EFL students’ belief that utilising ChatGPT will enhance their language skills and lead to better academic outcomes. Recent studies have shown that PE is a significant factor affecting behavioural intention (BI) to integrate AI tools in learning environments (Alhwaiti, 2023; Du & Liang,",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,8,"that utilising ChatGPT will enhance their language skills and lead to better academic outcomes. Recent studies have shown that PE is a significant factor affecting behavioural intention (BI) to integrate AI tools in learning environments (Alhwaiti, 2023; Du & Liang, 2024; Lin et al., 2022). Given the strong support from prior studies, we hypothesise: H1 PE will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Effort expectancy (EE) evaluates the perceived ease of use of ChatGPT among stu- dents, indicating the effort required for effective technology utilisation (Venkatesh et al., 2003). EE has been shown to be a vital determinant of technology adoption, directly impacting individuals’ behavioural intentions to adopt and engage with new technolo- gies (Alhwaiti, 2023; Du & Liang, 2024). In our study, we describe EE as the extent to which Chinese university EFL learners perceive interacting with ChatGPT to be easy and straightforward and requiring minimal effort. We hypothesise: H2 EE will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,8,"to which Chinese university EFL learners perceive interacting with ChatGPT to be easy and straightforward and requiring minimal effort. We hypothesise: H2 EE will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Social influence (SI) examines the impact of interpersonal and broader institutional dynamics and educational environment on students’ attitudes towards ChatGPT. This construct suggests that users’ BI is significantly influenced by their social circles, as indi- viduals often conform to social influences, particularly in short-term decisions such as technology adoption (Venkatesh, 2022). Researchers have emphasised that SI signifi- cantly affects BI across different AI applications (Kalınkara & Özdemir, 2024; Ragheb et al., 2022). We define SI as the extent to which Chinese university EFL learners per- ceive support or encouragement from key individuals in their social networks—such as classmates or instructors—concerning their use of ChatGPT. We hypothesise:",147
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,9,"Page 9 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 H3 SI will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Hedonic motivation (HM) is defined as the enjoyment users experience from inter- acting with technology, and it has been proven to significantly affect users’ intentions (Venkatesh et al., 2012). Prior investigations, such as those by Kumarasamy et al. (2023) and Habibi et al. (2023), demonstrate that students exhibit a higher propensity to sustain their utilisation of a technology if they perceive it as enjoyable. This high- lights the significance of affective factors in technology acceptance, extending beyond purely utilitarian considerations. In this study, HM is operationalised as the degree to which Chinese university EFL learners find ChatGPT engaging and enjoyable, and their overall pleasure in interacting with new AI tools. We hypothesise: H4 HM will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Facilitating conditions (FC) refer to the resources and assistance necessary to effec- tively use technological",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,9,"new AI tools. We hypothesise: H4 HM will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Facilitating conditions (FC) refer to the resources and assistance necessary to effec- tively use technological systems (Venkatesh, 2022). The UTAUT2 model highlights FC as a key determinant influencing both users’ behavioural intentions and actual usage behaviour. Studies have examined the significant connections between FC and both the intention to use and the actual utilisation of technological innovations, with a particular focus on AI (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Strzelecki, 2023; Venkatesh, 2022). For instance, Strzelecki (2023) and Biloš and Budimir (2024) found that, while FC did not have a notable impact on BI, it did significantly affect actual use behaviour. We hypothesise: H5 FC will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. H6 FC will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ ChatGPT use behav- iour in language learning practices. Habit (H) pertains to the impact of established routine behaviours on the accept- ance",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,9,"use ChatGPT in language learning practices. H6 FC will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ ChatGPT use behav- iour in language learning practices. Habit (H) pertains to the impact of established routine behaviours on the accept- ance and usage of ChatGPT, illustrating how consistently individuals engage with the technology. According to Venkatesh et al. (2012), habit plays a crucial role as a key factor that significantly predicts both BI and technology use. Recent studies have underlined the essential role of habit in determining intentions and engagement with",90
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,10,"Page 10 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 AI technologies (Lavidas et al., 2024; Strzelecki, 2023). This study conceptualises habit as the extent to which Chinese university EFL learners have established consist- ent patterns of interaction with ChatGPT, resulting in its automatic and regular usage for language learning purposes. This concept emphasises that frequent engagement can evolve into habitual behaviour, thereby influencing intentions and actual usage. We hypothesise: H7 Habit will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. H8 Habit will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ actual use of Chat- GPT in language learning practices. Price value (PV) is defined as the evaluation of a system’s perceived benefits compared to its financial costs (Venkatesh et al., 2012). In this study, PV pertains to the expenses incurred by Chinese university EFL students to access ChatGPT for learning. This con- struct assesses whether the cost of using ChatGPT is justified by the advantages it pro- vides to students. We hypothesise: H9 PV will",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,10,"pertains to the expenses incurred by Chinese university EFL students to access ChatGPT for learning. This con- struct assesses whether the cost of using ChatGPT is justified by the advantages it pro- vides to students. We hypothesise: H9 PV will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Personal innovativeness (PI) reflects an individual’s tendency to adopt and experiment with emerging technologies, such as ChatGPT, in their language learning processes. This construct measures the willingness of EFL learners to integrate innovative AI tools, in particular ChatGPT, into their language acquisition activities. High PI indicates a greater likelihood of adopting and effectively utilising ChatGPT for personalised and autono- mous language learning, potentially leading to enhanced educational outcomes. We hypothesise: H10 PI will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ BI to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Behavioural intention (BI) denotes an individual’s propensity and commitment to utilise a particular technology for task completion (Cortez et al., 2024). Prior research (Kalınkara & Özdemir, 2024; Lavidas et al., 2024) has investigated",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,10,"to use ChatGPT in language learning practices. Behavioural intention (BI) denotes an individual’s propensity and commitment to utilise a particular technology for task completion (Cortez et al., 2024). Prior research (Kalınkara & Özdemir, 2024; Lavidas et al., 2024) has investigated the relationship between BI and the practical application of technological innovations. This study",53
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,11,"Page 11 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 investigates BI to elucidated how inclined Chinese university EFL learners are to inte- grate ChatGPT into their language learning activities. We hypothesise: H11 BI will have a positive impact on Chinese university EFL students’ ChatGPT use behav- iour in language learning practices. Use behaviour (UB) seeks to elucidate users’ engagement and utilisation patterns of technology. Within this model’s framework, actual usage pertains to the frequency with which individuals use a specific technology to accomplish their task. In our study, the utilisation of ChatGPT was assessed using a seven-item scale ranging from ‘never’ (no engagement) to ‘several times a day’ (multiple daily interactions) to cap- ture the frequency of use among Chinese university EFL students. Our analysis was guided by a research model (see Fig. 1) that incorporated seven principal elements from the UTAUT2 framework (Venkatesh et al., 2012). To refine and enhance the pre- cision and comprehensiveness of our model, we included the personal innovativeness (PI) construct as identified by Agarwal and Prasad (1998). Fig. 1 A",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,11,"seven principal elements from the UTAUT2 framework (Venkatesh et al., 2012). To refine and enhance the pre- cision and comprehensiveness of our model, we included the personal innovativeness (PI) construct as identified by Agarwal and Prasad (1998). Fig. 1 A proposed model for investigating Chinese EFL students’ ChatGPT acceptance and use. PE Performance expectancy, EE Effort expectancy, SI Social influence, HM Hedonic motivation, FC Facilitating conditions, PV Price value, H Habit, PI Personal innovativeness, BI Behavioural intention, UB Use behaviour",80
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,12,"Page 12 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 Methods Instruments To investigate the acceptance and use of ChatGPT among Chinese university EFL learners, we employed a modified UTAUT2 survey instrument consisting of 33 items. Participants indicated their level of agreement utilising a 7-point Likert scale, where 1 denoted strong disagreement and 7 indicated strong agreement. The survey was adapted from previous research (Agarwal & Prasad, 1998; Venkatesh et al., 2012). To ensure content validity, five experts specialising in educational technology in language reviewed the questionnaire items. Their feedback prompted necessary revi- sions, resulting in a final version with 33 items, used in both the pilot and the main data collection phases. Table 1 outlines the questionnaire constructs, item counts, and applied scales. Procedure An initial pilot study was conducted with 38 Chinese EFL students (aged 18–25) to refine the questionnaire. The participants included 20 females and 18 males from two universities in Guangzhou, which were also used for the main data collection. Guangzhou, a first-tier city, was selected for its position as a leading educational",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,12,"18–25) to refine the questionnaire. The participants included 20 females and 18 males from two universities in Guangzhou, which were also used for the main data collection. Guangzhou, a first-tier city, was selected for its position as a leading educational hub in southern China, characterised by a diverse student population and significant investment in technology integration within higher education. The selected univer- sities include both public and private institutions, providing a balanced perspective and insights into the influence of institutional contexts on technology adoption. Fur- thermore, Guangzhou’s emphasis on technological innovation aligns closely with the objectives of this study, making it an ideal setting to examine how societal and institu- tional factors influence EFL learners’ acceptance of ChatGPT. Table 1 Constructs and items of the ChatGPT acceptance and use questionnaire Construct Number of items Item codes Sample items Scale Performance expectancy (PE) 4 PE1 – PE4 ChatGPT is beneficial to my academic work 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disa- gree to 7 = Strongly Agree) Effort expectancy (EE) 4 EE1 – EE4 I find ChatGPT simple to use Social",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,12,expectancy (PE) 4 PE1 – PE4 ChatGPT is beneficial to my academic work 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disa- gree to 7 = Strongly Agree) Effort expectancy (EE) 4 EE1 – EE4 I find ChatGPT simple to use Social influence (SI) 3 SI1 – SI3 People I value believe I should use ChatGPT Hedonic motivation (HM) 3 HM1 – HM3 Using ChatGPT is fun Facilitating conditions (FC) 3 FC1 – FC3 I possess the necessary skills to operate ChatGPT Habit (H) 5 H1 – H5 I have developed a habit of using ChatGPT Price value (PV) 3 PV1 – PV3 The cost of ChatGPT is reasonable Personal innovativeness (PI) 4 PI1 – PI4 I enjoy exploring novel informa- tion technologies Behavioural intention (BI) 3 BI1 – BI3 I plan to maintain my use of ChatGPT in the future Use behaviour (UB) 1 UB Please specify how often you use ChatGPT: 7-point scale from ‘never’ (1) to ‘several times a day’ (7),162
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,13,"Page 13 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 The pilot study confirmed the reliability and validity of each variable, as well as the dis- criminant validity (Hair et al., 2013). Following the pilot study, we distributed the revised online questionnaire to 340 Chinese university EFL learners, aged 18 to 25, from the same two universities in Guangzhou. The sample consisted of 85 males (25%) and 255 females (75%). Among them, 163 students (48%) were enrolled in a public university, and 177 students (52%) were from a private university. The distribution by year of study was as follows: 230 students (68%) were in their sophomore year, 65 (19%) were juniors, and 45 (13%) were in their final year of undergraduate studies. Students were informed that their responses would remain confidential, participation was voluntary, and they could respond anonymously without providing any personal identifiers. Table 2 Descriptive Statistics: Mean, Standard Deviation (SD), Kurtosis, Skewness, and Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) PE Performance expectancy, EE Effort expectancy, SI Social influence, HM Hedonic motivation, FC Facilitating conditions, PV Price value,",180
