A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,14,"Page 14 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 Data analysis Data normality was assessed by analysing the values of skewness and kurtosis, with val- ues between − 2 and + 2 suggesting a normal distribution (Habibi et al., 2023). Table 2 shows that all values are within the acceptable range, confirming the normality of the data in our study. This normality supports the validity and reliability of the subsequent statistical analyses, enabling accurate conclusions and meaningful interpretations. The descriptive statistics reveal that the items demonstrate acceptable mean (M) val- ues. The highest mean is observed in PE1 (M = 5.537; SD = 1.183) and PE3 (M = 5.537; SD = 1.193), while H3 has the lowest mean (M = 3.962; SD = 1.750). To ensure data integrity and mitigate potential biases, several steps were taken dur- ing data preparation. The survey was designed to require full completion, ensuring no missing values in the dataset. This approach eliminates concerns about incomplete data while enhancing the reliability of subsequent analyses. To mitigate potential common- method bias (CMB),",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,14,"dur- ing data preparation. The survey was designed to require full completion, ensuring no missing values in the dataset. This approach eliminates concerns about incomplete data while enhancing the reliability of subsequent analyses. To mitigate potential common- method bias (CMB), the survey included consent forms to ensure respondent anonymity and confidentiality, reducing evaluation apprehension. Harman’s single-factor test pro- duced a value of approximately 25.33%, indicating no substantial risk of CMB. Further- more, to check for CMB, we assessed the variance inflation factor (VIF) and found all values to be below the threshold of 4, indicating minimal risk of bias (Kock, 2015; Russo & Stol, 2021). This outcome underscores the reliability and normal distribution of the constructs utilised in this study. The descriptive statistics, including mean, SD, kurtosis, skewness, and VIF, validate the instrument measuring Chinese university EFL learners’ acceptance and use of ChatGPT. Thus, the constructs are suitable for further statistical analysis. Results To assess the proposed model, partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS- SEM) was employed, utilising the path weighting scheme available in the SmartPLS 4 software. We",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,14,"use of ChatGPT. Thus, the constructs are suitable for further statistical analysis. Results To assess the proposed model, partial least squares structural equation modelling (PLS- SEM) was employed, utilising the path weighting scheme available in the SmartPLS 4 software. We utilised default initial weights and allowed for a maximum of 3000 itera- tions. Additionally, bootstrapping was implemented to ascertain the statistical signifi- cance of the PLS-SEM estimates (Ringle et al., 2022; Strzelecki, 2024). The validity of the constructs was assessed by analysing the indicator loadings. An indicator loading above 0.708 signifies that the construct accounts for over 50% of the variance in the indica- tor, demonstrating acceptable item reliability (Sarstedt et al., 2022). Table 3 displays the measurement scale and factor loadings, all of which exceed this threshold. The model’s reliability was assessed using composite reliability (CR), with acceptable levels ranging from 0.70 to 0.95 (Hair et al., 2022). Cronbach’s alpha and reliability coef- ficient were employed to measure internal consistency reliability (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015). To assess convergent validity, the average variance extracted (AVE) was calculated for items. A",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,14,"ranging from 0.70 to 0.95 (Hair et al., 2022). Cronbach’s alpha and reliability coef- ficient were employed to measure internal consistency reliability (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015). To assess convergent validity, the average variance extracted (AVE) was calculated for items. A satisfactory AVE value was deemed to be 0.500 or higher (Ghanbari et al., 2024). As presented in Table 4, the constructs met all necessary quality criteria for reli- ability and validity. We utilised the heterotrait-monotrait (HTMT) correlation ratio technique, as sug- gested by Henseler et al. (2015) to measure the discriminant validity (DV) of the PLS- SEM model. The recommended HTMT threshold is 0.900; values exceeding this",107
