A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,16,"Page 16 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 threshold suggest potential issues with DV. As demonstrated in Table 5, all HTMT val- ues are below 0.900, suggesting no significant concerns regarding discriminant validity. This finding supports that HTMT values below 0.900 indicate distinct theoretical con- cepts, affirming the DV of the constructs used in this research (Franke & Sarstedt, 2019). The structural model was assessed using PLS-SEM via SmartPLS software. To evalu- ate model fit, the standardised root mean square residual (SRMR) was calculated, yield- ing a value of 0.061, which satisfies the recommended threshold for good model fit (Magno et al., 2024). Additionally, the squared Euclidean distance (d_ULS) and geodesic distance (d_G) were reported as 2.075 and 0.997, respectively, further supporting the model’s robustness (Table 6). These distance measures evaluate the discrepancy between the empirical correlation matrix and the model-implied correlation matrix, with lower values indicating better model fit. To determine good fit, bootstrap analysis with 5,000 samples was conducted, revealing that our obtained d_ULS value (2.075) and d_G value (0.997) were both below",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,16,"empirical correlation matrix and the model-implied correlation matrix, with lower values indicating better model fit. To determine good fit, bootstrap analysis with 5,000 samples was conducted, revealing that our obtained d_ULS value (2.075) and d_G value (0.997) were both below the 95% confidence interval upper bounds (d_ULS 95% = 2.742; d_G 95% = 1.322), indicating that the discrepancy is not statistically significant (Dijkstra & Henseler, 2015; Hair et al., 2019). The next phase involved assessing the coefficient of determination ­(R2) for each construct. The model’s explanatory power was quantified Table 4 Construct reliability and validity CR Composite reliability, AVE Average variance extracted, BI Behavioural intention, EE Effort expectancy, FC Facilitating conditions, H Habit, HM Hedonic motivation, PE Performance expectancy, PI Personal innovativeness, PV Price value, SI Social influence Cronbach’s alpha rho_a CR AVE BI 0.865 0.869 0.917 0.787 EE 0.864 0.887 0.907 0.710 FC 0.764 0.767 0.864 0.679 H 0.894 0.897 0.922 0.704 HM 0.916 0.922 0.947 0.857 PE 0.870 0.871 0.911 0.720 PI 0.852 0.868 0.900 0.694 PV 0.820 0.821 0.893 0.736 SI 0.857 0.858 0.913 0.777 Table 5",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,16,"0.887 0.907 0.710 FC 0.764 0.767 0.864 0.679 H 0.894 0.897 0.922 0.704 HM 0.916 0.922 0.947 0.857 PE 0.870 0.871 0.911 0.720 PI 0.852 0.868 0.900 0.694 PV 0.820 0.821 0.893 0.736 SI 0.857 0.858 0.913 0.777 Table 5 HTMT BI Behavioural intention, EE Effort expectancy, FC Facilitating conditions, H Habit, HM Hedonic motivation, PE Performance expectancy, PI Personal innovativeness, PV Price value, SI Social influence., UB Use behaviour BI EE FC H HM PE PI PV SI BI .887 EE .326 .842 FC .518 .464 .824 H .739 .387 .464 .839 HM .575 .376 .512 .518 .925 PE .632 .377 .596 .578 .618 .848 PI .603 .403 .514 .529 .588 .502 .833 PV .407 .375 .427 .442 .418 .466 .494 .858 SI .511 .253 .353 .476 .413 .404 .339 .405 .882 UB .568 .377 .470 .676 .427 .520 .486 .305 .362",143
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,17,"Page 17 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 by ­R2 values ranging from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating a greater proportion of variance explained by the model. As proposed by Hair et al. (2011), ­R2 values can be classified into three levels: high (0.75), moderate (0.50), and weak (0.25). Additionally, ­f2 values, which evaluate the impact of an independent variable, are categorised minimal (0.02), moderate (0.15), or significant (0.35), with values below 0.02 indicating an insig- nificant effect (Wall Emerson, 2023). Figure 2 presents the PLS-SEM results, displaying standardised regression coefficients and ­R2 values within the variable circles. The results demonstrate that habit (H) exerts the most substantial effect (β = 0.436) on behavioural intention (BI), followed by personal innovativeness (PI) with a beta value of 0.219, Table 6 Structural model path coefficients and hypothesis testing results PE Performance expectancy, EE Effort expectancy, SI Social influence, HM Hedonic motivation, FC Facilitating conditions, PV Price value, H Habit, PI Personal innovativeness, BI Behavioural intention, UB Use behaviour, ** = p < 0.01 *** =",180
