A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,17,"Fig. 2 Final model assessing the acceptance and utilisation of ChatGPT by Chinese university EFL learners. PE Performance expectancy, EE Effort expectancy, SI Social influence, HM Hedonic motivation, FC Facilitating conditions, PV Price value, H Habit, PI Personal innovativeness, BI Behavioural intention, UB Use behaviour",45
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,18,"Page 18 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 performance expectancy (PE) at β = 0.193, and social influence (SI) with β = 0.147. These findings, along with the significance testing for the coefficients, are detailed in Table 6. Hedonic motivation (HM) (β = 0.062) and facilitating conditions (FC) (β = 0.069) have positive but weak effects on BI, evidenced by p-values of 0.407 and 0.305, respectively, indicating non-significant relationships. Effort expectancy (EE) (β = − 0.070) and price value (PV) (β = − 0.073) also show non-significant negative relationships with BI, as reflected by p-values of 0.212 and 0.208, respectively, and negligible ­f2 effect sizes for these relationships. Regarding use behaviour (UB), habit (H) shows the most substantial effect (β = 0.534), and facilitating conditions (FC) (β = 0.181) also have a notable impact. Conversely, behavioural intention (BI) (β = 0.078) has a positive but relatively weak effect on UB. These three factors together explain 49.1% of the variance in UB. Discussion This study examined the factors influencing Chinese university EFL learners’ adoption and application",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,18,"behavioural intention (BI) (β = 0.078) has a positive but relatively weak effect on UB. These three factors together explain 49.1% of the variance in UB. Discussion This study examined the factors influencing Chinese university EFL learners’ adoption and application of ChatGPT in their educational processes. The findings offer a com- prehensive and multifaceted understanding of AI tool adoption in education, with some results aligning with previous research while others diverge. The analysis identified habit (H) as the most significant factor of behavioural intention (BI) (β = 0.436), indicating that regular engagement with ChatGPT significantly boosts students’ intentions to use the tool. This aligns with prior research suggesting that habit- ual behaviour strongly influences technology adoption (Lavidas et al., 2024; Strzelecki et al., 2024). Strzelecki (2024) also emphasised the crucial role of habitual behaviour in fostering acceptance of AI-driven educational tools within learning settings, reinforcing the current findings. However, our results contrast with the findings of Du and Liang (2024), Fithriya et al. (2019), and Ain et al. (2016), who observed no direct relationship between habit and behavioural intention. This",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,18,"tools within learning settings, reinforcing the current findings. However, our results contrast with the findings of Du and Liang (2024), Fithriya et al. (2019), and Ain et al. (2016), who observed no direct relationship between habit and behavioural intention. This discrepancy suggests that the impact of habit may be context-dependent, varying across different technological tools, highlight- ing the need for further investigation. Personal innovativeness (PI) also emerged as a crucial determinant for BI (β = 0.219), highlighting the importance of students’ willingness to embrace new technologies in enhancing their behavioural intentions. This result aligns with prior research that has linked PI to technology acceptance. For instance, Strzelecki (2023) and Biloš and Budimir (2024) found that PI exerts a positive impact on BI, although to a lesser extent than other factors. Performance expectancy (PE) (β = 0.193) and social influence (SI) (β = 0.147) also posi- tively influence BI, albeit to a lesser extent. These results suggest that students’ percep- tions of ChatGPT’s capacity to foster language proficiency and the influence of peers and educators are pivotal in determining their intention",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,18,"(β = 0.147) also posi- tively influence BI, albeit to a lesser extent. These results suggest that students’ percep- tions of ChatGPT’s capacity to foster language proficiency and the influence of peers and educators are pivotal in determining their intention to adopt the tool. These results are consistent with the UTAUT2 framework, which emphasises the relevance and impor- tance of PE and SI in technology adoption (Venkatesh et al., 2012). Similarly to our find- ings, Habibi et al. (2023) reported significant positive correlations between PE and BI in their studies of ChatGPT and other educational technologies. Additionally, our find- ings align with previous studies (Biloš & Budimir, 2024; Habibi et al., 2023), which have demonstrated that social influence (SI) is a key factor influencing behavioural intention.",126
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,19,"Page 19 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 However, this finding contradicts the study of Strzelecki (2023) on ChatGPT in higher education, which found minimal influence of SI on BI. In contrast, hedonic motivation (HM) (β = 0.062, p = 0.407) and facilitating conditions (FC) (β = 0.069, p = 0.305) showed weak, non-significant effects on BI. This suggests that the enjoyment derived from interacting with ChatGPT and the accessibility of resources and support have a negligible impact on students’ behavioural intentions. Our findings contradict those of several studies on AI adoption in higher education (Habibi et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023, 2024) which reported significant positive effects of hedonic moti- vation on behavioural intention, differing from the non-significant influence observed in this research. Similarly, Kalınkara and Özdemir (2024), Venkatesh (2022), and Lavidas et al. (2024) identified FC as an important and significant variable affecting BI. Specifi- cally, Habibi et al. (2023) revealed that FC emerged as the most crucial factor influencing students’ BI towards ChatGPT usage in universities across Indonesia, which is inconsist- ent with",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,19,"identified FC as an important and significant variable affecting BI. Specifi- cally, Habibi et al. (2023) revealed that FC emerged as the most crucial factor influencing students’ BI towards ChatGPT usage in universities across Indonesia, which is inconsist- ent with our results. Interestingly, effort expectancy (EE) (β = − 0.070) and price value (PV) (β = − 0.073) demonstrated non-significant negative relationships with BI, suggesting that neither perceived ease of use nor cost considerations play crucial roles in students’ intentions to adopt ChatGPT. Our findings challenge the common trend in technology adoption stud- ies (Azizi et al., 2020; Du & Liang, 2024), which report positive impacts of EE and PV on students’ BI. Instead, as mentioned above, we observed different dynamics between these variables in the adoption of AI technologies for language learning among Chi- nese university EFL learners. This insignificance arises because university EFL learners regard Internet-based resources as everyday utilities, making the use of ChatGPT rela- tively effortless in students’ learning processes. Similarly, the insignificant impact of PV on BI to use ChatGPT can be attributed to its",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,19,"insignificance arises because university EFL learners regard Internet-based resources as everyday utilities, making the use of ChatGPT rela- tively effortless in students’ learning processes. Similarly, the insignificant impact of PV on BI to use ChatGPT can be attributed to its availability for free at the time of the study, with respondents likely utilising the free version, ChatGPT 3.5. These findings align with Neves et al. (2025), who found EE and PV were not significant in influencing behavioural intention in their study. When examining use behaviour (UB), habit (H) again proved to be the most signifi- cant factor (β = 0.534), with facilitating conditions (FC) identified as the second most significant factor (β = 0.181). This indicates that habitual use and the availability of sup- portive resources significantly contribute to the actual usage of ChatGPT. Behavioural intention (BI) (β = 0.078), while positive, showed a relatively weak influence on UB, sug- gesting that other factors beyond intention, such as established habits and facilitating conditions, are more critical in determining actual use. These results align with Habibi et al. (2023), who also",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,19,"while positive, showed a relatively weak influence on UB, sug- gesting that other factors beyond intention, such as established habits and facilitating conditions, are more critical in determining actual use. These results align with Habibi et al. (2023), who also observed a strong correlation between facilitating conditions and the utilisation of ChatGPT. The strong impact of habit on actual use behaviour is further supported by studies on e-learning platforms in college English learning (Deng et al., 2023) and mobile-assisted language learning platforms (Moorthy et al., 2019). However, the relatively weak influence of BI on UB contrasts with findings by Venkatesh et al. (2012), who emphasised that BI is a crucial factor in determining patterns of tech- nology usage in their UTAUT model. This discrepancy may suggest that in the context of ChatGPT adoption among Chinese EFL learners, habitual behaviours and facilitat- ing conditions play more dominant roles than mere intention. As indicated previously, habit can exert a direct impact on human behaviour and may diminish the impact of",168
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,20,"Page 20 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 behavioural intention (Kumar & Bervell, 2019). In other words, the establishment of habitual usage patterns effectively reduces reliance on conscious decision-making pro- cesses; when students integrate ChatGPT into their routine practices, their engagement transitions from a deliberate choice to an automatic behaviour, thereby eliminating the need for intentional contemplation before use. This indicates that once students have developed a habit of using ChatGPT and have favourable facilitating conditions (e.g., access, resources, support), their actual use behaviour may be more strongly influenced by these factors than by their initial intentions. The findings of this study, while focused on Chinese university EFL learners, offer val- uable insights that may extend to other cultural contexts, albeit with crucial caveats. The strong influence of habit on both behavioural intention and use behaviour may manifest differently across cultures with varying degrees of technology integration in education. For instance, in Western educational contexts, where learner autonomy is often empha- sised, habit formation might be more individually driven and self-directed, whereas in collectivist cultures",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,20,"behaviour may manifest differently across cultures with varying degrees of technology integration in education. For instance, in Western educational contexts, where learner autonomy is often empha- sised, habit formation might be more individually driven and self-directed, whereas in collectivist cultures like China, habit formation could be more influenced by institu- tional structures and peer practices (Hofstede et al., 2010; Moradi, 2025). The role of personal innovativeness identified in this study may have different impli- cations in cultures with varying attitudes towards innovation and risk-taking. Societies with higher uncertainty avoidance (Hofstede et al., 2010) might exhibit more resistance to AI adoption regardless of individual innovativeness, while those with lower uncer- tainty avoidance might demonstrate accelerated adoption rates. Future cross-cultural studies could examine how cultural dimensions moderate the relationship between per- sonal innovativeness and AI acceptance. The influence of motivation factors may also vary significantly across cultural contexts. While hedonic motivation showed minimal impact in our study of Chinese EFL learners, this finding might differ in educational cultures that place greater emphasis on enjoy- ment in learning. Similarly, language anxiety—a factor not",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,20,"also vary significantly across cultural contexts. While hedonic motivation showed minimal impact in our study of Chinese EFL learners, this finding might differ in educational cultures that place greater emphasis on enjoy- ment in learning. Similarly, language anxiety—a factor not explicitly measured in our study—could interact with technology acceptance differently across cultures. In con- texts where face-saving is culturally significant, AI tools like ChatGPT might offer a low- stakes environment for language practice that reduces anxiety, potentially strengthening the performance expectancy factor (MacIntyre & Vincze, 2017; Muthmainnah et al., 2024; Wang, 2024). Future research should explicitly incorporate cultural dimensions as moderating variables in technology acceptance models when studying AI adoption in language learning contexts. Such research could elucidate how motivational factors, anxiety reduction, and social dynamics interact with cultural variables to influence AI acceptance and use across diverse educational settings. Limitations It is important to acknowledge several constraints in this study. First, the sample is lim- ited to Chinese university EFL learners, which may impinge upon the generalisability of the results to other contexts or populations. Second, the cross-sectional approach",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,20,"Limitations It is important to acknowledge several constraints in this study. First, the sample is lim- ited to Chinese university EFL learners, which may impinge upon the generalisability of the results to other contexts or populations. Second, the cross-sectional approach cap- tures a snapshot of the situation at a distinct temporal reference, thereby precluding the establishment of causal relationships between the variables under investigation. While this design effectively identifies associations among variables, it does not account for temporal changes or unmeasured factors that may influence the relationships between",88
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,21,"Page 21 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 UTAUT2 constructs and behavioural intention. Future research should adopt longitudi- nal designs to track learners’ attitudes and behaviours over time, enabling a more robust analysis of causal pathways and the long-term impact of ChatGPT on language learning outcomes. Third, this study does not explicitly account for the potential influence of cultural fac- tors on ChatGPT usage among Chinese EFL learners. Cultural norms such as societal attitudes towards technology adoption and the emphasis on academic excellence in China may uniquely shape learners’ acceptance and use of AI-powered tools like Chat- GPT. These cultural dynamics may play a significant role in shaping behavioural inten- tion and actual use behaviour, suggesting the need for future research to explore these cultural dimensions more comprehensively. Furthermore, the study did not assess the potential influence of English language proficiency on students’ adoption of ChatGPT. Variations in language competence could potentially result in differing experiences and perceptions of ChatGPT’s utility, thereby influencing students’ acceptance and usage patterns. For instance, learners with higher proficiency might",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,21,"potential influence of English language proficiency on students’ adoption of ChatGPT. Variations in language competence could potentially result in differing experiences and perceptions of ChatGPT’s utility, thereby influencing students’ acceptance and usage patterns. For instance, learners with higher proficiency might utilise ChatGPT more effectively for complex tasks, while those with lower proficiency might encounter barri- ers in interacting with the tool. Future research could address this limitation by catego- rising participants based on their English language proficiency levels to investigate how this factor mediates the adoption of AI tools in language learning contexts. Additionally, the rapid evolution of AI technologies like ChatGPT may lead to changes in user percep- tions and behaviours that this study does not account for. Future research should con- sider longitudinal designs and diverse populations to confirm and extend these insights. Implications This investigation yields significant empirical evidence that can inform strategic deci- sion-making regarding the incorporation of AI language models, particularly Chat- GPT, into language learning curricula for Chinese university EFL learners. Emphasising the development of habitual usage through regular and structured engagement with ChatGPT",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,21,"empirical evidence that can inform strategic deci- sion-making regarding the incorporation of AI language models, particularly Chat- GPT, into language learning curricula for Chinese university EFL learners. Emphasising the development of habitual usage through regular and structured engagement with ChatGPT can significantly enhance students’ acceptance and use of the tool. This can be achieved through daily tasks, interactive sessions, and continuous support, making ChatGPT an indispensable part of the learning process. Promoting a setting that nurtures individual creativity and innovativeness and empha- sises the practical advantages of using ChatGPT in terms of enhancing performance may significantly stimulate its adoption. Highlighting success stories, offering incentives for early adopters, and integrating ChatGPT into various aspects of the curriculum can help students see the tangible benefits of using this tool. Factors including hedonic motiva- tion, effort expectancy, and price value appear less critical, suggesting that promotional and training efforts might not need to heavily focus on these aspects. Resources can be better allocated towards areas that significantly impact students’ behavioural intentions and actual use. While social influence shows moderate importance, it could become",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,21,"suggesting that promotional and training efforts might not need to heavily focus on these aspects. Resources can be better allocated towards areas that significantly impact students’ behavioural intentions and actual use. While social influence shows moderate importance, it could become more critical as AI tools such as ChatGPT gain wider acceptance and institutional support. As more students and faculty members adopt these technologies, peer influ- ence could play a larger role in encouraging others to follow suit. Therefore, creating a supportive community that champions the use of AI in education can yield significant benefits.",94
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,22,"Page 22 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 When developing pedagogical strategies, it is crucial to consider varying levels of Eng- lish proficiency among university EFL learners. For beginners and lower-intermediate learners, educators should implement scaffolded approaches where ChatGPT interac- tions are initially structured with predefined prompts and guided tasks (Tran, 2025). These may include template-based conversations, error correction exercises, and vocab- ulary building activities that align with the students’ proficiency level (Billingsley & Gardner, 2024; Sun et al., 2024). Instructors should provide explicit instruction on for- mulating effective prompts that correspond to beginners’ language capabilities, gradu- ally increasing complexity as proficiency develops. For intermediate learners, a balanced approach combining guided and autonomous interactions is recommended. These students can benefit from utilising ChatGPT for more complex tasks, such as paragraph-level feedback, contextual vocabulary enhance- ment, and simulated dialogues on familiar topics. The strong relationship between habit and use behaviour identified in our study suggests the implementation of regular, struc- tured ChatGPT interaction sessions for these learners would be beneficial in developing productive usage patterns (Strzelecki,",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,22,"and simulated dialogues on familiar topics. The strong relationship between habit and use behaviour identified in our study suggests the implementation of regular, struc- tured ChatGPT interaction sessions for these learners would be beneficial in developing productive usage patterns (Strzelecki, 2023; Zare et al., 2025). Advanced learners can leverage ChatGPT more autonomously for sophisticated language development. Given our findings regarding personal innovativeness, instructors should encourage these stu- dents to explore creative applications such as stylistic analysis, academic writing sup- port, and cultural nuance discussions. For this group, the emphasis should be on critical evaluation of AI-generated content rather than mere acceptance, fostering both lan- guage proficiency and digital literacy (Sandu et al., 2024). Regular reflection activities can assist students in articulating their experiences with ChatGPT, fostering metacogni- tive awareness and promoting responsible AI use in language learning. A tiered implementation framework is therefore recommended, with differentiated objectives, prompting strategies, and assessment methods based on proficiency lev- els. This differentiated approach acknowledges that the factors influencing technology acceptance identified in our study—particularly habit, personal innovativeness, and per- formance expectancy—may manifest differently",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,22,"framework is therefore recommended, with differentiated objectives, prompting strategies, and assessment methods based on proficiency lev- els. This differentiated approach acknowledges that the factors influencing technology acceptance identified in our study—particularly habit, personal innovativeness, and per- formance expectancy—may manifest differently across proficiency levels. While habit formation is universally important, as indicated by our results (β = 0.534), the complex- ity of tasks that become habitual should align with proficiency levels. Similarly, perfor- mance expectancy (β = 0.193) might be fulfilled differently, with beginners valuing basic communication improvements and advanced learners seeking sophisticated linguistic development. By leveraging these insights, educators and policymakers can strategically promote AI integration and take targeted measures to encourage the uptake and optimised use of ChatGPT, ultimately enhancing the language learning experience for Chinese university EFL learners. Conclusion Using the UTAUT2 model, widely recognised in technology acceptance studies, this study underscores the substantial influence of habitual behaviour in driving Chinese uni- versity EFL learners’ acceptance and use of ChatGPT. Through the promotion of regular use and emphasising the innovative aspects of ChatGPT, educators can effectively pro- mote",180
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,22,"studies, this study underscores the substantial influence of habitual behaviour in driving Chinese uni- versity EFL learners’ acceptance and use of ChatGPT. Through the promotion of regular use and emphasising the innovative aspects of ChatGPT, educators can effectively pro- mote its integration into language learning practices. The notable correlation between",50
A116,A116.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00530-4,23,"Page 23 of 25 Moradi ﻿Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:30 performance expectancy and behavioural intention emphasises the need for educators to clearly communicate the practical benefits of ChatGPT in enhancing learning out- comes. Conversely, the non-significant influences of hedonic motivation, effort expec- tancy, facilitating conditions, and price value highlight areas where this study’s results diverge from previous research, suggesting that these factors may play different roles in this research context. Overall, this research contributes to the expanding scholarly dis- course regarding the integration of AI tools in higher education and offers practical rec- ommendations for leveraging ChatGPT to improve language learning experiences. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions The author, Hamzeh Moradi, is solely responsible for the conception, design, execution, and analysis of this research, as well as the preparation of the resulting manuscript. Funding Not applicable. Data availability The data are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Competing interests The author declares there are no competing interests. Received: 16 October 2024",167
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9 Abstract Today, there is no doubt that Artificial Intelligence (AI) presents both opportu­ nities and challenges in higher education. This study examines three key areas: (1) students’ use of ChatGPT, (2) their perceptions of its benefits and costs, and (3) the differences in attitudes toward AI integration in higher education between ChatGPT users and non-users. A sample of 737 undergraduate students at a Span­ ish university answered an online survey. The quantitative analysis revealed a high prevalence of ChatGPT use for academic and personal purposes, with students identifying its 24/7 accessibility as a major advantage, along with the time-saving benefits it offers. However, concerns were raised about potential costs, including the devaluation of university education when students rely on ChatGPT to complete assignments. Results indicate significant differences between ChatGPT users and non-users: users generally support AI integration in higher education, particularly in teaching methods, while non-users often oppose its integration, advocating for measures such as banning AI in universities. Our study provides valuable insights into student perspectives on the integration of ChatGPT in",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,1,"generally support AI integration in higher education, particularly in teaching methods, while non-users often oppose its integration, advocating for measures such as banning AI in universities. Our study provides valuable insights into student perspectives on the integration of ChatGPT in higher education, em­ phasizing the contrasting viewpoints of users and non-users. The findings underline the need for universities to actively involve students in shaping policies on artificial intelligence while offering targeted training to promote its responsible and ethical use. Furthermore, universities should support educators in adapting their teaching methodologies to the digital era by incorporating innovative strategies that enhance both teaching and learning. Keywords Artificial intelligence · ChatGPT · University · Student perceptions · Benefits · Costs Received: 2 September 2024 / Accepted: 10 April 2025 / Published online: 16 April 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Student perceptions of ChatGPT: benefits, costs, and attitudinal differences between users and non-users toward AI integration in higher education Joanna Blahopoulou1 · Silvia Ortiz-Bonnin1 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",173
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,1,"benefits, costs, and attitudinal differences between users and non-users toward AI integration in higher education Joanna Blahopoulou1 · Silvia Ortiz-Bonnin1 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",33
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 1 Introduction The integration of ChatGPT into higher education has generated considerable inter­ est and discussion due to its potential to transform teaching and learning (Lim et al., 2023). However, there remains a lack of consensus regarding the adoption and appli­ cation of AI tools within academia. ChatGPT, a conversational Artificial Intelligence language model developed by OpenAI, has emerged as the most widely recognized AI-powered chatbot to date. Responses from academic institutions to this innova­ tion vary considerably. Some universities, such as those affiliated with the Grupo 9 Universidades, have introduced training programs aimed at equipping staff with the knowledge and skills to utilize ChatGPT effectively (Grupo 9 Universidades, 2024). Others have revised their plagiarism policies to address concerns about academic integrity in the face of AI-generated content and have developed “new principles on the use of AI in education” (Russell Group, 2023). Conversely, certain institutions, such as Sciences Po Paris, have opted to prohibit the use of ChatGPT entirely, citing concerns over its potential implications for academic standards and ethics (Tlili et al.,",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,2,"the use of AI in education” (Russell Group, 2023). Conversely, certain institutions, such as Sciences Po Paris, have opted to prohibit the use of ChatGPT entirely, citing concerns over its potential implications for academic standards and ethics (Tlili et al., 2023). This divergence in institutional approaches underscores the ongoing need for dialogue and consensus on the role of AI in education. Despite the efforts of educational institutions to address the emergence of AI tools such as ChatGPT and prevent potential misuse, there is still a lack of a unified aca­ demic strategy to optimize its benefits in higher education (Jain & Raghuram, 2024). This is partially due to the novelty of incorporating ChatGPT in academia, which is still in the exploratory phase. A systematic scoping review by Ansari et al. (2024), which analyzed 69 articles, found that ChatGPT is primarily used in higher education to support individual academic tasks for both students and researchers. The study highlights the importance of AI literacy for its effective academic application. Fur­ thermore, they emphasize the necessity of institutional guidelines to ensure responsi­ ble",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,2,"used in higher education to support individual academic tasks for both students and researchers. The study highlights the importance of AI literacy for its effective academic application. Fur­ thermore, they emphasize the necessity of institutional guidelines to ensure responsi­ ble use. Similarly, Baig (2024) performed a systematic literature review analyzing 57 articles from various databases to examine the adaptability of ChatGPT at university. This study identified key factors influencing adoption and usage patterns, includ­ ing perceived ease of use, hedonic motivation, habit, performance expectancy, and personal innovativeness. While recent studies offer valuable insights into the role of ChatGPT in academic settings, there remains a scarcity of empirical, peer-reviewed, and quantitative research comparing the perceptions of AI integration between users and non-users (Abbasi et al., 2024; Crawford et al., 2023; Guo et al., 2024a, b). To shed light on this issue, the goal of this study is to analyze how university stu­ dents use ChatGPT, assess their perceptions of its value and costs, and gather their views on integrating AI technology into educational design in the university context. Additionally, given the",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,2,"the goal of this study is to analyze how university stu­ dents use ChatGPT, assess their perceptions of its value and costs, and gather their views on integrating AI technology into educational design in the university context. Additionally, given the early stage of its use, this study assesses whether there are attitudinal differences between users and non-users toward AI integration in higher education. The following research questions guided our study: ● RQ1: What are the current trends in ChatGPT usage behaviors among university students? ● RQ2: What are the students’ perceptions of the value and costs associated with 1 3 19742",101
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 ChatGPT usage? Do benefits outweigh the risks? ● RQ3: What are the students’ attitudes toward ChatGPT integration in education? Are there differences between ChatGPT users and non-users? The present study aims to contribute to the broader discourse on AI in higher educa­ tion by addressing the literature gaps. First, this study adds to the research area by exploring how students use and perceive ChatGPT. This research aims to identify the main benefits and costs of ChatGPT from the student perspective, examining whether this technology enhances or hinders the educational process at a Spanish public university. Thus, our study responds to the research gap identified by Mumtaz et al. (2024), who stressed the need for studies conducted in diverse cultural settings, given the significant influence of cultural context on attitudes toward AI usage. Sec­ ond, by providing a rigorous quantitative analysis, we address the methodological limitations of previous research, much of which relies on SWOT analyses or qualita­ tive discussions (Abbasi et al., 2024). What is novel about our study is its focus on exploring",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,3,"by providing a rigorous quantitative analysis, we address the methodological limitations of previous research, much of which relies on SWOT analyses or qualita­ tive discussions (Abbasi et al., 2024). What is novel about our study is its focus on exploring student opinions regarding the integration of ChatGPT in higher education. Unlike previous studies that primarily examine factors predicting the unregulated use of ChatGPT, our research provides insights into student perspectives on its formal implementation within academic environments. The findings are intended to guide educators and policymakers in making informed decisions about integrating AI tools like ChatGPT in higher education. In other words, the present study aims to contrib­ ute to the ongoing debate on how academic institutions should approach and integrate ChatGPT. 2 Literature review 2.1 Usage of ChatGPT in higher education Undergraduate students use ChatGPT for both entertainment and academic purposes. Their uses include completing homework, assisting with writing, solving problems, preparing for tests, analyzing data, clarifying concepts, supporting research, answer­ ing questions, and providing supplementary learning materials (Hasanein & Sobaih, 2023; Jishnu et al., 2023; Rahman & Watanobe,",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,3,"academic purposes. Their uses include completing homework, assisting with writing, solving problems, preparing for tests, analyzing data, clarifying concepts, supporting research, answer­ ing questions, and providing supplementary learning materials (Hasanein & Sobaih, 2023; Jishnu et al., 2023; Rahman & Watanobe, 2023). They also engage in con­ structing academic content, seeking information, writing assignments, summarizing, paraphrasing, and taking notes (Jishnu et al., 2023). Additionally, software engineer­ ing students reported that they use ChatGPT for translating texts, generating specific source code, and assisting with complex scientific writing tasks such as literature summarization (Neumann et al., 2023). While the focus of this paper is to analyze the students’ perceptions of ChatGPT, the evolving use of ChatGPT across higher education cannot be fully understood without acknowledging its relevance to other stakeholders. For instance, professors recognize ChatGPT as a versatile tool that supports various aspects of their work, such as designing lectures and assessments, while enhancing teaching methodologies by sourcing ideas, translating texts, and crafting questions that foster deeper student understanding. Additionally, it enables the efficient creation of engaging learning 1 3 19743",178
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,3,"of their work, such as designing lectures and assessments, while enhancing teaching methodologies by sourcing ideas, translating texts, and crafting questions that foster deeper student understanding. Additionally, it enables the efficient creation of engaging learning 1 3 19743",38
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 materials (Alwaqdani, 2024; Tlili et al., 2023; Zhai, 2023; Farhi et al., 2023). These uses provide context for understanding the broader ecosystem in which students are adopting ChatGPT in higher education. Zhang et al. (2024) point out that AI allows teachers to concentrate more on delivering personalized instruction and engaging with students. Thus, contrary to expectations, AI may help enhance human teaching rather than replacing it (Airaj, 2024). 2.2 Benefits and ethical challenges of Chatgpt in higher education Today’s publications in the field of AI tools discuss significant benefits and key advantages of AI chatbots. Among the most notable are the provision of personalized learning environments, immediate feedback, as well as improved access to informa­ tion. The capacity of AI tools to generate context-specific responses depending on the prompt given by the user enhances a dynamic exchange and promotes a deeper understanding of the course contents (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Additionally, they save students time in completing assignments (Das & J.V., 2024). ChatGPT users also acknowledge its potential for fostering innovative thinking and text",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,4,"a dynamic exchange and promotes a deeper understanding of the course contents (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Additionally, they save students time in completing assignments (Das & J.V., 2024). ChatGPT users also acknowledge its potential for fostering innovative thinking and text analy­ sis (Cao et al., 2025). Despite these benefits, it is crucial to acknowledge the potential drawbacks asso­ ciated with AI tools like ChatGPT. Although these tools have been recognized for their potential to foster innovative thinking (Cao et al., 2025), this advantage is not unequivocal. In certain contexts, excessive reliance on AI-generated responses may lead to repetitive patterns and a reduction in independent creative thought, render­ ing its impact on creativity highly contingent on the specific learning environment (Alzubi et al., 2025). Niloy et al. (2024) conducted an experiment demonstrating that the use of ChatGPT among university students negatively affected their creative writ­ ing skills. While ChatGPT can assist in the ideation process, this does not necessarily translate into greater originality or true innovation, as its outputs often reflect existing patterns rather than novel perspectives (Baltà-Salvador et al., 2025).",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,4,"affected their creative writ­ ing skills. While ChatGPT can assist in the ideation process, this does not necessarily translate into greater originality or true innovation, as its outputs often reflect existing patterns rather than novel perspectives (Baltà-Salvador et al., 2025). Moreover, in academic writing, AI-generated text may enhance organization and elaboration, yet it also increases textual similarity and reduces originality, underscoring the need for a balanced and critical approach to its use (Niloy et al., 2024). Further, ChatGPT presents notable ethical challenges that arise from its use. Concerns about reliability and accuracy have been raised as ChatGPT is prone to producing incorrect or illogical outputs, potentially spreading misinformation if not critically assessed (Liebrenz et al., 2023; Williams, 2024). Moreover, many students question the trustworthiness of the information provided by ChatGPT, highlighting its potential to cause uncertainty in academic settings (Das and J.V., 2024). In addi­ tion to these concerns, AI may perpetuate existing biases, leading to unbalanced or unfair perspectives in its generated content (Roshanaei, 2024). Another significant issue is the risk of plagiarism and academic dishonesty, as ChatGPT may",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,4,"and J.V., 2024). In addi­ tion to these concerns, AI may perpetuate existing biases, leading to unbalanced or unfair perspectives in its generated content (Roshanaei, 2024). Another significant issue is the risk of plagiarism and academic dishonesty, as ChatGPT may be used to produce essays or assignments dishonestly, especially in online assignments (Lo, 2023; Soumya R. & J.V., 2024). Finally, users may not always be fully aware of how their information is being stored or processed (Chung et al., 2025), raising privacy concerns about the potential exposure of sensitive or personal data during interac­ 1 3 19744",97
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 tions. While OpenAI implements safeguards, risks remain that user data could be misused or improperly handled, particularly if it is used to improve the AI model. Even though several studies have analyzed the advantages and disadvantages of ChatGPT (Chiu et al., 2023; Qadir et al., 2022), the literature on AI chatbots still lacks an essential component: gathering feedback from students to determine whether they believe the benefits outweigh the risks. Engaging students in this dialogue would pro­ vide valuable insights into their perceptions of ChatGPT’s utility, helping to balance its advantages and limitations in academic settings. 2.3 Integration of ChatGPT in higher education In higher education, a debate persists over how to handle the rise of AI technol­ ogy: should it be restricted or integrated thoughtfully into academic settings? Differ­ ent perspectives are crucial, especially from students who extensively use AI tools (Dempere et al., 2023; Muhie et al., 2023; Ngo, 2023; Zhou, 2023). Students have expressed a need for more structured institutional support, including organized train­ ing sessions and the access to paid",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,5,"especially from students who extensively use AI tools (Dempere et al., 2023; Muhie et al., 2023; Ngo, 2023; Zhou, 2023). Students have expressed a need for more structured institutional support, including organized train­ ing sessions and the access to paid ChatGPT accounts to fully leverage their advan­ tages (Loan et al., 2023). Moreover, disparities in academic performance may arise due to unequal access to advanced AI resources, potentially impacting the quality of students’ output (Kasneci et al., 2023). This raises important questions: Do students expect universities to regulate or ban AI tools? How do they view adapting teaching and evaluation methods for the AI era? Are they interested in learning to use these tools effectively and ethically? It is evident that students will not be the ones making the decision on how and whether AI is integrated into universities. This responsibility should lie in the hands of edu­ cationally trained professionals. However, relegating students to the sidelines of the conversation is an outdated approach that obscures transparency and that does not reflect the changing reality of education. Their exclusion not",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,5,"responsibility should lie in the hands of edu­ cationally trained professionals. However, relegating students to the sidelines of the conversation is an outdated approach that obscures transparency and that does not reflect the changing reality of education. Their exclusion not only disregards the valu­ able viewpoint of those most familiar with the context and needs of the educational environment but also undermines the effectiveness and ethics of implementation. Thus, an effective strategy to enrich the decision-making process for integrating AI into university systems involves a comprehensive approach that accounts for diverse stakeholder perspectives, notably those of students (Alwaqdani, 2024). Moreover, it is important to engage a representative sample of students, encompassing both users and non-users of AI tools such as ChatGPT. As the incorporation of AI tools into our daily lives, particularly in educational environments, is still relatively nascent, it is crucial to acknowledge the varying levels of familiarity among individuals within classrooms. 2.4 Rogers’ diffusion of innovations theory Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations Theory is an appropriate theoretical model for inves­ tigating the adoption and integration of technology in educational",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,5,"is crucial to acknowledge the varying levels of familiarity among individuals within classrooms. 2.4 Rogers’ diffusion of innovations theory Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations Theory is an appropriate theoretical model for inves­ tigating the adoption and integration of technology in educational contexts (Medlin, 2001; Parisot, 1995). According to the Diffusion of Innovations Theory (Rogers, 1995) there are several types of adopters for new technologies: innovators, early adopters, early majority, late majority, and laggards. Innovators are the first to adopt 1 3 19745",81
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 and are willing to take risks. Early adopters are influential in their communities and adopt new technologies before the average person. The early majority adopt innova­ tions slightly before the average time, followed by the late majority, who are skepti­ cal and adopt after the average time. Laggards are the last to adopt, typically resistant to change and influenced only by traditional values. We could categorize students using ChatGPT in the first year of its release as “early adopters”, while non-users could be classified as either part of the “late majority” or “laggards”. Early adopters are those who are quick to embrace new technologies, often because they see the potential benefits early on and are willing to take risks. This group often promotes the adoption of new technologies. Late majority and laggards are more cautious about innovations. The late majority may eventually adopt the technology once they per­ ceive that the risks are lower, and the benefits are more clearly demonstrated by the early adopters’ experiences. Laggards tend to be the last to adopt",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,6,"cautious about innovations. The late majority may eventually adopt the technology once they per­ ceive that the risks are lower, and the benefits are more clearly demonstrated by the early adopters’ experiences. Laggards tend to be the last to adopt an innovation, often due to resistance to change or because they have the least economic resources. These groups often resist the integration of new technologies. Accordingly, it would be interesting to explore if there are differences among users and non-users regarding their opinion about the integration of AI tools at university. 3 Method The study uses a quantitative approach, specifically an online survey, to gather data on students’ use of ChatGPT, their perceptions of its value and costs, and their atti­ tudes toward AI integration in higher education. This method was chosen because it allows for the collection of measurable data, making it well-suited to identifying patterns and drawing comparisons between users and non-users of AI tools, thus pro­ viding a clear, data-driven understanding of student attitudes. Moreover, quantitative methods are widely used in studies in higher education, as they",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,6,"data, making it well-suited to identifying patterns and drawing comparisons between users and non-users of AI tools, thus pro­ viding a clear, data-driven understanding of student attitudes. Moreover, quantitative methods are widely used in studies in higher education, as they enable researchers to involve larger samples and generalize findings to broader populations. This is essen­ tial for establishing trends in AI adoption, supporting reliable and scalable insights that can inform policy and educational practices. 3.1 Participants The 737 participants in our study were undergraduate students from the University of the Balearic Islandsd with an average age of 21 years (SD = 4.25), and 63.5% of them were women and 36.5% were men. Overall, 70% of the participants reported being ChatGPT users, with a mean age of 21 years (SD = 4.54), while the remaining 30% were non-users, also with a mean age of 21 years (SD = 3.42). Among women, 64.68% were users and 35.32% were non-users, whereas among men, 82.63% were users and 17.37% were non-users. Students from various degree programs partici­ pated in the study, as detailed in",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,6,"mean age of 21 years (SD = 3.42). Among women, 64.68% were users and 35.32% were non-users, whereas among men, 82.63% were users and 17.37% were non-users. Students from various degree programs partici­ pated in the study, as detailed in Annex 1. The University of the Balearic Islands, a public institution located in Palma de Mallorca(Spain), has been committed to academic excellence and educational inno­ vation since its founding in 1978. The University of the Balearic Islands offers a diverse range of undergraduate and postgraduate programs across disciplines such as 1 3 19746",93
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 sciences, engineering, humanities, social sciences, health sciences, law, and educa­ tion. With approximately 11,736 undergraduate students, the university is recognized for its pioneering research and strong community connections. It also actively pro­ motes internationalization through numerous exchange programs and collaborations, making it an ideal setting for exploring student attitudes on the integration of AI tools like ChatGPT in higher education. 3.2 Instruments The following scales were completed by all participants (users and non-users). Use of ChatGPT was measured by asking “Have you used the AI tool ChatGPT?”. The three answer options were: “No, never”, “Yes, the free version”, “Yes, the paid premium version”. Attitudes toward AI integration in education was measured by asking them six independent items which were developed based on the conclusions drawn from a workshop. Our university organized a voluntary AI awareness workshop for educa­ tors from various faculties. The aim of the workshop was to deepen understanding and encourage the dialogue on the integration of AI in university. Through guided group discussion and brainstorming activities, several key insights were identified",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,7,"awareness workshop for educa­ tors from various faculties. The aim of the workshop was to deepen understanding and encourage the dialogue on the integration of AI in university. Through guided group discussion and brainstorming activities, several key insights were identified regarding how to address the emergence of AI tools like ChatGPT in academia. These conclusions were shared with all participants by the workshop instructors via an inter­ nal university platform. We transformed the main collective reflections into six items used in this research. The items covered the following aspects: AI training necessity (“the university should provide trainings about how to use AI tools like ChatGPT as a teaching and learning tool”), Teaching AI adaptation (“professors should adapt their teaching methods and evaluation systems to the era of AI”), AI ethical education (“if my university offered a free course on the ethical use of AI tools like ChatGPT, I would enrol”), AI student assessment (“I endorse the use of Artificial Intelligence tools like ChatGPT by professors to evaluate students’ work and exams”), AI regula­ tion (“the use of Artificial Intelligence tools",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,7,"ethical use of AI tools like ChatGPT, I would enrol”), AI student assessment (“I endorse the use of Artificial Intelligence tools like ChatGPT by professors to evaluate students’ work and exams”), AI regula­ tion (“the use of Artificial Intelligence tools like ChatGPT should be regulated by public institutions: government, university, etc.”), University AI ban (“the use of AI tools like ChatGPT for academic tasks should be forbidden”). The response options were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree). The following scales were exclusively completed by participants who reported using ChatGPT, as they evaluate constructs directly related to the experience of using the tool. Use frequency of ChatGPT was measured by asking users “How often do you use the AI tool ChatGPT?”. The answer options were: “Once a month”, “Once a week”, “Every day”, “Several times a day”. Respondents purpose of using ChatGPT was measured by asking users “What do you use the ChatGPT AI tool for? (You can select multiple answers)”. The 8 answer options were: “Translations”, “Information search”, “Completion of homework or academic tasks”,",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,7,"a day”. Respondents purpose of using ChatGPT was measured by asking users “What do you use the ChatGPT AI tool for? (You can select multiple answers)”. The 8 answer options were: “Translations”, “Information search”, “Completion of homework or academic tasks”, “Generating texts that I ‘copy and paste’”, “Answering questions related to academic assignments”, “Summarizing documents”, “Entertainment”, and “Other purposes”. We selected these eight items based on a comprehensive list of usage purposes by Schepman & Rodway (2020). 1 3 19747",80
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,8,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 Risk-reward appraisal (Shahsavar & Choudhury, 2023) was measured by asking students: “To what extent do you agree with the following: The benefits of using ChatGPT outweigh any potential risks”. The response options were a four-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 4 (strongly agree). High scores reflect the perception that the advantages of using ChatGPT are surpassing any disadvantages associated with its use. Student perceived value of AI (Chan & Zhou, 2023) was used to understand stu­ dents’ perceptions about advantages of using AI tools like ChatGPT, with items like “I believe generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can improve my overall aca­ demic performance. The response options were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree). Student perceived cost of AI (Chan & Zhou, 2023) was used to measure students’ perception about disadvantages of using ChatGPT. It consists of 4 items; a sample item is “Generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT will hinder my development of generic or transferable skills such as teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership skills.” The",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,8,"measure students’ perception about disadvantages of using ChatGPT. It consists of 4 items; a sample item is “Generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT will hinder my development of generic or transferable skills such as teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership skills.” The response options were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly dis­ agree) to 5 (strongly agree). The last two scales demonstrated acceptable internal consistency, with Cronbach’s α = 0.84 and ω = 0.85 for perceived value, and Cronbach’s α = 0.70 and ω = 0.72 for perceived costs. High scores reflect greater perceived value or costs of ChatGPT. Reliability is supported by values above 0.70, consistent with psychometric standards (Nunnally & Bernstein, 1994). 3.3 Procedure A self-administered online questionnaire using google forms was distributed to undergraduate students at the University of the Balearic Islands, which has a total population of 11,736 undergraduate students. Based on a sample size calculation for finite populations and a confidence level of 99%, we determined that a sample size of 631 students would ensure a margin of error of 5%. By surpassing this threshold, our",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,8,"11,736 undergraduate students. Based on a sample size calculation for finite populations and a confidence level of 99%, we determined that a sample size of 631 students would ensure a margin of error of 5%. By surpassing this threshold, our sample size provides a high level of reliability for our findings. We used a combination of convenience sampling and purposive sampling to gather a broad representation of students across academic fields. We contacted professors from different degree programs to facilitate participation, which allowed us to reach students across multiple areas, including engineering, health, and social sciences. This approach ensured accessibility while aiming to achieve a balanced representa­ tion from the main academic branches within the university. The professors were informed per mail about the aim of the study and were asked to voluntarily transfer 15 min of their lecture time to our research project interfering as little as possible in the normal development of the course. We conducted data collections during ordi­ nary class schedule at the beginning of the academic year 2023/2024, in the weeks between October and November",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,8,"to our research project interfering as little as possible in the normal development of the course. We conducted data collections during ordi­ nary class schedule at the beginning of the academic year 2023/2024, in the weeks between October and November 2023. Prior to answering the questions, all respondents were provided with information about the study’s objectives and the confidential handling of their data. Participants were adequately informed of the voluntary nature of participation and their right to withdraw; informed consent was obtained online before participation; data confiden­ 1 3 19748",90
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 tiality was ensured. We also met the requirements of Spanish legislation (Organic Law 3/2018 of 5 December on Personal Data Protection and Digital Rights Guaran­ tees). The study conforms to internationally accepted ethical guidelines (Declaration of Helsinki) in relation to research with human beings. Our research has received formal approval from the independent ethics committee of the University of the Bale­ aric Islands.. 3.4 Data analysis All analyses were conducted using Jamovi version 1.2.22 (Jamovi, 2021). We com­ puted descriptive statistics, percentages, and non-parametric tests (Wilcoxon signed- rank test and Mann-Whitney U) to address the Research Questions (RQ). For RQ1, we performed descriptive analyses, reporting percentages to illustrate general usage trends of ChatGPT among students. For RQ2, we used the Wilcoxon signed-rank test to assess perceptions of value and costs associated with ChatGPT usage among ChatGPT users. For RQ3, we applied the Mann-Whitney U test to compare users and non-users across the full sample. Additionally, we calculated the rank biserial correlation as a measure of effect size, offering insights into the strength and direction",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,9,"among ChatGPT users. For RQ3, we applied the Mann-Whitney U test to compare users and non-users across the full sample. Additionally, we calculated the rank biserial correlation as a measure of effect size, offering insights into the strength and direction of differences observed between groups or conditions in each test. This measure provides a more nuanced interpreta­ tion of the results, complementing the statistical significance findings. 4 Results The first research question aimed to identify the current trends in ChatGPT usage behaviors among university students. Results show that the 70% of the participants have used ChatGPT. Only 1% of participants reported paying for a subscription to ChatGPT. 65% of the participants who use ChatGPT employ it once a month, 24% once a week, 10% almost every day and the 1% several times a day. The most com­ mon student’s purpose of using ChatGPT was information search (78.5%) followed by answering questions related to their studies (53.8%). Figure 1 illustrates the per­ centage distribution of various purposes from a predefined list for which students use ChatGPT. It’s noteworthy that students could",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,9,"of using ChatGPT was information search (78.5%) followed by answering questions related to their studies (53.8%). Figure 1 illustrates the per­ centage distribution of various purposes from a predefined list for which students use ChatGPT. It’s noteworthy that students could choose multiple options. The second research question aimed to identify the students’ perceptions of the value and costs associated with ChatGPT usage. First, we examined how the stu­ dents weigh the potential positive and negative consequences of using ChatGPT. The risk-reward scale was deliberately designed with response options from 1 to 4, thus without a neutral midpoint, to force respondents to make a decision. The mean score for risk-reward appraisal was 2.77 (SD = 0.84). A neutral score would have been 2.5. Thus, students tend to perceive that the benefits outweigh the risks. The average score of students perceived value of AI was 3.61 (SD = 0.73). Accord­ ing to(Pimentel, 2010) to interpret means by referring to the Likert Scale interval, a point mean falling in the range 1.00 to 1.80 indicates “strongly disagree”, 1.81 to 2.60 represents “disagree”, 2.61",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,9,"of AI was 3.61 (SD = 0.73). Accord­ ing to(Pimentel, 2010) to interpret means by referring to the Likert Scale interval, a point mean falling in the range 1.00 to 1.80 indicates “strongly disagree”, 1.81 to 2.60 represents “disagree”, 2.61 to 3.40 is regarded as “neutral”, 3.41 to 4.20 indicates “agree”, and 4.21 to 5.00 “strongly agree”. Thus, participants tend to express agree­ 1 3 19749",66
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,10,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 Item M SD 1. Students must learn how to use generative AI technologies well for their career. 3.93 1.12 2. I believe generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can improve my digital competence. 3.86 1.09 3. I believe generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can improve my overall academic performance. 3.46 1.21 4. I think generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can help me become a better writer. 3.02 1.26 5. I can ask questions to generative AI technolo­ gies such as ChatGPT that I would otherwise not voice out to my professor. 2.95 1.37 6. Generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT will not judge me, so I feel comfortable with it. 3.15 1.38 7. I think AI technologies such as ChatGPT is a great tool for student support services due to anonymity. 3.16 1.24 8. I believe generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can help me save time. 4.28 0.95 9. I believe AI technologies such as ChatGPT can provide me with unique insights and perspectives that I may not have thought of",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,10,1.24 8. I believe generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT can help me save time. 4.28 0.95 9. I believe AI technologies such as ChatGPT can provide me with unique insights and perspectives that I may not have thought of myself. 3.84 1.08 10. I think AI technologies such as ChatGPT can provide me with personalized and immediate feed­ back and suggestions for my assignments. 3.67 1.13 11. I think AI technologies such as ChatGPT is a great tool as it is available 24/7. 4.34 0.95 Total scale 3.61 0.73 Table 1 Descriptive results for the Student Perceived Value of IA Scale. Fig. 1 Respondents purpose of using ChatGPT 1 3 19750,112
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 ment with the concept. The three features of ChatGPT with the highest mean scores were “I believe that AI tools like ChatGPT can help me save time” (item 8), “I believe that AI tools like ChatGPT are a great tool since they are available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week” (item 11) and “Students must learn how to use generative AI technolo­ gies well for their career” (item 1). Table 1 shows the average scores and standard deviation for each item and for the total scale. The average score of students perceived cost of AI was 2.58 (SD = 0.94), indicat­ ing that students generally perceive low costs associated with AI usage. The costs of ChatGPT with the highest scores were “Using generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT to complete assignments undermines the value of a university education” (item 1) and “I can become over-reliant on generative AI technologies” (item 4). Table 2 shows the average scores and standard deviation for each item and for the total scale. Additionally, we calculated Wilcoxon",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,11,"the value of a university education” (item 1) and “I can become over-reliant on generative AI technologies” (item 4). Table 2 shows the average scores and standard deviation for each item and for the total scale. Additionally, we calculated Wilcoxon signed-rank test to compare whether stu­ dents’ perceptions of value were significantly higher than perceived costs. We used non-parametric tests because the Shapiro-Wilk results indicated that the data were not normally distributed. Results indicate that mean scores for perceived value (M = 3.59) and costs (M = 2.50) were significantly different (W = 130305, p <.001, rrb= 1), indi­ cating that students perceive significantly higher value than costs of using ChatGPT. The aim of the last research question was to explore the students’ attitudes toward ChatGPT integration in education. 60.6% of the participants believe that the univer­ sity should provide trainings about how to use AI tools like ChatGPT as a teaching and learning tool, and 43.6% believe that professors should adapt their teaching meth­ ods and evaluation systems to the era of AI. When students were asked if they",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,11,"trainings about how to use AI tools like ChatGPT as a teaching and learning tool, and 43.6% believe that professors should adapt their teaching meth­ ods and evaluation systems to the era of AI. When students were asked if they would like to be assessed by AI, the majority (51.3%) disagree with the idea that professors use ChatGPT to evaluate their work and exams. Furthermore, 40.5% would enrol in a free course on the ethical use of ChatGPT if offered by their university. Concerning the regulation of the use of ChatGPT, 39.8% of the participants expressed support for the proposition “the use of Artificial Intelligence tools like ChatGPT should be regulated by public institutions: government, university, etc.” and only the 19.7% of the participants endorsed prohibiting the use of ChatGPT at university (Fig. 2). Finally, we explored potential attitudinal differences toward AI integration between ChatGPT users and non-users through U Mann-Whitney. We employed non- Item M SD 1. Using generative AI technologies such as Chat­ GPT to complete assignments undermines the value of a university education. 2.90 1.25 2. Generative",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,11,"toward AI integration between ChatGPT users and non-users through U Mann-Whitney. We employed non- Item M SD 1. Using generative AI technologies such as Chat­ GPT to complete assignments undermines the value of a university education. 2.90 1.25 2. Generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT will limit my opportunities to interact with others and socialize while completing coursework. 2.20 1.23 3. Generative AI technologies such as ChatGPT will hinder my development of generic or transfer­ able skills such as teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership skills. 2.59 1.32 4. I can become over-reliant on generative AI technologies. 2.63 1.41 Total Scale 2.58 0.94 Table 2 Descriptive results for the Student Perceived Costs of IA Scale. 1 3 19751",116
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,12,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 parametric tests due to the Shapiro-Wilk results demonstrating that the data were not normally distributed. The results of the means comparison tests indicate significant differences between ChatGPT users and non-users in 5 out of 6 areas. Specifically, users score significantly higher than non-users in the following areas: “AI train­ ing necessity” (U = 42675, p <.001, rpb =.21), “Teaching AI adaptation” (U = 41916, p <.001, rpb =.22), and “AI student assessment” (U = 44365, p <.001, rpb =.18). Con­ versely, ChatGPT non-users, scores significantly higher than users in “AI regulation” (U = 44369, p <.001, rpb =.18). and “University AI ban” (U = 27200, p <.001, rpb =.50). No significant differences were found in “AI ethical education” (U = 53203, p =.72, rpb =.01). Rank biserial correlations (rpb) showed medium or large effect size follow­ ing the. Cohen’s (1988) general rule which considers r = ±.10 as small, r = ±.30 as medium and r = ±.50 as large effect sizes (Fig. 3). 5 Discussion The purpose of the study was to enrich",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,12,"size follow­ ing the. Cohen’s (1988) general rule which considers r = ±.10 as small, r = ±.30 as medium and r = ±.50 as large effect sizes (Fig. 3). 5 Discussion The purpose of the study was to enrich the current debate on how academic institu­ tions should approach and integrate AI tools like ChatGPT at university by giving students a voice. To capture the picture of students’ perceptions regarding the role of ChatGPT in academia, we conducted a quantitative study exploring three Research Questions (RQ). The discussion is structured to address these RQ while maintaining Fig. 2 Attitudes toward AI integration in education. Note: The categories have been obtained as fol­ lows: “Disagree” is the average of scores 1 (completely disagree) and 2 (disagree), “Neutral” is the score 3, and “Agree” is the average of scores 4 (agree) and 5 (completely agree)” 1 3 19752",147
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,13,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 clarity and a logical flow in the presentation of the findings. The first RQ aimed to investigate to what extent university students use ChatGPT and for what purposes. Our findings align with prior research (Appleby, 2023), indicating that ChatGPT is used by a significant proportion of students, though infrequently. Most report using it for academic and non-academic tasks, particularly for simplifying complex content and managing information overload (Adeshola & Adepoju, 2023; Farrokhnia et al., 2023). This suggests that AI tools like ChatGPT are becoming increasingly present in students’ academic and personal lives. Given this trend, outright bans may not be practical or effective (Baig, 2024). Therefore, it is important for universities to acknowledge this reality and consider how to appropriately address the role of AI in higher education. It is crucial to note that some students have never used ChatGPT. According to Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations Theory (1995), the adoption of new technologies follows a process where early adopters tend to explore a broader range of applica­ tions before a technology becomes widely accepted.",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,13,"some students have never used ChatGPT. According to Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations Theory (1995), the adoption of new technologies follows a process where early adopters tend to explore a broader range of applica­ tions before a technology becomes widely accepted. This could explain why some students use ChatGPT for diverse academic and non-academic purposes, while others remain hesitant. The coexistence of ChatGPT users and non-users in academic set­ tings poses significant challenges for universities such as the potential decline in stu­ dent morale due to perceived unfairness among non-users, who may dedicate more time and effort to their work yet still receive lower grades compared to those who utilize AI tools (Bergström et al., 2024). Addressing these disparities requires uni­ versities to establish clear guidelines and support systems that promote equity while embracing technological advancements (Ansari et al., 2024). The second RQ referred to the students’ perceptions of the benefits and risks asso­ ciated with ChatGPT usage. Consistent with sentiment analysis studies suggesting that most individuals express positive attitudes towards the tool (Adeshola & Ade­ Fig. 3 ChatGPT users’ and",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,13,second RQ referred to the students’ perceptions of the benefits and risks asso­ ciated with ChatGPT usage. Consistent with sentiment analysis studies suggesting that most individuals express positive attitudes towards the tool (Adeshola & Ade­ Fig. 3 ChatGPT users’ and non-users’ attitudes toward AI integration in education 1 3 19753,50
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,14,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 poju, 2023), our findings indicate that students generally viewed ChatGPT as more beneficial than harmful. Among the key advantages, students highlighted accessi­ bility and time efficiency, reinforcing prior research that identifies these features as ChatGPT’s main strengths (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023; Dwivedi et al., 2023). Its 24/7 availability allows students to access academic support at any time, benefiting those with unconventional schedules or limited access to other resources (Digital Learn­ ing Institute, 2024; Mastery Coding, 2024). This availability might reduce the per­ ceived need to attend classes, potentially altering the face-to-face learning patterns. This implies that universities must adapt their teaching strategies to engage students more effectively and encourage active participation, ensuring that in-person learning remains valuable and relevant. Further, the emphasis on “time saving” suggests a shift towards productivity, but it also prompts reflection about how that saved time will be used. Will students invest it in deeper learning, or will they simply consider the task finished by AI and focus on non-academic activities? While students appreci­ ate ChatGPT’s efficiency, this reliance raises",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,14,"reflection about how that saved time will be used. Will students invest it in deeper learning, or will they simply consider the task finished by AI and focus on non-academic activities? While students appreci­ ate ChatGPT’s efficiency, this reliance raises important questions about its long-term impact on academic development (Jain & Raghuram, 2024). Beyond these practical benefits, participants also emphasized the relevance of ChatGPT for their future careers. The results indicate their interest in learning how to use ChatGPT, driven by the belief that they will need to apply this knowledge in their professional lives. This is consistent with the perception of several scholars who emphasize the central importance of teaching generative AI technologies in higher education, ensuring that students are well-equipped for the world outside the campus (Abdelwahab et al., 2023; Chan & Zhou, 2023). Similarly, recent research on the development of digital competences underscores the significance of digital skills for higher education students, particularly emphasizing the value of digital proficiency for their employability (Martzoukou et al., 2022; Montañez-Juan et al., 2023). In line with these findings, Cao et",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,14,"the development of digital competences underscores the significance of digital skills for higher education students, particularly emphasizing the value of digital proficiency for their employability (Martzoukou et al., 2022; Montañez-Juan et al., 2023). In line with these findings, Cao et al. (2025) found that students are concerned about the implications of ChatGPT for their future career development, as they fear that the increasing reliance on AI could lead to the replacement of certain occupations. For universities, this highlights the urgent need to integrate AI literacy into the curricu­ lum, ensuring that students develop the necessary skills to navigate an increasingly AI-driven job market. While students recognized several advantages of ChatGPT, they also highlighted its potential drawbacks. The most prominent risk—overreliance on AI— is widely discussed in the higher education literature (e.g., Alzubi et al., 2025; Rahman & Watanobe, 2023; Seetharaman, 2023; Singh et al., 2022). Research analyzing discus­ sions on social media platforms highlights that early adopters of ChatGPT frequently debate the risk of excessive dependence, fearing that it may encourage superficial learning and weaken students’ critical thinking skills (Mogavi",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,14,"2023; Singh et al., 2022). Research analyzing discus­ sions on social media platforms highlights that early adopters of ChatGPT frequently debate the risk of excessive dependence, fearing that it may encourage superficial learning and weaken students’ critical thinking skills (Mogavi et al., 2024). Excessive dependence on AI for academic tasks can undermine critical thinking and creativ­ ity, as students may increasingly rely on AI-generated solutions instead of actively engaging with complex problems (Farhi et al., 2023). This can hinder the develop­ ment of original thought by limiting the use of essential cognitive skills. Additionally, the ‘fear of the blank page’ can arise, making it difficult to initiate or progress through assignments without AI assistance. Our findings indicate that students also perceive AI-assisted assignment completion as undermining the value of university educa­ 1 3 19754",134
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 tion. This aligns with Bergström et al. (2024), who found that Finnish university students refrained from using ChatGPT in academic settings when they viewed it as ethically problematic, primarily due to concerns about potential misuse for cheating. Academic dishonesty diminishes the value of university education by undermining the assurance that degree holders have genuinely acquired the necessary knowledge and skills. These findings underscore the necessity for proactive measures to uphold academic integrity in higher education, especially in the context of increasing use of AI tools. The third RQ investigated students’ attitudes toward the integration of AI in higher education. Our findings suggest that students generally support AI integration, partic­ ularly through structured university training on its effective use. However, attitudes differ significantly between users and non-users, revealing contrasting perspectives on AI’s role in academia. Users are more inclined to advocate for the adaptation of teaching methods and the provision of training about how to use AI as a teaching and learning tool, while non-users are more likely to demand regulation or even its prohibition",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,15,"Users are more inclined to advocate for the adaptation of teaching methods and the provision of training about how to use AI as a teaching and learning tool, while non-users are more likely to demand regulation or even its prohibition in academic tasks. However, it is interesting to note that, when it comes to using AI for student assessment, both users and non-users express opposition, with both groups scoring below 3 on a 1 to 5 scale. It would be insightful to explore whether this opposition arises from concerns about fairness and accuracy. Students may fear that AI tools could misinterpret their abilities or reduce the personal touch of evaluation, leading to a lack of trust in the assessment process (Hernández León et al., 2024). These findings challenge the common assumption that students over­ whelmingly support ChatGPT’s adoption, while faculty members are the primary skeptics. Instead, our results highlight that skepticism is also present among students, particularly among those who have not yet adopted the tool. One possible explana­ tion for these differences is provided by Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,15,"members are the primary skeptics. Instead, our results highlight that skepticism is also present among students, particularly among those who have not yet adopted the tool. One possible explana­ tion for these differences is provided by Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations Theory (1995). ChatGPT users can be classified as “early adopters”, who perceive ChatGPT as a valuable tool that enhances learning and efficiency, having directly experienced its benefits. Conversely, non-users can be classified as “laggards”, who may be more resistant to change due to ethical concerns, the absence of clear institutional guide­ lines, or uncertainty about its long-term impact on learning. Beyond adoption pat­ terns, this distinction also raises broader issues related to accessibility and equity. While some non-users hesitate due to skepticism, others may face financial barri­ ers that limit their ability to access AI tools with advanced features. Although some AI tools offer free access, many premium functions—such as faster response times, priority access, and enhanced capabilities—are restricted to paid versions. Prior research has shown that economic constraints influence students’ willingness to use AI (Romero-Rodríguez et al., 2023), and",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,15,"some AI tools offer free access, many premium functions—such as faster response times, priority access, and enhanced capabilities—are restricted to paid versions. Prior research has shown that economic constraints influence students’ willingness to use AI (Romero-Rodríguez et al., 2023), and disparities in access to these tools could exacerbate educational inequalities. As Kasneci et al. (2023) highlight, if AI becomes an integral part of university education, institutions must consider how to ensure fair and inclusive access, allowing all students to benefit equally, regardless of financial means. Despite these differences, both users and non-users share a common interest in university-provided training on the ethical use of AI. This is an encouraging finding, as it suggests a growing awareness of the need for digital literacy and responsible AI practices in academic settings (Atlas, 2023). Previous research has emphasized the 1 3 19755",139
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 importance of strengthening education in academic ethics and defining clear bound­ aries for AI use in university contexts (Graf & Bernardi, 2023). Universities should take advantage of this interest by implementing structured training programs that ensure students not only understand the benefits of AI but also its limitations and ethi­ cal considerations (Ansari et al., 2024; Baltà-Salvador et al., 2025). Addressing these concerns through educational activities may help bridge the gap between early adopt­ ers and students with reservations, fostering a more informed and balanced approach to AI in higher education. This study provides a crucial first insight into the current state of university stu­ dents and the adaptation of chatbots in higher education. Our findings go beyond merely describing user intentions, offering substantial empirical data on student atti­ tudes toward ChatGPT integration, grounded in insights from a large sample of stu­ dents within the Spanish cultural context. Building on Ma’s (2024) systematic review of AI-based chatbot integration in higher education—which highlights the underrep­ resentation of large-scale studies (defined as those with over 300",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,16,"in insights from a large sample of stu­ dents within the Spanish cultural context. Building on Ma’s (2024) systematic review of AI-based chatbot integration in higher education—which highlights the underrep­ resentation of large-scale studies (defined as those with over 300 participants)—our research addresses this gap by utilizing a larger sample, providing a more compre­ hensive perspective on the topic. Additionally, we designed a set of items to evaluate key aspects of AI integration in higher education, such as whether ChatGPT should be restricted or encouraged for academic tasks. These items provide valuable insights into students’ awareness, perceptions, and expectations regarding the use of AI tools in university settings. Our study contributes to the ongoing discourse on the necessity of regulating AI, providing technology education, updating assessment guidelines, and addressing eth­ ical concerns. More specifically, we anticipate that this study will inform university policies, emphasizing the ethical deployment of such tools in alignment with prin­ ciples of privacy and security. 5.1 Practical implications for higher education Based on our findings, we identify several practical implications that can guide the responsible integration",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,16,"inform university policies, emphasizing the ethical deployment of such tools in alignment with prin­ ciples of privacy and security. 5.1 Practical implications for higher education Based on our findings, we identify several practical implications that can guide the responsible integration of AI in education. First, although AI tools like ChatGPT are increasingly present in academic settings, not all students have adopted them due to unfamiliarity or limited access. To promote equitable opportunities, universities should provide structured training programs that equip students with the necessary digital skills while fostering critical thinking about its limitations. Institutions should also address how disparities in access to advanced AI features might affect student engagement, ensuring that financial constraints do not become a barrier to effective AI use. Second, despite AI’s advantages, students express concerns about overreliance and the potential erosion of essential skills. To address this, universities should main­ tain non-AI activities that promote critical thinking, collaborative problem-solving, and hand-on learning. Approaches like the flipped classroom model, where students acquire foundational knowledge independently and use class time for interactive exercises, have proven effective (Nguyen et",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,16,"this, universities should main­ tain non-AI activities that promote critical thinking, collaborative problem-solving, and hand-on learning. Approaches like the flipped classroom model, where students acquire foundational knowledge independently and use class time for interactive exercises, have proven effective (Nguyen et al., 2015; Singh et al., 2022). Finally, universities must establish clear ethical guidelines tailored to each dis­ cipline’s needs to ensure responsible AI use. One key issue among students is the 1 3 19756",74
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 potential misuse of generative AI technologies like ChatGPT for plagiarism, which they perceive as undermining the value of a university education. To mitigate this, educators should integrate ethical training into the curriculum, helping students develop critical awareness of AI’s strengths and limitations. Institutions could imple­ ment measures such as requiring an AI Usage Appendix in assignments, where stu­ dents document their interactions with AI tools including prompts, outputs, and their contributions to the final work. For team projects, a responsibility section detailing individual contributions and AI usage fosters accountability and ensures a transpar­ ent project workflow. These measures encourage ethical engagement with AI while maintaining academic integrity and fostering essential skills. 5.2 Limitations and future research Despite the valuable insights provided by this study, the use of a non-probability convenience sample of a single university constrains some limitations such as the generalisability of the findings. Future research should use quota sampling methods for more representative and equalized sub-sample sizes. In this way, comparisons between students across different academic disciplines could be studied. Second, conducting",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,17,"university constrains some limitations such as the generalisability of the findings. Future research should use quota sampling methods for more representative and equalized sub-sample sizes. In this way, comparisons between students across different academic disciplines could be studied. Second, conducting longitudinal studies might offer insights into the evolution of students’ perception and usage patterns of ChatGPT over time, thereby further examining the applicability of the Diffusion of Innovations Theory. As our study specifically shows that non-users report a more restrictive approach to how universi­ ties should deal with IA tools, future research should focus on addressing the disad­ vantages of AI and indentifying ways to integrate AI tools that align with consensus among all stakeholders, including professors etc. Future studies could for example explore how students and educators envision ChatGPT’s ideal features for academic use. Gathering user feedback would offer valuable insights for companies like Ope­ nAI to guide improvements and enhance its effectiveness in education. Further, given ChatGPT’s recent release, further research is needed to assess its impact on educa­ tional quality. Third, this study presents six useful items",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,17,"valuable insights for companies like Ope­ nAI to guide improvements and enhance its effectiveness in education. Further, given ChatGPT’s recent release, further research is needed to assess its impact on educa­ tional quality. Third, this study presents six useful items to assess the student attitudes toward AI integration in education. Forthcoming studies could adapt the implemented instru­ ment to explore AI related concerns, such as the worry that AI-based assessment might undermine students’ efforts and sense of achievement, making them feel that their work is less valued when evaluated by automated AI systems. Finally, there is an urgent need for in-depth studies to guide the adoption of AI technologies like ChatGPT while considering divergent perspectives. Qualitative studies might provide richer insights into how students and educators perceive ben­ efits and risks of using ChatGPT, including their views on its impact on learning, creativity, and critical thinking. Specifically, it would be interesting to explore how the perceived risk of overreliance on AI tools relates to the view of ChatGPT’s time- saving benefits. While efficiency is a key advantage, it may also",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,17,"on learning, creativity, and critical thinking. Specifically, it would be interesting to explore how the perceived risk of overreliance on AI tools relates to the view of ChatGPT’s time- saving benefits. While efficiency is a key advantage, it may also encourage depen­ dency. Comas-Forgas et al. (2010) linked time pressure to academic dishonesty - could speed, ChatGPT’s greatest asset, be a “Trojan horse”? Future studies could investigate whether its quick and easy solutions diminish the requirement for critical 1 3 19757",81
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19741–19764 thinking and analysis, potentially hindering the development of users’ creativity and reflective abilities (Tlili et al., 2023). 6 Conclusion Amid the debate surrounding the use of ChatGPT in academia, with some institutions banning it, the purpose of the study was to explore students’ perceptions of ChatGPT in academic settings, particularly focusing on its benefits and drawbacks. Key find­ ings show that many students perceive ChatGPT as a valuable learning tool, with most highlighting more advantages than disadvantages. However, a subset of non- users opposes its integration and prefers its prohibition. Universities must balance these perspectives by promoting responsible use while addressing the concerns of those who choose not to use the tool. One key recommendation is to ensure that non- users do not feel disadvantaged in comparison to those who might misuse ChatGPT for academic dishonesty, such as plagiarism to achieve better grades. To mitigate this, universities should implement robust academic integrity measures to prevent misuse. Additionally, providing equitable access to AI tools like ChatGPT is essential to avoid creating inequalities among students. It",180
A117,A117.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13575-9,18,"academic dishonesty, such as plagiarism to achieve better grades. To mitigate this, universities should implement robust academic integrity measures to prevent misuse. Additionally, providing equitable access to AI tools like ChatGPT is essential to avoid creating inequalities among students. It is important to note that the find­ ings from this study cannot be generalized to the entire student population. Further research is necessary to evaluate the long-term impact of AI integration in education, ensuring its use remains ethical, inclusive, and aligned with academic goals. 7 ANNEX Table 3 Sample description. Frecuencies and percentatges of degree programs Degree Frequency % Business Administration 57 7.7% Biology 4 0.5% Law 93 12.6% Hotel Management 33 4.5% Economics 28 3.8% Building Construction 4 0.5% Early Childhood Education 34 4.6% Primary Education 44 6.0% English Studies 55 7.5% Philosophy 15 2.0% Physics 39 5.3% Geography 1 0.1% Industrial and Automatic Electronic Engineering 38 5.2% Computer Engineering 41 5.6% Telematics Engineering 9 1.2% Catalan Language and Literature 11 1.5% Spanish Language and Literature 10 1.4% 1 3 19758",172
