A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,1,"Social Psychology of Education (2025) 28:113 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2 Abstract Academic dishonesty remains a persistent concern for educational institutions, threatening the reputation of universities. The emergence of Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools exacerbates this challenge as they can be used for chatting but also for cheating. Several scientific papers have analyzed the advantages and risks of using AI tools like ChatGPT in academia. On the one side it offers increased accessibil­ ity of information and facilitates personalized learning. On the other side several risks are associated with the usage of ChatGPT, including the generation of inac­ curate information, and the facilitation of academic dishonesty like cheating and plagiarism. This study examines how students’ Perception of Academic Dishonesty (PAD) and their perception of the risks associated with using ChatGPT, are linked to a reduced use frequency and intention to use this AI tool. A sample of 468 un­ dergraduate students answered an online survey. Our findings indicate a negative and significant relationship between perceived risk, and both use frequency and intention to use ChatGPT in the future. Additionally, perceived risk mediates the relationship between",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,1,"sample of 468 un­ dergraduate students answered an online survey. Our findings indicate a negative and significant relationship between perceived risk, and both use frequency and intention to use ChatGPT in the future. Additionally, perceived risk mediates the relationship between PAD and ChatGPT usage patterns. This is the first study to demonstrate that students with high PAD present higher levels of awareness of the risks associated with using ChatGPT, which is associated with lower frequency of use and intention to use this AI tool in higher education. These results highlight the importance of integrating such technologies into the university, while carefully considering ethical issues and students’ perceptions of risk. Keywords Artificial intelligence · ChatGPT · Higher education · Academic dishonesty · Risk Received: 3 August 2024 / Accepted: 19 May 2025 / Published online: 28 May 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Chat or cheat? Academic dishonesty, risk perceptions, and ChatGPT usage in higher education students Silvia Ortiz-Bonnin1,2 · Joanna Blahopoulou1,2 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",173
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,1,"2025 Chat or cheat? Academic dishonesty, risk perceptions, and ChatGPT usage in higher education students Silvia Ortiz-Bonnin1,2 · Joanna Blahopoulou1,2 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",33
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,2,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou 1 Introduction ChatGPT, developed by OpenAI, has rapidly emerged as the world’s most popular artificial intelligence chatbot (Aydin & Karaarslan, 2023; Statista, 2024). One striking metric illustrating ChatGPT’s dominance is that it reached 100 million active users within just two months of its public launch in November 2022, making it the fastest- growing consumer application in history (Reuters, 2023; Exploding Topics, 2023). This new AI-tool is based on a conversational artificial intelligence language model. Users can ask questions or give instructions (“prompts”), and the model, trained in various text data, generates human-like responses in a conversational style across diverse topics and tasks (Hasanein & Sobaih, 2023; Rahman & Watanobe, 2023). Given its impressive capabilities, scholars have begun to evaluate the implica­ tions of AI use in educational settings and are increasingly recognizing its potentially transformative impact on the teaching–learning process in higher education (Lim et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023). However, incorporating such tools into academic envi­ ronments requires not only acknowledging their potential to enhance productivity and efficiency but also engaging in critical reflection on the",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,2,"on the teaching–learning process in higher education (Lim et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023). However, incorporating such tools into academic envi­ ronments requires not only acknowledging their potential to enhance productivity and efficiency but also engaging in critical reflection on the ethical and pedagogical challenges they pose (Cordón García, 2023; Sullivan et al., 2023). A growing number of studies have examined the risks that students associate with using ChatGPT in university contexts (Chiu et al., 2023; Ma et al., 2024). These include overreliance on AI, privacy and security concerns, inaccuracies in generated content and nega­ tive effects on the development of cognitive and social skills (Ansari et al., 2024). Further, the tool’s ability to generate original-looking yet unreferenced content has raised alarms about its potential to facilitate academic dishonesty behavior such as cheating or (cyber) plagiarism (Bergström et al., 2024; Greitemeyer & Kastenmüller, 2023; Kangwa et al., 2024). While academic misconduct itself is not new, recent technological developments -including the internet and online learning- have transformed its nature and scale (Bilen & Matros, 2021; Comas-Forgas & Sureda-Negre, 2008). Studies show that",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,2,"& Kastenmüller, 2023; Kangwa et al., 2024). While academic misconduct itself is not new, recent technological developments -including the internet and online learning- have transformed its nature and scale (Bilen & Matros, 2021; Comas-Forgas & Sureda-Negre, 2008). Studies show that over 60% of Spanish university students have admitted to plagiarizing assignments (Comas-Forgas & Sureda-Negre, 2010), often citing convenience and lack of under­ standing as drivers. With the emergence of tools like ChatGPT, maintaining academic integrity has become even more challenging, as AI-generated content is difficult to detect (Demers, 2023; Chen et al., 2024), and students may submit it without proper attribution—a phenomenon now referred to as “AIgiarism” (Adeshola & Adepoju, 2023; Gleason, 2023). Developing a comprehensive institutional strategy to address the use of AI tools in academic settings is essential to ensure the quality and integrity of higher educa­ tion. This is especially important because a university degree implies specific knowl­ edge and competencies. Allowing students to graduate through dishonest means directly undermines public trust in professional expertise. As an illustrative example, Marsden et al. (2005, p.1) note, “patients would",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,2,"This is especially important because a university degree implies specific knowl­ edge and competencies. Allowing students to graduate through dishonest means directly undermines public trust in professional expertise. As an illustrative example, Marsden et al. (2005, p.1) note, “patients would not feel comfortable, or even safe, consulting physicians known to have cheated in physiology and anatomy courses”. Academic dishonesty, therefore, not only undermines the credibility and reputation of educational institutions but also devalues university education itself (Blahopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025; Holden et al., 2021). In response, universities have adopted 1 3 113 Page 2 of 21",96
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,3,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT diverse strategies, ranging from prohibiting AI tools to promoting training on their ethical use (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023; Cremer & Narayanan, 2023; González-Cala­ tayud et al., 2021). In summary, the growing use of AI tools in higher education has raised concerns about their ethical implications, particularly in relation to academic dishonesty and the risks associated with these technologies. This study explores how students’ per­ ceptions of these two dimensions influence their frequency and intention of ChatGPT use, drawing on Rational Choice Theory (Becker, 1968) and recent insights into AI integration in educational contexts (Gonzalo & Enriquez, 2023). Our study contributes to a growing body of research on AI in education by address­ ing important contextual and methodological gaps. First, it responds to the call by Mumtaz et al. (2024) to investigate AI use across different countries, acknowledging that cultural factors may significantly shape students’ attitudes and behaviors toward AI tools in academic settings. Second, it helps fill the gap identified in the systematic review by Ma et al. (2024) regarding the scarcity of large-scale",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,3,"different countries, acknowledging that cultural factors may significantly shape students’ attitudes and behaviors toward AI tools in academic settings. Second, it helps fill the gap identified in the systematic review by Ma et al. (2024) regarding the scarcity of large-scale studies—defined as those with over 300 participants—focused on AI-based chatbots in higher educa­ tion. Drawing on a substantial sample size, this research offers a broader and more representative understanding of the phenomenon (Lee et al., 2024; Sallam, 2023). Moreover, it introduces the construct of perceived academic dishonesty, thus expand­ ing the conceptual scope of current investigations on ChatGPT in university contexts. 2 Literature review and hypotheses 2.1 ChatGPT usage in higher education ChatGPT can be used for complex tasks such as answering questions, holding con­ versations, translating languages, summarizing literature, and producing essays suitable for university level (Jishnu et al., 2023). In fact, recent studies show that undergraduate students increasingly use ChatGPT for a wide range of academic pur­ poses, including completing assignments, preparing for exams, clarifying concepts, analyzing data, and supporting research (Hasanein & Sobaih, 2023; Jishnu et al.,",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,3,"2023). In fact, recent studies show that undergraduate students increasingly use ChatGPT for a wide range of academic pur­ poses, including completing assignments, preparing for exams, clarifying concepts, analyzing data, and supporting research (Hasanein & Sobaih, 2023; Jishnu et al., 2023; Rahman & Watanobe, 2023; Blahopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025). They also rely on it to generate and refine academic content—summarizing, paraphrasing, and note-taking included—as well as to act as a personal tutor for diverse learning needs (Ansari et al., 2024). Students may acknowledge the practical value of ChatGPT for academic support, yet they often raise concerns regarding the tool’s reliability and its potential to foster unethical academic practices, such as cheating and plagiarism (Chen et al., 2024; Sal­ lam et al., 2024). A recent study conducted at a Spanish university found that 8% of students acknowledged using ChatGPT to generate text they subsequently copied and pasted into their work (Blahopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025). Farrokhnia et al. (2023) caution that, since ChatGPT’s responses are not direct reproductions of existing texts, some students may use them unknowingly, without realizing they are committing",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,3,"text they subsequently copied and pasted into their work (Blahopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025). Farrokhnia et al. (2023) caution that, since ChatGPT’s responses are not direct reproductions of existing texts, some students may use them unknowingly, without realizing they are committing a form of plagiarism. This confusion is reflected in a recent survey of U.S. college students: although over 85% reported being aware of academic integrity policies and 1 3 Page 3 of 21 113",74
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,4,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou the widespread use of AI tools, only 21.3% considered such usage a violation of those policies (Chen et al., 2024). 2.2 Perceived risk and ChatGPT usage Recent studies have identified several risks associated with the use of ChatGPT in academic settings. The unprecedented ease and speed with which students can obtain instant, polished responses by ChatGPT may undermine their learning process and promote reliance on AI (Chen et al., 2024). In particular, the overreliance on AI-gen­ erated content could hinder the acquisition and development of essential academic skills such as critical thinking, writing, problem-solving and creativity (Farrokhnia et al., 2023; Singh et al., 2023). Additional risks include student privacy, data protection and cybersecurity. Further, since ChatGPT is trained in large volumes of internet- based text, it may produce plausible yet inaccurate or harmful content. These outputs can reflect and perpetuate societal biases embedded in the training data, potentially reproducing discriminatory narratives or reinforcing structural inequalities (Borji & Ai, 2023; Comas-Forgas et al., 2025; Farrokhnia et al., 2023; Gilson et al., 2023; Gonzalo & Enriquez, 2023; Gulati",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,4,"can reflect and perpetuate societal biases embedded in the training data, potentially reproducing discriminatory narratives or reinforcing structural inequalities (Borji & Ai, 2023; Comas-Forgas et al., 2025; Farrokhnia et al., 2023; Gilson et al., 2023; Gonzalo & Enriquez, 2023; Gulati et al., 2024; Kitamura, 2023; van Dis et al., 2023). Among the various risks associated with ChatGPT in educational settings, concerns related to academic integrity stand out as particularly pressing (Cotton et al., 2023; Sullivan et al., 2023). Such tools can help students to achieve better grades with mini­ mal effort, thus undermining academic integrity. Moreover, since ChatGPT’s output often closely resembles human writing, distinguishing AI-generated content from authentic student work becomes challenging, thereby complicating the processes of assessment and evaluation (Demers, 2023). The concept of perceived risk was first introduced by (Bauer, 1960) to explain how individuals respond to situations involving uncertainty and potential negative outcomes. In this framework, risk perception arises when an individual anticipates that a decision could lead to undesirable consequences. Prior research in social psy­ chology and technology adoption has shown that higher perceived risk",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,4,"situations involving uncertainty and potential negative outcomes. In this framework, risk perception arises when an individual anticipates that a decision could lead to undesirable consequences. Prior research in social psy­ chology and technology adoption has shown that higher perceived risk decreases the likelihood of engaging in a given behavior (Chiu et al., 2023; Essel, 2022; Lee et al., 2024; Mvondo et al., 2023; Sultana, 2020). In other words, high levels of perceived risk tend to decrease adoption rates, whereas lower perceived risk enhance the likeli­ hood of adoption (Essel, 2022; Lee et al., 2024; Sallam et al., 2024). Building on this theoretical framework, recent empirical studies have examined how perceived risk influences students’ behavior specifically in relation to ChatGPT. Gonzalo and Enriquez (2023) found that perceived risk significantly and negatively influences students’ intention to use ChatGPT—emerging as a stronger predictor than other commonly studied factors such as perceived usefulness and ease of use (Sallam et al., 2023). Their findings reveal that undergraduate students who perceive high risks when using ChatGPT are less likely to adopt the tool. Among the key",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,4,"than other commonly studied factors such as perceived usefulness and ease of use (Sallam et al., 2023). Their findings reveal that undergraduate students who perceive high risks when using ChatGPT are less likely to adopt the tool. Among the key concerns they considered was the protection of their private information from misuse. Further­ more, risk awareness adversely affects the willingness to pay for enhanced versions of the tool, such as ChatGPT-4 Premium (Jo, 2024). Similarly, a study about the user intentions to utilize ChatGPT for self-diagnosis found that a positive risk-reward appraisal influences the decision-making processes involving ChatGPT (Shahsavar 1 3 113 Page 4 of 21",106
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,5,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT & Choudhury, 2023). The authors define positive risk-reward appraisal as “the extent to which an individual perceives the advantages of using ChatGPT as surpassing any potential adverse consequences or risks” (Shahsavar & Choudhury, 2023, p.3). Rational Choice Theory (RCT), as developed by Becker (1968), offers a useful framework to interpret these findings and has been widely used to explain decision- making processes in academic misconduct context (Freiburger et al., 2017; Mvondo et al., 2023; Paternoster & Pogarsky, 2009; Williams & Hosek, 2003). According to RCT, individuals act to maximize personal utility by assessing the potential benefits and costs of different actions. In the context of ChatGPT usage students weigh the perceived benefits of using the tool—such as saving time, reducing effort, or obtain­ ing better grades—against potential costs, including the likelihood of detection and the severity of institutional sanctions (Mvondo et al., 2023). Research has confirmed that students tend to make calculated decisions and are more likely to engage in cheating behaviors when they believe that the rewards out­ weigh the risks (Williams &",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,5,"the severity of institutional sanctions (Mvondo et al., 2023). Research has confirmed that students tend to make calculated decisions and are more likely to engage in cheating behaviors when they believe that the rewards out­ weigh the risks (Williams & Hosek, 2003). While when they perceive a higher prob­ ability of being caught or punished, they are less likely to engage in questionable academic practices (Cochran et al., 1999; Freiburger et al., 2017; Mvondo et al., 2023; Williams & Hosek, 2003). Consequently, concerns about violating university policies or being accused of plagiarism may therefore lead some students to hesitate in their reliance on ChatGPT, ultimately affecting their willingness to use the tool for academic purposes. However, the perceived risk of formal punishment may be mitigated in the context of AI-generated content. Specifically, the low detectability of ChatGPT-based plagiarism—due to the current limitations of tools like Turnitin in identifying AI-generated text—weakens the deterrent effect of academic sanctions and may encourage more frequent or confident use (Zhang et al., 2025). Beyond the threat of institutional consequences, students may also perceive other types",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,5,"current limitations of tools like Turnitin in identifying AI-generated text—weakens the deterrent effect of academic sanctions and may encourage more frequent or confident use (Zhang et al., 2025). Beyond the threat of institutional consequences, students may also perceive other types of risks that influence their behavior. These include concerns about overreli­ ance on AI-generated content, doubts about the reliability and accuracy of the tool’s outputs, and risks related to data security and privacy. These concerns further shape students’ cost–benefit evaluations and reinforce the relevance of RCT as a conceptual lens to understand ChatGPT usage in academic contexts. As Menon and Shilpa (2023) point out, even if the risk of detection could be considered relatively low, these other forms of risk can continue to influence decision-making and shaping users’ cost- benefit evaluations. As a result, these persistent risks may discourage some students from fully embracing ChatGPT for academic purposes. Thus, our first hypotheses are: H1a: Perceived risk will be negatively related to the use frequency of ChatGPT. H1b: Perceived risk will be negatively related to the intention to use ChatGPT. 2.3",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,5,"students from fully embracing ChatGPT for academic purposes. Thus, our first hypotheses are: H1a: Perceived risk will be negatively related to the use frequency of ChatGPT. H1b: Perceived risk will be negatively related to the intention to use ChatGPT. 2.3 Perceptions of academic dishonesty and ChatGPT usage The decision to use ChatGPT at university is not always—or solely—the result of a rational evaluation of costs/risks and benefits; it is also influenced by students’ beliefs about what constitutes acceptable academic conduct (Paternoster & Pogarsky, 2009). We conceptualize this as Perceived Academic Dishonesty (PAD), which refers to “students’ subjective evaluation of behaviors that are indicative of academic dishon­ 1 3 Page 5 of 21 113",113
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,6,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou esty” (Ordóñez & Romero, 2021, p.115). This concept extends to actions that violate honor codes, a topic thoroughly examined in research (e.g., Krou et al., 2021). PAD captures the degree to which students judge these behaviors—whether active (e.g., copying during an exam, submitting plagiarized work) or passive (e.g., letting others copy, submitting someone else’s work)—as acceptable or unacceptable in academic contexts. Students with high levels of PAD thoroughly assess behaviors that consti­ tute academic dishonesty (Ordóñez & Romero, 2021; Paternoster & Pogarsky, 2009). Chala (2021) and Costley (2019) suggested that students who perceive dishonest behaviors as acceptable or trivial are more likely to engage in unethical actions. Con­ versely, students who judge specific actions (e.g. cheating on an exam with electronic devices or presenting someone else’s work as their own) as highly dishonest are less inclined to engage in such behavior. This brings us to an essential dimension often overlooked in purely rational mod­ els: the moral meaning students assign to the use of ChatGPT. A recent study with a sample of academics found that the",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,6,"to engage in such behavior. This brings us to an essential dimension often overlooked in purely rational mod­ els: the moral meaning students assign to the use of ChatGPT. A recent study with a sample of academics found that the perception of academic integrity was significantly and negatively related to ChatGPT usage (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023). This implies that the scholars who value honesty tend to use ChatGPT less in their work. They might perceive the use of ChatGPT as comparable to traditional forms of academic dishonesty and plagiarism, raising concerns about its potential to cause significant issues within the academic community. These findings would imply that ChatGPT is viewed as a tool primarily used for “cheating” rather than for “chatting”, indicating a tendency toward unethical applications. Additional evidence supports this interpretation. Various psychological constructs that serve as indicators of ethical orientation have been found to predict ChatGPT- related academic misconduct. For instance, moral disengagement has emerged as a key predictor of ChatGPT-based plagiarism, alongside perceived benefits and the influence of both formal and informal sanctions (Zhang et al., 2025).",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,6,"ethical orientation have been found to predict ChatGPT- related academic misconduct. For instance, moral disengagement has emerged as a key predictor of ChatGPT-based plagiarism, alongside perceived benefits and the influence of both formal and informal sanctions (Zhang et al., 2025). Personality traits also play a significant role in how students approach AI usage: individuals high in Machiavellianism—a manipulative trait linked to moral flexibility—are more likely to use ChatGPT covertly as they perceive questionable academic behaviors, such as cheating, as less unethical than their peers (de Winter et al., 2024; Elias, 2015; Hidalgo-Fuentes et al., 2022). In contrast, students who score high on the honesty- humility dimension—a personality trait closely associated with ethical behavior— tend to avoid using AI for dishonest purposes (Greitemeyer & Kastenmüller, 2023). This ethical framing is further supported by Bergström et al. (2024), who found that Finnish university students were reluctant to use ChatGPT in academic settings when they perceived it as ethically problematic, particularly due to concerns about its potential misuse for cheating. According to Simon’s (1955) concept of bounded rationality, individuals make decisions under cognitive",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,6,"university students were reluctant to use ChatGPT in academic settings when they perceived it as ethically problematic, particularly due to concerns about its potential misuse for cheating. According to Simon’s (1955) concept of bounded rationality, individuals make decisions under cognitive and contextual constraints—such as time pressure—that limit their ability to evaluate all available information. Consistent with this concept, students engage in unethical behaviour when completing assignments, especially under stress or when overwhelmed by academic workloads (Comas & Sureda, 2008). Similarly, Kahneman’s dual-process theory (2011) posits that people use two sys­ tems of thinking: Type I (fast, automatic, and intuitive) and Type II (slow, deliberate, and analytical). When faced with time pressure, students might default to Type I 1 3 113 Page 6 of 21",124
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,7,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT thinking, influenced by immediate impressions or societal norms, rather than thor­ oughly weighing risks of using AI tools like ChatGPT (Greitemeyer & Kastenmüller, 2024). Since one of the main reasons students use ChatGPT is its ability to save time (Blahopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025), it’s likely that, in situations of time pressure, they may default to the intuitive, quick decision-making process that these theories describe, relying on the convenience of AI without fully evaluating its costs and ethi­ cal implications. Thus, students may not engage in an extensive evaluation of the pros and cons of ChatGPT usage; instead, they are likely to rely on intuitive, heuris­ tic-based judgements. Their decisions may be shaped more by gut reactions, such as concerns about academic dishonesty, their sense of “what feels right”, rather than a reasoned, logical analysis. In sum, PAD reflects an internalized moral stance, shaped by institutional dis­ course and personal values. In this sense, it operates independently from instrumental concerns such as detection or punishment. Some students may avoid using ChatGPT not because they fear",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,7,"In sum, PAD reflects an internalized moral stance, shaped by institutional dis­ course and personal values. In this sense, it operates independently from instrumental concerns such as detection or punishment. Some students may avoid using ChatGPT not because they fear being sanctioned, but because they see it as “wrong,” even in the absence of explicit rules or consequences.This construct functions as a kind of moral lens, shaping how students evaluate the legitimacy of behaviors within the university context — including the use of emerging technologies such as ChatGPT. Accordingly, we hypothesize that students with higher levels of PAD will report lower frequency and intention of use. Thus, our second hypotheses are: H2a: PAD will be negatively related to the use frequency of ChatGPT. H2b: PAD will be negatively related to the intention to use ChatGPT. 2.4 Perception of academic dishonesty, perceived risk, and ChatGPT usage As previously argued, students who report high levels of PAD and perceive risk are generally less likely to use this tool – both in terms of frequency and intention. Beyond the direct effects, we propose",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,7,"perceived risk, and ChatGPT usage As previously argued, students who report high levels of PAD and perceive risk are generally less likely to use this tool – both in terms of frequency and intention. Beyond the direct effects, we propose that the relationship between PAD and Chat­ GPT usage may be mediated by how students interpret and evaluate the risks associ­ ated with such tools. Considering the novelty of students’ access to AI tools, to our knowledge, no stud­ ies have directly and quantitatively examined the relationship between PAD and the perception of risks arising from the use of new technologies such as ChatGPT. How­ ever, existing research indicates that ethical awareness may shape how individuals evaluate risks associated with new technologies. For example, studies in the domain of online commerce have shown that consumers with stronger ethical standards are more likely to be concerned about privacy and security risks (Bednar & Spieker­ mann, 2023; Yuniarti et al., 2022). By analogy, students who are particularly sensitive to academic dishonesty may conduct a more rigorous evaluation before deciding to use AI",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,7,"more likely to be concerned about privacy and security risks (Bednar & Spieker­ mann, 2023; Yuniarti et al., 2022). By analogy, students who are particularly sensitive to academic dishonesty may conduct a more rigorous evaluation before deciding to use AI technologies – carefully weighing potential risks related to privacy, data secu­ rity, misinformation, policy violations, and plagiarism. Conversely, students with lower levels of PAD may overlook these risks, perceiving plagiarism as a normalized behavior and thus underestimate the seriousness of using AI tools for academic tasks. This assumption is consistent with the findings of Williams and Hosek (2003), which show that individuals with weaker moral convictions are more prone to cheating 1 3 Page 7 of 21 113",118
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,8,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou when they perceive the potential rewards to surpass the perceived risks. Similarly, Comas-Forgas and Sureda-Negre (2010) found that students with a higher tendency to plagiarize are less aware of the seriousness of plagiarism, less academically moti­ vated, and more likely to procrastinate. These findings suggest that moral sensitivity shapes how individuals interpret and respond to potential academic risks. Accordingly, we propose that students with high PAD are more likely to perceive risks in using ChatGPT and that this perception par­ tially explains their lower usage rates. By integrating both RCT and PAD, we adopt a more comprehensive approach to understanding students’ decisions regarding Chat­ GPT. Whereas RCT accounts for rational evaluations of risk and benefit, PAD cap­ tures the ethical and normative considerations that also shape behavior. Accordingly, we hypothesize that risk perceptions will partially mediate the relationship between PAD and ChatGPT usage. Thus, our third hypotheses are: H3a: Perceived risk will act as a partial mediator between PAD and use frequency of ChatGPT. H3b: Perceived risk will act as a partial mediator between PAD and",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,8,"the relationship between PAD and ChatGPT usage. Thus, our third hypotheses are: H3a: Perceived risk will act as a partial mediator between PAD and use frequency of ChatGPT. H3b: Perceived risk will act as a partial mediator between PAD and intention to use ChatGPT. 3 Method 3.1 Participants The study included 468 students at a Spanish university, enrolled in various academic programs. The average age of participants was 20 years (standard deviation = 4.25) and 55% were female, 44% were male and 1% non-binary. 3.2 Instruments Besides socio-demographic variables, the questionnaire included measures of the four variables mentioned in the research model (Fig. 1). Fig. 1 Research model 1 3 113 Page 8 of 21",115
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,9,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT Use of ChatGPT was measured by asking “Have you used the AI tool ChatGPT?”. The three answer options were: “No, never”, “Yes, the free version”, “Yes, the paid premium version”. Use frequency of ChatGPT was measured by asking users “How often do you use the AI tool ChatGPT?”. The answer options of the frequency scale were: “Once a month” (= 1); “Once a week” (= 2), “Every day” (= 3) and “Several times a day” (= 4). Intention to use ChatGPT (Choung et al., 2023) was measured by asking users if they intend to continue using ChatGPT. The response options of the three items scale were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree) and demonstrated good internal consistency (Cronbach’s α = 0.98; McDonald’s ω = 0.98). High scores reflect greater user intentions. Perceived risk (Sallam et al., 2023) was used to measure students’ perceptions about the risks associated with using AI tools like ChatGPT. Examples of the five items scale are: “I am concerned about the reliability of the",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,9,"greater user intentions. Perceived risk (Sallam et al., 2023) was used to measure students’ perceptions about the risks associated with using AI tools like ChatGPT. Examples of the five items scale are: “I am concerned about the reliability of the information provided by ChatGPT” or “I am concerned that using ChatGPT would get me accused of pla­ giarism”. We added an additional item to the scale measuring concerns that authori­ ties do not effectively regulate how organizations and companies provide AI tools. The response options were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree) and demonstrated good internal consistency (Cronbach’s α = 0.78; McDonald’s ω = 0.79). High scores reflect greater perceived risk of ChatGPT. Perception of Academic Dishonesty (Ordóñez & Romero, 2021) was measured by presenting a list of behaviors identified as academically dishonest in the scientific literature and asking students to indicate to what extent they believe they are dishon­ est. Examples of the 15 items scale are: “Cheating on an exam using any unauthor­ ized means.” Or “Submitting work as one’s own creation",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,9,"the scientific literature and asking students to indicate to what extent they believe they are dishon­ est. Examples of the 15 items scale are: “Cheating on an exam using any unauthor­ ized means.” Or “Submitting work as one’s own creation when it has been done by someone else.” The response options were a five-point scale ranging from 1 (not at all dishonest) to 5 (very dishonest) and demonstrated a high degree of reliability (Cronbach’s α = 0.96; McDonald’s ω = 0.96). High scores on the scale denote higher academic integrity among students. 3.3 Procedure An online questionnaire administered via Google Forms was distributed to students at the University of the Balearic Islands, which has a total undergraduate population of 11,736. Of the 737 respondents, only those who reported having used ChatGPT (N = 468) were included in the analysis. Data collection took place during regular class schedules at the beginning of the academic year 2023/2024, spanning the weeks between October and November 2023. Thus, the research utilized convenience sampling, selecting students based on their accessibility (being present in class the",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,9,"collection took place during regular class schedules at the beginning of the academic year 2023/2024, spanning the weeks between October and November 2023. Thus, the research utilized convenience sampling, selecting students based on their accessibility (being present in class the day of data collection) and willingness to participate. While this approach may not ensure a representative sample, it does facilitate the efficient collection of data from many individuals. Prior to participation, informed consent of all participants was obtained online. Students were briefed about the study’s objectives, the voluntary nature of partici­ pation, the confidential handling of their answers and their right to withdraw. The study met the specific requirements of Spanish legislation (Organic Law 3/2018 of 5 1 3 Page 9 of 21 113",124
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,10,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou December on Personal Data Protection and Digital Rights Guarantees). Further, the methodology conforms to internationally accepted ethical guidelines (Declaration of Helsinki) in relation to research with human beings. Formal approval for our research was obtained in advance from the independent Ethics Committee of the University of the Balearic Islands. 3.4 Data analysis In this study, data were analyzed using Jamovi 2.3.18 program ​(​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​w​w​w​.​j​a​m​o​v​i​.​o​ r​g​/​)​. ​First, we conducted descriptive statistics—including means and standard devia­ tions—to provide an overview of the data distribution and characteristics. To assess normality, we examined skewness and kurtosis indices. Additionally, we evaluated multicollinearity among the variables using the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF). Cor­ relations among the main study variables were examined using Spearman rho cor­ relation coefficient (Table 1). As part of the preliminary analyses, we assessed the validity of the measure­ ment model through multiple complementary approaches. Discriminant validity was evaluated using the Heterotrait-Monotrait (HTMT) ratio of correlations, following Henseler et al. (2015). All HTMT values were well below the recommended thresh­ old of 0.85, with the highest being 0.40 between Perceived",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,10,"model through multiple complementary approaches. Discriminant validity was evaluated using the Heterotrait-Monotrait (HTMT) ratio of correlations, following Henseler et al. (2015). All HTMT values were well below the recommended thresh­ old of 0.85, with the highest being 0.40 between Perceived Risk and Intention to Use, indicating that the constructs are clearly distinguishable from one another. Con­ tent validity was supported using full, previously validated scales, developed and psychometrically tested by their original authors. Each scale was selected based on its theoretical alignment with the constructs of interest and its empirical support in prior research. Regarding convergent validity, the construct Intention to Use dem­ onstrated excellent results, with an AVE of 0.95 and a composite reliability (CR) of 0.98. Perceived Academic Dishonesty and Perceived Risk showed AVE values of 0.46 and 0.40, respectively. While these values fall slightly below the commonly recommended threshold of 0.50, they are accompanied by strong internal consistency (CR = 0.96 and 0.79, respectively), and the constructs are grounded in theoretically robust frameworks. According to Fornell and Larcker (1981), AVE values below 0.50 can be considered acceptable",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,10,"recommended threshold of 0.50, they are accompanied by strong internal consistency (CR = 0.96 and 0.79, respectively), and the constructs are grounded in theoretically robust frameworks. According to Fornell and Larcker (1981), AVE values below 0.50 can be considered acceptable when composite reliability exceeds 0.60. Simi­ larly, Hair et al. (2019) emphasize that slightly lower AVE values may still be valid Table 1 Means, standard deviations and correlations M SD 1 2 3 4 1. PAD 4.41 0.81 - 2. Perceived risk 3.32 0.86 0.13* - 3. Use frequency 1.48 0.71 − 0.02 − 0.21*** - 4. Intention to use 3.82 1.23 − 0.07 − 0.33*** 0.52*** - Skewness -2.38 − 0.23 1.33 − 0.79 Kurtosis 6.35 − 0.22 0.93 − 0.47 *p <.05, **p <.01, ***p <.001 Note. PAD = Perception of Academic Dishonesty; Non-parametric (Spearman) were applied because data (PAD) did not show normal distribution 1 3 113 Page 10 of 21",154
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,11,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT in models involving broader or more complex constructs, especially when supported by other forms of validity evidence. To test our hypotheses, we conducted mediation analysis using the “GLM Media­ tion analyses” within Jamovi. We employed a non-parametric resampling technique known as bootstrapping to derive confidence intervals based on percentiles (MacK­ innon et al., 2004). This method enabled the estimation of total, direct, and specific indirect effects (mediated by the proposed variable: perceived risk) of the predictor (PAD) on outcomes (use frequency, intention to use ChatGPT). The indirect effect was computed using 10,000 bootstrap samples to generate Bias-Corrected (BC) con­ fidence intervals (CI). An indirect effect was deemed statistically significant if the 95% CI did not encompass 0, indicating a non-zero association between the vari­ ables under consideration (Baron & Kenny, 1986). A significant level of p =.05 was employed. 4 Results The results of our descriptive statistics including means, standard deviations and cor­ relations are shown in Table 1. Additionally, the data were tested for normality and the values of skewness and kurtosis of",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,11,"of p =.05 was employed. 4 Results The results of our descriptive statistics including means, standard deviations and cor­ relations are shown in Table 1. Additionally, the data were tested for normality and the values of skewness and kurtosis of the variables ranged between − 2 and + 2, dem­ onstrating a normal distribution of the data, except for PAD. PAD data exhibit a nota­ ble negative skewness of -2.38, indicating that the distribution is skewed to the left. Also, the kurtosis value of 6.35 signifies a leptokurtic distribution with heavier tails and a more pronounced peak compared to a normal distribution (George & Mallery, 2019). Thus, non-parametric analyses were used. Furthermore, to assess multicol­ linearity, we estimated the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) for the predictor variables. The VIF values were 1.01, which is below the commonly recommended threshold of 5 (Hair et al., 2019). Thus, multicollinearity was not a concern in our study. PAD is significantly and positively related to perceived risk (ρ = 0.13, p <.05). In turn, perceived risk is significantly and negatively associated with both use",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,11,"5 (Hair et al., 2019). Thus, multicollinearity was not a concern in our study. PAD is significantly and positively related to perceived risk (ρ = 0.13, p <.05). In turn, perceived risk is significantly and negatively associated with both use frequency ( ρ = –0.21, p <.001) and intention to use (ρ = –0.33, p <.001). Additionally, use fre­ quency and intention to use are strongly and positively related (ρ = 0.52, p <.001). However, PAD is not significantly related to either use frequency (ρ = –0.02, n.s.) or intention to use (ρ = –0.07, n.s.). 4.1 Hypotheses testing First, perceived risk was significantly and negatively associated with use frequency (β = − 0.20, p <.001) and intention to use (β = − 0.46, p <.001), supporting H1a and H1b. Second, PAD was not directly related to use frequency (β = 0.04, p =.22) or inten­ tion to use ChatGTP (β = − 0.05, p =.33), thus rejecting H2a and H2b. Third, PAD was indirectly associated with both use frequency (95% CI [-0.05, − 0.01]) and intention to use (95%",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,11,"= 0.04, p =.22) or inten­ tion to use ChatGTP (β = − 0.05, p =.33), thus rejecting H2a and H2b. Third, PAD was indirectly associated with both use frequency (95% CI [-0.05, − 0.01]) and intention to use (95% CI [-0.11, − 0.02]) through perceived risk. Since the confidence intervals did not include zero, we conclude that perceived risk medi­ 1 3 Page 11 of 21 113",68
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,12,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou ates the relationship between PAD and both outcome variables. Therefore, H3a and H3b were supported. These results suggest that PAD is positively related to perceived risk, which, in turn, is negatively related to use frequency and the intention to use ChatGPT (Tables 2 and 3). According to Meule (2019) we found an indirect-only mediation because indirect effects are significant whereas the direct effect (from independent variable to dependent variable) and total effects are not significant. 5 Discussion The adoption of ChatGPT from university students has become a hot topic in the field of higher education. As a language model driven by AI it offers a wide range of benefits for search and learning processes (Chiu et al., 2023; Ma et al., 2024). Never­ theless, this new technological tool is raising concerns about academic honesty and plagiarism, as it facilitates the breach of ethical principles within the academic envi­ ronment (Mvondo et al., 2023). In this study, we explored how students’ Perception of Academic Dishonesty (PAD) and perceived risk associated with using ChatGPT impact their willingness to",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,12,"as it facilitates the breach of ethical principles within the academic envi­ ronment (Mvondo et al., 2023). In this study, we explored how students’ Perception of Academic Dishonesty (PAD) and perceived risk associated with using ChatGPT impact their willingness to use ChatGPT in higher education. First, our findings indicate that perceived risk was negatively and significantly related to students’ frequency of using ChatGPT and their intention to use it in the future (Hypothesis 1a and Hypothesis 1b supported). Thus, concerns about the accu­ racy of the information generated by ChatGPT, along with privacy and security risks and issues related to academic integrity, seem to deter students from using the tool. Table 2 Mediation effects model predicting use frequency Estimate SE Z p Direct effects PAD → Perceived risk 0.12 0.05 2.71 0.007 Perceived risk → Use frequency -0.20 0.04 -5.06 < 0.001 PAD → Use frequency 0.04 0.03 1.22 0.221 Indirect effects PAD → Perceived risks → Use frequency -0.02 0.01 -2.44 0.015 Total effects 0.02 0.04 0.49 0.623 PAD = Perception of Academic Dishonesty Table 3 Mediation effects model",180
