A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,13,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT These results find support in the RCT (Becker, 1968). According to the RCT, individ­ uals weigh the potential risks of using ChatGPT (such as the fear of violating univer­ sity policies) against the benefits (such as improved assignment outcomes) to make a rational decision about whether to use ChatGPT (Menon & Shilpa, 2023; Mvondo et al., 2023; Shahsavar & Choudhury, 2023). Moreover, our results are consistent with previous research that showed a link between perceived risk and the intention to use ChatGPT (Gonzalo & Enriquez, 2023; Sallam et al., 2023), and aligns with studies which suggest that fears of breaking academic rules and being accused of plagiarism discourage its use (Chiu et al., 2023; Mvondo et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2025). Second, our results indicate that PAD is not significantly related to ChatGPT usage (H2a and H2b not supported). This contrasts with the hypothesis that decision- making, such as the use of tools like ChatGPT, is often guided by fast, automatic, and intuitive processes, based on Kahneman’s (2011) concept of Type I thinking",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,13,"usage (H2a and H2b not supported). This contrasts with the hypothesis that decision- making, such as the use of tools like ChatGPT, is often guided by fast, automatic, and intuitive processes, based on Kahneman’s (2011) concept of Type I thinking and Simon’s (1955) theory of boundary rationality. These results also diverge from prior findings that identify academic integrity concerns as a direct deterrent to Chat­ GPT use among academics and researchers (Bin-Nashwan et al., 2023) or research, which highlights factors such as moral disengagement and a low level of the hon­ esty-humility personality dimension as key predictors of ChatGPT-based plagiarism (Greitemeyer & Kastenmüller, 2024; Zhang et al., 2025). A possible explanation for this discrepancy lies in the ambiguity surrounding what constitutes plagiarism when using AI-generated content. Despite awareness of academic integrity policies and AI tool usage, some students may not perceive their use as a policy violation and may unintentionally commit AIgiarism, as its outputs are not verbatim copies of existing texts (Farrokhnia et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2024). Additionally, other studies empha­ size the role of social norms",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,13,"perceive their use as a policy violation and may unintentionally commit AIgiarism, as its outputs are not verbatim copies of existing texts (Farrokhnia et al., 2023; Chen et al., 2024). Additionally, other studies empha­ size the role of social norms in shaping the likelihood of AI-facilitated academic misconduct, suggesting that perceived acceptability among peers may outweigh not only institutional policies but also students’ own perception of what is academically right or wrong—especially when such practices are widespread among their peers (Greitemeyer & Kastenmüller, 2024). Third, the findings of this study revealed that perceived risk mediates the relation­ ship between PAD and both the use frequency and the intention to use ChatGPT (Hypothesis 3a and 3b supported). In contrast to prior studies that emphasize moral sensitivity as a direct predictor of AI-assisted plagiarism (Zhang et al., 2025), our findings suggest a different pathway: rather than directly influencing ChatGPT use, PAD operates through risk perception. This implies that students may engage in AI- assisted misconduct not just because they disengage morally but because they weigh the risks associated with it. In other",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,13,"different pathway: rather than directly influencing ChatGPT use, PAD operates through risk perception. This implies that students may engage in AI- assisted misconduct not just because they disengage morally but because they weigh the risks associated with it. In other words, students who perceive specific actions (e.g., cheating on an exam with electronic devices, fabricating excuses to reschedule exams, or presenting someone else’s work as their own) as highly dishonest also tend to report greater awareness of the potential risks involved in using ChatGPT which is associated with lower ChatGPT usage. Conversely, students less concerned about ethical issues are less aware of the risks associated with using ChatGPT, which results in more frequent use and a greater intention to use it in the future, as they may primarily recognize the outstanding advantages of ChatGPT, such as increased productivity, as it reduces the time and effort required to complete assignments (Bla­ hopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025). This aligns with the finding that the pursuit of 1 3 Page 13 of 21 113",170
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,13,"reduces the time and effort required to complete assignments (Bla­ hopoulou & Ortiz-Bonnin, 2025). This aligns with the finding that the pursuit of 1 3 Page 13 of 21 113",30
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,14,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou quick and easy solutions is the primary reason why students engage in academically dishonest practices (Comas-Forgas & Sureda-Negre, 2010). Thus, our conclusions are consistent with those of Comas-Forgas and Sureda-Negre (2010), indicating that individuals with greater academic integrity are more aware of the potential conse­ quences of their academic actions. While the cross-sectional design of this study limits causal interpretations, it is possible that students who are more risk-aware may also be more inclined to endorse stricter moral evaluations of academic misconduct resulting in less frequent use of ChatGPT and a lower intention to use it in the future. The main contribution of the present study lies in the development of a theory- based conceptual model that integrates key variables influencing the use of ChatGPT: PAD and perceived risk. The model possesses substantial explanatory power and bridges an important research gap by empirically examining the relationships of the variables. To our knowledge, there is no study analyzing the factors that influence why certain students are more aware and sensitive to the potential risks of ChatGPT than",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,14,"bridges an important research gap by empirically examining the relationships of the variables. To our knowledge, there is no study analyzing the factors that influence why certain students are more aware and sensitive to the potential risks of ChatGPT than others. In other words, there is a lack of research about the antecedents of per­ ceived risk of using ChatGPT, whether for chatting or cheating. Our study is unique in that it shows a significant association between PAD and sensitivity toward risks associated with ChatGPT. 5.1 Practical implications This study suggests some important practical implications for AI developers, users, academic institutions, and educators. First, the perception of risk among students regarding ChatGPT is linked to a decrease in both the use frequency and their inten­ tion to use this AI tool. This sends a clear message to AI-developers: It is essential for users to feel safe while using ChatGPT. Thus, several aspects of ChatGPT should be improved such as data protection, cybersecurity, training data transparency and information’s accuracy by mentioning, for example, real references. Second, AI users must take responsibility",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,14,"essential for users to feel safe while using ChatGPT. Thus, several aspects of ChatGPT should be improved such as data protection, cybersecurity, training data transparency and information’s accuracy by mentioning, for example, real references. Second, AI users must take responsibility for reading and understanding the terms of use and privacy policies of AI systems they engage with. They must also ensure ethical use of these tools, understanding that AI systems are only as ethical as the humans who develop, deploy, and use them (De Cremer & Narayanan, 2023). It’s crucial to be aware of the hazards regarding credibility, authenticity, and accuracy of information produced by AI tools like ChatGPT. Even while using AI tools that respect authorship, copyright, and intellectual property, such as Perplexity, users must be aware that the responsibility for the utilization of this information and ethi­ cal decision-making remains in human hands. Third, educational institutions (e.g., universities) must prioritize efforts to promote academic integrity. One way to enhance academic integrity among students is imple­ menting educational programs to establish ethical guidelines for its responsible use, heightening the",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,14,"decision-making remains in human hands. Third, educational institutions (e.g., universities) must prioritize efforts to promote academic integrity. One way to enhance academic integrity among students is imple­ menting educational programs to establish ethical guidelines for its responsible use, heightening the awareness of the potential risks involved with the use of AI tools. This requires defining dishonest behaviors clearly and detailing the penalties that students may face for engaging in such actions. This is particularly crucial in a period where universities, and the whole society, are still in the process of formulating clear guidelines on the appropriate and inappropriate uses of AI tools. Guerrero-Dib et al. 1 3 113 Page 14 of 21",112
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,15,"Academic Dishonesty, Risk Perceptions, and ChatGPT (2020) argue that an institutional environment filled with uncertainty and unclear rules is creating a breeding ground for unethical behaviors. Additionally, the potential ramifications of the AI tool’s fraudulent usage on aca­ demic integrity need to be acknowledged and addressed by educators in their courses. This could involve implementing anti-plagiarism measures and adapting assessment systems accordingly (Cotton et al., 2023; Do Ba et al., 2017). Preciado Babb et al. (2017) also approach the promotion of academic integrity from a formative per­ spective, emphasizing the central role of educators and recommending four lines of action: (a) engaging in conversations about plagiarism, (b) teaching students’ skills for citing and referencing, (c) creating opportunities for feedback as part of the learn­ ing process, and (d) conducting assessments to include formative and summative feedback. Finally, implementing strategies to increase PAD is vital for upholding educational integrity and safeguarding the reputation of universities. Moreover, dis­ honest behaviors developed in academic contexts may transfer into the workplace potentially undermining professional ethics (Saana et al., 2016; Guerrero-Dib et al., 2020). 5.2",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,15,"increase PAD is vital for upholding educational integrity and safeguarding the reputation of universities. Moreover, dis­ honest behaviors developed in academic contexts may transfer into the workplace potentially undermining professional ethics (Saana et al., 2016; Guerrero-Dib et al., 2020). 5.2 Limitations and future research While this study offers valuable insights, some limitations and future research direc­ tions should be discussed. First, future research should aim to overcome the short­ comings of a non-probability convenience sample from a single university and the lack of an a priori power analysis, which limits our ability to assess the adequacy of the sample size and generalizability of the findings. Second, since this study is cross-sectional, any interpretations regarding causal relationships among the study variables should be approached with caution. Third, upon analyzing the data, we observed that the variable PAD did not strictly conform to assumptions of normality leading us to use non-parametric methods. Even with measures to ensure anonymity, students’ perceptions of dishonest behaviors could still be susceptible to the influence of social desirability bias. Thus, they could be swayed by a desire",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,15,"to assumptions of normality leading us to use non-parametric methods. Even with measures to ensure anonymity, students’ perceptions of dishonest behaviors could still be susceptible to the influence of social desirability bias. Thus, they could be swayed by a desire for positive self- presentation, resulting in a skewed distribution. Fourth, the focus of our study was to capture users’ risk perceptions and its importance for user behavior on a general level. Future research should investigate specific instances of dishonest behaviors associated with ChatGPT. This should include examining academic integrity viola­ tions, such as the unethical misuse of AI-tools. For this purpose, it will be necessary to define and reach a consensus on what constitutes ethical use of AI-tools to dif­ ferentiate chatting from cheating behaviors (Bergström et al., 2024). Finally, as the use of ChatGPT in higher education remains an emerging research area, conducting longitudinal studies might offer a picture about the evolution of students’ perception of risks of ChatGPT over time. 1 3 Page 15 of 21 113",169
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,16,"S. Ortiz-Bonnin, J. Blahopoulou 6 Conclusion This study shows that students with a higher Perception of Academic Dishonesty (PAD)—that is, those who consider academically dishonest behaviors more seri­ ous—also tend to perceive greater risks in using ChatGPT. These perceived risks are associated with a lower frequency of use and reduced intention to use the tool in the future. The study provides valuable insight into why some students are more aware and sensitive to the potential risks of ChatGPT than others. Further, these find­ ings highlight the need for universities to foster critical discussions around academic integrity and the responsible use of emerging technologies like ChatGPT. Acknowledgements We are deeply grateful to the students who participated in this study. We acknowl­ edge PhD Christian Peake for his fresh perspective and thorough review of our paper. Authors’ contributions JB and SOB designed the model which is tested in the paper, created the question­ naire and collected the data. SOB analyzed and interpreted student data regarding their perceptions of academic dishonesty, risk, use frequency as well as intention to use ChatGPT. Both authors",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,16,"the model which is tested in the paper, created the question­ naire and collected the data. SOB analyzed and interpreted student data regarding their perceptions of academic dishonesty, risk, use frequency as well as intention to use ChatGPT. Both authors interpreted the results. JB extracted the main practical implications and limitations of the study. Both read and approve the final manuscript. Funding Open Access funding provided thanks to the CRUE-CSIC agreement with Springer Nature. Not applicable. Data availability The datasets analysed during the current study are available in the The Open Science Framework (OSF) repository, https://osf.io/dashboard. [DOI https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO/S7QW6] Declarations Ethical approval This study received ethical approval from the Ethic Committee of the University of the Balearic Islands (Comité de Ética de la Investigación (CER), Approval Number: 353CER23 on September 21, 2023), before data collection, and all participants provided informed consent prior to their involvement in the study. Competing interests The authors declare that they do not have any competing interests. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution",180
A119,A119.pdf,10.1007/s11218-025-10080-2,16,"prior to their involvement in the study. Competing interests The authors declare that they do not have any competing interests. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n",149
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x Abstract AI has transformed education by reshaping teaching and learning processes. ChatG­ PT plays a significant role in enhancing these processes; however, effective educator training is essential for its optimal use. Educators and researchers are increasingly adopting AI chatbots, expecting them to improve learning experiences and reduce teacher workload. This study aims to identify the factors contributing to ChatGPT’s perceived value and analyze their impact on its continued usage among students. Additionally, the study introduces a new model and investigates the moderating ef­ fect of privacy risk on the relationship between perceived value and the continued use of ChatGPT. A total of 600 questionnaires were distributed to students enrolled in various universities in Malaysia, with 244 students indicating their willingness to continue using ChatGPT. Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling was used to analyze the data. The findings suggest that perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, problem-solving, idea generation, information quality, and effective learning are significant determinants of ChatGPT’s continuous use among students, both directly and indirectly through perceived value. The results show",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,1,"analyze the data. The findings suggest that perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, problem-solving, idea generation, information quality, and effective learning are significant determinants of ChatGPT’s continuous use among students, both directly and indirectly through perceived value. The results show that stu­ dents benefit from ChatGPT in enhancing their performance, problem-solving, and idea generation. In line with concerns about privacy risks in online applications, the study reveals that privacy concerns affect ChatGPT’s perceived value. Students prioritize its effectiveness as a study aid. Academic institutions can train students to use ChatGPT as a complementary learning tool, while instructors can leverage it to enhance their teaching practices. Keywords ChatGPT · AI in education · Student perceptions · Academic usage · Privacy concerns · Educational technology Received: 28 September 2024 / Accepted: 5 June 2025 / Published online: 18 June 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Unraveling the influential factors driving persistent adoption of ChatGPT in learning environments Sedigheh Moghavvemi1 · Farooq Ahmed Jam2,3 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",173
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,1,"Author(s) 2025 Unraveling the influential factors driving persistent adoption of ChatGPT in learning environments Sedigheh Moghavvemi1 · Farooq Ahmed Jam2,3 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",33
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 1 Introduction As technology advances, artificial intelligence (AI) is becoming increasingly inte­ grated into various aspects of society, including education (Adiguzel et al., 2023). The incorporation of AI and natural language processing (NLP) technologies into education has ushered in a new era of personalized and interactive learning (Vargas- Murillo et al., 2023). The use of AI-powered tools and applications such as ChatGPT, Google Gemini, GitHub Copilot, Scispace.com, Claude, and Zapier Agents – each built upon underlying large language models (LLMs) - has sparked significant inter­ est in the rapidly evolving intersection of education and technology, as they have the potential to enhance the learning process (Elbanna & Armstrong, 2023). These AI chatbots hold great promise for revolutionizing education by offering personal­ ized responses to student queries, supporting interactive learning, and improving the quality of education (Tlili et al., 2023). However, the effectiveness of these systems depends not only on their technological features but also on how users perceive and understand their results. For example, Lim et al. (2023) highlighted that, despite ChatGPT’s promising potential,",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,2,"education (Tlili et al., 2023). However, the effectiveness of these systems depends not only on their technological features but also on how users perceive and understand their results. For example, Lim et al. (2023) highlighted that, despite ChatGPT’s promising potential, its successful integration and continued use in edu­ cational settings rely on key factors influencing user experiences. This underscores the need for further research in this area. The global response to the acceptance or rejection of ChatGPT and similar systems in education remains an area yet to be fully explored (Sullivan et al., 2023). ChatGPT, with 400 million weekly active users globally, holds 62.5% of all AI tools market share (BACKLINKO, 2025). In the educational context, researchers have examined issues such as accuracy (Kung et al., 2023), the quality of ChatG­ PT’s responses (Fuchs, 2023), potential “hallucinations” in AI-generated outputs, the impact on critical thinking (Gerling et al., 2023), plagiarism (Kung et al., 2023), academic originality, and limitations highlighted by Rudolph et al. (2023). These limitations include challenges in understanding context, emotion, and creativity, as well as, concerns about misinformation,",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,2,"the impact on critical thinking (Gerling et al., 2023), plagiarism (Kung et al., 2023), academic originality, and limitations highlighted by Rudolph et al. (2023). These limitations include challenges in understanding context, emotion, and creativity, as well as, concerns about misinformation, inconsistent response quality, and the risk of unauthorized modifications. Additionally, ChatGPT raises academic and ethical challenges. Researchers (Tlili et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023) have addressed issues related to data privacy, biases, and user concerns. Interactions with ChatGPT may risk the disclosure of sensitive information. Despite these ethical concerns, educators and researchers believe AI chatbots like ChatGPT can enhance learning experiences and reduce teacher workload (Wu & Yu, 2024). They view ChatGPT as both an assis­ tant for educators and a tutor for students (Lo, 2023). A review of previous research indicates that while much has been done on the adoption and early acceptance of AI-driven technologies, significant knowledge gaps remain regarding the variables influencing their ongoing usage in educational envi­ ronments. Current research tends to focus on the early stages of adoption and accept­ ability, often neglecting the",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,2,"the adoption and early acceptance of AI-driven technologies, significant knowledge gaps remain regarding the variables influencing their ongoing usage in educational envi­ ronments. Current research tends to focus on the early stages of adoption and accept­ ability, often neglecting the factors and mechanisms that contribute to sustained use in educational settings. Further investigations are needed to identify the key factors influencing both students and instructors to adopt AI technologies - specifically Chat­ GPT or similar tools - in education. Moreover, exploring how academic institutions can derive benefits from these technologies, rather than restricting their use, becomes increasingly important. 1 3 22444",101
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 Despite the global prominence of the AI technology - particularly ChatGPT, which holds a significant market share - there remains a gap in understanding how stu­ dents respond to ChatGPT and how these responses relate to factors influencing its perceived value and continued use in education. This study aims to bridge this gap by exploring students’ perceptions of ChatGPT in education and examining how its perceived value - encompassing perceived usefulness, ease of use, problem-solving, idea generation, and information quality - correlates with sustained usage behavior. The objectives of this research are: 1. To identify the key factors influencing students’ perceptions of the value of using ChatGPT for academic purposes. 2. To examine the relationships between perceived value and continuous use in the context of ChatGPT usage in education. 3. To investigate whether privacy risk moderates the relationship between per­ ceived value and continuous usage intention in ChatGPT. This research focuses on ChatGPT users, as the platform is particularly popular among students in Malaysia. Data were collected from undergraduate and postgradu­ ate students, and",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,3,"risk moderates the relationship between per­ ceived value and continuous usage intention in ChatGPT. This research focuses on ChatGPT users, as the platform is particularly popular among students in Malaysia. Data were collected from undergraduate and postgradu­ ate students, and out of the 600 students contacted, 244 completed the questionnaire. Their responses were analyzed to investigate the factors influencing their intention to continue using ChatGPT for their studies. Through an in-depth analysis of these factors, the objective is to gain a comprehensive understanding of the elements that impact the ongoing use of ChatGPT in education. The goal is to provide educators, instructional designers, and AI developers with valuable insights for the effective implementation and sustained use of ChatGPT in educational settings. Understand­ ing these dynamics is crucial for successful integration. 2 Background of the study and hypotheses Previous research has demonstrated that the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) can be adapted to reflect the unique attributes and func­ tionalities of AI technologies, particularly in the context of AI-based tools (Jain et al., 2018). More recently, studies",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,3,"demonstrated that the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) can be adapted to reflect the unique attributes and func­ tionalities of AI technologies, particularly in the context of AI-based tools (Jain et al., 2018). More recently, studies have increasingly applied the UTAUT framework (Venkatesh et al., 2003) to investigate the acceptance and use of emerging chatbot technologies, such as ChatGPT (Bouteraa et al., 2024; Strzelecki, 2024; Strzelecki & ElArabawy, 2024). These studies have primarily focused on constructs such as ease of use, performance expectancy, social influence, self-efficacy, and user motivation – core elements of both UTAUT and Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (See Appendix A). While UTAUT and TAM remain fundamental in understanding technology adop­ tion, they were originally designed to explain general technology use and do not explicitly account for the specific purposes or functionalities of modern, task-ori­ ented technologies like generative AI. Their broad constructs – particularly perfor­ mance expectancy and ease of use – do not fully capture the nuances of contemporary AI tools, which are typically designed to be highly user-friendly and serve clearly",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,3,"task-ori­ ented technologies like generative AI. Their broad constructs – particularly perfor­ mance expectancy and ease of use – do not fully capture the nuances of contemporary AI tools, which are typically designed to be highly user-friendly and serve clearly 1 3 22445",43
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 defined functions. This evidence suggests that continued emphasis on a limited set of factors may not adequately address the complexities of AI use, particularly with tools like ChatGPT that are designed to be intuitive, adaptable, and goal specific. There is, therefore, a growing need for more refined, domain-specific models that integrate the distinct characteristics of AI technologies and the evolving expec­ tations of users. In the context of AI adoption, models should consider additional constructs such as task-specific performance expectancy, transparency, trust in AI decision-making, emotional trust, perceived social presence of AI, privacy risk, per­ ceived value, and information quality. These constructs reflect user perceptions of AI-generated outputs, including aspects such as idea generation and problem-solving -areas that require further exploration. This study aims to investigate the factors influencing the sustained use of Chat­ GPT in the education sector while recognizing the enduring relevance of traditional constructs such as performance expectancy and effort expectancy. Research has shown that problem-solving capabilities and perceived value are key determinants of ChatGPT use among students (Zhai, 2022; Lim",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,4,"GPT in the education sector while recognizing the enduring relevance of traditional constructs such as performance expectancy and effort expectancy. Research has shown that problem-solving capabilities and perceived value are key determinants of ChatGPT use among students (Zhai, 2022; Lim et al., 2023; Noy & Zhang, 2023; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023; Romero-Rodríguez et al., 2023). Other studies have explored the influence of idea generation (Bal & Pratima, 2023; Füller et al., 2022; Rico Sesé, 2023) and information quality (Walker et al., 2023; Lu et al., 2023; Menon & Shilpa, 2023) on perceived value and user engagement. Some researchers argue that the use of ChatGPT can enhance learning outcomes (Firaina & Sulisworo, 2023; AlAfnan et al., 2023; Rasul et al., 2023; Chinonso et al., 2023; Baidoo-Anu & Ansah, 2023), while others highlight concerns about privacy risks associated with AI usage (Shahid et al., 2023; Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023; Sharma & Sharma, 2023). A review of these studies reveals that although various factors influencing the value of AI use have been examined, there is a lack of comprehensive models that test",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,4,"(Shahid et al., 2023; Choudhury & Shamszare, 2023; Sharma & Sharma, 2023). A review of these studies reveals that although various factors influencing the value of AI use have been examined, there is a lack of comprehensive models that test how these factors affect continuous usage, particularly in light of privacy con­ cerns. While multiple technology acceptance models have been employed, few have been conceptualized specifically for AI use in an educational context. The behavioral expectations of students often differ from those in other domains; students typically seek systems that can answer questions, solve problems, generate ideas, and deliver reliable, instructor-like responses. In response to this gap, the present study introduces the Artificial Intelligence Acceptance and Use Model (AIA-UM) - a conceptual framework developed to eval­ uate both the initial adoption and sustained use of AI tools in academic settings. The AIA-UM builds upon established constructs from TAM and UTAUT, such as perfor­ mance expectancy and effort expectancy, while extending these models by integrat­ ingAI-specific factors particularly relevant to educational contexts. These include problem-solving, idea generation, information quality, and effective",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,4,"The AIA-UM builds upon established constructs from TAM and UTAUT, such as perfor­ mance expectancy and effort expectancy, while extending these models by integrat­ ingAI-specific factors particularly relevant to educational contexts. These include problem-solving, idea generation, information quality, and effective learning- dimen­ tsions that reflect how users engage with task-oriented generative AI technologies. A key innovation of this model is the inclusion of user-perceived value as a central mediating construct that links traditional acceptance variables with the intention to continue using AI tools. By incorprating both general and AI-specific predictors, the AIA-UM offers a more tailored and comprehensive explanation of AI adotion and continued use within academic environments. 1 3 22446",111
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 Moreover, the AIA-UM model introduces four novel constructs: problem-solving, idea generation, information quality, and effective learning. These dimensions cap­ ture how students interact with AI tools like ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot, and Google Gemini– not merely for convenience but to achieve meaningful academic outcomes. The AIA-UM model reflects the interplay between technology features, user cogni­ tion and perception, and sustained behavioral intentions. As a comprehensive frame­ work, AIA-UM aims to better capture the dynamic and evolving nature of AI use in education, bridging the gap between foundational theory and contemporary user needs (Fig. 1). The following section discusses each hypothesis and the components of the AIA-UM research model. 2.1 Perceived usefulness and perceived value of ChatGPT Perceived usefulness, as defined by Davis (1989), refers to consumers’ perception of the value and benefits associated with technology. In the context of ChatGPT, it concerns how users perceive the tool’s impact on their educational outcomes and overall knowledge acquisition. Davis (1989) found that perceptions of enhanced per­ formance strongly predict users’ intention to continue using a technology. In",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,5,"In the context of ChatGPT, it concerns how users perceive the tool’s impact on their educational outcomes and overall knowledge acquisition. Davis (1989) found that perceptions of enhanced per­ formance strongly predict users’ intention to continue using a technology. In the case of ChatGPT, and as defined by the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technol­ ogy (UTAUT), perceived usefulness pertains to the degree to which users believe that using ChatGPT will improve their ability to carry out tasks more effectively and effi­ ciently. Previous research on chatbots indicates that performance expectancy plays a crucial role in determining their acceptability and use (Menon & Shilpa, 2023). The perceived value of using ChatGPT may be influenced by factors such as its perceived utility, reliability, and impact on work performance (Kasilingam, 2020), as well as its ability to complete tasks, provide a sense of achievement, and increase engage­ ment (Mogaji et al., 2021). Based on these findings, this research proposes that the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT influences users’ perception of its value. H1: Perceived usefulness is positively related to the perceived",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,5,"a sense of achievement, and increase engage­ ment (Mogaji et al., 2021). Based on these findings, this research proposes that the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT influences users’ perception of its value. H1: Perceived usefulness is positively related to the perceived value of the using ChatGPT. Fig. 1 AI Acceptance and Use Model (AIA-UM) 1 3 22447",56
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 2.2 Perceived ease of use and perceived value of ChatGPT Perceived ease of use refers to the anticipated level of convenience associated with using a particular technology (Venkatesh et al., 2003). The UTAUT suggests that the perceived value of using a technology is influenced by several factors, including perceived ease of use (Muriithi et al., 2016). In an educational context, a complicated or time-consuming interface for ChatGPT may discourage students and instructors from using it (Javaid et al., 2023). Previous research on chatbots presents contradic­ tory findings regarding the perceived ease of use. Some studies have shown that the perceived ease of use of chatbots has a negative correlation with the desire to use them (Melián-González et al., 2021). In contrast, more recent research examining the implementation and use of chatbots in the banking industry (Mogaji et al., 2021) found that the level of effort required significantly influences chatbot adoption. For ChatGPT, a high level of perceived ease of use is likely to positively influence its acceptance and use. If users find ChatGPT easy",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,6,"industry (Mogaji et al., 2021) found that the level of effort required significantly influences chatbot adoption. For ChatGPT, a high level of perceived ease of use is likely to positively influence its acceptance and use. If users find ChatGPT easy to use, they are more likely to adopt and integrate it into their regular routines. H2: Perceived ease of use is positively related to the perceived value of the use of ChatGPT. 2.3 Problem-Solving and perceived value of ChatGPT Empirical evidence on the influence of problem-solving ability on the perceived value of ChatGPT remains limited (Noy & Zhang, 2023). Problem-solving tasks, such as essay writing, case study analysis, and decision-making dilemmas (Jonas­ sen, 2011), are widely used in education across various disciplines (van Gog et al., 2020). These tasks are designed to enhance creativity and innovation, as highlighted by previous scholars (DeHaan, 2009). However, the introduction of AI technologies like ChatGPT may significantly alter the problem-solving process, particularly in cases involving ill-defined problems (Zhai, 2022). ChatGPT is a highly versatile tool with the potential to revolutionize various industries including education,",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,6,"scholars (DeHaan, 2009). However, the introduction of AI technologies like ChatGPT may significantly alter the problem-solving process, particularly in cases involving ill-defined problems (Zhai, 2022). ChatGPT is a highly versatile tool with the potential to revolutionize various industries including education, healthcare, finance, tourism, and marketing (Dai et al., 2023). It facilitates problem-solving by offering multiple perspectives, providing relevant information, and generating pro­ totype solutions (Zhai, 2022). The growing reliance on AI-driven problem-solving has raised concerns among educators regarding its long-term impact on institutions of higher education (Lim et al., 2023). Research suggests that ChatGPT enhances the quality of problem-solving outcomes. For example, Yilmaz and Karaoglan Yilmaz (2023) found that ChatGPT improved cognitive efficiency in programming courses. Similarly, Romero-Rodríguez et al. (2023) observed that university students used ChatGPT for complex cognitive tasks and problem-solving. Moreover, Duhaylung­ sod and Chavez (2023) highlighted that ChatGPT supports technology-based learners by fostering creativity and innovation. Given these findings, this study posits that problem-solving is a crucial antecedent of the perceived value of ChatGPT. H3: Problem-solving is positively related to the perceived value of the using",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,6,"that ChatGPT supports technology-based learners by fostering creativity and innovation. Given these findings, this study posits that problem-solving is a crucial antecedent of the perceived value of ChatGPT. H3: Problem-solving is positively related to the perceived value of the using of ChatGPT. 1 3 22448",45
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 2.4 Idea generation and perceived value of ChatGPT The increasing demand for inventive and user-oriented product design has intensi­ fied the need for efficient idea-generation techniques. Designers employ a variety of methodologies, each with distinct advantages and limitations (Chulvi et al., 2013). These methodologies range from traditional brainstorming and structured techniques like TRIZ to innovative AI-driven approaches such as ChatGPT and Google Bard (Bal & Pratima, 2023; Füller et al., 2022). Conventional idea-generation methods include brainstorming, mind mapping, and visual sketching (Goldenberg & Wiley, 2019; Malycha & Maier, 2017; Novak et al., 1984). However, the digital transforma­ tion of education has led to the emergence of AI-assisted tools that facilitate ideation, including generative design software, data-driven methodologies, and natural lan­ guage processing technologies like ChatGPT and Google Bard (Rico Sesé, 2023). AI-powered tools, particularly ChatGPT, demonstrate significant potential in enhanc­ ing idea generation across various domains, including education. While digital tools streamline the creative process, challenges such as reduced originality and difficulties in translating abstract ideas into tangible outcomes remain (Rico Sesé, 2023). Given",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,7,"ChatGPT, demonstrate significant potential in enhanc­ ing idea generation across various domains, including education. While digital tools streamline the creative process, challenges such as reduced originality and difficulties in translating abstract ideas into tangible outcomes remain (Rico Sesé, 2023). Given its capacity to facilitate idea generation in educational contexts, this study explores the perceived value of ChatGPT as an AI-driven ideation tool. H4: Idea generation is positively related to the perceived value of using of ChatGPT. 2.5 Information quality and perceived value of ChatGPT The quality of information plays a crucial role in the educational context (Zwain, 2019), and ChatGPT’s ability to provide accurate and reliable information is of para­ mount importance (Walker et al., 2023). Users’ continued engagement with the sys­ tem largely depends on their confidence in the accuracy and trustworthiness of the information it generates (Lu et al., 2023). The perceived value of ChatGPT is strongly influenced by users’ assessment of its accuracy, relevance, and reliability. Chin and Kim (2015) highlighted the impact of information quality on users’ technological experiences, demonstrating that perceived content and system quality",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,7,"al., 2023). The perceived value of ChatGPT is strongly influenced by users’ assessment of its accuracy, relevance, and reliability. Chin and Kim (2015) highlighted the impact of information quality on users’ technological experiences, demonstrating that perceived content and system quality significantly influence perceived usefulness. Building on previous research, this study examines the perceived value of ChatGPT in educational settings, with a particular focus on how information quality shapes user perceptions. It is proposed that the quality of information generated by ChatGPT or similar AI-driven tools directly influences the perceived value of using ChatGPT. H5: Information quality is positively related to the perceived value of using ChatGPT. 2.6 Perceived value of ChatGPT and continuous use of ChatGPT The use of ChatGPT in higher education is generally perceived positively, with both students and instructors considering it a valuable and engaging learning tool (Elkhodr et al., 2023; Firaina & Sulisworo, 2023). Research by Elkhodr et al. (2023) suggests that ChatGPT enhances learning performance, user experience, and content compre­ 1 3 22449",168
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,8,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 hension compared to traditional search engines. However, concerns have been raised regarding its potential impact on cognitive abilities and the need for responsible usage (Vargas-Murillo et al., 2023). Despite these challenges, ChatGPT’s perceived value -such as its role in facilitating problem-solving and improving learning efficiency- is widely acknowledged (Firaina & Sulisworo, 2023; Malinka et al., 2023). Ngo (2023) found that students appreciate ChatGPT’s efficiency and personalized assistance, although they express concerns about the reliability of sources and the authenticity of citations. Similarly, Romero-Rodríguez et al. (2023) identified experience, per­ formance expectancy, and habit as key factors influencing students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Shoufan (2023) noted that while students recognized ChatGPT’s capabili­ ties, they also highlighted concerns regarding its accuracy and the need for founda­ tional knowledge. Additionally, Mogavi et al. (2023), examined ChatGPT’s primary applications in higher education and warned that it could encourage superficial learn­ ing behaviors. These findings suggest that while students acknowledge ChatGPT’s benefits, their willingness to continue using it depends on its perceived value. Based on this literature, we propose",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,8,"applications in higher education and warned that it could encourage superficial learn­ ing behaviors. These findings suggest that while students acknowledge ChatGPT’s benefits, their willingness to continue using it depends on its perceived value. Based on this literature, we propose that: H6: The perceived value of using ChatGPT is positively related to the continuous use of ChatGPT. 2.7 Effective learning and continuous use of ChatGPT Several studies have explored the role of ChatGPT in education, specifically its impacts on learning outcomes and efficiency. Firaina and Sulisworo (2023) found that ChatGPT enhances learning efficiency and output, particularly in knowledge retrieval and generation of alternative questions. Similarly, Fauzi et al. (2023) high­ lighted its role in improving student efficiency by facilitating information dissemi­ nation, enhancing language proficiency, and fostering collaboration. These findings suggest that effective learning is a key factor in the continuous use of ChatGPT in educational settings. ChatGPT can serve as a tool to support discussions on chal­ lenges encountered during assignments or to accelerate the learning process (Rasul et al., 2023). Additionally, AI-driven technologies can facilitate faster learning in",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,8,"continuous use of ChatGPT in educational settings. ChatGPT can serve as a tool to support discussions on chal­ lenges encountered during assignments or to accelerate the learning process (Rasul et al., 2023). Additionally, AI-driven technologies can facilitate faster learning in certain subjects, thereby enhancing overall learning effectiveness (Chinonso et al., 2023). The primary focus has been on improving the learning experience by assist­ ing students and streamlining repetitive tasks for instructors (Seetharaman, 2023). For IT-proficient learners, ChatGPT can expedite knowledge acquisition in emerging technologies. Well-structured queries further enhance learning efficiency by enabling ChatGPT to provide comprehensive explanations of key concepts or generate exam­ ple codes alongside official documentation (Malinka et al., 2023). Based on the exist­ ing literature, this study hypothesizes: H7: Effective learning is positively related to the continuous use of ChatGPT. 1 3 22450",136
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 2.8 Perceived Value of ChatGPT as a Mediator on the Relationship Between key Factors and Continuous Use of ChatGPT Extensive research has examined the potential and limitations of Generative AI technologies, such as ChatGPT, across various domains (Jimenez, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023; Kutela et al., 2023), including their applications in education (Adiguzel et al., 2023). Studies have analyzed AI adoption broadly, highlighting its benefits, difficulties, and ethical implications associated with using GenAI technologies in various contexts (Sallam, 2023; Tlili et al., 2023). Research consistently shows that performance expectancy –the belief that using ChatGPT enhances academic perfor­ mance- significantly influences its perceived value, which, in turn, drives continuous use (Binekas & Belgiawan, 2023; Kim & Oh, 2023; Li & Zhang, 2023; Romero- Rodríguez et al., 2023). Likewise, Shahsavar and Choudhury (2023) emphasized that effort expectancy – the ease of use – also affects the perceived value of ChatGPT, further contributing to its sustained adoption. Perceived value represents the user’s overall assessment of the system’s benefits, incorporating both functional aspects (usefulness, ease of use) and",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,9,"expectancy – the ease of use – also affects the perceived value of ChatGPT, further contributing to its sustained adoption. Perceived value represents the user’s overall assessment of the system’s benefits, incorporating both functional aspects (usefulness, ease of use) and experiential components (problem-solving, idea genera­ tion, information quality). When users perceive ChatGPT as highly beneficial due to these attributes, they are more likely to continue using it. Within the AIA-UM model, perceived value functions as a mediating variable between several key predictors— perceived usefulness, ease of use, problem-solving capabilities, idea generation, and information quality—and the intention to continuously use ChatGPT. Accordingly, this study hypothesizes: H8: Perceived value mediates the relationship between the perceived usefulness of ChatGPT and its continuous use among students. H9: Perceived value mediates the relationship between the perceived ease of use of ChatGPT and its continuous use among students. H10: Perceived value mediates the relationship between the problem-solving and continuous use of ChatGPT among students. H11: Perceived value mediates the relationship between idea generation and continu­ ous use of ChatGPT among students. H12: Perceived value mediates the",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,9,"among students. H10: Perceived value mediates the relationship between the problem-solving and continuous use of ChatGPT among students. H11: Perceived value mediates the relationship between idea generation and continu­ ous use of ChatGPT among students. H12: Perceived value mediates the relationship between the information quality and continuous use of ChatGPT among students. 2.9 Moderating Effect of Privacy Risk on the Relationship between Perceived Value of ChatGPT and Continuous Use of ChatGPT The relationship between the perceived value of ChatGPT and its continuous use is influenced by various factors, including privacy concerns. Cheng and Jiang (2020) highlighted the negative impact of perceived privacy risk on user satisfaction with AI-driven chatbots, which, in turn, affects their decision to continue using them. Sim­ ilarly, Yin et al. (2015) found that perceived information privacy risk moderates the relationship between fear of missing out and continuance intention in social network­ ing sites. These studies collectively suggest that privacy risk plays a crucial role in shaping users’ long-term engagement with technology. 1 3 22451",168
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,10,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 Shahid et al. (2023) found that users expressed concerns about potential privacy risks associated with ChatGPT, particularly regarding data retention and protection. Choudhury and Shamszare (2023) further emphasized that privacy is a key factor influencing user acceptance of ChatGPT. In the healthcare sector, Sharma and Sharma (2023) identified privacy concerns as significant ethical and security considerations, particularly in cases involving confidential medical data processed by AI-driven sys­ tems like ChatGPT. Building on prior research, privacy risk is expected to reduce the perceived value of ChatGPT ultimately lowering users’ willingness to continue using it. While privacy concerns have been widely studied about AI adoption, no research has explicitly examined privacy risk as a moderator in the relationship between the perceived value and the continuous use of ChatGPT. Thus, this study hypothesizes: H13: Privacy risk moderates the relationship between perceived value and continu­ ous use of ChatGPT. Specifically, for users with low privacy concerns, the positive relationship between perceived value and continuous use is expected to be stronger. Conversely, for users with high privacy concerns, this",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,10,"the relationship between perceived value and continu­ ous use of ChatGPT. Specifically, for users with low privacy concerns, the positive relationship between perceived value and continuous use is expected to be stronger. Conversely, for users with high privacy concerns, this relationship may weaken or even become insignificant. 3 Research methodology 3.1 Sampling procedure and data collection This research aimed to examine university students’ perspectives on the perceived value and continuous use of ChatGPT within the university setting. To achieve this objective, we collected data from a diverse group of students across multiple uni­ versities in Malaysia, including the University of Malaya, Universiti Utara Malay­ sia, Universiti Putra Malaysia, Taylor’s University, and Universiti Teknologi MARA in the Klang Valley. Before data collection, the necessary ethical approvals were obtained from the researchers’ institutional ethics committees, ensuring compliance with established protocols (Dalle et al., 2021). The researchers then engaged with the administrative offices of the respective universities to seek further authorization. The study’s objective was clearly explained to the university administrators, and upon receiving formal approval, students were invited to participate in the",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,10,"al., 2021). The researchers then engaged with the administrative offices of the respective universities to seek further authorization. The study’s objective was clearly explained to the university administrators, and upon receiving formal approval, students were invited to participate in the survey. Three researchers physically distributed printed questionnaires to ensure they reached the right sample. Before distribution, they confirmed that the respondents were ChatGPT users. A purposive sampling approach was initially used to target students familiar with ChatGPT. A filtering question ensured that only ChatGPT users could proceed with the survey. Subsequently, convenience sampling was employed, with questionnaires distributed in high-traffic student areas such as libraries, study rooms, university bus stops, and faculty buildings to maximize participation. The required sample size was determined using G*Power, which indicated a minimum of 160 participants. As noted by Kang (2021), G*Power is a reliable tool for sample size estimation. Using 1 3 22452",149
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 the inverse square root method with a significance level of 0.05 and a path coefficient of 0.2, the minimum required sample size was 155 (Kock & Hadaya, 2018). Initially, 600 students were approached, and a total of 497 responses were received. However, 126 students opted not to disclose information regarding their ChatGPT usage and left the questionnaire incomplete, resulting in a missing rate of approximately 30%. Additionally, 127 respondents expressed unwillingness to continue using ChatGPT due to concerns about privacy and data security. After removing these incomplete and ineligible responses, a total of 244 complete and valid responses were retained for further analysis, yielding a final usable response rate of approximately 49.1% will less than 2% item-level missing data. The final sample consisted of 56.1% male and 43.9% female participants. Regarding age distribution, 74.2% of respondents were aged 18–25, followed by 16.4% (26–30 years), 4.9% (31–35 years), 2.9% (36– 40 years), and 1.6% (41 years and older). In terms of educational level, 63.9% of respondents were undergraduate students, 29.1% were pursuing a master’s degree,",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,11,"of respondents were aged 18–25, followed by 16.4% (26–30 years), 4.9% (31–35 years), 2.9% (36– 40 years), and 1.6% (41 years and older). In terms of educational level, 63.9% of respondents were undergraduate students, 29.1% were pursuing a master’s degree, and 7.0% were Ph.D. candidates. 3.2 Measurement Participants were asked to evaluate survey items using a 5-point Likert scale rang­ ing from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. Perceived usefulness and per­ ceived ease of use were each measured using a six-item scale developed by Davis (1989). Problem-solving capabilities were assessed using three items based on the Task-Technology Fit (TTF) model adapted from Yang & Lin, 2015a); Shahzad et al. (2023); Ayyoub et al. (2023). These items were refined to align with ChatGPT’s role in addressing and resolving educational challenges. Idea generation was measured using six newly developed items grounded in the Creativity Support Systems (CSS) framework (Shneiderman, 2007), and recent literature highlighting ChatGPT’s role in ideation. Studies by Rahman et al. (2023), Dowling and Lucey (2023), and Filippi (2023) support the use of ChatGPT for stimulating",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,11,"developed items grounded in the Creativity Support Systems (CSS) framework (Shneiderman, 2007), and recent literature highlighting ChatGPT’s role in ideation. Studies by Rahman et al. (2023), Dowling and Lucey (2023), and Filippi (2023) support the use of ChatGPT for stimulating creativity and enhancing critical thinking. This construct was introduced to explore ChatGPT’s impact on idea gen­ eration, problem-solving confidence, and creative thinking in an educational setting. Information quality was characterized by accuracy, relevance, completeness, and understandability (DeLone & McLean, 1992). Four items were adapted from Fang et al. (2011), Zaied (2012), and Delone and McLean (2003) to assess the reliability and accuracy of information provided by ChatGPT for educational purposes. Perceived value was measured using four items adapted from Kim et al. (2007) and Wang and Wang (2010) to evaluate ChatGPT’s utility and experiential benefits in an educational context. Effective learning was conceptualized as the extent to which ChatGPT meets user expectations and proves useful post-adoption. This was measured using two items related to productivity, effectiveness, and overall usefulness, adopted from the Expectation-Confirmation Model (ECM) (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Liu et al.,",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,11,"learning was conceptualized as the extent to which ChatGPT meets user expectations and proves useful post-adoption. This was measured using two items related to productivity, effectiveness, and overall usefulness, adopted from the Expectation-Confirmation Model (ECM) (Bhattacherjee, 2001; Liu et al., 2020). Pri­ vacy risk was measured using five items focusing on concerns related to data security and privacy while using ChatGPT. These items were adapted from established scales by Featherman and Pavlou (2003); Yang et al., 2015b); Cheng and Jiang (2020); Vimalkumar et al. (2021). Continuous use Intention was measured using four items adapted from Bhattacherjee (2001). 1 3 22453",100
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,12,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 The surveys were conducted in English, as it is widely understood in Malaysian universities. To mitigate common method bias (CMB), we employed a research design that carefully addressed potential operational and statistical biases (Podsa­ koff et al., 2012). Several measures were taken, including: ensuring anonymity and voluntary participation, using clear and concise wording to enhance comprehension, and implementing the Harman single-factor technique, where all items were loaded into a single factor without a rotation matrix. The results showed that the first factor accounted for 28.86% of the variance, which is well below the 50% threshold (Mal­ hotra et al., 2017), confirming that CMB was not a concern in this study. 4 Results The first part of the questionnaire, which included demographic data, was analyzed using SPSS, while the second part, covering the measurement and structural models of the study, was assessed using SmartPLS (Ringle et al., 2022). 4.1 Measurement model The measurement model was evaluated using four key criteria in PLS-SEM: factor loadings, reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. Figure 2 presents the measurement",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,12,"structural models of the study, was assessed using SmartPLS (Ringle et al., 2022). 4.1 Measurement model The measurement model was evaluated using four key criteria in PLS-SEM: factor loadings, reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. Figure 2 presents the measurement model of the study. The factor loadings ranged from 0.743 to 0.953, as shown in Table 1. Reliability was assessed using Cronbach’s alpha and composite reliability (CR), both of which exceeded the recommended threshold of 0.70 (Hair et al., 2019). Consequently, all constructs met the required reliability criteria (Table 1). Convergent validity, which refers to the extent to which a set of indicators accurately measures a particular con­ struct (Hair et al., 2019), was assessed using the average variance extracted (AVE). Fig. 2 Measurement Model 1 3 22454",128
