A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,14,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 According to Hair et al. (2017), an AVE value above 0.50 indicates adequate con­ vergent validity. As shown in Table 1, all AVE values exceeded the 0.50 threshold, confirming convergent validity. To assess discriminant validity, Henseler et al. (2015) recommended using the HTMT ratio, which is considered a more robust method when applying SmartPLS. Following the guidelines of Franke and Sarstedt (2019), all HTMT ratios were below the recommended threshold of 0.85 (Table 2), indicating that the constructs in this study are distinct and measure different concepts. 4.2 Structural model The structural model was analyzed to examine the hypothesized relationships among key factors influencing perceived value and the continuous use of ChatGPT. The model was assessed using path coefficients (β), t-values, and p-values, which deter­ mine the statistical significance of the hypothesized relationship (Hair et al., 2017). To validate the hypothesized relationship, a bootstrapping technique with 5000 resa­ mples was employed (Hair et al., 2019). Figure 3 illustrates the structural model of the study. The results for the direct relationships indicated the following: perceived",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,14,"et al., 2017). To validate the hypothesized relationship, a bootstrapping technique with 5000 resa­ mples was employed (Hair et al., 2019). Figure 3 illustrates the structural model of the study. The results for the direct relationships indicated the following: perceived useful­ ness significantly influences the perceived value of ChatGPT (β = 0.159; p = 0.027) supporting H1; perceived ease of use has a significant positive effect on perceived value (β = 0.171; p = 0.029) supporting H2; problem-solving capabilities positively impact perceived value (β = 0.172; p = 0.044) supporting H3; idea generation signifi­ cantly enhances perceived value (β = 0.196; p = 0.009) supporting H4; information quality has a significant positive effect on perceived value (β = 0.098; p = 0.026) sup­ Table 2 Discriminant validity (Heterotrait-Monotrait Ratio) Constructs 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 Perceived usefulness Perceived ease of use 0.581 Problem-Solving 0.615 0.822 Idea Generation 0.347 0.484 0.423 Information Quality 0.053 0.173 0.163 0.150 Perceived Value 0.435 0.517 0.524 0.422 0.196 Effective Learning 0.285 0.339 0.439 0.203 0.263 0.146 Privacy Risk 0.049 0.186 0.169",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,14,"usefulness Perceived ease of use 0.581 Problem-Solving 0.615 0.822 Idea Generation 0.347 0.484 0.423 Information Quality 0.053 0.173 0.163 0.150 Perceived Value 0.435 0.517 0.524 0.422 0.196 Effective Learning 0.285 0.339 0.439 0.203 0.263 0.146 Privacy Risk 0.049 0.186 0.169 0.197 0.371 0.153 0.075 Continuous Use 0.187 0.314 0.288 0.155 0.133 0.310 0.163 0.276 Variables Items Loadings CA CR AVE Continuous Use 0.915 0.940 0.798 CUI1 0.906 CUI2 0.945 CUI3 0.906 CUI4 0.811 CA, Cronbach’s Alpha; CR, composite reliability; AVE, average variance extracted. Table 1 (continued) 1 3 22456",89
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 porting H5; a perceived value significantly influences the continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.274; p = 0.000) supporting H6; and effective learning has a significant positive impact on continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.111; p = 0.030) supporting H7. Thus, H1, H2, H3, H4, H5, H6, and H7 in this study are supported (Table 3). The results of the effect size test (f²) revealed that among predictors of perceived value, idea genera­ tion (f² = 0.043) and performance expectancy (f² = 0.0237) exert the most influence, although their effects remain within the small range. Following the evaluation of direct relationships, the study further examined the indirect relationships among variables. Specifically, the mediation effect of the per­ ceived value of ChatGPT on the relationship between key predictors and continuous use was assessed. The mediation analysis revealed the following statistically sig­ nificant indirect effects: perceived value mediates the relationship between perceived usefulness and continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.043; p = 0.044), supporting H8; perceived value mediates the relationship between perceived ease of",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,15,"mediation analysis revealed the following statistically sig­ nificant indirect effects: perceived value mediates the relationship between perceived usefulness and continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.043; p = 0.044), supporting H8; perceived value mediates the relationship between perceived ease of use and continu­ ous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.047; p = 0.044), supporting H9; perceived value mediates the relationship between problem-solving capability and continuous use of ChatGPT Table 3 Direct relationships No. Path Analysis β SD t-Statistic P-Value Results f² H1 Perceived Usefulness -> Perceived Value 0.159 0.082 1.933 0.027 Supported 0.0237 H2 Perceived Ease of Use -> Perceived Value 0.171 0.090 1.898 0.029 Supported 0.0178 H3 Problem Solving -> Perceived Value 0.172 0.101 1.707 0.044 Supported 0.0193 H4 Idea Generation -> Perceived Value 0.196 0.083 2.359 0.009 Supported 0.043 H5 Information Quality -> Perceived Value 0.098 0.050 1.947 0.026 Supported 0.0134 H6 Perceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.274 0.063 4.369 0.000 Supported H7 Effective Learning -> Continuous Use 0.111 0.059 1.876 0.030 Supported 0.0142 β, Original Sample; SM, Sample Mean; SD, Standard Deviation Fig. 3 Structural Model 1",180
