A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 (β = 0.047; p = 0.049), supporting H10; perceived value mediates the relationship between idea generation and continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.054; p = 0.031) sup­ porting H11; and perceived value mediates the relationship between information quality and continuous use of ChatGPT (β = 0.027; p = 0.045) supporting H12. Thus, H8, H9, H10, H11, and H12 are supported. Figure 3; Table 4 provide a detailed over­ view of the mediation analysis conducted in this study. Additionally, the study examined the moderating effect of privacy risk on the rela­ tionship between perceived value and the continuous use of ChatGPT. The results indicate that privacy risk significantly moderates this relationship (β = 0.098; p = 0.030), supporting H13. Table 5 presents a comprehensive summary of the modera­ tion analysis. 5 Discussion and conclusions The objective of this study was to investigate the utilization of ChatGPT among stu­ dents and identify factors that contribute to their continued usage. Data were col­ lected from undergraduate and postgraduate students enrolled in public and private universities in",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,16,"The objective of this study was to investigate the utilization of ChatGPT among stu­ dents and identify factors that contribute to their continued usage. Data were col­ lected from undergraduate and postgraduate students enrolled in public and private universities in the Klang Valley, Malaysia. During the data collection, it was observed that some students were reluctant to disclose their use of ChatGPT, possibly due to social pressure or the perception among lecturers that students should not rely on AI for their assignments. Additionally, concerns about privacy risks led some students to hesitate in using the tool. These concerns impacted their willingness to answer many questions in the questionnaire, leading to the omission of their responses from further analysis. Nevertheless, the findings indicate that a substantial number of university students across various educational levels perceive ChatGPT as a valuable learning Table 4 Mediating effect No. Path Analysis β SM SD t-Statistic P-Value Results H8 Perceived Usefulness -> Per­ ceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.043 0.043 0.026 1.705 0.044 Sup­ ported H9 Perceived Ease of Use -> Per­ ceived Value -> Continuous",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,16,"4 Mediating effect No. Path Analysis β SM SD t-Statistic P-Value Results H8 Perceived Usefulness -> Per­ ceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.043 0.043 0.026 1.705 0.044 Sup­ ported H9 Perceived Ease of Use -> Per­ ceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.047 0.049 0.027 1.702 0.044 Sup­ ported H10 Problem Solving -> Perceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.047 0.045 0.029 1.653 0.049 Sup­ ported H11 Idea Generation -> Perceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.054 0.055 0.029 1.865 0.031 Sup­ ported H12 Information Quality -> Perceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.027 0.027 0.016 1.699 0.045 Sup­ ported β, Original Sample; SM, Sample Mean; SD, Standard Deviation. Table 5 Moderating relationships No. Path Analysis β SM SD t-Statistic P-Value Results H13 Privacy Risk x Perceived Value -> Continuous Use 0.098 0.099 0.052 1.885 0.030 Sup­ ported β, Original Sample; SM, Sample Mean; SD, Standard Deviation. 1 3 22458",146
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 tool. It met their expectations regarding performance, effort, problem-solving, and idea generation. Furthermore, a majority of students expressed an intention to con­ tinue using AI tools such as ChatGPT in their future academic pursuits. This research enhances the understanding of student perceptions regarding the use of ChatGPT in higher education. The study found results supporting the relationship between perceived usefulness and the perceived value of using ChatGPT. These findings suggest that the better ChatGPT meets or exceeds user expectations in terms of performance, the higher its perceived value becomes. Collectively, these findings highlight that users’ percep­ tions of an AI technology’s performance significantly shape its perceived value. This relationship is supported by various studies, including Kuadey et al. (2022), Kim and Oh (2023), Emon et al. (2023), and Shahsavar and Choudhury (2023). The relationship between perceived ease of use and the perceived value of using ChatGPT highlights the importance of designing a system that is easy to use and requires minimal effort. Previous research shows that a complicated or time-con­ suming interface may discourage",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,17,"between perceived ease of use and the perceived value of using ChatGPT highlights the importance of designing a system that is easy to use and requires minimal effort. Previous research shows that a complicated or time-con­ suming interface may discourage students and instructors from utilizing ChatGPT (Javaid et al., 2023). If users find the system difficult to navigate, they are less likely to engage with it. The relationship between problem-solving and the perceived value of using Chat­ GPT indicates that the more effective ChatGPT is at solving problems, the more valu­ able users find it. This result reflects users’ expectations of modern AI tools, as users now expect AI to do more than just deliver information - they want it to play an active role in problem-solving.This finding aligns with studies by Noy and Zhang (2023), and Urban et al. (2023), which highlight the impact of effective problem-solving on enhancing the value of AI technologies. The results also support the relationship between idea generation and the perceived value of using ChatGPT, suggesting that users view its idea generation capabilities as",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,17,"(2023), which highlight the impact of effective problem-solving on enhancing the value of AI technologies. The results also support the relationship between idea generation and the perceived value of using ChatGPT, suggesting that users view its idea generation capabilities as a key component of its value. These findings are consistent with previous studies, such as those by Chan and Hu (2023), Rahman et al. (2023), and Duhaylungsod and Chavez (2023). ChatGPT’s ability to generate new ideas and solutions enhances the creative learning process, particularly in activities such as creative writing, brain­ storming sessions, or problem-based learning. These features stimulate critical think­ ing and foster creativity among learners, further emphasizing ChatGPT’s utility as a writing assistant and research aid, where its efficiency complements users’ critical thinking skills. The relationship between information quality and the perceived value of using ChatGPT highlights the importance of accuracy and reliability in making the system a valuable learning tool. This is consistent with findings from Walker et al. (2023). Users’ selection and continued engagement with AI systems largely depend on their confidence in the accuracy and",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,17,"importance of accuracy and reliability in making the system a valuable learning tool. This is consistent with findings from Walker et al. (2023). Users’ selection and continued engagement with AI systems largely depend on their confidence in the accuracy and trustworthiness of the information generated. This relationship is further supported by previous research, such as Lu et al. (2023). The relationship between perceived value and the continuance intention of using ChatGPT provides significant insight into user behavior and decision-making regarding AI tools. This relationship underscores the critical role of perceived value in determining users’ intentions to continue using ChatGPT. Echoing the findings of McLean and Osei-Frimpong (2019) and Akel and Armagan (2021), the results 1 3 22459",117
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 suggest that users are more likely to continue using ChatGPT if they perceive it as valuable. In this research, perceived value stems from both functional and hedonic aspects of ChatGPT. Privacy risk significantly influences the relationship between perceived value and the continuance intention of using ChatGPT. Although users’ decisions to continue using ChatGPT are influenced by its perceived value, privacy concerns can alter these decisions. The findings of the present study show that some students were unwill­ ing to use ChatGPT due to privacy concerns during data collection. This contrasts with the findings of Shaengchart et al. (2023), who found that privacy and security concerns did not directly influence ChatGPT use among higher education students in Bangkok. Previous studies have emphasized the significance of privacy issues in the acceptance and use of AI-driven technologies, such as chatbots (Cheng & Jiang, 2020; de Cosmo et al., 2021). This study’s findings indicate that privacy concerns pose a substantial obstacle to ChatGPT adoption. However, this contradicts earlier studies on the privacy paradox, which suggest that individuals may",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,18,"such as chatbots (Cheng & Jiang, 2020; de Cosmo et al., 2021). This study’s findings indicate that privacy concerns pose a substantial obstacle to ChatGPT adoption. However, this contradicts earlier studies on the privacy paradox, which suggest that individuals may be willing to sacrifice some degree of privacy in exchange for the benefits of using advanced tech­ nology (Hui & Lee, 2007; Vimalkumar et al., 2021). Additionally, the results show that the continuous use of ChatGPT is positively influenced by its effectiveness in facilitating learning. This suggests that ChatGPT offers interactive features and flexibility that support various forms of effective learn­ ing, such as exam preparation, dynamic content adaptation based on a student’s progress, and personalized feedback. When using ChatGPT for information retrieval and task completion, users may perceive clear advantages, particularly when privacy risks are not a concern. These findings indicate that students are mindful of privacy risks when using AI tools for educational purposes. Ultimately, ChatGPT can serve as a “guardian angel” by helping students engage with educational activities in an innovative and efficient manner. 5.1 Theoretical implications",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,18,"findings indicate that students are mindful of privacy risks when using AI tools for educational purposes. Ultimately, ChatGPT can serve as a “guardian angel” by helping students engage with educational activities in an innovative and efficient manner. 5.1 Theoretical implications Although the TAM and UTAUT models measure technology adoption and user per­ ception related to perceived usefulness and ease of use - factors that influence usage intention - they did not examine how these factors influence users’ perceived value and lead to continuous use. While perceived usefulness and ease of use are gen­ eral measures, AI technologies require more precise evaluations of how the systems respond to users’ needs, such as helping students answer questions, brainstorming, learning, and delivering high-quality results. Many AI systems share similar func­ tionalities, yet students gravitate toward those offering higher information quality and better outcomes. Previous models typically assess the direct relationship between performance expectancy and ease of use and their relationship with intention and usage, but few have explored how perceived value mediates this relationship and continuous use intention. Therefore, this study is one",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,18,"Previous models typically assess the direct relationship between performance expectancy and ease of use and their relationship with intention and usage, but few have explored how perceived value mediates this relationship and continuous use intention. Therefore, this study is one of the initial investigations into how perceived usefulness and ease of use impact perceived value, which in turn influences continu­ ous use intention. The findings highlight the mediating role of perceived value. 1 3 22460",75
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,19,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 Furthermore, the newly developed AIA-UM model examines how problem- solving, idea generation, and information quality predict perceived value and the intention to continue using ChatGPT or similar AI technologies. Alyhough ChatGPT shares core features with other AI tools - such as ease of use and perceived useful­ ness, which influence usage intention - its broader functionality introduces additional dimensions. Specifically, capabilities related to problem-solving, idea generation, and information quality, are particularly important for students’ academic learning. The AIA-UM model addresses this gap by offering a comprehensive and adaptable framework for researchers across various contexts, enabling more affective measure­ ment of multiple aspects of technology use and their contribution to perceived user value. 5.2 Practical implications The use of ChatGPT and other AI tools among students and academic staff is increas­ ing. Given its perceived value as a beneficial learning tool, academic institutions should consider integrating ChatGPT into educational practices. Institutions should adopt a student-centric approach when developing and implementing AI-driven tools, taking into account students’ preferences, needs, and concerns to foster an inclusive and",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,19,"value as a beneficial learning tool, academic institutions should consider integrating ChatGPT into educational practices. Institutions should adopt a student-centric approach when developing and implementing AI-driven tools, taking into account students’ preferences, needs, and concerns to foster an inclusive and supportive learning environment. One effective strategy is launching awareness campaigns for students and faculty to dispel myths about AI tools, encour­ age open discussions about their benefits, and reduce social pressures associated with their use in academic settings. Additionally, while privacy concerns have not significantly hindered ChatGPT adoption, institutions should still prioritize privacy education to help students make informed choices. Privacy awareness programs can help students gain a balanced understanding of both the benefits and risks of AI tools. Finally, institutions should promote the ethical use of AI in education, emphasizing responsible development, deployment, and usage. Establishing ethical guidelines and frameworks can support the positive and responsible adoption of AI in academic settings. Developers should also enhance ChatGPT’s functionality to address limita­ tions that hinder effective learning by incorporating interactive features, personalized feedback, and dynamic material modifications. 5.3 Limitations and",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,19,"frameworks can support the positive and responsible adoption of AI in academic settings. Developers should also enhance ChatGPT’s functionality to address limita­ tions that hinder effective learning by incorporating interactive features, personalized feedback, and dynamic material modifications. 5.3 Limitations and future research The first limitation of this study is the sample size. Some students may be reluc­ tant to admit their use of ChatGPT or other AI tools for studying, especially given that some universities in different countries initially prohibited their use. As a result, despite distributing 600 questionnaires, only 244 were returned, which may limit the representativeness of the sample. A key factor influencing the continuous use of ChatGPT in this study is the diversity of students from different institutions in Malaysia. Therefore, we suggest that future research consider both instructors and students when exploring the incorporation of AI technologies (such as ChatGPT) into educational and instructional practices. Additionally, this study was conducted exclusively in Malaysia, which may limit the generalizability of the findings to other contexts. Future research should examine the factors influencing ChatGPT adoption 1 3 22461",180
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,19,"technologies (such as ChatGPT) into educational and instructional practices. Additionally, this study was conducted exclusively in Malaysia, which may limit the generalizability of the findings to other contexts. Future research should examine the factors influencing ChatGPT adoption 1 3 22461",40
A120,A120.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13662-x,20,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:22443–22470 in different cultural contexts. Lastly, the study relied on self-reported data from par­ ticipants, which may be subject to social desirability bias, leading to overreporting of favorable behaviors or attitudes. 6 Appendix A Title Authors, Year Purpose/Theoretical Foundation The Extended UTAUT Framework for Chatbot Technology Kang et al. (2022) Extended the UTAUT model to measure chatbot use in the academic sector, examining the impact of habit, price value, and trust on user satisfaction and behavioral intention. Towards Explainable AI in Education: A Study on Learn­ ing Analytics Dashboards Li et al. (2023) Utilized the TAM model to evaluate instructors’ accep­ tance of learning analytics dashboards, examining how perceived usefulness and ease of use influence trust in the system and intention to use. Technology Acceptance of AI Tutors in Educational Institutions Lee et al. (2023) Utilized the UTAUT model to measure the impact of performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and facilitat­ ing conditions on usage intention, with gender, age, and voluntariness as moderating factors. Student Engagement with ChatGPT in Coursework Smith & Ali (2023) Utilized the",180
