A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"ChatGPT ensures that students have inclusive accessibility in higher education, supporting communication across diverse learning styles13,22. It guarantees broader engagement among students, enhancing academic achievement23. Academic success is vital in the constantly evolving landscape of higher education, where students often face hectic schedules and varying time zones. The easy accessibility of ChatGPT makes it an invaluable resource for students seeking assistance beyond standard university hours24. With ChatGPT, students can interact at their convenience, fostering a culture of self-directed learning and equipping them with the tools to take charge of their educational journeys25. ChatGPT is a helpful addition to free up teachers’ time and energy for higher-order learning objectives and mentorship, giving prompt feedback11. Teachers can focus on fostering students’ critical thinking and creativity13. Furthermore, ChatGPT can help with automatic feedback and grading common assignments through the cooperative synergy between instructors and ChatGPT11. Educating students to meet future workforce expectations is crucial in an era of fast technology breakthroughs26. Higher education students use ChatGPT to navigate and utilize AI technology with more excellent proficiency27 to critically assess and use technology for",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"and ChatGPT11. Educating students to meet future workforce expectations is crucial in an era of fast technology breakthroughs26. Higher education students use ChatGPT to navigate and utilize AI technology with more excellent proficiency27 to critically assess and use technology for both academic and professional goals11. Incorporating AI and data-driven insights into various disciplines is becoming more critical in higher education28. According to Ullah et al.29, using ChatGPT positively and significantly impacts decision-making, further enhancing financial literacy and promoting market trends. Given its importance, there is a critical need to develop procedures and policies for training to support the effective practice of ChatGPT in university settings30. Limited literature exists on the limitations of ChatGPT, which operates within specific parameters that can lead to bias. Based on large datasets sourced from the internet, ChatGPT may produce inherent biases in its results31. Ethically, ChatGPT can pose problems, such as concerns regarding users’ data privacy. It lacks a moral and legal accountability mechanism when using decision-making tools32. ChatGPT presents inconsistencies and inaccuracies as it generates knowledge hallucinations with incorrect facts33. Furthermore, according to Petrič34,",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"ChatGPT can pose problems, such as concerns regarding users’ data privacy. It lacks a moral and legal accountability mechanism when using decision-making tools32. ChatGPT presents inconsistencies and inaccuracies as it generates knowledge hallucinations with incorrect facts33. Furthermore, according to Petrič34, excessive use of ChatGPT impacts social communication and socialization due to the lack of common benchmarks for efficiency and effectiveness. After discussing both the merits and demerits of ChatGPT, there is a pressing need to examine its impacts on students’ academic performance in university classrooms. ChatGPT has the capabilities and facilitates students’ access to learning and information11, but a gap still exists regarding students’ academic performance35. Rasul et al.36 indicated the benefits and risks linked with using ChatGPT in higher education. Another study37 explained its effectiveness in students’ learning compared to traditional methods, but the implications of ChatGPT on academic performance remain unresolved38. In justifying the study, research indicates that higher education institutions in Pakistan encounter challenges such as resource scarcity, student-faculty ratios, and limited access to quality materials, leading to the reliance on ChatGPT39. Many students struggle to enhance",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"performance remain unresolved38. In justifying the study, research indicates that higher education institutions in Pakistan encounter challenges such as resource scarcity, student-faculty ratios, and limited access to quality materials, leading to the reliance on ChatGPT39. Many students struggle to enhance their learning through traditional rote methods. Instead, they turn to ChatGPT for inquiry-based learning, as it provides a foundation for easier reading and understanding40. The current study is designed to fill the gap and evaluate the effects of ChatGPT on the academic performance of higher education students. The main objective of the study is to examine how students use ChatGPT and its effect on academic performance in higher education classrooms. Theoretical framework and hypotheses of the study This study examines students’ intentions to use ChatGPT in their learning at universities in Pakistan. Furthermore, it investigates whether students’ use of ChatGPT positively impacts their academic performance. The study applied the TAM model41 as a theoretical framework to examine students’ behavioral intentions regarding using ChatGPT at higher education institutions in Pakistan. The following variables, presented as research hypotheses, were studied. Performance expectation",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"positively impacts their academic performance. The study applied the TAM model41 as a theoretical framework to examine students’ behavioral intentions regarding using ChatGPT at higher education institutions in Pakistan. The following variables, presented as research hypotheses, were studied. Performance expectation Within the TAM model, performance expectation is fundamental for the students to perform better42,43. In an educational setting, students like to use ChatGPT and other technologies to improve their learning8,44. According to Abuhassna et al.45, students utilize ChatGPT for academic achievement. Because higher expectations for performance make possible immediate concept clarification, individualized feedback, and problem-solving skills46. 1. H1. Performance expectancy has a positive effect on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Effort expectation It is an important aspect of TAM, and it opines the use of specific technology like ChatGPT47. According to PHAN et al.48, users’ behavioral intention to embrace technology is significantly anticipated by effort expectation49. ChatGPT’s usability in the classroom substantially impacts the study routine50. 2. H1. Effort expectancy has a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Social influence Conceptually derived from social psychology, social influence describes",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,2,"is significantly anticipated by effort expectation49. ChatGPT’s usability in the classroom substantially impacts the study routine50. 2. H1. Effort expectancy has a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Social influence Conceptually derived from social psychology, social influence describes how other people’s actions, beliefs, and behaviors affect a person’s ability to make decisions. Social impact plays a vital role in the adoption of technology51. Social conventions, cultural expectations, and the general educational culture help the adoption of ChatGPT and other similar technologies52. Social influence is crucial for successfully integrating ChatGPT and related technologies in educational contexts to change in the digital era50. 3. H1. Social influence has a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:16434 2 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",126
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,3,"Facilitation conditions Technical assistance, training opportunities, resource accessibility, and institutional policies are examples of facilitation conditions in the context of educational technology adoption53. Planning for integrating technology into the classroom requires understanding the factors that affect students’ behavioral intentions to utilize ChatGPT54. 4. H1. Facilitation conditions have a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Hedonic motivation Hedonic motivation is relevant to technology acceptance because it shapes students’ attitudes and perceptions of the tool, influencing their behavior55. ChatGPT facilitates positive hedonic motivation among students56. It incorporates gamification into ChatGPT in the educational setting to support a dynamic learning environment57. Creating an engaging learning environment incorporates challenges, interactive situations, and quizzes into ChatGPT, increasing students’ hedonic motivation and behavioral intention through technology58. 5. H1. Hedonic motivation has a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Price value Price value includes both the actual and perceived values, which include time, effort, and attention, in addition to the financial cost. Students’ behavioral intentions toward ChatGPT balance the concrete and intangible costs and advantages. Students use ChatGPT to analyze cost-benefit, weighing the",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,3,"includes both the actual and perceived values, which include time, effort, and attention, in addition to the financial cost. Students’ behavioral intentions toward ChatGPT balance the concrete and intangible costs and advantages. Students use ChatGPT to analyze cost-benefit, weighing the actual expenses and anticipated advantages59. ChatGPT provides more excellent learning results that are more efficient and convenient for positive behavioral intentions60. Students’ behavioral intentions are influenced by the cost of online learning technology to enable interactive learning61. 6. H1. Price value has a positive impact on students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Habitual usage Habitual usage, which plays a crucial and consistent role in technology adoption, is a repeated activity until it achieves an automatic and entrenched status62. Understanding behavioral intention to utilize ChatGPT is improved by looking at cognitive and behavioral processes63. Students often become accustomed to using ChatGPT to ask questions and incorporate technological learning64. Students continue using ChatGPT to take advantage of its automaticity, which grants habituation65. 7. H1: Habit has a positive impacts students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Behavioral attention Educational technology influences actual behaviors by interacting",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,3,"ask questions and incorporate technological learning64. Students continue using ChatGPT to take advantage of its automaticity, which grants habituation65. 7. H1: Habit has a positive impacts students’ intention to use ChatGPT. Behavioral attention Educational technology influences actual behaviors by interacting with students’ intention to use ChatGPT66. True application of technology to characterize students’ readiness determines behavioral intention67. Students’ ideas are influenced by their intention to use and integrate ChatGPT into learning activities as a helpful tool60,68. 8. H1. Students’ intention to use ChatGPT in their learning has a positive impact on their actual behaviors. Academic performance Flexible learning, problem-solving strategies, and critical thinking show that the practical use of ChatGPT in coursework has a positive impact on academic achievement13. Academic success is affected by better communication, immediate feedback, cooperative learning, and a continuous learning environment69. With ChatGPT, students may interactively look for information, answers, and clarifications70 to enhance their academic performance with instant access to pertinent information71. Using ChatGPT in learning activities improves abilities such as problem-solving and critical thinking72 through interactive conversations and challenging issues73, thereby promoting a deeper",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,3,"look for information, answers, and clarifications70 to enhance their academic performance with instant access to pertinent information71. Using ChatGPT in learning activities improves abilities such as problem-solving and critical thinking72 through interactive conversations and challenging issues73, thereby promoting a deeper understanding of the subject matter74. Thus, it encourages learners’ understanding and analytical thinking impact on academic achievement as students actively employ ChatGPT for problem-solving65. 9. H1. Students’ actual behavior of using ChatGPT in their learning positively impacts their academic perfor­ mance. Alshahrani50 highlights the active use of ChatGPT to enhance academic achievement. The efficiency in obtaining pertinent information through ChatGPT for research, studying, and information retrieval enhances learning experiences75. Real-world actions related to ChatGPT are crucial for overcoming learning obstacles76, allowing access to educational materials, addressing language issues, and aiding in articulating ideas77. Leveraging ChatGPT to boost academic performance ensures information availability and creates more equitable opportunities in the classroom78. The incorporation of ChatGPT in coursework positively influences academic achievement65,79. Utilizing ChatGPT in educational activities supports information retrieval, flexible learning techniques, improved problem-solving, and critical thinking69. Furthermore, it promotes collaborative",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,3,"information availability and creates more equitable opportunities in the classroom78. The incorporation of ChatGPT in coursework positively influences academic achievement65,79. Utilizing ChatGPT in educational activities supports information retrieval, flexible learning techniques, improved problem-solving, and critical thinking69. Furthermore, it promotes collaborative learning, sustained engagement in the learning process, improved communication, overcoming learning challenges, and feedback loops, all of which benefit academic success80. Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:16434 3 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",70
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"Research methodology The study adopted a quantitative descriptive approach for collecting information. Online survey using Google Forms was used to collect the data for the study from 352 higher education students across Pakistan from November 2023 to January 2024. The questionnaire was divided into two parts. The first part gathered detailed data about students’ demographic information, while the second part collected scientific data on their experiences using a five-point Likert Scale that ranged from ‘Strongly Disagree’ to ‘Strongly Agree’. Performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, facilitation conditions, price value, hedonic motivation, habit, behavioral intention, and actual use of behavior were included as independent variables in the questionnaire. However, students’ academic performance was identified as a dependent variable. The selection of variables and indicators from TAM followed the approach of Foroughi et al.81. The collected data was analyzed statistically using the PLS-SEM (Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling) with SmartPLS4, which is a vital tool for examining key predictors of the variable outcomes. Compared to CB-SEM, PLS-SEM provides higher item loadings, average variance extraction, and composite reliability. PLS-SEM consistently employs a",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"PLS-SEM (Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling) with SmartPLS4, which is a vital tool for examining key predictors of the variable outcomes. Compared to CB-SEM, PLS-SEM provides higher item loadings, average variance extraction, and composite reliability. PLS-SEM consistently employs a confirmatory approach instead of an exploratory one82. For developing the model, a reflective construct was followed in the format outlined by Ma et al.83. The study presented hypotheses within a theoretical framework supported by literature, which is echoed in the current study84. For data analysis, the format utilized by Ashraf et al.85 was followed. In the study, the measurement model assessed factor loadings, Cronbach’s alpha, Composite Reliability (CR), Average Variance Extraction (AVE), Variance Inflation Factors (VIF), Mean, and Standard Deviation (SD). The Fornell-Larcker Criterion was employed to determine discriminant validity. The structural model was implemented to find the effects of ChatGPT on academic performance, noting differences based on demographic information. Participants Table 1 displays the participants’ demographic information. Among the 352 participants, 167 (47.4%) were male, and 185 (52.6%) were female. The majority of the participants were undergraduate (BS) students,",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"ChatGPT on academic performance, noting differences based on demographic information. Participants Table 1 displays the participants’ demographic information. Among the 352 participants, 167 (47.4%) were male, and 185 (52.6%) were female. The majority of the participants were undergraduate (BS) students, with most falling within the 18–24 age group (296, 84.1%), while the remaining participants were enrolled in master’s and doctoral programs. Regarding family residence, 194 participants (55.1%) were from urban areas, while 158 (44.9%) were from rural areas. Participants at both public and private universities took part in the study, with 225 (63.9%) from public institutions and 127 (36.1%) from private institutions. The majority of the participants were from Khyber Pakhtunkhwa (139) and Punjab (136), with the rest belonging to other provinces of Pakistan. Further details about their experiences with online courses and the use of smart technologies are provided in Table 1. Ethical consideration Proper ethical standards were maintained throughout the study, in accordance with the guidelines provided by the National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects of Biomedical and Behavioral Research (1978). All participants received comprehensive information",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"in Table 1. Ethical consideration Proper ethical standards were maintained throughout the study, in accordance with the guidelines provided by the National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects of Biomedical and Behavioral Research (1978). All participants received comprehensive information regarding the study’s goals, the voluntary nature of their involvement, and their right to withdraw at any moment without any consequences. A formal consent form was provided, ensuring that participants had all the necessary information and willingly agreed to participate in the study. To protect participants’ privacy and confidentiality, pseudonyms were used instead of their real names. Additionally, both male and female participants were included and treated with consideration for their individual needs throughout the research process. The Ethical Committee at the University of Wah, Pakistan granted ethical approval for this study. The research complied with all applicable ethical guidelines and regulations concerning studies involving human participants86. Findings of the study SmartPLS 4.0 used a PLS-SEM to analyze the data87. Figure 1, in the theoretical framework, presents all the indicators and variables in the study. Multigroup analysis, measurement analysis, a",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"and regulations concerning studies involving human participants86. Findings of the study SmartPLS 4.0 used a PLS-SEM to analyze the data87. Figure 1, in the theoretical framework, presents all the indicators and variables in the study. Multigroup analysis, measurement analysis, a structural model with internal consistency reliability, discriminant validity, and convergent validity were all followed85. Table 2 presents an overview of the internal consistency reliability and convergent validity. All indicators and constructs were developed to follow the reflective measurement criteria. The discriminant validity of the constructs is shown by the Fornell-Larcker Criterion in Table 3 and the Heterotrait-Monotrait Ratio in Table 4. The convergent validity and internal consistency reliability are presented in the measurement model, as shown in Table 2. The reflective measurement83 criteria42,83 were followed in the development of all the indicators and constructs. The foundation of each indicator and construct was to follow the reflective measuring criteria. The indicators’ reliability is highlighted as (Factor Loadings > 0.726) at the minimum level for outer loadings. The Convergent Validity via Average Variance Extracted (AVE) is (AVE > 0.586) at the minimum",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"construct was to follow the reflective measuring criteria. The indicators’ reliability is highlighted as (Factor Loadings > 0.726) at the minimum level for outer loadings. The Convergent Validity via Average Variance Extracted (AVE) is (AVE > 0.586) at the minimum level. Composite Reliability (CR) is used to ensure Internal Consistency (CR > 0.790), which exceeds the required threshold value. The reliability indices for each construct are represented by rho-A at the minimum level (rho-A > 0.776). Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) assesses the collinearity of the items to measure the achieved criteria. Discriminant validity differentiates a construct from its counterparts, which determines the proposed discriminant validity and exceeds all the other values in rows and columns88. The diagonal values are more significant than their counterparts, as shown in Table 3, confirming the discriminant validity. The format of Zuo et al.89 was followed for the discriminant validity. Fornell-Larcker Criterion was followed as it was easy and flexible in use90 and constantly found in PLS-SEM literature. Table 4 further confirming the discriminant validity via Hetrotrait-Monotrait ratio with all the values > 0.850. Table",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,4,"followed for the discriminant validity. Fornell-Larcker Criterion was followed as it was easy and flexible in use90 and constantly found in PLS-SEM literature. Table 4 further confirming the discriminant validity via Hetrotrait-Monotrait ratio with all the values > 0.850. Table 5 shows the p-value, t-value, and effect size for the structural model. Table 2 shows the collinearity of the items where the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) was less than 5, indicating that there were no collinearity difficulties88. Structural model in Table 5; Fig. 2 is highlighted with subconstructs through the major factor of Scientific Reports | (2025) 15:16434 4 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports/",102
