A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,8,"behavioral intention effects on academic performance. The highly significant effect is of actual behavior on academic performance as (β = 0.558, t value = 9.571, p values = 0.000, p < .01). The effect of behavioral intention on academic performance is highly significant as (β = 0.648, t value = 16.293, p = .000, p < .05). Behavioral intention also has significant effect on actual behaviors as (β = 0.693, t value = 19.285, p = .000, p < .01). Habit also has a significant effect on academic performance as (β = 0.156, t value = 3.738, p 0.000, p < .01). Habit also has significant effect on actual behavior with the statistics (β = 0.167, t value = 3.723, p = .000, p < .01). Table 5 highlights the effect of habit on behavioral intention as (β = 0.240, t value = 3.779, p = .000, p < .01). The effect of hedonic motivation on academic performance is presented as (β = 0.193, t value = 4.244, p = .000, p < .01) which shows the result as highly",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,8,"t value = 3.779, p = .000, p < .01). The effect of hedonic motivation on academic performance is presented as (β = 0.193, t value = 4.244, p = .000, p < .01) which shows the result as highly significant. Hedonic motivation also has a significant effect on actual behavior as (β = 0.206, t value = 4.288, p = .000, p < .01). While the effect of hedonic motivation on behavioral intention is also highlighted as significant with the statistic (β = 0.297, t value = 4.763, p = .000, p < .01). The rest of the items have no significant effects on students’ academic performance. Table 6 highlights differences in participants’ views in relation to their demographic information, gender, age, background as urban or rural, education level, discipline, and university as public or private. The statistics in the table highlight a mild difference in relation to education (p = .059), and no significant difference was found (p > .05) in all the factors. Discussion The study utilized the TAM model and PLS-SEM to investigate the impact of",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,8,"the table highlight a mild difference in relation to education (p = .059), and no significant difference was found (p > .05) in all the factors. Discussion The study utilized the TAM model and PLS-SEM to investigate the impact of ChatGPT on students’ academic performance in higher education. The measurement model presented in Table 2 was used for developing all indicators and constructs. After confirming factor loadings, alpha reliability, composite reliability (CR), average variance extraction (AVE), variance inflation factor (VIF), mean, and standard deviation (SD), the study conducted the Forner-Larcker criterion analysis in Tables 3 and 4 using PLS-SEM. The key indicators in the study are performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, facilitation condition, price value, habit, and hedonic motivation, as highlighted in Fig. 1. Table 5 shows the impact of all these indicators on students’ intention in using ChatGPT, which influences their actual behaviors and academic performance. As per the results of this study, behavioral intention greatly influences students’ actual behavior regarding their usage of ChatGPT as a learning source60. According to Saif et al.91 students’ actual behavior is",180
A122,A122.pdf,10.1038/s41598-025-92625-1,8,"which influences their actual behaviors and academic performance. As per the results of this study, behavioral intention greatly influences students’ actual behavior regarding their usage of ChatGPT as a learning source60. According to Saif et al.91 students’ actual behavior is primarily influenced by their perceived utility of specific actions. Regarding ChatGPT usage, students’ learning activities grow as they believe it is a helpful tool that improves their learning experience and find it simple to use. The results also highlight that students’ actual behavior regarding ChatGPT usage positively and significantly Actual behavior Academic performance Behavioral intention Effort expectancy Facilitation condition Hedonic motivation Habit Performance expectancy Social influence Price value Actual behavior Academic performance 0.847 Behavioral intention 0.833 0.761 Effort expectancy 0.555 0.543 0.522 Facilitation condition 0.681 0.607 0.579 0.688 Hedonic motivation 0.840 0.788 0.733 0.643 0.698 Habit 0.728 0.626 0.677 0.483 0.673 0.703 Performance expectancy 0.527 0.539 0.550 0.752 0.600 0.677 0.483 Social influence 0.628 0.602 0.631 0.746 0.783 0.656 0.572 0.685 Price value 0.653 0.566 0.611 0.518 0.660 0.684 0.670 0.508 0.663 Table 4. Hetrotrait-Monotrait ratio (HTMT). Academic performance Actual",180
