A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"opinions (n = 2). Students also identified academic integrity and uni­ versity policy as limitations to its use in their studies (n = 8), and po­ tential negative impacts upon their own learning (n = 3). 3.3.2. Intentions to use ChatGPT Students within this cluster were most likely to use ChatGPT for their academic studies (n = 26). Intended uses included strategies associated with monitoring their understanding of content such as generating alternate explanations for complex or unfamiliar content (n = 8) and checking their own responses to assigned tasks (n = 2). Students also monitored their progress through writing support strategies focused on seeking feedback on grammatical features of their own writing (n = 2), or general writing support (n = 1). Students also intended to use ChatGPT for accessing or organizing learning content including sum­ marizing readings or subject materials (n = 5) or locating relevant sources or information (n = 3). Other intended uses included generating initial ideas (n = 3) or drafts (n = 2) for their own writing, formatting a reference list (n = 1),",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"subject materials (n = 5) or locating relevant sources or information (n = 3). Other intended uses included generating initial ideas (n = 3) or drafts (n = 2) for their own writing, formatting a reference list (n = 1), asking questions (n = 2), or generating or debugging code (n = 6). Students’ intentions to use it show the potential for using ChatGPT in productive ways. There were no reports of using ChatGPT to bypass learning, although, this is not to say that the students within this study may not have also used ChatGPT in ways that were less productive. Students within this cluster who did not intend to use ChatGPT (n = 15) expressed their perspective that uses of ChatGPT was not germane to their academic studies (n = 4), would breach academic misconduct (n = 3), limit their learning (n = 1) or was unethical (n = 1). Six students (n = 6) cited no reason. 3.4. Cautious users Cautious users also reported high levels of digital literacy, but unlike the enthusiastic users this cluster reported low",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"their learning (n = 1) or was unethical (n = 1). Six students (n = 6) cited no reason. 3.4. Cautious users Cautious users also reported high levels of digital literacy, but unlike the enthusiastic users this cluster reported low AI literacy. Cautious users described more limitations than benefits associated with ChatGPT underscored by a general caution with using ChatGPT. 3.4.1. Knowledge of ChatGPT Just over half of students within this cluster did not identify any benefits associated with the use of ChatGPT in their studies (n = 41). This included students who perceived no benefits (n = 26), who did not know of any benefits (n = 13), and two who did not respond (n = 2). The remaining 31 students described benefits but with less certainty than enthusiastic users highlighting their lack of knowledge. For example: “I see it as a good way to create questions on content, but other than that I am not sure of its ability as I haven’t had much exposure to it yet, but I am keen to learn.” These benefits included generating",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"it as a good way to create questions on content, but other than that I am not sure of its ability as I haven’t had much exposure to it yet, but I am keen to learn.” These benefits included generating alternate expla­ nations (n = 5), feedback or advice on structuring essays or writing (n = 5), getting feedback on their own writing (n = 4), generating ideas on a topic to inform their own research (n = 4), or generating summaries (n = 2). The limitations reported highlight the cautious approach to using ChatGPT in their academic studies. 27 students identified the outputs generated by ChatGPT were typically unreliable or could not be trusted, for example, “not necessarily reliable, don’t know where the informa­ tion comes from or exactly how it generates answers” and “I don’t trust it for research”. Other limitations related to its non-use were concerns around academic misconduct policies (n = 9), and concerns that its use may limit their own learning (n = 7). Other concerns about the func­ tionality of ChatGPT included low-quality or",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"research”. Other limitations related to its non-use were concerns around academic misconduct policies (n = 9), and concerns that its use may limit their own learning (n = 7). Other concerns about the func­ tionality of ChatGPT included low-quality or robotic generated text (n = 6), general descriptions of ChatGPT’s limited functions (n = 5), limita­ tions in generating responses relevant for academic studies (n = 5), and concerns about the lack of transparency of how ChatGPT works (n = 3). Seven students provided no response, and 13 more were not sure of any limitations. 3.4.2. Intentions to use ChatGPT This cluster had the highest proportion of students who indicated they did not intend to use ChatGPT in their studies in the current se­ mester (n = 63). Only four (n = 4) students in this cluster intended to use ChatGPT to work through questions assigned for their classwork (n = 1), to generate ideas for further research on a topic (n = 1), to generate a draft (n = 1) and to seek alternate explanations of unfamiliar topics (1).",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"ChatGPT to work through questions assigned for their classwork (n = 1), to generate ideas for further research on a topic (n = 1), to generate a draft (n = 1) and to seek alternate explanations of unfamiliar topics (1). For the students who were unsure or did not intend to use ChatGPT, some also acknowledged that they had no experience using ChatGPT or did not know about it (n = 21). Students also explained that they perceived no benefit (n = 16) or were concerned about risks associated with the university’s academic integrity policy (n = 6) and thus reported not to use it. It was evident that a majority of students within this cluster had little to no experience using ChatGPT that likely shaped their de­ cision not to use it for their studies. 4. Discussion Digital divides have long been examined in education technology and public policy research. This body of research describes: i) the nature of digital divides as experienced by individuals, and ii) contributing factors (Lythreatis et al., 2022). In this exploratory study, we focus",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"have long been examined in education technology and public policy research. This body of research describes: i) the nature of digital divides as experienced by individuals, and ii) contributing factors (Lythreatis et al., 2022). In this exploratory study, we focus on the former, adopting a person-centred approach. The study results represent an important snapshot in time that depicts variation across different profiles of students’ use and knowledge of GenAI. While the scope of the study findings is not intended to be generalised, the pro­ ceeding discussion is intended to prompt critical discourse on the im­ plications of an emerging divide between those who have access and skills to benefit from GenAI and those who do not. At a time of great interest in understanding the impacts of GenAI, we emphasise the imperative role of educational institutions in bridging, rather than exacerbating, these emerging disparities. Before discussing these findings, it is important to return to the risk- oriented context of higher education institutions’ policies and manage­ ment of applications of GenAI in which the study was conducted. Much of the initial",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"exacerbating, these emerging disparities. Before discussing these findings, it is important to return to the risk- oriented context of higher education institutions’ policies and manage­ ment of applications of GenAI in which the study was conducted. Much of the initial rhetoric was focused on the potential of students using GenAI to cheat or mitigating risks associated with assessment design (Moya et al., 2023; Playfoot et al., 2024). This focus is still evidenced in institutional responses as universities work towards ‘AI proof’ policy and practice. Yet, the findings presented in this paper show that students’ intentions to use GenAI (specifically ChatGPT) were perhaps not as widespread as the claims at the time and to some extent shaped by university policies. Research reporting on students’ intentions to use GenAI was limited at the time of the publication of this paper; and thus, the findings reported here offer a historical account of student per­ spectives at the critical diffusion phase. The aim of the study was to explore variations in students’ academic digital profiles. Specifically, examining clusters of students based on their self-reported",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"the findings reported here offer a historical account of student per­ spectives at the critical diffusion phase. The aim of the study was to explore variations in students’ academic digital profiles. Specifically, examining clusters of students based on their self-reported digital and AI literacy together with their perceptions of benefits, limitations, and intentions to use ChatGPT in their academic study. Three student profiles were identified demonstrating variation across students’ self-reported digital and AI literacies while providing qualitative insight into how and why students intended to use ChatGPT in their studies. The exploratory profiles of novice, enthusiastic and cautious users provide first of its kind empirical evidence about university students’ differential GenAI engagement during the critical diffusion phase in Australian higher education. Importantly providing early insight into the ways students’ differential AI literacy and use replicate historical digital inequalities. Notable patterns across the user profiles included variations in knowledge about ChatGPT reflective of students’ self- reported AI literacies across the three profiles. Patterns were also observed in the intended uses of ChatGPT across the three profiles with enthusiast users citing uses",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,6,"across the user profiles included variations in knowledge about ChatGPT reflective of students’ self- reported AI literacies across the three profiles. Patterns were also observed in the intended uses of ChatGPT across the three profiles with enthusiast users citing uses that could enhance their learning or pro­ ductivity, while novice users cited using ChatGPT to address challenges associated with their study or were less appropriate to incorporate into K. Beckman et al. The Internet and Higher Education 67 (2025) 101036 6",81
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"their academic study. Such patterns demonstrate that variations across digital and AI literacy of these user profiles may also be linked to uses of GenAI and differential benefits or outcomes. Finally, patterns were also observed in student perspectives of their access to ChatGPT for their academic studies. Enthusiastic users framed their perceptions of restrictive HEI policy as a limitation to their use, while the cautious and novice users were more likely to express concerns about breaching policy. The profiles show that while most students were generally acting with caution, students with higher levels of self-reported digital literacy and AI literacy were better placed to understand and use and benefit from GenAI within their studies. On the other hand, students with lower levels of self-reported digital literacy and AI literacy were largely un­ certain (and at times fearful) about the technology, and its impacts upon their learning. Like the well documented levels of the digital divide across access, capability and outcomes (Carter et al., 2020; Lythreatis et al., 2022), the distinct and varied profiles from this small sample of students illustrate",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"and its impacts upon their learning. Like the well documented levels of the digital divide across access, capability and outcomes (Carter et al., 2020; Lythreatis et al., 2022), the distinct and varied profiles from this small sample of students illustrate similar patterns of access, understanding and intention to use and possibly benefit from GenAI in academic studies. Importantly, the stu­ dent profiles support early assertions of scholars who highlight the ways that digital innovations perpetuate and extend to existing digital divides (Carter et al., 2020). Likewise, the profiles align with early studies of university students’ perceived willingness to use GenAI and positive correlations between knowledge of AI and frequency of use (Chan & Hu, 2023). Significantly these patterns reflect the nature of existing capa­ bility divides and as the digital divide literature shows, this exacerbation may have differential flow-on effects to non-digital outcomes, such as learning (Corrin et al., 2019, United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO), 2023). This student-centered understanding is critical in institutional re­ sponses to digital innovation. While the findings presented here show that higher levels",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"to non-digital outcomes, such as learning (Corrin et al., 2019, United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organisation (UNESCO), 2023). This student-centered understanding is critical in institutional re­ sponses to digital innovation. While the findings presented here show that higher levels of students’ self-reported digital literacy did not necessarily result in early adoption of technologies, they do illustrate the differential impacts of a risk discourse generated by the institutional policy ban on GenAI on students. In practice, this may occur as ‘enthusiastic’ students are better positioned to leverage their existing access and capabilities to benefit from GenAI in their studies despite institutional policy, while ‘cautious’ and ‘novice’ students may experi­ ence compound disadvantage via the risk discourse emerging from institutional uncertainty and/or differential access and capabilities to harness the benefits of GenAI in their studies. 4.1. Implications Higher education has a critical role to play in supporting all students to harness the benefits of GenAI technologies and ensuring a more even playing field. To achieve this means moving beyond a discourse of risk to student-centered institutional approaches (Dawson et al., 2023;",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"critical role to play in supporting all students to harness the benefits of GenAI technologies and ensuring a more even playing field. To achieve this means moving beyond a discourse of risk to student-centered institutional approaches (Dawson et al., 2023; Moya et al., 2023). Considering the body of research on digital literacy and divides (see for example Kitsara, 2022; Lythreatis et al., 2022) and the digital academic profiles presented in this study, Table 3 conceptualizes the ways university students may experience AI inequalities through their university study across three levels. We offer this conceptual framework to inform future, focused, student-centered approaches to higher education policy, practice, and research, with an emphasis on: • Equity in access, through the provision of high-quality, safe, and ethical GenAI applications for university study; • A systematic focus on capability development, promoting under­ standing of both the benefits and limitations of GenAI as a core component of academic integrity, and integrating AI literacies alongside digital literacies in teaching, learning, and assessment across academic programs; and • Targeted opportunities for critical reflection and evaluation of the",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"both the benefits and limitations of GenAI as a core component of academic integrity, and integrating AI literacies alongside digital literacies in teaching, learning, and assessment across academic programs; and • Targeted opportunities for critical reflection and evaluation of the outcomes of AI technology use in university study, everyday life, and future work. 4.2. Limitations It is important to acknowledge the limitations of this study. Firstly, the data was collected at a pivotal period during the first teaching ses­ sion after ChatGPT had been widely released and at a time when the initial institutional response was to ban its use within HEI. Due to this ban, student demographic data was not collected to ensure students were comfortable in participating in the study and that students using ChatGPT against policy could not be identified in accordance with ethics approval. We acknowledge this restrictive approach may have impacted students’ comfort in reporting on their true intentions to deploy GenAI in their study. However, the anonymity of the survey and the diverse and rich open-ended responses suggest that student reports depict an honest",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"this restrictive approach may have impacted students’ comfort in reporting on their true intentions to deploy GenAI in their study. However, the anonymity of the survey and the diverse and rich open-ended responses suggest that student reports depict an honest account of intentions. In addition, the mixed method survey provided the most appropriate format in which to collect timely data capturing students digital/AI literacy and intentions at a historically significant moment. We also recognize that using self-reported measures lacks an objective assessment of both students’ prior exposure to GenAI, and a genuine measure of the AI literacy at a time when students had no reference frameworks or institutional guidance. Further, the self- reported digital and AI literacy measures used serve as a preliminary step in this area of research. The exploratory profiles presented were generated from cluster analysis to reveal patterns within the data. While cluster analysis has limitations associated with difficulty in determining whether clustering has produced meaningful results, the point in time clusters and open- ended responses illustrate the ways that the diffusion of AI technolo­ gies in",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"patterns within the data. While cluster analysis has limitations associated with difficulty in determining whether clustering has produced meaningful results, the point in time clusters and open- ended responses illustrate the ways that the diffusion of AI technolo­ gies in HE aligns with established scholarly understandings of digital divides. As Dalmaijer et al. (2022) suggest, the best pragmatic approach is to investigate when cluster analysis can or cannot help to identify subgroups in a dataset. We invite future research that explores a range of individual, contextual, institutional and demographic factors in rela­ tionship with the patterns of digital and AI literacy identified in the cluster structures presented this study. Table 3 Conceptualization of GenAI use across three levels of the digital divide (adapted from Carter et al., 2020, italicized text shows links to student cluster profiles in this study). Level Ways students may experience GenAI inequalities Access divide ● Personal access to GenAI including quality of application. e.g. free vs paid subscriptions ● Student access to GenAI including availability of quality access through institutional access, policy and culture ● Personal",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,"Ways students may experience GenAI inequalities Access divide ● Personal access to GenAI including quality of application. e.g. free vs paid subscriptions ● Student access to GenAI including availability of quality access through institutional access, policy and culture ● Personal motivation to access or not associated with value, perceptions, policy settings, articulated connections to future work and lives Capability divide ● Student understanding of the nature of GenAI ● Student understanding and use of GenAI ● Student understanding and beliefs about appropriate uses of Gen AI for different contexts/purposes ● Student capability to use GenAI tools for their study and future work. ● Student capability to connect with networks of resources to support their use of GenAI for their study and future work ● Exposure and opportunities for the contextual development of digital literacy and AI literacy with content knowledge Outcome divide ● Student translation of AI access and capabilities for benefit in university study and future work opportunities. e.g. increases in efficiency in developing understanding and outcomes of assessment results K. Beckman et al. The Internet and Higher Education",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,7,Outcome divide ● Student translation of AI access and capabilities for benefit in university study and future work opportunities. e.g. increases in efficiency in developing understanding and outcomes of assessment results K. Beckman et al. The Internet and Higher Education 67 (2025) 101036 7,44
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,8,"5. Conclusion The findings presented in this paper demonstrate patterns of AI lit­ eracy and GenAI use that reflect existing digital divides at a time of rapid technological diffusion. As data was collected during the early adoption phase of GenAI in higher education when policies and guidelines were limited, this study provides a valuable historical reference point for future evaluations of student use of technology. Through drawing on the digital divide literature, we warn how this exacerbation may have dif­ ferential flow-on effects to non-digital outcomes, such as learning, everyday life and future work. As technology continues to evolve and institutions change at a slower rate, there is ongoing need for critical discussion and empirical research that builds upon existing empirical work to inform institutional approaches to innovation that are student- centered and support students to understand and harness the use of technological innovations such as GenAI, now and in their futures. CRediT authorship contribution statement Karley Beckman: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Tiffani Apps: Conceptuali­ zation,",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,8,"use of technological innovations such as GenAI, now and in their futures. CRediT authorship contribution statement Karley Beckman: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Tiffani Apps: Conceptuali­ zation, Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Formal analysis. Sarah Katherine Howard: Writing – original draft, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. Claire Rogerson: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Investigation, Conceptualization. Ann Rogerson: Writing – review & editing, Conceptualization. Jo Tondeur: Writing – original draft, Conceptualization. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. We confirm that this manuscript has been submitted solely to The Internet and Higher Education and that it is not published, in press, or submitted elsewhere. We confirm that all the research meets the ethical guidelines, including adherence to the legal requirements of the study country. The authors acknowledge that we have seen, read, and understood the journal’s guidelines on copyright.",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,8,"in press, or submitted elsewhere. We confirm that all the research meets the ethical guidelines, including adherence to the legal requirements of the study country. The authors acknowledge that we have seen, read, and understood the journal’s guidelines on copyright. Acknowledgments None. Appendix A. Relevant survey items Background information • Year of study (checkbox). Options: 1st year of my degree; 2nd, 3rd, or 4th year of study at uni; Postgraduate study; Higher degree research study • Field of study (checkbox). Social Sciences; Natural sciences; Hu­ manities; Business Measures of digital technology and AI knowledge and use • Tell us about your use of digital technologies by responding to the following statements: Likert scale (4 = “very true of me”, 3 = “true of me”, 2 = “somewhat true of me” and 1 = “not true of me”). o I regularly use digital technologies o I have a good understanding of digital technologies o I am confident using digital technologies in my everyday life o I am confident using digital technologies (e.g. Word, Google, etc.) in my study • Tell us",180
A125,A125.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2025.101036,8,"use digital technologies o I have a good understanding of digital technologies o I am confident using digital technologies in my everyday life o I am confident using digital technologies (e.g. Word, Google, etc.) in my study • Tell us about your use of digital technologies by responding to the following statements: Likert scale (4 = “very true of me”, 3 = “true of me”, 2 = “somewhat true of me” and 1 = “not true of me”). o I regularly use AI technologies o I have a good understanding of AI technologies o I am confident using AI technologies in my everyday life o I am confident using AI technologies (e.g. Grammarly, ChatGPT, etc.) in my study. Intended use in academic study • Do you intend to use ChatGPT this session? If so, describe how you will use it. • What do you see as the benefits of using a tool like ChatGPT in your studies? • What are the limitations of us",164
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7 AI in the classroom: Exploring students’ interaction with ChatGPT in programming learning Hacer Güner1 · Erkan Er1 Received: 22 September 2024 / Accepted: 3 January 2025 / Published online: 15 January 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Abstract As being more prevalent in educational settings, understanding the impact of artifi- cial intelligence tools on student behaviors and interactions has become crucial. In this regard, this study investigates the dynamic interactions between students and ChatGPT in programming learning, focusing on how different instructional inter- ventions influence their learning and AI-interaction. Conducted over three sessions, students were allowed to use ChatGPT to complete programming tasks. The first session had no guidance, the second included hands-on training in prompt writing and effective ChatGPT use, and the third provided a lab guide with sample prompts. After each session, students took a post-test on the activity’s subject. Analyzing stu- dents’ prompting behaviors, five AI interaction profiles were identified: AI-Reliant Code Generators, AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners, AI-Collaborative Cod- ers, AI-Assisted Code Refiners, and AI-Independent Coders. These profiles were",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,1,"session, students took a post-test on the activity’s subject. Analyzing stu- dents’ prompting behaviors, five AI interaction profiles were identified: AI-Reliant Code Generators, AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners, AI-Collaborative Cod- ers, AI-Assisted Code Refiners, and AI-Independent Coders. These profiles were examined to understand their evolution across interventions and their relationship with students’ learning performance. Findings revealed significant changes in profile distribution across interventions, and a notable difference between students’ post- test scores and their AI interaction profiles. Besides, training in prompting skills and effective use of AI significantly impacted students’ interactions with AI. These insights can contribute to the knowledge of integrating generative AI tools in educa- tion, highlighting how AI can enhance teaching practices. Understanding student-AI interaction dynamics can allow educators to tailor instructional strategies for opti- mal learning. This study also underscores the importance of guidance on effective AI use and prompting skills, which can lead students to use AI more meaningfully for their learning. Keywords Student-AI interaction · ChatGPT integration · Programming education · AI in education Extended author information available on the last page of the",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,1,"AI use and prompting skills, which can lead students to use AI more meaningfully for their learning. Keywords Student-AI interaction · ChatGPT integration · Programming education · AI in education Extended author information available on the last page of the article",41
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,2,"12682 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 1 Introduction The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into educational settings has implied significant changes in teaching and learning methodologies. AI-driven tools, such as intelligent tutoring systems, educational chatbots, and personalized learning plat- forms, have shown considerable potential in reshaping the educational practices (Seo et al., 2021; Roll & Wylie, 2016). Research has demonstrated that these AI- based educational technologies can enhance student engagement by offering interac- tive and adaptive learning experiences, delivering instant and detailed feedback, and customizing instructional content to align with individual student needs and learning paces (Dai & Liu, 2024; Holmes & Tuomi, 2022). Consequently, these advance- ments may enforce educators to transform their teaching approaches to get more effi- cient and effective learning outcomes (Srinivasa et al., 2022; Zhai et al., 2021). Among all educational fields, programming education can be one of the complex domains as requiring mastery of logic, syntax, and problem-solving skills, which can vary greatly among students (Topalli & Cagiltay, 2018). Traditional program- ming teaching methods may have difficulty to meet diverse learning needs due",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,2,"can be one of the complex domains as requiring mastery of logic, syntax, and problem-solving skills, which can vary greatly among students (Topalli & Cagiltay, 2018). Traditional program- ming teaching methods may have difficulty to meet diverse learning needs due to the inherent limitations of a one-size-fits-all approach, reliance on static resources, and limited personalized feedback (Cheah, 2020; Polat & Yilmaz, 2022). On the other hand, AI-driven tools can adapt to individual student progress, providing customized exercises, tutorials, and feedback that address to each learner’s specific strengths and weaknesses (Becker et al., 2023; Silva et al., 2024). In this regard, chatbots built using large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT have emerged as powerful tools that can support learners by generating code, offering explanations, and providing personalized feedback (Lo, 2023). These AI-driven capabilities can help students to understand complex programming concepts, debug their code, and develop prob- lem-solving skills, thereby facilitating a more interactive and supportive learning environment at their own pace (Lo et al., 2024; Philbin, 2023; Wu & Yu, 2024). Previous research has revealed that AI integration can significantly",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,2,"debug their code, and develop prob- lem-solving skills, thereby facilitating a more interactive and supportive learning environment at their own pace (Lo et al., 2024; Philbin, 2023; Wu & Yu, 2024). Previous research has revealed that AI integration can significantly enhance stu- dents’ performance in programming education (Zhou, 2023). The process of learn- ing to code often involves repetitive practice, debugging, and problem-solving, which can be time-consuming and frustrating for students. AI tools, such as Chat- GPT, can streamline these processes by offering instant guidance and corrections (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023a). This immediate feedback can help students understand their mistakes in real-time, reducing frustration and accelerating the learning pro- cess (Daun & Brings, 2023). However, proper integration of AI tools in learning environments can be crucial to get effective results (Muhie & Woldie, 2020). To increase the effectiveness of AI-based learning materials, Pesovski et al. (2024) underlines the importance of designing learning materials based on distinct needs of students with different performance levels. Silva et al. (2024) suggests that pro- viding students with clear guidelines on using ChatGPT can be",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,2,"learning materials, Pesovski et al. (2024) underlines the importance of designing learning materials based on distinct needs of students with different performance levels. Silva et al. (2024) suggests that pro- viding students with clear guidelines on using ChatGPT can be beneficial to sup- port their critical-thinking and problem-solving skills during programming learning. Yilmaz and Yilmaz (2023b) emphasize the importance of gaining prompt writing skills for students and AI-literacy for teachers to benefit from ChatGPT in a more efficient and effective way.",81
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,3,"12683 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 While interacting with AI-driven tools, students can display different behaviors, and reveal distinct profiles and patterns in terms of their engagement and utiliza- tion (Stojanov et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2023). For example, in programming, some students may use AI primarily to get information without much questioning, while others might use it for improving their knowledge or conceptual understanding (Sto- janov et al., 2024). Besides, students’ interaction with AI can be influenced by their prior knowledge about the subject matter (Xia et al., 2023). Therefore, understand- ing student-AI interaction patterns can be helpful for integrating AI in educational contexts effectively by meeting individual learning styles and needs (Wang et al., 2023). Despite the growing literature on the potential of LLM-based chatbots like Chat- GPT in programming education, there remains a significant gap in understanding how to best integrate these tools to enhance learning experiences and outcomes. There is limited research on how different AI integration approaches can lead to distinct interaction profiles among students, and the impact of students’ interac- tions with",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,3,"in understanding how to best integrate these tools to enhance learning experiences and outcomes. There is limited research on how different AI integration approaches can lead to distinct interaction profiles among students, and the impact of students’ interac- tions with ChatGPT on their performance. In this context, the aim of this study is to investigate the interactions between students and ChatGPT during programming learning by analyzing the effects of different instructional interventions for ChatGPT integration on student learning and experiences. The findings of this study are antic- ipated to significantly enhance the expanding body of knowledge on the integration of gen-AI tools in educational settings. By understanding the dynamics of student- AI interaction, practitioners can more effectively tailor their teaching methods and tools to support optimal learning experiences. This study addresses the following research questions: RQ1. What are the students’ profiles while interacting with AI in programming learning? RQ2. What is the impact of different interventions of AI integration on students’ AI interaction profiles? RQ3. What is the impact of previous programming knowledge on the students’ AI interaction profiles? RQ4.",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,3,"profiles while interacting with AI in programming learning? RQ2. What is the impact of different interventions of AI integration on students’ AI interaction profiles? RQ3. What is the impact of previous programming knowledge on the students’ AI interaction profiles? RQ4. How does students’ AI interaction profiles associate with students’ perfor- mance within each intervention? 2 Background 2.1 Use of ChatGPT in programming education Early implementations of chatbots in education, such as the use of rule-based sys- tems, were primarily focused on providing automated responses to common queries. Studies demonstrated that these chatbots could reduce the workload of educators by handling routine questions, thereby allowing instructors to focus on more complex student needs (Alemdag, 2023). However, the limitations of rule-based systems, particularly their inability to handle nuanced or unexpected questions, constrained their effectiveness (Khennouche et al., 2024; Wu & Yu, 2024).",140
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,4,"12684 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 On the other hand, the advent of new generation chatbots, based on Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT) architecture and LLMs, has significantly advanced their capabilities. These chatbots, like ChatGPT, can generate human-like text based on vast amounts of data (Briganti, 2024). Additionally, they can understand and respond to a broader array of queries more accurately by using machine learning algorithms (Khennouche et al., 2024). These features enforce the idea of integrat- ing LLM-based chatbots into programming education as a potential learning tool (Adeshola & Adepoju, 2023; Haindl & Weinberger, 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023; Sun et al., 2024). For instance, ChatGPT can explain complex concepts, provide code snippets, debug errors, and offer recommendations for best practices. Its ability to engage in dynamic and context-aware conversations can make it a valuable tool for both novice and advanced learners (Speth et al., 2023). Besides, ChatGPT can adapt to individual learning styles and provide contextually relevant assistance (Kooli, 2023; Wu & Yu, 2024; Zhang & Tur, 2024), which can be particularly effective in programming education, where timely",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,4,"and advanced learners (Speth et al., 2023). Besides, ChatGPT can adapt to individual learning styles and provide contextually relevant assistance (Kooli, 2023; Wu & Yu, 2024; Zhang & Tur, 2024), which can be particularly effective in programming education, where timely and specific feedback is crucial for mastering coding skills (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b). Several studies have discussed about the potential benefits of using ChatGPT in programming education. One significant advantage is its 24/7 availability, ena- bling students to seek help at any time (Sun et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023a). This continuous access can lead to increased student engagement and persistence in learning. Furthermore, ChatGPT can address to diverse learning paces, provid- ing personalized learning paths and adjusting to individual progress (Bravo & Cruz- Bohorquez, 2024; Sun et al., 2024). This adaptability helps in meeting the varied needs of students, promoting inclusivity in education. ChatGPT can also assist students while building algorithms, code debugging and code optimization if used properly (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023a). This can increase positively influence their learning outcomes, self-confidence, and motivation toward programming courses (Bravo",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,4,"students, promoting inclusivity in education. ChatGPT can also assist students while building algorithms, code debugging and code optimization if used properly (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023a). This can increase positively influence their learning outcomes, self-confidence, and motivation toward programming courses (Bravo & Cruz-Bohorquez, 2024; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023a). Despite its advantages, the use of ChatGPT in programming education has come with its challenges. Concerns regarding the accuracy of information provided, potential over-reliance on the tool, risk of plagiarism, and social inequities to access AI-features are some of the challenges associated with the use of ChatGPT (Brin- gula, 2024; Bucaioni et al., 2024; Silva et al., 2024; Lo, 2023). Recent study has reported that students tend to express their concerns about privacy issues, ethical considerations, and ChatGPT’s potential negative impact on learning and creativity if ChatGPT is fully allowed to be used in programming courses (Guner et al., 2024). 2.2 Students’ interaction with AI tools The integration of AI tools, like ChatGPT, into educational settings has revealed the importance of diverse student interaction profiles with different levels of engage- ment (Koltovskaia et",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,4,"in programming courses (Guner et al., 2024). 2.2 Students’ interaction with AI tools The integration of AI tools, like ChatGPT, into educational settings has revealed the importance of diverse student interaction profiles with different levels of engage- ment (Koltovskaia et al., 2024; Lo et al., 2024), dependency (Haindl & Weinberger, 2024; Stojanov et al., 2024), and efficacy (Urban et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b) in using these technologies. Research has indicated that students inter- act with AI tools in distinct ways, which can be broadly categorized into different",89
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,5,"12685 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 profiles based on their motivation and behaviors (Liu et al., 2024; Stojanov et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2023). For instance, some students may tend to utilize AI tools as a primary resource to get direct answers with a high level of dependency on AI (Liu et al., 2024; Sto- janov et al., 2024). This can lead to increased efficiency in completing assignments but may also hinder the creativity and development of independent problem-solving skills (Gruenhagen et al., 2024). Conversely, another group of students may use AI tools more carefully as viewing them as supplementary purposes rather than pri- mary sources of information (Stojanov et al., 2024; Koltovskaia et al., 2024). These students may tend to engage with AI in a more balanced manner by using the tool to verify their own solutions or to gain insights into complex problems (Koltovs- kaia et al., 2024). Moreover, some students might prefer not to use AI for learning purposes due to the concerns about its accuracy, reliability, and potential negative impacts on creativity (Haindl &",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,5,"or to gain insights into complex problems (Koltovs- kaia et al., 2024). Moreover, some students might prefer not to use AI for learning purposes due to the concerns about its accuracy, reliability, and potential negative impacts on creativity (Haindl & Weinberger, 2024). Students’ interaction with AI tools can also vary based on their prior knowledge and skills on the subject (Shoufan, 2023; Xia et al., 2023). Novice learners can use AI tools as a support system by helping them to overcome initial challenges in understanding basic concepts (Scholl and Kiesler, 2024; Xue et al., 2024). How- ever, this reliance can sometimes lead to superficial learning, where students apply solutions without fully understanding the logic behind them (Prather et al., 2024; Xue et al., 2024). On the other hand, more experienced students may tend to use AI tools in a complementary fashion as integrating them into their existing knowledge to enhance productivity and to solve complex problems (Xia et al., 2023). Different profiles in students’ interaction behaviors can imply the need for per- sonalized approaches to AI integration in educational context",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,5,"fashion as integrating them into their existing knowledge to enhance productivity and to solve complex problems (Xia et al., 2023). Different profiles in students’ interaction behaviors can imply the need for per- sonalized approaches to AI integration in educational context (Kim et al., 2024a; Stojanov et al., 2024). By recognizing and addressing the distinct ways in which students interact with AI, educators can better design instruction to meet individual needs, and maximize the educational benefits of using AI-driven tools like ChatGPT (Kim et al., 2024b). 2.3 Effective integration of AI into courses The integration of AI tools into educational settings can offer significant potential for enhancing learning outcomes and fostering student engagement. However, stu- dents might tend to rely on AI if its role is not planned properly (Darvishi et al., 2024). Therefore, it may be crucial to implement strategic instructional and peda- gogical interventions for effective use of AI tools for learning purposes. Effective AI integration may involve embedding AI into the curriculum in a way that comple- ments traditional teaching methods and supports students’ learning (Sun et al.,",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,5,"instructional and peda- gogical interventions for effective use of AI tools for learning purposes. Effective AI integration may involve embedding AI into the curriculum in a way that comple- ments traditional teaching methods and supports students’ learning (Sun et al., 2024; Tan et al., 2024). This can be achieved through concurrent instructor assistance and guidance (Darvishi et al., 2024; Paiva and Bittencourt, 2020). Incorporating AI into collaborative learning activities can also enhance peer-to-peer learning, as students can collectively refine their problem-solving approaches with AI support (Rasul et al., 2023; Suciati et al., 2024).",93
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,6,"12686 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 To effectively integrate AI technologies like ChatGPT into educational set- tings, the importance of AI literacy and prompting skills has been intensely dis- cussed in recent research (Chiu, 2023; Denny et al., 2024; Naamati-Schneider & Alt, 2024; Stolpe & Hallström, 2024). AI literacy can be defined as under- standing AI fundamentals, applications, and ethical considerations, enabling users to engage responsibly with AI systems. On the other hand, prompting skills infer writing clear, specific, and context-rich inputs to get accurate and relevant responses from AI. The previous research has revealed that users with a solid understanding of AI and adept prompting abilities can better harness the capabilities of ChatGPT, leading to more productive and meaningful inter- actions (Knoth et al., 2024). Besides, AI-literate users can communicate more effectively with ChatGPT, resulting in higher quality responses (Tzirides et al., 2024). Students who understand the underlying mechanisms and potential biases of AI systems can critically evaluate the responses provided by ChatGPT (Huber et al., 2024). Therefore, gaining AI literacy and effective prompt writing skills can support",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,6,"responses (Tzirides et al., 2024). Students who understand the underlying mechanisms and potential biases of AI systems can critically evaluate the responses provided by ChatGPT (Huber et al., 2024). Therefore, gaining AI literacy and effective prompt writing skills can support better decision-making, reduce the risk of relying on faulty informa- tion and promote responsible use of AI (Knoth et al., 2024). Guided practice and scaffolding can also be essential components of effec- tive AI integration in courses. Guided practice can involve structured learn- ing activities where students can explore and apply fundamental concepts with the support of AI (Foung et al., 2024). During these activities, instructors can provide step-by-step instructions or model how to effectively interact with the AI, including how to craft precise and relevant prompts (Darvishi et al., 2024). On the other hand, scaffolding, a pedagogical approach that involves providing temporary support to learners as they develop new skills, might involve instruc- tors offering sample prompts and clear examples of how to interact with the AI (Jang et al., 2024). As students become more familiar with the AI",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,6,"involves providing temporary support to learners as they develop new skills, might involve instruc- tors offering sample prompts and clear examples of how to interact with the AI (Jang et al., 2024). As students become more familiar with the AI tool, these supports can be gradually reduced, encouraging students to take more control of the learning process. This gradual release of responsibility can encourage stu- dents to not become overly dependent on the AI and instead develop the critical thinking and problem-solving skills necessary for independent learners (Darvi- shi et al., 2024; Tabib & Alrabeei, 2024; Umutlu & Gursoy, 2022). The integration of AI in education may also vary significantly across dif- ferent regions, particularly between developed and developing countries (Li, 2023; Pawar & Khose, 2024; Tang et al., 2024). Developed regions, with more resources and advanced infrastructure, can likely to have greater access to AI- driven educational tools and support systems, facilitating more extensive inte- gration of AI in programming education. In contrast, developing regions may face challenges such as limited access to high-tech resources, varying levels of digital",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,6,"to have greater access to AI- driven educational tools and support systems, facilitating more extensive inte- gration of AI in programming education. In contrast, developing regions may face challenges such as limited access to high-tech resources, varying levels of digital literacy, and reduced exposure to AI-driven tools. These disparities may impact how students and educators interact with AI in education, shaping diverse experiences and outcomes depending on regional access and familiarity with these technologies (Viberg et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024).",82
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,7,"12687 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 3 Methodology 3.1 Context and participants The context of this study was an introductory programming course, a mandatory offering during the 2023–2024 Fall Semester. The course was taught across three sections by the same instructor, who is also one of the authors, over the span of 14 weeks. All course materials were provided through the university’s Moodle-based learning management system (LMS). Lectures were conducted face-to-face for two hours each, during which the instructor utilized PowerPoint presentations and a code editor to explain concepts and demonstrate their practical application though coding. Students were encouraged to bring their laptops to class for hands-on practice along- side the instructor. Ten lab assignments were given throughout the semester, each with a one-week deadline. Additionally, graded in-class practice activities were held on the 4th, 7th, and 12th weeks of the semester. During these activities, students were tasked with completing a programming exercise and submitting it by the end of the session. Following each activity, a quiz was administered via the LMS. In total, 175 students were enrolled in",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,7,"of the semester. During these activities, students were tasked with completing a programming exercise and submitting it by the end of the session. Following each activity, a quiz was administered via the LMS. In total, 175 students were enrolled in this course. All registered students provided informed consent but 158 students participated in all research activities, forming the research sample. Of these students, 102 (64.56%) were freshman from the Depart- ment of Statistics (corresponding to two sections), and 56 (35.44%) were sophomore from the Department of Computer Education and Instructional Technology (corre- sponding to one section). That is, this course was part of both departments’ curricula at different academic years. The sample included 66 females (41.77%) and 92 males (58.23%). Participation in the activity sessions was voluntary. Students who agreed to participate in the study received bonus points and had the chance to enter a raffle to win a money award. 3.2 Research design and data collection The research study employed a mixed-method research design to effectively investi- gate the impact of three distinct different ChatGPT-integration approaches within the aforementioned",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,7,"the chance to enter a raffle to win a money award. 3.2 Research design and data collection The research study employed a mixed-method research design to effectively investi- gate the impact of three distinct different ChatGPT-integration approaches within the aforementioned Python programming course. This design involved a quasi-experi- mental component implemented through the graded in-class practice activities. In particular, at each practice activity, a particular ChatGPT-integration approach was applied as an intervention to support students’ programming learning. While these interventions will be explained in greater detail in the following section, the details on the research design and data collection are explained here. As illustrated in Fig. 1, the research design involved multiple steps, starting with a short online test during the first week of the semester via the LMS at the end of the lecture. This test, designed to measure students’ prior knowledge of Python programming, consisting of 15 multiple-choice questions and was prepared by the course instructor. Importantly, this assessment has been used consistently in the course for the past 3 years, establishing its reliability as a measure of",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,7,"students’ prior knowledge of Python programming, consisting of 15 multiple-choice questions and was prepared by the course instructor. Importantly, this assessment has been used consistently in the course for the past 3 years, establishing its reliability as a measure of baseline",41
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,8,"12688 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 knowledge. Students were informed that the only purpose of the test was to help the course instructor recognize their knowledge levels, allowing for adjustments to the instruction and course content to cater to the overall class proficiency. Importantly, students were assured that their test would not impact their grades in any way. Afterwards, on the 4th, 7th, and 12th weeks of the semesters (i.e., the weeks of practice sessions), interventions were conducted. After each session both qualita- tive and quantitative data were collected. The qualitative data included the ChatGPT interaction logs. Students were instructed to generate a shareable link to their logs and submit it via the LMS. They were explicitly informed that their ChatGPT inter- actions would remain confidential, inaccessible to the course instructor, and would not influence their grades. In addition, students were notified about a post-session quiz to be completed without any external help, including ChatGPT. This quantita- tive data collection aimed to assess individual student comprehension and retention of the concepts covered during the sessions. The quizzes, administered online",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,8,"students were notified about a post-session quiz to be completed without any external help, including ChatGPT. This quantita- tive data collection aimed to assess individual student comprehension and retention of the concepts covered during the sessions. The quizzes, administered online via the LMS, consisted of 18, 20, and 16 multiple-choice questions, respectively, for each of the three intervention sessions. 3.3 Interventions of AI integration This section outlines three interventions of ChatGPT integration, each applied in separate practice sessions. The initial intervention was conducted in the first practice session in the fourth week of the semester. In this session, students were given the task of developing a Python program to compute the area of a triangle and a rec- tangle. The targeted topics were variables and functions. The intervention allowed students to use ChatGPT for seeking help without any restrictions or guidance. The next intervention was applied in the second practice session on the seventh week, where students were tasked with creating a console-based Rock, Paper, Scissors game that allows multiple rounds of play against the computer and track- ing the",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,8,"guidance. The next intervention was applied in the second practice session on the seventh week, where students were tasked with creating a console-based Rock, Paper, Scissors game that allows multiple rounds of play against the computer and track- ing the score. The targeted topics were while loops and conditions. Similar to the Fig. 1 Research design and data collection procedure",60
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,9,"12689 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 first intervention, while students were again allowed to use ChatGPT freely, this time they received a 20-minute training from the course instructor on effective prompting and use of ChatGPT for programming learning. This training, con- ducted at the start of the session, involved the instructor demonstrating various strategies through a comprehensive ChatGPT chat log, prepared before the ses- sion. The strategies emphasized in the training included: • Asking help for specific sub-tasks: Breaking down the programming task into smaller, more manageable sub-tasks and seeking help on each one individu- ally. • Moving step-by-step focusing on a single task at a time: Focusing on completing one task at a time before moving on to the next, ensuring each step is understood and implemented correctly. • Requesting hints or guidance not the final code: Instead of asking for a complete solution, requesting hints or starting points to learn and actively participate in the problem-solving process. • Critically reviewing and testing generated code: Demonstrate a cautious approach towards the code provided by ChatGPT and verify its",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,9,"of asking for a complete solution, requesting hints or starting points to learn and actively participate in the problem-solving process. • Critically reviewing and testing generated code: Demonstrate a cautious approach towards the code provided by ChatGPT and verify its correctness before using it. • Resolving errors in the code: Sharing only the relevant part of code attempt and the specific error message encountered to enable ChatGPT to provide targeted and relevant guidance. • Asking to review own code: Requesting ChatGPT to review given code and pro- vide feedback for improvement. • Request code explanations: Requesting explanations of code snippets to under- stand the underlying logic and functionality. • Asking follow-up questions: Posing additional questions based on the explana- tions provided to deepen the understanding and clarify any doubts. • Seeking conceptual explanations: Requesting explanations of fundamental pro- gramming concepts with examples to build a solid understanding of the lan- guage. An illustrative example of these strategies in action is provided in Fig. 2. The complete ChatGPT chat log from this training session is also available as supple- mentary material",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,9,"with examples to build a solid understanding of the lan- guage. An illustrative example of these strategies in action is provided in Fig. 2. The complete ChatGPT chat log from this training session is also available as supple- mentary material for further reference. The final intervention, conducted on the twelfth week during the third activity session, students were challenged to create a Spanish-English dictionary program. The targeted topics were lists, strings, and file input and output. Similarly, students were allowed to use ChatGPT but with an additional layer of support. In particular, the lab instructions document included prompt examples under each sub-task, pro- viding students with clear guidance on how to effectively interact with ChatGPT to receive the appropriate assistance. In the session, students were reminded several times that they were free to use the example prompts provided or to craft their own prompts when interacting with ChatGPT. An example of these embedded prompts can be found in Fig. 3. The complete guide with all prompt examples is available in the supplementary resources.",173
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,9,craft their own prompts when interacting with ChatGPT. An example of these embedded prompts can be found in Fig. 3. The complete guide with all prompt examples is available in the supplementary resources.,33
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,10,"12690 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 3.4 Data analysis To answer the first research question, which explores students’ ChatGPT interac- tion profiles, a thematic analysis was conducted on their conversation histories based on the approach defined by Clarke and Braun (2017). Two researchers first independently coded each student prompt within a conversation according to its purpose. Then, they convened to share and discuss their codes and refined them. This process of independent coding and follow-up negotiation was repeated until a consensus was reached on the final set of codes to label the prompts. Next, based on the prompt labels, the researchers independently determined the inter- action profiles for each student, followed by a discussion to establish the final profiles. Throughout the coding and profiling process, a collaborative Google Spreadsheet was used, which facilitated the coordination and mutual control of coding accuracy. For the second research question, examining the impact of different AI integra- tion interventions on students’ AI interaction profiles, the McNemar-Bowker test (Krampe & Kuhnt, 2007) was conducted. To complement the statistical results, a Sankey diagram was utilized",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,10,"coding accuracy. For the second research question, examining the impact of different AI integra- tion interventions on students’ AI interaction profiles, the McNemar-Bowker test (Krampe & Kuhnt, 2007) was conducted. To complement the statistical results, a Sankey diagram was utilized to visually illustrate the transition of AI interaction Fig. 2 Example chat from demo session",55
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,11,"12691 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 profiles across the three sessions. Regarding the third research question, investi- gating the impact of previous programming knowledge on students’ use of AI, a one-way ANOVA test (Coolidge, 2020) was conducted using pre-knowledge test scores and students’ AI interaction profiles. To address the fourth research ques- tion, investigating if AI interaction profiles differ in their subsequent performance based on post-tests, one-way ANOVA was conducted. All the statistical analysis were conducted using IBM SPSS software. Since the number of students varied across the sessions, in the analysis we focused on the participants who both took the post-test and uploaded their ChatGPT conversation histories. 4 Results 4.1 Students’ AI interaction profiles based on their prompting behaviors (RQ1) 4.1.1 Prompt analysis on the students’ ChatGPT conversation history A total of 2388 prompts were thematically analyzed and coded across all sessions. The final version of prompt types and frequencies are given in Table 1. Although each session maintained a sample size of 146, it is important to acknowledge that there was not complete overlap in student participation",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,11,"coded across all sessions. The final version of prompt types and frequencies are given in Table 1. Although each session maintained a sample size of 146, it is important to acknowledge that there was not complete overlap in student participation across all sessions. Fig. 3 Sample prompts from lab guide document",51
