A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,13,"12693 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 Exact copy of instruction was more common in the first session (65) and decreased in subsequent sessions (to 32 and 27, respectively). This decline might be due to the demo training where students were advised to ask their questions in parts to get more accurate answers from ChatGPT. However, when considering the total written prompts across the three sessions, exact copy of instruction, partial copy of instruction, and summary of instruction collectively constituted the highest num- ber, totaling 465 prompts (19.47% of the total prompts). This suggests a reliance on ChatGPT while solving programming tasks and the ease of copy-pasting task instructions. On the other hand, coding task inquiry (264 prompts; 11.06% of total prompts) and refining GPT code (244 prompts; 10.22% of total prompts) were consistently high relative to other prompt types. This might reflect the students’ need for specific coding knowledge and improvements to the AI-generated solutions. Additionally, prompts related to reviewing their own code, refining their own code, and debugging their own code (356 prompts in total; 14.91% of total",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,13,"This might reflect the students’ need for specific coding knowledge and improvements to the AI-generated solutions. Additionally, prompts related to reviewing their own code, refining their own code, and debugging their own code (356 prompts in total; 14.91% of total prompts) demonstrate a lower reliance on ChatGPT, with students coding first and then utilizing AI for support. 4.1.2 Students’ AI interaction profiles Based on the prompt labels described previously, five AI interaction profiles were identified across all sessions: AI-reliant code generator, AI-reliant code generator & refiner, AI-collaborative coder, AI-assisted code refiner, and AI-independent coder. The description of these profiles and their distributions across intervention sessions are given in Table 2. In the first session, majority of the students exhibited behaviors as AI-Reliant Code Generators (30.82%) and AI-Assisted Code Refiners (27.40%). AI-Independ- ent Coders comprised 20.55% of the participants. The least represented categories were AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners (10.27%) and AI-Collaborative Coders (10.96%). In the second session, the most common interaction profiles were AI-Reli- ant Code Generator & Refiners (31.51%) and AI-Assisted Code Refiners (29.45%), followed by AI-Reliant Code Generators",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,13,"represented categories were AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners (10.27%) and AI-Collaborative Coders (10.96%). In the second session, the most common interaction profiles were AI-Reli- ant Code Generator & Refiners (31.51%) and AI-Assisted Code Refiners (29.45%), followed by AI-Reliant Code Generators (21.92%). The least common categories were AI-Collaborative Coders (11.64%) and AI-Independent Coders (5.48%). In the third session, nearly half of the students were AI-Collaborative Coders (51.37%). AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners accounted for 21.92%, while AI-Assisted Code Refiners (8.22%), AI-Reliant Code Generators (11.64%), and AI-Independent Coders (6.85%) were less prevalent. 4.2 Impact of different AI integration interventions on AI interaction profiles (RQ2) To identify the changes in students’ AI interaction profiles across three sessions, the McNemar-Bowker tests were conducted. The results showed significant differences between Session 1 and Session 2 (χ² (10, N = 135) = 34.478, p < .001), and Session 2 and Session 3 (χ² (10, N = 136) = 57.099, p < .001).",156
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,14,"12694 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 Table 2 Students’ AI interaction profiles based on their prompting behavior Interaction Profile 1st Session (N = 146) 2nd Session (N = 146) 3rd Session (N = 146) Profile Explanation AI-Reliant Code Generator 45 (30.82%) 32 (21.92%) 17 (11.64%) Students who directly paste the entire or partial programming task instructions and aim for quick, direct answers from ChatGPT without much engagement in the problem- solving process. AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiner 15 (10.27%) 46 (31.51%) 32 (21.92%) Students who first directly paste the entire or partial programming task instructions to get a direct answer from ChatGPT. Then, they write additional prompts to refine the answer to meet the requirements of the assignment. They may also ask for explanation or verification to understand the GPT solution. AI-Collaborative Coder 16 (10.96%) 17 (11.64%) 75 (51.37%) Students who inquire about specific coding knowledge or guidance related to a particu- lar task or requirement. These students concentrate on acquiring task-specific informa- tion or guidance to meet their programming task objectives. For example, instead of writing whole task",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,14,"Students who inquire about specific coding knowledge or guidance related to a particu- lar task or requirement. These students concentrate on acquiring task-specific informa- tion or guidance to meet their programming task objectives. For example, instead of writing whole task instruction, they just ask, “how to write a function producing random numbers in phyton” and then they try to understand and solve the task by themselves. AI-Assisted Code Refiner 40 (27.40%) 43 (29.45%) 12 (8.22%) Students who attempt the programming task themselves first, then seek ChatGPT’s help for revision, error fixing, or optimization. They also ask ChatGPT to check, refine or modify its initial responses, providing feedback, clarification, or additional informa- tion to tailor the answers better to meet their needs. AI-Independent Coder 30 (20.55%) 8 (5.48%) 10 (6.85%) Students who demonstrate a high level of independence and problem-solving skills by completing the activity without relying on ChatGPT assistance. They mostly did not get any help from ChatGPT and solve the activity on their own. Or some of them asked general questions which are not directly task related.",178
