A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,15,"12695 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 The Sankey diagrams (Figs. 4 and 5) illustrate the transitions of students between different AI interaction profiles across the three sessions. Each node rep- resents a profile at a specific session, and the links between the nodes represent the number of students transitioning from one profile to another. The width of each link corresponds to the number of students, providing a clear visual repre- sentation of how student profiles evolve over time. The change of students’ AI interaction profiles from 1st session to 2nd session and from 2nd session to 3rd session are given in Tables 3 and 4, respectively. Regarding the profile transitions from Session 1 to Session 2, there’s a significant shift away from the “AI-reliant code generator” profile towards the “AI-reliant code generator & refiner” profile, suggesting increased student engagement in refining AI-generated code. Additionally, a decrease in “AI-independent coders” coupled with an increase in “AI-assisted code refiner” indicates a growing reli- ance on AI tools for code refinement even among those initially coding indepen- dently. The overall trend indicates",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,15,"in refining AI-generated code. Additionally, a decrease in “AI-independent coders” coupled with an increase in “AI-assisted code refiner” indicates a growing reli- ance on AI tools for code refinement even among those initially coding indepen- dently. The overall trend indicates a dynamic adoption of AI assistance in coding practices, with students transitioning towards profiles that leverage AI for both generation and refinement of code. Regarding the transitions from Session 2 to Sessions 3, there is a substantial rise in the “AI-collaborative coder” profile, sug- gesting a growing preference for active collaboration with AI tools during cod- ing tasks. Additionally, a sharp decline in the “AI-reliant code generator” pro- file indicates a decreasing reliance on AI for sole code generation. These results Fig. 4 The change of students’ AI interaction profiles from 1st session to 2nd session Fig. 5 The change of students’ AI interaction profiles from 2nd session to 3rd session",151
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,16,"12696 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 suggest the impact of different interventions on students’ profiles of using AI in programming. 4.3 Impact of previous programming knowledge on AI interaction profiles (RQ3) To assess the students’ previous programming knowledge, a multiple-choice test comprising 15 questions about programming basics was applied at the beginning of the semester. Out of the total participants, 139 students participated in this assess- ment. The average score obtained was 6.95/15 points (M = 6.95, SD = 3.84). To examine the impact of previous knowledge on students’ AI interaction pro- files in 1st activity session, a one-way ANOVA was conducted. There were 127 participants both taking previous knowledge test and participating the 1st activ- ity session. The mean test scores for each profile types were as AI-Reliant Code Generators (M = 5.5, SD = 3.49), AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners (M = 6.0, SD = 2.92), AI-Assisted Code Refiners (M = 8.35, SD = 3.22), AI-Independent Cod- ers (M = 7.65, SD = 4.69), and AI-Collaborative Coders (M = 7.29, SD = 4.10). A one-way ANOVA revealed",180
