A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,19,"12699 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 5.1 Students’ AI interaction profiles based on their prompting behaviors (RQ1) Regarding the analysis of students’ prompting behaviors while they were interact- ing with ChatGPT, five distinct AI interaction profiles were identified as AI-Reliant Code Generators, AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners, AI-Assisted Code Refin- ers, AI-Independent Coders, and AI-Collaborative Coders. Building on the profiles described, many students tended to use AI for quick and easy assistance across all interventions. However, a specific group demonstrated a strategy of putting an initial effort to improve their existing code while collaborating with AI to complete certain tasks. On the other hand, a smaller group of students deliberately avoided using AI assistance. Recent study conducted by Stojanov et al. (2024) also explores profiles based on self-reported reliance level of students while interacting with ChatGPT. They iden- tify five distinct profiles of reliance as “versatile low reliers”, “knowledge seekers”, “pro-active learners”, “task-delegators”, and “all-rounders”. “Versatile low reliers” have the lowest self-reported reliance; “knowledge seekers” uses AI for content acquisition, information retrieval, and summarization; “pro-active learners” get help from",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,19,"five distinct profiles of reliance as “versatile low reliers”, “knowledge seekers”, “pro-active learners”, “task-delegators”, and “all-rounders”. “Versatile low reliers” have the lowest self-reported reliance; “knowledge seekers” uses AI for content acquisition, information retrieval, and summarization; “pro-active learners” get help from ChatGPT as obtaining feedback, planning, and quizzing; “task-delegators” relied on ChatGPT for completing assignments; and “all-rounders” has highest self- reported reliance on ChatGPT (Stojanov et al., 2024). Both studies highlight a spectrum of reliance on ChatGPT, from minimal (AI- Independent Coders and “versatile low reliers”) to high (AI-Reliant Code Genera- tors and “task-delegators”). Our AI-Reliant Code Generators and Stojanov et al.’s “task-delegators” both exhibit a high dependence on ChatGPT, though our profile focuses on direct answers for programming tasks, while theirs extends to drafting and writing assignments. AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners share similarities with “knowledge seekers”, as both involve refining and seeking explanations, though “knowledge seekers” focus more broadly on content acquisition. AI-Assisted Code Refiners and “proactive learners” both use ChatGPT for feedback and optimization, with a notable emphasis on initial independent effort from our AI-Assisted Code Refiners. Lastly,",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,19,"refining and seeking explanations, though “knowledge seekers” focus more broadly on content acquisition. AI-Assisted Code Refiners and “proactive learners” both use ChatGPT for feedback and optimization, with a notable emphasis on initial independent effort from our AI-Assisted Code Refiners. Lastly, AI-Collaborative Coders and “knowledge seekers” show a targeted approach to using ChatGPT for specific guidance, though “all-rounders” exhibit the highest reliance on the tool. These comparisons underscore the diversity in students’ interaction patterns and reliance on AI, reflecting varying degrees of independence and collaboration in task completion. 5.2 Impact of different AI integration interventions on AI interaction profiles (RQ2) Considerable shifts in student profiles were observed across three sessions, which may indicate the influence of the AI-integration strategies on how students inter- acted with ChatGPT. In the first session, students predominantly relied on AI for generating code with minimal engagement in the problem-solving process, which aligns with previous findings that students had tendency to use AI tools to get direct solutions (Ghimire & Edwards, 2024; Sun et al., 2024). However, after the training",173
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,19,"in the problem-solving process, which aligns with previous findings that students had tendency to use AI tools to get direct solutions (Ghimire & Edwards, 2024; Sun et al., 2024). However, after the training",33
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,20,"12700 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 session focused on effective ChatGPT usage and prompting skills in the second session, a significant transformation was noted in their interaction patterns. That is, many students began not only to use AI to generate code but also to refine and critically evaluate the AI’s outputs to align the solutions more closely with the task requirements. Similarly, in the third session in which students were provided with a guide document involving sample examples and prompts, students who initially relied heavily on AI for code generation, as well as those who refined AI-generated code or used AI for assistance, increasingly moved towards more collaborative inter- actions with AI. These transitions may suggest that both interventions were influen- tial in encouraging students to move beyond superficial use of AI and to engage in a more iterative and thoughtful coding process, as supported by studies recommend- ing that guidance for AI literacy and prompt writing can enhance the educational use of AI tools (Silva et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b; Yu, 2023). The significant growth in",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,20,"and thoughtful coding process, as supported by studies recommend- ing that guidance for AI literacy and prompt writing can enhance the educational use of AI tools (Silva et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b; Yu, 2023). The significant growth in the AI-collaborative profile in third session, where stu- dents used ChatGPT to seek specific guidance and integrate it into their problem- solving process, may be the impact of guidance document on sample prompts. This finding supports the notion that instructional interventions can transform AI from a passive source of answers into an active learning partner (Weng et al., 2024). The drop in using AI assistance for code refinement may suggest that as students gained confidence and skill in interacting with ChatGPT, they moved towards more collab- orative interactions, rather than utilizing AI for refining code. This shift is consistent with literature emphasizing the importance of developing students’ AI literacy and critical thinking skills (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b). On the other hand, there were some students who initially began to engage more thoughtfully with AI in the first session but reverted",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,20,"literature emphasizing the importance of developing students’ AI literacy and critical thinking skills (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b). On the other hand, there were some students who initially began to engage more thoughtfully with AI in the first session but reverted to seeking direct answers in the second and third sessions. This reversion could reflect a preference for efficiency and straightforward solutions, possibly driven by the demands of the task or time constraints, which is also discussed in previous research on learner behavior in digital environments (Beaudoin, 2002; Ellis & Bliuc, 2016). It might also suggest that while the training and guidance provided an initial boost in effective AI usage, its impact may have diminished over time, or that some students found it easier to return to a more passive role in their interactions with AI. 5.3 Impact of previous programming knowledge on AI interaction profiles (RQ3) The significant impact of previous programming knowledge on students’ AI interac- tion profiles in the initial activity session emphasizes the role of foundational under- standing in shaping how students engage with AI tools. The",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,20,"knowledge on AI interaction profiles (RQ3) The significant impact of previous programming knowledge on students’ AI interac- tion profiles in the initial activity session emphasizes the role of foundational under- standing in shaping how students engage with AI tools. The findings of the current study have demonstrated that students with higher previous programming knowl- edge were more likely to adopt the AI-Assisted Code Refiner profile compared to the AI-Reliant Code Generator profile. That is, students who have higher previous knowledge prefer to first try coding the task themselves, then they ask ChatGPT for revision or refinement. On the other hand, students having lower previous knowl- edge tend to copy-paste entire instruction and get the direct answer from ChatGPT without much engagement.",121
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,21,"12701 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 This finding is supported by previous research (Xia et al., 2023) which reports that students’ previous subject matter knowledge can directly influence students’ AI interaction. That is, students with higher prior knowledge can more tend to figure out how to approach a similar problem and how to use available sources (Xia et al., 2023). This may infer that students who have a certain base in programming funda- mentals are more likely to utilize AI tools in a way that complements and extends their capabilities, leading to more effective learning outcomes. On the other hand, novice learners may have difficulty in using AI tools in a way to support their learn- ing, and they may have tendency to use AI tools just to get their assignment done. Therefore, it can be important to recognize student profiles early in the semester to adopt a pedagogically sounder approach to AI integration to support students from all backgrounds or knowledge levels. 5.4 Association between AI interaction profiles and students’ performance (RQ4) The findings of the study have",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,21,"student profiles early in the semester to adopt a pedagogically sounder approach to AI integration to support students from all backgrounds or knowledge levels. 5.4 Association between AI interaction profiles and students’ performance (RQ4) The findings of the study have revealed significant differences in students’ post-test scores across different AI interaction profiles. In the first session, AI-Assisted Code Refiners and AI-Independent Coders outperformed AI-Reliant Code Generators. This finding suggests that students who engage more actively and critically with AI tools tend to achieve higher performance outcomes. Similarly, in the second session, AI-Assisted Code Refiners had significantly higher scores compared to AI-Reliant Code Generator & Refiners. These results align with previous studies that emphasize the benefits of active engagement and critical thinking in AI-supported learning environments (Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023b). Students who use AI tools to refine and improve their code, rather than relying on AI just for code generation, appear to develop a deeper understand- ing and mastery of the programming concepts. Another study conducted by Pes- ovski et al. (2024) indicated that high performing students were more reluctant to",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,21,"rather than relying on AI just for code generation, appear to develop a deeper understand- ing and mastery of the programming concepts. Another study conducted by Pes- ovski et al. (2024) indicated that high performing students were more reluctant to use AI-generated content to complete their tasks comparing to low performers. This can also be interpreted as high performing students might not prefer to primarily rely on AI but to engage with task by themselves at first. On the other hand, students who rely on AI for mere task completion might not get the benefit for improving their performance. This is also supported by Xia et al. (2023), reporting that current chatbot technologies might not contribute much to learning of students with relatively lower levels of subject proficiency. This may be because of that low performers may not be able to regulate their interaction with AI for learning purposes (Xia et al., 2023). 6 Conclusion This study provides valuable insights into the impact of previous programming knowledge and AI integration interventions on students’ AI interaction profiles and performance in a",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,21,"to regulate their interaction with AI for learning purposes (Xia et al., 2023). 6 Conclusion This study provides valuable insights into the impact of previous programming knowledge and AI integration interventions on students’ AI interaction profiles and performance in a programming course. Findings of the study highlight the importance of considering students’ prior knowledge when designing AI-enhanced",57
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,22,"12702 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:12681–12707 learning environments, as it significantly influences how students interact with AI tools. The results also imply that students can benefit from additional guidance train- ing about AI literacy and effective prompt writing skills. Moreover, the association between AI interaction profiles and student performance might indicate the impor- tance of promoting active and critical engagement with AI tools. Students who use ChatGPT for refining and improving their work, rather than solely for generation, tend to achieve better performance outcomes. Finally, the dynamic nature of stu- dents’ AI interaction profiles over time suggests that ongoing support and adaptive strategies are crucial for maximizing the educational benefits of AI integration. The findings indicate diverse levels of reliance on ChatGPT among students, with a significant portion initially preferring to work independently. However, as students become more comfortable with AI tools, their interaction with such tools might increases, suggesting that getting familiar with AI and having training about prompt- ing skills can enhance the integration of AI into learning processes. It might imply the need for targeted interventions",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,22,"with AI tools, their interaction with such tools might increases, suggesting that getting familiar with AI and having training about prompt- ing skills can enhance the integration of AI into learning processes. It might imply the need for targeted interventions to support students in becoming adept users of AI, maximizing its potential to enhance their learning experiences. This study also has several limitations. First, since it was conducted in a natural class- room environment, students might have used other resources without reporting them. Second, prompt analysis was based on the ChatGPT conversation history that students voluntarily uploaded, which may not capture all interactions; using an LMS-embedded LLM tool could have provided a more comprehensive record. Finally, the study involved students from the same university with similar backgrounds, so including a more diverse group of participants could improve the generalizability of the findings. Future research should explore the long-term impacts of AI integration in educa- tion, examining how different types of AI tools can be tailored to support diverse learner profiles effectively. Additionally, investigating the factors that influence the plateau in",180
A130,A130.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13337-7,22,"the findings. Future research should explore the long-term impacts of AI integration in educa- tion, examining how different types of AI tools can be tailored to support diverse learner profiles effectively. Additionally, investigating the factors that influence the plateau in performance gains, the role of task complexity, and potential challenges of AI tool nature in shaping learning outcomes can provide deeper insights into optimiz- ing AI-enhanced learning environments. Studies could also examine how regional disparities in AI access and digital literacy impact programming education outcomes, contributing to more equitable integration strategies across diverse educational set- tings. Addressing these gaps may require tailored support to ensure students world- wide benefit equitably from advancements in AI-driven programming education. Acknowledgements We would like to express our sincere gratitude to all the students who participated in this study for their invaluable contributions and insights. Funding sources Open access funding provided by the Scientific and Technological Research Council of Türkiye (TÜBİTAK). This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the pub- lic, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Data availability Data is available",180
