DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,1,"1 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026; 42:e70322 https://doi.org/10.1002/jcal.70322 WILEYlogo Journal of Computer Assisted Learning SPECIAL ISSUE Dynamics of Learning and Learners in The Changing Digital Landscape ORIGINAL ARTICLE OPEN ACCESS Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education: A Longitudinal Empirical Study Chrysanthi Melanou1 | Maik Beege2,3 | Martin Kimmig4 1Education Service Center, Baden-­Wuerttemberg Cooperative State University, Villingen-­Schwenningen, Germany | 2Digital Media in Education, Department of Psychology, University of Education Freiburg, Freiburg, Germany | 3Center for Interdisciplinary Research on Digital Education (CIRDE), University of Education, Freiburg, Germany | 4Business Informatics, Baden-­Wuerttemberg Cooperative State University, Villingen-­ Schwenningen, Germany Correspondence: Chrysanthi Melanou (chrysanthi.melanou@dhbw.de) Received: 31 October 2025 | Revised: 5 May 2026 | Accepted: 25 August 2026 Keywords: cognitive load | critical thinking | generative AI | knowledge gain | learning dynamics | motivation | reflective use ABSTRACT Background: Generative AI tools such as ChatGPT are increasingly integrated into higher education. Empirical evidence on how they shape learning dynamics over time remains limited. Previous studies have often examined single learning outcomes, neglecting the interplay between cognitive, motivational, and metacognitive",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,1,"Generative AI tools such as ChatGPT are increasingly integrated into higher education. Empirical evidence on how they shape learning dynamics over time remains limited. Previous studies have often examined single learning outcomes, neglecting the interplay between cognitive, motivational, and metacognitive processes that evolve throughout the semester. Objectives: This longitudinal study investigated how generative AI influences students' knowledge gain, motivation, cognitive load, critical thinking, and reflective use across an entire semester. It further explored whether prior knowledge and AI experi­ ence moderated these learning effects, addressing concerns about potential Matthew effects. Methods: A quasi-­experimental design was implemented with three parallel classes of business informatics students (N = 87) assigned to different instructional conditions: tutor-­AI use, unguided AI, and a control group without AI. Data were collected at three measurement points (T1–T3). Results and Conclusion: Students showed significant knowledge gains across all groups, but no additional benefits of AI in­ tegration. Motivation remained largely stable; germane cognitive load increased and was positively associated with knowledge gain. Critical thinking remained stable, while reflective use was higher in the AI condition and positively associated",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,1,"groups, but no additional benefits of AI in­ tegration. Motivation remained largely stable; germane cognitive load increased and was positively associated with knowledge gain. Critical thinking remained stable, while reflective use was higher in the AI condition and positively associated with critical thinking. No evidence emerged for a Matthew effect, indicating that AI did not intensify performance inequalities. Overall, the findings did not support the view of generative AI as a consistent learning outcome. Its impact depended on pedagogical framing and learners' metacognitive regulation, underscoring the need for structured guidance and reflective engagement in AI-­supported education. 1 | Introduction The rise of generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) is funda­ mentally reshaping higher education. Generative AI tools like ChatGPT have rapidly become integrated into students' aca­ demic routines, supporting tasks such as summarising texts, gen­ erating explanations, and completing assignments (Gruenhagen et al. 2024). Supporters argue that such tools provide equal ac­ cess to knowledge, personalise learning, and support students in ways traditional instruction cannot (Chiu 2024). However, concerns remain that excessive reliance on AI could lead to a decline in students'",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,1,"al. 2024). Supporters argue that such tools provide equal ac­ cess to knowledge, personalise learning, and support students in ways traditional instruction cannot (Chiu 2024). However, concerns remain that excessive reliance on AI could lead to a decline in students' independent thinking and learning efforts (Kirschner et al. 2006). These contrasting perspectives high­ light the need for systematic, theory-­driven research on how This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. © 2026 The Author(s). Journal of Computer Assisted Learning published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",109
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"2 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 generative AI affects the dynamics of learning in authentic uni­ versity contexts. 1.1 | Learning as a Dynamic Process Recent work conceptualises learning as a dynamic, context-­ sensitive system that evolves in non-­linear ways (Geng et al. 2023). When AI becomes part of the learning environ­ ment, it may transform how students engage, think, and prog­ ress (Gabriel 2024). However, most studies focus on isolated outcomes or short-­term effects rather than examining how learning evolves across multiple dimensions and time (Suprobo et al. 2025). Reviews further indicate that AI research in edu­ cation is dominated by cross-­sectional designs emphasising im­ mediate outcomes such as task performance (Zawacki-­Richter et al. 2019). In contrast, learning dynamics require a longitudinal per­ spective that captures trajectories across multiple dimensions (Saqr and López-­Pernas 2021). Motivation may rise or de­ cline, cognitive effort may shift, and learning strategies often change across the semester, especially in technology-­rich set­ tings (Bosch and Spinath 2023). This highlights that learning outcomes cannot be fully understood without considering their temporal development and",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"rise or de­ cline, cognitive effort may shift, and learning strategies often change across the semester, especially in technology-­rich set­ tings (Bosch and Spinath 2023). This highlights that learning outcomes cannot be fully understood without considering their temporal development and the interaction of multiple underlying processes. Early enthusiasm toward AI may later turn into fatigue, overreliance, or disengagement (Moy and Feldstein 2024). This aligns with theoretical perspectives that conceptualise learning as a complex dynamic system, where outcomes emerge from the interaction of multiple processes over time (Kaplan and Garner 2020). Based on this theoretical framing, the present study applies a multidimensional and longitudinal approach to capture these dynamic interactions. By simultaneously tracing cognitive, mo­ tivational, and metacognitive processes across a semester, it ex­ amines how generative AI influences learning as an evolving process across time rather than a set of isolated outcomes. 1.2 | Knowledge Gain & Matthew Effect A key concern in this context is whether AI meaningfully supports students' knowledge gain, an issue highlighted as a central challenge for future research (Hale et al. 2024). The Matthew effect",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"isolated outcomes. 1.2 | Knowledge Gain & Matthew Effect A key concern in this context is whether AI meaningfully supports students' knowledge gain, an issue highlighted as a central challenge for future research (Hale et al. 2024). The Matthew effect describes that students with stronger prior knowledge learn more efficiently, which can widen perfor­ mance gaps over time (Stanovich 1986). More broadly, the Matthew effect has been conceptualised as a cumulative advantage process in which pre-­existing advantages lead to widening disparities over time (Rigney 2010). In educational settings, such pre-­existing advantages may extend beyond domain-­specific baseline performance and include broader academic competencies or prior technological experience, which may shape students' learning outcomes. In the present study, this cumulative advantage perspective was operation­ alised through indicators of prior academic achievement and prior AI experience, reflecting broader learner characteristics that may influence benefits from AI-­supported learning. In AI-­supported learning, this effect may be reinforced, as effec­ tive use of AI requires competencies such as prompt engineer­ ing, source evaluation, and metacognitive regulation (Holmes et al. 2019). These skills are often more developed",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"benefits from AI-­supported learning. In AI-­supported learning, this effect may be reinforced, as effec­ tive use of AI requires competencies such as prompt engineer­ ing, source evaluation, and metacognitive regulation (Holmes et al. 2019). These skills are often more developed among stu­ dents who already perform well academically, enabling them to benefit more strategically (Huang and Ball 2024). Similarly, Zhao et al. (2025) highlight that generative AI tends to am­ plify existing differences in cognitive resources, as students with higher prior knowledge are better able to use the tech­ nology for elaborating ideas, summarising complex content, and generating meaningful responses. However, recent evi­ dence indicates that the Matthew Effect is not inevitable. In a recent quasi-­experimental study, prior knowledge did not significantly influence learning gains when AI was combined with critical thinking strategies (Zhao et al. 2025). This un­ derscores the need for longitudinal research that considers not only prior knowledge but also motivational and reflective dimensions. 1.3 | Motivation Motivation is crucial for sustained engagement. According to Self-­Determination Theory, learners remain motivated when their needs for autonomy and competence",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"the need for longitudinal research that considers not only prior knowledge but also motivational and reflective dimensions. 1.3 | Motivation Motivation is crucial for sustained engagement. According to Self-­Determination Theory, learners remain motivated when their needs for autonomy and competence are sup­ ported (Deci and Ryan 2000). AI tools may enhance autonomy through self-­directing exploration and competence through immediate feedback. However, they may also undermine in­ trinsic motivation if students rely too heavily on automated outputs. Automation bias suggests that people tend to defer to automated systems even when outputs are problematic (Parasuraman and Riley 1997). Similar concerns have been discussed in the context of AI trust, where over-­reliance may reduce cognitive effort (Glikson and Woolley 2020). Recent Lay Summary • What is currently known about this topic? ○ Generative AI tools are increasingly used in uni­ versity learning. ○ Research mainly reports short-­term or single-­ outcome effects. ○ There is limited longitudinal evidence from real university courses. • What does this paper add? ○ Provides authentic longitudinal empirical findings. ○ Investigates the association between cognitive, motivational, and metacognitive factors",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,2,"Research mainly reports short-­term or single-­ outcome effects. ○ There is limited longitudinal evidence from real university courses. • What does this paper add? ○ Provides authentic longitudinal empirical findings. ○ Investigates the association between cognitive, motivational, and metacognitive factors in AI-­ supported learning. ○ Highlights how generative AI influences knowl­ edge gain, motivation, cognitive load, critical thinking, and reflective use across the semester. • Implications for practice or policy? ○ The educational value of AI depends on instruc­ tional design rather than technology alone. ○ Scaffolding and reflective guidance are essential to promote higher-­order thinking. ○ Institutions should establish a structured frame­ work linking cognitive, motivational, and reflec­ tive dimensions for sustainable AI integration. 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",154
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"3 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 studies in education present a mixed picture. Some evidence shows motivational benefits of AI when it fosters autonomy (Boguslawski et al. 2025), while others report declines in en­ gagement once novelty fades (Polyportis 2024). A longitudinal approach is therefore needed to capture how motivational dy­ namics evolve over time. 1.4 | Cognitive Load Cognitive Load Theory emphasises that learning is constrained by the capacity of working memory (Sweller et al. 1998). It dis­ tinguishes intrinsic load (ICL) from task complexity, extraneous cognitive load (ECL) from unnecessary processing demands, and germane cognitive load (GCL) from productive effort. AI may reduce ECL by clarifying instructions or summarising complex texts, but it may also create additional demands when students must judge the reliability of outputs. Additionally, AI could foster GCL if students use it for elaboration and expla­ nation rather than copying answers (Zammouri et al. 2024). Few studies, however, track how cognitive load evolves across a semester (Sweller 1988; Leppink et al. 2014). Accordingly, it is essential to capture how mental effort evolves",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"for elaboration and expla­ nation rather than copying answers (Zammouri et al. 2024). Few studies, however, track how cognitive load evolves across a semester (Sweller 1988; Leppink et al. 2014). Accordingly, it is essential to capture how mental effort evolves throughout the semester. 1.5 | Critical Thinking Critical thinking, defined as the ability to analyse, evaluate, and construct arguments, is a core outcome of higher educa­ tion (Facione 1990). Generative AI can stimulate analytical reasoning but may also promote uncritical acceptance of plau­ sible outputs (Hou et al. 2025). As critical thinking develops gradually through extended learning cycles and sustained feed­ back (Halpern 2013), noticeable short-­term improvements are unlikely. Students who reason more deeply are more likely to achieve conceptual understanding and measurable knowledge gains (Entwistle 1991). According to Cognitive Load Theory (Sweller 1988; Leppink et al. 2014), they can ignore irrelevant information, reducing extraneous load, and invest cognitive effort in meaningful elaboration, increasing germane load. Germane cognitive load may therefore act as the mechanism through which critical thinking translates into learning out­ comes, as productive cognitive effort supports the",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"ignore irrelevant information, reducing extraneous load, and invest cognitive effort in meaningful elaboration, increasing germane load. Germane cognitive load may therefore act as the mechanism through which critical thinking translates into learning out­ comes, as productive cognitive effort supports the integration of new knowledge (Paas et al. 2003). 1.6 | Reflective Use Reflective use, a critical metacognitive process of monitoring and verifying information before adoption, involves checking sources, assessing plausibility, and regulating how AI outputs are integrated (Flavell 1979). Foundational metacognitive the­ ory suggests that such regulation supports learners gradually in directing attention and controlling cognitive effort to optimise learning outcomes (Flavell 1979). Research on civic online rea­ soning shows that students often struggle with this skill, even at the college level (McGrew et al. 2018). This aligns with broader perspectives of AI literacy, which consider reflective engage­ ment essential for technical, critical, and ethical dimensions of AI-­supported learning (Long and Magerko 2020). More recent work emphasises that reflective use is essential for higher-­order learning, as generative AI reshapes inquiry and evaluation (Oliveira et al. 2025). From a Cognitive Load",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"for technical, critical, and ethical dimensions of AI-­supported learning (Long and Magerko 2020). More recent work emphasises that reflective use is essential for higher-­order learning, as generative AI reshapes inquiry and evaluation (Oliveira et al. 2025). From a Cognitive Load Theory perspective, reflective use serves to manage cognitive resources by reducing extraneous process­ ing and directing effort to meaningful engagement, whereas critical thinking operates within that allocated space to perform deeper evaluative operations (Sweller et al. 2011). 1.7 | Research Gap and Aim Existing research on AI in education provides valuable but fragmented insights, with most studies focusing on isolated outcomes rather than their interrelations (Liu et al. 2025). Empirical work also remains dominated by cross-­sectional designs, offering observations of immediate effects rather than trajectories of change over longer periods of time (Zhai et al. 2021). While there are first attempts at longitudinal perspectives, they rarely link adoption patterns of AI to core educational outcomes such as knowledge gain, motivation, or critical thinking (Polyportis 2024). Recent findings also chal­ lenge the assumption that stronger learners always benefit more from AI,",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"attempts at longitudinal perspectives, they rarely link adoption patterns of AI to core educational outcomes such as knowledge gain, motivation, or critical thinking (Polyportis 2024). Recent findings also chal­ lenge the assumption that stronger learners always benefit more from AI, suggesting that outcomes depend on motiva­ tional profiles, strategic behaviours, and reflective use (Zhao et al. 2025). This underscores the need for multidimensional, longitudinal research that captures how these dynamics evolve together across a semester. As a result, it remains un­ clear whether generative AI reinforces existing educational inequalities, or whether, under certain conditions, it can en­ hance more equitably and reflective learning opportunities (Gabriel 2024). In light of these gaps, the present study investigates how gener­ ative AI shapes learning dynamics in a real university course. To understand the true impact of AI, we must move beyond single measurement points and examine how learners develop across multiple psychological dimensions over time. Adopting a longitudinal quasi-­experimental design, this study exam­ ines knowledge gain, motivation, cognitive load, and critical thinking across three measurement points during a semester. Conducted within a practice-­integrated",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"points and examine how learners develop across multiple psychological dimensions over time. Adopting a longitudinal quasi-­experimental design, this study exam­ ines knowledge gain, motivation, cognitive load, and critical thinking across three measurement points during a semester. Conducted within a practice-­integrated study programme, where students alternate between academic coursework and professional practice, the study situates AI-­supported learn­ ing in a highly applied higher education context. By empir­ ically examining how generative AI interacts with multiple learning dimensions across a semester, this study contributes to the growing body of research on generative AI and dynamic learning processes in higher education, as addressed in this Special Issue. 1.8 | Research Questions and Hypotheses Based on this reasoning, the study formulates five research questions that reflect the multidimensional nature of learning with AI, including knowledge gain, motivation, cognitive load, critical thinking, and reflective use. The study addresses the fol­ lowing research questions: 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,3,"The study addresses the fol­ lowing research questions: 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",47
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"4 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 • RQ1: How does students' knowledge develop over the se­ mester, and to what extent do these changes differ between instructional conditions and as a function of prior achieve­ ment or prior AI experience? • RQ2: How does students' motivation change across the se­ mester, and does motivation predict knowledge gains? • RQ3: How do different dimensions of cognitive load (intrin­ sic, extraneous, germane) develop across the semester, and do they predict knowledge gains? • RQ4: How does students' critical thinking develop over the semester, and how is it associated with knowledge gain and cognitive load? • RQ5: How does students' reflective use of AI develop during the semester, and how is it associated with knowledge gain, cognitive load, and critical thinking? ­ 1 2 | Method 2.1 | Research Design This study used a quasi-­experimental longitudinal design with three measurement points during one semester. The re­ search was conducted within a practice-­integrated bachelor's program where students alternate between company-­based practical training and intensive nine-­week theory phases at the university. A",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"This study used a quasi-­experimental longitudinal design with three measurement points during one semester. The re­ search was conducted within a practice-­integrated bachelor's program where students alternate between company-­based practical training and intensive nine-­week theory phases at the university. A cohort of approximately 90 students is reg­ ularly divided into three parallel classes. Students were as­ signed to parallel classes by the program administration prior to the start of their studies according to standard organisa­ tional procedures of the degree program. Class assignment was not influenced by the research design or by students' prior achievement. Participants were then assigned to conditions based on their existing class membership, resulting in pre-­ existing class groups rather than random allocation. To assess potential pre-­existing differences between groups, baseline comparisons were conducted. No significant differences were found across groups for prior achievement, knowledge, moti­ vation, or prior AI experience. As the study was embedded in existing course structures, the sample size was determined by the available cohort and could not be adjusted for statistical considerations. To reduce potential differences across groups, the same instructor",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"vation, or prior AI experience. As the study was embedded in existing course structures, the sample size was determined by the available cohort and could not be adjusted for statistical considerations. To reduce potential differences across groups, the same instructor taught all classes using identical teaching materials. The lecture period of the theory semester consisted of 36 teaching units, delivered in four-­hour blocks over nine successive weeks. The task was implemented on a weekly basis throughout the nine-­week theory phase. It was completed during the final phase of each four-­hour lecture session under the same class­ room conditions. A 20 min per session were allocated for com­ pleting the task. The educator monitored compliance with the assigned condition but did not provide further support during the task completion process. No structured cross-­class collabo­ ration or shared discussion forum was implemented, ensuring that condition-­specific guidance remained strictly separated across groups. The instructional scenario aimed to enhance engagement with the course materials through weekly, individ­ ual, in-­class assignments. Students reviewed the lecture slides and additional resources provided by the instructor and were",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"ensuring that condition-­specific guidance remained strictly separated across groups. The instructional scenario aimed to enhance engagement with the course materials through weekly, individ­ ual, in-­class assignments. Students reviewed the lecture slides and additional resources provided by the instructor and were asked to generate one question with a correct answer based on the content, since student-­generated questioning is known to foster active engagement and deeper material processing (Wong et al. 2024). The submitted question-­answer pairs were made available to all three courses as a shared learning card set after the final lecture, ensuring equal preparation opportunities for the exam across conditions. The instructional approach was de­ signed to foster sustained engagement with the course content across all conditions. Generative AI was integrated into this process via an institution-­provided, data-­protection-­compliant interface for large language models. GPT-­4o was set as the default model, although the students could manually switch to GPT-­3.5-­turbo. The interface also allowed the upload of course materials in multiple file formats (e.g., PDF, Word, PowerPoint), which supported the tutor scenario by enabling students to directly query their own learning resources.",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"although the students could manually switch to GPT-­3.5-­turbo. The interface also allowed the upload of course materials in multiple file formats (e.g., PDF, Word, PowerPoint), which supported the tutor scenario by enabling students to directly query their own learning resources. No model updates or in­ terface changes occurred during the study period. Depending on condition, students could use the AI chatbot to support their question creation. Group A received explicit instructional in­ troduction to the AI-­as-­tutor approach, including structured guidance on effective prompting (role-­task-­output framing) and explicit contrasts between appropriate and inappropriate forms of generative AI regarding the task. The instructional materials emphasised reflective engagement, positioning AI as a learning partner intended to support rather than replace students' independent cognitive effort. This condition did not involve human-­in-­the-­loop scaffolding or additional individ­ ualised support. All interactions occurred only between stu­ dents and the AI tool. Group B (Control) completed the same tasks without AI support, serving as a baseline. Group C (un­ guided AI) had access to the same generative AI interface and was instructed to review, reflect, and revise AI-­generated",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"and the AI tool. Group B (Control) completed the same tasks without AI support, serving as a baseline. Group C (un­ guided AI) had access to the same generative AI interface and was instructed to review, reflect, and revise AI-­generated outputs, but received no tutor-­role framing or structured prompting guidance beyond the standard task instructions, representing a naturalistic and comparatively unstructured approach to AI-­supported learning. The guidance did not constitute a separate formal prompt engineering training pro­ gramme. The absence of such training reflects authentic con­ ditions in higher education, where students typically engage with AI tools through informal, trial-­and-­error approaches rather than structured instruction (Knoth et al. 2024), thereby enhancing the ecological validity of the findings. The com­ plete instructional materials for each condition are provided in Appendix A of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​ doi.​org/​10.​5281/​zenodo.​22228595). This design reflects prior research showing that guided, tutor-­ like AI interaction promotes reflective engagement (Yuan and Hu 2024), while meaningful integration into active learning tasks enhances motivation and classroom participation (Kestin et al. 2025). The deployment of the chatbot adhered to educa­",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,4,"prior research showing that guided, tutor-­ like AI interaction promotes reflective engagement (Yuan and Hu 2024), while meaningful integration into active learning tasks enhances motivation and classroom participation (Kestin et al. 2025). The deployment of the chatbot adhered to educa­ tors' concerns regarding privacy and ethical risks (Melanou et al. 2026) and followed established principles of transpar­ ency, accountability and fairness in educational AI integration (Contreras and Jaimes 2024). 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",108
