DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,6,"6 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 2.2 | Participants At the beginning of the semester 87 students (19 females, 68 males; age 18–29) from the Bachelor's programme in Business Informatics participated in the study. They were in their second semester and distributed across three parallel classes (A: 33, B: 28, C: 26). Age range was based on enrolment records, and individ­ ual age data were unavailable for calculating mean and standard deviation. ­ ­ ­ ­ 1 2 ­ 1 ­ ­ 2.3 | Materials and Learning Context The study was conducted during the second-­semester lecture IT-­ Fundamentals II, which built on IT-­Fundamentals I and covered core computer science topics. Materials and assignments were provided via Moodle. Weekly assignments included written in­ structions specifying the required AI use per condition. 2.4 | Procedure Data were collected at three times: at the beginning of the course (T1, first lecture), in the middle of the semester (T2, fourth lec­ ture), and at the final class before the revision session for the exam (T3, eighth lecture). At each point, students",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,6,"three times: at the beginning of the course (T1, first lecture), in the middle of the semester (T2, fourth lec­ ture), and at the final class before the revision session for the exam (T3, eighth lecture). At each point, students completed an­ onymised online questionnaires via LimeSurvey during class. The questionnaires included validated scales and theory-­based self-­developed items. In addition, knowledge tests were provided by the instructor. Details are described in the Measures section. Participation was voluntary and anonymous. To enable lon­ gitudinal matching of responses while ensuring anonymity, participants generated pseudonyms used across all measure­ ment points. To support engagement and reduce dropouts, a small reward was offered. After data collection, two amusement park tickets were raffled per class. 2.5 | Measures 2.5.1 | Final School Grade As an indicator of students' prior achievement and general ac­ ademic proficiency, their final secondary school grade was collected at T1. This variable served as a proxy to differentiate between higher-­ and lower-­performing students, which is par­ ticularly relevant for examining how initial academic level may impact future achievement patterns (Piñeiro et",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,6,"their final secondary school grade was collected at T1. This variable served as a proxy to differentiate between higher-­ and lower-­performing students, which is par­ ticularly relevant for examining how initial academic level may impact future achievement patterns (Piñeiro et al. 2019). Grades were reported according to the country's grading system (1 = ex­ cellent, 6 = insufficient). To align the variable with the logic that ­ 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",106
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,7,"7 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 higher values represent better achievement, the grades were reverse-­coded before inclusion in the analysis. 2.5.2 | AI Experience At T1, students were asked whether they had prior experience with AI tools (response options: yes/no). The purpose of this variable was to examine whether prior AI experience moderated the ef­ fects of the intervention. Prior experience has been discussed as an important factor in AI literacy (Laru et al. 2025), technology acceptance (Börekci and Çelik 2024), and educational applications of AI (Cervantes and Navarro 2025). However, because almost all students reported having AI experience (N = 79), the variables showed insufficient variance to be meaningfully analysed further. 2.5.3 | Knowledge Knowledge was assessed at T1 and T3 using one identical knowledge test developed by the instructor. The test consisted of 14 multiple true-­false items, each with five binary options per stem. For every option, students decided whether it applied (tick) or not (left unticked). Scoring awarded 1 point for each correct decision (ticked when true, left unticked when false) and 0 oth­",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,7,"true-­false items, each with five binary options per stem. For every option, students decided whether it applied (tick) or not (left unticked). Scoring awarded 1 point for each correct decision (ticked when true, left unticked when false) and 0 oth­ erwise. The complete scale items are provided in Appendix B of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​doi.​org/​10.​5281/​ze­ nodo.​22228595). Multiple true-­false formats enable fine-­grained measurement of students' factual and structural knowledge in higher education (Diekhoff 1984). These formats have also been shown to reflect students' true knowledge more reliably than traditional formats and to support detailed analysis of learning gains over time (Lahner et al. 2018). Each item could score 0 to 5 points, leading to a total maxi­ mum score of 70 points per test. Raw scores were then con­ verted to percentages (0–100%), which were used as variables Knowledge_1 (T1) and Knowledge_3 (T3). Knowledge Gain was operationalised as the difference between post-­test and pre-­test percentage scores (T3–T1). The items were designed to reflect the course content and in­ tended learning outcomes, in line with the principle of construc­ tive alignment",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,7,"and Knowledge_3 (T3). Knowledge Gain was operationalised as the difference between post-­test and pre-­test percentage scores (T3–T1). The items were designed to reflect the course content and in­ tended learning outcomes, in line with the principle of construc­ tive alignment between instruction and assessment (Biggs 1996). Questions were not simple recall tasks but required knowledge and understanding expected to be acquired over the lecture pe­ riod. Therefore, higher scores at T3 indicate that students had acquired more knowledge over the semester. Reliability was not computed for the knowledge test because the items covered distinct content areas and were designed to assess a broad range of factual knowledge rather than a single latent construct. This approach aligns with standard practice in multi­ ple true–false knowledge assessments (Diekhoff 1984). 2.5.4 | Motivation Motivation was measured with self-­developed items that were theoretically guided by established perspectives on educational TABLE 2 | Detailed information on sample sizes for each construct across available measurement points (T1, T2, T3) and conditions (Group A: Tutor-­AI, Group B: Control, Group C: Unguided AI). Construct (measurement point) N condition",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,7,"by established perspectives on educational TABLE 2 | Detailed information on sample sizes for each construct across available measurement points (T1, T2, T3) and conditions (Group A: Tutor-­AI, Group B: Control, Group C: Unguided AI). Construct (measurement point) N condition A N condition B N condition C N total Knowledge (T1) 33 27 21 81 Knowledge (T3) 29 20 13 62 Final school grade (T1) 32 27 20 79 AI experience (T1) 32 27 20 79 Motivation (T1) 32 27 20 79 Motivation (T2) 23 16 11 50 Motivation (T3) 27 17 12 56 Critical thinking (T2) 23 16 11 50 Critical thinking (T3) 27 17 12 56 ICL (T2) 22 16 11 49 ICL (T3) 27 17 12 56 ECL (T2) 15 11 7 33 ECL (T3) 27 17 12 56 GCL (T2) 21 16 11 48 GCL (T3) 26 17 12 55 Reflective use (T2) 23 16 11 50 Reflective use (T3) 27 17 12 56 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,7,"26 17 12 55 Reflective use (T2) 23 16 11 50 Reflective use (T3) 27 17 12 56 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",57
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"8 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 motivation, particularly Self-­Determination Theory (Deci and Ryan 1985), and by current discussions on human–AI interac­ tion in learning contexts. At T1, one negatively worded item was used to capture potential motivational loss due to the availability of AI tools (‘I lose the motivation to engage with the content myself because I know that AI can take over the tasks’). This item was reverse-­coded so that higher scores indicate higher motivation. The inclusion of this baseline item was intended to capture students' initial motivational attitude in AI-­influenced learning environment (Gerlich 2025). At T2 and T3, motivation was measured with a self-­developed four-­item scale. Two items captured intrinsic aspects (e.g., ‘I remain motivated even when a task is challenging’). This de­ sign reflects the principles of self-­determination theory, which emphasises intrinsic motivation as driven by autonomy and competence (Deci and Ryan 1985). Two further items measured potential motivational loss through AI tools (e.g., ‘I lose the learning motivation when support tools take over too much of the work’). Both items were negatively worded",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"driven by autonomy and competence (Deci and Ryan 1985). Two further items measured potential motivational loss through AI tools (e.g., ‘I lose the learning motivation when support tools take over too much of the work’). Both items were negatively worded and thus reverse-­ coded. The complete questionnaire instruments are provided in Appendix C of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​doi.​ org/​10.​5281/​zenodo.​22228595). All items used a 5-­point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree). The items were identical across interven­ tion and control groups. Where necessary, wording was slightly adapted to fit the group-­specific context, but all responses were merged for analysis. Internal consistencies were low (αt2 = 0.499; αt3 = 0.51). 2.5.5 | Cognitive Load Cognitive load was assessed using an adapted version of the validated German-­language multidimensional cognitive load questionnaire developed by Krieglstein et al. (2022), which is grounded in Cognitive Load Theory. The instrument differenti­ ates intrinsic cognitive load (ICL; 5 items), extraneous cognitive load (ECL; 5 items), and germane cognitive load (GCL; 7 items) and has demonstrated satisfactory psychometric quality. The complete questionnaire instruments are",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"is grounded in Cognitive Load Theory. The instrument differenti­ ates intrinsic cognitive load (ICL; 5 items), extraneous cognitive load (ECL; 5 items), and germane cognitive load (GCL; 7 items) and has demonstrated satisfactory psychometric quality. The complete questionnaire instruments are provided in Appendix C of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​doi.​org/​10.​5281/​ze­ nodo.​22228595). All items were rated on a 5-­point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree), and mean scores were computed for each subscale. Higher values indicate higher levels of the respective type of cognitive load. ICL items addressed the inherent difficulty and complexity of the content (e.g., The learning contents were complex ECL items, by contrast, focused on the unnecessary effort caused by the didactic design', It was difficult to stay focused on the content while working on the task). Finally, GCL items were designed to capture active processing and integration of knowl­ edge (e.g., ‘I achieved a comprehensive understanding of the learning content’). The items were administered identically across all groups, with only minor wording adaptations to fit the respective learning conditions. All responses were merged",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"active processing and integration of knowl­ edge (e.g., ‘I achieved a comprehensive understanding of the learning content’). The items were administered identically across all groups, with only minor wording adaptations to fit the respective learning conditions. All responses were merged for analysis. Cognitive load was assessed at T2 and T3. It was not measured at T1, as students had not yet engaged with the learning material at T1. Internal consistencies were acceptable to excellent across both measurement points: ICL (αt2 = 0.77; αt3 = 0.81), ECL (αt2 = 0.69; αt3 = 0.82), and GCL (αt2 = 0.87; αt3 = 0.86). 2.5.6 | Critical Thinking Critical thinking in the context of AI use was measured with self-­developed items, guided by existing frameworks of criti­ cal reflection and critical thinking in education (Ennis 2011; Facione 1990). Critical Thinking was not measured at T1, as students had not yet engaged with the course material at T1. It was assessed at T2 and T3 using a nine-­item self-­developed self-­report scale de­ signed to capture critical thinking processes when interacting with AI tools. Items addressed",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"T1, as students had not yet engaged with the course material at T1. It was assessed at T2 and T3 using a nine-­item self-­developed self-­report scale de­ signed to capture critical thinking processes when interacting with AI tools. Items addressed three aspects of critical think­ ing. The first aspect concerned the evaluation of AI outputs, captured with items such as ‘I recognise when AI-­generated text sounds plausible but is weak in content’. The second as­ pect captured reflective thinking and awareness of personal biases, with items such as ‘I know that my own assumptions influence how I evaluate AI responses’. This dimension aligns with Kember et al.'s (2000) questionnaire measuring reflective thinking as a critical component of higher-­order learning. The third aspect addressed inquiry and reasoning, exemplified by items like ‘I use AI responses as a starting point to develop my own arguments’. This reflects Kuhn et al. (2000) developmen­ tal perspective on critical thinking, which emphasises stages of epistemological understanding and the active construction of arguments. This design incorporates recent debates on how generative AI reshapes critical inquiry by",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"my own arguments’. This reflects Kuhn et al. (2000) developmen­ tal perspective on critical thinking, which emphasises stages of epistemological understanding and the active construction of arguments. This design incorporates recent debates on how generative AI reshapes critical inquiry by fostering sys­ tematic search for evidence and open-­mindedness (Oliveira et al. 2025), highlighting the importance of cultivating both cognitive skills and enduring critical dispositions. The com­ plete questionnaire instruments are provided in Appendix C of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​doi.​org/​10.​5281/​ zenodo.​22228595). All items were rated on a 5-­point Likert scale (1 = strongly dis­ agree, 5 = strongly agree). The items were identical across inter­ vention and control groups. Minor wording changes were made to fit the respective group context, but all responses were merged for analysis. Internal consistency was acceptable (αt2 = 0.76; αt3 = 0.63). 2.5.7 | Reflective Use Reflective use was measured using self-­developed items, in­ formed by foundational metacognitive theory (Flavell 1979). Reflective use was not assessed at T1 due to lack of prior course engagement. It was measured at T2 and T3 using a four-­item",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,8,"Use Reflective use was measured using self-­developed items, in­ formed by foundational metacognitive theory (Flavell 1979). Reflective use was not assessed at T1 due to lack of prior course engagement. It was measured at T2 and T3 using a four-­item self-­developed self-­report scale. One reverse-­coded item captured the tendency to adopt outputs without reflec­ tion (e.g., ‘I trust the answers of AI tools such as AI chatbots and adopt their outcomes uncritically into my own work’). The 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",116
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,9,"9 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 remaining items assessed source-­evaluation (e.g., ‘I regularly consider whether an AI-­generated response is based on reli­ able sources’), plausibility assessment (e.g., ‘Even when an AI response sounds convincing, I do not automatically trust it’), and active regulation (e.g., ‘I verify AI-­generated responses using other sources before incorporating them into my work’). Item wording was minimally adapted for intervention and control groups to ensure contextual fit. Tendency toward un­ critical trust have been identified as a key barrier to reflective information use (Metzger and Flanagin 2013). Accordingly, the scale incorporates elements of civic online reasoning, such as scepticism toward surface plausibility and the eval­ uation of source credibility (McGrew et al. 2018). The com­ plete questionnaire instruments are provided in Appendix C of the Supporting Information (DOI: https://​doi.​org/​10.​5281/​ zenodo.​22228595). All items were rated on a 5-­point Likert scale (1 = strongly dis­ agree, 5 = strongly agree). Mean scores were calculated for each time point, with higher values indicating more reflective use. The reliability was sufficient (αt2 = 0.77; αt3 = 0.74).",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,9,"rated on a 5-­point Likert scale (1 = strongly dis­ agree, 5 = strongly agree). Mean scores were calculated for each time point, with higher values indicating more reflective use. The reliability was sufficient (αt2 = 0.77; αt3 = 0.74). 2.6 | Data Analysis All statistical analyses were conducted using JASP (Version 0.95; JASP Team 2024). Descriptive statistics were computed for all variables. To address RQ1–RQ4, repeated-­measures ANOVAs tested changes across time and groups, and regression models examined predictors of knowledge gains. For RQ5, linear re­ gressions tested associations of reflective use with knowledge gain, cognitive load, and critical thinking. Exploratory moder­ ation and mediation analyses were performed using PROCESS for JASP (Hayes and Little 2022). Effect sizes are reported as η2 p for ANOVAs and as standardised coefficients (β) and explained variance (R2) for regression-­based models, with significance set at α = 0.05. The study included three instructional groups: a tutor-­AI con­ dition (Group A), an unguided AI condition (Group C), and a control condition without AI (Group B). For the analyses, group comparisons were handled flexibly depending on",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,9,"at α = 0.05. The study included three instructional groups: a tutor-­AI con­ dition (Group A), an unguided AI condition (Group C), and a control condition without AI (Group B). For the analyses, group comparisons were handled flexibly depending on the theoretical focus and available statistical power. When the distinction between guided and unguided AI use was concep­ tually relevant (e.g., when analysing how different interaction designs affect learning processes), all three groups were an­ alysed separately. In contrast, when the research focus was on the general presence versus absence of AI support, or when sample sizes were reduced due to participant attrition, Groups A and C were combined into a single ‘AI’ condition. Comparisons in the results therefore refer either to the three individual groups or to AI versus control conditions, as indi­ cated for each analysis. Given the cohort-­based design and resulting sample size, no a priori power analysis was conducted. Consequently, statisti­ cal power was limited, particularly in analyses that typically require larger samples, as well as in models based on reduced effective sample sizes due to",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,9,"cohort-­based design and resulting sample size, no a priori power analysis was conducted. Consequently, statisti­ cal power was limited, particularly in analyses that typically require larger samples, as well as in models based on reduced effective sample sizes due to complete-­case approaches and lon­ gitudinal attrition. 2.7 | Ethical Considerations As the study involved adult university students and anonymous data collection within a low-­risk educational setting, formal eth­ ical approval was not required according to institutional guide­ lines. Participants were informed about the purpose of the study, the voluntary nature of their participation, and the anonymous handling of their data prior to data collection. The study was conducted in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Generative AI was accessed via an institution-­ provided, data-­protection-­compliant interface hosted on serv­ ers within the European Union, operated in accordance with GDPR. User accounts were anonymised, and the research team did not access, store, or analyse individual prompts. 2.8 | Data Availability & Open Science ­ ­ 1 ­ ­ Supporting Information ­ https://​doi.​org/​10.​5281/​zenodo.​22228595 3 | Results ­ 3 ­ ­",180
