DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"association with GCL (B = 0.09, p = 0.461, β = 0.11), whereas condition had a significant negative effect (B = −0.50, p = 0.012), indicating higher GCL in the con­ trol group. H5d was not supported. 4 | Discussion This study examined how generative AI shapes learning dy­ namics in higher education. While all groups showed substan­ tial knowledge gains, the expected advantages of AI integration were not consistently observed. Motivation slightly declined, GCL increased, and critical thinking remained stable, high­ lighting the importance of instructional framing. These findings partly support theoretical expectations. The increase in GCL aligns with Cognitive Load Theory, suggesting that AI can foster deeper processing when used effectively. However, the decline in motivation challenges assumptions from Self-­Determination Theory, suggesting that access to AI alone may be insufficient to foster sustained engagement without pedagogical structur­ ing. Reflective use, as a metacognitive strategy, showed strong effects on learning outcomes, consistent with metacognitive the­ ory and recent work on AI literacy. Overall, the results suggest that AI's impact depends less on access and more on guidance and regulation,",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"Reflective use, as a metacognitive strategy, showed strong effects on learning outcomes, consistent with metacognitive the­ ory and recent work on AI literacy. Overall, the results suggest that AI's impact depends less on access and more on guidance and regulation, underscoring the need for scaffolded theory-­ informed integration of AI in higher education and contribut­ ing to current discussion on how generative AI shapes dynamic learning processes in higher education. Of the 16 hypotheses tested, six were supported and one par­ tially supported. Reflective use showed significant effects, while other higher-­order and motivational outcomes appeared less sensitive to AI integration than expected. 4.1 | Knowledge Gain and Matthew Effect Students in all groups improved their knowledge from T1 to T3, reflecting effective course design. However, AI integration did not lead to additional gains, as indicated by a non-­significant Time × Group interaction. Descriptively, the unguided AI group showed the largest gains but without statistical significance, un­ derscoring that technology alone is insufficient without peda­ gogical framing. Based on the Matthew effect literature (Stanovich 1986), which has been more broadly conceptualised",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"Group interaction. Descriptively, the unguided AI group showed the largest gains but without statistical significance, un­ derscoring that technology alone is insufficient without peda­ gogical framing. Based on the Matthew effect literature (Stanovich 1986), which has been more broadly conceptualised as a cumulative advan­ tage process (Rigney 2010), it was examined whether students with stronger prior academic achievement or prior AI experience would derive greater benefit from AI-­supported learning. Neither moderation reached significance, suggesting that the use of AI did not systematically benefit higher-­performing students. The absence of a Matthew effect may suggest that the learning task itself provided sufficient structure to support students regard­ less of their prior academic achievement or AI experience. While students with prior AI experience performed slightly better, the small number of AI-­inexperienced participants (N = 2) limits in­ terpretation. Overall, the findings highlight that instructional context and learner regulation may influence more than prior ability when integrating AI. As knowledge was assessed using an objective multiple true-­false format, performance was inde­ pendent of writing quality or stylistic expression. Despite the small effect size of",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"instructional context and learner regulation may influence more than prior ability when integrating AI. As knowledge was assessed using an objective multiple true-­false format, performance was inde­ pendent of writing quality or stylistic expression. Despite the small effect size of between-­group differences (Cohen's f = 0.18), the low implementation cost of AI integration within existing course structures may still render such differences education­ ally relevant in practice. 4.2 | Motivation Motivation remained largely stable from mid-­ to end-­semester, suggesting that the use of generative AI neither enhanced nor undermined students' motivation over time. Descriptively, the tutor-­AI group showed the most stable motivation with a slight increase, whereas both the unguided AI and control groups declined slightly. Although non-­significant, this trend aligns with Self-­Determination Theory (Deci and Ryan 2000), which suggests that supportive structures help maintain engagement. In contrast to H2b, motivation did not significantly predict students' knowledge gains. This may indicate that motivation influences learning indirectly via cognitive or metacognitive processes. The lack of stronger effects may reflect limited pedagogical fram­ ing. In the tutor AI condition, students were merely",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"motivation did not significantly predict students' knowledge gains. This may indicate that motivation influences learning indirectly via cognitive or metacognitive processes. The lack of stronger effects may reflect limited pedagogical fram­ ing. In the tutor AI condition, students were merely instructed to use AI as a tutor without additional scaffolding. For educa­ tional practice, this implies that simply providing AI tools may be insufficient to promote sustained motivation without addi­ tional pedagogical structuring. Sustained engagement requires explicit instructional design that connects AI use with learning goals and supports autonomy and competence development. These results reflect mixed findings from prior research. The absence of decline suggests that automation bias (Parasuraman and Riley 1997) and over-­reliance effects (Glikson and Woolley 2020) were limited, possibly due to students' digital competence. However, the lack of motivational gains indicates that autonomy supportive tools alone may not be sufficient (Boguslawski et al. 2025). 4.3 | Cognitive Load Cognitive load provided useful insights into students' processing demands. ICL remained stable across the semester, while ECL decreased slightly but non-­significantly, suggesting that AI tools did not substantially reduce",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,13,"not be sufficient (Boguslawski et al. 2025). 4.3 | Cognitive Load Cognitive load provided useful insights into students' processing demands. ICL remained stable across the semester, while ECL decreased slightly but non-­significantly, suggesting that AI tools did not substantially reduce task-­irrelevant effort such as inter­ preting outputs. This challenges assumptions that AI naturally reduces ECL. 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",94
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"14 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 GCL increased significantly from T2 to T3, indicating deeper engagement and productive cognitive effort over time. Although GCL is often conceptualised as being induced by instructional design (Leppink et al. 2014), it may also reflect the learner's allocation of cognitive resources toward deeper understanding. As the instructional design remained constant throughout the semester, the observed increase likely reflects growing familiarity with the learning task and deeper en­ gagement over time. This interpretation aligns with Cognitive Load Theory and recent work highlighting the potential of AI-­supported learning environments to stimulate elaboration and explanatory processing (Zammouri et al. 2024). As ECL showed a slight decreasing trend, students may have been able to allocate more cognitive resources to meaningful knowledge integration, consistent with the capacity-­reallocation princi­ ple of Cognitive Load Theory (Sweller et al. 2011). The GCL increase occurred across both conditions, suggesting that the effect reflects general learning progress rather than AI-­ specific benefits. Regression analyses showed that both GCL and ECL predicted higher knowledge gains, with GCL being the stronger predictor. This",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"2011). The GCL increase occurred across both conditions, suggesting that the effect reflects general learning progress rather than AI-­ specific benefits. Regression analyses showed that both GCL and ECL predicted higher knowledge gains, with GCL being the stronger predictor. This unexpected ECL effect may indicate that some perceived ‘extraneous’ demands, such as evaluating AI outputs, were cog­ nitively meaningful. No moderation by condition was found, possibly due to the ab­ sence of structured scaffolding. This underscores the need for instructional support to help students manage cognitive effort more effectively. 4.4 | Critical Thinking Critical thinking remained mostly stable across the semester, regardless of the AI use. This supports the theoretical view that higher-­order reasoning develops gradually and is unlikely to im­ prove without sustained feedback (Halpern 2013). While AI can foster autonomy and feedback, it may also reduce active reason­ ing when used without scaffolding (Hou et al. 2025). The ab­ sence of measurable gains therefore underscores the importance of integrating structured reflection and evaluation prompts to stimulate deeper reasoning when working with AI-­generated content. Critical thinking did not significantly",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"ing when used without scaffolding (Hou et al. 2025). The ab­ sence of measurable gains therefore underscores the importance of integrating structured reflection and evaluation prompts to stimulate deeper reasoning when working with AI-­generated content. Critical thinking did not significantly predict knowledge gains, nor was this relationship mediated by GCL. Although GCL was positively associated with knowledge gain, critical thinking did not significantly relate to GCL, and the indirect effect was nonsignificant. Given that GCL was assessed at T3 and knowledge gain was operationalised as the difference be­ tween T1 and T3 scores, both measures incorporate T3 data and were therefore not temporally separated. The direct path between GCL and knowledge gain therefore reflects a statisti­ cal association rather than temporal causality. These findings contrast with assumptions that deeper reasoning promotes elaboration (Entwistle 1991) and productive cognitive effort (Paas et al. 2003). A small negative trend with ECL may in­ dicate that reasoning skills help reduce distraction. However, the lack of association with GCL suggests that this cognitive efficiency does not necessarily translate into deeper learning engagement. Overall, these findings",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"al. 2003). A small negative trend with ECL may in­ dicate that reasoning skills help reduce distraction. However, the lack of association with GCL suggests that this cognitive efficiency does not necessarily translate into deeper learning engagement. Overall, these findings point to a potential gap between students' analytical awareness and their ability to regulate learning effectively. 4.5 | Reflective Use Reflective use showed only a slight increase from T2 to T3, al­ though this change was not statistically significant. However, overall levels were significantly higher in the AI condition com­ pared to the control group, which may suggest that access to AI is associated with more conscious engagement. While reflective use was unrelated to knowledge gain, it showed a significant association with critical thinking, sug­ gesting that students reporting higher levels of critical thinking also tended to engage more reflectively with the learning pro­ cess. In the present study, reflective use was conceptualised as a parallel metacognitive learning dimension instead of a medi­ ating mechanism between instructional condition and critical thinking. Future research could examine whether reflective engagement functions as",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"with the learning pro­ cess. In the present study, reflective use was conceptualised as a parallel metacognitive learning dimension instead of a medi­ ating mechanism between instructional condition and critical thinking. Future research could examine whether reflective engagement functions as a mediating process linking instruc­ tional design and higher-­order reasoning. Such work would further contribute to understanding the learning dynamics as­ sociated with generative AI-­supported learning environments. These findings are consistent with theoretical distinctions be­ tween metacognitive monitoring (Flavell 1979) and cognitive reasoning (Kuhn 2000), indicating that reflective use and criti­ cal thinking represent complementary but distinct higher-­order processes. Interestingly, the control group reported higher GCL than the AI condition, which may indicate differences in how students engaged with the learning material across conditions. At the same time, students in the AI condition reported higher levels of reflective use, suggesting increased metacognitive awareness. However, these differences did not translate into higher knowl­ edge gains or greater cognitive engagement. These findings highlight the importance of scaffolding reflective use to support its effectiveness as a learning strategy (Long and Magerko 2020). This",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"increased metacognitive awareness. However, these differences did not translate into higher knowl­ edge gains or greater cognitive engagement. These findings highlight the importance of scaffolding reflective use to support its effectiveness as a learning strategy (Long and Magerko 2020). This supports the idea that AI literacy must be addressed as a multidimensional educational objective, encompassing techni­ cal, cognitive, and ethical domains (Ng et al. 2021). This aligns with recent findings that highlight the need to promote AI liter­ acy not only at the student level. Teachers' own AI literacy is a key driver for innovation, but its effectiveness depends on suf­ ficient autonomy, peer support, and structural resources (Zhao and Huang 2025). 4.6 | Limitations and Future Research While the present study offers valuable insights into the longitu­ dinal dynamics of human-­AI interaction in authentic university contexts, several limitations should be acknowledged. A key limitation of the present study lies in its quasi-­ experimental design. Participants were assigned to conditions based on pre-­existing class groups rather than random alloca­ tion, which may introduce potential sources of bias at the class",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,14,"be acknowledged. A key limitation of the present study lies in its quasi-­ experimental design. Participants were assigned to conditions based on pre-­existing class groups rather than random alloca­ tion, which may introduce potential sources of bias at the class level and may limit the ability to fully attribute observed effects 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",90
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"15 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 to the experimental conditions. Although the same instructor, materials, and learning activities were used across conditions to reduce potential differences, unobserved factors such as peer dynamics, baseline engagement, scheduling, or cohort composi­ tion may have influenced the results independently of the exper­ imental manipulation. Therefore, causal interpretations should be made with caution, as class-­level influences could not be fully ruled out. A further limitation concerns the relatively high dropout rate across the three measurement points. Attrition was not equally distributed across conditions or measurement points. At T3, dropout rates relative to T1 were highest in Group C (38.1%) compared to Group B (25.9%) and Group A (12.1%), which may have introduced selection bias in between-­group comparisons. Despite offering small rewards, participation declined over time. To address missing data, only complete cases were included, which reduced statistical power and led some analyses to focus on comparisons between AI-­supported and control conditions rather than distinguishing between the two AI conditions. Such fluctuations are common in longitudinal field studies (Cohen et al. 2023). Despite",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"were included, which reduced statistical power and led some analyses to focus on comparisons between AI-­supported and control conditions rather than distinguishing between the two AI conditions. Such fluctuations are common in longitudinal field studies (Cohen et al. 2023). Despite this, the quasi-­experimental, repeated-­ measures design enhanced ecological validity by capturing au­ thentic classroom dynamics. Small and uneven group sizes further limited the detection of in­ teraction effects, particularly for motivational outcomes. Given the relatively small sample size and the analytical complexity of the study, statistical power was limited, especially for detect­ ing small to moderate interaction effects. More complex effects, such as interaction effects, typically require substantially larger samples to be detected reliably. Therefore, the absence of signif­ icant interaction effects should be interpreted with caution, as some effects may have remained undetected due to limited sta­ tistical power. This issue is particularly relevant for the analyses involving three-­group comparisons, as well as for moderation and mediation analyses, where statistical power was further reduced due to smaller effective sample sizes resulting from complete-­case analyses and longitudinal attrition. The study",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"This issue is particularly relevant for the analyses involving three-­group comparisons, as well as for moderation and mediation analyses, where statistical power was further reduced due to smaller effective sample sizes resulting from complete-­case analyses and longitudinal attrition. The study was also situated within a specific business informat­ ics program at a practice-­oriented university. While this ensured internal consistency, it may limit generalisability to other dis­ ciplines or institutional contexts. Replication across different disciplines, student populations, and institutional settings is therefore needed to assess the broader applicability of the find­ ings. Practice-­oriented students may be more experienced and self-­regulated, potentially resulting in ceiling effects that re­ duced observable AI-­related gains. Moreover, the intervention did not include explicit scaffolding or structured prompting, which may have limited the pedagogical impact of AI support. Although the study adopted a longitudinal design across one se­ mester, the semester in this practice-­integrated university model spans approximately 3 months of intensive coursework. While this timeframe allowed for repeated measurement within an au­ thentic instructional cycle, it may still be insufficient to capture long-­term development of higher-­order",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"se­ mester, the semester in this practice-­integrated university model spans approximately 3 months of intensive coursework. While this timeframe allowed for repeated measurement within an au­ thentic instructional cycle, it may still be insufficient to capture long-­term development of higher-­order competencies such as critical thinking or durable AI literacy. Skill transfer and stable regulatory patterns may unfold over longer periods of repeated AI exposure. Future research should therefore assess whether these dynamics extend beyond the present three-­month semes­ ter to regular academic semesters or repeated exposure across multiple semesters. Furthermore, the introduction of generative AI tools may have elicited novelty-­related influences or Hawthorne effects. Increased attention, curiosity, or altered engagement patterns could partly reflect engagement in an innovative instructional setting, rather than stable pedagogical impact. As generative AI becomes more common in higher education, such novelty ef­ fects may fade. Replication across cohorts and institutional con­ texts is therefore necessary to distinguish temporary reactivity from sustained learning dynamics. Some constructs, including motivation, critical thinking, and reflective use, relied on self-­report data. The motivation scale showed relatively low internal consistency (α",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"Replication across cohorts and institutional con­ texts is therefore necessary to distinguish temporary reactivity from sustained learning dynamics. Some constructs, including motivation, critical thinking, and reflective use, relied on self-­report data. The motivation scale showed relatively low internal consistency (α ≈ 0.50), likely re­ flecting the short, self-­developed nature of the measure. Such brief scales can capture diverse motivational facets, lowering reliability despite theoretical coherence. Interestingly, both GCL and ECL were positively associated with knowledge gains. This unexpected pattern may reflect productive challenge rather than distraction and should be examined further using combined be­ havioural and process-­based measures. Future research should extend this longitudinal design across multiple courses, semesters, and institutions to strengthen external validity and trace long-­term learning trajectories. In addition, future studies should investigate potential process mechanisms linking instructional design and higher-­order learning outcomes. Designs with temporally separated mea­ surements may help clarify whether metacognitive processes such as reflective engagement mediate the relationship be­ tween AI-­supported learning environments and critical think­ ing. Further work should test how structured prompting, metacognitive guidance, and reflective scaffolding can trans­ form manageable",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"mea­ surements may help clarify whether metacognitive processes such as reflective engagement mediate the relationship be­ tween AI-­supported learning environments and critical think­ ing. Further work should test how structured prompting, metacognitive guidance, and reflective scaffolding can trans­ form manageable cognitive load into deeper learning outcomes. Designing adaptive support systems that help students regulate cognitive effort may enhance both learning efficiency and en­ gagement. More broadly, embedding AI literacy into curricula is essential, as effective AI-­supported learning requires not just access to tools, but also pedagogical design and sustained insti­ tutional support. 5 | Conclusion Despite these limitations, the study provides longitudinal ev­ idence on how generative AI interacts with key psychological dynamics of learning in higher education. In the present quasi-­ experimental study, findings do not support the view of gener­ ative AI as a static cognitive enhancer. Its educational impact appears embedded within interacting motivational, cognitive, and metacognitive dimensions. The findings highlight that gen­ erative AI does not automatically enhance learning outcomes, but depends on instructional framing and learners' metacogni­ tive regulation. Conceptually, this study advances human-­AI interaction",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,15,"educational impact appears embedded within interacting motivational, cognitive, and metacognitive dimensions. The findings highlight that gen­ erative AI does not automatically enhance learning outcomes, but depends on instructional framing and learners' metacogni­ tive regulation. Conceptually, this study advances human-­AI interaction re­ search by positioning generative AI as an embedded component within dynamic learning systems. The findings suggest that the 13652729, 2026, 5, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jcal.70322 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",98
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,16,"16 of 18 Journal of Computer Assisted Learning, 2026 educational value of generative AI is not inherent to the tech­ nology itself, but is contingent upon the broader instructional and regulatory context in which it is embedded. In particular, the absence of consistent AI-­specific effects across conditions underscores that without intentional scaffolding, learners may not fully benefit from the cognitive and metacognitive affor­ dances of AI tools. Integrating Cognitive Load Theory, Self-­ Determination Theory, and metacognitive perspectives within a longitudinal design, the study suggests that AI-­related learn­ ing effects emerge from evolving interaction processes rather than technological access alone. By empirically tracing these multidimensional developmental patterns across a semester, the findings contribute to a process-­oriented perspective of AI-­ supported learning. This dynamic perspective reframes AI not as an independent driver of educational improvement, but as a co-­regulatory element whose impact depends on how cognitive effort, motivation, and reflective regulation are dynamically integrated within authentic instructional contexts. By analyti­ cally distinguishing motivational, cognitive, and metacognitive dimensions, the study shows that these layers of learning evolve in distinct but connected ways, thereby",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,16,"depends on how cognitive effort, motivation, and reflective regulation are dynamically integrated within authentic instructional contexts. By analyti­ cally distinguishing motivational, cognitive, and metacognitive dimensions, the study shows that these layers of learning evolve in distinct but connected ways, thereby advancing a systemic understanding of human-­AI interaction in higher education. This multidimensional and longitudinal perspective advances current debates on human–AI interaction by embedding ob­ served learning dynamics in established theory and authentic classroom contexts. In line with the focus of this Special Issue, the findings further contribute to a deeper understanding of how generative AI can be integrated into higher education as part of evolving learn­ ing processes rather than as a standalone technological inter­ vention. As generative AI becomes increasingly embedded in educational practice, the central question shifts from whether AI enhances learning to under what conditions, and through which regulatory processes its integration becomes education­ ally meaningful. Author Contributions Chrysanthi Melanou: conceptualization, investigation, writing – original draft, methodology, validation, visualization, software, formal analysis, project administration, data curation, resources, supervision. Maik Beege: writing – review and editing, supervision, methodology,",180
DOC_004,Computer Assisted Learning - 2026 - Melanou - Generative AI and Learning Dynamics in Higher Education  A Longitudinal.pdf,10.1002/jcal.70322,16,"which regulatory processes its integration becomes education­ ally meaningful. Author Contributions Chrysanthi Melanou: conceptualization, investigation, writing – original draft, methodology, validation, visualization, software, formal analysis, project administration, data curation, resources, supervision. Maik Beege: writing – review and editing, supervision, methodology, software, conceptualization. Martin Kimmig: conceptualization, investigation. Acknowledgments Open Access funding enabled and organized by Projekt DEAL. Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Peer Review For transparency, the peer review documents associated with this arti­ cle are available at https://​doi.​",104
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"IEICE TRANS. INF. & SYST., VOL.E109–D, NO.7 JULY 2026 1003 PAPER Special Section on Advancements in Information and Communication Technologies for a Hyperconnected Society Facilitating Student Engagement and Learning Outcomes with GPT-Based Chatbots: Evidence from a Randomized Controlled Trial in Afghanistan Jawad Ahmad HAQBEEN†a), Soﬁa SAHAB†, Nonmembers, and Takayuki ITO†, Senior Member SUMMARY The integration of Generative AI (GenAI) technologies, such as ChatGPT, into online education is rapidly advancing; however, their eﬀectiveness in under-resourced contexts remains underexplored. This study examines the impact of a Large Language Model (LLM)-based conver- sational agent on student engagement and learning outcomes in Afghanistan, where access to formal education—particularly for women—is severely re- stricted. We conducted a two-day experiment with 80 undergraduate com- puter science students (40 male, 40 female), randomly assigned to control or treatment groups. All students attended identical 50-minute online lec- tures followed by 40-minute post-lecture discussions moderated by a human instructor. In the treatment condition, these discussions were co-facilitated by a GPT-4–based chatbot in addition to the human instructor. Self-report questionnaires were administered to subjectively assess students’ percep- tions of learning",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"followed by 40-minute post-lecture discussions moderated by a human instructor. In the treatment condition, these discussions were co-facilitated by a GPT-4–based chatbot in addition to the human instructor. Self-report questionnaires were administered to subjectively assess students’ percep- tions of learning outcomes, while post-discussion logs were analyzed to objectively evaluate interaction patterns and engagement levels. Results showed that the treatment group demonstrated signiﬁcantly higher partici- pation, with more posts and replies during discussions, and reported greater conﬁdence in understanding the course material presented during the online lectures. These ﬁndings highlight the potential of GPT-based chatbots to enhance interactive learning and promote educational inclusion, particularly for marginalized populations in low-resource environments. key words: AI in online education, AI-mediated discussions, AI tools, ChatGPT, GPT-based agents, engagement, learning outcome, Afghanistan 1. Introduction The popularity of online learning has grown signiﬁcantly worldwide in recent years [1]. This shift has been driven by several key factors, including limited space in traditional classrooms, rising educational costs, and an increasing de- mand for ﬂexible learning options [2]. External factors such as the COVID-19 pandemic have further accelerated the",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"[1]. This shift has been driven by several key factors, including limited space in traditional classrooms, rising educational costs, and an increasing de- mand for ﬂexible learning options [2]. External factors such as the COVID-19 pandemic have further accelerated the adoption of virtual education [3], [8]. In some countries, such as Afghanistan, internal conditions—particularly cul- tural barriers and technological constraints —have also re- stricted access to traditional, in-person education [4]. Since the Taliban regained control in 2021, girls and women in Afghanistan have been prohibited from attending sec- ondary and higher education institutions [4], contributing to a widening gender gap in learning outcomes. Recent sur- veys conﬁrm that female students are falling signiﬁcantly behind their male peers in both achievement and access to education [6], reﬂecting long-standing patterns of gender in- equality in the country [7]. Manuscript received May 7, 2025. Manuscript publicized February 3, 2026. †Kyoto University, Kyoto-shi, 606–8501 Japan. a) E-mail: jawad.haqbeen@i.kyoto-u.ac.jp DOI: 10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009 On the technological side, many households lack re- liable internet access, necessary digital devices, and stable electricity. As one study notes, “digital literacy and",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"2025. Manuscript publicized February 3, 2026. †Kyoto University, Kyoto-shi, 606–8501 Japan. a) E-mail: jawad.haqbeen@i.kyoto-u.ac.jp DOI: 10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009 On the technological side, many households lack re- liable internet access, necessary digital devices, and stable electricity. As one study notes, “digital literacy and lim- ited access to the internet and smartphones are prominent constraints to online digital education” in Afghanistan [4]. In Afghanistan, severe restrictions on girls’ access to traditional, in-person education have pushed many girls to- ward online classes, commonly delivered via onlime com- munication platforms such as Zoom. However, these envi- ronments frequently involve large class sizes, driven by the shortage of formal schooling options, and limited teaching resources and the high volume of learners competing for lim- ited online opportunities. This situation creates an overload in which a single instructor may be responsible for manag- ing extremely large virtual classes—sometimes exceeding one thousand students. Research has shown that increasing class size tends to reduce the quality of interaction [10], negatively aﬀect- ing student satisfaction and perceived learning [11]. Rus- sell and Curtis, reported that students and teachers perceive less meaningful",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"exceeding one thousand students. Research has shown that increasing class size tends to reduce the quality of interaction [10], negatively aﬀect- ing student satisfaction and perceived learning [11]. Rus- sell and Curtis, reported that students and teachers perceive less meaningful engagement in large-scale online language courses than in smaller ones [11]. A lack of meaningful peer and instructor interaction is consistently associated with re- duced student engagement and learning outcomes [12]. In- teractivity in online education—deﬁned as the dynamic ex- change among students, instructors, and course content—is therefore critical [13]. Eﬀective interactivity typically oc- curs across three domains: (1) student–student, (2) student– instructor, and (3) student–content [13]. In this context, online platforms have emerged as a vital alternative, oﬀering a “safe” space where students— particularly women—can continue their education despite systemic barriers [5]. According to the Community of In- quiry (CoI) framework by Garrison et al. [17], teaching presence—achieved through thoughtful instructional design and direct instruction—plays a critical role in enhancing student engagement and knowledge retention. However, large-scale online classes often pose challenges for human instructors in maintaining interactivity, especially",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,1,"(CoI) framework by Garrison et al. [17], teaching presence—achieved through thoughtful instructional design and direct instruction—plays a critical role in enhancing student engagement and knowledge retention. However, large-scale online classes often pose challenges for human instructors in maintaining interactivity, especially in post- lecture discussions. With limited capacity to interact per- sonally with each learner, instructors may struggle to main- tain student interest and involvement. In addition, the social presence component of the CoI framework—which empha- sizes emotional expression, open communication, and group cohesion—is often weakened in these large, impersonal set- Copyright © 2026 The Institute of Electronics, Information and Communication Engineers",101
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"1004 IEICE TRANS. INF. & SYST., VOL.E109–D, NO.7 JULY 2026 tings. One promising approach to improving interactivity in online platform is the integration of AI-powered conver- sational agents, or chatbots [14], [26]. These agents can answer questions, guide learners, and encourage participa- tion in ways that scale across large class sizes. Recent ad- vancements in Generative AI (GenAI), such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT [15], have signiﬁcantly enhanced the capabilities of these chatbots, enabling them to process complex queries and produce human-like responses [16]. A notable exam- ple is “Jill Watson,” an AI teaching assistant introduced in a MOOC setting, which leveraged AI to answer frequently asked questions based on course materials such as syllabi, slides, and lecture notes [18]. Similarly, Khan Academy’s Khanmigo, a GPT-4-based virtual tutor, oﬀers real-time ed- ucational support to learners [19]. Prior research suggests that AI-based chatbots can pos- itively aﬀect student learning outcome and engagement. For example, one study found that students supported by AI chat- bots outperformed those assisted by human tutors in engage- ment and task completion [20]. However, heavy reliance on AI-based technology",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"can pos- itively aﬀect student learning outcome and engagement. For example, one study found that students supported by AI chat- bots outperformed those assisted by human tutors in engage- ment and task completion [20]. However, heavy reliance on AI-based technology can also harm learning process. For instance, Bastani et al. conducted a ﬁeld experiment with nearly 1,000 high school students to assess GPT-4’s impact on mathematics learning. They compared two AI tutor conditions—GPT Base (standard ChatGPT interface) and GPT Tutor (prompt-engineered for learning support)— integrated into about 15% of classroom activities. Although both improved short-term performance (by 48% and 127%, respectively), students who later lost access to the AI per- formed worse than peers who never used it, indicating po- tential risks of longer-term dependency [61]. Building on literature primarily conducted in devel- oped contexts that emphasizes convenience and eﬃciency, this study examines GPT-based facilitation in Afghanistan’s higher education setting—a context characterized by gender- based participation restrictions, limited instructor availabil- ity, large online class sizes, and weak connectivity among students, instructors, and instructional content. Our study advances this line of",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"this study examines GPT-based facilitation in Afghanistan’s higher education setting—a context characterized by gender- based participation restrictions, limited instructor availabil- ity, large online class sizes, and weak connectivity among students, instructors, and instructional content. Our study advances this line of research by deploying GPT-4 as a conversational agent in an educational setting. Speciﬁcally, we employ a GPT-based chatbot—not the stan- dard ChatGPT interface, but a few-shot, prompt-engineered system designed for learning support—as a discussion fa- cilitator to enhance student engagement and learning out- comes in online courses. We use GPT-4 as the foundational LLM and tailor it for post-lecture facilitation—particularly its ability to (a) enable safe and anonymous participation for female students, (b) complement limited human moder- ation capacity, and (c) sustain discussion continuity despite infrastructural constraints. Speciﬁcally, we evaluate whether a GPT-based chatbot (few shot and prompt-engineered for learning support), when deployed alongside a human instructor, can improve student participation and learning in an Afghanistan context. To our knowledge, this is among the ﬁrst rigorous trials exploring the eﬀectiveness of GPT-based facilitation in a low-resource, and constrained setting such",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"support), when deployed alongside a human instructor, can improve student participation and learning in an Afghanistan context. To our knowledge, this is among the ﬁrst rigorous trials exploring the eﬀectiveness of GPT-based facilitation in a low-resource, and constrained setting such as Afghanistan. The study responds to the following research question (RQ): RQ: Can a GPT-4-powered chatbot (few shot and prompt-engineered for learning support), working in tan- dem with a human instructor, enhance student participation and learning outcomes during online education? To address this question, we proposed the following hypothesis (H): H: The presence of a GPT-based chatbot (few shot and prompt-engineered for learning support), in combina- tion with a human instructor in post-lecture discussion, will result in higher levels of student participation and perceived learning compared to discussions facilitated by a human in- structor alone. To test this hypothesis, we designed and implemented a randomized controlled trial (RCT) involving 80 undergrad- uate students in Afghanistan. Participants were divided into two groups: a treatment group that engaged in discussions facilitated by both a chatbot and a human instructor, and a control",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"designed and implemented a randomized controlled trial (RCT) involving 80 undergrad- uate students in Afghanistan. Participants were divided into two groups: a treatment group that engaged in discussions facilitated by both a chatbot and a human instructor, and a control group that interacted only with a human instructor. Self-report questionnaires were administered to sub- jectively assess students’ perceptions of learning outcomes, while post-discussion logs were analyzed to objectively eval- uate interaction patterns and engagement levels. Our ﬁnd- ings suggests that treatment group demonstrated signiﬁcantly higher participation, with more posts and replies during dis- cussions, and reported greater conﬁdence in understanding the course material presented during the online lectures. These ﬁndings highlight the potential of GPT-based chat- bots to enhance interactive learning and promote educational inclusion, particularly for marginalized populations in low- resource environments. It also highlights the potential for AI-mediated educational services to support marginalized learners, such as Afghan women, in contexts where tradi- tional education is severely restricted. The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section 2 reviews related research on role of AI-assisted chatbots to support",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,2,"educational services to support marginalized learners, such as Afghan women, in contexts where tradi- tional education is severely restricted. The remainder of this paper is structured as follows: Section 2 reviews related research on role of AI-assisted chatbots to support online discussion. Section 3 outlines our methodology, including sampling, experimental design, and analysis methods. Section 4 presents the results across experimental conditions. Section 5 discusses our ﬁndings, acknowledges study limitations, and Sect. 6 conclude our paper, and proposes directions for future research. 2. Related Work 2.1 Conversational Agents as Support Tools in the Educa- tional Domain Supporting teaching and learning through technology has a long history [9], [21], [22], with increasing attention to in- teractive agent technologies as scalable support tools in edu- cation [23]. Interactive agents are computer systems—often powered by artiﬁcial intelligence (AI)—capable of engaging in natural, adaptive exchanges through language or multi- modal inputs [24]. They are designed to respond, adapt, and",155
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"HAQBEEN et al.: FACILITATING STUDENT ENGAGEMENT AND LEARNING OUTCOMES WITH GPT-BASED CHATBOTS 1005 at times initiate communication in ways that are context- aware and pedagogically purposeful [25]–[27]. The evolution of conversational agents has been shaped by eﬀorts to overcome the rigidity and limited adaptivity of early systems. Early rule-based chatbots such as ELIZA (1966) [28] and ALICE (1995) [29] demonstrated the fea- sibility of automated conversation but lacked the ability to interpret nuance or sustain meaningful reasoning. These lim- itations motivated the creation of more sophisticated systems such as AutoTutor [30], which holding a natural language conversation with a student to facilitate learning. The transition from rule-based tutoring to generative AI and large language models (LLMs) was driven by the ped- agogical need for systems that could handle open-ended, context-rich communication, oﬀering ﬂexible and coher- ent responses beyond the scope of domain-speciﬁc scripts. This evolution reﬂects an enduring challenge in education— maintaining learner engagement through personalized, re- sponsive interaction that scales across diverse contexts. Over the past decade, a range of conversational agents has been investigated in education [14], [26],",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"of domain-speciﬁc scripts. This evolution reﬂects an enduring challenge in education— maintaining learner engagement through personalized, re- sponsive interaction that scales across diverse contexts. Over the past decade, a range of conversational agents has been investigated in education [14], [26], including text- based chatbots (e.g., ChatGPT [15]), voice assistants (e.g., Siri, Alexa, Google Assistant [41], [42]), and intelligent tu- toring systems that provide adaptive feedback. Across stud- ies, integrating these tools is reported as a scalable means of supporting both learners and instructors, particularly by extending feedback, facilitation, and access to help [38]. A notable example is Jill Watson [18], an AI teach- ing assistant developed to support MOOCs by responding to frequently asked student questions and reducing instruc- tor workload. Since then, conversational agents have been deployed across diverse instructional settings [31]–[35], en- hancing engagement in ﬂipped and blended classrooms through personalized feedback, discussion facilitation, and peer collaboration [36]. These approaches aim to address persistent engagement barriers in online education, such as limited immediacy, social isolation, and uneven instructor attention—issues that traditional learning management sys- tems often fail to",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"classrooms through personalized feedback, discussion facilitation, and peer collaboration [36]. These approaches aim to address persistent engagement barriers in online education, such as limited immediacy, social isolation, and uneven instructor attention—issues that traditional learning management sys- tems often fail to resolve [39], [40]. A recent systematic review [37] identiﬁed three primary domains where AI-powered chatbots provide educational beneﬁts: (1) assisting with study organization, (2) enhanc- ing learning experiences, and (3) developing cognitive skills such as questioning and argumentation. Collectively, this evidence underscores the growing recognition of conversa- tional AI as a mechanism to sustain interaction and motiva- tion in online learning environments. While much of this research has been conducted in well- resourced contexts, the present study extends this line of inquiry to low-resource settings, speciﬁcally Afghanistan’s higher education system. Such contexts face unique ped- agogical and infrastructural challenges—unstable internet connectivity, high instructor workloads, and gendered par- ticipation restrictions—that exacerbate disengagement. At the same time, these challenges create distinct opportuni- ties for GPT-based facilitation to enable safe, anonymous, and continuous interaction, complementing limited human moderation capacity. In this way, low-resource",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"connectivity, high instructor workloads, and gendered par- ticipation restrictions—that exacerbate disengagement. At the same time, these challenges create distinct opportuni- ties for GPT-based facilitation to enable safe, anonymous, and continuous interaction, complementing limited human moderation capacity. In this way, low-resource environ- ments represent not peripheral cases but crucial testbeds for evaluating how conversational AI can foster inclusion and resilience in education. 2.2 Extending Prior Work through GPT-Based Chatbot Fa- cilitation Building upon this literature, our study advances prior re- search by deploying GPT-4 as a few-shot, prompt-engineered conversational facilitator for post-lecture discussions in Afghanistan’s higher-education setting. Unlike general- purpose interfaces such as ChatGPT, our system was cus- tomized to support learning discussions by: (a) enabling safe and pseudonymous participation for female students; (b) complementing limited human instructor presence; and (c) maintaining post-lecture discussion continuity despite in- frastructural instability. To ensure safety and inclusivity, participants were as- signed pseudonymous IDs encoding gender, group (e.g., B3), and within-group identiﬁers (e.g., 8)—for example, Far- waB38 in Fig. 3. The AI facilitator operated alongside a human instructor, actively moderating and sustaining par- ticipation during",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"safety and inclusivity, participants were as- signed pseudonymous IDs encoding gender, group (e.g., B3), and within-group identiﬁers (e.g., 8)—for example, Far- waB38 in Fig. 3. The AI facilitator operated alongside a human instructor, actively moderating and sustaining par- ticipation during post-lecture discussions. This design di- rectly addresses the moderation and participation gap that human-only facilitation faces in large, resource-constrained classrooms. Our approach integrates both behavioral (objective) and attitudinal (subjective) measures—such as participation vol- ume, message content, and learner conﬁdence—this study provides triangulated evidence of how GPT-based facilita- tion can enhance engagement and learning outcomes in low- resource settings.. Theoretically, this work aligns with the Community of Inquiry (CoI) framework [17]: (a) Teach- ing presence is strengthened through continuous moderation and instructional scaﬀolding; (b) Social presence through inclusive, low-risk engagement opportunities—particularly for female and quieter students; and (c) Cognitive presence through reﬂection, questioning, and knowledge integration. By doing this, our study not only ﬁlls a gap in low- resource AI-mediated learning research but also demon- strates how conversational AI can extend human teaching capacity to promote equity, engagement, and sustainability in",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,3,"reﬂection, questioning, and knowledge integration. By doing this, our study not only ﬁlls a gap in low- resource AI-mediated learning research but also demon- strates how conversational AI can extend human teaching capacity to promote equity, engagement, and sustainability in resource-constrained higher education enviroments. 3. Methodology 3.1 Study Area and Participants The study was conducted with 80 undergraduate students, were joining remotely from various locations and enrolled in a two-day online workshop-style course titled “The Role of Information in Society.” Participants (average age ∼19 years) were all undergraduate Computer Science majors in Afghanistan. The recruited subjects (40 female, 40 male; age ∼19 years) come from a pool of 3,432 qualiﬁed appli- cants (out of 3,761 who initially registered) from Afghanistan",120
