DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,4,"met eligibility criteria (e.g. English proﬁciency and internet access). To ensure fairness in group allocation, we employed stratiﬁed random sampling: students were ﬁrst stratiﬁed by gender, then randomly assigned to one of two experimental conditions (Control vs. Treatment) with equal gender repre- sentation in each. This process yielded two groups of 40 (20 females and 20 males per group). The course was delivered entirely online via Zoom for the lecture components and via a custom discussion plat- form for the post-lecture discussions (described below). Two experienced instructors (each holding a Ph.D. and having >2 years online teaching experience) delivered the lectures. Both instructors used an identical slide deck and coordinated lesson plans to ensure a uniform learning experience across the two conditions. The medium of instruction was English. Figure 1 depicts the recruitment and assignment ﬂow. Eighty students were randomly allocated to two conditions (control vs. treatment; 40 each). For the post-lecture dis- cussions, each condition was subdivided into four groups of ten students (A1–A4 for the control condition and B1–B4 for the treatment condition). Using stratiﬁed random sam- pling,",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,4,"allocated to two conditions (control vs. treatment; 40 each). For the post-lecture dis- cussions, each condition was subdivided into four groups of ten students (A1–A4 for the control condition and B1–B4 for the treatment condition). Using stratiﬁed random sam- pling, we balanced gender within every group (5 females, 5 males). All participants provided informed consent before taking part in the study. 3.1.1 Attendance and Attrition On Day 1, the control group had 36 participants (two male and two female students were absent due to technical or per- sonal issues), while all treatment-group students attended. On Day 2, the control group included 38 participants (one male and one female absent), and one female student was absent from the treatment group. Absences were single-day cases—students who missed Day 1 participated on Day 2, and vice versa. All participants from both conditions com- pleted the post-intervention self-report questionnaire. For interaction analyses, we include only present students per day/condition (see Figs. 6, 7, 8, and 9). In Afghanistan context, where internet connectivity is unstable, the local participant-recruitment partner ensured Wi-Fi access to support reliable",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,4,"pleted the post-intervention self-report questionnaire. For interaction analyses, we include only present students per day/condition (see Figs. 6, 7, 8, and 9). In Afghanistan context, where internet connectivity is unstable, the local participant-recruitment partner ensured Wi-Fi access to support reliable student participation. Dis- cussion continuity was further safeguarded by the AI facili- tator’s time-based moderation, which advanced sessions ac- cording to the planned schedule even when individual partic- ipants experienced brief disconnections. Sessions were de- livered synchronously, with asynchronous fallback for those who missed the live discussion: students could rejoin later and review the full message history, preserving continuity and access to all post-lecture communications. Together, these design choices mitigated infrastructural volatility and helped maintain consistent engagement across cohorts. 3.2 Experimental Design We conducted a randomized controlled trial (RCT), and par- ticipants were randomly assigned to either a control or treat- ment condition [44]. Gender inequality in Afghanistan is a central motiva- tion of this study. To reﬂect this context and to test the our proposed appraoch in a mixed-gender learning environment, we conducted the experiment in a coeducational setting",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,4,"treat- ment condition [44]. Gender inequality in Afghanistan is a central motiva- tion of this study. To reﬂect this context and to test the our proposed appraoch in a mixed-gender learning environment, we conducted the experiment in a coeducational setting that approximates pre-2021 Afghan higher-education conditions. Historically, coeducation in Afghanistan dates to the 1920s and was formally introduced at the university level in the late 1970s [62]. In coeducation (mixed-gender or mixed-sex education), female and male students learn together under the same conditions, sharing resources, facilities, and edu- cational experiences equally [63]. Evidence from a study conducted in Afghanistan sug- gests that students in higher-education institutions hold pos- itive attitudes toward coeducation, attributing to it personal and social beneﬁts such as improved gender equality, and reduction of gender bias and stereotypes [62]. Because co- education is widely recognized as a necessary condition for advancing gender equality, our design deliberately adopted a gender-balanced sample and a coeducational classroom structure. Accordingly, our intervention design was imple- mented with balanced female and male participation to pro- mote equality and mutual respect. Each condition",180
