DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,5,"pairs derived from lecture content and aligned with the discussion guidelines. In the treatment condition, an AI facilitator was inte- grated alongside the instructor to support and stimulate the conversation. In contrast, the control condition relied solely on human facilitation by the instructor. This setup ensured that all participants took part in an online discussion follow- ing the lecture, with the key distinction being the presence or absence of AI facilitation. To enhance the consistency and instructional quality of the agent’s interventions, we employed a controlled prompt adaptation strategy [47]. Rather than allowing GPT-4 to generate facilitation content entirely from scratch, we cu- rated a predeﬁned set of 17 human-authored facilitation messages, which served as the core of the chatbot’s inter- action library. These messages were categorized into three facilitation phases during 40-minute post-lecture discussions phases, as outlined below 1. Key Point Generation (15 minutes): Participants were encouraged—by the human facilitator (instructor) and/or the AI agent—to identify and articulate the most signiﬁcant concepts presented in the lecture. 2. Bridging Theory with Practice (15 minutes): Partic- ipants were encouraged—by the facilitator",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,5,"Key Point Generation (15 minutes): Participants were encouraged—by the human facilitator (instructor) and/or the AI agent—to identify and articulate the most signiﬁcant concepts presented in the lecture. 2. Bridging Theory with Practice (15 minutes): Partic- ipants were encouraged—by the facilitator and/or the AI agent—to connect lecture content with real-world contexts or personal experiences. 3. Summary and Forward Reﬂection (10 minutes): Partic- ipants were encouraged—by the facilitator and/or the AI agent—to summarize the discussion, extract key insights, and oﬀer feedback or suggestions for future topics. These sessions corresponded to the temporal structure of post-lecture discussions. During the discussion sessions, the chatboteither posted one of predeﬁned facilitation messages verbatim or dynami- cally modiﬁed them using GPT-4 to better match the evolving context of the conversation. This hybrid approach ensured the facilitation remained aligned with pedagogical goals while allowing enough ﬂexibility to respond to diverse dis- cussion dynamics. In this setup, GPT-4 was not tasked with inventing novel facilitation content but with adapting ex- isting messages to ﬁt the conversation ﬂow. This method maintained a balance between coherence, responsiveness, and instructional consistency. To",180
