DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,7,"HAQBEEN et al.: FACILITATING STUDENT ENGAGEMENT AND LEARNING OUTCOMES WITH GPT-BASED CHATBOTS 1009 line. Illustratively we would like to give an example here, on Day 2 the AI Facilitator generated 692 turns (275 posts, 417 replies) versus 7 Human instructor turns in the treatment condition. For further details on Day 2 facilitation interactions, see Sect. 5. 3.4 Measures and Data Collection We employed the subject’s self-report to subjectively assess students’ perceptions and experiences on the learning out- come and analyze post-lecture discussion contents/log data generated by students during their interaction to objectively evaluate how the chatbot facilitation capabilities contributed to enhancing the engagement process, and the chatbot facil- itation impact on students’ experiences. All messages posted during the post-lecture discussions were automatically recorded by the platform. For each par- ticipant we compute messages (thread/posts), replies, total words, and likes from post-lecture discussion logs. From these logs, we computed several quantitative engagement metrics for each student: (1) Posts – number of thread- starting messages a student posted; (2) Replies – the number of replies to others’ messages; (3) Word count –",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,7,"post-lecture discussion logs. From these logs, we computed several quantitative engagement metrics for each student: (1) Posts – number of thread- starting messages a student posted; (2) Replies – the number of replies to others’ messages; (3) Word count – the total number of words the student wrote (used as as a quantita- tive proxy for opinion depth and cognitive engagement in discussion); and (4) Likes received – the number of “likes” reactions the student’s posts received from peers. These metrics were chosen based on common indicators of online discussion participation, and reﬂect diﬀerent dimensions of engagement: participation quantity (posts, replies), contri- bution length (words), and peer recognition (likes) [26], [27], [49]. We aggregated these metrics per student for each dis- cussion day. Immediately after the Day 2 discussion, students com- pleted an online post-intervention questionnaire. We employed the subject’s self-report to subjectively as- sess students’ perceptions [54] and experiences on the learn- ing outcome. This survey included items to gauge students’ percep- tions of the class experience. In particular, to assess per- ceived learning outcome, we designed a questionnaire",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,7,"self-report to subjectively as- sess students’ perceptions [54] and experiences on the learn- ing outcome. This survey included items to gauge students’ percep- tions of the class experience. In particular, to assess per- ceived learning outcome, we designed a questionnaire item on conﬁdence and asked from both groups: “As a result of this class setting, do you feel more conﬁdent in this area of study?” as shown in Fig. 10, and asked whether the human instructor was helpful and engaged during the post-lecture discussions: “The instructor engagement in post-lecture in- teractive session enhanced our course learning activities”. In addition, two supplementary items were included for the treatment group to assess their perceptions of the chatbot’s facilitation—speciﬁcally, the extent to which these messages motivated them to engage in the post-lecture dis- cussion: “The facilitator facilitated messages encouraged me to actively participate in the interactive discussion.” (see Fig. 12), and the ease of interacting with chatbot-generated messages: “I easily interacted with the facilitator messages during the post-lecture session.” (see Fig. 13). Fig. 5 Conceptual Model of Interaction and Facilitation Eﬀects on Stu-",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,7,"in the interactive discussion.” (see Fig. 12), and the ease of interacting with chatbot-generated messages: “I easily interacted with the facilitator messages during the post-lecture session.” (see Fig. 13). Fig. 5 Conceptual Model of Interaction and Facilitation Eﬀects on Stu- dent Learning Outcomes Students responded on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree, 7 = Strongly Agree). This question aimed to capture the students’ self-reported conﬁdence or under- standing gained from the two-day course in their given set- ting (chatbot-assisted or not). Finally, open-ended feedback was solicited about their experience. We acknowledge that the qualitative data collected through the open-ended item in the post-intervention questionnaire were not analyzed. Figure 5 illustrates the conceptual model that depicts how lectures and post-lecture discussions stimulate and fa- cilitate interaction and facilitation processes, which in turn promote enhanced student engagement. This engagement strengthens overall learning outcomes [58]. The diagram conceptualizes the dynamic relationship between instruc- tional delivery, facilitation quality, and student engagement, highlighting that eﬀective facilitation and interaction act as mediating mechanisms that translate teaching inputs into im- proved learning achievements. 3.5 Data",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,7,"overall learning outcomes [58]. The diagram conceptualizes the dynamic relationship between instruc- tional delivery, facilitation quality, and student engagement, highlighting that eﬀective facilitation and interaction act as mediating mechanisms that translate teaching inputs into im- proved learning achievements. 3.5 Data Analysis We used statistical tests [50] to compare the engagement metrics and survey results between the control and treatment groups. For each discussion metric (posts, replies, words, likes), we ﬁrst checked assumptions and then conducted independent-samples t-tests to assess diﬀerences between conditions. Day 1 and Day 2 were analyzed separately to observe any evolution. Because students were randomly as- signed, we treated the two groups as independent for analysis purposes. We report means, standard deviations, and t-test p-values. A signiﬁcance level of α = 0.05 (two-tailed) was used for all tests. Eﬀect sizes (Cohen’s d) were calculated to gauge the magnitude of diﬀerences. For the Self-report questionnaires, we used a Likert-",151
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,8,"1010 IEICE TRANS. INF. & SYST., VOL.E109–D, NO.7 JULY 2026 scale to to assess students’ perceptions of learning outcomes and conﬁdence, we likewise used an independent t-test to compare mean responses between the treatment and con- trol group. The Likert scale, developed by Rensis Likert in 1932 [51], is a reliable and valid method for measuring attitudes. Given the context of this study—an online education intervention assessing students’ perceptions, learning conﬁ- dence, and related constructs—a 7-point Likert scale was employed to capture ﬁner distinctions in participant re- sponses. Prior research suggests that 7-point items provide more accurate reﬂections of participants’ true evaluations compared to 5-point scales [60]. The aim was to subejctively capture whether the stu- dents felt the discussion format (with or without AI) im- proved their conﬁdence and perceived learning outcome in the subject. All quantitative analyses were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics (v28.0) [52]. Given the pilot nature of the study, we did not adjust α for multiple comparisons, but we interpret results in light of potential Type I error inﬂation. Where appropriate, we supplement p-values with",180
DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,8,"performed using IBM SPSS Statistics (v28.0) [52]. Given the pilot nature of the study, we did not adjust α for multiple comparisons, but we interpret results in light of potential Type I error inﬂation. Where appropriate, we supplement p-values with descriptive statistics and qualitative observations for a more nuanced in- terpretation. We believe using both behavioral (objective) and attitudinal (subjective) measures allows us to triangulate eﬀects and provide complementary evidence. This is im- portant because engagement is a key driver of learning, and prior work shows that higher engagement is associated with better learning outcomes [58], [59]. 3.6 Ethical Consideration The experiment methodology was conducted in line with the approval of the Ethics Committee of the Graduate School of Informatics, Kyoto University (KUIS-EAR-2021-020). All participants provided informed consent after being briefed on the study’s purpose and procedures. The study team took all necessary steps to ensure the ethical conduct of the study. 4. Results We ﬁrst report objective engagement metrics extracted from the post-lecture discussion logs—comparing control and AI- assisted (treatment) conditions on Day 1, and then replicating the analysis",180
