DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,9,"the control group. Moreover, the AI-supported participants con- tributed longer and more elaborated responses, reﬂected in a higher average word count (206.3 vs. 165.1). Interestingly, while the non-AI group received a higher average number of likes (2.77 vs. 0.70), this distribution appeared more concentrated on a few posts, suggesting a possible bandwagon eﬀect—where certain contributions at- tract disproportionate peer attention. By contrast, the AI group’s lower but more evenly distributed likes suggest a more balanced participation pattern, as participants appeared to focus more on active engagement metrics—such as gen- erating posts, replying to peers, and producing longer, more elaborated responses. Overall, these ﬁndings suggest that AI-assisted facilita- tion enhanced engagement quality and quantity, promoting more consistent participation across learners. This high- lights the potential of AI-supported interaction tools to foster inclusive and sustained engagement, particularly in contexts where women participation are not supported. This ﬁnding aligns with the results of study [49], which suggests that conversational AI can enhance women’s par- ticipation and contributions in online discussions. 4.1.2 Analysis of Engagement (Day 2) A comparative analysis of discussion engagement metrics",180
