DOC_005,E109.D_2025AHP0009.pdf,10.1587/transinf.2025AHP0009,9,"the treatment group produced a signiﬁcantly higher number of replies per student (M = 8.23) than the control group (M = 4.29), also signiﬁcant at p < .001. Furthermore, students in the treat- ment group wrote substantially more words per student on average (M ≈278 words) than their counterparts in the con- trol group (M ≈125 words), again with a highly signiﬁcant diﬀerence (p < .001). These results suggest a marked im- provement in both the quantity and depth of student engage- ment in the chatbot-supported discussions. In contrast, no signiﬁcant diﬀerence was observed in the number of peer “likes” received per student. Although the control group received a slightly higher average number of likes (M = 1.13) compared to the treatment group (M = 0.77), this diﬀerence did not reach statistical signiﬁcance (p = n.s.). This pattern may reﬂect a continued tendency for peer attention to be more focused in the control group, where fewer posts were made overall. Similar to Day 1, the AI-assisted group showed higher engagement across posts, replies, and word count, indicat- ing more active",180
