DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,17,"Page 16 of 21 Ellis et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:33 likely to report a negative perception of the value of GenAI (88.26%) than a positive one (30.77%). Table 10 shows a statistically significant and moderate relation between approaches to teaching with GenAI and perceptions of GenAI (φ = 0.450, p = 0.024). Teachers whose approaches were learning-focused tended to perceive the benefits of GenAI for developing student understanding provided that the output is verified. In contrast, teaching-focused approaches were related to negative perceptions of value proposition of GenAI. The relation between approaches to teaching with GenAI and conceptions of GenAI for higher education learning is presented in Table 11, which also shows statistically significant and moderate association (φ = 0.360, p = 0.029). Approaches to teaching that use of GenAI to develop critical thinking and reasoning amongst students tended to conceive of the value of GenAI from the student perspective for its contribution to learning. Conversely, approaches to teaching that were teaching-focused tended to fragment any learning-benefit from GenAI for the student experience. Discussion and",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,17,"reasoning amongst students tended to conceive of the value of GenAI from the student perspective for its contribution to learning. Conversely, approaches to teaching that were teaching-focused tended to fragment any learning-benefit from GenAI for the student experience. Discussion and conclusion To contribute to the emerging debate about the value of GenAI knowledge and skills for university students, this intensive study interviewed thirty teachers to explore variations in how they perceived, conceived of, and approached teaching with GenAI. The results are an interesting contribution to the current debate as they offer education leaders, teachers, and students an important perspective of teachers’ experience of teaching with GenAI and the extent to which it is valuable for a university education experience. Variations of teacher experiences of teaching with GenAI shaped by perceptions, conceptions, and conceptions The results of this study have noteworthy implications for those who believe that a university education should provide graduates with relevant GenAI knowledge and skills for their careers if they are going to be productive contributors to society and industry (Nartey, 2024; Prohorov et al., 2024). In",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,17,"implications for those who believe that a university education should provide graduates with relevant GenAI knowledge and skills for their careers if they are going to be productive contributors to society and industry (Nartey, 2024; Prohorov et al., 2024). In the current climate, most industries are starting to require GenAI literacy as a key graduate attribute of university students (Javaid et al., 2023). Consequently, employees need to know how to both use and critically evaluate GenAI output (Farrell et al., 2021; Javaid et al., 2023; TEQSA, 2024). These graduate workplace capabilities have fundamental implications for university teaching. Fig. 3 provides a visual summary of the variations in the teaching experience with GenAI Table 11 Association between approaches and conceptions Approaches to teaching with GenAI Conceptions of GenAI Total Coherent (A&B) Fragmented (C, D&E) Learning-focused approaches (A&B) 11 12 23 Teaching-focused approaches (C,D&E) 0 7 7 Total 11 19 30",149
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,18,"Page 17 of 21 Ellis et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:33 Amongst the teachers interviewed, an unfavourable experience emerged involving negative perceptions of GenAI for learning, involving doubt about its potential to help students develop understanding, to the point where some teachers were unlikely to use it; or if it was used, the approach emphasised risk management issues such as correcting errors and not engaging in plagiarism or other ethics-based infringements. While these last two issues need to be addressed in any student experience, these were threshold of concerns for a second view of GenAI in the learning and teaching experience, one that viewed it beyond just risk-management, and considered it from the perspective of students and its potential contribution to learning. Another experience of GenAI in teaching from the interviews perceived it as offering evaluative and reflective experiences of learning for students, experiences that helped students to contextualise analytical thinking in order to develop the knowledge and skills they will require for their future careers. This was a learning-focused view of GenAI in the university",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,18,"and reflective experiences of learning for students, experiences that helped students to contextualise analytical thinking in order to develop the knowledge and skills they will require for their future careers. This was a learning-focused view of GenAI in the university student experience that emphasised its use to stimulate critical thinking and reasoning. This view valued the importance of embedding GenAI knowledge and skills in the student experience where appropriate so that degree programs continued to be, and were perceived to be, relevant in the current trend to use AI not just in efficient ways, but in ways that added to student understanding and learning. Within these views of variation of teaching with GenAI, there was not an absolute categorisation of the participants involved, as the contingency tables showed that the associations amongst the qualitatively different categories were more nuanced. In other words, just because a participant who reported a fragmented concept of GenAI approached its use in a teaching-focused way, did not mean that all participants who reported fragmented concepts did the same. The qualitative variation revealed, however, does suggest",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,18,"other words, just because a participant who reported a fragmented concept of GenAI approached its use in a teaching-focused way, did not mean that all participants who reported fragmented concepts did the same. The qualitative variation revealed, however, does suggest a tendency amongst the categories of description which is useful for those concerned about supporting teaching development and the quality of future education, including how to most productively approach a use of GenAI in the university student experience. By addressing fragmented concepts, negative perceptions and teaching- focused approaches to become more aware of the student experience and needs of GenAI literacy, the potential for learning benefits from GenAI knowledge and skills for university students is more likely to be embraced by teachers. Interestingly, while the academic context of teacher experiences in teaching with GenAI have distinct features from those in traditional university teaching, the relational patterns amongst Avoidance Awareness Design Teach Embed Favourable Unfavourable Negative perceptions Fragmented conceptions Positive perceptions Cohesive conceptions Teaching-orientated approaches to teaching Learning-orientated approaches to teaching Fig. 3 A visual representation of qualitatively different experiences of",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,18,"traditional university teaching, the relational patterns amongst Avoidance Awareness Design Teach Embed Favourable Unfavourable Negative perceptions Fragmented conceptions Positive perceptions Cohesive conceptions Teaching-orientated approaches to teaching Learning-orientated approaches to teaching Fig. 3 A visual representation of qualitatively different experiences of teaching with GenAI",43
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,19,"Page 18 of 21 Ellis et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:33 conceptions, perceptions, and approaches resemble those observed from teacher experiences in other academic contexts (Prosser & Trigwell, 1997; Prosser et al., 1994; Trigwell & Prosser, 2020). Our study added empirical evidence into the 3P model of teaching–learning experience (Prosser & Trigwell, 2017). Implications for education leadership, teaching development and student learning If teachers’ views on GenAI are an important part of the emerging debate because of their centrality in a university education, then it is clear from those concerned about the quality and relevance of degree programs and the university student experience that an appropriate use of GenAI should be part of university student experiences of learning (Bozkurt et al., 2023; Reiss, 2021; Tlili et al., 2023). The views of employers and educators reported at the beginning of this paper suggest that students will need GenAI knowledge and skills to participate effectively in the workplace and this capability is likely to become an important graduate attribute from a university education. However, we are just at",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,19,"the beginning of this paper suggest that students will need GenAI knowledge and skills to participate effectively in the workplace and this capability is likely to become an important graduate attribute from a university education. However, we are just at the beginning of understanding what an appropriate use of GenAI in the student experience might look like, requiring responses from teaching team leaders and university leaders. For leaders of teaching teams, it is clear that some considerable work on better understanding the perceived benefits of GenAI for learning will be necessary, including how it can support student learning and approaches to teaching. Where GenAI is regarded as an important part of the student experience and curriculum design, some considerable work is likely to be required to help teaching teams understand relatively more productive ways of thinking about and using GenAI in the student experience (Koeslag-Kreunen et al., 2018), ways that focus more on the learning that can arising from using it than on only focusing on its limitations. For university leaders, the benefits arising from a use of GenAI in",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,19,"in the student experience (Koeslag-Kreunen et al., 2018), ways that focus more on the learning that can arising from using it than on only focusing on its limitations. For university leaders, the benefits arising from a use of GenAI in learning and teaching suggest it needs to become part of the learning environment, which will require significant planning and investment (Hoernig et al., 2024). This will include an appropriately governed and risk-managed approach to a systematic use of GenAI for learning and teaching across all faculties (Chiu, 2024). It will also include consideration of policy and infrastructure that enables sensitive university data and information to be ring-fenced from the frontier platforms capture of all data, as well as identity management so that students and teachers can have appropriate access to GenAI. To develop the capabilities of teaching teams, it will involve reconfiguring professional development programs and grants to highlight innovations in GenAI in teaching, along with other incentives such as promotion based on excellence in the use of GenAI for learning outcomes (Trigwell et al., 2012). Only a sustained focused",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,19,"involve reconfiguring professional development programs and grants to highlight innovations in GenAI in teaching, along with other incentives such as promotion based on excellence in the use of GenAI for learning outcomes (Trigwell et al., 2012). Only a sustained focused by teaching staff on the contributions of GenAI for learning will reveal how it can contribute to improving the student experience with the right amount of disciplinary variation in its deployment. While the results of this study offer an important perspective into the debate on GenAI in the university experience, the intensive nature of the investigation necessitated that relatively small population sample. To test the robustness of the conclusions suggested, the qualitatively different positions of the teachers’ perspective should be investigated further in replicated studies. Studies with large quantitative studies will help in this regard and allow the development of more extensive evidence to support the findings,",147
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,20,"Page 19 of 21 Ellis et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2025) 22:33 such as the extent to which disciplinary variation might contribute to differences in the way teachers approach teaching using GenAI. While this study is just a small piece of the puzzle the higher education sector is grappling with, only through similar and complementary studies will essential evidence be uncovered for effective teaching that is required to leverage significant learning benefit for students. No matter how pedagogy and expectations change, teachers continue to be responsible for the underlying program and course learning outcomes of the student experience, both in the way programs and are designed as well in how they are taught. As such, they are key contributors to the current debate on the emerging benefits for GenAI for learning in higher education. Acknowledgements Not available. Author contributions Conceptualization–RE, FH, HC; Data curation–RE, HC; Formal Analysis–RE, FH, HC; Investigation–RE, FH, HC; Methodology–RE, FH, HC, Project administration–RE, FH, HC; Resources–RE, FH, HC; Visualization–RE, FH, HC; Writing– original draft–RE, FH, HC; Writing–review & editing–RE, FH, HC. Funding This",180
DOC_007,Ellis10460483.pdf,10.1186/s41239-025-00532-2,20,"Author contributions Conceptualization–RE, FH, HC; Data curation–RE, HC; Formal Analysis–RE, FH, HC; Investigation–RE, FH, HC; Methodology–RE, FH, HC, Project administration–RE, FH, HC; Resources–RE, FH, HC; Visualization–RE, FH, HC; Writing– original draft–RE, FH, HC; Writing–review & editing–RE, FH, HC. Funding This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Availability of data and materials The datasets generated and/or analysed during the current study are not publicly available due to ethics requirements, but are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Competing interests The authors have no competing interests to declare. Generative AI and AI‑assisted technologies in the writing process During the preparation of this work the authors did not use any generative AI and AI-assisted technologies. Received: 16 January 2025 Accepted: 21 April 2025",134
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,1,"1 of 11 European Journal of Education, 2025; 60:e70131 https://doi.org/10.1111/ejed.70131 European Journal of Education ORIGINAL ARTICLE OPEN ACCESS ChatGPT in Higher Education: Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT-­Based Activities Anthemis Raptopoulou Department of Education, Södertörn University, Huddinge, Sweden Correspondence: Anthemis Raptopoulou (anthemis.raptopoulou@sh.se) Received: 28 June 2024 | Revised: 26 March 2025 | Accepted: 5 May 2025 ABSTRACT Recent advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI), particularly AI chatbots like ChatGPT, have sparked significant discussions in higher education regarding their impact on teaching and academic integrity. This study examines the integration of ChatGPT into a philosophy of education course for first-­year Education students at a Swedish university. Using a sociocultural approach and the concept of mediating devices, the study investigates how ChatGPT can be effectively integrated into higher education to support academic literacy and the challenges and opportunities it presents. The empirical material was collected through semi-­ structured focus group interviews, observational notes, and students' assignments. The findings suggest that when used inten­ tionally with clear pedagogical goals, ChatGPT can support academic literacy development. Its ability to generate coherent text, summarise complex concepts, and",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,1,"collected through semi-­ structured focus group interviews, observational notes, and students' assignments. The findings suggest that when used inten­ tionally with clear pedagogical goals, ChatGPT can support academic literacy development. Its ability to generate coherent text, summarise complex concepts, and provide initial structures for academic writing proved particularly beneficial for first-­year stu­ dents. However, limitations such as inaccuracies and lack of nuance provided opportunities for students to engage critically with the generated material and develop strategies to address these shortcomings. The findings highlight that both the teacher's guid­ ance and the social aspects of learning, such as group discussions and collaborative work, were essential in leveraging ChatGPT's strengths and addressing its limitations. Therefore, this paper advocates for a balanced approach that emphasises pedagogical grounding and responsible AI integration to ensure the ethical and effective use of AI tools. Understanding the interplay between ChatGPT's integration and the social dynamics of teaching is crucial for assessing its impact on students' learning experiences. 1 | Introduction In recent years, advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI) have revolutionised various sectors, including education (Rahiman and Kodikal",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,1,"interplay between ChatGPT's integration and the social dynamics of teaching is crucial for assessing its impact on students' learning experiences. 1 | Introduction In recent years, advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI) have revolutionised various sectors, including education (Rahiman and Kodikal 2024), transforming how knowledge is accessed, processed, and utilised within academic settings. These advancements have sparked discussions within higher ed­ ucation settings (Kramm and McKenna 2023; Peters et al. 2023; Popenici 2023), alongside concerns about their impact on teaching and academic integrity (Blackie 2024; Rawas 2023). An indication of AI's growing presence in education can be found in the most recent European Parliament Youth Survey 2024, where 57% of young people reported using AI-­based ap­ plications over the past year. The primary motivation for using these tools was for studying and conducting research (European Parliament 2025), pointing towards AI's increasing relevance in academic settings. Against this backdrop, AI chatbots are be­ coming increasingly prevalent in higher education, prompting the need to explore how they can be used, while also consider­ ing their broader implications for research, policy and practice (Bearman",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,1,"relevance in academic settings. Against this backdrop, AI chatbots are be­ coming increasingly prevalent in higher education, prompting the need to explore how they can be used, while also consider­ ing their broader implications for research, policy and practice (Bearman et al. 2023). Among AI tools, ChatGPT stands out for its capacity to generate text, provide personalised recommendations, and potentially improve student learning outcomes. ChatGPT belongs within the broader category of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI). It is a specific implementation of generative AI, which refers to systems designed to create new content—such as This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited and is not used for commercial purposes. © 2025 The Author(s). European Journal of Education published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd. [Correction added on 21 November 2025, after first online publication: The manuscript received date was incorrectly published as “26 March 2025” due to a metadata error and has been corrected to “28 June 2024”.]",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,1,"by John Wiley & Sons Ltd. [Correction added on 21 November 2025, after first online publication: The manuscript received date was incorrectly published as “26 March 2025” due to a metadata error and has been corrected to “28 June 2024”.]",40
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"2 of 11 European Journal of Education, 2025 text, images, or music—based on input data. Developed by OpenAI, ChatGPT was launched in November 2022. Built upon the Generative Pre-­trained Transformer (GPT) architec­ ture, ChatGPT produces human-­like responses to text input. As an advanced version of previous GPT models and due to its deep learning algorithms and built-­in programming, ChatGPT can generate sophisticated responses in multiple languages, translate text, complete text, and answer questions. These ca­ pabilities are due to its deep learning algorithms and built-­in programming. As expected, ChatGPT quickly captured global attention with claims that it “heralds an intellectual revolu­ tion” and “will redefine human knowledge, accelerate changes in the fabric of our reality, and reorganize politics and society” (Kissinger et al. 2023). However, as Pargman et al. (2024) argue, AI chatbots are trained to mimic language patterns rather than understand meaning. They generate responses based on internet text and are primarily developed for non-­educational purposes. AI chatbots have the potential to redefine current understandings of student writing competence, producing disembodied text without a point of view. Additionally, their",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"than understand meaning. They generate responses based on internet text and are primarily developed for non-­educational purposes. AI chatbots have the potential to redefine current understandings of student writing competence, producing disembodied text without a point of view. Additionally, their widespread and uninformed use raises further concerns about unethical and malicious practices (Alawida et al. 2024). Several studies have examined the use of ChatGPT in educa­ tion and in higher education in particular. For example, Ansari et al. (2023) argue that researchers, teachers, and students em­ ploy ChatGPT for various purposes, such as seeking personal benefits and reducing academic burdens. Challenges specifically associated with ChatGPT, such as plagiarism, academic integ­ rity, fictitious references, and the unintentional perpetuation of biases in AI-­generated content and inequalities in access, have been addressed (Teel et al. 2023; Chan and Colloton 2024; Al-­ Khresheh 2024; Memarian and Doleck 2023). Williams (2024) highlights various ethical concerns, including privacy issues and algorithmic bias, emphasising the need for careful consider­ ation when implementing AI technologies. Trust, reliability, and human factors have also been emphasised, highlighting the crit­ ical",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"Memarian and Doleck 2023). Williams (2024) highlights various ethical concerns, including privacy issues and algorithmic bias, emphasising the need for careful consider­ ation when implementing AI technologies. Trust, reliability, and human factors have also been emphasised, highlighting the crit­ ical importance of verifying and validating AI outputs to ensure their credibility (Thorne 2024), as well as other ethical challenges, such as the risks of overreliance on AI and the need for respon­ sible integration of AI tools in pedagogical practices (Waluyo and Kusumastuti 2024; Rudolph et al. 2023). These discussions extend to concerns about academic integrity and traditional as­ sessment strategies, particularly in light of the prevalence of AI-­ generated content (Farazouli 2024). Moreover, perceptions of ethical considerations and responsible AI use among students and educators have been explored, illuminating the broader implica­ tions of AI's integration into academic environments (Chan and Hu 2023; Avcı 2024). In response to these concerns, research calls for a reevaluation of academic integrity practices to address the challenges posed by AI tools in education (Rudolph et al. 2023; Farazouli et al. 2024). Additionally, since",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"(Chan and Hu 2023; Avcı 2024). In response to these concerns, research calls for a reevaluation of academic integrity practices to address the challenges posed by AI tools in education (Rudolph et al. 2023; Farazouli et al. 2024). Additionally, since most higher education institutions lack specialised guidelines for the use of AI (Ghimire and Edwards 2024), the development of AI policy frameworks is recommended to establish clear guidelines for roles and respon­ sibilities, which are essential for the successful integration of AI technologies in educational settings (Wang 2023; Chan 2023; Jin et al. 2024). A particular emphasis is placed on cultivating AI lit­ eracy as a foundational element for fostering sustainable and eth­ ical education (Chan and Colloton 2024; Southworth et al. 2023; Zhang 2023; Moorhouse et al. 2023). Several theoretical and empirical studies highlight potential im­ plementations of AI in higher education. For example, various scenarios for integrating ChatGPT in educational settings have been suggested, including serving as a debate partner, facilitat­ ing Socratic dialogues, engaging in reverse engineering of essays, and acting as a study buddy (Chan and",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"in higher education. For example, various scenarios for integrating ChatGPT in educational settings have been suggested, including serving as a debate partner, facilitat­ ing Socratic dialogues, engaging in reverse engineering of essays, and acting as a study buddy (Chan and Colloton 2024; Sabzalieva and Valentini 2023; Ziebell and Skeat 2023). Additionally, the in­ troduction of PAIGE, a conceptual framework designed to pro­ mote assignment integrity through generative AI (GAI), aims to uphold academic standards in educational contexts (Shanto et al. 2023). Acun and Acun (2023) also demonstrate the effec­ tiveness of using ChatGPT for role-­playing activities in history education, showcasing its potential to impact students' learning experiences. In Sweden, university students extensively use digital tools, including AI, to support their studies (Malmström et al. 2023). One example of AI integration in higher education is the study by Jiménez Romanillos and Andersson (2024), which explored the use of ChatGPT for teaching in robotic design. However, de­ spite its growing presence, there is still a need for more empirical research on AI's role in higher education (Aghaee 2024), partic­ ularly as concerns",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"Andersson (2024), which explored the use of ChatGPT for teaching in robotic design. However, de­ spite its growing presence, there is still a need for more empirical research on AI's role in higher education (Aghaee 2024), partic­ ularly as concerns about its use continue to rise. These concerns have been amplified by a recent increase in suspected cases of academic cheating (Johansson Westholm and Månsson 2025). In response, the Swedish Higher Education Authority (UKÄ) has been tasked with mapping strategies to combat AI-­assisted cheating in higher education (Regeringskansliet 2024). This ini­ tiative further underscores the need for a more informed and ethical approach to AI in Swedish higher education institutions. This paper builds on recent research on the impact and poten­ tial of AI in education, with a specific emphasis on higher edu­ cation. Despite limited empirical studies on AI chatbots in this context (McGrath et al. 2024), there is a pressing need for stu­ dents to develop new competencies to adapt to the evolving land­ scape shaped by AI-­driven technologies (Bearman et al. 2023). The study explores how integrating",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"chatbots in this context (McGrath et al. 2024), there is a pressing need for stu­ dents to develop new competencies to adapt to the evolving land­ scape shaped by AI-­driven technologies (Bearman et al. 2023). The study explores how integrating ChatGPT into university courses can contribute to academic literacy, addressing both the opportunities and challenges at the intersection of artificial in­ telligence and higher education. More specifically, the research questions guiding this study are: • RQ1: How can ChatGPT be effectively integrated into higher education courses to support and develop students' academic literacy? • RQ2: What are the key challenges and opportunities associ­ ated with using ChatGPT to facilitate academic literacy in higher education settings? 1.1 | A Sociocultural Approach to Academic Literacy and Mediating Devices Amidst the concerns posed by ChatGPT in higher education practices, this paper focuses on how ChatGPT can affect students' academic literacy. Academic literacy, described as an “embodi­ ment of higher-­order thinking and learning”, has historically de­ fied precise definition, evolving into a multidimensional concept (Li 2022). In this paper, Wingate's (2018) definition of academic",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,2,"how ChatGPT can affect students' academic literacy. Academic literacy, described as an “embodi­ ment of higher-­order thinking and learning”, has historically de­ fied precise definition, evolving into a multidimensional concept (Li 2022). In this paper, Wingate's (2018) definition of academic 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",79
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"3 of 11 literacy is going to be employed. According to Wingate, academic literacy is “the ability to communicate competently in an aca­ demic discourse community; this encompasses reading, evalu­ ating information, as well as presenting, debating and creating knowledge through both speaking and writing” (350). Recent perspectives characterise academic literacy as a social practice closely intertwined with disciplinary epistemology and personal identity (Gee 2015a). Geisler (2013) further describes academic literacy as a cultural practice with noticeable effects on several fronts: (i) the writing and interpretation of texts, (ii) the organ­ isation and management of reading and writing activities, (iii) the collaborative transmission of expertise between teachers and students, and (iv) the structuring of groups to generate and receive expert advice. Both perspectives view the acquisition of literacy as a sociocultural and dynamic process, emphasising learners' active role in collaboratively constructing knowledge rather than passively acquiring it. Studies of academic literacy are often paired with sociocul­ tural theories, which view literacy as an artefact of culture that evolves alongside societal changes. This perspective, as noted by Baker (2010), is particularly",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"constructing knowledge rather than passively acquiring it. Studies of academic literacy are often paired with sociocul­ tural theories, which view literacy as an artefact of culture that evolves alongside societal changes. This perspective, as noted by Baker (2010), is particularly useful for examining the nature of literacy and its transformations in response to cultural shifts. In the context of the increasing use of AI technologies in edu­ cation, this view becomes even more timely. This paper draws on sociocultural perspectives on learning and specifically uti­ lises Gee's (2008) concept of a “mediating device”. According to Gee, “a mediating device is any object, tool, or technology that a person can use to enhance performance beyond what could be done without the object, tool, or technology” (88). Such devices enable individuals to simplify and comprehend complex realities or concepts (Gee 2015b). As Gee (2008) further argues that ca­ pabilities and achievements of learners using a mediating device are significantly different from what they can achieve without it. When individuals engage with mediating devices, knowledge becomes distributed; some is manifested in their minds,",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"Gee (2008) further argues that ca­ pabilities and achievements of learners using a mediating device are significantly different from what they can achieve without it. When individuals engage with mediating devices, knowledge becomes distributed; some is manifested in their minds, some in their coordination with tools, and some in the tools themselves. However, the concept of “mediating devices” extends beyond tools to include other people as well. Specifically, individuals can also serve as “tools” for learners when they interact, mutually contributing to the acquisition and creation of knowledge. This framework informs the exploration of the impact of ChatGPT as a mediating device in enhancing students' academic literacy. 2 | Materials & Methods This study utilised a case study design, chosen for its suitability in exploring real-­life activities within a specific context. The context was a course on the philosophy of education, offered by the Education Department at a Swedish higher education in­ stitution, conducted on campus. The participants were 8 first-­ year Education majors, comprising 7 females and 1 male, aged 22 to 26. While most students were familiar with",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"education, offered by the Education Department at a Swedish higher education in­ stitution, conducted on campus. The participants were 8 first-­ year Education majors, comprising 7 females and 1 male, aged 22 to 26. While most students were familiar with ChatGPT, a couple had no prior experience using it. Out of 12 students enrolled in the course, only 8 agreed to participate in the re­ search, meaning that while the course included 12 students, data collection for this paper focused exclusively on the input of these 8 participants. Notably, the input from these 8 students was representative of the overall engagement and progress of the entire class. To address potential biases, it is important to note that although the participants were enrolled in a philos­ ophy of education course, they had no prior background in philosophy. Throughout the course, students were actively involved in various academic activities, including reading assigned litera­ ture, attending lectures, and participating in seminar sessions. During the seminars, the students were divided into two work­ ing groups of 4 students each, and this grouping was maintained",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"were actively involved in various academic activities, including reading assigned litera­ ture, attending lectures, and participating in seminar sessions. During the seminars, the students were divided into two work­ ing groups of 4 students each, and this grouping was maintained throughout all seminar activities. During the seminars, they were tasked with ChatGPT-­based activities. More specifically, the students accessed the free version of ChatGPT-­3.5 to gen­ erate responses to given questions using their laptops and pre­ pared seminar materials. Subsequently, they critically reviewed their responses within working groups, identifying strengths, weaknesses, and areas for improvement with reference to course literature and relevant quotes. The final step involved upload­ ing an edited response in continuous text format to the course's learning platform. Throughout the implementation phase, stu­ dents' engagement with ChatGPT-­generated texts was observed. The empirical material collection employed a qualitative ap­ proach, examining interview transcripts, students' seminar submissions, final exams, and observational notes. Specifically, the material included: semi-­structured focus group interviews conducted with the two student groups after the seminar activ­ ities, totalling 3 h of transcripts; observational notes from 6",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"proach, examining interview transcripts, students' seminar submissions, final exams, and observational notes. Specifically, the material included: semi-­structured focus group interviews conducted with the two student groups after the seminar activ­ ities, totalling 3 h of transcripts; observational notes from 6 h of seminar activities; and, to complement these sources, students' papers from group activities, final exams, and an anonymized course evaluation, amounting to 100 pages of additional mate­ rial. During the focus group interviews, students were asked about their experiences with ChatGPT, exploring both the chal­ lenges and opportunities it presented, its impact on reading and understanding course literature, and its role in supporting academic writing. The observation was conducted as partici­ pant observation, given my dual role as researcher and educa­ tor, and took place during the group-­based seminar activities. However, in active observer roles, such as in this case, there is a risk of power imbalances, biased views, sympathetic identifi­ cation, and projective distortion (Schwartz and Schwartz 1955). To mitigate the potential impact of my dual role on the study's outcomes and ensure student autonomy, several measures were",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"case, there is a risk of power imbalances, biased views, sympathetic identifi­ cation, and projective distortion (Schwartz and Schwartz 1955). To mitigate the potential impact of my dual role on the study's outcomes and ensure student autonomy, several measures were implemented to reduce bias. Participation was fully voluntary, with assurances that students' choices to participate would not affect their grades or academic progress. Around two-­thirds of the class chose to participate, reflecting adherence to voluntary participation principles. To address potential biases in my in­ terpretation of findings, additional steps were taken to validate the study's results. After the course, the program administration invited students to complete an anonymous course evaluation, allowing candid feedback unlinked to assessment concerns. Responses from these evaluations aligned with the study's find­ ings, adding credibility and reducing interpretive bias. Coding and thematic analysis were applied to the collected material to identify emerging patterns and themes. The coding process was entirely emergent, with codes developed as patterns and themes surfaced during the analysis. The empirical material was re­ viewed multiple times to refine the codes, and initial",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,3,"the collected material to identify emerging patterns and themes. The coding process was entirely emergent, with codes developed as patterns and themes surfaced during the analysis. The empirical material was re­ viewed multiple times to refine the codes, and initial codes from the interviews were cross-­checked with observational notes and additional materials to enhance consistency and mitigate poten­ tial biases. 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",99
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"4 of 11 European Journal of Education, 2025 To uphold ethical standards, several key measures were imple­ mented to ensure the protection and respect of all participants, particularly given the power dynamics between the researcher/ teacher and the student participants. Potential participants were fully informed about the nature and purpose of the study before deciding to take part. Students who chose not to partic­ ipate were assured that their course progress would not be af­ fected. Informed consent was obtained only after participants had ample opportunity to ask questions and fully understand the study's purpose and objectives, with the assurance that they could withdraw at any time without facing negative conse­ quences. Participant confidentiality was rigorously maintained throughout the study and all material collected was anonymized to ensure participants' privacy throughout the study. 2.1 | ChatGPT-­Based Activity The reasoning behind the choice of seminar activity was driven by necessity. The course literature, heavily based on classic texts from influential figures in education within the Swedish con­ text, such as Immanuel Kant, John Dewey, and Ellen Key, is widely available on",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"the choice of seminar activity was driven by necessity. The course literature, heavily based on classic texts from influential figures in education within the Swedish con­ text, such as Immanuel Kant, John Dewey, and Ellen Key, is widely available on the internet. This accessibility makes it easy to generate answers via tools like ChatGPT. To mitigate the risks of plagiarism, cheating, and the uninformed use of ChatGPT, but also to promote an informed and structured use of the tool, I divided students into small groups and assigned them questions related to the content of each lecture preceding the seminar. Each group was tasked with generating an answer to a given question using ChatGPT and then collaboratively editing the re­ sponse manually. These same questions were later used in their final examination. Before the activity, students were required to complete homework that involved reading the assigned lit­ erature, highlighting key quotes, and noting down points of re­ flection. On the day of the seminar, the students were assigned into groups, and they were given a task, followed by a discussion with",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"that involved reading the assigned lit­ erature, highlighting key quotes, and noting down points of re­ flection. On the day of the seminar, the students were assigned into groups, and they were given a task, followed by a discussion with the whole classroom. The activity had as follows: 2.1.1 | Phase 1: Group Activity In the first phase of each seminar, students began by logging into ChatGPT to generate an answer to a question related to the lecture content. For the seminar following the lectures on John Dewey, Ellen Key, and Immanuel Kant, the students were given one question per seminar: (i) “What is the relationship between the individual and society according to Dewey?”; (ii) “What is the relationship between preserving and changing according to Ellen Key?”; and (iii) “What is the relationship between education and freedom according to Kant?”. The questions were quite open-­ended, but they could be answered based on the lectures and the course literature. For those who did not have an account or preferred not to create one, they were instructed to ask their course instructor",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"The questions were quite open-­ended, but they could be answered based on the lectures and the course literature. For those who did not have an account or preferred not to create one, they were instructed to ask their course instructor for the necessary text. In small groups of four, students had one hour to critically review the generated response. They were asked to identify its strengths and weaknesses and to pinpoint aspects that could be further developed using their course literature, ideally in­ corporating quotes to support their points. Additionally, they needed to evaluate whether the response was connected to education and, if it was not, to create that connection using their course literature. Finally, each group was required to upload the edited and refined versions of the response to the course platform, ensuring it was presented as a coherent and well-­structured text following academic guidelines, including references. 2.1.2 | Phase 2: Group Discussion In the second phase of the seminar, all groups and the teacher gathered together to discuss the literature, starting from the given question. This phase facilitated",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"and well-­structured text following academic guidelines, including references. 2.1.2 | Phase 2: Group Discussion In the second phase of the seminar, all groups and the teacher gathered together to discuss the literature, starting from the given question. This phase facilitated a deeper discussion re­ garding the educational figure or philosopher in focus and their theories, allowing students to explore the material in greater depth and to develop a more nuanced understanding of the sub­ ject matter. 3 | Results In this section, the advantages and disadvantages of using ChatGPT for seminar activities, as expressed by the students, will first be presented. Next, these points will be discussed through the lens of the potential created by ChatGPT-­based ac­ tivities and the strategies developed by the students, focusing on their impact on academic literacy. Finally, the importance of the social aspects of learning and the role of ChatGPT as a mediat­ ing device will be highlighted. 3.1 | Advantages and Disadvantages of Using ChatGPT The study revealed a range of advantages and disadvantages associated with using ChatGPT for engaging with course litera­",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"learning and the role of ChatGPT as a mediat­ ing device will be highlighted. 3.1 | Advantages and Disadvantages of Using ChatGPT The study revealed a range of advantages and disadvantages associated with using ChatGPT for engaging with course litera­ ture, highlighting its impact on students' learning experiences. The main advantages centered around ChatGPT's ability to offer clear summaries and create accessible overviews of philosophi­ cal theories and concepts. The AI-­generated text facilitated their understanding of complex material by breaking down philo­ sophical ideas and academic language into simpler language. This made it easier for students to grasp challenging topics and navigate the course material. As one student noted: The literature is written academically, and the AI text is more “readable”, which gives me an even clearer picture of the theories—Student 4 This accessibility in language allowed students to engage with the content more effectively, using ChatGPT as a tool to support their comprehension. Another significant benefit identified was the structural sup­ port that ChatGPT provided for academic writing. By offering a foundational text, the AI tool helped students organise",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,4,"with the content more effectively, using ChatGPT as a tool to support their comprehension. Another significant benefit identified was the structural sup­ port that ChatGPT provided for academic writing. By offering a foundational text, the AI tool helped students organise their thoughts and build upon a basic structure, making the writ­ ing process less daunting. This can be particularly valuable in cases where students struggle to begin drafting their texts or feel overwhelmed by the complexity of academic concepts and theories. 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",120
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"5 of 11 It helped in such a way that the texts mostly provide an overall understanding/summary of the content we have read. It made it easier to gather our thoughts on the subject—Student 1 ChatGPT's strengths in structuring academic texts and summaris­ ing intricate concepts proved instrumental in guiding students through the learning process, aiding them in breaking down dense material and constructing coherent arguments. Despite these advantages, students also encountered several chal­ lenges and limitations in using ChatGPT. A primary concern was the accuracy and reliability of the AI-­generated content. Many students expressed scepticism regarding the credibility of the in­ formation produced by ChatGPT, particularly due to the absence of references or verifiable sources. Without citations or the ability to trace the origins of the information, students found it difficult to assess the validity of the AI's outputs. This lack of traceability and verification posed a significant drawback, as it undermined the credibility of the generated content and prompted students to cross-­check the information with other sources. Another related issue was ChatGPT's tendency to produce inaccurate or fabricated",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"This lack of traceability and verification posed a significant drawback, as it undermined the credibility of the generated content and prompted students to cross-­check the information with other sources. Another related issue was ChatGPT's tendency to produce inaccurate or fabricated information. The added task of fact-­checking AI-­generated texts not only increased the workload during seminar activities but also revealed a broader challenge: the potential for ChatGPT to produce misleading or false information without users being aware and the need to read it with a critical mind. Another challenge was connected to the variability of ChatGPT's responses. During the first seminar, all the members of one stu­ dent group generated the answer on their personal computers before they began working with it. They realised that ChatGPT was generating a different answer each time, primarily varying in word choice. The content was similar, but it was using synonyms to change the text ever so slightly. Academic texts, however, are highly dependent on semantics, making it confusing for the stu­ dents to work with different versions of the same text. They ulti­ mately",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"similar, but it was using synonyms to change the text ever so slightly. Academic texts, however, are highly dependent on semantics, making it confusing for the stu­ dents to work with different versions of the same text. They ulti­ mately selected the version that seemed most appropriate based on word choice and continued working with that one. A similar approach was taken with the structure of the generated content, where ChatGPT offered varied formats, such as flowing narrative, bullet points, or brief sections under numbered headings. Since the seminar activity required students to build upon the AI-­generated text to produce a coherent academic text to the given question, these different formats presented an additional challenge. A further limitation related to the content generation process was ChatGPT's dependency on the availability of online and open-­access sources. This reliance affected the quality and depth of the generated texts, particularly when dealing with lesser-­known philosophers or niche topics with limited online coverage. For well-­known figures like John Dewey or Immanuel Kant, ChatGPT could generate comprehensive and detailed re­ sponses. However, when the subject",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"and depth of the generated texts, particularly when dealing with lesser-­known philosophers or niche topics with limited online coverage. For well-­known figures like John Dewey or Immanuel Kant, ChatGPT could generate comprehensive and detailed re­ sponses. However, when the subject matter involved less promi­ nent philosophers, such as Ellen Key, the AI-­generated text was noticeably less informative and more reflective in nature, rather than fact-­based. As Student 3 observed: The text about Key was much shorter and less informative than the texts about Dewey and Kant. It did not provide any facts, but rather a reflection on Key's opinions—Student 3 This increased the difficulty for students and, to a certain extent, their scepticism. They could clearly see the limits of what ChatGPT could provide and realised they had to work harder to improve some texts over others. The challenges outlined above illustrate a fundamental tension in using ChatGPT as a learning tool: while it can simplify complex material and support the initial stages of academic writing, its limitations regarding accuracy, variability, and reliance on readily available sources necessitate a critical",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"above illustrate a fundamental tension in using ChatGPT as a learning tool: while it can simplify complex material and support the initial stages of academic writing, its limitations regarding accuracy, variability, and reliance on readily available sources necessitate a critical approach to its use. 3.2 | The Potential of Using the ChatGPT-­Based Activity From the second seminar onwards, the groups, now familiar with the activity's format, began developing strategies. Students realised that they could not entirely depend on ChatGPT for accurate or complete information. In preparation for the lectures and seminars, students begun gathering citations from their course literature and focusing on closely reading the main theories and concepts. The discussions became more critical and reflective, exploring what a citation truly means, how sentences can be interpreted based on the theories, and whether influential figures had written about specific topics or not. Uncertain about how ChatGPT texts were generated, the students were encouraged to discuss their individ­ ual understandings of the course literature and combine them to evaluate and improve the generated texts. Their initial scepticism about ChatGPT's reliability motivated",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,5,"or not. Uncertain about how ChatGPT texts were generated, the students were encouraged to discuss their individ­ ual understandings of the course literature and combine them to evaluate and improve the generated texts. Their initial scepticism about ChatGPT's reliability motivated them to adopt a proactive approach by developing their own strategies. To address the limitations of ChatGPT's responses, students began preparing more thoroughly for the seminar activities. Before the seminars, they focused on extensively reading the course litera­ ture, identifying key concepts, highlighting significant quotations, and noting terms that seemed particularly relevant to the topic at hand. They found that this preparatory work was instrumental in critically assessing the AI-­generated texts. By engaging deeply with the course literature, students became better equipped to ver­ ify the accuracy of ChatGPT's responses, identify any inconsisten­ cies, and make necessary improvements to the texts. To support the AI-­generated texts, we needed to find suitable quotes and references. In this way, we also ensured that what the AI claimed was accurate, as we removed what was not supported by the literature— Student 6 In",180
