DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"6 of 11 European Journal of Education, 2025 This iterative process of verification, evaluation, and enhance­ ment not only improved the quality of their academic writing but also deepened their engagement with the course material, trans­ forming AI use from a passive tool to an active component of the learning process. As one student argued, a good knowledge of the subject or topic is essential for fact-­checking and improving the generated texts. Thus, in the process of harnessing the benefits of and tackling the disadvantages of the ChatGPT-­generated texts, the students actively engaged with the literature, closely examin­ ing citations and considering how they could enhance their aca­ demic texts. According to the students, ChatGPT was able to provide better answers when more online material was available about the in­ dividuals in question, namely John Dewey and Immanuel Kant. However, when it came to Ellen Key, the quality and accuracy of ChatGPT's responses were lacking. To compensate for this, the students had to supplement their understanding by reading ad­ ditional sources. One group had an extensive discussion about whether a",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"when it came to Ellen Key, the quality and accuracy of ChatGPT's responses were lacking. To compensate for this, the students had to supplement their understanding by reading ad­ ditional sources. One group had an extensive discussion about whether a phrase attributed to Ellen Key was genuinely hers or mistakenly assigned to her. They spent time scrutinising all their materials and conducting online searches to fact-­check it. Unable to find any supporting evidence, they decided to remove the phrase from their text and replace it with a citation from their course liter­ ature. According to the students' reflections, this process allowed them to develop a more accurate and credible text. The text from ChatGPT was not entirely correct, including a quote that didn't match, then ChatGPT can provide good inspiration on how to start, but one should base it on the knowledge available in the literature—Student 4 On a similar note, another challenge the students faced was the generic or superficial nature of the generated texts, which often lacked deeper and more nuanced content. Through the group activities, the students",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"the knowledge available in the literature—Student 4 On a similar note, another challenge the students faced was the generic or superficial nature of the generated texts, which often lacked deeper and more nuanced content. Through the group activities, the students collaborated to enhance the texts by add­ ing layers of complexity, positioning them within the educational context, and grounding their arguments with supporting evidence from course readings. This process allowed the students to move beyond passively accepting ChatGPT's responses, encouraging them to approach the texts with a critical eye and aim to im­ prove them. The seminar activities thus encouraged a critical approach to using AI-­generated content. Students were tasked with evaluating the quality of the responses, discussing discrepancies, and solv­ ing problems arising from inaccuracies and superficiality in the texts. For example, students noticed that the AI sometimes pro­ vided a generalised overview rather than specific insights, prompt­ ing them to dive deeper into the literature to enrich the content. They systematically cross-­referenced ChatGPT's outputs with established academic sources, an exercise that not only validated the information but also",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"vided a generalised overview rather than specific insights, prompt­ ing them to dive deeper into the literature to enrich the content. They systematically cross-­referenced ChatGPT's outputs with established academic sources, an exercise that not only validated the information but also reinforced their own understanding. Additionally, by addressing ChatGPT's limitations in generating precise quotes, students were driven to engage more deeply with the literature, sourcing appropriate references to substantiate their arguments. This approach transformed the drawbacks of using AI into learning opportunities, promoting academic literacy by requiring students to critically analyse and improve AI-­generated content. As a result, ChatGPT served not as a “quick fix” but as a mediating tool that facilitated deeper learning. Rather than pas­ sively accepting the AI's outputs, students learned to navigate the complexities of academic writing, integrating their own insights and course knowledge to produce more refined and accurate ac­ ademic texts. 3.3 | ChatGPT and the Social Aspects of Learning One surprising finding of this study was that integrating ChatGPT into seminar activities, complemented by group collaboration, sig­ nificantly enhanced students' sense of togetherness and",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"refined and accurate ac­ ademic texts. 3.3 | ChatGPT and the Social Aspects of Learning One surprising finding of this study was that integrating ChatGPT into seminar activities, complemented by group collaboration, sig­ nificantly enhanced students' sense of togetherness and camara­ derie. As they worked collaboratively with the AI-­generated texts, students reported not only a better understanding of the material but also an enriching and interactive learning environment. This collaborative approach enabled students to actively engage with the course content in ways that solitary study could not facilitate. For instance, when discussing the nuances of a philosophical con­ cept generated by ChatGPT, a student remarked: Discussing with each other and critiquing the AI-­ generated texts confirmed that we understood it correctly—Student 6 This highlights that the act of collaborative critique allowed stu­ dents to build upon their understanding through social interac­ tion with each other. Student 8 agreed by adding: It was good to be able to sit in a group and discuss the answer we got. If I were alone, it would be difficult to judge if something in",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"interac­ tion with each other. Student 8 agreed by adding: It was good to be able to sit in a group and discuss the answer we got. If I were alone, it would be difficult to judge if something in the answer was incorrect— Student 8 The use of ChatGPT in a group-­work setting not only supported individual learning but also significantly enriched group discus­ sions, fostering an environment of collaborative learning that enhanced the overall educational experience. This dynamic un­ derscores the importance of collaborative discourse, as it allows students to validate and refine their individual interpretations of complex concepts through shared insights and collective in­ quiry. As another student noted: It has been great to work in groups on this because everyone has different perspectives and remembers different things, which gives everyone broader knowledge, and you learn from each other. And it goes deeper from person to person—Student 2 The above comment exemplifies the diversity of thought within the group and highlights how varied perspectives exchanged during discussions can lead to deeper engagement and a more nuanced understanding",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,6,"each other. And it goes deeper from person to person—Student 2 The above comment exemplifies the diversity of thought within the group and highlights how varied perspectives exchanged during discussions can lead to deeper engagement and a more nuanced understanding of the subject matter. Such collaborative interplay enhances individual comprehension while enriching the group's collective knowledge, enabling a thorough explora­ tion of complex ideas. ChatGPT's ability to distil complex concepts into concise sum­ maries enhanced students' comprehension of the material and 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",120
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"7 of 11 encouraged deeper discussions among them. For example, when exploring John Dewey's educational theories, the ChatGPT-­ generated text served as a springboard for their discussions. By comparing the AI's interpretation with their own readings, they were able to dissect and debate different aspects of Dewey's phi­ losophy, ultimately enhancing their collective comprehension. All students agreed, to varying degrees, that collaborating to re­ view literature and refine the generated text increased their con­ fidence in understanding the material and alleviated feelings of isolation that often accompany academic study. The students emphasised that discussing their understanding of the course literature with each other and comparing it with the ChatGPT-­ generated texts made them feel less alone in their learning pro­ cess and more assured of their comprehension. Such reflections illustrate how collaboration mitigated feelings of isolation and fostered a supportive learning environment. However, it is essential to note that while ChatGPT served as a useful tool in facilitating engagement with course literature, stu­ dents indicated that their lectures and group work were still the most significant contributors to their understanding",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"learning environment. However, it is essential to note that while ChatGPT served as a useful tool in facilitating engagement with course literature, stu­ dents indicated that their lectures and group work were still the most significant contributors to their understanding of course content. This finding underscores the importance of the social aspects of teaching in higher education. ChatGPT was viewed not as a replacement for traditional learning methods but as an augmentative tool that confirmed or enriched their under­ standing. By utilising the ChatGPT-­based seminar activities to rework and critique generated texts, students were able to clar­ ify complex concepts and confirm their grasp of the material. The iterative nature of these discussions, where students com­ bined AI-­generated content with group dialogue and course ma­ terials, significantly supported their learning experience. This critical engagement with AI technology, group collaboration, and course material embodies a more ecological perspective on teaching. This approach not only enhances individual under­ standing but also fosters a dynamic learning environment where students can collectively explore complex concepts, leveraging AI as a supportive tool in their discussions.",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"course material embodies a more ecological perspective on teaching. This approach not only enhances individual under­ standing but also fosters a dynamic learning environment where students can collectively explore complex concepts, leveraging AI as a supportive tool in their discussions. By integrating these elements, the educational experience can encourage deeper in­ quiry and shared knowledge among students. 3.4 | Utilising ChatGPT as a Mediating Device The concept of a mediating device posits that tools, objects, or technologies enhance human performance by extending cogni­ tive and practical capabilities beyond natural limits. According to Gee (2008), such devices enable users to accomplish tasks and understand concepts that would be unattainable without them. In the context of the suggested course activity, ChatGPT func­ tions as a mediating device, facilitating learning and knowledge production in ways that support students' academic literacy. More specifically, the empirical material demonstrated that ChatGPT—when used in a structured and intentional way— positively contributed to students' academic literacy. ChatGPT assisted by providing summarised and condensed information on key topics already discussed during lectures, as well as by simplifying complex philosophical",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"empirical material demonstrated that ChatGPT—when used in a structured and intentional way— positively contributed to students' academic literacy. ChatGPT assisted by providing summarised and condensed information on key topics already discussed during lectures, as well as by simplifying complex philosophical concepts and pedagogical ideas from influential thinkers. This access to information al­ lowed students to confirm their initial understanding and focus more on synthesising and critically assessing the material. Additionally, by offering a generated text with a clear academic structure, grammar, and content, ChatGPT provided an initial example of how a coherent academic text should look. This sup­ port is particularly important for first-­year university students, who might struggle with constructing academic texts and sum­ marising complex ideas. Utilising both the generated texts and the support of their classmates, students had the opportunity to evaluate information, debate and discuss knowledge by compar­ ing different sources, and practice academic speaking and writ­ ing by refining the generated texts. Gee (2008) argues that knowledge in mediated activity is dis­ tributed across minds, tools, and social interactions. ChatGPT's generated texts exemplify this distribution",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"by compar­ ing different sources, and practice academic speaking and writ­ ing by refining the generated texts. Gee (2008) argues that knowledge in mediated activity is dis­ tributed across minds, tools, and social interactions. ChatGPT's generated texts exemplify this distribution in several ways. When students use ChatGPT, some cognitive load is transferred to the device. Instead of memorising facts or passively copying quotes from the course literature, students could focus on de­ veloping higher-­order thinking skills like analysis and synthe­ sis, leveraging the tool for factual recall. From a sociocultural understanding of learning, instead of relying on ChatGPT for dynamic interactions and feedback, students engaged with each other for that purpose. Processing the AI-­generated text in groups helped to develop and consolidate the knowledge and information we had gathered from the course literature and lectures—Student 7 This added layer of interaction allowed students to use the gen­ erated texts and each other to scaffold their learning. They col­ lectively interacted with the device to discuss its content and quality, and to improve it, fostering a more holistic approach to their",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"interaction allowed students to use the gen­ erated texts and each other to scaffold their learning. They col­ lectively interacted with the device to discuss its content and quality, and to improve it, fostering a more holistic approach to their literacy. If in these seminar activities we perceive ChatGPT as a medi­ ating device, it enhances academic literacy by broadening the scope of what students can achieve independently. Traditional academic activities—such as communicating in an academic manner, actively reading and evaluating information, and de­ bating and creating knowledge through speaking and writing— can be supported by the device's capabilities. By challenging students to edit and improve ChatGPT-­generated texts together, it encourages deeper critical thinking and reflection on the course literature. In line with Gee's theory, ChatGPT can serve as a mediating device that contributes to the development of students' academic literacy; by distributing knowledge and sup­ porting cognitive processes, it enables students to accomplish more than they could unaided. 4 | Discussion To address the first research question on how ChatGPT can be implemented in higher education courses to support",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,7,"literacy; by distributing knowledge and sup­ porting cognitive processes, it enables students to accomplish more than they could unaided. 4 | Discussion To address the first research question on how ChatGPT can be implemented in higher education courses to support and de­ velop academic literacy, the study findings align with previous research that demonstrates integrating ChatGPT into higher ed­ ucation can positively influence students' learning experiences (see Chan and Colloton 2024; Acun and Acun 2023; Sabzalieva and Valentini 2023). ChatGPT's strengths lie in its ability to gen­ erate coherent academic text, explain concepts, and simplify 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",134
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"8 of 11 European Journal of Education, 2025 complex ideas. These features were leveraged in seminar ac­ tivities where students used ChatGPT to work collaboratively on key theories in the philosophy of education. The structured tasks required students to edit, fact-­check, and support AI-­ generated content with course literature, thus deepening their understanding of the material while simultaneously develop­ ing their writing and referencing skills (Acosta-­Enriquez et al. 2024). This process not only enhanced their comprehension of the course content but also encouraged deeper, more active in­ volvement in their learning. When applied in an informed and guided manner, ChatGPT contributed to students' academic lit­ eracy by helping them produce, critique, and refine their work. Regarding the second research question on the challenges and opportunities of using ChatGPT in educational settings, this study mirrors findings from previous research that highlight ChatGPT's limitations, including its restricted knowledge base, potential for misinformation, and tendency to generalise (Teel et al. 2023; Waluyo and Kusumastuti 2024; Pargman et al. 2024). Concerns about student overdependence, plagiarism, and lack of critical engagement (Bender 2024; Ciampa et",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"ChatGPT's limitations, including its restricted knowledge base, potential for misinformation, and tendency to generalise (Teel et al. 2023; Waluyo and Kusumastuti 2024; Pargman et al. 2024). Concerns about student overdependence, plagiarism, and lack of critical engagement (Bender 2024; Ciampa et al. 2023) have also been taken into consideration. Yet, this study demonstrates that structured activities requiring students to critically assess and edit AI-­generated content can transform these challenges into valuable learning opportunities. In addition, aspects of social learning, such as group discussions and collaborative editing, could play a vital role in harnessing ChatGPT's strengths while addressing its limitations; an area that has yet to be thoroughly explored. This research contributes to the existing literature by illustrating how collaborative ChatGPT-­based seminar ac­ tivities can foster peer discussion and collective understand­ ing, while also promoting the ethical use of AI tools; an issue that has been central to debates on AI use in higher education (Williams 2024). Structured interactions with ChatGPT allowed students to critically evaluate the validity and reliability of the information presented, enhancing their understanding of the practical and ethical",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"issue that has been central to debates on AI use in higher education (Williams 2024). Structured interactions with ChatGPT allowed students to critically evaluate the validity and reliability of the information presented, enhancing their understanding of the practical and ethical challenges associated with AI use. By ac­ tively engaging with the tool during the seminars, students were empowered to develop their own strategies for managing its lim­ itations, thereby enhancing their digital and academic literacy; skills that are increasingly argued to be essential for today's stu­ dents (Southworth et al. 2023). However, some challenges still persist, including the tendency of weaker students to rely more on AI-­generated content (Farrelly and Baker 2023) compared to other students. To mitigate this risk of overreliance, the seminar activities emphasised collaborative group work with ChatGPT rather than individual use. This ap­ proach encouraged students not to rely solely on ChatGPT but also to seek understanding and build common knowledge to­ gether in their groups, as students often turn to AI tools when feeling unsupported and in need of immediate feedback (Al-­ Hafdi and AlNajdi",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"not to rely solely on ChatGPT but also to seek understanding and build common knowledge to­ gether in their groups, as students often turn to AI tools when feeling unsupported and in need of immediate feedback (Al-­ Hafdi and AlNajdi 2024; Punar Özçelik and Yangın Ekşi 2024). Thus, the use of ChatGPT in a social manner allowed for an emancipatory interaction between the tool and the students, promoting a deeper, more engaged learning experience (Eybers and Paulet 2022), all the while addressing feelings of isolation. Apart from emphasising the need for a balanced approach to integrating ChatGPT in higher education, the findings also highlight the essential role of teachers as facilitators who guide and supervise students' use of AI tools. While ChatGPT is not a “quick fix”, this study demonstrated that it can be used ef­ fectively to enhance digital literacy and academic skills when implemented with clear pedagogical intentions. Teachers play a crucial role in educating students on the ethical use of AI and mitigating the risks associated with its misuse (Yu 2024; Al-­Khresheh 2024). The integration of ChatGPT",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"literacy and academic skills when implemented with clear pedagogical intentions. Teachers play a crucial role in educating students on the ethical use of AI and mitigating the risks associated with its misuse (Yu 2024; Al-­Khresheh 2024). The integration of ChatGPT in a higher education course, as ex­ plored in this study, further highlights the enduring relevance of certain educational challenges while also offering new ways to address them. Although ChatGPT and AI represent timely technological advancements, the core issues discussed in this paper—academic literacy and adherence to academic integ­ rity—are indeed timeless. Students have historically struggled with academic writing, understanding complex concepts, and the responsible use of sources. By incorporating ChatGPT into seminar activities, this study addresses these age-­old challenges through a contemporary lens. The course content, which draws on the works of influential thinkers such as Kant, Dewey, and Key, is traditionally approached in a conventional manner; how­ ever, using ChatGPT allows for a fresh pedagogical perspective. This counterintuitive combination of classic educational content and AI-­based methods opens up new possibilities for engaging with the material. While the initial",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"Key, is traditionally approached in a conventional manner; how­ ever, using ChatGPT allows for a fresh pedagogical perspective. This counterintuitive combination of classic educational content and AI-­based methods opens up new possibilities for engaging with the material. While the initial motivation for integrating ChatGPT into the course stemmed from concerns about poten­ tial misuse and student overreliance, a structured approach to its implementation shifted these challenges into valuable learning opportunities. By intentionally guiding the use of ChatGPT, the challenges associated with AI became opportunities for active learning and critical engagement, illustrating how even poten­ tial drawbacks can be strategically addressed to enhance educa­ tional outcomes. Using ChatGPT as a reflective and intentional tool can offer a solution for the growing number of students who feel that the technology is either being overlooked or not meaningfully integrated into teaching and learning (Skeat and Ziebell 2023). Since these educational challenges are universal, the findings of this study are applicable to other educational set­ tings, as they emphasise the broader skills of critically assessing information, collaboratively building understanding, and devel­ oping academic literacy",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"(Skeat and Ziebell 2023). Since these educational challenges are universal, the findings of this study are applicable to other educational set­ tings, as they emphasise the broader skills of critically assessing information, collaboratively building understanding, and devel­ oping academic literacy with ChatGPT. Even though this study was conducted in a Swedish context and relied on small-­group seminars, the core principles of structured ChatGPT integration and group collaboration can be adapted to larger or differently structured courses. However, scaling up this approach may re­ quire adjustments based on institutional resources, class size, and teaching format. While this study provides valuable insights into the use of ChatGPT in higher education, several limitations must be con­ sidered when interpreting the findings. One limitation of this study is that ChatGPT was used only for generating initial drafts, with the remainder of the process conducted without AI assistance. This was due to concerns about the lack of a con­ trolled environment and the risk of students relying on ChatGPT over course literature. The outcomes might have differed if students had engaged individually or in groups",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,8,"without AI assistance. This was due to concerns about the lack of a con­ trolled environment and the risk of students relying on ChatGPT over course literature. The outcomes might have differed if students had engaged individually or in groups with ChatGPT throughout the entire activity, which might have reduced op­ portunities for social interaction and collaborative learning. Additionally, during the seminars, students used the free ver­ sion of ChatGPT, specifically GPT-­3.5, which was available at 14653435, 2025, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejed.70131 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",115
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"9 of 11 the time the course was conducted. Given the differences in capabilities and accuracy across various versions of ChatGPT, both past and future, it's important to note that the limitations observed in this study are specific to this version. Furthermore, due to the small sample size, the findings should not be viewed as grounds for overgeneralization to the broader student popu­ lation. Despite this constraint, the empirical material provides valuable insights into students' experiences and interactions with ChatGPT, offering a foundation for further exploration in this area, but caution is needed when applying these findings to larger or more diverse groups. Another consideration is that the course's focus on the philosophy of education may have influ­ enced students' attitudes, as this subject naturally encourages discussion, reflection, and critical thinking. Similar outcomes could likely be observed in other humanities or social sciences courses that require engagement with abstract concepts, the un­ derstanding of complex academic texts, and reliance on widely available theories and sources, all while aiming to mitigate the risks of over-­reliance on AI-­generated material. However, the applicability",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"or social sciences courses that require engagement with abstract concepts, the un­ derstanding of complex academic texts, and reliance on widely available theories and sources, all while aiming to mitigate the risks of over-­reliance on AI-­generated material. However, the applicability of these findings to STEM subjects, which require a different approach to academic texts and literacy, remains un­ certain. Lastly, the environmental impact of using AI tools like ChatGPT at scale (Selwyn 2024; Li et al. 2023) and the resource limitations that vary by institution and geography should also be considered. While such challenges persist, integrating AI liter­ acy in university courses remains crucial for equipping students to engage with AI technologies critically and ethically. 5 | Conclusion This study explored the integration of ChatGPT into higher ed­ ucation teaching focusing on the challenges, opportunities, and strategies involved. The findings reveal that, when used inten­ tionally with clear pedagogical aims, ChatGPT can positively contribute to students' academic literacy. Furthermore, the in­ tegration of ChatGPT was placed within a sociocultural context highlighting how its integration paired with traditional meth­ ods and",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"reveal that, when used inten­ tionally with clear pedagogical aims, ChatGPT can positively contribute to students' academic literacy. Furthermore, the in­ tegration of ChatGPT was placed within a sociocultural context highlighting how its integration paired with traditional meth­ ods and social learning practices, like group discussions and collaborative editing, can enhance the learning experience and address some of its limitations. Nonetheless, the role of teachers remains crucial in guiding the ethical and effective use of AI tools in education, advocating for a balanced approach that emphasises pedagogical ground­ ing and informed integration of AI in higher education. By fostering informed and critical use of tools such as ChatGPT, educators can better prepare students to responsibly navigate and utilise these tools, thereby improving their academic skills and fostering deeper engagement with course content. Several practical strategies emerge from this study. First, it is essen­ tial to design structured activities that align with the course objectives, guiding students in critically assessing and editing AI-­generated content with the help of course literature. Clear guidelines should be provided on how to engage with AI",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"it is essen­ tial to design structured activities that align with the course objectives, guiding students in critically assessing and editing AI-­generated content with the help of course literature. Clear guidelines should be provided on how to engage with AI tools within the context of the course, emphasising the limitations of the tool as opportunities for learning. For instance, students should be encouraged to fact-­check the generated content and develop their own understanding based on reliable sources. Additionally, incorporating social elements and collabora­ tive group work is crucial. This study highlights that peer discussions help students validate their understanding and construct knowledge collectively, mitigating the risks of isola­ tion or overreliance on AI. Beyond this specific case study, this research offers broader insights into the role of AI tools like ChatGPT in higher edu­ cation. It advocates for the integration of AI literacy into curric­ ula, highlighting its potential to equip students with the skills to critically assess and ethically use AI while also enhancing competencies such as critical thinking and academic literacy. By illustrating how ChatGPT can be implemented",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"of AI literacy into curric­ ula, highlighting its potential to equip students with the skills to critically assess and ethically use AI while also enhancing competencies such as critical thinking and academic literacy. By illustrating how ChatGPT can be implemented in higher educa­ tion settings, this study emphasises the need for an intentional and reflective approach to AI integration, rather than dismissal or prohibition. Furthermore, it underscores the importance of pedagogical grounding when adopting such tools, suggesting that a well-­balanced approach where AI tools complement tra­ ditional teaching methods can enhance student learning expe­ riences while fostering critical thinking and problem-­solving skills. To strengthen these findings, further investigation could ex­ plore the use of ChatGPT across various educational contexts and with a larger number of students. For instance, extending this research to teacher education courses could provide valu­ able insights into how future educators perceive and interact with AI tools. Additionally, longitudinal studies would be ben­ eficial in understanding ChatGPT's long-­term effects on aca­ demic literacy and the sustainability of any observed benefits over time. Expanding research to include more",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,"into how future educators perceive and interact with AI tools. Additionally, longitudinal studies would be ben­ eficial in understanding ChatGPT's long-­term effects on aca­ demic literacy and the sustainability of any observed benefits over time. Expanding research to include more diverse student populations is also essential. Previous research indicates that minority and marginalised students may be more inclined to use ChatGPT for academic support, including instances of breach­ ing academic integrity. Investigating its impact on these groups could reveal whether the tool can be adapted to better support students with special needs or from diverse backgrounds, rather than perpetuating existing inequalities. Such exploration would not only enhance understanding of AI's role in higher education but also inform the development of more equitable and targeted strategies for its integration. Moreover, continuing to implement ChatGPT in the same course during the next academic year would present an opportunity to assess its impact on a different group of students. This could determine whether the observed benefits and challenges are consistent across various cohorts or if outcomes vary based on the unique characteristics and",180
DOC_008,Euro J of Education - 2025 - Raptopoulou - ChatGPT in Higher Education  Supporting Academic Literacy Through ChatGPT‐Based.pdf,10.1111/ejed.70131,9,academic year would present an opportunity to assess its impact on a different group of students. This could determine whether the observed benefits and challenges are consistent across various cohorts or if outcomes vary based on the unique characteristics and expe­ riences of each group. Conflicts of Interest The author declares no conflicts of interest. Data Availability Statement The research data are not shared in compliance with ethical guidelines and institutional policies.,72
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,1,"EDITED BY Danny Glick, Oranim Academic College, Israel REVIEWED BY Sarah Wolff, University of Nevada, Las Vegas, United States Rudaiba Adnin, Northeastern University, United States *CORRESPONDENCE Daniel H. Chang dth7@sfu.ca; dchang@athabascau.ca RECEIVED 09 March 2026 REVISED 20 July 2026 ACCEPTED 23 July 2026 PUBLISHED 18 August 2026 CITATION Chang DH, Lin MPC, Huang J-Y and Ryoo J (2026) “Should I tell my teacher?” Student AI disclosure practices, stigma, and self-regulated learning in higher education. Front. Educ. 11:1826174. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Chang, Lin, Huang and Ryoo. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. “Should I tell my teacher?” Student AI disclosure practices, stigma, and self-regulated learning in higher education Daniel H. Chang 1,2*, Michael Pin-Chuan Lin 1,3, Jing-Yuan Huang",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,1,"No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. “Should I tell my teacher?” Student AI disclosure practices, stigma, and self-regulated learning in higher education Daniel H. Chang 1,2*, Michael Pin-Chuan Lin 1,3, Jing-Yuan Huang 1 and Jeeho Ryoo 4 1Faculty of Education, Simon Fraser University, Burnaby, BC, Canada, 2Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, Athabasca University, Athabasca, AB, Canada, 3Faculty of Education, Mount Saint Vincent University, Halifax, NS, Canada, 4Olsen College of Engineering and Science, Fairleigh Dickinson University - Vancouver Campus, Vancouver, BC, Canada As generative artificial intelligence (GAI) tools become popular in education, questions about when and whether students disclose their AI use have emerged as a critical concern for academic integrity and self-regulated learning (SRL). Using SRL as an interpretive lens, we examine undergraduate students’ AI disclosure practices, investigating how their worries about negative consequences, AI usage behaviours, and demographic factors relate to their willingness to disclose AI use in academic settings. Drawing on survey data from 78 undergraduate students enrolled in an online education course, we developed study-specific survey items addressing",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,1,"about negative consequences, AI usage behaviours, and demographic factors relate to their willingness to disclose AI use in academic settings. Drawing on survey data from 78 undergraduate students enrolled in an online education course, we developed study-specific survey items addressing worry, disclosure, and AI use. Given the developmental status of the instrument, we retained two preliminary composite indicators, worry and disclosure to teachers, and analyzed less internally consistent peer-disclosure and AI-use items individually. Our findings reveal a pattern of co-occurring worries: fear of teacher judgment and fear of social stigmatization are strongly correlated, and both are associated with students’ active concealment of AI use. Interpretively, we describe two contrasting response patterns among students: a transparent-disclosure pattern, in which disclosing AI use to instructors appears to reflect part of SRL processes, and an anxious-concealment pattern, in which heavier AI users report less transparency with teachers. A further finding is that anxiety is not associated with reduced disclosure to teachers directly, but is associated with increased peer-only sharing and complete secrecy. Exploratory analyses also suggest demographic patterns worth further study, with academic",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,1,"less transparency with teachers. A further finding is that anxiety is not associated with reduced disclosure to teachers directly, but is associated with increased peer-only sharing and complete secrecy. Exploratory analyses also suggest demographic patterns worth further study, with academic discipline showing the largest association with disclosure and a marginal indication that monolingual students may disclose less than multilingual students. Given the sample size, we reserve our interpretations and treat these as hypotheses for future work rather than established effects. These findings suggest that institutional AI disclosure frameworks may benefit from addressing both policy compliance and the affective and disciplinary dimensions of students’ SRL decision-making. KEYWORDS academic integrity, AI in education (AIED), concealing AI use, generative artificial intelligence, help-seeking, higher education, self-regulated learning, student AI disclosure TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 18 August 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",140
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"Introduction As generative artificial intelligence1 (GAI) technologies become embedded in everyday learning practices, a paradox surfaces: tools designed to support learners may, under some conditions, adversely compromise the development of learners’ self-regulation and intellectual independence (Deneen, 2025). In higher education teaching and learning settings, students have increasingly relied on AI to support their writing and research (Hua and Cunningham, 2025). Yet, this shift raises a critical question: if educators permit student use of AI, are they cultivating agency or dependency? Rather than enabling reflective, strategic learners, the uncritical use of AI may produce passive utilization of to-be-learned information and surface learning. In our study, we engage this concern directly by examining undergraduate students’ AI disclosure practices as behaviours that may reflect their self-regulated learning (SRL) processes under certain conditions. Specifically, in this study, we carefully examine university students’ self-report of their reluctance of sharing AI use, stigma, and ethical uncertainty about AI use. Literature review SRL, as defined by Zimmerman (2002) and Pintrich (2004), includes the cognitive, metacognitive, motivational, and emotional processes through which learners plan, monitor, and adapt their",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"their reluctance of sharing AI use, stigma, and ethical uncertainty about AI use. Literature review SRL, as defined by Zimmerman (2002) and Pintrich (2004), includes the cognitive, metacognitive, motivational, and emotional processes through which learners plan, monitor, and adapt their strategic efforts towards their learning goals. Effective self-regulation and training supports task persistence, adaptive strategy use, and metacognitive reflection (Akamatsu et al., 2019; Azevedo et al., 2010; Xu et al., 2025; Zhang and Zou, 2024). However, how these SRL processes can operate when learners learn with GAI remains unclear and underexplored (Hadwin et al., 2025). Any learning decisions that learners make with GAI tools may signal any phases of SRL cycles. So, this contextual, strategic shift to student GAI use requires closer examination, so we can understand the nuances of students’ metacognitive development and behavioural enactment of SRL in AI-mediated environments. From previous intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) research to modern large language models (LLMs) applications, a review paper has documented the role of GAI in SRL, such as how GAI can be used to support SRL phases through its feedback",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"environments. From previous intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) research to modern large language models (LLMs) applications, a review paper has documented the role of GAI in SRL, such as how GAI can be used to support SRL phases through its feedback mechanisms, goal-setting prompts, and even affect-sensitive interventions (Lan and Zhou, 2025). Theoretically, models of Hybrid Human-AI Regulation (HHAIR) propose combining human oversight with AI affordances to support agency (Molenaar, 2022). However, most SRL frameworks are still grounded in humancentric views of regulation, while AI or GAI research tends to focus on technical performance and achievement, such as AI literacy (Bozkurt, 2024; Chiu, 2025; Ng et al., 2021). Thus, when learners make a decision (e.g., to disclose or conceal GAI), this decision regarding GAI use may reflect adaptive help-seeking under certain conditions (Chang et al., 2023; Lin and Chang, 2023), while these decisions may also equally reflect policy compliance or the management of social risk. Within this broader literature, learners’ decision to disclose their GAI use has recently become a direct object of empirical inquiry. When a person is willing to",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"these decisions may also equally reflect policy compliance or the management of social risk. Within this broader literature, learners’ decision to disclose their GAI use has recently become a direct object of empirical inquiry. When a person is willing to disclose their GAI use in academic work, it does not only imply that they are compliant with the policy, but it might signal a layered, entangled negotiation of asymmetric transparency (Chang et al., 2026; Luo, 2024), normative ambiguity (Barrett and Pack, 2023), psychological risk (Adnin et al., 2025; Gorichanaz, 2023), relational trust shaped by peer norms (Qu and Wang, 2026) and agency (Chang et al., 2026). In fact, in higher education, disclosure of GAI use carries perceived risks. Attitudes toward AI use can be shaped by perceptions of trust, stigma, and fairness (Fyfe, 2023; Hajian et al., 2025), and students may hesitate to disclose AI use due to fears of judgment or perceived policy ambiguity, echoing debates around authorship and stylistic bias (Alvero et al., 2024). Moreover, these risks are not evenly distributed. Ethical concerns surrounding AI detection technologies may",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"may hesitate to disclose AI use due to fears of judgment or perceived policy ambiguity, echoing debates around authorship and stylistic bias (Alvero et al., 2024). Moreover, these risks are not evenly distributed. Ethical concerns surrounding AI detection technologies may also undermine learner trust (Revell et al., 2024; Yeadon et al., 2024), especially for students whose linguistic or cultural backgrounds diverge from dominant training data. Disclosure practices: between compliance and self-regulation Empirical studies about what actually happens when GAI disclosure is required or encouraged in classroom settings is limited but instructive (Overono and Ditta, 2025). García Ramos (2025) introduced a mandatory GAI disclosure form in a general chemistry and honours citizen science course, requiring students to document each instance of GAI use, including the tool, purpose, assignment context, and perceived outcomes. Her qualitative analysis of student responses revealed four primary themes: 1) verification, 2) immediate academic aid, 3) procrastination, and 4) material obstacles. These themes are not merely policy-compliance categories; however, they may also be read as part of students’ SRL processes. For example, students who have used GAI for",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"themes: 1) verification, 2) immediate academic aid, 3) procrastination, and 4) material obstacles. These themes are not merely policy-compliance categories; however, they may also be read as part of students’ SRL processes. For example, students who have used GAI for verification could signal that they are engaging in self-monitoring. Also, those who cited procrastination or material obstacles may have been signaling motivational and contextual challenges to self-regulation. They situate the disclosure form within frameworks of AI literacy, arguing that if GAI use in curriculum is structured thoughtfully, disclosure of GAI use becomes a pedagogical instrument that fosters transparency, ethical engagement, and self-regulation rather than functioning purely as a surveillance or academic integrity mechanism. Overono and Ditta (2025), on the other hand, advanced a complementary argument in the context of undergraduate psychology education, proposing a structured disclosure format: the Model, Input, and Evaluation (MInE) framework. In their model, students declare the AI model used, the exact input provided, and how they evaluated the output. Their review of APA learning outcomes suggests that disclosure statements, compared to outright GAI bans or vague",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,2,"and Evaluation (MInE) framework. In their model, students declare the AI model used, the exact input provided, and how they evaluated the output. Their review of APA learning outcomes suggests that disclosure statements, compared to outright GAI bans or vague honour 1In this article, from time to time, we use GAI/AI interchangeably. In the study context, we are referring generative AI tools, such as ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude. Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",77
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"code policies, offer greater flexibility and pedagogical utility, enabling students to develop skills in ethical communication, critical evaluation of GAI outputs, and transparent attribution. Taken together, these suggest that in educational settings, disclosure might function best as a formative practice, which is the act of documenting GAI use. If structured deliberately, disclosure might promote the metacognitive reflection and ethical reasoning that characterize effective self-regulation. Stigma, fear of judgment, and the social dimensions of disclosure Whether or not students disclose their GAI use is shaped by either institutional policies or a complex social psychology of risk perception, stigma, and interpersonal trust. On one hand, survey research has shown that social influence is a consistent and robust predictor of students’ willingness to accept and adopt GAI tools (Acosta-Enriquez et al., 2025; Qu and Wang, 2026; Wu et al., 2022). On the other hand, if GAI use is stigmatized or institutional signals are ambiguous, concealing GAI use may make students feel safer than being transparent, even for students whose use of GAI is defensible (Qu and Wang, 2026). What actually predicts students’ openness",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"hand, if GAI use is stigmatized or institutional signals are ambiguous, concealing GAI use may make students feel safer than being transparent, even for students whose use of GAI is defensible (Qu and Wang, 2026). What actually predicts students’ openness about GAI may have less to do with ethical conviction than with confidence. Acosta-Enriquez et al. (2025) found that performance expectancy, social influence, and self-efficacy predicted AI adoption, while ethical awareness did not. Based on their findings, if ethical awareness does not independently drive GAI use, one can reasonably infer that it may not independently drive disclosure either. So, self-efficacy may emerge as a consistent predictor for GAI use (Chen et al., 2024; Wang and Li, 2024). Evidence about self-efficacy and GAI use reflects the possibility of SRL. For example, examining 429 Chinese college English-as-foreign-language (EFL) learners, Chen et al. (2024) have demonstrated that AI and self-efficacy directly reduced AI anxiety and enhanced students’ attitudes toward GAI, with both in turn predicting actual use of GAI tools. Similarly, Wang and Li (2024) showed that positive emotional experience with GAI use",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"(2024) have demonstrated that AI and self-efficacy directly reduced AI anxiety and enhanced students’ attitudes toward GAI, with both in turn predicting actual use of GAI tools. Similarly, Wang and Li (2024) showed that positive emotional experience with GAI use and confidence in using digital tools can be strong predictors of willingness for autonomous learning. Interpreting these findings from an SRL perspective, we can see that self- efficacy can play an important role in the forethought phase. It is because self-efficacy can shape the goals learners set, the strategies they choose to enact, and how they respond to tasks (Schunk and Ertmer, 2000). So, for students who feel capable of using GAI critically, they may also feel more able to be open about that use. These social dynamics also intersect with concerns about fairness and equity (Lin et al., 2024; Wang, 2025). In the context of academic publishing, Hosseini et al. (2025) argued that mandatory disclosure of GAI writing assistance could inadvertently increase bias against non-native English speakers, whose use of GAI tools for language support might attract disproportionate scrutiny.",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"Wang, 2025). In the context of academic publishing, Hosseini et al. (2025) argued that mandatory disclosure of GAI writing assistance could inadvertently increase bias against non-native English speakers, whose use of GAI tools for language support might attract disproportionate scrutiny. While their argument is directed at researchers rather than students, the underlying concern translates directly to higher education. This means that disclosure requirements that are formally neutral may carry uneven burdens for students whose linguistic or cultural backgrounds differ from the dominant academic norms. Demographic factors and differential experiences of AI disclosure Emerging evidence indicates that students’ experiences of GAI use and disclosure are not uniform but patterned along with equity reasons (Lin et al., 2024) or demographic cultural factors (i.e., Mouassine and Fatmi, 2025), with disciplinary context and language background standing out as two particularly consequential axes of variation. First, disciplinary context shapes both which ethical concerns students foreground and how confidently they engage with AI tools. Comparing teacher education and business students in the Philippines, Rivera and Osena (2025) found that although both groups acknowledged ethical dilemmas associated",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"First, disciplinary context shapes both which ethical concerns students foreground and how confidently they engage with AI tools. Comparing teacher education and business students in the Philippines, Rivera and Osena (2025) found that although both groups acknowledged ethical dilemmas associated with AI, their priorities diverged sharply. That is, teacher education students oriented toward bias, transparency, over-reliance, and equity, while business students concentrated on privacy and security. This discipline- specific pattern of concern extends to faculty as well. In a cross- disciplinary sample of 100 graduate students and faculty spanning ten disciplines, Gustilo et al. (2024) observed that educators expressed substantial reservations about academic integrity and output quality, alongside limited confidence in using the tools themselves. Taken together, these findings imply that the language in which GAI concerns are articulated is itself disciplinarily patterned. The institutional implication is that AI disclosure policies cannot be neutral with respect to discipline. Chan’s study (2023) also argues for differentiated pedagogical, governance, and operational approaches in which responsibilities are distributed across different groups. Whether the relevant factor is discipline or role, the overall message across",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"policies cannot be neutral with respect to discipline. Chan’s study (2023) also argues for differentiated pedagogical, governance, and operational approaches in which responsibilities are distributed across different groups. Whether the relevant factor is discipline or role, the overall message across these studies implies that any institutional framework for AI disclosure must accommodate the different epistemic cultures and ethical vocabularies through which stakeholders in different fields make sense of GAI use. A second demographic factor that is less systematically studied but directly relevant to GAI use disclosure is language background. For instance, Hu et al. (2025) identified five identity configurations through which participants navigated ChatGPT use in academic writing. The most surprising finding is that the participants conceal their AI use from colleagues and institutions while experiencing acute incongruence between their self-image and their ideal scholarly self. This identity-level finding acquires sharper force when paired with what is known about algorithmic scrutiny of non-native writing. On the other hand, Liang et al. (2023) demonstrated that commercial GPT detectors misclassify text produced by non-native English speakers as AI-generated at substantially higher rates",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,3,"sharper force when paired with what is known about algorithmic scrutiny of non-native writing. On the other hand, Liang et al. (2023) demonstrated that commercial GPT detectors misclassify text produced by non-native English speakers as AI-generated at substantially higher rates than text written by native speakers. This shows a bias that compounds more general reliability problems documented across GAI detection tools (Revell et al., 2024; Yeadon et al., 2024). Taken together, these suggest a distinctive double bind in a way that non-native English speakers face both an internal disclosure barrier (GAI use as a threat to linguistic legitimacy and scholarly voice) and an external one (a higher probability that Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",118
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"their writing will be flagged regardless of whether GAI was used). Current policy frameworks that treat disclosure as a neutral ethical requirement largely fail to recognize this asymmetry, suggesting not only academic integrity but also questions of equity in how AI governance is enacted. Taken together, the emerging evidence about student GAI disclosure has pointed to implications for psychological risks and classroom practices, but little evidence has examined how their worries about negative consequences, their GAI use behaviours and demographic background are associated with their willingness to disclose GAI use in higher education. Theoretical frameworks Our study draws on SRL perspectives as an interpretive lens for understanding students’ AI disclosure practices in higher education, specifically Zimmerman’s and Pintrich’s cyclical models of SRL, and the emerging literature on Hybrid Human- AI Regulation (HHAIR). The SRL frameworks focus more on the cognitive and motivational architecture of the learner, whereas the HHAIR framework attends to the relational and systemic contexts in which AI-mediated learning unfolds. Self-regulated learning Self-regulated learning (SRL), as theorized by Zimmerman (2002) and Pintrich (2004), describes a cyclical process through",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"and motivational architecture of the learner, whereas the HHAIR framework attends to the relational and systemic contexts in which AI-mediated learning unfolds. Self-regulated learning Self-regulated learning (SRL), as theorized by Zimmerman (2002) and Pintrich (2004), describes a cyclical process through which learners set goals, deploy cognitive and metacognitive strategies, monitor their progress, and adjust their approaches in response to feedback. Central to both accounts is the idea that effective learners are not passive recipients of instruction but active agents who orchestrate their own learning processes. Zimmerman’s three-phase model (i.e., forethought, performance, and self-reflection) provides a particularly useful scaffold for understanding how student GAI disclosure might reflect part of SRL behaviours. For example, a student who discloses their use of an AI tool may be engaging in self-monitoring and self- reflection, making visible the strategic choices they have made and inviting external feedback on those choices. On the other hand, Pintrich’s (2004) multidimensional model extends this account by foregrounding motivational and emotional dimensions of self-regulation alongside cognitive ones. In AI- mediated contexts, students’ decisions about whether to use GAI, how to",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"external feedback on those choices. On the other hand, Pintrich’s (2004) multidimensional model extends this account by foregrounding motivational and emotional dimensions of self-regulation alongside cognitive ones. In AI- mediated contexts, students’ decisions about whether to use GAI, how to use it, and whether to disclose that use are not purely cognitive but are shaped by their beliefs about competence, their fears of negative evaluation, and their perceptions of institutional fairness. The SRL construct of help- seeking is especially relevant. Traditionally understood as the willingness to request assistance from intelligent tutoring systems, instructors, or peers (Cheng et al., 2013; Chou et al., 2018), help-seeking in AI-mediated environments now includes the decision to consult an AI tool, which can be characterized as a form of help-seeking that may or may not be disclosed, and whose disclosure carries its own motivational valence. Students who disclose AI use are, in effect, making a help-seeking act visible; those who conceal it may be managing the social risks of that act rather than simply circumventing academic integrity norms. At the same time, disclosure and help-seeking",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"Students who disclose AI use are, in effect, making a help-seeking act visible; those who conceal it may be managing the social risks of that act rather than simply circumventing academic integrity norms. At the same time, disclosure and help-seeking should not be conflated. Help-seeking refers to the act of obtaining assistance (Cheng et al., 2013), whereas disclosure refers to making that assistance visible to others, and the two are logically and temporally separable. A student may disclose AI use before seeking assistance (e.g., checking with an instructor prior to using a tool), after the assistance has already been obtained (e.g., reporting AI use at submission), or independently of any help-seeking at all, as when disclosure simply fulfils a course policy requirement. Accordingly, we treat disclosure as a behaviour that may accompany and make visible adaptive help-seeking under certain conditions, while recognizing that it may also function as compliance with no regulatory significance. Hybrid human-AI regulation (HHAIR) The SRL framework, while foundational, was developed before the proliferation of LLMs and does not fully address the distinctive dynamics of AI-mediated regulation.",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"while recognizing that it may also function as compliance with no regulatory significance. Hybrid human-AI regulation (HHAIR) The SRL framework, while foundational, was developed before the proliferation of LLMs and does not fully address the distinctive dynamics of AI-mediated regulation. Molenaar’s (2022) Hybrid Human-AI Regulation (HHAIR) model offers a productive extension, proposing that in AI-enhanced learning environments, regulation is not located solely within the learner but distributed across a human-AI system. In the HHAIR model, AI tools can support regulatory processes, such as prompting goal setting, providing adaptive feedback, scaffolding reflection (Chang et al., 2023; Lin and Chang, 2023), but GAI tools can also displace them, taking over regulatory functions that, in a purely human context, the learner would perform themselves. Uncritical or undisclosed GAI use may signal not a failure of integrity alone, but can be a failure of the regulatory processes that disclosure is designed to make visible. Azevedo et al.’s (2022) work on GAI as a vehicle for new forms of SRL support extends SRL further, highlighting the affective and dialogic possibilities as regulatory partners, such as",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"the regulatory processes that disclosure is designed to make visible. Azevedo et al.’s (2022) work on GAI as a vehicle for new forms of SRL support extends SRL further, highlighting the affective and dialogic possibilities as regulatory partners, such as simulated self-talk, reflective prompting, and progress visualization. In our study, we examine students’ GAI use and their disclosure practices, which may serve as the bridge between SRL and learning. Taken together, these two SRL and HHAIR traditions can provide a multi-level framework for interpreting students’ AI disclosure in classroom settings for our study. However, we want to make it clear that we did not assess established SRL processes. Instead, we assume disclosure behaviours that may reflect SRL, while it may also reflect students’ awareness to policy compliance, academic integrity concerns, trust or fear of punishment (Gonsalves, 2025; Hosseini et al., 2025). Navigating within these assumptions, we examine whether concealment of GAI use or sharing of GAI use can relate to their worries, usage, and their demographic backgrounds. Inquiry questions Guided by the gaps identified in the literature, we propose three",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,4,"2025). Navigating within these assumptions, we examine whether concealment of GAI use or sharing of GAI use can relate to their worries, usage, and their demographic backgrounds. Inquiry questions Guided by the gaps identified in the literature, we propose three questions. Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",50
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"1. To what extent do students’ worries about negative consequences of GAI use (e.g., judgment, stigmatization, dependence, or replacement) relate to their disclosure behaviors in academic settings? 2. How do students’ GAI usage behaviors (e.g., never using GAI or uploading drafts for review) associate with their perceptions of SRL risks and disclosure patterns? 3. In what ways do demographic factors (e.g., years of study, major, language status, frequent GAI tool use) relate to students’ attitudes toward AI disclosure? Methods Student profiles and procedure There were 78 undergraduate students enrolled in a self-paced online education course at a western Canadian comprehensive university, serving as a required course for education degree programs but an elective for students in other majors. These 78 students participated in an anonymous end-of-course survey designed to gather insights into their experiences with AI tools utilized in the online course. The survey, which offered up to two percent of the course grade as an incentive based on the depth of responses, formed part of a department-wide initiative by the online learning hub to enhance the educational experience within",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"online course. The survey, which offered up to two percent of the course grade as an incentive based on the depth of responses, formed part of a department-wide initiative by the online learning hub to enhance the educational experience within online programs. Aimed at informing future course offerings and curriculum development, this initiative was granted an exemption by the institutional research ethics board. We acknowledge that this incentive may have influenced disclosure-related responding, particularly given that the survey addressed teacher judgment and academic practices; this is discussed further in the Limitations section. The survey was distributed via the Canvas learning management system during the twelfth week of the courses, coinciding with the completion of the core curriculum. This timing enabled students to provide reflective feedback on their overall course experience and AI tool usage. The survey instrument comprised 11 statements, rated on a Likert scale from 1 (disagree) to 5 (agree), as outlined in Table 1. These questions were specifically crafted to examine students’ use and perceptions of AI tools in their coursework, while also exploring the interplay between AI",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"rated on a Likert scale from 1 (disagree) to 5 (agree), as outlined in Table 1. These questions were specifically crafted to examine students’ use and perceptions of AI tools in their coursework, while also exploring the interplay between AI technologies and learning experiences in higher education. The student sample was diverse in years of study (Table 2), with the majority in early to mid-undergraduate levels: 13% in Year 1, 34% in Year 2, 31% in Year 3, 21% in Year 4 or beyond. Majors were predominantly in Psychology/Neuroscience (44%) and Education (26%), with smaller proportions in Business (7%), Economics (6%), Health/Biological Sciences (9%), and other fields, such as Communication, Criminology, or Undeclared (9%). In terms of language status, 46% of the students were monolingual (primarily English speakers), while 54% were multilingual (e.g., English plus Chinese, Punjabi, French, Hindi, or other languages). Regarding frequent use of generative AI (GAI) tools, 76% reported using at least one tool, with ChatGPT being the most common (64% of participants mentioned it), followed by Grammarly (11%), Deepseek (6%), and other tools like Copilot or",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"other languages). Regarding frequent use of generative AI (GAI) tools, 76% reported using at least one tool, with ChatGPT being the most common (64% of participants mentioned it), followed by Grammarly (11%), Deepseek (6%), and other tools like Copilot or Quillbot (each <5%). Approximately 24% reported no frequent GAI tool use. Survey items and variable operationalization Because no validated scale was available for examining AI disclosure in an SRL context, we developed 11 study-specific statements to operationalize three dimensions identified in the literature review, such as worries about negative consequences of AI use, disclosure behaviours across social and academic contexts, and actual AI usage behaviours. The worry items were grounded in SRL accounts of affective and motivational regulation, as concerns about judgment, stigmatization, dependence, and replacement may shape whether students use AI and whether they are willing to make that use visible (Fyfe, 2023; Hajian et al., 2025; Pintrich, 2004; Zimmerman, 2002). The disclosure items treated transparency as a socially situated regulatory behaviour, where students’ decisions to disclose to instructors or peers reflect help-seeking, perceived legitimacy, and ownership of academic",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"use visible (Fyfe, 2023; Hajian et al., 2025; Pintrich, 2004; Zimmerman, 2002). The disclosure items treated transparency as a socially situated regulatory behaviour, where students’ decisions to disclose to instructors or peers reflect help-seeking, perceived legitimacy, and ownership of academic work (Alvero et al., 2024; Molenaar, 2022). The usage items distinguished general non-use from active engagement with AI for draft review, providing behavioural indicators of whether AI was incorporated into students’ learning and writing processes (Azevedo et al., 2022; Hadwin et al., 2025; Winne, 2017). One standalone item captured total secrecy as an extreme form of non-disclosure, allowing comparison with more context-specific disclosure behaviours. We also clarify the rationale for combining the two teacher- directed disclosure items into a preliminary Disclosure to TABLE 1 The eleven survey items. Questions Statements Q1 I am worried that my teacher will judge me negatively if I disclose the use of AI Q2 I share my experiences of using AI tools to other people (including my friends and peers). Q3 I have never used any AI tools for my learning and writing/ research Q4",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,"negatively if I disclose the use of AI Q2 I share my experiences of using AI tools to other people (including my friends and peers). Q3 I have never used any AI tools for my learning and writing/ research Q4 I won’t let anyone in the world know if I use AI for my learning/writing/research Q5 I am worried that I will be replaced by AI Q6 I disclose my use of AI tools to my teachers when I use them for writing and research Q7 I am worried that I will be overly dependent on AI Q8 I am worried that I will be stigmatized if I disclose my use of AI Q9 When I use AI tools for writing and research, I always check with my instructor first Q10 I only disclose the use of AI to my friends or peers when we chat or learn together Q11 I upload my draft to AI tools and ask AI to review my draft (essay) Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",174
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,5,friends or peers when we chat or learn together Q11 I upload my draft to AI tools and ask AI to review my draft (essay) Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org,34
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"Teachers index. Although one item captures retrospective disclosure of GAI use and the other captures prospective permission-seeking, both involve voluntary transparency with instructional authority. Conceptually, both align with adaptive help-seeking in SRL, where the learner treats the instructor as a regulatory resource rather than concealing GAI use (Karabenick, 2004; Ryan and Pintrich, 1997). Empirically, the two items were strongly associated in this sample, supporting their use as a preliminary two-item indicator of teacher-directed transparency. We do not interpret this index as a validated construct. Future work should disaggregate retrospective disclosure and prospective permission-seeking and test whether they form a stable factor in a larger validation sample. The items were developed through an informal, multi-stage content-development process. Item generation began before the term in a research team meeting involving the first, second, and last authors, whose areas of expertise correspond to the dimensions the survey was designed to capture: GAI disclosure practices (first author), SRL (second author), and the technical dimensions of GAI use (last author); the third author, who served as research and teaching assistant, contributed to subsequent refinement. Candidate",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"the dimensions the survey was designed to capture: GAI disclosure practices (first author), SRL (second author), and the technical dimensions of GAI use (last author); the third author, who served as research and teaching assistant, contributed to subsequent refinement. Candidate items were drafted to map explicitly onto the three dimensions identified in the literature review, with phrasing adapted from ethical GAI studies and SRL self-report measures, and item wording was refined across multiple rounds of team discussion prior to distribution. The items were then piloted with the authors’ lab research assistants and five undergraduate volunteers, who completed the survey and confirmed the clarity and relevance of the items. We acknowledge that these procedures constituted informal expert review within the authorship team rather than formal content validation because no external expert panel was consulted, and no structured interviews or content validation was employed. We therefore treat the item set as a theory-informed developmental instrument, and we identify formal content validation as a priority for future work. Table 1 presents the final item wording. Analysis and variables Because the survey items were",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"was employed. We therefore treat the item set as a theory-informed developmental instrument, and we identify formal content validation as a priority for future work. Table 1 presents the final item wording. Analysis and variables Because the survey items were newly developed for this study, the proposed item groupings were treated as theory-informed candidate indices rather than validated scales. Our goal was not to establish a latent measurement model, but to determine whether limited aggregation was defensible for exploratory analysis while preserving item-level transparency. We therefore examined both the conceptual coherence of the item wording and the observed internal consistency of each candidate grouping. Two candidate indices were retained for exploratory analysis. The Worry Index averaged Q1, Q5, Q7, and Q8, which all addressed students’ worries about negative consequences of AI use, including judgment, stigma, dependence, and replacement. The Disclosure to Teachers Index averaged Q6 and Q9, which both addressed voluntary transparency with instructional authority, as described above. Two other candidate groupings were not retained. The proposed Disclosure to Peers Index, which averaged Q2 and Q10, showed weak internal consistency",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"to Teachers Index averaged Q6 and Q9, which both addressed voluntary transparency with instructional authority, as described above. Two other candidate groupings were not retained. The proposed Disclosure to Peers Index, which averaged Q2 and Q10, showed weak internal consistency (α = .34). After reviewing the item wording, we interpreted this as evidence that the items captured different disclosure behaviours: Q2 reflects general willingness to discuss AI experiences with others, whereas Q10 reflects disclosure restricted to friends or peers. We therefore analyzed Q2 and Q10 separately. Similarly, the proposed AI Usage Index, combining inverted Q3 and Q11, showed poor internal consistency (α = –.30). This result was consistent with the item content: Q3 distinguishes non-use from any use, whereas Q11 captures a specific and more intensive form of AI engagement, namely uploading drafts for AI review. We therefore analyzed Q3 and Q11 separately. This procedure was exploratory and should not be interpreted as construct validation. We did not revise the item groupings to maximize alpha, remove items to improve reliability, or claim that the retained indices represent validated scales. Instead,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"and Q11 separately. This procedure was exploratory and should not be interpreted as construct validation. We did not revise the item groupings to maximize alpha, remove items to improve reliability, or claim that the retained indices represent validated scales. Instead, we report the reliability results for all candidate groupings and use item-level analyses throughout to preserve transparency about the developmental status of the instrument. For the exploratory regression analysis addressing Question 3, academic discipline was collapsed from 21 reported categories into five broader groupings to ensure adequate cell sizes and stable parameter estimates. Groupings were determined based on theoretical similarity of disciplinary traditions and sample size considerations. Specifically, disciplines rooted in the natural and applied sciences, including Biology, Biomedical Physiology, Molecular Biology, Microbiology, Behavioural Neuroscience, Computing Science, Kinesiology, and Health Sciences, were combined into a single STEM and Health category, as several of these disciplines (e.g., Health Sciences, Kinesiology) had very few respondents when examined individually. Social science disciplines (i.e., Criminology, Psychology, Political Science, Economics, and Interactive Arts and Technology) were merged into a Social Sciences grouping based on their",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"as several of these disciplines (e.g., Health Sciences, Kinesiology) had very few respondents when examined individually. Social science disciplines (i.e., Criminology, Psychology, Political Science, Economics, and Interactive Arts and Technology) were merged into a Social Sciences grouping based on their shared emphasis on human behavior and social systems; students with undeclared majors were also placed in this category given their broad, exploratory orientation. Humanities disciplines, including English, Philosophy, History, and Communication, were combined on the basis of their common grounding in interpretive and text-based inquiry. Education was retained as a standalone category because education students, while potentially drawn from diverse disciplinary backgrounds, share a common pedagogical training that may uniquely shape their orientation toward AI disclosure in academic settings. Business served as the reference category. We acknowledge that the Business reference group is small (n ≈ 5; 7% of the sample), which reduces the stability of parameter estimates for that category. We retained Business as the reference because it represents a theoretically meaningful contrast, but we interpret the relative comparisons with caution. Reliability analysis Cronbach’s alpha was used to examine",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,6,"sample), which reduces the stability of parameter estimates for that category. We retained Business as the reference because it represents a theoretically meaningful contrast, but we interpret the relative comparisons with caution. Reliability analysis Cronbach’s alpha was used to examine the internal consistency of the four theory-informed candidate indices. These reliability estimates were used as preliminary evidence of item coherence, not as evidence of construct validity. Because the sample size was modest and the instrument was newly developed, we did not conduct confirmatory factor analysis or make claims about Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",98
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,7,"latent dimensionality. The Worry Index showed acceptable internal consistency (α = .71), supporting its use as a preliminary composite indicator of AI-related worry in this sample. The Disclosure to Teachers Index also showed acceptable internal consistency (α = .80). Because this was a two-item index, we also considered the inter-item association between Q6 and Q9 (r = .66), which supported treating the two items as a preliminary indicator of teacher-directed transparency. In contrast, the proposed Disclosure to Peers Index and AI Usage Index showed poor internal consistency (α = .34 and α = –.30, respectively). These results were reported rather than corrected through post hoc item removal. The items in these groupings were therefore analyzed separately. Table 3 reports the reliability estimates for all candidate indices. All computations were performed using a statistical software JASP, with missing data handled via listwise deletion to ensure consistency. Results Item-level correlation analysis To explore the relationships between students’ perceptions of AI, their anxieties regarding its use, and their disclosure behaviors, a series of Pearson correlation coefficients were calculated among the 11 survey items.",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,7,"listwise deletion to ensure consistency. Results Item-level correlation analysis To explore the relationships between students’ perceptions of AI, their anxieties regarding its use, and their disclosure behaviors, a series of Pearson correlation coefficients were calculated among the 11 survey items. The results of the correlation analysis are summarized in the Table 4 below and highlight several key patterns. Fear of Judgment and Fear of Stigma. The strongest bivariate relationship in the dataset was between fear of teacher judgment (Q1) and fear of social stigmatization (Q8), r = .686, p < .001. Students who worry about teacher judgment are also likely to worry about broader social stigma, suggesting that these concerns co-occur rather than operating as independent sources of anxiety. Both concerns were associated with the intention to conceal AI use entirely (Q4): Q1–Q4, r = .402, p < .001; Q8–Q4, r = .467, p < .001. We describe this as a pattern of co-occurring worries associated with concealment behaviour. We are careful not to characterize this pattern as a recursive loop, since our cross-sectional design cannot test whether worry and",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,7,"r = .467, p < .001. We describe this as a pattern of co-occurring worries associated with concealment behaviour. We are careful not to characterize this pattern as a recursive loop, since our cross-sectional design cannot test whether worry and concealment reinforce one another over time. Teacher-Directed Disclosure & Permission-Seeking. A second pattern in the data is consistent with adaptive help-seeking. Disclosure to teachers (Q6) and checking with instructors first (Q9) were strongly positively correlated (r = .663, p < .001), consistent with the interpretation of these items as joint indicators of proactive transparency with instructional authority. Students who reported disclosing to teachers were also less likely to restrict disclosure to peers, as indicated by the negative correlation between Q6 and Q10 (r = –.361, p = .001). This suggests that when students engage instructors transparently, they are less reliant on peer-only disclosure. Intensity of AI Use and Worries. Students who reported frequent use of AI for drafting and reviewing essays (Q11) also reported higher worry about dependence (Q7; r = .415, p < .001) and replacement (Q5; r =",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,7,"on peer-only disclosure. Intensity of AI Use and Worries. Students who reported frequent use of AI for drafting and reviewing essays (Q11) also reported higher worry about dependence (Q7; r = .415, p < .001) and replacement (Q5; r = .342, p = .002). Worry about dependence (Q7) was also associated with fear of teacher judgment (Q1; r = .365, p = .001). Casual peer sharing (Q2) was not significantly correlated with fear of teacher judgment (r = .088, p = .444) or fear of AI replacement (r = –.017, p = .879), indicating that general social discussion of AI occurs relatively independently of the academic anxieties implicated in more formal disclosure decisions. Descriptive statistics of AI perception and disclosure Before conducting the inferential analysis, descriptive statistics were calculated for all 11 survey items to establish the baseline of TABLE 2 Student sample demographics. Characteristic Category Percentage Year of Study Year 1 13% Year 2 34% Year 3 31% Year 4 or beyond 21% Major Psychology/Neuroscience 44% Education 26% Business 7% Economics 6% Health/Biological Sciences 9% Other (e.g., Communication, Criminology,",180
