DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,9,"student attitudes and behaviors. The results, summarized in the provided Table 5 below, indicate varying levels of anxiety and a generally high inclination toward transparency with instructors. Stigma of AI Use and Worries. These worries are relevant to the affective barriers that SRL accounts associate with help- seeking. Within the Worry Index, students reported the highest concern regarding teacher evaluation. Specifically, the statement “I am worried that my teacher will judge me negatively if I disclose the use of AI” (Q1) yielded the highest mean in this category (M = 3.231, SD = 1.357). Other anxieties, such as the fear of being replaced by AI (Q5; M = 2.615, SD = 1.321) or becoming overly dependent on the technology (Q7; M = 2.654, SD = 1.317), were reported at more moderate levels. Similarly, the fear of social stigmatization (Q8) showed a moderate mean of 2.679 with SD = 1.294. Disclosure Behaviours to Instructor. Despite reported worries about judgment for using AI, students indicated a relatively high level of transparency towards authority figures (i.e., instructors). The Disclosure To Teachers items showed",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,9,"a moderate mean of 2.679 with SD = 1.294. Disclosure Behaviours to Instructor. Despite reported worries about judgment for using AI, students indicated a relatively high level of transparency towards authority figures (i.e., instructors). The Disclosure To Teachers items showed strong mean scores, with students frequently checking with instructors first (Q9; M = 3.897, SD = 1.364) and disclosing AI use during the research process (Q6; M = 3.872, SD = 1.293). In contrast, while students were generally willing to share experiences with peers (Q2; M = 3.526, SD = 1.256), they were less likely to restrict disclosure to peers only (Q10; M = 2.679, SD = 1.284). Notably, the general disclosure (total secrecy) item (Q4), “I won’t let anyone in the world know if I use AI” received the lowest overall mean (M = 1.59, SD = 0.746), suggesting that total secrecy is rare among this respondent group. AI Usage Levels. Regarding general AI usage, the inverted score for Q3 (M = 3.551, SD = 1.052) suggests that a majority of respondents have integrated AI tools into their learning",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,9,"that total secrecy is rare among this respondent group. AI Usage Levels. Regarding general AI usage, the inverted score for Q3 (M = 3.551, SD = 1.052) suggests that a majority of respondents have integrated AI tools into their learning or research to some degree. However, more advanced or specific behaviors, such as uploading drafts for AI review (Q11), were less common, showing a lower average of 2.244 with SD = 1.416. Composite-level correlation analysis From Table 6 below, the correlation analysis for the composite variables and individual items provides a more detailed view of how students’ worries of using AI relate to their actual academic learning behaviors. Relationships Between Student Worries and AI Disclosure. The analysis indicates that general academic anxieties, as captured by the Worry Index, have a complex relationship with disclosure. While the Worry Index does not show a statistically detectable relationship with proactive Disclosure to Teachers (r = −0.141, p = .217), it is strongly and positively correlated with more secretive behaviors. For example, we have found that higher levels of worry are statistically associated with",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,9,"show a statistically detectable relationship with proactive Disclosure to Teachers (r = −0.141, p = .217), it is strongly and positively correlated with more secretive behaviors. For example, we have found that higher levels of worry are statistically associated with a desire for total non-disclosure (Q4) (r = 0.409, p < .001) and a tendency to limit disclosure strictly to friends and peers (Q10) (r = 0.292, p = .009). This tells us that while anxiety might not directly stop TABLE 5 Descriptive analysis of survey items. Survey Item Composite Scores Summary Variables Item Average Standard Deviation Avg Lower 95% CI Upper 95% CI SD Lower 95% CI Upper 95% CI Worry Index Q1 I am worried that my teacher will judge me negatively if I disclose the use of AI 3.231 2.93 3.532 1.357 1.173 1.611 Q5 I am worried that I will be replaced by AI 2.615 2.322 2.909 1.321 1.142 1.569 Q7 I am worried that I will be overly dependent on AI 2.654 2.361 2.946 1.317 1.138 1.564 Q8 I am worried that I will be",180
