DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"a student from telling a teacher, it in fact is associated with a greater likelihood that they will prefer hiding their AI use or keeping the conversation within safe social circles. Interpreting from the SRL angle, we have found that worried students did not simply stop talking about AI; however, they appeared to redirect and carry the conversation toward their peers. That is, teachers can give calibrated feedback on whether a student’s AI use is productive or not, but peers generally cannot. Thus, by avoiding teacher disclosure, worried students may miss the feedback that would have supported their SRL. AI Usage Patterns, Worry, and Disclosure. Student usage behaviors of AI also show strong relationships to both anxiety and disclosure patterns. Students who more frequently use AI for sophisticated academic learning tasks, such as uploading drafts for review (Q11), report statistically detectable higher scores on the Worry Index (r = 0.477, p < .001). These frequent users are also more likely to engage in secretive behaviors (Q4) (r = 0.305, p = .007) and peer-only disclosure (Q10) (r = 0.336, p",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"detectable higher scores on the Worry Index (r = 0.477, p < .001). These frequent users are also more likely to engage in secretive behaviors (Q4) (r = 0.305, p = .007) and peer-only disclosure (Q10) (r = 0.336, p = .003), yet they show no statistical detectable increase in transparency with their instructors (r = −0.146, p = .201). This means that higher engagement with AI tools in the current academic context may be associated with increased psychological worries and a reliance on informal (i.e., peers) rather than formal support systems. AI Disclosure. Our results further clarify the distinction between different targets of disclosure. Disclosure to Teachers is negatively correlated with peer-only disclosure (Q10) (r = −0.333, p = .003*), suggesting that students who are transparent with authority figures are less likely to rely on secretive peer-sharing. Interestingly, General Peer Sharing (Q2), such as the act of simply discussing AI experiences with friends, appears to be a neutral social behavior, as it shows no statistical detectable correlation with the Worry Index (r = 0.071, p = .534) or Teacher",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"Peer Sharing (Q2), such as the act of simply discussing AI experiences with friends, appears to be a neutral social behavior, as it shows no statistical detectable correlation with the Worry Index (r = 0.071, p = .534) or Teacher Disclosure (r = −0.019, p = .866). This implies that students’ social chatter about AI is widespread and independent of the specific academic anxieties that are associated with more secretive or formal disclosure habits. Exploratory regression analysis of demographic associations with AI disclosure to teachers Before reporting these results, we clarify how the regression analysis should be interpreted. With a sample of 78 students, the model is underpowered for confirmatory inference, and the five-category discipline variable produced small cells, especially for the Business reference group. We therefore treat this analysis as exploratory and hypothesis-generating. The coefficients are meant to describe the demographic patterns observed in this sample, not to imply any stable population-level effects, and no causal conclusions are drawn. Accordingly, the item- and composite-level descriptive and correlational analyses reported above should be read as the primary evidence, while the",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"the demographic patterns observed in this sample, not to imply any stable population-level effects, and no causal conclusions are drawn. Accordingly, the item- and composite-level descriptive and correlational analyses reported above should be read as the primary evidence, while the regression provides a preliminary indication of demographic variation that future studies with larger samples can test more directly. To examine whether demographic factors were associated with AI disclosure to teachers, a multiple regression analysis was conducted with the Disclosure_To_Teachers_Index as the dependent variable. Three demographic predictors were entered simultaneously: academic discipline (collapsed into five broad categories: Education, Humanities, STEM and Health, Social Sciences, and Business, language status (monolingual vs. multilingual), and years of study (treated as a continuous variable). Descriptive statistics indicated that the sample (N = 78) reported a mean Disclosure_To_Teachers_Index score of 3.89 (SD = 1.21) and a mean years of study of 2.68 (SD = 1.18). The overall regression model was statistically detectable, R2 = .22, adjusted R2 = .16, F(6, 71) = 3.42, p = .005, RMSE = 1.11. The model accounted for approximately 22.4% of",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"mean years of study of 2.68 (SD = 1.18). The overall regression model was statistically detectable, R2 = .22, adjusted R2 = .16, F(6, 71) = 3.42, p = .005, RMSE = 1.11. The model accounted for approximately 22.4% of the total variance in disclosure to teachers, with an adjusted R2 of.16 after correcting for the number of predictors. Part and partial correlations indicated that academic discipline had the largest unique association with the outcome (partial r = .36, part r = .34), followed by language status (partial r = .23, part r = .21), while years of study showed the weakest unique relationship (partial r = –.12, part r = –.11). The full regression results are presented in Table 7 below. Academic discipline emerged as the strongest predictor of AI disclosure to teachers. Relative to Business students, students in Education reported statistically detectably higher disclosure scores, B = 1.94, SE = 0.64, t(71) = 3.05, p = .003, 95% CI [0.67, 3.20]. Students in Social Sciences also scored statistically higher, B = 1.78, SE = 0.61, t(71) = 2.93,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"Education reported statistically detectably higher disclosure scores, B = 1.94, SE = 0.64, t(71) = 3.05, p = .003, 95% CI [0.67, 3.20]. Students in Social Sciences also scored statistically higher, B = 1.78, SE = 0.61, t(71) = 2.93, p = .004, 95% CI [0.57, 2.98], as did students in STEM and Health, B = 1.65, SE = 0.66, t (71) = 2.51, p = .014, 95% CI [0.34, 2.96]. On the other hand, students in Humanities did not differ detectably from the Business students, B = 0.92, SE = 0.85, t(71) = 1.08, p = .285, 95% CI [–0.78, 2.61]. In other words, three of the four discipline groupings showed statistically detectable higher disclosure scores compared to Business, with Education showing the largest difference, followed by Social Sciences and STEM and Health. These exploratory results suggest that SRL-relevant AI disclosure norms may vary across disciplinary contexts, but this interpretation should be tested in larger samples with more stable subgroup sizes. Language status was a marginally detectable predictor of disclosure to teachers. Monolingual students reported lower disclosure scores than",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"AI disclosure norms may vary across disciplinary contexts, but this interpretation should be tested in larger samples with more stable subgroup sizes. Language status was a marginally detectable predictor of disclosure to teachers. Monolingual students reported lower disclosure scores than multilingual students, B = –0.51, SE = 0.26, t(71) = –1.97, p = .053, 95% CI [–1.02, 0.01]. Although this effect did not reach conventional statistical detectability at the α = .05 level, the partial correlation (partial r = .23) and the direction of the coefficient show a pattern in a way that monolingual students tended to be less forthcoming about their AI use with instructors compared to their multilingual counterparts. It is noted that the 95% confidence interval for this coefficient was narrow and just barely included zero, indicating that the effect, while not statistically decisive at this sample size, warrants further investigation. In this regression analysis, Years of study was treated as a continuous predictor, and the variable did not detectably predict disclosure to teachers, B = –0.12, SE = 0.12, β = –0.12, t(71) = – 1.04,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,11,"size, warrants further investigation. In this regression analysis, Years of study was treated as a continuous predictor, and the variable did not detectably predict disclosure to teachers, B = –0.12, SE = 0.12, β = –0.12, t(71) = – 1.04, p = .303, 95% CI [–0.35, 0.11]. The standardized coefficient (β = –0.12) indicated a small negative association, suggesting a slight tendency for students further along in their studies to report lower disclosure, but this relationship was not reliable. This finding indicates that the number of years a student had Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",99
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"been studying was not systematically associated with disclosing AI use to teachers, after controlling for discipline and language status. From an SRL perspective, this null result suggests that AI disclosure norms do not develop automatically through academic progression and may require deliberate curricular scaffolding. In summary, the regression model was statistically detectable, F(6, 71) = 3.42, p = .005, with demographic factors collectively accounting for 22.4% of the variance in students’ AI disclosure to teachers. Academic discipline was the most influential predictor: students in Education, Social Sciences, and STEM and Health all reported detectably higher levels of disclosure than Business students, whereas Humanities students did not differ detectably. Language status showed a marginally detectable effect (p = .053), with monolingual students tending to disclose less than multilingual students. Years of study was not a detectable predictor. Discussion This study examined how undergraduate students’ worries about AI, their AI usage behaviours, and their demographic backgrounds relate to disclosure of AI use in academic settings. Across item-level correlations, composite-level correlations, and a regression model, our findings describe a tension between two features",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"how undergraduate students’ worries about AI, their AI usage behaviours, and their demographic backgrounds relate to disclosure of AI use in academic settings. Across item-level correlations, composite-level correlations, and a regression model, our findings describe a tension between two features of students’ AI experience: growing reliance on AI tools for learning, and a persistent culture of anxiety and selective silence surrounding that use. Our contribution to the existing literature on student AI disclosure (e.g., Adnin et al., 2025; Barrett and Pack, 2023; Luo, 2024) is threefold: we offer an SRL-informed interpretation of student-AI disclosure as a behaviour that may reflect their help-seeking, we document that worry appears to be associated with redirection of disclosure toward peers rather than its outright suppression, and we identify disciplinary variation that prior work has not systematically examined. We emphasize that our design is cross-sectional and our sample small; all claims below are interpretive and correlational. Moreover, because we did not directly measure SRL processes such as planning, monitoring, or strategy use, whether disclosure functions as an indicator of self- regulation remains an empirical question",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"and our sample small; all claims below are interpretive and correlational. Moreover, because we did not directly measure SRL processes such as planning, monitoring, or strategy use, whether disclosure functions as an indicator of self- regulation remains an empirical question for future research. So, in this section, we mainly use SRL as an interpretative framework to guide our understanding of student AI disclosure behaviours. Co-occurring worries about authority and peers In this study, the most striking finding from our current correlation analysis is the pair between fear of teacher judgment (Q1) and fear of social stigmatization (Q8) representing the strongest bivariate relationship in our results (r = .69). This pairing suggests that for many students, worries about AI disclosure co-occur across authority-related and peer-related dimensions rather than operating independently. Both worry items were associated with the intention to conceal AI use entirely (Q4) (Antepuesto et al., 2025; Steinberger et al., 2021). This finding is consistent with the scholarship about academic help-seeking, which has consistently shown that students who perceive a high social cost to seeking assistance are less likely to",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"use entirely (Q4) (Antepuesto et al., 2025; Steinberger et al., 2021). This finding is consistent with the scholarship about academic help-seeking, which has consistently shown that students who perceive a high social cost to seeking assistance are less likely to engage in adaptive help-seeking behaviours (Chang et al., 2020; Karabenick, 2004; Ryan and Pintrich, 1997; Winograd and Rust, 2014). What our study adds to the current understanding about help seeking is that this avoidance dynamic extends to AI disclosure specifically, as AI-mediated learning might be a context where the norms around “acceptable” using-AI-in- learning behaviour are still being negotiated across institutions (Celik et al., 2022; Chan, 2023; Cotton et al., 2023; Perkins, 2023). From the SRL perspective, these co-occurring worries might hinder learning and might be problematic because concealing AI use may stop students from receiving instructors’ feedback. More specifically, when students discuss their AI use with their instructors, disclosure can provide or create opportunities for mutual calibration, such as receiving feedback on whether their use is appropriate, productive, or turning into passive dependence (Atchley et al., 2024; Banihashem et",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"when students discuss their AI use with their instructors, disclosure can provide or create opportunities for mutual calibration, such as receiving feedback on whether their use is appropriate, productive, or turning into passive dependence (Atchley et al., 2024; Banihashem et al., 2025; Chang et al., 2023; Overono and Ditta, 2025; Schunk and Zimmerman, 2013). The co-occurring tendency might remove this regulatory mechanism, leaving students to navigate AI integration without external guidance. This finding resonates with recent arguments that the absence of clear institutional AI policies creates ambiguity that students resolve through avoidance rather than engagement (Gonsalves, 2025; Smit et al., 2025; Song, 2024). TABLE 7 Multiple regression results predicting disclosure to teachers from demographic factors. Predictor B SE β t p 95% CI [LL, UL] (Intercept) 2.733 0.729 — 3.748 <.001 [1.279, 4.188] Discipline (Education) 1.936 0.635 — 3.051 .003 [0.671, 3.201] Discipline (Humanities) 0.915 0.850 — 1.078 .285 [–0.779, 2.610] Discipline (STEM & Health) 1.651 0.658 — 2.511 .014 [0.340, 2.962] Discipline (Social Sciences) 1.776 0.605 — 2.934 .004 [0.569, 2.983] Status (Monolingual) –0.507 0.258 — –1.966 .053 [–1.022,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,12,"[0.671, 3.201] Discipline (Humanities) 0.915 0.850 — 1.078 .285 [–0.779, 2.610] Discipline (STEM & Health) 1.651 0.658 — 2.511 .014 [0.340, 2.962] Discipline (Social Sciences) 1.776 0.605 — 2.934 .004 [0.569, 2.983] Status (Monolingual) –0.507 0.258 — –1.966 .053 [–1.022, 0.007] Years of Study –0.120 0.116 –0.117 –1.037 .303 [–0.351, 0.111] Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",60
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"Two observed response patterns: transparent tendency and anxious-AI- concealing tendency From the correlations we observed, two interpretive response patterns can be described. We refer to these as interpretive tendencies rather than empirically derived profiles, as we did not conduct a formal latent-class or clustering analysis, and our sample size would be inadequate for such methods in any case. The first pattern, which we describe as transparent disclosure, is characterized by students who disclose AI use to teachers (Q6) and check with instructors before using AI (Q9). These behaviours were strongly correlated (r = .66), and students showing this pattern were less likely to rely on peer-only disclosure (Q6–Q10, r = –.36). Consistent with SRL models, this pattern treats instructor interaction as an external regulatory checkpoint in the learning process (Chang et al., 2023; Jin et al., 2023; Pintrich, 2004). The second pattern, which we describe as anxious concealment, is characterized by frequent use of AI for substantive academic tasks (Q11) combined with elevated worry about dependence (Q7), replacement (Q5), and stigma (Q8). Students showing this pattern reported no detectable increase",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"second pattern, which we describe as anxious concealment, is characterized by frequent use of AI for substantive academic tasks (Q11) combined with elevated worry about dependence (Q7), replacement (Q5), and stigma (Q8). Students showing this pattern reported no detectable increase in disclosure to teachers (r = –.15) but greater peer-only disclosure (Q10) and greater total secrecy (Q4). Although we do not claim to have identified a discrete “profile” of students, this interpretive pattern is consistent with Azevedo et al.’s (2022) description of maladaptive regulation, in which learners use tools without the metacognitive oversight needed to ensure productive learning. Importantly, the mean for Q11 was relatively low overall (M = 2.24, SD = 1.42), so intensive AI- assisted drafting appears to remain uncommon in this sample; future research with larger and more diverse samples could test whether this pattern replicates. Heavy AI use without regulatory oversight: risks of maladaptive regulation According to our data, we have shown that students who frequently upload drafts for AI review (Q11) reported the highest Worry Index scores in our dataset (r = .477), alongside elevated",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"AI use without regulatory oversight: risks of maladaptive regulation According to our data, we have shown that students who frequently upload drafts for AI review (Q11) reported the highest Worry Index scores in our dataset (r = .477), alongside elevated concerns about becoming overly dependent on AI (Q7; r = .415) and being replaced by it (Q5; r = .342). We interpret that these students showed no statistically detectable increase in disclosure to teachers (r = –.146), and were instead more likely to engage in complete secrecy (Q4; r = .305) and peer-only sharing (Q10; r = .336). Taken together, this is a tendency among students who are most deeply engaged with AI tools yet are simultaneously most disconnected from the formal instructor feedback that could have helped them evaluate whether that engagement is productive. From an SRL perspective, students’ intensive tool use paired with elevated worry and withdrawal from formal feedback is consistent with what has been described as maladaptive regulation (Azevedo et al., 2010). Adaptive SRL involves not only the deployment of strategies but the ongoing monitoring and",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"intensive tool use paired with elevated worry and withdrawal from formal feedback is consistent with what has been described as maladaptive regulation (Azevedo et al., 2010). Adaptive SRL involves not only the deployment of strategies but the ongoing monitoring and evaluation of whether those strategies are supporting genuine learning. For instance, as students use AI heavily, but they avoid the instructor feedback that would allow them to calibrate that use, they effectively short-circuit the monitoring and evaluation phases of the SRL cycle. The worry they report about dependency (Q7) and replacement (Q5) may itself signal that students sense something is not working in their regulation. But without the external formal scaffolding that instructor disclosure could have provided, that student worry does not translate into corrective SRL action. Also, it is worth noting that the mean for Q11 was relatively low overall (M = 2.244, SD = 1.416), suggesting that intensive AI-assisted drafting remains less common in this sample. However, among students who do engage in this behaviour, the pattern of elevated worry paired with concealment is consistent and statistically detectable.",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"overall (M = 2.244, SD = 1.416), suggesting that intensive AI-assisted drafting remains less common in this sample. However, among students who do engage in this behaviour, the pattern of elevated worry paired with concealment is consistent and statistically detectable. These students represent a group for whom the risks of passive, uncritical AI reliance are most acute, and who are simultaneously least likely to receive the instructional attention that could help address it. Due to limited context, we do not want to overinterpret our findings related to maladaptive regulation, but we do hope that future research should examine whether targeted instructional interventions for normalizing disclosure conversations around AI can interrupt this short- circuiting of the SRL cycle and redirect heavy users’ attention towards more adaptive regulatory pathways. Association between worry about AI use and peer sharing A further interpretive finding is that the Worry Index did not detectably relate to disclosure to teachers (r = –.14, p = .217) but was positively associated with total secrecy (Q4; r = .41) and peer-only sharing (Q10; r = .29). One interpretation is",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"is that the Worry Index did not detectably relate to disclosure to teachers (r = –.14, p = .217) but was positively associated with total secrecy (Q4; r = .41) and peer-only sharing (Q10; r = .29). One interpretation is that anxiety might not suppress disclosure wholesale but shifts its target, away from authority figures and toward safer social outlets. If this interpretation holds, it would have practical implications for the design of AI disclosure frameworks. Policies that rely solely on mandatory disclosure at the assignment level may be less effective than interventions that address the affective dimension of disclosure (normalizing AI use, reducing perceived judgment, creating psychologically safe spaces for discussion). We stress that this interpretation is exploratory and hypothesis- generating rather than confirmed. Longitudinal or experimental designs would be needed to test the redirection hypothesis directly. Disciplinary cultures & variations in disclosure practices In our exploratory regression analysis, we have shown there is a possible association between demographic factors (i.e., academic discipline) and the level of student disclosure of AI use to teachers. For our Inquiry Question 3,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,13,"& variations in disclosure practices In our exploratory regression analysis, we have shown there is a possible association between demographic factors (i.e., academic discipline) and the level of student disclosure of AI use to teachers. For our Inquiry Question 3, we have identified academic discipline showed the largest association with disclosure to teachers, with a partial correlation of 0.36. Students in Education, Social Sciences, and STEM and Health all reported detectably higher disclosure scores than Business students, while Humanities students did not differ detectably from the Business reference Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",97
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"group. Since these comparisons rest on small subgroups and an underpowered model, we would be cautious and interpret them as tentative and offer the interpretations that follow as hypotheses rather than conclusions. Consistent with previous studies on AI use in various disciplines, these differences likely reflect underlying disciplinary cultures around transparency, technology use, and academic integrity norms (Gustilo et al., 2024; Rivera and Osena, 2025). Education students’ higher disclosure scores are perhaps the least surprising, but most interpretable finding. Students enrolled in teacher education programs are being socialized into a professional identity that values transparency, reflective practice, and open communication with authority figures (Chan, 2023; Rivera and Osena, 2025). The act of disclosing AI use to a teacher may align naturally with the help-seeking and professional accountability norms embedded in education programs, and such a disclosure act may function in a manner consistent with adaptive SRL in the learning context. Moreover, we also want to emphasize that because the present sample was drawn from an education course, these students may have perceived the classroom environment as more receptive to honest",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"a manner consistent with adaptive SRL in the learning context. Moreover, we also want to emphasize that because the present sample was drawn from an education course, these students may have perceived the classroom environment as more receptive to honest discussion about AI use. The finding that Business students (the reference group) and Humanities students reported the lowest disclosure levels is more theoretically provocative. It is because for Business students, this may reflect a disciplinary culture that emphasizes competitive advantage and strategic information management, where disclosing the use of a productivity-enhancing tool might be perceived as revealing a competitive edge (Kemp, 2024; Mahabub et al., 2025; Rivera and Osena, 2025). For Humanities students, the lower disclosure may stem from disciplinary norms in fields like English and Philosophy, which have historically centered the individual voice and placed particular value on original, unassisted expression; however, in that regard, AI use may be perceived as at odds with that ethos (Fyfe, 2023; Hu et al., 2025; Revell et al., 2024). Future research should explore whether discipline-specific AI policies or instructor attitudes mediate these",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"unassisted expression; however, in that regard, AI use may be perceived as at odds with that ethos (Fyfe, 2023; Hu et al., 2025; Revell et al., 2024). Future research should explore whether discipline-specific AI policies or instructor attitudes mediate these disclosure patterns. The relatively high disclosure among STEM and Health students may reflect a more pragmatic orientation toward technology in these fields. In disciplines where computational tools, data analysis software, and digital resources are already normalized as part of the academic workflow, adding AI to that toolkit may carry less stigma (Boltsi et al. 2024; Rosli et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2022). Students in these fields may perceive AI as “just another tool” rather than a threat to academic authenticity, making disclosure a lower-stakes act. Language status – marginal finding requiring further investigation Monolingual students tended to report lower disclosure than multilingual students (B = –0.51, p = .053). Because this result was marginal and the confidence interval [–1.02, 0.01] barely excluded zero, we treat it as a direction of effect worth investigating rather than an established finding. We",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"disclosure than multilingual students (B = –0.51, p = .053). Because this result was marginal and the confidence interval [–1.02, 0.01] barely excluded zero, we treat it as a direction of effect worth investigating rather than an established finding. We do not commit to any single interpretation of this pattern; however, plausible hypotheses for future work include that multilingual students may frame AI as a legitimate linguistic support (Athanassopoulos et al., 2023; Crompton et al., 2024; Hu and Zhang, 2025), that multilingual students may be more accustomed to checking with instructors as a form of cross- cultural due diligence (Sivira-Gonzalez, 2023; Zhang-Wu, 2023), or that monolingual English speakers may perceive AI use as threatening a valued academic identity. We note these only as hypotheses. Replication with a larger sample is needed before any interpretation of this pattern can be advanced with confidence. Years of study The non-detectable effect of years of study on disclosure (β = – 0.12, p = .303) is a null result that merits brief discussion, too. One might reasonably hypothesize that more advanced students, having had",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"with confidence. Years of study The non-detectable effect of years of study on disclosure (β = – 0.12, p = .303) is a null result that merits brief discussion, too. One might reasonably hypothesize that more advanced students, having had more exposure to institutional norms and more experience with AI tools, would show different disclosure patterns. It could be either more disclosure (through increased confidence and familiarity) or less disclosure (through greater strategic sophistication about what to share). Our results have confirmed neither pattern. This null result suggests that attitudes toward AI disclosure are associated with who students are, such as their disciplinary socialization, linguistic background, and emotional orientation toward AI, but not by academic residence, such as how long they have been in university. From a practical standpoint, this implies that institutions cannot rely on time-in- program as a natural corrective; students do not simply “grow into” transparency about AI use. Instead, consistent with the SRL theoretical perspectives, disclosure norms may need to be explicitly cultivated through curricular interventions, departmental policies, and instructor modelling from the first year onward,",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"natural corrective; students do not simply “grow into” transparency about AI use. Instead, consistent with the SRL theoretical perspectives, disclosure norms may need to be explicitly cultivated through curricular interventions, departmental policies, and instructor modelling from the first year onward, suggesting that self-regulatory competencies do not develop automatically through academic progression but require deliberate scaffolding (Kramarski and Heaysman, 2021; Simón- Grábalos et al., 2025; Vosniadou et al., 2024). Concluding remarks & practical implications Taken together, our findings suggest that AI disclosure is not only a matter of policy compliance but could also be interpreted as an affective process that may implicate part of SRL processes. Students’ worries about judgment, stigma, dependence, and replacement were associated with greater concealment and peer- only disclosure, while teacher-directed disclosure was consistent with an adaptive form of help-seeking. Given the correlational design, modest sample size, and preliminary status of the survey instrument, we offer these implications as tentative directions for practice rather than prescriptions. First, disclosure policies that target behaviour alone, such as mandatory AI-use declarations on assignments, may be insufficient if they do not",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,14,"and preliminary status of the survey instrument, we offer these implications as tentative directions for practice rather than prescriptions. First, disclosure policies that target behaviour alone, such as mandatory AI-use declarations on assignments, may be insufficient if they do not also address the affective barriers surrounding disclosure. Classroom practices that normalize appropriate AI use, provide instructor-led examples, and reassure students that disclosure will not automatically be penalized which may help to reduce the perceived social cost of transparency. Second, the association between heavier AI-assisted drafting and higher worry suggests Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 14 frontiersin.org",98
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"the need for low-barrier disclosure mechanisms. Examples include in-assignment reflection prompts that invite students to describe how AI supported their work, opt-in consultation opportunities, or AI-use reflection tools embedded in learning management systems. Such designs could make disclosure feel less like confession and more like part of ordinary learning regulation. In our exploratory demographic findings, we have suggested possible variation across disciplinary and linguistic contexts, but these patterns should be treated as hypotheses for future research. Larger studies are needed before drawing practical conclusions about discipline-specific AI literacy interventions or language-status differences in disclosure. For now, the more defensible implication is that instructors and institutions might focus on creating honest disclosure environments that reduce judgment, support calibration, and help students discuss AI use as part of learning rather than as evidence of misconduct (Chang et al., 2026). Limitations and future directions Several limitations constrain the interpretation of these findings. First, the sample was drawn from a single online education course at one Western Canadian university and clustered in one institutional context, which strongly limits external validity. The course was housed",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"directions Several limitations constrain the interpretation of these findings. First, the sample was drawn from a single online education course at one Western Canadian university and clustered in one institutional context, which strongly limits external validity. The course was housed in an Education department, which may have created an environment more favourable to disclosure than other academic settings. Replication across departments, institutions, and national contexts is needed. Second, participation was incentivized with up to two percent of the course grade, tied to depth of response. This incentive may have influenced disclosure-related responding, particularly in a study that asks about teacher judgment and academic practices. Respondents may have over- reported disclosure or under-reported concealment in a graded context even under anonymity, and this is a plausible source of bias in the direction of our findings. Moreover, participation was voluntary, and students who elected to complete a survey about AI use may have been those already more comfortable discussing it, introducing a self-selection bias that would also inflate transparent AI disclosure. Third, the sample size was modest (N = 78), limiting statistical",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"who elected to complete a survey about AI use may have been those already more comfortable discussing it, introducing a self-selection bias that would also inflate transparent AI disclosure. Third, the sample size was modest (N = 78), limiting statistical power and the stability of regression estimates. This is especially relevant for the exploratory demographic analysis, where some disciplinary categories were small, including the Business reference group. For this reason, the regression results should be interpreted as hypothesis-generating patterns within this sample, not as reliable population-level effects. The marginal language-status pattern also requires replication with a larger sample before any substantive interpretation can be advanced. Fourth, the survey items were newly developed and have not undergone full psychometric validation. While the Worry Index (α = .71) and Disclosure to Teachers Index (α = .80) showed acceptable reliability, the initially proposed Disclosure to Peers and AI Usage composites did not, prompting us to analyze those items individually. Future research should refine and validate a comprehensive AI disclosure scale. Moreover, content validity evidence for the present items was limited to informal expert",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"Disclosure to Peers and AI Usage composites did not, prompting us to analyze those items individually. Future research should refine and validate a comprehensive AI disclosure scale. Moreover, content validity evidence for the present items was limited to informal expert review within the research team and a small pilot; future use of the instrument should be preceded by formal procedures such as external expert review, cognitive interviewing, and content validity indexing. Fifth, the cross-sectional design precludes causal interpretation. While we have discussed worry as a correlate of concealment, it is equally plausible that students who have previously experienced negative reactions when disclosing develop higher worry as a consequence. Longitudinal or experimental designs are needed to establish directionality. Finally, the study relied on self-report data, which may be affected by social desirability bias (Nederhof, 1985). Students may under-report behaviours they perceive as academically risky and over-report behaviours they perceive as responsible, such as instructor disclosure. Future research should examine instructor behaviour and departmental policy as moderators of the relationships observed here, use qualitative or mixed-methods approaches to capture students’ reasoning about",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"as academically risky and over-report behaviours they perceive as responsible, such as instructor disclosure. Future research should examine instructor behaviour and departmental policy as moderators of the relationships observed here, use qualitative or mixed-methods approaches to capture students’ reasoning about disclosure decisions, and test whether the redirection-to-peers interpretation holds up under longitudinal or experimental designs. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors upon request, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The study adhered to the Tri-Council Policy Statement: Ethical Conduct for Research Involving Humans (TCPS 2). Given that the study focused on collecting student experiences to inform teaching feedback and ensure quality assurance of online courses, it qualified as an exemption under Article 2.5 of the TCPS 2 guidelines. The study was reviewed and exempted from a full REB review by Simon Fraser University [Study Number: 30001484]. Author contributions DC: Resources, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Validation, Data curation, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Visualization, Supervision, Investigation, Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Software, Methodology. MP-CL: Writing – review",180
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,15,"review by Simon Fraser University [Study Number: 30001484]. Author contributions DC: Resources, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Validation, Data curation, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Visualization, Supervision, Investigation, Conceptualization, Writing – review & editing, Software, Methodology. MP-CL: Writing – review & editing, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization, Investigation. J-YH: Formal analysis, Writing – review & editing, Validation, Methodology. JR: Data curation, Investigation, Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Writing – review & editing, Methodology. Funding The author(s) declared that financial support was received for this work and/or its publication. This research was funded by Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC), grant number 430-2023-00368 (DC) and 435-2026- 1299 (MP-CL). Chang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174 Frontiers in Education 15 frontiersin.org",118
DOC_009,feduc-11-1826174.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1826174,16,"Conflict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. Generative AI statement The author(s) declared that generative AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. Generative AI use was used for editing and proofreading language errors and mistakes. Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside figures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artificial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify any issues, please contact us. Publisher’s note All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by th",165
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,1,"EDITED BY Vassilios Makrakis, University of Crete, Greece REVIEWED BY Ramazan Yilmaz, Bartin University, Türkiye Zhiwei Liu, Yangzhou University, China *CORRESPONDENCE Zeyu Zhang 3148738209@qq.com RECEIVED 27 March 2026 REVISED 30 April 2026 ACCEPTED 25 May 2026 PUBLISHED 22 June 2026 CITATION Zhang Z, Kanji M, Zhang M and Zhou Y (2026) Learning-oriented generative AI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety in higher education: a cross- sectional study. Front. Educ. 11:1840953. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Zhang, Kanji, Zhang and Zhou. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Learning-oriented generative AI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety in higher education: a cross-sectional study Zeyu Zhang 1,2*, Mahpiret Kanji 3, Min Zhang 4 and Yang Zhou 2 1Judicial Police College, Xinjiang University",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,1,"which does not comply with these terms. Learning-oriented generative AI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety in higher education: a cross-sectional study Zeyu Zhang 1,2*, Mahpiret Kanji 3, Min Zhang 4 and Yang Zhou 2 1Judicial Police College, Xinjiang University of Political Science and Law, Tumxuk, China, 2School of Educational Science, Xinjiang Normal University, Urumqi, China, 3Xinjiang Key Laboratory of Mental Development and Learning Science, Xinjiang Normal University, Urumqi, China, 4Teaching and Research Group, Suqian Chongwen Middle School, Suqian, China Generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GenAI) has become an increasingly common learning resource in higher education, yet existing research has focused mainly on adoption, acceptance, and perceived usefulness rather than on the learning processes and emotional experiences associated with students’ everyday academic use of GenAI. Drawing on self-directed/self-regulated learning theory and a control-related interpretation of learning anxiety, this study examined the relationships among learning-oriented GenAI use, self- directed learning, and learning anxiety among university students. A cross- sectional survey was conducted with 742 students from six higher education institutions in China. Learning-oriented GenAI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety were each measured",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,1,"among learning-oriented GenAI use, self- directed learning, and learning anxiety among university students. A cross- sectional survey was conducted with 742 students from six higher education institutions in China. Learning-oriented GenAI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety were each measured using six-item scales rated on a ﬁve- point Likert format. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, Pearson correlations, conﬁrmatory factor analysis, structural equation modeling, and bootstrapped mediation analysis. The three-factor measurement model showed good ﬁt to the data (χ2 = 287.46, df = 132, χ2/df = 2.18, CFI = .962, TLI = .955, RMSEA = .040, SRMR = .039). Learning-oriented GenAI use was positively associated with self-directed learning (β = .47, p < .001), self-directed learning was negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.41, p < .001), and GenAI use was also directly and negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.10, p = .018). Bootstrapping indicated a signiﬁcant indirect effect of GenAI use on learning anxiety through self-directed learning (indirect effect = −.19, 95% CI [−.25, −.13]), suggesting partial mediation. These ﬁndings indicate that the educational signiﬁcance of",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,1,"(β = −.10, p = .018). Bootstrapping indicated a signiﬁcant indirect effect of GenAI use on learning anxiety through self-directed learning (indirect effect = −.19, 95% CI [−.25, −.13]), suggesting partial mediation. These ﬁndings indicate that the educational signiﬁcance of GenAI depends not only on access or acceptance, but also on whether students use GenAI in ways that support planning, monitoring, reﬂection, and independent academic judgment. KEYWORDS generative artiﬁcial intelligence, higher education, learning anxiety, self-directed learning, structural equation modeling, university students TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 22 June 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",95
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"1 Introduction 1.1 From GenAI adoption to learning- oriented GenAI use in higher education Generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GenAI) has moved rapidly from a novel digital tool to an increasingly routine learning resource in higher education. University students now use GenAI tools to search for information, clarify concepts, summarize readings, generate examples, structure assignments, revise drafts, and obtain immediate feedback across a wide range of academic tasks. This shift has important implications for teaching and learning because GenAI is not only changing the availability of learning support, but also reshaping how students organize academic work, seek help, and make judgments about knowledge, authorship, and responsibility (Chan and Hu, 2023; Popenici and Kerr, 2017; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Much existing research has examined this transformation through adoption-oriented frameworks. Studies informed by the Technology Acceptance Model, UTAUT, and related approaches have shown that students’ and instructors’ use of GenAI is shaped by perceived usefulness, performance expectancy, facilitating conditions, habit, and anxiety about technology use (Budhathoki et al., 2024; Davis, 1989; Kaya and Adiguzel, 2025). Task-technology ﬁt perspectives also suggest that digital tools are",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"students’ and instructors’ use of GenAI is shaped by perceived usefulness, performance expectancy, facilitating conditions, habit, and anxiety about technology use (Budhathoki et al., 2024; Davis, 1989; Kaya and Adiguzel, 2025). Task-technology ﬁt perspectives also suggest that digital tools are more likely to support performance when their affordances align with users’ task requirements (Goodhue and Thompson, 1995). These frameworks are valuable for explaining whether and why students are willing to use AI tools. However, adoption-oriented explanations provide a limited account of what happens after GenAI has entered students’ everyday learning routines. They tell us relatively less about how students use GenAI for learning, how such use relates to self-directed learning processes, and whether these processes are associated with students’ emotional experiences. For this reason, the present study focuses on learning-oriented GenAI use rather than general acceptance or behavioral intention. Learning-oriented GenAI use refers to students’ use of GenAI as a learning support for understanding concepts, organizing materials, planning academic tasks, seeking feedback, and managing learning activities. This construct is intentionally narrower than general GenAI use and different from attitudes toward",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"GenAI use refers to students’ use of GenAI as a learning support for understanding concepts, organizing materials, planning academic tasks, seeking feedback, and managing learning activities. This construct is intentionally narrower than general GenAI use and different from attitudes toward AI. It emphasizes academic, process-oriented uses of GenAI that may support students’ planning, monitoring, reﬂection, and strategic adjustment. This distinction is important because GenAI may be used either as a shortcut for output generation or as a scaffold for learning regulation. The educational meaning of GenAI therefore depends not simply on tool access or adoption, but on the quality and purpose of students’ engagement with it. 1.2 Learning-oriented GenAI use and self- directed learning Self-directed learning provides a useful theoretical lens for understanding learning-oriented GenAI use. In higher education, students are expected to set goals, plan tasks, monitor understanding, seek appropriate resources, and adjust strategies when they encounter difﬁculty. These capacities overlap with core components of self-regulated learning and are central to academic success in contexts where students must manage increasing levels of autonomy (Broadbent, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). From this",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"appropriate resources, and adjust strategies when they encounter difﬁculty. These capacities overlap with core components of self-regulated learning and are central to academic success in contexts where students must manage increasing levels of autonomy (Broadbent, 2017; Zimmerman, 2002). From this perspective, GenAI may support learning not simply by providing answers, but by helping students manage the processes through which learning is planned, monitored, and revised. GenAI can support self-directed learning in several ways. It may reduce information barriers, provide immediate clariﬁcation, help students break complex assignments into manageable steps, suggest alternative explanations, and support reﬂection on drafts or problem-solving strategies. Recent work has suggested that generative AI can foster self-regulated learning when embedded in pedagogically structured environments that support motivation, strategic engagement, and reﬂection (Chiu, 2024). A systematic mapping review further shows that research at the intersection of artiﬁcial intelligence and self-regulated learning is expanding rapidly, particularly in higher education, while important gaps remain in explaining how AI tools support students’ regulation of learning processes (Banihashem et al., 2025). At the same time, this relationship should not be treated as",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"and self-regulated learning is expanding rapidly, particularly in higher education, while important gaps remain in explaining how AI tools support students’ regulation of learning processes (Banihashem et al., 2025). At the same time, this relationship should not be treated as automatic. GenAI may support learner autonomy when students use it to scaffold understanding and reﬂective adjustment, but it may weaken autonomy when students use it mainly for answer production, cognitive ofﬂoading, or uncritical dependence. Recent research indicates that the relationship between GenAI affordances and self-regulated learning depends on learner characteristics, digital competencies, metacognitive awareness, and the conditions under which AI use occurs (Karaoglan Yilmaz and Yilmaz, 2025; Wu and Chiu, 2025). Similarly, studies of students’ GenAI anxiety suggest that self-regulation skills and digital competencies are important for reducing negative emotional responses to AI-supported learning (Karaoglan Yilmaz et al., 2025). These ﬁndings support a conditional, process-based view of GenAI: its educational value depends on whether students are able to integrate it into active, strategic, and self-directed learning practices. Prior work closest to the present topic has shown that students’ familiarity with",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"ﬁndings support a conditional, process-based view of GenAI: its educational value depends on whether students are able to integrate it into active, strategic, and self-directed learning practices. Prior work closest to the present topic has shown that students’ familiarity with GenAI and frequency of use may be positively related to perceived self-learning impact (Saleh and ElSayary, 2026). However, perceived self-learning impact is not the same as self-directed learning as a broader process variable. It remains necessary to test whether learning-oriented GenAI use is associated with goal setting, planning, monitoring, resource seeking, and strategy adjustment. Therefore, we proposed the following hypothesis: H1: Learning-oriented GenAI use is positively associated with self-directed learning among university students. 1.3 Self-directed learning and learning anxiety in AI-supported academic contexts Learning anxiety is an educationally situated form of tension, worry, and uncertainty related to academic demands. In the present study, learning anxiety is not conceptualized as clinical anxiety or generalized mental health status. Rather, it refers to students’ anxiety about academic tasks, performance demands, independent learning, and AI-supported learning situations. This distinction is important because students’ emotional",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,2,"the present study, learning anxiety is not conceptualized as clinical anxiety or generalized mental health status. Rather, it refers to students’ anxiety about academic tasks, performance demands, independent learning, and AI-supported learning situations. This distinction is important because students’ emotional Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",49
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"experiences in AI-supported learning contexts may be shaped not only by conventional academic pressure, but also by uncertainty about the reliability, legitimacy, and consequences of GenAI use. AI-supported learning can affect student well-being through both supportive and disruptive pathways. On the one hand, AI tools may reduce uncertainty by offering timely explanations, examples, feedback, and personalized assistance. On the other hand, they may generate new forms of anxiety related to answer quality, dependency, technostress, academic integrity, and social judgment (Jukiewicz, 2025; Klimova and Pikhart, 2025). Recent empirical work on generative AI anxiety indicates that students’ concerns about AI are shaped by cognitive ﬂexibility, digital competence, and self-regulation skills (Karaoglan Yilmaz et al., 2025). This suggests that anxiety in AI-supported learning is not only a response to the tool itself, but also a response to students’ perceived ability to regulate learning and judge AI-generated support. Self-directed learning may therefore operate as a protective process. Students who can set goals, plan tasks, monitor understanding, seek resources, and adjust strategies are more likely to experience control over academic demands. From a control-related perspective,",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"and judge AI-generated support. Self-directed learning may therefore operate as a protective process. Students who can set goals, plan tasks, monitor understanding, seek resources, and adjust strategies are more likely to experience control over academic demands. From a control-related perspective, stronger perceived control is associated with more adaptive achievement emotions and lower anxiety (Pekrun et al., 2011). In AI-supported contexts, self- directed learners may also be better able to evaluate AI- generated information, recognize when human judgment is needed, and avoid unreﬂective dependence. Accordingly, we proposed the following hypothesis: H2: Self-directed learning is negatively associated with learning anxiety among university students. 1.4 Direct and indirect pathways from GenAI use to learning anxiety Learning-oriented GenAI use may also be directly associated with learning anxiety. When students use GenAI to clarify concepts, organize materials, plan tasks, and obtain feedback, the tool may reduce ambiguity and make academic work feel more manageable. In this sense, learning-oriented GenAI use may lower anxiety by providing timely support during moments of uncertainty. However, this direct pathway is unlikely to capture the full relationship. If GenAI contributes",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"reduce ambiguity and make academic work feel more manageable. In this sense, learning-oriented GenAI use may lower anxiety by providing timely support during moments of uncertainty. However, this direct pathway is unlikely to capture the full relationship. If GenAI contributes to lower learning anxiety, one important reason may be that it supports students’ capacity to regulate their own learning. The emotional value of GenAI may therefore operate partly through self-directed learning. This mediation logic responds to a gap in current research. Studies of AI and higher education have often examined acceptance, attitudes, perceived usefulness, academic integrity, or broad well-being outcomes. More recent work has begun to examine GenAI anxiety and GenAI attitude in relation to self- regulation, cognitive ﬂexibility, digital competence, and metacognitive awareness (Karaoglan Yilmaz et al., 2025; Karaoglan Yilmaz and Yilmaz, 2025). However, fewer studies have tested a process model in which learning-oriented GenAI use is linked to learning anxiety through self-directed learning. Such a model can clarify why the emotional implications of GenAI may vary across students: GenAI use may be associated with lower anxiety when it",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"process model in which learning-oriented GenAI use is linked to learning anxiety through self-directed learning. Such a model can clarify why the emotional implications of GenAI may vary across students: GenAI use may be associated with lower anxiety when it is integrated into strategic, reﬂective, and self-directed learning processes, but not when it encourages passive dependence. Accordingly, we proposed two further hypotheses: H3: Learning-oriented GenAI use is negatively associated with learning anxiety among university students. H4: Self-directed learning mediates the relationship between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety. 1.5 The present study The present study examined the relationships among learning- oriented GenAI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety among university students. It addressed three research questions: whether learning-oriented GenAI use is associated with self- directed learning, whether self-directed learning is associated with learning anxiety, and whether self-directed learning mediates the relationship between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety. By answering these questions, the study contributes to higher education research in three ways. First, it shifts the focus from GenAI adoption to learning-oriented use as a process-relevant construct. Second, it integrates",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"relationship between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety. By answering these questions, the study contributes to higher education research in three ways. First, it shifts the focus from GenAI adoption to learning-oriented use as a process-relevant construct. Second, it integrates a learning process variable and an emotional outcome within a single SEM framework. Third, it clariﬁes that the educational signiﬁcance of GenAI lies not only in access to AI tools, but also in whether students are supported in using them for planning, monitoring, reﬂection, and responsible academic judgment. The hypothesized conceptual model is presented in Figure 1. 2 Methods 2.1 Study design and participants This study employed a cross-sectional survey design. No instructional intervention, training program, or experimental manipulation was implemented. The purpose of the study was to examine naturally occurring associations among learning- oriented GenAI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety among university students. Participants were recruited from six higher education institutions in China, including comprehensive universities, teacher education universities, and applied universities. To improve sample heterogeneity, students from multiple disciplinary backgrounds were invited to participate, including humanities and",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"learning anxiety among university students. Participants were recruited from six higher education institutions in China, including comprehensive universities, teacher education universities, and applied universities. To improve sample heterogeneity, students from multiple disciplinary backgrounds were invited to participate, including humanities and social sciences, education, natural sciences and engineering, and health-related ﬁelds. The multi-institutional and multidisciplinary sampling strategy was intended to increase the diversity of the sample and improve the interpretability of the ﬁndings across higher education contexts. A total of 812 students responded to the online questionnaire. After excluding incomplete responses, patterned responses, responses that failed the attention-check item, and responses below the minimum completion-time threshold, 742 valid responses were retained for analysis, yielding an effective response rate of 91.38%. The ﬁnal sample included 458 women (61.7%) and 284 men (38.3%), with a mean age of 20.41 years (SD = 1.67, range = 17–27). In terms of year level, 204 participants (27.5%) were ﬁrst-year students, 192 (25.9%) were second-year students, 173 (23.3%) were third-year students, 132 (17.8%) were fourth-year students, and 41 (5.5%) were postgraduate students. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,3,"range = 17–27). In terms of year level, 204 participants (27.5%) were ﬁrst-year students, 192 (25.9%) were second-year students, 173 (23.3%) were third-year students, 132 (17.8%) were fourth-year students, and 41 (5.5%) were postgraduate students. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",44
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,4,"2.2 Procedure Data were collected through an anonymous online survey hosted on a secure questionnaire platform. Before formal administration, the questionnaire was pilot-tested with 36 university students to evaluate clarity, readability, contextual appropriateness, and completion time. The pilot process was used to reﬁne item wording, reduce ambiguity in AI-related statements, and ensure that the items were understandable to students from different disciplinary backgrounds. During formal administration, participants ﬁrst read an information sheet describing the study purpose, voluntary nature of participation, anonymity of responses, and conﬁdentiality of data. Electronic informed consent was obtained before participants could proceed to the questionnaire. Participants could withdraw at any point without penalty. To improve data quality, the survey included one attention-check item, a minimum response-time threshold, and two reverse-check questions. Responses were screened before analysis according to these criteria. 2.3 Measures The measures were organized according to their roles in the hypothesized model: learning-oriented GenAI use as the independent variable, self-directed learning as the mediator, learning anxiety as the dependent variable, and demographic and educational variables as controls. All three focal constructs were measured using",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,4,"according to their roles in the hypothesized model: learning-oriented GenAI use as the independent variable, self-directed learning as the mediator, learning anxiety as the dependent variable, and demographic and educational variables as controls. All three focal constructs were measured using six-item scales rated on a ﬁve-point Likert format ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree). Higher scores indicated higher levels of the corresponding construct. The ﬁnal questionnaire items are provided in Appendix A, and the anonymized dataset, scoring procedures, variable codebook, and analysis workﬂow are available through the OSF repository described in the Data availability statement. 2.3.1 Independent variable: learning-oriented GenAI use Learning-oriented GenAI use was conceptualized as the extent to which students used generative AI tools to support academic learning processes. The scale was designed to capture academic, process-oriented uses of GenAI, including understanding difﬁcult concepts, summarizing or organizing learning materials, planning or structuring academic tasks, and receiving feedback on work. The construct therefore differs from general GenAI familiarity, attitudes toward AI, behavioral intention to use AI, or perceived usefulness. It focuses on what students do with",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,4,"or organizing learning materials, planning or structuring academic tasks, and receiving feedback on work. The construct therefore differs from general GenAI familiarity, attitudes toward AI, behavioral intention to use AI, or perceived usefulness. It focuses on what students do with GenAI in learning situations rather than whether they generally approve of or intend to adopt the technology. The items were developed with reference to recent higher education research on students’ perceptions and learning-related uses of generative AI (Chan and Hu, 2023; Saleh and ElSayary, 2026). The wording was reﬁned through pilot testing to improve contextual clarity and reduce ambiguity. Psychometric adequacy was evaluated through conﬁrmatory factor analysis, internal consistency, composite reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. 2.3.2 Mediator: self-directed learning Self-directed learning referred to students’ ability to set learning goals, plan learning activities, monitor understanding, seek useful resources, and adjust strategies when necessary. A brief adapted version of the Self-Rating Scale of Self-Directed Learning was used to reduce respondent burden while retaining the core dimensions most relevant to higher education (Williamson, 2007). The items were selected to represent planning, monitoring,",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,4,"strategies when necessary. A brief adapted version of the Self-Rating Scale of Self-Directed Learning was used to reduce respondent burden while retaining the core dimensions most relevant to higher education (Williamson, 2007). The items were selected to represent planning, monitoring, resource seeking, strategy adjustment, and independent management of learning tasks. 2.3.3 Dependent variable: learning anxiety Learning anxiety was deﬁned as the tension, worry, and uncertainty students experience when facing academic demands, especially in AI-supported learning contexts. The measure drew on the anxiety-related logic of the Achievement Emotions Questionnaire while adding context-speciﬁc items to capture concerns about AI-generated content accuracy, social judgment, and possible dependence on AI-supported learning (Klimova and Pikhart, 2025; Pekrun et al., 2011). The measure was intended to capture educationally situated anxiety rather than clinical anxiety or generalized mental health status. FIGURE 1 Hypothesized conceptual model. The model speciﬁes learning-oriented generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GenAI) use as the independent variable, self- directed learning as the mediator, and learning anxiety as the outcome variable. H1 represents the expected positive association between learning- oriented GenAI use and self-directed learning. H2 represents",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,4,"speciﬁes learning-oriented generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GenAI) use as the independent variable, self- directed learning as the mediator, and learning anxiety as the outcome variable. H1 represents the expected positive association between learning- oriented GenAI use and self-directed learning. H2 represents the expected negative association between self-directed learning and learning anxiety. H3 represents the expected direct association between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety. H4 represents the expected indirect pathway from learning-oriented GenAI use to learning anxiety through self-directed learning. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",88
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,5,"2.3.4 Control variables The following control variables were included: gender, age, year level, disciplinary background, self-reported academic performance, prior AI training, weekly study time, and digital literacy. Gender was coded as 0 = male and 1 = female. Prior AI training was coded as 0 = no and 1 = yes. These variables were included because demographic, academic, and digital-experience differences may inﬂuence students’ use of GenAI, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety. 2.4 Ethical considerations This study was reviewed and approved by the Ethics Review Committee of the School of Educational Science, Xinjiang Normal University. All procedures were conducted in accordance with institutional requirements and the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Written informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. 2.5 Data analysis Data were analyzed using SPSS 27.0 and AMOS 26.0. The analysis proceeded in six steps. First, data screening was conducted to identify incomplete questionnaires, patterned responses, failed attention checks, responses below the minimum completion-time threshold, and distributional problems. Skewness and kurtosis were examined to assess univariate normality. Second, descriptive statistics and Pearson correlations",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,5,"six steps. First, data screening was conducted to identify incomplete questionnaires, patterned responses, failed attention checks, responses below the minimum completion-time threshold, and distributional problems. Skewness and kurtosis were examined to assess univariate normality. Second, descriptive statistics and Pearson correlations were computed for the focal variables. Third, common method bias was assessed using Harman’s single-factor test and by comparing the hypothesized three-factor measurement model with a one-factor model. Fourth, conﬁrmatory factor analysis was used to evaluate the measurement model. Model ﬁt was assessed using χ2/df, CFI, TLI, RMSEA, and SRMR. Internal consistency was evaluated using Cronbach’s alpha and composite reliability. Convergent validity was assessed using standardized factor loadings and average variance extracted, and discriminant validity was examined using the Fornell-Larcker criterion. Fifth, structural equation modeling was used to test the hypothesized relationships among learning-oriented GenAI use, self-directed learning, and learning anxiety. Control variables were included to examine whether the focal relationships remained signiﬁcant after accounting for demographic and educational background factors. Sixth, the mediating effect of self-directed learning was tested using 5,000 bootstrapped samples and bias-corrected 95% conﬁdence intervals. An",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,5,"anxiety. Control variables were included to examine whether the focal relationships remained signiﬁcant after accounting for demographic and educational background factors. Sixth, the mediating effect of self-directed learning was tested using 5,000 bootstrapped samples and bias-corrected 95% conﬁdence intervals. An indirect effect was considered statistically signiﬁcant when the conﬁdence interval did not include zero. 3 Results 3.1 Participant characteristics Table 1 presents the demographic and background characteristics of the participants. Among the 742 participants, 61.7% were female and 38.3% were male. Students from humanities and social sciences accounted for 29.1% of the sample, education majors for 21.0%, natural sciences and engineering for 34.8%, and health-related disciplines for 15.1%. A total of 347 students (46.8%) reported having received some form of prior AI-related training or guidance in academic settings. These characteristics indicate that the sample covered a reasonably diverse range of student backgrounds relevant to contemporary higher education contexts. 3.2 Preliminary analyses and common method bias After data cleaning, no serious missing-data problem remained. Skewness ranged from −.58 to .41 and kurtosis ranged from −.66 to .52, indicating acceptable univariate distributions.",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,5,"student backgrounds relevant to contemporary higher education contexts. 3.2 Preliminary analyses and common method bias After data cleaning, no serious missing-data problem remained. Skewness ranged from −.58 to .41 and kurtosis ranged from −.66 to .52, indicating acceptable univariate distributions. Harman’s single-factor test indicated that the ﬁrst unrotated factor accounted for 31.42% of the total variance, below the commonly used 40% threshold. As shown in Table 2, the one-factor model ﬁt the data poorly (χ2 = 1268.74, df = 135, χ2/df = 9.40, CFI = .619, TLI = .568, RMSEA = .106, SRMR = .113), whereas the hypothesized three-factor measurement model ﬁt the data substantially better. These results suggest that common method bias was unlikely to seriously distort the interpretation of the focal relationships. 3.3 Descriptive statistics and correlations Table 3 presents the descriptive statistics of the focal variables. The mean score for learning-oriented GenAI use was 3.62 (SD = 0.74), indicating a moderate-to-relatively high level of GenAI-supported learning. The mean score for self-directed learning was 3.71 (SD = 0.68), whereas the mean score for learning anxiety was 2.94 (SD",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,5,"mean score for learning-oriented GenAI use was 3.62 (SD = 0.74), indicating a moderate-to-relatively high level of GenAI-supported learning. The mean score for self-directed learning was 3.71 (SD = 0.68), whereas the mean score for learning anxiety was 2.94 (SD = 0.81). As shown in Table 4, learning-oriented GenAI use was positively correlated with self- TABLE 1 Demographic characteristics of the participants. Variable Category n % Gender Male 284 38.3 Female 458 61.7 Year level First year 204 27.5 Second year 192 25.9 Third year 173 23.3 Fourth year 132 17.8 Postgraduate 41 5.5 Discipline Humanities and social sciences 216 29.1 Education 156 21.0 Natural sciences and engineering 258 34.8 Health-related ﬁelds 112 15.1 Prior AI training No 395 53.2 Yes 347 46.8 N = 742. Percentages may not total exactly 100 due to rounding. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",144
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,6,"directed learning (r = .43, p < .001) and negatively correlated with learning anxiety (r = −.19, p < .001). Self-directed learning was also negatively correlated with learning anxiety (r = −.38, p < .001). All bivariate associations were in the hypothesized directions. 3.4 Measurement model The hypothesized three-factor measurement model demonstrated good ﬁt to the data (χ2 = 287.46, df = 132, χ2/ df = 2.18, CFI = .962, TLI = .955, RMSEA = .040, SRMR = .039). All standardized factor loadings were statistically signiﬁcant (p < .001) and ranged from .68 to .86. As shown in Table 5, reliability indices were satisfactory. Cronbach’s alpha coefﬁcients were .89 for learning-oriented GenAI use, .91 for self-directed learning, and .88 for learning anxiety. Composite reliability values ranged from .88 to .91, and average variance extracted values ranged from .56 to .63, indicating adequate internal consistency and convergent validity. In addition, the square roots of the AVE values were .76 for learning- oriented GenAI use, .79 for self-directed learning, and .75 for learning anxiety. These values exceeded the absolute inter- construct correlations",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,6,"indicating adequate internal consistency and convergent validity. In addition, the square roots of the AVE values were .76 for learning- oriented GenAI use, .79 for self-directed learning, and .75 for learning anxiety. These values exceeded the absolute inter- construct correlations reported in Table 4, which ranged from .19 to .43, thereby supporting discriminant validity according to the Fornell-Larcker criterion. 3.5 Group differences Independent-samples t tests showed no signiﬁcant gender difference in learning-oriented GenAI use, t(740) = 1.46, p = .145. Female students reported slightly higher learning anxiety than male students, t(740) = 2.51, p = .012, Cohen’s d = 0.19. One-way ANOVA showed a signiﬁcant year-level difference in self-directed learning, F(4, 737) = 3.86, p = .004. Post hoc comparisons indicated that ﬁrst- and second-year students reported slightly higher self- directed learning than fourth-year students. 3.6 Structural model and hypothesis testing The structural model showed acceptable ﬁt to the data: χ2 = 301.18, df = 134, χ2/df = 2.25, CFI = .958, TLI = .951, RMSEA = .041, SRMR = .043. The structural model showed acceptable ﬁt to the data",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,6,"testing The structural model showed acceptable ﬁt to the data: χ2 = 301.18, df = 134, χ2/df = 2.25, CFI = .958, TLI = .951, RMSEA = .041, SRMR = .043. The structural model showed acceptable ﬁt to the data (Figure 2): χ2 = 301.18, df = 134, χ2/df = 2.25, CFI = .958, TLI = .951, RMSEA = .041, SRMR = .043. As shown in Table 6, learning-oriented GenAI use was positively associated with self-directed learning (β = .47, SE = .05, CR = 8.94, p < .001), supporting H1. Self-directed learning was negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.41, SE = .06, CR = −6.83, p < .001), supporting H2. Learning-oriented GenAI use was also negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.10, SE = .04, CR = −2.37, p = .018), supporting H3. The model explained 22.1% of the variance in self-directed learning and 24.8% of the variance in learning anxiety. After entering control variables, the focal path TABLE 2 Model ﬁt indices for the measurement and structural models. Model χ2 df χ2/df CFI TLI RMSEA",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,6,"22.1% of the variance in self-directed learning and 24.8% of the variance in learning anxiety. After entering control variables, the focal path TABLE 2 Model ﬁt indices for the measurement and structural models. Model χ2 df χ2/df CFI TLI RMSEA SRMR One-factor model 1,268.74 135 9.40 .619 .568 .106 .113 Three-factor measurement model 287.46 132 2.18 .962 .955 .040 .039 Structural model 301.18 134 2.25 .958 .951 .041 .043 χ2, chi-square; df, degrees of freedom; CFI, comparative ﬁt index; TLI, Tucker–Lewis index; RMSEA, root mean square error of approximation; SRMR, standardized root mean square residual. TABLE 3 Descriptive statistics of the focal variables. Variable Items Mean SD Skewness Kurtosis Learning-oriented GenAI use 6 3.62 0.74 −.41 −.36 Self-directed learning 6 3.71 0.68 −.58 .52 Learning anxiety 6 2.94 0.81 .41 −.66 GenAI, generative artiﬁcial intelligence; SD, standard deviation. All variables were measured on a ﬁve-point Likert scale ranging from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. TABLE 4 Means, standard deviations, and correlations among the study variables. Variable M SD 1 2 3 1. Learning-oriented GenAI use 3.62",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,6,"were measured on a ﬁve-point Likert scale ranging from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. TABLE 4 Means, standard deviations, and correlations among the study variables. Variable M SD 1 2 3 1. Learning-oriented GenAI use 3.62 0.74 – 2. Self-directed learning 3.71 0.68 .43*** – 3. Learning anxiety 2.94 0.81 −.19*** −.38*** – N = 742. M, mean; SD, standard deviation; GenAI, generative artiﬁcial intelligence. ***p < .001. TABLE 5 Reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity of the measurement scales. Construct α CR AVE √AVE Learning-oriented GenAI use .89 .89 .58 .76 Self-directed learning .91 .91 .63 .79 Learning anxiety .88 .88 .56 .75 α, Cronbach’s alpha; CR, composite reliability; AVE, average variance extracted; √AVE, square root of AVE; GenAI, generative artiﬁcial intelligence. Values of CR above .70 and AVE above .50 indicate acceptable reliability and convergent validity. Discriminant validity was supported because each √AVE value exceeded the corresponding inter- construct correlations reported in Table 4. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",169
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,7,"coefﬁcients remained signiﬁcant, indicating that the hypothesized relationships were not explained by the included demographic and educational background variables. 3.7 Mediation analysis Table 7 presents the direct, indirect, and total effects of learning- oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety. Bootstrapping with 5,000 resamples showed that the indirect effect of learning-oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety through self-directed learning was signiﬁcant (indirect effect = −.19, SE = .03, 95% CI [−.25, −.13]). Because the conﬁdence interval did not include zero, H4 was supported. The direct effect of learning-oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety remained signiﬁcant after self-directed learning was included (direct effect = −.10, SE = .04, p = .018), indicating partial mediation. The total effect of learning-oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety was −.29 (SE = .04, p < .001). 4 Discussion 4.1 Overview of the ﬁndings This study examined whether learning-oriented GenAI use was associated with self-directed learning and learning anxiety among university students, and whether self-directed learning mediated the relationship between GenAI use and learning anxiety. Four ﬁndings emerged. First, learning-oriented GenAI use was positively associated with self-directed",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,7,"whether learning-oriented GenAI use was associated with self-directed learning and learning anxiety among university students, and whether self-directed learning mediated the relationship between GenAI use and learning anxiety. Four ﬁndings emerged. First, learning-oriented GenAI use was positively associated with self-directed learning. Second, self-directed learning was negatively associated with learning anxiety. Third, learning-oriented GenAI use was directly and negatively associated with learning anxiety. Fourth, self-directed learning partially mediated the relationship between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety. These ﬁndings extend current research on GenAI in higher education by shifting the focus from adoption to learning process. Prior studies have provided valuable evidence on students’ acceptance of GenAI, perceived usefulness, perceived self-learning impact, and AI-related concerns. The present study adds that learning-oriented GenAI use is meaningfully related to a theoretically important process variable–self-directed learning–and that this process is associated with students’ emotional experiences. In this sense, the study provides a process-based explanation of how GenAI may matter in higher education: GenAI is not educationally signiﬁcant merely because students use it, but because some forms of use may become integrated into planning, monitoring,",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,7,"experiences. In this sense, the study provides a process-based explanation of how GenAI may matter in higher education: GenAI is not educationally signiﬁcant merely because students use it, but because some forms of use may become integrated into planning, monitoring, resource seeking, and strategy adjustment. 4.2 Learning-oriented GenAI use and self- directed learning The positive association between learning-oriented GenAI use and self-directed learning suggests that students who used GenAI more often for academic and process-oriented purposes also reported TABLE 6 Structural path coefﬁcients. Path β SE CR p Learning-oriented GenAI use → Self-directed learning .47 .05 8.94 <.001 Self-directed learning → Learning anxiety −.41 .06 −6.83 <.001 Learning-oriented GenAI use → Learning anxiety −.10 .04 −2.37 .018 β, standardized regression coefﬁcient; SE, standard error; CR, critical ratio; GenAI, generative artiﬁcial intelligence. TABLE 7 Direct, indirect, and total effects of learning-oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety. Effect Estimate SE 95% CI p Direct effect: Learning-oriented GenAI use → Learning anxiety −.10 .04 [−.18, −.02] .018 Indirect effect via self-directed learning −.19 .03 [−.25, −.13] <.001 Total effect −.29 .04 [−.37, −.21]",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,7,"GenAI use on learning anxiety. Effect Estimate SE 95% CI p Direct effect: Learning-oriented GenAI use → Learning anxiety −.10 .04 [−.18, −.02] .018 Indirect effect via self-directed learning −.19 .03 [−.25, −.13] <.001 Total effect −.29 .04 [−.37, −.21] <.001 SE, standard error; CI, conﬁdence interval. Conﬁdence intervals were estimated using 5,000 bias-corrected bootstrap samples. The indirect effect was considered signiﬁcant when the 95% conﬁdence interval did not include zero. FIGURE 2 Structural equation model with standardized path coefﬁcients. The ﬁgure presents the ﬁnal structural model. Learning-oriented GenAI use was positively associated with self-directed learning (β = .47, p < .001), self-directed learning was negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.41, p < .001), and learning-oriented GenAI use was also directly and negatively associated with learning anxiety (β = −.10, p = .018). The indirect effect of learning-oriented GenAI use on learning anxiety through self-directed learning was signiﬁcant, indicating partial mediation. GenAI = generative artiﬁcial intelligence. Standardized path coefﬁcients are reported. ***p < .001; *p < .05. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",178
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,7,"GenAI use on learning anxiety through self-directed learning was signiﬁcant, indicating partial mediation. GenAI = generative artiﬁcial intelligence. Standardized path coefﬁcients are reported. ***p < .001; *p < .05. Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",38
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"stronger goal setting, planning, monitoring, resource seeking, and strategy adjustment. This ﬁnding is consistent with the view that GenAI can support self-regulated and self-directed learning when students use it as a scaffold for understanding and reﬂection rather than as a substitute for thinking (Banihashem et al., 2025; Chiu, 2024). This result also clariﬁes the difference between learning-oriented GenAI use and general GenAI adoption. Adoption-oriented models such as TAM and UTAUT help explain whether students are willing to use GenAI, but they do not fully explain how students incorporate GenAI into learning processes. The present ﬁnding suggests that a process-oriented construct is needed to capture whether students use GenAI for academic regulation. In this respect, the study builds on evidence that GenAI can inﬂuence perceived self-learning impact (Saleh and ElSayary, 2026), while extending that evidence to self-directed learning as a broader process variable. At the same time, the ﬁnding should not be interpreted as evidence that GenAI automatically improves self-directed learning. Recent work on GenAI attitude and anxiety shows that students’ AI-related experiences are shaped by self-regulation skills, digital competencies, cognitive",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"process variable. At the same time, the ﬁnding should not be interpreted as evidence that GenAI automatically improves self-directed learning. Recent work on GenAI attitude and anxiety shows that students’ AI-related experiences are shaped by self-regulation skills, digital competencies, cognitive ﬂexibility, and metacognitive awareness (Karaoglan Yilmaz et al., 2025; Karaoglan Yilmaz and Yilmaz, 2025). The present study is consistent with this conditional view. Learning-oriented GenAI use appears beneﬁcial when it is connected to active regulation, but different patterns of use–such as uncritical copying, over-reliance, or cognitive ofﬂoading–may have different implications. 4.3 Self-directed learning and learning anxiety The negative association between self-directed learning and learning anxiety is theoretically meaningful. Students with stronger self-directed learning are more likely to set clear goals, plan their work, monitor understanding, seek resources, and modify ineffective strategies. These capacities may strengthen perceived control over academic demands, which is closely related to lower anxiety in achievement settings (Pekrun et al., 2011). In AI-supported learning contexts, self-directed learning may be especially important because students must decide when to use GenAI, how to evaluate AI- generated output, and how",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"which is closely related to lower anxiety in achievement settings (Pekrun et al., 2011). In AI-supported learning contexts, self-directed learning may be especially important because students must decide when to use GenAI, how to evaluate AI- generated output, and how to maintain their own academic judgment. This ﬁnding supports an educationally situated interpretation of learning anxiety. The study does not assess clinical anxiety or generalized mental health status. Instead, it examines anxiety related to academic demands and AI-supported learning practices. The results suggest that students’ anxiety in these contexts is not only a reaction to the presence of AI tools, but also reﬂects how capable students feel in regulating their own learning. Students who are better able to regulate learning may be less vulnerable to uncertainty about AI accuracy, dependence, or social judgment. 4.4 Direct and indirect pathways from GenAI use to learning anxiety Learning-oriented GenAI use was directly and negatively associated with learning anxiety, although this direct effect was smaller than the indirect pathway through self-directed learning. The direct effect may reﬂect the immediate support that GenAI provides when",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"use to learning anxiety Learning-oriented GenAI use was directly and negatively associated with learning anxiety, although this direct effect was smaller than the indirect pathway through self-directed learning. The direct effect may reﬂect the immediate support that GenAI provides when students encounter uncertainty. For example, students may feel less overwhelmed when they can quickly obtain explanations, examples, feedback, or task-structuring support. In this way, GenAI may reduce ambiguity and make academic work feel more manageable. The mediation ﬁnding is the central contribution of the study. Self-directed learning partially mediated the relationship between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety, indicating that one reason GenAI use was associated with lower anxiety is that it was linked to stronger self-directed learning. This ﬁnding offers a more nuanced interpretation than either technological optimism or technological pessimism. GenAI does not appear inherently beneﬁcial or harmful. Rather, its emotional implications depend partly on whether students use it in ways that strengthen their capacity to plan, monitor, and adjust learning. This interpretation also helps explain why the relationship between AI use and anxiety may appear mixed across",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"Rather, its emotional implications depend partly on whether students use it in ways that strengthen their capacity to plan, monitor, and adjust learning. This interpretation also helps explain why the relationship between AI use and anxiety may appear mixed across studies. AI tools may reduce anxiety when they support control, competence, and strategic engagement, but they may increase anxiety when they create dependency, uncertainty, or pressure to use AI in unclear ways (Budhathoki et al., 2024; Klimova and Pikhart, 2025). The present ﬁndings therefore suggest that future studies should distinguish between different qualities of GenAI use rather than treating AI use as a single undifferentiated behavior. 4.5 Theoretical implications Theoretically, the study contributes to AI-supported higher education research in three ways. First, it demonstrates the value of moving beyond adoption models. TAM, UTAUT, and task- technology ﬁt frameworks remain useful for explaining acceptance and perceived ﬁt, but the present ﬁndings show that learning- oriented use and self-directed learning are needed to explain how GenAI relates to learning anxiety. Second, the study integrates a learning process variable and an emotional outcome",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"for explaining acceptance and perceived ﬁt, but the present ﬁndings show that learning- oriented use and self-directed learning are needed to explain how GenAI relates to learning anxiety. Second, the study integrates a learning process variable and an emotional outcome within one SEM model, thereby connecting research on AI-supported learning with research on achievement emotions. Third, the ﬁndings support a conditional process model of GenAI: the beneﬁts of GenAI are more likely to appear when students use it as part of self-directed and reﬂective learning practices. These implications resonate with broader discussions of pedagogy in the AI era. This is also consistent with broader evidence that the educational impact of AI in higher education depends on how AI tools are integrated with teaching methods and student engagement (Long et al., 2026). Recent work argues that AI-supported education requires adequate models of pedagogy rather than simple tool insertion (Nussbaum et al., 2025). Similarly, the idea of moving from transmission to relation in AI-era teaching emphasizes that educators’ roles involve designing meaningful relationships among learners, tools, tasks, feedback, and judgment rather than",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,8,"of pedagogy rather than simple tool insertion (Nussbaum et al., 2025). Similarly, the idea of moving from transmission to relation in AI-era teaching emphasizes that educators’ roles involve designing meaningful relationships among learners, tools, tasks, feedback, and judgment rather than merely delivering information (Nussbaum et al., 2026). The present student-level ﬁndings complement this pedagogical perspective by showing that the emotional value of GenAI depends partly on whether students develop self-directed ways of using it. 4.6 Practical implications First, at the course level, instructors should explicitly model how GenAI can be used for planning, monitoring, Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",103
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"questioning, feedback seeking, and reﬂection. Students need examples of process-oriented GenAI use, not only warnings against inappropriate use. Course activities can ask students to compare AI-generated explanations, critique AI feedback, document their revision decisions, and reﬂect on when human judgment is required. Second, at the student-support level, universities should integrate GenAI literacy with self-directed learning support. Workshops and advising services should help students evaluate AI-generated information, recognize the limits of automated responses, avoid unreﬂective dependence, and maintain conﬁdence in their own academic competence. GenAI literacy should therefore include cognitive, metacognitive, ethical, and emotional dimensions. This implication is consistent with recent work emphasizing the development of AI competencies in higher education (Petrova, 2026). Third, at the institutional level, policies should move beyond simple prohibition. Clear guidance on acceptable use, transparency, attribution, and independent academic work may reduce uncertainty and anxiety. At the same time, institutional policies should avoid treating students as merely compliant technology users. They should support students as developing self-directed learners who need guidance in using GenAI responsibly and reﬂectively. Overall, the ﬁndings suggest that the theoretical value of",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"the same time, institutional policies should avoid treating students as merely compliant technology users. They should support students as developing self-directed learners who need guidance in using GenAI responsibly and reﬂectively. Overall, the ﬁndings suggest that the theoretical value of learning-oriented GenAI use lies not only in its association with students’ adoption of digital tools, but also in its connection with self-directed learning as a process through which students manage academic demands and emotional uncertainty. This process-based interpretation summarizes the link between the theoretical discussion above and the practical implications for course design, student support, and institutional policy. 5 Limitations Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the cross- sectional design limits causal interpretation. Although the hypothesized model was theoretically grounded and statistically supported, the direction of the relationships cannot be established from the present data. Longitudinal, experimental, or intervention-based studies are needed to examine whether learning-oriented GenAI use leads to stronger self-directed learning and lower learning anxiety over time. Second, all focal variables were measured through self-report questionnaires. Although data screening procedures and common method bias checks were conducted, self-report",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"are needed to examine whether learning-oriented GenAI use leads to stronger self-directed learning and lower learning anxiety over time. Second, all focal variables were measured through self-report questionnaires. Although data screening procedures and common method bias checks were conducted, self-report data remain vulnerable to recall bias, social desirability, and subjective interpretation. Future research could combine survey data with behavioral indicators, learning analytics, classroom observations, or task-based evidence of GenAI-supported learning. Third, the measure of learning-oriented GenAI use was developed for the present higher education context. Although the scale showed satisfactory reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity in this sample, further validation is needed across different disciplines, institutions, and cultural settings. Future studies may also compare learning-oriented GenAI use with other types of GenAI use, such as efﬁciency-oriented use, entertainment-oriented use, or answer-generation use. Fourth, because the study focused on learning-oriented GenAI use, the ﬁndings should not be generalized to all forms of GenAI use, especially uses primarily aimed at answer generation, task substitution, or uncritical cognitive ofﬂoading. The negative association between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety does not mean",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"GenAI use, the ﬁndings should not be generalized to all forms of GenAI use, especially uses primarily aimed at answer generation, task substitution, or uncritical cognitive ofﬂoading. The negative association between learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety does not mean that GenAI use is always anxiety-reducing. Different patterns of use may produce different learning and emotional outcomes. Fifth, the sample was drawn from higher education institutions in China, which may limit the generalizability of the ﬁndings. Students’ experiences with GenAI are likely shaped by institutional policies, language environments, digital infrastructure, assessment cultures, and local norms concerning academic integrity. Replication in other national and institutional contexts would help clarify whether the observed relationships are stable across higher education systems. Finally, the model examined only one mediating mechanism. Self-directed learning is an important explanatory pathway, but it is unlikely to be the only one. Future research could examine additional mediators and moderators, such as AI literacy, academic self-efﬁcacy, digital competence, metacognitive awareness, trust in AI, academic integrity concerns, perceived teacher guidance, or prior AI training. Such work would provide a more complete",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"only one. Future research could examine additional mediators and moderators, such as AI literacy, academic self-efﬁcacy, digital competence, metacognitive awareness, trust in AI, academic integrity concerns, perceived teacher guidance, or prior AI training. Such work would provide a more complete account of when and for whom GenAI- supported learning is associated with lower or higher learning anxiety. 6 Conclusion This study examined three research questions concerning how learning-oriented GenAI use relates to self-directed learning and learning anxiety in higher education. Speciﬁcally, the study answers these research questions by showing that learning- oriented GenAI use was positively related to self-directed learning, self-directed learning was negatively related to learning anxiety, and self-directed learning partially explained the association between GenAI use and learning anxiety. These ﬁndings indicate that students who use GenAI for understanding, organizing, planning, feedback, and learning management tend to report stronger self-directed learning and lower learning anxiety. The study contributes to the literature by shifting attention from GenAI access, acceptance, and perceived usefulness to the learning processes through which GenAI may become educationally meaningful. Rather than treating GenAI use as",180
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,9,"stronger self-directed learning and lower learning anxiety. The study contributes to the literature by shifting attention from GenAI access, acceptance, and perceived usefulness to the learning processes through which GenAI may become educationally meaningful. Rather than treating GenAI use as a uniform behavior, the ﬁndings suggest that its value depends partly on whether students use it in learning-oriented ways that support planning, monitoring, reﬂection, resource seeking, and independent academic judgment. In this respect, self-directed learning provides an important explanatory pathway linking GenAI-supported learning practices with students’ emotional experiences. At the same time, the ﬁndings should be interpreted within the limits of the study’s cross-sectional and self-report design. The results do not establish causal effects, nor do they imply that all forms of GenAI use reduce learning anxiety. Instead, they suggest that learning-oriented GenAI use may be associated with lower anxiety when it is connected to stronger self-directed learning. For higher education, the key challenge is therefore not Zhang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",166
DOC_010,feduc-11-1840953.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1840953,10,"simply whether students should use GenAI, but how institutions and instructors can support forms of GenAI use that strengthen learner autonomy, reduce unnecessary academic uncertainty, and preserve meaningful engagement with learning. Data availability statement Publicly available datasets were analyzed in this study. This data can be found here: https://zenodo.org/records/20734538. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the Ethics Review Committee of the School of Educational Science, Xinjiang Normal University, Urumqi, China. The studies were conducted in accordance with institutional requirements and the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions ZZ: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Methodology, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Supervision. MK: Project administration, Resources, Validation, Writing – review & editing. MZ: Investigation, Resources, Validation, Writing – review & editing. YZ: Data curation, Investigation, Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declared that ﬁnancial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Conﬂict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any",180
