DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,1,"EDITED BY Mahadih Kyambade, Makerere University Business School, Uganda REVIEWED BY Farooq Ahmed Jam, Global Illuminators, Malaysia Mohammed El Messaoudi, Moulay Ismail University, Morocco *CORRESPONDENCE Cheng Zhang cheng.zhang.au@outlook.com RECEIVED 08 July 2026 REVISED 16 August 2026 ACCEPTED 26 August 2026 PUBLISHED 04 September 2026 CITATION Yang Y, Zhang C, Meng H and Shen J (2026) From effectiveness to sustainable use: understanding university students’ adoption of generative AI for academic writing through an extended UTAUT mixed-methods study. Front. Educ. 11:1920168. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Yang, Zhang, Meng and Shen. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. From effectiveness to sustainable use: understanding university students’ adoption of generative AI for academic writing through an extended UTAUT mixed- methods study Yan Yang",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,1,"academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. From effectiveness to sustainable use: understanding university students’ adoption of generative AI for academic writing through an extended UTAUT mixed- methods study Yan Yang 1, Cheng Zhang 2*, Hao Meng 2,3 and Jun Shen 2 1School of Marxism, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China, 2School of Computing and Information Technology, University of Wollongong, Wollongong, NSW, Australia, 3Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan, China Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is rapidly reshaping higher education and students’ academic writing practices. However, existing research has largely focused on technology acceptance and usage intention, with less attention to whether and how GenAI use can be sustained. This study investigates the determinants and dimensions of sustainable GenAI use among university students through a mixed-methods approach, combining an online survey of 1,084 Chinese university students with semi-structured interviews. An extended Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) model was employed to examine the quantitative relationships. The results indicated that performance expectancy (β = 0.195, p <",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,1,"online survey of 1,084 Chinese university students with semi-structured interviews. An extended Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) model was employed to examine the quantitative relationships. The results indicated that performance expectancy (β = 0.195, p < 0.001), effort expectancy (β = 0.094, p = 0.030), social influence (β = 0.147, p < 0.001), perceived enjoyment (β = 0.224, p < 0.001), and perceived creativity support (β = 0.211, p < 0.001) positively affected behavioral intention, whereas perceived risk had a negative effect (β = −0.044, p = 0.019). Facilitating conditions (β = 0.355, p < 0.001) and behavioral intention (β = 0.531, p < 0.001) were positively associated with actual usage behavior. Additionally, gender, educational level, usage experience, and disciplinary background exerted significant moderating effects. Qualitative findings identified three interconnected dimensions of sustainable GenAI use: long-term continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. By extending UTAUT beyond technology acceptance toward a multidimensional conceptualization of sustainable use, this study showed that sustainable GenAI use depended not only on technology acceptance but also on students’",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,1,"continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. By extending UTAUT beyond technology acceptance toward a multidimensional conceptualization of sustainable use, this study showed that sustainable GenAI use depended not only on technology acceptance but also on students’ ability to engage with GenAI critically, responsibly, and autonomously. The findings provide practical insights for fostering AI literacy and the appropriate integration of GenAI in higher education. KEYWORDS academic writing, GenAI, sustainable education, university students, UTAUT model TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 04 September 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",91
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"1 Introduction Academic writing is a complex process that entails university students conducting research, constructing structured argumentation, and conveying discipline-specific knowledge effectively (Malik et al., 2023). Not only does writing exhibit academic competence, but it also fosters students’ self-efficacy, creativity, and critical thinking (Collins et al., 2024). Such multifaceted tasks are especially challenging for second language (L2) students, who are required to simultaneously balance sentence-level proficiency (Lin and Morrison, 2021), knowledge of disciplinary norms (Maher et al., 2014), and the logic of content organization (Aldabbus and Almansouri, 2022). These challenges are compounded by disciplinary, regional, and socio- cultural differences. Escalating academic workloads and expectations for independent research further exacerbate challenges, while limited instructional resources constrain instructors’ ability to provide with timely and personalized academic writing guidance (Gupta et al., 2022). The rapid emergence of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), exemplified by large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT and DeepSeek, has introduced new possibilities for addressing these challenges (McGrath et al., 2025). This technology creates interactive, immersive, and adaptive digital learning environments, thereby partially compensating for the limited availability of instructors’ offline",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT and DeepSeek, has introduced new possibilities for addressing these challenges (McGrath et al., 2025). This technology creates interactive, immersive, and adaptive digital learning environments, thereby partially compensating for the limited availability of instructors’ offline writing support and accommodating students’ inquiry-based and personalized approaches to academic problem-solving (Deep and Chen, 2025). GenAI can offer distinct advantages across multiple stages of academic writing, from ideation and planning to drafting and revision (Kim et al., 2025b; Wu et al., 2026). By assisting with structure, logic, and language expression, it enables university students to concentrate on brainstorming, generating innovative ideas, and engaging in deeper thinking. Conversely, GenAI utilization has also raised concerns regarding technology-related issues (e.g., hallucination, limited contextual understanding, and lack of human awareness) (Kim et al., 2025b), student-related risks (e.g., technology addiction, degradation of writing skills, and diminished higher-order thinking) (Malik et al., 2023) and ethics-related challenges (e.g., privacy breaches, academics integrity, and inequity) (Yan, 2023). This tension has shifted research attention from whether GenAI is beneficial toward how its use can be sustained to support",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"and diminished higher-order thinking) (Malik et al., 2023) and ethics-related challenges (e.g., privacy breaches, academics integrity, and inequity) (Yan, 2023). This tension has shifted research attention from whether GenAI is beneficial toward how its use can be sustained to support students’ learning. Existing studies have documented both the benefits and challenges of GenAI-assisted academic writing, but limited attention has been paid to the factors that influence students’ adoption and continue use, or to how such use can remain balanced, responsible, and conductive to long- term academic development. Notably, existing UTAUT-based studies have largely focused on behavioral intention and use behavior as indicators of technology acceptance. Although UTAUT provides valuable explanations of why users adopt emerging technologies, it alone does not determine whether such use is pedagogically sustainable. Therefore, this study conceptualizes sustainable use beyond technology adoption, encompassing the capacity to maintain meaningful learning, avoid excessive dependence, and engage with AI in an ethical and responsible manner. The Chinese higher education setting offers an informative context for exploring GenAI adoption among university students, allowing the issue to be examined within a",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"maintain meaningful learning, avoid excessive dependence, and engage with AI in an ethical and responsible manner. The Chinese higher education setting offers an informative context for exploring GenAI adoption among university students, allowing the issue to be examined within a specific linguistic, educational, and sociocultural environment. This is especially relevant to academic writing, where language proficiency, educational expectations, and technology-use norms may affect students’ adoption decisions and experiences. Furthermore, quantitative studies alone may not fully capture students’ perceptions regarding the prudent and sustained use of GenAI, highlighting the need for complementary qualitative insights. To address these gaps, this study adopts an extended Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) mixed- methods approach to investigate what enables sustainable GenAI use in academic writing among university students. Moving beyond conventional UTAUT research, the framework examines the factors influencing students’ behavioral intention and use behavior in relation to sustainability, while also considering the moderating effects of individual characteristics, and incorporating qualitative evidence on students’ perceptions of balanced, responsible, and long-term GenAI use. By bridging technology acceptance and educational sustainability, this study",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"intention and use behavior in relation to sustainability, while also considering the moderating effects of individual characteristics, and incorporating qualitative evidence on students’ perceptions of balanced, responsible, and long-term GenAI use. By bridging technology acceptance and educational sustainability, this study advances understanding of the drivers and sustaining conditions of GenAI use among students. 2 Literature review 2.1 The use of GenAI in higher education GenAI has recently transformed higher education by enabling personalized instruction, enhancing digital learning tools, and streamlining administrative processes (Cui et al., 2025). It has sparked growing research interest in how GenAI is experienced by students, particularly regarding learning practices, active engagement, and academic outcomes. Literature studies have documented its application across various academic disciplines, such as language education (Lee et al., 2025) and medical education (Almazrou et al., 2025). Given its flexibility and versatility, GenAI has been primarily applied to curriculum design, research support, subject comprehension, and assignment evaluation, aiming to improve traditional pedagogy and complement personalized learning. This broader trend is also evident in AI-driven discovery across other fields, where intelligent systems support complex analytical,",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"been primarily applied to curriculum design, research support, subject comprehension, and assignment evaluation, aiming to improve traditional pedagogy and complement personalized learning. This broader trend is also evident in AI-driven discovery across other fields, where intelligent systems support complex analytical, predictive, and optimization tasks (Gondi et al., 2026). For instance, ChatGPT has been shown to function as a knowledgeable “conversational partner”, playing a pivotal role in encouraging students’ active and collaborative learning both inside and outside the classroom. When used for academic inquiries, it can generate instant responses, encourage continuous interactions, and provide customized recommendations (Lai et al., 2023). Acting as virtual tutors and digital peers, GenAI tools provide interactive dialogue and personalized learning experiences (Kim et al., 2025a), which can help stimulate curiosity, sustain engagement, and nurture a sense of accomplishment. Besides, Yusuf et al. (2024) surveyed university students and lecturers across 76 countries regarding the application of GenAI in higher education from multicultural perspectives. Their findings revealed that information retrieval and text paraphrasing were the primary uses of GenAI, highlighting cultural variations in Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,2,"and lecturers across 76 countries regarding the application of GenAI in higher education from multicultural perspectives. Their findings revealed that information retrieval and text paraphrasing were the primary uses of GenAI, highlighting cultural variations in Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",44
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"attitudes and expectations, particularly concerning academic dishonesty. These varied applications demonstrate the growing integration of GenAI into routine academic activities, while also underscoring the need to assess its educational value across diverse learning settings (Yue and Sukumaran, 2025). Regarding the impact of GenAI use in higher education, two main perspectives have emerged. Many studies have acknowledged the transformative potential of GenAI-assisted learning in enhancing academic performance, bolstering learning motivation and engagement, and facilitating reflective practice (Essel et al., 2024; McGrath et al., 2025). For example, Essel et al. (2024) conducted a controlled experiment to investigate the effects of ChatGPT on undergraduate students’ cognitive skills. The findings showed that ChatGPT significantly promoted students’ critical, reflective, and creative thinking, supporting its integration into higher education curricula. A recent review highlighted ChatGPT could enhance learning experience by serving as a conversational partner in areas such as writing and language learning, and aid skill development in fields like programming and engineering (McGrath et al., 2025). These perceived benefits enable students to overcome common academic challenges, including poor time management, information overload, and the lack",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"areas such as writing and language learning, and aid skill development in fields like programming and engineering (McGrath et al., 2025). These perceived benefits enable students to overcome common academic challenges, including poor time management, information overload, and the lack of timely guidance (Almulla, 2024). Empirically, Abbas et al. (2024) found that academic workload and time pressure were significant antecedents of ChatGPT usage among university students, whereas sensitivity to rewards negatively predicted such use, indicating its role as a coping mechanism under academic strain. Driven by their availability and accessibility, GenAI tools have also gained increasing popularity and user satisfaction among university students (Lund et al., 2026; Ng et al., 2025). Similar evidence from other technology-intensive contexts suggested that technology readiness was essential for translating technological availability into the effective use of new technologies (Ahmad, 2025). Satisfaction, defined as the extent to which students’ overall affective responses and evaluations of GenAI meet or exceed their academic needs, may further reinforce continued use (Baig and Yadegaridehkordi, 2025; Mehmood, 2025). However, these benefits should not be conflated with uniform positive outcomes, as",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"extent to which students’ overall affective responses and evaluations of GenAI meet or exceed their academic needs, may further reinforce continued use (Baig and Yadegaridehkordi, 2025; Mehmood, 2025). However, these benefits should not be conflated with uniform positive outcomes, as the broader educational and ethical dimensions of GenAI use remain contested. This debate has drawn attention to concerns associated with GenAI utilization in higher education contexts, including potential misuse, copyright issues, and the erosion of trust, might hinder independent thinking (Almazrou et al., 2025). Lee et al. (2025) further concluded that GenAI challenges in language learning could be categorized into technological issues (e.g., querying challenges and information bias) and educational issues (e.g., overreliance and pedagogical limitation). The concept of overreliance (or dependence) has attracted interests as a problematic pattern of GenAI use, typified by impaired self- control, obsessive cognitive engagement, and withdrawal-like responses. The Interaction of Person-Affect-Cognition-Execution (I-PACE) model offers a theoretical lens for elucidating the underlying cognitive and affective mechanisms (Brand et al., 2016; Revesai, 2025; Zhong et al., 2024). Recent applications of the I-PACE framework to GenAI-related research",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"and withdrawal-like responses. The Interaction of Person-Affect-Cognition-Execution (I-PACE) model offers a theoretical lens for elucidating the underlying cognitive and affective mechanisms (Brand et al., 2016; Revesai, 2025; Zhong et al., 2024). Recent applications of the I-PACE framework to GenAI-related research have demonstrated how factors such as academic self-efficacy, academic stress, and performance expectations may contribute to the development of problematic AI engagement patterns among university students (Zhang et al., 2024). In addition, perceived anthropomorphism, interactivity, and intelligence of GenAI tools have been shown to shape users’ cognitive, affective, and behavioral responses, thereby reinforcing maladaptive usage patterns. These processes may ultimately lead to the erosion of critical thinking abilities, degradation of personal skills, and reduced depth of knowledge acquisition (Revesai, 2025). By developing a dedicated measurement scale, Hou et al. (2025) further identified four distinct patterns of GenAI reliance: reflective use, cautious use, thoughtless use, and collaborative use. These reliance patterns were affected by students’ confidence level, prior exposure to GenAI, and perceived ease of use. 2.2 GenAI-assisted academic writing The advent of GenAI has accelerated a paradigm shift in academic",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"cautious use, thoughtless use, and collaborative use. These reliance patterns were affected by students’ confidence level, prior exposure to GenAI, and perceived ease of use. 2.2 GenAI-assisted academic writing The advent of GenAI has accelerated a paradigm shift in academic writing within higher education, reflecting the broader integration of technological innovation into education (Wu et al., 2026). Research suggested that university students were increasingly adopting GenAI to support their writing processes and outputs, particularly in L2 writing (Ou et al., 2024). This expanding use reflects the significant potential of GenAI to reduce linguistic and technical barriers and provide immediate and personalized writing support, yet its educational implications remain contested. The literature indicated that GenAI can improve writing performance, cognitive engagement, and affective experiences, while also raise concerns about over-reliance, cognitive offloading, and the erosion of independent writing. Previous studies have underscored that GenAI contributed to writing performance across multiple aspects, including language translation, literature summarization, outline preparation, content revision, proofreading and formatting (Malik et al., 2023). It could refine vocabulary, grammar, organization, coherence, and clarity, foster the expression of complex",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"underscored that GenAI contributed to writing performance across multiple aspects, including language translation, literature summarization, outline preparation, content revision, proofreading and formatting (Malik et al., 2023). It could refine vocabulary, grammar, organization, coherence, and clarity, foster the expression of complex concepts, and offer instant feedback to facilitate the quality of manuscripts (Moradi, 2026; Tseng and Lin, 2024). Such affordances not only promote writing proficiency and efficiency but also enhance writing across multiple genres (Kim et al., 2025b). However, improved writing performance does not necessarily translate into improved writing competence. Over-dependence on GenAI tools may diminish students’ engagement in the “reading-writing-revision” processes, impair language development and academic voice, and increase the risks of unintentional plagiarism and fabricated citations (Yan, 2023). Accordingly, GenAI may function either as a writing scaffold or as a substitute for writing practice, depending on the extent to which its use complements students’ own writing activities (Gabay et al., 2026). Beyond writing performance, GenAI has also been linked to university students’ cognitive engagement, including professional knowledge, critical thinking, creativity, and self-regulated learning. Existing literature indicated that such tools",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,3,"which its use complements students’ own writing activities (Gabay et al., 2026). Beyond writing performance, GenAI has also been linked to university students’ cognitive engagement, including professional knowledge, critical thinking, creativity, and self-regulated learning. Existing literature indicated that such tools were effective in enriching university students’ topic-related knowledge, stimulating new ideas and deeper thinking, and supporting alternative interpretations (Kong et al., 2024; Liu et al., 2025; Malik et al., 2023). For example, Wang et al. (2024) found that ChatGPT positively affected writing training and professional skill Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",95
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"development among medical students with limited research experience. Similarly, prior analyses of doctoral students’ use of GenAI for data interpretation, visualization, and auxiliary programming, have showed that highly interactive and iterative prompting was strongly associated with writing performance, higher-order thinking, and creative involvement (Nguyen et al., 2024). Nevertheless, GenAI-driven writing tools may substitute for core cognitive processes, such as drafting, evaluation, and revision, thereby encouraging cognitive offloading and undermining reasoning abilities and originality (Deep and Chen, 2025). This is a critical issue because short-term productivity does not always correspond to long-term cognitive development. GenAI-assisted academic writing also has great implications for university students’ affective experiences, including autonomy, self- efficacy, anxiety, and confidence (Chen et al., 2025; Malik et al., 2023). Most studies agreed that university students perceived GenAI positively, particularly when it was used as an interactive writing partner that provided engaging, accessible, and responsive assistance. Reported benefits included increased confidence in completing demanding academic tasks, reduced error anxiety, and stronger perceived ability to compensate for language proficiency deficiencies (Wang, 2025; Yaoqin, 2026). GenAI combined with guided prompts significantly improved",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"provided engaging, accessible, and responsive assistance. Reported benefits included increased confidence in completing demanding academic tasks, reduced error anxiety, and stronger perceived ability to compensate for language proficiency deficiencies (Wang, 2025; Yaoqin, 2026). GenAI combined with guided prompts significantly improved graduate students’ positive affective states, including inspiration, alertness, and attentiveness, particularly in L2 writing (Chen et al., 2025). Despite GenAI’s growing popularity, its application in academic writing carries risks such as limited contextual understanding (Malik et al., 2023), constraints on independent thinking and motivation (Mahapatra, 2024), and accessibility disparities between high- and low-income countries (Salvagno et al., 2023). These findings demonstrate that positive affective experiences need to be considered alongside students’ self-efficacy, trust, and ethical awareness when integrating GenAI into academic writing (Fasih, 2025; Zhang et al., 2026). Research on writing performance, cognitive engagement, and affective experiences suggests that GenAI-assisted academic writing is characterized by a benefit-risk trade-off. The educational value of GenAI hinges not merely on whether university students use it, but on how and under what conditions they incorporate it into their academic writing. From this perspective, sustainable",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"academic writing is characterized by a benefit-risk trade-off. The educational value of GenAI hinges not merely on whether university students use it, but on how and under what conditions they incorporate it into their academic writing. From this perspective, sustainable GenAI adoption refers to integrating GenAI to meet immediate academic needs while upholding students’ independent writing and critical thinking. Although prior studies have examined the effectiveness and risks of GenAI in academic writing, limited attention has been paid to the factors shaping students’ adoption decisions and to the variations in perceptions and experiences across different student groups. Hence, this study identifies the determinants of GenAI adoption and examines students’ motivations and concerns, offering a foundation for understanding how GenAI can be embedded with academic writing in responsible and sustainable ways. 3 Theoretical framework and hypotheses In this study, the UTAUT model is extended by integrating four additional experiential variables: perceived creativity support, perceived trust, perceived enjoyment, and perceived risk. The proposed model incorporates the original core determinants (performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, and facilitating conditions), and the relationships between",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"model is extended by integrating four additional experiential variables: perceived creativity support, perceived trust, perceived enjoyment, and perceived risk. The proposed model incorporates the original core determinants (performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, and facilitating conditions), and the relationships between behavioral intention and use behavior, as well as moderating variables established by Venkatesh et al. (2000) and Venkatesh et al. (2003). To test this extended model, fourteen hypotheses were formulated to explain university students’ use of GenAI-assisted academic writing. Performance expectancy (PE) is defined as the degree to which university students believe that using GenAI will improve their academic writing performance. Students who perceive that GenAI enhances writing efficiency and quality through instant and personalized feedback tend to acknowledge its utility and develop stronger behavioral intentions. This relationship is consistently corroborated by prior research, which identifies PE as a key determinant of an individual’s behavioral intention to adopt new technologies (Wang et al., 2009; Wu et al., 2022). Effort expectancy (EE) is conceptualized as the degree of ease associated with using GenAI tools for academic writing. The less effort required",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"determinant of an individual’s behavioral intention to adopt new technologies (Wang et al., 2009; Wu et al., 2022). Effort expectancy (EE) is conceptualized as the degree of ease associated with using GenAI tools for academic writing. The less effort required to learn and operate these tools, the more likely students are to adopt them. Thus, GenAI’s user-friendly interface and interactive features may strengthen perceived utility and willingness to engage. Prior studies have established EE as a direct predictor of behavioral intention, while also serving as a positive antecedent to PE (Han et al., 2025; Li, 2025; Wang et al., 2009). Social influence (SI) refers to the extent to which university students perceive that important others (e.g., institutions, teachers, and peers) expect or encourage them to use GenAI. Such social expectations can directly shape students’ their willingness to use GenAI and influence their perceptions of its academic usefulness, with previous research identifying a positive correlations between SI and PE (Al-Adwan et al., 2022; Li, 2025). Facilitating conditions (FC) encompass the resources and support that enable university students to use GenAI effectively",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"influence their perceptions of its academic usefulness, with previous research identifying a positive correlations between SI and PE (Al-Adwan et al., 2022; Li, 2025). Facilitating conditions (FC) encompass the resources and support that enable university students to use GenAI effectively including accessible tools, technical infrastructure, training opportunities, and institutional support. Such conditions can foster use behavior by reducing practical barriers and enhance EE by improving the ease of access and operation (Chatterjee and Bhattacharjee, 2020; Xu et al., 2025). Behavioral intention (BI) represents university students’ willingness to use GenAI for academic writing and acts as the proximal mechanism translating technology evaluations into use behavior. Students’ perceptions of GenAI’s usefulness and their motivation to engage with it can facilitate BI, which in turn positively predicts use behavior (Cui et al., 2025; Xu et al., 2025). Use behavior (UB) denotes the current practices of university students in employing GenAI to assist academic writing. UB is reflected in the actual use of GenAI tools across various stages of the writing process, such as preparation, drafting, and revision. While UTAUT captures the determinants of",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,4,"practices of university students in employing GenAI to assist academic writing. UB is reflected in the actual use of GenAI tools across various stages of the writing process, such as preparation, drafting, and revision. While UTAUT captures the determinants of technology acceptance, it offers less attention to experiential and uncertainty-driven evaluations that are particularly important in GenAI-assisted academic writing. Unlike conventional digital technologies, GenAI can generate content, stimulate ideas, and affords conversational interaction, while simultaneously raising concerns about reliability, academic integrity, and excessive dependence. Therefore, this study extends it with four complementary constructs. Perceived creativity support (PCS) Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",106
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,5,"reflects students’ belief that using GenAI for academic writing can promote idea generation, diverse perspectives, and creative expression in academic writing. It reflects a writing- specific source of value beyond general PE, with existing studies linking perceived novelty and idea-generation support to behavioral intention (Moghavvemi and, Jam 2025; Xu et al., 2025). Furthermore, prior research underscored the moderating role of novelty experience in enhancing active learning intention (Yang et al., 2025). Perceived trust (PT) refers to the degree to which university students perceive GenAI as reliable and dependable in the context of academic writing. Trust is particularly consequential when students turn to GenAI for accurate and valuable assistance with complex academic tasks. Empirical studies have confirmed that PT exerts a significant positive influence on students’ acceptance and use of new technologies (Chao, 2019; Han et al., 2025). Perceived enjoyment (PEJ) captures the intrinsic pleasure and sense of playfulness experienced during interaction with GenAI. Enjoyable interactions can bolster behavioral intention while enhancing students’ perceptions of PE and EE (Chao, 2019; Wang et al., 2009). Perceived risk (PR) is defined as students’",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,5,"captures the intrinsic pleasure and sense of playfulness experienced during interaction with GenAI. Enjoyable interactions can bolster behavioral intention while enhancing students’ perceptions of PE and EE (Chao, 2019; Wang et al., 2009). Perceived risk (PR) is defined as students’ perceived likelihood and severity of negative outcomes associated with GenAI, including academic misconduct, misinformation, and over-reliance. Although the preceding constructs account for anticipated benefits or positive evaluations, PR highlights the potential costs and uncertainties of GenAI use and is likely to diminish BI (Han et al., 2025). Importantly, the extended framework treats these constructs as interrelated evaluations as opposed to isolated predictors. EE can promote PE by lowering the effort involved in GenAI use, while SI shapes PE by social expectations and normative influence. PEJ can enhance both PE and EE through increasing perceived value and alleviating interaction burden, whereas FC facilitates EE by removing practical usage barriers. The four additional constructs further extend the UTAUT mechanism by incorporating creativity, experiential value, trust, and risk into students’ technology evaluations of GenAI. These multidimensional assessments jointly affect BI, which subsequently leads",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,5,"FC facilitates EE by removing practical usage barriers. The four additional constructs further extend the UTAUT mechanism by incorporating creativity, experiential value, trust, and risk into students’ technology evaluations of GenAI. These multidimensional assessments jointly affect BI, which subsequently leads to UB. Beyond the direct relationships specified in the extended UTAUT model, this study considers whether the effects of GenAI-related perceptions vary across students with different individual and contextual characteristics. Building on the original UTAUT model, which identified gender, age, experience, and voluntariness of use as key moderators (Venkatesh et al., 2003), this study examines four characteristics (gender, educational level, usage experience, and disciplinary background) to reflect established and context-specific sources of heterogeneity in GenAI adoption. While gender and prior experience align with the original UTAUT framework, educational level and disciplinary background broaden this moderating perspective to the specific academic writing context. The moderation analysis explore whether the strength of such relationships depends on who the student is, what stage of study they are in, how much experience they have with GenAI, and the disciplinary context in which they use",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,5,"writing context. The moderation analysis explore whether the strength of such relationships depends on who the student is, what stage of study they are in, how much experience they have with GenAI, and the disciplinary context in which they use it. The research hypotheses are outlined in Table 1, with the corresponding conceptual model presented in Figure 1. 4 Methods 4.1 Research design and data collection This study adopted an explanatory sequential mixed-methods approach to explore how university students employed GenAI to support their academic writing. Figure 1 represents the conceptual model of the quantitative strand and Figure 2 illustrates the overall mixed-methods research process. An online questionnaire survey was designed first to collect the quantitative data. Prior to distribution, a pilot survey was conducted with 20 university students to gather feedback and suggestions regarding the questionnaire items, response options and overall structure. Only respondents who reported having experience using GenAI for academic writing were eligible to participate in this survey. The questionnaire was distributed via the Tencent Questionnaire platform in November 2025 to university students in China, adhering to",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,5,"and overall structure. Only respondents who reported having experience using GenAI for academic writing were eligible to participate in this survey. The questionnaire was distributed via the Tencent Questionnaire platform in November 2025 to university students in China, adhering to the principle of voluntary participation. A total of 1,222 responses were collected. After excluding 138 invalid submissions (including those completed in less than 4 min and incomplete responses), 1,084 valid responses were retained for final analysis. TABLE 1 Research hypotheses. Construct No. Hypotheses Performance expectancy (PE) H1 PE is positively related to BI towards GenAI- assisted academic writing Effort expectancy (EE) H2 EE is positively related to BI towards GenAI- assisted academic writing H3 EE is positively related to PE towards GenAI-assisted academic writing Social influence (SI) H4 SI is positively related to BI towards GenAI- assisted academic writing H5 SI is positively related to PE towards GenAI- assisted academic writing Perceived creativity support (PCS) H6 PCS is positively related to BI towards GenAI-assisted academic writing Perceived trust (PT) H7 PT is positively related to BI toward GenAI- assisted academic",180
