DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,6,"The questionnaire comprised three sections. The first section collected demographic, background, and usage-related information from the participants, including gender, educational level, field of study, most frequently used GenAI tools, the relevance to GenAI to their studies, usage frequency, prior experience with GenAI tools, and self-assessed writing proficiency. The second section measured ten constructs related to key factors influencing university students’ use of GenAI- assisted academic writing. These constructs included performance expectancy (4 items), effort expectancy (4 items), social influence (4 items), facilitating conditions (5 items), perceived trust (4 items), perceived enjoyment (4 items), perceived creativity support (4 items), perceived risk (4 items), behavioral intention (5 items), and use behavior (4 items). All FIGURE 1 Conceptual model of university students’ adoption and use of GenAI-assisted academic writing. FIGURE 2 Explanatory sequential mixed-methods research design and integration process. Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",145
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,7,"items were rated on a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1 (“strongly disagree”) to 5 (“strongly agree”), with higher scores indicating stronger agreement. The third part comprised three open-ended questions designed to capture students’ perspectives on improving AI-assisted writing tools, concerns about their negative consequences, and recommendations for institutional guidance or regulation. The measurement items were originally developed in English and translated into Chinese by a bilingual researcher familiar with the higher education and GenAI context. Following the quantitative phase, a semi-structured interview protocol consisting of 10 open-ended questions was developed to further explore students’ experiences, perceptions, and concerns regarding the use of GenAI for academic writing. Interview recruitment continued until thematic saturation was reached, with no substantially new themes emerging from the later interviews. A total of 15 volunteer students participated in the interviews, including 7 males and 8 females, of whom 9 were undergraduate students and 6 were postgraduate students. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to the interviews. Each interview lasted approximately 25 min and was conducted individually via face-to-face or Tencent Meeting. All interviews",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,7,"females, of whom 9 were undergraduate students and 6 were postgraduate students. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to the interviews. Each interview lasted approximately 25 min and was conducted individually via face-to-face or Tencent Meeting. All interviews were audio-recorded using a digital voice recorder. We also took handwritten notes during each interview to record key points, contextual information, and issues requiring further clarification. The recordings were transcribed and checked against the original audio to correct transcription errors and ensure that responses were accurately represented. To protect participants’ confidentiality, all participants were assigned pseudonyms, and identifying information was removed in the transcripts. The interviews were conducted in Chinese. The recordings were transcribed in the original language and subsequently translated into English for reporting. The qualitative phase was connected to the quantitative phase through the use of quantitative findings to inform the focus of the subsequent interviews. The qualitative interviews were used to provide explanatory and contextual insights into students’ adoption decisions and, in particular, to explore how students interpreted continued, balanced, and responsible use of GenAI in academic",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,7,"to inform the focus of the subsequent interviews. The qualitative interviews were used to provide explanatory and contextual insights into students’ adoption decisions and, in particular, to explore how students interpreted continued, balanced, and responsible use of GenAI in academic writing. The two strands were subsequently brought together during interpretation to generate integrated conclusions about sustainable GenAI use. 4.2 Quantitative sample Table 2 presents the descriptive statistics of the study sample. Most responses were obtained from Guangdong (14.9%), Shandong (10.5%), Jiangxi (9.0%), and Jiangsu (8.4%) provinces. The gender distribution was relatively balanced, with 603 male (55.63%) and 481 female (44.37%) participants. In terms of educational level, over 65% respondents were undergraduates, while 34.69% were postgraduates (Master’s or PhD students). The majority (50.83%) of the respondents were enrolled in STEM disciplines and the remainder were distributed across fields such as Humanities and Social Sciences (20.76%), Economics, Management and Law (18.73%), as well as other disciplines including Arts and Design, Agricultural, and Medical Sciences (9.69%). Among the surveyed students, the highest proportion (35.89%) described their majors or research fields as being “Slightly",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,7,"and Social Sciences (20.76%), Economics, Management and Law (18.73%), as well as other disciplines including Arts and Design, Agricultural, and Medical Sciences (9.69%). Among the surveyed students, the highest proportion (35.89%) described their majors or research fields as being “Slightly relevant” to computer science (CS) (e.g., Data Analysis, Information Management), followed by “Partially relevant” (e.g., AI tool applications), “Highly relevant” (AI, Machine Learning), and “Not relevant at all”. The most frequently used GenAI tools were DeepSeek (43.54%), ChatGPT (23.06%), Doubao AI (20.11%), and ERNIE Bot (9.96%). Regarding usage frequency, 32.66% of students reported using GenAI tools “Often” (4–6 times per week) for academic writing, 31.83% used them “Sometimes” (1–3 times per week), and only 3.41% reported rarely using these tools. In terms of usage experience, 34.96% of respondents had used GenAI tools for 3–6 months, 33.21% for 6–12 months, and 25.92% for one year or more, whereas only 5.9% reported less than three months of experience. These patterns indicated that GenAI technologies have gained increasing popularity among students in higher education and were being actively applied to academic writing processes.",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,7,"25.92% for one year or more, whereas only 5.9% reported less than three months of experience. These patterns indicated that GenAI technologies have gained increasing popularity among students in higher education and were being actively applied to academic writing processes. Concerning self- assessed writing proficiency, the largest group of students (45.02%) considered themselves as “Fairly confident”, while acknowledging a need for guidance in certain aspects. Nearly equal proportions of students described themselves as “Very confident” or having “Moderate” confidence. A smaller share (10.33%) reported being “Not confident” and identified writing as a major challenge. 5 Results 5.1 Measurement model assessment The measurement model was evaluated to assess the reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity of the constructs and their corresponding items. The evaluation followed established criteria, requiring that each indicator’s estimated coefficient be statistically significant on its posited latent construct factor (Fornell and Larcker, 1981; Lee, 2010). A shown in Table 3, Cronbach’s alpha coefficients ranged from 0.824 to 0.893, indicating strong internal consistency for all constructs. Composite Reliability (CR) values also exceeded the recommended threshold of 0.8, ranging from",180
