DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,10,"(β = −0.044, p = 0.019). Although statistically significant, the magnitude of this association was small, suggesting that perceived risk acted as a relatively weak constraint on students’ intention to use GenAI for academic writing. Furthermore, use behavior was significantly predicted by facilitating conditions (β = 0.355, p < 0.001) and behavioral intention (β = 0.531, p < 0.001). However, perceived trust had no significant influence on behavioral intention. One possible reason is students could adopt GenAI without unconditional trust because critical verification enables instrumental use under uncertainty. Accordingly, hypotheses H1, H2, H4, H6, H8, H11, H12, and H14 were supported, while H7 was rejected. The findings also revealed that effort expectancy (β = 0.264, p < 0.001), social influence (β = 0.330, p < 0.001), and perceived enjoyment (β = 0.302, p < 0.001) positively affected performance expectancy. Additionally, effort expectancy was significantly influenced by perceived enjoyment (β = 0.366, p < 0.001) and facilitating conditions (β = 0.404, p < 0.001). Consequently, hypotheses H3, H5, H9, H10, and H13 were supported. 5.3 Moderating effects analysis This study explored",180
