DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,12,"relationship between perceived risk and behavioral intention. The effect was weaker for students with more than six months of GenAI usage experience compared to those with less than six months (β = 0.089, p = 0.026). This pattern suggests that greater familiarity with GenAI may reduce the extent to which perceived risks constrain students’ willingness to use the technology. Regarding discipline background, students majoring in fields that were “Highly relevant” or “Partially relevant” to CS exhibited a significantly stronger relationship between effort expectancy and performance expectancy than those in “Slightly relevant” or “Not relevant at all” fields (β = 0.278, p = 0.002). Similarly, the relationship between perceived enjoyment and effort expectancy was significantly stronger among students in more CS-relevant disciplines than in the less relevant fields (β = 0.309, p < 0.001). Disciplinary context may shape how students perceive and interact with GenAI. Accordingly, universities should avoid relying exclusively on generic, discipline-neutral GenAI training. 5.4 Qualitative findings To complement the quantitative findings and gain deeper insights into students’ experiences, semi-structured interviews were analyzed thematically. The researchers first read the",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,12,"and interact with GenAI. Accordingly, universities should avoid relying exclusively on generic, discipline-neutral GenAI training. 5.4 Qualitative findings To complement the quantitative findings and gain deeper insights into students’ experiences, semi-structured interviews were analyzed thematically. The researchers first read the transcripts and accompanying interview notes repeatedly. Meaningful segments related to students’ motivations, experiences, concerns, and practices concerning GenAI-assisted academic writing were then identified and assigned initial codes. Codes with related meanings were grouped into broader categories, after which candidate themes were developed by examining recurring patterns across participants. Thematic analysis of the interview transcripts revealed three overarching themes aligned with the three dimensions of sustainable use: long-term continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. Table 7 presents the thematic structure, including sub-themes and representative codes. 5.4.1 Long-term continuance The interview findings indicated that most participants viewed GenAI as a tool that would continue to play an important role in their academic writing. Rather than viewing AI as a temporary trend or a short-term aid, many students believed that its ability to improve writing efficiency, facilitate learning, and",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,12,"as a tool that would continue to play an important role in their academic writing. Rather than viewing AI as a temporary trend or a short-term aid, many students believed that its ability to improve writing efficiency, facilitate learning, and support skill development would encourage its sustained use in the future. Several participants highlighted that GenAI contributes not only to immediate writing outcomes but also to the development of transferable academic writing skills. For example, Jason, an undergraduate in medical science, described FIGURE 4 Simple-slope plots of significant moderating effects. Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",99
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"GenAI as a learning-oriented companion rather than a simple content generator: “I think it is more like a sparring coach than a simple writing assistant. It does not just help me put sentences together, but helps me improve the logic of my writing and learn more appropriate academic expressions. If one day AI is no longer available, I believe I would still be able to produce a competent paper because the writing frameworks and expression skills have already been developed through repeated interactions with the tool. The only difference might be that the writing process becomes slower.” A similar view was expressed by Luna, who is a PhD student in law, who perceived GenAI as an educational resource that supported intellectual growth and writing improvement: “I think AI plays the role of a mentor in academic writing. It has significantly improved my writing ability, particularly in generating ideas, refining language, and checking logical consistency. Even if I could no longer use AI, I believe I would still be able to write high-quality papers. However, using AI has broadened my academic",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"improved my writing ability, particularly in generating ideas, refining language, and checking logical consistency. Even if I could no longer use AI, I believe I would still be able to write high-quality papers. However, using AI has broadened my academic horizons and exposed me to new ways of thinking that I might not have considered otherwise.” Participants also emphasized that their intention to continue using GenAI was closely related to the efficiency gains it provides. Frank, a master’s student in management, explained: “I think I will continue using it in the long term because the efficiency gains are substantial, as long as I maintain an appropriate level of control. If universities impose stricter regulations, I would adjust my practices accordingly, such as using AI mainly for brainstorming rather than directly incorporating AI-generated content into formal manuscripts.” Although participants generally expressed positive attitudes towards sustained use, they tended to view GenAI as a supportive tool rather than a replacement for human authorship. Elena, an undergraduate student in economics, commented that “AI is ultimately just a tool,” while several others indicated that",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"positive attitudes towards sustained use, they tended to view GenAI as a supportive tool rather than a replacement for human authorship. Elena, an undergraduate student in economics, commented that “AI is ultimately just a tool,” while several others indicated that future use would depend on maintaining appropriate levels of personal engagement and control. Overall, these findings suggest that students’ long-term continuance intentions are primarily driven by the perceived educational value, efficiency gains, and skill-enhancement opportunities associated with GenAI-assisted writing. 5.4.2 Balanced and moderate use A prominent theme from the interviews was students’ awareness of the need to maintain a balanced relationship with GenAI. While participants acknowledged the substantial benefits of AI-assisted writing, many also expressed concerns about becoming overly dependent on the technology and losing their capacity for independent thinking. Several participants emphasized the need to remain the primary driver of the writing process. For example, Alex, who is a master student in engineering, explained that AI was mainly used to expand and refine ideas rather than generate the core intellectual content: “In many cases, I start with a vague",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"of the writing process. For example, Alex, who is a master student in engineering, explained that AI was mainly used to expand and refine ideas rather than generate the core intellectual content: “In many cases, I start with a vague idea and then ask AI to help me expand my thinking or add details. However, the central argument is still determined by myself. I do worry that relying on AI for too long might make me less willing to think independently, so whenever AI gives me suggestions, I first organize and evaluate them myself before deciding whether they are worth using.” Concerns regarding dependency were expressed even more explicitly by Jason: “AI is developing so rapidly that many of the ideas I obtain from it are not entirely my own. In the long run, it is easy to fall into a habit of dependence, and I am concerned that I may gradually lose my ability to think independently. Therefore, I believe it is important to keep learning by my- self, maintain rational thinking, and always remember that AI can make",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"a habit of dependence, and I am concerned that I may gradually lose my ability to think independently. Therefore, I believe it is important to keep learning by my- self, maintain rational thinking, and always remember that AI can make mistakes. We should never treat AI as something that is always correct.” In addition to maintaining autonomy and avoiding over- reliance, participants consistently positioned AI as an assistant rather than a replacement for their own intellectual work. Luna emphasized the importance of preserving academic agency while benefiting from AI support: “I think I will continue using AI in academic writing, but only on the premise that I maintain my own initiative and independence. …AI should remain a tool that supports TABLE 7 Thematic framework of sustainable GenAI use in academic writing. Theme Sub-theme Representative codes Long-term continuance Sustained intention Long-term use, efficiency gains, continued adoption Capability enhancement Learning improvement, writing skill development Balanced and moderate use Maintaining autonomy Own ideas, independent thinking, selective adoption Avoiding over- reliance Dependency concerns, cognitive decline concerns AI as assistant AI as tool, scaffold, support",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,13,"use, efficiency gains, continued adoption Capability enhancement Learning improvement, writing skill development Balanced and moderate use Maintaining autonomy Own ideas, independent thinking, selective adoption Avoiding over- reliance Dependency concerns, cognitive decline concerns AI as assistant AI as tool, scaffold, support rather than replacement Responsible and ethical use Information verification Fact-checking, source validation, cross-checking Academic integrity Original authorship, disclosure, avoiding misconduct Regulatory compliance Adapting to university/journal policies Data privacy Information security, protecting sensitive data Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",82
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"academic work rather than replacing the researcher’s intellectual contribution.” At the same time, participants generally did not advocate avoiding AI altogether. In-stead, they emphasized using AI in a complementary manner. For example, Jason de-scribed selectively incorporating AI-generated ideas only after personal evaluation. These findings indicate that sustainable GenAI use requires a careful balance between leveraging technological support and maintaining independent learning, critical thinking, and authorship. 5.4.3 Responsible and ethical use The interviews further revealed that responsible and ethical use constituted an essential component of sustainable GenAI adoption. Participants frequently discussed concerns related to academic integrity, information accuracy, privacy protection, and compliance with institutional regulations. A commonly strategy was to critically evaluate and verify AI-generated information before incorporating it into academic work. Cathy, an under-graduate student in social science, described how previous experiences with inaccurate outputs had shaped their current practices: “I do not completely trust the information generated by AI, especially when it comes to references and data. After encountering several errors, I developed a habit of checking everything myself. If AI provides references, I always verify the original sources",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"“I do not completely trust the information generated by AI, especially when it comes to references and data. After encountering several errors, I developed a habit of checking everything myself. If AI provides references, I always verify the original sources through academic databases before using them. Otherwise, it is easy to be misled by incorrect information.” Academic integrity was another recurring concern. Many participants stressed that AI should assist the writing process without replacing students’ own intellectual contributions. For example, Kevin, a postgraduate student in engineering, stated: “I am concerned about information leakage, over-reliance, and the possibility of being accused of academic misconduct. Therefore, I only use AI to polish language and improve sentence structures. The arguments, interpretations, and analyses must be written by myself. I also avoid uploading unpublished or sensitive research data to AI systems.” Participants further indicated a willingness to adapt their practices in response to institutional requirements. Several stated that if universities or academic journals introduced stricter AI regulations, they would modify their usage accordingly. Alex explained that he would continue using AI while limiting its",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"a willingness to adapt their practices in response to institutional requirements. Several stated that if universities or academic journals introduced stricter AI regulations, they would modify their usage accordingly. Alex explained that he would continue using AI while limiting its role to language refinement and idea development and ensuring compliance with relevant policies. 6 Discussion 6.1 Key factors affecting GenAI use for academic writing Our findings indicated that performance expectancy, effort expectancy and social influence exhibited significant positive effects on behavioral intention, aligning with previous studies (Al-Adwan et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2022). The influence of performance expectancy may be attributed to the substantial achievement pressure students face within performance-oriented academic environments (Cui et al., 2025). In this context, GenAI can support writing activities through enhancing efficiency, providing immediate feedback, and presenting tailored content, thereby mitigating cognitive load and reinforcing students’ intention to adopt these tools (Cui, 2025). Effort expectancy is also a critical factor, as the flexible and user-friendly interfaces of GenAI tools lower the perceived learning threshold and facilitate their seamless integration into academic writing practices. Supported",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"reinforcing students’ intention to adopt these tools (Cui, 2025). Effort expectancy is also a critical factor, as the flexible and user-friendly interfaces of GenAI tools lower the perceived learning threshold and facilitate their seamless integration into academic writing practices. Supported by digital-native confidence, students are more inclined to adopt technologies that are perceived as easy to learn and use (Xu et al., 2025). Technology designers should prioritize improvements in usability and functionality, particularly through innovation, to foster more positive acceptance of new technologies (Chang et al., 2017). Furthermore, social influence, including encouragement by peers and instructors, as well as institutional or policy-level support, significantly shapes students’ perception of GenAI as a legitimate learning aid. Exposure to peer usage further amplifies normative pressure and competitive motivation, which together accelerate the use of GenAI in academic writing contexts. Consistent with prior studies, this research confirmed a positive correlation between perceived enjoyment and behavioral intention (Chao, 2019; Wang et al., 2009). Perceived enjoyment enhances students’ engagement and task satisfaction by making interactions pleasurable, which is achieved through timely responses to academic inquiries and",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"this research confirmed a positive correlation between perceived enjoyment and behavioral intention (Chao, 2019; Wang et al., 2009). Perceived enjoyment enhances students’ engagement and task satisfaction by making interactions pleasurable, which is achieved through timely responses to academic inquiries and effective support for self- directed learning (Cui, 2025). The more students perceive GenAI-based academic writing as novel, playful or relaxing, the stronger their intention to use it. The qualitative findings further suggest that enjoyment may be intertwined with students’ perception of GenAI as an interactive learning partner. Therefore, it is essential to develop GenAI’ s potential for enabling gamified learning experiences and to promote students’ enjoyment when interactively engaging with it as a digital learning partner, as this in turn increases their usage intention in academic writing. Perceived creativity support was also found to be significantly and positively related to behavioral intention, consistent with the findings of Moghavvemi and Jam (2025). This relationship suggested that by refining grammatical expression, GenAI freed up students’ time and cognitive resources, allowing them to engage more deeply in brainstorming and to tackle complex academic",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,14,"behavioral intention, consistent with the findings of Moghavvemi and Jam (2025). This relationship suggested that by refining grammatical expression, GenAI freed up students’ time and cognitive resources, allowing them to engage more deeply in brainstorming and to tackle complex academic problems. Acting as a supportive mentor, GenAI helps students overcome mental stagnation, generate multiple perspectives, and express complex ideas more creatively through dynamic interaction with the tool. Perceived risk was found to produce a negative impact on university students’ intention to utilize GenAI for academic Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 14 frontiersin.org",94
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"writing. For example, interview participants identified inaccurate information, academic misconduct, privacy concerns, and over- reliance as reasons for limiting their use of GenAI. As noted by Wu et al. (2022), risks such as algorithm errors and negative external evaluations might undermine students’ intention to use AI in learning contexts. In contrast, perceived trust had no significant effect on behavioral intention, possibly because students already have sufficient confidence in the predictable, trustworthy and reliable operation of GenAI. Although trust did not directly predict behavioral intention, interview participants frequently described practices of verification and selective reliance. Therefore, it remains important to foster a positive group perception environment to alleviate any remaining apprehension or reluctance and to maintain trust in data security, system reliability, and perceived benefits through transparent communication when implementing new technologies (Joshi et al., 2025). The results also indicated that actual use behavior of GenAI- assisted academic writing was directly influenced by behavioral intention and facilitating conditions. Facilitating conditions, such as platform access, training, device availability, and relevant knowledge, enhance students’ motivation and create opportunities for adoption (Xu et al.,",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"actual use behavior of GenAI- assisted academic writing was directly influenced by behavioral intention and facilitating conditions. Facilitating conditions, such as platform access, training, device availability, and relevant knowledge, enhance students’ motivation and create opportunities for adoption (Xu et al., 2025). Strong motivation helps students overcome obstacles and act on their intention, thereby sustaining or increasing GenAI use. Furthermore, effort expectancy, social influence, and perceived enjoyment were found to positively affected performance expectancy in GenAI- assisted writing. Students who perceive new technologies as easy to use are more likely to consider them useful and continue using them (Al-Adwan et al., 2022). The link between perceived enjoyment and performance expectancy has been corroborated by (Chang et al., 2017). Additionally, effort expectancy was positively influenced by both perceived enjoyment and facilitating conditions, as supported by (Chatterjee and Bhattacharjee, 2020). 6.2 Moderating effects of individual characteristics The relationship between performance expectancy and behavioral intention was moderated by gender. One possible reason is that, compared with males, females tend to focus more on how GenAI enhances writing outcomes and efficacy, thereby strengthening perceived usefulness",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"of individual characteristics The relationship between performance expectancy and behavioral intention was moderated by gender. One possible reason is that, compared with males, females tend to focus more on how GenAI enhances writing outcomes and efficacy, thereby strengthening perceived usefulness and usage intention. Gender also moderated the effect of effort expectancy on performance expectancy. Males show a stronger preference for using GenAI as an effective assistant due to their emphasis on task engagement and time efficiency. The accessibility and customization features of GenAI further support their ability to manage multiple writing tasks efficiently (Li, 2025). In addition, gender mediated the relationship between perceived enjoyment and behavioral intention, suggesting that males place greater emphasis on active and experiential engagement when using GenAI. The influence of effort expectation on performance expectation was stronger among postgraduates than undergraduates. Facing high research pressure, postgraduates focus on their professional fields and are more willing to use GenAI for writing tasks due to its ease of use. In contrast, perceived creativity support had a stronger impact on behavioral intention for undergraduates, who value GenAI’s role in",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"focus on their professional fields and are more willing to use GenAI for writing tasks due to its ease of use. In contrast, perceived creativity support had a stronger impact on behavioral intention for undergraduates, who value GenAI’s role in facilitating cross-language and cross-disciplinary resources acquisition and providing mentor-like guidance. These functions enhance learning autonomy, reflective and creative thinking, and usage intention. Interestingly, the effect of perceived enjoyment on behavioral intention was also stronger for postgraduates than for undergraduates. This can be explained by the fact that postgraduates have more opportunities to engage with advanced tools, which may increase their intrinsic satisfaction and perceived competence. Usage experience mediated the relationship between perceived risk and behavioral intention, which keeps in line with the findings of Li (2025). Students with longer GenAI usage experience are more familiar with its functions and limitations, which reduces uncertainty concerns and reinforces its perceived auxiliary value (Cui et al., 2025). Disciplinary differences were also observed. Specifically, majors more closely related to computer science showed stronger moderating effects on the relationship between effort expectancy and performance expectancy,",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"which reduces uncertainty concerns and reinforces its perceived auxiliary value (Cui et al., 2025). Disciplinary differences were also observed. Specifically, majors more closely related to computer science showed stronger moderating effects on the relationship between effort expectancy and performance expectancy, as well as between perceived enjoyment and effort expectancy, compared to less relevant majors. This is likely because students in computer science who frequently use technology for programming and data analysis are more comfortable using GenAI. They leverage it to free up time for exploratory activities, thereby enhancing task engagement and achievement motivation (Jin et al., 2025). 6.3 Sustainable use of GenAI in academic writing The qualitative findings add an important dimension to the quantitative results by showing that actual use should not be equated with sustainable use. Three interrelated dimensions emerged from the interviews: long-term continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. Most participants expressed a willingness to continue using GenAI in the future because of its ability to improve writing efficiency, support idea generation, and enhance academic writing skills. Many students viewed GenAI not only",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"use, and responsible and ethical use. Most participants expressed a willingness to continue using GenAI in the future because of its ability to improve writing efficiency, support idea generation, and enhance academic writing skills. Many students viewed GenAI not only as a productivity tool but also as a mentor-like learning companion that could contribute to their long-term academic development (Fan and Liu, 2026). Meanwhile, students emphasized the importance of maintaining a balanced relationship with GenAI. Although they acknowledged its benefits, many were concerned that excessive reliance on AI might weaken independent thinking and creativity. As a result, participants generally preferred to use GenAI as a supplementary tool while retaining ownership of core ideas, arguments, and critical analysis. The educational value of GenAI may therefore be maximized when students remain active participants in knowledge construction rather than passive consumers of AI-generated content (Almufarreh, 2026). Additionally, responsible and ethical use was considered essential for sustainable GenAI adoption. Participants reported verifying AI-generated information, protecting sensitive data, and adapting their usage practices to comply with institutional regulations. These findings suggested that sustainable GenAI use",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,15,"content (Almufarreh, 2026). Additionally, responsible and ethical use was considered essential for sustainable GenAI adoption. Participants reported verifying AI-generated information, protecting sensitive data, and adapting their usage practices to comply with institutional regulations. These findings suggested that sustainable GenAI use Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 15 frontiersin.org",49
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"depended not only on technological usefulness but also on students’ capacity to engage critically, independently, and responsibly with AI-assisted writing tools. This observation supports recent calls for higher education institutions to move beyond restrictive approaches and instead cultivate students’ ability to engage with AI responsibly and transparently (Arbulú Ballesteros et al., 2024). Theoretically, this study extends the UTAUT framework by incorporating experiential and creativity- related factors into the explanation of GenAI adoption. The mixed-methods findings further distinguish technology adoption from sustainable use, suggesting that continued use must be accompanied by critical evaluation, cognitive autonomy, and responsible engagement. 6.4 Implications The findings offer practical implications for the sustainable use of GenAI in academic writing. First, students increasingly expect GenAI systems to move beyond basic text generation and provide more interactive, discipline-specific, and adaptive support. Many participants viewed GenAI not merely as a productivity tool but as a mentor-like learning companion. Therefore, developers should continue improving the accuracy, transparency, and personalization of GenAI systems while incorporating discipline-sensitive customization features that align outputs with domain-specific academic conventions. Furthermore, transparency-enhancing functions, such as traceable",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"a productivity tool but as a mentor-like learning companion. Therefore, developers should continue improving the accuracy, transparency, and personalization of GenAI systems while incorporating discipline-sensitive customization features that align outputs with domain-specific academic conventions. Furthermore, transparency-enhancing functions, such as traceable edits and calibration prompts, may help students better understand and critically evaluate AI-generated content while maintaining the usability of these tools (Robayo-Pinzon et al., 2025). The findings also highlight the importance of fostering balanced and moderate use. Although students recognized the benefits of GenAI for brainstorming, language refinement, and writing efficiency, they also expressed concerns about excessive dependence and the potential erosion of independent thinking. Rather than restricting AI use, educators should encourage reflective and self-regulated engagement strategies, helping students distinguish between assistive applications and excessive cognitive delegation (Huang et al., 2026). In practice, instructors could incorporate activities that require students to compare AI- generated content with authoritative sources, identify inaccuracies or weaknesses, and revise AI-generated suggestions. Embedding AI literacy, metacognitive training, and critical evaluation skills into writing curricula may help students maintain academic autonomy while benefiting from AI-assisted learning",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"students to compare AI- generated content with authoritative sources, identify inaccuracies or weaknesses, and revise AI-generated suggestions. Embedding AI literacy, metacognitive training, and critical evaluation skills into writing curricula may help students maintain academic autonomy while benefiting from AI-assisted learning (Zhang and Xu, 2025). Given the moderating effects identified across gender, academic level, and discipline, differentiated instructional approaches may also be necessary to accommodate students’ diverse learning needs and patterns of AI use. For example, undergraduate students may benefit more from activities focused on idea development and independent thinking, whereas postgraduate students may require greater emphasis on critical evaluation and the integration of GenAI into advanced academic writing. Responsible and ethical use should be regarded as a cornerstone of sustainable GenAI adoption. Students generally supported transparent governance rather than outright prohibition. Universities should therefore establish clear guidelines regarding acceptable AI use, disclosure requirements, and academic integrity standards (Kim et al., 2025a; Lund et al., 2026). Universities could also provide discipline-specific examples and case-based guidance to help students apply these principles to realistic academic writing situations. Assessment practices should place greater",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"use, disclosure requirements, and academic integrity standards (Kim et al., 2025a; Lund et al., 2026). Universities could also provide discipline-specific examples and case-based guidance to help students apply these principles to realistic academic writing situations. Assessment practices should place greater emphasis on research processes, critical reflection, and original intellectual contributions rather than solely evaluating final written outputs. In addition, case-based guidance and practical training on fact- checking, privacy protection, and responsible AI use can help students develop the competencies needed to engage with GenAI critically and ethically. For students with limited GenAI experience, such training could focus on basic risk recognition and verification strategies, whereas more experienced users could be supported in developing advanced skills. Overall, sustainable GenAI use requires a balanced approach that simultaneously supports long-term learning benefits, preserves students’ independent thinking, and promotes responsible engagement with AI technologies. Achieving this balance will require coordinated efforts from students, educators, institutions, and technology developers. 7 Conclusion This study explored how university students’ sustainable use of GenAI-assisted academic writing through a mixed-methods approach. The quantitative findings revealed that performance expectancy, effort",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"Achieving this balance will require coordinated efforts from students, educators, institutions, and technology developers. 7 Conclusion This study explored how university students’ sustainable use of GenAI-assisted academic writing through a mixed-methods approach. The quantitative findings revealed that performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, perceived enjoyment, and perceived creativity support significantly enhanced students’ behavioral intention to use GenAI, whereas perceived risk negatively influenced intention. Facilitating conditions and behavioral intention were found to be significant predictors of actual use behavior. Furthermore, gender, educational level, usage experience, and disciplinary background moderated several relationships within the model, highlighting the heterogeneous nature of students’ GenAI adoption patterns. These findings suggested that sustainable GenAI use was shaped not only by the perceived use-fulness and accessibility of the technology but also by individual and contextual factors that influenced students’ intentions and actual engagement with AI- assisted writing. The qualitative findings complement these results by revealing how students conceptualize sustainable GenAI use in practice. Three dimensions emerged from the interviews: long-term continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. While students generally viewed GenAI as a",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,16,"The qualitative findings complement these results by revealing how students conceptualize sustainable GenAI use in practice. Three dimensions emerged from the interviews: long-term continuance, balanced and moderate use, and responsible and ethical use. While students generally viewed GenAI as a valuable learning companion that supports writing efficiency and skill development, they also emphasized the importance of maintaining independent thinking, avoiding over- reliance, verifying AI-generated information, and complying with institutional regulations. Taken together, the findings indicated that sustainable GenAI use involved not only continued adoption of the technology but also the ability to engage with it critically, responsibly, and autonomously. By integrating technology acceptance factors with students’ perceptions of sustainable use, this study contributes to a more comprehensive understanding of sustainable AI-supported learning in higher education. Several limitations should be noted. First, the participants were limited to Chinese university students, resulting in a relatively homogenous sample. Given Yang et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168 Frontiers in Education 16 frontiersin.org",155
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,17,"variations across regions, cultures, educational systems, and languages, the findings may not be fully generalizable to students in other contexts. Future research should expand the sample across different countries, regions, and institutional settings and conduct cross-cultural comparative studies. Second, the study employed a cross-sectional survey design and relied primarily on self-reported data. Future studies could employ longitudinal designs. Such designs would allow longer-term tracking of the effects of GenAI-assisted academic writing across various stages of the writing process. Data availability statement The datasets presented in this study can be found in online repositories. The names of the repository/repositories and accession number(s) can be found below: 10.5281/zenodo.18240889. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the Human Research Ethics Committee, University of Wollongong (protocol code 2022/096; and approval date: 1 June 2022). The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions YY: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Writing – original draft. CZ: Conceptualization, Methodology, Visualization, Writing – original draft. HM: Writing –",180
DOC_012,feduc-11-1920168.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1920168,17,"local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Author contributions YY: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Writing – original draft. CZ: Conceptualization, Methodology, Visualization, Writing – original draft. HM: Writing – review & editing. JS: Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declared that financial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Conflict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. Generative AI statement The author(s) declared that generative AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. During the preparation of this manuscript, the authors used ChatGPT (OpenAI, GPT-4) for the purposes of improving the clarity and accuracy of English language grammar and syntax. Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside figures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artificial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify",180
