DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,1,"EDITED BY Eduardo Hernández-Padilla, Autonomous University of the State of Morelos, Mexico REVIEWED BY David Pérez-Jorge, University of La Laguna, Spain Pamela McCray, University of Texas at San Antonio, United States *CORRESPONDENCE Zeyu Zhang 3148738209@qq.com Xiaomei Lu 1908006355@qq.com RECEIVED 11 July 2026 REVISED 11 August 2026 ACCEPTED 24 August 2026 PUBLISHED 10 September 2026 CITATION Nie J, Zhang Z, Lu X, Zhang Y and Zhang M (2026) Development and validation of the generative AI assessment literacy scale for higher education students: psychometric evidence and associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity. Front. Educ. 11:1934632. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Nie, Zhang, Lu, Zhang and Zhang. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Development and validation of the generative AI assessment",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,1,"are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Development and validation of the generative AI assessment literacy scale for higher education students: psychometric evidence and associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity Juntao Nie 1, Zeyu Zhang 2*, Xiaomei Lu 3*, Yinlan Zhang 4 and Min Zhang 5 1School of Educational Science, Xinjiang Normal University, Urumqi, China, 2Judicial Police College, Xinjiang University of Political Science and Law, Tumxuk, China, 3Medical Appraisal Center, Sihong Hospital, Suqian, China, 4Department of Medical Technology, Nanjing Second Hospital, Nanjing, China, 5Teaching and Research Group, Suqian Chongwen Middle School, Suqian, China Introduction: Students now use generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) to interpret assignments, revise drafts, check evidence, and respond to feedback on assessed work. However, students’ capacity to use GenAI responsibly within assessment contexts remains insufficiently conceptualized and measured. This study developed and validated the Generative AI Assessment Literacy Scale (GAA-LS) for higher education students. Methods: A two-study scale development and validation design",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,1,"work. However, students’ capacity to use GenAI responsibly within assessment contexts remains insufficiently conceptualized and measured. This study developed and validated the Generative AI Assessment Literacy Scale (GAA-LS) for higher education students. Methods: A two-study scale development and validation design was used. An initial pool of 30 items was generated from literature on assessment literacy, AI-supported assessment, feedback engagement, and academic integrity. After expert review and pilot testing, Study 1 conducted item analysis and exploratory factor analysis with 486 students. Study 2 examined confirmatory factor analysis, reliability, convergent and discriminant validity, measurement invariance, criterion-related validity, known-group validity, and structural associations with 798 students. Results: The final 18-item scale supported a five-factor structure: assessment criteria awareness, AI-task appropriateness judgment, verification and evidence checking, ethical attribution and academic integrity, and feedback uptake and revision literacy. In Study 1, exploratory factor analysis indicated good sampling adequacy (KMO = .931) and a five-factor solution explaining 67.82% of the variance. In Study 2, the five-factor CFA model showed acceptable fit, χ2(125) = 344.72, CFI = .954, TLI = .944, RMSEA = .047, and SRMR =",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,1,"good sampling adequacy (KMO = .931) and a five-factor solution explaining 67.82% of the variance. In Study 2, the five-factor CFA model showed acceptable fit, χ2(125) = 344.72, CFI = .954, TLI = .944, RMSEA = .047, and SRMR = .042. Internal consistency was satisfactory to strong, with alpha ranging from .83 to .88 across subscales and .93 for the total scale. Evidence of convergent, discriminant, criterion-related, and known-group validity was obtained, and measurement invariance was supported across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency groups. GAA-LS scores were positively associated with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention, with a significant indirect association through feedback engagement. TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 10 September 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",118
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"Discussion: The GAA-LS provides a psychometrically supported instrument for assessing students’ assessment-specific capacity to use GenAI responsibly. The findings contribute to applied measurement, feedback design, and academic integrity policy in AI-supported higher education. KEYWORDS academic integrity, assessment literacy, feedback engagement, generative AI, higher education assessment, psychometric validation 1 Introduction Generative artificial intelligence has rapidly become part of higher education learning environments. Students increasingly use large language models and related tools to understand assignment requirements, generate outlines, revise drafts, translate ideas, check evidence, and obtain feedback on assessed work (Chan and Hu, 2023; Chiu, 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023; UNESCO, 2023). These practices create new opportunities for formative learning, but they also change the conditions under which assessment evidence is produced and interpreted. Existing research has documented the opportunities and risks of AI-supported assessment and feedback (Alnemrat et al., 2025; Lipnevich et al., 2026). Frameworks such as the Artificial Intelligence Assessment Scale highlight the need to specify when and how GenAI may be used in assessment tasks (Perkins et al., 2024), while recent work on authentic assessment and academic integrity shows",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"et al., 2026). Frameworks such as the Artificial Intelligence Assessment Scale highlight the need to specify when and how GenAI may be used in assessment tasks (Perkins et al., 2024), while recent work on authentic assessment and academic integrity shows that assessment design must respond to the possibility of AI-supported work (Kofinas et al., 2025; Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency, 2024). The use of GenAI also raises questions about academic integrity. When students use AI to clarify instructions, critique a draft, or check evidence, the use may be pedagogically defensible. When students submit AI-generated content without verification or disclosure, the same technology may undermine authorship, transparency, and fairness. The boundary between legitimate support and inappropriate substitution therefore depends not only on institutional rules but also on students’ assessment-specific judgment. AI literacy is an important construct in this context, but general AI literacy does not fully capture the assessment-specific knowledge, judgment, and disclosure practices required when students use GenAI for graded assignments, rubrics, feedback, and revision. Existing AI literacy frameworks emphasize understanding, use, evaluation, and ethical issues (Long and Magerko,",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"general AI literacy does not fully capture the assessment-specific knowledge, judgment, and disclosure practices required when students use GenAI for graded assignments, rubrics, feedback, and revision. Existing AI literacy frameworks emphasize understanding, use, evaluation, and ethical issues (Long and Magerko, 2020; Ng et al., 2021), yet they do not provide a focused measure of students’ capacity to interpret assessment criteria, judge task-specific AI-use boundaries, verify AI- generated content, and disclose AI support in assessed work. Generative AI assessment literacy is defined as students’ assessment-specific capacity to understand assessment criteria, judge the appropriateness of GenAI use, verify AI-generated content or feedback, disclose AI use ethically, and use AI- supported feedback for responsible revision in assessment- related learning tasks. It is not proposed as a replacement for general AI literacy, assessment literacy, feedback literacy, or academic integrity literacy. Rather, it is conceptualized as an integrative construct that becomes necessary when GenAI is used within assessed work, where students must make situated judgments about criteria, task boundaries, evidence quality, disclosure, and revision. The present study develops and validates the Generative AI Assessment Literacy",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"as an integrative construct that becomes necessary when GenAI is used within assessed work, where students must make situated judgments about criteria, task boundaries, evidence quality, disclosure, and revision. The present study develops and validates the Generative AI Assessment Literacy Scale (GAA-LS) for higher education students. The study is designed as a psychometric and applied measurement project rather than a general cross-sectional study of AI use. It focuses on scale development, dimensionality, reliability, validity, measurement invariance, and criterion- related associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention. The study makes three contributions. First, it clarifies GenAI assessment literacy as an assessment-specific integrative construct that links AI-related judgment to assessment criteria, evidence use, feedback uptake, and ethical disclosure. Second, it develops and validates a multidimensional scale with evidence from expert content validation, exploratory factor analysis, confirmatory factor analysis, reliability, validity, measurement invariance, and criterion-related associations. Third, it examines how GenAI assessment literacy is associated with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention, while interpreting these relationships as associations rather than causal effects. Unlike prior work focusing on learning-oriented GenAI use and learning",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"invariance, and criterion-related associations. Third, it examines how GenAI assessment literacy is associated with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention, while interpreting these relationships as associations rather than causal effects. Unlike prior work focusing on learning-oriented GenAI use and learning anxiety in higher education (Zhang et al., 2026), the present study focuses specifically on assessment-related GenAI literacy and applied measurement. 2 Literature review and theoretical framework 2.1 Generative AI in higher education assessment The integration of GenAI into higher education is particularly consequential for assessment. Students can use AI to interpret prompts, generate outlines, critique drafts, simulate feedback, and check evidence. These practices affect not only learning support but also the validity, transparency, and fairness of assessment decisions (Bearman et al., 2024; Moorhouse et al., 2023; Perkins et al., 2024). Recent work in AI-supported assessment emphasizes that the question is not simply whether AI should be allowed or prohibited. A more productive question is how assessment tasks can specify appropriate AI-use boundaries, require students to exercise evaluative judgment, and support transparent disclosure (Kofinas et al., 2025; Perkins et al., 2024;",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,2,"not simply whether AI should be allowed or prohibited. A more productive question is how assessment tasks can specify appropriate AI-use boundaries, require students to exercise evaluative judgment, and support transparent disclosure (Kofinas et al., 2025; Perkins et al., 2024; Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency, 2024). AI-generated criteria and feedback may be useful, but they require expert review, Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",68
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"pedagogical mediation, and local calibration before being used for educational decisions (Nábělková et al., 2026). From an applied measurement perspective, GenAI also affects the validity of assessment interpretations. If students use AI differently across tasks, courses, or disciplines, observed performance may reflect unequal access to or unequal understanding of AI-supported assessment practices. This makes assessment transparency and student assessment literacy more important in the GenAI era. 2.2 Assessment literacy and evaluative judgment Assessment literacy refers to the knowledge, skills, and dispositions needed to understand assessment purposes, criteria, evidence, feedback, and standards (Black and Wiliam, 1998; Brown, 2019; Sadler, 1989). In higher education, students’ assessment literacy is closely connected to evaluative judgment, because students must learn to recognize quality, apply criteria, and judge whether work meets explicit or implicit standards (Boud et al., 2018; Smith et al., 2013; Tai et al., 2018). Rubrics and criterion-based assessment can make standards more transparent, but students still need opportunities to interpret and use these criteria in practice (Andrade, 2019; Brookhart, 2013; Panadero and Jonsson, 2013). The proposed GAA-LS builds on this tradition but narrows",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"2018). Rubrics and criterion-based assessment can make standards more transparent, but students still need opportunities to interpret and use these criteria in practice (Andrade, 2019; Brookhart, 2013; Panadero and Jonsson, 2013). The proposed GAA-LS builds on this tradition but narrows the construct to GenAI-mediated assessment tasks. Assessment criteria awareness is included because students need to understand rubrics and task requirements before they can judge whether AI-generated suggestions are useful. AI-task appropriateness judgment is included because AI use may be legitimate in some assessment activities and inappropriate in others. Verification and evidence checking is included because AI- generated content may contain factual errors, fabricated references, or plausible but weak reasoning. Ethical attribution and academic integrity is included because transparent disclosure is central to responsible AI use. Feedback uptake and revision literacy is included because feedback is educationally valuable only when students can interpret and act on it critically. 2.3 AI-generated feedback and feedback engagement Feedback is a central process through which assessment can support learning (Carless and Boud, 2018; Hattie and Timperley, 2007; Henderson et al., 2019; Nicol and Macfarlane- Dick,",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"can interpret and act on it critically. 2.3 AI-generated feedback and feedback engagement Feedback is a central process through which assessment can support learning (Carless and Boud, 2018; Hattie and Timperley, 2007; Henderson et al., 2019; Nicol and Macfarlane- Dick, 2006; Winstone et al., 2017). However, feedback becomes useful only when students can understand, evaluate, and act on it. Feedback literacy research therefore emphasizes students’ capacity to appreciate feedback, make judgments, manage affective responses, and take action to improve work (Carless and Boud, 2018; Little et al., 2024; Molloy et al., 2020). The literature on AI-driven feedback shows substantial variation in tools, contexts, methods, and outcomes (Lipnevich et al., 2026). Some studies show that AI feedback can support writing and revision, while others highlight risks related to overreliance, generic comments, inaccurate suggestions, and weak alignment with assessment criteria (Alnemrat et al., 2025; Bearman et al., 2024). For this reason, students need not only access to AI feedback, but also the assessment literacy needed to interpret AI feedback critically. In the present study, feedback engagement is treated as an external criterion",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"al., 2025; Bearman et al., 2024). For this reason, students need not only access to AI feedback, but also the assessment literacy needed to interpret AI feedback critically. In the present study, feedback engagement is treated as an external criterion and a possible explanatory link between GenAI assessment literacy and academic integrity intention. This interpretation is informed by self-regulated learning and evaluative judgment perspectives, which emphasize that students learn from assessment when they can interpret criteria, monitor the quality of their work, use feedback strategically, and regulate subsequent action (Bandura, 1986; Carless and Boud, 2018; Nicol and Macfarlane-Dick, 2006; Pintrich, 2000; Tai et al., 2018; Zimmerman, 2000). Students who understand assessment criteria and AI-use boundaries may be more likely to engage with feedback as a learning resource rather than as an answer generator. Such engagement may also be associated with stronger academic integrity intention because students revise work while retaining responsibility for the final submission. 2.4 Academic integrity and ethical AI disclosure GenAI has intensified debates about plagiarism, authorship, originality, and academic integrity (Bittle and El-Gayar, 2025; Bretag, 2013; Dawson,",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"stronger academic integrity intention because students revise work while retaining responsibility for the final submission. 2.4 Academic integrity and ethical AI disclosure GenAI has intensified debates about plagiarism, authorship, originality, and academic integrity (Bittle and El-Gayar, 2025; Bretag, 2013; Dawson, 2020; Peterson, 2025). Detection-focused responses alone are insufficient because students may use AI in ways that range from legitimate support to inappropriate substitution of their own work. Institutional guidance increasingly emphasizes transparent rules, disclosure expectations, assessment redesign, and student education about responsible GenAI use (Perkins et al., 2024; Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency, 2024; UNESCO, 2023). Academic integrity intention is included in this study as a criterion variable because it reflects students’ willingness to comply with AI-use rules, disclose AI assistance when required, avoid unacknowledged AI substitution, and take responsibility for final submissions. In the GenAI era, academic integrity is not only a matter of avoiding misconduct. It also includes transparent, accountable, and evidence-based use of AI as part of learning. The ethical dimension of GAA-LS is therefore not a separate moral add-on. It is a central part of",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,3,"is not only a matter of avoiding misconduct. It also includes transparent, accountable, and evidence-based use of AI as part of learning. The ethical dimension of GAA-LS is therefore not a separate moral add-on. It is a central part of assessment literacy in the GenAI era. Students cannot be considered assessment literate if they can use AI efficiently but cannot judge when, how, and whether that use should be disclosed in assessed work. 2.5 Conceptual distinctiveness of GenAI assessment literacy The GAA-LS is not intended to replace general AI literacy, assessment literacy, feedback literacy, or academic integrity literacy. It is conceptualized as an assessment-specific integrative construct that becomes salient when GenAI is used within assessed work. General AI literacy addresses understanding and using AI systems, whereas assessment literacy concerns criteria, Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",139
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"standards, evidence, and feedback. GenAI assessment literacy focuses on the point at which these domains intersect: students must decide whether AI support is appropriate for a particular task, whether AI-generated information is evidentially reliable, whether AI assistance requires disclosure, and how AI- supported feedback can inform revision while preserving authorship. This boundary condition is what justifies the proposed construct. In assessment settings, GenAI does not simply add another digital tool; it changes the evidential conditions under which student work is produced and interpreted. The same AI action can be acceptable in one assessment context and inappropriate in another. Therefore, responsible GenAI use requires situated assessment judgment rather than generic technological competence alone. The five GAA-LS dimensions were derived to represent this sequence of situated judgments, moving from criteria and task boundaries to verification, disclosure, and feedback-based revision. Supplementary Table S8 summarizes how the construct differs from adjacent literacy domains. 2.6 The present study Drawing on the literature reviewed above, the GAA-LS was designed as a five-dimensional instrument. The proposed dimensions were assessment criteria awareness, AI–task appropriateness judgment, verification and evidence",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"summarizes how the construct differs from adjacent literacy domains. 2.6 The present study Drawing on the literature reviewed above, the GAA-LS was designed as a five-dimensional instrument. The proposed dimensions were assessment criteria awareness, AI–task appropriateness judgment, verification and evidence checking, ethical attribution and academic integrity, and feedback uptake and revision literacy. These dimensions were intended to capture the main forms of judgment students need when using GenAI in assessment-related tasks: understanding criteria, judging permissible support, checking AI-generated information, disclosing AI assistance, and using feedback for revision. This study addressed four research questions. First, what is the dimensional structure of the GAA-LS? Second, does the GAA-LS provide acceptable evidence of reliability, validity, and measurement invariance? Third, how is GenAI assessment literacy associated with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention? Fourth, does feedback engagement account for the indirect association between GenAI assessment literacy and academic integrity intention? Consistent with these questions, five hypotheses were tested. The GAA-LS was expected to show a five-factor structure, demonstrate acceptable reliability and validity evidence, and show measurement invariance across gender, discipline, and AI- use frequency",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"assessment literacy and academic integrity intention? Consistent with these questions, five hypotheses were tested. The GAA-LS was expected to show a five-factor structure, demonstrate acceptable reliability and validity evidence, and show measurement invariance across gender, discipline, and AI- use frequency groups. It was also expected that GenAI assessment literacy would be positively associated with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention, and that feedback engagement would partially mediate the association between GenAI assessment literacy and academic integrity intention. The overall development and validation procedure is summarized in Figure 1, and the conceptual structure of GenAI assessment literacy and its criterion-related associations is presented in Figure 2. A comparison between the GAA-LS and adjacent literacy constructs is provided in Supplementary Table S8. 3 Materials and methods 3.1 Study design This study used a two-study cross-sectional scale development and validation design, following established recommendations for scale development and validation research (Boateng et al., 2018; DeVellis and Thorpe, 2022). The design was instrumental and psychometric, rather than purely predictive. It was aligned with recent applied measurement studies that combine theoretical conceptualization, expert review, pilot",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"recommendations for scale development and validation research (Boateng et al., 2018; DeVellis and Thorpe, 2022). The design was instrumental and psychometric, rather than purely predictive. It was aligned with recent applied measurement studies that combine theoretical conceptualization, expert review, pilot testing, and psychometric validation (Angco et al., 2026; Roca et al., 2026). The study did not evaluate the performance of any particular GenAI platform; rather, it examined students’ assessment-specific literacy for responsible GenAI use. Study 1 examined item quality and the exploratory factor structure of the preliminary GAA-LS. Study 2 tested the factor structure using confirmatory factor analysis, compared alternative measurement models, examined reliability and validity evidence, tested measurement invariance across student groups, and evaluated criterion-related associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention. 3.2 Construct definition and item generation The construct was defined as students’ capacity to understand assessment criteria, judge the appropriateness of GenAI use, verify AI-generated content or feedback, disclose AI use ethically, and use AI-supported feedback for responsible revision in assessment-related learning tasks. This definition was used to ensure that the scale measured assessment-specific literacy rather",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"criteria, judge the appropriateness of GenAI use, verify AI-generated content or feedback, disclose AI use ethically, and use AI-supported feedback for responsible revision in assessment-related learning tasks. This definition was used to ensure that the scale measured assessment-specific literacy rather than general AI literacy or general digital competence. An initial pool of 30 items was generated from literature on assessment literacy, criterion-based assessment, AI-supported assessment, feedback engagement, academic integrity, and responsible AI use. The initial item pool contained five intended dimensions, with six items per dimension. Items were developed by mapping each proposed dimension to observable assessment- related behaviors, including interpreting rubrics, judging permitted and prohibited AI support, verifying AI-generated claims and references, disclosing AI assistance, and using feedback for revision. All items used a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. Items were written to refer explicitly to course assignments, assessed work, rubrics, feedback, disclosure rules, or revision decisions. 3.3 Expert review and content validity The initial item pool was reviewed by eight experts. The expert panel included specialists in educational assessment",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,4,"were written to refer explicitly to course assignments, assessed work, rubrics, feedback, disclosure rules, or revision decisions. 3.3 Expert review and content validity The initial item pool was reviewed by eight experts. The expert panel included specialists in educational assessment and measurement, AI in education, higher education teaching and learning, academic integrity, and quantitative research methods. Experts evaluated each item in terms of relevance, clarity, representativeness, assessment-context specificity, and Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",78
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,5,"FIGURE 1 Workflow for the development and validation of the GAA-LS. The workflow summarizes construct definition, item generation, expert review, pilot testing, exploratory factor analysis, confirmatory validation, and the final scale. FIGURE 2 Conceptual framework of generative AI assessment literacy. The framework presents the five assessment-specific dimensions of the GAA-LS and their expected associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",70
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"redundancy. For content validity ratings, experts used a four-point relevance scale from 1 = not relevant to 4 = highly relevant. Item-level content validity indices (I-CVI) and the scale-level average content validity index (S-CVI/Ave) were calculated following established content validity guidance (Polit et al., 2007; Polit and Beck, 2006). Items with low relevance or unclear wording were revised or removed. The expert review reduced the item pool from 30 to 26 items. 3.4 Cognitive interview and pilot testing A cognitive interview and pilot testing phase was conducted with 48 higher education students. Participants commented on item clarity, interpretation, ambiguity, response burden, and whether each item clearly referred to assessment-related GenAI use. Items that were too broad, overlapped with general AI literacy, or could be interpreted outside assessment contexts were revised or removed. Minor wording revisions were made to reduce ambiguity, avoid double-barreled wording, and ensure that the items referred to assessment tasks rather than general AI use. After pilot testing, 23 items were retained for the main survey. The pilot phase was used to refine the instrument before large- scale",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"avoid double-barreled wording, and ensure that the items referred to assessment tasks rather than general AI use. After pilot testing, 23 items were retained for the main survey. The pilot phase was used to refine the instrument before large- scale psychometric analysis and to ensure that the scale retained a clear assessment-specific focus. The item-level retention and removal decisions across expert review, pilot testing, and exploratory factor analysis are reported in Supplementary Table S7. 3.5 Participants and procedure A total of 1,386 questionnaires were returned from students enrolled in six higher education institutions in China. The participating institutions covered different higher education contexts and disciplinary areas rather than representing a single- institution sample. This multi-institutional recruitment was intended to reduce institution-specific bias and improve the interpretability of the psychometric evidence, although the sample was not designed to be nationally representative. After data screening, 1,284 valid responses were retained for analysis, yielding a valid response rate of 92.6%. The sample was randomly split into Study 1 for exploratory factor analysis (n = 486) and Study 2 for confirmatory factor analysis, validity",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"After data screening, 1,284 valid responses were retained for analysis, yielding a valid response rate of 92.6%. The sample was randomly split into Study 1 for exploratory factor analysis (n = 486) and Study 2 for confirmatory factor analysis, validity testing, measurement invariance, and structural modeling (n = 798). Inclusion criteria were: being enrolled in a higher education program, being at least 18 years old, having experience with course assignments or assessment tasks, and having awareness of or experience with GenAI tools. Responses were excluded when they showed excessive missing data, failed attention checks, unrealistically short completion time, straight-lining, or logically inconsistent responses. Participants completed the online questionnaire voluntarily and anonymously through an institutional online survey platform. The questionnaire included the GAA-LS item pool, feedback engagement items, academic integrity intention items, responsible AI use intention items, and demographic and educational variables. Before responding to the questionnaire, participants read a standardized description of generative AI tools. In this study, generative AI tools referred to text-generating or multimodal AI systems that can produce, revise, summarize, translate, evaluate, or provide feedback on academic",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"variables. Before responding to the questionnaire, participants read a standardized description of generative AI tools. In this study, generative AI tools referred to text-generating or multimodal AI systems that can produce, revise, summarize, translate, evaluate, or provide feedback on academic content, such as ChatGPT, DeepSeek, ERNIE Bot, Kimi, Doubao, or similar tools. The description emphasized that the study focused on students’ understanding of GenAI use in assessment-related tasks rather than on the evaluation of any single platform or model version. No GenAI system was used to generate participant responses, score the data, or make psychometric decisions. The demographic and educational characteristics of the two analytic samples are shown in Table 1. 3.6 Measures Generative AI Assessment Literacy Scale. The GAA-LS was developed in the present study. The final version includes 18 items across five dimensions: assessment criteria awareness (4 items), AI-task appropriateness judgment (4 items), verification and evidence checking (4 items), ethical attribution and academic integrity (3 items), and feedback uptake and revision literacy (3 items). Items were rated on a five-point Likert scale from 1 = strongly disagree to",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"AI-task appropriateness judgment (4 items), verification and evidence checking (4 items), ethical attribution and academic integrity (3 items), and feedback uptake and revision literacy (3 items). Items were rated on a five-point Likert scale from 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree. Subscale and total scores were computed as mean scores, with higher scores indicating stronger perceived capacity to use GenAI responsibly in assessment-related tasks. Sample items included: “I can judge whether AI-generated content meets the assessment criteria of a course task,” “I can decide when it is appropriate or inappropriate to use GenAI for an assessment task,” “I verify the accuracy of information, references, and reasoning provided by GenAI before using them in assessed work,” “I know how to disclose my use of GenAI in accordance with academic integrity requirements,” and “I use AI-generated feedback to revise my work critically rather than accepting it without reflection.” The full final item list and scoring instructions are provided in Supplementary Table S6. Feedback engagement. Feedback engagement was measured with 9 items adapted from feedback literacy and feedback engagement research.",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"my work critically rather than accepting it without reflection.” The full final item list and scoring instructions are provided in Supplementary Table S6. Feedback engagement. Feedback engagement was measured with 9 items adapted from feedback literacy and feedback engagement research. The items reflected cognitive engagement with feedback, behavioral uptake, and reflective use of feedback. Sample items included “I compare feedback with my original work carefully,” “I revise my work based on feedback,” and “I reflect on what feedback suggests about my learning.” Items were rated on the same five-point scale, and higher mean scores indicated stronger feedback engagement. Cronbach’s alpha in the present sample was .89. Academic integrity intention. Academic integrity intention was measured with 6 items adapted to the context of GenAI- supported assessment. The items assessed students’ intention to disclose GenAI use when required, avoid submitting AI- generated content as their own work without acknowledgement, comply with course-level AI-use rules, and take responsibility for final submissions. Sample items included “I intend to disclose my use of GenAI when this is required by the course or institution” and “I",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,6,"content as their own work without acknowledgement, comply with course-level AI-use rules, and take responsibility for final submissions. Sample items included “I intend to disclose my use of GenAI when this is required by the course or institution” and “I would not submit AI-generated content as Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",55
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,7,"my own work without proper acknowledgement.” Items were rated on the same five-point scale, and higher mean scores indicated stronger academic integrity intention. Cronbach’s alpha was .87. Responsible AI use intention. Responsible AI use intention was included as an additional external criterion rather than the main outcome. It was measured with 5 items assessing students’ willingness to verify AI outputs, maintain human responsibility for final submissions, avoid uncritical dependence on AI- generated material, and seek clarification when AI-use rules are unclear. Items were rated on the same five-point scale. Higher mean scores indicated stronger responsible AI use intention. Cronbach’s alpha was .88. Demographic and educational variables. These variables included gender, grade, discipline, institution type, AI-use frequency, prior AI training, and whether course-level AI disclosure rules had been provided. AI-use frequency was assessed by asking participants how often they had used GenAI tools for learning or assessment-related tasks during the current semester. Responses were categorized as low use, moderate use, or high use. Low use indicated never or rarely, moderate use indicated sometimes, and high use indicated often or very often.",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,7,"GenAI tools for learning or assessment-related tasks during the current semester. Responses were categorized as low use, moderate use, or high use. Low use indicated never or rarely, moderate use indicated sometimes, and high use indicated often or very often. 3.7 Data analysis Descriptive statistics, item analysis, reliability estimates, and known-group comparisons were conducted in SPSS 27.0. Exploratory factor analysis was conducted in SPSS 27.0 and cross-checked in R 4.3. Confirmatory factor analysis, measurement invariance testing, and structural equation modeling were conducted in Mplus 8.3 using robust maximum likelihood estimation. Because the questionnaire items used ordered Likert-type response categories, ordinal robustness checks were conducted using WLSMV estimation and compared with the robust maximum likelihood solution. Indirect effects were estimated with 5,000 bootstrap samples and 95% confidence intervals. All statistical tests were two-tailed, with p < .05 considered statistically significant. Data screening was conducted before the main analyses. Missing values, response time, straight-lining, attention checks, descriptive distributions, skewness, and kurtosis were examined. Cases with excessive missingness, failed attention checks, or invalid response patterns were removed before the final analytic sample was",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,7,"screening was conducted before the main analyses. Missing values, response time, straight-lining, attention checks, descriptive distributions, skewness, and kurtosis were examined. Cases with excessive missingness, failed attention checks, or invalid response patterns were removed before the final analytic sample was established. In Study 1, item analysis included item means, standard deviations, corrected item-total correlations, and item discrimination. Exploratory factor analysis was conducted using principal axis factoring with oblimin rotation because the dimensions were expected to be conceptually related. Parallel analysis and the scree plot were used to support factor retention. Items were retained when they showed acceptable corrected item-total correlations, salient primary loadings, low cross- loadings, and theoretical interpretability (DeVellis and Thorpe, 2022; Nunnally and Bernstein, 1994). In Study 2, confirmatory factor analysis tested the five-factor correlated model and compared it with alternative models, including a higher-order model, a three-factor collapsed model, and a one-factor model (Brown, 2015; Kline, 2023). Model fit was evaluated using chi-square, degrees of freedom, CFI, TLI, RMSEA, SRMR, and information criteria, following common TABLE 1 Sample characteristics of study 1 and study 2. Variable Category",180
