DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,7,"(10.9%) AI-use frequency Low 149 (30.7%) 243 (30.5%) 392 (30.5%) Moderate 182 (37.4%) 300 (37.6%) 482 (37.5%) High 155 (31.9%) 255 (32.0%) 410 (31.9%) Prior AI training Yes 190 (39.1%) 312 (39.1%) 502 (39.1%) No 296 (60.9%) 486 (60.9%) 782 (60.9%) Course AI disclosure rules Yes 210 (43.2%) 348 (43.6%) 558 (43.5%) No 276 (56.8%) 450 (56.4%) 726 (56.5%) Values are n (%). Study 1 was used for item analysis and exploratory factor analysis. Study 2 was used for confirmatory factor analysis, validity testing, measurement invariance, and structural modeling. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",98
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,8,"recommendations for structural equation modeling and applied psychometrics (Hair et al., 2019; Hu and Bentler, 1999; Kline, 2023). Reliability and validity were examined using Cronbach’s alpha, McDonald’s omega, composite reliability, average variance extracted, Fornell-Larcker evidence, and HTMT (Fornell and Larcker, 1981; Henseler et al., 2015). Measurement invariance was tested across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency groups because these characteristics represent theoretically relevant sources of possible variation in students’ assessment expectations, GenAI experience, and AI- supported learning practices (Baghaei et al., 2026; Putnick and Bornstein, 2016; Vandenberg and Lance, 2000). Configural, metric, and scalar invariance were examined. Undergraduate versus postgraduate invariance was not tested as a separate comparison because postgraduate respondents were grouped with senior students to avoid sparse subgroup cells. Because Likert-type items were ordinal indicators, robustness checks using ordinal estimation and threshold-based invariance logic were also considered (Rhemtulla et al., 2012; Wu and Estabrook, 2016). Criterion-related validity was examined through correlations with feedback engagement, academic integrity intention, and responsible AI use intention. Known-group validity was examined by comparing students with high versus low AI-use frequency, students with and without",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,8,"al., 2012; Wu and Estabrook, 2016). Criterion-related validity was examined through correlations with feedback engagement, academic integrity intention, and responsible AI use intention. Known-group validity was examined by comparing students with high versus low AI-use frequency, students with and without prior AI training, and students with and without course-level AI disclosure rules. Common-method bias was addressed through procedural design and statistical checks, including Harman’s single-factor test, a one-factor CFA comparison, and a common latent factor sensitivity check (Podsakoff et al., 2003). These procedures were used as diagnostic checks rather than as definitive evidence that common-method bias was absent. 3.8 Ethical considerations The study was reviewed and approved by the institutional ethics review body of Xinjiang University of Political Science and Law, Tumxuk, Xinjiang, China, which was responsible for institutional ethical review of this study. Participants were recruited through course-related online notices and were informed that participation was voluntary, anonymous, and unrelated to course grading. Electronic informed consent was obtained from all participants before data collection. 4 Results 4.1 Content validity and item refinement Expert review supported the content relevance of",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,8,"notices and were informed that participation was voluntary, anonymous, and unrelated to course grading. Electronic informed consent was obtained from all participants before data collection. 4 Results 4.1 Content validity and item refinement Expert review supported the content relevance of the item pool. Items with weak assessment-context specificity, conceptual overlap, or ambiguous wording were removed or revised. The item pool was reduced from 30 initial items to 26 items after expert review, 23 items after pilot testing, and 18 items after exploratory factor analysis. The final version retained at least three items per dimension. Table 2 summarizes the item generation, content validity review, pilot refinement, and final item retention process. 4.2 Exploratory factor analysis Study 1 examined the item properties and exploratory factor structure of the 23-item version. Item means ranged from 3.42 to 3.86, standard deviations ranged from .72 to .93, and corrected item-total correlations ranged from .52 to .71. The The KMO value was .931, and Bartlett’s test of sphericity was significant, χ2(153) = 4,862.17, p < .001, indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. Exploratory",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,8,"to .93, and corrected item-total correlations ranged from .52 to .71. The The KMO value was .931, and Bartlett’s test of sphericity was significant, χ2(153) = 4,862.17, p < .001, indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. Exploratory factor analysis supported a five-factor solution corresponding to the hypothesized dimensions. The final 18 items explained 67.82% of the total variance. The first unrotated factor explained 29.41% of the variance, suggesting that a single common factor did not dominate the item set. Factor loadings ranged from .63 to .84 across the five dimensions. Table 3 presents item descriptives, corrected item-total correlations, and exploratory factor loadings for the retained GAA-LS items. 4.3 Confirmatory factor analysis and model comparison Study 2 examined whether the factor structure identified in Study 1 could be replicated in an independent validation sample. The hypothesized five-factor correlated model showed good fit to the data, χ2(125) = 344.72, χ2/df = 2.76, CFI = .954, TLI = .944, RMSEA = .047, and SRMR = .042. As shown in Figure 3, all five dimensions were represented by their intended indicators,",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,8,"model showed good fit to the data, χ2(125) = 344.72, χ2/df = 2.76, CFI = .954, TLI = .944, RMSEA = .047, and SRMR = .042. As shown in Figure 3, all five dimensions were represented by their intended indicators, with TABLE 2 Item generation, content validity, and item retention process. Dimension Initial items After expert review I-CVI range After pilot test Final items Assessment criteria awareness 6 5 .88–1.00 5 4 AI-task appropriateness judgment 6 5 .83–1.00 5 4 Verification and evidence checking 6 6 .88–1.00 5 4 Ethical attribution and academic integrity 6 5 .83–1.00 4 3 Feedback uptake and revision literacy 6 5 .88–1.00 4 3 Total 30 26 .83–1.00 23 18 I-CVI, item-level content validity index; S-CVI/Ave, scale-level average content validity index. Items were removed because of conceptual overlap, ambiguous wording, weak item discrimination, or cross-loading in EFA. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",151
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,9,"standardized factor loadings falling within acceptable ranges. Model comparison further supported the five-factor correlated structure. Although the higher-order model also reached acceptable fit, it showed weaker fit indices than the five-factor model, whereas the three-factor collapsed model and the one-factor model fitted the data substantially less well. These results indicate that the GAA- LS dimensions were related but empirically distinguishable. The ordinal WLSMV robustness check produced a comparable solution (CFI = .958, TLI = .950, RMSEA = .045, SRMR = .039), supporting the stability of the model under an estimator appropriate for ordered categorical indicators. The full model comparison results are reported in Table 4, and additional robustness checks are reported in Supplementary Table S5. 4.4 Reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity Internal consistency was satisfactory to strong. Cronbach’s alpha ranged from .83 to .88 across the five subscales and was .93 for the total scale. McDonald’s omega ranged from .84 to .88 across subscales and was .94 for the total scale. Composite reliability values ranged from .84 to .89, and AVE values ranged from .60 to .66, supporting convergent",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,9,"was .93 for the total scale. McDonald’s omega ranged from .84 to .88 across subscales and was .94 for the total scale. Composite reliability values ranged from .84 to .89, and AVE values ranged from .60 to .66, supporting convergent validity. Discriminant validity was supported because the square root of AVE for each construct exceeded its correlations with other constructs, and HTMT values ranged from .54 to .76, below the .85 threshold. Factor correlations ranged from .46 to .63, indicating that the five dimensions were related but not redundant. Table 5 reports reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity evidence for the GAA-LS. 4.5 Measurement invariance Measurement invariance was examined across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency. Across all three grouping variables, configural, metric, and scalar models showed acceptable fit. Changes in CFI and RMSEA remained within TABLE 3 Item descriptives and exploratory factor loadings of the GAA-LS. Dimension Items retained Mean range SD range CITC range Loading range Assessment criteria awareness 4 3.58–3.80 .72–.86 .55–.68 .65–.81 AI-task appropriateness judgment 4 3.50–3.74 .76–.90 .52–.66 .63–.79 Verification and evidence checking 4 3.42–3.67 .78–.93",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,9,"exploratory factor loadings of the GAA-LS. Dimension Items retained Mean range SD range CITC range Loading range Assessment criteria awareness 4 3.58–3.80 .72–.86 .55–.68 .65–.81 AI-task appropriateness judgment 4 3.50–3.74 .76–.90 .52–.66 .63–.79 Verification and evidence checking 4 3.42–3.67 .78–.93 .59–.71 .69–.84 Ethical attribution and academic integrity 3 3.66–3.86 .74–.85 .56–.69 .71–.83 Feedback uptake and revision literacy 3 3.47–3.72 .77–.89 .58–.70 .68–.82 Overall EFA indicators 18 KMO = .931 Bartlett χ2 (153) = 4,862.17 p < .001 Variance = 67.82% CITC, corrected item-total correlation; EFA, exploratory factor analysis. Factor loadings are based on principal axis factoring with oblimin rotation. FIGURE 3 Standardized confirmatory factor analysis model of the GAA-LS. ACA, assessment criteria awareness; AIAJ, AI-task appropriateness judgment; VEC, verification and evidence checking; EAAI, ethical attribution and academic integrity; FURL, feedback uptake and revision literacy. Inter-factor correlations ranged from .46 to .63. For visual clarity, item residuals and all factor covariances are not displayed. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",162
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,10,"recommended thresholds, supporting the comparability of the GAA-LS across these groups. Measurement invariance results across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency groups are summarized in Table 6. 4.6 Criterion-related and known-group validity Criterion-related validity was supported by positive associations between GAA-LS scores and all external criteria. The GAA-LS total score correlated with feedback engagement (r = .60, p < .001), academic integrity intention (r = .51, p < .001), and responsible AI use intention (r = .59, p < .001). The pattern of subscale correlations was theoretically coherent: feedback uptake and revision literacy showed the strongest association with feedback engagement, whereas ethical attribution and academic integrity showed the strongest association with academic integrity intention. Because the ethical attribution and academic integrity dimension is conceptually close to academic integrity intention, the academic integrity criterion was treated as theoretically proximal criterion evidence. The interpretation of this association therefore emphasizes convergent alignment with responsible assessment behavior rather than complete construct independence. Known-group validity was also supported. Students with high AI-use frequency scored higher than those with low AI-use frequency, students with prior AI training",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,10,"interpretation of this association therefore emphasizes convergent alignment with responsible assessment behavior rather than complete construct independence. Known-group validity was also supported. Students with high AI-use frequency scored higher than those with low AI-use frequency, students with prior AI training scored higher than TABLE 4 Confirmatory factor analysis and model comparison. Model χ2 df χ2/df CFI TLI RMSEA SRMR AIC Five-factor model 344.72 125 2.76 .954 .944 .047 .042 468.72 Higher-order model 397.26 130 3.06 .944 .936 .051 .047 511.26 Three-factor collapsed model 693.84 132 5.26 .884 .862 .073 .069 805.84 One-factor model 1,265.43 135 9.37 .738 .704 .103 .097 1,371.43 CFI, comparative fit index; TLI, Tucker–Lewis index; RMSEA, root mean square error of approximation; SRMR, standardized root mean square residual; AIC, Akaike information criterion. TABLE 5 Reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity of the GAA-LS. Construct Items Mean SD α ω CR AVE √AVE ACA 4 3.70 .63 .86 .87 .87 .62 .79 AIAJ 4 3.62 .66 .85 .86 .86 .60 .77 VEC 4 3.56 .69 .88 .88 .89 .66 .81 EAAI 3 3.76 .65 .83 .84 .84 .64",180
