DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,10,"Discipline Configural .949 .939 .052 .048 – – Discipline Metric .947 .939 .052 .051 −.002 .000 Discipline Scalar .944 .938 .053 .054 −.003 .001 AI-use frequency Configural .952 .942 .050 .046 – – AI-use frequency Metric .950 .942 .050 .048 −.002 .000 AI-use frequency Scalar .947 .940 .051 .052 −.003 .001 Delta CFI and Delta RMSEA indicate changes relative to the previous invariance model. Configural, metric, and scalar invariance were evaluated across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency groups. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 10 frontiersin.org",87
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,11,"those without training, and students whose courses provided AI disclosure rules scored higher than those without such rules. The effects were small to moderate, consistent with the expectation that experience and instructional support are associated with stronger GenAI assessment literacy. Table 7 presents criterion-related validity and known-group validity evidence. 4.7 Structural model and mediation The hypothesized structural model demonstrated an acceptable fit to the data, χ2(218) = 498.35, χ2/df = 2.29, CFI = .955, TLI = .948, RMSEA = .040, and SRMR = .044. Higher GAA-LS scores were positively associated with feedback engagement (β = .56, p < .001), and feedback engagement, in turn, was positively associated with academic integrity intention (β = .37, p < .001). The direct association between GAA-LS scores and academic integrity intention also remained significant (β = .24, p < .001). Bootstrapping results further indicated a significant indirect association of GAA-LS scores with academic integrity intention through feedback engagement (β = .21, 95% CI [.15, .28]). The total effect was also significant (β = .45, 95% CI [.37, .53]). The model accounted for 31% of",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,11,"a significant indirect association of GAA-LS scores with academic integrity intention through feedback engagement (β = .21, 95% CI [.15, .28]). The total effect was also significant (β = .45, 95% CI [.37, .53]). The model accounted for 31% of the variance in feedback engagement and 36% of the variance in academic integrity intention. Given the cross-sectional nature of the data, these findings are interpreted as evidence of indirect association rather than causal mediation. Additional diagnostic checks did not indicate that the findings were driven by a single method factor. The first unrotated factor accounted for 29.41% of variance, the one-factor CFA showed poor fit, and the common latent factor sensitivity check changed the main structural paths by less than .04. These results are reported as robustness evidence rather than as definitive proof that common-method bias was absent. The standardized structural model is presented in Figure 4, and the corresponding standardized path coefficients, bootstrap confidence intervals, and explained variance are reported in Table 8. TABLE 7 Criterion-related and known-group validity. GAA-LS variable/comparison Feedback engagement Academic integrity intention Responsible AI use",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,11,"structural model is presented in Figure 4, and the corresponding standardized path coefficients, bootstrap confidence intervals, and explained variance are reported in Table 8. TABLE 7 Criterion-related and known-group validity. GAA-LS variable/comparison Feedback engagement Academic integrity intention Responsible AI use intention ACA .44*** .39*** .46*** AIAJ .49*** .43*** .51*** VEC .53*** .47*** .56*** EAAI .41*** .55*** .50*** FURL .58*** .46*** .53*** GAA-LS total .60*** .51*** .59*** High AI-use vs. low AI-use Mean difference = .34 [.26,.42] t = 8.72*** d = .62 Prior AI training vs. no training Mean difference = .19 [.13,.25] t = 5.91*** d = .34 Disclosure rules vs. no rules Mean difference = .18 [.12,.24] t = 5.42*** d = .32 Values in the first six rows are Pearson correlations. Known-group comparisons report mean differences with 95% confidence intervals, t values, and Cohen’s d. ***p < .001. FIGURE 4 Structural model linking GAA-LS, feedback engagement, and academic integrity intention. Standardized path coefficients are shown. The indirect association through feedback engagement was significant, β = .21, 95% CI [.15, .28]. The model explained 31% of the variance",180
