DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,12,"5 Discussion 5.1 Principal findings This study developed and validated the GAA-LS as a multidimensional measure of higher education students’ GenAI assessment literacy. The results supported an 18-item, five-factor structure. The five dimensions reflect assessment criteria awareness, AI-task appropriateness judgment, verification and evidence checking, ethical attribution and academic integrity, and feedback uptake and revision literacy. The scale showed satisfactory to strong reliability, convergent validity, discriminant validity, criterion-related validity, and known-group validity. Measurement invariance was supported across gender, discipline, and AI-use frequency groups, suggesting that the scale can be used to compare these student groups with greater confidence. The positive associations with feedback engagement and academic integrity intention further support the applied value of the scale. 5.2 Theoretical contribution Conceptually, the study extends assessment literacy research by specifying what assessment literacy means when GenAI becomes part of assignment interpretation, feedback use, evidence checking, and disclosure decisions. The revised theoretical position is intentionally integrative rather than substitutional: the GAA-LS does not claim that GenAI assessment literacy is wholly independent of AI literacy, assessment literacy, feedback literacy, or academic integrity literacy. Instead, it",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,12,"evidence checking, and disclosure decisions. The revised theoretical position is intentionally integrative rather than substitutional: the GAA-LS does not claim that GenAI assessment literacy is wholly independent of AI literacy, assessment literacy, feedback literacy, or academic integrity literacy. Instead, it identifies the assessment-specific junction at which these literacies are jointly activated. In this setting, students must interpret criteria, determine whether AI support is permissible, evaluate AI-generated evidence, disclose assistance when required, and use feedback without surrendering authorship of the final work. The five dimensions describe a set of interrelated assessment- specific judgments and practices. Students need to understand criteria and task boundaries, check the accuracy and evidential quality of AI output, decide whether and how AI support should be acknowledged, and use feedback for revision without giving up authorship of the final work. This structure explains why efficient tool use alone is not sufficient for responsible participation in AI-supported assessment. The positive association with feedback engagement also gives the construct practical meaning. Students with stronger GenAI assessment literacy appeared more likely to treat feedback as material for interpretation and revision",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,12,"alone is not sufficient for responsible participation in AI-supported assessment. The positive association with feedback engagement also gives the construct practical meaning. Students with stronger GenAI assessment literacy appeared more likely to treat feedback as material for interpretation and revision rather than as an automatic answer. This pattern is consistent with self-regulated learning and evaluative judgment accounts, where criteria interpretation, monitoring, and action on feedback are central to responsible learning behavior. In this sense, the scale connects assessment literacy, feedback literacy, and academic integrity within the same measurement framework, while the cross- sectional design limits the interpretation to associations rather than developmental or causal pathways. 5.3 Methodological contribution Methodologically, the study followed the standards expected of applied measurement research. It included expert content validation, pilot testing, item analysis, exploratory factor analysis, confirmatory factor analysis, model comparison, reliability testing, convergent and discriminant validity, measurement invariance, criterion-related validity, known-group validity, and indirect association testing. This combination is consistent with Frontiers-style psychometric validation studies in education and psychology (Angco et al., 2026; Evans and Zhu, 2023; Roca et al., 2026). Measurement invariance is",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,12,"discriminant validity, measurement invariance, criterion-related validity, known-group validity, and indirect association testing. This combination is consistent with Frontiers-style psychometric validation studies in education and psychology (Angco et al., 2026; Evans and Zhu, 2023; Roca et al., 2026). Measurement invariance is a particularly important contribution. Because GenAI experience, academic expectations, and disciplinary assessment cultures may differ across groups, a scale intended for broad higher education use should demonstrate that it measures the same construct in comparable ways. This is especially important for ordinal survey scales and applied measurement contexts where group comparisons may otherwise be misleading (Baghaei et al., 2026; Vandenberg and Lance, 2000; Wu and Estabrook, 2016). 5.4 Practical implications For instructors, the GAA-LS can help identify where students need support when AI is allowed or discussed in assessment. A class may be comfortable using AI feedback but less clear about disclosure rules, evidence checking, or the limits of acceptable assistance. The five subscales can therefore inform targeted teaching rather than broad warnings about AI use. TABLE 8 Structural model and mediation results. Path β SE p 95% CI GAA-LS",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,12,"about disclosure rules, evidence checking, or the limits of acceptable assistance. The five subscales can therefore inform targeted teaching rather than broad warnings about AI use. TABLE 8 Structural model and mediation results. Path β SE p 95% CI GAA-LS -> Feedback engagement .56 .04 <.001 [.48,.63] Feedback engagement -> Academic integrity intention .37 .05 <.001 [.28,.46] GAA-LS -> Academic integrity intention .24 .05 <.001 [.15,.34] Indirect effect .21 .03 <.001 [.15,.28] Total effect .45 .04 <.001 [.37,.53] Model fit CFI = .955 TLI = .948 RMSEA = .040 SRMR = .044 Standardized coefficients are reported. Confidence intervals are based on 5,000 bootstrap samples. Because the design was cross-sectional, mediation results are interpreted as indirect associations rather than causal effects. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",129
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,13,"For course design, the scale points to elements that should be made visible in assessment briefs: criteria, permitted and prohibited AI support, expectations for checking outputs, rules for acknowledging assistance, and ways to use feedback in revision. These elements can make AI-related assessment expectations more transparent to students. For institutions, the instrument offers a way to evaluate whether policy messages are becoming usable student knowledge. It may be particularly useful when universities introduce AI disclosure rules or redesign assessment tasks, because it measures the capabilities students need in order to follow such guidance responsibly. 5.5 Limitations and future research Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the design was cross-sectional and self-reported, so causal claims cannot be made. Although the structural model tested indirect associations, longitudinal and experimental studies are needed to examine whether GenAI assessment literacy predicts later feedback behavior, disclosure practices, and assessment outcomes. Second, self-report scales may be affected by social desirability and common-method variance. Although Harman’s single-factor test, the one-factor CFA comparison, and the common latent factor sensitivity check did not indicate a dominant common- method factor,",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,13,"disclosure practices, and assessment outcomes. Second, self-report scales may be affected by social desirability and common-method variance. Although Harman’s single-factor test, the one-factor CFA comparison, and the common latent factor sensitivity check did not indicate a dominant common- method factor, these procedures cannot fully rule out common- method bias (Podsakoff et al., 2003). Third, academic integrity intention may not fully predict actual behavior. In addition, the ethical attribution and academic integrity dimension of the GAA-LS is conceptually close to academic integrity intention, so the corresponding criterion-related correlations should be interpreted as theoretically proximal validity evidence rather than as evidence of complete construct separation. Future research should combine self-report data with behavioral indicators, such as revision logs, AI-use disclosure statements, rubric-aligned performance evidence, or trace data from AI-supported feedback activities. Such evidence would strengthen the ecological validity of the scale. Fourth, although the sample was recruited from multiple higher education institutions, it was limited to one national higher education context and was not designed to be nationally representative. The scale may require adaptation for secondary education, professional training, or international contexts.",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,13,"although the sample was recruited from multiple higher education institutions, it was limited to one national higher education context and was not designed to be nationally representative. The scale may require adaptation for secondary education, professional training, or international contexts. Cross-cultural validation is especially important because AI-use rules, assessment traditions, and disclosure expectations vary across systems (Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency, 2024; UNESCO, 2023). Future research should examine measurement invariance across languages, institutional contexts, and assessment cultures, and should also test invariance across clearly separated undergraduate and postgraduate samples when adequate subgroup sizes are available. Fifth, this validation study did not include established AI literacy or assessment literacy measures and therefore could not test whether the GAA-LS provides incremental validity beyond those adjacent constructs. Although the present findings support reliability, construct validity, measurement invariance, and criterion-related associations, they do not establish that GAA-LS scores explain additional variance beyond existing AI literacy or assessment literacy instruments. Future work should treat incremental validity as an important next step, alongside examinations of test-retest reliability, predictive validity, sensitivity to intervention, and item-level functioning",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,13,"not establish that GAA-LS scores explain additional variance beyond existing AI literacy or assessment literacy instruments. Future work should treat incremental validity as an important next step, alongside examinations of test-retest reliability, predictive validity, sensitivity to intervention, and item-level functioning using item response theory or differential item functioning analysis. 6 Conclusion The GAA-LS offers a focused measure of how higher education students understand and regulate GenAI use in assessment contexts. Its five dimensions specify the assessment- specific junction between general AI literacy, assessment literacy, feedback literacy, and academic integrity. The validation results support its use as a research and diagnostic tool for AI- supported higher education assessment, while future work should test its incremental, longitudinal, cross-cultural, and behavioral validity across languages, institutions, and assessment settings. Data availability statement The raw data supporting the conclusions of this article will be made available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the institutional ethics review body of Xinjiang University of Political Science and Law, Tumxuk, Xinjiang, China. The studies were conducted in accordance with the",180
DOC_013,feduc-11-1934632.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632,13,"available by the authors, without undue reservation. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the institutional ethics review body of Xinjiang University of Political Science and Law, Tumxuk, Xinjiang, China. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. Participation was voluntary and anonymous, and the participants provided electronic informed consent before taking part in the study. Author contributions JN: Data curation, Investigation, Project administration, Writing – review & editing. ZZ: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Methodology, Project administration, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. XL: Data curation, Supervision, Validation, Writing – review & editing. YZ: Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Writing – review & editing. MZ: Methodology, Validation, Writing – review & editing. Funding The author(s) declared that financial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Nie et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1934632 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",147
