DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,1,"Frontiers in Psychology 01 frontiersin.org TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 28 October 2026 DOI 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 OPEN ACCESS EDITED BY Daniel H. Robinson, The University of Texas at Arlington College of Education, United States REVIEWED BY Anthony Bloxham, Nottingham Trent University, United Kingdom Sara D. Sony, Northwest Missouri State University, United States *CORRESPONDENCE Peng Dong dongpeng.2022@tsinghua.org.cn RECEIVED 15 September 2026 REVISED 25 September 2026 ACCEPTED 27 September 2026 PUBLISHED 28 October 2026 CITATION Guo Y and Dong P (2026) University students’ overreliance on generative AI feedback in the learning process: the effects of perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and metacognitive monitoring. Front. Psychol. 17:1998976. doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Guo and Dong. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. University students’ overreliance",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,1,"author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. University students’ overreliance on generative AI feedback in the learning process: the effects of perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and metacognitive monitoring Yuanyuan Guo 1 and Peng Dong 2* 1School of Law, Ningbo University, Ningbo, China, 2Tsinghua University, Beijing, China Generative artificial intelligence (GAI) has introduced an important question in education, when AI provides immediate and reliable feedback, do students use it as a learning support tool or gradually rely on it as a substitute for their own cognitive efforts? Drawing on Cognitive Load Theory, Cognitive Offloading Theory, and Metacognitive Monitoring Theory, this study develops a theoretical model to explain the formation process of undergraduate students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. Specifically, this study examines the relationships among per­ ceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and overreliance on GAI feedback, while further exploring how metacognitive monitoring levels shape this process. A questionnaire survey was conducted",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,1,"of undergraduate students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. Specifically, this study examines the relationships among per­ ceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and overreliance on GAI feedback, while further exploring how metacognitive monitoring levels shape this process. A questionnaire survey was conducted among 600 undergraduate students, and structural equation modeling was performed using AMOS 31.0. The results showed that perceived cognitive load significantly and positively predicted cogni­ tive offloading, which further contributed to students’ overreliance on GAI feed­ back. Meanwhile, perceived cognitive load also directly predicted overreliance on GAI feedback. Bootstrap analysis further confirmed that cognitive offloading mediated the relationship between perceived cognitive load and overreliance on GAI feedback. In addition, this study established separate structural equation models for students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring and found distinct patterns in the formation of GAI feedback overreliance. Specifically, stu­ dents with lower metacognitive monitoring were more likely to be influenced by cognitive pressure during learning, showing a more prominent direct relationship between perceived cognitive load and GAI feedback overreliance. This finding suggests that these students tend to rely more directly on GAI",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,1,"metacognitive monitoring were more likely to be influenced by cognitive pressure during learning, showing a more prominent direct relationship between perceived cognitive load and GAI feedback overreliance. This finding suggests that these students tend to rely more directly on GAI feedback when facing higher cognitive demands. In contrast, students with higher metacognitive monitoring demonstrated a more evident cognitive regulation process, in which cognitive offloading played a more important role in linking perceived cogni­ tive load with GAI feedback overreliance. This indicates that these students may actively adjust their cognitive resource allocation when using GAI, although con­ tinuous cognitive offloading may still increase their dependence on AI-generated feedback. KEYWORDS cognitive load, cognitive offloading, cognitive regulation, educational psychology, metacognitive monitoring, digital society",120
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 02 frontiersin.org 1 Introduction University learning requires students not only to acquire knowl­ edge but also to regulate cognitive resources, evaluate information quality, and develop independent problem-solving abilities. With the rapid integration of generative artificial intelligence (GAI) into higher education, the way students obtain learning support has changed sig­ nificantly. Unlike traditional digital learning tools that mainly provide information retrieval functions, GAI can generate explanations, feed­ back, examples, and suggestions based on students’ needs, offering continuous support similar to intelligent tutoring systems (Yan et al., 2024; Law, 2024). In tasks such as academic writing, knowledge understanding, problem solving, and learning feedback, GAI shows the potential to improve learning efficiency and support personalized learning (Iqbal et al., 2025; Evmenova et al., 2024). Therefore, the role of GAI in education has become an important topic in academic discussions. On the one hand, appropriate use of generative AI tools, such as ChatGPT, can support knowledge exploration, improve learning effi­ ciency, and promote the development of critical thinking (Guo and Lee, 2023; Suriano et al., 2025). By",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"in academic discussions. On the one hand, appropriate use of generative AI tools, such as ChatGPT, can support knowledge exploration, improve learning effi­ ciency, and promote the development of critical thinking (Guo and Lee, 2023; Suriano et al., 2025). By providing immediate explanations and continuous support, GAI helps students overcome limitations of classroom time and available resources and obtain more flexible learn­ ing assistance (Tlili et al., 2023; Ng et al., 2025). On the other hand, GAI may also change how students engage with feedback and process knowledge. Feedback serves as an important mechanism in learning because it helps students identify gaps in understanding, adjust learn­ ing strategies, and develop knowledge. However, feedback only improves learning when students understand, evaluate, and apply the provided information (Shute, 2008). In GAI-supported learning envi­ ronments, automated feedback allows students to quickly obtain explanations and suggestions, but it may also reduce their opportuni­ ties to analyze, evaluate, and verify feedback actively. As a result, some students may accept AI-generated content without sufficient judgment (Yan et al., 2025; Bauer et al., 2025). When students rely",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"and suggestions, but it may also reduce their opportuni­ ties to analyze, evaluate, and verify feedback actively. As a result, some students may accept AI-generated content without sufficient judgment (Yan et al., 2025; Bauer et al., 2025). When students rely excessively on AI-generated answers and feedback, they may reduce information searching, analytical thinking, and evaluation processes, which may weaken their monitoring of understanding and affect the development of critical thinking, independent judgment, and problem-solving abilities (Fan et al., 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). These contrasting possibilities suggest that the influence of GAI on learning outcomes depends not only on the availability of AI tools but also on how students cognitively engage with, evaluate, and regu­ late AI-generated information. Existing studies suggest that technol­ ogy dependence does not simply result from tool availability but is influenced by students’ cognitive demands, task pressure, trust in technology, and self-regulation abilities (Zhang et al., 2024; Mariñas et al., 2025). When students face difficult learning tasks, limited cogni­ tive resources may encourage them to seek external support that reduces mental effort. Because GAI feedback can",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"pressure, trust in technology, and self-regulation abilities (Zhang et al., 2024; Mariñas et al., 2025). When students face difficult learning tasks, limited cogni­ tive resources may encourage them to seek external support that reduces mental effort. Because GAI feedback can quickly provide structured responses and explanations, students may view it as an effi­ cient cognitive resource (De Smedt et al., 2018; Butterfuss and Kendeou, 2018). Therefore, dependence on GAI feedback may not only reflect students’ preference for technology but also relate to how they manage cognitive resources during learning. Therefore, under­ standing students’ cognitive regulation processes is critical for explain­ ing not only why some students develop overreliance on GAI feedback but also why the learning benefits of GenAI may vary across individu­ als and learning contexts. Recent evidence suggests that the educational benefits of GenAI should be interpreted cautiously, as the current literature on AI-supported learning may be subject to publication bias, with posi­ tive effects being more likely to be reported than null or negative find­ ings. Beyond the availability of AI tools, the learning value of GenAI",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"as the current literature on AI-supported learning may be subject to publication bias, with posi­ tive effects being more likely to be reported than null or negative find­ ings. Beyond the availability of AI tools, the learning value of GenAI depends on how students cognitively engage with, evaluate, and regu­ late AI-generated information. Although GenAI can provide immedi­ ate and personalized feedback, students need sufficient feedback literacy to interpret and apply such feedback effectively (Hawkins et al., 2025). Moreover, the contribution of GenAI to higher-order cogni­ tive development appears to depend on its integration with appropri­ ate pedagogical approaches rather than AI use alone (Li et al., 2026). Importantly, increased efficiency in completing learning tasks does not necessarily translate into improved learning outcomes, as students may complete tasks faster without achieving deeper cognitive process­ ing (Molerov et al., 2026). Therefore, examining how students regulate their use of GAI feedback during learning is important for explaining why AI may support learning for some students but lead to overreli­ ance for others. One important context in which this issue becomes particularly relevant",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"examining how students regulate their use of GAI feedback during learning is important for explaining why AI may support learning for some students but lead to overreli­ ance for others. One important context in which this issue becomes particularly relevant is university education, where students frequently encounter complex tasks requiring substantial cognitive resources (Sweller et al., 2019a,b; Risko and Gilbert, 2016). This cognitive regulation issue becomes particularly important in university education because stu­ dents frequently encounter complex tasks, including academic writ­ ing, theoretical analysis, research design, and interdisciplinary problem solving. These tasks require students to process large amounts of information and engage in higher-level cognitive activities. Complex learning tasks can increase information-processing demands and influence students’ learning strategies and performance (De Smedt et al., 2018; Butterfuss and Kendeou, 2018). When students experience high cognitive demands, they may adopt strategies that reduce immediate mental effort, including using external tools to sup­ port or replace part of the cognitive work required for task completion (Sweller et al., 2019a,b; Risko and Gilbert, 2016). This tendency is also relevant in digital learning environments, where",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"reduce immediate mental effort, including using external tools to sup­ port or replace part of the cognitive work required for task completion (Sweller et al., 2019a,b; Risko and Gilbert, 2016). This tendency is also relevant in digital learning environments, where easy access to exter­ nal information sources may reduce individuals’ reliance on internal cognitive resources (Sparrow et al., 2011). In GAI-supported learning contexts, a similar process may occur, as students may transfer parts of their understanding, judgment, and feedback evaluation processes to AI systems (Sparrow et al., 2011). However, the extent to which such cognitive transfer leads to pro­ ductive AI use or overreliance may depend on students’ ability to regulate their own learning processes. Students who can effectively monitor their understanding and evaluate external information may use GAI as a supportive resource, whereas insufficient regulation may increase dependence on AI-generated feedback. This highlights the importance of examining the cognitive mechanisms that shape stu­ dents’ interaction with GAI. Despite increasing research interest in GAI-supported learning, several important issues remain unresolved. First, previous studies have mainly examined students’ attitudes toward AI,",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,2,"AI-generated feedback. This highlights the importance of examining the cognitive mechanisms that shape stu­ dents’ interaction with GAI. Despite increasing research interest in GAI-supported learning, several important issues remain unresolved. First, previous studies have mainly examined students’ attitudes toward AI, technology acceptance, or academic outcomes, while fewer studies have investigated the cognitive mechanisms that lead students to over-rely on AI feedback during learning activities (Klarin et al., 2024; Mariñas et al., 2025). Second, existing research on cognitive offloading has largely focused on traditional external tools, such as reminders, memory aids, and information systems. However, GAI represents a fundamentally different type of cognitive support because it can generate explanations, evaluate responses, and provide",112
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 03 frontiersin.org recommendations that directly influence learning decisions (Peng and Yeh, 2025; Seung and Basham, 2026). Third, limited attention has been given to the role of learner characteristics in shaping the relationship between cognitive demands, AI use, and dependence patterns (Yan et al., 2025; Bauer et al., 2025). This study contributes to research on AI-supported learning by shifting attention from whether students use GAI to how they cogni­ tively engage with AI-generated feedback. The present study focuses on university students and examines the relationships among per­ ceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and overreliance on GAI feedback during learning. Specifically, this study investigates whether perceived cognitive load is associated with students’ tendency to rely on GAI feedback through cognitive offloading and further compares the structural relationships among these variables across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. By grouping students according to their metacognitive monitoring levels, this study aims to clarify whether students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring exhibit different patterns in managing cognitive resources when inter­ acting with GAI. 2 Literature review 2.1 Theoretical",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"monitoring. By grouping students according to their metacognitive monitoring levels, this study aims to clarify whether students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring exhibit different patterns in managing cognitive resources when inter­ acting with GAI. 2 Literature review 2.1 Theoretical foundation This study adopts Cognitive Load Theory and Cognitive Offloading Theory as its theoretical foundations to explain university students’ overreliance on generative artificial intelligence (GAI) feed­ back. The integration of these two perspectives provides a more com­ prehensive explanation of students’ interactions with GAI during learning because neither theory alone can fully explain why students turn to AI feedback and how supportive AI use may gradually develop into excessive dependence. Cognitive Load Theory explains the cogni­ tive demands that motivate students to seek external support, whereas Cognitive Offloading Theory explains the process through which stu­ dents transfer cognitive activities to external tools. Together, these perspectives clarify the cognitive pathway from experiencing learning difficulties to relying on GAI feedback. Cognitive Load Theory provides the initial explanation for why students may seek assistance from GAI during learning. The theory proposes that human",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"external tools. Together, these perspectives clarify the cognitive pathway from experiencing learning difficulties to relying on GAI feedback. Cognitive Load Theory provides the initial explanation for why students may seek assistance from GAI during learning. The theory proposes that human working memory has limited capacity, and learning becomes more difficult when information processing demands exceed available cognitive resources (Sweller et al., 2019a,b). In university learning environments, students often encounter com­ plex tasks that require simultaneous information processing, reason­ ing, evaluation, and knowledge integration, such as academic writing, theoretical analysis, and problem solving. These tasks may increase students’ perceived cognitive demands and influence how they allo­ cate limited cognitive resources. When students experience excessive cognitive burden, they may search for strategies that reduce unneces­ sary mental effort and support task completion. However, Cognitive Load Theory alone cannot explain why students choose GAI rather than other forms of learning support, nor can it explain why reducing cognitive effort sometimes promotes learning while sometimes leads to dependence. The theory mainly focuses on the relationship between cognitive demands and learning efficiency, but it does",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"GAI rather than other forms of learning support, nor can it explain why reducing cognitive effort sometimes promotes learning while sometimes leads to dependence. The theory mainly focuses on the relationship between cognitive demands and learning efficiency, but it does not sufficiently explain how learners transfer cognitive activities to external resources. Therefore, another theoretical perspective is needed to understand the mechanism through which students use GAI feedback as an external cognitive support. Cognitive Offloading Theory addresses this limitation by explain­ ing how individuals reduce internal cognitive demands through exter­ nal tools and environmental resources (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). Cognitive offloading represents a common human strategy for man­ aging limited cognitive capacity. Individuals do not always complete cognitive tasks internally; instead, they strategically use external resources to store information, reduce memory demands, or improve task performance. Previous research has shown that cognitive offload­ ing can be beneficial when external tools support learning activities and allow individuals to devote more resources to higher-level think­ ing (Gilbert et al., 2020; Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022). The emer­ gence of GAI, however, has expanded the",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"cognitive offload­ ing can be beneficial when external tools support learning activities and allow individuals to devote more resources to higher-level think­ ing (Gilbert et al., 2020; Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022). The emer­ gence of GAI, however, has expanded the meaning of cognitive offloading in educational contexts. Unlike traditional external tools that mainly assist memory, organization, or information retrieval, GAI can generate explanations, evaluate responses, provide suggestions, and produce solutions. Therefore, GAI may support not only periph­ eral cognitive activities but also core learning processes, including understanding, reasoning, and evaluation (Yan et al., 2024; Seung and Basham, 2026). This unique characteristic creates a possibility that students may move beyond appropriate cognitive support and gradu­ ally transfer essential cognitive responsibilities to AI systems. Although Cognitive Offloading Theory explains how students transfer cognitive activities to GAI, it cannot independently explain why students decide to initiate such transfer. Cognitive offloading is not simply a result of tool availability; it often occurs when individuals perceive that internal cognitive effort is costly or insufficient for com­ pleting a task. Therefore, Cognitive Load Theory and Cognitive",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"decide to initiate such transfer. Cognitive offloading is not simply a result of tool availability; it often occurs when individuals perceive that internal cognitive effort is costly or insufficient for com­ pleting a task. Therefore, Cognitive Load Theory and Cognitive Offloading Theory address different but complementary aspects of GAI-supported learning. Cognitive Load Theory explains the psycho­ logical conditions that increase students’ motivation to reduce cogni­ tive demands, while Cognitive Offloading Theory explains the behavioral process through which students use external tools to achieve this reduction. The combination of these two theories is par­ ticularly valuable for understanding overreliance on GAI feedback. If only Cognitive Load Theory is considered, students’ use of GAI may be interpreted merely as a reasonable response to increased task dif­ ficulty, without explaining why some students become excessively dependent on AI-generated feedback. If only Cognitive Offloading Theory is considered, the theory can describe students’ transfer of cognitive activities but cannot fully explain why cognitive demands trigger such behavior. By integrating both perspectives, this study views GAI feedback overreliance as a cognitive process in which per­ ceived",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,3,"Theory is considered, the theory can describe students’ transfer of cognitive activities but cannot fully explain why cognitive demands trigger such behavior. By integrating both perspectives, this study views GAI feedback overreliance as a cognitive process in which per­ ceived cognitive demands encourage students to reduce mental effort through external support, and this support may become problematic when students increasingly rely on AI to perform essential cognitive activities. Therefore, this study integrates Cognitive Load Theory and Cognitive Offloading Theory to develop the Cognitive Resource Management Framework for GAI Feedback Overreliance, which explains how university students gradually shift from appropriate use of generative artificial intelligence (GAI) feedback to excessive depen­ dence on such feedback. The framework argues that overreliance on GAI feedback does not result directly from the availability of AI tools. Instead, it emerges from the interaction among students’ cognitive demands, their strategies for managing cognitive resources, and their increasing reliance on external intelligent support during learning (Figure 1).",159
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,4,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 04 frontiersin.org Based on this framework, the formation of GAI feedback overreli­ ance involves three connected cognitive processes, the emergence of cognitive demands, the redistribution of cognitive activities, and the increasing dependence on external cognitive support. First, when uni­ versity students encounter complex learning tasks, higher cognitive demands may increase their need to reduce mental effort and seek external assistance. Therefore, students’ perceived cognitive pressure during learning represents an important starting point for explaining their subsequent behavioral choices. Second, when students attempt to manage such cognitive pressure, they may transfer part of their learning-related cognitive activities to GAI, forming cognitive offload­ ing behaviors. Unlike traditional learning tools, GAI can generate explanations, feedback, and answers directly, which allows cognitive offloading to extend beyond simple assistance and involve essential learning processes that normally require students’ active engagement. Third, when students repeatedly rely on GAI to interpret feedback and make learning decisions, external cognitive support may gradually replace internal cognitive processing, resulting in overreliance on GAI feedback. Following this cognitive process, this study does not regard",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,4,"active engagement. Third, when students repeatedly rely on GAI to interpret feedback and make learning decisions, external cognitive support may gradually replace internal cognitive processing, resulting in overreliance on GAI feedback. Following this cognitive process, this study does not regard GAI feedback overreliance as a simple outcome of technology use. Instead, it considers overreliance as the final expression of how students manage cognitive resources during learning. Accordingly, this study focuses first on students’ perceived cognitive demands, namely per­ ceived cognitive load, to explain why students seek external cognitive support. It then examines cognitive offloading as the process through which students transfer part of their learning-related cognitive activities to GAI. Finally, it investigates whether this cognitive transfer develops into overreliance on GAI feedback, reflected in students’ reduced engagement in independent analysis, evaluation, and reflection. However, this integrated framework mainly explains the common cognitive pathway through which students may develop dependence on GAI feedback. It cannot fully explain why students with similar cognitive demands and similar access to AI tools may show different levels of dependence. Students’ responses to GAI feedback also",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,4,"common cognitive pathway through which students may develop dependence on GAI feedback. It cannot fully explain why students with similar cognitive demands and similar access to AI tools may show different levels of dependence. Students’ responses to GAI feedback also depend on whether they can monitor their understanding, evaluate external information, and adjust their learning strategies. Therefore, this study introduces metacognitive monitoring as a grouping variable to examine differences among university students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Students with stronger metacognitive monitoring may treat GAI feedback as supplementary learning infor­ mation and engage in verification and reflection. In contrast, students with weaker metacognitive monitoring may accept AI feedback more directly and become more likely to rely excessively on AI-generated suggestions. Therefore, the proposed framework explains how university stu­ dents develop overreliance on GAI feedback through the process of increased cognitive demands, cognitive activity transfer, and insuffi­ cient self-monitoring. It suggests that using GAI does not inevitably lead to dependence. Instead, overreliance may occur when students experience greater cognitive pressure, increasingly transfer learning- related cognitive activities to AI, and fail",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,4,"cognitive demands, cognitive activity transfer, and insuffi­ cient self-monitoring. It suggests that using GAI does not inevitably lead to dependence. Instead, overreliance may occur when students experience greater cognitive pressure, increasingly transfer learning- related cognitive activities to AI, and fail to maintain effective moni­ toring of their own learning processes. This framework provides the FIGURE 1 Cognitive resource management framework for GAI feedback overreliance.",64
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 05 frontiersin.org basis for examining the relationships among perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, GAI feedback overreliance, and differences across levels of metacognitive monitoring. 2.2 The effect of perceived cognitive load on overreliance on GAI feedback Perceived cognitive load may directly influence university stu­ dents’ overreliance on GAI feedback because students’ perceptions of cognitive demands can affect how they respond to learning difficulties and allocate cognitive effort. When students experience greater cogni­ tive pressure during learning tasks, they may find it more difficult to maintain sufficient attention, evaluation, and problem-solving effort. In such situations, GAI feedback provides an easily accessible source of explanations and solutions, which may reduce students’ willingness to complete learning-related judgments independently. Therefore, perceived cognitive load may increase students’ tendency to rely excessively on GAI feedback during learning. First, higher perceived cognitive load may reduce students’ capac­ ity to independently process and evaluate learning information. University learning requires students to integrate complex informa­ tion, construct understanding, and make judgments across different academic tasks. When students perceive that task demands exceed their",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"cognitive load may reduce students’ capac­ ity to independently process and evaluate learning information. University learning requires students to integrate complex informa­ tion, construct understanding, and make judgments across different academic tasks. When students perceive that task demands exceed their available cognitive resources, they may experience greater diffi­ culty maintaining active cognitive engagement (De Smedt et al., 2018; Butterfuss and Kendeou, 2018). Under such conditions, students may prefer external support that can simplify information processing and reduce immediate mental demands. Because GAI can rapidly provide organized explanations, summaries, and feedback, students experienc­ ing high cognitive load may be more likely to accept AI-generated information rather than invest additional effort in independent analysis. Second, higher perceived cognitive load may increase students’ reliance on GAI feedback by changing their evaluation of the useful­ ness of external assistance. Cognitive resources are limited, and stu­ dents need to decide how much effort they should invest in processing information during learning. Research on cognitive offloading sug­ gests that individuals tend to use external tools when these tools can reduce cognitive demands and support task completion",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"stu­ dents need to decide how much effort they should invest in processing information during learning. Research on cognitive offloading sug­ gests that individuals tend to use external tools when these tools can reduce cognitive demands and support task completion (Risko and Gilbert, 2016; Gilbert, 2024). Compared with traditional learning tools, GAI can provide direct responses, explanations, and recommen­ dations, making it a particularly attractive option when students expe­ rience cognitive pressure (Yan et al., 2024; Law, 2024). As students increasingly perceive GAI feedback as an efficient way to overcome learning difficulties, they may gradually reduce their reliance on per­ sonal reasoning and increase dependence on AI-generated suggestions. Third, perceived cognitive load may weaken students’ motivation to verify and critically examine GAI feedback. Effective learning requires students to actively interpret feedback, identify possible prob­ lems, and determine whether external information matches their own understanding (Shute, 2008). However, students who experience high cognitive demands may focus more on completing tasks than on care­ fully evaluating feedback quality. Since GAI can generate fluent and coherent responses, students may perceive AI feedback as",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"matches their own understanding (Shute, 2008). However, students who experience high cognitive demands may focus more on completing tasks than on care­ fully evaluating feedback quality. Since GAI can generate fluent and coherent responses, students may perceive AI feedback as sufficiently reliable and reduce further checking behaviors (Athaluri et al., 2023; Lee et al., 2023). Previous studies have suggested that excessive reli­ ance on AI systems may occur when students accept AI outputs with­ out adequate evaluation, which may weaken independent judgment and critical thinking during learning (Fan et al., 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). Fourth, perceived cognitive load may make students more vulner­ able to the convenience of GAI feedback. GAI differs from traditional information tools because it can participate in multiple stages of learn­ ing, including explanation, reasoning, and suggestion generation (Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022; Seung and Basham, 2026). When students experience learning pressure, this high level of support may encourage them to view GAI not only as a learning assistant but also as a substitute for parts of their own cognitive work. Although appro­ priate use of",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"2026). When students experience learning pressure, this high level of support may encourage them to view GAI not only as a learning assistant but also as a substitute for parts of their own cognitive work. Although appro­ priate use of GAI can support learning, excessive reliance may occur when students repeatedly use AI feedback to avoid cognitive effort and reduce independent consideration of learning problems (Gerlich, 2025; Yan et al., 2025). Overall, perceived cognitive load may directly contribute to uni­ versity students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. Higher cognitive demands may increase students’ need for immediate external support, reduce their willingness to independently evaluate information, and strengthen their dependence on AI-generated suggestions. Therefore, perceived cognitive load may represent an important cognitive condi­ tion that shapes how students interact with GAI feedback during learning. Based on this reasoning, the following hypothesis is proposed: H1: Perceived Cognitive Load positively influences university students’ Overreliance on GAI Feedback. 2.3 The effect of cognitive offloading on overreliance on GAI feedback Cognitive offloading provides an important perspective for understanding how students manage cognitive demands during GAI- supported",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"proposed: H1: Perceived Cognitive Load positively influences university students’ Overreliance on GAI Feedback. 2.3 The effect of cognitive offloading on overreliance on GAI feedback Cognitive offloading provides an important perspective for understanding how students manage cognitive demands during GAI- supported learning. Research shows that individuals do not always rely on internal cognitive abilities to complete tasks. Instead, they actively use external tools to reduce cognitive demands and improve task performance (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). Appropriate cognitive offloading allows learners to save limited cognitive resources and devote more attention to complex analysis and creative activities (Gilbert et al., 2020; Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022). For example, students may use digital tools to record information, organize learning materials, or obtain learning prompts, which can improve efficiency and support task completion. However, generative AI differs from traditional cognitive tools because it can directly produce explana­ tions, summaries, reasoning processes, and answers. As a result, GAI may not only support learning activities but also replace cognitive processes that students originally needed to complete themselves (Yan et al., 2024; Seung and Basham, 2026). Therefore, cognitive offloading",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,5,"explana­ tions, summaries, reasoning processes, and answers. As a result, GAI may not only support learning activities but also replace cognitive processes that students originally needed to complete themselves (Yan et al., 2024; Seung and Basham, 2026). Therefore, cognitive offloading in GAI-supported learning has both beneficial and problematic consequences. On the positive side, appropriate use of cognitive offloading can reduce unnecessary mental effort and support effective learning. For example, Iqbal et al. (2025) found that generative AI tool use could improve academic achieve­ ment through shared metacognition and cognitive offloading among preservice teachers. However, when students fail to evaluate AI-generated information actively, cognitive offloading may gradually become dependence on AI feedback, causing students to reduce their own cognitive involvement. Seung and Basham (2026) argued that cognitive offloading in the age of generative AI is not only related to efficiency but also concerns whether learners maintain active",146
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 06 frontiersin.org participation in the learning process. Similarly, Peng and Yeh (2025) showed that trust in external tools influences cognitive offloading decisions, and individuals who perceive tools as highly reliable are more likely to reduce their internal cognitive effort. Cognitive offloading may represent an important psychological pathway through which university students develop overreliance on GAI feedback. Although external tools can support learning efficiency, students may gradually transfer essential cognitive activities, such as organizing information, understanding concepts, and evaluating solu­ tions, to GAI when they frequently rely on AI assistance. Therefore, cognitive offloading may not only help students manage learning tasks but also increase the possibility that they accept GAI feedback without sufficient independent processing. First, cognitive offloading may directly reduce students’ involve­ ment in cognitive activities that are essential for effective learning. Cognitive offloading refers to the process through which individuals use external tools to reduce internal cognitive demands and complete tasks more efficiently (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). In traditional learning contexts, students may use external supports to manage information or reduce unnecessary",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"offloading refers to the process through which individuals use external tools to reduce internal cognitive demands and complete tasks more efficiently (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). In traditional learning contexts, students may use external supports to manage information or reduce unnecessary mental effort. However, GAI differs from ordi­ nary cognitive tools because it can generate explanations, summaries, suggestions, and solutions rather than simply store or present infor­ mation (Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022). When university students use GAI to complete parts of understanding, information processing, or problem solving, they may gradually reduce their own cognitive engagement with learning materials. Second, cognitive offloading may increase students’ tendency to accept GAI feedback without careful evaluation. Effective learning requires students to examine information, compare new knowledge with existing understanding, and judge whether feedback fits the learning goal. However, when students transfer these evaluative activi­ ties to GAI, they may reduce their responsibility for checking and interpreting AI-generated information. Dunn and Risko (2016) sug­ gested that cognitive offloading involves decisions about whether indi­ viduals should invest effort internally or rely on external support. In the context",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"to GAI, they may reduce their responsibility for checking and interpreting AI-generated information. Dunn and Risko (2016) sug­ gested that cognitive offloading involves decisions about whether indi­ viduals should invest effort internally or rely on external support. In the context of GAI-supported learning, students who frequently choose external support may become less willing to perform indepen­ dent evaluation of AI feedback. Peng and Yeh (2025) further indicated that trust in external tools influences cognitive offloading decisions, suggesting that students who perceive AI systems as reliable may more easily reduce their own cognitive effort. Third, cognitive offloading may encourage students to treat GAI feedback as a substitute for personal judgment rather than as addi­ tional learning support. The educational value of GAI depends on whether students use AI outputs as resources for reflection or as direct answers that replace their own thinking processes (Yan et al., 2024). When students rely on GAI to generate explanations, identify key information, or provide problem-solving directions, they may obtain immediate task completion benefits but reduce opportunities for inde­ pendent reasoning. Seung and Basham (2026) emphasized",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"thinking processes (Yan et al., 2024). When students rely on GAI to generate explanations, identify key information, or provide problem-solving directions, they may obtain immediate task completion benefits but reduce opportunities for inde­ pendent reasoning. Seung and Basham (2026) emphasized that cogni­ tive offloading in the era of generative AI concerns not only efficiency but also whether learners maintain active cognitive participation. Similarly, Fan et al. (2025) argued that frequent use of generative AI may reduce students’ metacognitive engagement and encourage pas­ sive acceptance of AI-generated information. Fourth, cognitive offloading may strengthen overreliance on GAI feedback because it changes students’ learning habits. Repeated reli­ ance on external cognitive support can create a pattern in which stu­ dents increasingly expect AI assistance whenever they encounter learning difficulties. Gilbert (2024) explained that individuals select cognitive offloading strategies according to perceived effort and expected benefits. Since GAI can provide rapid responses with rela­ tively little effort from students, university students may gradually develop a preference for AI-supported decision making. This ten­ dency becomes more problematic when students use GAI not only to reduce",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"expected benefits. Since GAI can provide rapid responses with rela­ tively little effort from students, university students may gradually develop a preference for AI-supported decision making. This ten­ dency becomes more problematic when students use GAI not only to reduce unnecessary cognitive demands but also to avoid necessary learning processes, such as analysis, reflection, and verification (Gerlich, 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). Moreover, previous studies on AI-supported learning indicate that cognitive offloading has a dual influence on students’ learning behav­ iors. Appropriate use of GAI can help students organize information and manage complex tasks, which may support learning efficiency (Iqbal et al., 2025). However, excessive cognitive outsourcing may weaken students’ independent thinking and increase dependence on AI-generated suggestions (Yan et al., 2025). Therefore, the key issue is not whether students use GAI, but whether they transfer essential cognitive responsibilities to AI during learning activities. When cogni­ tive offloading exceeds a reasonable level, students may become increasingly dependent on GAI feedback and reduce their own evalu­ ation and judgment processes. Based on this reasoning, cognitive offloading may serve as a direct",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"AI during learning activities. When cogni­ tive offloading exceeds a reasonable level, students may become increasingly dependent on GAI feedback and reduce their own evalu­ ation and judgment processes. Based on this reasoning, cognitive offloading may serve as a direct psychological factor influencing uni­ versity students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. Students who fre­ quently transfer learning-related cognitive activities to GAI may become more likely to accept AI feedback without sufficient verifica­ tion, depend on AI suggestions when making learning decisions, and reduce independent thinking during problem solving. Therefore, this study proposes the following hypothesis: H2: Cognitive offloading positively influences university stu­ dents’ overreliance on GAI feedback. 2.4 The effect of perceived cognitive load on cognitive offloading Perceived cognitive load may influence university students’ cogni­ tive offloading during GAI-supported learning. When students encounter complex learning tasks, they need to allocate limited cogni­ tive resources to information processing, comprehension, and prob­ lem solving. If students perceive that task demands exceed their available cognitive capacity, they may seek external support to reduce immediate mental effort. Therefore, perceived cognitive load may increase students’ tendency",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,6,"cogni­ tive resources to information processing, comprehension, and prob­ lem solving. If students perceive that task demands exceed their available cognitive capacity, they may seek external support to reduce immediate mental effort. Therefore, perceived cognitive load may increase students’ tendency to transfer part of their cognitive activities to external systems such as GAI. First, perceived cognitive load may encourage students to use cog­ nitive offloading as a strategy for managing limited cognitive resources. University students often complete tasks that require exten­ sive information processing, such as academic writing, theoretical analysis, and complex problem solving. These tasks require students to integrate multiple sources of information and maintain continuous cognitive control. Previous research shows that demanding learning activities can increase cognitive challenges and influence students’ strategy choices during learning (De Smedt et al., 2018; Butterfuss and Kendeou, 2018). When students experience greater cognitive pressure, they may prefer strategies that reduce unnecessary mental effort. Cognitive offloading provides such support by allowing individuals to use external resources to decrease internal cognitive demands (Risko and Gilbert, 2016).",172
