DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 07 frontiersin.org Second, perceived cognitive load may affect students’ evalua­ tion of the usefulness of external cognitive support. Cognitive offloading does not occur randomly; individuals decide whether to rely on external tools according to task requirements, expected benefits, and cognitive costs (Gilbert, 2024). When university stu­ dents perceive learning tasks as difficult, external tools become more attractive because they can help organize information, reduce memory demands, and provide guidance for completing tasks. In GAI-supported environments, students may use AI to arrange learning materials, explain difficult concepts, and generate possible solutions. Grinschgl and Neubauer (2022) suggested that modern technologies can function as cognitive partners that sup­ port human thinking. Thus, higher perceived cognitive load may increase students’ willingness to involve GAI in cognitive activities. Third, perceived cognitive load may influence the depth and range of students’ cognitive offloading. Moderate use of external support can help students manage complex tasks effectively, but high cognitive pressure may lead students to transfer more important cognitive activ­ ities to external systems. Dunn and Risko (2016) explained that indi­",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"of students’ cognitive offloading. Moderate use of external support can help students manage complex tasks effectively, but high cognitive pressure may lead students to transfer more important cognitive activ­ ities to external systems. Dunn and Risko (2016) explained that indi­ viduals make cognitive offloading decisions by balancing internal effort and external assistance. When students perceive that maintain­ ing independent cognitive processing requires excessive effort, they may increasingly rely on external tools to complete understanding, information organization, and problem solving. In this process, per­ ceived cognitive load may shift cognitive offloading from a supportive strategy toward a broader transfer of learning-related activities. Fourth, GAI may strengthen the relationship between perceived cognitive load and cognitive offloading because it provides more com­ prehensive cognitive support than traditional tools. Traditional learn­ ing tools usually assist specific tasks, whereas GAI can generate explanations, summaries, and problem-solving suggestions according to students’ needs (Yan et al., 2024). This feature allows students to transfer not only simple information management tasks but also higher-level cognitive activities to AI systems. Seung and Basham (2026) noted that cognitive offloading in the",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"problem-solving suggestions according to students’ needs (Yan et al., 2024). This feature allows students to transfer not only simple information management tasks but also higher-level cognitive activities to AI systems. Seung and Basham (2026) noted that cognitive offloading in the era of generative AI con­ cerns how learners distribute cognitive responsibilities between them­ selves and intelligent systems. Therefore, when students experience high cognitive load, GAI may become an especially appealing option for reducing mental demands. Furthermore, students’ trust in external tools may strengthen their tendency to engage in cognitive offloading. Individuals are more likely to rely on external support when they believe that the tool can provide accurate and useful assistance. Weis and Wiese (2019) found that per­ ceived reliability influences cognitive offloading decisions, and Peng and Yeh (2025) further showed that trust toward tools affects individu­ als’ willingness to reduce internal cognitive effort. In university learn­ ing contexts, students who experience high cognitive load and perceive GAI as reliable may show stronger intentions to transfer cog­ nitive tasks to AI. However, cognitive offloading does not always rep­ resent ineffective",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"reduce internal cognitive effort. In university learn­ ing contexts, students who experience high cognitive load and perceive GAI as reliable may show stronger intentions to transfer cog­ nitive tasks to AI. However, cognitive offloading does not always rep­ resent ineffective learning behavior. Appropriate cognitive offloading can help students manage complex tasks and reserve cognitive resources for deeper learning activities. For example, Iqbal et al. (2025) found that generative AI use combined with cognitive offload­ ing could support academic achievement among preservice teachers. The influence of perceived cognitive load on cognitive offloading therefore depends on how students use external support. When stu­ dents use GAI to reduce unnecessary cognitive demands, cognitive offloading may support learning efficiency. When students use GAI mainly to avoid necessary thinking processes, cognitive offloading may reduce active cognitive engagement. In summary, perceived cognitive load may serve as an important antecedent of cognitive offloading among university students. When students experience greater information-processing pressure and mental effort requirements, they may become more likely to rely on GAI to manage learning demands and transfer part of their cognitive activities to",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"an important antecedent of cognitive offloading among university students. When students experience greater information-processing pressure and mental effort requirements, they may become more likely to rely on GAI to manage learning demands and transfer part of their cognitive activities to external systems. Based on this reasoning, this study pro­ poses the following hypothesis: H3: Perceived cognitive load positively influences university stu­ dents’ cognitive offloading. 2.5 The mediating role of cognitive offloading between perceived cognitive load and overreliance on GAI feedback Although perceived cognitive load may influence students’ over­ reliance on GAI feedback, this relationship may not occur directly. Cognitive offloading may explain how cognitive pressure is trans­ formed into dependence on AI-generated feedback. In GAI-supported learning environments, students’ responses to cognitive demands depend not only on the level of difficulty they perceive but also on how they redistribute cognitive activities between themselves and external systems. Therefore, cognitive offloading may serve as a psychological pathway connecting perceived cognitive load with overreliance on GAI feedback. When students experience high cognitive demands, they may attempt to reduce cognitive burden by transferring part of",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"between themselves and external systems. Therefore, cognitive offloading may serve as a psychological pathway connecting perceived cognitive load with overreliance on GAI feedback. When students experience high cognitive demands, they may attempt to reduce cognitive burden by transferring part of learning-related activities to GAI. This process represents a change in the distribution of cognitive responsibility between students and external systems. Unlike traditional tools that mainly support infor­ mation storage or retrieval, GAI can participate in understanding, explanation, summarization, and problem-solving processes (Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022; Yan et al., 2024). As students increas­ ingly rely on GAI to manage these cognitive activities, they may grad­ ually reduce their involvement in evaluating and processing information independently. The transition from cognitive support to cognitive dependence may explain why cognitive offloading contributes to overreliance on GAI feedback. Cognitive offloading itself does not necessarily lead to negative learning outcomes because external support can help stu­ dents manage complex tasks effectively. However, when students transfer essential cognitive activities to GAI, they may reduce oppor­ tunities to construct knowledge, monitor understanding, and evaluate feedback quality. Risko and",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,7,"to negative learning outcomes because external support can help stu­ dents manage complex tasks effectively. However, when students transfer essential cognitive activities to GAI, they may reduce oppor­ tunities to construct knowledge, monitor understanding, and evaluate feedback quality. Risko and Gilbert (2016) argued that cognitive offloading reflects a balance between internal cognitive effort and external assistance, while Gilbert (2024) further suggested that indi­ viduals decide whether to rely on external resources according to per­ ceived cognitive costs and expected benefits. In GAI-supported learning contexts, repeated cognitive outsourcing may gradually shape students’ dependence on AI feedback. Furthermore, the characteristics of GAI may strengthen the mediating role of cognitive offloading. Because GAI can generate fluent explanations and seemingly complete solutions, students may perceive AI feedback as sufficient for addressing learning problems. When students have already transferred part of their cognitive work to GAI, they may become more likely to accept AI-generated suggestions without further verification. This pro­ cess may weaken independent judgment and increase reliance on",164
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,8,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 08 frontiersin.org external feedback sources (Dunn and Risko, 2016; Fan et al., 2025). Seung and Basham (2026) further emphasized that cogni­ tive offloading in the era of generative AI concerns whether learn­ ers maintain active participation in cognitive activities while using intelligent systems. Therefore, cognitive offloading may represent the mechanism through which perceived cognitive load develops into overreliance on GAI feedback. Higher perceived cognitive load may encourage students to transfer cognitive activities to GAI, and this increased cognitive offloading may subsequently strengthen their dependence on AI feedback during learning. In this process, cognitive offloading explains how students’ attempts to manage cognitive demands may unintentionally shift toward reduced independent evaluation and excessive reliance on GAI- generated information. Based on this reasoning, this study pro­ poses the following hypothesis: H4: Cognitive offloading mediates the relationship between per­ ceived cognitive load and university students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. The theoretical model of this study is presented in Figure 2. 2.6 Grouping role of metacognitive monitoring Pervious findings suggest that students’ perceived cognitive load may serve as",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,8,"between per­ ceived cognitive load and university students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. The theoretical model of this study is presented in Figure 2. 2.6 Grouping role of metacognitive monitoring Pervious findings suggest that students’ perceived cognitive load may serve as an important starting point for understanding GAI feed­ back overreliance, while cognitive offloading may explain the process through which this relationship develops. However, students do not respond to cognitive pressure in the same way. Some students may use GAI as a supportive learning resource, whereas others may gradually rely on AI to complete essential cognitive activities. This difference may depend on students’ ability to monitor and regulate their own learning processes. Metacognitive monitoring enables learners to evaluate their understanding, judge the quality of external informa­ tion, and adjust learning strategies accordingly (Zimmerman, 2002). In GAI-supported environments, students with stronger metacogni­ tive monitoring abilities may treat AI feedback as supplementary information and engage in comparison, verification, and reflection. In contrast, students with weaker metacognitive monitoring may accept AI feedback without sufficient evaluation, increasing the possibility of overreliance (Dunn and Risko, 2016;",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,8,"tive monitoring abilities may treat AI feedback as supplementary information and engage in comparison, verification, and reflection. In contrast, students with weaker metacognitive monitoring may accept AI feedback without sufficient evaluation, increasing the possibility of overreliance (Dunn and Risko, 2016; Fan et al., 2025). Although previous studies have examined the influence of genera­ tive AI on learning behaviors and cognitive processes, the psychologi­ cal mechanisms that lead university students to over-rely on GAI feedback remain unclear. Existing research has mainly focused on students’ acceptance of AI tools, frequency of use, or learning out­ comes, while less attention has been given to how students process AI feedback during actual learning activities and why they shift from using AI as learning support to depending on AI for cognitive work (Klarin et al., 2024; Mariñas et al., 2025). This issue is important because successful learning depends not only on obtaining correct information but also on students’ active involvement in understand­ ing, evaluating, and integrating knowledge (Yan et al., 2025). Research on AI-supported learning also reveals a complex pattern of student behaviors. On one",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,8,"successful learning depends not only on obtaining correct information but also on students’ active involvement in understand­ ing, evaluating, and integrating knowledge (Yan et al., 2025). Research on AI-supported learning also reveals a complex pattern of student behaviors. On one hand, GAI can reduce learning barriers, provide timely feedback, and support knowledge construction. For example, in writing education, generative AI can help students identify lan­ guage problems, improve text organization, and develop better expres­ sions (Evmenova et al., 2024; Goldman et al., 2025). In mathematics and problem-solving contexts, AI-generated feedback can provide additional explanations and support students’ understanding of dif­ ficult concepts (Cosentino et al., 2025). On the other hand, the high accessibility of AI feedback may reduce students’ active processing of information. When students focus mainly on completing tasks rather than understanding the reasoning behind feedback, AI may shift from a learning assistant to an external substitute for thinking (León- Domínguez, 2024; Fan et al., 2025). This concern is especially relevant for university students because higher education requires learners to handle open-ended, uncertain, and cognitively demanding tasks. Activities such",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,8,"from a learning assistant to an external substitute for thinking (León- Domínguez, 2024; Fan et al., 2025). This concern is especially relevant for university students because higher education requires learners to handle open-ended, uncertain, and cognitively demanding tasks. Activities such as academic writing, case analysis, research design, and complex problem solving require not only correct answers but also analysis, evaluation, and reflection (Butterfuss and Kendeou, 2018; Alkhatib, 2019). However, GAI can quickly generate responses that appear complete and logically orga­ nized, which may reduce students’ motivation to examine the accu­ racy, suitability, and limitations of AI feedback (Athaluri et al., 2023; Lee et al., 2023). When students regard AI feedback as an authoritative answer rather than learning material that requires further evaluation, overreliance may occur. To better understand this process, it is FIGURE 2 Theoretical model.",136
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 09 frontiersin.org necessary to examine how students manage cognitive resources during learning. Perceived cognitive load may influence students’ decisions to seek external support because students who experience excessive mental demands may prefer strategies that reduce immedi­ ate cognitive effort (De Smedt et al., 2018). Since GAI can provide direct explanations, summaries, and feedback, it offers an attractive way to reduce cognitive demands. However, if students reduce cogni­ tive effort mainly by transferring essential learning activities to AI, they may experience lower cognitive engagement and weaker knowl­ edge construction (Risko and Gilbert, 2016; Gerlich, 2025). Previous studies indicate that cognitive offloading itself is not a negative behavior but an adaptive strategy that helps individuals manage complex tasks. People decide whether to transfer cognitive activities to external tools based on task goals, expected benefits, and perceived tool reliability (Gilbert, 2024; Weis and Wiese, 2019). For example, using calendars to manage study schedules, search engines to obtain information, or software to perform calculations represents reasonable cognitive offloading. However, compared with traditional tools, GAI possesses stronger abilities",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"perceived tool reliability (Gilbert, 2024; Weis and Wiese, 2019). For example, using calendars to manage study schedules, search engines to obtain information, or software to perform calculations represents reasonable cognitive offloading. However, compared with traditional tools, GAI possesses stronger abilities in information generation and reasoning. Therefore, GAI-related cognitive offloading may involve more central learning activities, including understanding, explanation, evaluation, and decision making (Grinschgl and Neubauer, 2022; Seung and Basham, 2026). However, cognitive offloading does not inevitably result in overreliance on GAI feedback. The influence of cognitive offloading depends largely on whether students can regulate their own learning activities and evaluate external information effec­ tively. Metacognitive monitoring plays a central role in this process because it allows learners to judge their current understanding, iden­ tify knowledge gaps, and decide whether additional cognitive effort is necessary (Zimmerman, 2002). In traditional learning environments, effective learners continuously monitor comprehension and adjust strategies according to task requirements. In AI-supported learning environments, these abilities become even more important because students must determine when AI assistance is beneficial and when independent thinking is required. Metacognitive monitoring may",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"effective learners continuously monitor comprehension and adjust strategies according to task requirements. In AI-supported learning environments, these abilities become even more important because students must determine when AI assistance is beneficial and when independent thinking is required. Metacognitive monitoring may therefore explain why students with similar levels of cognitive load and access to GAI feedback may develop different patterns of AI use. Students with strong metacogni­ tive monitoring skills are more likely to question AI-generated responses, compare them with their own knowledge, and identify pos­ sible errors or limitations. Such students may use GAI as a cognitive partner that supports deeper learning rather than as a replacement for personal thinking (Shin, 2021; Buçinca et al., 2021). In contrast, stu­ dents with insufficient metacognitive monitoring may accept AI feed­ back without careful evaluation because they perceive the generated information as accurate and reliable. This tendency may increase their dependence on AI-generated suggestions and reduce their engage­ ment in independent reasoning (Fan et al., 2025). Recent studies have emphasized that the educational challenge of GAI is not whether stu­ dents should use",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"and reliable. This tendency may increase their dependence on AI-generated suggestions and reduce their engage­ ment in independent reasoning (Fan et al., 2025). Recent studies have emphasized that the educational challenge of GAI is not whether stu­ dents should use AI tools, but whether they can develop appropriate reliance on these technologies. Appropriate reliance requires learners to recognize the strengths and limitations of AI, determine when external support is needed, and maintain responsibility for evaluating information quality (Li and Little, 2023). This perspective shifts atten­ tion from simple AI adoption to the cognitive processes that shape how students interact with AI during learning. Understanding indi­ vidual differences in metacognitive monitoring is therefore essential for explaining why some students benefit from GAI feedback while others become overly dependent on it. Based on the above discussion, this study considers metacogni­ tive monitoring as an important individual difference that may shape students’ interaction with GAI feedback. However, the pres­ ent study does not aim to examine the moderating effect of meta­ cognitive monitoring through an interaction model. Instead, metacognitive monitoring is used as",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"as an important individual difference that may shape students’ interaction with GAI feedback. However, the pres­ ent study does not aim to examine the moderating effect of meta­ cognitive monitoring through an interaction model. Instead, metacognitive monitoring is used as a grouping lens to compare whether the proposed relationships among perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and overreliance on GAI feedback are consistent across students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Specifically, students are divided into high and low metacognitive monitoring groups based on their responses to the metacognitive monitoring measure. By comparing these two groups, this study aims to explore whether students with different levels of self-monitoring ability show similar or different patterns in managing cognitive resources and using GAI feedback during learning. 3 Method 3.1 Data source This study collected data from undergraduate students at Ningbo University, located in Jiangbei District, Ningbo, Zhejiang Province, China. The university was selected as the research setting because it provides a diverse undergraduate learning environment with students from multiple academic disciplines, allowing the study to examine students’ cognitive experiences across different learning tasks",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"Jiangbei District, Ningbo, Zhejiang Province, China. The university was selected as the research setting because it provides a diverse undergraduate learning environment with students from multiple academic disciplines, allowing the study to examine students’ cognitive experiences across different learning tasks and academic contexts. As a comprehensive university, Ningbo University includes students who frequently engage in activities such as academic writing, information search, knowledge acquisi­ tion, and problem solving, all of which increasingly involve the use of generative artificial intelligence (GAI) tools. Therefore, the uni­ versity context provides a suitable setting for investigating how undergraduate students perceive and use GAI feedback in learning processes. The study adopted an online questionnaire survey method and collected data through the Credamo research platform from August 1 to August 25, 2026. Credamo was selected because it provides a standardized procedure for questionnaire distribution, participant screening, and response management, which helps improve the con­ sistency and reliability of online data collection. Before data collec­ tion, the study established specific eligibility criteria to ensure that participants had sufficient experience with GAI-supported learning activities. Participants needed to meet",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,9,"and response management, which helps improve the con­ sistency and reliability of online data collection. Before data collec­ tion, the study established specific eligibility criteria to ensure that participants had sufficient experience with GAI-supported learning activities. Participants needed to meet two conditions: they had to be undergraduate students at Ningbo University, and they had to have previous experience using GAI tools for academic learning purposes. To ensure that respondents could provide experience-based evalu­ ations rather than general opinions about artificial intelligence, the questionnaire included a screening question regarding previous GAI use. Participants reported whether they had used commonly available GAI applications, including Doubao, DeepSeek, and ChatGPT. These platforms provide functions related to learning support, such as knowledge explanation, information summarization, writing assis­ tance, and problem-solving guidance. Only students who reported actual experience using at least one GAI platform were retained for subsequent analysis.",143
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 10 frontiersin.org The research team initially distributed 760 questionnaires through the Credamo platform. After completing data collection, the study conducted a multi-step data screening procedure to ensure data quality. First, questionnaires from participants who did not sat­ isfy the sampling criteria were removed. Second, incomplete responses were excluded. Third, responses with abnormal comple­ tion patterns, including extremely short response times and identical answers across all measurement items, were removed to reduce the influence of careless responding. After the screening process, 600 valid questionnaires remained, producing a valid response rate of 78.95%. The final dataset consisted of 600 undergraduate students with direct experience using GAI in learning contexts. These participants provided empirical information about how students experience cogni­ tive demands, transfer cognitive activities to GAI systems, and develop different levels of reliance on GAI feedback. Although the sample came from a single university, the clear inclusion criteria, experience- based participant selection, and strict quality-control procedures ensured that the data were appropriate for examining the cognitive mechanisms associated with university students’ overreliance on GAI feedback.",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"feedback. Although the sample came from a single university, the clear inclusion criteria, experience- based participant selection, and strict quality-control procedures ensured that the data were appropriate for examining the cognitive mechanisms associated with university students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. 3.2 Variable definitions and measurement items This study included four variables to examine the cognitive process through which university students develop overreliance on GAI feedback during learning activities. Although previous studies have examined cognitive load, cognitive offloading, AI dependence, and metacognitive regulation, existing measurement scales were mainly developed for traditional learning environ­ ments or general technology use contexts. These scales do not directly capture how university students experience cognitive demands, transfer cognitive activities, and evaluate GAI-generated feedback in current AI-supported learning environments. Therefore, this study developed measurement items based on pre­ vious theoretical and empirical studies while adapting the wording to the specific context of university students’ interactions with GAI during learning. Perceived Cognitive Load (PCL) was developed to assess stu­ dents’ subjective perception of cognitive demands during learning tasks. Previous research on cognitive load emphasizes that stu­ dents’ learning",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"to the specific context of university students’ interactions with GAI during learning. Perceived Cognitive Load (PCL) was developed to assess stu­ dents’ subjective perception of cognitive demands during learning tasks. Previous research on cognitive load emphasizes that stu­ dents’ learning experiences depend not only on the objective com­ plexity of learning materials but also on their perceived information-processing demands and required mental effort (Sweller et al., 2019a,b). Accordingly, this study measured whether students experience difficulty managing information, require sub­ stantial mental effort, and perceive complex relationships among learning contents. Cognitive Offloading (CO) was developed to measure the extent to which students transfer cognitive activities from them­ selves to GAI systems during learning. Cognitive offloading research suggests that individuals often use external tools to reduce cognitive demands and improve task performance (Risko and Gilbert, 2016). However, unlike traditional tools that mainly store or retrieve information, GAI can generate explanations, organize knowledge, summarize information, and provide prob­ lem-solving suggestions. Therefore, this study focused on four forms of GAI-related cognitive offloading, including information organization, understanding, information extraction, and problem solving. Overreliance on GAI",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"store or retrieve information, GAI can generate explanations, organize knowledge, summarize information, and provide prob­ lem-solving suggestions. Therefore, this study focused on four forms of GAI-related cognitive offloading, including information organization, understanding, information extraction, and problem solving. Overreliance on GAI Feedback (OGF) was developed to capture students’ excessive dependence on AI-generated feedback during learning. Previous research indicates that excessive reliance on AI sys­ tems may reduce independent evaluation, critical judgment, and active cognitive engagement (Zhai et al., 2024). Therefore, this study defined overreliance on GAI feedback as students’ tendency to accept, follow, or evaluate learning outcomes mainly according to GAI feed­ back rather than their own analysis and verification. Metacognitive Monitoring (MM) was included as a grouping vari­ able rather than a predictor or moderator. This variable reflects stu­ dents’ ability to monitor their own understanding and evaluate the appropriateness of GAI feedback before acceptance. Research on self- regulated learning emphasizes that metacognitive monitoring helps students judge their comprehension, identify knowledge gaps, and adjust learning strategies (Zimmerman, 2002). In this study, MM was used to classify students into high and",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"of GAI feedback before acceptance. Research on self- regulated learning emphasizes that metacognitive monitoring helps students judge their comprehension, identify knowledge gaps, and adjust learning strategies (Zimmerman, 2002). In this study, MM was used to classify students into high and low metacognitive monitoring groups to examine whether students with different monitoring abili­ ties show different patterns of GAI feedback use. All measurement items were rated on a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree). The measure­ ment items were developed according to the conceptual boundaries of each variable, and each item focused on a distinct aspect of the construct to avoid overlap among variables. Table 1 presents the oper­ ational definitions, measurement items, measurement focus, and ref­ erence basis of the four variables. 3.3 Sample information Table 2 presents the demographic characteristics and GAI usage patterns of the 600 undergraduate students. Among the participants, 286 students were male (47.67%) and 314 students were female (52.33%). The sample included students from all four academic years, with 150 freshmen (25.00%), 153 sophomores (25.50%), 148 juniors (24.67%), and",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"patterns of the 600 undergraduate students. Among the participants, 286 students were male (47.67%) and 314 students were female (52.33%). The sample included students from all four academic years, with 150 freshmen (25.00%), 153 sophomores (25.50%), 148 juniors (24.67%), and 149 seniors (24.83%). Regarding GAI use frequency, 48 students (8.00%) reported rarely using GAI tools, 112 students (18.67%) used them occasionally, 156 students (26.00%) used them sometimes, 178 students (29.67%) used them frequently, and 106 stu­ dents (17.66%) used them very frequently. For the metacognitive monitoring grouping variable, 309 students (51.50%) were classified into the high metacognitive monitoring group, while 291 students (48.50%) were classified into the low metacognitive monitor­ ing group. 3.4 Item analysis Before conducting reliability and validity analyses, this study per­ formed item analysis to examine the discriminating ability of each measurement item. The critical ratio method was applied by dividing participants into high-score and low-score groups based on their total scores for each construct, and independent-samples t-tests were conducted to examine group differences. As shown in Table 3, all items of perceived cognitive load (PCL1–PCL3), cognitive",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,10,"was applied by dividing participants into high-score and low-score groups based on their total scores for each construct, and independent-samples t-tests were conducted to examine group differences. As shown in Table 3, all items of perceived cognitive load (PCL1–PCL3), cognitive offloading (CO1–CO4), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF1–OGF4) showed significant differences between the high-score and low-score groups (p < 0.001). The critical ratios ranged from 18.266 to 22.640",68
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,11,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 11 frontiersin.org for cognitive offloading items, 18.724 to 20.831 for perceived cogni­ tive load items, and 19.920 to 21.272 for overreliance on GAI feed­ back items. These results indicate that all measurement items could effectively distinguish participants with different levels of the corre­ sponding constructs. Therefore, all items demonstrated satisfactory discriminating ability and were retained for subsequent reliability and validity analyses. 3.5 Reliability and validity testing This study conducted reliability analysis to examine the internal con­ sistency of the measurement scales. As shown in Table 4, the Cronbach’s α coefficients of perceived cognitive load (PCL), cognitive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF) were 0.772, 0.803, and 0.805, respectively. All values exceeded the commonly accepted thresh­ old of 0.70, indicating satisfactory internal consistency among the mea­ surement items. Furthermore, the corrected item-total correlation (CITC) values ranged from 0.574 to 0.653, which indicates that all items showed adequate correlations with their corresponding constructs. The Cronbach’s α values after deleting any item did not exceed the overall reliability coefficients, suggesting that removing any item",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,11,"correlation (CITC) values ranged from 0.574 to 0.653, which indicates that all items showed adequate correlations with their corresponding constructs. The Cronbach’s α values after deleting any item did not exceed the overall reliability coefficients, suggesting that removing any item would not improve the reliability of the scales. Therefore, all measurement items demonstrated acceptable reliability and were retained for subsequent validity analysis and hypothesis testing. TABLE 1 Measurement items. Variable Role in model Operational definition Item code Measurement item Measurement focus Perceived Cognitive Load (PCL) Independent Variable Students’ perceived level of cognitive demands, mental effort, and information- processing pressure when completing learning tasks. PCL1 I feel that some learning tasks require me to process more information than I can easily manage. Information-processing pressure PCL2 I often need to make considerable mental effort to understand complex learning materials. Mental effort requirement PCL3 I find that learning tasks often involve complex relationships among ideas that require substantial mental effort to understand. Content complexity and cognitive burden Cognitive Offloading (CO) Mediating Variable Students’ tendency to transfer part of their cognitive activities that would",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,11,"PCL3 I find that learning tasks often involve complex relationships among ideas that require substantial mental effort to understand. Content complexity and cognitive burden Cognitive Offloading (CO) Mediating Variable Students’ tendency to transfer part of their cognitive activities that would normally require their own mental effort to GAI during learning. CO1 I use GAI to organize learning materials that would otherwise require my own effort to arrange. Information organization offloading CO2 I use GAI to generate explanations that help me complete parts of the understanding process. Understanding-process offloading CO3 I use GAI to summarize key information that I would otherwise need to identify and extract by myself. Information extraction offloading CO4 I use GAI to provide initial ideas or directions when I need to solve learning problems. Problem-solving offloading Overreliance on GAI Feedback (OGF) Dependent Variable Students’ excessive dependence on GAI feedback during learning, reflected in reduced independent evaluation, verification, and judgment of AI-generated feedback. OGF1 I usually accept GAI feedback without carefully checking whether it is correct. Lack of feedback verification OGF2 I find it difficult to make learning",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,11,"GAI feedback during learning, reflected in reduced independent evaluation, verification, and judgment of AI-generated feedback. OGF1 I usually accept GAI feedback without carefully checking whether it is correct. Lack of feedback verification OGF2 I find it difficult to make learning decisions without relying on GAI feedback. Decision dependence OGF3 I tend to follow GAI suggestions even when I have not independently considered other possible solutions. Lack of independent thinking OGF4 GAI feedback has become my primary basis for judging whether my learning work is acceptable. External evaluation dependence Metacognitive Monitoring (MM) Grouping Variable Students’ ability to monitor their own understanding and evaluate the appropriateness of GAI feedback before accepting it. This variable is used to divide students into high and low metacognitive monitoring groups. MM1 Before accepting GAI feedback, I usually evaluate whether it matches my own understanding. Feedback evaluation monitoring",141
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,12,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 12 frontiersin.org This study conducted exploratory factor analysis (EFA) to exam­ ine the construct validity of the measurement items. The results are presented in Table 5. The Kaiser–Meyer–Olkin (KMO) value reached 0.940, indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. Bartlett’s test of sphericity was significant (χ2 = 2843.858, df = 55, p < 0.001), suggesting sufficient correlations among the measurement items. The EFA results identified three factors that corresponded to the proposed constructs: perceived cognitive load, cognitive offload­ ing, and overreliance on GAI feedback. The three factors jointly explained 65.057% of the total variance. After rotation, the explained variance of the three factors was 23.422%, 22.152%, and 19.482%, respectively, indicating that the extracted factors provided adequate representation of the measurement structure. Regarding factor load­ ings, all items showed strong loadings on their intended factors, with values ranging from 0.563 to 0.781, exceeding the commonly accepted threshold of 0.50. Specifically, CO1–CO4 loaded primarily on Factor 2, PCL1–PCL3 loaded primarily on Factor 3, and OGF1– OGF4 loaded primarily on Factor 1. In addition,",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,12,"their intended factors, with values ranging from 0.563 to 0.781, exceeding the commonly accepted threshold of 0.50. Specifically, CO1–CO4 loaded primarily on Factor 2, PCL1–PCL3 loaded primarily on Factor 3, and OGF1– OGF4 loaded primarily on Factor 1. In addition, the communality values ranged from 0.556 to 0.736, indicating that each item shared sufficient variance with the extracted factors. These results demon­ strate that the measurement items showed satisfactory construct validity and could effectively represent their corresponding variables. 4 Results To examine whether the relationships among variables differed across levels of metacognitive monitoring, this study classified participants into high and low metacognitive monitoring groups based on their responses to the metacognitive monitoring item. The classification criterion was determined according to the meaning of the response options on the five-point Likert scale. Scores of 1 and 2 indicate that students rarely engaged in metacognitive monitoring during learning, reflecting a relatively low level of monitoring ability. In contrast, scores of 3, 4, and 5 indicate that students showed at least a moderate level of metacognitive monitoring, suggesting that they were able to",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,12,"engaged in metacognitive monitoring during learning, reflecting a relatively low level of monitoring ability. In contrast, scores of 3, 4, and 5 indicate that students showed at least a moderate level of metacognitive monitoring, suggesting that they were able to monitor and regulate their learning process to some extent. Therefore, students who selected 1 or 2 were classified into the low metacognitive monitoring group, whereas students who selected 3, 4, or 5 were classified into the high metacognitive monitoring group. Based on this classification criterion, 309 students were included in the high metacognitive monitoring group and 291 stu­ dents were included in the low metacognitive monitoring group. 4.1 Descriptive statistical analysis To understand how university students experience and respond to GAI-supported learning, this study first conducted descriptive sta­ tistical analysis of all measurement items. The analysis included the mean, standard deviation, skewness, kurtosis, and coefficient of varia­ tion (CV). These indicators provided an initial overview of students’ perceived cognitive demands, their use of GAI as external cognitive support, and their tendency to rely on GAI feedback during learning activities. As",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,12,"deviation, skewness, kurtosis, and coefficient of varia­ tion (CV). These indicators provided an initial overview of students’ perceived cognitive demands, their use of GAI as external cognitive support, and their tendency to rely on GAI feedback during learning activities. As shown in Table 6, university students demonstrated a relatively high level of cognitive offloading (CO) when using GAI for learning. The four items measuring cognitive offloading (CO1–CO4) showed mean values ranging from 3.575 to 3.832, with standard deviations ranging from 0.775 to 0.959. Among these items, CO2 showed the highest mean score (M = 3.832, SD = 0.775), indicating that students frequently used GAI to support their understanding of learning con­ tent. The results also show that students did not use GAI only for obtaining information but also relied on it to organize materials, understand concepts, and solve learning problems. Meanwhile, TABLE 2 Descriptive statistical analysis. Item Option Frequency Percent (%) Gender Male 286 47.67 Female 314 52.33 Academic Year Freshman 150 25.00 Sophomore 153 25.50 Junior 148 24.67 Senior 149 24.83 GAI Use Frequency Rarely 48 8.00 Occasionally 112",180
