DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,14,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 14 frontiersin.org use mainly occurred in situations where students faced complex learn­ ing requirements rather than simple information acquisition. In addition, the results revealed that university students showed a certain degree of overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF) during learn­ ing. The four items measuring overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF1– OGF4) showed mean values ranging from 3.788 to 3.987, with standard deviations ranging from 0.832 to 0.906. OGF1 obtained the highest mean score (M = 3.987, SD = 0.845), suggesting that students tended to accept GAI feedback as an important reference when com­ pleting learning tasks. The similar scores across the four items indicate that students’ reliance on GAI feedback involved multiple aspects, including feedback acceptance, learning judgment, and task evalua­ tion. The distribution characteristics further indicated that all mea­ surement items showed acceptable statistical properties. The skewness values ranged from −1.070 to −0.703, and the kurtosis values ranged from 0.369 to 1.837, suggesting that the data did not show serious deviations from normal distribution. In addition, the coefficients of variation ranged from 20.232",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,14,"statistical properties. The skewness values ranged from −1.070 to −0.703, and the kurtosis values ranged from 0.369 to 1.837, suggesting that the data did not show serious deviations from normal distribution. In addition, the coefficients of variation ranged from 20.232 to 26.819%, indicating sufficient differ­ ences among students’ responses. These results suggest that the mea­ surement items could effectively capture individual differences in students’ experiences and behaviors related to GAI-supported learn­ ing. Overall, the descriptive results show that university students expe­ rienced considerable cognitive demands while learning with GAI and tended to use GAI as external cognitive support. At the same time, students also showed a tendency to depend on GAI feedback when making learning judgments. 4.2 Confirmatory factor analysis To examine whether the measurement model was appropriate for students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring, confirma­ tory factor analyses (CFA) were conducted separately for the low and high metacognitive monitoring groups. Students who selected 1 or 2 on the metacognitive monitoring item were classified into the low metacognitive monitoring group (n = 291), whereas students who selected 3, 4,",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,14,"(CFA) were conducted separately for the low and high metacognitive monitoring groups. Students who selected 1 or 2 on the metacognitive monitoring item were classified into the low metacognitive monitoring group (n = 291), whereas students who selected 3, 4, or 5 were classified into the high metacognitive monitor­ ing group (n = 309). The CFA results were used to evaluate whether the measurement items adequately represented the three latent con­ structs, including perceived cognitive load (PCL), cognitive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF). The results of the CFA analyses for both groups are presented in Tables 7, 8. The model fit indices indicated that the three-factor measurement model showed an acceptable fit in both groups. For the low metacog­ nitive monitoring group, the model demonstrated satisfactory fit (χ2 = 45.391, df = 41, χ2/df = 1.107, GFI = 0.972, AGFI = 0.955, CFI = 0.996, TLI = 0.995, RMR = 0.018, RMSEA = 0.019). Similarly, the high metacognitive monitoring group also showed acceptable model fit (χ2 = 80.200, df = 41, χ2/df = 1.956, GFI = 0.953,",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,14,"= 0.972, AGFI = 0.955, CFI = 0.996, TLI = 0.995, RMR = 0.018, RMSEA = 0.019). Similarly, the high metacognitive monitoring group also showed acceptable model fit (χ2 = 80.200, df = 41, χ2/df = 1.956, GFI = 0.953, AGFI = 0.925, CFI = 0.975, TLI = 0.966, RMR = 0.027, RMSEA = 0.056). These results suggest that the proposed three-factor measurement model adequately represented students’ responses across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. The standardized factor loadings of all measurement items were significant (p < 0.001) in both groups. For the low metacognitive monitoring group, the stan­ dardized loadings ranged from 0.691 to 0.708 for PCL, from 0.647 to 0.711 for CO, and from 0.660 to 0.712 for OGF. For the high metacog­ nitive monitoring group, the corresponding ranges were 0.720–0.766 for PCL, 0.702–0.739 for CO, and 0.693–0.770 for OGF. These findings indicate that the measurement items effectively represented their cor­ responding constructs in both groups. The convergent validity and reliability of the constructs were fur­ ther assessed using average variance extracted (AVE) and composite reliability (CR). For the",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,14,"OGF. These findings indicate that the measurement items effectively represented their cor­ responding constructs in both groups. The convergent validity and reliability of the constructs were fur­ ther assessed using average variance extracted (AVE) and composite reliability (CR). For the low metacognitive monitoring group, the CR values of PCL, CO, and OGF were 0.740, 0.787, and 0.780, respec­ tively, while the AVE values were 0.487, 0.481, and 0.470. Although the AVE values were slightly below 0.50, the constructs showed acceptable reliability, and all standardized factor loadings exceeded the recommended level. For the high metacognitive monitoring group, the CR values were 0.790, 0.811, and 0.822, and the AVE values were 0.556, 0.517, and 0.537, respectively. These results indicate satisfactory reliability and convergent validity of the measurement model across both groups. 4.3 Discriminant validity Discriminant validity was assessed using Pearson correlations and the square root of average variance extracted (AVE) for both metacog­ nitive monitoring groups. As shown in Tables 9, 10, the square root of TABLE 6 Descriptive statistical analysis. Item S. D. Sum Std. error Mean 95% CI (LL) Mean",180
