DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 15 frontiersin.org AVE values for PCL, CO, and OGF were higher than the correlations among the constructs in both groups, indicating satisfactory discrimi­ nant validity. For the low metacognitive monitoring group, the square root of AVE values for perceived cognitive load (PCL), cognitive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF) were 0.698, 0.694, and 0.686, respectively. The correlations among the three con­ structs ranged from 0.643 to 0.682, and all correlation coefficients were lower than the corresponding square root of AVE values. For the high metacognitive monitoring group, the square root of AVE values for PCL, CO, and OGF were 0.746, 0.719, and 0.733, respectively. The correlations among the constructs ranged from 0.674 to 0.695, which were also lower than the corresponding square root of AVE values. These results indicate that PCL, CO, and OGF represented distinct constructs across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. 4.4 Heterotrait-monotrait ratio The heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) was further examined to assess the discriminant validity of the measurement model across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. As shown in",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"CO, and OGF represented distinct constructs across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. 4.4 Heterotrait-monotrait ratio The heterotrait–monotrait ratio (HTMT) was further examined to assess the discriminant validity of the measurement model across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. As shown in Tables 11, 12, the HTMT values among perceived cognitive load (PCL), cogni­ tive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF) were below the recommended threshold of 0.90 in both groups, indicating satisfactory discriminant validity. For the low metacognitive monitor­ ing group, the HTMT values between PCL and CO, PCL and OGF, and CO and OGF were 0.844, 0.893, and 0.870, respectively. All values were below the recommended threshold of 0.90, suggesting that the three constructs showed adequate discriminant validity. For the high metacognitive monitoring group, the corresponding HTMT values were 0.845, 0.849, and 0.856, respectively. These values also remained below 0.90, indicating that PCL, CO, and OGF represented distinct but related constructs among students with higher levels of metacog­ nitive monitoring. Overall, the HTMT results supported the discrimi­ nant validity of the measurement model across both groups. 4.5 Structural",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"below 0.90, indicating that PCL, CO, and OGF represented distinct but related constructs among students with higher levels of metacog­ nitive monitoring. Overall, the HTMT results supported the discrimi­ nant validity of the measurement model across both groups. 4.5 Structural model results After validating the measurement model, this study further exam­ ined the structural relationships among perceived cognitive load (PCL), cognitive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF) across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Students who selected 1 or 2 on the metacognitive monitoring item were clas­ sified into the low metacognitive monitoring group (n = 291), whereas students who selected 3, 4, or 5 were classified into the high metacog­ nitive monitoring group (n = 309). The structural equation models were estimated using AMOS 31.0, and the results for both groups are presented in Tables 13, 14. The structural model results showed a similar pattern across the two groups. First, perceived cognitive load significantly and positively predicted cognitive offloading in both the low metacognitive monitor­ ing group (β = 0.844, p < 0.001) and the high metacognitive monitor­",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"structural model results showed a similar pattern across the two groups. First, perceived cognitive load significantly and positively predicted cognitive offloading in both the low metacognitive monitor­ ing group (β = 0.844, p < 0.001) and the high metacognitive monitor­ ing group (β = 0.840, p < 0.001). Therefore, H1 was supported in both groups. This finding indicates that when students experience greater cognitive demands during learning, they are more likely to transfer part of their cognitive activities to GAI. Although students with higher metacognitive monitoring may have stronger abilities to regulate their learning processes, complex academic tasks still require considerable cognitive resources. Therefore, students may use GAI as external cog­ nitive support when they encounter difficulties in information pro­ cessing, knowledge organization, or problem solving. Second, cognitive offloading significantly and positively predicted overreliance on GAI feedback in both groups. Specifically, the effect was significant in the low metacognitive monitoring group (β = 0.396, p = 0.008) and the high metacognitive monitoring group (β = 0.506, p < 0.001). These results support H2. The findings suggest that stu­ dents who",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"Specifically, the effect was significant in the low metacognitive monitoring group (β = 0.396, p = 0.008) and the high metacognitive monitoring group (β = 0.506, p < 0.001). These results support H2. The findings suggest that stu­ dents who increasingly transfer cognitive activities to GAI are more likely to rely on AI-generated feedback during learning. Although GAI can provide timely explanations and suggestions that facilitate learning, frequent dependence on external cognitive support may increase students’ tendency to accept AI feedback as an important basis for learning judgments. Third, perceived cognitive load showed a significant positive effect on overreliance on GAI feedback in both groups. The stan­ dardized path coefficient was β = 0.560 (p < 0.001) for the low metacognitive monitoring group and β = 0.419 (p < 0.001) for the high metacognitive monitoring group. Thus, H3 was supported. This result indicates that higher cognitive demands may directly increase students’ reliance on GAI feedback. When students face complex learning tasks that require substantial mental effort, they may seek immediate assistance from GAI to obtain explanations, suggestions, and feedback, thereby",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,15,"This result indicates that higher cognitive demands may directly increase students’ reliance on GAI feedback. When students face complex learning tasks that require substantial mental effort, they may seek immediate assistance from GAI to obtain explanations, suggestions, and feedback, thereby increasing their dependence on AI-generated information. TABLE 7 CFA for group 1. UNSTD S. E. C. R. P STD SMC AVE CR PCL1 ← PCL 1.000 0.708 0.501 0.487 0.740 PCL2 ← PCL 0.936 0.091 10.346 *** 0.691 0.477 PCL3 ← PCL 0.976 0.094 10.388 *** 0.694 0.482 CO1 ← CO 1.000 0.710 0.504 0.481 0.787 CO2 ← CO 0.871 0.081 10.795 *** 0.711 0.506 CO3 ← CO 0.837 0.085 9.897 *** 0.647 0.419 CO4 ← CO 0.907 0.085 10.714 *** 0.705 0.497 OGF1 ← OGF 1.000 0.712 0.507 0.470 0.780 OGF2 ← OGF 1.006 0.098 10.212 *** 0.660 0.436 OGF3 ← OGF 1.019 0.097 10.487 *** 0.679 0.461 OGF4 ← OGF 1.099 0.103 10.643 *** 0.690 0.476 The symbol *** indicates statistical significance.",164
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,16,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 16 frontiersin.org The mediating role of cognitive offloading was further examined using bootstrap analysis with 2,000 samples. The results showed that the indirect effect of perceived cognitive load on overreliance on GAI feedback through cognitive offloading was significant in both groups. For the low metacognitive monitoring group, the indirect effect was 0.335, with a bias-corrected bootstrap confidence interval ranging from 0.069 to 0.611. For the high metacognitive monitoring group, the indirect effect was 0.426, with a confidence interval ranging from 0.149 to 0.648. Since both confidence intervals excluded zero, H4 was supported in both groups. These results indicate that cognitive offloading partially explains how perceived cognitive load contributes to students’ overreliance on GAI feedback. In all, the structural model results indicate that the proposed rela­ tionships among perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and overreliance on GAI feedback were consistent across students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Higher cognitive demands were associated with greater cognitive offloading, which fur­ ther related to stronger reliance on GAI feedback. In addition, per­ ceived cognitive load also",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,16,"overreliance on GAI feedback were consistent across students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Higher cognitive demands were associated with greater cognitive offloading, which fur­ ther related to stronger reliance on GAI feedback. In addition, per­ ceived cognitive load also showed a direct association with overreliance on GAI feedback. These findings support the proposed research model across both metacognitive monitoring groups. 5 Discussion 5.1 Comparison of results between the two groups To examine whether metacognitive monitoring level influenced the relationships among perceived cognitive load (PCL), cognitive offloading (CO), and overreliance on GAI feedback (OGF), this study compared the structural paths between the low metacognitive moni­ toring group (Group 1) and the high metacognitive monitoring group (Group 2). The results showed that both groups supported the pro­ posed structural model, but the strength of several paths differed across groups. These differences indicate that metacognitive monitor­ ing may shape how students transform learning-related cognitive demands into GAI-supported behaviors. The effect of perceived cognitive load on cognitive offloading showed a strong positive relationship in both groups. Specifically, the standardized path coefficient was β",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,16,"indicate that metacognitive monitor­ ing may shape how students transform learning-related cognitive demands into GAI-supported behaviors. The effect of perceived cognitive load on cognitive offloading showed a strong positive relationship in both groups. Specifically, the standardized path coefficient was β = 0.844 for Group 1 and β = 0.840 for Group 2. The similar effect sizes suggest that cognitive demands during learning represent an important reason for students to use GAI as external cognitive support. When undergraduate students encoun­ ter complex learning materials, intensive information processing requirements, or difficult academic tasks, they may use GAI to assist TABLE 8 CFA for group 2. UNSTD S. E. C. R. P STD SMC AVE CR PCL1 ← PCL 1.000 0.766 0.587 0.556 0.790 PCL2 ← PCL 0.954 0.076 12.613 *** 0.751 0.564 PCL3 ← PCL 0.903 0.075 12.097 *** 0.720 0.518 CO1 ← CO 1.000 0.702 0.493 0.517 0.811 CO2 ← CO 0.797 0.069 11.479 *** 0.730 0.533 CO3 ← CO 0.987 0.085 11.599 *** 0.739 0.546 CO4 ← CO 0.904 0.081 11.115 *** 0.704 0.496 OGF1 ← OGF 1.000 0.770 0.593",180
