DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,17,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 17 frontiersin.org with information organization, knowledge understanding, and prob­ lem solving. This finding indicates that cognitive load plays a funda­ mental role in encouraging cognitive offloading, regardless of students’ metacognitive monitoring levels. The relationship between cognitive offloading and overreliance on GAI feedback showed a different pattern between the two groups. The path coefficient was β = 0.396 in Group 1 and increased to β = 0.506 in Group 2. Compared with students with lower metacognitive moni­ toring, students with higher metacognitive monitoring showed a stronger association between their use of cognitive offloading strate­ gies and reliance on GAI feedback. This result suggests that students with higher metacognitive monitoring may use GAI in a more deliber­ ate manner when managing learning tasks. However, when they fre­ quently transfer cognitive activities, such as information processing and evaluation, to GAI, their reliance on AI-generated feedback may also increase. Therefore, among students with higher metacognitive monitoring, cognitive offloading appears to play a more important role in explaining their reliance on GAI feedback. The direct effect of perceived",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,17,"and evaluation, to GAI, their reliance on AI-generated feedback may also increase. Therefore, among students with higher metacognitive monitoring, cognitive offloading appears to play a more important role in explaining their reliance on GAI feedback. The direct effect of perceived cognitive load on overreliance on GAI feedback also differed between the two groups. The path coeffi­ cient was β = 0.560 for Group 1 and β = 0.419 for Group 2. The stron­ ger direct effect in Group 1 indicates that students with lower metacognitive monitoring may rely more directly on GAI feedback when they experience greater cognitive pressure during learning. When students face challenging tasks and perceive higher mental demands, they may seek immediate assistance from GAI to obtain explanations, solutions, or feedback. In comparison, students with higher metacognitive monitoring may regulate their learning process through more diverse strategies, which may reduce the direct influ­ ence of cognitive load on GAI feedback dependence. The mediation analysis further revealed differences in the role of cognitive offloading between the two groups. The indirect effect of perceived cognitive load on overreliance on",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,17,"which may reduce the direct influ­ ence of cognitive load on GAI feedback dependence. The mediation analysis further revealed differences in the role of cognitive offloading between the two groups. The indirect effect of perceived cognitive load on overreliance on GAI feedback through cognitive offloading was 0.335 in Group 1 and 0.426 in Group 2. The larger indirect effect in Group 2 indicates that cognitive offloading plays a more prominent mediating role among students with higher metacognitive monitoring. For these students, cognitive demands may influence GAI feedback dependence mainly through their decision to use GAI as an external cognitive resource. In contrast, Group 1 showed a stronger direct relationship between perceived cognitive load and GAI feedback dependence, suggesting that cognitive pres­ sure itself had a more immediate influence on their reliance on GAI feedback. Overall, the comparison between the two groups demonstrates that metacognitive monitoring may influence the pathway through which students develop reliance on GAI feedback. Students with lower metacognitive monitoring showed a stronger direct association between cognitive load and GAI feedback dependence, whereas stu­ dents with higher metacognitive",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,17,"groups demonstrates that metacognitive monitoring may influence the pathway through which students develop reliance on GAI feedback. Students with lower metacognitive monitoring showed a stronger direct association between cognitive load and GAI feedback dependence, whereas stu­ dents with higher metacognitive monitoring showed a stronger rela­ tionship between cognitive offloading and GAI feedback dependence. These findings suggest that metacognitive monitoring does not simply determine whether students use GAI, but influences the process through which cognitive demands are managed and translated into GAI-supported learning behaviors. 5.2 Theoretical implications This study provides important theoretical implications for understanding cognitive and behavioral adjustment processes in generative artificial intelligence (GAI) supported learning environ­ ments. Unlike previous studies that mainly focus on technology acceptance, usage intention, or learning outcomes, this study adopts a cognitive psychological perspective to examine how cog­ nitive demands influence students’ cognitive offloading behaviors and subsequently contribute to overreliance on GAI feedback. The findings reveal the psychological mechanisms linking perceived TABLE 13 SEM results for group 1. UNSTD S. E. C. R. P STD CO ← PCL 0.977 0.109 8.953 *** 0.844 OGF ←",180
