DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 18 frontiersin.org cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and GAI feedback overreli­ ance, providing a new perspective for explaining how individuals allocate cognitive resources when interacting with intelligent systems. First, this study extends the application of cognitive offloading theory to the context of generative AI. Previous research on cogni­ tive offloading has primarily examined how individuals use exter­ nal tools to reduce memory demands, calculation efforts, and information-processing burdens. This study further demonstrates that, in GAI-supported learning environments, cognitive offload­ ing represents not only a strategy for reducing immediate cogni­ tive demands but also a process that may shape individuals’ interaction patterns with intelligent systems. When undergraduate students experience higher cognitive load, they are more likely to use GAI to undertake part of the information-processing and feedback-related tasks. Continuous reliance on such external cog­ nitive support may further strengthen students’ dependence on GAI feedback. Therefore, this study expands cognitive offloading theory from traditional external support behaviors to ongoing cognitive regulation processes in human-AI interaction. Second, this study enriches cognitive load theory by revealing its role",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"support may further strengthen students’ dependence on GAI feedback. Therefore, this study expands cognitive offloading theory from traditional external support behaviors to ongoing cognitive regulation processes in human-AI interaction. Second, this study enriches cognitive load theory by revealing its role in shaping technology-related dependence behaviors. Existing cognitive load research has mainly emphasized its influ­ ence on learning performance, task completion, and cognitive resource allocation. The present study extends this perspective by showing that cognitive load also affects how individuals choose and rely on external intelligent support. The findings indicate that perceived cognitive load can directly predict overreliance on GAI feedback and indirectly influence such reliance through cognitive offloading. This suggests that when individuals encounter cogni­ tive pressure, they may not simply reduce their engagement with learning tasks; instead, they may redistribute cognitive demands through external intelligent systems, which may gradually influ­ ence their patterns of technology use. Third, this study advances the understanding of the psycho­ logical mechanisms underlying overreliance on GAI feedback. Previous research on artificial intelligence use has largely focused on users’ acceptance, attitudes, and behavioral intentions, while",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"influ­ ence their patterns of technology use. Third, this study advances the understanding of the psycho­ logical mechanisms underlying overreliance on GAI feedback. Previous research on artificial intelligence use has largely focused on users’ acceptance, attitudes, and behavioral intentions, while less attention has been given to why individuals gradually depend on AI-generated information and suggestions. By incorporating cognitive offloading as a mediating mechanism, this study dem­ onstrates that GAI feedback overreliance is not merely a conse­ quence of technological characteristics but also reflects individuals’ cognitive responses to learning demands. The find­ ings highlight that AI dependence has an important cognitive foundation, and its development should be examined through individual psychological processes. Finally, this study extends the role of metacognitive monitor­ ing theory in intelligent learning environments. The results show that students with different levels of metacognitive monitoring followed different pathways in developing GAI feedback overreli­ ance. For students with lower metacognitive monitoring, per­ ceived cognitive load showed a stronger direct association with GAI feedback overreliance, whereas for students with higher metacognitive monitoring, cognitive offloading played a more prominent mediating role.",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"in developing GAI feedback overreli­ ance. For students with lower metacognitive monitoring, per­ ceived cognitive load showed a stronger direct association with GAI feedback overreliance, whereas for students with higher metacognitive monitoring, cognitive offloading played a more prominent mediating role. These findings suggest that metacogni­ tive monitoring does not simply determine whether students use GAI, but influences how they regulate their cognitive relationship with intelligent tools. Thus, this study broadens the application of metacognitive theory by explaining individual differences in AI-supported cognitive regulation. 5.3 Practical insights The findings of this study provide several practical implica­ tions for students, educators, and higher education institutions in the context of GAI-supported learning. By revealing how per­ ceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and metacognitive monitoring jointly influence overreliance on GAI feedback, this study offers practical guidance for promoting more effective and balanced use of generative AI in academic settings. First, the findings suggest that educators should pay attention to students’ cognitive demands during learning tasks when inte­ grating GAI into educational practices. Since perceived cognitive load significantly increased both cognitive offloading and over­ reliance",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"generative AI in academic settings. First, the findings suggest that educators should pay attention to students’ cognitive demands during learning tasks when inte­ grating GAI into educational practices. Since perceived cognitive load significantly increased both cognitive offloading and over­ reliance on GAI feedback, instructors should consider the diffi­ culty and complexity of learning activities before introducing GAI-based assistance. Instead of encouraging students to use GAI simply as a shortcut for completing tasks, educators can design learning activities that require students to engage in analysis, reflection, and knowledge construction while using GAI as a sup­ portive resource. Such an approach may help students benefit from AI assistance without excessively transferring cognitive responsibilities to the technology. Second, this study highlights the importance of developing students’ appropriate cognitive offloading strategies. Cognitive offloading itself is not necessarily harmful, as it can help students manage complex learning demands and improve efficiency. However, the findings indicate that greater cognitive offloading is associated with stronger overreliance on GAI feedback. Therefore, educators should help students distinguish between productive use of GAI and excessive dependence on AI-generated informa­ tion.",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"complex learning demands and improve efficiency. However, the findings indicate that greater cognitive offloading is associated with stronger overreliance on GAI feedback. Therefore, educators should help students distinguish between productive use of GAI and excessive dependence on AI-generated informa­ tion. Training programs can encourage students to use GAI for generating ideas, organizing information, and receiving supple­ mentary explanations while maintaining independent judgment and critical evaluation of AI feedback. Third, the findings emphasize the importance of strengthen­ ing students’ metacognitive monitoring abilities in GAI-supported learning environments. The results show that students with dif­ ferent levels of metacognitive monitoring followed different path­ ways in developing GAI feedback overreliance. Therefore, universities should provide learning guidance that improves stu­ dents’ awareness of their own cognitive processes, including how to evaluate when GAI assistance is necessary, how to monitor their reliance on AI feedback, and how to adjust their learning strate­ gies. Improving metacognitive monitoring may help students establish a more balanced relationship with GAI by encouraging active regulation rather than passive acceptance. Finally, higher education institutions should develop clear guidance for responsible GAI use",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,18,"how to adjust their learning strate­ gies. Improving metacognitive monitoring may help students establish a more balanced relationship with GAI by encouraging active regulation rather than passive acceptance. Finally, higher education institutions should develop clear guidance for responsible GAI use in academic contexts. Since GAI has become an increasingly accessible learning resource, universi­ ties should not focus only on restricting its use but should pro­ mote students’ ability to use AI effectively and critically. Educational policies and training programs should emphasize AI literacy, critical evaluation skills, and independent knowledge",89
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"Guo and Dong 10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976 Frontiers in Psychology 19 frontiersin.org construction. These efforts can help students obtain the benefits of GAI while reducing the risk of excessive dependence on AI-generated feedback. 5.4 Limitations and future research Although this study reveals the relationships among perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, metacognitive monitoring, and overreliance on GAI feedback, several aspects require further investigation. First, the cross-sectional design of this study limits the ability to establish causal relationships among the variables. Although the structural equation model supports the hypothesized associations and mediation pathways, the findings should be interpreted as relationships among variables rather than causal effects. Students’ interactions with GAI may involve dynamic processes in which cognitive load, cognitive offloading strategies, metacognitive monitoring, and feedback reliance influence each other over time. Future studies could adopt longitudinal designs, experimental methods, or experience sampling approaches to further examine the developmental and causal mechanisms underlying students’ interactions with GAI. Second, this study used a single-item measure to classify stu­ dents’ metacognitive monitoring levels. Although this approach provides a concise indicator of students’ perceived metacognitive monitoring and facilitates comparisons",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"examine the developmental and causal mechanisms underlying students’ interactions with GAI. Second, this study used a single-item measure to classify stu­ dents’ metacognitive monitoring levels. Although this approach provides a concise indicator of students’ perceived metacognitive monitoring and facilitates comparisons between groups, it may not fully capture the complexity and multidimensional character­ istics of metacognitive monitoring. Metacognitive monitoring involves various processes, including monitoring understanding, evaluating learning progress, and regulating cognitive strategies, which may not be sufficiently represented by a single item. Future research could employ validated multi-item scales or combine self- reported measures with behavioral indicators to obtain a more comprehensive assessment of students’ metacognitive monitoring abilities. Third, the comparisons between the low- and high-metacogni­ tive-monitoring groups in this study were based on differences in path coefficient patterns rather than formal statistical tests of between- group differences. Therefore, these results should be interpreted as variations in structural relationships across metacognitive monitoring levels rather than confirmed differences between groups. Future research could apply formal multi-group analysis approaches to fur­ ther examine whether the strength of relationships among variables significantly differs across",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"should be interpreted as variations in structural relationships across metacognitive monitoring levels rather than confirmed differences between groups. Future research could apply formal multi-group analysis approaches to fur­ ther examine whether the strength of relationships among variables significantly differs across different levels of metacognitive monitoring. Fourth, the present study mainly relied on self-reported question­ naires to measure students’ GAI use experiences, cognitive percep­ tions, and behavioral tendencies. Although self-reported measures are useful for capturing students’ subjective experiences, they may also be influenced by recall bias, social desirability, and individual inter­ pretation differences, which may introduce potential common method bias. Future research could integrate multiple data sources, such as GAI interaction logs, learning behavior records, task perfor­ mance data, and experimental measures, to provide a more objective understanding of students’ cognitive regulation processes during GAI use. Finally, the participants in this study were recruited from a single university, which may limit the generalizability of the findings. Students from different universities, academic disci­ plines, and educational contexts may differ in their access to AI technologies, learning environments, and patterns of GAI engage­",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"study were recruited from a single university, which may limit the generalizability of the findings. Students from different universities, academic disci­ plines, and educational contexts may differ in their access to AI technologies, learning environments, and patterns of GAI engage­ ment. Therefore, future studies should include samples from mul­ tiple institutions and diverse educational backgrounds to further validate the proposed model and examine its applicability across different learning contexts. 6 Conclusion This study investigated the psychological mechanisms under­ lying undergraduate students’ overreliance on GAI feedback by integrating perceived cognitive load, cognitive offloading, and metacognitive monitoring into a unified research model. Based on survey data from university students, this study examined how learning-related cognitive demands influence students’ use of GAI and how cognitive offloading contributes to the formation of GAI feedback dependence. The findings provide a cognitive perspec­ tive for understanding students’ interaction with generative arti­ ficial intelligence in learning environments. The results demonstrate that perceived cognitive load plays an important role in shaping students’ GAI-related behaviors. When students experience higher cognitive demands during learning, they are more likely to adopt",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"students’ interaction with generative arti­ ficial intelligence in learning environments. The results demonstrate that perceived cognitive load plays an important role in shaping students’ GAI-related behaviors. When students experience higher cognitive demands during learning, they are more likely to adopt cognitive offloading strategies by using GAI as external cognitive support. Cognitive offloading further contrib­ utes to overreliance on GAI feedback, indicating that students’ dependence on AI feedback is not only related to technological fac­ tors but also reflects their cognitive responses to learning pressure. In addition, perceived cognitive load directly influences overreliance on GAI feedback, suggesting that cognitive demands can affect students’ reliance on AI-generated information through both direct and indi­ rect pathways. Furthermore, this study reveals the role of metacognitive monitoring in shaping the relationship between cognitive demands and GAI feedback dependence. The comparison between groups with different levels of metacognitive monitoring shows that students follow different pathways when developing reliance on GAI feedback. Students with lower metacognitive monitoring show a stronger direct association between perceived cognitive load and GAI feedback overreliance, whereas students with higher metacognitive monitoring show",180
DOC_016,fpsyg-17-1998976.pdf,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1998976,19,"of metacognitive monitoring shows that students follow different pathways when developing reliance on GAI feedback. Students with lower metacognitive monitoring show a stronger direct association between perceived cognitive load and GAI feedback overreliance, whereas students with higher metacognitive monitoring show a stronger role of cognitive offloading in explaining GAI feedback dependence. These find­ ings suggest that metacognitive monitoring influences not only students’ learning regulation but also how they manage their cog­ nitive relationship with intelligent tools. Overall, this study extends the understanding of GAI- supported learning by demonstrating that overreliance on AI feedback emerges from the interaction between cognitive demands, external cognitive support, and self-regulatory pro­ cesses. The findings highlight that effective GAI use requires more than access to intelligent tools; it also depends on students’ ability to regulate cognitive resources and evaluate AI-generated infor­ mation appropriately. This study provides theoretical and practi­ cal insights into how university students can develop a more balanced and productive relationship with generative artificial intelligence in learning contexts.",164
