DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,3,"scale student survey, we measure capability development as a composite count of self-reported improve­ ments across four advanced domains (critical thinking, creativity, teamwork, and self-management). Meth­ odologically, we adopt a person-centred approach to identify latent profiles and estimate profile differences and profile-stratified support effects. By shifting attention from “how much” GenAI is used to “how” it is embedded in learning processes, the study offers a perspective on scalable yet targeted insti­ tutional scaffolding in the GenAI era. The remainder of the paper reviews related literature, develops hypoth­ eses, describes data and measures, presents results, and discusses implications. 2. Literature review 2.1. Outcome effects GenAI is increasingly discussed as a major driver of change in higher education, reflecting longer-standing expectations that AI-based technologies would transform university teaching and learning (Bearman et al., 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023; Ocaña-Fernández et al., 2019). Since the release of LLMs such as ChatGPT, univer­ sities have explored their potential for learning and student support, building on earlier applications of AI integrated into student-facing services across the learning lifecycle (Baidoo-Anu & Ansah, 2023; Fesenmaier & Wöber,",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,3,"Since the release of LLMs such as ChatGPT, univer­ sities have explored their potential for learning and student support, building on earlier applications of AI integrated into student-facing services across the learning lifecycle (Baidoo-Anu & Ansah, 2023; Fesenmaier & Wöber, 2023; Khare et al., 2018). Earlier research on AI in education focused on tutoring and analytics; recently, attention has shifted to direct learner interaction and its impacts on reasoning, writing, and problem-framing. Current studies examine GenAI’s effects on learning quality – operationalized as Ability­ Sum (a composite of self-reported improvements) – and student development. To consolidate these findings, meta-analyses and systematic reviews provide a consolidated view of overall trends (Crompton & Burke, 2023; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). ChatGPT use can positively influence learning performance and support higher-order thinking and learning perception, particularly when AI functions as a supportive scaffold rather than a substitute for student cognition (Singh, 2025). Systematic reviews further indicate that AI-supported learning is more effective when students are required to evaluate, revise, or explain AI- generated output. Recent studies also highlight challenges such as scalability, data quality,",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,3,"than a substitute for student cognition (Singh, 2025). Systematic reviews further indicate that AI-supported learning is more effective when students are required to evaluate, revise, or explain AI- generated output. Recent studies also highlight challenges such as scalability, data quality, and ethical gov­ ernance (Chan & Hu, 2023; Ghotbi et al., 2022; Pang & Wei, 2025). Taken together, existing findings suggest GenAI acts as both learning scaffold and cognitive effort substitute, explaining mixed observed outcomes (Han et al., 2025). 2.2. Heterogeneity Recent research shows that engagement with GenAI varies across students and learning contexts. Engage­ ment levels differ across disciplines, with applied fields having higher engagement in cognitive tasks than pure fields (Qu et al., 2024). The pedagogical purposes for which students and educators use AI tools are also diverse, from automated feedback to personalized research support (Kasneci et al., 2023). These findings confirm that students adopt diverse GenAI approaches, driven by varying purposes and reliance. Existing research, however, rarely examines how multidimensional use combines to form distinct learning practice patterns. Frequency-based metrics alone fail to capture these nuances, potentially",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,3,"2023). These findings confirm that students adopt diverse GenAI approaches, driven by varying purposes and reliance. Existing research, however, rarely examines how multidimensional use combines to form distinct learning practice patterns. Frequency-based metrics alone fail to capture these nuances, potentially misrepresenting GenAI’s learning effects (Gašević et al., 2015). 2.3. Institutional scaffolding After the initial integration into student services, the focus of the institution’s work includes guiding the engagement of GenAI (Khare et al., 2018). With the emergence of GenAI, institutions have begun to expand these efforts by providing guidance on AI literacy, responsible AI use, and pedagogical integration of AI tools (Jin et al., 2025; Kong et al., 2023; Sutedjo et al., 2024). From an ILE perspective, institutional support plays the role of a dialogic infrastructure, structuring interaction, feedback and tool-mediated learn­ ing. However, existing research shows that the effects of such support are not uniform (Simatupang et al., 2025). Some studies suggest that instructional guidance and structured tasks can encourage students to cri­ tically evaluate and revise AI-generated output, thereby strengthening learning outcomes. A comprehensive analysis of recent large-scale",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,3,"of such support are not uniform (Simatupang et al., 2025). Some studies suggest that instructional guidance and structured tasks can encourage students to cri­ tically evaluate and revise AI-generated output, thereby strengthening learning outcomes. A comprehensive analysis of recent large-scale cases suggests that the effectiveness of GenAI is closely related to the 2 J. ZHANG ET AL.",57
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,4,"engagement of students in using these tools in structured institutional support systems (Jeilani & Abubakar, 2025; Pang & Wei, 2025). Despite these advances, limitations remain. Most studies focus on the average effect and ignore how diversified use architectures affect learning. Specifically, existing research relies heavily on single-frequency engagement measurement rather than multi-dimensional models; frequency alone cannot capture key differences in purpose, tool selection or reliance, which reflects the multidimensionality of SRL and can bias research conclusions (Panadero, 2017). Institutional support has been widely discussed, but few studies have examined its operation in different use architectures. These gaps motivate the present study. 3. Theoretical framework This study conceptualizes students’ GenAI engagement as a multidimensional learning input whose conse­ quences depend not only on usage intensity (frequency/quantity) but also on use architecture, a higher- order configuration of purpose breadth, tool mix, and reliance orientation within academic contexts. This configuration aligns with the SRL processes of planning, monitoring and reflection. Students with similar intensity may nonetheless enact qualitatively different learning processes: some integrate AI into iterative cycles of planning, elaboration, checking, and revision,",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,4,"reliance orientation within academic contexts. This configuration aligns with the SRL processes of planning, monitoring and reflection. Students with similar intensity may nonetheless enact qualitatively different learning processes: some integrate AI into iterative cycles of planning, elaboration, checking, and revision, whereas others use AI primarily for rapid output. Sophisticated use architecture with diverse tool ecosystems facilitates co-regulation, enabling learners to coordinate and integrate AI system feedback, thereby strengthening capability development across critical thinking, creativity, teamwork and self-management. Such heterogeneity can yield systematically different outcomes, particularly when the outcome is broad capability development rather than performance on a single task (Figure 1). To clarify AI-supported learning differences, the concept of use architecture examines how students configure learning purposes, tool selection and reliance on GenAI in learning activities. This perspective extends self-regulated learning (SRL) frameworks, shifting focus from internal cognition to how AI-mediated configurations integrate into planning, monitoring and task execution (Panadero, 2017), while also highlight­ ing co-regulation processes in which learners coordinate with peers or AI systems to manage learning (Järvelä & Hadwin, 2013). By examining whether GenAI supports or replaces",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,4,"AI-mediated configurations integrate into planning, monitoring and task execution (Panadero, 2017), while also highlight­ ing co-regulation processes in which learners coordinate with peers or AI systems to manage learning (Järvelä & Hadwin, 2013). By examining whether GenAI supports or replaces cognitive effort, this configur­ ation determines how learners interact with GenAI and regulate feedback during learning activities. Use architecture is not simply a relabeling of SRL or scaffolding; it provides a unique configurational unit for ana­ lyzing AI-mediated learning. While SRL links outcomes with learner regulation, and the scaffolding–substi­ tution distinction centers on cognitive effort transfer, use architecture adds a new dimension: it captures the configuration of purposes, tool repertoires, and reliance orientations. This framework links process theory (SRL) with functional theory (scaffolding–substitution) through observable configurations, showing Figure 1. Conceptual model. INTERACTIVE LEARNING ENVIRONMENTS 3",135
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,5,"that GenAI’s impact depends not only on internal regulation or task function, but fundamentally on how lear­ ners integrate AI into broader practices – a dimension overlooked in existing discourses. As a framework, use architecture explains why different learning outcomes emerge under the same usage intensity within ILE, directly challenging the assumption that learning benefits are determined by frequency alone. From a learning-process perspective, GenAI can serve two competing functions. As cognitive scaffolding, it can support metacognitive monitoring, elaboration, and revision, thereby fostering gains across advanced domains such as critical thinking, creativity, teamwork coordination, and self-management. As cognitive sub­ stitution, it can outsource core processing to the system; even if substitution improves short-run task per­ formance, it is less likely to translate into broad capability gains because internal cognitive work is displaced. Crucially, these functions are not reducible to frequency alone; rather, they are shaped by use architecture – how purposes, tools, and reliance are organized across tasks – which shapes SRL integration, determining whether AI acts as scaffolding or substitution and driving capability development. Operationa­ lizing it clarifies the",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,5,"alone; rather, they are shaped by use architecture – how purposes, tools, and reliance are organized across tasks – which shapes SRL integration, determining whether AI acts as scaffolding or substitution and driving capability development. Operationa­ lizing it clarifies the specific configurations that define AI’s functional role. A second pillar concerns the institutional environment. Institutional learning support is an ILE dialogic infrastructure, which guides learning-with-AI practices through procedures, norms and strategies (Wang et al., 2024). Such supports can reduce uncertainty about appropriate use, prompt verification and reflection, and convert AI outputs into learning opportunities; therefore, broader support may be positively associated with broader capability gains (Chiu, 2024; Wut et al., 2025). Finally, support benefits are not uniform across learners. Support interacts with use architecture because the “fit” between institutional resources and students’ AI practices varies. For routine, institution-aligned users, additional supports complement course-embedded workflows and common academic constraints, strengthening the translation from GenAI engagement to broader gains. For self-directed multi-tool general­ ists with diversified tool ecosystems, incremental formal support may be partly redundant, yielding smaller marginal returns. This reflects person-environment",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,5,"supports complement course-embedded workflows and common academic constraints, strengthening the translation from GenAI engagement to broader gains. For self-directed multi-tool general­ ists with diversified tool ecosystems, incremental formal support may be partly redundant, yielding smaller marginal returns. This reflects person-environment fit: support works best when complementing, not dupli­ cating, existing practices. Taken together, the framework predicts (i) architecture-linked differences in capa­ bility breadth beyond usage intensity, and (ii) an architecture-contingent support–gain relationship. 3.1. Hypotheses H1 (Use-Architecture Dominance Hypothesis). Controlling for overall GenAI usage intensity (AI_Usage), students’ generative-AI use architecture (Profile) is significantly associated with the breadth of perceived learning gains (AbilitySum). H2a (Institutional Support Breadth Hypothesis). Controlling for overall generative-AI usage intensity (AI_Usage), students’ generative-AI use architecture (Profile), and other covariates, broader institutional learning support (SupportCount) is positively associated with the breadth of perceived learning gains (AbilitySum). H2b (Limited Profile-Contingent Heterogeneity Hypothesis). The positive association between institutional learning support breadth (SupportCount) and the breadth of perceived learning gains (AbilitySum) is expected to vary modestly across generative-AI use architectures (Profile), with stronger marginal returns for mainstream regular learners than for broad",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,5,"Heterogeneity Hypothesis). The positive association between institutional learning support breadth (SupportCount) and the breadth of perceived learning gains (AbilitySum) is expected to vary modestly across generative-AI use architectures (Profile), with stronger marginal returns for mainstream regular learners than for broad multi-tool generalists. 4. Description of dataset This study used an anonymous cross-sectional online survey to examine how GenAI engagement relates to self-reported ability improvements, accounting for learning context and institutional support. Distributed via online channels to students in Hubei, China, the survey concentrated on one focal public university. The plat­ form automatically flagged invalid responses based on completeness, time, consistency, and duplicate records; these were removed during data cleaning. Due to sparse data from other institutions, the final ana­ lytic sample was restricted to valid responses from the focal university. The effective analytic frame of this study should therefore be understood as valid student responses obtained through online convenience recruitment at the focal university, rather than as a probability-based sample drawn from a fully enumerated 4 J. ZHANG ET AL.",170
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,5,"understood as valid student responses obtained through online convenience recruitment at the focal university, rather than as a probability-based sample drawn from a fully enumerated 4 J. ZHANG ET AL.",30
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,6,"roster. Because the survey was distributed through open online channels rather than a fixed and fully enum­ erated invitation list, a conventional response rate could not be calculated. The resulting dataset should therefore be interpreted as a non-probability online convenience sample and should not be treated as fully representative of all higher-education students in Hubei Province or China. This study received approval from the authors’ institutional ethics committee (Approval No. WIT-3-2026-10). Participation was voluntary, and informed consent was obtained electronically before respondents proceeded to the questionnaire. The survey was anonymous, and no personally identifying information was collected or retained in the analytic dataset. The four outcome domains – Critical thinking, Creativity, Teamwork, and Self-management – are measured via a select-all-that-apply item and coded as binary indicators (1 = improvement reported; 0 = not reported). We construct AbilitySum, a composite count (0–4) of these domains, where higher values indi­ cate broader perceived gains. This index captures the breadth of perceived capability development rather than objective skill acquisition. This operationalization is appropriate because the study focuses on students’ perceived development across multiple",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,6,"(0–4) of these domains, where higher values indi­ cate broader perceived gains. This index captures the breadth of perceived capability development rather than objective skill acquisition. This operationalization is appropriate because the study focuses on students’ perceived development across multiple higher-order domains, which are not readily reducible to a single objective metric in a cross-sectional survey. Therefore, AbilitySum serves as a parsimonious breadth index of perceived gains rather than a direct measure of competence. The construction of AbilitySum and descrip­ tive statistics for its component items are reported in Appendix Tables A1–A3. Key explanatory variables capture generative-AI engagement along four dimensions (usage intensity, tool choice, usage purposes, and reliance), with frequency and reliance measured on ordered scales and tool types/purposes recoded into binary indicators. Institutional support is measured through multiple-response items on available support formats (e.g. online courses, workshops, school–industry activities, peer groups) and summarized as SupportCount (0–4), counting how many modalities each student selects to reflect support breadth rather than intensity; AI-related teaching methods and perceived teaching effects are like­ wise captured using multiple-response items and treated as categorical",180
