DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,7,"SD = 1.16; median = 2), whereas AI Benefit is concentrated toward the lower end of its bounded scale (mean = 1.11, SD = 0.45; median = 1). Institutional support is moderate on average (SupportCount mean = 2.00, SD = 1.09; median = 2). Tool choices, usage purposes, teaching methods, and dependence measures together indicate substantial heterogeneity in students’ GenAI engagement.Finally, IQR-based outlier flags for bounded ordinal variables are interpreted cautiously as distributional features rather than data-quality pro­ blems, since all items are constrained to predefined response ranges. 5. Profile identification Latent Class Analysis (LCA) was employed to identify unobserved (latent) subgroups of individuals who display similar response patterns across the observed indicators. LCA was chosen because the sample is unli­ kely to be homogeneous: individuals may differ not only in the overall intensity of the focal attribute (e.g.the five-category categorical measure Depend), but also in the configuration of categorical purposes (e.g. Purpose categories). Rather than characterizing the entire sample with a single average pattern, LCA adopts a person-centred approach that summarizes heterogeneity as a small number of qualitatively distinct",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,7,"categorical measure Depend), but also in the configuration of categorical purposes (e.g. Purpose categories). Rather than characterizing the entire sample with a single average pattern, LCA adopts a person-centred approach that summarizes heterogeneity as a small number of qualitatively distinct and interpretable profiles (classes), each defined by a specific distribution of the observed indicators. Conceptually, LCA posits that the associations among the indicators can be explained by membership in a latent categorical variable (the class variable). Conditional on class membership, the indicators are assumed to be locally independent, meaning that residual associations are largely accounted for by the latent class structure. Each class is characterized by class-specific parameters, including (i) the class-specific mean level (or distributional parameters) of the continuous indicator(s) (e.g. higher vs. lower Depend), and (ii) class-specific response probabilities for categorical indicators (e.g. the probability of endorsing each Purpose category within a class). These estimates provide a clear basis for substantive interpretation and profile labeling.To determine the optimal number of classes, models with varying class counts were esti­ mated and compared using standard fit and classification criteria, including",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,7,"Purpose category within a class). These estimates provide a clear basis for substantive interpretation and profile labeling.To determine the optimal number of classes, models with varying class counts were esti­ mated and compared using standard fit and classification criteria, including the log-likelihood, AIC/BIC, entropy, and the minimum class proportion. The final solution was selected to balance statistical fit, parsi­ mony, and interpretability, while avoiding solutions that create overly small and potentially unstable classes. Based on the model fit comparison in Table 2, the five-class solution (K = 5) was selected. Moving from K = 4 to K = 5 substantially improved model fit, reflected in a higher log-likelihood (from −16877.384 to −16567.108) and notable reductions in AIC (from 33928.767 to 33352.217) and BIC (from 34425.905 to 33975.068), indicating meaningful explanatory gains while accounting for model complexity. The K = 5 model also demonstrated good classification quality (entropy = 0.877) and maintained an acceptable minimum class proportion (minClassShare = 0.048), supporting the interpretability and practical stability of the derived profiles. Although the six-class solution produced slightly lower AIC and BIC (AIC",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,7,"model also demonstrated good classification quality (entropy = 0.877) and maintained an acceptable minimum class proportion (minClassShare = 0.048), supporting the interpretability and practical stability of the derived profiles. Although the six-class solution produced slightly lower AIC and BIC (AIC = 33201.887; BIC = 33950.451), it introduced a very small class (minClassShare = 0.032) without improving entropy (0.876), suggesting limited practical benefit and a higher risk of over-partitioning. Taken together, these results indicate that K = 5 provides the most parsimonious and interpretable balance between model fit, class size adequacy, and classification quality. Additional latent-profile diagnostics, including local-independence checks, likelihood-ratio tests, and substantive correspondence across adjacent class sol­ utions, are provided in Appendix Tables B1–B3. Table 2 summarizes the fit and classification diagnostics motivating the K = 5 solution. We interpret the resulting profiles by triangulating (i) class-specific conditional probabilities for purposes and tool types (Figure 2), (ii) expected dependence across Course, Homework, and Innovation contexts (Figure 3), and (iii) class prevalence in the sample (Figure 4). Table 2. Model fit comparison. No. of classes (K) logLik AIC BIC Gsq",180
