DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,8,"Beyond the dominant mainstream regular learners subgroup, two smaller classes show more distinctive patterns (Figure 4). Class 5 (less than 10% of the sample) displays the broadest engagement: conditional probabilities are high across all purposes (Fun, Homework, Research, and Study) and across multiple tool types (Code, Data, LLM, Write, and Other) (Figure 2), consistent with an “all-purpose, multi-tool” profile. Notably, despite this breadth, its expected dependence intensity remains mid-to-high (approximately 3.1 across contexts), indicating frequent engagement without reaching extreme reliance (Figure 3). By contrast, Class 2 is the smallest subgroup (approximately 5% of the sample; Figure 4), in line with the minimum class share reported for the selected K = 5 solution (Table 2). It shows comparatively stronger academic/research orientation (Figure 2) and exhibits near-ceiling dependence across Course, Homework, and Innovation con­ texts (around 4.9), consistent with a “high-dependence power user” profile (Figure 3). Overall, the K = 5 sol­ ution captures meaningful heterogeneity beyond the dominant mainstream regular learners class (Figures 2–4). Posterior membership probability diagnostics further suggest generally high classification certainty (with class-wise summaries concentrated near one), supporting",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,8,"user” profile (Figure 3). Overall, the K = 5 sol­ ution captures meaningful heterogeneity beyond the dominant mainstream regular learners class (Figures 2–4). Posterior membership probability diagnostics further suggest generally high classification certainty (with class-wise summaries concentrated near one), supporting the interpretability of the identified profiles; nevertheless, inference involving the smallest class should be treated cautiously given its limited prevalence (Figure 4 and Table 2). 6. Ordered logistic regression Having established five latent usage profiles, we turn to ordered-logit models of AbilitySum (1–4). The pro­ portional-odds specification exploits the ordinal scale and yields interpretable odds ratios as a common shift toward higher outcome categories; its restriction is evaluated using standard diagnostics. The proportional- odds assumption is further assessed in Appendix Figure C1. We report profile-only, intensity-only, and joint Figure 2. Tool purpose. Figure 3. Depend expectation. INTERACTIVE LEARNING ENVIRONMENTS 7",140
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,9,"specifications under a common set of controls to distinguish profile-compositional differences from usage- intensity effects. AbilitySum is theoretically defined on a 0–4 scale as the sum of four binary domain indi­ cators; however, in our sample no respondents scored 0, so the observed outcome ranges from 1 to 4. This article modelled the ordinal outcome Yi = AbilitySumi ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4} (ordered from low to high improve­ ment) using a proportional-odds ordered logit model with cutpoints c ∈ {1, 2, 3}. Let Pi denote an individual’s Profile category (reference group: Profile = 1), Ui denote AI Usage, and Zi = (Gradei, MajorGroupi, Leveli) denote the vector of controls. Define D(Pi) = (1{Pi = 2}, 1{Pi = 3}, 1{Pi = 4}, 1{Pi = 5})T as the set of profile dummy vari­ ables relative to Profile 1. The model is written in cumulative-logit form as log Pr (Yi ≤c) Pr (Yi . c) 􏼒 􏼓 = ac −hi, c = 1, 2, 3 where Pr(·) denotes probability, 1{·} is an indicator function (equal to 1 if the condition holds and",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,9,"written in cumulative-logit form as log Pr (Yi ≤c) Pr (Yi . c) 􏼒 􏼓 = ac −hi, c = 1, 2, 3 where Pr(·) denotes probability, 1{·} is an indicator function (equal to 1 if the condition holds and 0 other­ wise), (·)ᵀ denotes transpose, ac are threshold-specific cutpoints, and hi is the linear predictor. We estimated three specifications: Model A (profile main effects) with hi = βᵀD(Pi) + γᵀZi; Model B (AI usage main effect) with hi = β₁Ui + γᵀZi; and Model C (joint model) with ηi = βᵀD(Pi) + β₁Ui + γᵀZi, where β, β₁, and γ are the corresponding coefficient parameters. Results are reported as odds ratios (ORs) with 95% confidence intervals. Figure 4. Class share K5. Table 3. AbilitySum Ordered Logit ABC. Outcome Variable Model A OR [95% CI] Model A p Model B OR [95%CI] Model B p Model C OR [95% CI] Model C p AbilitySum (Ordered Logit, 1–4) Profile: 1 OR [95%CI]: 1.00 (Ref.) NA NA NA OR [95%CI]: 1.00 (Ref.) NA AbilitySum (Ordered Logit, 1–4) Profile: 2 0.62 [0.41, 0.95]*",180
