DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,10,"Table 3 shows that profile membership predicts AbilitySum beyond overall AI usage intensity. Relative to Profile 1, Profile 5 is associated with higher perceived capability breadth, whereas Profiles 2 and 4 are associ­ ated with lower breadth; Profile 3 does not differ significantly from the reference group. AI_Usage remains positively associated with AbilitySum in the joint model, but its coefficient attenuates after profile member­ ship is introduced. Together, these results suggest that perceived gains depend less on usage frequency per se than on how purposes, tools, and reliance are configured in practice. The between-profile patterns align closely with the substantive meaning of each profile. Profile 5 rep­ resents all-purpose, multi-tool, cross-context users who move across learning, assignments, research, and leisure and draw on a wider set of tools. This group is more likely to report stronger improvement, consistent with having more opportunities to practice iteratively, refine outputs across attempts, and transfer what they learn from one task to another. By contrast, Profile 4 captures low-dependence light users whose use is more selective, bounded to fewer contexts, and narrower in tool",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,10,"more opportunities to practice iteratively, refine outputs across attempts, and transfer what they learn from one task to another. By contrast, Profile 4 captures low-dependence light users whose use is more selective, bounded to fewer contexts, and narrower in tool coverage; this group is less likely to report high improvement, consistent with fewer occasions to repeatedly leverage AI across learning situations. Profile 3 reflects mainstream, routine study users and does not differ meaningfully from the reference group, suggesting that using AI as a standard study aid is not, by itself, associated with higher perceived gains once usage architectures are taken into account. Profile 2 corresponds to high-dependence, research- oriented power users and shows a lower average association with perceived improvement in the model summary. Substantively, this pattern may reflect diminishing marginal returns at very high reliance, or a more substitution-oriented mode of use in which AI is used to complete tasks rather than to build transfer­ able skills. Accordingly, we interpret this result as an average association rather than a uniform conclusion across all levels of improvement. Turning to usage",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,10,"mode of use in which AI is used to complete tasks rather than to build transfer­ able skills. Accordingly, we interpret this result as an average association rather than a uniform conclusion across all levels of improvement. Turning to usage intensity, higher overall AI usage is positively related to perceived improvement when intensity is modeled alone, but the association becomes weaker once profile membership is added. This attenuation indicates that part of the “more use, more improvement” relationship is explained by systematic differences across profiles in breadth, tool diversity, and the mix of purposes. In sum, perceived improvement is shaped less by sheer frequency and more by whether AI use is broad, diverse, and consistently applied across tasks and contexts in a way that supports an ongoing learning cycle. 7. Stratified models To evaluate whether the association between institutional learning-support breadth and perceived advanced-ability improvement varies across generative-AI usage profiles, we adopt a profile-stratified regression strategy. For each profile g, we estimate the same within-profile model, E[AbilitySumi|g] = ag + bg SupportCounti + g`Xi, so that heterogeneity is captured through",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,10,"breadth and perceived advanced-ability improvement varies across generative-AI usage profiles, we adopt a profile-stratified regression strategy. For each profile g, we estimate the same within-profile model, E[AbilitySumi|g] = ag + bg SupportCounti + g`Xi, so that heterogeneity is captured through profile-specific slopes bg without including interaction terms in a pooled specification. Here, AbilitySum is a 0–4 composite defined as the sum of four binary indicators – Thinking, Creativity, Teamwork, and Self-management – reflecting the number of domains in which a respondent reports improvement. SupportCount is a 0–4 count index measuring the breadth/diversity of institutional learning-support modalities used (e.g. online courses, workshops, school–industry/expert activi­ ties, and peer learning groups), interpreted as breadth rather than intensity or frequency. To facilitate cross- profile comparison on a common scale, we summarize the estimated relationship using average marginal effects (AMEs) of SupportCount, interpreted as the expected change in E[AbilitySum] associated with a one-unit increase in SupportCount within each profile. We report AMEs with 95% confidence intervals and visualize them in a forest plot to highlight differences in magnitude and uncertainty across profiles. Profile differences in",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,10,expected change in E[AbilitySum] associated with a one-unit increase in SupportCount within each profile. We report AMEs with 95% confidence intervals and visualize them in a forest plot to highlight differences in magnitude and uncertainty across profiles. Profile differences in baseline AbilitySum (H1) need not mirror differences in support responsiveness (H2). Table 4. stratified models. Profile n share Beta se z p OR ame lo hi 1 364 0.16250 0.49982 0.09994 5.00143 0.00000 1.64842 0.24261 0.14986 0.33691 2 108 0.04821 0.66122 0.17616 3.75345 0.00017 1.93715 0.25746 0.11047 0.40888 3 1315 0.58705 0.58662 0.05401 10.86201 0.00000 1.79790 0.29201 0.23557 0.34818 4 271 0.12098 0.55817 0.13018 4.28773 0.00002 1.74747 0.26278 0.13318 0.41989 5 182 0.08125 0.55840 0.13213 4.22612 0.00002 1.74788 0.15598 0.07870 0.23161 INTERACTIVE LEARNING ENVIRONMENTS 9,125
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,11,"A profile can resemble the reference group in baseline outcomes yet be more responsive to institutional scaffolding The stratified results yield a clear and robust within-profile pattern: support breadth is beneficial in every profile. In all five strata (Table 4 and Figure 5), the estimated AME of SupportCount is positive and the cor­ responding confidence interval lies fully above zero, indicating that broader institutional support is consist­ ently linked to broader perceived gains rather than concentrated improvement in just one domain. Substantively, adding one additional institutional support modality is associated with an increase of roughly 0.16 to 0.29 in the expected number of improvement domains.Importantly, the magnitude and pre­ cision vary in ways that align with the behavioral portraits and subgroup sizes. The largest stratum – Class 3, mainstream regular learners (about 58.7% of the sample) – shows the strongest and most precisely esti­ mated association (AME around 0.29, with a tight interval), suggesting that for routine, mainstream regular learners users, expanding institutional support channels translates efficiently into broader multi- domain gains. Class 1, study/assignment–focused and high-dependence users (about 16.2%),",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,11,"and most precisely esti­ mated association (AME around 0.29, with a tight interval), suggesting that for routine, mainstream regular learners users, expanding institutional support channels translates efficiently into broader multi- domain gains. Class 1, study/assignment–focused and high-dependence users (about 16.2%), also exhibits a sizable positive association (AME about 0.24), consistent with the idea that structured, task-centered AI use can benefit from additional institutional scaffolding to broaden perceived capability gains beyond a single course context. Two smaller groups display similarly positive point estimates but with wider uncertainty, reflecting limited sample sizes rather than a weaker underlying relationship. Class 2, research-oriented heavy users (about 4.8%), has an AME around 0.26 with a notably wider interval, implying that the direction is clearly positive but the data are less informative about the exact magnitude. Class 4, low-dependence light users (about 12.1%), shows a comparable AME (about 0.26) with reduced precision; this is consistent with the interpret­ ation that even selective, lower-intensity users benefit from having more support options available, although heterogeneity within this subgroup makes the estimate less tight.Finally, Class 5, all-purpose multi-tool gen­",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,11,"AME (about 0.26) with reduced precision; this is consistent with the interpret­ ation that even selective, lower-intensity users benefit from having more support options available, although heterogeneity within this subgroup makes the estimate less tight.Finally, Class 5, all-purpose multi-tool gen­ eralists (about 8.1%), exhibits the smallest – but still meaningful – association (AME about 0.16). A plausible interpretation is that this group already operates with broader self-directed strategies and diversified tool repertoires, so adding another institutional support channel yields smaller marginal expansion in the number of reported improvement domains. Even so, the positive estimate indicates that institutional support breadth remains relevant for generalists, not only for study-centered users. Overall, the stratified models reinforce a simple message: in every profile, broader institutional support is associated with broader perceived gains, with the strongest and most precisely estimated association for mainstream regular learners and smaller – yet still substantive – marginal returns for multi-tool generalists. Figure 5. Stratified AME of support count by profile. 10 J. ZHANG ET AL.",166
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,12,"Differences in point estimates across profiles should be treated as suggestive, given overlapping uncertainty, unless confirmed by explicit cross-profile difference tests. 8. Between-profile difference testing To assess whether the association between SupportCount and learning gains differs across generative-AI user profiles – beyond visual comparisons of stratified estimates – we implement a bootstrap-based test of between-profile effect heterogeneity without fitting interaction terms. The comparison strategy has two layers. First, consistent with H2b, we evaluate a pre-specified theory-led comparison as the primary between-profile contrast: the difference in the SupportCount AME between mainstream regular learners (Class 3) and broad multi-tool generalists (Class 5). Second, we examine the complete set of pairwise profile contrasts as an exploratory robustness exercise. For each bootstrap replicate (b = 1, . . . , B), we resample the full analytic dataset with replacement, refit the same within-profile model for each profile g, and recompute the profile-specific SupportCount effect. The between-profile contrast is defined as Dg,h = AMEg −AMEh where AMEg denotes the average marginal effect of a one-unit increase in SupportCount on E[AbilitySum] within profile g (and bg",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,12,"for each profile g, and recompute the profile-specific SupportCount effect. The between-profile contrast is defined as Dg,h = AMEg −AMEh where AMEg denotes the average marginal effect of a one-unit increase in SupportCount on E[AbilitySum] within profile g (and bg is the corresponding model coefficient). Repeating this procedure over Breplicates yields an empirical sampling distribution for Dg,h. We report percentile-based 95% confidence intervals(2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the bootstrap distribution) and compute two-sided p-values using the bootstrap sign proportion. When evaluating all pairwise profile comparisons, we control the family-wise error rate using the Holm adjustment. Results are summarized in a table of Dg,h estimates with confidence intervals and adjusted p-values and visualized in a forest plot to facilitate interpretation of heterogeneity in the Sup­ portCount effect across profiles. Building on the profile-stratified AMEs, we first examined the primary theory-led contrast implied by H2b: the difference in the SupportCount effect between mainstream regular learners (Class 3) and broad multi- tool generalists (Class 5). As shown in Table 5 and Figure 6, evidence for between-profile heterogeneity con­ centrates in a single contrast:",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,12,"implied by H2b: the difference in the SupportCount effect between mainstream regular learners (Class 3) and broad multi- tool generalists (Class 5). As shown in Table 5 and Figure 6, evidence for between-profile heterogeneity con­ centrates in a single contrast: the marginal effect is larger for mainstream regular learners (Class 3) than for multi-tool generalists (Class 5) (DAME{3,5} = 0.136, 95% CI [0.039, 0.231], pHolm = 0.040), implying greater marginal returns to an additional institutional support modality for Class 3. All remaining pairwise differences are not statistically supported after Holm adjustment (confidence inter­ vals include zero and adjusted p-values are large). Directionally, several contrasts involving Class 5 are posi­ tive, suggesting a tendency toward smaller marginal gains for generalists, but these estimates are not precise enough to distinguish from zero under conservative family-wise error control. Taken together, the results indicate that SupportCount is beneficial within each profile, while defensible between-profile differences are modest and primarily driven by the Class 3–Class 5 contrast. 9. Robustness checks We conducted a machine-learning robustness check using XGBoost with accumulated local effects (ALE) to verify",180
