DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,13,"parametric functional-form assumptions. The main analysis relies on a structured regression framework that yields interpretable marginal effects, but it necessarily imposes a specific link function and a relatively con­ strained relationship between predictors and the outcome. In contrast, XGBoost is a high-capacity nonpara­ metric learner that can flexibly capture nonlinearities and complex interactions among covariates without pre-specifying their forms. To retain interpretability under this flexible model, we use ALE to summarize the average local contribution of SupportCount to the model-predicted outcome across the observed data distribution, which is particularly suitable when predictors are correlated because it reduces extrapol­ ation bias relative to partial dependence approaches. If the SupportCount–AbilitySum relationship remains monotonically increasing in the ALE profile, this provides convergent evidence that the direction and quali­ tative shape of the main finding persist under a substantially different modeling strategy, strengthening the claim that the result is robust to alternative (nonparametric) specifications. The resulting ALE profile provides clear evidence of a robust positive pattern (Table 6 and Figure 7). Across the observed support levels, the ALE for SupportCount increases monotonically from −0.3888",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,13,"claim that the result is robust to alternative (nonparametric) specifications. The resulting ALE profile provides clear evidence of a robust positive pattern (Table 6 and Figure 7). Across the observed support levels, the ALE for SupportCount increases monotonically from −0.3888 at Support­ Count = 1 to 0.6491 at SupportCount = 4, with intermediate values of 0.1097 (SupportCount = 2) and 0.3201 (SupportCount = 3). Substantively, this means that, holding the joint distribution of the remaining predictors as observed in the data, individuals with broader institutional learning support are predicted by the flexible XGBoost model to have higher AbilitySum relative to the sample-average prediction. The sign change from negative at SupportCount = 1 to positive at SupportCount ≥ 2 indicates that moving away from the lowest observed support level is associated with a shift from below-average to above- average predicted learning gains, reinforcing the main conclusion that support breadth is beneficial. In addition to confirming the direction of the association, the curve shape reveals that the relationship is not purely linear. The largest local change occurs between SupportCount = 1 and",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,13,"gains, reinforcing the main conclusion that support breadth is beneficial. In addition to confirming the direction of the association, the curve shape reveals that the relationship is not purely linear. The largest local change occurs between SupportCount = 1 and 2 (ΔALE ≈ 0.50), followed by a smaller gain from 2 to 3 (ΔALE ≈ 0.21) and a further increase from 3 to 4 (ΔALE ≈ 0.33). This pattern suggests that the marginal benefit of additional support may be stage-dependent, with particularly Figure 6. Between–profile difference in SupportCount effect Table 6. ALE SupportCount. .type .value SupportCount ale −0.3888 1 ale 0.1097 2 ale 0.3201 3 ale 0.6491 4 12 J. ZHANG ET AL.",113
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,14,"pronounced improvements when moving from minimal support to at least moderate support, and contin­ ued – though non-uniform – gains thereafter. Taken together, the monotonic upward ALE profile under a high-capacity nonparametric learner provides convergent evidence that the SupportCount–AbilitySum association observed in the main analysis is robust to functional-form assumptions, while also indicating potential nonlinearity in how incremental support breadth is associated with predicted learning gains. 10. Discussion Viewing GenAI as an ILE, this study provides clear support for H1: different GenAI use architectures are related to systematic differences in students’ perceived capability development. This result is consistent with SRL research, which emphasizes that learning outcomes depend on how learners organize and regulate their learning processes, not just the use of tools. We also found support for H2a: institutional support is posi­ tively related to capability development, consistent with research in the field of ILE emphasizing the impor­ tance of dialogic interaction, feedback and structured scaffolding in supporting learning. For H2b, the results show heterogeneity: the effect of institutional support varies according to learner profiles, and is more effective when",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,14,"in the field of ILE emphasizing the impor­ tance of dialogic interaction, feedback and structured scaffolding in supporting learning. For H2b, the results show heterogeneity: the effect of institutional support varies according to learner profiles, and is more effective when it complements self-regulated or co-regulated practices than when AI is used for substitution. This research transcends the logic that “the more you use, the greater the benefit” and offers a more nuanced understanding. The results support H1, H2a and partially H2b, showing that capability development depends not so much on usage intensity as on the student’s use architecture – the combination of purpose, tool repertoires, and reliance orientations. These findings clarify conflicting evidence by correcting fre­ quency-based interpretations; unlike earlier studies focused on the average effect, this work expands the study of heterogeneity, where disciplinary differences and reliance patterns as configurations are more infor­ mative than isolated factors. The results also show a link between institutional scaffolding and perceived gains, following the person-environment fit model: the marginal returns of mainstream regular learners are stronger than for multi-tool generalists. Theoretically, use",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,14,"configurations are more infor­ mative than isolated factors. The results also show a link between institutional scaffolding and perceived gains, following the person-environment fit model: the marginal returns of mainstream regular learners are stronger than for multi-tool generalists. Theoretically, use architecture links SRL with the scaffolding–sub­ stitution distinction through practice configurations. Several limitations point to clear directions for future work. First, the cross-sectional design precludes strong causal inference; longitudinal and quasi-experimental studies are needed, and convenience sampling at a single university limits generalizability. Second, the outcome reflects perceived rather than objectively verified capability, which may introduce bias. In subsequent studies, self-reported measures can be paired Figure 7. ALE of SupportCount on predicted AbilitySum. INTERACTIVE LEARNING ENVIRONMENTS 13",117
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,15,"with objective indicators, such as performance or process data. Third, SupportCount measures the breadth of support, not the quality. A separate analysis of support types identifies the most effective mechanisms. Finally, replication across contexts would help clarify when GenAI functions as a learning tool rather than primarily an output tool. 11. Conclusion This study demonstrates that students’ capability gains from generative AI are shaped less by how often they use AI than by how they use it. Identifying use architectures defined by purpose, tool ecosystems, and reliance and showing that these predict capability development beyond usage intensity, the findings show that engagement patterns matter more than frequency. Institutional learning support is also positively associated with broader capability gains across profiles, yet its benefits are not uniform: mainstream regular learners show stronger returns than multi-tool generalists, suggesting that uniform scaffolding may be ineffi­ cient. Conceptually, use architecture extends SRL and shifts the focus from usage intensity to how students engage with AI. Together, these results support two practical conclusions for higher education: institutions should focus on cultivating higher-quality AI-supported learning practices",180
DOC_017,Generative AI use profiles and academic outcomes in higher education  the role of institutional support.pdf,10.1080/10494820.2026.2668610,15,"be ineffi­ cient. Conceptually, use architecture extends SRL and shifts the focus from usage intensity to how students engage with AI. Together, these results support two practical conclusions for higher education: institutions should focus on cultivating higher-quality AI-supported learning practices rather than merely increasing usage, and they should implement tiered support systems that provide scalable guidance for mainstream learners while offering more advanced, discipline-specific resources for highly proficient users. Consent to participate Informed consent was obtained from all participants involved in the study. Disclosure statement No potential conflict of interest was reported by the author(s). Funding This study was supported by the Hubei Provincial Department of Education through the Provincial Teaching Reform Research Projects for undergraduate institutions, including “Interdisciplinary Integrated Teaching Practice of a General AI Course Based on Large Language Models” (Project No. 2024314, 2024) and “Artificial Intelligence Reshaping the Teach­ ing System of Higher Education” (Project No. 2025303, 2025). Ethics approval This study was approved by the Academic Ethics Committee of Wuhan Institute of Technology (Approval No. WIT-3- 2026-10). Data availability statement All data generated or analyzed during",180
