DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,2,"ADULT & CONTINUING EDUCATION | RESEARCH ARTICLE Generative artificial intelligence in higher education: Students’ journey through opportunities, challenges, and the horizons of academic transformation Amani Abdullah Bin Jwair Department of Curriculum and Instruction, Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, Al-Kharj, Saudi Arabia ABSTRACT This study examined university students’ perceptions of generative artificial intelli- gence (Gen-AI) in supporting learning and academic achievement, using a descriptive quantitative method. Data were collected from a sample of 1,537 students across vari- ous academic disciplines and levels. Results revealed generally positive attitudes, with a high overall mean score (M ¼ 3.72), reflecting Gen-AI’s perceived benefits in enhanc- ing motivation, collaboration, academic performance, and decision-making. However, concerns were also reported (M ¼ 3.87), including data privacy, limited Arabic- language tools, and a lack of technical support. Statistically significant differences (p < 0.05) were observed across gender, academic stage, field of specialization, and computer experience. Male and diploma-level students reported the highest levels of engagement with Gen-AI tools. This study contributes to the growing body of research by providing empirical insights into how diverse student populations per- ceive",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,2,"stage, field of specialization, and computer experience. Male and diploma-level students reported the highest levels of engagement with Gen-AI tools. This study contributes to the growing body of research by providing empirical insights into how diverse student populations per- ceive Gen-AI in higher education. The findings highlight the need for tailored training, ethical guidelines, and inclusive policies to support responsible AI integration in education. ARTICLE HISTORY Received 25 July 2025 Revised 18 October 2025 Accepted 10 November 2025 KEYWORDS Generative artificial intelligence; technological ethics; higher education; pedagogical practices; digital literacy SUBJECTS Social Sciences; Education; Higher Education; Social Sciences; Education; Higher Education; Study of Higher Education; Social Sciences; Education; Higher Education; Teaching & Learning 1. Introduction Integrating Gen-AI into higher education is increasingly recognized as essential, particularly within the con- text of Saudi Arabia’s commitment to educational innovation and excellence (Al Balushi, 2024; Allen & Seaman, 2017). AI technologies have expanded educational access and enhanced flexible learning approaches tailored to diverse learner needs (Igbokwe, 2023). The increasing efficiency and accessibility of Gen-AI tools have accelerated their adoption, facilitating academic tasks such",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,2,"Balushi, 2024; Allen & Seaman, 2017). AI technologies have expanded educational access and enhanced flexible learning approaches tailored to diverse learner needs (Igbokwe, 2023). The increasing efficiency and accessibility of Gen-AI tools have accelerated their adoption, facilitating academic tasks such as drafting, planning, and error correction, thereby improving the efficiency of educational processes (Kim et al., 2025; Simelane & Kittur, 2025). Furthermore, AI-supported educational tools enable personalized learning by identifying indi- vidual students’ strengths and weaknesses and providing precise performance assessments (Chen et al., 2022; Qushem et al., 2021). The capability of Gen-AI to simulate human interactions further enhances the creation of tailored learning experiences and dynamic educational content, promoting the practical appli- cation of knowledge in real-world contexts (Liu et al., 2022; Mao et al., 2024; Roll & Wylie, 2016). Interactive technologies such as chatbots, virtual assistants, and adaptive learning systems have also proven effective in fostering engaging and interactive learning environments (Warschauer, 2004). Consequently, universities are progressively prioritizing the integration of AI to address contemporary educational challenges, with Gen-AI emerging as a transformative tool capable of reshaping both",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,2,"learning systems have also proven effective in fostering engaging and interactive learning environments (Warschauer, 2004). Consequently, universities are progressively prioritizing the integration of AI to address contemporary educational challenges, with Gen-AI emerging as a transformative tool capable of reshaping both teaching CONTACT Amani Abdullah Bin Jwair a.binjwair@psau.edu.sa Department of Curriculum and Instruction, Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, Al-Kharj, Saudi Arabia This article has been corrected with minor changes. These changes do not impact the academic content of the article.  2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. The terms on which this article has been published allow the posting of the Accepted Manuscript in a repository by the author(s) or with their consent. COGENT EDUCATION 2025, VOL. 12, NO. 1, 2589495 https://doi.org/10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495",163
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,3,"methodologies and research environments (Cope & Kalantzis, 2015; Lin & Chen, 2024; Luckin & Holmes 2016). Moving forward, the trajectory of AI adoption in academia will be shaped by ongoing dialogues aimed at maximizing its benefits while addressing and mitigating associated risks (Banh & Strobel, 2023). Despite these benefits, the widespread adoption of Gen-AI in higher education raises significant con- cerns. Ethical challenges include the potential for AI tools like ChatGPT to generate inappropriate or inaccurate content and to inadvertently encourage academic dishonesty, including plagiarism (Chen et al., 2020). Additionally, there is an apprehension regarding overreliance on AI, which may diminish critical human skills such as creativity, critical thinking, and collaboration (Siham & Sophia, 2024). Privacy and data security issues also necessitate robust protection mechanisms to safeguard personal informa- tion collected through AI-driven educational activities (Siham & Sophia, 2024). Moreover, institutional concerns about transparency and the integrity of academic practices present ongoing challenges in inte- grating AI tools responsibly (Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). As Gen-AI technologies, encompassing language models, image generators, and programming assistants, gain prominence,",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,3,"institutional concerns about transparency and the integrity of academic practices present ongoing challenges in inte- grating AI tools responsibly (Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). As Gen-AI technologies, encompassing language models, image generators, and programming assistants, gain prominence, it is critical to investigate how students engage with these tools and the consequent impact on their learning experiences (Sousa & Cardoso, 2025). Understanding students’ perceptions and attitudes toward Gen-AI is essential to inform strategic educational policies and ensure the responsible incorporation of AI technologies (Wu et al., 2020). The rapid evolution of Gen-AI presents promising opportunities to enhance educational outcomes and foster innovation within higher education institutions (Jain & Raghuram, 2024). Despite numerous studies examining AI applications across various countries, including the UK (Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Kim et al., 2025), Cambodia (Sreylet, 2025), Morocco (Haroud & Saqri, 2025), Kuwait (Al-Huwail et al., 2025), Oman (Alshamy et al., 2025), Hong Kong, and India (Chan & Hu, 2023), there remains a paucity of research focusing specifically on Saudi Arabian University students’ perspec- tives. This gap constrains a comprehensive understanding of",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,3,"Kuwait (Al-Huwail et al., 2025), Oman (Alshamy et al., 2025), Hong Kong, and India (Chan & Hu, 2023), there remains a paucity of research focusing specifically on Saudi Arabian University students’ perspec- tives. This gap constrains a comprehensive understanding of the nuanced attitudes toward Gen-AI adop- tion in the Saudi higher education context. Furthermore, prior research highlights demographic and disciplinary differences influencing AI’s edu- cational impact (Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Kim et al., 2025). Despite growing interest in Gen-AI tools, add- itional empirical studies are needed to evaluate their effectiveness across academic disciplines and course levels (Simelane & Kittur, 2025). In response to these research gaps, the present study aims to explore both the positive and negative effects of Gen-AI on students’ academic tasks and learning expe- riences, considering diverse academic backgrounds and levels. Following recommendations by Su and Yang (2023), this study will examine the benefits and chal- lenges of Gen-AI, its ethical implications, and its contextual effectiveness. Focusing on Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, the study seeks to generate contextually grounded insights that can guide the development of",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,3,"(2023), this study will examine the benefits and chal- lenges of Gen-AI, its ethical implications, and its contextual effectiveness. Focusing on Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, the study seeks to generate contextually grounded insights that can guide the development of AI integration strategies tailored to the Saudi higher education system. The study’s larger sample size further enhances its potential to inform effective policy-making and educational innovation across the Kingdom. This study seeks to answer the central question: How do students at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University perceive the opportunities and challenges related to the adoption of generative artificial intelligence (Gen-AI) for supporting learning and academic achievement? To address this, the study will explore the following specific questions: 1. What positive benefits and opportunities do students at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University asso- ciate with the use of Gen-AI in enhancing learning and academic performance? 2. What concerns and challenges do students perceive regarding the adoption of Gen-AI to support their learning and academic success? 3. Are there statistically significant differences in students’ perceptions of Gen-AI adoption measured at the 0.05",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,3,"learning and academic performance? 2. What concerns and challenges do students perceive regarding the adoption of Gen-AI to support their learning and academic success? 3. Are there statistically significant differences in students’ perceptions of Gen-AI adoption measured at the 0.05 significance level based on gender, academic discipline? 2. Theoretical framework 2.1. Generative AI in higher education: Opportunities and challenges Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) is rapidly transforming higher education through tools capable of generating essays, solving problems, translating languages, and producing multimedia content (Lim 2 A. A. B. JWAIR",88
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"et al., 2023). Technologies like ChatGPT are increasingly adopted by university students to support writ- ing, streamline research, and receive feedback (Chen et al., 2022; Eden et al., 2024; Johnston et al., 2024). In this study, Gen-AI refers to AI systems that mimic human cognition and are used by Saudi uni- versity students to enhance academic performance (Mustafa, 2015). Scholars emphasize Gen-AI’s potential to personalize learning, promote inclusivity, and support stu- dent autonomy (Al Balushi, 2024; Allen & Seaman, 2017; Lin & Chen, 2024; Simelane & Kittur, 2025; Song et al., 2025). Research also shows students use AI for grammar correction, citation support, and idea gen- eration, reducing anxiety and improving confidence (Johnston et al., 2024; Kırelli, 2023). Moreover, AI- enabled platforms can offer more adaptive and learner-centered instruction (Derakhshan, 2025). However, challenges remain. Key concerns include academic integrity risks, such as plagiarism and blurred authorship (Charles et al., 2025; Cotton et al., 2024), as well as ethical issues like bias, data privacy violations, and lack of transparency (Samala & Rawas, 2025). Other barriers include digital literacy gaps, implementation costs,",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"such as plagiarism and blurred authorship (Charles et al., 2025; Cotton et al., 2024), as well as ethical issues like bias, data privacy violations, and lack of transparency (Samala & Rawas, 2025). Other barriers include digital literacy gaps, implementation costs, and faculty resistance due to fears of diminished critical thinking and creativity (Al-Huwail et al., 2025; Alshamy et al., 2025; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). Fears of role displacement further highlight tensions between AI and traditional teaching models (Haroud & Saqri, 2025; Su & Yang, 2023). These issues point to the urgent need for clear policies, training, and ethical oversight in Gen-AI’s educational integration. 2.2. Student perceptions of Gen-AI: Global and regional perspectives Empirical studies from diverse educational contexts provide valuable insights into how students perceive and engage with Gen-AI. For instance, Sreylet (2025) found that Cambodian university students reported using Gen-AI tools for independent learning and group projects, although they also raised concerns about access, security, and data privacy. Similarly, Al-Huwail et al. (2025) documented a wide range of student and academic experiences with generative AI, highlighting benefits such as",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"Gen-AI tools for independent learning and group projects, although they also raised concerns about access, security, and data privacy. Similarly, Al-Huwail et al. (2025) documented a wide range of student and academic experiences with generative AI, highlighting benefits such as increased efficiency and personalized learning, as well as concerns around ethical ambiguity, academic integrity, and techno- logical reliability. In the UK, Kim et al. (2025) noted significant differences in Gen-AI usage across gender and academic disciplines, while also observing generally low usage rates, suggesting a need for targeted awareness and training programs. In the Middle Eastern context, recent research has begun to explore how cultural and institutional factors shape students’ attitudes toward Gen-AI. For example, Al-Huwail et al. (2025) conducted a mixed- methods study at Kuwait University and found that while students recognized the educational value of AI tools, they also expressed concerns about data privacy, implementation costs, and ethical risks. Alshamy et al. (2025), in a study at Sultan Qaboos University, reported frequent AI use among students and academics for academic support and innovation, yet noted apprehension about overreliance",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"also expressed concerns about data privacy, implementation costs, and ethical risks. Alshamy et al. (2025), in a study at Sultan Qaboos University, reported frequent AI use among students and academics for academic support and innovation, yet noted apprehension about overreliance and its impact on academic integrity. These findings are echoed by Haroud and Saqri (2025), who found that while Moroccan students viewed AI as a valuable tool for creativity and feedback, faculty members remained skeptical about its effect on critical thinking and digital literacy. Further contributing to this discourse, Arowosegbe et al. (2024) reported that 61% of students in UK uni- versities were familiar with Gen-AI, and more than half had used it. However, concerns about institutional policies, plagiarism, and data protection were widespread. In Serbia, Kuleto et al. (2021) noted that students appreciated the potential of AI to foster collaboration and enhance skill development, suggesting a strong link between AI integration and educational accessibility. Meanwhile, Su and Yang (2023), in a theoretical review, emphasized both the benefits and challenges of AI tools like ChatGPT in educational contexts, call- ing",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"collaboration and enhance skill development, suggesting a strong link between AI integration and educational accessibility. Meanwhile, Su and Yang (2023), in a theoretical review, emphasized both the benefits and challenges of AI tools like ChatGPT in educational contexts, call- ing for more context-specific research on their ethical and pedagogical implications. In light of this growing body of literature, the present study contributes new insights by focusing on the underrepresented context of Saudi Arabia. It explores students’ perceptions of Gen-AI at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, examining both the enabling and limiting factors that influence how students engage with these technologies. With a large and diverse sample size (N ¼ 1,537), the study offers robust generalizability and captures variations in perception across gender, academic level, and specialization. Unlike previous research that often focused narrowly on either benefits or risks, this study adopts a balanced perspective, investigating both the positive academic impacts and ethical concerns associated with Gen-AI use. In doing so, it provides actionable insights for policymakers, educators, and institutional leaders seeking to integrate AI in higher education responsibly and effectively.",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,4,"adopts a balanced perspective, investigating both the positive academic impacts and ethical concerns associated with Gen-AI use. In doing so, it provides actionable insights for policymakers, educators, and institutional leaders seeking to integrate AI in higher education responsibly and effectively. COGENT EDUCATION 3",43
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,5,"3. Methodology The current study relied on the quantitative, descriptive approach, which relies on studying reality or a phenomenon as it exists in reality and describing it accurately. This approach is expressed qualitatively or quantitatively. Qualitative expression describes the phenomenon and clarifies its characteristics, while quantitative expression describes it numerically, demonstrating its magnitude or size and the degree of its correlation with other phenomena. 3.1. Participants The current study population consists of all male and female students enrolled in the colleges of Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz during the second semester of the academic year 1446/2025. After receiving eth- ical approval from the Scientific Research Committee at PSA University No. SCBR-516/2025, on 24/08/ 2025, data collection began on 25/08/2025 and was completed on 2/09/2025. The participants were invited to participate in this survey. Verbal consent to participate in the study was obtained from all par- ticipants. The survey was distributed via official email to all male and female students in the university’s colleges. They were allowed to choose the appropriate time and place to complete the electronic survey, facilitating their freedom",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,5,"obtained from all par- ticipants. The survey was distributed via official email to all male and female students in the university’s colleges. They were allowed to choose the appropriate time and place to complete the electronic survey, facilitating their freedom to express their opinions (Robson, 2002). The number of responses received was 1,537 questionnaires, all of which were completed. Table 1 shows a description of the study sample according to the basic data related to the study, most notably gender, field of specialization, academic level, and university stage. As shown in Table 1, the study sample accurately reflected the characteristics of the target popula- tion. Normality tests using the Shapiro–Wilk, Kolmogorov–Smirnov Z, and Levene’s statistics revealed non-normal distribution (p < 0.05) for the variables of gender and field of specialization, prompting the use of non-parametric methods for these groups. In contrast, academic stage and experience showed normal distribution (p > 0.05), justifying the use of parametric analyses for these variables in addressing the third research question. 3.2. Research instrument The data for this study were collected using a researcher-designed questionnaire,",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,5,"contrast, academic stage and experience showed normal distribution (p > 0.05), justifying the use of parametric analyses for these variables in addressing the third research question. 3.2. Research instrument The data for this study were collected using a researcher-designed questionnaire, developed with reference to prior studies (e.g. Haroud & Saqri, 2025; Sreylet, 2025). The instrument comprised two main sections. The first section gathered demographic information, including gender, academic degree, field of specialization, and academic level. The second section addressed two core dimen- sions: perceived benefits and opportunities (13 items), and perceived concerns and challenges (10 items) related to the adoption of Generative AI (Gen-AI) in supporting learning and academic achievement. Responses were measured using a five-point Likert scale ranging from “strongly agree” to “strongly disagree.” Table 1. Description of the study sample according to basic data. Variable Category Frequency Percentage Gender Male 772 50.2% Female 765 49.8% Specialization Administration 381 24.8% Educational 370 24.1% Medicine 227 14.7% Science 199 13% Engineering 360 23.4% Experience of computer Beginner 883 57.4% advanced 462 30.1% Expert 192 12.5% academic Stage Diploma 321 20.9%",180
