DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,6,"3.2.1. Validity To ensure content validity, the initial 26-item questionnaire was reviewed by six experts in curricula and teaching methods, educational technology, and educational measurement. Reviewers assessed the instrument’s relevance to the study objectives, clarity of instructions and items, and the alignment of items with their respective themes. They also provided suggestions for the modification items. Based on their feedback, three items were removed, resulting in a final version of the questionnaire comprising 23 items. After confirming the apparent validity of the study tool, the researcher applied it in the field to a sur- vey sample of 40 male and female students from the study community. Internal consistency was tested by calculating the Pearson correlation coefficient between the score of each statement and the total score of its theme, as shown in Table 2. Table 2 shows that all correlation coefficients between the paragraph and its axis are statistically sig- nificant at a significance level of (a ¼ 0.001) or less. This indicates high consistency between the state- ments and their axis, which reassures the researcher of the validity of",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,6,"coefficients between the paragraph and its axis are statistically sig- nificant at a significance level of (a ¼ 0.001) or less. This indicates high consistency between the state- ments and their axis, which reassures the researcher of the validity of the questionnaire. 3.2.2. Reliability Table 3 presents the reliability coefficients for the responses of the study sample using Cronbach’s alpha method (Cronbach, 1951) to assess the internal consistency of the research instrument. As shown in Table 3, all Cronbach’s alpha coefficients exceed the commonly accepted threshold of 0.7 (Field, 2017), indicating satisfactory reliability for each axis as well as for the questionnaire as a whole. These findings confirm that the instrument demonstrates adequate internal consistency and is reliable for measuring students’ perceptions regarding the adoption of generative AI to support learning and academic achievement. 4. Results and discussion 4.1. Answering the main research question This study explored Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University students’ perceptions of the opportunities and challenges of adopting Generative AI (Gen-AI) in academic contexts. Descriptive statistics, including weighted means and standard deviations, were used to analyze key",180
