DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,7,"As indicated in Table 4, the overall mean score (M ¼ 3.79, SD ¼ 1.947) reflects a generally high level of awareness and engagement with Gen-AI technologies. Students expressed recognition of Gen-AI’s potential benefits (M ¼ 3.72, SD ¼ 1.929), including improved learning efficiency, real-time academic sup- port, and enhanced autonomy—findings that align with prior research highlighting the educational advantages of Gen-AI (Johnston et al., 2024; Lin & Chen, 2024; Simelane & Kittur, 2025). These results support the theoretical framework suggesting that Gen-AI enables more adaptive, inclusive, and student-centered learning environments (Al Balushi, 2024; Derakhshan, 2025). However, students also expressed strong concerns (M ¼ 3.87, SD ¼ 1.967), particularly regarding eth- ical risks, data privacy, potential academic dishonesty, and overdependence on AI tools. These concerns echo the warnings raised in recent scholarship about the challenges of Gen-AI implementation in educa- tional settings (Charles et al., 2025; Cotton et al., 2024; Samala & Rawas, 2025). The slight predominance of concerns over perceived benefits in the current study contrasts with some findings in the literature, such as Arowosegbe et al. (2024), where",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,7,"tional settings (Charles et al., 2025; Cotton et al., 2024; Samala & Rawas, 2025). The slight predominance of concerns over perceived benefits in the current study contrasts with some findings in the literature, such as Arowosegbe et al. (2024), where students prioritized the advantages of AI integration. This dis- crepancy may reflect contextual differences in institutional readiness, policy frameworks, or cultural atti- tudes toward technology in the Saudi higher education landscape. This study aligns with regional research (e.g. Al-Huwail et al., 2025; Alshamy et al., 2025), revealing that Gulf students view Gen-AI as both a valuable academic tool and a source of concern. Their cautious optimism underscores the need for institutional policies, ethical guidelines, and targeted training. These findings not only support global trends but also offer context-specific insights to help educators and policymakers balance opportunities and risks in Gen-AI integration. 4.1.1. First sub-question To answer the sub-question, “What are the positive benefits and opportunities associated with adopting generative AI (Gen-AI) to support learning and academic achievement from the perspective of students at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University?” Descriptive statistics",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,7,"integration. 4.1.1. First sub-question To answer the sub-question, “What are the positive benefits and opportunities associated with adopting generative AI (Gen-AI) to support learning and academic achievement from the perspective of students at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University?” Descriptive statistics were computed for each item under the first axis of the study. Specifically, weighted means and standard deviations were calculated to deter- mine the level of agreement and variability in students’ perceptions. The detailed results are summarized in Table 5. As shown in Table 5, the overall mean score for the “Benefits and Opportunities of Gen-AI” axis was high (M ¼ 3.72, SD ¼ 1.929), indicating that students generally perceived Gen-AI technologies as advan- tageous tools for enhancing academic performance and supporting various aspects of learning. All indi- vidual items in this dimension also received high mean scores, demonstrating broad consensus regarding the perceived benefits. Students reported that Gen-AI technologies helped them complete academic tasks more efficiently (M ¼ 3.36), transition from passive recipients to active knowledge seekers (M ¼ 3.66), and enhance their critical thinking skills (M ¼ 3.69).",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,7,"consensus regarding the perceived benefits. Students reported that Gen-AI technologies helped them complete academic tasks more efficiently (M ¼ 3.36), transition from passive recipients to active knowledge seekers (M ¼ 3.66), and enhance their critical thinking skills (M ¼ 3.69). Other highly rated benefits included increased motivation to learn (M ¼ 3.82), facilitated decision-making based on task demands (M ¼ 3.85), and personalized learning sup- port that considered individual differences (M ¼ 3.82). Furthermore, students appreciated the role of Gen-AI in reducing psychological barriers such as shyness (M ¼ 3.66), promoting collaboration (M ¼ 3.64), and providing real-time feedback and learning tracking (M ¼ 3.78). Notably, expert systems were also seen as valuable for offering guidance and academic advising (M ¼ 3.82). These findings align with a growing body of literature that emphasizes the transformative potential of Gen-AI in higher education. Prior studies (e.g. Al-Huwail et al., 2025; Alshamy et al., 2025; Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Haroud & Saqri, 2025; Sousa & Cardoso, 2025; Sreylet, 2025) have reported that students across diverse educational settings view Gen-AI as a valuable tool",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,7,"education. Prior studies (e.g. Al-Huwail et al., 2025; Alshamy et al., 2025; Arowosegbe et al., 2024; Haroud & Saqri, 2025; Sousa & Cardoso, 2025; Sreylet, 2025) have reported that students across diverse educational settings view Gen-AI as a valuable tool for independent learning, personalized instruction, and collaborative engagement. For instance, Johnston et al. (2024) and Simelane and Kittur (2025) highlight AI’s ability to enhance student autonomy and self-regulation by offering personalized, Table 4. Mean and standard deviation for each of the questionnaire axes. s Axis Weighted mean Standard deviation Rank Level 1 Benefits and Opportunities of GEN-AI 3.72 1.929 2 High 2 GEN-AI Concerns 3.87 1.967 1 High Overall arithmetic mean of the questionnaire 3.79 1.947 – High 6 A. A. B. JWAIR",123
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,8,"adaptive learning environments and real-time feedback. Similarly, Lin and Chen (2024) emphasize AI’s contribution to reducing writing anxiety and fostering academic confidence, especially through functions such as grammar correction and citation formatting. In the Middle Eastern context, studies such as Alshamy et al. (2025) and Haroud and Saqri (2025) have also documented positive student perceptions of Gen-AI’s capacity to improve academic support, engagement, and digital innovation, while recognizing its role in addressing specific cultural or institu- tional needs. The current findings reinforce this regional trend, offering further evidence that students in Saudi higher education institutions view Gen-AI not merely as a technological novelty but as a meaning- ful pedagogical resource. However, it is worth noting that while the present study reveals overwhelmingly positive attitudes, this perspective may not be universally shared. For example, Charles et al. (2025) observed student ambivalence toward Gen-AI’s use in formal assessment settings, citing concerns about overreliance, fair- ness, and academic integrity. Nonetheless, the high mean scores across all items in this study suggest that, at least within the context of Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University,",180
