DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,9,"calculated for each item within the second axis of the study. The detailed results are presented in Table 6. Table 6 reveals a high overall mean score (M ¼ 3.87, SD ¼ 1.967), indicating that students perceive substantial challenges related to the integration of Generative AI (Gen-AI) into their academic environ- ments. The most significant concern was unfamiliarity with Gen-AI terminology (M ¼ 4.09, SD ¼ 1.219), followed closely by limited technical support, general usability issues, and the lack of Arabic-language applications. These high scores across all items suggest that, despite potential interest, students encoun- ter consistent barriers that hinder effective adoption—both technical (e.g. infrastructure and support) and cognitive (e.g. knowledge and awareness). These findings align with prior studies in regional and international contexts. Al-Huwail et al. (2025) and Haroud and Saqri (2025) identified similar barriers, especially inadequate infrastructure and the absence of localized tools, as central challenges to Gen-AI integration in Middle Eastern higher educa- tion. The concern about Arabic-language limitations echoes their findings and emphasizes the digital inequities faced by non-English-speaking students. Moreover, the lack of foundational knowledge",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,9,"the absence of localized tools, as central challenges to Gen-AI integration in Middle Eastern higher educa- tion. The concern about Arabic-language limitations echoes their findings and emphasizes the digital inequities faced by non-English-speaking students. Moreover, the lack of foundational knowledge aligns with Sousa and Cardoso (2025), who highlighted digital literacy as a critical factor in effective AI use, and with Charles et al. (2025), who raised concerns about the risks of misuse and academic dishonesty due to poor understanding. Broader ethical concerns, such as data privacy and algorithmic trust, mirror findings by Samala and Rawas (2025), reinforcing the need for ethical frameworks. As Farrelly and Baker (2023) argue, without institutional readiness and strategic planning, technological adoption risks falling short of its educational promise—leading instead to frustration and disengagement. 4.1.3. Third sub-question To address the third sub-question, the researcher first tested for normality of data distribution. The Shapiro–Wilk test, along with the Kolmogorov–Smirnov Z test and Levene’s statistic, was used to assess the distribution across the relevant variables. Results indicated non-normal distribution (p < 0.05) for the variables of gender and",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,9,"for normality of data distribution. The Shapiro–Wilk test, along with the Kolmogorov–Smirnov Z test and Levene’s statistic, was used to assess the distribution across the relevant variables. Results indicated non-normal distribution (p < 0.05) for the variables of gender and field of specialization; thus, non-parametric statistical methods were employed for their analysis. In contrast, academic stage and experience showed normal distribution (p > 0.05), justi- fying the use of parametric methods for these variables in analyzing the third sub-question. 4.1.3.1. Differences by gender. The researcher utilized the Mann–Whitney U test to assess differences between gender groups. The Kolmogorov–Smirnov Z test (4.864, p ¼ 0.001) confirmed a non-normal data distribution. The results are detailed in Table 7. The findings from Table 7 demonstrate a consistent and statistically significant trend in which male students exhibit more positive and engaged attitudes toward Generative AI (Gen-AI) across all measured dimensions, including benefits, opportunities, concerns, and challenges. Specifically, male students reported a significantly higher mean rank (958.7) on the “Benefits and Opportunities” axis compared to female students (577.6), indicating that males are more likely to",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,9,"AI (Gen-AI) across all measured dimensions, including benefits, opportunities, concerns, and challenges. Specifically, male students reported a significantly higher mean rank (958.7) on the “Benefits and Opportunities” axis compared to female students (577.6), indicating that males are more likely to perceive Gen-AI as advantageous in sup- porting academic tasks. Likewise, in the “Concerns and Challenges” dimension, male students also showed greater awareness and engagement (M ¼ 942.7) than females (M ¼ 593.8). The overall higher mean rank of male students (874.3) suggests a more favorable, confident, and nuanced understanding of Gen-AI. These statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) reinforce the robustness of the observed gender disparity in attitudes toward Gen-AI in the academic context. Table 6. The mean and SD of responses on concerns and challenges of using generative AI. Axis Weighted mean Standard deviation Rank Level 14 Lack of trained personnel in the use of Gen-AI technologies 3.81 1.489 10 High 15 Poor awareness of Gen-AI technologies among students 3.86 1.491 5 High 16 Poor infrastructure (wireless communications, computers, and software) 3.83 1.498 7 High 17 Poor suitability of",180
