DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,10,"When compared with previous research, the present findings both align with and diverge from estab- lished literature. In agreement with Kim et al. (2025), this study supports the notion that male stu- dents—particularly those in STEM-oriented academic environments—tend to exhibit higher levels of engagement and self-efficacy with technology, including AI tools. Kim et al. (2025) observed similar gender-based differences among UK university students, attributing them to sociocultural conditioning, academic exposure, and confidence in digital environments. However, the current findings contrast with those of Al-Huwail et al. (2025), who reported no significant gender-based differences in Gen-AI percep- tions at a university in Kuwait. This discrepancy may be attributed to contextual differences such as insti- tutional culture, curriculum emphasis, or the proportional representation of genders across disciplines, indicating that the influence of gender on AI perceptions is not universal but shaped by broader envi- ronmental and educational variables. These results should be interpreted as reflective of systemic and contextual influences rather than fixed or innate gender traits. Cultural norms, disciplinary affiliation, and educational exposure likely shape students’ familiarity and comfort with Gen-AI",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,10,"broader envi- ronmental and educational variables. These results should be interpreted as reflective of systemic and contextual influences rather than fixed or innate gender traits. Cultural norms, disciplinary affiliation, and educational exposure likely shape students’ familiarity and comfort with Gen-AI tools. Male students’ overrepresentation in technology-related fields may explain their heightened perception of both the opportunities and risks associated with Gen-AI. As Kim et al. (2025) note, increased confidence and self-efficacy among male stu- dents contribute to their greater willingness to explore emerging technologies. To address this disparity, educational institutions should consider implementing inclusive strategies such as AI literacy workshops, mentorship programs, and curriculum integration tailored to female students, particularly in non-STEM fields. These interventions can help level the technological playing field and ensure equitable access and engagement with Gen-AI tools across genders. 4.1.3.2. Differences by academic stage. The researcher used an analysis of variance (ANOVA) to deter- mine the differences between groups according to academic level, and the Scheffe test for post hoc comparisons. The results of the Shapiro-Wilk test were at a significance level greater than 0.05, indicat-",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,10,"used an analysis of variance (ANOVA) to deter- mine the differences between groups according to academic level, and the Scheffe test for post hoc comparisons. The results of the Shapiro-Wilk test were at a significance level greater than 0.05, indicat- ing a normal distribution. The results are shown in Tables 8 and 9. The ANOVA and Scheffe post hoc analyses (Tables 8 and 9) revealed statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) in students’ perceptions of Gen-AI adoption based on academic stage. Diploma students con- sistently reported the highest mean scores across all measured dimensions: perceived benefits, concerns, and overall attitudes. These findings suggest that diploma-level learners, typically engaged in more applied and vocational training, are not only more favorable toward the use of Gen-AI but also more critically aware of its limitations. This contrasts with the relatively moderate perceptions expressed by bachelor’s students and the lower scores among master’s students, particularly regarding the overall util- ity of Gen-AI in academic tasks. Table 7. Mann–Whitney U test for independent samples to examine gender-based differences in the study sample. Variables Gender N",180
