DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,11,"These results align with prior studies emphasizing the impact of practical exposure on technology adoption. For instance, Alshamy et al. (2025) and Simelane and Kittur (2025) found that students in applied or technical programs demonstrate greater receptiveness to emerging technologies due to their hands-on engagement and contextualized learning environments. Similarly, Derakhshan (2025) high- lighted the effectiveness of AI-enhanced platforms in adaptive instruction within practice-oriented curric- ula. In this context, diploma students’ stronger perceptions may reflect their direct interactions with Gen-AI tools during training, which enhances their confidence and digital fluency attributes less empha- sized in more theory-driven bachelor’s and master’s programs. Conversely, the lower perceptions among master’s students may stem from a more critical awareness of AI-related risks, including academic integrity, data privacy, and algorithmic bias—concerns highlighted in graduate-level discourse (Charles et al., 2025; Samala & Rawas, 2025). While Kim et al. (2025) noted significant demographic and disciplinary differences in Gen-AI use, their findings did not account for variation by academic level. This study, therefore, contributes novel insight by identifying academic stage as a key moderator in student perceptions of",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,11,"et al. (2025) noted significant demographic and disciplinary differences in Gen-AI use, their findings did not account for variation by academic level. This study, therefore, contributes novel insight by identifying academic stage as a key moderator in student perceptions of Gen-AI. It underscores the importance of differenti- ated institutional strategies, tailored by academic level, to ensure that Gen-AI tools are both effectively adopted and ethically integrated into diverse higher education contexts. 4.1.3.3. Differences in computer experience. The researcher used an analysis of variance (ANOVA) to determine the differences between groups based on experience, and the Scheffe test for post hoc com- parisons. The results of the Shapiro-Wilk test were at a significance level greater than 0.05, indicating a normal distribution. The results are shown in Tables 10 and 11. The results of the one-way ANOVA presented in Table 10 reveal statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) in student perceptions of Gen-AI adoption across three main dimensions, perceived benefits, challenges, and overall attitudes, based on their level of computer experience. These findings were further clarified through Scheffe post hoc comparisons (Table",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,11,"statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) in student perceptions of Gen-AI adoption across three main dimensions, perceived benefits, challenges, and overall attitudes, based on their level of computer experience. These findings were further clarified through Scheffe post hoc comparisons (Table 11), which showed that students with beginner-level experience reported significantly higher mean scores on both the bene- fits and challenges of Gen-AI than those with advanced or expert experience. This pattern suggests that students with limited exposure to digital tools may exhibit more enthusiastic but potentially idealized per- ceptions of Gen-AI technologies. This outcome may also be related to the structure of the questionnaire itself, which relied on general items regarding the benefits and challenges of AI, leading beginners, who have not yet encountered the practical realities, to provide more positive (optimistic) responses. This result aligns with the broader concept of the Dunning-Kruger effect, wherein individuals with limited knowledge in a domain tend to overestimate their understanding or competence (Kruger & Dunning, 1999). In the context of Gen-AI, beginner users may perceive these tools as highly beneficial Table 9. Scheffe",180
