DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,12,"without fully appreciating their technical, ethical, or contextual complexities. In contrast, students with advanced computer experience likely possess greater digital literacy, enabling them to approach Gen-AI tools with greater skepticism or caution, especially concerning challenges such as data security, misuse, and the risk of academic dishonesty, concerns widely documented in recent literature (Charles et al., 2025; Samala & Rawas, 2025). This study is among the first to examine how computer experience affects students’ perceptions of Gen-AI in higher education. Unlike prior research focused on demographics like gender (Kim et al., 2025) and discipline (Al-Huwail et al., 2025), it highlights digital competency as a key factor shaping attitudes toward AI. The findings emphasize the need for AI training tailored to students’ technological skills, aca- demic level, and field of study. 4.1.3.4. Differences by specialization. The Kruskal–Wallis test was used to examine differences by spe- cialization due to non-normal data, as confirmed by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test (p < 0.05). Results are shown in Table 12. The Kruskal–Wallis test results (Table 12) revealed statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) in stu- dents’ perceptions",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,12,"differences by spe- cialization due to non-normal data, as confirmed by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test (p < 0.05). Results are shown in Table 12. The Kruskal–Wallis test results (Table 12) revealed statistically significant differences (p < 0.001) in stu- dents’ perceptions of generative AI (Gen-AI) based on their field of specialization across all dimensions, benefits and opportunities, concerns and challenges, and overall perceptions. Administrative students consistently recorded the highest mean ranks, indicating the most favorable attitudes toward Gen-AI adoption, followed by students in educational and health disciplines. Meanwhile, students from scientific and engineering fields reported comparatively lower scores across all variables. These differences sug- gest that academic specialization meaningfully influences both the perceived utility and challenges of Gen-AI in educational contexts. This pattern may be explained by the different ways Gen-AI is applied across disciplines. Students in administrative and educational fields may find Gen-AI tools particularly useful for generating content, improving productivity, and assisting with communication or documentation tasks that align with their curriculum. On the other hand, students in engineering and scientific fields, despite their likely greater technical proficiency, may",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,12,"may find Gen-AI tools particularly useful for generating content, improving productivity, and assisting with communication or documentation tasks that align with their curriculum. On the other hand, students in engineering and scientific fields, despite their likely greater technical proficiency, may adopt a more critical stance due to their deeper understanding of the limita- tions, risks, and ethical implications of AI technologies. This aligns with findings by Kim et al. (2025), who reported that while STEM students were more exposed to Gen-AI, they also exhibited a higher awareness of academic integrity risks and concerns about AI reliability. Table 11. Scheffe test results for post hoc comparisons on the variable of computer experience. Axis Variable Expert Advanced Benefits and Opportunities of GEN-AI Beginner 0.94 3.76 Expert 1 2.82 GEN-AI Concerns Beginner 0.54 2.42 Expert 1 1.88 Questionnaire as a Whole Beginner 0.39 1.34 Expert 1 0.95 Mean differences are significant at p < 0.05. Table 12. Kruskal–Wallis test results for questionnaire dimensions by specialization. Variables Field of specialization N Mean rank Kruskal–Wallis H df Sig. (p-value) Benefits and Opportunities of GEN-AI Administrative",180
