DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,13,"These results underscore the need for discipline-specific strategies when integrating Gen-AI tools into higher education. As also noted by Sousa and Cardoso (2025) and Alshamy et al. (2025), successful implementation of AI in education requires acknowledging the unique learning outcomes, pedagogical methods, and technological needs of each field. Students in fields with more theoretical or humanistic leanings may require more guidance and support to effectively engage with Gen-AI, while those in tech- nical disciplines may benefit from frameworks that promote ethical use and critical analysis of AI out- puts. Ultimately, this study reinforces that AI adoption in education cannot be treated uniformly; rather, it must be responsive to disciplinary contexts and tailored to students’ specific academic environments. 5. Conclusion This study aimed to explore students’ perceptions of Gen-AI as a tool for supporting learning and aca- demic achievement at Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University. Drawing on a descriptive quantitative methodology, data were collected from 1,537 students across various academic stages and disciplines. The findings revealed that students generally view Gen-AI positively, particularly in its ability to stream- line academic tasks,",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,13,"Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University. Drawing on a descriptive quantitative methodology, data were collected from 1,537 students across various academic stages and disciplines. The findings revealed that students generally view Gen-AI positively, particularly in its ability to stream- line academic tasks, enhance motivation, support collaboration, provide real-time feedback, and accom- modate diverse learning needs. These insights contribute to the evolving global discourse on AI in education by offering context-specific evidence from an Arab academic environment. At the same time, students expressed notable concerns regarding limited infrastructure, lack of Arabic-language AI tools, insufficient awareness, and risks related to data privacy and academic ethics. Significant differences in perceptions were also found across gender, academic stage, field of specializa- tion, and level of computer experience—indicating that students’ engagement with Gen-AI is shaped by both demographic and contextual variables. The study’s contribution lies not only in mapping these per- ceptions but also in emphasizing the need for targeted, inclusive, and ethical AI integration strategies in higher education. Despite its strengths, the study is limited by its single-institution focus, reliance on self-reported data, and cross-sectional design,",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,13,"in mapping these per- ceptions but also in emphasizing the need for targeted, inclusive, and ethical AI integration strategies in higher education. Despite its strengths, the study is limited by its single-institution focus, reliance on self-reported data, and cross-sectional design, which restrict the generalizability of the findings and the ability to track changes in student attitudes over time. Furthermore, the absence of qualitative data limits a deeper understanding of students’ nuanced perspectives and ethical concerns regarding Gen-AI usage. Future research should adopt longitudinal and mixed-method approaches to explore how students’ perceptions of Gen-AI evolve and how these tools influence academic outcomes, critical thinking, and academic integrity. Expanding the study across multiple institutions and cultural contexts will enhance generalizability, while incorporating qualitative methods such as interviews or focus groups will offer richer insights into the lived experiences and ethical considerations students encounter. Experimental studies could also assess the effectiveness of AI-based interventions in improving learning engagement and performance across different disciplines. For leaders in higher education, these findings underscore the need to develop comprehensive and context-sensitive strategies for Gen-AI integration. Institutions",180
DOC_018,Generative artificial intelligence in higher education  Students  journey through opportunities  challenges  and the horizons of academic transformati.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2025.2589495,13,"encounter. Experimental studies could also assess the effectiveness of AI-based interventions in improving learning engagement and performance across different disciplines. For leaders in higher education, these findings underscore the need to develop comprehensive and context-sensitive strategies for Gen-AI integration. Institutions should invest in infrastructure, support the development of Arabic-compatible AI tools, and design AI literacy initiatives that account for academic level, gender, and digital proficiency. Faculty members should be trained to integrate AI meaningfully into pedagogy, emphasizing both its instructional value and ethical boundaries. Ultimately, fostering inclusive, equitable, and responsible AI practices will ensure that Gen-AI contributes positively to student success across diverse academic settings. Acknowledgements The author gratefully acknowledges the students of Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University for their participation in this study. Author contributions CRediT: Amani Abdullah Bin Jwair: Data curation, Formal analysis, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. 12 A. A. B. JWAIR",149
