DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,1,"1 of 16 Higher Education Quarterly, 2025; 79:e70054 https://doi.org/10.1111/hequ.70054 Higher Education Quarterly RESEARCH ARTICLE OPEN ACCESS Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for Generative AI Usage in Higher Education Yingying Jiang1 | Lindai Xie2 | Xiaoyu Cao1 1Department of Educational Studies, Purdue University, West Lafayette, Indiana, USA | 2Department of Educational Psychology, Leadership, and Counseling, Texas Tech University, Lubbock, Texas, USA Correspondence: Yingying Jiang (jiang214@purdue.edu) Received: 28 February 2025 | Revised: 22 July 2025 | Accepted: 28 July 2025 Funding: This study was supported by the College of Education at Purdue University's 2023–2024 Small Research Grant Program, which provided funding for participant compensation as well as for data transcription and analysis software. Keywords: AI | AI policy | faculty perception | higher education | student perception ABSTRACT As generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) becomes more integrated into higher education, institutions struggle to communi- cate clear and effective policies. This multiple-­methods study examines survey and interview data collected with 124 undergrad- uates and seven faculty members from two U.S. R1 universities regarding their perceptions of institutional AI policies, supportive",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,1,"education, institutions struggle to communi- cate clear and effective policies. This multiple-­methods study examines survey and interview data collected with 124 undergrad- uates and seven faculty members from two U.S. R1 universities regarding their perceptions of institutional AI policies, supportive resources and challenges. Students reported that while classroom-­level policies are recognised, institutional guidelines remain unclear. Similarly, faculty highlighted the implementation challenges they face due to practical constraints and insufficient training. Additional findings show that students aware of AI policies are less likely to use AI for writing and research; discipli- nary and gender differences in institutional resources and policy awareness. Faculty interviews revealed themes such as AI use in research and varied colleague attitudes. Although this study is limited by the number of institutions and sample size, it high- lights the need for ongoing research to support policy refinement. Artificial Intelligence (AI) has significantly evolved over the past few decades, transitioning from traditional AI systems to advanced generative AI (GenAI) models. Traditional AI focused on pattern recognition, prediction and structured decision-­making (Johnston 2024). It began with rules-­based systems (1950s–1980s) using",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,1,"Intelligence (AI) has significantly evolved over the past few decades, transitioning from traditional AI systems to advanced generative AI (GenAI) models. Traditional AI focused on pattern recognition, prediction and structured decision-­making (Johnston 2024). It began with rules-­based systems (1950s–1980s) using predefined ‘if-­then’ logic, exem- plified by systems like MYCIN for medical diagnosis and the General Problem Solver (GPS) which was designed to mimic human problem-­solving (Johnston 2024). Machine learning (1990s–2010s) replaced rigid rules with data-­driven learning through supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement tech- niques (Johnston 2024). The deep learning revolution (2010s) introduced neural networks like convolutional neural net- works (CNNs) for image recognition in autonomous vehicles and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) for speech processing (Johnston 2024). Unlike traditional AI's predictive capabilities, GenAI represents a fundamental shift from analysis to content creation to pro- duces original text. This advancement was driven by trans- former models, introduced in the ‘Attention is All You Need’ (Vaswani et al. 2017), which use self-­attention mechanisms for superior context processing (Johnston 2024). Key tools include large language models (LLMs) like GPT (Generative Pre-­trained Transformer) for text generation",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,1,"trans- former models, introduced in the ‘Attention is All You Need’ (Vaswani et al. 2017), which use self-­attention mechanisms for superior context processing (Johnston 2024). Key tools include large language models (LLMs) like GPT (Generative Pre-­trained Transformer) for text generation and DALL-­E for image creation (Johnston 2024). This creative capacity distinguishes generative AI from its analytical predecessors (Johnston 2024), and has fundamentally transformed education and raised new policy challenges. GenAI use among students is rapidly increasing globally; a 2025 survey in the U.K. reported that 92% of students use tools like ChatGPT. However, only one-­third have received formal This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. © 2025 The Author(s). Higher Education Quarterly published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",143
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"2 of 16 Higher Education Quarterly, 2025 training, raising concerns about inadequate institutional guid- ance (The Guardian 2025). Similarly, a study involving 457 students and 180 faculty members across multiple Hong Kong institutions revealed overall positive attitudes toward GenAI, but also concerns about academic integrity, ethical implications and the need for AI literacy training (Chan 2023). While many institutions have begun to establish policies, students often re- main unsure whether or when they are allowed to use GenAI in coursework due to unclear or inconsistently communicated regulations (Mowreader 2024). 1 | International Policy The emergence of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) has prompted diverse and uneven policy responses worldwide. In May 2023, UNESCO surveyed over 450 institutions across its global networks of Associated Schools and University Chairs. Institutions from Europe and North America accounted for 44% of responses, followed by Asia and the Pacific (23%), Latin America and the Caribbean (17%), Africa (11%) and the Arab States (5%). The results revealed that fewer than 10% of sur- veyed institutions had formal policies or guidance on AI use in education (UNESCO 2023).",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"and the Pacific (23%), Latin America and the Caribbean (17%), Africa (11%) and the Arab States (5%). The results revealed that fewer than 10% of sur- veyed institutions had formal policies or guidance on AI use in education (UNESCO 2023). UNESCO has provided a range of frameworks and guidance over the last few years to collectively address the gaps. These include the 2019 Beijing Consensus on AI and Education, the 2019 I'd Blush If I Could publication on AI's gender implications, the 2021 AI and Education: Guidance for Policy-­Makers, and the 2021 Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (UNESCO 2019, 2021a, 2021b, 2023). Together, these doc- uments promote a humanistic approach to AI governance in ed- ucation, with inclusion, equity, diversity and quality as guiding principles. Similarly, the OECD (2021) emphasised that effective AI integration requires systemic efforts to strengthen assess- ment, pedagogy and workforce-­relevant learning design. More recently, UNESCO IESALC and IBM (2024) have co-­developed an AI Competence Framework to help educators and students acquire ethical, context-­sensitive AI skills, with particular at- tention to the Global South (UNESCO IESALC",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"assess- ment, pedagogy and workforce-­relevant learning design. More recently, UNESCO IESALC and IBM (2024) have co-­developed an AI Competence Framework to help educators and students acquire ethical, context-­sensitive AI skills, with particular at- tention to the Global South (UNESCO IESALC and IBM 2024). Regionally, the European Union's AI Act classifies educational uses of AI as ‘high-­risk’, requiring transparency, human oversight and accountability measures (European Commission 2024). A recent survey found that 80% of institutions across six European countries—Portugal, Germany, Finland, the Netherlands, Croatia and Denmark—either lacked AI policies or were unsure if they existed (Media and Learning Association 2023). Some of the reported barriers included insufficient resources, institu- tional resistance and complexity of implementation (Media and Learning Association 2023). Other countries have also responded to the growing presence of GenAI in higher education. Australia's national regulator, the Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency (TEQSA), issued system-­wide guidance in 2024 requiring all higher ed- ucation providers to submit institutional action plans address- ing the risks posed by generative AI. Providers were expected to align these plans with national quality standards, conduct",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"and Standards Agency (TEQSA), issued system-­wide guidance in 2024 requiring all higher ed- ucation providers to submit institutional action plans address- ing the risks posed by generative AI. Providers were expected to align these plans with national quality standards, conduct course-­level risk assessments and integrate AI-­related risks into institutional governance processes (Tertiary Education Quality and Standards Agency 2024). In another study that focuses on East Asian countries, Xie et al. (2024) reviewed GenAI-­related policy developments in China, Japan, Mongolia and the United States. China and Mongolia emphasised national sovereignty and safety, while Japan and the United States focused more on improving edu- cational methods and efficiency through a human-­centered ap- proach. The researchers also noted that despite shared references to inclusion and equity, few countries had specific policy mecha- nisms, particularly in addressing infrastructural and digital ac- cess gaps (Xie et al. 2024). Moreover, China's policies on GenAI mainly address service providers and users without specifying educational stages, while Japan focuses on higher education and targets university administrators and faculty. In contrast, U.S. policies emphasise K–12 education and address a",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"et al. 2024). Moreover, China's policies on GenAI mainly address service providers and users without specifying educational stages, while Japan focuses on higher education and targets university administrators and faculty. In contrast, U.S. policies emphasise K–12 education and address a broader audi- ence, covering various AI technologies beyond generative AI. 2 | Institutional Policies Although international organisations like UNESCO and the OECD have issued guidance on ethical and equitable AI use in education, these frameworks often address AI broadly, rather than grappling with the unique pedagogical and ethical com- plexities introduced by generative AI (GenAI). Studies claim that higher education institutions worldwide are increasingly developing policies to regulate their own use in teaching and learning. For example, the University of Hong Kong (HKU) has taken a more comprehensive approach to using GenAI in teach- ing and learning (University of Hong Kong 2023). In Australia, the University of Sydney has adapted its policies to allow stu- dents to use AI tools in open-­book assessments (University of Sydney 2023). More recently, after analysing documents among 116 U.S. universities, more than one-­third have",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"Kong 2023). In Australia, the University of Sydney has adapted its policies to allow stu- dents to use AI tools in open-­book assessments (University of Sydney 2023). More recently, after analysing documents among 116 U.S. universities, more than one-­third have provided guide- lines for classroom teaching (McDonald et al. 2025). While these examples suggest growing institutional engagement with GenAI policy, a broader look reveals ongoing challenges. Jin et al. (2025) surveyed 40 leading universities across Asia, Europe, North America and Oceania and found that while insti- tutional leaders generally expressed positive attitudes toward the integration of GenAI, formal policies often lacked clarity, coher- ence or actionable detail. Similarly, Xiao et al. (2023) examined QS ranking top 500 universities; at the time, less than one-­third of these universities had some regulations in place; among these universities, more than twice of the universities banned ChatGPT than embraced it. In Canada, Da Mota (2024) reported that there is no existing or proposed legislation that addresses AI use in re- search institutions, and the draft Artificial Intelligence and Data Act offers no specific guidance",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,2,"universities banned ChatGPT than embraced it. In Canada, Da Mota (2024) reported that there is no existing or proposed legislation that addresses AI use in re- search institutions, and the draft Artificial Intelligence and Data Act offers no specific guidance for protecting valuable scientific, cultural and historical information in the research institution context. In the absence of national policy, universities such as McMaster University, the University of Montreal and Western University have developed multi-­tiered responses: some offer basic classroom guidelines, while others focus on academic integ- rity, promote AI literacy or establish formal task forces and ethical frameworks. However, a recent report that surveyed Canadian post-­secondary institutions asked participants whether their insti- tutions had implemented broad policies on GenAI, and 56% were either unsure or disagreed (Rasky 2024). This gap between for- mal policy efforts and campus-­wide understanding is not unique to Canada—it echoes a broader global trend where the existence 14682273, 2025, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hequ.70054 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use;",180
