DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"3 of 16 of GenAI guidelines does not necessarily translate to meaningful guidance for day-­to-­day academic practice. 3 | U.S. Federal and State Policies Before the GenAI era, there was no specific law for traditional AI in education at the federal level, but agencies like the U.S. Department of Education have issued guidelines on data pri- vacy, fairness and equity that influenced institutional AI policies (U.S. Department of Education 2025). Meanwhile, state policies regarding traditional AI varied widely. For example, California required transparency in AI decision-­making, pushing universi- ties to adopt stricter governance (University of California 2025). In states with fewer specific AI regulations, universities devel- oped their own policies, leading to uneven practices across the U.S. (BCLP Report 2025). With the rise of generative AI and its expansion across higher ed- ucation beyond traditional STEM fields, federal efforts such as the AI Leadership to Enable Accountable Deployment Act have started to establish regulation frameworks for AI, including in educa- tional settings (National Conference of State Legislatures 2023). The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) re- leased an Artificial",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"as the AI Leadership to Enable Accountable Deployment Act have started to establish regulation frameworks for AI, including in educa- tional settings (National Conference of State Legislatures 2023). The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) re- leased an Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework that includes a profile for GenAI to promote responsible use (National Conference of State Legislatures 2023). Some states like California and Oregon have updated or introduced new laws addressing the use of generative AI in education, with clearer guidelines on aca- demic use (Dusseault and Lee 2023). In contrast, some states such as Illinois and Iowa do not provide state-­level guidance, leaving policy decisions to individual institutions at that time (Dusseault and Lee 2023). 4 | Students and Faculty's Perceptions of AI Policies GenAI adoption among students is widespread and may occur without institutional rules, for example, Coffey (2023) found students reported that they would continue using ChatGPT and other Gen AI tools even if they are banned. AT the same time, research on students' perspectives on AI policies remains largely unexplored, leaving a gap in",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"example, Coffey (2023) found students reported that they would continue using ChatGPT and other Gen AI tools even if they are banned. AT the same time, research on students' perspectives on AI policies remains largely unexplored, leaving a gap in understanding how these policies affect their learning and engagement. Rasky (2024) stated stu- dents generally have minimal involvement in shaping Gen AI policies, despite the significant impact these policies have on their educational experience. Other studies have shown that in- stitutional policies regarding Gen AI often use ambiguous lan- guage (e.g., ‘unauthorized AI assistance’); lack clear guidance and support on when and how Gen AI tools can be appropri- ately used (Fawns et al. 2024; Hashmi and Bal 2024; Klein 2024; Michel-­Villarreal et al. 2023). It is also concerning that Gen AI may pose challenges to academic integrity as students adapt to different policies and regulations across various courses (Mowreader 2024). Tyton Partners (2023) questioned this even further arguing that those policies mostly focused on academic integrity while neglecting issues like bias in large language models and accessibility challenges. All",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"adapt to different policies and regulations across various courses (Mowreader 2024). Tyton Partners (2023) questioned this even further arguing that those policies mostly focused on academic integrity while neglecting issues like bias in large language models and accessibility challenges. All sources point to gaps on policy development and fragmented implementation. Tyton Partners (2023) argue that to address these gaps, institutions need to refine initial Gen AI policies through collaboration and knowledge sharing (Tyton Partners 2023). Faculty perceptions of AI policies also remain underexplored; most research has focused on their views regarding AI usage. While faculty generally hold positive attitudes toward AI, con- cerns primarily centre on academic integrity, critical thinking and potential over-­reliance (Xie et al., Under Review; Ghimire et al. 2024). Disciplinary differences also shape faculty per- spectives on AI, with STEM instructors typically exhibiting a more positive stance compared to their non-­STEM counterparts (Parviz 2024). Despite growing awareness, faculty adoption of AI tools has been slow. A recent report indicates that only 22% of faculty members incorporate AI tools into their instructional practices (Tyton Partners 2023). Luckin and",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"compared to their non-­STEM counterparts (Parviz 2024). Despite growing awareness, faculty adoption of AI tools has been slow. A recent report indicates that only 22% of faculty members incorporate AI tools into their instructional practices (Tyton Partners 2023). Luckin and Cukurova (2019) highlighted that many educators lack a sufficient understand- ing of AI, leaving them vulnerable to ineffective implementa- tion and excessive reliance on technology companies—many of which have limited expertise in teaching and learning. Similarly, inadequate AI training remains a major barrier, with only 10% of surveyed faculty members expressing confidence in using AI tools (Toppo 2024). 5 | Research Problem and Theoretical Framework While previous studies have highlighted issues such as incon- sistent AI policies, unclear guidelines (Mowreader 2024), equity concerns (Xie et al. 2024) and enforcement challenges (Media and Learning Association 2023), many of these discussions have been rooted in traditional AI frameworks and approached from a top-­down perspective. They tend to emphasise the role of institu- tional leaders and administration in crafting and disseminating AI-­related policies, often lacking understandings of how these policies are interpreted or",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"been rooted in traditional AI frameworks and approached from a top-­down perspective. They tend to emphasise the role of institu- tional leaders and administration in crafting and disseminating AI-­related policies, often lacking understandings of how these policies are interpreted or applied by those at the ground level, such as faculty and students in higher education. To ensure that both faculty and student perspectives are adequately represented, this study examines how AI policies are understood and enacted in two U.S. higher education institutions through an integrated theoretical framework combining: (1) Policy Enactment theory to analyse institutional policy interpretations and (2) Technology Acceptance models to identify adoption barriers. 5.1 | Policy Enactment Framework We adopt the Policy Enactment Framework developed by Ball et al. (2012), which emphasises how institutional actors (e.g., faculty, staff, students) reinterpret and reshape top-­down poli- cies through local practices, beliefs and constraints. The frame- work identifies distinct ‘policy actor’ roles (e.g., translators, resisters) that mediate policy implementation. This perspec- tive aligns with observed disciplinary differences in literature, where STEM faculty historically prioritise research over teach- ing reforms (Fairweather",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,3,"practices, beliefs and constraints. The frame- work identifies distinct ‘policy actor’ roles (e.g., translators, resisters) that mediate policy implementation. This perspec- tive aligns with observed disciplinary differences in literature, where STEM faculty historically prioritise research over teach- ing reforms (Fairweather and Paulson 2008), and generally hold more positive views regarding GenAI than non-­STEM faculty (Parviz 2024). In addition, students' widespread use of formally prohibited AI tools (Coffey 2023) shows how policies are inter- preted and reshaped by practice. This theory highlights the gap in policy development and implementation and guides the im- portant research significance of student and faculty perspectives. 14682273, 2025, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hequ.70054 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",139
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"4 of 16 Higher Education Quarterly, 2025 5.2 | Technology Acceptance Model To understand individual-­level responses to institutional AI policies, this study incorporates elements from the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (Davis 1989; Venkatesh and Davis 2000), which posits that perceived usefulness and per- ceived ease of use influence attitudes and behavioural inten- tions (Granić and Marangunić 2019). In educational technology research, TAM has been widely extended through additions of external constructs like subjective norm (Park 2009); univer- sity support and computer self-­efficacy (Al-­Mushasha 2013); enjoyment (Chang et al. 2017; Zhang et al. 2008); and student attitudes (Granić and Marangunić 2019). Scholars also noted the importance of providing training on how to effectively use technological resources is recommended to enhance accep- tance, while noting the need to consider differences across user groups and cultural contexts in acceptance studies (Granić and Marangunić 2019). Empirical findings from recent literature illustrate how these TAM components apply to GenAI adop- tion. Faculty adoption remains low (22%) according to training shortages reported by Toppo (2024) likely constraining ease of use. Again, students' continued use of briefly",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"2019). Empirical findings from recent literature illustrate how these TAM components apply to GenAI adop- tion. Faculty adoption remains low (22%) according to training shortages reported by Toppo (2024) likely constraining ease of use. Again, students' continued use of briefly banned AI (Coffey 2023) may suggest that perceived usefulness may over- ride institutional regulations. While much of the existing GenAI policy research has focused pri- marily on document analysis, relatively little attention has been paid to the perspectives of faculty and students—those who are responsible for executing these policies in daily practice. By center- ing their voices, this study intends to bridge the gap between policy development and implementation, examining how institutional AI policies are perceived, negotiated and applied on the ground. This investigation is guided by these questions, 1. How do faculty perceive the development and effectiveness of AI regulation at their institutions? 2. How do faculty perceive their colleagues' attitudes toward AI at their institution? 3. How do faculty describe institutional support or resources with AI use? 4. What are perceptions of undergraduate students toward AI regulation at",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"regulation at their institutions? 2. How do faculty perceive their colleagues' attitudes toward AI at their institution? 3. How do faculty describe institutional support or resources with AI use? 4. What are perceptions of undergraduate students toward AI regulation at their institutions and in their classrooms? 5. How do students' perceptions of AI regulation, as well as their perceived AI-­related resources and AI limitations awareness, vary across different demographic groups in- cluding gender, year of study and discipline? 6 | Methods 6.1 | Research Design This study is considered a multiple methods design; the data is part of a larger mixed-­methods research project investigating faculty and students' utilisation of AI tools in higher education. During the design phase, while the student survey followed a structured format rooted in TAM constructs, the faculty in- terviews were more open-­ended (follows the survey sections), allowing participants greater flexibility to reflect on and react to institutional policy guidelines. Data collection took place con- vergently over a 45-­day period in Spring 2024. Although the use of partial data limits the study's ability to fully integrate",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"sections), allowing participants greater flexibility to reflect on and react to institutional policy guidelines. Data collection took place con- vergently over a 45-­day period in Spring 2024. Although the use of partial data limits the study's ability to fully integrate findings in a way that characterises mixed-­methods research, it allows researchers to explore a multi-­perspective view of GenAI policy implementation across institutional roles. Given the distinct participant groups and differing theoretical orientations, the researchers believe this design reflects multi- ple methods, more specifically a triangulation-­based framework (Turner et al. 2015), in which ‘different methods are used to capture different elements of the focal phenomenon’ (p. 603). In addition, the researchers utilised a parallel analysis approach which each dataset was analysed independently. Nonetheless, the findings were systematically compared in the Discussion to identify thematic convergence, contrast and complementarity. The interpretive integration aligned with a triangulation-­based multiple-­methods framework. 6.2 | Research Sites This study took place in two R1 public research universities in the United States; both of the institutions responded rapidly to the emergence of Gen AI during Spring and Summer",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"aligned with a triangulation-­based multiple-­methods framework. 6.2 | Research Sites This study took place in two R1 public research universities in the United States; both of the institutions responded rapidly to the emergence of Gen AI during Spring and Summer 2023 and have developed formal AI guidelines for the Fall 2023 semes- ter. Although these two institutions were selected due to re- searchers' professional connections, these two institutions offer contrasting profiles that enhance the relevance and representa- tiveness. One institution has prominent programmes in the Arts and Health (University A). The other is nationally recognised for STEM majors, especially in engineering and computer sci- ence (University B). While we do recognise the limited repre- sentativeness of the two research sites in terms of generalising to other institutional types, the inclusion of the two R1 universities provides meaningful insight into how Gen AI policy is experi- enced within large public research-­intensive settings. 6.3 | Qualitative Phase Interviews have been conducted with seven faculty members to gain insights into institutional-­level regulations and their colleagues' perceptions of AI usage. Interview questions in- clude:",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,4,"Gen AI policy is experi- enced within large public research-­intensive settings. 6.3 | Qualitative Phase Interviews have been conducted with seven faculty members to gain insights into institutional-­level regulations and their colleagues' perceptions of AI usage. Interview questions in- clude: (1) How do you perceive your university guidance on AI? (2) What about your colleagues' attitude? (3) What sup- port, services or training do you need to better prepare your AI-­integrated teaching? (4) What are some sources of educa- tion or guidance you have received for incorporating AI in your teaching? Faculty participants were selected through researcher networks at each institution. The researchers considered two institutions' disciplinary priorities and ensured an even distribution of partic- ipants across the two institutions. The participants were purpose- fully invited using maximum variation sampling to represent a diverse range of disciplines, including both STEM and non-­STEM fields; as well as varying levels of experience, from assistant to associate professors (See Table 1). Among the participants, five faculty members reported being very familiar with AI, while two 14682273, 2025, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hequ.70054 by INASP/HINARI",180
