DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,5,"5 of 16 indicated having some familiarity with AI. Six faculty members reported that they have not implemented AI in classroom teach- ing; therefore, classroom-­level AI policy was not discussed from the faculty perspective in the paper. The researchers acknowledge that it is difficult to fully represent all faculty perspectives with a small sample, participants were asked about their colleagues' atti- tudes toward Gen AI to represent wider discourse. Several major codes in the qualitative analysis were aligned with the study's theoretical frameworks. For example, the Institutional AI Policies and Guidance code draws directly on Policy Enactment Theory, noting how faculty interpret and adapt to institutional GenAI guidelines. In addition, Faculty Attitudes Toward AI, Support and AI in Research/Professional Work reflect key constructs from the Technology Acceptance Model, such as perceived usefulness, ease of use and organisational support. The codebook was further refined through a review of the transcripts (See Table 2 for codebook). Two researchers conducted indepen- dent data coding and interpretation. After reviewing the coding of the first transcripts, codes were discussed, and the codebook was updated accordingly.",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,5,"was further refined through a review of the transcripts (See Table 2 for codebook). Two researchers conducted indepen- dent data coding and interpretation. After reviewing the coding of the first transcripts, codes were discussed, and the codebook was updated accordingly. For example, sub-­code AI_training was added under major code AI_ethical_consideration to capture gaps in AI training and development that could lead to inequities in AI literacy. Interrater reliability was calculated, and no differences were found at the major code level. After coding, we conducted a thematic analysis focused on iden- tifying patterns and variations across participants. We exam- ined how and where faculty perspectives progressed, converged, diverged or were challenged by institutions and also colleagues. While we looked for common themes across interviews, we also paid attention to outlier insights that offered unique perspec- tives, even if they were not widely echoed. 6.4 | Quantitative Phase 6.4.1 | Data Clean Responses for international student status and different types of academic purposes (e.g., writing aid) were converted into a binary variable (Yes = 1, No = 0) for analysis. For categorical",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,5,"widely echoed. 6.4 | Quantitative Phase 6.4.1 | Data Clean Responses for international student status and different types of academic purposes (e.g., writing aid) were converted into a binary variable (Yes = 1, No = 0) for analysis. For categorical variables that include multiple categories and cannot be coded as binary, they were recoded based on the number of respondents. To facilitate analysis while preserving mean- ingful group distinctions, race was recoded into three cate- gories: Asian, White and Others (including American Indian or Alaska Native, Black/African American, Hispanic/Latinx, Middle Eastern or North African, Native Hawaiian/Other Pacific Islander, Other and Biracial). Similarly, participants' years of study were categorised into lower division (i.e., fresh- men and sophomores) and upper division (i.e., juniors and above). 6.4.2 | Participants Survey flyers were posted in multiple locations across two insti- tutions, and participants were incentivised through a drawing for eight Amazon gift cards. There is a total of 124 undergrad- uate students who responded to the survey invitations. Table 3 presents the demographic breakdown of survey participants, along with publicly available institutional profiles. Among",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,5,"incentivised through a drawing for eight Amazon gift cards. There is a total of 124 undergrad- uate students who responded to the survey invitations. Table 3 presents the demographic breakdown of survey participants, along with publicly available institutional profiles. Among these student participants, 29.8% identified as male, 55.6% as female, and 14.5% as non-­binary or preferred not to answer. In terms of year of study, 35.5% were in the upper division group, while 63.7% were in the lower division group. Regarding majors, 57.3% were enrolled in STEM programs, and 39.5% in non-­STEM pro- grams. Participants' racial identities included 29% Asian, 14.5% identifying as ‘Other’, 37.9% White and 18.5% Multiracial. The total percentage for certain categories does not sum to 100% due to missing data. When comparing participant demographics to institutional un- dergraduate data (Table 3), STEM majors were slightly overrep- resented compared to University A but aligned with profiles of STEM-­focused campuses (University B). Asian students were overrepresented relative to some institutions but consistent with those serving more Asian or international students. Gender diversity was notable, with a higher proportion",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,5,"resented compared to University A but aligned with profiles of STEM-­focused campuses (University B). Asian students were overrepresented relative to some institutions but consistent with those serving more Asian or international students. Gender diversity was notable, with a higher proportion of non-­binary students in our sample than typically reported/found in public reports. While the sample may not be fully representative of all undergraduate students, it reflects diverse U.S. institutional pro- files with limited generalisability. 6.4.3 | Data Analysis To examine the underlying structure of students' perceptions of AI-­related factors, an exploratory factor analysis (EFA) was first conducted. Prior to conducting EFA, assumption checks were performed to ensure the suitability of the data. The Kaiser-­ Meyer-­Olkin (KMO) measure of sampling adequacy was 0.66, with individual item values ranging from 0.53 to 0.75. Bartlett's test of sphericity was significant, χ2 (231) = 673.86, p < 0.001. These results confirm that the data were appropriate for factor analysis. Based on factor analysis results, items with low factor loadings (below 0.40) were removed. Figure 1 presents the final factor structure derived from the EFA",180
