DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,11,"results indicate that more than 77% of participants agreed that the classes they attended had policies re- garding AI usage, and 78% of the undergraduate students reported having restrictions on using AI for learning. Regarding university-­ level policy, only 47.5% of participants agreed that their institu- tion has clear guidance or policies regarding AI use in learning. Detailed proportions of participants' responses on each item were included in Table 6. Table 7 shows the correlation matrix among the AI-­related variables and reveals that the correlations among the three variables were weak. 7.2.2 | T-­Tests Several independent sample t-­tests were conducted to exam- ine whether students' AI-­related variables differed by categori- cal variables such as year of study, race and major. Significant findings are reported below. Welch's t-­tests were conducted to examine differences in students' responsible AI usage across academic years. Lower-­division and upper-­division students also differed in their perceptions of AI-­related policies, 95% CI [−0.78, −0.08], t (58.95) = −2.47, p = 0.017, with a medium ef- fect size (Hedges's g = −0.53). Lower-­division students reported significantly higher awareness of",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,11,"Lower-­division and upper-­division students also differed in their perceptions of AI-­related policies, 95% CI [−0.78, −0.08], t (58.95) = −2.47, p = 0.017, with a medium ef- fect size (Hedges's g = −0.53). Lower-­division students reported significantly higher awareness of AI-­related policy compared to upper-­division students (M = 11.4, SD = 1.82 vs. M = 10.1, SD = 3.19). The comparison of year-­of-­study groups (lower and upper division) revealed no significant differences in AI limita- tions awareness or AI-­related resources. These findings suggest that students' attitudes toward AI, including its perceived limita- tions and available resources, remain consistent across different stages of undergraduate study. In addition to students' years of study, their perceptions of AI policy also differed based on AI usage for academic tasks. Students who chose to use AI as a writing aid reported signifi- cantly different perceptions on AI policy than those who did not, t (112.62) = 2.95, p = 0.0038, 95% CI [0.39, 1.97]. Students who used AI for research assistance also scored significantly differently on perceived AI policy compared to those who did not,",180
