DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"12 of 16 Higher Education Quarterly, 2025 Other than perceived AI policy, significant group differences were also found among STEM and non-­STEM major students' perceived AI-­related resources, 95% CI [0.11, 1.82], t (118) = 2.24, p = 0.027. The small effect size (Hedges's g = 0.41) suggests that while STEM students reported significantly higher perceived AI-­related resources compared to non-­STEM students (M = 9.25, SD = 2.16 vs. M = 8.29, SD = 2.55), the difference may not sub- stantial. However, no significant differences were observed between STEM and non-­STEM students in the other two AI-­ related variables (i.e., AI limitations awareness and perceived AI-­related policy). These results suggest that while perceptions of AI-­related resources vary by academic discipline, students across different fields of study hold relatively similar views re- garding AI's limitations and policy of utilisation. In addition, two significant differences were found in AI lim- itation awareness between students who used or did not use AI for writing aids (95% CI [−2.03, −0.15], t (122) = −2.30, p = 0.023) and exam assistance (95% CI [−2.80, −0.02], t",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"significant differences were found in AI lim- itation awareness between students who used or did not use AI for writing aids (95% CI [−2.03, −0.15], t (122) = −2.30, p = 0.023) and exam assistance (95% CI [−2.80, −0.02], t (122) = −2.01, p = 0.046). The effect size was small for writing aids (Hedges's g = −0.42) and medium for the usage of exam assistance (Hedges's g = −0.54). Findings show that students who use AI as a writing aid reported slightly greater awareness of AI lim- itations than those who did not (M = 7.67, SD = 2.55 vs. M = 6.58, SD = 2.67). Similarly, students who used AI for exam assistance reported greater awareness of AI limitations than those who did not (M = 8.44, SD = 2.16 vs. M = 7.03, SD = 2.67). 7.2.3 | ANOVAs One-­way ANOVAs were conducted to examine whether differ- ences in gender identity, race and academic performance (GPA) were associated with variations in AI-­related variables. Results indicated a significant effect of gender on both AI limitations awareness (F (2,",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"| ANOVAs One-­way ANOVAs were conducted to examine whether differ- ences in gender identity, race and academic performance (GPA) were associated with variations in AI-­related variables. Results indicated a significant effect of gender on both AI limitations awareness (F (2, 118) = 12.14, p < 0.001) and perceived AI policy (F (2, 118) = 3.36, p = 0.038). As suggested by Cohen (2013), the effect sizes of ANOVA were interpreted using the conventional benchmarks for η2 (small = 0.01, medium = 0.06, large = 0.14). A large effect size (η2 = 0.17) indicates that gender accounts for 17% of the variance in AI limitations awareness, but it ex- plained a smaller proportion of the variance in perceived AI pol- icy (η2 = 0.05), which is classified as a small to medium effect. Post hoc Tukey's HSD tests revealed that both significant gen- der differences were primarily driven by the non-­binary group. ANOVA results also indicated a significant effect of race on AI limitations awareness (F (2, 98) = 3.40, p = 0.037). The observed effect size for race differences in",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"significant gen- der differences were primarily driven by the non-­binary group. ANOVA results also indicated a significant effect of race on AI limitations awareness (F (2, 98) = 3.40, p = 0.037). The observed effect size for race differences in AI limitations awareness was medium (η2 = 0.06), suggesting that race explained a meaningful proportion of variance in students' awareness of AI limitations. Post hoc Tukey's HSD tests showed that the significant differ- ence was primarily between White and ‘Other racial’ groups (p = 0.044), with the ‘Other racial’ group perceiving significantly more AI limitations. However, no significant differences were observed between other groups. However, it is important to note that the non-­binary gender group (14.5%) and the ‘Other’ racial group (18.5%) had relatively small sample sizes, which may limit the generalisability of these findings and reduce statistical power. Therefore, although the differences were statistically significant, the observed effects should be interpreted with caution, particularly regarding their practical implications. 8 | Discussion The findings of this study highlight significant gaps in both faculty and undergraduate student familiarity with and imple-",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"although the differences were statistically significant, the observed effects should be interpreted with caution, particularly regarding their practical implications. 8 | Discussion The findings of this study highlight significant gaps in both faculty and undergraduate student familiarity with and imple- mentation of AI policies, especially regarding Gen AI in higher education. While previous research has noted the lack of AI-­ related guidance, many of these studies were conducted before 2022 (e.g., Zawacki-­Richter et al. 2019). With the rise of GenAI and its increased accessibility, this study broadens the scope by targeting a wider population in higher education. The study's findings align with both Policy Enactment Theory and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), which together help explain observed gaps in GenAI policy implementation and stakeholder behaviours across both qualitative and quantitative phases. According to Policy Enactment Theory (Ball et al. 2012), institutional policies are not simply implemented as written, but are interpreted and reshaped by local actors-­in this case, faculty and students. The qualitative findings show that faculty viewed institutional-­level GenAI guidelines as vague or disconnected from their teaching and research",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"institutional policies are not simply implemented as written, but are interpreted and reshaped by local actors-­in this case, faculty and students. The qualitative findings show that faculty viewed institutional-­level GenAI guidelines as vague or disconnected from their teaching and research needs, especially at the graduate level. Meanwhile, the quantitative results revealed that students more often recognised GenAI-­related policies at the classroom level, suggesting that even unclear, faculty serve as ‘translators’ who clarify institutional expectations into course-­specific rules. The Technology Acceptance Model (Davis 1989; Venkatesh and Davis 2000) also helps explain adoption behaviours identified in both data phases. Faculty in the interviews frequently cited inadequate training as barriers to ease of use, which reinforces TAM's emphasis on institutional support (Al-­Mushasha 2013). More specifically, faculty members expressed two major con- cerns regarding existing training opportunities: (1) while some institutions offer AI-­related workshops, faculty noted that par- ticipation is often voluntary, leading to disparities in AI liter- acy; (2) most trainings are intro-­level and may not be effectively reaching or resonating with all instructors. These faculty perspectives align with student survey results showing",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"faculty noted that par- ticipation is often voluntary, leading to disparities in AI liter- acy; (2) most trainings are intro-­level and may not be effectively reaching or resonating with all instructors. These faculty perspectives align with student survey results showing that AI-­related support was poorly perceived, particu- larly between STEM and non-­STEM disciplines. A previous study found that 900 technology faculty members felt their institutions lacked adequate AI guidance for ethical use (Robert 2024); this study potentially extends this finding across both STEM and non-­STEM fields. In addition, students' continued use of AI for writing and research tasks, even when policy clarity was low, reflects TAM's assertion that perceived usefulness can override external constraints (Coffey 2023). Finally, the model's extended constructs, such as subjective norms (Park 2009), were evident in faculty reports of polarised colleagues' attitudes and both fac- ulty and students' concerns regarding equity. These include eco- nomic barriers to accessing GenAI tools (faculty) and disparities by academic fields and awareness gaps (students). More specif- ically, STEM students perceived more AI-­related support than non-­STEM students and those who are",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,12,"and students' concerns regarding equity. These include eco- nomic barriers to accessing GenAI tools (faculty) and disparities by academic fields and awareness gaps (students). More specif- ically, STEM students perceived more AI-­related support than non-­STEM students and those who are more aware of AI poli- cies are less likely to use generative AI for writing and research. Faculty also noted that existing AI policies primarily emphasise 14682273, 2025, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hequ.70054 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",105
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"13 of 16 restrictions rather than providing guidance on appropriate and ethical AI use. This reactive stance may result in framing AI as a potential threat and influence faculty and students' usage. Similarly, Tyton Partners (2023) found that AI policies in educa- tion predominantly focus on academic integrity, often neglect- ing issues such as bias in AI systems and the lack of accessibility support for students with disabilities. Furthermore, faculty expressed concerns that AI policies tend to be more focused on undergraduate students and provide less guidance for graduate students' learning and professional work. An earlier mixed-­methods research suggests that fac- ulty members impose stricter regulations on undergraduate students while adopting a more flexible approach towards graduate-­level education (Authors et al., unpublished). This may be related to one of the quantitative findings, which indi- cated that lower-­division students (freshman and sophomore) report significantly higher awareness of AI-­related policies compared to upper-­division students (junior and senior). Given faculty's varying strict levels of regulation regarding AI use across student levels, freshman and sophomore students who entered college right after the launch of",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"sophomore) report significantly higher awareness of AI-­related policies compared to upper-­division students (junior and senior). Given faculty's varying strict levels of regulation regarding AI use across student levels, freshman and sophomore students who entered college right after the launch of ChatGPT may experience stricter restrictions. Faculty noted their colleagues' attitudes toward AI varied widely, this indeed reflect broader institutional uncertainty. Some faculty embraced AI as a research and teaching assis- tant; others remained sceptical, noting ethical concerns and institutional support as barriers to effective AI integration. A subset of faculty members actively opposed AI adoption in academia, arguing that AI is not simply a neutral tool, but ‘a biased system shaped by the data it is trained on’. Although these faculty members are actively using AI for research, par- ticularly in tasks like literature reviews, data analysis and coding assistance. They also noticed its limitations in qualita- tive research that requires contextual understanding. Again, faculty expressed frustration that AI training at their institu- tions is less practical, they need hands-­on guidance needed to support research applications. Finally, additional equity con-",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"They also noticed its limitations in qualita- tive research that requires contextual understanding. Again, faculty expressed frustration that AI training at their institu- tions is less practical, they need hands-­on guidance needed to support research applications. Finally, additional equity con- siderations were shared. These faculty pointed to economic disparities in AI access, also noting the access to advanced AI models that require costly subscriptions. Bias in AI-­generated content and unreliability of AI detection tools to prevent aca- demic misconduct. Additional quantitative results revealed that students utilising AI for exam assistance and writing aids have a greater awareness of AI-­related limitations. AI usage guidelines and educators fre- quently emphasise the importance of manual review to ensure the accuracy and appropriateness of AI's writing (e.g., Doyal et al. 2023; Yang et al. 2022). Students who use AI for writing assistance and actively review its output tend to have a higher awareness of AI's limitations, as they may detect issues such as factual inaccuracies during the review process (Ma et al. 2023). Additionally, while using AI for exam assistance, the accuracy of AI's",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"review its output tend to have a higher awareness of AI's limitations, as they may detect issues such as factual inaccuracies during the review process (Ma et al. 2023). Additionally, while using AI for exam assistance, the accuracy of AI's response can directly impact students' scores, making its limitations more apparent. Therefore, encouraging students to use AI with proper guidance while critically assessing and ver- ifying AI-­generated content can be an effective way to enhance their awareness of AI's limitations (Lin 2024). Finally, the ANOVA results reveal significant differences in AI policy perceptions and perceived limitations across gender and racial identity. These findings suggest that certain student groups may experience AI policies and limitations differently, potentially due to varying levels of digital literacy, access to re- sources or prior exposure to AI tools. However, given the limited sample size, further research is needed to explore the extent of these disparities and their implications for policy-­making. 9 | Policy Implications At the institutional level, clearer and more practical poli- cies are needed; not only rules about academic integrity, but also guidance",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"research is needed to explore the extent of these disparities and their implications for policy-­making. 9 | Policy Implications At the institutional level, clearer and more practical poli- cies are needed; not only rules about academic integrity, but also guidance on appropriate and ethical use (especially with graduate-­level teaching and research). Universities also should move beyond voluntary, one-­size-­fits-­all workshops and invest in structured, discipline-­specific professional development. Finally, AI-­related resources should be equitably distributed across departments, especially for students in non-­STEM ma- jors. At the national level, faculty called for clearer national or federal guidance, because individual institutions often lack the capacity or authority to regulate GenAI comprehensively. Coordinated efforts across institutions could also provide mod- els for policy implementation, reducing the burden on individual ‘actors’. In addition, these dynamics are likely not unique to U.S. institutions. As GenAI adoption spreads globally, similar imple- mentation gaps and interpretive practices are likely to emerge. 10 | Limitations and Future Research While this study provides valuable insights into AI policy awareness and perceptions in higher education in U.S. institu- tions, it has several",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"globally, similar imple- mentation gaps and interpretive practices are likely to emerge. 10 | Limitations and Future Research While this study provides valuable insights into AI policy awareness and perceptions in higher education in U.S. institu- tions, it has several limitations. First and foremost, the findings are based on data collected during a specific timeframe (spring 2024) at two R1 U.S. institutions; institutional-­level AI policies may have continued evolving beyond the study period; the lim- ited sample of institutions may not fully capture the AI policy implementations across the U.S. Secondly, although the ANOVA results reveal significant differences in AI policy awareness and perceived limitations across gender and racial identity, the sam- ple size for certain demographic groups was limited. Future research needs to expand on diverse institutional types and po- tential sub-­groups of students to understand the complexity of AI policy implementation, ex. graduate students. Furthermore, with the rapid evolution of AI technology, the discussion of best practices for AI training programmes and instructional integra- tion is also necessary, particularly regarding how institutions can shift from a restriction-­based policy",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,13,"AI policy implementation, ex. graduate students. Furthermore, with the rapid evolution of AI technology, the discussion of best practices for AI training programmes and instructional integra- tion is also necessary, particularly regarding how institutions can shift from a restriction-­based policy approach to one that promotes responsible and effective AI usage. Finally, this study does not directly address the formation of such policies at the organisational or field level. Institutional theory (DiMaggio and Powell 1983; Meyer and Rowan 1977) offers important insights into how higher education institu- tions adopt formal policies not only for functional alignment but also to maintain legitimacy within their broader institu- tional field—often through symbolic or ceremonial adop- tion that may be decoupled from daily practices. Building 14682273, 2025, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hequ.70054 by INASP/HINARI - BHUTAN, Wiley Online Library on [01/11/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License",159
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,14,"14 of 16 Higher Education Quarterly, 2025 on this, the institutional logics perspective (Thornton and Ocasio 2008) emphasises how competing societal logics—such as professional, market and bureaucratic—structure organi- sational attention, decision-­making and resource allocation. While we reference these perspectives to help contextualise global policy variation (e.g., the bureaucratic rigour of the EU AI Act vs. market-­driven pushes in Australia), our study does not empirically examine how such logics operate within the two U.S. institutions under study. Future research could in- tegrate institutional logics more explicitly to examine how or- ganisational priorities and identity influence the creation and positioning of GenAI policies. 11 | Conclusion This study highlights the complexities surrounding AI policy implementation in higher education from faculty and students' perspectives in the United States. While students recognise that classroom-­level policies are more established, institutional AI governance remains ambiguous. Also, classroom policies tend to be restriction-­focused; students report greater awareness is associated with less usage of AI for writing and research. Faculty members also struggle to integrate AI into practice due to unclear policies and insufficient training. They noted that",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,14,"classroom policies tend to be restriction-­focused; students report greater awareness is associated with less usage of AI for writing and research. Faculty members also struggle to integrate AI into practice due to unclear policies and insufficient training. They noted that ‘voluntary’ opportunities may widen AI literacy gaps and limit conversations. Faculty also observed that many workshops are introductory. While many faculty acknowledge AI's effective- ness in research, guidance on AI in professional work is less developed compared to undergraduate teaching. Varying atti- tudes among colleagues further reflect the lack of clear policies. Faculty also advocate for state or federal-­level policies to provide more comprehensive guidance. Additional quantitative findings reveal disparities in AI policy perceptions across disciplines and demographic groups. STEM students report greater access to AI-­related support compared to their non-­STEM counterparts, while gender and racial identity also significantly influence AI policy awareness and perceived limitations. Recognising these policy challenges from faculty and students' perspective, this study advocates for the importance of ongo- ing policy refinement, institutional training programmes and federal-­level guidelines to ensure consistent and equitable AI governance. Future",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,14,"AI policy awareness and perceived limitations. Recognising these policy challenges from faculty and students' perspective, this study advocates for the importance of ongo- ing policy refinement, institutional training programmes and federal-­level guidelines to ensure consistent and equitable AI governance. Future research should also assess the effective- ness and equity of AI-­related training initiatives and potentially examine how institutions can transition from restrictive policies to ones that promote responsible and beneficial AI integration. Author Contributions Yingying Jiang conceptualised the study, contributed to data collection and analysis, and led the writing on the methods, results and discus- sion section. Lindai Xie contributed to data collection and analysis, and led the writing on the quantitative methods and results. Xiaoyu Cao conceptualised the study, contributed to qualitative data analysis and writing on the literature review section. All authors contributed to the interpretation of the findings, reviewed drafts of the manuscript and approved the final version for submission. Ethics Statement This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board at Virginia Commonwealth University (Protocol # HM20028984). Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of",180
DOC_019,Higher Education Quarterly - 2025 - Jiang - Exploring the Effectiveness of Institutional Policies and Regulations for.pdf,10.1111/hequ.70054,14,"findings, reviewed drafts of the manuscript and approved the final version for submission. Ethics Statement This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board at Virginia Commonwealth University (Protocol # HM20028984). Conflicts of Interest The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.",62
