DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,3,"1. Introduction In the current world, rapidly changing technologies have affected education, especially artificial intelligence (AI) (Chen et al., 2022; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). As new technologies have emerged, universities worldwide have been searching for better ways to enhance their students’ ability to face unknown challenges in the future (OECD, 2021; World Economic Forum, 2020). Among these skills, problem-solving skills are particularly important. The World Economic Forum (2020) identifies problem- solving as the top skill needed by employers in the future. In Sri Lankan universities, students face difficulties with problem-solving because traditional teaching methods rely on memorization and theoretical examinations, which do not help students improve their critical thinking and problem-solving skills (Karunanayaka & Weerakoon, 2020; Sabraz Nawaz et al., 2024). Karunanayaka and Weerakoon (2020) note that traditional teaching methods used in Sri Lankan higher education do not encourage active learning, and classrooms suffer from too many students and lack of resources. Recent evidence confirms that Sri Lankan undergraduates are actively adopting AI tools to compensate for these gaps: Nishshanka et al. (2025) found that ChatGPT was the most frequently",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,3,"learning, and classrooms suffer from too many students and lack of resources. Recent evidence confirms that Sri Lankan undergraduates are actively adopting AI tools to compensate for these gaps: Nishshanka et al. (2025) found that ChatGPT was the most frequently used AI tool among Gen Z undergraduates across Sri Lankan state universities and private institutions, with easy accessibility and peer influence identified as the primary adoption drivers. Jayarathne (2026), in a comparative study of Sri Lankan and Chinese undergraduates, further documented that while Sri Lankan students use AI tools at lower rates than their Chinese peers, they demonstrate higher levels of creative and independent thinking when solving problems a finding that underscores the importance of studying AI-assisted learning in this specific cultural context. ChatGPT, a popular AI tool, has become increasingly prominent in higher education. It is a computer programme capable of helping people solve problems, generate content and complete complex tasks by understanding natural language inputs and providing step-by-step guidance (Brown et al., 2020). Early studies show that ChatGPT can improve student problem-solving performance (Kasneci et al., 2023; Lo,",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,3,"capable of helping people solve problems, generate content and complete complex tasks by understanding natural language inputs and providing step-by-step guidance (Brown et al., 2020). Early studies show that ChatGPT can improve student problem-solving performance (Kasneci et al., 2023; Lo, 2023), and students who use it report greater confidence and improved understanding of complex ideas (Deng & Yu, 2023). However, ChatGPT also carries significant limitations that must be considered in educational contexts. Dwivedi et al. (2023) highlight that the tool can generate plausible but inaccurate information a phe­ nomenon known as ‘hallucination’ and may reflect disciplinary biases. Kasneci et al. (2023) caution that habitual reliance on ChatGPT, without critical evaluation of its outputs, may gradually erode students’ independent reasoning capacity. Baidoo-Anu and Owusu Ansah (2023) further warn that AI-generated responses can oversimplify complex problems, potentially reinforcing surface-level rather than deep learning. These limitations underscore the importance of structured, pedagogically balanced approaches when integrating ChatGPT into university settings. This study therefore examines both the performance benefits and the attitudinal complexities of ChatGPT use in Sri Lankan higher education. 1.1. Research problem",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,3,"learning. These limitations underscore the importance of structured, pedagogically balanced approaches when integrating ChatGPT into university settings. This study therefore examines both the performance benefits and the attitudinal complexities of ChatGPT use in Sri Lankan higher education. 1.1. Research problem and gap Although problem-solving is important for students’ success, many university students in Sri Lanka strug­ gle with these skills. Traditional teaching methods in Sri Lankan universities often involve teachers explaining solutions to students, but this does not help students learn how to solve new problems on their own. Freeman et al. (2014) found that when students only watch their teachers solve problems without trying themselves, they do not develop the thinking skills they need. The study institution, like many other Sri Lankan universities, seeks to find better ways to help stu­ dents develop problem-solving skills. While ChatGPT shows promise as a learning tool, existing Sri Lankan research has focused primarily on adoption patterns and technology acceptance rather than on measurable learning outcomes. Specifically, the impact of ChatGPT on students’ problem-solving per­ formance in Sri Lankan public universities and the",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,3,"as a learning tool, existing Sri Lankan research has focused primarily on adoption patterns and technology acceptance rather than on measurable learning outcomes. Specifically, the impact of ChatGPT on students’ problem-solving per­ formance in Sri Lankan public universities and the conditions under which it can be effectively inte­ grated into classroom practice remains underexplored. This creates an important research gap. While several studies such as Biyiri et al. (2025), Sabraz Nawaz et al. (2024) and Nishshanka et al. (2025) have examined ChatGPT adoption patterns, awareness factors and acceptance among Sri Lankan undergraduates, limited research has examined its direct impact on 2 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",106
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,4,"measurable learning outcomes such as problem-solving performance. Notably, Nishshanka et al. (2025) identified ‘understanding complex and unknown facts’ and ‘additional ideas and information’ as key per­ ceived benefits of AI tools among Sri Lankan students yet no study has experimentally verified whether these perceived benefits translate into actual performance gains in structured problem-solving tasks. Beyond the geographic gap, existing literature also reveals a theoretical deficiency: few studies have examined the specific mechanisms through which AI-mediated dialogue scaffolds the problem-solving process, particularly within supply chain management education. Without this knowledge, educators and university administrators cannot make good decisions about whether and how to use AI tools like ChatGPT in their teaching. 1.2. Research objectives 1.2.1. Main research question How does ChatGPT usage affect problem-solving skills development among undergraduate students at a public university in Sri Lanka? 1.2.2. Sub-questions 1. What are students’ attitudes towards using ChatGPT for learning and problem-solving? 2. How does ChatGPT usage influence students’ problem-solving performance compared to traditional learning methods? 3. What factors determine students’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes? 4. What are the benefits",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,4,"students’ attitudes towards using ChatGPT for learning and problem-solving? 2. How does ChatGPT usage influence students’ problem-solving performance compared to traditional learning methods? 3. What factors determine students’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes? 4. What are the benefits and challenges students experience when using ChatGPT for problem-solving tasks? The main objective of this study is to investigate how ChatGPT as an AI tool affects problem-solving skills among undergraduate students at the study institution. Specific objectives are:  To assess the impact of ChatGPT usage on students’ attitudes towards problem-solving  To evaluate students’ problem-solving performance when using ChatGPT compared to traditional learning methods  To identify factors that affect students’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes  To explore the benefits and challenges of using ChatGPT for problem-solving 2. Literature review 2.1. Artificial intelligence in educational contexts Since the first computer tutoring systems were created in the 1970s, AI in the education sector has come a long way. Today, computer systems can learn and adapt themselves, evolving from simple com­ puter programmes (Holmes et al., 2022; Woolf",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,4,"Since the first computer tutoring systems were created in the 1970s, AI in the education sector has come a long way. Today, computer systems can learn and adapt themselves, evolving from simple com­ puter programmes (Holmes et al., 2022; Woolf et al., 2013). Currently, in education, different types of AI tools exist, such as student-oriented learning platforms, automatic grading systems and chatbots that provide facilities for students to interact with AI and receive answers to their questions (Luckin et al., 2016; Xu & Ouyang, 2022; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Research shows that AI genuinely helps students learn better. Holmes et al. (2022) reviewed multiple studies on AI in education and found that students using technology-enhanced learning, including AI tools, demonstrated measurable gains in learning outcomes compared to students in traditional lecture settings. Kasneci et al. (2023) found that students were more engaged and remembered more of what they learned when supported by AI-driven tools. Most recently, Achuthan (2025), in a meta-analysis of 32 empirical studies involving 3029 participants, reported a large and statistically significant positive effect of AI-based interventions on",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,4,"more engaged and remembered more of what they learned when supported by AI-driven tools. Most recently, Achuthan (2025), in a meta-analysis of 32 empirical studies involving 3029 participants, reported a large and statistically significant positive effect of AI-based interventions on self-regulated learning (g ¼ 1.613, p ¼ 0.032), with AI functioning as an adaptive scaffold particularly effective during the forethought and planning phases of learning. These benefits were most pronounced for older learners engaged in longer interventions, a pattern directly relevant to the undergraduate context of this study. COGENT EDUCATION 3",91
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,5,"According to Popenici and Kerr (2017) and more recent reviews (Kasneci et al., 2023), AI helps students learn in three main ways. First, it provides immediate feedback, so students know right away whether they understand something. Second, it adjusts the difficulty of content to keep students challenged but not frus­ trated. Third, it can present information in different ways to match how different students learn best. 2.2. Problem-solving skills in higher education Problem-solving in university is a complex process that involves many thinking skills. Smith and Kosslyn (2007) identify four main components of good problem-solving: finding and understanding the problem, thinking of ways to solve it, trying out solutions and checking whether the solution worked. More recent cognitive science perspectives affirm these components while emphasising the role of metacognition and self-regulation in sustaining problem-solving performance (Deng & Yu, 2023; Zimmerman, 2002). To do this well, students need not only subject knowledge but also skills like critical thinking, creativity, and metacognitive awareness. Problem-solving is important beyond just doing well in university. The World Economic Forum (2020) identifies complex problem-solving as one",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,5,"2002). To do this well, students need not only subject knowledge but also skills like critical thinking, creativity, and metacognitive awareness. Problem-solving is important beyond just doing well in university. The World Economic Forum (2020) identifies complex problem-solving as one of the most important skills for future employment. Because of this, universities around the world are trying to find better ways to teach these important skills. Typically, universities teach problem-solving by demonstrating how to solve problems, providing examples, and having student’s practice. While these methods can work, Freeman et al. (2014) found they often do not help students learn how to solve new types of problems they have not encountered before a conclusion reinforced by more recent evidence on active learning efficacy (Holmes et al., 2022; Mollick & Mollick, 2023). Students often have trouble applying what they learned when they face differ­ ent situations. 2.3. ChatGPT as an educational tool OpenAI created ChatGPT as a chatbot that can have conversations with people. It uses Generative Pre- trained Transformer (GPT) technology to understand questions and provide answers (Brown et al., 2020).",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,5,"face differ­ ent situations. 2.3. ChatGPT as an educational tool OpenAI created ChatGPT as a chatbot that can have conversations with people. It uses Generative Pre- trained Transformer (GPT) technology to understand questions and provide answers (Brown et al., 2020). It is a useful AI tool for students because it can understand questions asked in natural language and explain concepts step by step. However, AI tools like ChatGPT also have significant limitations that warrant careful consideration. Dwivedi et al. (2023) point out that ChatGPT can produce misinformation that appears credible a phe­ nomenon known as ‘hallucination’ and may lack the newest information because it cannot update itself after its training cutoff. Baidoo-Anu and Owusu Ansah (2023) further caution that AI-generated responses may embed disciplinary biases and oversimplify complex problems, potentially leading stu­ dents towards superficial rather than deep learning. Additionally, over-reliance on AI is a documented concern: Kasneci et al. (2023) warn that if students habitually defer to ChatGPT without critically evaluat­ ing its outputs, their independent reasoning skills may atrophy over time. These concerns are borne out by recent",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,5,"over-reliance on AI is a documented concern: Kasneci et al. (2023) warn that if students habitually defer to ChatGPT without critically evaluat­ ing its outputs, their independent reasoning skills may atrophy over time. These concerns are borne out by recent empirical evidence. Laghari et al. (2025), in a mixed-methods study of university educators and students, found that while ChatGPT significantly enhanced creativity, active learning and efficiency, it also contributed to underdeveloped problem-solving skills and reduced critical thinking when used without structured pedagogical guidance. These findings strengthen the rationale for this study’s struc­ tured, rubric-based experimental design. These limitations underscore the importance of structured, bal­ anced pedagogical approaches when integrating ChatGPT into higher education. Several universities have integrated ChatGPT into formal educational settings. The University of Hong Kong introduced institutional guidelines enabling structured ChatGPT use in undergraduate courses (Lo, 2023). The Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) has explored AI-assisted problem-solving work­ shops in engineering programmes, finding that students who critically engage with AI-generated out­ puts develop stronger analytical skills (Mollick & Mollick, 2023). Similarly, Monash University in Australia has incorporated LLM-based",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,5,"Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) has explored AI-assisted problem-solving work­ shops in engineering programmes, finding that students who critically engage with AI-generated out­ puts develop stronger analytical skills (Mollick & Mollick, 2023). Similarly, Monash University in Australia has incorporated LLM-based tools into business case-study curricula to scaffold strategic thinking (Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023). ChatGPT is especially helpful for explaining complex ideas in differ­ ent ways in science, management, and mathematics subjects. 4 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",78
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,6,"Early research on ChatGPT in education shows promising results, though more studies are needed since it is still relatively new. Kasneci et al. (2023) found that when students used ChatGPT for practice, they performed better at solving new problems they had not seen before. Deng and Yu (2023) also found that students have positive attitudes about solving difficult problems after using ChatGPT. 2.4. Theoretical frameworks This study draws on three complementary theoretical frameworks that together explain how ChatGPT may scaffold problem-solving development: Vygotsky’s zone of proximal development (ZPD), cognitive load theory and the technology acceptance model (TAM). 2.4.1. Zone of proximal development (ZPD) Vygotsky (1978) defined the ZPD as the distance between what a learner can achieve independently and what they can achieve with knowledgeable guidance. In the context of ChatGPT-assisted prob­ lem-solving, the AI functions as a dynamic scaffold, helping students tackle supply chain challenges that would be beyond their independent reach such as designing multi-echelon inventory systems or evaluating trade-offs between sourcing strategies without directly providing answers. As students gain competence through AI-mediated interaction, they internalise problem-solving strategies",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,6,"helping students tackle supply chain challenges that would be beyond their independent reach such as designing multi-echelon inventory systems or evaluating trade-offs between sourcing strategies without directly providing answers. As students gain competence through AI-mediated interaction, they internalise problem-solving strategies and gradually reduce their dependence on external support, consistent with Vygotsky’s notion of scaf­ folded learning. 2.4.2. Cognitive load theory Sweller (1988) proposed that learning is constrained by the limited capacity of working memory. This framework has since been applied extensively in technology-enhanced learning contexts (Deng & Yu, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023). Achuthan’s (2025) meta-analysis provides recent empirical support for this mechanism, demonstrating that AI-based interventions produced moderate gains in cognitive and meta­ cognitive regulation (g ¼ 0.377, p < 0.001) by supporting learners’ planning, monitoring and self-reflec­ tion processes consistent with the cognitive load reduction hypothesis. Extraneous cognitive load arising from poorly designed tasks or unfamiliar concepts competes with germane load, which drives schema acquisition. ChatGPT may reduce extraneous load by providing immediate clarification of unfamiliar sup­ ply chain terminology or case-study context, thereby freeing cognitive resources for",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,6,"load arising from poorly designed tasks or unfamiliar concepts competes with germane load, which drives schema acquisition. ChatGPT may reduce extraneous load by providing immediate clarification of unfamiliar sup­ ply chain terminology or case-study context, thereby freeing cognitive resources for deeper analytical engagement. This is particularly relevant in the supply chain management context, where students must simultaneously manage domain knowledge, strategic frameworks and implementation planning within a constrained time period. 2.4.3. Technology acceptance model (TAM) Davis (1989) proposed that people are more likely to use technology if they perceive it as useful and easy to use. Scherer et al. (2019) added that for educational technology, it also matters whether the technology fits with how classes are taught and whether the school supports its use. TAM guides ana­ lysis of student willingness to adopt ChatGPT and helps identify implementation barriers that educators must address. Constructivist learning theory posits that people learn by actively building their own understanding, not just by receiving information (Piaget, 1976). Synthesising these frameworks, ChatGPT is conceptual­ ised in this study as a constructivist scaffold (Piaget, 1976) operating",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,6,"educators must address. Constructivist learning theory posits that people learn by actively building their own understanding, not just by receiving information (Piaget, 1976). Synthesising these frameworks, ChatGPT is conceptual­ ised in this study as a constructivist scaffold (Piaget, 1976) operating within the ZPD (Vygotsky, 1978) while managing cognitive load (Sweller, 1988), with adoption shaped by perceived usefulness and ease of use (Davis, 1989). Together, these theories predict that AI assistance should improve problem-solving performance by extending students’ cognitive reach, reducing extraneous mental effort and maintaining engagement provided students perceive the tool as both useful and accessible. COGENT EDUCATION 5",99
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,7,"2.5. Research gaps While several studies such as Biyiri et al. (2025) and Sabraz Nawaz et al. (2024) have examined ChatGPT adoption and acceptance in Sri Lanka, limited research has focused specifically on its impact on stu­ dents’ problem-solving skills in this context. This gap is significant: technology acceptance and actual learning outcomes are distinct constructs, and acceptance of ChatGPT does not automatically translate into improved problem-solving performance. Beyond the geographic gap, the existing literature also reveals a theoretical and methodological defi­ ciency. Most prior studies have examined AI in education through correlational surveys or self-report measures, without employing controlled experimental designs that isolate the causal effect of AI assist­ ance on problem-solving outcomes (Kasneci et al., 2023). Furthermore, the specific mechanisms through which conversational AI scaffolds higher-order thinking in professional disciplines such as supply chain management remain theoretically underdeveloped. This study addresses both gaps by employing a mixed-methods experimental design that combines quantitative performance measurement with qualita­ tive exploration of the learning process. 3. Research methodology 3.1. Research design This study uses a mixed-methods approach that combines quantitative and",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,7,"This study addresses both gaps by employing a mixed-methods experimental design that combines quantitative performance measurement with qualita­ tive exploration of the learning process. 3. Research methodology 3.1. Research design This study uses a mixed-methods approach that combines quantitative and qualitative research meth­ ods, following a convergent parallel design (Creswell & Creswell, 2018). This allows the researcher to measure ChatGPT’s effects on problem-solving performance while also understanding students’ experien­ ces and perspectives. The experimental component addresses causal inference, while the qualitative component illuminates the underlying mechanisms through which AI assistance shapes student learning. The quantitative component involves an experimental design where students in the experimental group receive ChatGPT assistance when solving problem-solving tasks, while students in the control group solve the same tasks using traditional methods without AI assistance. This parallel-group structure helps determine whether ChatGPT assistance improves problem-solving performance while controlling for task content and time on task. 3.2. Study hypotheses This study proposes the following hypotheses based on current literature and theoretical frameworks: Hypothesis 1: Students who use ChatGPT demonstrate significantly more positive attitudes towards problem-solving than students",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,7,"while controlling for task content and time on task. 3.2. Study hypotheses This study proposes the following hypotheses based on current literature and theoretical frameworks: Hypothesis 1: Students who use ChatGPT demonstrate significantly more positive attitudes towards problem-solving than students who do not use ChatGPT. Hypothesis 2: Students who use ChatGPT report significantly higher performance scores for solving complex problems than students who do not use ChatGPT. Hypothesis 3: Perceived usefulness, ease of use and prior experience significantly influence stu­ dents’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes. Hypothesis 4: ChatGPT usage significantly enhances problem-solving skills, as assessed by an ana­ lytic rubric across five dimensions, among undergraduate students at the study institution. 3.3. Participants and sampling 3.3.1. Target population The study focuses on undergraduate students from a public university in Sri Lanka who are studying supply chain management courses. Supply chain management involves planning how products move from manufacturers to customers, which requires strong problem-solving skills. 6 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",163
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,8,"3.3.2. Sample size and justification The study included 74 students (37 in each group). A priori power analysis using GPower (Faul et al., 2007) indicated that to detect a medium effect size (Cohen’s d ¼ 0.50) at a ¼ 0.05 with 80% power for an independent-samples t-test, a minimum of 64 participants (32 per group) would be required. The final sample of 74 students (37 per group) thus provides adequate statistical power to detect effects of at least medium magnitude. 3.3.3. Sampling method Participants were drawn from the population of third-year undergraduate students enrolled in supply chain management courses at the study institution (N  120 in the target cohort). A convenience sam­ pling approach was used, as participation was offered to all students in the relevant courses and 74 vol­ unteered. Although this approach limits strict generalizability, the use of random assignment within the volunteer sample after students agreed to participate preserves internal validity by ensuring that group differences in outcomes can be attributed to the experimental manipulation rather than pre-existing dif­ ferences. The gender distribution (86.5% female) reflects",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,8,"of random assignment within the volunteer sample after students agreed to participate preserves internal validity by ensuring that group differences in outcomes can be attributed to the experimental manipulation rather than pre-existing dif­ ferences. The gender distribution (86.5% female) reflects the natural enrolment pattern in commerce and management programmes at this institution, where female students substantially outnumber male students. This gender composition is consistent with broader trends in Sri Lankan business education (Sabraz Nawaz et al., 2024) and should be considered when generalising findings to mixed-gender cohorts. 3.3.4. Random assignment to groups Once students agreed to participate, they were randomly assigned to either the experimental group (with ChatGPT) or the control group (without ChatGPT), using a computer-generated random number sequence. Pre-experiment equivalence between groups on prior ChatGPT experience and demographic characteristics was confirmed, supporting the assumption that the groups were comparable at baseline. 3.4. Data collection instruments 3.4.1. Case study development The supply chain management lecturer created case studies (Appendix A) to ensure they are realistic and appropriate for undergraduate students. The cases involve scenarios such as managing disruptions when",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,8,"comparable at baseline. 3.4. Data collection instruments 3.4.1. Case study development The supply chain management lecturer created case studies (Appendix A) to ensure they are realistic and appropriate for undergraduate students. The cases involve scenarios such as managing disruptions when suppliers cannot deliver materials, choosing the best suppliers for a company, deciding how much inventory to keep in warehouses, and planning transportation and distribution networks. Table 2. Prior ChatGPT experience of participants. Control group (N ¼ 37) Experimental group (N ¼ 37) Both group (N ¼ 74) Yes No Yes No Yes No I do not know ChatGPT 0 37 0 37 0 74 I heard about it, but I have not used it yet 0 37 0 37 0 74 I have only used ChatGPT once for a test 0 37 0 37 0 74 I have used it several times, but I do not use it regularly 23 14 19 18 42 32 Use it several times a month 26 11 26 11 52 22 Use it several times a week 27 10 23 14 50 24 Use",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,8,"several times, but I do not use it regularly 23 14 19 18 42 32 Use it several times a month 26 11 26 11 52 22 Use it several times a week 27 10 23 14 50 24 Use it daily 16 21 14 23 30 44 Table 1. Analytic scoring rubric. Criteria Points Description Problem understanding 24 Can the student identify what the real problems are and who is affected? Strategic thinking 24 Does the student apply supply chain concepts and think about the bigger picture? Solution quality 24 Are the proposed solutions practical, creative and likely to work? Implementation planning 20 Does the student think about how to put their solutions into practice? Communication 8 Is the response clear, well-organised and professional? COGENT EDUCATION 7",128
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,9,"3.4.2. Analytic scoring rubric The study uses a 100-point scoring system (as shown in Table 1) that evaluates different aspects of problem-solving: TAM constructs (perceived usefulness, ease of use, intention to use) were measured using Likert-scale survey items adapted from Davis (1989) and Scherer et al. (2019). Internal consistency for each construct is reported in Section 4 alongside descriptive statistics. To ensure scoring reliability, two independent raters, the lead researcher and a second supply chain management lecturer scored a random sub-sample of 20 scripts (10 from each group) blind to group membership. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen’s Kappa (j ¼ 0.81), indicating strong agree­ ment (Landis & Koch, 1977). Discrepancies were resolved through discussion, and the final agreed-upon scoring criteria were applied consistently to all 74 scripts. Raters were blinded to group allocation throughout the scoring process. 3.4.3. Intervention description (TIDieR checklist) The ChatGPT-assisted intervention consisted of a 15-min structured orientation on effective prompt formulation (Brief name: ChatGPT-assisted problem-solving). The intervention was delivered in a single 60-min session (When/How much). Students used the ChatGPT web interface (GPT-4, accessed July",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,9,"description (TIDieR checklist) The ChatGPT-assisted intervention consisted of a 15-min structured orientation on effective prompt formulation (Brief name: ChatGPT-assisted problem-solving). The intervention was delivered in a single 60-min session (When/How much). Students used the ChatGPT web interface (GPT-4, accessed July 2025) on personal devices (How: individually, in a supervised classroom). The case studies (Appendix A) were identical for both groups; the only difference was access to ChatGPT (Tailoring). The control group used textbooks and course notes only. No modifications to the protocol were made during the study. 3.5. Data collection process 3.5.1. Phase 1: Baseline assessment Before the experiment began, all students completed surveys about background information (age, gen­ der, province), prior experience with ChatGPT, confidence in problem-solving abilities, and attitudes towards technology. Baseline equivalence between groups was subsequently verified using chi-square tests for categorical variables, confirming that the groups did not differ significantly on any demographic or prior-experience variable. 3.5.2. Phase 2: Problem-solving test 3.5.2.1. For the experimental group (with ChatGPT).  Students receive a 15-min orientation on how to use ChatGPT effectively for problem-solving.  Main assessment: 60",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,9,"did not differ significantly on any demographic or prior-experience variable. 3.5.2. Phase 2: Problem-solving test 3.5.2.1. For the experimental group (with ChatGPT).  Students receive a 15-min orientation on how to use ChatGPT effectively for problem-solving.  Main assessment: 60 min to complete comprehensive case studies (Appendix A) with ChatGPT assist­ ance available. 3.5.2.2. For the control group (without ChatGPT).  Main assessment: 60 min to complete the same case studies (Appendix A) using traditional methods.  Access to textbooks and course materials, but no internet or AI tools.  To minimise environmental variability between testing days, both sessions were conducted at the same time of day, in the same classroom, with identical written instructions distributed to partici­ pants and the same invigilator present. 3.5.3. Phase 3: Post-experiment assessment After completing the problem-solving tests, students participate in follow-up surveys about their experi­ ence and individual interviews with selected participants. 4. Data analysis Prior to inferential testing, the normality of score distributions within each group was examined using the Shapiro–Wilk test (experimental group: W ¼ 0.96, p ¼ 0.24; control group:",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,9,"their experi­ ence and individual interviews with selected participants. 4. Data analysis Prior to inferential testing, the normality of score distributions within each group was examined using the Shapiro–Wilk test (experimental group: W ¼ 0.96, p ¼ 0.24; control group: W ¼ .97, p ¼ 0.41), con­ firming that parametric tests were appropriate. Independent samples t-tests were then used to compare average problem-solving scores between the ChatGPT group and the traditional group, with further 8 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",80
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,10,"analysis examining whether factors like prior ChatGPT experience, gender, or background affected how much students benefited from AI assistance. For the qualitative component, interview transcripts were carefully analysed to identify common themes in student experiences, following Braun and Clarke’s (2006) six-phase thematic analysis framework, to understand why ChatGPT helps and how its integration can be improved. 4.1. Ethical considerations 4.1.1. Ethical approval This study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki principles forresearch involving human participants. Study approved by the Centre for Management Research (CMR), Faculty of the Commerce and Management Studies, University of Kelaniya, Sri Lanka(Letter dated July 05, 2025).The CMR reviewed and approved the study design,which involved minimal-risk educational activities using standard classroom problem-solvingexercises with anonymous data collection. 4.1.2. Informed consent All participants provided both written and verbal informed consent prior to participation in the study. Participants first received comprehensive written information sheets explaining the study purpose, pro­ cedures, potential risks and benefits and their right to withdraw at any time without penalty. Written consent forms were then signed before participation began. Verbal confirmation of willingness",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,10,"Participants first received comprehensive written information sheets explaining the study purpose, pro­ cedures, potential risks and benefits and their right to withdraw at any time without penalty. Written consent forms were then signed before participation began. Verbal confirmation of willingness to partici­ pate was additionally obtained immediately prior to each session. Participants were given adequate time to review the information and ask questions before providing consent. All participants were over 18 years of age, and no minors were included in this study. 4.1.3. Student welfare No student is harmed by participating. Both groups receive valuable learning experiences, just in differ­ ent ways. 4.1.4. Fair treatment After the study, control group students receive information about ChatGPT so they can benefit from the technology too. 4.1.5. Privacy protection All student data is kept confidential and anonymous. 4.2. Results and analysis 4.2.1. Participant demographics The study included 74 undergraduate students from a public university in Sri Lanka, representing a typ­ ical cross-section of business and management students in Sri Lankan higher education. Figure 1. Participant gender distribution. COGENT EDUCATION 9",177
