DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,11,"Gender distribution (as shown in Figure 1) showed a clear majority of female participants, with 64 stu­ dents (86.5%) compared to 10 male students (13.5%). This gender distribution reflects the natural enrol­ ment pattern in commerce and management programmes at the study institution, where female students substantially outnumber male students, consistent with broader trends in Sri Lankan business education (Sabraz Nawaz et al., 2024). Age distribution indicated that participants were primarily third- year undergraduate students. The majority were 22 years old (47 students, 63.5%), followed by 23-year- olds (15 students, 20.3%) and 21-year-olds (12 students, 16.2%). Provincial representation (as shown in Figure 2) showed concentration from the Western Province with 45 students (60.8%) from this region. Other provinces were represented proportionally: Central Province contributed 7 students (9.5%), while Northwestern, Sabaragamuwa, and Southern provinces each contributed 6 students (8.1%). 4.2.2. Prior ChatGPT experience Analysis of participants’ prior experience with ChatGPT revealed widespread awareness of the technol­ ogy, with no students reporting complete unfamiliarity. As shown in Table 2, the distribution showed: 42 students (56.8%) reported using ChatGPT several times but not",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,11,"ChatGPT experience Analysis of participants’ prior experience with ChatGPT revealed widespread awareness of the technol­ ogy, with no students reporting complete unfamiliarity. As shown in Table 2, the distribution showed: 42 students (56.8%) reported using ChatGPT several times but not regularly, 52 students (70.3%) several times monthly, 50 students (67.6%) several times weekly and 30 students (40.5%) daily usage. When comparing between control and experimental groups, usage patterns were remarkably similar, with no statistically significant differences in prior experience (v2 tests, all p > 0.05). These baseline simi­ larities in ChatGPT experience were crucial for the experimental design, as they ensured that any observed differences in performance could be attributed to the experimental manipulation rather than pre-existing differences in AI familiarity. Figure 2. Province distribution. Table 3. Descriptive statistics for control group. N Minimum Maximum Mean Std. deviation Variance Statistic Statistic Statistic Statistic Statistic Statistic Problem understanding 37 6.00 18.00 13.65 2.90 8.40 Strategic thinking 37 4.00 16.00 10.49 2.95 8.70 Solution quality 37 6.00 18.00 12.46 3.02 9.14 Implementation planning 37 2.00 12.00 5.32 2.52 6.34 Communication 37 2.00",180
