DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,12,"4.2.3. Problem-solving performance analysis The research question focused on whether ChatGPT assistance would improve problem-solving perform­ ance compared to traditional methods. The results provide clear evidence of substantial improvement across all measured dimensions. The control group, working without ChatGPT assistance, achieved a mean total score of 45.86 points (SD ¼ 11.75) out of a possible 100 points. In contrast, the experimental group using ChatGPT assistance achieved a significantly higher mean total score of 66.92 points (SD ¼ 11.38). This represents a mean dif­ ference of 21.05 points, or approximately 46% improvement over the control group’s performance. Statistical analysis confirmed the significance of this difference (t ¼ −7.829, p < 0.001), with the effect size (Cohen’s d ¼ 1.82) indicating not just statistical significance but substantial practical importance. The 95% confidence interval for the difference (−26.42 to −15.69) confirms the robustness of this improvement. Tables 3–5 present the detailed descriptive statistics and t-test analysis for both groups. 4.2.4. Individual performance criteria Problem understanding criteria (24 points): The control group achieved a mean score of 13.65 (SD ¼ 2.90), while the experimental",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,12,"of this improvement. Tables 3–5 present the detailed descriptive statistics and t-test analysis for both groups. 4.2.4. Individual performance criteria Problem understanding criteria (24 points): The control group achieved a mean score of 13.65 (SD ¼ 2.90), while the experimental group scored 17.78 (SD ¼ 2.85). The improvement of 4.14 points repre­ sents a 30% enhancement (t ¼ −6.189, p < 0.001, Cohen’s d ¼ 1.44). Strategic thinking criteria (24 points): The control group achieved a mean of 10.49 (SD ¼ 2.95), com­ pared to the experimental group’s 15.84 (SD ¼ 2.70). The improvement of 5.35 points represents a 51% enhancement (t ¼ −8.136, p < 0.001, Cohen’s d ¼ 1.89). Solution quality criteria (24 points): The control group scored 12.46 (SD ¼ 3.02), while the experimen­ tal group achieved 18.24 (SD ¼ 2.31). The improvement of 5.78 points represents a 46% enhancement (t ¼ −9.239, p < 0.001, Cohen’s d ¼ 2.15). This component showed the largest effect size among all dimensions. Implementation planning criteria (20 points): The control group achieved 5.32 (SD ¼ 2.52), compared to the experimental",180
