DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,16,"4.2.6. Student attitudes Students in the control group provided insights about AI-assisted learning based on their awareness of ChatGPT technology. When asked about ChatGPT’s potential usefulness, responses were strongly posi­ tive. Twenty-seven out of 37 students (72.97%, hereafter reported as 73.0%) agreed or strongly agreed that ChatGPT would be useful for completing tasks more effectively. These results are presented in Table 6. Students who used ChatGPT during problem-solving provided insights into the reality of AI-assisted learning. Regarding productivity improvement, 22 out of 37 students (59.5%) agreed or strongly agreed that ChatGPT improved their productivity. Ease of use experiences showed 22 students (59.5%) found ChatGPT easy to use and interact with. Overall satisfaction was balanced, with 18 students (48.6%) satisfied with their ChatGPT experience, 8 students (21.6%) dissatisfied, and 11 students (29.7%) neutral. Future usage intentions remained positive despite mixed satisfaction, with 22 students (59.5%) intending to continue using ChatGPT. These results are summarised in Table 7. Trust in ChatGPT information was notably cautious, with only 10 out of 37 students (27.0%) express­ ing trust, 19 students (51.4%) remaining neutral, and",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,16,"with 22 students (59.5%) intending to continue using ChatGPT. These results are summarised in Table 7. Trust in ChatGPT information was notably cautious, with only 10 out of 37 students (27.0%) express­ ing trust, 19 students (51.4%) remaining neutral, and 8 students (21.6%) expressing distrust. This indi­ cates that students maintained critical evaluation skills rather than accepting AI-generated information uncritically. 4.2.7. Qualitative insights Post-experiment interviews were conducted with 35 students drawn from both groups (17 from the experimental group and 18 from the control group), selected using purposive sampling (Creswell & Creswell, 2018) to maximise the diversity of perspectives captured. Specifically, participants were selected to ensure representation across three criteria: (1) performance level high scorers (top third of rubric scores), mid-range scorers (middle third), and lower scorers (bottom third) were included from each group; (2) prior ChatGPT experience both frequent users (daily or several times weekly) and less frequent users (sev­ eral times monthly or irregularly) were included; and (3) group membership both the experimental and control groups were represented to capture both actual-use and anticipated-use perspectives. All 74 partic­",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,16,"users (daily or several times weekly) and less frequent users (sev­ eral times monthly or irregularly) were included; and (3) group membership both the experimental and control groups were represented to capture both actual-use and anticipated-use perspectives. All 74 partic­ ipants were invited to volunteer for an interview; 35 agreed and were selected from among volunteers in a way that satisfied the above criteria. This purposive approach is consistent with the goal of qualitative inquiry in a convergent mixed-methods design, which is to illuminate the mechanisms and range of expe­ riences underlying the quantitative findings rather than to achieve statistical representativeness (Braun & Clarke, 2006). Thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2006) identified five major themes. 4.2.7.1. Theme 1: Balanced integration approach. The most common theme was preference for bal­ anced integration of ChatGPT with traditional learning methods rather than complete reliance on AI. This theme appeared in 28 out of 35 interview responses (80%). One student noted: ‘I think it is better if we use ChatGPT in our learning process but make sure it does not harm our own abilities’.",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,16,"reliance on AI. This theme appeared in 28 out of 35 interview responses (80%). One student noted: ‘I think it is better if we use ChatGPT in our learning process but make sure it does not harm our own abilities’. This qualita­ tive evidence supports the constructivist interpretation: students were actively negotiating the bounda­ ries of AI assistance, reflecting on their own cognitive processes, and seeking to preserve their independent reasoning precisely the metacognitive engagement that Vygotsky (1978) associates with internalising scaffolded learning. 4.2.7.2. Theme 2: Concerns about dependency. A significant concern expressed by 22 out of 35 stu­ dents (63%) focused on potential negative impacts of over-reliance on ChatGPT on cognitive abilities and skill development. One student stated: ‘ChatGPT is not always the better way because using it too much may reduce our creativity and thinking ability’. These concerns align with documented risks of AI over-reliance identified in the literature (Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023). 4.2.7.3. Theme 3: Contextual usage. Students demonstrated good understanding of when ChatGPT use was most appropriate, appearing in 19 out",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,16,"align with documented risks of AI over-reliance identified in the literature (Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023). 4.2.7.3. Theme 3: Contextual usage. Students demonstrated good understanding of when ChatGPT use was most appropriate, appearing in 19 out of 35 responses (54%). One student summarised: ‘When COGENT EDUCATION 15",51
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,17,"necessary, using ChatGPT for support is better’. This contextual awareness is consistent with self-regu­ lated learning behaviour (Zimmerman, 2002). 4.2.7.4. Theme 4: Learning enhancement. Despite concerns about dependency, 26 out of 35 students (74%) consistently recognised ChatGPT’s value for learning enhancement and accessing information. One student explained: ‘Yes, we can get the newest data and information from ChatGPT rather than tradi­ tional methods. But we should only use it to get a basic idea about our unknown topics’. 4.2.7.5. Theme 5: Educational integration support. Many students expressed support for formal inte­ gration of AI tools into educational systems. This theme appeared in 18 out of 35 responses (51%). One student stated: ‘I prefer AI tool-integrated education’. To ensure trustworthiness of the qualitative analysis, an audit trail was maintained throughout the process. This included: (1) verbatim transcription of all 35 interviews; (2) initial open coding conducted independently by both authors; (3) disagreements resolved through discussion until consensus was reached; (4) a codebook documenting theme definitions and exemplar quotes was retained and is avail­ able from the corresponding author on request. Member-checking",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,17,"(2) initial open coding conducted independently by both authors; (3) disagreements resolved through discussion until consensus was reached; (4) a codebook documenting theme definitions and exemplar quotes was retained and is avail­ able from the corresponding author on request. Member-checking was not conducted due to time con­ straints, which is acknowledged as a limitation 4.2.8. Hypothesis testing results 4.2.8.1. Hypothesis 1: Received partial support. Experimental group students developed positive atti­ tudes towards AI-assisted problem-solving; however, their attitudes were more measured than the expectations expressed by the control group, reflecting the gap between anticipated and experienced ease of use. 4.2.8.2. Hypothesis 2: Received strong statistical support. The experimental group achieved a 46% improvement in overall performance (p < 0.001, d ¼ 1.82) with statistically significant improvements across all five rubric components. 4.2.8.3. Hypothesis 3: Received descriptive support. Perceived usefulness showed a positive directional relationship with future usage intentions (perceived usefulness: M ¼ 3.49; behavioural intention: M ¼ 3.34), consistent with TAM predictions. However, formal inferential testing of the relationship between TAM constructs and intention to use was not conducted in this",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,17,"positive directional relationship with future usage intentions (perceived usefulness: M ¼ 3.49; behavioural intention: M ¼ 3.34), consistent with TAM predictions. However, formal inferential testing of the relationship between TAM constructs and intention to use was not conducted in this study, which is acknowledged as a methodological limitation. Future studies should employ regression or structural equation modelling to test these relationships inferentially. 4.2.8.4. Hypothesis 4: Received comprehensive support. Performance improvements were corroborated by qualitative evidence of enhanced understanding of complex concepts and improved strategic reasoning. 5. Discussion 5.1. Student attitudes towards using ChatGPT for learning and problem-solving Students held broadly positive but nuanced attitudes towards ChatGPT. In line with the TAM (Davis, 1989), ‘attitude’ here encompasses perceived usefulness, ease of use and overall acceptability as meas­ ured through the TAM survey. A notable expectation–experience gap emerged: 73.0% of control group students anticipated productivity benefits, yet only 59.5% of experimental group students reported them after actual use. Similarly, while 94.6% of control group students expected ChatGPT to be easy to use, only 59.5% of the experimental group found it so in",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,17,"students anticipated productivity benefits, yet only 59.5% of experimental group students reported them after actual use. Similarly, while 94.6% of control group students expected ChatGPT to be easy to use, only 59.5% of the experimental group found it so in practice—reflecting the cognitive demands of prompt formulation and critical evaluation of AI outputs. This expectation–experience gap is consistent with TAM predictions (Davis, 1989) and suggests that implementation challenges—including prompt for­ mulation difficulty, the need to critically evaluate AI responses, and time management demands—mod­ erated students’ actual experience of usefulness. This finding aligns with Albayati (2024), who found that 16 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",104
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,18,"undergraduate students held positive initial perceptions of ChatGPT as a learning assistant but reported that actual use revealed complexity and reliability concerns that tempered early enthusiasm. Trust levels were particularly cautious, with only 27.0% of experimental group students trusting ChatGPT-generated information, which, while indicative of critical thinking, may have limited their engagement with the tool. This cautious stance mirrors findings from Nishshanka et al. (2025), who reported that Sri Lankan Gen Z undergraduates valued ChatGPT primarily as a supplementary informa­ tion source rather than a trusted authority, reflecting a culturally grounded tendency towards critical verification of AI outputs. These findings suggest that positive initial attitudes are necessary but insuffi­ cient for effective AI-assisted learning; structured implementation support is essential to bridge the expectation–experience divide. Laghari et al. (2025) similarly found that students’ attitudes towards ChatGPT became more measured following actual academic use, with unguided integration consistently producing more mixed attitudinal outcomes than structured pedagogical approaches—reinforcing the case for institutional guidance over open-ended adoption. These findings address Research Objective 1 and Sub-question 1: students’ attitudes towards using ChatGPT for learning and",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,18,"academic use, with unguided integration consistently producing more mixed attitudinal outcomes than structured pedagogical approaches—reinforcing the case for institutional guidance over open-ended adoption. These findings address Research Objective 1 and Sub-question 1: students’ attitudes towards using ChatGPT for learning and problem-solving. Perceived usefulness moderated actual experience, and implementation support is critical for sustaining positive adoption attitudes. 5.2. Impact of ChatGPT on problem-solving performance compared to traditional learning Students using ChatGPT significantly outperformed their peers across all five rubric dimensions (mean: 66.92 vs. 45.86, d ¼ 1.82, p < 0.001), representing a 46% overall improvement. Solution Quality showed the largest effect (d ¼ 2.15), suggesting ChatGPT extended students’ problem-solving capacity consistent with Vygotsky (1978) ZPD AI functioned as a scaffold enabling performance beyond independent cap­ ability. Implementation Planning showed the greatest percentage gain (86%), reflecting ChatGPT’s value in reducing the high cognitive load of multi-step practical reasoning (Achuthan, 2025; Sweller, 1988). Qualitative evidence corroborates this: students reported using ChatGPT to explore unfamiliar frame­ works before independently refining their solutions, indicating active knowledge construction rather than passive copying. However, Fyfe and Rittle-Johnson",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,18,"cognitive load of multi-step practical reasoning (Achuthan, 2025; Sweller, 1988). Qualitative evidence corroborates this: students reported using ChatGPT to explore unfamiliar frame­ works before independently refining their solutions, indicating active knowledge construction rather than passive copying. However, Fyfe and Rittle-Johnson (2016) and Mollick and Mollick (2023) caution that premature AI feedback can impair deeper conceptual understanding, and the single-session design cannot confirm whether gains reflect durable skill development making longitudinal replication essential. These results address Research Objective 2 and Sub-question 2: evaluating ChatGPT’s impact on prob­ lem-solving performance compared to traditional methods. ChatGPT assistance produced significant, practically meaningful gains across all five performance dimensions. 5.3. Factors determining students’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes Consistent with TAM (Davis, 1989), perceived usefulness was the primary driver of students’ intention to continue using ChatGPT (perceived usefulness: M ¼ 3.49; behavioural intention: M ¼ 3.34). Students who found ChatGPT helpful for understanding complex supply chain concepts expressed stronger future usage intentions. Perceived ease of use, however, was conditional: prompt formulation difficulty and uncertainty about output reliability dampened adoption intention among less experienced",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,18,"M ¼ 3.34). Students who found ChatGPT helpful for understanding complex supply chain concepts expressed stronger future usage intentions. Perceived ease of use, however, was conditional: prompt formulation difficulty and uncertainty about output reliability dampened adoption intention among less experienced users, consist­ ent with Scherer et al. (2019) finding that ease of use is a context-dependent predictor in educational settings. Prior ChatGPT experience also mattered more frequent prior users reported greater confidence in evaluating outputs and sustained intention to use the tool. Institutionally, 51% of interviewees (18/35) expressed support for formal AI integration into the curriculum, suggesting that institutional endorse­ ment is itself a meaningful predictor of adoption intention, consistent with extended TAM frameworks (Scherer et al., 2019). These findings address Research Objective 3 and Sub-question 3: identifying factors determining stu­ dents’ intention to use ChatGPT for educational purposes. Perceived usefulness, prior experience, prompt confidence, and institutional support are the key determinants. COGENT EDUCATION 17",155
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,19,"5.4. Benefits and challenges experienced when using ChatGPT: qualitative insights The post-experiment interviews with 35 students identified both clear benefits and meaningful chal­ lenges of ChatGPT-assisted problem-solving. On the benefits side, 74% of students (26/35) valued ChatGPT for learning enhancement particularly for accessing structured explanations of unfamiliar con­ cepts and generating initial solution frameworks that they then independently refined. This use pattern reflects the ZPD scaffolding mechanism (Vygotsky, 1978): students used AI to extend their thinking rather than to replace it. Increased confidence in solution generation was also widely reported, especially in Implementation Planning where cognitive load is highest (Sweller, 1988). The primary challenge was prompt formulation students who struggled to articulate precise queries received unhelpful responses, reducing their perception of usefulness. Trust calibration was a second challenge: only 27.0% trusted ChatGPT’s outputs, and 63% (22/35) expressed concern about over-reli­ ance on AI eroding independent thinking, consistent with risks documented in the literature (Kasneci et al., 2023; Laghari et al., 2025). Notably, 80% of students (28/35) independently developed a balanced integration approach using ChatGPT alongside traditional methods without being instructed",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,19,"ance on AI eroding independent thinking, consistent with risks documented in the literature (Kasneci et al., 2023; Laghari et al., 2025). Notably, 80% of students (28/35) independently developed a balanced integration approach using ChatGPT alongside traditional methods without being instructed to do so reflecting strong metacognitive self-regulation (Zimmerman, 2002) and suggesting students are capable of responsible AI use when given the opportunity. These findings address Research Objective 4 and Sub-question 4: exploring the benefits and chal­ lenges students experience when using ChatGPT for problem-solving. AI-scaffolded reasoning and confi­ dence gains are the primary benefits; prompt difficulty, trust calibration, and over-reliance risk are the key challenges, with students independently developing balanced strategies to manage them. 5.5. Theoretical implications The study provides empirical support for the theoretical framework developed in Section 2.4. Students’ qualitative accounts describing how they used ChatGPT to explore unfamiliar supply chain concepts, refine their own thinking, and then critically evaluate AI-generated suggestions are consistent with Vygotsky (1978) ZPD: ChatGPT functioned as a more knowledgeable other, supporting performance within the zone until students internalised strategies sufficiently to proceed more",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,19,"unfamiliar supply chain concepts, refine their own thinking, and then critically evaluate AI-generated suggestions are consistent with Vygotsky (1978) ZPD: ChatGPT functioned as a more knowledgeable other, supporting performance within the zone until students internalised strategies sufficiently to proceed more independently. The study also extends the TAM by highlighting the importance of realistic expectations and imple­ mentation support in educational technology adoption. The gap between expected and actual ease of use underscores that technology acceptance in educational contexts requires more than perceived utility it requires adequate preparation and ongoing support structures. 5.6. Practical implications This study reveals several important considerations for implementing AI tools in education. Rather than banning AI use, educators should focus on developing balanced pedagogical approaches that leverage AI benefits while preserving opportunities for independent reasoning. Since AI is a relatively new tech­ nology, students need structured guidance on effective and critical AI use. Assignments should be rede­ signed to reward the quality of reasoning and implementation thinking rather than the accuracy of factual recall, as AI can readily supply the latter. 5.7. Limitations Single institution context:",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,19,"guidance on effective and critical AI use. Assignments should be rede­ signed to reward the quality of reasoning and implementation thinking rather than the accuracy of factual recall, as AI can readily supply the latter. 5.7. Limitations Single institution context: This research was conducted at one university in Sri Lanka, limiting generalisa­ tion to other institutional and cultural contexts. Convenience sampling: Because participants were volunteers from a specific cohort, findings may not be fully representative of the wider student population. Future studies should employ stratified sampling across multiple institutions. Short-term focus: This study examined immediate effects of ChatGPT use. Whether performance bene­ fits persist when AI assistance is removed, or whether they translate into durable problem-solving skills, requires longitudinal investigation. 18 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",126
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,20,"Testing day difference: Testing the groups on separate days introduced a potential source of environ­ mental variability, though steps were taken to standardise conditions as far as possible. Limited subject area: The focus on supply chain management may limit transferability to other disci­ plines. ChatGPT effectiveness may vary with domain and task type. Technology evolution: ChatGPT continues to evolve rapidly; findings based on the version used dur­ ing data collection (July 2025) may not apply to future iterations. 5.8. Conclusions This study demonstrated that ChatGPT assistance significantly enhanced problem-solving performance among undergraduate supply chain management students, with a 46% overall improvement and large effect sizes across all five rubric dimensions (p < 0.001 for all comparisons). Implementation Planning showed the largest percentage improvement (86%), and Solution Quality showed the largest effect size (d ¼ 2.15), indicating that AI was particularly valuable for generating and evaluating practical solutions. Beyond performance, qualitative evidence showed that students independently developed balanced approaches to AI use, critically evaluating AI-generated information and maintaining awareness of the risks of over-reliance. This finding supports the feasibility of AI-integrated",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,20,"valuable for generating and evaluating practical solutions. Beyond performance, qualitative evidence showed that students independently developed balanced approaches to AI use, critically evaluating AI-generated information and maintaining awareness of the risks of over-reliance. This finding supports the feasibility of AI-integrated pedagogy when students are provided with appropriate orientation and when assessments are designed to reward analytical reasoning. For the study institution, this study provides preliminary evidence, applicable specifically to third-year SCM undergraduates at one Sri Lankan public university, that a thoughtfully implemented ChatGPT inte­ gration can improve performance on complex problem-solving tasks. These findings should not be gen­ eralised beyond this specific population, discipline, and institutional context without further replication. 5.9. Recommendations for practice 5.9.1. For educators  Develop structured, balanced implementation approaches that leverage ChatGPT’s scaffolding bene­ fits while maintaining focus on independent skill development.  Provide explicit training on how to formulate effective prompts and critically evaluate AI-generated responses.  Redesign assessments to emphasise the quality of reasoning, implementation planning, and critical evaluation dimensions where students showed the greatest improvement with AI support. 5.9.2. For institutions  Develop",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,20,"on how to formulate effective prompts and critically evaluate AI-generated responses.  Redesign assessments to emphasise the quality of reasoning, implementation planning, and critical evaluation dimensions where students showed the greatest improvement with AI support. 5.9.2. For institutions  Develop clear, evidence-based policies for AI use in assessments, based on the finding that students who used ChatGPT performed significantly better on complex problem-solving tasks.  Invest in technical infrastructure and staff development to support AI-enhanced learning environments.  Integrate AI literacy including critical evaluation of AI outputs and awareness of hallucination risks into undergraduate curricula. 5.9.3. For future research  Conduct longitudinal studies to determine whether performance benefits persist after AI assistance is removed.  Examine ChatGPT effectiveness across different academic disciplines and task types.  Investigate the effect of structured versus unstructured AI use on the balance between performance gains and independent skill development.  Replicate the study with stratified samples across multiple institutions to strengthen generalizability. COGENT EDUCATION 19",161
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,21,"5.10. Final thoughts The World Economic Forum (2020) identified problem-solving skills and AI literacy as critical competen­ cies for future workforce success. This study demonstrates that AI integration in higher education is not only feasible but can produce meaningful, measurable improvements in student performance on com­ plex problem-solving tasks provided that integration is designed with attention to pedagogical goals, critical engagement, and balanced use. As AI tools continue to evolve, the challenge for educators is not to choose between AI and independent thinking, but to design learning environments in which each strengthens the other. Ethics approval statement This study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki principles for research involving human participants. Study approved by the Centre for Management Research (CMR), Faculty of the Commerce and Management Studies, University of Kelaniya, Sri Lanka (Letter dated July 05, 2025). The CMR reviewed and approved the study design, which involved minimal-risk educational activities using standard classroom problem-solving exer­ cises with anonymous data collection. Informed consent statement All participants provided both written and verbal informed consent prior to participation. Participants first",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,21,"The CMR reviewed and approved the study design, which involved minimal-risk educational activities using standard classroom problem-solving exer­ cises with anonymous data collection. Informed consent statement All participants provided both written and verbal informed consent prior to participation. Participants first received comprehensive written information sheets and completed written consent forms before the study began. Verbal confirmation of willingness to participate was additionally obtained immediately prior to each session. Students were informed about: (1) the study’s purpose and procedures; (2) the voluntary nature of participation; (3) their right to withdraw at any time without affecting their academic standing; (4) the anonymous data collection process with no personal identifiers; and (5) how the research findings would be used. All participants were adults (age 21– 23 years), and no minors were involved in this study. Author contribution statement CRediT: Dinuka Kannangara: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Software, Visualisation; Kaushalya Yatigammana: Conceptualization, Methodology, Resources, Supervision, Validation, Writing – review & editing. Disclosure statement The authors declare no conflicts of interest with respect",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,21,"analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing, Software, Visualisation; Kaushalya Yatigammana: Conceptualization, Methodology, Resources, Supervision, Validation, Writing – review & editing. Disclosure statement The authors declare no conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, or publication of this article. Declaration of funding This research received no specific grant from any funding agency in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. The study was conducted independently by the authors using institutional resources available at the participating university. About the authors Dinuka Kannangara is an Instructor in Computing and Technology at the University of Kelaniya, Kelaniya, Sri Lanka, where he oversees digital platforms and educational technology initiatives. He also serves as a visiting lecturer teaching graphic design and web design courses. His research interests include artificial intelligence in education, educational technology integration, and digital learning innovation in developing country contexts. ResearchGate: https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Dinuka-Kannangara-2 Prof. Kaushalya Yatigammana is a Professor in Digital Learning at the University of Kelaniya, Sri Lanka. She special­ izes in digital learning, management information systems and technology management. Prof. Yatigammana earned",180
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,21,"digital learning innovation in developing country contexts. ResearchGate: https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Dinuka-Kannangara-2 Prof. Kaushalya Yatigammana is a Professor in Digital Learning at the University of Kelaniya, Sri Lanka. She special­ izes in digital learning, management information systems and technology management. Prof. Yatigammana earned her Ph.D. in Management/Business from Management & Science University, Malaysia. ResearchGate: https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Kaushalya-Yatigammana 20 D. KANNANGARA AND K. YATIGAMMANA",58
DOC_022,Leveraging ChatGPT to enhance problem-solving skills and technology acceptance among undergraduates  an exploratory experimental study in Sri Lankan h.pdf,10.1080/2331186X.2026.2703353,22,"ORCID Dinuka Kannangara http://orcid.org/0000-0003-0462-9562 Kaushalya Yatigammana http://orcid.org/0000-0002-8057-8632 Data availability statement Due to ethical considerations and participant privacy agreements, the raw data cannot be made publicly available. However, de-identified summary data supporting the conclusions of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable req",45
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,1,"Systematically visualizing ChatGPT used in higher education: Publication trend, disciplinary domains, research themes, adoption and acceptance Ting Ma * Department of International Languages and Business, Shandong Foreign Trade Vocational College, China A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: Systematic review Bibliometric analysis ChatGPT Generative artificial intelligence Higher education A B S T R A C T Since it was released in November 2022, ChatGPT has been exerting revolutionary influence on the realm of higher education. In order to obtain a comprehensive understanding of the research landscape, we conduct a systematic literature review on the studies of ChatGPT used in higher education. Both quantitative and quali­ tative methods were adopted to bibliometrically examine the included literature selected from Web of Science and Scopus through the PRISMA protocol. Tools of VOSviewer and CitNetExplorer were employed to visualize the citation information. Our findings showed that the recent two years witnessed an ever-growing popularity of this research theme. Citation information analysis reveals the most influential authors, countries, sources, or­ ganizations and four focused topics. The disciplinary distribution of related",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,1,"the citation information. Our findings showed that the recent two years witnessed an ever-growing popularity of this research theme. Citation information analysis reveals the most influential authors, countries, sources, or­ ganizations and four focused topics. The disciplinary distribution of related research indicates a wide range of categories. More importantly, ChatGPT was found to be versatile in assisting teachers, students and researchers with a variety of tasks, and the factors influencing the acceptance of this technology among college students could be investigated through models like TAM, UTAUT and their extensions. We suggest future studies to focus on the ways to address the limitations and ethical issues of ChatGPT through AI literacy cultivation and joint efforts of all stakeholders. 1. Introduction As one of the most representative technologies in Industry 4.0, generative artificial intelligence (Gen AI) has taken the world by storm in recent years. ChatGPT, an generative pre-trained transformer that can produce human-like text and conduct interactive conversation, is one of the most representative Gen AI products. Since its advent in November 2022, it has been gaining popularity and exerting its",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,1,"in recent years. ChatGPT, an generative pre-trained transformer that can produce human-like text and conduct interactive conversation, is one of the most representative Gen AI products. Since its advent in November 2022, it has been gaining popularity and exerting its influence on the realm of higher education. Compared to previous educational technol­ ogies, ChatGPT stands out for its interactive and natural language pro­ cessing (NLP) capabilities. Its vast pre-trained data on extensive publicly available sources and reinforcement learning from human feedback made it a useful tool to perform various language processing tasks (Alqahtani et al., 2023). This feature significantly enhances user engagement and accessibility, enabling more intuitive and conversa­ tional learning experiences. Besides, ChatGPT can potentially provide personalized learning experiences tailored to individual students’ needs and abilities (Azaiz, Deckarm, & Strickroth, 2023; H. Lee, 2023). This represents a significant advancement over traditional educational tools, which generally offered non-specific content. The significance of ChatGPT in higher education is highlighted in its capabilities to assist teachers, students, and researchers with various educational tasks (Ansari, Ahmad, & Bhutta, 2023). Specific case studies have",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,1,"over traditional educational tools, which generally offered non-specific content. The significance of ChatGPT in higher education is highlighted in its capabilities to assist teachers, students, and researchers with various educational tasks (Ansari, Ahmad, & Bhutta, 2023). Specific case studies have demonstrated the revolutionary impact of ChatGPT on higher ed­ ucation since its release. On the one hand, ChatGPT significantly boosts educational support and accessibility. Its widespread availability offers comprehensive educational assistance, thereby reducing educational barriers and fostering a more inclusive learning atmosphere. For example, A university in the United Arab Emirates has integrated ChatGPT into its library service to provide 24/7 access to academic support. Students and faculty who need academic support and service outside the regular working hours can interact with the ChatGPT-powered robot to receive instant feedback and explanations (Lappalainen & Narayanan, 2023). On the other hand, ChatGPT en­ courages creativity and critical thinking, engaging learners with inno­ vative approaches and analytical mindsets. A chemistry course has incorporated ChatGPT into its curriculum to encourage students to engage in AI-based activities. Significant improvements were noticed in students’ confidence to",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,1,"en­ courages creativity and critical thinking, engaging learners with inno­ vative approaches and analytical mindsets. A chemistry course has incorporated ChatGPT into its curriculum to encourage students to engage in AI-based activities. Significant improvements were noticed in students’ confidence to ask insightful questions, analyze information, and comprehend complex concepts (Guo & Lee, 2023). These case * 201 Jufeng Road, Licang District, Qingdao, Shandong Province, 266000, China. E-mail addresses: luckynancy1989@163.com, 154465110@qq.com. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence journal homepage: www.sciencedirect.com/journal/computers-and-education-artificial-intelligence https://doi.org/10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336 Received 9 September 2024; Received in revised form 14 November 2024; Accepted 29 November 2024 Computers and Education: Artiϧcial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100336 Available online 14 December 2024 2666-920X/© 2024 The Author. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ).",134
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"studies demonstrate how ChatGPT has revolutionized higher education by enhancing learning support, driving innovation in teaching methods, streamlining administrative tasks, and fostering critical thinking and creativity among college teachers and students. Meanwhile, concerns and controversies regarding the challenges and potential risks came along. The academia worried that the use of ChatGPT would lead to more plagiarism in scholarly writing (Singh, 2023). And the hallucination issue, which refers to generating non-existent or incorrect content, raised people’s concerns on its lia­ bility (Alqahtani et al., 2023). Challenges also lie in privacy infringe­ ment, algorithmic bias, ethical considerations and so forth (Bannister, Urbieta, & Penalver, 2023; Dempere, Modugu, Hesham, & Ramasamy, 2023; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). Current research on ChatGPT in higher education, while making progress, still exhibits several notable gaps. Since potential risks and ethical considerations come along with the emergence of ChatGPT, some research overemphasized the negative impact while neglecting ChatGPT’s diverse applications and achievements in educational sector. Secondly, in view of the ever-growing popularity of Gen AI, the existing literature reviews failed to catch up with the latest studies, necessitating",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"ChatGPT, some research overemphasized the negative impact while neglecting ChatGPT’s diverse applications and achievements in educational sector. Secondly, in view of the ever-growing popularity of Gen AI, the existing literature reviews failed to catch up with the latest studies, necessitating inclusion of new publications. Thirdly, there is a lack of evidence to explain the causes behind ChatGPT’s varying performance across different subjects. Finally, while existing studies have explored ChatGPT’s usage in higher education from different perspectives, they have not yet done an in-depth investigation of each single theme. In order to address these research gaps, this systematic literature review will focus on the latest high-quality academic publications per­ taining to ChatGPT used in the context of higher education. Bibliometric approaches will be employed to analyze the publication trend, the most focused disciplinary domains, the main themes and the acceptance models. By doing so, it aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of the current research landscape. This will not only facilitate the identification of the most critical issues but also suggest directions for future studies. 2. Literature review Table 1 shows",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"By doing so, it aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of the current research landscape. This will not only facilitate the identification of the most critical issues but also suggest directions for future studies. 2. Literature review Table 1 shows four existing literature reviews investigating ChatGPT used within the context of higher education ranked in chronological order. Following the guideline proposed by Kraus et al. (2022), we categorized the included four reviews into systematic literature review (SLR) and non-systematic literature review (non-SLR) considering whether there was a protocol for guiding data curation and analysis. In these reviews, ChatGPT is sometimes synonymously referred to as “generative artificial intelligence”, “Gen AI” or “large language model (LLM)”. Given the fact that the four reviews were written in earlier 2023, it is essential to incorporate more recent advancements to provide a more comprehensive view of current academic landscape. To achieve this, we aim to include the most recent high-quality publications and analyze their features, so as to address the following research questions. RQ1: What is the publication trend of the literature in the field",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"of current academic landscape. To achieve this, we aim to include the most recent high-quality publications and analyze their features, so as to address the following research questions. RQ1: What is the publication trend of the literature in the field of ChatGPT-assisted higher education since the launch of ChatGPT? RQ2: Who are the top 10 authors, sources, organizations and coun­ tries based on citation data in the field of ChatGPT-assisted higher education? RQ3: What disciplinary domains have current studies mainly engaged with? RQ4: What are the major themes discussed by current publications? As a non-systematic review, Farrelly and Baker (2023) critically explored the multifaceted impact of Gen AI on higher education. They believe that ChatGPT, as a rising star in AI family, is more disruptive to higher education and society than previous inventions. Since the overall research was not systematically conducted, their research findings are far from convincing. Adopting the thematic analysis approach, Bannister et al. (2023) touched on a wide rage of themes regarding AI’s applications and im­ plications in higher education, including language instruction, student learning, assessment and",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"conducted, their research findings are far from convincing. Adopting the thematic analysis approach, Bannister et al. (2023) touched on a wide rage of themes regarding AI’s applications and im­ plications in higher education, including language instruction, student learning, assessment and feedback, research activity, academic integrity and policy. Dempere et al. (2023) generally discussed both the advan­ tages and potential risks of integrating ChatGPT into higher education. Ansari et al. (2023) mapped the global evidence of ChatGPT’s applica­ tions in higher education, offering an insight into the contextual, methodological and disciplinary scopes of this research field. Although these reviews provided a initial overview of Gen AI’s current status in higher education, a more in-depth investigation needs to be carried out to elucidate each research theme. In summary, existing reviews, while shedding light on the overall trend and disciplinary distribution of ChatGPT-related research in higher education, left some important issues under-explored, such as the major themes discussed, the specific roles of ChatGPT, and the dynamics of user acceptance. To fill these gaps, we propose the following research questions. RQ5: How can ChatGPT",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"research in higher education, left some important issues under-explored, such as the major themes discussed, the specific roles of ChatGPT, and the dynamics of user acceptance. To fill these gaps, we propose the following research questions. RQ5: How can ChatGPT be effectively integrated into the learning, teaching and research processes in higher education? RQ6: How does the performance of ChatGPT vary across different disciplines? RQ7: What is the extent of adoption and acceptance of ChatGPT among college students? These questions will focuses on mining out the existing research themes, summarizing the specific roles of ChatGPT in assisting college teaching, learning and research and exploring the factors influencing user’s adoption and acceptance of ChatGPT in higher education, areas that previously received limited attention. 3. Methods 3.1. Research design As shown in Fig. 1, we aimed at carrying out the systematic review with the guidance of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Re­ views and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) 2020 (Page et al., 2021), a protocol widely recognized for providing a robust framework for conducting systematic reviews. VOSviewer and CitNetExplorer, two powerful sci­ ence",180
DOC_023,NEW02.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2024.100336,2,"with the guidance of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Re­ views and Meta-Analysis (PRISMA) 2020 (Page et al., 2021), a protocol widely recognized for providing a robust framework for conducting systematic reviews. VOSviewer and CitNetExplorer, two powerful sci­ ence mapping tools, were employed to visualize and analyze biblio­ metric networks. VOSviewer is a software developed for constructing bibliometric maps based on the metadata from the included publica­ tions. Its versatile display techniques enable researchers to view a map in full detail (van Eck & Waltman, 2010). Similarly, CitNetExplorer Table 1 Previous reviews on ChatGPT-assisted higher education. Author(s), year Number and time span of the reviewed publications Types and methods Theme Farrelly and Baker (2023) around 50; from the release of ChatGPT to September 2023 non-SLR, critical analysis potential of Gen-AI on higher education and its impact on academic integrity Bannister et al. (2023) 68; from the release of ChatGPT to April 2023 SLR, thematic analysis implications of Gen-AI in higher education Dempere et al. (2023) 92; from the release of ChatGPT to April 16, 2023 SLR, thematic analysis explore",180
