DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 2 and educational benefits (or harms) (Cotton et al., 2024), and socially advantaged groups may better leverage the positive potentials of the tool (Fui-Hoon Nah et al., 2023). As the profound impacts of Generative AI (GenAI) and large language models continue to shape labor, education, content creation, and design (Eloundou et al., 2023), understanding college students’ attitudes to- wards and opinions of ChatGPT, the most widely used GenAI tool, is increasingly important. These attitudes and opinions can significantly influence learning processes, outcomes, and more broadly, the concep- tualization of educational goals, yet it remains unclear to what extent college users perceive ChatGPT uses in educational settings as accept- able and beneficial, given the as yet limited scholarly knowledge regarding people’s experience with, understanding of, and attitudes about AI and algorithms (Hargittai et al., 2020). More attitudinal research is warranted to further examine students’ perceived accept- ability of different ChatGPT uses, as a reflection of their understanding of AI domain expertise and conceptualization of responsible use. In addition, ChatGPT provides a compelling context",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"al., 2020). More attitudinal research is warranted to further examine students’ perceived accept- ability of different ChatGPT uses, as a reflection of their understanding of AI domain expertise and conceptualization of responsible use. In addition, ChatGPT provides a compelling context for exploring the digital divide, due to the contrast between its accessibility and ease of use (Buriak et al., 2023) and the opaque nature of AI and algorithmic decision-making (Hargittai et al., 2020). While ChatGPT diffuses rapidly by offering a user-friendly interface, natural interactions, easy regis- tration, and low cost, the inner workings of AI remain obscure to the general public as a “black box” (Hargittai et al., 2020). This lack of transparency, stemming from AI’s autonomy (Schuetz & Venkatesh, 2020), machine learning processes (Arif et al., 2023), and inscrutability (Long & Magerko, 2020), could make it harder for users to evaluate the tool’s risks and benefits. A deeper understanding of the generative na- ture of GenAI is particularly important for fostering responsible use (Ng et al., 2024), especially in educational contexts (Fui-Hoon Nah et al., 2023; Mhlanga, 2023). However,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"to evaluate the tool’s risks and benefits. A deeper understanding of the generative na- ture of GenAI is particularly important for fostering responsible use (Ng et al., 2024), especially in educational contexts (Fui-Hoon Nah et al., 2023; Mhlanga, 2023). However, underserved communities, already facing systemic barriers to acquiring this knowledge, can be dispro- portionately disadvantaged by this mix of easy use with process inscrutability when it comes to evaluating ChatGPT’s appropriate and inappropriate uses. Herein, we explore whether existing digital inequalities replicate in the adoption of and attitude toward ChatGPT, and contribute to AI attitude research, by exploring potential socioeconomic discrepancies in U.S. college students’ attitudes. Exploring social inequity in students’ attitudes and opinions of ChatGPT helps to respond to researchers’ calls for “a thorough understanding of the factors contributing to its accep- tance and use among students” (Raman et al., 2024, p. 17) and aids stakeholders and educators in making more informed decisions on the implementation and integration of ChatGPT in educational settings. In this study, we explore the effects of socioeconomic status, gender, and race/ethnicity, as core indicators",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"al., 2024, p. 17) and aids stakeholders and educators in making more informed decisions on the implementation and integration of ChatGPT in educational settings. In this study, we explore the effects of socioeconomic status, gender, and race/ethnicity, as core indicators of the digital divide, on American college students’ perceived acceptability and education benefits of ChatGPT. We further tested how the adoption of this technology may moderate such relationships to understand whether actual use of ChatGPT may amplify or reduce relationships between SES measures and attitudes. 2. Literature review 2.1. ChatGPT in college education ChatGPT has become increasingly popular among college students in the U.S. and beyond. An online survey conducted in January 2023 re- ported that 30% of U.S. college students had used ChatGPT to complete written assignments; 60% of those reported using it for over 50% of their written assignments (Intelligent, 2023). A 2023 survey of college stu- dents from five Middle Eastern countries found that just under half (46.8%) had heard of ChatGPT, and just over half (52.6%, or 24.6% overall) of those had used it. These statistics",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"assignments (Intelligent, 2023). A 2023 survey of college stu- dents from five Middle Eastern countries found that just under half (46.8%) had heard of ChatGPT, and just over half (52.6%, or 24.6% overall) of those had used it. These statistics highlight the growing reliance on AI tools in educational settings across different regions. Responding to this rapid diffusion, there are already many reviews and discussions of the diverse and growing educational applications of, implications about, and outcomes from AI in general and ChatGPT in particular. For example, Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) reviewed research aspects of 146 articles from educational research databases from 2007 to 2018 on AI applications throughout the student lifecycle. The four major themes (with 17 subcategories) were adaptive/person- alization, assessment/evaluation, profiling/prediction, and intelligent tutoring. However, they noted little attention in that period to critical reflection, challenges and risks, theory, and ethical and educational is- sues. Batista et al. (2024) identified three main themes in their sys- tematic review of research on educational uses of GenAI: applications, acceptance and perceptions, and types of uses. Yusuf, Pervin, and Rom´an-Gonz´alez",180
