DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"GenAI in higher education. Overviews now also include systematic reviews and even meta- analyses. Lo et al.’s (2024) systematic review of 72 studies identified two general types of involvement – engagement and disengagement – across three domains – cognitive (e.g., greater understanding, reduced critical thinking), emotional (satisfaction, anxiety), and behavioral (working with ChatGPT, dishonesty). Another systematic review high- lighted the potential and relevance of GenAI tools for higher education (e.g., personalized learning, usefulness for varied student purposes), as well as challenges (e.g., inefficient content, integration with pedagogy) (Mohebi, 2024). The systematic review by Saúde et al. (2024) especially noted how GenAI is disrupting higher education pedagogy. The most comprehensive systematic review so far (Yusuf, Pervin, Rom´an-- Gonz´alez, &; Noor, 2024Yusuf, Pervin, Rom´an-Gonz´alez, & Noor, 2024), identified eight themes from 407 publications, ranging from “application, impact and potential” and “perspectives and experiences” through “performance and intelligence”. Educational application themes included “pedagogical enhancement, and writing assistance and productivity” through “interdisciplinary learning tool”. The authors note areas receiving limited attention so far, such as cultural aspects of ethical concerns about GenAI. There has",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,2,"experiences” through “performance and intelligence”. Educational application themes included “pedagogical enhancement, and writing assistance and productivity” through “interdisciplinary learning tool”. The authors note areas receiving limited attention so far, such as cultural aspects of ethical concerns about GenAI. There has already been sufficient research for a meta-analysis. Sun and Zhou (2024) assessed 28 articles, finding a medium positive effect of GenAI on academic achievement, with moderation by types of activities, sample size, and type of content. Scholars, educators, the general public, and policymakers have various opinions on positive and negative educational uses of ChatGPT (Haque et al., 2022; Hwang & Chen, 2023; Ngo, 2023; Rasul et al., 2023; Tlili et al., 2023; Xu et al., 2024). The ongoing controversy about ChatGPT focuses on its potential to both support and displace learning in academic settings (Sallam et al., 2023). On the positive side, ChatGPT can enhance task efficiency (Alshater, 2022; Halaweh 2023; Sallam et al., 2023), student engagement and collaboration (Cotton et al., 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023), writing and language skills (Halaweh, 2023; Sal- lam et al., 2023; Taecharungroj, 2023),",180
