DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"in assignment preparation can hinder students’ learning outcomes (Hala- weh, 2023; Haque et al., 2022), introducing overreliance (Kasneci et al., 2023). ChatGPT challenges the established concept of academic integ- rity (Lee, Pope, et al., 2024; Yu, 2023) and can exacerbate fairness issues by putting non-users at a disadvantage (Cotton et al., 2024). Abdaljaleel et al. (2024) also mention biases from algorithm training datasets, issues of copyright and intellectual property, and concerns about privacy. Concerns include ethics (biases, manipulation), privacy, security and vulnerability, transparency (underlying processes obscure), and unin- tended consequences (misleading information) (Albayati, 2024). Sulli- van et al. (2023)’s content analysis of 100 news articles as of February 2023 identified five main concerns about or approaches toward ChatGPT in educational contexts: academic integrity, avoidance, policy, embrace, and voice. ChatGPT usage was associated with more procras- tination and memory loss and reduced academic performance in Abbas et al.’s (2024) longitudinal study. Others are concerned about students’ increasing dependence on AI chatbots and the associated disconnection from human interactions (Salah et al., 2024). Additional educational concerns include harming creativity, critical thinking, empathy,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"reduced academic performance in Abbas et al.’s (2024) longitudinal study. Others are concerned about students’ increasing dependence on AI chatbots and the associated disconnection from human interactions (Salah et al., 2024). Additional educational concerns include harming creativity, critical thinking, empathy, learning culture, originality, technology dependence, plagiarism, aca- demic integrity, research accuracy, privacy, security, misinformation, misinterpretation, and personalization (Albayati, 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Crawford et al., 2023; Islam & Islam, 2023; Raman et al., 2024). In sum, the educational application of ChatGPT elicits mixed feelings from the general public (Haque et al., 2022), academics (Tlili et al., 2023), and students (Xu et al., 2024). Among this already vast wealth of research on all aspects of educa- tional uses of GenAI, our study focuses specifically on perceptions by U. S. college students of acceptability and overall benefit of ChatGPT for educational activities. 2.2. The digital divide The review in sections 1 and 2.1 reveals mixed expert opinions about ChatGPT use for education, yet attitudes toward ChatGPT across soci- odemographics, especially by university students, remains under- investigated. Ngo (2023, p. 5) noted",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"activities. 2.2. The digital divide The review in sections 1 and 2.1 reveals mixed expert opinions about ChatGPT use for education, yet attitudes toward ChatGPT across soci- odemographics, especially by university students, remains under- investigated. Ngo (2023, p. 5) noted “a scarcity of studies that delve into students’ actual experiences with using ChatGPT in an educational context.” Research on the digital divide sheds light on the societal discrepancy in access, skills, and use outcomes of information commu- nication technology (Hargittai, 2002; van Dijk, 2005; 2020). Underlying the digital divide approach are two distinct models. The normalization model argues that disparities in access will eventually disappear as de- vice ownership saturates in later stages of adoption (Norris, 2001). However, the stratification model suggests that technology is funda- mentally an amplifier of underlying human and institutional intent and capacity; as such, it tends to exacerbate existing inequality (Toyama, 2011), as socioeconomically advantaged groups who own higher-quality devices and possess more skills will benefit from technology to a larger extent (Van Deursen & Helsper, 2015). The general digital divide perspective argues that the",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"tends to exacerbate existing inequality (Toyama, 2011), as socioeconomically advantaged groups who own higher-quality devices and possess more skills will benefit from technology to a larger extent (Van Deursen & Helsper, 2015). The general digital divide perspective argues that the inequality not only manifests in the form of unequal physical and material access to devices, connections, or applications (i.e., the first-level divide; Van Deursen & Van Dijk, 2019) but also points to the divergence in skills, knowledge, and use/usage (i. e., the second-level divide; Hargittai, 2002; Scheerder et al., 2017), as well as in tangible outcomes and benefits (i.e., the third-level divide; Van Deursen & Helsper, 2015). Compared with inequality in physical access, the second- and third-level digital inequalities may operate in more nuanced ways. Though younger generations are labeled as “digital natives” (Bennett et al., 2008), digital equity scholars still observe noticeable discrepancies in digital access, skills, and use within this population (e.g., Gonzales et al., 2020). Socioeconomic status (SES) is at the core of the digital divide scholarship (Katz & Rice, 2002; Van Dijk, 2020), and includes factors",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"still observe noticeable discrepancies in digital access, skills, and use within this population (e.g., Gonzales et al., 2020). Socioeconomic status (SES) is at the core of the digital divide scholarship (Katz & Rice, 2002; Van Dijk, 2020), and includes factors such as education, occupation, and economic resources (Brooks et al., 2011). Those with greater SES are more likely to have better access, adopt earlier, have or develop more skills, engage in more diverse or information-based activities, and benefit from such use than those with lower SES (Chytrý et al., 2022; Van Dijk, 2020), even among youth. For example, Hargittai and Hinnant (2008) observed disparities in self-perceived Internet understanding based on gender and education levels; more educated individuals were likelier to engage in activities that enhance social capital, regardless of experience or high-speed Internet access. Hargittai (2010) confirmed that college students from higher socioeconomic backgrounds reported higher online skills, with parental education influencing the diversity of Internet use even when access and experience were equal. Helsper and Eynon (2010) discovered that young people using the Internet for learning often came from",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"from higher socioeconomic backgrounds reported higher online skills, with parental education influencing the diversity of Internet use even when access and experience were equal. Helsper and Eynon (2010) discovered that young people using the Internet for learning often came from media-rich households. With the advent of AI technologies like ChatGPT, new questions arise about how digital inequality manifests in this evolving context. Although ChatGPT is a novel, relatively accessible, technology, differences in digital skill and use may manifest, given that the understanding of a new technology builds on that of past ones (Markauskaite et al., 2022). Indeed, Yilmaz et al. (2023) demonstrated that prior experience using other AI or chatbots positively predicted ChatGPT acceptance. Other demographic factors, including gender, race and ethnicity, sexuality, age, disabilities, and geographic regions may also matter for ChatGPT as they do for the Internet (Gunkel, 2003; Katz & Rice, 2002; Robinson et al., 2020; Scheerder et al., 2017; Yoon et al., 2020). This study investigates how socioeconomic status (SES), gender, and race/- ethnicity – three factors closely linked to digital inequality – are asso- ciated",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"2003; Katz & Rice, 2002; Robinson et al., 2020; Scheerder et al., 2017; Yoon et al., 2020). This study investigates how socioeconomic status (SES), gender, and race/- ethnicity – three factors closely linked to digital inequality – are asso- ciated with American college students’ attitudes toward using ChatGPT and its educational benefits. Despite the narrowing digital access gap in the U.S., gender disparities persist, with females often having less con- fidence in their digital skills (though performance tests showed no actual differences), and experiencing fewer beneficial outcomes (Hargittai & Hinnant, 2008; Van Deursen, 2010; Van Deursen & Helsper, 2018). Race and ethnicity also matter as they are complexly entangled with other markers of social inequalities, such as income and education, which affect digital access and skills (Pew Research Center, 2024). In the realm of GenAI, some studies have observed gender and racial/ethnic differ- ences. For example, the Pew Research Center (2023) reported that a higher percentage of boys (21%) use ChatGPT for schoolwork compared to girls (17%), and awareness of ChatGPT is greater among white teens (72%) than Hispanic (63%)",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,3,"and racial/ethnic differ- ences. For example, the Pew Research Center (2023) reported that a higher percentage of boys (21%) use ChatGPT for schoolwork compared to girls (17%), and awareness of ChatGPT is greater among white teens (72%) than Hispanic (63%) and Black teens (56%). While previous research has identified some preliminary gender differences in factors influencing ChatGPT adoption (Raman et al., 2023), more detailed in- sights into students’ attitudes toward various ChatGPT applications are needed. Racial and ethnic differences in attitudes toward ChatGPT have not been systematically examined (Lund et al., 2023), making this study a pioneering effort. 2.3. Acceptability of ChatGPT uses and attitude toward general benefits This study focuses on perceived acceptability – an attitude known to influence the way people adopt and interact with digital technologies – of ChatGPT use in educational settings. We distinguish between the C.(X. Zhang et al.",144
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 4 commonly used concept of acceptance (i.e., adoption, intention to use, or ongoing use and integration, especially related to computer-mediated communication or organizational information systems; see, for example, Hiltz & Johnson, 1989, or the technology acceptance model, Venkatesh et al., 2012) and acceptability (i.e., the extent to which it is perceived as acceptable or appropriate to use a technology in general or for specific purposes). A handful of existing studies that draw from technology acceptance perspectives focus on general attitudes towards ChatGPT among stu- dents (e.g., “ChatGPT is valuable”) (e.g., Albayati, 2024; Ma & Huo, 2023; Yilmaz et al., 2023). These tend to apply the technology accep- tance model, or the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology, which emphasize the ease of use, usefulness, affecting attitude toward use, performance and effort expectancy, social influence, facilitating conditions, and then behavioral intention (with demographic modera- tors), leading to use (Venkatesh & Davis, 2000; Venkatesh et al., 2012). For example, Albayati (2024) expanded the model to include influences of privacy, security, social influence,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"effort expectancy, social influence, facilitating conditions, and then behavioral intention (with demographic modera- tors), leading to use (Venkatesh & Davis, 2000; Venkatesh et al., 2012). For example, Albayati (2024) expanded the model to include influences of privacy, security, social influence, and trust. All of these except pri- vacy were positively associated with intention to use ChatGPT. Applying the diffusion of innovations model (Rogers, 2003), Raman et al. (2024) reported several gender differences in how a small sample of college students in India students perceived the innovation attributes (Rogers, 2003) of ChatGPT (ease of use and observability were rated higher by males, relative advantage and trialability by females). Studies from diverse countries (including Arab countries, China, Hong Kong, India, Indonesia, Kingdom of Saudia Arabia, Korea, Oman, Pakistan, Peru, Portugal, U.S., and Vietnam) have begun researching university students’ willingness to adopt, reported use of, or attitudes toward ChatGPT (Abbas et al., 2024; Abdaljaleel et al., 2024; Acos- ta-Enriquez et al., 2024; Albayti, 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Habibi et al., 2023; Jo, 2023; Lund et al., 2023; Mahmud et al., 2024; Ngo,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"use of, or attitudes toward ChatGPT (Abbas et al., 2024; Abdaljaleel et al., 2024; Acos- ta-Enriquez et al., 2024; Albayti, 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Habibi et al., 2023; Jo, 2023; Lund et al., 2023; Mahmud et al., 2024; Ngo, 2023; Raman et al., 2024; Salah et al., 2024; Saúde et al., 2024; Shahzad et al., 2024; Stojanov et al., 2024; ur Rehman et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024). Based on surveys among university students in multiple universities in Peru, both affective [interest, positive emotions, bordeom, negative emotions, perceived usefulness] and cognitive [importance, easy to use, perceived opportunity, perceived risk] components of attitudes toward ChatGPT influenced behavioral [intention to verify information, responsible use of ChatGPT] components (Acosta-Enriquez et al., 2024). Habibi et al. (2023) reported that behavioral intention to use ChatGPT for learning (their measure of acceptance) was positively associated with facilitating conditions (information, resources, time), performance ex- pectancy, hedonic motivation (enjoyable, interesting), and habit (automatic and routine use). Some of the above studies do analyze college students’ perceived acceptability of",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"for learning (their measure of acceptance) was positively associated with facilitating conditions (information, resources, time), performance ex- pectancy, hedonic motivation (enjoyable, interesting), and habit (automatic and routine use). Some of the above studies do analyze college students’ perceived acceptability of using ChatGPT for specific educational activities (e.g., helping structure writing or translation, answering exam questions, completing assignments – not acceptance or adoption). Yusuf, Pervin, and Rom´an-Gonz´alez (2024) analyzed seven purposes of using GenAI that were more general than the above activities, such as information search, playing around, or assignment. Table 1 summarizes common educational uses or activities of ChatGPT discussed in the literature and how they have been considered as recommended/acceptable (a supportive tool to aid homework and critical thinking) or not recommended/unacceptable (a tool displacing doing one’s own work and critical thinking). Overall, a general opinion from educators and academics is that ChatGPT can assist humans, and should only be used as a supportive tool, with critical thinking being the key to effective use, but cannot substitute for humans or displace learning (Atlas, 2023; Buriak et al., 2022; Mhlanga,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"academics is that ChatGPT can assist humans, and should only be used as a supportive tool, with critical thinking being the key to effective use, but cannot substitute for humans or displace learning (Atlas, 2023; Buriak et al., 2022; Mhlanga, 2023); and if mis- used or overused as a substitute for human instructors/learners, ChatGPT can truly harm learning. One critique within the second- and third-level divide literature is that categorization of digital activities as “good” or “bad” use may be biased against low-SES individuals by setting high-SES individuals’ use patterns as beneficial. Further, educators’ views do not always accu- rately reflect college students’ values, so an enforced typology may not represent students’ perceptions of such activities. Thus, we explore how students themselves perceive and evaluate the acceptability of use ac- tivities and of ChatGPT in general, without the pre-categorization in Table 1. However, few of the studies also consider students’ general opinion about the benefits of ChatGPT, and their associations with sociodemo- graphic factors. Thus we also asked respondents their opinion about how beneficial ChatGPT would be/is for college education,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"Table 1. However, few of the studies also consider students’ general opinion about the benefits of ChatGPT, and their associations with sociodemo- graphic factors. Thus we also asked respondents their opinion about how beneficial ChatGPT would be/is for college education, a more general attitude than activity acceptability. We then analyze whether SES, gender, race/ethnicity, and ChatGPT adoption are associated with ChatGPT acceptability and overall educational benefit (Our companion study, Zhang et al., 2024, showed how the use of different sets of these activities was differentially associated with two academic outcomes: efficacy and creativity.). 2.4. Research questions and conceptual model Studying such attitudes towards specific activities can help educators better understand students’ needs and potential uses of ChatGPT in educational settings and curate instructional materials and pedagogical approaches tailored to students’ needs. We thus pose the following research questions: RQ1. Among U.S. college student ChatGPT adopters and non- adopters, how are (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity asso- ciated with their perceived acceptability of ChatGPT for educational activities? RQ2. Among U.S. college student ChatGPT adopters and non- adopters, how are",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,4,"U.S. college student ChatGPT adopters and non- adopters, how are (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity asso- ciated with their perceived acceptability of ChatGPT for educational activities? RQ2. Among U.S. college student ChatGPT adopters and non- adopters, how are (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity asso- ciated with their opinion on how beneficial ChatGPT is for college education? Finally, despite the well-established relationships between various personal and positional characteristics (e.g., gender, race/ethnicity, and education contexts) and motivation for and attitude toward using technology in the digital divide literature (Van Dijk, 2020), and findings that technology trialability (i.e., the extent to which an innovation can Table 1 Common recommended and not recommended ChatGPT activities in college settings. “Recommended” Activities “Not Recommended” Activities • assist with brainstorming research ideas (Atlas, 2023) • assist with translation or language learning (Halaweh, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023) • conduct some initial information searches for academic assignments, followed by additional search and information validation (Arif et al., 2023; Halaweh, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023) • create customized learning plans (Fuchs, 2023) • enhance understanding",180
