DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 5 be tested prior to adoption) and previous use experience impact atti- tudes towards and adoption of new technology (Kim, 2008; Lee et al., 2011), little is known about whether the impact of SES on perceived ChatGPT acceptability and benefits differs based on the previous adoption. This poses an interesting question as to whether actual adoption experience may amplify or weaken digital inequalities in ChatGPT attitudes, which has important pedagogical implications as educators continue to integrate ChatGPT into classrooms. One can argue that inequalities in perceived acceptability and benefits may be due to a lack of access to technology. Once that access is achieved, SES may matter to a lesser extent (to some extent, a normalization perspective). However, ChatGPT adoption may amplify existing inequalities, as gaps in SES and digital skills may manifest in attitudes towards specific ChatGPT activities. In other words, once adopted, socioeconomically disadvantaged students may realize their lack of resources (e.g., finan- cial, temporal) and digital skills cannot fully support ChatGPT use, as suggested by the larger digital divide",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"manifest in attitudes towards specific ChatGPT activities. In other words, once adopted, socioeconomically disadvantaged students may realize their lack of resources (e.g., finan- cial, temporal) and digital skills cannot fully support ChatGPT use, as suggested by the larger digital divide theorizing (Toyama, 2012; Van Dijk, 2020). In this case, stronger effects should be observed among ChatGPT adopters than non-adopters. Given the lack of empirical evi- dence, we pose the following research questions: RQ3. How does ChatGPT adoption moderate the associations be- tween (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity and their perceived acceptability of ChatGPT for educational activities? RQ4. How does ChatGPT adoption moderate the associations be- tween (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity and their opinion on how beneficial ChatGPT is for college education? Fig. 1 presents the conceptual model summarizing core variables and relationships. 3. Method 3.1. Participants and procedures Based upon the literature and iterative discussions, we developed the overall model and an initial set of corresponding survey questions. We then conducted a study with two phases: a pilot (1) and main (2). Phase 1 included",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"Participants and procedures Based upon the literature and iterative discussions, we developed the overall model and an initial set of corresponding survey questions. We then conducted a study with two phases: a pilot (1) and main (2). Phase 1 included a pre-test to develop and explore the measures, through a small sample of college students at one university via focus groups and an online survey, and then assessed the revised measures and overall relationships of the model, through a convenience sample of college students at one university. Phase 2 formally tested for the consistency of the relationships analyzed in Phase 1 and provided responses to our research questions (RQ1-RQ4), using a national (but not representative) sample of U.S. college students. 3.1.1. Phase 1 At the beginning of Phase 1, six student research assistants took the draft initial survey for Phase 1, noted how long it took, and then participated in individual and joint discussions of each potential survey item. We reviewed and discussed their comments, incorporating their feedback by making minor adjustments to the formatting and phrasing to enhance the",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"1, noted how long it took, and then participated in individual and joint discussions of each potential survey item. We reviewed and discussed their comments, incorporating their feedback by making minor adjustments to the formatting and phrasing to enhance the survey’s pace, readability, and clarity as we finalized the design. To establish measurement reliability and validity and provide preliminary results, the survey component of Phase 1 was conducted from October 9 to November 14, 2023, with a convenience sample of 371 undergraduate students (18 or older) at a U.S. west coast university, who were filtered by indicating that they had already heard (were aware) of ChatGPT, and were compensated with extra credit. We retained 360 cases after filtering for completion and attention checks. The data analysis demonstrated good internal consistency using McDonald’s Omega, generated with maximum likelihood, which ad- dresses some limitations of Cronbach’s α (Hayes & Coutts, 2020). Additionally, exploratory factor analysis (EFA) with principal compo- nent analysis (PCA) extraction and Promax rotation supported reducing the SES measures from the initial thirteen items to seven. We also con- ducted",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"some limitations of Cronbach’s α (Hayes & Coutts, 2020). Additionally, exploratory factor analysis (EFA) with principal compo- nent analysis (PCA) extraction and Promax rotation supported reducing the SES measures from the initial thirteen items to seven. We also con- ducted multivariate analysis on the Phase 1 data using multiple re- gressions, which revealed preliminary differences in ChatGPT acceptability and opinion based on SES, gender, and race/ethnicity. 3.1.2. Phase 2 Using the Phase 1 results, we further revised the survey for Phase 2. We recruited participants via the Qualtrics service (https://www.qual trics.com) through their panelist pools, with each compensated for participation by Qualtrics. The data collection occurred during the second week of November 2023. We sought a sample (N = 1250) of individuals who were filtered by indicating that they had already heard (were aware) of ChatGPT, and, among those, with 75% ChatGPT adopters and 25% non-adopters, from U.S. adult college students (limited to 18 through 35 years old) from 4-year colleges/universities. The U.S. Census Population Sample (2022) reports the following per- centage of age categories among 2nd-year students at 4-year",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"75% ChatGPT adopters and 25% non-adopters, from U.S. adult college students (limited to 18 through 35 years old) from 4-year colleges/universities. The U.S. Census Population Sample (2022) reports the following per- centage of age categories among 2nd-year students at 4-year colleges: 15-19 (13.5%), 20-24 (64.6%), 25-35 (14.1%), 35+ (7.8%) (N = 7487). The Phase 2 sample had the following percentages: 18-19 (5.8%), 20-24 (31.7%), 25-35 (60.9%), and 35+ (1.7%). The survey was administered online, the participants were informed of the general purpose of this study, they provided online consent, and proceeded to the main ques- tionnaire, which took a mean of approximately 9 min. After filtering out problematic cases that failed attention checks or did not meet the recruitment criteria, the final delivered sample size was 1267. 3.2. Measures Table 2 presents descriptive statistics for the measures from Phase 1 and 2, except activity acceptability (see Table 3). Socioeconomic status. In Phase 2, SES was measured by 7 questions in Phase 2 concerning their first-generation college student status, per- centage of student loans, mother education, father education, estimated family income,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"and 2, except activity acceptability (see Table 3). Socioeconomic status. In Phase 2, SES was measured by 7 questions in Phase 2 concerning their first-generation college student status, per- centage of student loans, mother education, father education, estimated family income, perceived family social class, and socioeconomic ladder. We collapsed some infrequent categories, and recoded reversed items so that higher values on each item represented higher SES. Further explo- ration through EFA with PCA extraction and Promax rotation indicated two dimensions, and indicators had sufficient loadings onto the respective dimensions, according to rule proposed by Howard (2016): primary loadings >.40, alternative loadings <.30, the differences be- tween primary and alternative loadings >.20. The first factor was family SES, containing 5 measures (mother education, father education, esti- mated family income, perceived family social class, and socioeconomic ladder; Omega = .73). The second factor was in-college SES (first-- generation college student status, percentage of student loans (as Omega or Alpha cannot be computed for just two variables, we conducted a correlation, finding r = .18, p < .001; this is very low, but",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,5,"factor was in-college SES (first-- generation college student status, percentage of student loans (as Omega or Alpha cannot be computed for just two variables, we conducted a correlation, finding r = .18, p < .001; this is very low, but the explor- atory factor analysis showed acceptable loadings). Accordingly, factor scores (not mean scores) were generated respectively for the two scales. Respondents indicated their gender by whether they identified as male, female, non-binary, or preferred not to say. As the percentages for the last two categories are very small (Phase 1 4.1%, Phase 2 2.1%), only male (0) and female (1) were used in bivariate or multivariate analyses. Race/ethnicity was measured by seven commonly used identities, along with “other”. The distributions of Asian and Black/African American people for Phase 1 and 2 differ due to the sampling contexts. Fig. 1. The conceptual model. C.(X. Zhang et al.",147
