DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 8 displacement activities than did non-adopters (both p < .001). Table 4 (lower diagonal) records that SES, gender, and race/ ethnicity were in general not significantly associated with acceptability or opinion. Males and Asian students reported greater acceptability for academic displacement, and Black students reported lower opinions about ChatGPT as beneficial. However, the adoption of ChatGPT was significantly positively associated with both types of acceptability and, most strongly, with the overall opinion about its benefits for education. Associations with positive opinions about ChatGPT were significant (negatively) only for Black students. 5. Phase 2 results The means and t-tests in Table 3 show similar results to those of Phase 1, except the overall level of acceptability across both adopters and non-adopters was slightly lower for academic support (M = 5.20, SD = 1.17) and somewhat higher for academic displacement (M = 3.66, SD = 1.78). Again, Phase 2 adopters reported greater levels of both types of acceptability than did non-adopters (both p < .001), and they perceived significantly higher acceptability for academic support",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"and somewhat higher for academic displacement (M = 3.66, SD = 1.78). Again, Phase 2 adopters reported greater levels of both types of acceptability than did non-adopters (both p < .001), and they perceived significantly higher acceptability for academic support ac- tivities (M = 5.20 [more somewhat acceptable than acceptable]) than for displacement activities (M = 3.66 [between somewhat unacceptable and neutral], t = 30.71, p < .001, two-sided) (Appendix A provides Phase 2 differences for educational context: type, location, category, religious, historically Black college or university. Academic support acceptability was significantly higher only for university vs. college category. However, academic displacement was significantly higher for all contexts: public, online, college, religious affiliation, and HBCU.). Correlations (Table 4, upper diagonal) show more significant re- lationships with the three outcome measures than in Phase 1. Accept- ability of academic support activities was correlated with higher family SES, lower student SES, males, being Asian or being non-Hispanic (both relative to being White), and adoption. For academic displacement, significant associations beyond the two SES measures, gender, and adoption were a bit different: being",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"was correlated with higher family SES, lower student SES, males, being Asian or being non-Hispanic (both relative to being White), and adoption. For academic displacement, significant associations beyond the two SES measures, gender, and adoption were a bit different: being Black, but not White. Associations with positive opinions about ChatGPT were significant for lower student SES, males, and adoption. (Appendix B presents Phase 2 regression co- efficients of SES and demographics with each separate activity accept- ability. Four of the eight support items (#2, #3, #5, #6) were significantly associated with Student (negatively) and Family (posi- tively) SES, and one (#2) with gender (female). All four of the displacement items (#9 - #12) were significantly associated with Stu- dent (negatively) and Family (positively) SES, two (#11, #12) with gender (female), and one (#10) with race/ethnicity (lower for Asian and Hispanic students).) 5.1. RQ1 and RQ2 RQ1 explores the associations between respondents’ (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity, and their acceptability of ChatGPT ac- tivities. We tested the proposed direct effects path model from Fig. 1 with structural equation modeling",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"students).) 5.1. RQ1 and RQ2 RQ1 explores the associations between respondents’ (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity, and their acceptability of ChatGPT ac- tivities. We tested the proposed direct effects path model from Fig. 1 with structural equation modeling (SEM) after establishing an acceptable fit of the measurement model (see Table 5 for the overall CFA model fit). As Fig. 2 shows, for the overall sample (non-adopters and adopters) student SES was significantly negatively associated with acceptability of both academic support (β = -.09, p = .007) and aca- demic displacement (β = -.25, p < .001) activities, while family SES was positively associated with acceptability of displacement activities (β = .22, p < .001). Females were slightly more accepting of displacement than males, and Asian and Hispanic respondents were negatively asso- ciated with displacement acceptability (relative to White respondents). Altogether, these factors did not explain significant variance (1%, p = .11) for perceived acceptability of academic support but did explain 9.7% for academic displacement (p < .001). RQ2 explores the associations for the entire sample between re- spondents’",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"respondents). Altogether, these factors did not explain significant variance (1%, p = .11) for perceived acceptability of academic support but did explain 9.7% for academic displacement (p < .001). RQ2 explores the associations for the entire sample between re- spondents’ (a) SES, (b) gender, and (c) race/ethnicity, and their opinion on how beneficial ChatGPT is for college education. These factors explained 2.5% variance in students’ opinion (p = .005). Fig. 3 indicates that student SES (β = -.13, p < .001) was negatively, and family SES (β = .08, p = .011) positively, associated with the positive opinion,. Females tended to express more positivity (β = .06, p = .040) when SES and race/ ethnicity were controlled for. The association between Hispanic race/ ethnicity and opinion additionally revealed a negative association, relative to White respondents. 5.2. RQ3 and RQ4 RQ3 and RQ4 seek to examine the potential moderation effects of ChatGPT adoption on the aforementioned associations with the two dimensions of acceptability and the general opinion, which we addressed through multigroup SEM. In the preparatory stage, we tested the measurement",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"and RQ4 seek to examine the potential moderation effects of ChatGPT adoption on the aforementioned associations with the two dimensions of acceptability and the general opinion, which we addressed through multigroup SEM. In the preparatory stage, we tested the measurement model (via CFA) to assess measurement invariance across groups, and then the overall structural model (via SEM) using Mplus 8.10. In order to assess differences for non-adopters vs. adopters, we then compared path models for the two groups, and significance tests of the two sets of path coefficients. 5.2.1. Measurement model To evaluate the overall measurement model fit, we adopted an innovative approach proposed by McNeish and Wolf (2023), which calculates dynamic fit index (DFI) cutoff values based on Monte Carlo simulation. This method offers case-specific model fit cutoff recom- mendations considering various factors such as sample sizes, factor structures, factor loadings, factor correlations, and residual correlations. For the entire sample, the recommended DFI cutoff values at the 95% confidence interval were SRMR ≤.074, RMSEA ≤.126, and CFI ≥.895. Our two-factor model, analyzed using maximum likelihood (ML) esti- mation, demonstrated",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,8,"factor loadings, factor correlations, and residual correlations. For the entire sample, the recommended DFI cutoff values at the 95% confidence interval were SRMR ≤.074, RMSEA ≤.126, and CFI ≥.895. Our two-factor model, analyzed using maximum likelihood (ML) esti- mation, demonstrated an excellent fit, with SRMR = .059, RMSEA = .085, and CFI = .943. Additionally, the indicator loadings ranged be- tween .688 and .861, surpassing the .65 threshold proposed by DeVillis (2016). The two latent factors, perceived acceptability of academic support and of academic displacement, were moderately correlated (r = .36, p < .001). Table 5 shows model fit indices overall and across non- adopters and adopters. Table 5 Model fit information for multigroup CFA, Study 2. χ2 df Δ χ2 Δ df SRMR RMSEA CFI Δ SRMR Δ RMSEA Δ CFI Model Fit Overall 541.22 53 – – .059 .085 .943 – – – Adopters (N = 945) 389.28 53 – – .056 .082 .948 – – – Non-Adopters (N = 319) 262.76 53 – – .078 .111 .899 – – – Measurement Invariance 1. Configural 707.32 114 –",180
