DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,9,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 9 5.2.1.1. Measurement invariance. To properly assess the structural dif- ferences in the proposed model between non-adopters and adopters, it is crucial to establish measurement invariance. This step helps rule out alternative sources of observed differences in group means. We pro- ceeded with a multigroup CFA analysis, which involves four levels of measurement invariance testing: configural, metric, scalar, and residual (Putnick & Bornstein, 2016). We evaluated levels of measurement invariance with the commonly used threshold proposed by Cheung and Rensvold (2002) and regarded a CFI difference (ΔCFI) less than .01 between the baseline model and the more constrained model as evidence for retaining the null hypothesis of invariance. Our analysis did not show a significant shift in model fit when comparing the configural and metric models by constraining factor loadings across non-adopters and adopters (ΔCFI = -.004). Similarly, the scalar model did not exhibit a significantly worse fit than the metric model after fixing the factor means and variances to 0 and 1, respec- tively, for non-adopters and estimating the factor means",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,9,"and adopters (ΔCFI = -.004). Similarly, the scalar model did not exhibit a significantly worse fit than the metric model after fixing the factor means and variances to 0 and 1, respec- tively, for non-adopters and estimating the factor means and variances for adopters (ΔCFI = -.003). Furthermore, we tested the equivalence of item residuals and found that residual invariance was also supported (ΔCFI = -.004). In summary, our multigroup CFA analysis confirmed measurement invariance across non-adopters and adopters regarding the two latent variables, as evidenced by consistent fit indices across all four levels of invariance testing. Table 5 presents the fit indices for each level of invariance testing. 5.2.1.2. Mean comparisons, Non-Adopters and adopters. Accordingly, we compared group means on the two latent factors. Compared to non- adopters, adopters reported higher levels of acceptability of academic support activities than non-adopters (standardized mean difference = .31, SE = .067, p < .001); adopters also reported higher levels of acceptability of academic displacement activities (standardized mean difference = .39, SE = .052, p < .001). Clearly, adopters were more accepting of",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,9,"non-adopters (standardized mean difference = .31, SE = .067, p < .001); adopters also reported higher levels of acceptability of academic displacement activities (standardized mean difference = .39, SE = .052, p < .001). Clearly, adopters were more accepting of ChatGPT activities overall. 5.2.2. Structural model To stringently test for the overall moderation effects of adoption on relationships of SES and demographics to academic support, academic displacement, and general benefit, we proceeded to testing structural parameters with multigroup SEM, comparing the constrained model (χ2 = 845.232, df = 307) with the unconstrained model (χ2 = 817.998, df = 286) by constraining and freeing the path estimates across two groups. Because the chi-square difference (χ2 = 27.234, df = 21) was non- significant, p = .163, we conclude that constraining path estimates to be equal across non-adopters and adopters did not significantly decrease the model fit. Hence, adoption did not significantly moderate the overall model. Fig. 4 presents the significant path models for adopters and non- adopters. For non-adopters, SES, gender, and racial/ethnic background explained 3.9% of variance for support (p",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,9,"significantly decrease the model fit. Hence, adoption did not significantly moderate the overall model. Fig. 4 presents the significant path models for adopters and non- adopters. For non-adopters, SES, gender, and racial/ethnic background explained 3.9% of variance for support (p = .093), 2.3% for displace- ment (p = .21), and 3.6% for opinion (p = .09). For adopters, SES, Fig. 2. Standardized significant path coefficients for the direct model of acceptability for non-adopters and adopters, Phase 2. C.(X. Zhang et al.",81
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,10,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 10 gender, and racial/ethnic background explained 1.3% variance for support (p = .11), 10.8% for displacement (p < .001), and 1.4% for opinion (p = .08). It appears that the proposed model offers slightly more explanatory power for the adopter group, at least with regard to ChatGPT displacement activities. As Fig. 4 does indicate some path differences, we further examined the between-group differences in individual path estimates by imposing structural invariance and conducting z-score significance tests. In response to RQ3, we found significant differences in associations with Asian race/ethnicity (z = -3.10, p = .002) for acceptability of academic support, indicating Asian non-adopters responded more negatively to- wards support activities compared with White non-adopters (the referent group) than did Asian adopters compared with White adopters. For acceptability of academic displacement, the comparisons showed student SES had a significantly stronger path among adopters than that of non-adopters (z = 2.41, p = .016), and female respondents, compared to males, reacted more positively towards displacement activities among adopters than they do among non-adopters (z",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,10,"showed student SES had a significantly stronger path among adopters than that of non-adopters (z = 2.41, p = .016), and female respondents, compared to males, reacted more positively towards displacement activities among adopters than they do among non-adopters (z = -2.96, p = .003). In response to RQ4 (overall educational benefit of ChatGPT), no significant moderation was identified for any individual paths. 6. Discussion 6.1. Summary The present study examines how the digital divide framework may apply to the extraordinarily quickly diffusing AI technology of ChatGPT in the university setting. Overall, our observations reveal similar pat- terns across student adopters and non-adopters, with both considering activities from #1 to #8 (academic support) as more acceptable, and #9 to #12 (academic displacement) as less acceptable. These ratings align with researchers’ and educators’ perspectives on recommended vs. not recommended activities, summarized in Table 1. Further, adopters perceive all the activities more positively than do non- (or not yet) adopters. SES, gender, and race/ethnicity exhibit distinct associations with respondents’ perceived acceptability of academic support and displacement activities as well as with overall",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,10,"in Table 1. Further, adopters perceive all the activities more positively than do non- (or not yet) adopters. SES, gender, and race/ethnicity exhibit distinct associations with respondents’ perceived acceptability of academic support and displacement activities as well as with overall opinion of ChatGPT. Although the overall model is not moderated by adoption of ChatGPT, moderation effects are found for some individual paths, signaling de- creases in racial/ethnic and student SES discrepancies and an increase in positivity from females in perceived acceptability of displacement ac- tivities due to ChatGPT adoption. Because only some of the SES/de- mographics are significant, and the effect sizes are not large, we may tentatively infer that these results support the digital divide normali- zation model rather than the stratification model. That is, ChatGPT may Fig. 3. Standardized significant path coefficients for the direct model of opinion for non-adopters and adopters, Phase 2. C.(X. Zhang et al.",150
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 11 not (yet) suffer from the typical digital divide inequalities associated with new technologies. Our study identifies two distinct components of SES: student and family SES (Phase 1 r = .74, p < .001; Phase 2 r = .12, p < .05). One explanation for the very large difference in correlations is that as Phase 1 analyzed a small sample from one college, those students’ resources in school are much more in accordance with their family conditions. The weak correlation in Phase 2, from a large sample across many in- stitutions, indicates that the financial, cultural, and social capital that American college students possess on campus can be relatively inde- pendent of their family wealth, education, and social class, potentially due to the highly individualistic culture context. The two-factor SES model thus highlights the uniqueness of the college student context. Consistent with past digital divide research that suggests socioeco- nomically advantaged groups likely benefit from technology to a greater extent, we observe a positive association between family SES and acceptability and opinion,",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"highlights the uniqueness of the college student context. Consistent with past digital divide research that suggests socioeco- nomically advantaged groups likely benefit from technology to a greater extent, we observe a positive association between family SES and acceptability and opinion, indicating U.S. students with more resourceful family backgrounds are more accepting and supportive of ChatGPT activities and overall benefits, independently from their stu- dent SES. This observation is in accordance with the past digital divide and diffusion research that higher SES typically positively connects to positive attitudes toward technology (see Section. 2.2) Correspondingly, our data suggest that the social inequalities (measured by family SES) may at least slightly persist in the new technological context, enabling students from wealthier and more educated households to embrace and leverage the usability of ChatGPT. Their positivity may attributed to a variety of factors, as outlined by Van Dijk’s (2020) resources and appropriation theory: they may enjoy greater hardware access, digital connectivity, and software subscriptions to incorporate ChatGPT use (material resources), develop critical evaluation skills and mastery of GenAI (mental resources), live a lifestyle embracing",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"outlined by Van Dijk’s (2020) resources and appropriation theory: they may enjoy greater hardware access, digital connectivity, and software subscriptions to incorporate ChatGPT use (material resources), develop critical evaluation skills and mastery of GenAI (mental resources), live a lifestyle embracing AI-supported learning (cultural resources), and have more time for technological tri- als (temporal resources). Consequently, despite ChatGPT’s scalability and accessibility, discrepancies in students’ attitudes emerge, likely driven by differences in access to resources, critical evaluation skills, and the time to explore the technology. Conversely, the direct model unexpectedly shows that student SES is negatively associated with the acceptability of both support and displacement activities, and with general opinion of ChatGPT. Such findings add to the nuances of a ChatGPT-centered digital divide: in contrast with the positive implications of family SES, the less resourceful U.S. students tend to consider various ChatGPT activities, especially displacement activities, and this technology in general, to be more acceptable. One possibility is that under-resourced college students considered this technology, regardless of the activities, to be more cost- and time-efficient than other educational tools that are more",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"activities, especially displacement activities, and this technology in general, to be more acceptable. One possibility is that under-resourced college students considered this technology, regardless of the activities, to be more cost- and time-efficient than other educational tools that are more time- consuming (e.g., watching online tutorials, hiring private tutors) as they often have to balance other financial and social challenges (e.g., tuition loans, multiple jobs). In support of the normalization model, this finding highlights the significant potential of ChatGPT to mitigate educational inequalities by improving task efficiency and providing personalized learning support (e.g., Baidoo-Anu & Owusu Ansah, 2023; Cotton et al., 2024; Fuchs, 2023; Halaweh, 2023; Sallam et al., 2023; Stojanov et al., 2024) and gives hope to and the utopian view of tech- nology being an automatic force for democracy and equality (Katz & Rice, 2002). Yet this finding also echoes educators’ concerns regarding the risks and benefits of implementing ChatGPT in educational settings, as displacement activities were perceived to be more acceptable by students with lower SES. Nonetheless, more research, qualitative studies in particular, is needed to further",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"also echoes educators’ concerns regarding the risks and benefits of implementing ChatGPT in educational settings, as displacement activities were perceived to be more acceptable by students with lower SES. Nonetheless, more research, qualitative studies in particular, is needed to further unpack students’ perceptions of various types of ChatGPT activities and the associated educational implications. Furthermore, the data indicate that females have a more positive view of the acceptability of academic displacement activities and overall opinion than males. This contradicts the general recognition that fe- males are more conservative about technology adoption in general (Rogers, 2003) and less confident in their digital skills than males, as a persisting gender discrepancy in the digital divide research (see Section 2.2). As such, ChatGPT use for displacement can also elicit greater risks and harm of abuse and overreliance, so these gender differences should be further explored. Also, it is important to note when SES and race/- ethnicity are not controlled for, we observe a reversed trend (with males being more positive), which highlights the importance of including Fig. 4. Standardized significant path coefficients for",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,11,"further explored. Also, it is important to note when SES and race/- ethnicity are not controlled for, we observe a reversed trend (with males being more positive), which highlights the importance of including Fig. 4. Standardized significant path coefficients for non-adopters and adopters, Phase 2. C.(X. Zhang et al.",49
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 12 typical digital divide influences in analyses of ChatGPT adoption and evaluation and signals a possible interaction effect between gender and race/ethnicity in this process that warrants further investigation. Interestingly, we found that racial/ethnic minorities, including Asian, Hispanic, Middle Eastern, Pacific Islander, and Native American respondents, considered academic displacement activities to be less acceptable than did White respondents. However, no significant differ- ences were observed for the perceived acceptability of support activities. The racial/ethnic minority groups were more critical in their evaluation of different ChatGPT activities than White respondents, providing new insights into racial and ethnic differences in ChatGPT adoption. Poten- tially, non-white respondents were more aware of their vulnerability to the harms of ChatGPT due to a lack of other forms of support. Therefore, AI developers and educators should consider a prevention-focused approach – which emphasizes avoiding suffering and harm – instead of a promotion-focused approach that highlights desired outcomes (Scholer & Higgins, 2013) when developing better explanations for non- adoption and selective use. Finally, adoption was identified as a significant",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"prevention-focused approach – which emphasizes avoiding suffering and harm – instead of a promotion-focused approach that highlights desired outcomes (Scholer & Higgins, 2013) when developing better explanations for non- adoption and selective use. Finally, adoption was identified as a significant moderator of some associations between socioeconomic and demographic variables and perceived ChatGPT acceptability, although the overall models were not significantly different. Overall, Fig. 4 shows that SES and gender are more strongly associated for adopters compared with non-adopters, whereas racial/ethnic differences in perception and attitudes are more influential for non-adopters than for adopters. Specifically, higher stu- dent SES reduces perceived acceptability of support and displacement with greater magnitude for adopters, meaning the lack of in-college resources more intensely promotes college student adopters’ accep- tance and justification of their own uses. In the same vein, adopters with higher student SES consider their uses to be more acceptable and appropriate, partially reflecting the fact that high-SES individuals’ tech uses tend to be taken as standards for beneficial and capital-enhancing activities while low-SES individuals are more inclined to doubt their uses. Female respondents",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"their uses to be more acceptable and appropriate, partially reflecting the fact that high-SES individuals’ tech uses tend to be taken as standards for beneficial and capital-enhancing activities while low-SES individuals are more inclined to doubt their uses. Female respondents became more positive towards displacement compared to males after adoption, possibly suggesting more positive expectation violations for females in this process, given there is no gender difference prior to adoption. These differences would support a stratification assumption about the amplifying effects of new technology. On the other hand, the few racial/ethnicity differences in attitudes towards ChatGPT among adopters may potentially exemplify the power of adoption. This result would support a normalization assumption, because ensuring access to and use of ChatGPT by racial/ethnic mi- norities may be the first step in reducing digital inequalities in the AI era, regardless of differences in digital skills, knowledge, and outcomes. In summary, our findings provide some evidence for both the normalization and stratification models. As digital innovation spreads, more socioeconomically and demographically disadvantaged in- dividuals are leveraging the internet and technology to compensate for",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"in digital skills, knowledge, and outcomes. In summary, our findings provide some evidence for both the normalization and stratification models. As digital innovation spreads, more socioeconomically and demographically disadvantaged in- dividuals are leveraging the internet and technology to compensate for limited offline resources (DiMaggio & Garip, 2012; Gonzales, 2017; Mesch, 2012). However, existing social inequalities in education persist (Mhlanga, 2023), with those from higher socioeconomic SES families maintaining an advantage and exhibiting greater confidence and posi- tivity in their technology use. Excessive confidence and engagement in certain activities among individuals with higher family SES, lower stu- dent SES, and women may backfire, potentially leading to harmful uses and negative consequences. Conversely, racial and ethnic differences in perceived acceptability and educational benefits can promote critical evaluations of ChatGPT use. Paradoxically, those challenges faced by underserved racial and ethnic communities may lead them, through trial and error, to exercise greater agency and critically assess their use of generative AI. Baek et al. (2024) similarly concluded their article by emphasizing the potential of ChatGPT in educational settings to both empower and marginalize.Therefore, AI",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"them, through trial and error, to exercise greater agency and critically assess their use of generative AI. Baek et al. (2024) similarly concluded their article by emphasizing the potential of ChatGPT in educational settings to both empower and marginalize.Therefore, AI education should place greater emphasis on potential harms and risks. Additionally, adoption of ChatGPT can both exacerbate SES- and gender-based discrepancies in attitudes (supporting the stratification view) and mitigate racial and ethnic discrepancies (supporting the normalization view). This suggests that fundamental digital divide factors may interact in complex, non-linear ways and exhibit different patterns in the adoption and use of AI technology. Future research should consider this complexity and continue to disentangle the influences of various socioeconomic and demographic factors in the context of novel AI applications. 6.2. Theoretical and practical implications Findings of this study have several theoretical and pedagogical im- plications for the use of GenAI such as ChatGPT in college education. Theoretically, drawing from the digital divide perspective, we indicated that socioeconomic and demographic factors slightly, and differentially, shaped students’ perceived acceptability and benefits of ChatGPT use",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"pedagogical im- plications for the use of GenAI such as ChatGPT in college education. Theoretically, drawing from the digital divide perspective, we indicated that socioeconomic and demographic factors slightly, and differentially, shaped students’ perceived acceptability and benefits of ChatGPT use in college education. We encourage future research to investigate the impact of these social and group-based factors, such as family SES, gender, race/ethnicity, beyond traditional psychological factors, on at- titudes toward and adoption of educational technology. For example, applications of the technology acceptance model (Davis, 1989) that study perceived ease of use and trust in technology as predictors of adoption should incorporate digital divide factors as antecedents of such psychological variables. Therefore, we suggest an addition of digital divide theory to Wu (2023)’s list of theoretical frameworks for studying ChatGPT in classrooms. Practically, while ChatGPT shows promise in reducing educational inequalities, our study highlights the dire need for pedagogical devel- opment in guiding its appropriate use for various educational purposes. We found that inequalities manifest in perceived ChatGPT acceptability, such that those with low student-SES and females (vs. males) considered",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"inequalities, our study highlights the dire need for pedagogical devel- opment in guiding its appropriate use for various educational purposes. We found that inequalities manifest in perceived ChatGPT acceptability, such that those with low student-SES and females (vs. males) considered displacement activities to be more acceptable. These findings suggest that more attention from instructors and schools should be paid, and more educational resources should be allocated, to these groups. Group- based interventions, such as tailored workshops on using generative AI in classrooms attuned to marginalized groups may be particularly use- ful. At the school policy level, while the use of ChatGPT and other GenAI tools and their potential for academic misconduct should be addressed promptly, it is equally important to develop guidelines on their appro- priate and beneficial use. There remains optimism about the potential to bridge educational and social inequalities, and these opportunities should not be overlooked. 7. Limitations and future research Phase 1 of this study involves only students at one university, and Phase 2 involves a non-representative sample of U.S. college students in general; thus, results have",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"inequalities, and these opportunities should not be overlooked. 7. Limitations and future research Phase 1 of this study involves only students at one university, and Phase 2 involves a non-representative sample of U.S. college students in general; thus, results have limited generalizability to the population. Since our study involves only U.S. college student respondents, it will be valuable to conduct similar studies across a host of cultural, educational, technical, national, and governmental contexts (as Section 2.3 notes). Yusuf, Pervin, and Rom´an-Gonz´alez (2024) advocate for more multi- cultural perspectives on GenAI educational use and impacts. Their study surveyed respondents from 76 countries, showing varying familiarity with 13 GenAI applications, and finding strong associations between cultural dimensions and responses about benefits from and concerns about GenAI. Asn an example, uncertainty avoidance and long-term orientation were both positively associated with a perception of GenAI use by students as academic dishonesty. The cultural dimension of un- certainty avoidance was negatively correlated with a score measuring GenAI potential and positively with a concern score. Future research can also incorporate additional sociodemographic factors identified by previous",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,12,"GenAI use by students as academic dishonesty. The cultural dimension of un- certainty avoidance was negatively correlated with a score measuring GenAI potential and positively with a concern score. Future research can also incorporate additional sociodemographic factors identified by previous studies as contributors to the digital divide, such as age, disability, language, and rural/urban residency (Robinson et al., 2020). Also, expanding the scope to include global perspectives on these digital divide debates may further enhance our understanding of AI-related inequalities and paths toward C.(X. Zhang et al.",87
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,13,"Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence 8 (2025) 100385 13 normalization. Nonetheless, our large sample across the U.S. four-year colleges does provide a helpful initial foundation for further studies. For the establishment of causal relationships, future research should also gather longitudinal data (for example, see Lee, Pope, et al., 2024) as well as objective measures of ChatGPT use and outcomes to further investi- gate the impacts of ChatGPT use on the acceptability and opinions of college students. Future research may also wish to expand the list of educational activities for which ChatGPT is used and incorporate mea- sures of actual usage to examine the congruence between attitudes and behaviors. As with much social science research, these quantitative survey re- sults should be complemented with more qualitative, mixed method, big data, and large sample approaches, to identify subtle contexts and boundary cases, and large-scale trends. For example, Xu et al. (2024) conducted a qualitative study via peer interviews (guided by 10 open-ended questions derived from the technology acceptance model) involving 106 first-year students and 81 first-year postgraduate stu- dents, across disciplines, at",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,13,"and large-scale trends. For example, Xu et al. (2024) conducted a qualitative study via peer interviews (guided by 10 open-ended questions derived from the technology acceptance model) involving 106 first-year students and 81 first-year postgraduate stu- dents, across disciplines, at an East China university. Thematic analysis identified six themes (with 13 subthemes): Ways of learning about ChatGPT, practical use, reliability, safety, further improvements, atti- tude (via positive, neutral, or negative sentiment analysis), and role in life. With respect to the last theme, similar to our overall measure of benefits to education, they reported that “undergraduate and post- graduate students generally viewed ChatGPT as a useful and innovative technology that could assist them in their academic pursuits.” They did not find differences by gender, but post-graduate students were more positive than undergraduates. Finally, the focus on just the few demographics here hardly touches the surface of even a brief overview of the digital divide issue, levels, contexts, policies, and technologies (see esp. Van Dijk, 2020, among others). Further, our digital divide measures are appropriate for the U.S. context, but may not",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,13,"hardly touches the surface of even a brief overview of the digital divide issue, levels, contexts, policies, and technologies (see esp. Van Dijk, 2020, among others). Further, our digital divide measures are appropriate for the U.S. context, but may not be for other countries (e.g., college expense sub- sidies, education, income, class, race/ethnicity). 8. Conclusion Our study provides some support for both the normalization and stratification perspectives of the digital divide regarding ChatGPT, indicating that effects of sociodemographic differences are not a simple either-or matter. With incredibly rapid adoption rates, banning ChatGPT at school may be futile, and more detrimentally, can exacerbate digital inequality because students with home Internet access or wireless mobile phone access can use the tool outside of school but those with restricted access cannot, causing the latter disadvantages (Singer, 2023). To address the new challenges of the digital divide brought by GenAI, a more viable approach would involve careful consideration and promo- tion of AI-specific training and education in both pre-college and college education. For example, Lee, Arnold, et al. (2024) proposed some points of interventions",180
DOC_024,NEW03.pdf,10.1016/j.caeai.2025.100385,13,"the digital divide brought by GenAI, a more viable approach would involve careful consideration and promo- tion of AI-specific training and education in both pre-college and college education. For example, Lee, Arnold, et al. (2024) proposed some points of interventions for ChatGPT use in higher education, such as involve- ment of institutional forces and engagement with students. Several recommendations provided by the students in Ngo’s (2023) study included double-checking ChatGPT content, using it as a reference or consultant source but not as direct content producer, and that schools and teachers should help make students “aware of acceptable and non-acceptable use of this tool in school” (p. 11). Our study especially recommends (unsurprisingly) increasing the use of ChatGPT for aca- demic support activities and minimizing its use for academic displace- ment activities. 9. Open data, ethics, conflict of interest Open data: Relevant survey questions and data are available at https://osf.io/u4be8 and https://osf.io/4rjgq. Ethics: The study was approved by the university Institutional Review Board with ID: 35-23- 0476. Informed consent was obtained from all participants, with strict adherence to confidentiality. CRediT authorship",180
