DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,1,"Shaping generative AI governance in higher education: Insights from student perception Okky Putra Barus a,b,* , Achmad Nizar Hidayanto b, Eko Yon Handri b, Dana Indra Sensuse b, Chairote Yaiprasert c a Faculty of Information Technology, Universitas Pelita Harapan, Medan, Indonesia b Faculty of Computer Science, Universitas Indonesia, Depok, Indonesia c School of Science, Walailak University, Thasala, Nakhon Si Thammarat, Thailand A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: Generative AI Governance Higher education Student perspective Ethics A B S T R A C T This study explores student perspectives on generative AI governance in higher education to ensure responsible and ethical AI integration. Employing a mixed-methods approach with an explanatory sequential design, the study gathered data from 111 undergraduate students at Universitas Pelita Harapan through an online survey and semi-structured interviews with 53 students. The study covered key aspects of AI governance, such as ethics, curriculum integration, misuse detection, and academic sanctions, which are crucial for ensuring responsible AI implementation. The findings reveal that students are highly aware of the potential benefits of Generative AI",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,1,"study covered key aspects of AI governance, such as ethics, curriculum integration, misuse detection, and academic sanctions, which are crucial for ensuring responsible AI implementation. The findings reveal that students are highly aware of the potential benefits of Generative AI and support its integration into the curriculum. However, they also emphasize the need for clear guidelines, ethics training, and plagiarism detection mechanisms to prevent misuse and uphold academic integrity. These findings provide valuable insights for higher education institutions to develop a comprehensive AI governance framework that balances the potential of Generative AI with the ethical considerations of its use. 1. Introduction The development of Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, DALL-E, Gemini, and Bard marks a new era of Generative AI, which has emerged as a transformative tool across various sectors. A McKinsey survey in 2023 found that one-third of organizations have used Generative AI in at least one business function (Anon, 2023). A report from Goldman Sachs predicts that Generative AI could raise global GDP by 7 % in the coming years (Briggs, 2023). The development of generative",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,1,"of organizations have used Generative AI in at least one business function (Anon, 2023). A report from Goldman Sachs predicts that Generative AI could raise global GDP by 7 % in the coming years (Briggs, 2023). The development of generative AI has also entered the higher education sector (Leelavathi & Surendhranatha, 2024). This technology opens up new opportunities in implementing teaching and learning activities and research (Leelavathi & Surendhranatha, 2024; Chiu, 2024) and potentially significantly enhances the educational experience. However, the inte­ gration of Generative AI also raises ethical challenges such as data pri­ vacy, transparency, fairness, and concerns regarding accuracy, information bias, cybersecurity, and intellectual property issues, which have implications for academic integrity (Leelavathi & Surendhranatha, 2024; Chiu, 2024; Gow, 2024). Studies have shown a significant increase in plagiarism cases since the introduction of ChatGPT among students (Meyer et al., 2023). The ease with which students can now generate essays using ChatGPT, produce code with Bard, or even answer exam questions using Gemini raises fundamental questions about academic integrity and the authenticity of student work. Furthermore, ethical considerations arise",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,1,"al., 2023). The ease with which students can now generate essays using ChatGPT, produce code with Bard, or even answer exam questions using Gemini raises fundamental questions about academic integrity and the authenticity of student work. Furthermore, ethical considerations arise regarding transparency and fairness in Generative AI technologies. Despite the challenges, Generative AI offers potential benefits, such as enhancing the student learning experience and providing faculty sup­ port (Anon, 2023; Meyer et al., 2023). Students are also utilizing these tools for legitimate academic purposes, such as employing DALL-E to create visuals for presentations or using Gemini to conduct compre­ hensive literature reviews. However, to amplify this potential and address ethical challenges, a comprehensive and urgent governance framework incorporating student perspectives is urgently needed at the institutional level of higher education. Students are not merely passive recipients of AI in education; they are active users who engage with this technology daily. As such, they uniquely understand its practical implications and ethical challenges * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: okky.putra@office.ui.ac.id, okky.barus@uph.edu (O.P. Barus), nizar@cs.ui.ac.id (A.N. Hidayanto), eko.yon@ui.ac.id (E.Y. Handri), dana@cs.ui. ac.id (D.I. Sensuse),",180
