DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"within the higher education context. Their insights are invaluable for shaping AI governance frameworks that are truly responsive to the needs and concerns of the primary stakeholders in education. Previous research has explored the impact of Generative AI on ed­ ucation (Meyer et al., 2023). However, there still needs to be a gap in understanding student perspectives and how to formulate policies that address their needs (Gow, 2024). This study aims to bridge this gap by placing student voices at the center of the inquiry. By investigating their experiences, concerns, and recommendations, we can develop a more nuanced and effective approach to AI governance in higher education. The primary objective of this research is to explore and understand student perspectives on the governance of Generative AI in higher ed­ ucation. This includes investigating their views on the benefits, chal­ lenges, and ethical implications of using Generative AI in academic settings. The study also aims to gather student recommendations for developing and implementing effective AI governance frameworks that promote innovation while maintaining academic integrity. This study uses a mixed-methods approach with",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"and ethical implications of using Generative AI in academic settings. The study also aims to gather student recommendations for developing and implementing effective AI governance frameworks that promote innovation while maintaining academic integrity. This study uses a mixed-methods approach with an explanatory sequential design that utilizes quantitative surveys and qualitative in­ terviews (Creswell, 1994). The initial quantitative phase of this study aims to provide a general overview of student perceptions regarding Generative AI in higher education. This includes understanding their awareness and utilization of Generative AI tools, their perceived benefits and concerns, and their views on the urgency of establishing Generative AI governance frameworks. Researchers collect data from Universitas Pelita Harapan students quantitatively to understand student percep­ tions of the benefits of generative AI for academic purposes, student concerns about the implementation of generative AI in higher education, and the urgency of generative AI Governance in higher education. In addition, a qualitative analysis was conducted to identify key themes and recommendations for generative AI governance in universities from a student perspective. This data can be used to formulate comprehensive recommenda­",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"urgency of generative AI Governance in higher education. In addition, a qualitative analysis was conducted to identify key themes and recommendations for generative AI governance in universities from a student perspective. This data can be used to formulate comprehensive recommenda­ tions, including ethical guidelines for using Generative AI, risk mitiga­ tion strategies, and support needed by students in utilizing this technology responsibly. This study’s results are expected to guide uni­ versities in formulating Generative AI policies and contribute to the national discussion on the governance of this technology in higher education. 2. Literature review 2.1. Generative AI and the future of education Generative AI is poised to transform the educational landscape, of­ fering opportunities and challenges significantly. Table 1 shows some key insights, opportunities, and challenges of generative AI in education based on recent research: Looking ahead, the integration of generative AI in education should prioritize human-centric approaches that foster stronger teacher-student relationships and support social-emotional learning (Guettala et al., 2024; Kong & Yang, 2024). Continuous research is crucial to explore the evolving capabilities of generative AI, its impact across",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"generative AI in education should prioritize human-centric approaches that foster stronger teacher-student relationships and support social-emotional learning (Guettala et al., 2024; Kong & Yang, 2024). Continuous research is crucial to explore the evolving capabilities of generative AI, its impact across various aca­ demic disciplines, and the Development of new AI tools tailored to specific educational needs (Bahroun et al., 2023; Sekli et al., 2024). Furthermore, establishing clear guidelines and support mechanisms for the ethical and practical use of AI in education is essential (Gallent-Torres et al., 2023; Giannakos). While generative AI holds the potential to revolutionize education by making it more personalized, efficient, and accessible, it also necessi­ tates careful consideration of ethical, technical, and pedagogical chal­ lenges. By thoughtfully integrating AI into educational practices, educators and technologists can collaboratively create innovative and inclusive learning environments that meet the diverse needs of learners in the digital era. 2.2. Generative AI governance in higher education Generative AI is increasingly integrated into higher education in­ stitutions, necessitating robust governance frameworks to manage its impact effectively. Table 2 shows the key aspects of",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"needs of learners in the digital era. 2.2. Generative AI governance in higher education Generative AI is increasingly integrated into higher education in­ stitutions, necessitating robust governance frameworks to manage its impact effectively. Table 2 shows the key aspects of Generative AI governance in higher education: Effective governance of Generative AI in higher education requires comprehensive policies, ethical considerations, and coordinated strate­ gies to ensure responsible and equitable use of AI technologies. 2.3. Current landscape in generative AI governance The governance of Generative AI has become a complex and Table 1 Opportunities and challenges of generative AI In education. Opportunities Challenges Generative AI can create personalized content tailored to individual student needs, enhancing engagement and learning outcomes (Blake, 2023; Guettala et al., 2024; Baskara et al., 2024). This includes adaptive learning resources and real-time assessments that cater to diverse learning styles ( Mohamad et al., 2024). The use of generative AI raises concerns about academic dishonesty, such as plagiarism and the reliability of AI- generated content (Blake, 2023; Gallent-Torres et al., 2023). Ensuring the ethical use of AI in education",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"styles ( Mohamad et al., 2024). The use of generative AI raises concerns about academic dishonesty, such as plagiarism and the reliability of AI- generated content (Blake, 2023; Gallent-Torres et al., 2023). Ensuring the ethical use of AI in education is crucial (Giannakos). Tools like ChatGPT and other large language models (LLMs) can generate teaching materials, provide instant feedback, and support the creation of interactive and adaptive learning environments (Gallent-Torres et al., 2023; Xu, 2024; Faccia et al., 2023). This can save educators significant time and effort (Ho et al., 2024). Educators need to adapt to new roles, focusing more on facilitating learning and providing emotional and psychological support to students ( Kong & Yang, 2024; Deng & Meng, 2024). This shift requires significant professional development and changes in teaching methodologies (Kong & Yang, 2024). Generative AI can facilitate more interactive and engaging learning experiences through the integration of flipped classrooms and AI-driven collaborative activities (Mohamad et al., 2024). It also supports the development of skills such as critical thinking and problem-solving (Kong & Yang, 2024). The integration of AI",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,2,"interactive and engaging learning experiences through the integration of flipped classrooms and AI-driven collaborative activities (Mohamad et al., 2024). It also supports the development of skills such as critical thinking and problem-solving (Kong & Yang, 2024). The integration of AI in education must address issues related to data privacy, security, and the ethical implications of AI use (Gallent-Torres et al., 2023; Yu et al., 2023). Transparent and responsible AI models are essential to mitigate these risks (Bahroun et al., 2023). AI-driven tools can democratize education by making high-quality learning resources accessible globally, thus supporting educational equity and inclusion (Baskara et al., 2024; Yu et al., 2023). Implementing generative AI in educational settings involves navigating technical complexities and ensuring that AI tools are pedagogically sound and effective (Guettala et al., 2024; Giannakos). Table 2 Key aspects of generative AI governance in higher education. Aspects Details Policy Development Coordinated approach, clear guidelines, ethical use ( Mahon, 2024; Song, 2024). Ethical Considerations Privacy, bias, academic integrity, plagiarism detection ( Song, 2024; Williams, 2023). Accountability Transparency, human oversight, clear communication ( Dabis & Cs´aki,",180
