DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"multifaceted issue in recent years, especially in higher education. Various studies have explored key issues related to this governance, encompassing ethical, regulatory, social, technical, and operational dimensions. 1. Ethical and Social Issues Generative AI raises significant ethical concerns, including algo­ rithmic bias, data privacy, and the transparency of AI systems. The ethical use of AI is crucial to ensure fairness and accountability and prevent potential harm (Abinaya et al., 2024; Shankar, 2024; Cam­ panile et al., 2024). The proliferation of AI-generated content (AIGC) can lead to in­ formation overload, misinformation, and challenges in social infor­ mation governance (Yang et al., 2024b). Other societal impacts include the potential displacement of human jobs and the erosion of human connection in various sectors (Shankar, 2024; Wach et al., 2023). 2. Regulatory Challenges Robust regulatory frameworks are needed to manage the devel­ opment and use of Generative AI. This includes addressing the ob­ ligations of AI providers and deployers and ensuring compliance with existing laws (De Minico & Tuozzo, 2024; Migliorini, 2024; Chen & Chen, 2024). Some regions, such as China and the European",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"use of Generative AI. This includes addressing the ob­ ligations of AI providers and deployers and ensuring compliance with existing laws (De Minico & Tuozzo, 2024; Migliorini, 2024; Chen & Chen, 2024). Some regions, such as China and the European Union, have begun implementing specific regulations to govern Generative AI (Campanile et al., 2024; Migliorini, 2024). Soft law approaches, which create expectations without direct enforcement, are also being explored to govern AI. These approaches involve collaboration among various stakeholders, including gov­ ernment, industry, and academia (Gutierrez et al., 2021; Lao & You, 2024). 3. Technical and Operational Issues Ensuring the security and privacy of data used and generated by AI systems is a critical issue. Mechanisms for access control, data encryption, and identity verification are essential to protect content creators and users (Gallent-Torres et al., 2023; Yang et al., 2024b). Transparency in AI decision-making processes and accountability for AI-generated content are crucial to building trust and ensuring responsible use. This includes the need for clear labeling of synthetic content and the registration of algorithms (Migliorini, 2024; Can­ tens, 2024). 4.",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"Transparency in AI decision-making processes and accountability for AI-generated content are crucial to building trust and ensuring responsible use. This includes the need for clear labeling of synthetic content and the registration of algorithms (Migliorini, 2024; Can­ tens, 2024). 4. Collaborative Governance Effective governance of Generative AI requires the participation of diverse stakeholders, including government authorities, industry players, academia, and civil society. Collaborative models that bal­ ance diverse interests and ensure checks and balances are highly recommended (Yang et al., 2024b; Lao & You, 2024). Enhancing public understanding and literacy regarding Generative AI is essential to mitigate risks and promote responsible use. This involves educating users about the potential harms and benefits of AI technologies (Lao & You, 2024; Winkel, 2024). 5. Future Directions and Recommendations Regulatory compliance should be seen as a starting point, with ongoing efforts to improve ethical procedures and privacy risk as­ sessments (Campanile et al., 2024). Policymakers are encouraged to foster collaboration and innovation while addressing potential risks (Abinaya et al., 2024). Developing critical thinking skills among users and adapting to new technological advancements are",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"ethical procedures and privacy risk as­ sessments (Campanile et al., 2024). Policymakers are encouraged to foster collaboration and innovation while addressing potential risks (Abinaya et al., 2024). Developing critical thinking skills among users and adapting to new technological advancements are essential to navigate the evolving landscape of Generative AI (Gallent-Torres et al., 2023; Cantens, 2024). The governance of Generative AI involves addressing ethical, regu­ latory, social, technical, and operational challenges through collabora­ tive efforts and continuous improvement. Transparency, accountability, and public engagement are key to fostering responsible and beneficial use of Generative AI technologies. 3. Research methodology 3.1. Research stages The research stages in this study are adapted from Creswell and Plano Clark’s (2017) framework for mixed-methods research with an explanatory sequential design (Creswell & Plano. Clark, 2017; Fetters et al., 2013). This design was chosen because it allows for a compre­ hensive understanding of student perspectives on Generative AI gover­ nance by collecting quantitative data to gain a general overview, followed by qualitative data to explore the issues in greater depth (Chan & Lee, 2023a). The stages have been",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"a compre­ hensive understanding of student perspectives on Generative AI gover­ nance by collecting quantitative data to gain a general overview, followed by qualitative data to explore the issues in greater depth (Chan & Lee, 2023a). The stages have been modified to fit the specific context of this study and to align with the research objectives, which are to (1) explore student perceptions of the ethical and practical use of Genera­ tive AI in academic contexts and (2) identify recommendations proposed by students to ensure the ethical and practical use of Generative AI. Fig. 1 shows the research stages that have been compiled. The study process begins with a preparatory stage with clearly defined objectives and questions. The study population and sample are identified, and ethical approvals are obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Universitas Indonesia. The study instruments, such as questionnaires and interview protocols, are developed and validated. The questionnaire is validated through expert review by education and technology experts and pilot testing with a small group of students to ensure content validity, clarity, and reliability. The interview",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"such as questionnaires and interview protocols, are developed and validated. The questionnaire is validated through expert review by education and technology experts and pilot testing with a small group of students to ensure content validity, clarity, and reliability. The interview protocol is validated through peer review by fellow researchers and pilot interviews to ensure the questions are clear, relevant, and effective in eliciting the desired information. The next stage is data collection, which involves gathering quanti­ tative and qualitative data (Squires et al., 2013). Quantitative data is collected through online surveys to provide a general overview of stu­ dent perceptions and practices regarding generative AI. Qualitative data is collected through semi-structured interviews to investigate students’ awareness, utilization patterns, perceptions, and expectations concern­ ing generative AI in academic contexts. The interviews are conducted by trained researchers who follow the established interview protocol and maintain ethical research practices. The data analysis stage involves analyzing the collected quantitative and qualitative data. Quantitative data is analyzed using descriptive statistics, such as percentages and frequencies, to describe the charac­ teristics of the sample and identify",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"protocol and maintain ethical research practices. The data analysis stage involves analyzing the collected quantitative and qualitative data. Quantitative data is analyzed using descriptive statistics, such as percentages and frequencies, to describe the charac­ teristics of the sample and identify patterns in the data (Rowley, 2012). Qualitative data is analyzed using thematic analysis to identify key themes and patterns (Babchuk, 2019). Thematic analysis in this study employed an inductive coding approach. This involved carefully reading and rereading the interview transcripts to understand the data deeply. Initial codes were then identified and labeled on text segments that captured key ideas or concepts. These codes were then grouped into broader themes based on shared meanings and patterns. The software NVivo was utilized to facilitate the coding and categorization of quali­ tative data. The findings from both data types are then integrated and interpreted (Grossoehme, 2014). Data integration consists of comparing and contrasting the quantitative and qualitative data findings to develop a more comprehensive understanding of the phenomenon (Abraham et al., 2020). In the model development stage, the findings from the data analysis",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,3,"interpreted (Grossoehme, 2014). Data integration consists of comparing and contrasting the quantitative and qualitative data findings to develop a more comprehensive understanding of the phenomenon (Abraham et al., 2020). In the model development stage, the findings from the data analysis Fig. 1. Research stages. O.P. Barus et al. International Journal of Educational Research Open 8 (2025) 100452 3",58
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"are used to develop a conceptual model of generative AI governance in higher education (Chan, 2023). This model is based on the themes and patterns identified in the data analysis and is informed by relevant theoretical frameworks. The model is validated by discussing the study findings and the resulting model with experts in education and technology. Finally, in the conclusion and dissemination stage, the key findings and recommendations from the study are summarized and disseminated through various channels, such as peer-reviewed publications and con­ ference presentations. The implications of the findings for policy and practice are also discussed. The study’s limitations are acknowledged, and directions for future research are suggested. 3.2. Research design This research employs a mixed-methods explanatory sequential design to gain a more holistic and in-depth understanding of recom­ mendations for Generative AI governance from a student perspective (Chan & Hu, 2023). The explanatory sequential design is particularly well-suited to this study because it allows in-depth exploration of student perspectives on a relatively new and evolving phenomenon like Generative AI. We can identify key trends and patterns in",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"(Chan & Hu, 2023). The explanatory sequential design is particularly well-suited to this study because it allows in-depth exploration of student perspectives on a relatively new and evolving phenomenon like Generative AI. We can identify key trends and patterns in student perceptions and practices by starting with quantitative data collection. This quantitative data then informs the subsequent qualitative data collection, allowing us to focus on exploring the reasons behind those trends and patterns in greater depth. This iterative data collection and analysis process allows for a more comprehensive and nuanced understanding of the topic. This design is chosen because it allows for a comprehensive under­ standing of the phenomenon by first collecting quantitative data to identify patterns and trends, followed by qualitative data to explore those patterns in greater depth (Giannakopoulos et al., 2023). This approach is beneficial in this study as it allows for the exploration of student perceptions through both quantitative surveys and qualitative interviews, providing a richer and more nuanced understanding of the topic compared to using only one method. Quantitative data alone might show what students",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"study as it allows for the exploration of student perceptions through both quantitative surveys and qualitative interviews, providing a richer and more nuanced understanding of the topic compared to using only one method. Quantitative data alone might show what students think, but qualitative data helps explain why they hold those views. The questionnaire was developed based on the research objectives and validated through expert review by the Centre for Teaching and Learning UPH and pilot testing with a small group of students. This ensured the content validity and clarity of the questions. This model aims to provide an integrative perspective on Generative AI governance in higher education institutions, informed by student perspectives. The research method is broadly illustrated in Fig. 2. The quantitative data, collected through questionnaires, provides a general overview of student perceptions and practices regarding generative AI. This data then informs the selection of participants for the qualitative phase by selecting students with high levels of ethical awareness, involving semi-structured interviews to delve deeper into the reasons behind those perceptions and practices (Chen et al., 2024). The findings",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"data then informs the selection of participants for the qualitative phase by selecting students with high levels of ethical awareness, involving semi-structured interviews to delve deeper into the reasons behind those perceptions and practices (Chen et al., 2024). The findings from both phases are then integrated to develop a compre­ hensive understanding of student perspectives on Generative AI gover­ nance. For example, if the quantitative data shows widespread concern about plagiarism, the qualitative interviews can explore what specific aspects of AI use worry students, leading to more targeted recommendations. The informants for this study were selected from undergraduate students at Universitas Pelita Harapan, representing three faculties: the Faculty of Law, the Faculty of Computer Science, and the Faculty of Economics and Business. These faculties were selected due to their diverse academic disciplines, which are expected to offer varied per­ spectives on generative AI utilization and governance. While conve­ nience sampling was used to select participants within these faculties, the inclusion criteria ensured that the participants were active students who had taken at least one course utilizing learning management sys­ tems,",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"on generative AI utilization and governance. While conve­ nience sampling was used to select participants within these faculties, the inclusion criteria ensured that the participants were active students who had taken at least one course utilizing learning management sys­ tems, thus having some experience with technology integration in ed­ ucation. However, it is acknowledged that convenience sampling has limitations in terms of representativeness. Future research could consider purposive or stratified random sampling to include diverse student perspectives, including those from the humanities, to provide a more comprehensive and generalizable understanding of the topic. 3.3. Sample and participants The informants for this study were selected from undergraduate students at Universitas Pelita Harapan, representing three faculties: the Faculty of Law, the Faculty of Computer Science, and the Faculty of Economics and Business. These faculties were selected due to their diverse academic disciplines, which are expected to offer varied per­ spectives on generative AI utilization and governance. To determine the sample size for the quantitative phase (survey), we used the following formula: n = ( Z2 × p × (1 −p) ) /",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"which are expected to offer varied per­ spectives on generative AI utilization and governance. To determine the sample size for the quantitative phase (survey), we used the following formula: n = ( Z2 × p × (1 −p) ) / E2 Where: • n = sample size • Z = Z-score for desired confidence level (1.96 for 95 % confidence level) • p = estimated population proportion (0.5 used for conservative estimate) • E = margin of error (0.05 for 5 % margin of error) Using this formula, the minimum required sample size was 111. This sample size was sufficient to provide a reliable representation of the student population at Universitas Pelita Harapan. We used a stratified sampling approach for the qualitative phase (interviews) to ensure each faculty member was represented. Based on their responses and ethical awareness, 53 students were selected from the initial pool of 111 survey respondents for interviews. This sample size was deemed adequate to provide rich and in-depth qualitative data while considering the study’s time and resource constraints. While convenience sampling was used to select",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,4,"were selected from the initial pool of 111 survey respondents for interviews. This sample size was deemed adequate to provide rich and in-depth qualitative data while considering the study’s time and resource constraints. While convenience sampling was used to select participants within these faculties, the inclusion criteria ensured that the participants were active students who had taken at least one course utilizing learning management systems, thus having some experience with technology integration in education. However, it is acknowledged that convenience sampling has limitations in terms of representativeness. Future research could consider purposive or stratified random sampling to include diverse student perspectives, including those from the humanities, to provide a more comprehensive and generalizable understanding of the topic. Fig. 2. Research methodology. O.P. Barus et al. International Journal of Educational Research Open 8 (2025) 100452 4",135
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,5,"4. Results and discussion The following is a comprehensive analysis of the collected data based on the established research method protocol. It highlights key findings and their implications for Generative AI governance in higher education to offer a holistic understanding of the research questions discussed. 4.1. Quantitative findings on student perceptions 1. Demographic Information of the Informants The research informants are students of Universitas Pelita Harapan, totaling 111 respondents from three faculties: the Faculty of Computer Science, consisting of the Information Systems study program (25 re­ spondents) and Informatics (24 respondents); the Faculty of Economics and Business, consisting of the Accounting study program (26 re­ spondents) and Management (18 respondents); and the Faculty of Law with the Law study program (18 respondents). The students involved as respondents are those in their first year (73 %) and second year (26 %) of study. Additionally, the student representatives involved as informants are active members of student organizations and students with a GPA > 3 (on a 4-point scale). Fig. 3 shows the profile of the informants. 2. Awareness and Utilization Perspectives of",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,5,"of study. Additionally, the student representatives involved as informants are active members of student organizations and students with a GPA > 3 (on a 4-point scale). Fig. 3 shows the profile of the informants. 2. Awareness and Utilization Perspectives of Generative AI Based on the survey distributed, 96 % of respondents were aware of and had used Generative AI, such as ChatGPT, DALL-E, and Bard, for academic activities (Leelavathi & Surendhranatha, 2024; Giannako­ poulos et al., 2023). This high level of awareness (96 %) indicates that Generative AI is already quite popular among students actively trying out this technology. This aligns with research by Chen et al. (2024), which shows a high level of Generative AI adoption in higher education (Chen et al., 2024; Chan & Lee, 2023b; Estelle Smith et al., 2024). This high awareness indicates an urgency for universities to respond to this phenomenon by formulating clear policies and guidelines regarding using Generative AI in academic settings (Dabis & Cs´aki, 2024; Chan, 2023). These policies are needed to ensure that Generative AI is used ethically and responsibly and",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,5,"universities to respond to this phenomenon by formulating clear policies and guidelines regarding using Generative AI in academic settings (Dabis & Cs´aki, 2024; Chan, 2023). These policies are needed to ensure that Generative AI is used ethically and responsibly and maximize its benefits for the learning process (Beckingham et al., 2024; Chen et al., 2024). Students’ utilization of Generative AI for academic activities is described in Fig. 4. Fig. 4 shows that Generative AI is mainly used to find ideas for assignments (29 %) and to learn new topics (21 %). The use of writing code for programs or queries for journal searches is also quite significant (14 %). This shows that students have utilized Generative AI for various academic purposes, from finding ideas and learning new topics to assisting in writing program code (Fig. 5). This data shows that Generative AI can potentially increase the effectiveness and efficiency of the learning process (Beckingham et al., 2024). Therefore, universities need to guide so that the use of Generative AI remains by academic ethics and does not replace students’ critical thinking",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,5,"Generative AI can potentially increase the effectiveness and efficiency of the learning process (Beckingham et al., 2024). Therefore, universities need to guide so that the use of Generative AI remains by academic ethics and does not replace students’ critical thinking skills (Qadhi et al., 2024). Based on the distributed questionnaires, the primary sources of in­ formation obtained by students regarding how to utilize Generative AI for academic needs are social media (37 %), friends (26 %), lecturers (21 %), news or articles (15 %) and others (1 %). Social media is significant in disseminating information about Generative AI among students. This shows the importance of universities being active in providing accurate information and education regarding the use of generative AI through various channels, including social media and lecturers. The researchers also obtained data on the benefits of Generative AI and how important it is for higher education institutions to provide guidance or a governance framework to regulate the use of Generative AI. Tables 3 and 4 show the percentages obtained. Table 3 shows that most students (94.1 %) perceive Generative",180
