DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,6,"%). According to 69 % of students, the university needs courses that support ethical literacy in using Generative AI. Furthermore, the uni­ versity is asked to socialize more policies and guidelines for using Generative AI through workshops or seminars (39 %), new student orientation sessions (25 %), and providing self-paced learning courses (21 %). This data indicates that students are enthusiastic about Gener­ ative AI and have concerns about its potential negative impacts. Uni­ versities need to address these concerns by formulating appropriate policies, such as providing education on the responsible use of Genera­ tive AI, establishing clear rules about plagiarism, and providing critical guidance in evaluating information generated by Generative AI. 4.2. Thematic analysis Thematic analysis was conducted to identify the main themes and patterns that emerged from the interview data and to deeply understand student perspectives on Generative AI governance in higher education institutions. The thematic analysis process followed the stages proposed by Braun and Clarke, namely: (1) Familiarization with data; (2) Generating initial codes; (3) Searching for themes; (4) Reviewing themes; (5) Defining and naming themes; and",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,6,"AI governance in higher education institutions. The thematic analysis process followed the stages proposed by Braun and Clarke, namely: (1) Familiarization with data; (2) Generating initial codes; (3) Searching for themes; (4) Reviewing themes; (5) Defining and naming themes; and (6) Producing the report (Clarke & Braun, 2014). One hundred eleven students participated in an online survey to provide a general overview of student awareness, utilization, and per­ ceptions of Generative AI. Subsequently, 53 students out of the 111 survey respondents were selected using stratified sampling to participate in semi-structured interviews. The student sample consisted of repre­ sentatives from each study program. The interviews involved five lec­ turers as interview facilitators to gain a deeper understanding of student perspectives on Generative AI governance in higher education, covering aspects of ethics, curriculum integration, misuse detection, and aca­ demic sanctions. The questions for the qualitative study are formulated based on the result of the quantitative research, such as how students perceive the benefits of using Generative AI for academic purposes. The four questions addressed to the student representatives in the interviews included:",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,6,"for the qualitative study are formulated based on the result of the quantitative research, such as how students perceive the benefits of using Generative AI for academic purposes. The four questions addressed to the student representatives in the interviews included: (1) How do you think Generative AI can be effectively inte­ grated into the university curriculum? (2) How should the university detect the use of Generative AI that is not by applicable policies? (3) What sanctions do you think are appropriate for violations of Generative AI in academic settings? (4) What resources or support would help students use Generative AI responsibly and ethically? Thematic analysis was conducted for each question and response from all students. 1. Integrating Generative AI into the University Curriculum Based on the first question, ""How do you think Generative AI can be effectively integrated into the university curriculum?"" student responses were analyzed, and the results are presented in Table 3. The quotes in this table represent the views of the 53 students who participated in the interviews (Table 5). Students support the integration of Generative AI",180
