DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,7,"misuse of Generative AI, such as plagiarism (Sysoyev, 2024). They propose various approaches to detect the use of Generative AI that does not comply with policies. Some students suggest that universities utilize existing plagiarism detection software. A student from the Faculty of Law said, ""I think plagiarism detection software like Turnitin is already quite effective in detecting plagiarism, both from online sources and from Generative AI. Universities must improve the software’s capabilities to detect plagiarism from Generative AI more accurately."" Other students propose that universities develop independent detection sys­ tems specifically designed to detect the use of Generative AI. A student from the Faculty of Computer Science argued, ""Universities can collabo­ rate with experts in the field of artificial intelligence to develop a system that can identify the characteristics of text or program code generated by Generative AI. This system will be more effective than conventional plagia­ rism detection software."" In addition, some students also propose a pattern analysis and sim­ ilarity level approach. A Faculty of Economics and Business student said, ""Lecturers can be trained to recognize patterns or",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,7,"will be more effective than conventional plagia­ rism detection software."" In addition, some students also propose a pattern analysis and sim­ ilarity level approach. A Faculty of Economics and Business student said, ""Lecturers can be trained to recognize patterns or language styles typical of Generative AI. Also, the level of similarity between student answers can be an indicator of the use of Generative AI. If many students provide very similar answers, they may be using Generative AI."" The approaches proposed by these students align with several re­ searchers’ recommendations. For example, Perkins et al. (2024) emphasize the importance of effective and transparent detection mechanisms to prevent the misuse of Generative AI in academic settings (Perkins et al., 2024). They also recommend the use of plagiarism detection software and the development of more sophisticated detection systems (Sysoyev, 2024; Sysoyev, 2024). 3. Sanctions for Violations of Generative AI Use The third question asked, ""What sanctions do you think are appro­ priate for violations of Generative AI use in academic settings?"" Students have diverse views on appropriate sanctions for violations of Generative AI",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,7,"Sanctions for Violations of Generative AI Use The third question asked, ""What sanctions do you think are appro­ priate for violations of Generative AI use in academic settings?"" Students have diverse views on appropriate sanctions for violations of Generative AI use. These sanctions can range from grade reduction, assignment repetition, warnings, education and reflection to more severe sanctions such as not being allowed to graduate or expulsion. Table 7 explains the results of the thematic analysis for this question. Some students felt that assignment repetition could be a valuable learning experience. A Faculty of Computer Science student suggested, ""Instead of just punishing students, repeating the assignment allows them to learn from their mistakes and understand why using Generative AI in that instance was inappropriate."" Warnings and formal reprimands were also seen as essential sanctions (Sysoyev, 2024). A student from the Faculty of Law noted, ""Warnings serve as an initial deterrent and can be an official record for the university to monitor student behavior regarding the use of Generative AI."" Several students emphasized the importance of education and reflection as part",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,7,"Faculty of Law noted, ""Warnings serve as an initial deterrent and can be an official record for the university to monitor student behavior regarding the use of Generative AI."" Several students emphasized the importance of education and reflection as part of the sanctions. One student commented, ""Sanc­ tions should not only be punitive but also educational. Students should be required to reflect on their actions and understand the ethical implications of misusing Generative AI."" For severe violations, such as submitting an entirely AI-generated thesis, some students supported expulsion or a ban on graduation. A student from the Faculty of Computer Science argued, ""A thesis is a sig­ nificant final project, and submitting AI-generated work without proper attribution undermines the entire academic process. Such violations should have severe consequences."" The views expressed by students highlight the importance of having a clear and comprehensive policy on academic sanctions for Generative AI misuse. This policy should outline various types of violations and corresponding sanctions, ensuring fairness and proportionality. It should also emphasize the educational aspect of sanctions, encouraging students to learn from their",180
