DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"adequate competencies in using Generative AI will be more capable of actively participating in discussions and decision- making regarding the governance of this technology on campus. 3. Ethical Awareness and Responsibility Data triangulation shows that students have varying levels of awareness regarding ethical issues in using Generative AI. Survey results indicate that 29 % of students are worried about dependence on Generative AI technology, and 26 % are concerned about plagiarism. In interviews, some students expressed concerns about the misuse of Generative AI for academic cheating and the spread of misleading in­ formation. Literature studies confirm the importance of increasing stu­ dent awareness and ethical responsibility in using Generative AI (Blake, 2023; Baskara et al., 2024). Based on these findings, Proposition 3 is formulated: Ethical awareness and responsibility influence the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Students with high ethical awareness and commitment will be more likely to use Generative AI wisely and responsibly, thus supporting the creation of moral governance. 4. Prioritizing a Detective Approach Survey results indicate that all students support implementing",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"student perspective. Students with high ethical awareness and commitment will be more likely to use Generative AI wisely and responsibly, thus supporting the creation of moral governance. 4. Prioritizing a Detective Approach Survey results indicate that all students support implementing plagiarism detection software to maintain academic integrity in the context of Generative AI. However, some students interviewed expressed concerns about the accuracy and effectiveness of such soft­ ware. Literature studies show that the detective approach needs to be balanced with educational and preventive efforts (Walczak & Cellary, 2023; Dotan et al., 2024). Therefore, Proposition 4 is formulated: Prioritizing a detective approach influences the governance of Generative AI in higher educa­ tion institutions from the student perspective. A practical and trans­ parent detective approach can increase student trust in the assessment system and encourage the responsible use of Generative AI. 5. University Academic Sanctions for AI Ethics Violations Data triangulation shows that students want strict and fair academic sanctions for students who commit ethical violations using Generative AI. Survey results indicate that 90 % of respondents agree that plagia­ rism using",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"University Academic Sanctions for AI Ethics Violations Data triangulation shows that students want strict and fair academic sanctions for students who commit ethical violations using Generative AI. Survey results indicate that 90 % of respondents agree that plagia­ rism using Generative AI should be subject to academic sanctions. In­ terviews with students reveal that they expect clear and transparent rules regarding these academic sanctions. Literature studies confirm the importance of establishing proportionate and educational academic sanctions (Walczak & Cellary, 2023). Based on this, Proposition 5 is formulated: University academic sanctions for AI ethics violations influence the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Strict and fair academic sanctions can provide a deterrent effect and create an educational environment that upholds integrity and ethics in using Generative AI. 6. Key Propositions Student Perspectives on Generative AI Governance The propositions formulated in this study are suggestions developed based on thematic analysis of the qualitative data. These propositions provide a framework for understanding the factors influencing students’ views on using and regulating Generative AI in the educational context",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"Governance The propositions formulated in this study are suggestions developed based on thematic analysis of the qualitative data. These propositions provide a framework for understanding the factors influencing students’ views on using and regulating Generative AI in the educational context (Chan & Hu, 2023). P1: Pedagogical Orientation towards Generative AI influences the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative findings, this proposition suggests that students who experience pedagogical approaches that embrace AI are more likely to support governance frameworks that promote innovation and ethical AI integration. For example, some stu­ dents expressed a desire for Student-centered learning incorporating AI tools: ""I think Generative AI can be used to create personalized learning materials that cater to the specific needs of each student."" And A curriculum that integrates AI ethics and responsible use: ""It’s important for the cur­ riculum to not only teach us how to use AI tools but also how to use them ethically and responsibly."" These findings highlight the importance of pedagogical approaches that effectively leverage Generative AI while maintaining",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"""It’s important for the cur­ riculum to not only teach us how to use AI tools but also how to use them ethically and responsibly."" These findings highlight the importance of pedagogical approaches that effectively leverage Generative AI while maintaining the valuable aspects of human interaction in education. This aligns with the findings of (Bearman & Ajjawi, 2023; Chundur & Hall, 2023) those who demonstrated that inclusive and student-centered pedagogical models can enhance the acceptance of new technologies, including generative AI, in academic settings. P2: The development of Generative AI competencies for students influences the governance of Generative AI in higher education in­ stitutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative find­ ings, this proposition suggests that students equipped with the knowledge and skills to utilize generative AI effectively for learning tend to favor policies that encourage responsible AI use. For example, some students expressed a desire for Training and workshops on Generative AI: ""I think the university should offer workshops or training sessions on how to use Generative AI tools effectively for academic purposes."" Integrating Generative AI into",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"use. For example, some students expressed a desire for Training and workshops on Generative AI: ""I think the university should offer workshops or training sessions on how to use Generative AI tools effectively for academic purposes."" Integrating Generative AI into the curriculum: ""It would be helpful if it was integrated into the curriculum so that we can learn how to use it in a practical context."" Access to self-learning materials on Generative AI: ""I would like access to online tutorials or resources that can help me learn about Generative AI at my own pace."" These findings suggest that students who feel competent in using Generative AI are more likely to support policies that promote its responsible use. Conversely, students lacking these competencies may be more apprehensive about using Generative AI and advocate for stricter regulations. For instance, students proficient in using generative AI for research might support policies that allow AI assistance with proper attribution, while those less familiar with AI might prefer a complete ban on AI use in assessments. P3: Ethical awareness and responsibility influence the governance of",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"using generative AI for research might support policies that allow AI assistance with proper attribution, while those less familiar with AI might prefer a complete ban on AI use in assessments. P3: Ethical awareness and responsibility influence the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative findings, this proposition suggests that students with high ethical awareness tend to support the imple­ mentation of strict regulations and sanctions against the misuse of Generative AI. For example, one student from the Faculty of Law stated, ’Strict regulations are necessary to prevent plagiarism and ensure academic integrity in the use of Generative AI.’ Another student from the Faculty of Computer Science expressed, ’There should be consequences for those who misuse Generative AI, such as suspension or expulsion, to deter unethical behaviour.’. These findings suggest that students aware of the potential risks of Generative AI, such as plagiarism and the spread of misinfor­ mation, recognize the importance of having clear guidelines and con­ sequences for misuse (Baxter & undefined, 2023). In the quantitative analysis, 87.7 %",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,9,"students aware of the potential risks of Generative AI, such as plagiarism and the spread of misinfor­ mation, recognize the importance of having clear guidelines and con­ sequences for misuse (Baxter & undefined, 2023). In the quantitative analysis, 87.7 % of students agree that universities need to regulate the use of Generative AI, and 85.5 % agree that there should be penalties for Generative AI misuse. This data further supports the proposition that students’ ethical awareness and responsibility influence their views on the governance of Generative AI. Therefore, universities should consider incorporating student perspectives in the development and imple­ mentation of AI governance frameworks to ensure that the policies are effective and aligned with the ethical considerations of the student body. P4: Prioritizing a detective approach influences the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative findings, this proposition suggests O.P. Barus et al. International Journal of Educational Research Open 8 (2025) 100452 9",162
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"that students view a detective approach, which focuses on detecting and preventing AI misuse rather than solely punishing offenders, as a fairer and more effective way to address the ethical challenges of Generative AI. For example, some students suggested that universities should Utilize plagiarism detection software: ""Plagiarism detection software can help identify instances of AI-generated content being submitted as original work."" Develop independent detection systems: ""Universities should invest in developing their own AI detection systems that are specifically trained to identify AI-generated content."" and Analyze patterns and similarity levels: ""Lecturers should be trained to recognize patterns and similarities in student work that may indicate the use of AI."" These findings suggest that students favor a proactive approach to AI governance that prioritizes prevention and education. For instance, students may favor using AI detection tools to identify plagiarism and promote academic integrity, coupled with educational initiatives that raise awareness about responsible AI use. P5: University academic sanctions for AI ethics violations influence the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative findings, this",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"integrity, coupled with educational initiatives that raise awareness about responsible AI use. P5: University academic sanctions for AI ethics violations influence the governance of Generative AI in higher education institutions from the student perspective. Based on the qualitative findings, this propo­ sition suggests that students generally support strong academic sanc­ tions for AI ethics violations. For example, some students recommended Grade deductions: ""I think grade deductions are a fair consequence for those who use AI to cheat."" Assignment repetition: ""If someone is caught misusing AI, they should be required to redo the assignment."" And Suspension or expulsion for severe violations: ""In severe cases, like submitting an entirely AI-generated thesis, I think suspension or expulsion is warranted."". These findings suggest that students view academic sanctions as an essential deterrent for AI misuse and a means to uphold academic integrity. Clear and enforceable sanctions can encourage students to engage with AI ethically. For example, a university’s clear policy on AI use in assess­ ments and well-defined consequences for violations can encourage stu­ dents to engage with AI ethically. Fig. 6 presents an",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"enforceable sanctions can encourage students to engage with AI ethically. For example, a university’s clear policy on AI use in assess­ ments and well-defined consequences for violations can encourage stu­ dents to engage with AI ethically. Fig. 6 presents an initial proposal for a Generative AI governance model developed based on student perspectives. 5. Conclusion and future work This research reveals that students support the integration of generative AI in education but emphasize the importance of competency development, ethical awareness, and misuse prevention mechanisms. Students expect generative AI to be integrated into the curriculum and learning methods and to support their competency development in utilizing this technology. However, they also recognize the importance of ethics and responsibility in generative AI and the need for detection mechanisms and sanctions to prevent misuse. The quantitative survey indicated that students expect generative AI to be integrated into the curriculum and learning methods to support their competency in uti­ lizing this technology. The qualitative findings highlighted that students recognize the importance of ethics and responsibility in generative AI and the need for detection mechanisms",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"to be integrated into the curriculum and learning methods to support their competency in uti­ lizing this technology. The qualitative findings highlighted that students recognize the importance of ethics and responsibility in generative AI and the need for detection mechanisms and sanctions to prevent misuse. An essential contribution of this research is highlighting student perspectives, which are often overlooked in discussions on generative AI governance in higher education. The findings demonstrate that students deeply understand the potential and challenges of generative AI and can provide valuable input in formulating effective policies. Based on these findings, higher education institutions are recom­ mended to (1) establish an AI ethics committee that includes student representatives from various faculties, (2) develop AI competency training modules integrated into the curriculum, and (3) hold open discussions on AI ethics involving students and faculty regularly. By ensuring that student voices are heard in the generative AI governance process, we can realize the full potential of this technology to enhance the quality of higher education and prepare future genera­ tions for the challenges of the AI era. While",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"that student voices are heard in the generative AI governance process, we can realize the full potential of this technology to enhance the quality of higher education and prepare future genera­ tions for the challenges of the AI era. While this research provides valuable insights into student perspec­ tives on generative AI governance, it is essential to acknowledge its limitations. The study focused on one university and three specific fac­ ulties, which may not fully represent the diversity of viewpoints across different institutions and disciplines. Furthermore, the sample primarily consisted of high-achieving students, which may have excluded the perspectives of average or struggling students who might face different challenges when using AI tools. Future research should address these limitations by expanding the scope of the study to include a more diverse range of students from various universities, faculties, and academic backgrounds. This will provide a more comprehensive understanding of student perspectives on generative AI governance and contribute to developing more inclusive and effective AI policies in higher education. The quantitative study in this research successfully provided a gen­ eral overview",180
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,10,"backgrounds. This will provide a more comprehensive understanding of student perspectives on generative AI governance and contribute to developing more inclusive and effective AI policies in higher education. The quantitative study in this research successfully provided a gen­ eral overview of student perceptions towards Generative AI and iden­ tified important patterns further explored through the qualitative analysis. Future research could design quantitative studies with a confirmatory approach to test these propositions as hypotheses and statistically analyze the relationships between variables. Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the writing process While preparing this work, the authors used Gemini Advanced to improve the language and readability of the manuscript. After using this tool/service, the authors reviewed and edited the content as needed and took full responsibility for the publication’s content. Declarations This study was funded by Universitas Indonesia. Fig. 6. A Preliminary model for generative AI governance. O.P. Barus et al. International Journal of Educational Research Open 8 (2025) 100452 10",161
DOC_025,NEW04.pdf,10.1016/j.ijedro.2025.100452,11,"CRediT authorship contribution statement Okky Putra Barus: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Software, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Investigation, Funding acquisi­ tion, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Achmad Nizar Hidayanto: Validation, Supervision, Project administration, Method­ ology, Formal analysis, Data curation. Eko Yon Handri: Visualization, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Dana Indra Sensuse: Supervision, Methodology, Conceptualization. Chairote Yaiprasert: Visualization, Resources, Formal analysis. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgments The authors would like to express their sincere gratitude to Uni­ versitas Indonesia and Universitas Pelita Harapan for their invaluable support in conducting this research. This work was supported by Funding Rispro INSPIRASI No 2924/E3/AL.04/2024 and No 4809/UN1. P/HK.08.00/2024.",137
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,1,"Open Access © The Author(s) 2024. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the mate- rial. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http:// creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. RESEARCH ARTICLE Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7 International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education First‑year students AI‑competence as a predictor for intended and de facto use of AI‑tools for supporting learning processes in higher education",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,1,"et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7 International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education First‑year students AI‑competence as a predictor for intended and de facto use of AI‑tools for supporting learning processes in higher education Jan Delcker1* , Joana Heil1, Dirk Ifenthaler1,2, Sabine Seufert3 and Lukas Spirgi3 Abstract The influence of Artificial Intelligence on higher education is increasing. As important drivers for student retention and learning success, generative AI-tools like transla- tors, paraphrasers and most lately chatbots can support students in their learning processes. The perceptions and expectations of first-years students related to AI-tools have not yet been researched in-depth. The same can be stated about necessary requirements and skills for the purposeful use of AI-tools. The research work examines the relationship between first-year students’ knowledge, skills and attitudes and their use of AI-tools for their learning processes. Analysing the data of 634 first-year students revealed that attitudes towards AI significantly explains the intended use of AI tools. Additionally, the perceived benefits of AI-technology are predictors for students’ per- ception of AI-robots as cooperation partners",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,1,"their learning processes. Analysing the data of 634 first-year students revealed that attitudes towards AI significantly explains the intended use of AI tools. Additionally, the perceived benefits of AI-technology are predictors for students’ per- ception of AI-robots as cooperation partners for humans. Educators in higher educa- tion must facilitate students’ AI competencies and integrate AI-tools into instructional designs. As a result, students learning processes will be improved. Keywords: Artificial Intelligence, Higher education, Learning process, AI tool, Chatbot Introduction AI-robots are agents programmed to fulfill tasks traditionally done by humans (Dang & Liu, 2022). The number of interactions between humans and AI-robots is increasing, which is a strong indicator of the integration of AI-technology into the lives of humans (Kim et al., 2022). A popular example is the deployment of chatbots on a website. These AI-robots can guide users and respond to basic user requests (Larasati et al., 2022). The technology behind semi-automated and fully automated human-like task fulfillment is based on AI-methods and AI-algorithms (Gkinko & Elbanna, 2023). These AI-methods and -algorithms form the main programming characteristics of AI-robots (Woschank",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,1,"to basic user requests (Larasati et al., 2022). The technology behind semi-automated and fully automated human-like task fulfillment is based on AI-methods and AI-algorithms (Gkinko & Elbanna, 2023). These AI-methods and -algorithms form the main programming characteristics of AI-robots (Woschank et al., 2020). The features lead to an increasing similarity in the performance of humans and AI-robots (Byrd et al., 2021). Additionally, the appearance and behavior of AI-robots are becoming more human-like (Hildt, 2021). While most machines are easily distin- guishable from humans, AI-robots might be hard to identify (Desaire et al., 2023) and *Correspondence: delcker@uni-mannheim.de 1 University of Mannheim, L4, 1, 68161 Mannheim, Germany 2 Curtin University, Perth, Australia 3 University of St. Gallen, St. Gallen, Switzerland",118
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,2,"Page 2 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 the ability to identify AI-robots is one of the many challenges accompanying these new technologies. As a result, humans even start to attribute AI-robots with human-like understanding, as well as mental capacities (Roesler et al., 2021). Accordingly, new and changing demands in humans’ digital competencies are required to deal with the various applications of AI-robots in all sectors of human life (Seufert & Tarantini, 2022). One of these fields is higher education, which is strongly affected by introducing AI-technology and AI-robots (Ouyang et al., 2022; Popenici & Kerr, 2017). Future applications for AI-technology can be found at all levels of higher educa- tion (Ocaña-Fernández et al., 2019). On the student level, virtual AI teaching assistants (Kim et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2022) and intelligent tutoring systems (Azevedo et al., 2022; Latham, 2022) have the capability to guide individual learner paths (Brusilovsky, 2023; Rahayu et al., 2023). Educators might implement automated grading and assessment tools (Heil & Ifenthaler, 2023; Celik et al., 2022) or create",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,2,"tutoring systems (Azevedo et al., 2022; Latham, 2022) have the capability to guide individual learner paths (Brusilovsky, 2023; Rahayu et al., 2023). Educators might implement automated grading and assessment tools (Heil & Ifenthaler, 2023; Celik et al., 2022) or create educational content with gen- erative AI (Bozkurt & Sharma, 2023; Kaplan-Rakowski et al., 2023). The administration of higher education institutions has to adapt their policies to the new technology (Chan, 2023), while incorporating learning analytic tools to improve study conditions, reduce drop-out rates, and adapt their study programs (Aldowah et al., 2019; Ifenthaler & Yau, 2020; Ouyang et al., 2023; Tsai et al., 2020). These developments are embedded into national policy-making processes, such as creating ethics guidelines (Jobin et al., 2019) and competence frameworks (Vuorikari et al., 2022) for AI-technology. According to recent studies, first-year students enter institutions of higher learning with various perceptions and expectations about university life, for instance, in terms of social aspects, learning experiences, and academic support (Houser, 2004). While students’ generic academic skills appear to be well-established for coping with higher education requirements, their competencies",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,2,"learning with various perceptions and expectations about university life, for instance, in terms of social aspects, learning experiences, and academic support (Houser, 2004). While students’ generic academic skills appear to be well-established for coping with higher education requirements, their competencies related to AI seem to be limited (Ng et al., 2023). As of now, there are no conceptual frameworks that cover the use of human-like AI- technology, focusing on first-year students within the context of higher education. Thus, this study is targeting this research gap. For this purpose, seven functionalities of AI- tools have been conceptualized for their application in the context of higher education. This conceptualization is a helpful differentiation to analyze the intent and frequency of use, as well as possible indicators that might affect intent and frequency of use. As a result, implications for further implementing AI-tools in higher education learning pro- cesses will be derived. Background First‑year students First-year students’ perceptions and expectations and how they cope with academic requirements in higher education have been identified as important factors for learning success and student retention (Mah,",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,2,"in higher education learning pro- cesses will be derived. Background First‑year students First-year students’ perceptions and expectations and how they cope with academic requirements in higher education have been identified as important factors for learning success and student retention (Mah, & Ifenthaler, 2018; Tinto, 1994; Yorke & Longden, 2008). Several studies identified a mismatch between first-year students’ perceptions and academic reality (Smith & Wertlieb, 2005). Furthermore, research indicates that many first-year students do not know what is expected at university and are often academi- cally unprepared (Mah & Ifenthaler 2017; McCarthy & Kuh, 2006). Students’ prepared- ness is particularly relevant concerning generic skills such as academic competencies,",107
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,3,"Page 3 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 which they should possess when entering university (Barrie, 2007). Numerous aspects, including sociodemographic features, study choices, cognitive ability, motivation, per- sonal circumstances, and academic and social integration, have been linked to first-year students’ learning success and retention in higher education (Bean & Eaton, 2020; Sanavi & Matt, 2022). Mah & Ifenthaler (2017) identified five academic competencies for suc- cessful degree completion: time management, learning skills, self-monitoring, technol- ogy proficiency, and research skills. Accordingly, coping with academic requirements is an important driver of student retention in higher education (Thomas, 2002). Moreover, students’ perceptions of their first year can affect student success (Crisp et al., 2009). More recently, it has been argued that competencies related to AI are an important driver for student retention and learning success (Bates et al., 2020; Mah, 2016; Ng et al., 2023). Nonetheless, first-year students’ perceptions, expectations, and academic com- petencies for coping with academic requirements related to AI-tools have not yet been researched in-depth. Conceptualization of AI‑tools in higher education Dang",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,3,"(Bates et al., 2020; Mah, 2016; Ng et al., 2023). Nonetheless, first-year students’ perceptions, expectations, and academic com- petencies for coping with academic requirements related to AI-tools have not yet been researched in-depth. Conceptualization of AI‑tools in higher education Dang and Liu (2022) propose a differentiation of AI-robots, which is also used in this study. They categorize AI-robots into << mindful >> (AI-robots with increasingly human characteristics) and << mindless >> (AI-robots with machine characteristics) tools. The so-called mindful AI-robots can perform more complex tasks, react to the prompts of the users in a more meaningful way, and are designed to act and look like humans. On the other hand, mindless AI-robots perform fewer complex tasks and appear more like machines. In the following, a short overview of AI-tools is provided, including their main functionality and examples for practical use in higher education learning processes: Mindless AI‑robots 1) Translation text generators: These tools use written text as input and translate the text into a different language. Translation text generators can help to quickly translate text into the language a student",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,3,"in higher education learning processes: Mindless AI‑robots 1) Translation text generators: These tools use written text as input and translate the text into a different language. Translation text generators can help to quickly translate text into the language a student is most familiar with or to translate into a language that is required by the assignment. Many study programs require students to hand in (some) papers in a language different from the study program’s language (Galante, 2020). Two of the most prominent translation text generators are Google Translate and DeepL (Mar- tín-Martín et al., 2021). 2) Summarizing/rephrasing text generators: These tools use written text as input and can change the structure of the text. On the one hand, they are used to extract critical information, keywords, or main concepts out of structured text, reducing the complexity of the input text. In this way, they help the user focus on the input text’s most important aspects, allowing them to get a basic understanding of complex frameworks. Summariz- ing text, such as research literature or lecture slides, is an important learning strategy",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,3,"In this way, they help the user focus on the input text’s most important aspects, allowing them to get a basic understanding of complex frameworks. Summariz- ing text, such as research literature or lecture slides, is an important learning strategy in the context of higher education (Mitsea & Drigas, 2019). On the other hand, these text generators can rephrase text input, an important task when writing research papers: In most cases, written research assignments include some theoretical chapter based on existing research literature. Students must rephrase and restructure existing research literature to show their understanding of concepts and theories (Aksnes et al., 2019). Quillbot is an example of such a rephrasing tool (Fitria, 2021).",114
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,4,"Page 4 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 3) Writing assistants: Writing assistants can enhance the quality of written text. These tools automatically check for grammar and spelling mistakes, while the text is being cre- ated. Furthermore, these tools can give recommendations to the writer to improve the language used: they can provide suggestions for alternative formulations to avoid collo- quial language and unnecessary iterations. Writing assistants are usually a part of word processors (e.g., Microsoft Word), but standalone programs or extensions such as Gram- marly also exist (Koltovskaia, 2020). 4) Text generators: These tools can automatically generate written text. Text genera- tors take short prompts as input and produce text based on this input. The output text is mainly used for blog entries, text-based social media posts, or Twitter messages. They can be differentiated from chatbots as they cannot produce more complex pieces of text. WriteSonic is a such a text generator tool (Almaraz-López et al., 2023). Mindful AI‑robots 5) Chatbot: Chatbots are applications that simulate human interactions (Chong et al., 2021).",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,4,"be differentiated from chatbots as they cannot produce more complex pieces of text. WriteSonic is a such a text generator tool (Almaraz-López et al., 2023). Mindful AI‑robots 5) Chatbot: Chatbots are applications that simulate human interactions (Chong et al., 2021). In the context of business, they are generally used to answer customer ques- tions automatically. In education, these chatbots help to guide learners through online environments or administrative processes. With the release of ChatGPT, a new kind of chatbot was introduced. These chatbots can produce various output formats, including working algorithms, presentations, or pictures, based on prompts that are very similar to human interactions (Almaraz-López et al., 2023; Fauzi et al., 2023; Fuchs, 2023). Stu- dents can use chatbots to automatically produce content, which is traditionally being used as part of instructional design, especially final assessments. 6) Virtual avatars: Virtual avatars are digital representations of living beings. They can be used in online classroom settings to represent teachers and learners alike. In these classroom settings, virtual representations, such as Synthesia, have been shown to improve students’ learning performance, compared to",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,4,"avatars are digital representations of living beings. They can be used in online classroom settings to represent teachers and learners alike. In these classroom settings, virtual representations, such as Synthesia, have been shown to improve students’ learning performance, compared to classes without virtual represen- tation (Herbert & Dołżycka, 2022). 7) Social-humanoid robots: These tools not only simulate human behavior and per- form human tasks, but in many cases, social-humanoid robots are also built close to human complexity, featuring hands, legs, and faces (van Pinxteren et al., 2019). They can perform human-like mimic to various degrees. Currently, these social-humanoid robots are used as servers in restaurants and are tested in medical and educational institutions (Henschel et al., 2021). AI‑competencies and AI‑ethics The European DigComp Framework 2.2 is a comprehensive framework, that organizes different components of digital competencies deemed essential for digitally competent citizens (Vuorikari et al., 2022). Within this framework, AI literacy can be found in three dimensions: knowledge, proficiency, and attitudes. Basic ideas about the functionality and application areas of AI technology are allocated to the knowledge dimension. This dimension",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,4,"competent citizens (Vuorikari et al., 2022). Within this framework, AI literacy can be found in three dimensions: knowledge, proficiency, and attitudes. Basic ideas about the functionality and application areas of AI technology are allocated to the knowledge dimension. This dimension also holds theoretical knowledge about AI laws and regulations, such as the European data protection regulation. The ability of a person to take advantage of AI and use it to improve various aspects of their life can be found in the proficiency dimen- sion. Successfully deploying AI technology to solve problems requires the capability to",95
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,5,"Page 5 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 choose adequate tools and consequently control these chosen tools. Competent citi- zens must be able to form an opinion on AI technology’s benefits, risks, and disadvan- tages. This allows them to participate in political and social decision-making processes. Through a meta-analysis of guidelines, Jobin et al. (2019) identifies eleven ethical prin- ciples which must be considered when working with AI, such as transparency, justice, fairness and trust. Hands-on examples are the guidelines by Diakopoulos et al. (2016) as well as Floridi et al. (2018). The attitude dimension holds these competencies. As with many technological advancements, higher education will be one of the main drivers for facilitating digital AI-competencies (Cabero-Almenara et al., 2023; Ehlers & Kellermann, 2019). Furthermore, AI technology will change the various learning processes within higher education (Kim et al., 2022). This includes the perspective of educators (Kim et al., 2022), learners (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019), and administration alike (Leoste et al., 2021). Although research indicates these impacts, research on AI-robots in higher education",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,5,"within higher education (Kim et al., 2022). This includes the perspective of educators (Kim et al., 2022), learners (Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019), and administration alike (Leoste et al., 2021). Although research indicates these impacts, research on AI-robots in higher education is scarce, mainly because higher education institutions rarely use the different applications broadly (Kim et al., 2022; Lim et al., 2023). The functionalities of the different tools offer students various potential applications for learning processes. Following the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technol- ogy (UTAUT), the intent to use new digital tools as well as the actual usage of technol- ogy might be influenced by the expectation of performance, the expectation of effort, social influence, and facilitating conditions (Venkatesh et al., 2003). Strzelecki (2023) states that the assumptions made by UTAUT also hold for AI-tools, more specifically ChatGPT, although he could not identify a significant effect from facilitating condi- tions. In accordance with the DigComp 2.2 framework, this study focuses on students’ attitudes, proficiency, and knowledge regarding AI-technology as additional constructs influencing the intent to use and actual",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,5,"although he could not identify a significant effect from facilitating condi- tions. In accordance with the DigComp 2.2 framework, this study focuses on students’ attitudes, proficiency, and knowledge regarding AI-technology as additional constructs influencing the intent to use and actual usage of AI-tools. Furthermore, the study builds on the considerations by Dang and Liu (2022) and examines which constructs influence students’ perception of AI-technology as competi- tors and cooperation for humans: Research in the field of AI uncovers a range of pos- sible outcomes from increasing AI integration into human society (Einola & Khoreva, 2023). While some argue that AI technology will compete with humans in the work- place, leading to a massive job loss (Zanzotto, 2019) and deskilling of human workers (Li et al., 2023). On the other hand, AI has the potential to be a cooperation partner for humans by automating processes (Bhargava et al., 2021; Joksimovic et al., 2023) or relieving humans from physical and psychological stress (Raisch & Krakowski, 2021). Hypotheses This research project aims to better understand first-year students’ perceptions as well as the intended",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,5,"humans by automating processes (Bhargava et al., 2021; Joksimovic et al., 2023) or relieving humans from physical and psychological stress (Raisch & Krakowski, 2021). Hypotheses This research project aims to better understand first-year students’ perceptions as well as the intended and de facto use of AI-tools. While AI-competencies are understood as an essential driver for learning success and student retention (Ng et al., 2023), the following hypotheses emerge from the research gaps identified for the context of higher education: Hypothesis 1 The underlying constructs of AI-competencies (skills, attitude, knowl- edge) have a positive effect on the intention to use AI-robots, while the intention to use AI-robots has a positive effect on the actual use of AI-robots.",116
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,6,"Page 6 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 Hypothesis 2a Students’ AI-competencies and the perceived benefits of AI-technology are predictors for students’ perception of AI-robots as cooperation partners for humans. Hypothesis 2b Students’ AI-competencies and the perceived risks of AI-technology are predictors for students’ perception of AI-robots as competition for humans. Method Data collection and participants An online questionnaire was designed to collect data from first-year students at a Ger- man and a Swiss university. Possible participants were asked to take part in the survey through an e-mail, which was send through the universities e-mail system. In total, N = 638 first-year students participated in the survey. On average, they were 20.62 years old, with a standard deviation of 2.25 years. Of the N = 638 students, N = 309 identified as male, N = 322 as female, and N = 7 as non-binary. The lowest average use of the mind- less tools could be found in paraphrasing and summarizing tools (M = 1.13, SD = 1.51). The use of online writing assistants",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,6,"N = 322 as female, and N = 7 as non-binary. The lowest average use of the mind- less tools could be found in paraphrasing and summarizing tools (M = 1.13, SD = 1.51). The use of online writing assistants was slightly higher (M = 1.94, SD = 1.76), and the highest average usage could be found in online translation tools (M = 3.53, SD = 1.18). The average use of mindless robots was relatively low (M = 2.2, SD = 1.05). The willing- ness to use the robots ranged from the lowest in virtual avatars (M = 2.23, SD = 1.13) to the highest in online translation tools (M = 3.16, SD = 1.17). Instrument The online questionnaire consists of three parts. The instrument’s first part comprises questions regarding knowledge, skills, and attitudes regarding AI-technology (Vuori- kari et al., 2022). The different AI-robots are presented in part 2 of the questionnaire. For each tool, current and intended usage was gathered, following the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT) (Venkatesh et al., 2003). The items were for-",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,6,"2022). The different AI-robots are presented in part 2 of the questionnaire. For each tool, current and intended usage was gathered, following the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT) (Venkatesh et al., 2003). The items were for- mulated to match the different tools with those tasks that are relevant for students, such as writing assignments or preparing for exams. In addition, ethical considerations for each tool were prompted (Vuorikari et al., 2022). The actual use of the robots by the participants was evaluated with a 6-point Likert scale and their potential willingness to use them with the help of a 5-point Likert scale. The third part of the instrument sum- marizes items that collect demographic data. The instrument can be found in Additional file 1. Analysis A path analysis was conducted based on the factors of AI-competence, taken from the DigiComp2.2 framework (Skills, Attitude, Knowledge), in combination with the UTAUT models’ assumption that the intention to use technology influences the actual use of AI- tools. A visualization of the model can be found in Fig. 1. The",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,6,"taken from the DigiComp2.2 framework (Skills, Attitude, Knowledge), in combination with the UTAUT models’ assumption that the intention to use technology influences the actual use of AI- tools. A visualization of the model can be found in Fig. 1. The path analysis was done with RStudio, more specifically, the package lavaan (Rosseel, 2012). Multiple linear regression analyses were conducted in RStudio to answer Hypotheses 2a and 2b.",67
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,7,"Page 7 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 Results Hypothesis 1: the influence of skills, attitude, and knowledge on the intended use of AI‑tools The model has a relative well fit, with a non-significant chi-square (3, 638) = 7.3, p = 0.06, and the fit Comparative Fit Index (CFI) = 0.96, above the respective cut-off value of 0.95. The Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI) = 0.91 is slightly lower than 0.95. The RMSEA = 0.05 is below 0.08. The results indicate a significant positive influence of attitude (ß = 0.26, p < 0.01) and a significant negative influence of skills (β = − 0.1, p = 0.02) on the intention to use the tools. Knowledge seems to have no significant impact (β = − 0.06, p = 0.19). Further- more, the intention to use the AI-tools significantly predicts their actual use (β = 0.33, p < 0.01, R2 = 0.11). The path analysis is shown in Fig. 1. Hypothesis 2a: perceived benefits as indicators for AI as cooperation partner A multiple linear regression was conducted",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,7,"AI-tools significantly predicts their actual use (β = 0.33, p < 0.01, R2 = 0.11). The path analysis is shown in Fig. 1. Hypothesis 2a: perceived benefits as indicators for AI as cooperation partner A multiple linear regression was conducted to analyze the influencing factors on stu- dents’ rating of AI as cooperation partners. Concerning students’ rating of AI as a coop- eration opportunity, the influence of AI-competence and the perceived benefits of AI were included in the analysis. Both factors are significant predictors and explain 15.41% of the variation in the estimation of AI as a cooperation possibility for humans. F(2, 635) = 57.84, p < 0.01. Both AI-competence, β = 0.22, p < 0.01, t(637) = 5.9 and perceived benefits, β = 0.27, p < 0.01, t(637) = 7.2 are significant predictors. Hypothesis 2b: perceived risks as indicators for AI as competition A multiple linear regression was conducted to analyze the influencing factors on stu- dents’ rating of AI as a competitor for humans. When considering the influence of Fig. 1 Path analysis—skills, attitudes, and knowledge as predictors",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,7,"for AI as competition A multiple linear regression was conducted to analyze the influencing factors on stu- dents’ rating of AI as a competitor for humans. When considering the influence of Fig. 1 Path analysis—skills, attitudes, and knowledge as predictors for intended and de facto use of AI-tools",48
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,8,"Page 8 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 perceived risks and AI-competence on students’ rating of AI as competition, both fac- tors explain 2.26% of the variation in the dependent factor. F(2,635) = 7.33, p < 0.01. While the AI-competence is a significant predictor, β = 0.09, t(637) = 10.2, p < 0.01, per- ceived risk is not β = 0.03, t(637) = 1.64, p = 0.1. Discussion Findings The analyzed data provides insights into the actual use and implementation of AI-tools in students’ learning process in their entry phase. So far, mindless AI-tools are favored by the participants compared to mindful tools. These mindless AI-tools provide useful functionalities regarding tasks that can be considered as typical for higher education programs, such as written papers, presentations, or reports (Flores et al., 2020; Medland, 2016). These functionalities include translations (Einola & Khoreva, 2023) or summaries (Fitria, 2021). The analysis results show that the intention to use these tools is affected by students’ perceived skills, knowledge, and attitudes (Venkatesh et al., 2003). A positive attitude",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,8,"2016). These functionalities include translations (Einola & Khoreva, 2023) or summaries (Fitria, 2021). The analysis results show that the intention to use these tools is affected by students’ perceived skills, knowledge, and attitudes (Venkatesh et al., 2003). A positive attitude has a positive effect on the intended use of AI-tools. A positive attitude includes a general interest and an openness about AI technology, but also a strong interest in a critical discussion about AI technology. Students’ curiosity about the new technology leads to factual testing and might give students a better understanding of what the AI- tools have to offer them in practice, reflecting on the challenges and opportunities of AI-technology. The findings of the path analysis indicate that proficiency in controlling the tools does not have a positive effect on the intended use. This result can be explained through the aforementioned importance of attitude towards AI-technology (Almaraz- López et al., 2023; Vuorikari et al., 2022). Students’ curiosity for the new technology might outweigh their perceived need for a distinct AI proficiency. Additionally, many AI-tools can be easily accessed and",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,8,"the aforementioned importance of attitude towards AI-technology (Almaraz- López et al., 2023; Vuorikari et al., 2022). Students’ curiosity for the new technology might outweigh their perceived need for a distinct AI proficiency. Additionally, many AI-tools can be easily accessed and give the impression of being easy to use. The same argument holds for the construct of knowledge. The student’s intention to use AI-tools for learning processes appears to be independent of their theo- retical knowledge of the systems’ internal functionalities. While this knowledge might help students to understand better the results they receive from AI-tools or increase their ability to formulate adequate prompts (Zamfirescu-Pereira et al., 2023), the absence of theoretical knowledge does not present itself as a barrier to the intended use. Implications These findings do have important implications for the further implementation of AI-tools in higher education learning processes (Heil & Ifenthaler 2023; Celik et al., 2022; Kaplan- Rakowski et al., 2023; Latham, 2022; Liu et al., 2022; Ocaña-Fernández et al., 2019). At first glance, using AI-tools does not require prior practical and theoretical training from stu- dents.",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,8,"processes (Heil & Ifenthaler 2023; Celik et al., 2022; Kaplan- Rakowski et al., 2023; Latham, 2022; Liu et al., 2022; Ocaña-Fernández et al., 2019). At first glance, using AI-tools does not require prior practical and theoretical training from stu- dents. At the same time, students might not be able to fully apprehend the possibilities of AI-tools or effectively use them to improve their learning processes (Alamri et al., 2021; Børte et al., 2023). Educators should, therefore, integrate these tools into their instructional design practices and pair them with additional practices to facilitate the students’ AI-com- petencies (Lindfors et al., 2021; Sailer et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2023). As a result, students",112
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,9,"Page 9 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 will be able to use AI-tools to improve their learning processes, while simultaneously being able to critically reflect on the input, output, and influence of the respective AI-tools. The results of Hypotheses 2a and 2b show a significant effect of AI competence and the perceived benefits of AI-tools on the expected cooperation potential of AI technology (Bhargava et al., 2021; Raisch & Krakowski, 2021). Instructional designers and other stake- holders in higher education need to provide best-practice examples of how AI-tools can be used to positively influence learning processes if they want to facilitate the usage of the respective tools. Limitations and outlook ChatGPT was not yet openly accessible when the data for this survey was collected. The overall usage of AI tools has likely increased since ChatGPT was introduced to a broader user base (Strzelecki, 2023). The presence of ChatGPT in media and scientific discussions might have led students to look into other AI-tools, such as DeepL (Einola & Khoreva, 2023) or Quillbot (Fitria,",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,9,"increased since ChatGPT was introduced to a broader user base (Strzelecki, 2023). The presence of ChatGPT in media and scientific discussions might have led students to look into other AI-tools, such as DeepL (Einola & Khoreva, 2023) or Quillbot (Fitria, 2021) as well. The composition of the student sample also limits the study’s results. While the University in Switzerland is more open towards the usage of AI technology, policymakers in German universities tend to be more restrictive towards the use of AI (von der Heyde et al., 2023). To overcome the limitation of the sample size, future studies will include students from a broader range of academic years. As a result, the gener- alizability of the result will be improved. The present discussion about ChatGPT and the influence of AI-tools in general on higher education underlines the need to educate learners about AI and their respective AI-compe- tencies (Almaraz-López et al., 2023; Chong et al., 2021; Fauzi et al., 2023). A second study is currently being conducted to analyze how the introduction of ChatGPT to the public sphere has changed",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,9,"learners about AI and their respective AI-compe- tencies (Almaraz-López et al., 2023; Chong et al., 2021; Fauzi et al., 2023). A second study is currently being conducted to analyze how the introduction of ChatGPT to the public sphere has changed students’ attitudes toward AI and their use of AI-tools, both intended and factual. It can be assumed that the powerful tool leads to an increasing awareness of AI, as well as a broad usage over different study programs and for various tasks within higher education programs. Further studies should include additional research approaches to collect additional data about students’ experiences and usage of AI tools, such as a think- a-loud study or interviews with students. These approaches give insights into the teach- ing strategies which might help students to facilitate AI competences and improve their learning outcomes through AI tools. An example of such a strategy is a class that teaches students to write scientific texts with the support of ChatGPT. A comprehensive under- standing of necessary competencies and pedagogical are the foundation for holistic AI liter- acy programs. These",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,9,"An example of such a strategy is a class that teaches students to write scientific texts with the support of ChatGPT. A comprehensive under- standing of necessary competencies and pedagogical are the foundation for holistic AI liter- acy programs. These programs need to be accessible for all students and flexible enough to adhere to different levels of prior knowledge and learning preferences. Another important task for ongoing research projects is the analysis of the relationship between AI competen- cies, pedagogical concepts and the learning outcome of students, especially regarding the different tools which might be used in the future. Additional, longitudinal studies might be best suited to gather detailed data through out AI-supported learning process. Conclusion The increasing capabilities of AI-tools offer a wide range for possible application in higher education institutions. Once the gap between the theoretical chances and appli- cable solutions is closed, multiple stakeholders, such as administrator, educators and",152
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,10,"Page 10 of 13 Delcker et al. Int J Educ Technol High Educ (2024) 21:18 students, will be able to benefit from individualized learning paths, automated feedback or data-based decision-making processes. Lately, an increasing number of research work has been published to close this gap. The introduction of ChatGPT to the general public has fueled the discussions about AI technology, especially in the field of higher educa- tion institutions. One of the challenges encased in the implementation of AI into learn- ing processes is the facilitation of students’ AI competencies. Students need the practical skills, theoretical knowledge and comprehensive attitudes to unlock the potential of AI-technology for their learning processes. Educators and higher education institutions have the responsibility to create safe learning environments which foster points of con- tact with AI as well as possibilities to actively engage with AI. These learning environ- ments must provide students with access to relevant AI-tools and must be founded on holistic legal frameworks and regulations. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at https://​doi.​org/​10.​1186/​s41239-​024-​00452-7. Additional file 1. AI-Competence Instrument. Acknowledgements Not",180
DOC_026,NEW05.pdf,10.1186/s41239-024-00452-7,10,"These learning environ- ments must provide students with access to relevant AI-tools and must be founded on holistic legal frameworks and regulations. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at https://​doi.​org/​10.​1186/​s41239-​024-​00452-7. Additional file 1. AI-Competence Instrument. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions All authors participated in planning the study, designing the data collection tools, collecting and analyzing data for the study. The first author (corresponding author) led the writing up process, with contributions from the second and third authors. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding Not applicable. Availability of data and materials The data supporting this study’s findings are available on request from the corresponding author. The data are not publicly available due to privacy or ethical restrictions. Declarations Competing interests The authors declare no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influ- ence the work reported in this paper. The authors declare no conflict of interest. Received: 22 Septemb",159
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,1,"ARTICLE International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 https://doi.org/10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2 Abstract This study investigates the role of ChatGPT, a generative artificial intelligence tool, in enhancing learning within computer science and engineering education. Conducted during the Fall 2023 semester, the work utilized ChatGPT based on the GPT-3.5 model to assess its effectiveness across three educational domains: programming, analytical problem-solving, and hardware-related system design. A structured methodology was implemented wherein students from multiple academic levels attempted tasks inde- pendently and then used ChatGPT for support. Their interactions and reflections were systematically recorded and analyzed to evaluate the model’s utility and limitations in an educational setting. Results indicated that ChatGPT was particularly effective in programming tasks, where it could generate accurate code and useful explanations. However, its performance declined in more complex areas such as artificial intelligence and computer architecture, where deeper analytical reasoning and domain-specific ex- pertise were required. The study further highlights the value of training students in prompt engineering, which significantly improved their ability to formulate targeted queries, increased metacognitive awareness, and helped calibrate their trust in the tool’s",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,1,"analytical reasoning and domain-specific ex- pertise were required. The study further highlights the value of training students in prompt engineering, which significantly improved their ability to formulate targeted queries, increased metacognitive awareness, and helped calibrate their trust in the tool’s responses. Recommendations include future integration of graphical diagram support to aid comprehension in visually complex subjects, such as system design and architec- ture. Key challenges identified include limited transparency in training data, the lack of visual support, and the need to assess whether prompt engineering skills and improved AI usage persist and transfer across courses. While newer reasoning-oriented models such as GPT-4, Claude 3, and DeepSeek may offer enhanced capabilities, these were not available during the Fall 2023 study period and are suggested for future evaluation. The study also calls for future research using these newer, more advanced large language models to reassess findings and ensure continued relevance as these tools evolve. Over- all, this research provides valuable insights into the educational application of generative AI and informs future strategies for integrating AI tools into engineering pedagogy. Keywords Generative AI",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,1,"models to reassess findings and ensure continued relevance as these tools evolve. Over- all, this research provides valuable insights into the educational application of generative AI and informs future strategies for integrating AI tools into engineering pedagogy. Keywords Generative AI · ChatGPT · Large language models · Computer science · Computer systems engineering · Artificial intelligence Received: 27 April 2024 / Accepted: 3 October 2025 / Published online: 23 October 2025 © International Artificial Intelligence in Education Society 2025 Effectiveness of Generative AI Tools in Computer Science and Engineering Education Maria Waqas1 · Shehzad Hasan1 · Anita Ali1 Extended author information available on the last page of the article 1 3",111
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,2,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 Introduction Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is an offshoot of AI learning systems that has recently gained extreme popularity in a very short period of time, opening up exciting new possibilities for individuals and businesses. One of the most prominent applica- tions of this technology is the production of new content, including essays, emails, social media captions, images, poems, computer code, and more within seconds, thus transforming how people generate content across multiple domains. The most common AI tools for such content generation include Open AI’s ChatGPT, Google Gemini, GitHub Copilot, Midjourney, and DALL-E, some of which produce textual content while others generate images (Popular AI Tools among Developers World- wide 2024, 2024) (Top 5 AI Art Generator Tools Of 2024, 2024). Overall, the hype surrounding generative AI tools prompts debate about whether it reflects genuine transformative potential or inflated expectations. As researchers and educators con- tinue to explore the possibilities, these applications are poised to play an increas- ingly significant role in shaping the future of learning and knowledge dissemination. However,",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,2,"whether it reflects genuine transformative potential or inflated expectations. As researchers and educators con- tinue to explore the possibilities, these applications are poised to play an increas- ingly significant role in shaping the future of learning and knowledge dissemination. However, for now, the content produced by these AI tools can be inaccurate at times, and for the most part, we have little way of knowing where the information has come from and how it has been processed by the application to generate content. The purpose of this study is to explore the productive use of Generative AI tools, specifically ChatGPT, in technical education across different topics in computer sci- ence and engineering. Three topics were studied and compared: programming, ana- lytical problem-solving, and system design, particularly computer hardware-related tasks. ChatGPT, developed by OpenAI, is one of the most widely used generative AI models, known for its conversational abilities, versatility, and open accessibility (Job, 2024). This study was conducted in Fall 2023, during which the publicly accessible version of ChatGPT was based on the GPT-3.5 model. Advanced reasoning mod- els such",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,2,"generative AI models, known for its conversational abilities, versatility, and open accessibility (Job, 2024). This study was conducted in Fall 2023, during which the publicly accessible version of ChatGPT was based on the GPT-3.5 model. Advanced reasoning mod- els such as GPT-4, GPT-o3, Claude 3, and DeepSeek had not yet been released or widely adopted in educational settings. As such, they were not included in this study but represent important directions for future research. The primary research question guiding this study is: How well does ChatGPT perform in solving different problems related to the computer science and engineering education, comparing program- ming courses with analytical and system design tasks, particularly those involving computer systems hardware design? Our secondary research question explores: How can students be made aware of the proper use of AI-assisted tools like ChatGPT to enhance their productivity? To answer these questions, we adopted a structured methodology involving stu- dents from different courses and levels of complexity. Students were assigned sets of problems across various topics and were first asked to solve them independently. They then used ChatGPT",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,2,"To answer these questions, we adopted a structured methodology involving stu- dents from different courses and levels of complexity. Students were assigned sets of problems across various topics and were first asked to solve them independently. They then used ChatGPT as an assistive tool, and their experiences were systemati- cally recorded through feedback. This approach enabled an evaluation of ChatGPT’s effectiveness in different educational scenarios. The key strengths of this study are the inclusion of diverse levels of class tasks (programming exercises, analytical prob- lem-solving, and system design), and a well-structured study population covering different student groups, allowing for a broader understanding of ChatGPT’s impact across various learning stages. By collecting and analyzing student feedback, this 1 3 4003",119
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,3,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 study aims to provide insights into the strengths, limitations, and potential applica- tions of ChatGPT in engineering education. The findings could also guide institu- tional policies on the responsible and effective use of AI tools in education. Related Work ChatGPT, a widely recognized LLM, has gained prominence for its ability to facili- tate personalized and interactive education, assist in generating prompts for formative assessments, and support various teaching methodologies (Baidoo-Anu & Ansah, 2023). However, despite these advantages, LLMs like ChatGPT also have limita- tions, including contextual misunderstandings, potential biases in generated content, and challenges in handling sensitive or critical information. Additionally, ChatGPT’s responses may lack nuance and may not always align with human expectations or ethical considerations (Sharma & Yadav, 2022; Kasneci et al., 2023; Adeshola & Adepoju, 2024; Božić & Poola, 2023; Meyer et al., 2023). Research suggests that both teachers and students need to develop digital literacies and critical thinking skills, including strategies for prompt design, fact-checking, and understanding the limitations of large language models, to fully leverage these",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,3,"Poola, 2023; Meyer et al., 2023). Research suggests that both teachers and students need to develop digital literacies and critical thinking skills, including strategies for prompt design, fact-checking, and understanding the limitations of large language models, to fully leverage these tools in educational set- tings (Kasneci et al., 2023). These challenges, while notable in many educational contexts, become even more pronounced when LLMs are applied to specific aca- demic fields, especially in higher education. LLMs are increasingly being adopted by students in higher education for tasks such as personalized learning, brainstorming, language acquisition, artwork creation, and managing repetitive assignments (Kikalishvili, 2023; Jumriah et al., 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Ajlouni et al., 2023). Some other researches highlight the potential of students to foster adaptive learning and personalized feedback (Rasul et al., 2023; Fuchs, 2023; Senechal et al., 2023), deeper engagement with learning materials (Fuchs, 2023), enhanced self-advocacy skills (Senechal et al., 2023), and support for collaborative research and data analysis (Rasul et al., 2023). However, overreliance on these tools may hinder critical thinking and promote passive learning (Estrada- García, 2023;",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,3,"with learning materials (Fuchs, 2023), enhanced self-advocacy skills (Senechal et al., 2023), and support for collaborative research and data analysis (Rasul et al., 2023). However, overreliance on these tools may hinder critical thinking and promote passive learning (Estrada- García, 2023; Rasul et al., 2023; Fuchs, 2023). On the other hand, teachers are also leveraging LLMs to reduce stress and workload, shorten lesson planning time, increase access to learning resources, and accelerate assessments, enabling timely feedback (Waddell, 2023; Riyadini & Triastuti, 2023; Iqbal et al., 2022). Nonethe- less, both the students and educators encounter obstacles such as the need for tech- nical competence, dependence on AI tools, lack of contextual understanding, data privacy concerns, and challenges with curriculum integration (Riyadini & Triastuti, 2023). Ethical concerns regarding unregulated LLM use, including security risks, data leakage, threats to academic integrity, unfair practices like plagiarism, dimin- ished respect for educators, and reduced interest in traditional reading materials, have also surfaced (AĞMAZ, 2023; Di Paolo et al., 2024; Martínez Pico, 2023; Abdel- ghani et al., 2023; Estrada-García, 2023; Bucşă & Fotache, 2023; Rasul et al.,",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,3,"plagiarism, dimin- ished respect for educators, and reduced interest in traditional reading materials, have also surfaced (AĞMAZ, 2023; Di Paolo et al., 2024; Martínez Pico, 2023; Abdel- ghani et al., 2023; Estrada-García, 2023; Bucşă & Fotache, 2023; Rasul et al., 2023; Fuchs, 2023; Senechal et al., 2023; Michel-Villarreal et al., 2023; Firat, 2023). As these challenges persist, it becomes clear that while LLMs hold significant potential, their adoption must be approached with caution. 1 3 4004",76
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,4,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 One of the primary concerns with the use of LLMs in education is the reliability of the information they generate. This issue is particularly pronounced in specialized fields, such as medicine and engineering, where the accuracy and completeness of the content are critical. There are several studies, mostly from the medical domain, that have tested the reliability of LLM content generation tools. However, studies in the engineering domain remain limited, and this gap is one of the focal points of our research. In medical contexts, a study on sarcoma-related questions found inconsis- tent quality, especially in treatment-related queries, highlighting the need for caution when using ChatGPT for rare diseases (Valentini et al., 2024). Another study evaluat- ing ChatGPT’s responses to 284 medical questions across 17 specialties showed high accuracy and completeness, though it emphasized the need for continued refinement (Johnson et al., 2023). Concerns about ChatGPT’s potential to fabricate scientific work are rising, as it struggles with post-2021 knowledge and accurate citation gen- eration. However, evolving detection tools and peer review",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,4,"though it emphasized the need for continued refinement (Johnson et al., 2023). Concerns about ChatGPT’s potential to fabricate scientific work are rising, as it struggles with post-2021 knowledge and accurate citation gen- eration. However, evolving detection tools and peer review can help safeguard sci- entific integrity (Shen et al., 2023a, b). A large-scale study on ChatGPT’s reliability across 5,695 questions found performance inconsistencies, particularly in law and science, and vulnerability to adversarial examples, underscoring the need for ongo- ing improvements (X. Shen et al., 2023a, b). In the context of medical information on a major surgical condition, ChatGPT-4 provided modest-quality answers, aligning with clinical guidelines 60% of the time, suggesting future potential for medical use despite current limitations (Walker et al., 2023). Lastly, ChatGPT-4’s application in reliability engineering showed promising results with complex tasks, though its accu- racy and consistency remain limited, and further tool development is necessary for effective use (Hu et al., 2024). As both students and teachers continue to advocate for the integration of LLMs in education, there is a pressing need to revise curricula and develop",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,4,"remain limited, and further tool development is necessary for effective use (Hu et al., 2024). As both students and teachers continue to advocate for the integration of LLMs in education, there is a pressing need to revise curricula and develop comprehensive policies that ensure ethical and effective implementation. In engineering disciplines, there has been some exploration of LLM applications, with Abedi et al. (2023) examining ChatGPT’s role in fluid mechanics (mechanical engineering) and Tsai et al. (2023) in chemical engineering. In computer science and related fields, Prather et al. (2023) presents a literature review of 71 studies, survey data from students and instructors across 20 countries, and educator insights on pedagogical shifts. It discusses ethical concerns using the ACM Code of Ethics and benchmarks LLMs on computing education datasets, specifically focusing on programming (basic and advanced). ChatGPT’s role in computer science and allied engineering education is explored in Banerjee et al. (2025), focusing on program- ming and computation problems. The study highlights opportunities for LLMs as an information kiosk, explanation tool, summary generator, analogy creator, revi- sion aid, practice question",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,4,"science and allied engineering education is explored in Banerjee et al. (2025), focusing on program- ming and computation problems. The study highlights opportunities for LLMs as an information kiosk, explanation tool, summary generator, analogy creator, revi- sion aid, practice question bank, lesson planning assistant, and cybersecurity event assessor. In Kosar et al. (2024), a controlled experiment with first-year computer science students shows no significant difference in academic performance between those using ChatGPT and those not, suggesting safe integration into programming education. In Dengel et al. (2023), the use of LLMs like ChatGPT and BARD for qualitative research is analyzed, demonstrating that while qualitative content analy- sis methods can be applied, response variability makes them unreliable for formal research. Finally, Backström and Kihlert (2023) investigates ChatGPT’s effect on 1 3 4005",130
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,5,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 code quality in introductory programming courses. Using a static code analyzer, the study finds that ChatGPT improves student-written code but prioritizes resolving specific coding violations over others. While much of the prior research on AI-assisted learning in computer science and engineering has focused on the use of tools like ChatGPT in basic and advanced-level programming courses (Prather et al., 2023), this study aims to broaden the scope. Since AI models are particularly adept at handling programming-related tasks, previ- ous studies often highlight their effectiveness in coding assistance, debugging, and problem-solving within software development contexts. However, our study takes a broader perspective by investigating how ChatGPT performs in non-programming tasks, specifically in analytical problem-solving and system hardware design within computer science and engineering education. By assessing ChatGPT’s responses to diverse technical challenges beyond programming, this research provides novel insights into the tool’s applicability in areas where its effectiveness remains less explored. By evaluating AI-assisted learning across courses of varying complexity, this study offers a holistic view of how AI tools can support",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,5,"beyond programming, this research provides novel insights into the tool’s applicability in areas where its effectiveness remains less explored. By evaluating AI-assisted learning across courses of varying complexity, this study offers a holistic view of how AI tools can support students in different aspects of technical education. The findings from this study could help shape strat- egies for effectively integrating AI tools into engineering curricula, ensuring their optimal use across different learning environments. Ultimately, by addressing these gaps in the literature, this research aims to provide a nuanced understanding of how ChatGPT can be leveraged as a learning aid in diverse areas of computer science and engineering, contributing to more effective AI-assisted education strategies. Methodology The primary objective of this study was to gauge the effectiveness of generative AI tools, specifically ChatGPT, in technical courses pertaining to the domain of computer science and engineering. The perspective was to see how useful the tool is in solv- ing technical problems of varying complexity in various technical courses. Another relatively candid objective was to make students aware of the correct and productive",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,5,"computer science and engineering. The perspective was to see how useful the tool is in solv- ing technical problems of varying complexity in various technical courses. Another relatively candid objective was to make students aware of the correct and productive use of generative AI tools in such courses. The study was conducted during the Fall Semester 2023 in the Department of Computer and Information Systems Engineering at NED University of Engineering and Technology, spanning 15 weeks starting from October 2023. We utilized ChatGPT version 3.5, free-to-access version of ChatGPT available through OpenAI’s web interface, to investigate its effectiveness in vari- ous computer science and engineering courses. Communication and resource sharing occurred via Google Classroom, fostering collaboration and accessibility while also highlighting the use of accessible AI technology and digital platforms to enhance education in technical fields. Study Design: Workflow The study was carefully planned around these objectives and composed of three phases as follows. 1 3 4006",157
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,6,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 Phase 1 – Preparation The aim of this phase was to encourage students to thoroughly understand the task at hand without relying on external resources, which they would run on ChatGPT later. The idea was to prioritize both comprehension of the task and the develop- ment of problem-solving skills, which are essential for effectively utilizing AI tools like ChatGPT. To accomplish this, the students were given a pre-announced closed- book assessment. The assessment was graded by the course teacher, and the assessed scripts were returned to the students along with the correct solution. Class discus- sions were also carried out wherever needed, providing valuable feedback and clarifi- cation, helping students estimate their understanding and prepare for using ChatGPT later. Three different courses were selected for this activity, spanning two academic years, first and third year. Each course focused on a specific task category aligned with its learning outcomes: one on programming, one on analytical problem-solv- ing, and one on system design. As such, students attempted only the type of prob- lems",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,6,"first and third year. Each course focused on a specific task category aligned with its learning outcomes: one on programming, one on analytical problem-solv- ing, and one on system design. As such, students attempted only the type of prob- lems relevant to their respective course, not all three types. For each course, a set of five to eight problems was formulated, corresponding to the highest cognitive level expected from that course. All students attempted their full assigned set during the pre-test phase. In the subsequent ChatGPT-based phase, students revisited the same set of problems, allowing for a direct comparison of their individual performance with and without the use of AI assistance. Phase 2 - Conduct During this phase, the students were trained on ChatGPT and then used it to solve the same problems that were given in the assessment previously during Phase 1. A three-hour lab session was conducted to explain the working of the model, the functionalities of ChatGPT, and the basics of prompt engineering. The training was tailored to the students’ level of understanding and expertise in computer",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,6,"during Phase 1. A three-hour lab session was conducted to explain the working of the model, the functionalities of ChatGPT, and the basics of prompt engineering. The training was tailored to the students’ level of understanding and expertise in computer science and engineering. To reinforce the concepts, a supplementary video covering the same content was also provided through Google Classroom for continued guidance. The training sessions covered basic terminology related to generative AI and large lan- guage models, an explanation of how these AI tools function, and their limitations. It was emphasized that these models rely on machine learning mechanisms rather than logic-based approaches and are not explainable in the same way as traditional algo- rithms. Understanding that the models generate responses based on patterns learned from large datasets—rather than through analytical or mathematical reasoning—was essential to managing expectations about their capabilities. This knowledge was meant to help students appreciate the model’s flexibility and creativity in generating responses while also being aware that it may produce varying answers to the same prompt. This awareness encourages users to critically evaluate and",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,6,"their capabilities. This knowledge was meant to help students appreciate the model’s flexibility and creativity in generating responses while also being aware that it may produce varying answers to the same prompt. This awareness encourages users to critically evaluate and refine their prompts to guide the model toward desired outcomes. After making the students aware of these facts, they were given an assignment based on the assess- ment from Phase 1 and generative AI. They were asked to solve the same problems, now using the generative AI tool ChatGPT, and compare these new answers with 1 3 4007",98
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,7,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 their own solution and with the correct solution provided by the teacher. After com- pleting both sets of solutions, their own and the AI-generated ones, students were asked to reflect on the experience by filling out a structured questionnaire designed to capture their observations, insights, and evaluations of ChatGPT’s performance. Phase 3 – Analysis The student feedback was collected and analyzed to gauge their experience on the usefulness of ChatGPT for computer science and engineering courses. Course Selection Computer science and engineering curricula in Pakistan offer a well-balanced mix of technical, mathematics, and soft skills courses, reflecting the diverse skill set needed by professionals in related fields. The inclusion of technical courses in programming, analytics, and computer system design covers key domains essential for students to develop a strong foundation in these fields. Programming courses provide hands- on experience in coding and software development, analytical courses equip stu- dents with problem-solving and critical thinking skills, and computer system design courses focus on understanding and designing complex computing systems. To meet the",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,7,"these fields. Programming courses provide hands- on experience in coding and software development, analytical courses equip stu- dents with problem-solving and critical thinking skills, and computer system design courses focus on understanding and designing complex computing systems. To meet the objectives of the study, three courses of different complexity levels were selected from each of these key domains of computer science and engineering. The main aim was to have a well-rounded selection covering a broad spectrum of knowledge areas within computer science and engineering, with each selected course serving as the foundation or representative course for many other courses of similar technical nature. The complexity level of a course was assigned based on the corresponding learning outcomes and their associated Bloom’s Taxonomy cognitive levels. The hierarchical cognitive levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy help classify learning outcomes according to their complexity into six levels. These six levels, in increasing order of cognitive intelligence, are knowledge, comprehension, application, analysis, evaluation, and synthesis. Moreover, feedback from course instructors and students over the past ten years was also taken into account. The breakdown of the",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,7,"six levels. These six levels, in increasing order of cognitive intelligence, are knowledge, comprehension, application, analysis, evaluation, and synthesis. Moreover, feedback from course instructors and students over the past ten years was also taken into account. The breakdown of the course selection is as follows. Computer Programming This is a core foundational course from the computing domain. This course covers the fundamentals and semantics of computer programming and enables students to apply basic programming language structures to solve real-world problems of appropriate complexity. The programming language that was taught to the students for this study was Python, taught as a beginner’s programming language. The learning outcomes of this course correspond to the second and third cognitive levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy, namely comprehension and application. It is offered to freshmen in the first year of a four-year degree program. As per the analysis of course instructors and student feed- back over the past ten years, this course has been graded as a low-complexity-level course in the domain of computer systems engineering. Other computing courses of 1 3 4008",177
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,7,"the analysis of course instructors and student feed- back over the past ten years, this course has been graded as a low-complexity-level course in the domain of computer systems engineering. Other computing courses of 1 3 4008",37
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,8,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 a similar nature include object-oriented programming, data structures, database man- agement systems, and other programming courses. Artificial Intelligence This is a computer engineering core, breadth-level course, which is predominantly analytical in nature. In this course, the students explore contemporary techniques of artificial intelligence and use them to analyze and solve real-world problems using artificial intelligence-based computational methods and algorithms. These techniques include computational intelligence, searching methodologies, first-order logic, evo- lutionary computing, and knowledge representation and reasoning. The learning out- comes of this course correspond to the second, third, and fourth cognitive levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy, namely comprehension, application, and analysis. It is offered to senior students in the third year of a four-year degree program. As per the analysis of the authors and student feedback over the past ten years, this course has been graded as a medium-complexity-level course in the domain of computer systems engineer- ing. Other computing courses of a similar nature include algorithm analysis, machine learning and deep learning, digital signal processing, and operating systems. Computer Architecture This",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,8,"course has been graded as a medium-complexity-level course in the domain of computer systems engineer- ing. Other computing courses of a similar nature include algorithm analysis, machine learning and deep learning, digital signal processing, and operating systems. Computer Architecture This is a computer engineering core, breadth-level system design course, with a focus on designing and building complex and efficient computer systems. It covers multi- cycle processor design, including control unit design and instruction-level parallelism, covering pipelined and multi-issue processor design, as well as topics on data-level parallelism. It enables the students to explore different computer architectural solu- tions for performance improvement of computers, design instruction pipelines with minimal hazards, and compare ways to exploit instruction- and data-level parallelism for high-performance processors. The learning outcomes of this course correspond to the third, fourth, and fifth cognitive levels of Bloom’s Taxonomy, namely application, analysis, and evaluation. It is offered to senior students in the third year of a four- year degree program. As per the analysis of the authors and student feedback over the past ten years, this course has been graded",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,8,"application, analysis, and evaluation. It is offered to senior students in the third year of a four- year degree program. As per the analysis of the authors and student feedback over the past ten years, this course has been graded as a high-complexity-level course in the domain of computer systems engineering. Other computing courses of a similar nature include microprocessor-based system design, embedded systems, and other digital system design courses. Participants To create a diverse group of students with varying levels of experience and exper- tise, the selection of student groups for this study was based on two primary crite- ria: seniority year and their enrollment in courses of different complexity levels, as explained in the following text. 1 3 4009",121
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,9,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 Seniority Year Typically, seniority is determined by the number of years a student has been enrolled in the program or the number of credits they have completed. Senior students typi- cally have more advanced knowledge and skills compared to junior students. As can be concluded from the previous subsection, the student groups selected for the study are from the first (freshman) and third (senior) years of a four-year graduate com- puter systems engineering program. A total of 290 students participated in the study, including 146 first-year and 144 third-year students. All the students are top-merit students from a high-ranking public sector engineering university in Pakistan. Taking Multiple Courses This criterion suggests that the same students are enrolling in courses of varying difficulty or complexity levels. To fulfill this, the same group of third-year students was chosen for both medium- and high-complexity level courses, namely Artificial Intelligence and Computer Architecture, respectively. This was done to assess their experience of using ChatGPT in courses of different nature and complexity. First- year students were",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,9,"of third-year students was chosen for both medium- and high-complexity level courses, namely Artificial Intelligence and Computer Architecture, respectively. This was done to assess their experience of using ChatGPT in courses of different nature and complexity. First- year students were tested for one course only, namely Computer Programming. The course and student group selection criteria have been summarized in Table 1. All participating students are top-merit students from a high-ranking public sector engineering university in Pakistan, where English is the medium of instruction. All lectures, materials, and assessments are conducted in English, and students gener- ally exhibit above-average proficiency, particularly in reading and writing. Admis- sion to the program includes a pre-admission test with a dedicated English section, and faculty observations suggest that student proficiency typically aligns with IELTS bands of 7.0 to 9.0, though spoken fluency may vary due to diverse educational backgrounds. Table 1 Summary of selected courses and student groups for the study Courses Students Title Predomi- nant Nature Design Level Blooms Taxonomy Levels Complexity Year Seniority Count Computer Programming Solving coding problems Foundation Comprehen- sion Application",180
