DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,10,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 The Problem Sets The authors crafted tasks from the respective curricula of the three courses, ensuring a diverse range of problems for students to tackle. The problem sets were designed to align with the course learning outcomes, ensuring that students’ use of ChatGPT was relevant to key concepts. Each task underwent a thorough assessment, with the authors personally testing them using ChatGPT to gauge their suitability. Some tasks were tailored to be effortlessly solvable by ChatGPT, leveraging its capabilities to offer immediate feedback and guidance to students. Conversely, other tasks were deliberately chosen to challenge ChatGPT’s limits, providing students with opportu- nities to engage in problems that require deeper understanding and human intuition to solve. To ensure pedagogical consistency, the maximum complexity of each task was aligned with the highest cognitive level expected from the respective course, as defined in its learning and assessment objectives. This balanced approach aimed to not only foster critical thinking and problem-solving skills but also empower students to navigate a spectrum of challenges, preparing them for",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,10,"level expected from the respective course, as defined in its learning and assessment objectives. This balanced approach aimed to not only foster critical thinking and problem-solving skills but also empower students to navigate a spectrum of challenges, preparing them for real-world scenarios where automated solutions may fall short. Table 2 presents course-wise test cases of selected problems as run on ChatGPT along with the correct solutions. Here, the responses from ChatGPT have been short- ened due to space limitations. The computer programming tasks assessed students’ understanding of core programming concepts, including decision-making (utilizing conditional statements like if, else, elif) and iterative structures (employing loops like for and while) in Python. Students were tasked with analyzing code snippets, predict- ing outputs, and demonstrating comprehension of these fundamental programming structures. Table 2(a) presents two scenarios: the first row depicts a case where Chat- GPT provided the correct answer without additional prompting, while the second row showcases a situation where ChatGPT failed to deliver an accurate response despite repeated prompts. In the latter case, despite being prompted about its incorrect cal- culation of",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,10,"Chat- GPT provided the correct answer without additional prompting, while the second row showcases a situation where ChatGPT failed to deliver an accurate response despite repeated prompts. In the latter case, despite being prompted about its incorrect cal- culation of string length and missing loop nesting, the tool was unable to rectify its errors and provided inaccurate responses. In artificial intelligence, students were assessed on a mix of questions based on analytical problem-solving, quantitative approaches, and multiple choices. The prob- lem set covered topics including agents and task environments, problem space and searching methodologies such as breadth-first search, depth-first search, and heuris- tic-based methods like A*, and constraint satisfaction problems such as constraint propagation and scheduling tasks while considering resource limitations or meeting deadlines. Table 2(b) presents a test case where ChatGPT was able to generate the correct answer after several prompts. Initially, ChatGPT was generating and counting the root node, for which it was being prompted, but still, it generated the wrong sum, which was then later corrected by additional prompts. In computer architecture, the students were assessed on",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,10,"several prompts. Initially, ChatGPT was generating and counting the root node, for which it was being prompted, but still, it generated the wrong sum, which was then later corrected by additional prompts. In computer architecture, the students were assessed on their understanding of RISC-V Assembly (writing small programs and encoding instructions), multicycle design (adding a new instruction and making changes in the datapath), multicycle control unit design (designing a sequencer-based RISC-V control unit and writing microinstructions for a microprogrammed control unit), and the pipelined design of a five-stage RISC-V processor (modifying the datapath to reduce hazards in the pipe- 1 3 4011",102
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,11,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 line). It was observed that, apart from RISC-V Assembly, ChatGPT mostly failed to answer other design-based questions correctly. Table 2(c) presents one such test case, where ChatGPT was asked to determine the number of dispatch ROMs and the minimum size of the largest dispatch ROM for a sequencer-based control unit. The responses from ChatGPT indicate that even after multiple prompts, it was unable to provide the correct answer. Data Analyses and Results After completing the generative AI-based assignment, students were required to record their observations and level of satisfaction regarding the course using a post- reflection feedback form. The form was structured into three sections. The first sec- tion gathered information about students’ prior familiarity with the tool. The second section collected data on the correctness of problem solutions, both those attempted by the students and those generated by ChatGPT. The third section captured stu- dents’ overall experience with ChatGPT in relation to the course. The second section directly addressed our primary research question by evaluating ChatGPT’s perfor- mance in solving",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,11,"attempted by the students and those generated by ChatGPT. The third section captured stu- dents’ overall experience with ChatGPT in relation to the course. The second section directly addressed our primary research question by evaluating ChatGPT’s perfor- mance in solving course-related problems. Meanwhile, the third section provided insights into the secondary research question by assessing students’ awareness and perception of ChatGPT as an assistive tool. This feedback form was custom-designed for this study and was not based on any previously published survey instruments. However, the structure and intent of the questions align with common approaches in similar research that explore tool familiarity, problem-solving accuracy, and user perception in educational contexts. Details of the data obtained from the feedback questionnaire are discussed in the following sections. All the plots and statistical analyses presented in this section were generated using Python 3.13. Section 1 – Familiarity with the Tool This section examined students’ prior familiarity with ChatGPT. For all the figures of this section, the bars show the percentage representation for better comparison, while the legend tables below the bars show the",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,11,"Section 1 – Familiarity with the Tool This section examined students’ prior familiarity with ChatGPT. For all the figures of this section, the bars show the percentage representation for better comparison, while the legend tables below the bars show the actual numbers. Figure 1 presents both graphical and tabular results in this regard. As shown in Fig. 1(a), among the fresh- men, 21% students were new to the tool, 36% were familiar with it, and 43% used it frequently. For the seniors, these numbers were 1%, 18%, and 81%, respectively. This indicates that 21% of freshmen had no prior interaction with generative AI, whereas 81% of seniors were frequent users. Notably, only 1% of seniors were unfa- miliar with the tool. These findings suggest that most high school students entering university did not initially rely on generative AI tools for assistance, whereas univer- sity students, having explored such tools, find them useful across various domains. However, as depicted in Fig. 1(b), the top three reasons for using ChatGPT were similar for both groups. The tool was primarily used for linguistic",180
