DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,25,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 Discussion These results highlight the varying effectiveness of ChatGPT across three different courses, namely computer programming, artificial intelligence and computer archi- tecture, within the field of computer systems engineering education. While this work does not go to the level of demonstrating learning gains, it shows that differences in domain complexity can substantially impact the quality of LLM support even on basic issues such as correctly recognizing the answer to course questions. Fig. 6 Overall experience of the students with ChatGPT (a) Opinion on usage (b) Experience of use 1 3 4026",100
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,26,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 ● Freshman Programming: ChatGPT demonstrated its best problem solving skills in the computer programming course, particularly in tasks involving programming in Python. This success may be attributed to ChatGPT’s ability to understand programming syntax and provide relevant explanations and solutions. Its profi- ciency in this domain suggests that ChatGPT can serve as a valuable resource for students learning programming languages and completing coding assignments. ● Artificial Intelligence: In the artificial intelligence course, which likely involved complex theoretical concepts and analytical problem solving tasks, ChatGPT seemed to face greater challenges. Analytical problem solving in artificial intel- ligence often requires a deep understanding of algorithms, mathematical princi- ples, and logical reasoning, areas where ChatGPT was found to be struggling due to its limitations in contextual understanding and reasoning capabilities. ● Computer Architecture: ChatGPT struggled the most in the computer architecture course, which involves designing computer systems and understanding hardware components. This difficulty may stem from the highly technical and specialized nature of computer architecture, which requires knowledge of intricate details about processor design,",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,26,"struggled the most in the computer architecture course, which involves designing computer systems and understanding hardware components. This difficulty may stem from the highly technical and specialized nature of computer architecture, which requires knowledge of intricate details about processor design, memory systems, and other hardware components that may not be well-suited to ChatGPT’s current capabilities. Overall, while ChatGPT showed promise in supporting learning and problem-solving tasks in computer programming, its performance was less impressive in courses that demand deeper analytical reasoning and specialized technical knowledge, such as artificial intelligence and computer architecture. Recent studies have demonstrated that newer Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT-4 and Claude 3 outperform earlier versions such as GPT-3.5 across various complex reasoning benchmarks, including MMLU, GSM8K, and Big-Bench Hard. For instance, GPT-4 achieved an 86.4% accuracy on MMLU, while Claude 3 Opus scored 86.8% on the same benchmark. These advancements suggest that LLMs are becoming more capable of handling intricate tasks, potentially improving AI and sys- tems design support. Moreover, tool-augmented LLMs, which integrate external tools like APIs for simulation or code testing, show promise",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,26,"the same benchmark. These advancements suggest that LLMs are becoming more capable of handling intricate tasks, potentially improving AI and sys- tems design support. Moreover, tool-augmented LLMs, which integrate external tools like APIs for simulation or code testing, show promise in enhancing performance for technical tasks. However, their application remains limited in educational contexts, indicating an area ripe for further exploration (“Are Language Models Benchmark Savants or Real-World Problem Solvers?,” 2024). Despite these advancements, a significant portion of existing research highlights applications of LLMs in the medi- cal domain, where they have demonstrated significant successes. Studies have shown that GPT-4 outperforms GPT-3.5 and other engines in various medical applications, such as answering guideline-based medical questions (Tong et al., 2024; Wickra- marathna et al., 2025; Amin et al., 2023), achieving higher accuracy rates in medi- cal exams, (Canleblebi̇ci̇ et al., 2024; Ghosn et al., 2023), clinical decision-making scenarios (Javidan et al., 2024), orthopedic case evaluations (Agharia et al., 2024) and radiology report simplification tasks. However, there is a notable absence of literature exploring the use of LLMs in analytical and design",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,26,"Ghosn et al., 2023), clinical decision-making scenarios (Javidan et al., 2024), orthopedic case evaluations (Agharia et al., 2024) and radiology report simplification tasks. However, there is a notable absence of literature exploring the use of LLMs in analytical and design problems within com- puter engineering. These problems are inherently more complex, involving higher- level reasoning and design considerations, which may present additional challenges 1 3 4027",66
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,27,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 for current LLMs. This gap underscores the need for further research to assess the applicability and effectiveness of LLMs in tackling complex engineering problems. These findings reflect the experience of students in Pakistan, where English profi- ciency and exposure to generative AI tools may vary widely. Localized educational norms and resource limitations could influence students’ expectations and their inter- action with LLMs. While similar trends have been observed in other South Asian countries, few comparative studies exist. Our work adds new insight by emphasizing the importance of both course complexity and educational context in shaping LLM effectiveness. This work also shows that with appropriate training and reflection, students tend to develop a nuanced and critical understanding of the capabilities and limitations of tools like ChatGPT. Their experiences and opinions after using ChatGPT for course assignments can be summarized as follows. ● Students feel that ChatGPT can provide assistance and insights across various problem-solving scenarios in the domain of computer science and engineering. However, even for its strongest domains (e.g., introductory programming)",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,27,"ChatGPT for course assignments can be summarized as follows. ● Students feel that ChatGPT can provide assistance and insights across various problem-solving scenarios in the domain of computer science and engineering. However, even for its strongest domains (e.g., introductory programming) it is not infallible, and verification is crucial, especially in educational settings where accuracy is paramount. ● Students would generally not recommended for technical courses if the user does not have at least moderate knowledge of the course. These findings underscore the importance of understanding the limitations and strengths of AI tools like ChatGPT when integrating them into educational settings, as well as the need for continued research and development to enhance their capabilities in diverse domains. These findings align with previous studies that explore students’ experiences with ChatGPT in academic settings. A survey of 200 students revealed generally positive attitudes toward the tool, alongside calls for improved verification mechanisms and stronger safeguards for academic integrity (Ngo, 2023). Research involving senior computer engineering students found ChatGPT to be both helpful and motivating, yet emphasized concerns about accuracy and the necessity",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,27,"attitudes toward the tool, alongside calls for improved verification mechanisms and stronger safeguards for academic integrity (Ngo, 2023). Research involving senior computer engineering students found ChatGPT to be both helpful and motivating, yet emphasized concerns about accuracy and the necessity of foundational subject knowledge, concluding that appropriate guidance is essential for effective use (Shou- fan, 2023). Similarly, a study of 102 high school and university students underscored the importance of thoughtful integration of AI tools like ChatGPT, stressing the need to preserve critical thinking and intellectual development (Valova et al., 2024). MSc computer science students at the University of Hertfordshire echoed these sentiments, advocating for clearer guidelines to support the responsible and effective use of Chat- GPT in their studies (Singh et al., 2023). Finally, a study on undergraduate students using ChatGPT as a virtual tutor in a physics course found that, despite some inaccu- racies, students largely trusted the tool, highlighting the pressing need for enhanced AI literacy and structured guidance in STEM education (Ding et al., 2023). Additionally, pre-training users in prompt engineering can significantly enhance their ability",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,27,"found that, despite some inaccu- racies, students largely trusted the tool, highlighting the pressing need for enhanced AI literacy and structured guidance in STEM education (Ding et al., 2023). Additionally, pre-training users in prompt engineering can significantly enhance their ability to use ChatGPT effectively by enabling them to craft clearer, more tar- geted prompts. Studies have demonstrated that well-structured prompts improve the performance and output quality of large language models (LLMs) across domains 1 3 4028",76
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,28,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 such as healthcare, science, and education (Korzynski et al., 2023; Marvin et al., 2023). For instance, refining prompts through frameworks like the Query Transforma- tion Module (QTM) has led to measurable improvements in conversational response quality, even in low-data scenarios—underscoring the transformative potential of prompt engineering (Park et al., 2023). Concluding Remarks While this study primarily focused on classroom-based interactions, a potential extension of this study is conducting a controlled lab-based study to complement the classroom findings. A lab study would allow for a more structured and monitored environment, enabling precise tracking of students’ interactions with ChatGPT, their prompt engineering strategies, and the accuracy of generated responses. Comparing lab-based results with the classroom study could provide deeper insights into how structured guidance and controlled conditions influence the effectiveness of genera- tive AI tools in engineering education. In addition to the implications of the study, in applying ChatGPT to the computer systems engineering domain our research group noted three broader issues. ● As students use ChatGPT and other models frequently, they can",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,28,"AI tools in engineering education. In addition to the implications of the study, in applying ChatGPT to the computer systems engineering domain our research group noted three broader issues. ● As students use ChatGPT and other models frequently, they can contribute to the tool’s learning process through their interactions. As ChatGPT learns from user data, increased usage will provide more opportunities for it to refine its un- derstanding and problem-solving capabilities. Over time, this could lead to im- provements in ChatGPT’s performance across a broader range of courses within the computer systems engineering domain. However, on the converse, a lack of transparency and open-access for data sets may still limit the quality of educa- tional LLM examples. ● Integrating graphical diagram support into ChatGPT’s responses could enhance its effectiveness as a learning tool. Visual aids can help clarify complex concepts, reinforce understanding, and make explanations more engaging and accessible to students. In courses like computer architecture, where visualizing hardware com- ponents and system designs is crucial, this addition could significantly improve ChatGPT’s ability to assist students effectively. ● Further research",180
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,28,"reinforce understanding, and make explanations more engaging and accessible to students. In courses like computer architecture, where visualizing hardware com- ponents and system designs is crucial, this addition could significantly improve ChatGPT’s ability to assist students effectively. ● Further research is needed to study the impact and persistence of training to use LLM’s effectively. While our study provided structured guidance on prompt engi- neering and critical engagement, future work could investigate how this training influences students’ initial trust in ChatGPT and whether their improved meta- cognitive strategies persist and transfer across other courses. A pre-post design could help assess changes in their reliance on and calibration of the tool’s re- sponses. It would also be valuable to evaluate whether these training effects en- dure over time and in different academic contexts. Moreover, as new and more capable versions of ChatGPT and other LLMs are continually being released, it is important to re-evaluate these findings with updated models to ensure continued relevance and applicability. 1 3 4029",166
DOC_027,NEW06.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00525-2,29,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:4002–4033 By implementing these recommendations, educators and developers can enhance LLM utility and relevance in engineering education, enabling it to better support stu- dents in mastering challenging concepts and problem-solving tasks within the com- puter systems engineering domain. Additionally, ongoing research and development efforts should continue to address LLM limitations and refine its capabilities to meet the evolving needs of learners and educators in engineering disciplines. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at ​h​t​t​p​s​:​/​/​d​o​i​.​ o​r​g​/​1​0​.​1​0​0​7​/​s​4​0​5​9​3​-​0​2​5​-​0​0​5​2​5​-​2​.​ Author Contributions All authors contributed to the study conception and design, material preparation, data collection and analysis. The first draft of the manuscript was written by Maria Waqas and all authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding The authors declare that no funds, grants, or other support were received during the preparation of this manuscript. Data Availability No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. Declarations Competing Interests The authors declare no competing",171
