DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,1,"Does generative AI help in learning programming: Students’ perceptions, reported use and relation to performance Marina Lepp * , Joosep Kaimre Institute of Computer Science, University of Tartu, Estonia Narva mnt 18, 51009, Tartu, Estonia A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: Artificial Intelligence (AI) Programming education Higher education Student perceptions Academic performance A B S T R A C T In 2022, the release of ChatGPT marked a significant advancement in the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) chatbots, particularly impacting fields like computer science and education. The ability to generate code snippets using AI chatbots has introduced new opportunities and challenges in teaching programming. However, there is limited agreement on how students integrate them into their learning processes. This study aims to explore how students utilize AI chatbots in the ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course and examine the relationship between chatbot usage and academic performance. To address this, 231 students completed a survey assessing the fre­ quency and manner of chatbot usage. Descriptive statistical methods were employed to analyze usage and perceptions, while Spearman’s correlation was",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,1,"examine the relationship between chatbot usage and academic performance. To address this, 231 students completed a survey assessing the fre­ quency and manner of chatbot usage. Descriptive statistical methods were employed to analyze usage and perceptions, while Spearman’s correlation was used to investigate the connection between chatbot usage and course performance. Results indicated that students primarily relied on AI chatbots for programming tasks. Interestingly, students’ performance negatively correlates with the reported frequency of using these tools. These findings provide valuable insights for programming educators, offering a better understanding of students’ perceptions and use of AI chatbots. This knowledge can inform strategies for integrating these tools effectively into computer science education. 1. Introduction The year 2022 marked an important moment for Artificial Intelli­ gence (AI), highlighted by the public release of ChatGPT. This occasion provoked a huge interest in AI assistants and chatbots, while also raising concerns about their potential to replace human roles and make certain jobs redundant. One domain particularly influenced by this develop­ ment is Computer Science and its education (Denny et al., 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023;",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,1,"and chatbots, while also raising concerns about their potential to replace human roles and make certain jobs redundant. One domain particularly influenced by this develop­ ment is Computer Science and its education (Denny et al., 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023; Prather et al., 2024). The use of AI in computing education has sparked widespread discussions regarding its benefits and risks (Prather et al., 2023; Smith et al., 2024). The rapid advancement of AI has boosted a rise in research into topics such as AI proficiency in university curricula (Denny et al., 2024; Finnie-Ansley et al., 2022; Finnie-Ansley et al., 2023; Richards et al., 2024), its use as a learning tool (Denny et al., 2023; Jukiewicz, 2024; Kiesler et al., 2023), and its influence on study habits (Sun et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023a). Despite this growing interest, fewer studies specifically examine how Computer Science students perceive and use AI in programming courses and how this usage affects their academic performance. Some research highlights that using AI chatbots can enhance programming self-efficacy and computational skills during the learning process",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,1,"studies specifically examine how Computer Science students perceive and use AI in programming courses and how this usage affects their academic performance. Some research highlights that using AI chatbots can enhance programming self-efficacy and computational skills during the learning process (Gardella et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023a), while other studies report no statistically significant differences between users and non-users (Sun et al., 2024). In addition, supple­ mentary AI-driven educational materials have been developed (Wu et al., 2024), with findings suggesting increased student motivation when interacting with such resources. Generally, students seem to rely on AI tools more for coding tasks and less for explanations or other purposes (Sun et al., 2024). Nevertheless, the influence of AI on student performance and skill development in object-oriented programming courses remains underexplored. A recent systematic literature review found that only four out of 125 papers have examined AI usage in object-oriented programming courses (Raihan et al., 2025). Given that object-oriented programming is considered a threshold concept in Computer Science (Boustedt et al., 2007), it is essential to focus research efforts on this",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,1,"of 125 papers have examined AI usage in object-oriented programming courses (Raihan et al., 2025). Given that object-oriented programming is considered a threshold concept in Computer Science (Boustedt et al., 2007), it is essential to focus research efforts on this area. The main aim of this study is to examine students’ perceptions, self- reported usage, and opinions about AI assistants, as well as to explore the relationship between AI chatbot usage and academic performance in the ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course. Academic performance in * Corresponding author. Institute of Computer Science, University of Tartu, Narva mnt 18, 51009 Tartu, Estonia E-mail address: marina.lepp@ut.ee (M. Lepp). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Computers in Human Behavior Reports journal homepage: www.sciencedirect.com/journal/computers-in-human-behavior-reports https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642 Received 15 January 2025; Received in revised form 6 March 2025; Accepted 7 March 2025 Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 Available online 8 March 2025 2451-9588/© 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ).",167
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"this study is measured by the points earned on major graded assess­ ments, such as programming tests and the final exam, as well as the total course points, which determine the final grade. In this context, the terms ""AI assistants,"" ""AI tools,"" ""chatbots,"" and ""AI chatbots"" are used inter­ changeably to describe AI-based models that students can communicate with. To achieve the aim, the study addresses the following research questions: 1. How frequently and in what ways do students use generative AI tools during the course, as reported by them? 2. How helpful and likeable do students perceive AI assistants to be? 3. What are the attitudes among students regarding the effect of AI chatbots on their learning process? 4. What is the relationship between the reported use and perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and students’ performance in the course and their attitudes towards AI assistants? 2. Background The perceptions, usage, and opinions of students regarding AI chat­ bots in higher education have become a prominent research topic following AI’s recent surge in public awareness. This study, however, focuses on",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"and their attitudes towards AI assistants? 2. Background The perceptions, usage, and opinions of students regarding AI chat­ bots in higher education have become a prominent research topic following AI’s recent surge in public awareness. This study, however, focuses on computer science and programming courses—areas with a developing body of research and conflicting findings, yet where AI tools can provide additional support for students. For example, it has been suggested that code-generation AI tools can aid students by providing exemplary solutions, showcasing multiple approaches to solving a problem, and demonstrating code written with high quality and style (Becker et al., 2023; Prather et al., 2023). However, this should be done with caution because code generated by AI assistants often exhibits poor style, implying that their use for improving code quality should be approached with care (Cipriano & Alves, 2024). AI assistants can annotate code and offer comments and explanations for well-functioning code, helping students understand its structure and functionality and enhancing comprehension (Becker et al., 2023; Sarsa, Denny, Hellas, & Leinonen, 2022). These models tend to comment on every line;",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"assistants can annotate code and offer comments and explanations for well-functioning code, helping students understand its structure and functionality and enhancing comprehension (Becker et al., 2023; Sarsa, Denny, Hellas, & Leinonen, 2022). These models tend to comment on every line; however, the explanations often include minor inaccuracies, especially in detailed comparisons and specific scenarios. While much of the research in the field of Computer Science has concentrated on ChatGPT (e.g. Abdulla et al., 2024; Budhiraja et al., 2024; Rahman & Watanobe, 2023; Shoufan, 2023), some studies have explored other tools, such as GitHub Copilot (e.g. Finnie-Ansley et al., 2022) and OpenAI Codex (e.g. Kazemitabaar et al., 2023; Sarsa et al., 2022). AI-powered code explanation tools have also been integrated into development environments. For instance, GPTutor uses ChatGPT to offer in-editor explanations within Visual Studio Code (Chen et al., 2023). A similar tool, acting as an AI tutor, was developed to support students learning object-oriented programming (Wang, 2023). Both tools received positive feedback, suggesting that integrating AI code ex­ plainers into integrated development environments could facilitate programming education, though their limitations",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"similar tool, acting as an AI tutor, was developed to support students learning object-oriented programming (Wang, 2023). Both tools received positive feedback, suggesting that integrating AI code ex­ plainers into integrated development environments could facilitate programming education, though their limitations and the potential for errors must be clearly communicated. Additionally, AI-powered assis­ tants like WorkedGen, which generates step-by-step solution guides with code examples and comments, have been positively evaluated by stu­ dents (Jury et al., 2024). Most students found it useful and expressed willingness to use it for tasks and homework. These findings highlight the promise of AI assistants in computer science and programming ed­ ucation, provided their quality is thoroughly assessed. Research has also examined the influence of supplementary AI tools on students’ engagement and learning. It was observed that incorpo­ rating a chatbot designed to assist students in interacting with course content boosted their intrinsic motivation to study and engage with the material (Wu et al., 2024). This heightened motivation holds the po­ tential to enhance programming education (Chang et al., 2025). How­ ever, it remains unclear whether",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"interacting with course content boosted their intrinsic motivation to study and engage with the material (Wu et al., 2024). This heightened motivation holds the po­ tential to enhance programming education (Chang et al., 2025). How­ ever, it remains unclear whether the observed increase was specifically due to the chatbot’s AI capabilities or simply the result of having additional support materials, which are known to positively impact motivation. Furthermore, a comparison was conducted between AI-generated learning materials and resources created by students (Denny et al., 2023). The findings revealed that students rated both types of materials as equally valuable for their learning. The adoption of AI tools by university students was investigated using the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology frame­ work (Strzelecki, 2023) which highlighted that students’ habits, per­ formance expectations, and hedonic motivation played a significant role in their use of AI chatbots. Essentially, when students found AI tools effective and developed habits of consulting them, their usage became more frequent. Similarly, it was found that positive experiences and accurate responses from AI chatbots encouraged consistent usage",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"in their use of AI chatbots. Essentially, when students found AI tools effective and developed habits of consulting them, their usage became more frequent. Similarly, it was found that positive experiences and accurate responses from AI chatbots encouraged consistent usage among students (Lai et al., 2023). Moreover, an increase in perceived useful­ ness, ease of use, and intention to use AI assistants after engaging with them has been observed among students (Sun et al., 2024). However, their overall attitudes toward AI tools remained unchanged, suggesting that while students may grow more accustomed to and reliant on these tools, their broader perspectives on AI’s role in education do not necessarily shift. Furthermore, this can depend on the student’s disci­ pline, as students in technology and engineering fields reported signif­ icantly greater familiarity with ChatGPT compared to their peers in social sciences, humanities, medicine, and natural sciences (St¨ohr et al., 2024). They also used AI chatbots more frequently and demonstrated the lowest level of concern regarding the ethical implications of AI usage in education. However, some students may express concerns about the",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"humanities, medicine, and natural sciences (St¨ohr et al., 2024). They also used AI chatbots more frequently and demonstrated the lowest level of concern regarding the ethical implications of AI usage in education. However, some students may express concerns about the potential misuse of AI chatbots, particularly fearing accusations of plagiarism (Espartinez, 2024; Kizilcec et al., 2024). In addition, a trend was found that the use of AI tools is perceived as more appropriate in courses focused on applying programming rather than learning to pro­ gram, while also revealing that students remain interested in learning, often expressing skepticism about AI responses and recognizing inac­ curacies or unhelpful outputs (Keuning et al., 2024). Speed and constant availability have been identified as the primary benefits of AI chatbots for students, due to their capacity to expedite problem-solving and learning (Abdulla et al., 2024; Schefer-Wenzl et al., 2025; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023b). Some students even began perceiving AI as a teacher or on-demand tutor, reflecting a high level of reliance (Budhiraja et al., 2024). Furthermore, students increasingly perceive AI assistants as essential tools",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"Schefer-Wenzl et al., 2025; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023b). Some students even began perceiving AI as a teacher or on-demand tutor, reflecting a high level of reliance (Budhiraja et al., 2024). Furthermore, students increasingly perceive AI assistants as essential tools for seeking help when facing challenges, even to the point of preferring to consult them instead of asking their teachers (Keuning et al., 2024, pp. 1–12; Prather et al., 2024). However, drawbacks included occupational anxiety and in­ stances of incorrect answers. Students predominantly utilized AI tools for coding-related tasks, such as writing and debugging code, while using them less for activities like task decomposition or improvement of theoretical understanding (Kizilcec et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2024). However, in other studies, students have reported relatively limited chatbot use for tasks such as generating code compared to its more frequent application for explaining concepts or generating ideas (Lee et al., 2024; Lyons et al., 2024). In addition, ChatGPT has been used for searching for the appropriate syntax, identifying the right libraries to import, and pinpointing runtime errors in programs (Abdulla et",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"for explaining concepts or generating ideas (Lee et al., 2024; Lyons et al., 2024). In addition, ChatGPT has been used for searching for the appropriate syntax, identifying the right libraries to import, and pinpointing runtime errors in programs (Abdulla et al., 2024). It has also been discovered that students place less emphasis on using generative AI tools as support resources in introductory pro­ gramming courses compared to more advanced courses (Keuning et al., 2024) and they tend to use ChatGPT more for solving complex problems compared to simple ones (Rahman & Watanobe, 2023). These contra­ dictory results might suggest that students do not yet have strong as­ sociations with the use of AI assistants. The impact of AI tools on students’ programming skills has been explored but research remains inconclusive regarding the effect of AI on academic performance. Some studies (Abdulla et al., 2024; Gardella et al., 2024; Kazemitabaar et al., 2023; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023a) indicate that AI assistants seem to enhance the performance of computer programming students and students using chatbots during M. Lepp and J. Kaimre",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,2,"(Abdulla et al., 2024; Gardella et al., 2024; Kazemitabaar et al., 2023; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023a) indicate that AI assistants seem to enhance the performance of computer programming students and students using chatbots during M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 2",49
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"their learning process experience greater improvements in computa­ tional thinking skills and programming self-efficacy compared to those who do not use AI. Notable areas of improvement include creativity, algorithmic thinking, and programming itself. Furthermore, in general, ChatGPT enhances academic performance, fosters positive affective-motivational states, and promotes higher-order thinking skills (Deng et al., 2024; Shahzad et al., 2024). Conversely, other research has found no statistically significant differences in learning outcomes be­ tween students who use AI and those who do not (Sun et al., 2024). Moreover, significant differences have been observed in programming self-efficacy and post-test scores, with the students who used ChatGPT scoring significantly lower on both measures, indicating that novice students who wrote their own programs developed greater program­ ming ability than novice students who used ChatGPT (Johnson et al., 2024; Prather et al., 2024). However, the effect on learning outcomes can depend on the manner of use—utilizing AI as a personal tutor to seek explanations can enhance learning outcomes while over-relying on it to generate solutions is likely to hinder the learning progress (Lehmann et al., 2024; Smith",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"learning outcomes can depend on the manner of use—utilizing AI as a personal tutor to seek explanations can enhance learning outcomes while over-relying on it to generate solutions is likely to hinder the learning progress (Lehmann et al., 2024; Smith et al., 2024). In addition, it has been found that while ChatGPT serves as a useful learning resource for general programming inquiries, its effectiveness may diminish when addressing highly specific or context-dependent questions (Abdulla et al., 2024) and using it for long interventions (Wu & Yu, 2024). In summary, while initial findings suggest that integrating AI into programming education may benefit certain aspects of students’ skills, further research is needed to comprehensively assess the effects of AI usage on educational outcomes. As there are contradictory results from previous research about how students perceive and use AI assistants in computer science and what is the impact of this on performance, it is essential to analyze in-depth university students’ perceptions of generative AI tools, their experi­ ence of using AI assistants and the relationship between chatbot usage and academic performance. 3. Methods",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"and what is the impact of this on performance, it is essential to analyze in-depth university students’ perceptions of generative AI tools, their experi­ ence of using AI assistants and the relationship between chatbot usage and academic performance. 3. Methods 3.1. Context Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a commonly used paradigm in contemporary programming. At the University of Tartu, first-year university students learn this paradigm in the course ""Object-Oriented Programming,"" which is taught using the Java programming language. ""Object-Oriented Programming"" is primarily aimed at first-year Com­ puter Science students, as it is a mandatory part of their curriculum. However, it is also a popular elective among students from other fields, resulting in an annual enrollment of 270–330 students. Serving as the second programming course in the curriculum, it requires students to have completed an introductory Python programming course before­ hand (Leping et al., 2009). Since prior experience with object-oriented programming or Java is not expected, most students begin the course as complete novices in both the language and the paradigm. The course runs for 16 weeks and includes weekly lectures, home­",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"et al., 2009). Since prior experience with object-oriented programming or Java is not expected, most students begin the course as complete novices in both the language and the paradigm. The course runs for 16 weeks and includes weekly lectures, home­ work assignments, and practice seminars, all following a flipped- classroom model (Lepp & T˜onisson, 2015). Each week, students must watch lecture videos, complete a brief quiz, work on homework related to the weekly topic, and participate in practice seminars to deepen their understanding. Most homework assignments include automated tests that offer instant feedback, enabling students to resubmit their work if necessary. Over the course, students are required to complete two major programming tests, two group projects, and a final exam (in the form of a quiz). Students are allowed to use AI chatbots for their homework and group assignments. In the materials, it is even advised to ask AI for ex­ planations and help, e.g. ""If you don’t understand the code, you can ask an artificial intelligence (ChatGPT, Copilot, or something else) to explain this logic to you."" or ""You",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"the materials, it is even advised to ask AI for ex­ planations and help, e.g. ""If you don’t understand the code, you can ask an artificial intelligence (ChatGPT, Copilot, or something else) to explain this logic to you."" or ""You can also ask an artificial intelligence for help to find the location of the mistake."" However, it is not allowed to use AI assistants during programming tests and the exam. Students are notified about this at the beginning of the course, as well as before the tests and the exam. During the first lecture, it is stressed that students should not rely too much on AI when solving homework assignments. 3.2. Participants and data collection procedures The sample consisted of students enrolled in the ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course during the spring semester of the 2023/2024 ac­ ademic year, comprising 323 students, of whom 70% were male and 30% were female. Students’ use of and opinions on AI assistants were investigated through a survey conducted via the course Moodle page. The survey took place in the 8th week, approximately at the midpoint",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"of whom 70% were male and 30% were female. Students’ use of and opinions on AI assistants were investigated through a survey conducted via the course Moodle page. The survey took place in the 8th week, approximately at the midpoint of the course. The survey consisted of six sections, with most focusing on general feedback about lectures, seminars, tests, and homework as­ signments, including a question about the perceived difficulty of the homework assignments on a 5-point Likert scale ranging from ""Too difficult"" to ""Too easy"". One of the sections was dedicated to AI assis­ tants. Participation in the survey was voluntary, but completing it awarded students a bonus point. A total of 231 students responded, representing about 72% of the course participants. Out of the re­ spondents, 157 (68%) were male and 74 (32%) were female, which reflects a typical computer science student population in terms of gender distribution (Berdousis & Kordaki, 2019; Womentech, 2023). While the survey was not anonymous—allowing bonus points to be assigned and responses to be linked with individual scores—full anonymity was maintained during the",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"typical computer science student population in terms of gender distribution (Berdousis & Kordaki, 2019; Womentech, 2023). While the survey was not anonymous—allowing bonus points to be assigned and responses to be linked with individual scores—full anonymity was maintained during the analysis of the results. The AI section of the survey included a mix of single- and multiple- choice questions, open-ended responses, and Likert scale items. It began with a single-choice question asking whether the student had used any AI assistants for the course. Depending on their answer, students were presented with different follow-up questions: • Non-users answered a multiple-choice question about their reasons for not using AI assistants, with four predefined options: ""I want to learn on my own and don’t want to use aids.""; ""I haven’t thought about using them.""; ""I’m afraid of being accused of plagiarism.""; ""I’m afraid that the topics will remain unlearned by using them."" Addi­ tionally, they had the opportunity to elaborate in an open-ended response. • AI users were asked eight questions about their experiences with AI assistants. First, the frequency of use was",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"that the topics will remain unlearned by using them."" Addi­ tionally, they had the opportunity to elaborate in an open-ended response. • AI users were asked eight questions about their experiences with AI assistants. First, the frequency of use was measured with a single- choice scale (""Once,"" ""A couple of times,"" ""Every other week,"" ""Almost every week,"" ""Every week""). Secondly, students were asked to evaluate on a 5-point Likert scale (1 - not at all … 5 - always) the level of help they received from the assistants. Next, students re­ ported specific tasks for which they used AI with five predefined options: ""Understanding code examples,"" ""Solving homework tasks,"" ""Answering lecture quizzes,"" ""Doing the group project,"" and ""Study­ ing for the test."" Students also answered a multiple-choice question about the ways of using AI assistants, with five predefined options: ""To get ideas,"" ""To get answers to theory questions,"" ""To find errors in my own code snippet,"" ""To get code solutions when programming,"" ""To explain the logic of the existing code snippets."" The section also included three open-ended questions, allowing students",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,3,"get ideas,"" ""To get answers to theory questions,"" ""To find errors in my own code snippet,"" ""To get code solutions when programming,"" ""To explain the logic of the existing code snippets."" The section also included three open-ended questions, allowing students to elaborate on what they liked and disliked about AI assistants and to describe how they integrated these tools into their learning process. To assess students’ perceptions of AI assistants, a set of five statements was included, where respondents indicated their level of agreement using a 5-point Likert scale ranging from ""Strongly disagree"" (1) to ""Strongly agree"" (5). Additionally, points for programming test 1, programming test 2, the exam and total points were obtained for the 231 students who responded to the survey. The programming tests are worth 16 points each, with a passing score of 12 points for programming test 1. The exam is worth 33 M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 3",161
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,4,"points, with a threshold of 15 points. The potential maximum score was 103 points. 3.3. Data analysis A mixed-method approach was used for the study, using qualitative data to obtain an in-depth understanding of the results of the quanti­ tative data analysis. The Python Pandas library was used for data analysis. The survey questions were analyzed using descriptive statistics. As the Shapiro-Wilk test showed a significant departure from normality for all course points (W(231) in range .56-.78 and p < .001 in all cases) and ordinal scales were used in the survey, Spearman’s rank correlation analysis was performed to examine the strength of the relationship between different scores and reported use and helpfulness. The interpretation of the r value is performed according to Akoglu (2018), where Spearman’s correlation coefficient is interpreted as a weak relationship (r < 0.29), moderate relationship (0.30 < r < 0.39), strong relationship (0.40 < r < 0.69), and very strong relationship (r > 0.70). Inductive thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2006) was used to analyze students’ responses to open-ended survey questions. This approach allowed us",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,4,"(0.30 < r < 0.39), strong relationship (0.40 < r < 0.69), and very strong relationship (r > 0.70). Inductive thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2006) was used to analyze students’ responses to open-ended survey questions. This approach allowed us to identify patterns in students’ answers without imposing predefined categories. It included multiple readings of re­ sponses to gain familiarity with the data, the assignment of initial codes to describe key aspects, and the iterative grouping of these codes into broader categories. Both authors qualitatively coded the open-ended answers, and any differences were thoroughly discussed between them until a common ground was found. The inter-rater reliability score was 84%. 4. Results 4.1. The use and non-use of AI assistants by students First, students were asked if they had used any AI-based assistants for this course at all. 47 respondents (20.3%) had never used AI assistants in this course, and 184 respondents (79.7%) had used them at least once. Non-users were presented with a follow-up question to understand their reasons for not using AI assistants. This was a multiple-choice question where",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,4,"had never used AI assistants in this course, and 184 respondents (79.7%) had used them at least once. Non-users were presented with a follow-up question to understand their reasons for not using AI assistants. This was a multiple-choice question where students could select various reasons for their lack of engagement. The responses are shown in Fig. 1. Additionally, students were given an open-ended question to provide further explanations for their choice and 22 students used this possibility. The thematic analysis (Table 1) reveals that students’ unwillingness to use AI-based assistants is driven by their preference for traditional problem-solving methods, a strong commitment to independent learning, satisfaction with existing course materials, concerns about academic integrity and future learning, and, for some, a lack of time or perceived necessity. The students who had used AI assistants were presented with a different set of follow-up questions. The first question focused on their frequency of use (Fig. 2). Interestingly, usage frequency was split almost evenly: 47% of students reported using AI assistants only once or a few times, while 53% used them every other",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,4,"of follow-up questions. The first question focused on their frequency of use (Fig. 2). Interestingly, usage frequency was split almost evenly: 47% of students reported using AI assistants only once or a few times, while 53% used them every other week or more. Notably, only 5% of the students reported using AI assistants weekly, suggesting that reliance on such tools is still relatively low. When factoring in the stu­ dents who did not use AI assistants, the percentage drops further to 3.9%. Fig. 3 provides insights into the tasks and the ways the students used AI assistants. Students could select multiple options or just one. The most common use was for solving homework tasks. As these tasks are accompanied by materials and relevant code snippets, the next popular choice was understanding code examples. This aligns with the ways of use, as the most popular uses of AI assistants were identifying errors in Fig. 1. The reasons for not using AI assistants as reported by the students (n = 47). Table 1 Themes, codes, and examples of student responses about why",180
