DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,5,"code and explaining existing code snippets. Conversely, using AI assis­ tants for lecture quizzes was the least common application. Similarly, answering theoretical questions was among the least popular uses. 86 students provided responses to an optional open-ended question about how they used AI assistants in this course and for which tasks. The analysis resulted in four main categories (Table 2). While many of their answers echoed those from earlier questions, several unique uses emerged. These included generating data for group work or testing homework solutions, breaking down tasks to better understand where to begin solving them, and translating Python code to Java. The latter example highlights an intriguing possibility: leveraging familiarity with one programming language to facilitate learning another, showcasing AI’s potential in supporting language transitions in programming. Also, the use of AI assistant as a tutor emerged. 4.2. Opinions about the helpfulness and likeability of AI assistants Fig. 4 illustrates how helpful students found AI assistants during use. Overall, the feedback was positive, with only one student rating their experience as 1 (indicating that no help was received). The",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,5,"about the helpfulness and likeability of AI assistants Fig. 4 illustrates how helpful students found AI assistants during use. Overall, the feedback was positive, with only one student rating their experience as 1 (indicating that no help was received). The most com­ mon response was 4, just below ""always helpful."" This could suggest occasional issues with AI providing unhelpful responses or students struggling to formulate their queries effectively. Nevertheless, only approximately 17% rated their experience as 1 or 2, indicating that the majority found AI assistants at least somewhat helpful. The section included an open-ended question asking students what they liked about AI assistants. 139 students responded to this question, and the results of an analysis of their answers are provided in Table 3. The most common response highlighted their speed: AI assistants pro­ vide instant replies, eliminating the waiting time associated with asking a teaching assistant and offering the convenience of being available at all times. Students also noted that asking an AI assistant was faster than googling and often resulted in more specific answers. Several students also emphasized",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,5,"time associated with asking a teaching assistant and offering the convenience of being available at all times. Students also noted that asking an AI assistant was faster than googling and often resulted in more specific answers. Several students also emphasized the usefulness of AI assistants in quickly identifying errors in their code, helping them catch small mistakes or typos more efficiently. Additionally, many appreciated the clear and straightfor­ ward explanations provided by AI assistants, with the option to request an alternative wording if the initial explanation was unclear. One stu­ dent pointed out the benefit of AI being able to provide feedback in Estonian. Students were also given an open-ended question to share their dislikes about AI assistants and there were 129 answers to this question. The two most common concerns were related to errors and mis­ understandings (Table 4). Many students noted that AI assistants sometimes made mistakes, requiring multiple follow-up prompts to correct the errors, which could end up being time-consuming. Some students found the solutions proposed by AI assistants overly complex, incorporating advanced topics not covered in",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,5,"Many students noted that AI assistants sometimes made mistakes, requiring multiple follow-up prompts to correct the errors, which could end up being time-consuming. Some students found the solutions proposed by AI assistants overly complex, incorporating advanced topics not covered in the course, which made them difficult to understand and apply. Others expressed frustration when the AI did not understand their questions or prompts, resulting in irrelevant responses. Some pointed out that when asked to comment on code snippets, the AI often altered the code unnecessarily instead of simply providing comments. A few students mentioned that the conve­ nience of using AI assistants made it too tempting to rely on them instead of exploring solutions independently. One student noted that the AI was slow, while another criticized the fact that higher-performing AI tools often required payment. One individual highlighted a social stigma around using AI assistants, feeling judged negatively by others for relying on them. Interestingly, no students mentioned concerns about plagiarism or academic integrity in this context. 4.3. Attitudes regarding the effect of AI chatbots on the learning process In",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,5,"stigma around using AI assistants, feeling judged negatively by others for relying on them. Interestingly, no students mentioned concerns about plagiarism or academic integrity in this context. 4.3. Attitudes regarding the effect of AI chatbots on the learning process In addition, there were five statements where students had to answer about the effect of AI assistants on their learning process (Fig. 5). For many students, the use of AI assistants did not result in them trying fewer solution variants for tasks or having less struggles with homework and other graded assignments. The majority of the students strongly indicated that reliance on AI assistants instead of working through the materials on their own is not an issue. This is reinforced by the fact that no students picked the ""fully agree"" option and only 3 chose the ""agree"" option for this item. The statements about asking teaching assistants less for help and motivation to solve more homework tasks had quite an even distribution of answers. 4.4. Relationships between the use and helpfulness of AI assistants and students’ performance and attitudes towards AI",180
