DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,6,"academic performance. The results show statistically significant re­ lations between frequency of use and academic performance. The use of AI assistants is moderately negatively associated with points scored in programming test 1 (r(229) = -0.315) and weakly negatively associated with all other points. A weak positive correlation was found between the frequency of usage of chatbots and the perceived difficulty of homework to students (r(229) = 0.176). No statistically significant correlations were found between perceived helpfulness and academic performance. Also, Spearman’s rank-order correlation was run to determine the relationship between the reported frequency of use and the helpfulness of AI assistants. There was a moderate positive correlation, which was statistically significant (r(182) = 0.383, p = 0.000). Table 6 shows Spearman’s rho correlation coefficients between the reported frequency of use and the perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and the attitudes of students towards the effect of AI assistants on their learning process. The results show a statistically significant relation for all attitude questions except the one with helpfulness. The frequency of use has a strong positive correlation with students’ struggling",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,6,"of students towards the effect of AI assistants on their learning process. The results show a statistically significant relation for all attitude questions except the one with helpfulness. The frequency of use has a strong positive correlation with students’ struggling less with homework (r(182) = 0.442) and motivation to solve more homework tasks (r(182) = 0.402). The same attitude questions are moderately positively associated with the perceived helpfulness of AI chatbots. Moderate positive correlations were found between frequency of use and other attitude questions. The helpfulness of AI assistants is weakly positively associated with trying fewer different solutions (r(182) = 0.247) and asking teaching assistants less for help (r(182) = 0.268). No statistically significant correlation was found between the perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and preferring to use AI before working through the materials. 5. Discussion The first research question examined the extent and manner in which students utilized artificial intelligence-assisted methods during the course. Our findings align with previous research (Rahman & Watanobe, 2023; Schefer-Wenzl et al., 2025), showing that approximately 80% of students experimented with AI assistants. However,",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,6,"examined the extent and manner in which students utilized artificial intelligence-assisted methods during the course. Our findings align with previous research (Rahman & Watanobe, 2023; Schefer-Wenzl et al., 2025), showing that approximately 80% of students experimented with AI assistants. However, the limited fre­ quency of weekly usage (3.9%) suggests that while students recognize AI’s value, they have not fully integrated it into their learning routines. This contradicts concerns that AI might quickly become indispensable and lead to dependency (Denny et al., 2024; Espartinez, 2024; Keuning et al., 2024). Instead, students use AI selectively, primarily for debug­ ging and understanding code rather than generating entire solutions, mirroring prior research (Becker et al., 2023; Kizilcec et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2024). A particularly interesting insight is that non-users primarily cited a desire to learn independently as their reason for avoiding AI tools. This aligns with findings from Yilmaz and Karaoglan Yilmaz (2023b), which identified concerns over learning integrity in programmer discipline as key reasons for AI avoidance. However, this raises a deeper question: Is AI use perceived as a shortcut that",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,6,"tools. This aligns with findings from Yilmaz and Karaoglan Yilmaz (2023b), which identified concerns over learning integrity in programmer discipline as key reasons for AI avoidance. However, this raises a deeper question: Is AI use perceived as a shortcut that undermines fundamental learning? Prior research suggests students are more comfortable using AI in courses where learning to program is not the primary objective (Keuning et al., 2024). This distinction could explain why many students hesitate to use AI even when it offers efficiency. The low concern over plagiarism, coupled with the pragmatic view of AI as a helpful but sometimes unreliable tool, suggests that students do Table 2 Themes, codes, and examples of student responses about how they used AI assistants. Themes Codes Examples of student responses Solving Coding Problems Understanding error messages and debugging ""When the code doesn’t work and I can’t find the error."" ""To understand error messages that appear when running the program."" Syntax and structure guidance ""Figuring out the correct syntax, e.g., for generating a random number."" ""I used AI to check how different Java statements",180
