DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"not view AI-assisted programming as fundamentally different from traditional internet-based resources such as Stack Overflow or online tutorials (Smith et al., 2024). This aligns with previous findings that students in technical fields exhibit lower levels of concern about the ethical implications of AI use (St¨ohr et al., 2024). However, some stu­ dents reported that traditional search methods were faster than AI, reinforcing prior findings that inconsistent AI-generated responses can hinder adoption (Lai et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023). The most common AI use cases were debugging and code compre­ hension, confirming prior research (Kizilcec et al., 2024; Smith et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2024). Interestingly, generating complete solutions was the least favored use, suggesting students may actively avoid AI when they perceive a risk to their learning, as noted in previous studies (Espartinez, 2024; Keuning et al., 2024). Beyond these expected uses, students also reported novel applications such as generating test data and translating code across languages—potentially revealing new ways AI could enhance programming education. Answering the second research question about students’ opinions about the helpfulness and likeability of AI",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"expected uses, students also reported novel applications such as generating test data and translating code across languages—potentially revealing new ways AI could enhance programming education. Answering the second research question about students’ opinions about the helpfulness and likeability of AI chatbots, the study revealed that a significant proportion of students (72%) found AI assistants helpful, with perceived usefulness increasing alongside frequency of use. This is consistent with earlier research on student engagement with AI tools, which found that direct experience with AI leads to greater acceptance and integration into learning workflows (Lyons et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2024). Students valued AI’s speed, availability, language comprehension, and ability to refine queries—key advantages also noted in prior studies (Becker et al., 2023; Sarsa et al., 2022; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023b). The most cited drawbacks were AI’s errors, misinterpretations, and unnecessary code modifications—issues consistently reported in previous studies (Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023b). Some students also expressed occupational anxiety, fearing AI might limit their exploration of alternative solutions, a concern previously noted in AI-assisted learning (Sarsa et al., 2022). One of",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"code modifications—issues consistently reported in previous studies (Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023b). Some students also expressed occupational anxiety, fearing AI might limit their exploration of alternative solutions, a concern previously noted in AI-assisted learning (Sarsa et al., 2022). One of the more unexpected findings was the divergence of opinion on AI efficiency. While some students felt AI responses were faster than traditional search methods, others preferred conventional web searches, citing better reliability. This discrepancy suggests that past negative experiences with AI-generated errors may lead students to revert to traditional resources, highlighting a crucial area for AI tool improve­ ment. Earlier studies (Lai et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2023) similarly noted that student trust in AI diminishes when responses are inaccurate or misleading. Developers of AI-based educational tools may need to focus on enhancing response accuracy and reliability to encourage greater adoption. The third research question explored the attitudes among students regarding the effect of AI chatbots on their learning process as there is a critical concern in AI-assisted learning whether reliance on AI tools limits students’ problem-solving abilities. Contrary to some",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"The third research question explored the attitudes among students regarding the effect of AI chatbots on their learning process as there is a critical concern in AI-assisted learning whether reliance on AI tools limits students’ problem-solving abilities. Contrary to some fears, the majority of students did not feel that AI reduced their exploration of multiple solutions, though some reported a decrease in independent problem-solving efforts. This mixed finding suggests that while AI can facilitate learning, it may also create a cognitive shortcut that reduces engagement with complex problem-solving (Denny et al., 2024; Espar­ tinez, 2024). Educators should, therefore, consider structured AI usage guidelines that encourage students to critically assess AI-generated Table 3 Themes, codes, and examples of student responses about what they like about AI assistants. Themes Codes Examples of student responses Speed and Accessibility Instant responses ""It responds immediately."" ""It gives me an answer right away; I don’t have to spend time searching for material."" 24/7 availability ""You can get an answer at any time of the day."" ""It’s always available if I quickly need potential help."" Alternative to",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"""It gives me an answer right away; I don’t have to spend time searching for material."" 24/7 availability ""You can get an answer at any time of the day."" ""It’s always available if I quickly need potential help."" Alternative to Traditional Search Methods Faster alternative to Google ""It’s like a faster and more precise Google."" ""More effective than simply typing the problem into Google."" No advertisements or unreliable sources ""I like that there are no ads or pop- ups asking about cookie usage, and the response quality is quite stable."" ""Compared to forums, ChatGPT is much calmer and easier to use, and responses are generally more relevant – in forums, there are often answers from 2005."" Assistance in Debugging and Error Detection Identifying coding errors ""If I get stuck finding a bug in my code, AI quickly helps pinpoint the issue."" ""The fact that AI always finds where the errors in my code are, so I don’t waste time searching for small mistakes."" Understanding error messages ""When an error message isn’t helpful, AI provides additional clarification."" Clarity and Simplicity in",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"issue."" ""The fact that AI always finds where the errors in my code are, so I don’t waste time searching for small mistakes."" Understanding error messages ""When an error message isn’t helpful, AI provides additional clarification."" Clarity and Simplicity in Explanations Simplified explanations ""It explains like you would to a small child."" ""It can explain things very simply so that you can understand better. You can also ask additional questions."" Personalized and context-aware responses ""AI remembers the context within a conversation, so I can refer back to previous messages and get follow-up clarification."" ""It’s not possible to get such personal answers when searching on Google."" Visual and step-by- step explanations ""It explains step by step why and how the code works."" ""It provides visual examples along with theoretical explanations."" Learning and Problem-Solving Companion Private tutor ""It’s like a private teacher who answers my questions immediately."" ""I use AI to check my ideas and ensure I’m on the right track."" Asking ""dumb"" questions without judgment ""I can ask stupid questions without feeling embarrassed."" ""I don’t have to bother my instructor with",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,7,"who answers my questions immediately."" ""I use AI to check my ideas and ensure I’m on the right track."" Asking ""dumb"" questions without judgment ""I can ask stupid questions without feeling embarrassed."" ""I don’t have to bother my instructor with every little question."" Interactive and iterative learning ""I can repeatedly ask for clarifications with examples."" ""If AI doesn’t understand my question, I have to rethink my approach, which helps me learn better."" Alternative approaches to problem-solving ""Sometimes it suggests solutions I wouldn’t have thought of myself, which helps me improve my coding."" ""AI helps me gain a new perspective when I’ve been stuck on the same problem for a long time."" Table 3 (continued) Themes Codes Examples of student responses Encouraging critical thinking ""If AI doesn’t understand my question, I need to reformulate it, which makes me analyze my own understanding."" ""It doesn’t just give answers, but helps me structure my thinking."" M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 7",166
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"solutions rather than passively accepting them (Becker et al., 2023; Sarsa et al., 2022). The impact of AI on student-instructor interactions presents another nuanced finding. While previous studies suggested that AI tools could replace traditional help-seeking behavior (Keuning et al., 2024; Prather et al., 2024), our results indicate a more complex dynamic. Some stu­ dents found AI useful for initial troubleshooting, potentially reducing the demand for teaching assistants. However, a notable proportion of students still sought human guidance, reinforcing the idea that AI should complement, rather than replace, traditional instructional support. This suggests that AI integration should be designed to enhance, rather than undermine, student-instructor engagement, a concern also noted by Prather et al. (2023). Regarding motivation, most students remained neutral about whether AI assistants encouraged them to solve more homework, diverging from previous findings where course-specific chatbots enhanced motivation (Wu et al., 2024). This discrepancy may stem from the lack of a tailored AI assistant for this course, highlighting that while AI can enhance engagement in structured settings, general-purpose AI tools may not significantly boost motivation across all students.",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"(Wu et al., 2024). This discrepancy may stem from the lack of a tailored AI assistant for this course, highlighting that while AI can enhance engagement in structured settings, general-purpose AI tools may not significantly boost motivation across all students. How­ ever, those who agreed with the statement suggest that AI can still serve as a motivational aid for certain learners. The last research question is connected to the relationships between the reported use and perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and students’ performance in the course and attitudes towards AI assistants. One of the most significant findings of this study is the negative correlation be­ tween AI usage frequency and academic performance. Unlike previous studies that found either a neutral or positive effect (Abdulla et al., 2024; Deng et al., 2024; Kazemitabaar et al., 2023; Sun et al., 2024), our re­ sults suggest that frequent AI users tended to struggle more with cour­ sework. While one plausible explanation is that students who perceive the course material as difficult rely more on AI as a supplementary aid—aligning with prior findings that",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"re­ sults suggest that frequent AI users tended to struggle more with cour­ sework. While one plausible explanation is that students who perceive the course material as difficult rely more on AI as a supplementary aid—aligning with prior findings that lower-performing students tend to use additional resources more frequently (Lepp & Kaimre, 2022)—other factors could also contribute to this trend. For instance, over-reliance on AI for problem-solving, regardless of a student’s ability level, may hinder the development of fundamental programming skills, which was also suggested by prior research (Lehmann et al., 2024; Smith et al., 2024). This raises the question of whether students in our study were using AI as a supplement to their learning or as a primary tool for solving problems. Future research should differentiate between these usage patterns, as the impact of AI may vary based on how it is integrated into the learning process. Additionally, structured AI usage—such as guided AI-assisted exercises—could mitigate this performance gap. Wu et al. (2024) found that AI-enhanced coursework improved motivation and engagement when AI was embedded within course design. Rather",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"how it is integrated into the learning process. Additionally, structured AI usage—such as guided AI-assisted exercises—could mitigate this performance gap. Wu et al. (2024) found that AI-enhanced coursework improved motivation and engagement when AI was embedded within course design. Rather than students relying on AI independently, they may benefit more from structured integration of AI tools into the curriculum, ensuring that AI serves as a support mechanism rather than a substitute for problem-solving and conceptual understanding. Further investigation is needed to determine whether instructional scaffolding can help students develop programming competence while leveraging AI effectively. Beyond performance, AI usage was strongly linked to student atti­ tudes toward learning. Frequent users reported less struggle with homework and increased motivation, supporting previous research on AI’s role in enhancing engagement (Chang et al., 2025; Espartinez, 2024; Kasneci et al., 2023). However, a concerning trend emerged: the more students used AI, the more they bypassed alternative solutions and course materials. Notably, this behavior was unrelated to perceived AI helpfulness, suggesting that some students turned to AI assistants even when they did not find them",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"trend emerged: the more students used AI, the more they bypassed alternative solutions and course materials. Notably, this behavior was unrelated to perceived AI helpfulness, suggesting that some students turned to AI assistants even when they did not find them particularly useful. This raises critical questions about the role of AI in shaping study habits—whether it serves as a beneficial support tool or fosters over-reliance remains an open issue for further investigation. 6. Implications The findings of this study highlight several key implications for ed­ ucators and course designers in Computer Science and related disci­ plines. First, AI-based chatbots can serve as valuable supplementary tools for coding-related tasks, such as debugging and understanding code examples. Students appreciate the tools’ speed and accessibility, making them useful for independent study. However, their role should Table 4 Themes, codes, and examples of student responses about what they do not like about AI assistants. Themes Codes Examples of student responses Inaccuracy and Unreliability of Responses Incorrect or hallucinated answers ""If AI doesn’t know the answer, instead of admitting it, it just makes something up.""",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"responses about what they do not like about AI assistants. Themes Codes Examples of student responses Inaccuracy and Unreliability of Responses Incorrect or hallucinated answers ""If AI doesn’t know the answer, instead of admitting it, it just makes something up."" ""Quite often it generates code with logical errors or the code that simply does not compile."" Need for critical evaluation ""You have to be very critical when reviewing AI’s answers."" ""AI-generated answers need additional fact-checking, which can sometimes take more time than solving the issue myself."" Overcomplicated or irrelevant solutions ""AI sometimes suggests solutions that are unnecessarily complicated."" ""It often uses functions or libraries that we haven’t even learned yet."" ""When I ask for help fixing my mistake, it rewrites the entire code instead of just giving me hints."" Limited Understanding of User Input Difficulty interpreting questions ""Often, AI doesn’t understand exactly what I want."" ""What I don’t like about AI is that you have to formulate every question in great detail. Otherwise, you’ll get an answer from a completely different field."" Lack of context awareness ""Sometimes it forgets what",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"understand exactly what I want."" ""What I don’t like about AI is that you have to formulate every question in great detail. Otherwise, you’ll get an answer from a completely different field."" Lack of context awareness ""Sometimes it forgets what we were talking about and starts from scratch."" ""It randomly changes variable names in the code, making it harder to follow."" Impact on Learning and Problem- Solving Reduced critical thinking and effort ""The more I use AI, the less I think by myself."" ""There is always a risk of becoming comfortable with artificial intelligence and thereby slowing down your personal development."" Overdependence on AI ""I want to be able to solve problems on my own, so I avoid using AI too much."" ""If I keep using AI for everything, will I even be able to solve things without it in the future?"" Technical Limitations Slow or inconsistent performance ""Sometimes it doesn’t respond at all or just stalls."" ""It’s slow, and sometimes the answer doesn’t even load properly."" ""AI occasionally gets stuck in a loop and keeps repeating the same thing.""",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,8,"the future?"" Technical Limitations Slow or inconsistent performance ""Sometimes it doesn’t respond at all or just stalls."" ""It’s slow, and sometimes the answer doesn’t even load properly."" ""AI occasionally gets stuck in a loop and keeps repeating the same thing."" AI models with better accuracy require payment ""The best AI models cost money."" ""The free versions are too limited, and the good ones are expensive."" M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 8",79
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,9,"remain supportive rather than central to the learning process. To achieve this, educators can provide structured guidelines for using AI tools effectively, ensuring students approach them as assistants for learning rather than shortcuts that suppress critical problem-solving skills. This approach can help foster deeper learning and reduce over- reliance on AI technologies. A key contribution of this study is its focus on empirical evidence regarding the impact of AI chatbots in an ""Object-Oriented Program­ ming"" course. While prior research has explored AI in education, studies examining AI chatbot usage in the context of object-oriented program­ ming coursework remain limited. Our findings provide context-specific insights into the patterns of AI assistant usage, their perceived benefits and drawbacks, and their potential influence on student performance. The study also underscores the importance of targeted support for weaker students who are more inclined to rely on AI assistants and other supplementary materials. Prior research has suggested that struggling students often turn to external aids, but this study explicitly links AI chatbot ""dependency"" with lower academic performance, raising con­ cerns about the unstructured use of",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,9,"rely on AI assistants and other supplementary materials. Prior research has suggested that struggling students often turn to external aids, but this study explicitly links AI chatbot ""dependency"" with lower academic performance, raising con­ cerns about the unstructured use of AI tools. Educators should recognize this trend and implement additional interventions, such as offering guidance on how to critically evaluate AI-generated solutions or inte­ grating AI tools into classroom instruction in a way that enhances learning. Furthermore, this study highlights potential risks associated with overuse as the observed negative correlation between frequent AI usage and academic performance may still suggest that unstructured or excessive reliance on AI tools can inhibit learning outcomes. To address this, educators can implement practices that balance AI usage with traditional problem-solving strategies, such as offering tasks that encourage students to compare AI-generated solutions with their own work. Finally, concerns about AI limiting the exploration of alternative solutions highlight the need for educators to encourage creative problem-solving by designing tasks that require students to critically engage with AI responses and generate multiple approaches to a prob­",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,9,"own work. Finally, concerns about AI limiting the exploration of alternative solutions highlight the need for educators to encourage creative problem-solving by designing tasks that require students to critically engage with AI responses and generate multiple approaches to a prob­ lem. Additionally, improving AI tool reliability or training students to identify and manage AI errors could help address frustrations and improve the overall effectiveness of these tools. Fig. 5. Students’ responses about the effect of AI assistants (n = 184). Table 5 Correlation between reported frequency of usage and perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and academic performance. Frequency of use (n = 231) Helpfulness (n = 184) Spearman’s rho p-value Spearman’s rho p- value Test 1 points −0.315 0.000 −0.074 0.316 Test 2 points −0.227 <0.001 −0.049 0.509 Exam points −0.175 <0.01 −0.005 0.943 Total points −0.208 <0.01 −0.051 0.487 Perceived difficulty of homework 0.176 <0.01 −0.055 0.460 Table 6 Correlation between reported frequency of usage and perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and attitudes towards the effect of AI assistants on the learning process. Frequency of use (n = 184)",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,9,Perceived difficulty of homework 0.176 <0.01 −0.055 0.460 Table 6 Correlation between reported frequency of usage and perceived helpfulness of AI assistants and attitudes towards the effect of AI assistants on the learning process. Frequency of use (n = 184) Helpfulness (n = 184) Spearman’s rho p-value Spearman’s rho p-value I have tried fewer different solutions due to AI assistants. 0.384 0.000 0.247 <0.001 I struggled less with homework and other graded assignments due to AI assistants. 0.442 0.000 0.354 0.000 I didn’t go through the materials myself first but used an AI assistant. 0.321 <0.001 0.125 0.091 I asked teaching assistants less for help due to AI assistants. 0.338 0.000 0.268 <0.001 The availability of AI assistants motivated me to solve more homework tasks. 0.402 0.000 0.384 0.000 M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 9,143
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"7. Limitations and future research A key limitation of this study is its focus on a single course, a single university and one academic year, due to the relatively recent emer­ gence of AI chatbots. The policies and rules set by the university may have influenced students’ perceptions and behaviors, limiting the generalizability of the findings. Furthermore, the study was conducted in the context of an ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course, where problem-solving and coding tasks play a central role. The extent to which these findings apply to other computer science courses, such as theoretical computer science or human-computer interaction, remains uncertain. Additionally, while the study does not specify a particular AI chatbot, the timeframe suggests that most students likely used ChatGPT. Differences in AI assistant capabilities, such as variations in model up­ dates, interface design, or institution-specific AI tools, could further impact the generalizability of the results. Expanding future research to include multiple courses, universities and academic years would provide a broader perspective. In addition, future research could explore how to better integrate AI into learning environments and assess its long-term",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"impact the generalizability of the results. Expanding future research to include multiple courses, universities and academic years would provide a broader perspective. In addition, future research could explore how to better integrate AI into learning environments and assess its long-term impact on student performance and problem-solving abilities. Secondly, the sample of students who responded to the survey could have been biased towards those who were more motivated to participate due to the incentive offered—a bonus point for completing the ques­ tionnaire—which required students to supply their names. Despite as­ surances of confidentiality, some students might have been hesitant to disclose using AI assistants for unapproved purposes in the course, possibly due to concerns about potential negative consequences. This social desirability bias could lead to underreporting of AI use in certain contexts or misrepresentation of how students actually engaged with the technology. As a result, the findings may not fully reflect the broader student population. Future research could mitigate this limitation by allowing truly anonymous participation to gain a more comprehensive understanding of student perceptions and usage of AI tools. Thirdly,",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"technology. As a result, the findings may not fully reflect the broader student population. Future research could mitigate this limitation by allowing truly anonymous participation to gain a more comprehensive understanding of student perceptions and usage of AI tools. Thirdly, students’ self-reports may not always accurately reflect their actual behavior. This may influence the results in various ways; for instance, these respondents might overestimate or underestimate their perceived usefulness and impact. Future research could address this limitation by combining self-reported data with alternative methods, such as analyzing usage logs or conducting qualitative interviews, to gain a more accurate and nuanced understanding of AI usage in educational settings. While this paper primarily examined students’ use of AI tools, it is important to note that AI tools are also accessible to instructors. Exploring how teaching and grading automation can be integrated with AI assistants offers another promising direction for research that could yield valuable findings. 8. Conclusion The aim of this paper was to explore how students utilize AI-based chatbots in the ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course and to examine the relationship between their",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"AI assistants offers another promising direction for research that could yield valuable findings. 8. Conclusion The aim of this paper was to explore how students utilize AI-based chatbots in the ""Object-Oriented Programming"" course and to examine the relationship between their usage and academic performance. Data was collected through a questionnaire, focusing on frequency of use, methods of use, and the perceived impact on learning. The findings revealed that the majority of students used AI assistants, primarily for coding-related tasks such as debugging and understanding code exam­ ples. However, only a small fraction reported using AI assistants on a weekly basis, suggesting that students have not yet developed a de­ pendency on these tools. Students appreciated the AI assistants’ speed and availability but expressed frustration with errors and mis­ interpretations in the responses provided by the tools. Although some students raised concerns that AI chatbots might limit their exploration of alternative solutions, the results indicated that overall, AI tools did not reduce the variety of solutions students attempted and instead provided helpful support for studying course materials. Furthermore, students did not",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"concerns that AI chatbots might limit their exploration of alternative solutions, the results indicated that overall, AI tools did not reduce the variety of solutions students attempted and instead provided helpful support for studying course materials. Furthermore, students did not feel that using AI tools prevented them from learning the material or exploring different approaches to problem-solving. These insights contribute to the ongoing discussion on the pedagogical integration of AI tools, highlighting the need for educators to help students critically assess AI-generated content and engage with it actively rather than relying on it uncritically. A key finding of this study is that frequent AI assistant use was negatively correlated with academic performance. While this could be explained by weaker students relying more heavily on AI tools, it also raises important pedagogical questions about whether unstructured AI use supports or hinders learning. While prior research has provided contradictory results on the use of AI in education, this study contributes by providing quantitative evidence that frequent AI use does not necessarily translate into better academic outcomes in programming courses. The results of",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"learning. While prior research has provided contradictory results on the use of AI in education, this study contributes by providing quantitative evidence that frequent AI use does not necessarily translate into better academic outcomes in programming courses. The results of our study give new insights into how students perceive and use AI chatbots. These insights can be useful for other instructors of Computer Science courses, offering a better understanding of the re­ lations between students and AI-based tools. The findings of the study can enhance learning for students using AI and benefit education by aiding other educators and researchers in adapting AI assistants for teaching and learning. This knowledge can help inform changes to course design and educational practices. CRediT authorship contribution statement Marina Lepp: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Resources, Methodology, Inves­ tigation, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Data curation, Concep­ tualization. Joosep Kaimre: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Ethics statement Detailed information about the study was provided to participants before",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"tigation, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Data curation, Concep­ tualization. Joosep Kaimre: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Ethics statement Detailed information about the study was provided to participants before beginning the survey. Informed consent was obtained from all participants, and their privacy rights were strictly observed. The par­ ticipants were protected by hiding their personal information during the research process and restricting information that could identify individuals. Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the writing process During the preparation of this work, the authors used Grammarly and ChatGPT in order to improve readability and language in certain parts of the text. After using these tools/services, the authors reviewed and edited the content as needed and they take full responsibility for the content of the publication. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgements This work was supported by the Estonian Research Council grant ""Developing",180
DOC_028,NEW08.pdf,10.1016/j.chbr.2025.100642,10,"competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgements This work was supported by the Estonian Research Council grant ""Developing human-centric digital solutions"" (TEM-TA120). Data availability The authors do not have permission to share data. M. Lepp and J. Kaimre Computers in Human Behavior Reports 18 (2025) 100642 10",69
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,1,"Regular Article Exploring AI integration within an eclectic pedagogy to enhance university students’ genre writing skills across writing aspects Roderick Julian Robillos * Faculty of Education, Khon Kaen University, Khon Kaen, Thailand A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: AI integration Eclectic writing pedagogy Genre writing skills GPT-Based chatbots Writing aspects A B S T R A C T This study explores the effect of a GPT-based chatbot integrated within an eclectic writing pedagogy – combining process and genre approaches – on university students’ genre-specific writing skills across narrative, descriptive, process, and argumentative genres. It also examines improvements in key writing aspects: task response, coherence-cohesion, grammatical and structural accuracy, lexical resource, and logical order. Using a sequential mixed-method design with pre- and post-tests, 30 third-year university students participated in 24 sessions involving the chatbot-enhanced pedagogy. Research instruments included pre- and post-writing genre tasks, observation field notes, and semi-structured interviews. Quantitative results showed significant improvements across writing aspects and genres. Task response increased notably in descriptive (M = 2.05 to M = 3.35) and narrative writing",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,1,"Research instruments included pre- and post-writing genre tasks, observation field notes, and semi-structured interviews. Quantitative results showed significant improvements across writing aspects and genres. Task response increased notably in descriptive (M = 2.05 to M = 3.35) and narrative writing (M = 2.10 to M = 3.28). Coherence-cohesion gains were strong in process writing (M = 2.40 to M = 3.65, p < .001). Grammatical accuracy improved in process (M = 1.75 to M = 2.70, p < .001) and argu­ mentative genres (M = 1.60 to M = 2.30, p = .002). Lexical resources fluctuated but recovered by the final descriptive writing task (M = 3.10). Logical order steadily improved in narrative and process writing. Argu­ mentative writing showed modest gains in task response and grammar but a slight decline in coherence-cohesion. Qualitative data revealed positive participant responses and enhanced genre writing skills. However, occasional off-topic chatbot responses and weak genre awareness necessitated student critical evaluation and teacher mediation. Despite promising outcomes, the single-group design, small sample size, and cultural context limit generalizability. This study contributes to chatbot-assisted writing",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,1,"enhanced genre writing skills. However, occasional off-topic chatbot responses and weak genre awareness necessitated student critical evaluation and teacher mediation. Despite promising outcomes, the single-group design, small sample size, and cultural context limit generalizability. This study contributes to chatbot-assisted writing pedagogy, suggesting AI tools can support genre-specific writing when carefully integrated. 1. Introduction Effective communication, academic success, and professional achievement depend heavily on proficient writing skills (Flusser & Alunni, 2023). Writing not only conveys information but also shapes narratives, expresses thoughts, and articulates complex ideas. In higher education, students must not only communicate their understanding clearly but also engage in critical analysis and present their ideas persuasively. Similarly, in professional contexts, strong writing is essential for drafting correspondence, reports, proposals, and pre­ sentations, ensuring clear communication with colleagues, clients, and stakeholders (Robillos, 2023). Writing proficiency is critical across both academic and professional settings, highlighting the need for effective instructional strategies (Robillos & Bustos, 2023). Two key pedagogical approaches commonly used to guide writing instruction are the process-oriented approach and the genre-based approach. The process-oriented approach emphasizes the stages of writing",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,1,"and professional settings, highlighting the need for effective instructional strategies (Robillos & Bustos, 2023). Two key pedagogical approaches commonly used to guide writing instruction are the process-oriented approach and the genre-based approach. The process-oriented approach emphasizes the stages of writing (pre-writing, drafting, revising), encouraging stu­ dent engagement and continuous improvement in clarity and coherence (Kitajroonchai et al., 2022). Conversely, the genre-based approach fo­ cuses on helping students understand the various writing genres, allowing them to adapt their style to meet the needs of different audi­ ences and contexts (Purba et al., 2020). While these approaches have distinct foci, scholars such as Miller (1984) and Bazerman (2013) highlight the complementary nature of these strategies. When inte­ grated, they provide students with a versatile set of tools for effective communication, which is crucial in today’s complex, dynamic commu­ nication landscape (Rahimi & Zhang, 2022). The process-oriented approach to writing instruction is grounded in sociocognitive theory, which emphasizes learning as a socially mediated process involving scaffolding and interaction. Vygotsky’s (1978) concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) highlights how * Corresponding author.",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,1,"Zhang, 2022). The process-oriented approach to writing instruction is grounded in sociocognitive theory, which emphasizes learning as a socially mediated process involving scaffolding and interaction. Vygotsky’s (1978) concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) highlights how * Corresponding author. E-mail address: rodero@kku.ac.th. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Social Sciences & Humanities Open journal homepage: www.sciencedirect.com/journal/social-sciences-and-humanities-open https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886 Received 12 April 2025; Received in revised form 19 July 2025; Accepted 7 August 2025 Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 Available online 14 August 2025 2590-2911/© 2025 The Author. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by- nc-nd/4.0/ ).",108
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"learners benefit from guided assistance during tasks such as planning, drafting, and revising (Pacello, 2019). This approach relies heavily on teacher and peer feedback to develop students’ cognitive and meta­ cognitive writing strategies (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). In parallel, the genre-based approach draws on constructivist perspectives, asserting that learners actively construct knowledge by understanding the social context and communicative purposes of different text types (Purba et al., 2020; Rahimi & Zhang, 2022). Genre theory foregrounds the importance of teaching writing through familiarization with text structures and conventions, supporting learners in adapting their writing to diverse audiences (Purba et al., 2020). Together, these theo­ retical frameworks emphasize interaction, feedback, and contextual awareness as crucial components of writing development. Despite years of English instruction, many Thai university students continue to struggle with academic writing, particularly when it comes to adapting their writing for various academic disciplines. Thai students, in particular, face challenges such as limited exposure to diverse writing genres (Robillos & Bustos, 2023), an overemphasis on grammar drills, and outdated teaching methods (Wuttiphan, 2023) that focus more on rote memorization than",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"for various academic disciplines. Thai students, in particular, face challenges such as limited exposure to diverse writing genres (Robillos & Bustos, 2023), an overemphasis on grammar drills, and outdated teaching methods (Wuttiphan, 2023) that focus more on rote memorization than on critical thinking (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). These limitations hinder students’ ability to develop strong writing and critical thinking skills (Janenoppakarn & Thongrin, 2020). Furthermore, the lack of integration with digital tools reduces opportunities for students to engage in interactive, technology-enhanced writing activities, leaving them ill-prepared for the demands of writing in the digital age (Okonkwo & Ade-Ibijola, 2021; Su et al., 2023). Despite increasing interest in using AI tools in education, there is a noticeable lack of empirical studies that investigate how GPT-based chatbots can be integrated into pedagogically structured writing in­ struction particularly in EFL contexts. Existing research tends to focus on AI for general feedback or motivation (Shibani et al., 2017; Su et al., 2023), but few studies examine how ChatGPT can function as a scaf­ folded support tool during distinct stages of the writing process (Sinha",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"tends to focus on AI for general feedback or motivation (Shibani et al., 2017; Su et al., 2023), but few studies examine how ChatGPT can function as a scaf­ folded support tool during distinct stages of the writing process (Sinha et al., 2020; Zhao, 2022; Zoherey, 2023). Moreover, no prior studies, to our knowledge, have tested ChatGPT within an integrated process-genre writing approach in Thai higher education. This presents a clear gap: although Thai university students face persistent challenges in academic writing especially in planning, organizing genre-specific texts, and revising, there is insufficient research on how AI, and specifically ChatGPT, can be applied to target these difficulties within a structured, evidence-based pedagogical framework. Moreover, the current theoretical frameworks underlying writing instruction have not yet integrated the role of AI tools like ChatGPT. While sociocognitive and constructivist theories emphasize human interaction and personalized feedback as crucial for learning, they do not account for the new forms of real-time, automated feedback that AI provides. This theoretical gap limits understanding of how AI can complement or transform traditional scaffolding and genre awareness practices",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"and personalized feedback as crucial for learning, they do not account for the new forms of real-time, automated feedback that AI provides. This theoretical gap limits understanding of how AI can complement or transform traditional scaffolding and genre awareness practices in EFL writing. Bridging this gap is essential for developing pedagogical models that effectively combine AI capabilities with proven writing instruction methods. This gap in both genre awareness and technology integration ne­ cessitates a shift in instructional practices. Current literature on writing instruction reveals a need for more integrative and technology- supported approaches that allow for genre awareness to be cultivated alongside the exploration of digital tools (Su et al., 2023; Zhao, 2022). However, few studies have explicitly explored how GPT-based chatbots can be embedded into an eclectic pedagogy that fuses both process- and genre-based models to support EFL students’ academic writing development. This study responds to that gap by investigating how ChatGPT can be meaningfully integrated into a process-genre approach to improve the writing performance of EFL learners. Specifically, it integrates ChatGPT within an eclectic pedagogical framework, combining process",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"academic writing development. This study responds to that gap by investigating how ChatGPT can be meaningfully integrated into a process-genre approach to improve the writing performance of EFL learners. Specifically, it integrates ChatGPT within an eclectic pedagogical framework, combining process and genre-based approaches. The integration of AI provides students with personalized, real-time feedback that supports their writing develop­ ment at each stage: from brainstorming to revision. By utilizing the strengths of both pedagogical approaches and AI-driven assistance, this study aims to improve students’ genre writing skills, while addressing the evolving demands of 21st-century communication in the digital age. 2. Literature review 2.1. The eclectic instructional writing pedagogy In response to the limitations of single-method writing pedagogies, the eclectic instructional writing pedagogy has gained traction as a promising framework that synthesizes elements from both process- oriented and genre-based approaches (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). This hybrid model seeks to leverage the strengths of each method to provide a more holistic and context-sensitive approach to writing instruction (Rahimi & Zhang, 2022). The eclectic approach views writing not merely as a product or process,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"Waluyo, 2023). This hybrid model seeks to leverage the strengths of each method to provide a more holistic and context-sensitive approach to writing instruction (Rahimi & Zhang, 2022). The eclectic approach views writing not merely as a product or process, but as a multifaceted practice that integrates linguistic knowledge, communicative intent, contextual awareness, and cognitive strategies (Huang & Zhang, 2020; Nordin, 2017). At its core, this pedagogy merges the recursive stages of the process approach—such as planning, drafting, and revising—with the genre- based approach’s emphasis on the social purposes and structural fea­ tures of different text types (Hyland, 2003; Karatay, 2011). This inte­ gration enables a balanced instructional design that encourages creativity and personal expression while guiding students to master genre conventions and audience expectations (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). Consequently, learners acquire a broader repertoire of writing strategies applicable across academic and real-world contexts. A well-documented example of this integrated pedagogy is the four- stage framework proposed by Huang and Zhang (2020). This model includes: (1) Field-building, where learners activate prior knowledge and contextual understanding; (2) Modeling, which involves analyzing",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,2,"academic and real-world contexts. A well-documented example of this integrated pedagogy is the four- stage framework proposed by Huang and Zhang (2020). This model includes: (1) Field-building, where learners activate prior knowledge and contextual understanding; (2) Modeling, which involves analyzing sample texts to identify genre features; (3) Joint Construction, focusing on collaborative planning and drafting; and (4) Independent Construc­ tion, where students compose their own texts with ongoing teacher support. By combining genre awareness with process-oriented strate­ gies, this framework offers structured yet flexible scaffolding throughout the writing cycle, as detailed in Fig. 1. Huang and Zhang’s process-genre approach is particularly well- suited for integration with AI tools like ChatGPT because its struc­ tured stages align closely with the capabilities of AI to provide targeted, real-time feedback and scaffolding. For example, during the Field- Fig. 1. The process of process-genre writing approach (Huang & Zhang, 2020). R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 2",157
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"Building and Modeling stages, ChatGPT can assist learners in exploring ideas and analyzing genre conventions, while in the Joint Construction and Independent Construction phases, it can offer dynamic support for drafting and revising texts. This complementary fit allows the pedagogy to be enhanced by AI’s interactive and personalized assistance, which differs from prior work that either treats AI as a general feedback tool or lacks integration within a clear pedagogical framework (Chen et al., 2020). This study adopts Huang and Zhang’s (2020) eclectic process-genre approach as the foundational framework for instruction, integrating GPT-based chatbots into students’ writing processes across four genres to provide scaffolded, AI-supported learning. Empirical studies have consistently demonstrated the efficacy of the process-genre approach. For example, research indicates that this model enhances students’ performance across various writing genres, including narrative, descriptive, and argumentative texts (Babalola, 2012; Huang & Zhang, 2020). Students taught using this integrated approach show improvements in both linguistic form and communica­ tive function, benefiting from the dual focus of the pedagogy. However, despite growing evidence supporting the process-genre approach, important gaps remain. In particular,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"& Zhang, 2020). Students taught using this integrated approach show improvements in both linguistic form and communica­ tive function, benefiting from the dual focus of the pedagogy. However, despite growing evidence supporting the process-genre approach, important gaps remain. In particular, there is limited research exploring the integration of this pedagogy with emerging educational technologies such as AI-driven tools like ChatGPT. Espe­ cially within the Thai EFL context, empirical studies investigating how such intelligent digital technologies can be synergized with process- genre writing instruction to enhance student engagement, motivation, and writing performance are lacking. Addressing this gap offers an important opportunity to evolve traditional pedagogical frameworks, enriching both theoretical understanding and practical classroom ap­ plications through the incorporation of AI-enhanced writing support. 2.2. The role of GPT-based chat applications in writing Recent advancements in artificial intelligence, particularly GPT- based chat applications like ChatGPT, have significantly influenced the field of language education, including writing instruction (Moqbel & Al-Kadi, 2023; Su et al., 2023; Zhai, 2022). These tools leverage deep learning and natural language processing to simulate human-like in­ teractions, offering students real-time",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"like ChatGPT, have significantly influenced the field of language education, including writing instruction (Moqbel & Al-Kadi, 2023; Su et al., 2023; Zhai, 2022). These tools leverage deep learning and natural language processing to simulate human-like in­ teractions, offering students real-time feedback, personalized scaf­ folding, and interactive language practice (Shibani et al., 2017; Sinha et al., 2020). Such affordances have positioned ChatGPT as a potentially transformative tool in writing pedagogy, enhancing learners’ autonomy and engagement (Kasneci et al., 2023). The application of GPT-based tools in writing instruction has been shown to support various aspects of the writing process. For instance, Su et al. (2023) and Zoherey (2023) note the utility of these tools in guiding students through ideation, drafting, and revision. By providing grammar corrections, stylistic suggestions, and content prompts, ChatGPT func­ tions as both a writing assistant and a feedback mechanism (Fitria, 2023; Zhai, 2022). Moreover, its ability to simulate dialogic interaction fosters a dynamic feedback loop, encouraging learners to engage in iterative learning and reflection (Sinha et al., 2020; Zoherey, 2023). Beyond individual learner support, GPT-based applications are scalable",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"mechanism (Fitria, 2023; Zhai, 2022). Moreover, its ability to simulate dialogic interaction fosters a dynamic feedback loop, encouraging learners to engage in iterative learning and reflection (Sinha et al., 2020; Zoherey, 2023). Beyond individual learner support, GPT-based applications are scalable and capable of generating analytics that inform instructional decision-making (Okonkwo & Ade-Ibijola, 2021; Zoherey, 2024). However, while their potential is widely acknowledged, concerns persist regarding academic integrity and the possibility of overreliance on AI (Kelly, 2023). These concerns, though valid, are counterbalanced by emerging evidence suggesting that when integrated thoughtfully, ChatGPT can enrich rather than replace the writing process (Ali et al., 2023). Despite growing interest in AI-assisted writing, most empirical in­ vestigations have examined ChatGPT’s role in general EFL writing tasks or standalone contexts, often without considering its integration into specific pedagogical models. Particularly lacking are studies that explore how AI tools can be incorporated into eclectic instructional frameworks, such as the process-genre approach, which merges process- oriented writing practices with genre awareness (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). While previous research has separately highlighted the merits of the process-based (Rusinovci,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"how AI tools can be incorporated into eclectic instructional frameworks, such as the process-genre approach, which merges process- oriented writing practices with genre awareness (Peungcharoenkun & Waluyo, 2023). While previous research has separately highlighted the merits of the process-based (Rusinovci, 2015; Wuttiphan, 2023) and genre-based (Nagao, 2019; Pham & Bui, 2022; Robillos, 2024) ap­ proaches, limited attention has been given to their synergistic applica­ tion within AI-mediated environments. In the Thai EFL context, research remains sparse regarding the pedagogical integration of GPT-based tools into eclectic writing models. A few studies (e.g., Janenoppakarn & Thongrin, 2020; Peungchar­ oenkun & Waluyo, 2023) have begun exploring the process-genre approach, but these have yet to incorporate AI support. Addressing this gap is crucial, as GPT-based chat applications could enhance each stage of the writing process – planning, drafting, revising – while simultaneously reinforcing genre-specific structures and conventions. Moreover, by fostering learner independence, engagement, and tailored support, AI integration could help bridge theoretical pedagogy with the evolving realities of digital learning. Accordingly, this study seeks to investigate the integration of GPT- based chat applications within",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"structures and conventions. Moreover, by fostering learner independence, engagement, and tailored support, AI integration could help bridge theoretical pedagogy with the evolving realities of digital learning. Accordingly, this study seeks to investigate the integration of GPT- based chat applications within an eclectic process-genre writing framework, contributing to a more nuanced understanding of AI’s role in EFL writing instruction, particularly in underexplored contexts such as Thai secondary education. To achieve this, the following research questions are addressed: 1. How does the implementation of GPT-based chatbots within an eclectic writing framework (the intervention) influence students’ genre writing performances across different writing aspects (task response, coherence and cohesion, grammatical and structural ac­ curacy, lexical resources, logical order)? 2. How does the intervention affect students’ writing task performances across various genres (descriptive, narrative, process, argumenta­ tive) and writing aspects? 3. What insights into their learning have participants gained after the intervention was implemented? 3. Method 3.1. Design This study employed a sequential mixed methods research design (Creswell & Creswell, 2018), beginning with quantitative data collection to evaluate the impact of integrating GPT-based chatbots within",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"learning have participants gained after the intervention was implemented? 3. Method 3.1. Design This study employed a sequential mixed methods research design (Creswell & Creswell, 2018), beginning with quantitative data collection to evaluate the impact of integrating GPT-based chatbots within an eclectic approach to enhance students’ writing skills across four genres: descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative. Using a single-group pre- and post-test design, the same group of students was assessed before and after the intervention, enabling direct comparisons to determine its effectiveness. The qualitative phase followed, incorporating interviews and observation field notes to explore students’ learning experiences, offering deeper insight into the intervention’s impact. The focus on these four genres stems from their collective role in providing a well-rounded writing education, as they foster creativity, critical thinking, and communication skills, all of which are crucial for academic and pro­ fessional success (Driscoll et al., 2020). These genres help students develop versatile writing abilities, preparing them to adapt to various contexts and strengthening their overall writing competence. 3.2. Participants The participants in this study were 30 third-year university students (aged 19–20)",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,3,"success (Driscoll et al., 2020). These genres help students develop versatile writing abilities, preparing them to adapt to various contexts and strengthening their overall writing competence. 3.2. Participants The participants in this study were 30 third-year university students (aged 19–20) enrolled in the “Approaches to Writing” course at a Thai university. They represented the entire population of one intact class and were selected using purposive sampling (Best & Khan, 2012), based on predefined criteria aligned with the study’s objectives. The group consisted of 12 males and 18 females, all classified as having limited English proficiency corresponding to CEFR level A2. These students were ideal for the study, as they often struggle with writing across R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 3",125
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"different genres, particularly in organizing ideas and adapting to various writing styles. Their enrollment in a writing-focused course made them especially suited for the intervention, which integrated ChatGPT into an eclectic writing pedagogy. One advantage of selecting an intact class was the ability to implement the intervention within a naturally occurring educational context, enhancing ecological validity. However, this method may limit generalizability to broader populations. Nonetheless, focusing on students at this proficiency level allowed the research to explore how AI-supported pedagogy can meet the needs of learners who are not yet proficient writers. Additionally, while some students initially faced challenges using ChatGPT, the integration of technology provided real-time feedback and genre-based writing support. This contributed to deeper engagement with writing tasks and helped prepare students for digital literacy de­ mands in contemporary academic and professional contexts. 3.3. The intervention The intervention was carried out over 24 sessions, with each session lasting 3 h. It was structured around an eclectic writing framework grounded in the process-genre approach (Huang & Zhang, 2020), enhanced through the integration of GPT-based chatbots. As illustrated in",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"intervention was carried out over 24 sessions, with each session lasting 3 h. It was structured around an eclectic writing framework grounded in the process-genre approach (Huang & Zhang, 2020), enhanced through the integration of GPT-based chatbots. As illustrated in Fig. 1, the intervention followed four key stages of the process-genre model: Field-Building Knowledge, Modeling, Joint Construction, and Independent Construction, which were consistently applied to each writing genre introduced throughout the program. Sessions 1 to 4 focused on the administration of pre-writing tests for the four target genres, with one genre assessed per session. Prior to each test, students participated in pre-writing activities such as brain­ storming, posing questions, and activating background knowledge. These preparatory steps aligned with the Field-Building Knowledge stage of the process-genre model, serving to establish a foundational understanding of each genre’s features and expectations. After the fourth session, which assessed Argumentative writing, students were introduced to GPT-based chatbots. This orientation allowed students to explore how the chatbot could support them throughout the writing process—particularly in generating ideas, organizing content, revising drafts, and checking grammar and coherence.",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"fourth session, which assessed Argumentative writing, students were introduced to GPT-based chatbots. This orientation allowed students to explore how the chatbot could support them throughout the writing process—particularly in generating ideas, organizing content, revising drafts, and checking grammar and coherence. Sessions 5 to 8 focused on teaching Descriptive writing and followed the four phases of the process-genre approach. In the Field-Building Knowledge stage, students engaged in guided discussions to explore the purpose, audience, and structural features of descriptive texts, using GPT-based chatbot prompts to generate examples and deepen under­ standing. During the Modeling stage, they analyzed model texts with the support of GPT-based chatbots, which offered additional explanations of organizational patterns and language features. In the Joint Construction stage, students collaboratively planned and composed texts, using chatbots to brainstorm vocabulary, generate supporting sentences, and revise for clarity and cohesion. Finally, in the Independent Construction stage, students individually produced descriptive texts and refined them with peer and teacher feedback, while using the GPT-based chatbots to verify grammar, enhance sentence flow, and ensure overall accuracy. This instructional sequence was repeated for the",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"in the Independent Construction stage, students individually produced descriptive texts and refined them with peer and teacher feedback, while using the GPT-based chatbots to verify grammar, enhance sentence flow, and ensure overall accuracy. This instructional sequence was repeated for the remaining genres. Sessions 9 to 12 were dedicated to Narrative writing, Sessions 13 to 16 focused on Process writing, and Sessions 17 to 20 covered Argumenta­ tive writing. In each genre cycle, the same four-stage process was applied, and students were consistently encouraged to use GPT-based chatbots as cognitive and linguistic scaffolds across the writing tasks. Chatbots played a dynamic role in providing personalized feedback, reinforcing genre structures, and supporting self-revision and indepen­ dent learning. Finally, Sessions 21 to 24 were devoted to post-writing assessments, with one session for each genre. These assessments aimed to measure students’ progress and evaluate the overall effectiveness of the inter­ vention. By explicitly aligning each phase of instruction with the process-genre model and systematically incorporating GPT-based chat­ bot support throughout, the intervention created a structured and interactive learning environment that fostered students’ development of",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"the overall effectiveness of the inter­ vention. By explicitly aligning each phase of instruction with the process-genre model and systematically incorporating GPT-based chat­ bot support throughout, the intervention created a structured and interactive learning environment that fostered students’ development of genre-specific writing skills. 3.4. Data collection 3.4.1. Pre- and post- genre writing tests This study employed pre- and post-writing tests to evaluate the impact of the intervention on students’ genre-specific writing perfor­ mance. In the pre-test, students wrote essays on four genres, over the first four sessions. Each genre was assessed in a separate session: descriptive in the 1st session, narrative in the 2nd, process in the 3rd, and argumentative in the 4th. Prior to each test, students engaged in planning activities such as brainstorming and schema-building to sup­ port their writing. The post-tests were conducted after the intervention, during which students received targeted instruction for each genre. The post-tests followed the same genre sequence and structure, with students writing essays on descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative topics, similar to the pre-test, but designed to reflect the intervention’s focus. They",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"which students received targeted instruction for each genre. The post-tests followed the same genre sequence and structure, with students writing essays on descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative topics, similar to the pre-test, but designed to reflect the intervention’s focus. They were given 60 min to develop a writing topic using at least 250 words. The genre topics were carefully selected to align with the subjects covered in their writing course. To ensure content validity, the topics were reviewed by Experts in the field. Their feedback was used to refine the topics, ensuring alignment with the course objectives and curricu­ lum standards. Two English lecturers assessed the essays using a rubric developed by the researcher and was as well checked by the experts. The interrater reliability scores of 0.87 and 0.88 indicate strong consistency between the two raters. For the scoring scale, refer to the Test Marking section. 3.4.2. Genre writing tasks There were a total of 16 writing tasks implemented during the intervention. It started with ‘descriptive’, ‘narrative’, ‘process’, and finally, ‘argumentative’ genres. These tasks were parallel in difficulty level",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"scale, refer to the Test Marking section. 3.4.2. Genre writing tasks There were a total of 16 writing tasks implemented during the intervention. It started with ‘descriptive’, ‘narrative’, ‘process’, and finally, ‘argumentative’ genres. These tasks were parallel in difficulty level to allow a direct comparison of scores, providing a clear indication of any improvements. The purpose of these writing tasks was to evaluate students’ proficiency in writing essays across various genres. The topics covered in the tasks correspond with the course aims. Each written essay was rated based on a writing scoring guide that was similarly used in their pre- and post-writing tests. These writing tasks were designed by the researcher and validated through the Index of Item-Objective Congruence (IOC) by a panel of experts to ensure content validity. The items were adjusted based on feedback from the panel, ensuring align­ ment with genre writing skills and study objectives. The rubric consisted of five components (see test marking). 3.4.3. Observation field-notes The study employed observational field notes as a key instrument for documenting and analyzing emerging issues throughout the imple­",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"align­ ment with genre writing skills and study objectives. The rubric consisted of five components (see test marking). 3.4.3. Observation field-notes The study employed observational field notes as a key instrument for documenting and analyzing emerging issues throughout the imple­ mentation process. These notes, recorded by the teacher-researcher, provided valuable insights into classroom dynamics and participant behavior, enhancing the overall understanding of how the study unfolded. To preserve accuracy and detail, the teacher-researcher documented observations immediately after each class session. This timely approach minimized the risk of forgetting important elements and ensured the comprehensive capture of nuanced interactions and reflections relevant to the study’s objectives. 3.4.4. Semi-structured interviews These interviews were conducted one week after the intervention to gather in-depth insights into participants’ use of GPT-based chatbots within an eclectic pedagogy to improve their writing skills. A total of 20 participants voluntarily took part, with interviews lasting 30–45 min each. Participants were fully informed about the study’s aims and R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 4",170
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,4,"participants voluntarily took part, with interviews lasting 30–45 min each. Participants were fully informed about the study’s aims and R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 4",30
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,5,"processes and consented to participate. The interview prompts, devel­ oped based on research objectives and relevant literature, were reviewed by three experts in the English field to ensure clarity, rele­ vance, and comprehensiveness, leading to necessary revisions. 3.5. Test marking To evaluate the students’ written essays, a scoring rubric was uti­ lized, consisting of five criteria/writing aspects: ‘task response’ (demonstrating understanding by covering all parts comprehensively with well-developed ideas supported by appropriate details and exam­ ples), ‘coherence-cohesion’ (clarity and logical flow of ideas and the fit of the text’s parts), ‘grammatical and structural accuracy’ (evaluates the correctness of grammar, syntax, and sentence structure), ‘lexical re­ sources’ (the wide range and appropriateness of vocabulary used in context), and ‘logical order’ (overall organization of ideas to enhance the argument or narrative, including a logical order, a strong conclusion, with consistency maintained throughout the writing). This rubric, pre­ pared by the researcher, was evaluated for content validity through the Index of Item-Objective Congruence (IOC) by two English lecturers from the participating university and adjusted accordingly before use. Addi­ tionally, a descriptive checklist was",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,5,"the writing). This rubric, pre­ pared by the researcher, was evaluated for content validity through the Index of Item-Objective Congruence (IOC) by two English lecturers from the participating university and adjusted accordingly before use. Addi­ tionally, a descriptive checklist was developed to provide a standardized and consistent assessment of the written compositions. Each criterion had a maximum score of 20 points, with subsections within each cri­ terion—established through consensus among evaluators—assigned a maximum of four points each (4 = highest; 1 = lowest). To ensure reliability, the rubric’s internal consistency was assessed using Cron­ bach’s alpha, which yielded a value of 0.81, indicating good reliability. While scoring was mainly conducted by a single trained rater, repeated scoring trials and expert consultations were implemented to maintain scoring consistency. 3.6. Data analysis For quantitative data analysis, descriptive statistics such as fre­ quency, mean, and standard deviation were calculated and organized in tables. Inferential statistics were used to examine variations between students’ pre- and post-test results. Qualitative data were collected through semi-structured interviews with 20 voluntary participants designed based on established theoretical frameworks",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,5,"mean, and standard deviation were calculated and organized in tables. Inferential statistics were used to examine variations between students’ pre- and post-test results. Qualitative data were collected through semi-structured interviews with 20 voluntary participants designed based on established theoretical frameworks and reviewed by experts to ensure content validity and clarity. The interviews were audio-recorded and transcribed verbatim for accurate representation. The transcripts were systematically coded using a topical coding approach aligned with Creswell and Creswell’s (2018) methodology. Coding and interpretation were conducted solely by the researcher, with efforts to reduce subjective bias through repeated reviews and reflective memoing. Additionally, member checking was performed by sharing summaries of findings with several participants to confirm the accuracy of interpretations and enhance trustworthiness. Despite these measures, potential limitations include the inherent subjectivity of qualitative analysis and the absence of multiple coders, which may influence data interpretation. 4. Results 4.1. Quantitative analysis 4.1.1. Students’ genre writing performances across writing aspects Table 1 presents a comprehensive summary of students’ pre-test and post-test writing performance across four genres: First, the descriptive writing genre showed significant improvements",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,5,"influence data interpretation. 4. Results 4.1. Quantitative analysis 4.1.1. Students’ genre writing performances across writing aspects Table 1 presents a comprehensive summary of students’ pre-test and post-test writing performance across four genres: First, the descriptive writing genre showed significant improvements across all writing as­ pects after the intervention. For example, task response improved from a pre-test mean of 2.35 to a post-test mean of 3.55 (p < .001), with a large effect size of 1.12. Similarly, coherence-cohesion increased from 2.00 to 3.52 (p < .001, d = 1.11), and overall writing performance improved from 2.03 to 3.39 (p < .001, d = 0.95). All p-values were below the 0.001 threshold, indicating highly significant differences, and large ef­ fect sizes (0.87–1.12) suggest the intervention had a strong practical impact on students’ descriptive writing skills. In the narrative genre, it also demonstrated statistically significant gains across all assessed aspects. Task response increased from 2.45 to 3.78 (p < .001) with a medium-large effect size of 0.79. Coherence- cohesion rose from 2.01 to 3.25 (p < .001, d = 0.93), and the overall",180
