DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"With targeted feedback on vocabulary, structure, and grammar, the platform supports flexible, student-centered learning. As P14 noted, chatbot integration enhances engagement and accelerates writing improvement through tailored support. “The intervention improved my writing by adapting to my needs and enhancing my versatility across styles.”-P14 4.2.3.2. Genre-specific writing practices. The intervention enhances lin­ guistic versatility by offering genre-specific practice through ChatGPT. It helps students adapt tone, structure, and language to suit different genres like narrative or argumentative writing. As P9 expressed, this deepens their understanding of genre conventions and improves flexible communication. “Engaging with GPT within eclectic approach refined my skills and boosted adaptability across genres.”-P9 Fieldnote #4 aligns with the interview results mentioned earlier: “Students showed strong genre-specific writing skills, easily navi­ gating descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative tasks, demonstrating both adaptability and proficiency.” 4.2.4. Challenges of using the intervention on students’ genre writing processes (theme 4) 4.2.4.1. Time pressure. Time pressure challenged students in the eclectic writing approach, limiting their ability to fully engage in plan­ ning, drafting, and evaluating. These constraints hindered timely completion and affected the depth",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"on students’ genre writing processes (theme 4) 4.2.4.1. Time pressure. Time pressure challenged students in the eclectic writing approach, limiting their ability to fully engage in plan­ ning, drafting, and evaluating. These constraints hindered timely completion and affected the depth of their writing. P10 shared how difficult it was to manage all stages within the given timeframe. “Juggling through the stages of the eclectic pedagogy within the specified timeframe affected the depth of my engagement and overall quality of work.”-P10 The fieldnote#5 below was found to be in parallel with the interview results above: “Applying the process-genre approach revealed challenges due to time constraints. Students had to navigate each stage under pressure, which limited their ability to fully engage and may have affected the depth and quality of their writing.” 4.2.4.2. Challenges in deciphering instructions with chatbots. Students often struggle with logical order when using ChatGPT, as interpreting complex or ambiguous instructions can disrupt coherence. Mis­ understandings may affect structure and flow, hindering effective collaborative writing, as P2 expressed: “I find that unclear instructions cause the chatbot to disrupt my",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"with logical order when using ChatGPT, as interpreting complex or ambiguous instructions can disrupt coherence. Mis­ understandings may affect structure and flow, hindering effective collaborative writing, as P2 expressed: “I find that unclear instructions cause the chatbot to disrupt my draft’s flow..”-P2 The same results were found at researcher’s fieldnote #6 as follows: “Students sometimes struggled with the chatbot misinterpreting in­ structions, which disrupted the logical flow of their drafts, high­ lighting the need for clearer guidance to improve collaboration.” 4.2.4.3. Critical evaluation of off-topic and inconsistent AI feedback. This sub-theme captures how students recognized occasional off-topic re­ sponses and inconsistent genre-specific guidance from ChatGPT, which required them to critically assess the AI’s suggestions rather than accepting them blindly, underscoring the essential role of teacher mediation in facilitating effective AI-assisted writing. P3 shared: “Sometimes ChatGPT gave me answers that didn’t quite fit the genre I was writing in, so I had to be careful and decide which suggestions to use.” -P3 4.2.4.4. Challenge in transferring writing skills across other genres. This sub-theme highlights participants’ challenges in adapting writing skills across",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"quite fit the genre I was writing in, so I had to be careful and decide which suggestions to use.” -P3 4.2.4.4. Challenge in transferring writing skills across other genres. This sub-theme highlights participants’ challenges in adapting writing skills across genres. Despite chatbot assistance, applying proficiency smoothly to diverse styles remains difficult. Their experiences emphasize the need for clear instruction on genre-specific techniques, particularly in argu­ mentative writing, as P12 conveyed: “Adapting my writing skills across different genres has been challenging especially in the argumentative writing task, even with the ChatGPT.”- P12 Fieldnote #7 was found to be a verbatim match with the interview results above: “Students struggled to transfer writing skills across genres, facing structural challenges that impacted coherence and flow, especially when moving from factual to creative or argumentative writing.” 5. Discussion 5.1. Students’ genre writing performances across writing aspects The results of this study reveal substantial improvements in students’ genre-specific writing performances across four genres: descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative writing. In each genre, students demonstrated significant gains in various writing aspects—particularly task response, coherence-cohesion, lexical resource,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"writing aspects The results of this study reveal substantial improvements in students’ genre-specific writing performances across four genres: descriptive, narrative, process, and argumentative writing. In each genre, students demonstrated significant gains in various writing aspects—particularly task response, coherence-cohesion, lexical resource, and logical order—with all improvements achieving statistically significant p- values of 0.000. For example, in the descriptive genre, students’ mean score increased from 13.83 to 18.36, with task response showing the most significant gain (effect size = 2.12), followed by coherence- cohesion (2.01), indicating enhanced idea development and organiza­ tion. Similarly, in the narrative genre, the overall score rose from 13.94 to 18.61, with lexical resource improving markedly (effect size = 1.98), suggesting that students were able to expand and apply their vocabulary more effectively. In the process genre, the mean score increased from 13.89 to 18.25, with coherence-cohesion (1.96) and task response (1.88) both showing notable progress, indicating improved ability to follow logical steps and address the prompt clearly. Finally, in the argumen­ tative genre, mean scores rose from 13.67 to 18.17, with strong gains in task response (2.08)",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"and task response (1.88) both showing notable progress, indicating improved ability to follow logical steps and address the prompt clearly. Finally, in the argumen­ tative genre, mean scores rose from 13.67 to 18.17, with strong gains in task response (2.08) and coherence-cohesion (1.97), reflecting improved clarity and alignment with argumentative structure and logic. The significant improvements in students’ writing across the four genres can be attributed to the agentive and facilitative support pro­ vided by the integration of ChatGPT within an eclectic approach. This approach, with ChatGPT serving as a tool for immediate, personalized feedback (Sinha et al., 2020) helped students address their specific writing challenges. The process-based approach promoted drafting, revising, and reflection, deepening students’ cognitive engagement. The genre-based approach offered clear frameworks for understanding genre conventions, improving writing structure. ChatGPT supported both by providing ongoing feedback to enhance coherence, vocabulary, and task response, helping students better meet genre expectations. The upward trends in both mean scores and large effect sizes across genres suggest that this support helped scaffold students’ learning effectively, espe­ cially in their ability to construct",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,8,"coherence, vocabulary, and task response, helping students better meet genre expectations. The upward trends in both mean scores and large effect sizes across genres suggest that this support helped scaffold students’ learning effectively, espe­ cially in their ability to construct and organize ideas. Integrating ChatGPT within eclectic approach, students were able to refine their writing systematically, leading to substantial gains in all assessed as­ pects of their genre-specific writing. The significant improvements observed in students’ writing perfor­ mances align with the work of Su et al. (2023), who emphasized the role of GPT-based chatbots in supporting writing through tailored feedback. Moreover, the eclectic approach has been shown to enhance students’ writing by offering diverse strategies that promote development (Huang & Zhang, 2020; Kinik & Genc, 2022). GPT-based chatbots’ real-time corrections and feedback align with Peungcharoenkun and Waluyo (2023), who noted the effectiveness of such tools in improving R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 8",158
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"coherence and cohesion. However, despite the overall improvements, challenges remained, particularly in grammatical and structural accuracy, where smaller gains were observed. For instance, in the argumentative genre, while task response improved significantly (effect size = 2.08), grammatical accuracy showed only a modest gain (effect size = 0.91), with post-test mean scores still indicating room for improvement. This echoes the findings of Ferris (2002) and Robillos (2024), who noted that complex genres such as argumentative writing often present greater challenges in grammar and syntax. Additionally, the variability in lexical resource improvement across genres, especially in descriptive writing, aligns with Peungcharoenkun and Waluyo’s (2023) findings that different genres pose different lexical demands. This indicates a need for continued focus on genre-specific vocabulary instruction. While the findings demonstrate significant improvements in stu­ dents’; genre writing performances, it is important to note that this study employed a single-group pre/post design without a control group. Therefore, the observed gains cannot be definitively attributed solely to the integration of the eclectic writing framework enhanced by GPT- based chatbots. Other factors may have contributed, such as natural",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"study employed a single-group pre/post design without a control group. Therefore, the observed gains cannot be definitively attributed solely to the integration of the eclectic writing framework enhanced by GPT- based chatbots. Other factors may have contributed, such as natural language development (Plonsky, 2019; Ellis, 2015), repeated exposure and practice effects (Robillos & Bustos, 2023; Skehan, 2018), teacher or peer feedback (Yang et al., 2023), and the motivational influence of new AI tools (Shibani et al., 2017). As such, conclusions about the specific impact of the intervention should be interpreted with caution. Future studies using experimental designs and control groups would help better isolate and validate the effects of AI-supported eclectic writing instruction. 5.2. Students’ writing task performances across genres and aspects Across all genres, students demonstrated steady improvement in task response and coherence-cohesion, consistent with previous studies on the importance of clarity in writing (Hyland, 2013; Kroll, 2001). Narrative writing showed the most consistent progress, likely due to its familiar structure, which aided logical flow (Dunsmuir et al., 2015). However, the descriptive genre posed challenges in maintaining coherence, especially",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"importance of clarity in writing (Hyland, 2013; Kroll, 2001). Narrative writing showed the most consistent progress, likely due to its familiar structure, which aided logical flow (Dunsmuir et al., 2015). However, the descriptive genre posed challenges in maintaining coherence, especially in later tasks, as students struggled to link detailed descriptions and sensory details. This aligns with Segalowitz’s (2000) findings on the precision required in descriptive writing. Moreover, time constraints were identified as a significant challenge, with P10 com­ menting, “Juggling through the stages of the eclectic pedagogy within the specified timeframe affected the depth of my engagement and overall quality of work.” This student reflection echoes Wen and Walters (2022), who found that limited instructional time can constrain student performance in writing-focused interventions, particularly when dealing with cognitively demanding genres. This feedback emphasizes the need for better pacing to allow deeper engagement and richer content develop­ ment in collaborative writing approaches. Throughout the writing process following the stages of eclectic approach, ChatGPT was used as a support tool; however, it infrequently generated off-topic or irrelevant responses that at times disrupted",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"deeper engagement and richer content develop­ ment in collaborative writing approaches. Throughout the writing process following the stages of eclectic approach, ChatGPT was used as a support tool; however, it infrequently generated off-topic or irrelevant responses that at times disrupted stu­ dents’ writing flow and focus. Such occurrences reflect known limita­ tions of AI language models, often referred to as “hallucinations,” where the model produces plausible but inaccurate or unrelated content (Bender et al., 2021; Ji et al., 2023). These rare but notable issues required students to exercise critical judgment in selecting useful feed­ back, highlighting the tool’s limited genre-specific awareness and the need for human mediation. This aligns with findings from Huang et al. (2023), who noted that despite AI’s usefulness, its inconsistent domain or task-specific understanding can challenge learners and educators alike. Consequently, teacher support is essential to help students criti­ cally evaluate AI-generated suggestions and maintain focus on task re­ quirements and coherence. In terms of grammatical and structural accuracy, the students showed the most improvement in the process and argumentative genres, which emphasize clarity and precision,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"help students criti­ cally evaluate AI-generated suggestions and maintain focus on task re­ quirements and coherence. In terms of grammatical and structural accuracy, the students showed the most improvement in the process and argumentative genres, which emphasize clarity and precision, supporting the idea that struc­ tured frameworks enhance grammatical skills (Graham & Perin, 2007). Initially struggling, participants improved over time through iterative practice with drafts and peer feedback, as one student noted, “The more I practice, the more I refine my skills.” This observation supports Hassen et al. (2024), who emphasizes the role of repetition and scaffolded writing tasks in building grammatical competence in EFL learners. In contrast, improvement was less consistent in the narrative and descriptive genres, which have more flexible grammatical structures, aligning with Ferris’s (2002) findings that students struggle with less rigid genres. In terms of lexical resource, students showed genre-specific varia­ tion. The descriptive genre declined in later writing tasks as students struggled to provide precise vocabulary for complex details, in line with Robillos and Bustos (2023) on descriptive language challenges. The argumentative genre also fluctuated,",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"resource, students showed genre-specific varia­ tion. The descriptive genre declined in later writing tasks as students struggled to provide precise vocabulary for complex details, in line with Robillos and Bustos (2023) on descriptive language challenges. The argumentative genre also fluctuated, with a dip in Task #3 followed by a recovery in Task #4, likely due to students prioritizing clarity over vo­ cabulary expansion. In contrast, the process and narrative genres exhibited more consistent lexical usage, as their structured nature allowed students to rely on familiar vocabulary for sequencing and storytelling, supporting O’Malley and Chamot’s (1990) claim that structured genres facilitate vocabulary use. Students also expressed feeling more comfortable experimenting with vocabulary in ChatGPT’s low-pressure environment, with one noting, “I don’t feel any pressure of a live conversation using ChatGPT.” This sense of psychological safety is echoed in Shibani et al. (2017), who found that digital tools like ChatGPT reduce anxiety among EFL students and encourage risk-taking in language production. Field notes confirmed that the immediate feedback from ChatGPT helped refine vocabulary choices and boost student confidence. Regarding logical order, the",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"(2017), who found that digital tools like ChatGPT reduce anxiety among EFL students and encourage risk-taking in language production. Field notes confirmed that the immediate feedback from ChatGPT helped refine vocabulary choices and boost student confidence. Regarding logical order, the process and narrative genres showed the most consistent improvement due to their clear structural demands, step-by-step progression in process writing and the “beginning-middle- end” framework in narrative writing – helping students organize ideas effectively. In contrast, the descriptive and argumentative genres showed greater variability in logical order, with students struggling to maintain a clear progression in these more complex genres. Descriptive writing required logical connections between sensory details (Robillos & Bustos, 2023), while argumentative writing needed careful arrangement of premises and counterarguments. This complexity aligns with Jane­ noppakarn and Thongrin’s (2020) argument that genres with more ab­ stract organization, like argumentative writing, pose challenges in sustaining logical flow. Interview data also confirmed these difficulties, with P6 stating, “It’s hard to know how to arrange all the points in argu­ mentative writing without mixing things up,” illustrating the cognitive load such",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"argumentative writing, pose challenges in sustaining logical flow. Interview data also confirmed these difficulties, with P6 stating, “It’s hard to know how to arrange all the points in argu­ mentative writing without mixing things up,” illustrating the cognitive load such genres demand. This is consistent with Wingate (2012) and Robillos (2024) who found that novice writers often struggle with the sequencing of ideas in argumentative texts, particularly when writing in a second language. Overall, students performed best in the process and narrative genres, which exhibited the most consistent improvements across all aspects. This is likely due to their inherent structure, which provided a clear framework for organizing ideas. The descriptive genre showed improvement but experienced more fluctuations in coherence and lex­ ical resource, suggesting that students found it challenging to balance creativity with structural demands. The argumentative genre, while improving overall, showed the least consistent gains, particularly in lexical resource and logical order. This likely reflects the greater cognitive and linguistic demands of argumentative writing, which re­ quires students to engage in critical thinking and organize ideas in a more",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,9,"overall, showed the least consistent gains, particularly in lexical resource and logical order. This likely reflects the greater cognitive and linguistic demands of argumentative writing, which re­ quires students to engage in critical thinking and organize ideas in a more complex manner (Janenoppakarn & Thongrin, 2020). These findings support the use of an eclectic approach, as discussed by Valle­ dor et al. (2023), which promotes flexible and context-sensitive teaching methods that can address diverse learner challenges across genres. These findings provide valuable insights into students’ writing development across genres, highlighting how the eclectic approach R.J. Robillos Social Sciences & Humanities Open 12 (2025) 101886 9",105
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"enhanced by GPT-based chatbots may support improvements in various writing aspects. Nevertheless, given the single-group pre/post study design without a control group, the observed improvements should be interpreted as indicative rather than definitive evidence of the in­ tervention’s effectiveness. Specifically, it is possible that factors such as natural language development, increased familiarity with the writing tasks, or external instructional influences also contributed to the progress. 6. Conclusion The integration of ChatGPT within an eclectic writing framework, significantly contributed to the development of students’ writing skills across various genres. While students demonstrated notable improve­ ments in task response, coherence, grammatical accuracy, and lexical resource, the study revealed the unique challenges posed by different genres, with narrative and process writing showing the most consistent progress. The real-time feedback provided by ChatGPT played a crucial role in refining students’ writing, particularly in maintaining coherence and cohesion, while fostering a supportive, low-pressure environment that encouraged experimentation. However, the findings also high­ lighted the impact of time constraints, which hindered students’ ability to engage fully with the iterative stages of the writing process, poten­ tially",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"coherence and cohesion, while fostering a supportive, low-pressure environment that encouraged experimentation. However, the findings also high­ lighted the impact of time constraints, which hindered students’ ability to engage fully with the iterative stages of the writing process, poten­ tially affecting the depth of their work. This underscores the importance of balancing time management with the need for deeper engagement, suggesting that adjustments in pacing could further enhance the effec­ tiveness of such interventions in the future. The study also reveals the potential of GPT-based chatbots to pro­ mote reflective practices, offering new insights into effective writing instruction methods and emphasizing the need for tailored support to address specific writing challenges. These findings underscore the value of innovative tools and adaptive pedagogical approaches in improving writing proficiency in the digital era. However, given the single-group pre/post design without a control group, these positive outcomes should be interpreted with caution. Furthermore, as this study was conducted with only 30 Thai EFL students in a single course, the findings are context-specific and the generalizability to other cultural or insti­ tutional settings remains",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"group, these positive outcomes should be interpreted with caution. Furthermore, as this study was conducted with only 30 Thai EFL students in a single course, the findings are context-specific and the generalizability to other cultural or insti­ tutional settings remains limited. Future studies with larger and more diverse samples are essential to validate and extend these results across broader educational environments. Rigorous experimental designs will be necessary to confirm the specific effects of the eclectic writing framework enhanced by GPT-based chatbots and to explore its appli­ cability in varied contexts. 7. Limitations The study faced several limitations that may have impacted its out­ comes. The relatively short duration of the intervention may not have allowed enough time for students to fully refine their writing skills, particularly in complex areas like logical order and vocabulary use. Additionally, variability in students’ prior writing proficiency and fa­ miliarity with different genres could have influenced the results, as in­ dividual differences played a role in overall performance. While ChatGPT provided real-time feedback, its effectiveness may have been affected by students’ varying levels of comfort",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"writing proficiency and fa­ miliarity with different genres could have influenced the results, as in­ dividual differences played a role in overall performance. While ChatGPT provided real-time feedback, its effectiveness may have been affected by students’ varying levels of comfort with AI tools. Further­ more, time constraints, as highlighted by student feedback, impacted students’ ability to fully engage with the iterative writing process, indicating that more flexible pacing could enhance the depth of future interventions. Importantly, although ChatGPT served as a valuable support tool, it occasionally produced hallucinated or off-topic responses and demon­ strated inconsistent awareness of genre-specific conventions. These AI limitations may have disrupted students’ writing focus and compre­ hension, requiring them to critically evaluate and filter feedback, which could have introduced additional cognitive load and hindered smooth writing progression. This underscores the need for ongoing teacher mediation and further investigation into optimizing AI integration in genre-based writing instruction. The qualitative data analysis relied on a single researcher’s coding and interpretation of the semi-structured interviews, which may have introduced subjective bias despite efforts to enhance validity through member checking",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"further investigation into optimizing AI integration in genre-based writing instruction. The qualitative data analysis relied on a single researcher’s coding and interpretation of the semi-structured interviews, which may have introduced subjective bias despite efforts to enhance validity through member checking and reflective practices. Future studies could strengthen reliability by involving multiple coders and employing inter- rater reliability measures. Additionally, the use of purposive sampling to select participants may have introduced selection bias, limiting the representativeness of the sample and thereby affecting the generalizability of the findings. Furthermore, the dual role of the researcher as the students’ teacher could have influenced participants’ responses and behaviors, potentially introducing bias in both data collection and interpretation. These factors underscore the need for caution when extrapolating the results and suggest that future studies consider random sampling and separation of teaching and research roles to enhance objectivity. The study’s design also limits the generalizability of the findings. Using a single-group pre/post approach without a control group restricts the ability to attribute observed improvements solely to the interven­ tion. Other factors such as natural maturation, increased",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"enhance objectivity. The study’s design also limits the generalizability of the findings. Using a single-group pre/post approach without a control group restricts the ability to attribute observed improvements solely to the interven­ tion. Other factors such as natural maturation, increased familiarity with the tasks, or external instructional influences may also have contributed to the results. Moreover, the study’s relatively small sample size and its conduction within a specific cultural and institutional context of a single course in Thailand further restrict the wider applicability of the findings. Thus, future research should incorporate control or comparison groups and larger, more diverse samples across multiple contexts to more rigorously evaluate the specific effects of the eclectic writing framework enhanced by ChatGPT. Ethical statement This research was conducted in adherence to ethical standards for human subject research. All participants were fully informed about the purpose, objectives, and procedures of the study, based on the stated research aims. Prior to their participation, they were briefed on the voluntary nature of the study and were assured that their participation was entirely optional, with the right to",180
DOC_029,NEW09.pdf,10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101886,10,"purpose, objectives, and procedures of the study, based on the stated research aims. Prior to their participation, they were briefed on the voluntary nature of the study and were assured that their participation was entirely optional, with the right to withdraw at any stage without any negative consequences. Informed consent was obtained from all participants (and from parents or legal guardians in the case of minors). Participants were also informed that their responses would be kept confidential and used solely for academic and research purposes. Personal identifiers were removed or anonymized to protect privacy, and data has been securely stored. Declaration of AI-assisted technologies in the writing process During the preparation of this work the author used Quillbot app in order to enhance the manuscript’s readability and linguistic quality. After using this tool, the author reviewed and edited the content as needed and takes full responsibility for the content of the publication. Funding sources This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that",180
