DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,5,India Native (Bilingual) 16 Postgraduate Taught India Native (Bilingual) 17 Postgraduate Taught UK Native 18 Postgraduate Taught Greece Professional working proﬁciency 19 Postgraduate Taught India Native (Bilingual) 20 Postgraduate Taught India Native (Bilingual) 21 Postgraduate Taught India Native (Bilingual) 22 Postgraduate Research China Professional working proﬁciency 23 Postgraduate Taught Malaysia Professional working proﬁciency HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x ARTICLE HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | (2024) 11:482 | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x 5,72
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"If I don’t understand, repeat, repeat, repeat, repeat, repeat until I understand and then proceed. And that is how my process is. It’s a lot of repetition, a lot of reclarifying myself and always detecting all this reading and fact checking. [23, Malaysia] Participant 17 described using in complex way to support reading: I would copy literally like a whole article into it and then say, summarise this in bullet points. I’ll then write it on a piece of paper because that just helps me to like process it in my head as I write the words. Um, and then after that, and then I’ll read the paper like normal [17, UK] Participant 15 described using it directly in the writing process: Sometimes if I’ve written something, what I do is I ask for a feedback and I ask or like, you know, if I want a paraphrasing also. So what I do is I prompt it by asking that the check for ﬂow and paraphrase and then it will kind of give it suggestions and sometimes it adds its",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"like, you know, if I want a paraphrasing also. So what I do is I prompt it by asking that the check for ﬂow and paraphrase and then it will kind of give it suggestions and sometimes it adds its own things. And then if I don’t want something, I can just not have it. But most of the times what happens is you get a pretty good idea of like, you know, yeah, this is something good and then you can build up from there. So it gives you that initial kind of a boost and then it becomes easier to build your arguments or build your paragraphs. (15, India) Participant 20 stands out as potentially using it in a way to simply write ideas for him. He talked about “delegating” tasks to it, constructing himself as in control of the process: So when I break it down, so usually when I have an academic paper, say it’s 10 pages, I have one thought for each page, like one heading for each page in my head. And once I",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"of the process: So when I break it down, so usually when I have an academic paper, say it’s 10 pages, I have one thought for each page, like one heading for each page in my head. And once I have that set, then I just start off with ChatGPT straight up. [20, India] Such complex uses, often involved quite interactive exchanges. Participant 23 used the term “bouncing” ideas off it. I always have sort of like a conversation with it. [23, Malaysia] Indeed, one interesting dimension of these interactions was the sense that the experience was something quite close to discussing with another human. Just directly ask a question. Yes. As if I’m asking a human being. [22, China] But always, always, always remember it is still a tool. It is not a living being. That is that’s why I keep reminding myself it’s not a living being. Always cross check your back. [23, Malaysia] The quote implies a genuine struggle not to see ChatGPT as human. Equally, the value of ChatGPT was often linked to not needing to",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"I keep reminding myself it’s not a living being. Always cross check your back. [23, Malaysia] The quote implies a genuine struggle not to see ChatGPT as human. Equally, the value of ChatGPT was often linked to not needing to ask tutors or peers for help. Of course, it also gives immediate responses unlike them, but it raises the question of how ChatGPT is impacting the social dimension of learning. ChatGPT was also being commonly used for non-academic writing, such as for job applications. Several interviewees mentioned using it for computer coding. There were also very speciﬁc uses, such as for converting text to Latex. ChatGPT beneﬁts and worries. The way that they described it, ChatGPT and the other digital tools they used, gave participants a sense of being more efﬁcient and productive. Thinking about it, basically, there is no big change, because there are still steps in writing, mainly those steps, but the efﬁciency has been improved. [1, China] I think he saves me a lot of time when I’m reading like reference that’s really long. Helps me summarise",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"big change, because there are still steps in writing, mainly those steps, but the efﬁciency has been improved. [1, China] I think he saves me a lot of time when I’m reading like reference that’s really long. Helps me summarise it. I think it’s really efﬁcient. [11, China] This discourse implied that it did not really change what they did or learned, just speeded the process up and saved them time. This may be a rationalisation and disguise the loss of learning that the “efﬁciencies” might cause. A lot of the discourse around needing to use tools revolved around “laziness” but also a sense of a lack of conﬁdence and anxiety. Using ChatGPT was often justiﬁed for reducing stress. I’ll say I feel less stressful because we get to work more efﬁciently by using the tools, by using AI tools compared to the traditional way. [11, China] There was a sense of ChatGPT being the future and there is an inevitability to it overtaking “traditional” learning practices. Ten years ago, study style, study style, and the current one is very",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"compared to the traditional way. [11, China] There was a sense of ChatGPT being the future and there is an inevitability to it overtaking “traditional” learning practices. Ten years ago, study style, study style, and the current one is very different. [23, Malaysia] Yet, while it was being used rather extensively our participants were wary of ChatGPT for a number of reasons. Three were mentioned by most interviewees. The ﬁrst was the unreliability of the information it output and the need to “fact check” it. There was less mention of speciﬁc issues such as failing to acknowledge sources or making up references. A second major concern was that its use would be detected by plagiarism detection tools (a concern they held regardless of the fact that it is generally not detectable). More positively participant 19 wanted to preserve her “voice” I feel like it doesn’t sound like me and I do want to write in my own voice or in my own words as much as I can. [19, India] Similarly, participant 23 thought that ChatGPT created a text that",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"I feel like it doesn’t sound like me and I do want to write in my own voice or in my own words as much as I can. [19, India] Similarly, participant 23 thought that ChatGPT created a text that often read as “auto-generated” [23] so would not use it in many contexts, e.g. writing job applications because it would not effectively differentiate them from other candidates. Implicitly, they did not see this as a problem in the context of academic writing, suggesting that they did not see individuality as valued in that context. There were certainly some students who did see using ChatGPT as impacting their learning: But it sometimes it stopped me from thinking. [12, China] It will reduce the ability to think independently, that is, reduce the motivation to think independently. Some things need to be understood by yourself. [5, China] Thus a third major concern was a fear that the use of tools in general produced dependence. There were also a few comments that showed awareness of the risk to privacy of putting personal information into",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,6,"by yourself. [5, China] Thus a third major concern was a fear that the use of tools in general produced dependence. There were also a few comments that showed awareness of the risk to privacy of putting personal information into ChatGPT. Nearly all the concerns reﬂected on personal impact. Disappointingly, the societal impacts of ChatGPT were not often acknowledged. One interviewee expressed concerns about the impact on low-paid workers. But even when prompted the participants showed little awareness or concern about bias, or ethical issues, e.g., sustainability or the exploitative labour relations used to create ChatGPT. Nevertheless, it seemed that the controversy around ChatGPT had ﬁltered through to problematising its use, in ways that were expressed far less in relation to other tools such as Grammarly. ARTICLE HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x 6 HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | (2024) 11:482 | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x",145
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"Discussion The ﬁndings conﬁrm our earlier work from before the release of ChatGPT, that a wide range of tools is used during the writing process (Zhao et al., 2023). Perhaps ChatGPT will displace other tools, but at the time the study was conducted it was reaching a community of users well-versed in using digital tools. It was ﬁnding a unique place in helping understand assignment questions, aligning answers to the question and structuring ideas, but was also used to perform tasks such as summarisation and proofreading that others performed with pre-existing tools. Our ﬁndings suggested that students with higher English proﬁciency levels tended to use AI tools less frequently. The study conﬁrms previous research, that students are keen to use generative AI and see it as part of a general trend in tech- nology development (Chan and Hu, 2023). Our respondents tended to use discourses around time-saving, efﬁciency, and stress avoidance as justiﬁcation for using the tools. ChatGPT was used rather intensively and iteratively but in highly individualistic ways. This differential use may reﬂect that there was little input at",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"respondents tended to use discourses around time-saving, efﬁciency, and stress avoidance as justiﬁcation for using the tools. ChatGPT was used rather intensively and iteratively but in highly individualistic ways. This differential use may reﬂect that there was little input at this time from the institution to help students how to use and where to draw the line in terms of appropriate use. Students wanted guidance from the institution on ChatGPT, echoing Attewell’s (2023) ﬁndings. ChatGPT has brought advanced functions to digital writing but also intensiﬁed a sense of controversy in this area. For stu- dents, the worries focussed particularly on the unreliability of information it produced, fear of being accused of plagiarism if they used it and a concern about growing dependence on tech- nology. While often its use was claimed to be justiﬁed for its time and stress reduction this may have underestimated the overall impact on learning. Saving time on learning tasks may unin- tentionally remove signiﬁcant opportunities to learn. Many of the deeper ethical and societal issues such as around the exploitative way ChatGPT was developed were",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"this may have underestimated the overall impact on learning. Saving time on learning tasks may unin- tentionally remove signiﬁcant opportunities to learn. Many of the deeper ethical and societal issues such as around the exploitative way ChatGPT was developed were not fully under- stood. Yet it was clear that how writing was done digitally had become controversial. This could be seen as a beneﬁt of ChatGPT, in that a gradual inﬁltration of digital tools into writing was made more visible institutionally and the con- troversial dimensions of technology use in education brought to the fore. Using the model developed above we can point to strengths and weaknesses in students’ emergent generative AI literacy (Table 3). Overall our student interviewees showed signiﬁcant generative AI literacy in most areas, particularly when considering it was early days with their use of it and almost none of them had received support from their teachers or from their institution in understanding how to use it. The weakest area of development was probably appreciation of the societal impacts of generative AI. As instructors catch up with",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"almost none of them had received support from their teachers or from their institution in understanding how to use it. The weakest area of development was probably appreciation of the societal impacts of generative AI. As instructors catch up with students in understanding AI hopefully they can help students build up a more systematic understanding of pragmatic use of AI, a more reﬂective approach and a much more critical awareness of the social implications of AI. Conclusion While ChatGPT appears as a threat to longstanding practices in education, especially to some genres of academic writing such as essays, it can also be seen as productively bringing to the fore the controversial nature of AI writing technologies which were already creeping unacknowledged into common use. This context produces an opportunity for educators to actively engage with students in a discussion about how writing can be best supported. Our analysis points to areas of weakness in generative AI literacy that need to be strengthened through this process, such as the understanding of the need to select between apps, to have more sophisticated",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"how writing can be best supported. Our analysis points to areas of weakness in generative AI literacy that need to be strengthened through this process, such as the understanding of the need to select between apps, to have more sophisticated prompt engineering skills, to think more about bias in results, to be more reﬂective about its use’s impact on learning and have a much stronger appreciation of the societal impacts of generative AI. The paper is one of the ﬁrst to explore student use of generative AI in practice and discover in-depth their perception of its ben- eﬁts and worries about its drawbacks. It has also developed a framework of generative AI literacy as a way of assessing their use. This can be used to plan both institutional policy and instructor support by identifying gaps in AI literacy that need to be ﬁlled. For example, educational developers could use the fra- mework to facilitate discussions with teaching staff, aiding in the development of their AI literacy and enhancing their ability to teach AI literacy to students. In addition, universities could",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"to be ﬁlled. For example, educational developers could use the fra- mework to facilitate discussions with teaching staff, aiding in the development of their AI literacy and enhancing their ability to teach AI literacy to students. In addition, universities could apply the dimensions of the AI framework to formulate policies and provide concrete examples that guide learning and teaching practices. The framework also has the potential in evaluating student AI literacy. The paper has a number of limitations, pointing to where future research can build on its ﬁndings. Most participants in this study were using the free version of ChatGPT (3.5) and only a few used the paid version (4). We did not examine in detail the impact of using different versions. While ChatGPT was the main generative AI tool in use at the time of the study, there were Table 3 Participants’ Generative AI literacy. Generative AI literacy criteria Summary assessment of interviewees’ generative AI literacy 1a The individual can pick the right tool for the task, in the context of the proliferation of writing tools (including alternative generative",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"Table 3 Participants’ Generative AI literacy. Generative AI literacy criteria Summary assessment of interviewees’ generative AI literacy 1a The individual can pick the right tool for the task, in the context of the proliferation of writing tools (including alternative generative AI to ChatGPT) Students were aware of many AI writing assistants and tended to use them for speciﬁc tasks. 1b The individual learns to use the chosen tool effectively for a speciﬁc task A wide range of uses were being made to address individual needs. The observations did not show sophisticated prompt engineering. 1c The individual can interpret generative AI outputs critically, given an understanding of how they work and their limits There was awareness of problems of information accuracy, but less of bias. 2. Safety understanding: The individual can use generative AI safely There was good awareness of the privacy issue 3. Reﬂective understanding: The individual can assess and take action to manage the impacts of AI on their experience in the educational context Interviewees were concerned about issues such as the impact on their learning and to a",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,7,"privacy issue 3. Reﬂective understanding: The individual can assess and take action to manage the impacts of AI on their experience in the educational context Interviewees were concerned about issues such as the impact on their learning and to a lesser extent the social dimension of learning 4.Socio-ethical understanding: The individual understands the societal impacts of AI Interviewees showed limited awareness and tended to make light of these impacts 5. Contextual understanding: The individual understands how to use generative AI appropriately in a particular context and makes their own use explicit, as appropriate Concerns about appropriate uses were at the forefront of participants’ minds. Their call for institutional clariﬁcation reﬂected a desire to use tools in contextually appropriate ways HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x ARTICLE HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES COMMUNICATIONS | (2024) 11:482 | https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x 7",138
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,8,"others rapidly emerging in popularity. Future research would need to examine how choices of different apps were made and the impact of these choices on writing. Although the participants of this study come from a variety of countries, it is focused on one institution at a particular time. Given the speed of change in technology and educational policy and practice, it is likely that future research will identify rapid shifts in behaviour. But we emphasise the need to examine student writing practices in the context of signiﬁcant pre-existing use of digital writing tools. Our focus was on postgraduate students. We think less experienced students, such as undergraduates may be quicker and less dis- criminating in adopting the technology. As generative AI evolves there will be a need to update our deﬁnition of generative AI literacy and also to integrate it with notions such as algorithmic literacy, which point to the way that AI operates in rather hidden ways within the infrastructure. This research employed interviews and observations as its main data collection methods. These offer depth of insight but have",180
DOC_030,NEW11.pdf,10.1057/s41599-024-02904-x,8,"with notions such as algorithmic literacy, which point to the way that AI operates in rather hidden ways within the infrastructure. This research employed interviews and observations as its main data collection methods. These offer depth of insight but have less power of generalisability. Future studies could usefully seek to validate our ﬁndings through quantitative or mixed-methods approaches, such as surveys or experimental studies. Furthermore, future research could expand the scope of this study from AI literacy to the broader concept of writing digitisation, exploring the issue from other perspectives such as psychology and second language acquisition. Data availability The datasets analysed during the current study are not publicly available, but are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Received: 30 December 2023; Accepted: 28 February 2024;",128
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,1,"Discover Education Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 https://doi.org/10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5 RESEARCH Open Access Comparing ChatGPT and teacher feedback for EFL writing development Neda Naqdi Babaee1* ​*​C​o​r​r​e​s​p​o​n​d​e​n​c​e​:​ Neda Naqdi Babaee nedanaqdi01@gmail.com; Neda. Naghdi@ustmb.ac.ir 1Department of Foreign Languages, Faculty of Humanities and Management, Mazandaran University of Science and Technology, Babol, Iran © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,1,"included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n​s​ e​s​/​b​y​-​n​c​-​n​d​/​4​.​0​/​.​ Abstract This mixed-methods study compared ChatGPT-generated and teacher-written feedback conditions in university EFL writing and examined how learners perceived the revision value of each condition. A nonrandomized pretest-posttest design was combined with open-ended post-intervention responses. Participants were 46 upper- intermediate learners in a ChatGPT feedback condition (n = 25) or a teacher-written feedback condition (n = 21). Across four weeks, both conditions completed the same instructional sequence and two feedback-revision cycles. Writing was scored for task achievement, coherence and cohesion, lexical resource, grammatical accuracy, and an overall composite. Baseline-adjusted models showed a robust difference favoring the ChatGPT condition for overall writing, F(1, 43) = 16.86, p < .001, partial eta squared = 0.282. Unadjusted domain tests",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,1,"achievement, coherence and cohesion, lexical resource, grammatical accuracy, and an overall composite. Baseline-adjusted models showed a robust difference favoring the ChatGPT condition for overall writing, F(1, 43) = 16.86, p < .001, partial eta squared = 0.282. Unadjusted domain tests favored the ChatGPT condition for task achievement, coherence and cohesion, and lexical resource; after Holm adjustment across the four domains, only task achievement remained below 0.05. Grammatical accuracy did not differ between conditions. Learners described ChatGPT feedback as immediate and elaborated, particularly for content, organization, and vocabulary, while also reporting concerns about trust and contextual fit. Teacher-written feedback was perceived as selective, context-sensitive, and authoritative. The findings support a condition-level advantage in overall writing and suggest different perceived revision affordances, but they do not isolate feedback source from differences in feedback elaboration and immediacy. Keywords ChatGPT, Teacher feedback, EFL writing, Written corrective feedback, Generative artificial intelligence, Mixed-methods research ​1 Introduction Feedback is a central resource in second language writing pedagogy because writing development depends on opportunities to notice gaps, interpret evaluative informa­ tion, revise drafts, and gain control over language",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,1,"Written corrective feedback, Generative artificial intelligence, Mixed-methods research ​1 Introduction Feedback is a central resource in second language writing pedagogy because writing development depends on opportunities to notice gaps, interpret evaluative informa­ tion, revise drafts, and gain control over language and discourse. Writing in a second or foreign language requires writers to plan ideas, organize meanings, manage linguis­ tic resources, address audience expectations, and revise in relation to genre and task demands [4, 18, 23, 24]. In EFL contexts, limited exposure to English, large classes, and restricted opportunities for individualized guidance can intensify these demands.",95
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,2,"Page 2 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 Written feedback can therefore support development when it is timely, understandable, specific, and connected to revision [6, 15, 22, 25, 41]. The emergence of generative artificial intelligence has added a new layer to debates about feedback in L2 writing. Unlike earlier automated writing evaluation systems that often focused on grammar, mechanics, scoring, or limited feedback categories, large lan­ guage models such as ChatGPT can generate extended comments, reformulate learner texts, suggest alternative phrasings, respond to prompts, and provide feedback across content, organization, vocabulary, and grammar [3, 27, 47]. These capabilities have made ChatGPT especially relevant to writing instruction, where students often need more feedback than teachers can reasonably provide. Recent studies have reported that Chat­ GPT can support writing development, writing motivation, learner engagement, aca­ demic text revision, and feedback interaction when it is used within structured learning conditions rather than as an unrestricted text generator [9, 26, 28, 32, 42, 45, 46, 53, 54]. At the same time, ChatGPT feedback may be generic, redundant, overly extensive, inconsistent, or insufficiently sensitive",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,2,"when it is used within structured learning conditions rather than as an unrestricted text generator [9, 26, 28, 32, 42, 45, 46, 53, 54]. At the same time, ChatGPT feedback may be generic, redundant, overly extensive, inconsistent, or insufficiently sensitive to task and learner context [2, 20, 31, 43, 50]. Teacher-written feedback can draw on course goals, classroom history, learner needs, and the interpersonal dimensions of writing development [17, 25, 30]. Comparative evidence has therefore not established a uniform advantage for either source. A more specific question concerns whether classroom feedback conditions produce different profiles across content, organization, vocabulary, grammar, and overall writing, and how learners interpret the revision opportunities that each condition provides. The present study addresses this problem by combining baseline-adjusted outcome analysis with learner accounts of revision value. Its contribution lies in distinguishing a robust overall outcome from more tentative domain patterns and in interpreting the comparison at the level of the implemented feedback conditions rather than treating source as the only dif­ ference between them. 2 Literature review 2.1 Feedback and second language writing development Second",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,2,"from more tentative domain patterns and in interpreting the comparison at the level of the implemented feedback conditions rather than treating source as the only dif­ ference between them. 2 Literature review 2.1 Feedback and second language writing development Second language writing research has consistently emphasized that writing is not a sim­ ple product of grammatical knowledge but a complex process involving idea generation, rhetorical organization, linguistic formulation, revision, and interaction with feedback. Cognitive models of writing describe composing as a recursive process in which writ­ ers move between planning, translating, reviewing, and monitoring [18, 23]. In L2 writ­ ing contexts, these processes are shaped by language proficiency, genre knowledge, task familiarity, and access to instructional mediation [24, 33]. Sociocultural perspectives further emphasize that writing development is mediated by tools, texts, teachers, peers, and institutional expectations, making feedback a central form of pedagogical mediation [29, 49]. Written corrective feedback has been one of the most extensively examined areas in L2 writing research. Although early debates questioned the value of correction, subse­ quent research has shown that feedback can contribute to",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,2,"form of pedagogical mediation [29, 49]. Written corrective feedback has been one of the most extensively examined areas in L2 writing research. Although early debates questioned the value of correction, subse­ quent research has shown that feedback can contribute to language development and writing improvement when it is appropriate to learner needs, clearly communicated, and connected to opportunities for revision [5–7, 12, 15, 16]. Feedback may be direct or indirect, focused or unfocused, metalinguistic or reformulative, and each type may sup­ port different forms of learner engagement [12, 15]. Feedback on grammar may support",94
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,3,"Page 3 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 noticing and correction of local errors, while feedback on content, organization, and argumentation may require deeper rhetorical understanding and more complex revision decisions [6, 30]. Current feedback theory views feedback as a process rather than as information deliv­ ered from teacher to learner. Effective feedback helps learners understand goals, com­ pare current performance with desired standards, and take action to improve future work [22, 36, 41]. Feedback literacy research has extended this view by arguing that learners must understand, evaluate, and use feedback actively if feedback is to support learning [11, 52]. In L2 writing, engagement with feedback involves behavioral engage­ ment, such as revision; cognitive engagement, such as noticing, reasoning, and decision- making; and affective engagement, such as trust, confidence, frustration, or resistance [13, 21, 55, 56]. This view is especially relevant to AI-generated feedback because the availability of feedback does not guarantee writing development. Learners must still interpret, evaluate, and act on comments in ways that support learning. Teacher-written feedback remains a core component of L2 writing pedagogy because",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,3,"especially relevant to AI-generated feedback because the availability of feedback does not guarantee writing development. Learners must still interpret, evaluate, and act on comments in ways that support learning. Teacher-written feedback remains a core component of L2 writing pedagogy because teachers can integrate linguistic, rhetorical, contextual, and interpersonal knowledge. It can support accuracy, organization, genre awareness, and reader-oriented communica­ tion when it is specific and connected to revision [14, 17, 25, 30]. Teacher feedback is nevertheless constrained by workload, time, inconsistency, and the difficulty of provid­ ing detailed individual guidance across multiple drafts [15, 30]. These constraints have encouraged interest in peer feedback, automated writing evaluation, and AI-supported feedback systems [39, 40, 51, 55]. 2.2 AI-generated feedback and L2 writing Automated writing evaluation has historically offered immediate feedback and oppor­ tunities for repeated revision, but earlier systems were often criticized for limited accu­ racy, surface-level focus, and insufficient sensitivity to meaning and context [40, 51]. Generative AI has changed the feedback landscape because it can produce flexible and extended responses to learner writing. ChatGPT can comment on content, organization, lexical",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,3,"for limited accu­ racy, surface-level focus, and insufficient sensitivity to meaning and context [40, 51]. Generative AI has changed the feedback landscape because it can produce flexible and extended responses to learner writing. ChatGPT can comment on content, organization, lexical choice, grammar, style, tone, and clarity, and it can also generate model revisions or explanations when prompted [3, 27, 47]. This flexibility has led to increased interest in using ChatGPT as a formative writing tool, particularly in EFL and ESL contexts where individualized feedback is often limited. Empirical studies have reported generally positive effects of ChatGPT-supported instruction on L2 writing performance and learner-related outcomes. ChatGPT-based feedback has been associated with idea development, organization, vocabulary, gram­ mar, and overall writing in university contexts [1, 19, 32]. Experimental and quasi- experimental studies have also reported improvements in writing quality, motivation, engagement, and technology acceptance when ChatGPT is integrated into structured instruction [9, 42, 45, 46, 54]. Work with graduate and advanced multilingual writers further shows that learners may use ChatGPT productively for academic revision when they maintain agency, evaluate suggestions critically, and",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,3,"technology acceptance when ChatGPT is integrated into structured instruction [9, 42, 45, 46, 54]. Work with graduate and advanced multilingual writers further shows that learners may use ChatGPT productively for academic revision when they maintain agency, evaluate suggestions critically, and preserve authorial voice [26, 28, 44]. Comparative studies show that AI feedback is not uniformly superior to human feedback. ChatGPT can distribute extensive comments across several writing dimen­ sions, whereas teacher feedback is often more selective, personalized, and contextually",79
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,4,"Page 4 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 grounded [20]. GPT-assisted written corrective feedback may be accurate and exten­ sive but also redundant, variable, and inclined toward local or metalinguistic forms [31]. Human feedback can outperform ChatGPT feedback in clarity, prioritization, accuracy, and supportive tone, although ChatGPT can perform well when feedback is organized around explicit criteria [43]. Findings also vary by writing dimension. AI support has been associated with content and organization [32, 34], lexical development [37], and criteria-based argument feedback [50], but these affordances do not establish equal effectiveness for grammar or other local features. The literature also indicates that the educational value of AI feedback depends on how learners use it. Studies of learner perceptions show that students value ChatGPT for speed, accessibility, language support, idea generation, organization, and confi­ dence-building, but they also identify risks such as unreliable information, overreliance, loss of critical thinking, and academic integrity concerns [8, 35, 38, 53]. Active learn­ ing research has shown that students can become more critical of ChatGPT when they are guided to evaluate AI-generated writing against",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,4,"such as unreliable information, overreliance, loss of critical thinking, and academic integrity concerns [8, 35, 38, 53]. Active learn­ ing research has shown that students can become more critical of ChatGPT when they are guided to evaluate AI-generated writing against academic criteria such as evidence, critical insight, balance, objectivity, and referencing [48]. This suggests that AI feedback needs to be accompanied by instruction in feedback literacy and AI literacy. 2.3 The present study Research indicates that ChatGPT can support writing development and that AI-gener­ ated feedback has both affordances and limitations. Two gaps nevertheless remain. First, intervention studies often summarize writing performance globally even though feed­ back may relate differently to task fulfillment, organization, vocabulary, and grammati­ cal accuracy. Comparative studies have documented differences in feedback coverage and quality [20, 31, 43], but dimension-specific learner outcomes remain less consis­ tently examined. Second, outcome comparisons are rarely interpreted alongside learner accounts of how comments become revision decisions. Studies of engagement empha­ size that feedback matters through learner evaluation and uptake rather than through delivery alone [21, 39, 55]. The present study",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,4,"examined. Second, outcome comparisons are rarely interpreted alongside learner accounts of how comments become revision decisions. Studies of engagement empha­ size that feedback matters through learner evaluation and uptake rather than through delivery alone [21, 39, 55]. The present study therefore compared baseline-adjusted post­ test outcomes across four analytic dimensions and an overall composite, used alternative analyses to evaluate robustness, and examined learner-perceived revision value. The fol­ lowing questions guided the study: 1. Do baseline-adjusted posttest outcomes differ between ChatGPT-generated and teacher-written feedback conditions for overall writing quality and the four analytic writing dimensions? 2. How do learners perceive the revision value, usefulness, and limitations of ChatGPT- generated and teacher-written feedback? 3 Method 3.1 Research design The study adopted a mixed-methods design combining a nonrandomized two-con­ dition pretest-posttest comparison with qualitative learner accounts. The quantita­ tive strand examined whether posttest writing outcomes differed after adjustment for corresponding pretest scores. The qualitative strand examined perceived usefulness, revision value, and limitations after the intervention. Integration occurred during inter­ pretation: learner accounts were used to contextualize, rather than causally explain,",177
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,4,"writing outcomes differed after adjustment for corresponding pretest scores. The qualitative strand examined perceived usefulness, revision value, and limitations after the intervention. Integration occurred during inter­ pretation: learner accounts were used to contextualize, rather than causally explain,",37
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,5,"Page 5 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 the quantitative pattern. Feedback was operationally defined as written information on learner drafts intended to guide revision. ChatGPT-generated feedback comprised AI- produced comments organized around task achievement, coherence and cohesion, lexi­ cal resource, and grammatical accuracy. Teacher-written feedback comprised instructor comments addressing the same domains. Because timing, amount, specificity, and elab­ oration are properties of the implemented conditions, the comparison estimates the difference between two classroom feedback conditions and does not isolate source as a single causal component. 3.2 Participants and context Participants were 46 upper-intermediate EFL university students enrolled in academic writing courses at a large public university. They were 19–24 years old, majored in non- English disciplines, and took English as a required course. Course placement procedures established the reported proficiency level. The ChatGPT feedback condition included 25 learners, and the teacher-written feedback condition included 21 learners. Allocation was nonrandom, consistent with the quasi-experimental classroom design. Both condi­ tions were taught by the same instructor and followed the same instructional sequence, writing tasks, classroom activities, and two-cycle revision schedule.",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,5,"and the teacher-written feedback condition included 21 learners. Allocation was nonrandom, consistent with the quasi-experimental classroom design. Both condi­ tions were taught by the same instructor and followed the same instructional sequence, writing tasks, classroom activities, and two-cycle revision schedule. This arrangement reduced instructional differences without implying that feedback source was the only feature distinguishing the conditions. Participation was voluntary, and written informed consent was obtained before data collection. 3.3 Instruments and data sources Quantitative data came from pretest and posttest writing tasks. Each task required an approximately 200-word academic paragraph in response to an argumentative or expository prompt aligned with the course syllabus. Two additional tasks with compa­ rable genre expectations were used during the intervention. Writing was evaluated with adapted IELTS-based analytic descriptors. Task achievement represented fulfillment of the prompt, relevance, and development of ideas. Coherence and cohesion represented logical progression, organization, and the use of cohesive resources. Lexical resource represented range, precision, and appropriateness of vocabulary. Grammatical accuracy represented the range and control of sentence structures. The four domain scores were averaged to form the overall writing",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,5,"represented logical progression, organization, and the use of cohesive resources. Lexical resource represented range, precision, and appropriateness of vocabulary. Grammatical accuracy represented the range and control of sentence structures. The four domain scores were averaged to form the overall writing score. Qualitative data consisted of open-ended post-intervention responses concerning useful feedback features, difficulties in apply­ ing comments, perceived limitations, and revision decisions. No formal interviews were conducted. 3.4 Feedback procedures The intervention lasted four weeks and comprised two complete feedback-revision cycles. Both conditions completed the same writing tasks, received feedback dur­ ing the same instructional sequence, revised their drafts, and resubmitted them. In the ChatGPT condition, electronically submitted drafts received individualized AI- generated comments produced through a standardized instructional frame. The frame requested comments on strengths, problems, and possible revisions across task achieve­ ment, coherence and cohesion, lexical resource, and grammatical accuracy. Learners were instructed to evaluate suggestions rather than accept them automatically. In the",155
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,6,"Page 6 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 teacher-written condition, the instructor used marginal and end comments to address the same four domains, including idea relevance and clarity, organization, lexical appro­ priateness, and grammatical accuracy. The conditions were aligned in targeted con­ structs and revision opportunities, but the amount, immediacy, and elaboration of feedback were not experimentally equated. No peer feedback or additional writing-sup­ port tools were used during the treatment. Table 1 summarizes the common and distinct features of the conditions. 3.5 Data analysis Two trained raters with graduate qualifications in applied linguistics independently scored the pretest and posttest compositions using the adapted analytic descriptors. Calibration focused on shared interpretation of the four domains and the application of band scores to the paragraph-length tasks. Scripts were anonymized, and raters were blind to condition and testing phase. Discrepancies exceeding one band were discussed and resolved before the final analysis scores were produced. Quantitative analysis proceeded in three layers. Descriptive statistics summarized pretest, posttest, and gain scores. The principal inferential analyses were ordinary least- squares ANCOVA models in which each",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,6,"one band were discussed and resolved before the final analysis scores were produced. Quantitative analysis proceeded in three layers. Descriptive statistics summarized pretest, posttest, and gain scores. The principal inferential analyses were ordinary least- squares ANCOVA models in which each posttest outcome was predicted by feedback condition with the corresponding pretest score as covariate. Actual baseline compari­ sons were reported, and condition-by-pretest interactions tested homogeneity of regres­ sion slopes. Partial eta squared quantified adjusted condition effects. Because five related outcomes were examined, Holm adjustment was applied as a familywise sensi­ tivity analysis across the four domain-specific tests; overall writing was treated as the composite summary outcome. Welch tests, Mann-Whitney tests, gain-score compari­ sons, bootstrap confidence intervals, and Hedges g values served as robustness checks rather than parallel primary tests. The gain analyses were interpreted cautiously because pretest-gain associations can reflect mathematical coupling and regression to the mean. The exploratory responder analysis was removed because its thresholds did not repre­ sent validated criteria for meaningful change. The author analyzed the open-ended responses using reflexive thematic analysis [10]. Analysis involved repeated reading,",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,6,"mathematical coupling and regression to the mean. The exploratory responder analysis was removed because its thresholds did not repre­ sent validated criteria for meaningful change. The author analyzed the open-ended responses using reflexive thematic analysis [10]. Analysis involved repeated reading, initial coding of perceived usefulness, revision value, difficulties, limitations, and learner decision-making, comparison of codes across con­ ditions, and iterative development of broader themes. Candidate themes were checked against the full set of responses and revised to preserve differences within and between Table 1 Core features of the two feedback conditions Feature ChatGPT-generated feedback Teacher-written feedback Duration and cycles Four weeks; two feedback-revision cycles Four weeks; two feedback-revision cycles Writing input Same approximately 200-word course tasks Same approximately 200-word course tasks Target domains Task achievement; coherence and cohesion; lexical resource; grammatical accuracy Same four domains Feedback form Individualized electronic AI comments Instructor marginal and end comments Revision orientation Strengths, problems, and possible revisions Prioritized problems and revision guidance Learner action Evaluate comments, revise, and resubmit Interpret comments, revise, and resubmit Additional support No peer feedback or additional writing tools No",180
