DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,8,"Page 8 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 coherence and cohesion, t = 1.07, p = .292; lexical resource, t = 1.17, p = .249; grammati­ cal accuracy, t = -0.21, p = .838; and overall writing, t = 1.49, p = .143. At posttest, mean scores favored the ChatGPT condition for all outcomes, with the smallest difference for grammatical accuracy. Gain means also favored the ChatGPT condition, although uncertainty was greater for the individual domains. Table 3 reports the baseline-adjusted models. Homogeneity-of-regression-slope tests were nonsignificant for all outcomes, F values = 0.38–2.39, p values = 0.130-0.540. The adjusted condition difference was strongest and most stable for overall writing, F(1, 43) = 16.86, p < .001, partial eta squared = 0.282. Unadjusted domain-level p val­ ues favored the ChatGPT condition for task achievement, coherence and cohesion, and lexical resource, but not grammatical accuracy. After Holm adjustment across the four domains, task achievement remained below 0.05 (adjusted p = .031), whereas lex­ ical resource (adjusted p = .057) and coherence and cohesion (adjusted p = .081) did not. These latter",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,8,"but not grammatical accuracy. After Holm adjustment across the four domains, task achievement remained below 0.05 (adjusted p = .031), whereas lex­ ical resource (adjusted p = .057) and coherence and cohesion (adjusted p = .081) did not. These latter domain patterns are therefore interpreted as suggestive rather than confirmatory. Table 4 presents the robustness checks. Raw posttest Welch and Mann-Whitney tests favored the ChatGPT condition for task achievement, coherence and cohesion, lexical resource, and overall writing, and Hedges g values ranged from 0.69 to 1.27 for these outcomes. Gain-score evidence was more conservative: only overall writing reached the conventional 0.05 boundary, and the bootstrap interval excluded zero only for overall writing. The convergence of adjusted, raw-posttest, nonparametric, and gain analyses therefore supports the overall writing difference most clearly. Evidence for the individual Table 3 Baseline-adjusted posttest outcomes by feedback condition Outcome Ad­ justed M ChatGPT Adjusted M teacher Difference [bootstrap 95% CI] F(1, 43) p Partial eta squared Holm p for do­ mains Task achievement 5.42 4.36 1.06 [0.32, 1.81] 7.82 0.008 0.154 0.031 Coherence and cohesion 5.39 4.58",180
