DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,9,"Page 9 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 domains is less uniform, and no analytic approach indicated a reliable grammatical- accuracy difference. Taken together, the quantitative results identify a robust condition-level difference in overall writing and a differentiated domain profile. Task achievement retained support after adjustment for four domain comparisons, whereas coherence and cohesion and lexical resource showed favorable but less conclusive patterns. Grammatical accuracy did not differ reliably between conditions. 4.2 Learner-perceived revision value of ChatGPT-generated and teacher-written feedback Learner accounts indicated that the two conditions supported revision in different ways. Two themes were developed: actionable revision support and trust, control, and feed­ back authority. These themes contextualize how learners experienced the conditions; they do not establish that the reported perceptions caused the quantitative differences. Learners in the ChatGPT condition commonly described the feedback as immediately actionable. They emphasized content development, organization, transitions, and vocab­ ulary, particularly when comments connected an identified weakness with a possible revision. S8 illustrated how elaboration made a content problem easier to address, while S14 emphasized a shift from grammar-only checking toward organization:",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,9,"emphasized content development, organization, transitions, and vocab­ ulary, particularly when comments connected an identified weakness with a possible revision. S8 illustrated how elaboration made a content problem easier to address, while S14 emphasized a shift from grammar-only checking toward organization: When I received the ChatGPT feedback, it did not only say that my paragraph was weak. It showed me that my example was not enough and suggested how I could explain it more clearly. This helped me revise the content, not only the grammar (S8, ChatGPT group) For me, the most useful part was organization. It told me where the connection between sentences was not clear, and I could add linking words or change the order of ideas. Before that, I only checked grammar (S14, ChatGPT group) These accounts indicate that some learners experienced ChatGPT feedback as support for higher-order revision. The comments directed attention to idea development, logi­ cal sequence, and reader comprehension. This perceived affordance offers one possible interpretation of the task-achievement pattern, but the study did not analyze feedback uptake or draft-to-draft revision operations and therefore does",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,9,"revision. The comments directed attention to idea development, logi­ cal sequence, and reader comprehension. This perceived affordance offers one possible interpretation of the task-achievement pattern, but the study did not analyze feedback uptake or draft-to-draft revision operations and therefore does not establish a causal mechanism. Lexical development formed a related part of actionable revision support. Learn­ ers described receiving alternatives for repeated or imprecise wording while retaining responsibility for judging appropriateness. S3 emphasized the value of alternatives for academic expression, whereas S19 also identified the risk of suggestions that exceeded the intended level: Sometimes I repeated the same words many times, but ChatGPT gave me other choices. I did not use all of them, but I learned how to make the paragraph sound more academic (S3, ChatGPT group) It helped me with vocabulary because it suggested better words, but I had to check if the word was really suitable for my sentence. Some words were too formal for my level (S19, ChatGPT group) The lexical accounts combine availability with selection: the tool expanded the range of options, but learners still",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,9,"had to check if the word was really suitable for my sentence. Some words were too formal for my level (S19, ChatGPT group) The lexical accounts combine availability with selection: the tool expanded the range of options, but learners still evaluated whether suggestions suited the sentence and task.",48
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,10,"Page 10 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 These perceptions are consistent with, but do not prove the mechanism behind, the favorable lexical pattern in the adjusted and raw-posttest analyses. Learners in the teacher-written condition described actionable support as more selec­ tive and focused. They valued the prioritization of important problems but sometimes wanted more explanation or examples. S27 illustrated the distinction between identi­ fying an underdeveloped idea and knowing how to expand it, while S35 described the same issue for vocabulary: The teacher comment was useful because I understood which part was not good, but sometimes I wanted more explanation. For example, when it said develop this idea, I knew there was a problem, but I was not sure how much I should add (S27, teacher feedback group) Teacher feedback helped me focus on the important mistakes. It was not too much. But for vocabulary, I sometimes needed examples because the comment only showed that the word was not suitable (S35, teacher feedback group) These accounts portray teacher-written feedback as targeted rather than expansive. Par­ ticipants appreciated its",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,10,"was not too much. But for vocabulary, I sometimes needed examples because the comment only showed that the word was not suitable (S35, teacher feedback group) These accounts portray teacher-written feedback as targeted rather than expansive. Par­ ticipants appreciated its prioritization and manageable quantity, although some found concise comments less immediately actionable for content expansion or lexical refine­ ment. The qualitative comparison therefore identifies a perceived difference in revision support without attributing score differences to feedback quantity alone. The second theme concerned trust, control, and feedback authority. ChatGPT feed­ back was valued for access and detail but was not treated as uniformly authoritative. S11 described suggestions that altered intended meaning, and S21 emphasized the need to evaluate grammatical advice in context: ChatGPT gave many suggestions, and most of them were helpful, but I could not trust everything. Sometimes it changed my sentence in a way that was not exactly my idea (S11, ChatGPT group) It was useful because I could get feedback quickly, but I had to read carefully. Some suggestions were good for grammar, but the meaning became different,",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,10,"my sentence in a way that was not exactly my idea (S11, ChatGPT group) It was useful because I could get feedback quickly, but I had to read carefully. Some suggestions were good for grammar, but the meaning became different, so I did not use them (S21, ChatGPT group) These accounts indicate that ChatGPT feedback required evaluative engagement. Learners considered meaning, task fit, and proficiency appropriateness before deciding whether to revise. The educational value of the condition therefore depended partly on selective use rather than automatic acceptance. Immediacy also shaped perceived control. Some learners reported that prompt access to comments allowed revision while their intended meaning remained salient. S6 linked speed with comparison of draft and suggestion, while S17 associated independent checking with confidence: The fast feedback helped me because I could revise immediately. I did not forget what I wanted to say in the paragraph, and I could compare my first version with the suggestions (S6, ChatGPT group) I felt more independent because I could check my writing and revise before asking the teacher. It gave me confidence to",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,10,"wanted to say in the paragraph, and I could compare my first version with the suggestions (S6, ChatGPT group) I felt more independent because I could check my writing and revise before asking the teacher. It gave me confidence to continue writing (S17, ChatGPT group)",45
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,11,"Page 11 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 These reports suggest that immediate access supported revision momentum for some learners. They do not, however, demonstrate that immediacy alone produced the out­ come difference because immediacy was embedded within the broader ChatGPT feed­ back condition. Trust operated differently in the teacher-written condition. Learners valued comments from an instructor familiar with the course, task, and expected performance. S31 con­ nected trust with course knowledge, and S40 connected teacher marking with instruc­ tional relevance: When the teacher gave feedback, I trusted it because she knew what we studied and what the task wanted. Even if the comment was short, I felt it was important (S31, teacher feedback group) Teacher feedback was more direct for me. I knew that if the teacher marked some­ thing, I should pay attention because it was related to our lesson and our writing score (S40, teacher feedback group) These accounts show that teacher-written feedback carried contextual and institutional authority. Its perceived value derived less from quantity than from its relationship to course goals and assessment expectations. The",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,11,"to our lesson and our writing score (S40, teacher feedback group) These accounts show that teacher-written feedback carried contextual and institutional authority. Its perceived value derived less from quantity than from its relationship to course goals and assessment expectations. The authority of teacher-written feedback could also narrow the range of revisions learners considered. S29 described concentrating on marked locations, while S38 described following comments directly rather than exploring alternatives: With teacher feedback, I usually corrected the part she marked. I did not change many other parts because I thought the main problems were already shown (S29, teacher feedback group) The teacher feedback was clear, but I mostly followed it directly. I did not try many different versions because I wanted to correct what was marked (S38, teacher feed­ back group) The two conditions were thus perceived to organize revision differently. ChatGPT feed­ back offered a wider set of alternatives, whereas teacher-written feedback prioritized selected targets. This contrast concerns learner experience and should not be inter­ preted as evidence that either approach is universally preferable. Taken together, the qualitative findings indicate",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,11,"ChatGPT feed­ back offered a wider set of alternatives, whereas teacher-written feedback prioritized selected targets. This contrast concerns learner experience and should not be inter­ preted as evidence that either approach is universally preferable. Taken together, the qualitative findings indicate distinct perceived affordances and constraints. ChatGPT feedback was associated with immediacy, elaboration, and expanded revision options, together with concerns about trust and contextual fit. Teacher-written feedback was associated with prioritization, course alignment, and authority, together with requests for greater elaboration. These accounts contextual­ ize the quantitative profile while remaining analytically separate from claims about causation. 5 Discussion The study identified a robust difference favoring the ChatGPT feedback condition for overall writing and a more qualified domain-specific pattern. Task achievement remained supported after Holm adjustment across the four analytic domains; coherence and cohesion and lexical resource favored the ChatGPT condition in unadjusted and raw-posttest analyses but were not confirmed by gain scores or familywise adjustment.",154
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,12,"Page 12 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 Grammatical accuracy did not differ. Learner accounts further indicated different per­ ceived revision affordances: ChatGPT feedback was associated with elaboration and immediate alternatives, whereas teacher-written feedback was associated with prioriti­ zation and contextual authority. The findings therefore concern the implemented feed­ back conditions and not feedback source in isolation. The most defensible domain-specific result concerns task achievement. Learners described comments that prompted further explanation, idea development, and atten­ tion to prompt fulfillment. This pattern is compatible with intervention research show­ ing that generative AI can support higher-order revision when feedback is organized around explicit writing criteria [32, 34, 43]. Coherence and cohesion showed a favor­ able adjusted estimate before familywise correction, but the gain analysis was inconclu­ sive. The qualitative accounts therefore provide a plausible interpretation of the pattern rather than evidence of the mechanism that produced it. Lexical resource followed a similar but less conclusive pattern. Learners reported using alternative expressions and evaluating whether suggested vocabulary preserved meaning and matched the task. Such activity is consistent with research on AI-supported",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,12,"evidence of the mechanism that produced it. Lexical resource followed a similar but less conclusive pattern. Learners reported using alternative expressions and evaluating whether suggested vocabulary preserved meaning and matched the task. Such activity is consistent with research on AI-supported lexical experimentation and revision engagement [28, 37]. However, the lexical result did not remain below 0.05 after Holm adjustment and was not corroborated by the gain com­ parison. It should therefore be understood as a direction for further investigation rather than a settled advantage. The absence of a reliable grammatical-accuracy difference is practically important. Both conditions directed attention to grammar, and both groups improved descrip­ tively, but neither adjusted nor sensitivity analyses identified a condition advantage. Short interventions may be insufficient to distinguish grammatical development, which can require repeated practice and sustained attention to recurring error patterns [6, 16, 17]. The result does not establish complementary roles; it indicates only that the imple­ mented ChatGPT condition offered no clear grammatical advantage over teacher-writ­ ten feedback. The qualitative findings add information about learner evaluation of feedback. Par­ ticipants described balancing the",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,12,"The result does not establish complementary roles; it indicates only that the imple­ mented ChatGPT condition offered no clear grammatical advantage over teacher-writ­ ten feedback. The qualitative findings add information about learner evaluation of feedback. Par­ ticipants described balancing the accessibility and elaboration of ChatGPT comments against concerns about meaning, level, and contextual fit. This pattern aligns with research emphasizing active engagement, trust, and selective uptake in AI-assisted revi­ sion [26, 28, 39, 45]. Teacher-written feedback, by contrast, was trusted for its connec­ tion to course expectations. These accounts demonstrate perceived differences between the conditions, but the study did not trace individual comments through draft revi­ sions and therefore cannot identify which feedback feature produced a particular score change. The comparison also shows why source labels alone are insufficient. The ChatGPT condition combined AI generation with rapid access, extensive comments, and revision alternatives, whereas the teacher-written condition was more selective and contextually situated. Consequently, the estimated difference may reflect several bundled features, including amount, timing, specificity, and elaboration. This interpretation is consistent with comparative work showing systematic differences in the",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,12,"and revision alternatives, whereas the teacher-written condition was more selective and contextually situated. Consequently, the estimated difference may reflect several bundled features, including amount, timing, specificity, and elaboration. This interpretation is consistent with comparative work showing systematic differences in the coverage and prioritization of AI and teacher feedback [20, 31]. The study does not test complementarity because it includes no combined ChatGPT- plus-teacher condition. It instead shows that learners attributed different strengths and",73
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,13,"Page 13 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 limitations to the two conditions. A combined or sequenced approach is therefore a ped­ agogical proposition for future testing rather than an empirical conclusion of the present comparison. The contribution is twofold. Empirically, the study separates a robust overall writing difference from domain estimates that vary across analytic approaches. Conceptually, it demonstrates the importance of interpreting AI feedback as an implemented classroom condition rather than as an isolated technological source. This distinction avoids attrib­ uting outcomes to ChatGPT when immediacy, elaboration, revision alternatives, and learner evaluation are also involved. The findings support targeted rather than universal use of AI-generated feedback. ChatGPT feedback may be especially useful when learners need elaborated options for task fulfillment, organization, or lexical revision, provided that instruction requires them to evaluate suggestions against meaning and task criteria. The null grammatical-accu­ racy result indicates that AI feedback should not be assumed to replace explicit grammar instruction or sustained corrective-feedback practice. Teacher-written feedback remains valuable for prioritizing problems and aligning revision with course expectations. These implications follow from the",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,13,"The null grammatical-accu­ racy result indicates that AI feedback should not be assumed to replace explicit grammar instruction or sustained corrective-feedback practice. Teacher-written feedback remains valuable for prioritizing problems and aligning revision with course expectations. These implications follow from the observed outcome profile and learner perceptions; they do not require a claim that the two sources are empirically complementary. 6 Limitations Several boundaries qualify the findings. The conditions were nonrandomized and included a modest sample from one university, so baseline adjustment cannot remove all possible selection or classroom influences. The four-week intervention and absence of a delayed posttest limit conclusions about durability. The two conditions were aligned in instructional sequence and target domains but not experimentally equated for feedback amount, immediacy, specificity, or elaboration; the results therefore compare bundled feedback conditions rather than source alone. The qualitative strand examined learner perceptions rather than observed feedback uptake, and draft-to-draft revision opera­ tions were not coded. These boundaries support cautious generalization while leaving the robust overall writing difference and the reported learner experiences intact. 7 Conclusion This mixed-methods study compared ChatGPT-generated and",180
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,13,"perceptions rather than observed feedback uptake, and draft-to-draft revision opera­ tions were not coded. These boundaries support cautious generalization while leaving the robust overall writing difference and the reported learner experiences intact. 7 Conclusion This mixed-methods study compared ChatGPT-generated and teacher-written feedback conditions in university EFL writing. The clearest quantitative result was a baseline- adjusted difference in overall writing favoring the ChatGPT condition. Task achieve­ ment remained supported after familywise adjustment across four domains, whereas coherence and cohesion and lexical resource showed favorable but less conclusive pat­ terns. Grammatical accuracy did not differ. Learners associated ChatGPT feedback with immediate, elaborated revision options and teacher-written feedback with priori­ tization and contextual authority. These findings demonstrate different outcome profiles and perceived revision affordances, but they do not isolate feedback source or establish that the two approaches are complementary. For practice, AI-generated feedback is best treated as a structured revision resource whose suggestions require learner evaluation, while teacher feedback retains a distinct role in prioritization and course alignment. Further research should compare equated feedback conditions, examine actual uptake",175
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,13,"is best treated as a structured revision resource whose suggestions require learner evaluation, while teacher feedback retains a distinct role in prioritization and course alignment. Further research should compare equated feedback conditions, examine actual uptake",35
DOC_031,NEW13.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-02329-5,14,"Page 14 of 16 Babaee Discover Education (2026) 5:1211 across drafts, and test whether sequenced or combined feedback produces advantages beyond either condition alone. Author contributions NNB: Conceptualization, methodology, investigation, formal analysis, writing - original draft, and writing - review and editing. Funding Not applicable. Data availability The datasets used and/or analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval All procedures performed in this study involving human participants were in accordance with the ethical standards of the institutional research committee and with the 1964 Helsinki Declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. The study was approved by the ethics committee of Mazandaran University of Science and Technology (MUST-2025-812/20). Consent to participate Written informed consent was obtained from all participants before participation. Consent to publish Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Received: 18 May 2026 / Accepted: 13 October 2026",154
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,1,"Original Research Article SAGE Open Vol. 16(4): 1–20 © The Author(s) 2026 DOI: 10.1177/21582440261497008 journals.sagepub.com/home/sgo Aligning AI and Human Feedback in EFL Writing: A Mixed-Methods Comparison of ChatGPT, Peer, and Teacher Feedback Jui-Jung Tsao1 and Chih-yang Liu2 Abstract This mixed-methods study compared three feedback modalities—ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences—in an EFL writing course. Twenty-five second-year university English majors participated in a semester-long intervention, completing three writing assignments and receiving feedback according to their group. Quantitative analyses of the 22 participants with complete pretest and posttest data indicated significant overall improvement, with statistically significant gains observed only in the ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences group; between-group comparisons showed no significant differences. Qualitative analysis of 22 complete open-ended surveys revealed that each feedback method offered distinct advantages and challenges across four dimensions: satisfaction, motivation, engagement, and perceived effectiveness. ChatGPT feedback was valued for its immediacy and technical accuracy, but was seen as impersonal and less supportive of higher-order skills. Peer feedback",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,1,"each feedback method offered distinct advantages and challenges across four dimensions: satisfaction, motivation, engagement, and perceived effectiveness. ChatGPT feedback was valued for its immediacy and technical accuracy, but was seen as impersonal and less supportive of higher-order skills. Peer feedback was perceived as supporting collaboration and motivation, although inconsistencies in quality were noted. Teacher-supported feedback was valued for promoting clearer understanding by contextualizing ChatGPT suggestions, although motivational responses were mixed and conference time was limited. Findings suggest that a blended feedback approach integrating ChatGPT and human feedback may support writing improvement and engagement. Pedagogical recommendations include training students to interpret ChatGPT feedback, improving peer review practices, and increasing structured teacher-student interactions to complement technological affordances. Plain Language Summary This study examined how university students learning English as a foreign language experienced three approaches to writing feedback: ChatGPT feedback alone, ChatGPT combined with peer feedback using a checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT combined with teacher oral feedback during writing conferences. Twenty-five English majors participated in the study. Twenty-two students provided complete pretest and posttest writing data, and 22 completed",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,1,"combined with peer feedback using a checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT combined with teacher oral feedback during writing conferences. Twenty-five English majors participated in the study. Twenty-two students provided complete pretest and posttest writing data, and 22 completed an open-ended survey. Overall, students’ writing scores improved from the beginning to the end of the study. Students in the ChatGPT-plus- peer-feedback group showed a statistically significant improvement within that group, but the three groups did not differ significantly from one another after their initial writing performance was taken into account. Because the groups were small and there was no traditional control group, these results should be interpreted cautiously. Students also reported different strengths and limitations of each approach. ChatGPT was valued for providing immediate feedback on grammar, vocabulary, and organization, but some students found its feedback impersonal or difficult to interpret. Peer feedback, supported by a checklist and peer writing conferences, encouraged discussion, collaboration, and consideration of a reader’s perspective, 1Department of Applied English, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan 2English Language Center, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan Corresponding author:",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,1,"to interpret. Peer feedback, supported by a checklist and peer writing conferences, encouraged discussion, collaboration, and consideration of a reader’s perspective, 1Department of Applied English, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan 2English Language Center, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan Corresponding author: Jui-Jung Tsao, Department of Applied English, Ming Chuan University, Taoyuan, Taiwan. Email: a71747174@gmail.com Creative Commons Non Commercial CC BY-NC: This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) which permits non-commercial use, reproduction and distribution of the work without further permission provided the original work is attributed as specified on the SAGE and Open Access pages (https://us.sagepub.com/en-us/nam/open-access-at-sage).",103
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,2,"although the quality of peer comments varied. Teacher conferences helped students understand and contextualize ChatGPT suggestions, but limited conference time was a practical constraint. The findings suggest that ChatGPT may be most useful when combined with structured human support rather than being treated as a replacement for peer or teacher feedback. Writing teachers may therefore consider helping students critically interpret ChatGPT feedback while also providing structured opportunities for checklist-guided peer review, peer discussion, and teacher guidance. Keywords AI technology, peer feedback, teacher–student writing conferences, ChatGPT Received: 10 October 2025; Revised: 15 August 2026; Accepted: 28 September 2026 Introduction The ability to write effectively is a critical skill for success in academic, professional, and personal contexts (Lee & Schmidgall, 2020). Consequently, language teachers continually seek ways to provide meaningful and constructive feedback to support students’ writing development. A key focus in writing instruction has been the investigation of different feedback types (Ellis, 2009), including peer-written corrective feedback, teacher–student writing conferences (i.e., teacher oral corrective feedback), and feedback generated by artificial intelligence (AI) tools such as ChatGPT (Roumeliotis & Tselikas, 2023). Understanding",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,2,"writing instruction has been the investigation of different feedback types (Ellis, 2009), including peer-written corrective feedback, teacher–student writing conferences (i.e., teacher oral corrective feedback), and feedback generated by artificial intelligence (AI) tools such as ChatGPT (Roumeliotis & Tselikas, 2023). Understanding the effectiveness, benefits, and potential drawbacks of these approaches is essential for designing pedagogical strategies that optimally support students’ writing. Recent studies have shown that peer feedback can enhance writing skills while fostering collaborative learning, audience awareness, and self-efficacy, and can reduce writing anxiety and improve genre awareness and text cohesion, especially when reviewers are trained to provide meaning-focused feedback (Cui et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2023; Wu et al., 2022). However, peer feedback may still focus on surface-level issues, and cultural factors can influence how students give and receive comments (Allaei & Connor, 1990; Carson & Nelson, 1996; Keh, 1990). On the other hand, teacher–student writing conferences provide personalized feedback that mediates the writing process and addresses individual needs through collaboration and scaffolding, as teachers model writing strategies and students actively engage in discussion (Eickholdt, 2004; Hyland &",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,2,"Keh, 1990). On the other hand, teacher–student writing conferences provide personalized feedback that mediates the writing process and addresses individual needs through collaboration and scaffolding, as teachers model writing strategies and students actively engage in discussion (Eickholdt, 2004; Hyland & Hyland, 2006; Lantolf & Poehner, 2004; Shin, 2003). Nev­ ertheless, stress and power dynamics can hinder effective interaction (Ferris, 2003; Hyland & Hyland, 2006), even though conferences have been shown to flexibly address a wide range of topics, adapt to students’ needs, and improve writing skills, particularly for lower-proficiency learners (Do & Tran, 2020; Yang, 2022). ChatGPT, an AI-powered language model, has recently emerged as a valuable tool for writing instruction. Prior research has documented advantages such as saving time (Al-Alami, 2024), convenience (Bok & Cho, 2023), immediate feedback (Bok & Cho, 2023; Tseng & Lin, 2024), personalized feedback (Baskara, 2023), idea generation and summarization (Črček & Patekar, 2023), error correction (Bok & Cho, 2023), and paraphrasing and proofreading (Črček & Patekar, 2023). However, studies have also highlighted several concerns. Carefully scaffolded instruction is still needed to address issues such",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,2,"2023), idea generation and summarization (Črček & Patekar, 2023), error correction (Bok & Cho, 2023), and paraphrasing and proofreading (Črček & Patekar, 2023). However, studies have also highlighted several concerns. Carefully scaffolded instruction is still needed to address issues such as the lack of explicit error explanations and the risk of diminished authorship (Al-Alami, 2024; Bok & Cho, 2023; Woo et al., 2024). Drawing on Sociocultural Theory and Cognitive Load Theory, this study conceptualizes feedback from teachers, peers, and AI tools such as ChatGPT as mediational resources that scaffold writing development while imposing distinct cognitive demands. Given this framework, it is crucial to determine whether ChatGPT can reliably replace traditional peer review (Tseng & Lin, 2024) and teacher feedback. We must also consider whether students still require human-mediated support to navigate AI’s limitations and develop higher-order writing skills (Algaraady & Mahyoob, 2023; Baskara, 2023; Nguyen, 2023). Additionally, the use of AI introduces specific cognitive loads that must be managed to prevent overwhelming the learner (Woo et al., 2024). It is also important to examine how students perceive and experience different",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,2,"Mahyoob, 2023; Baskara, 2023; Nguyen, 2023). Additionally, the use of AI introduces specific cognitive loads that must be managed to prevent overwhelming the learner (Woo et al., 2024). It is also important to examine how students perceive and experience different feedback configurations and whether their perceptions align with measurable improvements in writing performance. Therefore, the aim of this research was to examine how English major students perceive and experience three feedback methods—ChatGPT-only feedback (Group A), ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences (Group B), and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences (Group C)—across four key dimensions: satisfaction, motivation, engagement, and perceived effectiveness for writing development. In addition, the study in­ vestigated whether students’ writing performance improved from pretest to posttest under these feedback conditions, and whether differences existed among the three groups. 2 SAGE Open 16(4)",141
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,3,"Literature Review Theoretical Frameworks for English as a Foreign Language (EFL) Writing Feedback Sociocultural Theory (SCT) and Mediation Rooted in the work of Vygotsky (1978), SCT provides a powerful foundation for viewing feedback as a socially mediated process. The central concept is the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD)—the space where a learner can advance from their independent level to a potential level with the assistance of a more capable individual (Vygotsky, 1978). In the context of EFL writing, feedback from teachers, peers, and AI tools such as ChatGPT functions as graduated mediation or scaffolding that supports learners’ performance within their ZPD, and promotes the internalization of new forms and strategies (Lantolf et al., 2016; Yu & Poehner, 2025). Activities such as teacher–student conferencing and collaborative peer review exemplify this joint construction of knowledge (Liu et al., 2023; Yang, 2022). In recent work on language teacher education, ChatGPT has been conceptualized as a non-human mediational tool that provides immediate feedback and scaffolds both cognitive and socio-affective dimensions of teaching and learning (Ebrahimi et al., 2025). Analyzing feedback uptake across these diverse",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,3,"recent work on language teacher education, ChatGPT has been conceptualized as a non-human mediational tool that provides immediate feedback and scaffolds both cognitive and socio-affective dimensions of teaching and learning (Ebrahimi et al., 2025). Analyzing feedback uptake across these diverse sources reveals how socially and technologically mediated feedback processes can support learners’ self-regulation and overall writing development (Chen et al., 2023; Su et al., 2023; Tran, 2025; Villamil & de Guerrero, 2006). Cognitive Load Theory (CLT) and Feedback Processing Developed by Sweller (1988), CLT offers a critical lens for examining how learners process feedback from teachers, peers, and AI tools, especially the immediate, high-volume output generated by ChatGPT. CLT posits a limited-capacity working memory, which necessitates the management of three loads: intrinsic load (task difficulty), extraneous load (mental effort arising from suboptimal information presentation or instructional design), and germane load (effort devoted to genuine learning and schema construction) (Ouwehand et al., 2025; Sweller, 1988). When learners receive a large quantity of detailed, unprioritized corrections, whether from teachers, peers, or AI tools such as ChatGPT, the resulting information can increase extraneous",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,3,"(effort devoted to genuine learning and schema construction) (Ouwehand et al., 2025; Sweller, 1988). When learners receive a large quantity of detailed, unprioritized corrections, whether from teachers, peers, or AI tools such as ChatGPT, the resulting information can increase extraneous cognitive load (Sweller et al., 2011). This risk may be particularly pronounced with ChatGPT-mediated writing, as learners may experience substantial cognitive load when interpreting AI output and engineering prompts (Woo et al., 2024). When working memory is saturated with extraneous load, capacity for germane load (the effort to understand and apply feedback) is reduced, diminishing learning effectiveness (Sweller et al., 2011). ChatGPT Feedback in Language Education ChatGPT, developed by OpenAI, is a major AI-driven conversational tool that uses natural language processing to generate contextually appropriate responses (Barrot, 2023). Large language models such as ChatGPT have been characterized as “stochastic parrots” that reproduce linguistic patterns without grounding those patterns in meaning (Bender et al., 2021), with limited capacity for literature searching and critical analysis (Arif et al., 2023). These limitations underscore the need for AI literacy and critical use in education,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,3,"that reproduce linguistic patterns without grounding those patterns in meaning (Bender et al., 2021), with limited capacity for literature searching and critical analysis (Arif et al., 2023). These limitations underscore the need for AI literacy and critical use in education, as well as continued human oversight to ensure contextually appropriate application (Brew et al., 2023; Kong et al., 2025; Yang et al., 2021). In L2 writing, ChatGPT can support revision by providing immediate, personalized feedback on linguistic accuracy and text organization, which many students value for its real-time support and convenience (Bok & Cho, 2023; Hong, 2023; Kasneci et al., 2023; Rasul et al., 2023; Roumeliotis & Tselikas, 2023). However, it tends to be more effective in terms of detecting surface-level errors than addressing deeper structural or pragmatic issues (Algaraady & Mahyoob, 2023). Empirical studies have reported generally positive perceptions and, in some cases, learning gains comparable to teacher feedback (Aksakallı & Daşer, 2025; Alsofyani & Barzanji, 2025). ChatGPT can also function as a collaborative virtual peer that enables dialogic feedback, engagement, and reflection (Hartwell & Aull, 2023; Hutson &",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,3,"and, in some cases, learning gains comparable to teacher feedback (Aksakallı & Daşer, 2025; Alsofyani & Barzanji, 2025). ChatGPT can also function as a collaborative virtual peer that enables dialogic feedback, engagement, and reflection (Hartwell & Aull, 2023; Hutson & Plate, 2023; Kostka & Toncelli, 2023), although some learners experience cognitive overload when engineering prompts or processing extensive AI feedback, indicating a need for explicit scaffolding (Woo et al., 2024). Consequently, recent studies have increasingly advocated hybrid feedback designs in which ChatGPT’s strengths are complemented by human feedback to support critical thinking, writing proficiency, and individual voice (Su et al., 2023; Tseng & Lin, 2024). For example, ChatGPT can act as a useful supplementary rater, but still struggles with context-dependent judgments such as evaluating source use and paraphrasing (Jin, 2025). Overall, the literature suggests that ChatGPT is most effective when integrated with human mediation, yet little is known about how different configurations of AI and human feedback influence EFL writing outcomes and learner experiences. Tsao and Liu 3",168
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,4,"Peer Feedback in L2 Writing Peer feedback has long been recognized as a valuable component of L2 writing instruction. Research indicates that peer review can promote writing improvement, motivation, and self-efficacy, while reducing anxiety and improving cohesion, particularly when students are trained to provide meaning-focused comments (Cui et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2023; Wu et al., 2022). Peer feedback also encourages audience awareness, critical thinking, and collaborative learning (Bouwer et al., 2024; Peterson, 2010; Topping, 2009), with the social and scaffolding dimensions of peer interaction enhancing engagement (Chen et al., 2023; Villamil & de Guerrero, 1996). However, peer feedback is not without its limitations. It often concentrates on surface-level errors rather than deeper semantic or rhetorical concerns, and the accuracy of peer corrections, especially for grammar, can be inconsistent (Keh, 1990; Ruegg, 2015). Cultural and individual factors may further influence the effectiveness of peer review, sometimes leading to reluctance or reduced engagement (Allaei & Connor, 1990; Banister, 2023; Carson & Nelson, 1996). Many learners still prefer teacher feedback due to perceived authority and expertise, suggesting that peer feedback alone",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,4,"influence the effectiveness of peer review, sometimes leading to reluctance or reduced engagement (Allaei & Connor, 1990; Banister, 2023; Carson & Nelson, 1996). Many learners still prefer teacher feedback due to perceived authority and expertise, suggesting that peer feedback alone may not fully substitute for instructor guidance (Gielen et al., 2010; Tsui & Ng, 2000). To address these issues, scholars have emphasized the importance of structured instruction in peer review, including clear criteria, modeling, and practice, so that students can provide more specific, useful comments and take greater ownership of the feedback process (Ma, 2023; Min, 2005; Sackstein, 2017). Overall, peer feedback is a valuable but imperfect tool, the impact of which may be enhanced when integrated with teacher or AI feedback and supported by adequate training and guidance. Teacher–Student Writing Conferences Teacher–student writing conferences provide individualized, interactive feedback that can substantially support L2 writing development (Hyland & Hyland, 2006). Grounded in sociocultural perspectives, conferences function as mediational tools within the learner’s ZPD, enabling teachers to scaffold instruction and tailor feedback to students’ immediate needs (Lantolf & Poehner, 2004; Vygotsky,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,4,"can substantially support L2 writing development (Hyland & Hyland, 2006). Grounded in sociocultural perspectives, conferences function as mediational tools within the learner’s ZPD, enabling teachers to scaffold instruction and tailor feedback to students’ immediate needs (Lantolf & Poehner, 2004; Vygotsky, 1978). Through one-on-one dialogue, teachers can clarify ambiguities, address linguistic and rhetorical issues, and help students set concrete revision goals (Chang, 2021; Eckstein, 2013; González, 2010). Empirical studies show that conferences foster negotiation of meaning, idea development, and learner autonomy, especially when teachers employ scaffolding strategies such as modeling, questioning, and affirmation, which can also build students’ confidence and enhance their engagement and writing skills (Chen, 2021; Eickholdt, 2004; Hyland & Hyland, 2006; Shin, 2003). At the same time, conferences are shaped by institutional and affective constraints. Power dynamics can inhibit student engagement, and some learners experience stress or confusion, which may limit the uptake of feedback (DeMott, 2006; Ferris, 2003; Hyland & Hyland, 2006). Students’ perceptions of conferences are further mediated by prior learning experiences, cultural expectations, and the quality of teacher–student interaction (Han, 1996; Liu, 2009). In sum,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,4,"which may limit the uptake of feedback (DeMott, 2006; Ferris, 2003; Hyland & Hyland, 2006). Students’ perceptions of conferences are further mediated by prior learning experiences, cultural expectations, and the quality of teacher–student interaction (Han, 1996; Liu, 2009). In sum, conferences offer rich opportunities for individualized, dialogic feedback, but are resource-intensive and highly sensitive to relational factors. Research Gap Existing research has documented the affordances and constraints of ChatGPT feedback, peer feedback, and teacher–student writing conferences, as well as initial attempts to combine AI with human feedback. However, prior work has rarely examined how specific constellations of AI and human feedback function as mediational systems in EFL writing, or how they differentially shape learners’ cognitive processing of feedback and writing development. In particular, there is limited empirical evidence of whether ChatGPT alone can be a substitute for peer and teacher feedback, or whether human mediation remains indispensable for addressing AI’s limitations, managing cognitive load, and supporting higher-order writing development. Guided by SCT and CLT, the present study addresses this gap by systematically comparing three feedback configurations: (a) ChatGPT-only feedback, (b)",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,4,"feedback, or whether human mediation remains indispensable for addressing AI’s limitations, managing cognitive load, and supporting higher-order writing development. Guided by SCT and CLT, the present study addresses this gap by systematically comparing three feedback configurations: (a) ChatGPT-only feedback, (b) ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and (c) ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences. Specifically, the study investigated how English major students perceived and experienced these feedback methods across four dimensions (satisfaction, motivation, engagement, and perceived effectiveness), and whether gains in analytic writing scores differed across the feedback configurations from pretest to posttest. 4 SAGE Open 16(4)",103
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,5,"Methodology Research Questions Based on the reviewed literature and the research purpose presented above, this study addressed the following research questions: 1. How do English major students perceive and experience different feedback methods: ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences, across four key dimensions: satisfaction, motivation, engagement (cognitive, behavioral, affective, and social), and perceived effectiveness for writing development? 2. Do English major students’ writing scores show significant pretest–posttest improvement after receiving different feedback methods, and are there differences across groups? Specifically, RQ1 was examined through thematic analysis of open-ended survey responses, while RQ2 was examined through quantitative analysis of pretest and posttest scores. Participants A total of 34 second-year English majors (12 males, 22 females; mean age = 19 years) enrolled in an intact writing course in the Department of English were initially invited to participate. All had completed English Writing Courses 1 and 2 in their first year, and had demonstrated intermediate-level writing proficiency. Of these 34 students, 25 (12 males, 13 females) agreed to",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,5,"in the Department of English were initially invited to participate. All had completed English Writing Courses 1 and 2 in their first year, and had demonstrated intermediate-level writing proficiency. Of these 34 students, 25 (12 males, 13 females) agreed to participate and were included in the study. However, because of class absences or incomplete submission of one or more required tasks, complete data were not available from all 25 participants for every analytic component. The quantitative analytic sample comprised 22 participants with complete pretest and posttest data (Group A, n = 6; Group B, n = 8; Group C, n = 8). The qualitative analytic sample also comprised 22 participants who completed the end-of-study open-ended survey (Group A, n = 7; Group B, n = 8; Group C, n = 7). Thus, the quantitative and qualitative analytic samples were equal in size but differed slightly in terms of their group composition. The remaining nine students in the course did not participate in the study; therefore, their assignments were not included in the data analysis. Within this intact writing course, students",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,5,"in size but differed slightly in terms of their group composition. The remaining nine students in the course did not participate in the study; therefore, their assignments were not included in the data analysis. Within this intact writing course, students were first stratified by their pretest writing scores and were then assigned to the three feedback groups (ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences) based on timetable and conferencing constraints; thus, group assignment was quasi-experimental rather than fully randomized. In making these assignments, practical factors such as ensuring gender balance, students’ willingness to collaborate, and group compatibility were also considered. Although descriptive mean differences were observed at the pretest (Group B’s mean was lower; see the Quantitative Data: Pretest– Posttest Comparisons section), a Kruskal–Wallis test confirmed no statistically significant differences across groups, H(2) = 2.93, p = .232. The original 25 participants were initially allocated to the three feedback groups as follows: · Group A (7 students; 3 males, 4 females): ChatGPT-only feedback · Group B",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,5,"no statistically significant differences across groups, H(2) = 2.93, p = .232. The original 25 participants were initially allocated to the three feedback groups as follows: · Group A (7 students; 3 males, 4 females): ChatGPT-only feedback · Group B (10 students; 5 males, 5 females): ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences · Group C (8 students; 4 males, 4 females): ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences The original design included a no-feedback (control) group, but this condition was omitted after pilot participants, during a pilot study conducted from mid-February to June 2024, expressed concerns about not receiving feedback. As a result, in the formal study (mid-September 2024 to mid-January 2025), all enrolled participants received feedback interventions. Although the ChatGPT-only group was not a traditional control condition, it served as a practical baseline for exploratory comparisons of the added contribution of peer and teacher feedback, with ChatGPT feedback serving as the common component across all three groups. Recruitment Process and Ethical Considerations Since this study was conducted within a regular course in the Department",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,5,"exploratory comparisons of the added contribution of peer and teacher feedback, with ChatGPT feedback serving as the common component across all three groups. Recruitment Process and Ethical Considerations Since this study was conducted within a regular course in the Department of English, no external advertisements were used. All students in the course were invited to participate, and students who did not participate still completed the same course instruction and assignments, but their work was excluded from the research dataset. A written consent form was provided to all students which described the study objectives, procedures, potential risks and benefits, confidentiality protections, and Tsao and Liu 5",105
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"participants’ rights. Participation was entirely voluntary, and students were informed that they could withdraw at any time without academic consequences. To limit the risk of harm, the study employed minimal-risk procedures aligned with routine educational practice. Data were derived from normal writing tasks and feedback activities, no deception was involved, no sensitive personal data were collected, and no personally identifying information was included in the research dataset. All datasets were anonymized prior to analysis, and only aggregated results were reported. Potential risks were therefore minimal and primarily limited to mild discomfort related to receiving feedback or reflecting on writing performance. The potential benefits were judged to outweigh these minimal risks. Participants could benefit from structured op­ portunities to interpret feedback, revise drafts, and reflect on writing strategies, while the study may benefit society by informing evidence-based integration of AI and human feedback in EFL writing instruction. The study was conducted in accordance with the applicable national research ethics regulations in Taiwan. Under these regulations, research conducted in ordinary educational settings to evaluate teaching techniques or instructional effectiveness may qualify for",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"human feedback in EFL writing instruction. The study was conducted in accordance with the applicable national research ethics regulations in Taiwan. Under these regulations, research conducted in ordinary educational settings to evaluate teaching techniques or instructional effectiveness may qualify for exemption from formal ethics committee review (Department of Health, Executive Yuan, 2012). Because the present study involved adult university students, routine classroom-based educational activities, and minimal-risk data collection, it was treated as falling within this exempt category; therefore, formal IRB review was not sought and no approval number is available. Written informed consent to participate was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. Participants were informed that participation was voluntary and that they could withdraw at any time without academic consequences. All participants were adults and were at least 19 years of age. Writing Assignments The three groups each completed three writing assignments, namely an expository, a cause/effect, and a compare/contrast essay, over the course of the semester, following targeted lectures on each genre. For each assignment, students revised their work using the designated feedback method(s) assigned to their",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"completed three writing assignments, namely an expository, a cause/effect, and a compare/contrast essay, over the course of the semester, following targeted lectures on each genre. For each assignment, students revised their work using the designated feedback method(s) assigned to their group, and submitted their completed Writing Assignment Journals to the Moodle system. Research Procedures This minimal-risk classroom study used anonymized course-generated writing data; participation was voluntary and based on written informed consent obtained prior to data collection. The study comprised two stages: a pilot study (mid-February to June 2024) conducted to refine the research methodology and instruments, and a formal study (mid-September 2024 to mid- January 2025) implemented using the group assignments described above. At the beginning of the fall semester 2024, all groups received initial training in reflective writing and feedback interpretation, and additional support in feedback communication strategies was provided as necessary throughout the study. To ensure the authenticity of students’ writing abilities, participants completed all assignments in class without access to external resources, such as smartphones or AI writing tools, as submitting AI-generated text as one’s own",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"was provided as necessary throughout the study. To ensure the authenticity of students’ writing abilities, participants completed all assignments in class without access to external resources, such as smartphones or AI writing tools, as submitting AI-generated text as one’s own was prohibited under the course policy. After completing each writing assignment in class and prior to submission, students took a picture of their handwritten draft. Subsequently, outside of class, they received ChatGPT feedback on their writing using a structured file called the Writing Assignment Journal: Initial Draft, ChatGPT Feedback, Revisions, and Reflections. This digital file comprised four components: the initial draft, a list of errors identified by ChatGPT using the GPT-4o model, the draft revised according to ChatGPT feedback, and the student’s own analysis and reflection on the feedback process. GPT-4o was the specific model of ChatGPT used to generate feedback in the present study. It was selected because it was the version available during the formal study period and was accessible to students through the free ChatGPT service, making it feasible for classroom implementation without requiring a paid subscription.",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"feedback in the present study. It was selected because it was the version available during the formal study period and was accessible to students through the free ChatGPT service, making it feasible for classroom implementation without requiring a paid subscription. However, the study did not compare different GPT versions; therefore, the findings should be interpreted as reflecting this specific instructional implementation rather than as evidence of differences among GPT-3.5, GPT-4, and GPT-4o. Accordingly, the term “ChatGPT feedback” is used throughout the manuscript to refer to feedback generated by ChatGPT using the GPT-4o model in the present study. To measure students’ improvement over the semester, a pretest and a posttest writing task were administered. To enhance comparability while reducing direct recall effects, both prompts addressed the same topic but were slightly rephrased. The pretest topic was “The Impact of Social Media on Modern Communication Skills,” and the posttest topic was “How Social Media Shapes Modern Communication Skills.” Of the 25 participants who initially joined the study, 22 completed both the pretest and posttest (Group A, n = 6; Group B, n",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,6,"on Modern Communication Skills,” and the posttest topic was “How Social Media Shapes Modern Communication Skills.” Of the 25 participants who initially joined the study, 22 completed both the pretest and posttest (Group A, n = 6; Group B, n = 8; Group C, n = 8); therefore, only these 22 participants were included in the quantitative analyses. Before formal scoring, the two raters received training on the analytic rubric and practiced scoring five sample essays selected by the researcher to represent a range of proficiency levels (excellent, good, average, fair, and weak). These calibration essays were not part of the study sample but were used to ensure that both raters applied the rubric 6 SAGE Open 16(4)",118
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,7,"consistently. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using a two-way mixed-effects, absolute-agreement ICC based on the average ratings of the two raters. Reliability was high for both the pretest (Cronbach’s α = .94; average-measures ICC = .95, p < .001) and the posttest (Cronbach’s α = .96; average-measures ICC = .92, p < .001). The ICC values indicate excellent agreement between the two raters (Koo & Li, 2016). Each essay’s score was averaged across the two raters for subsequent analyses. Open-Ended Question Survey At the end of the study, participants completed an anonymous, open-ended survey in Chinese to share their perspectives on the feedback methods used. The responses were first analyzed in Chinese to preserve participants’ intended meanings; illustrative excerpts reported in English were translated by the researcher and were checked against the original Chinese responses for semantic accuracy. The survey collected basic demographic information and focused on four key dimensions: participants’ satisfaction, motivation, engagement (cognitive, behavioral, affective, and social), and perceived effectiveness for writing development. Of the 25 participants, 22 completed the survey (overall response rate = 88%; Group A =",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,7,"basic demographic information and focused on four key dimensions: participants’ satisfaction, motivation, engagement (cognitive, behavioral, affective, and social), and perceived effectiveness for writing development. Of the 25 participants, 22 completed the survey (overall response rate = 88%; Group A = 7, Group B = 8, Group C = 7). For example, questions included: (1) Satisfaction: “What aspects of this feedback method did you find most satisfying or helpful?” (2) Motivation: “Did this feedback method motivate you to improve your writing? Why or why not?” (3) Engagement: (a) Cognitive: “How did you think about applying ChatGPT feedback to improve your writing? For example, did you analyze the feedback and plan how to use it?” (b) Behavioral: “How did this feedback method influence your participation and involvement in the writing and revision process?” (c) Affective: “Did you feel encouraged or frustrated when receiving this feedback? How did it affect your emotions or learning attitude?” (d) Social: “Did you discuss ChatGPT feedback with others, such as classmates or teachers, to improve your writing?” (4) Perceived Effectiveness: “In what ways do you think this",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,7,"receiving this feedback? How did it affect your emotions or learning attitude?” (d) Social: “Did you discuss ChatGPT feedback with others, such as classmates or teachers, to improve your writing?” (4) Perceived Effectiveness: “In what ways do you think this feedback method helped (or did not help) you improve your writing skills?” Separate but parallel versions of the survey were designed for each group (ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences) to ensure alignment with the feedback methods they experienced. Data Analysis As outlined above, different analytic approaches were applied to address the research questions. Students’ perceptions of the feedback methods were examined through thematic analysis of open-ended survey responses (Research Question 1), while their writing performance was investigated through quantitative analyses of pretest and posttest scores (Research Question 2). Thematic Analysis Thematic analysis of students’ open-ended survey responses was conducted following Braun and Clarke’s (2012) six-step framework. Two independent raters coded the qualitative data, and inter-rater reliability was calculated using percentage agreement. The raters reached",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,7,"scores (Research Question 2). Thematic Analysis Thematic analysis of students’ open-ended survey responses was conducted following Braun and Clarke’s (2012) six-step framework. Two independent raters coded the qualitative data, and inter-rater reliability was calculated using percentage agreement. The raters reached agreement on 86 of 100 coding decisions (86% agreement), indicating a high level of consistency in coding decisions (Stemler, 2004). The analysis focused on students’ experiences of the three feedback methods, and generated four overarching themes: (1) satisfaction with feedback, (2) impact on writing motivation, (3) engagement in the feedback process (cognitive, behavioral, affective, and social), and (4) perceived effectiveness for writing development. In this study, “satisfaction” refers to students’ evaluative judgments of each feedback method, “motivation” to their willingness and desire to continue writing and revising, and “engagement” to how they cognitively, behaviorally, affectively, and socially interacted with the feedback; “perceived effectiveness” captures their overall appraisal of the degree to which the feedback improved their writing. Table 1 summarizes these key themes, which are elaborated in the following sections with illustrative student responses. Tsao and Liu 7",178
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,7,"feedback; “perceived effectiveness” captures their overall appraisal of the degree to which the feedback improved their writing. Table 1 summarizes these key themes, which are elaborated in the following sections with illustrative student responses. Tsao and Liu 7",38
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,8,"Quantitative Analysis First, a paired-samples t test was conducted to compare pretest and posttest scores for the 22 participants with complete paired data, with the effect size expressed as Cohen’s dz (t/√n). Second, within-group comparisons were performed for each feedback condition using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, given the relatively small group sample sizes. Effect sizes for the Wilcoxon tests were calculated as r = |Z|/√N, with values of approximately .10, .30, and .50 interpreted as small, medium, and large effects, respectively. Third, Kruskal–Wallis H tests were conducted to examine between-group differences in pretest and posttest scores. Finally, to examine between-group differences in posttest performance while statistically controlling for initial writing proficiency, a one-way ANCOVA was conducted with pretest scores entered as the covariate. Findings Qualitative Data Given the small number of participants in each group, qualitative response frequencies are reported as simple counts, and are interpreted descriptively rather than as statistical comparisons across groups. Group A: ChatGPT-Only Feedback Satisfaction With ChatGPT Feedback Five of the seven students reported being satisfied with ChatGPT’s feedback, particularly valuing its clarity, logical organization, and grammatical",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,8,"simple counts, and are interpreted descriptively rather than as statistical comparisons across groups. Group A: ChatGPT-Only Feedback Satisfaction With ChatGPT Feedback Five of the seven students reported being satisfied with ChatGPT’s feedback, particularly valuing its clarity, logical organization, and grammatical corrections. For example, Student A noted that ChatGPT “helped me improve the logicality between sentences… [and] how to write an interesting hook that could attract more attention,” while Student B emphasized that it “helped me clarify my organization and use the correct words and grammar to express my ideas.” However, two of the seven students expressed dissatisfaction, pointing to occasional lack of precision and a perceived loss of personal voice. For instance, Student C felt that some feedback was “not always clear enough,” requiring more specific instructions, and Student D remarked that it “changes a lot of the phrases that I use, which takes away my personality or style.” Impact on Writing Motivation With respect to motivation, responses were mixed: four of the seven students reported increased motivation, primarily because ChatGPT reduced writing anxiety and offered clear, structured guidance. Student",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,8,"I use, which takes away my personality or style.” Impact on Writing Motivation With respect to motivation, responses were mixed: four of the seven students reported increased motivation, primarily because ChatGPT reduced writing anxiety and offered clear, structured guidance. Student E, for instance, felt the feedback “doesn’t feel judgmental,” which “reduces pressure” and “encourages” further effort. Student A likewise noted feeling less discouraged and more efficient, adding that it “helps me process my work faster and gives me more confidence.” By contrast, three of the seven students described feeling demotivated, attributing this to a rigid, impersonal correction style. Student C Table 1. Final Themes Identified in the Study Theme Group A (ChatGPT-only feedback) Group B (ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences) Group C (ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences) Satisfaction with Feedback Positive overall; immediate but impersonal Positive; peer-supported interpretation, variable quality Positive; teacher clarification, limited conference time Impact on Writing Motivation Mixed; reduced anxiety but impersonal feedback Generally positive; clearer revision goals and peer accountability, some pressure Mixed; teacher encouragement and clear guidance,",180
