DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,9,"characterized the process as “mechanical,” saying it “takes away my personality and makes writing feel like a chore,” and Student D echoed that constant error-pointing can be discouraging and create a sense that “my writing isn’t good enough.” Engagement in the Feedback Process Cognitive Engagement Five of the seven students reported active cognitive engagement, describing how they reflected on and compared ChatGPT’s suggestions with their original writing. Student F noted, “Before using ChatGPT, I would write an essay first, then compare which suggestions made my writing stronger and apply them.” By contrast, two of the seven students showed lower cognitive uptake; for example, Student G remarked, “Sometimes I just forget about the feedback and don’t apply it because it doesn’t always fit my writing style.” Behavioral Engagement Mirroring the cognitive pattern, five of the seven students applied ChatGPT feedback in subsequent tasks. Student B explained, “I used the suggestions in my next writing assignment and also learned new words and sentence structures in the process.” However, two of the seven students struggled to incorporate revisions consistently; as Student D admitted,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,9,"subsequent tasks. Student B explained, “I used the suggestions in my next writing assignment and also learned new words and sentence structures in the process.” However, two of the seven students struggled to incorporate revisions consistently; as Student D admitted, “I don’t always know how to use the feedback properly. Sometimes it feels like it changes too much.” Affective Engagement Affective engagement with ChatGPT feedback revealed a mixed pattern of responses among students. Three of the seven students found ChatGPT reassuring, with Student A commenting, “It feels like I finally have ‘someone’ to discuss writing with. ChatGPT is always there to help when others might not have time.” In contrast, three of the seven students reported frustration with its impersonal tone; as Student C put it, “It constantly points out mistakes, and I feel like it’s judging everything I write.” One student did not report a clear affective stance. Social Engagement Six of the seven students did not discuss ChatGPT feedback with peers or teachers. Student G reflected, “I didn’t discuss it with anyone… We were focused on completing our",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,9,"One student did not report a clear affective stance. Social Engagement Six of the seven students did not discuss ChatGPT feedback with peers or teachers. Student G reflected, “I didn’t discuss it with anyone… We were focused on completing our assignments.” Nevertheless, one student suggested that brief discussions could help clarify vague suggestions; as Student F noted, talking with a teacher or peer “might have helped.” Perceived Effectiveness of ChatGPT Feedback in Writing Development Five of the seven students perceived ChatGPT’s feedback as beneficial for writing development, most notably in three areas: grammar and vocabulary, sentence structure and coherence, and awareness of strengths and weaknesses. Student B remarked, “ChatGPT helped me avoid grammar mistakes and introduced me to better vocabulary.” Student C noted gains in organization: “It helped me make my writing clearer and more logical,” and Student A highlighted metacognitive awareness: “I realized that my strength is brainstorming ideas, but I often struggle with sentence logic.” By contrast, two of the seven students questioned its overall effectiveness. Student D was reluctant to adopt every suggestion because they felt it",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,9,"metacognitive awareness: “I realized that my strength is brainstorming ideas, but I often struggle with sentence logic.” By contrast, two of the seven students questioned its overall effectiveness. Student D was reluctant to adopt every suggestion because they felt it was “rigid,” and Student G commented that, while the tool clarified “basic academic writing structure,” it did not “change my writing in a big way.” Group B: ChatGPT Plus Peer Feedback with Checklist and Peer Writing Conferences Satisfaction With ChatGPT and Peer Feedback All eight students expressed positive views of the combined approach. Students valued ChatGPT for grammar, sentence structure, and vocabulary checking, whereas peer review helped them consider content clarity, logic, and persuasiveness from a reader’s perspective. For instance, Student H noted that ChatGPT provided clear grammar corrections, whereas a peer pointed out which parts of the argument were unconvincing. Student I similarly highlighted that ChatGPT quickly flagged language issues while the peer suggested ways to strengthen the ideas. At the same time, limitations were reported. Two of the eight students found that peer feedback could be too vague",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,9,"unconvincing. Student I similarly highlighted that ChatGPT quickly flagged language issues while the peer suggested ways to strengthen the ideas. At the same time, limitations were reported. Two of the eight students found that peer feedback could be too vague or uneven in quality, leaving uncertainty about what to change, whereas one student felt that ChatGPT’s suggestions were overly “advanced” in lexis or were insufficiently tailored. Impact on Writing Motivation Five of the eight students reported increased motivation because dual-source feedback clarified the revision goals and reduced uncertainty. Student L wrote that “seeing my mistakes immediately through ChatGPT and then getting detailed Tsao and Liu 9",106
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,10,"suggestions from my peer made me want to revise my work thoroughly,” and Student M added that knowing a classmate would review the draft “made me want to make my work better before sharing it.” By contrast, three of the eight students reported pressure or frustration, especially when feedback felt extensive or difficult to reconcile. Student N described not always knowing “whether to follow ChatGPT’s suggestion or my peer’s advice,” while Student O noted feeling “discouraged” when “there were too many corrections … to fix at once.” Engagement in the Feedback Process Cognitive Engagement Seven of the eight students reported active cognitive engagement, describing how they reviewed ChatGPT’s language-level suggestions and then used peer comments to evaluate content clarity and logical flow. For example, Student H described first examining ChatGPT’s grammar corrections and then checking whether ideas were logically presented before revising organization. However, Student K struggled with an inconsistency between ChatGPT feedback and peer feedback, saying that “ChatGPT said my sentence was grammatically correct, but my peer thought it was awkward. I wasn’t sure which advice to follow.” The",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,10,"before revising organization. However, Student K struggled with an inconsistency between ChatGPT feedback and peer feedback, saying that “ChatGPT said my sentence was grammatically correct, but my peer thought it was awkward. I wasn’t sure which advice to follow.” The group generally demonstrated deliberate comparison and integration of dual-source feedback during planning and revision. Behavioral Engagement Students actively revised their writing based on feedback. Four of the eight students reported concrete actions (e.g., fixing grammar, restructuring sentences, adjusting word choice), two focused mainly on expression/coherence improvements, and the remaining two reported little or no action. For instance, Student M described, “I fixed five grammar mistakes using ChatGPT and rewrote two paragraphs based on my peer’s comments,” whereas Student O struggled to combine inputs and “just made small changes.” Affective Engagement Affective responses were generally positive. Five of the eight students reported that the combined approach boosted confidence or enjoyment, while two of the eight students felt pressure or frustration when suggestions were lengthy or highly critical; one student offered a mixed/neutral stance. For instance, Student L shared, “Having two types",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,10,"reported that the combined approach boosted confidence or enjoyment, while two of the eight students felt pressure or frustration when suggestions were lengthy or highly critical; one student offered a mixed/neutral stance. For instance, Student L shared, “Having two types of feedback made me feel more confident about my revisions,” whereas Student N noted feeling overwhelmed by “too many suggestions at once.” Social Engagement Peer review fostered some social interaction, but depth varied. Three of the eight students described effective discussions that clarified feedback and supported problem solving, another three reported limited discussion (mostly written comments with little talk), and the remaining two noted constraints (e.g., unfamiliar partners, hesitancy to speak directly). For example, Student H remarked that talking with a peer “helped me understand what needed to be fixed,” while Student K noted, “We mostly just wrote feedback on paper and didn’t really discuss it in detail.” Perceived Effectiveness of ChatGPT and Peer Feedback in Writing Development Seven of the eight students perceived the combined approach as being effective for writing development, especially for grammar, organization, and clarity. As",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,10,"paper and didn’t really discuss it in detail.” Perceived Effectiveness of ChatGPT and Peer Feedback in Writing Development Seven of the eight students perceived the combined approach as being effective for writing development, especially for grammar, organization, and clarity. As Student I commented, “ChatGPT helped me with grammar, and my peer helped me make my arguments stronger.” However, one student questioned the overall impact, and two of the eight students also noted that some peer comments were too general to act on. For instance, Student M remarked, “Some of my peer’s comments were too general, like ‘your ideas need more depth,’ but they didn’t tell me how to fix them.” Group C: ChatGPT Plus Teacher Oral Feedback During Writing Conferences Satisfaction With ChatGPT and Teacher Feedback Five of the seven students expressed satisfaction with the combined approach, emphasizing that ChatGPT offered quick, structured, language-level feedback, whereas teacher conferences provided personalized explanations that clarified why errors occurred and how to fix them. For example, Student P noted that ChatGPT “helps analyze and identify areas for improvement,” while the teacher “fills in",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,10,"offered quick, structured, language-level feedback, whereas teacher conferences provided personalized explanations that clarified why errors occurred and how to fix them. For example, Student P noted that ChatGPT “helps analyze and identify areas for improvement,” while the teacher “fills in the gaps” by explaining concepts in an accessible way. Student Q similarly observed that ChatGPT “quickly points out grammar issues,” but the teacher tailors explanations to the student’s level, which makes their feedback “more useful.” At the same time, five of the seven students also found ChatGPT’s feedback mechanical or hard to interpret without teacher clarification; Student R explained that even after ChatGPT corrected their grammar, they did not fully understand the reason 10 SAGE Open 16(4)",117
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,11,"until the teacher explained it. Time constraints also limited the depth of some conferences; as Student S put it, teacher feedback greatly improved logic and structure, but there was “not enough time to cover everything in detail.” Impact on Writing Motivation Motivation was mixed, with four of the seven students reporting increased motivation because the dual-source feedback clarified the next steps, with ChatGPT quickly identifying weaknesses and the teacher providing targeted explanations. Additionally, three of the seven students explicitly emphasized that teacher encouragement strengthened their confidence and persistence. For instance, Student T shared, “ChatGPT helps me quickly identify my weaknesses, and the teacher’s explanations show me how to improve. This motivates me to refine my writing.” At the same time, four of the seven students reported pressure or frustration, most often when ChatGPT suggestions felt repetitive or when the overall volume/depth of feedback was extensive, which sometimes made progress feel slow or uncertain. For example, Student U noted that initial motivation gave way to doubt when ChatGPT “kept repeating the same points,” and Student V remarked that detailed feedback created",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,11,"volume/depth of feedback was extensive, which sometimes made progress feel slow or uncertain. For example, Student U noted that initial motivation gave way to doubt when ChatGPT “kept repeating the same points,” and Student V remarked that detailed feedback created pressure: “There are so many things to fix, and sometimes I feel like I’m not improving fast enough.” Notably, some students who felt motivated also reported pressure, indicating an overlap between increased direction and heightened processing demands. Engagement in the Feedback Process Cognitive Engagement All seven students described some form of deliberate processing of feedback, although strategies varied: three of the seven students explicitly compared ChatGPT’s revised text with the original sentence by sentence to locate needed improvements; two of the seven students emphasized tracking recurrent issues to avoid them in future drafts; and two reported checking rules/ reorganizing paragraphs or balancing language-level and higher-order concerns. For example, Student P noted, “I compare ChatGPT’s revised version with my original draft sentence by sentence to see where improvements are needed.” When ChatGPT and teacher input differed, one student reported no major",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,11,"or balancing language-level and higher-order concerns. For example, Student P noted, “I compare ChatGPT’s revised version with my original draft sentence by sentence to see where improvements are needed.” When ChatGPT and teacher input differed, one student reported no major conflict and chose to implement both sources of feedback. This pattern suggests that students generally integrated dual-source feedback without substantial confusion during planning and revision. Behavioral Engagement Students actively revised their writing based on feedback. Four of the seven students reported making concrete, immediate revisions to their current drafts (e.g., correcting grammar and word choice, restructuring sentences). For example, Student R shared, “After receiving feedback, I rewrite my draft by correcting grammar and word choice. I also apply these suggestions in my future writing assignments.” Two of the seven students applied feedback indirectly, indicating that they did not always revise their current draft but carried the suggestions forward to subsequent assignments. As Student S admitted, “I don’t always rewrite my drafts, but I remember the feedback and try to avoid the same mistakes next time.” One student provided limited detail",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,11,"their current draft but carried the suggestions forward to subsequent assignments. As Student S admitted, “I don’t always rewrite my drafts, but I remember the feedback and try to avoid the same mistakes next time.” One student provided limited detail about actions taken. Affective Engagement Affective responses were largely positive. Five of the seven students reported feeling encouraged or satisfied, describing a balance between ChatGPT’s efficiency and the teacher’s reassurance/encouragement. For example, Student T expressed, “I feel more confident because the teacher reassures me that my writing is improving.” By contrast, two of the seven students reported pressure or strain stemming from the volume/detail of feedback or limited time to process it. As Student U noted, “There’s a lot to process, and sometimes I feel like I can’t fix everything at once.” Social Engagement All seven students reported that one-on-one teacher–student conferences enhanced their social engagement by enabling questions, clarification, and collaborative problem solving, often beyond what they could glean from ChatGPT alone. Student P explained, “In the conference, I have the opportunity to ask questions about what I don’t",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,11,"teacher–student conferences enhanced their social engagement by enabling questions, clarification, and collaborative problem solving, often beyond what they could glean from ChatGPT alone. Student P explained, “In the conference, I have the opportunity to ask questions about what I don’t understand and receive suggestions in return, which I apply to the next writing assignment.” Student Q noted that the teacher’s guidance was easier to understand than that of ChatGPT, and clarified why revisions were needed: “The teacher’s guidance is easier to understand than ChatGPT’s, and the teacher explains why certain sentences should be rewritten or worded differently.” Similarly, Student R contrasted ChatGPT’s “rigid” feedback with the specificity of the conference: “ChatGPT’s feedback is mostly rigid; in a one-on-one meeting, the teacher directly addresses my weaknesses, which makes the points memorable.” Tsao and Liu 11",134
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,12,"Perceived Effectiveness of ChatGPT and Teacher Feedback in Writing Development All seven students perceived the combined approach as being effective for developing their writing, describing a complementary division of labor: ChatGPT delivered fast, structured, language-level corrections (e.g., grammar, word choice, sentence concision), whereas teacher conferences provided contextualized explanations and higher-order guidance (organization, coherence, and argumentation). Student Q reflected, “ChatGPT corrects my grammar, but my teacher helps me organize my paragraphs better.” At the same time, five of the seven students noted that ChatGPT feedback could feel mechanical or difficult to interpret without explanation; hence, teacher input was critical for clarifying intent and tailoring revisions to individual needs. Quantitative Data: Pretest–Posttest Comparisons Overall Pretest–Posttest Comparison A paired-samples t test was conducted to compare 22 students’ writing performance before and after the intervention. Students performed significantly better on the posttest (M = 87.23, SD = 6.09) than on the pretest (M = 79.32, SD = 13.76), with a mean gain of 7.91 points, 95% CI [3.19, 12.63], t(21) = 3.48, p = .002, Cohen’s dz = 0.74. This medium-to-large within-sample effect indicated",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,12,"87.23, SD = 6.09) than on the pretest (M = 79.32, SD = 13.76), with a mean gain of 7.91 points, 95% CI [3.19, 12.63], t(21) = 3.48, p = .002, Cohen’s dz = 0.74. This medium-to-large within-sample effect indicated a substantial pretest–posttest increase in writing scores over the study period. Within-Group Comparisons To examine whether students in each feedback condition improved their writing scores, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were conducted due to the relatively small group sample sizes. · Group A (ChatGPT-only feedback): Students’ mean scores increased descriptively from pretest (M = 83.33, SD = 8.09) to posttest (M = 88.17, SD = 7.41). Although the Wilcoxon signed-rank test did not reach conventional statistical significance, Z = −1.89, p = .058, the observed effect size was large, r = .77. Given the very small group size, this effect size estimate should be interpreted cautiously. · Group B (ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences): Students demonstrated significant improvement from pretest (M = 73.50, SD = 14.75) to posttest (M = 84.88, SD = 5.67). The Wilcoxon signed-rank",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,12,"should be interpreted cautiously. · Group B (ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences): Students demonstrated significant improvement from pretest (M = 73.50, SD = 14.75) to posttest (M = 84.88, SD = 5.67). The Wilcoxon signed-rank test indicated a significant increase in scores, Z = −2.12, p = .034, r = .75 (large effect). · Group C (ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences): Students’ scores increased from pretest (M = 82.13, SD = 15.57) to posttest (M = 88.88, SD = 5.44). Although the Wilcoxon test did not reach conventional significance, Z = −1.75, p = .080, the observed within-group effect was large, r = .62. Thus, although only Group B reached conventional statistical significance, the observed within-group effect sizes were large across all three feedback conditions (r = .62–.77). Given the very small group sizes, however, these effect-size estimates should be interpreted cautiously and should not be taken as evidence that any feedback condition was superior to another. Between-Group Comparisons Kruskal–Wallis H tests were conducted to examine between-group differences in pretest and posttest",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,12,"group sizes, however, these effect-size estimates should be interpreted cautiously and should not be taken as evidence that any feedback condition was superior to another. Between-Group Comparisons Kruskal–Wallis H tests were conducted to examine between-group differences in pretest and posttest scores. Results showed no significant differences in either pretest scores, H(2) = 2.93, p = .232, or posttest scores, H(2) = 1.98, p = .372. Thus, the three groups did not differ significantly in either baseline writing performance or unadjusted posttest performance. Descriptively, Group B showed the largest mean gain. To further verify this result while controlling for pretest performance, a one-way ANCOVA was conducted with pretest scores as the covariate. The homogeneity-of-regression-slopes assumption was not violated, as the interaction between pretest scores and feedback group was non-significant, F(2, 16) = 1.32, p = .295. Levene’s test confirmed the assumption of homogeneity of variances, F(2, 19) = 1.90, p = .177. The covariate (pretest) was significant, F(1, 18) = 12.62, p < .002, ηp 2 = .41, representing a large effect and indicating that initial writing performance strongly predicted posttest",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,12,"of homogeneity of variances, F(2, 19) = 1.90, p = .177. The covariate (pretest) was significant, F(1, 18) = 12.62, p < .002, ηp 2 = .41, representing a large effect and indicating that initial writing performance strongly predicted posttest performance. By contrast, the adjusted group effect was small, F(2,18) = 0.20, p = .821, ηp 2 = .02, indicating little evidence of meaningful adjusted differences among the three feedback conditions in this sample. The assumption of normality was satisfied (Shapiro–Wilk p = .144; Kolmogorov–Smirnov p = .200), indicating that the standardized residuals were normally distributed. The ANCOVA findings further showed that, after controlling for initial writing proficiency (i.e., pretest scores), posttest performance did not differ significantly among the three feedback groups, despite Group B showing the largest descriptive mean gain. 12 SAGE Open 16(4)",135
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,13,"Discussion This section interprets the findings in light of previous research, and discusses the pedagogical implications. The study compared three feedback configurations—ChatGPT-only feedback (Group A), ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences (Group B), and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences (Group C)—in terms of students’ satisfaction, motivation, engagement, and writing development. Students improved significantly from pretest to posttest, but only Group B showed a statistically significant within-group gain. However, this within-group significance does not indicate superiority over the other groups, as between-group comparisons remained non-significant. The three groups did not differ significantly in their posttest scores when pretest performance was controlled. Given the small sample sizes and use of multiple complementary tests, these quantitative findings should be considered exploratory and be interpreted with caution, particularly the non-significant patterns. From a classroom perspective, the overall mean increase of 7.91 points, accompanied by a medium-to-large within- participant effect (dz = 0.74), suggests that the pretest–posttest improvement was potentially relevant to students’ writing development over the semester. The improvement occurred in a course where students repeatedly received,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,13,"mean increase of 7.91 points, accompanied by a medium-to-large within- participant effect (dz = 0.74), suggests that the pretest–posttest improvement was potentially relevant to students’ writing development over the semester. The improvement occurred in a course where students repeatedly received, interpreted, and applied feedback during revision, suggesting that structured opportunities to engage with feedback may have practical value in EFL writing instruction. However, because all three groups received ChatGPT feedback, no no-feedback or traditional- feedback control group was included, and the adjusted between-group effect was small (ηp 2 = .02) and non-significant, the improvement cannot be attributed to any particular feedback configuration or to the intervention alone. Satisfaction and Motivation Satisfaction Students across all three groups reported positive aspects of their feedback experiences, while students in Groups B and C also emphasized the value of peer or teacher mediation. In Group A, students valued ChatGPT’s immediacy, structure, and technical accuracy, but some perceived the feedback as impersonal or occasionally misaligned with their intended meaning, consistent with concerns that AI feedback can underserve the affective and interpersonal dimensions of learning (Barrot,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,13,"Group A, students valued ChatGPT’s immediacy, structure, and technical accuracy, but some perceived the feedback as impersonal or occasionally misaligned with their intended meaning, consistent with concerns that AI feedback can underserve the affective and interpersonal dimensions of learning (Barrot, 2023; Bok & Cho, 2023). In Groups B and C, peer or teacher input helped contextualize and interpret ChatGPT’s suggestions, which increased perceived usefulness, while also revealing the practical constraints (e.g., variability in peer comments and limited conferencing time) reported in prior work (Cui et al., 2022; Hyland & Hyland, 2006). Motivation In Group A, some students reported reduced anxiety and greater willingness to revise because ChatGPT felt non-judgmental, whereas others felt demotivated when feedback seemed mechanical or threatened authorial voice. In Group B, peer accountability and shared responsibility generally strengthened motivation, although students sometimes experienced cognitive pressure when ChatGPT and peer suggestions diverged (Chen et al., 2023). In Group C, teacher encouragement and tailored explanations were motivating, yet the volume of feedback and limited conferencing time sometimes heightened pressure. Across the three groups, students’ accounts suggested that ChatGPT feedback",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,13,"and peer suggestions diverged (Chen et al., 2023). In Group C, teacher encouragement and tailored explanations were motivating, yet the volume of feedback and limited conferencing time sometimes heightened pressure. Across the three groups, students’ accounts suggested that ChatGPT feedback may be particularly motivating when paired with human mediation that helps preserve authorial agency and manage workload. Engagement in the Feedback Process Cognitive Engagement Across groups, students described active cognitive engagement, carefully examining feedback and considering how to integrate it into their writing. Group A primarily engaged in solitary comparison between their drafts and ChatGPT’s suggestions. Group B extended this process by weighing both ChatGPT and peer input, while Group C benefited from teacher explanations that connected language-level corrections with higher-order writing concerns. This pattern suggests that all three modalities may support cognitive engagement, while human mediation may help learners connect feedback to broader writing goals. Behavioral Engagement Students in Groups B and C described active revision based on both ChatGPT and human feedback. They frequently incorporated grammar corrections, content revisions, and organizational improvements suggested by peers or the teacher,",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,13,"connect feedback to broader writing goals. Behavioral Engagement Students in Groups B and C described active revision based on both ChatGPT and human feedback. They frequently incorporated grammar corrections, content revisions, and organizational improvements suggested by peers or the teacher, indicating active engagement with the revision process. In contrast, some students in Group A struggled to fully apply ChatGPT’s suggestions in the absence of additional guidance. This pattern supports Sackstein’s (2017) argument that Tsao and Liu 13",77
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"meaningful participation in the feedback process fosters ownership over learning outcomes, and suggests that structured scaffolding may support students’ uptake of ChatGPT feedback. Affective Engagement Students in Groups B and C frequently reported positive affective experiences associated with human interaction. Many described the feedback process as both enjoyable and supportive because of the interactive and personalized nature of the peer and teacher input. While some Group A students valued ChatGPT’s objective and immediate responses, others experienced the feedback as impersonal and low in empathy, which reduced their emotional connection to the tasks. This finding is consistent with Villamil and de Guerrero (1996), who emphasized the affective dimensions of feedback in creating a supportive learning environment, underscoring the human elements in feedback systems for sustaining emotional engagement. Social Engagement Group A students generally treated ChatGPT feedback as an individual activity, and rarely interacted with classmates or the teacher. Group B students reported some peer interaction, although the depth of discussion varied, whereas Group C students consistently described the teacher–student conferences as providing opportunities for dialogue and clarification. These results support Peterson’s",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"rarely interacted with classmates or the teacher. Group B students reported some peer interaction, although the depth of discussion varied, whereas Group C students consistently described the teacher–student conferences as providing opportunities for dialogue and clarification. These results support Peterson’s (2010) claim that peer and teacher feedback cultivate audience awareness and social skills, which are important for holistic development, suggesting that although AI tools aid individual learning, they may not fully foster the collaborative skills essential in language education. Perceived Versus Actual Effectiveness of Feedback Comparing perceptions with performance helps to explain how students’ experiences translated into measurable gains. In Group A, most students believed that ChatGPT improved their grammar, vocabulary, and organization, but they also recognized its limits in addressing higher-order concerns or preserving personal voice. Quantitatively, this group showed a positive but non-significant gain. This discrepancy is consistent with studies showing that AI tools are effective for surface- level accuracy yet may not, on their own, lead to robust improvements in analytic writing performance without human scaffolding (Al-Alami, 2024; Algaraady & Mahyoob, 2023; Bok & Cho, 2023). In",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"with studies showing that AI tools are effective for surface- level accuracy yet may not, on their own, lead to robust improvements in analytic writing performance without human scaffolding (Al-Alami, 2024; Algaraady & Mahyoob, 2023; Bok & Cho, 2023). In Group B, perceptions and outcomes converged. Students reported that ChatGPT handled many language-level issues, while peer feedback enhanced clarity, logic, and persuasiveness. This group was the only one to achieve statistically significant improvement. However, this within-group significance should not be interpreted as evidence that Group B was superior to Groups A or C. Because the between-group comparisons (including ANCOVA controlling for pretest per­ formance) were non-significant and the group sizes were small, the quantitative findings do not support differential effectiveness across conditions; rather, they indicate broadly comparable posttest performance, with Group B showing a comparatively larger descriptive gain in this sample. One plausible explanation is that Group B operationalized a form of distributed cognition (Zhang & Patel, 2006), in which the cognitive and evaluative demands of revision were shared across multiple resources (ChatGPT, checklist criteria, and a peer reader), rather",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"sample. One plausible explanation is that Group B operationalized a form of distributed cognition (Zhang & Patel, 2006), in which the cognitive and evaluative demands of revision were shared across multiple resources (ChatGPT, checklist criteria, and a peer reader), rather than being borne by the writer alone. Peer accountability likely increased effort and persistence because students anticipated a real audience and felt responsible for making revisions that were explainable to a classmate. In addition, the peer writing conferences created iterative negotiation of meaning, allowing students to clarify intentions, test interpretations of feedback, and refine revisions through dialogue. This staged, peer-mediated process may have increased feedback uptake and helped translate perceived benefits into measurable gains. Instructionally, Group B followed a sequenced scaffold: students first used a structured ChatGPT prompt that asked for feedback on organization, content, grammar/sentence pattern variety, and vocabulary/word choice, then applied a peer-review checklist covering the same dimensions, and finally discussed revisions in peer writing conferences using a common feedback framework to revisit these aspects collaboratively. This multi-stage design may have distributed the evaluative workload, reduced individual processing",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"choice, then applied a peer-review checklist covering the same dimensions, and finally discussed revisions in peer writing conferences using a common feedback framework to revisit these aspects collaboratively. This multi-stage design may have distributed the evaluative workload, reduced individual processing demands, and created repeated opportunities for collaborative meaning-making and negotiation of revisions, which is consistent with Sociocultural Theory and Cognitive Load Theory. In Group C, all students perceived the combination of ChatGPT and teacher oral feedback during writing conferences as being effective. Teacher guidance was seen as crucial for clarifying meaning, providing context, and extending ChatGPT’s corrections. Nevertheless, quantitative analyses did not identify significant gains in this group. These findings resonate with prior work showing that conferences can meaningfully support writing development but are sensitive to institutional time and workload constraints (Do & Tran, 2020; Hyland & Hyland, 2006; Yang, 2022). Importantly, the fact that statistical significance differed across groups should not obscure the magnitude of the observed within-group changes. The Wilcoxon effect sizes were large for Group A (r = .77), Group B (r = .75), and Group C",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,14,"2022). Importantly, the fact that statistical significance differed across groups should not obscure the magnitude of the observed within-group changes. The Wilcoxon effect sizes were large for Group A (r = .77), Group B (r = .75), and Group C (r = .62). These estimates indicate appreciable within-group change across all three conditions; however, their precision is limited by 14 SAGE Open 16(4)",63
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,15,"the very small group sizes, and they do not establish differential effectiveness across feedback conditions. Group B began with a lower pretest mean (M = 73.50) than Group C (M = 82.13) and showed a larger descriptive mean gain (11.38 vs. 6.75 points), which may have provided greater scope for measurable improvement. In addition, given the very small group sizes, the statistical significance of the within-group changes was highly sensitive to the magnitude and consistency of individual pretest–posttest changes. Thus, the different within-group significance patterns should not be interpreted as evidence that Group B was more effective than Group C, particularly because the between-group comparisons were non- significant. Viewed together, the qualitative and quantitative findings suggest that perceived value and measurable writing per­ formance captured different aspects of the feedback experience. Students in Group C particularly valued teacher-supported feedback for its clarification, reassurance, and higher-order guidance, yet these perceived benefits did not translate into statistically distinguishable performance across groups. The qualitative findings therefore complement the quantitative results by showing that teacher-supported feedback may provide benefits in understanding, confidence, and strategic",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,15,"its clarification, reassurance, and higher-order guidance, yet these perceived benefits did not translate into statistically distinguishable performance across groups. The qualitative findings therefore complement the quantitative results by showing that teacher-supported feedback may provide benefits in understanding, confidence, and strategic awareness that were not fully captured by a single posttest measure and may require longer-term transfer to become visible. These patterns also suggest that the cognitive demands associated with feedback processing may have differed across the three feedback configurations. In Group A, the need to evaluate ChatGPT suggestions independently may have increased students’ decision-making demands because they had to determine which comments to accept or reject. In Group B, checklist-guided peer review and peer writing conferences may have helped distribute this interpretive burden across ChatGPT feedback, peer criteria, and peer discussion. In Group C, teacher explanations supported understanding, but the amount and depth of feedback within the limited conferencing time may have increased processing demands and contributed to pressure. Pedagogical Implications and Recommendations Given the advantages and limitations of each feedback method, EFL writing courses may consider incorporating a blended",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,15,"amount and depth of feedback within the limited conferencing time may have increased processing demands and contributed to pressure. Pedagogical Implications and Recommendations Given the advantages and limitations of each feedback method, EFL writing courses may consider incorporating a blended feedback model that combines ChatGPT feedback with structured human support. To improve clarity for practitioners, the recommendations below are presented in prioritized order (first, second, third), followed by an additional enhancement. These recommendations reflect both students’ perceptions and the quantitative findings. First: Train Students in ChatGPT Feedback Interpretation Given the very small sample, Group A’s non-significant gain does not establish whether ChatGPT-only feedback is sufficient to improve writing performance. However, students’ reported difficulties in interpreting and selectively applying ChatGPT feedback highlight the importance of equipping them with strategies for critically evaluating feedback (Tran, 2025), distinguishing between useful and irrelevant suggestions, and adapting feedback to their individual writing styles. Second: Strengthen Peer Feedback Training Group B was the only group to demonstrate statistically significant within-group improvement in the quantitative analyses; however, this result should not be taken as evidence that Group",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,15,"and adapting feedback to their individual writing styles. Second: Strengthen Peer Feedback Training Group B was the only group to demonstrate statistically significant within-group improvement in the quantitative analyses; however, this result should not be taken as evidence that Group B outperformed the other groups, because between-group comparisons remained non-significant. Rather, the finding suggests that peer feedback may meaningfully support students’ use of ChatGPT feedback when they are adequately prepared to provide constructive, specific, and actionable comments (Camarata & Slieman, 2020; Cui et al., 2022). Workshops, rubrics, and collaborative norms should be implemented to ensure consistency and reliability in peer feedback. Third: Increase Structured Teacher–Student Discussions Although Group C students valued teacher support, some reported that limited conference time constrained the depth of the teacher feedback. To address this practical limitation, teachers could adopt more efficient strategies such as rotating one-on- one sessions, small-group tutorials, or asynchronous e-feedback, particularly in contexts where class sizes are large or teacher availability is limited (Nusrat et al., 2022). These approaches can preserve the benefits of teacher input while mitigating the constraints of limited",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,15,"one-on- one sessions, small-group tutorials, or asynchronous e-feedback, particularly in contexts where class sizes are large or teacher availability is limited (Nusrat et al., 2022). These approaches can preserve the benefits of teacher input while mitigating the constraints of limited instructional time. Additionally: Refine ChatGPT Feedback Prompts Across all groups, the quality of ChatGPT’s suggestions was central to students’ engagement and writing development. Recent research highlights that the specificity and contextual appropriateness of prompts are critical for maximizing the value of ChatGPT feedback (Izadi & Forouzanfar, 2024; Jacobsen & Weber, 2025). Writing teachers should therefore tailor ChatGPT prompts to align with specific learning objectives, genres, and proficiency levels, while incorporating ongoing student feedback to improve ChatGPT feedback processes. Tsao and Liu 15",122
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,16,"For ease of classroom implementation, the bullet point list below provides a quick guide to selecting each feedback configuration. · ChatGPT-only feedback (Group A): May be useful for early-stage revision focused on surface-level accuracy (grammar, vocabulary, sentence-level clarity), especially when teacher time is limited. It can be paired with brief training on interpreting and selectively applying ChatGPT suggestions to avoid over-reliance and loss of authorial voice. · ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences (Group B): May be particularly useful for strengthening higher-order writing quality (clarity, logic, persuasiveness) through iterative discussion. Use rubric/ checklist training and structured conferencing routines to promote accountability and negotiation of meaning. · ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences (Group C): May be appropriate when students need contextualized explanations and individualized diagnosis for higher-order concerns (organization, coherence, argumentation). To manage time constraints, prioritize targeted conferencing (recurring problems, rotating con­ ferences, small-group tutorials, or asynchronous e-feedback). Conclusion This study examined students’ experiences and writing outcomes across three feedback approaches: ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,16,"constraints, prioritize targeted conferencing (recurring problems, rotating con­ ferences, small-group tutorials, or asynchronous e-feedback). Conclusion This study examined students’ experiences and writing outcomes across three feedback approaches: ChatGPT-only feedback, ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences, and ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences. Results indicated that students perceived ChatGPT feedback as supporting technical aspects of writing, while integrating ChatGPT feedback with peer or teacher mediation appeared to offer complementary benefits. Specifically, students in the ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences group (Group C) particularly valued teacher-supported clarification and reported positive engagement alongside mixed motivational responses. The ChatGPT plus peer feedback with checklist and peer writing conferences group (Group B) supported peer collaboration and was the only group to show a statistically significant within-group pretest–posttest gain, although inconsistencies in peer- feedback quality persisted. The ChatGPT-only feedback group (Group A) valued the immediacy and technical support provided by ChatGPT, but some students perceived the feedback as impersonal and less supportive of higher-order writing concerns. Although only Group B showed statistically significant within-group improvement, the between-group",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,16,"ChatGPT-only feedback group (Group A) valued the immediacy and technical support provided by ChatGPT, but some students perceived the feedback as impersonal and less supportive of higher-order writing concerns. Although only Group B showed statistically significant within-group improvement, the between-group com­ parisons were non-significant. Accordingly, these findings should be interpreted cautiously and suggest that ChatGPT feedback and human mediation may serve complementary roles in supporting different aspects of EFL writing development. Limitations and Directions for Future Research First, the study involved a small convenience sample of 25 English majors from a single intact course at one university. Twenty-two participants were included in each analytic component, although the quantitative and qualitative analytic samples differed slightly in terms of their group composition. The quantitative group sizes were small and unequal. Students were assigned to groups based on their pretest scores and practical considerations rather than random allocation, and neither a no-ChatGPT control group nor a traditional-feedback-only control group was included. Together, these features limit generalizability and preclude strong causal claims about the relative effectiveness of the three feedback configurations. Because the study",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,16,"considerations rather than random allocation, and neither a no-ChatGPT control group nor a traditional-feedback-only control group was included. Together, these features limit generalizability and preclude strong causal claims about the relative effectiveness of the three feedback configurations. Because the study was conducted with English majors at one university, within a single cultural and institutional context and one writing course, students from other disciplines, institutions, cultures, or instructional settings may respond differently to ChatGPT-, peer-, and teacher-supported feedback. Nevertheless, the findings may be transferable to comparable tertiary EFL writing contexts using structured ChatGPT feedback alongside peer or teacher mediation. Future multi-institutional and cross-cultural studies should examine whether these patterns extend across disciplines, learner populations, and instructional contexts. Second, the quantitative analyses relied on multiple complementary tests conducted with very small group sizes, increasing the risk of both Type I and Type II errors. Accordingly, the statistical results should be interpreted as exploratory, with attention to the overall pattern of findings rather than isolated p-values. Importantly, the non-significant quantitative gain in the ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences condition (Group",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,16,"II errors. Accordingly, the statistical results should be interpreted as exploratory, with attention to the overall pattern of findings rather than isolated p-values. Importantly, the non-significant quantitative gain in the ChatGPT plus teacher oral feedback during writing conferences condition (Group C) should not be interpreted as evidence against teacher feedback; rather, it may reflect limited conferencing time and dosage, as well as limited statistical power in this classroom setting. Third, the study relied on self-reported survey data and pre/posttest comparisons, and did not include fine-grained analyses of students’ draft sequences or text-level changes over time. Future research could address these gaps through larger-scale, multi-site studies with random or stratified assignment, inclusion of traditional feedback-only control conditions, systematic text-analytic tracking of writing development, and longitudinal designs to examine sustained effects. In addition, because the 16 SAGE Open 16(4)",137
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,17,"pretest and posttest addressed the same topic using slightly rephrased prompts, topic familiarity or practice effects cannot be completely ruled out as contributors to the observed pretest–posttest improvement. Acknowledgments The authors wish to express their sincere gratitude to all participants for their involvement in this research. ORCID iD Jui-Jung Tsao https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2322-0394 Ethical Considerations Formal institutional review board (IRB) review was not sought because, under applicable national research ethics regulations in Taiwan, research conducted in ordinary educational settings to evaluate teaching techniques or instructional effectiveness may qualify for exemption from formal ethics committee review. The present study involved adult university students, routine classroom-based ed­ ucational activities, and minimal-risk data collection, and was treated as falling within this exempt category; therefore, no IRB approval number is available. Written informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to data collection, and no personally identifying information was included in the research dataset. Consent to Participate Written informed consent to participate was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. All participants were adults (at least 19 years old) and were informed that they could",180
DOC_032,NEW14.pdf,10.1177/21582440261497008,17,"identifying information was included in the research dataset. Consent to Participate Written informed consent to participate was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. All participants were adults (at least 19 years old) and were informed that they could withdraw from the study at any time without academic penalty. Funding The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Declaration of Conflicting Interests The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Data Availability Statement The data supporting this study’s findings are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable re",107
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,1,"Unpacking the Rejection of L2 Students Toward ChatGPT- Generated Feedback: An Explanatory Research Ziqi Chen (陈子淇) Macao Polytechnic University Wei Wei (韦卫) Macao Polytechnic University Xinhua Zhu (祝新华) The Hong Kong Polytechnic University Yuan Yao (姚远) The Hong Kong Polytechnic University Abstract Purpose: Drawing on the Uniﬁed Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), this sequential mixed-method study explored L2 learners’ challenges in seeking corrective feedback in an AI-assisted educational environment. Design/Approach/Methods: The data was collected from 45 university students in a Computer Science program who were encouraged to seek corrective feedback from ChatGPT for their argumentative writing report. Self-reﬂection data were qualitatively and quantitatively analyzed. Findings: Findings suggested that 45.9% of the AI-generated feedback was rejected by students, with a higher rejection rate observed for content-focused feedback (58.7%) compared to form- focused feedback (41.3%). AI-generated content-focused feedback was rejected due to misalign- ments between expected and received feedback, anticipated high workload to respond, mismatch Corresponding author: Wei Wei, Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Room P325, Pearl Jubilee Building, Taipa, Macao. Email: weiweitesting@hotmail.com Creative Commons Non Commercial",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,1,"rejected due to misalign- ments between expected and received feedback, anticipated high workload to respond, mismatch Corresponding author: Wei Wei, Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Room P325, Pearl Jubilee Building, Taipa, Macao. Email: weiweitesting@hotmail.com Creative Commons Non Commercial CC BY-NC: This article is distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) which permits non-commercial use, reproduction and distribution of the work without further permission provided the original work is attributed as speciﬁed on the SAGE and Open Access page (https://us.sagepub.com/en-us/nam/open-access-at-sage). Research Article ECNU Review of Education 2026, Vol. 9(1) 1–20 © The Author(s) 2025 Article reuse guidelines: sagepub.com/journals-permissions DOI: 10.1177/20965311241305140 journals.sagepub.com/home/roe",107
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,2,"between feedback from AI and external references, and impeding conditions for students to engage with the feedback. Meanwhile, the rejection of form-focused feedback was largely asso- ciated with the high level of expected effort. Originality/Value: This study is one of the few existing studies on the L2 learners’ challenges in seeking corrective feedback from generative AI systems. Notably, the study differentiates between form-focused and content-focused feedback and identiﬁes the distinct challenges and learning opportunities that impact students’ rejection of AI feedback. Keywords Academic writing, ChatGPT, corrective feedback, generative AI, uniﬁed theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT) Date received: 20 February 2024; revised: 19 March 2024; accepted: 21 April 2024 Introduction Technology integration with classroom instructions and informal learning outside of the classroom has been investigated for decades (Hinostroza et al., 2015; Lai, 2015; Morales & Roig, 2002). The technology of Open AI and chatbots present extraordinary potential in promoting better language learning and teaching practice, such as timely feedback, ease of use, personalized support, interlocutors, simulations of the learning environment, the transmission of information, helpline, and individualized recommendation",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,2,"technology of Open AI and chatbots present extraordinary potential in promoting better language learning and teaching practice, such as timely feedback, ease of use, personalized support, interlocutors, simulations of the learning environment, the transmission of information, helpline, and individualized recommendation on learning materials (Huang et al., 2022; Lu et al., 2024). However, its limitations and challenges to support teaching and learning cannot be overlooked. Reviewing the challenges in previous studies, this study aims to conduct an in-depth analysis of the feedback exchange process between students and ChatGPT-3.5. Through the research identifying the challenges that arise during this engagement and their potential impact on student behaviors, we aim to gain valuable insights into how these obstacles, as perceived by students, impede the effective acceptance and utilization of feedback. Literature review Numerous research studies have examined the challenges of integrating distinct technology in edu- cational settings (Bećirović, 2023). However, studies that explore the challenges of integrating Generative AI in academic writing are still limited. With the new launch of ChatGPT, a generative AI-assisted chatbot, we can anticipate potential limitations by analyzing previous",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,2,"in edu- cational settings (Bećirović, 2023). However, studies that explore the challenges of integrating Generative AI in academic writing are still limited. With the new launch of ChatGPT, a generative AI-assisted chatbot, we can anticipate potential limitations by analyzing previous research on chatbot-assisted language learning and teaching. 2 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",53
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,3,"Technological limitations Technology itself is reported as the challenge and barrier to technology integration with classroom instruction and self-directed learning activities. It includes (1) incapability to offer answers and responses in a reliable and expected way (Kuhail et al., 2023; Winkler et al., 2020), (2) unavailabil- ity of emotional support to the users (Escalante et al., 2023; Gallacher et al., 2018; Goda et al., 2014), and (3) limitation in engaging and maintaining conversations (Yang & Zapata-Rivera, 2010). In relation to the ﬁrst point, chatbots may not be able to accurately respond to users’ inquiries due to the limitations or insufﬁciency of training datasets in the early stage of the development of the AI systems. For example, Winkler et al. (2020) designed a conversational bot named Sara aimed to solve the challenge of creating meaningful interactions in online video lectures. They eval- uated the bot by collecting self-reﬂection reports from 182 participants who were undergraduate and graduate students. The ﬁndings indicated that bots with limited datasets posed challenges to learn- ing, including the inability to provide correct answers and respond to",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,3,"eval- uated the bot by collecting self-reﬂection reports from 182 participants who were undergraduate and graduate students. The ﬁndings indicated that bots with limited datasets posed challenges to learn- ing, including the inability to provide correct answers and respond to questions. These limitations led to learners’ frustrations and thus possibly inﬂuenced their learning process. Furthermore, chatbots lack human-like emotional support, which can be linked to unnatural and ambiguous voices and the use of non-academic language. According to Goda et al.’s (2014) study, students have reported feeling that computer-generated voices are unnatural compared to human voices, ultimately limiting their engagement with the immersive learning environment. Moreover, Gallacher et al. (2018) noted that the absence of emotional and visible cues during inter- actions with chatbots undermined students’ positive state of language learning. While Escalante et al. (2023) collected 48 undergraduate students’ perceptions of feedback provided by teachers and feedback given by generative AI, the result revealed that half preferred feedback provided by teachers rather than AI-generated feedback. The primary reason cited for this preference was the positive emotional impact of in-person communication.",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,3,"of feedback provided by teachers and feedback given by generative AI, the result revealed that half preferred feedback provided by teachers rather than AI-generated feedback. The primary reason cited for this preference was the positive emotional impact of in-person communication. Lastly, the limitation of chatbots in engaging in extended conversations is another drawback. Research by Yang and Zapata-Rivera (2010) revealed that students’ ideas diverge in unforeseen directions as communication progresses, and chatbot systems fail to recognize and address these constantly shifting topics. The challenges and barriers to teachers and learners Regarding chatbot limitations in education, a subset of research has primarily focused on teachers and learners. Four areas were identiﬁed from the perspective of teachers: (1) teachers’ negative beliefs and attitudes toward the limited use of technology in teaching (Howard, 2013; Shin et al., 2014), (2) teachers’ discomfort or lack of conﬁdence in working with technology, which can lead to the rejection of new technology (Leem & Sung, 2019), (3) teachers’ low level of Chen et al. 3",168
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,4,"computer literacy can make it difﬁcult for them to operate chatbot technology (Su et al., 2023), and (4) limited access to resources in schools can also pose a challenge that can hinder the implemen- tation of AI tools (Reiser, 2002; Shin et al., 2014). More importantly, from the learners’ perspective, several factors may be relevant to the limita- tions of implementing new technologies in teaching and learning: (1) students’ low perceived use- fulness can lead to negative behaviors during their interactions with the technology (Chen et al., 2020; Kaur et al., 2021; Marchante, 2022), (2) students’ limited competence and computer literacy may hinder their effective use of chatbot technology (Celik, 2023; Hatlevik et al., 2018; Fryer et al., 2020), and (3) the disconnect between students’ external requirements and their interactions with technology has resulted in minimal use of chatbots in their learning process (Ericsson et al., 2023; Guo et al., 2023; Hew et al., 2023). To be more speciﬁc, students’ attitudes and beliefs toward the use of technology in supporting their learning process refer to their technological acceptance. This encompasses",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,4,"their learning process (Ericsson et al., 2023; Guo et al., 2023; Hew et al., 2023). To be more speciﬁc, students’ attitudes and beliefs toward the use of technology in supporting their learning process refer to their technological acceptance. This encompasses their willingness to experiment with new technology, perceived usefulness, and overall attitudes toward technology as an educational tool. For instance, Chen et al. (2020) found that students’ beliefs about chatbot use- fulness in Chinese language learning signiﬁcantly impacted their acceptance and utilization of the technology. Moreover, an insightful study by Hatlevik et al. (2018) examined the connection between technology and students’ self-efﬁcacy in nearly 60,000 individuals from 15 countries. The results revealed that familiarity with technology signiﬁcantly inﬂuenced students’ information and communication technology self-efﬁcacy. Fryer et al.’s (2020) study revealed that learners are unlikely to develop an interest in oral tasks during language classes without sufﬁcient training in feedback-seeking strategies and computer literacy. As a result, human-supported oral training ses- sions achieved better outcomes, with higher levels of motivation and engagement. Furthermore, the successful implementation of chatbots in educational environments",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,4,"tasks during language classes without sufﬁcient training in feedback-seeking strategies and computer literacy. As a result, human-supported oral training ses- sions achieved better outcomes, with higher levels of motivation and engagement. Furthermore, the successful implementation of chatbots in educational environments depends on the alignment between students’ learning interest in AI and assignment requirements established by educators (Hew et al., 2023). Framework and research questions Currently, no existing research utilizes a theoretical framework to systematically analyze the chal- lenges associated with chatbot-assisted writing and corrective feedback practices. This study aims to ﬁll that gap by utilizing the Uniﬁed Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), ﬁrst introduced by Venkatesh et al. (2003). Upon examining the breadth of research on Generative AI within educational settings, we found that the UTAUT model elucidates multiple barriers to the optimal and efﬁcient deployment of these AI systems. The model identiﬁes four principal areas impacting effectiveness and integration, which have already been widely discussed separately in the ﬁeld of AI-assisted learning and teaching. Firstly, there is “Performance Expectancy” related 4 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,4,"these AI systems. The model identiﬁes four principal areas impacting effectiveness and integration, which have already been widely discussed separately in the ﬁeld of AI-assisted learning and teaching. Firstly, there is “Performance Expectancy” related 4 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",40
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,5,"to the extent to which AI can visualize and be assisted in achieving learning outcomes. For instance, previous studies have shown that AI is a reliable tool for scoring and peer review (Jiang et al., 2023; Kaebnick et al., 2023). Secondly, “Effort Expectancy” considers the ease with which these tools can be used to facilitate the learning process, and sometimes even in an unethical manner, such as pla- giarism and academic integrity issues (Cotton et al., 2023). Thirdly, “Social Inﬂuence” is tied to the endorsement and contempt from other stakeholders in the educational community. McDonald et al. (2024) reviewed the policies of 116 US higher education institutions on the use of Generative AI, and most of the policy concerns were issues of ethical practice of AI, with most references to its application remaining ambiguous. Lastly, “Facilitating Conditions” refers to the infrastructural and resource-related aspects that enable AI utilization. As indicated in prior research (Reiser, 2002; Shin et al., 2014), constraints related to accessing necessary resources in academic settings can greatly hinder the successful implementation and effective use of AI technologies.",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,5,"infrastructural and resource-related aspects that enable AI utilization. As indicated in prior research (Reiser, 2002; Shin et al., 2014), constraints related to accessing necessary resources in academic settings can greatly hinder the successful implementation and effective use of AI technologies. In our study, the four core factors are “Mismatch with learners’ expectations,” “Anticipated high workload to respond,” “Mismatch with external references,” and “Impending conditions.” We have provided deﬁnitions for each of these core factors in Table 2. These potential challenges can be suggested by observing students’ revision strategies to deal with AI-generated feedback. As for the framework for coding students’ revision strategies, two cat- egories are identiﬁed from previous corrective feedback studies based on the rate of uptake or acceptance of received feedback (Cheong et al., 2023): (1) accept and revise accordingly and (2) show a certain level of rejection, such as ignorance, disagreement, and confusion by arguing with the feedback providers, especially for non-error corrective feedback (Mohebbi, 2021; Yang et al., 2023; Zhang, 2020). Moreover, we used two established frameworks, called “levels of revi- sions” and “taxonomy of revision",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,5,"such as ignorance, disagreement, and confusion by arguing with the feedback providers, especially for non-error corrective feedback (Mohebbi, 2021; Yang et al., 2023; Zhang, 2020). Moreover, we used two established frameworks, called “levels of revi- sions” and “taxonomy of revision strategies,” to label the dependent variable. These frameworks were informed by prior research on corrective feedback and automatic written feedback. Fan and Xu’s (2020) research shows two types of feedback: form-focused and content-focused. Form-focused feedback, typically given by teachers or peers, targets errors in language usage, such as sentence structures, vocabulary, and verb conjugations. Interestingly, Fan and Xu (2020) found that content-focused feedback was primarily delivered through negotiation. This means that feedback providers prefer to engage in oral discussions and debates with receivers on topics related to evidence and examples. Without any doubt, partnerships with Generative AI technology in language teaching and learn- ing will be one of the trends. The use of AI technologies in language teaching and learning has gained signiﬁcant attention in recent years (Hinostroza et al., 2015; Lai, 2015; Morales & Roig, 2002). Although the digital",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,5,"teaching and learn- ing will be one of the trends. The use of AI technologies in language teaching and learning has gained signiﬁcant attention in recent years (Hinostroza et al., 2015; Lai, 2015; Morales & Roig, 2002). Although the digital affordance of these AI-driven learning systems has been widely inves- tigated (Fitria, 2021; Ghufron & Rosyida, 2018), the challenges of using Generative AI technolo- gies have not been adequately examined. Open AI released a powerful generative AI technology Chen et al. 5",82
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,6,"called ChatGPT in late November 2022. To date, most of the research related to ChatGPT has mainly concentrated on academic writing (Rozencwajg & Kantor, 2023; Salvagno et al., 2023; Zimmerman, 2023). However, an in-depth exploration of ChatGPT’s distinctive inﬂuence on enhancing student engagement and their responses to formative corrective feedback is notably limited. In that case, this research aims to incorporate ChatGPT technology into the language learning feed- back process. Guided by the UTAUT, the study aims to identify the key factors that lead to negative responses to ChatGPT feedback. Consequently, the study is guided by two primary questions: 1. What are the challenges faced by L2 learners when engaging with ChatGPT-generated feedback? 2. What are the underlying factors that contribute to L2 learners’ rejection of form- and content-focused feedback generated by ChatGPT? Methods In this study, we used an exploratory sequential mixed methods design to investigate the use and reception of AI-generated feedback in academic writing among university students. The study was conducted with 45 students enrolled in a computer science program who were encouraged to seek corrective feedback",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,6,"exploratory sequential mixed methods design to investigate the use and reception of AI-generated feedback in academic writing among university students. The study was conducted with 45 students enrolled in a computer science program who were encouraged to seek corrective feedback from ChatGPT on their argumentative writing reports. In an initial qualitative phase, self-reﬂective data was collected from the students, providing a rich, contextual understanding of their experiences and reactions to the AI-generated feedback. We subsequently conducted a quantitative analysis of the data to extrapolate the initial ﬁndings to the wider student population. Participants Undergraduate students from a Computer Science program at a public university in Macao, aged between 20 and 21 (SD = 1.72), were invited to participate in an academic writing course. The sample consisted of 52 male and seven female students who had all completed the ﬁrst-semester courses and understood computer operational systems, AI technologies, and English academic writing well. The language of instruction in the classroom was English, so the participants felt com- fortable communicating with the AI system in English. Among the sample, 55 were native",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,6,"and understood computer operational systems, AI technologies, and English academic writing well. The language of instruction in the classroom was English, so the participants felt com- fortable communicating with the AI system in English. Among the sample, 55 were native Cantonese or Mandarin speakers, and the remaining four were Portuguese, English, and Filipino native speakers. Most participants were at the B1 or B2 level at the CEFR band scale (Common European Framework of Reference for Languages), based on their scores in the English language test of the university entrance examination. At the time of data collection, most participants had 6 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",105
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,7,"already used ChatGPT, the chatbot, for academic and entertainment purposes since its ofﬁcial launch at the end of November 2022. These students were ﬁrst contacted to participate in this study via Canvas, a centralized online learning system. Forty-ﬁve students expressed interest in the study, and written consent forms were obtained from each of them. Data protection strategies included removing all personal identiﬁers and replacing them with ID numbers. Data collection After that, these students were briefed about the procedure of data collection. First, they were invited to draft a 250-word essay individually without the assistance of ChatGPT in a classroom condition. The writing task is “To what extent do you agree or disagree that AI technologies may replace human working forces?”. This task was selected for several reasons: (1) this topic has been discussed in one of their compulsory subjects in their ﬁrst semester, Ethics and Professional Issues, and (2) they were familiar with relevant ideas, scientiﬁc reports, and linguistics resources. Ethics approval was obtained by the organization of the corresponding author. The data collection for this study is an",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,7,"in their ﬁrst semester, Ethics and Professional Issues, and (2) they were familiar with relevant ideas, scientiﬁc reports, and linguistics resources. Ethics approval was obtained by the organization of the corresponding author. The data collection for this study is an integral part of our routine work. No additional academic work is placed on participants speciﬁcally for this study. Secondly, they were trained to use ChatGPT to offer corrective feedback on their draft and revise their essay in 2-week advance. The training includes (1) the academic integrity issues in the use of ChatGPT to complete academic assignments, (2) the university guidelines on the use of ChatGPT for academic purposes, and (3) the guidelines and examples of writing reﬂective journals to report their feedback experiences with ChatGPT. The participants in the study were asked to interact with ChatGPT in two ways: (1) by providing corrective feedback on their essay, which they attached, and (2) by making suggestions to improve their essay based on the attached marking rubrics. These rubrics were designed to evaluate both the form-focused aspects of writing (grammatical accuracy and",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,7,"by providing corrective feedback on their essay, which they attached, and (2) by making suggestions to improve their essay based on the attached marking rubrics. These rubrics were designed to evaluate both the form-focused aspects of writing (grammatical accuracy and lexical sophistication) as well as the content-focused aspects (quality of argument structures and use of source evidence) (Wei et al., 2022). Participants were encouraged to ask ChatGPT follow-up questions about its feedback, including asking for clariﬁcations, providing more examples, or even disputing the feedback if they disagreed. Finally, they were required to submit their responses to ChatGPT’s feedback item by item in either their ﬁrst language or English. The collected data included the participants’ ﬁrst draft of their essays, the corrective feedback and suggestions from ChatGPT, and the learners’ responses and revisions, as shown in Table 1. Data coding To answer the ﬁrst research question, the UTAUT model was adapted and developed to identify and code possible barriers to students’ negative responses. The ﬁrst author and corresponding author Chen et al. 7",172
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,7,"answer the ﬁrst research question, the UTAUT model was adapted and developed to identify and code possible barriers to students’ negative responses. The ﬁrst author and corresponding author Chen et al. 7",32
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,8,"jointly coded the responses of the ﬁrst 15 participants and developed the framework collaboratively. After resolving all disagreements, they coded the rest of the responses from the remaining 30 par- ticipants with an agreement rate of 87%. The speciﬁc explanation of coding factors is presented in Table 2. To answer the second research question, an Ordinal Logistic Regression model (OLR) was developed to estimate the prediction power of the identiﬁed barriers to learners’ rejection of the AI-generated feedback. To label the dependent variable, the coding process was guided by two established frameworks: levels of revisions and the taxonomy of revision strategies, both informed by prior research on corrective feedback and automatic written feedback. This study considered four critical, independent variables in this model, including “Mismatch with learners’ expecta- tions,” “Anticipated high workload to respond,” “Mismatch with external references,” and “Impeding conditions” to predict users’ intentions to engage with ChatGPT-generated feedback. The OLR model can create an analysis model that treats the dependent variable as an ordered cat- egorical variable based on the cumulative odds principle, similar to a conventional multiple",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,8,"conditions” to predict users’ intentions to engage with ChatGPT-generated feedback. The OLR model can create an analysis model that treats the dependent variable as an ordered cat- egorical variable based on the cumulative odds principle, similar to a conventional multiple regres- sion approach (Hosmer et al., 2013). Table 1. Example of L2 writers’ response on ChatGPT generated feedback. Feedback from ChatGPT Your response 1. Use proper grammar and punctuation—here are several grammatical errors in the essay, including improper use of commas and run-on sentences. Make sure to proofread your work before submitting it. I agreed that, and I provide made revision accordingly. I change “get in touch with people” to “easily connect with people”, “had convenient our daily lives” to “has made our daily lives more convenient”, “book for position” to “make reservations”. 2. Use appropriate language—Choose your words carefully and use appropriate language for your audience and purpose. Avoid using overly technical terms or jargon that your readers may not understand. Also, make sure to vary your sentence structure to keep your writing interesting. I don’t agree that, think",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,8,"and use appropriate language for your audience and purpose. Avoid using overly technical terms or jargon that your readers may not understand. Also, make sure to vary your sentence structure to keep your writing interesting. I don’t agree that, think my words are all very easy. I don’t use any jargon in my essay. The evidence I chosen were closed to our lives. 3. Clarify and elaborate on your points—Some of your arguments could be more speciﬁc and detailed. For example, you could provide examples of jobs that have been replaced by machines or describe how addiction to virtual technology affects young people’s lives. I agree with that, but I can’t do so. Because that will over the word count. 8 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",126
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,9,"Result Qualitative ﬁndings To answer the ﬁrst research question, the research conducted a thorough analysis of the feedback provided by L2 learners to understand their rejection of AI-generated feedback better. We carefully examined the transcript, identiﬁed relevant themes that could serve as suitable study questions, and answered the ﬁrst research question. By analyzing 167 L2 students’ rejections of generative AI feedback, we found that students rejected the feedback more often because of “Mismatch with lear- ners’ expectations” (37.1%) and “Anticipated high workload to respond” (34.1%) than “Mismatch with external references” (15.0%) and “Impeding conditions” (13.8%). Speciﬁcally, the ﬁndings have been summarized in Table 3, which provides an overview of the barriers encountered by L2 learners when attempting to act upon ChatGPT-generated feedback guided by the UTAUT model. The “Mismatch with learners’ expectations” aspect of feedback refers to the feedback provided by ChatGPT that led students to think that using this technology would not help them improve their work or study performance. First, ChatGPT may not fully understand the nuances and subtleties of the learners’ writing. As a result, it may",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,9,"by ChatGPT that led students to think that using this technology would not help them improve their work or study performance. First, ChatGPT may not fully understand the nuances and subtleties of the learners’ writing. As a result, it may misinterpret the authors’ intentions and make revisions that deviate from the original meaning. For example: I think ChatGPT’s comment is wrong because I just want to speak from a neutral point of view to show some good and bad things coming out. Therefore, I will not make any changes on this point. Table 2. Coding framework on the students’ negative responses to ChatGPT-generated feedback. Categories Explanations Mismatch with learners’ expectations ChatGPT provided unhelpful feedback, such as (1) misinterpreting authors’ intentions and ideas, (2) lack of clarity and examples, and (3) extraneous feedback, which made students believe using this technology would not help them attain work/study performance gains. Anticipated high workload to respond ChatGPT provided inconvenient feedback, such as (1) information overload in feedback and (2) a general description rather than highlighting speciﬁc mistakes, which decreased students’ degree of ease associated",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,9,"not help them attain work/study performance gains. Anticipated high workload to respond ChatGPT provided inconvenient feedback, such as (1) information overload in feedback and (2) a general description rather than highlighting speciﬁc mistakes, which decreased students’ degree of ease associated with using the particular technology in their work. Mismatch with external references ChatGPT gave feedback that did not align with the expectations or preferences of other important people (e.g., peers and teachers), making students refuse to take the AI feedback. Impeding conditions ChatGPT did not provide personalized suggestions based on proﬁciency levels and lacked emotional support, causing discomfort and unexpected behavior in students. Chen et al. 9",107
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,10,"Nevertheless, the learners pointed out that the suggestions lacked clarity and speciﬁc examples, which in turn made it challenging for them to identify and address their particular areas of improve- ment, as one student stated: I do not understand what “changed the formatting” means; therefore, I cannot make revisions. In ChatGPT, extraneous feedback refers to the frustrating experience of L2 learners when they receive irrelevant corrections from AI models meant to assist them. Extraneous feedback frustrates L2 learners who use AI language models for assistance, wasting time and confusion. For example: I think ChatGPT’s comment is wrong because my ﬁrst sentence of the paragraph is, “On the other hand, technology also has some disadvantages.” And ChatGPT gave me the same sentence. The “Anticipated high workload to respond” challenges in AI feedback decreased students’ degree of ease in using the particular technology in their work. For instance, overload-speciﬁc sug- gestions given by ChatGPT are often disregarded and rejected by the learners. The abundance of tips can overwhelm learners and cause them to overlook valuable insights or fail to allocate their attention",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,10,"technology in their work. For instance, overload-speciﬁc sug- gestions given by ChatGPT are often disregarded and rejected by the learners. The abundance of tips can overwhelm learners and cause them to overlook valuable insights or fail to allocate their attention appropriately. For example: Its summary is too long to be suitable for its summary. It will be better if it gives me a short one. Yes, I agree with this feedback. I use some examples to support my arguments. For example, when a student has questions, WeChat software can help students connect; if the teacher has something import- ant to say, it can also help the teacher send the information to students faster. (However, the ChatGPT suggested to put more statistics, quotes, or research studies in writing.) Table 3. The reasons behind students’ rejection of AI feedback guided by the UTAUT model. Factors of the UTAUT model Sub-category Mismatch with learners’ expectations misinterpreting authors’ intentions and ideas (31) lack of clarity and examples (21) extraneous feedback (10) Anticipated high workload to respond a general description rather than highlighting speciﬁc mistakes",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,10,UTAUT model. Factors of the UTAUT model Sub-category Mismatch with learners’ expectations misinterpreting authors’ intentions and ideas (31) lack of clarity and examples (21) extraneous feedback (10) Anticipated high workload to respond a general description rather than highlighting speciﬁc mistakes (33) information overload (24) Mismatch with external references not aligned with other teachers’ and peers’ expectations or preferences (25) Impeding conditions lack of personalized suggestions based on proﬁciency levels (18) lack of emotional support (5) 10 ECNU Review of Education 9(1),81
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,11,"Furthermore, ChatGPT provided a generic explanation of an error, resulting in L2 learners failing to make the necessary revisions and seeking clariﬁcation on the speciﬁc occurrences of these errors. This challenge may hinder the learners’ ability to recognize and rectify their linguistic shortcomings by not highlighting the exact locations of errors. For example: ChatGPT did not give me the exact sentences, like which sentence is unclear; it just told me to revise all the paragraphs, so I will not make any changes on this point. The “Mismatch with external references” challenge in AI feedback refers to ChatGPT providing feedback that does not align with the expectations or preferences of essential people, such as peers and teachers, leading students to reject the AI feedback from ChatGPT despite its accuracy and helpfulness. Additionally, students may prioritize the feedback of individuals they trust over AI feedback, as one student mentioned: I think ChatGPT is wrong and right. More examples can support a convincing essay. Nevertheless, as I write above, I should follow the requirements of the 3 + 7 + 7 + 3.",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,11,"over AI feedback, as one student mentioned: I think ChatGPT is wrong and right. More examples can support a convincing essay. Nevertheless, as I write above, I should follow the requirements of the 3 + 7 + 7 + 3. So, for all the advantages and disad- vantages, I can only add one example to each of them. Our research identiﬁed challenges concerning “Impeding conditions” related to students’ reluc- tance to use AI feedback in their essays. Speciﬁcally, ChatGPT could not provide personalized sug- gestions based on proﬁciency levels and lacked emotional support, leading to discomfort and negative behavior in some students. This challenge might have made the learning process tedious and demotivating for students, as some students stated: I agree with that, but I cannot do much to avoid it because of my weak English skills. I also want to express myself in simpler language, but my vocabulary is small, and I can only express myself as much as possible. Quantitative ﬁndings In order to answer the second research question, descriptive statistical analysis was used to demon- strate the",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,11,"to express myself in simpler language, but my vocabulary is small, and I can only express myself as much as possible. Quantitative ﬁndings In order to answer the second research question, descriptive statistical analysis was used to demon- strate the main challenges of the interaction between students and AI. Although the AI chatbot pro- vides more content-focused feedback (58.8%) than form-focused feedback (41.2%), the participants rejected more content than form-focused feedback. It suggests that they are less pre- pared to engage with content-focused than form-focused feedback. A comparison of rejected AI feedback between form-focused and content-focused revealed a similar pattern, with “Mismatch with learners’ expectations” and “Anticipated high workload to respond” being rated as the top two reasons for rejection. As shown in Table 4, “Mismatch with external references” is mentioned Chen et al. 11",135
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,12,"less frequently to reject form-focused AI feedback, while “Impeding conditions” is reported less frequently to reject content-focused feedback. Ordinal logistic regression was conducted to determine which factors were statistically signiﬁ- cant in predicting learners’ rejection of AI feedback after controlling pre-experiment academic writing proﬁciency. Regardless of the focus of the feedback, the chi-square test shows that the assumption of parallelism tested was fulﬁlled, including Form-focused (χ2 = .000, p > .05) and Content-focused (χ2 = .000, p > .05) feedback. As shown in Table 5, the model ﬁtness (χ2 = 129.912, p < .05) showed a relationship between Form-focused feedback rejected by students and the challenges from the interaction. An increase in the “Anticipated high workload to respond” feedback results in a faster decline in the overall number of students rejecting feedback, which is the only factor statistically signiﬁcant (Wald χ2(1) = 2.432, p < .05). In other words, stu- dents are more likely to reject form-focused feedback (such as formatting and grammatical errors) when they are cognitively overloaded by the overwhelming amount of feedback. As for the Content-focused AI",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,12,"χ2(1) = 2.432, p < .05). In other words, stu- dents are more likely to reject form-focused feedback (such as formatting and grammatical errors) when they are cognitively overloaded by the overwhelming amount of feedback. As for the Content-focused AI feedback, the model ﬁtness (χ2 = 151.836, p < .05) showed a rela- tionship between Content-focused feedback rejected by students and the challenges from the inter- action. Notably, all the main factors in the model had statistical signiﬁcance, as demonstrated in Table 6. The feedback provided by AI had a greater impact on students’ rejection in terms of “Mismatch with learners’ expectations” (Wald χ2(1) = 13.677, p < .000), “Anticipated high work- load to respond” (Wald χ2(1) = 18.253, p < .000) and “Mismatch with external references” (Wald χ2(1) = 14.541, p < .000), when compared to “Impeding conditions” (Wald χ2(1) = 6.877, p < .000). In summary, it may be interpreted from four perspectives. Firstly, AI-generated content- focused feedback may not align with participants’ expectations or be perceived as useful, creating a disconnect between the AI’s output and the",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,12,"χ2(1) = 6.877, p < .000). In summary, it may be interpreted from four perspectives. Firstly, AI-generated content- focused feedback may not align with participants’ expectations or be perceived as useful, creating a disconnect between the AI’s output and the student’s learning objectives. Secondly, the level of effort required to engage with AI feedback can be signiﬁcantly higher than what students are able to accommodate, leading to cognitive overload and disengagement. Thirdly, AI-generated feedback may not correspond to or support the feedback provided by teachers and peers, causing confusion and inconsistency in the learning process. Lastly, students may lack the necessary skills to compre- hend and respond to AI-generated content-focused feedback, further exacerbating the issue. Table 4. Descriptive data and proportion of the students’ rejection. Factors of UTAUT Form—Rejection Content—Rejection Mismatch with learners’ expectations 25 36.2% 37 37.8% Anticipated high workload to respond 24 34.8% 33 33.7% Impeding conditions 16 23.2% 7 7.1% Mismatch with external references 4 5.8% 21 21.4% Sum of rejection 69 98 12 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",172
