DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,14,"time, according to statistical analysis, peer and teacher encouragement and support have a limited impact. Interestingly, this ﬁnding differs somewhat from previous research, which suggested that lear- ners’ high-level utilization of chatbots with the conditions aligned with assignment and learning tasks (Hew et al., 2023). The observed discrepancy can potentially be attributed to the fact that students pri- oritize self-evaluation on AI feedback over external evaluations such as teachers or other evaluators. Another aim of this study was to examine the impact of various factors on students’ negative behaviors with ChatGPT, with a speciﬁc focus on the feedback types related to form and content. Concerning form-focused feedback rejected by students, it was found that only the “Anticipated high workload to respond” feedback signiﬁcantly affected the rejection of AI feed- back. Upon conducting a text analysis, we identiﬁed two signiﬁcant challenges students face when receiving ChatGPT-generated feedback. The ﬁrst challenge is the issue of overwhelming information in their feedback, making it difﬁcult for students to absorb and comprehend. The second challenge is the tendency for feedback to be too general rather",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,14,"students face when receiving ChatGPT-generated feedback. The ﬁrst challenge is the issue of overwhelming information in their feedback, making it difﬁcult for students to absorb and comprehend. The second challenge is the tendency for feedback to be too general rather than highlighting speciﬁc mistakes. These ﬁndings were also reported by Yang et al. (2023), who indicated that feedback lacking examples and contextual information receives lower student utilization rates than error- corrective and general feedback. Several factors could explain this observation. Firstly, students prefer a passive learning approach, commonly practiced in traditional classrooms, as it appears less time-consuming and effort-intensive than active learning. Another possible explanation is that students may not possess adequate feedback literacy to understand and act upon external feed- back. This is also supported by Carless and Boud (2018), who stated that seeking and applying feedback effectively are two essential features of learners with high feedback literacy. Regarding the content-focused feedback rejected by students, it is surprising that all four types of feedback signiﬁcantly affected the rejection of AI feedback. The ordinal regression result shows that the challenge",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,14,"two essential features of learners with high feedback literacy. Regarding the content-focused feedback rejected by students, it is surprising that all four types of feedback signiﬁcantly affected the rejection of AI feedback. The ordinal regression result shows that the challenge of “Mismatch with learners’ expectations” and “Mismatch with external refer- ences” has the most signiﬁcant predictive power in determining the rejection rate of students. In contrast, “Impeding conditions” have a minimal effect. In other words, the rejection rate of AI-generated feedback is primarily inﬂuenced by perceived usefulness and its degree of alignment with teacher and peer feedback. AI-generated content-focused feedback that does not align with the suggestions of teachers and peers and is perceived as less useful in improving the quality of assign- ments is less likely to be accepted and responded to positively. These results support earlier research that examined the link between an individual’s self-perception of usefulness and their likelihood to use technology, highlighting that the perceived usefulness of technology is a signiﬁcant predictor of behavioral intention (Chen et al., 2020). However, the negative emotions and feelings students",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,14,"examined the link between an individual’s self-perception of usefulness and their likelihood to use technology, highlighting that the perceived usefulness of technology is a signiﬁcant predictor of behavioral intention (Chen et al., 2020). However, the negative emotions and feelings students experience while seeking feedback and learning with an AI chatbot are not the primary factors con- tributing to their rejection of ChatGPT-generated feedback. This interesting result could be due to students’ valuing the utility of the feedback over their emotional response to it. 14 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",89
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,15,"Limitations Despite the valuable ﬁndings of this research, several limitations must be acknowledged. Firstly, while our study provided an analysis of feedback, a more systematic and in-depth analysis could have more signiﬁcant pedagogical implications for both language teachers and learners. The scope of our analysis was limited, which only targeted mistakes and errors at two levels: form-focused and content-focused. A more comprehensive examination of feedback, considering various aspects such as tone, clarity, speciﬁcity, and relevance, could provide more nuanced insights into the efﬁcacy of AI-generated feedback. Secondly, the sample size of our study was relatively small, and the participants were predominantly from higher education. This speciﬁc demographic might only be representative of some L2 learners, potentially limiting the generalizability of our ﬁndings to other learner groups. Thirdly, although our study focuses on learners’ engagement strat- egies, it is worth noting that data triangulation, incorporating multiple data sources (such as lear- ners’ self-reported data), could enhance the reliability and validity of our ﬁndings. This approach could allow future studies to generalize the ﬁndings on a larger scale and provide a more",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,15,"that data triangulation, incorporating multiple data sources (such as lear- ners’ self-reported data), could enhance the reliability and validity of our ﬁndings. This approach could allow future studies to generalize the ﬁndings on a larger scale and provide a more compre- hensive understanding of L2 learners’ experiences with AI-generated feedback. In conclusion, future research should address these limitations and further explore this complex issue. Conclusion Guided by the UTAUT model, our research aimed to investigate the challenges encountered by L2 learners when seeking corrective feedback on academic writing from ChatGPT. Initially, this study identiﬁes four signiﬁcant challenges faced by L2 learners when receiving feedback generated by ChatGPT, encompassing factors such as “Mismatch with learners’ expectations,” “Anticipated high workload to respond,” “Mismatch with external references,” and “Impeding conditions.” Subsequently, our investigation delves into the impact of the four barriers on students’ rejection of form-focused and content-focused feedback. Concerning form-focused feedback, we ﬁnd that the main signiﬁcant predictor of student rejection is the extraordinarily high level of effort required to comprehend feedback. Conversely, the rejection rate of AI-generated content-focused feedback is inﬂuenced",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,15,"rejection of form-focused and content-focused feedback. Concerning form-focused feedback, we ﬁnd that the main signiﬁcant predictor of student rejection is the extraordinarily high level of effort required to comprehend feedback. Conversely, the rejection rate of AI-generated content-focused feedback is inﬂuenced by various factors, including the disparity between students’ expectations and the actual feedback received, feedback necessitating substantial comprehension time, AI-generated feedback conﬂicting with teacher suggestions, and individual limitations in cognitive and emotional abilities. These ﬁndings offer valuable insights into the challenges L2 learners encounter in accept- ing and utilizing AI-generated feedback. In response to our study’s ﬁndings on the challenges L2 learners face when using ChatGPT for feedback in academic writing, we propose speciﬁc pedagogical strategies. We recommend Chen et al. 15",122
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,16,"integrating AI feedback with reliable external learning resources to mitigate inconsistencies and developing comprehensive support materials to assist students in comprehending formative correct- ive AI comments. To address the signiﬁcant effort required to process feedback, we suggest estab- lishing courses on computational thinking and designing materials to enhance critical evaluation skills (Ng et al., 2022). These strategies are intended to develop students’ ability to interpret and utilize AI-generated feedback, thus enhancing their academic writing. Contributorship Ziqi Chen was responsible for analyzing the data, composing both the Abstract and the bulk of the main body, and addressing the reviewers’ comments. Wei Wei focused on compiling the recent literature regarding the impact of technology-assisted feedback on second language teaching and learning, contributing signiﬁcantly to the paper’s Literature Review and Discussion sections. Xinhua Zhu and Wei Wei examined various aspects of the dissertation, including theoretical analyses of the challenges students encounter while revising their essays with feedback generated by ChatGPT, and how these challenges inﬂuence students’ rejection of ChatGPT-generated feedback. Yuan Yao was responsible for ﬁnalizing the thesis and offered valuable guidance throughout",180
DOC_033,NEW15.pdf,10.1177/20965311241305140,16,"including theoretical analyses of the challenges students encounter while revising their essays with feedback generated by ChatGPT, and how these challenges inﬂuence students’ rejection of ChatGPT-generated feedback. Yuan Yao was responsible for ﬁnalizing the thesis and offered valuable guidance throughout the writing process. Declaration of conﬂicting interests The authors declared no potential conﬂicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publica- tion of this article. Ethical statement The project adhered to all ethical regulations, with participants providing active and passive written consent for the use of the information they shared for research purposes. It was conducted with the authorization and coordination of Macao Polytechnic University under project number RP/FCA-08-2023. Funding The authors disclosed receipt of the following ﬁnancial support for the research, authorship, and/or publi- cation of this article: This study was supported by the Macao Polytechnic University Research Grant (RP/FCA-08-2023). ORCID iDs Ziqi Chen https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3139-9723 Yuan Yao https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0665-7065 16 ECNU Review of Education 9(1)",157
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) Education Tech Research Dev https://doi.org/10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z RESEARCH ARTICLE Development and validation of Generative Artificial Intelligence Readiness and Perception (GenAI‑RP) Scale for faculty and students in higher education Stella Y. Kim1 · Ji Yae Bong1 · Daniel Maxwell1 · Beth Oyarzun1 Received: 7 April 2025 / Accepted: 15 July 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Abstract This study developed and validated a suite of Generative Artificial Intelligence Readiness and Perception (GenAI-RP) Scale for faculty and students in higher education. Three sub- scales, including a) Readiness, b) Benefit, and c) Challenge were created based on a review of relevant literature and validated through content experts’ review and confirmatory factor analysis (CFA). The Readiness scale includes three factors: a) GenAI Comprehension, b) Ethical Awareness of GenAI, and c) GenAI Utilization and Proficiency. The Benefit scale consists of two factors: a) Effectiveness and b) Empowerment. Lastly, the Challenge scale has three factors: a) Ethics and Privacy Concerns, b) Negative Educational Impact, and c) Accuracy and Sensitivity. All scales demonstrated a satisfactory model fit for both groups based on CFA, except the challenge scale for faculty.",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,1,"Lastly, the Challenge scale has three factors: a) Ethics and Privacy Concerns, b) Negative Educational Impact, and c) Accuracy and Sensitivity. All scales demonstrated a satisfactory model fit for both groups based on CFA, except the challenge scale for faculty. Keywords Generative Artificial Intelligence · Readiness to use AI · Perceptions on AI Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has rapidly gained popularity in various aspects of our lives, including personal life, work environments, and education (Zhang & Vil- lanueva, 2023). Unlike task-specific or rule-based AI, GenAI is extremely versatile, capa- ble of producing diverse outputs such as texts, images, codes, audio and videos (Brown et al., 2020). This unique and appealing feature has led to numerous applications across various domains (Qadir, 2022). Studies have shown that GenAI can enhance teaching effectiveness (Chaudhry & Kazim, 2021; Kexin et al., 2020; Weng & Chiu, 2023) and promote student learning * Stella Y. Kim skim113@charlotte.edu Ji Yae Bong jbong@charlotte.edu Daniel Maxwell dmaxwel8@charlotte.edu Beth Oyarzun beth.oyarzun@charlotte.edu 1 University of North Carolina at Charlotte, 9201 University City Blvd., Charlotte, NC 28223, USA",176
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,1,"Chiu, 2023) and promote student learning * Stella Y. Kim skim113@charlotte.edu Ji Yae Bong jbong@charlotte.edu Daniel Maxwell dmaxwel8@charlotte.edu Beth Oyarzun beth.oyarzun@charlotte.edu 1 University of North Carolina at Charlotte, 9201 University City Blvd., Charlotte, NC 28223, USA",36
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,2,"S. Y. Kim et al. through personalized and adaptive learning (Chiu et al., 2023; Kabudi et al., 2021) as well as elevated motivation (Chen et al., 2020; Hwang et al., 2020). Given this significant soci- etal transformation, recent studies highlighted the potential significant impacts of Gen-AI in education and emphasized the need for both instructors and students to be adequately prepared for this new teaching and learning environment (Alphonso, 2023; Chiu, 2024; Gökoğlu & Erdoğdu, 2025; Kohnke et al., 2023). Considering the widespread use of GenAI and its potential benefits, it is crucial to accurately measure the preparedness and perceptions of both instructors and students. In technology-integrated educational settings, instructors and students are central stakehold- ers because they interact with each other, and each occupies a unique role in the teaching and learning process (Li, 2007). Studying only one group provides an incomplete picture of the dynamics at play (Li, 2007). While researchers have included both instructor- and student-related variables, these are often surveyed only among students (Diep et al., 2017; Keengwe, 2007; Selim, 2007; Sharif-Nia et al., 2024) within",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,2,"provides an incomplete picture of the dynamics at play (Li, 2007). While researchers have included both instructor- and student-related variables, these are often surveyed only among students (Diep et al., 2017; Keengwe, 2007; Selim, 2007; Sharif-Nia et al., 2024) within their specific educational contexts, which limits the ability to fully capture the perspectives or disconnects of each group in the same educational context. In contrast, Lai et al. (2016) addressed this limita- tion by surveying both instructors and students within a single study, using identical survey items for both groups to enable direct comparison. Their approach revealed structural dif- ferences between instructors and students in mobile learning. However, this approach still overlooked the need for items that reflect the distinct roles within technology-integrated environments. To our knowledge, no measurement scale has been developed specifically for both instructors and students in the context of AI-enhanced education. The current literature tends to focus on either instructors (Akanzire et al., 2025; Zhang & Villanueva, 2023) or students (Lemke et al., 2023), rather than both. To address this gap, the present study intro- duces",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,2,"the context of AI-enhanced education. The current literature tends to focus on either instructors (Akanzire et al., 2025; Zhang & Villanueva, 2023) or students (Lemke et al., 2023), rather than both. To address this gap, the present study intro- duces the Generative Artificial Intelligence Readiness and Perception (GenAI-RP) Scale, designed to capture the unique perspectives of instructors and students within an AI-inte- grated learning environment and validated for each group using confirmatory factor analy- sis (CFA). The scale comprises three subscales: (a) readiness to use GenAI for education, (b) perceived benefits of GenAI for education, and (c) perceived challenges of using GenAI for education. While each subscale covers the same core variables for both groups, the sur- vey items themselves are tailored to the unique roles of instructors and students. Existing framework‑based readiness and perception scales Chan and Zhou (2023) developed and validated an Expectancy-Value Theory (EVT)- based instrument to measure student perceptions of GenAI and their intention to use GenAI tools. Their study included 405 student responses from a Hong Kong university, with participants representing undergraduate and postgraduate students",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,2,"developed and validated an Expectancy-Value Theory (EVT)- based instrument to measure student perceptions of GenAI and their intention to use GenAI tools. Their study included 405 student responses from a Hong Kong university, with participants representing undergraduate and postgraduate students across STEM and non-STEM disciplines. Their instrument featured 23 five-point Likert-scale items intended to measure knowledge, perceived value, and perceived cost. Their perceived value construct was broken into the EVT-aligned categories of attainment value, intrin- sic value, and utility value (Wigfield & Eccles, 2000). For example, attainment value items assessed student beliefs that GenAI could support career preparation, academic performance, and digital competence; intrinsic value items assessed anonymity, com- fort, and willingness to ask questions; and utility value items assessed tool availability, unique insights, and time savings. Perceived cost items then assessed student concerns",134
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,3,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… of GenAI’s potential to undermine education values, contribute to overreliance, limit social interaction, and hinder the development of transferable skills such as problem solving or teamwork. Chan and Zhou (2023) used several evaluation criteria to validate their EVT-based instrument to measure student perceptions, including Cronbach’s alpha, CFA, average variance extracted, composite reliability, and heterotrait-monotrait ratio. Internal consist- ency was acceptable for the knowledge, perceived value, and perceived cost constructs, with Cronbach’s alpha values of .812, .876, and .746 for each. CFA results also supported acceptable model fit for knowledge, perceived value, and perceived cost constructs, and additional convergent and discriminant validity evidence was reported through composite reliability, average variance extracted, and HTMT ratios. Using this instrument, Chan and Zhou (2023) reported positive correlations between perceived value and intention to use GenAI and a weak negative correlation between perceived cost and intention to use, not- ing their decision to leverage an EVT-based instrument due to EVT’s inclusion of “cost” as a concept (Wigfield & Eccles, 1992), which is not as clearly reflected in models like",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,3,"negative correlation between perceived cost and intention to use, not- ing their decision to leverage an EVT-based instrument due to EVT’s inclusion of “cost” as a concept (Wigfield & Eccles, 1992), which is not as clearly reflected in models like Technology Acceptance Model (Davis, 1989) or Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (Venkatesh et al., 2003). The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (Venkatesh et al., 2003) was employed as a framework in multiple previous GenAI readiness and perception studies to evaluate perceptions of performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, and facilitating conditions (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Yilmaz et al., 2023). The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology 2, with its expanded addition of hedonic motivation, price value, and habit as concepts, was also featured in multiple studies (Foroughi et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2024). Yilmaz et al. (2023) developed an instrument that leveraged the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology to measure students’ acceptance of GenAI. The development of this instrument featured quality measures like expert reviews, pilot testing, and exploratory factor",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,3,"Yilmaz et al. (2023) developed an instrument that leveraged the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology to measure students’ acceptance of GenAI. The development of this instrument featured quality measures like expert reviews, pilot testing, and exploratory factor analysis (EFA) and CFA of their scale, which included performance expectancy, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions, and social influence as structures. Yilmaz et al. (2023) reported the reliability and validity of their instrument as robust based on Cronbach’s alpha coefficient of 0.97 and subsequent 0.95 using the test–retest method. Strzelecki (2024) and Foroughi et al. (2023) adopted Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology 2 to assess student acceptance and use of OpenAI’s ChatGPT. Str- zelecki’s (2024) instrument integrated perceptions of personal innovativeness, and found that performance expectancy, habit, and hedonic motivation are positively correlated with intention to use ChatGPT for future tasks. Like Strzelecki (2024), Foroughi et al. (2023) also included the variable of personal innovativeness in their study, and Foroughi et al. (2023) also included information accuracy as a variable. The Technology Readiness Index TRI measures the propensity",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,3,"for future tasks. Like Strzelecki (2024), Foroughi et al. (2023) also included the variable of personal innovativeness in their study, and Foroughi et al. (2023) also included information accuracy as a variable. The Technology Readiness Index TRI measures the propensity of an individual to embrace and utilize a new technology, including technology readiness factors like opti- mism, innovativeness, and discomfort and insecurity as factors (Parasuraman & Colby, 2015). Lemke et al. (2023) combined the Technology Readiness Index with the Technol- ogy Acceptance Model (Davis, 1989) to measure readiness and acceptance of Large Lan- guage Models by leveraging optimism and discomfort as variables that influence perceived usefulness and ease of use. Wang et al. (2023) developed an AI Readiness Framework to measure teacher percep- tions and readiness for GenAI, with their instrument featuring four factors to measure teacher readiness and perceptions: cognition, ability, vision, and ethics. Comparative analysis revealed three distinct clusters of teacher readiness: high, intermediate, and low levels of readiness.",161
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,4,"S. Y. Kim et al. Teachers with high readiness had a lower threat perception and higher perception of job satis- faction (Wang et al., 2023). Leveraging the Theory of Planned Behavior (Ajzen, 1985) and Control-Value Theory (Pekrun, 2006) as theoretical frameworks, Luo and Zou (2024) developed and validated their 18-item University Learners’ Readiness for ChatGPT-Assisted English Learning Scale to evaluate learners’ perceptions of their ability, attitudes, and emotional responses as they used ChatGPT for English language learning. CFA revealed a good fit for the seven subscales to the data, with exploratory structural equation modeling leveraged to confirm the seven-factor structure. Cronbach’s alpha revealed high internal consistency reliability of the instrument, with convergent and discriminant validity also tested to affirm that the Learners’ Readiness for ChatGPT-Assisted English Learning Scale is a valid and reliable instrument for assessing the readiness of university students to use ChatGPT to assist in English learning. Amani and colleagues (2023) developed a survey to assess the perceptions of students, fac- ulty and university staff from Texas A&M University on ChatGPT. The student survey gath- ered responses from",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,4,"students to use ChatGPT to assist in English learning. Amani and colleagues (2023) developed a survey to assess the perceptions of students, fac- ulty and university staff from Texas A&M University on ChatGPT. The student survey gath- ered responses from 813 students on their familiarity with ChatGPT, their use of ChatGPT for academic work, perceptions of appropriate use, perceptions of academic dishonesty, and perceived effects of ChatGPT on numerous skills including critical thinking, problem solv- ing, teamwork, and student engagement. The faculty and staff version, completed by 243 par- ticipants, included parallel questions about familiarity and use, but also included questions to ascertain perceptions on comfort with students using ChatGPT, perceived likelihood of honor code violations before and after ChatGPT’s public release, acceptable external resources for students, and perceived benefits and effects on student outcomes. While their survey is valua- ble in capturing early perceptions from both higher education students and faculty/staff shortly after ChatGPT’s release, the instrument appears to have been developed more for an institu- tion-specific, exploratory needs assessment. While several of their topics were based on prior",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,4,"ble in capturing early perceptions from both higher education students and faculty/staff shortly after ChatGPT’s release, the instrument appears to have been developed more for an institu- tion-specific, exploratory needs assessment. While several of their topics were based on prior literature, Amani et al. (2023) did not report a formal construct map, proposed factor structure, or validity or reliability estimates for their instrument. As such, this present study primarily utilized the instrument developed by Amani et al. (2023) as a source for identifying relevant, applied perception and challenge items like uses of ChatGPT, comfortability, and potential for future disciplinary impact. Considered together, these prior instruments provide foundations for examining GenAI perceptions and readiness, but they also reflect the contexts in which they were developed and implemented. Several instruments featured student-only samples (e.g., Chan & Zhou, 2023; Lou & Zou, 2024). Other studies were situated in particular institutional contexts (e.g., Amani et al., 2023). Consideration of these contextual factors is important as perceptions may vary based on policy, disciplinary expectations, prior exposure to GenAI, and other institutional or environmental norms. As",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,4,"Other studies were situated in particular institutional contexts (e.g., Amani et al., 2023). Consideration of these contextual factors is important as perceptions may vary based on policy, disciplinary expectations, prior exposure to GenAI, and other institutional or environmental norms. As a result, instruments developed or implemented in a single context may not transfer directly to broader higher education populations without additional measures of validation. In response to this potential limitation, the present instrument was developed in an effort to be used across institutional contexts and between student and faculty populations while still capturing perceptions of readiness, benefits, and challenges in higher education. Validity and reliability of existing instruments These studies employed a range of measures to ensure validity and reliability in their instruments of either student or faculty perceptions. To explore construct validity amongst these instruments, EFA and CFA were among the most commonly used meth- ods (Bautista et al., 2024; Chan & Zhou, 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Yilmaz et al., 2024),",163
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,5,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… with Luo and Zou (2024) also utilizing exploratory structural equation modeling to analyze their dataset. Composite reliability and average variance extracted were pop- ular methods of evaluating convergent validity across studies (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Foroughi et al., 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Strzelecki, 2024; Wang et al., 2023; Yilmaz et al., 2023). Chan and Zhou (2023), Foroughi et al. (2023), and Luo and Zou (2024) employed heterotrait-monotrait ratio, while Yilmaz et al. (2023) and Wang et al. (2023) used Fornell-Larcker criterion to measure discriminant validity. Cronbach’s alpha was utilized across many studies to measure the internal consistency reliability of devel- oped instruments (Bautista et al., 2024; Chan & Zhou, 2023; Foroughi et al., 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Strzelecki, 2024; Wang et al., 2023; Yilmaz et al., 2023), with Yilmaz et al. (2023) also utilizing test–retest reliability within their study. Factors influencing GenAI adoption amongst instructors or students Previous studies reported a range of motivational factors that influence GenAI per- ceptions amongst instructors or students. Motivational factors such as perceived value (Wigfield &",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,5,"also utilizing test–retest reliability within their study. Factors influencing GenAI adoption amongst instructors or students Previous studies reported a range of motivational factors that influence GenAI per- ceptions amongst instructors or students. Motivational factors such as perceived value (Wigfield & Eccles, 2000), hedonic motivation (Kang et al., 2015; Kasneci et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2024), and habit (Strzelecki, 2024) are a significant predictor of GenAI intention and utilization. Diverse cognitive factors, such as knowledge, awareness, and understanding, fos- ter positive dispositions toward GenAI tools (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Kaya et al., 2024). Performance expectancy (Foroughi et al., 2023; Strzelecki, 2024; Yilmaz et al., 2023) and effort expectancy (Strzelecki, 2024) are shown to be likely predictors of AI accept- ance, as the easier it is for a user to utilize a tool for a given purpose, influences their perception of that tool as useful. Multiple affective factors, such as optimism and enjoyment (Lemke et al., 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Wang et al., 2023), are shown to positively influence AI percep- tions, especially as users reflect on prior experience with technological tools.",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,5,"as useful. Multiple affective factors, such as optimism and enjoyment (Lemke et al., 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Wang et al., 2023), are shown to positively influence AI percep- tions, especially as users reflect on prior experience with technological tools. How- ever, additional affective factors like perceived cost, anxiety or discomfort, and bore- dom, have been shown to negatively impact perceptions of these technologies (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024; Venkatesh et al., 2003; Wang & Xue, 2024; Wang et al., 2023; Wu et al., 2024). Contextual factors include social influence and facilitating conditions. Social influ- ence has been found to have varying impacts on technology acceptance (Strzelecki, 2024; Yilmaz et al., 2023), while facilitating conditions like a supportive environ- ment and infrastructure, indirectly influence perceptions of these technologies (Akan- zire et al., 2025; Foroughi et al., 2023; Kaya et al., 2023; Mozelius, 2024; Strzelecki, 2024). Last but certainly not least, ethical considerations play an outsized role in GenAI perceptions (Akanzire et al., 2025). Concerns surrounding bias, privacy, alignment to human values, and inaccurate responses negatively affect perceptions",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,5,"Kaya et al., 2023; Mozelius, 2024; Strzelecki, 2024). Last but certainly not least, ethical considerations play an outsized role in GenAI perceptions (Akanzire et al., 2025). Concerns surrounding bias, privacy, alignment to human values, and inaccurate responses negatively affect perceptions of these tools, especially for educational purposes (Dotan et al., 2024; Jobin et al., 2019; Kaplan et al., 2024; Kutty et al., 2024; Lindahl & Saeid, 2023; Nyaaba et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024). These ethical considerations highlight the need for AI literacy, effective curricular integration, and professional learning experiences (Cervantes et al., 2024; Michel-Villarreal et al., 2023; Yeralan & Lee, 2023).",103
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,6,"S. Y. Kim et al. Limitations of existing studies Analysis of instruments and methods in prior research reveals several limitations and opportunities for future research. First, many studies that utilize valid and reliable instru- ments rely heavily on self-reported data (Bautista et al., 2024; Luo & Zou, 2024), where a potential for response bias is noted (Chan & Zhou, 2023). Additionally, many prior studies have limited diversity amongst samples, with smaller homogenous samples present across studies (Akanzire et al., 2025; Chan & Zhou, 2023; Lemke et al., 2023; Maaß et al., 2024). These studies often focus on specific populations, i.e., students (Lemke et al., 2023; Luo & Zou, 2024) or teachers (Akanzire et al., 2025; Zhang & Villanueva, 2023) but do not examine both simultaneously, thereby limiting a more comprehensive understanding that integrates both perspectives. Many instruments are also designed to target specific sub- jects, limiting the generalizability of findings as well as the utility of those instruments in researching other contexts (Luo & Zou, 2024). Furthermore, lack of exploration of individual differences like cultural background, prior experience, or learning",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,6,"to target specific sub- jects, limiting the generalizability of findings as well as the utility of those instruments in researching other contexts (Luo & Zou, 2024). Furthermore, lack of exploration of individual differences like cultural background, prior experience, or learning preferences and their potential impact on perceptions and use limits our understanding of how those individual differences may influence perceptions (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Lemke et al., 2023). This limitation may stem from the absence of valid and reliable instruments designed for a broader population. These limitations point to a need for a validated instrument designed for both faculty and students that captures each group’s unique perspectives while remaining applicable across higher education contexts. Research questions 1. To what extent does GenAI-RP demonstrate construct-related validity, as supported by confirmatory factor analysis for students? 2. How well does GenAI-RP show construct-related validity, as indicated by confirmatory factor analysis for faculty? Method This study was carried out in two stages for scale development and validation. In the first stage, relevant studies addressing AI, GenAI, or ChatGPT; students and/or instructors; and perceptions, acceptance,",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,6,"validity, as indicated by confirmatory factor analysis for faculty? Method This study was carried out in two stages for scale development and validation. In the first stage, relevant studies addressing AI, GenAI, or ChatGPT; students and/or instructors; and perceptions, acceptance, or readiness were identified, selected, and reviewed. To capture recent trends in AI tools in education, only studies published in 2023 or 2024 and retrieved via Google Scholar at the time of the search were considered. Following screening, 13 sur- vey studies employing various theoretical frameworks or custom-built scales, three qualita- tive studies, and one commentary article were thoroughly reviewed to generate an initial pool of 56 survey items. These items were categorized into readiness (n = 18), perceived benefits (n = 16), and perceived challenges (n = 22) after removing irrelevant, duplicate, or similar items. The instruments were then modified to suit GenAI contexts and to ensure applicability for both instructors and students. To ensure content adequacy and readability, three content experts reviewed the adapted instrument, and their feedback was incorpo- rated into further revisions. As a result, the",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,6,"modified to suit GenAI contexts and to ensure applicability for both instructors and students. To ensure content adequacy and readability, three content experts reviewed the adapted instrument, and their feedback was incorpo- rated into further revisions. As a result, the instructor version comprised 17 items for readi- ness, 11 for perceived benefits, and 21 for perceived challenges, while the student version included 13 items for readiness, 14 for perceived benefits, and 21 for perceived challenges.",75
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,7,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… The second stage focused on validating the hypothesized factor structure through factor analysis. We conducted EFA to identify and verify the number of factors when no specific factor structure existed. CFA was then performed to finalize the factor structure. We refer to our instrument as the Generative Artificial Intelligence Readiness and Per- ception (GenAI-RP) Scale. The perception part encompasses both the benefits and chal- lenges to provide a balanced view. Therefore, the GenAI-RP scale comprises three dis- tinct subscales: a) Readiness scale, b) Benefit scale, and c) Challenge scale. Our validation efforts were focused on each subscale individually. Each subscale includes parallel factors for both student and instructor versions. Since students and instructors have distinct roles and interact with GenAI differently in the same educational context, the survey items do not need to be identical. This approach supports a comprehensive understanding of GenAI integration from both perspectives and ensures the scale is broadly applicable in higher education. Scale development process Readiness scale This subscale is specifically designed to assess an individual’s readiness to use",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,7,"identical. This approach supports a comprehensive understanding of GenAI integration from both perspectives and ensures the scale is broadly applicable in higher education. Scale development process Readiness scale This subscale is specifically designed to assess an individual’s readiness to use GenAI for education, defined as “the perceived state of preparedness of an individual (student or instructor) in terms of cognitive understanding, ethical consideration, and practical skills regarding the use of generative artificial intelligence for teaching or learning.” This defini- tion is adapted from prior research on readiness (Karaca et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023) and tailored to the context of this study, which targets both students and instructors across diverse disciplines in higher education. The Readiness scale was developed by adapting the AI-readiness survey (Wang et al., 2023). This survey tool has undergone statistical validation and demonstrated high internal consistency reliability. Although the survey tool was intended for primary school contexts, its focus on general teaching and learning aspects of AI was considered highly relevant for the present study. The original scale includes four factors: Cognition (5 items), Ability",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,7,"consistency reliability. Although the survey tool was intended for primary school contexts, its focus on general teaching and learning aspects of AI was considered highly relevant for the present study. The original scale includes four factors: Cognition (5 items), Ability (6 items), Vision (3 items), and Ethics (4 items). We selected and modified 10 survey items from these factors to ensure that our scale is applicable to both faculty and students across diverse disciplines in higher education, with a specific focus on GenAI rather than on gen- eral AI technologies. Four items from Wang et al (2023) were excluded due to concerns regarding respondents’ varying interpretations (Items CO1, CO2) and limited generaliz- ability across learning contexts (Items CO5, ET4). For Factor 3, GenAI Utilization and Proficiency, we included a new item: “I am proficient and ready to utilize GenAI tools for [learning/teaching].” Additionally, the faculty version of Factor 3 includes four extra survey items from Wang et al. (2023) to account for the distinct use cases in the teaching context. After content validation and factor-structure validation (discussed in the results",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,7,"for [learning/teaching].” Additionally, the faculty version of Factor 3 includes four extra survey items from Wang et al. (2023) to account for the distinct use cases in the teaching context. After content validation and factor-structure validation (discussed in the results section below), the final scale comprised three factors rather than four: a) GenAI Comprehension, b) Ethical Awareness of GenAI, and c) GenAI Utilization and Proficiency. GenAI Com- prehension included modified survey items from the Cognition and Vision factors of Wang et al. (2023), while the other two factors incorporated items adapted from Ethics and Abil- ity, respectively. The study’s findings also prompted revisions to the definitions of each factor (Karaca et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2023) to ensure accuracy. The definitions of each factor are as follows:",128
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,8,"S. Y. Kim et al. GenAI Comprehension: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] comprehends how GenAI technologies are trained, their functionality, and their application in educa- tional contexts. Ethical Awareness of GenAI: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] is aware of the ethical and safety aspects of GenAI technologies. GenAI Utilization and Proficiency: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] feels capable to use GenAI technologies proficiently for [learning/teaching]. Note that the brackets are used to highlight differing definitions between the student and instructor scales. The term listed first within the brackets applies to the student ver- sion, while the term that follows applies to the instructor version of the scale. Benefit scale We defined benefits as “perceived benefits of an individual in terms of effectiveness and empowerment regarding the use of generative AI for learning or teaching.” The final Benefit Scale consisted of two primary dimensions: effectiveness and empowerment. Survey items for each factor were adapted from constructs identified in previous studies, including Utility Value, Attainment Value, and Intrinsic Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023), Performance Expectancy and Learning Value (Foroughi",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,8,"Scale consisted of two primary dimensions: effectiveness and empowerment. Survey items for each factor were adapted from constructs identified in previous studies, including Utility Value, Attainment Value, and Intrinsic Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023), Performance Expectancy and Learning Value (Foroughi et al., 2023), Optimism (Lemke et al., 2023), and AI-Enhanced Innovation (Wang et al., 2023). For further details on these studies and factors, refer to the Existing Framework-Based Readiness and Percep- tion Scales section. The final survey items for each factor were developed by combining and modifying items from multiple factors across studies that employed different theo- retical frameworks, such as the Extended Unified Theory of Acceptance 2 (Foroughi et al., 2023), the Technology Readiness Index (Lemke et al., 2023), and Expectancy- Value Theory (Chan & Zhou, 2023). This approach was intentional and aligned with the purpose of these theories, which is to identify enablers and inhibitors of technology adoption, avoidance, or resistance. The subscales, Benefits and Challenges, in this study represent enablers and inhibitors, respectively. Although empirical data on this aspect were not the primary focus, the main objective",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,8,"which is to identify enablers and inhibitors of technology adoption, avoidance, or resistance. The subscales, Benefits and Challenges, in this study represent enablers and inhibitors, respectively. Although empirical data on this aspect were not the primary focus, the main objective was to develop the scales. The definition of Effectiveness was informed by Performance Expectancy (Foroughi et al., 2023), Utility Value, and Attainment Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023), as these factors emphasize practical benefits for performance and outcomes, aligning with the intended meaning of the selected survey items. Both the student and faculty versions included seven survey items relevant to learning or teaching perspectives. The student version contained an additional item addressing the increasing importance of GenAI skills in the job market, adapted from Chan and Zhou (2023). The Empowerment factor is conceptually related to Intrinsic Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023) and AI-Enhanced Innovation (Wang et al., 2023). In the student version, Empow- erment aligns more closely with Intrinsic Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023), whereas in the faculty version, it is more closely associated with AI-Enhanced Innovation (Wang et al., 2023).",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,8,"and AI-Enhanced Innovation (Wang et al., 2023). In the student version, Empow- erment aligns more closely with Intrinsic Value (Chan & Zhou, 2023), whereas in the faculty version, it is more closely associated with AI-Enhanced Innovation (Wang et al., 2023). The Empowerment factor captures the differing perspectives of instructors and students, comprising six items for students and four for faculty (Table 1). The two factors assessed by the student version of the benefits scale are defined as follows:",78
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,9,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… Effectiveness: The degree to which GenAI technologies impact productivity, achieve- ment, and efficiency of learning. Empowerment: The degree to which GenAI technologies provide personalized, non- judgmental support and enhance student autonomy and comfort in the learning process. The two major factors measured by the instructor scale are defined as: Effectiveness: The degree to which GenAI technologies impact the ability of an instruc- tor to facilitate student learning and improve their academic performance. Empowerment: The degree to which GenAI technologies empower instructors to inno- vate teaching methods and personalize learner support. Challenge scale We define challenges in using GenAI for education as “perceived concerns of an individual in terms of ethical awareness, educational impact, and accuracy regarding the use of gen- erative AI for learning or teaching.” The development of the challenge scale was primarily informed by six studies: Amani et al. (2023), Chan and Zhou (2023), Hmoud et al. (2024), Jo (2023), Sullivan et al. (2023), and Wang et al. (2023). The Ethics and Privacy Concerns factor comprises five survey items adapted from both",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,9,"informed by six studies: Amani et al. (2023), Chan and Zhou (2023), Hmoud et al. (2024), Jo (2023), Sullivan et al. (2023), and Wang et al. (2023). The Ethics and Privacy Concerns factor comprises five survey items adapted from both survey and qualitative findings from four studies (Amani et al., 2023; Hmoud et al., 2024; Jo, 2023; Sullivan et al., 2023). The Table 1 Benefit scale Items in brackets [] apply only to the Faculty instrument Items in parentheses () apply only to the Student instrument Factor Items Factor 1 (Effectiveness) 1. GenAI allows me to accomplish [learning/teaching] tasks more quickly 2. GenAI increases my productivity in [learning/teaching] 3. GenAI enhances my effectiveness in [learning/teaching] 4. GenAI can improve my [students’] academic performance 5. GenAI provides me with unique insights and perspectives that I may not have thought of myself 6. GenAI helps me achieve my [students’] learning goals 7. AI technologies enable me to accomplish tasks that were previously difficult to do without them (8). Students will need to use GenAI/ChatGPT for future careers Factor 2 (Empowerment) 9. GenAI",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,9,"6. GenAI helps me achieve my [students’] learning goals 7. AI technologies enable me to accomplish tasks that were previously difficult to do without them (8). Students will need to use GenAI/ChatGPT for future careers Factor 2 (Empowerment) 9. GenAI provides me with personalized and immediate feedback and sugges- tions for my [students’] assignments 10. GenAI gives students more control over their studies (11). I can ask questions to GenAI that I would otherwise not ask an instructor (12). GenAI will not judge me, so I feel comfortable with it (13). GenAI is useful for student support services due to anonymity (14). I am more comfortable receiving feedback from GenAI than an instructor [15]. GenAI allows me to experiment with innovative pedagogy [16]. GenAI enables me to organize teaching innovatively",130
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,10,"S. Y. Kim et al. definition of this factor was operationalized to accurately reflect the constructs measured by these items. The Negative Educational Impact factor includes nine survey items adapted from the Student-Perceived Cost of AI factor in Chan and Zhou (2023) and all survey items from the Perceived Threats from AI factor in Wang et al. (2023). Accordingly, the definition of this factor was also adapted from the definitions and descriptions of its respective factors. The Accuracy and Sensitivity factor’s survey items were adapted from all five items developed by Chan and Zhou (2023) to measure the factor, Students’ Knowledge of AI. Accordingly, the definition of the Accuracy and Sensitivity factor was also adapted from the operationalized definition provided by Chan and Zhou (2023). For the challenge scale, only a few minor changes were made between the instructor and student versions, as shown in Table 2. Also, the identification and definitions of the major factors remain similar between the two versions, as follows: Ethics and Privacy Concerns: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] is concerned about privacy, security, trust,",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,10,"student versions, as shown in Table 2. Also, the identification and definitions of the major factors remain similar between the two versions, as follows: Ethics and Privacy Concerns: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] is concerned about privacy, security, trust, ethics, and integrity of GenAI technologies. Negative Educational Impact: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] is concerned about the negative impacts of GenAI on education, social interaction, skill development, and instructor/student roles. Accuracy and Sensitivity: The degree to which a [learner/instructor] is concerned about inherent limitations, risks, biases, and accuracy of GenAI outputs. Content review for validity Once the research team identified the relevant factors and selected items – either adapted from existing measures or developed by the team – content experts were invited to review the items for clarity and relevance. This process is crucial to ensure alignment between the factors and items and to refine instruments for improved readability. Three subject matter experts reviewed the initial version of the scales and provided written feedback. Two sub- ject matter experts were experts in both teaching and researching in online",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,10,"the factors and items and to refine instruments for improved readability. Three subject matter experts reviewed the initial version of the scales and provided written feedback. Two sub- ject matter experts were experts in both teaching and researching in online learning, while the third was a graduate student in computer science with a similar research focus. To highlight a few comments from the reviewers, one suggested adding a definition of GenAI, as some may not fully understand the distinction between GenAI and AI. Another reviewer confirmed that the items generally covered the necessary content for the target constructs, while offering some minor editorial suggestions. Participants Data was collected in the spring of 2024 via an electronic survey. Invitations to participate were sent to several listservs of professional organizations in education, such as the Ameri- can Educational Research Association (AERA) Online Teaching and Learning SIG and the Association for Educational Technology (AECT). This study collected data from two distinct populations: a) students, and b) instruc- tors in higher education. The inclusion criterion was that participants must be students or instructors in",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,10,"Teaching and Learning SIG and the Association for Educational Technology (AECT). This study collected data from two distinct populations: a) students, and b) instruc- tors in higher education. The inclusion criterion was that participants must be students or instructors in higher education. Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval was obtained from the researchers’ institution and informed consent was collected from all",60
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,11,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… participants by requiring them to click on the “Yes, I consent” option on the front page of the online survey. The data included responses from 460 students and 132 faculty members. Demo- graphic information for the study participants is presented in Table 3. Student partici- pants were recruited from 41 states in the U.S., and one participant from outside the U.S. Also, slightly more than half of the students were female (55%) and between 18 and 24 years old (58.3%). Female students comprised 58% of U.S. higher education enrollment, while male students comprised 42%, which closely resembles the distribu- tion observed in our sample. Nearly half attended a state or public college or university (49.6%) and 43.9% were undergraduate students. Faculty participants came from 32 dis- tinct states and had an average of 11.25 years of teaching experience in higher educa- tion, with a minimum of 1 year and a maximum of 50 years. Also, nearly half of the Table 2 Challenge scale Factor Items Factor 1 (Ethics and Privacy Concerns) 1. When using",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,11,"years of teaching experience in higher educa- tion, with a minimum of 1 year and a maximum of 50 years. Also, nearly half of the Table 2 Challenge scale Factor Items Factor 1 (Ethics and Privacy Concerns) 1. When using GenAI, I am concerned about whether my personal information is protected 2. I have a feeling of anxiety resulting from not fully trusting the information the GenAI provides 3. GenAI enables academic dishonest behaviors 4. Students violate the Honor Code more now that GenAI is available 5. Instructors should use a tool(s) to detect students’ use of GenAI Factor 2 (Negative Educational Impact) 6. Using GenAI to complete assignments undermines the value of a university education 7. GenAI limits opportunities to interact with others and socialize while completing coursework 8. GenAI hinders my [students’] development of generic or transferable skills such as teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership skills 9. GenAI weakens the importance of teachers in education 10. GenAI has reduced the frequency of face-to-face communica- tion with [colleagues and students/teachers] 11. Students’ overreliance on GenAI undermines the relationship between teachers",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,11,"such as teamwork, problem-solving, and leadership skills 9. GenAI weakens the importance of teachers in education 10. GenAI has reduced the frequency of face-to-face communica- tion with [colleagues and students/teachers] 11. Students’ overreliance on GenAI undermines the relationship between teachers and students 12. Frequent use of GenAI technologies to assist [teaching / learning] reduces the thinking and decision-making abilities of [teachers/students] 13. Overuse of GenAI reduces the necessity of human teachers in the classroom 14. GenAI ultimately does not help student learning Factor 3 (Accuracy and Sensitivity) 15. GenAI has limitations in its ability to handle complex tasks 16. GenAI can generate output that is factually inaccurate 17. GenAI can generate output that is out of context or inappro- priate 18. GenAI can exhibit biases and unfairness in their output 19. GenAI has limited emotional intelligence and empathy, which can lead to output that is insensitive or inappropriate",148
