DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,13,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… predetermined factor structure, as items were intentionally selected to represent each factor. Consequently, CFA was conducted based on the pre-specified factor structure. Factor analyses require certain assumptions, such as the absence of extreme outliers, normality, factorability, and linearity. No extreme outliers were identified, but the normal- ity assumption was not satisfied, as indicated by the Shapiro–Wilk test with p < .001 for all the data examined. This suggests that a typical maximum likelihood estimator may not be ideal. Therefore, we used the robust maximum likelihood estimator with Huber-White standard errors (correction), which does not require multivariate normality. Bartlett’s test of sphericity was significant, indicating that the data were suitable for factor analysis. CFA was performed using the R package, lavaan (Rosseel et al., 2012). To evaluate the adequacy of the hypothesized model, we used a set of fit statistics, including the chi-square goodness of fit test, comparative fit index (CFI), Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI), root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA), and standardized root mean squared residual (SRMR). Specifically, RMSEA and SRMR values below 0.08",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,13,"a set of fit statistics, including the chi-square goodness of fit test, comparative fit index (CFI), Tucker-Lewis Index (TLI), root mean square error of approximation (RMSEA), and standardized root mean squared residual (SRMR). Specifically, RMSEA and SRMR values below 0.08 were considered to indicate a good fit (Kline, 2015), while CFI and TLI values above 0.90 were regarded as satisfactory (Kline, 2015). Results Readiness scale Student version The initial examination of EFA results suggested that the original four-factor solution pro- posed by Wang et al. (2023) was not tenable, as items measuring “Cognition” and “Vision” clustered together. A review of these items indicated that they potentially measure a single construct. For instance, Item 4 was originally in the “Vision” factor, but it can also be viewed as assessing the cognitive aspect of GenAI technologies. Thus, these two factors were merged into “GenAI Comprehension.” The final factor structure, along with items measuring each factor, is presented in Fig. 1. Results from CFA analyses confirmed the three-factor solution for the student data. Table 4 shows the model fit statistics for the three-factor",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,13,"into “GenAI Comprehension.” The final factor structure, along with items measuring each factor, is presented in Fig. 1. Results from CFA analyses confirmed the three-factor solution for the student data. Table 4 shows the model fit statistics for the three-factor solution. Additionally, all standardized factor loadings exceeded 0.40, supporting the hypothesized three-factor solution (Fig. 1). Faculty version There were four additional items, as noted in Table 5 with brackets []. As noted in Table 4, the initial three-factor model did not show adequate fit. A review of the modification index suggested that Item 9 might load on both Factors 2 and 3. While this item concerns secu- rity, it also assesses whether individuals can take appropriate “actions.” Thus, it seemed reasonable for the item to load on both factors. Additionally, the modification index suggested correlated errors between Items 6 and 15 as well as Items 15 and 16. A review of Items 6 and 15 revealed potential content overlap, as both items are essentially focused on future-oriented thinking. Similarly, the correla- tion between Items 15 and 16 is likely due",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,13,"15 as well as Items 15 and 16. A review of Items 6 and 15 revealed potential content overlap, as both items are essentially focused on future-oriented thinking. Similarly, the correla- tion between Items 15 and 16 is likely due to their shared reliance on GenAI as a tool for improving teaching. Based on this, the authors concluded that these correlations of error",63
