DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,16,"S. Y. Kim et al. were reasonable and explainable. After incorporating these two model modifications, the final model for instructors also revealed adequate fit. This final model is presented in Fig. 1. Benefit scale For the benefit scale, we identified two major factors for each version of the scale and pro- ceeded with CFA using the two-factor solution. Student version The initial two-factor solution yielded an excellent model fit according to the fit indices, as presented in Table 6. Consequently, the model was finalized with two factors. However, the correlation between two factors was estimated at 1.00 (Fig. 2), suggesting that two factors may essentially assess the same construct. Upon reviewing the items, the research team concluded that the two factors are qualitatively different, and the two-factor structure was maintained. Faculty version Like the student version, the two-factor structure for the faculty version produced a rea- sonable model fit, except for RMSEA, which had a value of 0.116. However, RMSEA is known to be inflated with small sample sizes and not recommended for such cases (Kenny et al., 2015). Given",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,16,"the faculty version produced a rea- sonable model fit, except for RMSEA, which had a value of 0.116. However, RMSEA is known to be inflated with small sample sizes and not recommended for such cases (Kenny et al., 2015). Given the faculty sample size of 132, which is considered small for CFA, the RMSEA value may have been overestimated. As with the student version, the correlation between the two factors was close to 1.00, which again suggests a potential unidimensional structure. However, the research team decided to maintain the two factors due to the dis- tinct content each factor measures. Challenge scale A review of the literature and existing instruments suggested three major dimensions that should be addressed by the challenge scale. The research team carefully selected and adapted the items and proposed a three-factor structure for CFA analysis. Table 6 Goodness-of-fit statistics for benefit scale Model N 휒2 df CFI TLI RMSEA SRMR Student Model.d: 2-factor 454 184.006 76 0.91 0.90 0.063 0.049 Faculty Model.e: 2-factor 130 96.824 42 0.93 0.91 0.116 0.053",174
