DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,18,"S. Y. Kim et al. Faculty version Unlike the student version, the faculty version of the challenge scale did not yield a sat- isfactory model fit, as shown in Table 7. The CFI and TLI values did not meet the 0.90 threshold, and both RMSEA and SRMR were higher than .08, indicating poor fit. We also reviewed modification index values, but found that none of the suggestions were theoreti- cally or empirically justifiable. This poor model fit could potentially be attributed to the small sample size and the larger number of items on this scale. According to the rule-of- thumb for CFA (Nunnally & Bernstein, 1967), we typically need 10 cases per indicator variable. With 19 items on the challenge scale, we would have needed at least 190 par- ticipants for accurate CFA results. Given the limited faculty sample size, the factor struc- ture may not have been clear. Nonetheless, the factor loadings suggest that the three-factor model is still viable, with all factor loadings exceeding .40. Further research with a larger sample size is needed to confirm our findings.",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,18,"the factor struc- ture may not have been clear. Nonetheless, the factor loadings suggest that the three-factor model is still viable, with all factor loadings exceeding .40. Further research with a larger sample size is needed to confirm our findings. Discussion Given the increasing popularity of GenAI in education, it is crucial for both instructors and students to gauge their preparedness and perceptions of using GenAI for teaching or learn- ing purposes. Such information can help higher education institutions identify the neces- sary resources to create more effective and adaptive teaching and learning environments. Despite the growing presence of GenAI in educational contexts, to the best of our knowl- edge, no validated instrument has been proposed that is applicable to both students and instructors in higher education. This study developed and validated three subscales, includ- ing a) Readiness, b) Perceived benefits, c) Perceived Challenges, designed for both student and faculty populations. However, the Perceived Challenges scale for faculty was not fully validated due to a limited sample size. The development of these subscales was guided by a review of the",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,18,"benefits, c) Perceived Challenges, designed for both student and faculty populations. However, the Perceived Challenges scale for faculty was not fully validated due to a limited sample size. The development of these subscales was guided by a review of the literature and existing instruments, with additional validation provided by subject matter expert reviews. The factor structure of each subscale was further tested and confirmed through confirmatory factor analysis (CFA). CFA results confirmed a three-factor solution for the readiness subscale for both student and faculty versions. This subscale includes three major components: a) GenAI comprehension, b) ethical awareness of GenAI, and c) GenAI utilization and profi- ciency. While this instrument was adapted from Wang et al. (2023), who developed an AI Readiness framework, our approach differs in that we conceptualized readiness with three components instead of four (Cognitive, Vision, Ethics, and Ability). The finding may be due to the different target populations (students only vs. both faculty and students) and the unique characteristics of GenAI, which focuses on generating Table 7 Goodness-of-fit statistics for challenge scale Model N 휒2 df CFI",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,18,"Ability). The finding may be due to the different target populations (students only vs. both faculty and students) and the unique characteristics of GenAI, which focuses on generating Table 7 Goodness-of-fit statistics for challenge scale Model N 휒2 df CFI TLI RMSEA SRMR Student Model.f: 3-factor 450 376.444 149 0.91 0.90 0.066 0.057 Faculty Model.g: 3-factor 128 356.766 148 0.87 0.84 0.110 0.091",63
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,19,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… new content such as text, images, or other media. Perceptions of AI and GenAI can differ because GenAI’s ability to create new content may be viewed as more mysteri- ous or unpredictable, which may blur the line between ethical concerns and usefulness (Lindahl & Saeid, 2023). This may lead to mixed perceptions, particularly concerning Student Version (model.f) Faculty Version (model.g) Fig. 3 Challenge scale factor structure",72
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,20,"S. Y. Kim et al. the authenticity of content and potential issues like deepfakes or plagiarism (Mozelius, 2024). Further research is needed to explore this distinction in greater depth. The benefits scale encompasses two major components: effectiveness and empower- ment. Previous studies have highlighted two primary benefits of using GenAI in edu- cational settings: increased efficiency in academic performance (Chaudhry & Kazim, 2021; Kexin et al., 2020; Weng & Chiu, 2023) and greater autonomy or empower- ment due to personalized or adaptive learning (Chan & Zhou, 2023; Chiu et al., 2023; Kabudi et al., 2021). While most previous studies have primarily focused on the posi- tive impact of GenAI on student learning, this study confirmed that the two factors – effectiveness and empowerment – are also relevant for instructors. CFA results for the two-factor solution showed satisfactory model fit for both instructor and student scales. However, the correlation between the two factors was close to 1, suggesting a potential unidimensional structure. Although we maintained the two-factor struc- ture due to the distinct qualitative content each factor measures, future studies should",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,20,"both instructor and student scales. However, the correlation between the two factors was close to 1, suggesting a potential unidimensional structure. Although we maintained the two-factor struc- ture due to the distinct qualitative content each factor measures, future studies should explore this further with different samples to refine the factor structure. The challenge subscale examines three main factors: a) ethics and privacy concerns, b) negative educational impact, and c) accuracy and sensitivity. Unlike the benefits scale, our literature review suggested more specific challenges related to the use of GenAI, which necessitates a larger number of items (19) for this subscale. While the student data confirmed the three-factor solution, the factor structure for the faculty scale did not meet the desired model fit. This discrepancy may be due to the smaller sample size of faculty compared to students, as recruiting many instructors is more challenging. It is also possible that faculty perceive challenges differently from stu- dents. Despite the discrepancy, these results highlight concerns of GenAI use in teach- ing and learning that other research has identified, such as bias, privacy,",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,20,"instructors is more challenging. It is also possible that faculty perceive challenges differently from stu- dents. Despite the discrepancy, these results highlight concerns of GenAI use in teach- ing and learning that other research has identified, such as bias, privacy, alignment to human values, and inaccurate responses of these tools (Kutty et al., 2024; Lindahl & Saeid, 2023; Nyaaba et al., 2024; Zhou et al., 2024). Future research should consider further validating the faculty scale with a larger sample data and further investigate the factor structure. Limitations and future studies It is important to acknowledge several limitations of this study. First, this study pri- marily focused on content-related validity and construct-related validity, as assessed through CFA. Future research could enhance the validity evidence by correlating the scales with criteria such as individuals’ actual ability to use GenAI in educational or instructional contexts (criterion-related validity). Second, readers should be cautious about generalizing the results. While the research team made efforts to recruit peo- ple from the target population by employing multiple venues for recruitment, the sam- ple may be limited and",180
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,20,"instructional contexts (criterion-related validity). Second, readers should be cautious about generalizing the results. While the research team made efforts to recruit peo- ple from the target population by employing multiple venues for recruitment, the sam- ple may be limited and biased due to the use of convenience sampling. Future studies should aim to replicate this study with more representative and larger samples. Third, although the study included data from both instructors and students, the factor struc- ture was examined separately for each group. This was necessary because some word- ing and items differ between the two versions of the scale. However, researchers may be interested in the measurement invariance of the scale across the two populations. Future research may explore the differential validity of the instrument through meas- urement invariance testing.",131
DOC_034,NEW16.pdf,10.1007/s11423-026-10713-z,21,"Development and validation of Generative Artificial… Educational and practical implications The instrument developed and validated in this study can serve as a valuable diagnostic tool for higher education institutions and instructors. Data collected using this instru- ment can help identify areas where additional resources, training, or support are needed to enhance student learning through the adoption of GenAI. Particularly, GenAI tools such as ChatGPT can provide a personalized and interactive learning experience (Kokku et al., 2018), and inaccurate measures of students’ readiness may lead to unequal access to these benefits across learners. Instructors can also benefit from the feedback provided by GenAI to optimize their teaching practices (Glaser, 2023); however, providing appro- priate resources to maximize these benefits depends on accurately assessing teachers’ readiness and their perceptions of using GenAI in teaching. Additionally, researchers can use the instrument to explore correlations between perceptions of GenAI and exter- nal variables, such as motivation for learning and teaching, academic performance, or teaching effectiveness. Appendix See Tables 8, 9, 10.",167
