DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,1,"ORIGINAL RESEARCH ARTICLE Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science https://doi.org/10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3 Segun Michael Ojetunde sojetunde@gmail.com Umesh Ramnarain uramnarain@uj.ac.za 1 Department of Mathematics, Science and Technology Education, University of Johannesburg, Johannesburg, South Africa Abstract This study investigates the perceptions of higher education students regarding the use of GPT variants for online guided inquiry (OGI). Historically, technology has supported teaching and learning without significant adverse effects; however, the growing concern about its potential to replace human involvement, particularly in guided inquiry created impetus for this study. A cross-sectional study using mixed-methods approach was conducted with 150 students from Science, Mathematics, and Engineering disciplines at both undergraduate and graduate levels. A nine-construct model was devel­ oped and validated using both newly developed and adapted instruments. The results indicated an acceptable model fit (SRMR = 0.12, NFI = 0.89) and explained variance of R2 = 0.461R2. Despite concerns about inaccuracy, misuse, and over- dependence on generative AI, students generally held positive views about its use for OGI. Factors contributing to this positive perception included familiarity with technology (β = 0.346), help-seeking behavior (β = 0.21),",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,1,"0.461R2. Despite concerns about inaccuracy, misuse, and over- dependence on generative AI, students generally held positive views about its use for OGI. Factors contributing to this positive perception included familiarity with technology (β = 0.346), help-seeking behavior (β = 0.21), perceived enjoy­ ment (β = 0.281), and multitasking abilities (β = 0.25). Conversely, social influences, such as subjective norms, did not significantly impact perceptions. It can be concluded that academic help-seeking, enjoyment derived from its use, and the nature of being multitasking are key factors that encourage students to adopt GPT variants, in contrast to the prevailing subjective norms regarding their use. Consequently, higher education institutions should implement strategies to monitor and regulate the use of generative AI to enhance educational experiences and outcomes. Keywords Artificial intelligence · Generative AI · Guided inquiry · Inquiry-based learning · ChatGPT Received: 6 May 2025 / Revised: 19 May 2026 / Accepted: 25 August 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Exploring Factors Influencing Concerns for the Use of Generative Pretraining Transformer (GPT) Variants for the Online Guided Inquiry (OGI) among Higher Education Students Segun",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,1,"May 2025 / Revised: 19 May 2026 / Accepted: 25 August 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Exploring Factors Influencing Concerns for the Use of Generative Pretraining Transformer (GPT) Variants for the Online Guided Inquiry (OGI) among Higher Education Students Segun Michael Ojetunde1 · Umesh Ramnarain1 the emergence of Artificial Intelligence (AI), particularly generative AI, has generated increasing concern among educational stakeholders. Public concerns expressed by influential individuals such as Elon Musk regarding the potential risks of AI further intensified debates about its implications for society and education (Metz & Schmidt, 2023). Empirical studies have also identified concerns relat­ ing to the accuracy of AI-generated outputs (Ouyang et al., 2022; Frieder et al., 2023), as well as the unfair academic advantage students may gain through AI usage, thereby threatening the credibility of assessment systems if grades no longer reflect learners’ actual abilities (Ramnarain et al., 2024). Consequently, there is a growing need to understand the factors influencing students’ concerns regarding the use of generative AI for online inquiry in higher education. The increasing demand to improve academic perfor­ mance, encourage self-directed",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,1,"abilities (Ramnarain et al., 2024). Consequently, there is a growing need to understand the factors influencing students’ concerns regarding the use of generative AI for online inquiry in higher education. The increasing demand to improve academic perfor­ mance, encourage self-directed learning, and simplify learn­ ing tasks has made inquiry-based learning (IBL) an essential Background Technology has become one of the most influential resources employed in education for facilitating teaching and learning activities (Omojola & Ojetunde, 2020). The integration of technology into education dates back to the introduction of educational radio before the Second World War, and tech­ nological innovations have continued to shape instructional practices since then (Omojola & Ojetunde, 2020). However,",112
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science pedagogical approach in contemporary education. This is reflected in curriculum reforms across countries such as Australia, Singapore, and the United States, where inquiry- oriented learning approaches have been integrated into educational frameworks (Australian Curriculum Assess­ ment and Reporting Authority [ACARA], 2012; Ministry of Education of Singapore, 2007; National Research Council, 2012). Inquiry-based learning encourages learners to iden­ tify problems, formulate questions, establish learning goals, and independently seek appropriate solutions and resources (Ramnarain et al., 2024). As a learner-centred strategy, IBL promotes autonomy, active participation, and critical think­ ing, making it highly compatible with technology-driven learning environments. Bunterm et al. (2014) categorised inquiry-based learn­ ing into structured, guided, confirmatory, and open inquiry approaches. Guided inquiry is particularly relevant because learners are required to generate solutions to problems while receiving procedural guidance from facilitators (Sotakova et al., 2020). The integration of digital technologies into inquiry-based learning has transformed learning processes by supporting information gathering, experimentation, and data analysis, while reducing excessive teacher dependence and encouraging active learner participation (Bell et al., 2010). In the",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"(Sotakova et al., 2020). The integration of digital technologies into inquiry-based learning has transformed learning processes by supporting information gathering, experimentation, and data analysis, while reducing excessive teacher dependence and encouraging active learner participation (Bell et al., 2010). In the era of the Fourth Industrial Revolution (4IR), technologies such as robotics and AI increasingly support inquiry-oriented learning activities requiring independent exploration and problem-solving abilities (Ojetunde & Ramnarain, 2023). Artificial Intelligence has demonstrated substantial edu­ cational potential across different learning contexts. Studies have shown that AI can enhance creativity and imagination among preschool learners (Pawar et al., 2023) and sup­ port advancements in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics education (Manyika, 2017). The emer­ gence of generative AI has further transformed learning by enabling students to perform complex cognitive tasks such as writing, problem-solving, and content creation more efficiently. Earlier AI systems were largely based on recur­ rent neural network (RNN) models that executed repetitive programmed instructions (Rahman et al., 2020). However, recent developments in Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT) models have introduced AI systems capable of ana­ lysing contexts, generating human-like responses,",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"were largely based on recur­ rent neural network (RNN) models that executed repetitive programmed instructions (Rahman et al., 2020). However, recent developments in Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT) models have introduced AI systems capable of ana­ lysing contexts, generating human-like responses, and pro­ viding solutions based on extensive internet data (Brown et al., 2020; Petricini, 2023). These capabilities align closely with guided inquiry processes, thereby positioning gen­ erative AI as a potentially effective tool for inquiry-based learning. Despite these benefits, concerns regarding generative AI continue to increase. Studies have reported issues relating to cultural bias, misinformation, and inaccuracies in AI-gener­ ated responses (Ouyang et al., 2022). Frieder et al. (2023), for instance, found that ChatGPT demonstrated mathemati­ cal abilities below the standard expected of average math­ ematics students, while Kung et al. (2023) reported that ChatGPT only marginally met minimum requirements in medical examinations. These findings suggest that although generative AI can support learning, its outputs may not always be reliable without human verification. Another major concern relates to academic dishonesty and excessive dependence on AI-generated content. Stud­ ies have shown",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"in medical examinations. These findings suggest that although generative AI can support learning, its outputs may not always be reliable without human verification. Another major concern relates to academic dishonesty and excessive dependence on AI-generated content. Stud­ ies have shown that many students, particularly in pro­ gramming-related disciplines, rely heavily on AI-generated outputs instead of independently developing problem- solving and analytical skills (Justin, 2023; Manjeet & Dan, 2023). Such practices may weaken the critical thinking abilities associated with inquiry-based learning. Concerns have also been raised regarding fairness in academic assess­ ment, as students using AI tools may gain unfair advantages over those who do not utilise such technologies (Christine, 2023). Xie et al. (2023) further argued that if students’ aca­ demic performance is largely based on AI-generated outputs rather than genuine competencies, the credibility of institu­ tional assessment systems may be undermined. The rapid development of generative AI technologies has intensified their adoption among higher education students. Following the release of ChatGPT-3.5 and Chat­ GPT-4 (OpenAI-Team, 2023), several related AI platforms such as Bard, Meta LLaMA, Gemini, and Copilot emerged",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"be undermined. The rapid development of generative AI technologies has intensified their adoption among higher education students. Following the release of ChatGPT-3.5 and Chat­ GPT-4 (OpenAI-Team, 2023), several related AI platforms such as Bard, Meta LLaMA, Gemini, and Copilot emerged for academic and inquiry-related purposes (Xie, 2023). The widespread availability of these technologies has increased students’ opportunities to engage in online inquiry using AI- supported platforms. However, students’ concerns regard­ ing generative AI may depend on several personal and social factors. One important factor is digital citizenship status. Pren­ sky (2001) distinguished between digital natives, who were born into technologically rich environments, and digital immigrants, who adapted to digital technologies later in life. Research has shown that digital natives and immigrants differ in technological habits and preferences (Helsper & Eynon, 2010; Zimerman, 2012). Teo (2016) and Zhao and Zhao (2021) further identified growing up with technology, comfort with multitasking, and preference for instant grati­ fication as major characteristics of digital natives. These characteristics may influence students’ acceptance of or concerns regarding generative AI in inquiry-based learning environments (Ojetunde & Nweze,",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,2,"further identified growing up with technology, comfort with multitasking, and preference for instant grati­ fication as major characteristics of digital natives. These characteristics may influence students’ acceptance of or concerns regarding generative AI in inquiry-based learning environments (Ojetunde & Nweze, 2021). Self-regulated learning may also influence students’ engagement with generative AI. Wang et al. (2022) defined self-regulated learning as learners’ ability to manage their",64
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science learning processes through metacognition and independent learning skills. Components such as goal setting, help-seek­ ing, and task strategies are particularly important in inquiry- based learning and may determine whether students use AI responsibly or become overly dependent on it (Papamitsiou & Economides, 2019; Feyzioğlu, 2019). Similarly, subjec­ tive norm, defined as social influence on behavioural inten­ tion (Ajzen, 1980), may shape students’ concerns regarding generative AI. In addition, perceived enjoyment has been identified as a significant factor influencing technology adoption, as students who enjoy using digital technologies are more likely to adopt them for learning purposes (Chao, 2019; Hunde et al., 2022). Although several studies have examined the educational applications and challenges of generative AI (Manjeet & Dan, 2023; Xie et al., 2023; Frieder et al., 2023; Ouyang et al., 2022), limited attention has been devoted to investigat­ ing the factors underlying students’ concerns regarding its use for guided inquiry in higher education. This gap in the literature therefore provides the justification for the present study. Theoretical Background The study was predicated",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"attention has been devoted to investigat­ ing the factors underlying students’ concerns regarding its use for guided inquiry in higher education. This gap in the literature therefore provides the justification for the present study. Theoretical Background The study was predicated on the Unified Theory of Accep­ tance and Use of Technology proposed by Venkatesh et al. (2003) which is an offshoot of the theory of planes behav­ iour and technology acceptance model. The unified theory of acceptance and use of technology renamed some con­ structs in the theory of planned behaviour such as subjective norms as social influence and emphasised the role of indi­ vidual characteristics such as self-efficacy, etc. This study also hypothesised that some personal characteristics such as digital nativity (Growing up with technology, comfortable with multitasking, thriving with instant gratification), per­ ceived enjoyment of using AI and self-regulated learning (Goal Setting, Task Strategies, Help Seeking and Self Eval­ uation) in addition to the subjective norm (social influence) could influence the use of technology such as generative AI. This study assumes that an individual’s inclination to follow guided",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"self-regulated learning (Goal Setting, Task Strategies, Help Seeking and Self Eval­ uation) in addition to the subjective norm (social influence) could influence the use of technology such as generative AI. This study assumes that an individual’s inclination to follow guided inquiry procedures depends on their characteristics such as the ability to Engage, Explore, Explain, Elaborate, and Evaluate (5Es model of inquiry-based learning) which could be enhanced or attenuated with the use of generative AI or societal view about its use (Subjective norm). Effect of Social Influence on the Use of Generative AI Factors influencing the adoption of technology by indi­ viduals do not only depend on the individual, according to Fishbein and Ajzen (2010), but the subjective norm that connotes people’s influence on individual action plays a sig­ nificant role in determining an individual’s acceptance or rejection of an idea or object. A study conducted by Hus­ sein (2018) among higher institution students revealed that subjective norms are a significant factor in affecting uni­ versity students’ intention to use technology for learning or e-learning. Studies by Venkatesh et al. (2003),",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"or object. A study conducted by Hus­ sein (2018) among higher institution students revealed that subjective norms are a significant factor in affecting uni­ versity students’ intention to use technology for learning or e-learning. Studies by Venkatesh et al. (2003), and Sun et al. (2013) also revealed that social influence or subjective norm is a significant factor in the acceptance or rejection of the use of technology-related innovation. Effects of Individual Factors on the Use of Generative AI Most theories such as the theory of planned behaviour (Fishbein & Ajzen, 1981) and the theory of reasoned action (Fishbein & Ajzen, 2010) reported that individuals or per­ sonal characteristics are germane to the acceptance of tech­ nology-related innovations. Studies by Aharony and Gazit (2019) and Çoklar et al. (2017) revealed that online con­ tent search strategies and information literacy have a direct and significant relationship with digital nativity. Addition­ ally, an individual’s digital language and behaviour have a positive correlation with the characteristics of being a digi­ tal native (Chen et al., 2016; Calvo-Ferrer, 2017). Hence, there is a high probability",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"direct and significant relationship with digital nativity. Addition­ ally, an individual’s digital language and behaviour have a positive correlation with the characteristics of being a digi­ tal native (Chen et al., 2016; Calvo-Ferrer, 2017). Hence, there is a high probability that digital nativity will predict individual’s concerns regarding the use of generative AI. Teo (2013) developed a digital native assessment scale and it was found that growing up with technology, being comfortable with multitasking, and thriving with instant gratification and reward are part of the constructs of digi­ tal nativity. It was reported that growing up with technol­ ogy will enhance an individual’s self-efficacy to be fluent in the digital language of computers such as the internet, video games, and other innovations (Prensky, 2005). Also, the individual’s capacity or inability to attempt multiple par­ allel tasks otherwise known as multitasking could influence the inclination for generative AI. Jeong and Fishbein (2007) reported that media multitasking among high school stu­ dents and undergraduates is a potential learning behaviour that could lead to the use of AI. Teo (2016) reported that exposure",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,3,"could influence the inclination for generative AI. Jeong and Fishbein (2007) reported that media multitasking among high school stu­ dents and undergraduates is a potential learning behaviour that could lead to the use of AI. Teo (2016) reported that exposure to a series of multimedia platforms or devices by a digital native individual could lead to faster means of com­ munication and conformability to graphic-rich rather than text-rich messages. Berk (2009) was also of the opinion that being conformable to graphic-rich media could lead to the quest for faster tools such as generative AI for sourcing and sending information online. Similarly, Prensky (2001) sub­ mitted that the speed of gathering information or the satis­ faction that comes with instant gratification and the reward of searching the internet growing up with technology is one",133
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science of the enjoyable claims among digital natives. Small and Vorgan (2008) reported that instant download of files from the internet and the speed of sending text have created a quest for tools such as generative AI to enhance the speed and immediacy of task completion. More so, SRL could influence individual concerns about the use of generative AI. The essence of self-regulated learning is that learning in a traditional environment is dif­ ferent from online. A report revealed that online learning relied not only on the instruction given as it is in classroom interaction but also on individual characteristics (Artino & Stephens, 2009). Song and Hills found SRL to be crucial in massive open online courses where guided inquiry is the prominent teaching method. The absence of self-regulated learning among students who are exposed to online learning could generate concerns especially if exposed to the genera­ tive AI. For instance, individuals who are involved in help- seeking as one of the constructs of self-regulated learning could eventually find generative AI as a",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"are exposed to online learning could generate concerns especially if exposed to the genera­ tive AI. For instance, individuals who are involved in help- seeking as one of the constructs of self-regulated learning could eventually find generative AI as a virtual assistant and exploit its advantages. Moreover, being constrained to com­ plete a task as a part of the goal set by an individual could propel or restrict an individual to employing generative AI because self-regulated learning is personal reinforcement that drives individuals to achieve a set goal (Wang, 2021). Perceived enjoyment of online activities may lead to a decrease in self-regulated learning. An empirical study has shown that perceived enjoyment is positively correlated with the intention to use online technologies, such as gen­ erative AI (Vickers, 2017; Park et al., 2012). However, this enjoyment can raise concerns when self-regulation or other moderating factors are lacking. While there are numerous concerns regarding the use of generative AI in academic settings, few studies have explored the specific factors contributing to these concerns among staff and students. Additionally, the scarcity of empirical",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"or other moderating factors are lacking. While there are numerous concerns regarding the use of generative AI in academic settings, few studies have explored the specific factors contributing to these concerns among staff and students. Additionally, the scarcity of empirical research on concerns related to generative AI and students’ knowledge and per­ ceptions of online guided inquiry highlights the need for this study. Research Questions The background of the study raises the following questions to guide the research: 1. What are higher education students’ perceptions of using generative AI for online guided inquiry? 2. Do social factors (subjective norms) and individual fac­ tors (goal setting, task strategies, multitasking, help-seek­ ing, growing up with technology, instant gratification, and perceived enjoyment) predict concerns about using generative AI for online guided inquiry among higher education students? Methodology Design The study employs a mixed-methods design that integrates both quantitative and qualitative data from higher educa­ tion students at a public university in Nigeria. It utilizes an explanatory concurrent Quant-dominant approach, which involves the simultaneous collection of data (Creswell & Clark, 2017; Ojetunde, 2019). While",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"design that integrates both quantitative and qualitative data from higher educa­ tion students at a public university in Nigeria. It utilizes an explanatory concurrent Quant-dominant approach, which involves the simultaneous collection of data (Creswell & Clark, 2017; Ojetunde, 2019). While the primary focus is on a survey, the study adopts a multi-method triangula­ tion approach, gathering both open-ended (qualitative) and closed-ended (quantitative) data within a single quantita­ tive framework. The combination of statistical and thematic analyses, along with the integration of findings from these analyses, characterizes the study as mixed-methods. A sur­ vey was conducted to gather students’ opinions in science and science-related faculties—particularly the Faculties of Science, Engineering, and Science Education within the Faculty of Education—using both in-person and online methods. Participants The study population comprises undergraduate and post­ graduate students at the public university. Because the study is interested in investigating students’ online inquiry activi­ ties, particularly guided inquiry, students in sciences and science-oriented faculties (Engineering, and Science and Technology Education) were sampled for the study. After securing approval from Ethical Committee of the university, a total of",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"interested in investigating students’ online inquiry activi­ ties, particularly guided inquiry, students in sciences and science-oriented faculties (Engineering, and Science and Technology Education) were sampled for the study. After securing approval from Ethical Committee of the university, a total of 150 students were selected out of the three facul­ ties, 50 students from each of the faculty and different lev­ els were sampled. The printed version of the questionnaire was administered to students in their respective faculties and the electronic version (https://tinyurl.com/5b3njebv) was also distributed via students’ WhatsApp groups. The total of 34 responses received on open-ended section of the instrument was subjected qualitative analysis while the total of 150 completely filled close-ended section of the survey were subjected to quantitative analysis. The demographic characteristics of the150 respondents revealed that 54.0% of the respondents were males, 24.0% were in the age range of 16-25years, 65.3% were 26-35years, 9.3% were 36-45years and 1.3% were 46years and above. Additionally, 14.7% were undergraduates, 79.3% were master’s degree students and 6.0% were PhD students. Instrument and Measures The research instrument used for the study",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,4,"age range of 16-25years, 65.3% were 26-35years, 9.3% were 36-45years and 1.3% were 46years and above. Additionally, 14.7% were undergraduates, 79.3% were master’s degree students and 6.0% were PhD students. Instrument and Measures The research instrument used for the study was compiled by including items that were both developed and adapted",51
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,5,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science from previous studies. This is because the wording of some items did not address online guided inquiry or focus on teaching-learning activities. The instrument consists of five constructs, some of which have multiple indicators. For instance, digital nativity has four indicators according to Teo (2016) but three (Comfortable with multitasking (MT = 4 items), growing up with technology (GUT = 3 items, and thriving on instant gratification or reward (TGR = 5 items) were adapted for the study. Also, self-regulated learning (SRL) has five constructs according to Wang et al. (2022) but three; goal setting (GS = 5 items), task strategies (TS = 4 items), and help-seeking (HS = 4 items) were adapted and used in the study. The indicator for each of the construct has at least 3 items. The subjective norm (SN = 3 items) and perceived enjoyment (PE = 4 items) constructs were also adopted without indicators while concern for the use of gen­ erative AI (CUG = 5 items) was developed and comprised five items. Overall, the instrument",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,5,"norm (SN = 3 items) and perceived enjoyment (PE = 4 items) constructs were also adopted without indicators while concern for the use of gen­ erative AI (CUG = 5 items) was developed and comprised five items. Overall, the instrument comprised 37 items and used a 4-point modified Likert scale, ranging from strongly agree to strongly disagree. The sources of the adapted con­ structs are presented in Table 1. Also, two open-ended questions which elicit responses on students’ knowledge and perception about the use of generative AI was included in the survey instrument. The research instrument validation procedures are presented in Tables 3 and 4. Matter around Validity and Reliability Two statistical procedures were followed to validate and revalidate the items of the instrument, and these included the estimation of reliability and validity coefficients. Reliability of the Constructs The reliability of the instrument was established using the partial least square approach of structural equation model­ ing otherwise known as PLS-SEM. The Cronbach’s alpha reliability coefficient, composite reliability, and average variance extracted were estimated and the results are pre­ sented in",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,5,"of the instrument was established using the partial least square approach of structural equation model­ ing otherwise known as PLS-SEM. The Cronbach’s alpha reliability coefficient, composite reliability, and average variance extracted were estimated and the results are pre­ sented in Table 2. The result in Table 2 revealed the coefficients of Cron­ bach’s Alpha ranging from approximately 0.70 (0.698 for GUT) to 0.871 against the benchmark of 0.70. This shows that the instrument is reliable. The composite reliability values for the constructs range from 0.735 to 0.861 exceed­ ing the cut-off point of 0.70, indicating that the constructs measured by the instrument show internal consistency. The average variance extracted ordinarily should have a value range from 0.5 and above. The results in Table 2 show that constructs have acceptable average variance extracted values which implies that the constructs exhibit convergent validity. Discriminant Validity The discriminant validity of the constructs in the study was assessed using the Heterotrait-Monotriat Method (HMM). This was considered suitable because of the ease of inter­ pretation and the relatively new approach. The result is pre­ sented",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,5,"validity. Discriminant Validity The discriminant validity of the constructs in the study was assessed using the Heterotrait-Monotriat Method (HMM). This was considered suitable because of the ease of inter­ pretation and the relatively new approach. The result is pre­ sented in Table 3. The rationale behind HMM is that for constructs to show discriminant validity, the inter-correlation coefficients between them must be less than 1. Specifically, the inter- correlation value between a construct and other constructs must be less than 0.85 (Henseler, Ringle, and Sarstedt, 2015). From Table 4, it is only the inter-correlation coef­ ficient between GS and CUG that is a little bit higher than 0.85. Therefore, it can be concluded that constructs in the study demonstrate discriminant validity, implying that they are distinct and cannot be substituted for one another. Analytical Procedure Although 34 participants provided responses to the open-ended section of the survey. These responses were subjected to the­ matic analysis, whereby responses exhibiting similar patterns were categorized into themes. The online survey’s closed- ended section—together with responses from the hardcopy instruments—yielded 150 fully completed questionnaires.",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,5,"to the open-ended section of the survey. These responses were subjected to the­ matic analysis, whereby responses exhibiting similar patterns were categorized into themes. The online survey’s closed- ended section—together with responses from the hardcopy instruments—yielded 150 fully completed questionnaires. Responses obtained through the online survey were assigned Table 1 Construct and Source of their Items Construct Source Digital Nativity (GUT, TGR and MT) Teo (2016) Self-Regulated Learning (HS, GS and TS) Wang et al. (2022) Subjective Norm (SN) Hunde et al. (2022) Perceived Enjoyment (PE) Hunde et al. (2022) and Punnoose (2012) Concern for the Use of Generative AI (CUG) Developed Table 2 Reliability of the Constructs Construct Cron­ bach’s alpha Com­ posite reliability (rho_c) Average variance extracted (AVE) Concern with use of GPT (CUG) 0.786 0.735 0.519 Goal setting (GS) 0.725 0.776 0.538 Growing Up with Technology (GUT) 0.698 0.771 0.529 Help-seeking (HS) 0.753 0.768 0.529 Multitasking (MT) 0.871 0.861 0.608 Perceived Enjoyment (PE) 0.820 0.822 0.607 Subjective Norm (SN) 0.770 0.757 0.526 Thrive with instant Gratification or Reward (TGR) 0.710 0.761 0.524 Task Strategies (TS) 0.764 0.818",180
