DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,6,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science numerical values based on the magnitude specified during the survey design and were combined with data extracted from the hardcopy instruments. Where necessary, some codes were reversed. The resulting dataset was then converted into a Comma-Separated Values (CSV) file and uploaded to Smart PLS-SEM software for analysis. Partial Least Squares Struc­ tural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM) was used to analyse the quantitative data. PLS-SEM is a variance-based structural equation modelling technique that allows the measurement and structural models to be assessed concurrently. It also offers an advantage for studies with smaller sample sizes compared with covariance-based SEM. The data were analysed using the path algorithm and a bootstrapping procedure in Smart PLS-SEM. Results The results of the study were organized according to the research questions posed. Research Question 1 was addressed using qualitative data gathered from open-ended questions in the questionnaire, while quantitative responses were utilized to answer Research Question 2. Qualitative/Thematic analysis Thematic analysis of the responses collected from students revealed three key themes: Adaptability, Advancement, and Addiction. The responses were tagged",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,6,"data gathered from open-ended questions in the questionnaire, while quantitative responses were utilized to answer Research Question 2. Qualitative/Thematic analysis Thematic analysis of the responses collected from students revealed three key themes: Adaptability, Advancement, and Addiction. The responses were tagged based on the respon­ dents’ departments because data was collected alongside with quantitative information and the name or identities of the respondents was not collected. Adaptability The concept of “adaptability” in relation to student responses arises from the ability of students to integrate online learn­ ing with the advantages gained from real-life, inquiry-based learning experiences. The adaptability of the online guided inquiry is the most appreciated feature enjoys by students, students see it as less teacher-involved and versatile as reported by a respondent that: “Online guided inquiry is an educational approach that combines the benefits of online learning with the guidance of a teacher or mentor. It involves present­ ing learners with open-ended questions or problems, encouraging them to explore and investigate, and pro­ viding support and feedback throughout the process” (Science and Tech Edu. I). This implies that OGI",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,6,"guidance of a teacher or mentor. It involves present­ ing learners with open-ended questions or problems, encouraging them to explore and investigate, and pro­ viding support and feedback throughout the process” (Science and Tech Edu. I). This implies that OGI is not devoid of teachers’ guidance or mentoring and supports. However, its versatility in produc­ ing divers learning outcome among study was commented that it: “Using online guided inquiry for learning can be a highly effective because it promotes active learning, critical thinking, and problem-solving skills. Online guided inquiry engages students in the learning pro­ cess by encouraging them to explore, investigate, and apply concepts. This active involvement helps students retain information better compared to passive learn­ ing methods” (Science & Tech Edu IV). This explain further the OGI is better compared with other teaching or learning method as it could enhance students’ acquisition of learning process skills such as critical think­ ing and problem-solving. Generally, the recurrent response is that OGI has the element of traditional guided inquiry as reported by a student that: Table 3 Monotrait-Heterotrait Value of",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,6,"could enhance students’ acquisition of learning process skills such as critical think­ ing and problem-solving. Generally, the recurrent response is that OGI has the element of traditional guided inquiry as reported by a student that: Table 3 Monotrait-Heterotrait Value of the Constructs CUG GS GUT HS MT PE SN TGR TS CUG GS 0.899 GUT 0.297 0.228 HS 0.62 0.634 0.542 MT 0.288 0.405 0.694 0.565 PE 0.393 0.294 0.348 0.611 0.255 SN 0.478 0.364 0.436 0.606 0.318 0.662 TGR 0.693 0.351 0.414 0.491 0.297 0.365 0.666 TS 0.677 0.74 0.251 0.573 0.28 0.424 0.484 0.364 Table 4 Fit Indicators of the Model Fit Indicator Saturated model Estimated model SRMR 0.112 0.122 d_ULS 5.088 7.724 d_G 1.498 1.662 Chi-square 783.843 827.815 NFI 0.891 0.889",124
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,7,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science Online guided inquiry is a powerful approach to learning that combines the benefits of technology with the effectiveness of inquiry-based learning, offer­ ing personalized learning, increased engagement, improved understanding, accessibility, flexibility, and real-time feedback. The vast array of resources and materials from online help bridge the gap for students who may not have access to traditional educational resources, making learning more inclusive (Science Student IX). The responses from the students show that OGI is more adaptable and result-oriented compared to other teaching or learning methods and to some extent fosters educational inclusion. Advancement The term “advancement” signifies that OGI represents a departure from conventional practices and serves as an exploration of the future landscape of teaching and learning activities. Not only that OGI is more flexible and versatile, it was also reported that generative AI for OGI reflects what the future education will be as reported by an engineering student: I feel this will be the future of learning. I believe we have no need for physical theoretical learning when this can",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,7,"that generative AI for OGI reflects what the future education will be as reported by an engineering student: I feel this will be the future of learning. I believe we have no need for physical theoretical learning when this can be performed online in a conducive environ­ ment (Engineering Student XII). It was said as presented that using generative AI for OGI is more advanced not only as teaching or learning method but far better that previous online tools like Google and You­ Tube as reported by Engineering student. It’s definitely more user friendly than Google, because the answers generated can be tailored to the user’s best words or format of assimilation and better under­ standing (Eng. Student V). Aside superiority of over other online tools, learning or teaching methods, it was reported that OGI using generative AI helps to provide explanation to some abstract concepts or topics as reported: It’s very necessary sometimes to use it for explanation if the person don’t understand the concept of a topic (Science Student XV). This implies that using generative AI tool for",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,7,"provide explanation to some abstract concepts or topics as reported: It’s very necessary sometimes to use it for explanation if the person don’t understand the concept of a topic (Science Student XV). This implies that using generative AI tool for OGI is seen as advanced way of conduction inquiry by undergraduates compared to conventional means. Addiction/Overdependence The term “addiction” is used to describe the tendency to become reliant on and comfortable with assistance from online intelligence agents, potentially leading to the neglect of human or personal efforts. The third theme emanated reported concern of students about the use of generative AI for OGI, despite its adapt­ ability and versatility, it was reported that generative AI has some fallibilities and negative sides. As reported: I think it has its pros and cons. Pros of using online guided inquiry are the speed, depth, and accessibility of information. The cons of using online guided inqui­ ries for learning are over-dependence, reduced social engagement, and laziness (Science & Tech. Edu. VII). The more complex issue was reported by one of the science students that",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,7,"depth, and accessibility of information. The cons of using online guided inqui­ ries for learning are over-dependence, reduced social engagement, and laziness (Science & Tech. Edu. VII). The more complex issue was reported by one of the science students that the use of generative AI for online inquiry need to be approached with caution after highlighting it usefulness that: The use of generative AI for online inquiry offers sig­ nificant advantages such as 24/7 accessibility, a broad knowledge base, efficiency, and personalized responses. However, it also presents challenges, including potential inaccuracies, context limitations, dependency risks, and ethical concerns like data privacy and biases. While it can be a powerful tool for quick and diverse information retrieval, users should approach it critically and verify information with reliable sources to ensure accuracy and maintain their research skills (Science student X). Concerns regarding inaccuracy, misuse, and over-depen­ dence on generative AI for online guided inquiry highlight the need to investigate the factors influencing these appre­ hensions. This exploration is crucial and constitute the basis for research question 2. Quantitative Analysis The constructs of",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,7,"inaccuracy, misuse, and over-depen­ dence on generative AI for online guided inquiry highlight the need to investigate the factors influencing these appre­ hensions. This exploration is crucial and constitute the basis for research question 2. Quantitative Analysis The constructs of social factors, individual factors and con­ cern about using generative AI for online guided inquiry were modelled to assess the influence of factors on the con­ cern regarding the use of generative AI for guided inquiry. The model was presented in (Fig. 1) alongside the quality assessment criteria of the model.",91
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,8,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science The measurement model shows the number of vari­ ables of a construct and their corresponding factor load­ ings. The structural model revealed the path coefficients for the direct effects of the exogenous variables on the dependent variable as well as the R2 value. The fit indi­ cators for the model are presented in Table 4. It revealed a Standardised Root Mean Square Residual (SRMR) of 0.12, d_ULS of 7.724, Chi-square of 827.815, d_G of 1.662, and Norm Fit Index of 0.889 which implies that the proposed model fit into observed data and that the proposed model improves the fit by about 90% relative to the null model. The model also revealed the R2 of 0.461 which implies that all the constructs taken together accounted for 46.1% of total variance in dependent vari­ able (CUG). The SRMR is considered a reliable measure of fit for variance-based SEM. The acceptable value for SRMR is around 0.1 or below (Henseler et al., 2014). d_ULS and d_G do not asses model fit but rather the bootstrap­",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,8,"able (CUG). The SRMR is considered a reliable measure of fit for variance-based SEM. The acceptable value for SRMR is around 0.1 or below (Henseler et al., 2014). d_ULS and d_G do not asses model fit but rather the bootstrap­ ping value of the model. The chi-square, although it has no specific range of values, is sensitive to the number of respondents and is considered an unreliable measure of model fit. The NFI value (which ordinarily ranges from 0.9 to 1) is also a less reliable measure as its value depends on the number of constructs in the model. According to the results, the SRMR of 0.14 indicates an acceptable model fit. Factors Predicting Concern for the Use of Generative AI for Inquiry-Based Learning Figure 1 shows that multi-tasking (MT) has a positive direct effect (β = 0.221) on task strategy (TS) and accounted for 4.9% (0.049) of the design of TS. However, SN (β =-0.024), MT (β=-0.208) and HS (β =-0.048) have a negative influ­ ence on the CUG. The result further revealed that all the constructs accounted for",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,8,"strategy (TS) and accounted for 4.9% (0.049) of the design of TS. However, SN (β =-0.024), MT (β=-0.208) and HS (β =-0.048) have a negative influ­ ence on the CUG. The result further revealed that all the constructs accounted for 46.1% of the total variance for concerns regarding the use of generative AI. The results of the direct effect of factors that influence the concern about the use of generative AI are presented in Table 5. Discussion The qualitative analysis revealed that respondents (stu­ dents in science and science-related fields) possess a solid understanding of online guided inquiry and hold a positive view on the effectiveness of generative pre-trained trans­ formers for facilitating such interactions. However, students expressed significant concerns that using generative AI for inquiry could lead to misinformation, decrease learning engagement, and foster over-dependence and misuse. These worries echo findings from authors like Ouyang et al. (2022) and Frieder et al. (2023), who noted that generative AI can be prone to errors and inaccuracies. Additionally, this aligns Fig. 1 Measurement and Structured Models for the Constructs in the",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,8,"echo findings from authors like Ouyang et al. (2022) and Frieder et al. (2023), who noted that generative AI can be prone to errors and inaccuracies. Additionally, this aligns Fig. 1 Measurement and Structured Models for the Constructs in the Study",41
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science with Elon Musk’s warning that generative AI could pose risks to civilization (Metz & Schmidt, 2023), akin to situ­ ations where students’ grades fail to accurately reflect their academic abilities (Ramnarain et al., 2024). The findings from quantitative analysis revealed that individual characteristics such as goal setting can influence students’ tendency to develop positive attitudes towards using generative AI. Students who have a short period to accomplish a task may not see anything detrimental in using generative AI compared to those with more time. This result aligns with the findings of Doo et al. (2021) and Antúnez et al. (2020), which revealed that self-regulation, particularly in goal setting, is an important predictor of the nature and pattern of learning Matter Around Validity and Reliability Engagement Among Students Therefore, goal setting will encourage students to create a timeline for their activities and employ the fastest tools, such as generative AI, to complete them within the stipu­ lated time. The results further revealed that growing up with technology has a positive influence on students’",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"students to create a timeline for their activities and employ the fastest tools, such as generative AI, to complete them within the stipu­ lated time. The results further revealed that growing up with technology has a positive influence on students’ attitudes toward the use of generative AI. This could be because most of the students sampled were digital natives, inclined to embrace and explore the latest technology tools to simplify their tasks. The findings support those of Wang et al. (2019), who noted that while “growing up with technology” does not significantly predict addiction to technology, it equips individuals with the skills to tackle tasks using technology tools. The findings also revealed that the habit of multitask­ ing could foster a positive attitude toward using generative AI for IBL. The reason could be that students’ concurrent involvement in many tasks may lead them to seek faster tools, such as generative AI, to manage them efficiently. This finding contradicts other findings in the literature, which reported that multitasking is a significant distraction from learning (Chen & Yan, 2016), negatively correlates with",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"may lead them to seek faster tools, such as generative AI, to manage them efficiently. This finding contradicts other findings in the literature, which reported that multitasking is a significant distraction from learning (Chen & Yan, 2016), negatively correlates with learning achievement (Ravizza et al., 2014), and adversely influences students’ learning online (Burak, 2012). Prominent among the findings is that help-seeking habits among students in science and science-related fields gen­ erate a positive attitude toward the use of generative AI. Although the guided inquiry is characterised by discovery and personal engagement in problem-solving activities, individual versatility with technology tools can encour­ age the search for useful technology in IBL, leading to a positive attitude toward using generative AI. These findings align with those reported by Song and Hill (2009a, b), who noted that help-seeking is crucial in a learning environment where technology is integrated into the learning process and guided inquiry is the prominent teaching method. The findings also revealed that perceived enjoyment of using technology and the tendency to seek instant gratifica­ tion and reward independently influence positive attitudes",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,where technology is integrated into the learning process and guided inquiry is the prominent teaching method. The findings also revealed that perceived enjoyment of using technology and the tendency to seek instant gratifica­ tion and reward independently influence positive attitudes toward the use of generative AI. The reasoning behind this is that individuals who thrive on instant gratification from Table 5 Effect of Factors Influencing Concern for the Use of Genera­ tive AI for IBL Path Original sample (O) Sample mean (M) Standard deviation (STDEV) T statistics (|O/STDEV|) p-val­ ues GS -> CUG 0.494 0.487 0.116 4.268 0.000 GS -> HS 0.292 0.307 0.137 2.138 0.033 GUT -> CUG 0.399 0.346 0.118 3.881 0.001 GUT -> PE 0.158 0.18 0.154 1.023 0.306 HS -> CUG 0.248 0.21 0.113 2.194 0.017 MT -> CUG 0.208 0.205 0.096 2.181 0.029 MT -> GS 0.291 0.318 0.116 2.517 0.012 MT -> HS 0.296 0.297 0.113 2.617 0.009 MT -> TS 0.221 0.25 0.101 2.194 0.028 PE -> CUG 0.291 0.281 0.127 2.291 0.005 SN -> CUG -0.024 -0.027 0.118 0.203 0.839 TGR ->,180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"GS 0.291 0.318 0.116 2.517 0.012 MT -> HS 0.296 0.297 0.113 2.617 0.009 MT -> TS 0.221 0.25 0.101 2.194 0.028 PE -> CUG 0.291 0.281 0.127 2.291 0.005 SN -> CUG -0.024 -0.027 0.118 0.203 0.839 TGR -> CUG 0.335 0.318 0.091 3.697 0.000 TGR -> PE 0.189 0.211 0.116 1.631 0.103 TS -> CUG 0.138 0.129 0.095 1.457 0.145 The results revealed that goal setting (GS), whether long or short- term, positively influences students’ concern for the use of genera­ tive AI (CUG) or not (β = 0.487, t = 4.268, p < 0.05). Also, growing up with technology (GUT) positively influences the decision to use generative AI or not (β = 0.346, t = 3.881, p < 0.05). The results further revealed that the habit of seeking help (HS) by students positively influences their concern about the use of generative AI (β = 0.21, t = 2.194, p < 0.05). In addition, multitasking (MT) by students posi­ tively influences students’ concerns about using generative AI for inquiry-based learning. (β = 0.25, t = 2.181, p <",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"the use of generative AI (β = 0.21, t = 2.194, p < 0.05). In addition, multitasking (MT) by students posi­ tively influences students’ concerns about using generative AI for inquiry-based learning. (β = 0.25, t = 2.181, p < 0.05). Also, the per­ ceived enjoyment (PE) that students derived from using technology positively influences their concern regarding the use of generative AI (β = 0.281, t = 2.291, p < 0.05). The results also revealed that stu­ dents thrive with instant gratification and reward (TGR) positively influences their concern about the use of generative AI (β = 0.318, t = 3.697, p < 0.05). However, the subjective norm (SN) of the sampled students (β = 0.027, t = 0.203, p > 0.05) and students’ task strategies (TS) (β = 0.129, t = 1.457, p > 0.05) have no significant influence on the concern about the use of generative AI. It is also noteworthy that the mean influence of SN is negative, which implies that the sampled students has concerns about the use of generative AI for IBL",178
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,9,"influence on the concern about the use of generative AI. It is also noteworthy that the mean influence of SN is negative, which implies that the sampled students has concerns about the use of generative AI for IBL",38
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science using technology are likely to find it enjoyable and, conse­ quently, develop a positive attitude toward the quick results provided by technology tools such as generative AI. Previous studies have reported a significant positive influence of delay of gratification, the opposite of instant gratification, which correlates positively with students’ self- regulation, an important predictor of learners’ academic per­ formance (Zheng & Li, 2016). However, the concerns about the use of generative AI may not follow the same pattern as academic achievement, as the former is a bipolar construct. The findings also show that subjective norms and task strategies have no significant influence on students’ con­ cerns regarding the use of generative AI for IBL. This result could be due to the perceived usefulness of generative AI outweighing any social or individual beliefs. This finding aligns with the one reported by Saadé et al. (2008), who found that subjective norms (SN) might not be significant because compliance with social expectations or beliefs may occur only in compulsory situations, not in voluntary ones. Based",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"This finding aligns with the one reported by Saadé et al. (2008), who found that subjective norms (SN) might not be significant because compliance with social expectations or beliefs may occur only in compulsory situations, not in voluntary ones. Based on the foregoing discussion, it can be inferred that students who exhibit digital native characteristics tend to develop positive attitudes toward using generative AI for IBL contrary to the subjective norms of the school commu­ nity and the nature of the task involved. The results support the findings of Xie (2023), who investigated the influence of AI-driven inquiry teaching on learning outcomes and reported that AI-driven guided inquiry is more appealing compared to other science teaching methods. The findings also align with those of Siantuba et al. (2023), who investigated the impact of an online inquiry- based learning environment in addressing misconceptions about students’ performance. It was discovered that in online inquiry, students’ misconceptions of circular motion were easily addressed because the procedure of IBL was enjoy­ able. Therefore, the use of GPT variants among higher edu­ cation students sustains",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"addressing misconceptions about students’ performance. It was discovered that in online inquiry, students’ misconceptions of circular motion were easily addressed because the procedure of IBL was enjoy­ able. Therefore, the use of GPT variants among higher edu­ cation students sustains the positive attitude students have toward other online inquiry tools because of the perceived enjoyment that comes from their use and their effectiveness in help-seeking activities. Education Implication of the Study The findings of this study imply that the contemporary higher education students, being digital natives, are inclined to develop positive attitudes toward the use of generative AI due to their exposure. Therefore, teachers are expected to be more flexible, adaptive, and in tune with the trend by assum­ ing the role of guides rather than instructors. Since genera­ tive AI can be used for many academic tasks and functions, teachers can guide students into discovery by engaging them in higher-order cognitive tasks during guided inquiry processes. The findings also have implications for the institution’s assessment practices, as academic staff need to ensure that students’ claims of what they know",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"teachers can guide students into discovery by engaging them in higher-order cognitive tasks during guided inquiry processes. The findings also have implications for the institution’s assessment practices, as academic staff need to ensure that students’ claims of what they know align with their actual knowledge, so the integrity and certification procedures of the institution are not questioned. Recommendations Based on the findings of this study, it can be inferred that many factors accounted for students’ adoption and concerns about the use of generative AI for IBL. Therefore, instead of taking action against students’ inclination toward using gen­ erative AI, the higher institution community should embark on incorporating checks and moderations into the use of generative AI for improved teaching-learning activities and better learning outcomes. Additionally, students should understand that the use of generative AI may conflict with existing educational policies and instructional modalities. Therefore, they should strike a balance between adopting AI in a risk-taking manner (using it without modification) and strictly adhering to the conventional IBL procedures by refraining from AI use (refuge-taking). Excessive risk- taking (using generative AI)",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"and instructional modalities. Therefore, they should strike a balance between adopting AI in a risk-taking manner (using it without modification) and strictly adhering to the conventional IBL procedures by refraining from AI use (refuge-taking). Excessive risk- taking (using generative AI) may lead to many mistakes or errors, and excessive refuge-taking (adherence to conven­ tional methods) may stifle creativity, speed, and precision. Moreover, focusing on conventional learning procedures may lead to efficiency loss. Recommendations can also be made to ICT policymakers to develop a policy that moder­ ates the content and modality of generative AI use for aca­ demic purposes. Conclusion Based on the findings of this study, it can be concluded that there are concerns regarding inaccuracy, misuse, and over- dependence on generative AI for online guided inquiry were highlighted, however, personal factors, particularly the char­ acteristic of being a digital native, play a significant role in determining whether students’ concern about using genera­ tive AI for guided inquiry will be positive or negative. The characteristics associated with being a digital native such as multitasking, help-seeking and growing up with",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,10,"digital native, play a significant role in determining whether students’ concern about using genera­ tive AI for guided inquiry will be positive or negative. The characteristics associated with being a digital native such as multitasking, help-seeking and growing up with technol­ ogy are in alignment with conducting online guided inquiry, popular with the use of generative AI. Social beliefs—par­ ticularly subjective norms—did not significantly influence students’ concerns. This may be because students’ opinions were not translated into policy. In addition, the absence of existing policies to guide and implement AI use may have contributed to the non-significant effect. Moreover, many generative AI tools incorporate features that resonate with modern learners and align seamlessly with their learning processes. As a result, complete abstinence",122
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,11,"1 3 Journal of Technology in Behavioral Science from AI tools may be challenging for students in this era. Consequently, it can be concluded that the current genera­ tion of students is accustomed to coexisting with generative AI, and their overall attitude toward its use remains positive. Limitations of the Study This study explored factors influencing concern regarding using generative AI for guided inquiry (GI) among higher education students in science and science-related fields. However, limitations associated with the methodology used in this study is associated with use of single univer­ sity which could reduce the external validity of the findings. Although online guided inquiry was emphasised in the study as opposed to conventional guided inquiry, which requires the implementation of planned treatment or experiment, the absence of a control experiment could threaten the internal validity of the study. Additionally, the study relied on stu­ dents’ written responses (both open- and closed-ended items) for both quantitative and qualitative data. Incorporating par­ ticipants’ views through in-depth interviews or key infor­ mant discussions could have provided a richer understanding of the information",180
DOC_035,NEW17.pdf,10.1007/s41347-026-00706-3,11,"the study relied on stu­ dents’ written responses (both open- and closed-ended items) for both quantitative and qualitative data. Incorporating par­ ticipants’ views through in-depth interviews or key infor­ mant discussions could have provided a richer understanding of the information collected via the quantitative approach. Furthermore, although researcher-developed instruments were used, their broader application by other researchers is necessary to enhance the generalizability of the findings. Direction for Future Studies This study explored factors that generate concerns regarding the use of generative AI for guided inquiry among higher edu­ cation students using a mixed-methods approach. It is advised that future studies adopt a more in-depth approach, particu­ larly collecting qualitative information using key informant or in-depth interview, to gain deeper insights into the under­ lying factors. The possibility of using generative AI for other forms of IBL can also be explored by future investigators. Appendix Concern for the use of Generative AI 1. Using generative AI technologies to complete assign­ ments makes guided inquiry less effective. 2. The use of generative AI technologies will reduce stu­ dents' involvement in the",180
