DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,1,"RESEARCH ARTICLE Open Access International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4 Understanding the role of learning analytics and generative artificial intelligence on decision-making and learning design practice in higher education Alrike Claassen1* , Daniel Ebbert1 , Vitomir Kovanović1 , Negin Mirriahi1 and Shane Dawson1 *Correspondence: Alrike Claassen alrike.claassen@adelaide.edu.au 1Adelaide University, Adelaide, Australia © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use,",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,1,"article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Abstract High-quality learning design is an important contributor to student success. Higher education institutions can leverage the combined potential of learning analytics (LA) and Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) to enhance instructors’ learning design practices. However, the way LA and GenAI are being used in combination for learning design is not well understood. In this context, one theory that can provide valuable insights into instructors’ technology integration into learning design (LD) practice is Self-Determination Theory (SDT). Using SDT as an interpretative lens, this study illuminates the factors in instructors’ learning design practices when utilising LA and GenAI. For this purpose, we employed quantitative ethnography to explore how instructors utilise LA and GenAI for LD. Eleven focus groups were conducted with instructors",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,1,"lens, this study illuminates the factors in instructors’ learning design practices when utilising LA and GenAI. For this purpose, we employed quantitative ethnography to explore how instructors utilise LA and GenAI for LD. Eleven focus groups were conducted with instructors from a large Australian university. Using thematic analysis, we created a codebook consisting of ten codes that were applied to our dataset and then analysed using Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA). Our results show that instructors’ LA-informed LD discussions primarily revolved around course, contextual, and creative problem-solving considerations, as well as student support. The predominant GenAI factors were assessment design, contextual consideration, creative problem solving and design for student self-determination. Based on our results, we discuss the integration of LA and GenAI in LD practice and decision-making, highlighting the practical implications of our findings. Keywords Learning design, Decision-making, Epistemic network analysis, Generative artificial intelligence, Learning analytics, Creative problem solving Introductions The alignment between learning design (LD) and learning analytics (LA) has the poten­ tial to improve LD practice in higher education. LA offers instructors and learning designers insights into learner behaviours",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,1,"Generative artificial intelligence, Learning analytics, Creative problem solving Introductions The alignment between learning design (LD) and learning analytics (LA) has the poten­ tial to improve LD practice in higher education. LA offers instructors and learning designers insights into learner behaviours and engagement patterns, thereby support­ ing processes of reflection and sensemaking that can improve student learning experi­ ences (Mangaroska & Giannakos, 2018). Central to this endeavour is the articulation of",70
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,2,"Page 2 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 pedagogical intent and its comparison with observed learner activity, a dual perspective that enables instructors to evaluate the extent to which design goals are realised (Lock­ yer et al., 2013). In this framing, LD specifies the pedagogical intent in terms of what learners are expected or anticipated to do and how they are assessed. LA then provides evidence of the student’s behaviour and engagement. The alignment between LD and LA is essential for meaningful interpretation of future revisions (Buckingham Shum et al., 2023; Lockyer et al., 2013). The research literature has demonstrated the importance of framing and reporting on LD as a means to understand and interpret LA data within meaningful LD contexts. For example, the Open University Learning Design Initiative (OULDI) (Rienties & Toete­ nel, 2016) demonstrated that faculty design decisions significantly influence weekly stu­ dent engagement patterns (LA). The temporal analyses attributed roughly two-thirds of weekly engagement to implemented course design (Nguyen et al., 2017; Rienties & Toetenel, 2016). In follow-up",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,2,"& Toete­ nel, 2016) demonstrated that faculty design decisions significantly influence weekly stu­ dent engagement patterns (LA). The temporal analyses attributed roughly two-thirds of weekly engagement to implemented course design (Nguyen et al., 2017; Rienties & Toetenel, 2016). In follow-up studies, activities were framed using a taxonomy that high­ lighted the specific design choices that were associated with student retention and sat­ isfaction outcomes. While LA has the potential to provide strong predictive elements, these are simply snapshots in time that reveal engagement patterns without identifying the underlying reasons for an educator’s design decisions. Mixed-method research has shown that LA reveals common behavioural patterns across many courses, yet “why” educators implement a specific course design remains hidden without further qualitative inquiry (Nguyen et al., 2022). We argue in this paper for a process-oriented, theory-led approach to understanding design intentions alongside trace-based outcomes. There remains a notable gap between the potential and the actual use of LA-informed design across institutions (Alzahrani et al., 2023). Adoption is uneven, and transferring “successful” approaches between contexts requires attention to local social, organisa­ tional, and",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,2,"trace-based outcomes. There remains a notable gap between the potential and the actual use of LA-informed design across institutions (Alzahrani et al., 2023). Adoption is uneven, and transferring “successful” approaches between contexts requires attention to local social, organisa­ tional, and cultural conditions (Dawson et al., 2018; Tsai et al., 2020; Viberg et al., 2018). The practicable LA perspective foregrounds this point: the social artefact, what instruc­ tors and students actually do together, determines whether LA will be used; “what is not practicable is not likely to be used” (Viberg & Grönlund, 2023). Human-centred LA (HCLA) therefore emphasises participatory design, usability, and usefulness in real set­ tings, and calls out the design dimension of LA as underdeveloped relative to theory and data science (Buckingham Shum et al., 2019; Dimitriadis et al., 2021). To date, LA has provided snapshots to support educator reflection. However, instruc­ tors increasingly need more process-oriented insights that connect fine-grained activity data with evolving LD intentions. Research to link LD more tightly to LA (e.g., mov­ ing beyond “click counts” to time-on-activity and on-track status) has been suggested",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,2,"reflection. However, instruc­ tors increasingly need more process-oriented insights that connect fine-grained activity data with evolving LD intentions. Research to link LD more tightly to LA (e.g., mov­ ing beyond “click counts” to time-on-activity and on-track status) has been suggested to make interventions more timely and actionable (Nguyen et al., 2022). GenAI offers addi­ tional potential in this space, not as an autonomous solution, but as a scaffold for con­ tinuous, nuanced feedback on design and for assistance with specific design tasks (e.g., assessment prompts, exemplar feedback, multimedia development). Early evidence sug­ gests that clusters of teaching and learning activities can be leveraged to build LA-and- GenAI supports that can help educators to make more informed, evidence-based design decisions (Albuquerque et al., 2025). However, there remains a lack of a nuanced under­ standing of how educators undertake the practice of design when engaging with LA and GenAI. While predictive insights and technical affordances are valuable, they only par­ tially illuminate the lived realities of LD. To fully understand the uptake and impact of",173
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,2,"design when engaging with LA and GenAI. While predictive insights and technical affordances are valuable, they only par­ tially illuminate the lived realities of LD. To fully understand the uptake and impact of",33
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,3,"Page 3 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 LA and GenAI, research must attend to the motivational and relational dynamics that underpin instructors’ LD practices. Self-determination theory (SDT) provides a valuable framework for understanding instructors’ decisions in workplace settings (Deci & Ryan, 1985). This theory posits that human motivation and optimal performance are supported when three innate psycho­ logical needs are met: autonomy, competence, and relatedness. When instructors’ basic psychological needs are fulfilled in higher education, this can promote collaborative relationships among institutions, faculty, and students, ultimately improving motiva­ tion, well-being, engagement, and academic outcomes (Hutomo & Kurniawati, 2024). Conversely, when these needs are thwarted, their motivation and, consequently, perfor­ mance decline (Ryan et al., 2021). Examining instructors’ discourse about LA and GenAI through an SDT theoretical lens illuminates the underlying factors that influence their LD choices and practice. Utilising a quantitative ethnography (QE) approach and epistemic network analysis (ENA) (Shaffer, 2017), this research examines instructors’ discussions when referring to LA versus GenAI as technologies that can support LD practice. By",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,3,"factors that influence their LD choices and practice. Utilising a quantitative ethnography (QE) approach and epistemic network analysis (ENA) (Shaffer, 2017), this research examines instructors’ discussions when referring to LA versus GenAI as technologies that can support LD practice. By using ENA, we not only identify the factors that influence instructors’ decision-making but also under­ stand the relationship between the factors (Shaffer & Ruis, 2017). With this approach, we examined instructors’ discussions relating to LA and GenAI use in LD practice. Recognising that LA and GenAI not only offer different capabilities but also different approaches to LD, our study informs the development of more effective LA and GenAI integration that aligns with instructors’ naturalistic design practices. Literature review Learning design Learning design is a complex domain situated in a particular academic context. The design process aims to create epistemic, social, and physical environments that facili­ tate students in reaching the intended outcomes (Goodyear et al., 2021). There is often a gap between learning designs and students’ actual activities. These activities can only be imagined, and once implemented, students’ autonomy influences",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,3,"and physical environments that facili­ tate students in reaching the intended outcomes (Goodyear et al., 2021). There is often a gap between learning designs and students’ actual activities. These activities can only be imagined, and once implemented, students’ autonomy influences the actual learning outcomes (Goodyear et al., 2021). Despite this, learning design has a strong influence on students’ behaviour and ultimate academic success (Rienties & Toetenel, 2016). There­ fore, it is important to understand instructors’ design practices to create optimal con­ ditions for instructors to flourish. Instructors’ learning design practice is influenced by contextual aspects, such as university constraints (Bennett et al., 2011). Gaining insight into the contextual factors that influence learning design is essential for supporting instructors in making effective and responsive design decisions (Stefaniak & Xu, 2020). In navigating LD complexity, LA provides instructors with information about student behaviour in a learning environment through the analysis of learner trace data, enabling them to make adjustments to their designs (Lockyer et al., 2013). It also provides instructors with valuable insights during reflective practice, helping them understand student engagement and",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,3,"behaviour in a learning environment through the analysis of learner trace data, enabling them to make adjustments to their designs (Lockyer et al., 2013). It also provides instructors with valuable insights during reflective practice, helping them understand student engagement and make informed decisions about student support (Wise & Jung, 2019). To align with instructors’ practices, it is important to consider how they navigate design and iterative adaptations. For this reason, Wang and Law (2025) emphasised the importance of LD granularity. LD granularity refers to the different levels of design (i.e., curriculum level, course level and task level). The curriculum component refers to the",103
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,4,"Page 4 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 learning outcomes targeted and the topics that enable the attainment of these outcomes. The course level refers to the aggregated course (a series of connected learning events), while the task level refers to individual tasks (i.e. a single learning event) and access to resources (Law & Liang, 2020). For example, OULDI (Rienties & Toetenel, 2016) con­ ducted research on the task level and found that communication activities had a posi­ tive impact on student success. In a follow-up study at OULDI, collaborative activities proved difficult for tutors to maintain due to student resistance (Nguyen et al., 2022). These findings highlight the complexity underpinning learning processes. To support educational practice, GenAI is increasingly being used to suggest ideas for learning out­ comes and topics (Divjak et al., 2025) and to provide personalised support to students, as well as to support instructional scaffolding (Bahroun et al., 2023). However, GenAI use also faces significant challenges around academic integrity, bias, ethics and the impact on student critical",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,4,"(Divjak et al., 2025) and to provide personalised support to students, as well as to support instructional scaffolding (Bahroun et al., 2023). However, GenAI use also faces significant challenges around academic integrity, bias, ethics and the impact on student critical thinking. Hence, it is crucial to understand how GenAI can be effec­ tively, ethically, and strategically implemented to support teaching and learning (Bear­ man et al., 2023). Many LA and GenAI research studies overlook the learning outcomes, pedagogical approach, context, and subject matter (Topali et al., 2024). Thus, understanding the educational potential of both LA and GenAI requires holistic approaches that con­ sider instructors’ contextual considerations rather than treating LA and GenAI as iso­ lated technologies. Human-centred approaches are necessary to fully understand how LA and GenAI can support LD. While considerable progress has been made in utilising LA to inform design practices, the impact has not yet reached its full potential due to research predominantly focusing on the technology (Dawson et al., 2019). However, as posited by Buckingham Shum et al., (2019), integrating technologies into practice is not only",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,4,"inform design practices, the impact has not yet reached its full potential due to research predominantly focusing on the technology (Dawson et al., 2019). However, as posited by Buckingham Shum et al., (2019), integrating technologies into practice is not only a technical endeavour but also a multidimensional human challenge influenced by leadership (Dawson et al., 2018). Stakeholder uptake can be unequal and exacerbated by individuals’ emotional and cognitive dimensions, along with their contextual circum­ stances, constructs within Self-Determination Theory (SDT), which can help explain how instructors choose to use LA and GenAI to support their LD. Self-determination theory SDT is a motivational theory that encompasses the innate psychological need of people for autonomy, competence and relatedness (Deci & Ryan, 1985). Autonomy encompasses an individual’s ability to make decisions freely that are self-endorsed and free from exter­ nal interference; competence involves one’s perception of mastery and effectiveness, and relatedness concerns the quality of interpersonal connections (Deci & Ryan, 2000). When these basic needs are met, individuals can grow and exhibit psychological well- being (Deci & Ryan, 2000). Conversely, contexts that hinder",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,4,"one’s perception of mastery and effectiveness, and relatedness concerns the quality of interpersonal connections (Deci & Ryan, 2000). When these basic needs are met, individuals can grow and exhibit psychological well- being (Deci & Ryan, 2000). Conversely, contexts that hinder autonomy, competence, or relatedness result in diminished motivation and compromised well-being (Deci & Ryan, 2000). The theory has demonstrated broad applicability across various contexts, includ­ ing higher education and LD. Given the role of motivation in professional performance, understanding how these psychological needs influence instructors in educational set­ tings is critical and an under-researched area. Psychological needs are fundamental to educational contexts because instruc­ tors’ psychological needs satisfaction produces cascading benefits for student learn­ ing environments. For example, the relationship between instructor motivation and",124
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,5,"Page 5 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 autonomy-supportive practices represents a significant mechanism through which edu­ cators can foster students’ psychological need satisfaction (Ryan & Deci, 2020). When instructors both value a task and experience agency in that task, they experience deeper, more positive emotions, such as enjoyment, contentment, and pride (Thies & Kordts- Freudinger, 2019). University instructors who are intrinsically motivated towards teach­ ing tend to be less controlling and more autonomy-supportive towards students (Yasué et al., 2019). Aelterman et al. (2019) expand this conceptualisation by distinguishing between two principal components of autonomy-supportive teaching: i) offering stu­ dents choices and ii) finding ways to understand and accommodate students’ perspec­ tives. In LD, autonomy support, competence support, and relatedness support principles can lead to increased student engagement and motivation (Martin et al., 2018). Auton­ omy-supportive design in MOOCs, for example, can be achieved by minimising reliance on rigid deadlines, offering meaningful choice, and reducing emphasis on controlling rewards (Martin et al., 2018). Understanding and supporting instructors’ psychological needs becomes essential",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,5,"et al., 2018). Auton­ omy-supportive design in MOOCs, for example, can be achieved by minimising reliance on rigid deadlines, offering meaningful choice, and reducing emphasis on controlling rewards (Martin et al., 2018). Understanding and supporting instructors’ psychological needs becomes essential for creating LDs where SDT teaching practices can flourish and thereby benefit student learning. Research questions The literature review highlighted the significance of LD design on student outcomes and the potential of LA and GenAI in enhancing LD. To progress the LD field, a nuanced understanding of how LA and GenAI can be strategically applied is needed. Under­ standing instructors’ discussions is essential because it illuminates the gap between technological potential and actual implementation, helping researchers and institutions develop more effective support strategies that align with instructors’ lived experiences and needs. Therefore, this study aims to identify the factors that influence LD decision- making and practice in the context of LA and GenAI, investigated through the following research questions: • RQ 1: How do factors that influence LD in the context of LA associate with each other? • RQ 2:",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,5,"influence LD decision- making and practice in the context of LA and GenAI, investigated through the following research questions: • RQ 1: How do factors that influence LD in the context of LA associate with each other? • RQ 2: How do factors that influence LD in the context of GenAI associate with each other? • RQ 3: What are the similarities and differences between the factors that influence LD in the context of LA and GenAI? Methodology Study methodology To understand the complexity and nuance inherent in instructors’ decision-making pro­ cesses, while enabling the exploration of potential contradictions and tensions within their reported experiences, we collected qualitative data through a focus group study (Kamberelis & Dimitriadis, 2013). Our primary goal was to gain a deeper understanding and obtain additional warrants and evidence, rather than generate generalisable findings (Damşa & Barany, 2023). To do this, we adopted a QE approach (Shaffer, 2017). QE inte­ grates qualitative and quantitative methods to gain a better understanding of discourse and, thereby, culture. Specifically, we employed Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) (Shaffer, 2017), a widely",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,5,"Barany, 2023). To do this, we adopted a QE approach (Shaffer, 2017). QE inte­ grates qualitative and quantitative methods to gain a better understanding of discourse and, thereby, culture. Specifically, we employed Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) (Shaffer, 2017), a widely used qualitative research methodology for understanding dis­ courses. By using ENA as the analysis methodology, it is possible to identify and visualise",62
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,6,"Page 6 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 persistent patterns in the data (Damşa & Barany, 2023). Data were collected through focus groups with instructors regarding their use of technology in LD. The participants consisted of university instructors employed at a large public Australian university, all of whom taught within one faculty, either pre-service teacher education programs or ter­ tiary preparation enabling programs, to ensure consistent leadership. Study procedure The study commenced after obtaining institutional ethical approval. To recruit instruc­ tors with relevant expertise in technology-informed course design, we employed a purposive sampling methodology (Merriam & Tisdell, 2016). Recruitment invitations were distributed via email to instructors, asking those with experience using LA (i.e., who had previously used the institutional LA dashboard) to participate in the LA focus group. The same invitation process was used for the GenAI focus group, requesting par­ ticipation from instructors who had utilised GenAI (i.e. ChatGPT or Claude) to inform their LD practice. Six focus groups, comprising a total of 21 participants, explored LA- informed decision-making, while",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,6,"process was used for the GenAI focus group, requesting par­ ticipation from instructors who had utilised GenAI (i.e. ChatGPT or Claude) to inform their LD practice. Six focus groups, comprising a total of 21 participants, explored LA- informed decision-making, while five focus groups, involving a total of 18 participants, explored GenAI-informed practices. Group composition ranged from two to five par­ ticipants to optimise both intimate discussion and diverse perspectives. A total of 14 participants attended both the LA and GenAI focus groups. Each session was conducted online, using Zoom, ranging between 45 and 60 min under the guidance of an experi­ enced moderator to ensure systematic data collection. The focus group discussion were structured by using focus group guiding questions (Appendix A). The focus groups were recorded and subsequently professionally transcribed and checked by the first author for accuracy. Data analysis The data analysis was performed in three phases: (1) thematic analysis and the creation of a codebook, (2) co-coding of the two groups (LA focus groups and GenAI focus groups) based on the Codebook, and (3) ENA analysis. Thematic",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,6,"analysis The data analysis was performed in three phases: (1) thematic analysis and the creation of a codebook, (2) co-coding of the two groups (LA focus groups and GenAI focus groups) based on the Codebook, and (3) ENA analysis. Thematic analysis and creation of a code book Phase 1 involved a thematic analysis using qualitative coding analysis, as described by Saldaña, (2021). The first cycle analysis employed In Vivo qualitative codes, which were created by using words provided by participants to preserve their meaning, in combina­ tion with the level of meaning segmentation. The In Vivo coding resulted in 339 codes across the LA groups and 195 initial codes across the GenAI focus groups. Using par­ ticipants’ language meant the research language remained close to and was interpretable by the community it was intended for (Saldaña, 2021). The second cycle coding involved Pattern Coding, meaning codes were grouped into categories. The Codebook was devel­ oped by drawing on these categories and refining them through constant comparative analysis across the GenAI and LA groups (Boeije, 2002). The final list of codes",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,6,"coding involved Pattern Coding, meaning codes were grouped into categories. The Codebook was devel­ oped by drawing on these categories and refining them through constant comparative analysis across the GenAI and LA groups (Boeije, 2002). The final list of codes included in the Codebook and subsequently used for the data co-coding and ENA analysis is shown in Table 1.",59
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,7,"Page 7 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 Coding of the focus group data During Phase 2, the transcript data were segmented at the sentence level by the first author. The first two authors coded all focus group transcripts, one focus group at a time. Table 1 shows Cohen’s Kappa for all LA focus groups combined, while Table 2 shows Cohen’s Kappa for all GenAI Focus groups combined. All tables below show Cohen’s Kappa calculated on combined datasets (all focus groups merged together for each grouping). All data were coded in their entirety by two independent coders, with disagreements resolved through social moderation (Shaffer, 2017). The final agreement decisions were made by referring to the established Codebook definitions as shown in Table 3, and iter­ ations to the Codebook were made to reflect the changes. The final coded data, reflecting 100% agreement obtained through social moderation, were used for the ENA. Table 4 shows an example of the coded data with the moving window (MW) example indicating a five-line length unit.",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,7,"to reflect the changes. The final coded data, reflecting 100% agreement obtained through social moderation, were used for the ENA. Table 4 shows an example of the coded data with the moving window (MW) example indicating a five-line length unit. The ENA analysis Phase 3 involved the application of ENA analysis. The application of ENA to instruc­ tor discourse about GenAI and LA enables examination of the complex ways educa­ tors discuss their design practice. The strength of ENA lies in its capacity to model the structural connections within data, capturing not only the meaningful features (codes) present in conversations but also the patterns of co-occurrence that show how these features interrelate (Shaffer, 2017). By quantifying the co-occurrence of codes within Table 1 LA Topic Kappa Code Coder 1 N Coder 2 N Agreement % κ Off-topic 1010 1299 68.60 0.121 CTX 1469 1379 64.90 0.140 D.AUTO 82 95 98.58 0.592 COURSE 815 956 72.49 0.059 CPS 1234 1025 68.04 0.090 ACT 313 314 90.48 0.192 ASSESS 339 320 89.12 0.118 TECH 335 385 93.79 0.536 D.SDT 134 71 96.21",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,7,0.121 CTX 1469 1379 64.90 0.140 D.AUTO 82 95 98.58 0.592 COURSE 815 956 72.49 0.059 CPS 1234 1025 68.04 0.090 ACT 313 314 90.48 0.192 ASSESS 339 320 89.12 0.118 TECH 335 385 93.79 0.536 D.SDT 134 71 96.21 0.061 SUPP 180 311 92.09 0.157 I.E 38 67 98.34 0.202 Table 2 GenAI Topic Kappa Code Coder 1 N Coder 2 N Agreement % κ Off-topic 89 77 93.69 0.616 CTX 278 382 73.23 0.426 D.AUTO 23 11 97.39 0.282 COURSE 50 41 95.10 0.480 CPS 144 113 80.74 0.201 ACT 82 35 91.84 0.323 ASSESS 141 210 85.53 0.536 TECH 322 342 81.07 0.590 D.SDT 148 56 83.46 0.183 SUPP 42 36 97.82 0.732 I.E 22 14 97.17 0.264,121
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,8,"Page 8 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 conversations, ENA generates weighted networks of these relationships, accompanied by visualisations. All networks are analysed simultaneously, enabling both visual and sta­ tistical comparisons across the dataset. Although initially developed to study epistemic games, the ENA method has been applied in other domains. For example, student task performance (D’Angelo et al., 2020) and making sense of teacher agency (Pantić et al., 2022) were both examined using ENA methodology. The key assumption of the method is that the structure of connections in the data is meaningful (Shaffer, 2017). ENA is thus a valuable technique for modelling educational decision-making, as it can model the relationships among LA and GenAI use in LD decision-making as they occur within an LD community. We applied ENA to examine the co-occurrences of codes in the focus group discourse (Shaffer & Ruis, 2017). The analysis was conducted using the ENA Web Tool (ver­ sion 1.7.0) (Marquart et al., 2021). We defined the units of analysis as all lines of data Table",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,8,"co-occurrences of codes in the focus group discourse (Shaffer & Ruis, 2017). The analysis was conducted using the ENA Web Tool (ver­ sion 1.7.0) (Marquart et al., 2021). We defined the units of analysis as all lines of data Table 3 The developed Codebook based on In Vivo thematic analysis Code (abbreviation) Description and example Contextual information (CTX) A statement that describes instances where information or an evaluation is obtained across areas such as students, tutors, pedagogy, knowledge about technology, other courses or programmes “So we really had to understand the graduate qualities, we wanted to get beyond just what students are to know and what they’re able to do, as in knowledge and skills” Course design decision making Autonomy (D.AUTO) This code captures utterances about the influences of power in LD, including govern­ ment regulations, institutional policies, policy bottlenecks, leadership dynamics, and technological constraints that impact instructors’ autonomy and decision-making “And we had sort of the trust of our leaders that we were then becoming the experts in the area” Course level design (COURSE) A statement that describes course-level",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,8,"leadership dynamics, and technological constraints that impact instructors’ autonomy and decision-making “And we had sort of the trust of our leaders that we were then becoming the experts in the area” Course level design (COURSE) A statement that describes course-level conceptual framing, including alignment with learning outcomes and sequencing of content “I do a conceptual framing of what the course needs to be in order to meet the objectives with the materials that I am knowledgeable about and have access to” Creative problem solv­ ing (CPS) The code describes dynamic and collaborative nature of course design, inquiry, problem-solving, creative design, human decision-making, use of external resources, co-design and co-review processes, and participation in communities of practice “have people to bounce those ideas off and to help take abstract sort of throw away thoughts and help get them into something that can be really valuable for the students.” Learning activity design (ACT) A statement that describes activity design or redesign “I like to try to create activities that are not only helpful, but easy to engage with the students and like",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,8,"can be really valuable for the students.” Learning activity design (ACT) A statement that describes activity design or redesign “I like to try to create activities that are not only helpful, but easy to engage with the students and like interactive, like H5P.” Assessment design (ASSESS) The code captures utterances related to the design and redesign of formative and summative assessments “And then you have to think also with the assessment design, and then how that links through” Technology role (TECH) Instructors active or passive use of technology (LA or GenAI) or using technology to learn about technology “I’ve used feedback practices and things like OnTask very highly” Design approach, design for student self- determination (D.SDT) Utterances that describe LD for student Autonomy, Competence or Relatedness “I want to do something that’s really a co-constructed, co-led course where the students are partners, as opposed to just students” Student support (SUPP) A statement encapsulating support either through personalisation, feedback or scaffolding “So for me it’s about, thinking about how I’m going to scaffold the students across the semester” Instructor emotion (I.E)",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,8,"are partners, as opposed to just students” Student support (SUPP) A statement encapsulating support either through personalisation, feedback or scaffolding “So for me it’s about, thinking about how I’m going to scaffold the students across the semester” Instructor emotion (I.E) A statement encapsulating an instructor’s affective state, negative or positive “And yeah, most of the time, the data is a bit sad.” Off-topic (OFF) Statements unrelated to the case study “Scissors, paper, rock, maybe?”",74
