DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,10,"Page 10 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 (segmentation was performed at the sentence level) associated with a single value of theme, subsetted by focus group and theme (i.e., LA and GenAI). The ENA algorithm uses a moving window to construct a network model for each line in the data, show­ ing how codes in the current line are connected to codes that occur previously (Siebert- Evenstone et al., 2017). We defined the moving window to consist of five lines (each line plus the four previous lines), because five lines encapsulated the necessary context within the coded data following a unit of meaning qualitative analysis (Siebert-Evenstone et al., 2017). Four additional window lengths ranging from three to seven were analysed to compare the results with the selected five lines. To assess how sensitive the ENA results are to the choice of moving-window lengths, four additional window lengths (three, four, six, and seven lines) were analysed to compare the results with the initial 5-window analysis. The lines were compared using co-registration correlations",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,10,"how sensitive the ENA results are to the choice of moving-window lengths, four additional window lengths (three, four, six, and seven lines) were analysed to compare the results with the initial 5-window analysis. The lines were compared using co-registration correlations to assess the goodness of fit between the visualisation and the original high-dimensional model as window length varies, and Mann–Whitney U tests, which assess whether the observed difference between the Learning Analytics (N = 21) and Artificial Intelligence (N = 18) lies along each dimension. The results showed consistency across window lengths 3–7 (Table 5). In addition, the selected window size aligns with Shaffer's (2017) recommendation stating that five is an appropriate window size for discussion data which aligns with this study design. The resulting networks are aggregated for all lines for each unit of analysis in the model. In this model, we aggregated networks using a binary summation in which the networks for a given line reflect the presence or absence of the co-occurrence of each pair of codes. Alternative models were tested using three alternative weighted transfor­ mations,",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,10,"this model, we aggregated networks using a binary summation in which the networks for a given line reflect the presence or absence of the co-occurrence of each pair of codes. Alternative models were tested using three alternative weighted transfor­ mations, Square Root, Natural Log and Product (Appendix B). Plots and network visu­ alisations were interpreted through a qualitative lens based on the initial qualitative analysis, thereby adding interpretive meaning to the underlying code co-occurrences beyond what the statistical output alone can convey (Shaffer, 2017). The last code in the Codebook, Off-topic, was excluded during analysis. We defined conversations as all lines of data associated with a single value of a theme subsetted by the group. For example, one conversation consisted of all the lines associated with a single theme (e.g., Learning Analytics) and a specific group (e.g., LAFG01). The ENA model normalised the networks for all units of analysis before they were subjected to a dimensional reduction, which accounts for the fact that different units of analysis may have different numbers of coded lines in the data. For the dimensional",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,10,"model normalised the networks for all units of analysis before they were subjected to a dimensional reduction, which accounts for the fact that different units of analysis may have different numbers of coded lines in the data. For the dimensional Table 5 Window length overview range Win­ dow length Variance dimension 1 co- registration correlations Variance dimension 2 co- registration correlations Mann–Whitney U test X axis (SVD 1) Mann–Whitney U test Y axis (SVD 2) 3 lines 0.94 (Pearson) and 0.94 (Spearman) 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) U = 375.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.98 U = 154.00, p = 0.34, r = 0.19 4 lines 0.95 (Pearson) and 0.95 (Spearman) 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) U = 373.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.97 U = 200.00, p = 0.77, r = − 0.06 5 lines 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) U = 370.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.96 U = 200.00, p = 0.77, r = 0.06 6 lines 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.97",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,10,"0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) U = 370.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.96 U = 200.00, p = 0.77, r = 0.06 6 lines 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.97 (Spearman) U = 368.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.95 U = 209.50, p = 0.57, r = − 0.11 7 lines 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.97 (Spearman) 0.97 (Pearson) and 0.97 (Spearman) U = 368.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.95 U = 204.00, p = 0.69, r = − 0.08",93
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,11,"Page 11 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 reduction, ENA uses a singular value decomposition (SVD), which produces orthogonal dimensions that maximise the variance explained by each dimension (Bowman et al., 2021). Networks were visualised as network graphs, where nodes represented codes and edges indicated the relative frequency of co-occurrence between two codes. Each unit of analysis was shown both as a plotted point marking the location of that unit’s network in a low-dimensional projected space and as a weighted network graph. Node positions within the network graphs were fixed using an optimisation routine that minimised the distance between plotted points and their corresponding network centroids. Because the network graphs and projected space are co-registered, the positions of nodes and their connecting edges can be used to interpret the dimensions of the projected space and explain the locations of plotted points (Bowman et al., 2021). In the present analy­ sis, means rotation was not performed because preliminary examination revealed that group means were already well-aligned along the X-axis of the original",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,11,"the projected space and explain the locations of plotted points (Bowman et al., 2021). In the present analy­ sis, means rotation was not performed because preliminary examination revealed that group means were already well-aligned along the X-axis of the original SVD space. Given this existing alignment, additional means rotation would not have provided meaningful improvement to group separation or interpretability of the dimensional space. ENA can be used to compare units of analysis in terms of their plotted point positions, individual networks, mean plotted point positions, and mean networks, which aver­ age the connection weights across individual networks. To understand how the GenAI discourse and LA discourse in LD compare, we compared our networks using network difference graphs. For this purpose, the subtraction network (Fig. 4) was calculated by subtracting the weight of each connection in the LA network from the corresponding connections in the GenAI network. To test for differences, we applied a Mann–Whitney U test to the locations of points in the projected ENA space for units in the LA group and the AI group. Results The results",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,11,"the corresponding connections in the GenAI network. To test for differences, we applied a Mann–Whitney U test to the locations of points in the projected ENA space for units in the LA group and the AI group. Results The results indicate that the visualisation of the model had co-registration correlations of 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) for the first dimension and co-registration cor­ relations of 0.96 (Pearson) and 0.96 (Spearman) for the second. These measures indicate a strong goodness of fit between the visualisations and the original model. The results of the Mann–Whitney U test indicate a significant difference between the AI and LA dis­ cussions on SVD 1, but not on SVD 2, as shown in Fig. 1. This figure indicates the loca­ tions of the points of the projected space along the X (SVD 1) and Y axis (SVD 2). Along the X axis (SVD 1), the Mann–Whitney U test showed that the LA group (Mdn = − 0.23, N = 21) was significantly different from the GenAI group (Mdn = 0.32, N = 18 U = 370.00,",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,11,"(SVD 2). Along the X axis (SVD 1), the Mann–Whitney U test showed that the LA group (Mdn = − 0.23, N = 21) was significantly different from the GenAI group (Mdn = 0.32, N = 18 U = 370.00, p < 0.001, r = − 0.96). Along the Y axis (SVD 2), the Mann–Whitney U test showed that the LA discussion (Mdn = 0.04, N = 21) was not significantly different from the AI discus­ sion (Mdn = − 0.05, N = 18 U = 200.00, p = 0.77, r = 0.06). Figure 1 indicates the combined LA and GenAI visualisation in the projected space. The LA centroid is positioned on the left, and the GenAI centroid is positioned on the right. RQ 1: How do factors that influence decision-making in the context of LA associate with each other? The network demonstrates the connection strength among the themes. The strongest connections are observed between contextual information and technology role (CTX and TECH: 0.32), contextual information and course level design (CTX and COURSE:",173
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,11,"The network demonstrates the connection strength among the themes. The strongest connections are observed between contextual information and technology role (CTX and TECH: 0.32), contextual information and course level design (CTX and COURSE:",33
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,12,"Page 12 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 0.23), and contextual information and creative problem solving (CTX and CPS: 0.32), forming the most densely connected clusters within the network. These themes show the highest co-occurrence rates, indicating that participants frequently discussed these concepts together when describing their LA decision-making processes. Moderate edge weights were found between technology role and learning activity design (TECH and ACT: 0.17), between student support and technology role (SUPP and TECH: 0.19), and between decision-making autonomy and context (D.AUTO and CTX: 0.14). Weaker connections include several pairings with lower co-occurrence rates: technology role and course level design (TECH and COURSE: 0.11), technology role and course design decision-making power (TECH and D.AUTO: 0.07), technology role and assessment redesign (TECH and ASSESS: 0.08) and technology role and design for student self- determination (TECH and D.SDT: 0.09). While these connections are less prominent in the discourse, they represent less frequently articulated relationships in instructors’ decision-making discussions. The network structure suggests that instructors’ discourse about LA centres primarily revolves around the",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,12,"student self- determination (TECH and D.SDT: 0.09). While these connections are less prominent in the discourse, they represent less frequently articulated relationships in instructors’ decision-making discussions. The network structure suggests that instructors’ discourse about LA centres primarily revolves around the intersection of contextual consider­ ations, course design requirements, and creative problem-solving approaches. This is evident in the triad contextual information and technology role (CTX and TECH: 0.32), contextual information and course level design (CTX and COURSE: 0.23), and contex­ tual information and creative problem solving (CTX and CPS: 0.32), suggesting that instructors actively use LA in combination with contextual information when engaging in creative problem-solving at the course level. Figure 2 presents the average epistemic Fig. 1 ENA Subtraction graph between LA theme (red on the left) and GenAI theme (blue on the right). The com­ parison plot was created using the binary model",144
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,13,"Page 13 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 network for all participants from LA focus groups. Table 6 indicates the edge weights for the LA focus group network, ordered by strength. RQ 2: How do factors that influence decision-making in the context of GenAI associate with each other? The network indicates the strongest connections between the following themes: contex­ tual information and creative problem solving (CTX and CPS: 0.26), technology role and assessment design (TECH and ASSESS: 0.26), and technology role and design for stu­ dent self-determination (TECH and D.SDT: 0.15). This dense interconnection suggests that participants frequently discussed these concepts together when considering the implementation of GenAI in their LD processes. Moderate connections form the second tier of associations, particularly between learning activity design and technology role (ACT and TECH: 0.15) and between technology role and course level design (TECH and COURSE: 0.14). These relationships indicate secondary but meaningful conceptual Table 6 ENA co-occurance edge strengths for the Learning Analytics theme Code pair Weight CTX and CPS 0.32 CTX and",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,13,0.15) and between technology role and course level design (TECH and COURSE: 0.14). These relationships indicate secondary but meaningful conceptual Table 6 ENA co-occurance edge strengths for the Learning Analytics theme Code pair Weight CTX and CPS 0.32 CTX and TECH 0.32 CTX and COURSE 0.23 SUPP and TECH 0.19 TECH and ACT 0.17 D.AUTO and CTX 0.14 TECH and COURSE 0.11 TECH and D.SDT 0.09 TECH and ASSESS 0.08 TECH and D.AUTO 0.07 Fig. 2 ENA Subtraction graph between LA theme (red on the left) and GenAI theme (blue on the right). The com­ parison plot was created using the Natural Log Weighted Model,105
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,14,"Page 14 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 linkages in instructors’ discussions about GenAI. Weaker connections are observed between LD decision-making power and technology (D.AUTO and TECH: 0.06), as well as between course design decision-making power and student support (D.AUTO and SUPP: 0.01). Notably, the connection between the technology role and student support (TECH and SUPP: 0.03) demonstrates minimal co-occurrence, suggesting that instruc­ tors rarely discuss these concepts together when considering GenAI applications in their design practice. The network reveals that instructors’ GenAI discourse mainly focuses on contextual information and assessment redesign. Figure 3 presents the average ENA network derived from the combined GenAI focus group data, revealing the patterns of association between factors. Table 7 presents the connection weights for the GenAI focus group epistemic network, ranked by strength. Table 7 ENA co-occurance edge strengths for the GenAI theme Code pair Weight CTX and CPS 0.26 TECH and ASSESS 0.26 TECH and COURSE 0.14 TECH and D.SDT 0.15 ACT and TECH 0.15 D.AUTO and TECH 0.06 TECH and SUPP 0.03",180
