DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,15,"Page 15 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 RQ3: What are the similarities and differences between the factors that influence LD in the context of LA and GenAI? This subtractive network reveals distinct emphases and differences in how instructors discuss LA and GenAI. The plot shows stronger associations in the LA group for several key themes. The technology role demonstrates high co-occurrence with student support (TECH and SUPP: 0.16), indicating that instructors more frequently discussed student support in relation to LA than GenAI. Additionally, the LA discourse shows a stron­ ger co-occurrence between technology role and course (TECH and COURSE: − 0.03), LD decision-making autonomy and technology (D.AUTO and TECH: 0.01), and activity design and technology (ACTIVITY and TECH: − 0.02). The instructor’s emotion code had low co-occurrences with four ties. These are instructor emotion and decision-mak­ ing autonomy (I.E and D.AUTO: 0.01), instructor emotion and activity (I.E and ACT 0.02), instructor emotion and support (I.E and SUPP: 0.02). In contrast, the GenAI group exhibits stronger associations between technology role and",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,15,"These are instructor emotion and decision-mak­ ing autonomy (I.E and D.AUTO: 0.01), instructor emotion and activity (I.E and ACT 0.02), instructor emotion and support (I.E and SUPP: 0.02). In contrast, the GenAI group exhibits stronger associations between technology role and assessment design (TECH and ASSESS: − 0.18) and between technology role and design for student self-determination (TECH and D.SDT: − 0.06). Instructor emotion had low co-occurrences, with five ties within GenAI: Instructor emotion and assessment (I.E and ASSESS. − 0.04), instructor emotion technology (I.E and TECH: − 0.03), instruc­ tor emotion and creative problem solving (I.E and CPS: − 0.03), and instructor emotion and design for student self-determination (I.E and D.SDT: − 0.01). The co-occurrence of creative problem-solving and technology was more prominent in GenAI discussions, although creative problem-solving maintained a central position across both groups (CPS and TECH: − 0.13). This pattern suggests that when discussing GenAI, instructors more frequently consider GenAI in relation to assessment design and design approaches (i.e. student self-determination). The centrality of the CTX code across both networks, highlights the intricate nature of LD when",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,15,"0.13). This pattern suggests that when discussing GenAI, instructors more frequently consider GenAI in relation to assessment design and design approaches (i.e. student self-determination). The centrality of the CTX code across both networks, highlights the intricate nature of LD when drawing on LA and GenAI. Figure 4 illustrates the subtraction network comparison between GenAI and LA, high­ lighting the differences in themes and connections. Discussion This study examined the factors influencing instructors’ LD decision-making and prac­ tice when integrating LA and GenAI into their LD practices. Drawing on a thematic analysis of focus group discussions and ENA, we investigated how instructors discuss LD in relation to GenAI and LA, and identified factors that influence LD practice. While both technologies offer powerful capabilities for enhancing LD practice, instructors’ adoption decisions are shaped by multiple intersecting factors, including their emo­ tions, creative problem-solving skills, perceptions of their autonomy, and contextual considerations. Their LD decisions and practices related to building their students’ self- determination, iteratively enhancing their LD at the course and activity levels, and better supporting their students and designing assessment tasks.",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,15,"problem-solving skills, perceptions of their autonomy, and contextual considerations. Their LD decisions and practices related to building their students’ self- determination, iteratively enhancing their LD at the course and activity levels, and better supporting their students and designing assessment tasks. Combined, these factors pro­ vide an understanding of instructors’ lived experience when utilising LA and GenAI, and offer insight into instructors’ LD decision-making. Our analysis reveals distinct patterns of reasoning that characterise instructors’ use of LA versus GenAI, illuminating complex dynamics that influence technology integration in higher education. The following sec­ tions present our results systematically by research question.",99
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,16,"Page 16 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 RQ 1: How do factors that influence learning design in the context of LA associate with each other? The ENA revealed that instructors approached the use of LA to support their problem- solving by drawing on LA ( i.e. teaching dashboards) in tandem with information about their educational context to build a holistic picture of the changes they would make to their LD. When discussing LA, the ENA results indicate that instructors spoke most frequently about contextual considerations, course design, and creative problem-solv­ ing approaches. The prominence of contextual considerations in instructor discussions reflects the broader recognition that LA cannot be meaningfully interpreted without understanding the pedagogical intent (Lockyer et al., 2013). This highlights the impor­ tance of the educational context on instructors’ decisions. In the LA discussions, the instructors often referred to activity design (ACT) and stu­ dent support (SUPP), and their educational context influenced their autonomy in deci­ sion-making. Leadership LD decision-making autonomy refers to instructors’ capacity to make choices about",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,16,"decisions. In the LA discussions, the instructors often referred to activity design (ACT) and stu­ dent support (SUPP), and their educational context influenced their autonomy in deci­ sion-making. Leadership LD decision-making autonomy refers to instructors’ capacity to make choices about their LD practices based on their LD expertise, design approach, and within the external mandates or systemic constraints. While constraints cannot be avoided, complexity leadership approaches can help navigate tensions arising from envi­ ronmental pressure (Dawson et al., 2018). This approach gives instructors shared auton­ omy in institutional decision-making. Co-occurrence in the discussions highlights LA’s value in identifying where students need additional support as well as designing supportive scaffolds. In a study by Lim et al. (2023), instructors most often used LA data to identify where students were struggling, Fig. 4 ENA Subtraction graph between LA theme (red on the left) and GenAI theme (blue on the right). The com­ parison plot was created using the Square Root Weighted Model",160
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,17,"Page 17 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 allowing them to provide personalised feedback. Our findings show that most instruc­ tors talk about different levels of course granularity (i.e. course level, activity level, student support) when making use of LA to improve their LD. The LA-informed LD practices of the instructors strongly support the findings of Law and Liang (2020), sug­ gesting the need for multilevel structures that recognise that LD operates at different levels, from broad learning outcomes down to specific activities. In our study, instruc­ tors made iterations to their activities based on trace data. These findings show that instructors rely on LA and contextual information to inform their creative problem- solving related to their course design, individual activities, and student support. RQ 2: How do factors that influence learning design in the context of GenAI associate with each other? The ENA identified strong co-occurrences between GenAI (TECH) and assessment design (ASSESS) as well as GenAI (TECH) and design for student self-determination (D.SDT). Instructors created first drafts of assessment",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,17,"learning design in the context of GenAI associate with each other? The ENA identified strong co-occurrences between GenAI (TECH) and assessment design (ASSESS) as well as GenAI (TECH) and design for student self-determination (D.SDT). Instructors created first drafts of assessment elements (i.e. case studies and rubrics) using GenAI. This echoes the findings of a recent systematic review where Nikolic et al. (2024) found that instructors value GenAI for creating assignments and tests. The use of SDT as an learning design framework has been suggested by Martin et al. (2018), where design elements are organised around autonomy, competence and relatedness. In our study, the design for student self-determination was evident as an instructional approach, where student autonomy, for example, was considered by co- designing course elements with students. Ryan and Deci, (2020) state that autonomy is best facilitated when students are provided with meaningful choices and when learning tasks align with their personal interests and values. For example, in our study, instructors co-designed assessment rubrics with students in initial teacher education (i.e. pre-ser­ vice teachers), scaffolding their competence in learning design",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,17,"provided with meaningful choices and when learning tasks align with their personal interests and values. For example, in our study, instructors co-designed assessment rubrics with students in initial teacher education (i.e. pre-ser­ vice teachers), scaffolding their competence in learning design and providing them with autonomy over their learning experience. Similarly, Zheng et al. (2020) found that the opportunity for students to contribute to the creation of assessment rubrics increased their motivation. However, there was less co-occurrence of GenAI (TECH) and course level (COURSE). This can be attributed to a range of factors, such as instructors stating that they value backward design and constructive alignment, yet not mentioning the use of GenAI for this purpose. Divjak et al. (2025) found similar adoption patterns, where instructors tend to trust GenAI more in the design of units and activities, as well as in generating ideas, rather than for generating topics and learning outcomes. Similarly, the ENA results showed low co-occurrence between GenAI (TECH) and student support (SUPP), as well as between GenAI (TECH) and decision-making autonomy (D.AUTO). This suggests that when instructors discussed",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,17,"generating ideas, rather than for generating topics and learning outcomes. Similarly, the ENA results showed low co-occurrence between GenAI (TECH) and student support (SUPP), as well as between GenAI (TECH) and decision-making autonomy (D.AUTO). This suggests that when instructors discussed GenAI, they rarely focused on how it could enhance stu­ dent support or how the extent of their autonomy affected their LD decisions. Instead, instructor discussions were primarily focused on assessment design in relation to their design approach. In addition, instructors’ sense of LD agency was more influenced by their educational context than by GenAI.",95
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,18,"Page 18 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 RQ 3: What are the similarities and differences between the factors that influence LD in the context of LA and GenAI? Across both LA and GenAI outcome networks, the co-occurrence of technology role (TECH), contextual information (CTX), and creative problem solving (CPS) was high. This indicates that instructors drew on LA and GenAI in combination with contextual considerations and course design requirements to solve instructional problems, utilis­ ing their creativity and collaborating with colleagues. Similarly, Toetenel and Rienties (2016a, 2016b) found that collaboration during workshops made learning design more creative and student-centred. Prioritising collaboration could yield positive benefits, as this supports the need for relatedness, a key SDT construct. In addition, co-design pro­ cesses can provide an opportunity to develop LD competence as a community of prac­ tice, building on each other’s skillsets and through conversations that unpack contextual considerations, thereby fostering instructors’ relatedness, another key SDT construct. According to SDT, supporting autonomy, relatedness, and competence is critical for need satisfaction (Ryan &",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,18,"a community of prac­ tice, building on each other’s skillsets and through conversations that unpack contextual considerations, thereby fostering instructors’ relatedness, another key SDT construct. According to SDT, supporting autonomy, relatedness, and competence is critical for need satisfaction (Ryan & Deci, 2020), which is a significant predictor of favourable work outcomes, including enhanced performance and better workplace adaptation (Baard et al., 2004). These findings highlight the importance of creating a supportive environment for instructors (Varga-Atkins et al., 2021). Interestingly, instructor emotion (I.E) was linked to various codes across both LA and GenAI networks. The creative nature of LD means that initial attempts may require mul­ tiple iterations, requiring emotional resilience (Sharpe et al., 2022). While instructor emotion was evident in both LA and GenAI focus groups, the GenAI discussion elicited a more emotional response from instructors. From an SDT perspective, both positive and negative emotions can aid sensemaking and inform choices (Roth et al., 2019), and recognising instructors’ emotional dimensions is essential to the iterative design process. Giving instructors the opportunity to explore a full range of emotions without judgment",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,18,"both positive and negative emotions can aid sensemaking and inform choices (Roth et al., 2019), and recognising instructors’ emotional dimensions is essential to the iterative design process. Giving instructors the opportunity to explore a full range of emotions without judgment or trying to control their emotional responses, either by an external party (i.e., support staff, university leadership) or by the instructors themselves, could lead to better integra­ tive emotional regulation, resulting in enhanced decision-making and creativity. Implications We identified the following factors that influence instructors’ LD when integrating LA and GenAI: contextual information, course design decision-making autonomy, cre­ ative problem solving, learning activity design, assessment design, design approach (i.e. design for students’ self-determination), and student support. Instructors can draw on the affordances of LA and GenAI in combination with the aspects identified in this study enabling a holistic technology integrated approach (Nguyen et al., 2025). Keeping assess­ ments current through the thoughtful use of GenAI supports academic integrity, LA- informed decision-making, and alignment with students’ learning needs. Using LA to understand engagement and GenAI to guide course and assessment redesign",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,18,"approach (Nguyen et al., 2025). Keeping assess­ ments current through the thoughtful use of GenAI supports academic integrity, LA- informed decision-making, and alignment with students’ learning needs. Using LA to understand engagement and GenAI to guide course and assessment redesign enables more proactive, student-centered support. For example when instructors assess course level analytics, instructors may identify patterns of student difficulty and subsequently leverage GenAI to help generate targeted support material (Bahroun et al., 2023). In addition, creative problem-solving emerges as a primary factor as instructors navigate the implementation of their educational intent while integrating information from learn­ ing analytics alongside contextual factors. Supporting psychological needs satisfaction",106
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,19,"Page 19 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 does not only benefits instructors’ well-being (Thies & Kordts-Freudinger, 2019) but also provides better support for students (Yasué et al., 2019). To support instructors’ autonomy, competence, and relatedness in educational deci­ sion-making practice, universities can implement several key strategies. Firstly, from a leadership perspective it is important to ensure contextual and technology integration information flow across the institution in a transparant way. The exchange of design relevant networking can be facilitated by supporting collaboration. In addition, Tsai et al. (2020) suggest creating a culture of experimentation, brokering activities and net­ working opportunities. Collaborative design interventions have been shown to improve creativity (Toetenel & Rienties, 2016a, 2016b). Instructors’ emotional well-being and motivation can be enhanced through collaborative design processes and communities of practice. Secondly, sharing decision-making between leadership and instructors with regards to GenAI integrity, course design policy and professional development would enable a better understanding of constraints and opportunities and allow a more rubust co-ordinated effort. Dawson et al., (2018) describes repommendations for",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,19,"sharing decision-making between leadership and instructors with regards to GenAI integrity, course design policy and professional development would enable a better understanding of constraints and opportunities and allow a more rubust co-ordinated effort. Dawson et al., (2018) describes repommendations for distributed leadership and shared decision-making at the institutional level. Involving instructors in decisions about LA and GenAI tool selection and implementation processes, and pro­ viding instructors with meaningful choices in how they integrate these technologies into their teaching practices. These collaborative environments support key psychological need-satisfaction aspects such as competence, by providing and receiving feedback and fostering relatedness. Limitations and future studies This research has several limitations that inform future work. First, participants were recruited from one faculty, consisting of instructors teaching in pre-service and enabling programs, from one large Australian university, through voluntary participation. In addition, there was an substantial overlap in participants across the LA and GenAI focus groups (14 of the participants contributed to both groups). The context of pre-service teaching influenced the results pertaining to participants engaging in co-design with their students, which may not necessarily",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,19,"substantial overlap in participants across the LA and GenAI focus groups (14 of the participants contributed to both groups). The context of pre-service teaching influenced the results pertaining to participants engaging in co-design with their students, which may not necessarily transfer to other discipline contexts. Future studies could extend the scope of investigation beyond pre-service and enabling pro­ grams by including different disciplines and contexts to encompass a broader range of disciplines and institutional settings, thereby enhancing the generalisability of these findings. Second, the initial low pre-moderation K-values for certain codes (Tables 1 and 2) reflect initial uncertainty during independent coding. Notably, the codes D.AUTO and I.E. exhibited high raw agreement but disproportionately low kappa values, a pattern attributable to the low base rate of these codes rather than to substantive disagreement between coders (Feinstein & Cicchetti, 1990). The coding decisions involved subjective interpretation that may have influenced the results. In addition, ENA networks capture patterns in discourse, specifically, how frequently codes co-occur within the segments of talk analysed rather than direct observations of participant behaviour. Where the discus­ sion",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,19,"decisions involved subjective interpretation that may have influenced the results. In addition, ENA networks capture patterns in discourse, specifically, how frequently codes co-occur within the segments of talk analysed rather than direct observations of participant behaviour. Where the discus­ sion draws connections between low or high co-occurrence and instructors' discussions, the interpretations should be understood as researcher inferences grounded in discourse patterns. A further limitation concerns the choice of aggregation method and ENA mov­ ing window used in the ENA model. This study employed binary aggregation and this",88
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,20,"Page 20 of 24 Claassen et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:43 approach does not account for the intensity or repetition of connections within a given unit of analysis. Conclusion This study examined instructors’ discussions concerning LD practice when integrat­ ing LA and GenAI. Drawing on thematic analysis of focus groups followed by ENA, we investigated how instructors navigate the evolving landscape of educational technology integration in their design practices. The ENA results demonstrated that LA discussions were characterised by co-occurrences between contextual considerations, course-level design, and creative problem-solving approaches, while GenAI discussions showed strong co-occurrences between assessment design, context, and design approaches (i.e., for student self-determination). Across both LA and GenAI networks, high co-occur­ rence patterns emerged between the technology’s role, contextual information, and cre­ ative problem-solving, indicating that instructors consistently combine technological affordances with contextual considerations and creative problem-solving. These findings reveal that instructors utilise LA for problem-solving, from broad course design to spe­ cific learning activities, assessment creation, and targeted student support. Meanwhile, instructors use GenAI for ideation and development of assessment",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,20,"with contextual considerations and creative problem-solving. These findings reveal that instructors utilise LA for problem-solving, from broad course design to spe­ cific learning activities, assessment creation, and targeted student support. Meanwhile, instructors use GenAI for ideation and development of assessment elements, guided by their own design approach. Supporting instructors’ basic psychological needs —auton­ omy, competence, and relatedness — will foster creative problem-solving approaches in LD. Appendix A Table 8 outlines the guiding questions developed for the Learning Analytics (LA) focus group discussions. Table 9 provides an overview of the guiding questions that structured the Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) focus group. Appendix B Testing of window length sensitivity Co-registration correlations were consistently strong across all window sizes. At a win­ dow size of three lines, the model had co-registration correlations of 0.94 (Pearson) and Table 8 Focus group guiding questions- LA Data collection phase 1: LA How do you go about course design? How does data help your course design? How does data influence your motivation? Do you feel a sense of autonomy when you design? Do you design as a",180
DOC_036,NEW19.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00619-4,20,Data collection phase 1: LA How do you go about course design? How does data help your course design? How does data influence your motivation? Do you feel a sense of autonomy when you design? Do you design as a collaborative process? Table 9 Focus group guiding questions – GenAI Data collection phase 2: GenAI How are you learning about GenAI? Have you made changes to your courses because of GenAI technologies like ChatGPT or BARD? Are you currently using AI to help you design your course? What barriers do you face when using AI in course design process?,99
