DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,1,"OPEN Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 1 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 How instructional design shapes higher-order thinking among higher education students in generative artificial intelligence- supported teaching contexts Yujia Chen1 & Jinling Wang2 Against the backdrop of the widespread integration of generative artificial intelligence (GAI) into higher education, previous research has yielded divergent findings regarding its relationship with students’ higher-order thinking (HOT). Moving beyond outcome-oriented discussions of technology use itself, this study examines the association between HOT performance, instructional design elements, and cognitive processes in GAI-supported teaching contexts from an instructional design perspective. A multimethod analysis combining structural equation modeling (SEM) and fuzzy-set qualitative comparative analysis (fsQCA) was conducted. The findings indicate that HOT performance cannot be explained by technology use alone. Rather, it is more likely to be associated with cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration processes shaped by instructional design. Different instructional design elements played differentiated roles in triggering cognitive conflict and supporting cognitive elaboration. The overall influence of disciplinary context on these cognitive processes was limited, although one individual path showed a significant difference. The configurational analysis further showed that",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,1,"instructional design elements played differentiated roles in triggering cognitive conflict and supporting cognitive elaboration. The overall influence of disciplinary context on these cognitive processes was limited, although one individual path showed a significant difference. The configurational analysis further showed that high HOT performance did not correspond to a single pathway but was associated with multiple combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive processes, demonstrating configurational multiplicity and asymmetry. These findings suggest that differences in HOT performance in GAI-supported learning contexts may stem from variations in instructional design and the cognitive pathways associated with it, rather than from technology use alone. The study provides a mechanism-based explanation of how human–AI learning relationships in higher education can be understood through instructional design and cognitive processes. Keywords Generative artificial intelligence, Instructional design, Higher-order thinking, Higher education, Fuzzy-set qualitative comparative analysis Rapid advances in next-generation digital technologies, including big data, cloud computing, and artificial intelligence, are driving systemic changes in occupational structures and ways of working1,2. Tasks traditionally dependent on rule execution and knowledge repetition are increasingly being replaced by algorithms and intelligent systems,",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,1,"next-generation digital technologies, including big data, cloud computing, and artificial intelligence, are driving systemic changes in occupational structures and ways of working1,2. Tasks traditionally dependent on rule execution and knowledge repetition are increasingly being replaced by algorithms and intelligent systems, while the ability to make value judgments, conduct integrative analyses, and reach complex decisions under conditions of high uncertainty has become a core competence for adapting to technological and social change3,4. In this context, the goals of higher education must shift from knowledge transmission toward the systematic cultivation of students’ higher-order thinking (HOT), placing new demands on educational philosophies and teaching models. As an important stage in the evolution of artificial intelligence, generative artificial intelligence (GAI), which is based on large-scale pretrained models, offers substantial advantages in language comprehension, knowledge integration, semantic reasoning, and content generation5–7. Unlike earlier educational technologies oriented toward rule-based or closed tasks, GAI can provide multiple response pathways, immediate feedback, and dynamic support in open-ended learning tasks and is rapidly permeating teaching practice in higher education8–11. 1Department of Education, Kyungnam University, 7 Gyeongnamdaehak-ro, Masanhappo-gu, Changwon-si 51767,",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,1,"oriented toward rule-based or closed tasks, GAI can provide multiple response pathways, immediate feedback, and dynamic support in open-ended learning tasks and is rapidly permeating teaching practice in higher education8–11. 1Department of Education, Kyungnam University, 7 Gyeongnamdaehak-ro, Masanhappo-gu, Changwon-si 51767, Gyeongsangnam-do, Republic of Korea. 2School of Languages and Media, Anhui University of Finance and Economics, 962 Caoshan Road, Bengbu 233030, Anhui, China. email: jinlingwangty@sina.com www.nature.com/scientificreports",65
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 2 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports Accordingly, the use of GAI in higher education, particularly its relationship with the development of students’ HOT, has become an important focus of educational technology research12–15. Previous studies generally suggest that GAI can support the development of students’ HOT when it is used with appropriate instructional guidance16–19. These positive findings coexist, however, with continuing concerns about possible adverse effects. Excessive reliance on GAI tools during learning may weaken students’ cognitive engagement in analysis, reflection, and reasoning and may therefore constrain the development of independent thinking20,21. These divergent findings suggest that the relationship between GAI and students’ HOT is neither linear nor unconditional but depends on how the technology is used within specific teaching contexts and on the guidance mechanisms provided. Existing research has nevertheless concentrated largely on overall effects, often treating appropriate guidance as an assumed precondition rather than asking which specific instructional design elements enact such guidance, which cognitive mechanisms underlie their operation, and whether different elements work together in multiple combinations to support HOT. Building on higher education teaching",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"appropriate guidance as an assumed precondition rather than asking which specific instructional design elements enact such guidance, which cognitive mechanisms underlie their operation, and whether different elements work together in multiple combinations to support HOT. Building on higher education teaching practice, this study takes teacher-led course design and instructional intervention as its point of departure. From a cognitive-process perspective, it examines the relationships among instructional design elements, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT performance in GAI- supported teaching contexts. SEM and fsQCA are combined to analyze variable relationships and configurational pathways, respectively, thereby providing a more comprehensive account of the associated mechanisms and structural characteristics. Accordingly, this study addresses the following research questions: RQ1. What is the relationship between instructional design elements and students’ HOT performance in GAI-supported learning experiences? RQ2. Through which cognitive processes are instructional design elements associated with students’ HOT performance? RQ3. Is this relational structure stable across different disciplinary contexts? RQ4. Do instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms form different combinations that are associated with multiple and asymmetric pathways to high HOT? Literature review Generative artificial",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"with students’ HOT performance? RQ3. Is this relational structure stable across different disciplinary contexts? RQ4. Do instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms form different combinations that are associated with multiple and asymmetric pathways to high HOT? Literature review Generative artificial intelligence and higher-order thinking: findings and divergent perspectives HOT is generally understood as learners’ capacity to analyze, reason, synthesize, and solve problems in complex contexts and is a central educational goal for preparing students to address societal complexity and future occupational challenges22–24. As GAI has become widely used in education, research attention has shifted from technical feasibility to its potential relationship with students’ higher-order cognitive capabilities, producing a substantial body of research25,26. Most current evidence supports a positive relationship between GAI use and HOT in higher education. A recent meta-analysis integrating empirical findings across teaching contexts found an overall improvement in HOT, although the magnitude of the effect varied with teaching context and task characteristics18. Another meta-analysis similarly showed that students in GAI-supported learning settings generally demonstrated stronger HOT than those in conventional settings17. At the level of teaching practice,",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"in HOT, although the magnitude of the effect varied with teaching context and task characteristics18. Another meta-analysis similarly showed that students in GAI-supported learning settings generally demonstrated stronger HOT than those in conventional settings17. At the level of teaching practice, qualitative evidence indicates that the content-generation support and ease of use provided by GAI may strengthen creativity and information literacy27. Human–AI collaboration has also been associated with greater engagement and higher- order cognition during complex problem solving19, while institutional AI capability may be indirectly associated with HOT through teaching systems and curriculum planning16. These positive findings are not consistent across all instructional contexts. Without explicit guidance and task constraints, GAI may be adversely associated with HOT20,21. Strong reliance on AI tools can reduce learners’ independent analysis, reflection, and reasoning, thereby weakening cognitive engagement and deep processing28. Experimental evidence has also indicated that GAI-supported creative work can constrain design- thinking dispositions, associative ability, and learner autonomy29. Accordingly, this study shifts the focus from discussions of the overall educational effects of GAI to an analysis of the relationships among instructional design elements,",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"indicated that GAI-supported creative work can constrain design- thinking dispositions, associative ability, and learner autonomy29. Accordingly, this study shifts the focus from discussions of the overall educational effects of GAI to an analysis of the relationships among instructional design elements, cognitive processes, and HOT performance in GAI-supported learning contexts. It focuses on how teachers may use course design and instructional intervention to guide students in comparing, verifying, and reflecting on generated content, and how such processes may be associated with HOT performance. Artificial intelligence-supported learning from an instructional design perspective Against the background of conflicting findings concerning GAI and HOT, research on technology-supported learning suggests that learning outcomes do not depend solely on the technological tool but are closely related to instructional design and the organization of learning activities30–32. GAI is generally not regarded as an independent factor that directly determines students’ HOT. Its educational value depends substantially on how teachers use instructional design to structure students’ participation in learning5,33–35. The organization of instructional tasks, teacher support, and assessment and feedback mechanisms have been identified as key conditions shaping AI-supported",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,2,"determines students’ HOT. Its educational value depends substantially on how teachers use instructional design to structure students’ participation in learning5,33–35. The organization of instructional tasks, teacher support, and assessment and feedback mechanisms have been identified as key conditions shaping AI-supported learning outcomes36,37. Problem-oriented and open-ended tasks can create cognitively challenging situations and encourage students to use GAI as a tool for thinking and exploration rather than as a direct substitute for obtaining answers, thereby sustaining engagement in higher-order cognitive activity38. Cognitive and emotional support from teachers can alleviate anxiety when students face complex tasks and uncertain situations and can reduce overreliance on GAI under high cognitive pressure39,40. Assessment and feedback mechanisms are also important. Process- oriented assessment can prompt students to reflect on and revise GAI-generated results, identify limitations in",130
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 3 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports their understanding, and adjust their learning strategies34. Conversely, in the absence of explicit feedback and guidance, particularly when teaching is focused only on outcomes, students are more likely to use GAI to reduce cognitive effort, weakening its potential relationship with HOT41. Cognitive construction of higher-order thinking: a constructivist perspective Once the central role of instructional design in AI-supported learning has been established, the mechanisms through which HOT itself develops must also be considered. Constructivist learning theory provides an important framework for understanding this process. It proposes that HOT is not directly transmitted by instructional content or technological tools but is gradually constructed when learners encounter challenging tasks, experience cognitive disequilibrium, engage in deep cognitive processing, and continuously reorganize their knowledge structures42,43. Cognitive conflict is regarded as an important starting point in this process. It refers to the state of cognitive disequilibrium that arises when learners become aware of deficiencies in their existing understanding or inconsistencies between perspectives. Its principal function is to activate problem awareness and prompt further thought44,45. In AI-supported",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"this process. It refers to the state of cognitive disequilibrium that arises when learners become aware of deficiencies in their existing understanding or inconsistencies between perspectives. Its principal function is to activate problem awareness and prompt further thought44,45. In AI-supported learning, the uncertainty and diversity of technology-generated results may expose limitations in learners’ understanding and trigger cognitive conflict46,47. Cognitive conflict alone, however, is insufficient to account for the development of HOT; its role may be realized through subsequent cognitive elaboration. Cognitive elaboration refers to the deep processing activities—including explanation, reasoning, comparison, and integration—through which learners seek to understand and resolve problems after experiencing cognitive conflict. It is an important mechanism of cognitive adaptation and knowledge restructuring48. Through this process, initial disequilibrium can be transformed into more advanced understanding and thought. Cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration therefore constitute a sequential construction process rather than two parallel influences: the former initiates cognitive construction, whereas the latter advances it49. Both are accordingly included in the same serial mediation framework. The cognitive construction of HOT may not proceed identically in every learning context. Learners’",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"process rather than two parallel influences: the former initiates cognitive construction, whereas the latter advances it49. Both are accordingly included in the same serial mediation framework. The cognitive construction of HOT may not proceed identically in every learning context. Learners’ self- efficacy, self-regulation, and emotional engagement can affect the depth of sustained cognitive processing in GAI- supported learning39,43. These individual differences primarily influence the intensity of engagement and cannot by themselves explain systematic changes in the structure of cognitive mechanisms across learning contexts. Structural differences among disciplines in teaching approaches, task forms, and assessment philosophies may be more likely to affect how cognitive conflict is triggered and transformed into cognitive elaboration50. The humanities and social sciences, for example, emphasize interpretation, argumentation, and the construction of multiple perspectives, whereas science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) disciplines place greater emphasis on logical reasoning and empirical problem solving. The study therefore distinguishes between STEM and non-STEM groups and uses multigroup SEM to examine whether the paths involved in the cognitive construction of HOT differ between disciplinary contexts. Research design and methods Research design",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"reasoning and empirical problem solving. The study therefore distinguishes between STEM and non-STEM groups and uses multigroup SEM to examine whether the paths involved in the cognitive construction of HOT differ between disciplinary contexts. Research design and methods Research design and conceptual framework In GAI-supported teaching contexts, this study used SEM as the primary analytical method to systematically examine the association patterns between instructional design and students’ HOT development and to test the structural relationships and paths among multiple variables. In the research framework, instructional design was specified as the independent variable and consisted of inquiry-based learning, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms. Cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration were modeled as sequential mediators to capture the key cognitive processes experienced by learners in GAI-supported teaching contexts. HOT was specified as the outcome variable, reflecting students’ abilities in analysis, reasoning, synthesis, and problem solving. Through SEM, this study examined the direct relationships between instructional design elements and HOT performance, as well as the serial mediation pathway formed through cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration. To assess whether these structural relationships were applicable across",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"problem solving. Through SEM, this study examined the direct relationships between instructional design elements and HOT performance, as well as the serial mediation pathway formed through cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration. To assess whether these structural relationships were applicable across different disciplinary contexts, disciplinary type was further introduced as a grouping variable, and multigroup SEM was used to compare path relationships across disciplinary samples and analyze structural stability. On this basis, considering that HOT development may be associated with multiple combinations of conditions rather than a single linear pathway, this study further employed fsQCA to identify configurational relationships among instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms, thereby complementing SEM from a configurational perspective in explaining complex associations. The overall research framework is shown in Fig. 1. Research hypotheses Instructional design and cognitive conflict Instructional design may be related to students’ awareness of informational differences and deficiencies in understanding, and may further be associated with the emergence of cognitive conflict. Different instructional design elements may play different roles in activating cognitive conflict. H1. Inquiry-based learning is significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,3,"of informational differences and deficiencies in understanding, and may further be associated with the emergence of cognitive conflict. Different instructional design elements may play different roles in activating cognitive conflict. H1. Inquiry-based learning is significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive conflict. H2. Teacher support is significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive conflict. H3. Assessment mechanisms are significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive conflict. Instructional design elements and cognitive elaboration In GAI-supported learning, instructional design may not only be related to the emergence of cognitive conflict, but also to whether students further engage in cognitive elaboration activities such as explanation, reasoning,",100
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,4,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 4 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports and integration. The strength of the relationships between different instructional design elements and cognitive elaboration may vary. H4. Inquiry-based learning is significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive elaboration. H5. Teacher support is significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive elaboration. H6. Assessment mechanisms are significantly positively associated with students’ cognitive elaboration. Cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration As a state of cognitive disequilibrium, cognitive conflict may encourage students to engage in deeper cognitive processing. Further analysis of and reflection on informational differences may contribute to cognitive elaboration. H7. Students’ cognitive conflict is significantly positively associated with their cognitive elaboration. Cognitive mechanisms, instructional design, and higher-order thinking In GAI-supported teaching, HOT may emerge from sustained cognitive elaboration or may be activated through cognitive conflict and the direct roles of instructional design. Instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms may therefore be jointly associated with HOT through multiple paths. H8. Students’ cognitive elaboration is significantly positively associated with their HOT performance. H9. Inquiry-based learning is significantly positively associated with students’ HOT performance. H10. Teacher support is",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,4,"and cognitive mechanisms may therefore be jointly associated with HOT through multiple paths. H8. Students’ cognitive elaboration is significantly positively associated with their HOT performance. H9. Inquiry-based learning is significantly positively associated with students’ HOT performance. H10. Teacher support is significantly positively associated with students’ HOT performance. H11. Assessment mechanisms are significantly positively associated with students’ HOT performance. H12. Students’ cognitive conflict is significantly positively associated with their HOT performance. Research Instruments The questionnaire measured six focal constructs: inquiry-based learning, teacher support, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT. All items were rated on a seven-point Likert scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 7 (strongly agree). To align the measures with the study context, the items addressed generative artificial intelligence-supported instructional tasks, teacher guidance, assessment and feedback, judgments of generative artificial intelligence outputs, information comparison, and evidence evaluation. In adapting the items, this study followed the principle of balancing theoretical continuity, contextual appropriateness, and measurement feasibility in scale adaptation51. Specifically, while retaining the core meanings of the original constructs, the item wording was embedded in concrete learning situations",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,4,"In adapting the items, this study followed the principle of balancing theoretical continuity, contextual appropriateness, and measurement feasibility in scale adaptation51. Specifically, while retaining the core meanings of the original constructs, the item wording was embedded in concrete learning situations involving generative artificial intelligence-supported instructional tasks, teacher guidance, assessment and feedback, judgments of generative artificial intelligence outputs, information comparison, and evidence evaluation, so as to enhance the fit between the measurement instrument and the research context. The measurement items are presented in Table 1. Data collection and sample Data were collected through an online questionnaire from respondents with higher education experience. Before completing the questionnaire, respondents answered a screening question asking them to recall whether, while taking a course in higher education (undergraduate level or above), they had used GAI tools to support learning with a teacher’s permission or guidance. Only respondents who passed the screening question proceeded to the main questionnaire. Participants represented a range of disciplinary backgrounds and were classified into STEM and non-STEM groups according to their field of study, supporting the subsequent multigroup SEM analysis. To",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,4,"Only respondents who passed the screening question proceeded to the main questionnaire. Participants represented a range of disciplinary backgrounds and were classified into STEM and non-STEM groups according to their field of study, supporting the subsequent multigroup SEM analysis. To broaden geographical coverage, the questionnaire was distributed nationwide through “Wenjuanxing”, a major online data-collection platform in China, and covered most regions of the country. The geographical distribution of the sample is shown in Fig. 2. Fig. 1. Overall research framework.",80
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,5,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 5 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports Fig. 2. Geographical distribution of the sample. Construct Item Source IBL The tasks designed by the teacher require me to use GAI to explore, compare, or verify problems rather than obtain a standard answer directly. 38,48 After using GAI, the tasks assigned by the teacher require me to raise my own questions, explanations, or judgments. Even when GAI provides an answer, the course task still requires me to explain my own reasoning process and supporting basis. The teacher encourages me to analyze and evaluate different perspectives provided by GAI and infer the algorithmic logic underlying them. TS The teacher guides me to use GAI as a tool that supports thinking rather than as a direct substitute for my own learning or thought. 34,39 When I am confused or uncertain about a result produced by GAI, the teacher guides me in checking and correcting it. The teacher reminds me that GAI outputs may contain errors, biases, or incomplete information. The teacher emphasizes academic integrity when using GAI and warns me about possible",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,5,"result produced by GAI, the teacher guides me in checking and correcting it. The teacher reminds me that GAI outputs may contain errors, biases, or incomplete information. The teacher emphasizes academic integrity when using GAI and warns me about possible academic misconduct. AM Course assessment considers not only the final answer but also my thinking process when using GAI. 34,36 The teacher provides feedback on how I use GAI, such as how I verify, revise, and reflect on its outputs. Course assessment requires me to explain the specific role of GAI in my learning and my own judgment process. The assessment mechanism encourages me to submit the final assignment only after repeatedly revising and refining my ideas with GAI. CC I experience a discrepancy when the content provided by GAI is inconsistent with my existing understanding. 44,46 I experience a conflict when information provided by GAI differs from the textbook or the teacher’s perspective. After using GAI, I realize that relying on GAI alone is insufficient to solve the learning problem. The content provided by GAI sometimes exceeds my current",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,5,"conflict when information provided by GAI differs from the textbook or the teacher’s perspective. After using GAI, I realize that relying on GAI alone is insufficient to solve the learning problem. The content provided by GAI sometimes exceeds my current abilities or level of understanding. CE When a GAI response contains problems, I analyze the reasons further. 49 I compare different perspectives presented by GAI, textbooks, teachers, and other sources. I use evidence, logic, or reasoning to verify the reliability of GAI outputs. I revise and deepen GAI-generated content myself rather than accepting the result directly. HOT When learning with GAI, I evaluate whether the evidence underlying its conclusions is sufficient. 12,52 I can identify possible logical gaps or unreasonable claims in GAI outputs. I remain critical of answers provided by GAI rather than accepting them directly. When GAI provides multiple solutions, I can compare them and make a reasoned choice. I can integrate information based on GAI outputs to develop a more complete solution or perspective. Table 1. Measurement items for the focal constructs.",175
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,5,"multiple solutions, I can compare them and make a reasoned choice. I can integrate information based on GAI outputs to develop a more complete solution or perspective. Table 1. Measurement items for the focal constructs.",35
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,6,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 6 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports After collection, incomplete questionnaires, responses with implausibly short completion times, cases that failed the contextual screening question, and logically inconsistent responses were excluded. Of the 540 returned questionnaires, 514 valid responses were retained for analysis. The survey was anonymous, and all respondents participated voluntarily after providing informed consent. All methods were performed in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and relevant guidelines and regulations. The study was approved by the Research Ethics Committee of Anhui University of Finance and Economics (Approval No. AUFE-2026-0106). Data analysis SPSS 27.0 was used for descriptive statistics, reliability analysis, and tests of common method bias, while AMOS 26.0 was used for confirmatory factor analysis, SEM, and multigroup SEM. Model parameters were estimated using maximum likelihood. Because incomplete and invalid questionnaires were removed before the formal analysis, the final sample of 514 cases contained no missing values for the focal variables, and all subsequent analyses used complete cases. SEM was used to test the paths among instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT, and mediation effects",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,6,"the final sample of 514 cases contained no missing values for the focal variables, and all subsequent analyses used complete cases. SEM was used to test the paths among instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT, and mediation effects were assessed using bootstrapping. Multigroup SEM was then used to compare paths between the STEM and non-STEM groups. Finally, fsQCA was conducted to examine combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms, complement the linear relationships estimated through SEM, and identify multiple configurations associated with HOT. Data analysis and results Sample characteristics and descriptive statistics Sample characteristics The final sample comprised 514 valid responses. Men accounted for 47.7% of the sample and women for 52.3%, indicating a relatively balanced gender distribution. Most respondents were aged 18–40 years, and those aged 26–40 constituted the largest group (57.8%), broadly reflecting the characteristics of higher education learners. More than 70% held at least a bachelor’s degree. The STEM and non-STEM groups were also nearly balanced (50.2% vs. 49.8%), providing an appropriate basis for multigroup analysis. Most respondents reported using GAI tools to varying",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,6,"of higher education learners. More than 70% held at least a bachelor’s degree. The STEM and non-STEM groups were also nearly balanced (50.2% vs. 49.8%), providing an appropriate basis for multigroup analysis. Most respondents reported using GAI tools to varying degrees during learning, and 51.7% reported frequent or high-frequency use, indicating a strong fit between the sample and the GAI-supported teaching context examined. Descriptive statistics The means of all constructs were above the scale midpoint (M = 4.25–4.47), and standard deviations ranged from 1.48 to 1.59. These results indicate individual variation in AI-supported learning experiences and cognitive performance without marked ceiling or floor effects, providing an appropriate basis for SEM. Reliability and validity Reliability and convergent validity were first evaluated for the six latent constructs. Cronbach’s alpha values ranged from 0.862 to 0.940, exceeding the recommended threshold of 0.70 and indicating good internal consistency. Composite reliability (CR) values ranged from 0.862 to 0.940, and average variance extracted (AVE) values ranged from 0.610 to 0.758, exceeding the respective thresholds of 0.70 and 0.50. The constructs therefore demonstrated satisfactory convergent validity (Table",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,6,"indicating good internal consistency. Composite reliability (CR) values ranged from 0.862 to 0.940, and average variance extracted (AVE) values ranged from 0.610 to 0.758, exceeding the respective thresholds of 0.70 and 0.50. The constructs therefore demonstrated satisfactory convergent validity (Table 2). Confirmatory factor analysis indicated a good overall fit for the measurement model: χ²/df = 1.610, RMSEA = 0.034, GFI = 0.938, CFI = 0.980, NFI = 0.949, and TLI = 0.977. All indices met commonly recommended criteria for SEM (Table 3). Discriminant validity was further examined using the Fornell–Larcker criterion. For each construct, the square root of AVE exceeded its correlations with all other constructs, indicating satisfactory discriminant validity (Table 4). Index χ²/df RMSEA GFI CFI NFI TLI Value 1.610 0.034 0.938 0.980 0.949 0.977 Criterion < 5 < 0.08 > 0.90 > 0.90 > 0.90 > 0.90 Table 3. Measurement model fit indices. Construct Items Standardized loading range Cronbach’s α CR AVE IBL 4 0.749–0.799 0.862 0.862 0.610 TS 4 0.792–0.816 0.879 0.879 0.645 AM 4 0.742–0.812 0.865 0.866 0.617 CC 4 0.753–0.808 0.865 0.865 0.615 CE 4",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,6,"3. Measurement model fit indices. Construct Items Standardized loading range Cronbach’s α CR AVE IBL 4 0.749–0.799 0.862 0.862 0.610 TS 4 0.792–0.816 0.879 0.879 0.645 AM 4 0.742–0.812 0.865 0.866 0.617 CC 4 0.753–0.808 0.865 0.865 0.615 CE 4 0.763–0.807 0.864 0.864 0.615 HOT 5 0.860–0.888 0.940 0.940 0.758 Table 2. Reliability and convergent validity of the measurement model. CR, composite reliability; AVE, average variance extracted.",67
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,7,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 7 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports Data quality assessment Because the data were obtained through a self-report questionnaire administered at a single time point, common method bias was a potential concern. To reduce this risk, responses were collected anonymously, and respondents were informed that there were no standard answers and that the data would be used only for academic research, thereby reducing evaluation apprehension and social desirability bias. Harman’s single-factor test was used as an initial statistical assessment. The unrotated exploratory factor analysis extracted six factors with eigenvalues above 1, and the first factor explained 38.148% of the variance, below the 50% criterion. A single factor therefore did not account for most of the common variance, suggesting that common method bias was unlikely to have severely affected the results. Collinearity diagnostics were additionally used as a supplementary data-quality assessment and to examine multicollinearity in the structural model. Variance inflation factors ranged from 1.337 to 1.645, below the commonly used thresholds of 3.3 and 5, while tolerance values ranged from 0.608 to 0.748, above 0.1. These results indicate",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,7,"data-quality assessment and to examine multicollinearity in the structural model. Variance inflation factors ranged from 1.337 to 1.645, below the commonly used thresholds of 3.3 and 5, while tolerance values ranged from 0.608 to 0.748, above 0.1. These results indicate no serious multicollinearity and further suggest that common method bias did not materially interfere with the conclusions. Structural model and hypothesis testing After evaluating the measurement model, SEM was used to examine the structural relationships among instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT. The path estimates are presented in Table 5.Inquiry-based learning (β = 0.164, P = 0.001), teacher support (β = 0.172, P < 0.001), and assessment mechanisms (β = 0.480, P < 0.001) were all positively associated with cognitive conflict, with assessment mechanisms showing the largest coefficient. In GAI-supported teaching, inquiry-oriented tasks, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms may therefore help students recognize discrepancies among GAI outputs, course content, and their own understanding, thereby activating cognitive conflict. H1, H2, and H3 were supported. Inquiry-based learning (β = 0.159, P = 0.003) and teacher support (β = 0.207, P",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,7,"may therefore help students recognize discrepancies among GAI outputs, course content, and their own understanding, thereby activating cognitive conflict. H1, H2, and H3 were supported. Inquiry-based learning (β = 0.159, P = 0.003) and teacher support (β = 0.207, P < 0.001) were positively associated with cognitive elaboration, whereas the direct path from assessment mechanisms to cognitive elaboration was not statistically significant (β = 0.091, P = 0.128). These findings suggest differentiated roles for instructional design elements in cognitive construction: inquiry-based learning and teacher support may be more closely related to deep processing activities such as explanation, comparison, verification, and integration, whereas the relationship of assessment mechanisms with cognitive elaboration may operate primarily through other cognitive paths. H4 and H5 were supported, whereas H6 was not supported. Cognitive conflict was positively associated with cognitive elaboration (β = 0.322, P < 0.001). When students perceived inconsistent information, deficiencies in their understanding, or conflicting perspectives during GAI- supported learning, they were more likely to undertake further analysis, explanation, and integration. H7 was therefore supported. Path B β S.E. C.R. P CC ←",180
