DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,8,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 8 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports Both cognitive elaboration (β = 0.153, P = 0.003) and cognitive conflict (β = 0.239, P < 0.001) were positively associated with HOT, indicating that experiences of cognitive disequilibrium and deep processing during GAI- supported learning were closely related to HOT. H8 and H12 were supported. Inquiry-based learning (β = 0.149, P = 0.002), teacher support (β = 0.127, P = 0.009), and assessment mechanisms (β = 0.195, P < 0.001) were also positively associated with HOT. Thus, instructional design elements were related to HOT both through cognitive processes and through direct paths. H9, H10, and H11 were supported (Fig. 3). Mediation analysis Bootstrapping was used to estimate indirect relationships between instructional design and HOT, with significance determined by whether the 95% confidence interval included zero. As shown in Table 6, the total effects of inquiry-based learning, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms on HOT were all significantly positive. The direct and total indirect effects for all three paths were also significant, indicating that instructional design elements were both directly and indirectly",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,8,"the total effects of inquiry-based learning, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms on HOT were all significantly positive. The direct and total indirect effects for all three paths were also significant, indicating that instructional design elements were both directly and indirectly associated with HOT through cognitive processes. All three relationships therefore exhibited partial mediation. The serial indirect path from instructional design through cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration to HOT was significant for all three instructional design elements, supporting a sequential mediating role for cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration. Assessment mechanisms showed the largest total and total indirect effects, suggesting that assessment and feedback design may be particularly likely to relate to HOT through the cognitive mediation chain in GAI-supported teaching. The direct effects remained significant after the mediators were included, however, indicating that HOT was not associated solely with a single mediation chain and may also reflect task form, interaction structure, learning support, and other factors. Multigroup analysis across disciplines To assess the applicability of the structural model across disciplinary contexts, the sample was divided by field into a STEM group",180
