DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,9,"were examined using the critical ratios for differences reported by AMOS (Table 8). Only the path from inquiry-based learning to cognitive conflict differed significantly between the groups (|z| > 1.96). The path was not significant in the STEM group (β = 0.009, P = 0.902) but was significant in the non- STEM group (β = 0.266, P < 0.001). The relationship between inquiry-based learning and cognitive conflict may therefore vary by disciplinary context and appears stronger in non-STEM fields. No other between-group path difference was statistically significant, indicating that the overall structural relationships among instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT were broadly stable across STEM and non-STEM groups. The proposed cognitive construction pathways thus demonstrated structural stability across disciplines, although the role of an individual instructional design element may still depend on disciplinary type. Configurational pathway analysis using fsQCA Calibration and necessary-condition analysis Configurational analysis was conducted using fsQCA 4.1. Based on the research framework, inquiry-based learning, teacher support, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration were selected as antecedent conditions, with HOT specified as the outcome. All",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,9,"fsQCA Calibration and necessary-condition analysis Configurational analysis was conducted using fsQCA 4.1. Based on the research framework, inquiry-based learning, teacher support, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration were selected as antecedent conditions, with HOT specified as the outcome. All variables were calculated using the means of their corresponding items to ensure that the SEM and fsQCA analyses were based on the same variable measures. During variable calibration, this study adopted the direct calibration method53, setting the 90th, 50th, and 10th percentiles of each variable as the thresholds for full membership, the crossover point, and full non- membership, respectively. Percentile-based calibration anchors were used mainly because all variables in this study were measured using a seven-point Likert scale, and existing literature does not provide external theoretical thresholds that can directly distinguish high or low levels of instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, or HOT. Under these circumstances, using the 90th, 50th, and 10th percentiles of the sample distribution makes it possible to distinguish relatively clear membership, maximum ambiguity, and relatively clear non-membership on the basis of empirical data. Specifically, the",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,9,"cognitive elaboration, or HOT. Under these circumstances, using the 90th, 50th, and 10th percentiles of the sample distribution makes it possible to distinguish relatively clear membership, maximum ambiguity, and relatively clear non-membership on the basis of empirical data. Specifically, the 90th percentile indicates that a case is relatively clearly within the corresponding condition set, the 10th percentile indicates that a case is relatively clearly outside the corresponding condition set, and the 50th percentile serves as the crossover point, representing the point of maximum ambiguity between membership and non-membership. This approach avoids mechanically equating scale endpoints with full membership or full non-membership and improves the fit between the calibration procedure and the data characteristics of this study. The specific calibration anchors Path STEM Non-STEM z β B S.E. P β B S.E. P CC ← IBL 0.009 0.008 0.066 0.902 0.266 0.239 0.066 < 0.001 2.479 CC ← TS 0.220 0.185 0.065 0.005 0.095 0.090 0.065 0.162 -1.028 CC ← AM 0.487 0.450 0.077 < 0.001 0.407 0.429 0.081 < 0.001 -0.187 CE ← IBL 0.165 0.162 0.074 0.029 0.142 0.141",180
