DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,10,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 10 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports are presented in Table 9. To avoid calibrated membership scores of exactly 0.5 affecting subsequent Boolean operations, 0.001 was added to such cases. After completing the calibration, this study first conducted a necessary-condition analysis to determine whether any single condition was necessary for high HOT. The results are presented in Table 10. The consistency values of all antecedent conditions and their absence were below the 0.90 threshold for necessary conditions54. Cognitive conflict showed the highest consistency value (0.796), followed by cognitive elaboration (0.793) and assessment mechanisms (0.774), but none reached the necessary-condition threshold. This indicates that neither the presence nor the absence of any single condition was sufficient to constitute a necessary condition for high HOT. It was therefore necessary to further examine the configurational pathways formed by combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms. Truth-table construction and configurational analysis Based on the principles of Boolean algebra55 and recommendations from previous fsQCA research56, this study specified the parameters for truth-table construction. Because five antecedent conditions were included, 32 condition combinations",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,10,"design elements and cognitive mechanisms. Truth-table construction and configurational analysis Based on the principles of Boolean algebra55 and recommendations from previous fsQCA research56, this study specified the parameters for truth-table construction. Because five antecedent conditions were included, 32 condition combinations were theoretically possible. To ensure the stability of the configurational results and reduce the influence of low-frequency combinations, the case-frequency threshold was set to 3. This setting was mainly based on the sample size and the number of conditions in this study. With 514 valid cases, a frequency threshold of 3 could exclude very rare or incidental combinations from affecting the results while retaining sufficient empirical diversity for subsequent configurational interpretation. Meanwhile, the raw consistency threshold was set to 0.80. This threshold is commonly used in fsQCA studies to assess whether a condition combination has sufficient relevance to an outcome, and it helps identify combinations that have a relatively stable set-theoretic relationship with high HOT. To further reduce the possibility that a condition combination simultaneously points to both the presence and absence of the outcome, PRI consistency was also used",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,10,"and it helps identify combinations that have a relatively stable set-theoretic relationship with high HOT. To further reduce the possibility that a condition combination simultaneously points to both the presence and absence of the outcome, PRI consistency was also used as a supplementary criterion for evaluating the truth-table results. On this basis, the intermediate solution was selected as the main basis for analysis, and interpretation was conducted by considering the presence or absence of each condition across different pathways57. To examine whether the configurational results depended on specific threshold settings, this study further conducted robustness checks by increasing the raw consistency threshold from 0.80 to 0.82 and increasing the case- frequency threshold from 3 to 4. Under the alternative threshold settings, the main condition combinations were broadly consistent with the main model results, indicating that the configurational pathways associated with high HOT identified in this study were reasonably robust. Four configurational pathways associated with high HOT were identified (Table 11). The consistency of each pathway exceeded 0.80, indicating that these condition combinations had satisfactory sufficiency. The overall solution coverage was",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,10,"high HOT identified in this study were reasonably robust. Four configurational pathways associated with high HOT were identified (Table 11). The consistency of each pathway exceeded 0.80, indicating that these condition combinations had satisfactory sufficiency. The overall solution coverage was 0.521, and the overall solution consistency was 0.850, suggesting that the model explained a certain proportion of high-HOT cases and that the overall configurational solution showed satisfactory sufficiency consistency. Condition Consistency Coverage IBL 0.714 0.703 ~IBL 0.548 0.530 TS 0.745 0.733 ~TS 0.545 0.527 AM 0.774 0.741 ~AM 0.524 0.521 CC 0.796 0.733 ~CC 0.495 0.513 CE 0.793 0.729 ~CE 0.524 0.544 Table 10. Necessary-condition analysis for high HOT. The tilde (~) indicates the absence of a condition. Consistency below 0.90 indicates that the condition is not necessary for high HOT. Variable Full non-membership Crossover Full membership IBL 2.88 4.25 5.71 TS 2.50 4.25 6.75 AM 2.00 4.25 6.50 CC 2.08 4.00 6.50 CE 2.00 4.00 6.75 HOT 2.40 4.40 7.00 Table 9. Calibration anchors for the fsQCA variables. Full non-membership, crossover, and full membership correspond to the 10th, 50th,",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,10,"5.71 TS 2.50 4.25 6.75 AM 2.00 4.25 6.50 CC 2.08 4.00 6.50 CE 2.00 4.00 6.75 HOT 2.40 4.40 7.00 Table 9. Calibration anchors for the fsQCA variables. Full non-membership, crossover, and full membership correspond to the 10th, 50th, and 90th percentiles, respectively.",44
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,11,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 11 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports Configurational results Path 1 reflects a cognitive conflict activation logic. In this path, inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms are present, teacher support is not a key condition, and cognitive conflict is associated with high HOT performance. This indicates that when course tasks have a strong inquiry-oriented nature and assessment mechanisms require students to explain their thinking processes and judgment criteria, students are more likely to notice inconsistencies between GAI outputs and their own understanding, course content, or other information sources. In this situation, cognitive conflict is not merely a source of confusion or an obstacle in learning; rather, it may become a trigger that encourages students to re-examine generated content, raise questions, and engage in reflection. In other words, inquiry tasks and assessment requirements can themselves form a strong external structure, enabling students to critically examine GAI outputs even when teacher support is relatively limited. Path 2 highlights a cognitive elaboration–oriented logic. Unlike Path 1, this path does not treat cognitive conflict as a key condition. Instead, when inquiry-based learning and",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,11,"enabling students to critically examine GAI outputs even when teacher support is relatively limited. Path 2 highlights a cognitive elaboration–oriented logic. Unlike Path 1, this path does not treat cognitive conflict as a key condition. Instead, when inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms are both present, cognitive elaboration is associated with high HOT performance. This suggests that GAI-supported learning does not always need to begin with pronounced cognitive disequilibrium. As long as task design and assessment requirements continue to encourage students to explain, compare, verify, and integrate GAI outputs, students may still engage in deeper cognitive processing. For example, students may compare differences between GAI- generated solutions and course materials under task requirements, or explain the logic and evidence they used to verify GAI outputs according to assessment criteria. These processes are closely related to HOT performance. Path 3 can be understood as a complete cognitive construction logic under instructional guidance. This path includes inquiry-based learning, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration, and it also has the highest raw coverage among the four pathways, indicating that it explains a relatively",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,11,"understood as a complete cognitive construction logic under instructional guidance. This path includes inquiry-based learning, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration, and it also has the highest raw coverage among the four pathways, indicating that it explains a relatively large proportion of cases in the sample. Compared with the first two pathways, Path 3 presents a more complete cognitive process chain. Inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms first provide an external instructional framework for students. Differences between GAI outputs and students’ existing understanding, course content, or other materials then trigger cognitive conflict. Students subsequently engage in cognitive elaboration through questioning, comparison, verification, and revision. This path corresponds to the serial mediation logic tested in SEM, suggesting that the combination of instructional design, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration represents one important association pattern related to high HOT performance. Path 4 reflects the compensatory role of teacher support. Unlike the first three pathways, this path appears in contexts where inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms are relatively weak. Under such conditions, the combination of teacher support, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration may still",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,11,"the compensatory role of teacher support. Unlike the first three pathways, this path appears in contexts where inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms are relatively weak. Under such conditions, the combination of teacher support, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration may still be associated with high HOT performance. This suggests that when a course lacks sufficient inquiry-oriented task design or a process-oriented assessment framework, direct teacher guidance may become an important condition for sustaining deep learning. For example, teachers can help students identify factual errors in GAI outputs, suggest appropriate verification methods, encourage students to revise their judgments, and provide feedback. Through such guidance, students may be more likely to transform the uncertainty in GAI outputs into cognitive conflict and further engage in deep processing, such as analysis, verification, and revision. Therefore, teacher support is not a key condition in all pathways, but it may have a more evident compensatory function when instructional design conditions are not sufficiently developed. Discussion, conclusions, and implications Discussion Instructional design and higher-order thinking Based on the path analysis results from SEM, this study found that inquiry-based",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,11,"but it may have a more evident compensatory function when instructional design conditions are not sufficiently developed. Discussion, conclusions, and implications Discussion Instructional design and higher-order thinking Based on the path analysis results from SEM, this study found that inquiry-based learning, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms were all significantly positively associated with students’ HOT. This result indicates that, in the teaching experiences reported by the sample, instructional design is an important dimension for understanding differences in students’ HOT. The data did not show that HOT performance had a direct or automatic strong association with students’ experience of using GAI technology itself. Rather, it was more likely Condition Path 1 Path 2 Path 3 Path 4 IBL ● ● ● ⊗ TS ⊗ ⊗ ● AM ● ● ● ⊗ CC ● ● ● CE ● ● ● Consistency 0.855 0.830 0.880 0.850 Raw coverage 0.215 0.200 0.398 0.149 Unique coverage 0.038 0.023 0.210 0.062 Overall coverage 0.521 Overall consistency 0.850 Table 11. Multiple configurations associated with high HOT. ● indicates the presence of a condition; ⊗ indicates its absence; a",180
