DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 12 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports to be related to students’ perceptions of whether the technology was embedded in an instructional structure with explicit cognitive requirements, process guidance, and assessment constraints. Assessment mechanisms played a particularly prominent role in the model. Their standardized coefficient for cognitive conflict was the largest (β = 0.480), substantially exceeding those of inquiry-based learning and teacher support. Assessment mechanisms also had the largest total effect on HOT (total effect = 0.435). In practical terms, process-oriented assessment and feedback may be more likely than task assignment alone or general teacher support to focus students’ attention on the reasonableness, evidential basis, and limitations of GAI outputs. When assessment does not focus only on a final answer but requires students to document how they used GAI, explain their verification criteria, justify their revisions, and present their own judgments, students may be more likely to shift from directly accepting GAI outputs to examining, comparing, and revising them. Such practices are, in turn, associated with stronger HOT. Although inquiry-based learning and teacher support were also positively associated",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"their own judgments, students may be more likely to shift from directly accepting GAI outputs to examining, comparing, and revising them. Such practices are, in turn, associated with stronger HOT. Although inquiry-based learning and teacher support were also positively associated with HOT, their coefficients were more modest. This does not imply that they are unimportant; rather, they may operate primarily by creating open learning contexts, providing cognitive support, and sustaining participation. Inquiry- oriented tasks create space for exploring problems and comparing perspectives, while teacher support may help students avoid blind reliance and manage cognitive demands when facing uncertain information. Together with assessment mechanisms, these elements create instructional conditions that make it more likely that students will use GAI as a cognitive resource rather than merely as an answer-generation tool. Therefore, the findings are consistent with previous discussions of differentiated educational effects of GAI. In the teaching experiences reported by students, the use of GAI itself did not necessarily correspond to higher HOT scores. Its association with students’ HOT was more likely to depend on how specific instructional designs organized students’",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"effects of GAI. In the teaching experiences reported by students, the use of GAI itself did not necessarily correspond to higher HOT scores. Its association with students’ HOT was more likely to depend on how specific instructional designs organized students’ interactions with GAI outputs. In contexts lacking explicit instructional structure and process guidance, GAI may reduce learners’ cognitive engagement because of its efficiency in generating information, leading students toward outcome-oriented, substitutive use. By contrast, when GAI is embedded in inquiry-oriented learning tasks and process-oriented instructional arrangements, and when its use is regulated and constrained through assessment mechanisms, its function is more likely to shift from an “answer provider” to a “cognitive support tool,” thereby forming a closer association with higher-order cognitive activities such as analysis, evaluation, and reflection. Cognitive construction of higher-order thinking The SEM results showed that both cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration were positively associated with HOT and that cognitive conflict was positively associated with cognitive elaboration. These findings are consistent with the constructivist view that HOT is not directly transmitted through content or technology but is more",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"conflict and cognitive elaboration were positively associated with HOT and that cognitive conflict was positively associated with cognitive elaboration. These findings are consistent with the constructivist view that HOT is not directly transmitted through content or technology but is more likely to form gradually as learners confront informational differences, deficiencies in understanding, or conflicting perspectives and engage in continuing explanation, comparison, verification, and integration. In GAI-supported learning, students encounter information from course materials, teachers, peers, and GAI outputs. Inconsistencies among these sources may reveal the limits of existing understanding, produce cognitive conflict, and prompt deeper processing. The relationship between cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration was comparatively strong (β = 0.322), indicating that students who perceived discrepancies between GAI outputs and their existing understanding, course content, or other sources were more likely to analyze, explain, and verify the information. The direct coefficient from cognitive conflict to HOT (β = 0.239) was also larger than that from cognitive elaboration to HOT (β = 0.153). Cognitive conflict may therefore be associated not only with the initiation of cognitive elaboration but also more directly",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"coefficient from cognitive conflict to HOT (β = 0.239) was also larger than that from cognitive elaboration to HOT (β = 0.153). Cognitive conflict may therefore be associated not only with the initiation of cognitive elaboration but also more directly with problem awareness, critical judgment, and reflective thinking. Moderate informational uncertainty and differences in perspectives may themselves serve as important triggers of higher- order cognitive activity in GAI-supported teaching. The mediation analysis further indicated that inquiry-based learning, teacher support, and assessment mechanisms were indirectly associated with HOT through the serial path from cognitive conflict to cognitive elaboration. Although these serial mediation effects were statistically significant, their magnitudes were small. The cognitive conflict–cognitive elaboration–HOT sequence should therefore be understood as an identifiable cognitive channel rather than as the only or dominant mechanism. Significant direct and other indirect effects remained, suggesting that the development of HOT may jointly reflect task structure, assessment requirements, teacher guidance, learning norms, and students’ own cognitive engagement. This interpretation provides a more detailed account of cognition in GAI-supported teaching. The educational value of GAI does not lie",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"the development of HOT may jointly reflect task structure, assessment requirements, teacher guidance, learning norms, and students’ own cognitive engagement. This interpretation provides a more detailed account of cognition in GAI-supported teaching. The educational value of GAI does not lie in directly providing more complete or higher-quality answers. Rather, the uncertainty, diversity, and comparability of its outputs create opportunities for students to reconsider a problem. When instructional design encourages students to question the basis of an output, compare differences, identify limitations, and make revisions instead of simply accepting the response, cognitive conflict is more likely to develop into deep processing and to show a stable relationship with HOT. HOT is thus better understood as a process jointly involving external instructional design, GAI-generated content, and students’ cognitive processing than as the direct consequence of a single path or variable. Differences across disciplinary contexts The multigroup SEM results indicated that the overall path structure linking instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT was broadly consistent across STEM and non-STEM contexts. With the exception of the path from inquiry-based learning to cognitive",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,12,"disciplinary contexts The multigroup SEM results indicated that the overall path structure linking instructional design, cognitive conflict, cognitive elaboration, and HOT was broadly consistent across STEM and non-STEM contexts. With the exception of the path from inquiry-based learning to cognitive conflict, no between-group path difference was statistically significant. The proposed cognitive construction mechanism therefore does not appear to be specific to a single discipline and demonstrates a degree of structural stability in GAI-supported higher education. Although disciplines differ in how knowledge is organized and in their task types and assessment logics, the",92
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 13 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports basic structure through which instructional design is associated with HOT via cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration was relatively stable. The inquiry-based learning–cognitive conflict path nevertheless differed significantly across the two groups. It was not significant in the STEM group but was significant in the non-STEM group, indicating that the relationship between inquiry-based learning and cognitive conflict was more evident in non-STEM contexts. How inquiry-oriented teaching triggers cognitive conflict may therefore be partly discipline dependent. In non-STEM fields, which place greater emphasis on interpretation, argumentation, multiple perspectives, and open-ended discussion, inquiry tasks may more readily expose students to inconsistencies among viewpoints, interpretive frameworks, and GAI outputs, making tensions between existing understanding and new information more salient. STEM tasks more often emphasize rules, procedures, derivations, or verification. Students may consequently use GAI mainly for problem solving, computation, or answer checking, and the relationship between inquiry-oriented teaching and cognitive conflict was not evident in the present STEM sample. Therefore, the findings do not support a broad moderating role of disciplinary context in the overall",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"for problem solving, computation, or answer checking, and the relationship between inquiry-oriented teaching and cognitive conflict was not evident in the present STEM sample. Therefore, the findings do not support a broad moderating role of disciplinary context in the overall structural model. Instead, disciplinary differences were mainly reflected in one individual path. In other words, in GAI-supported teaching, the relational structure among instructional design, cognitive processes, and HOT performance was generally stable across disciplines, although the role of a specific instructional design element may still be influenced by the characteristics of disciplinary tasks. For non-STEM disciplines, inquiry-based learning may be more suitable for activating students’ cognitive conflict through comparisons among multiple perspectives, argumentative tensions, and the reconstruction of interpretive frameworks. For STEM disciplines, instructional design may need to more deliberately incorporate error diagnosis, model comparison, hypothesis testing, or reflection on solution strategies, so that inquiry tasks can be more effectively transformed into opportunities for cognitive conflict and deeper thinking. Multiple pathways associated with higher-order thinking Unlike SEM, which emphasizes average effects among variables, fsQCA further revealed the conditional combinations associated",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"solution strategies, so that inquiry tasks can be more effectively transformed into opportunities for cognitive conflict and deeper thinking. Multiple pathways associated with higher-order thinking Unlike SEM, which emphasizes average effects among variables, fsQCA further revealed the conditional combinations associated with high HOT. The configurational analysis showed that high HOT performance did not correspond to any single condition or unique optimal pathway, but was associated with different combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms. This result suggests that, in GAI-supported teaching contexts, the formation of HOT is more likely to have a multiple-pathway structure. Specifically, Path 1 showed that when inquiry-based learning, assessment mechanisms, and cognitive conflict were simultaneously present, this combination was still associated with high HOT performance even though teacher support was not a key condition. This result corresponds to the cognitive conflict activation logic, suggesting that in courses with inquiry requirements and process-oriented assessment constraints, students may be more likely to develop problem awareness and critical examination by noticing inconsistencies between GAI outputs and their own understanding. Path 2 reflected a cognitive elaboration–oriented logic. When inquiry-",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"in courses with inquiry requirements and process-oriented assessment constraints, students may be more likely to develop problem awareness and critical examination by noticing inconsistencies between GAI outputs and their own understanding. Path 2 reflected a cognitive elaboration–oriented logic. When inquiry- based learning and assessment mechanisms were both present, students could still be associated with high HOT performance through continuous explanation, comparison, verification, and integration of GAI outputs, even when cognitive conflict was not a key condition. Path 3 included inquiry-based learning, assessment mechanisms, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration, and had the highest raw coverage, indicating that the complete cognitive construction chain had relatively strong explanatory power in the sample and corresponded to the serial mediation results in SEM. Path 4 showed that when inquiry-based learning and assessment mechanisms were relatively insufficient, the combination of teacher support, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration could still correspond to high HOT performance, suggesting that teacher guidance may play a compensatory role when the instructional structure is insufficient. From the relationship between SEM and fsQCA, the two methods provided complementary rather than repetitive evidence. The",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"elaboration could still correspond to high HOT performance, suggesting that teacher guidance may play a compensatory role when the instructional structure is insufficient. From the relationship between SEM and fsQCA, the two methods provided complementary rather than repetitive evidence. The SEM results showed that assessment mechanisms had relatively strong average effects across multiple paths, especially with a high path coefficient for cognitive conflict. fsQCA further showed that assessment mechanisms also appeared in multiple configurational pathways associated with high HOT, indicating that assessment and feedback design was not only prominent in the linear model but may also serve as an important instructional condition in different combinations of conditions. At the same time, fsQCA further revealed the conditional role of teacher support: it was not a key condition in all pathways, but when inquiry- based learning and assessment mechanisms were insufficient, it could combine with cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration to form an alternative pathway. This finding suggests that teaching practice does not need to pursue a single fixed model. Instead, teachers can flexibly combine inquiry tasks, process-oriented assessment, teacher support, and",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"could combine with cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration to form an alternative pathway. This finding suggests that teaching practice does not need to pursue a single fixed model. Instead, teachers can flexibly combine inquiry tasks, process-oriented assessment, teacher support, and other instructional elements according to course objectives, disciplinary characteristics, and students’ cognitive states. Conclusions This study shows that, when GAI is widely integrated into higher education teaching, HOT should not be understood as a learning outcome that naturally arises from technology use. Rather, it should be viewed as a cognitive outcome that gradually develops within specific learning activities under the guidance of instructional design. Based on a sample of students with GAI-supported learning experiences, this study found that differences in HOT performance cannot be fully explained by technology use itself, but are more likely to be associated with the instructional design elements, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration processes experienced by students. Therefore, the key to HOT performance is not only whether a technological tool is available, but more importantly whether instructional design can guide learning activities away from the direct",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,13,"design elements, cognitive conflict, and cognitive elaboration processes experienced by students. Therefore, the key to HOT performance is not only whether a technological tool is available, but more importantly whether instructional design can guide learning activities away from the direct acquisition of generated content and toward active examination and judgment of its reasonableness, evidential basis, and appropriateness.",57
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 14 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports This study further shows that the formation of HOT has clear processual and cumulative characteristics. It is not directly determined by a single instructional element or an isolated cognitive variable, but gradually develops through continuing interactions among instructional tasks, teacher support, assessment mechanisms, and students’ cognitive processing. Cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration provide important perspectives for understanding this process, but their roles should not be understood as the only channel. Instead, they should be situated within a more complex teaching context and learning process. In addition, this study found that the formation of HOT in GAI-supported teaching showed a degree of structural stability, while also displaying limited disciplinary differences. Although disciplines differ in knowledge organization, task forms, and assessment logics, the basic logic by which instructional design is associated with HOT through cognitive processes did not fundamentally change. The configurational analysis further showed that the formation of HOT did not depend on a single pathway, but could be associated with different combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms, demonstrating multiple",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"through cognitive processes did not fundamentally change. The configurational analysis further showed that the formation of HOT did not depend on a single pathway, but could be associated with different combinations of instructional design elements and cognitive mechanisms, demonstrating multiple pathways, complementarity, and conditional configurations. Overall, this study advances the relationship between GAI and HOT from a question of technological effects alone to one concerning the joint roles of instructional design, cognitive processes, and learning contexts, thereby revising linear and static interpretations of the mechanisms through which HOT is formed. Practical implications Higher education should move beyond the managerial question of whether students are permitted to use GAI and consider how it should be embedded in course objectives, learning tasks, and assessment mechanisms. Teachers should not simply require or prohibit GAI use; instructional design should specify how students use the technology, make judgments, revise outputs, and explain the process. Only when GAI is integrated into explicit learning tasks and cognitive requirements is it likely to shift from an answer-generation tool to a cognitive resource for analysis, comparison, judgment, and reflection.",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"technology, make judgments, revise outputs, and explain the process. Only when GAI is integrated into explicit learning tasks and cognitive requirements is it likely to shift from an answer-generation tool to a cognitive resource for analysis, comparison, judgment, and reflection. First, course assessment should shift from outcome evaluation toward process evaluation. When criteria focus only on a final answer, product, or text, students may use GAI primarily to reduce the cognitive cost of completing the task. Instead, assignments, reports, and project assessments can require students to document the prompts they used, identify which parts of a GAI output they accepted or rejected, explain how they verified its reliability and revised it using course knowledge, and justify their final judgment. Such mechanisms redirect attention from obtaining an answer to explaining why it is valid and can encourage evidence evaluation, logical analysis, and reflective learning. Second, learning tasks should not be closed activities that GAI can complete directly. Open-ended questions, contextualized cases, comparative analyses, and argumentative tasks can encourage students to question, verify, and reconstruct GAI-generated content. Rather than merely submitting a",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"and reflective learning. Second, learning tasks should not be closed activities that GAI can complete directly. Open-ended questions, contextualized cases, comparative analyses, and argumentative tasks can encourage students to question, verify, and reconstruct GAI-generated content. Rather than merely submitting a GAI-assisted report, students can be required to compare a GAI-generated proposal with textbook perspectives, classroom content, or real cases and explain the reasons for their final revisions. GAI output then becomes a starting point for further analysis and deeper understanding rather than the endpoint of the learning task. Third, teacher support should guide judgment rather than replace it. Teachers should do more than provide instructions for using GAI or tell students whether an output is correct. They should help students develop criteria for judgment, identify factual errors, logical gaps, insufficient arguments, biased expressions, and limits of applicability, and use course knowledge, external sources, and personal reasoning for verification. When students encounter uncertain, contradictory, or difficult-to-evaluate information, teacher support can help transform confusion into a question for further thought rather than encouraging dependence on a conclusion supplied by GAI or the",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"knowledge, external sources, and personal reasoning for verification. When students encounter uncertain, contradictory, or difficult-to-evaluate information, teacher support can help transform confusion into a question for further thought rather than encouraging dependence on a conclusion supplied by GAI or the teacher. Instructional design need not follow a single fixed model but can flexibly combine elements according to course conditions. In courses with well-developed task structures and assessment mechanisms, inquiry tasks and process assessment can be used to guide comparison, verification, and reflection on GAI outputs. When course structure is looser or assessment requirements are limited, teacher support can perform a stronger compensatory role through classroom discussion, formative feedback, and revision guidance, helping students maintain a critical approach to GAI outputs. There is no single optimal model for GAI-supported teaching; the important issue is whether instructional elements form a mutually supportive combination. Finally, disciplinary differences should be reflected in specific instructional designs. In non-STEM fields, open questions, comparisons among perspectives, textual interpretation, value judgments, and argumentative conflict can help students identify differences between GAI outputs and alternative viewpoints or interpretive frameworks.",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"combination. Finally, disciplinary differences should be reflected in specific instructional designs. In non-STEM fields, open questions, comparisons among perspectives, textual interpretation, value judgments, and argumentative conflict can help students identify differences between GAI outputs and alternative viewpoints or interpretive frameworks. In STEM fields, error diagnosis, model comparison, hypothesis testing, explanations of solution pathways, and result verification can encourage students to move beyond using GAI to obtain an answer and instead analyze the reasoning process, evaluate a solution strategy, and explain the conditions under which the result holds. The purpose of GAI-supported teaching should not be to increase the efficiency of answer generation but to use course tasks, assessment rules, and teacher guidance to require continuing examination, comparison, verification, and reconstruction of GAI outputs. Limitations and future research This study was based on cross-sectional survey data and examined the relationships among students’ perceptions of instructional design elements, cognitive processes, and HOT performance among students with GAI- supported learning experiences. Although the research model specified an analytical path from instructional design to cognitive processes and then to HOT performance on the basis",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,14,"students’ perceptions of instructional design elements, cognitive processes, and HOT performance among students with GAI- supported learning experiences. Although the research model specified an analytical path from instructional design to cognitive processes and then to HOT performance on the basis of theoretical assumptions, cross- sectional data cannot strictly determine the temporal order among variables. Therefore, the possibility of reverse relationships or reciprocal influence cannot be completely excluded. At the same time, this study did not include a non-GAI-supported teaching context as a control group, nor did it experimentally manipulate whether GAI",91
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,15,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:30855 15 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1 www.nature.com/scientificreports was used. Therefore, the results should not be interpreted as causal evidence that GAI technology itself promotes or inhibits students’ HOT. A more cautious interpretation is that, among students who reported having used GAI to support learning with teacher permission or guidance, their perceptions of instructional design elements may be systematically associated with their self-reported HOT performance through cognitive conflict and cognitive elaboration. Second, this study mainly used self-report questionnaire data. Although anonymous administration, Harman’s single-factor test, and collinearity diagnostics were used to reduce and assess the influence of common method bias, potential bias arising from self-report and common-source data cannot be completely ruled out. In addition, the sample was drawn from the Chinese higher education context, and the findings may be influenced by course organization, teacher guidance practices, academic norm requirements, and institutional policies regarding GAI use in Chinese universities. Therefore, caution is needed when generalizing the findings to other countries, educational systems, or teaching cultures. In addition, respondents were asked to retrospectively answer the questionnaire based on course experiences",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,15,"requirements, and institutional policies regarding GAI use in Chinese universities. Therefore, caution is needed when generalizing the findings to other countries, educational systems, or teaching cultures. In addition, respondents were asked to retrospectively answer the questionnaire based on course experiences in which they had used GAI to support learning with teacher permission or guidance during higher education. Because some respondents were concentrated in the 26–40 age range and the sample included a relatively large proportion of highly educated participants, their responses may have been affected by recall bias, differences in learning stage, and variation in specific course contexts. Future research could collect real-time learning data during actual course implementation or conduct longitudinal tracking of currently enrolled students to improve the contextual precision of interpretation. Finally, the fsQCA analysis in this study mainly focused on pathways associated with high HOT and did not further analyze condition combinations associated with low HOT. Because configurational analysis emphasizes outcome asymmetry, the pathways associated with high HOT are not necessarily equivalent to the reverse pathways associated with low HOT. Future studies could further compare the",180
DOC_037,NEW20.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-66335-1,15,"did not further analyze condition combinations associated with low HOT. Because configurational analysis emphasizes outcome asymmetry, the pathways associated with high HOT are not necessarily equivalent to the reverse pathways associated with low HOT. Future studies could further compare the configurational differences between high and low HOT to provide a more complete explanation of the mechanisms underlying different learning outcomes in GAI-supported teaching. Although this study conducted robustness checks for the fsQCA thresholds, future research could incorporate additional alternative calibration criteria and case-sensitivity analyses to strengthen the stability of configurational conclusions. Data availability The datasets generated and/or analyzed during the current study are not publicly available due to participant privacy and institutional regulations, but are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Received: 2 Ma",126
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,1,"EDITED BY Constantin Aurelian Ionescu, Valahia University of Târgoviște, Romania REVIEWED BY Qian Bao, Hanyang University, Republic of Korea Jesús Catherine Saldaña Bocanegra, Universidad César Vallejo, Peru *CORRESPONDENCE Kai Cao caokai19910101@gmail.com RECEIVED 19 March 2026 REVISED 19 April 2026 ACCEPTED 23 April 2026 PUBLISHED 25 May 2026 CITATION Fu G and Cao K (2026) A cross-cultural study on the adoption intention of generative artiﬁcial intelligence among international students in China. Front. Educ. 11:1833616. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Fu and Cao. This is an open- access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. A cross-cultural study on the adoption intention of generative artiﬁcial intelligence among international students in China Guijun Fu 1,2 and Kai Cao 3* 1School of Education, Faculty of Education, Qufu Normal",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,1,"is permitted which does not comply with these terms. A cross-cultural study on the adoption intention of generative artiﬁcial intelligence among international students in China Guijun Fu 1,2 and Kai Cao 3* 1School of Education, Faculty of Education, Qufu Normal University, Qufu, China, 2School of Geography and Tourism, Qilu Normal University, Jinan, China, 3Library of Qinghai University, Qinghai University, Xining, China Against the backdrop of the deep integration of higher education internationalization and generative artiﬁcial intelligence (GAI), international students in China, as a distinctive cross-cultural group, have not been sufﬁciently explored regarding the internal mechanisms underlying their GAI adoption behavior. This study integrates the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and the Uniﬁed Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT2), innovatively introduces the GAI-speciﬁc construct “perceived intelligence”, and takes disciplinary background and cultural values (individualism/collectivism) as moderating variables to construct an integrated adoption mechanism model of “external drivers – cognitive/affective evaluation – behavioral intention”. Using stratiﬁed multi-stage sampling, 219 international students from 12 universities in eastern, central, and western China were selected as research samples, and partial least squares structural equation",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,1,"integrated adoption mechanism model of “external drivers – cognitive/affective evaluation – behavioral intention”. Using stratiﬁed multi-stage sampling, 219 international students from 12 universities in eastern, central, and western China were selected as research samples, and partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) was employed for empirical testing. The results show that performance expectancy and perceived intelligence have signiﬁcant positive effects on perceived usefulness; effort expectancy and facilitating conditions have signiﬁcant positive effects on perceived ease of use; subjective norms and perceived behavioral control have signiﬁcant positive effects on attitude; perceived usefulness and attitude signiﬁcantly and positively drive GAI adoption intention. Perceived usefulness plays a fully mediating role between performance expectancy, perceived intelligence and adoption intention, while attitude plays a fully mediating role between subjective norms, perceived behavioral control and adoption intention. Perceived ease of use has neither a direct impact on adoption intention nor a mediating role. Moderation effect analysis reveals that disciplinary background only has a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderation effect on the “perceived ease of use→adoption intention” path, and cultural values only have a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderation",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,1,"on adoption intention nor a mediating role. Moderation effect analysis reveals that disciplinary background only has a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderation effect on the “perceived ease of use→adoption intention” path, and cultural values only have a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderation effect on the “attitude→adoption intention” path. This study enriches the theoretical system of emerging technology acceptance in cross-cultural educational contexts, provides empirical support for the contextual expansion of classic technology acceptance models, and offers practical references for universities to optimize GAI application strategies in international student education and promote the coordinated development of artiﬁcial intelligence and higher education internationalization. KEYWORDS adoption intention, cross-culture, generative artiﬁcial intelligence, international students, PLS-SEM, TAM-UTAUT TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 25 May 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",124
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"1 Introduction GAI is an intelligent technological paradigm with autonomous content generation (Pescapè, 2024), in-depth semantic analysis (Liang et al., 2024), and cross-modal interaction capabilities (Stappen et al., 2023). Its core characteristic is the ability to generate logically consistent, standardized, and functional text, data, and multimedia content in response to user instructions (Sengar et al., 2025). This technology is profoundly reshaping the teaching ecology and knowledge production paradigm of global higher education (Creely and Janssen, 2025). In the ﬁeld of international student education, it has formed four core application scenarios: academic writing assistance (Bannister et al., 2024), cross-linguistic communication support (Liu, 2025), literature analysis empowerment (Shi and Xu, 2025), and personalized learning adaptation (Wang et al., 2023). Compared with local students, GAI has more prominent supporting value for international students in China who face triple challenges of language barriers, cultural adaptation, and differences in academic norms (Fan et al., 2025). According to Ministry of Education statistics, the number of international students in China reached 447,000 in 2022 (Chang, 2024), constituting one of the most dynamic international education groups in the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"adaptation, and differences in academic norms (Fan et al., 2025). According to Ministry of Education statistics, the number of international students in China reached 447,000 in 2022 (Chang, 2024), constituting one of the most dynamic international education groups in the world. However, current research on GAI applications for international student education is still in its infancy. Its adoption mechanism, inﬂuencing factors, and differentiated paths have not been systematically explored (Farrelly and Baker, 2023), and there is a signiﬁcant tension between theoretical research gaps and the technological demands of higher education internationalization. The core crux lies in the unique matching relationship between the creative and cross-modal interactive characteristics of GAI and the cross- cultural learning pain points of international students, whereas traditional technology acceptance models, built on efﬁciency- oriented tools, fail to capture this speciﬁc “technology- characteristic–group-demand” matching mechanism. Research on technology acceptance behavior has long centered on the interaction between cognitive decision- making and social inﬂuence. The TAM and its extended theories have conﬁrmed the driving roles of core variables such as perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use (Saad",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"technology acceptance behavior has long centered on the interaction between cognitive decision- making and social inﬂuence. The TAM and its extended theories have conﬁrmed the driving roles of core variables such as perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use (Saad et al., 2025). Nevertheless, their applicability limitations become prominent in the face of GAI’s creative characteristics and the cross-cultural context of international students: they neither fully consider the dominant position of GAI output quality in decision-making, nor systematically incorporate cross-cultural contextual variables. In addition, existing studies mostly focus on local students (Tian et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2021), with insufﬁcient attention paid to the cross-cultural technology acceptance characteristics of international students. The technology decision-making process of international students combines individual cognitive attributes and context- embedded characteristics, in which language proﬁciency, cultural orientation, digital literacy, disciplinary paradigms, and other factors are deeply intertwined (Ma et al., 2024). Speciﬁcally, language barriers caused by inadequate accuracy in academic Chinese expression can be alleviated through GAI’s non-native writing revision functions; cultural adaptation pressures related to China’s academic evaluation system and research models can",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"are deeply intertwined (Ma et al., 2024). Speciﬁcally, language barriers caused by inadequate accuracy in academic Chinese expression can be alleviated through GAI’s non-native writing revision functions; cultural adaptation pressures related to China’s academic evaluation system and research models can be mitigated with GAI’s cross-cultural academic scenario simulation capabilities; and differences in academic norms such as citation formats and paper structures can be optimized via GAI’s format generation and compliance checking functions. Notably, divergences in academic integrity perceptions caused by cultural background differences and cross-national heterogeneity in digital literacy should be integrated into the analytical framework of technology acceptance models. As a basic unit of knowledge production and teaching practice, disciplinary background has been proven to reshape the logic of technology value evaluation through differences in knowledge attributes and research paradigms. According to Becher’s disciplinary classiﬁcation theory (Becher, 1994), science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) disciplines are centered on structured knowledge and positivist methodologies, emphasizing operational ﬂuency and functional adaptability of technologies (Pinar et al., 2025); humanities and social sciences (HSS) are rooted in unstructured knowledge and interpretive thinking, paying",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) disciplines are centered on structured knowledge and positivist methodologies, emphasizing operational ﬂuency and functional adaptability of technologies (Pinar et al., 2025); humanities and social sciences (HSS) are rooted in unstructured knowledge and interpretive thinking, paying more attention to the creativity and perspective inspiration of technologies (Zhang and Zhang, 2024). Such epistemological differences are particularly signiﬁcant among international students (Spangler, 2025), who must simultaneously cope with disciplinary knowledge barriers and adaptation to cross-cultural academic environments, which may amplify the moderating effect of disciplinary background on technology acceptance. However, few existing studies incorporate disciplinary background as a key boundary condition into cross-cultural technology acceptance models, making it difﬁcult to reveal the contextual particularity of the mechanism. Current research on GAI applications in education presents three progressive gaps. At the theoretical level, cross-cultural technology acceptance studies either focus on traditional technologies while ignoring the creativity of GAI, or center on GAI while detaching from the international student context; although Venkatesh et al.’s cross-culturally extended UTAUT model incorporates cultural dimensions (Venkatesh et al., 2012), it cannot adapt to GAI’s",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"on traditional technologies while ignoring the creativity of GAI, or center on GAI while detaching from the international student context; although Venkatesh et al.’s cross-culturally extended UTAUT model incorporates cultural dimensions (Venkatesh et al., 2012), it cannot adapt to GAI’s core feature of “output quality prioritizing operational processes”, resulting in insufﬁcient explanatory power. At the practical level, GAI technology supply mostly adopts a one- size-ﬁts-all model without optimization for the cross-cultural needs of international students, rooted in the failure of theories to reveal differentiated acceptance paths, especially the moderating role of disciplinary background (Ittefaq et al., 2025). At the methodological level, existing studies mostly examine mediating or moderating effects in isolation (Farrelly and Baker, 2023; Panmei, 2025), lacking an integrated analysis of the chain mechanism of “external drivers–cognitive/affective evaluation–behavioral intention”, which makes it difﬁcult to portray the complex decision-making process of multi-factor interaction. These gaps not only restrict theoretical deepening but also limit the precision of university technology promotion strategies. Based on the intersection of higher education internationalization and intelligentization, this study takes the GAI adoption behavior of international students",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,2,"of multi-factor interaction. These gaps not only restrict theoretical deepening but also limit the precision of university technology promotion strategies. Based on the intersection of higher education internationalization and intelligentization, this study takes the GAI adoption behavior of international students in China as the research object and achieves ﬁve innovations. Breaking the existing TAM/UTAUT variable integration paradigm, it introduces attitude as a mediating variable between social norms and perceived behavioral control, sets disciplinary background and cultural values as moderating variables, and constructs an Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",92
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"integrated model of “external drivers – cognitive/affective evaluation – behavioral intention”; its incremental contribution lies in verifying the mediating role of affective factors in cross- cultural contexts, and its breakthrough contribution lies in revealing the context-dependent effect of perceived ease of use and disciplinary moderating boundaries. Coupling the creative and cross-modal characteristics of GAI with the cross-cultural needs of international students, it breaks through traditional research focus on “efﬁciency-oriented tools” and expands the applicable boundary of TAM in emerging technologies and special groups. Using PLS-SEM to integrally analyze mediating and moderating effects, combined with multi-group analysis (MGA) and robustness tests, it accurately portrays multi-path interaction mechanisms and provides methodological references for model testing in complex contexts. Introducing the GAI- speciﬁc construct “perceived intelligence” as an independent antecedent variable, it compensates for the neglect of AI core characteristics in traditional technology acceptance models, constructs an integrated framework, and improves the model’s adaptability to GAI scenarios and international student groups. Incorporating cultural values as a core cross-cultural variable, it conducts comparative analyses of groups with different cultural backgrounds, strengthens the cross-cultural positioning",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"acceptance models, constructs an integrated framework, and improves the model’s adaptability to GAI scenarios and international student groups. Incorporating cultural values as a core cross-cultural variable, it conducts comparative analyses of groups with different cultural backgrounds, strengthens the cross-cultural positioning of the study, and highlights the unique inﬂuence of cross- cultural contexts. Accordingly, this study proposes four core research questions: (1) Under cross-cultural contexts, through what chain paths do external variables such as performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and perceived intelligence inﬂuence international students’ GAI adoption intention? (2) What mediating roles do perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, and attitude play between external variables and adoption intention? (3) Do disciplinary background and cultural values respectively moderate the relationships between core cognitive/affective variables and adoption intention? If so, how are their mechanisms reﬂected? (4) Are there signiﬁcant differences in GAI adoption paths between international students with different cultural backgrounds, and what are the characteristics of these differences? This study conducted a questionnaire survey at 12 universities in eastern, central, and western China (with 219 valid samples). Based on a cultural values scale,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"between international students with different cultural backgrounds, and what are the characteristics of these differences? This study conducted a questionnaire survey at 12 universities in eastern, central, and western China (with 219 valid samples). Based on a cultural values scale, the sample was divided into an individualism-oriented group (n = 98) and a collectivism- oriented group (n = 121). Combined with MGA, it systematically tested the causal relationships between variables, cross-cultural differences, and boundary conditions. By introducing the AI- speciﬁc construct (perceived intelligence), it enriches the construct system of technology acceptance models and remedies the neglect of emerging AI technological characteristics in traditional models; by incorporating cultural variables and conducting cross-cultural comparisons, it clariﬁes the differentiated impacts of cross-cultural contexts on technology acceptance paths; it provides empirical support for the cross- cultural extension of TAM/UTAUT, speciﬁes the mechanism revision of core variables in scenarios of special groups and emerging technologies, reveals the dual moderating effects of disciplinary background and cultural values, and offers a new perspective for constructing contextualized TAMs. Based on the research conclusions, this study proposes implementable four-",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"core variables in scenarios of special groups and emerging technologies, reveals the dual moderating effects of disciplinary background and cultural values, and offers a new perspective for constructing contextualized TAMs. Based on the research conclusions, this study proposes implementable four- dimensional strategies of “AI function adaptation – cross- cultural guidance – disciplinary customization–trust cultivation”, helping universities accurately match international students’ technological needs, improve the effectiveness of GAI applications in education, promote the deep integration of GAI and higher education internationalization, and provide clear guidance for AI-supported cross-cultural teaching practices, learning strategy optimization, and relevant policy design. 2 Theoretical foundations 2.1 Technology acceptance model (TAM) The core contribution of the TAM proposed by Davis lies in extracting two core constructs, namely perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, providing a concise and persuasive analytical framework for explaining the psychological mechanisms of information system adoption behavior (Davis, 1989). Speciﬁcally, perceived usefulness refers to users’ subjective expectation that a technology can improve their task performance, while perceived ease of use focuses on users’ perception of the simplicity of the technology operation process; both",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"information system adoption behavior (Davis, 1989). Speciﬁcally, perceived usefulness refers to users’ subjective expectation that a technology can improve their task performance, while perceived ease of use focuses on users’ perception of the simplicity of the technology operation process; both jointly inﬂuence users’ technology adoption intention through the mediating role of attitude. The evolution of TAM has always centered on the core path of integrating contextual factors and individual traits. TAM2, proposed by Venkatesh and Davis, introduced variables such as subjective norms and voluntariness on the basis of the original model, which not only revealed the moderating effect of social factors on perceived usefulness but also clariﬁed the direct impact of subjective norms on adoption intention in mandatory contexts (Amouri et al., 2025; Venkatesh and Davis, 2000). Subsequently, TAM3 developed by Venkatesh and Bala further integrated individual traits such as computer self-efﬁcacy and perceived enjoyment, forming a multi-path theoretical model of “external variables → core cognitive constructs → behavioral intention” (Algeraﬁ et al., 2023; Venkatesh and Bala, 2008). Among them, computer self-efﬁcacy enhances perceived ease of use by alleviating users’",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"computer self-efﬁcacy and perceived enjoyment, forming a multi-path theoretical model of “external variables → core cognitive constructs → behavioral intention” (Algeraﬁ et al., 2023; Venkatesh and Bala, 2008). Among them, computer self-efﬁcacy enhances perceived ease of use by alleviating users’ technology anxiety and lowering the psychological threshold of operation, which provides important theoretical support for explaining the heterogeneity of technology acceptance behavior among cross-cultural groups. The neglect of irrational factors in traditional TAM has been gradually compensated in subsequent theoretical integration. The construct of perceived behavioral control proposed by Ajzen emphasizes users’ subjective evaluation of their ability to master a technology and the possibility of accessing relevant resources (Ajzen, 1991), while subjective norms reﬂect the implicit pressure from reference groups on users’ technology use behavior; both indirectly affect adoption intention through the mediating role of attitude, making the model more suitable for groups signiﬁcantly inﬂuenced by social and cultural factors such as international students. Nevertheless, neither traditional TAM nor its subsequent extended models incorporate constructs related to the exclusive characteristics of AI, making them difﬁcult to adapt to the unique",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,3,"suitable for groups signiﬁcantly inﬂuenced by social and cultural factors such as international students. Nevertheless, neither traditional TAM nor its subsequent extended models incorporate constructs related to the exclusive characteristics of AI, making them difﬁcult to adapt to the unique attributes of GAI, namely intelligent output, black-box operation, and trust dependence, which is also the core logical starting point for this study to introduce perceived intelligence as a key antecedent variable. Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",80
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"2.2 Uniﬁed theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT) The UTAUT proposed by Venkatesh et al. constructs a systematic analytical framework of “core antecedent variables → adoption intention” by integrating eight classic theories including TAM and the Theory of Reasoned Action (TRA), and clariﬁes the moderating effects of individual traits and contextual factors on technology acceptance behavior (Perez, 2024; Venkatesh et al., 2003). There is a clear homology between the core constructs of this model and TAM: performance expectancy corresponds to perceived usefulness in TAM, effort expectancy corresponds to perceived ease of use, social inﬂuence is equivalent to subjective norms, and facilitating conditions are highly consistent with the construct of resource accessibility, laying a foundation for the integration of the two theories. UTAUT2 adds constructs such as hedonic motivation, price value, and habit on the basis of UTAUT, expanding the applicable context of the model from organizational scenarios to consumer scenarios and further enriching the explanatory dimensions of technology acceptance behavior (Sergeeva et al., 2025). Among them, hedonic motivation emphasizes the pleasure and emotional satisfaction brought about by the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"the applicable context of the model from organizational scenarios to consumer scenarios and further enriching the explanatory dimensions of technology acceptance behavior (Sergeeva et al., 2025). Among them, hedonic motivation emphasizes the pleasure and emotional satisfaction brought about by the technology use process, providing an important reference for explaining the emotional driving mechanism of GAI; habit reﬂects the automation degree of technology use behavior, suggesting that long-term use may weaken users’ cognitive evaluation of technology and thus directly affect adoption intention. Although this study does not include the construct of price value (as generative AI is mostly free or low-cost in international students’ academic scenarios), the attention of UTAUT2 to emotional factors provides important enlightenment for understanding the attitude formation mechanism of international students toward GAI. The limitations of UTAUT and TAM are obviously complementary: the former focuses on the integration of external contexts and individual factors but ignores the intermediate process of cognitive–affectivetransformation, resulting in insufﬁcient explanation for the emotional driving characteristics of emerging technologies; the latter accurately depicts the core chain of “cognition → attitude → intention” but",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"integration of external contexts and individual factors but ignores the intermediate process of cognitive–affectivetransformation, resulting in insufﬁcient explanation for the emotional driving characteristics of emerging technologies; the latter accurately depicts the core chain of “cognition → attitude → intention” but fails to fully integrate external contextual factors and AI- speciﬁc characteristics, making it difﬁcult to explain the technology decision logic of cross-cultural groups toward GAI. Even the extensions of TAM2 and TAM3 cannot adapt to the core characteristics of GAI — output quality priority, intelligence dominance, and trust dependence — and the particularity of international student groups, nor can they explain the independent driving effect of perceived intelligence, an AI-speciﬁc factor, on adoption intention, which highlights the necessity of theoretical integration and expansion. 2.3 Integration of TAM and UTAUT and introduction of AI-speciﬁc constructs The unique advantage of TAM in revealing the internal cognitive–affective mechanism of technology acceptance complements the signiﬁcant value of UTAUT in integrating external contextual factors, providing a solid theoretical foundation for the construction of the research model in this paper. To strengthen theoretical innovation and compensate",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"revealing the internal cognitive–affective mechanism of technology acceptance complements the signiﬁcant value of UTAUT in integrating external contextual factors, providing a solid theoretical foundation for the construction of the research model in this paper. To strengthen theoretical innovation and compensate for the neglect of unique AI characteristics in traditional models, this study introduces perceived intelligence as a core antecedent variable. Perceived intelligence speciﬁcally refers to international students’ subjective evaluation of the accuracy, creativity, and scenario adaptability of generative AI output content, which is the core feature distinguishing GAI from traditional information technology, directly reﬂects the intelligent level of the technology itself, and exerts a signiﬁcant positive impact on perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use (Naidu, 2024; Singh et al., 2025). As see in Figure 1, the speciﬁc logic of theoretical integration in this study is as follows. First, performance expectancy, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions in UTAUT, and the AI-speciﬁc construct—perceived intelligence—are jointly positioned as antecedent variables of TAM core cognitive constructs: performance expectancy directly positively affects perceived usefulness, effort expectancy improves perceived ease of use by reducing users’ technology",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"expectancy, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions in UTAUT, and the AI-speciﬁc construct—perceived intelligence—are jointly positioned as antecedent variables of TAM core cognitive constructs: performance expectancy directly positively affects perceived usefulness, effort expectancy improves perceived ease of use by reducing users’ technology anxiety and operation costs, and facilitating conditions provide resource guarantees for technology use; as a core AI-speciﬁc construct, perceived intelligence positively empowers perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use by enhancing the precise adaptation and creativity of generated content, further strengthening the formation of cognitive constructs. Second, subjective norms and perceived behavioral control are directed separately to attitude; international students’ demand for social identity strengthens the shaping effect of subjective norms on attitude, while perceived behavioral control directly affects users’ emotional tendency toward technology use. Third, attitude is introduced as an emotional mediator connecting subjective norms, perceived behavioral control, and adoption intention, consistent with the independent driving effect of emotional tendency on technology acceptance behavior in cross-cultural contexts. Fourth, cultural values of individualism/collectivism are incorporated as core cross-cultural moderating variables to explore their moderating effects on various theoretical paths and",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"adoption intention, consistent with the independent driving effect of emotional tendency on technology acceptance behavior in cross-cultural contexts. Fourth, cultural values of individualism/collectivism are incorporated as core cross-cultural moderating variables to explore their moderating effects on various theoretical paths and cross- cultural group differences. 2.4 Theoretical expansion and supplementary cross-cultural perspective Applying classic TAMs to GAI and international student education scenarios requires full adaptation to technological characteristics, group particularity, and cross-cultural contexts (Lang et al., 2025). The black-box attribute of generative AI shifts users’ focus of technology evaluation from operation efﬁciency to output quality, which strengthens the impact of performance expectancy on perceived usefulness and weakens the direct effect of perceived ease of use; its cross-modal interaction feature signiﬁcantly lowers users’ cognitive threshold, shifting the core focus of effort expectancy from operation complexity to instruction accuracy and debugging cost; the introduction of perceived intelligence as an AI-speciﬁc construct further improves the model’s adaptability to AI technological characteristics. Among international student groups, cross-cultural differences are core contextual factors affecting technology Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",178
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,4,"introduction of perceived intelligence as an AI-speciﬁc construct further improves the model’s adaptability to AI technological characteristics. Among international student groups, cross-cultural differences are core contextual factors affecting technology Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 04 frontiersin.org",38
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,5,"acceptance behavior. This study mainly incorporates cultural values (individualism/collectivism) as a key cross- cultural variable. Among them, international students with a strong individualist tendency pay more attention to personal needs and subjective experience, are more sensitive to perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, and emphasize the practical utility of technology for personal academic tasks; international students with a strong collectivist tendency focus more on group norms and social identity, are more strongly inﬂuenced by subjective norms on attitude and adoption intention, and attach greater importance to the group consistency of technology use. In addition, disciplinary differences constitute another key boundary condition (Wong et al., 2025): international students in STEM emphasize the ﬂuency of technology operation and the accuracy of output content more, with a more prominent impact of perceived ease of use on adoption intention (Susilawati et al., 2023); international students in HSS pay more attention to the creativity and inspiration of generated content, with a relatively weakened role of perceived ease of use and a more signiﬁcant impact of subjective norms and attitude (Massoro and Adewale, 2019). 3",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,5,"international students in HSS pay more attention to the creativity and inspiration of generated content, with a relatively weakened role of perceived ease of use and a more signiﬁcant impact of subjective norms and attitude (Massoro and Adewale, 2019). 3 Hypothesis development 3.1 Performance expectancy The core function of performance expectancy is to shape users’ cognitive evaluation of technological utility (Alyoussef, 2022). Combined with the output quality priority characteristic of GAI and the cross-cultural learning pain points of international students, the direct driving effect of performance expectancy on perceived usefulness is clearly supported (Sair and Danish, 2018). The content generation and cross-modal interaction capabilities of GAI can precisely solve core problems faced by international students, such as language barriers and high academic adaptation costs (Gao et al., 2024). When international students expect that GAI can improve academic output efﬁciency, optimize cross-cultural knowledge comprehension, or adapt to Chinese academic norms, such efﬁcacy expectancy will directly strengthen their cognition of the technology’s perceived usefulness (Cao et al., 2025). Performance expectancy also indirectly inﬂuences perceived ease of use through the mechanism of “utility",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,5,"cross-cultural knowledge comprehension, or adapt to Chinese academic norms, such efﬁcacy expectancy will directly strengthen their cognition of the technology’s perceived usefulness (Cao et al., 2025). Performance expectancy also indirectly inﬂuences perceived ease of use through the mechanism of “utility expectancy → psychological buffering → improved perceived ease of use” (Gai, 2024). Different from the direct evaluation of ease of use by effort expectancy, performance expectancy follows the logic of outcome value offsetting process costs. The black-box attribute of generative artiﬁcial intelligence makes international students focus more on output accuracy and academic adaptability rather than operational complexity (Liu et al., 2023). The higher the performance expectancy, the higher the tolerance threshold for operational friction among international students; they will regard initial operational difﬁculties as a necessary cost to obtain high-value outputs, subjectively weaken the negative perception of operational complexity, and strengthen the judgment of ease of use (AlAjmi et al., 2025). This mechanism is particularly prominent in high-pressure academic tasks, where international students’ attention tends to focus on outcome output rather than process obstacles (Chow et al., 2023). Accordingly, the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,5,"strengthen the judgment of ease of use (AlAjmi et al., 2025). This mechanism is particularly prominent in high-pressure academic tasks, where international students’ attention tends to focus on outcome output rather than process obstacles (Chow et al., 2023). Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H1a: Performance expectancy positively affects perceived ease of use; H1b: Performance expectancy positively affects perceived usefulness. FIGURE 1 Conception model. Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",73
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"3.2 Effort expectancy Effort expectancy focuses on users’ direct cognitive and behavioral input evaluation of technology operation and is a core antecedent of perceived ease of use (Chen et al., 2025a). Combined with the cross-modal interaction feature of GAI and the language barrier pain point of international students, this effect shows stronger contextual particularity. The natural language interaction of GAI lowers the operational threshold of traditional technologies (Kar et al., 2023), but prompt engineering has become a new source of effort. For international students, the linguistic complexity of the operation interface and the requirement for accurate instruction expression may amplify cognitive load due to language barriers. Therefore, when international students anticipate low mental effort required to use GAI, their technological self- efﬁcacy improves signiﬁcantly, directly forming the cognitive perception of ease of use that operation is simple with no extra burden. Empirical studies have conﬁrmed that the positive impact of effort expectancy on perceived ease of use is particularly signiﬁcant among international students (Nayanajith et al., 2019), as non-native speakers are more sensitive to operational complexity. Effort expectancy also indirectly",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"burden. Empirical studies have conﬁrmed that the positive impact of effort expectancy on perceived ease of use is particularly signiﬁcant among international students (Nayanajith et al., 2019), as non-native speakers are more sensitive to operational complexity. Effort expectancy also indirectly inﬂuences perceived usefulness through the cognitive resource releasemechanism, and this path is signiﬁcantly strengthened in cross-cultural contexts. A low operational threshold can reduce the cognitive load of international students, making them focus less on how to operate and more on evaluating the actual utility of the technology in solving cross-cultural academic pain points (Wang et al., 2023). Especially for international students with uneven levels of digital literacy, low effort expectancy can alleviate technology anxiety and make them more acutely capture the core value of generative artiﬁcial intelligence. Therefore, the following hypotheses are proposed: H2a: Effort expectancy positively affects perceived ease of use; H2b: Effort expectancy positively affects perceived usefulness. 3.3 Facilitating conditions As an external support system, facilitating conditions are centrally deﬁned as the accessibility of resources required for technology use, completeness of infrastructure, and organizational support, which are consistent",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"of use; H2b: Effort expectancy positively affects perceived usefulness. 3.3 Facilitating conditions As an external support system, facilitating conditions are centrally deﬁned as the accessibility of resources required for technology use, completeness of infrastructure, and organizational support, which are consistent with the resource accessibility construct in TAM3 (Hamadeh et al., 2025). Combined with international students’ cross-cultural adaptation pressure and GAI’s functional adaptation demand, the impact of facilitating conditions on perceived ease of use presents distinct contextual particularity: for international students facing language barriers, multilingual operation guides and real-time bilingual technical support can directly reduce operational anxiety; a stable network environment and exclusive university access rights can ensure continuous use, avoid operational interruptions caused by resource shortages, and further strengthen perceived ease of use. Empirical studies have conﬁrmed that the positive impact of facilitating conditions on perceived ease of use is signiﬁcantly stronger in cross-cultural educational contexts than in local contexts (Jung and Lee, 2020). Facilitating conditions also indirectly inﬂuence perceived usefulness through the chain mechanism of “resource accessibility → functional adaptability → improved utility perception” (Shin, 2010). Firstly, resource accessibility",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"signiﬁcantly stronger in cross-cultural educational contexts than in local contexts (Jung and Lee, 2020). Facilitating conditions also indirectly inﬂuence perceived usefulness through the chain mechanism of “resource accessibility → functional adaptability → improved utility perception” (Shin, 2010). Firstly, resource accessibility reduces external constraints and ensures international students’ continuous use of core functions of GAI, avoiding weakened utility perception caused by resource shortages (Huynh and Khoa, 2025). Secondly, functional adaptability strengthens the matching degree between technology and academic tasks (Lang et al., 2025). Thirdly, targeted training helps international students master prompt engineering and output calibration skills, efﬁciently transforming technological functions into academic outputs, and strengthening the utility perception that technology improves task performance (Li et al., 2025). Research by Marginson also conﬁrmed that the more complete the technical support system for international students, the stronger their perception of technology usefulness (Marginson, 2012), as external support can maximize the practical application value of technology. Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H3a: Facilitating conditions positively affect perceived ease of use; H3b: Facilitating conditions positively affect perceived usefulness. 3.4 Perceived intelligence As the core",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"2012), as external support can maximize the practical application value of technology. Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H3a: Facilitating conditions positively affect perceived ease of use; H3b: Facilitating conditions positively affect perceived usefulness. 3.4 Perceived intelligence As the core exclusive construct distinguishing GAI from traditional information technology, perceived intelligence directly reﬂects international students’ subjective evaluation of the technology’s output quality and adaptation capability, and is a key antecedent affecting the formation of their cognitive constructs (Moussawi et al., 2021). Its positive impact on perceived ease of use stems from the implicit offset of operational costs by intelligent output: the accurate semantic understanding and cross-scenario adaptation capability of GAI can reduce international students’ effort input in prompt debugging and output calibration, weaken the perception of operational complexity, and enable quick demand matching through intelligent interaction even with language barriers, indirectly improving ease of use cognition. The driving effect of perceived intelligence on perceived usefulness is more direct and precisely matches international students’ cross- cultural academic pain points: the accuracy and creativity of its output content can effectively alleviate non-native academic",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,6,"improving ease of use cognition. The driving effect of perceived intelligence on perceived usefulness is more direct and precisely matches international students’ cross- cultural academic pain points: the accuracy and creativity of its output content can effectively alleviate non-native academic writing dilemmas, adapt to Chinese academic norms and research paradigms, and empower literature analysis and cross-linguistic communication, directly strengthening international students’ cognition of the technology’s utility in improving academic performance. High perceived intelligence can enhance the adaptability of the technology to international students’ academic needs, thereby positively shaping their judgment of technology usefulness. Accordingly, the following hypotheses are proposed: H4a: Perceived intelligence positively affects perceived ease of use; H4b: Perceived intelligence positively affects perceived usefulness. 3.5 Subjective norms As the core carrier of social factors, subjective norms shape users’ technological affective tendency by transmitting value Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",144
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"expectations from important reference groups, and its positive impact on attitude has sufﬁcient theoretical basis (Zhang and Wang, 2025). Combined with international students’ social identity needsand GAI’s academic tool attribute, this relationship is signiﬁcantly strengthened in cross-cultural contexts. As cultural adapters, international students are more sensitive to normative signals from the academic community (Maringe and Jenkins, 2015). Social cues such as supervisor recommendations and peer usage behavior can effectively reduce their uncertainty perception regarding the usage boundaries of generative artiﬁcial intelligence (Wang et al., 2020). Extended studies on UTAUT2 have conﬁrmed the robustness of the positive effect of subjective norms on attitude in educational technology contexts (Ursavaş et al., 2019), and the creative characteristic of GAI further ampliﬁes this effect—since technology use is accompanied by cognitive thresholds and risk perception, international students rely more on normative guidance from signiﬁcant others to establish trust (Riad Shams, 2017). Empirical studies show that the positive inﬂuence coefﬁcient of subjective norms on attitude in cross-cultural technology acceptance is signiﬁcantly higher than that in local groups, which is highly consistent with the particularity of the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"to establish trust (Riad Shams, 2017). Empirical studies show that the positive inﬂuence coefﬁcient of subjective norms on attitude in cross-cultural technology acceptance is signiﬁcantly higher than that in local groups, which is highly consistent with the particularity of the international student population (Yang, 2006). Subjective norms positively shape international students’ attitude toward GAI through the dual mechanisms of social identity and risk reduction (Zhang and Jiang, 2025). Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H5: Subjective norms positively affect attitude. 3.6 Perceived behavioral control Perceived behavioral control reﬂects an individual’s comprehensive evaluation of their ability to master a technology and access relevant resources, and its positive inﬂuence on attitude is a robust conclusion in technology acceptance research (Chen et al., 2025b). Combined with the operational complexity of GAI and the cross-cultural adaptation pain points of international students, this relationship demonstrates distinct contextual particularity. Requirements such as prompt engineering and output calibration in GAI pose additional cognitive challenges for non-native speakers, while international students’ unfamiliarity with the Chinese digital ecosystem further reduces initial perceived behavioral control. When international students perceive that",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"distinct contextual particularity. Requirements such as prompt engineering and output calibration in GAI pose additional cognitive challenges for non-native speakers, while international students’ unfamiliarity with the Chinese digital ecosystem further reduces initial perceived behavioral control. When international students perceive that they possess sufﬁcient operational skills and adequate environmental support, their technology use anxiety decreases signiﬁcantly, thereby forming a positive attitude toward generative artiﬁcial intelligence (Avcı, 2024). Extended research on TAM3 conﬁrms that computer self- efﬁcacy indirectly strengthens attitude by reducing technology anxiety (Celik and Yesilyurt, 2013), and this mechanism is more prominent among international students. Language barriers lower international students’ technological operational self-efﬁcacy, whereas perceived behavioral control becomes crucial for building technology trust by reinforcing the belief that technology can effectively solve cross-cultural academic problems. Perceived behavioral control positively inﬂuences attitude through the dual mechanisms of anxiety reduction and trust construction (Bajwa, 2024). Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H6: Perceived behavioral control positively affects attitude. 3.7 Perceived ease of use Perceived ease of use reﬂects users’ subjective evaluation of the simplicity of technology operation (Deng et al., 2025). Its",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"construction (Bajwa, 2024). Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H6: Perceived behavioral control positively affects attitude. 3.7 Perceived ease of use Perceived ease of use reﬂects users’ subjective evaluation of the simplicity of technology operation (Deng et al., 2025). Its positive impact on adoption intention follows the mechanism of “reduced operational cost → increased usage frequency → strengthened intention”, which needs to be adapted to the characteristics of GAI and the international student context (Hu et al., 2025). According to the aforementioned theoretical expansion, the cross-modal interaction of generative artiﬁcial intelligence lowers the threshold of traditional operational skills (Wang et al., 2025b), but prompt engineering has become a new dimension for evaluating ease of use. For international students, perceived ease of use also contains cross-culturally speciﬁc connotations such as convenience of instruction expression and simplicity of output calibration, which can directly reduce the operational cognitive load of non-native speakers. Although the black-box attribute of GAI makes output quality the core evaluation criterion, perceived ease of use still indirectly inﬂuences adoption intention by alleviating technology anxiety and increasing usage frequency (Chiu,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"directly reduce the operational cognitive load of non-native speakers. Although the black-box attribute of GAI makes output quality the core evaluation criterion, perceived ease of use still indirectly inﬂuences adoption intention by alleviating technology anxiety and increasing usage frequency (Chiu, 2025). For international students, language barriers amplify the negative effect of operational complexity — even with high output quality, cumbersome operations may be abandoned due to excessive cognitive load. Empirical studies conﬁrm that the positive impact of perceived ease of use on adoption intention remains signiﬁcant in cross-cultural technology acceptance contexts, especially for international students with low digital literacy, where operational simplicity is the foundation for establishing usage intention (Dalle et al., 2024). Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H7: Perceived ease of use positively affects adoption intention. 3.8 Perceived usefulness Perceived usefulness is the most robust predictor of technology adoption intention, and this relationship has been fully veriﬁed in UTAUT and its extended models (Zhang and Wareewanich, 2024). Combined with the content generation and cross-modal adaptation characteristics of GAI and the cross- cultural academic pain points of international students,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"adoption intention, and this relationship has been fully veriﬁed in UTAUT and its extended models (Zhang and Wareewanich, 2024). Combined with the content generation and cross-modal adaptation characteristics of GAI and the cross- cultural academic pain points of international students, this inﬂuence shows stronger contextual particularity (Zhao et al., 2025). Through creative functions such as non-native writing revision, multilingual literature analysis, and academic norm adaptation, GAI precisely solves the core challenges of international students, expanding the connotation of perceived usefulness from traditional efﬁciency improvement to enhanced cross-cultural academic adaptation ability, which better aligns with international students’ needs. Perceived usefulness has the highest positive impact coefﬁcient on adoption intention in the educational application of GAI, and this effect is further ampliﬁed in cross-cultural contexts — international students’ perception of technology usefulness is directly related to whether language barriers can be alleviated and academic adaptation pressure reduced (Yin, 2025). Davis’s classic study shows that the inﬂuence of perceived usefulness on adoption intention is independent of perceived ease of use, and the output quality priority feature of GAI makes this Fu and Cao",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,7,"and academic adaptation pressure reduced (Yin, 2025). Davis’s classic study shows that the inﬂuence of perceived usefulness on adoption intention is independent of perceived ease of use, and the output quality priority feature of GAI makes this Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 07 frontiersin.org",46
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"independence more prominent. Even with certain operational complexity, high perceived usefulness can still drive international students to form strong adoption intention. A meta-analysis of cross-cultural technology acceptance also conﬁrms the universality of this relationship among international student groups (Schepers and Wetzels, 2007). As the core cognitive evaluation of international students toward the value of GAI, perceived usefulness positively drives adoption intention (Jin et al., 2025). Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H8: Perceived usefulness positively affects adoption intention. 3.9 Attitude Attitude serves as an affective hub connecting cognitive evaluation and behavioral intention (Wang and Yoon, 2024). Its positive impact on adoption intention follows the mechanism of “affective identiﬁcation → reduced psychological resistance → strengthened usage intention”. Within the integrated TAM- UTAUT framework, the core value of attitude lies in integrating cognitive factors and psychosocial factors into a stable affective tendency. This mechanism demonstrates distinct adaptability to the characteristics of GAI and the cross-cultural affective needs of international students. Firstly, trust in attitude can alleviate anxiety about the uncertainty of GAI output quality (Silalahi, 2024) — due to differences in language",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"This mechanism demonstrates distinct adaptability to the characteristics of GAI and the cross-cultural affective needs of international students. Firstly, trust in attitude can alleviate anxiety about the uncertainty of GAI output quality (Silalahi, 2024) — due to differences in language and academic norms, international students have greater concerns about the compliance and accuracy of technology output, and a positive attitude can reduce risk perception (Wu et al., 2025). Secondly, identiﬁcation can satisfy cross-cultural adaptation needs — when international students regard GAI as a tool for integrating into the Chinese academic environment, affective belonging strengthens continuous usage intention (Ma et al., 2025). Thirdly, sense of control can offset psychological resistance to technology use—international students’ unfamiliarity with the Chinese digital ecosystem may lead to adoption hesitation, and a positive attitude can enhance psychological security (Chen and Chen, 2025). Empirical studies conﬁrm the robustness of the positive inﬂuence of attitude on adoption intention in emerging technology acceptance, and international students’ affective needs further strengthen this effect (Liang and Mao, 2025). As an affective driver, attitude positively inﬂuences international students’ GAI adoption intention by",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"robustness of the positive inﬂuence of attitude on adoption intention in emerging technology acceptance, and international students’ affective needs further strengthen this effect (Liang and Mao, 2025). As an affective driver, attitude positively inﬂuences international students’ GAI adoption intention by reducing psychological resistance to cross-cultural technology adoption. Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H9: Attitude positively affects adoption intention. 3.10 Moderating variables 3.10.1 Disciplinary background Based on Becher’s disciplinary classiﬁcation theory (Becher, 1987) and Kuhn’s scientiﬁc paradigm theory (Kindi, 1995), disciplinary background exerts differential moderating effects on the path strength between core variables and adoption intention through the chain mechanism of “disciplinary epistemology → differences in technological demand → differentiation of acceptance paths” (Hou, 2025). As convergent disciplines, STEM focus on structured knowledge and standardized processes, with a stronger demand for the efﬁciency attributes of GAI (Marzano, 2025). Operational ﬂuency directly affects academic task efﬁciency, so the positive inﬂuence of perceived ease of use on adoption intention is more signiﬁcant among STEM international students. As divergent disciplines, HSS are rooted in unstructured knowledge and critical thinking, paying more attention to",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"directly affects academic task efﬁciency, so the positive inﬂuence of perceived ease of use on adoption intention is more signiﬁcant among STEM international students. As divergent disciplines, HSS are rooted in unstructured knowledge and critical thinking, paying more attention to the creative attributes of GAI (Qian and Xie, 2026), with higher tolerance for operational complexity and a relatively weakened inﬂuence of perceived ease of use. Since perceived usefulness focuses on the core value of GAI in alleviating cross-cultural academic adaptation, it possesses interdisciplinary universality, and the moderating effect of disciplinary background on the path between perceived usefulness and adoption intention is weak. The driving effect of attitude on adoption intention is not restricted by discipline; positive attitude strengthens adoption intention regardless of discipline, and the moderating effect of disciplinary background is insigniﬁcant. Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H10a: Disciplinary background partially moderates the relationships between perceived ease of use/usefulness, attitude, and adoption intention. 3.10.2 Cultural values (individualism/collectivism) Based on Hofstede’s cultural dimension theory, individualism/ collectivism, as a core cross-cultural variable, moderates the relationships between core cognitive/affective variables and adoption",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"background partially moderates the relationships between perceived ease of use/usefulness, attitude, and adoption intention. 3.10.2 Cultural values (individualism/collectivism) Based on Hofstede’s cultural dimension theory, individualism/ collectivism, as a core cross-cultural variable, moderates the relationships between core cognitive/affective variables and adoption intention through the mechanism of “value orientation→priority of needs→technology acceptance path” (Hofstede, 2001). International students with an individualist tendency focus on personal utility and subjective experience, with higher sensitivity to perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, and the driving effects of both on adoption intention are stronger. International students with a collectivist tendency pay more attention to group norms and social identity, the inﬂuence of attitude derived from subjective norms on adoption intention is more signiﬁcant, and the roles of perceived ease of use and perceived usefulness are relatively weakened. Since perceived usefulness focuses on the core value of GAI in alleviating cross- cultural academic adaptation, its moderating effect is weak; the moderating difference in attitude stems from the inﬂuence of culture on affective identiﬁcation, and the driving effect of attitude on adoption intention is stronger in collectivist groups.",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,8,"GAI in alleviating cross- cultural academic adaptation, its moderating effect is weak; the moderating difference in attitude stems from the inﬂuence of culture on affective identiﬁcation, and the driving effect of attitude on adoption intention is stronger in collectivist groups. Accordingly, the following hypothesis is proposed: H10b: Cultural values partially moderate the relationships between perceived ease of use/usefulness, attitude, and adoption intention. 4 Research instruments and data collection 4.1 Questionnaire design 4.1.1 Scale development and contextual adaptation This study employed a structured questionnaire as the data collection instrument. Scale development strictly followed the Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 08 frontiersin.org",102
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"standardized procedure of “theoretical anchoring–cross-cultural adaptation–pretest optimization” to ensure measurement reliability, validity, and ecological adaptability. The questionnaire consisted of three sections: the ﬁrst section contained informed consent and completion instructions, clearly stating the research purpose, data usage, and anonymity protection policy; formal participation was only allowed after respondents gave voluntary consent. The second section collected demographic information, including core variables such as gender, continent of origin, discipline, generative AI usage frequency, and Chinese proﬁciency. The third section comprised measurement items for core constructs, which were adaptively revised based on the integrated TAM-UTAUT framework and the cross-cultural learning context of international students in China. All measurement items for core constructs were primarily adopted from mature scales validated by multiple empirical studies, with semantic adjustments made for certain items in the cross-cultural educational context to ensure content validity (see Table 1). Speciﬁcally, items for performance expectancy, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions, and subjective norms were adapted from the original UTAUT scale by Venkatesh et al. (2012); perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, attitude, and adoption intention were referenced from Davis’s classic TAM scale;",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"items for performance expectancy, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions, and subjective norms were adapted from the original UTAUT scale by Venkatesh et al. (2012); perceived usefulness, perceived ease of use, attitude, and adoption intention were referenced from Davis’s classic TAM scale; perceived behavioral control used the version revised by Zhang et al. for cross-cultural international student groups (Zhang and Wareewanich, 2024); perceived intelligence was adapted from scales developed for generative AI scenarios by Naidu and Singh et al. (Naidu, 2024; Singh et al., 2025), with semantic adjustments tailored to the cross-cultural academic needs of international students in China, focusing on measurement dimensions of output accuracy, creativity, and cross-scenario adaptability. Based on exploratory factor analysis (EFA) results in the pretest, two items were ﬁnally retained for the facilitating conditions construct, with the following deletion rationale: among the initial three items, one item had a factor loading of 0.62 (below the 0.7 threshold) and semantic overlap with another item. After deletion, the composite reliability (CR) of this construct increased from 0.78 to 0.81, and the average variance extracted (AVE) rose from 0.61 to",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"had a factor loading of 0.62 (below the 0.7 threshold) and semantic overlap with another item. After deletion, the composite reliability (CR) of this construct increased from 0.78 to 0.81, and the average variance extracted (AVE) rose from 0.61 to 0.84, meeting the measurement model criteria proposed by Hair Jr et al., balancing measurement parsimony and effectiveness (Hair et al., 2011). Although only two items were retained, they covered the core connotations of facilitating conditions in the UTAUT framework — resource accessibility and organizational support — with factor loadings of both items above 0.86, effectively reﬂecting the core characteristics of the construct without excessive weakening of theoretical connotation. 4.1.2 Content validity optimization To ensure scale reliability, validity, and applicability, two rounds of pretests were conducted before the formal survey. In the ﬁrst round, three senior scholars in the ﬁeld of TAMs, two experts in cross-cultural education research, and one English- native bilingual researcher evaluated the theoretical appropriateness, clarity of expression, and cross-cultural applicability of the questionnaire items. Expert feedback indicated issues such as redundant expression in some items and culture-speciﬁc",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"TAMs, two experts in cross-cultural education research, and one English- native bilingual researcher evaluated the theoretical appropriateness, clarity of expression, and cross-cultural applicability of the questionnaire items. Expert feedback indicated issues such as redundant expression in some items and culture-speciﬁc phrasing in others; for example, “improving learning efﬁciency” and “accelerating task completion” in perceived usefulness were semantically overlapping and aligned with domestic academic conventions, which might cause comprehension bias among international students from non- Chinese backgrounds. Accordingly, one redundant item was deleted, culture-speciﬁc expressions were revised to universal language, and operational deﬁnition notes were added. In the second round, a pre-survey was administered to 30 international students matching the characteristics of the formal survey sample, and exploratory factor analysis was used to test construct validity: the KMO value was 0.872 (p < 0.001), and Bartlett’s test of sphericity was signiﬁcant (χ2 = 2,845.32, df = 595, p < 0.001), indicating the data were suitable for factor analysis. Three items with factor loadings below 0.7 were deleted (from effort expectancy, perceived behavioral control, and attitude respectively), and two ambiguously phrased items",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"(χ2 = 2,845.32, df = 595, p < 0.001), indicating the data were suitable for factor analysis. Three items with factor loadings below 0.7 were deleted (from effort expectancy, perceived behavioral control, and attitude respectively), and two ambiguously phrased items were revised (e.g., “Using GAI is convenient” was revised to “The operational process of using GAI to complete academic tasks is convenient”). Finally, 41 items were retained. After pretesting, Cronbach’s α coefﬁcients for each construct ranged from 0.76 to 0.90 (all above 0.75), ensuring internal consistency of the scale. 4.1.3 Bias control In response to the cross-cultural and linguistic heterogeneity of the international student sample, this study implemented multiple cross-cultural measurement adaptation measures. The questionnaire was provided in both Chinese and English versions, with the translation process strictly adhering to Brislin’s “translation–back- translation–consistency checking standard” (Brislin, 1970): ﬁrst, two bilingual researchers independently translated Chinese items into English; then one English-native scholar unfamiliar with the original questionnaire back- translated the English version into Chinese; the original Chinese items and back-translated version were compared, items with semantic deviations were revised, and ﬁve",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"researchers independently translated Chinese items into English; then one English-native scholar unfamiliar with the original questionnaire back- translated the English version into Chinese; the original Chinese items and back-translated version were compared, items with semantic deviations were revised, and ﬁve English-native international students tested the comprehensibility of the English version to ensure no ambiguous expressions. For linguistic proﬁciency adaptation, a self-rated Chinese proﬁciency item (Levels 1–3 corresponding to beginner, intermediate, and advanced) was placed on the ﬁrst page of the questionnaire, allowing respondents to select the questionnaire language based on their proﬁciency. English annotations were added to professional terms in the Chinese version to avoid comprehension bias. For cultural adaptation optimization, culture- speciﬁc expressions were removed and replaced with internationally universal academic language, and brief explanations were added to items involving Chinese academic scenarios to assist international students from non-Chinese backgrounds in understanding. Regarding measurement details, clear anchor deﬁnitions for the 5-point Likert scale were provided before the items, stating: “Please rate the following statements according to your actual situation, where 1 = strongly disagree and 5 = strongly agree”.",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,9,"backgrounds in understanding. Regarding measurement details, clear anchor deﬁnitions for the 5-point Likert scale were provided before the items, stating: “Please rate the following statements according to your actual situation, where 1 = strongly disagree and 5 = strongly agree”. To reduce response bias, two reverse-coded items were set in the constructs of attitude and perceived behavioral control, which were reverse-coded during data processing. Operational deﬁnitions were clearly speciﬁed for demographic variables: usage frequency was deﬁned as the average weekly frequency of GAI use in the past three months (divided into ﬁve levels); Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 09 frontiersin.org",102
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,10,"Chinese proﬁciency was categorized into beginner, intermediate, and advanced according to HSK standards; disciplines were merged into humanities and social sciences (HSS) and science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) based on Becher’s classiﬁcation framework for subsequent moderating effect analysis (Becher, 1994). 4.1.4 Common method bias test Since all core variables in this study were collected via the same questionnaire at the same time point, three commonly used methods were adopted to test and avoid interference from common method bias on research results, ensuring result robustness. (1) Harman’s single-factor test: unrotated exploratory factor analysis was performed on all 41 core construct items. The results showed that 10 factors with eigenvalues greater than 1 were extracted, and the ﬁrst factor accounted for 27.36% of the variance, far below the 40% critical threshold, indicating no severe common method bias. (2) Full collinearity VIF test: combined with previous collinearity test results, VIF values of all antecedent predictor variables for endogenous variables ranged from 1.341 to 2.207, all below the strict critical value of 3.3 (Kock, 2015), further verifying no signiﬁcant common method bias. (3)",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,10,"test: combined with previous collinearity test results, VIF values of all antecedent predictor variables for endogenous variables ranged from 1.341 to 2.207, all below the strict critical value of 3.3 (Kock, 2015), further verifying no signiﬁcant common method bias. (3) Marker variable method: “duration of Chinese learning” was selected as the marker variable (theoretically unrelated to core constructs), and its correlation coefﬁcients with each core construct were tested. Results showed that all correlation coefﬁcients ranged from 0.032 to 0.087 (p > 0.05), and after controlling for the marker variable, no signiﬁcant changes were observed in path coefﬁcients and signiﬁcance levels, conﬁrming again that common method bias had minimal impact on research ﬁndings. 4.2 Data collection and sample characteristics 4.2.1 Sampling strategy and implementation process To ensure sample representativeness and structural rationality, this study adopted stratiﬁed multistage sampling with three stratiﬁcation dimensions: region, institution type, and discipline. At the regional level, four universities were randomly selected from eastern, central, and western China respectively (12 in total) according to differences in higher education development levels. At the institutional level, the four universities",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,10,"dimensions: region, institution type, and discipline. At the regional level, four universities were randomly selected from eastern, central, and western China respectively (12 in total) according to differences in higher education development levels. At the institutional level, the four universities selected in each region included 2 comprehensive, 1 science and engineering, and 1 humanities and social sciences institution, covering 6 Double First-Class universities, 4 non-Double First- Class universities, and 2 specialized institutions. At the disciplinary level, stratiﬁed sampling was conducted in each university for humanities and social sciences (HSS) and science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) disciplines based on Becher’s classiﬁcation framework, with a preset 1:1 ratio. Due to the fact that the number of international students majoring in STEM was signiﬁcantly larger than that in HSS at some surveyed universities, and some HSS international students did not participate in the survey due to busy academic tasks, the ﬁnal sample consisted of 82 HSS students (37.44%) and 137 STEM students (62.56%). To correct for sample distribution bias, weighted least squares was employed in this study, with weights set according to",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,10,"the survey due to busy academic tasks, the ﬁnal sample consisted of 82 HSS students (37.44%) and 137 STEM students (62.56%). To correct for sample distribution bias, weighted least squares was employed in this study, with weights set according to the overall proportion of HSS and STEM international students at each university. After weighting, the HSS sample proportion was 48.72% and the STEM proportion was 51.28%, resulting in a more balanced sample distribution and ensuring result robustness. TABLE 1 Sources, revision basis, and example items of core constructs. Construct Number of Items Original Source/Revision Basis Key Item Example Performance Expectancy 4 (Du and Lv, 2024; Venkatesh et al., 2003) GAI can signiﬁcantly improve the quality of my non-native academic writing. Effort Expectancy 4 (Gazit et al., 2026; Venkatesh et al., 2003) I can easily learn to use GAI’s core functions. Facilitating Conditions 2 (Sergeeva et al., 2025; Venkatesh et al., 2003) My university provides a stable network environment to support my GAI use. Subjective Norm 4 (Ittefaq et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025a) Most of my peers use GAI",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,10,"Facilitating Conditions 2 (Sergeeva et al., 2025; Venkatesh et al., 2003) My university provides a stable network environment to support my GAI use. Subjective Norm 4 (Ittefaq et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025a) Most of my peers use GAI to complete learning tasks. Perceived Behavioral Control 4 (Ajzen, 1991; Falebita and Kok, 2025) When encountering difﬁculties in using GAI, I can obtain timely help. Perceived Ease of Use 5 (Chiu, 2025; Davis, 1989) Using GAI does not require excessive effort to learn operational methods. Perceived Usefulness 4 (Cho and Ofosu-Anim, 2025; Davis, 1989) Using GAI can reduce barriers in my cross-cultural learning. Attitude 5 (Liu, 2025; Zheng et al., 2024) I believe using GAI to assist academic learning is a wise choice. Adoption Intention 3 (Siu and White, 2025; Venkatesh et al., 2012) I will use GAI more frequently to complete academic tasks in the future. Perceived Intelligence 4 (Naidu, 2024; Singh et al., 2025) GAI can generate accurate and context- appropriate content that meets my academic needs. Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 10 frontiersin.org",178
