DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"Data collection was conducted from April to May 2025, a period avoiding the early-semester adaptation stage and the ﬁnal examination peak to reduce interference from temporal factors on usage behavior. A standardized research instrument was built via Wenjuanxing, and multiple quality control measures were implemented, including IP address restriction, device binding, and mobile phone veriﬁcation to prevent duplicate responses. Multiple screening criteria were established to eliminate invalid questionnaires: completion time less than 5 min, identical choices for 10 consecutive items, logically contradictory responses, and missing key information. A total of 300 questionnaires were distributed (100 in each region), with 219 valid questionnaires recovered, yielding a valid response rate of 73%. Rather than relying solely on the 10-times rule of path numbers, this study used multiple cross-validation methods to conﬁrm sample adequacy. At the basic principle level, following the minimum sample size standard for PLS-SEM proposed by Hair Jr et al., the model contained 9 latent variables and 21 paths, requiring a minimum sample size of 210 (10 times the number of paths), which was satisﬁed by the actual 219 samples",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"size standard for PLS-SEM proposed by Hair Jr et al., the model contained 9 latent variables and 21 paths, requiring a minimum sample size of 210 (10 times the number of paths), which was satisﬁed by the actual 219 samples (Hair et al., 2023). At the G*Power analysis level, with effect size f2 = 0.15, signiﬁcance level α=0.05, statistical power 1-β=0.95, and model degrees of freedom = 21, the calculated minimum sample size was 187. At the Monte Carlo simulation level, 1000 Bootstrap samples were run using SmartPLS 4.0 with minimum factor loading = 0.7 and R2 = 0.3; results showed that when sample size ≥ 200, the model parameter estimation bias was less than 5% with good convergence stability (see Table 2). Comprehensive veriﬁcation indicated that the 219 samples possessed sufﬁcient statistical power to support complex path tests including mediation and moderation. 4.2.2 Sample structure overview The demographic characteristics of respondents were evenly distributed (see Table 3): 120 males (54.79%) and 99 females (45.21%); regions of origin were dominated by Asia (94, 42.92%), Africa (43, 19.63%), and Europe (45,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"mediation and moderation. 4.2.2 Sample structure overview The demographic characteristics of respondents were evenly distributed (see Table 3): 120 males (54.79%) and 99 females (45.21%); regions of origin were dominated by Asia (94, 42.92%), Africa (43, 19.63%), and Europe (45, 20.55%); surveyed regions were evenly distributed across eastern (75), central (72), and western China (72), each accounting for more than 32%; institution types included Double First-Class universities (112, 51.14%), non-Double First-Class universities (76, 34.70%), and specialized institutions (31, 14.16%); GAI usage frequency was mainly 5–6 times per week (73, 33.33%) and 1–2 times per week (62, 28.31%). Control variable tests showed that Chinese proﬁciency had no statistically signiﬁcant effect on adoption intention (β = 0.087, p = 0.213) and was thus excluded from the ﬁnal model; regional variables showed weak correlation with core mechanisms and were not analyzed further. 4.3 Data analysis method and model selection rationale This study employed SmartPLS 4.0 for partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), strictly following the methodological framework proposed by Hair et al. (2019). Given that the model contained 10 latent variables and",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"Data analysis method and model selection rationale This study employed SmartPLS 4.0 for partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), strictly following the methodological framework proposed by Hair et al. (2019). Given that the model contained 10 latent variables and 41 measurement indicators, PLS-SEM was chosen as the core analytical method mainly due to its signiﬁcant advantages in handling complex causal networks and non-normally distributed data. This method is particularly suitable for multivariate exploratory analysis at the theory development stage, providing a robust statistical foundation for subsequent hypothesis testing. 4.4 Ethical statement This study strictly complied with the ethical guidelines of the Declaration of Helsinki. All research procedures were approved by the Science and Technology Ethics Committee of Qinghai University (Ethics Approval No.: PJ202501 -250). Before the survey, all respondents were clearly informed of the research purpose, data usage, anonymity protection policy, and voluntary nature of participation. Respondents signed informed consent forms prior to participation. Personal information of respondents was strictly protected during the survey. Questionnaire data were used solely for academic research without disclosing any personal privacy, and anonymized",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"protection policy, and voluntary nature of participation. Respondents signed informed consent forms prior to participation. Personal information of respondents was strictly protected during the survey. Questionnaire data were used solely for academic research without disclosing any personal privacy, and anonymized archiving was performed after data processing to ensure compliance with academic ethical requirements. 5 Empirical results This study employed SmartPLS 4.0 for partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), strictly following the methodological framework proposed by Hair et al. (2017). Given that the model contained 10 latent variables and 41 measurement indicators, PLS-SEM was chosen as the core analytical method mainly due to its signiﬁcant advantages in handling complex causal networks and non-normally distributed data. This method is particularly suitable for multivariate exploratory analysis at the theory development stage, providing a robust statistical foundation for subsequent hypothesis testing. Notably, this study adopted a cross-sectional, self-reported non- experimental design, which can only examine structural associations among variables but cannot conﬁrm strict causality. All path relationships are interpreted based on theoretical logic and statistical correlations rather than causal inferences. TABLE 2 Multiple",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,11,"study adopted a cross-sectional, self-reported non- experimental design, which can only examine structural associations among variables but cannot conﬁrm strict causality. All path relationships are interpreted based on theoretical logic and statistical correlations rather than causal inferences. TABLE 2 Multiple validation results of sample size calculation. Validation Method Core Parameter Settings Minimum Sample Size Requirement Met by Actual Sample Size? 10-times Path Number Rule Number of model paths = 21 210 Yes (219 > 210) G*Power Analysis (F-test) Effect size f2 = 0.15, α = 0.05, 1-β = 0.95, df = 21 187 Yes (219 > 187) Monte Carlo Simulation Factor loading ≥0.7, R2 = 0.3, 1000 bootstrap samples 200 Yes (219 > 200) Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 11 frontiersin.org",123
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,12,"5.1 Measurement model assessment Assessment of the measurement model is a critical step in structural equation modeling, with the core objective of ensuring the rationality and theoretical consistency of relationships between latent variables and observed indicators. This study systematically veriﬁed measurement quality from three dimensions: reliability assessment, convergent validity assessment, and discriminant validity assessment, to guarantee the scientiﬁcity and reliability of the measurement instrument. 5.1.1 Reliability assessment Reliability assessment was conducted to verify the internal consistency of the scale, mainly evaluated using Cronbach’s α and composite reliability (CR), with the criterion that both α and CR values should be no less than 0.7. As shown in Table 4, Cronbach’s α coefﬁcients for all core constructs ranged from 0.701 to 0.881, with perceived ease of use recording the highest α (0.881) and perceived intelligence the lowest (0.701), all meeting the critical threshold. Composite reliability (CR) values ranged from 0.729 to 0.883, consistent with the trend of α coefﬁcients: perceived ease of use had the highest CR (0.883) and facilitating conditions the lowest (0.729), both above the minimum requirement of 0.7. These",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,12,"threshold. Composite reliability (CR) values ranged from 0.729 to 0.883, consistent with the trend of α coefﬁcients: perceived ease of use had the highest CR (0.883) and facilitating conditions the lowest (0.729), both above the minimum requirement of 0.7. These results indicate that each construct in the scale possessed good internal consistency, the measurements were reliable, and no internal reliability deﬁciency was observed, thus supporting subsequent structural model assessment and hypothesis veriﬁcation. 5.1.2 Validity assessment Validity assessment focused on two core dimensions: convergent validity and discriminant validity. Convergent validity was evaluated using average variance extracted (AVE), TABLE 3 Demographic characteristics of respondents. Characteristic Dimension Speciﬁc Category Number of Respondents Percentage Remarks Gender Male 120 54.79% – Female 99 45.21% – Continent of Origin Africa 43 19.63% – Asia 94 42.92% – Europe 45 20.55% – North America 23 10.50% – Oceania 4 1.83% – South America 10 4.57% – Survey Region Eastern China 75 34.25% Covering 4 universities Central China 72 32.88% Covering 4 universities Western China 72 32.87% Covering 4 universities University Type Double First-Class Universities 112 51.14% 3",180
