DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,17,"corrected Bootstrap samples to clarify indirect paths through which external antecedent variables inﬂuence adoption intention; test results are presented in Table 11. Mediation effect tests strictly followed the three-step veriﬁcation logic of “total effect – direct effect – indirect effect”, judging the existence and type of mediation by effect signiﬁcance and conﬁdence intervals to ensure robust conclusions. Tests of total indirect effects revealed that among the six external antecedent variables, total indirect effects of performance expectancy, effort expectancy, perceived behavioral control, perceived intelligence, and subjective norms on adoption intention were all signiﬁcant (p < 0.05), while only the total indirect effect of facilitating conditions was insigniﬁcant (β=0.030, p = 0.131), indicating that most external variables can indirectly affect international students’ GAI adoption intention through mediating paths. Among them, the total indirect effect of performance expectancy was the strongest (β=0.232, p < 0.001), followed by subjective norms (β=0.134, p < 0.001) and perceived behavioral control (β=0.086, p < 0.001), while those of perceived intelligence (β=0.053, p = 0.016) and effort expectancy (β=0.075, p = 0.019) were relatively weak, consistent with the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,17,"< 0.001), followed by subjective norms (β=0.134, p < 0.001) and perceived behavioral control (β=0.086, p < 0.001), while those of perceived intelligence (β=0.053, p = 0.016) and effort expectancy (β=0.075, p = 0.019) were relatively weak, consistent with the direct effect strengths of core variables in path analysis. Speciﬁc indirect effect tests further revealed differentiated characteristics of mediating paths. The mediating role of perceived usefulness was mainly reﬂected in the inﬂuences of performance expectancy, perceived intelligence, and effort expectancy on adoption intention: the indirect effect of performance expectancy via perceived usefulness was signiﬁcant (β=0.231, p < 0.001) with no signiﬁcant direct effect, indicating that perceived usefulness plays a full mediating role between performance expectancy and adoption intention, verifying the chain path “performance expectancy → perceived usefulness → adoption intention”. The indirect effect of perceived intelligence via perceived usefulness was signiﬁcant (β = 0.052, p = 0.015) and, combined with its non-signiﬁcant direct effect on adoption intention, also exhibited full mediation. The indirect effect of effort expectancy via perceived usefulness was marginally signiﬁcant (β = 0.036, p = 0.086), suggesting",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,17,"signiﬁcant (β = 0.052, p = 0.015) and, combined with its non-signiﬁcant direct effect on adoption intention, also exhibited full mediation. The indirect effect of effort expectancy via perceived usefulness was marginally signiﬁcant (β = 0.036, p = 0.086), suggesting contextual dependence of this mediating path. The mediating role of attitude was concentrated in the inﬂuences of subjective norms and perceived behavioral control on adoption intention, both showing full mediation: the indirect effect of subjective norms via attitude was signiﬁcant (β = 0.134, p < 0.001), and that of perceived behavioral control via attitude was signiﬁcant (β = 0.086, p < 0.001), with neither having a direct path to adoption intention. This conﬁrms that social factors and individual perceived control must be transformed into adoption intention through affective tendencies, consistent with the driving role of affective identiﬁcation in technology acceptance within cross-cultural contexts. TABLE 9 Collinearity test (VIF) results. Pathway Name VIF R2 f2 Q2 Attitude -> Adoption Intention 1.807 0.445 0.083 0.298 Effort Expectancy -> Perceived Ease of Use 2.207 0.605 0.490 0.405 Effort Expectancy -> Perceived Usefulness 2.207",180
