DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,18,"Notably, the mediating role of perceived ease of use was unsupported: all speciﬁc indirect effects through perceived ease of use were insigniﬁcant (p > 0.15), consistent with the non- signiﬁcant direct effect of perceived ease of use on adoption intention in path analysis. This indicates that perceived ease of use plays neither a direct nor a mediating role in GAI adoption decisions, primarily because the output quality priorityfeature of GAI leads international students to focus more on technological utility and affective experience rather than operational convenience. In summary, mediation effect analysis identiﬁed three core indirect paths: “performance expectancy → perceived usefulness → adoption intention”, “perceived intelligence → perceived usefulness → adoption intention”, and “subjective norms/ perceived behavioral control → attitude → adoption intention”, further reﬁning the chain mechanism of “external drivers – cognitive/affective evaluation – behavioral intention” and providing solid support for subsequent moderating effect analysis and research conclusion extraction. 5.4 Moderation effect analysis Moderation effects are crucial for revealing the boundary conditions of relationships among core variables, and their analytical outcomes directly affect the comprehensiveness and rigor of research",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,18,"support for subsequent moderating effect analysis and research conclusion extraction. 5.4 Moderation effect analysis Moderation effects are crucial for revealing the boundary conditions of relationships among core variables, and their analytical outcomes directly affect the comprehensiveness and rigor of research conclusions. To ensure the scientiﬁcity and reliability of moderation effect analysis, this study adopted the multiple group analysis method (MICOM) in PLS-SEM to conduct systematic tests by dividing subgroups for two moderating variables — disciplinary background (STEM vs. HSS) and cultural values (individualism vs. collectivism). The testing procedure strictly followed the core steps of the MICOM approach, sequentially verifying conﬁgural invariance, metric invariance, and structural invariance, laying a solid foundation for accurate subsequent moderation effect analysis and avoiding result bias caused by inconsistent measurement structures or metric standards across subgroups. The three core dimensions of the MICOM test each have clear testing objectives and judgment criteria, which together constitute the prerequisites for moderation effect analysis. Among them, conﬁgural invariance, as the basic test, aims to conﬁrm that the corresponding relationships between measurement items and latent variables remain consistent across different",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,18,"clear testing objectives and judgment criteria, which together constitute the prerequisites for moderation effect analysis. Among them, conﬁgural invariance, as the basic test, aims to conﬁrm that the corresponding relationships between measurement items and latent variables remain consistent across different subgroups; that is, all subgroups adopt the same factor structure with no cross-loading items, which is the primary prerequisite for ensuring the validity of subsequent tests. Metric invariance veriﬁes that there are no signiﬁcant differences in factor loadings of measurement items corresponding to the same latent variable across subgroups, indicating that the measurement instrument possesses consistent measurement precision and validity in different subgroups. Structural invariance focuses on verifying that there are no signiﬁcant differences in correlation coefﬁcients of latent variables across subgroups, ensuring the stability of intrinsic associations among latent variables in different groupings. The test results (Table 12) showed that all subgroups fully satisﬁed the requirements of conﬁgural invariance, with each item stably assigned to its corresponding latent variable and no cross-loading items observed. In the metric invariance test, the average differences in factor loadings (Δλ) across subgroups were",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,18,"that all subgroups fully satisﬁed the requirements of conﬁgural invariance, with each item stably assigned to its corresponding latent variable and no cross-loading items observed. In the metric invariance test, the average differences in factor loadings (Δλ) across subgroups were all less than 0.05, with corresponding p-values greater than 0.05, indicating no TABLE 11 Mediating effect analysis results. Pathway Name Original sample STDEV T statistics P values Total Indirect Effects Effort Expectancy -> Adoption Intention 0.075 0.032 2.355 0.019 Facilitating Conditions -> Adoption Intention 0.030 0.020 1.512 0.131 Perceived Behavior Control -> Adoption Intention 0.086 0.021 4.081 0.000 Perceived Intelligence -> Adoption Intention 0.053 0.022 2.415 0.016 Performance Expectancy -> Adoption Intention 0.232 0.038 6.088 0.000 Subjective Norms -> Adoption Intention 0.134 0.028 4.784 0.000 Speciﬁc Indirect Effects Perceived Intelligence -> Perceived Ease of Use -> Adoption Intention 0.000 0.004 0.110 0.912 Performance Expectancy -> Perceived Usefulness -> Adoption Intention 0.231 0.038 6.030 0.000 Perceived Behavior Control -> Attitude -> Adoption Intention 0.086 0.021 4.081 0.000 Performance Expectancy -> Perceived Ease of Use -> Adoption Intention 0.001 0.004 0.258 0.796",180
