DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"signiﬁcant differences in item factor loadings across subgroups. In the structural invariance test, the average differences in latent variable correlation coefﬁcients (Δr) across subgroups were also all less than 0.05, with p-values greater than 0.05, demonstrating that the relationships among latent variables remained stable across subgroups. The above results fully meet the full invariance criteria of the MICOM method, sufﬁciently proving that the prerequisites for the current moderation effect analysis are satisﬁed, and the subsequent analytical results possess high reliability and persuasiveness. On the basis of completing the MICOM test and conﬁrming subgroup invariance, this study further adopted 5,000 bias- corrected Bootstrap samples to test the moderation effects of disciplinary background and cultural values on three core paths respectively (see Table 13): “perceived usefulness → adoption intention, attitude → adoption intention”, and “perceived ease of use → adoption intention”. The Bootstrap sampling method can effectively avoid sample distribution bias, construct conﬁdence intervals through repeated sampling, improve the accuracy of moderation effect tests, and ensure that judgments on moderation effects of each path are statistically signiﬁcant. This test focused on the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"Bootstrap sampling method can effectively avoid sample distribution bias, construct conﬁdence intervals through repeated sampling, improve the accuracy of moderation effect tests, and ensure that judgments on moderation effects of each path are statistically signiﬁcant. This test focused on the original sample values, standard deviations, t-statistics, and p-values of moderation effects, where p < 0.05 indicates a signiﬁcant moderation effect, 0.05 ≤ p < 0.10 indicates a marginally signiﬁcant moderation effect, and p ≥ 0.10 indicates an insigniﬁcant moderation effect. Detailed test results are shown in the table below. Combined with the above test results, the moderation effect hypotheses H10a and H10b proposed in this study were veriﬁed, and the ﬁnal conclusion is partially supported. Speciﬁcally, the moderating effect of disciplinary background on the three core paths showed differentiated characteristics: its moderating effects on the paths of “perceived usefulness → adoption intention” and “attitude → adoption intention” were both insigniﬁcant (p = 0.419 and 0.504 respectively, both greater than 0.10), and only a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect existed on the path of “perceived ease of use → adoption intention”",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"adoption intention” and “attitude → adoption intention” were both insigniﬁcant (p = 0.419 and 0.504 respectively, both greater than 0.10), and only a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect existed on the path of “perceived ease of use → adoption intention” (β = −0.070, p = 0.085). This implies that the strength of the positive inﬂuence of perceived ease of use on adoption intention differs among international students of different disciplinary backgrounds, speciﬁcally showing that this positive inﬂuence is weaker among STEM international students than among HSS students. In-depth analysis suggests that the main reason lies in the core characteristic of GAI — output quality priority. GAI can provide high-quality auxiliary support for both professional calculation and data processing required by STEM disciplines and text creation and viewpoint extraction required by HSS disciplines. This universal advantage weakens differences in adoption intention among international students of different disciplinary backgrounds, with only slight disciplinary differences emerging in the inﬂuence of perceived ease of use. The moderating effect of cultural values also showed selective characteristics: its moderating effects on the paths of “perceived usefulness",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"among international students of different disciplinary backgrounds, with only slight disciplinary differences emerging in the inﬂuence of perceived ease of use. The moderating effect of cultural values also showed selective characteristics: its moderating effects on the paths of “perceived usefulness → adoption intention” and “perceived ease of use → adoption intention” were both insigniﬁcant (p = 0.694 and 0.745 respectively, both greater than 0.10), and only a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect existed on the path of “attitude → adoption intention” (β = −0.111, p = 0.077), indicating that the positive driving effect of attitude on adoption intention differs among international students with different cultural values, with the driving effect of attitude being weaker among collectivist-oriented students than among individualist- oriented students. The core reason for this phenomenon is inferred to be that international students studying in China have long been in a cross-cultural adaptation environment, where their native cultural values collide and integrate with local Chinese culture, gradually blurring the boundary between collectivism and individualism, thereby weakening the moderating effect of cultural values on the relationship between attitude and",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"been in a cross-cultural adaptation environment, where their native cultural values collide and integrate with local Chinese culture, gradually blurring the boundary between collectivism and individualism, thereby weakening the moderating effect of cultural values on the relationship between attitude and adoption intention, with only marginally signiﬁcant differences observed. Notably, the driving effect of perceived usefulness on adoption intention was not signiﬁcantly inﬂuenced by either disciplinary background or cultural values, indicating that regardless of discipline or cultural orientation, perceived usefulness is a core common factor affecting international students’ GAI adoption intention with strong universality, which is also consistent with the test results of the core model in this study. 6 Discussion and conclusions Based on the empirical results presented above, 9 of the 13 main hypotheses in this study were supported (H1b, H2a, H2b, H3a, H4b, H5, H6, H8, H9) while four remained unsupported (H1a, H3b, H4a, H7), with both moderation hypotheses H10a and H10b receiving partial support. Integrating the technological characteristics of GAI, the cross-cultural learning context of international students, the design of measurement instruments, and theoretical logic, this section",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"four remained unsupported (H1a, H3b, H4a, H7), with both moderation hypotheses H10a and H10b receiving partial support. Integrating the technological characteristics of GAI, the cross-cultural learning context of international students, the design of measurement instruments, and theoretical logic, this section conducts an in- depth analysis of the reasons behind unsupported hypotheses, extracts theoretical implications, and enhances the completeness and depth of the research. The lack of support for H1a, which proposed a relationship between performance expectancy and perceived ease of use, stems from the black-box nature of GAI that separates international students’ evaluation of outcomes from their experience of processes. Empirical data indicate the path coefﬁcient from performance expectancy to perceived usefulness reached 0.569 (p < 0.001), demonstrating that international students’ performance expectancy for GAI centers on output quality speciﬁcally tailored to practical academic needs such as non-native academic writing reﬁnement, cross-cultural knowledge interpretation, and adaptation to Chinese academic norms. Perceived ease of use by contrast focuses on operational simplicity, evaluating procedural experiences including prompt engineering difﬁculty, interface complexity, and functional learning costs, creating an inherent distinction between the two",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,19,"reﬁnement, cross-cultural knowledge interpretation, and adaptation to Chinese academic norms. Perceived ease of use by contrast focuses on operational simplicity, evaluating procedural experiences including prompt engineering difﬁculty, interface complexity, and functional learning costs, creating an inherent distinction between the two dimensions that results in extremely low correlation (path coefﬁcient = 0.017, p = 0.748). Furthermore, the theoretically proposed mechanism where outcome value offsets process costs was signiﬁcantly weakened among international students by language barriers and heterogeneous digital literacy across countries; constrained by non-native expression, even students with high performance expectancy for GAI must invest extra effort in prompt tuning to ensure output accuracy and appropriateness, preventing them from subjectively reducing perceived operational complexity and strengthening ease-of-use perceptions. Revisions to performance expectancy items during Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 19 frontiersin.org",132
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,20,"the pretest that emphasized utility expectancy further weakened its association with perceived ease of use as a procedural experience, ultimately leading to an insigniﬁcant path. H3b, positing a link between facilitating conditions and perceived usefulness, was unsupported due to a mismatch between the supportive nature of facilitating conditions and the core needs of international students using GAI. As deﬁned earlier, facilitating conditions represent external safeguards for technology use that directly enhance perceived ease of use (path coefﬁcient = 0.150, p = 0.001) rather than directly strengthening perceptions of core technological utility. International students’ primary motivation for using GAI is to solve critical pain points in cross-cultural academic settings such as language barriers, divergent academic norms, and difﬁculties in literature analysis, yet facilitating conditions only provide basic infrastructure for usage and cannot directly improve GAI’s ability to address these core challenges, failing to break the bottleneck in translating operational support into utility perception. A stable network for instance only ensures unobstructed GAI access but cannot enhance the academic appropriateness and accuracy of outputs while multilingual support only resolves operational hurdles without",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,20,"failing to break the bottleneck in translating operational support into utility perception. A stable network for instance only ensures unobstructed GAI access but cannot enhance the academic appropriateness and accuracy of outputs while multilingual support only resolves operational hurdles without optimizing core GAI functionality, thus exerting no direct positive effect on perceived usefulness (path coefﬁcient = 0.052, p = 0.239). Additionally, the facilitating conditions construct retained only two items covering resource accessibility and organizational support, excluding dimensions such as targeted training that could facilitate translation from operational support to utility perception and further weakening its potential inﬂuence on perceived usefulness. The lack of support for H4a, which proposed a relationship between perceived intelligence and perceived ease of use, arises from the fundamental distinction between the output-oriented nature of perceived intelligence and the operation-oriented nature of perceived ease of use. As a GAI-speciﬁc construct, perceived intelligence reﬂects international students’ evaluation of output accuracy, creativity, and scenario adaptability (path coefﬁcient = 0.128, p = 0.004) while perceived ease of use focuses on operational simplicity as independent evaluation dimensions. Although theoretically perceived intelligence",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,20,"a GAI-speciﬁc construct, perceived intelligence reﬂects international students’ evaluation of output accuracy, creativity, and scenario adaptability (path coefﬁcient = 0.128, p = 0.004) while perceived ease of use focuses on operational simplicity as independent evaluation dimensions. Although theoretically perceived intelligence was expected to indirectly improve ease of use by reducing prompt-tuning costs, empirical results show a path coefﬁcient of only 0.007 (p = 0.890) linked to GAI’s black-box operation — students cannot perceive the internal logic of intelligent GAI outputs and even when acknowledging output quality must invest effort in prompt tuning to match personal needs, preventing high perceived intelligence from reducing operational complexity. Wide disparities in digital literacy among international students also mean some remain unfamiliar with GAI operational logic despite high-quality outputs, further weakening the effect of perceived intelligence on perceived ease of use. H7, suggesting a connection between perceived ease of use and adoption intention, was unsupported in alignment with GAI’s TABLE 12 Results table of measurement invariance test (MICOM). Test Step Grouping Variable Test Indicator Test Result Criterion Conﬁgural Invariance Disciplinary Background (STEM/ HSS) Consistency of",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,20,"between perceived ease of use and adoption intention, was unsupported in alignment with GAI’s TABLE 12 Results table of measurement invariance test (MICOM). Test Step Grouping Variable Test Indicator Test Result Criterion Conﬁgural Invariance Disciplinary Background (STEM/ HSS) Consistency of factor structure All items load on the intended latent variables; no cross-loadings Uniform factor structure Cultural Values (Individualism/ Collectivism) Consistency of factor structure All items load on the intended latent variables; no cross-loadings Uniform factor structure Metric Invariance Disciplinary Background (STEM/ HSS) Mean difference in factor loadings (Δλ) Δλ = 0.035, p = 0.192 Δλ < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Cultural Values (Individualism/ Collectivism) Mean difference in factor loadings (Δλ) Δλ = 0.042, p = 0.167 Δλ < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Structural Invariance Disciplinary Background (STEM/ HSS) Mean difference in latent variable correlations (Δr) Δr = 0.041, p = 0.173 Δr < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Cultural Values (Individualism/ Collectivism) Mean difference in latent variable correlations (Δr) Δr = 0.047, p = 0.121 Δr < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Δλ denotes the average difference in",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,20,"p = 0.173 Δr < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Cultural Values (Individualism/ Collectivism) Mean difference in latent variable correlations (Δr) Δr = 0.047, p = 0.121 Δr < 0.05 and p > 0.05 Δλ denotes the average difference in factor loadings across subgroups; Δr denotes the average difference in latent variable correlation coefﬁcients across subgroups. p-values were calculated using 5000 Bootstrap samples; p > 0.05 indicates no signiﬁcant difference, ensuring the statistical reliability of test results and reducing the impact of sampling errors. TABLE 13 Moderation analysis. Pathway Name and Moderators Original sample STDEV T statistics P values Discipline*Perceived Usefulness -> Adoption Intention −0.037 0.045 0.809 0.419 Discipline*Attitude -> Adoption Intention 0.024 0.036 0.669 0.504 Discipline*Perceived Ease of Use -> Adoption Intention −0.070 0.041 1.72 0.085 Cultural Values*Perceived Usefulness -> Adoption Intention −0.025 0.063 0.393 0.694 Cultural Values*Attitude -> Adoption Intention −0.111 0.063 1.771 0.077 Cultural Values*Perceived Ease of Use -> Adoption Intention 0.014 0.044 0.326 0.745 Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 20 frontiersin.org",167
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"output-quality-prioritized technological characteristics and international students’ utility-oriented usage demands. Path analysis shows perceived usefulness strongly predicts adoption intention (β = 0.406, p < 0.001) while perceived ease of use shows no signiﬁcant effect (β = 0.060, p = 0.157), indicating international students base GAI adoption decisions on actual utility rather than operational experience. For international students, the core value of GAI lies in resolving key cross- cultural academic adaptation challenges, and as long as they perceive high usefulness such as effective non-native writing correction and alignment with Chinese academic norms, they maintain strong adoption intention despite moderate operational complexity including prompt engineering and functional learning costs. Mediation analysis further conﬁrmed no signiﬁcant mediating role of perceived ease of use with all indirect effects through this construct being insigniﬁcant, reinforcing that perceived ease of use plays neither a direct nor mediating role in GAI adoption decisions. This result echoes Chiu’s ﬁndings on GAI adoption among researchers (Chiu, 2025), highlighting the dominance of technological utility over operational experience in academic contexts. Important theoretical and practical implications emerge from these unsupported hypotheses. Theoretically,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"role in GAI adoption decisions. This result echoes Chiu’s ﬁndings on GAI adoption among researchers (Chiu, 2025), highlighting the dominance of technological utility over operational experience in academic contexts. Important theoretical and practical implications emerge from these unsupported hypotheses. Theoretically, they reveal contextual limitations of classic TAMs in cross-cultural education and emerging AI scenarios, as general paths such as performance expectancy to perceived ease of use and facilitating conditions to perceived usefulness from traditional TAM/UTAUT require revision in international students’ GAI adoption contexts to reﬂect technological traits and group particularities, underscoring the importance of contextualized theoretical integration. Practically, the rejection of H7 suggests universities should prioritize improving GAI utility over mere operational simplicity when promoting GAI, while the rejection of H3b indicates optimizing facilitating conditions should be combined with utility enhancement — for instance pairing basic technical support with targeted training to help international students translate operational convenience into tangible academic beneﬁts and thereby enhance the practical value of the research. 6.2 Core ﬁndings and theoretical contributions Based on empirical results, this study identiﬁes four core conclusions that advance the",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"training to help international students translate operational convenience into tangible academic beneﬁts and thereby enhance the practical value of the research. 6.2 Core ﬁndings and theoretical contributions Based on empirical results, this study identiﬁes four core conclusions that advance the theoretical system of technology acceptance in cross-cultural contexts and drive the extension and innovation of classic technology acceptance models. This research constructs and validates an integrated model of external drivers – cognitive/ affective evaluation – behavioral intention, breaking the variable integration paradigm of traditional TAM/ UTAUT. It incorporates performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and facilitating conditions from UTAUT alongside the GAI-speciﬁc construct perceived intelligence as antecedents, perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use from TAM as cognitive evaluation variables, and attitude as an affective evaluation variable to establish a GAI adoption model tailored to international students. Empirical results demonstrate that performance expectancy (β = 0.569, p < 0.001) and perceived intelligence (β = 0.128, p = 0.004) signiﬁcantly and positively predict perceived usefulness, while effort expectancy (β = 0.654, p < 0.001) and facilitating conditions (β = 0.150, p = 0.001)",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"expectancy (β = 0.569, p < 0.001) and perceived intelligence (β = 0.128, p = 0.004) signiﬁcantly and positively predict perceived usefulness, while effort expectancy (β = 0.654, p < 0.001) and facilitating conditions (β = 0.150, p = 0.001) signiﬁcantly and positively predict perceived ease of use. Subjective norms (β = 0.463, p < 0.001) and perceived behavioral control (β = 0.299, p < 0.001) signiﬁcantly and positively predict attitude, with perceived usefulness (β = 0.406, p < 0.001) and attitude (β = 0.289, p < 0.001) also signiﬁcantly and positively predicting adoption intention. These ﬁndings reﬁne the chain mechanism of technology acceptance, address limitations of traditional models in neglecting AI-speciﬁc features and cross-cultural affective factors, and offer a new perspective for applying technology acceptance theory to emerging technologies and special populations. The introduction of the GAI-speciﬁc construct perceived intelligence remedies the neglect of core AI characteristics in traditional TAMs. Empirical results conﬁrm that perceived intelligence signiﬁcantly and positively predicts perceived usefulness (β = 0.128, p = 0.004) and exerts a full mediating effect on adoption intention via perceived",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"intelligence remedies the neglect of core AI characteristics in traditional TAMs. Empirical results conﬁrm that perceived intelligence signiﬁcantly and positively predicts perceived usefulness (β = 0.128, p = 0.004) and exerts a full mediating effect on adoption intention via perceived usefulness (β = 0.052, p = 0.015), identifying it as an important antecedent of international students’ GAI adoption. This innovation transcends the traditional view of AI as an efﬁciency-oriented tool, highlights GAI’s core traits of intelligent output and scenario adaptation, enriches the construct system of TAMs, provides a new construct reference for AI context technology acceptance research, and validates the unique path “perceived intelligence → perceived usefulness → adoption intention”. This study reveals differentiated mediation patterns and reﬁnes the dual cognitive–affective mediation mechanism. Mediation analysis shows perceived usefulness and attitude play key but distinct mediating roles: perceived usefulness fully mediates the effects of performance expectancy and perceived intelligence on adoption intention, with performance expectancy’s indirect effect being the strongest (β = 0.231, p < 0.001), while attitude fully mediates the effects of subjective norms and perceived behavioral control on adoption",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"the effects of performance expectancy and perceived intelligence on adoption intention, with performance expectancy’s indirect effect being the strongest (β = 0.231, p < 0.001), while attitude fully mediates the effects of subjective norms and perceived behavioral control on adoption intention, with subjective norms’ indirect effect (β = 0.134, p < 0.001) exceeding that of perceived behavioral control (β = 0.086, p < 0.001). These ﬁndings revise traditional TAM’s oversimpliﬁed treatment of cognitive–affective translation, conﬁrm affective factors’ independent driving role in cross- cultural contexts, enrich technology acceptance mediation research, and highlight the unique pathway “social identiﬁcation → affective formation → behavioral intention” among international students. This study also clariﬁes the marginal moderating effects of disciplinary background and cultural values, uncovering technology acceptance boundary conditions. Moderation analysis reveals disciplinary background only exerts a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect on the path perceived ease of use → adoption intention (β = −0.070, p = 0.085), with the positive effect weaker among STEM than HSS students, aligning with Becher’s disciplinary classiﬁcation cognitive differences. Cultural values only exert a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,21,"perceived ease of use → adoption intention (β = −0.070, p = 0.085), with the positive effect weaker among STEM than HSS students, aligning with Becher’s disciplinary classiﬁcation cognitive differences. Cultural values only exert a marginally signiﬁcant negative moderating effect on the path attitude → adoption intention (β = −0.111, p = 0.077), with attitude-driven intention weaker among collectivist-oriented students due to blurred cross- cultural individualism-collectivism boundaries. These ﬁndings address traditional technology acceptance research’s neglect of Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 21 frontiersin.org",85
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"boundary conditions and provide an important basis for constructing contextualized models. 6.3 Practical implications Drawing on core ﬁndings and addressing key pain points and path characteristics in international students’ GAI adoption, this study proposes a four-dimensional practical strategy encompassing AI function adaptation, cross-cultural guidance, disciplinary customization, and trust cultivation to help universities precisely align with international students’ technological needs, enhance GAI educational efﬁcacy, and deepen the integration of GAI and higher education internationalization. Enhancing perceived usefulness and optimizing GAI functional adaptation should be prioritized. Focused on the core path of performance expectancy to perceived usefulness to adoption intention, universities should collaborate with technology developers to develop GAI functional modules tailored to international students’ cross-cultural academic scenarios, emphasizing non-native writing correction, multilingual literature analysis, alignment with Chinese academic norms, and cross-cultural knowledge interpretation to improve output accuracy and scenario appropriateness. Leveraging the mechanism of perceived intelligence, universities should also optimize GAI’s semantic understanding and cross- modal interaction to reduce international students’ prompt- tuning costs and indirectly strengthen perceived usefulness. Cross-cultural guidance should be strengthened to establish a norm–attitude cultivation system. Targeting",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"the mechanism of perceived intelligence, universities should also optimize GAI’s semantic understanding and cross- modal interaction to reduce international students’ prompt- tuning costs and indirectly strengthen perceived usefulness. Cross-cultural guidance should be strengthened to establish a norm–attitude cultivation system. Targeting the paths of subjective norms to attitude to adoption intention and perceived behavioral control to attitude to adoption intention, universities should construct a dual-layer normative system led by instructors and driven by peers. Instructors guide proper GAI use through academic workshops and case demonstrations to share applied experience in academic learning, while peer exchange platforms within international student communities reinforce the positive shaping of attitude through group identiﬁcation. Multilingual operation guides, stepwise training courses, and real-time technical support should also be provided to enhance technological mastery, alleviate technology anxiety, strengthen perceived behavioral control, and foster positive usage attitudes. Disciplinary customization should be implemented to meet the needs of students in different ﬁelds. Guided by the marginal moderating effect of disciplinary background, professional software integration plugins should be developed for STEM international students to streamline GAI workﬂows and lower operational barriers",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"should be implemented to meet the needs of students in different ﬁelds. Guided by the marginal moderating effect of disciplinary background, professional software integration plugins should be developed for STEM international students to streamline GAI workﬂows and lower operational barriers for specialized tasks, while GAI functions related to content creativity, source tracing, and critical thinking instruction should be enhanced for HSS students to support text creation and in-depth inquiry, with appropriate improvements to operational simplicity to reduce cognitive load. Technological trust should be cultivated to alleviate cross- cultural usage concerns; universities should establish GAI usage norms and quality evaluation systems to guide responsible use and prevent academic misconduct, while case demonstrations and expert interpretations boost trust in GAI, strengthen attitude-driven adoption intention, and facilitate the translation of intention into actual usage behavior. 6.4 Research limitations and future directions Despite theoretically and practically valuable ﬁndings derived from rigorous research design and empirical analysis, this study has several limitations that require attention in future investigations. The sample structure exhibits certain bias: although weighted least squares adjusted the distribution (post-weighting HSS = 48.72%,",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"and practically valuable ﬁndings derived from rigorous research design and empirical analysis, this study has several limitations that require attention in future investigations. The sample structure exhibits certain bias: although weighted least squares adjusted the distribution (post-weighting HSS = 48.72%, STEM = 51.28%), the original sample included only 37.44% HSS students and 62.56% STEM students, potentially undermining the statistical stability of disciplinary moderation effects. The sample covers 12 universities across eastern, central, and western China but excludes institutions in Hong Kong, Macao, and Taiwan, and its relatively modest size (219) – while meeting PLS-SEM requirements – may limit conclusion generalizability. The cross-sectional, self-reported non-experimental design only reveals structural variable associations without verifying causal temporal order; even though Harman’s single-factor test, full collinearity VIF test, and marker variable method ruled out severe common method bias, cross-sectional data collection still carries potential bias. Construct measurement is incomplete: the facilitating conditions construct, with only two items covering resource accessibility and organizational support, fails to capture dimensions such as training and equipment support, reducing measurement comprehensiveness, while perceived intelligence, adapted from mature scales, does",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"bias. Construct measurement is incomplete: the facilitating conditions construct, with only two items covering resource accessibility and organizational support, fails to capture dimensions such as training and equipment support, reducing measurement comprehensiveness, while perceived intelligence, adapted from mature scales, does not distinguish perceptions across different GAI platforms, lowering measurement precision. Variable inclusion is insufﬁcient, as key factors like digital literacy, academic pressure, and technology anxiety are excluded, and only adoption intention – not actual usage behavior – is measured, leaving the intention–behavior translation mechanism unexplored; cultural values are limited to individualism/collectivism, overlooking other dimensions (e.g., power distance, uncertainty avoidance). Moderation analysis is underdeveloped, focusing only on disciplinary background and cultural values’ individual moderating roles without exploring their interactive effects or adoption mechanism differences across students with varying Chinese proﬁciency or country of origin. Future research should address these limitations by expanding and optimizing the sample, adopting longitudinal or experimental designs, reﬁning construct measurement, extending the variable system, and deepening moderation analysis. In summary, this study enriches technology acceptance theory’s contextual application, ﬁlls cross-cultural GAI adoption research gaps, and supports universities",180
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,22,"expanding and optimizing the sample, adopting longitudinal or experimental designs, reﬁning construct measurement, extending the variable system, and deepening moderation analysis. In summary, this study enriches technology acceptance theory’s contextual application, ﬁlls cross-cultural GAI adoption research gaps, and supports universities in optimizing GAI strategies, with future investigations needed to further explore adoption mechanisms and provide precise guidance for AI-supported cross-cultural teaching. Data availability statement The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article/Supplementary Material, further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Ethics statement Ethical review and approval was not required for the study on human participants in accordance with the local legislation and Fu and Cao 10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616 Frontiers in Education 22 frontiersin.org",117
DOC_038,NEW22.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1833616,23,"institutional requirements. Written informed consent from the participants was not required to participate in this study in accordance with the national legislation and the institutional requirements. Author contributions GF: Visualization, Funding acquisition, Project administration, Validation, Writing – review & editing, Data curation, Writing – original draft, Investigation, Conceptualization, Methodology. KC: Data curation, Visualization, Formal analysis, Resources, Validation, Project administration, Writing – review & editing, Investigation, Methodology, Software, Writing – original draft, Conceptualization, Supervision. Funding The author(s) declared that ﬁnancial support was not received for this work and/or its publication. Acknowledgments We would like to express our gratitude to editors and reviewers for their extraordinarily helpful comments. Conﬂict of interest The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or ﬁnancial relationships that could be construed as a potential conﬂict of interest. Generative AI statement The author(s) declared that generative AI was not used in the creation of this manuscript. Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside ﬁgures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artiﬁcial intelligence and reasonable efforts have",180
