DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,"AI-based cognitive offloading by students in higher education in China Yali Jiang * , Haiping Chen Department of Psychology, Beijing Normal University, 19 Xinjiekou Outer Street, Haidian District, Beijing, 100875, China A R T I C L E I N F O Keywords: Cognitive offloading Perceived value of AI AI literacy Value-based decision-making Human AI collaboration Higher education A B S T R A C T Artificial intelligence (AI) tools, such as ChatGPT, are becoming increasingly embedded in higher education for assisting students in tasks ranging from information retrieval to essay writing and problem solving in China. Although such tools provide powerful cognitive support, the growing dependence on them raises concerns on the types of cognitive tasks students choose to offload to AI tools and the conditions under which such offloading occurs. Drawing on a value-based decision-making framework, this work investigated the factors influencing AI- based cognitive offloading by university students. Two sequential studies were conducted. Study 1 developed and validated a scale that distinguishes between AI-based lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading. Three-sample analyses indicated that the scale exhibited robust",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,"investigated the factors influencing AI- based cognitive offloading by university students. Two sequential studies were conducted. Study 1 developed and validated a scale that distinguishes between AI-based lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading. Three-sample analyses indicated that the scale exhibited robust psychometric properties. Study 2 tested the relationship be­ tween the perceived value of AI, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and both types of AI-based cognitive offloading by analyzing the behavior of 667 university students. All data were collected in mainland China. Results indicate that the perceived value of AI is positively associated with both types of cognitive off­ loading through AI-usage capability. This indirect association was conditioned by the AI evaluation capability: it became nonsignificant for lower-order offloading under a high evaluation capability but remained significant for higher-order offloading. These findings highlight the conditional nature of AI-based cognitive offloading in higher education. Moreover, they extend the cognitive offloading theory from tool-based to human AI collab­ orative contexts, offering theoretical and practical insights for fostering reflective and value-driven human AI interaction in learning environments. The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI)",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,"higher education. Moreover, they extend the cognitive offloading theory from tool-based to human AI collab­ orative contexts, offering theoretical and practical insights for fostering reflective and value-driven human AI interaction in learning environments. The rapid development of artificial intelligence (AI) has profoundly reshaped higher education. Recent surveys have indicated that more than three-quarters of university students worldwide use AI tools such as ChatGPT, Grammarly, or Copilot to support academic work (Al Zaidy, 2024; Lund et al., 2025). In China, policy initiatives such as Education Modernization 2035 have further accelerated the integration of AI into teaching, learning, and research (CPC Central Committee, State Council, 2019). Consequently, AI has become an integral component of students' academic practices across disciplines. A growing body of evidence sug­ gests that AI may enhance learning performance by providing person­ alized feedback, supporting self-regulated learning, and fostering motivation (Khan et al., 2025). However, whether AI contributes to the development of students higher-order cognitive abilities remains debatable. While some studies indicate that AI can promote critical thinking and creativity when used as a scaffold (Benvenuti et al., 2023),",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,"motivation (Khan et al., 2025). However, whether AI contributes to the development of students higher-order cognitive abilities remains debatable. While some studies indicate that AI can promote critical thinking and creativity when used as a scaffold (Benvenuti et al., 2023), others warn that overreliance on AI may reduce cognitive engagement and hinder the acquisition of domain knowledge (Pikhart & Al-Obaydi, 2025). Neurocognitive evidence also suggests that excessive AI assistance may weaken neural activation during learning tasks (Kosmyna et al., 2025a). These findings highlight the need to examine the ways by which students use AI for cognitive offloading. Against this backdrop, the Chinese higher-education context serves primarily as the empirical setting, in which the present scale and model were developed and tested, rather than as a defining component of the construct. This setting is nevertheless meaningful because students AI use in China is likely to be shaped, at least in part, by local policy ini­ tiatives and research-oriented academic practices (CPC Central Com­ mittee, State Council, 2019). Accordingly, this study does not treat its findings as universally generalizable across all higher-education",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,"China is likely to be shaped, at least in part, by local policy ini­ tiatives and research-oriented academic practices (CPC Central Com­ mittee, State Council, 2019). Accordingly, this study does not treat its findings as universally generalizable across all higher-education sys­ tems. Instead, it offers a context-specific scale development and initial model test conducted in China, serving as a basis for future validation in other national and institutional settings. This study had a primary objective and a secondary objective. The primary objective was to develop and initially validate the AI-based cognitive offloading (AIBCO) scale for research-related learning and academic activities in higher education. Grounded in Bloom's Revised * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: 201321060046@mail.bnu.edu.cn (Y. Jiang), chenhaiping@bnu.edu.cn (H. Chen). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Computers in Human Behavior: Artificial Humans journal homepage: www.journals.elsevier.com/computers-in-human-behavior-artificial-humans https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327 Received 26 February 2026; Received in revised form 30 May 2026; Accepted 31 May 2026 Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 Available online 2 June 2026 2949-8821/© 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,1,form 30 May 2026; Accepted 31 May 2026 Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 Available online 2 June 2026 2949-8821/© 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by- nc-nd/4.0/ ).,45
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"Taxonomy (BRT), the scale captures learners' tendency to offload lower- and higher-order cognitive tasks to AI. The secondary objective was to provide theory-informed supporting evidence for the AIBCO scale by examining the relationship between the perceived AI value, AI usage capability, and AI evaluation capability and the lower- and higher-order AIBCO. Accordingly, the main contribution of this study lies not in introducing AIBCO as an entirely new phenomenon, but in offering a more differentiated, scale-based operationalization of lower- and higher-order AIBCO in research-oriented higher-education contexts. 1. Literature review 1.1. AI-based cognitive offloading Cognitive offloading is commonly defined as the use of external ac­ tions or tools (e.g., notes, diagrams, and digital devices) to reduce the internal cognitive demands of encoding, storing, transforming, and retrieving information, or controlling behavior (Risko & Gilbert, 2016). From this perspective, cognitive offloading is a meta-cognitively regu­ lated costbenefit strategy, whereby individuals shift parts of their cognitive work to external tools to save effort, time, or limited working-memory resources (Prachi Pandey et al., 2020). A more recent work has further emphasized that offloading decisions are value-based,",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"regu­ lated costbenefit strategy, whereby individuals shift parts of their cognitive work to external tools to save effort, time, or limited working-memory resources (Prachi Pandey et al., 2020). A more recent work has further emphasized that offloading decisions are value-based, highlighting the intentional and (boundedly) rational nature of deciding which cognitive skills to offload and which to retain internally (Gilbert, 2024), rather than viewing offloading as mere passive reliance on external resources. AI systems can likewise be understood as powerful cognitive offloading tools (Grinschgl & Neubauer, 2022), because they can support not only information retrieval and storage, but also expla­ nation, organization, evaluation, and content generation (Gerlich, 2025). However, although prior research has shown that AI use can shape cognition and reduce cognitive load (Messeri & Crockett, 2024), it has not clearly conceptualized AIBCO as a distinct construct. Herein, AIBCO refers specifically to the extent to which learners use AI during task execution to take over cognitive tasks that they could, in principle, perform themselves, thereby reducing their own cognitive effort. Although AIBCO is related to other forms of AI-supported",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"refers specifically to the extent to which learners use AI during task execution to take over cognitive tasks that they could, in principle, perform themselves, thereby reducing their own cognitive effort. Although AIBCO is related to other forms of AI-supported task per­ formance, it should not be treated as interchangeable with them. In particular, AIBCO differs from delegation, AI-assisted scaffolding, and AI-mediated collaboration. Delegation emphasizes the transfer of task execution, judgment, or functional responsibility to AI systems, whereas the offloading construct focuses specifically on reducing learners' own cognitive effort during task execution (Fügener et al., 2022). AI-assisted scaffolding refers to the types of support intended to guide learners' thinking and foster capability development, rather than simply taking over cognitive work on their behalf (Dhillon et al., 2024; Pea, 2004). AI-mediated collaboration, in turn, emphasizes sustained interdepen­ dence, shared goals, and dynamic coordination between humans and AI (McGrath et al., 2025). These forms of humanAI interaction may overlap with cognitive offloading in practice, but they are analytically distinct. These distinctions help clarify the specific focus of this study on learners use",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"dynamic coordination between humans and AI (McGrath et al., 2025). These forms of humanAI interaction may overlap with cognitive offloading in practice, but they are analytically distinct. These distinctions help clarify the specific focus of this study on learners use of AI to reduce their own cognitive effort during task execution. Therefore, to fully understand this construct, examining the extent to which learners offload cognitive processes associated with different levels of cognitive demand to AI during learning is necessary. To differentiate the aforementioned levels of AIBCO, this study uti­ lized BRT as an analytical framework. According to BRT, cognitive processes can be divided into lower-order levels, including remem­ bering, understanding, and applying, and higher-order levels, including analyzing, evaluating, and creating (Anderson & Krathwohl, 2001). Although BRT was not originally designed for self-report classification, its hierarchical structure provides a useful framework for distinguishing the levels of cognitive tasks that learners offload to AI during learning. Singh et al. (2025) systematically examined the influence of AI across the six levels of human cognitive processing, providing an important point of reference for this",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"for distinguishing the levels of cognitive tasks that learners offload to AI during learning. Singh et al. (2025) systematically examined the influence of AI across the six levels of human cognitive processing, providing an important point of reference for this study. In addition, the cognitive load theory provides complementary support for this classification by suggesting that higher-order cognitive processes typically require greater cognitive effort and impose heavier cognitive demands than lower-order processes (Sultanova, 2025). Building on this logic, this study used BRT as a task-based framework for differentiating AIBCO levels according to the levels of cognitive tasks that learners hand over to AI during task execution, and aimed to distinguish between lower-order and higher-order AIBCO. Lower-order AIBCO refers to situations in which learners use AI to take over cognitive tasks involving remembering, understanding, and applying (Anderson & Krathwohl, 2001). Higher-order AIBCO refers to situations in which learners use AI to take over cognitive tasks involving analyzing, evaluating, and creating. This distinction reflects the hier­ archical structure of BRT and the assumption, supported by the cogni­ tive load theory, that higher-order",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"AIBCO refers to situations in which learners use AI to take over cognitive tasks involving analyzing, evaluating, and creating. This distinction reflects the hier­ archical structure of BRT and the assumption, supported by the cogni­ tive load theory, that higher-order forms of task processing typically require greater cognitive effort and impose heavier cognitive demands than lower-order forms (Sultanova, 2025). Importantly, in this study, the classification of lower-order versus higher-order AIBCO was deter­ mined by the cognitive level of the task handed over to AI during task execution, rather than by the quality or depth of the learner's subsequent engagement with the AI output. Concretely, the six levels of AIBCO were operationalized as follows. Remember-level offloading refers to using AI to retrieve, store, or recall factual information instead of relying on one's own memory. Understand-level offloading refers to using AI to interpret, clarify, or summarize information on the learner's behalf. Apply-level offloading refers to using AI to perform practical or procedural tasks that involve applying learned knowledge or rules to specific problems. Analyze-level offloading refers to using AI to break down,",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"interpret, clarify, or summarize information on the learner's behalf. Apply-level offloading refers to using AI to perform practical or procedural tasks that involve applying learned knowledge or rules to specific problems. Analyze-level offloading refers to using AI to break down, categorize, compare, or structure information. Evaluate-level offloading refers to using AI to judge the quality, logic, or soundness of ideas, arguments, methods, or written work. Create-level offloading refers to using AI to generate novel content, ideas, frameworks, or academic products that a learner could otherwise construct independently. Although these six forms of AIBCO are analytically distinguishable, they are interdependent. In real learning contexts, many tasks are composite rather than discrete, often involving both lower-order and higher-order cognitive processes. Therefore, higher-order offloading may build upon, and in some cases subsume, lower-order forms of task processing. In this study, tasks were classified according to the highest- level cognitive operation that learners hand over to AI during task execution. Thus, offloading a higher-order task may also entail off­ loading the lower-order processes embedded within it. Accordingly, this study treated the six levels as",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"according to the highest- level cognitive operation that learners hand over to AI during task execution. Thus, offloading a higher-order task may also entail off­ loading the lower-order processes embedded within it. Accordingly, this study treated the six levels as unique yet interconnected dimensions. 1.2. Relationships among perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and AI-based cognitive offloading Drawing on value-based decision-making and cognitive offloading theories (Gilbert, 2024; Risko & Gilbert, 2016), learners engagement with AI as an external tool for cognitive offloading may be influenced by their costbenefit evaluation of using AI. These include the perceived value of AI, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability. This study conceptualized these variables as interconnected rather than causal, focusing on their associative patterns within higher education. The perceived AI value was conceptualized in this study as a broad evaluative appraisal of AI, reflecting the extent to which learners perceive AI as useful, trustworthy, explainable, fair, accountable, transparent, and low in risk (Yanit et al., 2023). Because AI systems often operate as black boxes for non-expert users, such value judg­ ments are likely",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,2,"reflecting the extent to which learners perceive AI as useful, trustworthy, explainable, fair, accountable, transparent, and low in risk (Yanit et al., 2023). Because AI systems often operate as black boxes for non-expert users, such value judg­ ments are likely to be shaped by accessible heuristic cues rather than by detailed technical understanding. In this study, these cues included fairness, accountability, transparency, explainability, and perceived risk. Shin (2021) showed that users' perceptions of fairness, account­ ability, transparency, and explainability function as salient heuristic cues in shaping trust and the perceived usefulness of AI systems. Y. Jiang and H. Chen Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 2",109
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"Similarly, perceived risk can function as an intuitive and affective heuristic cue that shapes individuals evaluations of the value and credibility of AI, thereby influencing acceptance and use intentions (Hong, 2025; Van Schaik et al., 2017). From a value-based decision-­ making perspective, external tools that are perceived as more valuable are more likely to be used for cognitive offloading (Gilbert, 2024). Accordingly, a higher perceived AI value may be associated with a greater tendency to engage in AIBCO. AI-usage capability is another important factor associated with AIBCO. This study conceptualized it in a relatively narrow sense as learners' operational proficiency in applying and exploiting AI technol­ ogy to accomplish academic tasks, rather than as a comprehensive measure of AI literacy. Following Wang et al. (2023), this construct refers to the ability to use AI tools effectively, adapt them to task re­ quirements, and integrate them into ongoing workflows. In this sense, the AI-usage capability captures practical fluency in interacting with AI systems across different task contexts, without necessarily implying deeper critical reflection or evaluative judgment (Held & Heubeck, 2025). This",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"re­ quirements, and integrate them into ongoing workflows. In this sense, the AI-usage capability captures practical fluency in interacting with AI systems across different task contexts, without necessarily implying deeper critical reflection or evaluative judgment (Held & Heubeck, 2025). This narrower operationalization is intentional, as this study distinguished the AI-usage capability from the AI evaluation capability, which refers to a learner's ability to critically assess, monitor, and judge AI outputs(Wang et al., 2023). From a value-based decision-making perspective, greater operational fluency may lower the perceived effort and time costs of using AI as an external cognitive resource (Gilbert, 2024). The AI-usage capability can be considered a central factor that reduces the motor and temporal costs associated with interacting with AI systems. As users become more proficient, the effort required to invoke AI tools decreases, enabling them to be seamlessly accessed as internal cognitive resources. Ac­ cording to the extended mind thesis (Farkas, 2012), frequent and fluent interactions enable external technologies to become stably embedded in daily cognitive routines and assume functional roles within the broader cognitive system. Accordingly, learners with",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"as internal cognitive resources. Ac­ cording to the extended mind thesis (Farkas, 2012), frequent and fluent interactions enable external technologies to become stably embedded in daily cognitive routines and assume functional roles within the broader cognitive system. Accordingly, learners with stronger AI-usage capa­ bility may be more likely to engage in AIBCO. AI usage capability is positively related to the perceived value of AI. Positive heuristic perceptions of AI, such as fairness, transparency, explainability, and low risk, enhance the trust of users and acceptance of AI technologies (Fahnenstich et al., 2024). A higher perceived value motivates users to explore, experiment with, and repeatedly engage with AI tools, strengthening their usage capabilities (Held & Heubeck, 2025). Therefore, the perceived value of AI may be related to the AI usage capability, and an indirect association may exist between the perceived value and AIBCO through the AI usage capability. While the cross-sectional design precludes causal inference, this pattern is consistent with the theoretical proposition that a higher perceived value of AI positively influences usage capability development, which in turn aligns with greater AIBCO.",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"AIBCO through the AI usage capability. While the cross-sectional design precludes causal inference, this pattern is consistent with the theoretical proposition that a higher perceived value of AI positively influences usage capability development, which in turn aligns with greater AIBCO. While the AI usage capability may account for the pathway through which the perceived value relates to AIBCO, this relationship is unlikely to be uniform across individuals. In particular, learners' AI evaluation capability may condition the strength of this association. The AI eval­ uation capability refers to the ability to analyze, select, and critically assess AI systems and their outputs (Held & Heubeck, 2025). Gilbert (2024) further reinforced the significance of evaluative capability in determining offloading behavior. Beyond motor and temporal costs, Gilbert argued that individuals consider the epistemic cost of relying on external cognitive resources, specifically the risk of adopting inaccurate or unreliable information. According to Zimmerman's (2002) Self-Regulated Learning model, effective learning depends not only on the ability to use tools but also on continuous monitoring and evaluation throughout the learning cycle, followed by strategic adjustment of subsequent",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"adopting inaccurate or unreliable information. According to Zimmerman's (2002) Self-Regulated Learning model, effective learning depends not only on the ability to use tools but also on continuous monitoring and evaluation throughout the learning cycle, followed by strategic adjustment of subsequent behaviors. Individuals with high AI evaluation capabilities tend to prudently assess AI outputs, leading them to devalue the perceived worth of such outputs (Chong et al., 2022).Thus, individuals with high AI evaluation capabilities have stringent criteria for evalu­ ating AI outputs and perceive their value as lower (Gilbert, 2024). Therefore, even if the AI-usage ability is high for such individuals, their AI-based lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading is lower. By contrast, individuals with low AI evaluation capabilities offload lower- and higher-order cognitive tasks to AI. Overall, these insights suggest that the AI evaluation capability plays a critical role, and that the as­ sociation between the perceived AI value and AIBCOvia the usage capabilitymay be conditioned by learners' levels of AI evaluation capability (see Fig. 1.). 2. Study 1 Study 1 developed and validated a psychometrically sound scale measuring AIBCO in",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"the as­ sociation between the perceived AI value and AIBCOvia the usage capabilitymay be conditioned by learners' levels of AI evaluation capability (see Fig. 1.). 2. Study 1 Study 1 developed and validated a psychometrically sound scale measuring AIBCO in research-oriented higher education. 2.1. Item generation The initial item pool for the AIBCO scale was developed using a multistep procedure that integrated both theory- and data-driven ap­ proaches following established guidelines for scale development (Boateng et al., 2018). First, a comprehensive review of existing ques­ tionnaires on cognitive offloading and related constructs was conducted, along with an analysis of the instruments used in studies on AI-supported learning and the delegation of academic tasks to AI. Based on this, the target construct was defined as AIBCO in research-intensive higher education contexts, rather than general AI use across all disci­ plinary settings. This scope restriction was intentional rather than incidental. Because AIBCO is expressed through concrete task behaviors, the behavioral manifestations are likely to vary across educational contexts and disciplines. In the initial stage of scale development, attempting to cover all possible",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"This scope restriction was intentional rather than incidental. Because AIBCO is expressed through concrete task behaviors, the behavioral manifestations are likely to vary across educational contexts and disciplines. In the initial stage of scale development, attempting to cover all possible higher-education tasks within a single item pool would have risked producing items that were overly generic, weakly interpretable, or unevenly relevant across student populations. Therefore, this study anchored the scale in research-intensive academic contexts, wherein students routinely engaged in tasks such as reading scholarly literature, learning research methods, working with data, and producing academic writing. These activities provide a coherent and theoretically meaningful context in which different levels of AIBCO can be systematically observed and measured. To complement this literature review with empirical evidence, open- ended interviews were conducted with 20 students (10 undergraduates and 10 postgraduates) enrolled in research-focused programs, such as those involving research-methods courses, term papers, capstone pro­ jects, or theses. During these interviews, participants were asked whether they use AI in their learning and research, followed by ques­ tions about the specific AI tools they engage",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"programs, such as those involving research-methods courses, term papers, capstone pro­ jects, or theses. During these interviews, participants were asked whether they use AI in their learning and research, followed by ques­ tions about the specific AI tools they engage with. To ensure consistent interpretation, AI was defined for participants as any AI-powered tool or application that can generate text, retrieve information, analyze data, or assist with academic tasks. For descriptive purposes, the reported AI tools were grouped into six categories: general models (e.g., ChatGPT), literature search tools (e.g., Research Rabbit), literature reading tools (e. g., UPDF), data processing tools (e.g., PyCharm), writing assistance tools (e.g., Propal), process management tools. Among the surveyed AI tools, general models (such as ChatGPT and DeepSeek) were reported as the most widely used, with usage rates far surpassing other types of AI tools. This finding highlights the central role of general-purpose AI tools in learners' academic and research activities. Participants were specifically instructed to consider AI tools that are accessible in mainland China. This ensured that the responses were based on AI tools commonly",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,3,"finding highlights the central role of general-purpose AI tools in learners' academic and research activities. Participants were specifically instructed to consider AI tools that are accessible in mainland China. This ensured that the responses were based on AI tools commonly used and available within the local context. Participants were also asked to describe the tasks they typically hand over to AI in their academic and research activities, using the prompt: Which tasks do you hand over to AI in your daily study and research activities, and how frequently? The responses were analyzed to identify recurring themes and behaviors, which were subsequently transformed into candidate items for the scale. During this process, this study intentionally formulated the items at a functional level rather than in narrowly discipline-bound terms. For Y. Jiang and H. Chen Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 3",143
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"example, items referred to activities such as summarizing academic ar­ guments, learning unfamiliar methodologies, creating materials for empirical inquiry, and structuring or revising academic text. This wording strategy was intended to preserve comparability across research-intensive disciplines while still reflecting concrete academic behaviors. However, the item pool was not designed to represent all possible forms of AI use across the full range of higher-education programs. Based on these two methods, an initial pool of 30 items was gener­ ated. To enhance content validity, expert feedback from two PhD psy­ chologists and three Master's students in education were sought. Each expert independently evaluated the items for clarity, conciseness, and conceptual alignment using the definition of AIBCO used herein. Items that were redundant, ambiguously worded, or weakly related to the constructs were revised or removed. The final version of the scale con­ sisted of 24 self-report items that collectively represented a broad spectrum of AIBCO behaviors in higher education. 2.2. Questionnaire administration The AIBCO scale was administered using a five-point Likert-type frequency scale, with response options ranging from 1 (never) to 5 (al­",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"of 24 self-report items that collectively represented a broad spectrum of AIBCO behaviors in higher education. 2.2. Questionnaire administration The AIBCO scale was administered using a five-point Likert-type frequency scale, with response options ranging from 1 (never) to 5 (al­ ways) (Boateng et al., 2018). Following established guidelines for scale development (Boateng et al., 2018), this study ensured adequate sample sizes for each phase of validation. Specifically, an exploratory factor analysis (EFA) was conducted with 158 participants (approximately five respondents per item), and the confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) with 239 participants, exceeding the minimum recommended thresholds for robust factor so­ lutions. The third subsample (n = 68) was used only for preliminary criterion-related validity (CRV) testing. While modest in size, it served as an initial check of external correlations rather than a confirmatory test. To assess the CRV, the Revised Study Process Questionnaire was used (Biggs et al., 2001), which assessed students approaches to learning through 20 items rated on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree; 5 = strongly agree). The surface learning approach (10 items; e.g., ",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"Study Process Questionnaire was used (Biggs et al., 2001), which assessed students approaches to learning through 20 items rated on a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree; 5 = strongly agree). The surface learning approach (10 items; e.g.,  I find I can pass most assessments by memorizing key sections rather than understanding them. ) reflects rote memorization and me­ chanical use of knowledge. The deep learning approach (10 items; e.g.,  I find that studying gives me a feeling of deep personal satisfaction. ) reflects intrinsic motivation and active knowledge construction. These two approaches correspond theoretically to lower- and higher-order cognitive processes in BRT. Specifically, surface learning aligns with remembering, understanding, and applying, whereas deep learning aligns with analyzing, evaluating, and creating. Thus, the two scales serve as criterion measures to assess the external validity of the AIBCO scale. The questionnaire was hosted on Credamo, an online survey plat­ form. The survey link was distributed via university administrators and course instructors who invited students to participate during their free time outside of class. The study was conducted in",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"scale. The questionnaire was hosted on Credamo, an online survey plat­ form. The survey link was distributed via university administrators and course instructors who invited students to participate during their free time outside of class. The study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, and ethical approval was obtained from the Institutional Review Board of Beijing Normal University (IRB No. BNU202601050002). To enhance transparency, this study was retro­ spectively registered on the Open Science Framework on February 9, 2026. This retrospective registration is not equivalent to prospective preregistration and does not provide the same protection against post hoc hypothesizing. Rather, it is reported here as a transparency measure that documents the study design, measures, and analytical procedures in a public record. The raw data supporting the findings of this manuscript have been uploaded to the Figshare repository and assigned a unique, permanent Digital Object Identifier (DOI): https://figshare.com/s/a75841ef1f6 c005696d0. This link is currently set to private status and is accessible only to the reviewers and editors. The data will be made publicly available immediately upon the formal publication of",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"assigned a unique, permanent Digital Object Identifier (DOI): https://figshare.com/s/a75841ef1f6 c005696d0. This link is currently set to private status and is accessible only to the reviewers and editors. The data will be made publicly available immediately upon the formal publication of the paper. All participants gave their informed consent electronically before taking part in the study. Descriptive characteristics of the three samples are listed in Table 1. 2.3. Results First, EFA was conducted to examine the dimensional structure and item quality of the scale. Because lower- and higher-order AIBCO are conceptually related, principal axis factoring with Promax oblique rotation was used as the primary extraction and rotation method. Principal component analysis with varimax rotation was performed (Terluin et al., 2023). The Kaiser Meyer Olkin (KMO) measure of sampling adequacy was 0.93, indicating that the data were highly suitable for factor analysis. Bartlett's test of sphericity yielded a chi-square value of 4330.37, with a significance level of p < 0.05, sug­ gesting that the correlation matrix significantly deviated from the identity matrix and that sufficient correlations existed between the variables to meet",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,4,"Bartlett's test of sphericity yielded a chi-square value of 4330.37, with a significance level of p < 0.05, sug­ gesting that the correlation matrix significantly deviated from the identity matrix and that sufficient correlations existed between the variables to meet the prerequisites for factor analysis. Items were removed if they showed (1) communalities <0.35, (2) factor loadings <0.45, (3) fewer than three items loaded on a factor, (4) cross-loadings >0.45 on multiple factors, or (5) poor theoretical interpretability (Carpenter, 2018; Costello & Osborne, 2005). Based on this, 12 items that did not meet the criteria were deleted. After removing the problematic items, the scale retained 12 valid items (see Table 2). Together, these two factors accounted for 76.11% of the total variance, significantly exceeding the recommended threshold of 40% reported in previous studies (Reio & Shuck, 2015). For robustness, we also conducted a supplementary principal Fig. 1. Proposed research model. Y. Jiang and H. Chen Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 4",165
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,5,"component analysis with Varimax rotation, which yielded the same two- factor structure. The oblique solution reproduced the same two-factor structure as the original analysis, with all items loading on their theo­ retically expected factors and Pattern Matrix loadings ranging from 0.57 to 0.96. The factor correlation matrix showed a substantial correlation between the two factors (r = 0.75), indicating that the dimensions are strongly related yet empirically distinguishable. These findings suggest that the substantive two-factor structure is robust across extraction and rotation choices. The internal consistency was assessed, and the results indicated that the Cronbach's  coefficients for both factors were greater than 0.90, demonstrating high internal consistency reliability for the subscales (Table 2). These results suggest some overlap between lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading, which is theoretically reasonable given that higher-order cognitive processes typically build upon lower- order processes. Therefore, rather than reflecting item redundancy, this pattern is consistent with the hierarchical nature of cognitive offloading. Second, a two-factor CFA was conducted on Sample 2 to examine the structural validity of the AIBCO scale. The model showed an overall",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,5,"Therefore, rather than reflecting item redundancy, this pattern is consistent with the hierarchical nature of cognitive offloading. Second, a two-factor CFA was conducted on Sample 2 to examine the structural validity of the AIBCO scale. The model showed an overall fit that was acceptable on several indices:  2/df = 3.00, GFI = 0.91, CFI = 0.95, NFI = 0.93, RMR = 0.05, and RMSEA = 0.09. Although the RMSEA value was slightly above the conventional 0.08 cutoff (Hu & Bentler, 1999), the remaining fit indices exceeded the recommended threshold of 0.90, suggesting that the hypothesized two-factor model provides an adequate, though not ideal, representation of data. To evaluate whether the two-factor structure provided a better fit than a unidimensional alternative, a one-factor CFA model was esti­ mated in which all 12 items loaded on a single general AIBCO factor. The one-factor model showed poor fit ( 2/df = 6.21, GFI = 0.72, CFI = 0.82, NFI = 0.80, RMSEA = 0.18), substantially worse than the two-factor model across all indices. These results provide strong evidence that the lower-/higher-order distinction",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,5,"The one-factor model showed poor fit ( 2/df = 6.21, GFI = 0.72, CFI = 0.82, NFI = 0.80, RMSEA = 0.18), substantially worse than the two-factor model across all indices. These results provide strong evidence that the lower-/higher-order distinction reflects a meaningful structural difference rather than an artifact of the analysis. Convergent validity was supported by the average variance extracted (AVE) and composite reliability (CR) for both dimensions. The AVE values were 0.62 for lower-order cognitive offloading and 0.65 for higher-order cognitive offloading, both exceeding the recommended threshold of 0.50. The corresponding CR values were 0.89 and 0.92, respectively, both above the recommended cutoff of 0.70, indicating satisfactory internal consistency and convergent validity. Discriminant validity was assessed using the Fornell Larcker crite­ rion. The square roots of the AVE values for lower-order cognitive off­ loading (0.79) and higher-order cognitive offloading (0.80) were both greater than the latent correlation between the two dimensions (r = 0.73). Although the correlation was substantial, this pattern suggests that the two dimensions are empirically distinguishable. At the same time, given their strong association and",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,5,"offloading (0.80) were both greater than the latent correlation between the two dimensions (r = 0.73). Although the correlation was substantial, this pattern suggests that the two dimensions are empirically distinguishable. At the same time, given their strong association and shared theoretical domain, we interpret lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading as related but distinguishable dimensions of AIBCO rather than as fully independent constructs. Criterion-related validity was further examined using Sample 3. The results indicate that the surface learning approach is positively associ­ ated with both AI-based lower-order cognitive offloading (r = 0.26) and AI-based higher-order cognitive offloading (r = 0.28), indicating small to moderate effect sizes (both ps < 0.05). By contrast, the deep learning approach is not significantly related to either offloading dimension (r = 0.17, 0.20; ps > 0.05). 2.4. Discussion Study 1 presents initial psychometric evidence from three indepen­ dent student samples that these two dimensions capture meaningful variations in, whereby learners redistribute different levels of cognitive processes to AI tools. The CRV evidence from Sample 3 further clarified how AIBCO is situated within broader learning approaches",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,5,"three indepen­ dent student samples that these two dimensions capture meaningful variations in, whereby learners redistribute different levels of cognitive processes to AI tools. The CRV evidence from Sample 3 further clarified how AIBCO is situated within broader learning approaches of students. Using Biggs two-factor Study Process Questionnaire as an external criterion, deep learning orientation was observed to be not significantly correlated with either lower- or higher-order AI-based offloading, whereas surface learning orientation showed moderately positive correlations with either dimension. This pattern is theoretically important because it clarifies what higher-order AIBCO does and does not capture. In this study, higher-order AIBCO refers to the extent to which learners hand over cognitively complex tasks, such as analyzing, evaluating, and creating, to AI. It does not imply that learners themselves engage in deeper, more reflective, or more elaborative learning. Deep learning is Table 1 Demographic statistics of the participants in Sample 1, Sample 2, and Sample 3. Parameter Value Sample 1 (EFA) Sample 2 (CFA) Sample 3 (CRV) Frequency Percentage (%) Frequency Percentage (%) Frequency Percentage (%) Gender Male 78 49.4 92",180
