DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"generally characterized by sustained cognitive engagement, integration of ideas, and meaning-making (Dolmans et al., 2016), and typically re­ quires substantial internal cognitive effort in complex learning contexts (Vogel-Walcutt et al., 2011). The positive association between both forms of AIBCO and surface learning suggests that even when students offload higher-order tasks to AI, their overall approach to learning may still remain instrumental, task-completion-oriented, and relatively su­ perficial (Gerlich, 2025). In this sense, higher-order AIBCO should not be interpreted as evidence of deep learning; rather, it may reflect the AI-assisted completion of cognitively complex academic tasks without the corresponding deep cognitive engagement by the learner. This finding is consistent with the conceptual clarification that the low­ er-/higher-order distinction classifies the cognitive level of the task transferred to AI, rather than the depth of learning ultimately achieved by a student. 3. Study 2 Building on Study 1, Study 2 examined the interrelationships among the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and AIBCO, providing preliminary construct-validity evidence that the AIBCO dimensions are meaningfully related to theoretically relevant AI- related constructs. 3.1. Sample",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"Building on Study 1, Study 2 examined the interrelationships among the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and AIBCO, providing preliminary construct-validity evidence that the AIBCO dimensions are meaningfully related to theoretically relevant AI- related constructs. 3.1. Sample Study 2 employed convenience sampling at a single university in mainland China. Accordingly, the sample should not be treated as a representative of all higher-education students. University administra­ tors distributed survey links generated through Credamo to students. After screening for inattentive responses using a validity check item, 667 valid questionnaires were retained. Among the participants, 78.41% were women. 3.2. Measurement AIBCO was measured using the scale developed in Study 1. In the Study 2 sample, the two sub-dimensions demonstrated strong internal consistency ( = 0.90 and 0.93). The fairness, accountability, transparency, and explainability (FATE) of the perceived value of AI were measured using items adapted from Shin (2021), with three items for each dimension (12 items in total). Example items include the following:  The system has no favoritism and does not discriminate against people (fairness);  The system requires",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"were measured using items adapted from Shin (2021), with three items for each dimension (12 items in total). Example items include the following:  The system has no favoritism and does not discriminate against people (fairness);  The system requires a person in charge who should be accountable for its potential adverse effects (accountability);  The evaluation criteria of the algorithms should be publicly released and understandable (transparency); and  I find the algorithms easy to understand (explainability). The FATE subscale demonstrated excellent internal consistency ( = 0.96). The risk dimension of the perceived value of AI was measured using three items adapted from Wu et al. (2022), including  Information or answers provided by AI may be incorrect. The internal consistency of the Risk Subscale was 0.88. To obtain a composite indicator, all risk items were reverse-coded and aggregated with the value-related items to compute the perceived AI value in learning. Higher scores reflected a higher perceived value and lower scores, perceived risk in AI-supported learning environments. The internal consistency of the combined scale was 0.96. The AI-usage capability",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"with the value-related items to compute the perceived AI value in learning. Higher scores reflected a higher perceived value and lower scores, perceived risk in AI-supported learning environments. The internal consistency of the combined scale was 0.96. The AI-usage capability was assessed using items adapted from Held and Heubeck (2025). An example item is as follows:  I can skillfully use AI to help me with my daily work. The initial three-item scale demonstrated low internal consistency ( = 0.64). Item-level inspection showed that the reverse-coded item ( It is usually hard for me to learn to use a new GenAI application ) had weak correlations with the other two items (r = 0.24 and 0.18). Conceptually, this item reflects the perceived difficulty in learning new AI tools rather than the capability of an individual to effectively use AI for current tasks. Therefore, the item was excluded from the analysis. The remaining two items showed good in­ ternal consistency ( = 0.84). The aforementioned refinements were theoretically justified and consistent with the psychometric recommendations for construct puri­ fication (DeVellis, 2017).",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"current tasks. Therefore, the item was excluded from the analysis. The remaining two items showed good in­ ternal consistency ( = 0.84). The aforementioned refinements were theoretically justified and consistent with the psychometric recommendations for construct puri­ fication (DeVellis, 2017). The theoretical meaning of the constructs re­ mains unchanged. The AI evaluation capability was measured using three items adapted from Held and Heubeck (2025). An example item is as follows:  I can choose a proper solution from various solutions provided by AI. The scale demonstrated high internal consistency ( = 0.90). 3.3. Results 3.3.1. Descriptive statistics and correlation As listed in Table 3, the perceived value of AI positively correlates with the AI-usage capability. The perceived value of AI is positively correlated with both AI-based lower- and higher-order cognitive off­ loading. Given the significant correlations among the main variables, multicollinearity was examined by inspecting the VIF values. The maximum VIF was 2.11, which is below the commonly accepted threshold of 3.00, indicating that multicollinearity was not a concern in this study (Hair, 2010). The diagonal elements listed in Table",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"multicollinearity was examined by inspecting the VIF values. The maximum VIF was 2.11, which is below the commonly accepted threshold of 3.00, indicating that multicollinearity was not a concern in this study (Hair, 2010). The diagonal elements listed in Table 3 repre­ sent the AVE values of each construct. All AVE values exceeded 0.60, which is higher than the recommended cutoff of 0.50, suggesting good convergent validity. The square root of each construct's AVE was greater than its correlations with other constructs, supporting discriminant validity (Fornell & Larcker, 1981). Harman's single-factor test showed that the first factor accounted for 46.52% of the variance, which was below the 50% threshold, suggesting that a common method bias was unlikely to substantially influence the findings. Furthermore, CFA was conducted to assess the potential impact of common method variance. The one-factor model demonstrated poor fit ( 2/df = 19.45, GFI = 0.36, RMSEA = 0.16, RMR = 0.16, CFI = 0.57, and NFI = 0.56), whereas the five-factor model showed a substantially better, though not perfect, fit ( 2/df = 7.53, GFI = 0.75,",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,6,"fit ( 2/df = 19.45, GFI = 0.36, RMSEA = 0.16, RMR = 0.16, CFI = 0.57, and NFI = 0.56), whereas the five-factor model showed a substantially better, though not perfect, fit ( 2/df = 7.53, GFI = 0.75, RMSEA = 0.09, RMR = 0.11, CFI = 0.86, and NFI = 0.81). Overall, these results indicate that the data do not support a single-factor structure dominated by common method variance, and that the results do not suggest that CMV is likely to account for the observed factor structure. 3.3.2. conditional process analysis The PROCESS macro (Model 14) was used to test the proposed re­ lationships among the variables. This study specified 5000 bootstrap samples to obtain 95% bias-corrected confidence intervals (CIs). Because the study included two dependent variables, AI-based lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading, separate analyses were con­ ducted for each variable. All predictor variables were mean-centered Table 3 Mean, standard deviation, and correlations among the main variables. Variables M SD 1 2 3 4 5 1.AI-based lower- order cognitive offloading 3.46 0.80 0.78 ​ ​ ​ ​ 2.AI-based",180
