DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"< 0.001). 3.4. Discussion Study 2 showed that the perceived AI value was indirectly associated with both types of cognitive offloading via the AI-usage capability. This indirect association was conditioned by the AI evaluation capability: it became nonsignificant for lower-order offloading under a high evalua­ tion capability but remained significant for higher-order offloading. This pattern is broadly consistent with the framework established by Gilbert (2024), who proposed that individuals decide whether to offload cognitive tasks based on a rational cost benefit evaluation of the cognitive effort. The findings of Study 2 also resonate with prior evi­ dence indicating that the positive perceived value of AI is associated with greater willingness to rely on AI assistance (Zhai et al., 2024). This interpretation is also consistent with the opportunity cost model of cognitive effort (Sharot et al., 2023), which suggests that people may allocate mental resources in ways that minimize effort while preserving the perceived value through external assistance. Notably, under a high evaluation capability, the indirect association between the perceived value of AI and lower-order cognitive offloading via the AI-usage capability",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"may allocate mental resources in ways that minimize effort while preserving the perceived value through external assistance. Notably, under a high evaluation capability, the indirect association between the perceived value of AI and lower-order cognitive offloading via the AI-usage capability became insignificant. This finding is consis­ tent with prior evidence suggesting that stronger monitoring or more cautious evaluation has been linked to lower reliance on automation (Siau & Wang, 2020). However, when the dependent variable was higher-order cognitive offloading, the indirect effect remained signifi­ cant even under a high evaluation capability. At first glance, this pattern may appear somewhat paradoxical, given that the AI evaluation capa­ bility was positively correlated with both lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading, while also weakening the indirect association be­ tween the perceived AI value and offloading via the AI-usage capability. One plausible interpretation is that students with a stronger evaluation capability may still engage in AIBCO and may even do so relatively often overall, but their offloading is less dependent on usage fluency alone (Siemens et al., 2022). Instead, their decisions may be shaped more",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"students with a stronger evaluation capability may still engage in AIBCO and may even do so relatively often overall, but their offloading is less dependent on usage fluency alone (Siemens et al., 2022). Instead, their decisions may be shaped more strongly by critical monitoring, selective judgment, and task-specific assessment of when AI support is warranted (Kosmyna et al., 2025b). Importantly, higher-order AIBCO herein refers to offloading cogni­ tively demanding tasks to AI, rather than the evidence that learners themselves engaged in higher-order thinking or deeper learning. One possible interpretation is that higher-order cognitive offloading typi­ cally involves tasks with greater cognitive demands, which may sustain reliance on AI even among individuals with relatively strong evaluative capability. In such cases, learners may not rely on AI uncritically, but may instead combine AI use with ongoing monitoring and selective judgment. This pattern may be more consistent with a collaborative form of AI-supported cognition than with simple passive reliance. However, this pattern should not be interpreted as direct evidence of cognitive enhancement or high-quality human AI collaboration. An alternative interpretation is that higher-order",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"may be more consistent with a collaborative form of AI-supported cognition than with simple passive reliance. However, this pattern should not be interpreted as direct evidence of cognitive enhancement or high-quality human AI collaboration. An alternative interpretation is that higher-order tasks remain sufficiently demanding that even students with relatively strong evaluation capa­ bility continue to offload them to AI (Gilbert, 2024). From this perspective, the observed pattern may reflect a more selective or so­ phisticated form of reliance driven by task difficulty and cognitive effort reduction, rather than genuinely enhanced cognition (Eckhardt et al., 2026). Accordingly, the continued significance of the indirect effect under a high evaluation capability suggests that learners may continue to use AI in far more demanding cognitive tasks while maintaining some degree of evaluative oversight. However, whether this reflects critically regulated collaboration or more sophisticated reliance remains an open question (Yang et al., 2026). 4. Discussion 4.1. Theoretical implications The core contribution of this work is highlighted through Study 1, wherein a scale that distinguishes between AI-based lower- and higher- order cognitive offloading was developed and",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"remains an open question (Yang et al., 2026). 4. Discussion 4.1. Theoretical implications The core contribution of this work is highlighted through Study 1, wherein a scale that distinguishes between AI-based lower- and higher- order cognitive offloading was developed and validated. Based on this scale, Study 2 was conducted, which provided theory-informed sup­ porting evidence by revealing a patterned set of associations among the perceived value of AI, AI usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and both forms of AIBCO. Overall, these findings highlight that, in the age of AI, the issue of cognitive offloading is no longer only about whether individuals offload cognition or how frequently they do so, but also about how and why they choose to hand over different forms of cogni­ tive work to AI systems. The findings of this work contribute to the literature in several ways. First, the study advances the measurement of AIBCO by establishing a scale that explicitly distinguishes between lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading. Existing measures often rely on general in­ dicators such as AI-usage frequency or technological dependence, which do not",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"ways. First, the study advances the measurement of AIBCO by establishing a scale that explicitly distinguishes between lower- and higher-order cognitive offloading. Existing measures often rely on general in­ dicators such as AI-usage frequency or technological dependence, which do not fully capture the complexity of cognitive offloading in learning contexts (Morales-García et al., 2024). Grounded in BRT, the developed scale is better suited to capturing differences in the types of cognitive tasks that learners hand over to AI, especially in research-related learning and academic activities in higher education in China (Fowler, 2023; Singh et al., 2025). Second, this study refines the theoretical understanding of cognitive offloading by showing that in AI-supported higher education, offloading should not be treated as a unitary or undifferentiated process. Tradi­ tional discussions of cognitive offloading often focus on whether in­ dividuals rely on external aids to reduce cognitive effort (Risko & Gilbert, 2016). Herein, this perspective is extended by demonstrating that learners may hand over qualitatively different levels of cognitive tasks to AI, ranging from lower-order tasks such as remembering, un­ derstanding, and applying to",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"to reduce cognitive effort (Risko & Gilbert, 2016). Herein, this perspective is extended by demonstrating that learners may hand over qualitatively different levels of cognitive tasks to AI, ranging from lower-order tasks such as remembering, un­ derstanding, and applying to higher-order tasks such as analyzing, evaluating, and creating. This suggests that AIBCO is better understood as a differentiated process structured by the cognitive level of the work transferred to AI, rather than as a single general tendency toward external support. This distinction is theoretically important because offloading a lower-order task is not equivalent to offloading a higher-order task, as the two involve different forms of cognitive work and different levels of cognitive demand (O'Brien et al., 2022). Third, this study provides empirical evidence that is broadly consistent with Gilbert's (2024) value-based decision model of cognitive offloading in AI-supported contexts. By showing that learners' percep­ tions of AI value and capability are systematically associated with their cognitive offloading, this study connects the cognitive offloading theory with the study of AI perceptions and extends the current understanding of when and how learners",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,8,"showing that learners' percep­ tions of AI value and capability are systematically associated with their cognitive offloading, this study connects the cognitive offloading theory with the study of AI perceptions and extends the current understanding of when and how learners may make offloading decisions in AI-assisted learning contexts. Fig. 3. Moderating effect of AI-evaluation capability on the relationship be­ tween AI-usage capability and AI-based higher-order cognitive offloading. Note: Plotted values are model-predicted scores of AI-based higher-order cognitive offloading based on the mean-centered variables. High and low levels of AI evaluation capability represent +1 SD and −1 SD from the mean, respectively. Y. Jiang and H. Chen Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 8",116
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"4.2. Practical implications The study findings have practical implications for educators, learners, and designers of AI systems in higher education. (1) Implications for educators and institutions First, the results highlight the importance of shifting from a limited focus on using AI to systematically cultivate abilities of students to evaluate AI. In developing AI literacy initiatives and course curricula, higher education institutions should explicitly incorporate learning ob­ jectives such as identifying the limitations and risks of AI-generated outputs, understanding the sources of uncertainty, and making evidence-based judgments about AI suggestions. These evaluative competencies should be considered core learning outcomes rather than supplementary skills attached to prompt engineering or tool operations. Second, universities should strengthen their disciplinary foundations and competencies. When integrating AI into professional curricula, ed­ ucators should avoid considering it as a shortcut that lowers disciplinary rigor (Zhong et al., 2025). Instead, domain-specific knowledge and critical reasoning training should serve as the cognitive foundation for developing the AI evaluation capabilities of students. Educators should adopt different instructional strategies for different learning tasks. Because lower- and higher-order AIBCO involve different types",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"2025). Instead, domain-specific knowledge and critical reasoning training should serve as the cognitive foundation for developing the AI evaluation capabilities of students. Educators should adopt different instructional strategies for different learning tasks. Because lower- and higher-order AIBCO involve different types of task demands, they may require different instructional re­ sponses. In some cases, heavy reliance on AI for lower-order tasks may be associated with more superficial engagement, whereas AI use in higher-order tasks may create opportunities for guided reflection, critique, and structured support for thinking. (2) Implications for AI learners The study findings underscore the importance of metacognitive regulation and reflective use of AI for learners. Students should recog­ nize that not all cognitive tasks are equally suitable for offloading to AI. Different forms of offloading may present different pedagogical risks and opportunities. For example, reliance on AI for relatively routine task components may sometimes be associated with more passive engage­ ment, whereas AI use in more open-ended tasks may invite greater monitoring, comparison, and revision. Learners are encouraged to actively monitor when to use, how to use, and when",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"routine task components may sometimes be associated with more passive engage­ ment, whereas AI use in more open-ended tasks may invite greater monitoring, comparison, and revision. Learners are encouraged to actively monitor when to use, how to use, and when to disengage from AI assistance, as well as reflect on the influence of AI interaction on their own understanding and skill development. Through such reflective engagement, learners may be better positioned to use AI in ways that preserve cognitive agency while still benefiting from AI-supported task assistance. (3) Implications for AI system and platform designers Finally, this study offers insights into the design of human-centered AI systems. At the system level, AI platforms should be designed to enhance human agency by reserving space for user decision-making and reflection. For instance, systems could require users to specify learning goals before generating outputs, present multiple alternative solutions rather than a single best answer, and explicitly indicate uncertainty or risk levels to support informed choice. These design strategies may reduce uncritical forms of offloading and encourage more reflective engagement. AI interfaces can incorporate",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"outputs, present multiple alternative solutions rather than a single best answer, and explicitly indicate uncertainty or risk levels to support informed choice. These design strategies may reduce uncritical forms of offloading and encourage more reflective engagement. AI interfaces can incorporate external scaffolds to support evaluative reasoning. Practical implementations include displaying information sources alongside the generated content, providing built-in questioning prompts (e.g., What assumptions underlie this answer? or What opposing views might exist?), requiring users to specify the reasons for adopting or rejecting AI suggestions or embedding peer and instructor review mechanisms for AI-generated outputs. Such scaffolds may prompt evaluative processing during AI use and may, over time, support the development of learnersAI evaluation capabilities (Gerlich, 2025). 4.3. Limitations and future research directions First, although two sequential studies were conductedone for establishing the AIBCO scale and the other for testing the proposed modelboth studies relied on self-reports and cross-sectional data. In particular, Study 2 used a single-source design; therefore, the observed relationships among the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and AIBCO should be interpreted as associative rather than",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"proposed modelboth studies relied on self-reports and cross-sectional data. In particular, Study 2 used a single-source design; therefore, the observed relationships among the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, AI evaluation capability, and AIBCO should be interpreted as associative rather than causal, and they do not establish temporal ordering. In addition, common-method variance cannot be ruled out. The observed associations may partly reflect a more general positive orientation to­ ward AI rather than the fully distinct underlying mechanisms. Other unmeasured factors such as prior AI experience, frequency of AI use, degree level, disciplinary background, or institutional norms may have contributed to the observed pattern. Moreover, Study 2 relied on con­ venience sampling from a single university in mainland China, raising concerns regarding the external validity and limiting the generaliz­ ability of the findings to other institutional, disciplinary, and cultural contexts. Future research should adopt longitudinal, experimental, multi-site, and multi-source designs to test the temporal dynamics of AIBCO, reduce common-method concerns, and examine whether the study findings hold across broader higher-education populations. Second, almost all participants were university students; this may have",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"research should adopt longitudinal, experimental, multi-site, and multi-source designs to test the temporal dynamics of AIBCO, reduce common-method concerns, and examine whether the study findings hold across broader higher-education populations. Second, almost all participants were university students; this may have limited the external validity of the findings. Previous studies have shown that university teachers tend to adopt more cautious and reflec­ tive attitudes toward AI integration compared to students. Therefore, future research could explore the determinants and mechanisms of AIBCO among teachers, whose perceptions, responsibilities, and peda­ gogical contexts may lead to distinct offloading behaviors and decision patterns (Saito et al., 2025). Third, the AIBCO scale was developed in a research-intensive higher- education context, and its behavioral referents reflect that scope. Although the items were coded at a functional level to extend the scale across a range of research-oriented disciplines, some items are less applicable to practice-based, studio-based, or performance-oriented programs in which students academic tasks take substantially different forms. This indicates that the current scale should be inter­ preted as a context-specific measure of AIBCO in research-intensive academic settings",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"some items are less applicable to practice-based, studio-based, or performance-oriented programs in which students academic tasks take substantially different forms. This indicates that the current scale should be inter­ preted as a context-specific measure of AIBCO in research-intensive academic settings rather than as a universally applicable measure for all higher-education contexts. Future research can extend the scale to other disciplinary environ­ ments by retaining the underlying conceptual distinction among the levels of AIBCO, while revising the behavioral referents of the items to fit the task demands and academic practices of those contexts. For example, in non-research-intensive contexts, items could be reformulated around discipline-specific activities such as design ideation, composition, studio critique, lesson planning, clinical reasoning, or performance prepara­ tion. Subsequent studies should then re-evaluate the scale's content validity, factor structure, and measurement invariance in those contexts. Fourth, while the psychometric properties of the AIBCO scale met conventional thresholds, the authors acknowledge that the sample sizes in Study 1particularly for the CRV analysiswere relatively small. Accordingly, the findings should be viewed as preliminary evidence for the scale's dimensional structure. Future research",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,9,"properties of the AIBCO scale met conventional thresholds, the authors acknowledge that the sample sizes in Study 1particularly for the CRV analysiswere relatively small. Accordingly, the findings should be viewed as preliminary evidence for the scale's dimensional structure. Future research with larger and more diverse samples is needed to replicate these results and further test the scale's stability and generalizability across academic contexts. Fifth, although the CFA results were generally supportive of the proposed two-factor structure, the RMSEA value was slightly above the conventional thresholds for a close fit. This suggests that the measure­ ment model should be interpreted with caution and that further refinement of the scale structure may be warranted in future research. In addition, the post hoc refinements of the measurement scales in Study 2 require further validation. While these adjustments can improve reli­ ability and conceptual coherence, they may reduce comparability with Y. Jiang and H. Chen Computers in Human Behavior: Artiϧcial Humans 8 (2026) 100327 9",161
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,10,"prior studies employing the original scales. Therefore, future research should validate the modified scales using independent samples and further examine whether the refined operationalization yield consistent structural relationships. Finally, this work was conducted within Chinese higher-education universities, which are shaped by specific cultural, institutional, and technological environments. The patterns of AIBCO observed here may not fully represent those in other cultural or educational systems where AI accessibility, ethical norms, and learning practices differ. Future research should therefore extend sampling to cross-cultural and cross- regional contexts, enabling a more comprehensive and comparative understanding of the manifestation of AIBCO across diverse educational and sociotechnical settings. 5. Conclusion This study described the structure and associative patterns of AIBCO in higher education through the development of a measurement scale and an examination of their interrelationships. The AIBCO scale differ­ entiates between lower- and higher-order offloading, offering a practical instrument for future studies. The study further identified how the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, and AI evaluation capability are associated with AIBCO. In the context of the growing integration of AI into higher education, this",180
DOC_039,NEW23.pdf,10.1016/j.chbah.2026.100327,10,"offloading, offering a practical instrument for future studies. The study further identified how the perceived AI value, AI-usage capability, and AI evaluation capability are associated with AIBCO. In the context of the growing integration of AI into higher education, this integrative framework offers a nuanced perspective on the cognitive dimensions of humanAI collaboration. Furthermore, the study suggests theoretical directions for designing educational and technological practices that encourage collaborative and reflective engagement with AI, rather than uncritical cognitive delegation. CRediT authorship contribution statement Yali Jiang: Writing  review & editing, Writing  original draft, Visualization, Supervision, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Haiping Chen: Writing  review & editing, Validation, Supervision, Resources. Consent to participate Informed consent was obtained electronically from all individual participants prior to their participation in the study. Consent to publish Not applicable. Ethics approval This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Beijing Normal University (IRB No. BNU202601050002) and was conducted in accordance with relevant ethical standards. Materials availability All study materials are described in detail within the article. Addi­ tional materials are",180
