DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,1,"EDITED BY Elliot Pirie, Robert Gordon University, United Kingdom REVIEWED BY John Mark R. Asio, Gordon College, Philippines Lóránt Dénes Dávid, John von Neumann University, Hungary *CORRESPONDENCE Lloy Pinedo lloy.pinedo@uwiener.edu.pe RECEIVED 26 May 2026 REVISED 14 September 2026 ACCEPTED 11 October 2026 PUBLISHED 29 October 2026 CITATION Pinedo L, Chang-Tam R, Vidaurrazaga- Sferrazza S, Garcia-Huamantumba E, Zevallos-Cordova P and Jaque- Reategui S (2026) Adoption of generative artificial intelligence by university marketing students. Front. Educ. 11:1892057. doi: 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 COPYRIGHT © 2026 Pinedo, Chang-Tam, Vidaurrazaga-Sferrazza, Garcia- Huamantumba, Zevallos-Cordova and Jaque-Reategui. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Adoption of generative artificial intelligence by university marketing students Lloy Pinedo 1*, Raúl Chang-Tam 2, Sandro Vidaurrazaga-Sferrazza 1, Elvira Garcia-Huamantumba 1, Paola Zevallos-Cordova 1 and",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,1,"practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Adoption of generative artificial intelligence by university marketing students Lloy Pinedo 1*, Raúl Chang-Tam 2, Sandro Vidaurrazaga-Sferrazza 1, Elvira Garcia-Huamantumba 1, Paola Zevallos-Cordova 1 and Sebastián Jaque-Reategui 1 1Grupo de Investigación Transformación Digital Empresarial, Facultad de Negocios, Universidad Privada Norbert Wiener, Lima, Perú, 2Facultad de Ciencias Económicas, Administrativas y Hospitalidad, Universidad Latina de Costa Rica, San José, Costa Rica The growing adoption of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is transforming marketing education and increasing the need to understand the factors that drive its academic use among university students. This study aimed to analyze the determinants of GenAI adoption among university marketing students using the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology 2 (UTAUT2). A quantitative, explanatory, cross-sectional study was conducted with 310 marketing students from two Latin American universities in Peru and Costa Rica during the first academic semester of 2026. Participants were selected through non-probability purposive sampling. Data were collected using a structured questionnaire and analyzed through Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM)",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,1,"two Latin American universities in Peru and Costa Rica during the first academic semester of 2026. Participants were selected through non-probability purposive sampling. Data were collected using a structured questionnaire and analyzed through Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) with SmartPLS version 4.1.1.8. The findings indicate that habit is the main determinant of both behavioral intention and actual use of GenAI tools, while hedonic motivation, facilitating conditions, and price value also show significant positive effects. Conversely, performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influence do not significantly influence behavioral intention. It is concluded that the adoption of GenAI among university marketing students is increasingly driven by habitual academic use rather than by traditional technology acceptance factors. These findings provide evidence to support institutional strategies for the responsible integration of GenAI into higher education. KEYWORDS artificial intelligence, digital skills, digital transformation, educational technology, higher education, technology adoption, university students 1 Introduction The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence is redefining higher education, creating new opportunities and challenges for universities seeking to prepare graduates for an increasingly digital workforce (AlBlooshi, 2026; Asio and",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,1,"educational technology, higher education, technology adoption, university students 1 Introduction The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence is redefining higher education, creating new opportunities and challenges for universities seeking to prepare graduates for an increasingly digital workforce (AlBlooshi, 2026; Asio and Soriano, 2025). The emergence of GenAI has marked a turning point in the business ecosystem and, specifically, in the academic and marketing fields (Bencsik et al., 2026; Ravšelj et al., 2025). Tools based on Large Language Models (LLMs), the fundamental technological foundation of GAI, are redefining functions ranging from content creation (text, audio, video, etc.) and audience segmentation to advanced predictive analytics (Borimnejad and Borimnejad, 2025; Li and Xiao, 2026). According to Alcivar-Chiluiza et al. (2025), Dong et al. (2026) and Sun et al. (2026), this technological transformation not only TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 29 October 2026 DOI 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 01 frontiersin.org",144
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"optimizes operational efficiency but also alters the core competencies required by various industries that increasingly value Artificial Intelligence (AI) literacy. In this context, Lin et al. (2026) argue that higher education faces the challenge of adapting its curricula to bridge the gap between academic training and the needs of an AI-driven labor market. For marketing students, mastery of GenAI is essential for their future employability (Hazari, 2025); however, the effective integration of these technologies into learning processes is a complex undertaking. Lara-Navarra et al. (2025) suggest that, although there is widespread use of GenAI tools such as ChatGPT and Copilot for basic tasks, doubts persist regarding the depth of their curricular integration, the achievement of digital literacy, and the technical preparedness of faculty Elhajjar et al. (2020) note that students, faculty, and marketing professionals perceive AI as enhancing the analytical capabilities, digital skills, and employability of future professionals, while also increasing interest in courses related to this technology. For their part, Barger et al. (2025) note that the incorporation of GenAI tools into marketing courses promotes experiential learning, AI literacy,",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"capabilities, digital skills, and employability of future professionals, while also increasing interest in courses related to this technology. For their part, Barger et al. (2025) note that the incorporation of GenAI tools into marketing courses promotes experiential learning, AI literacy, and greater student engagement in academic activities. Complementarily, Narang et al. (2025) argue that AI can assume different roles within the educational process, acting as a tutor, collaborator, and teaching support tool. However, the rapid growth of GenAI in higher education also raises concerns regarding its ethical and responsible use (Alnsour et al., 2025; Echeverria-Caicedo et al., 2026). Reina Marín et al. (2025), Gasaymeh et al. (2024), and Morales-García et al. (2024) warn that GenAI tools can lead to inappropriate academic practices, including plagiarism, excessive reliance on GenAI tools, and a decline in critical thinking among students. Likewise, Yang et al. (2025) raise concerns about data privacy, algorithmic biases, and a lack of transparency in the responses generated by AI models, which can affect the quality of learning and academic assessment processes. In the field of marketing, these limitations are",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"al. (2025) raise concerns about data privacy, algorithmic biases, and a lack of transparency in the responses generated by AI models, which can affect the quality of learning and academic assessment processes. In the field of marketing, these limitations are relevant because professional training requires the development of analytical, creative, and ethical skills for decision-making in digital environments. Despite these challenges, the evolution of GenAI continues to transform various economic and educational sectors (Chang Tam et al., 2022; Espinosa-Luna et al., 2025; McDonald et al., 2025). The emergence of new platforms based on multimodal models, intelligent automation, and advanced content generation has increased interest in integrating these tools into teaching and learning processes (Cordero et al., 2024; Johnston et al., 2024). In particular, the marketing field faces a growing demand for professionals capable of using AI tools for content creation, market segmentation, predictive analytics, and digital campaign management, among other activities (Guha et al., 2024; Wong, 2024). Consequently, understanding the factors that drive or limit the use of AI Gen among college students has become a critical need for both",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"market segmentation, predictive analytics, and digital campaign management, among other activities (Guha et al., 2024; Wong, 2024). Consequently, understanding the factors that drive or limit the use of AI Gen among college students has become a critical need for both educational institutions and the business sector. While the scientific literature shows a growing interest in analyzing the adoption and use of GenAI tools in the field of marketing, academic research in the Latin American context remains very limited. This situation is significant because learning processes, technological conditions, and educational dynamics can vary depending on the geographic and sociocultural context. Therefore, there remains a scientific gap regarding the understanding of the factors that influence the use of GenAI among marketing students in Latin America. In this context, we seek to answer the question: What are the determining factors in the use of GenAI in the education of marketing students? To this end, the study aimed to understand technology adoption behavior at two Latin American universities, as well as to generate evidence that contributes to the design of institutional strategies for the",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"of GenAI in the education of marketing students? To this end, the study aimed to understand technology adoption behavior at two Latin American universities, as well as to generate evidence that contributes to the design of institutional strategies for the effective and responsible integration of GenAI tools in higher education, aligning with SDG 4 (Quality Education) to ensure inclusive, equitable, and quality education, while promoting the digital competencies necessary to meet the challenges of the contemporary professional environment. This study contributes to the body of knowledge by extending the application of the UTAUT2 model to the adoption of GenAI among marketing students in Latin America, a context that remains underrepresented in the literature. Furthermore, by simultaneously examining behavioral intention, actual use, predictive performance, and moderating effects, the study offers a comprehensive understanding of the factors driving the adoption of generative AI in higher education. The findings contribute to the advancement of research on technology adoption and provide evidence to support institutional decision- making regarding the responsible integration of GenAI into higher education. 2 Theoretical framework and hypotheses 2.1 Artificial intelligence",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"AI in higher education. The findings contribute to the advancement of research on technology adoption and provide evidence to support institutional decision- making regarding the responsible integration of GenAI into higher education. 2 Theoretical framework and hypotheses 2.1 Artificial intelligence Fetzer (1990) argues that the difficulty in defining AI lies in the complexity of the concept of intelligence itself, noting that artificially intelligent systems are products created by human ingenuity with the ability to replicate certain cognitive properties. From this perspective, AI represents a combination of computational processes and capabilities designed to perform functions similar to human thought. For his part, Morandín- Ahuerma (2022) defines AI as the ability of a machine or computer system to perform tasks that traditionally require human intelligence, through the use of algorithms and machine learning techniques. AI can be classified into narrow AI, general AI, and superintelligent AI. Narrow AI focuses on developing systems designed to perform specific tasks, such as virtual assistants, recommendation systems, or content generation tools, and is currently the most widely used form. On the other hand, general AI refers",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,2,"AI, and superintelligent AI. Narrow AI focuses on developing systems designed to perform specific tasks, such as virtual assistants, recommendation systems, or content generation tools, and is currently the most widely used form. On the other hand, general AI refers to systems with capabilities similar to human reasoning, capable of learning and adapting to multiple contexts. Superintelligent AI posits a hypothetical scenario in which machines would surpass human cognitive capabilities, although its development is still considered a future prospect (Marin- Rodriguez et al., 2026; Mukhamediev et al., 2022). Currently, LLMs represent one of the most significant advances in the contemporary development of AI. These models utilize deep learning techniques and large-scale neural networks Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 02 frontiersin.org",122
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"trained on vast amounts of data, enabling them to understand, process, and generate natural language in a coherent and context-aware manner (Liu et al., 2025; Zalasar et al., 2026). Their operation is primarily based on Transformer-type architectures, designed to identify complex patterns in language and predict textual sequences with high levels of accuracy (Caro-Soto et al., 2026; Raza et al., 2025). Thanks to these capabilities, LLMs can perform tasks related to text writing, machine translation, code generation, and information analysis, among others (Annepaka and Pakray, 2025). 2.2 GenIA in marketing GenAI is one of the most disruptive technologies within the business and marketing ecosystem, due to its ability to generate content using advanced deep learning algorithms (Saleh and Zeebaree, 2025). Chan and Choi (2025) note that the rapid growth of tools such as ChatGPT has driven a significant transformation in the interaction between organizations and consumers, enabling the automation of processes related to content creation, information analysis, experience personalization, and more. Likewise, Basu et al. (2025) highlight that LLMs have enabled the development of systems capable of understanding and producing",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"the interaction between organizations and consumers, enabling the automation of processes related to content creation, information analysis, experience personalization, and more. Likewise, Basu et al. (2025) highlight that LLMs have enabled the development of systems capable of understanding and producing responses similar to human language, fostering new dynamics in communication and strategic marketing management Verma et al. (2025) note that, in the field of marketing, GenAI is used in activities related to the creation of advertising materials, audience segmentation, consumer behavior analysis, and the automation of digital campaigns. According to Beyari and Hashem (2025), organizations use GenAI tools to develop more personalized and efficient strategies, strengthening consumer engagement and experience through automated recommendations and dynamic content generation. Likewise, Pagani and Wind (2025) note that GenAI improves human- computer interaction processes by offering more personalized responses tailored to user preferences. In this context, Guha et al. (2024) and Grewal et al. (2025) highlight the importance of understanding how GenAI is being incorporated into academic marketing education, given that digital competencies and mastery of AI-based tools are strategic elements for professional performance",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"this context, Guha et al. (2024) and Grewal et al. (2025) highlight the importance of understanding how GenAI is being incorporated into academic marketing education, given that digital competencies and mastery of AI-based tools are strategic elements for professional performance in today’s business environments. In particular, understanding the factors that influence the use of AI in marketing among college students allows us to comprehend the processes of technology adoption, as well as to identify opportunities and challenges associated with its integration into marketing teaching and learning processes. 2.3 Unified theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT2) This study is based on UTAUT2, proposed by Venkatesh et al. (2012), which explains the factors that influence the intention to use and actual use of technologies in different contexts. The model expands upon the original version of UTAUT by incorporating variables focused on consumer behavior, allowing for a more precise analysis of the adoption of digital technologies in organizational settings (Tamilmani et al., 2021). UTAUT2 posits that variables such as performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, enabling conditions, hedonic motivation, perceived value,",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"on consumer behavior, allowing for a more precise analysis of the adoption of digital technologies in organizational settings (Tamilmani et al., 2021). UTAUT2 posits that variables such as performance expectancy, effort expectancy, social influence, enabling conditions, hedonic motivation, perceived value, and habit positively influence usage intention and technology adoption behavior (García de Blanes Sebastián et al., 2022). Recent evidence continues to demonstrate the versatility of the UTAUT2 model in explaining the adoption of emerging digital technologies in various contexts. Beyond user behavior, the UTAUT2 model has recently been applied to examine the acceptance of artificial intelligence and other digital innovations in the fields of healthcare, agriculture, public services, and education, consistently demonstrating strong explanatory power for behavioral intention and technology use (De Witte et al., 2026; Limpasirisuwan et al., 2025; Vargáné Dudás and Dávid, 2024). This growing body of evidence reinforces the suitability of UTAUT2 as a theoretical framework for investigating the determinants of generative AI adoption among college marketing students. In this vein, UTAUT2 is used to understand the factors that determine the use of AI-based tools among marketing",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"reinforces the suitability of UTAUT2 as a theoretical framework for investigating the determinants of generative AI adoption among college marketing students. In this vein, UTAUT2 is used to understand the factors that determine the use of AI-based tools among marketing students. Given the rapid growth of AI-based technologies in activities related to content creation, data analysis, automation, and the personalization of digital strategies, it is important to identify how perceptions, technological conditions, and social factors influence the intention to adopt these tools in the academic setting. Thus, UTAUT2 provides a solid foundation for explaining technology acceptance behavior and guiding strategies for integrating AI-Gen into university education. Table 1 presents the conceptual definition of the latent variables in UTAUT2, as well as the hypotheses proposed in this study. Additionally, Venkatesh et al. (2012) argue that the UTAUT2 model incorporates moderating variables such as age, gender, and experience, which can influence the relationship between the model’s constructs and the intention to use technology. These variables help us understand how individual characteristics affect the levels of technology acceptance and adoption. However, in the",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"such as age, gender, and experience, which can influence the relationship between the model’s constructs and the intention to use technology. These variables help us understand how individual characteristics affect the levels of technology acceptance and adoption. However, in the present study, the experience variable was not considered a moderator, since the study population consisted of university students with relatively homogeneous characteristics regarding access to and use of GenAI tools. Instead, the university variable was incorporated to compare the adoption behavior of GenAI tools among students from two Latin American universities, considering the possible institutional or contextual differences present in each educational environment. Table 2 presents the moderating hypotheses proposed in the UTAUT2 model, considering the influence of the variables age, gender, and university on the relationships between the constructs associated with behavioral intention and the use of GenAI tools among university marketing students. Based on the above, Figure 1 shows the design of the proposed conceptual model: 3 Methodology 3.1 Research design A quantitative study with an explanatory focus and a non- experimental cross-sectional design was conducted to determine",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,3,"among university marketing students. Based on the above, Figure 1 shows the design of the proposed conceptual model: 3 Methodology 3.1 Research design A quantitative study with an explanatory focus and a non- experimental cross-sectional design was conducted to determine the factors that influence the use of GenAI tools among college Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 03 frontiersin.org",60
