DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,5,"students majoring in marketing. This design is appropriate for explaining the relationships between latent variables and testing models of technology adoption using partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM), which is widely used in research on the acceptance and use of digital technologies (Hair et al., 2021). 3.2 Participants A total of 310 college students from two private universities in Latin America located in Peru (n = 201) and Costa Rica (n = 109) participated in the study. They were selected through non- probability sampling by intention during the first academic semester of 2026. Marketing students were chosen because they represent one of the academic groups with the greatest exposure to the use of GenIA tools, which are increasingly used in activities related to content creation, consumer analysis, market research, and digital strategy development skills considered essential for professional practice in today’s marketing context (Grassini et al., 2024). Of the total participants, 62% were female and 38% were male. Furthermore, the average age was 23 years, with a standard deviation of 7 years. Inclusion criteria included students enrolled during the",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,5,"in today’s marketing context (Grassini et al., 2024). Of the total participants, 62% were female and 38% were male. Furthermore, the average age was 23 years, with a standard deviation of 7 years. Inclusion criteria included students enrolled during the first academic term of 2026 in programs or courses related to marketing who voluntarily agreed to participate by providing informed consent. Questionnaires that were not fully completed were excluded. The sample size obtained exceeds the methodological recommendations for research conducted using PLS-SEM. According to Hair et al. (2021), sample adequacy must ensure sufficient statistical power to estimate the structural model, while Kock and Hadaya (2018) note that methods such as the inverse square root and gamma-exponential tests allow for confirming the adequacy of the sample size in this type of analysis. Consequently, the 310 participants provide a sufficient basis for the reliable estimation of the proposed model. 3.3 Instrument and data collection process The information was collected using a structured questionnaire adapted from instruments previously used by Mistry et al. (2025), Tang et al. (2025), Cabero-Almenara et al. (2025), Grassini",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,5,"the reliable estimation of the proposed model. 3.3 Instrument and data collection process The information was collected using a structured questionnaire adapted from instruments previously used by Mistry et al. (2025), Tang et al. (2025), Cabero-Almenara et al. (2025), Grassini et al. (2024), and Ren et al. (2026) to explain the adoption of emerging technologies and artificial intelligence in educational contexts. Adapting the items allowed us to preserve the model’s conceptual structure and incorporate terminological adjustments specifically tailored to the context of marketing college students’ use of AI-based tools. Initially, the instrument consisted of 57 items designed to measure the model’s latent variables using a five-point Likert- FIGURE 1 Conceptual model based on UTAUT2 applied to the use of GenAI tools among marketing students. Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 05 frontiersin.org",133
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"type ordinal scale, where 1 represented “strongly disagree” and 5 represented “strongly agree.” Regarding moderator variables, age was measured as a discrete quantitative variable, while gender and university were considered dichotomous variables. In addition, the questionnaire included an open-ended question designed to identify the GenIA tools used by students during their academic studies, with the aim of identifying the platforms most frequently used in the university setting. The instrument was validated using the procedure established for the PLS-SEM measurement model (Hair et al., 2021). To this end, the individual reliability of the indicators was assessed, as well as internal consistency using Cronbach’s alpha and composite reliability; convergent validity was evaluated using average variance extracted (AVE); and discriminant validity was assessed using the HTMT criterion. As a result of this evaluation, four indicators were eliminated for failing to meet the established psychometric criteria, resulting in a final instrument consisting of 53 items (Supplementary Appendix 1). Consequently, the final version demonstrated adequate levels of reliability and validity for use in the evaluation of the structural model. The questionnaire was administered online via",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"psychometric criteria, resulting in a final instrument consisting of 53 items (Supplementary Appendix 1). Consequently, the final version demonstrated adequate levels of reliability and validity for use in the evaluation of the structural model. The questionnaire was administered online via the Google Forms platform between March and June 2026. The link was distributed via institutional emails and WhatsApp groups used by the students. Additionally, support was provided by faculty members responsible for courses related to marketing, who helped disseminate the instrument within their academic settings. Participation was voluntary, anonymous, and required prior acceptance of an electronic informed consent form. 3.4 Statistical analysis Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS- SEM) was employed due to its ability to analyze complex models with multiple latent constructs and simultaneous relationships, as well as its predictive orientation and suitability for explanatory research in the social sciences (Hair et al., 2021). The analysis was performed using SmartPLS software version 4.1.1.8, following the two-stage evaluation procedure proposed by Hair et al. (2019): (1) measurement model evaluation and (2) structural model evaluation. In the evaluation of the",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"social sciences (Hair et al., 2021). The analysis was performed using SmartPLS software version 4.1.1.8, following the two-stage evaluation procedure proposed by Hair et al. (2019): (1) measurement model evaluation and (2) structural model evaluation. In the evaluation of the measurement model, the individual reliability of the indicators was analyzed using external factor loadings, with values above 0.708 considered acceptable. Subsequently, the internal consistency of the constructs was evaluated using Cronbach’s alpha, rho_A, and composite reliability (CR), for which recommended values should be greater than 0.70 (Hair et al., 2021). Likewise, convergent validity was examined using Average Variance Extracted (AVE), with minimum values of 0.50 considered acceptable according to the criteria of Fornell and Larcker (1981). Discriminant validity was verified using the Heterotrait-Monotrait Ratio (HTMT), adopting values below 0.85 as a reference, in accordance with the recommendations of Henseler et al. (2015). To evaluate the structural model, multicollinearity among predictor constructs was examined using the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF), with values below 5 considered acceptable (Hair et al., 2019). Subsequently, path coefficients (β), t-values, and p-values were estimated using a",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"al. (2015). To evaluate the structural model, multicollinearity among predictor constructs was examined using the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF), with values below 5 considered acceptable (Hair et al., 2019). Subsequently, path coefficients (β), t-values, and p-values were estimated using a bootstrapping procedure with 5,000 subsamples to test the proposed hypotheses, with t-values greater than 1.96 and p-values less than 0.05 serving as criteria for significance (Hair et al., 2021). The explanatory power of the model was evaluated using the coefficient of determination (R2), with values of 0.75, 0.50, and 0.25 considered to represent substantial, moderate, and weak levels, respectively (Hair et al., 2019). The effect size of the exogenous constructs was analyzed using the f2 statistic, with reference to Cohen’s (2013) criteria of 0.02 (small), 0.15 (medium), and 0.35 (large). The model’s predictive power was examined using the PLSpredict procedure and the Stone-Geisser Q2 statistic. To this end, a cross-validation scheme was configured with 10 folds and 10 repetitions, using the Early Antecedents approach and a fixed-seed random number generator to ensure the stability and reproducibility of the predictive estimates.",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"and the Stone-Geisser Q2 statistic. To this end, a cross-validation scheme was configured with 10 folds and 10 repetitions, using the Early Antecedents approach and a fixed-seed random number generator to ensure the stability and reproducibility of the predictive estimates. Likewise, the predictive performance of the PLS-SEM model was compared to that of the linear model (LM) using the RMSE and MAE metrics. In accordance with the criteria of Shmueli et al. (2019), Q2_predict values greater than zero were considered evidence of the model’s predictive relevance regarding the endogenous indicators analyzed. Additionally, an IPMA (Importance-Performance Map Analysis) was conducted to identify the constructs with the greatest importance and influence on the intention to use GenAI tools, as well as their level of performance as perceived by the students. This analysis allowed us to identify the strategic variables that require greater attention to strengthen the adoption of GenAI tools among marketing students, following the methodological recommendations proposed by Ringle and Sarstedt (2016). Regarding the open-ended question aimed at identifying the GenAI tools used by students, the responses underwent a process of",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"attention to strengthen the adoption of GenAI tools among marketing students, following the methodological recommendations proposed by Ringle and Sarstedt (2016). Regarding the open-ended question aimed at identifying the GenAI tools used by students, the responses underwent a process of cleaning, standardization, and coding, during which the recorded names were reviewed, grouping lexical variants, abbreviations, and spelling differences corresponding to the same GenAI tool. Subsequently, the data was processed using Python v3.12 in Google Colab, allowing us to calculate frequency of occurrence and generate a word cloud to visualize the platforms most widely adopted among marketing students. 4 Results 4.1 Evaluation of the measurement model Table 3 shows adequate levels of individual reliability for the items, with external factor loadings exceeding the recommended minimum value of 0.708 a criterion accepted for confirming the consistency and representativeness of items with respect to their latent constructs in PLS-SEM models (Hair et al., 2021). The results demonstrate that the retained indicators exhibit a high capacity to explain the variance of their respective dimensions, reinforcing the robustness of the measurement model. Likewise, the adequate",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,6,"to their latent constructs in PLS-SEM models (Hair et al., 2021). The results demonstrate that the retained indicators exhibit a high capacity to explain the variance of their respective dimensions, reinforcing the robustness of the measurement model. Likewise, the adequate magnitude of the external factor loadings confirms the internal consistency among the items associated with the constructs of the UTAUT2 model (Venkatesh et al., 2012), supporting the conceptual validity of the instrument applied in the context of the use of GenAI tools among marketing university students. Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 06 frontiersin.org",95
