DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,9,"the use of GenAI tools is explained by the proposed structural relationships. Likewise, Hair et al. (2019) argue that adjusted R2 provides a more conservative and robust estimate of explanatory power, as it accounts for the number of predictors and the model’s complexity. In this regard, the small difference between the R2 and adjusted R2 values reflects stability in the estimates and supports the predictive consistency of the UTAUT2 model applied to the context of marketing college students. The effect size of the exogenous constructs was assessed using the f2 statistic, taking into account the interpretation criteria proposed by Cohen (2013). The results in Table 8 show that habit (HT) had the greatest effect on behavioral intention to use (BI) (f2 = 0.254) and on the use of GenAI tools (UI) (f2 = 0.250), reflecting a moderate effect size in both cases. Likewise, behavioral intention to use showed a small effect on UI (f2 = 0.125), as did facilitating conditions on UI (f2 = 0.096). In contrast, performance expectancy (PE), effort expectancy (EE), social influence (SI), hedonic motivation (HM), and",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,9,"both cases. Likewise, behavioral intention to use showed a small effect on UI (f2 = 0.125), as did facilitating conditions on UI (f2 = 0.096). In contrast, performance expectancy (PE), effort expectancy (EE), social influence (SI), hedonic motivation (HM), and price value (PV) reported small effect sizes on BI, indicating a relatively smaller contribution within the structural model. As noted, habit is the construct with the greatest practical relevance in explaining the use of GenAI tools among marketing college students. 4.3 Testing of research hypotheses The results in Table 9 show that the relationships regarding performance expectancy (PE → BI; β = 0.037; t = 0.510; p = 0.610), effort expectancy (EE → BI; β = 0.054; t = 1.086; p = 0.277), and social influence (SI → BI; β = 0.048; t = 0.958; p = 0.338) did not reach statistical significance; therefore, hypotheses H1, H2, and H3 were rejected. In contrast, the facilitating conditions showed a positive and significant relationship with the use of GenAI tools (FC → UI; β = 0.219; t = 4.297; p < 0.001),",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,9,"reach statistical significance; therefore, hypotheses H1, H2, and H3 were rejected. In contrast, the facilitating conditions showed a positive and significant relationship with the use of GenAI tools (FC → UI; β = 0.219; t = 4.297; p < 0.001), accepting hypothesis H4. Likewise, hedonic motivation (HM → BI; β = 0.196; t = 2.670; p = 0.008), price value (PV → BI; β = 0.187; t = 2.621; p = 0.009), and habit (HT → BI; β = 0.426; t = 5.522; p < 0.001) showed positive and statistically significant effects on behavioral intention to use, confirming hypotheses H5, H6, and H7. Similarly, the habit showed a positive and significant relationship with the use of GenAI tools (HT → UI; β = 0.412; t = 5.920; p < 0.001), confirming hypothesis H8. Finally, behavioral intention to use had a positive and significant effect on the use of GenAI tools (BI → UI; β = 0.324; t = 4.325; p < 0.001), supporting hypothesis H9. Among the significant structural relationships, habit reported the highest β coefficients for both behavioral intentions",180
