DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"lower overall importance regarding the use of GenAI tools. Similarly, facilitating conditions (FC), price value (PV), and social influence (SI) exhibited low levels of relative importance within the structural model. In this regard, the IPMA analysis identified habit and behavioral intention to use as the constructs with the highest strategic priority within the UTAUT2 model applied to marketing college students. 4.5 GenAI tools adopted by students Figure 3 shows that ChatGPT was the GenAI tool with the highest adoption rate among marketing students, followed by Gemini and Copilot, which stand out visually due to their higher frequency of mention in the collected responses. The use of other platforms such as Perplexity, Canva, Claude, and DeepSeek was also identified, although with a relatively lower presence. The findings demonstrate a strong student preference for LLM-based tools and conversational assistants geared toward content generation, information search, support for academic activities, etc., further highlighting a growing diversification in the ecosystem of GenAI platforms used in university marketing education. 5 Discussion The findings show that habit is the primary determinant of both the behavioral intention",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"generation, information search, support for academic activities, etc., further highlighting a growing diversification in the ecosystem of GenAI platforms used in university marketing education. 5 Discussion The findings show that habit is the primary determinant of both the behavioral intention to use and the actual use of GenAI tools, as evidenced by the highest structural coefficients within the UTAUT2 model. Thus, the use of GenAI tools tends to become established as a recurring and integrated practice within the academic activities of marketing students at the two Latin American universities analyzed, favoring more frequent usage patterns in tasks related to content generation, data analysis, and the development of marketing-related educational activities (Ravšelj et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2026). The predominance of habit as the main predictor of GenAI use aligns with the findings of Mistry et al. (2025), who identified that habit exerts a significant influence on behavioral intention and AI usage behavior among students, reaching explanatory levels of R2 = 0.801 for behavioral intention and R2 = 0.705 for actual usage. Similarly, Grassini et al. (2024) found that habit",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"that habit exerts a significant influence on behavioral intention and AI usage behavior among students, reaching explanatory levels of R2 = 0.801 for behavioral intention and R2 = 0.705 for actual usage. Similarly, Grassini et al. (2024) found that habit and performance expectations were the most influential factors in the intention to use ChatGPT among European university students. Therefore, the recurring use of GenAI tools is beginning to establish itself as a common academic practice within university settings internationally. In contrast, performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influence did not have statistically significant effects on behavioral intention to use in the present study. These findings partially differ from those reported by Grassini et al. (2024), who found that performance expectancy is the strongest predictor of the intention to use ChatGPT. Similarly, Acikgul and Sad (2026), using the UTAUT2 model with 280 educators from schools and universities in Turkey, identified performance expectancy, hedonic motivation, and habit as significant determinants of behavioral intention toward AI software. In contrast, the present study did not find a significant effect of performance expectancy among college",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"280 educators from schools and universities in Turkey, identified performance expectancy, hedonic motivation, and habit as significant determinants of behavioral intention toward AI software. In contrast, the present study did not find a significant effect of performance expectancy among college marketing students, suggesting that students may rely less on perceived utility and more on habitual interaction when adopting generative AI tools in their academic activities. Acosta-Enriquez et al. (2024) argue that performance expectancy and effort expectancy are recurring factors in studies on AI adoption in higher education. However, the results obtained from marketing students at Latin American universities suggest that GenAI tools are no longer perceived solely as emerging or complex technologies, but rather as academic resources incorporated on a daily basis into university educational dynamics, thereby reducing the explanatory influence of factors associated with perceived utility or ease of use. On the other hand, hedonic motivation, perceived value, and enabling conditions had positive and significant effects on behavioral intention and actual use of GenAI tools. These results are consistent with those reported by Arbulú Ballesteros et al. (2024), who",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"use. On the other hand, hedonic motivation, perceived value, and enabling conditions had positive and significant effects on behavioral intention and actual use of GenAI tools. These results are consistent with those reported by Arbulú Ballesteros et al. (2024), who found that enabling conditions had a significant influence on behavioral intention and actual use of ChatGPT among college students. Similarly, Mistry et al. (2025) found that social influence and facilitating conditions significantly promote the use of AI tools when academic environments provide constant access to technological platforms. In this regard, the findings of the present study demonstrate that the availability of technological resources, connectivity, and continuous access to digital tools continue to play a significant role in consolidating the academic use of GenAI within higher education. With regard to moderating variables, age differences influence how marketing students perceive the user experience and establish habitual patterns of interaction with GenAI tools. In particular, students with greater technological familiarity may develop more sustained usage behaviors, thereby strengthening the effect of habit on usage intention. Younger students automate usage through routine, while older",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,12,"experience and establish habitual patterns of interaction with GenAI tools. In particular, students with greater technological familiarity may develop more sustained usage behaviors, thereby strengthening the effect of habit on usage intention. Younger students automate usage through routine, while older students require other conscious stimuli to decide to use it. Similarly, the moderating effect of gender on hedonic motivation indicates differences in the perception of enjoyment, interest, or interaction with GenAI- based tools within the academic environment. Regarding the moderating effect of the university, the findings suggest that institutional contexts, the level of academic FIGURE 3 Word cloud of the GenAI tools most frequently used by marketing students. Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 12 frontiersin.org",117
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"digitization, and access to technological resources influence the transition from behavioral intention to the actual use of GenAI tools, demonstrating that the dynamics of technology adoption do not depend solely on individual factors, but also on the educational and technological conditions present at each university. The moderating variables analyzed have a selective and targeted impact within the UTAUT2 model. While the general structure of technology acceptance tends to be homogeneous across most direct pathways, individual characteristics such as age, gender, and institutional environment act as catalysts in managing enjoyment and in the transition from habit to the effective use of GenAI. The main contribution of this study lies in demonstrating that the adoption of GenAI tools by marketing students does not stem solely from traditional factors of technology acceptance, but is instead emerging as a structural academic practice integrated into university educational processes. In this regard, the findings suggest an evolution in usage behavior from an approach centered on the initial perception of usefulness or ease of use toward more automated, recurring dynamics contextualized by students’ digital academic routines. Thus,",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"university educational processes. In this regard, the findings suggest an evolution in usage behavior from an approach centered on the initial perception of usefulness or ease of use toward more automated, recurring dynamics contextualized by students’ digital academic routines. Thus, the research provides an updated perspective on how GenAI technologies are redefining forms of interaction, academic production, and the development of digital competencies in Latin American higher education. From a theoretical perspective, the results expand the applicability of the UTAUT2 model proposed by Venkatesh et al. (2012) regarding the use of GenAI in higher education in marketing. Although the model posits that constructs such as performance expectancy, effort expectancy, and social influence significantly influence the intention to use technology, the findings of this study show that, among marketing students, variables associated with recurring patterns of interaction, such as habit, take on greater explanatory relevance. In this regard, the results suggest that the adoption of GenAI tools may be transitioning from initial stages of technological acceptance toward more established dynamics of academic integration, characterized by automated, frequent forms of use linked",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"on greater explanatory relevance. In this regard, the results suggest that the adoption of GenAI tools may be transitioning from initial stages of technological acceptance toward more established dynamics of academic integration, characterized by automated, frequent forms of use linked to university digital routines. 6 Conclusions The research reveals a shift in the dynamics of technology adoption within higher education, where the use of GenAI tools is increasingly driven less by initial perceptions of usefulness or ease of use and more by recurring patterns of academic integration. In this context, habit is the primary factor associated with both behavioral intention and the actual use of GenAI tools, reflecting a gradual process of incorporating these technologies into the learning routines of marketing students. Likewise, the relevance of hedonic motivation, enabling conditions, and price value demonstrates that the interaction experience, technological access, and availability of digital resources continue to play an important role in consolidating the academic use of AI-based tools. Therefore, students no longer view AI Gen as a novelty; they use it automatically in their daily lives. Students recognize its",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"and availability of digital resources continue to play an important role in consolidating the academic use of AI-based tools. Therefore, students no longer view AI Gen as a novelty; they use it automatically in their daily lives. Students recognize its true value when the institution promotes its responsible use, does not reject it, and incorporates it as a core competency of the programs it offers. The results also suggest that the integration of AI-based tools among marketing students is taking place within increasingly digitized academic environments, where AI-based technologies are beginning to establish themselves as fundamental resources for content generation, task automation, and support for learning processes. The differences identified based on moderating variables such as age, gender, and university suggest that the dynamics of technology adoption are also influenced by institutional factors present in each university environment. Consequently, the real driving force for students is that the classroom experience be stimulating and that the university provide them with the necessary infrastructure and technical support to enhance what they already know how to do, thereby fostering user experience, habit, and",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"real driving force for students is that the classroom experience be stimulating and that the university provide them with the necessary infrastructure and technical support to enhance what they already know how to do, thereby fostering user experience, habit, and a positive attitude toward its responsible use. 6.1 Practical implications Based on the findings, universities need to develop professional training strategies that incorporate skills related to the critical and strategic use of AI tools within academic marketing programs. The results show that the use of GenAI tools is already part of students’ routine learning dynamics; therefore, higher education institutions must move toward curricular models that integrate AI literacy, digital ethics, and the practical application of emerging technologies in real-world marketing and digital business scenarios. It is also pertinent for universities to establish institutional guidelines to regulate the academic use of GenAI tools (Perdomo and Alberto González, 2025), thereby reducing risks associated with technological dependence, misinformation, and inappropriate academic practices. In the field of teacher education, the findings suggest a need to strengthen pedagogical competencies related to the design of AI-",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"GenAI tools (Perdomo and Alberto González, 2025), thereby reducing risks associated with technological dependence, misinformation, and inappropriate academic practices. In the field of teacher education, the findings suggest a need to strengthen pedagogical competencies related to the design of AI- mediated learning experiences (Xu et al., 2024). Given the importance of habit and hedonic motivation within the structural model, teachers could promote activities focused on experiential learning, case studies, content creation, and simulations supported by GenAI tools, thereby encouraging more active student participation. Furthermore, the growing use of GenAI-based platforms requires teachers to adopt new assessment approaches focused on creativity, critical analysis, and the strategic application of knowledge, moving beyond traditional activities that are susceptible to automation. On the other hand, the implications for students relate to the need to develop skills focused on the responsible, efficient, and professional use of AI-based tools (Puyén Fernández, 2024). The widespread adoption of platforms such as ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot demonstrates that students already interact constantly with AI-based technologies; however, the current challenge lies not only in using them but also in understanding",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,13,"of AI-based tools (Puyén Fernández, 2024). The widespread adoption of platforms such as ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot demonstrates that students already interact constantly with AI-based technologies; however, the current challenge lies not only in using them but also in understanding their limitations, validating the generated information, and employing them as complementary tools for decision-making, creativity, and academic productivity. In this regard, mastery of GenAI tools could progressively become a distinguishing competency within professional profiles related to digital Pinedo et al. 10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057 Frontiers in Education 13 frontiersin.org",86
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,14,"marketing and business digital transformation; therefore, students require institutional training programs that seek to incorporate AI in a specialized manner, fostering habits that support their professional development. The practical implications also extend to the business sector, as organizations are seeking professionals capable of interacting with AI-mediated digital environments. The results suggest that skills related to automation, content generation, and AI-assisted analysis, among others, will begin to establish themselves as key competencies for employability in marketing, communication, and digital business. Therefore, strengthening the integration of AI tools into higher education curricula could help bridge the gap between the skills developed in university and the technological demands of today’s job market. 6.2 Limitations and future research directions This study has several limitations that should be considered when interpreting the results. First, the research was conducted exclusively among marketing students at two Latin American universities, which limits the generalizability of the findings to other academic disciplines, institutional contexts, or geographic regions. Furthermore, the cross-sectional design does not allow for an analysis of changes in patterns of adoption and use of GenAI tools over",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,14,"American universities, which limits the generalizability of the findings to other academic disciplines, institutional contexts, or geographic regions. Furthermore, the cross-sectional design does not allow for an analysis of changes in patterns of adoption and use of GenAI tools over time, especially given the rapid technological evolution of these platforms. Similarly, the data were collected through participants’ self-reports, so there may be biases associated with subjectivity, social desirability, or variations in prior experience with GenAI-based tools. In this regard, future research could expand the analysis by incorporating multicultural and multidisciplinary samples, thereby enabling a comparison of differences in the adoption processes of AI-based tools across various fields of professional training and educational contexts. Furthermore, it is recommended that longitudinal studies be conducted to assess changes in the use of AI-based tools among college students as these technologies continue to be integrated into academic processes. Similarly, future research could incorporate additional variables related to digital literacy, AI ethics, critical thinking, academic performance, or employability skills, with the aim of deepening our understanding of the educational and professional effects associated with the",180
DOC_040,NEW24.pdf,10.3389/feduc.2026.1892057,14,"integrated into academic processes. Similarly, future research could incorporate additional variables related to digital literacy, AI ethics, critical thinking, academic performance, or employability skills, with the aim of deepening our understanding of the educational and professional effects associated with the use of GenAI tools in higher education. Data availability statement The datasets presented in this study can be found in online repositories. The names of the repository/repositories and accession number(s) can be found in the article/Supplementary Material. Ethics statement The studies involving humans were approved by the Institutional Committee on Ethics and Scientific Integrity at Norbert Wiener Private University approved the project under file number A0085-2025. The studies were conducted in accordance with the local legislation and institutional requirements. The participants provided their written informed consent to participate in this study. Written informed consent was obtained from the minor(s)’ legal guardian/next of kin for the publication of any potentially identifiable images or data included in this article. Author contributions LP: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Resources, Software, Supervision, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing –",180
