DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,2,"Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence Andrew Williams Department of Education, Division of Medicine, University College London, London, UK ABSTRACT Generative AI tools such as ChatGPT are increasingly impacting the academic landscape, provoking discussions about their potential role in student learning. A clearer understanding of the extent to which students are adopting generative AI is critical to enable educators and universities to properly adapt to advances in AI technology. This longitudinal study examines the attitudes and usage patterns of first-year undergraduate students towards gen­ erative AI and whether these have evolved over a 12-month period. The results indicate a substantial increase in student engagement with generative AI, highlighting a growing familiarisation and con­ fidence in using these tools. Moreover, there is a significant increase in students willing to incorporate generative AI into their studies, suggesting a notable shift towards students recognising the bene­ fits of AI. The rapid evolution of student adoption of generative AI underscores the importance of higher education institutions adapt­ ing to and supporting student engagement with generative AI, emphasising",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,2,"studies, suggesting a notable shift towards students recognising the bene­ fits of AI. The rapid evolution of student adoption of generative AI underscores the importance of higher education institutions adapt­ ing to and supporting student engagement with generative AI, emphasising the need for flexible institutional policies, targeted technology support frameworks and innovative curriculum designs to align with the evolving competencies and acceptance of AI among students. ARTICLE HISTORY Received 24 March 2025 Accepted 15 February 2026 KEYWORDS Generative artificial intelligence; higher education; student learning; AI; ChatGPT; large language models Introduction The application and use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) and large language models (LMMs) have gained much interest in higher education, particularly since the release of GPT3.5 in November 2022. The ability of generative AI tools, like ChatGPT, to produce human-like output in response to various prompts has deepened discussions about the possibility of teachers and students employing AI for various educational tasks. However, the rapid proliferation of generative AI tools and the advances in their capabilities have presented challenges to higher education systems regarding plagiar­ ism, bias, effectiveness and",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,2,"about the possibility of teachers and students employing AI for various educational tasks. However, the rapid proliferation of generative AI tools and the advances in their capabilities have presented challenges to higher education systems regarding plagiar­ ism, bias, effectiveness and their acceptable ethical application (Cotton, Cotton, and Shipway 2024; Farrelly and Baker 2023; Zeb, Ullah, and Karim 2024). Despite these challenges, generative AI is a disruptive and potentially transformative technology that could be harnessed to positively impact teaching and learning activities, assessment design and university curricula (Adiguzel, Kaya, and Cansu 2023; Farrelly and Baker 2023; Michel-Villarreal et al. 2023). CONTACT Andrew Williams andrew.williams@ucl.ac.uk Department of Education, Division of Medicine, University College London, Rayne Building, 5 University Street, London WC1E 6JF, UK JOURNAL OF FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION https://doi.org/10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035 © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group. This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any med­ ium, provided the original work is properly cited, and is",180
