DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,3,"There are many opportunities to deploy AI tools in higher education environments, including using LLMs as an instrument to enhance student learning (Chan and Hu 2023; Kurtz et al. 2024; Wang 2023). For example, students could employ AI as a personal tutoring system, an idea generator, writing assistant, a repository of easily accessible knowledge and as a provider of immediate feedback (Deng et al. 2024; Miao and Shiohira 2024; Ogunleye et al. 2024). However, using AI to enrich the educational experience must be balanced with robust ethical guidelines, transparent institutional policies and sub­ stantial training for students on the responsible use of generative AI tools (Dai, Liu, and Lim 2023; Xia et al. 2024). Higher education providers therefore face considerable chal­ lenges to rapidly adopt and adapt to these new and emergent AI technologies. Academics and higher education institutions have raised concerns about the impact that generative AI may have on assessments, academic dishonesty, academic integrity and the possible degradation of student cognitive and critical thinking skills (Larson et al. 2024; Moongela et al. 2024). Conversely, many educators and students",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,3,"raised concerns about the impact that generative AI may have on assessments, academic dishonesty, academic integrity and the possible degradation of student cognitive and critical thinking skills (Larson et al. 2024; Moongela et al. 2024). Conversely, many educators and students advocate for the benefits that AI tools may bring to the learning process, with some evidence suggesting that AI may enhance academic performance, affective-motivational states and higher- order thinking, while concomitantly reducing mental effort (Deng et al. 2024). Integrating AI into teaching practices and assessment modalities is further considered to provide students with opportunities to understand the limitations and capabilities of LLMs and thereby enhance authentic skills that are likely to be desired by future employers (Nartey 2024; A. Williams 2025). However, the success of integrating AI into higher education is contingent on the willingness and ability of educators to embrace and implement technological advances (Kaplan-Rakowski et al. 2023; Lee et al. 2024). Furthermore, the potential for hallucinations or inaccurate content, and concerns surrounding algorithmic bias may require considerable tutoring and user oversight (Chan and Tsi 2024; R. T. Williams",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,3,"embrace and implement technological advances (Kaplan-Rakowski et al. 2023; Lee et al. 2024). Furthermore, the potential for hallucinations or inaccurate content, and concerns surrounding algorithmic bias may require considerable tutoring and user oversight (Chan and Tsi 2024; R. T. Williams 2024). Student perceptions on the possible benefits of generative AI are varied and may depend on the frequency of use and expectations about individual learning outcomes (Chan and Tsi 2024). The impact of student perception on learning outcomes also plays a crucial role in the successful adoption of technological innovations (Biggs, Tang, and Kennedy 2022). In general, students are more positive about using generative AI tools than most teachers, which may reflect their comfort and familiarity with using new technologies (Arowosegbe, Alqahtani, and Oyelade 2024). Students appreciate the rapid and iterative quality of generative AI, the immediate feedback and the support for academic tasks that promote their learning (Baidoo-Anu et al. 2024; R. T. Williams 2024). However, student concerns include the inappropriate use of generative AI in assessments and the possibility of receiving incorrect or misleading information (Chan and Hu",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,3,"support for academic tasks that promote their learning (Baidoo-Anu et al. 2024; R. T. Williams 2024). However, student concerns include the inappropriate use of generative AI in assessments and the possibility of receiving incorrect or misleading information (Chan and Hu 2023; Johnston et al. 2024). Students are also aware of the inherent biases of LLMs and associated ethical considerations, including disparities in access and technological com­ petencies. Concerns regarding the over-reliance on AI and the deterioration in critical thinking and problem-solving skills have also been highlighted (Chan and Tsi 2024). Despite these apprehensions, students agree that AI utilisation will likely increase and that universities should incorporate AI into curricula (Arowosegbe, Alqahtani, and Oyelade 2024). Student engagement with generative AI likely depends on their attitude and accep­ tance of this emerging technology (Chan and Hu 2023). This will subsequently influence 2 A. WILLIAMS",142
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,4,"the extent to which generative AI is incorporated into teaching and learning practices (Wood and Moss 2024). Higher education institutions and programmes must therefore adapt to the changing technological landscape to best support the student learning experience in the context of generative AI. This includes the provision of appropriate guidelines and AI support frameworks, which are developed in response to consensual or prevailing views on academic and student requirements. The research therefore aims to elucidate whether student attitudes and willingness to use generative AI tools have changed over time. The principal research questions being proposed were: RQ1. Has the use of generative AI by students changed over time? RQ2. Have university students’ application of generative AI for their studies changed over time? The research employs an in-class evaluation survey of first-year undergraduate students enrolled in a medical science degree programme at a leading UK university. Students were surveyed across two consecutive years of study to gain a snapshot of possible changes in generative AI engagement and use. The study highlights a rapid shift in student attitudes on the use of",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,4,"at a leading UK university. Students were surveyed across two consecutive years of study to gain a snapshot of possible changes in generative AI engagement and use. The study highlights a rapid shift in student attitudes on the use of generative AI. The shift to a more receptive attitude, in a relatively short span of time, may therefore impact the approaches that institutions and educators should take towards the deployment and integration of AI in teaching, learning and assessment practices. Methods Course design Students were enrolled in an integrated medical sciences undergraduate programme. The first year of study consisted of eight modules incorporating human physiology, biochem­ istry, cell biology and data science, which included a varied assessment portfolio consist­ ing of group work, presentations, academic writing and standard multiple-choice question examinations. The current study was drawn from aspects of an academic integrity and assessment workshop, which aimed to train and support students during their formative and summative assessments. In addition to guidance on institutional academic integrity and plagiarism policies, the workshops incorporated guidance and training on the responsible and ethical",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,4,"academic integrity and assessment workshop, which aimed to train and support students during their formative and summative assessments. In addition to guidance on institutional academic integrity and plagiarism policies, the workshops incorporated guidance and training on the responsible and ethical use of generative AI. Participant information and research approach All participants were first-year undergraduate students at a UK university, enrolled in a medical sciences BSc programme and undertaking an in-course workshop on aca­ demic integrity and the use of generative artificial intelligence. The survey used an in- class Mentimeter (https://www.mentimeter.com/) session to gain insights into the use and aptitude of current undergraduate students regarding generative AI. The primary investigator invigilated the workshop and in-class evaluation survey in both academic years. The in-class survey was anonymised throughout. The questions posed to JOURNAL OF FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION 3",137
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,5,"students were as follows: 1) Have you used ChatGPT or other generative AI tools; 2) What generative AI tools have you used; 3) How confident are you using ChatGPT or other generative AI tools; 4) Would you consider using generative AI in your studies; and 5) What aspects of your learning would generative AI be useful for? Participants were sampled in term 1 of the 2023–2024 and term 1 of the 2024–2025 academic year cycles. Survey analysis The Mentimeter survey consisted of four questions using a combination of yes/no, open ended and Likert scale questions. Comparison of the yes/no responses between the two student cohorts was statistically analysed using descriptive statistics, Pearson’s Chi- squared and Mann–Whitney U tests. Responses to the Likert scale questions were ana­ lysed using a Mann–Whitney U test. In all cases, a p-value below 0.05 was deemed statistically significant. Open-ended text responses for question 2 were collated and ordered, and each generative AI tool used by students was summated. The percentage of students using each tool was then calculated. Open-ended text responses for question 5 were",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,5,"deemed statistically significant. Open-ended text responses for question 2 were collated and ordered, and each generative AI tool used by students was summated. The percentage of students using each tool was then calculated. Open-ended text responses for question 5 were collated and ordered, and each generative AI tool usage or application was summated. The data were familiarised, and recurrent keywords were identified. All keywords were descriptive and therefore coded without alteration. Thematic analysis was then applied to the data according to the methods employed by Braun and Clarke (2006), grouping keywords based on the shared relationship to student learning activity (Braun and Clarke 2006). Ethics statement The study involved the use of a non-sensitive, completely anonymised educational survey as part of an existing in-class academic integrity information workshop. The participants were not defined as vulnerable nor did it induce undue psychological stress or anxiety. Therefore, specific ethics approval was not required under institutional and national guidelines. Results This longitudinal study investigated changes in first-year undergraduate students’ atti­ tudes towards and engagement with generative AI over a 12-month period. Tracking student",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,5,"psychological stress or anxiety. Therefore, specific ethics approval was not required under institutional and national guidelines. Results This longitudinal study investigated changes in first-year undergraduate students’ atti­ tudes towards and engagement with generative AI over a 12-month period. Tracking student attitudes and engagement with AI is vital to inform on the adaptation of existing higher education institution policies and frameworks regarding generative AI. Data were collected via an in-class evaluation survey administered to cohorts of BSc Medical Sciences students at a UK university, encompassing questions regarding AI usage, tool familiarity, confidence levels and perceived utility for academic purposes. Student use of ChatGPT or other generative AI tools The first research question aimed to ascertain the proportion of students that were actively engaged with or were inclined to use generative AI tools, and whether this 4 A. WILLIAMS",137
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,6,"attitude changed over a 12-month period. In 2023 (n = 82), 52.4% of students stated they had used ChatGPT or another generative AI tools, compared to 48.6% who had not (Figure 1). In 2024 (n = 78), 85.9% of students stated they had used ChatGPT or another generative AI tool, compared to 14.1% who had not. Therefore, in a 12-month period, the proportion of students actively engaged with generative AI increased by 33.5%, suggest­ ing a rapid normalisation of generative AI within the student population over a relatively short timeframe and reflecting increased awareness and accessibility of these technologies. Types of generative AI tools used by students To gain an insight into the type of generative AI tools students were using, an open question was asked. In 2023 and 2024, the dominant generative AI tool used was ChatGPT (Table 1), with more students in 2024 using ChatGPT relative to other tools Figure 1. Student use of generative AI in 2023 versus 2024 (Pearson’s Chi2 Asymp. Sig. <0.001; Mann– Whitney U = 0.00026). Table 1. Students use of generative AI tools.",180
