DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,7,"compared to 2023 (61.8 versus 71.4%). In 2023, Google Bing/Gemini was the second most popular AI tool (12.7%), which decreased in popularity in 2024 (5.3%), with Microsoft Copilot (12.3%) and Anthropic’s Claude (3.5%) increasing in popularity in 2024, suggesting students may be consolidating their use around more versatile chatbot platforms that offer broader functionality beyond simple grammar checking. As a further example, in 2023, students stated they used both Grammarly (9.1%) and Quillbot (7.3%) but the popularity of these AI tools diminished in 2024. The other AI tools that students used included MyAI, ChatSonic, Baidu, NotionAI, Stable Diffusion and SunoAI, although the proportion of students that used any one of these was <1.8%. Student confidence in generative AI tools use The confidence and aptitude of students using generative AI tools is an important consideration, as equity in technological competency is critical for the successful integra­ tion of AI into contemporary university curricula. In 2023 (n = 79), students who stated they were not confident using generative AI were 39.2%, while in 2024 (n = 71) this decreased to 16.9% (Figure",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,7,"critical for the successful integra­ tion of AI into contemporary university curricula. In 2023 (n = 79), students who stated they were not confident using generative AI were 39.2%, while in 2024 (n = 71) this decreased to 16.9% (Figure 2). Those students who stated they were somewhat confident were 43% in 2023 compared to 57.7% in 2024. Furthermore, the proportion of students who stated they were very confident in 2023 was 17.7%, while in 2024 the proportion of students who were very confident at using generative AI increased to 25.4%, indicating an increase in the confidence of students using generative AI over a 12-month period. When combined, the proportion of students indicating they are somewhat or very confident using ChatGPT or other generative AI increased significantly in 2024 (83.1%) compared to Figure 2. Student confidence in suing generative AI in 2023 versus 2024 (somewhat and very confident - Mann–Whitney U p-value = 0.02). 6 A. WILLIAMS",157
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,8,"2023 (60.8%), indicating not only greater exposure to generative AI but also a growing sense of competence among students in its application. Considerations for using generative AI in study Although students may use generative AI for a variety of personal activities, previous studies indicate that some may be reluctant to incorporate this technology into their study and learning process (Johnston et al. 2024). However, it is unclear how student attitudes have changed over time. The results indicate that in 2023, the proportion of students who stated that they would not use generative AI in their studies was 20.3%, while in 2024 this proportion decreased to 6.25% (Figure 3). Conversely, the proportion of students who stated they would definitely use generative AI in 2023 was 25.3%, which increased in 2024 to 40.6%, indicating an increase in students use over a 12-month period. While approximately half of students remained undecided (maybe), this shift underscores a growing recognition of the potential of generative AI for academic studies. Aspects of student study where generative AI is being used Both groups of students stated that",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,8,"period. While approximately half of students remained undecided (maybe), this shift underscores a growing recognition of the potential of generative AI for academic studies. Aspects of student study where generative AI is being used Both groups of students stated that they would use generative AI for a variety of learning tasks, with 34 different keywords identified for the 2023 cohort and 48 different keywords identified for the 2024 cohort, thereby indicating an increase in task diversity (Table 2). Analysis of open-ended responses revealed that summaris­ ing and explaining complex topics, grammar checking and idea generation were consistently identified as the most valuable applications of generative AI by Figure 3. Student attitude to using generative in their studies in 2023 versus 2024 (definitely use generative AI - Mann–Whitney U p-value = 0.02034). JOURNAL OF FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION 7",138
