DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,10,"Discussion This longitudinal study presents a snapshot of the rapid changes in university student attitudes towards ChatGPT and generative AI use over a 12-month period. A clearer understanding of university student acceptance and use of generative AI tools is impor­ tant for educators and higher education institutions to properly align their policies, teaching practices and assessment modalities considering the advances in AI technolo­ gies. The results indicate a rapid shift in student attitudes and acceptance of the technol­ ogy. The findings highlight a notable increase in student engagement with generative AI tools over a relatively short timeframe, suggesting that generative AI has quickly become widely recognised as a valuable resource for students within higher education settings. It is clear from this and other studies that university students are quickly adopting generative AI technologies. For example, a 2023 poll conducted by the Higher Education Policy Institute (HEPI) in the UK revealed that at least 53% of surveyed students had used generative AI to help with assessments and that 66% consider it acceptable to use AI to explain concepts, with more than",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,10,"the Higher Education Policy Institute (HEPI) in the UK revealed that at least 53% of surveyed students had used generative AI to help with assessments and that 66% consider it acceptable to use AI to explain concepts, with more than 50% stating they would use AI to summarise research articles and to suggest research ideas (Freeman 2024). A follow-up HEPI survey in 2025 revealed that 92% of students were using AI in at least some aspects of their studies (Freeman 2025), indicting a significant increase in the adoption of AI technology. This aligns with the current study that demonstrated a 33.5% increase in the adoption of generative AI in just 1 year. In another study, it was revealed that both undergraduate and postgraduate students are adopting AI, although the acceptance of generative AI tech­ nologies may be age-dependent, with postgraduate students more likely to look favour­ ably on its use (Arowosegbe, Alqahtani, and Oyelade 2024). However, the 2023 HEPI survey did caution that a higher proportion of students from more privileged back­ grounds are using AI compared to those from",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,10,"students more likely to look favour­ ably on its use (Arowosegbe, Alqahtani, and Oyelade 2024). However, the 2023 HEPI survey did caution that a higher proportion of students from more privileged back­ grounds are using AI compared to those from less privileged backgrounds, which may necessitate higher education institutions to consider the impact of generative AI on widening the digital divide. ChatGPT was the dominant generative AI tool used among students in both 2023 and 2024, although the data revealed a shift in the secondary tools used, with Microsoft Copilot and Anthropic Claude gaining popularity in 2024. Conversely, the popularity of specialised writing support tools like Grammarly dropped to zero in 2024, indicating that students may be adopting generative AI chatbots that possess broader applications or possess multimodal functionalities. Previous studies have indicated that students are using generative AI tools for grammar and writing assistance (Freeman 2024), although the switch to chatbot use may indicate that students perceive these tools to be of more value. These findings further suggest that consistent updating of information on application usage by students is",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,10,"for grammar and writing assistance (Freeman 2024), although the switch to chatbot use may indicate that students perceive these tools to be of more value. These findings further suggest that consistent updating of information on application usage by students is required, to keep pace with these rapid changes in AI tool preferences. In addition, these preferences could easily change should emergent applications appear that better suit student’s needs. At present, ChatGPT appears to be the AI tool of choice, which was the focus of a recent global survey of 23,218 students from 109 countries (Ravšelj et al. 2025). The study revealed that students primarily used ChatGPT for brainstorming, summarising texts, finding research articles and for academic and creative writing. They considered ChatGPT to be capable of improving their learning and grades, although the study also revealed students had concerns about ChatGPT JOURNAL OF FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION 9",148
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,11,"potentiating cheating, plagiarism and social isolation. The study further indicated that students considered ChatGPT to be unreliable at providing information and that it did not promote critical thinking skills. Furthermore, the current study revealed that summarisation, research and idea generation also dominated student AI usage, aligning with previous studies. In the current study, a key finding was the growing confidence among students in using AI tools. Between 2023 and 2024, the proportion of students reporting high levels of confidence in using generative AI increased. This implies that students are rapidly gaining familiarity and competence in generative AI applications. This shift in confidence high­ lights the importance of providing guidance, training and support to ensure equitable access and skill development across student populations. Importantly, higher education institutions should acknowledge the normalisation of generative AI among students and adapt their AI policies and frameworks accordingly. Across the higher education sector, guidelines and policies on the use of AI vary, including those on the permitted use of AI for assessments (Chan 2023). Navigating institutional AI regulations can be challenging for students, and the",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,11,"and frameworks accordingly. Across the higher education sector, guidelines and policies on the use of AI vary, including those on the permitted use of AI for assessments (Chan 2023). Navigating institutional AI regulations can be challenging for students, and the sector is some distance away from agreeing a unified AI framework. Nevertheless, individual institutions should provide clear and precise guidelines for students, set boundaries early on and support students by engaging and training them on the responsible and ethical use of generative AI (Kurtz et al. 2024). The growing influence of generative AI in higher education is recognised by both students and educators, although their attitudes towards its use, implications and gov­ ernance often differ (Chan and Tsi 2024). Students tend to emphasise the practical utility and potential for learning enhancement, whereas educators are more concerned about the impact of AI on academic integrity, erosion of cognitive development and institutional readiness (Khlaif et al. 2024; Smolansky et al. 2023). It is therefore imperative that educators are also trained on the ethical and responsible use of generative AI and its capabilities",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,11,"AI on academic integrity, erosion of cognitive development and institutional readiness (Khlaif et al. 2024; Smolansky et al. 2023). It is therefore imperative that educators are also trained on the ethical and responsible use of generative AI and its capabilities and limitations in higher education. Keeping pace with emergent technolo­ gies is challenging and costly in terms of resources and time. However, this continued professional development is important for the successful implementation of institutional policies and for the progressive integration of AI into teaching and learning modalities. Furthermore, embedding AI literacy into both curricula and professional training is key to closing disparities between the attitudes of students and educators towards the practical applications of AI. The increased willingness to incorporate AI into academic work further empha­ sises the acceptance of these tools among students. The proportion of students stating that they would ‘definitely use’ generative AI nearly doubled over the 12- month period, suggesting a growing recognition of AI’s potential to benefit their academic studies. However, it is worth noting that approximately half of the stu­ dents remained in the",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,11,"they would ‘definitely use’ generative AI nearly doubled over the 12- month period, suggesting a growing recognition of AI’s potential to benefit their academic studies. However, it is worth noting that approximately half of the stu­ dents remained in the ‘maybe’ category each year, possibly reflecting ongoing concerns about the reliability of AI-generated content. Despite students having a positive reception towards ChatGPT and perceiving it as a tool for idea creation and cognitive offloading, debate persists as to the benefit of generative AI on student learning and performance (Deng et al. 2024). Although AI may offer some beneficial opportunities for students, including perceived immediate and unbiased feedback, maintaining student academic integrity remains a significant concern for educators (Xia et al. 2024). Students have also acknowledged the potential of AI as 10 A. WILLIAMS",133
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,12,"a source of knowledge, a resource for explaining concepts and improving technical understanding, an idea generator and as a personal learning agent or tutor (Almassaad, Alajlan, and Alebaikan 2024). It is therefore important for educators to be prepared for changes to assessment modalities and for the integration of AI technologies into teaching practices. In relation to assessments, teachers are encouraged to focus on fostering critical thinking skills, problem-solving and creativity by incorporating these elements into the design of assign­ ments while mitigating for the challenges associated with AI and academic integrity (Xia et al. 2024). This could include adopting innovative assessments that either elude AI interference, for example by introducing more problem-solving or higher-order thinking tasks or fully integrating AI into the assessment design, which has the added benefits of promoting AI literacy among students and emphasises authentic professional competen­ cies (Sharples 2023; A. Williams 2025). Regarding teaching practices, AI has the potential to supplement creative and inno­ vative learning experiences. For example, AI could cultivate critical thinking by presenting students with complex problems that require higher-order thinking, generating case",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,12,"cies (Sharples 2023; A. Williams 2025). Regarding teaching practices, AI has the potential to supplement creative and inno­ vative learning experiences. For example, AI could cultivate critical thinking by presenting students with complex problems that require higher-order thinking, generating case studies or problem-solving tasks, enhance personalised learning pathways and enhance the virtual learning environment (Groza and Marginean 2023; Walter 2024). In addition, higher education institutions must rethink their assessment and teaching approaches and AI policies to uphold academic integrity while providing a meaningful learning experi­ ence. Supporting AI literacy training, including on the capabilities and limitations of AI, as well as the ethics and responsible use of AI, will be critical to establish an educational environment where students and teachers can succeed in a rapidly evolving digital landscape. Taken together, these results point to a rapid normalisation of generative AI usage among university students, including shifts in tool preference, confidence levels and willingness to employ AI into learning and study practices. The need for flexible, transparent and adaptable institutional policies on generative AI use, targeted technology support frameworks and innovative",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,12,"usage among university students, including shifts in tool preference, confidence levels and willingness to employ AI into learning and study practices. The need for flexible, transparent and adaptable institutional policies on generative AI use, targeted technology support frameworks and innovative curriculum design should reflect the growing com­ petencies and acceptance of generative AI among students. Future work To sustain AI integration in higher education, institutions should adopt proactive and iterative strategies. However, for this approach to be successful, further research in this area is therefore required on the impact of AI on assessment, the impact on student critical thinking and cognitive development and the impact of incorporating AI into curricula and teaching modalities. Additional studies on the effect of ethical and AI literacy training, on both students and educators, are also needed to further explore and understand the utility of AI in higher education. Although the current study included longitudinal data, it represents only a snapshot of student acceptance of generative AI technology, as data are from a limited cohort and discipline. Future research should incorporate students from multiple disciplines",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,12,"AI in higher education. Although the current study included longitudinal data, it represents only a snapshot of student acceptance of generative AI technology, as data are from a limited cohort and discipline. Future research should incorporate students from multiple disciplines within STEM subjects and the humanities, including subjects such as the social sciences and languages. Although beyond the scope of the current study, these studies would offer a broader perspective on student uptake of AI and potentially reveal subject-specific differences in its application and acceptance. In addition, sampling the attitudes and AI use JOURNAL OF FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION 11",100
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,13,"among academics and professional service staff would enable parallel tracking of AI usage across both students and staff, thereby helping to align policy across institutions. As well as longitudinal and disciplinary comparisons of student use, confidence and application of generative AI technologies, future studies could focus on the reasons why students are turning to AI for their learning and assessments. Student motivations for using generative AI include cognitive offloading and avoiding risk, thereby circumventing difficult learning tasks (Zhai, Wibowo, and Li 2024), although individual student motiva­ tions for doing so remain unclear (Pavone 2025). Other reasons include perceived improvement in academic quality and exploiting the language capabilities of LLMs to improve grammar and writing, especially where non-native language support is required (Chan and Hu 2023; Sousa and Cardoso 2025; Sun and Zhou 2024). However, the motiva­ tional needs of students are multifaceted and require deeper exploration across diverse student populations to fully understand the breadth of incentives for using generative AI tools at university. Tracking student engagement with generative AI over multiple academic years to assess trends in tool preference,",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,13,"are multifaceted and require deeper exploration across diverse student populations to fully understand the breadth of incentives for using generative AI tools at university. Tracking student engagement with generative AI over multiple academic years to assess trends in tool preference, confidence and academic outcomes would further reveal the changing attitudes of students longitudinally. This could further include investigating the digital divide in AI adoption, particularly among students from underprivileged back­ grounds. This would highlight areas of generative AI literacy and practice that could be targeted to ensure equitable training and access. Cross-departmental, faculty and institu­ tional studies would further determine digital and AI inadequacies, which would have important implications for institutional and national AI policies across the sector. These studies would be important to undertake to promote consistency and best practice in adopting generative AI technologies in higher education. Recommendations (1) Develop clear institutional policies for generative AI use. Create comprehensive guidelines that define acceptable uses and misuses of generative AI in coursework and assessments. Ensure these policies address concerns such as plagiarism, academic integrity and data security while remaining",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,13,"(1) Develop clear institutional policies for generative AI use. Create comprehensive guidelines that define acceptable uses and misuses of generative AI in coursework and assessments. Ensure these policies address concerns such as plagiarism, academic integrity and data security while remaining flexible to adapt to evolving technologies. (2) Integrate comprehensive AI literacy into curricula. Embed training modules for students that cover the strengths and limitations of generative AI, its potential biases and ethical considerations. (3) Redesign assessments to mitigate or integrate AI. Transition away from traditional assessments that focus on rote memorisation towards assignments that require critical analysis, synthesis, creativity and problem-solving. For example, incorporate project-based tasks and real-world scenarios that challenge students to apply their knowledge in innovative ways. (4) Promote generative AI as a teaching tool. Use generative AI not only as a study aid but also as an active component of teaching. For example, explore blended learn­ ing approaches where AI complements traditional instruction, thereby enriching the virtual learning environment and enhancing student engagement. (5) Ensure equitable access and support for students. Address the digital divide by ensuring",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,13,"active component of teaching. For example, explore blended learn­ ing approaches where AI complements traditional instruction, thereby enriching the virtual learning environment and enhancing student engagement. (5) Ensure equitable access and support for students. Address the digital divide by ensuring all students can access technology and training. This may involve 12 A. WILLIAMS",53
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,14,"developing support frameworks that ensure all learners can benefit from genera­ tive AI tools. (6) Commit to ongoing professional development for educators. Invest in continuous faculty development so that educators are aware of emerging AI technologies and effective pedagogical strategies. Conclusion The study confirms a rapid and significant shift in university student attitudes towards generative AI, with a marked increase in adoption, confidence and will­ ingness to use AI for academic purposes over a 12-month period. This underscores the need for higher education institutions to adapt swiftly to emerging and evolving iterations of generative AI technologies. This adaption is vital to update institutional policies and support frameworks and to responsibly embed AI literacy into curricula and training programmes. While generative AI presents opportunities to enhance learning, support critical thinking and improve academic outcomes, it also poses challenges related to academic integrity, bias and equity. The future success of integrating generative AI into higher education will rely on balancing innovation with responsible implementation. Universities that proactively integrate AI into teaching, assessment and support systems will better position themselves to prepare students",180
DOC_042,Rapidly changing attitudes of university students on the use of generative artificial intelligence.pdf,10.1080/0309877X.2026.2634035,14,"integrity, bias and equity. The future success of integrating generative AI into higher education will rely on balancing innovation with responsible implementation. Universities that proactively integrate AI into teaching, assessment and support systems will better position themselves to prepare students for an AI-driven society. Disclosure statement No potential conflict of interest was reported by the author(s). ORCID Andrew Williams http://orcid.org/0000-0",60
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5 Abstract This study explores the arrival of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) tech­ nologies in a collaborative Problem-Based Learning (PBL) environment from the perspective of students. It examines the emerging practices students develop indi­ vidually and in groups as well as the tensions they navigate in response to GenAI’s sudden arrival, its inherent limitations and multifaceted affordances, as well as the evolving landscape of formal and informal rules that frame its use. Grounded in Cultural-Historical Activity Theory, the study adopts an exploratory research ap­ proach, utilizing both qualitative and quantitative data from a survey administered to 1st and 5th semester IT students in a systemic PBL university (n = 83). Quantita­ tive data were analyzed using descriptive statistics and principal component analy­ sis, while qualitative data were analyzed through thematic analysis. The findings indicate a high adoption rate and generally favorable attitudes toward GenAI across both semesters. While 1st semester students were more likely to report no prior experience with GenAI compared to 5th semester students, no notable differences were observed regarding frequency or",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,1,"a high adoption rate and generally favorable attitudes toward GenAI across both semesters. While 1st semester students were more likely to report no prior experience with GenAI compared to 5th semester students, no notable differences were observed regarding frequency or patterns of use. Overall, the arrival of GenAI manifests in the students’ individual and collaborative study practices by enabling or obstructing both epistemic and pragmatic mediations of the objects. However, these interactions have been found to be accompanied by several tensions– specifi­ cally between the mediation of different objects, between students and their project groups, and between students and the rules framing GenAI use, which are discussed and contextualized in light of theory and contemporary research. Keywords Generative artificial intelligence · Artificial intelligence in education · Problem-based learning · Collaborative learning · Cultural-historical activity theory Received: 25 September 2024 / Accepted: 18 March 2025 / Published online: 7 April 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Disruptive tensions and emerging practices: an exploratory inquiry into student perspectives on generative Artificial Intelligence in a problem-based learning environment Sofie Otto1 · Stine Ejsing-Duun1 ·",180
