DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 1 Introduction In late 2022, educational ecosystems were faced with a worldwide disruptive shock­ wave catalyzed by the release of, widespread availability to, and polarized hype surrounding OpenAI’s ChatGPT; a Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) pow­ ered chatbot with sophisticated conversational capabilities. While the broader fields of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and AI in Education (AIEd) have been evolving for decades, the current wave of GenAI models signifies notable advancements due to unprecedented capabilities in generating contextually appropriate and diverse con­ tent modalities indistinguishable from human artifacts (García-Peñalvo & Vázquez- Ingelmo, 2023; Łodzikowski et al., 2024). Many state-of-the-art Large Language Models (LLMs), such as ChatGPT, leverage a transformer architecture pre-trained on large-scale datasets and fine-tuned for specific tasks or domains, enabling the gen­ eration of predictions conveyed through a user interface (Aydin & Karaarslan, 2023; Cukurova, 2024). The development and technical setup behind these models present a wide range of ethical issues, broadly including data bias, lack of design transpar­ ency and algorithmic explainability, exploitive business models, as well as potentially detrimental environmental costs (Driessens &",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,2,"2024). The development and technical setup behind these models present a wide range of ethical issues, broadly including data bias, lack of design transpar­ ency and algorithmic explainability, exploitive business models, as well as potentially detrimental environmental costs (Driessens & Pischetola, 2024; Łodzikowski et al., 2024; Selwyn, 2024), which has been widely discussed in contemporary educational research. One of the most contentious aspects, however, pertains to the affordances of generating content indistinguishable from human artifacts– and how students leverage these affordances. In other words, how students use GenAI, and how this use might challenge their learning as well as structural long-standing practices that rely on text-based artifacts as the end-product of learning and the primary means of assessing learning. Thus, concerns raised over the likelihood of students using GenAI to fraudulently produce written assignments manifested in public discussions predominantly revolving around concerns related to academic integrity in early 2023 (Lodge et al., 2023; Selwyn, 2024; Sullivan et al., 2023). From contemporary research on student perspectives, however, more nuanced insights are beginning to emerge. First of all, high levels of student",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,2,"around concerns related to academic integrity in early 2023 (Lodge et al., 2023; Selwyn, 2024; Sullivan et al., 2023). From contemporary research on student perspectives, however, more nuanced insights are beginning to emerge. First of all, high levels of student GenAI adoption have been widely reported (Chan & Hu, 2023; Malmström et al., 2023; Ravšelj et al., 2025; Sánchez-Ruiz et al., 2023), especially within STEM disciplines (Baek et al., 2024; Budhiraja et al., 2024; Kelly et al., 2023), with common applications including brainstorming, summarization, finding research articles, and writing (Ravšelj et al., 2025). This shows that GenAI technologies have become an integral part of educa­ tional practices, however, the integration appears, in many cases, to be initiated and driven by students themselves. Thus, drawing on Reich and Dukes’ (2024) concep­ tualization and distinction between arrival and adoption technologies, GenAI can be understood as an arrival technology that integrates into educational practices through the spontaneous and unguided actions of students, as opposed to the deliberate and structured process of planning and procurement associated with adoption technolo­ gies. This arrival has been",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,2,"be understood as an arrival technology that integrates into educational practices through the spontaneous and unguided actions of students, as opposed to the deliberate and structured process of planning and procurement associated with adoption technolo­ gies. This arrival has been reported to be influenced by diverse factors related to the students and their social structures, including institutional policy and type of institu­ tion (Baek et al., 2024), and socio-demographic and geographic factors (Ravšelj et al., 2025). At the same time, it has been reported to be surrounded by uncertainty regarding formal institutional policy and rules as well as fear of unintentional pla­ 1 3 19112",105
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 giarism (Baek et al., 2024; Budhiraja et al., 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Malmström et al., 2023). The nascent stage of the field of GenAI in education does not allow for any defini­ tive conclusions to be made regarding the long-term impact of GenAI use. In con­ junction with the vast and fast adoption by students and its associated uncertainty as well as the ethical issues mentioned above, this creates a complex, ambiguous, and uncertain landscape that is challenging to navigate for all agents in the educational core. Previous research has identified active and student-centered approaches, such as Problem-Based Learning (PBL) as resilient in addressing the ambiguity and uncer­ tainty posed by other arrival technologies, such as the shift to emergency online instruction during COVID-19 (Otto et al., 2024). These approaches have also been framed as possible mitigation strategies for addressing students’ potentially prob­ lematic use of GenAI as a replacement for learning (Dai et al., 2023; Geerling et al., 2023). Central to these pedagogical models is the promotion of deep and flexible approaches",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,3,"framed as possible mitigation strategies for addressing students’ potentially prob­ lematic use of GenAI as a replacement for learning (Dai et al., 2023; Geerling et al., 2023). Central to these pedagogical models is the promotion of deep and flexible approaches to learning, emphasizing higher-order thinking and student collabora­ tion– often contrasted with traditional teacher-centered university pedagogy (Børte et al., 2023; Prince, 2004). Given the student-centered dynamics of these environ­ ments, it becomes important to understand the arrival and adoption of GenAI from the perspective of students to inform future educational practice and research. This study employs an exploratory approach to examine student experiences and attitudes toward GenAI in both individual and collaborative PBL study practices through a quantitative and qualitative survey, guided by the following research question: What characterizes students’ perspectives on the utilization of GenAI for edu­ cational purposes in a PBL environment, and how does the utilization manifest in their individual and collaborative study practices? The remainder of the article is structured as follows: Sect. 1.1 will present the theo­ retical lens guiding the data collection and analysis.",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,3,"in a PBL environment, and how does the utilization manifest in their individual and collaborative study practices? The remainder of the article is structured as follows: Sect. 1.1 will present the theo­ retical lens guiding the data collection and analysis. Sect. 2 will detail the material and methods used to structure and frame the scientific inquiry, including factor analy­ sis and thematic analysis. Sect. 3 will present the results of each analysis, and lastly, the main findings will be discussed in Sect. 4. 1.1 Theoretical framing The study is theoretically grounded in Cultural-Historical Activity Theory (CHAT) to frame our examination of the intricate dynamics surrounding the arrival of GenAI into a dynamic and collaborative PBL environment. CHAT offers a holistic lens for understanding, analyzing, and shaping the complex nature of human activity within social and cultural contexts. Central to this lens is the dialectical role of mediational tools, which both shape and are shaped by the social context in which they are used (Engeström & Sannino, 2009; Roth & Lee, 2007). The activity is performed by indi­ viduals, i.e., subjects,",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,3,"lens is the dialectical role of mediational tools, which both shape and are shaped by the social context in which they are used (Engeström & Sannino, 2009; Roth & Lee, 2007). The activity is performed by indi­ viduals, i.e., subjects, who frame the goal to which the activity is directed and medi­ ate their action through the use of culturally historically developed tools. The activity occurs in a community comprised of a division of labor and rules that govern how 1 3 19113",83
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 the subjects use tools to achieve objects. These interrelated elements constitute the notion of an activity system, as depicted in Fig. 1. A central tenet of CHAT is that activity systems harbor inner contradictions, which serve as the driving forces for change and development within and between activity systems (Engeström & San­ nino, 2009). Conflicts, dilemmas, disturbances, and tensions– all situated at the level of short-term action, may be analyzed as manifestations of contradictions (ibid.). To further nuance our conceptualization of GenAI as a mediating tool in the activ­ ity system, we will draw on Rabardel’s (2002) instrumental approach as a comple­ mentary theoretical lens. Grounded in the seminal work preceding activity theory, Rabardel posits that the instrument is a mixed entity linked to both the tool itself as well as the construction or appropriation of utilization schemes the subject associates with it. Subjects can use these instruments to mediate their relationships with the activity’s object, themselves, and others. Object mediations can be epistemic, aiming to understand the object (e.g., a microscope), or pragmatic,",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,4,"appropriation of utilization schemes the subject associates with it. Subjects can use these instruments to mediate their relationships with the activity’s object, themselves, and others. Object mediations can be epistemic, aiming to understand the object (e.g., a microscope), or pragmatic, aiming to act upon the object (e.g., a hammer), both of which are in constant interaction within the activity (Laisney & Chatoney, 2018; Rabardel & Bourmaud, 2003; Rabardel, 2002). This study centers its line of inquiry on the subject view in the activity system, which is conceptualized as a network of interconnected components that collectively shape the arrival and integration of GenAI in the PBL environment. The operational­ ization of each element of the activity system is detailed in Table 1. Furthermore, the inquiry places a central focus on identifying and making sense of the tensions that arise from the arrival of GenAI as a mediating instrument. 2 Materials and methods This research employs an exploratory methodology to address the research question. Exploration, as defined by Stebbins (2001) is “a broad-ranging, purposive, system­ atic, prearranged undertaking”, which serves as a",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,4,"arrival of GenAI as a mediating instrument. 2 Materials and methods This research employs an exploratory methodology to address the research question. Exploration, as defined by Stebbins (2001) is “a broad-ranging, purposive, system­ atic, prearranged undertaking”, which serves as a valuable methodological approach when a topic has received limited or no systematic empirical scrutiny (Stebbins, 2001; Swedberg, 2020). While exploratory research typically leans towards induc­ tive, qualitative approaches, deduction, as Casula et al. (2021) argued, is also well suited for explorative approaches, especially when using a working hypothesis to Fig. 1 Activity system 1 3 19114",96
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 move the purposeful inquiry forward, while not being bound by relational expecta­ tions. In this study, the working hypothesis posits that the arrival of GenAI has led to corresponding complex and multifaceted tensions within the collective activity system. To explore this hypothesis, the research employs a combination of quantita­ tive and qualitative approaches in a questionnaire, providing both a broad overview of perspectives and dimensions on GenAI arrival and a nuanced, in-depth picture of the emerging practices and tensions from the perspective of students. The aim is not to offer final, conclusive evidence-based answers to the research question but rather to explore the topic and its complex nuances situated in a specific time and place to inform future action and change. The following sections will describe the local research context in which the study is situated, detail the methods used for data col­ lection and analysis, and outline the sampling of participants. Table 1 Elements of activity system and their operationalization in this study (definitions based on engeström & Sannino, 2009; Roth & Lee,",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,5,"is situated, detail the methods used for data col­ lection and analysis, and outline the sampling of participants. Table 1 Elements of activity system and their operationalization in this study (definitions based on engeström & Sannino, 2009; Roth & Lee, 2007; Wilson, 2014) CHAT element Definition Operationalization Subject The individual whose point of view is chosen as the perspec­ tive of the analysis The individual student responding to the survey Object The carrier of the motive of the activity, differentiated into generalized (broader societal meaning) and specific (per­ sonal sense of the subject) In this context, the object is primarily specific, and closely tied to the subject’s individual sense of learning and education within a PBL environment. The core objective is to complete a project and engage in learning through this process. Community Individuals and subgroups sharing the same general object The community is viewed on three levels: 1) Micro-level relating to the project group sharing the specific object of collaborative learning and project work 2) Meso-level relating to faculty (including super­ visors and teachers) who share the same general­",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,5,"object The community is viewed on three levels: 1) Micro-level relating to the project group sharing the specific object of collaborative learning and project work 2) Meso-level relating to faculty (including super­ visors and teachers) who share the same general­ ized object and define informal expectations while navigating the formal regulations 3) Macro-level relating to management of the organization who share the same generalized object and define formal regulations and policy Division of labor The distribution of tasks and power within the community and internal to the subject, in­ cluding roles and obligations The distribution of tasks internal to the subject and the micro-community, as well as the outsourcing of tasks to GenAI and how it affects the division of labor in the activity system Rules Explicit and implicit regula­ tions, norms, habits, conven­ tions, and standards that guide actions within the activity system Formal macro-community policies on GenAI use and implicit and explicit norms and rules negoti­ ated within the micro- and meso-community Tools Instruments, including tools and signs, that mediate the subjects’ interaction with the object Students use",180
