DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 2.1 Research context The study was undertaken at Aalborg University (AAU), where PBL is practiced at the curriculum level rather than restricted to individual courses (Chen et al., 2021). Referred to as the AAU-PBL model, the university-wide pedagogical approach takes its point of departure in student-led collaborative processes, where students work in small groups on semester-long complex and authentic problem-oriented projects under continuous supervision (Kolmos & de Graaff, 2014). These projects afford stu­ dents a high degree of autonomy in selecting their research problems, managing how and where they collaborate, and choosing which technologies to support various work processes, including inquiry and exploration, resource management, dialogue and communication, and production (Ryberg, 2019). As part of the university’s response to GenAI, students are permitted to use GenAI on par with other aids in relation to project work, provided they adhere to proper source referencing and good scientific practices. While these general guidelines apply universally, individual study boards may introduce additional regulations governing the use of GenAI in project work and examinations within their respective degree",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,6,"project work, provided they adhere to proper source referencing and good scientific practices. While these general guidelines apply universally, individual study boards may introduce additional regulations governing the use of GenAI in project work and examinations within their respective degree programs. The overarching policy and student guidelines regarding GenAI are subject to ongoing updates. 2.2 Data collection The data collection takes its point of departure in the development of a questionnaire that aims to explore students’ experiences with and attitudes towards the utilization of GenAI for educational purposes. The questionnaire is divided into three main sec­ tions: experiences, attitudes, and demographics. On the first page, respondents were informed that their participation was entirely voluntary, and they were required to give their consent by clicking on a consent button. The first section included four close-ended questions focusing on the respondents’ experiences with the utilization of GenAI for educational purposes. Initially, respon­ dents were asked about their prior experience with GenAI. Those who indicated prior experience were further asked about frequency of use. Additionally, all respondents were asked to rate the perceived",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,6,"with the utilization of GenAI for educational purposes. Initially, respon­ dents were asked about their prior experience with GenAI. Those who indicated prior experience were further asked about frequency of use. Additionally, all respondents were asked to rate the perceived frequency of their classmates’ use of GenAI. The frequency ratings were measured on a scale ranging from 1 (daily) to 5 (never), with an additional “I don’t know” option. The second section assessed attitudes towards GenAI for educational purposes, subdivided into attitudes towards GenAI in gen­ eral and individual study practices, as well as collaborative study practices. The atti­ tude questions for individual study practices consisted of nine items on a five-point Likert scale from 1 (strongly agree) to 5 (strongly disagree). Similarly, attitudes towards collaborative study practices were measured using seven items on the same scale. Furthermore, the attitude section contained two open-ended questions asking respondents to describe their most important benefit and concern regarding the use of GenAI for educational purposes. The third section gathered demographic informa­ tion, including age, semester, study program, and gender. All attitude questions",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,6,"section contained two open-ended questions asking respondents to describe their most important benefit and concern regarding the use of GenAI for educational purposes. The third section gathered demographic informa­ tion, including age, semester, study program, and gender. All attitude questions were designed to explore the interactions between the dif­ ferent elements of the activity system from the subject’s perspective. Accordingly, CHAT has served as the foundational basis for identifying and formulating questions 1 3 19116",75
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 pertaining to the core elements of the activity system. The subject (S) and tool (T) are integral to all items, with a particular emphasis on examining the interactions between these two components and the other elements—the object (O), community (C), rules (R), division-of-labor (D)– of the activity system, as illustrated in Table 2. To ensure the comprehensiveness and relevance of the questionnaire, patterns identified in contemporary literature, along with insights gained from discussions with colleagues possessing substantial domain expertise, were incorporated. Addi­ tionally, the questionnaire was pilot-tested with seven 2nd semester software students Table 2 Attitude questions and their relation to activity system Section Question AS components interaction* S T O C R D Gen­ eral and individ­ ual study practices Q1. My teachers have discussed the responsible use of ChatGPT or similar text-based generative AI with our class X X X X Q2. It is clear to me where Aalborg University draw the line be­ tween use and misuse of ChatGPT or similar text-based generative AI for educational purposes X X X X",180
