DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 years (M = 22.3, SD = 2.1). Detailed demographic characteristics are summarized in Table 3. 2.4 Data analysis Responses to experience, attitude, and demographic questions were analyzed using descriptive and non-parametric statistical tests. Subsequently, responses to the atti­ tude questions were subjected to further analysis through exploratory factor analysis. Both the descriptive and non-parametric statistical tests, as well as the factor analy­ sis, were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics (version 29). Lastly, the qualitative responses obtained from the open-ended questions were analyzed using thematic analysis. The subsequent sections will elaborate further on the analytical methods employed in the factor analysis (Sect. 2.4.1) and thematic analysis (Sect. 2.4.2). 2.4.1 Factor analysis Exploratory factor analysis (EFA) was performed for all attitude questions to exam­ ine the underlying sources of variation among the measured items (Singh, 2007; Wat­ kins, 2018). Prior to extracting the constructs, Bartlett’s test of sphericity (Bartlett, 1950) was performed to assess that the correlation matrix was not random, and Kaiser-Meyer-Olkin (KMO) (Dziuban & Shirkey, 1974) was performed to assess the sampling adequacy. The",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,9,"Wat­ kins, 2018). Prior to extracting the constructs, Bartlett’s test of sphericity (Bartlett, 1950) was performed to assess that the correlation matrix was not random, and Kaiser-Meyer-Olkin (KMO) (Dziuban & Shirkey, 1974) was performed to assess the sampling adequacy. The results of Bartlett’s test of sphericity indicated that the correlation matrix was not random (p <.001), suggesting that the observed correla­ tion matrix is statistically different from a singular matrix (Beavers et al., 2013). The KMO statistic was 0.67, which is considered mediocre but above the minimum standard for conducting factor analysis (Kaiser & Rice, 1974). Thus, the correlation matrix was considered suitable for exploratory factor analysis. Principal component analysis (PCA) was chosen as the extraction method over common factor analysis because the intent was to identify the main patterns in the data without assuming any underlying structure caused by latent variables (Taher­ doost et al., 2022). The optimal number of factors was determined by the eigenvalue threshold 1.0 and the cumulative percentage of variance above 60% (Taherdoost et al., 2022). Due to the nature of the items, it was",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,9,"latent variables (Taher­ doost et al., 2022). The optimal number of factors was determined by the eigenvalue threshold 1.0 and the cumulative percentage of variance above 60% (Taherdoost et al., 2022). Due to the nature of the items, it was assumed that factors could be cor­ related, and thus, an oblique rotation method was employed (Beavers et al., 2013), namely Promax. Pattern coefficients > 0.40 were considered salient. The PCA with Promax rotation yielded five interpretable components with eigen­ values greater than 1.00, cumulatively accounting for 60.75% of the total variance. Specifically, component 1 explained 20.6% of the variance, component 2 explained 14%, component 3 explained 11.7%, component 4 explained 7.8%, and component 5 explained 6.7% of the total variance (Fig. 2). 2.4.2 Qualitative analysis The questionnaire included two open-ended questions asking respondents to briefly describe: (1) the most important benefit of using ChatGPT or similar text-based gen­ erative AI for educational purposes, and (2) what concerns you the most about using 1 3 19119",165
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,10,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 ChatGPT or similar text-based generative AI for educational purposes. Although these questions were optional, the majority of respondents provided elaborate and detailed responses. Specifically, 67 out of 83 respondents provided answers, resulting in a total quantity of 134 qualitative responses dispersed across the two questions, constituting the dataset for subsequent analysis. The data was analyzed using a thematic analysis approach to identify, analyze, and report repeated patterns of meaning within the dataset. The thematic analysis fol­ lowed the six phases outlined by Braun and Clarke (2006), which include: (1) famil­ iarization with the data, (2) generating initial codes, (3) searching for themes, (4) reviewing themes, (5) defining and naming themes, and (6) producing the report. The initial coding was inductive and data-driven, aiming to identify as many potential patterns as possible. This coding process was primarily conducted by the lead author, who systematically coded the entire dataset through three iterations. To ensure inter- rater reliability, a co-author independently coded a random subset of 30 items, after which the results were compared and deliberated upon.",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,10,"was primarily conducted by the lead author, who systematically coded the entire dataset through three iterations. To ensure inter- rater reliability, a co-author independently coded a random subset of 30 items, after which the results were compared and deliberated upon. This process led to the identi­ fication of 12 distinct benefits and 10 distinct concerns, as depicted in Fig. 3. Fig. 3 Overview of codes and frequency Fig. 2 Scree plot 1 3 19120",74
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 Subsequently, the identified codes with more than four instances were iteratively sorted into themes using an abductive approach, with the construction of themes being informed by the theoretical lenses outlined in 1.1. The sorting process revealed a pattern of certain interactions between elements of the activity system being fore­ grounded (Table 4). As detailed by Baun and Clarke (2006), a theme is not dependent on quantity, but rather on whether it captures something important relating to the overall research question. Consequently, some themes consist of a few codes, while still capturing important aspects of student perspectives. 3 Results The results are presented in a three-part structure aligned with the main sections of the questionnaire and the applied analytical methods. First, Sect. 3.1 will present the descriptive results from the experience, attitude, and demographic questions. Second, Sect. 3.2 will present the results from the PCA based on the attitude questions. Third, Sect. 3.3 will present the results from the thematic analysis of open-ended questions. 3.1 Overview of survey results 3.1.1 Experiences with GenAI The descriptive",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,11,"Second, Sect. 3.2 will present the results from the PCA based on the attitude questions. Third, Sect. 3.3 will present the results from the thematic analysis of open-ended questions. 3.1 Overview of survey results 3.1.1 Experiences with GenAI The descriptive results from the experience questions reveal a generally high adoption rate, with the majority of respondents (83%) reporting prior experience using GenAI for educational purposes, while only 14% reported no prior experience (Table 5). Prior experience using GenAI for educational purposes was significantly different by semester (χ2(2, N = 83) = 8,398, p <.05), with 1st semester students more likely to report no prior experience with GenAI compared to 5th semester. Most respondents reported high frequency of use, with 20% on a daily basis and 44% on a weekly basis. No differences in self-reported frequency of use were observed between 1st and 5th Theme Benefits Concerns Pragmatic mediations Reduce workload Task catalyst Information search Debugging Epistemic mediations Reduce complexity Diminution Idea generation Missed learning Scaffolding opportunity Coding Interpersonal mediations Adaptive support Diminution Others’ ability Plagiarism Instrument characteristics Unreliable No concerns",180
