DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,13,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 3.1.2 Attitudes towards GenAI The descriptive results from the general and individual attitude questions reveal a predominantly positive view of GenAI (Fig. 4). The majority either strongly (64%) or somewhat (24%) agree that GenAI can be a useful learning aid (Q3). Notably, 8 out of the 70 respondents who agreed had no prior experience with GenAI for edu­ cational purposes. Similarly, most respondents either strongly (37%) or somewhat (40%) agree that it can help them think in new directions during idea generation (Q5), with 8 out of the 64 respondents who agreed reporting no prior experience. This indi­ cates that both respondents with prior experience (n = 69), as well as no prior expe­ rience (n = 12), generally hold positive expectations towards the potential of using GenAI for educational purposes. Responses were varied on other aspects, including whether teachers have discussed the responsible use of GenAI with their class (Q1), the clarity of where the university draws the line between use and misuse (Q2), as well as apprehensions about using GenAI for educational",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,13,"on other aspects, including whether teachers have discussed the responsible use of GenAI with their class (Q1), the clarity of where the university draws the line between use and misuse (Q2), as well as apprehensions about using GenAI for educational purposes (Q4). When it comes to negative expectations and concerns, more than one-third of the respondents strongly (11%) or somewhat (25%) agree that outsourcing tasks to GenAI can limit their access to essential learning opportunities (Q9), while over 40% of respondents strongly (16%) or somewhat (25%) agree that they worry about overreliance. The descriptive results of the collaborative attitude questions also reveal a pre­ dominantly positive view of GenAI (Fig. 5). The majority strongly (36%) or some­ what (40%) agree that GenAI can enhance their project work (Q12). When it comes to group members’ use of GenAI, nearly half strongly (19%) or somewhat (30%) agree that they have clear and shared expectations on how to use GenAI responsibly with their group members (Q11). Furthermore, the majority strongly (45%) or some­ what (30%) agree that they trust their group members’ responsible",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,13,"(19%) or somewhat (30%) agree that they have clear and shared expectations on how to use GenAI responsibly with their group members (Q11). Furthermore, the majority strongly (45%) or some­ what (30%) agree that they trust their group members’ responsible use of GenAI (Q13), while the majority strongly (69%) or somewhat (12%) disagree that the use of GenAI has led to conflicts in their project group (Q16). This suggests that the respon­ dents hold predominantly positive attitudes and experiences when it comes to their group members’ use of GenAI. Fig. 5 Attitudes on GenAI in relation to collaborative study practices (N = 83) 1 3 19123",106
