DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 this five-factor structure, some items cross-loaded in two components (Q3, Q4, Q10, Q15), indicating that the items are related to more than one underlying source of variation. These items were preserved in both components to preserve the nuanced relationships within the data. The EM component reflects the enabling role of GenAI in object mediation within both individual and collaborative study practices. The items primarily highlight the subject-tool-object and community-tool-object relationships, where GenAI serves as an instrument for both pragmatic mediation (e.g., decision-making) and epistemic mediation (e.g., thinking in new directions). Contrary, the OM component reflects the obstructing role of GenAI in object mediation concerning individual study practices, with items relating to the subject-tool-object triad. Given that all items predomi­ nantly reflect epistemic mediations, the combination and loading of items indicate that this component captures how the instrument becomes a barrier to epistemic object mediation. The ME component relates to the quartet of relationships between subject-tool- community-rules, representing the clarity and communication of GenAI-related rules within the community. All items center on the rules element",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,15,"how the instrument becomes a barrier to epistemic object mediation. The ME component relates to the quartet of relationships between subject-tool- community-rules, representing the clarity and communication of GenAI-related rules within the community. All items center on the rules element of the activity system, emphasizing how dialogues with teachers and supervisors contribute to the perceived clarity of macro-level rules. The CD component primarily relates to the interaction between the micro-community and the tool, reflecting dynamics related to GenAI as a group member, source of discussion or conflict, as well as the feeling of appre­ hensiveness. The loading of discussions with supervisors regarding responsible use indicates the role of supervisors in mediating these dynamics. The MR component concerns the community-tool-rules triad as well as the interaction between the sub­ ject and the tool. The negative loading of individual apprehension in conjunction with the clear expectations and trust-related items may indicate that respondents who have high levels of trust in their group members and share clear expectations regard­ ing the use of GenAI might experience less apprehension about using it for educa­",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,15,"with the clear expectations and trust-related items may indicate that respondents who have high levels of trust in their group members and share clear expectations regard­ ing the use of GenAI might experience less apprehension about using it for educa­ tional purposes. 3.2.1 Component overview The analysis identified five distinct components that each foreground certain inter­ actions within the activity system. The two components that account for the most variability in the data, presenting over one-third of the total variance, pertain to the use of GenAI as an enabler for epistemic and pragmatic object mediations (EM) and as a barrier for epistemic object mediations (OM). The former component accounts for 20.6% of the total variance, underscoring the central role of GenAI in enabling object interactions. Component EM OM ME CD MI EM - - - - - OM -,234 - - - - ME -,079 -,053 - - - MD ,001 ,142 -,006 - - MI ,177 -,171 ,170 -,038 - Table 7 Component correlation matrix 1 3 19125",169
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 Regarding the relationship between components, the correlation matrix presented in Table 7 indicates generally weak correlations. A weak inverse relationship is observed between EM and OM, suggesting that the enabling of object mediation is inversely related to the obstruction of epistemic object mediation. However, given the weak association, these components are nearly independent of each other. This absence of significant correlations implies that the components do not share much common variance and operate relatively independently within the activity system. Consequently, the components identified through this analysis highlight distinct dimensions of the subjects’ experiences and attitudes. This finding underscores the inherent complexity and multifaceted nature of how different dimensions of experi­ ences and attitudes interact within the context of GenAI arrival. 3.3 Thematic analysis The following sections present the results of our thematic analysis of the two open- ended questions, which asked respondents to describe the most important benefits and concerns associated with GenAI in education. As detailed in 2.4.2, we consoli­ dated initial codes into five broader themes: Pragmatic mediations, Epistemic media­ tions, Interpersonal",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,16,"the two open- ended questions, which asked respondents to describe the most important benefits and concerns associated with GenAI in education. As detailed in 2.4.2, we consoli­ dated initial codes into five broader themes: Pragmatic mediations, Epistemic media­ tions, Interpersonal mediations, Instrument attributes, and Meso/macro-community rules. The first three themes pertain to the utilization of GenAI, while the fourth reflects characteristics of the instrument itself (which also influence its use), and the final theme concerns the community-level rules governing its use. Each theme is elaborated in detail below with excerpts from student responses. It should be noted that some codes intersect multiple themes but are categorized based on the theme in which they appeared most frequently. 3.3.1 Pragmatic object mediation The codes in this theme pertain to the subject’s utilization of GenAI as an instru­ ment for acting upon object(s). This pragmatic mediation manifests in various ways, particularly through enhancing task efficiency and perceived productivity, such as by enabling the delegation of entire tasks or parts thereof to GenAI. This is seen in the frequently coded benefit, ‘reduce workload’, mentioned",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,16,"This pragmatic mediation manifests in various ways, particularly through enhancing task efficiency and perceived productivity, such as by enabling the delegation of entire tasks or parts thereof to GenAI. This is seen in the frequently coded benefit, ‘reduce workload’, mentioned in 17 responses. Overall, respondents describe the value in outsourcing simple, monotonous, and mundane tasks to GenAI, such as grammar checking, generating bulk text, condensing bullet points, summarizing texts, and briefly exploring new topics, allowing them to priori­ tize other, more complex elements of their project work or individual assignments. One respondent explained: “It allows for grammar checking which really isn’t a rel­ evant discipline for humans and it can make generating bulk text faster & easier”. Another respondent emphasized the benefits of outsourcing such tasks: “I feel like I’m more productive”, indicating an acceleration of action through pragmatic media­ tion. Additionally, GenAI can serve as a ‘task catalyst’ by assisting the students in ini­ tiating new tasks when they face uncertainty about where to begin. This benefit was noted in five responses, where GenAI provides a starting point that",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,16,"tion. Additionally, GenAI can serve as a ‘task catalyst’ by assisting the students in ini­ tiating new tasks when they face uncertainty about where to begin. This benefit was noted in five responses, where GenAI provides a starting point that accelerates prog­ ress, but still requires subsequent effort from the subject. One respondent explained this utility: “It is a useful tool, to start your reach if you have no clue where to start”. 1 3 19126",76
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 Another manifestation is ‘information search’, coded as a benefit in nine responses predominantly related to pragmatic mediation. GenAI is frequently compared to con­ ventional search interfaces, particularly Google search. One respondent positioned GenAI as a “glorified search machine”, a sentiment corroborated by other respon­ dents, who highlighted it as more efficient, affording a more nuanced and interactive search experience by aiding with synthesizing information from diverse sources. This is further elaborated by another respondent, who highlighted the utility in helping with “collecting the information from different sources so the painful process can be a little less painful, because you still need to understand what is happening”. The explicit comparison between GenAI and search machines indicates an assimilation of the artifact into a pre-existing search utilization scheme, although it appears adapted to the dialogic affordances of GenAI. Closely related to information search is the prevalent benefit of ‘debugging’, iden­ tified in nine responses. Respondents highlighted the assistance GenAI provides in identifying and correcting errors in their code, appreciating the direct and instant aid it offers",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,17,"of GenAI. Closely related to information search is the prevalent benefit of ‘debugging’, iden­ tified in nine responses. Respondents highlighted the assistance GenAI provides in identifying and correcting errors in their code, appreciating the direct and instant aid it offers compared to searching for solutions elsewhere. One respondent emphasized this utility, noting that GenAI: Helps with coding, troubleshooting etc. Also due to ChatGPT’s errors, some­ times you will get a faulty code fix, and then you will have to use the logic it explains to rewrite the code, which in a funny way means the faulty answers is the best for learning. This example illustrates how the outsourcing of troubleshooting can become a barrier to immediate task completion, while supporting learning, illustrating how GenAI can serve a dual role as a mediating barrier to pragmatic object mediation and an enabler for epistemic object mediation. 3.3.2 Epistemic object mediation The codes in this theme pertain to the utilization of GenAI as an instrument for understanding objects by augmenting the subject’s thinking and knowledge con­ struction during learning processes. One manifestation of",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,17,"for epistemic object mediation. 3.3.2 Epistemic object mediation The codes in this theme pertain to the utilization of GenAI as an instrument for understanding objects by augmenting the subject’s thinking and knowledge con­ struction during learning processes. One manifestation of this mediation is to ‘reduce complexity’, identified as a benefit in 17 responses. These relate to the use of GenAI to break down complex topics or concepts to aid the respondents’ understanding of these. For example, one respondent noted: “It can be used as a tool to understand the broad strokes of some complex concepts”. Other examples relate to recognizing connections between different subjects, as explained by another respondent: “They can help finding connections between subjects and help with making it click”. A simi­ lar application is the utilization of GenAI for ‘scaffolding’ learning, identified as a benefit in 15 responses. Respondents often used the metaphor of a personal tutor to describe their relationship and interaction with GenAI, initiated and prompted by the student through various scaffolding techniques during or after learning activities. One respondent explained: 1 3 19127",179
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,17,"responses. Respondents often used the metaphor of a personal tutor to describe their relationship and interaction with GenAI, initiated and prompted by the student through various scaffolding techniques during or after learning activities. One respondent explained: 1 3 19127",39
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 The AI can help with exercises and compared to e.g. tools on the internet, it will not only give you a result but also more importantly, explain the methodology behind how to solve the exercise. This example indicates agency on the part of the student, who, rather than outsourc­ ing their effort in completing the exercise, highlights the utility of GenAI as an instru­ ment for explaining the ‘methodology’ which enables an understanding of as well as action directed towards the object (solve the exercise), thus intersecting epistemic and pragmatic mediation. Other respondents linked the tutor role to more spontane­ ous and informal learning, for example: “It helps me remember or learn technical terms on the go, in a “teach me like I’m 9” type of way”. Another manifestation of how GenAI is instrumentalized for epistemic mediation is seen in the coded benefit ‘idea generation’, identified in 14 responses. The majority of these responses were succinct, with respondents shortly expressing the utility of GenAI in stimulating inspiration and creative thinking. For example, one respondent",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,18,"for epistemic mediation is seen in the coded benefit ‘idea generation’, identified in 14 responses. The majority of these responses were succinct, with respondents shortly expressing the utility of GenAI in stimulating inspiration and creative thinking. For example, one respondent noted: “It can give answers that makes you think different instead of telling you the answer”, suggesting GenAI can stimulate new ways of thinking. Another respondent highlighted GenAI’s utility in addressing blind spots by stating: “It can help bring up new possibilities that has been overlooked”. These examples indicate an augmenta­ tion rather than a replacement of thinking, highlighting how the subject preserves agency and makes judgments while using GenAI for creative stimulus. Lastly, the epistemic mediation includes supporting the learning of fundamental ‘coding’ knowledge and skills. In four instances, participants reported using GenAI to support their learning of coding and programming rather than having it execute tasks on their behalf. This benefit primarily reflects epistemic mediation– enhancing understanding and skill acquisition– while also incorporating aspects of pragmatic mediation. One respondent explained: While I wouldn’t use the code it outputs",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,18,"and programming rather than having it execute tasks on their behalf. This benefit primarily reflects epistemic mediation– enhancing understanding and skill acquisition– while also incorporating aspects of pragmatic mediation. One respondent explained: While I wouldn’t use the code it outputs for a project or assignment, it can help make coding more accessible since it *mostly* understands how to write simple programs. Saves a lot of headache trying to make sense of syntax and code flow While the above examples highlight the enabling role of GenAI in these mediations, responses also reveal concerns regarding its potential obstructing role impeding epistemic engagement. In the qualitative responses, a distinction emerges between the diminution of learning (becoming less competent and knowledgeable) and the stagnation of learning (not advancing competence and knowledge). The former is observed and categorized under the code of ‘diminution’, which was identified in 15 responses. Among these, eight responses referred to others’ overreliance, four responses expressed the respondents’ concerns about their own overreliance, and three responses were general, without specifying whether they pertained to the respondents themselves or others. When it",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,18,"identified in 15 responses. Among these, eight responses referred to others’ overreliance, four responses expressed the respondents’ concerns about their own overreliance, and three responses were general, without specifying whether they pertained to the respondents themselves or others. When it comes to concerns regarding their own overreliance, several respondents expressed a fear that extensive reliance on GenAI will result in making them “lazy” or less dependent on themselves, even “minimizing the skills you actually gain from attending courses and working on projects”. The stagnation of learning is coded as ‘missed learning opportunity’, identified in eight 1 3 19128",98
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,19,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 responses spanning various tasks and skills. For example, in academic writing, one respondent stated: I know that I am already not strong in writing academically, and with ChatGPT I can write a normal text and ask it to translate it to more academic writing - this way I won’t learn to do it myself. This example demonstrates an awareness of how GenAI utilization can impede the subject’s interaction with the object (learn to write academically). This further indi­ cates a recognition of the epistemic costs involved in outsourcing academic writing thereby reducing engagement with cognitive processes required for mastering it. 3.3.3 Interpersonal mediation The codes in this theme relate to the use of GenAI in social and collaborative con­ texts, highlighting its influence on interpersonal dynamics and collective activities, particularly within the micro-community. Rather than how GenAI directly facilitates student-student interaction, this theme relates to the social dynamics that emerge as a result of its use– primarily identified as concerns. A general pattern across the coded concerns involves the subject problematizing how members of",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,19,"than how GenAI directly facilitates student-student interaction, this theme relates to the social dynamics that emerge as a result of its use– primarily identified as concerns. A general pattern across the coded concerns involves the subject problematizing how members of the project group use the tool for object mediation. This mediation often conflicts with the subject’s orien­ tation towards the object, indicating tension in the interaction between the subject, community, object, and tool. One example can be found under the code ‘diminution’, identified in 15 responses, with eight specifically referring to others’ overreliance. Several respondents linked this concern to their group members, often rooted in neg­ ative past experiences. For example, one respondent noted: I have had previous group members that over relied on ChatGPT which created some problems when they could not explain to me why they wrote the code they did. This statement reflects a tension between the subject and micro-community, who, upon submitting their project, are collectively responsible for all text and code pro­ duced. During the project period, students alternate between different modes of col­ laboration",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,19,"code they did. This statement reflects a tension between the subject and micro-community, who, upon submitting their project, are collectively responsible for all text and code pro­ duced. During the project period, students alternate between different modes of col­ laboration and cooperation, thus collaborating closely together and delegating tasks (Sørensen, 2022). If one group member outsources their part of the work to a GenAI, it might pose problems for the entire group during subsequent oral examinations if they cannot explain the code. In addition to tensions regarding the outsourcing of work during cooperation modes, other examples relate to tensions regarding the outsourcing of work dur­ ing collaboration modes. One respondent details how a group member outsourced their effort during a collaborative brainstorming task, which disrupted the flow of collaboration: I find ChatGPT to be a huge disrupting factor (…) in the problem-based learn­ ing environment. I’ve had a fellow student instead of actually partaking in our 1 3 19129",159
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,20,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 brainstorm, writing a prompt in ChatGPT and showing us the screen, and say­ ing “look what the chatbot says”. So, in other words; it always gets in the way one way or another, and it’s always on the mind of some, rather than actually being present. The is a large amount of uncertainty and distrust towards each other, because we will never know if any of us actually used generated text from a language model in our report. Very unnerving to be honest. This example shows how GenAI utilization affects the respondent’s engagement with peers, resulting in uncertainty and distrust toward each other due to the perceived disruption of collaborative processes and authenticity of contributions. A related ten­ sion can be found under the code ‘plagiarism’, which reflects concerns about the possibility of unintentionally committing plagiarism. This concern was raised in four responses, with three relating to concerns about the actions of group members or peers. For instance, one respondent noted: “That my group members just copy paste recklessly, and therefore nullifies our project",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,20,"committing plagiarism. This concern was raised in four responses, with three relating to concerns about the actions of group members or peers. For instance, one respondent noted: “That my group members just copy paste recklessly, and therefore nullifies our project as copy paste”. Another respondent expressed concerns about committing unintentional plagiarism themselves and how it might impact the group project: “that it will be plagiat. If I myself write a text to translate or something, that it will ruin the project, because of my disadvantage of English or Danish not being my first language.” Another concern frequently mentioned is ‘others’ ability’, identified as a concern in 12 responses. This concern encompasses expressed distrust in other people’s skills, competencies, and knowledge to use GenAI responsibly. Within this code, respon­ dents have expressed concerns about how other people blindly rely on it, using it to search for information or perform tasks uncritically. Several respondents link this problem to their fellow students’ use of GenAI, not specifically within their project group but more broadly among their peers. In addition, a few respondents expressed",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,20,"using it to search for information or perform tasks uncritically. Several respondents link this problem to their fellow students’ use of GenAI, not specifically within their project group but more broadly among their peers. In addition, a few respondents expressed distrust in their fellow students’ integrity. One respondent articulated this sentiment: “I don’t see a problem with the tool itself. I have a problem with the way in which fel­ low students may use the tools to proclaim knowledge and skill they do not possess”. These examples show how the subject problematizes members of the community’s interaction with GenAI– not because it conflicts directly with their objects, but more because of their lack of adherence to the rules of the activity system. In addition to concerns about how community members interact with the instru­ ment, there are also examples of how this interaction can benefit the micro-commu­ nity through ‘adaptive support’, which was identified as a benefit in six responses. Respondents highlighted the utility of GenAI in offering support to students facing specific challenges and needs, particularly cognitive deficits associated",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,20,"how this interaction can benefit the micro-commu­ nity through ‘adaptive support’, which was identified as a benefit in six responses. Respondents highlighted the utility of GenAI in offering support to students facing specific challenges and needs, particularly cognitive deficits associated with condi­ tions such as dyslexia and ADHD. The responses both emphasized the provision of support for the individual respondent, while also extending to their peers and group members, as demonstrated in the following response: I have two dyslexic guys in my group, they use it extensively to overhaul their work - and I am very glad. I’ve seen their raw text a few times, it’s barely read­ able. But the text written with AI assistance is as good as mine, and they express that it is an accurate representation of what they wanted to say. 1 3 19130",139
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,21,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 This example demonstrates how a previous barrier is alleviated through the use of GenAI by a group member, and thus how the tool becomes a mediator for overcom­ ing tension in the micro-community. Overall, this support empowers students with a sense of independence, as articulated by one respondent: LLM is giving independence to those of us whom needs a lot of concepts explained a couple of times, and in different ways. Independence in that we can solve our confusion by own means, without becoming a nuisance to our peers. 3.3.4 Instrument characteristics This theme encompasses two coded concerns that both relate to the instrument itself. The most prevalent code, ‘unreliable’, was identified as a concern in 23 responses. The majority of responses within this code pertain to respondents’ concerns for the trustworthiness of information outputted by GenAI and its tendency to hallucinate, as articulated by this respondent: “Most LLM tools do not have any kind of fact- checking built in and hallucinations in them are still very prevalent”. The problem extends beyond the unreliability",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,21,"outputted by GenAI and its tendency to hallucinate, as articulated by this respondent: “Most LLM tools do not have any kind of fact- checking built in and hallucinations in them are still very prevalent”. The problem extends beyond the unreliability of information and encompasses the difficulty in discerning the validity of the information presented, as articulated by another respon­ dent: “It somewhat often represents incorrect information as fact”. In addition to concerns about hallucinations, some respondents raised issues regarding copyright violation and the risk of replicating copyrighted material when prompting a GenAI. Others expressed worries about the technology’s inherent biases, which could lead to a one-sided perspective on a given topic, thus affecting epistemic engagement. To mitigate these issues, several respondents described proactive measures they take to ensure accuracy and keep themselves in the loop of control. For example, one respon­ dent mentioned: “You cannot rely on it being 100% true, so you should never use it directly or as your source. Always find sources that back what ChatGPT is saying”. In contrast to the abovementioned concerns about the technology",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,21,"respon­ dent mentioned: “You cannot rely on it being 100% true, so you should never use it directly or as your source. Always find sources that back what ChatGPT is saying”. In contrast to the abovementioned concerns about the technology and its inherent limitations, several respondents explicitly stated that they had no concerns, rather than skipping the question. These responses were coded as ‘no concern’ and identi­ fied in six instances. The majority of these were brief, such as: “No concerns as for now”. It is noteworthy that the same respondents provided specific, albeit also brief, examples of benefits. One of the more elaborate exceptions of concerns is found in this example: “Nothing– I study IT, we think its cool”. This example illustrates how the respondent’s excitement about GenAI might overshadow the risks perceived and articulated by other respondents. 3.3.5 Meso/Macro-community rules This theme includes a single, yet frequently highlighted code: ‘missing guidance’, identified as a concern in 13 responses. A prevalent pattern within this code is respon­ dents’ uncertainty regarding the explicit macro-community rules and expectations and the lack",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,21,"Meso/Macro-community rules This theme includes a single, yet frequently highlighted code: ‘missing guidance’, identified as a concern in 13 responses. A prevalent pattern within this code is respon­ dents’ uncertainty regarding the explicit macro-community rules and expectations and the lack of education in using “AI-powered tools”. Consequently, there is a notable call for clear guidance to address these gaps. For example, one respondent mentioned: 1 3 19131",67
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,22,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 It would be nice to have a little crash-course to specify exact guidelines of how to use generative AI for educational purposes, as to not misuse it and how to benefit discussions or ideas generated by AI. This example highlights a need for support in understanding the explicit regulations (exact guidelines) and implicit norms (how to benefit discussions or ideas generated by AI) that define how the subject is expected to behave in their interaction with the instrument. Other respondents advocate for such support not only for their benefit but also for peers as we see in the following example: It confidently lies. The better it gets, the harder it will become to detect the lies. The students most likely to use such tech are also the least likely to detect those lies. As the tech can no longer be contained, guiding students at how to check the work generative algorithms should be a priority of the faculty. Several respondents caution that the absence of support or guidance may lead to inappropriate use of",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,22,"the tech can no longer be contained, guiding students at how to check the work generative algorithms should be a priority of the faculty. Several respondents caution that the absence of support or guidance may lead to inappropriate use of GenAI technologies, driven by a lack of awareness rather than malicious intent, as articulated by this respondent: It is very important to educate how to use tools responsibly, especially tools that we know will be used no matter what policy is in place. Banning tools such as these will only lead to incorrect use due to not knowing better. It is noteworthy that a considerable proportion of respondents have singled out miss­ ing guidance as their foremost concern regarding the use of GenAI for educational purposes. This indicates a significant tension between the subject, macro-community, and rules, which also appears to be related to the division of labor within the activity system. The respondents’ expressions suggest that educating students on how to use the tool is perceived as a responsibility of the meso- and macro-community, rather than the individual subject.",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,22,"to be related to the division of labor within the activity system. The respondents’ expressions suggest that educating students on how to use the tool is perceived as a responsibility of the meso- and macro-community, rather than the individual subject. 4 Discussion The study has shown a diverse and multifaceted array of perspectives on the utiliza­ tion of GenAI for educational purposes in a PBL environment. The findings indicate a high adoption rate alongside generally favorable attitudes toward GenAI, which is consistent with contemporary research on student perspectives across educational environments (Budhiraja et al., 2024; Chan & Hu, 2023; Kelly et al., 2023; Malm­ ström et al., 2023; Ravšelj et al., 2025; Sánchez-Ruiz et al., 2023). Overall, the adop­ tion manifests in the students’ study practices by enabling or obstructing the students and their project groups to epistemically or pragmatically mediate their interactions with educational objects. These mediations have been reported to foster a greater sense of independence, reducing students’ reliance on instructors or peers for support, and even compensating for cognitive deficits such as dyslexia. Similar benefits are reported",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,22,"mediate their interactions with educational objects. These mediations have been reported to foster a greater sense of independence, reducing students’ reliance on instructors or peers for support, and even compensating for cognitive deficits such as dyslexia. Similar benefits are reported by Tanay et al. (2024), who observed that students experienced increased 1 3 19132",54
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,23,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 self-sufficiency through the use of LLMs, citing their accessibility and availability as advantages. Nevertheless, alongside these reported benefits, the use of GenAI is also surrounded by several tensions with other interacting elements of the activity system, namely between different object mediations, between the subject and micro- community, and between the subject and rules. These tensions will be elaborated on in the subsequent sections. 4.1 Tension in the object mediation The first tension centers on the object element of the activity system, more specifically on the interaction between students’ utilization of GenAI as enabling or obstructing both epistemic and pragmatic mediations of objects. In terms of epistemic mediation, students report using GenAI as a cognitive tool to augment thinking and knowledge construction by helping them grasp complexity, support divergent thinking, and pro­ vide scaffolding during learning processes. These findings are supported by Ravšelj et al. (2025), who reported that students found ChatGPT particularly useful in simpli­ fying complex information and summarizing extensive information, thereby enhanc­ ing their understanding of the content. When it comes to",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,23,"learning processes. These findings are supported by Ravšelj et al. (2025), who reported that students found ChatGPT particularly useful in simpli­ fying complex information and summarizing extensive information, thereby enhanc­ ing their understanding of the content. When it comes to pragmatic object mediation, students report using GenAI to support or replace task performance during knowl­ edge construction, e.g., debugging and coding support, information gathering, and as a task catalyst for initiating tasks. Additionally, students report using GenAI for outsourcing repetitive or routine tasks, such as generating bulk text, thereby enhanc­ ing productivity and efficiency and freeing up mental capacity for other, more com­ plex aspects of their project work. The benefits of this type of outsourcing are also seen in other contemporary works on GenAI in education (Hamid et al., 2023; Tsai, 2023). For example, Yilmaz and Yilmaz (2023) reported that by saving time on basic coding tasks, students devoted more time to creative thinking, algorithmic think­ ing, problem-solving, and critical thinking. These examples illustrate how pragmatic mediation can concurrently enable epistemic mediation. However, respondents expressed apprehension about the potential",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,23,"that by saving time on basic coding tasks, students devoted more time to creative thinking, algorithmic think­ ing, problem-solving, and critical thinking. These examples illustrate how pragmatic mediation can concurrently enable epistemic mediation. However, respondents expressed apprehension about the potential impact of these mediations on their learning. This tension was consistently evident throughout the analysis, revealing a high level of awareness and caution among students regarding the risk of outsourcing their thinking and learning processes. These concerns align with findings from previous studies, such as Møgelvang et al. (2023), who reported that many students did not want to use AI chatbots due to fear of dependence and loss of critical thinking skills. Similarly, Tanay et al. (2024) highlighted concerns about overreliance on LLMs, with some students warning that excessive use could foster dependency and encourage behaviors detrimental to learning. However, the extent of this reliance may also be context-dependent and influenced by external pressures such as academic workload. Budhiraja et al. (2024) found that students’ use of ChatGPT increased under high workload conditions, particularly when fac­ ing time constraints on",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,23,"the extent of this reliance may also be context-dependent and influenced by external pressures such as academic workload. Budhiraja et al. (2024) found that students’ use of ChatGPT increased under high workload conditions, particularly when fac­ ing time constraints on assignments. This suggests that objects evolve in response to changing circumstances, which in turn influences how instruments are employed to mediate them. The factor analysis showed that the enabling and obstructing nature of these mediations can coexist in the students’ experiences and attitudes, highlighting the 1 3 19133",88
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,24,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 context-dependency when it comes to students’ orientations towards the objects and mediations. As curriculum developers, it is essential to understand where these shifts occur to create environments that encourage a meaningful balance between epistemic and pragmatic object mediations. For students and educators, navigating this balance necessitates reflective, adaptive strategies while fostering agency in their interaction with GenAI. 4.2 Tension between subject and micro-community The second tension emerges between the student and their project group, which underscores how the instrument shapes and is shaped by the immediate social context in which it is used. The analysis highlighted instances where students experienced frustration and uncertainty due to discrepant uses of GenAI within their groups– par­ ticularly when group members employed it as a replacement for effort during syn­ chronous collaboration and asynchronous coordination modes. This tension reflects an underlying contradiction in perceptions of meaningful object mediation. Within the same brainstorming activity, one student may perceive GenAI as an enabler of pragmatic mediation, leveraging it to accelerate task progression. Meanwhile, another student may view the same process",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,24,"reflects an underlying contradiction in perceptions of meaningful object mediation. Within the same brainstorming activity, one student may perceive GenAI as an enabler of pragmatic mediation, leveraging it to accelerate task progression. Meanwhile, another student may view the same process as an obstruction to epistemic media­ tion, seeing it as a barrier to deep cognitive engagement and knowledge construction. Thus, while the two group members are expected to collaborate toward a shared object (their project), their orientations towards the object in a given activity may differ. Tanay et al. (2024) similarly found that computing students in a collaborative PBL environment held divergent views on which tasks should be delegated to LLMs, with some students viewing routine or tedious tasks as appropriate for offloading, while others saw these same tasks as integral to their learning process. This misalign­ ment of object orientations can have several implications for group dynamics and trust. According to some respondents, it has manifested in increased uncertainty and distrust among students. This negative impact on group dynamics does not appear to be widespread, confer the majority agreeing",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,24,"orientations can have several implications for group dynamics and trust. According to some respondents, it has manifested in increased uncertainty and distrust among students. This negative impact on group dynamics does not appear to be widespread, confer the majority agreeing that they trust their group members’ responsible use of GenAI, however, it appears to hold substantial meaning to a subset of respondents. Interestingly, these findings somewhat contradict those of Hamid et al. (2023), who explored students’ perceptions of the use of GenAI in process-driven PBL in pharmacy education. The study reports a unanimous agreement among par­ ticipants regarding the positive influence of GenAI on collaborative aspects of PBL (ibid.). This discrepancy may underscore the role of contextual factors– such as dis­ ciplinary norms and institutional guidelines– that shape how GenAI is integrated into group work. To address these tensions, it is essential to support student-driven strategies for negotiating the role of GenAI in group work, including externalizing and aligning their object orientations early and throughout the collaboration, in order to estab­ lish clear and shared expectations regarding when and how",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,24,"is essential to support student-driven strategies for negotiating the role of GenAI in group work, including externalizing and aligning their object orientations early and throughout the collaboration, in order to estab­ lish clear and shared expectations regarding when and how GenAI should be used. This further includes individual and collective group reflection on GenAI’s impact on learning and project work through open dialogue and experimentation. 1 3 19134",68
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,25,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 4.3 Tension between subject and rules The third tension arises from the interaction between the subject and the rules within the activity system, reflecting students’ difficulties in navigating formal and informal regulations, norms, and expectations that guide and constrain their use of GenAI as a mediating tool. Similar challenges are noted in other student perspectives studies (Baek et al., 2024; Hamid et al., 2023; Møgelvang et al., 2023; Prather et al., 2023), indicating a widespread issue across educational environments. One manifestation of this tension is students’ fear of unintentional plagiarism, a concern echoed in Baek et al.’s (2024) study, which identifies apprehension about being accused of cheating for using GenAI as a primary issue among students. Consequently, students call for clear guidance and support in navigating rules and policies surrounding GenAI use in their study practices. The complexity of this tension is further amplified by the policy landscape sur­ rounding GenAI, which has evolved alongside technological advancements. For instance, in Denmark, universities initially imposed bans on GenAI tools in early 2023, only to later",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,25,"study practices. The complexity of this tension is further amplified by the policy landscape sur­ rounding GenAI, which has evolved alongside technological advancements. For instance, in Denmark, universities initially imposed bans on GenAI tools in early 2023, only to later introduce guidelines on their appropriate use by mid-year (Dries­ sens & Pischetola, 2024). These initial bans, coupled with public discourse framing tools like ChatGPT as instruments of cheating and plagiarism, implicitly conveyed a distrust in students (ibid.), which may have impacted students’ emerging utilization schemes (Rabardel & Bourmaud, 2003) associated with GenAI as a tool of miscon­ duct rather than an instrument for epistemic or pragmatic mediation. These regulatory and discursive framings may have constrained some students’ willingness to engage with GenAI and fostered a cautious approach that prioritizes compliance over explo­ ration and experimentation. This might especially be the case for 1st semester stu­ dents, who, in this study, were more likely to report no prior experiences with GenAI for educational purposes– although still reporting positive expectations and beliefs regarding its potential to support learning. Beyond academic integrity, students",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,25,"case for 1st semester stu­ dents, who, in this study, were more likely to report no prior experiences with GenAI for educational purposes– although still reporting positive expectations and beliefs regarding its potential to support learning. Beyond academic integrity, students are also navigating a broader set of ethical issues, such as privacy, bias, exploitative busi­ ness models, environmental impacts, etc., all of which influence how, when, and for what purposes GenAI is and should be used. This complex and dynamic nature of practice may require a correspondingly adaptive approach to policy and rules. As suggested by Mills et al. (2023), a policy model building on open educational practices (OEP) can offer a way forward through ongoing collaboration between agents, institutions, systems, educational levels, and nations to build emergent responses to intricate and emerging educational practices. They highlight the value of collective action, noting: “In a moment of overwhelm, we can turn toward each other and toward students and share imperfect, incomplete insights and experiments”. By articulating, sharing, and reflecting on ongoing itera­ tive experimentations, experiences, and emerging insights locally and",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,25,"collective action, noting: “In a moment of overwhelm, we can turn toward each other and toward students and share imperfect, incomplete insights and experiments”. By articulating, sharing, and reflecting on ongoing itera­ tive experimentations, experiences, and emerging insights locally and beyond, we as educators can, together with students, engage in co-constructing collective utili­ zation schemes for GenAI practices (and development) that balance technological affordances, pedagogy, and ethical considerations. This adaptive strategy may have the potential to mitigate the tensions experienced by students and contribute to an expansive learning process, in which we collectively shape the future of GenAI in education. 1 3 19135",103
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,26,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 4.4 Limitations We recognize several limitations when interpreting the results of this study. First, the study relies on self-reported data, a methodological choice that introduces inherent limitations that can affect the reliability and validity of the findings, including social desirability bias and recall bias (Paulhus & Vazire, 2007). Moreover, self-reports may not fully capture the complexity and depth of student-student-GenAI interactions and relations. Future research should therefore expand the scope of inquiry by incorpo­ rating direct observations or interactions with GenAI. Second, the framing of open- ended questions as the most important benefit and concern may have inadvertently introduced bias. This framing potentially led respondents to emphasize extreme viewpoints, either highly positive or highly negative, rather than capturing the more prevalent, substantial, or nuanced practices associated with GenAI use. Third, while the questionnaire generated insight into the adoption of GenAI into collaborative study practices, these insights were drawn solely from the perspective of the indi­ vidual students. This approach might overlook the complexity of group dynamics and technology integration in collaborative learning environments. Future",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,26,"insight into the adoption of GenAI into collaborative study practices, these insights were drawn solely from the perspective of the indi­ vidual students. This approach might overlook the complexity of group dynamics and technology integration in collaborative learning environments. Future studies should adopt methodologies that examine group-level interactions more holistically to bet­ ter understand how GenAI adoption shapes collaborative learning– and vice versa. Finally, the sample size limits the extent to which the findings can be generalized across broader populations. While generalizability was not the primary aim– rather, the goal was to provide an exploratory overview of GenAI adoption in a systemic PBL environment from a student perspective– it is important to note that the small sample size restricts the representativeness of the findings. In a rapidly evolving land­ scape of educational technology, where tools and practices are continuously chang­ ing, achieving a fully generalizable result is challenging. The insights from this study, therefore, should be viewed as a snapshot of current practices and tensions rather than definitive conclusions about the long-term effects of GenAI integration. 5 Concluding remarks This",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,26,"ing, achieving a fully generalizable result is challenging. The insights from this study, therefore, should be viewed as a snapshot of current practices and tensions rather than definitive conclusions about the long-term effects of GenAI integration. 5 Concluding remarks This study explored IT students’ perspectives on the utilization of GenAI technolo­ gies in a dynamic and collaborative PBL environment. Overall, the findings indicate a high adoption rate and generally favorable attitudes toward the integration of GenAI into both individual and collaborative study practices. From an activity theoretical lens, this integration manifests as epistemic object mediation by augmenting thinking and knowledge construction, as well as pragmatic object mediation by supporting or replacing task performance and execution. However, these mediations are accom­ panied by several tensions– specifically between the mediation of different objects, between students and their project groups, and between students and the rules fram­ ing GenAI use. While GenAI may expose existing tensions– such as differing object orientations across agents and contexts– the tensions identified in this study are inher­ ently disruptive. They highlight a system in expansion and an",180
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,26,"and the rules fram­ ing GenAI use. While GenAI may expose existing tensions– such as differing object orientations across agents and contexts– the tensions identified in this study are inher­ ently disruptive. They highlight a system in expansion and an opportunity to rethink and reframe how GenAI technologies and pedagogy co-evolve. We argue that this process necessitates an open, reflective, experimental, and most importantly collab­ 1 3 19136",68
DOC_043,s10639-025-13533-5.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13533-5,27,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:19111–19140 orative response from all agents in the educational core, grounded in the pedagogical values that underpin our educational practices. This study has demonstrated that stu­ dents have valuable insights to contribute, which can help inform the evolving land­ scape of GenAI in education, particularly in student-centered learning environments. Acknowledgements We extend our gratitude to all the students who contributed their time and effort to this study, both as respondents and as pilot testers. Funding Open access funding provided by Aalborg University Data availability The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are not publicly available. Declarations Ethical approval This study has been approved by the Human Research Ethics Committee at Aalborg University, Denmark (2023-505-00072). Competing interests The authors have no competing interests to declare that are relevant to the content of this article. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and",180
