DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 1 Introduction The rapid growth of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) has emerged as one of the fastest technology adoption phenomena in human history (Giannakos et al., 2024). As a subset of artificial intelligence (AI), GenAI is capable of generating diverse types of human-like content, including text, images, music, code, video and 3D assets (Kanbach et al., 2023; Zhang et al., 2023). Its applications span a wide range of domains, transforming industries such as education by enhancing personalised learning, adaptive assessment, learning outcomes, and intelligent tutoring systems (Bahroun et al., 2023a; Borah et al., 2024). In the education sector, educators and academic leaders recognise GenAI’s potential to reduce administrative burdens and offer valuable support in areas such as brainstorming, language assistance, immedi­ ate feedback, professional development, and improving writing and communication skills (Kutty et al., 2024). Within the domain of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) educa­ tion, GenAI tools have gained rapid acceptance among students as a valuable learn­ ing resource for improving their learning performance, surpassing the capabilities of traditional search engines (Elkhodr et",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,2,"2024). Within the domain of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) educa­ tion, GenAI tools have gained rapid acceptance among students as a valuable learn­ ing resource for improving their learning performance, surpassing the capabilities of traditional search engines (Elkhodr et al., 2023). There is also a growing need for the effective integration of GenAI to support diverse aspects of ICT education, including programming, networking, cybersecurity, research and project management. With increasing calls to transform higher education by integrating GenAI tools to equip students with the skills required for an AI-powered workforce (Chiu, 2024), it is essential to investigate how GenAI tools can be used as a learning assistant in ICT education. Educators and students in computer education highlight several benefits of GenAI, such as offering alternative perspectives on programming concepts and providing a convenient source of assistance for learners (Zastudil et al., 2023). GenAI enables students to develop a deeper understanding of complex concepts through person­ alised assistance, potentially transforming educational methods by fostering more flexible and tailored learning experiences. However, while students generally view GenAI positively in higher education,",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,2,"et al., 2023). GenAI enables students to develop a deeper understanding of complex concepts through person­ alised assistance, potentially transforming educational methods by fostering more flexible and tailored learning experiences. However, while students generally view GenAI positively in higher education, they also raise significant concerns about its accuracy, privacy, over-reliance, equity and ethical implications (Chan & Hu, 2023; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). Addressing these complexities, this study investigates the effectiveness of GenAI as a learning assistant in ICT education, focusing on its ability to deliver personalised learning, guidance, and feedback. Specifically, this study examines the utility, challenges, ethical considerations, learning behaviours, skills development, workforce preparedness, and academic out­ comes associated with the integration of GenAI tools as learning assistants in ICT education. In doing so, the study also seeks to provide an understanding of how these tools influence students’ educational development and readiness for profes­ sional environments. To achieve these aims, this study addresses the following research questions (RQ): 1 3 23694",162
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 RQ1: How do students perceive the integration of GenAI tools as a learning assistant in their ICT education, and how does this impact their learning experiences and attitudes towards AI? RQ2: What are the challenges and ethical considerations associated with the use of GenAI tools as a learning assistant in ICT education, and how can these be addressed? RQ3: How do GenAI tools influence students’ learning behaviours, skills develop­ ment, and preparedness for the workforce? RQ4: How does the use of GenAI tools as a learning assistant impact student aca­ demic performance and learning outcomes in ICT subjects? Considered holistically, these four research questions form a cohesive inquiry that moves from students’ current experiences with GenAI, through perceived benefits and risks, to their views on its potential role and benefits in enhancing student perfor­ mance. By capturing student perspectives, the findings of this study aim to develop a framework for integrating GenAI tools in ways that enhance learning while address­ ing potential concerns. The remainder of the paper is structured as follows: the next",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,3,"mance. By capturing student perspectives, the findings of this study aim to develop a framework for integrating GenAI tools in ways that enhance learning while address­ ing potential concerns. The remainder of the paper is structured as follows: the next section presents the literature review, followed by the methodology. Subsequently, the results and analy­ sis are detailed, leading to a discussion of the findings. Following this, we present the GROW AI framework, developed directly from empirical findings with ICT students, and justify it as an effective model for integrating GenAI into educational delivery structures. The paper concludes with a summary and limitations. 2 Literature review As GenAI continues to revolutionise education, its integration as a learning assistant in ICT education presents both significant opportunities and challenges. This section examines existing research on GenAI’s role in enhancing teaching and learning, user experiences, ethical considerations, and its capacity to support personalised learning, skill development, and trust. Given the relatively recent emergence of GenAI tools in universities, this review also incorporates insights beyond ICT education to inform the study. GenAI has gained significant",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,3,"experiences, ethical considerations, and its capacity to support personalised learning, skill development, and trust. Given the relatively recent emergence of GenAI tools in universities, this review also incorporates insights beyond ICT education to inform the study. GenAI has gained significant attention in education due to its capability to produce human-like text, images, and other content, offering valuable resources for educa­ tors and students alike (Chan & Lee, 2023; Farrelly & Baker, 2023). For example, Haroud and Saqri (2025) emphasise that while GenAI enhances access and flexibil­ ity, it may hinder students’ critical thinking without structured support, underscoring the need for digital literacy and careful pedagogical framing. Recent research high­ lights the diverse roles of GenAI in education, which presents both opportunities and challenges. Recent scholarship has proposed several frameworks to guide the effec­ tive and ethical integration of AI into higher education. Zhou and Schofield (2024) present an AI literacy framework for educators, emphasising the importance of AI competence for student employability and global citizenship while also acknowl­ edging the barriers educators face in adopting AI tools. Complementing this, Chan",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,3,"higher education. Zhou and Schofield (2024) present an AI literacy framework for educators, emphasising the importance of AI competence for student employability and global citizenship while also acknowl­ edging the barriers educators face in adopting AI tools. Complementing this, Chan 1 3 23695",43
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 (2023) introduces the AI Ecological Education Policy Framework, which outlines pedagogical, governance, and operational dimensions for managing AI’s integration in universities. These frameworks provide essential context for the development of future models, which build on these principles through a student-informed lens, cap­ turing the lived experiences, expectations, and ethical concerns of learners engaging with GenAI in academic settings. Moreover, in a global policy scan, Jin et al. (2025) reveal the wide variance in how institutions approach GenAI integration, recom­ mending ethical guidelines, curriculum redesign, and professional development as foundational for effective use. GenAI tools can reshape education by providing personalised, real-time assistance(Kanont et al., 2024; Nyaaba et al., 2024). For instance, Nyaaba et al. (2024) investigated the use of GenAI applications among pre-service teachers’ and found these tools served as effective “learning buddies”, providing access to reading materi­ als, detailed explanations, and practical examples. Similarly, Yilmaz and Karaoglan Yilmaz (2023) examined the impact of using ChatGPT on programming education and reported significant improvements in computational thinking skills, program­ ming self-efficacy, and motivation among students",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,4,"access to reading materi­ als, detailed explanations, and practical examples. Similarly, Yilmaz and Karaoglan Yilmaz (2023) examined the impact of using ChatGPT on programming education and reported significant improvements in computational thinking skills, program­ ming self-efficacy, and motivation among students using the tool. GenAI tools have also been shown to enhance educators’ self-efficacy and AI literacy, fostering innova­ tive teaching practices and aiding tasks like content creation and assessment prepara­ tion (Kanont et al., 2024; Meli et al., 2024). Chugh et al. (2025) emphasise that while students in ICT disciplines found GenAI valuable for debugging and ideation, they also highlighted the need for digital literacy training and guided usage to maximise its pedagogical value. Despite these benefits, the use of GenAI in education raises concerns regarding accuracy, trustworthiness, and ethical implications. While educators generally hold positive attitudes towards GenAI tools (Kanont et al., 2024; Nyaaba et al., 2024), there are persistent challenges related to the reliability of AI-generated content. For example, incorrect or biased outputs can mislead students, necessitating better train­ ing for educators and students on effectively using these",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,4,"(Kanont et al., 2024; Nyaaba et al., 2024), there are persistent challenges related to the reliability of AI-generated content. For example, incorrect or biased outputs can mislead students, necessitating better train­ ing for educators and students on effectively using these tools. Ethical concerns, such as academic integrity, are particularly pressing. The poten­ tial for students to submit AI-generated work as their own raises issues around pla­ giarism and undermines the development of critical thinking and problem-solving skills (Farrelly & Baker, 2023; Susnjak & McIntosh, 2024; Wood & Moss, 2024). Over-reliance on GenAI tools may discourage students from engaging deeply with learning materials, thereby hindering their intellectual growth. There is a need for clear institutional guidelines and educator-led interventions to curb misuse (Khlaif et al., 2024). Beyond ethics, GenAI is seen as a bridge to future workforce readiness. Studies demonstrate that ChatGPT enhances university students’ creative problem-solving capabilities (Urban et al., 2024) and critical thinking skills (Almazrou et al., 2024; García-Alonso et al., 2024). Furthermore, research by Rathnayake and Gunawardana (2023) explores how GenAI supports human resource management by streamlining processes",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,4,"ChatGPT enhances university students’ creative problem-solving capabilities (Urban et al., 2024) and critical thinking skills (Almazrou et al., 2024; García-Alonso et al., 2024). Furthermore, research by Rathnayake and Gunawardana (2023) explores how GenAI supports human resource management by streamlining processes such as hiring, training, and performance evaluations. Brynjolfsson et al. (2023) note that while GenAI can increase productivity, its impact varies widely among individuals, underscoring the need for tailored applications in different educa­ tional and professional contexts. Batista et al. (2024) echo this, noting that successful 1 3 23696",89
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 preparation for AI-driven futures depends on fostering equitable access, digital flu­ ency, and flexible skills across student cohorts. The integration of GenAI tools into curricula requires strategic planning to enhance, rather than replace, essential educational processes. Effective implementa­ tion involves teaching students prompt-writing skills, improving educators’ AI lit­ eracy, and addressing broader ethical concerns such as data privacy and algorithmic bias (Kanont et al., 2024; So et al., 2024; Yilmaz & Karaoglan Yilmaz, 2023). With­ out such measures, the full potential of GenAI in education may remain untapped. Questions remain about using GenAI effectively to enhance academic perfor­ mance and who should take responsibility for ensuring its ethical use in education. To optimise the benefits of new technology, GenAI in ICT requires strategic integra­ tion rather than just adoption. Through a methodical approach and strategic choice of integration tactics, the technology discipline can successfully leverage the poten­ tial of GenAI to drive innovation (Dwivedi et al., 2023). Embracing GenAI requires constant learning and adaptation, but those who do so with a clear plan and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,5,"strategic choice of integration tactics, the technology discipline can successfully leverage the poten­ tial of GenAI to drive innovation (Dwivedi et al., 2023). Embracing GenAI requires constant learning and adaptation, but those who do so with a clear plan and clear vision will be well-positioned to lead in the digital age (Reznikov, 2024). Although it is disappointing that most national AI policy strategies have not yet addressed AI in education and its ethical implications, the AI community still has the chance to become more involved with policymakers and influence policy discourse, regulation, and the ethics of AI in education for the greater good (Kenthapadi et al., 2023; Schiff, 2021). The AI Act and related regulatory frameworks must consider the unique char­ acteristics of trained models to address their implications in educational contexts (Hacker et al., 2023). 3 Methodology This study adopted a qualitative research methodology, which is well-suited for exploring complex phenomena and capturing detailed participant perspectives (Tenny et al., 2023). Qualitative methods facilitate identifying emergent themes, sup­ port participant-focused exploration, and address ethical considerations (Nowell et al., 2017). To",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,5,"adopted a qualitative research methodology, which is well-suited for exploring complex phenomena and capturing detailed participant perspectives (Tenny et al., 2023). Qualitative methods facilitate identifying emergent themes, sup­ port participant-focused exploration, and address ethical considerations (Nowell et al., 2017). To achieve the study’s objectives, focus groups were selected as the pri­ mary data collection method, providing a structured and facilitated setting for small groups of individuals to discuss specific topics (Kitzinger, 1995). Three universities with a strong focus on information and communication tech­ nology education were purposefully selected to ensure that participant experiences were drawn from institutions where GenAI integration is particularly relevant. These activities incorporated GenAI tools such as Microsoft Copilot and OpenAI ChatGPT into the learning process across five ICT subjects: Business Process Management, Cybersecurity, ICT Project Management, Networking, and Research Projects involv­ ing software development. The in-class activities were designed to enhance students’ understanding of ICT concepts, enable them to generate answers to subject-related questions, and provide personalised learning experiences through the use of a GenAI tool. For instance, in the Research Project, the activities focused on",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,5,"activities were designed to enhance students’ understanding of ICT concepts, enable them to generate answers to subject-related questions, and provide personalised learning experiences through the use of a GenAI tool. For instance, in the Research Project, the activities focused on helping students understand Python programming code and convert and validate the output, whereas in Cybersecurity, 1 3 23697",58
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 they centred on identifying active devices within a private network, analysing open ports and services, explaining the function of these services, and assessing potential vulnerabilities. These activities aimed to provide students with practical, hands-on exposure to GenAI tools within the context of their ICT education, thereby preparing them for the subsequent focus group discussions. Following the completion of the in-class activities, five subject-based focus group discussions were conducted. Each session lasted up to 90 min and included a total of 19 student participants who volunteered after completing the activities. The number of focus groups and participants was informed by the empirical findings of Guest et al. (2016), which indicate that more than 90% of all themes can typically be identified within three to six focus groups. The discussions were guided by semi-structured questions directly aligned with the study’s research questions. This format provided participants with a forum to share their experiences, discuss diverse perspectives, and engage in meaningful dialogue (Wilkinson, 1998). Focus groups were particularly advantageous for this study as they captured the complexity",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,6,"aligned with the study’s research questions. This format provided participants with a forum to share their experiences, discuss diverse perspectives, and engage in meaningful dialogue (Wilkinson, 1998). Focus groups were particularly advantageous for this study as they captured the complexity of participants’ responses and context-specific challenges, offering deeper insights into their views and enabling the researcher to explore emerging themes more thoroughly (Gibbs, 1997). Ethical clearance for the study was obtained from the lead researcher’s univer­ sity Human Research Ethics Committee. All participants provided informed consent, ensuring their voluntary participation and the safeguarding of their privacy. Partici­ pants and institutional identities were anonymised during data analysis and reporting to maintain confidentiality. Personal details were removed or replaced with generic descriptors to protect the privacy of individuals and institutions. These procedures were conducted in accordance with ethical research standards approved by the Ethics Committee, ensuring participant confidentiality was maintained throughout the study. The qualitative data collected from the focus groups were analysed using thematic analysis, a widely used method for identifying, analysing, and reporting patterns (themes) within qualitative data. The thematic",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,6,"the Ethics Committee, ensuring participant confidentiality was maintained throughout the study. The qualitative data collected from the focus groups were analysed using thematic analysis, a widely used method for identifying, analysing, and reporting patterns (themes) within qualitative data. The thematic analysis process involved a structured process comprising several stages, which included initial familiarisation through transcript readings, coding by classifying relevant data segments against focus group questions, grouping codes into preliminary themes, refining and naming themes to accurately reflect the data, and, finally, preparing a detailed analysis with identified patterns and supporting participant quotes (Nowell et al., 2017). The results and anal­ ysis are presented in the next section. 4 Results and analysis This section presents the findings from the focus group discussions and thematic analysis conducted using NVivo software. NVivo provided a comprehensive envi­ ronment for analysing qualitative data (Nvivo, 2025). NVivo cases were created for each participant across the three institutions. These participant codes are referenced in the discussion section where necessary. The initial phase of the thematic analysis involved coding the overarching research questions and sub-questions from the",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,6,2025). NVivo cases were created for each participant across the three institutions. These participant codes are referenced in the discussion section where necessary. The initial phase of the thematic analysis involved coding the overarching research questions and sub-questions from the focus groups into NVivo. This deductive 1 3 23698,49
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 approach provided a hierarchical structure of codes (Fig. 1), which formed the basis for allocating participant statements. Since the focus group questions were closely aligned with the study’s research questions, most responses corresponded directly to the workshop facilitator’s questions. An additional inductive analysis of the data was performed but did not reveal insights beyond those captured in the deductive coding process. 4.1 Sentiment analysis To gauge participant attitudes towards the adoption of GenAI tools, sentiment analy­ sis was conducted using NVivo’s auto-coding feature. This tool analysed individual sentences within the coded data, extracting keywords to categorise sentiment as pos­ itive, mixed, neutral, or negative. Figure 2 summarises the sentiment distribution across responses to the focus group questions. Although this analysis is not intended to provide a precise measure of sentiment strength regarding the research sub-ques­ tions, it offers valuable insights into areas of strength and potential challenges for AI adoption from a participant perspective. For instance, responses to Research Sub- Question 2-a—which examined the ease of use of GenAI tools—were generally positive. However, sentiments",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,7,"sub-ques­ tions, it offers valuable insights into areas of strength and potential challenges for AI adoption from a participant perspective. For instance, responses to Research Sub- Question 2-a—which examined the ease of use of GenAI tools—were generally positive. However, sentiments regarding the trustworthiness of AI-generated outputs were more mixed, with participants expressing reservations about the reliability and dependability of these tools. This data is further quantified in Fig. 3, which breaks down the number of state­ ments categorised as very negative, moderately negative, moderately positive and very positive across all focus groups. Overall, most focus group responses exhibited moderately positive sentiments, with notable exceptions for themes related to respon­ sibility and accountability (RC4_c) and skill development (RC3_c). 4.2 Key themes from coded comments The thematic analysis identified key issues and themes from participants’ comments, categorised by the research sub-questions. Table 1 summarises the top three signifi­ Fig. 1 NVIVO coding structure 1 3 23699",154
