DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,12,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 5 Discussion This section discusses the findings, integrating quotes (structured for better read­ ability) from participants in relation to each of the 16 detailed focus group questions. Participant quotes are tagged with a reference ID in the format: [Focus_Group_Num­ ber]_[Participant_Number]_[Occurrence_Count]. For instance, the fourth quote from Participant 5 in Focus Group 1 would be referenced as (1_5_c4). The discussion is organised according to the sequence of research sub-questions outlined in Table 1. 5.1 Student perceptions of GenAI integration (RQ1) 5.1.1 Initial thoughts and changing attitudes (RQ1_a) Participants generally expressed enthusiasm for the integration of GenAI tools in their academic studies, particularly highlighting gains in task efficiency. The imme­ diacy of response and capacity to provide useful prompts were seen as valuable in facilitating learning. One participant commented:“Generative AI has been very help­ ful for me… it improves efficiency and helped a lot with problem-solving” (5_P1_c1). These benefits were often qualified by recognition of the tools’ limitations, espe­ cially around precision and reliability. As another participant explained: “Using an AI tool in my academics… it’s",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,12,"me… it improves efficiency and helped a lot with problem-solving” (5_P1_c1). These benefits were often qualified by recognition of the tools’ limitations, espe­ cially around precision and reliability. As another participant explained: “Using an AI tool in my academics… it’s quite efficient and not too accurate… because I have to dig deeper on some details. If the question is not precise, it gives different answers” (1_P1_c1). This tension between usefulness and caution aligns with observations by Bozkurt (2024), who emphasises that GenAI’s effectiveness depends heavily on the user’s ability to craft precise and contextually relevant prompts. Students reported using the tools to support specific ICT tasks, such as system design and technical troubleshooting: “For example, if I want to make a network design… I ask how to add the devices… for a basic understanding” (4_P1_c1). Another noted: However, alongside the functional advantages, students voiced a consistent con­ cern about the absence of structured institutional guidance on ethical boundaries and acceptable use. One noted: “I was kind of excited… but I wanted better-defined guide­ lines on where AI can assist and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,12,"functional advantages, students voiced a consistent con­ cern about the absence of structured institutional guidance on ethical boundaries and acceptable use. One noted: “I was kind of excited… but I wanted better-defined guide­ lines on where AI can assist and where it breaches academic integrity” (3_P4_c1). This gap in instructional support reflects a wider issue reported in recent studies. Chan and Hu (2023) emphasise that students desire clear institutional guidance and boundaries when using GenAI tools, particularly to avoid breaching academic integ­ rity policies. Without such scaffolding, students may resort to ad hoc approaches that risk misuse or reinforce misconceptions. Overall, while GenAI was seen to boost confidence and learning efficiency, stu­ dents’ initial impressions also emphasised the need for critical oversight and struc­ tured support. These findings emphasise the importance of institutional interventions that equip students with prompt engineering skills and raise awareness of ethical constraints (Bozkurt, 2024; Al Ali et al., 2024). 1 3 23704",157
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,13,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 5.1.2 Interaction and engagement (RQ1_b) Participants expressed varied views on whether interacting with GenAI tools enhanced their engagement in the learning process. Some reported limited impact, suggesting that AI tools functioned more as passive aids than active contributors to engagement: “Personally, I do not think it really affected my engagement… I use it just like an aid” (2_P2_c3). Others noted that the impact on engagement depended significantly on subject matter and task complexity: “If it is a subject that would traditionally be considered easier, it will engage me more… it saves the effort of researching everything your­ self” (2_P1_c1). A recurring theme was that GenAI tools were largely viewed as supplementary to traditional learning resources. One participant remarked: “In terms of using AI to help get a better understanding… it works as an addition to textbooks, lectures, and tutorials” (3_P4_c2). These insights are consistent with Triberti et al. (2024), who found that AI tools can improve engagement when they function as instructional companions rather than replacements. In particular, students appreciated the exploratory value of",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,13,"to textbooks, lectures, and tutorials” (3_P4_c2). These insights are consistent with Triberti et al. (2024), who found that AI tools can improve engagement when they function as instructional companions rather than replacements. In particular, students appreciated the exploratory value of GenAI, which could introduce related concepts and expand the scope of inquiry. Several participants described how GenAI offered step-by-step support, acting as an interac­ tive guide. The effectiveness of GenAI in fostering engagement was closely tied to prompt formulation. As one participant observed: “It is really good at providing information, but only as good as the prompts you provide” (3_P3_c2). This supports Bozkurt’s (2024) assertion that prompt engineering is a critical liter­ acy in GenAI-supported learning. Students who crafted clearer prompts were able to obtain more meaningful and tailored responses, reinforcing the importance of train­ ing learners in this skill. Moreover, Kanont et al. (2024) found that higher education students’ perceived enjoyment and engagement with GenAI tools are significantly influenced by their interactivity and perceived usefulness, suggesting that responsiveness to learner input fosters more sustained engagement. However, this benefit is",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,13,"Moreover, Kanont et al. (2024) found that higher education students’ perceived enjoyment and engagement with GenAI tools are significantly influenced by their interactivity and perceived usefulness, suggesting that responsiveness to learner input fosters more sustained engagement. However, this benefit is contingent on learn­ ers’ engagement with the tool, not merely its availability. Thus, GenAI’s potential to enhance engagement appears to depend on its alignment with learners’ goals, prompt quality, and contextual integration into teaching design. 5.1.3 Dependence on AI (RQ1_c) Participants expressed diverse views on whether GenAI tools affected their indepen­ dence in learning. While some students regarded them as helpful yet supplementary, others acknowledged a growing reliance due to the tools’ efficiency and integration into routine academic activities. Several participants emphasised their continued autonomy, describing GenAI as a support mechanism rather than a primary source: “I don’t really… just depend on AI itself. Although it’s helpful, I want to make sure what I’m passing to my tutor is not all about AI” (1_P1_c2), and “I don’t think right now I’m very dependent on AI myself… 1 3 23705",179
