DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,14,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 but someone new to AI could definitely get very dependent on it, especially if they’re not told to second guess it” (2_P2_c4). Conversely, others acknowledged increasing dependence, especially in contexts where GenAI helped them save time or overcome difficult programming tasks: “Yes, it definitely made me more dependent. Before I had it, I had to actively try… espe­ cially with coding” (2_P1_c2). Another compared it to everyday tools: “It’s a good tool and I rely on it every time… it’s just like using a car or a bicycle” (5_P3_c1). A nuanced view emerged from participants who described GenAI as a starting point or fallback tool. These students valued its foundational support but still engaged with alternative sources for verification or deeper understanding: “You can feel inde­ pendent while using the tool… but also in understanding what it’s doing for you, not just inputting” (3_P7_c1), and. “If I want to know about something, I usually search it up first, and if those results don’t yield what I’m after, I might lean back to ChatGPT” (3_P4_c3).",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,14,"understanding what it’s doing for you, not just inputting” (3_P7_c1), and. “If I want to know about something, I usually search it up first, and if those results don’t yield what I’m after, I might lean back to ChatGPT” (3_P4_c3). Despite these benefits, concerns about over-reliance and the passive acceptance of GenAI outputs were evident: “We shouldn’t be very dependent on AI… it’s right most of the time, but it can be wrong because it’s not human” (4_P2_c2). These findings reflect what Zhai et al. (2024) argue that over-reliance on AI dia­ logue systems can compromise students’ critical cognitive capabilities, reducing opportunities for independent reasoning and reflection. Maleki et al. (2024) clarify the phenomenon of AI hallucinations—plausible yet incorrect or fabricated out­ puts—warning that such issues may lead to misinformation and undermine trust if users lack the skills to detect and challenge these inaccuracies. In sum, GenAI tools have the potential to support autonomous learning, especially when used as scaffolding rather than solution engines. Their effect on student inde­ pendence is contingent on the user’s critical thinking skills and the",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,14,"and challenge these inaccuracies. In sum, GenAI tools have the potential to support autonomous learning, especially when used as scaffolding rather than solution engines. Their effect on student inde­ pendence is contingent on the user’s critical thinking skills and the presence of insti­ tutional guidance. Embedding digital literacy and critical evaluation skills into higher education curricula can help mitigate the risks of dependency by enabling students to use GenAI tools responsibly and reflectively (Chugh et al., 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). 5.1.4 Sense of achievement (RQ1_d) Participants expressed diverse views on whether GenAI tools contributed to or detracted from their sense of achievement. For some, these tools enhanced accom­ plishment by enabling rapid resolution of complex tasks: “I had to spend a lot of time on an assessment… stuck on one task for three hours. Using ChatGPT, within a few minutes I figured out what I had to do” (3_P5_c1). Conversely, others felt that using GenAI diminished their satisfaction, as it reduced the intellectual effort involved in problem-solving: “The sense of satisfaction is a bit lower when using GenAI compared",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,14,"minutes I figured out what I had to do” (3_P5_c1). Conversely, others felt that using GenAI diminished their satisfaction, as it reduced the intellectual effort involved in problem-solving: “The sense of satisfaction is a bit lower when using GenAI compared to traditional methods” (5_P2_c1). Another noted that usefulness was closely tied to subject area, indicating GenAI’s value varied across disciplines: “For me, it depends on the subject. I’ve had subjects where I literally couldn’t use GenAI” (2_P1_c3). Some participants also likened GenAI to a collaborative peer or ‘study buddy’, describing it as a supportive tool that offered personalised help in ways comparable 1 3 23706",105
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 to peer assistance: “It’s like a study buddy… if you ask another programmer for help, ‘Hey, I’m having trouble with this’” (3_P4). GenAI’s utility was further reflected in how it simplified complex academic instructions, helping students interpret assessment criteria: “I copy a paragraph and paste it in ChatGPT to make it simpler, to understand what the assessment criteria is asking for” (3_P8_c1). These findings highlight that the effect of GenAI on students’ sense of achievement is multifaceted and context-dependent. For some, the tool fosters accomplishment by reducing barriers to understanding and providing immediate feedback. Others, however, associate a higher sense of achievement with independent cognitive effort. AlAli et al. (2024) note that when GenAI is used to support rather than replace student thinking, it can promote engagement with higher-order cognitive processes. Moreover, subject matter plays a crucial role in shaping perceptions of utility. Qu et al. (2024) found that students in applied disciplines such as engineering and business demonstrated higher engagement with GenAI tools than those in pure disciplines, suggesting that subject-specific relevance significantly",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,15,"plays a crucial role in shaping perceptions of utility. Qu et al. (2024) found that students in applied disciplines such as engineering and business demonstrated higher engagement with GenAI tools than those in pure disciplines, suggesting that subject-specific relevance significantly influences both usage and satisfaction. Equity concerns also emerge when different subscription tiers affect access to advanced features such as more accurate responses or deeper contextual understand­ ing. This raises the issue of fairness and consistent learner experience. Ultimately, institutions must prepare students to use GenAI tools strategically, balancing efficiency with academic integrity. Training should focus not only on tool functionality but also on cultivating reflective judgement to ensure students derive both academic value and a genuine sense of achievement. 5.2 Challenges and ethics of GenAI tool use (RQ2) 5.2.1 Ease of use (RQ2_a) Participants widely agreed that GenAI tools were user-friendly and intuitive. Many appreciated the simplicity of the interface and the natural, conversational design that resembled human communication. As one participant noted: “It’s like you want to have a conversation with it, almost just like texting someone” (3_P4_c4).",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,15,"were user-friendly and intuitive. Many appreciated the simplicity of the interface and the natural, conversational design that resembled human communication. As one participant noted: “It’s like you want to have a conversation with it, almost just like texting someone” (3_P4_c4). This ease of interaction was supported by the accessible layout, which made it easy to input questions and receive usable responses. Several participants com­ mented that the tools’ responsiveness and ability to streamline searches reduced the time and effort typically associated with online research. One explained: “It is very user-friendly because it’s quite simple. The layout is just there; you just need to type in the question” (1_P2_c1). Some participants also commented on the evolving functionality of these tools, particularly the inclusion of features like visual content generation. However, limi­ tations were acknowledged, specifically in areas requiring more complex outputs such as diagram creation. One participant observed: “It’s user-friendly for giving text answers, but for diagrams, it’s not” (4_P3_c1). A recurring theme was the importance of crafting clear and specific prompts. Par­ ticipants reported that imprecise or vague questions often",180
