DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 ing responses, indicating that successful use of GenAI is contingent on users’ skills in prompt engineering. As one noted: “You have to ask the question in the right way to get the best response… and then understand what the response actually means” (2_P2_c5). These findings reinforce that ease of use is a central strength of GenAI tools, particularly their conversational interfaces, simple layouts, and quick outputs. This aligns with previous research emphasising functionality, response time, and interac­ tivity as key determinants of user satisfaction in ICT education (Perera et al., 2024). However, effective use remains highly dependent on user skill in prompt engineer­ ing. Poorly framed queries can produce suboptimal responses, echoing Bozkurt’s (2024) argument that prompt literacy is an essential component of GenAI literacy. While the addition of image generation features expands the potential of these tools, current limitations in areas like diagram creation suggest the need for further development. Addressing these gaps may broaden GenAI’s applicability across more technical or design-focused disciplines. 5.2.2 Challenges encountered (RQ2_b) Participants reported a range of challenges",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,16,"potential of these tools, current limitations in areas like diagram creation suggest the need for further development. Addressing these gaps may broaden GenAI’s applicability across more technical or design-focused disciplines. 5.2.2 Challenges encountered (RQ2_b) Participants reported a range of challenges and barriers when using GenAI tools. While one participant encountered no significant issues due to the structured and straightforward nature of the tools, others described key difficulties, particularly related to ambiguity and inaccuracy in GenAI outputs. As one participant reflected: “Sometimes it gives me a struggle to get full context of a query… leading to irrel­ evant or inappropriate responses” (1_P1_c5). These inaccuracies often required additional effort to interpret or verify responses:“Part of it is wrong, and you have to figure out how to fix that or get it to say the right answer” (2_P2_c7). To overcome such limitations, participants commonly turned to supplementary resources. One noted: “Broken links, broken information… having to go back to the website to find that information because it wasn’t available in ChatGPT” (3_P8_c2). Prompt refinement emerged as a key strategy to resolve confusion: “I",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,16,"commonly turned to supplementary resources. One noted: “Broken links, broken information… having to go back to the website to find that information because it wasn’t available in ChatGPT” (3_P8_c2). Prompt refinement emerged as a key strategy to resolve confusion: “I need to refine it and retype, explaining, ‘This is not what I wanted; I meant this’” (5_P2_c2). Some also noted the tools’ limitations in dealing with specialised queries: “If something is too niche… it just doesn’t know. That’s sometimes a challenge I face” (3_P4_c5). Plagiarism was another concern, with some participants pointing out the risks of relying heavily on GenAI tools: “The only challenges I faced were like plagiarism— AI plagiarism” (4_P3_c2). The challenges identified by participants include ambiguity, inaccuracy and limi­ tations in specialised knowledge, which highlight the reliance of GenAI tools on the quality of their training data and their inability to fully contextualise specific queries. These findings align with observations by Baidoo-Anu and Ansah (2023), who note that GenAI tools often lack the depth required to handle niche knowledge effectively. Ambiguity in outputs, often linked to poorly",180
