DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 necessitates critical evaluation and verification of outputs to avoid inaccuracies or misinformation. Concerns about plagiarism further emphasise the need for ethical guidelines and user training in academic settings (Bhullar et al., 2024). Such training can help stu­ dents understand how to appropriately credit AI-generated content and use it as a learning aid rather than a shortcut that bypasses original thought. Implementing a clearly formulated plan that dynamically adapts the use of these technologies in response to changing circumstances can have benefits (Reznikov, 2024) and help mitigate the problem of inaccurate outputs. Such an approach would involve regular updates to the tools’ capabilities, ongoing user education, and the integration of real-time feedback mechanisms to improve their reliability. 5.2.3 Ethical considerations (RQ2_c) Participants identified a range of ethical and data privacy concerns associated with the use of GenAI tools in education. While one participant expressed a fundamentally negative view of AI technologies, dismissing concerns as dystopian (“I genuinely don’t care… I hate this stuff on a fundamental level” [2_P1_c4]), this perspective was not representative of the",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,17,"of GenAI tools in education. While one participant expressed a fundamentally negative view of AI technologies, dismissing concerns as dystopian (“I genuinely don’t care… I hate this stuff on a fundamental level” [2_P1_c4]), this perspective was not representative of the broader consensus. Most participants acknowledged key ethical risks, including data security, intel­ lectual property, and biases in AI-generated content. As one participant put it: “Ethi­ cal concerns including data privacy, potential bias in AI responses… I think all of them are important” (5_P2_c3). Concerns about plagiarism were widespread, with a clear emphasis on transpar­ ency in acknowledging AI-assisted work:“In the assignment, we need to acknowl­ edge whether we used AI or not to assist us” (1_P2_c2). Participants also raised the issue of intellectual property, particularly regarding how AI is trained on copyrighted materials: “The biggest concern would be in terms of images, artists… where AI has just been scanning it” (2_P2_c8). Data privacy emerged as a recurring theme. Participants cautioned against input­ ting sensitive data: “I would never put in… employees’ information, or data from your company that is protected…",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,17,"of images, artists… where AI has just been scanning it” (2_P2_c8). Data privacy emerged as a recurring theme. Participants cautioned against input­ ting sensitive data: “I would never put in… employees’ information, or data from your company that is protected… like driver information or birthdays. I typically don’t enter any of that” (3_P7_c3). Some also worried about AI inadvertently leaking sensitive information used in training: “AI tools may use or leak sensitive personal information that was part of its training data” (4_P3_c3). Participants called for greater transparency from AI developers regarding data col­ lection and usage. They suggested that platforms should clearly inform users about what data is collected and seek explicit consent: “They should confirm consent and fully inform us on what data is being collected about me” (1_P1_c6). These concerns align with literature highlighting ethical risks such as plagiarism, intellectual property violations, and data misuse (Al-kfairy et al., 2024). The reli­ ance of these tools on training data introduces additional risks, as such datasets may inadvertently include sensitive or biased information, leading to privacy breaches or systemic inaccuracies",180
