DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 linked to the quality of their training datasets and the transparency of their outputs (Tsamados et al., 2021). To address these issues, scholars such as Bahroun et al. (2023b); Dwivedi et al. (2023) advocate for ethics education in university curricula, including covering pla­ giarism avoidance, data privacy, and intellectual property. They also emphasise the importance of educating students about consent and responsible AI use. Ultimately, institutional policies must go beyond technical training and engage students in reflec­ tive discussions about the ethical implications of AI, helping ensure not just compe­ tent but conscientious usage. 5.2.4 Societal implications (RQ2_d) Participants reflected on the broader societal implications of integrating GenAI into education, identifying both long-term benefits and challenges. A common view was that GenAI’s role in society is inevitable and accelerating. As one participant observed: “You can’t put the genie back in the bottle. ChatGPT, generative AI, it is a part of our world, and it is becoming a part of our world faster and faster” (3_P7_c4). Time-saving and productivity-enhancing benefits were widely recognised, par­ ticularly",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,18,"can’t put the genie back in the bottle. ChatGPT, generative AI, it is a part of our world, and it is becoming a part of our world faster and faster” (3_P7_c4). Time-saving and productivity-enhancing benefits were widely recognised, par­ ticularly in both academic and workplace settings. Participants remarked: “I think it’s very useful… it actually saves our time” (4_P3_c4), and “It can boost our perfor­ mance way, way faster for the white-collar worker” (5_P3_c2). Despite these advantages, participants voiced concerns about the impact of GenAI on learning quality. There was a shared worry that excessive reliance on AI could hinder deep engagement, reduce conceptual understanding, and compromise funda­ mental learning. As one participant explained: “One of the bigger challenges is just people getting an answer and not understanding it” (3_P1_c2). This reflects broader concerns about superficial learning, where AI-generated out­ puts may be accepted uncritically, bypassing reflection or comprehension. The risk of an emerging “AI divide” also emerged, where differences in AI literacy and access could widen educational inequalities. One participant captured this tension: “Some people not using it… suffer",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,18,"puts may be accepted uncritically, bypassing reflection or comprehension. The risk of an emerging “AI divide” also emerged, where differences in AI literacy and access could widen educational inequalities. One participant captured this tension: “Some people not using it… suffer from not being able to understand it. And some people using it… suffer from not having it properly integrated to a point where they’re no longer learning” (2_P1_c5). Ethical concerns were raised about the outsourcing of cognitive effort to machines, and the risk that this could normalise intellectual passivity: “Outsourcing thought is not something that should be done willy-nilly” (3_P8_c3). These findings highlight a central tension in GenAI’s societal integration. While it can increase efficiency and productivity, there is a real risk of undermining users’ critical thinking, problem-solving, and independent learning. Gerlich (2025) warns of long-term erosion of cognitive autonomy when users rely on AI outputs without reflection or evaluation. The notion of an “AI divide” echoes equity challenges previously noted by Dwivedi et al. (2023), who stress that differential access to AI tools and training can entrench existing disparities.",180
