DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,19,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 Finally, the ethical dimensions of delegating intellectual tasks to machines require attention. Dakakni and Safa (2023) argue that ethical awareness and human agency must remain at the centre of educational practice, especially when integrating tools that can so easily supplant human reasoning. 5.3 GenAI tool influence on behaviour and learning (RQ3) 5.3.1 Understanding complex concepts (RQ3_a) Participants widely regarded GenAI tools as effective aids for understanding com­ plex ICT concepts, often likening them to supportive peers or mentors, resources that complemented traditional support networks such as tutors or classmates. As one participant remarked:“I think I would have struggled to learn a lot of these concepts without external help anyway, like a friend who already understands it or reaching out to my tutor or lecturer” (2_P1_c6). A key benefit cited was GenAI’s usefulness in programming contexts. Participants described using the tools to verify code, resolve syntax errors, and interpret unfa­ miliar programming logic, with one participant noting: “GenAI helped me verify my answers, especially in coding… even though it gave me different answers, it was",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,19,"contexts. Participants described using the tools to verify code, resolve syntax errors, and interpret unfa­ miliar programming logic, with one participant noting: “GenAI helped me verify my answers, especially in coding… even though it gave me different answers, it was still really helpful” (1_P1_c7). GenAI was also valued for simplifying technical terminology and unpacking new conceptual content. Several participants reported that its ability to provide defini­ tions and explain jargon made it easier to approach unfamiliar topics. One partici­ pant stated: “In research and study, GenAI helped me a lot… when getting into new concepts, I normally do not know where to navigate or about new ideas and glossary terms” (5_P3_c3). A common approach involved breaking down large problems into smaller, step- by-step tasks, with GenAI acting as a guide throughout the process: “I try to break down those complex problems into smaller, simpler problems. If it’s something big, I ask: How do I do the first part? The second part? The third part?” (2_P2_c10). Underlying these experiences was the importance of prompt clarity. Participants frequently acknowledged that the usefulness",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,19,"problems into smaller, simpler problems. If it’s something big, I ask: How do I do the first part? The second part? The third part?” (2_P2_c10). Underlying these experiences was the importance of prompt clarity. Participants frequently acknowledged that the usefulness of GenAI depended heavily on how well the question was framed. One noted: “As long as I give it a really good prompt… there’s probably an 80% chance it will give me the answer I am after” (3_P4_c6). Collectively, these findings support the view that GenAI tools act as valuable supplementary resources in ICT education, rather than replacements for formal instruction. This reflects the early assertions by Ohsuga (2001), who proposed that AI learning systems should be autonomous, practical, and general to be effective learning partners. However, the extent to which GenAI can support increasingly specialised knowl­ edge areas remains uncertain and warrants further exploration. The participants’ experiences highlight the need for students to develop skills in prompt engineering and critical evaluation of AI-generated responses. These competencies are essential to collaborating effectively with GenAI tools, ensuring they are used as",180
