DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,20,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 5.3.2 Personalised learning (RQ3_b) Participants highlighted several aspects of GenAI tools that facilitated personalised learning. One of the most frequently cited advantages was 24/7 availability, which allowed students to access support beyond conventional learning hours: “It’s a 24/7 available software… providing us with learning assistance whenever we need, with­ out the limitations of school or tutor schedules” (4_P3_c6).1 The provision of immediate feedback was also seen as critical, enabling real-time responses to learning challenges and improving the efficiency of self-guided study: “It provided me a lot of resources and also gave instant feedback” (5_P1_c3). Despite these benefits, some participants questioned whether GenAI tools offered true personalisation, noting that the tools operated more like generic assistants than tailored educational solutions: “There’s no personalisation… it’s not like a personal education product for me. It’s just a thing that I use” (2_P1_c7). However, others emphasised the user’s role in shaping the learning experience. By crafting precise prompts and controlling the structure of the interaction, partici­ pants reported that they could direct the tool to better suit their",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,20,"thing that I use” (2_P1_c7). However, others emphasised the user’s role in shaping the learning experience. By crafting precise prompts and controlling the structure of the interaction, partici­ pants reported that they could direct the tool to better suit their learning styles. For example: “In terms of personalised learning, it’s pretty good… because you’re the one fully asking the questions and mostly in control of the conversation, you can tai­ lor it to how you like to work and learn” (3_P4_c7). These findings suggest that while GenAI systems may not intrinsically personalise content, they enable quasi-personalised learning when users proactively guide the interaction. This reflects broader insights from Chen et al. (2024), who argue that large language models contribute to individualised learning through their responsive reasoning and broad informational access. However, the level of personalisation is reliant on user engagement and input. This finding aligns with Ziebell and Skeat (2023), who noted that GenAI tools can serve as effective learning companions, but their effectiveness depends on how actively users interact with the technology. Students who refine their interactions and leverage",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,20,"and input. This finding aligns with Ziebell and Skeat (2023), who noted that GenAI tools can serve as effective learning companions, but their effectiveness depends on how actively users interact with the technology. Students who refine their interactions and leverage the flexibility of these tools can create tailored learning experiences that better meet their unique needs and preferences. 5.3.3 Skill development (RQ3_c) Participants reported that using GenAI tools enhanced several non-ICT skills, includ­ ing communication, problem-solving, critical thinking, and research capabilities. These tools were viewed as supportive in both academic and professional contexts, with particular emphasis on improving written communication. One participant noted: “First, the communication and the collaboration is very helpful for my com­ munication, especially with grammar and stuff like that.” (1_P1_c8). Others cited GenAI’s utility in improving the tone, structure, and clarity of formal correspondence, such as emails: “I can just tell ChatGPT or any software what I 1 The term ‘school’ refers to academic units within these universities (e.g., School of Information Technol­ ogy), not to primary or secondary education contexts. 1 3 23712",178
