DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,21,"competencies in these areas. Engaging with the tool, they argued, helped refine such skills further: “You should really have those critical thinking and researching skills before using AI, and then you can improve them during the use of AI.” (2_P2_c12). The feedback accentuates the alignment of GenAI tools with Bloom’s taxonomy of learning, which encompasses knowledge, comprehension, analysis, application, synthesis, and evaluation (Martin & Nesbit, 2024). Participants’ experiences suggest that GenAI tools can engage multiple levels of this taxonomy by by enabling learners to apply concepts, analyse outputs, and evaluate information accuracy. However, the development of communication skills was seen as double-edged. While GenAI offered grammatically refined outputs, participants expressed concern that these polished responses might lack originality, potentially diluting personal voice and reducing authenticity. This reflects a broader pedagogical challenge: lever­ aging AI-generated support without sacrificing creativity or critical self-expression. To maximise the developmental potential of GenAI tools, educators should encourage intentional use, prompting students to reflect on when and how to engage AI effectively while maintaining their unique contributions. Structured guidance in this area could ensure that cognitive",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,21,"self-expression. To maximise the developmental potential of GenAI tools, educators should encourage intentional use, prompting students to reflect on when and how to engage AI effectively while maintaining their unique contributions. Structured guidance in this area could ensure that cognitive and interpersonal skill development is not com­ promised by over-reliance on generic AI outputs. As Roe and Perkins (2024) caution, poorly integrated AI may reduce learner agency and active engagement. Similarly, Fan et al. (2025) warn that overuse of GenAI may lead to metacognitive laziness and undermine critical thinking and deeper learning. 5.3.4 Preparation for the workforce (RQ3_d) Participants expressed mixed views on whether GenAI tools effectively prepare stu­ dents for the workforce. While some were sceptical, viewing GenAI as more benefi­ cial in academic contexts, others recognised its potential to support workplace tasks if used strategically. One participant described GenAI as “really great at helping you do assignments” but “useless in the workforce” (2_P1_c9), while another questioned how technical tasks would integrate GenAI meaningfully in professional settings (1_P2_c4). In contrast, other participants framed GenAI as a supportive aid rather",180
