DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,22,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 “With ChatGPT, I think it’s very easy.” (4_P3_c7). However, the consensus emphasised the need for domain-specific training and professional adaptation. As one participant observed, the effectiveness of GenAI depends on job type and the extent to which educational experiences align with workplace expectations (2_P2_c13). Some participants viewed GenAI positively for continuous learning and upskill­ ing, particularly in rapidly evolving industries. They cited its ability to support mas­ tery of core competencies and awareness of current trends, especially when used intentionally to complement professional development (1_P1_c9). These findings align with Noy and Zhang (2023), who reported that GenAI tools, such as ChatGPT, improved worker efficiency by reducing task completion times and enhancing the quality of outputs. Nonetheless, the gap between academic utility and real-world applicability persists. As Ali and Aysan (2024) note, effective GenAI integration in the workforce requires tailored applications, particularly in regulated fields like healthcare. Bridging this divide requires rethinking how GenAI is embedded into educational contexts. Institutions should implement targeted training focused on industry-rele­ vant applications, critical thinking, and ethical use. Emphasising",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,22,"in the workforce requires tailored applications, particularly in regulated fields like healthcare. Bridging this divide requires rethinking how GenAI is embedded into educational contexts. Institutions should implement targeted training focused on industry-rele­ vant applications, critical thinking, and ethical use. Emphasising GenAI as a comple­ ment to human expertise can enhance adaptability, digital fluency, and professional readiness, thereby preparing students to navigate AI-augmented work environments. 5.4 GenAI impact on ICT learning outcomes (RQ4) 5.4.1 Impact on academic performance (RQ4_a) Participants reported mixed experiences regarding the impact of GenAI tools on their academic performance and learning outcomes in ICT subjects. Many highlighted significant benefits, particularly in terms of efficiency, comprehension, and task management. One participant noted a clear improvement in their academic results after using GenAI tools: “Yeah, it helped me in a previous task that we mentioned before, so in the end for the result I saw the improvement before using AI and after using genera­ tive AI.” (5_P3_c5). Another participant praised the tools’ ability to streamline the learning process, enabling them to manage their workload across multiple subjects: “Yes, it’s",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,22,"end for the result I saw the improvement before using AI and after using genera­ tive AI.” (5_P3_c5). Another participant praised the tools’ ability to streamline the learning process, enabling them to manage their workload across multiple subjects: “Yes, it’s probably made them better. I don’t know. It actually takes a lot of time away from doing assign­ ments, so that is good and lets me focus more on multiple classes rather than just a couple that are hard.” (2_P1_c10). The time-saving benefits of GenAI tools were frequently mentioned. One partici­ pant noted their utility in simplifying concepts and troubleshooting: “It’s definitely benefited… It’s been a big time saver, at the minimum, for me to understand concepts and stuff like that. Maybe the troubleshooting, that sort of thing.” (3_P4_c10). However, concerns about over-reliance on GenAI tools emerged as a recurring theme. One participant observed that dependency on these tools could hinder the development of critical problem-solving skills: “For example, they are addicted to using it, so they do not think or independently find the answers to their questions. They are",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,22,"theme. One participant observed that dependency on these tools could hinder the development of critical problem-solving skills: “For example, they are addicted to using it, so they do not think or independently find the answers to their questions. They are very addicted to ChatGPT, so they have very limited problem-solving skills 1 3 23714",54
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,23,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 because of that. They do not know how to solve it manually if someone asks—they are only dependent on the ChatGPT answer.” (4_P3_c8). GenAI tools demonstrate strong potential to enhance academic performance by fostering creativity, simplifying complex concepts, and improving task efficiency. These tools can also assist in multitasking and managing the demands of multiple subjects. Such findings align with existing literature, highlighting the role of GenAI in supporting knowledge acquisition and boosting academic and job performance (Ahn, 2024). Nonetheless, the risk of over-reliance on GenAI tools warrants attention. An over- reliance on these tools has the potential to undermine critical thinking skills, as it may discourage learners from engaging deeply with problem-solving and indepen­ dent analysis (Ali et al., 2024). As participants indicated in their responses, there are positive benefits to using GenAI in learning, such as saving time and breaking down complex topics into man­ ageable chunks. However, these advantages must be tempered by its drawbacks, including its potentially addictive nature, which may detract from developing essen­ tial cognitive and analytical skills.",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,23,"in learning, such as saving time and breaking down complex topics into man­ ageable chunks. However, these advantages must be tempered by its drawbacks, including its potentially addictive nature, which may detract from developing essen­ tial cognitive and analytical skills. To address these concerns, universities could incorporate this topic into preliminary AI training sessions, equipping students with the knowledge and strategies needed to use GenAI effectively while mitigating its potential drawbacks. 5.4.2 Trust in AI (RQ4_b) Participants shared mixed views on the trustworthiness of GenAI tools, with many expressing concerns about their reliability and accuracy. One participant commented: “Usually at the core it’s telling you what to do or it’s giving you something to go on but as a whole usually it comes out with the wrong thing.” (2_P2_c14). Another participant highlighted the inherent limitations of GenAI in addressing complex or nuanced issues: “You have to understand that you can’t trust it like it’s some freaking master at the subject. It’s not. It’s just giving you the most likely answer that you’re expecting.” (2_P1_c11). The accuracy of responses was also",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,23,"or nuanced issues: “You have to understand that you can’t trust it like it’s some freaking master at the subject. It’s not. It’s just giving you the most likely answer that you’re expecting.” (2_P1_c11). The accuracy of responses was also seen to depend on the clarity of user inputs. Poorly constructed prompts often resulted in vague or irrelevant outputs: “Because sometimes if you ask like a vague question it will also give us a vague answer.” (1_P1_c11). To mitigate these limitations, participants emphasised the need for indepen­ dent research to validate AI-generated information. One participant described their approach: “I have a bit of limited trust with AI because I find when I’ve prompted or asked questions, it’s generally misleading, which plays into the fact that you have to do your own research.” (3_P3_c5). Cross-referencing AI outputs with other sources was frequently suggested as a strategy to ensure reliability: “If I’m not sure of it, I will get the answer from Gen­ erative AI and compare it against what we can actually get from traditional Google Search and supporting sources.” (1_P2_c5).",180
