DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,24,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 Participants highlighted the limited suitability of GenAI for specialised or techni­ cal queries: “If it’s a niche thing, maybe something like finding a part number for a car, I don’t really trust it.” (3_P4_c11). Despite these concerns, participants recognised the value of GenAI for brainstorm­ ing and idea generation: “For tasks like brainstorming or receiving explanations on topics we are learning, ChatGPT can be a helpful guide.” (4_P3_c9). These findings align with existing research, which highlights trust as a critical fac­ tor in the adoption of GenAI tools (Kanont et al., 2024; Nyaaba et al., 2024). Trust is influenced by factors, including user competency, the tool’s design features, and the context in which it is applied (Kaplan et al., 2023). Participants’ feedback highlights that the effectiveness of GenAI depends not only on the technology itself but also on users’ ability to critically evaluate its outputs. Poorly constructed prompts and over-reliance on GenAI tools were seen as significant contributors to inaccurate or misleading responses. Further, as emphasised by Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) and Popenici and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,24,"itself but also on users’ ability to critically evaluate its outputs. Poorly constructed prompts and over-reliance on GenAI tools were seen as significant contributors to inaccurate or misleading responses. Further, as emphasised by Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) and Popenici and Kerr (2017), cultivating digital literacy and fostering users’ epistemic vigilance, defined as the ability to evaluate information credibility, can enhance trust calibration. Encour­ aging users to adopt reflective practices and verify AI responses against authoritative sources is critical to ensuring GenAI’s effective and responsible use in education. 5.4.3 Responsibility and accountability (RQ4_c) Participants widely agreed that ensuring the ethical use of GenAI tools in education requires shared accountability among various stakeholders, including students, edu­ cators, policymakers, and developers. One participant captured the essence of this consensus: “I think it’s everyone. I think everyone has a part of accountability to play when it comes to AI.” (3_P7_c5). Many participants emphasised that students bear significant responsibility for ethi­ cal AI use, particularly in avoiding plagiarism and over-reliance on AI-generated out­ puts: “We should use AI responsibly to avoid plagiarism and misuse of",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,24,"when it comes to AI.” (3_P7_c5). Many participants emphasised that students bear significant responsibility for ethi­ cal AI use, particularly in avoiding plagiarism and over-reliance on AI-generated out­ puts: “We should use AI responsibly to avoid plagiarism and misuse of AI. Because a lot of students think that it will be easy for us to just use AI, just copy and paste that one.” (1_P1_c12). Students were also viewed as being at the “endpoint” of ethical responsibility: “Ultimately, it is the user who is asking the question and getting that information… It’s their responsibility to use it properly.” (3_P8_c4). Participants stressed that educators must guide students on ethical AI usage by providing clear boundaries and training: “The educators could give us a little guid­ ance or support on the ethical and effective ways of using AI.” (1_P1_c13). Institu­ tions, too, were seen as key players in regulating and facilitating ethical AI practices: “In educational institutes, I think the educational commissions are the ones who should introduce these kinds of things and implement them.” (4_P2_c5). Some participants attributed the greatest responsibility",180
