DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,25,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 ing younger generations: “The government must have a policy or rules to limit AI use for the young generation because it will highly affect them.” (4_P3_c10). Developers were also seen as integral to ethical AI use, particularly in ensuring the reliability and fairness of the tools they create: “I think it should be developers who are responsible for this.” (5_P1_c4). The findings suggest a consensus that responsibility for ethical AI use cannot rest solely on one group. This view is consistent with prior research emphasising the criti­ cal role of the IT community in influencing the development of policies, regulations, and the ethical integration of GenAI tools into education systems (Kenthapadi et al., 2023; Schiff, 2021). Delgado et al. (2024) emphasise the importance of cultivat­ ing dialogue among academics, governments, and private-sector actors to ensure the equitable and responsible integration of GenAI tools into educational systems. Key principles such as transparency, equity, responsibility, and beneficence should underpin these collaborations. Policymakers, educators, and developers must work together to create regulatory frameworks that minimise risks of",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,25,"ensure the equitable and responsible integration of GenAI tools into educational systems. Key principles such as transparency, equity, responsibility, and beneficence should underpin these collaborations. Policymakers, educators, and developers must work together to create regulatory frameworks that minimise risks of marginalisation and harm while ensuring the equitable distribution of AI’s benefits. 5.4.4 Future use and recommendations (RQ4_d) Participants broadly supported the continued use of GenAI tools in ICT education, acknowledging their value while highlighting areas for improvement. Many noted that the tools could significantly enhance learning outcomes when coupled with an understanding of their functionalities: “In terms of IT, I think the way we’re going is fine because we have that general understanding of how to use generative AI.” (2_P2_c16). Another participant echoed this view but stressed responsible use: “Yes, definitely, especially in the IT sector. It’s very useful, and I really do recommend using AI tools across various things. However, we should be mindful of how we use it.” (1_P1_c14). Participants called for clear boundaries and guidelines to regulate GenAI use, rec­ ognising the potential for misuse if left",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,25,"I really do recommend using AI tools across various things. However, we should be mindful of how we use it.” (1_P1_c14). Participants called for clear boundaries and guidelines to regulate GenAI use, rec­ ognising the potential for misuse if left unchecked: “I definitely think that it should continue to be… Maybe not entirely encouraged, but as someone said, that barrier is set. You’re more than welcome to use it, as long as you keep this, and this, and this in mind.” (3_P4_c12). One participant advocated for higher education policies that clearly outline permissible applications: “My recommendation for ICT education is that there should be some sort of higher-up guideline or policy on how to use AI. My university already has that, and it should be crystal clear what exactly you can use it for.” (2_P1_c13). Participants suggested several improvements to enhance the tools’ effectiveness, such as better support for image-based inputs and clearer distinctions between free and paid feature sets. One participant explained: “The only feature I wanted was the picture one. They recently updated it so we can upload",180
