DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,26,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 They also emphasised the importance of improving accuracy and reducing bias in AI-generated outputs:“AI tools should integrate real-time fact-checking to ensure the responses provided are accurate.” (4_P3_c12) and""AI tools should be regularly updated to ensure accuracy and minimise biases.” (5_P1_c5). While participants showed enthusiasm for GenAI tools, they stressed the need for continuous improvement and robust regulatory frameworks. These concerns align with existing research advocating for plans and policies that address ethical and prac­ tical challenges in AI use in education (Bhullar et al., 2024; Reznikov, 2024). To maximise the benefits of GenAI, educational institutions should collaborate with policymakers and developers to establish ethical standards and guidelines. This includes addressing issues of accessibility and ensuring that all students can ben­ efit from advanced features regardless of pricing models. Additionally, institutions should implement training programs to teach students how to critically evaluate AI outputs and use these tools responsibly. As Kenthapadi et al. (2023) and Delgado et al. (2024) argue, multi-stakeholder collaboration, encompassing educators, policy­ makers, and developers, is essential to establishing ethical standards and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,26,"to teach students how to critically evaluate AI outputs and use these tools responsibly. As Kenthapadi et al. (2023) and Delgado et al. (2024) argue, multi-stakeholder collaboration, encompassing educators, policy­ makers, and developers, is essential to establishing ethical standards and guidelines for AI integration. The findings of this study emphasise the potential of GenAI tools in ICT educa­ tion while highlighting the complexities of their adoption. Balancing benefits such as enhanced efficiency and personalised learning with challenges like ethical concerns and reliance on accuracy requires a deliberate and informed approach. Moreover, this emphasises the importance of moving beyond isolated implementations towards a more structured, comprehensive framework that can address these multifaceted needs. Moving forward, it is also important to consider broader systemic implications. From a technological perspective, the biases inherent in GenAI training data (Hanna et al., 2025) may influence the content and quality of support students receive, poten­ tially reinforcing existing inequities. Educationally, there is a risk that sustained reliance on AI tools for guidance, feedback, or content generation could inhibit the development of students’ independent critical thinking and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,26,"the content and quality of support students receive, poten­ tially reinforcing existing inequities. Educationally, there is a risk that sustained reliance on AI tools for guidance, feedback, or content generation could inhibit the development of students’ independent critical thinking and metacognitive skills (Çela et al., 2024). At a societal level, the integration of GenAI may exacerbate the digi­ tal divide, disproportionately benefiting students with consistent access to advanced technologies and institutional support (Bentley et al., 2024). These concerns under­ score the importance of ethical foresight and inclusive planning in the implementa­ tion of GenAI within higher education. 6 The GROW-AI framework The GROW-AI framework was developed through the thematic analysis of quali­ tative data gathered from structured focus group discussions. Recurring student concerns and aspirations were synthesised into five core components: Guidelines, Resources, Oversight, Workforce Preparation, and Awareness. These components reflect students’ expectations for ethical, practical, and future-oriented use of GenAI in education. Table 2 summarises how emerging themes from the focus groups directly informed the design of each component of the framework. 1 3 23718",176
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,26,"reflect students’ expectations for ethical, practical, and future-oriented use of GenAI in education. Table 2 summarises how emerging themes from the focus groups directly informed the design of each component of the framework. 1 3 23718",36
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,27,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 Figure 4 visualises the GROW-AI framework, positioning ‘AI Integration in Edu­ cation’ at the centre, surrounded by its five interdependent components. The radial design highlights the holistic and iterative nature of the model, illustrating how these elements reinforce one another to support sustainable and responsible GenAI adoption. To address the complexities identified in the focus group data, the GROW-AI framework provides a structured and actionable guide for integrating GenAI tools into ICT education. It addresses not only the technical implementation of AI, but also the pedagogical, ethical, and practical dimensions raised by students. By doing so, it aims to enhance learning outcomes while supporting responsible and student- informed AI engagement. The following subsections elaborate on each of the five components of the framework. 6.1 Guidelines - establishing standards and policies Clear institutional guidelines and policies are essential to ensure the ethical and effec­ tive use of GenAI tools. These policies should delineate acceptable practices, particu­ larly in the context of academic integrity, by providing boundaries around plagiarism prevention and the use of AI in",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,27,"policies are essential to ensure the ethical and effec­ tive use of GenAI tools. These policies should delineate acceptable practices, particu­ larly in the context of academic integrity, by providing boundaries around plagiarism prevention and the use of AI in assessments. Participants in the study expressed a strong desire for such clarity, noting confusion about when and how GenAI use con­ stitutes a breach of academic conduct. Institutional guidelines should operationalise these policies at the course level, while national frameworks can promote consistency across sectors and address overarching concerns such as intellectual property, data privacy, and bias mitigation. Ethical principles such as transparency, accountability, and fairness should underpin these policies, ensuring that the integration of GenAI aligns with broader societal and educational goals. Framework component Thematic source from focus group discussions Supporting sub-question codes Guidelines Students expressed a need for clear policies on acceptable AI use, ethical boundaries, and academic integrity. Uncertainty over what constitutes plagia­ rism and misuse was common. RQ1_a, RQ2_c, RQ4_c, RQ4_d Resources Strong demand for training in prompt engineering, digital literacy, and use-case examples. Students also",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,27,"clear policies on acceptable AI use, ethical boundaries, and academic integrity. Uncertainty over what constitutes plagia­ rism and misuse was common. RQ1_a, RQ2_c, RQ4_c, RQ4_d Resources Strong demand for training in prompt engineering, digital literacy, and use-case examples. Students also highlighted inequities in access to paid features and desired more robust AI capabilities. RQ2_a, RQ2_b, RQ3_a, RQ3_b, RQ3_c, RQ4_d Oversight Emphasis on shared responsibility, educator inter­ vention, monitoring of AI misuse, and ongoing tool improvement. Calls for institutional scaffolding and support were frequent. RQ2_b, RQ2_c, RQ4_b, RQ4_c Workforce Preparation Students highlighted the importance of aligning educational use of GenAI with future employment contexts. Many saw value in GenAI as a support tool to assist with professional tasks and upskilling. RQ3_c, RQ3_d, RQ4_a Awareness Concerns about social inequity, over-reliance, data privacy, and intellectual passivity. Participants stressed the importance of critical use and a broader understanding of GenAI’s role. RQ1_c, RQ2_c, RQ2_d, RQ4_b, RQ4_d Table 2 Mapping of themes to GROW-AI framework components 1 3 23719",164
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,28,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 6.2 Resources - providing support for effective use This component focuses on educational support during formal learning. It includes instructional materials, skills training (e.g. prompt engineering), and digital literacy initiatives aimed at improving students’ use of GenAI for academic tasks. Resources are institutional enablers, designed to optimise GenAI use within the curriculum. Comprehensive training programs must be designed to equip both students and educators with the necessary skills to engage with these tools effectively. Training should focus on areas such as prompt engineering, critical thinking, and ethical con­ siderations, enabling users to optimise GenAI’s potential. Institutions should also address subscription-based inequities, as some students noted that advanced features are locked behind paywalls, creating an uneven playing field. Students also reported that task complexity affects how satisfied they feel when using GenAI tools. To sup­ port effective learning, resources should be scaffolded to match varying levels of complexity, with worked examples, tutorials, and troubleshooting guides that contex­ tualise GenAI use across both routine and cognitively demanding tasks. For example, resources might include a prompt-engineering workshop",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,28,"port effective learning, resources should be scaffolded to match varying levels of complexity, with worked examples, tutorials, and troubleshooting guides that contex­ tualise GenAI use across both routine and cognitively demanding tasks. For example, resources might include a prompt-engineering workshop to help students deconstruct a networking concept or a quick-reference guide to using GenAI in project plan­ ning. Investing in technical enhancements, such as retrieval-augmented responses with verifiable source citations and expanded functionalities like AI-assisted diagram generation, would further increase the pedagogical value of these tools across diverse educational contexts. Fig. 4 GROW-AI framework 1 3 23720",97
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,29,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 6.3 Oversight - monitoring and continuous improvement Regular oversight and monitoring mechanisms should be established to evaluate the effectiveness of GenAI tools in improving learning outcomes, fostering engagement, and supporting skill development. Such oversight could include periodic reviews of AI usage data, educator-led checks of student interactions with GenAI tools, and formalised feedback channels for students to report concerns or limitations. Feed­ back systems can capture insights from students and educators, allowing for itera­ tive refinement of the tools and their implementation strategies. Participants in the study highlighted the need for shared responsibility and structured guidance to ensure ethical and pedagogically sound use of GenAI. Institutions should also periodically assess the impact of these tools on academic performance, ensuring that their inte­ gration aligns with broader educational objectives. This iterative process will ensure that GenAI tools remain relevant, effective, and aligned with the evolving needs of students and educators. 6.4 Workforce preparation - bridging academic and professional applications This component focuses on aligning GenAI use with professional practice, ensur­ ing students are ready to",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,29,"GenAI tools remain relevant, effective, and aligned with the evolving needs of students and educators. 6.4 Workforce preparation - bridging academic and professional applications This component focuses on aligning GenAI use with professional practice, ensur­ ing students are ready to engage with AI in real-world work environments. While ‘Resources’ support learning within the university, Workforce preparation ensures students can transfer those capabilities to industry-relevant scenarios, reflecting employer expectations and evolving professional norms. Tailored training programs should reflect real-world scenarios and address industry-specific needs, enabling stu­ dents to transition seamlessly into AI-driven workplaces. The focus should extend beyond technical proficiency to include the development of lifelong learning, adapt­ ability, and critical thinking skills. Collaboration with industry stakeholders is essen­ tial to align training with professional standards and to ensure that students are equipped to meet the demands of an AI-enabled workforce. For example, integrating GenAI into industry-based projects or offering simulation exercises that mirror work­ place uses (e.g. writing code explanations for clients using GenAI) prepares students to use such tools appropriately in the workforce. 6.5 Awareness - promoting ethical and",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,29,"For example, integrating GenAI into industry-based projects or offering simulation exercises that mirror work­ place uses (e.g. writing code explanations for clients using GenAI) prepares students to use such tools appropriately in the workforce. 6.5 Awareness - promoting ethical and responsible use Raising awareness of the ethical and societal implications of GenAI is critical to fostering its responsible use in education. Educational programs should include workshops and discussions on topics such as plagiarism, data privacy, bias, and the potential risks of over-reliance. Encouraging a balanced approach, where GenAI tools complement traditional pedagogies rather than replace them, can help students engage critically with these technologies. Awareness campaigns should also include a discussion of access disparities and the implications of differential access to GenAI tools (free vs. paid tiers). Addressing these issues transparently helps build a culture of fairness and inclusion. Moreover, institutions should emphasise the importance of validating AI-generated outputs to avoid uncritical dependence, ensuring that stu­ dents develop the skills necessary for informed and ethical engagement with AI tools. 1 3 23721",172
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,29,"institutions should emphasise the importance of validating AI-generated outputs to avoid uncritical dependence, ensuring that stu­ dents develop the skills necessary for informed and ethical engagement with AI tools. 1 3 23721",32
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,30,"Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:23693–23728 7 Conclusion This study explored the integration of GenAI tools as learning assistants in ICT edu­ cation, addressing their potential benefits, associated challenges, and ethical consid­ erations. The findings amplify the potential of GenAI tools in enhancing learning outcomes, personalising educational experiences, and equipping students with skills for an AI-driven workforce. However, the study also reveals critical complexities, including accuracy limitations, risks of over-reliance, and ethical implications such as privacy concerns and academic integrity challenges. This study engaged students through focus groups and employed thematic anal­ ysis to interpret their perceptions, behaviours, and experiences with GenAI tools. The results highlight the need for a structured and multidimensional approach to AI integration, culminating in the proposed GROW-AI Framework. This framework offers practical guidance by emphasising the importance of clear guidelines, robust resources, continuous oversight, workforce-aligned preparation, and heightened ethi­ cal awareness to ensure the effective and responsible adoption of GenAI tools. While this study offers meaningful insights into the integration of GenAI in ICT education, several limitations should be acknowledged. The focus group sample, com­",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,30,"preparation, and heightened ethi­ cal awareness to ensure the effective and responsible adoption of GenAI tools. While this study offers meaningful insights into the integration of GenAI in ICT education, several limitations should be acknowledged. The focus group sample, com­ prising ICT students from three Australian universities, was relatively small, which may limit the breadth of perspectives captured. Consequently, the generalisability of the findings may be constrained not only by disciplinary and institutional context but also by the limited sample size. Caution should, therefore, be exercised when apply­ ing these findings to other disciplines, regions, or educational settings. Additionally, the qualitative methodology, grounded in focus group discussions, relies on subjec­ tive self-reported data, which, while rich in detail and contextually insightful, may not capture the full diversity of student experiences across broader populations. The inclusion of complementary quantitative data could have enhanced the breadth of the findings by offering a broader cross-sectional perspective and enabling comparisons across demographic or institutional variables. Practically, the findings call for targeted interventions, such as tailored training programs to develop critical skills in prompt engineering",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,30,"have enhanced the breadth of the findings by offering a broader cross-sectional perspective and enabling comparisons across demographic or institutional variables. Practically, the findings call for targeted interventions, such as tailored training programs to develop critical skills in prompt engineering and ethical decision-mak­ ing, alongside the establishment of institutional policies to mitigate risks. Moreover, they reinforce the importance of collaboration among stakeholders, including stu­ dents, educators, policymakers, and AI developers, to ensure GenAI tools serve as enablers of meaningful learning rather than substitutes for intellectual engagement. Future research should build on these findings to explore the longitudinal impact of GenAI tools on student outcomes, including their readiness for dynamic profes­ sional environments. Investigating domain-specific applications, diverse educational contexts, and emerging AI functionalities will further deepen our understanding of the evolving role of GenAI in education. In conclusion, while GenAI tools hold immense potential to transform ICT educa­ tion, their effective integration requires a balanced, ethical, and inclusive approach. The GROW-AI framework provides a roadmap to guide this process, ensuring that AI technologies fulfil their promise of enhancing learning while preparing",180
DOC_044,s10639-025-13686-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-025-13686-3,30,"hold immense potential to transform ICT educa­ tion, their effective integration requires a balanced, ethical, and inclusive approach. The GROW-AI framework provides a roadmap to guide this process, ensuring that AI technologies fulfil their promise of enhancing learning while preparing students to navigate the complexities of a GenAI-powered world. 1 3 23722",52
