DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3 Abstract The emergence of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) technology and the increasing sophistication of such tools have raised concerns about their impact on teaching, learning, and assessment in higher education. To gain an understanding of how students are using GenAI tools for studying and assessments, and to explore their perceptions of its benefits and challenges in higher education, this qualitative study examined the views of Psychology students from two UK universities. Six focus groups (N = 28), consisting of postgraduate psychology students and a few UG students were conducted using semi-structured interview questions to explore their experiences and attitudes toward the use of GenAI during their studies. Stu­ dents reported using GenAI to summarise content, clarify complex ideas, and ease the writing process. While they recognised its value, especially for accessibility and time management, they also expressed concerns about plagiarism, reduced critical thinking, and unclear boundaries of ethical use. Many felt anxious about academic integrity due to inconsistent guidance. Notably, some students viewed AI use as ethically problematic, particularly in group work. The",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,1,"management, they also expressed concerns about plagiarism, reduced critical thinking, and unclear boundaries of ethical use. Many felt anxious about academic integrity due to inconsistent guidance. Notably, some students viewed AI use as ethically problematic, particularly in group work. The findings underscore the ur­ gent need for clearer institutional policies and targeted training to promote ethical, effective engagement with GenAI in academic contexts. Keywords AI in higher education · GenAI and learning · Technology and learning Received: 30 June 2025 / Accepted: 5 February 2026 / Published online: 16 March 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Studying with GenAI: Student views on the opportunities and risks of GenAI in higher education Elida Cena1 · Aideen McParland1 · Wilhelmiina Toivo2 · Blinne Dalton1 · Megan Mundy1 · Patrick A. O’Connor1 · Ashley E. Robertson2 · Maxine Swingler2 · Paul Wilson1 · Connie Duncan1 Elida Cena E.cena@qub.ac.uk Aideen McParland a.mcparland@qub.ac.uk 1 School of Psychology, Queen’s University Belfast, Belfast, UK 2 School of Psychology and Neuroscience, University of Glasgow, Glasgow, UK 1 3",169
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 1 Introduction The emergence of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) tools such as ChatGPT, has raised challenging questions about the nature of teaching, learning, and assess­ ments in Higher Education (HE). These GenAI tools have recently gained significant popularity in academic settings due to their capabilities of assisting with literature reviews, essay writing, research, and other academic projects (Bahroun et al., 2023; Stojanov, 2023; Strzelecki, 2023). One pertinent question is how GenAI can poten­ tially transform the learning landscape as we know it in, HE and the impact this will have on student experiences. Pedagogical research to date offers a fusion of find­ ings rooted in opportunities and challenges (Nanaware & Sharma, 2021) of GenAI in teaching, learning, and assessment. Opportunities relate to personalised learning according to the preferences of each student (Bahroun et al., 2023) and the ability to control iterations of an assessed topic to improve quality of their work (Akinwalere & Ivanov, 2022), with challenges related to concerns around accuracy of content, comprehensiveness of information, and potential replacement of the learning experi­",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,2,"2023) and the ability to control iterations of an assessed topic to improve quality of their work (Akinwalere & Ivanov, 2022), with challenges related to concerns around accuracy of content, comprehensiveness of information, and potential replacement of the learning experi­ ence for students (Akinwalere & Ivanov, 2022). Despite these polarising findings, it is apparent GenAI tools will only embed deeper into the learning experiences of students in HE and inevitably change the way Higher Education works. The current study aims to explore the perspectives and experiences of students using GenAI for their learning whilst studying in Higher Education institutions. 1.1 Students’ learning experiences in higher education A recent monumental shift in teaching, learning, and assessment practices in Higher Education came with the worldwide pandemic of COVID-19 in 2020. Whilst there has been wide discussion concerning the impact of COVID-19 on teaching practices and the shift to online learning (Anderson, 2020; Cena et al., 2024; Watermeyer et al., 2021). significantly less attention has been paid to the impact of the pandemic on stu­ dent’s learning experiences. Ultimately, this led to poor",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,2,"teaching practices and the shift to online learning (Anderson, 2020; Cena et al., 2024; Watermeyer et al., 2021). significantly less attention has been paid to the impact of the pandemic on stu­ dent’s learning experiences. Ultimately, this led to poor learning experiences for students as they navigated a unique set of challenges independently. On the other hand, recent research has also shown that online teaching, learning, and assessment in Higher Education promotes students’ motivation, satisfaction, outcomes, attitudes, skills (Neji et al., 2023) and plays a mediating role in academic self-efficacy (Acosta- Enriquez et al., 2025). In many ways, the rapid pivot to online learning during the COVID-19 pandemic paved the way for students and educators to embrace the current surge of Artificial Intelligence (AI) with its heightened experience of using adaptive systems to sup­ port learning processes, receiving online support, and seeking information in relevant search engines. Generative AI, however, has become much more advanced in the last few years and now focuses on mimicking human cognitive functions such as deci­ sion-making, problem-solving, and learning (Bellman, 1978) and is now",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,2,"and seeking information in relevant search engines. Generative AI, however, has become much more advanced in the last few years and now focuses on mimicking human cognitive functions such as deci­ sion-making, problem-solving, and learning (Bellman, 1978) and is now believed to have the potential to transform the education arena than any technology before it (Ifenthaler et al., 2024). Currently, GenAI is used to assist with making data-driven decisions based on student engagement data, for example, through Virtual Learning 1 3 3656",82
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 Environments. It has also been used to identify students at risk of dropping out or experiencing mental health issues using predictive analytics to track ‘at-risk’ behav­ iours and trigger alerts to educators/advisors (Herodotou et al., 2021). Similarly, GenAI has provided support with student experience with HE institutions, develop­ ing an automated digital assistant that supports students’ academic and emotional wellbeing. This GenAI tool allows users to navigate their timetable, contact members of staff, request important documents, engage with their students’ union (SU) and extracurricular activities, and much more (König et al., 2024). 1.2 GenAI and teaching, learning, and assessment practices A recent scoping review by Hay et al. (2024) concluded that overall, GenAI added three opportunities to students’ learning experiences— perceived unbiased feedback, diverse sources of information, and self-assessment of their writing. The main chal­ lenge faced was in relation to student academic integrity (Xia et al., 2024). In terms of these opportunities, there is a prevailing consensus that GenAI exhibits satisfactory competence in generating coherent texts and feedback to students through ChatGPT (Dai",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,3,"chal­ lenge faced was in relation to student academic integrity (Xia et al., 2024). In terms of these opportunities, there is a prevailing consensus that GenAI exhibits satisfactory competence in generating coherent texts and feedback to students through ChatGPT (Dai et al., 2023). HE organisations, such as Advance HE, have provided tentative steps to help the process of learning and assessment redesign in light of GenAI by: (1) understanding how specific assessments allow for AI collusion, (2) mapping stu­ dent learning against relevant parameters including context, format etc., and (3) rede­ signing learning by creating assessments that involve multidimensional tasks which encourage AI collaboration (Advance HE, 2023). Whilst these steps provide help­ ful suggestions, they also reinforce the need for teaching, learning, and assessment reform in GenAI, a timely task for any HE institution. The challenge highlights the concern that many students can simply bypass the learning process and submit AI- generated work. In the absence of any robust and reliable AI detection tools (Lodge et al., 2023), students can gain an unfair advantage in their assessments (Cotton et al.,",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,3,"many students can simply bypass the learning process and submit AI- generated work. In the absence of any robust and reliable AI detection tools (Lodge et al., 2023), students can gain an unfair advantage in their assessments (Cotton et al., 2024). Already, this is providing many issues in Higher Education institutions, which were left with the pertinent question: is a student colluding if they use GenAI at any point throughout the learning process (research, planning, writing)? This ulti­ mately led to the development of GenAI policies to provide a blueprint of acceptable and unacceptable use of GenAI. A relevant example is the GenAI policy guidelines co-designed by 24 Russell Group universities in the UK, which emphasises how universities should support students and staff to become AI-literate while attending to issues of privacy and plagiarism (Russell Group Committee, 2023). The ongoing debate of whether AI detection tools should be used in teaching, learning, and assess­ ment practices of HE remains at a lively stage. 1.3 Adoption of using GenAI in higher education Some important aspects of considering GenAI in HE relate",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,3,"debate of whether AI detection tools should be used in teaching, learning, and assess­ ment practices of HE remains at a lively stage. 1.3 Adoption of using GenAI in higher education Some important aspects of considering GenAI in HE relate to: (1) skills which educa­ tors have using the technology and (2) their ability to successfully integrate this into curricula. Rather than speculating whether GenAI will replace academic and faculty staff in HE, it is now widely accepted that understanding the advantages that GenAI offers 1 3 3657",88
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 educators is a more reasonable approach (Hrastinski et al., 2019). Prior to the recent growth, GenAI has long been used to allow educators to check the authenticity of written assignments submitted by students using AI-based systems (e.g. Turnitin). This has now progressed to educators using GenAI to provide feedback on assess­ ments, with a consensus that it improves marking turnaround times (Mumford & Atay, 2021) and ensures consistency with feedback to students (Ellis et al., 2022). Indeed, one study has also demonstrated students’ preference for assignment feed­ back that has been augmented by AI (Schultze-Gerlach et al., 2024). A recent qualita­ tive study by Mutanga et al. (2024) explored lecturers’ attitudes towards AI adoption into their learning activities and assessment and feedback. Researchers found largely mixed responses with some lecturers eager to embrace AI technologies while others are more resistant to their use in the absence of institutional/policy guidance and examples of best practice (Mutanga et al., 2024). This is corroborated in a study by Opesemowo et al. (2024), which investigated lecturers’ attitudes and",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,4,"technologies while others are more resistant to their use in the absence of institutional/policy guidance and examples of best practice (Mutanga et al., 2024). This is corroborated in a study by Opesemowo et al. (2024), which investigated lecturers’ attitudes and perceptions regarding the use of ChatGPT for instructional assessment. The study found that lecturers held low perceptions in terms of the viability of use and had poor percep­ tion levels when it came to exploring the potential for ChatGPT in their teaching and learning practices. Perhaps this is best conceptualised in terms of ‘cautious opti­ mism’ amongst educators regarding the use of GenAI in their pedagogical methods (Mutanga et al., 2024). 1.4 Students’ experiences of using GenAI Whilst literature on students’ experiences of implementing AI into their learning is small but growing, one study by Playfoot et al. (2024) examined the relationship between degree apathy and the willingness of students to use AI tools in their stud­ ies. Researchers found that students with higher levels of degree apathy were more inclined to misuse AI tools, highlighting a significant risk factor",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,4,"the relationship between degree apathy and the willingness of students to use AI tools in their stud­ ies. Researchers found that students with higher levels of degree apathy were more inclined to misuse AI tools, highlighting a significant risk factor for academic mis­ conduct. These findings further reinforce the appeal of GenAI tools such as ChatGPT to those students who find themselves disengaged with the programme and course content. However, AI tools can be incorporated into learning practices in a positive way if students educate themselves on the uses of GenAI and indeed the limitations. For example, students have reported reduced stress when interacting with GenAI chatbots whilst practicing new skills in healthcare delivery as this allows real-time user feedback and tailored information based on their input (Korngiebel & Mooney, 2021). Interestingly, it was also found that high-performing students were signifi­ cantly more likely to utilise AI tools compared to low-performing students (Sweeney, 2023). The main purpose is to acquire additional support which is consistent with the notion that high-performing students utilise a wide range of educational technologies while lower-achieving",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,4,"signifi­ cantly more likely to utilise AI tools compared to low-performing students (Sweeney, 2023). The main purpose is to acquire additional support which is consistent with the notion that high-performing students utilise a wide range of educational technologies while lower-achieving students are more likely to employ contract cheating services to complete assignments for them (Sweeney, 2023). Indeed, high-achieving students are more likely to recognise the benefits of using AI tools to aid their expertise when completing assignments and attempting to better understand course content. An important issue is whether students’ use of AI actually improves their over­ all academic performance. Currently, available studies demonstrate that the overall effect of using GenAI is unclear, and there is no consensus on mediating factors that 1 3 3658",125
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 influence this effect, for example, degree programme, assessment type, student abil­ ity etc. (Zhang et al., 2024). Some studies have found that students using ChatGPT excelled in their academic performance (Ji et al. 2023a, b) and that using ChatGPT enhanced the quality of essays and overall marks for college students (Bašić et al., 2023). However, a study by Bernabei et al. (2023) examined the use of AI in engi­ neering education while students were required to use ChatGPT for an essay assign­ ment. The researchers reported that the AI-generated submissions were of reasonably high quality, but the use of AI had limited effects on overall student performance. In a similar study by Smerdon (2024), researchers found no significant effect of AI use on research proposal marks among second-year undergraduate economics students at an Australian university. Thus, it is evident that the impact of GenAI on students’ aca­ demic achievements when completing assessments has not been widely established and lacks convincing evidence. 1.5 Student perceptions and acceptance of GenAI Another key consideration relevant to the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,5,"university. Thus, it is evident that the impact of GenAI on students’ aca­ demic achievements when completing assessments has not been widely established and lacks convincing evidence. 1.5 Student perceptions and acceptance of GenAI Another key consideration relevant to the current study is students’ perceptions of using GenAI in their learning journey in HE institutions. Student perceptions of their learning environment play a significant role in their overall engagement with course content, and more generally, in their academic and career outcomes. This is highlighted in Biggs’ 3P (Presage–Process–Product) model of teaching and learning (Biggs & Tang, 2011), which links positive perceptions of the learning environment (e.g., curriculum content, teaching methods, learning resources, assessment and feed­ back practices) to greater confidence and empowerment in students’ abilities to adopt deep learning approaches. When considered in the context of GenAI, students who perceive technological innovations positively are more likely to be willing to use these tools in their learn­ ing. A study by Dahmash et al. (2020) found that students with a good understanding of AI expressed lower levels of anxiety about its",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,5,"technological innovations positively are more likely to be willing to use these tools in their learn­ ing. A study by Dahmash et al. (2020) found that students with a good understanding of AI expressed lower levels of anxiety about its use. This highlights the importance of educating students on how to effectively use GenAI in their academic journey, enabling them to achieve more favourable outcomes. A more recent study by Chan and Hu (2023) in Hong Kong, found that students demonstrated positive attitudes toward using GenAI technologies in their learning, research, and future careers. Inter­ estingly, these positive perceptions were positively correlated with the frequency of GenAI use. The study also identified ongoing concerns students have when using GenAI, such as issues around accuracy, plagiarism, and ethics (also noted in Dah­ mash et al., 2020), which are now common themes in GenAI literature. In relation to perceptions, recent studies have also explored students’ acceptance of GenAI tools through the lens of the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM; Davis, 1985). This inclusion is particularly relevant, as the TAM framework suggests that students’",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,5,"in GenAI literature. In relation to perceptions, recent studies have also explored students’ acceptance of GenAI tools through the lens of the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM; Davis, 1985). This inclusion is particularly relevant, as the TAM framework suggests that students’ perceptions of a technology’s usefulness and ease of use are key deter­ minants of their intention to adopt it. A study by Almassaad et al. (2024) revealed that 78.7% of higher education students (from a sample of 859 in Saudi Arabia) fre­ quently used GenAI tools, primarily for defining and clarifying concepts, translation, generating writing ideas, and summarising academic literature. ChatGPT emerged as the most widely adopted tool, with 86.2% of students who use GenAI selecting 1 3 3659",119
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 it as their tool of choice. The widespread adoption of GenAI highlights its growing prominence and underscores the importance for educators in higher education to be knowledgeable about the capabilities of tools like ChatGPT (Almassaad et al., 2024). Understanding students’ willingness to use—and concerns about—GenAI can help educators better integrate these technologies into the learning process, ensuring they complement and enhance traditional teaching methods. The current study aims to explore the perspectives and experiences of final-year undergraduate students and MSc conversion students regarding the integration of GenAI. The research questions the current study aims to explore are: ● How do university students perceive the role of GenAI in their studies, and how do they incorporate it into their learning? ● How do students engage with generative AI in their learning, and what risks, needs, and benefits do they identify? 2 Method This study used inductive Reflexive Thematic Analysis situated within an interpre­ tivist paradigm which recognises researcher and participant subjectivity (Braun & Clarke, 2022). Our research adopted a critical realist ontology (Maxwell, 2012), using",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,6,"and benefits do they identify? 2 Method This study used inductive Reflexive Thematic Analysis situated within an interpre­ tivist paradigm which recognises researcher and participant subjectivity (Braun & Clarke, 2022). Our research adopted a critical realist ontology (Maxwell, 2012), using student led focus groups to capture the situated, interpreted realities of stu­ dents’ experiences and perceptions of using Gen AI. This approach facilitated sensi­ tivity to students’ personal, social, cultural and educational contexts, and how these contexts can shape their approach to and engagement with GenAI tools. 2.1 Participants Using an opportunity, snowball-sampling strategy, psychology students recruited 29 psychology student participants. The majority of the participants were MSc con­ version students (this is a UK-specific one-year MSc programme, which provides students from non-psychology backgrounds with a degree that is accredited by the British Psychological Society). Some of the participants were undergraduate students (see table below for participant backgrounds). Students were recruited from Queen’s University Belfast (QUB) and the University of Glasgow (UofG). Six focus groups (FGs) were conducted with a total of 13 participants (12 females and 1 male) from",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,6,"undergraduate students (see table below for participant backgrounds). Students were recruited from Queen’s University Belfast (QUB) and the University of Glasgow (UofG). Six focus groups (FGs) were conducted with a total of 13 participants (12 females and 1 male) from QUB and 16 participants from UofG. The sample was composed of local and inter­ national students. Participants were aged 18 or older and were enrolled as students at their respective institutions at the time of the study. Research ethics approval was granted by the Queen’s University Belfast Engineering and Physical Sciences Fac­ ulty Research Ethics Committee. 2.2 Data collection A semi-structured interview guide was developed through discussions in the research group. The team at QUB developed the initial interview guide, which was then fur­ 1 3 3660",127
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,7,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 ther refined by the team at UofG (e.g., making changes to the way some questions were worded). A final version was agreed upon between the two teams, which was the one used by the students. The interview guide included questions about how students used GenAI for their studies and what their perceptions were about the usefulness and limitations of these tools for their studies. The guide was piloted to test the questions and ensure smooth communication between the interviewees. The questions aimed to capture how students were using these tools for their studies and assignments, their views on GenAI’s role in learning and any concerns they had about using them (see interview guide in Appendix B). All focus groups were conducted online between May and July 2024 by students, with one acting as the moderator and the other as an assistant, who took field notes and asked clarifying questions at the end of the discussions. Each FGD took approxi­ mately one hour, with engaging conversations and a friendly atmosphere. These sessions used semi-structured interview",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,7,"moderator and the other as an assistant, who took field notes and asked clarifying questions at the end of the discussions. Each FGD took approxi­ mately one hour, with engaging conversations and a friendly atmosphere. These sessions used semi-structured interview questions to maintain consistency while allowing for fluid and natural conversation. In using this qualitative method, we aimed to create a comfortable setting for students to discuss their diverse perspectives candidly (Gill et al., 2008). Participants were given the option to select a pseudonym; those who did are referred to accordingly, while pseudonyms were assigned to those who did not. The use of GenAI in academic learning and assessment remains a controversial topic. To ensure that participants could openly express their views on the use of such tools, they were recruited and interviewed by two students. Given the nature of the topic, an insider perspective that would allow participants to feel safe and be candid about the use of GenAI tools was essential. Researcher students were trained by staff to facilitate the discussions and ensure that all participants took part",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,7,"the topic, an insider perspective that would allow participants to feel safe and be candid about the use of GenAI tools was essential. Researcher students were trained by staff to facilitate the discussions and ensure that all participants took part in the discus­ sions. Focus group discussions were digitally recorded and transcribed verbatim. 2.3 Analytical approach Data were analysed inductively by four researchers: one academic staff member (lec­ turer in HE) and three student researchers. The analysts (Authors 1, X, X and X) first familiarised themselves with the recorded data and conducted multiple readings of the transcripts. Each researcher independently coded the transcripts, followed by group discussions to compare interpretations and deepen the understanding of the data from both student and lecturer perspectives. To explore patterns of meaning across the dataset, we clustered together potentially connected codes until a number of working, provisional themes were developed by individual researchers. In devel­ oping themes, we focused on patterns of shared meaning that captured significance in relation to the research questions, prioritizing depth and interpretative insight. Recognising that lecturers and students may",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,7,"of working, provisional themes were developed by individual researchers. In devel­ oping themes, we focused on patterns of shared meaning that captured significance in relation to the research questions, prioritizing depth and interpretative insight. Recognising that lecturers and students may hold different views on the use of GenAI, this collaborative approach allowed for sensitivity to context by exploring alternative interpretations of the data (Yardley, 2008). Discussions between student and staff researchers helped challenge interpretations and surface different under­ standings, especially regarding power dynamics or insider/outsider perspectives. We focused on identifying contradictions in data and seeking insights into emerging complexities that can result in data that grows both in depth and breadth (Cena et 1 3 3661",116
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,8,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 al., 2024; Yardley, 2008). The process of theme development was documented, and after researchers shared their interpretations of data, initial themes were discussed, and any disagreements were resolved through further discussions to ensure that each theme aligned with the research questions. To ensure quality in research, we followed the criteria for good reflexive thematic analysis (Braun & Clarke, 2013). To enhance transparency, an example of staff and student researcher notes and discussions during the analytical process is provided in Appendix B. 2.4 Findings We identified three themes within the data that capture students’ perceptions and experiences of using GenAI at the time the data were collected. These themes high­ light the importance students place on using GenAI for their learning, with a focus on emphasising their endorsement of these tools, which are becoming increasingly popular among students, as well as the need to learn how to use them appropriately. The themes discussed below, as shown in Fig. 1, are: (1) GenAI in Student Life: A Necessary Innovation or a Shortcut? (2) GenAI as a",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,8,"popular among students, as well as the need to learn how to use them appropriately. The themes discussed below, as shown in Fig. 1, are: (1) GenAI in Student Life: A Necessary Innovation or a Shortcut? (2) GenAI as a ‘double-edged sword’; and (3) Ethical and Acceptable Use of AI. The first theme addresses the first research ques­ tion, whereas the second and third theme addresses the second research question. RQ1 How do university students perceive the role of GenAI in their studies, and how do they incorporate it into their learning? 1. GenAI in Student Life: A Necessary Innovation or a Shortcut? This theme explored how students used GenAI as a tool to support their learning at the time the data were collected. The data suggest the growing recognition among all students of the transformative potential of GAI tools in educational environments. Fig. 1 Thematic Map 1 3 3662",150
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,9,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 It highlights significant advantages of its use, particularly when students feel under pressure, as well as its ability to promote inclusive education. “It Just Helps You Keep Up” – GenAI as a Study Aid Under Academic Pressure One main reason that students use GenAI is the time pressure associated with their stud­ ies. They highlighted that their MSc programs are hectic and require them to perform well and quickly, which drives them towards using AI tools that facilitate fast learn­ ing and help them keep up with the reading load of an accelerated program. I am so stressed that it’s really difficult to be thinking critically in some situa­ tions and I feel the pressure which I’ve talked to other students about as well to use tools like this again to outsource some of that just in order to keep up with the quota of work that you have to be producing.—Cory [UoG, FG2]. According to them, GenAI tools ‘save time’ and help explain complex topics in lay­ man’s terms, making them more accessible",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,9,"that just in order to keep up with the quota of work that you have to be producing.—Cory [UoG, FG2]. According to them, GenAI tools ‘save time’ and help explain complex topics in lay­ man’s terms, making them more accessible in a short amount of time. In most cases, these tools are used as study aids to practice learning and provide clarification when students don’t fully understand something from lectures or reading, especially in sub­ jects with constantly evolving information or ‘dense’ content that is difficult to get through. They also appear to be widely used for revision by summarising readings, creating prompts to assess learning, and helping students understand difficult con­ cepts. For students who learn R at this level of study, GenAI is used to troubleshoot errors with R. In this context, such tools were found helpful for finding potential solutions, especially when students struggled to understand why something didn’t work rather than why it did. Concerning the use of GenAI for assessments, several students reported finding it helpful to avoid the ‘blank page’ challenge, referring to the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,9,"finding potential solutions, especially when students struggled to understand why something didn’t work rather than why it did. Concerning the use of GenAI for assessments, several students reported finding it helpful to avoid the ‘blank page’ challenge, referring to the overwhelming feeling of starting work from scratch. As two of the students described: I feel like one of the hardest things about writing frequently is looking at a blank page and being like overwhelmed by the quantity that you need to write. And then, almost like the writing process in a lot of ways is the process of editing and revising. – Aisling [QUB, FG5]. I would sort of input a bunch of information into ChatGPT or whatever, and then get like a really bad first draft, but then be able to work with that as a starting point instead of a blank page and almost use it as an outline. - Clodagh [QUB, FG 6]. As indicated above, students value the draft they get from these tools as a scaffold or outline, a way to get past the initial",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,9,"of a blank page and almost use it as an outline. - Clodagh [QUB, FG 6]. As indicated above, students value the draft they get from these tools as a scaffold or outline, a way to get past the initial hurdle and start shaping their ideas. From all the participants, there was no indication of using AI output as final products, but as a product to lower the barrier of writing. A Tool for Equity and Inequity Findings highlighted the complexities surround­ ing GenAI as both a tool for equity and a potential driver for inequity in learning. 1 3 3663",101
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,10,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 According to students, GenAI provides a lot of support for neurodivergent and ESL learners. Students with learning difficulties (dyslexia or ADHD) benefit from GenAI in getting explanations in simpler language, making content clearer and more man­ ageable and editing work. I mean in my own experience and a few other people I’ve talked to at the uni as well, but with ADHD, it’s, it’s been very like transformative for a lot of people, particularly in an educational context, but also in like a workplace context or even just their daily lives. - Cory [UoG, FG3]. Whereas, for international students who are non-native English speakers, data sug­ gest that GenAI functioned as a linguistic support tool, helping refine grammar and expression in ways that may reduce language-related anxiety. For international students, I think one thing that’s really important- uh one thing that’s really useful um, about recent AI is about the language about language refinement [group nods], grammar and things like that. – Lulu [UoG, FG2.This is an important aspect in terms of how GenAI",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,10,"that’s really important- uh one thing that’s really useful um, about recent AI is about the language about language refinement [group nods], grammar and things like that. – Lulu [UoG, FG2.This is an important aspect in terms of how GenAI is viewed as a tool that reduces structural language disadvantage by helping students express what they know in a more effective way. It is also perceived as a useful tool for bridging educational gaps so that students from underprivileged backgrounds can use GenAI to supplement their learning. An example mentioned was the advantage that some students have access to resources, such as parents who can assist with assignments, while others lack this crucial sup­ port. Therefore, GenAI is perceived to help level the playing field by providing equi­ table access to resources and information, especially for those without similar help at home. However, some students highlighted that there is a disparity in who can use the advanced tools and who can’t, indicating that the cost of advanced AI tools may disadvantage students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds, emphasising the need for",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,10,"at home. However, some students highlighted that there is a disparity in who can use the advanced tools and who can’t, indicating that the cost of advanced AI tools may disadvantage students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds, emphasising the need for universities to ensure fair access to these tools. You can’t have some people having access to ChatGPT, like the paid version, Grammarly, plus this, that the other, and then other people just having access to ChatGPT that only does 2021. Do you know what I mean? Like that’s not the same, that’s not equal. So, I guess my point would be, if they are going to endorse something, it needs to be equitable — Maisy [UoG, FG1]. Another important point was the growing concern over those who over-rely on using those tools to write their assessments. As some students are reported to be using GenAI more heavily than others, participants believe that this gives certain students an unfair advantage over those who put in the effort themselves. “Imagine you’re trying so hard in class and you’re, like, killing yourself for",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,10,"reported to be using GenAI more heavily than others, participants believe that this gives certain students an unfair advantage over those who put in the effort themselves. “Imagine you’re trying so hard in class and you’re, like, killing yourself for good marks. And then somebody shows up and they’re like, this is an essay that a computer wrote.” – Anna [QUB, FG,5]. The frustration expressed by Anna, who feels that students who work hard for their grades are at a disadvantage, underscores the friction between techno­ logical advancements and traditional academic expectations. 1 3 3664",95
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 RQ2 How do students engage with generative AI in their learning, and what risks, needs, and benefits do they identify? 2. GenAI as a ‘double-edged sword’. The second theme highlights the significant challenges associated with the use of GenAI in education, particularly issues related to the accuracy of information, depen­ dency and critical thinking. This theme is divided into two sub-themes: (1) ‘Accuracy and Responsible Use in Education’ and (2) ‘GenAI as a Diminishing Learning Tool’. Accuracy and Responsible Use in Learning Questions were raised about the accuracy and reliability of AI-generated content, especially for complex analysis. A common concern was that the tool generates inaccurate information on the topic of inquiry and may produce incorrect content. Specifically, there is a strong concern that AI can generate details that sound convincing but are not factual. In most cases, they empha­ sised ‘needing to go back and check requires extra time’, reflecting that while fast, GenAI is not viewed as reliable, emphasising the need to verify its answers, which can be time-consuming. For this reason,",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,11,"factual. In most cases, they empha­ sised ‘needing to go back and check requires extra time’, reflecting that while fast, GenAI is not viewed as reliable, emphasising the need to verify its answers, which can be time-consuming. For this reason, knowing what they were doing in advance was essential for using it effectively, and all students remarked that GenAI output should not be used as a sole source of information. According to them, a ‘baseline understanding’ is crucial for getting good results. Sometimes it gives you things that are not true or it combines two different pieces of information, and you can notice it if you already have that baseline of understanding (…), and if you don’t have that kind of understanding and use the information for coursework, that could ruin your entire assignment - Arthur [UoG, FG 1]. If it’s helping you, if it’s facilitating the learning process and it enriches what they’ve already learned in class, then it’s just like any other learning tool. But if it takes over and does the work for you, then it might",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,11,"it’s helping you, if it’s facilitating the learning process and it enriches what they’ve already learned in class, then it’s just like any other learning tool. But if it takes over and does the work for you, then it might become a problem - G [UoG, FG1]. As the above suggests, using these tools to build on prior knowledge is essential, rather than relying on them to complete the work. For them, it is acceptable to use AI as long as they are not relying on it to write the entire assignment or using answers provided by AI without modification. GenAI as a diminishing learning tool An important point made was the concern that using GenAI consistently can have long-term implications by diminishing learn­ ing. They suggested that over-relying on GenAI can lead to ‘cognitive dependency’, where students increasingly outsource problem-solving to AI as a shortcut, ultimately compromising the development of essential long-term learning skills. For example, many students expressed concerns about over-reliance on GAI, which they felt could erode their critical thinking and writing abilities, ultimately leading to a",180
