DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,12,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 provide quick, ready-made answers made it tempting to bypass the original thought of engaging in the full learning process, ultimately disrupting the creative process. The pressure to complete work quickly and efficiently leaves them little time and tempts them to use these tools, which can lead to relying too heavily on GenAI rather than focusing on developing these learning skills. There’s so much pressure to get things done well and quickly that it sometimes feels like there isn’t enough time to develop the important skills we should be learning for our degree. ChatGPT makes it even easier to bypass that learning process, which can be a real concern. – Elaine [UoG, FG 1]. Some argue that using AI for tasks like writing or generating ideas can stifle creativ­ ity, as students may not come up with their ideas or learn to think outside the box. For example, one student shared their experience of using AI to generate cover letters for job applications, which led to them not retaining important details or understand­ ing the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,12,"up with their ideas or learn to think outside the box. For example, one student shared their experience of using AI to generate cover letters for job applications, which led to them not retaining important details or understand­ ing the job requirements fully. They acknowledge that while AI can assist, it doesn’t necessarily promote deeper learning or retention, as they would need to rely on AI repeatedly rather than internalising the knowledge themselves. I sometimes use AI to generate an outline for my assignments by providing bullet points, which I find helpful. However, I don’t find ChatGPT’s output very useful overall—it’s often vague, overly wordy, and lacks specificity. It doesn’t replace the critical thinking needed for an assignment. - Iona [UoG, FG2]. Relying on GenAI output to complete assignments, without developing their writing and research skills, is seen as problematic: “If AI is doing the thinking for you, you are not learning”, Sonia (UoG, FG3). Therefore, they indicated that GenAI should be used with a lot of caution for assessments and that it is more helpful for studying than for",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,12,"“If AI is doing the thinking for you, you are not learning”, Sonia (UoG, FG3). Therefore, they indicated that GenAI should be used with a lot of caution for assessments and that it is more helpful for studying than for assessments. Conversely, other students argue that GenAI tools do not nega­ tively impact their critical thinking skills. They believe that due to GenAI’s inher­ ent inaccuracies, students still need a fundamental understanding before using these tools effectively and that GenAI should be used as a supplementary tool rather than a replacement for deep learning and critical analysis: “If you don’t understand the topic and you’re just using that only like it’s going to give you faulty information and it’s not going to be good, But if you like to blend what you know with it I find that’s amazing” Anna – [QUB, FG5]. 3. Ethical and Acceptable Use of AI. Findings highlighted the tension students experience between the need to upgrade their skills and learn to use GenAI and the dilemma of how to use it properly. One of the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,12,"– [QUB, FG5]. 3. Ethical and Acceptable Use of AI. Findings highlighted the tension students experience between the need to upgrade their skills and learn to use GenAI and the dilemma of how to use it properly. One of the main concerns remains the lack of clarity around how to use it for assessments, particularly due to fears of plagiarism. They worry that they may unknowingly use GenAI in ways that could be considered cheating or academic dishonesty. The con­ fusion stems from a lack of clarity about how others are using these tools and the 1 3 3666",99
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,13,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 absence of clear boundaries between acceptable and excessive use, leaving students uncertain about what constitutes plagiarism when using such technologies. You don’t know how much other people are using this technology. You don’t know what is, like, an appropriate amount. You don’t know what plagiarism is, and what is not. That’s what like Cory was saying is like, where’s the line? It’s a personal judgement. - Iona [UoG, FG 2]. Academic integrity issues are especially prominent in group work, where students receive the same mark for group coursework. This stress is amplified when some students submit work believed to be written by GenAI tools. Beyond the perceived unfairness faced by students who put genuine effort into their coursework, the use of AI in group assessments is described as particularly disheartening, as the inclusion of AI-generated work may affect the overall group grade and undermine the contri­ butions of those who completed their work authentically. Concerning assessments, another concern was students’ perceptions of lecturers using GenAI for assessment and providing feedback. They express a clear",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,13,"AI-generated work may affect the overall group grade and undermine the contri­ butions of those who completed their work authentically. Concerning assessments, another concern was students’ perceptions of lecturers using GenAI for assessment and providing feedback. They express a clear preference for their work to be marked by lecturers and feedback to reflect genuine engagement with their work, not some­ thing GenAI generated. They associate human feedback with care, recognition of effort and emotional reciprocity. Statements such as “…I would hope that they’re, you know, reading the material and giving you like specific feedback… - Ramina [QUB, FG4] ” or “ I’ve done all this work… You should be giving me your personal time now… Aoife [QUB, FG5]” indicates that if staff use GenAI to reduce their own work with marking, they perceive this as an unfair thing to the efforts they put into their coursework. Students suggested incorporating AI-related skills into the curriculum to prepare them for future job markets. According to them, there is a ‘defensive criticism’ of GenAI within academia, reflecting resistance towards technological advancements and concerns",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,13,"efforts they put into their coursework. Students suggested incorporating AI-related skills into the curriculum to prepare them for future job markets. According to them, there is a ‘defensive criticism’ of GenAI within academia, reflecting resistance towards technological advancements and concerns about their impact on learning and future employability opportunities. The resistance of people to learning about AI as they fear that AI will replace us in jobs. However, they think we are very far behind where we are frightened that it is. We shouldn’t miss the opportunity of something that will become ubiq­ uitous. – Caoimhe [QUB, FG 4]. It’s an amazing part of technology that you know has been taking time to develop, and I think for us to just say, oh, it’s all bad, would be naive and it’s not for us to say. – Ayne [QUB, FG 5]. GenAI is already prevalent and unavoidable, and thus, they emphasised that it is the responsibility of the HE institutions to teach students how to engage with these ‘new sources of learning’. As a result, the university’s stance on GenAI",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,13,"5]. GenAI is already prevalent and unavoidable, and thus, they emphasised that it is the responsibility of the HE institutions to teach students how to engage with these ‘new sources of learning’. As a result, the university’s stance on GenAI and guidance on its appropriate use are seen as crucial. Students argue that the fear among academic staff should not discourage using those tools, and that academic dishonesty has always existed and that GenAI is simply another potential tool for misuse, rather than the 1 3 3667",87
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,14,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 cause “Dishonest working like this… (generative AI) hasn’t created that. It’s always been there. It’s just maybe a different opportunity to do it.” Anna [QUB, FG6]. Students emphasised that the mindset around GenAI needs to evolve. Since uni­ versities are meant to prepare students for the world beyond, and AI is set to play a significant role in the future workforce, they are eager to learn more about it and develop the skills necessary to use it effectively. It’s a fact that it’s just growing, and it is going to take over the majority of the industries around us. And I think the only right way is to know how to use it and like to maximise the kind of efficiency. So yes, it would be helpful to kind of train people on how they could use it and you know, get the most out of it. - Farah [QUB, FG6) Participants highlighted that they didn’t receive any formal training on using AI tech­ nology, and the fear of using it inappropriately stems from",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,14,"they could use it and you know, get the most out of it. - Farah [QUB, FG6) Participants highlighted that they didn’t receive any formal training on using AI tech­ nology, and the fear of using it inappropriately stems from the fact that different disciplines use AI for different purposes: “I don’t think anyone has had any real training on it, but I think there’s definitely a better way. You sort of work out ways to do things that are more efficient, but not having guidance is a concern.” — Arthur [UoG, FG2]. According to them, universities need to set boundaries on the use of AI and also instruct on how to use it effectively. Lecturers, as educators, are perceived as crucial in the process of setting parameters for acceptable AI use. They asserted that if lectur­ ers are using GenAI, they should discuss how they are using it and be more willing to create clearer guidelines on how students are expected to use it. For example, stu­ dents believed it was widely understood that GenAI should not be used",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,14,"GenAI, they should discuss how they are using it and be more willing to create clearer guidelines on how students are expected to use it. For example, stu­ dents believed it was widely understood that GenAI should not be used to complete entire assignments, yet the lack of clarity around the extent to which GenAI is per­ mitted is a source of frustration for them. Given the variety of GenAI tools, students believe universities should explicitly specify which types of GenAI are appropriate for different tasks and adopt a progressive approach to GenAI by developing poli­ cies that promote the ethical use of these tools. To support this, students suggested several forms of guidance, including introductory sessions at the start of their stud­ ies, releasing videos or short clips, blog posts, training sessions, detailed guides, and establishing an AI support hub with regular updates. They held the belief that such guidelines would benefit not only themselves but also academic staff, who need to assess whether the work is generated by students or GenAI. “If SLd could release even just little",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,14,"hub with regular updates. They held the belief that such guidelines would benefit not only themselves but also academic staff, who need to assess whether the work is generated by students or GenAI. “If SLd could release even just little clips, you know that you could erm or like a little kind of blog post or something, you know, every so often, that’d be really useful as well as, you know, the more standardised guidance - Heather [UoG, FG2]. 3 Discussion and conclusion The aim of this study was to understand how students use GenAI technologies in higher education and the perceptions of the benefits and challenges of using such tools to enhance their learning. Our study provides important insights into a highly 1 3 3668",126
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 relevant yet underexplored area at the intersection of higher education and technol­ ogy. By examining student perceptions of the benefits and challenges of using GenAI in their studies, we gain valuable insights into how these tools are influencing learn­ ing and HE. Our findings highlight the opportunities and challenges GenAI presents for students. Students recognise the growing role of GenAI in education and future careers. They are familiar with and use the tools to aid with their studies and assessments, particularly in demanding programs at the postgraduate level. AI tools provide a con­ venient solution to this, considering academic pressure and time constraints. Some of the ways in which they use GenAI are to summarise readings, clarify difficult concepts and troubleshooting errors in subjects like R programming. This is in sup­ port of previous literature suggesting that familiarity with using GenAI tools and the ease of completing tasks and solving problems make such tools convenient and easy to use (Yang & Zhang, 2024). This can also enhance their academic performance in the short term",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,15,"literature suggesting that familiarity with using GenAI tools and the ease of completing tasks and solving problems make such tools convenient and easy to use (Yang & Zhang, 2024). This can also enhance their academic performance in the short term (Rahman & Watanobe, 2023). In the context of writing assessments, GenAI appears to help students overcome the ‘blank page’ problem by reducing cog­ nitive load through generating an initial draft and lowering anxiety by providing a starting point. One of the most significant advantages of GenAI is its ability to promote inclusive education by providing substantial support for neurodiverse learners, ESA learners, and non-English speaking students. These findings support previous research sug­ gesting that such innovative systems are capable of tailoring educational content and methods to enable personalised learning and delivery (Chen et al., 2020; Roshanaei et al., 2023). From an equity perspective, our findings suggest that GenAI functioned as tools that helped mitigate structural disadvantages and reduce students’ anxiety. Nevertheless, GenAI may also exacerbate inequities, as the cost of advanced AI tools could disadvantage students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,15,"an equity perspective, our findings suggest that GenAI functioned as tools that helped mitigate structural disadvantages and reduce students’ anxiety. Nevertheless, GenAI may also exacerbate inequities, as the cost of advanced AI tools could disadvantage students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds or cul­ tural backgrounds, highlighting its potential to amplify existing inequalities. This reflects concerns raised by other research suggesting that GenAI’s statistical bias toward dominant knowledge systems and worldviews reinforces existing inequalities (Bender et al., 2021), further marginalising those whose voices are already underrep­ resented. These findings emphasise the need for universities to ensure fair access to these tools. Injustice may also arise when some students use GenAI more extensively than others, creating an uneven playing field where those who rely heavily on AI can achieve higher marks with less effort. This raises concerns about equity and academic integrity, as students fear that such overreliance could devalue genuine learning and hard work. Our findings resonate with other research suggesting that the use of AI tools can lead to grade inflation for some, while others continue to invest legitimate effort in",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,15,"students fear that such overreliance could devalue genuine learning and hard work. Our findings resonate with other research suggesting that the use of AI tools can lead to grade inflation for some, while others continue to invest legitimate effort in their studies (Foster, 2024). Findings also indicate that student motivation and learning may be negatively impacted when hardworking students feel disheart­ ened and perceive a sense of injustice, as the distinction between genuine effort and AI-generated work becomes blurred. These findings align with recent work suggest­ ing that such disparities can demotivate students who work hard, and that poses a threat to the overall academic integrity (Davidson, 2023). 1 3 3669",111
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 Findings revealed several concerns surrounding the use of GenAI in academic settings. A primary issue is its potential to hinder authentic learning, critical thinking, and creativity. Many students reported a lack of clarity about how to appropriately use GenAI, particularly in relation to assignments. While some saw value in using the tool to support their understanding of content, there was widespread caution about over-reliance. This hesitation is grounded in concerns about the lack of accuracy in information, the risk of distorting facts, details that sound convincing but are not factual, and issues that align with research suggesting that GenAI can introduce bias and reduce the accuracy of information (Yang & Zhang, 2024). Findings indicated that without foundational knowledge gained through recommended readings, stu­ dents may struggle to evaluate AI-generated content effectively, finding it confusing or misleading. The ease of access to GenAI tools, combined with academic pressure, may tempt students to depend on them excessively. This reliance can disrupt the development of essential academic skills such as critical thinking, problem-solving, and the creative application",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,16,"or misleading. The ease of access to GenAI tools, combined with academic pressure, may tempt students to depend on them excessively. This reliance can disrupt the development of essential academic skills such as critical thinking, problem-solving, and the creative application of knowledge. As recent literature notes, GenAI can provide quick and effortless content that often bypasses meaningful engagement with learning materi­ als (Kostanek & Li, 2025; Thorp, 2023). Consequently, students may develop a false sense of mastery, mistaking tool proficiency for genuine understanding (Brown & Smith, 2024). To address these risks, students must be made aware of the long-term consequences of bypassing traditional learning processes and promoting a balanced approach where GenAI is used as a support tool rather than a substitute can help mitigate these issues. Further, educators can employ familiar assessment pedagogical strategies that develop student evaluative judgment, but adapted to GenAI. According to Bearman et al. (2024), this can be done in three ways: by allowing the learner to assess GenAI outputs; the learner developing their own GenAI processes, by assess­ ing the quality of the prompts",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,16,"but adapted to GenAI. According to Bearman et al. (2024), this can be done in three ways: by allowing the learner to assess GenAI outputs; the learner developing their own GenAI processes, by assess­ ing the quality of the prompts used to interrogate ChatGPT; and finally, employing GenAI to assess their own evaluative judgment. Students are increasingly aware of both the opportunities and risks that GenAI poses to their academic development and future employability. While many recog­ nise its potential as an integral part of modern life, concerns remain about its ethical use, particularly in relation to plagiarism and academic integrity. A strong appetite exists among students to learn how to use GenAI responsibly, yet many expressed uncertainty about what constitutes acceptable versus excessive use. This lack of clarity generates anxiety and confusion, especially in disciplines like psychology, where original thinking and deep theoretical engagement are essential. Findings challenge previous research suggesting students do not view GenAI use as unethi­ cal (Consuegra-Fernández et al., 2024). In contrast, students in the current study expressed ethical concerns but struggled to determine the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,16,"thinking and deep theoretical engagement are essential. Findings challenge previous research suggesting students do not view GenAI use as unethi­ cal (Consuegra-Fernández et al., 2024). In contrast, students in the current study expressed ethical concerns but struggled to determine the boundaries of appropri­ ate use. These challenges are amplified in collaborative settings, such as group work, where reliance on GenAI by one member may affect the fairness of shared assessments. The fear of plagiarism and the erosion of originality in student work were consistent themes across the dataset. These concerns align with broader lit­ erature highlighting the risks of over-reliance on language models, which lack criti­ 1 3 3670",109
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 cal thinking, subject-specific understanding, and the capacity to generate original academic insights (Liu et al., 2023; Weidmann, 2024). Without structured policies and targeted training, students risk becoming overly dependent on GenAI tools, potentially compromising their intellectual development. To mitigate these risks, students clearly advocated for the development of standardised university guide­ lines, including discipline-specific examples and accessible training on ethical and effective use. 3.1 Limitations This study has some potential limitations. First, the data were collected in mid-2024, and given that the GenAI landscape continues to evolve rapidly, student perceptions of these tools may change over time. Second, most of the data were collected from postgraduate students and only a few undergraduate students, which limits the abil­ ity to understand the perceptions of undergraduate students in the use of AI tools. Third, the sample in this study is gender imbalanced, with more females than males. However, this reflects the wider demographics of psychology students, with women comprising approximately three-quarters of undergraduate and postgraduate cohorts (Odic & Wojsik, 2020). Fourth, universities have adopted GenAI policies",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,17,"in this study is gender imbalanced, with more females than males. However, this reflects the wider demographics of psychology students, with women comprising approximately three-quarters of undergraduate and postgraduate cohorts (Odic & Wojsik, 2020). Fourth, universities have adopted GenAI policies to varying degrees, providing different levels of guidance - for example, some prohibit its use in all assessments, while others allow it if acknowledged (for a non-exhaustive list of UK policies, see Barker, 2025). Based on the findings, this variation can contribute directly to uncertainty and anxiety about what constitutes acceptable use, particularly in assessment and group work contexts. Rather than reflecting individual misunder­ standing, these experiences were shaped by navigating inconsistent or ambiguous institutional expectations. As a result, students were left to rely on personal judgment, which heightened fears of inadvertent plagiarism and ethical misconduct. Therefore, the conclusions of this study should be interpreted with caution. While insight was provided into the uses and challenges of what students perceive regarding AI technol­ ogy, the impact of whether a cause-effect relationship exists between student usage of AI and academic performance",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,17,"this study should be interpreted with caution. While insight was provided into the uses and challenges of what students perceive regarding AI technol­ ogy, the impact of whether a cause-effect relationship exists between student usage of AI and academic performance can only be drawn out through experimental designs. 3.2 Implications These findings have significant implications for academic institutions. As AI tech­ nology advances, educational institutions must also adapt their strategies to protect the integrity of academic achievements and uphold the high standards that form the foundation of higher education. The use of GenAI presents challenges for students and educators, particularly in terms of its potential impact on student learning skills. Students may not always be aware of how their reliance on such tools can affect their development of these skills, which could have consequences for their future. Addi­ tionally, the use of technology to write assignments creates challenges in identifying how and where these tools are used, especially when students do not explicitly or honestly disclose their use, and when no detection tools are available to identify such 1 3",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,17,"use of technology to write assignments creates challenges in identifying how and where these tools are used, especially when students do not explicitly or honestly disclose their use, and when no detection tools are available to identify such 1 3 3671",41
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 instances (Kostanek & Li, 2025). This uncertainty makes it difficult to evaluate per­ formance, track progress, and provide meaningful feedback, ultimately complicating the assessment process and the support of student learning. HE institutions should consider adopting the use of GenAI in a more systematic way that is useful for students for their studies, as well as preparing them for future employment. This involves educating students on how to effectively use GenAI in their academic journey, including the benefits and limitations of these tools, as well as what constitutes acceptable and unacceptable use. Since the time data were col­ lected, various HE institutions have worked hard to develop student guides on how to use GenAI and understand its potential in an ethical way. There should be infor­ mation on the importance of lifelong learning skills, which are important for future employability, and cannot be replaced or learned otherwise. Such information should be formalised with clear guidelines to help students understand the boundaries of such tools and use them responsibly. Emphasis should be given to the",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,18,"which are important for future employability, and cannot be replaced or learned otherwise. Such information should be formalised with clear guidelines to help students understand the boundaries of such tools and use them responsibly. Emphasis should be given to the long-term effects that engaging in the misuse of AI tools can have on essential learning skills gained at the Higher education level. Enhancing early AI literacy across all programmes allows students to learn about the usefulness and limitations of tools as well as responsible use that does not com­ promise student learning and academic integrity (Kostanek & Li, 2025). One way to achieve this would be designing assessments that preserve the development of essen­ tial cognitive skills and reflect authentic learning rather than work generated from AI tools. An important consideration is to design assessments that preserve essen­ tial traditional learning skills, that focus on higher-order thinking skills. This means developing assessments that require students to brainstorm ideas using GenAI but do not write the assignment for them (e.g., assessments that require students to critically evaluate GenAI output (Bearman et",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,18,"learning skills, that focus on higher-order thinking skills. This means developing assessments that require students to brainstorm ideas using GenAI but do not write the assignment for them (e.g., assessments that require students to critically evaluate GenAI output (Bearman et al., 2024). Development of assessments and best practices collaboratively with students can be useful in understanding the types of assignments and pedagogical approaches that ensure the ethical use of AI. Finally, the study reveals a complex landscape where the potential benefits of GenAI in education are counterbalanced by significant concerns from students. It is clear that GenAI tools could enhance learning, promote inclusivity, and reduce student workload. However, these advantages must be weighed against the risks of dependency, academic dishonesty, and the decline of learning skills. These findings highlight that the swift evolution of AI technologies has found universities unpre­ pared and uncertain about their acceptability and the potential AI has in supporting student learning and adapting to a changing employment landscape because of such technologies. High exposure to AI integration in universities is unavoidable. AI is already widely used",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,18,"pared and uncertain about their acceptability and the potential AI has in supporting student learning and adapting to a changing employment landscape because of such technologies. High exposure to AI integration in universities is unavoidable. AI is already widely used by students. Therefore, HE institutions need to adopt a balanced approach towards integrating AI technologies into higher education. Our evidence underscores the ongoing and future transformation of academia in response to these technological changes. 1 3 3672",77
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,19,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 Appendix A Appendix B Interview Guide for Focus Groups with Students Hello and thank you for agreeing to participate in this interview. The purpose of this study is to understand your views in the use of Generative AI in higher education in Psychology. Your insights will provide valuable information about the impact of AI on your academic journey. Please note that your responses will remain confiden­ tial and will only be used for research purposes. Please avoid disclosing identifiable information about students/staff members. The researchers would like to remind all participants to respect the privacy of fellow participants and not repeat what is said in the focus group to others. Equally, it is important that the information discussed dur­ ing this focus group is not discussed outside of the room. We also request that during the interview participants respect each other’s opinions and to have care in the use of language. Using discriminatory or derogatory language will result in exclusion. ● Introductions (participants – their names). ● Check that it is okay to record",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,19,during the interview participants respect each other’s opinions and to have care in the use of language. Using discriminatory or derogatory language will result in exclusion. ● Introductions (participants – their names). ● Check that it is okay to record the session. ● Ask if they have any questions. Table 1 Participant Information 1 3 3673,56
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,20,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3655–3681 As stated previously, this project aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of your views in the use of Generative AI or ChatGPT in higher education in Psychol­ ogy and how it can be used. Do you think students should use AI for their studying or assignments? 1. Have you ever used AI tools or applications to support your learning in psychol­ ogy courses during your time at university? a If yes, could you tell us why you have used them? b If not, why have you not used them? 2. What have you used it for (e.g. doing assignments, making summaries of the lecture?) and did you find it helpful for your learning for these things? a If you have found it helpful, what things has it been more helpful for and why? b If you haven’t found that it’s helpful, why do you think not? 3. What are the main concerns you have about using AI in your studies or assignments? 4. From your perspective or experience, how can you use AI in",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,20,"If you haven’t found that it’s helpful, why do you think not? 3. What are the main concerns you have about using AI in your studies or assignments? 4. From your perspective or experience, how can you use AI in a safe way to sup­ port your learning? 5. Do you think lecturers should integrate AI in their teaching and assessment? If yes, in what way? If not, why do you think it should not be integrated? 6. How do you think university teaching staff could help students understand the appropriate and safe ways of using AI? a For example, would you find guides helpful or should there be training ses­ sions to guide students through the steps of does and don’ts in using AI? b Here, you could also discuss any examples of guidance to AI that you’ve seen or been given and how/why you found them helpful? 7. Ask if they would like to add anything else to the discussion. Appendix C Additional information about the analysis process Student and staff researchers made notes on the transcripts independently,",180
DOC_045,s10639-026-13923-3.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13923-3,20,"or been given and how/why you found them helpful? 7. Ask if they would like to add anything else to the discussion. Appendix C Additional information about the analysis process Student and staff researchers made notes on the transcripts independently, then discussed their analytic observations and insights, noting any contradictions. This informed development of themes. 1 3 3674",58
