DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w Abstract This study examines the pedagogical impact of artificial intelligence (AI) tools on student engagement and academic performance in two undergraduate economics and business modules at a UK university. Using a mixed-methods design, the study compares three academic periods: the pre-AI era (2022/23, prior to the widespread adoption of generative AI tools such as ChatGPT), the AI era (2023/24, character­ ized by unguided AI use), and the AI intervention period (2024/25, involving struc­ tured pedagogical guidance). Quantitative data from institutional learning platforms, complemented by qualitative survey data, inform a two-stage mediation analysis as­ sessing engagement as a mediator between AI use and academic outcomes. Results indicate that unguided AI use does not consistently improve performance and may negatively affect problem-solving in summative assessments. In contrast, structured AI interventions significantly increase independent study engagement, enhance crit­ ical thinking, communication skills, and problem-solving, and improve overall per­ formance. Engagement emerges as a robust mediator, highlighting the importance of pedagogical framing in effective AI integration. The findings emphasize the need for instructional scaffolding and provide practical",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,1,"enhance crit­ ical thinking, communication skills, and problem-solving, and improve overall per­ formance. Engagement emerges as a robust mediator, highlighting the importance of pedagogical framing in effective AI integration. The findings emphasize the need for instructional scaffolding and provide practical guidance for institutions seeking to promote responsible AI use, active learning, and deeper cognitive engagement in AI-enhanced educational environments. Keywords Artificial Intelligence (AI) · Student-centred active learning (SAL) · Engagement · Mediation analysis and academic performance JEL Classification I23 · I21 · O33 · D83 Received: 2 May 2025 / Accepted: 23 February 2026 / Published online: 18 March 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Leveraging artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance student engagement and academic performance in higher education Abubakar Hassan1 Abubakar Hassan ahassan001@dundee.ac.uk 1 Economics Studies, School of Business, University of Dundee, Dundee DD1 4HN, UK 1 3",138
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,2,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 1 Introduction The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into higher education has generated increasing interest due to its potential to transform teaching and learning practices across disciplines. Advances in AI technologies have expanded opportunities for personalised learning, adaptive feedback, and student-centred active learning (SAL) approaches (Almasri, 2024; Crompton & Burke, 2023; Zouhaier, 2023). Within SAL frameworks, learners engage more actively in critical inquiry, collaboration, and problem-solving, while instructors increasingly act as facilitators rather than sole transmitters of knowledge (Chassignol et al., 2018). Generative AI tools such as ChatGPT, Claude, Copilot, Gemini, Consensus and other recently developed AI tools have accelerated this transformation by supporting learning design, assessment innovation, and personalised engagement, particularly in applied fields such as eco­ nomics and business education (Aoun, 2017; Koltovskaia, 2020; Yue et al., 2022; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). These technologies can assist students in identifying conceptual gaps, stimulat­ ing analytical reasoning, and supporting independent learning trajectories (Ng et al., 2025. Their capabilities to simulate real-world scenarios and support large-scale data interpretation creates new opportunities for experiential learning and",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,2,"These technologies can assist students in identifying conceptual gaps, stimulat­ ing analytical reasoning, and supporting independent learning trajectories (Ng et al., 2025. Their capabilities to simulate real-world scenarios and support large-scale data interpretation creates new opportunities for experiential learning and skill develop­ ment (Aoun, 2017; Baker et al., 2016; Kaplan & Haenlein, 2019). This is particularly relevant in economics and business disciplines, where applied reasoning, data analy­ sis, and decision-making skills are central to learning outcomes. Consequently, AI has increasingly been positioned as a potential catalyst for enhancing student engage­ ment and active learning in higher education contexts. Despite these opportunities, AI integration raises important pedagogical and ethi­ cal challenges. Concerns on academic integrity, algorithmic bias, data privacy, and potential widening of educational inequalities require careful instructional over­ sight (Beurer-Kellner et al., 2023; Holmes et al., 2019; Reich & Ito, 2017). Emerg­ ing literature increasingly distinguishes between unguided AI use and structured pedagogical integration. Unguided AI use may encourage superficial engagement, overreliance on generic AI outputs, and reduced independent analytical effort, par­ ticularly given the risk of inaccuracies and hallucinations",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,2,"2017). Emerg­ ing literature increasingly distinguishes between unguided AI use and structured pedagogical integration. Unguided AI use may encourage superficial engagement, overreliance on generic AI outputs, and reduced independent analytical effort, par­ ticularly given the risk of inaccuracies and hallucinations in generative AI systems (Alqahtani et al., 2023). Conversely, structured instructional approaches involving guided AI interaction, scaffolded tasks, and reflective learning activities are associ­ ated with deeper engagement and more effective skill development (Aldosari, 2020; Chen et al., 2023). However, empirical evidence directly comparing structured AI integration with unguided AI use in higher education remains limited, especially on student engagement and academic performance outcomes. These limits understand­ ing of how AI can be effectively embedded within student-centred active learning environments. This study addresses these gaps by evaluating the effectiveness of structured AI interventions on engagement, critical thinking, problem-solving ability, communi­ cation skills, and academic performance across two undergraduate economics and business modules delivered over three consecutive academic sessions. By examining both classroom-based and independent AI use, the study contributes to empirical evi­ dence on how pedagogically guided AI integration influences",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,2,"cation skills, and academic performance across two undergraduate economics and business modules delivered over three consecutive academic sessions. By examining both classroom-based and independent AI use, the study contributes to empirical evi­ dence on how pedagogically guided AI integration influences student-centred active 1 3 3836",45
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,3,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 learning outcomes and offers practical insights for responsible AI adoption in higher education while maintaining academic integrity and preparing students for increas­ ingly AI-mediated academic and professional landscapes. 2 Literature review The emergence of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) as an increasingly accessible educational technology has contributed to significant shifts in higher edu­ cation pedagogies and learning practices (Crompton & Burke, 2023; Ng et al., 2025). Contemporary GenAI platforms such as ChatGPT, Claude AI, and Gemini are being integrated into educational environments as facilitators of student-centred active learning (SAL), providing personalized feedback, adaptive resource navigation, and on-demand content generation that support learner autonomy, inquiry-driven knowl­ edge construction, and collaborative engagement (Chassignol et al., 2018; Luckin et al., 2016). These developments position AI not merely as a technological enhance­ ment but as a catalyst for pedagogical transformation. Empirical research increasingly suggests that AI-supported learning environments can positively influence engagement and academic performance, particularly when embedded within structured pedagogical frameworks. Studies highlight AI’s capacity to enhance formative feedback, adaptive learning pathways, and self-regulated learn­ ing processes, all",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,3,"transformation. Empirical research increasingly suggests that AI-supported learning environments can positively influence engagement and academic performance, particularly when embedded within structured pedagogical frameworks. Studies highlight AI’s capacity to enhance formative feedback, adaptive learning pathways, and self-regulated learn­ ing processes, all of which are strongly associated with improved academic outcomes (Chen, 2023; Crompton & Burke, 2023; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). Research on technology-mediated learning further indicates that engagement behaviours such as regular interaction with learning materials, independence study, and active participa­ tion are critical predictors of academic achievement (Bognár et al., 2024; Dixson, 2015; Gašević et al., 2015; Henrie et al., 2015). These findings suggest that AI’s impact on academic performance often operates indirectly through engagement- related mechanisms rather than through technological access alone. Discipline-specific applications provide further evidence of AI’s pedagogical potential. In economics and business education, AI tools facilitate market simulation, complex data analysis, and application of theoretical models to practical contexts (Kaplan & Haenlein, 2019). AI-supported platforms enable students to analyze real- time datasets, test economic/business hypotheses, and model regulatory scenarios, thereby enhancing analytical reasoning, decision-making skills, and",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,3,"market simulation, complex data analysis, and application of theoretical models to practical contexts (Kaplan & Haenlein, 2019). AI-supported platforms enable students to analyze real- time datasets, test economic/business hypotheses, and model regulatory scenarios, thereby enhancing analytical reasoning, decision-making skills, and experiential learning outcomes (Aoun, 2017; Silva & Janes, 2020). Such applications are particu­ larly relevant in quantitatively oriented disciplines where applied reasoning and data interpretation are central determinants of academic success. However, the literature also highlights important risks associated with unguided AI use. Studies indicate that unstructured interaction with AI tools may promote superficial learning, technological dependency, or academic misconduct, especially when students lack sufficient AI literacy or critical evaluation skills (Holmes et al., 2019; Reich & Ito, 2017). The phenomenon of AI hallucination, involving plausible but inaccurate content generation, further raises concerns about misinformation, bias, and academic integrity when AI outputs are accepted uncritically (Alqahtani et al., 2023; Beurer-Kellner et al., 2023; Selwyn, 2019). These findings suggest that access 1 3 3837",163
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,4,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 to AI technologies alone does not guarantee improved academic performance and may, under certain conditions, undermine deeper learning processes. Consequently, recent scholarships increasingly emphasize structured AI integra­ tion supported by explicit pedagogical guidance. Studies by Chen et al. (2023) and Bai̇doo-Anu and Owusu Ansah (2023) demonstrate that AI tools embedded within guided instructional frameworks enhance engagement, self-paced learning, and aca­ demic outcomes. Chiu et al. (2023) further identify educator presence as a critical mediating factor that helps students use AI productively while avoiding over-reliance on generic AI outputs. Within this framework, student engagement emerges as a central mechanism through which AI integration influences academic performance. Engagement, operationalized through independent study, classroom participation, and critical interaction with course content, has consistently been linked to improved academic achievement in technology-enhanced learning environments (Dixson, 2015; Gašević et al., 2015; Henrie et al., 2015). Overall, the literature suggests that the educational effectiveness of AI depends less on technological availability itself than on the pedagogical frameworks that guide its use. While structured AI interventions appear capable of enhancing engagement",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,4,"al., 2015; Henrie et al., 2015). Overall, the literature suggests that the educational effectiveness of AI depends less on technological availability itself than on the pedagogical frameworks that guide its use. While structured AI interventions appear capable of enhancing engagement and academic performance, empirical comparisons between structured and unguided AI use remain limited, particularly within analytically intensive disciplines such as economics and business education. This study addresses these gaps by empirically examining whether structured AI integration, mediated through student engagement, improves learning outcomes across two undergraduate modules. 3 Methodology This study adopts a mixed-methods research design to explore the impact of AI inter­ ventions on student engagement and academic performance in two undergraduate economics and business modules at a UK university: BU31010 (Competition and Market Regulation) and BU32006 (Big Data Analytics for Business Transformation). These two modules were selected based on their emphasis on higher-order cognitive skills that align with the contemporary AI capabilities, their diverse student cohorts, and consistent assessment structures across the study period. Also, the lead instructor remained constant, ensuring consistency in delivery and reducing instructor-related variability.",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,4,"on their emphasis on higher-order cognitive skills that align with the contemporary AI capabilities, their diverse student cohorts, and consistent assessment structures across the study period. Also, the lead instructor remained constant, ensuring consistency in delivery and reducing instructor-related variability. The analytical framework distinguishes between three periods: (i) the Pre-AI Era (2022/23), (ii) the AI Era (2023/24, marked by unguided AI use), and (iii) the AI Intervention period (2024/25, involving structured, pedagogically guided AI inter­ vention). This periodisation allows for a comparative evaluation of the evolving effects of AI on student outcomes. 3.1 Data description and sources The study utilizes both quantitative and qualitative data. This approach allows for a richer, triangulated understanding of the educational processes under investigation, and aligns with best practices in educational research where explanatory quantita­ 1 3 3838",133
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,5,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 tive trends are contextualized by qualitative insights (Creswell & Clark, 2017). Also, Greene et al. (1989) noted that triangulating quantitative and qualitative findings in educational research enhances interpretive coherence by revealing the meanings behind observed patterns. 3.1.1 Quantitative data The quantitative dataset, which was gathered from institutional digital learning plat­ forms, includes 31 student-level observations for the BU31010 module and 103 for the BU32006 module. These datasets cover student performance in terms of grades, engagement, and attendance across three academic sessions. Specifically, the data was sourced from Blackboard VLE Analytics (independent study hours), SEAtS App (attendance data), and eVision (final module results). Table 1 provides a summary of variables used in the quantitative analysis. 3.1.2 Qualitative data Qualitative data, which complemented the quantitative metrics, was collected through an anonymized Microsoft Form Survey at the end of the 2024/25 academic session. This survey captured students’ perceptions of how AI tools influenced their engagement, confidence, and understanding. To comply with ethical protocols, no Variable Definition/Measurement Data Source Engagement Log of independent study hours Black­ board VLE",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,5,"end of the 2024/25 academic session. This survey captured students’ perceptions of how AI tools influenced their engagement, confidence, and understanding. To comply with ethical protocols, no Variable Definition/Measurement Data Source Engagement Log of independent study hours Black­ board VLE Analytics Attendance Normalised attendance rate SEAtS App Problem-Solving Midterm and final exam grades BU31010 Exam Records Critical Thinking Essay/report grades BU31010 & BU32006 Essay Grades Communication Skills Presentation grades BU32006 Presentation Grades Overall Performance Final module grade eVision Final Results AI Intervention Dummy for structured AI use (1 = 2024/25, 0 = Otherwise) Constructed AI Era Dummy for unguided AI use (0 = 2022/23; 2023/24 to 2024/25 = 1) Constructed Survey Data Student perception on how AI tools influenced their en­ gagement, confidence, and understanding Online Mi­ crosoft Form Survey Table 1 Variable descriptions and sources of data 1 3 3839",141
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,6,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 personally identifying information, such as names or student IDs, was collected from the 9 students in the BU31010 module and the 18 students in the BU32006 module who participated in the survey. Descriptive analyses of the survey responses show strong support for structured AI interventions, with majority of students reporting improved problem-solving and critical/analytical thinking skills in both two modules (See Table 2). The AI inter­ vention survey results demonstrate predominantly positive outcomes across the two modules, with notable strengths in decision tree design workshops (72.2% finding AI “Helpful” or “Extremely Helpful”), problem-solving during tutorials (66.6% rating it “Effective” or “Highly Effective”), and case study research (66.7% reporting bene­ fits). Students also reported significant improvements in learning engagement (62.9% noting “More” or “Significant” improvement) and homework preparation (66.7% finding AI feedback helpful). While classroom participation confidence showed more varied results, a majority (70.3%) still reported at least some positive impact. These findings suggest AI interventions were most effective as practical learning tools for specific tasks. 3.2 Descriptive and comparative statistics To contextualise the regression",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,6,"participation confidence showed more varied results, a majority (70.3%) still reported at least some positive impact. These findings suggest AI interventions were most effective as practical learning tools for specific tasks. 3.2 Descriptive and comparative statistics To contextualise the regression analysis, we provide comparative statistics of student performance, engagement, and attendance across the three academic years. These are summarised in Tables 3 and 4 for BU31010 and BU32006 modules, respectively. The results show noticeable increases in engagement and attendance during the AI inter­ vention period compared to the preceding academic periods (see Fig. 1, Tables 11 and 12 in Appendix A & B, respectively). 3.3 Analytical approach: two-stage mediation analysis The study employs a two-stage analytical approach to investigate how AI interven­ tion affects student performance, focusing on engagement as a mediating factor. 3.3.1 Stage 1: mediator validation First, we validate whether engagement serves as a true mediator in the AI-perfor­ mance relationship. This validation involves: Probit Regression Model – This is specified to estimate the probability of struc­ tured AI use (AI Intervention) or unguided AI use (AI Era)",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,6,validate whether engagement serves as a true mediator in the AI-perfor­ mance relationship. This validation involves: Probit Regression Model – This is specified to estimate the probability of struc­ tured AI use (AI Intervention) or unguided AI use (AI Era) as function of engage­ ment levels (measured by independent study hours): P [AI_Intervention = 1 AI_Era = 1 ] = Φ(α0 + α1 lnE ngagementi + εi)  (1) 1 3 3840,72
