DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,10,"through engagement. If θ1(AI Era) is insignificant or negative, then unguided AI use does not impact student performance. But is both θ1and θ2are both positive and statistically significant, then AI has both direct and indirect effects on performance. This analytical framework enables us to distinguish between the direct impact of AI availability and the indirect benefits that occur through increased engage­ ment, providing a comprehensive understanding of how different AI implementation approaches affect educational outcomes. 3.4 Estimation techniques This study employs two complementary estimation techniques to ensure robust and reliable findings given the characteristics of the student performance and engagement datasets. OLS regression served as the baseline estimation technique due to its wide­ spread application and interpretability in educational research. However, boxplots (see Fig. 2 in Appendix C) revealed substantial outliers across multiple variables in our dataset, particularly in engagement hours and assessment scores. Such outli­ ers, coupled with potential heteroskedasticity typical in student performance data, could substantially bias OLS estimates. To address these methodological concerns, we employed Robust Least Squares (RobustLS) estimation as a complementary tech­ nique. RobustLS mitigates",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,10,"hours and assessment scores. Such outli­ ers, coupled with potential heteroskedasticity typical in student performance data, could substantially bias OLS estimates. To address these methodological concerns, we employed Robust Least Squares (RobustLS) estimation as a complementary tech­ nique. RobustLS mitigates the influence of outliers by assigning lower weights to observations that deviate significantly from general patterns, while simultaneously addressing heteroskedasticity through robust standard errors. This approach is par­ ticularly valuable in our context, where small sample sizes make each observation potentially influential and where student engagement patterns showed considerable 1 3 3844",92
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,11,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 variability. The dual estimation approach allows us to verify the consistency of find­ ings across different methodological assumptions. When both techniques yield simi­ lar results, we can have greater confidence in the robustness of our findings regarding the mediating role of engagement in the relationship between AI intervention and student performance. 4 Results This section presents the empirical findings from a two-stage mediation analysis exploring the differential impacts of unguided and structured AI use on student per­ formance in two undergraduate modules. By integrating quantitative metrics (e.g., assessment grades, independent study hours, attendance rates) with qualitative sur­ vey responses, the analysis distinguishes between direct effects of AI use and the indirect effects that occur through enhanced student engagement. The findings are broken down by skill areas—problem-solving, critical thinking, and communication abilities—as well as academic periods to offer a thorough analysis of the impact of AI interventions on educational performance. 4.1 Impact of AI use on student performance in BU31010 module The analysis reveals compelling evidence regarding how different approaches to AI integration affect student",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,11,"periods to offer a thorough analysis of the impact of AI interventions on educational performance. 4.1 Impact of AI use on student performance in BU31010 module The analysis reveals compelling evidence regarding how different approaches to AI integration affect student performance in the Competition and Market Regulation module (BU31010). As shown in Table 5, unguided AI use (AI Era) has a significant negative impact on overall student performance, with consistent coefficients across both OLS (θ₁ = −3.80, p < 0.01) and RobustLS (θ₁ = −3.77, p < 0.01) models. This negative effect aligns with Holmes et al. (2022) findings that merely providing access to AI tools without appropriate instructional scaffolding may lead to superficial learn­ ing approaches and potential academic misconducts (e.g. cheating in exams). In contrast, engagement (representing structured AI intervention) has a substan­ tial positive effect on performance (OLS: θ₂ = 9.5, p < 0.01; RobustLS: θ₂ = 7.98, p < 0.01). This finding underscores the importance of guided interactions with AI tools in fostering deeper learning processes, consistent with Luckin and Cukurova (2023) golden triangle framework, which",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,11,"(OLS: θ₂ = 9.5, p < 0.01; RobustLS: θ₂ = 7.98, p < 0.01). This finding underscores the importance of guided interactions with AI tools in fostering deeper learning processes, consistent with Luckin and Cukurova (2023) golden triangle framework, which provides a comprehensive model for integrating AI into education. Their framework connects three crucial components: educators, researchers, and EdTech developers and highlights the pivotal role of teachers in orchestrating educational experiences. It advocates for educators to carefully select AI tools and implement them effectively to ensure they enhance learning outcomes and support the development of students’ cognitive and analytical skills. The high explanatory power of the models (R² = 0.82 in OLS and Rw² = 0.92 in RobustLS) indicates that the combined effect of AI use, engagement, and attendance accounts for a significant proportion of variation in student performance. When examining critical thinking skills (Table 6), unguided AI use maintains a negative though statistically insignificant effect (OLS: θ₁ = −1.57; RobustLS: θ₁ = −1.55), while engagement demonstrates a marginally significant positive impact (OLS: θ₂ = 3.19, p < 0.1; RobustLS:",180
