DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,15,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 4.3 Robustness checks and diagnostic tests To ensure the reliability of our findings, comprehensive diagnostic tests were con­ ducted across all models. The high explanatory power across models (R² ranging from 0.20 to 0.82 for OLS and Rw² from 0.33 to 0.92 for RobustLS) indicates strong fit to the underlying data patterns. All models demonstrate strong overall significance with F-statistics ranging from 8.23 to 40.82 (p < 0.01), confirming the joint relevance of the selected predictors. Heteroskedasticity testing using the Breusch-Pagan-Godfrey approach revealed potential issues in several models, particularly in the BU32006 module and the final exam model for BU31010 (Table 7). The presence of heteroskedasticity in these models justifies our dual estimation approach, with RobustLS estimations providing more reliable coefficient estimates in the presence of non-constant error variance, as recommended by White (1980) and MacKinnon and White (1985). Multicollinearity diagnostics confirm no problematic correlation among predic­ tors, with Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) consistently below 3 for BU31010 and below 2 for BU32006, well under the conventional threshold of 10 suggested by Hair et",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,15,"and MacKinnon and White (1985). Multicollinearity diagnostics confirm no problematic correlation among predic­ tors, with Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) consistently below 3 for BU31010 and below 2 for BU32006, well under the conventional threshold of 10 suggested by Hair et al. (2019). The Jarque-Bera tests indicate deviations from normality in several models, particularly in the BU32006 module, though this is less concerning given the robust estimation techniques employed and the moderate sample sizes, in line with Schmidt and Finan’s (2018) recommendations for educational research. AI Effect Independent Variables OLS Coeff. (SE) RobustLS Coeff. (SE)1 Direct Effect AI Era β₁=0.43 (1.1223) β₁=1.7**1 (0.8571) Attendance β₂=6.77*** (1.3447) β₂=4.54*** (1.0268) Indirect Effect Engagement δ₁=3.61*** (1.0218) δ₁=3.96*** (0.7917) Attendance δ₂= 4.56*** (1,4077) δ₂=2.59** (1.0907) Total Effect AI Era θ₁=0.30*** (1.0714) θ₁=1.19 (0.8022) Engagement θ₂ =3.61*** (1.0338) θ₂=3.63*** (0.7741) Attendance θ3= 4.57*** (1.4903) θ3= 2.15** (1.0661) Model Diagnostic Check (Total Effect) OLS Model RobustLS Model Model explanatory power R² =0.30 Rw2 =0.40 Overall Model Significance F-Stat = 14.11*** Not Available Heteroskedasticity Test (B-P-G) F-Stat = 5.33*** (Reject H0) Robust to Het­ eroskedasticity Multicollinearity Test (VIF)",180
