DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,16,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 The consistency of results across different estimation techniques and model speci­ fications confirms the robustness of our findings, particularly on the positive impact of engagement and the mixed or negative effects of unguided AI use. These diagnos­ tic checks strengthen confidence in the validity of our conclusions about the mediat­ ing role of engagement in the AI-performance relationship. 4.4 Survey results In addition to the quantitative data analysis, qualitative insights were gathered from an anonymised post-intervention survey conducted at the end of the 2024/25 session. These responses provide deeper contextual understanding of how students perceived the integration of AI tools into their learning process. Table 2 presents a thematic summary of key insights, highlighting four dominant patterns: (1) increased cogni­ tive confidence, (2) improved time management, (3) appreciation for guided AI use, and (4) ethical concerns about overreliance. These results suggest that the structured AI intervention was perceived as beneficial, especially for tasks requiring indepen­ dent problem-solving and critical thinking. AI Effect Independent Variables OLS Coeff. (SE) RobustLS Coeff. (SE)1 Direct Effect AI Era",180
