DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,17,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 5 Discussion This section interprets the findings within broader pedagogical and theoretical frame­ works. The findings of this study provide empirical evidence that the educational impact of AI technologies depends less on access alone and more on how these tools are pedagogically integrated. Structured AI interventions were consistently associ­ ated with higher engagement levels and improved academic performance, whereas unguided AI use showed limited or negative effects in some assessment domains. This distinction highlights the importance of instructional scaffolding in ensuring that AI functions as a cognitive support tool rather than a substitute for independent analytical reasoning. The section also discusses the limitations and outlines future research directions to support responsible, ethical and effective AI implementation in learning environments. 5.1 Mediation role of engagement in AI-performance relationship Our findings provide strong empirical support for engagement as a critical medi­ ating factor in the relationship between AI intervention and student performance. The validation of engagement as a mediator (Tables 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10) confirms that structured AI use significantly increases student independent",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,17,"engagement as a critical medi­ ating factor in the relationship between AI intervention and student performance. The validation of engagement as a mediator (Tables 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10) confirms that structured AI use significantly increases student independent study hours, while unguided AI access does not consistently lead to higher engagement. This mediating AI Effect Independent Variables OLS Coeff. (SE) RobustLS Coeff. (SE)1 Direct Effect AI Era β₁=1.75 (1.1824) β₁= 3.29*** (0.7811) Attendance β₂= 5.08*** (1.4167) β₂= 2.82*** (0.9359) Indirect Effect Engagement δ₁= 2.95*** (1.1149) δ₁= 1.88* (0.8083) Attendance δ₂= 3.58** (1.5360) δ₂= 1.82 (1.1136) Total Effect AI Era θ₁= 1.41 (1.1604) θ₁= 3.12*** (0.7895) Engagement θ₂ = 2.79** (1.1196) θ₂=1.81** (0.7618) Attendance θ3= 3.37** (1.5420) θ3= 1.51 (1.0491) Model Diagnostic Check (Total Effect) OLS Model OLS Model Model explanatory power R² =0.20 Rw² =0.33 Overall Model Significance F-Stat = 8.23*** Not Available Heteroskedasticity Test (B-P-G) F-Stat = 3.92** (Reject H0) Robust to Het­ eroskedasticity Multicollinearity Test (VIF) VIF < 2 for all the variables Not Available Normality (Jarque-Bera Test) JB- Stat = 114.91*** JB- Stat = 192.01***",180
