DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,18,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 relationship explains why AI intervention produces positive educational outcomes whereas mere AI availability often fails to enhance learning, supporting Gašević et al.‘s (2015) theoretical framework on the importance of mediating factors in educa­ tional technology effectiveness. The differential impact of unguided versus structured AI use reflects fundamen­ tal differences in how students interact with AI technologies. Unguided AI use may encourage passive consumption of AI-generated content without deeper process­ ing, potentially fostering dependency rather than autonomous learning skills (Roll & Wylie, 2016). In contrast, structured AI intervention promotes active engagement with learning materials, encouraging students to leverage AI as a complementary tool rather than a substitute for their cognitive processes. As Kalyuga (2009) argues, adaptive technology-enhanced learning environments must be designed to manage learners’ cognitive load effectively. Without appropriate scaffolding, ICT-based tools, including AI systems can overwhelm novice learners or misdirect attention, particu­ larly when students are required to process unfamiliar content independently. This theoretical lens reinforces the importance of structured AI interventions that are tai­ lored to students’ cognitive readiness and aligned with",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,18,"systems can overwhelm novice learners or misdirect attention, particu­ larly when students are required to process unfamiliar content independently. This theoretical lens reinforces the importance of structured AI interventions that are tai­ lored to students’ cognitive readiness and aligned with instructional goals. The observed improvements in independent study hours and performance met­ rics in the structured AI cohort (2024/25) are reinforced by the qualitative evidence summarized in Table 2. Students reported feeling more confident in their ability to engage with analytical tasks and manage their time effectively, echoing the signifi­ cant increases observed in engagement and assessment outcomes. These self-reported perceptions lend further credibility to the argument that pedagogically guided AI use can enhance learner autonomy and cognitive engagement. 5.2 Skill-specific impacts of AI intervention Our analysis reveals important variations in how AI intervention affects differ­ ent academic skills. The strongest positive impacts of engagement are observed in problem-solving (BU31010) and critical thinking (BU32006), suggesting that struc­ tured AI use particularly enhances higher-order cognitive skills when appropriately integrated into the curriculum. This is consistent with findings from Hui (2024), who",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,18,"strongest positive impacts of engagement are observed in problem-solving (BU31010) and critical thinking (BU32006), suggesting that struc­ tured AI use particularly enhances higher-order cognitive skills when appropriately integrated into the curriculum. This is consistent with findings from Hui (2024), who explored the integration of Bloom’s taxonomy in AI-supported learning envi­ ronments to enhance problem-solving and critical thinking, and Elim (2024), who investigated the impact of AI-generated text on critical thinking within UK business schools, extend and reinforce our findings about skill-specific impacts of AI interven­ tion. These studies highlight how structured AI use, when thoughtfully integrated into the curriculum, bolsters higher-order cognitive skills such as problem-solving in BU31010 and critical thinking in BU32006, mirroring the strongest positive impacts of engagement observed in our analysis. As shown in Table 2, over 70% of students described the structured AI intervention as “helpful” or “very helpful” for improving problem-solving and analytical reasoning. These perceptions support the quantitative improvements recorded in assessment components tied to these cognitive skills (e.g., midterm exams, essays), strengthening the overall validity of the mixed-methods findings. 1 3 3852",179
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,18,"or “very helpful” for improving problem-solving and analytical reasoning. These perceptions support the quantitative improvements recorded in assessment components tied to these cognitive skills (e.g., midterm exams, essays), strengthening the overall validity of the mixed-methods findings. 1 3 3852",39
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,19,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 The more modest effects on communication skills suggest that certain competen­ cies may require additional pedagogical approaches beyond AI support, consistent with Hooker (2016) findings on the impact of digital technology on the interpersonal communication skills of Millennials. Hooker highlights that while digital technology offers positive opportunities for maintaining long-distance relationships, it has also contributed to a decline in interpersonal communication skills, necessitating more targeted educational interventions. The negative impact of unguided AI use on problem-solving in final exams raises concerns about potential over-reliance on AI tools without developing independent analytical capabilities. This underscores the importance of designing AI interventions that scaffold learning toward autonomous problem-solving rather than providing ready-made solutions, supporting Selwyn (2019) research on the risks of technologi­ cal dependence in educational settings. 5.3 Practical implications for AI integration in higher education These findings carry important implications for higher education institutions inte­ grating AI technologies. First, simply providing access to AI tools is insufficient and may be counterproductive without appropriate pedagogical structure, supporting Tsai et al. (2019) emphasis on the role of",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,19,"These findings carry important implications for higher education institutions inte­ grating AI technologies. First, simply providing access to AI tools is insufficient and may be counterproductive without appropriate pedagogical structure, supporting Tsai et al. (2019) emphasis on the role of instructional design in effective technol­ ogy integration. Educators should therefore embed AI use within structured learning activities, provide clear guidance on responsible use, and design tasks that encourage critical engagement rather than passive reliance on AI outputs. Second, the mediating role of engagement highlights the need for AI interventions that promote sustained study effort and meaningful interaction with learning materi­ als, consistent with Dixson (2015) findings on engagement as a key determinant of success in technology-enhanced learning. Practical strategies include integrating AI- supported formative tasks, guided problem-solving exercises, and reflective activities that foster deeper cognitive engagement. The disciplinary differences observed suggest that AI integration should be tai­ lored to specific subject areas, taking account of distinct learning challenges and skill requirements (Mishra & Koehler, 2006). Finally, the variation in skill-level outcomes indicates that AI interventions should target clearly defined learning",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,19,"suggest that AI integration should be tai­ lored to specific subject areas, taking account of distinct learning challenges and skill requirements (Mishra & Koehler, 2006). Finally, the variation in skill-level outcomes indicates that AI interventions should target clearly defined learning objectives rather than adopt one-size-fits-all approaches, aligning with Puentedura’s (2014) SAMR framework for purposeful educational technology integration. 5.4 Limitations and future research directions Despite the robust analytical approach employed, several limitations warrant con­ sideration. The relatively small sample size, particularly in the BU31010 mod­ ule, may limit the generalizability of findings, a common challenge in educational technology research (Tondeur et al., 2017). However, to ensure robustness, we employed a dual estimation approach using both OLS and RobustLS. The con­ sistency of findings across these techniques, alongside comprehensive diagnos­ tic checks, strengthens confidence in the validity of conclusions on the mediating role of engagement. While engagement was operationalised primarily through 1 3 3853",152
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,20,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 independent study hours derived from institutional learning analytics (blackboard VLE analytics), this proxy may not fully capture its multidimensional nature in AI-enhanced active learning contexts. Future research could incorporate more direct behavioural indicators, including in-class participation, collaborative learning activities, formative assessment engage­ ment, classroom discussions, and AI interaction data such as prompt histories or usage logs, which may provide more granular insight into learning processes (Henrie et al., 2015; Selwyn, 2019). Future studies should expand sample size and disciplin­ ary scope to assess the consistency of these relationships across diverse academic contexts. Longitudinal investigations examining the sustained effects of AI interven­ tions beyond a single academic year would further clarify their long-term educational impact (Reich, 2020). Qualitative analyses of how students engage with AI tools under different instructional frameworks would complement these findings, offer­ ing deeper understanding of how engagement mediates AI’s influence on learning outcomes, consistent with mixed-methods approaches advocated by (Lai & Bower, 2019). 6 Conclusions This study provides empirical evidence on the differential impacts of AI integra­ tion approaches in higher",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,20,"ing deeper understanding of how engagement mediates AI’s influence on learning outcomes, consistent with mixed-methods approaches advocated by (Lai & Bower, 2019). 6 Conclusions This study provides empirical evidence on the differential impacts of AI integra­ tion approaches in higher education, demonstrating that the relationship between AI use and student performance is significantly mediated by engagement. Using a two- stage mediation analysis across two undergraduate modules, the findings show that unguided AI use, defined as simple access to AI tools without pedagogical struc­ ture, generally fails to improve academic performance and may negatively affect outcomes, particularly in problem-solving assessments. This challenges assumptions that technological access alone enhances learning and highlights the risks of imple­ mentation without appropriate pedagogical frameworks. The analysis further confirms engagement as a critical mediating mechanism in the AI–performance relationship. Structured AI interventions significantly increase stu­ dent engagement, measured through independent study hours, which in turn supports improvements in academic performance across multiple skill domains. However, the impact varies across disciplines and skill areas, with stronger effects observed in problem-solving and critical thinking than in communication skills.",180
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,20,"engagement, measured through independent study hours, which in turn supports improvements in academic performance across multiple skill domains. However, the impact varies across disciplines and skill areas, with stronger effects observed in problem-solving and critical thinking than in communication skills. These differ­ ences indicate the need for context-sensitive AI integration strategies aligned with disciplinary requirements and targeted learning outcomes. The practical implications are highlighted for higher education institutions seek­ ing effective AI adoption. Rather than prioritising technology provision alone, insti­ tutions should emphasise structured pedagogical interventions that guide students to use AI as a complement to, rather than a substitute for, cognitive effort. Faculty development initiatives should focus on pedagogical frameworks that enhance engagement, scaffold autonomous learning, and promote responsible AI use. As AI technologies continue to evolve, successful integration will depend less on techno­ logical sophistication than on pedagogical design and institutional support. 1 3 3854",147
DOC_046,s10639-026-13946-w.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-13946-w,21,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:3835–3861 Appendices Appendix A Overall, while AI holds considerable potential to enhance higher education, its effectiveness ultimately depends on how it is integrated into teaching and learning practices. Structured AI interventions that promote engagement, critical thinking, and responsible use can improve student outcomes while preparing graduates for increasingly AI-mediated academic and professional environments. Fig. 1 Impact of AI Use on Overall Student Performance 1 3 3855",71
