DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,1,"Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2 Extended author information available on the last page of the article Received: 21 August 2025 / Accepted: 15 July 2026 / Published online: 15 August 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Exploring student use of generative AI in higher education: A dual-institutional study Åse Nygren1 · Anna Eriksson1 · Jeanette Sjöberg2 · Emil Alégroth1 Abstract Although a growing body of research has examined students’ attitudes toward gen­ erative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in higher education, few studies have com­ pared perceptions across contrasting institutional contexts or explored how students’ reported uses of GenAI relate to broader learning practices. This study addresses that gap by examining university students’ perceptions, self-reported competence, and use of GenAI at two Swedish universities with different academic profiles: a technology-oriented institution and a broader multidisciplinary institution. The study is based on an exploratory questionnaire survey administered to all enrolled students at both universities, yielding 1,097 responses (University A response rate: 11.27%, University B response rate: 14.06%) from students across diverse dis­ ciplines, including engineering, nursing, and criminology. Quantitative data were analyzed",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,1,"an exploratory questionnaire survey administered to all enrolled students at both universities, yielding 1,097 responses (University A response rate: 11.27%, University B response rate: 14.06%) from students across diverse dis­ ciplines, including engineering, nursing, and criminology. Quantitative data were analyzed using reliability analysis, exploratory factor analysis, and non-parametric group comparisons, supplemented by thematic analysis of qualitative responses. The analysis identified three reliable constructs: perceived learning benefit, per­ ceived institutional support and integration, and self-reported technical knowledge and competence. Across both institutions, students reported generally positive at­ titudes toward GenAI and described using it primarily for information retrieval, text refinement, and text analysis, but also as a discussion partner or personal tutor in ways that suggest both surface-level and more dialogic forms of engagement. Comparisons between the two universities showed broad similarity across most measures, with the only statistically significant difference relating to perceived institutional support and integration, which was rated higher by students at the technology-oriented university. Students at both institutions also viewed GenAI pri­ marily as a complement to, rather than a replacement for, traditional teaching, while reporting only",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,1,"relating to perceived institutional support and integration, which was rated higher by students at the technology-oriented university. Students at both institutions also viewed GenAI pri­ marily as a complement to, rather than a replacement for, traditional teaching, while reporting only moderate trust in AI-generated outputs. These findings suggest that GenAI is already embedded in students’ study practices, but that its use is largely self-directed rather than strongly shaped by institutional context. The study thus contributes comparative empirical evidence on student engagement with GenAI",83
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,2,"1 3 8704 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 across contrasting higher education settings and highlights the need for pedagogi­ cal and institutional strategies that support critical, reflective, and responsible use. Keywords Higher education · Generative AI · Student perceptions · Learning approaches 1 Background In higher education, the significance of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) lies not only in its growing availability but also in its potential to fundamentally reshape how students learn. As GenAI tools become increasingly embedded in everyday aca­ demic practice, it becomes more important to examine not only whether students use them, but also how such use may influence the quality of learning. In particular, the distinction between deep and surface learning is highly relevant in this context. GenAI may reinforce surface learning when it is used for uncritical reproduction, mechanical paraphrasing, or low-engagement task completion, thereby perpetuat­ ing the hollow learning practices described by Mintz (2024). At the same time, it may also support deeper learning by functioning as a scaffold for idea exploration, iterative critique of AI-generated output, synthesis with students’ own reasoning, and",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,2,"thereby perpetuat­ ing the hollow learning practices described by Mintz (2024). At the same time, it may also support deeper learning by functioning as a scaffold for idea exploration, iterative critique of AI-generated output, synthesis with students’ own reasoning, and reflection on limitations such as bias and hallucinations. In this sense, GenAI can either foster passive engagement or support the development of the “demonstrated human capability” that Mintz (2024) argues universities must prioritize with increas­ ing urgency. This duality makes it especially important to examine how students perceive, value, and apply GenAI in educational contexts. In response, this study examines university students’ perceptions of the usefulness and value of GenAI, as well as their knowledge and attitudes towards these technologies, in relation to both higher education and their future professional lives. By identifying patterns in the collected data and discussing these findings in relation to relevant pedagogical frame­ works, the study aims to contribute to future pedagogical development in the context of GenAI in higher education. To provide a theoretical foundation for analyzing these dynamics, this study draws on the",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,2,"these findings in relation to relevant pedagogical frame­ works, the study aims to contribute to future pedagogical development in the context of GenAI in higher education. To provide a theoretical foundation for analyzing these dynamics, this study draws on the concepts of deep and surface learning from educational psychology. Origi­ nally introduced by Marton and Säljö (1976) and later developed by scholars such as Biggs (1988) and Entwistle (1991) these concepts describe qualitatively differ­ ent ways in which students engage with learning based on motivation, information processing, and level of participation. Surface learning is characterized by a focus on memorizing facts and reproducing information to meet external demands, such as passing examinations. Deep learning, by contrast, reflects an intrinsic motivation to understand concepts, connect ideas, and apply knowledge in meaningful ways. Students may alternate between these approaches depending on the task, subject, or pedagogical context. In the higher-education literature, this distinction has been widely used to examine how students’ approaches are shaped by the learning envi­ ronment rather than treated solely as individual dispositions. Research by Trigwell and Prosser (1991);",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,2,"task, subject, or pedagogical context. In the higher-education literature, this distinction has been widely used to examine how students’ approaches are shaped by the learning envi­ ronment rather than treated solely as individual dispositions. Research by Trigwell and Prosser (1991); Prosser and Trigwell (1999); Ramsden (2003); Biggs and Tang (2011) shows that teaching context, assessment design, and course structure play an",61
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,3,"1 3 8705 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 important role in whether students orient toward meaning-making or toward strategic reproduction. This line of work has been especially influential in demonstrating that assessment regimes emphasizing recall, reproduction, and performance under exter­ nal pressure tend to encourage surface approaches, whereas pedagogies emphasizing conceptual understanding, active engagement, and constructive alignment are more likely to support deep learning. This makes the framework of deep and surface learning a relevant conceptual backdrop for the present study. However, since the study relies on self-reported per­ ceptions and described use patterns rather than direct observation or validated mea­ sures of learning approaches, the framework is used here as an orienting lens rather than an analytical tool. Interpretations drawing on this distinction should therefore be read as tentative and illustrative rather than as empirical claims about students’ actual learning behavior. The concepts of deep and surface learning have also been widely used to examine how students’ perceptions of the teaching context, such as clarity of expectations, quality of feedback, workload, and course coherence, shape whether they adopt",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,3,"actual learning behavior. The concepts of deep and surface learning have also been widely used to examine how students’ perceptions of the teaching context, such as clarity of expectations, quality of feedback, workload, and course coherence, shape whether they adopt more meaning-oriented or more reproduction-oriented learning strategies (Prosser and Trigwell, 1999; Ramsden, 2003; Trigwell and Prosser, 1991). Within the literature on constructive alignment, the deep and surface learning distinction has been used to argue that deep learning is more likely when intended learning outcomes, teach­ ing activities, and assessment are aligned around conceptual understanding rather than mere content reproduction (Biggs and Tang, 2011). The framework has further been used to explore how students’ learning approaches vary across disciplinary con­ texts and task demands, showing that the same student may move between deep and surface approaches depending on workload, assessment pressure, and the perceived educational purpose of the task (Kember et al., 1996). Complementing this theoretical foundation, the study also draws interpretively on the concept of student agency in technology-enhanced learning contexts. Agency is understood here as students’ perceived capacity to",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,3,"and the perceived educational purpose of the task (Kember et al., 1996). Complementing this theoretical foundation, the study also draws interpretively on the concept of student agency in technology-enhanced learning contexts. Agency is understood here as students’ perceived capacity to control, influence, or take respon­ sibility for their interactions with technology (Marín et al., 2020). Although agency is not directly measured through dedicated scales, it serves as a sensitizing concept for interpreting patterns in self-reported use, attitudes toward GenAI integration, and tensions in relation to institutional expectations and guidelines. This interpretive lens helps illuminate how perceived autonomy and ownership may shape whether GenAI supports deeper engagement or more superficial forms of academic participation. Although research on GenAI in higher education has expanded rapidly, much of it has focused on students’ attitudes, adoption patterns, and ethical concerns rather than on how GenAI use relates to the quality of learning. This is an important limitation, because GenAI has a dual educational potential: it can be used either to accelerate task completion through superficial substitution or to support conceptual exploration, reflection, and critical",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,3,"GenAI use relates to the quality of learning. This is an important limitation, because GenAI has a dual educational potential: it can be used either to accelerate task completion through superficial substitution or to support conceptual exploration, reflection, and critical engagement. The present study addresses this gap by examin­ ing university students’ perceptions of the usefulness and value of GenAI, alongside their knowledge and attitudes toward these technologies, and by interpreting these in relation to deep and surface learning. The study further contributes by comparing two contrasting Swedish universities – one with a technological orientation and one with a broader academic profile – in order to explore whether institutional orientation",110
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,4,"1 3 8706 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 may shape students’ perceptions, uses, and interpretations of GenAI. In doing so, the study aims to offer both a theoretically grounded and practically relevant contribution to ongoing pedagogical development in higher education. 2 Related work Recent research examining university students’ perceptions of GenAI in higher edu­ cation has highlighted both its practical benefits and the challenges it poses. Stu­ dents generally report positive attitudes, viewing GenAI as a useful tool for clarifying concepts, generating ideas, refining written work, and improving efficiency in study tasks (Chiu, 2024; Obenza et al., 2024; Suonpää et al., 2024). They value it particu­ larly for information retrieval, text refinement, brainstorming, and accessibility sup­ port. At the same time, these positive views are tempered by concerns about output quality and trustworthiness, including inaccurate or fabricated information, absent sourcing, limited contextual relevance, and risks of plagiarism or inappropriate authorship (Chiu, 2024; Cho and Ofosu-Anim, 2025; Dai et al., 2026; Suonpää et al., 2024). Students also worry that overreliance on GenAI may erode creativity, critical thinking, and meaningful engagement",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,4,"sourcing, limited contextual relevance, and risks of plagiarism or inappropriate authorship (Chiu, 2024; Cho and Ofosu-Anim, 2025; Dai et al., 2026; Suonpää et al., 2024). Students also worry that overreliance on GenAI may erode creativity, critical thinking, and meaningful engagement with academic material (Chan and Hu, 2023; Farhat, 2025). In short, GenAI is seen by students as a valuable yet epistemically and ethically risky resource. These patterns are echoed in recent focus group study among UK psychology students (Cena et al., 2026). While participants appreciated GenAI for summarizing content, clarifying ideas, and easing the writing process, they expressed anxiety about plagiarism, diminished critical thinking, and unclear ethical boundaries, particularly in collaborative work. A prominent theme in the literature concerns the institutional and policy contexts that shape student perceptions of GenAI. Many students report uncertainty about what constitutes acceptable or ethical use from their universities’ perspectives, and this uncertainty appears to contribute to anxiety around academic integrity and appropriate participation in assessment. This concern reflects broader tensions in how universities conceptualize authorship, originality, and independent work in the context of AI-mediated",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,4,"use from their universities’ perspectives, and this uncertainty appears to contribute to anxiety around academic integrity and appropriate participation in assessment. This concern reflects broader tensions in how universities conceptualize authorship, originality, and independent work in the context of AI-mediated learning. A critical review by Luo (2024) on GenAI policies across 20 leading universities demonstrates that institutional responses often frame the central problem of GenAI as the risk that students will submit work that is no longer “original.” While policies acknowledge other concerns, such as reliability or unequal access, Luo argues that they primarily position GenAI as a form of external assistance that undermines independent intellectual contribution. This framing risks oversimplifying both the nature of student work and the increasingly distributed, collaborative, and technologically mediated character of knowledge production in contemporary higher education. A key ""critical silence"" in many institutional policies therefore concerns how originality itself is being transformed in the digital age. This framing matters because it aligns with, and may reinforce, the ambiguity students report. When institutional guidance is unclear or overly restrictive, students struggle to distinguish between",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,4,"policies therefore concerns how originality itself is being transformed in the digital age. This framing matters because it aligns with, and may reinforce, the ambiguity students report. When institutional guidance is unclear or overly restrictive, students struggle to distinguish between responsible augmentation and unacceptable substitution. Sev­ eral studies therefore recommend more explicit, context-sensitive, and co-created",55
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,5,"1 3 8707 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 policies that help students navigate GenAI ethically rather than merely warning against misuse (Johnston et al., 2024). Although much of the literature focuses on attitudes, adoption, and ethical con­ cerns, a growing body of work suggests that students’ perceptions of GenAI are closely linked to questions of agency. In this context, agency refers broadly to stu­ dents’ perceived control over their learning, their confidence in their own academic abilities, and their capacity to use technology intentionally rather than passively. Evi­ dence suggests that students who feel more academically capable or agentic tend to adopt GenAI more selectively and strategically. For example, Johnston et al. (2024), in a large-scale survey of 2,555 students at the University of Liverpool, found that students who were more confident in their academic abilities were less likely to use GenAI for high-stakes tasks such as essay writing and were also less supportive of such use by others. By contrast, students who viewed GenAI as enhancing their con­ trol over learning, such as through brainstorming, grammar support, or",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,5,"to use GenAI for high-stakes tasks such as essay writing and were also less supportive of such use by others. By contrast, students who viewed GenAI as enhancing their con­ trol over learning, such as through brainstorming, grammar support, or idea organiza­ tion, were more favorable toward its use. The authors argue that involving students in the co-creation of GenAI policies may strengthen their sense of agency and encour­ age more responsible engagement. Related work on technology acceptance and GenAI adoption supports this inter­ pretation. Shahzad et al. (2024) found that students’ perceived control over Chat­ GPT’s functionalities significantly influenced adoption intentions and positive attitudes, with agency playing an important mediating role through trust and per­ ceived intelligence. Similarly, Hsiao and Tang (2025) report that personal innova­ tiveness and self-efficacy are important individual predictors of students’ willingness to adopt GenAI-supported learning applications. Studies on technology-enhanced learning likewise show that students tend to respond more positively to technologies that support autonomy, ownership, and active participation than to those perceived as restrictive or externally imposed (Marín et al., 2020). Taken together, these",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,5,"learning applications. Studies on technology-enhanced learning likewise show that students tend to respond more positively to technologies that support autonomy, ownership, and active participation than to those perceived as restrictive or externally imposed (Marín et al., 2020). Taken together, these studies suggest that agency is a useful lens for understanding why students’ perceptions of GenAI are often ambivalent rather than simply positive or negative. GenAI can be experienced as empowering when it supports autonomy, confidence, and purposeful engagement, or as undermining when it encourages dependency, weakens ownership of learning, or is constrained by unclear institutional rules. Despite the rapid growth of research on student perceptions of GenAI, little attention has been given to how these perceptions relate to students’ actual learning approaches. This is a notable gap, because GenAI has a dual educational potential. It can be used either to accelerate task completion through superficial substitution, or to support conceptual exploration, reflection, and critical engagement. To analyze this distinction, the present study draws interpretively on the well-established concepts of deep and surface learning. A student may use GenAI in a",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,5,"accelerate task completion through superficial substitution, or to support conceptual exploration, reflection, and critical engagement. To analyze this distinction, the present study draws interpretively on the well-established concepts of deep and surface learning. A student may use GenAI in a surface-oriented way, for example to quickly generate text, paraphrase existing material, or obtain ready-made answers with minimal scrutiny. Equally, a student may use GenAI in a deep-oriented way, for example to clarify difficult concepts, compare explanations, test understand­ ing, generate counterarguments, or refine ideas through iterative dialogue. Existing literature increasingly hints at this duality, even when it does not explicitly frame it in terms of deep and surface learning. Studies on AI literacy, self-regulated learn­ ing, and metacognitive engagement suggest that students with stronger disciplinary",125
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,6,"1 3 8708 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 knowledge, higher AI literacy, and greater self-efficacy are more likely to critically evaluate outputs, use iterative prompting, and integrate GenAI into reflective learn­ ing processes, rather than treating it as a substitute for thinking (Chiu, 2024; Mirriahi et al., 2025; Zheng et al., 2025). The present study examines university students’ perceptions of the utility and value of GenAI, alongside their knowledge and attitudes toward these technologies, across two contrasting Swedish universities, one with a technological orientation and one with a broader academic profile. Rather than directly measuring learning approaches, the study uses the concepts of deep and surface learning as a sensitizing framework, a conceptual vocabulary for describing and contextualizing patterns in how students report using GenAI. Agency serves a similar interpretive function, helping to illumi­ nate how perceived autonomy and institutional constraints may relate to the ways stu­ dents engage with the technology. In doing so, the study contributes a comparative, empirically grounded account of student perceptions of GenAI, while acknowledging that connections to actual learning behavior remain speculative and",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,6,"institutional constraints may relate to the ways stu­ dents engage with the technology. In doing so, the study contributes a comparative, empirically grounded account of student perceptions of GenAI, while acknowledging that connections to actual learning behavior remain speculative and beyond the scope of the data collected. 3 Research questions and methodology This study was conducted at two universities in the south of Sweden: University A, a technically focused institution, and University B, a broader, more generalist university. The universities were selected through convenience sampling – Conve­ nience sampling acknowledged as a limitation, further discussed in Section 5.8 – owing to the authors’ institutional affiliations. Despite this, the sample is considered adequate for the study’s purposes, as the two institutions differ meaningfully in their educational focus areas, providing a sufficient sample frame for eliciting students’ attitudes, knowledge, and perceptions of GenAI in their education. The diversity of respondent contexts also offers insights into how students anticipate using the tech­ nology in their future careers. To address the study’s objective, we pose the following research questions: RQ1 What are students’ self-reported",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,6,"GenAI in their education. The diversity of respondent contexts also offers insights into how students anticipate using the tech­ nology in their future careers. To address the study’s objective, we pose the following research questions: RQ1 What are students’ self-reported levels of familiarity and proficiency with generative AI technologies? RQ2 To what extent do students from two different Swedish universities perceive the added value of GenAI to their studies? RQ3 What are students’ attitudes toward the increasing integration of GenAI in higher education? RQ4 How do students think GenAI will impact their future learning and aca­ demic assessment? 3.1 Data collection Data were collected through a questionnaire survey distributed to all students enrolled in degree programmes at the two universities. The survey consisted of 23 questions examining what, how, and why students use GenAI in their education. The question­ naire was developed following a top-down approach, in which the research objective",151
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,7,"1 3 8709 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 of exploring students’ use and knowledge of GenAI gave rise to the study’s four research questions, which in turn guided the creation of the survey items. These were informed by the authors’ knowledge of pedagogical frameworks for higher educa­ tion and their familiarity with the general use of GenAI, including its application in industrial practice. Additional inspiration was drawn from related works and from the authors’ previous joint pedagogical research. However, the questionnaire is consid­ ered bespoke for this study. The questions were developed iteratively, with each author contributing items that were incrementally refined through discussion and reflective analysis. Since GenAI misuse can be construed as academic misconduct according to university guidelines, the final draft was presented to the ethical Review Board at University B. Following feedback, a question was removed from the questionnaire regarding whether students had intentionally used GenAI when they knew it was not allowed. The questionnaire was developed during autumn 2024 with the aim of distributing it in early spring 2025 (Q1), taking into account that other",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,7,"from the questionnaire regarding whether students had intentionally used GenAI when they knew it was not allowed. The questionnaire was developed during autumn 2024 with the aim of distributing it in early spring 2025 (Q1), taking into account that other surveys were planned at University A during late spring 2025. The review process took longer than expected, which prevented a pilot study from being conducted. This introduces a potential threat to validity that is discussed in Section 5.8. The questionnaire began with an introductory section explaining the purpose of the study, a statement of respondent anonymity, and contact information for the researchers. The survey items consisted of 17 ten-point Likert-scale questions, three single-choice questions, one multiple-choice question, and two open-ended quali­ tative questions (see Appendix A). Likert-scale items ranged from totally disagree to totally agree and were intended for analysis using descriptive and frequentist statistics (Harpe, 2015). The open-ended questions provided insight into students’ perceptions. Fewer open-ended questions were included relative to the number of Likert-scale items in order to optimize the response rate by reducing completion time. The questionnaire",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,7,"using descriptive and frequentist statistics (Harpe, 2015). The open-ended questions provided insight into students’ perceptions. Fewer open-ended questions were included relative to the number of Likert-scale items in order to optimize the response rate by reducing completion time. The questionnaire was distributed electronically in February 2025 using the ArtoLogik Survey&Report system, chosen for its functionality and familiarity to both students and researchers through its use in course evaluations at both universi­ ties. It was available online for three weeks, and two reminder emails were sent to students: the first after one week and the second after two weeks. The sample frame consisted of 8,337 programme students (2,713 at University A and 5,624 at University B). A total of 1,097 responses were collected: 306 from University A (11.27%) and 791 from University B (14.06%). Although the response rates are low, the total number of responses is considered sufficient to draw general conclusions about students’ knowledge, perceptions, and attitudes toward GenAI in education. No significant difference was found between early and late respondents, reducing concerns about non-response bias. Limitations are discussed further",180
