DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,8,"merged into a single dataset, with university affiliation included as a categorical variable to enable group comparisons. Prior to analysis, the dataset was preprocessed to ensure consistency and analytical validity. Variable names and coding schemes were verified, system-defined missing values (coded as 999) were recoded as NA, and ""don’t know"" responses in Likert-scale items were treated as missing to avoid distortion in scale construction (Little and Rubin, 2019). Duplicate variables and non-informative columns were removed, and all variables were aligned with the questionnaire codebook. Only Likert-scale items were used for construct development and inferential analysis; remaining items were subjected to descriptive statistics or qualitative analysis. Constructs were initially defined based on the study’s theoretical framework and research questions, aiming to capture perceived learning benefit, institutional support and integration, and technical knowledge and competence. To evaluate and refine the construct structure, an exploratory factor analysis (EFA) was conducted on the Likert-scale items (Fabrigar et al., 1999). Factor extraction was performed using both principal axis factoring and maximum likelihood estimation, yielding consistent fac­ tor structures across methods. Varimax rotation was applied to",180
