DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,10,"the analyses. To assess potential non-response bias, an early-versus-late responder analysis was conducted separately for each university, using response order as a proxy for response timing. Responses were assumed to be approximately evenly distributed over the data collection period and were therefore partitioned into early and late groups based on the first and last quartiles of submissions. Closed-ended survey items were analyzed, excluding free-text responses and non-substantive codes (e.g., “don’t know” or missing values). Given the ordinal nature of most survey items, differences",83
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,11,"1 3 8713 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 between early and late respondents were evaluated using the Mann–Whitney U test, while categorical (binary) variables were analyzed using chi-square tests or Fisher’s exact tests where appropriate. To account for multiple comparisons across survey items, p-values were adjusted using the Benjamini–Hochberg false discovery rate (FDR) procedure. Statistical significance was assessed at inline-eq-IEq11 = 0.05. Although a small number of items showed unadjusted p-values below 0.05, none remained statistically significant after FDR correction in either dataset. These results indicate no systematic differences between early and late respondents, suggesting that non-response bias is unlikely to have materially influenced the findings. Descriptive statistics were calculated for each Likert-scale item, with responses categorized into agreement, neutral, and non-agreement groups to support initial comparisons. Questions with nominal response scales (Q1, Q2, and Q14) were ana­ lyzed descriptively only and omitted from inferential analysis. The two open-ended questions were analyzed using a combination of computa­ tional and manual qualitative methods. An initial inductive summary was generated using ChatGPT (GPT-5.3) to identify preliminary themes, which were subsequently verified",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,11,"descriptively only and omitted from inferential analysis. The two open-ended questions were analyzed using a combination of computa­ tional and manual qualitative methods. An initial inductive summary was generated using ChatGPT (GPT-5.3) to identify preliminary themes, which were subsequently verified and refined through manual thematic analysis. All responses were read care­ fully, meaningful elements were coded, and codes were grouped into broader themes capturing recurring patterns. These were compared with AI-generated patterns, and differences were discussed among the authors with reference to explicit quotations from the survey. To mitigate researcher bias, the analysis was conducted iteratively, reviewed by multiple authors, and elements of the Delphi method (Crisp et al., 1997) were applied to refine and align the final interpretation of results. 4 Findings A total of 1,080 student (out of 1,097) responses were included in the analysis, com­ prising participants from University A (n inline-eq-IEq12 280) and University B (n inline-eq-IEq13 760, with slight variation across variables due to missing data). Overall, students at both uni­ versities reported generally positive perceptions of GenAI, particularly with respect to perceived learning benefit",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,11,"from University A (n inline-eq-IEq12 280) and University B (n inline-eq-IEq13 760, with slight variation across variables due to missing data). Overall, students at both uni­ versities reported generally positive perceptions of GenAI, particularly with respect to perceived learning benefit and self-reported competence. Following reliability analysis and exploratory factor analysis, three composite constructs were retained for further analysis: perceived learning benefit, perceived institutional support and integration, and self-reported technical knowledge and com­ petence. Items that did not demonstrate sufficient internal consistency were analyzed individually. A visualization of the factor loading from the exploratory factor analysis is presented in Fig. 1. The analysis suggested a four-factor structure. However, since the subsequent reliability analysis indicated that one factor, related to future impact and concern, did not demonstrate sufficient internal consistency (Cronbach’s i nline-eq-IEq14 0.6), it was excluded as a composite construct and items analyzed individually. Perceived learning benefit Students at both universities reported high levels of per­ ceived learning benefit from GenAI. The mean score was slightly higher for Univer­ sity B (M = 7.73) than for University A (M = 7.54).",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,11,"items analyzed individually. Perceived learning benefit Students at both universities reported high levels of per­ ceived learning benefit from GenAI. The mean score was slightly higher for Univer­ sity B (M = 7.73) than for University A (M = 7.54). However, the Mann–Whitney U test indicated that this difference was not statistically significant (U = 97,166, p =",58
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,12,"1 3 8714 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 0.177), with a negligible effect size (rank-biserial r = -0.055). Regression analysis confirmed this result, showing a small and non-significant difference between univer­ sities (inline-eq-IEq15 = 0.19, 95% CI [-0.13, 0.52], p = 0.248, Rinline-eq-IEq16 = 0.001). Perceived institutional support and integration A statistically significant difference was observed in perceived institutional support and integration of GenAI. Students at University A reported higher levels (M = 3.61) than students at University B (M = 3.06). The Mann–Whitney U test confirmed this difference (U = 121,617, p < 0.001), although the effect size was small (rank-biserial r = 0.139). Regression analysis yielded consistent results, indicating lower perceived support among students at Uni­ versity B (inline-eq-IEq17 = -0.56, 95% CI [-0.86, -0.25], p < 0.001, Rinline-eq-IEq18 = 0.012). Self-reported technical knowledge and competence Self-reported technical knowl­ edge and competence did not differ meaningfully between the two institutions. The mean score was 6.32 for University A and 6.36 for University B. The Mann–Whitney U test showed no statistically significant difference (U = 103,977, p =",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,12,"technical knowl­ edge and competence did not differ meaningfully between the two institutions. The mean score was 6.32 for University A and 6.36 for University B. The Mann–Whitney U test showed no statistically significant difference (U = 103,977, p = 0.535), with a negligible effect size (rank-biserial r = -0.025). Regression analysis supported this finding (inline-eq-IEq19 = 0.04, 95% CI [-0.27, 0.36], p = 0.786, Rinline-eq-IEq20 < 0.001). Fig. 1 Rotated factor loadings for Likert-scale items included in the exploratory factor analysis. The heatmap illustrates the clustering of items into interpretable factors, supporting the construct structure used in the subsequent analyses",101
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,13,"1 3 8715 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 A visualization of mean construct score comparisons, between Univerity A and Uni­ versity B, is shown in Fig. 2. Several items did not form reliable constructs based on internal consistency and factor analysis. As noted, these items were analyzed individually to provide addi­ tional insight into specific aspects of students’ perceptions. Students’ views on whether GenAI could replace or complement traditional teaching, addressed in RQ4, did not form a coherent scale and were analyzed sepa­ rately. Across both institutions, students tended to rate GenAI more strongly as a complement rather than a replacement for traditional teaching. The minor differences observed between universities were not statistically significant and effect sizes were small, indicating broadly similar perceptions across contexts. Items related to institutional rules and constraints (e.g., awareness of rules and perceived limitations on use) did not demonstrate sufficient internal consistency to form a composite measure. Analysis of these items suggested variation in students’ awareness and perceptions of institutional restrictions; however, differences between universities were modest and did not consistently reach statistical significance.",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,13,"on use) did not demonstrate sufficient internal consistency to form a composite measure. Analysis of these items suggested variation in students’ awareness and perceptions of institutional restrictions; however, differences between universities were modest and did not consistently reach statistical significance. This indicates that while institutional policies may differ, students’ perceptions of rules and constraints are not strongly differentiated between the two contexts. The item capturing whether students reported using GenAI beyond formal require­ ments was also analyzed independently. Responses indicated that a substantial pro­ portion of students engaged with GenAI outside explicitly assigned coursework, with no clear or consistent difference between universities. In summary, the results indicate a high degree of similarity between the two uni­ versities in students’ perceived learning benefit and self-reported technical knowl­ edge and competence related to GenAI. These similarities were reflected in both Fig. 2 Mean construct scores by university for perceived learning benefit, perceived institutional sup­ port and integration, and self-reported technical knowledge and competence. Error bars represent stan­ dard errors. Differences are statistically significant only for institutional support",175
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,13,"Mean construct scores by university for perceived learning benefit, perceived institutional sup­ port and integration, and self-reported technical knowledge and competence. Error bars represent stan­ dard errors. Differences are statistically significant only for institutional support",35
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,14,"1 3 8716 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 non-parametric and regression analyses, with negligible effect sizes. The only sta­ tistically significant difference identified concerned perceived institutional support and integration, with students at University A reporting higher levels than those at University B. However, this difference was small in magnitude and explained only a limited proportion of variance. Item-level analyses further suggest that students across both institutions share similar views regarding the role of GenAI in education, particularly in viewing it as a complement rather than a replacement for traditional teaching, and in their engage­ ment with the technology beyond formal institutional requirements. For questions employing a nominal scale (i.e., Q1, Q2, and Q14), the findings indicate that the majority of students at both universities either engaged with GenAI (GenAI) sporadically for information retrieval or used it weekly to support their aca­ demic studies. Furthermore, most students reported moderate trust in AI-generated outputs, and a majority reported using GenAI outside their educational context on a weekly basis. 4.1 RQ1: What are students’ self-reported levels of familiarity and proficiency with GenAI",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,14,"their aca­ demic studies. Furthermore, most students reported moderate trust in AI-generated outputs, and a majority reported using GenAI outside their educational context on a weekly basis. 4.1 RQ1: What are students’ self-reported levels of familiarity and proficiency with GenAI technologies? Despite variations in the frequency of GenAI use, the most common use cases were diverse. The top three, based on number of responses, were (1) information retrieval (N=689), (2) text refinement (N=508), and (3) text analysis (N=429). Additional use cases, provided through free-text answers in Q3, included idea generation, transla­ tion, comprehension of tasks or information, and explanations of tasks or informa­ tion. Analysis of free-text responses indicates that students frequently used GenAI as a virtual tutor, using it for immediate support and guidance in problem-solving. One respondent described their experience as follows: “I utilize it as a collaborative part­ ner, posing questions, seeking opinions, and engaging in broad discussions.” Another emphasized its explanatory role, stating: “It aids in contextual understanding, clari­ fies distinctions between concepts, and provides illustrative examples for challenging situations or ideas.” In addition, students reported",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,14,"part­ ner, posing questions, seeking opinions, and engaging in broad discussions.” Another emphasized its explanatory role, stating: “It aids in contextual understanding, clari­ fies distinctions between concepts, and provides illustrative examples for challenging situations or ideas.” In addition, students reported using several GenAI models or tools, as exemplified by one respondent’s statement: “I use OpenAI, Claude, Perplexity, and various local models via HuggingFace, continually exploring their functionalities.” On average, students reported using 1.4 distinct GenAI tools (standard deviation 1.17) and Chat­ GPT was the most frequently cited tool, followed by Copilot. The construct analysis shows that students at University A reported statistically higher levels of institutional support for GenAI. However, the mean support rating ranged from 4.86 at University A to 2.7 at University B. These findings indicate that while a majority of students engage with GenAI,universities provide only a moderate to low level of support in terms of knowledge, methodologies, or tools.",153
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,15,"1 3 8717 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 4.2 RQ2: To what extent do students from two different Swedish universities perceive the added value of GenAI to their studies? Figure 3 shows the distribution of University A’s student responses to question Q6, indicating that the majority of students (66%) reported good knowledge (response above 5) of how to use GenAI in their education. Similarly, Fig. 4 shows that 66% of students from University B reported that they know how to use GenAI in their edu­ cation. These descriptive results are complemented by qualitative analysis of Q15, which explored why students use GenAI in their studies and yielded approximately 1,078 free-text responses. An inductive thematic analysis was conducted, resulting in four themes that capture distinct patterns in the data. Responses were coded into more than one theme, reflecting the multifaceted ways in which students engage with GenAI. Fig. 4 Distribution of student responses from University B regarding student self-reported knowledge of how to use GenAI for their studies (N=791) Fig. 3 Distribution of student responses from University A regarding student",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,15,ways in which students engage with GenAI. Fig. 4 Distribution of student responses from University B regarding student self-reported knowledge of how to use GenAI for their studies (N=791) Fig. 3 Distribution of student responses from University A regarding student self-reported knowledge of how to use GenAI for their studies (N=306),51
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,16,"1 3 8718 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 GenAI as a study aid The most prominent theme concerns students using GenAI as a tool to become more efficient and productive, and as a general study support tool. Use cases included using GenAI to quickly retrieve information, gain overviews of topics, or get started with assignments. In this theme, GenAI is primarily positioned as a way to complete tasks more quickly rather than as a tool for reflection or con­ ceptual exploration. Students frequently compared GenAI with traditional search engines, emphasizing its speed and convenience. Approximately 705 responses were associated with this theme, including references to general study support, informa­ tion retrieval and time efficiency. Representative comments include: ""It’s much faster than searching for information myself."" and ""I mainly use AI to save time and get an overview"". GenAI as a tool for understanding and cognitive support The second theme cap­ tures students use of GenAI to support understanding and conceptual clarification. Students emphasized GenAI’s ability to explain difficult concepts, rephrase course content in simpler terms, and provide alternative explanations",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,16,"a tool for understanding and cognitive support The second theme cap­ tures students use of GenAI to support understanding and conceptual clarification. Students emphasized GenAI’s ability to explain difficult concepts, rephrase course content in simpler terms, and provide alternative explanations when course literature or lectures were difficult to understand. In contrast to the efficiency-oriented theme, students described a more dialogic and interactive use of GenAI, often comparing it to a private tutor available on demand. Approximately 277 responses were coded into this theme. Illustrative comments include: ""It explains things in a way that makes sense to me."" and ""I use it to understand concepts I struggle with in the course"". GenAI as a writing, reflection, and dialogic partner The third theme highlights GenAI’s role in academic writing, reflection and cognitive support. Students reported using GenAI to improve language and grammar, organize text structure, develop arguments, generate ideas and receive feedback on drafts. Several respondents explicitly described GenAI as a critical reader, mentor, or discussion partner. This theme reflects a more proactive use of GenAI, where students shape and evaluate AI-generated",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,16,"organize text structure, develop arguments, generate ideas and receive feedback on drafts. Several respondents explicitly described GenAI as a critical reader, mentor, or discussion partner. This theme reflects a more proactive use of GenAI, where students shape and evaluate AI-generated output instead of passively accepting it. Approximately 375 responses were associated with this theme. Examples include: ""I use it as a critical reader of my text."" and ""It helps me reflect on my arguments and structure my reasoning"". One student described it as ""I use AI as a ’study buddy’ when I can’t be on campus to study with others. I believe I learn better through conversations with other people about dif­ ferent topics, and AI can be a good substitute when I’m studying alone."" Skepticism, contraints and non-use of GenAI The final theme captures students expressing skepticism toward GenAI or choosing complete non-use. A substantial group of students reported that they do not use GenAI at all, citing concern related to academic integrity, ethical considerations, lack of trust in AI-generated outputs, or uncertainty about university rules. Approximately 230 responses",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,16,"or choosing complete non-use. A substantial group of students reported that they do not use GenAI at all, citing concern related to academic integrity, ethical considerations, lack of trust in AI-generated outputs, or uncertainty about university rules. Approximately 230 responses were coded into this theme. Representative statements include: ""I don’t trust the answers."" and ""I avoid AI because I’m unsure if it counts as cheating"". One student elaborated: ""After learning about the technology and how it works, I’ve chosen not to use AI. As it stands, the technology is too unreliable and actually gets in the way of my learning and under­ standing, especially when it comes to sources. Another factor is ethical concerns, as well as the large amount of energy it consumes"".",124
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,17,"1 3 8719 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 4.3 RQ3: What are students’ attitudes toward the increasing integration of GenAI in higher education? The results indicate that most students use GenAI to complete assignments (mean score 5.67 at University A and 6.3 at University B). Students also demonstrated a measured attitude towards GenAI output, reporting that they trust it only to some extent. This cautious stance is also reflected in their knowledge of the rules governing GenAI use (Q11). The results show some difference in this regard, with students at University A reporting slightly greater familiarity with the applicable rules. However, students at both institutions perceive these rules as restricting their use of GenAI in education, suggesting they see more potential use cases for the technology than are currently permitted (Q12). Students did not regard GenAI as a replacement for traditional teaching (Q19). While the statistical analysis does not allow us to conclude that it is therefore per­ ceived as a complement, the mean response to the question of whether GenAI is a suitable complement to traditional teaching (Q20)",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,17,"for traditional teaching (Q19). While the statistical analysis does not allow us to conclude that it is therefore per­ ceived as a complement, the mean response to the question of whether GenAI is a suitable complement to traditional teaching (Q20) was 8.00 at University A and 7.79 at University B, suggesting broad agreement with this view. Students at both institu­ tions also perceived GenAI as likely to affect their future careers, with mean scores of 8.01 at University A and 7.78 at University B. At the same time, students indicated moderate concern that GenAI will affect their careers, with students at University A expressing slightly greater worry (mean 5.63) than those at University B (mean 5.11). Regarding ethical perspectives, students at both universities reported that they had reflected on the ethical implications of GenAI (mean 7.11 at University A and 7.13 at University B), as measured by Q23. 4.4 RQ4: How do students think GenAI will impact the future of learning and academic assessment? Students at both University A and University B reported that GenAI will have an influence on",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,17,"7.13 at University B), as measured by Q23. 4.4 RQ4: How do students think GenAI will impact the future of learning and academic assessment? Students at both University A and University B reported that GenAI will have an influence on their future professional careers, with students at University A viewing its impact as slightly more significant than those at University B. Regarding the use of GenAI for completing assignments (Q10), students expressed moderate agree­ ment, with mean scores of 5.67 at University A and 6.31 at University B, indicat­ ing a cautious acceptance of the technology in this context. However, institutional restrictions on permissible uses appear to constrain its potential benefits and suggest a desire for greater flexibility among students. Their responses reinforce this view, revealing limited agreement that they have received adequate training or been given GenAI-focused assignments. While the data do not explicitly signal a demand for more such assignments, students reported limited opportunities to engage with the technology through coursework. Although these items do not combine into formal constructs, considered individually they suggest that students view GenAI",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,17,"the data do not explicitly signal a demand for more such assignments, students reported limited opportunities to engage with the technology through coursework. Although these items do not combine into formal constructs, considered individually they suggest that students view GenAI as an inte­ gral part of their future.",48
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,18,"1 3 8720 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 5 Discussion and contributions This study investigated students’ perceptions and use of GenAI across two university contexts, focusing on perceived learning benefit, institutional support and integra­ tion, and self-reported technical knowledge and competence. By combining theory- driven construct development with empirical validation through reliability analysis and exploratory factor analysis, the study provides a structured and methodologically robust assessment of these dimensions. 5.1 Perceived learning benefit and student practices A central finding is the consistency across institutions in perceived learning benefit. Students in both contexts reported relatively high levels of perceived support in terms of efficiency, overview, and understanding, with no statistically significant differ­ ences and negligible effect sizes. This suggests that these perceptions are not strongly shaped by institutional context, but rather by student-level factors such as individual learning strategies and task-specific needs. Quantitative results further show strong agreement that GenAI enhances time- efficiency (Q16) and provides helpful overviews (Q17), while perceived support for understanding (Q18) received comparatively lower, though still positive, agree­ ment. This pattern suggests that students primarily perceive",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,18,"task-specific needs. Quantitative results further show strong agreement that GenAI enhances time- efficiency (Q16) and provides helpful overviews (Q17), while perceived support for understanding (Q18) received comparatively lower, though still positive, agree­ ment. This pattern suggests that students primarily perceive GenAI as beneficial for efficiency-oriented tasks such as rapid information retrieval, text refinement, and task completion. While this pattern is consistent with what the literature describes as surface-oriented use, it is important to note that these items capture perceived benefit rather than observed learning behavior, and no direct inference about stu­ dents’ actual learning approaches can be drawn from these scores alone. Qualitative findings provide additional texture. The dominant theme, ""GenAI as a study aid,"" describes GenAI as an efficient support tool valued for productivity and broad com­ prehension, a pattern of described use that resembles what the literature associates with surface-oriented engagement. A smaller subset of responses describes more dia­ logic and iterative use, such as treating GenAI as a virtual tutor or discussion partner, as captured in the theme ""GenAI as a tool for understanding and cognitive support.""",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,18,"with surface-oriented engagement. A smaller subset of responses describes more dia­ logic and iterative use, such as treating GenAI as a virtual tutor or discussion partner, as captured in the theme ""GenAI as a tool for understanding and cognitive support."" These described uses are consistent with what might support deeper engagement, though whether they reflect deeper learning in practice cannot be determined from self-reported data alone. From a theoretical perspective, these findings align with the view that students integrate new technologies into existing learning practices in ways that complement rather than replace them. Importantly, the construct captures perceived learning ben­ efit rather than objective outcomes, and should therefore be interpreted as reflecting students’ subjective experiences rather than measurable performance gains. 5.2 Institutional support and its limited influence In contrast to the other constructs, perceived institutional support and integration differed significantly between universities, with students at University A reporting higher levels of access, training, and curricular integration. This indicates that insti­",160
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,19,"1 3 8721 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 tutional policies can influence the visibility and formal support of GenAI. However, the effect size was small and regression analysis showed that university affiliation explains only a limited proportion of variance. Moreover, item-level analyses of rules and constraints revealed no substantial differences between institutions, and these items did not form reliable constructs. This suggests that while institutional structures differ formally, they may have limited influence on students’ everyday engagement with GenAI. Students also reported limited formal training and relatively few GenAI-focused assignments (Q9–Q10), while perceiving institutional rules as constraining beneficial use of the technology (Q13), which in turn appeared to impede their sense of agency. This indicates a gap between institutional provision and actual student practices. 5.3 Technical knowledge and informal learning No meaningful differences were found between universities in self-reported technical knowledge and competence. Students across both institutions reported similar levels of understanding of how GenAI works and how to use it in their studies. This finding reflects the widespread accessibility of GenAI tools, which are largely independent of institutional",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,19,"technical knowledge and competence. Students across both institutions reported similar levels of understanding of how GenAI works and how to use it in their studies. This finding reflects the widespread accessibility of GenAI tools, which are largely independent of institutional systems and require minimal formal training. It suggests that com­ petence is primarily developed through informal, self-directed learning processes, reinforcing the interpretation of GenAI as a general-purpose technology not strongly tied to formal educational provision. 5.4 Agency Although student agency was not directly measured through validated scales, it pro­ vides an important interpretive lens for several observed patterns (Chan and Hu, 2023; Marín et al., 2020). Students demonstrated considerable autonomy through self-taught proficiency, proactive exploration of tools, and the use of GenAI beyond formal requirements. At the same time, the inability of items related to rules, con­ straints, and usage beyond assignments to form reliable constructs highlights the heterogeneity of student practices. Rather than representing a limitation alone, this suggests that engagement with GenAI is complex and not reducible to a single dimen­ sion such as ""agency"" or ""constraint"". Students",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,19,"beyond assignments to form reliable constructs highlights the heterogeneity of student practices. Rather than representing a limitation alone, this suggests that engagement with GenAI is complex and not reducible to a single dimen­ sion such as ""agency"" or ""constraint"". Students simultaneously navigate institutional expectations, informal norms, and personal learning strategies. 5.5 Implications for educational practice The findings suggest that GenAI is already embedded in student learning practices in ways that are relatively independent of institutional context. While institutions influence visibility and formal integration, they play a limited role in shaping per­ ceived learning benefits and competence. This has several implications. First, institu­ tions should move beyond access provision toward supporting effective, critical, and responsible use. Second, the importance of informal learning highlights the need to align pedagogical strategies with actual student practices. Third, educational design should aim to support deeper engagement emphasizing critical evaluation of AI-gen­",146
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,20,"1 3 8722 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 erated content, encouraging reflection on learning processes, promoting synthesis across sources, and designing assessments that prioritize higher-order thinking. Such approaches may help reposition GenAI as a tool for conceptual understanding rather than merely a productivity aid. Finally, students’ moderate concern about career implications (RQ4) suggests a need for institutions to better address the societal and professional impact of GenAI, thereby preparing students as active participants in an evolving technological landscapes. 5.6 Methodological contributions A key contribution of this study lies in its analytical rigor. Rather than aggregat­ ing Likert-scale items without validation, the study employed reliability analysis and exploratory factor analysis to derive empirically grounded constructs. Equally important, items that did not meet internal consistency criteria were ana­ lyzed at the item level rather than forced into composite measures. This increases transparency and avoids imposing artificial structure on complex phenomena. The low reliability of the future impact and concern construct (Cronbach’s inline-eq-IEq21 = 0.508) deserves particular attention. Rather than treating this solely as a measurement fail­ ure, it can be read",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,20,"transparency and avoids imposing artificial structure on complex phenomena. The low reliability of the future impact and concern construct (Cronbach’s inline-eq-IEq21 = 0.508) deserves particular attention. Rather than treating this solely as a measurement fail­ ure, it can be read as a substantively meaningful result. Students’ attitudes toward the future implications of GenAI appear to be multidimensional and internally inconsis­ tent in the way that distinguishes them from constructs such as perceived learning benefit or technical competence. A student may simultaneously anticipate significant professional impact and feel relatively unconcerned about it, or may view GenAI as a valuable complement to teaching while remaining anxious about career implications. This fragmentation of attitudes around future impact may reflect the genuine uncer­ tainty and novelty of GenAI’s role in professional life, where students lack stable reference points for forming coherent evaluative stances. While item-level analysis preserves some insight into these dimensions, the inability to form a reliable com­ posite construct limits the study’s capacity to draw stronger conclusions about how students collectively orient toward the future implications of GenAI. This represents a boundary",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,20,"item-level analysis preserves some insight into these dimensions, the inability to form a reliable com­ posite construct limits the study’s capacity to draw stronger conclusions about how students collectively orient toward the future implications of GenAI. This represents a boundary of the present study that future work, using purpose-built instruments for measuring attitudes toward AI’s professional and societal impact, would be well positioned to address. The use of non-parametric testing, effect size estimation, and regression analy­ sis provides complementary perspectives on statistical and practical significance, strengthening the robustness of the findings. 5.7 Limitations Several limitations should be acknowledged. The study relies on self-reported data and does not measure objective learning outcomes. Additionally, the sample is limited to two universities within a specific national context, which may affect generalizability. Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the study relies on self-reported data and does not measure objective learning outcomes. Second, the sample is limited to two universities within a specific national context, which may affect generalizabil­",164
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,21,"1 3 8723 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 ity. Third, regression models were limited to bivariate comparisons, which, although considered appropriate given the exploratory nature of this study, could have been expanded with additional variables of greater explanatory power. textcolorblueSuch analysis falls outside the scope of the present study but represents a natural direc­ tion for future work. Moreover, the inability to construct reliable scales for certain dimensions limits higher-level abstraction of these aspects, although it also reveals important heterogeneity in student practices. Furthermore, regression models were limited to bivariate comparisons, which, although perceived suitable given the exploratory nature of this study, could have been expanded with additional variables of more explanatory power. Such analysis is however out-of-scope for this study, but could be entertained in the future. Finally, while the concepts of deep and surface learning inform the interpretive framing of this study, it should be explicitly noted that learning approaches were not directly measured. The study does not include validated instruments for assessing whether students adopt deep or surface approaches to learning, and no causal or behavioral",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,21,"interpretive framing of this study, it should be explicitly noted that learning approaches were not directly measured. The study does not include validated instruments for assessing whether students adopt deep or surface approaches to learning, and no causal or behavioral conclusions about learning quality can be drawn from the self-reported perceptions and described use patterns collected here. Connections between reported GenAI use and learning approaches are therefore speculative and should be treated as hypoth­ eses for future investigation rather than as findings of the present study. As a result, findings related to students’ perceptions of GenAI’s future professional impact and associated concerns should be interpreted with particular caution, as they reflect item-level responses rather than a validated attitudinal construct. Developing and validating purpose-built scales for measuring students’ orientations toward the longer-term professional and societal implications of GenAI represents an important methodological priority for future research in this area. 5.8 Threats to validity Internal validity Although the questionnaire yielded over one thousand responses (N = 1,097), the response rate was relatively low (11.27% at University A and 14.06% at University B).",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,21,"methodological priority for future research in this area. 5.8 Threats to validity Internal validity Although the questionnaire yielded over one thousand responses (N = 1,097), the response rate was relatively low (11.27% at University A and 14.06% at University B). This raises the possibility of non-response bias, where respondents may represent more intrinsically motivated or technologically engaged students (Groves, 2006). To investigate the possibility of non-response bias, a Mann-Whitney U test (as described in Section 3.2) was performed. The test showed no statistical difference between early and late respondents, indicating a low but not negligible probability of non-response bias. Furthermore, due to time constraints, it was not possible to pilot the survey. This introduces the possibility of question ambiguity and the risk that respondents may have misinterpreted some items. We consider this threat minor, given the authors’ rig­ orous review of the questionnaire and the additional scrutiny provided by the ethical review board at University B. On this basis, we argue that systematic misinterpreta­ tion of the survey questions (available for review in Appendix A) is unlikely to have affected",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,21,"of the questionnaire and the additional scrutiny provided by the ethical review board at University B. On this basis, we argue that systematic misinterpreta­ tion of the survey questions (available for review in Appendix A) is unlikely to have affected either the statistical or the qualitative analysis. In addition, the study relies on self-reported perceptions rather than objective measures of learning outcomes, which",63
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,22,"1 3 8724 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 may introduce reporting bias. The inability to construct reliable composite constructs for certain dimensions (e.g., future impact and concerns) further reflects measure­ ment challenges and limits the internal consistency of some aspects of the analysis. However, analyzing these items at the individual level partially mitigates this issue by preserving nuance. External validity The generalizability of the findings may be limited by convenience sampling bias (Bethlehem, 2010). The participating universities, while differing in orientation, may not represent broader institutional contexts in terms of policies, culture, or technological infrastructure. Nevertheless, the inclusion of students from diverse disciplines (e.g., software engineering, nursing, criminology, and economics) increases variability and supports broader relevance. Given the rapid evolution of GenAI, the results should be interpreted as a snapshot of current adoption patterns, which may shift as technologies and practices mature. Conclusion validity The study combines quantitative analysis with qualitative insights from a limited number of open-ended questions. While analytical triangu­ lation strengthens interpretation, there remains a risk of researcher bias in linking qualitative themes to quantitative findings.",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,22,"and practices mature. Conclusion validity The study combines quantitative analysis with qualitative insights from a limited number of open-ended questions. While analytical triangu­ lation strengthens interpretation, there remains a risk of researcher bias in linking qualitative themes to quantitative findings. To mitigate this risk integration of data is primarily restricted to the discussion section of the paper, allowing more a more interpretative reading of the results. This approach was strengthened further by com­ parison to AI-generated results (GPT-5.3), enabling the identification of similarities and differences in identified patterns. All differences were discussed, reviewed and decided upon by the authors. However, prior biases in sampling and measurement may still influence conclusions, as such biases would be inherent to the data itself and cannot be fully excluded. he findings should therefore be interpreted as indica­ tive rather than definitive, particularly given the evolving nature of GenAI use in education. We also recognize a potential threat to the statistical analysis arising from the GenAI- supported analytical procedure. Although all steps, including the code, have been thoroughly reviewed, recalculated on multiple occasions, and confirmed",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,22,"evolving nature of GenAI use in education. We also recognize a potential threat to the statistical analysis arising from the GenAI- supported analytical procedure. Although all steps, including the code, have been thoroughly reviewed, recalculated on multiple occasions, and confirmed across mul­ tiple methods, minor errors cannot be fully excluded. However, given the consistent results across all analytical approaches, we are confident that any such errors have not influenced the conclusions. 6 Conclusions Generative AI (GenAI), including tools such as ChatGPT and Copilot, is increas­ ingly present across a wide range of domains, including higher education. Although its use among students and teachers appears to be growing, there remains a need for empirical understanding of how students use these tools, how they perceive their role in learning, and how they believe GenAI may affect their future professional lives. This survey study, based on responses from 1,097 students at two Swedish uni­ versities with different teaching profiles, found no statistically significant differences",161
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,23,"1 3 8725 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 between the student groups across the main validated constructs. However, although the sample is adequate to draw the study’s conclusions, the findings are perceived to have generalizability constraints due to the convenience sampling of the two partici­ pating universities and low response rates (11.27% from University A and 14.06% from University B). Regardless, the findings suggest that GenAI is already integrated into many students’ study practices, but that this use is largely self-directed and informally developed rather than supported through structured institutional teaching. While universities may provide access to GenAI tools, students reported limited for­ mal training and limited curricular integration. At the same time, the results show that students use GenAI in multiple ways, including for information retrieval, text refine­ ment, and analysis, and in some cases as a discussion partner or tutor-like support. The results further indicate that students primarily perceive GenAI as supporting efficiency and providing overviews, while perceived support for deeper conceptual understanding appears more conditional. Together with the qualitative findings, this suggests that students predominantly describe",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,23,"partner or tutor-like support. The results further indicate that students primarily perceive GenAI as supporting efficiency and providing overviews, while perceived support for deeper conceptual understanding appears more conditional. Together with the qualitative findings, this suggests that students predominantly describe GenAI as a general study aid, while a subset describes uses that are more dialogic and iterative in character. Whether these different patterns of use correspond to meaningfully different learning outcomes or approaches was not assessed in this study and remains an open question for future research. At the same time, students reported only moderate trust in GenAI outputs, suggesting an awareness of both its usefulness and its limitations. This highlights the importance of avoiding overreliance and of developing students’ ability to critically assess the accuracy, relevance, and reliability of AI-generated content. Taken together, the findings suggest that GenAI may function as a complement to, rather than a replacement for, disciplinary teaching and foundational learning. Students’ ability to use GenAI productively appears to depend not only on access to the technology, but also on their ability to formulate meaningful prompts,",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,23,"function as a complement to, rather than a replacement for, disciplinary teaching and foundational learning. Students’ ability to use GenAI productively appears to depend not only on access to the technology, but also on their ability to formulate meaningful prompts, interpret responses, compare outputs with other sources, and judge when GenAI is appropri­ ate to use. This points to a more specific set of educational implications than simply expanding general AI literacy. More specifically, the findings point to two pedagogical priorities. The first is strengthening students’ critical evaluation of GenAI outputs in order to mitigate over­ reliance and support informed assessment of AI-generated content. The second is designing learning activities that structure collaborative human–AI workflows, rather than treating GenAI as either a substitute for disciplinary reasoning or an unregulated productivity tool. Both priorities call for teaching practices that emphasize source verification, comparison of AI-generated and non-AI sources, and reflection on the reliability and limitations of generated content, as well as learning activities in which GenAI is used as a discussion partner, feedback tool, or iterative scaffolding resource rather than as",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,23,"verification, comparison of AI-generated and non-AI sources, and reflection on the reliability and limitations of generated content, as well as learning activities in which GenAI is used as a discussion partner, feedback tool, or iterative scaffolding resource rather than as a substitute for independent analysis. In addition, assessment design and institutional guidance should more clearly reflect how students are already using GenAI in practice. This may involve creat­ ing assignments that require documentation and reflection on how GenAI was used, encouraging iterative prompting and critique, and co-developing clearer guide­ lines that distinguish between acceptable support, overreliance, and misuse. Such approaches may better align institutional policy with student practice while support­ ing more reflective and responsible engagement with the technology.",119
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,24,"1 3 8726 Education and Information Technologies (2026) 31:8703–8730 The findings also suggest that GenAI use in higher education can be understood across interconnected student, institutional, and system-level dimensions. At the stu­ dent level, the results highlight how perceptions, attitudes, AI literacy, self-efficacy, and agency may be associated with whether students describe using GenAI primarily for efficiency and task completion or in more dialogic and exploratory ways. Whether these differences in described use reflect meaningfully different learning approaches or outcomes remains beyond what the present data can establish, and represents an important direction for future empirical work. At the institutional level, students’ practices appear to be strongly conditioned by disciplinary norms, assessment design, policy clarity, and the extent of teacher guidance. At the system level, the findings reflect broader shifts in higher education, where GenAI challenges conventional assumptions about originality, places greater emphasis on learning processes rather than products alone, and reinforces the need for institutions to prioritize meaningful learning over purely credential-oriented performance. Viewed in this way, GenAI is not only a tool adopted by individual students, but also",180
DOC_047,s10639-026-14100-2.pdf,10.1007/s10639-026-14100-2,24,"places greater emphasis on learning processes rather than products alone, and reinforces the need for institutions to prioritize meaningful learning over purely credential-oriented performance. Viewed in this way, GenAI is not only a tool adopted by individual students, but also a catalyst for reconsidering how higher education supports learning, judgment, and academic development in increasingly AI-mediated environments. Overall, the findings suggest a need for pedagogical and institutional responses that move beyond access provision alone. By focusing on critical evaluation, struc­ tured human–AI workflows, and clearer alignment between policy and actual stu­ dent use, higher education institutions may better support students in engaging with GenAI in ways that strengthen learning, preserve academic integrity, and prepare them for future professional contexts. Appendix A No. Question (English translation) Response type 1 To what extent have you used GenAI in your studies? 6-point frequency scale (Never inline-eq-IEq22 Daily + Don’t know) 2 To what extent do you trust the results from GenAI? 5-point extent scale (Not at all inline-eq-IEq23 Fully + Don’t know) 3.1 In which areas have you used GenAI in your studies",180
