DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,1,"Journal of Academic Ethics (2026) 24:82 https://doi.org/10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI Among Undergraduate Students Janet M. Reed1 · Richard E. Ferdig2 · Tracy M. Dodson1 · John Gunstad3 · Joel W. Hughes3 Received: 27 February 2026 / Accepted: 3 August 2026 / Published online: 19 August 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is increasingly embedded in higher education, shaping how students learn and obtain knowledge. Although AI education is expanding, limited empirical research has examined how undergraduate students exercise ethical judgment about using GenAI in academic situations, particularly in relation to their AI literacy. This study examined undergraduate students’ ethical judgment regarding GenAI use in educational contexts and its relationship to AI literacy and student demographics. A cross-sectional survey was conducted with a convenience sample (n = 531) recruited through two psychology courses at one public Midwestern university. The survey included demographics, six GenAI ethical scenarios, and the Generative AI Literacy Assessment Test (GLAT). Participants generally demonstrated high agreement when classifying the six predesignated scenarios, distinguishing GenAI use that supported learning",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,1,"psychology courses at one public Midwestern university. The survey included demographics, six GenAI ethical scenarios, and the Generative AI Literacy Assessment Test (GLAT). Participants generally demonstrated high agreement when classifying the six predesignated scenarios, distinguishing GenAI use that supported learning from use that substituted AI-generated content for original academic work. 57.6% of students correctly classified all six scenarios, indicating a pronounced ceiling effect, with uncertainty greatest when AI-generated content was minimally modified before submission. AI literacy demonstrated a small positive association with classification accuracy, Spearman’s ρ = 0.234, p < .001, and emerged as a significant predictor across the six scenarios. In the adjusted binomial model, each additional correct response on the objective AI literacy measure was associated with a 13.9% increase in the modeled odds of correctly classifying an ethical scenario. By focusing on applied ethical judgment in academic scenarios, this study highlights the need to prospectively evaluate how AI literacy instruction and ethics education may support undergraduate students’ preparation for future professional practice. Keywords Generative AI · AI literacy · Ethics · Higher education · Academic integrity ·",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,1,"scenarios, this study highlights the need to prospectively evaluate how AI literacy instruction and ethics education may support undergraduate students’ preparation for future professional practice. Keywords Generative AI · AI literacy · Ethics · Higher education · Academic integrity · Digital ethics Janet M. Reed Jreed56@kent.edu 1 College of Nursing, Kent State University, Kent, OH, USA 2 College of Aeronautics and Engineering, Kent State University, Kent, OH, USA 3 Department of Psychological Sciences, Kent State University, Kent, OH, USA",79
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,2,"1 3 82 Page 2 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is reshaping how students learn, create knowledge, and demonstrate competency in higher education, presenting both opportunities and ethical challenges (Nguyen, 2025). For many students, the transition into higher education represents their first sustained context for making independent decisions about GenAI use (An et al., 2025; Uanachain & Aouad, 2025). Ethical judgments on the appropriate use of AI formed during this transition period may impact teacher-student relationships, with potential for both positive and negative outcomes (i.e., plagiarism; see Hadinejad et al., 2025). Digital ethics, defined as the moral principles guiding responsible use of digital technologies, are increasingly important as teachers and students navigate the ethics of AI use in academia. Moreover, as GenAI raises questions about originality, critical thinking, and the boundaries of intellectual work, there is a critical need for shared ethical frameworks in higher education (Samala et al., 2025). As students transition into colleges and universities, they are immersed not only in knowledge acquisition but also formative environments where students develop ethical norms",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,2,"there is a critical need for shared ethical frameworks in higher education (Samala et al., 2025). As students transition into colleges and universities, they are immersed not only in knowledge acquisition but also formative environments where students develop ethical norms related to technology use. Decision-making patterns established in academic contexts are particularly important in fields incorporating AI that rely on human judgment, accountability, and trust, including psychology, medicine, nursing, counseling, social work, education, business, and public administration (Abuadas et al., 2025; Reamer, 2023). Ethical lapses in professional practice rarely arise abruptly; rather, they often stem from behaviors normalized during training, particularly when expectations and boundaries are unclear. When students experience ethical lapses in GenAI use, often under the pressures of increased workload and time pressure, these behaviors can solidify into ethically unsound decision- making patterns that become entrenched and difficult to reverse (Dodson et al., 2025). Therefore, without a collective and interdisciplinary understanding of AI digital ethics in academia, higher education risks normalizing practices that weaken ethical sensitivity and blur accountability. Because ethical judgment on GenAI use develops through guidance",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,2,"to reverse (Dodson et al., 2025). Therefore, without a collective and interdisciplinary understanding of AI digital ethics in academia, higher education risks normalizing practices that weaken ethical sensitivity and blur accountability. Because ethical judgment on GenAI use develops through guidance with repeated practice, higher academia has a critical window for influencing students’ ethical judgment formation. Without empirical evidence on how students judge ethical GenAI use, and the role AI literacy plays in that judgment, educators risk designing interventions that address awareness without improving actual ethical decision-making. Much of the existing literature on AI ethics in higher education has focused on students’ self-reported knowledge, learning competence, and attitudes regarding AI use (Carolus et al., 2023; Yang et al., 2025). Comparatively little empirical work has examined how undergraduate students evaluate the ethical use of GenAI in commonly encountered in academic contexts. Additionally, there is limited data on students’ ability to distinguish ethical learning-support uses of GenAI from unethical ones, as well as the extent to which uncertainty persists for decision-making in ambiguous situations. Moreover, although AI literacy has been proposed as a",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,2,"there is limited data on students’ ability to distinguish ethical learning-support uses of GenAI from unethical ones, as well as the extent to which uncertainty persists for decision-making in ambiguous situations. Moreover, although AI literacy has been proposed as a foundation for responsible GenAI use, its relationship to students’ real-life ethical judgment remains underexplored. Addressing these gaps, the purpose of this study was to examine undergraduate students’ ethical judgment regarding the use of GenAI in educational contexts and to investigate the relationship between ethical judgment accuracy and students’ levels of AI literacy. The following research questions guided the study: RQ1. What levels of ethical judgment do undergraduate students demonstrate when responding to GenAI ethical scenarios common in educational contexts?",119
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,3,"1 3 Page 3 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… RQ2. Is there a relationship between undergraduate students’ ethical judgment scores and their overall AI literacy? RQ3. Do AI literacy and student demographics predict ethical judgment accuracy? Literature Review Recent scholarship identifies GenAI as a powerful catalyst for efficiency in education, capable of streamlining literature reviews, democratizing access to resources, and serving as a collaborative “partner” in problem-solving (Llerena-Izquierdo & Ayala-Carabajo, 2025; Samala et al., 2025). However, these efficiencies require students to shoulder the burden of distinguishing truth from hallucinated content and fabricated data. Without a shared framework for ethical AI use, students may develop cognitive shortcuts that prioritize speed and convenience over learning, increasing the risk of overreliance on AI tools (Zhai et al., 2024). This lack of ethical discernment may contribute to what has been described as “numbing of ethical awareness,” in which repeated technological decision- making occurs with lessening regard for long-term consequences (Llerena-Izquierdo & Ayala-Carabajo, 2025, p.19). Collectively, this tension suggests that without intentional pedagogical intervention, the convenience and speed",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,3,"what has been described as “numbing of ethical awareness,” in which repeated technological decision- making occurs with lessening regard for long-term consequences (Llerena-Izquierdo & Ayala-Carabajo, 2025, p.19). Collectively, this tension suggests that without intentional pedagogical intervention, the convenience and speed of GenAI may unintentionally erode the very intellectual autonomy higher education seeks to cultivate. Despite the growing normalization of using GenAI tools in higher education, undergraduate students’ competency with AI ethics remains inconsistent and fragmented, with existing literature presenting mixed and conflicting findings. While some studies suggest that students can readily articulate foundational ethical principles (Dodson et al., 2025), emerging evidence indicates a weaker understanding of more complex and system- level principles, such as justice and explicability, as they relate to AI-driven technologies (Colón-Aguirre & Bright, 2025). Explicability refers to the degree that AI systems can be understood, interpreted, and meaningfully explained to humans responsible for their use. When students only view AI ethics through the lens of academic integrity or plagiarism, they may overlook broader ethical concerns such as algorithmic bias, erosion of critical thinking, and the homogenization of",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,3,"meaningfully explained to humans responsible for their use. When students only view AI ethics through the lens of academic integrity or plagiarism, they may overlook broader ethical concerns such as algorithmic bias, erosion of critical thinking, and the homogenization of ideas. As a result, without intentional educational interventions, students may be less prepared to critically evaluate or ethically use AI technologies in real-world contexts; contexts where the consequences extend beyond individual coursework or academic performance. For example, in healthcare fields, overreliance on GenAI could reduce nuanced clinical reasoning, contribute to misdiagnoses, or introduce documentation inaccuracies (Topaz et al., 2025). In education, habitual use of AI-generated lesson plans or feedback could perpetuate pedagogical biases and limit student agency (Chen et al., 2025). In counseling and psychology, uncritical GenAI use could violate confidentiality or overlook cultural considerations essential to safe client interactions (Saeidnia et al., 2024). At a societal level, unethical AI use may contribute to fabricated misinformation, algorithmic discrimination, and declining public trust. Longitudinal research is needed to determine whether patterns of GenAI use developed during college persist into professional practice.",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,3,"(Saeidnia et al., 2024). At a societal level, unethical AI use may contribute to fabricated misinformation, algorithmic discrimination, and declining public trust. Longitudinal research is needed to determine whether patterns of GenAI use developed during college persist into professional practice. A review of existing literature indicates that most empirical studies focus on ethical awareness, attitudes, or self-reported confidence in ethical AI use rather than ethical judgment (Wu et al., 2025). Ethical awareness reflects recognition that an issue exists,",78
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,4,"1 3 82 Page 4 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. while ethical knowledge reflects familiarity with principles or terminology. Ethical judgment, by contrast, involves evaluating a specific situation, weighing competing values, and determining a course of action in real-world scenarios as right, wrong, good, or bad (Holzmann et al., 2025). Ethical judgment is distinct from behavioral intention, which reflects a person’s anticipated course of action, and actual behavior, which reflects what the person does in practice. Although ethical judgment has been associated with intentions and patterns of continued AI use (Holzmann et al., 2025), the present study does not assess intentions or real-world conduct. This distinction is important because GenAI-related decisions may require contextual reasoning rather than knowledge of rules alone. Assessing ethical judgment, therefore, offers an authentic indicator of students’ preparedness to navigate AI-related dilemmas they are likely to encounter in both academic and professional settings. Despite this gap in ethical judgment focused research, there is a growing body of scholarship on AI literacy development in higher education. Four aspects of AI literacy include: knowing and understanding",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,4,"likely to encounter in both academic and professional settings. Despite this gap in ethical judgment focused research, there is a growing body of scholarship on AI literacy development in higher education. Four aspects of AI literacy include: knowing and understanding AI, using AI, evaluating and creating with AI, and AI ethics (Ng et al., 2021). Yang et al. (2025) similarly conceptualized AI literacy as comprising three interrelated dimensions: AI ethics knowledge, attitude, and competence. Their findings suggest that ethical knowledge acts as the foundational driver; shaping students’ ethical attitudes, which in turn strengthens their practical ethical competence (Yang et al., 2025). Ethical competence is conceptualized as a complex broad professional capacity that includes ethical awareness, personal qualities, moral reasoning, reflective practice, and the ability to apply ethical knowledge into action; whereas ethical judgment refers, specifically to the cognitive evaluation and decision of what is morally right in a given situation (Andersson et al., 2022; Halldorsdottir & Bryngeirsdottir, 2025). Ethical judgment can be therefore conceptualized as a critical subcomponent of ethical competence, which encompasses broader skills such as ethical awareness, reflection,",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,4,"decision of what is morally right in a given situation (Andersson et al., 2022; Halldorsdottir & Bryngeirsdottir, 2025). Ethical judgment can be therefore conceptualized as a critical subcomponent of ethical competence, which encompasses broader skills such as ethical awareness, reflection, and implementation of decisions (Andersson et al., 2022). Rest (1986) positions moral sensitivity and knowledge as necessary preconditions for ethical judgment. Drawing on this framework along with Long and Magerko’s (2020) conception of AI literacy, the present study conceptualizes ethical judgment as a skills- based outcome of AI literacy development. Recent literature supports this theoretical grounding, suggesting that AI literacy is increasingly conceptualized as a foundational precursor to ethical AI use and ethical competence, particularly when literacy is measured as applied knowledge or performance rather than self-reported attitudes or confidence (Jin et al., 2025). Educational interventions that integrate critical and reflective AI literacy approaches have demonstrated improvements in responsible AI use, supporting the notion that ethical awareness emerges from problem solving with AI tools (Kong & Zhu, 2025). Scholars increasingly frame AI literacy as an ethical imperative, emphasizing its role",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,4,"reflective AI literacy approaches have demonstrated improvements in responsible AI use, supporting the notion that ethical awareness emerges from problem solving with AI tools (Kong & Zhu, 2025). Scholars increasingly frame AI literacy as an ethical imperative, emphasizing its role in supporting ethical judgment, accountability, and safe practice in AI-enabled environments (Andersson et al., 2022). Yet despite the growing recognition for building AI literacy, there is limited agreement on how to effectively operationalize, teach, and measure applied AI ethical judgment and decision making (Wiese et al., 2025). While the literature establishes the importance of shaping students’ AI literacy and ethical judgment, there is limited empirical research examining what factors specifically influence the development of AI ethical judgment.",117
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,5,"1 3 Page 5 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… Accordingly, the present study aims to address these gaps by examining undergraduate students’ foundational AI literacy and ethical judgment in classifying six GenAI-related ethical scenarios. Building on the work of Dodson et al. (2025), this study replicates and extends their investigation of incoming freshman nursing students to a larger undergraduate sample representing multiple majors. The adapted six ethical scenarios assess students’ ability to apply ethical principles in realistic academic contexts rather than relying on self- reported knowledge or confidence. In doing so, the study contributes empirical evidence regarding scenario-based ethical judgment and provides a foundation for the prospective development and evaluation of AI ethics education. Methods Design and Sample This study used a cross-sectional survey design conducted in Fall 2025, following Institutional Review Board approval (IRB No. 2188, Exempt, 10.13.2025). The convenience sample of undergraduate students included a total of 619 responses were recorded, of which 88 were excluded for failed attention checks, “speeding” (i.e., completing the survey in < 1/3 the median completion",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,5,"approval (IRB No. 2188, Exempt, 10.13.2025). The convenience sample of undergraduate students included a total of 619 responses were recorded, of which 88 were excluded for failed attention checks, “speeding” (i.e., completing the survey in < 1/3 the median completion time), “straight-lining” (i.e., exhibiting no response variability on a measure with reverse-coded items), and missing data. Cases with missing data on any study variable included in the analyses were excluded using complete-case analysis; no missing values were imputed. The final sample of 531 students included 114 men and 417 women, with an average age of about 19 years (M = 18.77, SD = 2.64). The sample was predominantly non-Hispanic White (76.5%), with an overrepresentation of first-year students (40.5%) and psychology majors (39.9%). Additional participant characteristics including major and year in college are presented in Table 1. Instruments The survey included the demographics items found in Table 1, six brief ethical scenarios adapted from Dodson et al. (2025), an objective assessment of AI literacy, the Generative AI Literacy Assessment Test (GLAT) (Jin et al., 2025), and other measures (not reported here).",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,5,"the demographics items found in Table 1, six brief ethical scenarios adapted from Dodson et al. (2025), an objective assessment of AI literacy, the Generative AI Literacy Assessment Test (GLAT) (Jin et al., 2025), and other measures (not reported here). Ethical Scenarios Six ethical scenarios were modified with permission from Dodson et al.’s (2025) study. Ethical scenarios were chosen as they can provide more valid and reliable measures of opinions than attitude surveys (Alexander & Becker, 1978). While attitudinal instruments can be useful, ethical scenarios and vignettes capture the divergence between thoughts and actual decision-making (Kmetty & Stefkovics, 2025), address moral responsibility (Bartels et al., 2015), and have strong predictive validity (Smith et al., 2022). Dodson et al.’s scenarios attempted to move beyond abstract knowledge focusing on practical decision-making when students encountered generative AI in their coursework. For instance, instead of asking students to define terms like beneficence or justice, the scenarios presented dilemmas mirroring common academic situations where AI might be",162
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,6,"1 3 82 Page 6 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. used by students. The goal was to capture how students act when faced with ambiguous or ethically challenging choices. Response options included ethical, unethical, or unsure, allowing researchers to quantify students’ ethical judgment in particular situations. These were originally designed to assess nursing students’ ability to apply ethical principles in realistic educational contexts rather than knowledge or confidence about AI ethics. They were adapted by the authors with permission to reduce nursing specificity for students coming from a variety of majors. Modifications included replacing selected nursing-specific roles, clinical settings, and specialized terminology with more broadly applicable equivalents. Internal consistency for the six-item measure was modest (KR-20 = 0.61). Because the items were scored dichotomously as correct or incorrect, KR-20 was used as the appropriate reliability coefficient. The pronounced ceiling effect and limited variability in scores may have contributed to the modest internal consistency. Scenario cases are presented in Table 2. Characteristic Mean or n SD or % Age 18.77 2.64 Sex Assigned at Birth Male 114 21.5 Female",180
