DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,7,"1 3 Page 7 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… Generative AI Literacy AI literacy was assessed using the Generative AI Literacy Assessment Test (GLAT) by Jin et al. (2025). The GLAT is a 20-item multiple-choice performance assessment of demonstrated generative AI knowledge, skills, and ethical understanding. Each item includes one correct response and three distractors. The domains covered include: Know & understand (8 items), Scenario Ethical Scenario Description Responses Ethical Scenarios Q1 Sarah struggles to understand the patho­ physiology of heart failure. She inputs her textbook notes into a generative AI tool and asks it to summarize key points. Sarah verifies the information with her professor and uses the summary to create notecards for studying. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Q2 James is writing a research paper for a class. He drafts the paper independently and then uploads it into an AI-powered writing assistant to check grammar and improve sentence structure. He reviews the sugges­ tions and rewrites the text in his own words before submitting the final paper. Ethical (1) Unethical",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,7,"the paper independently and then uploads it into an AI-powered writing assistant to check grammar and improve sentence structure. He reviews the sugges­ tions and rewrites the text in his own words before submitting the final paper. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Q3 Maria is completing an assignment that requires a literature review. She uses generative AI to generate relevant search terms and suggest databases. Maria then in­ dependently locates peer-reviewed sources, critically evaluates them, and writes the lit­ erature review based on her own synthesis. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Unethical Scenarios Q4 For a homework assignment, Michael is required to submit a reflection jour­ nal describing his personal experiences. Instead of writing the reflection himself, he provides prompts to AI and asks it to generate a realistic reflection based on his general experiences. Michael submits the AI-generated text without making changes or adding personal insights. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Q5 David has a writing assignment due and feels overwhelmed by his workload. He inputs the assignment prompt into a gen­ erative AI tool, which",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,7,"AI-generated text without making changes or adding personal insights. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Q5 David has a writing assignment due and feels overwhelmed by his workload. He inputs the assignment prompt into a gen­ erative AI tool, which produces a detailed response. Finding the response well-written and relevant, David makes only minor wording changes before submitting the assignment. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Q6 Laura, a nursing student, is writing a paper on medication errors in nursing practice. She asks AI to generate references for her bibliography. Although some citations cannot be verified as real sources, Laura includes them in her paper to meet assign­ ment requirements. Ethical (1) Unethical (2) Unsure (3) Table 2 Ethical Scenarios for as­ sessing undergraduate students’ ethical judgment *Modified with permission from Dodson et al. (2025) **Note: The scenarios appeared in a different order for participants, and the labels of “Ethical” and “Unethical” scenarios were not shown to participants",158
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,8,"1 3 82 Page 8 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. Use & apply (3 items), Evaluate and create (4 items), and Ethics (5 items). Each correct response received one point, producing a total score ranging from 0 to 20, with higher scores indicating greater demonstrated GenAI literacy. Because the study outcome also assessed ethical judgment, including the five-item Ethics subscale for GLAT could introduce conceptual overlap or criterion contamination. Therefore, a 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT score was calculated by summing the other subscales: Know and Understand, Use and Apply, and Evaluate and Create items. Non-Ethics GLAT scores then ranged from 0 to 15, with higher scores indicating greater objective GenAI literacy. The Non-Ethics score was used in the primary correlational and multivariable analyses to examine its association with ethical-scenario classification accuracy without direct overlap from ethics-specific GLAT content. The full 20-item score was retained for descriptive purposes and secondary sensitivity analyses. Scores were calculated only for participants with complete responses to the applicable items; missing responses were not imputed. The full 20-item GLAT demonstrated acceptable internal consistency in the",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,8,"full 20-item score was retained for descriptive purposes and secondary sensitivity analyses. Scores were calculated only for participants with complete responses to the applicable items; missing responses were not imputed. The full 20-item GLAT demonstrated acceptable internal consistency in the present sample (KR- 20 = 0.72), while the 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT demonstrated modest internal consistency (KR-20 = 0.65). Procedures All students from a variety of majors and academic levels who were enrolled in either General Psychology or Research Methods in Psychology were eligible to participate in this study for partial course or extra credit through the Sona Systems® participant recruitment platform. After enrolling, participants were directed to a QualtricsXM survey link. Following informed consent, participants completed a 45-minute survey. The ethical judgment scenarios (Table 2) were presented in a fixed, non-sequential order (Q1, Q2, Q4, Q6, Q3, Q5) to reduce response patterning (e.g., straight-lining). An attention check item (“Please choose ‘Unsure’ for this item”) was embedded between Q4 and Q6, and respondents who failed this check were excluded from analysis. This approach was intended to promote attentive responding while minimizing",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,8,"response patterning (e.g., straight-lining). An attention check item (“Please choose ‘Unsure’ for this item”) was embedded between Q4 and Q6, and respondents who failed this check were excluded from analysis. This approach was intended to promote attentive responding while minimizing patterned responses; however, the fixed order may introduce potential order effects. Data collection occurred from October 14 through November 22, 2025. Data Analysis All analyses were conducted using SPSS Version 28. Descriptive statistics were computed to summarize participants’ demographics and key study variables including ethical judgment and AI literacy scores. Prior to inferential analysis, data were screened for accuracy, missing values, outliers, and adherence to assumptions. Ethical judgment was reported primarily as the number of scenarios correctly classified, ranging from 0 to 6, with percentages provided as secondary descriptive information. Cases with missing data on variables included in an analysis were excluded using complete-case analysis; missing values were not imputed. Statistical significance was evaluated at an alpha level of 0.05 (two-tailed). To answer the first research question, descriptive statistics were computed for each ethi­ cal scenario and for the overall",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,8,"were excluded using complete-case analysis; missing values were not imputed. Statistical significance was evaluated at an alpha level of 0.05 (two-tailed). To answer the first research question, descriptive statistics were computed for each ethi­ cal scenario and for the overall ethical judgment score. Responses to each scenario were dichotomized as correct (1) or incorrect (0), with “unsure” responses coded as incorrect, consistent with prior scenario-based ethical judgment research (Dodson et al., 2025). A total ethical judgment score was calculated by summing correct responses across the six scenar­",87
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,9,"1 3 Page 9 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… ios (possible range = 0–6), with higher scores indicating greater ethical judgment. Student performance was also summarized as percent correct across scenarios to facilitate interpre­ tation and comparison across participants. To address the second research question, Spearman’s rank-order correlation was used to examine the association between the 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT score and the number of ethical scenarios correctly classified. Spearman’s rho was selected because the ethical- judgment outcome was discrete and demonstrated a pronounced ceiling effect. Pearson’s correlation was retained as a secondary sensitivity analysis to permit comparison with the original analysis. The Non-Ethics GLAT score was used to reduce conceptual overlap or criterion contamination arising from inclusion of the five GLAT Ethics items in both the predictor and the ethical-judgment outcome. Effect sizes were reported to indicate the strength of associations. The third research question was examined using a binomial generalized linear model with a logit link to determine whether sex, academic status, and AI literacy were associated with accuracy in classifying the",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,9,"were reported to indicate the strength of associations. The third research question was examined using a binomial generalized linear model with a logit link to determine whether sex, academic status, and AI literacy were associated with accuracy in classifying the six ethical scenarios. The number of scenarios correctly classified was entered as the number of events, with six specified as the number of trials. Sex and academic status were entered as categorical predictors, and the 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT score was entered as a continuous predictor. Female participants and seniors served as the reference groups for sex and academic status, respectively. Results RQ1: Ethical Judgment for Scenarios The first research question set out to explore ethical judgment of undergraduate students when presented with GenAI-based ethical scenarios. To address this question, overall ethical judgment accuracy across the six scenarios was calculated. The mean percentage of ethical scenarios answered correctly was 88.54%, indicating generally high levels of ethical judgment. Score distributions revealed a pronounced ceiling effect, with more than half of participants (57.6%, n = 306) correctly identifying all six scenarios (100%). An",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,9,"mean percentage of ethical scenarios answered correctly was 88.54%, indicating generally high levels of ethical judgment. Score distributions revealed a pronounced ceiling effect, with more than half of participants (57.6%, n = 306) correctly identifying all six scenarios (100%). An additional 28.8% (n = 153) correctly identified five of six scenarios (86.4% cumulative). In contrast, 13.5% (n = 72) correctly identified four or fewer scenarios, including 1.1% (n = 6) who could not correctly identify any scenario (see Table 3). Scenarios depicting ethical AI uses for learning support, such as using AI to summarize notes with subsequent verification (Sarah, Q1), improving grammar in a student-authored paper (James, Q2), or generating search terms while independently evaluating sources Number of scenarios cor­ rectly idenitified Total # of Participants % of Total 6 306 57.6% 5 153 28.8% 4 39 7.3% 3 14 2.6% 2 9 1.7% 1 4 0.8% 0 6 1.1% Table 3 Ethical judgment scores",155
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,10,"1 3 82 Page 10 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. (Maria, Q3) were overwhelmingly classified as ethical (84.7%–91.7% correct classifica­ tions). Conversely, scenarios depicting substitutionary AI use were predominantly classi­ fied as unethical. These included submitting unmodified AI-generated reflection journals (Michael Q4; 94.5% identified as unethical), minimally edited AI-generated assignment responses (David Q5; 83.4%), and submitting AI-generated references without verification (Laura Q6; 85.3%). Uncertainty was highest in scenarios involving minimal human modi­ fication or unverifiable AI outputs, with “unsure” responses ranging from 2.6% to 9.9% across scenarios (see Table 4). RQ2 Relationship Between AI Literacy and Ethical Judgment Accuracy The second research question explored any significant relationship between ethical judg­ ment and AI literacy. Because the ethical-judgment outcome was discrete and demonstrated a pronounced ceiling effect, Spearman’s rank-order correlation was used as the primary analysis. A small positive association was found between Non-Ethics GLAT scores and the number of ethical scenarios correctly classified, ρ = 0.234, p < .001, N = 531. Pearson’s correlation was conducted as a sensitivity analysis and produced a similarly small positive association, r",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,10,"association was found between Non-Ethics GLAT scores and the number of ethical scenarios correctly classified, ρ = 0.234, p < .001, N = 531. Pearson’s correlation was conducted as a sensitivity analysis and produced a similarly small positive association, r (529) = 0.213, p < .001. Thus, participants with higher demonstrated Non- Ethics AI literacy tended to classify more scenarios correctly, although the magnitude of the association was small. RQ3: Relationships Between Student Demographics, AI Literacy, and Ethical Judgment The third research question examined whether student demographics and AI literacy could collectively predict ethical judgment accuracy. Race/ethnicity and academic major were not included in the model because several categories had insufficient sample sizes, which could Scenario Scoring Key Ethical (n, %) Unethi­ cal (n, %) Un­ sure (n, %) Q1 Student uses AI to sum­ marize textbook notes and verifies information with professor Ethical 487 (91.7) 19 (3.6) 25 (4.7) Q2 Student uses AI to improve grammar and sentence clarity in a self-written paper Ethical 451 (84.9) 37 (7.0) 43 (8.1) Q3 Student uses AI to generate search terms and",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,10,"information with professor Ethical 487 (91.7) 19 (3.6) 25 (4.7) Q2 Student uses AI to improve grammar and sentence clarity in a self-written paper Ethical 451 (84.9) 37 (7.0) 43 (8.1) Q3 Student uses AI to generate search terms and databases, then conducts own literature review Ethical 484 (91.1) 23 (4.3) 24 (4.5) Q4 Student submits AI- generated reflection journal without personal input Unethical 15 (2.8) 502 (94.5) 14 (2.6) Q5 Student minimally edits AI-generated assignment and submits as own work Unethical 37 (7.0) 443 (83.4) 51 (9.6) Q6 Student submits AI-gener­ ated, unverifiable references Unethical 25 (4.7) 453 (85.3) 53 (9.9) Table 4 Student responses to ethical AI use scenarios (n = 531)",114
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,11,"1 3 Page 11 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… have produced unstable parameter estimates. A binomial generalized linear model with a logit link and robust covariance estimates was conducted using the number of scenarios correctly classified as the number of events and six as the number of trials. The fitted model provided a statistically significant improvement over the intercept-only model, likelihood-ratio χ²(5) = 76.04, p < .001. Goodness-of-fit statistics indicated extra- binomial dispersion, with deviance/df = 1.64 and Pearson χ²/df = 1.91; therefore, inferential results should be interpreted cautiously. The 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT score was signifi­ cantly and positively associated with classification accuracy, B = 0.130, SE = 0.033, Wald χ²(1) = 15.37, p < .001, OR = 1.14, 95% CI [1.07, 1.22]. Holding sex and academic status constant, each additional correct response on the Non-Ethics GLAT was associated with a 13.9% increase in the modeled odds of correctly classifying a scenario. Academic status had a significant overall association with classification accuracy, Wald χ²(3) = 13.24, p = .004. Using seniors as the",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,11,"on the Non-Ethics GLAT was associated with a 13.9% increase in the modeled odds of correctly classifying a scenario. Academic status had a significant overall association with classification accuracy, Wald χ²(3) = 13.24, p = .004. Using seniors as the reference group, sophomores had lower modeled odds of correctly classifying a scenario, B = − 0.482, SE = 0.240, Wald χ²(1) = 4.02, OR = 0.62, 95% CI [0.39, 0.99], unadjusted p = .045. However, this contrast did not remain significant following Bonferroni adjustment for the three academic-status comparisons, adjusted p = .135. Freshmen, B = − 0.172, SE = 0.225, Wald χ²(1) = 0.59, OR = 0.84, 95% CI [0.54, 1.31], p = .443, and juniors, B = 0.441, SE = 0.285, Wald χ²(1) = 2.40, OR = 1.55, 95% CI [0.89, 2.71], p = .121, did not differ significantly from seniors. Accordingly, the individual academic-status contrasts were interpreted as exploratory. Sex was not significantly associated with classification accuracy, Wald χ²(1) = 0.65, p = .420. Using females as the reference group, males had comparable modeled odds of correctly",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,11,"significantly from seniors. Accordingly, the individual academic-status contrasts were interpreted as exploratory. Sex was not significantly associated with classification accuracy, Wald χ²(1) = 0.65, p = .420. Using females as the reference group, males had comparable modeled odds of correctly classifying a scenario, B = − 0.132, SE = 0.164, OR = 0.88, 95% CI [0.64, 1.21]. Discussion The current findings have several important implications for higher education as institutions continue to make decisions about GenAI and its use in teaching, learning, and assessment practices. Participants demonstrated high agreement when classifying the six ethical scenarios, however more research is needed to determine whether students possess a broadly shared ethical framework across other AI-related situations. This is consistent with prior evidence that students can differentiate structured, verification-oriented use of GenAI for learning from patterns involving overreliance, substitution of student work, and inadequate citation practices (Dodson et al., 2025; Dogaru et al., 2025). The high accuracy and pronounced ceiling effect may instead indicate that several scenarios presented relatively clear or obvious distinctions between acceptable and unacceptable GenAI use. Consequently, the six scenarios",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,11,"inadequate citation practices (Dodson et al., 2025; Dogaru et al., 2025). The high accuracy and pronounced ceiling effect may instead indicate that several scenarios presented relatively clear or obvious distinctions between acceptable and unacceptable GenAI use. Consequently, the six scenarios may have had limited ability to differentiate students’ ethical judgment in more ambiguous situations. Although the high scores indicate that participants demonstrated substantial agreement when classifying these six predesignated scenarios, they should not be interpreted as evidence that students possess a broadly shared ethical framework for GenAI use. In fact, Lund et al. (2025) found limited consensus regarding what constitutes fair use of AI and reported that students’ personal ethical beliefs may be more influential than institutional policies or policy awareness. In the present study, AI use that supports their learning, such as summarizing content, improving grammar, or assisting with literature searches was consistently classified as ethical when paired with verification, critical thinking, and original student authorship.",157
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,12,"1 3 82 Page 12 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. In contrast, unmodified or minimally edited AI-generated work and unverifiable references were consistently identified as unethical. A subset of participants expressed uncertainty, particularly in scenarios involving modified AI-generated content. This highlights the need for clearer guidance, as boundaries between AI assistance and meaningful human authorship remain unclear (Formosa et al., 2025; Wiese et al., 2025). This suggests the need for institutions to clearly articulate boundaries between AI-assisted learning and AI-enabled academic misconduct, particularly for the students who remain unsure. In this study, AI literacy measured using the 15-item Non-Ethics GLAT score, demonstrated a small positive association with accuracy in classifying the six scenarios. In the adjusted model, each additional correct response was associated with 13.9% greater modeled odds of correctly classifying a scenario. Our findings align with prior work linking stronger AI literacy to more accurate ethical evaluation of AI use (Yang et al., 2025). This relationship may reflect differences in cognitive engagement with AI systems, including understanding of limitations, bias, and appropriate use contexts. Notably, the university",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,12,"work linking stronger AI literacy to more accurate ethical evaluation of AI use (Yang et al., 2025). This relationship may reflect differences in cognitive engagement with AI systems, including understanding of limitations, bias, and appropriate use contexts. Notably, the university where this study was conducted did not offer AI literacy courses or formal training at the time of data collection. Because participants’ prior sources of AI ethics knowledge were not measured, the study cannot determine how these judgments developed. The findings provide a rationale for prospectively testing whether foundational AI literacy and ethics instruction improves scenario-based ethical judgment (Abuadas et al., 2025). Given that students’ personal AI ethics appear to form early in their transition to higher education, institutions may consider leveraging and building on this clarity by intentionally embedding explicit AI ethics instruction early in the curricula of academic programs (Abuadas et al., 2025). Doing so may help reinforce collective ethical norms, reduce ambiguity for unsure learners, and promote consistency across disciplines. A scaffolded approach may help students refine ethical reasoning as they encounter more complex AI applications over",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,12,"programs (Abuadas et al., 2025). Doing so may help reinforce collective ethical norms, reduce ambiguity for unsure learners, and promote consistency across disciplines. A scaffolded approach may help students refine ethical reasoning as they encounter more complex AI applications over time. At the course level, instructors can incorporate structured, scenario-based discussions with guided decision-making prompts, structured reflection on AI use, and assignments requiring transparency, such as disclosure statements or process documentation. Instructors may also require students to justify AI use decisions or identify ethical risks within assignments. These strategies align with areas of uncertainty and are supported by literature emphasizing applied, case-based, and reflective AI ethics education (Wiese et al., 2025). The findings also suggest the value of moving beyond rigid, binary prohibitions toward more nuanced, guidance-oriented policies (Nguyen, 2025). While students demonstrated agreement on clearly unethical uses, uncertainty in more complex scenarios suggests that strict policies alone may not fully address the realities of AI integration. Faculty instruction that emphasizes role models as ethical decision-makers may better support students in navigating real-world academic contexts. Faculty development is also important,",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,12,"more complex scenarios suggests that strict policies alone may not fully address the realities of AI integration. Faculty instruction that emphasizes role models as ethical decision-makers may better support students in navigating real-world academic contexts. Faculty development is also important, as instructors play a key role in modeling ethical AI use and communicating expectations. However, relatively little research has examined how to support educators in integrating AI ethically (Tan et al., 2025). Professional development focused on both pedagogy and ethics may support more consistent implementation across disciplines. Importantly, ethical judgment does not always translate into ethical behavior. It is worth noting that college students frequently engage in AI-enabled academic misconduct, even if they know it violates their ethical beliefs or academic misconduct policies (Adzima, 2025; Sholikhah et al., 2024). Adzima (2025) found that student attitudes toward AI cheating were strongly influenced by perceived ambiguity in institutional policies and beliefs that AI",151
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,13,"1 3 Page 13 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… use supports efficiency rather than dishonesty. Additionally, students’ ethical decisions are influenced by situational factors such as workload, assessment design, and unclear policies, leading many students to morally rationalize AI cheating (Sholikhah et al., 2024). As such, formal AI literacy courses may benefit from moving beyond conceptual understanding about AI to practice-based learning, discussions and self-analyses of motivations and rationalizations for AI misuse, with opportunities to apply ethical frameworks to their own academic situations. Such instruction may help address the gap between ethical awareness and ethical behavior. Academic status had a significant overall association with ethical judgment scenario classification accuracy. Although sophomores had lower modeled odds than seniors before adjustment, this contrast did not remain significant following Bonferroni correction. Individual academic-status comparisons should therefore be considered exploratory and sample-specific. Future research using longitudinal designs and more complex scenarios could clarify how ethical judgment develops over time. Similarly, sex was not associated with ethical judgment accuracy in this study. Although prior work has reported gender",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,13,"be considered exploratory and sample-specific. Future research using longitudinal designs and more complex scenarios could clarify how ethical judgment develops over time. Similarly, sex was not associated with ethical judgment accuracy in this study. Although prior work has reported gender differences in related constructs, such as ethical awareness, highlighting the importance of inclusive instructional design (Wang et al., 2025). Taken together, the findings suggest that while many students demonstrate a baseline ability to evaluate ethical AI use, targeted, practice-oriented educational strategies may help reduce uncertainty and promote more consistent ethical decision-making across academic contexts. Future Directions Although ethical scenarios were used to assess ethical judgment in this study, the ceiling effect suggests that several scenarios may not have sufficiently differentiated students’ ethical judgment. Future research should therefore develop and validate more nuanced and complex scenarios with graded levels of ambiguity, varying institutional-policy contexts, discipline-specific applications, and conflicts among ethical principles. Post adaptation content validation and cognitive interviewing with students from different majors would strengthen the scenarios’ clarity, relevance, and scoring. Multi-institutional studies are also needed to replicate these findings with",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,13,"varying institutional-policy contexts, discipline-specific applications, and conflicts among ethical principles. Post adaptation content validation and cognitive interviewing with students from different majors would strengthen the scenarios’ clarity, relevance, and scoring. Multi-institutional studies are also needed to replicate these findings with more diverse and discipline-stratified samples. Longitudinal research could examine whether students’ ethical judgment changes over time and whether scenario-based classifications are associated with subsequent intentions or behavior. Experimental studies should prospectively evaluate whether AI ethics instruction, clearer policy guidance, and reflective learning activities improve ethical judgment. For example, Kong and Zhu’s (2025) findings regarding project- based learning and reflective writing provide a basis for testing whether these instructional approaches improve ethical awareness. Future studies should also examine whether ethics-specific AI knowledge, general AI literacy, policy clarity, academic workload, and moral disengagement make independent contributions to ethical judgment and behavior. Measures such as the Artificial Intelligence Ethical Awareness Scale (Kong & Zhu, 2025) and AI Ethical Reflection Scale (Wang et al., 2025) may complement scenario-based measures when evaluating related constructs such as awareness and reflection. However, these constructs should remain distinguished",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,13,"such as the Artificial Intelligence Ethical Awareness Scale (Kong & Zhu, 2025) and AI Ethical Reflection Scale (Wang et al., 2025) may complement scenario-based measures when evaluating related constructs such as awareness and reflection. However, these constructs should remain distinguished from applied ethical judgment. Incorporating behavioral measures and examining contextual influences such as academic pressures, peer norms, and institutional culture could help explain when and why students’ stated judgments align, or fail to align, with their actual use of GenAI.",80
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,14,"1 3 82 Page 14 of 18 J. M. Reed et al. Limitations This study used a convenience sample collected at one public university which limits its generalizability. The sample was predominantly White and female and included a large pro­ portion of first-year students. Although students represented multiple academic disciplines, recruitment through psychology courses may have produced a sample that differed from the broader undergraduate population. Analyses by race/ethnicity and academic discipline were not conducted because several categories contained insufficient sample sizes. Future research with larger, more diverse samples across institutions would allow for more robust subgroup analyses. Because “unsure” responses were classified as incorrect, the results may not fully capture students’ ethical reasoning in cases of uncertainty. Selecting “unsure” may reflect epistemic humility or thoughtful deliberation rather than a true lack of understanding, potentially leading to an underestimation of students’ ethical discernment. On the other hand, this scoring approach addresses whether students can identify ethical and unethical scenarios, which is aligned with giving credit only for correct answers. The fixed presentation order of the scenarios is another limitation, as",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,14,"students’ ethical discernment. On the other hand, this scoring approach addresses whether students can identify ethical and unethical scenarios, which is aligned with giving credit only for correct answers. The fixed presentation order of the scenarios is another limitation, as order or priming effects cannot be excluded. A pronounced ceiling effect was observed, with 57.6% of participants correctly classifying all six scenarios. The restricted score range may have limited the ability of the measure to distinguish among students and may have attenuated associations with AI literacy and demographic characteristics. The six-item scenario measure also demonstrated modest internal consistency (KR-20 = 0.61), which may reflect both the small number of items and limited response variability. Consequently, high classification accuracy should not be interpreted as evidence of comprehensive ethical competence or a broadly shared ethical framework. The scenarios may instead have presented relatively clear distinctions between acceptable and unacceptable GenAI use. The full GLAT contains five ethics-specific items, creating potential conceptual over­ lap with the ethical-judgment outcome. The primary analyses used a 15-item non-Ethics GLAT score to reduce this potential criterion contamination.",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,14,"relatively clear distinctions between acceptable and unacceptable GenAI use. The full GLAT contains five ethics-specific items, creating potential conceptual over­ lap with the ethical-judgment outcome. The primary analyses used a 15-item non-Ethics GLAT score to reduce this potential criterion contamination. However, removing the ethics items narrowed the construct being measured, and the non-Ethics score demonstrated mod­ est internal consistency (KR-20 = 0.65). Moreover, conceptual overlap between general AI literacy and ethical judgment may not have been eliminated completely. Finally, the binomial generalized linear model was appropriate for the bounded number of correctly classified scenarios, but the deviance and Pearson chi-square statistics indicated extra-binomial dispersion. Robust covariance estimates were therefore used to reduce its effects on standard errors, confidence intervals, and hypothesis tests. However, this adjustment does not eliminate the underlying heterogeneity or account for potential differences in difficulty across scenarios; therefore, the findings should still be interpreted cautiously. Future studies could use models that explicitly account for additional variability across participants and scenarios. The cross-sectional design also precludes conclusions about causality or changes in ethical judgment over time. Because participants",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,14,"therefore, the findings should still be interpreted cautiously. Future studies could use models that explicitly account for additional variability across participants and scenarios. The cross-sectional design also precludes conclusions about causality or changes in ethical judgment over time. Because participants evaluated hypothetical, low-stakes situations, their responses should not be interpreted as evidence of their intentions or actual conduct when facing academic pressures or opportunities to misuse GenAI.",67
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,15,"1 3 Page 15 of 18 82 AI Literacy and Scenario-based Ethical Judgment About Generative AI… Conclusion Undergraduate students in this study generally demonstrated an ability to differentiate ethical from unethical GenAI uses. The study identified a small positive association between objective AI literacy and ethical judgment accuracy, suggesting that AI literacy may contribute to, but does not fully explain, how students evaluate GenAI use. The presence of “unsure” responses, particularly in situations involving authorship and modified AI-generated content, further highlights continuing ambiguity surrounding acceptable use. Institutions may address these uncertainties by providing formal AI literacy and ethics instruction earlier in the undergraduate curriculum. Moreover, these gray areas underscore the need for clearer institutional guidance and intentional instructional efforts that help students refine ethical judgment. By building on students’ existing ethical intuitions and providing explicit guidance through curriculum design, assessment practices, and faculty development, institutions can support the ethical integration of generative AI while preserving academic integrity and human authorship. Future prospective and experimental research should test whether formal AI literacy instruction, explicit institutional policies, and guided practice with ethically",180
DOC_048,s10805-026-09757-w.pdf,10.1007/s10805-026-09757-w,15,"practices, and faculty development, institutions can support the ethical integration of generative AI while preserving academic integrity and human authorship. Future prospective and experimental research should test whether formal AI literacy instruction, explicit institutional policies, and guided practice with ethically ambiguous scenarios improve students’ ethical judgment and responsible use of GenAI. AI Acknowledgement Claude AI (Anthropic) and ChatGPT 5.2 were used to assist with SPSS syntax development, statistical output interpretation, and editing. All content was reviewed and verified by the authors, who retain full responsibility for this work. Acknowledgements N/A Authors’ Contributions JH, JR, RF, and JG designed the study. JR drafted the manuscript. RF, JH, and JR completed data analysis. TD assisted with literature review, writing/editing, and interpretation of results. All authors contributed to final editing. All authors read and approved of the final manuscript. Funding None Data Availability The datasets generated and/or analyzed during the current study are not publicly available due to the data being part of a larger ongoing research project with the full dataset not yet ready for public release. However, the data used for",180
