DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,1,"Vol:.(1234567890) TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5 ORIGINAL PAPER Evolving Perspectives on Generative AI in Higher Education: Insights from Two Years of University Stakeholder Analysis Chuhao Wu1 · John M. Carroll2 Received: 26 May 2025 / Accepted: 30 October 2025 / Published online: 17 November 2025 © The Author(s) 2025 Abstract Technology innovations play an important role in urging higher education institutions to adapt to changing societal values and needs, yet the adoption of new technologies can be a complex process requiring multi-stakeholder engagement. This study examines the evolving perceptions of Generative AI (GenAI) within a university community through the analysis of survey data collected over two consecutive years. Through Latent Class Analysis, we identify different groups of perceptions among students, faculty, and staff. Findings highlight the role of experience with AI in shaping participants’ attitudes. While faculty may recognize more of the values in GenAI applications, the level of comfort with using AI in education settings still varies. Meanwhile, students express persistent concerns related to job security, academic integrity, and ethical AI use. The study highlights the need for institution-wide AI",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,1,"the values in GenAI applications, the level of comfort with using AI in education settings still varies. Meanwhile, students express persistent concerns related to job security, academic integrity, and ethical AI use. The study highlights the need for institution-wide AI literacy initiatives, adaptive learning frameworks, and structured policy interventions to support equitable and effective GenAI integration. Keywords Higher education · Generative artificial intelligence · Technology acceptance · Innovation diffusion Introduction The integration of new technologies into education can be a complex and spiral process accompanied by both optimism and skepticism. Historically, the introduction of calculators in mathematics education provides one illustrative example: initial resistance centered on fears that calculators would erode students’ basic arithmetic skills (Banks, 2011). Yet over time, educators came to view them as tools for enhanc- ing problem-solving and conceptual understanding (Brown et al., 2007). This shift was shaped by professional develop- ment, curricular reforms, public perceptions, and broader cultural changes (Ruthven, 1995, 1996; Schmidt, 1998). More recently, breakthroughs in Generative AI (GenAI), in particular the public release of ChatGPT, have sparked a fervent and almost unprecedented",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,1,"was shaped by professional develop- ment, curricular reforms, public perceptions, and broader cultural changes (Ruthven, 1995, 1996; Schmidt, 1998). More recently, breakthroughs in Generative AI (GenAI), in particular the public release of ChatGPT, have sparked a fervent and almost unprecedented round of discussion about AI in society. Although applications of AI in education (AIEd) have been receiving attention for their potential to assist administration, instruction, and learning (Chen et al., 2020), GenAI still came as a surprise to many educators. As Costa and Murphy, (2025) caution, drawing a direct paral- lel between calculators and GenAI risks oversimplification. Unlike calculators, large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT are capable of processing complex textual infor- mation and synthesizing cross-domain knowledge to respond to user inquiries (Brown et al., 2020; Stokel-Walker & Van Noorden, 2023). It has been reported that GPT can achieve human-level performance on various professional and aca- demic exams, with scores comparable to those of top human test takers (OpenAI et al., 2024). Therefore, GenAI tools do not merely automate routine tasks; they generate language, synthesize information across domains, and",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,1,"human-level performance on various professional and aca- demic exams, with scores comparable to those of top human test takers (OpenAI et al., 2024). Therefore, GenAI tools do not merely automate routine tasks; they generate language, synthesize information across domains, and influence how knowledge itself is produced and validated. Despite unsolved issues with AI hallucination (Athaluri et al., 2023; Sok & Heng, 2023) and ongoing debates on AI plagiarism (Cotton et al., 2023), it seems inevitable that students and university employees will use GenAI tools at least personally (Baytas & Ruediger, 2024). In January 2025, the release of DeepSeek, a new AI model developed by a Chinese startup, has rocked people once again and upended * Chuhao Wu chuhaow@clemson.edu John M. Carroll jmc56@psu.edu 1 University Libraries, Clemson University, Clemson, SC 29634, USA 2 College of Information Sciences and Technology, The Pennsylvania State University, University Park, PA 16802, USA",147
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"347 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 several fundamental assumptions about AI progress (Roose, 2025). The surprising rapid development of AI urges Higher Education Institutions (HEIs) to make timely adaptations in their technology integration strategies, which should not only address immediate needs but also enhance the capabili- ties to respond to future technological innovations. There- fore, HEIs and their leadership are increasingly interested in evaluating the impact of GenAI and developing effective policies that govern its usage (McDonald et al., 2024; Wu et al., 2024). Recent scholarships suggest that many univer- sity AI policies tend to be fragmented or insufficient. For example, in a critical review of the world’s top 20 universi- ties, Luo, (2024) highlights that institutional GenAI policies are often reactive and lack coherence. A survey of top U.S. universities also found that fewer than half of the institutions provide detailed assessment policies, and only a minority offer guidance for faculty and researchers (An et al., 2025). It is emphasized that the next generation of GenAI policies must be human-centered, inclusive, and responsive to criti- cal stakeholder perspectives (Holmes & Miao,",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"assessment policies, and only a minority offer guidance for faculty and researchers (An et al., 2025). It is emphasized that the next generation of GenAI policies must be human-centered, inclusive, and responsive to criti- cal stakeholder perspectives (Holmes & Miao, 2023; Kim et al., 2025). The literature has illustrated a wide range of perceived benefits and concerns of GenAI (Chan & Hu, 2023; Liu et al., 2024; Petricini et al., 2023). However, investigations mainly rely on cross-sectional designs, offering a snapshot of opinions at a single point in time. While one-time surveys or interviews provide useful baseline information, people’s attitudes may change as they gain more exposure to AI tech- nologies and respond to new institutional policies. The rapid development of AI itself further complicates this landscape, as improvements in AI capabilities can alter users’ perceptions. In addition, previous studies focused on profiling students’ or faculty’s attitudes towards GenAI as a group, with limited investigations of potential subgroups and their differences. The current study seeks to address this gap by analyzing sur- vey data collected over two consecutive years from",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"focused on profiling students’ or faculty’s attitudes towards GenAI as a group, with limited investigations of potential subgroups and their differences. The current study seeks to address this gap by analyzing sur- vey data collected over two consecutive years from the same institution, providing a unique opportunity to illustrate how the community’s attitudes toward GenAI may have shifted or remained stationary during the time period. Through this comparison, this research aims to answer critical questions: 1) Whether and how do perceptions of GenAI in the uni- versity community form groups? 2) Whether and how do these perceptions evolve over time? Related Work Perceptions of GenAI in Higher Education Implementing GenAI successfully requires a comprehensive understanding of the perceptions, concerns, and expectations of key stakeholders of higher education, especially students and faculty. Across studies, students commonly report ben- efits such as help with idea generation, writing support, and clarifying difficult concepts, alongside persistent worries about accuracy, bias, privacy, and academic integrity (Chan & Hu, 2023; Liu et al., 2024). A nationally representative survey conducted in the UK found that 92% of",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"help with idea generation, writing support, and clarifying difficult concepts, alongside persistent worries about accuracy, bias, privacy, and academic integrity (Chan & Hu, 2023; Liu et al., 2024). A nationally representative survey conducted in the UK found that 92% of undergradu- ates now use GenAI, mainly to explain concepts and summa- rize readings, while concerns about hallucinations and being accused of misconduct remain widespread and patterned by gender and socio-economic status (Freeman, 2025). At the same time, emerging work on trust indicates that students’ reliance on GenAI depends on calibrated trust rather than simple enthusiasm. An exploratory survey of 253 students found heterogeneous trust levels that correlate with motiva- tion and confidence, emphaszing the need to teach “trust calibration” rather than mere tool use (Amoozadeh et al., 2024). Faculty perspectives are similarly nuanced. Multi-insti- tution interview studies show many instructors are experi- menting with GenAI for AI-literacy activities yet continue to voice concerns about assessment validity, research quality, and uneven institutional support (Baytas & Ruediger, 2025). In one U.S. survey with 982 students and 76 faculty, the two groups reported",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"are experi- menting with GenAI for AI-literacy activities yet continue to voice concerns about assessment validity, research quality, and uneven institutional support (Baytas & Ruediger, 2025). In one U.S. survey with 982 students and 76 faculty, the two groups reported broadly similar attitudes overall, diverging mainly on ease/fun of use and perceived effects on grades, with notable differences across gender and STEM vs. non- STEM majors (Kim et al., 2025). Finally, several reviews and discipline-focused studies converge on academic- integrity risks and equity issues as cross-cutting concerns that institutions must address through clearer policies and targeted support (Ahmed et al., 2024; Kofinas et al., 2025; Mamo et al., 2024; Petricini et al., 2023). Together, these studies illustrate a wide range of perceived benefits and con- cerns, highlighting the need to further investigate underlying factors that shape people’s perceptions. Diffusion of Innovations in Higher Education The Diffusion of Innovations (DOI) framework is one of the most influential lenses for understanding how new prac- tices spread in organizations and social systems (Greenhalgh et al., 2004; Rogers, 2003). DOI explains adoption as a",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,2,"Innovations in Higher Education The Diffusion of Innovations (DOI) framework is one of the most influential lenses for understanding how new prac- tices spread in organizations and social systems (Greenhalgh et al., 2004; Rogers, 2003). DOI explains adoption as a func- tion of perceived relative advantage, compatibility, complex- ity, trialability, and observability, unfolding through com- munication channels, decision structures, and social-system norms (Rogers, 2003; Sahin, 2006; Wisdom et al., 2014). A core contribution of DOI is its account of adopter hetero- geneity. Innovations typically diffuse through five adopter categories distinguished by motivations, risk tolerance, and communication behavior: 1) Innovators: risk-takers who are the first to adopt and experiment with new technolo- gies; 2) Early Adopters: influential individuals who quickly recognize the value of innovations and advocate for their",128
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,3,"348 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 adoption; 3) Early Majority: a pragmatic group that adopts innovations after observing their success and viability; 4) Late Majority: more skeptical individuals who adopt inno- vations once they are widely accepted and proven effective; 5) Laggards: conservative individuals who resist adoption until the innovation becomes unavoidable. Applications of DOI theory in higher education confirm that technology acceptance is stratified across adopter cat- egories and that effectiveness depends on tailoring imple- mentation to those segments. Systematic review evidence shows academics’ uptake of learning technologies varies with perceived innovation attributes, user characteristics, and institutional context (Liu et al., 2020) Comparative studies of the “reluctant majority” versus early adopters find that mainstream faculty require stronger pedagogical value propositions, clearer incentives, and structured pro- fessional development, cautioning against extrapolating from early-adopter enthusiasm (Hixon et al., 2012). At the institutional level, multi-case analyses of blended-learning initiatives show that support must evolve as adoption moves from innovators/early adopters to the early and late majority (Porter et al., 2014, 2016). Collectively, the literature high- lights the importance of understanding adopter categories and designing",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,3,"multi-case analyses of blended-learning initiatives show that support must evolve as adoption moves from innovators/early adopters to the early and late majority (Porter et al., 2014, 2016). Collectively, the literature high- lights the importance of understanding adopter categories and designing differentiated support mechanisms to success- fully advance innovation diffusion in higher education. Methods Instruments and Data Collection The survey items were collaboratively developed by the research team through an iterative process that included reviewing emerging literature on GenAI in education, consulting with faculty and educational technology experts, piloting drafts for tests, and revising for clarity and relevance. This approach ensured that the instrument reflected diverse stakeholder perspectives and addressed issues salient to the institutional context. While the items were designed for exploratory purposes rather than drawn directly from existing theoretical frameworks, the research team tested their readability and empirical validity through pilot testing and expert reviews. The first-year question- naire contained six groups of closed-ended items. Sum- marizing descriptions of survey items in each group are listed in Table 1. All items are framed in a Likert scale format, where",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,3,"through pilot testing and expert reviews. The first-year question- naire contained six groups of closed-ended items. Sum- marizing descriptions of survey items in each group are listed in Table 1. All items are framed in a Likert scale format, where respondents are requested to rate their level of agreement with the list of statements from Strongly Disagree to Strongly Agree. For the Year 2 Questionnaire, survey items were adjusted to reflect the fast-evolving understanding of AI technologies and changes in the institution and study con- text. For example, instead of using the statement “I have received instruction about how to use ChatGPT or similar tools”, it was changed to “I have received sufficient train- ing about how to use generative AI”. More importantly, the study was initially designed to examine only student and faculty perceptions in Year 1. After completing Year 1 data collection, however, staff members expressed a strong interest in participating. In response, we expanded the survey scope in Year 2 to include staff as potential participants and standardized the wording of survey items to improve comparability of",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,3,"Year 1 data collection, however, staff members expressed a strong interest in participating. In response, we expanded the survey scope in Year 2 to include staff as potential participants and standardized the wording of survey items to improve comparability of results across students, fac- ulty, and staff. The summarizing descriptions of the Year 2 questionnaire are listed in Table 2. Participants only receive items applicable to their primary affiliation with the university. The study was conducted at a public, research-intensive university in the mid-Atlantic region of the United States and approved by the institution’s IRB office. Recruitment materials containing a link to the questionnaire were sent to faculty, students, and staff via their university email addresses. Participants volunteered to participate in the study and respond to the questionnaires online. The Year 1 Table 1 Survey items groups for year 1 questionnaire Group Description Number of Items Experience and Training with AI (E) Self-reported levels of familiarity, experience, and training with GenAI Students: 5 Faculty: 5 Prevalence of AI (PR) Perception of how prevalent AI usage is in education Students: 3",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,3,"questionnaire Group Description Number of Items Experience and Training with AI (E) Self-reported levels of familiarity, experience, and training with GenAI Students: 5 Faculty: 5 Prevalence of AI (PR) Perception of how prevalent AI usage is in education Students: 3 Faculty: 2 AI Policies (P) Attitudes towards regulating or restricting AI usage Students: 3 Faculty: 4 Perception of Successful AI Usage (S) Perception of how successful AI is used by themselves or others Students: 4 Faculty: 2 Comfort with GenAI usage (C) Levels of comfort with AI usage in academic settings Students: 3 Faculty: 6 Value of AI (V) Perception of the potential positive and negative value of AI Students: 4 Faculty: 3",113
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,4,"349 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 survey was open from March to May in 2023, and the Year 2 survey was open from March to May in 2024, allowing sufficient time for participation. It should be noted that this study was conducted at a sin- gle institution, and its specific policy and environment may not be representative of the broader higher education land- scapes. As context, between 2023 and 2025, the university’s stance on GenAI seems to shift from a primarily prohibitive and integrity-focused approach to a more nuanced, flexible framework. For example, in 2023, the sample syllabus state- ment it provided emphasized academic misconduct, warning that “you must complete all course work entirely on your own” unless explicitly permitted, framing AI use largely as a potential violation with “severe consequences”. In 2025, the updated sample statement has recognized diverse instruc- tional goals, offering instructors multiple options ranging from full prohibition to conditional or process-focused use, noting that “students may use generative AI tools to brain- storm ideas, to create an outline… and to improve their use of grammar,” provided they cite",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,4,"goals, offering instructors multiple options ranging from full prohibition to conditional or process-focused use, noting that “students may use generative AI tools to brain- storm ideas, to create an outline… and to improve their use of grammar,” provided they cite their tools. Data Analysis Data analyses were performed using R and R Studio. Par- ticipants’ responses to all Likert Scale items were converted to numeric values based on the five-point scale: 1 = strongly disagree, 2 = disagree, 3 = neutral, 4 = agree, 5 = strongly agree. Latent Class Analysis (LCA, Vermunt & Magidson, 2002) was used to identify unobserved subgroups (latent classes) within the survey respondents based on their response patterns. This method allows for the classification of individuals into mutually exclusive groups based on their shared characteristics, as inferred from the observed data (Collins & Lanza, 2009). A series of models with increasing numbers of latent classes were estimated using the poLCA package (Linzer & Lewis, 2011), and model fit was assessed using the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) and Akaike Information Criterion (AIC). The determination of the",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,4,"A series of models with increasing numbers of latent classes were estimated using the poLCA package (Linzer & Lewis, 2011), and model fit was assessed using the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) and Akaike Information Criterion (AIC). The determination of the opti- mal number of classes was guided by model fit indices, with lower BIC and AIC values indicating better model fit. The final number of latent classes was chosen based on the fit indices and practical interpretability. Models for students, faculty, and staff in each year were estimated and evaluated separately. After identifying the latent classes through LCA, further statistical tests were conducted to examine differences in the mean responses across the identified classes. To account for unequal variances between classes and the ordinal nature of the responses, Welch's ANOVA was used to test for differ- ences in mean responses across the latent classes. Following a significant Welch ANOVA result, Dunn’s post hoc test with Bonferroni correction was applied to perform pairwise comparisons between the latent classes. The significance level for all tests was set at 훼= 0.05 . The",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,4,"across the latent classes. Following a significant Welch ANOVA result, Dunn’s post hoc test with Bonferroni correction was applied to perform pairwise comparisons between the latent classes. The significance level for all tests was set at 훼= 0.05 . The results of these tests were used to identify specific pairwise differences in the mean responses across latent classes. Results In Year 1, we collected 590 valid survey responses in total, which contained 334 responses from students and 256 from faculty. In Year 2, we collected 364 valid survey responses in total, which contained 80 responses from faculty, 219 from staff, and 65 from students. The average ratings of each sur- vey item are presented in Tables 3, 4 and 5. The results of LCA are presented in the following sections. Latent Classes in Year 1 Student Classes For the 334 students in Year 1, LCA suggests that three classes exist in the data. The characteristics of each class are illustrated in Fig. 1. The height of each bar represents the average response value to a survey item in the class.",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,4,"students in Year 1, LCA suggests that three classes exist in the data. The characteristics of each class are illustrated in Fig. 1. The height of each bar represents the average response value to a survey item in the class. The white break line indicates that the average has reached the neutral value of 3, so a value above three would indicate “agreement”, and vice versa, a value below three would indi- cate “disagreement”. The percentage of each class among the respondents is also indicated in the center. The post- hoc analysis showed that the three classes have significant Table 2 Survey items groups for year 2 questionnaire Group Description Number of Items Experience and Training with AI (E) Self-reported levels of familiarity, experience, and training with GenAI 3 Risks and Concerns of AI (R) Perception of potential risks associated with AI 4 AI Policies (P) Attitudes towards classroom and university-level AI policies 4 Academic Integrity (A) Perception of academic integrity issues associated with AI usage 4 Perception of Successful AI Usage (S) Perception of how successful AI is used",180
