DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,5,"350 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 differences across several survey items, and the statistics are presented in Table 6. The three classes are named Advocates, Explorers, and Skeptics based on their profiles, which are described below: Class 1 (Advocates): The Advocates are most experi- enced with AI and show the most positive attitude about its usage in education. This class makes up 34% of the student respondents and features a high level of familiar- ity with the concept of ChatGPT (E1) and AI (E2), as well as a moderate level of experience with ChatGPT (E3), with average ratings all exceeding 3. They are also highly open to receiving more instructions about AI (E5). This class shows a preference for less restrictive AI poli- cies by agreeing that students should not be restricted from using AI (P1) and disagreeing that the university needs to stop student use of AI (P2). Additionally, these students perceive the value of AI in education (V1) and that AI is used for good and helpful reasons (V2). Class 2 (Explorers): The Explorers (16% of students) exhibit a more",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,5,"to stop student use of AI (P2). Additionally, these students perceive the value of AI in education (V1) and that AI is used for good and helpful reasons (V2). Class 2 (Explorers): The Explorers (16% of students) exhibit a more neutral stance. The profile is similar to that of Advocates in some aspects, featuring a high level of familiarity with AI, disagreement toward restrictive AI policies, and neutral attitudes toward the use of AI in education settings. While Explorers still perceive good values in AI, the ratings are also significantly lower than those of Advocates, suggesting a more balanced view in comparison. Interestingly, these students have received more instructions about AI (E4), and their instructors have addressed and integrated AI into courses to a certain extent (S1 and S2), which may have contributed to their balanced perceptions. Class 3 (Skeptics): Skeptics (50% of students) have sig- nificantly lower levels of familiarity with AI (E1-E3). These students report a mediocre interest in receiving instructions (E5) and agree that student use of AI should be restricted (P1 and P2) and that using",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,5,"Skeptics (50% of students) have sig- nificantly lower levels of familiarity with AI (E1-E3). These students report a mediocre interest in receiving instructions (E5) and agree that student use of AI should be restricted (P1 and P2) and that using AI in coursework would violate academic integrity policy (P3). Not only do they feel uncomfortable using AI for coursework (C3), but they also prefer instructors not to use AI for grading assignments (C1) or creating syllabi (C2). Compared with their neutral opinions on the positive value of AI (V1 and V2), they perceive more danger and misuse of AI in education (V3 and V4). Faculty Classes For faculty respondents, we also identified three latent classes, and their profiles are illustrated in Fig. 2. These pro- files are consistent with those of students, aligning closely Table 3 Year 1 student survey items and average ratings ID Item Mean E1 I am familiar with ChatGPT 3.47 E2 I am familiar with the concept of artificial intelligence (AI) 4.04 E3 I have experience using ChatGPT 2.62 E4 I have received instructions about how",180
