DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,6,"351 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 with Advocates, Explorers, and Skeptics. Post-hoc analysis of group comparisons is presented in Table 7. Class 1 (Advocates): Advocates, who make up 27% of the faculty, are familiar and experienced with AI (E1- E3) but also highly interested in receiving further train- ing (E5). They reported a significantly lower agreement to statements for restricting students’ use of AI (P1) or considering using AI in coursework as cheating (P3) and a violation of academic integrity (P4). Nonetheless, they agree that students will need to be taught how to use AI tools appropriately (P2). These faculty are also comfort- able with using AI for various teaching and research activities (C1 – C6) and are likely to perceive the ben- efits AI may bring to students (V1). They lean towards neutrality regarding the potential dangers and misuse of AI (V2 and V3). Class 2 (Explorers): Compared to Advocates, Explorers (52% of the faculty) are less experienced with ChatGPT (E3) and moderately agree that using AI in coursework violates academic integrity policies. They still agree that it is necessary to",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,6,"(V2 and V3). Class 2 (Explorers): Compared to Advocates, Explorers (52% of the faculty) are less experienced with ChatGPT (E3) and moderately agree that using AI in coursework violates academic integrity policies. They still agree that it is necessary to teach students how to use AI (P2). Their level of comfort with using AI in various educational con- texts is below the neutral value. And their perceptions of the benefits and dangers of using AI are balanced, both slightly above the neutral value. Class 3 (Skeptics): Skeptics (21% of the faculty) are familiar with AI concepts (E1 and E2) but relatively inexperienced with ChatGPT (E3) and lack interest in receiving more instructions (E5). These faculty also dis- play strong agreement that students’ use of AI should be restricted (P1) and that using AI in coursework is cheat- ing (P3). They are unlikely to have addressed AI issues or integrated AI tools into instructions (S1 and S2). Their level of comfort with using AI is significantly lower than the other two classes (C1-C6), and they perceive more dangers and misuse associated",180
