DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,10,"355 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 issues than Advocates (R1) and are neutral about the risk of AI replacing jobs (R4). Staff Classes Staff make up the largest population of the respondents in Year 2. Similar to faculty, staff could be categorized into two distinct classes, which were labeled as Advocates and Skep- tics. Their profiles are illustrated in Fig. 5, and the post-hoc analysis of class differences is presented in Table 9. Class 1 (Advocates): This class constitutes 66% of the staff and is experienced with using GenAI (E1) but has not received sufficient training about GenAI (E2) and strongly agrees to receive more instructions about it (E3). They stay neutral about whether using AI in coursework violates academic integrity policies (A1). Meanwhile, they desire clearer AI policies at the university level (P1) but stay neutral about using AI regardless of the univer- sity policy (P4). They slightly agree that students mis- use AI (S4) and that students should be informed about instructors’ use of AI for instructional support (S5). They strongly agree that the university needs to teach students more",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,10,"the univer- sity policy (P4). They slightly agree that students mis- use AI (S4) and that students should be informed about instructors’ use of AI for instructional support (S5). They strongly agree that the university needs to teach students more about using AI responsibly (S6). These staff perceive all risks associated with AI except that AI may replace their jobs (R4). Overall, the high level of experience and strong interest in knowing more about AI qualify Class 1 as Advocates. Fig. 2 Faculty latent class characteristics in year 1 Fig. 3 Student latent class char- acteristics in year 2",99
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,11,"356 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 Class 2 (Skeptics): These staff (34%) have a significantly lower level of experience and training related to GenAI (E1 and E2). They are still willing to receive more instruc- tions, but to a significantly lesser extent than Advocates (E3). They strongly believe that using AI in coursework violates academic integrity policies (A1). Their need for clearer AI policies is consistent with Advocates, but they are unlikely to use AI regardless of the university policy (P4). Discussion The Staged Adoption of New Technologies Modern society is increasingly shaped by technology, as it pervades all aspects of modern life and influences how we interact with the world and each other (Ellul, 2021). Educa- tion, in particular, has witnessed a series of opportunities and challenges brought by technology innovations in the past decades, such as massive open online courses (Batu- ray, 2015), hybrid learning (Raes et al., 2020), and virtual reality (VR) applications (Freina & Ott, 2015). The suc- cessful adoption of new technologies has the potential to improve the accessibility of the learning environment (Alper & Raharinirina, 2006),",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,11,"(Batu- ray, 2015), hybrid learning (Raes et al., 2020), and virtual reality (VR) applications (Freina & Ott, 2015). The suc- cessful adoption of new technologies has the potential to improve the accessibility of the learning environment (Alper & Raharinirina, 2006), enhance student engagement (Liu Experience and Training with AI Academic Integrity AI Policies Perception of Successful AI Usage Comfort with GenAI usage Risks and Concerns of AI Fig. 4 Faculty latent class characteristics in year 2 Table 8 Dunn's test of multiple comparisons of year 2 faculty latent classes (statistical significance: ns=not significant, *=p<0.05, **=p<0.01, ***=p<0.001, ****=p<0.0001) ID Comparison z-value p-value Significance E1 Class 1–2 −5.22 <.001 **** E2 Class 1–2 −3.81 <.001 *** E3 Class 1–2 −0.70 .485 ns R1 Class 1–2 3.31 <.001 *** R2 Class 1–2 0.57 .566 ns R3 Class 1–2 1.06 .290 ns R4 Class 1–2 2.76 .006 ** P1 Class 1–2 0.94 .349 ns P2 Class 1–2 −1.41 .159 ns P3 Class 1–2 0.97 .332 ns P4 Class 1–2 −3.04 .002 ** A1 Class 1–2 5.27 <.001 **** A2 Class 1–2 1.33 .184 ns",180
