DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,12,"357 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 et al., 2020), and eventually, improve education outcomes (Tamim et al., 2011). However, the implementation of new technologies in education is not always a smooth and obsta- cle-free process. In fact, a number of factors, such as faculty and student resistance, privacy and security concerns, and infrastructure and maintenance costs, could impact the adop- tion of technology in the educational context. Previous studies have applied existing theoretical frame- works to model the process of technology adoption in higher education (Fathema et al., 2015; Pinho et al., 2021). These frameworks attempt to understand factors that impact the technology adoption process, not only from the individual perspective but also considering the organizational and soci- etal dynamics. As an exploratory study, our survey items were empirically derived rather than theory-driven. None- theless, the survey content aligns closely with constructs in widely used technology adoption frameworks, such as the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (Davis et al., 1989) and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technol- ogy (UTAUT) (Venkatesh et al., 2016). For example, items capturing perceptions of the",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,12,"in widely used technology adoption frameworks, such as the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) (Davis et al., 1989) and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technol- ogy (UTAUT) (Venkatesh et al., 2016). For example, items capturing perceptions of the value and success of AI usage parallel the Perceived Usefulness construct in TAM and the Performance Expectancy dimension in UTAUT, both of which assess beliefs about technology’s ability to enhance outcomes. Similarly, items on comfort with AI usage and experience/training reflect Perceived Ease of Use in TAM and Effort Expectancy in UTAUT, emphasizing the role of familiarity and confidence in reducing adoption barriers. Constructs such as AI policies, risks/concerns, and academic integrity align with UTAUT’s Facilitating Conditions and Social Influence dimensions, highlighting organizational and societal factors that shape technology acceptance. Thus, even though the survey items were not explicitly theory- based, they could map onto established constructs, sup- porting their conceptual validity and situating our findings within the broader technology adoption literature. More importantly, our results align with the DOI theory by identifying the existence of three latent classes in",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,12,"based, they could map onto established constructs, sup- porting their conceptual validity and situating our findings within the broader technology adoption literature. More importantly, our results align with the DOI theory by identifying the existence of three latent classes in the sample population: Advocates who are experienced with GenAI and exhibit positive attitudes towards its applica- tion in education, Explorers who are aware of the risks of GenAI but are willing to learn about it, and Skeptics who are inexperienced with GenAI and concerned about its nega- tive impact on education. We did not use the DOI categories (Innovators, Early Adopter, Early Majority, Late Major- ity, and Laggards) to label the classes mainly because of a widely critiqued limitation of the theory: the assumption that innovation is inherently beneficial. Rogers’ broader treat- ment of innovations throughout the theory and especially the usage of the label “Laggards”, tends to equate adoption with progress and resistance with backwardness, thus fram- ing non-adoption as a deficiency rather than a potentially rational or context-sensitive decision (Wolfe, 1994). This Fig. 5 Staff latent class characteristics",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,12,"the usage of the label “Laggards”, tends to equate adoption with progress and resistance with backwardness, thus fram- ing non-adoption as a deficiency rather than a potentially rational or context-sensitive decision (Wolfe, 1994). This Fig. 5 Staff latent class characteristics Table 9 Dunn's test of multiple comparisons of year 2 staff latent classes (statistical significance: ns=not significant, *=p<0.05, **=p<0.01, ***=p<0.001, ****=p<0.0001) ID Comparison z-value p-value Significance E1 Class 1–2 −10.43 <.001 **** E2 Class 1–2 −7.28 <.001 **** E3 Class 1–2 −6.63 <.001 **** R1 Class 1–2 2.41 .016 * R2 Class 1–2 −1.10 .269 ns R3 Class 1–2 0.91 .364 ns R4 Class 1–2 1.89 .059 ns P1 Class 1–2 0.61 .540 ns P3 Class 1–2 1.22 .221 ns P4 Class 1–2 −5.91 <.001 **** A1 Class 1–2 6.82 <.001 **** S4 Class 1–2 2.09 .036 * S5 Class 1–2 0.29 .774 ns S6 Class 1–2 −2.37 .018 *",150
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"358 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 bias limits the theory’s capacity to critically assess the nega- tive ethical, social, or environmental impact of innovations such as GenAI (Kenthapadi et al., 2023). This limitation is significant in the higher education context, where the fram- ing of innovation as inherently desirable can also silence critical discussions about whether technologies align with educational values or learning outcomes (Selwyn, 2013). Therefore, we used more neutral labels that acknowledge the informed skepticism rooted in legitimate concerns about AI. Nonetheless, the differences across three classes and their evolution over time remain consistent with the staged adop- tion process described by Rogers, and the implications for higher education are discussed in the following sections. How Experiences Shape AI Perceptions According to Rogers et al. (2014), the innovation-decision process begins with individuals becoming aware of the inno- vation and then forming a favorable or unfavorable attitude toward the innovation. Therefore, while initial positive atti- tudes can lead to adoption and use, the outcomes of that experience, in turn, can further influence attitudes, suggest- ing a reciprocal relationship. Our data shows",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"forming a favorable or unfavorable attitude toward the innovation. Therefore, while initial positive atti- tudes can lead to adoption and use, the outcomes of that experience, in turn, can further influence attitudes, suggest- ing a reciprocal relationship. Our data shows that the com- position of different classes varies between Year 1 and Year 2, yet the characteristics of each class remain consistent. Specifically, Advocates are more experienced with GenAI and, at the same time, more positive toward its usage in education scenarios. This aligns well with the descriptions of Innovators who are more open to exploring new technolo- gies and affirming their usefulness within the community (Ghimire & Edwards, 2024). Moreover, the clear distinction in experience levels across three classes indicates that the increased experience, training, and education on GenAI are more likely to lead to a positive instead of negative percep- tion of AI, which echoes the findings from prior research on the relationship between experience and perception of AI (Horowitz & Kahn, 2021; Kim & Lee, 2024). Noticeably, in Year 1’s results, regardless of the experi- ence level,",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"negative percep- tion of AI, which echoes the findings from prior research on the relationship between experience and perception of AI (Horowitz & Kahn, 2021; Kim & Lee, 2024). Noticeably, in Year 1’s results, regardless of the experi- ence level, all classes agreed that students’ use of GenAI in coursework would be inevitable. However, Skeptics are inclined toward restrictive policies that stop students’ use of GenAI and view it as a violation of academic integrity. This inclination may represent the apprehensions and skep- ticism often associated with the initial adoption phases of disruptive technologies, which can stem from a combination of societal and cultural factors, such as the sense of losing control (Bostrom, 2014), lack of understanding (Kastania, 2024), and ethical concerns (Zohny et al., 2023). In edu- cational settings, the introduction of new technologies has historically sparked concerns regarding academic integrity and the potential for diminished student learning (Aagaard, 2015; Mobilio et al., 2024; Roberts, 1980). Nonetheless, it is well established in the literature that fostering a positive and supportive educational environment is preferable to punitive actions for addressing",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"academic integrity and the potential for diminished student learning (Aagaard, 2015; Mobilio et al., 2024; Roberts, 1980). Nonetheless, it is well established in the literature that fostering a positive and supportive educational environment is preferable to punitive actions for addressing academic integrity issues (De Maio & Dixon, 2022). Therefore, recent publications have been advocating that the ethical integration of GenAI will not undermine but instead improve education (Tan & Maravilla, 2024), which is supported by preliminary evidence for pilot implementation of GenAI-supported assessment in higher education (Furze et al., 2024). Findings from the current study highlight the importance of increasing the level of experience and training with GenAI among faculty. This can mitigate the negative presupposition towards disruptive technology and facilitate a more supportive and educational approach to GenAI. How AI Perceptions Evolve over Time It should be noted that there was a substantial shift in sample composition between Year 1 (334 students, 256 faculty) and Year 2 (65 students, 80 faculty, 219 staff). This shift com- plicates direct comparisons across time because the reduced number of student and faculty",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"there was a substantial shift in sample composition between Year 1 (334 students, 256 faculty) and Year 2 (65 students, 80 faculty, 219 staff). This shift com- plicates direct comparisons across time because the reduced number of student and faculty participants may amplify the perspectives of a smaller subset of respondents, while the absence of staff members in Year 1 prevents comparison analysis for that group altogether. Nevertheless, the fact that similar categorizations and characteristics emerged, with three classes among students and faculty in Year 1, and two classes among faculty and staff in Year 2, suggests the possibility of evolving perspectives across the univer- sity community despite differences in sample composition. Specifically, for students in Year 2, there are only Explor- ers who are experienced with GenAI but also aware of its risks and concerns. The disappearance of Advocates and Skeptics, either due to their unwillingness to participate in the study or reduced proportion in the population, indicates that more students may have gained exposure to GenAI, but in a protected manner. Studies show that many universities have published",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"and Skeptics, either due to their unwillingness to participate in the study or reduced proportion in the population, indicates that more students may have gained exposure to GenAI, but in a protected manner. Studies show that many universities have published guidelines for faculty and students, provid- ing definitions and examples for trustworthy AI tools and appropriate AI usage (McDonald et al., 2024; Moorhouse et al., 2023). Meanwhile, the concern over being accused of plagiarism is an important factor in lowering students’ inten- tion to use AI tools (Ortiz-Bonnin & Blahopoulou, 2025). Despite concerns, the Explorers are still willing to receive more training in AI. Therefore, there is still a need for con- sistent university-wide training programs that help improve AI literacy among all students. For faculty, we observed 43% of Advocates in Year 2. While the difference between Year 1 and Year 2 is possibly attributed to changes in sample compositions, the absolute value still indicates a significant number of faculty who are more familiar with AI. Moreover, despite discomfort with AI usage in various scenarios, Skeptics’ agreement on integrat-",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,13,"and Year 2 is possibly attributed to changes in sample compositions, the absolute value still indicates a significant number of faculty who are more familiar with AI. Moreover, despite discomfort with AI usage in various scenarios, Skeptics’ agreement on integrat- ing GenAI into instruction in the future has reached slightly",50
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"359 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 over neutral (C1 = 3.2) in Year 2. The two classes also form a consensus that students should be informed about AI usage in instructional support and that the university needs to teach them about responsible AI usage. These trends align with recent studies reporting the overall cautious yet optimistic stance of universities and the emphasis on faculty’s responsi- bilities of integrating GenAI into curriculum and assessment (Cacho, 2024; Jin et al., 2024). However, despite the raised awareness that GenAI will be an indispensable part of higher education, the low experience level and strong interest in training among the Skeptics suggest a lack of effective AI literacy improvement programs during the period. In addi- tion, viewing AI usage as a violation of academic integ- rity policies is still a key aspect that differentiates the two classes. Admittedly, GenAI’s impact on academic integrity is a complicated topic that varies based on context (Yusuf et al., 2024). Existing recommendations include a more comprehensive policy and more adaptive assessments to accommodate the use of GenAI (Rasul et al., 2024),",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"Admittedly, GenAI’s impact on academic integrity is a complicated topic that varies based on context (Yusuf et al., 2024). Existing recommendations include a more comprehensive policy and more adaptive assessments to accommodate the use of GenAI (Rasul et al., 2024), which would require changes in the faculty’s pedagogical prac- tices. Faculty who are less experienced with GenAI may perceive more challenges and workloads associated with these changes. How Students, Faculty, and Staff Perceptions Align/ Misalign Involving multi-stakeholders in the processes of designing education technology solutions enhances inclusivity, trans- parency, and empowerment, leading to improved educational quality (Iqbal et al., 2024). Therefore, it’s important to take a holistic approach to understand the potential impact of GenAI on students, faculty, and staff. Each of the groups has distinct experiences and responsibilities on campus, and our survey reveals nuanced differences among their percep- tions of GenAI. In Year 1, there is a larger group of Skeptics among students, taking up 50% of respondents compared to 21% among faculty. This contradicts prior studies that observed students generally view GenAI more positively than faculty or",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"tions of GenAI. In Year 1, there is a larger group of Skeptics among students, taking up 50% of respondents compared to 21% among faculty. This contradicts prior studies that observed students generally view GenAI more positively than faculty or instructors (Chan & Lee, 2023; Zastudil et al., 2023). Students could feel more uncertain about GenAI due to more strict university or classroom policies, which seemed true for Year 1, as faculty agree that the use of AI to complete coursework is cheating (P3 = 3.66) and a vio- lation of academic integrity policies (P4 = 3.66). Students in Year 2 also showed intense concern over academic integrity issues and a lower level of comfort with AI usage in class- rooms. Also noticeable is that across the years, students have low levels of agreement on instructors’ successful use of GenAI. A lack of successful GenAI usage in instruction may contribute to a more negative view of AI among students. Out of all the concerns associated with GenAI, the future job security concern is more evident for students than other groups,",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"A lack of successful GenAI usage in instruction may contribute to a more negative view of AI among students. Out of all the concerns associated with GenAI, the future job security concern is more evident for students than other groups, echoing literature underscoring the fears about auto- mation and AI-driven displacement among younger genera- tions preparing to enter the workforce (Choi & Kang, 2019). This anxiety about future job security can lead to disengage- ment among students, as they may feel that their educational efforts are being undermined by the rapid adoption of AI in professional settings. As higher education is already experi- encing issues with enrollment and student retention (Eather et al., 2022), it is critical to address the AI challenges appro- priately. Faculty may need to teach students how to collabo- rate with AI effectively in writing or design tasks and shift their perspective towards seeing AI as a valuable skill in the workplace. Institutions should recognize students' fears and offer career guidance that highlights emerging job opportu- nities created by AI. Creating partnerships with industries that actively",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"design tasks and shift their perspective towards seeing AI as a valuable skill in the workplace. Institutions should recognize students' fears and offer career guidance that highlights emerging job opportu- nities created by AI. Creating partnerships with industries that actively integrate AI into their workflows can help students gain firsthand experience with AI tools, reducing uncertainty and building confidence in their ability to adapt to AI-enhanced workplaces (Rane, 2023). Non-academic staff also play important roles in support- ing teaching, learning, and research activities at universities, and their perceptions of AI are critical for ensuring ethical, inclusive, and collaborative adoption of AI, which ultimately improves student outcomes. While we only surveyed staff perceptions in Year 2, the results show similar patterns to those of other groups. Specifically, we observed the exist- ence of Advocates and Skeptics, which made up 66% and 34% of the staff responses, respectively. The larger propor- tion of Advocates suggested that staff may be more open to gaining experience with AI. One potential explanation is that they are less involved with the academic integrity controversies, as evidenced",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"34% of the staff responses, respectively. The larger propor- tion of Advocates suggested that staff may be more open to gaining experience with AI. One potential explanation is that they are less involved with the academic integrity controversies, as evidenced by their neutral attitudes toward whether AI usage violates academic integrity and whether they adhere to university policies on AI usage. Nonetheless, they are still aware of AI’s risks in causing academic integ- rity issues, advancing bias, and compromising privacy. Staff members, especially IT personnel, often provide essential support to faculty in adopting and using digital technolo- gies, and they can encounter challenges in unclear commu- nications, limited resources, and resistance to change (Polly et al., 2021). Therefore, institutions need to provide timely resources and professional development for staff so that they can better support faculty and students in addressing GenAI issues effectively. Limitations and Future Work This study employed a primarily quantitative approach to explore the university community’s attitudes toward GenAI and to identify latent attitudinal profiles through survey data. While the multifaceted questionnaire was designed to capture a",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,14,"GenAI issues effectively. Limitations and Future Work This study employed a primarily quantitative approach to explore the university community’s attitudes toward GenAI and to identify latent attitudinal profiles through survey data. While the multifaceted questionnaire was designed to capture a wide range of perspectives, it was exploratory",47
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,15,"360 TechTrends (2025) 70:346–363 in nature. The items were empirically derived rather than grounded in established theoretical frameworks, which lim- its their capacity to reliably measure underlying psychologi- cal constructs without further validation. Future research should validate and revise the items based on established theories such as TAM or UTAUT, to strengthen construct validity and facilitate comparisons across studies. Addition- ally, the study was conducted within a single institutional setting, shaped by the university’s unique cultural, techno- logical, and policy environment surrounding GenAI, which limits the generalizability of the findings. Future work could expand to multiple institutions with diverse profiles to test the transferability of findings and account for broader con- textual influences. Moreover, the dramatic shift in sample composition between Year 1 and Year 2 complicates claims about evolving perceptions over time, as differences in respondent character- istics may partly account for observed changes. Several fac- tors may have contributed to the drop-off among students and faculty. Institutional priorities likely shifted between years, limiting opportunities for coordinated outreach and reducing the study’s visibility among potential participants. In addi- tion,",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,15,"partly account for observed changes. Several fac- tors may have contributed to the drop-off among students and faculty. Institutional priorities likely shifted between years, limiting opportunities for coordinated outreach and reducing the study’s visibility among potential participants. In addi- tion, at a large research institution, the high frequency of cam- puswide surveys can quickly lead to survey fatigue, lowering participants’ willingness to engage. Notably, the number of studies focusing on GenAI grew substantially from 2023 to 2024, potentially diluting interest in any single survey on the topic. Future research should consider more robust sampling strategies to support evaluations of longitudinal change. Qualitative methods such as interviews or focus groups could also enrich our understanding of the motivations and concerns underlying GenAI attitudes, offering deeper insight into themes surfaced through quantitative analysis. In addi- tion, employing more advanced analytical approaches, such as structural equation modeling, could strengthen the evi- dence for patterns of technology adoption and the mecha- nisms driving attitudinal change. Overall, while this study offers a foundational view of GenAI perceptions in a univer- sity context, future research is",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,15,"as structural equation modeling, could strengthen the evi- dence for patterns of technology adoption and the mecha- nisms driving attitudinal change. Overall, while this study offers a foundational view of GenAI perceptions in a univer- sity context, future research is needed to validate its instru- ments, broaden its scope, and deepen its insights. Conclusion This study expands our understanding of how GenAI is shaping higher education by examining the evolving per- ceptions of students, faculty, and staff. Through two years of survey data and latent class analysis, we identified distinct attitudinal profiles and shifts in GenAI perceptions across campus roles. While perceptions vary by experience and role, a clear pattern emerged: greater familiarity with GenAI tends to foster more positive attitudes and openness toward its use in education. The findings highlight the reciprocal relationship between experience and perception, suggesting that targeted training and exposure can play a pivotal role in promoting thoughtful GenAI adoption. Institutions should prioritize comprehensive AI literacy initiatives that not only equip faculty with tools for ethical and innovative teaching but also support students in navigating AI's",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,15,"targeted training and exposure can play a pivotal role in promoting thoughtful GenAI adoption. Institutions should prioritize comprehensive AI literacy initiatives that not only equip faculty with tools for ethical and innovative teaching but also support students in navigating AI's impact on career pathways and academic expectations. Ultimately, the suc- cessful integration of GenAI into higher education depends on inclusive, role-sensitive approaches that address the con- cerns and aspirations of the entire academic community. Funding Open access funding provided by the Carolinas Consortium. Declarations Competing interests The authors declare no potential conflicts of inter- est for this research and received no outside funding for the study. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attri- bution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adapta- tion, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons",180
DOC_050,s11528-025-01150-5.pdf,10.1007/s11528-025-01150-5,15,"appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright",87
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4 ORIGINAL PAPER A Semester with Generative AI Tools: University Students Lived Experiences and Insights Leytisha Jack1 · Moon‑Heum Cho1 · Amona Da Conceição Major1 · Adam Peruta2 Received: 21 August 2025 / Accepted: 28 April 2026 / Published online: 25 May 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools are becoming increasingly embedded in higher education. Despite growing interest in GenAI integration, empirical research examining students’ semester-long experiences with multiple GenAI tools remains limited. This qualitative study explored the experiences and perspectives of college students enrolled in a 15-week advanced media studies course on GenAI tools. Semi-structured interviews with five students centered participants’ voices, uncovering feelings, insights, and perspectives shaped by their engagement with GenAI tools. Guided by the UNESCO AI competency framework for students, analysis revealed four themes comprising 14 categories: participants (a) actively engaged in learning GenAI tools using diverse strategies, (b) developed an understanding of what it means to use AI, (c) became aware of potential issues arising from GenAI use, and (d) gained insights into the implications of",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,1,"participants (a) actively engaged in learning GenAI tools using diverse strategies, (b) developed an understanding of what it means to use AI, (c) became aware of potential issues arising from GenAI use, and (d) gained insights into the implications of using GenAI tools in professional settings. Based on these findings, suggestions for GenAI integration in higher education are provided, includ- ing: (a) designing learning workflows that encourage critical and self-directed engagement with GenAI tools; (b) providing campus-wide access to GenAI tools paired with structured pedagogical guidance; and (c) incorporating discipline-specific activities that address both the affordances and ethical concerns of GenAI use. Keywords AI-driven course · AI tools learning experiences · College students · Generative AI tools · Qualitative study · Media studies · UNESCO AI competency framework for students Artificial intelligence (AI) refers to the capability of a machine to use, adapt to, and learn from inputs, and to engage in tasks deemed human-related, leveraging large- scale advancements in structured and unstructured data (Collins et al., 2021; Duan et al., 2019). Generative artifi- cial intelligence (GenAI) tools can generate",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,1,"to use, adapt to, and learn from inputs, and to engage in tasks deemed human-related, leveraging large- scale advancements in structured and unstructured data (Collins et al., 2021; Duan et al., 2019). Generative artifi- cial intelligence (GenAI) tools can generate texts, images, videos, and audio outputs in seconds without requiring users to have much technical skills and knowledge. With college students gaining increased access to GenAI tools (Golding et al., 2024) and these technologies continuing to evolve with frequent and innovative feature upgrades (Tang et al., 2020), higher education has expressed significant concerns about academic integrity (Bittle & El-Gayar, 2025). These concerns are particularly acute regarding students’ use of GenAI to produce synthetic course assignments that may go undetected during periods of summative assessment (Eke, 2023). Nevertheless, artificial intelligence tools continue to penetrate both the educational sector and various indus- tries where college students seek employment after their studies (Coffey, 2024; IBM, 2024). One field notably disrupted by the influx of AI tools is media, including journalism, advertising, public relations, TV/film, and broadcasting, due to its inherent reliance on information",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,1,"tries where college students seek employment after their studies (Coffey, 2024; IBM, 2024). One field notably disrupted by the influx of AI tools is media, including journalism, advertising, public relations, TV/film, and broadcasting, due to its inherent reliance on information technology and digital tools (Guzman & Lewis, 2024). IBM (2024) has noted that 59% of enterprise-scale com- panies exploring and deploying AI tools have accelerated their technology investments. Of those, 55% of AI deploy- ments are for the automation of information technology processes and digital labor (IBM, 2024). Consequently, current college graduates are more likely to encounter a workforce where AI tools are used and encouraged. More importantly, about 70% of college students graduates believe that AI tools should be integrated into courses (Coffey, 2024). Coffey (2024) also expressed that 55% of * Moon‑Heum Cho mhcho@syr.edu 1 Instructional Design, Development & Evaluation, Syracuse University, 414 Huntington Hall, Syracuse, NY 13244, US 2 Advanced Media Management, Syracuse University, Syracuse, NY, US",160
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"782 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 them claimed that “…their degree programs did not pre- pare them to use new technology tools in the workforce” (para. 2). This suggests that strong AI preparedness among college students is inevitable. In response to the rapidly evolving development of AI tools within the educational and industrial domains (Schroeder, 2024), higher education curricula integrating AI tools are slowly adapting to accommodate emerging AI tools to better prepare students for their future careers (Southworth et al., 2023). Programs such as Syracuse Uni- versity’s Advanced Media Management Master of Science program and the University of Florida’s campus-wide AI initiative approach are beginning to intentionally train stu- dents to increase AI literacy (Southworth et al., 2023). Also, the literature offers fair synopses of how to enhance and expand the curriculum around AI tools. Some of these include: AI preparedness of administration, faculty, and students in some aspects of academic training (Gander & Harris, 2024; Johnson et al., 2024), promotion of crucial understanding of AI concepts for higher education stu- dents (Kong et al., 2021), and ultimately preparing col- lege",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"of administration, faculty, and students in some aspects of academic training (Gander & Harris, 2024; Johnson et al., 2024), promotion of crucial understanding of AI concepts for higher education stu- dents (Kong et al., 2021), and ultimately preparing col- lege students, both technically and ethically, for a work- ing environment that has embraced AI tools (Hagendorff, 2020). Despite strong interest in offering AI curricula and train- ing, very little research has examined college students’ responses to AI curricula that focused on AI tools over a semester. The majority of existing studies are quantitative and examine students’ AI adoption intentions without actu- ally using AI in courses. For instance, established models such as the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) or the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) demonstrated that college students adopt AI tech- nologies based on perceived usefulness and ease of use (Li, 2023), social influence and performance, and effort expec- tancy (Aldreabi et al., 2025). Therefore, understanding stu- dents’ lived experiences qualitatively, using AI in this con- text, will provide real insights about how they used them",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"and ease of use (Li, 2023), social influence and performance, and effort expec- tancy (Aldreabi et al., 2025). Therefore, understanding stu- dents’ lived experiences qualitatively, using AI in this con- text, will provide real insights about how they used them and how their experiences shape their perspectives on AI tools. The purpose of the study is, therefore, to uncover college students’ lived learning experiences and perspectives after engaging with more than 20 AI tools over a semester-long course. Compounding this with the growing need to offer GenAI courses at the degree level, examining this complex social phenomenon in an emerging learning setting, is quin- tessential for qualitative methods (Hoepfl, 1997). Qualita- tive methods allow participants to share rich, in-depth, con- text-specific data (Cooley, 2013; Sherman & Webb, 2004) that may not be quantifiable. Using qualitative research in this study’s context can contribute towards social validity (Kozleski, 2017), as exposing students’ lived experiences in this emerging context can inform pedagogical decisions surrounding AI literacy. UNESCO AI Competency Framework This study draws on research examining AI integration in higher education and GenAI",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"can contribute towards social validity (Kozleski, 2017), as exposing students’ lived experiences in this emerging context can inform pedagogical decisions surrounding AI literacy. UNESCO AI Competency Framework This study draws on research examining AI integration in higher education and GenAI use in media studies, a literature highlighting growing demand for AI literacy yet limited empirical attention to students’ lived experiences. We adopt the UNESCO AI Competency Framework for Students to interpret students’ experiences. The UNESCO AI competency framework for students (UNESCO AI framework) guides this study. This frame- work prescribes instructional directions for AI literacy under four dimensions: (a) human-centered mindset, (b) ethics of AI, (c) AI techniques and applications, and (d) AI system design (Miao et al., 2024). Each component defines what must be learnt and provides instructional guidance under three progressive levels: understand, apply, and cre- ate. To understand each component is to grasp the con- ceptual knowledge of AI and to explain it in everyday life. The ‘Apply’ level involves students transferring conceptual knowledge into techniques that leverage AI usage. The last level, ‘Create’, focuses on students",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"understand each component is to grasp the con- ceptual knowledge of AI and to explain it in everyday life. The ‘Apply’ level involves students transferring conceptual knowledge into techniques that leverage AI usage. The last level, ‘Create’, focuses on students becoming conscien- tious co-creators, designers of AI systems, and responsible users of AI technologies (Miao et al., 2024). The first component, human-centered thinking, is achieved when students recognize that AI is human- led and controlled, and that humans are responsible for the outcomes of its use. Additionally, this component is achieved when students experiment with AI tools and comprehend that there are limits to AI when high-stakes decisions are to be made (Miao et al., 2024). To foster informed ethics of AI, the framework advo- cates that students be exposed to, discuss, and research local and global ethical issues, relevant laws and policies, and practice safe application of AI tools with ethics in mind. To enhance students’ AI techniques and applica- tions, the framework states that students’ basic knowledge of AI, focusing on data and algorithms, stems from experi- menting",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,2,"laws and policies, and practice safe application of AI tools with ethics in mind. To enhance students’ AI techniques and applica- tions, the framework states that students’ basic knowledge of AI, focusing on data and algorithms, stems from experi- menting with diverse AI tools and from critically evaluat- ing and leveraging these technologies (Miao et al., 2024). Finally, AI systems design is achieved when students skillfully use datasets, programming tools, and computer resources to increase their human agency and responsible usage of the technologies (Miao et al., 2024). Succinctly, the UNESCO AI framework for students highlights the importance of building prerequisite skills, such as digi- tal skills, media and information literacy, and exposing students to the implications of AI for human rights, using both technical and non-technical disciplines (Miao et al., 2024). This interdisciplinary approach guides the frame- work’s core beliefs, providing discrete indicators of what and how students should learn to cultivate AI literacy.",155
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"783 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 AI in Interdisciplinary Media Studies Courses Gaining literacy in GenAI technologies and ethical appli- cations is paramount for media studies students. To incor- porate new technologies, media studies programs such as journalism, public relations, advertising, etc., have leaned towards an interdisciplinary procedure (Bright, 2020) since this approach is often adopted to learn com- plex phenomena (Newell & Green, 2012) to better prepare students for intricate working environments (Adam, 2001; Bright, 2020). College students enrolled in these programs are likely to have overlapping professional experience across varied sectors of the media industry that have cur- rently embraced AI on the basis of AI literacy and ethics (Wenger et al., 2025). Importantly, the literature presents three main points on the affairs of integrating AI in. interdisciplinary media studies courses. First, faculty are torn between wanting students to learn about GenAI and worrying about losing the core ethical values of jour- nalism due to AI use (Wenger et al., 2025). While media studies programs are saturated with innovative technol- ogy, AI tools have significantly disrupted both training and professional",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"about GenAI and worrying about losing the core ethical values of jour- nalism due to AI use (Wenger et al., 2025). While media studies programs are saturated with innovative technol- ogy, AI tools have significantly disrupted both training and professional settings when misused. In the case of visual and mass communication, AI tools such as Midjourney and Sora not only reduce students’ manual effort in pro- ducing visual or video products; however, concerns arise regarding students’ creativity, attention to accuracy, and appropriate use of AI tools during assessment (Hwang & Wu, 2025; Wenger & Irme, 2026; Yan & Qianjun, 2025). These concerns sow doubt among instructors who have no guidance on integrating AI tools in their courses. Much of this doubt can be attributed to limited research on stu- dents’ perspectives on the ethical use of AI tools, particu- larly when prolonged exposure to these technologies has occurred in the classroom. Second, researchers have acknowledged that the study of GenAI cannot be conducted within the confines of jour- nalism; exploration across disciplines such as computer science, the social sciences, and",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"when prolonged exposure to these technologies has occurred in the classroom. Second, researchers have acknowledged that the study of GenAI cannot be conducted within the confines of jour- nalism; exploration across disciplines such as computer science, the social sciences, and AI technologies is vital (Broussard et al., 2019). Yan and Qianjun (2025) found that AI tools help students in interdisciplinary settings better comprehend content across disciplines. This idea is aligned with the UNESCO AI framework, which proposes a multidisciplinary approach to AI literacy including soci- etal implications, technical applications, and improving AI system design through programming skills (Miao et al., 2024). This provides a comprehensive approach to learn- ing AI technologies. Lastly, media industries, such as journalism and public relations, have historically developed alongside the rise of emerging technologies, including the radio, television, and the internet (Pavlik, 2010), making this junction with GenAI pivotal. Therefore, considerations for integrating AI tools into courses are crucially dependent on under- standing students’ lived experiences with an AI-driven course that consolidates multiple AI tools in an interdis- ciplinary course delivered in a typical 15-week",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"junction with GenAI pivotal. Therefore, considerations for integrating AI tools into courses are crucially dependent on under- standing students’ lived experiences with an AI-driven course that consolidates multiple AI tools in an interdis- ciplinary course delivered in a typical 15-week semester. Unfortunately, very little empirical research observing students’ experience in a semester-long course has been conducted in a media studies interdisciplinary course. Research Purpose The purpose of this study was to explore the lived learning experiences of college students after completing a semester of an AI-driven course. We are particularly interested in how university students learn with AI tools and what they learn in a semester-long AI-driven advanced interdisciplinary media studies course. Methods Research Participants The research was conducted in the AI for Media Profes- sionals course offered at a northeastern university. Twelve students made up the course cohort, and all completed the course activities presented in Table 3. Of the 12 students, five students emailed the leading author of their interest to participate in the research. Participation in the interviews was 100% voluntary; choosing not to volunteer did not",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"all completed the course activities presented in Table 3. Of the 12 students, five students emailed the leading author of their interest to participate in the research. Participation in the interviews was 100% voluntary; choosing not to volunteer did not affect final grades. Those who volunteered, three were undergradu- ates and two were graduate students. These participants were a mix of full-time undergraduate and graduate students, all domestic students aged 20 to 25; four were media studies majors, and one was enrolled in a different program. Par- ticipants’ program of studies included: audio-visual arts, journalism, public relations, and sports management. All students had used multimedia software prior to the course, and all had varied levels of interaction with an artificial intel- ligence tool for other course-related work and personal usage prior to the AI-driven course. Pseudonyms were applied for each participant for confidentiality as presented in Table 1. Research Context – AI‑Driven Course Content The AI for Media Professionals course aimed to have stu- dents learn and use AI tools for media and mass communi- cations purposes in professional settings.",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,3,"participant for confidentiality as presented in Table 1. Research Context – AI‑Driven Course Content The AI for Media Professionals course aimed to have stu- dents learn and use AI tools for media and mass communi- cations purposes in professional settings. Hence, students learned a repertoire of GenAI tools (see Table 2). GenAI and Large Language Models (LLMs) can produce text, images, video, and audio outputs, primarily in response to text and/or",71
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,4,"784 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 image prompts. Offered by the university’s School of Public Communications, the course was an elective open to any undergraduate and graduate student during the fall semester. It was designed for those interested in using digital technolo- gies and advanced media communication in professional or educational environments. Each class was 1 h and 20 min long, for a total of 2 h and 40 min of instruction per week over 15 weeks. Table 2 shows the variety of AI tools derived from instructional delivery in the classroom. More detailed descriptions of how students used various AI tools are provided below. Text‑to‑Text Text-to-text GenAI tools and LLMs output text-related content when prompted (text-to-text generation) as shown in Fig. 1. Once texts are used for prompting, most often these models respond by producing texts or typed scripts for participants. Participants used LLMs such as ChatGPT, Claude, and others during the course. Table 1 Participants’ Profile Participant Program Major Gender/ Ethnicity Level of study Prior AI Experience Alex Audio-visual Male/Black Master student - final year Limited prompting experience with an",180
