DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"787 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 Data Analysis The interview transcripts were analyzed using thematic analysis, following Braun and Clarke’s (2006) six-phase framework and adopting an inductive approach. Each tran- script served as a unit of analysis. During coding, participant responses were further segmented into meaning units—dis- tinct comments or utterances that conveyed specific ideas or perceptions. First, the two authors independently read all five interview transcripts multiple times to immerse them- selves in the data. They discussed emerging patterns and collaboratively drafted an initial coding book based on their observations. Second, the authors independently blind-coded two transcripts using an Excel spreadsheet. They then met to compare codes, resolve discrepancies, and refine the initial coding book based on their interpretations. Third, using the updated coding book, the authors independently blind-coded the remaining three transcripts. They reconvened to address inconsistencies and further refine the coding framework. Fourth, the authors re-coded all five transcripts using the finalized coding book. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion until full consensus (100% agreement) was achieved. Fifth, the entire research team reviewed the final- ized codes and collaboratively identified",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"the coding framework. Fourth, the authors re-coded all five transcripts using the finalized coding book. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion until full consensus (100% agreement) was achieved. Fifth, the entire research team reviewed the final- ized codes and collaboratively identified broader categories and overarching themes grounded in the data. Sixth, themes were further refined and defined (see Table 4) through itera- tive team discussions, ensuring they accurately represented participants’ perspectives and were clearly aligned with the study’s research questions. Results We discovered four themes and 14 categories. The first theme, “Learning AI tools actively”, reflects how students learn AI tools. The remaining three themes, including “Understanding what using AI tools means”, “Being aware of ethical concerns when using AI tools”, and “Understand- ing what AI means in a professional field”, reflect respec- tively what students learn about AI in a semester-long AI-driven media course. Using UNESCO’s artificial intel- ligence competency framework for students, we interpret our findings that contribute to three components: human- centered mindset, ethics of AI, and AI techniques and appli- cations, across two of its progressive levels:",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"semester-long AI-driven media course. Using UNESCO’s artificial intel- ligence competency framework for students, we interpret our findings that contribute to three components: human- centered mindset, ethics of AI, and AI techniques and appli- cations, across two of its progressive levels: understanding and applying (Miao et al., 2024). Understanding What Using AI Tools Means As students became incrementally exposed to GenAI tools within the advanced media course, our study found that pro- gressive application of multiple GenAI tools led to a deeper comprehension of how the tools can be used. Students discovered that the tools provided affordances beyond the usual and shallow avails. Bianca recognized that complex, novel knowledge, and hands-on practice could easily be bro- ken down by requesting GenAI tools to provide scaffolding. She noted: I would ask AI to like kind of like break it down [in] steps. For example, if I use it for a class to help me with a test, I don’t ask it, ‘What is AI?’. I asked, ‘make me a study guide’, which really helps me. These sentiments are also reflected in Christine’s",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"For example, if I use it for a class to help me with a test, I don’t ask it, ‘What is AI?’. I asked, ‘make me a study guide’, which really helps me. These sentiments are also reflected in Christine’s and Alex’s experiences. They found creative ways to manipu- late GenAI tools through altering outputs, utilize AI-cross referencing (that is, using similar inputs and comparing var- ied outputs from multiple GenAI tools), and using GenAI tools as a springboard to flesh out and jumpstart ideas. For Christine, as she spent each week in the semester exploring new GenAI tools, she has fostered an understanding that “… AI does it all. So instead of working harder, you’re working smarter.” Alex also described how he was able to manipu- late AI tools to enrich and optimize prompting behaviors for other GenAI tools. He reported: It was like a game of chess, you had to think like two or three steps ahead … One of the things that helped me was using the persona [method] to generate prompts from ChatGPT. [I would] use",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"He reported: It was like a game of chess, you had to think like two or three steps ahead … One of the things that helped me was using the persona [method] to generate prompts from ChatGPT. [I would] use that for the prompt for another AI tool. Thus, the pattern shows that students are likely to enhance their manipulation of AI tools and recognize the efficiency of AI tools when multiple technologies are involved. From participants’ lived experiences, increased manipulation has led to stronger metacognition, efficient prompt engineer- ing, and deeper computational thinking when working with AI tools. The UNESCO AI framework’s human-centered mindset component states that literacy and competency are achieved when students foster an understanding that GenAI tools are human-led. Human control over decision-making and deployment can address concerns that AI will replace human thinking and actions, according to the framework. Thus, the participants have cultivated a human-centered mindset in which human intellect is synonymous with the effective, feasible, and meaningful use of GenAI tools. Learning AI Tools Actively Our findings also show that developing a keen",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,7,"according to the framework. Thus, the participants have cultivated a human-centered mindset in which human intellect is synonymous with the effective, feasible, and meaningful use of GenAI tools. Learning AI Tools Actively Our findings also show that developing a keen understand- ing of AI tools meant that students were actively engaged in learning the GenAI technologies. Several participants employed self-directed research by seeking out commu- nity-based learning through online forums. For instance, Alex relied on YouTube to master complex software and",81
