DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,9,"789 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 Bianca sought Reddit to capture a specific AI generated photographic effect. She expressed: I wanted to make a Polaroid with three people in it, and there wasn’t anything in class to do with that. So, I went on online … I used Reddit a lot. Seeing what other people have done in the past really helped me. This pattern shows that students are proactive in expanding their learning on AI tools by using commu- nity-based learning platforms such as online forums. A component of UNESCO AI framework, AI application and techniques, explains that students increase their literacy and competency by investigative means as well as con- necting the conceptual with daily activities. Digital spaces such as social media forums are commonly used by college students due to the prevalence of mass content creation. Hence, learning about GenAI tools occurs beyond formal instructions and inevitably infiltrates the student’s personal and routine digital spaces. Also, through 15 weeks of experimenting with over 20 GenAI tools that generated images, videos, text, and audio, our study discovered that students creatively",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,9,"GenAI tools occurs beyond formal instructions and inevitably infiltrates the student’s personal and routine digital spaces. Also, through 15 weeks of experimenting with over 20 GenAI tools that generated images, videos, text, and audio, our study discovered that students creatively com- bined the tools to achieve desirable generated outputs. For instance, Emily and Christine described that to achieve certain coursework activities, they would intentionally select from the plethora of GenAI tools they perceived as most effective and appropriate. However, when in doubt, they retreated to large language models like ChatGPT and Claude to provide effective prompts for other AI tools. For instance, Emily shared her experience: I would go to one of the image generators and I… start generating still images … I would use Chat- GPT or Claude to give me specific prompts for those images that would fall in alignment with the visual look of the [video] campaign. Christine also expressed how valuable it was to move still images from Midjourney (image generator) and inte- grate them via skillful prompts to RunwayML and Flux (video generators) for video",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,9,"in alignment with the visual look of the [video] campaign. Christine also expressed how valuable it was to move still images from Midjourney (image generator) and inte- grate them via skillful prompts to RunwayML and Flux (video generators) for video qualities. Daniel claimed to notice that some GenAI tools were compatible, and over time, it was easier for him to navigate from one tool to another. This finding shows that students’ learner agency increased when using multiple AI tools. Students engaged in learning behaviors such as side-by-side testing and par- allel testing of outputs from multiple AI tools to determine efficiency and ease of navigation with the technologies. These patterns suggest increased critical and evaluative thinking. The UNESCO AI framework component, AI techniques and applications, validates that literacy is fos- tered as students progress from conceptual knowledge of AI tools to informed application of the tools. Table 4 (continued) Themes Categories Codes Example Statements Understanding what AI means in a professional field Time efficiency Reduce working time “You’re cutting down potentially hours and days of travel to shoot a 30",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,9,"to informed application of the tools. Table 4 (continued) Themes Categories Codes Example Statements Understanding what AI means in a professional field Time efficiency Reduce working time “You’re cutting down potentially hours and days of travel to shoot a 30 s ad, you could whip something out of a couple hours/days” Creativity Impact on creativity, by allowing a different per- spective “It provides a very new perspective. It should be something that is enhancing your work” Job loss Concerns about AI replacing jobs “It’s a hard trade, you’re more efficient with your tasks. I can use something Midjourney to make a trailer video, but there’s also illustrators and video editors, people who are really good at it and who have studied their art.” Professional settings Perceptions of using AI in the workplace “If you were presenting an important thing to your boss, you wouldn’t want to just solely have relied on AI”",152
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"790 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 Being Aware of Ethical Concerns when Using AI Tools As a result of completing a semester-long AI-driven course in advanced media, our study also discovered that partici- pants gained several ethical concerns about GenAI usage. First, concerns about misinformation and deepfakes were particularly significant for those in public relations and journalism programs. Bianca expressed that when she dis- covered that specific GenAI tools can mimic human voice and facial qualities, she became concerned about how AI tools can be used to spread misinformation through deepfake videos. She scrutinized the nonconsensual use of someone’s likeness and voice to spread rhetoric, which can have damag- ing ramifications. Emily, a journalism major, also noted the prospect of GenAI tools being used to produce misleading information via news articles. Second, participants gained ethical concerns about the data used by the technologies. For instance, Alex expressed his uneasiness after entering personal data into ChatGPT and Daniel questioned whether the visual outputs generated from Midjourney were his own, considering that the image data used for training was from people’s work. Emily also",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"technologies. For instance, Alex expressed his uneasiness after entering personal data into ChatGPT and Daniel questioned whether the visual outputs generated from Midjourney were his own, considering that the image data used for training was from people’s work. Emily also equally conveyed that I think for me I was opened to the idea of [using] human images over the use of avatar within certain AI tool, because it felt more real. But then when I learned about deepfakes and all, people’s data can be used. I had to make some shift on that. Finally, participants highlighted environmental concerns that they consider when using large language models. Both Emily and Bianca were particularly disquieted by the mag- nitude of natural resources consumed to keep GenAI tools functioning. Bianca revealed that: … I think [an AI tool] it’s such a cool thing but also, it’s not very environmentally friendly. You have to use big servers and … the servers get so hot you have to use water to cool them down … every single time you ask ChatGPT a question, it’s a",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"but also, it’s not very environmentally friendly. You have to use big servers and … the servers get so hot you have to use water to cool them down … every single time you ask ChatGPT a question, it’s a lot of water usage. These patterns demonstrated that students experienced a paradigm shift in their understanding of GenAI tools as they used and applied the technologies. Working with AI tools alongside pedagogical workflows (discussions, applications, research, and experimentation), college students acquired ethical perspectives and considerations for: usage of AI tools for malicious intent, data privacy and exploitation, ownership of generated outputs, and environmental conse- quences. Thus, AI ethics, a component of the UNESCO AI framework, supports the recognition that the principles of protecting personal data, addressing the potential harms of unethical AI use, and responsible use of AI tools are critical to AI literacy. Also, AI literacy and competency are incom- plete if students apply AI tools without reflective and critical considerations of the impact that the technologies have on the natural environment. Therefore, fostering self-awareness and consideration when using",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"critical to AI literacy. Also, AI literacy and competency are incom- plete if students apply AI tools without reflective and critical considerations of the impact that the technologies have on the natural environment. Therefore, fostering self-awareness and consideration when using AI tools are necessary parts of being literate in these technologies. Understanding What Using AI Means in the Professional Environment The application of AI tools in the professional context was imperative for participants, as most were nearing the com- pletion of their programs. Several patterns emerged from our analysis: participants were able to highlight the affor- dances of GenAI tools within their respective professional spaces. First, participants reported that AI tools can reduce time and enhance work efficiency by completing tasks that are not necessarily pertinent, such as creating search engine optimization (SEO) headlines for journalists who may have other, more crucial tasks to do. Emily, a graduate journalism student, suggested that the technologies can permit journal- ists to put their skills and training to better use than creating SEO tags. Daniel also shared that in cases where projects demand",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"more crucial tasks to do. Emily, a graduate journalism student, suggested that the technologies can permit journal- ists to put their skills and training to better use than creating SEO tags. Daniel also shared that in cases where projects demand ideas, and there are difficulties in doing so, GenAI tools can evoke relevant ideas. Most notably, participants expressed their value for the human element in workspaces. They appraised the replace- ment of trained people, assessed AI tools’ lack of human conditions, and highlighted the responsibility to provide human-made content to the public. Alex conveyed that while Midjourney can be used to make a trailer video, illustra- tors and editors are qualified to do their jobs effectively, and Emily downplayed the artistic integrity of generated or automated artwork. Participants also expressed that AI tools do not possess human qualities to independently create meaningful prod- ucts for other humans. Alex raised a significant point that the music creation process does not need to include ChatGPT. Emily also stated that AI image creators are not artists, and inputting prompts and retrieving generated images",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,10,"create meaningful prod- ucts for other humans. Alex raised a significant point that the music creation process does not need to include ChatGPT. Emily also stated that AI image creators are not artists, and inputting prompts and retrieving generated images do not require artistic skills. Bianca further elaborated that humans have emotions and feelings that machines cannot acquire; in decision-making, empathy and other human qualities are necessary. And both Emily and Christine conveyed that jour- nalists are the voices for the people. Christine expressed: … they trust you. They listen to you, and they expect you to disseminate quality coverage, not something generated by a computer. The participants’ expressions aligned with the UNE- SCO AI framework’s human-centered mindset component, in which AI literacy occurs when students can query the",129
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,11,"791 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 automation of human writing and artistic feats and the repercussions that result. Overall, from these patterns, we note that participants, when faced with the nuances of GenAI tools and their overall affordances, still vouched for human agency and the relevance of human involvement in high-stakes deci- sions and cultural contributions. These findings reveal that participants prioritized veritable skills development over total reliance on AI tools. Participants trusted human embodiments such as emotions, creativity, and authen- ticity over the sophisticated features of AI tools. Also, participants acknowledged that human consumption of transparent and accurate information is reliant on respon- sible societal stewardship. The UNESCO AI framework’s human-centered mindset component corroborated the need to recognize that human agency is required for stu- dents to develop AI literacy and an understanding of the human-machine dynamics for comprehension and applica- tion. For the framework, to achieve purposeful use of AI tools, it is imperative that students nurture an attitude that automation has limits and that illiteracy and irresponsible use of AI tools can undermine human intellect. Discussion The primary purpose",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,11,"tion. For the framework, to achieve purposeful use of AI tools, it is imperative that students nurture an attitude that automation has limits and that illiteracy and irresponsible use of AI tools can undermine human intellect. Discussion The primary purpose of this qualitative study was to reveal college students’ lived learning experiences and perspectives about GenAI tools after completing a 15-week semester that consolidated over 20 generative AI tools. Table 5 presents connections between the study’s four main themes, related findings, and the UNESCO framework’s components and progressive levels with which they align. Findings were situ- ated within UNESCO’s four components, where the “Under- standing” level was achieved to a greater extent than the “Apply” level. In our study, we did not identify evidence of AI systems design or the “Create” level, as students’ course experiences did not reflect the systemic or engineering design of AI tools, nor did they demonstrate an ethics-by- design approach. Table 5 Summarized Contributions to the UNESCO AI Framework UNESCO AI competency framework Study themes Study findings Progressive levels Human-centered mindset Gained insights into the",180
