DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"792 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 Unlike existing concerns that AI tool use can lead to over-reliance (Pan et al., 2024) and reduced critical thinking (Weng et al., 2025), we found that students engaged in active learning behaviors when learning about multiple AI tools. These included self-directed research using community- based online platforms, such as Reddit, to enhance compre- hension and practical application of GenAI tools. Students also engaged in experimentation and evaluation of AI tech- niques to promote desirable generated outputs. For instance, the use of parallel testing and AI personas indicates that stu- dents, over time, moved towards more critical and computa- tional thinking when working and learning with multiple AI tools. These findings support the UNESCO AI framework’s human-centered mindset component, which holds that if instructors want to cultivate strong AI literacy, pedagogical design must foster students’ awareness that AI is human-led, human-controlled, and governed by human intellect (Miao et al., 2024). Therefore, we recommend that instructional designers develop critical learning workflows that encourage college students to engage in self-directed learning. These include independently researching, exploring relevant com- munity",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"AI is human-led, human-controlled, and governed by human intellect (Miao et al., 2024). Therefore, we recommend that instructional designers develop critical learning workflows that encourage college students to engage in self-directed learning. These include independently researching, exploring relevant com- munity or social media platforms where ideas are shared, hands-on experimentation with AI tools within the context of their program or career interest, and in-class oral reports on discoveries. These instructional approaches can cultivate students’ human-led attitude towards GenAI tools. Our study also highlights that while actively learning about GenAI tools, students recognized and applied the technologies beyond superficial uses. Over time, students expressed that they used AI tools for scaffolding support, intentionally selected AI tools that were pertinent towards their learning goals and expected outcomes, used LLMs to gain optimum prompts to generate efficient outputs for image and video AI tools, and made logical choices when combin- ing AI tools to complete course activities. This finding goes beyond the literature that has focused on how a single LLM output can contribute to college students’ higher-order think- ing skills when using",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"tools, and made logical choices when combin- ing AI tools to complete course activities. This finding goes beyond the literature that has focused on how a single LLM output can contribute to college students’ higher-order think- ing skills when using an AI tool (Du et al., 2025). While the UNESCO AI framework’s AI techniques and applications component advocates for strong computer programming training to enhance AI literacy (Miao et al., 2024), our findings suggest a different pathway. Students’ experiences indicate that, when working with multiple and varied GenAI tools, they leverage familiarity with one tool to use unfamiliar tools more efficiently by discovering new techniques and refining applications. For example, students used ChatGPT to learn about Midjourney and to develop or improve prompts. This also indicates adaptation to new and unfamiliar technologies, a non-technical skill set that is desirable in the workplace (Sá & Serpa, 2022). Therefore, recommendations for instructional designers and key stake- holders include granting college students access to campus- wide AI tools and accommodations for subscribed AI tools. In that vein, exposure to contextually relevant yet nuanced",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"the workplace (Sá & Serpa, 2022). Therefore, recommendations for instructional designers and key stake- holders include granting college students access to campus- wide AI tools and accommodations for subscribed AI tools. In that vein, exposure to contextually relevant yet nuanced text, image, video, and/or audio GenAI tools, once inte- grated with critical pedagogical workflows (formal instruc- tions, inviting professionals to present on applications and techniques, in-class discussions, and promoting discovery learning in coursework activities), can increase overall AI literacy. In addition to the robust application of multiple AI tools, our study found that students highly valued human skill sets over the affordances of automation. This finding aligns with existing literature (Hwang & Lee, 2025), especially for jour- nalism students who believe that mass communication duties are high-stakes and should involve full human involvement rather than AI tools (Veenstra et al., 2024). Our findings also support the UNESCO AI framework in promoting a human-centered mindset by implementing case studies on societal dilemmas related to human control of AI. We also advocate to instructional designers that college students not only discuss the",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"2024). Our findings also support the UNESCO AI framework in promoting a human-centered mindset by implementing case studies on societal dilemmas related to human control of AI. We also advocate to instructional designers that college students not only discuss the implications of poorly designed and poorly regulated AI tools but also safely explore GenAI tools rel- evant to their field, while taking note of the potential reper- cussions of detrimental GenAI. Such exploration can bring to light the ethical pitfalls of poorly controlled automation across education and the workplace, thereby enhancing lit- eracy in AI tools. Finally, students cultivated ethical considerations while working with multiple GenAI tools. Across the ethics board, malicious use of AI, data privacy concerns, own- ership queries, and environmental considerations were shared concerns among students. This finding substantially strengthened the UNESCO AI framework for the AI ethics component. According to the UNESCO framework (Miao et al., 2024), understanding human rights, social justice, and climate change within the confines of AI usage is critical to AI literacy. Pedagogical suggestions from the framework include having students create infographics",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"ethics component. According to the UNESCO framework (Miao et al., 2024), understanding human rights, social justice, and climate change within the confines of AI usage is critical to AI literacy. Pedagogical suggestions from the framework include having students create infographics and participate in group discussions on relevant societal happenings (Miao et al., 2024). We suggest that instructional designers create activities that help students engage with AI in different ways (see Table 3). We note the importance of discipline-targeted activities such as creating a campaign video or advertise- ment, which allow students to not only experience the affordances of AI tools but also to understand the potential harms, such as deepfakes, that can occur in their respective fields. Such relevance not only piques students’ interest but also increases their motivation and volition (Keller, 2008) throughout the course. Our study contributes to the literature on college stu- dents’ experiences with AI tools in three ways. First, the qualitative findings provide an in-depth understanding of students’ lived learning experiences and the perspec- tives gained through sustained engagement with AI tools over an entire",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,12,"the literature on college stu- dents’ experiences with AI tools in three ways. First, the qualitative findings provide an in-depth understanding of students’ lived learning experiences and the perspec- tives gained through sustained engagement with AI tools over an entire semester. Other researchers concerned",44
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,"793 TechTrends (2026) 70:781–795 with interdisciplinary and media learning contexts have found that after working with multiple AI tools, students recognized the benefits in their design and content out- put (Hwang & Wu, 2025; Mou, 2026; Yan & Qianjun, 2025). However, given the limited qualitative research in this area, our study further demonstrates that while stu- dents recognized the benefits of using AI tools in their disciplines, they acquired nuanced perspectives along the way. They particularly questioned whether AI-generated design outputs were their own or products of the data the model was trained on and defined what creativity meant when multiple AI tools formed part of the design process. Second, our study captures semester-long experiences with more than 20 diverse AI tools rather than just a few tools, offering new insights into this emerging learning context. Prior research has typically examined research- designed workshops implemented for short durations, such as four weeks or two hours, with limited AI tools (Hwang & Wu, 2025; Yan & Qianjun, 2025; Zhong et al., 2024). Researchers have acknowledged that these constrained timelines may limit",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,"examined research- designed workshops implemented for short durations, such as four weeks or two hours, with limited AI tools (Hwang & Wu, 2025; Yan & Qianjun, 2025; Zhong et al., 2024). Researchers have acknowledged that these constrained timelines may limit their findings (Zhong et al., 2024). Therefore, the breadth and duration of engagement likely shaped students’ perspectives in unique ways, and our study illuminates these distinctive experiences. Third, our study applied the UNESCO AI Competency Framework for Students and provided empirical evidence supporting its applicability. By systematically organizing and compar- ing our findings against this framework, we strengthened its applicability to higher education contexts. Limitations Although this study offers valuable insights into college stu- dents’ experiences and perspectives after a full semester of exposure to generative AI tools, some limitations should be acknowledged. First, only five of the twelve enrolled stu- dents participated in the study, which may limit the repre- sentativeness of the findings and may not fully capture the experiences and perspectives of all students in the course. Furthermore, data saturation was not explicitly assessed; additional interviews with",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,"stu- dents participated in the study, which may limit the repre- sentativeness of the findings and may not fully capture the experiences and perspectives of all students in the course. Furthermore, data saturation was not explicitly assessed; additional interviews with the remaining participants could have revealed new themes or perspectives. Future studies should consider employing purposive sampling strategies with larger samples to strengthen the transferability of find- ings. Second, the study was conducted within the context of an advanced media course, so the results specifically reflect students’ use of GenAI tools for media- and mass communication-related tasks. In other disciplines, where tasks and learning objectives differ, students might engage with GenAI tools differently. Therefore, caution should be exercised when generalizing these findings beyond the con- text of media and mass communication education. Appendix A: Interview Questions Protocol A. When presented with a wide range of AI tools, what varied ways do students use AI tools to conduct media production? 1. Tell me about how you learn an AI tool. 2. When attempting your assignments or projects how do you determine",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,"with a wide range of AI tools, what varied ways do students use AI tools to conduct media production? 1. Tell me about how you learn an AI tool. 2. When attempting your assignments or projects how do you determine which AI tools to use? 3. What techniques did you discover when using these AI tools for media production? 4. How does knowing about these tools and their functions affect the way you prepare and approach media projects versus when you did not know about them? B. How did college students apply multiple AI tools in their professional practice? 1. How do you perceive the efficiency of AI tools in profes- sional settings, particularly in terms of saving time and being helpful even without extensive prior knowledge or skills? 2. How has the quality of your work been impacted by using AI tools? Do you feel that AI has improved the quality of your work? If so, in what ways? If not, why do you think that is? 3. How do AI tools influence your creativity in professional tasks? Do",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,"using AI tools? Do you feel that AI has improved the quality of your work? If so, in what ways? If not, why do you think that is? 3. How do AI tools influence your creativity in professional tasks? Do they generate new ideas or approaches you might not have considered without their help? 4. What does using AI tools in a professional context mean to you? How do you feel about incorporating AI into your professional work? C. What ethical perspectives do college students gain, eliminate or sustain in an AI-driven course over time? 1. Can you recall any moment you were concerned about ethical issues related to AI usage? What was it? How did you address it? 2. Before taking this course what were your thoughts on the ethical implications of AI tools on the media production industry or other fields? 3. Were there moments while doing your assignments/pro- jects or during class where you had to reconsider any of your existing ethical views on AI tools? 4. What ethical concerns do you think are most important when",180
DOC_051,s11528-026-01191-4.pdf,10.1007/s11528-026-01191-4,13,or other fields? 3. Were there moments while doing your assignments/pro- jects or during class where you had to reconsider any of your existing ethical views on AI tools? 4. What ethical concerns do you think are most important when using AI tools in educational and professional set- tings? 5. How has your view on AI ethics changed since you started this AI course?,64
