DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,7,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 Business Understanding This research follows the CRISP-DM methodology, where the Business Understand­ ing phase identifies practical objectives for the use of data science. In an educational context, the “business” aim is enhancing assessment process in higher education using automation to grade open-ended student responses. The aim is to improve summative feedback timeliness, consistency, and objectivity, mainly in large-scale courses where manual grading can be time-consuming for the educator. Traditional manual grading approaches, while thorough, often face challenges related to scalabil­ ity, consistency, and timely feedback (Burrows et al., 2015). These limitations can hinder student learning and retention, particularly in courses with large enrollments (Gao et al., 2024). This study aims to evaluate the capability of state-of-the-art models to address these challenges by providing accurate and reliable assessments that closely align with human grading standards. A systematic comparison of the performance of vari­ ous GenAI models was done, including GPTo1, Claude3, PaLM2, Mistral7B, Cohere, Llama2, and sentence embedding models, including SBERT, USE, T5, RoBERTa, BERT against human grading benchmarks. By analyzing each",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,7,"human grading standards. A systematic comparison of the performance of vari­ ous GenAI models was done, including GPTo1, Claude3, PaLM2, Mistral7B, Cohere, Llama2, and sentence embedding models, including SBERT, USE, T5, RoBERTa, BERT against human grading benchmarks. By analyzing each model’s ability to cap­ ture semantic nuances and evaluate content depth, it was assessed how well these models replicate human judgment in grading open-ended questions. Similarity scores generated by the models are mapped to a predefined grading scale, enabling a direct comparison of their grading accuracy with human evaluators. Beyond grading accu­ racy, the study emphasizes the reliability of GenAI models in aligning with human evaluators. Inter-rater reliability metrics were utilized, such as Cohen’s Kappa, to quantify agreement between human graders and compare it with model performance, which offers a critical evaluation of automated assessment (L. Jiang & Bosch, 2024). A key focus is on minimizing grading errors, particularly false positives (over-grad­ ing) and false negatives (under-grading), to ensure consistent and fair assessments. This analysis is vital for evaluating the models’ objectivity and their ability to provide Fig. 1 An",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,7,"A key focus is on minimizing grading errors, particularly false positives (over-grad­ ing) and false negatives (under-grading), to ensure consistent and fair assessments. This analysis is vital for evaluating the models’ objectivity and their ability to provide Fig. 1 An Outline of the CRISP-DM Model that has been Adopted 1 3 3819",52
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,8,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 reliable feedback to students. To better understand how GenAI models interact with student responses, this research examines the impact of response features—such as length, complexity, and semantic coherence—on model performance. By leverag­ ing Natural Language Inference techniques, was evaluated how logical consistency and relevance in student answers influence model accuracy and error patterns. These insights based on related research (Condor et al., 2021; Hutt et al., 2024) aim to identify specific response types that may pose challenges for automated grading and inform future improvements in model design. This research explores the broader implications of integrating GenAI models into educational assessment processes. By examining their potential to streamline grading workflows while maintaining high standards of accuracy and fairness, the goal is to establish these models as practical tools for educators. With their advanced language processing capabilities, GenAI models offer a pathway to managing large volumes of student responses efficiently, providing timely feedback without com­ promising on quality or integrity. Through this approach, the aim is to identify the most suitable GenAI models",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,8,"advanced language processing capabilities, GenAI models offer a pathway to managing large volumes of student responses efficiently, providing timely feedback without com­ promising on quality or integrity. Through this approach, the aim is to identify the most suitable GenAI models for automated assessment and to propose a framework for their deployment in diverse educational contexts. The findings should contribute to advancing objective and efficient grading practices, benefiting both online and traditional learning environments. By comparing the performance of various models against human graded answers, the study seeks to answer several critical research questions: ● RQ1: Which generative AI model offers the most accurate assessment of open- ended questions compared to human evaluations? ● RQ2: To what extent can generative AI models reduce grading errors, specifically false positives (over-grading) and false negatives (under-grading), while main­ taining consistency with human evaluators? ● RQ3: How do specific characteristics of student responses (e.g., length, complex­ ity, or semantic coherence) impact the performance and reliability of generative AI models in automated grading? Firstly, a reliable benchmark was established by having two human graders indepen­ dently",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,8,"RQ3: How do specific characteristics of student responses (e.g., length, complex­ ity, or semantic coherence) impact the performance and reliability of generative AI models in automated grading? Firstly, a reliable benchmark was established by having two human graders indepen­ dently assess a representative set of student responses. This step ensured a robust comparison standard for evaluating the performance of GenAI models. The perfor­ mance of selected state-of-the-art models was tested, including BERT, RoBERTa, T5, GPTo1, and others, by comparing their assessments to the human baseline (RQ1). Special focus was placed on analyzing grading errors, particularly false positives (overestimating a student’s response) and false negatives (underestimating a student’s response), to determine the consistency and reliability of each model’s performance (RQ2). To explore how specific features of student responses affect AI model perfor­ mance, a detailed analysis of the data was conducted (RQ3). This involved segment­ ing responses based on features such as length, syntactic complexity, and semantic coherence. Additionally, Natural Language Inference (NLI) techniques were used to evaluate the logical consistency and relevance of responses to the posed questions (MacCartney et",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,8,"(RQ3). This involved segment­ ing responses based on features such as length, syntactic complexity, and semantic coherence. Additionally, Natural Language Inference (NLI) techniques were used to evaluate the logical consistency and relevance of responses to the posed questions (MacCartney et al., 2008; MacCartney & Manning, 2008). By using NLI, the aim was to assess how well the models understand and align student responses with the 1 3 3820",68
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,9,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 intended meaning of the question prompts. This combined approach had allowed to evaluate how these characteristics—response structure, coherence, and logical align­ ment—influence the model’s accuracy and error patterns. For example, NLI methods can highlight cases where the model’s grading diverges from human evaluators due to misinterpretation of nuanced arguments or logical relationships. This analysis had helped to identify potential response types or features that may pose challenges for automated grading systems, paving the way for targeted improvements in both pre­ processing and model training. The findings from each step inform improvements in preprocessing techniques and model optimization. Moreover, they provide actionable insights into how GenAI can be better integrated into educational contexts to enhance grading accuracy and efficiency at scale (Fu et al., 2024). By following this methodology, the aim is to thoroughly evaluate the performance of GenAI models, uncover their limitations, and propose practical recommendations for their effective deployment in online learning environments. Data Understanding The dataset used in this study consists of anonymized responses from students enrolled in a",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,9,"to thoroughly evaluate the performance of GenAI models, uncover their limitations, and propose practical recommendations for their effective deployment in online learning environments. Data Understanding The dataset used in this study consists of anonymized responses from students enrolled in a blended Software Engineering course within the Applied Informatics study program at Constantine the Philosopher University in Nitra. The course was delivered face-to-face with the support of the Moodle Learning Management System (LMS), which was used for distributing assignments and collecting students answers. The course included 24 open-ended questions distributed across various modules, covering essential concepts in software engineering, such as software requirements, UML diagrams, software development models, and agile methodologies. These ques­ tions were designed to assess students’ comprehension and critical thinking, with responses reflecting a range of understanding from basic definitions to complex explanations. The dataset used in this study was specifically created by authors as part of the research activities conducted under the project “Design and Evaluation of New Models for Automated Grading of Open-Ended and Programming Assignments with Generative Artificial Intelligence Support” (project code: 09I03-03-V04-000596). In total,",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,9,"study was specifically created by authors as part of the research activities conducted under the project “Design and Evaluation of New Models for Automated Grading of Open-Ended and Programming Assignments with Generative Artificial Intelligence Support” (project code: 09I03-03-V04-000596). In total, the dataset includes 1,885 responses from 110 unique students. Each record in the dataset contains the following fields: ● Student ID: An anonymized identifier for each student. ● Question: The specific open-ended question to which the student responded. ● Student Answer: The full text of the student’s answer. ● Reference Answer: A model answer based on course materials, serving as a base­ line for evaluating the student’s response. ● Grade by Rater 1 and Rater 2: Scores assigned by two human evaluators, ensur­ ing that the dataset reflects multiple perspectives for comparison with automated grading. Given that the course was voluntary and spanned a 13-week semester, the dataset reveals a decline in engagement as the semester progressed. While the initial questions were 1 3 3821",165
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,10,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 answered by approximately 110 students, participation dropped to around 60 students for later questions. This decrease in engagement is partially attributed to delays in receiving feedback, which likely affected students’ motivation to continue participating actively. During in-person lab sessions in blended course, students frequently expressed frustration with the long delays in receiving feedback on their answers. They indi­ cated that waiting extended periods for grades made it difficult to understand what mistakes they had made or how to improve, which they perceived as demotivating. This feedback suggests that timely assessment could play a key role in maintaining engagement and motivation throughout the course. Therefore, this dataset, with its varied levels of engagement across different stages of the course, offers a valuable foundation for evaluating the potential of GenAI models to provide timely and accu­ rate assessments, ultimately addressing the challenge of sustaining student motiva­ tion through prompt feedback. Data Preparation The answers were obtained from the LMS Moodle and transformed into raw texts. Reference answers were created based on course materials",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,10,"timely and accu­ rate assessments, ultimately addressing the challenge of sustaining student motiva­ tion through prompt feedback. Data Preparation The answers were obtained from the LMS Moodle and transformed into raw texts. Reference answers were created based on course materials and together with the question, student answer and grades by two teachers constructed the evaluated data­ set. Overall, the student answers are short. The average answer length is around 23 words of which almost 10 are stop words (e.g. the, and, of). This means that relevant information in answer is contained in around 13 words. The students focused mainly on nouns (34%) and less on adjectives (9%) in their answers. Verbs (12%), adposi­ tions (10%) and determiners (11%) had a similar proportion in the student answers. This suggests that students focused on key phrases and definition-like responses without unnecessary repetition. This is confirmed by the lexical diversity metrics results, where Type-Token Ratio (TTR = 0.876) score shows that answers consist mainly of unique words. The results of lexical diversity confirm that students used a wide and varied vocabulary that could",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,10,"This is confirmed by the lexical diversity metrics results, where Type-Token Ratio (TTR = 0.876) score shows that answers consist mainly of unique words. The results of lexical diversity confirm that students used a wide and varied vocabulary that could indicate advanced writing skills. Complexity of answers was examined using readability metric (Kincaid et al., 1975), where high scores of Flesch-Kincaid Grade (FKG = 14.79) indicate that the answers are complex and suitable for readers with college-level education. These readability scores sug­ gest that the answers are appropriate for students at a college level, reflecting their expected linguistic and cognitive skills. Overall, these results confirm that students used sophisticated and varied vocabulary, which is suitable for academic-level writ­ ing. Importantly, although some questions initially appeared to assess only factual recall, students were explicitly required to explain their answers in their own words. Responses that consisted solely of listing terms without any accompanying explana­ tion (e.g., simply stating “functional and non-functional” for software requirements) were considered incomplete and consistently graded as ‘Fx’ by human evaluators. Therefore, the linguistic properties of student",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,10,"own words. Responses that consisted solely of listing terms without any accompanying explana­ tion (e.g., simply stating “functional and non-functional” for software requirements) were considered incomplete and consistently graded as ‘Fx’ by human evaluators. Therefore, the linguistic properties of student answers—such as clarity, coherence, and richness of expression—were essential not only for demonstrating knowledge but also for achieving passing grades. This context highlights the relevance of ana­ lyzing lexical diversity and complexity when evaluating the challenges faced by automated grading models in interpreting open-ended responses. 1 3 3822",88
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,11,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 The responses were evaluated by two independent graders. Unweighted Cohen’s Kappa was used to examine the agreement between the two teachers. The classifica­ tion of agreement in case of various agreement metrics in this paper is as follows: poor: < 0.00; slight: 0.00–0.20; fair: 0.21–0.40; moderate: 0.41–0.60; substantial: 0.61–0.80; almost perfect: 0.81–1.00. Almost perfect agreement (Kappa = 0.84) was identified between the human graders using unweighted Cohen’s Kappa. This score is already high but the unweighted Cohen’s Kappa does not consider the degree of difference between the grades. Open-ended questions aim is not to create a perfect definition and because of that, quadratic weighted Cohen’s Kappa (QWK) was used to compare the teacher’s agreement, which was identified as almost perfect agreement (QWK = 0.95). Based on this it was possible to compare the output of GenAI and sentence embed­ ding models with two human graders and obtain a relevant picture of the quality of the automatic assessment of open-ended questions. This high agreement was not obtained for all questions, question 13",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,11,"compare the output of GenAI and sentence embed­ ding models with two human graders and obtain a relevant picture of the quality of the automatic assessment of open-ended questions. This high agreement was not obtained for all questions, question 13 focused on waterfall model showed only a substantial agreement (QWK = 0.66) and confirmed that the assessment was done independently, and also human graders can assess the open-ended questions with a slight difference. Modeling Modeling is an essential part of the CRISP-DM methodology (Fig. 1), in which the automated assessment of open-ended student responses was applied, and a combi­ nation of state-of-the-art GenAI models and high-performance sentence embedding models was employed, each selected for their distinct strengths in semantic similarity computation and contextual understanding. This integration provides a comprehen­ sive framework for evaluating student responses with precision. Below, the utilized models and their specific configurations are outlined: SBERT (Sentence-BERT), implemented through the sentence-transformers library (Reimers & Gurevych,2019), was configured with the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 vari­ ant. This model is optimized for semantic similarity tasks and transforms text into dense vector embeddings, enabling",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,11,"models and their specific configurations are outlined: SBERT (Sentence-BERT), implemented through the sentence-transformers library (Reimers & Gurevych,2019), was configured with the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 vari­ ant. This model is optimized for semantic similarity tasks and transforms text into dense vector embeddings, enabling rapid cosine similarity calculations. It is ideal for large-scale assessments requiring efficient and accurate sentence-level comparisons. Universal Sentence Encoder (USE), was accessed via TensorFlow Hub and used in its v5 variant. It generates high-dimensional sentence embeddings designed for general-purpose semantic similarity tasks (Cer et al., 2018). This lightweight model excels in providing a robust baseline for simpler responses while main­ taining computational efficiency. BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers), was accessed via Hugging Face’s API using the bert-base-uncased model. Known for its robust bidirectional contextual understanding, it serves as a foundational model for evaluating student responses against reference answers (Devlin et al., 2019). RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach) was implemented through Hugging Face’s API using the roberta-large configuration. This model’s enhanced pretraining methodology provides strong performance for Natural Language Under­ standing tasks, particularly in semantic similarity evaluations (Liu et",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,11,"et al., 2019). RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach) was implemented through Hugging Face’s API using the roberta-large configuration. This model’s enhanced pretraining methodology provides strong performance for Natural Language Under­ standing tasks, particularly in semantic similarity evaluations (Liu et al., 2019). 1 3 3823",45
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,12,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 T5 (Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer) was accessed via Hugging Face’s API using the t5-base variant. This model reframes all NLP tasks as text-to-text problems, making it a versatile option for evaluating both simple and complex student responses (Raffel et al., 2020). GPTo1 was accessed via OpenAI’s API endpoint o1-2024-12-17. It was employed for its advanced contextual understanding and nuanced evaluation capabilities (OpenAI et al. 2024); Wu et al., 2024). It processes responses using custom prompts designed to interpret context dynamically, rather than relying on pre­ defined reference answers. For Cohere, the embed-english-v3.0 endpoint was used via its API. This embedding model generates high-quality vector representations for text, allowing efficient semantic similarity calculations between student responses and reference answers (Cao, 2024). Its robust embedding capabilities complement other models in captur­ ing nuanced similarities, making it an essential part of the evaluation framework. Llama2 was accessed using the Hugging Face transformers library in its Llama-2-7b- hf configuration. This model’s lightweight architecture and optimized perfor­ mance allowed for efficient contextual evaluation and semantic similarity scoring",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,12,"making it an essential part of the evaluation framework. Llama2 was accessed using the Hugging Face transformers library in its Llama-2-7b- hf configuration. This model’s lightweight architecture and optimized perfor­ mance allowed for efficient contextual evaluation and semantic similarity scoring on systems with limited resources (Touvron et al., 2023). Its ability to handle both simple and complex questions made it a versatile addition to the framework. Designed for high-quality text generation and contextual understanding, Mistral- 7B-Instruct was implemented to complement other models by generating context- aware evaluations. Its efficient design allowed it to deliver strong performance in grading open-ended responses, leveraging its ability to balance nuance and accuracy in diverse textual inputs (A. Q. Jiang et al. 2023). Claude 3 Opus accessed via Anthropic’s API endpoint claude-3-opus-latest, this model was employed for its advanced contextual reasoning and ability to handle large-scale, open-ended assessments. Claude 3 Opus excels in processing long- form content and providing nuanced evaluations (Anthropic, 2024), making it particularly well-suited for assessing the depth, relevance, and completeness of student responses. Its robust contextual understanding allowed it to effectively",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,12,"large-scale, open-ended assessments. Claude 3 Opus excels in processing long- form content and providing nuanced evaluations (Anthropic, 2024), making it particularly well-suited for assessing the depth, relevance, and completeness of student responses. Its robust contextual understanding allowed it to effectively interpret complex responses and align them with the grading criteria, ensuring a precise and reliable evaluation. PaLM 2 accessed via Google’s API, this model excels at understanding and interpret­ ing complex, open-ended text inputs. Designed for tasks requiring contextual reasoning, text-bison-001 was employed to evaluate student responses by assess­ ing their depth, relevance, and alignment with the question’s intent. Its ability to dynamically interpret nuanced answers made it particularly effective in grading diverse student responses (Anil et al., 2023). Additionally, its contextual under­ standing capabilities ensured that creative or alternative phrasing was evaluated fairly, without undue reliance on predefined reference answers. Sentence Embedding Models Reference-Based Evaluation Sentence embedding models, including SBERT, USE, T5, BERT, and RoBERTa, are designed to transform textual inputs into dense vector embeddings, effectively captur­ ing semantic nuances and enabling precise comparisons in a multi-dimensional space. 1",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,12,"answers. Sentence Embedding Models Reference-Based Evaluation Sentence embedding models, including SBERT, USE, T5, BERT, and RoBERTa, are designed to transform textual inputs into dense vector embeddings, effectively captur­ ing semantic nuances and enabling precise comparisons in a multi-dimensional space. 1 3 3824",42
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,13,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 The evaluation process leveraged cosine similarity to measure the semantic align­ ment between a student’s response and the reference answer. Cosine similarity is a mathematical metric that calculates the cosine of the angle between two vectors, producing a value between 0 and 1: ● 1: Represents perfect semantic alignment, indicating that the two texts share iden­ tical meaning. ● 0: Denotes no semantic similarity, indicating that the texts are entirely unrelated. The reference-based evaluation has the potential to be particularly powerful in sce­ narios where the reference answers comprehensively outline critical ideas from the course materials. By converting textual responses into dense vector representations, embedding models can capture semantic relationships between student answers and references, enabling a nuanced assessment of alignment. This method offers a con­ sistent and efficient framework for evaluating open-ended responses by focusing on conceptual understanding rather than superficial word matching. The ability of embedding models to measure semantic similarity through cosine similarity provides a structured approach to assess how well a student’s answer aligns with the intended",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,13,"evaluating open-ended responses by focusing on conceptual understanding rather than superficial word matching. The ability of embedding models to measure semantic similarity through cosine similarity provides a structured approach to assess how well a student’s answer aligns with the intended meaning of the reference. This approach is especially promising in cases where ref­ erence answers serve as a clear benchmark for the depth and accuracy of responses. By leveraging these models, educators can explore an objective and scalable way to assess diverse expressions of the same core concepts, potentially improving the fair­ ness and reliability of automated grading systems. GenAI Contextual Understanding and Scoring Generative AI models, such as GPTo1, Claude3, PaLM 2, Llama2, Cohere, and Mis­ tral7B, inherently differ from embedding models. Rather than relying on reference answers, these models are designed to evaluate responses based on their understanding of the question’s context. To align their capabilities with the precision offered by embed­ ding models, a tailored approach to scoring was developed. The goal was to enable generative models to calculate similarity scores that closely resemble cosine similarity metrics,",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,13,"of the question’s context. To align their capabilities with the precision offered by embed­ ding models, a tailored approach to scoring was developed. The goal was to enable generative models to calculate similarity scores that closely resemble cosine similarity metrics, thereby ensuring fair comparability across all models used in the study. The first attempt to align GenAI models with the capabilities of sentence embedding models focused on using reference answers. These references served as a baseline for comparing student responses and calculating a similarity score. The similarity score was designed to replace traditional grades with a precise dec­ imal value between 0.000000 and 1.000000, offering a six-decimal representation to mimic the granularity of cosine similarity. To ensure the evaluation captured a well-rounded perspective of what constitutes a good response, the references included Reference derived from course materials, designed to reflect the key con­ cepts and expected level of understanding for the question. High-quality responses graded ‘A’ by human evaluators (Grade1 or Grade2) provided additional context and real-world examples of how students could effectively answer the question. The components of the",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,13,"the key con­ cepts and expected level of understanding for the question. High-quality responses graded ‘A’ by human evaluators (Grade1 or Grade2) provided additional context and real-world examples of how students could effectively answer the question. The components of the generative AI prompt were informed by prior research on automated grading and prompt engineering in educational contexts. Specifically, 1 3 3825",61
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,14,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 the approach of including both reference answers derived from instructional mate­ rials and exemplary student answers graded ‘A’ by human evaluators was inspired by the design principles discussed by Su and Yang (2023) in their work on using LLMs for automated short-answer grading (Ferreira Mello et al., 2025). This study emphasizes the importance of providing LLMs with high-quality examples of correct answers to improve grading accuracy and consistency. Additionally, the prompt structure was guided by recommendations from recent literature on prompting strategies for educational tasks, such as the research by Hutt et al. (2024), which highlights the value of clear instructions, explicit scoring rubrics, and concrete examples for aligning LLM outputs with human expectations in educa­ tional assessment. By combining reference answers with authentic Grade ‘A’ student responses, the prompt aimed to capture both correctness and real-world variation in high-quality answers, supporting the model’s ability to evaluate responses that may differ in word­ ing but remain semantically accurate. This design choice reflects findings across mul­ tiple studies on the benefits of",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,14,"to capture both correctness and real-world variation in high-quality answers, supporting the model’s ability to evaluate responses that may differ in word­ ing but remain semantically accurate. This design choice reflects findings across mul­ tiple studies on the benefits of few-shot or example-based prompting for improving large language models’ grading performance. The following Prompt 1 was crafted to guide the model in performing the comparison. Prompt 1 1 3 3826",70
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,15,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 Despite the thorough setup of the first prompt, the results did not show a meaning­ ful improvement over ensemble embedding models. These models are highly effec­ tive at capturing semantic relationships and aligning responses with references, but they faced significant challenges when evaluating student answers. Given the poten­ tial variability of open-ended responses, an alternative prompt strategy was explored to address potential limitations of the initial reference-based approach. One possible reason behind the not very satisfying outcomes of similarity scores that was con­ sidered was that student answers, although potentially accurate, may not have been perfectly aligned with the phrasing or structure of the reference answers provided in the course materials. This misalignment could have impacted the similarity scores generated by the models. It was hypothesized that students’ use of varied expressions and personalized language (even thou of academic-level writing) to explain concepts could result in responses that captured the correct ideas but did not perfectly match the phrasing or structure of the reference materials. This potential misalignment may have influ­",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,15,"expressions and personalized language (even thou of academic-level writing) to explain concepts could result in responses that captured the correct ideas but did not perfectly match the phrasing or structure of the reference materials. This potential misalignment may have influ­ enced the models’ similarity scores, as the reference-based approach required close semantic alignment. Although this reasoning was based on deduction rather than con­ firmed evidence, it motivated the exploration of a different strategy to address these challenges. To reduce the reliance on reference answers and better accommodate the diver­ sity in student responses, a contextual evaluation prompt was developed. This new approach focused on evaluating the response solely based on its relevance and com­ pleteness concerning the given question, without relying on external references. By emphasizing the context of the question, the model was guided to assess the depth and quality of the answer more holistically. Prompt 2 was crafted to guide the model in performing the comparison based on contextual evaluation. Prompt 2 1 3 3827",167
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,16,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 The introduction of the contextual evaluation prompt marked a significant improve­ ment in the model’s ability to accurately assess open-ended student responses. By eliminating the reliance on predefined reference answers and focusing exclusively on the context of each question, this approach allowed the model to better capture the depth, relevance, and completeness of students’ answers. This shift proved par­ ticularly effective in addressing the diverse ways students articulated their under­ standing, as the evaluation was no longer constrained by the phrasing or structure of reference materials. Given the natural variability in how students articulated their responses, it became evident that the likelihood of two students independently submitting identical answers was extremely low. This deduction was grounded in the inher­ ent creativity and interpretation involved in answering open-ended questions. The strict reliance on reference-based scoring in earlier prompts did not adequately account for this variability, further emphasizing the need for a more flexible, context-sensitive approach. To enhance the evaluation process further and maintain academic integrity, a mechanism to detect and handle duplicate",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,16,"on reference-based scoring in earlier prompts did not adequately account for this variability, further emphasizing the need for a more flexible, context-sensitive approach. To enhance the evaluation process further and maintain academic integrity, a mechanism to detect and handle duplicate answers was incorporated into the final refinement of the prompt. In addition to addressing potential plagiarism, this mechanism also served as a practical way to verify the models’ ability to produce consistent and fair grading. By including duplicate detection, the evaluation tested whether models assigned the same grade to identical or nearly identical responses, ensuring that automated grading systems handled equivalent answers predictably and objectively. This refinement ensured that any response matching a previously submitted answer would automatically be assigned a failing grade (‘Fx’), as such duplication could indicate potential academic misconduct. This approach strength­ ened the reliability of the assessment by confirming that the models were stable and did not give inconsistent grades when faced with identical or very similar answers. While duplicate detection was applied to most questions, there were two specific cases where identical answers were reasonable",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,16,"assessment by confirming that the models were stable and did not give inconsistent grades when faced with identical or very similar answers. While duplicate detection was applied to most questions, there were two specific cases where identical answers were reasonable and expected due to the nature of the questions: ● Question 3: “What are the main categories of software requirements?” ● Question 10: “What are the two main categories of UML diagrams?” For these questions, duplicates were permitted since they required straightforward factual responses with limited variation. As a result, questions with IDs 3 and 10 were explicitly excluded from the duplicate detection process to avoid false accusa­ tions of misconduct. The final version of the contextual evaluation prompt (Prompt 3) included a mechanism to detect duplicates, ensuring fairness and consistency while maintaining accuracy. 1 3 3828",137
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,17,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 Prompt 3. The grading scale used in the course provided a clear and structured framework for mapping the similarity scores generated by the models to standard grades. The mapping was as follows: ● ‘A’ (1.0): Scores between 1.00 and 0.95. ● ‘B’ (1.5): Scores between 0.949 and 0.90. ● ‘C’ (2.0): Scores between 0.899 and 0.85. ● ‘D’ (2.5): Scores between 0.849 and 0.80. ● ‘E’ (3.0): Scores between 0.799 and 0.70. ● ‘Fx’ (4.0): Scores below 0.699 and 0.00. By aligning the models’ outputs with this grading scale, the results were not only interpretable but also directly applicable within the educational context. The granular scoring system allowed for a seamless transition from the model-generated values to traditional academic grades, ensuring consistency with the evaluation standards of the course. This compatibility reinforced the validity of the grading approach and facilitated a fair comparison with other methods, such as sentence embedding models. Evaluation The evaluation phase aimed to analyze the performance of the chosen GenAI mod­ els and sentence embedding models in",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,17,"reinforced the validity of the grading approach and facilitated a fair comparison with other methods, such as sentence embedding models. Evaluation The evaluation phase aimed to analyze the performance of the chosen GenAI mod­ els and sentence embedding models in the automated assessment of open-ended questions. This included examining agreement with human graders, identifying dis­ crepancies in grades, and exploring the limitations of reference-based evaluation approaches. 1 3 3829",69
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,18,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 The examined model’s (GenAIs and sentence embeddings) scores and grades deviate significantly from normality. Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Lilliefors test identified statisti­ cally significant differences of the models at significance level of 0.01 and confirmed the non-normality of similarity scores and grades. Shapiro-Wilk test identified statistically significant differences at the significance level of 0.001. The results of the Kolmogorov- Smirnov, Lilliefors, and Shapiro-Wilk tests confirm significant deviations from normal­ ity, based on that a non-parametric method was used for further comparison. The Friedman test showed statistically significant differences (p < 0.01) in the case of obtained scores of assessments of student answers (Friedman ANOVA Chi Sqr. (N = 1885, df = 10) = 2035.0140; p = 0.0000; Coefficient of Concordance = 0.1080; Average rank r = 0.1075). The small Coefficient of Concordance (0.1080) can indi­ cate a weak overall effect and because of that the following Wilcoxon signed-rank test was used with Bonferroni correction. Rank-Biserial Correlation (RBC) that ranges between − 1 and 1, shows which of the models tends to have higher",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,18,"can indi­ cate a weak overall effect and because of that the following Wilcoxon signed-rank test was used with Bonferroni correction. Rank-Biserial Correlation (RBC) that ranges between − 1 and 1, shows which of the models tends to have higher scores. Common Language Effect Size (CLES) iden­ tifies the probability that a randomly chosen answer from the first model has a higher similarity score to the student/reference answer than a randomly chosen answer from the second model. The comparison of the models’ grades showed that there were some differences between the GenAI models’ assessment (Table 2a). A significant difference was identified between GPTo1 and Cohere, where a negative RBC (RBC = −0.2791) suggests that GPTo1 grades with better marks than Cohere, where a CLES score 0.4813 shows moderate probability that Cohere used worse grades for assess­ ment. Similar results were obtained also in comparison to Llama2 and Mistral7B. On the other hand, GPTo1 performs comparably to PaLM2 and Claude3 (p > 0.05). PaLM2 and Claude3 show significant difference with Llama2, Cohere and Mistral7B at significance level of 0.001, where in",180
