DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,19,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 grades in comparison to Llama2, Cohere and Mistral7B. Comparison among Llama2, Mistral7B and Cohere showed no significant differences (p > 0.05) with near-zero RBC values and CLES values around 0.50. This shows practical equivalence between these models on the grade-level. Sentence embedding models’ assessment showed significant differences between SBERT and BERT and RoBERTa at significance level of 0.001 (Table 2b). A CLES of 0.45, which suggests a 45% likelihood that SBERT assigns better grades com­ pared to the latter models. On the other hand, USE and T5 generate similar grades as SBERT (p > 0.05). Statistically significant differences were identified between BERT, USE and T5 and between RoBERTa, USE and T5. In both cases, BERT and RoBERTa assessed the answers with worse grades than USE and T5. Similar outputs were generated by models BERT and RoBERTa, and USE and T5, where no signifi­ cant differences were identified (p > 0.05). The estimated grades were compared to human graders that assessed the student answers on the same scale. Based on the almost",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,19,"by models BERT and RoBERTa, and USE and T5, where no signifi­ cant differences were identified (p > 0.05). The estimated grades were compared to human graders that assessed the student answers on the same scale. Based on the almost perfect agreement between human annotators (QWK = 0.95) it was possible to compare the output of GenAI and BERT models and obtain a relevant picture of the quality of the automatic assessment of open-ended questions. Fleiss’ Kappa and Krippendorff’s Alpha were used to measure inter-rater agree­ ment between the examined models and human grading. Both measures are suitable for ordinal data, Fleiss’ Kappa does not consider the level of disagreement, Krippendorff’s Alpha considers the rank order and magnitude of differences. Fleiss’ Kappa was used to examine the model’s performance on a stricter level. Based on the results (Fig. 2) almost Fig. 2 Fleiss’ Kappa Agreement Scores Between the Examined Models and Human Graders 1 3 3831",156
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,20,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 perfect agreement was identified only in the case of GPTo1 (Fleiss’ Kappa = 0.82), most of the models obtained a substantial agreement (0.61–0.80) and BERT, RoBERTa, T5 scored only a moderate agreement (0.41–0.60) with the human graders. Overall, the GenAI models outperformed the sentence embedding models apart from Llama2 and SBERT that achieved a similar substantial agreement. These results show that in the case of a strict alignment of grades with the teachers’ assessment of student answers, only GPTo1 produces comparable assessments. PaLM2 and Claude3 provide substan­ tial agreement with teachers grades and are close to almost perfect agreement. Most of the sentence embedding models’ agreement is at moderate level and would be not appropriate to use these models for open-ended questions assessment without further human supervision. This could be because these models are focused on the reference answer comparison and open-ended questions offer the student to write the answer using more logical approach and different wording. That is why GenAI models could better perform if they focused only on the",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,20,"these models are focused on the reference answer comparison and open-ended questions offer the student to write the answer using more logical approach and different wording. That is why GenAI models could better perform if they focused only on the context of the answer. Krippendorff’s Alpha offers a less strict approach to the comparison of grad­ ing. Most of the models obtained almost perfect agreement (Alpha > 0.80) except BERT, RoBERTa and T5 that obtained a substantial agreement with the teachers (Fig. 3). The GenAI models’ agreement with human graders was almost similar to the teacher agreement with each other (Alpha = 0.94). GPTo1 and Claude3 had the highest agreement, where PaLM2, Mistral7B, Cohere and Llama2 followed closely. Sentence embedding models obtained higher agreement compared to Fleiss’ Kappa. However, only USE and SBERT achieved an almost perfect agreement and still are significantly lower than any other GenAI model. These results suggest that GPTo1 Fig. 3 Krippendorff’s Alpha Agreement Scores Between the Examined Models and Human Graders 1 3 3832",169
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,21,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 is the most suitable model to use for grading open-ended questions with comparable assessment as human evaluators. Based on the Wilcoxon sign-test no significant dif­ ferences were identified (p > 0.05) between GPTo1 and one of the human graders. Fleiss’ Kappa (Fig. 2) highlights GPTo1 as the only model achieving almost perfect agreement with human graders (Fleiss’ Kappa = 0.82), while other GenAI models, such as PaLM2 and Claude3, achieve substantial agreement (0.75–0.80). Krippendorff’s Alpha (Fig. 3), which accounts for rank order, reveals higher overall agreement, with most GenAI models achieving almost perfect agreement (Fleiss’ Kappa > 0.80). These findings highlight the potential of GPTo1 for automated assess­ ment of open-ended questions, with comparable reliability to human evaluators. The results of the agreement score offer another insight into the usage of GenAI models in assessment of open-ended questions. Evaluation of open-ended questions is time consuming for the teacher, however this paper identified possible GenAI mod­ els that could help reduce the workload and produce relevant assessment. Another approach would be to",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,21,"GenAI models in assessment of open-ended questions. Evaluation of open-ended questions is time consuming for the teacher, however this paper identified possible GenAI mod­ els that could help reduce the workload and produce relevant assessment. Another approach would be to use a combination of two models (similar to two teachers’ grading) and evaluate the agreement. Because only GPTo1 achieved almost perfect agreement using Fleiss’ Kappa with human graders, Quadratic weighted Cohen’s Kappa (QWK) was chosen to identify the agreement between selected models as a less strict metric (BERT, RoBERTa, and T5 were omitted as their agreement with Fig. 4 Heatmap of QWK Agreement Scores Between Models 1 3 3833",109
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,22,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 teachers was not sufficient). Figure 4 describes the agreement between the models, where all GenAI models achieved almost perfect agreement with each other. How­ ever, the highest agreement score was obtained for GPTo1, PaLM2 and Claude3, where between GPTo1 and Claude3 the identified score was 0.91. These three models achieved the highest score between the examined models. This shows that the combi­ nation of two of these models offers a very high agreement of the answers assessment and the teacher can only serve as a control element. Combination of grades using two models offers a more objective approach as assessment using only one model. To further understand the models’ evaluation behavior, an analysis of student answers using Natural Language Inference (NLI) was conducted. In the context of evaluating student responses, NLI was used to compare each student’s answer with a predefined reference answer. The model categorizes the relationship between the two texts into one of three categories (Bowman et al., 2015; Condoravdi et al., 2003): ● Entailment: The student’s answer fully",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,22,"was used to compare each student’s answer with a predefined reference answer. The model categorizes the relationship between the two texts into one of three categories (Bowman et al., 2015; Condoravdi et al., 2003): ● Entailment: The student’s answer fully supports or aligns with the reference an­ swer, indicating semantic agreement. ● Neutral: The student’s answer neither contradicts nor aligns strongly with the reference, suggesting partial relevance or incomplete understanding. ● Contradiction: The student’s answer conflicts with the reference, indicating se­ mantic misalignment or a misunderstanding. The probabilities for the three NLI categories—Contradiction, Neutral, and Entail­ ment—are summarized in Fig. 5. NLI provides a nuanced approach to analyzing stu­ dent responses beyond simple similarity scoring. By categorizing responses into 3 categories, it allows educators to assess not only how similar a student’s answer is to the reference but also the nature of the differences. This makes it particularly valuable in the context of open-ended questions, where students are encouraged to use their own words to explain concepts, often resulting in diverse expressions of the same idea. Fig. 5 Boxplot of",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,22,"nature of the differences. This makes it particularly valuable in the context of open-ended questions, where students are encouraged to use their own words to explain concepts, often resulting in diverse expressions of the same idea. Fig. 5 Boxplot of Probabilities for NLI Categories 1 3 3834",47
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,23,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 The boxplot (Fig. 5) illustrates the probability distributions of NLI categories for student responses relative to the reference answers. The findings reveal that a significant portion of student responses falls into the contradiction category, with lower probabilities for entailment. This suggests that many student answers, although potentially valid, are not semantically aligned with the reference answers. This result highlights a critical limitation of strict reference-based evaluation methods. When students write in their own words, their answers may deviate in phrasing, structure, or approach from the reference answers, even if the underlying meaning is accurate. The low probability distributions of the entailment and neutral categories show a struggle of the NLI to classify these categories. This was probably caused by the short nature of the students’ answers to compare with the reference answer. This finding aligns with the challenges observed in reference-based evaluation approaches, where strict alignment with reference answers may penalize valid but differently phrased responses. The high prevalence of contradictions reinforces the importance of context-focused evaluation strategies, as implemented in",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,23,"answer. This finding aligns with the challenges observed in reference-based evaluation approaches, where strict alignment with reference answers may penalize valid but differently phrased responses. The high prevalence of contradictions reinforces the importance of context-focused evaluation strategies, as implemented in GenAI models. Deployment The deployment phase of the CRISP-DM methodology focuses on evaluating the readiness of the examined models for real-world application in grading open-ended student responses. Instead of directly implementing these models, this phase aims to analyze their performance through key metrics such as precision, recall, F1-score, and error analysis, offering insights into their suitability for deployment in educational settings. The comparison was done using the mean grade of the two human graders. To assess the performance of the models, classification report heatmaps (Fig. 6) were created for all 11 models, showcasing their precision, recall, and F1-scores across all grade categories (‘A’, ‘B’, ‘C’, ‘D’, ‘E’, and ‘Fx’). These heatmaps provide a detailed visualization of how well each model performs in distinguishing between different grades. GenAI models like GPTo1 consistently demonstrated high perfor­ mance, with F1-scores above 0.85 for",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,23,"grade categories (‘A’, ‘B’, ‘C’, ‘D’, ‘E’, and ‘Fx’). These heatmaps provide a detailed visualization of how well each model performs in distinguishing between different grades. GenAI models like GPTo1 consistently demonstrated high perfor­ mance, with F1-scores above 0.85 for most grade categories, including the challeng­ ing ‘Fx’ (failing grade). This indicates that GPTo1 excels in capturing the semantic and contextual nuances required for evaluating diverse student responses. Similarly, PaLM2 and Claude3 exhibited strong performance, particularly in higher grades such as ‘A’, ‘B’, and also lower grade ‘E’, although they showed slightly lower recall for certain grades compared to GPTo1. In contrast, models like BERT, RoBERTa, and T5, which heavily rely on refer­ ence-based evaluation, struggled to maintain high F1-scores, particularly for the ‘Fx’ grade. These models often failed to capture the complexity and variability inherent in open-ended responses. Sentence embedding models, such as SBERT and USE, dis­ played moderate performance, but their inability to fully account for the context and diversity of student answers limited their effectiveness in lower-performing grades like ‘Fx’ and ‘D’. In the context of this",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,23,"embedding models, such as SBERT and USE, dis­ played moderate performance, but their inability to fully account for the context and diversity of student answers limited their effectiveness in lower-performing grades like ‘Fx’ and ‘D’. In the context of this study, which evaluated open-ended responses mapped to a grading scale with categories ‘A’–‘E’ (indicating passing answers) and ‘Fx’ (indicat­ ing failure), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN), visualized in Fig. 7, were 1 3 3835",76
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,24,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 defined not by minor grade differences, but by critical classification errors crossing the pass/fail threshold: ● False Positive (FP): Occurs when a student response that was labeled ‘Fx’ (fail) by human graders was assigned any passing grade (‘A’–‘E’) by the model, thus incorrectly recognizing a fundamentally incorrect or insufficient answer as ac­ ceptable. ● False Negative (FN): Occurs when a response that human graders evaluated as passing (grades ‘A’–‘E’) was rated as ‘Fx’ by the model, incorrectly rejecting an answer that demonstrated sufficient knowledge. This binary boundary reflects the practical educational implications more accurately than minor differences between adjacent passing grades (e.g., ‘A’ vs. ‘B’), which are Fig. 6 Classification Report Heatmaps for Evaluated Models Across All Grade Categories 1 3 3836",131
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,25,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 often subjective and can vary between human graders. This approach aligns with best practices in educational assessment research, where identifying critical misclassifica­ tions (e.g., pass predicted as fail or vice versa) is more meaningful than penalizing small, often subjective, grade differences (Hutt et al., 2024; Qian et al., 2022). By focusing on this threshold, the study emphasizes the real impact of grading errors on student outcomes, particularly in contexts where failing grades (‘Fx’) can affect course completion, motivation, and retention. Models like BERT, RoBERTa, and USE recorded the highest FP and FN rates, highlighting their susceptibility to misclassification, particularly in context-driven scenarios. Conversely, GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 maintained significantly lower FP and FN rates, demonstrating their reliability in accurately grading responses. The FP analysis (Fig. 7) revealed that BERT, RoBERTa, and USE often overesti­ mated the quality of suboptimal responses, leading to fairness concerns in grading. On the other hand, GPTo1 and Claude3 showed a strong ability to avoid such errors, making them more consistent and fairer. The FN analysis (Fig.",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,25,"USE often overesti­ mated the quality of suboptimal responses, leading to fairness concerns in grading. On the other hand, GPTo1 and Claude3 showed a strong ability to avoid such errors, making them more consistent and fairer. The FN analysis (Fig. 7) indicated that mod­ els like BERT and RoBERTa frequently penalized valid answers due to their rigid reliance on reference alignment. In contrast, GPTo1 and Claude3 effectively captured the meaning and intent of responses, ensuring inclusivity by minimizing penalties for alternative phrasing. The combined insights from classification reports and error analysis highlight the deployment readiness of the models. GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 emerged as the strongest candidates for deployment, balancing accuracy, fairness, and inclusivity in grading. Their ability to consistently produce high-quality results across diverse sce­ narios makes them suitable for use in automated grading systems. However, models like BERT, RoBERTa, and USE would require further refinement or hybrid deploy­ ment strategies, such as combining them with human oversight for complex cases. Figures 6 and 7 collectively provide a comprehensive evaluation of the models’ strengths and limitations. These results emphasize",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,25,"RoBERTa, and USE would require further refinement or hybrid deploy­ ment strategies, such as combining them with human oversight for complex cases. Figures 6 and 7 collectively provide a comprehensive evaluation of the models’ strengths and limitations. These results emphasize the potential of advanced GenAI models, particularly GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2, to transform the grading pro­ Fig. 7 Analysis of the Frequency of FP and FN Errors to Assess Model Robustness and Accuracy 1 3 3837",76
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,26,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 cess in educational contexts by offering fast, reliable, efficient, and fair assessment solutions. Discussion The discussion section synthesizes the findings of this study to address the posed research questions (RQs) and contextualize the performance of the evaluated GenAI models and sentence embedding models for grading open-ended questions. By exam­ ining classification metrics, agreement scores, false positives (FP), false negatives (FN), and Natural Language Inference (NLI) results, this discussion highlights both the strengths and limitations of the models. RQ1: Which generative AI model offers the most accurate assessment of open- ended questions compared to human evaluations? The evaluation results reveal that GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 consistently outperformed other models in terms of classification metrics, inter-rater agreement, and error rates. GPTo1 achieved the highest agreement with human graders, with Fleiss’ Kappa of 0.82, approaching the agreement observed between human evalu­ ators themselves. Similarly, Krippendorff’s Alpha for GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 exceeded 0.80, reflecting almost perfect agreement with human grades. In terms of classification metrics (Fig. 6), GPTo1 demonstrated the highest preci­ sion, recall,",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,26,"approaching the agreement observed between human evalu­ ators themselves. Similarly, Krippendorff’s Alpha for GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 exceeded 0.80, reflecting almost perfect agreement with human grades. In terms of classification metrics (Fig. 6), GPTo1 demonstrated the highest preci­ sion, recall, and F1-scores, maintaining values above 0.85 across most grade catego­ ries, including ‘Fx’ (failing grade). Claude3 and PaLM2 closely followed GPTo1 in performance, with strong results for higher-grade categories (e.g., ‘A’, ‘B’, ‘E’) but slightly reduced recall for ‘Fx’. In contrast, BERT, RoBERTa, and T5, which rely on reference-based evalua­ tion, exhibited moderate performance, particularly for grades ‘Fx’ and ‘D’, where open-ended responses often require contextual understanding. Sentence embedding models like SBERT and USE achieved reasonable results for straightforward grades (‘A’ and ‘B’) but struggled with nuanced or less-aligned answers, highlighting their inability to fully grasp the context of diverse responses. To better illustrate the difference between the models grading capability, sample answers to the third question “What are the main categories of software require­ ments?” are listed below. The reference answer used by sentence embedding models was following: “Functional",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,26,"responses. To better illustrate the difference between the models grading capability, sample answers to the third question “What are the main categories of software require­ ments?” are listed below. The reference answer used by sentence embedding models was following: “Functional requirements describe what the system will do and how it will function to meet user needs, giving a clear description of how the system should react to specific commands functions and user expectations. Nonfunctional requirements explain the constraints and limitations of the system being developed these requirements do not affect the functionality of the application.” Sample student answers to the third question are the following: ID 191: Requirements are divided into functional, what the system does and non­ functional, how it does it. User interface requirements, how it looks and works and regulatory, legal standards. ID 192: Functional requirements describe what the system will do and how it will function to meet user needs, giving a clear description of how the system 1 3 3838",165
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,27,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 should react to specific commands functions and user expectations. Nonfunc­ tional requirements explain the constraints and limitations of the system being developed. These requirements do not affect the functionality of the application. ID 198: There are two main types of requirements: functional and nonfunctional. Functional requirements define specific features and functions the system must provide, while nonfunctional requirements deal with qualitative aspects, such as performance security, user experience and availability, but not specific functions. ID 203: System properties are called functional, e.g. the possibility of comment­ ing and sharing nonfunctional are not related to functionality but are equally important, e.g. the possibility of supporting various operating systems. ID 204: There are two basic types of requirements. Functional requirements, these requirements describe what the system must do in terms of functionality, include information about how the system should respond to various inputs or situations. Examples of functional requirements from text are creating and edit­ ing text notes, password protecting sensitive notes and automatic recording of changes to the server. Nonfunctional requirements, these",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,27,"information about how the system should respond to various inputs or situations. Examples of functional requirements from text are creating and edit­ ing text notes, password protecting sensitive notes and automatic recording of changes to the server. Nonfunctional requirements, these requirements do not deal with the functionality of the system but various other aspects that are equally important, include, for example, performance requirements, security compatibil­ ity with other systems and other examples of nonfunctional requirements from the text are: the support of the iOS operating system and the provision of docu­ mentation and support. Table 3 supports the results by comparing model and human grades (Grade1 and Grade2) on the five student responses to the same question. Short answers (ID 203) containing limited relevant information received lower grades from both human graders and most models. On the other hand, student answer (ID 204), which contains detailed, well-structured content, was correctly graded as ‘A’ by both teachers and the GenAI models, but misclassified by T5, USE, and BERT. This misalignment shows the limitations of sentence embedding models, which tend to penalize",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,27,"answer (ID 204), which contains detailed, well-structured content, was correctly graded as ‘A’ by both teachers and the GenAI models, but misclassified by T5, USE, and BERT. This misalignment shows the limitations of sentence embedding models, which tend to penalize responses that deviate from reference, even when they are contextually accurate. Answer ID 191 192 198 203 204 Grade1 C B A Fx A Grade2 C A A Fx A GPTo1 C A A Fx A PaLM2 C A B Fx A Claude3 D A A Fx A Llama2 D D A Fx B Mistral7B D B A E A Cohere C B A Fx A SBERT C D A E A BERT D B E Fx D RoBERTa C B A Fx C USE C A A E Fx T5 C E A Fx Fx Table 3 Sample student answers to third question with GenAI and sentence embedding mod­ els’ grades 1 3 3839",155
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,28,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 Thus, GPTo1 emerges as the most accurate model for assessing open-ended ques­ tions on the computer science domain, followed by Claude3 and PaLM2. Their abil­ ity to consistently align with human evaluations underscores their suitability for real-world deployment. RQ2: To what extent can generative AI models reduce grading errors, specifically false positives (over-grading) and false negatives (under-grading), while maintain­ ing consistency with human evaluators? The analysis of FP and FN rates (Fig. 7) highlights significant differences in the reliability of the models. GPTo1, Claude3, and PaLM2 demonstrated the lowest FP and FN rates, reflecting their robustness in avoiding over-grading and under-grading errors. For instance, GPTo1 recorded only 11 false positives and 39 false negatives, significantly lower than models like BERT (345 FP, 126 FN) or RoBERTa, which showed high susceptibility to misclassification. The higher false positive rates observed in encoder-based models (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, USE) can be attributed to their reliance on surface-level semantic similar­ ity to reference answers. These models can misinterpret partial overlaps in terminol­ ogy or individual keywords",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,28,"misclassification. The higher false positive rates observed in encoder-based models (e.g., BERT, RoBERTa, USE) can be attributed to their reliance on surface-level semantic similar­ ity to reference answers. These models can misinterpret partial overlaps in terminol­ ogy or individual keywords as strong semantic agreement, even when the response lacks sufficient context or completeness. Consequently, suboptimal answers contain­ ing a few relevant terms are sometimes incorrectly scored as high-quality responses, resulting in overestimation. This limitation aligns with findings from prior research (Qian et al., 2022) indicating that sentence embedding models can inflate similarity scores when student responses share isolated phrases or technical terms with the ref­ erence text, despite failing to provide a coherent or complete explanation. GPTo1 and Claude3 excelled in minimizing both FP and FN rates by prioritiz­ ing contextual understanding over strict reference matching. This adaptability makes them more aligned with human evaluators, as their grading decisions account for semantic meaning and alternative phrasing, ensuring fairness and inclusivity in assessments. In summary, GPTo1 and Claude3 significantly reduce grading errors while main­ taining consistency with human graders, a critical",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,28,"more aligned with human evaluators, as their grading decisions account for semantic meaning and alternative phrasing, ensuring fairness and inclusivity in assessments. In summary, GPTo1 and Claude3 significantly reduce grading errors while main­ taining consistency with human graders, a critical factor for their deployment in edu­ cational settings. RQ3: How do specific characteristics of student responses (e.g., length, complex­ ity, or semantic coherence) impact the performance and reliability of generative AI models in automated grading? The NLI analysis (Fig. 5) provides valuable insights into how specific characteris­ tics of student responses affect model performance. Although some questions, like Q3 or Q10, might seem to require only factual recall, students were explicitly instructed to explain their answers in their own words, not just list terms. This requirement for explanation means students had to demonstrate understanding by elaborating on key concepts, making clarity, coherence, and vocabulary diversity essential for earning passing grades. The probabilities for entailment, neutral, and contradiction categories revealed that a significant proportion of student responses fell into the contradiction category relative to reference answers. This finding underscores the challenges",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,28,"clarity, coherence, and vocabulary diversity essential for earning passing grades. The probabilities for entailment, neutral, and contradiction categories revealed that a significant proportion of student responses fell into the contradiction category relative to reference answers. This finding underscores the challenges faced by reference-based evaluation methods, as responses written in students’ own words often deviate semantically from the reference despite being correct in meaning. 1 3 3840",66
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,29,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 Interestingly, the analysis also revealed that the average student response consisted of approximately 22 words, of which 10 were stop words, leaving an average of only 12 relevant words per response. This brevity and limited informational content in responses likely contributed to the low confidence observed in NLI models. The sparse semantic signals in such short responses make it particularly challenging for models like BERT and RoBERTa, which heavily rely on strict alignment with refer­ ence answers, to accurately assess the quality of the response. However, this does not mean a decline in the quality of the student responses. The complexity of the answers met the requirements of college-degree writing (Flesh Kincaid Grade = 14.79). The students used in their responses a wide variety of vocabulary with great diversity and sophistication. A higher portion of nouns (34%) and unique words (TTR = 0.876) shows that students focused on key definitions and used diverse word­ ings to produce the answer to open-ended questions. GenAI models, such as GPTo1 and Claude3, performed better",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,29,"A higher portion of nouns (34%) and unique words (TTR = 0.876) shows that students focused on key definitions and used diverse word­ ings to produce the answer to open-ended questions. GenAI models, such as GPTo1 and Claude3, performed better in these scenarios due to their ability to leverage con­ textual understanding and interpret even sparse and partially structured information. These models demonstrated higher recall and lower false negative rates, effectively evaluating responses that diverged linguistically or structurally from the reference but were semantically correct. For example, GPTo1 excelled at assessing answers where relevant content was embedded in otherwise unstructured phrasing, capturing the intended meaning despite the high ratio of stop words (more than 40% on average per answer). This flexibility is critical for evaluating open-ended questions, where students may phrase their responses creatively or use non-standard expressions, often leading to penaliza­ tion by rigid reference-based approaches. These findings highlight the importance of models that prioritize contextual understanding and semantic coherence over strict reference alignment, especially in scenarios with sparse or noisy data. GPTo1 and Claude3 emerged as the most",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,29,"to penaliza­ tion by rigid reference-based approaches. These findings highlight the importance of models that prioritize contextual understanding and semantic coherence over strict reference alignment, especially in scenarios with sparse or noisy data. GPTo1 and Claude3 emerged as the most reli­ able models for handling such responses, ensuring fairness and equitable assessment for all students. However, the prevalence of short, stop-word-heavy responses further emphasizes the need for more refined evaluation methods that can robustly handle low-content answers. Conclusions This study explored the applicability of Generative AI and sentence embedding mod­ els for the automated grading of open-ended student responses. By systematically analyzing the models’ performance using metrics such as precision, recall, F1-score, and error rates, as well as evaluating their alignment with human graders, key insights were identified into their potential and limitations. Among the models evaluated, GPTo1 consistently outperformed others in terms of both accuracy and agreement with human evaluators. The model achieved almost perfect agreement with human graders, as demonstrated by Fleiss’ Kappa and Krip­ pendorff’s Alpha scores, and maintained low false positive and false negative rates. Its",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,29,"others in terms of both accuracy and agreement with human evaluators. The model achieved almost perfect agreement with human graders, as demonstrated by Fleiss’ Kappa and Krip­ pendorff’s Alpha scores, and maintained low false positive and false negative rates. Its ability to evaluate responses flexibly, without over-reliance on rigid reference alignment, ensured high performance even for responses that deviated structurally 1 3 3841",63
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,30,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 or linguistically from predefined answers. This robustness is particularly critical for the assessment of open-ended questions, which inherently encourage diverse and cre­ ative expressions of understanding. In contrast, other GenAI models, such as Claude3 and PaLM2, showed substan­ tial agreement with human evaluators and performed well in higher grades like ‘A’, ‘B’, and lower grade ‘E’ but struggled with certain grade categories such as ‘Fx’. Sentence embedding models, including BERT, RoBERTa, and SBERT, demon­ strated moderate performance but exhibited significant limitations in handling the context and variability of student responses. These models often relied heavily on reference-based evaluation, leading to misclassifications, especially for nuanced or creatively expressed answers. The analysis also highlighted the importance of Natural Language Inference as a diagnostic tool. NLI revealed that a significant proportion of student responses fell into the contradiction category relative to reference answers. This finding exposed the limitations of strict reference-based evaluation, as correct responses phrased in diverse ways were often penalized. GenAI models like GPTo1 and Claude3 addressed this limitation by focusing on",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,30,"fell into the contradiction category relative to reference answers. This finding exposed the limitations of strict reference-based evaluation, as correct responses phrased in diverse ways were often penalized. GenAI models like GPTo1 and Claude3 addressed this limitation by focusing on semantic coherence and contextual under­ standing, resulting in higher recall and reduced false negatives and higher agreement with human graders. Despite its promising results, this study has several limitations that should be addressed in future research. A key limitation lies in the study’s focus on a single the­ matic domain—open-ended questions within a computer science domain. While this domain provided a consistent basis for evaluation, it does not capture the diversity of topics, question types, or instructional methods that exist across various educational settings. Expanding the scope to include other courses and subject areas would intro­ duce challenges related to mapping grading criteria across multiple instructors and their unique teaching styles. Additionally, such diversity might require more flexible or domain-specific training for the models, which could complicate the standardiza­ tion and scalability of automated grading solutions. Future research should focus",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,30,"mapping grading criteria across multiple instructors and their unique teaching styles. Additionally, such diversity might require more flexible or domain-specific training for the models, which could complicate the standardiza­ tion and scalability of automated grading solutions. Future research should focus on extending the applicability of GenAI models to a wider variety of question types beyond open-ended responses. This includes evaluat­ ing their performance on nested questions, where individual sub-questions contribute to an overall grade and carry varying levels of weight or importance. Such ques­ tions would test the models’ ability to understand hierarchical structures and assign nuanced evaluations. Another promising avenue for future research is focusing exclusively on the capa­ bilities of GenAI models. This includes their ability to go beyond assigning grades and provide constructive, text-based feedback to students. Generative AI models have the potential to deliver personalized insights that not only identify errors or areas for improvement but also suggest ways to enhance the quality of responses. This feature could be invaluable in promoting a deeper understanding of concepts and fostering student growth. Finally, improving the explainability of",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,30,"that not only identify errors or areas for improvement but also suggest ways to enhance the quality of responses. This feature could be invaluable in promoting a deeper understanding of concepts and fostering student growth. Finally, improving the explainability of model outputs should be a priority. Providing educators with clear rationales for assigned grades and generated feedback would increase trust in automated grading systems and encourage broader adoption. By focusing on these areas, future research can ensure that GenAI models 1 3 3842",83
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,31,"International Journal of Artificial Intelligence in Education (2025) 35:3813–3846 are not only more robust and versatile but also better aligned with the practical needs of educators and students alike. One of the most significant findings of this study is that GPTo1 is the only model that can be deployed independently without requiring oversight from human eval­ uators or additional models. However, it is important to acknowledge that GPTo1 comes with financial costs due to its use of proprietary APIs and high-performance infrastructure, which can limit its scalability in resource-constrained settings. This makes its scalability and adoption a challenge for institutions with limited resources. Beyond comparing models, this study contributes a replicable methodological frame­ work that integrates reference-based and context-sensitive prompt strategies, setting thresholds for grading errors, and checking consistency through duplicate detection. In conclusion, the findings highlight that the future work should focus on finding hybrid strategies that combine advanced GenAI models with affordable solutions or human oversight may offer a more practical path forward. These approaches would ensure that educational institutions can adopt automated grading systems without compromising on",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,31,"work should focus on finding hybrid strategies that combine advanced GenAI models with affordable solutions or human oversight may offer a more practical path forward. These approaches would ensure that educational institutions can adopt automated grading systems without compromising on accuracy, inclusivity, or financial feasibility. Acknowledgements This work was supported by EU NextGenerationEU through the Recovery and Resil­ ience Plan for Slovakia under the project no. 09I03-03-V04-000596. Author Contributions All authors contributed to the study conception and design. J. P. and M. D. per­ formed data collection and designed the methodology. Data analysis was performed by J. P. and L. B., M. D. supervised the experiment. The first draft of the manuscript was written by J. P. and L. B. and all authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding Open access funding provided by The Ministry of Education, Science, Research and Sport of the Slovak Republic in cooperation with Centre for Scientific and Technical Information of the Slovak Republic. Data Availability The data analyzed in this study is openly available",180
DOC_053,s40593-025-00517-2.pdf,10.1007/s40593-025-00517-2,31,"access funding provided by The Ministry of Education, Science, Research and Sport of the Slovak Republic in cooperation with Centre for Scientific and Technical Information of the Slovak Republic. Data Availability The data analyzed in this study is openly available in anonymized form at the author’s GitHub repository:​h​t​t​p​s​:​/​/​g​i​t​h​u​b​.​c​o​m​/​J​-​P​e​c​u​c​h​o​v​a​/​g​e​n​a​i​-​a​u​t​o​m​a​t​e​d​-​g​r​a​d​i​n​g Declarations Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a",180
