DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,1,"International Journal of STEM Education Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 https://doi.org/10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w RESEARCH Open Access Student GenAI use under changing institutional policies: a mixed-methods case study in undergraduate physics Zachery Quince1* and Emily Faulconer2* ​*​C​o​r​r​e​s​p​o​n​d​e​n​c​e​:​ Zachery Quince zach.quince@scu.edu.au Emily Faulconer emily.faulconer@monash.edu 1Centre for Learning and Teaching, Southern Cross University, Gold Coast, Australia 2School of Biological Sciences, Monash University, Melbourne, Australia © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,1,"this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​ v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n​s​e​s​/​b​y​-​n​c​-​n​d​/​4​.​0​/​.​ Abstract Background The rise of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in higher education and the resulting institutional responses have prompted research to support evidence-based policy decisions. As policies change to adjust to this disruptive technology, it demands agility from educators. The impacts of these policy changes on student behaviours and outcomes are currently poorly understood. This study examines how students used GenAI across three assessments in an undergraduate physics unit after assessment redesign forced by institutional policy that permitted purpose-specific and open use. Results Using a mixed-methods approach, student declarations of GenAI use were coded into four functional categories and",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,1,"used GenAI across three assessments in an undergraduate physics unit after assessment redesign forced by institutional policy that permitted purpose-specific and open use. Results Using a mixed-methods approach, student declarations of GenAI use were coded into four functional categories and mapped to the Substitution, Augmentation, Modification, and Redefinition (SAMR) framework. Learning and Formatting dominated early use and aligned with substitution and augmentation for a presentation assessment type. Later assessments showed a marked growth in Feedback uses, which spanned all SAMR levels and became the most common pattern. ‘Doing Task’ uses appeared in smaller numbers but illustrated modification and redefinition when GenAI generated step-by-step solutions for unfamiliar problems. Across all assessments, GenAI users achieved slightly higher scores, although differences were not statistically significant, so this trend should be interpreted as a tentative observation. Conclusions Findings suggest that assessment design, which rewards understanding and requires student judgement, aligns with the theoretical markers of evaluative judgement. While this study did not directly measure internal cognitive states, the observed change in functional behaviour, specifically the transition toward iterative feedback loops, suggests that GenAI could",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,1,"understanding and requires student judgement, aligns with the theoretical markers of evaluative judgement. While this study did not directly measure internal cognitive states, the observed change in functional behaviour, specifically the transition toward iterative feedback loops, suggests that GenAI could serve as a catalyst for learning. This study provides empirical evidence on how assessment redesign based on policy shifts influences GenAI use patterns and proposes design principles for authentic assessment in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) contexts. It highlights authentic assessment and constructive alignment as guiding principles for sustainable policy and pedagogy in the GenAI era, ensuring coherence among learning outcomes, activities, and assessment. Keywords Generative AI (GenAI), Institutional policy change, SAMR framework, Authentic assessment",116
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"Page 2 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 ​Introduction The rapid and widespread integration of generative arti­ ficial intelligence (GenAI) into society has presented a profound and pressing challenge to established assess­ ment practices in higher education (Weng et al., 2024). As institutions adapt to this disruptive technology, understanding how policy changes influence student engagement with GenAI is critical. Tools such as Chat­ GPT, Copilot, and Gemini can now produce sophisti­ cated written, visual, and conceptual outputs in seconds, fundamentally disrupting traditional assumptions about authorship, originality, and the valid demonstration of student learning (Gonsalves, 2025). In contrast, this tech­ nology presents potential new ways to learn and receive feedback (Huesca et al., 2025). Whilst the technology can be a powerful tool, there is a concern that stands as a cen­ tral issue for academic integrity, forcing institutions to grapple with a critical question: how can we design and evaluate assessments that are authentic, meaningful, and fair in an era where advanced GenAI is ubiquitous (Kofi­ nas et al., 2025). This study responds to that",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"integrity, forcing institutions to grapple with a critical question: how can we design and evaluate assessments that are authentic, meaningful, and fair in an era where advanced GenAI is ubiquitous (Kofi­ nas et al., 2025). This study responds to that question by examining policy-driven assessment redesign and its implications for authentic assessment principles such as constructive alignment and evaluative judgment. Initial reactive institutional responses primarily focused on detection and misconduct policies, which are unsustainable due to reliability issues and inherent biases. GenAI detection tools have demonstrated significant reliability issues, including high rates of false positives and inherent biases against non-native English speakers (Liang et al., 2023; Pratama, 2025). The GenAI technol­ ogy itself evolves faster than any detection mechanism can keep pace, creating a perpetually reactive cycle. A move away from prohibition is not just a policy shift but requires pedagogical evolution. A purely prohibitive or punitive stance fails to address the underlying pedagogical challenge and misses the opportunity to prepare students for a future in which GenAI will be an integral part of professional practice. The central problem moves",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"pedagogical evolution. A purely prohibitive or punitive stance fails to address the underlying pedagogical challenge and misses the opportunity to prepare students for a future in which GenAI will be an integral part of professional practice. The central problem moves beyond ensuring ethical use, but how to evolve assessment design so that it remains a valid measure of student learning and fosters the critical and adaptive skills required in the 21st century (Lodge et al., 2026). By reframing GenAI as an integral component of professional practice, we shift the focus from ‘detecting use’ to ‘evaluating the sophistication of use’ through the lens of authentic assessment and constructive alignment. This study begins this exploration through a case study in a science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM) course. It seeks to explore the central problem of the actual nature of student GenAI engagement in STEM education. Specifically, we examine the outcomes of a GenAI-enabled physics unit. This will examine two dif­ ferent affordances offered within the institutional policy, which is restricted (purpose-specific use with scaffolded and guided use of specific tools for",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"engagement in STEM education. Specifically, we examine the outcomes of a GenAI-enabled physics unit. This will examine two dif­ ferent affordances offered within the institutional policy, which is restricted (purpose-specific use with scaffolded and guided use of specific tools for distinct assessment sub-tasks), to open-use policies influence the student use, measured through the substitution, augmentation, modification, and redefinition (SAMR) framework, and whether these behaviours support or undermine the intended constructive alignment of the curriculum. These theoretical constructs form a unified conceptual framework for this study, in which constructive align­ ment provides the baseline for assessment validity, and authentic assessment serves as the design intervention to re-establish it when it is threatened by GenAI, foster­ ing evaluative judgment so that students critically engage with GenAI rather than offload their cognitive effort onto it. The SAMR framework serves as an analytical bridge to measure the use of GenAI, mapping student behav­ iours to SAMR levels to determine whether assessment changes influenced student engagement with GenAI tools toward the redefinition required for professional STEM practice. By analysing student GenAI use with two different",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"to measure the use of GenAI, mapping student behav­ iours to SAMR levels to determine whether assessment changes influenced student engagement with GenAI tools toward the redefinition required for professional STEM practice. By analysing student GenAI use with two different policy conditions, this study provides empirical evidence to inform assessment design in STEM education. Spe­ cifically, we use the SAMR framework to operationalise students’ use of GenAI, enabling us to evaluate whether constructive alignment is maintained or bypassed under different conditions for GenAI use policies. By examin­ ing the relationship between the GenAI use behaviours and academic achievement, we seek to understand how authentic assessment design supports the capability to judge the quality of students, and others’ work, defined as evaluative judgment. This study is guided by the follow­ ing research questions: 1. What are students using GenAI for when assessments are revised for policy change? 2. What levels of the SAMR framework best describe the students’ use of GenAI as a measure of functional behaviour change? 3. What is the relationship between students’ GenAI use and their academic performance",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,2,"assessments are revised for policy change? 2. What levels of the SAMR framework best describe the students’ use of GenAI as a measure of functional behaviour change? 3. What is the relationship between students’ GenAI use and their academic performance within an authentic assessment context? Literature review Institutional and policy responses to GenAI Early reactive approaches: prohibition and detection Early institutional responses to GenAI in higher educa­ tion were largely characterised by urgency and risk aver­ sion. In the initial months following the release of large language models (LLM) such as ChatGPT, universities commonly adopted prohibition and detection strate­ gies to safeguard assessment integrity (Curtis, 2025; Liu & Bridgeman, 2023). While these measures provided immediate reassurance, they were soon recognised as",121
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"Page 3 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 unsustainable and misaligned with the pedagogical reali­ ties of higher education. Detection tools, in particular, proved unreliable and inequitable, with research identify­ ing false positives and linguistic bias against non-native English speakers (Liang et al., 2023; Pratama, 2025). Such approaches, while reactive, often reinforced a compliance culture that overlooked the transformative potential of GenAI for learning, feedback, and creative assessment design (Lee & Moore, 2024). Shift toward purposeful integration The sector-wide shift from prohibition toward purpose­ ful integration necessitates a tighter coupling of policy with the principles of constructive alignment. National agencies such as the Australasian Academic Integrity Network (AAIN, 2023) and the Tertiary Education Qual­ ity and Standards Agency (TEQSA, 2025) have called for policy frameworks that emphasise capability build­ ing, transparency, and ethical literacy rather than puni­ tive enforcement. Recent evidence from science and engineering graduate students supports this educative response, with most participants identifying universi­ ties as having an essential role in developing ethical AI awareness and in connecting ethics education with tech­",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"rather than puni­ tive enforcement. Recent evidence from science and engineering graduate students supports this educative response, with most participants identifying universi­ ties as having an essential role in developing ethical AI awareness and in connecting ethics education with tech­ nical preparation and future professional practice (Usher et al., 2025). However, the practical implementation of these goals requires a shift in how we define authentic evidence of learning, considering this disruptive technol­ ogy. GenAI forces a transition from attempts to contain its use in assessments it can mimic to assessment designs that require and develop evaluative judgement. Evalua­ tive judgement is “the capability to make decisions about the quality of work of self and others” (Tai et al., 2018, p. 472). It involves understanding what constitutes qual­ ity and applying that understanding when appraising work against relevant disciplinary or professional stan­ dards (Tai et al., 2018). In the context of GenAI, the con­ struct extends to judging the quality and appropriateness of GenAI-generated outputs, the ethicality of GenAI- enabled processes, and critically assessing AI-generated evaluations of human work (Bearman et",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"stan­ dards (Tai et al., 2018). In the context of GenAI, the con­ struct extends to judging the quality and appropriateness of GenAI-generated outputs, the ethicality of GenAI- enabled processes, and critically assessing AI-generated evaluations of human work (Bearman et al., 2024). This educative approach reframes GenAI as a catalyst for rethinking what constitutes valid and meaningful evi­ dence of learning in the digital era. Drawing on Rittel and Webber’s (1973) concept of wicked problems, Corbin et al. (2026) characterise GenAI and assessment as a com­ plex and evolving challenge that cannot be definitively formulated or resolved through a single correct solution. Instead, potential responses involve context-dependent trade-offs, and attempts to address one concern may cre­ ate new tensions or unintended consequences. This fram­ ing means that assessment policies and practices must be continually reviewed, adapted, and refined as GenAI technologies and patterns of use evolve. Emerging frameworks for authentic assessment with GenAI In line with this international movement, many univer­ sities including Southern Cross University have begun evolving their policies from binary restrictions toward more nuanced, educative frameworks. Recent",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"technologies and patterns of use evolve. Emerging frameworks for authentic assessment with GenAI In line with this international movement, many univer­ sities including Southern Cross University have begun evolving their policies from binary restrictions toward more nuanced, educative frameworks. Recent work by Nikolic et al. (2025) introduced the Project-work Artifi­ cial Intelligence Integration Framework (PAIIF), a Con­ ceive, Design, Implement, and Operate (CDIO)-based model designed to guide the authentic integration of AI in STEM education. This framework demonstrates how systematic approaches can support educators in aligning institutional policy with practical implementation. Beyond the Australian context, Chen et al. (2026) pro­ posed a GenAI Competence Framework for Engineer­ ing Curriculum Enhancement that combines progressive competence development with curriculum integration, implementation strategies, and impact evaluation. The framework supports students’ progression from founda­ tional GenAI literacy to specialised technical proficiency and domain-specific innovation, while embedding ethi­ cal, legal, and regulatory considerations throughout the engineering curriculum. Similarly, Nathaniel et al. (2025) developed the GenAI-Ped framework for programming education, integrating learner-needs analysis, self-regu­ lated learning, Bloom’s taxonomy, universal design prin­ ciples, iterative feedback, and gradually",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"while embedding ethi­ cal, legal, and regulatory considerations throughout the engineering curriculum. Similarly, Nathaniel et al. (2025) developed the GenAI-Ped framework for programming education, integrating learner-needs analysis, self-regu­ lated learning, Bloom’s taxonomy, universal design prin­ ciples, iterative feedback, and gradually reduced GenAI support. Complementing these pedagogical models, empiri­ cal evaluations of student performance in AI-supported STEM environments highlight a parallel need to assess evaluative judgment rather than output alone. For exam­ ple, Zhang and Zhang (2026) observed that students readily leverage GenAI to navigate procedural tasks, but AI-assistance does not inherently yield deeper concep­ tual mastery. These emerging models often distinguish between guided and open uses of GenAI within assessment, inte­ grating explicit student declarations, scaffolded learning opportunities, and staff capability development (Green­ away & Quince, 2025). The evolution of these approaches from reactive control to proactive, pedagogically- informed adaptation reflects a growing recognition that sustainable policy must balance academic integrity with innovation and inclusivity. Collectively, the literature positions GenAI integration as both a compliance issue and a driver of authentic assessment design, construc­ tive alignment, and capacity building –",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,3,"reflects a growing recognition that sustainable policy must balance academic integrity with innovation and inclusivity. Collectively, the literature positions GenAI integration as both a compliance issue and a driver of authentic assessment design, construc­ tive alignment, and capacity building – principles that underpin this study’s analysis. GenAI integration can be a cornerstone of authentic and future-ready assessment practice in higher education.t GenAI use and student performance Research on the impact of GenAI use on student per­ formance in higher education is of acute interest to",85
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"Page 4 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 debate regarding whether assessment tasks designed to evaluate the synthesis of complex information from mul­ tiple sources can maintain validity when GenAI tools can produce similar outputs with minimal student cog­ nitive effort or engagement. This misalignment has criti­ cal implications for STEM education, where the goal is not just the recall of facts but the development of deep conceptual understanding and practical problem-solving skills. The risk is that students may use GenAI to produce superficially correct answers to physics problems or lab reports without engaging in the foundational reason­ ing, calculation, or analytical processes that are the true learning objectives. Recognising this threat, higher education institutions have developed resources to modify assessment. As an example, the Assessment Adaptation Model – GenAI (AAM-GenAI) framework implemented at Southern Cross University (Quince et al., 2025a) is fundamentally an exercise in re-establishing constructive alignment. By compelling educators to systematically analyse the vul­ nerability of existing assessments, the framework forces a crucial question: in the presence of GenAI, does this",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"Southern Cross University (Quince et al., 2025a) is fundamentally an exercise in re-establishing constructive alignment. By compelling educators to systematically analyse the vul­ nerability of existing assessments, the framework forces a crucial question: in the presence of GenAI, does this task still require students to think and act like a physicist or an engineer? If not, the assessment must be redesigned not just to ‘beat the GenAI,’ but to realign the task with the authentic cognitive work of the discipline. This alignment to the discipline is a critical pathway forward for authen­ tic assessment. Authentic assessment Authentic assessment is regarded as an effective and ped­ agogically sound method for re-establishing constructive alignment in the GenAI era (Powell & Forsyth, 2024). As Wiggins (1998) argued long before the advent of GenAI, authentic tasks require judgement, creativity, and the application of skills in context. Its value is particularly pronounced in STEM disciplines. An integrative review by Tan and Kidman (2021) defines authentic assessment as activities that engage students in inquiry processes involving higher-order thinking and real-world problem- solving, countering the traditional “siloed”",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"in context. Its value is particularly pronounced in STEM disciplines. An integrative review by Tan and Kidman (2021) defines authentic assessment as activities that engage students in inquiry processes involving higher-order thinking and real-world problem- solving, countering the traditional “siloed” view of STEM by emphasising transdisciplinary problem-solving. For instance, an authentic assessment in this study’s phys­ ics context might not just ask for the solution to a circuit problem but require students to design a circuit for a spe­ cific real-world application, justify their design choices, and communicate their findings to a non-expert audi­ ence. Such a task is argued to be more resistant to GenAI because it requires a synthesis of technical knowledge, contextual understanding, and communication skills. While authentic assessment tasks aim to require evalu­ ative judgment, the rapid evolution of GenAI in solving complex and context-specific problems suggests that authenticity may be a moving target. There are design educators. The recent emergence of this technology has revealed significant gaps in the data that warrant fur­ ther investigation. Existing studies rely on self-reported impacts to performance rather than",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"suggests that authenticity may be a moving target. There are design educators. The recent emergence of this technology has revealed significant gaps in the data that warrant fur­ ther investigation. Existing studies rely on self-reported impacts to performance rather than direct measures. Assessment type may affect perceived impacts on criti­ cal thinking and creativity when using GenAI (Kizilcec et al., 2024), potentially shaping performance outcomes. For problem-set-style assessments common in STEM disciplines, students report using GenAI to generate solutions, bypassing the problem-solving process (Wang et al., 2025). Findings to date are contradictory: some studies report improved performance with GenAI for feedback (Qadeer, 2025), while others report decreased performance on summative assessments (Wecks et al., 2025). These studies only explored limited types of assessment tasks, did not use direct measures of perfor­ mance, and were unclear on the policy for GenAI use in place for the assessment tasks. This discrepancy may stem from inconsistent boundary definitions of the level of integration, for example, by conflating substitution use (where GenAI use may bypass cognitive effort) with modification use (where GenAI use may",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"use in place for the assessment tasks. This discrepancy may stem from inconsistent boundary definitions of the level of integration, for example, by conflating substitution use (where GenAI use may bypass cognitive effort) with modification use (where GenAI use may support cogni­ tive effort). Furthermore, the lack of clarity regarding the institutional policy contexts in these studies stems from the technology or the degree of permission granted to students for its use. Foundational principles of assessment design Building on the literature that positions GenAI inte­ gration as central to authentic assessment, this section outlines foundational principles that guide assessment redesign in the GenAI era. The challenge posed by GenAI to assessment integrity is not only technical, but deeply pedagogical. It compels a return to the first principles of curriculum design and a critical examination of how stu­ dent learning is fostered and evidenced. Institutional pol­ icy interventions should proceed as a deliberate effort to maintain constructive alignment, catalyse a shift towards more authentic assessment practices, evaluate the sophis­ tication of technology integration, and ultimately foster the development of students’ evaluative",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,4,"fostered and evidenced. Institutional pol­ icy interventions should proceed as a deliberate effort to maintain constructive alignment, catalyse a shift towards more authentic assessment practices, evaluate the sophis­ tication of technology integration, and ultimately foster the development of students’ evaluative judgement. Constructive alignment The principle of constructive alignment, as proposed by Biggs (1996), forms the foundation of modern curricu­ lum design. It posits that effective teaching and learning environments are created only when learning outcomes, teaching activities, and assessment tasks are explicitly and coherently aligned. Within this framework, assess­ ment is the central driver motivating students to engage in specific higher-order cognitive processes. The advent of GenAI has been characterised as a direct and significant threat to this pedagogical coher­ ence (Corbin et al., 2026). There is an ongoing scholarly",130
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"Page 5 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 cases in STEM that argue for GenAI supported authen­ tic assessment (Chen et al., 2025), though there is cur­ rently limited empirical evidence demonstrating whether assessments designed for authenticity actually change how students use GenAI within varying policy condi­ tions. Recent engineering education research also shows the potential of GenAI to support more contextualised problem design. Das et al. (2026) found that students performed better on GenAI-generated mechanical engi­ neering problems than on traditional textbook problems and proposed using GenAI-generated problems to sup­ port scaffolded authenticity rather than replacing con­ ventional problems. Authentic assessment must also be aware of the ethical considerations. Quince and Nikolic (2026) developed a 32-item taxonomy for the social, eco­ nomic, and environmental implications of GenAI inte­ gration in higher education. It was further extended to the ethical use in higher education (Quince et al., 2025b). Both studies showed that ethical understanding was gov­ erned by task scope and scaffolded learning. The advent of GenAI does not invalidate authentic assessment; rather, it",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"was further extended to the ethical use in higher education (Quince et al., 2025b). Both studies showed that ethical understanding was gov­ erned by task scope and scaffolded learning. The advent of GenAI does not invalidate authentic assessment; rather, it acts as a powerful catalyst for its more urgent and thoughtful implementation. When faced with tools that can instantly generate generic essays or solve stan­ dard textbook problems, educators are forced to design assessments that demand deeper engagement. A system­ atic literature review by Vlachopoulos and Makri (2024) found that authentic assessment methods are critical for developing the 21st-century skills such as critical think­ ing, problem-solving, and collaboration. This does not alone protect against the impact of GenAI but should be a part of the pedagogical design in response (Kofinas et al., 2025). To measure the success of this constructive alignment and authentic assessment, we employ the SAMR frame­ work. Rather than viewing SAMR as a taxonomy of technology use, we position it as a metric for the depth of constructive alignment, assessing whether GenAI use remains at the substitution",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"alignment and authentic assessment, we employ the SAMR frame­ work. Rather than viewing SAMR as a taxonomy of technology use, we position it as a metric for the depth of constructive alignment, assessing whether GenAI use remains at the substitution level or reaches the levels of modification and redefinition. While other frameworks focus on the frequency or type of GenAI tool used, the SAMR framework is uniquely appropriate because it focuses on the functional behaviour change in the learn­ ing task, distinguishing between use as a passive cogni­ tive shortcut (substitution) or as a cognitive partner for deep inquiry (redefinition). SAMR framework The SAMR model provides a structured way to under­ stand how technology integrates into education through four progressive levels: Substitution, Augmentation, Modification, and Redefinition (Puentedura, 2006). Sub­ stitution occurs when technology replaces a traditional tool without changing the task. Augmentation introduces functional improvements. Modification allows for a significant redesign of the learning task. Redefinition enables new types of learning experiences that were previously not possible. Although widely used in higher education, researchers caution against viewing SAMR as a",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"task. Augmentation introduces functional improvements. Modification allows for a significant redesign of the learning task. Redefinition enables new types of learning experiences that were previously not possible. Although widely used in higher education, researchers caution against viewing SAMR as a simple hierarchy. Instead, it should be applied contex­ tually, recognising that each level can be pedagogically appropriate depending on the learning goals (Hamilton et al., 2016; Zamri & Mohamad, 2024). Current research shows that most student GenAI use in higher education aligns with the Substitution and Aug­ mentation levels. Haroud and Saqri (2025) found that stu­ dents primarily use GenAI tools for immediate answers, explanations, or feedback instead of engaging in deeper reasoning. In STEM disciplines, this pattern is common when students input physics or mathematics problems into GenAI tools to receive step-by-step solutions instead of working through the reasoning themselves. This aligns with empirical observations in STEM tasks, where stu­ dents frequently rely on GenAI to navigate procedural hurdles and step-by-step execution without necessar­ ily deepening their conceptual understanding (Zhang & Zhang, 2026). These uses are examples of substitution,",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"themselves. This aligns with empirical observations in STEM tasks, where stu­ dents frequently rely on GenAI to navigate procedural hurdles and step-by-step execution without necessar­ ily deepening their conceptual understanding (Zhang & Zhang, 2026). These uses are examples of substitution, where GenAI replaces manual effort, or augmentation, where it enhances clarity and speed. These uses increase efficiency but do not transform learning or foster higher- order thinking. Some uses of GenAI are emerging at the Modifica­ tion level, where the technology begins to reshape how students approach learning tasks. Belkina et al. (2025) found that GenAI can restructure learning by enabling real-time collaboration, generating prompts, and scaf­ folding inquiry-based activities. In STEM education, students have used GenAI as a collaborative tool during laboratory work. Kilde-Westberg et al. (2025) described a physics experiment on acoustic levitation where students used GenAI to ask procedural and theoretical questions in real time, which changed both how the experiment was conducted and how students reflected on it. In these cases, GenAI becomes part of the learning design rather than a support tool, allowing students to",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,5,"ask procedural and theoretical questions in real time, which changed both how the experiment was conducted and how students reflected on it. In these cases, GenAI becomes part of the learning design rather than a support tool, allowing students to engage in more complex, dynamic, and reflective learning experiences. At the redefinition level, Belkina et al. (2025) identified cases involving new forms of learning activity, includ­ ing virtual role-playing, interactive simulations, dynamic brainstorming, and assignments that would have been difficult to implement without GenAI. In a STEM con­ text, this may include students using GenAI to analyse raw data, generate hypotheses, and test potential models, which enables inquiry at a level of sophistication that tra­ ditional teaching cannot provide. Such applications rede­ fine the role of the student from a problem-solver to a collaborator within a GenAI-augmented learning system,",139
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"Page 6 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 expanding the boundaries of what can be learned and assessed in higher education. An empirical gap remains in understanding the rela­ tionship between GenAI use and directly measured aca­ demic performance, defined in this study as students’ assessment scores. Much of the existing evidence is qualitative or based on students’ and educators’ percep­ tions of GenAI’s effects rather than comparisons using observed assessment results (Kizilcec et al., 2024; Weng et al., 2024). Automated GenAI-detection tools are not a reliable alternative for identifying student use because they may produce false classifications and cannot indi­ cate the purpose for which GenAI was used (Liang et al., 2023; Pratama, 2025). Understanding purpose is important because GenAI used for feedback, conceptual clarification, or verification may relate to learning differ­ ently from GenAI used to generate answers. This study, therefore, addresses a disciplinary and contextual gap by examining reported GenAI-use behaviours alongside directly measured assessment performance in an under­ graduate physics setting. As the SAMR framework illustrates, the impact of GenAI",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"from GenAI used to generate answers. This study, therefore, addresses a disciplinary and contextual gap by examining reported GenAI-use behaviours alongside directly measured assessment performance in an under­ graduate physics setting. As the SAMR framework illustrates, the impact of GenAI on learning is largely determined by the depth of its integration into the task. While substitution and augmentation can provide efficiencies, they often fail to leverage the transformative potential of GenAI and can even undermine constructive alignment if the GenAI performs the task for the student, thereby short-circu­ iting cognitive processes. To move student engagement toward modification and redefinition levels, educators must move beyond just integrating GenAI and focus on the nature of the task itself. Methodology This study employs a single-case study methodology (Harland, 2014) to analyse students’ use of GenAI and their academic performance on assessments in response to a shift in GenAI policy in an undergraduate STEM class. This approach examines the response of insti­ tutional policy and student behaviour in a specific, real-world educational context. GenAI use cannot be separated from its policy and assessment context,",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"to a shift in GenAI policy in an undergraduate STEM class. This approach examines the response of insti­ tutional policy and student behaviour in a specific, real-world educational context. GenAI use cannot be separated from its policy and assessment context, mak­ ing a case study design appropriate for exploring these interconnected factors (Cobian et al., 2022). While case studies have limited generalisability, this approach offers explanatory power and rich, contextual data on the mechanisms by which a specific GenAI use policy influ­ ences student academic performance and technology use strategies, which might be missed in a purely experimen­ tal or correlational study. The authors of this work are both STEM educators at two different universities in Australia. Author 1 was the lead researcher and unit (course/subject) coordina­ tor (UA) for the case study presented. As this work was based on changes made in response to policy changes, obtaining ethics approval prior to implementation was not possible, and an exemption was approved after deliv­ ery by the Southern Cross University’s Ethics Chair. The research was conducted using data collected as part",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"changes made in response to policy changes, obtaining ethics approval prior to implementation was not possible, and an exemption was approved after deliv­ ery by the Southern Cross University’s Ethics Chair. The research was conducted using data collected as part of the unit’s regular teaching and learning activities. As such, all reported data and analysis were undertaken post-unit completion, which was not built into the rede­ sign. It presents a rich case study of real changes forced by policy. This reduced the power dynamics and bias pre­ sented, as this work was not undertaken during the unit’s teaching. To mitigate potential investigator bias, the sec­ ond author was brought in to lead the research design. They are an experienced STEM education researcher who was independent of the unit’s design, delivery, and the student cohort, bringing an external impartial perspec­ tive to allow objectivity in the analysis and evaluation. Research context and design The study was conducted between April and May 2025 within a second-year physics unit at Southern Cross Uni­ versity, a regional university in Australia. The unit serves a",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"to allow objectivity in the analysis and evaluation. Research context and design The study was conducted between April and May 2025 within a second-year physics unit at Southern Cross Uni­ versity, a regional university in Australia. The unit serves a diverse cohort of students from the Bachelor of Engi­ neering and Bachelor of Education programs. Before the term began, the unit was systematically reviewed and updated to align with the new policies and proce­ dures. In 2025, the policy shifted from a five-tier model of GenAI use in assessments to a three-tier model, defin­ ing it as ‘No Use’, ‘Purpose-Specific Use’, and ‘Open Use’. Further details of this shift are documented in (Green­ away & Quince, 2025). When reviewing the assessments, the incoming UA noted that none of the three assess­ ments allowed for any GenAI use. The use of ‘No Use’ is restricted to assessments for students in person or in some enabling pathways units. To align the assessments with policy, modifications were made to the assess­ ment structure. The largest shift was in assessment 3 (A3), which moved",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"‘No Use’ is restricted to assessments for students in person or in some enabling pathways units. To align the assessments with policy, modifications were made to the assess­ ment structure. The largest shift was in assessment 3 (A3), which moved from an online multiple-choice quiz (MCQ) to a take-home quiz. Although structural changes were minimal, task setting and framing were significantly revised to enhance authenticity by requiring the applica­ tion of concepts to real-world scenarios. For full details of the redesign process, see (Quince & Faulconer, 2025) and the assessment overview in Appendix A. The first assessment (A1) operated under a ‘Purpose- Specific Use’ policy, followed by an ‘Open Use’ policy for the subsequent two assessments of the term. Regardless of the policy approach, students were still required to submit work that was demonstrably their own, as com­ municated to them, and to exercise their own cognitive function and full evaluative judgment on any GenAI out­ puts. For the first assessment, an oral presentation (20% of the unit), a ‘Purpose-Specific Use’ policy was in place, permitting students to use GenAI",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,6,"them, and to exercise their own cognitive function and full evaluative judgment on any GenAI out­ puts. For the first assessment, an oral presentation (20% of the unit), a ‘Purpose-Specific Use’ policy was in place, permitting students to use GenAI for specific, defined",43
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"Page 7 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 purposes such as background research, idea generation, slide generation and script development, but prohibited its use for generating the final content of the presenta­ tion slides or narrative. In other words, students could use GenAI for all parts of the assessment if they reviewed and understood how GenAI is produced. A purpose- specific model was selected, as their presentation record­ ing must be of their authentic selves, rather than a digital twin or other AI-generated figure. This created a struc­ tured, scaffolded environment for GenAI engagement. For the second assessment (A2) (a laboratory workbook – worth 50%) and A3 (a final quiz – worth 30%), an ‘Open Use’ policy was in place, permitting unrestricted use of GenAI tools. This created all three assessments that had a more authentic, less constrained environment designed to mimic professional practice, where individuals have access to a full suite of digital tools. This design reflects real-world conditions, which intro­ duces potential confounding variables such as learn­ ing progression, increasing tool familiarity,",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"more authentic, less constrained environment designed to mimic professional practice, where individuals have access to a full suite of digital tools. This design reflects real-world conditions, which intro­ duces potential confounding variables such as learn­ ing progression, increasing tool familiarity, and task complexity. Therefore, this study does not aim to estab­ lish a causal link between policy and student outcomes. Instead, it presents a data-informed observational time- series analysis that provides a detailed account of how an institutional policy shift may have contributed to changes in student behaviour and performance. These factors are acknowledged in the interpretation of findings. Data collection and participants The study analysed data from the full student cohort for each assessment. There were 36 students enrolled in the unit, and several submitted incomplete work for A1 or A2, or omitted the required academic integrity declara­ tion, which is where their GenAI use statement is located. These students were removed from the analysis for A1 and A2, which presents a limitation of this study, as their performance or GenAI use could not be measured. All students submitted",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"which is where their GenAI use statement is located. These students were removed from the analysis for A1 and A2, which presents a limitation of this study, as their performance or GenAI use could not be measured. All students submitted complete work and declarations for A3. The final dataset included 31 submissions for A1, 31 submissions for A2, and 36 submissions for A3, all with complete work and integrity declarations. Due to ethical approval, it was not possible to track each student’s per­ formance between assessments, and therefore, it needs to be treated separately. As such, this presents a limita­ tion that future research can overcome through a more research-focused investigation with full ethics approval. For each of the three assessments, two primary data sources were collected from all participating students to measure the mediating and dependent variables: student GenAI use strategy and academic performance, respec­ tively. Academic performance data was collected from the learning management system (LMS) after marking by a single marker (the UA). To enable robust and valid comparison across the three assessments, which had different maximum",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"use strategy and academic performance, respec­ tively. Academic performance data was collected from the learning management system (LMS) after marking by a single marker (the UA). To enable robust and valid comparison across the three assessments, which had different maximum scores (/20, /50, and /30, respec­ tively), all marks were converted to percentages. GenAI usage data was collected as a mandatory component of each assessment submission in the LMS, where all stu­ dents were required to complete a formal declaration acknowledging their use or non-use of GenAI tools. The declaration stated: I acknowledge that I [did / did not] use Genera­ tive AI tools in the completion of this assignment. If used, the tool(s) I used were [X]. I used them for the purpose(s) of [X]. I confirm that any use of AI was ethical, and in support of my own learning, and that the final submission reflects my original understand­ ing, effort and is demonstrably my own work. The student-provided descriptions of the “purpose(s) of [X]” served as the raw qualitative data, or “use cases,” for analysing their application",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"own learning, and that the final submission reflects my original understand­ ing, effort and is demonstrably my own work. The student-provided descriptions of the “purpose(s) of [X]” served as the raw qualitative data, or “use cases,” for analysing their application strategies. These use cases were drawn from a list that guides the use of the ‘Purpose Specific Use’, even for A2 and A3 scaffolded at ‘Open Use’. These uses are pedagogically informed and developed from a CDIO-based AI use framework (Nikolic et al., 2025). Whilst this list describes uses of GenAI, students ultimately provided their own descriptions. This pres­ ents a further limitation of this study, namely the mea­ surement of both academic performance and GenAI use. GenAI use was based on students’ self-reported declara­ tions, which may be incomplete, open to interpretation, or strategically framed. These data should therefore be interpreted with caution. Although students’ declarations were generally reflected in the submitted work, this align­ ment was judged by the lead author (Z.Q), who was also the UA and marker. This provides contextual familiar­ ity with the cohort and",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,"be interpreted with caution. Although students’ declarations were generally reflected in the submitted work, this align­ ment was judged by the lead author (Z.Q), who was also the UA and marker. This provides contextual familiar­ ity with the cohort and assessment tasks, but it does not provide independent validation of GenAI use. Similarly, academic performance was assessed by a single marker, meaning that inter-rater reliability cannot be claimed. Marking was conducted using the approved assessment rubric and was subject to standard unit moderation pro­ cesses to support consistency with the assessment cri­ teria. However, the potential for marker bias remains a limitation, and the findings should be interpreted within the unit’s specific assessment and moderation context. Data analysis A mixed-methods approach was used to examine how students’ reported GenAI-use behaviours related to their learning and academic performance in undergraduate physics. The quantitative strand used assessment grades to compare the performance of students who reported using GenAI with those who did not. The qualita­ tive strand examined students’ declarations to identify what they reported using GenAI for and how these uses",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,7,The quantitative strand used assessment grades to compare the performance of students who reported using GenAI with those who did not. The qualita­ tive strand examined students’ declarations to identify what they reported using GenAI for and how these uses,40
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"Page 8 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 aligned with the SAMR framework. The two strands were analysed separately and then integrated at the assessment level to provide a more comprehensive interpretation of students’ GenAI use and performance. Performance The academic performance of two distinct groups (GenAI users vs. non-users) was compared for each of the three assessments. Mean percentage scores were calculated to measure the central tendency of each group, while standard deviations were calculated to measure the vari­ ability or dispersion of performance within each group. A t-test was used to determine whether the differences between the two groups were statistically significant, and Cohen’s d was used to assess the effect size given the sample size. Due to the scope of the ethics approval and previously discussed data limitations, students could not be tracked longitudinally across assessments. Therefore, each assessment was analysed separately. As the analysis was conducted at the assessment level rather than longi­ tudinally, the results are interpreted cautiously and are not intended to establish causal or confirmatory claims. GenAI",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"be tracked longitudinally across assessments. Therefore, each assessment was analysed separately. As the analysis was conducted at the assessment level rather than longi­ tudinally, the results are interpreted cautiously and are not intended to establish causal or confirmatory claims. GenAI technology use The 120 + unique GenAI use cases reported by students across the three assessments were analysed using a descriptive categorisation. The purpose of this analysis was not to generate interpretive themes, but to classify the clearly stated pedagogical function of each reported GenAI use. Rather than focusing solely on which tools were used, the analysis examined what students reported using GenAI in their assessment work. Each student disclosure was treated as a single unit of meaning, and the full response was reviewed to identify the dominant reported function of GenAI use. As the declarations were explicit and task-focused, most uses could be readily categorised (Table 1). For example, dec­ larations involving clarification, feedback, verification, or checking results were grouped under the broader category of feedback and refinement, while declarations involving researching, understanding, conceptualising, or refining understanding were grouped",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"most uses could be readily categorised (Table 1). For example, dec­ larations involving clarification, feedback, verification, or checking results were grouped under the broader category of feedback and refinement, while declarations involving researching, understanding, conceptualising, or refining understanding were grouped under learning. A descriptive synthesis method using conventional qualita­ tive content analysis was used (Hsieh & Shannon, 2005). Extracted data on reported uses of GenAI were coded inductively and grouped into overarching categories. Initial categories were developed inductively from stu­ dents’ declarations during an in-person meeting with the lead researcher and a second academic. The two academ­ ics collaboratively coded the reported uses, examined the clarity and consistency of the proposed groupings, and discussed any differences in interpretation until consen­ sus was reached. No major disagreements arose, reflect­ ing the descriptive and explicit nature of the reported GenAI uses. As coding decisions were made collabora­ tively rather than independently, an intercoder reliability coefficient was not calculated. This coding process was originally undertaken as part of the quality assurance associated with the unit review. GenAI was used only as a brainstorming",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"coding decisions were made collabora­ tively rather than independently, an intercoder reliability coefficient was not calculated. This coding process was originally undertaken as part of the quality assurance associated with the unit review. GenAI was used only as a brainstorming and drafting aid to assist with possible category labels and descriptions. This use aligns with emerging methodological work suggesting that LLMs can support early-stage coding and theme suggestion, provided that final interpretive decisions remain the responsibility of researchers (Turobov et al., 2024; Wen et al., 2026). In this study, all final categorisation decisions, category names, and interpretations remained under the researcher’s control. Once the framework was established and all use cases were coded, the frequency of instances within each of the four categories was calculated for each of the three assessments. This analysis provided the primary evidence for evaluating student GenAI use. To move beyond a sim­ ple classification of GenAI use, the framework developed in this case study was compared with the SAMR frame­ work to provide a deeper understanding of how GenAI is being used. That is, if",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"GenAI use. To move beyond a sim­ ple classification of GenAI use, the framework developed in this case study was compared with the SAMR frame­ work to provide a deeper understanding of how GenAI is being used. That is, if students are simply substitut­ ing or augmenting their assessment tools, or if GenAI is allowing students to modify or redefine how they can be provided help during their assessment. The map­ ping followed the definitions of the SAMR framework, Table 1 Classification of students’ GenAI uses by learning function and theme Student uses Coding Theme Researching, Understanding, Conceptualis­ ing, Refining Understanding 1. Researching → Information gathering and exploration 2. Understanding → Basic comprehension of concepts 3. Conceptualising → Building mental models and frameworks 4. Refining Understanding → Deepening and correcting existing knowledge Learning Formatting and Assistance in Admin Tasks 1. Formatting → Structure, organisation, and presentation 2. Admin Tasks → Supporting administrative and organisational work Formatting Calculations 1. Direct computational assistance → Performing calculations Doing Task Clarification, Feedback, Verification, Check­ ing Results 1. Clarification → Ensuring understanding and clarity 2.",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,8,"Formatting → Structure, organisation, and presentation 2. Admin Tasks → Supporting administrative and organisational work Formatting Calculations 1. Direct computational assistance → Performing calculations Doing Task Clarification, Feedback, Verification, Check­ ing Results 1. Clarification → Ensuring understanding and clarity 2. Feedback → Evaluative responses and suggestions 3. Verification → Confirming accuracy and validity 4. Checking Results → Quality control and validation Feedback",62
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,9,"Page 9 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 as described by Puentedura (2006), and is displayed in Table 2. This mapping was interpretive and context-depen­ dent rather than a fixed classification. What constitutes substitution, augmentation, modification, or redefini­ tion may vary across disciplines, assessment types, and learning contexts. Therefore, the SAMR mapping in this study should be understood within the context of this case study and the specific assessment design. To sup­ port this process, the mapping was benchmarked against prior applications of SAMR in educational technology research, including Blundell et al. (2022), Romrell et al. (2014), and recent work by Belkina et al. (2025) mapping GenAI use to SAMR. The qualitative and quantitative findings were inte­ grated by assessment. The coded GenAI-use declara­ tions were converted into category frequencies for A1, A2, and A3 and considered alongside the corresponding academic performance results. This enabled the analysis to examine both what students reported using GenAI for and whether reported GenAI use was associated with dif­ ferences in assessment performance. As students could not be",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,9,"A3 and considered alongside the corresponding academic performance results. This enabled the analysis to examine both what students reported using GenAI for and whether reported GenAI use was associated with dif­ ferences in assessment performance. As students could not be tracked longitudinally and the assessments dif­ fered in format and policy conditions, this integration was exploratory and was not used to infer that GenAI caused changes in student performance. Results GenAI tools used Students are allowed to use any GenAI tool they want for their assessments, except DeepSeek, due to the insti­ tution’s limitations. Whilst they are allowed to use any tool, Copilot is the only approved tool for use at Southern Cross University due to the institutional licence protect­ ing data. Students were also allowed to use multiple tools if they declared all use. The average reported tool use per assessment was 1.33, 1.27 & 1.23 for A1, A2, and A3, respectively. For A1, 2 students used more than 2 tools (1 used 4 and 1 used 3). For A2, only 1 student used more than 2 tools (3).",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,9,"per assessment was 1.33, 1.27 & 1.23 for A1, A2, and A3, respectively. For A1, 2 students used more than 2 tools (1 used 4 and 1 used 3). For A2, only 1 student used more than 2 tools (3). For A3, no students used more than 2 tools. Students’ reported tool use followed a consistent trend across the three assessments, with some variations on peripheral uses by individual students, as shown in Fig. 1. The results show that ChatGPT was the most used tool across all three assessments (A1 n = 16, A2 n = 13, A3 n = 21). Copilot was the next most frequently used tool, although its uptake was notably lower (A1 n = 5, A2 n = 5, A3 n = 5). Other tools, such as Gemini, Grok, Perplex­ ity, and mybib, were used by fewer than 10% of students, while Claude, Manus, and Microsoft Edge showed mini­ mal engagement. GenAI use types A total of 23 unique GenAI uses were reported across the three assessments. This can be seen in Table 3. These are",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,9,"fewer than 10% of students, while Claude, Manus, and Microsoft Edge showed mini­ mal engagement. GenAI use types A total of 23 unique GenAI uses were reported across the three assessments. This can be seen in Table 3. These are the direct uses from the students’ declarations and were not typically elaborated. As an example of the extent of the elaboration, the following were extracted from a student declaration in A3: “Clarifying the viability of formulas” and “Redefining electrical concepts for my Table 2 Student use definitions for each SAMR stage SAMR level Student-use definition Substitution Students use technology as a direct replace­ ment for a traditional tool, with no functional change to the task. Augmentation Students use technology as a replacement with functional improvement that enhances the task, but does not change its core purpose. Modification Students use technology to significantly redesign the task so the learning process is meaningfully different. Redefinition Students use technology to create new tasks previously impossible without it, fundamen­ tally expanding what they can do. Fig. 1 Student GenAI reported tool use across assessments",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,9,"to significantly redesign the task so the learning process is meaningfully different. Redefinition Students use technology to create new tasks previously impossible without it, fundamen­ tally expanding what they can do. Fig. 1 Student GenAI reported tool use across assessments",40
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,10,"Page 10 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 understanding,” as well as from a student statement in A3. For each assessment, the quantitative value of use is also provided in the following sections. A summary table of the frequency of student use mapped to each theme is presented in Table 4. The analysis of the student-reported use cases revealed an evolution in how students applied GenAI tools. In Assessment 1 (‘Purpose-Specific’ Use), student usage was dominated by Learning (54.5%) and Formatting (40.9%). Students primarily used GenAI for foundational tasks, such as brainstorming ideas, conducting back­ ground research, checking grammar, and structuring their presentations. Assessment 2 (A2) marked a notable shift. Reliance on the lower-order Formatting category decreased to 17.6%. By Assessment 3 (A3), Feedback had become the dominant application, accounting for 66.7% of all reported uses. Students described using GenAI to check the correctness of their calculations, verify answers to quiz questions, and clarify complex concepts. SAMR mapping Positioning GenAI use along the SAMR continuum helps educators understand whether technology merely increases efficiency or",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,10,"reported uses. Students described using GenAI to check the correctness of their calculations, verify answers to quiz questions, and clarify complex concepts. SAMR mapping Positioning GenAI use along the SAMR continuum helps educators understand whether technology merely increases efficiency or fundamentally transforms learn­ ing. By positioning the four ‘use case’ categories of Learning, Formatting, Doing Task and Feedback along the SAMR continuum, this analysis highlights how GenAI use ranges from simple substitution of routine tasks to the redefinition of learning and assessment practices (Table 5). This framework offers a valuable approach to understanding how students interact with GenAI, whether it supports or transforms their learning processes. Learning Within the learning category, student use of GenAI pri­ marily operates at the substitution and augmentation levels of the SAMR framework, replacing traditional resources and accelerating access to tailored informa­ tion. Students can still undertake core learning tasks such as researching, reading, and building conceptual understanding without GenAI. Therefore, in this cate­ gory, GenAI functions primarily as a supportive tool that enhances existing learning practices rather than trans­ forming them. Formatting Formatting-related GenAI",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,10,"still undertake core learning tasks such as researching, reading, and building conceptual understanding without GenAI. Therefore, in this cate­ gory, GenAI functions primarily as a supportive tool that enhances existing learning practices rather than trans­ forming them. Formatting Formatting-related GenAI use most closely aligns with the substitution level of the SAMR framework. Students can format their work manually using existing software and tools, so the use of GenAI in this area generally sub­ stitutes for, rather than transforms, students’ capabilities. Common examples include generating reference lists, proofreading for grammatical accuracy, rephrasing sen­ tences for clarity, or adjusting document structure. These tasks require minimal critical engagement and focus instead on presentation and technical accuracy. Doing the task In SAMR terms, this movement extends from modifi­ cation to redefinition. At the modification level, GenAI replaces routine algebraic or numerical operations, improving efficiency but leaving conceptual control with the student. At the redefinition level, however, the technology transforms the nature of the task, enabling students to generate complete, reasoned solutions to complex or unfamiliar problems they may not be able to solve independently.",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,10,"but leaving conceptual control with the student. At the redefinition level, however, the technology transforms the nature of the task, enabling students to generate complete, reasoned solutions to complex or unfamiliar problems they may not be able to solve independently. Feedback Feedback represents a unique category that spans all four levels of the SAMR framework, reflecting the diverse ways students use GenAI to evaluate and refine their work. At the substitution level, GenAI replaces basic Table 3 Student GenAI use extracted from declarations Analysis Conceptualisation Graph generation Rewording Analysis and formula help Drafting Image generation Summarising Brainstorming / ideation Editing and refinement Organisation / structure Theoretical understanding Calculations Feedback Referencing Tutor Checking / verification Formatting Refining understanding Understand­ ing Clarification Grammar and spelling Research Table 4 Coding frequencies for student use cases across assessment tasks Student use theme A1 A2 A3 Learning 54.50% (n = 18) 83.33% (n = 15) 59.26% (n = 16) Formatting 40.90% (n = 14) 17.6% (n = 11) 14.81% (n = 4) Doing Task 4.50% (n = 2) 22.22% (n = 4) 14.81% (n =",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,10,"A3 Learning 54.50% (n = 18) 83.33% (n = 15) 59.26% (n = 16) Formatting 40.90% (n = 14) 17.6% (n = 11) 14.81% (n = 4) Doing Task 4.50% (n = 2) 22.22% (n = 4) 14.81% (n = 4) Feedback 0.00% (n = 0) 11.11% (n = 2) 66.67% (n = 18) Table 5 SAMR level matched to the category of learning Category Core description SAMR level Learning Researching, understanding, conceptu­ alising, refining understanding Substitution → Augmentation Formatting Structuring, organising, presenting, and administrative tasks Substitution Doing Task Performing calculations, executing procedures, solving problems Modification → Redefinition Feedback Clarification, evaluation, verification, quality checking Substitution → Redefinition (All Levels)",109
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,"Page 11 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 feedback tools by identifying surface-level errors or sug­ gesting minor edits. Augmentation occurs when GenAI offers targeted explanations, helping students understand why something needs improvement. As students engage in more iterative interactions, GenAI personalises its feedback by tailoring responses to the student’s writing style, reasoning, or structure. At the highest level, redefi­ nition, GenAI enables forms of feedback that were previ­ ously unattainable, such as ongoing dialogue that allows students to question, test, and immediately apply advice in real time. Performance The scaffolded presentation (A1), GenAI users scored 15.76 ± 1.06 compared with 14.80 ± 1.08 for non- users, yielding a moderate effect size (Cohen’s d = 0.63) (Table 6). In the lab workbook (A2), users achieved a mean of 41.18 ± 3.64, while non-users averaged 40.60 ± 4.95 (d = 0.12), indicating a negligible effect. In the final take-home exam (A3), users again performed slightly better (23.44 ± 2.23 vs. 22.77 ± 3.73; d = 0.19). Discussion Students’ use of GenAI in response to policy",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,"40.60 ± 4.95 (d = 0.12), indicating a negligible effect. In the final take-home exam (A3), users again performed slightly better (23.44 ± 2.23 vs. 22.77 ± 3.73; d = 0.19). Discussion Students’ use of GenAI in response to policy change The findings suggest that student GenAI use is better understood through assessment purpose than through tool access alone. Ammari et al. (2025) identify common undergraduate uses of ChatGPT, including informa­ tion seeking, content generation, language refinement, metacognitive engagement, and conversational repair. Those categories are visible in this study, but the assess­ ment context helps explain when uses became relevant. Students did not use GenAI only because it was avail­ able; they used it for the kinds of support each task made useful. A presentation task made planning, language, structure, and communication support more relevant. A laboratory workbook and take-home quiz made check­ ing, clarification, and verification more relevant. This shifts the interpretation from “students use GenAI for common academic tasks” to “assessment design shapes which GenAI functions become meaningful.” This perspective complements general technology adoption models in higher education.",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,"quiz made check­ ing, clarification, and verification more relevant. This shifts the interpretation from “students use GenAI for common academic tasks” to “assessment design shapes which GenAI functions become meaningful.” This perspective complements general technology adoption models in higher education. While factors like self-efficacy, perceived trust, and performance expec­ tancy can drive intention to use GenAI (Rosli et al., 2026), our findings suggest that task structure influences how those tools are operationalised. The functional cat­ egory of use (e.g., learning, formatting) shifts accord­ ing to the specific cognitive demands and design of the task. This extends work on GenAI acceptance. Kaya and Adıgüzel (2025) frame ChatGPT adoption through con­ structs such as performance expectancy and effort expec­ tancy, where users are more likely to adopt a technology when they believe it will help them complete work effec­ tively and with less effort. The present study suggests that these expectations are not fixed properties of the tool. They are produced by the task. In the presentation, the perceived value of GenAI appears to sit in helping stu­ dents organise, phrase, and",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,"less effort. The present study suggests that these expectations are not fixed properties of the tool. They are produced by the task. In the presentation, the perceived value of GenAI appears to sit in helping stu­ dents organise, phrase, and prepare. In the workbook and quiz, its value lies more in checking whether disciplinary reasoning, calculations, or conceptual explanations are adequate. GenAI’s usefulness is therefore not a general academic purpose; it is task contingent. This task-contingent interpretation is important for policy. A purpose-specific or open-use label can define what is permitted, but it cannot determine how students will use GenAI. The assessment itself does that work. Where a task rewards polished communication, students are likely to use GenAI for language and structure. When a task requires problem-solving, students are likely to use GenAI to test answers, clarify reasoning, or generate pro­ cedural support. Policy may set the boundary of accept­ able use, but assessment design creates the demand for forms of use. Feedback-related uses are the clearest example of this interaction between policy and task. Zhan and Yan (2025) show",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,"pro­ cedural support. Policy may set the boundary of accept­ able use, but assessment design creates the demand for forms of use. Feedback-related uses are the clearest example of this interaction between policy and task. Zhan and Yan (2025) show that students’ engagement with ChatGPT feedback can involve cognitive, behavioural, affective, and meta­ cognitive dimensions, but that students do not always engage critically with GenAI feedback. Carless and Boud’s (2018) work on feedback literacy helps explain why this matters in the context of GenAI use. Feedback only supports learning when students can interpret it, judge its usefulness, and act on it. In this study, stu­ dents’ declarations of checking, clarification, and veri­ fication suggest that GenAI was being used as part of a feedback process, but the educational value of that pro­ cess depends on whether students questioned and evalu­ ated the output. A correctness check can be shallow. Comparing a GenAI explanation with the student’s own reasoning can support learning. This presents the first recommendation arising from this case study. If students Table 6 Assessment performance of students using",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,11,ated the output. A correctness check can be shallow. Comparing a GenAI explanation with the student’s own reasoning can support learning. This presents the first recommendation arising from this case study. If students Table 6 Assessment performance of students using and not using GenAI tools A1(/20) A2 (/50) A3 (/30) Use No GenAI use Use No GenAI use Use No GenAI use Mean 15.76 14.80 41.18 40.60 23.44 22.77 Standard Deviation 1.063 1.077 3.63 4.95 2.23 3.734 Significance Not significant (P > 0.05) Cohen’s d = 0.63 Not significant (P > 0.05) Cohen’s d = 0.12 Not significant (P > 0.05) Cohen’s d = 0.19,105
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"Page 12 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 engage with GenAI for feedback, they should highlight the feedback and how they may or may not have refined their work in response to it. This will allow educators to better understand students’ acceptance of GenAI-gener­ ated information and can provide the first steps toward increasing students’ feedback literacy. This will also help interpret students’ evaluative judgments of GenAI use. Bearman et al. (2024) argue that GenAI increases the need for students to judge GenAI outputs, AI-supported processes, and the quality of their own work. The findings also resist a simple narrative of academic integrity. Jensen et al. (2025) argue that early discussion of GenAI in higher education often positioned students as passive users or potential misusers. The declarations in this study show a broader range of student purposes. Stu­ dents reported GenAI use for preparation, clarification, refinement, and verification, not only for answer pro­ duction. This does not remove the risks associated with self-reported data or undeclared use, but it shows why integrity responses based",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"of student purposes. Stu­ dents reported GenAI use for preparation, clarification, refinement, and verification, not only for answer pro­ duction. This does not remove the risks associated with self-reported data or undeclared use, but it shows why integrity responses based only on restriction or suspicion are too limited. The key design question is whether the assessment requires them to demonstrate the judgement, reasoning, and accountability that GenAI cannot simply declare on their behalf. These findings further suggest that purpose-specific and open-use policies should be paired with assessment requirements that make students’ evaluative judgement visible. Where students use GenAI for feedback, clarifica­ tion, or verification, they could be required to identify the feedback received and briefly explain whether and how it informed revisions to their work. Greater restriction may be appropriate where GenAI can perform the intended cognitive work without students demonstrating their own reasoning or understanding. SAMR framework to describe the students’ use of GenAI The SAMR mapping is useful because it distinguishes uses of GenAI that resemble earlier forms of academic support from those that provide students with access",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"demonstrating their own reasoning or understanding. SAMR framework to describe the students’ use of GenAI The SAMR mapping is useful because it distinguishes uses of GenAI that resemble earlier forms of academic support from those that provide students with access to new forms of support. Learning and formatting uses are closer to substitution and augmentation because stu­ dents can already seek explanations, summaries, refer­ ence help, grammar support, and presentation advice through existing tools and people. GenAI changes the speed, availability, and personalisation of that support, but the underlying activity remains familiar. In these cases, GenAI appears to streamline existing study prac­ tices rather than fundamentally alter the learning task. For students working with unfamiliar physics con­ cepts, language, or assessment expectations, GenAI may provide a useful scaffold for entering the task. The limitation is that these uses mostly support learning conditions rather than requiring students to demon­ strate deeper reasoning. This aligns with Hamilton et al.’s (2016) caution that movement along SAMR should not be treated as automatic evidence of better learning. A substitution-level use can be educationally useful,",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"learning conditions rather than requiring students to demon­ strate deeper reasoning. This aligns with Hamilton et al.’s (2016) caution that movement along SAMR should not be treated as automatic evidence of better learning. A substitution-level use can be educationally useful, while a higher-level use can still be weak if it removes the student from the reasoning process. The stronger shift occurs when GenAI is used for feed­ back. Students have always been able to seek feedback from teachers, peers, exemplars, worked solutions, and computational tools. What GenAI changes is the imme­ diacy and interactivity of that feedback which can be defined by categories of abundance (Lindsay et al., 2026). It can respond during independent study, rephrase expla­ nations, generate alternative approaches, and allow stu­ dents to keep questioning an answer until it becomes clearer. It creates a form of iterative, student-controlled feedback that was not previously available at the same scale or speed. Recent higher education research sup­ ports this shift, showing that students value GenAI feed­ back for its accessibility, timeliness, volume, clarity, and lower social risk compared with",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"student-controlled feedback that was not previously available at the same scale or speed. Recent higher education research sup­ ports this shift, showing that students value GenAI feed­ back for its accessibility, timeliness, volume, clarity, and lower social risk compared with seeking feedback from teachers, although they still tend to view teacher feed­ back as more trustworthy and contextually informed (Henderson et al., 2026). This suggests that students may accept GenAI feedback not because it is efficient, but because it changes the conditions under which feedback can be accessed and used. However, acceptance should not be confused with educational effectiveness. Weidlich et al. (2025) found that students perceived teacher feed­ back as harder to accept than peer or LLM feedback and reported less willingness to revise from it, yet teacher feedback produced stronger improvements in students’ scientific argumentation and formal quality. Zhan and Yan (2026) similarly show that students’ engagement with ChatGPT feedback can vary in depth, so feedback use should not be treated as automatically transformative. Its value depends on whether students use feedback to com­ pare reasoning, identify errors,",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,"Zhan and Yan (2026) similarly show that students’ engagement with ChatGPT feedback can vary in depth, so feedback use should not be treated as automatically transformative. Its value depends on whether students use feedback to com­ pare reasoning, identify errors, and make informed revi­ sions rather than simply accept an answer. GenAI can generate steps, identify equations, interpret prompts, and explain methods in ways that tools such as Wolfram Alpha or MATLAB generally cannot, unless the student already knows how to frame the problem. This was a key point identified in a study by Quince et al. (2026) during interviews with undergraduate engineering students. They stated that GenAI will force students to understand the ‘basics’ better. This can support learning when students use GenAI to understand why a method works or to check their reasoning. However, it can also weaken assessment evidence when students submit a complete response they cannot explain or justify. Liu et al. (2026) similarly identified a tension between GenAI as cognitive scaffolding and as a mechanism for cogni­ tive offloading in undergraduate programming educa­ tion. Zhang",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,12,assessment evidence when students submit a complete response they cannot explain or justify. Liu et al. (2026) similarly identified a tension between GenAI as cognitive scaffolding and as a mechanism for cogni­ tive offloading in undergraduate programming educa­ tion. Zhang and Xu (2025) show this tension in higher,48
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"Page 13 of 16 Quince and Faulconer International Journal of STEM Education (2026) 13:61 education, where GenAI use can support task comple­ tion while also increasing technological dependence. Bas­ tani et al. (2025) similarly warn that GenAI can improve immediate performance while reducing learning when students rely on it without appropriate guardrails. The same technology can therefore support or obscure learn­ ing depending on whether the task requires students to account for their reasoning. Relationship between students’ GenAI use and their academic performance The performance findings are best read as exploratory, but they are still useful within the wider sector debate. Students who declared GenAI use achieved slightly higher mean scores across the assessments, although the differences were not statistically significant. This does not support a claim that GenAI improved performance. It does, however, show that declared GenAI use was not associated with poorer achievement in this case. This sits within a mixed and still-developing evidence base. Stadler et al. (2024) found that students using LLMs experienced lower cognitive load but produced weaker reasoning and argumentation than students using tradi­",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"not associated with poorer achievement in this case. This sits within a mixed and still-developing evidence base. Stadler et al. (2024) found that students using LLMs experienced lower cognitive load but produced weaker reasoning and argumentation than students using tradi­ tional search engines. Abbas et al. (2024) similarly found that heavier ChatGPT use was associated with poorer academic performance, alongside procrastination and memory loss. These studies support caution, particularly where GenAI reduces effort without requiring students to demonstrate reasoning. Other evidence suggests that the effect of GenAI depends on how it is used. Pallant et al. (2026) found stronger learning outcomes when stu­ dents used GenAI to construct and augment knowledge, while more procedural uses were associated with lower- level learning. This reinforces recent higher education STEM research indicating that GenAI-assisted task exe­ cution does not inherently guarantee higher conceptual mastery or performance boosts (Zhang & Zhang, 2026). Collectively, these works align with the present study, where the most educationally defensible uses were not simply “GenAI use” but feedback, clarification, and veri­ fication uses that kept students engaged with their",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"mastery or performance boosts (Zhang & Zhang, 2026). Collectively, these works align with the present study, where the most educationally defensible uses were not simply “GenAI use” but feedback, clarification, and veri­ fication uses that kept students engaged with their own work. The finding also aligns with emerging case-study literature showing that GenAI implementation var­ ies substantially by discipline, task type, and assessment design (Belkina et al., 2025). The current study therefore adds a STEM case to this wider pattern. Its contribu­ tion is not evidence of a performance gain, but evidence that declared GenAI use did not correspond with poorer achievement when assessments required students to remain responsible for explanation, reasoning, and final judgement. Conclusions The findings demonstrate a noticeable progression in how students have utilised GenAI over time. Initially, stu­ dents used GenAI primarily for learning and formatting, either as a substitute or to enhance efficiency. As restric­ tions on GenAI use eased, students increasingly relied on it for feedback and verification of their work. This shift may indicate that students began to engage with GenAI in more",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"either as a substitute or to enhance efficiency. As restric­ tions on GenAI use eased, students increasingly relied on it for feedback and verification of their work. This shift may indicate that students began to engage with GenAI in more sophisticated ways, aligning with theoretical markers of deeper cognitive engagement. In these sce­ narios, GenAI served as a valuable resource for students to review and understand their work, rather than simply generating final answers. Although the improvements in student performance were modest and not statistically significant, there is tentative evidence that GenAI may help a greater proportion of students achieve passing grades when tasks are thoughtfully designed. These outcomes highlight the importance of assess­ ment design in ensuring the responsible and effec­ tive integration of GenAI. Specifically, assessments that require students to explain their reasoning, jus­ tify their answers, and check for errors can help ensure that GenAI is used as a cognitive partner rather than a shortcut. Transitioning from a purpose-specific to an open-use GenAI policy provided students with opportu­ nities to exercise evaluative judgement, a crucial skill for",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"check for errors can help ensure that GenAI is used as a cognitive partner rather than a shortcut. Transitioning from a purpose-specific to an open-use GenAI policy provided students with opportu­ nities to exercise evaluative judgement, a crucial skill for graduates. However, the findings of this study should be inter­ preted with its limitations in mind. Being conducted in a single institutional setting with a small disciplinary cohort may limit the generalisability of the results. Sec­ ond, the study relied on students’ self-reported GenAI use rather than direct observation, which may be sub­ ject to recall bias. While behavioural shifts align with theoretical models of learning, this study did not directly measure cognitive states or the long-term develop­ ment of evaluative judgment, so these findings should be interpreted as evidence of changed engagement pat­ terns rather than definitive proof of improved cognitive capability. Future research should include larger and longer-term studies, direct assessments of evaluative judgement, and exploration of GenAI’s impact across various disciplines to gain a comprehensive understanding of when GenAI truly transforms learning and assessment. Qualitative interviews would",180
DOC_054,s40594-026-00651-w.pdf,10.1186/s40594-026-00651-w,13,"improved cognitive capability. Future research should include larger and longer-term studies, direct assessments of evaluative judgement, and exploration of GenAI’s impact across various disciplines to gain a comprehensive understanding of when GenAI truly transforms learning and assessment. Qualitative interviews would more deeply probe the “why” behind student behaviour. Further investigation is needed to determine the statistical significance of GenAI’s impact on student performance, particularly as the technology and student GenAI literacy continue to evolve. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at ​h​t​t​p​s​:​/​/​d​o​i​.​o​r​ g​/​1​0​.​1​1​8​6​/​s​4​0​5​9​4​-​0​2​6​-​0​0​6​5​1​-​w​.​",86
