DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,1,"International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9 *Correspondence: Mohammadreza Farrokhnia m.farrokhnia@utwente.nl 1Department of Learning, Data Analytics, and Technology, University of Twente, Enschede, The Netherlands 2Department of Educational Technology, Kharazmi University, Tehran, Iran 3Institute for Teaching and Learning Innovation, The University of Queensland, St Lucia, Australia 4Education and Learning Sciences Group, Wageningen University & Research, Wageningen, The Netherlands Generative AI offers more, but students revise less: comparing the effects of teacher and AI feedback on student essay revisions Mohammadreza Farrokhnia1* , Saeed Latifi2 , Pantelis M. Papadopoulos1 , Loes Hogenkamp1 , Hannie Gijlers1 , Hassan Khosravi3 and Omid Noroozi4 International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Abstract Providing high-quality feedback on student writing is essential yet increasingly difficult due to rising class sizes and limited instructional capacity. Generative AI (GenAI) offers a promising and scalable alternative, but its effectiveness compared to traditional teacher feedback, particularly across different prompting techniques, remains uncertain. This study employed a quantitative, randomized three-group experimental design with 70 graduate students to compare the effects of teacher feedback and GenAI feedback generated using",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,1,"but its effectiveness compared to traditional teacher feedback, particularly across different prompting techniques, remains uncertain. This study employed a quantitative, randomized three-group experimental design with 70 graduate students to compare the effects of teacher feedback and GenAI feedback generated using two prompting techniques: Zero-shot and chain-of-thought (CoT). The study explored how these feedback sources affect feedback quality and students’ uptake during essay revision. It involved a two-stage process in which students first wrote an argumentative essay and then revised it based on the feedback received. Feedback and essay quality were evaluated using a rubric based on Toulmin’s model of argumentation and analysed using inferential statistical methods. Results showed that CoT prompting produced higher quality feedback than both Zero-shot prompting and teacher feedback, suggesting that stepwise reasoning in CoT aligns GenAI outputs more closely with the cognitive demands of argumentative writing. However, this higher feedback quality did not lead to significantly greater improvements in revisions. Teacher feedback, although rated lower in quality, resulted in comparable gains in essay quality. In addition, GenAI feedback quality was significantly associated with students’ initial essay",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,1,"However, this higher feedback quality did not lead to significantly greater improvements in revisions. Teacher feedback, although rated lower in quality, resulted in comparable gains in essay quality. In addition, GenAI feedback quality was significantly associated with students’ initial essay quality, whereas teacher feedback quality showed no such association. These findings indicate that feedback quality alone is insufficient to enhance writing outcomes; rather, students’ engagement with and uptake of feedback play a critical role. Overall, the results highlight the potential of hybrid intelligent feedback systems in which teachers support students in interpreting and applying GenAI feedback to meaningfully improve their writing. Keywords Generative AI, Prompt engineering, Argumentative writing, Feedback, Higher education",111
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,2,"Page 2 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Introduction The ability to articulate ideas through well-written text is widely recognised as essential for success in both academic and professional settings (Baker et al., 2025). In academic contexts, writing often takes the form of assignments such as essays, research papers, reports, and reflections, serving as a means of formative assessment. These tasks allow students to foster critical thinking (Butcher, 2022) and enhance essential communication skills (Bora, 2023). In addition, writing assignments support the development of gen­ eral and domain-specific learning (Alexander et al., 2023). Supporting this view, results from a large-scale, multi-institutional study suggest that effective writing practices are positively associated with deep approaches to learning, as well as with perceived gains in learning and development (Anderson et al., 2015). Among academic writing tasks, argumentative essays are particularly common in higher education (Latifi et al., 2021). Such assignments require students to investigate a topic, evaluate evidence, take a position, and construct a coherent, well-reasoned argu­ ment (Noroozi et al., 2016). However, despite",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,2,"writing tasks, argumentative essays are particularly common in higher education (Latifi et al., 2021). Such assignments require students to investigate a topic, evaluate evidence, take a position, and construct a coherent, well-reasoned argu­ ment (Noroozi et al., 2016). However, despite their pedagogical value, many students struggle with writing argumentative essays, often producing texts with weak struc­ ture, limited reasoning, and insufficient elaboration (Kellogg & Whiteford, 2009; Latifi et al., 2021). These difficulties may arise from a lack of understanding of what consti­ tutes a strong argumentative essay (Bacha, 2010), challenges in applying this knowledge (Noroozi et al., 2016), or difficulties in navigating disciplinary conventions (Noroozi et al., 2018). Furthermore, argumentative writing is a cognitively demanding, high-level learning activity requiring advanced reasoning and critical evaluation (Conner et al., 2014; Fan & Chen, 2021). Taken together, these challenges highlight the need for struc­ tured support to help students strengthen their argumentative writing skills. Feedback provision is a well-established means of supporting students’ writing devel­ opment (Henderson et al., 2019), helping them bridge the gap between current and desired performance by clarifying expectations",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,2,"struc­ tured support to help students strengthen their argumentative writing skills. Feedback provision is a well-established means of supporting students’ writing devel­ opment (Henderson et al., 2019), helping them bridge the gap between current and desired performance by clarifying expectations and guiding revision (Nicol & Macfar­ lane‐Dick, 2006). Traditionally, teachers and peers have provided such feedback (Wu et al., 2022); however, recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) now offer immediate, structured, and a more scalable alternatives (Usher, 2025). GenAI tools can reduce teachers’ workloads (Wu et al., 2025) while improving the accessibility and consistency of feedback (Henderson et al., 2025). Despite this potential, research on the quality and relevance of GenAI feedback remains inconclusive (Çağlar‐Özhan et al., 2025), likely due to differences in language models used (Dai et al., 2024) and, more importantly, in the prompts crafted to guide GenAI responses (Wu et al., 2025), a pro­ cess referred to as “prompt engineering” (Lee & Palmer, 2025). Although scholars have emphasised the importance of effective prompt design (Çağlar‐Özhan et al., 2025) and the need to align prompts with the",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,2,"GenAI responses (Wu et al., 2025), a pro­ cess referred to as “prompt engineering” (Lee & Palmer, 2025). Although scholars have emphasised the importance of effective prompt design (Çağlar‐Özhan et al., 2025) and the need to align prompts with the cognitive demands of the task (Chen et al., 2024), limited research has systematically examined how dif­ ferent prompting strategies influence the quality and pedagogical usefulness of GenAI feedback, particularly for cognitively demanding tasks such as argumentative writing. Addressing this gap, the present study investigates how GenAI feedback generated under two prompting conditions, namely Zero-shot and Chain of Thought (CoT), compares with teacher feedback in terms of (1) feedback quality and (2) usefulness for students’ essay revisions, as reflected in their uptake of feedback. Furthermore, by implementing the study in Persian, a linguistically underrepresented language in large language model",138
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,3,"Page 3 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 (LLM) training, this research extends current understanding of GenAI feedback beyond English-dominant contexts. Together, these efforts aim to demonstrate how GenAI can be effectively used as a feedback source to support students’ argumentative writing in higher education, particularly in underrepresented linguistic settings. Related work and theoretical framing This study has an exploratory nature in which an inductive reasoning approach was adopted to identify the research gap. Rather than conducting an exhaustive review, we synthesised recent studies to highlight key patterns and limitations that shaped the study’s focus. Building on this synthesis, this section reviews three interconnected strands of literature. The first examines feedback as a mechanism to support writing, outlining how high-quality feedback on written assignments is defined in the current study. The second explores GenAI as a feedback source, highlighting its potential while noting persistent challenges related to precision and pedagogical alignment. The third addresses prompt engineering, a critical factor influencing the quality of feedback gener­ ated by GenAI. Feedback to support",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,3,"second explores GenAI as a feedback source, highlighting its potential while noting persistent challenges related to precision and pedagogical alignment. The third addresses prompt engineering, a critical factor influencing the quality of feedback gener­ ated by GenAI. Feedback to support writing Feedback is defined as “information communicated to the learner that is intended to modify his or her thinking or behavior to improve learning” (Shute, 2008, p. 153), aiming to bridge the gap between students’ current performance and the desired level (Sadler, 1989). Extensive research confirms the effectiveness of feedback in enhancing learning outcomes (Morris et al., 2021) and improving students’ academic writing (Alvarez et al., 2012). Specifically, in the context of argumentative essays, studies showed that feedback can help university students enhance essay quality, acquire domain-specific knowledge, and strengthen peer-learning processes (Latifi et al., 2021; Noroozi et al., 2016). Despite this potential, not all feedback leads to meaningful improvement. Its effective­ ness largely depends on the extent to which it fosters students’ understanding of their performance and how to improve it, thereby increasing the likelihood of implementation (Nelson &",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,3,"Despite this potential, not all feedback leads to meaningful improvement. Its effective­ ness largely depends on the extent to which it fosters students’ understanding of their performance and how to improve it, thereby increasing the likelihood of implementation (Nelson & Schunn, 2009; Wu & Schunn, 2020). To facilitate this process, high-quality feedback should contain features that make its purpose specific and actionable. Research has demonstrated that localized comments and concrete solutions can strengthen stu­ dents’ understanding and implementation, whereas overly elaborate explanations may obscure meaning and reduce clarity (Nelson & Schunn, 2009). Similarly, focused and substantively oriented comments often lead to higher-quality revisions (Patchan et al., 2016), while clear and concise solutions help sustain engagement with feedback (Wu & Schunn, 2020). Moreover, suggestive feedback has been found to encourage reflection and foster deeper argumentative improvement (Alvarez et al., 2012). Therefore, effective feedback should identify specific problems in relation to expected standards or learning goals and provide concrete suggestions for improvement (Nicol & Macfarlane‐Dick, 2006). However, offering such high-quality and individualized feed­ back consistently can be challenging in typical educational settings.",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,3,"effective feedback should identify specific problems in relation to expected standards or learning goals and provide concrete suggestions for improvement (Nicol & Macfarlane‐Dick, 2006). However, offering such high-quality and individualized feed­ back consistently can be challenging in typical educational settings. Teachers remain the primary source of feedback (van der Kleij & Adie, 2020), yet factors such as large class sizes, heavy workloads, and modular curricula may limit opportunities for timely and detailed responses (Maringe & Sing, 2014; Yan et al., 2021). Consequently, teacher",83
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,4,"Page 4 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 feedback may not always be able to provide the depth or frequency needed to fully sup­ port students’ ongoing learning and revision processes. GenAI as a feedback source In response to the challenges university teachers face in providing feedback on students’ written assignments, GenAI has been proposed as a scalable and accessible alternative feedback source (Farrokhnia et al., 2025a; Soleimani et al., 2025). A systematic review by Lee and Moore (2024) concluded that GenAI enhances feedback timeliness, personaliza­ tion, and learner motivation while reducing teacher workload. In a multilevel meta-anal­ ysis, Fleckenstein et al. (2023) reported a moderate effect size for automated feedback on written assignments across 84 samples. Banihashem et al. (2024) evaluated a GenAI tool powered by GPT-3.5 and found that it provided more descriptive feedback, par­ ticularly on argumentative writing, than peer reviewers. Dai et al. (2024) demonstrated that feedback generated by a GPT-4-based system was more readable, structured, and pedagogically aligned than feedback provided by teachers. In domain-specific writing",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,4,"it provided more descriptive feedback, par­ ticularly on argumentative writing, than peer reviewers. Dai et al. (2024) demonstrated that feedback generated by a GPT-4-based system was more readable, structured, and pedagogically aligned than feedback provided by teachers. In domain-specific writing tasks, Jürgensmeier and Skiera (2024) reported that GPT-4-generated feedback closely mirrored expert evaluations. Similarly, Usher (2025) found that a GPT-3.5-based tool delivered more detailed and structured feedback than peer reviewers. However, despite these encouraging developments, important limitations of GenAI feedback remain. Comparative studies consistently indicate that such feedback often lacks diagnostic precision and contextual nuance. For instance, Dai et al. (2024) found that GPT-3.5 produced feedback with lower alignment to effective pedagogical princi­ ples compared to teacher-generated feedback. Similar results have emerged in writing contexts, where GenAI feedback, though descriptively rich, was less effective at identify­ ing specific problems and lacked the critical nuance found in peer or instructor input (Banihashem et al., 2024). Usher (2025) similarly observed that while feedback gener­ ated by GenAI tools often provided more detailed insights and specific suggestions than peer feedback, it occasionally included",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,4,"lacked the critical nuance found in peer or instructor input (Banihashem et al., 2024). Usher (2025) similarly observed that while feedback gener­ ated by GenAI tools often provided more detailed insights and specific suggestions than peer feedback, it occasionally included irrelevant or contradictory elements. In contrast, human feedback tended to be more personalized and context-sensitive. Similarly, Guo et al. (2024) reported that a substantial portion of GenAI feedback contained excessive praise or unwarranted inferences, which is consistent with earlier findings by Guo and Wang (2024). In the specific context of argumentative writing, Jansen et al. (2024) found that only 59% of participants rated feedback generated by a GPT-3.5-powered tool as useful, compared to 88% for feedback from human experts. These shortcomings have been attributed not only to model limitations but also to how feedback is elicited. While GPT-4 generally outperforms earlier GPT versions in accuracy, consistency, and educational alignment (Dai et al., 2024; Jürgensmeier & Ski­ era, 2024), its effectiveness still largely depends on the quality of the input it receives. For example, Jürgensmeier and Skiera (2024) reported that GPT-4’s",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,4,"earlier GPT versions in accuracy, consistency, and educational alignment (Dai et al., 2024; Jürgensmeier & Ski­ era, 2024), its effectiveness still largely depends on the quality of the input it receives. For example, Jürgensmeier and Skiera (2024) reported that GPT-4’s feedback accuracy sig­ nificantly improved only when correct exercise solutions were included in the prompt. This is in line with previous findings showing that poorly structured prompts can lead to generic or misleading feedback, underscoring the importance of prompt engineering (Evmenova et al., 2024; Jukiewicz, 2024).",86
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,5,"Page 5 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Prompt engineering Prompt engineering, also referred to as prompt design, prompt programming, or simply prompting, is an emerging discipline within the field of AI, with a particular empha­ sis on optimizing human–LLM interactions (Oppenlaender et al., 2024). It serves as a “steering mechanism” (Cain, 2024, p. 51) through which human users shape AI behav­ ior by carefully crafting and structuring input prompts to elicit accurate, relevant, and contextually appropriate responses (Singh et al., 2024). A growing body of research has demonstrated that prompt formulation can significantly influence the quality and con­ tent of outputs, even when the same underlying model is used (Chen et al., 2025; Wei et al., 2022). Importantly, this impact extends beyond general applications into educational contexts (Chen et al., 2024), influencing outcomes such as automated essay scoring (Lee et al., 2024) and facilitating complex educational tasks like scientific project planning (Knoth et al., 2024). Prompt engineering strategies can be broadly categorized by their function and the level of guidance",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,5,"2024), influencing outcomes such as automated essay scoring (Lee et al., 2024) and facilitating complex educational tasks like scientific project planning (Knoth et al., 2024). Prompt engineering strategies can be broadly categorized by their function and the level of guidance provided to the model. A key distinction exists between approaches requiring minimal task-specific training, such as Zero-shot and Few-shot prompting, and those that explicitly model reasoning, such as CoT prompting (Sahoo et al., 2024). In Zero-shot prompting, the model receives a simple task description, often with con­ textual and/or role-based instructions (e.g., “Act as an academic writing teacher and provide feedback based on the uploaded rubric”), but no labeled examples. The model generates a response based solely on its general knowledge (Chen et al., 2025). In con­ trast, Few-shot prompting includes explicit input–output examples to guide the model by demonstrating how similar tasks have been completed. For instance, solved problems precede a new one to help the model infer task structure and expectations. CoT prompt­ ing is a more advanced technique that prompts the model to “think through” a problem",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,5,"how similar tasks have been completed. For instance, solved problems precede a new one to help the model infer task structure and expectations. CoT prompt­ ing is a more advanced technique that prompts the model to “think through” a problem step-by-step, enhancing logical coherence and accuracy (Wei et al., 2022). CoT can be embedded in both Zero-shot and Few-shot contexts and is especially effective for tasks requiring complex, structured reasoning (Kojima et al., 2022). The effectiveness of prompting strategies depends largely on the cognitive demands of the task (Chen et al., 2024). For straightforward tasks such as classification or factual retrieval, Zero-shot prompting is often sufficient. In contrast, tasks involving structured reasoning or stepwise evaluation benefit more from CoT prompting, which guides the model to reason explicitly and sequentially (Meincke et al., 2025). For instance, CoT has shown clear advantages in educational tasks requiring analytical judgment, improving the accuracy of automated scoring for open-ended science assessments (Cohn et al., 2024) and student essays (Lee et al., 2024). In the latter, CoT prompting combined with contextual item stems and scoring rubrics",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,5,"in educational tasks requiring analytical judgment, improving the accuracy of automated scoring for open-ended science assessments (Cohn et al., 2024) and student essays (Lee et al., 2024). In the latter, CoT prompting combined with contextual item stems and scoring rubrics improved the accuracy of GPT-4’s automated scoring when compared to baseline models lacking these components. Specifically, CoT with context and rubrics improved Zero-shot scoring accuracy by 13.44% compared to Zero-shot without CoT or context, and improved Few-shot accuracy by 3.7% over few- shot without CoT or context. These accuracy rates were calculated based on compari­ sons to human expert scores, which served as the reference standard. Despite promising advances, prompting strategies remain underexplored in GenAI feedback research. Most studies, such as Er et al. (2025) and Banihashem et al. (2024), rely on zero-shot prompts without exemplars or rubric-based walkthroughs. Both stud­ ies also emphasise the sensitivity of LLM outputs to prompt formulation, reinforcing the",154
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,6,"Page 6 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 need for more cognitively aligned approaches. This gap is especially evident in feedback on argumentative writing, which follows a structured format and requires careful evalu­ ation of claims, coherence, and supporting evidence, the kind of complex reasoning CoT prompting is designed to elicit. Moreover, existing research has focused mainly on Eng­ lish-written tasks and other languages well represented in LLM training corpora, with English being a prime example (Chimirri et al., 2025). This limits the generalizability of findings to underrepresented languages. The issue is critical because, despite advances in multilingual capabilities, LLMs still perform inconsistently across languages. For instance, research on Chilean Spanish found that complex verb forms and sentence structures significantly affected GenAI’s response quality, length, and interpretation (Viveros-Muñoz et al., 2025). Similarly, Arora and Srivastava (2024) showed that gen­ dered syntax, such as pronouns in English and verb conjugations in Hindi, influenced gender representation in GPT-4o outputs. Together, these findings suggest that GenAI remains highly sensitive to features of the initial",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,6,"Similarly, Arora and Srivastava (2024) showed that gen­ dered syntax, such as pronouns in English and verb conjugations in Hindi, influenced gender representation in GPT-4o outputs. Together, these findings suggest that GenAI remains highly sensitive to features of the initial draft, including linguistic structure. Research objective and questions This study addresses the critical gap in prompt engineering for educational feedback by systematically comparing Zero-shot and CoT prompting engineering techniques in the generation of feedback on argumentative essays. By shifting the focus from what GenAI can produce to how we interact with it, we aim to determine whether guiding an LLM’s reasoning process through CoT yields feedback that better aligns with the cognitive and instructional demands of argumentative writing tasks. Our aim is not to position prompt engineering as a general pedagogical solution, but to investigate its role in shap­ ing the quality and usefulness of GenAI feedback within specific educational contexts. In this regard, prompt engineering supports principles of eXplainable AI (XAI), which promotes the use of methods that generate transparent explanations and clear reasoning behind AI decisions (Khosravi et",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,6,"the quality and usefulness of GenAI feedback within specific educational contexts. In this regard, prompt engineering supports principles of eXplainable AI (XAI), which promotes the use of methods that generate transparent explanations and clear reasoning behind AI decisions (Khosravi et al., 2022). Applying XAI in feedback generation helps educators and students understand how outputs are produced, fostering greater trust, interpretability, and alignment with educational goals. To further extend the relevance and contributions of our findings, this study was con­ ducted in Persian, a language underrepresented in LLM training corpora and largely absent from prior GenAI feedback research. Persian features distinct linguistic charac­ teristics, including flexible word order, rich morphological structure, and the frequent omission of explicit subjects (Megerdoomian, 2020). These characteristics pose chal­ lenges for natural language processing applications (Moniri et al., 2024) and may thereby interfere with GenAI’s ability to generate accurate and contextually appropriate feed­ back. Conducting the study in this linguistic context thus helps test the generalizability of GenAI feedback beyond English-dominant settings. To evaluate the effectiveness of these prompting strategies, we compare GenAI feed­ back with",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,6,"to generate accurate and contextually appropriate feed­ back. Conducting the study in this linguistic context thus helps test the generalizability of GenAI feedback beyond English-dominant settings. To evaluate the effectiveness of these prompting strategies, we compare GenAI feed­ back with that provided by experienced teacher, offering a pedagogically meaningful benchmark for assessing quality and impact of GenAI outputs. Specifically, we ask: RQ1 How does the quality of feedback provided by a teacher differ from that provided by ChatGPT using Zero-shot and CoT prompt techniques? RQ2 How do different feedback sources (teacher, ChatGPT with Zero-shot prompt, ChatGPT with CoT prompt) respond to variations in student essay quality?",106
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,7,"Page 7 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 RQ3 To what extent does the source of feedback (teacher, ChatGPT with Zero-shot prompt, ChatGPT with CoT prompts) influence the improvement in the quality of argu­ mentative essays written by students? Method Participant This study was conducted during the 2024–2025 academic year at a public university in a Persian-speaking country. Participants were recruited using a combination of purpo­ sive and convenience sampling, whereby all students who met the inclusion criteria in programs accessible to the research team were invited to participate. To ensure a homo­ geneous sample aligned with the study objectives, participants were required to (1) be native Persian speakers, (2) be enrolled in the field of educational sciences, (3) be at the same educational level, and (4) have previously completed the compulsory Academic Writing course, which introduced argumentative essay writing. Based on these criteria, students enrolled in two compulsory courses, “Educational Psychology” and “Research Methodology,” were targeted. All students enrolled in these courses received an online invitation to participate. Recruitment procedures",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,7,"compulsory Academic Writing course, which introduced argumentative essay writing. Based on these criteria, students enrolled in two compulsory courses, “Educational Psychology” and “Research Methodology,” were targeted. All students enrolled in these courses received an online invitation to participate. Recruitment procedures and instructions were standardized across both courses. Participants were informed that participation was voluntary, responses would remain anonymous, academic standing would not be affected. In addition, one extra course credit was offered as an incentive for participation upon completing all required tasks, regardless of essay quality. A total of 70 bachelor’s-level students participated in the study. All were in their fifth semester (third year) and were enrolled in closely related programs within educational sciences. Detailed demographic characteristics of the sample are presented in Table 1. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to data collection. Study design and procedure This study adopts a quantitative approach with an exploratory orientation, employing a randomized three-group experimental design and a multi-step data collection pro­ cedure. This type of design is widely recommended for educational research because it allows controlled comparison among conditions",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,7,"This study adopts a quantitative approach with an exploratory orientation, employing a randomized three-group experimental design and a multi-step data collection pro­ cedure. This type of design is widely recommended for educational research because it allows controlled comparison among conditions and supports the identification of potential causal relationships (Creswell & Creswell, 2017). It was particularly suitable for distinguishing between traditional teacher feedback and two GenAI prompting strate­ gies, capturing both human–AI and intra-GenAI variations in feedback quality and usefulness. As shown in Fig. 1, participants were randomly assigned to one of three experimental conditions. In Condition 1 (n = 24), students received written feedback from a teacher with over 10 years teaching experience and expert in the field of argumentative writ­ ing. In Conditions 2 (n = 24) and 3 (n = 22), feedback was generated by ChatGPT using a Table 1 The participants’ demographic information Frequency Precent Gender Female 66 94.3 Male 4 5.7 Program Educational technology 46 65.7 Curriculum studies 24 34.3 Age Mean Std. deviation 20.38 0.54",170
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,7,Table 1 The participants’ demographic information Frequency Precent Gender Female 66 94.3 Male 4 5.7 Program Educational technology 46 65.7 Curriculum studies 24 34.3 Age Mean Std. deviation 20.38 0.54,30
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,8,"Page 8 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Zero-shot prompt and a CoT prompt, respectively. All communication throughout the study, including essay submissions and feedback delivery, was conducted via the univer­ sity’s learning management system, a platform routinely used for instructional purposes at the institution. Considering the linguistic context of the study, all essays were written in Persian. Data collection began with an introductory session in which the procedures and gen­ eral purpose of the study were explained. Students were informed that the research focused on how feedback supports argumentative essay writing. To minimize bias, they were not told the primary research objective or the specific source of the feedback they would receive. Following this, students were given one week to compose an argu­ mentative essay on the topic: “To use or not to use? Generative AI tools in education.” After submitting their initial drafts, participants received written feedback within two days, according to their assigned condition. This brief delay allowed sufficient time for the teacher to provide feedback on the",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,8,"or not to use? Generative AI tools in education.” After submitting their initial drafts, participants received written feedback within two days, according to their assigned condition. This brief delay allowed sufficient time for the teacher to provide feedback on the essays submitted by participants in Condition 1. After receiving the feedback, participants had one week to revise their drafts. This interval aligns with recommended practices in feedback provision, as time for reflection and planning can help students better understand the feedback, thereby increasing the likelihood of effective implementation (Wu & Schunn, 2020). Additionally, allowing time to revise written work based on feedback can shift students’ expectations about its pur­ pose, encouraging them to view it not as a judgment of themselves but as an evaluation of their work (Yeatman & Hewitt, 2021), thereby supporting greater motivation and self- esteem when engaging with feedback (Nicol & Macfarlane‐Dick, 2006). Fig. 1 Study procedure",151
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,9,"Page 9 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 At the conclusion of the study, participants were debriefed to disclose the full purpose of the research and the sources of the feedback they had received, as this information had been intentionally withheld to reduce bias. They were then given the option to with­ draw their data from the analysis, but no participants chose to do so. GPT-4o was selected as the ChatGPT language model for this study due to its demon­ strated effectiveness in generating high-quality feedback and its enhanced multilingual capabilities. Compared to earlier models, GPT-4o exhibits significantly improved com­ prehension and response generation in non-English languages, making it well-suited for multilingual educational contexts such as the present study (Hurst et al., 2024). Further­ more, recent research has shown that, at the current stage of AI development, GPT-4 variants outperform both human peers and earlier models in producing accurate, rubric- based assessments (e.g., Dai et al., 2024; Jürgensmeier & Skiera, 2024). The prompts used in both AI-based conditions were informed by",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,9,"the current stage of AI development, GPT-4 variants outperform both human peers and earlier models in producing accurate, rubric- based assessments (e.g., Dai et al., 2024; Jürgensmeier & Skiera, 2024). The prompts used in both AI-based conditions were informed by established feedback rubrics employed in the assessment of argumentative writing (Latifi et al., 2021; Noroozi et al., 2016). These rubrics are based on Toulmin’s argumentation model (1958), a well- regarded framework for evaluating essays. The model outlines the essential components of high-quality argumentative writing. While the Zero-shot prompt consisted of a direct instruction to generate feedback based on the rubric, the CoT prompt was designed to guide the model through a step-by-step evaluation process with a well-elaborated exam­ ple provided (see Appendix 1). Following the feedback intervention, students were given one additional week to revise their essays. All participants were instructed to incorpo­ rate the feedback into their revisions, resulting in a final draft of the same argumentative essay. Measurements Coding scheme to assess the quality of essay writing To assess the quality of students’ argumentative essays, we used a",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,9,"instructed to incorpo­ rate the feedback into their revisions, resulting in a final draft of the same argumentative essay. Measurements Coding scheme to assess the quality of essay writing To assess the quality of students’ argumentative essays, we used a rubric adopted by pre­ vious studies (Latifi et al., 2021; Valero Haro et al., 2024). The rubric was grounded in the Toulmin model of argumentation and included seven key components: introduction, position statement, supporting arguments and related evidence, counterarguments and related evidence, and conclusion. Each component was scored on a three-point scale: a score of 0 was assigned if the component was absent, 1 if it was present but insufficiently developed, and 2 if it was both present and sufficiently elaborated (see Appendix 2). The cumulative scores across all components were aggregated to generate a total essay quality score, ranging from 0 to 14. To minimize potential bias in scoring, two trained coders with expertise in education independently evaluated the essays, with all submissions pseudonymized and the coders blind to participants’ identities and group assignments. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,9,"from 0 to 14. To minimize potential bias in scoring, two trained coders with expertise in education independently evaluated the essays, with all submissions pseudonymized and the coders blind to participants’ identities and group assignments. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen’s Kappa, which indicated a substantial level of agreement between the coders, with 84 percent agreement (κ = 0.81, 95 percent CI [0.76, 0.86]; z = 28.12, p < .001). This reflects a statistically significant and reliable level of consensus in the scoring process. Coding scheme to assess the quality of feedback The quality of feedback provided to students was assessed using a rubric adopted from previous studies (Latifi et al., 2021; Valero Haro et al., 2024). The rubric was also grounded in the Toulmin model of argumentation and included seven key components.",133
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,10,"Page 10 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Each component was scored on a three-point scale: 0 if the component was absent, 1 if it was present but insufficiently developed (for example, only identifying a problem with limited justification), and 2 if it was both present and sufficiently elaborated, including a clear identification of the problem and concrete suggestions for improvement (see Appendix 3). This rubric aligns with the definition of high-quality feedback used in this study, as it is specific, focused on distinct components of an argumentative essay, and sufficiently elaborated to not only highlight issues but also provide actionable guidance for improvement. The cumulative scores across all components were summed to derive an overall feed­ back quality score. The same two evaluators who scored the essays also assessed the quality of feedback. Inter-rater reliability was evaluated using Cohen’s Kappa, which indicated a substantial level of agreement, with 77 percent agreement (κ = 0.78, 95 per­ cent CI [0.71, 0.85]; z = 21.76, p < .001), reflecting a statistically significant",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,10,"quality of feedback. Inter-rater reliability was evaluated using Cohen’s Kappa, which indicated a substantial level of agreement, with 77 percent agreement (κ = 0.78, 95 per­ cent CI [0.71, 0.85]; z = 21.76, p < .001), reflecting a statistically significant and consistent level of consensus between raters. Analysis To ensure the appropriateness of the data for statistical analyses, initial diagnostic tests were conducted. First, Levene’s test assessed the homogeneity of variances across the three feedback conditions, followed by the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test to examine data normality. Results confirmed that the assumptions of homogeneity and normality were met. To address RQ1, investigating differences in feedback quality among teacher- generated feedback, ChatGPT using Zero-shot prompting, and ChatGPT using CoT prompting, a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) with Tukey HSD post-hoc tests was utilized. For RQ2, which explored the extent to which different feedback sources respond to variations in student essay quality, Pearson bivariate correlation analyses were used. Finally, RQ3, which examined how feedback from different sources influ­ enced the improvement of argumentative essay quality from initial drafts to revised ver­ sions, was addressed",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,10,"sources respond to variations in student essay quality, Pearson bivariate correlation analyses were used. Finally, RQ3, which examined how feedback from different sources influ­ enced the improvement of argumentative essay quality from initial drafts to revised ver­ sions, was addressed using multiple methods. First, a repeated-measures ANOVA was conducted to assess within-subject changes in essay quality from initial to revised drafts across feedback conditions. Second, a one-way analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) was performed to compare the quality of revised drafts across feedback sources, controlling for the quality of initial drafts. In addition, multiple regression analyses were conducted across all conditions to determine the extent to which feedback quality and initial draft quality predicted the quality of students’ revised drafts. Results Feedback quality analysis (RQ1) First, this study investigated quality differences between feedback provided by a teacher, ChatGPT using Zero-shot, and ChatGPT using CoT prompt techniques. A one-way ANOVA was conducted to determine differences among the three conditions in terms of overall feedback quality (Table 2). The analysis revealed a significant effect of the feedback source on the quality of feedback,",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,10,"ChatGPT using CoT prompt techniques. A one-way ANOVA was conducted to determine differences among the three conditions in terms of overall feedback quality (Table 2). The analysis revealed a significant effect of the feedback source on the quality of feedback, F(2, 67) = 6.09, p = .004, ηp 2 = 0.15, indicating a moderate effect size. Post hoc comparisons using the Tukey HSD test indicated that feedback provided by ChatGPT using a CoT prompt (M = 12.90, SD = 1.26) significantly outperformed feedback pro­ vided by ChatGPT using a Zero-shot prompt (M = 11.25, SD = 1.98, p = .01), as well as",103
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,11,"Page 11 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 teacher-generated feedback (M = 11.20, SD = 2.18, p = .008). However, there were no sig­ nificant differences between the quality of feedback provided by ChatGPT using a Zero- shot prompt and the teacher-generated feedback (p = .99). Initial draft and feedback quality (RQ2) The second question explored the extent to which the quality of feedback from vari­ ous sources correlated with the quality of the initial essays (see Table 3). The findings indicated a weak and statistically non-significant correlation between teacher feedback and initial essay quality (r = 0.21, p > .05), suggesting that the quality of teacher feedback is less dependent on the initial quality of essays. A moderate correlation was shown between GenAI feedback generated using Zero-shot prompt and initial essay quality (r = 0.53, p < .01), indicating a significant reliance on the quality of the initial essays for generating feedback. Similarly, a moderate correlation was observed between GenAI feedback generated using a CoT prompt and initial essay quality",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,11,"essay quality (r = 0.53, p < .01), indicating a significant reliance on the quality of the initial essays for generating feedback. Similarly, a moderate correlation was observed between GenAI feedback generated using a CoT prompt and initial essay quality (r = 0.42, p < .05), though this correlation was weaker compared to the Zero-shot prompt condition, suggesting somewhat less dependence on the initial draft quality. Feedback source and revision performance (RQ3) The descriptive data shown in Table 4 indicate that, regardless of the source of feedback, there was a notable improvement in the quality of argumentative essays from the initial to the revised drafts. This was further supported by a repeated measures ANOVA, which revealed a significant main effect of time, F(1, 67) = 255.82, p < .001, partial ηp 2 = .79, indi­ cating a large effect size. Descriptive statistics indicated that the mean quality of revised essays was highest in the teacher feedback condition (M = 10.66, SD = 2.31), followed by the Zero-shot prompt Table 2 Differences among feedback quality in teacher feedback, Zero-shot prompt, and",180
