DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,12,"Page 12 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 condition (M = 10.08, SD = 2.20), and lowest in the CoT prompt condition (M = 9.81, SD = 3.00). A one-way ANCOVA was conducted to examine whether the type of feed­ back significantly affected the quality of revised essays, after controlling for the quality of the initial drafts. After adjusting for initial draft quality, there was no significant effect of feedback condition on revised essay quality, F(2, 64) = 1.19, p = .30, ηp 2 = .03, suggesting that the type of feedback did not significantly influence revision outcomes. However, ini­ tial essay quality was a significant covariate, F(1, 64) = 34.25, p < .001, ηp 2 = .35, indicating a strong and significant relationship between students’ baseline writing ability and their revised performance. In addition, multiple regression analyses were conducted across all conditions to determine the extent to which feedback quality and initial draft quality in various condi­ tions predicted the quality of students’ revised drafts. In the teacher feedback condition,",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,12,"revised performance. In addition, multiple regression analyses were conducted across all conditions to determine the extent to which feedback quality and initial draft quality in various condi­ tions predicted the quality of students’ revised drafts. In the teacher feedback condition, the overall model was significant, F(2, 21) = 9.53, p = .001, and explained 47% of the vari­ ance in revised draft scores (R2 = .47). Both predictors contributed significantly: for every one‐point increase in feedback quality, revised draft quality increased by 0.57 points (B = 0.57, SE = 0.17, β = .53, t = 3.30, p = .003), and for every one‐point increase in initial draft quality, revised draft quality increased by 0.38 points (B = 0.38, SE = 0.18, β = .33, t = 2.07, p = .05). In the Zero-shot prompt condition, the regression model again reached significance, F(2, 21) = 9.61, p = .001, accounting for 48% of the variance (R2 = .48). Here, only initial draft quality emerged as a significant predictor: each one‐point increase in draft qual­ ity was associated with a 0.51‐point increase",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,12,"significance, F(2, 21) = 9.61, p = .001, accounting for 48% of the variance (R2 = .48). Here, only initial draft quality emerged as a significant predictor: each one‐point increase in draft qual­ ity was associated with a 0.51‐point increase in revised draft quality (B = 0.51, SE = 0.19, β = .49, t = 2.64, p = .01). The influence of feedback quality, although positive (B = 0.32, β = .29), did not reach significance (t = 1.56, p = .13). And finally, in the CoT prompt con­ dition, the model was significant, F(2, 19) = 8.80, p = .002, and explained 48% of variance (R2 = .48). Again, only initial draft quality significantly predicted revised draft quality: a one‐point increase in initial draft quality predicted a 0.96‐point increase in revised Table 4 Quality of initial and revised essays in various conditions Components of argumentative essays (scale: 0–2) Teacher feedback (n = 24) Zero-shot prompt (n = 24) CoT prompt (n = 22) M SD M SD M SD The introduction on the topic init 0.66 0.76 1 0.97",180
