DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,13,"Page 13 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 scores (B = 0.96, SE = 0.23, β = .75, t = 4.14, p = .001). Feedback quality showed a small, non‐significant negative coefficient (B = − 0.50, β = − .21, t = − 1.16, p = .26). Discussion Feedback quality (RQ1) The first research question examined the comparative quality of feedback produced by ChatGPT using GPT-4o language model under two prompting conditions, i.e., Zero- shot and CoT, and by an experienced teacher. The findings revealed that feedback gener­ ated using CoT prompting significantly surpassed both Zero-shot and teacher-generated feedback in overall quality. Under the CoT condition, the feedback received perfect scores on some components and outperformed teacher feedback across all components of argumentative essays. This finding supports growing evidence in the literature sug­ gesting that CoT prompting enhances the cognitive depth and sequencing of GenAI outputs in educational contexts (Wan & Chen, 2024). More importantly, it aligns with theoretical perspectives asserting that prompting strategies must be tailored to the cog­ nitive",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,13,"literature sug­ gesting that CoT prompting enhances the cognitive depth and sequencing of GenAI outputs in educational contexts (Wan & Chen, 2024). More importantly, it aligns with theoretical perspectives asserting that prompting strategies must be tailored to the cog­ nitive complexity of the task (Chen et al., 2024). Providing feedback on argumentative essay writing, which requires structured reasoning and layered critique, clearly fits the profile of complex assessment tasks for which CoT has shown clear advantages over Zero-shot approaches (e.g., Cohn et al., 2024; Lee et al., 2024). An additional finding was that feedback generated by ChatGPT under the Zero-shot condition was rated on par with that of human teachers. However, this result contrasts with prior studies in the same domain, which have consistently reported the superiority of teacher feedback over that of ChatGPT when providing feedback on argumentative essays (e.g., Jansen et al., 2024; Steiss et al., 2024). There are three plausible explanations for this discrepancy. First, earlier studies employed less advanced language models, pri­ marily GPT-3.5, which have repeatedly been shown to underperform in comparison to the more",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,13,"(e.g., Jansen et al., 2024; Steiss et al., 2024). There are three plausible explanations for this discrepancy. First, earlier studies employed less advanced language models, pri­ marily GPT-3.5, which have repeatedly been shown to underperform in comparison to the more recent GPT-4o model used in the current study (Dai et al., 2024; Jürgensmeier & Skiera, 2024). This suggests that, when paired with a more capable model, even mini­ mally prompted GenAI systems can generate feedback that meets the required standards (Meincke et al., 2025). This perspective is echoed by Jansen et al. (2024), who recom­ mend replicating similar studies using more powerful models such as GPT-4 to better understand potential improvements in feedback quality. Second, differences in prompt design may account for the variation in outcomes. Unlike previous studies that withheld explicit rubrics in their Zero-shot prompts, the present study included a detailed and relevant rubric for providing feedback, even in the Zero-shot condition. This design choice is significant, as it may have enabled the model to provide feedback that explicitly referred to criteria in rubrics or standards, which is",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,13,"included a detailed and relevant rubric for providing feedback, even in the Zero-shot condition. This design choice is significant, as it may have enabled the model to provide feedback that explicitly referred to criteria in rubrics or standards, which is a key characteristic of effective feedback (Nicol & Macfarlane‐Dick, 2006). The significance of including a rubric is further supported, albeit indirectly, by findings from Steiss et al. (2024), who reported that although human feedback generally outperformed GenAI feedback, GenAI exceeded human evaluators in the “criterion-based” category when the quality of feedback was coded. In line with this, Yu and Xie (2025) found that when GPT-3.5 was provided with an explicit rubric to guide feedback on students’ lan­ guage essays, covering aspects such as spelling, grammar, word choice, mechanics, and sentence structure, the feedback it generated was comparable to that of a teacher. This may be attributed to GenAI’s tendency to reference rubric elements explicitly, which is a",157
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,14,"Page 14 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 hallmark of effective feedback, even in cases where the rubric is not directly included in the prompt. Third, differences in how feedback quality was assessed may help explain this discrep­ ancy. In the present study, feedback was evaluated using an objective rubric grounded in Toulmin’s model, which emphasised the structural completeness of argumenta­ tive essays. This focus was reinforced through the prompt design, ensuring alignment between the feedback task and the evaluation criteria. In contrast, prior studies often employed more subjective evaluation frameworks. For example, Steiss et al. (2024) assessed feedback based on clarity of suggestions, accuracy, and tone, while Jansen et al. (2024) relied on user-centered measures such as perceived helpfulness and useful­ ness. These dimensions, by their nature, may have favored human-generated feedback, which tends to be more personalized and emotionally attuned. Nevertheless, as dem­ onstrated by Yu and Xie (2025), when the same objective rubric was applied to GenAI feedback using GPT-3.5, its performance was found to be comparable to",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,14,"human-generated feedback, which tends to be more personalized and emotionally attuned. Nevertheless, as dem­ onstrated by Yu and Xie (2025), when the same objective rubric was applied to GenAI feedback using GPT-3.5, its performance was found to be comparable to that of human teachers. In addition, the capabilities of the language model itself appear to influence the perceived quality of feedback. According to Escalante et al. (2023), even when feedback quality was assessed using subjective criteria, responses generated by GPT-4 were rated similarly to those of human evaluators, a finding that is consistent with the results of the present study. Taken together with the substantial quality gap observed between the Zero-shot and CoT conditions in the current study, these findings underscore that prompt engineer­ ing is not merely a technical adjustment but a fundamental pedagogical strategy (Wal­ ter, 2024). The clarity, structure, and scaffolding embedded in a prompt can significantly influence how GenAI functions as a feedback provider. Moreover, the contrast between the results of this study and those of earlier research in comparable contexts suggests that both the evaluation",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,14,"clarity, structure, and scaffolding embedded in a prompt can significantly influence how GenAI functions as a feedback provider. Moreover, the contrast between the results of this study and those of earlier research in comparable contexts suggests that both the evaluation criteria employed and the capabilities of the language model are critical in shaping perceptions of feedback quality. As more advanced models are intro­ duced, the gap between human- and GenAI feedback appears to be narrowing across both objective and subjective aspects of quality. Relationship between quality of initial draft and feedback (RQ2) The second research question examined the relationship between the quality of stu­ dents’ initial essay and the quality of feedback received across conditions. A key find­ ing was that in both GenAI conditions, Zero-shot and CoT, the quality of feedback was significantly correlated with the quality of the initial essays, with a stronger dependency observed in the CoT condition. This pattern suggests that GenAI feedback tends to scale with input quality: the more the initial draft adheres to the main components of an argu­ mentative essay, the more",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,14,"initial essays, with a stronger dependency observed in the CoT condition. This pattern suggests that GenAI feedback tends to scale with input quality: the more the initial draft adheres to the main components of an argu­ mentative essay, the more targeted and specific the feedback becomes. However, this finding stands in contrast to the results reported by Banihashem et al. (2024), who found no significant correlation between the quality of GenAI feedback and the quality of students’ initial argumentative essays. This divergence is likely attributable to several methodological differences between the two studies. In addition to variations in prompt design, Banihashem et al. (2024) analysed essays written in English, whereas the present study focused on essays composed in Persian, which is a language with dis­ tinct syntactic structures and discourse conventions. The linguistic features of Persian are known to pose unique challenges for natural language processing systems (Moniri",148
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,15,"Page 15 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 et al., 2024), which may influence GenAI’s ability to effectively distinguish among vary­ ing levels of input quality. For instance, such linguistic differences can affect essay length and content density, factors that have been shown to make language models more vul­ nerable to conflating verbosity with value (Hu et al., o2024). Furthermore, and perhaps more critically, Banihashem et al. (2024) employed two dis­ tinct evaluation frameworks: one for assessing the quality of students’ argumentative essays and another for evaluating the quality of the feedback provided. While the former aligned closely with the rubric used in the present study, focusing on the structural com­ ponents of argumentation, the latter incorporated different criteria, including subjective aspects such as emotional tone. This divergence in evaluation focus may have weakened the alignment between input quality and feedback quality, thereby obscuring any poten­ tial correlation. In contrast, the methodological coherence of the current study enabled a more direct and objective assessment of how effectively the feedback addressed spe­",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,15,"may have weakened the alignment between input quality and feedback quality, thereby obscuring any poten­ tial correlation. In contrast, the methodological coherence of the current study enabled a more direct and objective assessment of how effectively the feedback addressed spe­ cific argumentative features in the student texts, thereby enhancing the sensitivity of the analysis to differences in input quality. In addition to the previous finding, the current study found no correlation between initial essay quality and feedback quality in the teacher feedback condition. This aligns with earlier research showing that essay quality has minimal impact on how human (i.e., peer) feedback is provided (Gao et al., 2025; Huisman et al., 2018). This highlights a key limitation of GenAI in education: while GenAI tends to mirror the quality (or tex­ tual features) of the input it receives, human teachers engage in adaptive pedagogical reasoning, adjusting their feedback to support learners regardless of the initial draft’s quality. This adaptability likely stems from teachers’ ability to diagnose deeper learning needs and draw on contextual knowledge about the assessment, course, and students (Henderson et",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,15,"adaptive pedagogical reasoning, adjusting their feedback to support learners regardless of the initial draft’s quality. This adaptability likely stems from teachers’ ability to diagnose deeper learning needs and draw on contextual knowledge about the assessment, course, and students (Henderson et al., 2025). As a result, teacher feedback is less constrained by input qual­ ity and more attuned to the learner’s cognitive and affective development. This view is supported by the “matching principles” in peer feedback research (Gielen et al., 2010), which suggest that feedback is most effective when it matches the recipient’s ability to understand and apply it. From this perspective, teacher-generated feedback may not always be more detailed than GenAI’s, but it is often more pedagogically precise and strategically tailored to support the learner’s next developmental step. In short, the current study’s findings underscore a fundamental difference between GenAI and human feedback. While GenAI feedback is strongly influenced by the qual­ ity and, potentially, surface features of student input, teacher feedback remains peda­ gogically adaptive and responsive regardless of the initial essay’s quality. This sensitivity to input quality means",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,15,"and human feedback. While GenAI feedback is strongly influenced by the qual­ ity and, potentially, surface features of student input, teacher feedback remains peda­ gogically adaptive and responsive regardless of the initial essay’s quality. This sensitivity to input quality means that GenAI tends to provide more structured and specific rec­ ommendations on various components of argumentative writing to higher-performing students, or even to those whose essays are longer or information-dense, even if they do not conform to the criteria for a well-written argumentative essay. This asymme­ try risks reinforcing a “rich get richer, poor get poorer” dynamic, commonly referred to as the “Matthew effect” (Stanovich, 2009), in which stronger students continue to improve while their peers fall further behind. This concern is further supported by find­ ings from Wetzler et al. (2024), who reported that GenAI grading systems (GPT 3.5 and GPT 4o) were more lenient with weaker submissions and more stringent with stronger ones, a pattern of “proportional bias” that, while technically explainable, is pedagogically problematic.",167
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,16,"Page 16 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Feedback source and revision performance (RQ3) The third research question examined the impact of feedback source on students’ revi­ sion performance. Although students in all conditions significantly improved their revised essays, the feedback source, whether teacher, Zero-shot, or CoT condition, did not significantly affect final performance when initial draft quality was controlled. The strongest predictor of revision performance was students’ writing ability, as reflected in the quality of their initial essays, a measure commonly applied in feedback research (e.g., Guo et al., 2024; Patchan & Schunn, 2016). In addition, the findings from the regression analyses revealed a deeper distinction: in the teacher condition, feedback quality signifi­ cantly predicted revision performance, whereas in both GenAI conditions, it did not. The lack of a significant effect of feedback source on revision outcomes suggests that students were able to benefit from feedback regardless of whether it originated from a teacher or GenAI. This finding is particularly relevant in educational contexts where scalable and timely feedback solutions",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,16,"effect of feedback source on revision outcomes suggests that students were able to benefit from feedback regardless of whether it originated from a teacher or GenAI. This finding is particularly relevant in educational contexts where scalable and timely feedback solutions are increasingly adopted. However, the similar­ ity in revision outcomes across conditions should not be interpreted as evidence that all feedback functions equally. Instead, it emphasises the importance of how students engage with feedback during revision. This process, often referred to as feedback uptake, plays a central role in shaping revision outcomes (Wichmann et al., 2018). Uptake can take various forms, including accepting, elaborating on, modifying, or disregarding feed­ back, each reflecting a distinct way of integrating feedback into one’s work (Gao, 2025). These behaviors depend not only on the quality of the feedback but also on learner- related characteristics that influence how feedback is interpreted and acted upon (Aben et al., 2022). In light of this, the finding that students’ writing ability was the strongest predictor of revision performance further underscores the role of individual differences in how feedback",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,16,"that influence how feedback is interpreted and acted upon (Aben et al., 2022). In light of this, the finding that students’ writing ability was the strongest predictor of revision performance further underscores the role of individual differences in how feedback is processed and applied. Students with stronger writing skills may possess more developed strategies for identifying which feedback is most relevant, understand­ ing its implications, and translating it into effective revisions. Prior research has shown that writing proficiency supports the development of feedback literacy, which includes the capacity to interpret, evaluate, and apply feedback constructively (Zhou et al., 2024). Feedback-literate students are better positioned to recognise the usefulness of feedback and to engage with it in a productive manner (Carless & Boud, 2018). These findings highlight the importance of not only providing high-quality feedback but also support­ ing students’ ability to engage with it effectively, particularly for those with less devel­ oped writing skills. The final and perhaps most nuanced finding is that feedback quality significantly pre­ dicted revision outcomes in the teacher condition but not in the GenAI conditions.",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,16,"to engage with it effectively, particularly for those with less devel­ oped writing skills. The final and perhaps most nuanced finding is that feedback quality significantly pre­ dicted revision outcomes in the teacher condition but not in the GenAI conditions. This suggests that teacher feedback may have been more effectively aligned with stu­ dents’ needs, thereby supporting more meaningful uptake. Teachers often draw on their knowledge of students’ previous work and learning progress to deliver feedback that is targeted and pedagogically appropriate. Even when teacher feedback does not cover every component of an essay, as reflected by its lower rated quality compared to the GenAI conditions in this study, it is often adapted to address the most pressing issues for each individual. This form of personalized support likely enhanced students’ perception of the feedback’s usefulness, which in turn facilitated deeper engagement during revi­ sion (Kerman et al., 2023). As Hattie and Timperley (2007) emphasise, the true impact of",158
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,17,"Page 17 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 feedback lies not in what is said, but in how much of it students understand, value, and use to improve their work. In contrast, GenAI feedback did not significantly predict the quality of revised drafts. A likely explanation is that such feedback tends to follow a standardized structure, addressing all prompted elements uniformly without adapting to the specific context or needs of individual students. While this ensures coverage and consistency, it limits the ability of GenAI systems to prioritize feedback that is most pedagogically relevant. Sup­ porting this interpretation, van den Berg and Papadopoulos (2024) argue that teachers are uniquely positioned to personalize their feedback based on an understanding of stu­ dents’ learning histories and individual challenges. In contrast, GenAI tools, in their cur­ rent form, lack this contextual sensitivity, which may limit their capacity to support the kind of meaningful engagement that leads to improved revisions. In sum, the findings suggest that the effectiveness of feedback depends less on its source and",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,17,"cur­ rent form, lack this contextual sensitivity, which may limit their capacity to support the kind of meaningful engagement that leads to improved revisions. In sum, the findings suggest that the effectiveness of feedback depends less on its source and more on how well it aligns with learners’ needs, influencing students’ percep­ tion of its usefulness and their willingness to act on it. Teacher feedback appeared more attuned to individual differences, offering pedagogically sensitive guidance that likely supported more meaningful revisions. In contrast, GenAI feedback, though consistent and readily available, lacked the contextual understanding needed to tailor support and tended to follow structural cues from the prompt rather than addressing students’ spe­ cific challenges. Limitations and suggestions for future research This study offers important insights into the comparative effectiveness of teacher- ver­ sus GenAI feedback using Zero-shot and CoT prompting in the context of argumenta­ tive essay writing. However, several limitations remain, opening meaningful avenues for future research. First, the sample was limited to graduate students from one Iranian university, mostly female and from educational sciences. As Jayawardena et al.",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,17,"in the context of argumenta­ tive essay writing. However, several limitations remain, opening meaningful avenues for future research. First, the sample was limited to graduate students from one Iranian university, mostly female and from educational sciences. As Jayawardena et al. (2025) highlight, single-site, homogeneous samples reduce generalizability when comparing AI and human feedback. Similarly, Chan et al. (2024) warn that diverse education systems and cultures compli­ cate the transferability of AI-in-education findings. Future work should therefore test these feedback effects with larger, more diverse populations to examine their stability across disciplines, institutions, and student profiles. Second, the writing task in this study required students to produce and revise only a single essay draft in a short-term, controlled setting. This one-shot design limits eco­ logical validity, as academic writing typically evolves through multiple drafts, feedback cycles, and periods of reflection. As such, the study captures only a narrow, “single loop” view of how feedback contributes to learning, focused on immediate surface-level cor­ rection. In contrast, Carless’s (2019) concept of “feedback spirals” emphasises iterative, long-term engagement with feedback that supports deeper learning",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,17,"such, the study captures only a narrow, “single loop” view of how feedback contributes to learning, focused on immediate surface-level cor­ rection. In contrast, Carless’s (2019) concept of “feedback spirals” emphasises iterative, long-term engagement with feedback that supports deeper learning and self-regulation. Future research should explore how GenAI can be leveraged not only to deliver feedback but also to facilitate these ongoing, developmental processes. For example, GenAI could scaffold students’ work across multiple assignments by prompting reflection (e.g., Wei et al., 2025) and supporting self-assessment between tasks (e.g., Ali et al., 2023). Investigat­ ing which prompt engineering strategies best support such sustained engagement could",104
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,18,"Page 18 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 help reposition GenAI from a reactive evaluator to a formative, pedagogically grounded partner that contributes meaningfully to students’ longer-term writing development. Third, this study used a widely accepted rubric with high inter-rater reliability to assess feedback quality in the context of argumentative writing (e.g., Latifi et al., 2021; Valero Haro et al., 2024). However, the rubric focused exclusively on structural elements, such as the presence of an introduction, supporting evidence, counterarguments, and a conclusion. This limited scope may have overstated the quality of GenAI feedback, as responses that meet structural criteria may still lack the depth, clarity, or motivational tone typical of high-quality human feedback. Future research could replicate this study using alternative feedback quality rubrics that also account for subjective and affective dimensions, such as tone (Steiss et al., 2024), perceived usefulness (Jansen et al., 2024), and emotional content, including positive or negative sentiment (Banihashem et al., 2024), alongside structural indicators. This would help validate and extend the current findings and offer",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,18,"dimensions, such as tone (Steiss et al., 2024), perceived usefulness (Jansen et al., 2024), and emotional content, including positive or negative sentiment (Banihashem et al., 2024), alongside structural indicators. This would help validate and extend the current findings and offer a more nuanced understanding of the relative strengths and limita­ tions of GenAI and human feedback, both generally and in linguistically underrepre­ sented contexts in LLM training, such as Persian. Fourth, the study found that GenAI feedback was moderately sensitive to the qual­ ity of the initial essay. This was attributed in part to the task’s language, Persian, unlike prior studies using English essays, which showed no such significant correlation (Bani­ hashem et al., 2024). However, attributing this solely to language remains speculative. Future research should test this directly by varying the input language while controlling for other factors. Moreover, although previous studies have provided empirical evidence that GenAI systems are sensitive to surface-level textual features such as length, syntac­ tic complexity, and discourse structure (e.g., Hu et al., 2024; Viveros-Muñoz et al., 2025), this study did not examine such",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,18,"although previous studies have provided empirical evidence that GenAI systems are sensitive to surface-level textual features such as length, syntac­ tic complexity, and discourse structure (e.g., Hu et al., 2024; Viveros-Muñoz et al., 2025), this study did not examine such linguistic dimensions, as that was beyond its scope. Future work could analyse these features using established discourse frameworks, such as McNamara et al. (2010), to better understand how they influence GenAI feedback and its educational value, for example, by including them as covariates in predictive models (see Jansen et al., 2024). In addition, future research could explore prompting mecha­ nisms that explicitly compensate for lower-quality or shorter submissions to ensure that all students receive feedback that is equally informative and actionable, regardless of their starting point. Finally, although students improved their essays across all feedback conditions, no significant differences in revision performance were found between groups. This lack of between-group effects may mask important differences in how students engaged with feedback, as comparable outcomes may have resulted from different revision processes. To capture these nuances, future research should adopt more",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,18,"revision performance were found between groups. This lack of between-group effects may mask important differences in how students engaged with feedback, as comparable outcomes may have resulted from different revision processes. To capture these nuances, future research should adopt more fine-grained frameworks for analyzing feedback uptake, such as distinguishing whether feedback is accepted, elaborated, modified, or rejected (Gao, 2025). Such distinctions could clarify how differ­ ent feedback sources support or hinder learning and whether GenAI and human feed­ back promote equally meaningful revisions. In addition, examining students’ emotional responses to feedback, for example through facial expressions (Sahraie et al., 2024), may illuminate affective factors influencing feedback processing. Moreover, future studies could explore the role of external supports, such as computer-supported scaffolds (Far­ rokhnia et al., 2025b), in fostering deeper engagement with feedback and more effective integration of revisions into students’ writing (Zhang et al., 2025).",145
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,19,"Page 19 of 28 Farrokhnia et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:6 Conclusion This study examined how GenAI feedback, using Zero shot and CoT prompting strate­ gies, compares to teacher feedback in the context of argumentative writing in Persian, a linguistically underrepresented language in LLM training data. The findings demon­ strated that with well-designed prompts, particularly CoT, GenAI could produce feed­ back matching or even surpassing teacher-generated feedback in structural quality. This suggests promising potential for GenAI to support writing instruction across languages that are less represented in LLM development. At the same time, the study revealed that GenAI feedback, regardless of the prompt­ ing technique used, was significantly influenced by the quality of students’ initial drafts, unlike teacher feedback. Higher-quality essays tended to receive more detailed and criterion-aligned feedback, while weaker submissions were given less targeted support. Interestingly, this pattern contrasts with findings from studies conducted in English, suggesting that linguistic features such as Persian’s syntactic structure and discourse conventions may influence how GenAI interprets and responds to student writing. For example, differences in",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,19,"less targeted support. Interestingly, this pattern contrasts with findings from studies conducted in English, suggesting that linguistic features such as Persian’s syntactic structure and discourse conventions may influence how GenAI interprets and responds to student writing. For example, differences in essay organization and content density may cause language mod­ els to conflate verbosity with quality, particularly in underrepresented languages. These insights underscore the need to critically examine how GenAI systems perform across diverse linguistic and academic contexts. Taken together, these findings reinforce the need for a hybrid feedback model that combines the scalability and structural consistency of GenAI with the contextual aware­ ness and pedagogical sensitivity of human educators. GenAI can offer immediate, rubric-aligned feedback efficiently across assignments, while teachers can enrich and adapt this input based on deeper knowledge of students’ learning needs. Recent work by Banihashem et al. (2025) has proposed a framework for “hybrid intelligent feed­ back”, identifying five foundational principles: shared agency, complementarity, iterative refinement, dynamic adaptability, and enhanced personalization. Implementing such models will require teacher training in prompt engineering, clear protocols for human and AI",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,19,"(2025) has proposed a framework for “hybrid intelligent feed­ back”, identifying five foundational principles: shared agency, complementarity, iterative refinement, dynamic adaptability, and enhanced personalization. Implementing such models will require teacher training in prompt engineering, clear protocols for human and AI collaboration, and ethical safeguards to ensure responsible integration of GenAI into educational settings. Ultimately, the goal is not to replace teachers but to design systems in which GenAI enhances the feedback process, promoting reflection, uptake, and deeper learning, particularly in contexts where linguistic diversity or resource limi­ tations demand flexible and inclusive feedback solutions. Appendix 1: The prompts used in condition 2 and 3 Zero-shot prompt You are a professor of academic writing at the undergraduate level. Your students have submitted an argumentative essay as an assignment for this course on the role of artifi­ cial intelligence in education, which should be no more than 500 words in length. You now need to provide each student with feedback on the quality of their argumentative writing, identifying problems in their essays and suggesting solutions. Your feedback should be given in a",180
DOC_055,s41239-026-00579-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00579-9,19,"should be no more than 500 words in length. You now need to provide each student with feedback on the quality of their argumentative writing, identifying problems in their essays and suggesting solutions. Your feedback should be given in a paragraph containing 250–300 words and address the following questions: • Has the student provided an introduction relevant to the topic?",60
