DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,1,"RESEARCH Open Access International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y Supporting self-regulated learning through generative AI feedback in online higher education: the importance of student perceptions of the source of feedback Mutluhan Yilmaz1,2*, Hilmi Bahadir Temur3,4, Levent Emmungil2, Embiya Çelik5, Andrea Gauthier1 and Mutlu Cukurova1 *Correspondence: Mutluhan Yilmaz dtnvyil@ucl.ac.uk 1Institute of Education, University College London, London, UK 2OSTIM Technical University, Ankara, Turkey 3Giresun University, Giresun, Turkey 4Hacettepe University, Ankara, Turkey 5Atatürk University, Erzurum, Turkey © The Author(s) 2026, modified publication 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,1,"to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons. org/licenses/by/4.0/. Abstract Given the increased autonomy and limited direct instructor presence in online learning settings necessitating advanced self-regulated learning skills (SRLs), many online students face significant learning difficulties, such as low course completion rates. External support, particularly in the form of feedback, is crucial in addressing these difficulties. However, providing timely and personalised feedback at scale remains a key challenge. This study examines the effects of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) feedback on students' SRLs in an online higher education context. The study compares GenAI feedback, informed by student trace data and learning analytics, with tutor-generated",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,1,"scale remains a key challenge. This study examines the effects of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) feedback on students' SRLs in an online higher education context. The study compares GenAI feedback, informed by student trace data and learning analytics, with tutor-generated feedback, assessing both student perceptions of feedback and their SRLs development. Employing a mixed-methods research design, we collected survey data, trace-based SRL indicators, and open- ended responses from 46 university students. Quantitative findings revealed that students rated GenAI feedback more positively than tutor-generated feedback, with a statistically significant difference in Genuineness. Moreover, the treatment group showed significant improvement in the Task Strategies dimension of SRLs, suggesting that personalised GenAI feedback may enhance specific regulatory behaviours. Qualitative insights revealed varying student awareness of the feedback source, with some prioritising content over the source of feedback. In contrast, others indicated their attitudes might have differed had they known the provider, highlighting the socio-emotional aspects of feedback adoption and impact. This study concludes that GenAI has the potential to scale SRLs feedback in online learning environments, but the student perceptions of feedback should",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,1,"might have differed had they known the provider, highlighting the socio-emotional aspects of feedback adoption and impact. This study concludes that GenAI has the potential to scale SRLs feedback in online learning environments, but the student perceptions of feedback should be carefully managed to observe the expected impact. Keywords Self-regulated learning, Generative artificial intelligence, Learning analytics, Online higher education, Feedback perception",61
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,2,"Page 2 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Introduction Feedback is widely recognised as a critical component of effective learning and student achievement (Hattie, 2003; Hattie & Timperley, 2007). However, providing timely and personalised feedback remains a significant challenge, especially within large and diverse student populations typically found in online learning environments (Julia et al., 2021). Due to limited tutor resources and increasing student–teacher ratios, feedback often becomes generic and delayed, failing to adequately meet students' individualised learn­ ing needs (Dai et al., 2024; Meyer et al., 2024; Nazaretsky et al., 2024). Consequently, students often experience ""feedback gaps,"" in which the feedback provided is misaligned with their individual learning needs. This discrepancy can diminish motivation and hin­ der their capacity to engage with feedback in a meaningful and constructive manner (Nicolini & Cole, 2019). These gaps frequently lead to student dissatisfaction, as learners tend to value feedback that is personalised, timely, and contextually relevant (Blair et al., 2013; Holmes & Papageorgiou, 2009). This underscores the pressing need to investi­ gate innovative",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,2,"& Cole, 2019). These gaps frequently lead to student dissatisfaction, as learners tend to value feedback that is personalised, timely, and contextually relevant (Blair et al., 2013; Holmes & Papageorgiou, 2009). This underscores the pressing need to investi­ gate innovative feedback mechanisms capable of providing personalised and actionable insights at scale. Self-regulated learning skills (SRLs) play a critical role in enabling learners to effec­ tively manage their learning processes (Pintrich, 2000; Winne & Hadwin, 1998; Zimmer­ man, 2000). While the positive impact of feedback on SRLs is well-documented (Butler & Winne, 1995; Hattie & Timperley, 2007), students in online learning contexts often struggle with engagement and self-regulation due to inadequate or ineffective feed­ back (Kizilcec et al., 2017; Viberg et al., 2020). Learning Analytics (LA) has emerged as a promising avenue for addressing these challenges by providing data-driven insights for personalised learning support (Zimmerman, 2008). Automated LA-based systems, such as OnTask, have demonstrated potential in enhancing self-regulation of learning through personalised feedback interventions (Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). How­ ever, these systems typically utilise hard-to-scale human feedback",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,2,"personalised learning support (Zimmerman, 2008). Automated LA-based systems, such as OnTask, have demonstrated potential in enhancing self-regulation of learning through personalised feedback interventions (Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). How­ ever, these systems typically utilise hard-to-scale human feedback distribution or fixed, rule-based approaches to feedback generation, limiting their adaptability to students' evolving needs and face issues to be scaled (Garrison, 1995; Troussas et al., 2023). Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI), particularly through Large Language Models (LLMs), offer promising new opportunities for mitigating these limitations by providing dynamic, context-sensitive, and human-like feedback at scale (Bozkurt, 2023; Milana et al., 2024). Recent studies suggest GenAI-based exam­ ples can deliver feedback of comparable quality to tutors across various tasks, including data science projects (Dai et al., 2023, 2024), argumentative writing (Meyer et al., 2024; Weber et al., 2024), and in statistics and psychology courses in higher education con­ texts (Zhang et al., 2025). Despite these encouraging findings, more in-depth research is required to examine the impact of GenAI feedback on students’ perceptions, levels of engagement, and overall learning outcomes",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,2,"in statistics and psychology courses in higher education con­ texts (Zhang et al., 2025). Despite these encouraging findings, more in-depth research is required to examine the impact of GenAI feedback on students’ perceptions, levels of engagement, and overall learning outcomes within authentic educational contexts. Most studies investigate the impact on content acquisition and learning gains, yet there is limited research looking at the impact of GenAI feedback on higher-order thinking skills, such as students’ SRLs. In addition, understanding students’ perceptions is of par­ ticular significance, as these perceptions, as conceptualised by Nazaretsky et al. (2024), encompassing objectivity, usefulness, genuineness, and credibility of the provider, shape not only students’ engagement with feedback but also the extent to which they inter­ pret and act upon it, ultimately influencing their overall satisfaction with the learning",132
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,3,"Page 3 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 process (Mulliner & Tucker, 2017). By foregrounding both students' perceptions of feed­ back and the role of SRLs as a determinant of academic success, this study examines how these two dimensions are shaped in response to varying feedback modalities, including those generated by GenAI. In doing so, it offers novel insights into the learning experi­ ences associated with GenAI feedback interventions in higher education contexts, while also shedding light on how students' awareness of feedback source shapes their attitudes towards feedback. Specifically, this study aims to address the following research questions: RQ1: How do students perceive GenAI versus tutor-generated feedback regarding its objectivity, usefulness, genuineness, and the credibility of the feedback provider? RQ2: How do feedback interventions generated by tutors and GenAI influence stu­ dents' SRLs? RQ3: How does students' awareness of feedback source influence their attitudes towards feedback? Addressing these questions offers insights into the opportunities and challenges of using GenAI feedback in higher education settings. First, examining student perceptions uncovers critical",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,3,"stu­ dents' SRLs? RQ3: How does students' awareness of feedback source influence their attitudes towards feedback? Addressing these questions offers insights into the opportunities and challenges of using GenAI feedback in higher education settings. First, examining student perceptions uncovers critical determinants influencing their acceptance of and engagement with GenAI interventions. Second, evaluating the effects of different feedback regimes on the development of SRLs provides evidence that can contribute to the development of feedback strategies to better support students’ SRLs in online higher education environ­ ments. Third, analysing students’ qualitative reflections offers nuanced insights into how the perceived source of feedback shapes their interpretations and attitudes. Related work Shifting paradigms for educational feedback Feedback interventions are well-established as critical for promoting student achieve­ ment and success in educational settings (Butler & Winne, 1995; Hattie, 2003; Hattie & Timperley, 2007; Shute, 2008; Winne & Hadwin, 1998). Hattie and Timperley (2007) emphasise that effective feedback must explicitly address learners’ performance by answering three fundamental questions: ""What are the goals?"", ""What progress is being made toward these goals?"", and ""What actions are necessary to",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,3,"Winne & Hadwin, 1998). Hattie and Timperley (2007) emphasise that effective feedback must explicitly address learners’ performance by answering three fundamental questions: ""What are the goals?"", ""What progress is being made toward these goals?"", and ""What actions are necessary to enhance progress?"" (p. 86). In line with this conceptualisation, feedback serves primarily to reconcile the dis­ parity between actual performance and achievement targets, thereby distinguishing it from alternative instructional strategies. Thus, effective feedback necessitates the inclu­ sion of personalised attributes (Brinko, 1993; Shute, 2008), customised to address the specific proficiencies (Lipnevich et al., 2016) and weaknesses of individual students (Brinko, 1993). However, the provision of personalised, learner-specific, and effective feedback remains labour-intensive (Dai et al., 2024; Meyer et al., 2024; Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Wiley, 2006), necessitating a thorough consideration of each student's unique learning trajectory (Brinko, 1993). As a result, despite strong theoretical justifications, the prac­ tical application of effective feedback often faces significant limitations (Henderson et al., 2025; Lipnevich et al., 2016). This frequently leads learners to perceive feedback as inconsistent, impersonal, or insufficient (Carless et al., 2011;",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,3,"result, despite strong theoretical justifications, the prac­ tical application of effective feedback often faces significant limitations (Henderson et al., 2025; Lipnevich et al., 2016). This frequently leads learners to perceive feedback as inconsistent, impersonal, or insufficient (Carless et al., 2011; Perera et al., 2008), thereby contributing to widespread dissatisfaction and adversely affecting learning outcomes (Blair et al., 2013; Carless & Boud, 2018; Mulliner & Tucker, 2017).",66
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,4,"Page 4 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Although traditional feedback models seek to accommodate the individual attributes, goals, and trajectories of learners, their reliance on manual, labour-intensive processes poses significant challenges to scalability. This often results in generic and delayed feed­ back, failing to sufficiently meet students' individual learning needs (Dai et al., 2024; Meyer et al., 2024; Nazaretsky et al., 2024). These limitations have encouraged a shift toward more adaptive approaches that are capable of delivering personalised feedback at scale (Carless et al., 2011; Nicol & Macfarlane-Dick, 2006). This shift represents a depar­ ture from labour-intensive practices, advocating for more sustainable methods that are responsive to learners’ evolving needs (Nicol & Macfarlane-Dick, 2006; Sadler, 2010). Building on these evolving perspectives, emerging approaches increasingly integrate automated and data-driven solutions to enhance the efficiency and scalability of feed­ back practices. Recent scholarship has sought to exemplify the practical implications of this evolv­ ing feedback paradigm by examining adaptive, scalable feedback examples in authentic learning environments and investigating students’ perceptions of feedback",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,4,"enhance the efficiency and scalability of feed­ back practices. Recent scholarship has sought to exemplify the practical implications of this evolv­ ing feedback paradigm by examining adaptive, scalable feedback examples in authentic learning environments and investigating students’ perceptions of feedback provided by data-informed GenAI and tutor-generated traditional methods. For instance, Naza­ retsky et al. (2024) explored student perceptions of feedback generated by both tutors and GenAI based on task-centred responses, using a framework that includes four key dimensions: objectivity, usefulness, genuineness, and the credibility of the feedback pro­ vider. Objectivity relates to a fair, factual alignment between the content provided and the recipient's actual performance; usefulness measures how meaningful, informative, and pertinent the feedback is for improvement; genuineness indicates authenticity and personal significance; and credibility concerns whether the feedback provider is seen as trustworthy and well-intentioned. In their study, GenAI feedback was produced using GPT-4, informed by student responses and task descriptions. Conducted within authen­ tic university course settings, their findings revealed that under blind review condi­ tions, students rated GenAI feedback more favourably. However, once the identity of the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,4,"feedback was produced using GPT-4, informed by student responses and task descriptions. Conducted within authen­ tic university course settings, their findings revealed that under blind review condi­ tions, students rated GenAI feedback more favourably. However, once the identity of the feedback provider was disclosed, perceptions of GenAI feedback declined, while tutor feedback was rated more positively, highlighting both the potential and limitations of GenAI in educational feedback contexts. This pattern suggests that although learners may find AI-generated feedback applicable, they view its credibility as fragile. Moreover, it prompts a shift from prioritising perceived quality to critically examining the extent to which such practices lead to observable improvements. Specifically, the key question is whether students' perceptions of feedback meaningfully contribute to progress, espe­ cially in challenging areas like higher-order thinking skills, such as SRLs. Taken together, these considerations point out the importance of further empirical research into how learners perceive, interpret, and respond to GenAI feedback. Self-regulated learning in higher education Self-regulated learning (SRL) refers to the proactive management of cognitive, emo­ tional, and behavioural processes by students in pursuit of",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,4,"importance of further empirical research into how learners perceive, interpret, and respond to GenAI feedback. Self-regulated learning in higher education Self-regulated learning (SRL) refers to the proactive management of cognitive, emo­ tional, and behavioural processes by students in pursuit of their academic objectives (Zimmerman, 2000). Proficient self-regulated learners typically achieve greater academic success and develop essential skills for lifelong learning (Zimmerman, 2002). While students typically receive guidance from teachers in early education, higher education increasingly expects them to manage their learning processes independently. Despite this expectation, many students in higher education settings still struggle significantly",95
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,5,"Page 5 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 with self-regulation of learning (Bjork et al., 2013; Kizilcec et al., 2017). A significant contributing factor is the reluctance to seek help due to the fear of appearing incom­ petent (Zimmerman, 2002). This reluctance highlights the importance of external, non- intrusive support mechanisms that encourage learners without triggering such anxieties. Self-regulation challenges are particularly pronounced in online learning environ­ ments, where increased autonomy and reduced direct instructor presence necessitate advanced SRLs (Afacan Adanir & Gulbahar Guven, 2023). To better understand and support SRLs in these environments, Barnard et al. (2009) proposed a comprehensive six-dimensional framework. This framework includes goal setting (GS), task strategies (TS), environment structuring (ES), time management (TM), help-seeking (HS), and self-evaluation (SE). Each of these dimensions plays a crucial role in enabling students to manage their learning processes independently. Facilitating the development of these skills requires intentionally designed and adaptive support mechanisms, among which personalised feedback stands out as particularly effective (Hattie, 2003). Nevertheless, as highlighted in meta-reviews of the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,5,"in enabling students to manage their learning processes independently. Facilitating the development of these skills requires intentionally designed and adaptive support mechanisms, among which personalised feedback stands out as particularly effective (Hattie, 2003). Nevertheless, as highlighted in meta-reviews of the field (e.g. Viberg et al., 2020), the provision of timely, personalised feedback to effectively foster SRLs remains limited across many online edu­ cation environments. To confront these challenges, LA-based solutions show significant promise by leverag­ ing learners’ digital traces to generate personalised feedback. These digital performance traces can represent students' SRLs status through observable indicators, such as GS (for example, engagement with the syllabus or rubric), TS (for example, course logins or the number of item visits), and TM (for example, the average time spent on modules), among others (Ye & Pennisi, 2022). Empirical studies indicate that feedback informed by LA can enhance self-regulation by offering timely and actionable insights (Chen, 2024; Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). However, many current LA systems depend on raw interaction data (e.g., number of clicks or total logins), which often neglects",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,5,"LA can enhance self-regulation by offering timely and actionable insights (Chen, 2024; Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). However, many current LA systems depend on raw interaction data (e.g., number of clicks or total logins), which often neglects these nuanced observable indicators of SRLs. While such raw interaction trace data can be used to assess SRLs, this approach has limitations that hinder a comprehensive under­ standing of students' broader needs and skill-based SRL performance (Ye & Pennisi, 2022). For example, it may not capture the full range of skill-based indicators (e.g., GS, TS), restricting a comprehensive view of students’ learning needs, underscoring the need for trace-based frameworks that are explicitly aligned with SRLs constructs. Fur­ thermore, many existing studies rely on rule-based systems for automating personalised feedback delivery (Araka et al., 2020; Lim et al., 2021; Pardo et al., 2019; Tsai et al., 2021). These pre-set tools often consist of similar messages and lack the flexibility to adapt to learners' evolving needs in a timely manner (Garrison, 1995; Troussas et al., 2023). As a result, the feedback they",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,5,"al., 2019; Tsai et al., 2021). These pre-set tools often consist of similar messages and lack the flexibility to adapt to learners' evolving needs in a timely manner (Garrison, 1995; Troussas et al., 2023). As a result, the feedback they provide may fail to address students’ changing learning chal­ lenges, thereby limiting its relevance (Jansen et al., 2024). Thus, there is an imperative need to explore more responsive feedback systems that alleviate the rigidity of rule- based systems and better align with learners’ evolving needs, dynamically adjusting to their SRLs profiles as they engage with tasks. Addressing this need requires not only more adaptive feedback mechanisms but also a trace-based framework capable of cap­ turing comprehensive SRLs indicators from trace data, a gap this study aims to address through the framework proposed by Ye and Pennisi (2022).",137
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,6,"Page 6 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Exploring more responsive feedback practices Considering the findings above, exploring more responsive feedback approaches con­ stitutes a valuable contribution to the evolving paradigm of feedback provision. Com­ pared to current systems, which rely on predefined rules and static templates and often lack the flexibility to adapt to learners' evolving needs in a timely manner (Garrison, 1995; Troussas et al., 2023), GenAI models, particularly LLMs, offer a more dynamic and adaptable alternative. Over the past decades, researchers have developed an inter­ est in human-like computational models that go beyond deterministic systems (e.g., Arf, 1959). GenAI models exemplify this evolution, as they can produce flexible, context-sen­ sitive language outputs that approximate human communication and reasoning (Kos­ myna et al., 2025). In educational environments, natural language prompts allow users to guide these models in generating human-like, tailored outputs that support individual learning goals (Bozkurt, 2023; Milana et al., 2024). While the technical flexibility of GenAI offers clear advantages over rule-based sys­ tems, empirical research examining its",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,6,"prompts allow users to guide these models in generating human-like, tailored outputs that support individual learning goals (Bozkurt, 2023; Milana et al., 2024). While the technical flexibility of GenAI offers clear advantages over rule-based sys­ tems, empirical research examining its actual effectiveness in educational contexts is still in its early stages. Thus, there is a need to better understand the impact of GenAI-based interventions (Henderson et al., 2025; Meyer et al., 2024). Some recent studies have begun addressing this gap (e.g., Kumar et al., 2025; Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Steinbach et al., 2025; Zhang et al., 2025). Kumar et al. (2025), for example, showed that explana­ tory LLM-generated feedback produced greater learning gains than answer-only feed­ back in mathematics. Interestingly, learners were sometimes able to extract helpful strategies and benefit from explanations even when they contained errors, a pattern also observed by Steinbach et al. (2025) in their work on hallucinated feedback. These find­ ings suggest that learners’ characteristics may play an important role in how they inter­ pret and respond to feedback, even when its quality is imperfect. However,",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,6,"observed by Steinbach et al. (2025) in their work on hallucinated feedback. These find­ ings suggest that learners’ characteristics may play an important role in how they inter­ pret and respond to feedback, even when its quality is imperfect. However, this research also raises equity concerns, as not all learners may possess the proficiency to detect and adapt to misleading outputs without support. Since AI-generated outputs can be erroneous, it is crucial that AI-based feedback interventions are designed to strengthen learners’ critical capacities, such as self-regulation, reflection, and evaluation, to prevent learning from being undermined. Learners’ engagement with feedback depends not only on its accuracy but also on their capacity to self-regulate. Accordingly, responsive feed­ back practices should address both the correctness of the feedback and the ways it can foster learners’ regulatory skills, enabling them to benefit even from imperfect feedback. Beyond the challenges of feedback accuracy and learners' characteristics, another limi­ tation of current evidence is that most studies examine GenAI feedback in short-term, single-session contexts, overlooking longitudinal processes through which critical skills like SRL develop. Additionally, GenAI",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,6,"imperfect feedback. Beyond the challenges of feedback accuracy and learners' characteristics, another limi­ tation of current evidence is that most studies examine GenAI feedback in short-term, single-session contexts, overlooking longitudinal processes through which critical skills like SRL develop. Additionally, GenAI feedback practices are often evaluated against static feedback rather than authentic tutor-based outputs, which do not fully reflect real educational contexts. For example, Meyer et al. (2024) conducted an experimental study to explore the potential of GenAI in supporting essay writing through revision feedback. Students who received GenAI feedback demonstrated significantly greater improve­ ments in essay scores, motivation, perceived usefulness of feedback, and positive emo­ tions compared to those in the control group who received only static (i.e., standardised feedback messages) feedback. While these findings underscore the promise of GenAI for short-term revision outcomes, it remains unclear whether such feedback also fosters long-term benefits, longitudinal processes such as SRLs development. Moreover, rather",152
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,7,"Page 7 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 than comparing GenAI feedback with a static feedback context, assessing its real-world classroom applicability requires comparisons with tutor-generated feedback. Taken together, these studies offer insights for improving responsive feedback prac­ tices, which not only provide timely and adaptive guidance but also foster learners’ self- regulation. In response, the present study introduces and evaluates GenAI-generated feedback intervention, examining students’ perceptions of this feedback alongside mea­ surable improvements in their longitudinal SRLs performance. Specifically, the study compares the effectiveness of tutor-generated and GenAI feedback in an authentic higher-education context, providing insights into the practical value and real-world fea­ sibility of GenAI-supported feedback systems. Methodology Research context Ethical approval for this study was granted by the University College London (UCL) Institute of Education Ethics Committee. The study was embedded within a nine-week distance-learning core module entitled Basic Statistics, designed for students in higher education. The module aimed to enhance students’ comprehension of the role and appli­ cation of statistics in both academic inquiry and professional practice.",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,7,"study was embedded within a nine-week distance-learning core module entitled Basic Statistics, designed for students in higher education. The module aimed to enhance students’ comprehension of the role and appli­ cation of statistics in both academic inquiry and professional practice. Throughout the nine-week period, students engaged in a multifaceted learning experience that incorpo­ rated weekly slide presentations, moderated discussion forums, core and supplementary readings, as well as interactive workshops. Participants The research participants include 46 higher education students enrolled in Basic Sta­ tistics core module at a university during the second term of 2024–2025 academic year. Participants were randomly assigned to two equal groups: the GenAI feedback group (n = 23) and the tutor feedback group (n = 23). All students received detailed informa­ tion about the research via an information sheet and were asked to provide informed consent at the commencement of the course. Participation was entirely voluntary, and participants could withdraw at any time. Invitations to participate were disseminated via email and a MOODLE course announcement. Students were blinded to their group allo­ cation and the source of",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,7,"the commencement of the course. Participation was entirely voluntary, and participants could withdraw at any time. Invitations to participate were disseminated via email and a MOODLE course announcement. Students were blinded to their group allo­ cation and the source of feedback to avoid negative attitudes towards GenAI feedback. The information sheet informed participants that they would receive personalised writ­ ten feedback during the course, but did not disclose whether this would be generated by a tutor or by GenAI during the experimental phase. Data collection Data collection to measure feedback perception To investigate students’ perceptions of the feedback they received, a post-intervention questionnaire adapted from Nazaretsky et al. (2024) was administered. The question­ naire assessed various dimensions of feedback perception, including perceived objec­ tivity, usefulness, genuineness, and credibility of the feedback provider. Data collection took place during the final week of the course. All participants were invited to com­ plete the questionnaire via an online form distributed through MOODLE. Notably, all participants were still blind to the feedback source at the time they completed the",175
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,7,"of the course. All participants were invited to com­ plete the questionnaire via an online form distributed through MOODLE. Notably, all participants were still blind to the feedback source at the time they completed the",35
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,8,"Page 8 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 questionnaire. The perception instrument demonstrated high internal consistency in the present sample (Cronbach's α = .923), with a mean score of 3.15 (SD = 0.70) on a 0–4 scale. Data collection for SRLs Feedback studies grounded in LA have faced growing scrutiny regarding their validity. In response to these concerns, researchers have increasingly adopted evidence-centred frameworks (e.g., Bauer et al., 2025; Divjak et al., 2024) to facilitate more robust and reli­ able interpretations of learner data. This study, therefore, adopts the SRLs framework proposed by Ye and Pennisi (2022), as the course structure and resources from which the data were collected closely align with the indicators outlined in this framework. A detailed summary of the SRLs adopted in this study and their corresponding indicators is provided in Table A1 in Appendix 1. Their framework provides a structured basis for modelling trace data by linking observable indicators, derived from raw online interac­ tion data, with significant SRLs (e.g. • GS: engagement with the syllabus,",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,8,"indicators is provided in Table A1 in Appendix 1. Their framework provides a structured basis for modelling trace data by linking observable indicators, derived from raw online interac­ tion data, with significant SRLs (e.g. • GS: engagement with the syllabus, rubric, and other planning resources; • TS: course logins, number of item visits, and other learning navigation behaviours; • TM: average time spent on modules, use of supplementary materials, among others; • HS: frequency of questions and asking for an extension; • SE: revisiting completed modules, optional self-assessments, and related activities.) This framework integrates Pintrich's (2000) model of SRL, comprising forethought, plan­ ning, activation, monitoring, control, and reflection, with the Online Self-Regulated Learning Questionnaire (OSLQ; Barnard et al., 2009), as a foundational lens for data collection. Student activity within MOODLE was segmented into three distinct phases (please see Figure. 1), corresponding to key periods of the intervention. Data mining procedure was also employed to map raw log data into analytically usable SRLs. This multi-step process comprised: I. Importing raw trace data from the MOODLE system. II. Filtering out records generated",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,8,corresponding to key periods of the intervention. Data mining procedure was also employed to map raw log data into analytically usable SRLs. This multi-step process comprised: I. Importing raw trace data from the MOODLE system. II. Filtering out records generated by non-student users. Fig. 1 Scheduled feedback interventions,48
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,9,"Page 9 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 III. Processing date and time stamps to align with the study's timeframe. IV. Selecting events relevant to the SRLs framework as defined by Ye and Pennisi (2022). V. Calculating the SRLs-related events as SRLs scores (i.e. GS, TM, and more). VI. Standardising the calculated data across the full sample. We processed data using Python with the scikit-learn library to facilitate statistical anal­ ysis and applied z-score standardisation. Subsequent data analysis was conducted on this standardised dataset. The data mining procedure processed approximately 48,000 log records, which were filtered, mapped, and standardised to yield analytically func­ tional SRLs proxy scores for each participant. As a result, the standardised SRLs com­ posite scores demonstrated adequate internal consistency (pre: Cronbach’s α = .872; post: Cronbach’s α = .845), with observed ranges of − 2.08 to 1.74 (pre) and − 2.12 to 2.74 (post), and a mean of 0 (SD = 1). Open-ended questions To gain qualitative insights, we included two open-ended questions related to the feed­",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,9,"= .845), with observed ranges of − 2.08 to 1.74 (pre) and − 2.12 to 2.74 (post), and a mean of 0 (SD = 1). Open-ended questions To gain qualitative insights, we included two open-ended questions related to the feed­ back, which were administered only to the treatment group. The first question explored their awareness of the feedback source. The second question aimed to examine whether recognising the source, particularly if it was AI, led to any changes in their attitudes and whether those who could not identify the source would have reacted differently had they known it. Table 1 presents the open-ended questions. Notably, participants in the treatment group were informed regarding the source of feedback before the time they answered the questions. To investigate RQ1 (students’ perceptions of feedback) and RQ2 (the impact of feed­ back on SRLs), a quasi-experimental design was employed. For RQ1, the feedback perception questionnaire was administered in the final week of the study. For RQ2, to examine the impact of the feedback intervention on students’ SRLs engagement, we compared participants’ pre- (before the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,9,"a quasi-experimental design was employed. For RQ1, the feedback perception questionnaire was administered in the final week of the study. For RQ2, to examine the impact of the feedback intervention on students’ SRLs engagement, we compared participants’ pre- (before the first intervention) and post- (after the first intervention) standardised scores across SRLs dimension proxies described in Sec­ tion “Data collection for SRLs”. In addition, over the first two questions, rank-biserial correlation effect sizes were calculated (Tomczak & Tomczak, 2014). Although the origi­ nal SRLs framework includes indicators such as Optional Self-Assessments (SAssCom) and Bonus Quiz Completion (BQCom), these were not applicable in our course context. To address RQ3, we collected qualitative data in the final week through the questions were asked only of students in the treatment group, who had been informed just prior to this stage that their feedback was generated by a GenAI system. The analysis was carried out collaboratively by the first two authors, following qualitative data analysis proce­ dures outlined by Yildirim and Simsek (2013), which involved coding, theme develop­ ment, and interpretation of findings. Table",180
