DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,10,"Page 10 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Intervention design The intervention design was facilitated as summarised below in Figure. 1. In a quasi- experimental design, participants in the control group received tutor-generated feed­ back, while those in the experimental group received GenAI feedback. The first intervention was based on SRLs scores calculated from trace data collected up to the fifth week. The second intervention, conducted in the seventh week, updated these scores to reflect timely changes in SRLs proxies observed since the first interven­ tion. For example, if a learner’s HS score decreased from 1.7 to 1.3, the resulting change of –0.4 was incorporated into the second intervention. The interval between interven­ tions aligns with recommended feedback practices targeted at SRLs, as allowing time for reflection helps students better comprehend the feedback (Butler & Winne, 1995). During the intervention, each participant received two written feedback messages via email, delivered simultaneously to both groups and following a consistent structure (e.g., personalised salutation, feedback message, and closing remarks). Tutor-feedback intervention Studies on",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,10,"the feedback (Butler & Winne, 1995). During the intervention, each participant received two written feedback messages via email, delivered simultaneously to both groups and following a consistent structure (e.g., personalised salutation, feedback message, and closing remarks). Tutor-feedback intervention Studies on tutor-led feedback often highlight challenges related to the level of expertise required to draft effective and meaningful feedback (Bennett, 2011). This study devel­ oped a tool, called RefleXED, to support tutors in interpreting and utilising behavioural data. This tool simplifies data interpretation and reduces the expertise barrier commonly faced by instructors. It presents each student’s standardised scores derived from observ­ able indicators of SRLs behaviours (hereafter referred to as proxy scores), along with detailed observations on the five SRLs pillars adopted in the study. Proxy scores were calculated by aggregating raw interaction data into SRLs-relevant indicators for each pillar (GS, TS, TM, HS, SE) and then standardising them (z-scores) to enable compari­ son across students. For example, the number of logins and time spent on modules were combined to calculate a composite proxy score for TM, following the same procedure",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,10,"TS, TM, HS, SE) and then standardising them (z-scores) to enable compari­ son across students. For example, the number of logins and time spent on modules were combined to calculate a composite proxy score for TM, following the same procedure applied to the other SRLs. For each pillar, the interface displays the z-standardised score of SRLs proxies, along with the associated observable indicators and their respective calculation methods, enabling tutors to formulate tailored feedback messages aligned with individual student needs. Scores are centred around zero, with negative values meaning performance below the cohort average, corresponding to a need for negative feedback, as explained in the following section. Conversely, positive values, meaning above-average performance, correspond to a need for positive/reinforcement feedback. While the tool is designed to lower the expertise barrier, it still benefits from being used by the tutor with familiar­ ity with data interpretation. In this study, the tutor possessed a background in statistics, which supported the effective use of the system. Figure 2 illustrates the RefleXED inter­ face, and Figure 3 shows the feedback provision process by the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,10,"familiar­ ity with data interpretation. In this study, the tutor possessed a background in statistics, which supported the effective use of the system. Figure 2 illustrates the RefleXED inter­ face, and Figure 3 shows the feedback provision process by the two sources. GenAI-feedback intervention To generate GenAI feedback messages, we employed the ChatGPT-4 language model via the OpenAI API, guided by a custom-designed set of prompts implemented through a purpose-built tool developed by the authors. The model parameters were a temperature of 0.9 and a top-p value of 1.0 to foster generative flexibility, tailored to students’ varying performance profiles, and we kept these settings constant for all students. ChatGPT-4",109
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,11,"Page 11 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 was chosen as it was, at the time of the intervention, among the most widely adopted LLMs for text generation (Nojoud & Yuhao, 2024). Initially, the model provided detailed information regarding SRLs, including the indicators of skills adopted in the research with prompt engineering. Importantly, the structured prompts also fed the model with each learner’s proxy z-scores across the SRLs pillars, reflecting the data ground used in the tutor-generated feedback condition. This input helped the model tailor its feedback to each student's needs based on their performance. Before the deployment, we compre­ hensively tested the model's stability across a range of performance metrics. Finally, a Fig. 2 An Example of the Interface of RefleXED. *This figure shows an illustrative representation of RefleXED, sum­ marising its fundamental functionalities",144
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,12,"Page 12 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 structured prompt given below was developed to guide the model in producing person­ alised feedback, incorporating each student's proxy z-scores across the SRLs: [Based on the information provided, write a feedback message like a tutor for {stu­ dent_id}, considering proxy Z-scores for Goal-Setting {student_id_GS_score}, Task Strategy {student_id_TS_score}, Time Management {student_id_TM_score}, Help- Seeking {student_id_HS_score}, and Self-Evaluation {student_id_SE_score}. The feedback should include suggestions regarding the behavioural variables adopted in this study (as detailed in Table A1 in Appendix 1). Appreciate positive Z-scores and provide constructive criticism for negative Z-scores]. Each item class in the structured prompt (e.g., {student_id_GS_score} and others) is connected to the individual student’s z-scores from the database, which personalises the feedback. The placeholder {student_id} is static and represents the student's unique identifier. The GenAI feedback focused on the polarity of students' standardised scores, which was sensitive to the current SRLs proficiency of participants. It provided negative feed­ back, such as: 'You need to focus more on {related behaviours like average time spent",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,12,"The GenAI feedback focused on the polarity of students' standardised scores, which was sensitive to the current SRLs proficiency of participants. It provided negative feed­ back, such as: 'You need to focus more on {related behaviours like average time spent on each module}. Conversely, positive reinforcement, such as 'Great job! Keep up the good work,' was given if observed proxies were performing positively. Importantly, this adap­ tive functionality enables the system to modify both the tone and specificity of feedback based on SRLs proxy values. Skills, for example, with Z-scores around + 2 are highlighted as notable strengths, whereas those around 0 + receive positive but more cautious feed­ back messages. All GenAI feedback was monitored by the first author and reviewed for ethical consid­ erations prior to its delivery to students, given the potential ethical concerns associated with AI-generated content. No change to the GenAI-generated feedback was deemed necessary during the intervention period, thanks to the quality of the GenAI feedback being high without any observed hallucinations. Notably, students in the GenAI feed­ back group were not informed that",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,12,"content. No change to the GenAI-generated feedback was deemed necessary during the intervention period, thanks to the quality of the GenAI feedback being high without any observed hallucinations. Notably, students in the GenAI feed­ back group were not informed that their feedback was generated by an AI system during the intervention, which helped prevent any adverse attribution effects. Only after the experiment concluded, and before their open-ended answers were collected, students in the GenAI feedback condition were fully debriefed and informed about the source of feedback. The rest of the students were briefed after the project was completed due to ethical requirements. Fig. 3 Feedback provision process",107
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,13,"Page 13 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Results Feedback perception To evaluate students’ perceptions of feedback, a Mann–Whitney U test was conducted to compare perception scores between the two feedback provider groups statistically, as the assumption of normality was not met across the perception dimensions (Shapiro– Wilk test, p < .05). Figure 4 presents the mean scores by provider across the dimensions, while Table 2 summarises the mean scores and the Mann–Whitney U test results based on mean ranks. Although 46 participants took part in the study, only 41 fully completed the perception questionnaire. While GenAI-generated feedback received higher ratings across all perception dimensions, a statistically significant difference was observed only for the Genuineness dimension, favouring the GenAI group and yielding a moderate effect size (p = .036, r = .36). The impact on SRLs A Mann–Whitney U test was used to assess differences in students’ SRLs engagement scores between the two groups at pre- and post-intervention stages, since the normality assumption was not met across skills (Shapiro–Wilk test, p",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,13,".36). The impact on SRLs A Mann–Whitney U test was used to assess differences in students’ SRLs engagement scores between the two groups at pre- and post-intervention stages, since the normality assumption was not met across skills (Shapiro–Wilk test, p < .05). As shown in Table 3 Table 2 Mean scores and Mann–Whitney U test results for feedback perceptions across groups Perception Provider N Mean score Mean rank Sum of rank U Z p Effect size Objectivity GenAI (Blind) 20 3.37 22.90 458.0 172.0 − 1.013 .311 .18 Tutor 21 3.06 19.19 403.0 Usefulness GenAI (Blind) 20 3.24 22.40 448.0 182.0 − .740 .459 .13 Tutor 21 3.01 19.67 413.0 Genuineness GenAI (Blind) 20 3.40 24.85 497.0 133.0 − 2.098 .036 .36 Tutor 21 2.79 17.33 364.0 Credibility GenAI (Blind) 20 3.30 22.15 443.0 187.0 − .610 .542 .11 Tutor 21 3.06 19.90 418.0 Fig. 4 Mean perception scores of dimensions",151
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,14,"Page 14 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 which presents the mean standardised SRLs scores for both groups at pre- and post- intervention stages, along with the Mann–Whitney U test results based on mean ranks, no statistically significant differences were observed in pre-scores across any dimension, suggesting that the groups were comparable at baseline. However, a significant differ­ ence was found in post scores for the Task Strategies skill, favouring the treatment group and yielding a moderate effect size (p = .044, r = .35). The Time Management skill yielded a marginally significant difference with a moderate effect size (p = .057, r = .33), suggest­ ing a potential trend that warrants further investigation. No significant differences were observed for the remaining skills (GS, HS, SE). Thematic analysis of open-ended responses To address RQ3, a thematic analysis of the open-ended responses was conducted. While the treatment group consisted of 23 participants, only 17 students completed the open- ended questions. A categorical coding scheme was used to determine whether students were aware",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,14,"To address RQ3, a thematic analysis of the open-ended responses was conducted. While the treatment group consisted of 23 participants, only 17 students completed the open- ended questions. A categorical coding scheme was used to determine whether students were aware of the feedback source (""Yes"" or ""No""). Students who responded ""Yes"" were directed to a follow-up question that explored how their attitude changed upon recognising the source, while those who responded ""No"" were directed to a subsequent question asking whether their attitude would have changed had they known the source. A more detailed line-by-line coding was conducted for both the follow-up and subsequent questions to document students' attitudinal responses. During this process, student responses were labelled with four descriptive codes: No Change, Would Not Change if Known, Condi­ tional/Unsure, and Would Change if Known. These codes were then synthesised into four overarching themes: Awareness- Stable Perception, Unaware- but Content-Focused, Unaware- but Open to Change, and Unaware- Would Reconsider if Aware. To ensure analytical rigour and consistency, the first two authors independently applied the coding scheme to the entire set of",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,14,"four overarching themes: Awareness- Stable Perception, Unaware- but Content-Focused, Unaware- but Open to Change, and Unaware- Would Reconsider if Aware. To ensure analytical rigour and consistency, the first two authors independently applied the coding scheme to the entire set of qualitative responses, resulting in a Cohen's kappa of κ = .830, indicating almost perfect agreement (Viera & Garrett, 2005). Figure 5 illustrates the distribution of students' attitudinal responses by awareness of feedback source, while Table 4 presents the themes, associated codes, definitions, and representative quotes. Table 3 Mean (z) SRLs scores and Mann–Whitney U test results for pre- and post-intervention comparisons across groups Skills Stage Mean (z) score (GenAI, n = 23) Mean (z) score (Tutor, n = 23) Mean rank (GenAI, n = 23) Mean rank (Tutor, n = 23) U Z p Ef­ fect size (r) GS Pre -0.02 0.02 23.87 23.13 256.00 − .187 .852 .03 Post 0.01 -0.01 22.78 24.22 248.00 − .363 .717 .06 TS Pre 0.17 -0.17 25.52 21.48 218.00 − 1.022 .307 .17 Post 0.27 -0.27 27.48 19.52 173.00 − 2.010 .044 .35",180
