DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,15,"Page 15 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Among students who were aware of the feedback source, all (n = 5) stated that their attitude toward the feedback did not change, even though they knew it was GenAI-gen­ erated (No Change). Their responses consistently prioritised content reliability over pro­ vider identity, with several explicitly stating that the source was irrelevant as long as the feedback was accurate and useful. In contrast, a wider variety of responses was observed among students who were unaware of the source of the feedback. Five participants indi­ cated that their attitudes would not have changed even with prior knowledge (Would Not Change if Known). Their motivations varied; however, some prioritised alignment with their performance, emphasising the importance of feedback that accurately reflects their learning behaviour, while others expressed a broadly positive disposition toward GenAI, highlighting its potential benefits for the learning process. Six unaware students provided conditional or uncertain statements (Conditional/Unsure). Some expressed concern that knowing the source was AI might raise doubts about whether the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,15,"others expressed a broadly positive disposition toward GenAI, highlighting its potential benefits for the learning process. Six unaware students provided conditional or uncertain statements (Conditional/Unsure). Some expressed concern that knowing the source was AI might raise doubts about whether the feedback was truly personalised to their needs. Others indicated that source awareness would prompt them to scrutinise the feedback more critically, suggesting a change in how they accept feedback. Finally, only one student explicitly stated that the attitude towards the feedback would have changed had they known the source from the outset (Would Change if Known). No further elaboration was provided, leaving the direction of this anticipated change unclear. Table 4 Attitudinal themes by source awareness, codes, definitions, and representative quotes Source Awareness Attitudinal Theme Code Definition Representative quote Yes Awareness- Sta­ ble Perception No Change Students already knew or as­ sumed it was AI and reported no effect on their attitude “Did not change. If the con­ tent is reliable, the provider doesn’t matter.” (P13) No Unaware- but Content-Focused Would Not Change if Known Students didn’t know it was",180
