DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,16,"Page 16 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Discussion This study examined students’ perceptions of feedback and its impact on their SRLs in an online higher education setting, while also investigating how awareness of the feedback source influences students' attitudes towards feedback. Our hypotheses were grounded in the well-documented challenges that tutors face in delivering timely, personalised, and meaningful feedback (Henderson et al., 2025; Lipnevich et al., 2016; Wiley, 2006). Recent developments in LLMs offer promising opportunities to address these limita­ tions (Bozkurt, 2023; Milana et al., 2024). Of particular importance, this study does not claim that GenAI feedback is superior to that provided by experienced and well-sup­ ported human tutors. Rather, we aimed to explore the feasibility of using LA-informed GenAI as a potential solution to alleviate persistent challenges in traditional feedback practices. In educational contexts where the provision of high-quality, individualised feedback is constrained by tutor workload and limited resources, our approach aligns with Chan and Tsi’s (2024, p. 2) argument of “liberating teachers from time-consuming tasks and allowing",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,16,"traditional feedback practices. In educational contexts where the provision of high-quality, individualised feedback is constrained by tutor workload and limited resources, our approach aligns with Chan and Tsi’s (2024, p. 2) argument of “liberating teachers from time-consuming tasks and allowing them to concentrate on higher-level responsibilities.” In such settings, the proposed model may serve as a viable complementary support mechanism. RQ1-perceptions of feedback First, perception scores (RQ1) indicated that the treatment group evaluated GenAI feed­ back they received more highly than tutor-generated feedback. Although the small sam­ ple size prevents firm conclusions, the findings suggest that students respond positively to GenAI-generated feedback, which may help alleviate the longstanding dissatisfaction with traditional feedback practices (Blair et al., 2013; Mulliner & Tucker, 2017). Several factors may account for this outcome. Most notably, the GenAI-generated feedback in this study was constructed using personalised prompts that were sensitive to students’ performance, incorporating learners’ personalised LA-based data. This likely contrib­ uted to the contextual, timely relevance of the feedback, thereby enhancing its perceived quality. Interestingly, the Genuineness dimension showed a statistically significant differ­ ence in",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,16,"prompts that were sensitive to students’ performance, incorporating learners’ personalised LA-based data. This likely contrib­ uted to the contextual, timely relevance of the feedback, thereby enhancing its perceived quality. Interestingly, the Genuineness dimension showed a statistically significant differ­ ence in favour of the treatment group with a moderate effect. At first glance, this may appear contradictory. However, this finding aligns with previous work by Nazaretsky et al. (2024), who also observed that students often attribute greater genuineness to GenAI-generated feedback, especially under blind conditions. Our results also resonate with Henderson et al. (2025), who reported that GenAI feedback was perceived as less negative in tone and emotional impact, slightly more utility (usable), and more objec­ tive than tutor feedback, reflecting similar patterns in student perceptions in our study. The only divergence was in perceived credibility (referred to as 'reliability' in Henderson et al (2025)), which may be attributed to methodological differences: To the best of our knowledge, Nazaretsky et al. (2024) involved a diverse group of instructors across mul­ tiple courses in the collaborative design of tutor-generated feedback. In contrast,",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,16,"Henderson et al (2025)), which may be attributed to methodological differences: To the best of our knowledge, Nazaretsky et al. (2024) involved a diverse group of instructors across mul­ tiple courses in the collaborative design of tutor-generated feedback. In contrast, in our study, the tutor-generated feedback was produced by a single course instructor, without the benefit of team-based refinement. Additionally, unlike Henderson et al. (2025), who relied on students' prior experiences with using AI for educational feedback, we pro­ vided timely, tailored, and performance-specific feedback, which may have shaped cred­ ibility perception differently.",93
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,17,"Page 17 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 Returning to the Genuineness dimension, one plausible explanation lies in the design of the feedback prompts. Instructions such as “write like a tutor” combined with learner-performance sensitivity may have led the AI to adopt a tone that is consistently supportive, promotive, and empathetic, emphasising encouragement, clarity, and action­ able suggestions. This linguistic consistency may bring a sense of care and attention that students associate with genuineness. As a result, GenAI stability may have been inter­ preted by students as a sign of sincerity and authenticity. While these findings challenge common assumptions about the emotional superiority of human feedback, they point to important opportunities for designing feedback systems that simulate human-like com­ munication in beneficial ways. Regarding the remaining three perception dimensions (Objectivity, Usefulness, and Credibility), GenAI-generated feedback received higher mean scores; however, no sta­ tistically significant differences were observed between groups. While we acknowledge the statistical power limitations associated with our relatively small sample, this study was undertaken in an ecologically valid context",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,17,"and Credibility), GenAI-generated feedback received higher mean scores; however, no sta­ tistically significant differences were observed between groups. While we acknowledge the statistical power limitations associated with our relatively small sample, this study was undertaken in an ecologically valid context with real students. The relatively small cohort size in this study is indeed a limitation (n = 23 in the intervention group) but comparable to ecologically valid studies in the field (e.g., 17 students in the tutor-led group in Er et al., 2025). RQ2-tangible improvements in SRLs engagement With respect to improvements in SRLs (RQ2), students’ SRLs engagement showed a sta­ tistically significant improvement in the Task Strategies skill for the treatment group. Although this finding should be interpreted with caution, given the relatively small sample of the current study, it is consistent with previous research suggesting that LA- informed, automated feedback with different tools can positively influence self-regu­ lation of learning development (Chen, 2024; Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). It also resonates with recent studies that employed GenAI feedback and reported promis­ ing improvements (Kumar et",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,17,"automated feedback with different tools can positively influence self-regu­ lation of learning development (Chen, 2024; Lim et al., 2021; Tsai et al., 2021). It also resonates with recent studies that employed GenAI feedback and reported promis­ ing improvements (Kumar et al., 2025; Meyer et al., 2024; Steinbach et al., 2025). This improvement may reflect students’ relatively higher perceptions of the practical value of GenAI-generated feedback, as prior studies have estimated a clear link between feed­ back perception and engagement (Carless, 2006; Er et al., 2025). The observed moderate effect size may reflect both the statistical power limitations of the relatively small sample and the granular nature of the trace-based indicators used in this study. Unlike stud­ ies relying on raw interaction data, these indicators derived from raw interactions and explicitly mapped to specific SRLs proxies. For instance, Task Strategies were evaluated using four specific observable indicators (please see Table A1 in Appendix 1). While this enhances the interpretability of SRLs by providing separate measures for each skill, it may have limited the range of detectable changes, potentially accounting for the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,17,"evaluated using four specific observable indicators (please see Table A1 in Appendix 1). While this enhances the interpretability of SRLs by providing separate measures for each skill, it may have limited the range of detectable changes, potentially accounting for the moder­ ate effect sizes observed in our study. The remaining four SRLs (GS, TM, HS, and SE) did not show statistically significant between-group differences. However, TM approached significance with a moderate effect size, indicating a potentially meaningful trend that warrants examination in larger samples. Although the GenAI-supported design produced promising effects on the TS proxy, these findings suggest that the value of feedback lies not in technical sophistica­ tion alone, but in the extent to which a design enables students to act on the feedback they receive. In this regard, while GenAI feedback may inherently tend toward greater",138
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,18,"Page 18 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 elaboration at scale, the length of feedback alone does not guarantee its effectiveness. We align with Shute (2008), who argues that overly detailed feedback can sometimes obscure rather than clarify the intended message. The effectiveness of feedback is deter­ mined not by the quantity of information provided, but by how targeted, focused, and responsive it is, as emphasised by Black and Wiliam (2009) and Shute (2008). Accord­ ingly, GenAI should not be viewed as indisputably superior, but rather as a feasible and scalable complement to human feedback (Henderson et al., 2025; Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2025). In addition, one notable pattern appeared in the control group: specific SRLs proxies, such as TS and TM, showed a lowered trend over the intervention. This decline may be connected to students’ existing SRLs levels, aligning with Kizilcec et al. (2017), who present that students, particularly those with lower SRLs, are more prone to disengage­ ment or even withdrawal when support is inconsistent or",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,18,"intervention. This decline may be connected to students’ existing SRLs levels, aligning with Kizilcec et al. (2017), who present that students, particularly those with lower SRLs, are more prone to disengage­ ment or even withdrawal when support is inconsistent or insufficient over time. Fur­ thermore, as Steinbach et al. (2025) note, learners with initially low performance benefit most from targeted feedback and may struggle to stay engaged when feedback fails to meet their evolving needs. Taken together, these patterns suggest that students’ engage­ ment with feedback may be influenced by learner-related characteristics (Daumiller & Meyer, 2026), which shape how feedback is interpreted and enacted. RQ3-feedback source awareness Finally, feedback source awareness (RQ3) among students in the treatment group revealed variations. While some students were aware that the feedback was generated by GenAI, others did not recognise the source. This pattern aligns with findings from stud­ ies employing Turing test-like designs, which similarly observed variation in students' ability to identify whether feedback was AI- or tutor-generated (Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2025). Interestingly, students who were aware of the",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,18,"aligns with findings from stud­ ies employing Turing test-like designs, which similarly observed variation in students' ability to identify whether feedback was AI- or tutor-generated (Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2025). Interestingly, students who were aware of the source generally reported that their atti­ tude toward the feedback remained unchanged. This contrasts with prior studies, which found that awareness of the source, as a form of disclosure, usually led to less favour­ able ratings. This difference may be explained by our longitudinal, process-integrated design. Instead of merely assessing perceptions before and after disclosure, we gathered students’ views at the end of an extended learning process. During this time, students received feedback tailored to their changing needs. This ongoing approach is highly likely to have provided meaningful learning benefits over time and may have reinforced students’ trust in the feedback, regardless of its origin, as Meyer et al. (2024) argued. In this context, the perceived value of the feedback appears to have outweighed concerns about its source, resulting in a more stable and resilient acceptance than what is typically",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,18,"feedback, regardless of its origin, as Meyer et al. (2024) argued. In this context, the perceived value of the feedback appears to have outweighed concerns about its source, resulting in a more stable and resilient acceptance than what is typically observed in short-term or one-time feedback scenarios. This aligns with the statement of Ackerman-Barger et al. (2016), who emphasise that students value feedback mainly for its perceived benefit and relevance to their actual performance. As Carless (2006) notes, when this relevance is clear, students are more likely to focus on how the feedback can support their learning. In addition, many students who were unaware of the feedback source expressed sim­ ilar views, suggesting that the perceived value of the feedback content outweighed its",123
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,19,"Page 19 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 source and reflected a pragmatic, content-first orientation. Motivations varied; however, some prioritised alignment with performance goals, while others expressed a gener­ ally positive disposition toward AI, potentially reflecting their familiarity with AI-based applications and the benefits they derive from everyday use. However, a subset of stu­ dents noted that their approach might have differed had they known the feedback was generated by GenAI from the beginning, as similar studies (e.g., Nazaretsky et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2025) reported hesitation, more critical scrutiny and a subsequent decrease in perception. These contrasting views may reflect individual learner characteristics, such as academic proficiency, existing self-regulation level, and prior AI experience. As Carless et al. (2011) argue, capable learners are more likely to focus on the functional value of feedback, while those with lower achievement may be more sensitive to its delivery mode or source (Zimmerman, 2002). Notably, only one student explicitly stated that knowing the source from the outset would have altered their attitude towards",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,19,"functional value of feedback, while those with lower achievement may be more sensitive to its delivery mode or source (Zimmerman, 2002). Notably, only one student explicitly stated that knowing the source from the outset would have altered their attitude towards the feedback, suggesting that attitudinal sensitivity to source disclosure, while present, was not the prevailing response. Limitations and future research This section discusses the limitations of the present study, directions for future research, and broader implications for integrating GenAI into educational practice. Limitations The findings of this study should be interpreted in light of several limitations. First, the relatively small sample size (n = 46) from a single online course limits both statistical power and generalisability. Accordingly, the results should be interpreted as context-spe­ cific, and replication with larger and more diverse samples is needed. Second, the study primarily focused on perception and behavioural outcomes; accordingly, broader rela­ tional aspects of feedback quality, such as the extent to which feedback accurately and comprehensively addressed the targeted needs, were beyond its current scope. Third, the first author reviewed the GenAI-generated feedback",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,19,"on perception and behavioural outcomes; accordingly, broader rela­ tional aspects of feedback quality, such as the extent to which feedback accurately and comprehensively addressed the targeted needs, were beyond its current scope. Third, the first author reviewed the GenAI-generated feedback prior to delivery to ensure compli­ ance with ethical requirements and contextual appropriateness. Although the cognitive demands of reviewing text are not necessarily equivalent to those of composing feedback from scratch (Xavier et al., 2026), this reviewing process was not formally measured or accounted for as a workload factor in the present study. Fourth, the tutor's awareness of the GenAI feedback condition may have influenced their approach to feedback drafting in unquantifiable ways, leading them to engage in greater deliberation or inadvertently alter their usual practice. Future research Given the promising patterns observed here and the relatively small, single-course cohort on which they are based, future research should investigate whether these findings per­ sist across diverse educational settings and larger, more diverse student populations. Future studies should also compare GenAI and tutor feedback with a no-feedback con­ dition to provide",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,19,"on which they are based, future research should investigate whether these findings per­ sist across diverse educational settings and larger, more diverse student populations. Future studies should also compare GenAI and tutor feedback with a no-feedback con­ dition to provide more robust insights into the longitudinal impact of different feedback",50
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,20,"Page 20 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 regimes on learning progress. Given that the tutor in this study was aware of the GenAI feedback condition, future studies should consider withholding this information until all interventions are completed, to ensure that tutor-generated feedback remains consis­ tent with usual practice and is not influenced by awareness of the experimental condi­ tions. Future studies should also consider tracking the same students' SRLs performance in contexts without feedback, enabling a more robust assessment of whether observed changes persist and translate into sustainable skill development beyond the intervention period. In addition, future studies should examine both feedback perception and uptake, encompassing processes such as understanding, accepting, elaborating on, and critically engaging with feedback (Meyer et al., 2025), to develop a more nuanced understanding of how feedback interventions shape learning. This distinction matters because higher perception scores alone should not be interpreted as evidence that feedback functions effectively in all respects. To enable more rigorous comparisons of feedback quality across conditions and their association with observed",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,20,"feedback interventions shape learning. This distinction matters because higher perception scores alone should not be interpreted as evidence that feedback functions effectively in all respects. To enable more rigorous comparisons of feedback quality across conditions and their association with observed outcomes, future studies should consider content-based evaluation techniques, such as precision, recall, and F1-based analyses of how accurately feedback addresses targeted learning needs, as well as den­ sity-based metrics, such as the proportion of targeted needs addressed per unit of the text, and their association with learning outcomes. Finally, future studies should inves­ tigate whether GenAI-generated feedback is equally effective for students from diverse backgrounds and learning profiles, attending to questions of educational equity (Stein­ bach et al., 2025). How individual characteristics such as prior AI experience and cur­ rent self-regulation level interact with source awareness to shape feedback engagement remains an important question. Broader implications and critical considerations While our promising findings suggest potential, they do not mean that GenAI should be integrated into educational settings merely because of its practicality. As Cukurova (2025) argued, such decisions require careful",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,20,"remains an important question. Broader implications and critical considerations While our promising findings suggest potential, they do not mean that GenAI should be integrated into educational settings merely because of its practicality. As Cukurova (2025) argued, such decisions require careful planning and ongoing evaluation to ensure effective implementation. The mere availability of automation does not justify its ped­ agogical adoption. Furthermore, automation systems may stress a reliance on external factors that could inadvertently undermine learners’ ability to regulate their learning. Learning is fundamentally an intrinsic human capability; historically, we have flour­ ished by adapting and learning in complex and uncertain environments (Cukurova, 2025; Zimmerman, 2000). Without careful planning, GenAI integration risks encour­ aging superficial learning or metacognitive laziness (Fan et al., 2025), whereby students bypass the process of synthesising information from memory, potentially hindering their understanding and long-term benefits (Kumar et al., 2025). Similarly, providing targeted, accurate information to all students, regardless of their level, should be approached with caution. Research in AI in education has consistently underscored the concept of “pro­ ductive failure” (Kumar et al., 2025; Steinbach et",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,20,"et al., 2025). Similarly, providing targeted, accurate information to all students, regardless of their level, should be approached with caution. Research in AI in education has consistently underscored the concept of “pro­ ductive failure” (Kumar et al., 2025; Steinbach et al., 2025), allowing students to identify and correct misleading information, thereby self-regulating and reinforcing their histori­ cally intrinsic learning capability (Cukurova, 2025; Zimmerman, 2002). Accordingly, we",66
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,21,"Page 21 of 25 Yilmaz et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:16 argue that it is crucial to explore the potential benefits of GenAI-supported interven­ tions while ensuring that such technologies complement, rather than replace, learners’ intrinsic learning abilities. Conclusion This study examined the impact of GenAI-generated feedback on students’ perceptions and SRLs in online higher education. Findings indicate that, overall, students evaluated GenAI feedback more positively than tutor feedback, with the treatment group show­ ing significant improvement in TS skill and the Genuineness perception dimension. This suggests that our feedback provision model may enhance specific feedback perception and SRLs. Qualitative insights further revealed variation in students’ awareness of the feedback source and its perceived relevance. While many students prioritised the quality and alignment of feedback content regardless of its origin, others indicated that know­ ing the source would have influenced their perception, underscoring the role of feedback and AI literacy and individual learner characteristics. Rather than positioning GenAI and tutor feedback as opposing options, the results suggest that both can serve complementary functions, with",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,21,"ing the source would have influenced their perception, underscoring the role of feedback and AI literacy and individual learner characteristics. Rather than positioning GenAI and tutor feedback as opposing options, the results suggest that both can serve complementary functions, with neither of these feedback regimes being overwhelmingly superior. Alongside tutors’ guidance and pedagogical sense, GenAI feedback offers scalability and adaptability. Leveraging the strengths of each may help institutions and instructors design feedback ecosystems that better sup­ port learning. Despite the limitations inherent in any initial study, such as a smaller, non- representative sample, our findings provide authentic and valuable insights from actual educational environments. The patterns that emerged highlight promising directions for further exploration. Appendix 1. Theoretical Mapping of SRL Proxies This appendix represents a mapping of observable LA indicators to SRL constructs, adapted from Ye and Pennisi (2022). The selected proxies are grounded in Pintrich's phases of SRL and align with the constructs measured in the OSLQ. This mapping strat­ egy is consistent with the approach taken in Yilmaz et al. (2026), in which log-based indi­ cators were similarly",180
DOC_056,s41239-026-00592-y.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00592-y,21,"selected proxies are grounded in Pintrich's phases of SRL and align with the constructs measured in the OSLQ. This mapping strat­ egy is consistent with the approach taken in Yilmaz et al. (2026), in which log-based indi­ cators were similarly interpreted within the same data collection framework. The indicators presented in Table A1 should therefore be understood as theoretically informed proxies of underlying regulatory processes rather than direct measures of latent constructs. Engagement with planning resources reflects GS behaviours associated with the Forethought phase. Navigation patterns, such as course logins and item visits, indi­ cate TS use during the Monitoring phase. Time-related indicators capture TM and point to the Control phase. Finally, HS behaviours, operationalised through question-asking patterns, and SE behaviours, reflected in revisiting completed content and retaking quiz­ zes after grades are published, indicate the Reaction and Reflection phase. This mapping approach rests on the assumption that observable trace patterns reflect underlying regulatory activity, a position widely adopted in LA research to operationalise complex constructs such as SRL.",169
