DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,1,"International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6 Guarded adoption of generative AI in higher education: high-achieving students, successful- student identity, and epistemic agency in a single-university mixed-methods survey Jason Zagami1* *Correspondence: Jason Zagami j.zagami@griffith.edu.au 1Griffith University, Gold Coast, Australia © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,1,"material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Abstract Generative artificial intelligence is becoming part of university learning, but students do not engage with it uniformly. This mixed-methods survey of students at one Australian university examined whether successful-student identity and student-reported academic achievement were associated with different patterns of generative AI engagement. The institution-wide survey yielded 484 responses, including 469 with valid GPA-band data. Higher-achieving students reported lower active AI engagement, positive affect, perceived learning impact, and AI-related disengagement, alongside slightly higher negative affect. Item-level analyses showed less endorsement of AI-supported autonomy, effective learning, and active engagement, and greater agreement that reliance on AI hinders critical thinking and independent problem solving. Open-ended responses indicated that these students used AI selectively for clarification, summarisation, and workflow support while checking outputs and keeping them subordinate to their own judgment. The paper interprets this pattern as",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,1,"AI hinders critical thinking and independent problem solving. Open-ended responses indicated that these students used AI selectively for clarification, summarisation, and workflow support while checking outputs and keeping them subordinate to their own judgment. The paper interprets this pattern as guarded adoption: selective, bounded, and verification- intensive use in defence of epistemic control and successful-student identity. Keywords Generative AI, Higher education, Student-reported academic achievement, Successful-student identity, Epistemic agency, AI literacy, Assessment, Mixed methods Introduction There lies, in contemporary higher education, a space between two increasingly unstable stances. On one side is the familiar academic world in which success has long been tied to visible effort, disciplined reading, independent synthesis, and the capacity to produce work that can be recognised as one’s own. On the other is the emerging world of gen­ erative artificial intelligence, where explanation, summarisation, drafting, restructuring, and even the appearance of insight can be produced with startling speed and fluency by automated systems. It is in the space between these stances that a new educational tension has begun to take shape. Universities are no longer confronting only",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,1,even the appearance of insight can be produced with startling speed and fluency by automated systems. It is in the space between these stances that a new educational tension has begun to take shape. Universities are no longer confronting only whether,41
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,2,"Page 2 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 students will use artificial intelligence, but how its capabilities may reconfigure what it means to be, and to feel oneself to be, a successful student. The rapid rise of generative AI in higher education has often been narrated in terms of disruption, efficiency, and pedagogical possibility. Students can ask for clarification at any time, obtain feedback-like responses instantly, and generate prose, plans, summa­ ries, and examples with very little delay. Yet alongside this promise sits a more difficult question. If capabilities once taken as signs of academic competence can now be par­ tially delegated to a machine, what happens to those students whose sense of themselves as successful learners has been built through precisely those capacities? The issue, then, is not merely whether AI helps or hinders learning. It is whether AI unsettles the rela­ tionship between achievement, judgment, and identity in ways that matter educationally (Hall, 2024; Jensen et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024). The expansion of generative AI in higher education has",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,2,"or hinders learning. It is whether AI unsettles the rela­ tionship between achievement, judgment, and identity in ways that matter educationally (Hall, 2024; Jensen et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024). The expansion of generative AI in higher education has unfolded against an older field of artificial intelligence in education and educational technology research. Zawacki- Richter et al. (2019), in a major review of AI applications in higher education prior to the ChatGPT moment, found that most work clustered around profiling and predic­ tion, assessment and evaluation, adaptive systems and personalisation, and intelligent tutoring systems, with relatively limited educator-centred critical reflection. Webb et al. (2021) similarly argued that machine learning was already becoming increasingly conse­ quential for education through applications such as curriculum design, grade prediction, course recommendation, student modelling, and intelligent tutoring, while also stress­ ing that explainability and accountability needed to be built into educational machine learning systems from the outset. Crompton and Burke (2023) later showed that AI in higher education became markedly more visible in the period from 2016 to 2022, with growing interest in adaptive",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,2,"accountability needed to be built into educational machine learning systems from the outset. Crompton and Burke (2023) later showed that AI in higher education became markedly more visible in the period from 2016 to 2022, with growing interest in adaptive support, feedback, prediction, and administrative uses. These reviews matter for the present study because they show that generative AI did not arrive in a conceptual vacuum. Universities were already confronting questions about personalisation, automation, educational judgment, explainability, and accountability in AI-supported learning environments (Crompton & Burke, 2023; Dwivedi et al., 2023; Webb et al., 2021; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). What generative AI changed was not the existence of these concerns, but their visibility, accessibility, and immediacy for everyday student use. This shift matters because students do not enter this new terrain equally. They do not come to generative AI only as users of a tool, but as learners already shaped by histories of success and struggle, by habits of study, by confidence or uncertainty, and by differing senses of what legitimate academic work requires. For some students, AI may appear",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,2,"as users of a tool, but as learners already shaped by histories of success and struggle, by habits of study, by confidence or uncertainty, and by differing senses of what legitimate academic work requires. For some students, AI may appear primarily as support: a means of clarifying difficult material, reducing friction, overcom­ ing writer’s block, or making study more manageable. For others, however, the same capabilities may be more troubling. They may seem to erode the very processes through which understanding is formed, or to blur the distinction between assistance and substi­ tution. Most significantly for the present study, they may challenge an identity organised around being capable, independent, and honestly assessed. It is this latter possibility that provides the central problem for the paper. The study begins from the observation that student enthusiasm for generative AI is not evenly distributed. In this sample, students in higher student-reported GPA bands, hereafter referred to as high-achieving students, were less likely to describe themselves as active",164
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,3,"Page 3 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 and positively oriented users of AI and less likely to say that AI improved their learn­ ing. They were also more likely to agree that reliance on AI could weaken critical think­ ing and independent problem-solving. These findings do not suggest that high-achieving students reject AI outright, nor that they stand outside the transformations now under­ way. Rather, they suggest a more selective and bounded relation to AI, one in which use­ fulness is acknowledged but kept under conditions of scrutiny, verification, and control. The argument of this paper is that this pattern is best understood as guarded adop­ tion. By this is not meant simple scepticism, technophobia, or refusal. Guarded adoption refers instead to a form of selective engagement in which students use AI, but under conditions that seek to preserve epistemic control, intellectual ownership, and the legiti­ macy of their own learning. The concern is not only whether AI is accurate, but whether it remains subordinate to the learner’s judgment. It is not",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,3,"but under conditions that seek to preserve epistemic control, intellectual ownership, and the legiti­ macy of their own learning. The concern is not only whether AI is accurate, but whether it remains subordinate to the learner’s judgment. It is not only whether AI saves time, but whether that saving comes at the cost of displacing the struggle through which suc­ cessful students have often come to know themselves as successful. This study thus asks not simply how students use AI, but how students who have learned to see themselves as successful may respond when AI begins to perform tasks once taken as evidence of that very success. “Successful-student identity” is used here as an integrative analytical label for this study, not as an established or previously validated construct. The contribution is intentionally narrower and more disciplined than broader claims sometimes made in current AI discourse. The paper does not propose a general theory of student identity under artificial intelligence conditions, nor does it present stu­ dent-reported GPA bands as a transparent or exhaustive indicator of academic selfhood. Instead, it examines",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,3,"made in current AI discourse. The paper does not propose a general theory of student identity under artificial intelligence conditions, nor does it present stu­ dent-reported GPA bands as a transparent or exhaustive indicator of academic selfhood. Instead, it examines whether achievement-patterned differences in AI engagement are visible across one Australian university, whether those differences hold at the item level, whether the stage of study adds contextual variation, and whether students’ open-ended responses suggest tensions between AI capability and successful-student identity. The paper proceeds from the premise that these questions are worth asking because generative AI touches not only what students can do, but what they may no longer feel they need to do, and therefore what kinds of learners they may fear becoming. If univer­ sities are to understand student engagement with AI in ways that go beyond adoption counts, enthusiasm ratings, or integrity warnings, they need to attend more carefully to the meanings students attach to AI capabilities themselves. The study addresses five research questions: RQ1. How is student-reported GPA band associated with active AI engagement, AI- related",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,3,"counts, enthusiasm ratings, or integrity warnings, they need to attend more carefully to the meanings students attach to AI capabilities themselves. The study addresses five research questions: RQ1. How is student-reported GPA band associated with active AI engagement, AI- related disengagement, positive affect, negative affect, and perceived positive learning impact? RQ2. Do item-level analyses support the same pattern as the descriptive indices? RQ3. Does the stage of study, including undergraduate year level and postgraduate or research status, reveal additional variation worthy of interpretation? RQ4. How do high-achieving students describe the benefits, limits, and risks of AI-sup­ ported learning?",98
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,4,"Page 4 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 RQ5. How do high-achieving students construct and protect a self-identity as successful students, and in what ways do they describe generative AI capabilities as potentially chal­ lenging, unsettling, or reconfiguring that identity? Literature review The speed with which generative AI entered higher education has sometimes obscured the fact that the most important questions it raises are not purely technical. They are educational questions, and more specifically, questions about learning, judgment, authorship, trust, and the kinds of work universities still expect students to do for them­ selves. Early public discourse often cast generative AI either as a revolution in person­ alised learning or as an existential threat to academic integrity, but these binary framings quickly proved inadequate. Students did not simply divide into supporters and resisters, nor did educational value map neatly onto either enthusiasm or suspicion. Rather, what began to emerge was a more complex field of use in which convenience, productivity, curiosity, dependence, caution, and critique were all present at once (Chan & Hu,",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,4,"nor did educational value map neatly onto either enthusiasm or suspicion. Rather, what began to emerge was a more complex field of use in which convenience, productivity, curiosity, dependence, caution, and critique were all present at once (Chan & Hu, 2023; Jensen et al., 2025; Johnston et al., 2024; Yusuf et al., 2024). This mixed terrain is now visible across the higher education literature. Students commonly describe generative AI as helpful for brainstorming, summarising, drafting, restructuring written work, clarifying difficult ideas, and providing rapid conversational support when teachers or peers are unavailable (Chan & Hu, 2023; Johnston et al., 2024; Qian, 2025). Yet these same studies also report persistent concerns about hallucinated information, fabricated references, shallow learning, over-reliance, and the displacement of thinking by production (Chan & Hu, 2023; Xia et al., 2024; Yusuf et al., 2024). What is important in this body of work is not merely the coexistence of benefit and concern, but the fact that the educational meaning of AI remains unsettled. The same capabil­ ity can be experienced as liberating in one context and compromising in",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,4,"in this body of work is not merely the coexistence of benefit and concern, but the fact that the educational meaning of AI remains unsettled. The same capabil­ ity can be experienced as liberating in one context and compromising in another. A summary can function as scaffolding or as a substitution. A generated explanation can help a student move forward or allow them to bypass the productive struggle through which understanding is ordinarily formed. This instability is one reason generative AI has become such a consequential object of study in higher education. It does not simply add a new tool to existing practice. It disturbs familiar assumptions about what counts as study, what counts as assistance, and what counts as evidence of learning (Hall, 2024; Xia et al., 2024). The present study enters this conversation at a point where one broad finding is already well established: students are ambivalent. Generative AI is rarely encountered by students as either wholly enabling or wholly threatening. Rather, it is more commonly described as useful for clarification, structuring, and productivity, while at the same",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,4,"broad finding is already well established: students are ambivalent. Generative AI is rarely encountered by students as either wholly enabling or wholly threatening. Rather, it is more commonly described as useful for clarification, structuring, and productivity, while at the same time raising concerns about accuracy, dependence, shallow learning, and the weakening of independent academic judgment (Chan & Hu, 2023; Johnston et al., 2024; Qian, 2025; Saúde et al., 2024). That finding, however, is no longer sufficient in its presumed homo­ geneity. As the literature matures, the more interesting question is how that ambivalence is distributed. Which students describe AI as empowering, which students describe it as risky, and which students attempt to hold both positions at once? Existing literature has often treated students as a broad category or differentiated them mainly by discipline, prior exposure, or general confidence. There is still relatively limited work examining",145
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,5,"Page 5 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 whether students positioned differently within academic hierarchies, or at different stages of study, engage with AI in systematically different ways. To understand why that gap matters, it is useful to recall that generative AI did not emerge into a field that was previously untouched by artificial intelligence. Long before conversational systems became easily available to students, higher education researchers were already examining AI-supported profiling, prediction, assessment, tutoring, and personalisation. Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) found that the dominant clusters of work centred on profiling and prediction, intelligent tutoring systems, assessment and evalua­ tion, and adaptive systems and personalisation. Their review is important not only for its taxonomy, but also because it showed how strongly the field was oriented towards opti­ misation, support, and automation, often with comparatively limited critical attention to the educational implications of delegating judgment to AI systems. Webb et al. (2021) help clarify what changed with generative AI. The underlying edu­ cational issues were not entirely new. Universities were already confronting the implica­",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,5,"comparatively limited critical attention to the educational implications of delegating judgment to AI systems. Webb et al. (2021) help clarify what changed with generative AI. The underlying edu­ cational issues were not entirely new. Universities were already confronting the implica­ tions of systems that personalised, predicted, classified, and guided. What changed was the visibility and intimacy of AI. Generative systems made these questions immediate for everyday student use. The machine was no longer working mainly in the background of analytics, adaptive delivery, or institutional decision-making. It had become an acces­ sible interlocutor in the student’s study process itself (Crompton & Burke, 2023; Dwivedi et al., 2023; Webb et al., 2021). Most existing work on generative AI in higher education still concentrates on what the technology can do for students or what risks it poses to institutional practices. These are necessary questions, but they do not fully capture what is educationally at stake. The present study proceeds from a more specific concern: that generative AI may not only support or disrupt academic work, but also challenge how some students understand themselves",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,5,"questions, but they do not fully capture what is educationally at stake. The present study proceeds from a more specific concern: that generative AI may not only support or disrupt academic work, but also challenge how some students understand themselves as successful learners. This concern has been only partially visible in the literature to date. It is present, though often indirectly, in work on academic integrity, assessment, and authorship. Xia et al. (2024) argue that AI transforms assessment because it unsettles assumptions about what performances can be taken as evidence of student learning. When polished text, explanation, or even problem-solving pathways can be machine-generated, the link between output and underlying competence becomes less secure. This is not merely a technical problem for assessment design. It is also a problem for students, because suc­ cess in higher education has long depended on producing forms of work that can be socially recognised as one’s own and that signal effort, understanding, and judgment. Hall (2024) makes this point in more theoretical terms, arguing that generative AI should not be interpreted only through an",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,5,"on producing forms of work that can be socially recognised as one’s own and that signal effort, understanding, and judgment. Hall (2024) makes this point in more theoretical terms, arguing that generative AI should not be interpreted only through an efficiency lens. The deeper issue is what kinds of pedagogical and intellectual relations are being reconfigured when AI becomes a nor­ mal presence in learning. The present study extends these conversations by suggesting that one of the key changes may lie in how some students experience AI as a challenge not simply to assessment procedures, but to the basis on which they have come to think of themselves as successful students. Research on academic self-concept and motivation has long shown that students’ his­ tories of success shape how they approach tasks, evaluate support, and position them­ selves in relation to challenge. Guay et al. (2010) found that academic self-concept and autonomous academic motivation were closely connected to academic achievement,",159
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,6,"Page 6 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 suggesting that stronger students often carry not only higher performance records but also more consolidated beliefs about themselves as capable academic actors. If a student has been successful through persistent reading, repeated drafting, care­ ful interpretation, and the slow development of judgment, then a tool that can instantly summarise, generate, or rephrase may not be experienced only as helpful. It may also be experienced as potentially compromising, especially if it seems to collapse the dif­ ference between support and substitution. This is one reason the present study focuses on achievement as a differentiating variable. The question is not whether high-achieving students use AI less because they are conservative. The question is whether they apply stricter conditions to what counts, for them, as legitimate assistance. Markus and Nurius (1986) help make sense of this through the concept of possible selves, which captures how people orient action in relation to who they hope to become, expect to become, or fear becoming. A successful student identity is not",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,6,"and Nurius (1986) help make sense of this through the concept of possible selves, which captures how people orient action in relation to who they hope to become, expect to become, or fear becoming. A successful student identity is not just a record of prior achievement. It also projects a future self: the capable graduate, the scholar, the professional, the person whose effort and competence can be trusted. Oyserman’s (2009) work on identity-based motivation similarly suggests that people are more likely to sus­ tain behaviours that feel congruent with valued identities. From this perspective, AI use is not only a pragmatic decision. It may also be an identity decision. A closely related body of work concerns self-authorship. Baxter Magolda (2007) uses this concept to describe students’ development towards internally grounded ways of knowing and deciding. Although self-authorship has typically been discussed in broader developmental terms, it is especially resonant in the present context because genera­ tive AI introduces a new and unusually persuasive form of external assistance. Students are no longer dealing only with teacher expectations, textbook authority, or peer",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,6,"discussed in broader developmental terms, it is especially resonant in the present context because genera­ tive AI introduces a new and unusually persuasive form of external assistance. Students are no longer dealing only with teacher expectations, textbook authority, or peer influ­ ence. They are also dealing with a system that can produce plausible language, direction, structure, and argument on demand. The educational significance of this is not simply that students might outsource work. It is that the line between assistance and authorship becomes harder to hold. When a student says that AI helped them write, structure, or clarify, what remains theirs? The present study does not attempt to solve that problem philosophically, but it does suggest that some students are acutely aware of it. While the above concepts are helpful, epistemic agency provides the strongest primary frame for the paper. Nieminen and Ketonen (2024) define epistemic agency in ways that foreground students’ active relation to knowledge, assessment, and society. This mat­ ters because the key question raised by generative AI is not whether students possess information, but whether they remain",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,6,"Nieminen and Ketonen (2024) define epistemic agency in ways that foreground students’ active relation to knowledge, assessment, and society. This mat­ ters because the key question raised by generative AI is not whether students possess information, but whether they remain responsible agents in relation to information that increasingly arrives pre-processed, pre-phrased, and apparently ready for use. Epistemic agency helps shift attention from tool adoption to knowledge positioning. A student may use AI extensively and still exercise strong epistemic agency if they treat outputs as provisional, interrogate their warrants, verify their claims, and subordinate them to their own judgment. Conversely, a student may use AI relatively lightly but weakly if they accept outputs uncritically or allow them to displace thinking. This dis­ tinction is crucial. It means that low AI use is not automatically educationally valuable, and high AI use is not automatically suspect. What matters is the relation the learner establishes with the output.",154
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,7,"Page 7 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 Motivation-related concepts remain relevant to the study, but they do not organise the analysis. Ryan and Deci (2020) argue that autonomy and competence are central to high- quality motivation, and it is easy to see why generative AI might appear need-supportive. Yet the same technology may also appear autonomy-undermining or competence-erod­ ing if students feel they are no longer doing enough of the intellectual work themselves. This ambivalence can help explain why some survey items concerning autonomy, confi­ dence, and perceived effectiveness are important. Even so, the present paper does not attempt to test a full motivational model. The sur­ vey measures are not psychometrically strong enough for that, and the educational prob­ lem at stake is not reducible to motivation. Similarly, engagement-disaffection theory provides a useful vocabulary for distinguishing active, focused involvement from passive or disengaged use (Skinner et al., 2008, 2009), but it is not the central explanatory frame. The literature suggests that higher education research on generative AI is now at a",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,7,"a useful vocabulary for distinguishing active, focused involvement from passive or disengaged use (Skinner et al., 2008, 2009), but it is not the central explanatory frame. The literature suggests that higher education research on generative AI is now at a point where general statements about student ambivalence are no longer enough. We know students find AI both useful and risky. What requires closer attention now is the possibility that these tensions are stratified: distributed unevenly across students with different achievement histories, self-concepts, and study positions. This is where the present paper locates its contribution. It does not claim that student- reported GPA band offers a complete account of student identity, nor that high-achiev­ ing students form a homogeneous group. It does argue, however, that achievement may matter because success is not only a matter of outcome. It is also a matter of how stu­ dents come to understand themselves, how they learn to trust their own judgment, and how they interpret technologies that can now perform tasks once treated as evidence of their competence. Method The study employed a mixed-methods",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,7,"matter of how stu­ dents come to understand themselves, how they learn to trust their own judgment, and how they interpret technologies that can now perform tasks once treated as evidence of their competence. Method The study employed a mixed-methods survey design. The quantitative component examined the relationship between student-reported GPA band and a set of descriptive indices and individual items related to active AI engagement, disengaged AI use, affec­ tive response, and perceived learning impact. The qualitative component examined how students described the role of AI in their learning, including its benefits, its limits, and the ways in which it might support or unsettle their sense of themselves as successful learners. The study was conducted at a large Australian comprehensive public university in South East Queensland with more than 44,000 students enrolled across undergraduate, postgraduate coursework, higher degree by research, and non-award programmes span­ ning a broad range of academic disciplines. The survey was open to all currently enrolled students regardless of programme, discipline, campus, or stage of study. Recruitment occurred over a two-month period at the beginning of",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,7,"research, and non-award programmes span­ ning a broad range of academic disciplines. The survey was open to all currently enrolled students regardless of programme, discipline, campus, or stage of study. Recruitment occurred over a two-month period at the beginning of 2025 through the University’s central research participation channels, including a study advertisement published on the University’s website and promoted through the University’s digital student news­ letter. The advertisement invited students to participate voluntarily in an anonymous research study examining experiences of using generative artificial intelligence for learn­ ing. Interested students accessed the survey via an online link, with participation entirely voluntary and no incentives offered. Only respondents who provided informed consent",111
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,8,"Page 8 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 and completed the self-reported GPA item were included in analyses involving academic achievement. Large language models and a literature discovery and analytics platform were used in limited ways during manuscript preparation, including literature search support, critical review, proofreading, and summarisation of text; all analytic interpretation, argumenta­ tion, and responsibility for the final manuscript remained with the author. The survey yielded 484 respondents. Of these, 469 provided valid responses for the student-reported GPA item and were therefore included in analyses involving academic achievement. The sample represented a broad cross-section of the university population. Half of respondents identified as Generation Z (50.2%), followed by Millennials (27.9%), Generation X (17.1%), Baby Boomers (2.9%), and the Silent Generation (1.9%). The sample was predominantly female (67.8%), with 27.7% identifying as male, 3.5% as non- binary or a third gender, and 1.0% preferring not to disclose their gender. Participants were enrolled across all stages of university study, including first-year (27.1%), second- year (16.7%), third-year (7.6%), and fourth-year or above (12.0%) undergraduate",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,8,"male, 3.5% as non- binary or a third gender, and 1.0% preferring not to disclose their gender. Participants were enrolled across all stages of university study, including first-year (27.1%), second- year (16.7%), third-year (7.6%), and fourth-year or above (12.0%) undergraduate pro­ grammes, together with postgraduate coursework (29.5%) and higher degree by research (7.0%) programmes. For the exploratory cohort analyses, respondents were grouped into two broad study-stage categories: undergraduate (first year, second year, third year, and fourth year or above) and postgraduate/research (postgraduate coursework and higher degree by research). For readability, the manuscript refers to respondents with GPA bands of 5.5 or above as high-achieving students. The questionnaire (Appendix A) was developed specifically for this exploratory study through an iterative construct-informed design process drawing on contemporary lit­ erature concerning generative AI in higher education, student engagement, academic motivation, AI-related attitudes, and perceived learning impacts. Existing measures of academic self-efficacy, self-regulated learning, and emerging AI literacy informed con­ struct identification and item development (Owen & Froman, 1988; Pintrich et al., 1991; Wang et al., 2023). However, because no single instrument adequately captured",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,8,"perceived learning impacts. Existing measures of academic self-efficacy, self-regulated learning, and emerging AI literacy informed con­ struct identification and item development (Owen & Froman, 1988; Pintrich et al., 1991; Wang et al., 2023). However, because no single instrument adequately captured students’ educational use of generative AI at the time of study design, AI-specific behavioural and perception items were developed alongside construct-informed adaptations of estab­ lished educational measures. Draft items were iteratively reviewed by the researcher for content relevance, conceptual alignment, and clarity before being organised into five conceptual groupings: active AI engagement, AI-related disengagement, positive affect, negative affect, and perceived learning impact. These groupings were intended as descriptive summaries of related behaviours and attitudes rather than measures of latent psychological constructs and were therefore analysed as descriptive indices rather than validated psychometric scales. The survey contained demographic items, closed-response attitudinal items, and four open-ended questions. For descriptive purposes, the closed-response items were grouped into five areas: active AI engagement, AI-related disengagement, positive affect towards AI, negative affect towards AI, and perceived positive learning impact. The first four groups used a",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,8,"items, and four open-ended questions. For descriptive purposes, the closed-response items were grouped into five areas: active AI engagement, AI-related disengagement, positive affect towards AI, negative affect towards AI, and perceived positive learning impact. The first four groups used a four-point response format ranging from Not at all true to Very true. The learning-impact items used a five-point agreement scale ranging from Strongly dis­ agree to Strongly agree. Academic achievement was measured through student-reported GPA band. The valid categories were less than 3; 3.0 to 3.4; 3.5 to 3.9; 4.0 to 4.4; 4.5 to 4.9; 5.0 to 5.4; 5.5 to 5.9;",101
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,9,"Page 9 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 6.0 to 6.4; and more than 6.5. For the purposes of this study, “high-achieving students” refers to respondents reporting GPA bands of 5.5 or above on the seven-point institu­ tional GPA scale. The multi-item survey sets are not treated as validated psychometric scales; accord­ ingly, the indices should be interpreted only as descriptive summaries and not as evi­ dence of latent psychological constructs. In the sample, internal consistency was modest to weak: active AI engagement α = 0.57; AI-related disengagement α = 0.27; positive affect α = 0.57; negative affect α = 0.21; and perceived positive learning impact α = 0.52. The grouped items are therefore used only as descriptive indices, while the principal substantive claims are checked through item-level analyses. Because the questionnaire was designed to describe multiple dimensions of AI engage­ ment rather than to measure latent psychological constructs, exploratory factor analysis was not undertaken. The modest internal consistency observed for several indices fur­ ther supported this decision. Accordingly, substantive interpretation is based",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,9,"designed to describe multiple dimensions of AI engage­ ment rather than to measure latent psychological constructs, exploratory factor analysis was not undertaken. The modest internal consistency observed for several indices fur­ ther supported this decision. Accordingly, substantive interpretation is based primarily on individual item analyses, with the grouped indices used only as descriptive summa­ ries because item-level findings provide a more defensible basis for interpretation than composite scores when internal consistency is weak. Complete item-level Spearman correlations for all closed-response survey items, including bootstrap 95% confidence intervals, sample sizes, and p values, are provided in Supplementary Table S1. The four open-ended questions asked students how AI had changed their approach to learning, what they saw as the main benefits of AI tools such as ChatGPT, what chal­ lenges they had experienced, and whether AI helped or hindered their ability to achieve high academic performance, and why. Qualitative responses were analysed using Braun and Clarke’s (2006) reflexive thematic analysis. Analysis was conducted by the author and began with repeated reading of all responses to achieve familiarity with the data­ set. Initial",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,9,"achieve high academic performance, and why. Qualitative responses were analysed using Braun and Clarke’s (2006) reflexive thematic analysis. Analysis was conducted by the author and began with repeated reading of all responses to achieve familiarity with the data­ set. Initial inductive codes were then generated to capture recurring ideas and patterns relevant to students’ experiences of AI-supported learning. Coding was undertaken manually without the use of qualitative data analysis software. Codes were iteratively reviewed, compared, and refined throughout the analytic process before being organised into broader candidate themes. These themes were subsequently reviewed against the complete dataset to ensure they accurately represented participants’ accounts, refined to improve conceptual coherence and distinctiveness, and assigned descriptive names that reflected their central organising ideas. Representative quotations were selected to illustrate each theme while preserving participant anonymity. Consistent with reflexive thematic analysis, the themes are presented as interpretive constructions developed by the researcher rather than objective categories assumed to emerge directly from the data (Braun & Clarke, 2006). The qualitative findings are presented as interpretive explanations that complement, rather than validate, the quantitative analyses.",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,9,"are presented as interpretive constructions developed by the researcher rather than objective categories assumed to emerge directly from the data (Braun & Clarke, 2006). The qualitative findings are presented as interpretive explanations that complement, rather than validate, the quantitative analyses. Their purpose is to deepen understanding of how students described the educational value, limitations, and identity implications of generative AI use, rather than to establish causal explanations for the statistical relation­ ships observed. The quantitative analysis proceeded in four stages: descriptive indices for the five grouped areas, Spearman correlations between student-reported GPA band and each index, item-level Spearman correlations, and regression models as sensitivity checks controlling for age group, gender, and year category. In the regression models reported",118
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,10,"Page 10 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 in Table 3, student-reported GPA band was entered as a single ordinal predictor coded from 1 (less than 3) to 9 (more than 6.5). Quantitative analyses were conducted in R using the base stats package for Spearman correlations and regression models and the boot package for bootstrap confidence intervals. Percentile-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals were calculated using 10,000 resamples with a fixed random-number seed. Additional exploratory analyses examined undergraduate year-level associations and GPA-by-cohort interactions for selected outcomes. These stage-of-study findings are presented as exploratory contextual variation, not as evidence of a fixed developmental sequence. Results The results are presented in four parts. The first identifies the broad achievement-related pattern visible in the descriptive indices. The second tests whether that pattern remains visible at item level. The third examines whether stage of study adds any further varia­ tion. The fourth turns to the open-ended responses in order to clarify what lower enthu­ siasm for AI among high-achieving students actually meant in practice. Taken together, these analyses suggest",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,10,"third examines whether stage of study adds any further varia­ tion. The fourth turns to the open-ended responses in order to clarify what lower enthu­ siasm for AI among high-achieving students actually meant in practice. Taken together, these analyses suggest not a simple refusal of AI, but a more selective, bounded, and verification-intensive relation to it. At the level of the full sample, students reported moderate levels of engagement with generative AI and moderate positive orientation towards its use, alongside compara­ tively low levels of AI-related disengagement. This places the sample within the now familiar terrain of higher education AI use: students neither rejecting the technology outright nor embracing it without reservation, but engaging with it in mixed and prag­ matic ways (Chan & Hu, 2023; Johnston et al., 2024; Qian, 2025). What matters for the present study, however, is not the overall average so much as how that average shifts when student-reported GPA band is brought into view. A clear overall pattern emerged. As shown in Table 1, high-achieving students reported lower active AI engagement, lower positive affect towards",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,10,"not the overall average so much as how that average shifts when student-reported GPA band is brought into view. A clear overall pattern emerged. As shown in Table 1, high-achieving students reported lower active AI engagement, lower positive affect towards AI, and lower per­ ceived positive learning impact. High-achieving students also reported lower AI-related disengagement, alongside a small positive association with negative affect. This combi­ nation of findings is important because it rules out two simplistic interpretations. The first would be that high-achieving students are simply more positive and more intensive users of AI because they are academically stronger. The second would be that they are less engaged with AI because they are disengaged from technology altogether. Neither interpretation fits the pattern observed here. Although statistically significant, the observed relationships ranged from small to moderate magnitude. The strongest associations were observed for perceived learning impact (ρ = −0.395), positive affect (ρ = −0.359), and active AI engagement (ρ = −0.357), Table 1 Spearman correlations between student-reported GPA band and descriptive AI indices Variable Spearman’s ρ p Active AI engagement −",180
