DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,12,"Page 12 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 A second exploratory analysis examined whether the relationship between student- reported GPA band and the key AI variables differed between undergraduate and post­ graduate or research cohorts. As shown in Table 5, the central pattern was present in both groups, but it was stronger in the advanced-study cohort for active AI engagement and perceived positive learning impact. Exploratory interaction models suggested that the GPA slope was significantly stron­ ger in postgraduate or research students for active AI engagement (p = .001) and per­ ceived positive learning impact (p = .002). No significant interaction was observed for positive affect. The qualitative analysis suggests not blanket distrust, nor straightforward rejection, but a more complex effort to keep AI useful while preventing it from becoming epistem­ ically dominant. Four themes were especially prominent: conditional usefulness, verifi­ cation as part of the work, fear of cognitive substitution, and successful-student identity under pressure. High-achieving students often described AI as useful, but their positive accounts were typically qualified. AI could be",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,12,"Four themes were especially prominent: conditional usefulness, verifi­ cation as part of the work, fear of cognitive substitution, and successful-student identity under pressure. High-achieving students often described AI as useful, but their positive accounts were typically qualified. AI could be helpful for explanation, summarisation, preparation for literature reviews, question generation, organising thoughts, or reducing the friction in routine work. Yet this usefulness was repeatedly bounded. One student wrote, ‘I believe they can be used like a tutor. As long as you’re confirming accuracy. I can “talk out” a problem with AI and continue to ask it questions to clarify.’ Another explained, ‘The main reason I have used AI is mainly with collecting, summarising and breaking down the process of writing literature reviews. The synthesising of information is solely my own.’ These responses frame AI as useful under strict conditions: when it acts as a sup­ port for clarification, not as a stand-in for synthesis or judgment. A recurring pattern among high-achieving students was the insistence that AI outputs must be checked. One student wrote, ‘Information is usually incorrect in some",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,12,"it acts as a sup­ port for clarification, not as a stand-in for synthesis or judgment. A recurring pattern among high-achieving students was the insistence that AI outputs must be checked. One student wrote, ‘Information is usually incorrect in some way or another. This is why I tend to give AI my own information, and ask it to manipulate it.’ Another stated, ‘Mostly help, when AI does what I actually want it to, then AI is very helpful. It is only on some topics, usually the more specific the topic, the worse AI is, that require large amounts of time to fact check everything.’ These comments matter because they show that some students do not experience AI primarily as a reduction of effort. They experience it as a shift in the kind of effort required: from first-order genera­ tion to second-order verification, selection, and control. The most distinctive qualitative theme concerned the risk that AI could replace rather than support the work of learning. One respondent wrote, ‘I would never want to place all my learning into the tool as",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,12,"second-order verification, selection, and control. The most distinctive qualitative theme concerned the risk that AI could replace rather than support the work of learning. One respondent wrote, ‘I would never want to place all my learning into the tool as I gain nothing from it, no critical thinking skills or the feeling of success when you solve a problem on your own or a concept finally clicks.’ Another commented, ‘It helps in the sense that it reduces brainstorming time, but if I were to rely upon it like a crutch it would hinder me.’ A third stated, ‘AI hinders. If used for anything other than light help, students can become reliant on it and miss out on developing their skills.’ These responses align strongly with the item-level finding that Table 5 Cohort-specific correlations between student-reported GPA band and key AI variables Outcome Undergraduates ρ (p) Postgraduate/research ρ (p) Active AI engagement − 0.290 (< 0.001) − 0.457 (< 0.001) Positive affect towards AI − 0.383 (< 0.001) − 0.336 (< 0.001) Perceived positive learning impact − 0.318 (< 0.001) −",180
