DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,13,"Page 13 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 high-achieving students were more likely to agree that reliance on AI hinders critical thinking and independent problem solving. A final theme made the identity dimension of this pattern more visible. One respon­ dent wrote, ‘Nil. Rather rely on my own abilities even if it would be to the detriment of my grade. I’d rather have an honest assessment of what I’m capable of.’ Another stated, ‘I don’t use them at all; I do not trust their output to be accurate. It also feels as if it would interfere with my process of learning new information and translating it into my own words.’ A third raised a related concern: ‘You put a lot of work into an assignment, weeks of writing it, the final product is excellent and because of this you can be accused of using AI.’ What emerges here is concern about the relationship between effort, compe­ tence, and recognition. These responses suggest that, for some high-achieving students, AI capabilities may be experienced as",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,13,"excellent and because of this you can be accused of using AI.’ What emerges here is concern about the relationship between effort, compe­ tence, and recognition. These responses suggest that, for some high-achieving students, AI capabilities may be experienced as unsettling a successful-student identity organised around being capable, independent, and honestly assessed. Discussion The question driving this study was not whether students use generative AI. The more difficult question is how students position themselves in relation to AI capabilities that can now perform, simulate, or accelerate forms of work once closely associated with academic competence itself. In this sample, that positioning was not evenly distributed. High-achieving students were less likely to describe AI as central to active engagement, less likely to report positive affect towards it, and less likely to say that it improved their learning. At the same time, they were more likely to agree that reliance on AI hinders critical thinking and independent problem solving, and less likely to endorse disengaged or dependency-prone forms of AI use. The strongest interpretation of this pattern is not that high-achieving students",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,13,"they were more likely to agree that reliance on AI hinders critical thinking and independent problem solving, and less likely to endorse disengaged or dependency-prone forms of AI use. The strongest interpretation of this pattern is not that high-achieving students reject artificial intelligence. The qualitative evidence makes clear that many of these students do use AI and can see practical value in doing so. What distinguishes them in this sample is not the complete absence of use, but the conditions under which use remains accept­ able. Their responses suggest a more selective relation to AI: one in which clarification, summarisation, structuring, and preliminary support are acceptable, but only insofar as they remain subordinate to the student’s own judgment. The central issue is not whether AI is useful. It is whether it remains in its place. The paper proposes guarded adoption as one plausible interpretation of this pattern. Guarded adoption is not simple scepticism. It is not refusal, abstention, or anti-technol­ ogy sentiment. Nor is it merely low trust. It refers to a pattern of AI use characterised by lower general",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,13,"as one plausible interpretation of this pattern. Guarded adoption is not simple scepticism. It is not refusal, abstention, or anti-technol­ ogy sentiment. Nor is it merely low trust. It refers to a pattern of AI use characterised by lower general enthusiasm, more selective and bounded uptake, stronger insistence on verification, and greater concern that AI may displace important forms of thinking. The student who uses AI as a tutor-like conversational aid, but only while ‘confirming accu­ racy’, is engaged in guarded adoption. So too is the student who allows AI to assist with collecting and organising material, but insists that synthesis remains ‘solely my own’. The concept of epistemic agency provides the clearest way of understanding why this pattern matters. Nieminen and Ketonen (2024) argue that higher education should be attentive not merely to what students know, but to how they relate to knowledge, assess­ ment, and responsibility. Generative AI sharpens that concern because it offers outputs that are often rhetorically finished before they have been epistemically earned. It can",170
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,13,"they relate to knowledge, assess­ ment, and responsibility. Generative AI sharpens that concern because it offers outputs that are often rhetorically finished before they have been epistemically earned. It can",30
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,14,"Page 14 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 produce summaries, explanations, and plausible formulations that feel ready for use, even when they remain inaccurate, partial, or conceptually thin. The qualitative responses are consistent with an interpretation that some high-achiev­ ing students adopted a more agentic stance towards AI outputs. Their concern with checking, constraining, and reworking outputs suggests that they were attempting to keep themselves in the role of judge rather than recipient. In this sense, lower enthu­ siasm for AI does not necessarily imply lower openness to learning with technology. It may instead indicate a stronger insistence that the learner remain answerable for the work of discrimination, synthesis, and interpretation. The qualitative responses suggest one possible interpretation of these achievement- related associations. Some students were not only worried about incorrect information or reduced critical thinking, but also about what AI capability might do to the basis on which they understood themselves as successful learners. Success in higher education is not merely a record of high grades. It is also a way of",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,14,"reduced critical thinking, but also about what AI capability might do to the basis on which they understood themselves as successful learners. Success in higher education is not merely a record of high grades. It is also a way of being recognised and, often, a way of recognising oneself. When generative AI begins to perform tasks once tied to that sense of competence, the issue is not simply whether the student saves time. It is whether the relationship between effort, capability, and recognition remains stable. This is where supporting theories such as possible selves and identity-based motiva­ tion help clarify the educational stakes. AI use may feel threatening not only because it changes workflow, but because it can feel misaligned with a successful student self- organised around independence, intellectual ownership, and recognisable competence (Markus & Nurius, 1986; Oyserman, 2009). The relevance of self-authorship follows from the same tension. The present study cannot claim to observe self-authorship in a formal developmental sense from brief sur­ vey comments. Yet the comments do suggest that some students are concerned with whether their work",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,14,"relevance of self-authorship follows from the same tension. The present study cannot claim to observe self-authorship in a formal developmental sense from brief sur­ vey comments. Yet the comments do suggest that some students are concerned with whether their work still feels like theirs in a meaningful educational sense. AI introduces a new kind of external assistance, one that is capable not just of prompting or reminding, but of producing academically plausible language and structure. In doing so, it raises a question that students themselves are already beginning to articulate: when assistance becomes sufficiently powerful, how does one decide what remains one’s own work? The exploratory stage-of-study findings complicate the picture further. The achieve­ ment-related pattern was present across both undergraduate and postgraduate/research cohorts, but it appeared stronger in the advanced-study group for active AI engagement and perceived positive learning impact. At the same time, later undergraduate year level was positively associated with AI-related disengagement. These two results do not form a tidy developmental sequence. They do, however, suggest that the stage of study mat­ ters, though perhaps in different",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,14,"impact. At the same time, later undergraduate year level was positively associated with AI-related disengagement. These two results do not form a tidy developmental sequence. They do, however, suggest that the stage of study mat­ ters, though perhaps in different ways for different parts of the student population. It is important to state clearly what this paper is not claiming. It is not claiming that high-achieving students are morally superior, more authentic, or more educationally serious than their peers. It is not claiming that lower-achieving students use AI care­ lessly or without reflection. It is not claiming that all AI use undermines thinking, nor that non-use is inherently virtuous. The observed relationships are probabilistic and aggregate, not categorical. The argument is more restrained. In this sample, student ambivalence about generative AI was stratified. One visible form of that stratification involved high-achieving students",142
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,15,"Page 15 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 reporting less positive and less benefit-centred relations to AI, alongside comments that suggested more bounded, verification-intensive, and identity-protective use. Universities should first avoid assuming that lower enthusiasm for AI reflects a deficit in capability or openness. For some students, especially high-achieving ones, scepticism may represent a thoughtful judgment about the boundaries of legitimate support. Second, AI literacy should be taught as epistemic calibration, not just operational flu­ ency. Students need support not only in prompting systems effectively, but in deciding when outputs require verification, how to maintain control over source quality, and how to distinguish support for understanding from substitution for thinking. Third, assessment design should preserve visible spaces for human reasoning, judg­ ment, and ownership. If students are worried that AI may undermine critical thinking or make excellent work appear suspect, then assessment cannot rely solely on polished final products. Finally, research on generative AI in higher education now needs more differentiated accounts of how AI enters educational life differently for different students, under",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,15,"thinking or make excellent work appear suspect, then assessment cannot rely solely on polished final products. Finally, research on generative AI in higher education now needs more differentiated accounts of how AI enters educational life differently for different students, under differ­ ent conditions, and with different meanings attached to its use. Several limitations set the bounds of the present study. First, academic achievement was measured through student-reported GPA bands rather than official institutional records. Second, the data are cross-sectional, so no causal claims can be made. Third, participation was voluntary, creating potential self-selection bias: students with particu­ larly strong views or experiences relating to generative AI may be over-represented. A meaningful response rate could not be calculated because the open, institution-wide recruitment channels did not provide a defined invitation denominator. Fourth, the grouped survey items had only modest to weak internal consistency, which is why the manuscript relies heavily on item-level robustness checks. Fifth, the qualitative data consist of short written survey responses rather than inter­ views, reflective narratives, or longitudinal accounts. Sixth, the stage-of-study analyses are exploratory. Finally, the",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,15,"internal consistency, which is why the manuscript relies heavily on item-level robustness checks. Fifth, the qualitative data consist of short written survey responses rather than inter­ views, reflective narratives, or longitudinal accounts. Sixth, the stage-of-study analyses are exploratory. Finally, the survey was institution-wide but limited to one Australian university. The findings may travel conceptually, but they should not be assumed to gen­ eralise straightforwardly across all universities, disciplines, or policy settings. The findings should be read as a 2025 snapshot of generative AI adoption in higher education, capturing student responses at a particular moment in the early normalisa­ tion of these tools, and future research should examine how such patterns of engage­ ment, scepticism, and identity negotiation may shift over time as AI capabilities, institutional policies, and student practices continue to evolve. Conclusion Generative AI has often been discussed in higher education as though the central issue were whether students will adopt it. The more important question is what students are willing to let AI do, what they insist on retaining for themselves, and how those deci­ sions are shaped",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,15,"higher education as though the central issue were whether students will adopt it. The more important question is what students are willing to let AI do, what they insist on retaining for themselves, and how those deci­ sions are shaped by their histories of achievement, their sense of academic legitimacy, and their understanding of what successful learning still requires. This study shows that, in one university-wide sample, student ambivalence about gen­ erative AI was not uniform. High-achieving students were less likely to describe AI as central to active engagement, positive academic feeling, or improved learning. They were also more likely to agree that relying on AI can hinder critical thinking and independent",112
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,16,"Page 16 of 20 Zagami International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:47 problem solving. Their open-ended responses repeatedly suggested more bounded, verification-intensive, and agency-protective forms of use. For some of these students, qualitative responses suggested that AI capabilities may be experienced as identity- challenging. They raised the possibility that success might no longer feel like an honest reflection of one’s own capacities if too much of the work had been shifted elsewhere. The paper interprets this pattern as guarded adoption. Guarded adoption is not refusal. It is a form of selective engagement in which AI is used, but kept within limits that preserve epistemic control, intellectual ownership, and the legitimacy of one’s own learning. That finding matters because it reframes lower AI enthusiasm. In at least some cases, it is not a sign of lagging behind innovation. It is an educational judgment about where responsibility for thinking should remain. Appendix A Survey of Student Engagement with Generative Artificial Intelligence in Higher Education Purpose This survey examined university students’ experiences of using generative artificial intel­ ligence (AI) tools",180
DOC_057,s41239-026-00625-6.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00625-6,16,"It is an educational judgment about where responsibility for thinking should remain. Appendix A Survey of Student Engagement with Generative Artificial Intelligence in Higher Education Purpose This survey examined university students’ experiences of using generative artificial intel­ ligence (AI) tools such as ChatGPT to support learning. It investigated patterns of AI engagement, attitudes towards AI, perceived impacts on learning, and relationships with self-reported academic achievement. Participation was voluntary and anonymous. Individual responses were not identifiable. Section A. Demographic Information A1. Age Group Please indicate your age. • Generation Z (12–27) • Millennial (28–43) • Generation X (44–59) • Baby Boomer (60–78) • Silent Generation (79–96) A2. Gender Please indicate your gender. • Female • Male • Non-binary/third gender • Prefer not to say A3. Current Level of Study Which best describes your current enrolment? • First Year • Second Year • Third Year • Fourth Year+ • Postgraduate Studies",149
