DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,1,"International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9 REVIEW Open Access Mapping generative AI in higher education teaching and learning: a scoping review Michael Pin-Chuan Lin1,2* , Daniel H. Chang3,2 , Philip H. Winne2 , G. Michael Bowen1 and Jeeho Ryoo4 *Correspondence: Michael Pin-Chuan Lin michael.lin@msvu.ca 1Faculty of Education, Mount Saint Vincent University, Halifax, Canada 2Faculty of Education, Simon Fraser University, Burnaby, Canada 3Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, Athabasca University, Athabasca, Canada 4Olsen College of Engineering and Science, Fairleigh Dickinson University, Vancouver, Canada © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,1,"and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) has rapidly entered higher education, but empirical research on its roles in teaching and learning remains fragmented across instructional activities, outcomes, assessment approaches, and stakeholder perspectives. This scoping review followed PRISMA-ScR guidelines to map 394 empirical studies of GenAI use in higher education published from November 2022 through December 2025. The review examined how GenAI tools were integrated into authentic teaching and learning activities, what outcomes or skills were targeted, how studies assessed those outcomes, and how students and instructors perceived GenAI use. Across",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,1,"from November 2022 through December 2025. The review examined how GenAI tools were integrated into authentic teaching and learning activities, what outcomes or skills were targeted, how studies assessed those outcomes, and how students and instructors perceived GenAI use. Across the reviewed studies, GenAI was most often used to support existing academic tasks, especially feedback, tutoring, and writing. The most frequently targeted outcomes included engagement, usability and acceptance, critical thinking, disciplinary knowledge, academic performance, writing quality, and AI literacy. Self-regulated learning, ethics, creativity, feedback quality, and equity received less attention. Assessment practices relied mainly on surveys, tests or rubrics, artifact analysis, and interviews, suggesting that much of the evidence base is shaped by short-term and readily implemented methods. Student and instructor perceptions were predominantly mixed, reflecting perceived benefits alongside concerns about pedagogy, integrity, workload, and governance. The review highlights the need for stronger alignment between learning constructs and assessment methods, more process-sensitive evidence, clearer reporting of governance practices, and greater attention to equity in GenAI-supported higher education. Keywords Generative AI, Large language models, Higher education, Teaching and learning, Assessment, Stakeholder",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,1,"need for stronger alignment between learning constructs and assessment methods, more process-sensitive evidence, clearer reporting of governance practices, and greater attention to equity in GenAI-supported higher education. Keywords Generative AI, Large language models, Higher education, Teaching and learning, Assessment, Stakeholder perceptions, Scoping review Introduction Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly reshaping education regarding how students learn, how instructors teach, and how institutions organize educational practice (Chang et al., 2023; Ouyang & Jiao, 2021). Over the past decade, AI in education (AIEd) has developed into a broad field of study encompassing AI applications such as prediction, personalization, feedback, learning analytics, and instructional support, alongside mul­ tiple conceptual and ethical questions these technologies present (Chiu et al., 2023; Crompton & Burke, 2023; Ouyang & Jiao, 2021; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019; Zhai et al.,",130
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,2,"Page 2 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 2021). More recently, GenAI, especially large language model-based tools such as Chat­ GPT, has accelerated shifts in higher education. Unlike many earlier AIEd systems, these tools are directly accessible by students and instructors, usable across disciplines, and readily integrated into everyday academic tasks. In higher education, AI systems broadly have been used to evaluate academic prog­ ress, predict students at-risk (Embarak & Hawarna, 2024), offer personalized assistance and adaptive learning support (Chang et al., 2023; Yao & González-Vélez, 2025), and support instructional design and learning analytics (Crompton & Burke, 2023; Darvi­ shi et al., 2022; Liang & Luo, 2024). Applications of GenAI support activities such as writing, coding, idea generation, feedback, language learning, and content comprehen­ sion, particularly in contexts where text- or code-based work is central (Guo et al., 2024). Most adoptions appear pragmatic. Rather than fundamentally restructuring curricu­ lum, GenAI has mainly been used to augment existing learning activities by providing on-demand assistance, accelerating task completion, or expanding access to feedback",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,2,"code-based work is central (Guo et al., 2024). Most adoptions appear pragmatic. Rather than fundamentally restructuring curricu­ lum, GenAI has mainly been used to augment existing learning activities by providing on-demand assistance, accelerating task completion, or expanding access to feedback and support (Zhai et al., 2021). These developments have intensified debate about what counts as meaningful learning when students use GenAI and how its educational value should be examined in higher education (Noroozi et al., 2024). Although empirical research on GenAI in higher education is expanding rapidly, the literature remains fragmented in several key ways. First, studies vary widely in how GenAI is used in teaching and learning, outcomes or skills are targeted, and how out­ comes are conceptualized. Prior work (e.g., Chiu et al., 2023; Yu et al., 2024) has exam­ ined learning gains, writing quality, critical thinking, engagement, efficiency, usability, and affective responses, often using an idiosyncratic framework connecting outcomes to specific uses of GenAI. As a result, it remains difficult to discern which types of edu­ cational activities receive the most empirical attention and which learning priorities",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,2,"efficiency, usability, and affective responses, often using an idiosyncratic framework connecting outcomes to specific uses of GenAI. As a result, it remains difficult to discern which types of edu­ cational activities receive the most empirical attention and which learning priorities are being foregrounded. Second, how outcomes are assessed is often unclear. This matters because claims about the educational value of GenAI depend not only on which outcomes are examined, but also on how researchers operationalize and evaluate them. When studies rely primarily on self-report, immediate performance, or post-task perceptions, the kinds of conclu­ sions that can be drawn differ from those supported by trace data, longitudinal artifact analysis, external ratings, or observation. Mapping assessment approaches is therefore crucial for understanding the current evidence base and the kinds of conclusions that evidence can validly support. Third, stakeholder perspectives have become central to discussions of GenAI in higher education (Chiu et al., 2023; Ifenthaler et al., 2024). Students and instructors are posi­ tioned differently in relation to these tools and may interpret their usefulness, risks, and legitimacy differently. Existing studies have raised",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,2,"to discussions of GenAI in higher education (Chiu et al., 2023; Ifenthaler et al., 2024). Students and instructors are posi­ tioned differently in relation to these tools and may interpret their usefulness, risks, and legitimacy differently. Existing studies have raised recurring concerns about academic integrity, responsible use, disclosure, and institutional accountability, while also pointing to potential benefits for efficiency, support, and learning (Chang et al., 2023; Gruenha­ gen et al., 2024; Yusuf et al., 2024). However, empirical work varies considerably regard­ ing which perspectives are examined and how they are reported. This makes it difficult to compare students’ with instructors’ viewpoints, and to understand how governance and policy issues are being addressed in higher education contexts in practice. Existing systematic reviews and scoping reviews offer valuable classifications of how AI applications have been used in K-12 (Ma et al., 2025) or teacher education (Sperling",144
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,3,"Page 3 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 et al., 2024), how AI has been incorporated in education (Chiu et al., 2023; Yu et al., 2024) or how ethical considerations have been conceptualized in AI-mediated learn­ ing environments (Wiese et al., 2025). These reviews have made important contribu­ tions, but several limitations remain. Many aggregate AI technologies broadly instead of distinguishing GenAI from earlier AIEd systems; some focus on sectors other than higher education; and others emphasize conceptual, policy, or meta-level discussions more than actual empirical use in authentic teaching and learning settings. Given the speed with which GenAI has been adopted, there is a need for an updated mapping of recent empirical work that focuses specifically on higher education and simultaneously considers instructional use, targeted outcomes, assessment practices, and stakeholder perceptions. In response, this scoping review maps recent empirical studies of GenAI use in higher education. Unlike prior reviews that synthesize AI or GenAI in education at broader levels (e.g., Bahroun et al., 2023; Crompton & Burke, 2023; Noroozi et",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,3,"perceptions. In response, this scoping review maps recent empirical studies of GenAI use in higher education. Unlike prior reviews that synthesize AI or GenAI in education at broader levels (e.g., Bahroun et al., 2023; Crompton & Burke, 2023; Noroozi et al., 2024), this review focuses specifically on enacted, task-anchored GenAI use in authentic higher education teaching and learning contexts. It examines how GenAI is being incorporated into teaching and learning activities, what educational outcomes or skills are targeted, how assessment methods are used across studies, and how students and instructors per­ ceive its use, including concerns related to ethics, governance, and policy. By doing so, this review clarifies current patterns, identifies recurring emphases, and reveals gaps that need filling to develop more theoretically grounded and methodologically robust research. Specifically, we address these research questions (RQs): 1. How have GenAI tools been incorporated into authentic teaching and learning activities in higher education, and what educational outcomes or skills have empirical studies targeted? 2. What methods have empirical studies used to evaluate the educational outcomes or skills associated with GenAI use in",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,3,"been incorporated into authentic teaching and learning activities in higher education, and what educational outcomes or skills have empirical studies targeted? 2. What methods have empirical studies used to evaluate the educational outcomes or skills associated with GenAI use in higher education? 3. How do empirical studies of enacted GenAI use in higher education report students’ and instructors’ perceptions concerning effectiveness, ethical issues, and governance or policy implications? Methods This scoping review provides a comprehensive account of the scope, volume, and focus of the research in higher education. The methodology follows the checklist of the PRISMA extension for scoping reviews (PRISMA-ScR) (Peters et al., 2020; Tricco et al., 2018) and the methodological framework outlined in Arksey and O’Malley (2005). The review process encompassed five key stages: (1) identifying scoping review RQs, (2) identifying relevant studies, (3) selecting studies, (4) charting the data, and (5) summa­ rizing and reporting results. Literature search The literature search was conducted in January 2026 to capture research on LLM-based GenAI in higher education teaching and learning following ChatGPT’s public release in November 2022, which accelerated",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,3,"the data, and (5) summa­ rizing and reporting results. Literature search The literature search was conducted in January 2026 to capture research on LLM-based GenAI in higher education teaching and learning following ChatGPT’s public release in November 2022, which accelerated GenAI uptake in higher education. To maximize cov­ erage in a rapidly evolving field and to capture early-online and conference-linked jour­ nal articles that are subsequently indexed, we searched EBSCOhost and Web of Science",74
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,4,"Page 4 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 Core Collection. Together, these resources offer broad cross-disciplinary indexing and reduce reliance on any single database’s coverage. The following search terms were used with Boolean logic for text appearing in a title or abstract: (“generative AI” OR “genera­ tive artificial intelligence” OR “ChatGPT” OR “large language model*” OR “LLM*”) AND (“teaching” OR “learning” OR “instruction” OR “education”) AND (“higher education” OR “college” OR “university” OR “post-secondary” OR “postsecondary” OR “undergrad­ uate”). Search limits were set to peer-reviewed journal articles, English language, and publication dates from November 2022 through December 2025. The full database-spe­ cific search strategies, limits, and exported record counts are provided in Appendix A. Inclusion and exclusion criteria To maintain methodological rigor and conceptual alignment with task-anchored GenAI use in higher education, we applied prespecified inclusion and exclusion criteria at both screening and full-text stages of review. Studies were included if they (a) reported original empirical research (qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods); (b) were situated in a higher education context (college,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,4,"education, we applied prespecified inclusion and exclusion criteria at both screening and full-text stages of review. Studies were included if they (a) reported original empirical research (qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods); (b) were situated in a higher education context (college, university, or other post-secondary settings) involving learners and/or instructors; (c) examined the use of GenAI tools (e.g., ChatGPT and any other large language model– enabled system); and (d) documented use in higher education teaching and learning. Specifically, GenAI use had to be embedded in a course-related task or instructional activity, such as an assignment, assessment, feedback cycle, instructional intervention, or instructor-led class activity, and involve students and/or instructors using GenAI. Studies were excluded if they (a) lacked direct evidence of GenAI use embedded in an education-related task or learning activity; (b) involved GenAI primarily as a research instrument to generate artifacts that were then evaluated, rated, or benchmarked (for example, output-quality evaluation, prompt-engineering comparisons, rubric-alignment checks) without evidence that the outputs were used within an authentic teaching/ learning activity; (c) were not situated in higher education; (d) were non-empirical (for",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,4,"artifacts that were then evaluated, rated, or benchmarked (for example, output-quality evaluation, prompt-engineering comparisons, rubric-alignment checks) without evidence that the outputs were used within an authentic teaching/ learning activity; (c) were not situated in higher education; (d) were non-empirical (for example, opinion pieces, editorials, conceptual papers) or review articles; or (e) did not provide sufficient methodological detail to verify that GenAI use occurred in an authen­ tic educational context, in which case the record was conservatively coded as unclear and excluded at full-text. Study selection Eligibility screening was conducted using the inclusion and exclusion criteria described above. Titles and abstracts of the records were screened, and records that appeared eligible or could not be confidently excluded were advanced to full-text review. Three researchers participated in the full-text eligibility assessment of 523 articles. Before syn­ thesis, eligibility decisions were checked against the finalized inclusion and exclusion criteria. Ambiguous or borderline cases were discussed by the review team and resolved by consensus. Records that did not meet the finalized criteria were excluded before analysis. Figure 1 summarizes the study identification and selection",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,4,"the finalized inclusion and exclusion criteria. Ambiguous or borderline cases were discussed by the review team and resolved by consensus. Records that did not meet the finalized criteria were excluded before analysis. Figure 1 summarizes the study identification and selection process using a PRISMA- style flow diagram. The database search returned 2,778 records (EBSCO = 976; Web of Science = 1,802). After removing 514 duplicates, 2,264 unique records remained for title and abstract screening. Screening excluded records that were not about GenAI (n = 60),",85
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,5,"Page 5 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 not about teaching and learning (n = 387), not situated in higher education (n = 42), opinion or review articles (n = 406), perception-only studies with no enacted course use (n = 710), or unclear (n = 136), yielding 523 articles for full-text eligibility assessment. Full-text review resulted in 394 included empirical studies, with exclusions due to not about GenAI (n = 1), not about teaching and learning (n = 20), opinion/review (n = 3), perception-only studies with no enacted course use (n = 66), or unclear (n = 39). Coding and data analysis Data charting was conducted using a master coding spreadsheet aligned with the RQs. Extracted fields captured study and sample characteristics, GenAI tool informa­ tion, descriptive evidence for GenAI use-case coding, targeted outcomes, assessment methods, stakeholder perceptions, and ethics/policy indicators. Before full data chart­ ing, three researchers jointly coded a calibration set of 10 included studies to align coding interpretations, evidence rules, and decision rules for ambiguous cases. After Fig.",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,5,"coding, targeted outcomes, assessment methods, stakeholder perceptions, and ethics/policy indicators. Before full data chart­ ing, three researchers jointly coded a calibration set of 10 included studies to align coding interpretations, evidence rules, and decision rules for ambiguous cases. After Fig. 1 PRISMA diagram of article inclusion",46
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,6,"Page 6 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 calibration, the remaining included studies were divided among the three researchers for primary charting. Coders flagged ambiguous cases in the master spreadsheet and dis­ cussed them during virtual team check-ins. When needed, a second researcher reread the article before a final decision was entered. Final coding decisions were made by con­ sensus. For reporting and synthesis, the analytic variables used in the descriptive map­ ping were compiled in Supplementary Table S1. Coding focused on five sets of variables aligned with the aims of this review: (1) study profile and design, (2) GenAI use cases and targeted outcomes, (3) assessment methods, (4) stakeholder perceptions, and (5) ethics/policy and governance indicators. These vari­ ables were operationalized through predefined sub-categories and, where appropriate, refined through calibration coding, evidence-based coding rules, and consensus discus­ sion. Table 1 summarizes the main analytic coding categories used for data charting and synthesis. The full operational codebook, including allowable values, coding rules, exam­ ples, and decision rules for ambiguous cases, is",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,6,"coding, evidence-based coding rules, and consensus discus­ sion. Table 1 summarizes the main analytic coding categories used for data charting and synthesis. The full operational codebook, including allowable values, coding rules, exam­ ples, and decision rules for ambiguous cases, is provided in Appendix B. For RQ1, each study was assigned one single-label “use_case_category.” Coders first recorded how each article described the purpose of GenAI use and the enacted learning activity or task; these descriptive entries served as coding evidence rather than separate analytic outcomes. The final “use_case_category” reflected the GenAI-supported activ­ ity most central to the study aim, intervention, task design, enacted activity, or primary outcome. Initial categories were informed by prior AI/GenAI education scoping review frameworks (Joksimovic et al., 2023; Su & Yang, 2022; Xia et al., 2024), and final catego­ ries were refined against the included corpus through calibration coding, review-team discussion, and consensus adjudication. Final use-case categories are listed in Table 1 and operationalized in Appendix B. Within the targeted outcomes code, achievement/task performance referred to dem­ onstrated performance on assessed tasks, such as grades, scores, rubric-based",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,6,"calibration coding, review-team discussion, and consensus adjudication. Final use-case categories are listed in Table 1 and operationalized in Appendix B. Within the targeted outcomes code, achievement/task performance referred to dem­ onstrated performance on assessed tasks, such as grades, scores, rubric-based outcomes, task accuracy, or task completion, whereas domain knowledge/conceptual understand­ ing referred to subject-matter understanding or knowledge acquisition not necessarily measured through formal performance evaluation. Because studies could target more than one educational outcome or skill, targeted outcomes were coded as multi-label variables. Assessment methods were also coded as multi-label variables because studies could use more than one method to evaluate targeted outcomes or skills. These codes captured the methods used to generate empirical evidence about GenAI-related learning, perfor­ mance, perception, or process outcomes, including surveys, tests/quizzes/rubrics, arti­ fact analysis, interviews/focus groups, analytics/logs, observation, expert ratings, and physiological or biometric measures. For student and instructor perception, initial coding drew on sensitizing concepts from prior review literature (e.g., Chan, 2023; Kim et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024), and can­ didate themes were refined inductively through calibration and check-in discussions in",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,6,"measures. For student and instructor perception, initial coding drew on sensitizing concepts from prior review literature (e.g., Chan, 2023; Kim et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024), and can­ didate themes were refined inductively through calibration and check-in discussions in which researchers compared interpretations, consolidated overlapping labels, and agreed on final theme definitions. Perception direction was coded separately for student and instructor evidence as Positive, Negative, Mixed, or Not reported. Within the ethics and policy category, we first coded three separate binary indica­ tors: (a) whether ethics or policy issues were mentioned in relation to GenAI use, (b) whether the authors recommended a concrete policy, guideline, rule set, or governance",110
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,7,"Page 7 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 action, and (c) whether the study reported instructor- or course-level rules governing GenAI use in the enacted learning context. These indicators were allowed to co-occur within the same study. For synthesis, we then created a derived single-label variable, “policy_presence”, to classify each study according to the highest level of governance explicitness reported. The hierarchy was applied as follows: if “AI_policy_recommend” = Yes, the study was classified as “institutional policy recommended”; otherwise, if “AI_ policy_by_instructor” = Yes, it was classified as “instructor-level rules set”; otherwise, if “ethics_policy_issues_mentioned” = Yes, it was classified as “ethics/policy mentioned only”; otherwise, it was classified as “none mentioned.” This derived variable enabled each study to be counted once in the descriptive synthesis while preserving the underly­ ing binary indicators in the coded dataset (Arksey & O’Malley, 2005; Levac et al., 2010; Tricco et al., 2018). Analysis combined descriptive frequency counts with inductive content analysis to synthesize patterns within open-text fields and narrative findings (Mayring, 2015). For single-label variables, each",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,7,"in the coded dataset (Arksey & O’Malley, 2005; Levac et al., 2010; Tricco et al., 2018). Analysis combined descriptive frequency counts with inductive content analysis to synthesize patterns within open-text fields and narrative findings (Mayring, 2015). For single-label variables, each study was counted once for the relevant variable. For multi- label variables, including targeted outcomes, assessment methods, and student/instruc­ tor perception themes, each category was treated as a binary present/absent indicator so that a study could contribute to more than one category when supported by article evi­ dence. Assessment methods and targeted outcomes were coded as separate study-level multi-label variables; accordingly, the synthesis reports the distribution of assessment methods across studies rather than assigning specific methods to specific outcomes within multi-method studies. The derived “policy_presence” variable was generated after the three ethics/policy indicators were finalized and was used only as a single-label summary variable in the descriptive synthesis. Results Characteristics of included studies We first summarize the methodological and technological profile of the 394 included studies. As shown in Fig. 2, mixed methods was the most common research design (n",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,7,"a single-label summary variable in the descriptive synthesis. Results Characteristics of included studies We first summarize the methodological and technological profile of the 394 included studies. As shown in Fig. 2, mixed methods was the most common research design (n = 213, 54%) followed by quantitative studies (n = 109, 28%) and qualitative studies (n = 72, 18%). This pattern represented research on enacted GenAI use in higher educa­ tion employed diverse empirical designs. Figure 3 presents the distribution of AI tools and platforms used across the included studies. The most common category was multiple AI tools (n = 102), followed by Chat­ GPT (unspecified version) (n = 94), ChatGPT-3/3.5 family tools (n = 66), other specific named tools (n = 62), and ChatGPT-4 family tools (n = 52). GenAI/LLM tools without a specified platform were least common (n = 18). As a result, ChatGPT-related applica­ tions accounted for a large share of the tool/platform categories, while other named plat­ forms also appeared in the corpus. Across the 394 included studies, participant focus was coded as students (n = 352),",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,7,"18). As a result, ChatGPT-related applica­ tions accounted for a large share of the tool/platform categories, while other named plat­ forms also appeared in the corpus. Across the 394 included studies, participant focus was coded as students (n = 352), instructors (n = 15), students and instructors (n = 26), and studies including multiple stakeholder groups (n = 1), as shown in Fig. 4. This indicates that the evidence base con­ tributing to RQ3 was dominated by student-focused studies.",79
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,8,"Page 8 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 Category Code Definition Example Basic Information Author Year Authoring information Year of publication John & Chang 2024 Purpose and Domain Use-case category A single-label analytic variable capturing the primary enacted GenAI-supported teaching or learning activity in each study. Initial coding was informed by prior AI/GenAI education scoping reviews (Joksimovic et al., 2023; Su & Yang, 2022; Xia et al., 2024), and final categories were refined against the included corpus. Each study was assigned one primary use-case category based on the activity most central to the study aim, intervention, task design, or primary outcome. Final categories were: Feedback/tutoring; Writing support; Lesson planning; Language learning/translation; Programming/debugging; Assessment/grading; Idea genera­ tion; Research/literature support; General academic support; Simulation/role-play; Content creation/multimodal; Improve critical thinking; Reflection/SRL. A study in which students used ChatGPT to receive formative com­ ments on draft essays was coded as Feedback/tutoring. Study Design Research Design Whether the methodology is qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods. Qualitative Participants The group of individuals involved in the study. Undergraduate",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,8,"which students used ChatGPT to receive formative com­ ments on draft essays was coded as Feedback/tutoring. Study Design Research Design Whether the methodology is qualitative, quantitative, or mixed methods. Qualitative Participants The group of individuals involved in the study. Undergraduate students Targeted Skills/ Educational outcomes Based on Weng et al. (2024) and Xia et al. (2024), this code identifies the skills or learning outcomes that studies aimed to develop or measure through GenAI applications in higher education. These outcomes represent the dependent variables used to evaluate the educational impact of Examples include critical thinking, achievement, domain knowledge, writing quality, AI literacy, and SRL/metacognition. GenAI tools. Programming performance Assessment methods The tools or methods used to evaluate the dependent variables or outcomes in the study. Pretest and posttest User Perception Student perception This code captures the dimensions of students’ perceptions toward GenAI in higher education (Kim et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024). During coding, responses and findings were grouped into themes such as engagement/motivation, learning and skill develop­ ment, reliability/usability, pedagogy/assessment, efficiency/workload, language support/writing quality, critical thinking, ethics/ integrity, creativity/idea",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,8,"GenAI in higher education (Kim et al., 2025; Xia et al., 2024). During coding, responses and findings were grouped into themes such as engagement/motivation, learning and skill develop­ ment, reliability/usability, pedagogy/assessment, efficiency/workload, language support/writing quality, critical thinking, ethics/ integrity, creativity/idea generation, dependence/overreliance, social and career impacts, equity/inclusion, privacy/data security, and policy/disclosure/governance. Survey examining students’ per­ ceptions of ChatGPT’s usefulness and convenience for coursework. Instructor perception This code captures the dimensions of instructors’ perceptions regarding the pedagogical, practical, and ethical implications of GenAI (Xia et al., 2024; Chan, 2023). During coding, findings were grouped into themes such as efficiency/workload, pedagogy/ assessment, ethics/integrity, professional learning/AI literacy, learning and skill development, reliability/usability, affect/tech­ nostress, engagement/motivation, equity/inclusion, critical thinking, policy/disclosure/governance, dependence/overreliance, creativity/idea generation, privacy/data security, and social and career impacts. Interview responses on ChatGPT’s impact on assessment design and instructional practices. Ethics and Policy Ethics/policy is­ sues mentioned Whether the study addressed ethical considerations, policy implications, or guidelines related to AI use in educational contexts. Students raised academic integrity and privacy concerns when using ChatGPT for coursework. Table 1 Summarized coding scheme",178
