DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,9,"Page 9 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 Category Code Definition Example AI policy recommend Whether the authors explicitly recommend a concrete policy, guideline, rule set, or governance action for GenAI use (institutional, departmental, program, or course level) in the authors’ own voice (typically Discussion/Conclusion/Implications). The authors recommend that universities establish disclosure guidelines for GenAI-assisted work. AI policy by instructor Whether the study reports that the instructor/course set explicit rules or parameters governing GenAI use for the learning task(s) (e.g., permitted/prohibited uses, disclosure requirement, boundaries for prompting, or use of a structured framework to define allowable use). Students were required to disclose prompts and could use GenAI for brainstorming but not for final drafting. Policy presence (derived) A derived single-label variable summarizing the highest level of ethics/policy or governance explicitness reported in each study. The variable was constructed from three independent binary indicators: “ethics_policy_issues_mentioned”, “AI_policy_recom­ mend”, and “AI_policy_by_instructor.” Because these indicators could co-occur, a hierarchy was applied so that each study was counted once in the descriptive synthesis: (3) institutional policy",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,9,"in each study. The variable was constructed from three independent binary indicators: “ethics_policy_issues_mentioned”, “AI_policy_recom­ mend”, and “AI_policy_by_instructor.” Because these indicators could co-occur, a hierarchy was applied so that each study was counted once in the descriptive synthesis: (3) institutional policy recommended; (2) instructor-level rules set; (1) ethics/policy mentioned only; (0) none mentioned. If “AI_policy_recommend” = Yes, classify as “institutional policy recommended,” even if ethics/ policy issues and instructor rules were also coded Yes. If “AI_policy_ recommend” = No but “AI_poli­ cy_by_instructor” = Yes, classify as “instructor-level rules set.” If only “ethics_policy_issues_mentioned” = Yes, classify as “ethics/policy mentioned only.” Note. SRL = self-regulated learning Table 1 (continued)",106
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,10,"Page 10 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 RQ1: Use-case categories and targeted educational outcomes Figure 5 shows the actual use of GenAI in higher education is dominated by two instruc­ tional activities: feedback/tutoring and writing support. Other activities included les­ son planning, language learning/translation, programming/debugging, and assessment/ grading. Less frequent uses included idea generation, research/literature support, simu­ lation/role-play, content creation/multimodal activities, explicit critical-thinking sup­ port, and reflection/SRL. Overall, these categories show that feedback/tutoring and writing support were the most frequently reported enacted uses in the included studies. Fig. 3 Distribution of AI tools/platforms examined across the included studies Fig. 2 Distribution of research designs across the included studies",118
