DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,14,"Page 14 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 rubric measures (n = 185; 47.0%), artifact analysis (n = 167; 42.4%), and interview/focus group methods (n = 143; 36.3%). Other reported methods included analytics/logs (n = 76; 19.3%), observation (n = 30; 7.6%), expert judgment/ratings (n = 13; 3.3%), and physi­ ological/biometric measures (n = 8; 2.0%). Because studies could employ more than one assessment method, the percentages do not sum to 100%. RQ 3. Student and instructor perceptions, ethics/policy signals, and governance-related reporting Given that the included corpus was predominantly student-focused, perception evi­ dence synthesized for RQ3 reflects student experiences more strongly than instructor perspectives. Within these enacted-use studies, perceptions were reported in relation to effectiveness, ethical concerns, and governance-related issues. Perception direction was coded for 315 student-related cases and 35 instructor-related cases (Table 4). Among student perceptions, mixed evaluations were most common (n = 228, 72.4%), followed by positive evaluations (n = 85, 27.0%) and negative evaluations (n = 2, 0.6%). Among instructor perceptions, mixed evaluations were also most common",180
