DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,15,"development (n = 9), affect/technostress (n = 8), and critical thinking (n = 8). Less frequently coded themes included creativity/idea generation (n = 5), depen­ dence/overreliance (n = 4), engagement/motivation (n = 3), equity and inclusion (n = 3), privacy and data security (n = 2), and social and career impacts (n = 1). Ethics/policy and governance-related reporting was summarized using the derived policy presence variable (Table 5). Across all studies, institutional policy recom­ mended only was the most frequent category (n = 151), followed by ethics/policy men­ tioned (n = 148), instructor-level rules set (n = 52), and none mentioned (n = 43). When Table 5 Policy presence by participant focus Policy Presence Multiple stakehold­ er groups Instructors Students Students & Instructors Total Institutional policy recommended 0 11 125 15 151 Ethics/policy mentioned only 1 4 138 5 148 Instructor-level rules set 0 0 47 5 52 None mentioned 0 0 42 1 43 Total 1 15 352 26 394 Note. Policy presence is a derived four-level variable summarizing the highest level of governance explicitness reported in each study",180
