DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,16,"Page 16 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 cross-tabulated by participant focus, Institutional policy recommended was coded in instructor (n = 11), student (n = 125), and students and instructors (n = 15) studies. Eth­ ics/policy mentioned only was distributed across multiple stakeholder groups (n = 1), instructor (n = 4), student (n = 138), and students and instructors (n = 5) studies. Instruc­ tor-level rules set was coded in student (n = 47) and students and instructors (n = 5) stud­ ies. None mentioned appeared in student (n = 42) and students and instructors (n = 1) studies. Discussion and conclusion This scoping review mapped how LLM-based GenAI has been applied in higher educa­ tion teaching and learning, what educational outcomes empirical studies have targeted, what assessment methods have been used to evaluate those outcomes, and how students and instructors have perceived enacted GenAI use. Across 394 studies, the field appears expansive but still consolidating. GenAI is being used across a growing range of higher education tasks, yet the evidence",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,16,"used to evaluate those outcomes, and how students and instructors have perceived enacted GenAI use. Across 394 studies, the field appears expansive but still consolidating. GenAI is being used across a growing range of higher education tasks, yet the evidence base remains concentrated around recurring instruc­ tional uses, readily measurable outcomes, and short-cycle assessment strategies. These findings should be interpreted as descriptive trends in the empirical literature, not as causal or effectiveness claims. As a scoping review, this study did not conduct formal critical appraisal or estimate effect sizes; accordingly, the findings indicate where empiri­ cal attention has concentrated rather than whether GenAI improves learning outcomes (Arksey & O’Malley, 2005; Levac et al., 2010; Tricco et al., 2018). From instructional uptake to pedagogical priorities A central finding of this review is that GenAI use in higher education has primarily focused on a limited number of task-oriented applications. Feedback/tutoring and writ­ ing support were the most common use cases, followed by language learning/translation, lesson planning, assessment/grading, general academic support, and programming/ debugging. This pattern suggests that higher education has adopted GenAI",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,16,"on a limited number of task-oriented applications. Feedback/tutoring and writ­ ing support were the most common use cases, followed by language learning/translation, lesson planning, assessment/grading, general academic support, and programming/ debugging. This pattern suggests that higher education has adopted GenAI mainly as a tool for augmenting existing academic tasks, especially those involving feedback, text production, academic assistance, and instructional preparation, instead of as a catalyst for fundamentally reconfiguring curriculum or pedagogy (Bahroun et al., 2023; Jin et al., 2025). This interpretation aligns with prior AIEd and GenAI reviews showing that edu­ cational applications often emerge rapidly in practice before theoretical and pedagogi­ cal models fully explain how they support learning (Lin et al., 2026; Chen et al., 2020; Noroozi et al., 2024; Zawacki-Richter et al., 2019). The distribution of targeted outcomes reinforces this interpretation. The most fre­ quently coded outcomes were engagement/motivation/affect, usability/UX and accep­ tance, critical thinking/reasoning, domain knowledge/conceptual understanding, achievement/performance, writing quality, and AI literacy/prompting. By contrast, SRL/ metacognition, ethics/academic integrity, creativity/idea generation, feedback qual­ ity, and equity/inclusion/access were less frequently emphasized. The outcome profile is revealing. On",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,16,"were engagement/motivation/affect, usability/UX and accep­ tance, critical thinking/reasoning, domain knowledge/conceptual understanding, achievement/performance, writing quality, and AI literacy/prompting. By contrast, SRL/ metacognition, ethics/academic integrity, creativity/idea generation, feedback qual­ ity, and equity/inclusion/access were less frequently emphasized. The outcome profile is revealing. On one hand, the literature is not solely focused on achievement; it also address reasoning, motivation, usability, and user experience, suggesting that research­ ers recognize GenAI as both a learning resource and a socio-technical intervention (Yan et al., 2024). On the other hand, the prominence of usability and acceptance indi­ cates that much of the empirical literature remains organized around adoption-phase",100
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,17,"Page 17 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 questions: whether GenAI tools are usable, motivating, accepted, and helpful within existing instructional arrangements. This adoption-phase orientation is understandable in a fast-moving field, but it also creates a limitation. If research remains focused primarily on whether GenAI fits current assignments, feedback practices, and assessment routines, the field may under-examine more ambitious questions about how higher education should redesign learning activi­ ties, redistribute human and AI roles, and cultivate capacities that are not easily cap­ tured through short-term usability or satisfaction measures. Prior work has similarly argued that AIEd research needs stronger theoretical grounding and closer alignment between technology use, learning mechanisms, and educational purpose (Bauer et al., 2025; Chen et al., 2020; Noroozi et al., 2024). More ambitious studies may also require design constraints, comparison conditions, or forms of experimental control that par­ tially interfere with naturally occurring classroom activity. This creates a methodological tension: studies need authenticity, but they also need enough structure to explain how and why GenAI may support or",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,17,"constraints, comparison conditions, or forms of experimental control that par­ tially interfere with naturally occurring classroom activity. This creates a methodological tension: studies need authenticity, but they also need enough structure to explain how and why GenAI may support or hinder learning. What counts as evidence of learning The assessment profile identified in this review shows that empirical studies relied most often on survey scales, test/quiz/rubric measures, artifact analysis, and interview/focus group methods. These approaches are common and valuable in educational research, especially for early-stage mapping of user experience, perceived learning, and task performance. However, they constrain the strength of claims that can be made about learning processes. Self-report measures can provide important evidence about percep­ tions, motivation, and perceived usefulness, but they are limited indicators of learning, metacognition, or knowledge change when used alone (Brown & Zhao, 2023; Craig et al., 2020; Sitzmann et al., 2010). This is especially important because several targeted outcomes in this review, including critical thinking, feedback uptake, and SRL, are pro­ cess-oriented constructs that require evidence for beyond post-task perception or single end-point measures",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,17,"et al., 2020; Sitzmann et al., 2010). This is especially important because several targeted outcomes in this review, including critical thinking, feedback uptake, and SRL, are pro­ cess-oriented constructs that require evidence for beyond post-task perception or single end-point measures (Barnett & Ceci, 2002; Rovers et al., 2019; Sitzmann et al., 2010; Winne, 2022). The evidence base does extend beyond perception. Performance measures, artifact analysis, and interviews were also frequently used, suggesting that the field is beginning to examine GenAI-supported learning through multiple forms of evidence. Yet, process- focused methods such as trace data, observation, expert ratings, and physiological or biometric measures remained comparatively uncommon. As a result, much of the learn­ ing activity associated with GenAI use, including prompting, revision, feedback uptake, monitoring, verification, and reliance management, remains only partially visible (Lin et al., 2026). This limitation is consequential because GenAI use is often iterative and inter­ actional. The current literature offers useful insight into what students and instructors report, produce, and achieve, but less into how students regulate AI use over time, how reliance develops or is restrained,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,17,"because GenAI use is often iterative and inter­ actional. The current literature offers useful insight into what students and instructors report, produce, and achieve, but less into how students regulate AI use over time, how reliance develops or is restrained, and how instructional design shapes these trajectories. This gap is particularly notable given calls for more methodologically robust research on GenAI assessment and learning outcomes in higher education (Noroozi et al., 2024; Xia et al., 2024; Weng et al., 2024).",80
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,18,"Page 18 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 Ambivalent uptake: students, instructors, and governance The findings for RQ3 suggest that enacted GenAI use in higher education is character­ ized less by simple enthusiasm or rejection than by ambivalence, negotiation, and gov­ ernance. For both students and instructors, mixed perceptions were more common than purely positive or negative perceptions. This pattern challenges polarized narratives that cast GenAI either as an unequivocal pedagogical benefit or as a straightforward threat to academic integrity. Instead, the evidence suggests that both groups are navigating its affordances and risks simultaneously, consistent with emerging work on GenAI percep­ tions, academic integrity, and responsible use in higher education (Colbran et al., 2026; Dai & Chan, 2026; Gruenhagen et al., 2024; Kim et al., 2025; Yusuf et al., 2024). At the same time, students and instructors appeared to frame those tensions differ­ ently. Students most often emphasized learning and skill development, engagement/ motivation, and reliability/usability, suggesting that they encounter GenAI primarily through its immediate utility for academic support, task completion,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,18,"the same time, students and instructors appeared to frame those tensions differ­ ently. Students most often emphasized learning and skill development, engagement/ motivation, and reliability/usability, suggesting that they encounter GenAI primarily through its immediate utility for academic support, task completion, perceived learn­ ing assistance, and efficiency. Instructors, by contrast, most often emphasized pedagogy/ assessment, efficiency/workload, professional learning/AI literacy, reliability/usability, and ethics/integrity. These differences point to a meaningful divergence in stakeholder standpoint. Students often approach GenAI through immediate academic tasks and out­ comes, whereas instructors are more likely to evaluate GenAI in relation to longer-term learning, assessment validity, academic integrity, and professional responsibility. Prior studies of educational technology adoption similarly show that students and instructors can evaluate the same technology through different experiential and institutional lenses (Chugh et al., 2023; Li, 2007; Nikolic et al., 2024). Both perspectives are legitimate, but they can generate tension. Students’ interest in utility and efficiency may make them less receptive to desirable difficulties that sup­ port deeper learning, while instructors’ caution about integrity and assessment valid­ ity may protect educational quality but also discourage productive",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,18,"they can generate tension. Students’ interest in utility and efficiency may make them less receptive to desirable difficulties that sup­ port deeper learning, while instructors’ caution about integrity and assessment valid­ ity may protect educational quality but also discourage productive experimentation (Dai & Chan, 2026; Gruenhagen et al., 2024; Kim et al., 2025). This tension is especially important in GenAI contexts because the same tool can support learning, accelerate task completion, obscure authorship, or reduce engagement with difficult cognitive work depending on task design and governance. The challenge for higher education is there­ fore not to privilege one perspective over the other, but to design learning environments where efficiency, experimentation, accountability, and meaningful learning are held together. The governance findings reinforce this point. Across the reviewed studies, policy was most often framed as a general ethical or policy concern or as a recommendation for institutional policy, while fewer studies reported explicit instructor-level rules governing GenAI use in enacted learning contexts. This suggests that ethical awareness has become widespread, but concrete governance remains uneven. The field appears to be moving from",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,18,"as a recommendation for institutional policy, while fewer studies reported explicit instructor-level rules governing GenAI use in enacted learning contexts. This suggests that ethical awareness has become widespread, but concrete governance remains uneven. The field appears to be moving from recognizing the problem toward experimenting with responses. This pattern aligns with broader higher education literature calling for clearer institutional guidance, disclo­ sure norms, and pedagogically informed policy frameworks (Chan, 2023; Ifenthaler et al., 2024; Jin et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2024; Chang et al., 2026). Importantly, governance should not be understood only as a compliance issue. In course settings, governance includes explicit guidance about what forms of AI assis­ tance are permitted, how students should disclose use, how AI-supported work will be",123
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,19,"Page 19 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 assessed, and which parts of the learning process require human judgment. Without such guidance, students may interpret GenAI use primarily through convenience and task completion, while instructors may experience it primarily as a threat to assessment validity. More productive governance would support bounded experimentation, where students can learn to use GenAI responsibly while instructors retain clarity about learn­ ing goals, evidence of student understanding, and acceptable forms of support. Equity as a peripheral but consequential gap The equity and inclusion findings deserve particular attention. Equity/inclusion/access was rarely targeted as an outcome, and equity-related perception themes appeared much less often than themes related to engagement, learning support, usability, pedagogy, effi­ ciency, or integrity. This does not mean equity is unimportant in GenAI use; rather, it might suggest that equity remains peripheral in enacted higher education studies. This is notable because GenAI use may intersect with access to paid tools, linguistic privilege, disability support, uneven AI literacy, institutional rules, and discipline-specific norms (An et al.,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,19,"it might suggest that equity remains peripheral in enacted higher education studies. This is notable because GenAI use may intersect with access to paid tools, linguistic privilege, disability support, uneven AI literacy, institutional rules, and discipline-specific norms (An et al., 2025; Lin, et al., 2026; Weng & Fu, 2025). These concerns are consistent with broader discussions of AI literacy, inclusion, and differential access in educational tech­ nology research (Ballantyne et al., 2025; Lin et al., 2024; Sperling et al., 2024). The equity implications of GenAI will likely vary across jurisdictions, institutions, dis­ ciplines, and student populations, so broad claims should be made cautiously. Neverthe­ less, the low visibility of equity in this corpus is itself a meaningful finding. In contrast to emerging work on AI literacy and AI ethics in K-12 and teacher education contexts, higher education studies of enacted GenAI use appear to have focused more strongly on adoption, usability, performance, and integrity than on differential access or inclu­ sion (e.g., Ma et al., 2025; Sperling et al., 2024; Wiese et al., 2025). Future studies should therefore treat equity",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,19,"GenAI use appear to have focused more strongly on adoption, usability, performance, and integrity than on differential access or inclu­ sion (e.g., Ma et al., 2025; Sperling et al., 2024; Wiese et al., 2025). Future studies should therefore treat equity not as a secondary concern but as a core dimension of educational impact. Implications for research Several implications follow for future research. First, the field would benefit from shift­ ing from documenting adoption toward explaining learning. Current studies provide substantial evidence about where and how GenAI is being used, but less about how these uses support or constrain learning. Research grounded in learning sciences frameworks, especially those related to feedback, self-regulation, and metacognitive monitoring, may help move the literature beyond descriptive mapping toward explanatory insight (Hattie & Timperley, 2007; Winne, 2022). Second, future studies should better align targeted outcomes with assessment strate­ gies. If a study claims to examine self-regulated learning, critical thinking, AI literacy, or disciplinary reasoning, these constructs should be measured in ways that go beyond self-reported satisfaction or perceived benefit. Stronger designs would combine perfor­ mance measures,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,19,"assessment strate­ gies. If a study claims to examine self-regulated learning, critical thinking, AI literacy, or disciplinary reasoning, these constructs should be measured in ways that go beyond self-reported satisfaction or perceived benefit. Stronger designs would combine perfor­ mance measures, trace data, artifact analysis, observation, and qualitative process evi­ dence to examine not only whether students report using AI successfully, but how they used it, under what instructional conditions, and with what trade-offs in learning, effort, and achievement. Third, multi-stakeholder designs remain important. Because students and instructors often perceive GenAI differently, studies focusing on only one stakeholder group risk",99
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,20,"Page 20 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 overlooking tensions that shape actual classroom use. Comparative designs examining the same instructional context from both student and instructor perspectives would be particularly valuable for understanding mismatches between learner motivations, assess­ ment design, and institutional expectations. Such designs could examine, for example, how students use GenAI to expedite drafting while instructors use it to manage feed­ back or grading workload, and how these parallel uses alter the meaning of authorship, feedback, and assessment. Fourth, more attention is needed to understand underrepresented outcomes. The rela­ tively modest attention to self-regulated learning/metacognition, ethics/academic integ­ rity, creativity, and especially equity/inclusion/access points toward important blind spots in the current literature. Given known concerns about uneven access, disclosure pressure, linguistic privilege, and variable AI literacy, future research should treat these not as peripheral issues but as core dimensions of GenAI’s educational impact. Implications for practice and policy For practice, the findings suggest that GenAI should be treated as an instructional design issue, not simply a decision about adopting",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,20,"treat these not as peripheral issues but as core dimensions of GenAI’s educational impact. Implications for practice and policy For practice, the findings suggest that GenAI should be treated as an instructional design issue, not simply a decision about adopting a tool. Because the most common applica­ tions involve feedback and writing support, instructors need to clarify how GenAI can contribute to those tasks, what forms of support are permitted, and where human judg­ ment remains essential. Prior work has similarly emphasized the importance of explicit instructional guidance, transparency, and alignment between GenAI use and learning goals to reduce ambiguity and support responsible educational use (Chan, 2023; Chang et al., 2023; Jin et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2024). For policy, the results suggest that a sole focus on ethics is insufficient. While many studies mentioned ethical or policy issues, fewer reported implemented instructor-level rules, and fewer still reflected explicit governance structures embedded in course activ­ ity. Institutions therefore need frameworks that go beyond broad warnings or prohibi­ tions. More productive approaches are likely to emphasize transparency, disclosure, pedagogical alignment,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,20,"fewer reported implemented instructor-level rules, and fewer still reflected explicit governance structures embedded in course activ­ ity. Institutions therefore need frameworks that go beyond broad warnings or prohibi­ tions. More productive approaches are likely to emphasize transparency, disclosure, pedagogical alignment, and staff development while supporting instructors in designing assessments that remain meaningful in AI-infused environments (Chan, 2023; Ifenthaler et al., 2024; Jin et al., 2025). The perception findings also imply that one-size-fits-all policy is unlikely to suffice. Students’ practical interest in efficiency and support, and instructors’ stronger empha­ sis on assessment, ethics, and workload, suggest that effective governance must address both user experience and instructional responsibility. Flexible, discipline-sensitive guid­ ance may therefore be more effective than generic institutional statements, especially when GenAI use differs across writing-intensive, programming-intensive, clinical, pro­ fessional, and design-based learning contexts (An et al., 2025; Ballantyne et al., 2025; Lin et al., 2024; Weng & Fu, 2025). Limitations and concluding remarks Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, as a scoping review, this study mapped the characteristics and patterns within the evidence base but did not assess study",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,20,"al., 2025; Lin et al., 2024; Weng & Fu, 2025). Limitations and concluding remarks Several limitations should be acknowledged. First, as a scoping review, this study mapped the characteristics and patterns within the evidence base but did not assess study qual­ ity or estimate effect sizes. Second, the review was intentionally restricted to empirical studies reporting enacted, task-anchored use in authentic higher education contexts. This decision enhanced conceptual precision, but it also excluded perception-only",74
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,21,"Page 21 of 24 Lin et al. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education (2026) 23:55 studies, conceptual papers, reviews, and studies primarily focused on evaluating AI-gen­ erated outputs as research artifacts. Third, coding necessarily involved abstraction from complex instructional settings, which may have simplified overlapping uses, outcomes, stakeholder groups, and governance considerations. Fourth, assessment methods and targeted outcomes were coded as separate study-level multi-label variables; therefore, this review reports the distribution of assessment methods across studies rather than assigning specific methods to specific outcomes within multi-method studies. Finally, the field is evolving rapidly, so the patterns reported here should be interpreted as a structured overview of a dynamic field rather than a final account of GenAI in higher education. This scoping review shows that empirical research on GenAI in higher education has moved beyond speculative commentary and now documents a substantial body of classroom and course-related use. Meanwhile, the evidence base remains concentrated around a relatively narrow set of uses, especially feedback/tutoring and writing support, and a familiar set of outcomes, such as engagement, usability, critical thinking, achieve­",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,21,"documents a substantial body of classroom and course-related use. Meanwhile, the evidence base remains concentrated around a relatively narrow set of uses, especially feedback/tutoring and writing support, and a familiar set of outcomes, such as engagement, usability, critical thinking, achieve­ ment, knowledge development, and writing quality. Assessment has relied heavily on surveys, performance measures, artifact analysis, and interviews, while more process- sensitive approaches remain less common. Stakeholder perceptions were predominantly mixed, not polarized, and governance often appeared more as ethical concern or policy recommendation than as fully articulated instructional or institutional rule structures. Overall, the field is no longer asking only whether GenAI can be used in higher educa­ tion. It is increasingly confronting a more demanding question: under what pedagogical, assessment, and governance conditions does GenAI support meaningful learning with­ out displacing the capacities higher education seeks to cultivate? Advancing this field will require more theoretically grounded outcome selection, stronger alignment between constructs and assessment, greater attention to self-regulation and equity, and clearer institutional frameworks that support responsible, instructionally coherent use. By clari­ fying current patterns and gaps, this",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,21,"Advancing this field will require more theoretically grounded outcome selection, stronger alignment between constructs and assessment, greater attention to self-regulation and equity, and clearer institutional frameworks that support responsible, instructionally coherent use. By clari­ fying current patterns and gaps, this review provides a foundation for more analytically rigorous and educationally consequential research on GenAI in higher education. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9. Supplementary Material 1 Supplementary Material 2 Acknowledgements We thank the research assistants who supported the preliminary stages of this project, including early literature screening, data organization, and related research activities. As the scope and analytic approach of the review evolved, the study underwent substantial further verification, recoding, analysis, and manuscript development by the authors. We also thank the anonymous reviewers for their constructive feedback, which helped strengthen the manuscript. This research was supported by the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC), grant numbers 430-2024-00269 and 435-2026-1299 (PI: Michael Lin). Author contributions Conceptualization: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., and P. H. W.; Methodology: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C.,",180
DOC_058,s41239-026-00630-9.pdf,10.1186/s41239-026-00630-9,21,"supported by the Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada (SSHRC), grant numbers 430-2024-00269 and 435-2026-1299 (PI: Michael Lin). Author contributions Conceptualization: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., and P. H. W.; Methodology: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., and P. H. W.; Investigation: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., and J. R.; Formal analysis: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., and J. R.; Visualization: M. P.-C. L.; Writing—original draft preparation: M. P.-C. L.; Writing—review and editing: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., P. H. W., G. M. B., and J. R.; Validation: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., P. H. W., G. M. B., and J. R.; Supervision: M. P.-C. L. and P. H. W.; Funding acquisition: M. P.-C. L., D. H. C., J. R., and P. H. W. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Authorship order is contribution-based. Jeeho Ryoo is listed last to reflect disciplinary conventions in his field. Data availability No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.",167
