DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,2,"Thoughtless Use of Generative Artificial Intelligence and College Students’ Self-Directed Learning: A Multi-Group SEM Analysis of Gender Differences Hui Zhao1, Huijuan Gu2* 1 School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Zhoukou Normal University, Zhoukou, China 2 School of Foreign Languages, Zhoukou Normal University, Zhoukou, China *Corresponding Author: Huijuan Gu E-mail address: valley2012@126.com Abstract ：In recent years, generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) technologies have developed rapidly and have been increasingly integrated into educational settings. While students benefit from the convenience and efficiency provided by these tools, a growing tendency toward the thoughtless use of Generative Artificial Intelligence (TUGA) has emerged. However, the specific pathways and underlying mechanisms through which such thoughtless use influences undergraduates’ self-directed learning (SDL) remain insufficiently examined. Grounded in Social Cognitive Theory (SCT), this study surveyed 487 undergraduate students from Henan Province, China, using a snowball sampling approach to collect self-reported data. Structural equation modeling (SEM) was employed to investigate the relationships among the TUGA, self-efficacy (SE), motivation (MOV), and SDL. The results demonstrate that the TUGA exerts a significant negative effect on undergraduates’ SDL. Furthermore, SE and MOV partially",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,2,"self-reported data. Structural equation modeling (SEM) was employed to investigate the relationships among the TUGA, self-efficacy (SE), motivation (MOV), and SDL. The results demonstrate that the TUGA exerts a significant negative effect on undergraduates’ SDL. Furthermore, SE and MOV partially mediate this relationship. Multi- group analysis further reveals substantial gender differences in the impact of the TUGA on SDL, MOV, and SE. By elucidating the internal mechanisms through which the TUGA undermines undergraduates’ SDL, this study provides both theoretical and empirical contributions, offering practical implications for higher education institutions seeking to promote responsible AI use and strengthen students’ SDL. Keywords: generative artificial intelligence; thoughtless use; self-efficacy; motivation; self- directed learning 1. Introduction Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is rapidly transforming the learning practices of university students. Represented by tools such as ChatGPT, GenAI applications—owing to their powerful capabilities in content generation and human–computer interaction—have become important auxiliary resources supporting students’ self-directed learning (SDL) (Li et al., 2023). Recent studies indicate that more than 70% of university students have used GenAI tools to assist with academic tasks, with approximately 40% reporting at",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,2,"and human–computer interaction—have become important auxiliary resources supporting students’ self-directed learning (SDL) (Li et al., 2023). Recent studies indicate that more than 70% of university students have used GenAI tools to assist with academic tasks, with approximately 40% reporting at least weekly use (Ajalo et al., 2025; Sousa & Cardoso, 2025). By providing extensive learning resources, personalized learning recommendations, and efficient problem-solving support, GenAI tools can enhance learning efficiency and broaden students’ academic perspectives (Mittal et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024; Yan et al., 2024). However, the integration of GenAI into learning processes does not always produce positive outcomes. In practical learning contexts, students’ interactions with AI systems sometimes involve irrational or unconscious usage patterns. One emerging concern is the thoughtless use of generative artificial intelligence (TUGA), which refers to situations in which users adopt AI-generated answers, texts, or ideas without critically evaluating, verifying, or deeply understanding the outputs (Fischer et al., 2024). Some students may become overly dependent on AI tools and unreflectively copy generated content into academic work. Such practices may hinder the development of critical thinking",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,2,"ideas without critically evaluating, verifying, or deeply understanding the outputs (Fischer et al., 2024). Some students may become overly dependent on AI tools and unreflectively copy generated content into academic work. Such practices may hinder the development of critical thinking and problem- solving skills and may also raise concerns about academic integrity (Gerlich, 2025; Zhai et al., 2024). Although GenAI technologies provide significant learning support, excessive or unreflective reliance on these tools may weaken students’ cognitive engagement in the learning process. ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",89
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,3,"Several related constructs have been proposed to describe problematic patterns of AI use. For example, Fan et al. (2024) introduced the concept of unreflective reliance on ChatGPT, which highlights learners ’ tendency to accept AI-generated responses without sufficient metacognitive monitoring. Similarly, Sardi et al. (2025) discussed excessive dependence on generative AI, emphasizing behavioral overreliance on AI tools during learning tasks. Other studies have examined the general use of generative AI in educational contexts (Li et al., 2024), which mainly captures the extent or frequency of AI usage rather than problematic usage patterns. In contrast, the concept of TUGA proposed by Hou et al. (2025) emphasizes a low-reflection cognitive processing pattern in which users automatically adopt AI-generated outputs without critical evaluation or verification. Compared with general AI usage constructs, TUGA more directly captures a maladaptive interaction pattern between learners and AI systems and is therefore particularly suitable for examining potential negative consequences for learning outcomes such as SDL. Although Hou et al. (2025) reported that the proportion of TUGA ranges from approximately 10% to 16.7%, this behavioral pattern may still have",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,3,"systems and is therefore particularly suitable for examining potential negative consequences for learning outcomes such as SDL. Although Hou et al. (2025) reported that the proportion of TUGA ranges from approximately 10% to 16.7%, this behavioral pattern may still have disproportionately negative consequences for students’ learning processes. SDL has long been recognized as a key competency in higher education. Existing research on SDL has gradually shifted from a focus on basic cognitive skills toward higher- order thinking and contextualized learning practices. From a theoretical perspective, scholars have examined the role of digital technologies in shaping SDL through multiple frameworks, including the Technology Acceptance Model (Mirriahi et al., 2025), Self- Determination Theory (Chiu, 2024), Self-Regulated Learning Theory (Chang et al., 2023), and Self-Directed Learning Theory (Li et al., 2024). Within these perspectives, technology use may influence SDL through mediating mechanisms such as cognitive processes, motivation, and environmental conditions (Efklides & Schwartz, 2024; Xu et al., 2025). Among these frameworks, Social Cognitive Theory (SCT) provides a particularly useful lens for understanding learning behavior in technology-mediated environments. SCT emphasizes the triadic reciprocal interaction",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,3,"as cognitive processes, motivation, and environmental conditions (Efklides & Schwartz, 2024; Xu et al., 2025). Among these frameworks, Social Cognitive Theory (SCT) provides a particularly useful lens for understanding learning behavior in technology-mediated environments. SCT emphasizes the triadic reciprocal interaction among personal cognition, behavioral patterns, and environmental factors, suggesting that learning outcomes are shaped by the dynamic interplay between individuals and their technological environments. Prior studies applying SCT in educational contexts have highlighted the central roles of self-efficacy (SE) and motivation (MOV) in shaping students’ learning engagement and autonomous learning behaviors (Gieure et al., 2020). Methodologically, these relationships have often been examined using structural equation modeling and experimental approaches to explore how psychological factors influence learning motivation and SDL (Guibin & Qianyu, 2021; Lim et al., 2020; Mazandarani, 2024). Despite these advances, several important research gaps remain. First, existing studies have largely emphasized the enabling and positive effects of educational technologies, while relatively little attention has been paid to the potential negative consequences of maladaptive technology use behaviors such as TUGA. Second, although gender has been identified as an important",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,3,"have largely emphasized the enabling and positive effects of educational technologies, while relatively little attention has been paid to the potential negative consequences of maladaptive technology use behaviors such as TUGA. Second, although gender has been identified as an important moderator of technology-related psychological processes and learning behaviors (Emre et al., 2023), the ways in which gender shapes the relationships between AI usage behaviors, psychological mechanisms, and SDL remain insufficiently understood. Against this backdrop, the present study aims to examine how the TUGA influences undergraduates’ SDL and the psychological mechanisms underlying this relationship. Specifically, this study addresses three key questions: (1) Does the TUGA significantly influence undergraduates’ SDL? (2) Do psychological factors such as SE and MOV mediate the relationship between TUGA and SDL? (3) Are there significant gender differences in these relationships? This study makes three main contributions. First, by introducing the concept of TUGA into SDL research, it shifts the focus from the predominantly positive narrative surrounding educational technologies toward a more balanced examination of their potential cognitive and motivational risks. Second, by integrating SE and MOV within",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,3,"by introducing the concept of TUGA into SDL research, it shifts the focus from the predominantly positive narrative surrounding educational technologies toward a more balanced examination of their potential cognitive and motivational risks. Second, by integrating SE and MOV within an SCT framework, the study clarifies the psychological pathways through which AI-related behaviors influence ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",62
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,4,"learning outcomes. In particular, the identification of the sequential mediation pathway from SE to MOV provides new insights into how psychological resources shape autonomous learning in AI-mediated environments. Third, through multi-group analysis, this study examines the moderating role of gender, offering a novel perspective for understanding individual differences in human–AI collaborative learning contexts. The remainder of this article is structured as follows. Section 2 presents the literature review. Section 3 develops the research hypotheses and proposes the theoretical model. Section 4 describes the methodology. Section 5 reports the empirical results. Section 6 discusses the findings and their implications, and the final section concludes the study. 2. Literature Review 2.1 Social Cognitive Theory To establish the theoretical foundation of this study, Social Cognitive Theory (SCT) is adopted as the core analytical framework. Initially proposed by the psychologist Bandura (1986), SCT conceptualizes human functioning as the dynamic interaction among three core dimensions: personal factors, behavior, and environment. Specifically, the personal dimension encompasses individuals’ cognitions, beliefs, emotions, and physiological states; the behavioral dimension refers to individuals’ actions, decision-making processes, and self- regulatory capacities;",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,4,"human functioning as the dynamic interaction among three core dimensions: personal factors, behavior, and environment. Specifically, the personal dimension encompasses individuals’ cognitions, beliefs, emotions, and physiological states; the behavioral dimension refers to individuals’ actions, decision-making processes, and self- regulatory capacities; and the environmental dimension includes both physical settings and sociocultural contexts. These dimensions do not operate in isolation but form a cyclical system of triadic reciprocal determinism, whereby personal attitudes and beliefs influence learning choices (behavior), behavioral outcomes modify environmental feedback, and environmental conditions, in turn, further shape individuals’ cognition and perceptions (Bandura, 1986). With the continuous evolution of technological contexts, the application trajectory of SCT has undergone three key stages: from explaining traditional learning behaviors, to elucidating motivational mechanisms in digital learning, and ultimately to human–AI co- regulation in the age of intelligent agents. This developmental trajectory not only reflects the theoretical extensibility of SCT but also highlights its robust explanatory power across diverse technological contexts. In its early stage, SCT was primarily employed to explain the cognitive, behavioral, and motivational mechanisms underlying learning in traditional educational environments. Research",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,4,"reflects the theoretical extensibility of SCT but also highlights its robust explanatory power across diverse technological contexts. In its early stage, SCT was primarily employed to explain the cognitive, behavioral, and motivational mechanisms underlying learning in traditional educational environments. Research during this period mainly focused on classic topics, including academic MOV, learning performance, and career development (Burney, 2008; Schunk & Usher, 2012; Zikic & Saks, 2009). For instance, Schunk and Usher (2012) demonstrated that students’ mathematics SE significantly predicts their use of problem- solving strategies and academic achievement, underscoring the central role of self-beliefs in learning processes. Additionally, goal setting and self-monitoring were identified as crucial mechanisms for promoting deep learning and sustaining learning MOV (Zimmerman, 2002). With the widespread adoption of Internet technologies and the rise of digital learning, the application scope of SCT has progressively expanded to areas such as blended learning, MOOCs, digital literacy, and health-related behaviors (El-Sayad et al., 2021; Kuo et al., 2021; Panigrahi et al., 2022; Wong et al., 2021). In particular, the large-scale implementation of online teaching during the COVID-19 pandemic stimulated a",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,4,"such as blended learning, MOOCs, digital literacy, and health-related behaviors (El-Sayad et al., 2021; Kuo et al., 2021; Panigrahi et al., 2022; Wong et al., 2021). In particular, the large-scale implementation of online teaching during the COVID-19 pandemic stimulated a series of empirical studies grounded in this theoretical framework. For example, drawing on SCT, El-Sayad et al. (2021) investigated the mechanisms through which online learning engagement influenced learning satisfaction among Egyptian university students during the pandemic. Their findings indicated that teaching presence and academic SE were critical factors in enhancing students’ behavioral and emotional engagement, with emotional engagement subsequently emerging as a central construct influencing learning satisfaction. Research at this stage not only validated the applicability of SCT in non-traditional learning environments but also deepened scholarly understanding of the dynamic interplay among technological environments, individual cognition, and behavioral regulation. In recent years, with the rapid advancement of artificial intelligence technologies, the application of SCT has been further extended to emerging topics such as digital health interventions, sustainable development, and human–machine collaboration (Ghorbanzadeh et al., 2025; Maleki et al., 2025; Peng",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,4,"recent years, with the rapid advancement of artificial intelligence technologies, the application of SCT has been further extended to emerging topics such as digital health interventions, sustainable development, and human–machine collaboration (Ghorbanzadeh et al., 2025; Maleki et al., 2025; Peng et al., 2025; Shahzad et al., 2025). Researchers have ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",57
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,5,"increasingly focused on GenAI as a novel environmental factor and examined how it interacts with the cognitive mechanisms and learning behaviors of university students. For example, Shahzad et al. (2025) reported that generative AI technologies—huge language models (LLMs)—based tools, such as ChatGPT and conversational chatbots, significantly influence students’ learning performance through mechanisms including SE, perceived fairness and ethics, and creativity. Taken together, SCT offers a robust and integrative theoretical lens for understanding the psychological and behavioral mechanisms underlying human–AI interaction in AI-mediated learning environments. Despite the continuous expansion of its application domains and the substantial progress achieved, the limitations of SCT have become increasingly evident as technological environments grow more complex and emerging technologies evolve at a rapid pace. First, within its traditional framework, SCT has broadly conceptualized environmental factors in terms of physical settings and conventional sociocultural contexts. However, in technology- rich environments dominated by GenAI, technology itself exhibits dynamic, autonomous, and interactive characteristics. Features such as real-time feedback and personalized content generation, enabled by large language models, extend beyond the static conception of the environment embedded in classical",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,5,"in technology- rich environments dominated by GenAI, technology itself exhibits dynamic, autonomous, and interactive characteristics. Features such as real-time feedback and personalized content generation, enabled by large language models, extend beyond the static conception of the environment embedded in classical SCT, rendering the theory somewhat lagging in its explanation of the evolving “technology–cognition–behavior” interaction processes (Amemiya et al., 2022). Second, existing SCT-based studies have predominantly focused on individual-level variables, such as SE and self-regulation, while investigations into the underlying mechanisms of gender differences remain fragmented. In particular, there is a notable lack of systematic theoretical support for explaining differentiated pathways across gender groups in terms of cognitive beliefs, behavioral decision-making, and environmental perceptions within the context of GenAI use (Chan, 2022). Therefore, to address the limitations above, SCT requires theoretical extension so that it can better accommodate the novel technological environments constructed by GenAI and provide more systematic explanations for phenomena such as gender differences within these contexts. Building on these theoretical constraints, the present study incorporates the TUGA into the SCT framework to examine the role of this behavior",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,5,"environments constructed by GenAI and provide more systematic explanations for phenomena such as gender differences within these contexts. Building on these theoretical constraints, the present study incorporates the TUGA into the SCT framework to examine the role of this behavior within the triadic interaction system. Specifically, the triadic reciprocal model of SCT enables the integrative consideration of thoughtless use behavior, personal factors such as students’ SE and MOV, and the environmental conditions constituted by GenAI, forming a dynamic system that elucidates how these elements jointly influence SDL. Moreover, SCT’s emphasis on individual differences in cognition and beliefs provides a robust theoretical basis for interpreting the gender differences observed in this study, thereby offering insights into the differentiated patterns of GenAI use and SDL across male and female students. Accordingly, under an extended SCT framework, this study employs multi-group analysis to systematically investigate the effects of the TUGA on SDL and the associated gender differences. By uncovering the underlying mechanisms of these effects, the study aims to contribute meaningful theoretical insights and empirical evidence for the application and advancement of SCT",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,5,"systematically investigate the effects of the TUGA on SDL and the associated gender differences. By uncovering the underlying mechanisms of these effects, the study aims to contribute meaningful theoretical insights and empirical evidence for the application and advancement of SCT in emerging technology-enhanced learning environments. 2.2 Self-Directed Learning SDL refers to an individual’s capacity to independently, proactively, and consciously accomplish learning tasks and is regarded as a core competence in modern education and lifelong learning (Wei, 2021). Existing research on SDL spans multiple domains, including online learning (Doo & Zhu, 2023), metacognition and learning (Marantika, 2021), and self- regulated learning (Papamitsiou & Economides, 2019). Prior studies have examined this construct from diverse disciplinary perspectives. For instance, research in the fields of metacognition and learning strategies emphasizes learners’ monitoring of their own cognitive processes and the application of optimization strategies. In contrast, self-regulated learning research conceptualizes SDL as a dynamic, cyclical system encompassing goal setting, process monitoring, and motivational regulation. With the continued evolution of technological environments, the online learning literature has increasingly focused on how features of online learning environments—such",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,5,"contrast, self-regulated learning research conceptualizes SDL as a dynamic, cyclical system encompassing goal setting, process monitoring, and motivational regulation. With the continued evolution of technological environments, the online learning literature has increasingly focused on how features of online learning environments—such as platform functionalities, resource formats, and interaction patterns—influence learners’ ability to autonomously set goals, manage ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",63
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,6,"learning processes, and evaluate learning outcomes, as well as how instructional design optimization can enhance SDL efficiency. In recent years, artificial intelligence has emerged as a new research frontier in this domain. Ng and Sing (2025) noted that artificial intelligence can exert a profound influence on students’ SDL by providing personalized learning resources and enhancing learners’ autonomy in the learning process. Collectively, these studies elucidate the formation mechanisms and influencing factors of SDL from multiple perspectives, offering a solid foundation for understanding its development across diverse learning contexts. Although SDL is closely related to self-regulated learning (SRL), the two constructs emphasize different aspects of learner autonomy. SRL primarily focuses on learners’ cognitive and metacognitive regulation processes within specific learning tasks, including planning, monitoring, and strategy adjustment. In contrast, SDL represents a broader construct that refers to learners’ overall capacity to independently initiate, manage, and evaluate their learning activities across different learning contexts. In this sense, SRL can be regarded as an important process-level component within the broader framework of SDL. The present study adopts SDL as the focal outcome variable",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,6,"independently initiate, manage, and evaluate their learning activities across different learning contexts. In this sense, SRL can be regarded as an important process-level component within the broader framework of SDL. The present study adopts SDL as the focal outcome variable because it captures learners’ comprehensive autonomy in AI-supported learning environments rather than only their task- level regulatory strategies. Current research on SDL has drawn on a variety of theoretical frameworks, including Self-Determination Theory (Earl, 2019), Cognitive Learning Theory (Qi, 2012), Self- Regulated Learning Theory (Boulahmel et al., 2025), and Social Cognitive Theory (Ponton & Rhea, 2006). Among these theoretical perspectives, SCT offers a remarkably comprehensive analytical lens by emphasizing the triadic reciprocal determinism among personal factors, behavior, and environmental influences. For example, grounded in SCT, Ponton and Rhea (2006) empirically demonstrated that learners’ SE and levels of self-regulatory strategies have a significant positive effect on their SDL. A key strength of SCT lies in its capacity to move beyond purely psychological or environmental determinism, providing a dynamic explanation of how internal beliefs, external influences, and learning behaviors continuously interact to",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,6,"a significant positive effect on their SDL. A key strength of SCT lies in its capacity to move beyond purely psychological or environmental determinism, providing a dynamic explanation of how internal beliefs, external influences, and learning behaviors continuously interact to shape the development of SDL jointly. Existing research has systematically examined the driving mechanisms of SDL from two complementary dimensions: internal individual factors and external environmental factors. First, internal personal factors serve as the intrinsic engine of SDL, encompassing elements such as intrinsic interest (Howard et al., 2021), goal setting (Wei, 2021), and self-motivation (Marantika, 2021). Among these factors, SE and intrinsic goal orientation provide learners with the initial impetus and sustained engagement in learning activities. At the same time, metacognitive abilities enable them to plan, monitor progress, and evaluate outcomes, thereby facilitating active regulation of the learning process. Second, external environmental factors provide the social and behavioral supports that promote SDL. These factors encompass teacher support (Mammadov & Schroeder, 2023), peer interaction (Rajib & Jahan, 2024), curriculum design (Inayati et al., 2021), technological platforms (Didane, 2025), as well as",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,6,"external environmental factors provide the social and behavioral supports that promote SDL. These factors encompass teacher support (Mammadov & Schroeder, 2023), peer interaction (Rajib & Jahan, 2024), curriculum design (Inayati et al., 2021), technological platforms (Didane, 2025), as well as learning strategies related to information access (Dai, 2025) and time management (Xiulin et al., 2022). By providing guidance, resources, and opportunities for practice, these external elements collectively shape and influence learners’ self-regulatory behaviors. Taken together, SDL emerges from the dynamic interaction between individuals’ internal motivational drivers and external environmental supports. This integrative framework provides a robust theoretical foundation for further investigating how specific contextual variables—such as GenAI—affect the development of SDL. However, current research on SDL is subject to several notable limitations. First, existing studies have largely overlooked the potential negative impact of the TUGA as a specific behavioral tendency on SDL. Research examining the educational effects of technology has predominantly focused on the enabling and supportive roles of GenAI, with limited critical attention paid to how excessive reliance on such tools may undermine students’ metacognitive processes and self-regulatory capacities.",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,6,"on SDL. Research examining the educational effects of technology has predominantly focused on the enabling and supportive roles of GenAI, with limited critical attention paid to how excessive reliance on such tools may undermine students’ metacognitive processes and self-regulatory capacities. Second, the existing literature has paid insufficient attention to gender as a key individual difference variable when examining relevant influencing factors. As a result, it has failed to systematically uncover potential ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",79
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,7,"gender-based differences in technology use patterns, self-regulatory strategies, and intrinsic MOV, thereby constraining the generalizability of findings and the precision of educational interventions. By focusing on the relationship between the TUGA and undergraduates’ SDL, and by incorporating gender as a moderating variable through multi-group analysis, this study seeks to address these research gaps and provide more nuanced and differentiated empirical evidence for understanding the development of SDL in the era of artificial intelligence. 3. Hypothesis 3.1 Thoughtless Use of GenAI and Self-Directed Learning Existing research indicates that the TUGA is a significant negative predictor of SDL. For example, Fan et al. (2024) examined university students’ performance on writing tasks under different forms of learning support (ChatGPT, human experts, writing-check tools, and no support). They found that unreflective reliance on ChatGPT during writing led to metacognitive laziness and reduced self-monitoring, thereby weakening students’ SDL. Similarly, Sardi et al. (2025) reported that excessive dependence on GenAI may deprive some students of opportunities for independent thinking, consequently hindering the development of their SDL. Based on the existing literature, this study proposes the following",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,7,"weakening students’ SDL. Similarly, Sardi et al. (2025) reported that excessive dependence on GenAI may deprive some students of opportunities for independent thinking, consequently hindering the development of their SDL. Based on the existing literature, this study proposes the following hypothesis: H1: The TUGA has a significant negative effect on undergraduates’ SDL. 3.2 Thoughtless Use of GenAI, Self-Efficacy, and Self-Directed Learning Ability SE refers to an individual’s belief in their ability to perform specific tasks and achieve desired goals successfully (Lee et al., 2020). Prior research has shown that the TUGA exerts a significant negative influence on SE. For instance, Zhang et al. (2024) found that TUGA not only directly undermines students’ academic SE but also produces additional detrimental effects by fostering cognitive laziness, inhibiting creativity, and weakening critical and independent thinking abilities. Moreover, Li et al. (2025) conducted a quasi-experimental study among physical education majors comparing programming learning outcomes with and without the use of generative AI. Their findings indicated that although the unreflective use of GenAI could superficially enhance students’ programming SE by enabling rapid problem- solving, this",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,7,"quasi-experimental study among physical education majors comparing programming learning outcomes with and without the use of generative AI. Their findings indicated that although the unreflective use of GenAI could superficially enhance students’ programming SE by enabling rapid problem- solving, this apparent improvement was primarily grounded in dependence on external tools rather than genuine skill acquisition. As a result, such cognitive offloading may impair long- term knowledge mastery and confidence, ultimately leading to a decline in SE. Based on the existing evidence, this study proposes the following hypothesis: H2: The TUGA has a significant negative effect on undergraduates’ SE. Existing literature indicates that SE is a significant positive predictor of SDL. For example, Ou et al. (2025) found that AI-assisted practice applications enhanced students’ SE by providing support mechanisms such as real-time feedback, goal setting, and self- monitoring, which in turn significantly promoted their SDL. In addition, Liang et al. (2023) examined the effects of students’ interactions with GenAI on learning achievement. They reported that SE enhanced learning achievement by increasing students’ levels of cognitive engagement. Cognitive engagement, which reflects deeper",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,7,"significantly promoted their SDL. In addition, Liang et al. (2023) examined the effects of students’ interactions with GenAI on learning achievement. They reported that SE enhanced learning achievement by increasing students’ levels of cognitive engagement. Cognitive engagement, which reflects deeper thinking and greater involvement in problem-solving during the learning process, indirectly strengthens students’ SDL. Based on the existing evidence, this study proposes the following hypotheses: H3: Undergraduates’ SE has a significant positive effect on SDL. H4: SE mediates the relationship between the TUGA and SDL among undergraduates. 3.3 Thoughtless Use of GenAI, Motivation, and Self-Directed Learning MOV refers to the psychological processes and driving forces that energize individuals to initiate behavior and persist in striving toward specific goals (Schunk & DiBenedetto, 2020). Existing studies suggest a significant negative association between TUGA and MOV. For instance, Dong et al. (2025) found, in their investigation of the misuse of GenAI in programming instruction, that TUGA undermines students’ intrinsic learning MOV by weakening their SDL and reducing task challenge and feelings of accomplishment. Similarly, Im et al. (2025) examined how artificial intelligence influences",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,7,"in their investigation of the misuse of GenAI in programming instruction, that TUGA undermines students’ intrinsic learning MOV by weakening their SDL and reducing task challenge and feelings of accomplishment. Similarly, Im et al. (2025) examined how artificial intelligence influences students’ learning MOV in the digital era. They demonstrated that TUGA leads to declines in critical thinking ability, diminishes learners’ MOV for autonomous analysis and creative thinking, and consequently directly reduces learning MOV. ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",81
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,8,"Based on the existing literature, this study proposes the following hypothesis: H5: The TUGA has a significant negative effect on undergraduates’ MOV. Existing research demonstrates that MOV is a significant positive predictor of undergraduates’ SDL. For example, Lin et al. (2024) investigated the impact of autonomous MOV on knowledge-based short-video learning contexts. They found that autonomous MOV— particularly intrinsic MOV—significantly enhances learners’ SDL by strengthening learning interest, goal identification, and self-driven engagement. Similarly, Siziba et al. (2025) investigated the mechanisms through which learning MOV influences SDL among students in township schools in South Africa. Their findings indicated that MOV can indirectly promote the development of SDL through coordinated support from family, school, and broader social systems. Based on the existing evidence, this study proposes the following hypotheses: H6: Undergraduates’ MOV has a significant positive effect on SDL. H7: MOV mediates the relationship between the TUGA and SDL among undergraduates. 3.4 Thoughtless Use of GenAI, Self-Efficacy, Motivation, and Self-Directed Learning A growing body of research has identified a significant positive association between SE and MOV in the context of GenAI use.",180
