DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,8,"the existing literature, this study proposes the following hypotheses: H8: Undergraduates’ SE has a significant positive effect on MOV. H9: SE and MOV jointly mediate the relationship between the TUGA and SDL among undergraduates. 3.5 Gender Differences in the Relationships among the Thoughtless Use of GenAI, Self-Efficacy, Motivation, and Self-Directed Learning Existing research suggests that gender plays a significant role in the relationship between the careless use of GenAI and undergraduates’ SE. For example, Cachero et al. (2025) examined gender bias in university students’ perceptions of artificial intelligence, perceived impacts, and attitudes toward AI support. Their findings revealed that female students reported significantly lower self-assessments than male students in terms of AI- related knowledge, application skills, and confidence. Such lower self-perceptions may lead female students to experience reduced confidence when engaging with AI technologies. Similarly, Møgelvang et al. (2024) investigated gender differences in the use of GenAI chatbots in higher education, examining their associated characteristics and effects. The results showed that male students were more likely to view generative AI as a tool for enhancing efficiency and to use it",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,8,"gender differences in the use of GenAI chatbots in higher education, examining their associated characteristics and effects. The results showed that male students were more likely to view generative AI as a tool for enhancing efficiency and to use it extensively to strengthen their technological SE. In contrast, female students tended to place greater emphasis on the credibility and critical use of GenAI. While this orientation helps preserve cognitive autonomy, it may simultaneously constrain the accumulation of technological confidence. Based on the existing literature, this study proposes the following hypothesis: H10: The effect of the TUGA on undergraduates’ SE differs significantly by gender. Existing studies suggest that gender moderates the relationship between the TUGA and undergraduates’ MOV. For example, Xia et al. (2022) demonstrated that, in AI-assisted learning contexts, the pathways through which autonomy, competence, and relatedness influence SDL are significantly moderated by gender. Specifically, when female students experience lower satisfaction of autonomy and competence needs, motivational variables exhibit a more substantial predictive effect on SDL, whereas male students display different pathway patterns. In addition, drawing on Diffusion of Innovations",180
