DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,9,"Human Research Ethics Committee of Zhoukou Normal University (Approval No. ZNU-2025-05-0007) and complied with the Declaration of Helsinki. The survey was conducted on the Wenjuanxing platform (https://www.wjx.cn/) from June 10 to August 12, 2025. Wenjuanxing was selected because it enables the rapid collection of large-scale data, supports multi-platform distribution, and allows for real-time monitoring of survey responses. A snowball sampling method was employed during the data collection process to ensure both the quantity and quality of responses, with participants encouraged to invite other eligible respondents to participate. Additionally, the platform’s response time- tracking function was utilized to monitor and assess data quality. Respondents who completed the survey and provided valid responses received a random cash incentive. After the necessary preparatory procedures were completed, data were collected from 598 undergraduate students across four universities in Henan Province, China. To ensure sample quality, this study followed the guidelines recommended by Wu et al. (2020) and applied two criteria to screen the questionnaires. First, a pilot test indicated that the questionnaire usually required 1–5 minutes to complete. Responses completed in less than 60",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,9,"quality, this study followed the guidelines recommended by Wu et al. (2020) and applied two criteria to screen the questionnaires. First, a pilot test indicated that the questionnaire usually required 1–5 minutes to complete. Responses completed in less than 60 seconds were considered to reflect careless or rushed participation and were therefore deemed invalid. Second, questionnaires in which all items were answered identically were also excluded from further analysis. Following this rigorous screening process, 111 invalid ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",84
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,10,"questionnaires were removed, resulting in a final sample of 487 valid responses for subsequent analysis. In the proposed research model, the maximum number of arrows pointing to any endogenous latent construct was three. According to Hair (2021), to achieve a minimum explanatory power of R² = 0.10 at the 1% significance level, a minimum sample size of 176 is required. The final sample of 487 participants in this study substantially exceeds this threshold, thereby ensuring the robustness and reliability of the empirical results. In the context of this study, GenAI tools commonly used by participants included conversational AI systems such as ChatGPT and similar large language model–based platforms. Students typically used these tools for various academic purposes, including generating ideas for assignments, obtaining explanations of complex concepts, assisting with writing tasks, and solving academic problems. These usage patterns reflect the increasing integration of generative AI technologies into students’ everyday learning environments. From the perspective of SCT, such AI-supported learning environments constitute an important contextual factor that may shape learners’ cognitive beliefs and behavioral patterns, thereby influencing their self-directed learning processes.",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,10,"increasing integration of generative AI technologies into students’ everyday learning environments. From the perspective of SCT, such AI-supported learning environments constitute an important contextual factor that may shape learners’ cognitive beliefs and behavioral patterns, thereby influencing their self-directed learning processes. Among the 487 valid questionnaires, 105 respondents were male (21.6%), and 382 were female (78.4%). In terms of academic year, 122 participants were freshmen (25.1%), 124 were sophomores (25.5%), 207 were juniors (42.5%), and 34 were seniors (7.0%). With respect to disciplinary background, 222 students majored in the humanities (45.6%), 72 in the social sciences (14.8%), and 193 in the natural sciences (39.6%) (Table 1). This representative sample provides a solid foundation for examining the effects of the TUGA on undergraduates’ SDL. The diversity and size of the sample contribute to the generalizability of the findings to a broader undergraduate population within the study context. Table 1. Demographic Characteristics of the Sample (N = 487) Demographic Variable Category Frequency Percentage(%) Male 105 21.6 Gender Female 382 78.4 Freshman 122 25.1 Sophomore 124 25.5 Junior 207 42.5 Academic Year Senior 34",180
