DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,10,"Compared with a 7-point Likert scale, a 5- point Likert scale has been shown to offer notable advantages in enhancing scale reliability and validity (Finstad, 2010), reducing response bias, and increasing the sensitivity of statistical analyses (Wakita et al., 2012). Accordingly, all constructs were measured using a 5-point Likert scale, with response options ranging from (1) “strongly disagree” to (5) “strongly agree.” The TUGA scale was adapted from Hou et al. (2025). It was designed to assess undergraduates’ automatic reliance on generative AI during learning, particularly their lack of purposefulness and reflective engagement when using such tools. The scale consists of four items (e.g., “I copy learning tasks or problem statements into generative AI to seek answers or ideas”). The scale demonstrated good reliability, with a Cronbach’s alpha coefficient of 0.899. ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",139
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,11,"The SE scale, adapted from Kim and Kim (2025), was used to assess undergraduates’ beliefs regarding their learning capabilities, problem-solving abilities, and perceived control over learning outcomes during the process of SDL. The scale comprises four items (e.g., “I believe that I can independently master complex learning content”). The reliability of the scale was satisfactory, with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.85. The SDL scale, adapted from Han (2022), was used to evaluate undergraduates’ self- management skills, goal-setting capacity, use of learning strategies, and levels of self- monitoring in learning. The scale consists of nine items (e.g., “I actively check my understanding of the learning content to ensure that I have grasped the key points”). The scale demonstrated excellent reliability, with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.941. The MOV scale, adapted from González-Mohíno et al. (2024), was designed to measure undergraduates’ levels of intrinsic and extrinsic MOV in SDL, including their subjective perceptions of learning interest, perceived value, achievement MOV, and sensitivity to external rewards and sanctions. The scale comprises five items (e.g., “I am curious about and interested in the content",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,11,"of intrinsic and extrinsic MOV in SDL, including their subjective perceptions of learning interest, perceived value, achievement MOV, and sensitivity to external rewards and sanctions. The scale comprises five items (e.g., “I am curious about and interested in the content of self-directed learning”). The scale demonstrated good reliability, with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.909. The full wording of all measurement items used in this study is presented in Appendix A. 4.3 Data Analysis This study adopted a two-stage analytical approach to test the hypotheses and develop the predictive model. In the first stage, partial least squares structural equation modeling (PLS-SEM) was employed to identify the linear relationships between exogenous and endogenous variables. Considering the prediction-oriented objective of this study and the relatively complex model incorporating multiple mediating mechanisms and multi-group analysis, PLS-SEM was deemed an appropriate analytical approach. PLS-SEM is particularly suitable for research focusing on prediction and variance explanation of endogenous constructs, whereas covariance-based SEM (CB-SEM) is typically used for theory confirmation and model fit evaluation (Hair et al., 2017; Hair et al., 2022; Sarstedt et al., 2022). In",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,11,"particularly suitable for research focusing on prediction and variance explanation of endogenous constructs, whereas covariance-based SEM (CB-SEM) is typically used for theory confirmation and model fit evaluation (Hair et al., 2017; Hair et al., 2022; Sarstedt et al., 2022). In addition, PLS-SEM offers methodological advantages in handling non-normally distributed data and complex research models (Hair, 2021). The present study is based on a sample of 487 participants and includes four constructs measured by 22 items, forming a relatively complex analytical framework (Schuberth, 2023). Therefore, PLS-SEM was considered a robust and appropriate technique for the analysis. In the second stage, to more thoroughly examine the moderating role of gender, this study incorporated multi-group analysis (MGA). MGA is an effective technique for assessing moderating effects within research models and is widely used to test differences across categorical variables, such as gender and nationality (Cheah et al., 2020). Moreover, prior research has highlighted the strengths of MGA in uncovering group heterogeneity and in analyzing complex models with relatively small samples (Cheah et al., 2023; Hair et al., 2019). By integrating SEM with MGA,",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,11,"nationality (Cheah et al., 2020). Moreover, prior research has highlighted the strengths of MGA in uncovering group heterogeneity and in analyzing complex models with relatively small samples (Cheah et al., 2023; Hair et al., 2019). By integrating SEM with MGA, this study enhances the depth and precision of the data analysis, enabling a more comprehensive understanding of the effects of the TUGA on undergraduates’ SDL, as well as the moderating role of gender. 5. Results 5.1 Measurement Model The measurement model was evaluated using the following criteria. Step 1: Indicator reliability: Indicator reliability was assessed by examining outer loadings, which should be equal to or greater than 0.70 (Hair, 2014). Step 2: Internal consistency reliability: Internal consistency reliability was evaluated using two indices: Cronbach’s alpha (α), McDonald’s omega (ω) and composite reliability (CR). Cronbach’s alpha is the traditional measure of internal consistency, whereas McDonald’s omega provides a more accurate estimate of reliability under congeneric measurement models. Composite reliability is widely recommended in PLS-SEM because it does not assume equal indicator loadings. For all three indices, values of 0.70 or higher",180
