DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,15,"effects of TUGA on SE, MOV, and SDL among undergraduates (Sarstedt et al., 2011). This approach is particularly robust in handling unequal variances and unequal sample sizes between groups, thereby providing more reliable statistical inferences (Henseler, Hubona, et al., 2016). Table 12 presents the results of the gender-based path difference tests. The results indicate significant gender differences in the effects of the TUGA on MOV, SE, and SDL. Specifically, the negative impact of thoughtless use on learning MOV was significantly more substantial among male students than among female students, suggesting that female students’ learning MOV is more susceptible to the influence of TUGA. Conversely, the effects of thoughtless use on SE and SDL were significantly weaker among male students than among female students, indicating that male students’ SE and SDL are more vulnerable to the detrimental effects of thoughtless technology use. Table 12. Multi-Group Analysis Constructs Mean-Original difference (male-female) Mean- Permutationmean difference (male-female) 95% CI (LL) 95% CI (UL) Permutatio n p value Equal mean values MOV 0.056 0.001 -0.219 0.221 .621 Yes SDL 0.034 0.001 -0.218 0.220 .760 Yes",180
