DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,16,"Hypothesi s Path β Difference (Male-Female) t value p value H10 TUGA → SE -0.21 3.03 .005 H11 TUGA → MOV 0.22 2.19 .028 H12 TUGA → SDL -0.16 2.82 .003 6. Discussion Grounded in SCT, this study employed SEM to examine the pathways through which the TUGA influences undergraduates’ SDL. The findings indicate that TUGA not only exerts a direct effect on SDL but also indirectly affects it through two mediating variables—MOV and SE. Moreover, significant gender differences were observed in the effects of TUGA on SE, MOV, and SDL. These results not only support the proposed hypotheses but also extend the existing literature in multiple dimensions. This section systematically addresses the research questions from both theoretical mechanism and empirical validation perspectives and further discusses the study’s theoretical contributions, practical implications, and directions for future research. First, this study found that the TUGA has a significant and direct negative effect on undergraduates’ SDL (p < 0.001), which is consistent with prior findings (Sardi et al., 2025). From an SCT perspective, thoughtless use, as a form of learning behavior, interacts",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,16,"found that the TUGA has a significant and direct negative effect on undergraduates’ SDL (p < 0.001), which is consistent with prior findings (Sardi et al., 2025). From an SCT perspective, thoughtless use, as a form of learning behavior, interacts negatively with students’ personal factors (e.g., SE and metacognitive skills) and the technology-mediated learning environment. Specifically, such behavior deprives students of critical opportunities to build academic SE through overcoming cognitive challenges, thereby weakening their core beliefs about their own learning capabilities. Moreover, it short-circuits essential metacognitive processes required for SDL—such as planning, monitoring, and reflection—preventing students from developing effective self-regulatory strategies. Finally, the immediate-feedback environment provided by generative AI behaviorally reinforces dependence on rapid answers rather than sustained cognitive engagement, thereby creating a learning environment that suppresses, rather than promotes, SDL. Accordingly, from an SCT standpoint, this instrumental form of “thoughtless use” undermines undergraduates’ SDL through its negative interactions with personal cognition and the learning environment, eroding SE, distorting learning MOV, and fostering an environment that inhibits deep thinking and metacognitive engagement. Further analysis revealed that the effect of the",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,16,"“thoughtless use” undermines undergraduates’ SDL through its negative interactions with personal cognition and the learning environment, eroding SE, distorting learning MOV, and fostering an environment that inhibits deep thinking and metacognitive engagement. Further analysis revealed that the effect of the TUGA on SDL is primarily transmitted through two significant mediating pathways: learning MOV (p < 0.001) and SE (p = 0.012). This finding is consistent with prior research (Wu et al., 2024) and can be interpreted through the lens of SCT’s triadic reciprocal determinism. Specifically, thoughtless use does not directly “eliminate” students’ SDL; instead, it erodes critical internal psychological resources that underpin autonomous learning. The capacity of generative AI to provide immediate and complete solutions deprives students of opportunities to accumulate mastery experiences through exploration and problem-solving, thereby directly undermining their belief in their ability to handle learning tasks (i.e., SE) successfully. At the same time, this readily accessible problem-solving mode transforms intrinsic learning motives (such as curiosity and interest) into instrumental pursuits of external outcomes (e.g., completing assignments quickly), leading to a substantial decline in learning MOV. Ultimately, these",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,16,"SE) successfully. At the same time, this readily accessible problem-solving mode transforms intrinsic learning motives (such as curiosity and interest) into instrumental pursuits of external outcomes (e.g., completing assignments quickly), leading to a substantial decline in learning MOV. Ultimately, these weakened personal factors—lower SE and diminished MOV—feed back into students’ behavioral patterns, reducing their willingness and confidence to set goals and persist in effort proactively. Collectively, this dynamic manifests as a decline in overall SDL. Second, the results reveal a significant sequential mediation pathway (p = 0.003), whereby the TUGA reduces SE, which in turn weakens learning MOV and ultimately diminishes SDL. This finding is consistent with the conclusions of Wu et al. (2024), who integrated the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and the Theory of Reasoned Action in their analysis. Specifically, the lack of mastery experiences gained through overcoming learning challenges via one’s own effort directly undermines students’ core beliefs in their learning competence, namely SE. SE, in turn, serves as a powerful driver of MOV; when individuals do not believe they can succeed, their willingness to invest effort in",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,16,"challenges via one’s own effort directly undermines students’ core beliefs in their learning competence, namely SE. SE, in turn, serves as a powerful driver of MOV; when individuals do not believe they can succeed, their willingness to invest effort in learning ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",49
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,17,"naturally declines. Consequently, diminished SE leads to a further erosion of both intrinsic curiosity and extrinsic goal-oriented MOV. The disintegration of this combined “belief–MOV” system strikes at the psychological foundation of the SDL process, resulting in a decline in SDL. This sequential pathway highlights the crucial bridging role of psychological factors— specifically, SE and learning MOV—in fostering learning autonomy within AI-mediated learning environments. In addition, the results reveal significant gender differences in the relationship between the TUGA and SDL (p = 0.003). Specifically, first, the effect of TUGA on MOV was significantly stronger among male undergraduates than among female undergraduates. From a SCT perspective, males are often socialized to adopt a more instrumental orientation toward technology use. Prior studies have shown that male students tend to focus more on efficiency and task completion when interacting with digital technologies, whereas female students are more likely to adopt reflective and evaluative learning strategies (Ong & Lai, 2006; Venkatesh & Morris, 2000). Thoughtless use aligns with an underlying belief that “technology equals efficiency,” thereby directly reinforcing extrinsic, task-completion– oriented MOV. In contrast, female",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,17,"students are more likely to adopt reflective and evaluative learning strategies (Ong & Lai, 2006; Venkatesh & Morris, 2000). Thoughtless use aligns with an underlying belief that “technology equals efficiency,” thereby directly reinforcing extrinsic, task-completion– oriented MOV. In contrast, female students may place greater emphasis on understanding and relational meaning in learning and thus hold a more cautious stance toward the TUGA. This internalized critical orientation weakens the link between such behavior and motivational enhancement, making male students’ MOV more susceptible to this usage pattern. Second, the effect of TUGA on SE was significantly weaker among male students than among female students, reflecting gender differences in the mechanisms through which SE is formed. Male students’ SE tends to rely more heavily on hands-on mastery experiences and a sense of direct control. Thoughtless use deprives them of deep engagement and problem-solving processes; consequently, these “successes” are difficult to internalize as genuine competence, yielding limited or even inhibitory effects on SE. Female students, by contrast, may be more likely to view AI as a supportive learning tool. Previous research has suggested that",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,17,"processes; consequently, these “successes” are difficult to internalize as genuine competence, yielding limited or even inhibitory effects on SE. Female students, by contrast, may be more likely to view AI as a supportive learning tool. Previous research has suggested that female learners often demonstrate stronger reflective engagement and adaptive learning strategies in technology-mediated learning environments (Cai et al., 2017). Successfully leveraging AI may therefore be interpreted as positive feedback, thereby maintaining or slightly enhancing confidence in completing tasks with technological assistance. Third, the effect of TUGA on SDL was significantly weaker among male students than among female students. This overall difference reflects the principle of triadic reciprocal determinism in SCT. Gender, as a sociocultural environmental factor, systematically shapes individuals’ cognitive beliefs and behavioral patterns in their interactions with technology. Male students may be more prone to habitual, low-reflection usage patterns, whereas female students may maintain higher levels of reflectiveness. These environmentally mediated differences in behavior subsequently exert differentiated influences on SDL, which relies heavily on self-regulation, ultimately resulting in significant gender-based moderating effects. 7 Conclusion Grounded in SCT, this study",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,17,"whereas female students may maintain higher levels of reflectiveness. These environmentally mediated differences in behavior subsequently exert differentiated influences on SDL, which relies heavily on self-regulation, ultimately resulting in significant gender-based moderating effects. 7 Conclusion Grounded in SCT, this study systematically examined the effects of the TUGA on undergraduates’ SDL and the psychological mechanisms underlying this relationship. Using survey data collected from 487 undergraduate students across four universities in Henan Province, China and analyzed through SEM, the findings reveal that TUGA significantly undermines undergraduates’ SDL both directly and indirectly. Specifically, SE and MOV serve as key mediating mechanisms through which thoughtless AI use influences learning outcomes. Furthermore, multi-group analysis indicates that gender significantly moderates the relationships between TUGA and several psychological factors, suggesting that male and female students exhibit different patterns in how AI usage behaviors influence motivation, self-beliefs, and autonomous learning. These findings provide several important theoretical contributions. First, this study introduces the concept of thoughtless use of generative AI into the research domain of SDL, shifting the focus from the predominantly positive narrative surrounding educational technologies toward a",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,17,"autonomous learning. These findings provide several important theoretical contributions. First, this study introduces the concept of thoughtless use of generative AI into the research domain of SDL, shifting the focus from the predominantly positive narrative surrounding educational technologies toward a more balanced examination of their potential cognitive and motivational risks. Second, by integrating SE and MOV as mediating mechanisms within an ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",69
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,18,"SCT framework, this study clarifies the internal psychological pathways through which AI- related behaviors influence learning outcomes. In particular, the identification of the sequential mediation pathway of SE and MOV highlights the importance of psychological resources in sustaining autonomous learning in AI-mediated environments. Third, the findings demonstrate that gender functions as an important moderating factor shaping how students interact with generative AI technologies. This insight expands current theoretical understandings of human–AI interaction and highlights the importance of considering sociocultural variables when examining technology-enhanced learning. The findings also offer practical implications for multiple stakeholders in higher education. From the perspective of policymakers, clear regulatory frameworks should be established to guide the responsible integration of generative AI technologies in higher education. Educational authorities may formulate explicit academic integrity guidelines that define appropriate uses of generative AI while discouraging excessive reliance on AI- generated content in academic tasks. In addition, policymakers should support the development of AI literacy initiatives and allocate funding for educational technology infrastructure that promotes responsible and transparent AI use. At the institutional level, university administrators should design policies and",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,18,"generated content in academic tasks. In addition, policymakers should support the development of AI literacy initiatives and allocate funding for educational technology infrastructure that promotes responsible and transparent AI use. At the institutional level, university administrators should design policies and support systems that help students use generative AI as a learning aid rather than a substitute for independent thinking. Universities may incorporate AI literacy and critical digital skills into curricula to strengthen students’ ability to evaluate and interpret AI-generated information. Institutional learning platforms may also employ learning analytics to identify students who show declining engagement or motivation in technology-mediated learning environments and provide targeted academic support. From the perspective of teaching practice, educators should redesign instructional strategies to ensure that generative AI complements rather than replaces students’ cognitive engagement. For instance, instructors may design assignments that require students to combine AI-assisted exploration with independent analysis and reflection. Teachers can also encourage students to critically evaluate AI-generated outputs by comparing them with authoritative academic sources and explaining their reasoning processes. Such strategies can help maintain students’ motivation and self-efficacy while preventing",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,18,"AI-assisted exploration with independent analysis and reflection. Teachers can also encourage students to critically evaluate AI-generated outputs by comparing them with authoritative academic sources and explaining their reasoning processes. Such strategies can help maintain students’ motivation and self-efficacy while preventing excessive dependence on automated solutions. Despite these contributions, several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the sample was drawn exclusively from universities in Henan Province, China, and the gender distribution was uneven, which may limit the generalizability of the findings. Future studies should expand the sampling scope to include a wider range of universities and achieve a more balanced demographic distribution. Second, this study focused primarily on SE and MOV as mediating variables. However, additional factors—such as learning strategies, metacognitive skills, and social support—may also influence the relationship between AI use behaviors and SDL. In addition, MOV was treated as an overall construct rather than distinguishing between intrinsic and extrinsic motivation, as the validated measurement scale conceptualizes motivation as a unified construct reflecting students’ overall willingness to engage in learning activities. Future research could further differentiate these motivational dimensions to provide",180
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,18,"overall construct rather than distinguishing between intrinsic and extrinsic motivation, as the validated measurement scale conceptualizes motivation as a unified construct reflecting students’ overall willingness to engage in learning activities. Future research could further differentiate these motivational dimensions to provide more nuanced insights into how generative AI usage patterns influence students’ learning processes. Finally, this study relied on cross-sectional data to examine the proposed mediation relationships. Although the mediation model was theoretically grounded in Social Cognitive Theory, cross-sectional mediation analyses cannot establish definitive causal ordering among variables. Therefore, the findings should be interpreted as evidence of statistical mediation rather than causal mediation. Future research could employ longitudinal designs to examine the developmental trajectories and long-term consequences of thoughtless generative AI use, thereby offering more profound insights into its temporal dynamics and sustained impacts on students’ SDL. Data availability: The datasets generated during and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT ARTICLE IN PRESS ARTICLE IN PRESS",165
DOC_060,s41598-026-54337-y_reference.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-54337-y,19,"Funding: This research was supported by the Henan Provincial Higher Education Research Project (2025SXHLX214) and the Zhoukou Normal University Education and Teaching Reform Project (J2025025). Author Contributions: H. Z.: Conceptualization, methodology, validation, formal analysis, data curation, writing—original draft preparation. H. G.: Supervision, resources, writing— review and editing, project administration. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.",62
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,1,"OPEN Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 1 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z An integrated framework of ChatGPT adoption in higher education using TAM and UTAUT models Dongmin Ma1, Huma Akram2 & Shengji Li2 In the realm of Artificial intelligence (AI) driven platforms, ChatGPT has built up an immense popularity in higher education. While vast array of the existing studies delves into students’ utilization through various theoretical frameworks; however, a comprehensive mechanism through which it contextualizes students’ utilization remain fragmentarily figured out. The present analysis thus, sought to unravel the factors that determine students’ intentions to use ChatGPT by employing Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAT) framework, while also exploring the moderating role of hedonic motivation in the association between theoretical predictors and students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT. Utilizing a survey methodology, we evaluated students’ perceptions from diverse schools of a Chinese university. We opted for questionnaire as the principal tool for data acquisition, which was then assessed using structural equation modelling, accompanied by inferential, and descriptive statistical approaches. The analysis yielded noteworthy findings, indicating",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,1,"perceptions from diverse schools of a Chinese university. We opted for questionnaire as the principal tool for data acquisition, which was then assessed using structural equation modelling, accompanied by inferential, and descriptive statistical approaches. The analysis yielded noteworthy findings, indicating that both TAM and UTAT predictors act as significant aspects affecting students’ willingness to use ChatGPT, with hedonic motivation acted as a moderating variable between this association. This complex interplay among variables suggests the significance of a user-friendly interface while accentuating the concrete benefits of ChatGPT. These insights offer a primary evidence in determining TAM and UTAT as an aligned process, that are associated with students’ intentions to use ChatGPT, through their hedonic motivational states. Keywords ChatGPT, Artificial intelligence, Higher education, Unified theory of acceptance and use of technology, Technology acceptance model, Attitudes toward use, Behavioral intentions Emerging as a revolutionary force in contemporary times, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has reshaped several aspects of our lives, including the aptness to revolutionize conventional pedagogical and learning approaches in education1,2. Its impact has spread to the field of education, proving that it could",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,1,"revolutionary force in contemporary times, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has reshaped several aspects of our lives, including the aptness to revolutionize conventional pedagogical and learning approaches in education1,2. Its impact has spread to the field of education, proving that it could revolutionize conventional teaching techniques by tailoring them to each student’s unique requirements3,4. ChatGPT has emerged as game- changing generative AI-based application in the sphere of learning technology, created by OpenAI in 2022. It operates as a large language model (LLM), modeled to establish text-based responses based on user inputs5. Programmed with extensive language training, this AI model demonstrates a remarkable ability to comprehend natural language and context, performing tasks such as translating text, creating marketing content, simplifying articles, and scripting6. In education, its potential is multifaceted, as it can offer tailored educational opportunities by familiarizing material difficulty to individual student progress. It also provides immediate feedback on students’ work and streamlines administrative tasks like grading and record-keeping7. Furthermore, it extends its utility beyond traditional classrooms, allowing students the opportunity to gain assistance outside physical class boundaries8. A multitude of variables",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,1,"progress. It also provides immediate feedback on students’ work and streamlines administrative tasks like grading and record-keeping7. Furthermore, it extends its utility beyond traditional classrooms, allowing students the opportunity to gain assistance outside physical class boundaries8. A multitude of variables are required to effectively incorporate AI into learning settings, such as robust technological infrastructure9, sound institutional policies5, rich self-efficacy10, and appropriate ICT literacy11. In parallel, individual perspectives and beliefs hold immense importance in determining how well AI is used in educational practices. Literature has consistently demonstrated that students’ attitudes make a substantial 1School of Foreign Studies, North China University of Water Resources and Electric Power, Zhengzhou, Henan, China. 2School of International Education, North China University of Water Resources and Electric Power, Zhengzhou, Henan Province, PR China. email: akramhuma@ncwu.edu.cn www.nature.com/scientificreports",129
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,2,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 2 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports contribution to their actual learning experiences in AI-driven learning routines12,13. Their inclination to choose a certain digital platform aligns with their learning patterns and requirements, reflecting their active engagement in the learning process14. This harmonization is driven by a shared goal of enhancing their learning experience through the strategic use of technology15. Contrarily, individuals who possess an adverse perspective on a particular technological platform are less inclined to embrace it for their learning16. In addition, newly introduced technologies are supposed to undergo a comprehensive review of individuals’ perceptions to assess their perceived relevance17, usability18, and usefulness16. Therefore, students’ perspectives on ChatGPT’s role in their learning routines must be examined to comprehend the complexities of their engagement with this cutting- edge AI tool. Typically, the literature has examined ChatGPT adoption through the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM)19 and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAT) framework8. However, a comprehensive examination using both models is lacking. Consequently, this study aims to bridge this gap and illuminate attitudes and behavioral intentions within",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,2,"Technology Acceptance Model (TAM)19 and the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAT) framework8. However, a comprehensive examination using both models is lacking. Consequently, this study aims to bridge this gap and illuminate attitudes and behavioral intentions within this specific context by utilizing both the TAM and UTAT models by addressing following objectives: RO1: To explore attitudes and behavioral intentions of students toward ChatGPT use in light of TAM and UTAT models. RO2:To identify the underlying factors shaping students’ attitudes and behavioral intentions toward using ChatGPT. Theoretical underpinning and hypotheses development When a new technology is launched into learning environments, researchers often wonder how much traction it really gets from the relevant bodies. In particular, examining users’ attitudes remains the central focus to analyze its effectiveness in academic programs. An extensive number of empirical investigations and theoretical frameworks have been established to examine users’ intentions to employ a certain technology18. Some of the well-known theoretical foundations include Davis’s20 Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Venkatesh et al.‘s21 Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) (see Fig. 1).",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,2,"have been established to examine users’ intentions to employ a certain technology18. Some of the well-known theoretical foundations include Davis’s20 Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Venkatesh et al.‘s21 Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) (see Fig. 1). Employing these theories to our study is anticipated to understand the elements that learners consider when deciding to use ChatGPT into their educational routines. In the TAM model, there are two main components that are thought to play a key part in deciding the degree of user engagement. Perceived usefulness (PU) PU reflects the level wherein consumers think utilizing a technology would enhance their academic progress20. Echoing Jain and Raghuram’s22 assertion, students are inclined to use those ICT platforms in their learning routines effectively if they find them more advantageous. Talking about PU in this study, it discusses how students find ChatGPT valuable to assist them in their academic endeavors and enhance their learning outcomes. Several studies clearly demonstrate that PU acts a crucial predictor of utilizing various technologies in educational settings. Based on quantitative data with 842 university students",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,2,"find ChatGPT valuable to assist them in their academic endeavors and enhance their learning outcomes. Several studies clearly demonstrate that PU acts a crucial predictor of utilizing various technologies in educational settings. Based on quantitative data with 842 university students in Lesotho, Ayanwale and Ndlovu23 employed TAM framework to examine their willingness to adopt chatbots. Their analysis demonstrated that the level of student engagement with chatbots was significantly influenced by their degree of perception of how useful they think it is. Likewise, employing quantitative data with 278 students and teachers at a Korean university, Park and Kim16 shed light on the significance of PU in forming ones’ profiles toward their willingness to implement digital applications. Their examination verified an important role of perceived usefulness in predicting digital mental Fig. 1. Conceptual underpinning.",132
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 3 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports healthcare application adoption by both students and teachers. In a similar vein, Bandura24 demonstrates how important it is to consider individuals’ perceived usefulness when trying to anticipate their behavior. Ultimately, PU is the degree to which someone perceives a behavior, once done well, is connected to valuable results. On the other hand, users who do not believe technology is valuable could have trouble implementing it21. Given these observations, it becomes apparent that students are inclined more to employ technology in their studies if they perceive it as relevant and practical. Therefore, it can be posited that: H1- PU significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Perceived ease of use (PEOU) The term PEOU reflects to how easy and comfortable an individual finds a certain technological platform20, which shapes their usage behavior. Echoing Syed et al.’s25 claim, students’ are inclined to adopt those AI platforms in their learning routines effectively if they find them easy to navigate. In their cross-sectional study, 391 undergraduates from a Malaysian institution",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"which shapes their usage behavior. Echoing Syed et al.’s25 claim, students’ are inclined to adopt those AI platforms in their learning routines effectively if they find them easy to navigate. In their cross-sectional study, 391 undergraduates from a Malaysian institution showed that students’ opinions of the platform’s user-friendliness greatly impacted their engagement with AI. Talking about PEOU in this study, discusses how students find ChatGPT user-friendly in incorporating this application to their studies. Lewis26 deems it one of the key factors affecting how effectively educational technologies are used. Similarly, university-based, 242 participants from India were recruited by Jain and Raghuram22 to contribute toward the literature on the role of PEOU based framework in generative AI adoption. Supporting previous studies, their statistical findings concluded that students’ engagement for using GenAI was found to be significantly affected by their perceived ease of use. Relatedly, Bandura24 offers a vivid sense of the necessity to account for the perceived easiness or difficulty people associate with behavior when attempting to predict that behavior. Finally, PEOU is the capability to properly execute the tasks required to",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"use. Relatedly, Bandura24 offers a vivid sense of the necessity to account for the perceived easiness or difficulty people associate with behavior when attempting to predict that behavior. Finally, PEOU is the capability to properly execute the tasks required to deal with future situations. On the other hand, individuals who feel technology is not easy to use or simple may perceive it as difficult to utilize21. Supporting this identification, Barrett et al.27 argue that an individual may be reluctant to fully adopt a particular digital platform due to perceived technological barriers. In a different quasi-experimental research by Elfeky and Elbyaly28 on the use of learning management system by female learners, they concluded that PEOU strongly influenced the students’ behavior toward using LMSs. In view of these findings, it can be surmised that students are expected to want to use their e-learning platforms in learning if the design is easy to use. Hence, it can be hypothesized: H2- PEOU significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. The UTAUT model consists of the following four primary constructs.",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"e-learning platforms in learning if the design is easy to use. Hence, it can be hypothesized: H2- PEOU significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. The UTAUT model consists of the following four primary constructs. Performance expectancy (PE) Performance expectancy (PE) refers to users’ beliefs that AI tools would enhance their abilities to perform well in their studies21. In the present investigation, it refers to how ChatGPT can improve students’ efficiency or learning outcomes. Abbad29 characterizes it as one of the key factors influencing how successfully educational systems and facilities are implemented. Strzelecki8 also corroborates that learners’ intentions in utilizing novel instructional tools are significantly impacted by their performance expectations. It is further studied by Abbad29 in the context of learning management systems LMS, Sewandono et al.30 to mobile learning, while Albashtawi and Al Bataineh31 applied it to Google Classroom. Therefore, students’ expectations of how much they can accomplish with ChatGPT are relevant to performance expectancy in this research. Thus, it can be assumed: H3- PE significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"applied it to Google Classroom. Therefore, students’ expectations of how much they can accomplish with ChatGPT are relevant to performance expectancy in this research. Thus, it can be assumed: H3- PE significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Effort expectancy (EE) Effort expectancy (EE) involves the perceived ease of navigating AI tools for performing academic tasks. In other words, it involves evaluation of learning systems to examine their effectiveness in term of their simplicity and user friendliness. When it comes to adopt newly introduced digital platforms, its importance has been highlighted in previous studies in adopting various educational technologies. For instance, an experimental study conducted by Du and Lv32 found EE as a significant determinant of students’ attitudes toward using generative AI. Likewise, Jakkaew and Hemrungrote33 suggest EE as one of the crucial predictors of successful use of Google Classroom. Their study, which involved 2,789 students in a quantitative survey, demonstrated that the level of student engagement in learning via Google Classroom is significantly influenced by their degree of perception they think it is easy",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"successful use of Google Classroom. Their study, which involved 2,789 students in a quantitative survey, demonstrated that the level of student engagement in learning via Google Classroom is significantly influenced by their degree of perception they think it is easy to use. Similarly, Al-Adwan et al.34 assert that EE is vital for maintaining students’ interest in using learning management systems. In this study, it refers to how much users assume ChatGPT is easy to adopt and navigate. In light of this, this hypothesis can be projected: H4- EE significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Social influence (SI) Social influence (SI) reflects the impact of social networks’ opinions regarding students’ willingness to adopt a certain system. The social circle of students may include interactions with peers, teachers, educational leaders, and other influencers. This influence suggests a positive association with ones’ inclination to utiliza a technology, as people apt to follow the views of people they are influenced from35. In educational settings, various studies reflect the importance of SI in adopting new technologies. In particular, a",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,3,"a positive association with ones’ inclination to utiliza a technology, as people apt to follow the views of people they are influenced from35. In educational settings, various studies reflect the importance of SI in adopting new technologies. In particular, a quantitative study conducted by VanDerSchaaf et al.36 found SI as a significant determinant of students’ attitudes to use software for accessing",61
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 4 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports university services in a public university in the United States. In another study, Changalima et al.37 further illustrate that social impact not only directly impacts students’ behaviors but can also shape their perceptions with the help of other UTAT factors, further reinforcing technology adoption. Employing survey analysis, their study revealed that social influence facilitates users’ intentions to use Generative AI in their studies. In this study, it refers to how students find ChatGPT in their social circle to use in their academics. Thus, it can be hypothesized: H5- SI significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Facilitating conditions (FC) Facilitating conditions (FC) include access to fundamental resources and help in using AI platforms. These might comprise hardware, fast access to the internet, sound coordination, sufficient support and training. Supporting these identifications, Syed et al.25 endorse the presence of advanced and innovative infrastructure for sustaining learners’ motivation in utilizing effectively. Based on a survey data, Bhatt and Muduli14 have examined the extent to which effective training is",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"and training. Supporting these identifications, Syed et al.25 endorse the presence of advanced and innovative infrastructure for sustaining learners’ motivation in utilizing effectively. Based on a survey data, Bhatt and Muduli14 have examined the extent to which effective training is making learners to behave towards AI usage behavior in learning. They found a strong effect of training programs on behavioural effectiveness of learners in AI-enabled learning systems. Likewise, supporting conditions for new technology acceptance are also demonstrated in multiple studies. For example, in a quantitative study carried out by Abbad29, FC emerged as one of the determinants of students’ attitudes to use Moodle. Similarly, Almaiah et al.38 assert that creating a conducive environment holds value in keeping students’ concentration in using mobile learning system. Their research, involving 697 students from universities in Jordon in a quantitative survey demonstrated that the level of improved ICT infrastructure is significantly linked with students’ perceptions to use mobile learning systems. Engaging with ChatGPT and facilitating its use for students involves ensuring they receive adequate technical support and training, particularly during peak demand periods. Thus,",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"level of improved ICT infrastructure is significantly linked with students’ perceptions to use mobile learning systems. Engaging with ChatGPT and facilitating its use for students involves ensuring they receive adequate technical support and training, particularly during peak demand periods. Thus, it can be postulated: H6- FC significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Hedonic motivation (HM) Hedonic motivation, as a core psychological variable, is a drive that fuels individual behaviours to get satisfaction and pleasure while using AI tools. It may involves solving problems in gamified learning environments, collaborating with peers to, or real-time feedback systems. It refocused the focus from what motivates education users to use technology to why consumers are interested in using technology. Acknowledging the significance of the same, Venkatesh et al.39 included this variable in their independent UTAUT2 model. It shows that the perceived enjoyment in using a particular AI tool is significant and facilitates achievement of learning objectives. Building on this idea, Alemayehu and Chen40 pointed out how motivation can reshape students’ self-perception regarding the use of AI tools in",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"that the perceived enjoyment in using a particular AI tool is significant and facilitates achievement of learning objectives. Building on this idea, Alemayehu and Chen40 pointed out how motivation can reshape students’ self-perception regarding the use of AI tools in the classroom. Students use some tools not just because they help them to learn, but because they take them as fun. In this sense, this learning behavior can be very beneficial for students in their informal learning. When learners perceive a certain application interesting, it enhances their engagement with that application which also reinforces creativity among them41. Correspondingly, various studies reflect the importance of HM in shaping learners’ behaviors in adopting new technologies. Based on a quantitative data, the analysis of Kumar and Bervell42 suggested a substantial influence of HM on learners’ behavioral intentions to use Google Classroom. It allows them to engage in mobile learning creatively, which reinforces their level of engagement with technologies in higher education. In another study, Liu et al.42 recruited university students in an online survey and taken data across two time points. They found",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"them to engage in mobile learning creatively, which reinforces their level of engagement with technologies in higher education. In another study, Liu et al.42 recruited university students in an online survey and taken data across two time points. They found that hedonic value had a strong effect on learners’ continuance intention to use GenAI. Based on these evidences, the current study postulates that: H7- HM significantly positively anticipates students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Moderating role of hedonic motivation (HM) The importance of hedonic motivation in using ICT-driven tools is well notified in several studies and theoretical frameworks. But beyond direct effects, the association of HM is also seen with the combination of other variables to get a comprehensive picture of technology adoption. In a meta-analysis conducted by Tamilmani44 identified that positive feelings from intrinsic motivation can magnify the impact of perceived usefulness on users’ behavioral intentions toward using AI tools. In parallel, its association with other variables has been documented based on type of usage. For instance, recruiting 246 students from higher education institutions Deng and",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,"magnify the impact of perceived usefulness on users’ behavioral intentions toward using AI tools. In parallel, its association with other variables has been documented based on type of usage. For instance, recruiting 246 students from higher education institutions Deng and Yu45 (2023) extended literature by examining the role of HM based model in adopting TikTok. Their findings revealed a significant involvement of hedonic motivation in the association between learners’ behavioral intentions to continue using GenAI. In another study, Sitar-Tăut46 identified that HM act as a significant mediator between UTAT predictors and m‐learning adoption in students. Despite considerable support for the significant relationships between HM and UTAT factors, a noteworthy gap remains in understanding how HM alters the relationship between both models’ constructs and students’ behavioural intentions toward ChatGPT use. Thus, it led us to posit the following hypotheses: H8a- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of PU with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8b- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of PEOU with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8c- HM would significantly",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,4,would significantly moderate the relationship of PU with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8b- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of PEOU with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8c- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of PE with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies.,55
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 5 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports H8d- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of EE with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8e- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of SI with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. H8f- HM would significantly moderate the relationship of FC with students’ behavioural intentions of utilizing ChatGPT in their studies. Behavioral Intention A user’s probable tendency to use a specified tool in the due course constitutes their behavioral intention. Various factors affect an individual’s drive, as well as their level of diligence and drive to complete a given task. A strong intention increases the likelihood that the behavior is going to be intentional20. A substantial amount of evidence indicates that users’ behavioral choices regarding system utilization profoundly affect their attitudes toward its use38. For instance, a mixed-method examination by Wang et al.47 suggests BI shapes users’ attitudes favorably when they gain positive experience. Their cross-sectional study, which involved students from various universities in Zhejiang, China, demonstrated a positive reciprocal correlation between students’ BI and",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"instance, a mixed-method examination by Wang et al.47 suggests BI shapes users’ attitudes favorably when they gain positive experience. Their cross-sectional study, which involved students from various universities in Zhejiang, China, demonstrated a positive reciprocal correlation between students’ BI and their attitudes toward using AI in their learning routines. In another study, Strzelecki8 examined how BI shapes individuals’ perceptions toward using ChatGPT in light of the UTAUT model. Recruiting 528 students from a university in Poland, their quantitative analysis revealed a strong influence of students’ BI in shaping their use behaviors toward ChatGPT utilization in their learning. Discoursing BI in this study, discusses how students intend to use ChatGPT in their studies. Venkatesh et al.21 consider it as one of the most important aspects impacting how successfully educational technologies are utilized. Extending this concept, Ma et al.48 highlighted the significance of effectively utilizing newly introduced technological systems. They noted that students often feel hesitant to adopt new applications in their learning settings because of limited knowledge. In another study, Almaiah et al.38 in their quantitative study revealed a positive influence",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"of effectively utilizing newly introduced technological systems. They noted that students often feel hesitant to adopt new applications in their learning settings because of limited knowledge. In another study, Almaiah et al.38 in their quantitative study revealed a positive influence of students BI on their actual use of mobile learning in Jordanian universities. In light of these evidences, the current study postulates that: H9- Students’ behavioral intentions significantly positively anticipate their attitude to use ChatGPT in their studies. Methodology The study used a quantitative research approach, collecting empirical data from a two-step online questionnaire to assess the research hypotheses. The first section focused on the demographic data of the participants, while the second section comprised items based on the TAM and UTAT models. The scales PU and PEOU scales were adapted from Lewis26, comprised of six items each construct. Other scales, i.e., PE, EE, FC, SI, AU were adopted from Venkatesh et al.21 which included 4 items each, and BI consists of three items. The scale for HM was adopted from Venkatesh et al. and also comprises three items. All",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"scales, i.e., PE, EE, FC, SI, AU were adopted from Venkatesh et al.21 which included 4 items each, and BI consists of three items. The scale for HM was adopted from Venkatesh et al. and also comprises three items. All questions were graded on 5-point response criteria, where 1 signified strongly disagreeing and 5 referred to strongly agreeing. The study targeted only ChatGPT users, using a screening question to filter respondents based on their usage of ChatGPT for educational purposes. To ensure the tool’s content quality and consistency, the survey items were reviewed by both English and Chinese linguistic experts to adequately evaluate whether students understand both versions. They were then further tested for contextual relevance and cultural suitability through a pilot study with 50 participants49, resulting in the removal of two questions. Each individual gave their informed authorization to participate in the study and was made aware of their right to discontinue participation at any moment. Moreover, they were given an explanation of the goal of the study and their rights through a cover letter that was included with",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"participate in the study and was made aware of their right to discontinue participation at any moment. Moreover, they were given an explanation of the goal of the study and their rights through a cover letter that was included with the online questionnaire. Further approval to carry out the study came from North China University of Water Resources and Electric Power’s ethics committee and academic advisory council. Hence, all the procedures adhered to the pertinent laws regarding human subjects. Participants The study utilized convenience sampling method that facilitates data collection from accessible respondents50. The sample size was estimated using the method identified by Al-Subaihi51, entailing a minimum sample size of 385. Subsequently, all (both domestic and international) students at a Chinese university were invited to complete an online inquiry form using the WeChat application. After data screening, 234 valid cases from diverse schools were included in the study, with 31.6% female and 68.4% male participants (see Table 1), aged between 17 and 29 years. The majority of participants were pursuing undergraduate studies (88.9%), while 11.1% were postgraduate students. Regarding academic",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,5,"from diverse schools were included in the study, with 31.6% female and 68.4% male participants (see Table 1), aged between 17 and 29 years. The majority of participants were pursuing undergraduate studies (88.9%), while 11.1% were postgraduate students. Regarding academic fields, over 40% of students were from Applied Sciences, over 30% from Social Sciences, and over 20% from Natural Sciences. Majority of enrolled students were international (67.9%), while 32.1% were Chinese. Results We analyzed our data through a comprehensive approach using descriptive, inferential tests, and Structural Equation Modelling (SEM). Descriptive particulars presented the configurations of participants’ baseline features, such as mean scores and data distribution. The inferential analysis identified differences among demographic groups by t-tests and ANOVA. To analyze the proposed model, we employed SEM analysis using the maximum-likelihood covariance-based approach because SEM is an ideal tool for managing detailed statistical representations52. Treating all measures as latent constructs, this study characterized PU, PEOU, PE, EE, SI, and FC as predictors, HM predictor as well as a moderator, and BI and AU as the outcomes, with each construct as a measure",180
