DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,6,"Harman single-factor test with the statistical program SPSS version 26.0. It is usual practice to utilize this method to find out whether there is any partiality in the responses of the respondents. If one factor explains more than half of the entire variance identified, it indicates that CMB is present in the study56. In this work, we investigated the findings of the Harman single-factor assessment. The analysis’s results disclosed that the dataset did not contain any CMB problems. One factor was determined to account for 47.336% of the total variation. It can be inferred that the study did not exhibit any major common method bias, given this value is below the 50% level that is advised56. This discovery enhances the credibility and validity of the findings by demonstrating that no single bias significantly influenced the respondents’ answers. Because of this, researchers can have more faith in the study’s data and its following analysis. Descriptive analysis The descriptive analysis indicated that all constructs exhibited higher mean values (see Table 4), with every predictor achieving a mean score above 3.5. Specifically, the",180
