DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,9,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 9 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports The findings further exemplified that students’ AU and BI vary by their educational level and nationality. The notable prevalence of positive AU and BI among undergraduate students is an intriguing result that invites exploration. Swed et al.58 also elaborated on a significant difference between students’ attitudes toward AI concerning their qualification level, indicating a higher prevalence among undergraduates than graduate students. Likewise, Pinto dos Santos et al.59 found that medical undergraduates recognize the potential uses of AI in their studies. Several factors may contribute to this contrast. It’s one reason may be that postgraduate studies typically entail more in-depth academic tasks. As a result, post-graduate students may perceive ChatGPT as less suitable for their particular requirements, leading to a lower inclination to use it than undergraduates, who may find it more aligned with their learning needs7. Furthermore, postgraduate students may emphasize work autonomy due to the nature of their advanced studies60, potentially causing a lesser inclination in using AI technologies in their studies, as they prefer to rely on their own",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,9,"aligned with their learning needs7. Furthermore, postgraduate students may emphasize work autonomy due to the nature of their advanced studies60, potentially causing a lesser inclination in using AI technologies in their studies, as they prefer to rely on their own knowledge and established research methodologies61. Regarding nationality, the finding highlights a significant disparity among students, depicting higher adoption of ChatGPT by international students than Chinese. This finding is in agreement with Kim and Lee’s35 observation that students’ sociocultural identities greatly impact their views on AI. In parallel, international students represent high proportion in the sample. Given that the results should be understood as reflecting ChatGPT adoption among students enrolled in the participating Chinese universities rather than Chinese students only. International students may have distinct motivations for using ChatGPT, such as academic support, translation, and cultural backgrounds48. In addition, they desire internationally recognized standards of education to stay competitive in a global job market62. Secondly, Language proficiency significantly shapes students’ interactions with technology, as higher skills improve understanding and navigation of AI-driven tools63. Furthermore, digital resources are often provided in English,",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,9,"internationally recognized standards of education to stay competitive in a global job market62. Secondly, Language proficiency significantly shapes students’ interactions with technology, as higher skills improve understanding and navigation of AI-driven tools63. Furthermore, digital resources are often provided in English, which allows international students to proficiently utilize them. However, Chinese students, often less fluent in English, may face challenges and hold differing views on utilizing AI technologies in higher education64. This language barrier introduces an additional layer of complexity in adopting AI technologies, underscoring the importance of understanding the language-related dimensions of technology acceptance. Realizing the impact of contextual and academic experiences, relevant bodies should create interventions that take into account students’ varied cultural and educational backgrounds. In order to create a more welcoming classroom for all students, it is important to address possible language barriers with language-specific support and intuitive interfaces. Additionally, cross-cultural seminars and individualized technology training can help improve users’ impressions of the system and its user friendliness. The findings further demonstrate that students’ gender, age, and major do not significantly associate with their AU and BI",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,9,"intuitive interfaces. Additionally, cross-cultural seminars and individualized technology training can help improve users’ impressions of the system and its user friendliness. The findings further demonstrate that students’ gender, age, and major do not significantly associate with their AU and BI toward ChatGPT use. Swed et al.58 also did not find a significant difference between students’ attitudes toward AI use for their gender, and similar results were observed by Kwak et al.10 for nursing students influenced by their age. However, Kim and Lee35 noted a significant influence of gender on general students’ attitudes and BI, though not among disadvantaged students. These mixed results suggest the need for ongoing research with diverse student groups to further understand these dynamics. Educational leaders should continuously Fig. 2. Structural Model Assessment Significant at: **p < .01; *p < .05.",134
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,10,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 10 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports monitor student attitudes toward emerging technologies, reassessing the impact of demographic parameters in adopting ChatGPT or other similar AI tools. Implementing periodic surveys and feedback mechanisms can provide valuable insights, helping institutions tailor their approaches effectively. The findings regarding predictors of behavioral intentions present a compelling narrative about the complex effects of both theoretical models. An analysis of the constructs of the TAM shows that both PU and PEOU significantly influence students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT. This trend corresponds to existing literature suggesting that optimistic insights of PU and PEOU are vital elements of technology utilization16. A more promising attitude toward technology’s use in daily practices is typically fostered by positive views, which in turn raise inclinations to employ it28. In terms of contribution, the stronger effect of PU than PEOU is also noted by Ayanwale and Ndlovu23 underlining the importance of PU in the acceptance of new technologies. It is, therefore, advisable to prioritize and enhance the practical benefits and user-friendly aspects of the technological application in educational settings.",180
