DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 11 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports increasing users’ understanding of ChatGPT’s utility and ease of use could contribute to more positive behaviors. Additionally, continuous evaluation and improvement of the user interface and functionality can further encourage broader acceptance of ChatGPT among students. Furthermore, it is advisable for programmers and instructors to emphasize functionality and practicality when integrating ChatGPT into educational settings. While maintaining user-friendliness, they should prioritize and effectively convey the concrete advantages that ChatGPT offers to enhance students’ learning experiences. In terms of UTAT constructs, the results indicated a significant impact of all AI-acceptance indicators on students’ behavioural intentions toward using ChatGPT. This observation is also noted by Bayaga65, which indicated that PE, FC, and EE significantly influence users’ behaviors to use AI platforms in higher education. The observation that PE had a greater impact than other constructs on students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT adds an intricate dimension to the study’s findings. It suggests how students perceive ChatGPT’s ability to enhance their learning effectiveness or outcomes. The findings presented by Salem66 also revealed that performance",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"other constructs on students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT adds an intricate dimension to the study’s findings. It suggests how students perceive ChatGPT’s ability to enhance their learning effectiveness or outcomes. The findings presented by Salem66 also revealed that performance expectancy emerged as one of the strongest influencing factors on behavioural intentions toward using AI-powered NLP tools. Likewise, Du and Lv31 identified that 38% of the variance in users’ behaviors toward using AI writing assistants could be attributed to performance expectancy. One possible explanation is that if students find any technological tool effective, particularly in dealing with their educational needs, they would be more inclined to utilize it18. Venkatesh’s21 conceptualization also illustrates that learners are more inclined to use specific technologies when they perceive clear academic benefits from them. While performance expectancy was a prevailing factor, the influence of other UTAUT constructs was also significant. Effort expectancy is particularly important in the context of ChatGPT, as students may prefer a more intuitive and user-friendly interface. Additionally, social influence emerged as a critical factor, aligning with previous research indicating that support",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"UTAUT constructs was also significant. Effort expectancy is particularly important in the context of ChatGPT, as students may prefer a more intuitive and user-friendly interface. Additionally, social influence emerged as a critical factor, aligning with previous research indicating that support from peers and faculty can facilitate the adoption of new educational technologies67. Furthermore, supporting conditions—such as institutional support, infrastructure, and access— played a crucial role, highlighting that adoption is not entirely independent of the broader educational context. Thereby, educational authorities should recognize the practical utility of ChatGPT by incorporating it into their coursework, demonstrating its applications in research, general writing, and problem-solving. Additionally, tangible results can enhance students’ performance expectancy and adoption rates. To facilitate ease of use with AI tools, training workshops and orientation sessions should be organized, accompanied by simplified guidelines to minimize perceived effort. Furthermore, teachers and student leaders can serve as role models by employing and promoting ChatGPT within established academic standards. Less confident users can benefit from peer support to help build their confidence. To strengthen facilitating conditions, universities must ensure reliable internet access, as",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"student leaders can serve as role models by employing and promoting ChatGPT within established academic standards. Less confident users can benefit from peer support to help build their confidence. To strengthen facilitating conditions, universities must ensure reliable internet access, as well as provide institutional licenses and clear policies on the ethical and effective use of ChatGPT. This support system can enhance the feasibility of continued use for individuals. Future studies might explore the persistence of performance expectancy across different fields, cultural contexts, and levels of digital literacy. To gain a deeper understanding, longitudinal studies could be conducted to assess whether these perceptions evolve as students get accustomed with AI applications. The findings further highlighted a significant moderating role of hedonic motivation between all (TAM and UTAT) predictors and students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT. This suggests that intrinsic motivation influences learners’ perceptions of technology, ultimately affecting their intentions to use it by strengthening the relationships between these factors. This finding resonates with Qu and Wu’s19 conceptualization of the multifaceted function of hedonic motivation on individuals’ engagement with AI tools in",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"learners’ perceptions of technology, ultimately affecting their intentions to use it by strengthening the relationships between these factors. This finding resonates with Qu and Wu’s19 conceptualization of the multifaceted function of hedonic motivation on individuals’ engagement with AI tools in their learning practices. Such involvement strengthens their enjoyment, fosters persistence, and empower them to overcome challenges when using these tools68. HM is also viewed as a necessary precondition for UTAT2, as it enables students to undertake challenges on their own and scaffolds their learning by promoting technology use39. Additionally, it is suggested that hedonic motivation facilitates sustained use of AI tools in students’ learning routines46,69. Given this perspective, students with higher hedonic motivation are expected to actively engage in tasks using AI tools and demonstrate satisfactory academic performance. Given that, programmers and educators should integrate features that make ChatGPT more engaging. This may include using interactive prompts, gamified elements, and personalized learning experiences to sustain student engagement. Additionally, teachers should receive professional trainings on how to effectively incorporate ChatGPT in ways that promote both positive learning outcomes and enjoyable interactions,",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"engaging. This may include using interactive prompts, gamified elements, and personalized learning experiences to sustain student engagement. Additionally, teachers should receive professional trainings on how to effectively incorporate ChatGPT in ways that promote both positive learning outcomes and enjoyable interactions, such as through creative assignments or exploratory dialogue. To better understand student preferences, governing bodies should survey students about what they find enjoyable in AI tools and adjust integration strategies accordingly. This approach would help maximize both cognitive and affective engagement among students. Furthermore, additional studies should examine how hedonic motivation, in relation to other factors associated with AI use, impacts students’ learning outcomes to gain deeper understanding. We further found an intricate connection between students’ behavioral intentions and their attitude toward using ChatGPT. This finding aligns with established trends in existing research. For example, studies on the digital mental healthcare platform application16, Learning Management Systems (LMS)28, and other AI- driven tools in higher education13, indicate that when students find certain technologies engaging, they are more likely to feel empowered to continue using those applications over time. Additionally, both the",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,11,"healthcare platform application16, Learning Management Systems (LMS)28, and other AI- driven tools in higher education13, indicate that when students find certain technologies engaging, they are more likely to feel empowered to continue using those applications over time. Additionally, both the TAM and UTAT frameworks highlight the significance of the connection between behavioral intentions and attitudes in the adoption of various technologies. This relationship indicates that the influence on initial intention is an upstream effect of becoming a technology user and AI integrator. Therefore, interventions should focus on promoting students’ initial behavioral intentions by providing trial opportunities, such as course-integrated tasks or gamified exercises that require the use of ChatGPT. Additionally, students should be supported with practical use cases, including brainstorming, language practice, and text summarization, to guide them in appropriate usage. In parallel, institutions must establish clear guidelines on academic integrity and offer training for ethical usage to prevent concerns about plagiarism or misuse from diminishing interest. Furthermore, teachers can",160
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,12,"Scientific Reports | (2026) 16:33284 12 | https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z www.nature.com/scientificreports model positive ethical use by exploring ways to incorporate ChatGPT in educational settings, which would strengthen students’ intentions and contribute to the cultivation of responsible beliefs. To achieve a deeper understanding, longitudinal studies could be conducted across different educational levels, disciplines, and contexts. These studies would assess how students’ perceptions develop into long-term attitudes toward the use of ChatGPT over time. Even though the current investigation offers insightful information, there are a few limitations that need to be cautiously taken into account when interpreting the results. Firstly, we enrolled students from a single Chinese university, which limits the study’s external applicability, requiring to recruit large samples across diverse universities and contexts in subsequent investigations to extend our study’s applicability. Moreover, the study follows both TAM and UTAUT models incorporating hedonic motivation as a moderating variable. Future research should expand this framework by incorporating other measures of AI integration in the association of motivation. Additionally, the study was dependent on self-described data from students, which may bring partiality to the findings. Integrating",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,12,"as a moderating variable. Future research should expand this framework by incorporating other measures of AI integration in the association of motivation. Additionally, the study was dependent on self-described data from students, which may bring partiality to the findings. Integrating these results with data gathered from other sources or methods, such as focus group discussions and interviews, or classroom observations might offer us richer insights regarding ChatGPT use. Conclusions This study provides novel visions regarding the predictors that shape students’ attitudes and behavioral intentions toward using ChatGPT for educational purposes, framed within the TAM and UTAT theoretical frameworks. The complex interplay among the components of the TAM and UTAT frameworks and underscores the critical importance of their determinants in shaping students’ intentions to utilize ChatGPT. Among the various constructs, students’ behavioral intentions were significantly influenced by the perceived usefulness from the TAM framework and performance expectancy from the UTAT framework. Moderation analysis further illustrates deeper insights into how hedonic motivation enhances the positive relationship between the predictors from both frameworks and students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT. Consequently, the study",180
DOC_061,s41598-026-63076-z.pdf,10.1038/s41598-026-63076-z,12,"the TAM framework and performance expectancy from the UTAT framework. Moderation analysis further illustrates deeper insights into how hedonic motivation enhances the positive relationship between the predictors from both frameworks and students’ behavioral intentions to use ChatGPT. Consequently, the study stresses the significance of both TAM and UTAT frameworks in motivating students to adopt ChatGPT- driven learning practices. The enjoyment students derive from their learning tasks illustrates the platform’s capacity to foster confidence and autonomy in learners, contributing to a rich learning environment. These findings collectively support the principles of technology adoption outlined in TAM and UTAT, indicating that all determinants interact with hedonic motivation to shape students’ intentions to use AI-powered platforms in their educational routines. The insights gained from this study are valuable for educators, developers, and educational organizations aiming to effectively integrate ChatGPT into higher education by highlighting its tangible benefits. As technology increasingly influences education, grasping these nuanced dynamics is essential for optimizing user experience and ensuring successful adoption. Received:",164
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,1,"THEMENTEIL https://doi.org/10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2 Unterrichtswissenschaft (2026) 54:325–356 The relationship between AI-chatbots use, student assessment performance and learning outcomes in higher education Dimitri Molerov · Denis Federiakin · Olga Zlatkin-Troitschanskaia · Kevin Shenavai · Lukas Trierweiler · Marie-Theres Nagel Received: 26 May 2025 / Revised: 15 December 2025 / Accepted: 15 January 2026 / Published online: 5 February 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Abstract In this study in German higher education, we draw on the ﬁrst panel of dataset from a longitudinal assessment of domain-speciﬁc (DOM) Critical On- line Reasoning (COR) in economics in the winter terms 2023/24 (t0) and 2024/25 (t1). DOM-COR tasks were completed as a digital assessment, combining open web search, information evaluation, and reasoning to solve domain-speciﬁc scenarios. For this, many students used Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI) chatbots employing Large Language Models (LLMs). This offered a natural quasi-experiment in a real-life learning setting to compare students’ DOM-COR performance with and without LLMs. Students also reported on LLM-tools use for study and everyday purposes as well as on their learning outcomes (e.g., credit points, exams). This study investigates both the relationships",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,1,"real-life learning setting to compare students’ DOM-COR performance with and without LLMs. Students also reported on LLM-tools use for study and everyday purposes as well as on their learning outcomes (e.g., credit points, exams). This study investigates both the relationships between LLM-tools use and students DOM-COR assessment performance as well as their learning outcomes at universities. We explicitly dis- tinguish between the qualitative indicators of learning and the speed thereof in the Dimitri Molerov and Dr. Denis Federiakin share ﬁrst authorship. Dimitri Molerov · Denis Federiakin ·  Olga Zlatkin-Troitschanskaia · Kevin Shenavai · Lukas Trierweiler · Marie-Theres Nagel Business and Economics Education, Johannes Gutenberg University, Mainz, Germany E-Mail: troitschanskaia@uni-mainz.de Dimitri Molerov E-Mail: dmolerov@uni-mainz.de Denis Federiakin E-Mail: denis.federiakin@uni-mainz.de Kevin Shenavai E-Mail: kshenava@uni-mainz.de Lukas Trierweiler E-Mail: trierweiler@uni-mainz.de Marie-Theres Nagel E-Mail: marie.nagel@uni-mainz.de K",131
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,2,"326 D. Molerov et al. context of LLM-chatbots use. By comparing the time students spent completing DOM-COR tasks and their performance between LLM-users and non-users, we ex- amine if, and to what extent, LLM-users had an advantage. While LLM-users were faster, they did not perform better on written task responses. The relationships with learning outcomes trend in the same direction, indicating that regular LLM-users passed more exams, but did not appear to achieve better grades. The ﬁndings point to opportunities and risks when using LLM for domain-speciﬁc COR-like tasks as well as for regular study assignments. Keywords Critical Online Reasoning · Digital Assessment · Log Data · Use of AI Tools · ChatGPT · Learning Outcomes Zusammenhänge zwischen der Nutzung von KI-Chatbots und den Test- sowie Lernergebnissen von Studierenden im Hochschulbereich Zusammenfassung Diese Studie basiert auf einer längsschnittlichen Untersuchung des kritischen Online-Denkens (Critical Online Reasoning, COR) in der Ökono- mie (DOM-COR) im Wintersemester 2023/24 (t0) und 2024/25 (t1). Die DOM- COR Aufgaben wurden im digitalen Assessment bearbeitet und kombinierten offe- ne Websuche, Informationsbewertung und Argumentation, um domänenspeziﬁsche Szenarien zu lösen.",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,2,"kritischen Online-Denkens (Critical Online Reasoning, COR) in der Ökono- mie (DOM-COR) im Wintersemester 2023/24 (t0) und 2024/25 (t1). Die DOM- COR Aufgaben wurden im digitalen Assessment bearbeitet und kombinierten offe- ne Websuche, Informationsbewertung und Argumentation, um domänenspeziﬁsche Szenarien zu lösen. Mehrere Studierende verwendeten Chatbots mit künstlicher In- telligenz (KI), die Large Language Models (LLMs) einsetzen, um die Aufgaben zu lösen. Diese Situation bot ein ,natürliches Quasi-Experiment‘ in den realen Lern- settings, um die Leistung der Studierenden bei DOM-COR Aufgaben ohne und mit Nutzung von LLMs zu vergleichen. Zudem berichteten die Studierenden über die regelmäßige Nutzung von KI-Tools für Studien- und Alltagszwecke sowie ihre Ler- nergebnisse (z.B. ECTS, Prüfungen). Diese Studie untersucht die Zusammenhänge zwischen der Nutzung von KI-Tools und den DOM-COR-Leistungen der Studieren- den sowie ihren Lernergebnissen an Universitäten. Dabei unterscheiden wir explizit zwischen den qualitativen Indikatoren des Lernens und der Geschwindigkeit des Lernens im Zusammenhang mit der Verwendung von LLM-Chatbots. Insbesonde- re durch den Vergleich der Bearbeitungszeit und der Leistung der Studierenden untersuchen wir, ob und inwieweit LLM-Nutzer:innen im Vorteil waren. Während LLM-Nutzer:innen schneller waren, erzielten sie jedoch bei",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,2,"der Geschwindigkeit des Lernens im Zusammenhang mit der Verwendung von LLM-Chatbots. Insbesonde- re durch den Vergleich der Bearbeitungszeit und der Leistung der Studierenden untersuchen wir, ob und inwieweit LLM-Nutzer:innen im Vorteil waren. Während LLM-Nutzer:innen schneller waren, erzielten sie jedoch bei den schriftlichen Aufga- benlösungen keine (signiﬁkant) besseren Ergebnisse. Die Zusammenhänge mit den Lernergebnissen weisen in die gleiche Richtung: LLM-Nutzer:innen absolvierten zwar mehr Prüfungen, erzielten aber offenbar keine besseren Noten. Die Befunde deuten auf Chancen und Risiken hin, die sich aus der Nutzung von LLMs bei COR- ähnlichen Aufgaben sowie im Zusammenhang mit Lernergebnissen abzeichnen. Schlüsselwörter Kritisches Online-Denken · Digitales Assessment · Log Daten · Nutzung von KI Tools · ChatGPT · Lernergebnisse K",113
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,3,"The relationship between AI-chatbots use, student assessment performance and learning... 327 1 Research focus and objectives The rapid development of Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AI) has signiﬁcantly inﬂuenced higher education in recent years (Al-Zahrani and Alasmari 2024; Handa et al. 2025). University students report using chatbots such as ChatGPT in their studies (Kelly 2024; Labadze et al. 2023). Given the tendency of Large Language Models (LLMs) to generate hallucinations (Sahoo et al. 2024) and to reproduce unvetted information sources (Zhou et al. 2024; Rejeleene et al. 2024), students are exposed to a ﬂood of unreliable, false, or unchecked information of varying quality, which they must critically evaluate themselves. Owing to the ﬂexibility of LLMs (Herbold et al. 2023; OpenAI 2023), the appeal of LLM-powered chatbots has steadily increased alongside their capabilities (Yu et al. 2024). Although many competing chatbots are available from different providers, ChatGPT remains among the most widely used (Morell-Mengual et al. 2025). In this paper, we therefore do not differentiate between speciﬁc chatbots; rather, we generalize ﬁndings from research conducted on ChatGPT to LLM-based artiﬁcial intelligence chatbots (AI chatbots)",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,3,"different providers, ChatGPT remains among the most widely used (Morell-Mengual et al. 2025). In this paper, we therefore do not differentiate between speciﬁc chatbots; rather, we generalize ﬁndings from research conducted on ChatGPT to LLM-based artiﬁcial intelligence chatbots (AI chatbots) more broadly. The expectations of how AI chatbots inﬂuence higher-education students’ learn- ing outcomes vary greatly (Wang et al. 2024): while “techno-optimists” claim that LLMs can enhance student learning in unprecedented ways (Kasneci et al. 2023), “techno-pessimists” warn of deskilling, challenges to content authority, and difﬁ- culties in assessment delivery (Liang et al. 2023; Royer 2024). Despite the rapid adoption of LLMs, there is a lack of balanced, empirical evidence on their im- pact on student learning (Bozkurt et al. 2024), particularly regarding Critical Online Reasoning (COR) skills and measurable outcomes such as course credits and exam performance (on student learning outcomes and their measurement in higher educa- tion, see Zlatkin-Troitschanskaia et al. 2020). Understanding these effects is crucial for educators and researchers aiming to design effective learning environments, as- sess digital tool adoption, and maintain rigorous educational standards (Grassini",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,3,"learning outcomes and their measurement in higher educa- tion, see Zlatkin-Troitschanskaia et al. 2020). Understanding these effects is crucial for educators and researchers aiming to design effective learning environments, as- sess digital tool adoption, and maintain rigorous educational standards (Grassini et al. 2024; Swargiary 2024). A key distinction for assessing learning outcomes in relation to COR lies between Closed Information Space tasks and Open Information Space tasks (Hartig et al. 2025). Closed tasks, such as standardized essays, involve a ﬁxed amount of infor- mation with limited access to additional sources (Shavelson et al. 2019). In contrast, Open tasks allow students to draw on virtually unlimited information, constrained only by time, skill, and access (Wineburg et al. 2022). Open tasks more closely reﬂect real-world problem solving but are rare in educational research, as open- ended search complicates isolating the skill of interest (Mislevy 2018). Nonetheless, assessing LLM use in Open Information contexts is essential to capture their actual impact on authentic problem-solving (Sparks et al. 2024). Although research on AI tools such as ChatGPT is growing rapidly, many studies are conducted",180
DOC_062,s42010-026-00242-2.pdf,10.1007/s42010-026-00242-2,3,"interest (Mislevy 2018). Nonetheless, assessing LLM use in Open Information contexts is essential to capture their actual impact on authentic problem-solving (Sparks et al. 2024). Although research on AI tools such as ChatGPT is growing rapidly, many studies are conducted in artiﬁcial settings (Wang and Fan 2025). While controlled designs facilitate causal analysis, they often overlook real teaching contexts and learning outcomes such as course credits and exams, limiting practical applicability (Biesta 2007). Investigating the relationships between AI and students’ learning outcomes is therefore a necessary step toward effective and scalable integration of these tools in (higher) education (Salas-Martínez and Ramírez-Martinell 2025). Existing research K",105
