DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,1,"Vol.:(0123456789) Postdigital Science and Education https://doi.org/10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7 RESEARCH Literacy Demands Placed on Students in Student–GenAI Entanglements in Higher Education Zhiru Sun1 · Nina Bonderup Dohn1 · Martin Rehm1 Received: 30 March 2026 / Revised: 10 September 2026 / Accepted: 11 September 2026 © The Author(s) 2026 Abstract Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has fundamentally changed how students engage with technology in higher education, placing them in an active epistemic role — prompting, evaluating, and co-producing with the system. Yet existing sys- tematic reviews of AI in higher education classify AI by institutional function and were conducted largely before GenAI entered educational practice at scale, leaving a fundamental question unaddressed: what distinct forms of student–GenAI engage- ment exist, which are prevalent, and which remain marginal? This paper argues that the uneven distribution of student–GenAI engagement reflects the literacy demands that different forms of engagement place on students and the degree to which those demands align with the practice logic of higher education. Drawing on Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications and Dohn’s context-level framework of competence demands, we classify 102 empirical studies",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,1,"engagement place on students and the degree to which those demands align with the practice logic of higher education. Drawing on Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications and Dohn’s context-level framework of competence demands, we classify 102 empirical studies of GenAI in higher educa- tion by entanglement type and examine the literacy demands each type generates. We find that Assistant and Enabler entanglements dominate the corpus, while Gen- erator, Reimaginator, and Synthesizer entanglements remain marginal — a pattern that reflects not technological limitation but increasing tension between epistemic work distribution and institutional competence expectations. These findings suggest three design principles for teachers seeking to develop students’ literacy across the full range of entanglements that a postdigital world requires. Keywords Generative AI · Higher education · Student–GenAI entanglement · Literacy demands * Zhiru Sun zhiru@sdu.dk Nina Bonderup Dohn nina@sdu.dk Martin Rehm mareh@sdu.dk 1 Department of Design, Media, and Educational Science, University of Southern Denmark, Kolding, Denmark",156
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,2,"Postdigital Science and Education Introduction Generative AI (GenAI) refers to AI systems that learn patterns from large- scale training data and generate outputs such as text, images, or code from user prompts (Banh and Strobel 2023). It has rapidly changed what literacy means and demands in higher education (Jiang et al. 2024; Rapanta et al. 2025). While peda- gogy and technology have long been understood as mutually constitutive (Fawns 2022), GenAI makes this relationship more visible and institutionally significant. Students no longer only use digital tools to access information; they now prompt, interpret, evaluate, revise, and co-produce with the system (Zeivots et al. 2025). These practices raise new questions about judgement, authorship, agency, and responsibility in academic work. As GenAI becomes increasingly taken up in higher education, these shifts make it important to ask not only how students use AI, but what it now takes to be literate in AI-entangled educational contexts. A growing body of systematic review research has mapped the use of AI in higher education over the past decade (Zawacki-Richter et al. 2019; Ouyang et al. 2022;",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,2,"but what it now takes to be literate in AI-entangled educational contexts. A growing body of systematic review research has mapped the use of AI in higher education over the past decade (Zawacki-Richter et al. 2019; Ouyang et al. 2022; Crompton and Burke 2023). Across diverse institutional contexts, these reviews show a consistent pattern: AI has been used mainly for prediction, personal- ization, assessment, and student support. This work has been important in clarifying what institutions deploy AI to accomplish. However, it offers less insight into how students engage with AI in practice, how such engagement redistributes epistemic work, and what these emerging forms of participation demand of students as learn- ers. Because most of these reviews were conducted before GenAI entered educa- tional practice at scale, they leave key questions unanswered: what distinct forms of student–GenAI engagement now exist in higher education, which are prevalent, and which remain marginal? Answering these questions is important not only for understanding GenAI use, but also for understanding literacy as it takes shape in a postdigital educational world (Jiang et al. 2024; Rapanta",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,2,"in higher education, which are prevalent, and which remain marginal? Answering these questions is important not only for understanding GenAI use, but also for understanding literacy as it takes shape in a postdigital educational world (Jiang et al. 2024; Rapanta et al. 2025). This paper addresses these questions by treating student–GenAI engagement as entanglement, by which we mean relations of mutual constitution among students, GenAI systems, tasks, and educational setting, through which agency, authorship, and knowledge production are dynamically negotiated rather than pre-allocated to individual actors (Barad 2003; Fawns 2022; Christensen et al. 2025). This fram- ing highlights the impossibility of determining in advance how epistemic work is concretely distributed in any given student–GenAI activity. Here, epistemic work refers to the production, evaluation, transformation, and responsible use of knowl- edge claims, whether carried out by student, GenAI systems, or both (Christensen et al. 2025). Approached in this way, GenAI in higher education makes a long- standing tension newly visible: the tension between institutionally dominant indi- vidualized forms of competence which locate capability within the student and assess it as independent",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,2,"(Christensen et al. 2025). Approached in this way, GenAI in higher education makes a long- standing tension newly visible: the tension between institutionally dominant indi- vidualized forms of competence which locate capability within the student and assess it as independent performance (Anderson et al. 1996) and ‘person-plus’ forms of competence in which competence is understood as situated in the joint action of the person and their tools (Perkins 1997; Wertsch 1998). The distribu- tion of student–GenAI engagement documented in the literature, we will argue, reflects this tension and the institutional accommodations it requires.",93
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,3,"Postdigital Science and Education To investigate the questions, we extend Dohn’s three-level framework of compe- tence demands (Dohn 2009a, b) to GenAI in higher education, analyzing literacy demands as they emerge across domain-internal, activity-internal, and activity-fram- ing levels. To operationalize this framework across a corpus of empirical studies, we extend Strobel et al.’s (2024) taxonomy of GenAI applications — originally derived from commercial products — into a classification lens for the distribution of epis- temic work between student and GenAI in educational settings. Where our extended taxonomy, building on Strobel et al., lets us see how student–GenAI engagements distribute epistemic work across the empirical literature, Dohn’s framework lets us explain why certain distributions sit comfortably with institutional expectations of individual competence and assessment, while others do not, and what this asymme- try demands of students. The article makes two main contributions. Empirically, it classifies GenAI in higher education research through an entanglement-oriented lens, revealing a pat- tern of epistemic work distribution that functional classifications of AI use cannot capture. Theoretically, it explains the resulting patterns by showing how the literacy",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,3,"contributions. Empirically, it classifies GenAI in higher education research through an entanglement-oriented lens, revealing a pat- tern of epistemic work distribution that functional classifications of AI use cannot capture. Theoretically, it explains the resulting patterns by showing how the literacy demands of different entanglement types align — or conflict — with the individual- istic competence expectations that govern higher educational practice. In doing so, the paper contributes to current discussions of literacy, authorship, and agency in a postdigital world. It also derives three design principles from the findings to support teachers in developing students’ literacy across a broader range of human–AI entan- glements in higher education. Background: Landscape of AI Applications in Higher Education Research on AI in higher education has grown substantially over the past decade, with the pace of publication accelerating markedly in the early 2020 s (Crompton and Burke 2023). Across this expanding body of work, a set of recurring func- tional classifications have been consistently identified. By functional classification, we refer to approaches that group AI applications according to the broad educa- tional purpose they are",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,3,"and Burke 2023). Across this expanding body of work, a set of recurring func- tional classifications have been consistently identified. By functional classification, we refer to approaches that group AI applications according to the broad educa- tional purpose they are reported to serve. Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) categorize AI applications into four clusters: profiling and prediction, assessment and evalua- tion, adaptive systems and personalization, and intelligent tutoring systems. Ouyang et al. (2022), focusing on online higher education, report closely aligned functions, including prediction of learning status and performance, resource recommenda- tion, automatic assessment, and improvement of learning experience. Similarly, Crompton and Burke (2023) identify assessment and evaluation, learning manage- ment and support, and prediction of academic success as dominant AI applications. Taken together, these systematic reviews converge on a consistent picture: within the reviewed literature, AI in higher education has primarily been reported in relation to prediction, personalization, assessment, and support. These reviews, however, have two limitations for the present study. First, most were conducted before GenAI entered educational practice at scale and there- fore reflect an earlier technological landscape",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,3,"primarily been reported in relation to prediction, personalization, assessment, and support. These reviews, however, have two limitations for the present study. First, most were conducted before GenAI entered educational practice at scale and there- fore reflect an earlier technological landscape in which AI operated primarily as institutional infrastructure — acting on students rather than with them. Second,",57
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,4,"Postdigital Science and Education and more fundamentally, they classify AI by functional purpose, that is, by what institutions and instructors deploy it to accomplish. This orientation is valuable for showing what AI has been used for, but it offers limited insight into how students engage with AI in practice or how work is distributed between student and system. This limitation is particularly consequential in the era of GenAI. Unlike earlier AI systems, GenAI enables students to participate more directly in prompting, revising, evaluating, and co-producing knowledge with AI (Ahmed and Ali 2024). The educational significance of GenAI therefore lies not only in what technology does, but in how students work with it in situated practice. From this perspective, functional classifications are insufficient on their own. They cannot adequately capture how different forms of student–GenAI engage- ment redistribute creative and epistemic work or why some forms of engagement become more prevalent than others. To address this gap, we draw on two complementary frameworks: Dohn’s context-level framework of competence demands (Dohn 2009a, b) and Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications (Strobel",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,4,"work or why some forms of engagement become more prevalent than others. To address this gap, we draw on two complementary frameworks: Dohn’s context-level framework of competence demands (Dohn 2009a, b) and Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications (Strobel et al. 2024). Together, they allow us to classify forms of student–GenAI entanglement and analyze what those forms require of students. Our classification therefore focuses on the reported role GenAI plays within the learning activity, rather than on the GenAI system as a technical object. The next section introduces these frameworks in more detail. Theoretical Framework This section introduces the two complementary conceptual lenses that guide the analysis. Dohn’s context-level framework of competence demands (Dohn 2009a, b) provides the analytical foundation for examining literacy demands across domain-internal, activity-internal, and activity-framing levels. It offers a way of considering how higher education shapes what students are expected to know, do, and justify when working with GenAI. Strobel et al.’s (2024) taxon- omy of GenAI applications, extended here into educational settings, provides the classificatory lens for mapping student–GenAI engagements as different distribu- tions",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,4,"shapes what students are expected to know, do, and justify when working with GenAI. Strobel et al.’s (2024) taxon- omy of GenAI applications, extended here into educational settings, provides the classificatory lens for mapping student–GenAI engagements as different distribu- tions of epistemic work. In this sense, Strobel et al.’s taxonomy helps identify the forms that student–GenAI engagement takes, while Dohn’s framework supports analysis of the competence demands and institutional conditions under which these forms become institutionally accommodated. Together, the frameworks allow the paper to move from classification to interpretation: Strobel’s taxonomy maps distributions of epistemic work, while Dohn’s framework helps explain their literacy demands and institutional implications. The final subsection brings the two frameworks together to surface the central tension in this paper: between competence understood as individually possessed and as distributed across the person-plus-tools entanglement. This tension, we argue, shapes which forms of student–GenAI engagement higher education has accommodated, and which it has not.",155
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,5,"Postdigital Science and Education Dohn’s Framework: Literacy Demands Across Context Levels We follow Gee (2015) in understanding literacy as participation in societal forms of Discourse. From this perspective, literacy in education engaging GenAI can be understood as the ability to participate in academic practices with GenAI in ways that are valued within those practices. These valued ways, whether implicit or explicit, transparent or contested, constitute literacy demands on students: to be recognized as competent is to act in ways that align with the expectations of the practice. Given the postdigital entanglement of the digital and non-digital in today’s world, such participation will necessarily be entangled and emergent, and, as stressed by Christensen et al. (2025), one cannot ahead of investigation presuppose any specific distribution of roles that human and non-human actors will have in any given situation. This, however, does not imply that absolutely anything could happen — the features of technologies, the life history of the humans, and the social norms of the institutional setting are all aspects that will constrain distribution possibilities. Therefore, it still makes sense to",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,5,"not imply that absolutely anything could happen — the features of technologies, the life history of the humans, and the social norms of the institutional setting are all aspects that will constrain distribution possibilities. Therefore, it still makes sense to speak of affordances of technologies, provided they are understood relationally rather than deterministically, as relative to the interaction potential of the people present, that is, as relative to what they (personally, socially, ethically, culturally etc.) can do in that situation (Dohn 2009c). This is the role we assign to Strobel et al.’s taxonomy, to be presented in the next section: it helps cate- gorize existing uses of GenAI in educational practices, without presupposing a fixed specific distribution of agency between student and GenAI. Utilizing Bourdieu’s concept of practice logic (Bourdieu 1977, 1990), we further inquire into how the practice logic of a given setting — the inherent expectations, values, presuppositions, patterns of agency etc. — play into deciding, in the concrete situation, what the people ‘can do’, and, especially, what counts as valued ways of entangling with the technologies, that",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,5,"of a given setting — the inherent expectations, values, presuppositions, patterns of agency etc. — play into deciding, in the concrete situation, what the people ‘can do’, and, especially, what counts as valued ways of entangling with the technologies, that is, as literacy in education engaging GenAI. For this inquiry, we introduce Dohn’ (2009a, b) competence level framework. Dohn used this framework to show that the introduction of Web 2.0 tools and activities into education led to incoherent competence demands on students, because of con- flicting understandings of knowledge inherent in the practice logics of educational systems and Web 2.0, respectively. Dohn argued that, in consequence, the most important skill for students in practice became the ability to maneuver between the conflicting demands and, in particular, to attune to the teacher’s (implicit) expecta- tions for how this maneuvering should be performed. Notably, Dohn uses the termi- nology of competence, not literacy. However, she states that ‘Exercising competence in any situation consists in acting adequately in relation to the complex of demand characteristics of the situation.’ (Dohn 2009a: 116). If one",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,5,"performed. Notably, Dohn uses the termi- nology of competence, not literacy. However, she states that ‘Exercising competence in any situation consists in acting adequately in relation to the complex of demand characteristics of the situation.’ (Dohn 2009a: 116). If one understands ‘the com- plex of demand characteristics’ as the requirements of ‘societal forms of Discourse’, and ‘acting adequately’ as ‘participation’ in accordance with these requirements, this broad understanding of competence aligns with Gee’s (2015) view of literacy, utilized here. Therefore, Dohn’s framework can be considered to apply to literacy demands as well. Dohn explicates three context levels, at which situational demands are placed on students. The levels are analytical; that is, they are not claimed to exist or exert influ- ence in isolation from one another. Rather, the point is that demands interact with",134
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,6,"Postdigital Science and Education — frame, concretize, interfere and contradict — each other across the levels, as peo- ple respond to them, resulting in a unity of demands which is concretely and dynam- ically negotiated in every concrete situation and may well be incoherent. From a postdigital perspective, the unity of demands cannot be predicted in advance but emerges in the dynamic interplay of humans, technologies, institutional structures, and norms. The three levels are as follows: (1) The domain-internal level, determined by the academic domain in question, for example literary novels, organic chemistry, electromagnetism, linguistic patterns — and GenAI (as domain). Literacy demands at this level concern domain- specific traits, facts, and perspectives. Examples include that magical happenings are appropriate in fairy tales, that coupling resistances in parallel decreases the total resistance, and that GenAI can be biased due to bias in the training data. (2) The activity-internal level, determined by the activity itself, for instance group discussions, reading a book, listening to a lecture, or writing an essay, letter, or report (individually or collaboratively) with GenAI support. Literacy demands",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,6,"bias in the training data. (2) The activity-internal level, determined by the activity itself, for instance group discussions, reading a book, listening to a lecture, or writing an essay, letter, or report (individually or collaboratively) with GenAI support. Literacy demands at this level concern how the activity is performed, for example, integrating inputs from GenAI to form a coherent piece of writing. (3) The activity-framing level, determined by the actual setting in which the activity is taking place, for instance education, leisure practice, or working life. Literacy demands at this level concern how the activity is framed (norm-set) by the set- ting in question. For example, in professional life, writing a report to one’s boss that presents key points in a precise, clear, and preferably brief manner, and, in school, not handing in a report as one’s own that has been written by someone else. The framework is useful for this paper’s analysis because it enables literacy demands to be examined not as a single generic set but as context-sensitive expectations that may align or conflict across the three levels.",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,6,"been written by someone else. The framework is useful for this paper’s analysis because it enables literacy demands to be examined not as a single generic set but as context-sensitive expectations that may align or conflict across the three levels. Strobel’s Taxonomy: Mapping Types of Entanglement Strobel et al. (2024) developed an empirically grounded taxonomy of GenAI struc- tured analysis of 100 real-world applications across commercial and professional domains. Employing an iterative empirical-to-conceptual design approach (Kun- disch et al. 2022), they identified five distinct types of GenAI use: Generator, Reimaginator, Synthesizer, Assistant, and Enabler. The types differ systematically along two underlying dimensions: (1) the degree of creativity — the extent to which GenAI output aims to innovate rather than improve, and 2) the degree of modifica- tion — the extent to which the GenAI output departs from the input. These two dimensions position the five types within a coherent conceptual space (see Fig. 1). Although the taxonomy was originally designed to describe commercial GenAI applications, for our purpose, its organizing logic can also apply to educational set- tings. At its",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,6,"two dimensions position the five types within a coherent conceptual space (see Fig. 1). Although the taxonomy was originally designed to describe commercial GenAI applications, for our purpose, its organizing logic can also apply to educational set- tings. At its core, Strobel et al.’s framework captures the relationship between user input and GenAI output, and the way different types of GenAI use distribute crea- tive and epistemic work between human users and system. This relational structure also characterizes student–GenAI engagement, where the analytic question concerns",85
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,7,"Postdigital Science and Education how epistemic work is shared between student and GenAI within a learning activity. The five categories thus provide a useful lens for classifying different forms of stu- dent–GenAI engagement according to their distribution of epistemic work. Table 1 presents each category’s original definition alongside its operational definition in higher education. Within our extended taxonomy, Generator captures engagements in which GenAI produces educational artifacts from minimal student input with the student’s role being primarily evaluative, for instance, a nursing student prompting GenAI to draft a patient care plan form a brief case description. Reimaginator refers to cross-modal or stylistic transformation of existing materials, such as converting an English-lan- guage health document into a Thai-language explainer video. Synthesizer captures the generation of artificial but disciplinary grounded materials, such as synthetic questions, cases, or scenarios for student practice. Assistant describes bounded sup- port, including feedback, correction, or suggestions on work authored by the student, such as writing assistant providing structural feedback on a student-authored essay. The fifth category, Enabler, requires more substantial interpretation. In Stroebel et al.’s (2024) original",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,7,"describes bounded sup- port, including feedback, correction, or suggestions on work authored by the student, such as writing assistant providing structural feedback on a student-authored essay. The fifth category, Enabler, requires more substantial interpretation. In Stroebel et al.’s (2024) original taxonomy, Enabler denotes infrastructure components such as platforms or model pipelines. In educational settings, infrastructure provision is rarely the central pedagogical concern. We therefore reinterpret Enabler as a com- petence-scaffolding role, in which the primary pedagogical purpose is the develop- ment of higher-order student competences, including critical thinking, self-regulated learning, creativity, and literacy, rather than the completion of an immediate task. A structured argumentation exercise in which a student engages GenAI to strengthen their critical thinking exemplifies this configuration: the educational purpose lies in developing the competence itself. Extended in this way, the taxonomy building on Strobel et al. (2024) enables anal- ysis of how different forms of student–GenAI engagement distribute epistemic work, Fig. 1 A framework to position GenAI roles in entangled learning (Strobel et al. 2024)",168
