DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,11,"Postdigital Science and Education but it does not explain why some distributions become institutionally accommo- dated, while others remain contested. The section ‘Tensions in Literacy Demands’ addresses this gap by bridging our extended taxonomy to the three contextual levels of Dohn’s framework. Tensions in Literacy Demands Dohn argued that integrating Web 2.0 tools and practices into education generated conceptual discrepances between two understandings of knowledge: knowledge as an individually possessable object (implicit in educational practices), and knowledge as participation and situated doing (implicit in Web 2.0 practices) (Dohn 2009a, b). Very similarly, implementing GenAI in education leads to a clash of understand- ings of tool-mediated competence. The situation is more complex for GenAI than for Web 2.0, because GenAI allows for much more comprehensively entangled dis- tributions of authorship between humans and machines than is possible with Web 2.0. In addition, GenAI contributions are opaque as regards origin and process, because they utilize statistical models building non-transparently on training data which may furthermore be biased. However, as educational settings dominantly involve an understanding of tools as external to competence, for",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,11,"addition, GenAI contributions are opaque as regards origin and process, because they utilize statistical models building non-transparently on training data which may furthermore be biased. However, as educational settings dominantly involve an understanding of tools as external to competence, for the present purpose of analyzing literacy demands, it suffices to consider the basic discrepancy between this understanding and one that views tool-use as an inherent part of situated doing. The understanding dominant in educational settings is individualistic and person- focused, viewing competence as an object that resides in — and must be evaluated for — the individual (Anderson et al. 1996). On this view, tools are to be evalu- ated by their potential to let individuals express their individually possessed com- petence. Tools that radically change the level of competence at which an individual can perform are viewed as problematic as they obscure the true expression of the individually possessed competence. Historically, the pocket calculator and the flex- ible pole have been seen by proponents of this approach as cheating, as the tools did not allow the true expression of",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,11,"as they obscure the true expression of the individually possessed competence. Historically, the pocket calculator and the flex- ible pole have been seen by proponents of this approach as cheating, as the tools did not allow the true expression of competence in calculating and pole vaulting, respec- tively (Wertsch 1998). Today, both tools are widely accepted. Wertsch, as exponent of a sociocultural approach to tool-mediated competence, argues that this historical development shows that a tool’s status as affording cheating or, alternatively, compe- tence-expression is cultural-historically dependent and changes over time. The sec- ond understanding, characteristic of professional practice with GenAI, is sociocul- tural: competence is situated doing distributed between persons and tools (Perkins 1997; Wertsch 1998; Code 2025). Perkins speaks of the ‘person-plus’, arguing for a view of competence as what the person-plus-tools can accomplish together in the situation. As Strobel et al.’s (2024) taxonomy documents, GenAI use in professional practice operates predominantly on this premise: what matters is what the entan- gled person-plus-GenAI can produce, not the internal distribution of work within the entanglement. Dohn (2009a, b) argued that",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,11,"Strobel et al.’s (2024) taxonomy documents, GenAI use in professional practice operates predominantly on this premise: what matters is what the entan- gled person-plus-GenAI can produce, not the internal distribution of work within the entanglement. Dohn (2009a, b) argued that introducing Web 2.0 tools and practices into educa- tion meant introducing at the activity-internal level a view of knowledge as situ- ated doing. She further pointed out that this creates tensions because the educational",74
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,12,"Postdigital Science and Education settings — operating at the activity-framing level — inherently involve a view of knowledge as individually possessable object. Very similarly, when GenAI activi- ties are introduced into educational practice, this may correspond to introducing the ‘person-plus-competence’ logic at the activity-internal level (depending on how GenAI is used). As the educational setting — at the activity-framing level — remains governed by individualistic and person-focused competence expectations, this predictably can generate tensions. These tensions are amply illustrated by the extensive discussions in research and practice of GenAI as cheating (Corbin et al. 2025; Korseberg and Elken 2025) or, alternatively, as legitimate learning support (Ng et al. 2024; Nguyen et al. 2024). Importantly, however, these tensions do not arise uniformly across all forms of student–GenAI entanglement. Because the five entanglement types represented in our extended taxonomy distribute epistemic work between student and GenAI differently, the conflict between practice logics emerges to different degrees and in different forms across the types. Method We conducted a structured rapid review of the GenAI in higher education literature. We adopted the PRISMA-ScR reporting",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,12,"between student and GenAI differently, the conflict between practice logics emerges to different degrees and in different forms across the types. Method We conducted a structured rapid review of the GenAI in higher education literature. We adopted the PRISMA-ScR reporting extension (Tricco et al. 2018) as a guiding framework for transparency, while acknowledging the scope limitations appropri- ate to an analytical paper. A full account of the search strategy, including the search string and inclusion and exclusion criteria, is provided in Appendix 1. Scopus was selected as the main database on the basis of its broad and comple- mentary coverage of peer-reviewed research in educational technology and GenAI in higher education. The initial search returned 5762 records. Title and abstract screening against the pre-specified criteria (see Appendix 2) yielded a final corpus of 102 papers. One distinct feature of our inclusion criteria was the requirement that the abstract identify GenAI performing a specific function within teaching or learn- ing activities. This requirement reflected the study’s focus on the reported role of GenAI in learning activities, rather than the mere presence",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,12,"inclusion criteria was the requirement that the abstract identify GenAI performing a specific function within teaching or learn- ing activities. This requirement reflected the study’s focus on the reported role of GenAI in learning activities, rather than the mere presence of a GenAI tool. We also excluded studies that mentioned GenAI but did not provide sufficient informa- tion to determine the form of student–GenAI engagement, such as studies examining adoption, attitudes, or general impact without specifying what the tool was used for in learning. This criterion was designed to ensure that the corpus contained studies capable of supporting classification by our extended taxonomy. Classification proceeded in four steps. First, the authors jointly developed a cod- ing schema that operationalized Strobel et al.’s five GenAI application types for higher education contexts. As shown in Table 1, the schema includes each category’s original definition, its operational definition contextualized in higher education, and anchor examples from the corpus. Second, two authors independently classified the final corpus using the schema. The unit of analysis was the published research study, and the analytical question was",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,12,"original definition, its operational definition contextualized in higher education, and anchor examples from the corpus. Second, two authors independently classified the final corpus using the schema. The unit of analysis was the published research study, and the analytical question was which type of student–GenAI entanglement each study foregrounded as central to its research design and outcomes, Since this was a single-valued analytical question, each study was assigned one primary category. Third, inter-rater reliability was 91.09%, indicating strong agreement (Krippendorff",79
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,13,"Postdigital Science and Education 2019). Finally, disagreements were discussed and resolved by consensus, with the coding schema used as the reference point. The resolved classification forms the basis for the analysis presented in the following section. Results Distribution of Student–GenAI Entanglement Types in Higher Education Research Based on the structured rapid review, we found a skewed distribution across Strobel et al.’s GenAI application types (see Table 2). Assistant and Enabler applications dominate the corpus, each accounting for 42.16% of studies, whereas Synthesizer, Generator, and Reimaginator applications are comparatively rare and together con- stitute less than one fifth of the corpus. This pattern is unlikely to be attributed to technological limitations, as the same large language models (e.g., ChatGPT) that underpin Assistant and Enabler applications are also fully capable of support- ing Generator, Reimaginator, or Synthesizer functions. The observed distribu- tion therefore reflects less what GenAI can do than which types of student–GenAI entanglement have become legitimate and adoptable within higher education prac- tice. Essentially, this asymmetry raises a question: why do Assistant and Enabler applications more readily fit existing institutional",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,13,"therefore reflects less what GenAI can do than which types of student–GenAI entanglement have become legitimate and adoptable within higher education prac- tice. Essentially, this asymmetry raises a question: why do Assistant and Enabler applications more readily fit existing institutional structures, while Generator and Reimaginator applications remain marginal? Addressing this requires shifting the analytical focus from technological capability to demands on students, using Dohn’s (2009a, b) framework to examine how these entanglements align with or challenge existing higher education literacy expectations. Literacy Demands Across Types of Student–GenAI Entanglement The following analysis examines each entanglement type through Dohn’s (2009a, b) three context levels: domain-internal, activity-internal, and activity-framing. It shows a gradual movement from coherence to tension. Assistant, which accounts for 42.16% of the corpus, generates the most coher- ent pattern of literacy demand across the three levels. At the domain-internal level, disciplinary expectations about the subject matter in question are not affected much because GenAI functions as a refinement tool rather than as a source of disciplinary Table 2 Distribution of 102 papers across Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,13,disciplinary expectations about the subject matter in question are not affected much because GenAI functions as a refinement tool rather than as a source of disciplinary Table 2 Distribution of 102 papers across Strobel et al.’s taxonomy of GenAI applications Strobel category N (paper) % of corpus Assistant 43 42.16% Enabler 43 42.16% Syntehsizer 5 4.90% Reimaginator 3 2.94% Generator 8 7.84% Total 102 100%,65
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,14,"Postdigital Science and Education judgement. At the activity-internal level, GenAI operates at the margins of the stu- dents’ work, offering feedback, corrections, and suggestions on outputs that are pri- marily student-produced. Examples include GenAI providing feedback on students’ writing (Mahapatra 2024; Zhang et al. 2025) and discipline-specific question-and- answer support (Al-Abri 2025). Students first develop their work independently and consult GenAI only to qualify or refine it. At the activity-framing level, this distribution aligns straightforwardly with institutional expectations that students demonstrate individually possessed competence, using tools only to express such competence. In this entanglement, disciplinary expectations, activity demands, and institutional recognition are closely aligned. Enabler, which accounts for 42.16% of the corpus, also appears coherent across the three levels, although the coherence rests on a latent tension. At the domain- internal level, Enabler engagements target higher-order competences, such as criti- cal thinking, self-regulated learning, and literacy, which are conventionally framed as individual capacities, even when developed with GenAI support. At the activity- internal level, GenAI functions as a developmental partner rather than as a producer of assessable outputs. Examples include",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,14,"cal thinking, self-regulated learning, and literacy, which are conventionally framed as individual capacities, even when developed with GenAI support. At the activity- internal level, GenAI functions as a developmental partner rather than as a producer of assessable outputs. Examples include the use of GenAI for enhancing critical thinking (de la Puente et al. 2024; Nasr et al. 2025), creativity (Toma and Yánez- Pérez 2024; Zhang and Ge 2025), and self-regulated learning (Wang et al. 2025). At the activity-framing level, this entanglement remains institutionally legitimate because GenAI is positioned as a means through which students develop compe- tences that are ultimately attributed to them. The tension lies in the fact that these competences are developed, and often exercised, through continued entanglement with GenAI. The boundary between individually possessed competence and person- plus competence is inherently blurred. This tension is rarely made explicit, as it remains unnoticed at the activity-framing level. Synthesizer, which accounts for 4.90% of the corpus, marks the point at which coherence across levels begins to weaken. At the domain-internal level, synthetic cases and scenarios introduce ambiguity because they",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,14,"explicit, as it remains unnoticed at the activity-framing level. Synthesizer, which accounts for 4.90% of the corpus, marks the point at which coherence across levels begins to weaken. At the domain-internal level, synthetic cases and scenarios introduce ambiguity because they can replicate the structure of authentic disciplinary materials such as writing samples (AbdAlgane et al. 2026)1 and medical case scenarios (Shen et al. 2025a, b), without originating in discipli- nary practice. At the activity-internal level, students engage these outputs both as learning resources and as objects of critical scrutiny. They must evaluate, critique, and build on material that resembles disciplinary content that has been generated artificially. At the activity-framing level, this dual orientation creates tension with the institutional logic of individual competence. Synthetic outputs are acceptable as learning resources, but when students use them generatively, the boundary between using a resource and co-producing knowledge with GenAI becomes less clear. The institutional setting can accommodate this tension, but mainly by treating synthetic material as a resource rather than an epistemic contribution. Generator, which accounts for 7.84% of the corpus, intensifies the",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,14,"resource and co-producing knowledge with GenAI becomes less clear. The institutional setting can accommodate this tension, but mainly by treating synthetic material as a resource rather than an epistemic contribution. Generator, which accounts for 7.84% of the corpus, intensifies the conflicts across three levels. At the domain-internal level, its legitimacy depends strongly on 1 This paper was published online ahead of print before the December 2025 cutoff and indexed by Sco- pus accordingly; its 2026 date reflects the assigned issue, and it was retained on these grounds.",87
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,15,"Postdigital Science and Education disciplinary and professional context. In fields where professional practice already includes GenAI-generated outputs such as visual arts (Ansone et al. 2025), clinical assessment item construction (Zhang et al. 2025), or nursing care planning (Shen et al. 2025a, b), Generator engagements can align with emerging domain expec- tations. Where such professional framing is absent, this alignment is weaker. At the activity-internal level, GenAI plays the primary generative role, while students contribute through prompting, curating, and evaluating outputs. This distribution reflects a ‘person-plus-competence’ logic, in which the educational artifact emerges from the student–GenAI entanglement. At the activity-framing level, however, this arrangement conflicts with the dominant institutional expectation that students pro- duce the primary educational artifact themselves. The corpus thus suggests that Generator engagement becomes institutionally legitimate mainly where an addi- tional professional framing can justify the distribution of epistemic work. Where the professional framing is absent, the Generator entanglement is institutionally illegiti- mate and consequently less visible in the corpus. The Reimaginator, accounting for 2.94% of the corpus, generates the most acute conflict across three context levels. At",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,15,"epistemic work. Where the professional framing is absent, the Generator entanglement is institutionally illegiti- mate and consequently less visible in the corpus. The Reimaginator, accounting for 2.94% of the corpus, generates the most acute conflict across three context levels. At the domain-internal level, students must judge whether a transformed GenAI output remains epistemically faithful to the source material. This judgement requires disciplinary expertise that students are often still developing, as in the conversion of English-language health information scripts into personalized patient information videos in Thai (Badawy et al. 2025). At the activity-internal level, the conflict becomes especially pronounced. Higher edu- cation typically expects students to demonstrate individually possessed competence, yet the Reimaginator entanglement blurs the boundaries of authorship and epistemic ownership. Thus, what the activity requires and what the institution recognizes as legitimate competence becomes difficult to align, making the entanglement espe- cially incoherent within existing educational structures. Discussion The results indicate that the skewed distribution of GenAI use in higher education is not primarily a technological phenomenon but a pedagogical one. The dominance of Assistant and Enabler entanglements reflects",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,15,"entanglement espe- cially incoherent within existing educational structures. Discussion The results indicate that the skewed distribution of GenAI use in higher education is not primarily a technological phenomenon but a pedagogical one. The dominance of Assistant and Enabler entanglements reflects the gravitational pull of educational practice toward distributions of epistemic work that are compatible with individual- istic person-focused competence. Synthesizer, Generator, and Reimaginator entan- glements remain marginal not because they are technologically demanding, but because their literacy demands fracture across three levels: what the activity asks students to do at the activity-internal level diverges from what the institutional set- ting recognizes as legitimate competence at the activity-framing level. If students are to develop the full range of literacy demanded by a postdigital world — one in which all five types of entanglement are operative in professional and civic life — then expanding teachers’ repertoire of GenAI activities may not be sufficient. Students also need support in recognizing, navigating, and negotiating the literacy demands of different types of entanglement. We therefore propose three design principles.",174
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,15,"then expanding teachers’ repertoire of GenAI activities may not be sufficient. Students also need support in recognizing, navigating, and negotiating the literacy demands of different types of entanglement. We therefore propose three design principles.",34
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,16,"Postdigital Science and Education Transparency: Making Literacy Demand Explicit The first design principle is transparency: teachers could make the literacy demands of each type of entanglement explicit to students. As shown in the results section, Generator, Reimaginator, and Synthesizer entanglements often involved conflicting demands that are rarely acknowledged, leaving students to navigate tensions they may not fully understand. For Assistant and Enabler activ- ities, where literacy demands largely cohere across levels, transparency might involve clarifying what individual competence means in GenAI-supported work, including where the boundary lies between legitimate use and overreliance, and why that boundary matters. For Generator, Reimaginator, and Synthesizer activi- ties, where literacy demands are more fractured across levels, transparency might involve naming the tension directly. For example, in Reimaginator entangle- ment, students are asked to take epistemic responsibility for outputs they did not produce in a conventional sense, while still being evaluated as if the work were individually authored. Teachers could therefore clarify how epistemic work may be distributed between students and GenAI, what counts as legitimate participa- tion, and how the activity relates to the",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,16,"conventional sense, while still being evaluated as if the work were individually authored. Teachers could therefore clarify how epistemic work may be distributed between students and GenAI, what counts as legitimate participa- tion, and how the activity relates to the institutional expectations that frame it. When students can identify the tensions embedded in an activity, they are bet- ter positioned to respond to them productively rather than experiencing them as confusion. Progression: Sequencing Activities Across Entanglement Types The second design principle is progression: teachers could consider designing activ- ities that span multiple entanglement types. The current dominance of Assistant and Enabler entanglements means that many students encounter mainly the most institu- tionally coherent distributions of epistemic work throughout their higher education experience. This narrowness has consequences: students may graduate into profes- sional and civic contexts where Generator, Reimaginator, and Synthesizer entangle- ments are increasingly common, without having developed the literacy needed to navigate them. Teachers can address this by designing activity sequences that move gradually across the entanglement spectrum. A workable starting point is Assis- tant or Enabler activities, where",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,16,"entangle- ments are increasingly common, without having developed the literacy needed to navigate them. Teachers can address this by designing activity sequences that move gradually across the entanglement spectrum. A workable starting point is Assis- tant or Enabler activities, where literacy demands are relatively coherent and stu- dents can focus on calibrating GenAI’s role in relation to their own work. From this foundation, teachers can then introduce Synthesizer, Generator, and Reimaginator activities to develop students’ confidence, critical awareness, and capacity to man- age more distributed forms of epistemic work. This progression is not simply about exposure to different GenAI tools. Its pedagogical purpose is to develop students’ competence to recognize how epistemic work is being distributed in a given GenAI activity, to evaluate whether that distribution is appropriate to the context, and to take informed responsibility for their own role within it. In this way, progression across the entanglement spectrum could expand both the range of epistemic distri- butions students can navigate and the forms of literacy they can exercise.",170
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,16,"within it. In this way, progression across the entanglement spectrum could expand both the range of epistemic distri- butions students can navigate and the forms of literacy they can exercise.",30
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,17,"Postdigital Science and Education Reflection and Negotiation: Developing Meta‑level Literacy The third design principle is reflection and negotiation, not just compliance. As Dohn (2009a, b) argued for Web 2.0, and as we showed above for GenAI, differ- ent entanglement types distribute epistemic work in structurally distinct ways. These distributions generate different, and sometimes conflicting, literacy demands across the three levels. The key competence students need is therefore not mastery of any single entanglement type, but the capacity to recognize the implicit expectations of a given context and negotiate tensions when those expectations conflict. This is a meta-level literacy demand: the ability to understand what an activity requires, how epistemic responsibility is distributed, and how to act appropriately within that entanglement. Teachers can design for this capacity by building explicit reflection into GenAI activities: asking students not only to complete a task with GenAI but to articulate what the activity demanded of them, where they experienced tension or uncertainty, and how they navigated it. Over time, such reflective practice can develop students’ competence to recognize the literacy demands of novel entangle-",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,17,"task with GenAI but to articulate what the activity demanded of them, where they experienced tension or uncertainty, and how they navigated it. Over time, such reflective practice can develop students’ competence to recognize the literacy demands of novel entangle- ment types they have not previously encountered. Conclusion This study examined student–GenAI engagement in higher education through the lens of entanglement, asking not only how GenAI is used, but what different forms of use demand of students as participants in educational practice. Drawing on a structured rapid review of 102 empirical studies, we identified a markedly uneven distribution across five forms of student–GenAI entanglement. Assistant and Ena- bler forms dominate, while Synthesizer, Generator, and Reimaginator forms remain comparatively marginal. We argue that this pattern is not primarily explained by technological capability. Rather, it reflects how different distributions of epistemic work align with the prac- tice logic of higher education. Assistant and Enabler entanglements largely preserve the expectation that competence resides in, and can ultimately be attributed to, the individual student. By contrast, Generator, Reimaginator, and, to a lesser extent, Synthesizer",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,17,"align with the prac- tice logic of higher education. Assistant and Enabler entanglements largely preserve the expectation that competence resides in, and can ultimately be attributed to, the individual student. By contrast, Generator, Reimaginator, and, to a lesser extent, Synthesizer entanglements more visibly distribute epistemic work across the stu- dent–GenAI relation, creating tensions between what learning activities require and what educational institutions recognize as legitimate competence. Understanding these tensions is consequential because students will increasingly encounter distributed forms of human–AI competence beyond higher education. Pre- paring them for this postdigital world therefore requires more than teaching effective or responsible GenAI use. It requires pedagogical opportunities to engage students with different forms of human–AI entanglement, understand how epistemic work and responsibility are distributed within them, and negotiate the potentially competing lit- eracy demands they generate. Transparency, progression across entanglement types, and explicit reflection can help make these demands pedagogically visible. From this perspective, GenAI literacy is not simply competence in using AI, but competence",163
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,18,"Postdigital Science and Education in navigating what it means to know, act, and take responsibility within entangled human–AI practices. Limitations Three methodological limitations should be noted. First, each study was classified according to a single primary Strobel type. This approach was appropriate for identify- ing which form of student–GenAI engagement each study foregrounded, but it sim- plifies the hybrid nature of many actual entanglements. Some studies included multi- ple forms of GenAI use, which a multi-label coding approach could examine in future research. Second, the literacy demands identified through Dohn’s (2009a, b) framework are theoretically inferred rather than empirically validated. The analysis is based on how GenAI use is described in published studies, not on students’ own accounts of what they experience as demanding, confusing, or contradictory. Future research could address this through interviews, reflective accounts, or classroom observation. Third, the corpus reflects what has been studied and published, rather than the full range of GenAI use enacted in classrooms. The observed distribution may therefore reflect publication conventions, research tractability, or funding priorities, as well as patterns of institutional accommodation.",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,18,"the corpus reflects what has been studied and published, rather than the full range of GenAI use enacted in classrooms. The observed distribution may therefore reflect publication conventions, research tractability, or funding priorities, as well as patterns of institutional accommodation. Classroom-level studies are needed to examine how GenAI entanglements are actually practiced in higher education. Appendix 1: Search Strategy Searches were conducted in February 2026 and covered publications from January 2022 to December 2025, capturing the period following the public release of ChatGPT in November 2022, which marks the beginning of the current wave of AI research in higher education. The following search string was applied to titles, abstracts and key words: Search String (‘generative artificial intelligence’ OR ‘GAI’ OR ‘GenAI’ OR ‘ChatGPT’ OR ‘large language model’ OR ‘LLM’ OR ‘GPT’) AND (‘higher education’ OR ‘university’ OR ‘tertiary education’ OR ‘undergraduate’ OR ‘postgraduate’) AND (‘learning’ OR ‘teaching’ OR ‘student’ OR ‘pedagogy’ OR ‘instruction’) No language restrictions were applied at the search stage. Only English-language papers were retained at the screening stage, for reasons of analytical consistency across the author team.",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,18,"‘undergraduate’ OR ‘postgraduate’) AND (‘learning’ OR ‘teaching’ OR ‘student’ OR ‘pedagogy’ OR ‘instruction’) No language restrictions were applied at the search stage. Only English-language papers were retained at the screening stage, for reasons of analytical consistency across the author team. The search string was intentionally broad at the identification stage; precision was introduced progressively through the screening and eligibility procedures described below.",62
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,19,"Postdigital Science and Education Appendix 2: Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria Inclusion and exclusion criteria were established prospectively — that is, prior to any screening — in order to minimize post hoc selection bias (Moher et al. 2009). Papers were included if they met all of the following criteria: • Published in a peer-reviewed journal between January 2022 and December 2025 • Reported an empirical study, defined as involving systematic data collection from participants, classrooms, or documented educational practice • Situated in a higher education context, defined as undergraduate or postgradu- ate university study • Examined a specific application of a named AI tool (e.g., ChatGPT, DALL-E, GitHub Copilot, Gemini, Claude) in the context of student learning or teach- ing practice • Available in full text Papers were excluded if they: • Focused solely on student or educator perceptions of, or attitudes toward, AI in the abstract, without examining a specific AI application in practice • Were set exclusively in K–12, vocational, or professional training contexts rather than higher education • Were purely theoretical, opinion-based, or policy-oriented, without empirical data •",180
DOC_063,s42438-026-00697-7.pdf,10.1007/s42438-026-00697-7,19,"attitudes toward, AI in the abstract, without examining a specific AI application in practice • Were set exclusively in K–12, vocational, or professional training contexts rather than higher education • Were purely theoretical, opinion-based, or policy-oriented, without empirical data • Were not available in English • Were not available in full text Author Contribution The corresponding author, A, conceived and designed the research, conducted the systematic literature search, classified the research studies using Strobel’s taxonomy, and wrote the main manuscript text. The second author, B, contributed to the development of the research ideas and wrote sections of the manuscript. The author C assisted with the classification of research studies using Strobel’s taxonomy, contributed to the initial draft, and provided comments on earlier drafts. All authors reviewed and approved the final manuscript. Funding Open access funding provided by University of Southern Denmark. Data Availability The corpus of 102 studies classified in this analysis, including the coding of each study according to Strobel’s taxonomy, is available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Competing Interests The authors declare no competing interests.",180
