DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,1,"obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n​s​ e​s​/​b​y​-​n​c​-​n​d​/​4​.​0​/. Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 https://doi.org/10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8 *Correspondence: Woong Suh nanwoong@gknu.ac.kr 1Institute of General Education, Gyeongguk National University, Andong, Korea Generative AI integration in higher education shifts students’ attitudes from tool use to innovation Woong Suh1* 1 Introduction Recent advances in generative AI are calling for new changes in education. In particular, the emergence of generative AI, such as ChatGPT, has highlighted the importance of AI literacy in higher education. AI literacy goes beyond the simple use of tools and includes understanding how AI works, critically evaluating it, and utilizing it ethically [16]. As a result, universities are seeking to innovate their educational programs to move away from traditional ICT education and toward strengthening AI literacy. However, despite this proliferation of AI education, there is a lack of empirical research on how students’ attitudes change after experiencing generative AI in practice. The importance of understanding students’ attitudes toward AI in higher education cannot be overstated. Recent research demonstrates that students’ attitudes signifi­ cantly predict",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,1,"is a lack of empirical research on how students’ attitudes change after experiencing generative AI in practice. The importance of understanding students’ attitudes toward AI in higher education cannot be overstated. Recent research demonstrates that students’ attitudes signifi­ cantly predict their engagement with AI tools, with responsible use intentions and posi­ tive emotions serving as strongest predictors of constructive AI adoption. As generative AI becomes ubiquitous in educational environments, understanding how pedagogical interventions shape these attitudes from initial apprehension to confident innovation is critical for developing effective AI literacy programs. Discover Education Abstract The rapid development of generative AI has brought significant changes to the field of education. This study examines the impact of a project using generative AI in a class on the use of productivity software at university and university students’ attitudes towards AI. This study used a pre-post survey design to measure changes in students’ cognitive, affective, and behavioral attitudes toward AI. The results indicate a significant positive shift in students’ perspectives. This study provides empirical evidence that it is important to expand hands-on training and create",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,1,"pre-post survey design to measure changes in students’ cognitive, affective, and behavioral attitudes toward AI. The results indicate a significant positive shift in students’ perspectives. This study provides empirical evidence that it is important to expand hands-on training and create an environment where students can experience and use AI when designing AI curricula for university curricula. Keywords Generative AI, AI literacy, Student attitudes, Higher education",65
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,2,"Page 2 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 The urgency of this research is underscored by the rapid adoption of AI tools in aca­ demic settings, with over 77% of university students aged 13–18 reporting use of gen­ erative AI in 2024, up from 37% in 2023 [15]. However, students’ attitudes toward these tools remain complex and multifaceted, encompassing cognitive understanding, affective responses, and behavioral intentions that collectively determine whether AI enhances or undermines educational outcomes [6]. Attitudes toward AI significantly impact students’ motivation to learn, acceptance of technology, and ability to utilize AI, so empirical analysis is needed. This study aims to analyze the impact of a hands-on software course using generative AI on changing university students’ attitudes toward AI. To this end, this study designed a semester-long course on productivity software utilizing ChatGPT and other genera­ tive AI tools and measured the change in students’ perceptions through pre- and post- surveys. In particular, attitude change was analyzed in terms of cognitive, affective, and behavioral aspects to gain a more sophisticated understanding [12]. This study was conducted within",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,2,"AI tools and measured the change in students’ perceptions through pre- and post- surveys. In particular, attitude change was analyzed in terms of cognitive, affective, and behavioral aspects to gain a more sophisticated understanding [12]. This study was conducted within the Korean higher education context, where stu­ dents typically enter university at age 19–20 after completing 12 years of primary and secondary education. Korean universities follow a four-year undergraduate system simi­ lar to North American institutions, with students declaring majors upon admission. The course in this study was offered as an elective within the general education curriculum, accessible to students across all academic years and disciplines, reflecting the Korean emphasis on interdisciplinary learning in foundational courses. This study is unique because it is an experimental study that focuses on changing stu­ dents’ attitudes toward AI instead of previous AI education discussions focused on tech­ nical skills. Furthermore, while previous studies emphasize conceptual understanding of AI literacy, this study is different in that it measures whether students’ attitudes change substantially as they practice generative AI. As a result, the following contributions",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,2,"discussions focused on tech­ nical skills. Furthermore, while previous studies emphasize conceptual understanding of AI literacy, this study is different in that it measures whether students’ attitudes change substantially as they practice generative AI. As a result, the following contributions can be expected from this study. • Instructional Design Implications By analyzing the impact of AI-powered instruction on students’ attitudes, this study provides empirical evidence for effective AI instructional design. • Suggest policy directions for AI literacy education Suggest directions for university education to build positive attitudes toward AI. • Examine real-world applicability Support curriculum improvements that align with the generative AI literacy skills required by organizations and educational institutions. This research will help higher education adapt to the AI era and contribute to the cre­ ation of educational environments where students can more actively utilize AI. 2 Theoretical background There has been a growing interest in AI literacy in recent years, especially with the rise of generative AI, increasing the opportunities for hands-on experience with AI in higher education. As AI technology advances rapidly, students are being asked to",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,2,"There has been a growing interest in AI literacy in recent years, especially with the rise of generative AI, increasing the opportunities for hands-on experience with AI in higher education. As AI technology advances rapidly, students are being asked to consume AI and use and critically evaluate it [19] defines AI literacy as an extension of digital literacy, breaking it down into four core components: AI comprehension, practical application,",69
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,3,"Page 3 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 assessment, and ethics. In this shift, AI literacy is expanding beyond the acquisition of technology, including the ability to collaborate with and creatively utilize AI. However, despite the proliferation of AI education, there is a lack of empirical research on how students’ attitudes toward AI change. While existing studies focus on the need for AI-enabled education or policy directions, there is a relative lack of systematic research on how students’ attitudes change after experiencing AI in real-world educa­ tional settings. This study aims to take a step forward in the existing AI literacy debate by empirically analyzing the impact of generative AI practices on students’ attitudinal changes toward AI. 2.1 AI literacy and the transformation of college liberal arts education In higher education, Information and Communication Technology(ICT) literacy has tra­ ditionally focused on developing proficiency in office software such as word processing, presentations, and spreadsheets. However, with advances in AI technology and the pro­ liferation of automation, it is increasingly recognized that mere tool proficiency is not enough [10]. In particular,",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,3,"focused on developing proficiency in office software such as word processing, presentations, and spreadsheets. However, with advances in AI technology and the pro­ liferation of automation, it is increasingly recognized that mere tool proficiency is not enough [10]. In particular, as we enter an era where AI is being used for content creation and decision-making, understanding AI, assessing its reliability, and considering ethical issues is essential. Ng et al. [16] analyzed 30 AI literacy studies published between 2016 and 2021 and reported that interest in AI education is growing rapidly, with various attempts at cur­ riculum design and pedagogy. However, researchers have noted that there is still a lack of development in AI liberal arts courses accessible to non-majors. Yeter et al. [21] also emphasized that most AI education is designed for majors and that there is a lack of AI literacy education for general students and suggested that policy efforts are needed to address this. Recent studies on AI literacy in higher education have emphasized the importance of developing students’ critical evaluation skills alongside technical understanding. Research indicates that",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,3,"AI literacy education for general students and suggested that policy efforts are needed to address this. Recent studies on AI literacy in higher education have emphasized the importance of developing students’ critical evaluation skills alongside technical understanding. Research indicates that students who develop comprehensive AI literacy demonstrate better discernment in AI tool use and show more balanced attitudes toward AI capabili­ ties and limitations [20]. Cross-cultural studies reveal significant disparities in AI liter­ acy among university students, particularly favoring those with technical backgrounds, though well-designed interventions can reduce these gaps [14]. In this context, the productivity software utilization liberal arts course in this study attempts to innovate the curriculum by incorporating generative AI into traditional basic IT knowledge education. The goal of AI literacy education is not just to learn how to use AI tools but to understand how AI technologies work in social, ethical, and aca­ demic contexts and to critically embrace them. This study empirically analyzes how this pedagogical approach affects students’ attitudes toward AI. 2.2 Educational uses of generative AI and student attitudes Generative AI is a",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,3,"technologies work in social, ethical, and aca­ demic contexts and to critically embrace them. This study empirically analyzes how this pedagogical approach affects students’ attitudes toward AI. 2.2 Educational uses of generative AI and student attitudes Generative AI is a technology that generates new content by learning from vast amounts of data, and it has excellent potential for use in education [9]. Conversational AI, such as ChatGPT, has the potential to support students’ self-directed learning and serve as a learning partner. Existing studies have reported that generative AI can provide stu­ dents with immediate feedback and personalized explanations to improve learning comprehension.",102
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,4,"Page 4 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 Research on student attitudes toward generative AI in educational settings reveals complex relationships between exposure, understanding, and acceptance. Studies have found that direct experience with AI tools often leads to more nuanced attitudes, with students developing better understanding of both capabilities and limitations through hands-on interaction [4]. However, survey research indicates that students maintain concerns about accuracy, privacy, and over-reliance on AI tools, with 80% of students reporting that AI in universities is not fully meeting expectations [6]. The ethical issues of generative AI also remain a significant educational challenge. Ma, [13] for example, notes that AIs such as ChatGPT may increase the likelihood that stu­ dents will be encouraged to plagiarize or ghostwrite assignments and argues that univer­ sities should respond by innovating their assessment practices. For example, there are discussions about including creative questions in assessments that AIs cannot quickly write or allowing AIs to be used but requiring clarity of process and sources. Rather than focusing solely on risks, recent educational approaches emphasize devel­ oping critical AI literacy—the",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,4,"discussions about including creative questions in assessments that AIs cannot quickly write or allowing AIs to be used but requiring clarity of process and sources. Rather than focusing solely on risks, recent educational approaches emphasize devel­ oping critical AI literacy—the ability to evaluate outputs, identify biases, and make informed decisions about appropriate use. This positive framing positions students as empowered consumers rather than passive users, treating bias detection and accuracy assessment as valuable competencies rather than cautionary warnings. This study aims to empirically analyze whether generative AI labs can play a role in shaping students’ positive attitudes toward AI while helping them evaluate reliability issues and make ethical considerations. 2.3 Analytical framework for student attitude change This study shares Breckler’s [3] theoretical background of the three components of atti­ tude to analyze students’ AI attitudes in terms of cognitive, affective, and behavioral aspects. • Cognitive attitudes How much do you understand how AI works and what it can do? • Affective attitudes Feelings of excitement, fear, etc. when experiencing AI. • Behavioral attitudes The extent to which AI is practically",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,4,"and behavioral aspects. • Cognitive attitudes How much do you understand how AI works and what it can do? • Affective attitudes Feelings of excitement, fear, etc. when experiencing AI. • Behavioral attitudes The extent to which AI is practically utilized. The three-component attitude structure provides a robust framework for understanding attitude change in educational contexts. Educational interventions targeting cognitive understanding often produce downstream effects on emotional responses and behav­ ioral intentions, with research suggesting strong pathways from knowledge to affect and from affect to behavior [4, 20]. In this study, I empirically analyze how generative AI education changes students’ atti­ tudes toward AI and suggest future AI literacy education directions. 3 Research methods 3.1 Research design and context This study employed a single-group pretest-posttest design to examine attitude changes following a 15-week course. While acknowledging the limitations of this design, includ­ ing potential testing effects and lack of control group, the intensive nature of the intervention and educational context made random assignment infeasible. The study addressed these limitations through careful statistical analysis and conservative inter­ pretation of results. To",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,4,"design, includ­ ing potential testing effects and lack of control group, the intensive nature of the intervention and educational context made random assignment infeasible. The study addressed these limitations through careful statistical analysis and conservative inter­ pretation of results. To minimize testing effects, we employed several strategies: (1) a",49
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,5,"Page 5 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 15-week interval between pre- and post-tests to reduce memory effects, (2) embedding survey items within broader course evaluations to reduce response bias, and (3) using effect size calculations and bootstrapping methods to strengthen statistical inference despite the absence of a control group. Additionally, we conducted subgroup analyses by gender and major to examine consistency of effects across different student populations. This study focused on students who took the course “SW Practical Business”, a liberal arts course at A University in Korea. The course was a liberal arts course that dealtwith the convergence of productivity software and business for undergraduate students and could be taken by anyone, regardless of major. The students studied were 76 first-year undergraduate students who agreed to respond to the survey, and their characteristics are presented in Table 1. The course was taught once a week for 3 h for 15 weeks and included creating presen­ tations and utilizing data management tools. In this study, I designed and implemented a project that utilized generative AI tools in addition",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,5,"1. The course was taught once a week for 3 h for 15 weeks and included creating presen­ tations and utilizing data management tools. In this study, I designed and implemented a project that utilized generative AI tools in addition to the practical exercises. 3.2 Course structure and AI integration The 15-week course integrated generative AI tools progressively. Weeks 1–3: Foundation Phase- Introduction to productivity software basics- Over­ view of AI concepts and capabilities- Initial exposure to ChatGPT for simple tasks. Weeks 4–8: Application Phase - Excel automation using ChatGPT for formula gen­ eration- Presentation design with AI-assisted content creation- Data visualization with AI recommendations- Students completed weekly assignments using both traditional methods and AI tools for comparison. Weeks 9–12: Integration Phase- Complex project work combining multiple AI tools- Critical evaluation of AI-generated outputs- Ethical considerations and source verifica­ tion practices- Group projects requiring AI collaboration. Weeks 13–15: Innovation Phase- Independent projects leveraging AI for creative solutions- Peer review of AI-enhanced work- Reflection on AI’s role in productivity enhancement. 3.3 Instrument validation This study utilized the Scale Measuring Student Attitudes",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,5,"practices- Group projects requiring AI collaboration. Weeks 13–15: Innovation Phase- Independent projects leveraging AI for creative solutions- Peer review of AI-enhanced work- Reflection on AI’s role in productivity enhancement. 3.3 Instrument validation This study utilized the Scale Measuring Student Attitudes Toward Artificial Intelligence (SATAI) developed by [18]. The scale’s construction is well suited to comprehensively capture changes in students’ perceptions of AI (cognitive components), changes in feelings and interest (affective components), and changes in willingness to use (behav­ ioral components) before and after an educational intervention. In addition, studies on attitudes toward AI in various fields, such as education and nursing, have cited the SATAI, making it one of the standard tools for measuring AI attitudes [1, 2, 11]. These Table 1 Demographics of the study population Attributes Properties Numbers Gender Male 30 Female 46 Major Humanities 21 Engineering 38 Arts 12 Liberal Studies 5",145
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,6,"Page 6 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 connections to prior research support the appropriateness and universality of using the SATAI scale in this study. A pilot test was conducted with 30 students who did not participate in the main study to ensure instrument clarity and appropriateness for the Korean university context. The main study data (n = 76) demonstrated acceptable to excellent internal consistency, con­ firming the scale’s reliability in our research context (see Results section for detailed reli­ ability coefficients). The SATAI scale demonstrated strong psychometric properties in its original development study [18], with excellent internal consistency across all three components (Cronbach’s α ranging from 0.905 to 0.956) and confirmed construct valid­ ity through confirmatory factor analysis (Average Variance Extracted [AVE] > 0.5, Com­ posite Reliability [CR] > 0.9, Comparative Fit Index [CFI] = 0.937, Tucker-Lewis Index [TLI] = 0.928). In the current study with 76 participants, reliability analysis revealed that the SATAI scale maintained acceptable to excellent internal consistency across both measurement points. For the pre-test, Cronbach’s alpha coefficients were: cognitive component (α = 0.76), affective",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,6,"[TLI] = 0.928). In the current study with 76 participants, reliability analysis revealed that the SATAI scale maintained acceptable to excellent internal consistency across both measurement points. For the pre-test, Cronbach’s alpha coefficients were: cognitive component (α = 0.76), affective component (α = 0.82), behavioral component (α = 0.87), and overall scale (α = 0.91). For the post-test, the coefficients were: cognitive component (α = 0.79), affective component (α = 0.85), behavioral component (α = 0.89), and over­ all scale (α = 0.93). These values indicate that the scale demonstrated strong reliability throughout the study, with slight improvements in internal consistency from pre- to post-test, suggesting that participants’ responses became more coherent as they devel­ oped deeper understanding of AI through the course. 3.4 Descriptive statistics and normality tests Descriptive statistics were calculated for 26 survey questions (Q1 through Q26; full item descriptions are provided in Appendix A) and found that overall, participants’ attitudes toward AI were above average. The mean of the responses for each question ranged from 2.513 to 4.179 in the pre-survey and from 2.718 to 4.256",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,6,"full item descriptions are provided in Appendix A) and found that overall, participants’ attitudes toward AI were above average. The mean of the responses for each question ranged from 2.513 to 4.179 in the pre-survey and from 2.718 to 4.256 in the post-survey. The skew­ ness and kurtosis values were within ± 1.0 for all questions, indicating no significant skewness or kurtosis. A Shapiro-Wilk normality test was performed to assess the nor­ mality of survey responses, and normality violations were identified for some questions (e.g., question Q 1, p =.003). In particular, The analysis observed a few questions with gently skewed distributions due to the nature of the Likert scale. Based on these results, non-parametric tests were utilized in case the normality assumption was not met for a conservative interpretation. Given the sample size (n = 76), this study had adequate power to detect medium to large effects at α = 0.05. The analysis strategy included comprehensive descriptive sta­ tistics, assumption testing through normality assessments, paired-samples comparisons using appropriate statistical tests, effect size calculations, and subgroup analyses for gen­ der",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,6,"adequate power to detect medium to large effects at α = 0.05. The analysis strategy included comprehensive descriptive sta­ tistics, assumption testing through normality assessments, paired-samples comparisons using appropriate statistical tests, effect size calculations, and subgroup analyses for gen­ der and major comparisons. Table 2 presents the descriptive statistics for all 26 survey items, showing the mean scores, standard deviations, and distribution characteristics for both pre- and post-intervention measurements. 4 Research results Prior to hypothesis testing, we examined the reliability of the SATAI scale in our sam­ ple. Table 3 presents the Cronbach’s alpha coefficients for each subscale and the overall instrument at both measurement points.",106
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,7,"Page 7 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 All subscales exceeded the recommended threshold of.70 for acceptable reliability [17], with the overall scale demonstrating excellent reliability at both time points. The slight increase in reliability coefficients from pre- to post-test suggests improved response consistency following the intervention.” 4.1 Three-component attitude structure analysis Analysis of the three attitude components revealed meaningful changes across cognitive, affective, and behavioral dimensions. The cognitive component demonstrated substan­ tial improvement, reflecting enhanced understanding and appreciation of AI’s educa­ tional value. The affective component showed moderate improvements in emotional responses and interest levels. The behavioral component exhibited significant gains in usage intentions and confidence in applying AI tools. These findings align with [4] who found that hands-on experience with AI tools leads to more nuanced attitudes. However, our effect sizes (d = 0.4–0.6) are larger than those reported by [20] who found smaller effects (d = 0.2–0.3) in shorter interventions. This suggests that the extended 15-week Table 2 Descriptive statistics for Pre- and Post-Survey responses Mean_Pre SD_Pre Skew_Pre Kurt_Pre Mean_Post SD_Post Skew_Post Kurt_Post Q_1 3.282 0.999",180
