DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,8,"Page 8 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 engagement period may be crucial for meaningful attitude change.The persistence of some cautious attitudes, particularly regarding ethical concerns, supports Digital Edu­ cation Council’s (2024) finding that 80% of students maintain reservations about AI accuracy and privacy. Rather than viewing this as a limitation, this balanced perspective development aligns with Mansoor et al.‘s [14] argument that comprehensive AI literacy includes critical evaluation skills alongside technical competence. 4.2 Analyze pre-post differences (Wilcoxon signed-rank test) A paired sample Wilcoxon signed-rank test was conducted to analyze the difference between the pre-test and post-test of AI attitudes. The results showed that only some of the 26 questions showed significant differences. In the cognitive component, items Q_1, Q_2, Q_3, and Q_4 showed statistically signif­ icant improvement (p <.05), indicating that students developed positive attitudes toward the importance of AI and the need for education. This can be interpreted as a result of the students experiencing the need for AI while learning the concept and social impact of AI directly through the class. The cognitive items demonstrated the",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,8,"of AI and the need for education. This can be interpreted as a result of the students experiencing the need for AI while learning the concept and social impact of AI directly through the class. The cognitive items demonstrated the most consistent improvements, with all four items showing significant positive changes, suggesting com­ prehensive enhancement in AI understanding and appreciation for its educational value. However, some questions, such as Q_5 and Q_6, did not show statistically signifi­ cant changes (p >.05). It is possible that the students were already aware of the positive impact of AI on society to some extent, and the training did not significantly change their perceptions. In the affective component, items Q_10, Q_12, and Q_14 increased significantly from pre to post (p <.01). This suggests that students became more familiar with AI and had more positive feelings about it. In particular, the experience of using AI tools in class reduced their resistance to AI and strengthened their positive perception of its practi­ cal value. Several affective items showed significant improvements, particularly in areas related to AI",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,8,"feelings about it. In particular, the experience of using AI tools in class reduced their resistance to AI and strengthened their positive perception of its practi­ cal value. Several affective items showed significant improvements, particularly in areas related to AI familiarity, personal relevance, and recognition of potential benefits. However, the changes were not significant for some items, such as Q_8 and Q_9. Rather than viewing this as a limitation, the persistence of some cautious attitudes reflects the development of balanced perspective—students who maintain thoughtful consideration of AI’s implications while appreciating its potential demonstrate sophisti­ cated understanding rather than uncritical enthusiasm. This is likely due to lingering concerns about ethical issues and possible misuse of AI, suggesting that short-term training may not be enough to change these perceptions significantly. In the behavioral component, there was a significant increase in Q_18, Q_20, and Q_22 (p <.05), indicating that students gained confidence in using AI. In particular, it is analyzed that the students have improved their ability to utilize AI in practice by con­ ducting AI-based projects themselves. Multiple behavioral items showed significant",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,8,"and Q_22 (p <.05), indicating that students gained confidence in using AI. In particular, it is analyzed that the students have improved their ability to utilize AI in practice by con­ ducting AI-based projects themselves. Multiple behavioral items showed significant improvements, with notable gains in areas related to usage intentions, confidence in applying AI tools, and willingness to explore innovative applications. However, some items, such as Q_24 and Q_26, were not statistically significant. This may be because students have learned how to utilize AI, but they are not yet confident in their ability to apply AI independently, or they have experienced the limitations of AI and remain cautious.",108
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,9,"Page 9 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 While there were significant changes in some of these components, we did not see consistent gains across all questions. This could mean that AI training was effec­ tive in certain areas (e.g., utilizing AI concepts and fundamentals) but had less impact on topics such as the social impact of AI or ethical considerations. This suggests that future research should consider these differences and design more in-depth training approaches. Table 4 summarizes the Wilcoxon signed-rank test results for all 26 items, including test statistics, p-values, mean differences with confidence intervals, and effect sizes. 4.3 Bootstrapping to calculate confidence intervals and effect sizes To increase the confidence in the analysis results, bootstrapping techniques were uti­ lized to estimate 95% confidence intervals for the pre-post differences(Fig. 1). After bootstrapping 1,000 times for the overall attitude score difference that showed a signifi­ cant improvement according to the Wilcoxon test, the 95% confidence interval for the mean difference was estimated to be approximately [0.282, 0.974]. This confidence inter­ val does not include zero, supporting that the",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,9,"attitude score difference that showed a signifi­ cant improvement according to the Wilcoxon test, the 95% confidence interval for the mean difference was estimated to be approximately [0.282, 0.974]. This confidence inter­ val does not include zero, supporting that the pre-post score difference is likely not due to chance. Bootstrapping was also performed on the score differences for each sub-fac­ tor, and all confidence intervals contained no zeros, confirming that the post-program attitude improvement was statistically significant and had a substantial range of change. Cohen’s d and the r value of the paired-sample Wilcoxon test were also calculated to assess the effect size of the change. Cohen’s d value for the overall AI attitude score was approximately 0.589, which generally corresponds to a moderate to large effect size Table 4 Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test results Wilcoxon Z Wilcoxon p-value Mean Difference 95% CI Lower 95% CI Upper Cohen’s d Q_1 10 0.001 0.641 0.282 0.974 0.589 Q_2 19.5 0.003 0.462 0.205 0.744 0.541 Q_3 19.5 0.001 0.590 0.308 0.897 0.611 Q_4 27 0.016 0.487 0.128 0.872 0.410 Q_5 48 0.089 0.256",180
