DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,10,"Page 10 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 [5]. The r value from the Wilcoxon signed-rank test statistic was also 0.541, which is interpreted as a moderate effect. This means that the impact of the training program on learner attitudes is of a size that is above what is expected in pedagogical research [7]. These effect sizes are substantial for educational interventions, indicating that the program produced meaningfully large improvements in student attitudes beyond what might be expected from typical classroom experiences. 4.4 Analyzing differences by gender and major Finally, the study further analyzed whether the magnitude of change in AI attitudes dif­ fered by gender and major. For this purpose, a Wilcoxon-Mann Whitney U test was per­ formed to compare the mean rank of the pre-post score difference (Δ) between male and female students, and the same procedure was performed by major (e.g., science and engineering vs. humanities and social sciences). The analysis showed that the difference in attitude change by gender was not statistically significant. Both male and female stu­ dents improved their attitudes towards AI after",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,10,"was performed by major (e.g., science and engineering vs. humanities and social sciences). The analysis showed that the difference in attitude change by gender was not statistically significant. Both male and female stu­ dents improved their attitudes towards AI after the program, with no significant dif­ ference in the degree of improvement. When analyzing by field of study, no significant differences were found in attitude improvement between major groups (p >.05). For example, both engineering/science majors and non-majors showed similarly sized atti­ tude score gains, and no statistically differential changes were found when comparing groups. These results suggest that the effects of the AI training program are universal and not specific to any particular group. In other words, positive attitude changes were observed across all subgroups, regardless of gender or major, confirming the program’s equitable impact. 5 Discussion This study analyzed the impact of a generative AI class for university students on their attitudes toward AI. The findings contribute to growing theoretical understanding of how structured educational interventions can facilitate positive attitude change toward emerging technologies. Fig. 1 Effect Size",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,10,analyzed the impact of a generative AI class for university students on their attitudes toward AI. The findings contribute to growing theoretical understanding of how structured educational interventions can facilitate positive attitude change toward emerging technologies. Fig. 1 Effect Size Analysis,41
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,11,"Page 11 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 5.1 The attitude change effect of AI training Descriptive statistics and Wilcoxon signed-rank tests showed that students’ attitudes towards AI changed significantly and positively from pre- to post-intervention. This con­ firms previous research that direct experience with AI has a positive impact on attitude change. As their understanding of AI increased, students became more familiar with it and recognized its applicability in real life. The Table 5 below is an example of how stu­ dents’ attitudes toward AI changed. Bootstrapping confidence interval analysis also showed that the pre-post differences were within the statistical significance range, and Cohen’s d values mainly were between 0.4 and 0.6, indicating moderate to large effect sizes. This suggests that the generative AI-based lessons provided in this study had a substantial impact on changing students’ attitudes. 5.2 Pedagogical innovation and attitude development The progressive curriculum structure—moving from foundational concepts through hands-on experience to critical evaluation and creative application—appears to have facilitated comprehensive attitude development. This approach aligns with educational theories suggesting that meaningful learning occurs when students",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,11,"Pedagogical innovation and attitude development The progressive curriculum structure—moving from foundational concepts through hands-on experience to critical evaluation and creative application—appears to have facilitated comprehensive attitude development. This approach aligns with educational theories suggesting that meaningful learning occurs when students engage with new technologies through multiple modes of interaction [8]. The finding that students maintained some cautious perspectives while developing overall positive attitudes reflects sophisticated learning outcomes. Rather than produc­ ing uncritical enthusiasm, the intervention appears to have fostered balanced under­ standing that appreciates AI’s potential while recognizing its limitations. 5.3 Analyzing differences by gender and major Further analysis of attitude changes by gender and major revealed no statistically sig­ nificant differences. This finding is particularly noteworthy given previous research sug­ gesting differential receptivity to technology based on gender and academic background. The results demonstrate that thoughtfully designed AI literacy education can achieve equitable learning outcomes across diverse student populations. This suggests that AI training had an equalizing effect on both genders. While previous studies have reported that men tend to have more positive attitudes toward AI technology, this study",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,11,"education can achieve equitable learning outcomes across diverse student populations. This suggests that AI training had an equalizing effect on both genders. While previous studies have reported that men tend to have more positive attitudes toward AI technology, this study suggests that AI training can offset this gap. No significant differences were also found when ana­ lyzing by major. This shows that AI literacy can be applied universally to all learners, not just those in specific majors, which is important because AI education can provide equal learning benefits to students of different majors. Table 5 Examples of attitude change Components of attitude Classroom Activities Examples of attitude change Cognitive Brainstorming with ChatGPT Recognizes that AI is useful for generat­ ing creative ideas Affective Validating and ethically considering information provided by AI Recognize AI trust issues and the importance of source verification when using AI Behavioral AI-powered automated tasks (Excel, VBA) Recognize that AI can improve workplace productivity and take advantage of it",162
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,12,"Page 12 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 5.4 Study limitations and future research directions As a single-group pretest-posttest design, this study cannot definitively establish causal relationships between the intervention and observed changes. The absence of a control group limits our ability to rule out alternative explanations such as maturation effects or external influences during the 15-week study period. Future research should employ ran­ domized controlled designs to strengthen causal inference. The reliance on self-reported data presents additional limitations, as attitude measures may not directly translate to actual AI usage behaviors. Future research should incorporate behavioral observation methods, usage analytics, and performance assessments to validate whether positive attitude changes correspond to improved AI utilization competencies. This study has several limitations. First, this study focused on classes at a specific uni­ versity, which limits the generalizability of the results. Future research should examine the effectiveness of AI education in different universities and learning environments. Second, this study measured short-term changes in attitudes and did not determine whether attitudes were maintained over the long term. Future research should examine the lasting",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,12,"research should examine the effectiveness of AI education in different universities and learning environments. Second, this study measured short-term changes in attitudes and did not determine whether attitudes were maintained over the long term. Future research should examine the lasting effects of AI education through long-term follow-up. Third, this study relied on questionnaire-based self-reported data, which is limited by the difficulty of verify­ ing the association with actual behavior change. Future studies will need to use more sophisticated experimental designs and qualitative research to conduct more in-depth analyses. Additionally, the relatively homogeneous sample from a single Korean university may limit generalizability to other cultural and educational contexts. Cross-cultural studies would help establish the broader applicability of these findings to different educational systems and student populations. 6 Conclusion This study analyzed the impact of teaching with generative AI on students’ attitudes toward AI and drew educational implications. The research demonstrates that struc­ tured AI literacy education can produce meaningful shifts in student attitudes across cognitive, affective, and behavioral dimensions, supporting the importance of compre­ hensive educational approaches to AI integration. First,",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,12,"toward AI and drew educational implications. The research demonstrates that struc­ tured AI literacy education can produce meaningful shifts in student attitudes across cognitive, affective, and behavioral dimensions, supporting the importance of compre­ hensive educational approaches to AI integration. First, students’ attitudes toward AI changed significantly and positively after partici­ pating in the AI training program. In particular, the largest changes were observed in the cognitive and behavioral components, indicating that AI education is effective in raising students’ willingness to use AI. Second, the differences in attitude change by gender and major were not statistically significant. This suggests that AI training can have an equal impact on all learners rather than being limited to a specific group. Key pedagogical insights from this study include the importance of progressive curriculum design that moves from basic understanding to practical application, the value of hands-on experience with AI tools in building con­ fidence and competence, and the effectiveness of balanced approaches that address both opportunities and challenges associated with AI technology. Third, our findings are consistent with existing research emphasizing the importance of",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,12,"of hands-on experience with AI tools in building con­ fidence and competence, and the effectiveness of balanced approaches that address both opportunities and challenges associated with AI technology. Third, our findings are consistent with existing research emphasizing the importance of AI literacy education and support the need for AI education to be included in broader curricula.",56
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,13,"Page 13 of 15 Suh Discover Education (2025) 4:508 Fourth, the study found that AI education can help increase students’ interest in AI and build their confidence in using the technology. Students are beginning to see AI as a resource for creative problem-solving rather than just a tool, which increases the likeli­ hood that they will actively use AI in real-world learning and work in the future. The results of this study show that AI education in SW practical classes can contribute to a positive change in students’ attitudes toward AI beyond the acquisition of technical knowledge. Therefore, when designing future university liberal arts curricula, it is impor­ tant to provide an environment where students can experience and utilize AI in practice. In addition, it is necessary to evaluate the lasting effects of AI education through studies that track long-term attitude changes and develop more systematic education models. These findings contribute to the growing body of evidence supporting the integration of AI literacy education in higher education curricula. As AI technologies continue to evolve and become more prevalent in academic",180
DOC_065,s44217-025-00914-8.pdf,10.1007/s44217-025-00914-8,13,"attitude changes and develop more systematic education models. These findings contribute to the growing body of evidence supporting the integration of AI literacy education in higher education curricula. As AI technologies continue to evolve and become more prevalent in academic and professional contexts, developing students’ capacity to understand, evaluate, and effectively utilize these tools becomes increasingly important for preparing graduates for future challenges and opportunities. Further research should include long-term attitude change studies to analyze whether the effects of AI education are sustained over time and behavioral data-driven studies to measure how students actually use AI quantitatively. This will allow for more sophis­ ticated pedagogical approaches to ensure that AI education contributes to real-world competencies and a greater sense of technological and ethical responsibility. This study empirically demonstrates that AI education can influence learners’ attitudes and mindsets beyond mere skill acquisition. Therefore, when designing AI curricula, it is important to expand hands-on training and create an environment where students can actually experience and utilize AI. Appendix A See Table 6. Table 6 Student attitude toward artificial intelligence (SATAI) [18] Domain",180
