DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,1,"visit http://crea​tivecommons​.org/licens​ es/by-nc​-nd/4.0/. Abstract This study examines the association between generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tool use and the academic performance of social‑science undergraduates in Hong Kong, drawing on Bourdieu’s concept of technological capital. It conceptualizes GenAI as a potential form of educational capital whose value depends on how effectively students mobilize it across tasks of differing cognitive complexity. Using cross‑sectional survey data from 107 students at The Chinese University of Hong Kong, the study analyzes how the frequency of GenAI use for lower‑, middle‑, and higher‑order academic tasks relates to students’ cumulative GPA. Multiple regression models controlling for demographic, attitudinal, and familial factors indicate that GenAI use for lower‑order tasks (e.g., proofreading, summarizing, organizing information) is positively associated with GPA, while usage for middle‑ and higher‑order tasks shows no significant association. These findings suggest that GenAI functions as a form of technological capital that supports efficiency in routine learning but is less strongly linked with performance in tasks requiring analytical and conceptual reasoning. The study advances sociological understanding of technological capital in higher education, emphasizing that the academic value of",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,1,"of technological capital that supports efficiency in routine learning but is less strongly linked with performance in tasks requiring analytical and conceptual reasoning. The study advances sociological understanding of technological capital in higher education, emphasizing that the academic value of GenAI depends on its reflective and field‑specific integration within the norms of social‑science learning. Keywords Generative artificial intelligence, Academic performance, Cognitive complexity, Technological capital, Social science 1 Introduction The rapid advancement of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) technologies is reshaping higher education worldwide, influencing how students learn, think, and produce academic work. Tools such as ChatGPT, Google Gemini, and DeepSeek are increasingly integrated into university learning environments, offering support for writ­ ing, research, and data interpretation ([37]; Wang et al. [40]). Hong Kong—an inter­ national education hub within a knowledge-based economy—illustrates this trend. Its",133
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,2,"Page 2 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 universities are incorporating GenAI into teaching and assessment while continuing to uphold long-standing commitments to academic integrity and excellence [16]. In this context, examining how students engage with GenAI provides valuable insight into how digital technologies are associated with evolving notions of effort, legitimacy, and achievement in contemporary higher education. Within the social sciences, the educational implications of GenAI use are particularly complex. These disciplines involve a wide range of cognitive engagement—from descrip­ tive understanding and empirical analysis to critical synthesis and theoretical reflec­ tion [9, 42]. Unlike technical or professional fields that emphasize procedural accuracy or standardized problem-solving, social-science education relies heavily on interpre­ tive reasoning, argumentation, and reflexivity. Consequently, the relationships between GenAI use and learning are likely to differ depending on how the tools are employed— whether to streamline information processing, organize arguments, or support concep­ tual exploration [43]. Building on this perspective, the present study examines patterns of GenAI use among social-science students and how these patterns are associated with their academic per­ formance. The",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,2,"whether to streamline information processing, organize arguments, or support concep­ tual exploration [43]. Building on this perspective, the present study examines patterns of GenAI use among social-science students and how these patterns are associated with their academic per­ formance. The educational relevance of GenAI use may differ according to the cogni­ tive complexity of the tasks involved. Social-science coursework spans a continuum of complexity—from relatively routine descriptive or empirical work to advanced critical and theoretical reasoning (Bandi et al., [6]; Lu et al. [27]). It is therefore plausible that GenAI use aligns more closely with lower-order tasks such as informatndi et al. 2023ion retrieval, summarization, or basic content organization, while being less strongly related to higher-order activities that require independent analysis and conceptual synthesis (Bandi et al. [6]; Lu et al. [27]). Accordingly, this study conceptualizes GenAI use as dif­ ferentiated by cognitive demand within the social-science curriculum and investigates how engagement across these task levels is associated with students’ cumulative grade point averages (GPA). Specifically, it first provides a descriptive overview of GenAI usage patterns among undergraduates and then",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,2,"ferentiated by cognitive demand within the social-science curriculum and investigates how engagement across these task levels is associated with students’ cumulative grade point averages (GPA). Specifically, it first provides a descriptive overview of GenAI usage patterns among undergraduates and then examines how different forms of engagement correspond to variations in academic outcomes. This study contributes to the growing literature on generative AI in higher education in three main ways. First, on the empirical level, it provides an early quantitative account of how patterns of generative AI use—across lower-, middle-, and higher-order aca­ demic tasks—are associated with students’ academic performance in the social sciences. By focusing on a non-STEM context, the study adds new evidence to an area that has so far received limited empirical attention. Second, from a theoretical standpoint, it builds on Selwyn’s [33] concept of technological capital to consider how the educational rele­ vance of generative AI may vary with disciplinary norms and forms of valued knowledge. The findings suggest that AI can be understood as a conditional form of technological capital—associated with advantages in procedural, lower-order tasks",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,2,"consider how the educational rele­ vance of generative AI may vary with disciplinary norms and forms of valued knowledge. The findings suggest that AI can be understood as a conditional form of technological capital—associated with advantages in procedural, lower-order tasks but less strongly linked to outcomes in higher-order, interpretive work that draws on students’ own ana­ lytical capacities. Third, methodologically, the study applies a cognitively differentiated framework for measuring AI use and employs both fixed-effects and OLS estimation to enhance transparency and account for disciplinary variation. Taken together, these contributions clarify the conditions under which generative AI use is associated with differences in academic performance, offering a nuanced and sociologically informed understanding of its place in non-technical higher-education settings.",119
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,3,"Page 3 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 The remainder of this paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 reviews theoretical per­ spectives on technological capital and its educational implications, framing GenAI use within Bourdieu’s sociology of practice. Section 3 details the methodological approach, variable construction, and model estimation procedures. Section 4 presents descriptive results and regression analyses on the association between GenAI use and cumulative GPA. Section 5 discusses the reasons behind the results, implications, as well as limita­ tions and future research directions. 2 Literature review and hypothesis development 2.1 GenAI and technological capital Pierre Bourdieu’s [12] theory of capital offers a compelling framework for understand­ ing how technological engagement becomes both a product and producer of social inequality. Within educational fields, capital—whether economic, cultural, or social— determines individuals’ positions and capacities to accumulate further advantage. Stu­ dents equipped with greater cultural capital (e.g., academic literacy), economic capital (e.g., access to digital devices), and social capital (e.g., supportive networks) are better positioned to master the technological competencies increasingly required for educa­ tional success. Selwyn’s [33] extension",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,3,"Stu­ dents equipped with greater cultural capital (e.g., academic literacy), economic capital (e.g., access to digital devices), and social capital (e.g., supportive networks) are better positioned to master the technological competencies increasingly required for educa­ tional success. Selwyn’s [33] extension of Bourdieu’s framework introduces technological capital as the accumulated technological knowledge, skills, and dispositions that enable learners to mobilize technology effectively for learning and advancement. Like cultural capital, it is unequally distributed, shaped by individuals’ habitus—their ingrained dis­ positions toward technology—and by the institutional and structural forces that valo­ rize certain digital competencies over others. Beckman et al. [7] refine this concept by arguing that technological capital is not a discrete new category but a transmutation of existing capitals within a digital field. Economic resources translate into hardware and connectivity; cultural capital manifests as digital literacy and critical awareness; and social capital emerges through networks that circulate technological know‑how. Thus, technological capital acts as a conversion mechanism—a means through which pre‑existing capitals are reinvested and amplified in digital contexts. This reconceptual­ ization positions technologies such as Generative AI (GenAI) as",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,3,"social capital emerges through networks that circulate technological know‑how. Thus, technological capital acts as a conversion mechanism—a means through which pre‑existing capitals are reinvested and amplified in digital contexts. This reconceptual­ ization positions technologies such as Generative AI (GenAI) as arenas where the strug­ gle for distinction and advantage is reproduced, rather than neutralized. Emerging research substantiates this view. Tang [39] and Bekirsky [8] both concep­ tualize GenAI proficiency as a capital‑like resource embedded within the broader logic of social reproduction. Tang [39] demonstrates that Chinese university students with greater stocks of cultural and social capital—manifested through academic literacy, institutional familiarity, and peer networks—derive disproportionately higher academic returns from GenAI usage. Bekirsky [8] likewise frames mastery of AI tools as a form of technological capital, in which access, expertise, and pedagogical support interact to determine how effectively students convert digital competence into improved grades and academic recognition. In both cases, GenAI does not democratize learning; rather, it functions as a capital multiplier that reinforces stratification by rewarding those already positioned advantageously within the educational field. However, alternative evidence challenges the",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,3,"competence into improved grades and academic recognition. In both cases, GenAI does not democratize learning; rather, it functions as a capital multiplier that reinforces stratification by rewarding those already positioned advantageously within the educational field. However, alternative evidence challenges the determinism of this reproduction logic. Buragohain et al. [13] argue that when technological systems are pedagogically scaf­ folded, they can redistribute technological capital by offering all learners active, embod­ ied participation in knowledge creation. Within such environments, economic advantage",79
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,4,"Page 4 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 exerts less influence, as engagement, collaboration, and experiential learning foster col­ lective skill acquisition and embodied cultural capital in digital form. These immersive systems demonstrate how institutional design can transform technology from a resource that reproduces inequality into a catalyst for capital conversion, enabling students with limited initial endowments to acquire digital fluency and academic competence through participation. As such, these strands reveals an important theoretical tension. Some studies frame GenAI as a site of social reproduction, where pre‑existing capital distributions determine learners’ opportunities to leverage AI for academic gain. Oth­ ers illustrate possibilities for social transformation, where inclusive pedagogies enable the accumulation and equitable conversion of technological capital. Consequently, the academic performance effects of GenAI cannot be understood outside the relational dynamics of capital conversion within the educational field. Whether GenAI amplifies or attenuates inequality depends on how institutions structure access, cultivate disposi­ tions, and legitimize particular forms of digital competence as academically valuable. 2.2 Existing research on impact of GenAI on students’ academic performance in higher education",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,4,"field. Whether GenAI amplifies or attenuates inequality depends on how institutions structure access, cultivate disposi­ tions, and legitimize particular forms of digital competence as academically valuable. 2.2 Existing research on impact of GenAI on students’ academic performance in higher education Recent research presents a nuanced yet divided picture of how generative artificial intel­ ligence (GenAI) influences academic performance outcomes in higher education. Across multiple studies, the consensus is that GenAI’s effectiveness in improving grades, com­ prehension, and higher‑order thinking depends less on the technology itself than on how purposefully and reflectively it is embedded in learning practices. Sharples, [36] and Sun and Zhou and [38] both demonstrate that when GenAI supports collaborative reflection and critical dialogue, it strengthens students’ capacity for conceptual understanding and problem‑solving. Sun and Zhou’s [38] meta‑analytic findings—showing a moderate but significant positive effect on academic achievement—empirically substantiate Sharples’s [36] claim that guided, reflective integration of AI leads to measurable performance gains in comprehension, retention, and engagement. The strength and consistency of these performance gains, however, appear to be medi­ ated by contextual and behavioral factors as",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,4,"substantiate Sharples’s [36] claim that guided, reflective integration of AI leads to measurable performance gains in comprehension, retention, and engagement. The strength and consistency of these performance gains, however, appear to be medi­ ated by contextual and behavioral factors as noted in the existing studies. Alotaibi, [2] and Iqbal et al. [22] align in identifying learners’ trust, technical competence, and meta­ cognitive skills as pivotal to whether GenAI elevates or undermines outcomes such as GPA, test results, and project quality. Similarly, Shahzad et al. [35] confirm that GenAI enhances measurable learning outcomes—such as improved course grades, critical thinking scores, and problem‑solving accuracy—by facilitating collaborative e‑learning, personalized feedback, and adaptive pacing. These findings collectively portray GenAI as most impactful on academic performance when it complements rather than replaces students’ cognitive effort, reinforcing self‑regulated and socially enriched learning. Conversely, research also cautions that uncontrolled or excessive reliance on GenAI can produce negative academic outcomes. Abbas et al. [1] and Hashmi and Bal [21] report that students who depend heavily on automated outputs exhibit declines in mem­ ory retention, analytical reasoning, and cumulative",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,4,"that uncontrolled or excessive reliance on GenAI can produce negative academic outcomes. Abbas et al. [1] and Hashmi and Bal [21] report that students who depend heavily on automated outputs exhibit declines in mem­ ory retention, analytical reasoning, and cumulative grade averages. Similarly, Shahzad et al. [34] reveal a paradoxical outcome in which the same tools that boost performance in moderated settings can erode critical thinking and long‑term learning when used with­ out reflection. Together, these studies underscore that the academic performance impact of GenAI is inherently dual and conditional: its benefits—higher achievement, improved",94
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,5,"Page 5 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 comprehension, and deeper analytical capacity—are realized when use is guided by metacognition, ethical awareness, and skillful pedagogy; its drawbacks—lower reten­ tion, reduced originality, and academic underperformance—emerge when use is unre­ flective or unregulated. In sum, the current literature positions GenAI as a performance enhancer when employed critically and reflectively, and as a performance inhibitor when used passively or without academic scaffolding. Achieving sustained improvements in learning outcomes thus depends on aligning GenAI use with pedagogical design, learner self‑regulation, and institutional guidance. 2.3 Use of generative AI across different levels of cognitive complexity and its impact on academic performance Recent research increasingly agrees that GenAI exerts a considerable influence on aca­ demic performance, but the nature and direction of that influence depend on the cog­ nitive level of the task and the conditions under which AI is integrated into learning. Wang and Fan [41] and Deng et al. [17] demonstrate that AI tools such as ChatGPT enhance students’ learning outcomes across cognitive levels, though gains are most con­ sistent in",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,5,"and the conditions under which AI is integrated into learning. Wang and Fan [41] and Deng et al. [17] demonstrate that AI tools such as ChatGPT enhance students’ learning outcomes across cognitive levels, though gains are most con­ sistent in moderately complex tasks—structured problem-solving, skills-based exercises, and concept-building activities. In these contexts, GenAI acts as a cognitive support system, providing scaffolding and feedback that reduce cognitive overload and improve conceptual transfer. Yet these improvements appear contingent on instructional design, suggesting that AI’s pedagogical alignment rather than its technological sophistication determines its educational value. Expanding this perspective, Buragohain and Chaudhary [14] show that when AI- mediated learning is personalized and interactive, it strengthens academic perfor­ mance through heightened engagement, motivation, and communicative competence. However, their findings also expose the risk of functional inversion: excessive reliance on automation dilutes creative thinking and weakens students’ ethical responsibility for their own learning. This same tension between enhanced efficiency and diminished depth reappears in Janiunienė et al. [24], who find that while GenAI sharply increases research productivity for social science academics, it simultaneously generates concerns",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,5,"weakens students’ ethical responsibility for their own learning. This same tension between enhanced efficiency and diminished depth reappears in Janiunienė et al. [24], who find that while GenAI sharply increases research productivity for social science academics, it simultaneously generates concerns about originality, critical reasoning, and methodological rigor. Together these studies reveal a paradox—GenAI optimizes academic processes yet threatens the very cognitive and ethical qualities that sustain high-level scholarship. In lower-order cognitive domains, consensus is strong that GenAI improves imme­ diate academic results. Mekheimer [28] and Jaboob et al. [23] report marked progress in students’ written accuracy, organization, and cohesion through AI-assisted feedback systems such as Grammarly and ChatGPT. Patac and Patac [32] further observe that by simplifying complex information and restructuring problems, AI reduces cognitive load and enhances procedural fluency, particularly in quantitative subjects. These findings converge on the view that GenAI acts as a learning accelerator, streamlining repetitive or rule-based tasks and fostering more consistent short-term performance outcomes. Evidence diverges more sharply in higher-order cognitive contexts. Chen et al. [15] demonstrate that integrating ChatGPT within argument-mapping frameworks cultivates analytical",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,5,"that GenAI acts as a learning accelerator, streamlining repetitive or rule-based tasks and fostering more consistent short-term performance outcomes. Evidence diverges more sharply in higher-order cognitive contexts. Chen et al. [15] demonstrate that integrating ChatGPT within argument-mapping frameworks cultivates analytical and creative reasoning, while Wang and Fan [41] find that its tutoring capac­ ity stimulates reflective and metacognitive development in demanding STEM settings. Conversely, Etkin et al. [18] show that over-scaffolding through AI can stifle curiosity",76
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,6,"Page 6 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 and inhibit critical depth, especially among advanced learners who require cognitive dis­ sonance to achieve mastery. Similar concerns resonate in Janiunienė et al. [24], where accelerated output among researchers is frequently accompanied by superficial interpre­ tation and diminished evaluative rigor—an academic parallel to shallow learning among students who allow AI to dominate rather than complement their reasoning. Across these studies, two patterns of cognitive action emerge. For lower-order pro­ cesses, GenAI consistently enhances performance by improving recall, procedural accu­ racy, and linguistic precision—metrics often used to assess immediate achievement. In contrast, its influence on higher-order cognition is conditional: meaningful gains occur when AI is used to stimulate reflection, dialogue, and self-regulation, but its benefits col­ lapse into dependency when engagement becomes passive or instrumental. As a result, GenAI functions simultaneously as an enabler and a constraint—strengthening com­ prehension, autonomy, and achievement when ethically and pedagogically mediated, yet undermining originality and deep reasoning when treated as a surrogate for human thought. 2.4 Literature gap and research contribution Despite the extensive",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,6,"simultaneously as an enabler and a constraint—strengthening com­ prehension, autonomy, and achievement when ethically and pedagogically mediated, yet undermining originality and deep reasoning when treated as a surrogate for human thought. 2.4 Literature gap and research contribution Despite the extensive research on generative AI and student learning, several important gaps remain in the existing literature on its relationship with academic performance. First, most studies to date have focused on technical or applied disciplines, leaving limited empirical evidence on how generative AI is associated with learning processes and outcomes in the social sciences. These fields emphasize interpretive reasoning, theoretical reflection, and value‑based analysis—forms of knowledge construction that differ substantially from the procedural or computational focus common in STEM edu­ cation. Yet, relatively little is known about how GenAI tools interact with these distinct epistemological traditions, how students employ them to support conceptual or theoret­ ical work, or how such engagement relates to academic achievement. This study there­ fore examines patterns of GenAI use and their association with academic performance among undergraduate students in the social‑science disciplines—a context largely over­ looked in",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,6,"conceptual or theoret­ ical work, or how such engagement relates to academic achievement. This study there­ fore examines patterns of GenAI use and their association with academic performance among undergraduate students in the social‑science disciplines—a context largely over­ looked in current AI‑in‑education research. By doing so, it contributes new empirical evidence from a non‑STEM setting, highlighting how students in interpretive and the­ ory‑driven subjects use GenAI across tasks of varying cognitive complexity. In this way, the study moves beyond the STEM‑dominated literature to explore whether and how AI‑assisted learning aligns with the development of analytical reasoning, reflexivity, and conceptual synthesis—skills central to social‑science education. Second, while previous research often distinguishes between responsible and exces­ sive AI use, most studies have examined only a single cognitive dimension—typically focusing on either lower‑order or higher‑order tasks—without comparing how different levels of complexity may jointly relate to academic achievement. Existing findings tend to highlight GenAI’s benefits for basic academic tasks such as summarizing, proofread­ ing, or organizing information [23, 28], whereas others emphasize its potential to foster critical reasoning and reflective learning [17, 41].",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,6,"jointly relate to academic achievement. Existing findings tend to highlight GenAI’s benefits for basic academic tasks such as summarizing, proofread­ ing, or organizing information [23, 28], whereas others emphasize its potential to foster critical reasoning and reflective learning [17, 41]. However, little research systematically explores how students employ GenAI across the full spectrum of cognitive demands, or how these differentiated usage patterns are associated with measurable academic outcomes such as GPA. By comparing the associations between GenAI use at lower‑, middle‑, and higher‑order levels, this study addresses that limitation and clarifies how",92
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,7,"Page 7 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 varying modes of AI‑assisted learning may correspond to different aspects of academic performance. Third, within the broader landscape of higher education, existing studies on generative AI and academic outcomes are predominantly concentrated in Western contexts (e.g., Al Emran et al. [3]) and Mainland China (e.g., [19]), with limited empirical work focus­ ing on Hong Kong. Despite Hong Kong’s prominence as an international education hub, few studies have examined the determinants of academic performance among its uni­ versity students, particularly regarding the role of emerging technologies such as GenAI. The territory’s tertiary sector occupies a unique position, blending Western pedagogical traditions that emphasize critical thinking and learner autonomy with Confucian edu­ cational values that stress diligence, hierarchy, and examination success [25]. Existing studies in Hong Kong have largely addressed AI adoption, digital literacy, or attitudes toward GenAI (e.g. [26, 30]) rather than their associations with academic outcomes. Investigating these relationships within Hong Kong’s hybrid educational environment thus provides valuable insight into how students navigate emerging technologies to sup­ port their",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,7,"adoption, digital literacy, or attitudes toward GenAI (e.g. [26, 30]) rather than their associations with academic outcomes. Investigating these relationships within Hong Kong’s hybrid educational environment thus provides valuable insight into how students navigate emerging technologies to sup­ port their learning, offering a more context‑specific understanding of GenAI’s role in higher education. Drawing on these gaps, the present study contributes by providing (a) empirical evi­ dence from a non‑technical, social‑science context, (b) a cognitive‑level comparison of GenAI use that captures its differentiated associations with academic performance, and (c) a regionally distinctive perspective that situates these associations within Hong Kong’s unique educational culture. Together, these contributions refine our understand­ ing of when and how generative AI use—as a form of technological capital—is associ­ ated with variation in student achievement across diverse learning contexts. 2.5 Mechanism, research question, and hypothesis Drawing explicitly from Bourdieu’s [12] theory of capital and Selwyn’s [33] extension of technological capital, this study conceptualizes generative AI use as a resource whose educational value depends on its association with outcomes valued within the academic field. The mere possession or",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,7,"from Bourdieu’s [12] theory of capital and Selwyn’s [33] extension of technological capital, this study conceptualizes generative AI use as a resource whose educational value depends on its association with outcomes valued within the academic field. The mere possession or frequent use of this technological capital does not auto­ matically yield advantage; its convertibility is mediated by students’ habitus—their dis­ positions, skills, and learned tendencies—and by the cognitive demands of the academic tasks they perform. In the social sciences—where intellectual success depends more on interpretive reasoning and theoretical reflection than procedural accuracy—the capac­ ity to strategically apply GenAI is associated with whether technological capital is effec­ tively transformed into academic capital. Existing evidence suggests that students’ engagement with GenAI differs across lev­ els of cognitive complexity. Most students employ GenAI frequently for lower‑order tasks such as summarizing or proofreading, where its automated functions align closely with procedural learning demands [23, 28]. In contrast, GenAI use for middle‑ and higher‑order tasks—such as conceptual synthesis or critical evaluation—occurs less consistently and produces more ambiguous outcomes [15, 18]. This differentiation underscores that what",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,7,"its automated functions align closely with procedural learning demands [23, 28]. In contrast, GenAI use for middle‑ and higher‑order tasks—such as conceptual synthesis or critical evaluation—occurs less consistently and produces more ambiguous outcomes [15, 18]. This differentiation underscores that what matters is not the volume of use, but the socially and cognitively appropriate association between technological capital and learning outcomes within the field’s rules of the game.",67
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,8,"Page 8 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 RQ1 What are the patterns of generative AI usage among social science faculty university students in Hong Kong? The proposed mechanism for H1 centers on the distinction between merely using technology and effectively associating it with meaningful academic capital. Within Bourdieu’s [12] framework, technological capital represents a resource whose academic value is associated with how successfully it can be connected to outcomes that are legiti­ mate within a given field. In the academic field of social science—where the “rules of the game” emphasize critical interpretation, theoretical reasoning, and originality—the association between GenAI use and academic performance is determined not by fre­ quency of use alone but by the strategic alignment between tool use and disciplinary expectations. The overall non‑significant relationship likely emerges because frequent use cap­ tures two contrasting behavioral patterns. For some students, higher frequency signals strategic conversion of technological capital—using GenAI appropriately for suitable tasks—to enhance efficiency and accuracy [36]. For others, however, frequent use indi­ cates uncritical dependence, implying limited cultural capital or academic discernment regarding",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,8,"behavioral patterns. For some students, higher frequency signals strategic conversion of technological capital—using GenAI appropriately for suitable tasks—to enhance efficiency and accuracy [36]. For others, however, frequent use indi­ cates uncritical dependence, implying limited cultural capital or academic discernment regarding when and how GenAI should be applied. This habitual or indiscriminate use can undermine deep learning processes, as prior studies note that excessive reliance on AI encourages procrastination and reduces critical engagement [1, 34], 2026b). When these opposing dynamics coexist in the same population, the positive patterns of stra­ tegic use are offset by the negative consequences of misuse—resulting in an overall non‑significant association between GenAI use frequency and academic performance. H1 More frequent use of generative AI is not positively associated with higher academic performance among Hong Kong social science faculty university students. The mechanism for H2 reflects the high degree of alignment between the technologi­ cal capital embodied in GenAI and the specific requirements of lower‑order academic tasks. In these contexts, technological capital exhibits a high level of convertibility, as the expected outputs—such as proofreading, grammar checking, or",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,8,"high degree of alignment between the technologi­ cal capital embodied in GenAI and the specific requirements of lower‑order academic tasks. In these contexts, technological capital exhibits a high level of convertibility, as the expected outputs—such as proofreading, grammar checking, or summarizing—are rule‑based, procedural, and easily automated. Generative AI demonstrates clear asso­ ciations with improved performance in these domains by providing quick, accurate feedback and synthesis [28]. When students apply GenAI to such tasks, they effectively connect technological capital with academic advantage. This occurs through cognitive offloading, where repetitive mechanical work is delegated to the tool, freeing cognitive resources for more demanding stages of academic work—such as constructing argu­ ments or analyzing theoretical concepts [23]. By optimizing efficiency and enhancing the technical precision of assignments, this strategic association between technological capital and academic work allows students to meet academic expectations more effec­ tively, thereby translating directly into improved performance. H2 More frequent use of generative AI for lower-order tasks is positively associated with higher academic performance among social-science university students. However, for middle‑order and higher‑order tasks, Bourdieu’s [12] framework high­ lights",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,8,"effec­ tively, thereby translating directly into improved performance. H2 More frequent use of generative AI for lower-order tasks is positively associated with higher academic performance among social-science university students. However, for middle‑order and higher‑order tasks, Bourdieu’s [12] framework high­ lights the unequal capacity of different forms of capital to be connected within a given field. In the context of higher‑education learning, technological capital—such as GenAI skills—has restricted association in areas where cultural capital is more decisive. The",77
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,9,"Page 9 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 intellectual work of the social sciences—developing arguments, connecting theories, and producing original interpretations—depends fundamentally on the habitus that students have built through prior educational and cultural experiences. These ingrained dispositions and competencies allow them to engage critically with knowledge in ways that technology cannot easily replicate or replace [25]. Although GenAI can efficiently process, organize, and present information [18], its connection to genuine critical think­ ing and conceptual innovation remains limited. Within Bourdieu’s [12] logic of capital conversion, technological resources alone cannot be transformed into academic success when the field rewards original reasoning and theoretical depth. As a result, frequent use of GenAI for such relatively higher‑order purposes may not show a significant asso­ ciation with improved outcomes. In these contexts, academic achievement continues to depend on the student’s embodied cultural capital—personal understanding, analytical skill, and disciplinary habitus—rather than on technological proficiency [1, 18]. H3 More frequent use of generative AI for middle-order and higher-order tasks is not significantly associated with academic performance among social-science university students. 3 Methodology",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,9,"embodied cultural capital—personal understanding, analytical skill, and disciplinary habitus—rather than on technological proficiency [1, 18]. H3 More frequent use of generative AI for middle-order and higher-order tasks is not significantly associated with academic performance among social-science university students. 3 Methodology 3.1 Study focus and data collection This study employs a cross-sectional survey of 107 respondents conducted through face-to-face data collection at a public university in Hong Kong—The Chinese Univer­ sity of Hong Kong (CUHK)—during the first semester of the 2025–2026 academic year, from late October to early November 2025. Consistent with existing research ([20]; Ko et al. [25]), the study focuses on a single institution to enable a more nuanced exami­ nation of students’ perceptions and behaviors within a distinct academic and cultural environment, thereby ensuring internal consistency and contextual relevance in explor­ ing the adoption of generative AI in higher education. Concentrating on one university also reduces potential variability arising from differences in grading practices, academic rigor, and assessment standards across institutions in Hong Kong. The educational environment at CUHK provides a compelling context for exploring Hong Kong’s growing",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,9,"higher education. Concentrating on one university also reduces potential variability arising from differences in grading practices, academic rigor, and assessment standards across institutions in Hong Kong. The educational environment at CUHK provides a compelling context for exploring Hong Kong’s growing role as an emerging educational hub in East Asia, particularly in the evolving era of GenAI. Positioned at the crossroads of Eastern and Western edu­ cational traditions, Hong Kong embodies a unique blend of pedagogical philosophies, combining global innovation with cultural norms rooted in Confucian values such as academic rigor, perseverance, and respect for authority [25]. This distinctive environ­ ment creates an ideal setting to investigate how students incorporate GenAI tools into their learning processes while upholding high academic standards and adapting to institutional expectations. As universities in Hong Kong strive to balance technological advancement with a commitment to academic excellence, CUHK serves as a microcosm for understanding how GenAI adoption influences academic performance. Insights derived from this context can contribute to broader regional discussions on how East Asian higher-education systems can responsibly integrate GenAI to enhance learning outcomes within",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,9,"excellence, CUHK serves as a microcosm for understanding how GenAI adoption influences academic performance. Insights derived from this context can contribute to broader regional discussions on how East Asian higher-education systems can responsibly integrate GenAI to enhance learning outcomes within culturally and competitively driven environments. To ensure consistency among respondents, the study focuses on full-time undergradu­ ate students from the Faculty of Social Science, whose coursework and assessments",68
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,10,"Page 10 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 span a wide range of lower-, middle-, and higher-order cognitive tasks. Freshmen were excluded from the study, as they typically do not have grade point average information available at that time. Nonetheless, this diversity provides an ideal setting to examine how generative AI is used across different academic activities and how such use may influence performance. Unlike faculties such as Science or Engineering, which empha­ size calculation-based problem-solving, laboratory experimentation, and algorithmic precision, the Social Science curriculum centers on open-ended inquiry, interpretation, and argumentation. Undergraduate students in this faculty are more likely to engage in summarizing readings (lower-order), interpreting data and developing theoretical argu­ ments (middle-order), and conducting critical analyses or independent research (higher- order). Part-time, postgraduate, associate-degree, and higher-diploma students were excluded to maintain sample comparability, as their programs often differ in curriculum intensity, assessment structures, and grading systems—frequently adopting pass–fail or competency-based evaluation methods—which could introduce inconsistencies when analyzing academic performance, as noted by Ko et al. [25]. A purposive convenience sampling approach was adopted, mainly due",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,10,"programs often differ in curriculum intensity, assessment structures, and grading systems—frequently adopting pass–fail or competency-based evaluation methods—which could introduce inconsistencies when analyzing academic performance, as noted by Ko et al. [25]. A purposive convenience sampling approach was adopted, mainly due to field acces­ sibility and the absence of a centralized list of enrolled students. Participants were recruited through face‑to‑face enumeration conducted by the authors in common areas of the CUHK campus where many Social Science classes were held. Potential participants were asked whether they were full‑time undergraduate students majoring in a Social Science discipline and enrolled from sophomore to senior year; only those meeting these criteria were invited to complete the survey. To encourage participation, respondents who completed the questionnaire were eligible for a small incentive draw for a HKD 50 voucher. Contact information for the draw (such as email or phone num­ ber) was collected separately from survey responses and deleted immediately after the draw. All respondents were provided with clear information on the first page of the ques­ tionnaire regarding the study’s purpose, voluntary participation, right to withdraw",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,10,"or phone num­ ber) was collected separately from survey responses and deleted immediately after the draw. All respondents were provided with clear information on the first page of the ques­ tionnaire regarding the study’s purpose, voluntary participation, right to withdraw at any time, and confidentiality assurances. Only fully completed responses were retained for analysis. All data remained fully anonymized throughout the research process and were stored securely on password‑protected devices accessible only to the research team, in compliance with institutional research ethics requirements and the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Taken together, these procedures ensured that recruitment and data handling adhered to ethical standards while also providing adequate coverage of the Faculty’s major aca­ demic concentrations. The final sample (N = 107) included variation in gender, year of study, and academic background and represented approximately 4–5% of the Faculty of Social Science’s undergraduate population (about 2,400 students). A power analysis indi­ cated that this size provides around 95% confidence with a ± 9% margin of error—ade­ quate for exploratory statistical analysis and consistent with previous single-institution research in similar",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,10,"of Social Science’s undergraduate population (about 2,400 students). A power analysis indi­ cated that this size provides around 95% confidence with a ± 9% margin of error—ade­ quate for exploratory statistical analysis and consistent with previous single-institution research in similar higher-education contexts (e.g., [25]). As illustrated in Figs. 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5, the sample composition broadly reflects the char­ acteristics of the actual undergraduate population within the faculty. Respondents were distributed across different years of study (19.6% sophomores, 44.9% juniors, and 35.5% seniors or above) and exhibited a near-balanced gender ratio (55.1% male and 44.9% female). The age profile also aligns with that of a typical undergraduate cohort, with most participants aged 20 (32.7%) or 21 (21.5%). Local students constituted 72.9% of the",125
