DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,12,"Page 12 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 sample, and non-locals 27.1%. In terms of academic specialization, respondents repre­ sented a wide range of disciplines within the Faculty of Social Science, including Global Studies (18.7%), Geography and Resource Management (14%), Economics (11.2%), as well as Urban Studies, Journalism and Communication, Government and Public Admin­ istration, and Sociology. This diversity enhances the contextual validity of the findings and supports their representativeness for the broader social-science undergraduate pop­ ulation at CUHK. 3.2 Variables For the key dependent variable, academic achievement is operationalized using students’ cumulative grade point average (CGPA) as of the 2025–2026 academic year. Although GPA is not a perfect indicator, it remains one of the most widely used and practical mea­ sures of academic performance (e.g., Ko et al., [25]). In this study, cumulative GPA—rep­ resenting students’ overall academic record since entering CUHK—is adopted instead of a single‑semester GPA because generative AI tools, particularly ChatGPT, became Fig. 5 Distribution of respondents’ study majors Fig. 4 Distribution of respondents by student origin status",175
