DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,13,"Page 13 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 widely accessible and popular in early 2023, shortly after the current cohort of seniors began their studies in late 2022. This timing allows for a broader assessment of how pat­ terns of GenAI use may correspond to academic performance accumulated over mul­ tiple semesters rather than short‑term fluctuations. To facilitate data collection and reduce non‑response bias, respondents were asked to report their GPA within 0.3‑point intervals (e.g., 3.7–4.0, 3.3–3.699, etc.), which were then converted to midpoint values for analysis. This categorical‑to‑continuous conver­ sion helps maintain comparability across responses while reducing participants’ reluc­ tance to disclose exact scores, particularly in the Hong Kong context, where grades hold relatively high social and academic importance among university students and where the education system—including tertiary education—tends to place strong emphasis on academic success [25, 29]. Although this interval‑based approach may modestly reduce measurement precision, its influence on regression estimates is likely limited given the narrow four‑point GPA scale and the preservation of relative differences between respondents through midpoint conversion. Overall, this operationalization provides",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,13,"[25, 29]. Although this interval‑based approach may modestly reduce measurement precision, its influence on regression estimates is likely limited given the narrow four‑point GPA scale and the preservation of relative differences between respondents through midpoint conversion. Overall, this operationalization provides a reasonable balance between accuracy and response feasibility. To measure the use of generative AI (GenAI) in academic contexts, this study employs four single‑item variables designed to capture the extent to which respondents engage with GenAI tools in their academic activities. To establish a baseline, respondents first reported their overall frequency of GenAI use across common academic tasks, including exam preparation, writing assignments, creating presentations, and completing reading tasks. This measure, termed GenAI Overall Task Use, was rated on a five‑point Likert scale ranging from Almost Never / Never (1) to Always (5). In addition, three variables were constructed to reflect GenAI use at distinct cognitive levels based on Bloom’s tax­ onomy of educational objectives [4, 10], which differentiates lower‑order cognitive pro­ cesses—such as remembering and understanding—from higher‑order processes—such as analyzing, evaluating, and creating. Accordingly, the GenAI Lower‑Order Task Use",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,13,"reflect GenAI use at distinct cognitive levels based on Bloom’s tax­ onomy of educational objectives [4, 10], which differentiates lower‑order cognitive pro­ cesses—such as remembering and understanding—from higher‑order processes—such as analyzing, evaluating, and creating. Accordingly, the GenAI Lower‑Order Task Use variable captures use for basic academic activities such as grammar checking, proofread­ ing, translating text, and summarizing articles or books. The GenAI Middle‑Order Task Use variable measures use for intermediate tasks such as rewriting or paraphrasing text and explaining complex concepts. Finally, the GenAI Higher‑Order Task Use variable represents use for cognitively demanding activities such as brainstorming and generat­ ing ideas, outlining essays or reports, solving complex problems or coding, and critiqu­ ing or evaluating one’s own arguments. This structured design ensures consistent measurement and meaningful comparison of generative AI use across levels of cognitive engagement. Single‑item indicators were employed to minimize respondent burden and enhance direct comparability across the four dimensions of AI use on the same scale. Given that the primary objective was to contrast patterns of engagement rather than to evaluate latent psychological con­ structs, single‑item measures",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,13,"to minimize respondent burden and enhance direct comparability across the four dimensions of AI use on the same scale. Given that the primary objective was to contrast patterns of engagement rather than to evaluate latent psychological con­ structs, single‑item measures provide an efficient and transparent approach suitable for cross‑sectional survey research of this scope. However, single‑item indicators inevitably limit reliability and may not capture the full nuance of students’ varied GenAI practices. Future research should develop and validate multi‑item scales to strengthen measure­ ment precision and construct validity. The first control variable in this study is Openness of GenAI use for academic contexts, which may influence students’ academic performance. Students with higher openness",113
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,14,"Page 14 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 levels are generally more willing to explore and integrate generative AI as a legitimate learning aid, allowing them to leverage its capabilities for idea generation, clarification of complex concepts, and refinement of academic work. Such proactive and confident use can enhance learning efficiency, comprehension, and the overall quality of academic outputs, which may, in turn, lead to improved GPA outcomes [2]. This variable is mea­ sured as a composite index, calculated as the average score (1 = Strongly Disagree to 5 = Strongly Agree) across five items assessing perceptions of acceptability, ethical align­ ment, comfort, and lack of guilt when using generative AI for academic tasks. The scale demonstrated excellent internal construct validity (Cronbach’s α = 0.91) and showed strong item‑total correlations (r = 0.74–0.82), indicating that each item contributed meaningfully to the overall construct reliability. In addition, several demographic control variables were included—Gender, Age, Student Origin, and Study Year—as these factors may also influence academic perfor­ mance. Gender may affect GPA through differences in learning attitudes, study habits,",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,14,"contributed meaningfully to the overall construct reliability. In addition, several demographic control variables were included—Gender, Age, Student Origin, and Study Year—as these factors may also influence academic perfor­ mance. Gender may affect GPA through differences in learning attitudes, study habits, and help‑seeking behaviors observed across male and female students. Age can influ­ ence academic achievement, as older students may possess greater maturity that could enhance their focus, self‑discipline, and ability to manage academic responsibilities effectively. Student Origin (local vs. non‑local) is relevant because cultural adjustment and academic support networks may vary among groups, potentially affecting perfor­ mance. Lastly, Study Year was considered because students’ academic experience and workload differ by progression level, with senior students typically demonstrating greater academic skill and adaptation to university expectations, which could lead to higher GPAs. In addition, this study also incorporates two institutionalized parental cultural capi­ tal variables—mother’s and father’s education levels—as parents with higher educational attainment are typically more familiar with the academic culture and expectations val­ ued by schools. According to Bourdieu [11, 12], this familiarity allows them to transmit educational norms",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,14,"cultural capi­ tal variables—mother’s and father’s education levels—as parents with higher educational attainment are typically more familiar with the academic culture and expectations val­ ued by schools. According to Bourdieu [11, 12], this familiarity allows them to transmit educational norms and strategies to their children, which can positively influence stu­ dents’ GPA. Hence, this study further examines whether such an association persists in a higher‑education context, where students may have developed greater independence from parental influence but where inherited cultural familiarity may still shape academic outcomes. Together, these demographic, attitudinal, and familial variables serve as con­ trol factors to account for alternative explanations and isolate the specific effects of gen­ erative AI usage on students’ academic performance. All descriptive statistics are shown in Table 1. 3.3 Analytical method and empirical modelling To begin, descriptive statistics will be presented to provide an overview of the respon­ dents’ characteristics on the frequency and patterns of generative AI usage across vari­ ous academic purposes. This descriptive analysis offers important contextual insights prior to the regression analysis, providing an overview of the sample’s academic",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,14,"an overview of the respon­ dents’ characteristics on the frequency and patterns of generative AI usage across vari­ ous academic purposes. This descriptive analysis offers important contextual insights prior to the regression analysis, providing an overview of the sample’s academic profile and illustrating general trends in generative AI adoption among students. To examine the relationship between generative AI usage and students’ academic per­ formance, this study employs multiple linear regression analysis. The models estimate how different dimensions of GenAI use—including overall lower‑order, middle‑order, and higher‑order task usage—are associated with students’ cumulative GPA, controlling",93
