DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,16,"Page 16 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 majors under the Faculty of Social Science: Global Studies, Sociology, Government and Public Administration, Urban Studies, Geography and Resource Management, Data Sci­ ence and Policy Studies, Global Communication, Architectural Studies, Global Econom­ ics and Finance, Journalism and Communication, Economics, Psychology, and Social Work. These fixed effects capture each major’s mean characteristics; hence, no explicit reference category is required, and the resulting coefficients reflect within‑major varia­ tion among students. It should be noted, however, that while the inclusion of fixed effects accounts for broad disciplinary differences, the models do not control for differences in course‑specific assessment formats—such as examinations, essays, or project‑based evaluations—which can vary both across and within majors, as students often choose from courses with diverse grading structures. Such variation may influence learning strategies and, consequently, the associations observed in the models. Specifically, a series of five regression models will be estimated: Model 1 regresses cumulative GPA on overall generative AI usage with all control variables; Model 2 focuses on lower-order task usage; Model 3 examines middle-order task",180
