DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,16,"each model progressively isolates the influence of different dimensions of generative AI use on students’ cumulative GPA, while accounting for control variables and fixed effects to ensure robust and unbiased estimation. In addition, as a sensitiv­ ity analysis, we re‑estimated the same five models using ordinary least squares (OLS) without including the major‑level fixed effects to assess the robustness of the findings. This additional analysis helps verify that the observed relationships are not driven by the fixed‑effects specification or by between‑major differences in academic grading. The corresponding model specifications are presented in Appendix Table 3. 4 Results 4.1 Patterns of GenAI usage Figure 6 presents the percentages of respondents who reported using specific genera­ tive AI tools for academic purposes. ChatGPT shows the highest usage rate, with 86% of students indicating they have used it, followed by Google Gemini (51.4%), DeepSeek (31.8%), and Microsoft Bing (29.9%). Use of other tools is comparatively limited: Claude",154
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,17,"Page 17 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 (15%), image generators (7.5%), and GitHub Copilot (4.7%). The data indicate a clear concentration of usage around a few text-based AI platforms, with ChatGPT being by far the most prevalent among respondents. Meanwhile, Fig. 7 summarizes the frequency of generative AI use across different types of academic tasks. Overall, most students report moderate to frequent engagement with generative AI tools. Across all task categories, the majority of responses fall within the “sometimes” and “often” ranges, indicating that generative AI has become a regular part of students’ academic practices rather than an occasional aid. Usage is highest for lower-order tasks, with over two-thirds (66.4%) of respondents indicating “sometimes” or “often” use. Engagement is slightly lower for middle-order tasks (61.7% combined for “sometimes” and “often”) and higher-order tasks (63.5% combined). Few students report “always” using generative AI across categories (ranging from 7.5% for overall tasks to 11.2% for higher-order tasks), while only a small proportion—between 1.9% and 8.4%— say they “almost never” use it. Taken together, the distribution suggests a broadly",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,17,"students report “always” using generative AI across categories (ranging from 7.5% for overall tasks to 11.2% for higher-order tasks), while only a small proportion—between 1.9% and 8.4%— say they “almost never” use it. Taken together, the distribution suggests a broadly con­ sistent pattern of adoption across cognitive levels, with lower-order, middle-order, and higher-order tasks each showing similar proportions of moderate to high usage. This Fig. 7 Distribution of GenAI tool usage across task types Fig. 6 Usage distribution of specific GenAI tools for academic purposes",85
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,18,"Page 18 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 indicates that generative AI has become a common supplementary tool across a wide range of academic activities rather than being limited to specific task types. 4.2 Regression results Table 1 presents the results of five multiple linear regression models examining the associations between generative AI (GenAI) usage and students’ cumulative GPA. All models include demographic, attitudinal, and familial background controls, with faculty‑level fixed effects applied to account for unobserved heterogeneity across aca­ demic disciplines. The models show stable explanatory power: adjusted R‑squared val­ ues range between 0.52 and 0.55, indicating that roughly half of the variation in GPA is explained by the included predictors. The corresponding Variance Inflation Factors (VIFs), reported in square brackets, remain below 3, suggesting no evidence of prob­ lematic multicollinearity. Total and residual sums of squares are consistent and decline modestly across models (TSS = 10.429; RSS ≈ 3.7–4.1), reflecting numerical coherence and incremental improvements in model fit. Regarding overall fit, the fixed‑effects models yield R‑squared values between 0.61 and 0.65 and adjusted values between",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,18,"squares are consistent and decline modestly across models (TSS = 10.429; RSS ≈ 3.7–4.1), reflecting numerical coherence and incremental improvements in model fit. Regarding overall fit, the fixed‑effects models yield R‑squared values between 0.61 and 0.65 and adjusted values between 0.52 and 0.55, while the OLS models estimated with­ out fixed effects (Appendix Table 3) show comparable results (R‑squared = 0.62–0.66; adjusted R‑squared = 0.58–0.62). Although somewhat high for cross‑sectional data, these figures appear largely driven by the inclusion of theoretically relevant control variables—such as openness toward GenAI, demographic characteristics, and paren­ tal education—that capture key sources of variation in GPA. The similarity in adjusted R‑squared values across the two model types suggests that model fit primarily reflects comprehensive specification rather than inflation from fixed effects, though a modest degree of inflation cannot be ruled out. Turning to individual effects, Model 1 indicates that overall GenAI usage is not sig­ nificantly associated with GPA (β = − 0.0063, p > 0.10), implying that the general frequency of use does not meaningfully predict academic performance. When disaggre­ gated by task type,",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,18,"Model 1 indicates that overall GenAI usage is not sig­ nificantly associated with GPA (β = − 0.0063, p > 0.10), implying that the general frequency of use does not meaningfully predict academic performance. When disaggre­ gated by task type, Model 2 shows that lower‑order GenAI usage is positively related to GPA (β = 0.0526, p < 0.05), meaning that a one‑unit increase in lower‑order use on the five‑point scale corresponds to an estimated 0.05‑point increase in GPA, holding other factors constant. In contrast, Model 3 finds a small, nonsignificant negative associa­ tion for middle‑order usage (β = − 0.0158, p > 0.10), and Model 4 reveals no significant link for higher‑order usage (β = 0.0020, p > 0.10). When all task‑specific variables are entered simultaneously in Model 5, the positive coefficient for lower‑order use remains statistically significant (β = 0.0711, p < 0.05), indicating that each one‑point increase in lower‑order AI use is associated with a 0.07‑point rise in cumulative GPA. The coef­ ficients for middle‑order (β = − 0.0575, p > 0.10) and higher‑order (β = 0.0374, p",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,18,"0.0711, p < 0.05), indicating that each one‑point increase in lower‑order AI use is associated with a 0.07‑point rise in cumulative GPA. The coef­ ficients for middle‑order (β = − 0.0575, p > 0.10) and higher‑order (β = 0.0374, p > 0.10) uses remain statistically nonsignificant. The positive effect of lower‑order AI usage, though modest, demonstrates a consistent pattern across specifications and merits con­ sideration of its practical significance. Across all models, openness toward GenAI shows a robust, positive, and statistically significant relationship with GPA (β ≈ 0.22–0.27, p < 0.01), suggesting that students with more favorable attitudes toward GenAI tend to achieve higher grades. Other control variables—including gender, age, student origin, year of study, and parental education— display small and nonsignificant coefficients. Overall, these results indicate that while",129
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,19,"Page 19 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 the overall frequency of GenAI use shows no meaningful association with academic performance, more frequent use of GenAI for lower‑order academic tasks (e.g., gram­ mar checking, summarizing, translating) is modestly but positively related to GPA. As a robustness check, the same five models were re‑estimated using ordinary least squares (OLS) without faculty‑level fixed effects. The OLS results (Appendix Table 3) closely mirror the main findings: lower‑order usage and openness toward GenAI remain posi­ tively associated with GPA, while middle‑ and higher‑order uses continue to be non­ significant. Openness toward GenAI is the only control variable consistently showing a significant positive relationship with GPA, reinforcing the stability and robustness of the principal findings. 5 Discussion and conclusion 5.1 Reasons for the results The observed patterns of generative AI (GenAI) adoption align with the theoretical expectations derived from Bourdieu’s [12] theory of capital and Selwyn’s [33] extension of technological capital. Students’ widespread use of GenAI across cognitive levels dem­ onstrates that this form of technological capital has become accessible within the",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,19,"align with the theoretical expectations derived from Bourdieu’s [12] theory of capital and Selwyn’s [33] extension of technological capital. Students’ widespread use of GenAI across cognitive levels dem­ onstrates that this form of technological capital has become accessible within the social- science field, but the results also confirm that its possession does not necessarily coincide with uniform academic advantage. As discussed in the theoretical framework, techno­ logical capital functions as a conversion mechanism through which existing resources are reinvested in digital learning contexts [7]. Within the social sciences, where the field rewards interpretation, critical reasoning, and conceptual synthesis [9, 42], the extent to which GenAI use translates into measurable academic gains depends on how effectively it can be converted into outcomes that the field recognizes as legitimate. The overall non‑significant association between total GenAI use and GPA corresponds with H1 and reflects the tension between reproduction and transformation described in studies of technological capital [8, 13, 39]. Frequent use of GenAI appears to encompass diverse forms of engagement: for some students, it reflects deliberate and strategic inte­ gration into study",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,19,"and reflects the tension between reproduction and transformation described in studies of technological capital [8, 13, 39]. Frequent use of GenAI appears to encompass diverse forms of engagement: for some students, it reflects deliberate and strategic inte­ gration into study routines, whereas for others, it may signal habitual or compensatory dependence. These contrasting orientations—rooted in differences in habitus and levels of cultural capital—offset one another, producing no uniform pattern. Similar dynam­ ics are noted in the broader literature, where reflective and guided integration tends to correspond with more positive academic performance outcomes [36, 38], while uncriti­ cal or excessive reliance is often associated with weaker learning engagement that poses risk to one’s academic performance outcome [1, 34]. The present findings are consistent with this expectation, suggesting that technological capital’s academic value emerges only when its use is mediated by reflective dispositions and aligned with the institutional “rules of the game.” The positive association between GenAI use for lower‑order tasks and GPA, corre­ sponding with H2, supports the expectation that technological capital yields greater educational advantage when it enhances efficiency within",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,19,"dispositions and aligned with the institutional “rules of the game.” The positive association between GenAI use for lower‑order tasks and GPA, corre­ sponding with H2, supports the expectation that technological capital yields greater educational advantage when it enhances efficiency within the cognitive and proce­ dural structure of academic work. In rule‑based activities such as proofreading, gram­ mar checking, and summarizing, the functions of GenAI correspond closely with the procedural demands of the task. Prior studies [23, 28, 32] likewise show that AI use in such contexts improves workflow efficiency and consistency, allowing learners to com­ plete mechanical components more quickly and reliably. By streamlining effort and",106
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,20,"Page 20 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 automating routine aspects of production, this strategic use of technological capital enables students to allocate cognitive resources toward interpretation and synthesis. In Selwyn’s [33] framework, this reflects a condition under which the academic field recog­ nizes efficiency itself as a valued outcome, thereby facilitating the conversion of techno­ logical competence into measurable academic capital and higher performance. The absence of significant associations between GenAI use for middle‑ and higher‑order tasks and performance, as predicted in H3, reflects the restricted convert­ ibility of technological capital in domains dominated by cultural capital. In social‑sci­ ence education, achievements at higher cognitive levels depend on analytical autonomy, theoretical synthesis, and reflexive interpretation—skills that draw on embodied cultural capital and intellectual habitus [25]. While GenAI is useful for organizing information [18], its operational strengths are less aligned with the social science field’s evaluative criteria, which privilege originality and conceptual insight. Similar patterns have been noted in research observing that GenAI’s associations with performance weaken as cog­ nitive complexity increases [24, 41]. These findings are",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,20,"are less aligned with the social science field’s evaluative criteria, which privilege originality and conceptual insight. Similar patterns have been noted in research observing that GenAI’s associations with performance weaken as cog­ nitive complexity increases [24, 41]. These findings are consistent with the theoretical expectation that capital retains legitimacy only within contexts that recognize its value: technological proficiency without conceptual engagement does not easily convert into academic achievement where disciplinary legitimacy relies on interpretive reasoning. Beyond differences in cognitive demand, alternative explanations may also inform these associations. One possibility—although not measured in this study—is that the positive relationship for lower‑order tasks reflects differences in students’ baseline aca­ demic ability or digital literacy. Higher‑performing students may possess stronger aca­ demic discipline, more deliberate study strategies, or greater metacognitive awareness, which make their use of GenAI strategic rather than compensatory [22, 31]. Conversely, students with weaker academic foundations may rely on GenAI more heavily but less effectively, using it as a substitute for understanding rather than as an instrument of reinforcement. In this sense, the results may reflect both the enabling and",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,20,"Conversely, students with weaker academic foundations may rely on GenAI more heavily but less effectively, using it as a substitute for understanding rather than as an instrument of reinforcement. In this sense, the results may reflect both the enabling and stratifying functions of technological capital, as described by Bekirsky [8]: those who already hold cultural or digital advantages are better equipped to convert GenAI proficiency into tan­ gible academic gains. The positive association observed between openness toward GenAI and GPA also complements these findings. Openness suggests a learning orientation marked by con­ fidence with technology and readiness to experiment with new forms of support [5, 44]. Students who view GenAI as a legitimate academic resource appear more inclined to blend its use with self-regulation and critical reflection. This tendency aligns with Sel­ wyn’s [33] notion that technological capital includes not only technical skill but also attitudes that guide how technology is incorporated into learning. Findings reported by Alotaibi [2] and Deng et al. [17] identify similar associations, showing that constructive engagement with AI often coincides with stronger study organization and",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,20,"only technical skill but also attitudes that guide how technology is incorporated into learning. Findings reported by Alotaibi [2] and Deng et al. [17] identify similar associations, showing that constructive engagement with AI often coincides with stronger study organization and more reflec­ tive learning behaviors that could lead to better academic performance outcomes. Overall, these results correspond with wider research indicating that the relationship between GenAI use and academic performance is associative and shaped by context. GenAI appears to be linked with gains in procedural and mechanical aspects of course­ work when used within structured boundaries, while showing limited association with outcomes that depend on conceptual exploration or disciplinary creativity. This pattern",112
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,21,"Page 21 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 supports the interpretation of GenAI as a form of technological capital whose relevance is conditional on the cognitive and institutional settings in which it is applied. 5.2 Implications The associations identified in this study provide several implications for educators, insti­ tutions, and policymakers seeking to integrate generative AI (GenAI) within higher edu­ cation in a thoughtful and evidence‑based manner. The positive association between GenAI use for lower‑order tasks and students’ academic performance indicates that when applied to activities such as information retrieval, language correction, and text summarization, these tools can complement learning by improving efficiency and pro­ cedural accuracy. In practice, incorporating GenAI as a formative aid—especially during the early stages of coursework or within academic writing instruction—may help stu­ dents complete routine tasks more effectively while allowing greater attention to con­ ceptual understanding and analytical engagement. For teaching practice, the results suggest that effective use of GenAI depends on how educators guide students to work with it. Rather than framing AI as something to permit or prohibit, instructors",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,21,"attention to con­ ceptual understanding and analytical engagement. For teaching practice, the results suggest that effective use of GenAI depends on how educators guide students to work with it. Rather than framing AI as something to permit or prohibit, instructors might integrate short reflective components into assignments that prompt students to explain how they used GenAI and to assess its usefulness in their learning process. Such exer­ cises can promote awareness of academic integrity, foster digital literacy, and encour­ age critical engagement with technology as part of the learning process rather than as a separate technical skill. At the institutional level, the results point to the value of developing discipline‑specific GenAI literacy frameworks. In subjects such as the social sciences—where interpreta­ tion, reflexivity, and theoretical reasoning are central—GenAI should be introduced as an aid that supports clarity and organization while leaving analytical judgment to the learner. Universities could expand professional development programs that equip faculty to demonstrate responsible use of GenAI, formulate clear course‑based guidance, and share teaching resources that illustrate effective practice. Collaborative initiatives such as departmental workshops, faculty–student",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,21,"leaving analytical judgment to the learner. Universities could expand professional development programs that equip faculty to demonstrate responsible use of GenAI, formulate clear course‑based guidance, and share teaching resources that illustrate effective practice. Collaborative initiatives such as departmental workshops, faculty–student projects, or curated repositories of instruc­ tional examples could strengthen institutional capacity and consistency. Likewise, at a policy level, the varied associations across cognitive tasks highlight the need for gover­ nance that reflects disciplinary differences. A uniform regulatory approach would over­ look the diverse ways that GenAI interacts with learning. Policies grounded in empirical evidence can help ensure equitable access to GenAI tools, consistent ethical standards, and appropriate training for students, while upholding academic integrity and support­ ing the cultivation of higher‑order reasoning. Policymakers might consider mechanisms such as integrating AI literacy into curricula, establishing ethical‑use guidelines, and conducting periodic reviews of new technologies as part of existing quality‑assurance processes. Overall, these implications emphasize that the educational value of GenAI depends on the context in which it is used. When institutions cultivate conditions that align techno­ logical adoption with pedagogical",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,21,"reviews of new technologies as part of existing quality‑assurance processes. Overall, these implications emphasize that the educational value of GenAI depends on the context in which it is used. When institutions cultivate conditions that align techno­ logical adoption with pedagogical purpose and ethical integrity, GenAI can contribute to improved learning efficiency and procedural competence. In the absence of such align­ ment, benefits are likely to be limited or uneven. Framing GenAI as a form of technolog­ ical capital within a broader culture of inquiry and disciplinary relevance may therefore",89
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,22,"Page 22 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 ensure its contribution to the analytical rigor and intellectual independence that remain central to higher education. 5.3 Limitations and future research directions While this study provides valuable insights into the relationship between generative AI tool use and social‑science students’ academic performance, several limitations should be acknowledged. First, the analysis is associative in nature and based on cross‑sectional data, which precludes any inference of causality between GenAI use and academic outcomes. The observed relationships capture correlations at a single point in time rather than direc­ tional or developmental effects. It is therefore possible that students with higher GPAs already possess stronger study habits, metacognitive awareness, or access to academic resources that shape both their learning performance and their strategic use of GenAI tools. In this sense, the results may partly reflect a selection effect, whereby academi­ cally stronger students employ GenAI more deliberately and effectively, while lower‑per­ forming students may rely on it more indiscriminately. Future research should examine possible reverse‑causality dynamics between academic performance and GenAI adop­ tion—for instance,",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,22,"reflect a selection effect, whereby academi­ cally stronger students employ GenAI more deliberately and effectively, while lower‑per­ forming students may rely on it more indiscriminately. Future research should examine possible reverse‑causality dynamics between academic performance and GenAI adop­ tion—for instance, whether high‑achieving students use AI selectively to sustain their success. Longitudinal or mixed‑methods designs, combining quantitative analyses with qualitative data on students’ motivations and learning behaviors, could clarify whether GenAI usage patterns are associated with subsequent academic outcomes or merely coincide with pre‑existing performance differences. Second, this research is situated in the specific context of Hong Kong, where access to GenAI tools is widespread and universities are actively integrating them into teach­ ing and assessment. The findings may therefore be context‑dependent, reflecting Hong Kong’s advanced digital infrastructure, strong institutional support, and academi­ cally competitive culture. These contextual factors could shape both the extent and the purposes of GenAI adoption in ways that may not be directly comparable to other higher‑education systems. Future studies could employ comparative designs across regions, institutional types, and disciplines to examine how diverse educational envi­ ronments",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,22,"both the extent and the purposes of GenAI adoption in ways that may not be directly comparable to other higher‑education systems. Future studies could employ comparative designs across regions, institutional types, and disciplines to examine how diverse educational envi­ ronments mediate the observed associations between GenAI engagement and academic performance. Third, the study relies on self‑reported survey data, which are inherently subject to recall and social‑desirability biases in how respondents describe the frequency and pur­ pose of their GenAI use. Although the sample adequately represents the Faculty of Social Science at CUHK, response bias cannot be fully ruled out: students with stronger opin­ ions about or greater familiarity with GenAI may have been more likely to participate. The operationalization of key variables also introduces certain constraints. All GenAI usage indicators were measured through single‑item self‑report questions, which—while practical—are generally less reliable than multi‑item scales and may not fully capture the nuance and diversity of students’ engagement with GenAI tools beyond frequency alone. Single‑item indicators were selected to reduce respondent burden and enable consistent comparison across the four task dimensions using",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,22,"reliable than multi‑item scales and may not fully capture the nuance and diversity of students’ engagement with GenAI tools beyond frequency alone. Single‑item indicators were selected to reduce respondent burden and enable consistent comparison across the four task dimensions using a unified scale. Given that the study sought to identify broad patterns of engagement rather than to measure underlying psy­ chological constructs, this approach was considered suitable for a cross‑sectional survey of this scope. Nonetheless, it is acknowledged that single‑item measures inherently limit",83
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,23,"Page 23 of 27 Tang et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:550 reliability and precision. Future research should therefore aim to refine these indicators through validated multi‑item scales or incorporate objective data sources—such as ano­ nymized platform usage logs or learning‑management‑system analytics—to enhance measurement validity and mitigate potential sources of bias while maintaining ethical data‑handling practices. Fourth, the measurement of academic performance through GPA intervals provides a pragmatic means of encouraging participation and reducing respondent reluctance to disclose exact scores; however, this approach may reduce precision and slightly attenu­ ate the sensitivity of regression estimates. Although categorization minimizes reporting bias, the interval averaging process could obscure small variations in GPA that might influence the estimated relationships. Future research might use exact GPA scores or institutional records, subject to consent and privacy considerations, to enhance the robustness of results and assess whether similar associations hold with more precise measures of academic performance. Finally, the models cannot fully account for unobserved factors that may influence both GenAI usage and GPA, such as differences in assessment methods (e.g., exami­ nations versus essay‑based evaluations), perceived",180
DOC_066,s44217-026-01486-x.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01486-x,23,"similar associations hold with more precise measures of academic performance. Finally, the models cannot fully account for unobserved factors that may influence both GenAI usage and GPA, such as differences in assessment methods (e.g., exami­ nations versus essay‑based evaluations), perceived grading stringency across courses, variation in instructor feedback practices, or disparities in access to academic support resources. These elements could contribute to residual heterogeneity in the observed associations. Future studies could incorporate course‑level or instructor‑level variables to help disentangle these contextual influences and evaluate their role in shaping pat­ terns of GenAI engagement and academic performance. Despite these limitations, the study provides an important empirical foundation for understanding how different forms of GenAI use are associated—rather than causally linked—with variation in stu­ dent achievement. Continued research that refines measurement, incorporates longitu­ dinal perspectives, and examines contextual and institutional dynamics will be essential for deepening understanding of GenAI’s evolving role in higher education.",152
