DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,1,"Discover Education Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 https://doi.org/10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2 ChatGPT use in higher education writing and its relationship with critical thinking autonomy struggle and authorship Andi Hamsiah1*, Andi Rizal1 and Agung Rinaldy Malik2 ​*​C​o​r​r​e​s​p​o​n​d​e​n​c​e​:​ Andi Hamsiah andi.hamsiah@universitasbosowa. ac.id 1Faculty of Education and Literature, Universitas Bosowa, Urip Sumoharjo Street, Makassar, Indonesia 2Faculty of Language and Literature, Universitas Negeri Makassar, Daeng Tata Raya Street, Makassar, Indonesia © The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,1,"from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n​s​ e​s​/​b​y​-​n​c​-​n​d​/​4​.​0​/​.​ Abstract Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is increasingly shaping higher education, particularly in academic writing and learning efficiency. Yet empirical evidence on its effects on students’ critical thinking, learning agency, and authorship perceptions remains limited. This study examines how ChatGPT influences undergraduate academic writing, with particular attention to critical thinking dispositions, including epistemic autonomy, reflective judgment, persistence in problem solving, and responsibility for authorship. Using a convergent mixed-methods design, the study integrated survey data from 270 students across two Indonesian universities with semi-structured interviews involving 15 purposively selected participants. Quantitative data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, reliability testing, independent-samples",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,1,"solving, and responsibility for authorship. Using a convergent mixed-methods design, the study integrated survey data from 270 students across two Indonesian universities with semi-structured interviews involving 15 purposively selected participants. Quantitative data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, reliability testing, independent-samples t-tests, and Pearson correlation analysis, while qualitative interview data were examined through thematic analysis. Quantitative findings showed generally positive perceptions of GenAI literacy and digital readiness, with students reporting particularly favorable views of efficiency, perceived writing quality, academic confidence, and learning enjoyment. Cognitive engagement was positive but less pronounced, whereas behavioral engagement, especially in collaborative activities, was less evident. Despite increased confidence, Students also expressed concerns about originality, authorship, and potential over-reliance on AI. Qualitative findings revealed three recurring mechanisms: creeping dependency, erosion of cognitive struggle, and shifting notions of authorship. These findings informed a three-part model of AI-mediated learning consisting of acceleration, attenuation, and ambivalence. Overall, GenAI enhances task efficiency while reshaping critical thinking dispositions. Limitations include the focus on two universities and a small interview sample, which may affect generalizability. Future research should examine diverse contexts and longitudinal",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,1,"consisting of acceleration, attenuation, and ambivalence. Overall, GenAI enhances task efficiency while reshaping critical thinking dispositions. Limitations include the focus on two universities and a small interview sample, which may affect generalizability. Future research should examine diverse contexts and longitudinal impact of AI on higher-order learning. Keywords Generative AI, ChatGPT, Academic writing, Critical thinking disposition, Student engagement, Authorship",58
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,2,"Page 2 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 ​1 Introduction The rapid emergence of ChatGPT and other generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools since late 2022 has substantially transformed higher education worldwide. Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into students’ academic practices, supporting activities such as literature searching, idea generation, and the drafting of academic texts. Recent studies indicate that more than 60% of university students glob­ ally have experimented with GenAI for academic purposes, either openly or discreetly [4, 36, 66]. This widespread uptake represents not merely a technological development, but a structural shift in academic writing practices, whereby processes traditionally characterized by individual cognitive effort are increasingly mediated through human– AI interaction. Concurrently, scholarly discourse has emphasized critical thinking as a central compe­ tence in AI-mediated learning environments. Academic writing is no longer conducted in isolation; instead, students engage with AI systems that function as tutors, assistants, or quasi-interlocutors [9, 30, 43]. While such interactions may broaden perspectives and support reflective engagement, concerns have been raised regarding the potential erosion of students’ critical thinking dispositions,",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,2,"isolation; instead, students engage with AI systems that function as tutors, assistants, or quasi-interlocutors [9, 30, 43]. While such interactions may broaden perspectives and support reflective engagement, concerns have been raised regarding the potential erosion of students’ critical thinking dispositions, defined as enduring tendencies to question assumptions, evaluate evidence, tolerate ambiguity, and take responsibility for knowledge construction. Empirical studies have identified several benefits associated with GenAI use in aca­ demic contexts. Students report gains in efficiency, reductions in perceived cognitive effort, and increased confidence in writing tasks [41, 52]. For second-language learners in particular, AI-assisted support for grammar, vocabulary, and textual coherence has been shown to alleviate linguistic barriers and enhance academic self-efficacy [27, 56]. From a pedagogical perspective, GenAI provides continuous and individualized scaffold­ ing that may complement or, in some cases, substitute for traditional forms of feedback. However, these affordances are accompanied by significant risks. Researchers have cautioned against over-reliance and cognitive offloading, whereby learners increas­ ingly delegate intellectual effort to AI systems, potentially diminishing their capacity for independent reasoning, argument construction, and reflection [61]. Ongoing con­ cerns",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,2,"these affordances are accompanied by significant risks. Researchers have cautioned against over-reliance and cognitive offloading, whereby learners increas­ ingly delegate intellectual effort to AI systems, potentially diminishing their capacity for independent reasoning, argument construction, and reflection [61]. Ongoing con­ cerns related to plagiarism, algorithmic bias, and hallucinated content further compli­ cate issues of academic integrity and epistemic reliability [55]. Recent work grounded in cognitive load theory suggests that while generative AI may effectively reduce extrane­ ous cognitive load by offloading surface-level linguistic and organizational demands, it may simultaneously constrain germane cognitive load, which is critical for deep learn­ ing, knowledge construction, and the development of higher-order thinking skills [18]. Experimental and neurocognitive studies further indicate that sustained reliance on AI-assisted writing can lead to cognitive offloading effects, whereby learners engage less in effortful reasoning and memory consolidation processes that underpin durable learning [32]. Similarly, Niloy et al. [40] demonstrate that although AI-supported writ­ ing enhances task efficiency, it may attenuate reflective engagement and analytical depth when students interact with AI outputs uncritically. This tension highlights a fundamen­ tal dilemma: GenAI",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,2,"durable learning [32]. Similarly, Niloy et al. [40] demonstrate that although AI-supported writ­ ing enhances task efficiency, it may attenuate reflective engagement and analytical depth when students interact with AI outputs uncritically. This tension highlights a fundamen­ tal dilemma: GenAI can operate both as a cognitive scaffold that supports learning and as a cognitive substitute that constrains it. Despite the growing body of research on GenAI in higher education, an impor­ tant gap remains. Much of the existing literature prioritizes performance-oriented",81
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,3,"Page 3 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 outcomes—such as writing fluency, speed, and surface-level quality—while offering lim­ ited empirical insight into how GenAI influences students’ critical thinking dispositions during the writing process itself [51]. This omission is consequential, as dispositional orientations shape whether students engage with AI in reflective and strategic ways or rely on it uncritically as a replacement for their own reasoning. In Asian higher education contexts, including Indonesia, Thailand, and China, these challenges are further intensified by digital divides, uneven levels of AI literacy, and assessment cultures that continue to prioritize product over process [23, 37]. A growing body of research shows that although students increasingly use GenAI tools for language support, idea generation, and writing efficiency, their engagement often remains more instrumental than critically reflective [12, 34, 39]. Other studies further suggest that AI literacy contributes meaningfully to learning only when accompanied by critical think­ ing and evaluative control [46, 63]. At the same time, the potential of GenAI to support critical thinking depends strongly on pedagogical guidance that encourages students to",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,3,"that AI literacy contributes meaningfully to learning only when accompanied by critical think­ ing and evaluative control [46, 63]. At the same time, the potential of GenAI to support critical thinking depends strongly on pedagogical guidance that encourages students to question, verify, and regulate AI-generated outputs rather than simply use them to com­ plete tasks [38] . Taken together, these studies suggest that GenAI use in Asian higher education is expanding rapidly, but not always alongside adequate pedagogical and insti­ tutional readiness. As a result, students may gain practical benefits from AI without fully developing the reflective, self-regulatory, and authorship-aware dimensions needed for higher-order learning. Addressing these gaps, the present study investigates how generative artificial intel­ ligence is understood, practiced, and integrated into academic writing in higher edu­ cation, with particular emphasis on students’ critical thinking dispositions. Employing a convergent mixed-methods design, the study combines survey and interview data to examine students’ GenAI literacy, digital readiness, engagement, academic confidence, and experiences of dependency, cognitive struggle, and authorship. Critical thinking is conceptualized not as a generic skill, but as a disposition",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,3,"mixed-methods design, the study combines survey and interview data to examine students’ GenAI literacy, digital readiness, engagement, academic confidence, and experiences of dependency, cognitive struggle, and authorship. Critical thinking is conceptualized not as a generic skill, but as a disposition enacted through writing practices within AI-mediated learning environments. This study contributes to the lit­ erature by foregrounding critical thinking dispositions in AI-mediated writing, integrat­ ing quantitative and qualitative evidence, and providing context-sensitive insights from Indonesian higher education. More specifically, the study examines how efficiency, ethical awareness, and author­ ship practices are shaped by students’ self-regulated learning strategies, instructional design, and institutional policies. The inquiry is guided by two research questions: (1) How does the use of ChatGPT support students’ academic writing processes while influ­ encing their critical thinking dispositions? and (2) How do students’ experiences with generative AI shape their academic autonomy, cognitive struggle, and perceptions of authorship in higher education? By integrating quantitative trends with qualitative insights, this study contributes to ongoing debates on generative AI, academic writing, and critical thinking in higher education. Rather than adopting a deterministic",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,3,"academic autonomy, cognitive struggle, and perceptions of authorship in higher education? By integrating quantitative trends with qualitative insights, this study contributes to ongoing debates on generative AI, academic writing, and critical thinking in higher education. Rather than adopting a deterministic stance, the study seeks to clarify the conditions under which GenAI use supports or undermines the cultivation of critical thinking, thereby informing pedagogical practice, assessment design, and institutional policy.",69
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,4,"Page 4 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 2 Literature review 2.1 Generative AI and the transformation of academic writing The emergence of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), particularly ChatGPT, has redefined academic writing practices in higher education. Unlike traditional digital tools—such as grammar checkers, spell checkers, predictive dictionaries, and speech-to- text systems—that primarily supported surface-level linguistic accuracy, GenAI operates at epistemic and rhetorical levels by generating ideas, organizing arguments, and simu­ lating dialogic interaction, thereby positioning itself as an active participant in academic knowledge construction [14]. Empirical studies indicate that students increasingly per­ ceive ChatGPT as a multi-functional assistant capable of brainstorming, revising drafts, and refining language use, particularly in second-language contexts where linguistic barriers are pronounced [26, 58]. These affordances suggest a qualitative shift in writing practices—from an individual cognitive activity toward a hybrid human–AI collabora­ tive process. Beyond text production, GenAI has increasingly been described as a virtual tutor or digital peer. Syahid et al. [49] found that students frequently engage with ChatGPT con­ versationally, using it to test ideas, receive immediate feedback, and explore",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,4,"tive process. Beyond text production, GenAI has increasingly been described as a virtual tutor or digital peer. Syahid et al. [49] found that students frequently engage with ChatGPT con­ versationally, using it to test ideas, receive immediate feedback, and explore alternative perspectives. This interactional mode differentiates GenAI from earlier writing technol­ ogies by embedding AI within the cognitive and metacognitive dimensions of writing, rather than limiting its function to post hoc correction. Such developments align with broader pedagogical shifts in higher education toward process-oriented writing instruc­ tion, where drafting, revising, and reflecting are emphasized over final products [20, 42]. However, the increasing integration of AI into meaning-making processes raises critical questions regarding authorship, originality, and intellectual ownership. Despite the rapid expansion of research on GenAI-assisted writing, much of the lit­ erature remains outcome-oriented, focusing on measurable improvements in grammar, lexical variation, fluency, and coherence [10]. Relatively few studies interrogate the cog­ nitive and dispositional dimensions of writing, particularly whether sustained GenAI use cultivates or constrains students’ critical thinking dispositions. This gap is consequential: the production of polished academic texts does",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,4,"variation, fluency, and coherence [10]. Relatively few studies interrogate the cog­ nitive and dispositional dimensions of writing, particularly whether sustained GenAI use cultivates or constrains students’ critical thinking dispositions. This gap is consequential: the production of polished academic texts does not necessarily indicate the development of independent reasoning, evaluative judgment, or reflective skepticism, which are cen­ tral to higher education learning outcomes. 2.2 Benefits and risks of ChatGPT in higher education The benefits of ChatGPT in higher education are well documented across recent empiri­ cal and pedagogical studies. Rather than constituting a direct or automatic reduction of cognitive load, ChatGPT is more accurately understood as reshaping task demands by supporting lower-level linguistic and procedural processes, such as lexical retrieval, sentence formulation, and preliminary structuring of ideas. Through this reallocation of effort, students may experience a reduction in perceived cognitive burden, which can potentially allow greater attentional capacity for higher-order activities, including syn­ thesis, integration of sources, and evaluative reasoning [44]. Research further suggests that AI-enhanced writing support can increase academic confidence and self-efficacy, particularly among multilingual students and those with",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,4,"which can potentially allow greater attentional capacity for higher-order activities, including syn­ thesis, integration of sources, and evaluative reasoning [44]. Research further suggests that AI-enhanced writing support can increase academic confidence and self-efficacy, particularly among multilingual students and those with limited prior writing experience [1]. For many learners, ChatGPT functions as a form of immediate scaffolding, assist­ ing with idea clarification, argument structuring, and lexical choice, thereby supporting",68
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,5,"Page 5 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 iterative revision processes [13]. From an instructional perspective, AI-mediated feed­ back may complement traditional feedback mechanisms by accelerating revision cycles and increasing opportunities for formative learning [60]. However, recent cognitive and educational research urges caution in interpreting cog­ nitive load reduction as inherently beneficial. Drawing on cognitive load theory, scholars distinguish between extraneous cognitive load, which interferes with learning, and ger­ mane cognitive load, which is essential for schema construction and deep understand­ ing. While ChatGPT may alleviate extraneous load, there is growing concern that it may simultaneously suppress germane cognitive load by reducing students’ engagement in productive cognitive struggle [18]. In this sense, efficiency gains may come at the cost of diminished opportunities for critical thinking development. The risks associated with over-reliance on ChatGPT are therefore substantial. Zhai et al. [61] describe a pattern of cognitive offloading in which students increasingly defer reasoning, decision-making, and evaluation to AI-generated outputs. Additional con­ cerns relate to plagiarism, ethical misuse, algorithmic bias, and hallucinated content, all of which threaten academic integrity",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,5,"et al. [61] describe a pattern of cognitive offloading in which students increasingly defer reasoning, decision-making, and evaluation to AI-generated outputs. Additional con­ cerns relate to plagiarism, ethical misuse, algorithmic bias, and hallucinated content, all of which threaten academic integrity and epistemic reliability [24]. These risks fore­ ground a central paradox of GenAI in higher education: the same features that enhance accessibility and efficiency may also undermine intellectual independence, authorship responsibility, and critical engagement. Consequently, contemporary debates increasingly revolve around whether ChatGPT should be conceptualized as an augmentative technology that amplifies human reason­ ing or as a substitutive technology that displaces it [19]. While some educators argue that critically guided AI use can foster creativity and reflective learning, others caution that unregulated adoption promotes passivity and weakens students’ sense of author­ ship and agency [16]. This unresolved tension underscores the need to examine not only writing outcomes, but also the evolution of students’ thinking dispositions in AI-medi­ ated writing environments. 2.3 Theoretical perspectives for understanding human–AI interaction Several theoretical frameworks offer insight into how ChatGPT shapes learning and thinking in",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,5,"need to examine not only writing outcomes, but also the evolution of students’ thinking dispositions in AI-medi­ ated writing environments. 2.3 Theoretical perspectives for understanding human–AI interaction Several theoretical frameworks offer insight into how ChatGPT shapes learning and thinking in higher education. Kim et al. [31] propose the concept of shared agency, sug­ gesting that AI can function as a co-participant in learning if students retain control over decision-making and evaluative judgment. Complementing this perspective, Järvelä et al. [25] emphasize human–machine synergy, arguing that while AI excels in speed, pat­ tern recognition, and information retrieval, humans contribute contextual interpreta­ tion, ethical reasoning, and value-based judgment. Together, these frameworks position AI not as a cognitive replacement, but as a conditional collaborator whose educational value depends on learner agency. The AI-Enhanced Self-Regulated Learning (AI-SRL) framework further conceptual­ izes GenAI as a metacognitive support that assists learners in planning, monitoring, and reflecting on their work [57]. However, this framework also cautions that AI-supported regulation strengthens learning only when students actively engage in goal-setting and self-monitoring; otherwise, excessive reliance on AI may weaken self-regulatory",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,5,"support that assists learners in planning, monitoring, and reflecting on their work [57]. However, this framework also cautions that AI-supported regulation strengthens learning only when students actively engage in goal-setting and self-monitoring; otherwise, excessive reliance on AI may weaken self-regulatory capac­ ity. Finally, the critical thinking disposition framework provides a lens for evaluating deeper learning outcomes, focusing on whether learners develop curiosity, truth-seek­ ing, open-mindedness, and reflective skepticism in AI-mediated contexts [50].",72
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,6,"Page 6 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 Integrating these theoretical perspectives enables a more nuanced understanding of the dual role of GenAI in higher education. GenAI can either support or erode criti­ cal thinking dispositions depending on how learners regulate their engagement and how instructional contexts frame AI use. This synthesis highlights the need for empiri­ cal research that examines the balance between support and dependency, particularly in non-Western contexts where GenAI adoption intersects with issues of digital readiness, institutional guidance, and educational equity. 3 Method 3.1 Research design This study adopted a mixed-methods design, integrating quantitative and qualitative strands to obtain both generalizable patterns and in-depth insights regarding the influ­ ence of ChatGPT on students’ critical thinking dispositions in academic writing (Hirose and Creswell, [21]). The quantitative component employed a cross-sectional survey to measure students’ generative AI literacy, digital readiness, academic engagement, and academic confidence. The qualitative component utilized semi-structured interviews to capture students’ subjective experiences, reflective strategies, and perceptions of sup­ port versus dependency in AI-assisted learning. The mixed-methods design was selected to address",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,6,"generative AI literacy, digital readiness, academic engagement, and academic confidence. The qualitative component utilized semi-structured interviews to capture students’ subjective experiences, reflective strategies, and perceptions of sup­ port versus dependency in AI-assisted learning. The mixed-methods design was selected to address the complexity of examining criti­ cal thinking dispositions, which involve both measurable tendencies and lived cognitive experiences. While quantitative data enabled the identification of overall patterns and relationships among key constructs, qualitative narratives provided explanatory depth regarding how and why students perceived ChatGPT as either a cognitive scaffold or a source of dependency. This design aligns with contemporary methodological recom­ mendations in AI-in-education research, which emphasize the need to integrate statisti­ cal trends with learner-centered perspectives when investigating human–AI interaction. 3.2 Participants The participants of this study were 270 undergraduate students enrolled in academic writing and language-related courses across two Indonesian universities with compa­ rable curricular structures and assessment practices. A purposive sampling strategy was employed to ensure inclusion of students who (1) were actively enrolled in academic writing courses, (2) had at least basic familiarity with generative AI tools",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,6,"Indonesian universities with compa­ rable curricular structures and assessment practices. A purposive sampling strategy was employed to ensure inclusion of students who (1) were actively enrolled in academic writing courses, (2) had at least basic familiarity with generative AI tools such as Chat­ GPT, and (3) voluntarily consented to participate. Demographic characteristics of the survey participants are summarized in Table 1. Participants represented a range of disciplines, including education, linguistics, engi­ neering, social sciences, business administration, and health sciences. Ages ranged from 18 to 23 years (M = 20.3, SD = 1.1), with 61.5% female and 38.5% male. For the qualita­ tive component, 15 students were purposively selected using a maximum variation sam­ pling strategy to represent diversity in gender, academic discipline, and frequency of AI use (low, moderate, and high). This criterion-based selection was intended to capture contrasting experiences of GenAI engagement and levels of reliance, thereby strength­ ening the explanatory power of the qualitative analysis. The quantitative sample size (N = 270) was sufficient to detect medium effect sizes with adequate statistical power [7] and exceeds common thresholds",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,6,"of GenAI engagement and levels of reliance, thereby strength­ ening the explanatory power of the qualitative analysis. The quantitative sample size (N = 270) was sufficient to detect medium effect sizes with adequate statistical power [7] and exceeds common thresholds for survey-based educational research [8]. For the qualitative strand, thematic saturation was reached after the thirteenth interview, with",58
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,7,"Page 7 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 no substantially new codes emerging; nevertheless, fifteen interviews were retained to ensure analytical robustness. More detailed participant characteristics are presented in Tables 1 and 2. 3.3 Instrument The quantitative strand employed a structured questionnaire consisting of four main constructs with established sub-dimensions. Generative AI Literacy (12 items) was adapted from Wang et al. [53] , comprising three subscales: knowledge and skills in using AI, ethical and critical awareness, and effectiveness of AI use in academic contexts. Digi­ tal Readiness was measured using the Digital Readiness for Academic Engagement [22], which includes five domains: Digital Tool Application (DTA), Information-Seeking Skills (ISS), Information-Sharing Behavior (ISB), Digital Media Awareness (DMA), and Digi­ tal Application Usage (DAU). Student Engagement was assessed using the framework of Fredricks et al. [15] and Apicella et al. [2], capturing three dimensions: behavioral engagement, emotional engagement, and cognitive engagement. Academic Confi­ dence, adapted from Stankov [47], covered two domains: positive emotions (enjoyment, confidence) and negative emotions (anxiety, worry) in AI-assisted writing. All items Table 1 Demographic profile of",180
