DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,8,"Page 8 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 employed a 5-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). The inclu­ sion of a neutral midpoint was intended to allow respondents to express ambivalence or uncertainty toward GenAI use, which has been identified in prior research as a mean­ ingful indicator of unresolved attitudes rather than mere response bias. Content validity was established through expert review, and a pilot study (N = 50) confirmed satisfactory reliability, with Cronbach’s alpha values exceeding 0.80 for all constructs. A summary of the constructs and sub-dimensions is presented in Table 3. 3.4 Research procedure The study was conducted over eight weeks, following a structured three-phase proce­ dure. The eight-week duration was designed to correspond with a typical academic writ­ ing cycle in higher education, allowing students sufficient time to engage meaningfully with AI-assisted writing practices. In the orientation phase (Weeks 1–2), participants were informed about the study objectives, ethical considerations, and standardized institutional guidelines for ChatGPT use in academic contexts to ensure shared under­ standing. In the quantitative",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,8,"to engage meaningfully with AI-assisted writing practices. In the orientation phase (Weeks 1–2), participants were informed about the study objectives, ethical considerations, and standardized institutional guidelines for ChatGPT use in academic contexts to ensure shared under­ standing. In the quantitative phase (Weeks 3–4), the online survey was administered to eligible participants and completed within a two-week window to capture students’ baseline perceptions and dispositions toward GenAI use.In the qualitative phase (Weeks 5–8), a purposive subsample of 15 students participated in semi-structured interviews conducted via Zoom (30–45 min). This sequencing allowed interviewees to reflect on sustained experiences with ChatGPT rather than initial or superficial use, enhancing the depth and credibility of qualitative insights. 3.5 Data analysis Data analysis followed an explanatory sequential mixed-methods approach, in which quantitative findings informed subsequent qualitative interpretation. Quantitative data were analyzed using descriptive statistics (means and standard deviations) to summarize students’ perceptions across the four constructs. Reliability was examined using Cron­ bach’s alpha, with all values exceeding 0.80. Independent-samples t-tests were conducted to explore differences across disciplinary backgrounds, and Pearson correlation analyses examined relationships among AI",180
