DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,9,"Page 9 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 interpreted as indicative of general attitudinal tendencies rather than fine-grained group distinctions. For the qualitative strand, interview transcripts were analyzed themati­ cally following Braun and Clarke’s [6] six-phase procedure. Thematic analysis focused on identifying patterns related to perceived support, cognitive struggle, dependency, and authorship awareness, which were conceptually aligned with the study’s focus on criti­ cal thinking dispositions. Credibility was enhanced through peer debriefing and member checking. Triangulation was achieved by integrating quantitative trends with qualita­ tive themes. While survey data revealed broad patterns of GenAI engagement, inter­ view narratives provided explanatory insight into why students experienced ChatGPT as either a learning scaffold or a source of creeping dependency, thereby strengthening interpretive validity. 4 Result The primary aim of this study is to examine how ChatGPT simultaneously supports and constrains students’ academic writing processes while shaping their critical thinking dis­ positions in higher education. In response to Reviewer concerns regarding clarity and focus, the findings are explicitly organized according to the research questions and ana­ lytically framed around dimensions",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,9,"constrains students’ academic writing processes while shaping their critical thinking dis­ positions in higher education. In response to Reviewer concerns regarding clarity and focus, the findings are explicitly organized according to the research questions and ana­ lytically framed around dimensions directly related to critical thinking, autonomy, and engagement. 4.1 RQ1: How does the use of ChatGPT support students’ academic writing processes while influencing their critical thinking dispositions? Four interrelated dimensions emerged from the quantitative analysis: Generative AI Literacy, Digital Readiness, Student Engagement, and Academic Confidence. Together, these dimensions represent not only students’ technical competence but also their reflective awareness, self-regulation, and affective orientations—core components of critical thinking disposition in AI-mediated academic writing. The results for each dimension are presented below, with emphasis on patterns relevant to support, reliance, and critical vigilance. Before presenting each construct in detail, it is important to note a broad response pattern across the survey. Most items showed relatively similar distributions, with responses clustering around “agree” and “strongly agree,” indicating a generally favorable orientation toward GenAI across domains. At the same time, a non-trivial minority of",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,9,"to note a broad response pattern across the survey. Most items showed relatively similar distributions, with responses clustering around “agree” and “strongly agree,” indicating a generally favorable orientation toward GenAI across domains. At the same time, a non-trivial minority of responses remained negative across items, typically ranging from approximately 8% to 15%. Rather than suggesting sharply differentiated attitudes across constructs, these pat­ terns indicate broad positive endorsement accompanied by a smaller group of students who may be more cautious, resistant, uncertain, or less well supported in AI-mediated academic writing. 4.1.1 Generative AI literacy As shown in Table 4, students reported generally positive perceptions of generative AI literacy, with mean scores ranging from 3.60 to 3.93. The responses were relatively con­ sistent across items, with most participants selecting “agree” or “strongly agree.” The highest ratings were associated with efficiency (M = 3.93) and perceived improvement in writing quality (M = 3.91), suggesting that students widely perceive ChatGPT as helpful for managing academic writing tasks. Other items related to idea generation, argument",170
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,9,"3.93) and perceived improvement in writing quality (M = 3.91), suggesting that students widely perceive ChatGPT as helpful for managing academic writing tasks. Other items related to idea generation, argument",30
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,10,"Page 10 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 structuring, and initial literature exploration were also rated positively (M = 3.79–3.83), indicating broadly favorable perceptions of AI-supported writing. At the same time, responses related to ethical caution and evaluative monitoring were somewhat less positive, although the differences were modest. For example, cau­ tion against over-reliance (M = 3.60), perceived skill in evaluating AI-generated text (M = 3.63), and awareness of bias (M = 3.67) remained within the same generally positive range. Importantly, each item also included a smaller proportion of negative responses, suggesting that not all students experienced AI support in the same way. Taken together, these findings pointless to sharply contrasting dimensions than to an overall positive orientation toward AI, accompanied by some caution, uncertainty, and uneven critical confidence. 4.1.2 Digital readiness As presented in Table 5, students demonstrated generally positive digital readiness, with mean scores ranging from 3.65 to 3.87 across the five domains. As with the other con­ structs, the response pattern was relatively homogeneous, with most students report­ ing agreement across items. Ratings were",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,10,"students demonstrated generally positive digital readiness, with mean scores ranging from 3.65 to 3.87 across the five domains. As with the other con­ structs, the response pattern was relatively homogeneous, with most students report­ ing agreement across items. Ratings were highest for digital tool application (M = 3.87), use of digital applications for collaboration (M = 3.86), and routine use of academic plat­ forms (M = 3.82–3.85), indicating that students were broadly comfortable navigating dig­ ital learning environments. Items related to source evaluation and online discussion received slightly lower mean scores, such as evaluating accuracy (M = 3.79), comparing source credibility (M = 3.75), and discussing course materials in online forums (M = 3.65). However, these differences were modest and should be interpreted cautiously. Rather than indicating a strong divide between technical and critical readiness, the findings suggest an overall positive level of digital readiness, with some areas of evaluative and collaborative engagement appearing somewhat less pronounced for a subset of students. 4.1.3 Student engagement with GenAI As shown in Table 6, students reported generally positive engagement with GenAI, with",180
