DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,13,"Page 13 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 in students’ experiences with AI-assisted writing. Most students appeared to perceive AI as helpful, while a meaningful minority also expressed unease about autonomy, authen­ ticity, and intellectual ownership. Taken together, the findings for RQ1 indicate a broadly positive orientation toward ChatGPT in academic writing, particularly in relation to efficiency, confidence, and gen­ eral engagement. At the same time, the relatively homogeneous response pattern across items suggests that contrasts among dimensions should be interpreted cautiously. A more notable finding is that, despite the overall positive trend, a smaller but consistent proportion of students reported negative responses across items. This indicates that AI- supported writing is not experienced uniformly and that some students may remain cau­ tious, disengaged, or insufficiently supported in developing reflective and collaborative forms of AI use. 4.2 RQ2: How do students’ experiences with generative AI shape their academic autonomy, cognitive struggle, and perceptions of authorship in higher education? Beyond the survey results, interview data revealed underlying mechanisms through which students experience generative AI in academic writing contexts.",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,13,"RQ2: How do students’ experiences with generative AI shape their academic autonomy, cognitive struggle, and perceptions of authorship in higher education? Beyond the survey results, interview data revealed underlying mechanisms through which students experience generative AI in academic writing contexts. The qualitative findings extend the quantitative patterns by explicating how engagement with ChatGPT reshapes students’ critical thinking dispositions, particularly in relation to academic autonomy, engagement in cognitive struggle, and perceptions of authorship. Rather than merely documenting frequency of use, these findings illustrate how critical thinking pro­ cesses are reorganized within AI-mediated learning practices. 4.2.1 Invisible dependency Students across disciplines consistently reported that ChatGPT, initially adopted as a supplementary support tool, gradually evolved into a default starting point for academic writing tasks. This transition often occurred implicitly rather than through conscious strategic choice, indicating the emergence of an invisible form of dependency that subtly constrains independent reasoning. As reliance increased, students’ autonomy in initiat­ ing ideas and framing academic problems became progressively diminished. A prominent pattern involved changes in the sequencing of cognitive activity. Sev­ eral participants indicated that ChatGPT was",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,13,"dependency that subtly constrains independent reasoning. As reliance increased, students’ autonomy in initiat­ ing ideas and framing academic problems became progressively diminished. A prominent pattern involved changes in the sequencing of cognitive activity. Sev­ eral participants indicated that ChatGPT was consulted prior to thoroughly reading or interpreting assignment instructions. This finding suggests a displacement of early-stage critical thinking processes—such as task interpretation, analytical framing, and goal for­ mulation—by AI-generated structures. Instead of engaging in initial cognitive struggle, students allowed AI outputs to guide the direction of their thinking from the outset. Participants also expressed ambivalence toward this dependency. While ChatGPT was perceived as enhancing efficiency and reducing academic anxiety, students simultane­ ously acknowledged concerns regarding declining initiative, weakened evaluative judg­ ment, and reduced confidence in generating original arguments. This tension reflects a critical shift in thinking dispositions, wherein supportive scaffolding risks transforming into cognitive substitution, thereby limiting opportunities for reflective and independent reasoning. Taken together, these responses indicate that invisible dependency functions as both an enabler and a constraint within AI-mediated learning environments. ChatGPT facilitates productivity and helps students overcome",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,13,"risks transforming into cognitive substitution, thereby limiting opportunities for reflective and independent reasoning. Taken together, these responses indicate that invisible dependency functions as both an enabler and a constraint within AI-mediated learning environments. ChatGPT facilitates productivity and helps students overcome writing barriers; however, it also",45
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,14,"Page 14 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 attenuates sustained cognitive struggle, a core condition for the development of criti­ cal thinking dispositions. By minimizing engagement with uncertainty and self-directed sense-making, such dependency reshapes how students enact critical thinking during academic writing. “At first I used ChatGPT only to correct grammar, but now I feel I can’t start an essay without it. It has become a habit I hardly notice anymore.” (P3, Social Sci­ ences). “Sometimes I open ChatGPT even before reading the instructions, just to see how it frames the task. It has turned into something automatic.” (P7, Education). “It makes me productive, but I often wonder if I could still manage without it. I rely too much on the first ideas it provides.” (P11, Education). “I save time, but I feel I’m losing the ability to start my own ideas. My thinking seems to wait for AI to move first.” (P9, Business). 4.2.2 Erosion of cognitive struggle Students across disciplines consistently indicated that the use of ChatGPT reduced the level of productive cognitive struggle typically",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,14,"to start my own ideas. My thinking seems to wait for AI to move first.” (P9, Business). 4.2.2 Erosion of cognitive struggle Students across disciplines consistently indicated that the use of ChatGPT reduced the level of productive cognitive struggle typically involved in academic writing tasks. Rather than persisting through uncertainty, ambiguity, or conceptual difficulty, students increasingly relied on AI-generated responses to resolve problems quickly. This pattern reflects a shift away from effortful cognitive engagement—an essential condition for crit­ ical thinking development—toward efficiency-oriented task completion. Several participants explicitly acknowledged that ChatGPT diminished opportunities to independently grapple with academic challenges. For instance, P5 emphasized the loss of problem-solving experiences that previously required sustained reasoning, while P10 noted that enhanced efficiency often replaced the intellectual effort needed to refine ideas. These accounts suggest that AI use alters not only learning outcomes but also the underlying dispositions that support analytical persistence and reflective judgment. Other students described changes in how they responded to confusion or difficulty. P6 reported a decline in attempting multiple strategies when encountering obstacles, opt­ ing instead for immediate AI assistance.",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,14,"the underlying dispositions that support analytical persistence and reflective judgment. Other students described changes in how they responded to confusion or difficulty. P6 reported a decline in attempting multiple strategies when encountering obstacles, opt­ ing instead for immediate AI assistance. Similarly, P14 highlighted the absence of per­ sonal growth traditionally associated with academic struggle. Together, these narratives illustrate how cognitive struggle—understood as deliberate engagement with complex­ ity, uncertainty, and self-regulation—is increasingly bypassed in AI-mediated learning contexts. This cluster of responses underscores a fundamental tension between convenience and depth of learning. While ChatGPT effectively reduces frustration and accelerates task completion, it simultaneously weakens resilience, metacognitive monitoring, and evaluative reasoning, all of which are central to critical thinking. The erosion of cogni­ tive struggle therefore represents not merely a change in learning strategy, but a recon­ figuration of how students engage in higher-order thinking processes. When struggle is prematurely resolved by AI, opportunities for hypothesis testing, error recognition, and reflective revision are substantially reduced. “When I get stuck, I just ask ChatGPT and the problem is solved. It saves time, but I",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,14,"higher-order thinking processes. When struggle is prematurely resolved by AI, opportunities for hypothesis testing, error recognition, and reflective revision are substantially reduced. “When I get stuck, I just ask ChatGPT and the problem is solved. It saves time, but I realize I lose the chance to figure things out myself.” (P5, Engineering).",52
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,15,"Page 15 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 “I don’t push myself as much as before. AI makes everything smoother, and some­ times I miss the challenge that used to force me to think harder.” (P10, Computer Science). “I used to try different approaches when I was confused, but now I just rely on Chat­ GPT. It makes me efficient, but I don’t learn as deeply.” (P6, Business Administra­ tion). “I finish assignments quickly, but I feel the struggle that made me grow is gone. It’s easier, but not always better for my learning.” (P14, Education). 4.2.3 Shifting notions of authorship The third theme emerging from the interview data concerns students’ shifting notions of authorship and intellectual ownership in AI-assisted academic writing. Participants expressed growing uncertainty regarding the extent to which their written work could still be considered genuinely their own when ChatGPT contributed substantially to idea generation, textual formulation, and structural organization. This uncertainty reflects a deeper epistemic challenge related to critical judgment, agency, and ownership of knowledge. Several students articulated concerns about blurred boundaries between",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,15,"considered genuinely their own when ChatGPT contributed substantially to idea generation, textual formulation, and structural organization. This uncertainty reflects a deeper epistemic challenge related to critical judgment, agency, and ownership of knowledge. Several students articulated concerns about blurred boundaries between personal ideas and AI-generated content. For instance, P12 questioned whether a text could still be classified as “their writing” when core arguments originated from ChatGPT. Similarly, P8 highlighted the perceived loss of originality when AI-generated texts appeared overly polished, creating a sense that the work no longer reflected the student’s authentic voice. These responses suggest that students struggle to evaluate and delimit their intellectual contribution, a core component of critical thinking in academic contexts. Other participants emphasized emotional and ethical dimensions of authorship. P4 described a sense of detachment from the final submission, indicating weakened cog­ nitive and affective ownership of the academic product. P13 expressed anxiety that extensive reliance on AI might undermine academic integrity, even when no explicit misconduct was intended. Such concerns reveal that AI-mediated writing complicates students’ ability to exercise evaluative judgment about originality, responsibility, and",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,15,"of the academic product. P13 expressed anxiety that extensive reliance on AI might undermine academic integrity, even when no explicit misconduct was intended. Such concerns reveal that AI-mediated writing complicates students’ ability to exercise evaluative judgment about originality, responsibility, and accountability for knowledge claims. Collectively, these findings demonstrate that ChatGPT reshapes not only the mechan­ ics of writing but also students’ understanding of authorship as a cognitive and ethical practice. Rather than functioning solely as a neutral writing aid, AI becomes an epistemic collaborator whose contributions challenge traditional assumptions about independent reasoning and intellectual agency. While AI use enhances efficiency and confidence, it simultaneously destabilizes students’ sense of authorship, raising critical questions about who is responsible for ideas, arguments, and interpretations. From a critical thinking perspective, this shift is particularly significant. Authorship involves reflective decision-making, justification of claims, and conscious ownership of reasoning processes. When these elements are partially delegated to AI, students’ oppor­ tunities to practice evaluative reasoning and metacognitive control are reduced. These findings therefore underscore the need for explicit pedagogical guidance on authorship, originality, and responsible AI",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,15,"ownership of reasoning processes. When these elements are partially delegated to AI, students’ oppor­ tunities to practice evaluative reasoning and metacognitive control are reduced. These findings therefore underscore the need for explicit pedagogical guidance on authorship, originality, and responsible AI use, ensuring that students retain both academic confi­ dence and intellectual independence in AI-supported learning environments.",56
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,16,"Page 16 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 “If many of the arguments in my paper come from ChatGPT, I start to question whether it is really my writing or not.” (P12, Public Administration). “Sometimes the text looks too polished after using AI, and I feel like my originality is lost in the process.” (P8, Education). “I submitted the assignment, but it didn’t fully feel like my effort. Some of the ideas were mine, but not all of them.” (P4, Computer Science). “I worry that if I rely too much on AI, my work may be seen as less authentic or lack­ ing in academic integrity.” (P13, Social Sciences). The findings indicate that the use of ChatGPT in academic writing leads to substantive shifts in students’ critical thinking dispositions rather than a straightforward improve­ ment or deterioration of cognitive skills. While students demonstrate higher efficiency, confidence, and emotional–cognitive engagement, the evidence simultaneously reveals emerging patterns of invisible dependency, diminished cognitive struggle, and blurred perceptions of authorship. These patterns suggest a reconfiguration of dispositions related to independent judgment,",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,16,"of cognitive skills. While students demonstrate higher efficiency, confidence, and emotional–cognitive engagement, the evidence simultaneously reveals emerging patterns of invisible dependency, diminished cognitive struggle, and blurred perceptions of authorship. These patterns suggest a reconfiguration of dispositions related to independent judgment, sustained reasoning, and epistemic responsibility, as AI increasingly influences how students initiate tasks, frame problems, and evaluate knowledge. Critical thinking, therefore, is not eliminated but reshaped—moving from effortful, self-regulated inquiry toward efficiency-oriented, AI-mediated engagement, underscoring the importance of examining generative AI not only as a learning aid but as a force that transforms how students think, struggle, and claim intellectual ownership. 5 Discussion This study examined how generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is understood, prac­ ticed, and integrated in higher education through an analysis of students’ literacy, digital readiness, engagement, and academic confidence, complemented by qualitative evidence of dependency, cognitive struggle, and shifting perceptions of authorship. Critical think­ ing is conceptualized in this study not as a discrete cognitive skill, but as a constellation of dispositions, including epistemic autonomy, reflective judgment, persistence in prob­ lem solving, and responsibility for authorship, as",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,16,"and shifting perceptions of authorship. Critical think­ ing is conceptualized in this study not as a discrete cognitive skill, but as a constellation of dispositions, including epistemic autonomy, reflective judgment, persistence in prob­ lem solving, and responsibility for authorship, as enacted within AI-mediated academic writing. This framing allows the discussion to move beyond descriptive accounts of technology adoption toward a more analytically grounded understanding of how GenAI reshapes higher-order learning orientations. The findings indicate a broadly positive student orientation toward GenAI, particu­ larly in relation to efficiency and perceived writing support. At the same time, the survey responses were relatively homogeneous across items, suggesting that differences among dimensions were modest rather than sharply contrasting. For this reason, the results are better interpreted as reflecting a generally favorable pattern of engagement with AI, accompanied by a smaller but consistent group of students who expressed caution, uncertainty, or discomfort. This minority pattern is important because it suggests that AI-supported writing may not be equally enabling for all learners. Previous studies like­ wise show that productivity gains associated with ChatGPT can coexist with",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,16,"students who expressed caution, uncertainty, or discomfort. This minority pattern is important because it suggests that AI-supported writing may not be equally enabling for all learners. Previous studies like­ wise show that productivity gains associated with ChatGPT can coexist with concerns about dependency, reduced autonomy, and insufficient scrutiny of outputs [45, 48]. From a self-regulated learning perspective, the present findings suggest that students are generally able to use AI productively for task support, while reflective monitoring and evaluative engagement may be less pronounced for some learners, consistent with Lee et",90
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,17,"Page 17 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 al. [33]. In this sense, the issue is not the absence of critical thinking, but the uneven way in which AI-mediated support is experienced and enacted across students. A similar pattern emerges in students’ digital readiness. While participants demon­ strate strong operational competence in using digital tools, platforms, and learning man­ agement systems, their involvement in dialogic and collaborative academic practices remains limited. This distinction underscores the gap between technical fluency and critical, interactive engagement. Drawing on Fredricks et al. [15], digital readiness that lacks interactional depth restricts opportunities for argumentation, perspective-taking, and collective meaning making, all of which are essential for developing critical think­ ing. Similarly, Kim and Kim [29] highlight that even when students can effectively use AI-based educational tools, their learning outcomes depend on guided, reflective use rather than mere operational skill. Thus, digital competence alone does not guarantee epistemically rich engagement. Engagement patterns further reinforce this interpretation. Emotional engagement, reflected in confidence, enjoyment, and motivation, was consistently rated higher than behavioral engagement, particularly in activities",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,17,"use rather than mere operational skill. Thus, digital competence alone does not guarantee epistemically rich engagement. Engagement patterns further reinforce this interpretation. Emotional engagement, reflected in confidence, enjoyment, and motivation, was consistently rated higher than behavioral engagement, particularly in activities involving discussion or collabora­ tion. Expectancy–value theory provides a useful explanatory lens, as AI use increases students’ expectations of success and the perceived utility of academic tasks, thereby enhancing emotional involvement [67]. However, in the absence of instructional designs that require students to articulate, justify, and revise their reasoning, such engagement remains largely internal and unchallenged. Evidence from Zheng et al. [65] indicates that when AI is embedded within structured collaborative tasks, behavioral engagement increases significantly, suggesting that the observed limitations are pedagogical rather than technological in nature. Academic confidence also exhibits a dual pattern. Students report heightened assur­ ance regarding writing quality and task performance, yet simultaneously express anxiety concerning originality, authorship, and intellectual ownership. This ambivalence reflects a critical thinking tension between perceived competence and epistemic responsibility. While AI reduces extraneous cognitive load and supports fluency [17], unclear",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,17,"regarding writing quality and task performance, yet simultaneously express anxiety concerning originality, authorship, and intellectual ownership. This ambivalence reflects a critical thinking tension between perceived competence and epistemic responsibility. While AI reduces extraneous cognitive load and supports fluency [17], unclear boundar­ ies of contribution may weaken students’ sense of ownership and agency [54]. Bouteraa et al. [5] similarly found that extensive AI involvement in writing can destabilize stu­ dents’ confidence in the authenticity of their academic work. These findings under­ score that critical thinking in AI-assisted writing extends beyond reasoning accuracy to include accountability for ideas and decisions. The qualitative findings provide deeper theoretical insight by revealing three interre­ lated dispositional shifts in AI-mediated writing. The first, invisible dependency, illus­ trates how AI increasingly functions as the initial cognitive reference point in academic tasks, gradually relocating the early stages of problem framing and idea generation from the learner to the tool. This shift is theoretically significant because it weakens epis­ temic agency and positions GenAI as an emerging epistemic authority, or even a surro­ gate knower, in the learning process",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,17,"problem framing and idea generation from the learner to the tool. This shift is theoretically significant because it weakens epis­ temic agency and positions GenAI as an emerging epistemic authority, or even a surro­ gate knower, in the learning process rather than merely a support tool [28]. The second, erosion of cognitive struggle, indicates that efficiency gains may come at the expense of persistence and resilience. While AI alleviates frustration and supports fluency, it may also reduce the productive difficulty and mental effort through which deeper reasoning, revision, and critical dispositions are cultivated, as suggested by recent meta-analytic and conceptual work on ChatGPT, productive failure, and desirable difficulties [11, 35,",110
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,18,"Page 18 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 62]. The third, shifting notions of authorship, reflects students’ uncertainty about intel­ lectual ownership when AI-generated text significantly shapes academic outputs. This concern is not only procedural but epistemic, because authorship in academic writing entails accountability for ideas, judgments, and knowledge claims. Recent studies fur­ ther show that without explicit critical-thinking support, students may be more likely to adopt AI-generated content directly, whereas pedagogical intervention can reduce reli­ ance and improve originality [3, 64]. These concerns also align with current scholarly and policy positions asserting that AI cannot assume authorship and must remain a tool rather than an epistemic agent [59]. Taken together, the findings support a three-part conceptualization of AI-mediated learning comprising acceleration, attenuation, and ambivalence. Acceleration refers to gains in efficiency, fluency, and confidence; attenuation denotes the weakening of epis­ temic agency, cognitive struggle, and collaborative engagement; and ambivalence cap­ tures persistent uncertainty regarding authenticity, reliability, and authorship. This framework constitutes a key contribution of the study by positioning critical thinking as a dynamic disposition shaped by",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,18,"weakening of epis­ temic agency, cognitive struggle, and collaborative engagement; and ambivalence cap­ tures persistent uncertainty regarding authenticity, reliability, and authorship. This framework constitutes a key contribution of the study by positioning critical thinking as a dynamic disposition shaped by instructional conditions, AI literacy, and pedagogical guidance, rather than as a static outcome of task performance alone. In this sense, the qualitative findings extend current debates beyond whether GenAI is simply beneficial or harmful, showing instead that its educational value depends on whether learners are supported to question, verify, and regulate AI outputs rather than merely rely on them for completion and convenience. Recent research similarly suggests that while GenAI can improve performance and motivation, deeper critical-thinking dispositions and responsible academic use remain uneven unless accompanied by explicit instructional scaffolding and ethical guidance. This study has limitations, including reliance on self-reported data from a limited insti­ tutional context and the absence of behavioral trace measures. Future research should examine longitudinal changes in critical thinking dispositions, experimentally test pedagogical interventions that reintroduce cognitive struggle, and explore equity impli­ cations related to",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,18,"self-reported data from a limited insti­ tutional context and the absence of behavioral trace measures. Future research should examine longitudinal changes in critical thinking dispositions, experimentally test pedagogical interventions that reintroduce cognitive struggle, and explore equity impli­ cations related to AI access and use. In conclusion, while generative AI enhances effi­ ciency and confidence in academic writing, it simultaneously reshapes critical thinking dispositions by influencing autonomy, struggle, and authorship. Higher education must therefore move beyond reactive regulation toward intentional pedagogical and institu­ tional designs that align AI use with epistemic responsibility and reflective judgment. When efficiency is coupled with accountability and structured reflection, generative AI can function not merely as a tool for task completion but as a catalyst for higher-order learning. 6 Conclusion This study examined how generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is understood and applied in higher education by integrating survey measures of literacy, digital readiness, engagement, and academic confidence with qualitative insights into dependency, cogni­ tive struggle, and shifting authorship. The findings reveal a paradox: while students ben­ efit from increased efficiency and writing quality, their ethical vigilance,",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,18,"integrating survey measures of literacy, digital readiness, engagement, and academic confidence with qualitative insights into dependency, cogni­ tive struggle, and shifting authorship. The findings reveal a paradox: while students ben­ efit from increased efficiency and writing quality, their ethical vigilance, perseverance, and collaborative engagement remain uneven. This “capability–vigilance gap” under­ scores the challenge of translating technical competence into reflective and responsible practice. Quantitative results indicated strong emotional and cognitive engagement but",71
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,19,"Page 19 of 22 Hamsiah et al. Discover Education (2026) 5:580 limited behavioral participation, highlighting a preference for efficiency over collabora­ tion. Qualitative analysis identified three mechanisms shaping AI-mediated learning: creeping dependency, erosion of cognitive struggle, and shifting notions of authorship. These mechanisms informed a three-part model comprising acceleration (e.g., faster task completion), attenuation (e.g., reduced cognitive effort), and ambivalence (e.g., mixed perceptions of AI reliance). The study carries practical implications for pedagogy and institutional policy. At the pedagogical level, AI integration should follow a structured sequence of human-led fore­ thought, AI-supported drafting, and reflective human auditing. Process accountability— including prompt logs, reflective authorship statements, and revision histories—should be embedded in assessment, while productive struggle and collaborative enactments are encouraged through staged tasks and AI-off periods. At the institutional level, clear poli­ cies on authorship and academic integrity, supported by faculty training and curriculum integration of AI literacy, are essential. Overall, GenAI holds promise not merely as a tool for efficiency but as a catalyst for reflective, resilient, and responsible learning. By aligning technical capability with ethical and cognitive engagement,",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,19,"faculty training and curriculum integration of AI literacy, are essential. Overall, GenAI holds promise not merely as a tool for efficiency but as a catalyst for reflective, resilient, and responsible learning. By aligning technical capability with ethical and cognitive engagement, higher education can leverage AI to foster higher-order learning outcomes. 7 Implications The findings of this study offer several practical implications for higher education. Peda­ gogically, instructors should structure AI use around human-led forethought, AI-sup­ ported drafting, and reflective auditing to ensure that efficiency gains do not undermine critical thinking and cognitive struggle. Assessment practices can incorporate process accountability, such as prompt logs, revision histories, and reflective authorship state­ ments, while staged tasks and AI-off periods help preserve productive struggle. Collab­ orative activities should be emphasized to enhance behavioral engagement and dialogic participation. Institutionally, universities should establish clear policies on authorship, academic integrity, and responsible AI use, supported by faculty training and integration of AI literacy into curricula. By aligning technical competence with ethical and cognitive engagement, AI can serve not only as a tool for efficiency but also as",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,19,"authorship, academic integrity, and responsible AI use, supported by faculty training and integration of AI literacy into curricula. By aligning technical competence with ethical and cognitive engagement, AI can serve not only as a tool for efficiency but also as a catalyst for reflec­ tive, resilient, and responsible learning, promoting higher-order outcomes across diverse academic contexts. Author contributions A.H. conceptualized the study and led the overall research design. A.R. contributed to the development of instructional and digital literacy frameworks. A.R.M. focused on the digital learning and critical thinking perspectives, and supported the analysis of academic writing practices. All authors collaboratively wrote and revised the manuscript. Funding This research received no specific grant from any funding agency in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Data availability The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Ethics Committee of Universitas Bosowa Makassar (Approval No. 235.1.03/LPPM-UNIBOS/III/2025), ensuring that all procedures adhered to established ethical standards for research involving",180
DOC_067,s44217-026-01627-2.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01627-2,19,"upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Ethics Committee of Universitas Bosowa Makassar (Approval No. 235.1.03/LPPM-UNIBOS/III/2025), ensuring that all procedures adhered to established ethical standards for research involving human participants. Consent for publication All participants consented to the publication of anonymized data and illustrative quotes from the interviews. Personal identifiers have been removed to ensure confidentiality and privacy.",70
