DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,2,"Page 2 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 ​1 Introduction 1.1 Background and rationale Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) tools can produce texts, images, and other outputs that appear human-created, and they are being increasingly integrated into workplaces and higher education. Despite decades of debate surrounding Artificial Intel­ ligence in Education (AIEd), many educators still find the effective pedagogical applica­ tion of these tools at scale to be unclear [58]. This uncertainty is contrasted by the rapid diffusion of publicly available GenAI tools, which has brought about an urgent need for institutional governance and updated curricular decisions [53]. Prior research highlights a range of opportunities of GenAI for education such as support for learning and productivity, personalized assistance, feedback provision, and more efficient completion of tasks [1, 28, 29, 37, 56, 59]. Empirical studies focusing on students show that GenAI tools are frequently used for pragmatic purposes such as writing support, idea generation, and information synthesis [11]. Despite their poten­ tial use in education, GenAI tools pose recurring challenges that may hinder adoption in authentic contexts, including transparency, readiness, and",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,2,"tools are frequently used for pragmatic purposes such as writing support, idea generation, and information synthesis [11]. Despite their poten­ tial use in education, GenAI tools pose recurring challenges that may hinder adoption in authentic contexts, including transparency, readiness, and privacy concerns. These concerns align with broader debates about the risks of AIEd and professional settings, including misinformation and hallucinations, bias, privacy and data protection issues in addition to overreliance and the potential erosion of critical thinking skills [10, 17, 18, 20, 39, 48, 49, 57]. 1.2 Conceptual foundations for GenAI preparedness The concept of AI literacy and competency have emerged as critical frameworks for understanding what students need to know and be able to do in AI-rich environments. AI literacy encompasses not only the ability to use AI tools, but also to understand how they work, critically evaluate their outputs, and engage with them in ethically informed ways [13, 36, 41]. These competencies extend beyond technical proficiency to include epistemic and ethical dimensions that are increasingly recognized as foundational for participation in both educational and professional life. Recent empirical",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,2,"outputs, and engage with them in ethically informed ways [13, 36, 41]. These competencies extend beyond technical proficiency to include epistemic and ethical dimensions that are increasingly recognized as foundational for participation in both educational and professional life. Recent empirical work further clarifies why AI literacy and competency should be treated as a multi-dimensional com­ petence rather than as simple tool familiarity. Bećirović et al. [5], for example, distin­ guish technological understanding, practical application, and critical appraisal as core AI-literacy dimensions and show that technical understanding and practical application positively influence AI self-efficacy, while practical application also supports perceived AI output quality. Similarly, Shomotova et al. [50] found a strong relationship between digital competence and AI literacy among undergraduates, with digital evaluation skills emerging as the strongest predictor. These findings support the present study’s focus on students’ perceived preparedness as responsible GenAI use appears to depend not only on frequency of use but also on evaluative, ethical, and applied competencies. Furthermore, AIEd raises broader pedagogical questions while GenAI in particular introduces acute challenges related to assessment and academic integrity. Universities",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,2,"responsible GenAI use appears to depend not only on frequency of use but also on evaluative, ethical, and applied competencies. Furthermore, AIEd raises broader pedagogical questions while GenAI in particular introduces acute challenges related to assessment and academic integrity. Universities are actively reconsidering how to define acceptable AI use and how to preserve trust­ worthy assessment in a context where students have access to advanced text-generation tools [31, 45]. At the same time, research suggests that AI can be meaningfully integrated into established pedagogical practices without replacing the human component when its use is carefully designed and pedagogically aligned [38, 43]. Emerging work on hybrid",105
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,3,"Page 3 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 intelligent feedback specifically demonstrates that GenAI and human judgment serve complementary rather than interchangeable functions in educational settings. GenAI can provide consistent, descriptive feedback at scale, while human educators contrib­ ute contextual sensitivity, problem identification, and relational guidance that automated systems cannot replicate [2, 3]. Technology acceptance research also suggests that student uptake of GenAI cannot be explained only by access to tools. Perceived usefulness, ease of use, and enjoyment significantly shape students’ intentions to use GenAI-supported learning applica­ tions, while ethical awareness of consequences negatively influences behavioral inten­ tion, which indicates that students may simultaneously recognize GenAI’s benefits and remain cautious about its academic integrity implications [30]. Relatedly, technology acceptance and learner engagement have been found to be significantly associated with critical thinking, with learner engagement mediating the relationship between technol­ ogy acceptance and critical thinking [25]. These studies reinforce the need to explore not only whether students use GenAI, but also how pedagogical design can convert accep­ tance into engaged, critical, and responsible learning. From a governance perspective, widely",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,3,"technol­ ogy acceptance and critical thinking [25]. These studies reinforce the need to explore not only whether students use GenAI, but also how pedagogical design can convert accep­ tance into engaged, critical, and responsible learning. From a governance perspective, widely used ethical frameworks also emphasize “human agency and oversight” and describe human-in-the-loop approaches as central safeguards [27]. UNESCO [53] warns that fast-moving GenAI releases risk outpacing regulation, potentially leaving educational institutions and user privacy insufficiently protected. Together, these developments underscore the need to clarify what students should learn about AI, how AI may be used during learning, and how assessment prac­ tices should be redesigned. 1.3 Dual higher education and work-integrated learning The aforementioned issues are particularly salient in German dual study programs, which combine academic study with structured workplace learning and operate at the intersection of higher education and vocational education and training [34]. From a work-integrated learning (WIL) perspective, such programs can be understood as pur­ posefully designed curricula that bridge theory and practice by embedding students within authentic workplace contexts [6, 19, 23, 24]. WIL theory holds",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,3,"vocational education and training [34]. From a work-integrated learning (WIL) perspective, such programs can be understood as pur­ posefully designed curricula that bridge theory and practice by embedding students within authentic workplace contexts [6, 19, 23, 24]. WIL theory holds that meaningful occupational preparation requires not only academic instruction but also the deliberate integration of workplace experiences into the curriculum, which is an alignment that is institutionally structured in dual study programs but remains contingent on the active reconciliation of academic and workplace demands by students themselves. The ques­ tion of how GenAI fits into this integration process is therefore not merely technological but fundamentally curricular and pedagogical. Recent WIL-oriented curriculum research reinforces this point by arguing that aca­ demic curricula need to synchronize with dynamic industry landscapes through flex­ ible course design, student and industry input, and faculty alignment with innovation ecosystems [22]. This is relevant to dual study students as their workplace experience can provide early evidence of emerging professional competence demands. In such pro­ grams, partner companies are not merely employment contexts but constitute a second learning",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,3,"with innovation ecosystems [22]. This is relevant to dual study students as their workplace experience can provide early evidence of emerging professional competence demands. In such pro­ grams, partner companies are not merely employment contexts but constitute a second learning environment in which students encounter professional tools, organizational expectations, and workplace specific norms. During their practical phases, students may also conduct workplace-based research, carry out experiments, document findings, and",69
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,4,"Page 4 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 later present the results during the theoretical phases of their studies. This makes the alignment between academic curricula and company-based practice particularly impor­ tant when emerging technologies such as GenAI enter these settings. In university continuing education, curricula are described as operating at the inter­ section of academic standards and professional applicability. Work-related learning needs should be adaptable to evolving professional demands, professional skill develop­ ment, decision making, problem solving, judgement, role performance, and teamwork [52]. Although dual higher education differs from university continuing education, this perspective is still useful because both settings require curricula to respond to chang­ ing workplace demands while maintaining academic standards. In this study, GenAI preparedness is therefore conceptualized as a work-related learning need as well as an academic competence. 1.4 Research gap and study focus Empirical evidence from Germany suggests that students already make extensive use of AI-based tools in their studies, primarily for supporting understanding and complet­ ing tasks, yet this use often occurs independently of formal curricular structures [55]. Similar patterns have been",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,4,"Empirical evidence from Germany suggests that students already make extensive use of AI-based tools in their studies, primarily for supporting understanding and complet­ ing tasks, yet this use often occurs independently of formal curricular structures [55]. Similar patterns have been reported in other dual higher education contexts, where widespread GenAI use contrasts with limited curricular anchoring of AI-related com­ petencies and uneven institutional support [35]. These findings suggest that the central challenge lies less in student readiness than in systematic curricular and institutional integration. Despite expanding debate about GenAI in higher education, there remains limited empirical evidence on how students themselves believe universities should respond, particularly with respect to curriculum integration and academic integrity policies [31]. This gap is especially important as AI literacy frameworks consistently emphasize the need for formal curricular structures that help students develop the competencies required to work critically and responsibly with GenAI rather than relying on informal, individual experimentation alone [36, 41]. Furthermore, the dual study context intro­ duces additional complexity, as the simultaneous demands of academic learning and workplace practice require students to navigate",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,4,"to work critically and responsibly with GenAI rather than relying on informal, individual experimentation alone [36, 41]. Furthermore, the dual study context intro­ duces additional complexity, as the simultaneous demands of academic learning and workplace practice require students to navigate AI-related expectations across two dis­ tinct institutional environments without necessarily receiving systematic guidance from either. Student perspectives are therefore significant because they reveal everyday use patterns, perceived preparedness for AI-supported work, and the legitimacy conditions under which institutional change is likely to be accepted. In addition, recent pedagogical models emphasize that GenAI integration changes the relations among teachers, students, tasks, and assessment. Creely and Carabott’s [14] Integrated AI-Oriented Pedagogy Model conceptualizes GenAI integration through teacher positionality, relationality, and functionality. Teachers move from sole knowl­ edge authorities toward facilitators, AI becomes part of the learning ecology rather than a neutral tool, and learning tasks and assessment can become more adaptive and feed­ back oriented. This perspective strengthens the present study’s framing of curriculum integration as a pedagogical and institutional design issue, rather than as a matter of individual student adaptation alone.",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,4,"and learning tasks and assessment can become more adaptive and feed­ back oriented. This perspective strengthens the present study’s framing of curriculum integration as a pedagogical and institutional design issue, rather than as a matter of individual student adaptation alone. In short, to strengthen the conceptual grounding of this inquiry, the study draws on three complementary perspectives. First, AI literacy frameworks conceptualize",62
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,5,"Page 5 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 preparedness as the capacity to understand AI systems, use them effectively, evalu­ ate their outputs critically, and reflect on ethical, social, and affective implications [12, 36, 41, 46]. Second, technology acceptance theory helps explain why perceived useful­ ness, ease of use, and social and professional relevance may shape students’ adoption of GenAI tools [54]. Third, WIL theory foregrounds the need to connect academic learning with workplace practices, making it especially relevant for dual higher education where students continuously move between institutional and company-based learning environ­ ments [6, 40]. Together, these perspectives frame GenAI preparedness as a combination of operational, epistemic, ethical, and professional competences. 2 Research questions This study investigates the perceived GenAI preparedness gap in dual higher education by addressing three research questions: • How do dual study students report using GenAI in academic and company-based contexts? • How do dual study students evaluate their curriculum’s preparation for responsible and workplace-relevant GenAI use? • Which competencies, instructional formats, and assessment conditions do dual study students prioritize for GenAI integration? 3",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,5,"using GenAI in academic and company-based contexts? • How do dual study students evaluate their curriculum’s preparation for responsible and workplace-relevant GenAI use? • Which competencies, instructional formats, and assessment conditions do dual study students prioritize for GenAI integration? 3 Methodology 3.1 Research design and method This study adopted a mixed-methods survey design to investigate students’ use of GenAI tools, their attitudes toward curricular integration, and their expectations regarding AI use in assessment within the German dual higher education context. A mixed-methods approach was chosen to capture both quantitative patterns in AI use and perceptions, and qualitative insights into students’ reasoning, concerns, and expectations. Combining quantitative and qualitative data enables a more comprehensive understanding of com­ plex and emerging phenomena and is particularly suitable for exploratory research in educational contexts where practices and norms are still evolving [15, 32]. The survey design was cross-sectional and descriptive, focusing on students’ self- reported experiences and views at a specific point in time. This approach is commonly used in research on technology adoption and educational innovation, especially when the aim is to map",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,5,"survey design was cross-sectional and descriptive, focusing on students’ self- reported experiences and views at a specific point in time. This approach is commonly used in research on technology adoption and educational innovation, especially when the aim is to map practices, attitudes, and perceived gaps rather than to establish causal relationships [16, 54]. It should be acknowledged, however, that cross-sectional designs capture a single point in time and therefore cannot account for developmental changes in students’ AI-related practices or attitudes. 3.2 Participants and sampling The study employed a purposive, census-style sampling strategy within a bounded institutional context. All students enrolled in four undergraduate dual study programs (mechanical engineering, business administration, energy and building technology, and laboratory and process engineering) at a German dual higher education institution were invited to participate (n= 424). The sampling frame was defined by enrollment status rather than random selection, reflecting the study’s aim to capture context-specific per­ spectives from a theoretically relevant population.",158
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,6,"Page 6 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 Purposive sampling is appropriate when research focuses on a group that is expected to have particular experiences with the phenomenon under investigation [44]. Dual study students constitute such a group because their programs explicitly integrate academic study with workplace learning and operate within multi-actor governance arrangements involving higher education institutions and partner companies [23, 40]. Their perspectives are therefore especially relevant for examining perceived curricular preparedness for AI-supported professional work. A total of 180 students completed the survey, corresponding to a response rate of approximately 42%. Participation was voluntary and responses were anonymous. While self-selection may introduce response bias, this approach is common and methodologi­ cally acceptable in survey research on emerging technologies, where interest, familiarity, and perceived relevance influence participation [16]. The achieved sample size exceeds commonly cited minimum thresholds for descriptive survey research and mixed-meth­ ods studies aimed at identifying patterns and themes rather than making population- level causal claims [32]. The sample included students from four undergraduate degree programs, with varia­ tion in gender and age groups, supporting",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,6,"for descriptive survey research and mixed-meth­ ods studies aimed at identifying patterns and themes rather than making population- level causal claims [32]. The sample included students from four undergraduate degree programs, with varia­ tion in gender and age groups, supporting analytic breadth within the institutional con­ text. Table 1 summarizes the sample characteristics. 3.3 Data collection Data were collected through an anonymous online questionnaire administered via Google Forms. The survey consisted of both closed-ended and open-ended items. Closed-ended items addressed: (i) familiarity with AI tools, (ii) frequency of AI use for study-related tasks, (iii) commonly used AI tools, (iv) perceived curricular preparedness for AI-supported work, (v) attitudes toward integrating AI into the curriculum, pre­ ferred skills, and instructional formats, and (vi) expectations regarding AI use in assess­ ment and acceptable levels of oversight. Open-ended prompts invited participants to elaborate on perceived benefits, risks, and concerns related to AI use in their studies and future professional contexts. The survey constructs were aligned with the theoretical framing. Tool familiarity and frequency of use captured technology acceptance and perceived usefulness. Curricular preparedness and",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,6,"perceived benefits, risks, and concerns related to AI use in their studies and future professional contexts. The survey constructs were aligned with the theoretical framing. Tool familiarity and frequency of use captured technology acceptance and perceived usefulness. Curricular preparedness and preferred instructional formats reflected the relationship between aca­ demic provision and work-integrated learning. Desired AI-related skills captured AI lit­ eracy as an operational, critical, ethical, and professional competence. Lastly, assessment items captured students’ expectations regarding human oversight and accountability in Table 1 Participant characteristics Variable Category n % Gender Male 122 67.8 Female 49 27.2 Non-binary/Preferred not to say 9 5.0 Degree program Mechanical engineering 62 34.4 Business administration 47 26.1 Energy and building technology 45 25.0 Laboratory and process engineering 26 14.4 Age group 17–21 125 69.4 22–26 45 25.0 27 and over 10 5.6",136
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,7,"Page 7 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 GenAI-supported evaluation. The survey was designed as an exploratory instrument for mapping students’ reported practices, perceived preparedness, and expectations regard­ ing curricular and assessment-relevant GenAI integration. To enhance clarity and accessibility, the questionnaire was provided in both German and English. Prior to data collection, the instrument was reviewed by four subject- matter specialists in language education, curriculum and instruction, and informatics education. In addition, the survey was piloted with 19 students to identify ambiguities and usability issues. Minor revisions to wording and layout were implemented based on expert and pilot feedback, following recommended practices for survey validation and refinement [16]. In terms of construct operationalization, the closed-ended items were designed to capture discrete response categories corresponding to each research question. Items addressing curricular preparedness and AI use in partner companies employed binary or limited-option response formats (e.g., yes/no/not sure) to enable clear descriptive mapping across the student population. While such formats facilitate comparability and efficient data collection, they constrain the granularity of individual responses and do not capture the nuance or",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,7,"binary or limited-option response formats (e.g., yes/no/not sure) to enable clear descriptive mapping across the student population. While such formats facilitate comparability and efficient data collection, they constrain the granularity of individual responses and do not capture the nuance or variability of students’ actual experiences. The open-ended items were therefore included to complement the structured items and allow participants to elaborate on their reasoning, concerns, and contextual consider­ ations that closed-ended formats could not adequately represent. Data collection took place over a four-week period at the end of the 2024–2025 aca­ demic year. Participants were invited via institutional e-mail. Participation was volun­ tary, and no incentives were offered. 3.4 Data analysis Closed-ended survey data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, including frequen­ cies and percentages. Descriptive analysis was considered appropriate given the explor­ atory nature of the study and its focus on mapping current practices and perceptions rather than testing causal hypotheses [21]. Unless otherwise stated, percentages for single-response items are based on respondents or valid item responses. For multiple- response items, frequencies indicate the number of selections and percentages are",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,7,"on mapping current practices and perceptions rather than testing causal hypotheses [21]. Unless otherwise stated, percentages for single-response items are based on respondents or valid item responses. For multiple- response items, frequencies indicate the number of selections and percentages are based on total selections, so categories are not mutually exclusive. Open-ended responses were analyzed using an inductive thematic analysis approach. All qualitative responses were imported into MAXQDA, which was used to support sys­ tematic coding, retrieval, and organization of the data. The analysis followed established guidelines for inductive qualitative analysis, beginning with repeated reading of the responses to achieve familiarization, followed by the generation of initial codes captur­ ing recurring ideas, concerns, and evaluations. These codes were then iteratively refined, compared, and clustered into broader themes and higher-level categories [7, 8]. To enhance the credibility and trustworthiness of the qualitative analysis, two addi­ tional validation steps were undertaken. First, the coding scheme and thematic structure were cross-checked using MAXQDA’s AI-assisted coding support, which was used as a supplementary tool to identify potential overlaps, omissions, or inconsistencies in the manually developed",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,7,"analysis, two addi­ tional validation steps were undertaken. First, the coding scheme and thematic structure were cross-checked using MAXQDA’s AI-assisted coding support, which was used as a supplementary tool to identify potential overlaps, omissions, or inconsistencies in the manually developed codes. This AI-supported step did not replace human interpretation but served as an additional layer of analytic reflection and consistency checking. Second, the coding framework and resulting themes were reviewed by an external expert, who examined the alignment between the data excerpts, codes, and higher-level",85
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,8,"Page 8 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 themes. Feedback from this expert review led to minor refinements in code definitions and theme boundaries, thereby strengthening interpretive coherence. The combination of researcher-led inductive coding, software-supported verification, and expert review aligns with recommended practices for enhancing analytic rigor in qualitative and mixed-methods research [15, 44]. Integration of quantitative and qualitative findings occurred during the presentation and interpretation of results. Rather than treating the thematic analysis as a separate layer, qualitative responses were used to conceptualize the descriptive statistics in the findings sections. This allowed students’ comments to explain why particular quantita­ tive patterns were meaningful within the dual study context. This integrative approach is consistent with mixed-methods principles emphasizing complementarity and explana­ tory value [32]. 4 Findings 4.1 Current AI use and productivity-oriented adoption Most students reported at least moderate familiarity with AI tools: 60.0% (n = 108) were somewhat familiar, 21.7% (n = 39) very familiar, 17.2% (n = 31) slightly familiar, and 1.1% (n = 2) not familiar at all. ChatGPT was the most frequently used tool by",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,8,"familiarity with AI tools: 60.0% (n = 108) were somewhat familiar, 21.7% (n = 39) very familiar, 17.2% (n = 31) slightly familiar, and 1.1% (n = 2) not familiar at all. ChatGPT was the most frequently used tool by a large margin (Table 2). In terms of frequency, 43.3% (n = 78) reported using AI tools several times a week and 17.2% (n = 31) used them daily. The remainder used AI once a week (12.2%, n = 22), sev­ eral times a month (11.7%, n = 21), or rarely (13.3%, n = 24). Only 2.2% (n = 4) reported never using AI tools. The open-ended responses help explain this high level of adoption. Students primarily described GenAI as an enabler of productivity and efficiency. Many participants empha­ sized AI’s utility in automating mundane and repetitive tasks, thereby freeing up human resources for more complex work. For example, AI is expected to take over “tedious” and “simple, repetitive tasks” such as “paperwork” allowing individuals to “focus better on important tasks and real communication.” This automation is directly linked to",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,8,"up human resources for more complex work. For example, AI is expected to take over “tedious” and “simple, repetitive tasks” such as “paperwork” allowing individuals to “focus better on important tasks and real communication.” This automation is directly linked to faster task completion, with comments such as “It can complete standardized tasks faster and with comparable quality.” Beyond mere speed, participants noted AI’s potential to improve the quality and effectiveness of work. AI “can strongly influence the quality of work as well as employee productivity” and “increase effectiveness.” One respondent noted that AI “permanently improves [their] skills, since [they] can continuously have [their] performance analyzed and evaluated by these tools.” AI’s capacity to “structure and organize many things better” and assist in tasks such as “analyzing tables” and “filling out drawings” further contrib­ utes to optimized performance. Table 2 Most frequently used AI tools AI tool f % of total selections ChatGPT 84 41.0 DeepL 41 20.0 Microsoft 19 9.3 Copilot 18 8.8 Gemini 11 5.3 Other 32 15.6 Total 205 100.0",172
