DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,9,"Page 9 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 AI was also identified as a valuable tool for fostering creativity and facilitating access to information. It “helps with idea generation” and can provide “new impulses for thought.” Furthermore, AI “provides a good overview of topics, enables fast learning, and deepens knowledge on demand.” It is “easier to generate knowledge quickly” and “to reduce the research effort” with AI tools. Regarding workplace practice, 23.3% (n = 42) reported that their partner company uses AI, 48.3% (n = 87) reported no AI use, and 28.3% (n = 51) were not sure. Students most frequently reported AI use in data analysis and reporting, customer service/chatbots, and process automation in partner companies (Table 3). 4.2 Curricular preparedness and demand for AI literacy To assess perceived curricular preparedness for workplace AI use, students were asked whether their current curriculum equips them to work with AI in their partner com­ panies. This item was answered by 118 out of 180 participants (65.6%). Of those who responded, the majority (83.9%, n = 99) reported that it does",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,9,"whether their current curriculum equips them to work with AI in their partner com­ panies. This item was answered by 118 out of 180 participants (65.6%). Of those who responded, the majority (83.9%, n = 99) reported that it does not. Selected comments are provided below to contextualize these responses: “No, the current study plan does not prepare us for the use of AI in companies. This is mainly because AI is almost never incorporated by the lecturers in the courses.” S36 “No, not at all. It is really disappointing, considering that AI will be a huge factor in the future.” S57 “Not really. AI is very powerful, but it also makes mistakes and is already being mis­ used by others today. It should definitely be taught how to deal with it, how to rec­ ognize AI, and also how to take steps to report false information to the right places.” S65 “I see a lot of potential in the use of AI in certain areas of my company, but my cur­ rent study plan hardly prepares me for it",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,9,"to take steps to report false information to the right places.” S65 “I see a lot of potential in the use of AI in certain areas of my company, but my cur­ rent study plan hardly prepares me for it at all. On my own, I have too little knowl­ edge to really integrate AI into my tasks in the company.” S94 A further 14.4% (n = 17) reported that the curriculum partially equips them for future work that may involve AI tools. These responses suggested that AI was addressed indi­ rectly or in isolated activities rather than systematically. For instance, S4 noted: “Yes and no. We are discussing the digitalization of companies, where AI naturally plays a certain role. However, AI is not explicitly mentioned.” Similarly, S21 referred to limited exposure through a single input: “Only a little through a presentation on AI in relation to academic work, which was sent by email.” Others emphasized the growing relevance of AI and the need for more structured training, as S31 explained: “I think the topic is addressed too little here.",180
