DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,10,"participant added, “AI is almost indispensable in our modern life, and in the future, more and more opportunities and challenges will arise. We, as humans, must adapt to this, and therefore I think it is very good to come into contact with AI during our university studies.” On the other hand, a small minority (1.7%, n = 2) reported that the curriculum does not prepare them; but did not view this as a problem. For example, S137 stated: “No, the use of AI in the curriculum is not necessary, since sufficient software solutions are already available for all problems.” When asked whether AI should be integrated into the curriculum, three quarters of the participants either agreed completely or supported integration in specific areas (Table 4). Similarly, most students expressed moderate to high enthusiasm about AI literacy becoming part of their education (Table 5). The most frequently selected AI-related skills students wanted in the curriculum were using AI tools to improve efficiency and productivity, critical thinking when working with AI-generated content, and basic AI literacy and understanding of ethical issues (Table",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,10,"education (Table 5). The most frequently selected AI-related skills students wanted in the curriculum were using AI tools to improve efficiency and productivity, critical thinking when working with AI-generated content, and basic AI literacy and understanding of ethical issues (Table 6). The full pattern of responses also shows interest in interpreting AI-generated data, ensuring data privacy, understanding AI’s impact on society and job market, and collaborating with AI in addition to AI programming and prompt-related skills. These priorities may mirror the open-ended responses where students called for education and clear guidelines. Respondents suggested that “basic knowledge about AI should be taught, as well as its effects on society.” This includes “workshops on handling Table 4 Student views on integrating AI into the curriculum Response option f % Yes, but only in specific areas 98 54.4 Yes, definitely 52 29.0 Not sure 20 11.0 No, it is not necessary 10 5.6 Total 180 100.0 Table 5 Student enthusiasm about AI literacy becoming part of curriculum Response option f % 1 = Not excited at all 12 6.7 2 22 12.2 3",180
