DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,11,"Page 11 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 and using AI, discussions on ethical questions,” and teaching how to “formulate prob­ lems correctly in order to explain to AI exactly what we need.” One respondent asked that “various functions of AI tools should be demonstrated so that students do not use these tools to create plagiarized work.” Another respondent added more insight “There is no overview of which AI is good for what in the study plan at the moment. We need a comparison of the strengths and weaknesses of different AIs. In addition, there is a lack of guidance on how best to use AI and how to work with it. Everyone is using AI in whatever way they think is best, without a real plan.” One respondent suggested “… less focus on ‘ChatGPT writes academic papers and invents facts’ and more focus on the application of AI as a tool for recognizing patterns for example detecting diseased cells in medical appli­ cations, image processing etc.” Students’ preferred instructional formats also indicate a desire for applied and prac­",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,11,"invents facts’ and more focus on the application of AI as a tool for recognizing patterns for example detecting diseased cells in medical appli­ cations, image processing etc.” Students’ preferred instructional formats also indicate a desire for applied and prac­ tice-oriented integration. The strongest preference was for AI-assisted projects (f = 52, 29.0%) and hands-on workshops (f = 43, 24.0%), followed by lectures and seminars (f = 31, 17.0%), online modules (f = 31, 17.0%) and industry case studies (f = 23, 13.0%). 4.3 Benefits, risks, and responsible use Students were also asked about their main concerns regarding AI use in education. The most frequently selected concerns were reduced critical thinking, overreliance on tech­ nology, and misuse, including plagiarism and cheating (Table 7). The open-ended responses also highlighted several critical concerns and the need for thoughtful, responsible integration of AI into educational and professional practices. Participants highlighted risks related to privacy, reliability, authenticity, academic integ­ rity, and over-reliance. Challenges such as “enormous privacy issues with official matters” Table 6 AI-related skills students want included in the curriculum Skill f %",180
