DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,12,"Page 12 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 and “data protection and plagiarism/data theft” were raised. The reliability of AI was questioned, with one participant noting that “faster completion of tasks leads to more mistakes” or “just incorrect even in technical issues.” The potential use of AI to generate “application letters that are no longer authentic” also underscored concerns about integrity. One respondent said, “Perhaps people will get a job because they rely only on AI. The one who actually has the knowledge may be disad­ vantaged.” Another added, “I fear that AI will be used to cheat in exams.” The concern about reduced critical thinking was also evident in comments about deskilling. One participant feared that “humans unlearn their skills because of AI tools.” There was also concern that individuals might “rely only on AI and don’t know anything themselves.” Some responses also connected AI to broader professional change. Some viewed AI primarily as a supportive tool; “AI can support someone at work but not replace them.” Others raised concerns about job displacement. Specific professions like “program­ mers,",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,12,"anything themselves.” Some responses also connected AI to broader professional change. Some viewed AI primarily as a supportive tool; “AI can support someone at work but not replace them.” Others raised concerns about job displacement. Specific professions like “program­ mers, lawyers and engineers” were mentioned as being at risk. Concerns were raised that “Human activities and professions are being fully or partly replaced by AI,” and “some jobs could be lost as a result.” One respondent said “Of course, in twenty years, I’m really concerned about the large-scale loss of jobs in many fields.” 4.4 Assessment expectations and human oversight Students reported multiple ways GenAI tools could support teaching and learning activ­ ities, such as generating post-lecture summaries (f = 124, 14.6%), delivering quizzes for feedback (f = 113, 13.3%), providing visual aids for complex topics (f = 91, 10.7%), acting as a virtual tutor (f = 71, 8.3%) and adapting materials to different levels and styles (f = 49, 5.7%). They also saw value for coursework support, including idea generation and outlin­ ing (f = 114, 13.4%), finding sources",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,12,"acting as a virtual tutor (f = 71, 8.3%) and adapting materials to different levels and styles (f = 49, 5.7%). They also saw value for coursework support, including idea generation and outlin­ ing (f = 114, 13.4%), finding sources (f = 103, 12.1%), getting feedback on writing tasks or presentations (f = 100, 11.8%), and checking for plagiarism and originality (f = 81, 9.5%). However, when asked whether they trust AI tools to help with assessment, an over­ whelming majority supported AI involvement only with human review. 76.1% (n = 137) preferred AI-assisted assessment with human oversight only, 20.0% (n = 36) preferred human-only assessment, and a mere 3.9% (n = 7) supported AI-only assessment. 5 Discussion This study examined how dual study students in a German higher education context use GenAI tools, how they evaluate curricular preparedness for AI-supported work, and how they perceive acceptable AI involvement in curriculum and assessment. By fore­ grounding student perspectives in a work-based and multi-actor governance setting, the findings extend the growing evidence base on GenAI in higher education to a context",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,12,"AI-supported work, and how they perceive acceptable AI involvement in curriculum and assessment. By fore­ grounding student perspectives in a work-based and multi-actor governance setting, the findings extend the growing evidence base on GenAI in higher education to a context where academic learning is expected to align closely with workplace practice. More spe­ cifically, the dual study setting is distinctive because students must interpret AI-related expectations across two interdependent learning environments, the university and the partner company, rather than within the university alone [40]. This alignment challenge resonates with broader discussions about integrating emerging technologies into struc­ tured learning environments, particularly in contexts where educational and workplace expectations must be systematically reconciled [6].",113
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,13,"Page 13 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 A central result is the pronounced preparedness gap, where 83.9% of the respondents reported that their curriculum does not equip them to work with AI in their partner companies. Rather than suggesting a lack of student readiness or interest, the broader response pattern may point to a structural coordination problem. GenAI capabilities and public availability have expanded quickly, while curricula and assessment regimes typically change more slowly due to governance processes, uncertainty about acceptable use, and concerns regarding data protection and accountability [53, 58]. In dual study programs, these frictions may be amplified because AI-related expectations are likely to involve higher education institutions, partner companies, and the students, whose incentives and constraints are not always aligned [19, 24]. From a WIL perspective, this suggests a perceived misalignment between institutional provisions and students’ lived workplace experiences although integration is central to effective occupational prepara­ tion [6]. The perceived gap can therefore be read not only as an AI-literacy issue but also as a curriculum design challenge. Thus, industry-responsive curricula should be contin­",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,13,"students’ lived workplace experiences although integration is central to effective occupational prepara­ tion [6]. The perceived gap can therefore be read not only as an AI-literacy issue but also as a curriculum design challenge. Thus, industry-responsive curricula should be contin­ uously updated through student, industry, and faculty input so that academic provision remains aligned with the evolving technological landscape [22]. This interpretation is reinforced by the relatively large proportion of students who were unsure whether AI is used in their partner companies. This uncertainty may indi­ cate that workplace AI adoption is uneven, opaque, or insufficiently communicated to students. In a dual study setting, this uncertainty is relevant because students’ academic preparation is expected to connect with workplace practices. This opacity is consis­ tent with Bearman and Ajjawi’s [4] characterization of AI as a “black box,” where the logic behind AI-generated outputs is not fully traceable and therefore requires users to develop evaluative judgment. This matters because curricular preparation should include not only tool use, but also the ability to ask appropriate questions about organi­ zational AI policies, data protection,",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,13,"AI-generated outputs is not fully traceable and therefore requires users to develop evaluative judgment. This matters because curricular preparation should include not only tool use, but also the ability to ask appropriate questions about organi­ zational AI policies, data protection, and permitted use. Students’ preferences help specify what they mean by preparedness. Their strongest demand concerned productivity-oriented tool use, but this was closely accompanied by calls for critical evaluation of AI outputs and foundational AI literacy and ethics. Taken together, the data suggest a multi-dimensional preparedness profile: (i) operational competence, knowing how to use tools effectively and how to integrate them into real­ istic study and work settings; (ii) epistemic competence, validating, triangulating, and appropriately qualifying AI-generated content; (iii) ethical and compliance competence, understanding privacy and data protection risks, and recognizing when tool use may be inappropriate or require additional safeguards; and (iv) professional and domain compe­ tence, applying GenAI within discipline-specific settings, standards, and accountability expectations. This competence profile aligns with conceptualizations of AI literacy that emphasize not only tool use but also critical evaluation and reflection on ethical",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,13,"safeguards; and (iv) professional and domain compe­ tence, applying GenAI within discipline-specific settings, standards, and accountability expectations. This competence profile aligns with conceptualizations of AI literacy that emphasize not only tool use but also critical evaluation and reflection on ethical and societal impli­ cations [26, 36, 41]. It also resonates with recent national-level evidence from Germany indicating that, despite high levels of digital tool use, many students report limited or unclear formal opportunities to develop AI-related competencies within their studies [9]. In this sense, students’ responses do not call for GenAI to be added as a stand-alone “tool training” module; rather, they point toward integrated learning opportunities that make verification, judgment, and responsibility explicit within disciplinary practice. The open-ended responses further suggest that students do not only want generic writing",130
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,14,"Page 14 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 support, but also guidance on selecting appropriate tools for different academic and pro­ fessional purposes and applying AI in domain-specific contexts. The findings show a high baseline of GenAI adoption and frequent use; however, students largely conceptualize GenAI as a pragmatic productivity aid that accelerates routine tasks, supports idea generation, and reduces time spent on menial tasks. This pattern is consistent with prior work that identifies productivity gains and task sup­ port as central drivers of adoption in both educational and professional contexts [11, 17, 20, 47] and with German evidence showing widespread student use that often occurs outside formal instructional designs [55]. At the societal level, rapid public awareness and generally positive evaluations of ChatGPT in Germany shortly after its release pro­ vide additional context for why such tools may have become quickly normalized among students [33]. However, students’ frequent use of GenAI should not be equated with deeper competence, learning quality, or academically valid performance without further instructional scaffolding as AI-literacy dimensions do not automatically translate into academic performance",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,14,"have become quickly normalized among students [33]. However, students’ frequent use of GenAI should not be equated with deeper competence, learning quality, or academically valid performance without further instructional scaffolding as AI-literacy dimensions do not automatically translate into academic performance [5]. Students’ most prominent concerns, such as reduced critical thinking, overreliance, and misuse, indicate that adoption does not imply uncritical acceptance. Instead, the data suggest a pragmatic stance. Students perceive immediate value in GenAI but are aware of its risks. This tension supports calls in the AIEd literature to treat GenAI inte­ gration as a pedagogical design challenge rather than a purely technological adoption issue [38, 49]. Students overwhelmingly endorsed AI involvement in assessment only with human oversight (76.1%), while 20.0% preferred human-only assessment and only 3.9% sup­ ported AI-only assessment. This preference is consistent with ethical frameworks that foreground human agency and oversight [27], with policy-oriented work showing that institutions are still negotiating appropriate responses to GenAI in relation to assess­ ment and academic integrity [31], and with recent evidence that AI-generated feedback is most pedagogically useful when paired",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,14,"human agency and oversight [27], with policy-oriented work showing that institutions are still negotiating appropriate responses to GenAI in relation to assess­ ment and academic integrity [31], and with recent evidence that AI-generated feedback is most pedagogically useful when paired with human interpretation, dialogue, and judg­ ment [2, 3]. Students generally positioned AI as a support system rather than a substitute for human judgment. Concerns about plagiarism, cheating, inaccurate outputs, authen­ ticity, and unfair advantage point to the same underlying expectation that AI may assist learning and assessment-related processes, but final evaluative responsibility should remain human. These findings also raise construct-validity questions for assessment in GenAI-rich environments. Traditional assessment methods may no longer reliably indicate indi­ vidual competence when students can easily outsource drafting, summarization, or even problem decomposition. Students’ emphasis on human oversight can therefore be read as a concern for accountability and legitimacy in evaluative judgment. For institu­ tions, this suggests shifting assessment design toward formats that make reasoning vis­ ible and attributable, for example through process documentation, explicit justification of decisions, oral defenses, or in-class components [31].",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,14,"concern for accountability and legitimacy in evaluative judgment. For institu­ tions, this suggests shifting assessment design toward formats that make reasoning vis­ ible and attributable, for example through process documentation, explicit justification of decisions, oral defenses, or in-class components [31]. Importantly, such redesign can complement detection approaches, which may be unreliable and can create adversar­ ial dynamics. It should also be noted that higher education institutions have struggled to respond coherently to GenAI, with some reverting to traditionally proctored exams rather than fundamentally rethinking assessment design [51].",87
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,15,"Page 15 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 When it comes to academic integrity, concerns about plagiarism and cheating were evident. Yet students also expressed interest in guidance, training, and clear expecta­ tions. This indicates that academic integrity in the GenAI era is not only an enforce­ ment problem, but also a curriculum-and-norms problem [45]. Clear institutional rules, discipline-appropriate examples of acceptable versus unacceptable use, and routine dis­ closure practices, such as AI-use statements, can support integrity while acknowledging that GenAI is already embedded in students’ everyday workflows. These expectations are consistent with students’ preference for structured AI education, including work­ shops, ethical discussions, and demonstrations of responsible use. For dual higher education, the broader implication is that AI-related competencies should not emerge solely from students’ individual experimentation but should be scaf­ folded through explicit curricular design, and where possible, coordinated with partner companies. Institutions need clarity on which tools are used or prohibited, which data can be processed, and which competencies are expected. Curriculum redesign should therefore include compliance-aware workplace scenarios, such as anonymization exer­ cises, safe data",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,15,"possible, coordinated with partner companies. Institutions need clarity on which tools are used or prohibited, which data can be processed, and which competencies are expected. Curriculum redesign should therefore include compliance-aware workplace scenarios, such as anonymization exer­ cises, safe data handling, and tool selection under constraints, that approximate work­ place complexity without exposing sensitive information [53]. 6 Limitations Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the findings. First, the study relies on self-reported data. It therefore captures what students say they use, value, and expect, but it does not provide behavioral or performance-based evidence. This limi­ tation is important as perceptions do not necessarily correspond to actual learning or performance and perceived learning and actual learning should not be treated as inter­ changeable [42]. Thus, future studies should combine survey data with performance- based tasks, observational data or artifact analysis to examine how students actually use GenAI and how this affects learning quality, workplace transfer, and assessment validity. Second, the sample was drawn from one institutional context and was weighted toward engineering-related programs and male participants. This may have contributed",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,15,"to examine how students actually use GenAI and how this affects learning quality, workplace transfer, and assessment validity. Second, the sample was drawn from one institutional context and was weighted toward engineering-related programs and male participants. This may have contributed to the emphasis on productivity and efficiency. Future research should examine whether students in other dual study fields, institutions, or more gender-balanced samples articu­ late different expectations regarding AI literacy, ethics, or assessment. Third, not all participants answered every item; the reported percentages therefore reflect valid responses for each item. Fourth, workplace AI adoption was measured through student reports, which capture perceived and communicated practice rather than audited organizational AI maturity. Future research should include partner com­ pany perspectives to clarify which AI tools are used, which restrictions apply, and which AI-related competencies companies expect from students. Finally, although the open-ended responses added depth, the qualitative component was limited by the survey format. Some prompts elicited relatively short or dichotomous responses, which restricts the richness of interpretation. Follow-up interviews, focus groups, or longitudinal reflective journals would allow a deeper analysis",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,15,"open-ended responses added depth, the qualitative component was limited by the survey format. Some prompts elicited relatively short or dichotomous responses, which restricts the richness of interpretation. Follow-up interviews, focus groups, or longitudinal reflective journals would allow a deeper analysis of how students negotiate AI use across academic and workplace settings. These limitations mean that the conclusions should be read as context-specific and exploratory. The study identifies a perceived preparedness gap and students’ expectations for responsible integration, but",78
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,16,"Page 16 of 18 Citil Discover Education (2026) 5:518 it does not claim to measure actual AI competence or to identify causal effects of GenAI use on learning outcomes. 7 Conclusion Overall, the findings indicate that, within this context-specific sample, dual study stu­ dents have already incorporated GenAI into their academic routines primarily as a productivity and support tool, while perceiving formal curricular preparation for responsible, workplace-relevant use as limited. The findings therefore point to a pre­ paredness gap at the interface of academic and company-based learning. Students’ responses suggest that AI literacy in dual higher education should be understood as a multidimensional competence that combines efficient tool use with critical evaluation, ethical and data protection awareness, domain-specific judgment, and the ability to reflect on appropriate human-AI collaboration. The study also shows that students are open to AI-supported learning and assessment but expect human oversight in evaluative contexts. This has implications for curriculum and assessment design. GenAI should not be left to informal individual experimentation, but should be addressed through transparent policies and assessment formats that make students’ reasoning and",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,16,"assessment but expect human oversight in evaluative contexts. This has implications for curriculum and assessment design. GenAI should not be left to informal individual experimentation, but should be addressed through transparent policies and assessment formats that make students’ reasoning and individual contribution visible. For dual higher education insti­ tutions, this requires coordinated curriculum development with partner companies so that academic learning, workplace practice, and assessment expectations remain aligned as GenAI enters professional settings. Given its reliance on self-reported data from one institution, the study should be read as exploratory. Future research should include behavioral or performance-based evi­ dence, such as task outcomes, artifact analysis, observation of AI use, or longitudinal designs, to examine how students actually use GenAI and how AI-related competencies develop over time. In the dual study context, future studies should also include lectur­ ers and partner company supervisors to clarify how AI-related expectations, restric­ tions, and competence demands are distributed across academic and workplace learning environments. Author contributions C.C. conceived and designed the study, developed the research instrument, conducted data collection, performed the quantitative and qualitative analyses,",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,16,"supervisors to clarify how AI-related expectations, restric­ tions, and competence demands are distributed across academic and workplace learning environments. Author contributions C.C. conceived and designed the study, developed the research instrument, conducted data collection, performed the quantitative and qualitative analyses, prepared the tables and figures, and wrote and revised the manuscript. Funding This research received no external funding. Data availability The datasets generated and analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate All procedures performed in this study involving human participants were conducted in accordance with the ethical standards of the relevant institutional and national research frameworks and with the 1964 Helsinki Declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. As the author’s institution does not have a formal institutional ethics committee or Institutional Review Board (IRB) for the review of this type of educational survey research, ethical review was determined in accordance with the guidelines of the German Research Foundation (DFG; Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft) regarding the requirement for an ethics application. As none of the conditions",180
DOC_068,s44217-026-01787-1.pdf,10.1007/s44217-026-01787-1,16,"(IRB) for the review of this type of educational survey research, ethical review was determined in accordance with the guidelines of the German Research Foundation (DFG; Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft) regarding the requirement for an ethics application. As none of the conditions requiring a formal ethics application applied to the present study, a formal ethics application was not required. In addition, prior to data collection, the scope of the research project and the survey instrument were submitted for institutional review and administrative approval to the Directorate of the Riesa campus of the Duale Hochschule Sachsen – The Cooperative State University of Saxony, and permission to conduct the study was granted. Participation in the study was voluntary. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to completing the survey. Participants were informed about the purpose of the study, the anonymous nature of data collection, and their right to withdraw at any time without consequences.",151
