DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,1,"will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit ​h​t​t​p​:​/​/​c​r​e​a​t​i​v​e​c​o​m​m​o​n​s​.​o​r​g​/​l​i​c​e​n​s​ e​s​/​b​y​-​n​c​-​n​d​/​4​.​0​/. Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 https://doi.org/10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5 *Correspondence: Mohammed Hamdan moh_hamdan@najah.edu 1Department of Methods of Teaching English Language, An- Najah National University, Nablus, Palestine 2Department of English Language and Literature, An-Najah National University, Nablus, Palestine 3Department of English Language Curriculum and Instruction, Al- Quds Open University, Ramallah, Palestine Qualitative insights into EFL students’ use of ChatGPT-assisted autonomous learning for enhancing self-directed learning in Palestinian higher education Aya Al-atrash1, Mohammed Hamdan2* and Khaled Dweikat3 1 Learning autonomously with AI: an overview Learner autonomy has become a central concept in contemporary educational discourse, particularly within language education, where students are increasingly encouraged to take responsibility for their learning processes. This article investigates how Palestinian Discover Global Society Abstract This qualitative thematic study examines Palestinian EFL students’ perceptions of ChatGPT-assisted learning and its role in supporting autonomous and self- directed learning in higher education. Drawing on semi-structured interviews with 30 undergraduate students from two Palestinian universities, the study explores how learners",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,1,"thematic study examines Palestinian EFL students’ perceptions of ChatGPT-assisted learning and its role in supporting autonomous and self- directed learning in higher education. Drawing on semi-structured interviews with 30 undergraduate students from two Palestinian universities, the study explores how learners engage with ChatGPT as a learning scaffold to manage academic tasks, clarify linguistic concepts, and regulate learning beyond immediate teacher supervision. The findings indicate that students perceive ChatGPT as an accessible and responsive tool that supports idea generation, comprehension, and language revision while enhancing confidence, motivation, and perceived control over learning processes. At the same time, participants expressed critical concerns regarding overreliance on AI-generated output, potential misinformation, reduced cognitive engagement, and the absence of human-mediated feedback. Importantly, students emphasized that AI-supported autonomy is most effective when complemented by pedagogical guidance, ethical awareness, and critical reflection. Situated within the resource-constrained and politically complex context of Palestinian higher education, the study argues that ChatGPT can facilitate self-directed learning not as a replacement for instruction but as a contextually adaptive support mechanism. The article concludes by highlighting the need for guided, theory-informed integration",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,1,"politically complex context of Palestinian higher education, the study argues that ChatGPT can facilitate self-directed learning not as a replacement for instruction but as a contextually adaptive support mechanism. The article concludes by highlighting the need for guided, theory-informed integration of AI tools that balances learner autonomy with instructional responsibility and proposes directions for future research on AI-assisted language learning in constrained educational environments. Keywords AI in education, ChatGPT, Autonomous learning, Self-directed learning, EFL students in Palestine",77
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,2,"Page 2 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 university students experience and negotiate autonomous and self-directed learning through the use of artificial intelligence (AI), with a particular focus on ChatGPT as an emerging educational tool. Rather than conceptualizing autonomy as an abstract peda­ gogical ideal or an individual trait, the study approaches it as a situated and negotiated practice shaped by institutional structures, technological affordances, and socio-political realities. In higher education contexts marked by political instability, infrastructural fragil­ ity, and uneven access to instructional support, which characterize many Palestinian universities, students are often compelled to seek alternative pathways to sustain aca­ demic continuity and progress. Such constraints not only disrupt conventional teach­ ing and learning practices but also amplify existing inequalities in access to educational resources, guidance, and timely feedback. Within these conditions, the demand for flex­ ible, learner-controlled tools capable of compensating for interruptions in instructional access has become increasingly pronounced. AI-driven technologies such as ChatGPT have consequently gained heightened relevance, as they offer immediate, personalized, and low-threshold forms of academic assistance that can be",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,2,"flex­ ible, learner-controlled tools capable of compensating for interruptions in instructional access has become increasingly pronounced. AI-driven technologies such as ChatGPT have consequently gained heightened relevance, as they offer immediate, personalized, and low-threshold forms of academic assistance that can be accessed independently of time and place [3, 30]. However, the growing incorporation of AI into learning environ­ ments also foregrounds a set of critical pedagogical, cognitive, and ethical questions. Scholars caution that while AI may enhance access and efficiency, it may simultaneously reconfigure learner agency, redistribute responsibility for learning, and recalibrate the professional role of teachers in ways that warrant careful scrutiny [38, 45]. Against this backdrop, the present study addresses these tensions by examining how EFL students themselves perceive both the affordances and limitations of ChatGPT, with particular attention to how such perceptions shape their autonomous and self-directed learning practices within a resource-constrained educational context. 1.1 Learner autonomy, self-directed learning, and personalized learning Learner autonomy is commonly defined as the learner’s capacity and willingness to assume responsibility for decisions concerning learning objectives, strategies, pac­ ing, and evaluation [21, 28].",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,2,"practices within a resource-constrained educational context. 1.1 Learner autonomy, self-directed learning, and personalized learning Learner autonomy is commonly defined as the learner’s capacity and willingness to assume responsibility for decisions concerning learning objectives, strategies, pac­ ing, and evaluation [21, 28]. Within EFL contexts, autonomy has been associated with enhanced motivation, strategic competence, metacognitive awareness, and sustained engagement beyond formal classroom instruction [7, 4]. Autonomous learners are not merely independent or isolated; rather, they actively regulate their learning by setting goals, monitoring progress, and critically reflecting on outcomes [31]. Closely related to autonomy is the concept of self-directed learning, which refers to the practical enact­ ment of learner agency in organizing and managing learning activities. Self-directed learning emphasizes process—how learners initiate learning, select resources, employ strategies, and evaluate progress—while autonomy foregrounds responsibility and own­ ership. Personalized learning, by contrast, focuses on the adaptation of instructional content, feedback, and pacing to individual learner needs, often facilitated by digital and AI-driven systems. Although these concepts are frequently used interchangeably in edu­ cational research, they are analytically distinct. Autonomy concerns who controls learn­ ing,",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,2,"the adaptation of instructional content, feedback, and pacing to individual learner needs, often facilitated by digital and AI-driven systems. Although these concepts are frequently used interchangeably in edu­ cational research, they are analytically distinct. Autonomy concerns who controls learn­ ing, self-direction concerns how learning is managed, and personalization concerns how learning experiences are tailored. In AI-mediated learning environments, the distinction between personalization, self- direction, and autonomy becomes analytically crucial. While systems such as ChatGPT",74
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,3,"Page 3 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 can personalize learning through adaptive responses and facilitate self-direction by pro­ viding immediate, on-demand assistance [19], autonomy is realized only when learners exercise agency, metacognitive control, and evaluative judgment over how such support is used. Personalization, therefore, operates primarily at the level of instructional deliv­ ery, whereas autonomy concerns learners’ capacity to define goals, regulate strategies, and critically assess outcomes. Recent scholarship cautions against equating algorith­ mic responsiveness with pedagogical empowerment, noting that adaptive systems may streamline learning while simultaneously reducing opportunities for cognitive struggle and epistemic uncertainty, which are essential conditions for the development of auton­ omous learners [8, 24, 35]. Without deliberate pedagogical mediation, AI-supported learning risks fostering a form of delegated cognition, in which responsibility for sense- making is subtly transferred from learner to system rather than cultivated within the learner. This tension underscores the need to conceptualize ChatGPT not as an auton­ omous learning agent, but as a contingent resource whose educational value depends on how learners are guided to interrogate, appropriate, and regulate",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,3,"rather than cultivated within the learner. This tension underscores the need to conceptualize ChatGPT not as an auton­ omous learning agent, but as a contingent resource whose educational value depends on how learners are guided to interrogate, appropriate, and regulate its contributions within broader human-centered learning ecologies. The shift from teacher-centered to learner-centered paradigms in language educa­ tion reflects a broader reorientation in educational philosophy, one that reconceptual­ izes learners as active epistemic agents rather than passive recipients of knowledge [7, 25]. Within this framework, autonomy does not imply the withdrawal of pedagogical guidance but the gradual development of learners’ capacity to make informed decisions through structured support, dialogic interaction, and reflective practice. As Benson [7] emphasizes, learner autonomy is inherently relational and socially mediated rather than an individualistic state of independence. From this perspective, the educational value of technology must be evaluated not merely in terms of efficiency or convenience, but in relation to its impact on learner agency, critical judgment, and responsibility. Educa­ tional theorists have cautioned that digital tools which prioritize task completion and speed may inadvertently",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,3,"must be evaluated not merely in terms of efficiency or convenience, but in relation to its impact on learner agency, critical judgment, and responsibility. Educa­ tional theorists have cautioned that digital tools which prioritize task completion and speed may inadvertently constrain opportunities for deliberation and self-regulation [10, 15, 36]. Conversely, when embedded within reflective pedagogical designs, technology can function as a mediational resource that supports the internalization of autonomous learning practices rather than their delegation to external systems. 1.2 Artificial intelligence and autonomous language learning Recent advances in artificial intelligence have introduced new possibilities for support­ ing autonomous and self-directed learning across educational contexts. AI-driven tools employ machine learning, natural language processing, and adaptive algorithms to gen­ erate personalized feedback, explanations, and learning pathways [17, 34]. In language education, such technologies have been associated with increased learner engagement, flexibility, and access to learning resources beyond classroom boundaries [26, 33]. Chat­ GPT, as a conversational AI system, represents a particularly significant development due to its capacity to simulate dialogic interaction. Through natural language processing, ChatGPT responds dynamically to learner input, offering",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,3,"access to learning resources beyond classroom boundaries [26, 33]. Chat­ GPT, as a conversational AI system, represents a particularly significant development due to its capacity to simulate dialogic interaction. Through natural language processing, ChatGPT responds dynamically to learner input, offering explanations, paraphrases, and examples that resemble human-like dialogue. These affordances position ChatGPT as a potentially powerful scaffold for self-directed language learning, enabling learners to seek clarification, practice language use, and explore content independently [39]. In this regard, AI-supported learning environments can enhance motivation, reduce anxiety,",85
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,4,"Page 4 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 and promote learner confidence when integrated within pedagogically informed frame­ works [27, 41, 11]. At the same time, a growing body of scholarship cautions against the uncritical nor­ malization of AI technologies in educational contexts, particularly when their peda­ gogical implications are insufficiently interrogated. Researchers have increasingly problematized the risks of cognitive outsourcing, superficial engagement, and epistemic passivity, whereby learners may come to rely on AI-generated outputs without cultivat­ ing deep conceptual understanding or transferable cognitive skills [5, 20, 46]. From this perspective, the efficiency and immediacy offered by AI tools risk reconfiguring learn­ ing as a process of optimization rather than inquiry. As Wei [44] persuasively argues, AI-mediated support can enhance motivation and productivity only when embedded within reflective, metacognitively oriented learning practices that preserve learners’ responsibility for meaning-making. Similarly, Dwivedi et al. [14] contend that genera­ tive AI should be positioned as a co-constructive pedagogical resource, not as an epis­ temic authority that substitutes learners’ judgment with algorithmic output. Extending this critique, recent educational theorists warn",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,4,"for meaning-making. Similarly, Dwivedi et al. [14] contend that genera­ tive AI should be positioned as a co-constructive pedagogical resource, not as an epis­ temic authority that substitutes learners’ judgment with algorithmic output. Extending this critique, recent educational theorists warn that when AI systems are treated as authoritative sources of knowledge, they may inadvertently erode learners’ tolerance for ambiguity, struggle, and intellectual risk-taking—conditions that are central to durable learning and autonomy [8]. These debates foreground a fundamental tension at the heart of AI-assisted learning: whether such technologies operate as cognitive scaffolds that extend learners’ capacities, or as shortcuts that bypass essential processes of delibera­ tion, critique, and self-regulation. This tension becomes particularly pronounced in dis­ cussions of learner autonomy, where independence cannot be reduced to technological self-sufficiency but must be anchored in ethical responsibility, critical judgment, and sustained reflective engagement [23]. 1.3 Pedagogical mediation and the role of the teacher The expanding integration of AI tools into educational environments has reignited long-standing debates concerning the transforming – yet not diminishing – role of the teacher in contemporary pedagogy. While AI",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,4,"[23]. 1.3 Pedagogical mediation and the role of the teacher The expanding integration of AI tools into educational environments has reignited long-standing debates concerning the transforming – yet not diminishing – role of the teacher in contemporary pedagogy. While AI systems such as ChatGPT can offer imme­ diate feedback, adaptive explanations, and individualized learning trajectories, they remain fundamentally limited in their capacity to engage with the pedagogical, ethical, and affective dimensions of teaching. Teaching, as extensive educational scholarship has demonstrated, is not reducible to information delivery or feedback provision, but involves relational judgment, contextual interpretation, and moral responsibility [9, 38]. In this regard, several studies emphasize that effective AI integration depends not on technological sophistication alone, but on teachers’ ability to function as pedagogical mediators who frame, contextualize, and critically regulate learners’ engagement with AI-generated content [22, 43]. Rather than positioning students as autonomous users of technology by default, mediated AI use foregrounds the teacher’s role in cultivating discernment, epistemic caution, and reflective awareness of the limits and assumptions embedded in algorithmic systems [42]. From this perspective, AI-supported learning is",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,4,"positioning students as autonomous users of technology by default, mediated AI use foregrounds the teacher’s role in cultivating discernment, epistemic caution, and reflective awareness of the limits and assumptions embedded in algorithmic systems [42]. From this perspective, AI-supported learning is more productively conceptualized as a hybrid pedagogical ecology in which technological tools complement, but never substi­ tute, human instruction and judgment. ChatGPT may facilitate exploratory learning by enabling learners to test ideas, revise drafts, and rehearse understanding independently",78
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,5,"Page 5 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 [16]; however, the development of higher-order thinking, contextual sensitivity, and ethical reasoning remains deeply contingent on teacher-guided dialogue and feedback. Educational theorists caution that excessive reliance on AI-driven efficiency risks reori­ enting learning toward speed, optimization, and surface performance, thereby margin­ alizing struggle, uncertainty, and interpretive complexity; elements that are central to deep learning and intellectual formation [8, 24]. Teacher mediation thus serves a cru­ cial counterbalancing function: it helps learners distinguish between assistance and dependence, encourages critical interrogation of AI outputs, and anchors autonomous learning within shared academic values and disciplinary norms. In this sense, autonomy in AI-enhanced environments is not achieved through technological self-sufficiency, but through pedagogically structured independence, where learners are supported in assuming responsibility for their learning while remaining ethically and intellectually accountable. 1.4 Autonomy and learning under Palestinian contextual constraints The pedagogical relevance of ChatGPT-assisted learning must be interpreted in rela­ tion to the distinct socio-political, institutional, and material conditions that shape Pal­ estinian higher education. Universities in Palestine operate within a landscape",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,5,"Autonomy and learning under Palestinian contextual constraints The pedagogical relevance of ChatGPT-assisted learning must be interpreted in rela­ tion to the distinct socio-political, institutional, and material conditions that shape Pal­ estinian higher education. Universities in Palestine operate within a landscape marked by prolonged political instability, movement restrictions, fragmented territoriality, and chronic resource limitations, all of which exert profound influence on teaching practices and learning opportunities. Palestinian education has consistently demonstrated that these structural conditions disrupt academic continuity, constrain institutional capacity, and render access to consistent instructional support uneven and precarious [2, 18, 40]. Within such contexts, learner autonomy cannot be understood merely as a pedagogical orientation or curricular goal; rather, it often emerges as a pragmatic mode of survival, through which students negotiate interruptions, uncertainty, and limited access to for­ mal academic support. From this perspective, AI-driven tools such as ChatGPT may function as adaptive compensatory resources, enabling learners to maintain a degree of academic continu­ ity by offering immediacy, flexibility, and individualized assistance beyond institutional timeframes and physical constraints. However, autonomy under conditions of con­ straint is neither uniform",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,5,"ChatGPT may function as adaptive compensatory resources, enabling learners to maintain a degree of academic continu­ ity by offering immediacy, flexibility, and individualized assistance beyond institutional timeframes and physical constraints. However, autonomy under conditions of con­ straint is neither uniform nor unproblematic. Palestinian scholars caution that structural inequalities—including disparities in digital access, internet reliability, and technological literacy—shape who is able to benefit from such tools and to what extent [1]. Conse­ quently, AI-supported autonomy may simultaneously enable self-directed learning for some students while deepening existing inequities for others [29]. Moreover, an overreli­ ance on technological solutions risks obscuring deeper questions of institutional respon­ sibility, educational justice, and the political conditions that necessitate autonomy in the first place. As Abu El-Haj [2] argues, educational practices in Palestine must be read not only through pedagogical lenses but also through the broader dynamics of power, restriction, and resilience that shape everyday learning. Despite a growing global literature on AI in education, empirical research that fore­ grounds students’ lived experiences of AI-assisted learning within the Palestinian con­ text remains notably scarce. Existing studies tend",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,5,"of power, restriction, and resilience that shape everyday learning. Despite a growing global literature on AI in education, empirical research that fore­ grounds students’ lived experiences of AI-assisted learning within the Palestinian con­ text remains notably scarce. Existing studies tend to universalize AI’s pedagogical potential without sufficiently attending to how autonomy is enacted, constrained, and reconfigured in politically and materially restricted environments. This study responds",65
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,6,"Page 6 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 directly to this gap by examining how Palestinian EFL students themselves perceive and utilize ChatGPT as part of their autonomous and self-directed learning practices. By centering student voices, the article offers context-sensitive insights into the pos­ sibilities of AI-supported autonomy, offering broader debates on language education, learner agency, and digital pedagogy in constrained higher education settings, while also informing pedagogical decision-making and policy deliberation within and beyond the Palestinian context. 2 Methodology This study adopts a qualitative research design to explore Palestinian EFL students’ per­ ceptions of ChatGPT-assisted learning and its role in supporting autonomous and self- directed learning in higher education. A qualitative approach is particularly employed for this investigation because it allows for an in-depth examination of students’ subjec­ tive experiences, interpretations, and meaning-making processes in relation to emerg­ ing AI technologies. Rather than measuring predefined variables through quantifiable metrics, the study prioritizes participants’ perspectives to identify patterns, tensions, and contextual influences shaping their engagement with ChatGPT as an autonomous learning resource. The qualitative design enables",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,6,"relation to emerg­ ing AI technologies. Rather than measuring predefined variables through quantifiable metrics, the study prioritizes participants’ perspectives to identify patterns, tensions, and contextual influences shaping their engagement with ChatGPT as an autonomous learning resource. The qualitative design enables the researchers to capture how stu­ dents describe their interaction with ChatGPT, how they interpret its role in manag­ ing learning tasks, and how they negotiate autonomy, dependence, and responsibility within constrained educational environments. In this sense, the study does not aim to generalize findings to all Palestinian higher education contexts but to provide a contex­ tually grounded understanding of how AI-supported learning is experienced by students within specific institutional and socio-political conditions. The study is informed by constructivist and experiential perspectives on learning, par­ ticularly the view that knowledge emerges through active engagement, reflective inquiry, and interaction with mediating tools, rather than through passive reception of informa­ tion. In drawing on experiential learning traditions associated with Dewey, the study adopts his fundamental insistence that learning is not the accumulation of ready-made knowledge but a process of meaning-making that develops",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,6,"tools, rather than through passive reception of informa­ tion. In drawing on experiential learning traditions associated with Dewey, the study adopts his fundamental insistence that learning is not the accumulation of ready-made knowledge but a process of meaning-making that develops through reflective action in response to experience [12, 13]. Crucially, however, the present research aligns with Dewey’s own constructive critique of prescriptive educational theory, which cautions against treating theoretical frameworks as fixed templates imposed upon lived experi­ ence. As Dewey [12] argues, when theory is used to predetermine educational out­ comes or to justify practices in advance, it risks distorting the very experiences it seeks to explain. Accordingly, this study does not deploy constructivist or experiential the­ ory as an a priori analytic grid for coding or categorizing students’ responses. Instead, theoretical perspectives are mobilized interpretively and retrospectively in the Discus­ sion section, where they serve to illuminate empirical patterns that emerge inductively from students’ accounts. This analytic positioning allows theory to function, in Deweyan terms, as a resource for reflective interpretation rather than as an external authority, thereby preserving",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,6,"Discus­ sion section, where they serve to illuminate empirical patterns that emerge inductively from students’ accounts. This analytic positioning allows theory to function, in Deweyan terms, as a resource for reflective interpretation rather than as an external authority, thereby preserving the openness, contingency, and contextual sensitivity that qualitative inquiry requires.",50
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,7,"Page 7 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 2.1 Study population, data collection, and research design 2.1.1 Participants and context The study involved semi-structured interviews with 30 undergraduate students enrolled at two Palestinian universities, anonymized here as University A and University B. Par­ ticipants were recruited through purposive sampling to ensure that all interviewees had prior experience using ChatGPT for academic purposes. The sample included students majoring in English language, English literature, and translation, representing differ­ ent years of study. While participants’ English proficiency levels varied, all were suffi­ ciently proficient to engage with ChatGPT in English for learning-related tasks. All of the participants are familiar with the context of learning within the Palestinian higher education, which is shaped by political instability, movement restrictions, uneven digital infrastructure, and limited institutional resources. These conditions influence students’ access to instructional support, learning materials, and technological tools. Such con­ straints are not peripheral to the study but central to understanding why students turn to AI-assisted learning tools and how autonomy is enacted under multiple conditions of limited educational continuity.",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,7,"to instructional support, learning materials, and technological tools. Such con­ straints are not peripheral to the study but central to understanding why students turn to AI-assisted learning tools and how autonomy is enacted under multiple conditions of limited educational continuity. Despite the relatively small sample size in this study, the researchers acknowledge that this sample limits broad generalization; however, the aim of the study is analytic depth rather than representativeness. 2.1.2 Data collection methods Data were collected through semi-structured interviews conducted via WhatsApp, Mes­ senger, and email due to political and logistical constraints that limited face-to-face interaction. WhatsApp and Messenger interviews were primarily semi-synchronous, allowing for follow-up questions and clarification, while email interviews were asyn­ chronous and tended to generate more concise, reflective responses. The use of mul­ tiple communication modes enabled broader participation but also introduced variation in response depth and spontaneity. The interview questions were designed to elicit students’ experiences with ChatGPT in relation to learning autonomy, self-directed practices, confidence development, and perceived limitations of AI-assisted learn­ ing. Open-ended questions encouraged participants to reflect freely on their learning",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,7,"depth and spontaneity. The interview questions were designed to elicit students’ experiences with ChatGPT in relation to learning autonomy, self-directed practices, confidence development, and perceived limitations of AI-assisted learn­ ing. Open-ended questions encouraged participants to reflect freely on their learning experiences, allowing for the emergence of unanticipated themes. Follow-up prompts were used where possible to deepen responses and clarify meanings. The researchers acknowledge that conducting interviews within institutional contexts may introduce social desirability bias, as participants might frame responses in ways they perceive as academically acceptable. This limitation is addressed through cautious interpretation of findings and reflexive awareness during analysis. 2.1.3 Data analysis The data were analyzed using thematic analysis following the six-phase framework pro­ posed by Braun and Clarke. This approach was selected because it offers a systematic yet flexible method for identifying, analyzing, and interpreting patterns of meaning across qualitative data. The analysis involved: (1) familiarization with the data through repeated reading of interview transcripts and written responses; (2) generation of initial codes capturing recurring ideas and experiences; (3) clustering codes into candidate themes; (4) reviewing and refining",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,7,across qualitative data. The analysis involved: (1) familiarization with the data through repeated reading of interview transcripts and written responses; (2) generation of initial codes capturing recurring ideas and experiences; (3) clustering codes into candidate themes; (4) reviewing and refining themes to ensure internal coherence and analytic distinction; (5) defining and naming themes; and (6) producing the final analytic narrative. Coding,61
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,8,"Page 8 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 was conducted manually through iterative comparison of participants’ responses, with analytic decisions documented to ensure transparency. The analysis combined inductive attention to participants’ language and experiences with deductive sensitivity to con­ cepts from learner autonomy and self-directed learning literature. This hybrid approach allowed themes to emerge from the data while remaining theoretically informed. 2.1.4 Trustworthiness and reflexivity To enhance the trustworthiness of the study, several strategies were employed. Credibil­ ity was supported through sustained engagement with the data and peer discussion of emerging themes. Dependability was addressed by maintaining a clear audit trail of cod­ ing decisions and thematic development. The researchers also acknowledge their posi­ tionality as Palestinian EFL academics, which may influence both participants’ responses and interpretive perspectives. Reflexive awareness was maintained throughout the research process to minimize bias and foreground participants’ voices. While member checking and inter-coder reliability measures were constrained by time and contextual limitations, transparency in methodological reporting and cautious interpretation of findings strengthen the study’s analytic integrity. 3 Data analysis This section",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,8,"to minimize bias and foreground participants’ voices. While member checking and inter-coder reliability measures were constrained by time and contextual limitations, transparency in methodological reporting and cautious interpretation of findings strengthen the study’s analytic integrity. 3 Data analysis This section presents the findings of a qualitative thematic analysis of interviews with Palestinian EFL students, examining how they perceive and enact ChatGPT-assisted learning within their autonomous practices. The analysis focuses on recurring pat­ terns and tensions across participants’ accounts rather than isolated responses, moving beyond descriptive reporting to interpret how ChatGPT functions as both a resource and a constraint. Findings are organized thematically, with each subsection introducing a central analytic focus and supported by selected participant excerpts. The first theme explores ChatGPT as a learning scaffold, highlighting how students use AI support to manage linguistic tasks and regulate learning within constrained educational contexts. 3.1 ChatGPT as a learning scaffold in autonomous EFL practices Analysis of the interview data indicates that students generally perceive ChatGPT as a supportive learning scaffold that facilitates autonomous engagement with English lan­ guage learning tasks. Rather than",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,8,"educational contexts. 3.1 ChatGPT as a learning scaffold in autonomous EFL practices Analysis of the interview data indicates that students generally perceive ChatGPT as a supportive learning scaffold that facilitates autonomous engagement with English lan­ guage learning tasks. Rather than positioning the tool as a substitute for instruction, participants described using ChatGPT to manage learning processes independently, particularly in writing, translation, exam preparation, and conceptual clarification. Sev­ eral students emphasized ChatGPT’s capacity to provide immediate, adaptive feedback that supports iterative learning. Lina (English Language major, University A) explained that ChatGPT offered “real-time feedback that helps me correct my writing and under­ stand mistakes while I am working,” allowing her to revise texts independently without waiting for teacher input. Similarly, Omar (Translation minor, University B) reported using ChatGPT to support translation tasks, noting that the tool helped him compare linguistic options and refine accuracy under time constraints. These accounts suggest that ChatGPT functions as an on-demand learning resource that enables students to regulate their learning pace and manage academic tasks autonomously. Participants also highlighted ChatGPT’s usefulness in organizing ideas and supporting",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,8,"and refine accuracy under time constraints. These accounts suggest that ChatGPT functions as an on-demand learning resource that enables students to regulate their learning pace and manage academic tasks autonomously. Participants also highlighted ChatGPT’s usefulness in organizing ideas and supporting pre-writing activities. Sara (English Literature major, University A) described the tool",51
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,9,"Page 9 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 as “very helpful for brainstorming essays and organizing ideas before I start writing,” particularly when preparing presentations or argumentative assignments. For students facing heavy coursework and limited instructional availability, ChatGPT was perceived as a practical aid that reduced cognitive overload and facilitated task planning. Impor­ tantly, students framed this support not as replacing their own thinking, but as helping them structure and develop ideas they already possessed. Exam preparation emerged as another domain in which students valued ChatGPT’s scaffolding function. Ahmad (Eng­ lish Language major, University B) explained that ChatGPT helped him “review material quickly and remember information I had forgotten,” especially when dealing with dense or fragmented course content. Several students reported that ChatGPT allowed them to condense large amounts of material into manageable summaries, making revision more efficient. While this efficiency was framed positively, it also raises questions about whether such practices promote deep understanding or encourage surface-level engage­ ment, which is an issue addressed further in the Discussion section. When comparing ChatGPT to traditional online",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,9,"more efficient. While this efficiency was framed positively, it also raises questions about whether such practices promote deep understanding or encourage surface-level engage­ ment, which is an issue addressed further in the Discussion section. When comparing ChatGPT to traditional online resources, students frequently described it as faster, more focused, and less distracting. Maha (Translation minor, Uni­ versity A) contrasted ChatGPT with search engines, explaining that it provided “direct answers without opening many pages or getting distracted.” This preference for concise, customized explanations reflects students’ desire for controlled learning environments where they can address specific gaps in understanding. Several participants described asking ChatGPT to simplify complex concepts (e.g., requesting explanations suitable for younger learners), which they perceived as helpful for grasping difficult grammatical or theoretical material. Beyond cognitive support, some students emphasized the affec­ tive benefits of interacting with ChatGPT. Yousef (English Language major, University B) noted that practicing English through conversational interaction with ChatGPT felt “comfortable and stress-free,” particularly compared to speaking in class. This sense of emotional safety appeared to encourage experimentation with language use, thereby increasing confidence and",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,9,"(English Language major, University B) noted that practicing English through conversational interaction with ChatGPT felt “comfortable and stress-free,” particularly compared to speaking in class. This sense of emotional safety appeared to encourage experimentation with language use, thereby increasing confidence and willingness to engage independently. Such accounts suggest that ChatGPT may support autonomy not only cognitively but also emotionally, by low­ ering anxiety associated with language production. Despite these perceived benefits, students did not present uncritical endorsements of ChatGPT. Ethical concerns and fears of misuse emerged as an important counterpoint. Hala (English Literature major, University A) explicitly rejected using ChatGPT as a shortcut, stating that she preferred “to improve my abilities rather than cheat by copy­ ing.” Similarly, Khaled (Translation minor, University B) warned that uncritical use could reduce motivation and creativity, arguing that ChatGPT should “push students to think more, not replace thinking.” These perspectives reveal a tension between empowerment and dependence, highlighting students’ awareness that autonomy requires active engage­ ment rather than passive reliance on AI-generated output. Taken together, these findings indicate that students primarily perceive ChatGPT as a",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,9,"not replace thinking.” These perspectives reveal a tension between empowerment and dependence, highlighting students’ awareness that autonomy requires active engage­ ment rather than passive reliance on AI-generated output. Taken together, these findings indicate that students primarily perceive ChatGPT as a learning scaffold that supports autonomous EFL practices by facilitating task management, conceptual understanding, and confidence-building. However, the data also reveal students’ critical awareness of the ethical and cognitive risks associated with overreliance, suggesting that autonomy supported by AI remains contingent upon reflective use and pedagogical guidance.",86
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,10,"Page 10 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 3.2 Negotiating motivation: instrumental support, confidence, and emotional regulation Students’ accounts revealed diverse and sometimes conflicting views regarding ChatG­ PT’s influence on motivation, suggesting that motivation in AI-assisted learning is nei­ ther uniform nor unidimensional. Rather than describing ChatGPT as a direct source of inspiration, many participants framed its motivational impact in instrumental terms, i.e., helping them sustain engagement, manage cognitive load, and persist with academic tasks. Several students associated motivation with ChatGPT’s ability to provide rapid, relevant responses that reduced frustration during learning. Rami (English Literature major, University A) explained that ChatGPT helped him remain engaged because it “quickly gives me the information I need without wasting time,” particularly when work­ ing on literary topics such as Mahmoud Darwish’s poetry. For Rami, motivation emerged not from excitement but from efficiency and clarity, as ChatGPT helped him identify themes and directions for further exploration. Similarly, Dina (English Language major, University B) reported that ChatGPT made research-based assignments less intimidat­ ing by simplifying abstract or philosophical concepts that she",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,10,"but from efficiency and clarity, as ChatGPT helped him identify themes and directions for further exploration. Similarly, Dina (English Language major, University B) reported that ChatGPT made research-based assignments less intimidat­ ing by simplifying abstract or philosophical concepts that she previously struggled to understand. This sense of clarity increased her willingness to engage with complex mate­ rial independently. However, students did not uniformly equate efficiency with motivation. Some partici­ pants explicitly rejected the idea that ChatGPT could inspire learning in a deeper sense. Nour (Translation minor, University A) stated that she did not view ChatGPT as a moti­ vational force, arguing that “real motivation comes from inside the learner, not from a tool that answers questions.” This perspective reflects a distinction made by students between intrinsic motivation, which is driven by curiosity and personal interest, and instrumental motivation driven by task completion and performance. For Nour, Chat­ GPT was useful but emotionally neutral, lacking the human qualities she associated with inspiration. Concerns about the absence of human interaction further complicated stu­ dents’ motivational experiences. Tariq (English Literature major, University B)",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,10,"task completion and performance. For Nour, Chat­ GPT was useful but emotionally neutral, lacking the human qualities she associated with inspiration. Concerns about the absence of human interaction further complicated stu­ dents’ motivational experiences. Tariq (English Literature major, University B) described ChatGPT as “too robotic,” emphasizing that it could not replicate the emotional engage­ ment or contextual sensitivity required for interpreting literary texts. He contrasted ChatGPT with other tools, such as collaborative reading platforms and spaced-repeti­ tion applications, which he felt encouraged deeper engagement through interaction and discussion. These reflections suggest that while ChatGPT may support task persistence, it may not satisfy learners’ need for dialogic and affective engagement. Students also expressed ambivalence toward emerging multimodal AI features. Some participants suggested that visual elements such as images, color, or interactive repre­ sentations could enhance motivation by making abstract concepts more tangible. Heba (English Language major with a minor in French, University A) commented that visual support could make learning “more engaging and easier to remember.” At the same time, most students indicated a continued preference for conventional learning practices, including",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,10,"tangible. Heba (English Language major with a minor in French, University A) commented that visual support could make learning “more engaging and easier to remember.” At the same time, most students indicated a continued preference for conventional learning practices, including reading printed texts, note-taking, and classroom discussion, particularly for deep comprehension. This preference highlights a cautious openness toward AI innova­ tion rather than uncritical acceptance. Yet, a smaller group of students articulated a bal­ anced view, describing ChatGPT as a supportive but non-essential learning companion. Samer (Translation minor, University B) explained that while ChatGPT did not motivate him directly, it helped him organize ideas and plan tasks, which indirectly encouraged him to complete assignments independently. Others echoed this view, emphasizing that",121
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,11,"Page 11 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 ChatGPT’s value lay in offering good organization and structure rather than inspiration. Students repeatedly warned that overreliance on AI could discourage problem-solving and independent thinking if used unreflectively. Despite these reservations, some participants explicitly described ChatGPT as a “study partner.” Laila (English Literature major with a minor in education, University A) stated that she used ChatGPT to discuss characters and themes in postcolonial literature, find­ ing the interaction motivating because it felt dialogic and responsive. Similarly, Hussein (English Language major, University B) reported that ChatGPT supported exam prepa­ ration by offering concise explanations when studying difficult poems, enabling him to manage time more effectively and reduce cognitive fatigue. For these students, moti­ vation was closely tied to emotional reassurance and a sense of companionship during solitary study. Across these accounts, motivation emerged less as excitement or inspi­ ration and more as a form of emotional regulation and confidence-building. Students frequently described feeling more secure when working independently, particularly in group projects and presentations. Mariam (English Language major, University",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,11,"these accounts, motivation emerged less as excitement or inspi­ ration and more as a form of emotional regulation and confidence-building. Students frequently described feeling more secure when working independently, particularly in group projects and presentations. Mariam (English Language major, University A) noted that ChatGPT helped her contribute ideas during teamwork, making her feel “more con­ fident and prepared in front of classmates.” This confidence encouraged further indepen­ dent exploration, suggesting that ChatGPT may support motivation by lowering anxiety rather than generating intrinsic interest. Overall, the findings indicate that ChatGPT’s motivational influence is indirect and contingent. While it does not replace intrinsic motivation or human inspiration, it sup­ ports persistence, confidence, and task management, which are critical components of self-directed learning. These nuanced perspectives underscore the importance of fram­ ing AI-assisted motivation not as a universal benefit but as a context-dependent process shaped by learners’ goals, values, and expectations. 3.3 Conditional support for critical thinking: constraint, agency, and reflective use Students expressed divergent views regarding ChatGPT’s capacity to enhance criti­ cal thinking, with many emphasizing that its impact depends largely on",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,11,"process shaped by learners’ goals, values, and expectations. 3.3 Conditional support for critical thinking: constraint, agency, and reflective use Students expressed divergent views regarding ChatGPT’s capacity to enhance criti­ cal thinking, with many emphasizing that its impact depends largely on how the tool is used rather than on its technical features alone. For several participants, ChatGPT was perceived as limiting critical engagement due to its tendency to provide immedi­ ate, finalized answers that reduce opportunities for reflection and independent reason­ ing. Some students explicitly rejected the idea that ChatGPT promotes critical thinking. Yazan (English Language major, University B) stated, “No, I don’t think it helps my criti­ cal thinking because when I use it, I rely on it without thinking.” This perception reflects students’ awareness that speed and convenience can undermine cognitive effort, partic­ ularly when answers are accepted uncritically. Other participants echoed this concern, describing ChatGPT as a tool that “just answers questions” without encouraging deeper inquiry or reflection. For these students, critical thinking was associated with struggle, uncertainty, and active meaning-making; they felt such processes were bypassed by",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,11,"Other participants echoed this concern, describing ChatGPT as a tool that “just answers questions” without encouraging deeper inquiry or reflection. For these students, critical thinking was associated with struggle, uncertainty, and active meaning-making; they felt such processes were bypassed by AI- generated responses. However, not all students viewed ChatGPT as cognitively restrictive. Some partici­ pants argued that when used selectively, ChatGPT could prompt further exploration rather than replace thinking. Reem (English French minor, University A) explained that ChatGPT often provided broad overviews that encouraged her to investigate topics more deeply, especially in literature courses. She noted that while ChatGPT summarized",100
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,12,"Page 12 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 themes, it frequently lacked cultural or contextual detail, which motivated her to consult additional sources and develop her own interpretations. In this way, the tool functioned as a starting point rather than an endpoint for critical inquiry. Students also described how dialogic use of ChatGPT could support analytical thinking. Ahmad (Translation minor, University A) referred to an “answer–question sequence” in which ChatGPT’s explanations led him to re-examine textbook material, particularly in phonology. Rather than accepting responses passively, he used them to interrogate assumptions and refine understanding. These accounts suggest that critical thinking emerged when students actively questioned, evaluated, and extended AI-generated content. Several participants proposed that ChatGPT could better support critical thinking if it were designed to encourage interaction rather than provide definitive answers. Nada (English language major, University B) suggested that ChatGPT should offer partial responses and prompt students to complete or extend them, arguing that this would “force the student to think and participate.” Others recommended that the tool ask follow-up “why” or “how” questions to",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,12,"University B) suggested that ChatGPT should offer partial responses and prompt students to complete or extend them, arguing that this would “force the student to think and participate.” Others recommended that the tool ask follow-up “why” or “how” questions to sustain dialogue and reflection. These sugges­ tions indicate students’ desire for AI systems that challenge rather than resolve cogni­ tive uncertainty. At the same time, students cautioned against overdependence. Ola (Translation minor, University A) noted that ChatGPT could be useful for generating initial ideas, such as synonyms in translation tasks, but emphasized that relying entirely on these suggestions would be counterproductive. She argued that critical thinking develops when learners build upon initial input independently rather than reproduce it. Similarly, Kareem (English Literature major, University B) warned that ChatGPT “limits thinking” when it presents answers as fixed and complete, discouraging exploration of alternative perspectives. Across participants’ accounts, a recurring theme was the distinction between passive consumption and reflective engagement. Students did not reject ChatGPT outright; instead, they emphasized that its value for critical thinking depends on deliberate, reflec­ tive use. When",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,12,"of alternative perspectives. Across participants’ accounts, a recurring theme was the distinction between passive consumption and reflective engagement. Students did not reject ChatGPT outright; instead, they emphasized that its value for critical thinking depends on deliberate, reflec­ tive use. When employed as a prompt for inquiry, comparison, and verification, Chat­ GPT could support analytical processes. When used as a shortcut to answers, however, it risked undermining the very cognitive skills students associated with critical learn­ ing. Overall, the findings suggest that ChatGPT does not inherently enhance or diminish critical thinking. Rather, its impact is mediated by students’ learning strategies, goals, and levels of metacognitive awareness. These insights highlight the need for pedagogical guidance that helps learners use AI tools as instruments for inquiry rather than substi­ tutes for thinking, a theme further developed in the Discussion section. 3.4 Autonomy under constraint: personalized learning, dependence, and human mediation Students’ accounts revealed a complex and often ambivalent relationship between Chat­ GPT and autonomous learning. While many participants described ChatGPT as a tool that supported self-directed learning through personalization and accessibility, they simultaneously",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,12,"constraint: personalized learning, dependence, and human mediation Students’ accounts revealed a complex and often ambivalent relationship between Chat­ GPT and autonomous learning. While many participants described ChatGPT as a tool that supported self-directed learning through personalization and accessibility, they simultaneously emphasized that autonomy remained incomplete without human media­ tion, contextual understanding, and ethical guidance. Rather than portraying autonomy as unrestricted independence, students framed it as a negotiated practice shaped by technological affordances and social interaction. Several participants expressed skepti­ cism about relying entirely on AI for autonomous learning. Ayman (English Literature",91
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,13,"Page 13 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 major, University B) stated, “I cannot depend fully on AI. It helps with learning, but dis­ cussion with people who have expertise is more beneficial.” Students repeatedly high­ lighted ChatGPT’s limitations in addressing culturally embedded knowledge, such as social traditions, religious practices, and context-sensitive meanings. For these learn­ ers, autonomy did not imply isolation from others but required dialogic engagement to interpret nuanced content. At the same time, many students emphasized ChatGPT’s flexibility and adaptability as key enablers of personalized learning. Rana (Translation minor, University A) described how ChatGPT adjusted explanations to different levels of complexity, noting that it could produce responses suitable “for academic writing or even for a five-year-old.” This adaptability allowed students to tailor learning experiences to their individual needs, learning pace, and proficiency levels. Participants perceived this feature as particularly valuable when instructors or peers were unavailable. ChatGPT’s role in supporting writ­ ing development emerged prominently. Hassan (English Literature major, University B) reported using ChatGPT extensively for proofreading and revision, explaining that repeated cycles",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,13,"Participants perceived this feature as particularly valuable when instructors or peers were unavailable. ChatGPT’s role in supporting writ­ ing development emerged prominently. Hassan (English Literature major, University B) reported using ChatGPT extensively for proofreading and revision, explaining that repeated cycles of correction helped him notice recurring punctuation and grammar errors. Others appreciated the tool’s ability to store conversation history, which enabled them to track progress and revisit explanations. These practices contributed to students’ sense of ownership over learning and reinforced habits of self-monitoring. However, not all students experienced ChatGPT as empowering. Some participants argued that its current use could undermine autonomy by narrowing exposure to diverse learning resources. Salma (English Language major, University A) cautioned that exces­ sive reliance on ChatGPT reduced engagement with alternative pedagogies and sources, limiting opportunities to develop independent research skills. Others questioned whether ChatGPT could provide meaningful feedback, suggesting that its responses sometimes lacked depth or pedagogical sensitivity. A recurring concern across inter­ views was the risk of replacing, rather than supplementing, teachers’ roles. Majd (Eng­ lish Literature major, University B) emphasized that removing teachers",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,13,"provide meaningful feedback, suggesting that its responses sometimes lacked depth or pedagogical sensitivity. A recurring concern across inter­ views was the risk of replacing, rather than supplementing, teachers’ roles. Majd (Eng­ lish Literature major, University B) emphasized that removing teachers from the learning process would be “a serious mistake,” arguing that while AI can support learning, it can­ not substitute human mentorship, judgment, and encouragement. Students consistently advocated for a balanced model in which ChatGPT complements, rather than displaces, instructional guidance. Despite these concerns, students repeatedly described ChatGPT as a valuable sup­ port for self-directed learning under constrained conditions. Nisreen (Education minor, University A) explained that she relied on ChatGPT late at night when teachers were unavailable, describing it as “a tutor that is always ready to help.” This accessibil­ ity reduced anxiety and increased confidence, enabling her to engage with challenging material independently. For learners facing limited access to academic support, Chat­ GPT functioned as a form of academic safety net rather than a replacement for instruc­ tion. Participants also highlighted ChatGPT’s role in vocabulary development and exam preparation.",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,13,"with challenging material independently. For learners facing limited access to academic support, Chat­ GPT functioned as a form of academic safety net rather than a replacement for instruc­ tion. Participants also highlighted ChatGPT’s role in vocabulary development and exam preparation. Omar (English Language major, University B) noted that requesting syn­ onyms and contextual examples helped him expand his vocabulary and reuse expres­ sions in later assignments. Others described using ChatGPT to create structured study plans, diagnostic quizzes, and practice exercises tailored to their learning gaps. These practices enabled learners to plan, monitor, and evaluate learning – core components of self-directed learning.",101
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,14,"Page 14 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 Overall, students perceived ChatGPT as a tool that can foster autonomy by enabling personalization, accessibility, and self-regulation. However, autonomy was consistently described as conditional rather than absolute. The effectiveness of ChatGPT depended on reflective use, learner responsibility, and continued human involvement. These find­ ings underscore that AI-supported autonomy in EFL learning is best understood as a hybrid process, situated within broader pedagogical, cultural, and institutional contexts rather than as a purely technological achievement. 4 Discussion of interview findings This study set out to explore how Palestinian EFL students perceive and negotiate Chat­ GPT-assisted learning in relation to autonomous and self-directed learning. Rather than treating autonomy as a uniform outcome of AI integration, the findings reveal autonomy as a conditional and context-dependent process shaped by students’ learning strategies, technological affordances, and the continued presence of human mediation. The dis­ cussion that follows interprets these findings through established autonomy and con­ structivist frameworks, while situating them within the socio-institutional realities of Palestinian higher education. Consistent with classical definitions of learner",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,14,"affordances, and the continued presence of human mediation. The dis­ cussion that follows interprets these findings through established autonomy and con­ structivist frameworks, while situating them within the socio-institutional realities of Palestinian higher education. Consistent with classical definitions of learner autonomy [7, 21, 28], students in this study demonstrated increased capacity to manage learning goals, regulate study prac­ tices, and evaluate progress when using ChatGPT as a learning scaffold. The tool enabled learners to organize ideas, revise language output, plan study schedules, and access explanations independently, which are key features of self-directed learning. However, the findings also indicate that autonomy was not experienced as complete independence from instructional support but as a form of guided self-regulation mediated by AI and human judgment. This distinction is critical for avoiding what may be termed pseudo- autonomy: a condition in which learners appear independent while relying uncritically on AI-generated output. Several students explicitly warned against this risk, noting that excessive reliance on ChatGPT could undermine critical engagement, creativity, and exposure to diverse learning resources. From a constructivist perspective, autonomy develops not through the",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,14,"independent while relying uncritically on AI-generated output. Several students explicitly warned against this risk, noting that excessive reliance on ChatGPT could undermine critical engagement, creativity, and exposure to diverse learning resources. From a constructivist perspective, autonomy develops not through the absence of support but through meaningful engagement with learning challenges. When ChatGPT functioned as a shortcut rather than a scaffold, stu­ dents themselves recognized that autonomy was weakened rather than strengthened. Dewey’s experiential and constructivist philosophy offers a particularly powerful lens for interpreting the ambivalent role of ChatGPT in students’ autonomous learning prac­ tices. For Dewey, learning is not produced by exposure to information or by the rapid acquisition of answers, but by a reflective reconstruction of experience, in which learn­ ers actively inquire into problems, test ideas, and revise understanding through delib­ eration [12, 13]. Viewed through this framework, the findings suggest that ChatGPT supported learner autonomy only insofar as it was appropriated as a tool for inquiry— that is, when students used it to question assumptions, clarify conceptual difficulties, and extend lines of thought beyond initial responses. In",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,14,"the findings suggest that ChatGPT supported learner autonomy only insofar as it was appropriated as a tool for inquiry— that is, when students used it to question assumptions, clarify conceptual difficulties, and extend lines of thought beyond initial responses. In contrast, when AI-generated outputs were accepted uncritically or treated as authoritative solutions, the reflective cycle that Dewey identifies as central to educative experience was effectively short-cir­ cuited. Such use aligns with what Dewey [12] warns against as mis-educative experi­ ence, where efficiency and immediacy replace sustained inquiry and meaning-making. From a Deweyan perspective, therefore, ChatGPT’s educational value cannot be located",100
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,15,"Page 15 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 in its speed, accuracy, or convenience alone, but in the extent to which it is pedagogi­ cally situated within practices that demand reflection, judgment, and learner responsi­ bility. Without these conditions, AI-assisted learning risks reproducing passive forms of dependence that undermine, rather than cultivate, the habits of mind necessary for genuine autonomy. The findings further foreground the irreplaceable role of teachers as pedagogi­ cal mediators, a role that becomes even more salient when interpreted through Dew­ ey’s constructivist philosophy of education. Although ChatGPT effectively supported task management, language revision, and individualized pacing, students consistently emphasized that it could not substitute the relational, ethical, and interpretive dimen­ sions of teaching. Dewey’s insistence that education is fundamentally a social and moral practice—one grounded in communication, shared inquiry, and guided judgment— helps illuminate why AI-mediated assistance alone cannot sustain educative experience [12, 13]. Students’ accounts suggest that while AI may facilitate procedural aspects of learning, it lacks the capacity to engage learners in the kinds of dialogic interaction, con­ textual interpretation,",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,15,"helps illuminate why AI-mediated assistance alone cannot sustain educative experience [12, 13]. Students’ accounts suggest that while AI may facilitate procedural aspects of learning, it lacks the capacity to engage learners in the kinds of dialogic interaction, con­ textual interpretation, and ethical deliberation that are particularly crucial in culturally embedded domains such as literature, translation, and discourse analysis. This finding resonates with recent scholarship cautioning against technological determinism, which assumes that pedagogical value can be derived directly from technological capability rather than from instructional mediation and purpose [20, 37]. Rather than diminish­ ing the teacher’s role, the integration of AI appears to reconfigure educators as reflective facilitators, responsible for helping learners interrogate AI outputs, contextualize knowl­ edge, and cultivate critical judgment [6]. Importantly, the Palestinian higher-education context adds a distinctive dimension to this dynamic. Under conditions of political instability, restricted mobility, and uneven access to instructional support, autonomous learning often emerges as a pragmatic necessity rather than a pedagogical choice. Within these constraints, ChatGPT functioned as an adaptive resource that mitigated disrup­ tion by providing low-threshold academic support during periods",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,15,"mobility, and uneven access to instructional support, autonomous learning often emerges as a pragmatic necessity rather than a pedagogical choice. Within these constraints, ChatGPT functioned as an adaptive resource that mitigated disrup­ tion by providing low-threshold academic support during periods when teachers were unavailable. Yet, students’ acute awareness of AI’s limitations, particularly its inability to replace human guidance, reveals a nuanced understanding of autonomy that aligns closely with Dewey’s view of independence as socially situated, ethically bounded, and pedagogically cultivated, rather than as a purely technological achievement. The study’s findings align with recent research indicating that AI tools can enhance self-directed learning when integrated thoughtfully but risk fostering dependency when used unreflectively [32, 46]. What distinguishes the present study is its emphasis on stu­ dents’ critical awareness of this balance. Participants did not simply celebrate ChatG­ PT’s utility; they articulated nuanced judgments about when and how AI supports or undermines autonomy. This reflexivity challenges simplistic narratives of AI either as an educational solution or as a pedagogical threat. Taken together, the discussion under­ scores that ChatGPT-assisted learning supports autonomy most",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,15,"nuanced judgments about when and how AI supports or undermines autonomy. This reflexivity challenges simplistic narratives of AI either as an educational solution or as a pedagogical threat. Taken together, the discussion under­ scores that ChatGPT-assisted learning supports autonomy most effectively when framed as a scaffold for inquiry, reflection, and self-regulation, rather than as a replacement for thinking or teaching. Genuine autonomy emerges through the interplay of learner agency, AI affordances, and pedagogical guidance. For educators and policymakers, this suggests that the integration of AI in EFL education should prioritize critical engage­ ment, ethical awareness, and contextual sensitivity, particularly in resource-constrained environments such as the Palestinian higher education.",108
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,16,"Page 16 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 5 Concluding remarks This qualitative study has examined Palestinian EFL students’ experiences with Chat­ GPT-assisted learning to understand how AI-mediated practices intersect with autono­ mous and self-directed learning in higher education. Drawing on students’ narratives, the study demonstrates that ChatGPT functions most effectively as a learning scaffold that supports goal-setting, self-regulation, and confidence-building rather than as a replacement for instruction or independent thinking. Students’ experiences reveal that autonomy supported by AI is not automatic but emerges through reflective, selective, and ethically informed use of technological tools. The findings contribute empirically by foregrounding students’ nuanced and often ambivalent perspectives toward ChatGPT. While participants valued the tool’s imme­ diacy, personalization, and accessibility, particularly under conditions of limited instruc­ tional availability, they also expressed critical awareness of its limitations. These included concerns about overreliance, reduced cognitive engagement, and the inability of AI to address culturally embedded meanings, ethical judgment, and creative interpretation. Such insights challenge overly optimistic narratives of AI in education and highlight stu­ dents’ capacity to critically negotiate technological",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,16,"concerns about overreliance, reduced cognitive engagement, and the inability of AI to address culturally embedded meanings, ethical judgment, and creative interpretation. Such insights challenge overly optimistic narratives of AI in education and highlight stu­ dents’ capacity to critically negotiate technological support. From a theoretical perspective, the study advances autonomy research by distinguish­ ing between genuine autonomy and pseudo-autonomy in AI-assisted learning contexts. Genuine autonomy was evident when students used ChatGPT as a scaffold for inquiry, reflection, and self-monitoring, whereas pseudo-autonomy emerged when learners relied uncritically on AI-generated output. This distinction reinforces constructivist and experiential views of learning, which emphasize agency, reflection, and meaning-making as essential to educational development. Autonomy, therefore, should be understood not as independence from support but as the ability to engage critically with available resources. Contextually, this study contributes to the limited body of research on AI- assisted learning in Palestinian higher education. Operating within conditions of politi­ cal instability, restricted mobility, and uneven access to educational support, students often experience autonomous learning as a necessity rather than a pedagogical prefer­ ence. In this setting, ChatGPT served",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,16,"assisted learning in Palestinian higher education. Operating within conditions of politi­ cal instability, restricted mobility, and uneven access to educational support, students often experience autonomous learning as a necessity rather than a pedagogical prefer­ ence. In this setting, ChatGPT served as an adaptive resource that mitigated disruption and supported learning continuity. At the same time, students’ insistence on the con­ tinued centrality of teachers pinpoints the social, ethical, and relational dimensions of learning that technology alone cannot replicate. The pedagogical implications of these findings are significant. Rather than integrat­ ing ChatGPT as a standalone solution, educators should adopt guided approaches that promote critical engagement, ethical awareness, and reflective use. Teachers play a cru­ cial role in modeling responsible AI practices, designing tasks that require interpreta­ tion and evaluation, and ensuring that AI-supported learning complements rather than replaces intellectual effort. While the researchers show that such approaches are par­ ticularly vital in EFL contexts, where language learning involves cultural sensitivity, cre­ ativity, and dialogic interaction, they also acknowledge several limitations. The relatively small, non-representative sample and the use of online interview",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,16,"the researchers show that such approaches are par­ ticularly vital in EFL contexts, where language learning involves cultural sensitivity, cre­ ativity, and dialogic interaction, they also acknowledge several limitations. The relatively small, non-representative sample and the use of online interview platforms may limit the transferability of findings. Additionally, variations in students’ language proficiency, prior AI experience, and access to digital resources were not examined in depth. These limitations point to important directions for future research, including comparative",77
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,17,"Page 17 of 19 Al-atrash et al. Discover Global Society (2026) 4:16 studies across proficiency levels, longitudinal investigations of autonomy development, and classroom-based research examining pedagogically guided AI integration. Nonetheless, it can be concluded that ChatGPT holds promise for supporting self- directed learning in higher education when used thoughtfully and critically. Its educa­ tional value lies not in replacing teachers or thinking but in extending learners’ capacity to engage with knowledge autonomously within supportive pedagogical frameworks. Future research should continue to explore how AI tools can be integrated responsibly to enhance (not diminish) human-centered education, particularly in contexts marked by structural and resource-related constraints. Acknowledgements The authors gratefully acknowledge the administrative and academic support provided by An-Najah National University and Al-Quds Open University, which was essential to the successful completion of this study. Author contributions Aya Al-atrash was responsible for designing the questionnaire and assisting in data collection. Mohammed Hamdan conceptualized the study, coordinated the research project, and led the writing and revision of the manuscript. Khaled Dweikat contributed to shaping and refining the research methodology and supported the analysis",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,17,"designing the questionnaire and assisting in data collection. Mohammed Hamdan conceptualized the study, coordinated the research project, and led the writing and revision of the manuscript. Khaled Dweikat contributed to shaping and refining the research methodology and supported the analysis process. All authors contributed to the interpretation of findings and approved the final version of the manuscript. Funding This research received no external funding. Data availability The datasets generated and analyzed during the current study, which comprise anonymized questionnaire responses are not publicly available due to institutional policy but are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The ethics approval is correct. This study was conducted in accordance with the ethical standards of An-Najah National University and in line with the principles of the 1964 Helsinki Declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. Ethical approval for the study was granted by the Department of Humanities and Educational Sciences, Faculty of Graduate Studies, An-Najah National University. The research procedures were carried out in accordance with the guidelines and regulations of the",180
DOC_069,s44282-026-00350-5.pdf,10.1007/s44282-026-00350-5,17,"comparable ethical standards. Ethical approval for the study was granted by the Department of Humanities and Educational Sciences, Faculty of Graduate Studies, An-Najah National University. The research procedures were carried out in accordance with the guidelines and regulations of the approving department. Prior to participation, all individuals received detailed information regarding the purpose and procedures of the study, and written informed consent was obtained. Participation was entirely voluntary, and participants retained the right to withdraw at any stage without any negative consequences. Anonymity and confidentiality were strictly maintained throughout the research process. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Received: 1 November 2025 / Accepted: 6 February 2026",109
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,1,"Tam, A., Abraham, D., Williams, A. (2026). Integrating generative AI into university coursework assessments – A student perspective. Intersection: A Journal at the Intersection of Assessment and Learning, Early View Integrating Generative AI into University Coursework Assessments – A Student Perspective Angela Tam, Ph.D., David Abraham, Ph.D. Andrew Williams, Ph.D. Author Note Angela Tam, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8322-180X David Abraham, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8952-6057 Andrew Williams, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0908-0364 We have no conflicts of interest to disclose Intersection: A Journal at the Intersection of Assessment and Learning Early View Abstract: Generative AI is reshaping higher education and prompting a re‑evaluation of traditional assessment practices. This study examines undergraduate university students’ experiences and perceptions of undertaking an AI-integrated coursework assessment, whereby students were tasked with critically evaluating ChatGPT generated scientific output. An AI-integrated assessment case study is presented, followed by a mixed-methods analysis of questionnaire data, open-ended responses, and focus group discussions. Students reported that generative AI supports university assessments through idea generation, summarization, and clarification of complex concepts, but expressed concerns about academic integrity, and the reliability and scientific accuracy of AI generated outputs. AI‑enabled assessments were viewed",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,1,"focus group discussions. Students reported that generative AI supports university assessments through idea generation, summarization, and clarification of complex concepts, but expressed concerns about academic integrity, and the reliability and scientific accuracy of AI generated outputs. AI‑enabled assessments were viewed positively as they encouraged critical evaluation of AI‑generated content, supported independent research and promoted understanding of the limitations of AI. However, students also identified potential long-term risks to critical thinking associated with AI use and emphasized the importance of clearer institutional guidance and further support for digital literacy development. Although the study was limited to a single student cohort, the findings suggest that thoughtfully integrating AI into the assessment structure can preserve critical thinking, promote AI literacy competencies and maintain assessment authenticity. Keywords: generative artificial intelligence, ChatGPT, assessment, coursework, higher education, student perceptions Literature Review Large language models (LLMs) are a type of artificial intelligence (AI) model designed to understand and generate human-like text. These models are trained on vast amounts of data from the corpus of information on the internet. For example, it has been estimated that ChatGPT was",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,1,"a type of artificial intelligence (AI) model designed to understand and generate human-like text. These models are trained on vast amounts of data from the corpus of information on the internet. For example, it has been estimated that ChatGPT was trained on hundreds of terabytes of data. LLMs use machine learning algorithms that predict and produce coherent and contextually appropriate text, based on the prompt they receive. The application of AI has now expanded across myriad spheres of society, including business, technology, science and education (Borger et al., 2023; Haque & Li, 2024). Therefore, many consider generative AI to have the potential to transform higher education practice, with educators encouraged to embrace its capabilities (Cooper, 2023).",116
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,2,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ The emergence of ChatGPT, and the wider public awareness of its abilities, has led educators and higher education institutions (HEIs) to raise concerns about its impact on academic integrity and the current usefulness of traditional academic assessments. The user-friendly interface and ease of access for most students raises concerns that students will use LLMs to generative substantive text for their assessments. This presents major concerns about plagiarism and academic integrity, whereby students could easily leverage generative AI to generate text, images and diagrams and submit these as their own work (Farrelly & Baker, 2023; Matheis & John, 2024; Yusuf et al., 2024). This raises several challenges for HEIs, for example how to recognize plagiarized AI-generated text, whether existing assessment strategies maintain high standards of academic rigor, and how to evaluate the impact of AI on student learning (Jones, 2023; Onesi-Ozigagun et al., 2024; Vieriu & Petrea, 2025). To counter AI-generated plagiarism, some HEIs have adopted applications that support AI detection algorithms (McDonald et al., 2024), such as Turnitin",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,2,"to evaluate the impact of AI on student learning (Jones, 2023; Onesi-Ozigagun et al., 2024; Vieriu & Petrea, 2025). To counter AI-generated plagiarism, some HEIs have adopted applications that support AI detection algorithms (McDonald et al., 2024), such as Turnitin that is embedded in virtual learning environments. However, others have refrained from using AI detection software due to accuracy and reliability concerns, particularly regarding the possibility of detecting false positives (Dalalah & Dalalah, 2023; Saqib & Zia, 2024; Weber-Wulff et al., 2023). Therefore, existing AI text detection tools are limited in their capacity to accurately detect AI generated text, while current AI detection tools may unfairly discriminate against students whose first language is not English, by mistaking text written by the student with AI generated text (Fröhling, 2021; Tsigaris & da Silva, 2026). This is an important consideration when applying detection tools fairly across diverse student cohorts. The rapid development of generative AI technologies, and the release of new versions with improved capabilities, may outpace existing detection software and therefore entering a digital arms race may prove futile (Elkhatat et",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,2,"detection tools fairly across diverse student cohorts. The rapid development of generative AI technologies, and the release of new versions with improved capabilities, may outpace existing detection software and therefore entering a digital arms race may prove futile (Elkhatat et al., 2023; Kar et al., 2025). A more pragmatic approach is for universities to employ alternative strategies to assess their students (Perkins et al., 2024; Williams, 2025). A return to in-person examinations, especially post COVID-19, and a reduction in the reliance on online assessments might mitigate some of the issues concerning plagiarism. However, assessment strategies that integrate AI may provide a more progressive and contemporary approach to assessment design. Integrating AI into assessments may provide opportunities to improve student learning, promote personalized feedback and enhance AI literacy (Gamage et al., 2023; JISC, 2024; Moorhouse et al., 2023; Owan et al., 2023). Although teachers and HEIs face considerable challenges in adapting to new AI technologies, universities have a responsibility to train their students in the ethical and proper use of AI, and to provide students with skills for a career within",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,2,"2023). Although teachers and HEIs face considerable challenges in adapting to new AI technologies, universities have a responsibility to train their students in the ethical and proper use of AI, and to provide students with skills for a career within an AI-driven society (Aoun, 2024). Integrating AI into the structure of coursework assessments may therefore promote AI literacy and provide an authentic assessment modality (Salinas- Navarro et al., 2024a, 2024b). Furthermore, the challenges associated with generative AI in higher education extend beyond plagiarism and academic integrity. Important questions are being asked about authorship, independence and the purpose of assessment. If coursework assessments can be completed without demonstrating the intended knowledge, judgement or disciplinary thinking, then the issue is one of assessment validity as well as academic misconduct (Kofinas et al., 2025). This reinforces the argument that assessments require a structural re-evaluation, rather than the mere implementation of mitigation strategies, for them to be sustainable in higher education settings (Curtis, 2025).",160
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,3,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Student Engagement with and Perception of Generative AI Recent evidence shows a rapid normalization and acceptance of generative AI in student academic work (Williams, 2026). For example, a recent Higher Education Policy Institute (HELPI) report from the UK described near universal AI use, as 95% of students surveyed reported using AI in some capacity, and 94% reported using generative AI to help with assessed work (Freeman, 2025). Students use AI for a variety of tasks, including writing support, concept clarification, note-taking, revision, research assistance, immediate feedback, translation, and job preparation (Ravšelj et al., 2025; Strzelecki, 2024). However, students do not see all uses of AI as equivalent. Although students are generally enthusiastic about using generative AI to support learning, they worry about the unclear boundary between ethical use and cheating, especially in assessment (O’Dea et al., 2025; Shuhaiber et al., 2025). Student perceptions of AI may also be shaped by personal values and judgement, as well as confidence, discipline, linguistic demands, access to digital technology, and prior educational",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,3,"and cheating, especially in assessment (O’Dea et al., 2025; Shuhaiber et al., 2025). Student perceptions of AI may also be shaped by personal values and judgement, as well as confidence, discipline, linguistic demands, access to digital technology, and prior educational experience (Freeman, 2025; JISC, 2025). Many universities are adopting a redesign of assessments in ways that preserve academic rigor while also developing students’ capacity to work critically and ethically with AI (Khlaif et al., 2025). Despite this, students and academics often have disparate views on the value of integrating AI into assessment. (Kizilcec et al., 2024; Smolansky et al., 2023). While academic staff tend to be more sympathetic to its adoption as an authentic tool that encourages critical thinking, students perceive this as a potential threat to their creativity. Conceptual research on evaluative judgement further suggests that students need to learn how to assess the quality of AI outputs and the appropriateness of using AI at different stages of disciplinary inquiry (Bearman et al., 2024). However, there is limited evidence on how students perceive coursework assessments that explicitly integrate generative",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,3,"learn how to assess the quality of AI outputs and the appropriateness of using AI at different stages of disciplinary inquiry (Bearman et al., 2024). However, there is limited evidence on how students perceive coursework assessments that explicitly integrate generative AI into the task design or require students to critically evaluate its output. Therefore, there is value in examining discipline- specific cases in which generative AI is incorporated into an authentic coursework assessment, particularly where students are asked to critically evaluate AI generated output and then reflect on their experiences. Student AI Literacy Historically, artificial intelligence has been defined as the science and engineering of creating intelligent computer programs (McCarthy, 2007). However, the lack of a universally accepted definition of AI complicates the establishment of a standardized framework for AI literacy in higher education. There is also a tendency to conflate general AI literacy with generative AI literacy, due to the immediate and transformative impact of LLMs (Holmes et al., 2022). Student AI literacy is often viewed as the specific ability to use generative tools to produce an output. However,",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,3,"conflate general AI literacy with generative AI literacy, due to the immediate and transformative impact of LLMs (Holmes et al., 2022). Student AI literacy is often viewed as the specific ability to use generative tools to produce an output. However, AI literacy extends beyond basic technical operation to encompass the ability to critically evaluate technologies, make judgements on AI generated outputs and collaborate with AI tools effectively (Long & Magerko, 2020). For university assessments to remain robust, AI literacy must be framed as a competency that enables students to use AI as a scaffold for deeper inquiry rather than a shortcut to a final product or as a replacement for cognitive effort (Holmes, 2024). Recent AI literacy frameworks emphasize a multidimensional approach to generative AI literacy (Annapureddy et al., 2025; Ng et al., 2021). This includes technical proficiency coupled with an",141
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,4,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ understanding of ethical implications, inherent biases, and systemic limitations. More specifically, integrating these competencies into coursework is essential for promoting critical thinking, as students should be taught to interrogate AI output critically (Gonsalves, 2026; Nguyen & Hayward, 2025). Integrating AI into coursework assessments therefore has the potential to fulfil two critical elements: by promoting student AI literacy skills and engaging students with critical thinking. For AI-integrated assessments to be successful, foundational AI literacy proficiencies are required by both staff and students (Hackl et al., 2026). For example, educators require an appreciation of the capabilities and limitations of generative AI to adopt these technologies for disciplinary-specific assessment design. Concurrently, students require training and a working understanding of the responsible use of LLMs, including bias, hallucinations and the ethical limitations in academic work. AI literacy is contained within the broader pedagogical framework of digital literacy, which supports learning through the application of education technology (Gilster, 1997). AI literacy also falls within the paradigm of assessment for learning, which promotes evaluative",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,4,"in academic work. AI literacy is contained within the broader pedagogical framework of digital literacy, which supports learning through the application of education technology (Gilster, 1997). AI literacy also falls within the paradigm of assessment for learning, which promotes evaluative judgement and feedback literacy (Boud, 2012; Winstone & Boud, 2022). In the context of AI-enabled assessments, critically engaging students with AI-generated output promotes critical thinking and judgement, and therefore reinforces AI literacy proficiencies (Holmes et al., 2019). The structural design of the AI-integrated assessment presented in this paper is therefore aligned with both digital (AI) literacy and assessment for learning pedagogies. Theoretical Framework In higher education practice, assessments are often designed through constructive alignment, whereby the assessment aligns with intended learning outcomes, teaching and learning activities, and assessment criteria (Biggs, 1996). In parallel, authentic assessment emphasizes assignments and student outputs that are based on meaningful tasks that resemble real-world practice rather than proxy tests (Ashford-Rowe et al., 2014). More recently, the framework of authentic assessment has been extended to include AI-enabled or AI-integrated tasks that reflect modern workplace practices, which",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,4,"that are based on meaningful tasks that resemble real-world practice rather than proxy tests (Ashford-Rowe et al., 2014). More recently, the framework of authentic assessment has been extended to include AI-enabled or AI-integrated tasks that reflect modern workplace practices, which increasingly involve AI-augmented writing, analysis and design (Swiecki et al., 2022; Williams, 2025). Creating assessments that are both constructively aligned and authentic by design has become increasingly challenging in higher education settings due to the rapid proliferation of generative AI technologies. However, opportunities exist to embed AI into the structural design of assessments, particularly coursework assignments, while maintaining academic rigor and integrity (Corbin et al., 2025). As generative AI operates via probabilistic patterns rather than critical evaluation, this poses a direct challenge to the authenticity and reliability of AI generated content (Hummel, 2025). Although AI can generate superficially convincing text, it lacks the capacity for deep disciplinary reasoning (Jose et al., 2025). The ease with which probabilistically generated text mimics authoritative discourse can tempt students to accept such output uncritically and outsource their reasoning (Misiejuk et al., 2026). This creates",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,4,"it lacks the capacity for deep disciplinary reasoning (Jose et al., 2025). The ease with which probabilistically generated text mimics authoritative discourse can tempt students to accept such output uncritically and outsource their reasoning (Misiejuk et al., 2026). This creates an opening for cognitive offloading, where the ease of AI generation replaces the intellectual aptitude necessary for student cognitive growth (Gerlich, 2025; Tang, 2025). To mitigate these risks and to avoid the homogenization of university submissions, and potentially biased academic output, it is no longer sufficient to simply police AI use. Instead, a fundamental redesign of coursework",97
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,5,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ assessments is required (Corbin et al., 2025), which ensures pedagogical accountability is maintained by creating assessments requiring human-centric critical thinking and independent verification. When re-designing assessments to account for the challenges associated with generative AI, universities can bridge the gap between institutional expectations and the actual student experience by involving students in the co-design process (Deeley & Bovill, 2017). This partnership allows educators to maintain their role as arbiters of knowledge while drawing on student perspectives to create assessments that are authentic and more resistant to AI interference (Williams & Ingle, 2025). In addition to a collaborative approach to assessment design, the student perspective provides a more contextual understanding of what factors promote independent learning and authenticity (Smolansky et al., 2023). This approach may also offer a more sustainable assessment template as AI technologies become more sophisticated in the future. We therefore present a case study on the integration of generative AI into an undergraduate coursework assessment, as part of a UK university medical sciences program. The assessment",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,5,"sustainable assessment template as AI technologies become more sophisticated in the future. We therefore present a case study on the integration of generative AI into an undergraduate coursework assessment, as part of a UK university medical sciences program. The assessment invited students to critically evaluate AI-generated content within an authentic assessment framework, while engaging students with concepts related to AI literacy. The study is also informed by a student-as- partner approach, providing evidence that assessment design can benefit from student co-creation and partnership (Bryson, 2016; Cook-Sather et al., 2018), including a discussion surrounding AI-integrated assessment design (Williams & Ingle, 2025). To explore student perspectives on AI integration in university assessment, we report questionnaire and focus group findings on students' experiences critically evaluating AI-generated scientific output in their assessments and broader learning. Methods Case Study: Integrating AI into Coursework Assessment We present a case study of a coursework assessment, which was a component for a second year undergraduate BSc module at a leading UK university. The assessment structure is presented as an example of how AI can be integrated into the",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,5,"present a case study of a coursework assessment, which was a component for a second year undergraduate BSc module at a leading UK university. The assessment structure is presented as an example of how AI can be integrated into the structure of coursework assessment, while retaining traditional elements of scientific writing and critical evaluation. The AI-integrated coursework was also designed to allow students to engage in AI literacy competencies. Students were asked to critically evaluate a grant proposal that had previously been generated by generative AI (ChatGPT 3.5, https://openai.com/index/chatgpt/). This version of ChatGPT was the best version available at the inception of the assessment re-design. The study was undertaken in the 2023/2024 academic year. Research grant proposals are familiar documents that provide an overview of the research project’s objectives, methodology and anticipated outcomes. As such, the assessment represents a form of authentic assessment, reflecting professional practice in scientific research environments. As generative AI is becoming more widespread and accessible, it can be a useful tool to assist in scientific writing, including the grant writing process. However, it is important to",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,5,"of authentic assessment, reflecting professional practice in scientific research environments. As generative AI is becoming more widespread and accessible, it can be a useful tool to assist in scientific writing, including the grant writing process. However, it is important to consider the ethical implications and scientific accuracy of AI-generated output, and the need for critical thinking to maintain the integrity and quality of scientific research and writing. The assessment therefore was designed around learning outcomes that reflected scientific understanding, independent engagement",81
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,6,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ with the scientific literature and critical evaluation of AI generated content. The third learning outcome also represented a way to promote AI literacy proficiencies. The learning outcomes for the assessment were three-fold: 1. Describe the etiology, epidemiology, cellular, molecular and pathogenic mechanisms of a human disease. 2. Engage with and consolidate the scientific literature. 3. Critically evaluate the limitations and capabilities of ChatGPT-generated scientific content. Students were provided with coursework workshops that outlined the assessment purpose and desired learning outcomes and included a mandatory formative assessment that allowed them to practice for the summative assignment. The marking criteria are outlined in Table 1 and were designed so students could critically evaluate the full scope of the AI generated grant proposal across five domains. Across the five domains, tutors (academics responsible for teaching and supporting students on the module) were able to assess students on whether they met the three learning outcomes. Table 1 Marking criteria for module coursework assessment Criteria Scientific Accuracy Scientific Background Rationale for the Study",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,6,"(academics responsible for teaching and supporting students on the module) were able to assess students on whether they met the three learning outcomes. Table 1 Marking criteria for module coursework assessment Criteria Scientific Accuracy Scientific Background Rationale for the Study Experiential Design Outcome and Critical Evaluation Weighting 20% 20% 20% 20% 20% Institutional AI and Assessment Policy The authors’ institution has a robust AI and assessment policy. The policy is structured on a tiered traffic-light system consisting of the following three categories: Category 1 - GenAI Tools Cannot Be Used. The use of AI is strictly prohibited. The assessment evaluates skills where AI substitution is inappropriate. For context, this is the default setting for in-person proctored exams, practicals, and oral assessments. Category 2 - GenAI tools can be used in an assistive role. Students are permitted to use GenAI in a limited way. The student should still be the author of their own work and GenAI should not be used for completing the assessment (entirely or only in part) on the student’s behalf. Category 3 - GenAI has an integral",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,6,"a limited way. The student should still be the author of their own work and GenAI should not be used for completing the assessment (entirely or only in part) on the student’s behalf. Category 3 - GenAI has an integral role. GenAI is used as a primary tool during the assessment process. The assessment provides an opportunity to demonstrate effective and responsible use of GenAI. Guidance in using the relevant tools should be provided by the tutor in lectures, seminars or workshops. This includes generating initial content to be critiqued by students. For the purposes of the re-designed assessment presented in the current case study, the assessment was considered a Category 3 assessment, whereby the grant proposal was initially generated by",121
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,7,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ ChatGPT and the content critiqued by students, which was central to the assessment task. Tutors provided mandatory generative AI literacy workshops to support students throughout the module and for the summative assessment. These included an overview on the basics of AI literacy, such as limitations and capabilities of generative AI, systemic bias, hallucinations and misinformation, and responsible and ethical use of AI (e.g. validation of AI generated outputs, data security and sustainability). An additional tutor-led workshop provided an opportunity for students to engage in a formative assessment that included a mock grant proposal on an unrelated human disease and critical evaluation exercise. Questionnaire The research conducted in this study received ethical approval from UCL Research Ethics Committee (Project i.d. 6006/002). Invited participants were drawn from the same undergraduate cohort that partook in the module coursework assessment. Participants were provided with a project information sheet and asked to complete a study consent form prior to completing the questionnaire. All submitted responses were anonymized. The main research questions were: •",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,7,"cohort that partook in the module coursework assessment. Participants were provided with a project information sheet and asked to complete a study consent form prior to completing the questionnaire. All submitted responses were anonymized. The main research questions were: • RQ1. What are the students’ experiences and perceptions of using generative AI in their studies? • RQ2. What are the students’ experiences and perceptions of using generative AI in assessments? The questionnaire comprised a combination of five-point Likert scale questions and open-ended questions, to capture participant perspectives and opinions on the research themes. Five-point Likert scale questions comprised the following responses: 1) strongly disagree; 2) disagree; 3) neither agree nor disagree; 4) agree; 5) strongly agree. The full questionnaire can be found in the supplementary material (Appendix A). Statistical Analysis For the analysis of questionnaire responses to the Likert scale questions, a combination of descriptive statistics and One-Sample Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test analysis was performed using SPSS software (IBM). The mid-point response (neither agree nor disagree) was used to infer response divergence. The median and mode were calculated for each",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,7,"questions, a combination of descriptive statistics and One-Sample Wilcoxon Signed Rank Test analysis was performed using SPSS software (IBM). The mid-point response (neither agree nor disagree) was used to infer response divergence. The median and mode were calculated for each response, as this represented ordinal data, and the distribution of observations displayed as stacked bar charts. Binomial responses were analyzed using One-Sample Binomial Test and a Chi-squared test. A p-value < 0.05 was considered to be statistically significant. Focus Group The focus group received ethical approval from UCL Research Ethics Committee (Project i.d. 6006/002). Participants were provided with a project information sheet and asked to complete a study consent form prior to taking part. Participants were provided with an anonymous identifier so that they could not be identifiable in the transcription. The focus group included a third research question: • RQ3. What are the student perceptions on the broader implications of generative AI?",153
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,8,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ The focus group was led by the authors and began with an outline of the project, the aims of the research and an outline of the instructions for participants. Participants were then asked to discuss several predetermined questions about their experiences and perceptions of using AI in their assessment and studies, which provided a framework for the focus group. The focus group was undertaken online and the audio and video recorded using Microsoft Teams. The recording was stored securely on an institutional server and only the authors were allowed access. An anonymized transcription of the audio recording was made, reviewed and edited for accuracy by the authors. The audio and video recording was then destroyed to ensure General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and institutional data security compliance. Thematic Analysis Inductive thematic analysis of the transcript was undertaken according to the methods of Braun and Clarke (2006) and the guidelines according to the Association for Medical Education in Europe (AMEE) (Braun & Clarke, 2006; Kiger & Varpio, 2020). Open",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,8,"Analysis Inductive thematic analysis of the transcript was undertaken according to the methods of Braun and Clarke (2006) and the guidelines according to the Association for Medical Education in Europe (AMEE) (Braun & Clarke, 2006; Kiger & Varpio, 2020). Open text responses to questionnaire questions were analyzed for keywords (codes) and organized into emergent themes. Coded text was grouped and developed into themes using NVivo software by grouping similar phrases or repeated concepts to form coherent themes (Appendix B). A summary for each theme is provided. For the focus group analysis, separate thematic analysis was performed on each of the research questions, to identify data codes. Coded text was grouped and developed into themes using NVivo software (Appendix C). Codes were then reviewed independently to ensure that they fit coherently into the themes. In this way, thematic analysis was systematically applied to the text, ensuring a robust and reliable identification of codes and emergent themes. A summary of each theme is provided. Codes were used to identify and annotate the transcript and relevant quotations from participants were extracted from the",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,8,"applied to the text, ensuring a robust and reliable identification of codes and emergent themes. A summary of each theme is provided. Codes were used to identify and annotate the transcript and relevant quotations from participants were extracted from the text. Evaluation of Student Outcomes To examine whether the introduction of a redesigned coursework assessment incorporating generative AI had any deleterious effects on student outcomes, a retrospective comparative analysis of module performance data was conducted. This analysis therefore included a fourth research question: • RQ4. Did the introduction of a redesigned coursework assessment incorporating a generative AI component have any deleterious effect on student academic outcomes? Student achievement data were collected from four academic years spanning two years before and two years after implementation of the redesigned assessment. The pre-intervention period comprised the 2021/2022 and 2022/2023 academic years, during which the original assessment design was used. The post-intervention period comprised the 2023/2024 and 2024/2025 academic years, following implementation of the redesigned coursework assessment that included a generative AI component. For each academic year, cohort size (N), mean module mark (%),",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,8,"the original assessment design was used. The post-intervention period comprised the 2023/2024 and 2024/2025 academic years, following implementation of the redesigned coursework assessment that included a generative AI component. For each academic year, cohort size (N), mean module mark (%), score range (%), and standard deviation (%) were extracted from institutional assessment records. A Cohen’s d test and Welch t-test were performed to evaluate differences in student outcome and performance pre- and post- intervention.",74
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,9,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Results The study aimed to explore the student perspective on incorporating generative AI (ChatGPT) into coursework assessments. A mixed methods approach was used comprising a questionnaire and a structured focus group. The questionnaire covered student familiarity and comfort with generative AI, perceived educational value, ethical considerations and views on academic misconduct (RQ1 and RQ2). The focus group further explored student experiences and perceptions of generative AI in their studies and assessments and included a broader discussion on the wider implications of AI (RQ3). Questionnaire and Focus Group Characteristics Participants were second year undergraduate students studying a module in which the coursework assessment was redesigned to incorporate an element of ChatGPT. Students were tasked with critically evaluating the scientific output generated by ChatGPT. Out of 230 students enrolled in the module, 30 completed and returned the questionnaire (13% response rate). This included a combination of UK and international students (Table 2). The focus group enlisted seven participants from the pool of those who completed the questionnaire. Table 2 Questionnaire",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,9,"the module, 30 completed and returned the questionnaire (13% response rate). This included a combination of UK and international students (Table 2). The focus group enlisted seven participants from the pool of those who completed the questionnaire. Table 2 Questionnaire and focus group characteristics (n = 30) Data Point Number Percent Number of questionnaire respondents 30 13% UK students 12 40% International students 18 60% English first language 16 53.3% English not first language 14 46.7% Number of focus group participants 7 23.3% Student Familiarity with Generative AI The questionnaire was first designed to ascertain the level of familiarity and comfort that students consider they have regarding the use of generative AI. The majority of questionnaire participants either agreed (53.3%) or strongly agreed (13.3%) that they were familiar with using generative AI tools (Figure 1 and Table 3). Of the remaining participants, none strongly disagreed and few disagreed (10%), with the remaining neither agreeing nor disagreeing (23.3%). The overall response therefore suggested that most participants were familiar with using generative AI tools (p < 0.001). When asked whether they felt",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,9,"remaining participants, none strongly disagreed and few disagreed (10%), with the remaining neither agreeing nor disagreeing (23.3%). The overall response therefore suggested that most participants were familiar with using generative AI tools (p < 0.001). When asked whether they felt comfortable using generative AI tools (Figure 1 and Table 3), the majority reported that they either agreed (43.3%) or strongly agreed (13.3%), with none strongly disagreeing and few disagreeing (6.7%). The remaining participants neither agreed nor disagreed (36.7%). The overall response therefore suggested that the majority of participants were comfortable with using generative AI tools (p < 0.001).",98
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,10,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Figure 1 Student familiarity with and perceptions of generative AI at university Table 3 Student Familiarity with Generative AI Item Median Mode One-sample Wilcoxon signed ranked test I am familiar with using generative AI technologies 4 (agree) 4 (agree) p < 0.001 I am comfortable using generative AI technologies 4 (agree) 4 (agree) p < 0.001 I consider generative AI to be beneficial to my studies 4 (agree) 4 (agree) p < 0.001 Learning about generative AI technologies at university will help me in my future career 4 (agree) 4 (agree) p < 0.001 Student perceptions of AI in study The next question was designed to ascertain the extent to which students perceived generative AI to be useful in their studies. Most participants either agreed (53.3%) or strongly agreed (10%) that using generative AI tools was beneficial for their studies (Figure 1 and Table 3). None of the participants strongly disagreed, with the remaining who disagreed (13.3%) or neither agreed nor disagreed (23.3%). Overall, the results indicated that",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,10,"agreed (10%) that using generative AI tools was beneficial for their studies (Figure 1 and Table 3). None of the participants strongly disagreed, with the remaining who disagreed (13.3%) or neither agreed nor disagreed (23.3%). Overall, the results indicated that most participants thought that generative AI tools were beneficial for their studies (p < 0.01). In a follow-up questions participants were asked whether they considered learning about generative AI tools in their studies would be beneficial for their future careers (Figure 1 and Table 3). The majority of participants agreed (43.3%) or strongly agreed (33.3%), with none",97
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,11,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ strongly disagreeing, only 6.7% disagreeing and the remaining neither agreeing nor disagreeing (16.7%). Therefore, the results strongly indicated that students agreed that learning about generative AI tools would be beneficial for their future careers (p < 0.001). Student Perceptions of Generative AI in Assessments As the study was conceived following the redesign of a coursework assessment component, an important aspect of the study was to ascertain student perceptions on incorporating generative AI into the assessment structure, whereby a grant proposal was initially generated by ChatGPT and the content subsequently critiqued by students. In response to the statement, incorporating generative AI into assessments helps me learn how to use generative AI, 40% or participants agreed and 23.3% strongly agreed (Figure 2 and Table 4). Of the remaining responses, 13.3% strongly disagreed, 10% disagreed, and 13.3% neither agreed nor disagreed. Therefore, while the majority of respondents agreed that incorporating generative AI into assessments helps participants the overall response was varied (p > 0.05). However, when asked whether generative AI should",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,11,"strongly disagreed, 10% disagreed, and 13.3% neither agreed nor disagreed. Therefore, while the majority of respondents agreed that incorporating generative AI into assessments helps participants the overall response was varied (p > 0.05). However, when asked whether generative AI should be incorporated into all forms of coursework assessment, the response was more definitive (Figure 2 and Table 4). Most participants either strongly disagreed (33.3%) or disagreed (53.3%), compared to only 3.3% that agreed or 3.3.% that strongly agreed (the remaining 6.7% neither agreed nor disagreed). Therefore, this suggests that most respondents did not consider that generative AI should be incorporated into all forms of coursework assessment (p < 0.001). When asked whether participants were comfortable using generative AI technologies to help with assessments, the overall response was mixed (p > 0.05). With 13.3% strongly disagreeing, 20% disagreeing, 36.7% agreeing and 6.7% strongly agreeing (Figure 2 and Table 4). The remining respondents neither agreed nor disagreed (23.3%). Therefore, these results indicate that participants had varying levels of comfort using generative AI in coursework assessments (p > 0.05). Figure 2 Student Perceptions",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,11,"6.7% strongly agreeing (Figure 2 and Table 4). The remining respondents neither agreed nor disagreed (23.3%). Therefore, these results indicate that participants had varying levels of comfort using generative AI in coursework assessments (p > 0.05). Figure 2 Student Perceptions of Generative AI in Assessments",45
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,12,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Student Views on Plagiarism The last section of the questionnaire aimed to ascertain student views on whether they consider AI use to be a form of plagiarism and whether AI should be prohibited from assessments. In response to the question, I consider using generative AI technologies when writing my assessments as a form of plagiarism, the results indicated that participants had a varied opinion (Figure 2 and Table 4; p > 0.05). Most participants neither agreed nor disagreed (33.3%), with 3.3% strongly disagreeing and 23.3% disagreeing. Only 26% of participants agreed and 13.3% strongly agreed that using generative AI technologies when writing assessments is a form of plagiarism. When asked whether students should be prohibited from using generative AI technologies to aid their assessments, the majority of students disagreed (46.7%), with 16.7% participants strongly disagreeing (Figure 2 and Table 4). Of the remaining participants, 20% neither agreed nor disagreed, 13.3% agreed and only 3.3% strongly agreed. Therefore, the overall sentiment suggests that participants did not think that students",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,12,"disagreed (46.7%), with 16.7% participants strongly disagreeing (Figure 2 and Table 4). Of the remaining participants, 20% neither agreed nor disagreed, 13.3% agreed and only 3.3% strongly agreed. Therefore, the overall sentiment suggests that participants did not think that students should be prohibited from using generative AI in their assessments (p < 0.01). Table 4 Student Perceptions of Generative AI in Assessments Item Median Mode One-sample Wilcoxon signed ranked test Incorporating generative AI into assessments helps me learn how to use generative AI 4 (agree) 4 (agree) p > 0.05 Generative AI should be incorporated into all forms of coursework assessment 2 (disagree) 2 (disagree) p < 0.001 I am comfortable using generative AI technologies to help me with my assessments. 3 (neutral) 4 (agree) p > 0.05 I consider using generative AI technologies when writing my assessment as a form of plagiarism 3 (neutral) 3 (neutral) p > 0.05 Students should be prohibited from using generative AI technologies to aid their assessments 2 (disagree) 2 (disagree) p < 0.01 Overall Student Use of AI in Studies The final closed",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,12,"form of plagiarism 3 (neutral) 3 (neutral) p > 0.05 Students should be prohibited from using generative AI technologies to aid their assessments 2 (disagree) 2 (disagree) p < 0.01 Overall Student Use of AI in Studies The final closed question simply asked whether students had ever used generative AI in their studies. Most participants responded yes (93.3%), indicting the widespread use of generative AI among the participants sampled (p < 0.0001). This result is aligned with other recent studies on student engagement with generative AI (Stephenson & Armstrong, 2026). Benefits and Challenges of Using Generative AI Further analysis of open questions indicated that users predominantly value AI for its ability to save time, simplify complex concepts, correct grammar, enhance professional writing, generate ideas and assist with research (Table 5 and Appendix B). These sentiments allude to the use of generative in positively supporting the coursework assessment process.",148
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,13,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Table 5 Benefits of Using Generative AI in Coursework Assessments Theme Summary Time saving and efficiency Phrases like ""saving time,"" ""time saving,"" ""reduces individual research time significantly,"" and ""quicker to complete the coursework"" reflect a strong theme of efficiency and time-saving benefits. Clarification and simplification Concepts such as ""explaining it in simpler terms,"" ""simpler language,"" ""clarification of instructions,"" and ""understanding concepts"" show a recurring theme of making complex information more understandable. Grammar, writing and sentence structure Frequent mentions of ""checking grammar,"" ""sentence structure,"" and ""word choice"" indicate that grammar correction and sentence structuring are key uses. References to ""make it sound more professional,"" ""formally,"" and ""comprehensible way"" indicate a focus on improving the professionalism and readability of writing. Idea generation and summarization Common references to ""generate ideas,"" ""summarize the concepts,"" ""summarizing research papers,"" and ""quick summary of essays"" highlight a theme of using AI for idea generation and summarizing information. Research Assistance Terms like ""research,"" ""literature research,"" ""relevant published paper,"" and ""research papers"" suggest that AI is frequently",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,13,"the concepts,"" ""summarizing research papers,"" and ""quick summary of essays"" highlight a theme of using AI for idea generation and summarizing information. Research Assistance Terms like ""research,"" ""literature research,"" ""relevant published paper,"" and ""research papers"" suggest that AI is frequently used to aid in research processes. Help with Coursework Statements about ""completing the coursework,"" ""providing areas to look into further,"" and ""helping topics that you don't understand"" show that AI is often used to support academic work. Thematic analysis of responses regarding the challenges associated with using generative AI in coursework assessment indicate that users predominantly had concerns about the accuracy, reliability, outdated information, biases, the lack of reliable references and potential for misleading information from AI generated output (Table 6). In addition, respondents worried about the temptation to misuse AI for academic purposes, the implications for plagiarism and academic integrity and raised concerns regarding inequality of access and performance issues. Table 6 Challenges of Using Generative AI in Coursework Assessments Theme Summary Accuracy and reliability Phrases like ""not always accurate,"" ""can be inaccurate or misleading,"" ""provides some incorrect answers,""",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,13,"integrity and raised concerns regarding inequality of access and performance issues. Table 6 Challenges of Using Generative AI in Coursework Assessments Theme Summary Accuracy and reliability Phrases like ""not always accurate,"" ""can be inaccurate or misleading,"" ""provides some incorrect answers,"" and ""accuracy of information not good"" indicate a strong concern about the accuracy and reliability of AI-generated content. Concepts such as ""misleading,"" ""false information,"" ""fake information,"" and ""gaps in information"" highlight the issue of AI potentially providing incorrect or deceptive information. Lack of references Frequent mentions of ""not able to provide references,"" ""no references with the info provided,"" and ""cannot provide beneficial references"" show a recurring theme of AI's inability to provide verifiable sources. Common references to ""lack of most recent information,"" ""outdated,"" and ""not up-to-date"" suggest that AI may often provide outdated information.",134
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,14,"INTEGRATING GENERATIVE AI INTO UNIVERSITY COURSEWORK ASSESSMENTS – A STUDENT PERSPECTIVE _____________________________________________________________________________________________________ Theme Summary Dependence and temptation Statements like ""tempted to just ask the question,"" ""tempting to use generative AI technologies for coursework,"" and ""making us lazier"" reflect concerns about dependence on AI and the temptation to use it for academic tasks. Plagiarism and cheating Terms like ""plagiarism,"" ""cheating,"" ""considered plagiarism,"" and ""preventing plagiarism"" indicate significant worries about the potential for academic dishonesty facilitated by AI. Bias and inequality Concerns about ""prone to biases,"" ""bring inequality,"" and ""representative of students' own logic"" reflect issues related to biases in AI responses and the fairness of its use in academic settings. Processing and performance Statements about ""slow internet,"" ""servers sometimes lag,"" and ""heavy traffic problem"" highlight performance-related issues with using AI. Focus Group The focus group (n=7 participants) utilized structured questions to further gain insight into student perspective of using generative AI in university studies and assessment (RQ1 and RQ2). Focus group responses to structured questions were analyzed by thematic analysis (Appendix C). The analysis highlighted the varied experiences and uses of",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,14,"further gain insight into student perspective of using generative AI in university studies and assessment (RQ1 and RQ2). Focus group responses to structured questions were analyzed by thematic analysis (Appendix C). The analysis highlighted the varied experiences and uses of ChatGPT among university students, encompassing its role as a summarizing tool, language aid, revision resource and an assistant for assessments. The need for careful verification and effective prompting was a common theme (Table 7), reflecting both the benefits and challenges of integrating AI into academic routines. Table 7 What do Students use Generative AI for at University? Theme Summary Example Responses Generative AI for student study support Useful as a summary tool to condense large amounts of information and to compare similar concepts. Useful for quick definitions and summaries, reducing the need to sift through extensive sources. “… if I have lots of stuff to read through, but I only have limited amount of time, I'll probably ask ChatGPT to summarize it for me.” “I tend to use ChatGPT when I'm trying to delve into specific definitions, and this may",180
DOC_070,164293-integrating-generative-ai-into-university-coursework-assessments-a-student-perspective.pdf,10.61669/001c.164293,14,"have lots of stuff to read through, but I only have limited amount of time, I'll probably ask ChatGPT to summarize it for me.” “I tend to use ChatGPT when I'm trying to delve into specific definitions, and this may be if I've misunderstood the concept in class, so it's quite helpful because it's very direct compared to making a Google search.” Generative AI for assessment support Useful as a starting point for assessments, and as a starting point for deeper research. Useful to explain MCQ answers and correct misunderstandings. Concerns about its limitations with regard to detail and specific higher education content. “With regards to assessments, I've generally used it as a good gauge of what I need to start.” “You can prompt it to give you references and that can be a useful starting point of where you want to build your search. You could use ChatGPT as initial essay and then research around it to help you improve your evidence.”",163
