NEW07,NEW07.pdf,10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002,13,"nascent technology. To explore how well their model reflected reality, students then completed an experimental lab session and compared the data obtained to their model in the post-lab work. As described herein, the model (which used literature values for discharge coefficient) pro­ vided a reasonably good prediction of the experimental data, especially for a tank with a larger geometry. As GAI, and LLMs in particular, are only just being incorporated into higher education curricular, pre- and post-lab surveys were conducted in order to gain insight into students’ experiences and perceptions of using this new technology. By carefully analysing the output, comparing this with their own model derived from first principles, and consulting relevant lecture material and published literature, it was hoped that students would meet three distinct LOs. Most people acknowledged that there was a clear need to critically analyse the output, and indeed a significant proportion thought that they already knew how. However, correlating these responses with students’ marks for that section of the coursework showed that, often, students were not able to demonstrate this critical analysis ability at",180
NEW07,NEW07.pdf,10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002,13,"the output, and indeed a significant proportion thought that they already knew how. However, correlating these responses with students’ marks for that section of the coursework showed that, often, students were not able to demonstrate this critical analysis ability at a 2(i) or First class standard. Following the lab session and feedback on the pre-lab work, students generally thought that this teaching activity had improved their understanding of how to use GAI and how to use it ethically (LO1), along with skills in critiquing the output (LO2) and identifying improvements (LO3). However, to confirm whether these outcomes had actually been met, a follow-up AI- focussed assignment would need to be completed. Reflexive thematic analysis was applied to the qualitative data ob­ tained from the free text comments in the pre- and post-lab surveys. This revealed eight distinct themes which generally confirmed the quanti­ tative survey data, namely that students generally had limited prior experience of using GAI, that there were limited intentions with regards to its’ future use, and that there was a need to critique the output. However, it",180
NEW07,NEW07.pdf,10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002,13,"confirmed the quanti­ tative survey data, namely that students generally had limited prior experience of using GAI, that there were limited intentions with regards to its’ future use, and that there was a need to critique the output. However, it also offered a richness beyond the quantitative data and revealed that students were aware of specific pitfalls such as this LLM’s ignorance of mathematical logic and need for high-quality input prompts. As GAI, including LLMs, continue to gain prevalence in both edu­ cation and workplace settings, it is essential that future graduates are equipped with the knowledge and skills to use it ethically, responsibly, and safely. It is hoped that the lab session described herein provides a valuable case study, exampling how chemical engineering curricula could be augmented to expose students to LLMs. While this is the re­ sponsibility of educators, it is the responsibility of students to develop the critical analysis skills to ensure that they are fully literate in future AI-enabled environments. Engagement with practical, problem-based teaching activities such as this provide the opportunity for students to develop",180
NEW07,NEW07.pdf,10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002,13,"educators, it is the responsibility of students to develop the critical analysis skills to ensure that they are fully literate in future AI-enabled environments. Engagement with practical, problem-based teaching activities such as this provide the opportunity for students to develop these skills. Author Contributions This article has been prepared in whole by the authors Drs. Matthew Keith, Christopher Windows-Yule, Iain Kings, Philip Robbins, and Ms. Eleanor Keiller. Conceptualization, Drs. Matthew Keith and Christopher Windows-Yule; Methodology, Drs. Matthew Keith and Christopher Windows-Yule; Validation, Drs. Iain Kings and Philip Robbins; Investi­ gation, Dr. Matthew Keith; Resources, Drs. Matthew Keith, Christopher Windows-Yule, and Philip Robbins; Formal analysis, Dr. Matthew Keith and Ms Eleanor Keiller; Visualisation, Dr. Matthew Keith; Writing – Original Draft, Dr. Matthew Keith; Writing – Review & Editing, Drs. Christopher Windows-Yule, Iain Kings, Philip Robbins and Ms. Eleanor Keiller; Supervision, Dr. Philip Robbins. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. Funding This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Declaration of Competing Interest The authors",180
NEW07,NEW07.pdf,10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002,13,"Philip Robbins. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. Funding This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Appendix A. Supporting information Supplementary data associated with this article can be found in the online version at doi:10.1016/j.ece.2025.01.002. Data availability Additional surve",87
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,1,"An empirical study of ChatGPT use in engineering education: Prompting and performance☆ Lauren Genith Isaza Dominguez a,b,*, Antonio Robles-G´omez c, Rafael Pastor-Vargas c a Programa de Doctorado en Tecnologías Industriales, Escuela Internacional de Doctorado UNED (EIDUNED), Universidad Nacional de Educaci´on a Distancia (UNED), 28015 Madrid, Spain b Facultad de Ciencias B´asicas e Ingeniería, Universidad de los Llanos UNILLANOS, Villavicencio 500017, Colombia c Escuela T´ecnica Superior de Ingeniería Inform´atica (ETSI Inform´atica), Universidad Nacional de Educaci´on a Distancia (UNED), 28015 Madrid, Spain A R T I C L E I N F O Dataset link: An Empirical Study of ChatGPT-4o Use in Engineering Education: Prompting and Performance (Original data), An-Empirical-Research-Study-of-ChatGPT-4o- Use-in-Engineering-Education (Original data) Keywords: AI ChatGPT Prompt engineering Engineering education Academic performance Learning analytics Human–AI interaction A B S T R A C T This study investigates how specific prompting behaviors influence the academic performance of engineering students using ChatGPT. A total of 128 fourth-year students across four engineering programs participated in weekly sessions involving rotating task types: case analysis, engineering design, multi-step problem-solving, and experimental data analysis. A Python-based interface connected",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,1,"the academic performance of engineering students using ChatGPT. A total of 128 fourth-year students across four engineering programs participated in weekly sessions involving rotating task types: case analysis, engineering design, multi-step problem-solving, and experimental data analysis. A Python-based interface connected students to ChatGPT-4o via the API, logging all interactions and implementing stratified randomization of AI access across the 16-week semester. Written as­ signments were used for both grading and analyzing AI content integration. Ten metrics were designed to capture student behaviors—eight focusing on prompting (AI Query Count, Query Depth and Structure, Query Efficiency, Prompt Refinement Depth, Response Utility, Response Complexity, Response Reliance, and AI-Driven Problem- Solving) and two assessing writing quality (Structural Complexity Score and Content Richness and Information Density Score). Inter-metric analysis examined how these behaviors related to one another and to academic performance. Results showed that AI Query Efficiency, which reflects how effectively students crafted clear, well- structured prompts, and AI-Driven Problem-Solving, which captures how strategically they integrated AI output into their reasoning, were the strongest predictors of academic success. These findings were supported by Mann–Whitney U tests",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,1,"Efficiency, which reflects how effectively students crafted clear, well- structured prompts, and AI-Driven Problem-Solving, which captures how strategically they integrated AI output into their reasoning, were the strongest predictors of academic success. These findings were supported by Mann–Whitney U tests comparing AI and non-AI groups, as well as Spearman correlations, Random Forest re­ gressors, partial dependence plots, principal component analysis, and mixed-effects modeling, and remained significant even after accounting for students' cumulative GPA. The results suggest that prompting strategy plays a meaningful role in shaping how effectively students use AI in engineering education. 1. Introduction 1.1. Context and rationale Since 2022, ChatGPT has attracted widespread scholarly attention across higher education (Ma, 2025). In engineering education, students increasingly use Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools such as ChatGPT to support mathematical problem-solving, programming, laboratory anal­ ysis, technical writing, and iterative design-oriented learning tasks (Ghosh, 2024). Despite this growing integration, current research on ChatGPT within the discipline relies largely on self-reported perceptions rather than observable behavioral evidence (Qadir, 2023). Existing studies frequently examine attitudes, satisfaction, and perceived usefulness instead of examining how students interact",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,1,"(Ghosh, 2024). Despite this growing integration, current research on ChatGPT within the discipline relies largely on self-reported perceptions rather than observable behavioral evidence (Qadir, 2023). Existing studies frequently examine attitudes, satisfaction, and perceived usefulness instead of examining how students interact with ChatGPT during academic tasks, including how they prompt AI systems, refine generated responses, or integrate AI-generated content into academic work (Haindl & Weinberger, 2024; Sajawal & Kittur, 2024). Experi­ mental and task-based studies have similarly focused on isolated as­ signments or short-term activities, providing limited visibility into sustained interaction patterns across learning contexts (Nikolic et al., 2023; Shoufan, 2023b). Consequently, there remains limited evidence of whether AI-assisted learning contributes to measurable differences in academic achievement and engagement (Ritchie & Warren, 2024; Sawalha, Taj, & Shoufan, 2024; Shuhaiber, Kuhail, & Salman, 2024; Tsai, Ong, & Chen, 2023). Understanding these interaction patterns is also important because ☆This article is part of a Special issue entitled: ‘Learning Analytics’ published in The Internet and Higher Education. * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: lisaza@unillanos.edu.co (L.G. Isaza Dominguez), arobles@scc.uned.es (A. Robles-G´omez), rpastor@scc.uned.es (R. Pastor-Vargas). Contents lists",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,1,"patterns is also important because ☆This article is part of a Special issue entitled: ‘Learning Analytics’ published in The Internet and Higher Education. * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: lisaza@unillanos.edu.co (L.G. Isaza Dominguez), arobles@scc.uned.es (A. Robles-G´omez), rpastor@scc.uned.es (R. Pastor-Vargas). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect The Internet and Higher Education journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/iheduc https://doi.org/10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105 Received 2 August 2025; Received in revised form 26 May 2026; Accepted 20 July 2026 The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 Available online 23 July 2026 1096-7516/© 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. This is an open access article under the CC BY license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ).",102
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"prompting behaviors may reflect underlying self-regulatory and cogni­ tive engagement processes associated with AI-assisted learning. Recent studies suggest that iterative, reflective, and analytical prompting stra­ tegies may be associated with improved academic outcomes (Güner & Er, 2025; Kock, Salinas-Hern´andez, & Pepin, 2025), whereas superfi­ cial or copy-based AI use may hinder deeper engagement with learning tasks (Banerjee, Srivastava, Adjeroh, et al., 2023; Lo, Hew, & Jong, 2024). Scholars have therefore called for theory-driven, longitudinal, and behavior-based investigations capable of examining the cognitive and metacognitive dimensions of AI use in classroom settings (Holstein, Holstein, & Aleven, 2024; Jeon, Shin, & Rosenberg, 2024). Nevertheless, longitudinal evidence examining how prompting, refinement, and AI- response integration behaviors relate to cognitive and self-regulatory engagement processes remains limited within engineering learning en­ vironments, where academic tasks frequently depend on iterative reasoning, mathematical problem-solving, and conceptual development (Diyab, Frost, Fedoruk, & Diyab, 2025; Ghosh, 2024; Lee, Zhao, & Martin, 2024). To address these gaps in the literature, this study develops and val­ idates a set of behavioral AI interaction metrics designed to capture patterns of prompting, response",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"(Diyab, Frost, Fedoruk, & Diyab, 2025; Ghosh, 2024; Lee, Zhao, & Martin, 2024). To address these gaps in the literature, this study develops and val­ idates a set of behavioral AI interaction metrics designed to capture patterns of prompting, response refinement, and AI-response integration during academic and laboratory tasks. By examining these interaction patterns across a semester-long educational context, the study seeks to provide deeper insight into AI-assisted engagement and support more effective, evidence-based integration of AI systems within engineering education. Methodologically, the study focuses on the development and vali­ dation of behavioral interaction metrics, while educationally it seeks to examine how these interaction patterns relate to academic performance, engagement, and AI-assisted academic behaviors. Data were collected using an adapted logging interface based on a prior version developed by the authors (Isaza Domínguez, Velasquez Clavijo, Robles-G´omez, & Pastor-Vargas, 2024), enabling detailed behavioral analysis of stu­ dent–AI interactions across a university semester. The study additionally employed a triangulated analytical framework integrating statistical modeling, machine-learning analyses, and qualitative thematic valida­ tion to examine relationships between prompting behaviors, AI-assisted interaction patterns, and academic",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"detailed behavioral analysis of stu­ dent–AI interactions across a university semester. The study additionally employed a triangulated analytical framework integrating statistical modeling, machine-learning analyses, and qualitative thematic valida­ tion to examine relationships between prompting behaviors, AI-assisted interaction patterns, and academic performance outcomes. 1.2. Theoretical framework and objectives The study examined student performance through graded written assignments completed during tutorial and laboratory sessions across a semester-long randomized investigation involving engineering students from four courses: Advanced Software Engineering, Intelligent Control Systems, Process Optimization, and Environmental Impact Assessment. To expose students to varied cognitive and disciplinary demands, graded written assignments were structured around a rotating four-week task cycle consisting of Case Study Analysis, Design Reports, Multi-Step Problem Solving, and Data Analysis activities. These assignment types were selected because they represent complementary forms of engi­ neering reasoning, technical communication, analytical interpretation, and procedural problem-solving commonly used within engineering education. Case Study Analysis activities were grounded in construc­ tivist and situated learning theory (Johri & Olds, 2011; Kantar, 2013), requiring students to interpret engineering scenarios and justify analytical or design decisions within contextualized problem settings.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"commonly used within engineering education. Case Study Analysis activities were grounded in construc­ tivist and situated learning theory (Johri & Olds, 2011; Kantar, 2013), requiring students to interpret engineering scenarios and justify analytical or design decisions within contextualized problem settings. Design Report activities were informed by design- and problem-based learning principles (G´omez Puente, van Eijck, & Jochems, 2013), emphasizing iterative reasoning, constraint evaluation, technical communication, and solution development. Multi-Step Problem-Solving activities were aligned with cognitive apprenticeship frameworks (Moseley & McCord, 2014), guiding students through structured analytical reasoning processes involving procedural decomposition and stepwise problem articulation. Data Analysis activities were framed within inquiry-based learning and quantitative reasoning approaches (Chen & Chen, 2025; Roohr, Graf, & Liu, 2014), requiring students to interpret numerical evidence and justify conclusions through empirical analysis. Guided by this educational and task-based study design, the analysis examined two complementary dimensions of AI-assisted academic per­ formance. The first focused on characteristics of students' written aca­ demic work through writing-quality metrics applied to assignment submissions. The second focused on behavioral interaction patterns derived from student–AI exchanges recorded during assignment completion.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"complementary dimensions of AI-assisted academic per­ formance. The first focused on characteristics of students' written aca­ demic work through writing-quality metrics applied to assignment submissions. The second focused on behavioral interaction patterns derived from student–AI exchanges recorded during assignment completion. Together, these analytical components were used to examine how prompting behaviors, writing characteristics, and AI- assisted interaction patterns related to observed academic perfor­ mance outcomes across the evaluated engineering activities. Writing quality was evaluated using two complementary metrics: Structural Complexity and Content Richness and Information Density. Together, these metrics examined linguistic sophistication, information density, syntactic organization, and cognitive elaboration within stu­ dents' written academic work across AI-assisted and non-AI learning conditions. From a theoretical perspective, Structural Complexity re­ flects aspects of higher-order reasoning and metacognitive organization during written expression (Torrance, Thomas, & Robinson, 2010), whereas Content Richness and Information Density reflects aspects of cognitive elaboration and academic discourse sophistication through lexically rich and information-dense language use (Biber, 2006). These measures were included to examine whether AI-assisted writing was associated with differences in the quality and organization of written academic work.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"reflects aspects of cognitive elaboration and academic discourse sophistication through lexically rich and information-dense language use (Biber, 2006). These measures were included to examine whether AI-assisted writing was associated with differences in the quality and organization of written academic work. It is important to note that assignment scores represented perfor­ mance outcomes on engineering tasks completed during tutorial and laboratory sessions, while the writing-quality metrics evaluated char­ acteristics of students' written expression within those assignments. These measures were therefore interpreted as indicators of academic task performance rather than direct evidence of learning itself. The second component of the study focused on student–AI interac­ tion behaviors and was grounded in self-regulated learning and cognitive-engagement theory, which view learning as an active process of planning, monitoring, and evaluating one's understanding (Ng, Leung, Chu, & Qiao, 2021). Within these theoretical perspectives, be­ haviors such as prompting, response evaluation, prompt refinement, and integration of AI-generated information into written assignments and laboratory activities may reflect broader processes associated with metacognitive regulation, feedback interpretation, cognitive engage­ ment, and adaptive learning. ChatGPT's adaptive feedback may reduce extraneous",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"such as prompting, response evaluation, prompt refinement, and integration of AI-generated information into written assignments and laboratory activities may reflect broader processes associated with metacognitive regulation, feedback interpretation, cognitive engage­ ment, and adaptive learning. ChatGPT's adaptive feedback may reduce extraneous cognitive load and scaffold higher-order reasoning (Wu, Lee, Li, Huang, & Huang, 2024), although these benefits depend on delib­ erate and reflective use aligned with instructional goals (Kasneci et al., 2023; Lo et al., 2024). Accordingly, prompting behaviors with ChatGPT may serve as observable indicators of cognitive and metacognitive engagement dur­ ing AI-assisted academic tasks. In this study, behavioral interaction metrics refer to quantitative measures derived from student–AI in­ teractions, including patterns related to prompting, response refine­ ment, and integration of AI-generated content into student work. These theoretical perspectives provided the conceptual basis for interpreting observable AI-interaction behaviors during task completion. To support interpretation of these interaction patterns, the metrics were organized into three broader behavioral dimensions representing related forms of prompting, reasoning, and AI-assisted interaction. These dimensions provided a higher-level conceptual structure for the analysis, while the individual metric",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,2,"completion. To support interpretation of these interaction patterns, the metrics were organized into three broader behavioral dimensions representing related forms of prompting, reasoning, and AI-assisted interaction. These dimensions provided a higher-level conceptual structure for the analysis, while the individual metric sections later in the methodology describe the specific theoretical rationale and computational procedures underlying each metric. The first dimension, Strategic & Reasoning Behaviors, captures how students used AI to support purposeful problem-solving, cognitive effi­ ciency, and structured reasoning during academic tasks. These behav­ iors reflect strategic effort regulation, metacognitive planning, and use L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 2",106
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"of AI as a cognitive support tool during analytical and procedural tasks. The dimension is grounded in self-regulated learning, cognitive load theory, scaffolding, and strategic reasoning processes associated with effective problem-solving in engineering education. Prior research has shown that AI-supported learning environments may facilitate concep­ tual integration and structured reasoning when students actively engage with generated content and scaffolded support (Xu & Ouyang, 2022). The second dimension, Linguistic & Iterative Sophistication Behav­ iors, reflects how students refined prompts, interpreted AI-generated responses, and transformed generated information into more concep­ tually integrated academic work. These behaviors represent deeper forms of metacognitive engagement, semantic elaboration, self- monitoring, and constructive interaction with AI systems. The dimen­ sion is guided by self-regulation, depth-of-processing theory, knowledge transformation, and theories of progressive internalization within AI- supported learning environments. Prior studies have associated prompt refinement and iterative AI interaction with self-monitoring and evolving cognitive strategies during AI-assisted tasks (Chang, Lin, Hajian, & Wang, 2023; Shi, Liu, & Hu, 2025). The third dimension, Regulation & Over-Prompting Behaviors, ex­ amines patterns associated with prompting frequency, dependence on AI-generated material, and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"interaction with self-monitoring and evolving cognitive strategies during AI-assisted tasks (Chang, Lin, Hajian, & Wang, 2023; Shi, Liu, & Hu, 2025). The third dimension, Regulation & Over-Prompting Behaviors, ex­ amines patterns associated with prompting frequency, dependence on AI-generated material, and regulation of external cognitive support during academic tasks. These behaviors are connected to cognitive load management, epistemic agency, and task regulation within AI- supported educational environments. Recent studies have identified prompting frequency, dependence on generated responses, and AI reuse patterns as important dimensions of AI literacy and self-regulated engagement during academic work (Ammari, Chen, Zaman, & Garimella, 2025; Lee & Palmer, 2025). In contrast to the previous dimensions, which focus on strategic reasoning and iterative refinement, this dimension examines how stu­ dents regulated reliance on AI systems during task completion. High prompting frequency or excessive reuse of generated content may indicate reduced metacognitive regulation or greater dependence on external support, whereas balanced prompting behavior and selective integration of AI-generated information may reflect more self-regulated and reflective interaction patterns. Methodologically, the study sought to develop and validate behav­ ioral interaction metrics",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"indicate reduced metacognitive regulation or greater dependence on external support, whereas balanced prompting behavior and selective integration of AI-generated information may reflect more self-regulated and reflective interaction patterns. Methodologically, the study sought to develop and validate behav­ ioral interaction metrics capable of capturing prompting patterns, response refinement behaviors, and AI-assisted interaction characteris­ tics during assignment completion. Educationally, the study examined how these interaction patterns related to academic performance out­ comes, writing-quality characteristics, and students' integration of AI- generated content into coursework. Accordingly, the study pursued the following methodological and educational objectives: 1. To develop and validate behavioral interaction metrics that capture how engineering students interact with ChatGPT during academic tasks. 2. To examine whether AI-assisted learning conditions were associated with differences in the quality and organization of students' written academic work. 3. To examine how prompting behaviors theoretically associated with self-regulation and cognitive engagement relate to variation in assignment performance. 4. To analyze how students adapt and integrate AI-generated content into engineering assignments. By examining associations between prompting behavior and aca­ demic performance outcomes on assignments, this study contributes to",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"self-regulation and cognitive engagement relate to variation in assignment performance. 4. To analyze how students adapt and integrate AI-generated content into engineering assignments. By examining associations between prompting behavior and aca­ demic performance outcomes on assignments, this study contributes to emerging frameworks for AI literacy and provides evidence to inform curriculum design that promotes effective and responsible use of generative AI in engineering education. Moreover, the strong predictive relevance of prompting behaviors within the evaluated models in this study suggests that strategic and reflective AI use may represent an important component of effective AI-supported academic practice in engineering education. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section 2 details the methodology and the development and validation of eight AI- interaction metrics; Section 3 presents the results; Section 4 discusses and interprets these results in the context of current literature on AI in education; Section 5 extends this discussion to pedagogical implications and integration into engineering curricula; and Section 6 outlines the study's limitations, directions for future research, and conclusion. 2. Methods 2.1. Overview of methodological framework This study",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"literature on AI in education; Section 5 extends this discussion to pedagogical implications and integration into engineering curricula; and Section 6 outlines the study's limitations, directions for future research, and conclusion. 2. Methods 2.1. Overview of methodological framework This study consisted of two complementary methodological compo­ nents: (1) the development and validation of writing quality and behavioral AI-interaction metrics, and (2) the experimental and analytical evaluation of these metrics within AI-assisted engineering education contexts. The following sections first describe the theoretical grounding, validation procedures, computational structure, and cate­ gorization of the developed metrics, followed by the study design, participant structure, data collection procedures, and statistical and machine-learning analyses used to evaluate relationships between prompting behaviors, academic performance, and AI-assisted academic engagement. 2.2. Metric design and multi-stage validation framework Drawing from the theoretical framework presented in Section 1.2, each metric was designed to translate theoretically grounded processes into quantifiable writing and behavior-based indicators. Individual metrics were structured and calculated through theoretically grounded subcomponents and weighting schemes reflecting the educational pro­ cesses each metric was intended to capture, with all final metric",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"designed to translate theoretically grounded processes into quantifiable writing and behavior-based indicators. Individual metrics were structured and calculated through theoretically grounded subcomponents and weighting schemes reflecting the educational pro­ cesses each metric was intended to capture, with all final metric outputs normalized to a standardized 0–100 scale to support comparability across metrics. The metric validation process is outlined below: 1. Expert Review of Components: Eight experts in engineering edu­ cation, AI literacy, and academic writing evaluated the rationale, components, and weightings using Likert-scale and open-ended feedback. When consensus emerged, weights were adjusted using the average of suggested values; otherwise, original weights were retained to balance theoretical consistency with expert feedback. The surveys are available at OSF (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a). 2. Artifact Scoring and Rater Triangulation: A sample of 30 student artifacts was independently scored by three expert raters to validate each metric. Triangulating across disciplinary domains improved interpretive validity and reduced potential rater bias (Schlunegger, Zumstein-Shaha, & Palm, 2024). Scoring rubrics were grounded in the theoretical basis of each metric and applied on 0–100 scales, following best practices in content",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"each metric. Triangulating across disciplinary domains improved interpretive validity and reduced potential rater bias (Schlunegger, Zumstein-Shaha, & Palm, 2024). Scoring rubrics were grounded in the theoretical basis of each metric and applied on 0–100 scales, following best practices in content analysis (Krippendorff, 2018) and construct validation (Rusticus, 2023). The sample size aligns with instrument development norms (Johanson & Brooks, 2010). These scores are available at OSF (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a). 3. Quantitative Validity and Reliability Checks: Construct validity for each metric was assessed through Spearman's rank correlations between expert ratings and automated metric outputs, following established validation practices in AI-supported education (Dai et al., 2024). Inter-rater reliability was evaluated using the two-way random-effects Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC[2,k]), con­ firming stable agreement across independent raters. All correlation and reliability values met or exceeded accepted benchmarks for moderate-to-high validity (ρ > 0.50) and reliability (ICC > 0.60), indicating that the automated scoring procedures aligned closely with expert judgments. A complete summary of the quantitative L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 3",175
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,3,"reliability (ICC > 0.60), indicating that the automated scoring procedures aligned closely with expert judgments. A complete summary of the quantitative L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 3",35
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"validation results, including metric-specific Spearman and ICC values, is provided in Appendix A. 5. Qualitative Thematic Validation: In the final week, a brief written survey with nine open-ended questions was administered. Questions were grouped by the three most predictive AI metrics (based on post- semester modeling). Top and bottom quartile students (n = 32 per group) were selected by average metric scores. Responses were deductively coded using binary scoring (0–1) for three predefined conceptual themes per metric. Three independent raters indepen­ dently coded all responses. ICC exceeded 0.85 across metrics, and internal consistency across themes yielded a Cronbach's alpha of 0.78. Full computational procedures, normalization methods, and imple­ mentation details are described in detail in Appendix A. Sections 2.3–2.6 describe the theoretical foundations and weighting rationale underlying each metric, while Section 2.7 details the computational procedures used for their calculation. 2.3. Writing quality metrics The writing quality metrics included the Structural Complexity Score and the Content Richness and Information Density Score, which evalu­ ated characteristics of students' written academic expression, syntactic sophistication, lexical richness, and information density across the evaluated",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"2.3. Writing quality metrics The writing quality metrics included the Structural Complexity Score and the Content Richness and Information Density Score, which evalu­ ated characteristics of students' written academic expression, syntactic sophistication, lexical richness, and information density across the evaluated engineering tasks. 2.3.1. Structural complexity score Structural Complexity captures the syntactic sophistication of stu­ dent writing by quantifying both sentence length and grammatical complexity. Syntactic elaboration is a recognized proxy for higher-order cognitive activity in academic texts (Scott & Balthazar, 2013; Beers & Nagy, 2011). From a learning theory perspective, structural complexity may reflect aspects of reasoning, metacognitive planning, and expressive capacity—particularly valuable in engineering educa­ tion, where students must articulate logical processes and layered ar­ guments (Pikulski & Templeton, 2004; Torrance et al., 2010). Accordingly, Structural Complexity helps evaluate how effectively stu­ dents organize and express technical knowledge in writing. The metric was computed by combining two weighted sub­ components: Sentence Length (40%) and Grammatical Complexity (60%). Sentence length measured the average number of words per sentence, and grammatical complexity assessed the frequency of advanced syntactic structures such as",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"writing. The metric was computed by combining two weighted sub­ components: Sentence Length (40%) and Grammatical Complexity (60%). Sentence length measured the average number of words per sentence, and grammatical complexity assessed the frequency of advanced syntactic structures such as subordinate and relative clauses. 2.3.2. Content richness and information density score Content Richness and Information Density assess how effectively students express complex ideas using lexically rich, information-dense language. It targets two core dimensions of academic writing quality: lexical diversity and lexical density—both widely recognized as in­ dicators of cognitive elaboration and expressive depth (Biber, 2006; Nasseri & Thompson, 2021; Uccelli et al., 2015). This metric integrates two subcomponents: the type–token ratio, which measures the ratio of unique word types to total tokens and re­ flects vocabulary diversity (Kettunen, 2014), and lexical density, which captures the proportion of content words—nouns, verbs, adjectives, and adverbs—relative to total word count (Mohamed & Abdallah, 2021). These features represent complementary aspects of linguistic sophisti­ cation: lexical diversity has been associated with conceptual elaboration and expressive sophistication and flexible language use, while lexical density reflects information",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"adjectives, and adverbs—relative to total word count (Mohamed & Abdallah, 2021). These features represent complementary aspects of linguistic sophisti­ cation: lexical diversity has been associated with conceptual elaboration and expressive sophistication and flexible language use, while lexical density reflects information compression and meaning concentration (Crossley & McNamara, 2012). Equal weighting of type–token ratio and lexical density was used, consistent with studies affirming their balanced theoretical value for writing quality (Johansson, 2008; Malvern, Richards, Chipere, & Dur´an, 2004). 2.4. Strategic & reasoning behaviors This dimension included the AI Query Efficiency Score and the AI- Driven Problem-Solving Score, which examined how students used AI to support strategic prompting, cognitive efficiency, structured reasoning, and problem-solving during academic tasks. 2.4.1. AI query efficiency score AI Query Efficiency measures how effectively students engaged with AI by quantifying the ratio between prompt quality and frequency. It evaluates how much cognitive and linguistic sophistication a student achieved per prompt submitted during a session. High values may reflect efficient engagement, issuing few but well-structured, strategic prompts, while low values often indicate inefficiency, such as excessive trial-and- error or",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"evaluates how much cognitive and linguistic sophistication a student achieved per prompt submitted during a session. High values may reflect efficient engagement, issuing few but well-structured, strategic prompts, while low values often indicate inefficiency, such as excessive trial-and- error or vague queries with limited precision. This metric aligns with models of self-regulated learning, particu­ larly those emphasizing resource allocation and strategic effort. Zimmerman (2002) described proficient learners as those who optimize task engagement by planning and regulating effort. In this context, such regulation is observable through efficient prompting, where fewer prompts yield greater informational value. This metric also draws on cognitive load theory, which posits that instruction should minimize unnecessary effort while maximizing task- relevant processing (Sweller, Van Merri¨enboer, & Paas, 2019). Stu­ dents with high scores may be managing extraneous load by articulating their needs clearly in fewer, more effective queries. This metric was computed as the ratio of a student's AI Query Depth & Structure Score to their total number of prompts submitted during a session. 2.4.2. AI-driven problem-solving score This metric evaluates how effectively students leveraged AI-",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"effective queries. This metric was computed as the ratio of a student's AI Query Depth & Structure Score to their total number of prompts submitted during a session. 2.4.2. AI-driven problem-solving score This metric evaluates how effectively students leveraged AI- generated content to support structured reasoning, conceptual integra­ tion, and original idea development, which have been identified as key indicators of deep problem-solving in engineering education (English, 2023). It comprises three subcomponents: Stepwise Alignment (40%), Conceptual Application (40%), and Independent Expansion (20%). Stepwise Alignment draws on scaffolding theory and cognitive apprenticeship, where students benefit from modeled reasoning pro­ cesses provided by an expert (or AI tutor). Following the AI's logic can reduce cognitive load and promote internalization of expert-like stra­ tegies (Fern´andez, Wegerif, Mercer, & Rojas-Drummond, 2015). Conceptual Application reflects schema acquisition from Cognitive Load Theory (Sweller, Paas, & Renkl, 2003). Students reuse domain- specific concepts introduced by the AI to build transferable mental models. Renkl (2014) emphasized that learning from examples is most effective when students abstract principles rather than copy surface features—a process captured by this metric. Xu",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"2003). Students reuse domain- specific concepts introduced by the AI to build transferable mental models. Renkl (2014) emphasized that learning from examples is most effective when students abstract principles rather than copy surface features—a process captured by this metric. Xu and Ouyang (2022) similarly found that AI tools enhance learning when they scaffold knowledge application and conceptual integration. Independent Expansion assesses whether students move beyond AI content through original contributions. Semantic novelty captures divergence from the AI's ideas, while syntactic complexity, indexed via clause elaboration, reflects linguistic maturity. Crossley, Muldner, and McNamara (2016) and Casal and Lee (2019) suggest that semantic elaboration and syntactic depth predict writing quality. Crossley and Kim (2022) further demonstrated that advanced academic writing evolves through increased syntactic embedding and structural control. 2.5. Linguistic & iterative sophistication behaviors This dimension included the Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Utility Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Query Depth & Structure Score, which evaluated how students refined prompts, interpreted AI-generated responses, and transformed gener­ ated information into more linguistically and conceptually sophisticated L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,4,"AI Response Utility Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Query Depth & Structure Score, which evaluated how students refined prompts, interpreted AI-generated responses, and transformed gener­ ated information into more linguistically and conceptually sophisticated L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 4",49
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"academic work. 2.5.1. Prompt refinement depth score Prompt Refinement Depth captures the extent to which students iteratively enhanced their prompts during sessions by increasing syn­ tactic complexity and semantic depth. This metric is grounded in Vygotsky and Cole's (1978) theory of progressive internalization and reflects metacognitive control within model-supported learning envi­ ronments. Prompt refinement has been closely tied to self-regulated learning (Arora et al., 2025; Chang et al., 2023), serving as an exter­ nalization of evolving cognitive strategies. Shi et al. (2025) emphasize the value of refined prompts as indicators of self-monitoring, adaptive goal management, and literacy in interacting with AI. Each measure of Prompt Refinement Depth was derived from a sequence of related prompts that demonstrated increasing complexity and elaboration. The final value was calculated by averaging weighted measures of syntactic gain and semantic evolution across all identified transitions across prompts within a session. 2.5.2. AI response utility score This metric quantifies how students incorporate AI-generated con­ tent into their writing, prioritizing originality, semantic transformation, and selective integration, three hallmarks of pedagogically sound AI use. As Stojanovi´c, Cvetkovi´c, and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"across prompts within a session. 2.5.2. AI response utility score This metric quantifies how students incorporate AI-generated con­ tent into their writing, prioritizing originality, semantic transformation, and selective integration, three hallmarks of pedagogically sound AI use. As Stojanovi´c, Cvetkovi´c, and Stankovi´c (2023) argue, meaningful engagement with generative AI involves discernment, not reproduction. The Semantic Novelty component (45%), weighted most heavily, cap­ tures how much a student's writing diverges from AI output. This focus on originality is grounded in educational theory: Wang (2024) contends that preserving one's voice in AI-assisted writing enhances creativity and learning, while Barrot (2023) warns that overreliance may reduce learning and raise plagiarism risks. Semantic novelty is further anchored in Craik and Lockhart's (1972) depth-of-processing framework, which posits that rephrasing and reconstructing ideas fosters deeper compre­ hension than copying. The Conceptual Transformation subcomponent (40%) assesses how students rephrase or reinterpret AI suggestions—an act of “constructive learning” per Chi and Wylie (2014). This aligns with the knowledge- transforming model (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 1987), where meaning­ ful reworking of input fosters higher-order thinking. Uemlianin (2000) and Thai (2021) also",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"rephrase or reinterpret AI suggestions—an act of “constructive learning” per Chi and Wylie (2014). This aligns with the knowledge- transforming model (Scardamalia & Bereiter, 1987), where meaning­ ful reworking of input fosters higher-order thinking. Uemlianin (2000) and Thai (2021) also highlight paraphrasing as a metacognitive and memory-enhancing strategy, reinforcing this component's foundations. The third subcomponent, Contribution to Final Assignment, mea­ sures selective reuse of AI-generated ideas, weighted modestly at 15% to avoid rewarding superficial similarity. It draws from source-based writing literature (Hirvela & Du, 2013) and is supported by Fangzhou, Lin, and Lai (2024), who found that students using self-regulated stra­ tegies integrated and adapted AI content more meaningfully. Ng et al. (2021) echo this view, framing ethical and selective application of generative tools as central to AI literacy. 2.5.3. AI response complexity score AI Response Complexity provides a self-reported measure of stu­ dents' perceived cognitive engagement when interpreting and applying AI-generated content. While behavioral metrics in this study assess observable interactions (e.g., prompt logs or writing samples), response complexity offers complementary insight into learners' internal pro­ cessing, specifically, how",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"measure of stu­ dents' perceived cognitive engagement when interpreting and applying AI-generated content. While behavioral metrics in this study assess observable interactions (e.g., prompt logs or writing samples), response complexity offers complementary insight into learners' internal pro­ cessing, specifically, how challenging, interpretable, and adaptable the AI responses felt. This metric is grounded in five constructs from recent literature: Semantic Interpretation Depth (Baek, Tate, & Warschauer, 2024), Rephrasing Behavior (Gerlich, 2025), Confidence in Clarity (Cau & Spano, 2025), Usefulness Complexity (Tierney, Peasey, & Gould, 2025), and Cognitive Effort (Gkintoni, Antonopoulou, Sortwell, & Halkiopoulos, 2025). These dimensions reflect core concerns in AI- integrated education: depth of understanding, adaptation difficulty, mental workload, and student trust in AI outputs. The instrument consists of 10 Likert-scale items (two per construct), administered immediately after every AI-access session. Responses were retrospective and focused on the session as a whole. A two-phase vali­ dation process supported the reliability of this metric. First, three ex­ perts in AI education reviewed each item for clarity and construct alignment. Second, internal consistency was tested using 30 responses from Week 5 (all",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"a whole. A two-phase vali­ dation process supported the reliability of this metric. First, three ex­ perts in AI education reviewed each item for clarity and construct alignment. Second, internal consistency was tested using 30 responses from Week 5 (all four engineering courses), yielding Cronbach's α = 0.81. The full survey can be found in OSF (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a). 2.5.4. AI query depth & structure score This metric was developed to evaluate the cognitive and linguistic complexity of student prompts submitted to AI. This metric serves as a behavioral proxy for self-regulated learning and cognitive engagement, grounded in Zimmerman's (2002) framework of metacognitive planning and Sweller et al.'s (2019) cognitive load theory. It also reflects insights from Schreiter and Gipp (2023), who identified syntactic diversity and logical reasoning as primary dimensions of prompt quality in technical education. High values in Query Depth and Structure reflect students' ability to organize, segment, and articulate queries that promote meaningful model responses and support deeper educational engagement. This metric was computed from three weighted subcomponents: lexical and syntactic structure (35%), multi-step problem-solving depth (35%),",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"Query Depth and Structure reflect students' ability to organize, segment, and articulate queries that promote meaningful model responses and support deeper educational engagement. This metric was computed from three weighted subcomponents: lexical and syntactic structure (35%), multi-step problem-solving depth (35%), and query focus and clarity (30%). 2.6. Regulation & Over-Prompting Behaviors This dimension included AI Query Count and the AI Response Reli­ ance Score, which evaluated prompting frequency, dependence on AI- generated content, and regulation of external cognitive support during AI-assisted academic tasks. 2.6.1. AI query count (QC) AI Query Count captures the number of distinct prompts issued by students during each session, serving as an indicator of engagement and task persistence. This behavioral metric is grounded in empirical and theoretical literature. Ammari et al. (2025), in a large-scale analysis of log data, found that university students submitted an average of 6.1 prompts per session—establishing prompt frequency as a behavioral marker of user interaction depth. Similarly, Lee and Palmer (2025) identified prompt frequency as an underexplored but important dimension of literacy and task regulation. Thus, this metric functions as a",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"of 6.1 prompts per session—establishing prompt frequency as a behavioral marker of user interaction depth. Similarly, Lee and Palmer (2025) identified prompt frequency as an underexplored but important dimension of literacy and task regulation. Thus, this metric functions as a foundational interaction metric reflect­ ing how often students engage with AI during academic tasks. 2.6.2. AI response reliance score This metric captures the extent to which students reused or inte­ grated AI-generated content into their written assignments. Developed as a behavioral proxy for epistemic agency and self-regulated learning, this metric is grounded in theories of cognitive load (Sweller et al., 2019) and instructional scaffolding (Van de Pol, Volman, & Beishuizen, 2010). High values may indicate overreliance on external tools and diminished metacognitive regulation (Azevedo, Moos, Johnson, & Chauncey, 2010; Zimmerman, 2002), whereas moderate values may reflect strategic support-seeking during task execution. This metric is a composite measure derived from five weighted subcomponents: text similarity (40%), direct copy-pasting (10%), structural similarity (20%), prompt–response consistency (20%), and query-to-submission linkage (10%). In the results, the inverse value was reported so that higher scores",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,5,"execution. This metric is a composite measure derived from five weighted subcomponents: text similarity (40%), direct copy-pasting (10%), structural similarity (20%), prompt–response consistency (20%), and query-to-submission linkage (10%). In the results, the inverse value was reported so that higher scores represent less reliance. 2.7. Computation of writing and AI interaction metrics All metric outputs were rescaled to a 0–100 range prior to statistical and machine-learning analysis, allowing for cross-metric comparability despite differences in their original computation methods. Full L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 5",92
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"computational definitions, internal normalization and implementation procedures for each metric are provided in Appendix A. The complete Python codebase for these procedures is available via Zenodo (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b). Illustrative examples of high- and low-scoring cases for each metric, with sample excerpts and explana­ tions of their scoring rationale, are presented in Appendix B. Table 1 provides a consolidated overview of the ten validated met­ rics, including their purposes, subcomponents, computational logic, and theoretical grounding; the Calculation column also indicates the location in parentheses of each metric's detailed computation in Appendix A. 2.8. Overview of the full analytical workflow Following the theoretical grounding, development, and validation of the writing quality and behavioral interaction metrics described in Sections 2.2–2.7, the study proceeded to the experimental imple­ mentation and analytical evaluation stages. First, the experimental structure included participant recruitment (Section 2.9), ethical and consent procedures (Section 2.10), the semester-long randomized experimental design (Section 2.11), and multiple bias-reduction and procedural control mechanisms (Section 2.12). Fig. 1 summarizes the complete experimental structure, including participant enrollment, randomization, task rotation, and data collection procedures across the",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"and consent procedures (Section 2.10), the semester-long randomized experimental design (Section 2.11), and multiple bias-reduction and procedural control mechanisms (Section 2.12). Fig. 1 summarizes the complete experimental structure, including participant enrollment, randomization, task rotation, and data collection procedures across the 16-week investigation. Second, all raw experimental outputs were consolidated through the multi-stage aggregation and matching procedures described in Section 2.13. Fig. 2 summarizes the complete computational pipeline from raw data collection to final analytical dataset construction, including preprocessing, integration, metric computation, and dataset export procedures. Third, the validated metrics presented in Sections 2.3–2.7 were computationally applied to the aggregated dataset to generate stan­ dardized writing-quality and behavioral interaction measurements across all AI-assisted sessions and written assignments. Fourth, the resulting analytical dataset underwent comparative sta­ tistical analysis, correlational analysis, predictive machine-learning modeling, dimensionality-reduction procedures, and mixed-effects modeling to evaluate relationships between prompting behaviors, writing quality, AI-assisted interaction patterns, and assignment per­ formance outcomes. The statistical and machine-learning procedures applied during this stage are summarized in Section 2.14 and Table 2. Finally, additional robustness, sensitivity, and diagnostic procedures were conducted to evaluate",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"prompting behaviors, writing quality, AI-assisted interaction patterns, and assignment per­ formance outcomes. The statistical and machine-learning procedures applied during this stage are summarized in Section 2.14 and Table 2. Finally, additional robustness, sensitivity, and diagnostic procedures were conducted to evaluate model stability, predictor independence, repeated-measures adequacy, and cross-validation consistency. These procedures, described in Section 2.15, included bootstrap resampling, Monte Carlo stability analyses, multicollinearity diagnostics, and repeated-measures adequacy checks to support the reliability and interpretability of the analytical results. Detailed computational procedures, preprocessing protocols, vali­ dation outputs, reproducibility information, software implementation details, and supplementary analytical materials are provided in Appendices A–E. 2.9. Participants The study was conducted over a 16-week semester (January–June 2025) at the Universidad de los Llanos. • Enrollment and Exclusion: Of 151 students initially enrolled, 16 were excluded for low attendance (below 80%) and 7 dropped out, resulting in a final sample of 128 students. • Course Distribution: Students were enrolled in four fourth year engineering courses: Advanced Software Engineering (n = 22), Intelligent Control Systems (n = 42), Process Optimization (n = 28), and Environmental Impact Assessment",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"in a final sample of 128 students. • Course Distribution: Students were enrolled in four fourth year engineering courses: Advanced Software Engineering (n = 22), Intelligent Control Systems (n = 42), Process Optimization (n = 28), and Environmental Impact Assessment (n = 36). Participation was entirely voluntary, with no academic incentives or penalties for participation or withdrawal. 2.10. Consent and investigation approval Procedures followed established ethical guidelines for minimal-risk educational research: • Informed Consent: During the first week, students signed consent forms confirming voluntary participation, withdrawal rights, and anonymization of academic work. AI interaction logging was dis­ closed, but specific prompt-response tracking was omitted to pre­ serve natural behavior, an accepted practice in minimal-risk educational research (Cohen, Manion, & Morrison, 2018). • Anonymized Identification: Each student received a random ID used for AI logins and assignment labeling. This allowed matching logs and assignments without exposing personal data. • CGPA Data Use: Separate consent to access cumulative GPA (CGPA) was obtained post-semester to avoid influencing student behavior during the study. • Ethics Approval: The study was approved by the General Direc­",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"logs and assignments without exposing personal data. • CGPA Data Use: Separate consent to access cumulative GPA (CGPA) was obtained post-semester to avoid influencing student behavior during the study. • Ethics Approval: The study was approved by the General Direc­ torate of Research, Universidad de los Llanos (Protocol 009, September 13, 2023). In accordance with the approved ethics protocol, CGPA and raw textual data (student prompts, AI responses, and written assignments) were treated as confidential educational records and were not shared publicly. Only a derived, anonymized dataset, containing assignment scores, computed AI interaction and writing-quality metrics, and contextual variables (course, week, attendance, AI access condition, and activity type), was released for reproducibility purposes, consistent with institutional data protection policies and participant consent (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a). Ethical safeguards addressed potential risks such as overreliance on AI, reduced autonomy, and diminished critical thinking. These were mitigated by restricting AI use to classroom sessions under instructor supervision, alternating AI and non-AI conditions weekly, and incor­ porating reflection-based tasks designed to promote independent reasoning and metacognitive awareness. 2.11. Experimental design The semester spanned sixteen",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"thinking. These were mitigated by restricting AI use to classroom sessions under instructor supervision, alternating AI and non-AI conditions weekly, and incor­ porating reflection-based tasks designed to promote independent reasoning and metacognitive awareness. 2.11. Experimental design The semester spanned sixteen weeks and was conducted during regularly scheduled tutorial and laboratory sessions across the four en­ gineering courses. Sessions were conducted during standard 2-h class periods. Experimental sessions followed a repeating four-week cycle consisting of Case Study Analysis, Engineering Design Reports, Multi- Step Problem Solving, and Experimental Data Analysis activities. Week 1 included both the initial AI training workshops—where students learned the interface, login procedures, and ethical prompt construction using ChatGPT-4o—and the first experimental laboratory session, marking the start of the cycle. Each week, either the tutorial or labo­ ratory session served as the experimental session, alternating weekly across the semester. During each session, professors first introduced or reviewed the relevant engineering concepts, task objectives, and pro­ cedural requirements associated with the weekly activity during an approximately 15-min instructional briefing. Students then completed graded written assignments during the remaining session time",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,6,"During each session, professors first introduced or reviewed the relevant engineering concepts, task objectives, and pro­ cedural requirements associated with the weekly activity during an approximately 15-min instructional briefing. Students then completed graded written assignments during the remaining session time under either AI-assisted or non-AI conditions. Across the semester, each task type appeared four times—twice in tutorials and twice in laboratories—ensuring balanced exposure, cognitive variety, and pro­ cedural consistency. Assignment activities were therefore embedded within the normal instructional progression of each course rather than treated as isolated experimental exercises. The experimental design is outlined below, detailing the interface configuration, logging procedures, randomization, assignment L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 6",117
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,7,"structure, grading, and task rotation. 1. Interface Setup: The interface was adapted from a prior version developed by the authors (Isaza Domínguez, Velasquez Clavijo, Robles-G´omez, & Pastor-Vargas, 2024), originally designed to sup­ port FSLSM-based learning through a survey-linked ChatGPT-3.5 interface. For this study, it was modified to log student–AI in­ teractions using the ChatGPT-4o API. 2. Model Use: All sessions used ChatGPT-4o through the OpenAI API between January and June 2025. According to OpenAI's changelog, no major model updates occurred during this period, though minor model drift is possible. Findings therefore apply specifically to this model version and timeframe. All API calls used a standardized interface configuration and identical system-level initialization across sessions. No custom fine-tuning, parameter optimization, or persistent conversational memory was used. Each interaction was processed as an independent request, and the interface logged prompts, responses, timestamps, and activity-type metadata to sup­ port subsequent behavioral analysis and reproducibility. 3. System Access and Logging: Students used only university-issued laptops; personal devices were prohibited. Prompts and responses Table 1 Summary of all metrics developed and used in this study. Metric",180
