NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"in the project's Zenodo repository (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b) and is described in detail in Appendix D.2. 5. Assignment Structure: Students completed written assignments during tutorial or laboratory sessions under either AI-assisted or non- AI conditions. After the instructional introduction and task briefing, AI and non-AI students were seated separately for assignment completion. Written responses were chosen over short-answer or multiple-choice formats to support deeper analysis of AI usage and writing patterns. 6. Grading and Controls: Students wrote their anonymous ID (also used for AI login) on each assignment. Grading was blind, performed by teaching assistants using standardized rubrics. Graders were briefed in advance. Although attendance filters and uniform scaf­ folding were used, unmeasured motivational differences may have affected outcomes. Assignments were identical across groups, and professors were instructed not to alter content between conditions. 7. Rotating Task Design: A rotating four-week task cycle was imple­ mented to ensure balanced exposure to varied cognitive and disci­ plinary demands within engineering education. The cycle consisted of four recurring activity types: Case Study Analysis, Design Reports, Multi-Step Problem Solving, and Data Analysis tasks.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"four-week task cycle was imple­ mented to ensure balanced exposure to varied cognitive and disci­ plinary demands within engineering education. The cycle consisted of four recurring activity types: Case Study Analysis, Design Reports, Multi-Step Problem Solving, and Data Analysis tasks. These activities were designed to expose students to complementary forms of reasoning, analytical decision making, procedural problem-solving, and written academic integration across AI-assisted and non-AI learning conditions. This procedure established a framework for comparing AI-assisted and non-AI learning conditions across all four engineering courses. Fig. 1 presents the study flow diagram, summarizing participant enrollment, randomization, task rotation, and data collection across the 16-week experimental structure. 2.12. Bias avoidance To preserve the validity of the experiment, multiple sources of po­ tential bias were addressed through procedural, instructional, and sta­ tistical controls. 1. Hardware and Software Controls: All students in the AI group used university-issued laptops preconfigured with the custom Python interface limited to ChatGPT-4o. Browser access was blocked, per­ sonal devices were prohibited, and mobile phones were collected before each session to prevent unauthorized AI use. This setup ensured uniform access",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"group used university-issued laptops preconfigured with the custom Python interface limited to ChatGPT-4o. Browser access was blocked, per­ sonal devices were prohibited, and mobile phones were collected before each session to prevent unauthorized AI use. This setup ensured uniform access to AI tools and aligns with equity-centered design principles that promote fairness and consistency in technology-supported learning (Reich & Ito, 2017). 2. Ethical Logging and Consent: AI interactions were automatically logged with anonymized student IDs and timestamps, saved in .txt format, and securely emailed to the research team. Although stu­ dents consented to their data being used, they were not informed that individual queries would be recorded—an ethically permissible practice in behavioral research when necessary to preserve natural behavior, provided participants are debriefed (Cohen et al., 2018). 3. Task and Instructional Equivalence: Both AI and non-AI students completed identical assignments under identical conditions. Uni­ formity in task type, workload, and instruction supports valid com­ parison and is grounded in constructivist learning theory (Jonassen, 1999). AI access was assigned using stratified randomization (Torgerson & Torgerson, 2003), yielding an equal number of",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"under identical conditions. Uni­ formity in task type, workload, and instruction supports valid com­ parison and is grounded in constructivist learning theory (Jonassen, 1999). AI access was assigned using stratified randomization (Torgerson & Torgerson, 2003), yielding an equal number of AI and non-AI sessions per student across the semester. 4. Exposure Symmetry: Session logs were monitored throughout the semester to ensure balanced AI exposure across students. When minor imbalances in the number of AI-access sessions occurred, excess entries were randomly removed post hoc to maintain sym­ metrical exposure. This approach mirrors random under-sampling techniques commonly used to reduce bias in imbalanced datasets (Fern´andez et al., 2018; Wang & Singh, 2021). 5. Grading Anonymity and Calibration: Submissions were anony­ mized, and teaching assistants used uniform rubrics without knowing condition assignment. Inter-rater reliability was checked mid-semester by double-scoring a subset of 60 assignments, with discrepancies reviewed and resolved collaboratively. Inter-rater consistency was high (Intraclass Correlation Coefficient, ICC = 0.82), indicating reliable scoring across graders. 6. Training and Attendance Controls: All students participated in two standardized AI training workshops during the first two",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"assignments, with discrepancies reviewed and resolved collaboratively. Inter-rater consistency was high (Intraclass Correlation Coefficient, ICC = 0.82), indicating reliable scoring across graders. 6. Training and Attendance Controls: All students participated in two standardized AI training workshops during the first two sessions of the semester, providing scaffolding for prompt construction and responsible use (Vygotsky & Cole, 1978; Hammond, 2001). Students with <80% attendance were excluded as a proxy for academic engagement. Surveys on prior AI use and motivation were omitted due to reliability concerns in high-integrity settings, where self- report is often inaccurate (Tossell, Tenhundfeld, Momen, Cooley, & de Visser, 2024). However, during these training sessions, it was evident that all students had prior experience using ChatGPT or similar large-language-model tools, reducing the likelihood that differences in AI familiarity acted as a confounding factor. 7. Academic Covariate Control: CGPA was included as a post hoc covariate, retrieved from institutional records after the study concluded. CGPA served as a stable proxy for prior academic per­ formance and enabled checks for baseline group differences, consistent with educational measurement theory (Brookhart, 2011). To further",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"as a post hoc covariate, retrieved from institutional records after the study concluded. CGPA served as a stable proxy for prior academic per­ formance and enabled checks for baseline group differences, consistent with educational measurement theory (Brookhart, 2011). To further assess potential sources of bias, baseline equivalence was tested between AI and non-AI groups for CGPA, attendance, and course distribution (all p > .10). Multicollinearity diagnostics indicated low variance inflation (Variance Inflation Factor < 2.0) across predictors, indicating low multicollinearity among covariates. All model estimates were re-run with bootstrapped standard errors, which yielded consistent significance patterns (standard deviation <0.02), demonstrating robustness to sampling variation and supporting the stability of the observed patterns. 2.13. Data aggregation and matching process Following the procedures implemented to minimize bias and ensure data integrity, all experimental outputs were systematically consoli­ dated through a multistep data processing pipeline that aligned AI prompts, responses, written assignments, and grading records on a per- session basis. This process enabled the computation of all writing- quality and behavioral interaction metrics across 936 AI logs and 1866 assignments. Fig. 2 summarizes",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,"processing pipeline that aligned AI prompts, responses, written assignments, and grading records on a per- session basis. This process enabled the computation of all writing- quality and behavioral interaction metrics across 936 AI logs and 1866 assignments. Fig. 2 summarizes the overall aggregation and metric-computation workflow, while detailed preprocessing, normali­ zation, and dataset-merging procedures are provided in Appendix C. Repository access, reproducibility resources, and data-sharing details are described in the Data Availability Statement. 2.14. Statistical analysis and modeling procedures To address Objective 2, assignment-level analyses were first used to compare overall performance outcomes between AI and non-AI condi­ tions. These procedures included Mann–Whitney U tests comparing Assignment Score, Structural Complexity, and Content Richness across experimental conditions. To address Objectives 3 and 4, AI-session-level analyses were then conducted to examine relationships across the complete set of AI- interaction metrics during individual AI-assisted sessions. Spearman correlation analyses evaluated monotonic associations among all 8 AI- interaction metrics and assignment performance. Additional correlation-structure analyses examined interrelationships among the AI-interaction metrics through Spearman correlation heatmaps. Random Forest modeling was subsequently conducted using all 8 AI-",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,9,AI-assisted sessions. Spearman correlation analyses evaluated monotonic associations among all 8 AI- interaction metrics and assignment performance. Additional correlation-structure analyses examined interrelationships among the AI-interaction metrics through Spearman correlation heatmaps. Random Forest modeling was subsequently conducted using all 8 AI- L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 9,54
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"interaction metrics to evaluate predictive relationships and relative feature importance across the complete behavioral interaction frame­ work. The Random Forest analyses were supported by 936 AI-session observations relative to the number of evaluated predictors, reducing the likelihood of instability or overfitting within the estimated models. To determine whether relationships between AI-interaction metrics and assignment performance remained stable after accounting for prior ac­ ademic ability, CGPA-adjusted analyses were subsequently conducted. A General Linear Model (GLM) was used to examine whether AI access condition remained associated with assignment performance after con­ trolling for CGPA. In parallel, a CGPA-adjusted Random Forest Regressor incorporating all eight AI-interaction metrics and CGPA was imple­ mented to evaluate the relative predictive contribution of behavioral interaction patterns beyond prior academic performance. To further address Objective 3, additional dimensionality-reduction and latent-structure analyses were conducted using selected prompting-behavior metrics identified in the earlier correlation, correlation-structure, and predictive analyses. AI Query Efficiency Score was excluded from the Principal Component Analysis (PCA) analyses because it incorporated prompt frequency into the metric calculation itself, which could distort the PCA component structure. To determine",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"in the earlier correlation, correlation-structure, and predictive analyses. AI Query Efficiency Score was excluded from the Principal Component Analysis (PCA) analyses because it incorporated prompt frequency into the metric calculation itself, which could distort the PCA component structure. To determine whether relationships between prompting behaviors and assignment performance remained significant after controlling for prior academic ability and repeated within-student observations, Linear Mixed-Effects Models (LMMs) were subsequently estimated. As assignment observations were repeated within the same students across multiple sessions and courses, repeated-measures dependencies were modeled through LMMs using student-level random intercepts, with 1866 assignment observations nested within 128 students across the semester. These procedures were used to evaluate whether associ­ ations between the evaluated prompting metrics and assignment per­ formance remained significant after accounting for both prior academic ability through CGPA and within-student variance across repeated tasks. In addition, qualitative thematic analyses were conducted on stu­ dents' written reflections describing their prompting strategies. For qualitative comparison, top- and bottom-quartile groups were identified based on metric distributions, and thematic alignment across reflections was examined in relation to the quantitative interaction metrics.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"thematic analyses were conducted on stu­ dents' written reflections describing their prompting strategies. For qualitative comparison, top- and bottom-quartile groups were identified based on metric distributions, and thematic alignment across reflections was examined in relation to the quantitative interaction metrics. Machine-learning procedures were used for predictive and explor­ atory modeling of interaction patterns and relative predictive impor­ tance within the evaluated models, whereas inferential statistical procedures (e.g., GLM, LMM, and correlational analyses) were used to evaluate statistical associations and repeated-measures relationships. Table 2 summarizes the analytical techniques, their purposes, dependent variables, predictors or metrics, analytical levels, statistical parameters, and reported outputs. The “Predictors / Metrics and Analytical Level” column specifies whether each procedure was con­ ducted at the assignment, AI-session, or student-aggregated level, and identifies whether analyses incorporated all 8 AI-interaction metrics or the focused prompting metrics. Complete details regarding computa­ tional environment, software libraries, hardware configuration, and implementation procedures are provided in Appendix D (Software Environment and Reproducibility). 2.15. Power and sensitivity checks To ensure that all inferential and modeling results were statistically robust and interpretable, a series of",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"computa­ tional environment, software libraries, hardware configuration, and implementation procedures are provided in Appendix D (Software Environment and Reproducibility). 2.15. Power and sensitivity checks To ensure that all inferential and modeling results were statistically robust and interpretable, a series of power and diagnostic checks were conducted before and after analysis. These evaluations confirmed that the dataset size, model stability, predictor independence, and repeated- measures balance were sufficient for the applied statistical, dimensionality-reduction, and machine-learning procedures. A priori power analysis using G*Power 3.1 was conducted for the Mann–Whitney U and Spearman correlation analyses. Monte Carlo simulation analyses were used to assess parameter stability for the Random Forest Regressor models and Linear Mixed-Effects Models. Variance Inflation Factor diagnostics were applied to the GLMs and Random Forest analyses to evaluate multicollinearity among predictors. Bootstrap resampling procedures were used to evaluate the robustness of p-values and confidence intervals in the Mann–Whitney U, GLM, and Spearman analyses. Repeated-measures balance checks were conducted to confirm equal AI and non-AI exposure across sessions for the Linear Mixed-Effects Model analyses. PCA suitability was evaluated using the Kai­ ser–Meyer–Olkin",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"p-values and confidence intervals in the Mann–Whitney U, GLM, and Spearman analyses. Repeated-measures balance checks were conducted to confirm equal AI and non-AI exposure across sessions for the Linear Mixed-Effects Model analyses. PCA suitability was evaluated using the Kai­ ser–Meyer–Olkin (KMO) measure and Bartlett's test of sphericity. For the machine-learning analyses, sample adequacy was supported by the relatively large number of AI-session observations (n = 936) compared with the limited number of predictors included in the Random Forest models, reducing the likelihood of model instability or overfitting. For the Linear Mixed-Effects Models, the dataset included 1866 assignment-level observations nested within 128 students, providing sufficient repeated observations for stable estimation of fixed and random effects. Complete diagnostic procedures, parameters, and in­ terpretations are provided in Appendix E (Table E.1). 3. Results 3.1. Comparison of AI and non-AI student performance As shown in Table 3, students with access to ChatGPT consistently received higher scores across all evaluated metrics. On average, AI- assisted students showed assignment scores that were roughly seven points higher than those without access. Differences extended beyond overall grades, with",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"in Table 3, students with access to ChatGPT consistently received higher scores across all evaluated metrics. On average, AI- assisted students showed assignment scores that were roughly seven points higher than those without access. Differences extended beyond overall grades, with AI-assisted ses­ sions also showing higher Structural Complexity and Content Richness scores. 3.2. Relationships among all AI interaction metrics and assignment performance This section evaluates relationships across the complete set of AI- interaction metrics to identify overall associations between prompting behaviors, AI-response integration patterns, and assignment perfor­ mance during AI-assisted sessions. It also examines whether these re­ lationships remained stable after controlling for prior academic ability through CGPA-adjusted analyses. 3.2.1. Correlational relationships across all AI interaction metrics Analysis of 936 AI-assisted sessions showed consistent associations among AI-interaction metrics and assignment performance. Spearman rank correlations showed that AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score had the strongest positive association with assignment scores (ρ = 0.77, p < .001), followed by AI Query Efficiency Score and Prompt Refinement Depth Score. In contrast, AI Query Count was negatively correlated with assignment performance. 3.2.2. Correlation structure across AI",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,10,"the strongest positive association with assignment scores (ρ = 0.77, p < .001), followed by AI Query Efficiency Score and Prompt Refinement Depth Score. In contrast, AI Query Count was negatively correlated with assignment performance. 3.2.2. Correlation structure across AI interaction metrics To further examine relationships among the evaluated AI-interaction metrics, Fig. 3 presents a Spearman correlation heatmap summarizing interconnections between prompting behaviors and assignment performance. The heatmap showed two dominant relationship patterns. Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score exhibited strong mutual correlations, whereas AI Query Efficiency Score and AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score showed closer relationships with assignment performance. These patterns sug­ gest distinct clustering structures across the evaluated AI-interaction metrics. 3.2.3. Predictive modeling across all AI interaction metrics A Random Forest Regressor incorporating all eight AI-interaction metrics showed similar patterns, achieving moderate predictive L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 10",154
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"accuracy (R2 = 0.58, MAE = 6.94, RMSE = 8.03) without signs of overfitting. Permutation-based feature-importance analysis indicated that AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score represented nearly two-thirds of total predictive importance within the evaluated model, followed by AI Response Complexity Score and AI Query Efficiency Score. 3.2.4. Controlling for prior academic ability across all AI metrics To determine whether relationships between AI-assisted interaction patterns and assignment performance remained significant after ac­ counting for prior academic ability, CGPA-adjusted analyses were con­ ducted using both inferential and machine-learning procedures. The GLM examined whether AI access was associated with higher assignment scores independently of prior academic ability. Both CGPA and AI access were significant predictors, with the model yielding R2 = 0.312 and RMSE = 10.42, indicating a moderate overall fit. Table 5 summarizes the regression coefficients, confidence intervals, and sig­ nificance levels. To compare the relative predictive contribution of AI-interaction metrics and prior academic ability, a CGPA-adjusted Random Forest Regressor incorporating all eight AI-interaction metrics and CGPA was estimated. The model achieved moderate predictive accuracy (R2 = 0.55, MAE = 6.72, RMSE = 8.36)",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"the relative predictive contribution of AI-interaction metrics and prior academic ability, a CGPA-adjusted Random Forest Regressor incorporating all eight AI-interaction metrics and CGPA was estimated. The model achieved moderate predictive accuracy (R2 = 0.55, MAE = 6.72, RMSE = 8.36) without signs of overfitting. AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score (37%), AI Query Efficiency Score (21%), and AI Response Complexity Score (13%) showed the highest predictive importance within the evaluated model, whereas CGPA demonstrated comparatively lower predictive contribution (8%). Additional feature- importance visualizations are provided in Appendix F. 3.3. Principal component structure of prompting behaviors To examine relationships among the most strongly associated AI interaction metrics identified in the correlation, correlation-structure, and machine learning analyses, a PCA analysis was performed on z- standardized values of Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, AI Response Utility Score, and AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score. AI Query Efficiency Score was excluded because it was calculated using prompt frequency as part of the metric itself, which could distort the PCA component structure. Two components accounted for 87.41% of the total variance (PC1 = 68.78%, PC2 = 18.63%).",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"Query Efficiency Score was excluded because it was calculated using prompt frequency as part of the metric itself, which could distort the PCA component structure. Two components accounted for 87.41% of the total variance (PC1 = 68.78%, PC2 = 18.63%). Loadings, shown in Table 6, indicated that Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score loaded strongly on PC1, whereas AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score loaded primarily on PC2. As illustrated in Fig. 4, the PCA biplot provides a visual representa­ tion of the component structure across the evaluated prompting metrics. Each point corresponds to an individual student positioned according to their composite metric values across PC1 and PC2. PC1 was defined primarily by Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score, whereas PC2 was driven primarily by AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score. The color scale displayed on the right side of the plot represents AI- Driven Problem-Solving Score values, ranging from blue (low values) to red (high values). Vector arrows denote the direction and relative magnitude of each metric's contribution to the component",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"color scale displayed on the right side of the plot represents AI- Driven Problem-Solving Score values, ranging from blue (low values) to red (high values). Vector arrows denote the direction and relative magnitude of each metric's contribution to the component space, with longer arrows indicating stronger loadings on the principal components. Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score vectors showed similar loading directions along PC1, whereas the AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score vector showed stronger alignment with PC2 relative to the other evaluated metrics. Opposing vector orientations along PC1 indicated contrasting loading directions across the evaluated metrics. 3.4. Repeated-measures modeling of prompting behaviors and assignment performance To account for within-student variance, a LMM was estimated using with restricted maximum likelihood and Wald Z tests. All predictors were significant, with the model achieving an R2 = 0.354, conditional R2 = 0.418, and RMSE = 9.82. AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score and AI Query Efficiency Score remained the strongest positively associated predictors, confirming the patterns observed in the GLM and Random Forest models. Table 7 reports fixed-effect estimates and confidence",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"conditional R2 = 0.418, and RMSE = 9.82. AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score and AI Query Efficiency Score remained the strongest positively associated predictors, confirming the patterns observed in the GLM and Random Forest models. Table 7 reports fixed-effect estimates and confidence intervals. Across all three analytical approaches, the AI-Driven Problem-Solv­ ing Score and AI Query Efficiency Score metrics consistently showed the strongest predictive associations with assignment performance relative to the other evaluated variables. 3.5. Thematic analysis of prompting behaviors To qualitatively validate the three quantitative metrics showing the strongest associations with academic performance in the previous analyses—AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score, AI Query Efficiency Score, and Prompt Refinement Depth Score—we conducted a thematic analysis of students' written reflections describing their prompting strategies. Table 8 summarizes the quantitative contrasts between top- and bottom-quartile students on these metrics, alongside the proportion of participants whose reflections aligned with at least two emergent themes. Higher-scoring students consistently demonstrated stronger thematic coherence with their quantitative profiles. Three dominant themes emerged across students' reflections. The first, strategic decomposition and iterative testing, described how stu­ dents used ChatGPT to break",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"aligned with at least two emergent themes. Higher-scoring students consistently demonstrated stronger thematic coherence with their quantitative profiles. Three dominant themes emerged across students' reflections. The first, strategic decomposition and iterative testing, described how stu­ dents used ChatGPT to break complex problems into smaller compo­ nents and compare alternative solution approaches. Their reflections described multiple rounds of querying, comparison, and revision during task completion. As one student explained, “I usually start by asking ChatGPT to list different ways to solve the problem, then I go through each one to see which fits best with what the question is really asking.” Quantitatively, these students showed higher AI-Driven Problem-Solv­ ing Score values and appeared in the cluster associated with higher AI- Driven Problem-Solving Score values in the PCA biplot (Fig. 4). The second theme, efficiency through targeted query design, char­ acterized students who described using shorter, more specific, and more structured prompts during task completion. Students reported that including specific constraints and formatting instructions produced re­ sponses they considered more useful. One participant wrote, “At first, I would type long questions and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,11,"who described using shorter, more specific, and more structured prompts during task completion. Students reported that including specific constraints and formatting instructions produced re­ sponses they considered more useful. One participant wrote, “At first, I would type long questions and get vague answers. Now I make my prompts short but clear—if I include the topic, the limits, and the format all at once, I usually get what I need the first time.” This theme aligns with the strong positive correlation (ρ = 0.58, p < .001) between AI Table 3 Comparison of AI and Non-AI student performance. Measure Group Mean ± SD Mann–Whitney U p (two-tailed, Holm-adj.) Effect Size (r) 95% CI for r Assignment Score AI 73.90 ± 11.69 Significant < 0.001 0.39 [0.29, 0.48] ​ Non-AI 66.52 ± 15.17 ​ ​ ​ ​ Structural Complexity Score AI 74.64 ± 8.67 Significant < 0.001 0.42 [0.31, 0.51] ​ Non-AI 62.76 ± 12.03 ​ ​ ​ ​ Content Richness Score AI 70.12 ± 9.55 Significant < 0.001 0.44 [0.33, 0.52] ​ Non-AI 62.78 ± 12.03 ​ ​ ​ ​ L.G.",180
