NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,12,"Query Efficiency Score and assignment scores shown in Table 4. Finally, iterative prompt revision after evaluating AI responses described students' approaches to modifying prompts following evaluation of AI-generated outputs. These students described rereading outputs critically, identifying weaknesses in their questions, and rewriting them toward responses perceived as more useful or logically structured. One student noted, “If the response wasn't clear, I'd look at what I asked, figure out what was missing, and rephrase the prompt before trying again.” This pattern was observed alongside the moderate but significant positive association for Prompt Refinement Depth Score in the mixed-effects model (β = 1.89, p = .004). 4. Discussion The following discussion interprets the findings relative to the study objectives and the behavioral dimensions introduced in the theoretical framework. We first examine the differences in writing quality and assignment performance between AI and non-AI conditions, followed by an integrative synthesis of the AI-interaction metrics and their theoret­ ical interpretation. Subsequent sections discuss the observed prompting behaviors across the Strategic & Reasoning, Linguistic & Iterative So­ phistication, and Regulation & Over-Prompting behavioral dimensions. Fig.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,12,"non-AI conditions, followed by an integrative synthesis of the AI-interaction metrics and their theoret­ ical interpretation. Subsequent sections discuss the observed prompting behaviors across the Strategic & Reasoning, Linguistic & Iterative So­ phistication, and Regulation & Over-Prompting behavioral dimensions. Fig. 3. Spearman rank correlation heatmap showing relationships among interaction metrics and assignment performance. The color scale (−1 to +1) represents correlation strength, with darker shades indicating stronger positive correlations and lighter shades representing weaker or negative associations. Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score exhibit strong inter-correlations, while AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score and AI Query Efficiency Score show close association with assignment scores, showing distinct clustering relationships among the evaluated metrics. Correlation coefficients closer to +1 indicate stronger positive associations between metrics, whereas values closer to −1 indicate inverse relationships across the evaluated behavioral interac­ tion patterns. Table 4 Summary of correlations and predictive weights of AI interaction metrics. Metric ρ (Perf) 95% CI Observed Association RF Weight (%) AI-Driven Problem- Solving 0.77 [0.72, 0.82] Strong positive relationship 65.2 AI Query Efficiency Score 0.58 [0.49,",180
