NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,13,"4.1. Writing quality and assignment performance Students who used the AI interface consistently achieved higher assignment scores, as well as higher Structural Complexity and Content Richness scores, than students in the non-AI condition. The moderate effect sizes observed across all three outcomes suggest that these dif­ ferences extended beyond isolated grading variation and were also re­ flected in the syntactic organization and lexical density of students' written academic work. These findings are consistent with Manteufel and Karimi (2024), who observed that engineering students using ChatGPT produced responses with clearer organization, stronger argu­ mentation, and improved technical writing. Higher Structural Complexity scores suggest that AI-assisted students produced more syntactically elaborate writing involving greater grammatical sophistication and organizational depth. Similarly, higher Content Richness scores indicate that AI-assisted students produced more lexically diverse and information-dense responses during engi­ neering assignments. From a theoretical perspective, these findings may reflect scaffolded writing support, greater elaboration in written re­ sponses, and improved organization during AI-assisted writing tasks. The broader interaction analyses further support this interpretation, as assignment performance was more strongly associated with strategic prompting behaviors,",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,13,"perspective, these findings may reflect scaffolded writing support, greater elaboration in written re­ sponses, and improved organization during AI-assisted writing tasks. The broader interaction analyses further support this interpretation, as assignment performance was more strongly associated with strategic prompting behaviors, including AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score and AI Query Efficiency Score, than with prompting frequency alone. Together, these findings suggest that improvements in writing quality may have been associated not only with AI access itself, but also with students' ability to strategically structure, refine, and integrate AI-supported reasoning during academic writing tasks. Some of the observed improvements may also reflect differences in prior writing ability, familiarity with academic discourse, or previous Fig. 4. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) biplot of prompting behavior metrics (Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, AI Response Utility Score, and AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score). Axes represent the two principal components: PC1 (68.78% variance) and PC2 (18.63% variance). Each point denotes a student, color-coded by AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score value (blue = low, red = high), as indicated by the color bar on the right. Vectors show the direction and",180
