NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"experience using generative AI tools that were not directly measured in this study. In addition, AI-assisted students may have benefited from enhanced organizational support, faster idea generation, or reduced cognitive effort during assignment completion. 4.2. Integrative synthesis of AI behavioral metrics and theoretical interpretation To facilitate integrative interpretation across the multiple analytical procedures employed in this study, Table 9 summarizes the AI- interaction metrics showing the strongest empirical findings alongside their theoretical interpretation. The table provides a cross-analytical synthesis linking observed behavioral interaction patterns to theoret­ ical perspectives such as self-regulated learning, metacognitive engagement, cognitive processing, and scaffolded reasoning. The syn­ thesis includes findings from both the complete AI-interaction frame­ work and the focused analyses centered on the four core prompting metrics. 4.3. Behavioral interpretation of AI interaction metrics The following subsections discuss the observed prompting behaviors in relation to prior research on AI-assisted learning and engineering education. The discussion is organized according to the student-AI behavioral metric dimensions developed in this study. 4.3.1. Strategic and reasoning metrics Further analysis of prompting behaviors showed that AI Query Ef­ ficiency Score",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"prior research on AI-assisted learning and engineering education. The discussion is organized according to the student-AI behavioral metric dimensions developed in this study. 4.3.1. Strategic and reasoning metrics Further analysis of prompting behaviors showed that AI Query Ef­ ficiency Score demonstrated a strong positive association with assign­ ment performance across the correlation, Random Forest, and mixed- effects analyses, suggesting that students who submitted fewer but more structured prompts tended to achieve stronger task outcomes. This trend aligns with Güner and Er (2025), who found that prompt refine­ ment was associated with more strategic engagement, and with Liao et al. (2024), who reported that scaffolded AI use enhanced creativity and algorithmic reasoning. Students in the top quartile of assignment grades exhibited stronger iterative prompting strategies as measured by the AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score and Prompt Refinement Depth Score metrics. Prior studies have linked similar behaviors to greater cognitive and metacognitive engagement during AI-assisted tasks. Tiwari, Bhat, Khan, Subramaniam, and Khan (2023) found that students' perceptions of AI's usefulness in­ fluence productive engagement, while Singh, Guan, and Rieh (2025) showed that using",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"have linked similar behaviors to greater cognitive and metacognitive engagement during AI-assisted tasks. Tiwari, Bhat, Khan, Subramaniam, and Khan (2023) found that students' perceptions of AI's usefulness in­ fluence productive engagement, while Singh, Guan, and Rieh (2025) showed that using metacognitive scaffolds during prompting was asso­ ciated with deeper understanding. This suggests that iterative and reflective prompting behaviors may be associated with more deliberate cognitive engagement when interacting with AI systems. Among the evaluated prompting metrics, AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score showed the strongest association with academic performance (ρ = 0.77). This relationship remained significant after controlling for CGPA, suggesting that structured reasoning with AI remained independently associated with assignment performance outcomes. The Random Forest analyses ranked AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score as the variable with the highest predictive importance after controlling for CGPA, exceeding the predictive contribution of prior academic ability within the evalu­ ated models. In the Linear Mixed-Effects Model, AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score again held the largest fixed effect (β = 3.48, p < .001), followed by AI Query Efficiency Score, CGPA, Prompt Refinement Depth Score, and AI Response Complexity Score.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"evalu­ ated models. In the Linear Mixed-Effects Model, AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score again held the largest fixed effect (β = 3.48, p < .001), followed by AI Query Efficiency Score, CGPA, Prompt Refinement Depth Score, and AI Response Complexity Score. Prior research has shown similar patterns, suggesting that GPA alone may not fully capture patterns of effective AI use (Haindl & Weinberger, 2024; Lo et al., 2024; Nikolic et al., 2023; Pabreja & Pabreja, 2024; Sajawal & Kittur, 2024). Together with these studies, the present find­ ings suggest that prompting behaviors involving structured reasoning and purposeful integration of AI output may represent complementary dimensions of AI-assisted academic performance beyond traditional indicators of prior academic achievement. However, although these findings suggest that structured prompting and AI-supported reasoning were associated with higher assignment performance, alternative explanations should also be considered. Some of the advantages observed may reflect greater efficiency in task execution, improved navigation of assignment requirements, or stronger ability to formulate precise and goal-oriented prompts during AI- assisted activities. In addition, students with greater familiarity with AI-supported workflows or stronger procedural",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"the advantages observed may reflect greater efficiency in task execution, improved navigation of assignment requirements, or stronger ability to formulate precise and goal-oriented prompts during AI- assisted activities. In addition, students with greater familiarity with AI-supported workflows or stronger procedural problem-solving skills may have been better positioned to use these systems effectively during complex engineering tasks. 4.3.2. Linguistic and iterative metrics Prompt Refinement Depth Score (ρ = 0.53) and AI Response Complexity Score (ρ = 0.50) were moderately predictive of achieve­ ment, suggesting that students who refined prompts iteratively and generated more cognitively complex and meaningful responses tended to show higher assignment performance. AI Response Utility Score, though somewhat lower (ρ = 0.39), reflected a complementary cognitive process. This behavior aligns with depth-of-processing theory (Craik & Lockhart, 1972), which proposes that reworking information rather than reproducing it may be associated with more elaborate forms of cognitive processing during academic tasks. As shown in the heatmap, AI Response Utility Score correlated strongly with Prompt Refinement Depth Score (ρ = 0.74) and AI Response Complexity Score (ρ = 0.84), suggesting that content",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"with more elaborate forms of cognitive processing during academic tasks. As shown in the heatmap, AI Response Utility Score correlated strongly with Prompt Refinement Depth Score (ρ = 0.74) and AI Response Complexity Score (ρ = 0.84), suggesting that content trans­ formation, prompt refinement, and indicators of cognitive engagement frequently co-occur within broader patterns of metacognitive interac­ tion with AI tools. These findings are consistent with Sun, Boudouaia, Yang, and Xu (2024), who found that prompt-based learners out­ performed passive users, and Bai, Gonda, and Hew (2024), who reported positive associations between iterative evaluation and refinement of AI- generated material and improved learning outcomes. This progression toward deliberate, strategic prompting parallels what Kock et al. (2025) describe as an instrumental genesis process, wherein learners may gradually engage with AI as an integrated cognitive support tool. Güner and Er (2025) similarly observed that students who engaged in iterative, analytical interactions achieved higher performance, supporting the interpretation that Prompt Refine­ ment Depth Score and AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score may reflect complementary dimensions of prompting behavior. PCA revealed two distinct behavioral dimensions: PC1 (68.78%",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"observed that students who engaged in iterative, analytical interactions achieved higher performance, supporting the interpretation that Prompt Refine­ ment Depth Score and AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score may reflect complementary dimensions of prompting behavior. PCA revealed two distinct behavioral dimensions: PC1 (68.78% variance), defined primarily by Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score, and PC2 (18.63%), defined primarily by AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score. The strong PC1 loadings of Prompt Refinement Depth Score, AI Response Complexity Score, and AI Response Utility Score suggest shared lin­ guistic and structural interaction patterns associated with iterative prompting, response transformation, and syntactic elaboration during AI-assisted tasks. In contrast, AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score loaded primarily on PC2, indicating a separate reasoning-oriented dimension associated with procedural decomposition and strategic AI-supported problem-solving behaviors. Students spanned the full PC1 range regardless of PC2 scores, suggesting that linguistic sophistication and strategic reasoning represented partially separable prompting di­ mensions within the evaluated interaction patterns. The opposing vector orientations observed across PC1 further indicate contrasting prompting behaviors between concise procedural interaction styles and more syn­ tactically elaborate prompting approaches. This",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,14,"sophistication and strategic reasoning represented partially separable prompting di­ mensions within the evaluated interaction patterns. The opposing vector orientations observed across PC1 further indicate contrasting prompting behaviors between concise procedural interaction styles and more syn­ tactically elaborate prompting approaches. This finding aligns with Xue, Chen, Bai, Tairas, and Huang (2024), who showed that deeper engage­ ment with AI-generated content was associated with stronger perfor­ mance than surface-level reuse, and with Onal (2023), who emphasized that critical thinking and analytical engagement are important for meaningful AI-supported learning. Overall, these findings suggest that prompting behaviors clustered across two complementary behavioral dimensions involving L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 14",114
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"linguistic–iterative sophistication and strategic reasoning processes during AI-assisted academic tasks. This conclusion aligns with Zheng, Xu, Wang, and Lu (2024), who found that AI-supported project learning enhanced higher-order thinking and structured problem- solving engagement in engineering education. Although these findings suggest that iterative refinement and selec­ tive transformation of AI-generated content were associated with stronger assignment performance, alternative interpretations remain possible. Some improvements may reflect enhanced writing fluency, structural organization, or AI-assisted content elaboration. Moreover, students with stronger prior writing ability or greater familiarity with reflective prompting strategies may have been more capable of using AI systems effectively during assignment completion. 4.3.3. Regulation and over-prompting metrics Conversely, students who produced a high volume of prompts tended to show lower performance, as indicated by the strong negative corre­ lation with AI Query Count. Similar concerns were raised by Bernabei, Colabianchi, Falegnami, and Costantino (2023) and Baturalp and Bozkurt (2025), who observed that overreliance on AI can undermine learning. Isiaku, Muhammad, Kefas, and Ukaegbu (2024) also cautioned that uncritical AI use may weaken critical thinking, a pattern partially reflected in the negative",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"and Costantino (2023) and Baturalp and Bozkurt (2025), who observed that overreliance on AI can undermine learning. Isiaku, Muhammad, Kefas, and Ukaegbu (2024) also cautioned that uncritical AI use may weaken critical thinking, a pattern partially reflected in the negative association observed between AI Query Count and assignment performance in this study. Relatedly, the AI Response Reliance Score showed moderate associations with both AI Query Count and AI Query Depth and Structure Score, suggesting that students who interacted more extensively with AI systems and produced more struc­ tured prompts also tended to incorporate greater amounts of AI- generated material into their assignments. Unlike perspectives that frame AI reliance primarily as passive dependence, these patterns sug­ gest that integration of AI-generated content may also occur alongside cognitively sophisticated prompting behaviors. This suggests that AI- supported academic work may involve a more complex interaction be­ tween strategic engagement and dependence on generated outputs, rather than a simple distinction between effective and ineffective AI use. While these findings highlight the potential educational relevance of deliberate prompting behaviors, they also underscore potential risks associated",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"complex interaction be­ tween strategic engagement and dependence on generated outputs, rather than a simple distinction between effective and ineffective AI use. While these findings highlight the potential educational relevance of deliberate prompting behaviors, they also underscore potential risks associated with unequal prompting proficiency and overreliance on AI- generated reasoning. Not all students may possess the linguistic or cognitive skills required to formulate effective prompts, which could widen performance gaps between students who adapt quickly and those who struggle to use AI systems effectively during academic tasks. Excessive dependence on AI feedback may be associated with fewer opportunities for independent reasoning, problem decomposition, and creative exploration, which are core competencies in engineering edu­ cation. These patterns suggest that strategic prompting may be associ­ ated with improved learning experiences, but its benefits may not be universally accessible without guided instruction. Addressing these disparities may require pedagogical frameworks that explicitly teach students how to engage in AI critically, responsibly, and equitably. At the same time, high prompting frequency may not necessarily reflect ineffective learning-related behavior in all cases. Some students may have",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"these disparities may require pedagogical frameworks that explicitly teach students how to engage in AI critically, responsibly, and equitably. At the same time, high prompting frequency may not necessarily reflect ineffective learning-related behavior in all cases. Some students may have used repeated prompting as a compensatory strategy while learning unfamiliar material, clarifying technical concepts, or exper­ imenting with different prompting approaches during complex tasks. Consequently, elevated AI Query Count may partly reflect differences in confidence, prior knowledge, or exploratory learning behaviors in addition to potential overreliance on AI systems. 5. Methodological contributions and pedagogical implications This study developed a framework of AI-interaction metrics groun­ ded in cognitive and educational theory and examined associations be­ tween these behaviors and academic performance through a triangulated analytical framework integrating statistical modeling, machine learning, and qualitative thematic analysis. Using direct Table 9 Cross-analytical synthesis of AI-interaction metrics, principal empirical findings, and theoretical interpretation. Metric Primary Empirical Finding Interpretation Relative to Theory AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score Showed the strongest positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.77), demonstrated the highest predictive importance after controlling for",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"of AI-interaction metrics, principal empirical findings, and theoretical interpretation. Metric Primary Empirical Finding Interpretation Relative to Theory AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score Showed the strongest positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.77), demonstrated the highest predictive importance after controlling for CGPA in the Random Forest analyses (37%), loaded primarily on the strategic-reasoning PCA dimension (PC2 = +0.781), and remained the strongest predictor in the Linear Mixed-Effects Model (β = 3.48, p < .001). May support scaffolded cognition and schema- construction perspectives by suggesting that structured AI-supported reasoning may be associated with procedural integration, strategic decomposition, and more elaborate reasoning processes during engineering tasks. AI Query Efficiency Score Showed a strong positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.58), demonstrated high predictive importance after controlling for CGPA in the Random Forest analyses (21%), and remained one of the strongest significant predictors in the Linear Mixed- Effects Model (β = 2.73, p < .001). Qualitative reflections further showed that higher- performing students relied on concise, context-rich prompting strategies. Suggests that efficient prompting may reflect metacognitive regulation, strategic cognitive-resource allocation, and deliberate task",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"in the Linear Mixed- Effects Model (β = 2.73, p < .001). Qualitative reflections further showed that higher- performing students relied on concise, context-rich prompting strategies. Suggests that efficient prompting may reflect metacognitive regulation, strategic cognitive-resource allocation, and deliberate task management associated with self-regulated learning processes. Prompt Refinement Depth Score Showed a moderate positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.53), loaded strongly on the linguistic–structural PCA dimension (PC1 = −0.873), remained significant in the Linear Mixed-Effects Model (β = 1.89, p = .004), and was associated qualitatively with reflective self-correction behaviors during prompting. Suggests that iterative prompt revision may represent reflective self- monitoring and adaptive refinement processes associated with metacognitive engagement and progressive refinement and self-monitoring during AI-assisted tasks. AI Response Complexity Score Showed a moderate positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.50), demonstrated high predictive importance after controlling for CGPA in the Random Forest analyses (13%), loaded strongly on the linguistic–structural PCA dimension (PC1 = −0.894), and remained a significant predictor in the Linear Mixed-Effects Model (β = 1.14, p = .028). Suggests that cognitively elaborate interpretation",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,15,"controlling for CGPA in the Random Forest analyses (13%), loaded strongly on the linguistic–structural PCA dimension (PC1 = −0.894), and remained a significant predictor in the Linear Mixed-Effects Model (β = 1.14, p = .028). Suggests that cognitively elaborate interpretation and processing of AI-generated responses may reflect deeper engagement, increased semantic processing, and more active integration of AI-supported information. AI Response Utility Score Showed a moderate positive correlation with assignment performance (ρ = 0.39), clustered strongly with Prompt Refinement Depth and AI Response Complexity in the PCA analysis (PC1 = −0.904), and demonstrated strong inter- correlations with refinement and complexity metrics in the heatmap analysis. Suggests that selective transformation and reinterpretation of AI- generated information may reflect more constructive engagement with AI- supported content. AI Query Count Showed a moderate negative correlation with assignment performance (ρ = −0.56), indicating that higher prompt frequency was associated with lower academic performance. Suggests that prompting frequency alone may not represent productive engagement and may instead reflect inefficient regulation, excessive trial-and-error interaction, or greater dependence on external cognitive support. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al.",180
