NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"behavioral interaction logging, the study examined how engineering students prompted ChatGPT, refined responses, and integrated AI- generated content across a randomized semester-long design involving four engineering courses. The stratified within-semester randomization ensured balanced AI and non-AI exposure, supporting within-subject comparisons across classroom conditions while reducing potential allocation bias. Together, these methodological components provide a structured approach for analyzing AI-supported learning behaviors through a triangulated analytical framework integrating statistical modeling, machine learning, and qualitative thematic analysis, while contributing evidence relevant to AI literacy, instructional design, and the integration of generative AI within engineering education. The use of direct behavioral log data enabled analysis of student–AI interactions while reducing potential recall and desirability biases associated with self-reported measures. The metric framework was additionally informed through expert review, construct alignment, inter-rater reliability procedures, and qualitative thematic validation, providing complementary perspectives across the analytical workflow. Ethical and methodological controls, including CGPA-adjusted ana­ lyses and balanced AI/non-AI exposure, further supported interpretation of the observed associations. Transparency and reproducibility were also supported through the public release of the anonymized dataset, Python codebase, interface scripts, metric computation",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"workflow. Ethical and methodological controls, including CGPA-adjusted ana­ lyses and balanced AI/non-AI exposure, further supported interpretation of the observed associations. Transparency and reproducibility were also supported through the public release of the anonymized dataset, Python codebase, interface scripts, metric computation procedures, and detailed appendices through OSF, Zenodo, and GitHub repositories. By encompassing software, control, process, and environmental engineer­ ing contexts, the study additionally provides preliminary cross- disciplinary evidence suggesting that similar prompting behaviors may emerge across multiple engineering domains. The findings highlight the need for intentional instructional design that transforms the risks of unequal or excessive AI use into opportu­ nities for structured learning and reflective practice. Academic success with AI was associated with iterative refinement, structured reasoning, and purposeful integration of AI output, indicating that effective AI use may be associated with more deliberate and self-regulated interaction patterns. Among the evaluated behavioral metrics, AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score consistently demonstrated the strongest association with assignment performance across the correlation, predictive, and repeated-measures analyses, including after controlling for CGPA, sug­ gesting that structured AI-supported reasoning and procedural problem decomposition may represent",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"the evaluated behavioral metrics, AI-Driven Problem- Solving Score consistently demonstrated the strongest association with assignment performance across the correlation, predictive, and repeated-measures analyses, including after controlling for CGPA, sug­ gesting that structured AI-supported reasoning and procedural problem decomposition may represent particularly important dimensions of effective AI-assisted learning in engineering education. These results are consistent with findings by Ngo, Tran, An, and Nguyen (2024), Tossell et al. (2024), and Song et al. (2024), who showed that scaffolded AI engagement was associated with improved academic performance out­ comes and student confidence. Promoting strategic prompting aligns closely with emerging AI lit­ eracy frameworks. Z¨onnchen, Thurner, and B¨ottcher (2024) emphasized teaching students to craft, evaluate, and verify prompts; Kim, Palomares, Li, and Ahn (2024) proposed a four-domain literacy model—practical, critical, ethical, and creative—that places prompting at the center of responsible AI use; and Zha et al. (2025) demonstrated that embedding AI literacy instruction within disciplinary contexts may support con­ ceptual understanding. Together, these perspectives frame prompting as both a cognitive and ethical practice, an idea broadly consistent with the behavioral interaction patterns observed in",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"et al. (2025) demonstrated that embedding AI literacy instruction within disciplinary contexts may support con­ ceptual understanding. Together, these perspectives frame prompting as both a cognitive and ethical practice, an idea broadly consistent with the behavioral interaction patterns observed in this study. This study also aligns with the perspectives of Shoufan (2023a) and Viorennita, Dewi, and Riyana (2023), who advocate for integrating ChatGPT into education through guided prompting activities and the critical evaluation of AI-generated output to foster responsible and reflective student engagement. By quantifying these behaviors through interaction logs and examining their associations with assignment performance and writing quality, this study offers a behavioral perspective on how AI literacy-related prac­ tices may emerge during classroom-based AI use. While the observed associations support the educational relevance of structured and reflective AI engagement during academic tasks, the following pedagogical recommendations should be interpreted as pro­ spective instructional applications informed by the present findings. To translate these findings into pedagogical practice, engineering curricula can embed prompting instruction within existing design, computation, and communication courses. Structured frameworks such as CRISPE—Capacity/Role, Request/Insight, Instruction/Statement, Style/",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"be interpreted as pro­ spective instructional applications informed by the present findings. To translate these findings into pedagogical practice, engineering curricula can embed prompting instruction within existing design, computation, and communication courses. Structured frameworks such as CRISPE—Capacity/Role, Request/Insight, Instruction/Statement, Style/ Personality, and Purpose/Experiment—provide a practical foundation for teaching prompt design and refinement. Discipline-specific work­ shops or modules could guide students to define the purpose and context of each interaction, craft precise prompts, and iteratively refine AI re­ sponses. Complementary reflective journals or peer-review exercises would encourage students to analyze their prompting strategies and assess AI feedback over time. Complementary reflective journals or peer- review exercises would encourage students to analyze their prompting strategies and assess AI feedback over time. Integrating such activities into laboratory reports, design projects, and coding tasks could support more reflective and strategically regulated AI use during engineering learning activities. These pedagogical interventions also address con­ cerns raised by Lo et al. (2024), Banerjee et al. (2023), and Isiaku et al. (2024), who warn that superficial or over reliant AI use may weaken independent reasoning. Teaching students",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"engineering learning activities. These pedagogical interventions also address con­ cerns raised by Lo et al. (2024), Banerjee et al. (2023), and Isiaku et al. (2024), who warn that superficial or over reliant AI use may weaken independent reasoning. Teaching students to critically evaluate and refine AI output may encourage more deliberate engagement with AI- generated information during engineering education contexts. To implement these principles more broadly, educators can adopt a scaffolded approach to prompting literacy that mirrors the progression of other engineering competencies. Introductory modules might focus on identifying effective and ineffective prompts through guided exam­ ples, intermediate activities could involve collaborative refinement tasks, and advanced courses might include reflective portfolios where students document prompt evolution and evaluation. Institutionally, prompting literacy can be reinforced through faculty-development workshops, shared rubrics for assessing AI engagement, and inclusion of prompt-engineering competencies in accreditation frameworks. These measures may help support more structured pedagogical ap­ proaches to AI use, helping position prompting as a deliberate and assessable dimension of engineering education. The resulting set of validated AI-interaction metrics also contributes a reusable methodological framework for",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"frameworks. These measures may help support more structured pedagogical ap­ proaches to AI use, helping position prompting as a deliberate and assessable dimension of engineering education. The resulting set of validated AI-interaction metrics also contributes a reusable methodological framework for future investigations of prompting behaviors and AI-supported learning across higher-education environments. By providing measurable approaches for examining how students strategically interact with generative AI systems, these metrics may also support the integration of prompting literacy into university curricula, helping prepare future professionals to engage with AI criti­ cally, ethically, and creatively across contemporary professional contexts. 6. Limitations, future work, and conclusion It is necessary to acknowledge the methodological and contextual limitations that shape interpretation of this study's findings. Due to ethical and institutional restrictions associated with student privacy and academic records, the raw textual datasets used in this study, including student prompts, AI responses, and written submissions, cannot be publicly released. Although this limits exact reproduction of the original textual dataset and generated linguistic outputs, the study provides extensive methodological documentation to support approximate replication. The complete computational pipeline, metric",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,16,"student prompts, AI responses, and written submissions, cannot be publicly released. Although this limits exact reproduction of the original textual dataset and generated linguistic outputs, the study provides extensive methodological documentation to support approximate replication. The complete computational pipeline, metric definitions, preprocessing procedures, statistical workflows, validation protocols, and analysis scripts are openly available through the referenced re­ positories and appendices. Future researchers may therefore reproduce the overall study design and analytical framework using institutionally approved educational datasets collected under similar experimental conditions. Although all students had prior experience using ChatGPT, individ­ ual differences in familiarity, prompting proficiency, and prior exposure L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 16",114
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"to AI tools may have influenced the observed patterns. These differences were not directly measured and may represent an additional source of variance warranting further investigation. However, the study design incorporated uniform AI training, balanced AI/non-AI exposure through stratified randomization, and CGPA-adjusted analyses to help reduce potential confounding effects associated with prior experience and ac­ ademic ability. A further limitation concerns the Python-based AI interface used for data collection. While its functionality appeared stable overall, it was not formally validated for user experience within this cohort. However, a prior evaluation with an independent group of engineering students reported uniformly positive perceptions of its usability and features (Isaza Domínguez, Velasquez Clavijo, Robles-G´omez, & Pastor-Vargas, 2024), suggesting that interface-related issues likely had minimal impact on engagement. Although all course tasks followed standardized rubrics and rotation schedules, minor variations in task complexity may have introduced uncontrolled difficulty effects. Grading bias was mitigated through anonymized submissions and inter-rater reliability checks, yet some degree of subjectivity inherent to human assessment cannot be elimi­ nated. These potential confounds, AI familiarity, task variability, and evaluator subjectivity, should",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"have introduced uncontrolled difficulty effects. Grading bias was mitigated through anonymized submissions and inter-rater reliability checks, yet some degree of subjectivity inherent to human assessment cannot be elimi­ nated. These potential confounds, AI familiarity, task variability, and evaluator subjectivity, should be considered when interpreting the findings. In addition, motivational differences, self-regulation, task engagement, and individual adaptation to prompting strategies may also have contributed to variation in AI-supported learning behaviors despite the randomized AI-access design and standardized instructional conditions. Some observed performance improvements may partly reflect AI- assisted support for writing quality, task completion, or content orga­ nization rather than learning alone. Consequently, assignment scores and writing-quality metrics should not be interpreted as direct measures of deeper conceptual understanding, long-term knowledge retention, or durable learning outcomes. Although mixed-effects modeling, balanced AI/non-AI exposure, and CGPA-adjusted analyses were implemented to reduce potential bias, the findings should still be interpreted within the broader complexity of educational environments, where behavioral, contextual, and instructional factors may interact in ways not fully captured by the evaluated variables. Moreover, as this investigation was conducted within a single insti­",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"the findings should still be interpreted within the broader complexity of educational environments, where behavioral, contextual, and instructional factors may interact in ways not fully captured by the evaluated variables. Moreover, as this investigation was conducted within a single insti­ tution and disciplinary context, the findings should be interpreted with caution regarding their broader applicability. Replication across diverse universities, academic disciplines, and cultural settings is strongly rec­ ommended to determine whether similar prompting behaviors and performance associations emerge in other educational contexts. Despite these constraints, the findings suggest that variation in stu­ dents' prompting behaviors was associated with differences in academic performance across AI-assisted tasks. This study's focus on fourth-year engineering students ensured internal consistency but also limits external generalizability. Including broader student populations and additional task types, such as creative writing, group work, or oral presentations, could reveal additional prompting patterns. Additionally, because the study utilized ChatGPT-4o, future work should consider potential model drift and newer model versions that may influence prompting effectiveness and metric reliability as generative AI systems continue to evolve. While post-semester qualitative analysis provided insight",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"prompting patterns. Additionally, because the study utilized ChatGPT-4o, future work should consider potential model drift and newer model versions that may influence prompting effectiveness and metric reliability as generative AI systems continue to evolve. While post-semester qualitative analysis provided insight into stu­ dents' prompting strategies and intentionality, further research is needed to explore their attitudes, motivations, and perceived learning outcomes. Richer qualitative approaches, such as interviews, think- aloud protocols, or reflective journals, could deepen understanding of how students cognitively and emotionally engage with AI across tasks. A promising avenue for future research lies in testing targeted in­ terventions to strengthen students' prompting skills. Studies could examine the impact of AI literacy courses, scaffolded prompting in­ struction, or reflective assignments that track how students refine their queries over time. Educators might incorporate prompt-evaluation rubrics, peer-review activities, or comparative analyses of effective and ineffective prompts to reinforce metacognitive reflection and critical engagement. Future work should also empirically evaluate the curricular ap­ proaches proposed in this paper. Controlled trials could test the effec­ tiveness of frameworks such as CRISPE, scaffolded workshops, or reflection-based",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"and ineffective prompts to reinforce metacognitive reflection and critical engagement. Future work should also empirically evaluate the curricular ap­ proaches proposed in this paper. Controlled trials could test the effec­ tiveness of frameworks such as CRISPE, scaffolded workshops, or reflection-based assignments in improving prompting skill and aca­ demic performance. Comparing outcomes across course types, instruc­ tional models, and cultural contexts would help identify which strategies most effectively develop prompting literacy and transfer to broader problem-solving contexts. Longitudinal designs could then assess whether these interventions are associated with sustained improve­ ments in prompting skill and academic achievement, informing evidence-based curriculum design for responsible and effective AI integration in engineering education. In conclusion, this study provides empirical, methodological, and pedagogical insight into how engineering students interact with gener­ ative AI during varied academic tasks. Through a randomized semester- long design integrating behavioral interaction logging, statistical anal­ ysis, machine learning, and qualitative validation, the findings suggest that prompting behaviors associated with strategic reasoning, iterative refinement, and efficient AI engagement were consistently associated with higher academic performance. By translating prompting and AI-interaction behaviors into",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"interaction logging, statistical anal­ ysis, machine learning, and qualitative validation, the findings suggest that prompting behaviors associated with strategic reasoning, iterative refinement, and efficient AI engagement were consistently associated with higher academic performance. By translating prompting and AI-interaction behaviors into validated behavioral metrics, this study contributes to ongoing AI-literacy research beyond perception-based approaches and offers a structured approach for examining AI-supported learning in higher education. The findings further suggest that effective educational use of generative AI may depend not simply on access to AI systems, but also on students' ability to engage these tools critically, strategically, and reflectively within disciplinary learning contexts. The findings of this study further suggest that prompting behaviors may represent learnable cognitive and metacognitive strategies with potential pedagogical relevance in engineering education. As generative AI systems continue to evolve in sophistication and accessibility, the ability to interact with them through deliberate, reflective, and strate­ gically structured prompting may become an increasingly valuable ac­ ademic and professional skill for future engineers. CRediT authorship contribution statement Lauren Genith Isaza Dominguez: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"interact with them through deliberate, reflective, and strate­ gically structured prompting may become an increasingly valuable ac­ ademic and professional skill for future engineers. CRediT authorship contribution statement Lauren Genith Isaza Dominguez: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Project administration, Methodology, Investi­ gation, Data curation, Conceptualization. Antonio Robles-G´omez: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Supervision, Conceptualization. Rafael Pastor-Vargas: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Supervision, Conceptualization. Funding This research received no external funding. Declaration of competing interest The authors confirm that there are no known conflicts of interest, financial or personal, that could have potentially influenced the out­ comes of this research. Acknowledgements We would like to thank the professors, teaching assistants and un­ dergraduate students at the Universidad de los Llanos who participated in this study. This contribution is also supported within the framework of the CiberCSI UNED research group and CiberGID innovation group. We would like to acknowledge the support of the Spanish Government (through the Cybersecurity Institute of Spain, INCIBE), with the L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,17,"supported within the framework of the CiberCSI UNED research group and CiberGID innovation group. We would like to acknowledge the support of the Spanish Government (through the Cybersecurity Institute of Spain, INCIBE), with the L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 17",48
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,18,"Strategic Research Project “Analysis of mobile applications from the perspective of data protection: Cyber-protection and Cyber-risks of cit­ izen information” and the International Chair “Smart Rural IoT and Secured Environments” Smart Rural IoT and Secured Environments' within the context of the Recovery, Transformation, and Resilience Plan financed by the European Union (NextGenerationEU/PRTR). Appendix A. Validation, calculation and implementation of metrics This appendix provides the technical and validation procedures used to calculate each of the AI-interaction and. writing-quality metrics introduced in the main body of the article. Each metric was implemented in Python using widely adopted natural language processing libraries, including spaCy, NLTK, scikit-learn, and sentence-transformers.Where applicable, subcomponents were normalized and weighted based on theoretical and empirical rationale. The corresponding Python codebase used to compute all metrics—covering preprocessing, text simi­ larity, dependency parsing, and scaling—is available via Zenodo (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b). A.1. Metric validation summary Construct validity for each metric was assessed through Spearman's rank correlations between expert ratings and automated metric outputs, while inter-rater reliability was estimated using the two-way random-effects Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC[2,k]). All coefficients met accepted thresholds",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,18,"A.1. Metric validation summary Construct validity for each metric was assessed through Spearman's rank correlations between expert ratings and automated metric outputs, while inter-rater reliability was estimated using the two-way random-effects Intraclass Correlation Coefficient (ICC[2,k]). All coefficients met accepted thresholds for moderate-to-high validity (ρ > 0.50) and reliability (ICC > 0.60), confirming the consistency and robustness of the automated scoring framework. The results are summarized in Table A1. Table A.1. Metric validation summary. Metric Spearman (ρ) ICC(2,k) Structural Complexity Score (SC) 0.68 0.80 Content Richness (CR) 0.65 0.78 AI Response Reliance (ARR) 0.62 0.64 AI Query Depth & Structure (QD) 0.73 0.30 Prompt Refinement Depth (PRD) 0.52 0.60 AI Response Utility (RU) 0.503 0.832 AI-Driven Problem-Solving (PS) 0.84 0.90 AI Response Complexity (ARCS) – α = 0.81 A.2. Structural Complexity Score (SC) The Structural Complexity Score (SC) captures syntactic depth and fluency in student writing. It was computed for each written assignment using two linguistic subcomponents: • Sentence Length (SL): Calculated as the average number of words per sentence using nltk.sent_tokenize() and nltk.word_tokenize(). SL was linearly scaled to a 0–100",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,18,"and fluency in student writing. It was computed for each written assignment using two linguistic subcomponents: • Sentence Length (SL): Calculated as the average number of words per sentence using nltk.sent_tokenize() and nltk.word_tokenize(). SL was linearly scaled to a 0–100 range using fixed thresholds: 5 words per sentence mapped to 0, and 50 words per sentence mapped to 100, based on the observed dataset distribution. • Grammatical Complexity (GC): Derived using syntactic dependency parsing with spaCy. Following Strübbe, Grünwald, Sidorenko, and Lampe (2025), the GC score was calculated as the average number of advanced grammatical structures per sentence, including: • Subordinate clauses (mark) • Relative clauses (relcl) • Adjectival clauses (acl) • Coordination structures (cc) GC was normalized by the total number of sentences in the assignment and scaled to a 0–100 range using an empirical upper bound of 4 target structures per sentence. The final SC score for each assignment was calculated as a weighted combination of both components: SC = 0.40⋅SL + 0.60⋅GC This approach balances fluency and syntactic variety, normalizing structural complexity across diverse writing styles while",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,18,"target structures per sentence. The final SC score for each assignment was calculated as a weighted combination of both components: SC = 0.40⋅SL + 0.60⋅GC This approach balances fluency and syntactic variety, normalizing structural complexity across diverse writing styles while preventing inflation from unusually long or highly fragmented sentences. A.3. Content richness and information density score (CR) The Content Richness and Information Density Score (CR) was computed for each written assignment to assess lexical variety and semantic density. It consists of two balanced subcomponents: • Type-Token Ratio (TTR): Calculated using nltk.word_tokenize() following Kettunen (2014). To mitigate TTR's sensitivity to text length, a fixed-size sliding window of 50 words was applied. If a text was shorter than 50 words, the entire excerpt was used. TTR was computed as the ratio of unique word types to total tokens. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 18",150
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"• Lexical Density (LD): Computed via part-of-speech tagging using spaCy, following the method of Spring and Johnson (2022). Content-bearing words—nouns, verbs, adjectives, and adverbs—were divided by the total number of alphabetic tokens to produce a normalized LD value be­ tween 0 and 1. The CR score was computed as the average of TTR and LD, scaled to a 0–100 range by multiplication. If the result exceeded 100, it was capped. To prevent artificially inflated TTR values in short responses, CR was only computed for texts longer than 100 words (Kettunen, 2014). Unlike metrics with unbounded syntactic complexity (e.g., SC), CR relies on inherently bounded ratios, so no additional normalization was needed. CR = 0.50⋅TTR + 0.50⋅LD A.4. AI Query Count (QC) The AI Query Count (QC) metric was computed at the session level to capture the number of distinct prompts submitted by each student during an AI-access session. Prompt logs were parsed using the pandas library in Python. Each prompt was extracted from the session-level interaction log (one file per assignment). To avoid overcounting slight rephrasings or repeated queries, a",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"submitted by each student during an AI-access session. Prompt logs were parsed using the pandas library in Python. Each prompt was extracted from the session-level interaction log (one file per assignment). To avoid overcounting slight rephrasings or repeated queries, a Levenshtein similarity filter was applied: any pair of prompts with a similarity score of ≥85% was grouped and counted as a single unique query. This prompt grouping technique follows the method described by Gaikwad and Bogiri (2015). QC was only computed for students assigned to the AI condition. No normalization by time or task complexity was applied, as QC is treated as a raw engagement indicator reflecting the quantity of student–AI interaction. Later sections examine QC alongside Prompt Refinement Depth (PRD) to explore both the volume and quality of prompting behavior. A.5. AI response reliance score (ARR) Each AI response block was compared to the corresponding full written assignment from the same session. All ARR subcomponents were computed at the block level (i.e., full AI output vs. full student submission), rather than at the sentence alignment level, to reflect holistic",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"block was compared to the corresponding full written assignment from the same session. All ARR subcomponents were computed at the block level (i.e., full AI output vs. full student submission), rather than at the sentence alignment level, to reflect holistic reliance patterns rather than granular reuse. • Text Similarity (TS, 40%): Computed as the mean of Cosine Similarity (using sentence-transformers with model all-MiniLM-L6-v2), Jaccard Similarity, and normalized Levenshtein distance. This follows hybrid similarity detection methods from Korade et al. (2024). • Direct Copy-Pasting (CP, 10%): Flagged near-verbatim reuse using sentence-wise Levenshtein ratios ≥90%, prioritizing full-sentence structural matches over token overlap (cf. Anbananthen, Kannan, Busst, Muthaiyah, & Lurudusamy, 2022). • Structural Similarity (SS, 20%): Captured syntactic overlap via dependency parsing with spaCy, comparing distributions of grammatical relations (e.g., nsubj, aux, prep). Adapted from Yamamoto, Matsumoto, and Watanabe (2022). • Prompt–Response Consistency (PRC, 20%): Measured conceptual alignment between student prompt and AI response using cosine similarity on sentence embeddings (cf. Abdalgader & Matroud, 2024). • Query-to-Submission Link Score (QLS, 10%): Used spaCy's Named Entity Recognition to compute overlap between entities in student",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"(PRC, 20%): Measured conceptual alignment between student prompt and AI response using cosine similarity on sentence embeddings (cf. Abdalgader & Matroud, 2024). • Query-to-Submission Link Score (QLS, 10%): Used spaCy's Named Entity Recognition to compute overlap between entities in student prompts and their final submissions, adapted from Hu et al. (2023). All five components were normalized to a 0–1 scale before applying the weighted average. The formula below was used to compute the final score: ARR = 0.40⋅TS + 0.10⋅CP + 0.20⋅SS + 0.20⋅PRC + 0.10⋅QLS Because higher similarity between AI responses and student writing indicates greater reliance on AI, the raw ARR score was inverted so that higher values reflect greater independence and cognitive transformation. After computing the weighted average of the five components, the score was subtracted from 1 and multiplied by 100. This ensured that a student who reused large portions of AI output received a lower ARR score, while a student who meaningfully transformed or diverged from the AI responses received a higher score. The final ARR score thus reflects reduced reliance on AI content, consistent",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"who reused large portions of AI output received a lower ARR score, while a student who meaningfully transformed or diverged from the AI responses received a higher score. The final ARR score thus reflects reduced reliance on AI content, consistent with the intended construct definition. A.6. AI query depth & structure score (QD) For each session, all student prompts were compiled into a single chronological text block. The AI Query Depth & Structure Score (QD) was computed over this block, treating all prompts in the session as a unified document. This approach enabled holistic analysis of structural, procedural, and clarity-related features rather than isolated prompt-level scoring. • Lexical and Syntactic Structure (LS, 35%): Calculated using the Flesch-Kincaid readability score, Gunning Fog index, and lexical diversity metrics. These were computed using NLTK and spaCy, based on the methods described in Lee and Lee (2024). Prompts exhibiting greater syntactic variety, richer vocabulary, and deeper grammatical constructions received higher scores. • Multi-Step Problem-Solving Depth (MSD, 35%): Assessed using spaCy's dependency parser, following Altinok (2021). This subcomponent iden­ tified procedural and logical operators (e.g.,",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,19,"and Lee (2024). Prompts exhibiting greater syntactic variety, richer vocabulary, and deeper grammatical constructions received higher scores. • Multi-Step Problem-Solving Depth (MSD, 35%): Assessed using spaCy's dependency parser, following Altinok (2021). This subcomponent iden­ tified procedural and logical operators (e.g., “calculate,” “if,” “therefore”) as well as subordinate clause structures to capture evidence of multi- stage reasoning and task decomposition. • Query Focus and Clarity (FC, 30%): Measured using two complementary analyses: (1) lexical repetition, quantified via n-gram overlap using NLTK, and (2) syntactic compactness, evaluated through part-of-speech patterning and dependency tree depth via spaCy, following the approach of Shaib et al. (2024). Prompts that were concise, well-scaffolded, and non-redundant received higher scores. All three subcomponents were normalized to a 0–100 scale using a combination of z-score and max-range scaling across the full prompt dataset. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 19",148
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,20,"The final QD score was calculated as follows: QD = 0.35⋅LS + 0.35⋅MSD + 0.30⋅FC A.7. Prompt refinement depth score (PRD) This metric captures the extent to which students refined and elaborated their prompts within a session. Prompt logs (typically 50–100 entries per session) were scanned for semantically related sequences of prompts that reflected progressive development or clarification of the same query. • Refinement Sequence Extraction: A refinement transition was defined as a pair of prompts within the same session that were semantically similar (cosine similarity between 0.80 and 0.90 using sentence embeddings from all-MiniLM-L6-v2) but not merely superficial edits. To exclude trivial variations, Levenshtein distance thresholds were applied. Multiple transitions could occur within a single session, and each was analyzed independently. • Syntactic Complexity Gain (C): For each prompt in a refinement transition, syntactic complexity was calculated using spaCy, incorporating: • Average dependency tree depth • Number of subordinate, coordinate, and relative clauses • Number of unique syntactic dependency types • Total token count The syntactic complexity for each prompt was computed as: C = 1.5⋅AvgTreeDepth + 2.0⋅ClauseCount +",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,20,"incorporating: • Average dependency tree depth • Number of subordinate, coordinate, and relative clauses • Number of unique syntactic dependency types • Total token count The syntactic complexity for each prompt was computed as: C = 1.5⋅AvgTreeDepth + 2.0⋅ClauseCount + 0.5⋅DependencyTypeCount + 005⋅TokenCount • Semantic Elaboration (S): Measured as the cosine distance between the sentence embeddings of the initial and final prompts in each transition, capturing conceptual growth. • PRD Calculation: For each refinement transition, the raw Prompt Refinement Depth score was computed as: PRD{raw} = 0.7⋅ ( C{final} −C{initial} ) + 0.3⋅S All transition scores were averaged at the session level. The result was scaled to a 0–100 range using the observed dataset maximum (Δmax = 8): PRD{scaled} = ( PRD{session} ÷ Δ{max} ) × 100 A.8. AI response utility score (RU) This metric evaluates the extent to which students meaningfully transformed or built upon AI-generated content. It operates on aligned pairs of text segments between the AI response and the final student-written assignment. • Segment Matching Strategy: Both the AI response and student assignment were split into paragraph-level",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,20,"students meaningfully transformed or built upon AI-generated content. It operates on aligned pairs of text segments between the AI response and the final student-written assignment. • Segment Matching Strategy: Both the AI response and student assignment were split into paragraph-level segments using double line breaks and sentence boundary heuristics. Sentence embeddings were generated using all-MiniLM-L6-v2 from sentence-transformers, and cosine similarity was used to compute the semantic alignment between each AI segment and student paragraph. For each AI segment, the single most similar student paragraph was retained, provided the similarity was ≥0.70. Segments under 100 words were excluded to ensure substantive comparisons. • Semantic Novelty (SN, 45%): Calculated as (1 − cosine similarity) × 100(1 - \text{cosine similarity}) \times 100(1 − cosine similarity) × 100, rewarding divergence from AI content. Higher values indicate greater novelty in the student's expression and conceptual framing. This adaptation of Ormerod, Martínez del Rinc´on, and Devereux (2021) minimizes false inflation from short or repetitive texts and ensures originality is rewarded. • Conceptual Transformation (CT, 40%): Measured the degree of lexical and structural transformation using: • Jaccard",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,20,"framing. This adaptation of Ormerod, Martínez del Rinc´on, and Devereux (2021) minimizes false inflation from short or repetitive texts and ensures originality is rewarded. • Conceptual Transformation (CT, 40%): Measured the degree of lexical and structural transformation using: • Jaccard Similarity (to assess word-level overlap), and • Levenshtein Distance (to capture structural edits and rephrasings). These measures were averaged per aligned segment pair, adapting the transformation framework proposed by Ormerod et al. (2021). • Contribution to Final Assignment (CF, 15%): Quantified the reuse of core terminology and ideas from AI segments in the student assignment using cosine similarity and Named Entity Recognition (NER) via spaCy. This follows the reuse-tracking approach outlined in Woolever and Wettstein (2024). Each score (SN, CT, CF) was computed at the segment pair level, then averaged across all valid segment matches within the session to yield a session-level RU score. The final RU score was computed as a weighted average: RU = 0.45⋅SN + 0.40⋅CT + 0.15⋅CF L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 20",175
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,20,a session-level RU score. The final RU score was computed as a weighted average: RU = 0.45⋅SN + 0.40⋅CT + 0.15⋅CF L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 20,35
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,21,"A.9. AI-driven problem-solving score (PS) This metric evaluates how students adapted AI-generated solutions when completing structured tasks such as case analysis, quantitative reasoning, or design activities. It emphasizes alignment in logic, application of concepts, and evidence of independent problem-solving expansion. • Stepwise Alignment (SA, 40%): Both AI responses and student assignments were segmented into sentence-level blocks. Using sentence- transformers, cosine similarity was computed between all sentence pairs to identify logical alignment and structural overlap. For each sentence in the AI output, the top-matching sentence from the student submission was retained if similarity exceeded 0.70. All valid sentence-level similarity scores were averaged and scaled to a 0–100 range. This procedure follows logic-model alignment approaches in Ormerod et al. (2021). • Conceptual Application (CA, 40%): Key domain-specific terms and named entities (e.g., technical vocabulary, process names, variables) were extracted from each AI response using spaCy's Named Entity Recognition (NER). These terms were then searched for in the student's assignment using both exact string matching and cosine similarity at the sentence level. This component reflects domain-sensitive concept transfer, based on the entity-matching strategies",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,21,"response using spaCy's Named Entity Recognition (NER). These terms were then searched for in the student's assignment using both exact string matching and cosine similarity at the sentence level. This component reflects domain-sensitive concept transfer, based on the entity-matching strategies of Dychka, Potapova, Vovk, Meliukh, and Vedenieieva (2024). Results were normalized and scaled to 0–100. • Independent Expansion (IE, 20%): This subcomponent captured the extent to which students expanded beyond AI content. • Semantic Expansion: Cosine similarity was computed between the AI response block and the student submission. Scores were inverted (1 − similarity) to reflect divergence. • Syntactic Complexity: spaCy's dependency parser was used to count features associated with complex reasoning, such as subordinate clauses, embedded phrases, and flexible word order (e.g., pre-verbal elaboration), following McNamara, Crossley, and McCarthy (2010) and Okhapkin, Okhapkina, Iskhakova, and Iskhakov (2021). The final IE score was the average of the semantic and syntactic measures, scaled to 0–100. All three subcomponent scores were combined using the following weighted formula: PS = 0.40⋅SA + 0.40⋅CA + 0.20⋅IE A.10. AI response complexity score (ARCS) The AI",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,21,"IE score was the average of the semantic and syntactic measures, scaled to 0–100. All three subcomponent scores were combined using the following weighted formula: PS = 0.40⋅SA + 0.40⋅CA + 0.20⋅IE A.10. AI response complexity score (ARCS) The AI Response Complexity Score (ARCS) captures students' self-reported perceptions of how cognitively demanding it was to understand, adapt, and apply AI-generated responses. This table presents the interpretive bands used to contextualize raw ARCS values on a 0–100 scale, offering a standardized framework to distinguish levels of perceived complexity and engagement. Table A.2. gives a breakdown of what each score represents. Table A.2. ARCS interpretation. Score Interpretation 90–100 Maximal Perceived Complexity: Student found the AI responses cognitively rich, requiring deep understanding, meaningful adaptation, and sustained mental effort. Reflects high engagement and deep processing. 70–89 High Complexity Engagement: AI content was useful but required effortful integration. Indicates thoughtful use and non-trivial adaptation, likely tied to active prompting behavior. 50–69 Moderate Complexity Experience: Some adaptation or cognitive effort occurred, but without deep semantic engagement. Reflects functional but partial integration. 30–49 Low Complexity Engagement: AI",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,21,"required effortful integration. Indicates thoughtful use and non-trivial adaptation, likely tied to active prompting behavior. 50–69 Moderate Complexity Experience: Some adaptation or cognitive effort occurred, but without deep semantic engagement. Reflects functional but partial integration. 30–49 Low Complexity Engagement: AI responses were easy to apply without much thought or reworking. Suggests shallow use, possibly superficial processing. 0–29 Minimal Perceived Complexity: Content was either overly simple, unclear, or discarded. Reflects low engagement, confusion, or poor task alignment. A.11. Reproducibility and software environment All analyses were conducted in Python 3.10.13 (Windows 11 Pro x64) using standard open-source scientific libraries. Detailed descriptions of the software environment, preprocessing pipeline, and model parameters are provided in Appendix D (Software Environment and Reproducibility). Data aggregation procedures are described in Appendix C. Appendix B. Illustrative Examples To demonstrate how the AI interaction and writing metrics were applied in practice, this appendix presents selected excerpts from student as­ signments, prompts, and AI responses. While all metrics were computed at the full session level using entire prompt blocks, response blocks, and written submissions (see Appendix A), only representative segments",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,21,"in practice, this appendix presents selected excerpts from student as­ signments, prompts, and AI responses. While all metrics were computed at the full session level using entire prompt blocks, response blocks, and written submissions (see Appendix A), only representative segments are shown here for brevity and readability. The selected excerpts preserve the linguistic and conceptual features that contributed most significantly to each metric's outcome. B.1. Structural complexity score (SC) To illustrate how the SC Score reflects differences in syntactic construction, two student writing samples are presented below. Both excerpts were submitted as part of written assignments from fourth-year engineering students in different course contexts. The first sample, drawn from a Process Optimization task, received a high SC score, while the second, from an Environmental Impact Assessment assignment, received a low score. These examples demonstrate the metric's sensitivity to sentence structure, grammatical layering, and syntactic variation. High SC Example SC Score: 92. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 21",165
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,22,"B.1.1. Student assignment excerpt – process optimization “Although lean manufacturing techniques have been widely adopted to reduce waste and increase throughput, their implementation often requires significant restructuring of existing workflows, particularly in legacy systems where operational inertia and rigid scheduling protocols can inhibit adaptive optimization.” Subcomponent Scores: Sentence Length (SL) = 80. Grammatical Complexity (GC) = 100. SC = 0.40 ⋅ 80 + 0.60 ⋅ 100 = 92. This excerpt demonstrates high structural complexity, characterized by embedded clauses, coordinating structures, and elaborate noun phrases. The student articulates a multilayered argument involving conditional relationships and implementation barriers, indicating advanced syntactic control and conceptual fluency. Low SC Example SC Score: 10. B.1.2. Student assignment excerpt – environmental impact assessment “The project was evaluated for environmental risks. Several pollution sources were identified. These include chemicals, noise, and waste from nearby facilities.” Subcomponent Scores: Sentence Length (SL) = 8. Grammatical Complexity (GC) = 11. SC = 0.40 ⋅ 8 + 0.60 ⋅ 11 = 10. While this passage is clear and well-organized, it reflects low structural complexity. The student uses short, simple sentences without",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,22,"Sentence Length (SL) = 8. Grammatical Complexity (GC) = 11. SC = 0.40 ⋅ 8 + 0.60 ⋅ 11 = 10. While this passage is clear and well-organized, it reflects low structural complexity. The student uses short, simple sentences without subordination or elaboration. The ideas are expressed through direct statements, resulting in limited syntactic depth and minimal demonstration of advanced reasoning in written form. B.2. Content richness and information density score (CR) The Content Richness and Information Density Score (CR) quantifies the expressive quality of student writing by evaluating both vocabulary diversity and the proportion of content-bearing words. The following examples are drawn from a fourth-year Control Systems Engineering course, where students were asked to explain the function of adaptive control strategies in nonlinear systems. These samples highlight the contrast between precise, information-rich expression and vague, repetitive language, demonstrating how CR reflects both lexical sophistication and conceptual density. High CR Example. CR Score: 84.8. B.2.1. Student assignment excerpt – control systems engineering “Adaptive control techniques address the limitations of linear models by dynamically adjusting system parameters. These algorithms use recursive",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,22,"CR reflects both lexical sophistication and conceptual density. High CR Example. CR Score: 84.8. B.2.1. Student assignment excerpt – control systems engineering “Adaptive control techniques address the limitations of linear models by dynamically adjusting system parameters. These algorithms use recursive estimation and Lyapunov-based stability criteria to maintain performance under varying load and noise conditions.” Subcomponent Scores: Type-Token Ratio (TTR) = 0.97. Lexical Density (LD) = 0.73. CR = (0.97 + 0.73) ÷ 2 × 100 = 84.8. This excerpt demonstrates rich and precise vocabulary, incorporating multiple technical terms such as adaptive control, recursive estimation, and Lyapunov-based criteria. The phrasing is compact and content-dense, offering a high ratio of meaningful terms relative to grammatical scaffolding. These features contribute to the strong CR score and reflect advanced technical fluency. Low CR Example. CR Score: 58.1. B.2.2. Student assignment excerpt – control systems engineering “The system has a controller that makes the system respond. It adjusts the system when it notices a change. The system works with the controller to keep things working well. This helps the user when things happen and makes",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,22,"engineering “The system has a controller that makes the system respond. It adjusts the system when it notices a change. The system works with the controller to keep things working well. This helps the user when things happen and makes the system better. The controller is used to help the system deal with problems.” Subcomponent Scores: Type-Token Ratio (TTR) = 0.59. Lexical Density (LD) = 0.57. CR = (0.59 + 0.57) ÷ 2 × 100 = 58.1. Although this passage addresses the general behavior of a control system, it contains vague and repetitive phrasing. The frequent repetition of system and controller limits vocabulary diversity, while the content density remains modest. These features result in a lower CR score, reflecting a basic level of technical expression. B.3. AI response reliance score (ARR) The AI Response Reliance Score (ARR) measures the degree to which students reuse language, structure, and concepts from AI-generated responses in their own writing. Note that the raw similarity score was inverted in the final analysis, so higher ARR scores reflect greater independence but in this example high ARR",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,22,"to which students reuse language, structure, and concepts from AI-generated responses in their own writing. Note that the raw similarity score was inverted in the final analysis, so higher ARR scores reflect greater independence but in this example high ARR scores reflect heavy reuse—potentially indicating overreliance—while low scores suggest independent synthesis. The following examples are drawn from a laboratory session in a fourth-year Process Optimization course, where students were tasked with optimizing L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 22",86
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,23,"manufacturing workflows using Lean methodologies and discrete-event simulation tools. Excerpts are paired with their corresponding AI prompts and responses to demonstrate how ARR is computed. High ARR Example. ARR Score: 79. Associated AI Prompt. “What Lean methods can reduce manufacturing lead time and improve throughput in a bottlenecked production line?” B.3.1. AI response excerpt “Reducing lead time and improving throughput involves identifying bottlenecks and applying Lean tools like takt time analysis, line balancing, and workload redistribution. Simulation software such as ARENA helps validate process changes. Buffer zones before high-variability steps stabilize flow. Cross-training workers can also increase operational flexibility.” B.3.2. Student assignment excerpt “Reducing lead time and increasing throughput depends on identifying bottlenecks and applying Lean methods such as takt time evaluation, workload balancing, and task redistribution. Simulation tools like ARENA are used to validate proposed changes. Buffer zones placed before steps with high variability help stabilize flow. Training workers across roles can further improve flexibility.¨. Subcomponent Scores: Text Similarity (TS) = 76. Copy-Pasting (CP) = 75. Structural Similarity (SS) = 93. Prompt–Response Consistency (PRC) = 64. Query-to-Submission Link Score",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,23,"before steps with high variability help stabilize flow. Training workers across roles can further improve flexibility.¨. Subcomponent Scores: Text Similarity (TS) = 76. Copy-Pasting (CP) = 75. Structural Similarity (SS) = 93. Prompt–Response Consistency (PRC) = 64. Query-to-Submission Link Score (QLS) = 100. ARR = 0.40⋅76 + 0.10⋅75 + 0.20⋅93 + 0.20⋅64 + 0.10⋅100 = 79. This submission reflects a high degree of reliance on AI-generated output. The student reproduced large segments of the AI response with only minor rephrasing, resulting in high similarity scores across all dimensions. Although technically accurate and relevant, the limited evidence of in­ dependent synthesis suggests low epistemic agency and passive integration of AI support. Low ARR Example. ARR Score: 34. B.3.3. Associated AI prompt “What Lean methods can reduce manufacturing lead time and improve throughput in a bottlenecked production. line?” B.3.4. AI response excerpt “Reducing lead time and improving throughput involves identifying bottlenecks and applying Lean tools like takt time analysis, line balancing, and workload redistribution. Simulation software such as ARENA helps validate process changes. Buffer zones before high-variability steps stabilize flow. Cross-training workers",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,23,"“Reducing lead time and improving throughput involves identifying bottlenecks and applying Lean tools like takt time analysis, line balancing, and workload redistribution. Simulation software such as ARENA helps validate process changes. Buffer zones before high-variability steps stabilize flow. Cross-training workers can also increase operational flexibility.” B.3.5. Student assignment excerpt – process optimization “To reduce manufacturing delays, we analyzed each production stage separately using time-and-motion studies. Rather than applying AI, we adjusted the shift schedule and changed the sequence of subassembly tasks. Although simulation was discussed, we prioritized insights from real-time observations and direct worker feedback.” Subcomponent Scores: Text Similarity (TS) = 23. Copy-Pasting (CP) = 0. Structural Similarity (SS) = 57. Prompt–Response Consistency (PRC) = 64. Query-to-Submission Link Score (QLS) = 0. ARR = 0.40⋅23 + 0.10⋅0 + 0.20⋅57 + 0.20⋅64 + 0.10⋅0 = 34. This response demonstrates minimal reliance on the AI output. The student addressed the prompt using original structure, vocabulary, and ideas, with little to no lexical or syntactic overlap with the AI-generated content. The low ARR score reflects strong independent reasoning and minimal external scaffolding, suggesting",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,23,"on the AI output. The student addressed the prompt using original structure, vocabulary, and ideas, with little to no lexical or syntactic overlap with the AI-generated content. The low ARR score reflects strong independent reasoning and minimal external scaffolding, suggesting deeper metacognitive engagement and more autonomous problem-solving. B.4. AI query depth & structure score (QD) The AI Query Depth & Structure Score (QD) evaluates the cognitive complexity and linguistic sophistication of student prompts submitted to ChatGPT. It is composed of three subcomponents—Lexical and Syntactic Structure (LS), Multi-Step Problem-Solving Depth (MSD), and Focus and Clarity (FC)—each capturing a different dimension of prompt quality. The following examples are drawn from a laboratory session in a fourth-year Environmental Engineering course on anaerobic digestion for wastewater treatment. Students were asked to design optimized systems using biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) data and methane yield efficiency. These examples illustrate how the QD metric differentiates between shallow, underdeveloped prompts and those that demonstrate clear structure, specificity, and strategic depth. High QD Example. QD Score: 89. B.4.1. Student prompt – environmental engineering “Given any influent BOD levels and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,23,"These examples illustrate how the QD metric differentiates between shallow, underdeveloped prompts and those that demonstrate clear structure, specificity, and strategic depth. High QD Example. QD Score: 89. B.4.1. Student prompt – environmental engineering “Given any influent BOD levels and target methane yield, how can I design an anaerobic digestion system that minimizes retention time while L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 23",70
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,24,"maximizing gas recovery across varying temperature conditions?” Subcomponent Scores: Lexical and Syntactic Structure (LS) = 100. Multi-Step Problem-Solving Depth (MSD) = 80. Focus and Clarity (FC) = 86. QD = 0.35 ⋅ 100 + 0.35 ⋅ 80 + 0.30 ⋅ 86 = 89. This prompt demonstrates high syntactic and conceptual sophistication. It includes discipline-specific terminology (influent BOD, retention time, methane yield), articulates competing design goals, and introduces a realistic constraint (temperature conditions). The grammatical structure includes subordinate clauses and modifiers, while the task itself reflects procedural reasoning and optimization—indicators of advanced, self-regulated cognitive engagement. Low QD Example. QD Score: 52. B.4.2. Student prompt – environmental engineering “Using BOD levels and target methane yield, design experiment”. Subcomponent Scores: Lexical and Syntactic Structure (LS) = 80. Multi-Step Problem-Solving Depth (MSD) = 0. Focus and Clarity (FC) = 80. QD = 0.35 ⋅ 80 + 0.35 ⋅ 0 + 0.30 ⋅ 80 = 52. While this prompt references relevant concepts, it lacks grammatical structure, contextual clarity, and logical depth. The sentence is syntactically sparse and fails to convey an explicit method, constraint, or",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,24,"80 + 0.35 ⋅ 0 + 0.30 ⋅ 80 = 52. While this prompt references relevant concepts, it lacks grammatical structure, contextual clarity, and logical depth. The sentence is syntactically sparse and fails to convey an explicit method, constraint, or objective. It does not guide the AI model toward a well-defined task or process, suggesting minimal planning or reflective engagement. The low QD score reflects underdeveloped cognitive articulation and limited prompting strategy. These examples demonstrate how the QD metric captures both linguistic structure and problem-solving complexity, distinguishing well-formed, goal-oriented prompts from vague or superficial input. B.5. Prompt refinement depth score (PRD) The Prompt Refinement Depth Score (PRD) evaluates the extent to which students iteratively enhance their prompts during an AI-assisted session. It captures both syntactic complexity and semantic elaboration across successive prompt revisions. The following examples are drawn from a fourth- year Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) engineering tutorial in which students explored the ecological risks of biotechnology. As part of the task, students were asked to draft AI-assisted position statements on the environmental implications of genetic engineering. The PRD metric",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,24,"fourth- year Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) engineering tutorial in which students explored the ecological risks of biotechnology. As part of the task, students were asked to draft AI-assisted position statements on the environmental implications of genetic engineering. The PRD metric illustrates how meaningful prompt refinement can reflect deeper cognitive engagement and planning. High PRD Example. PRD Score: 100.00. B.5.1. Student A – environmental impact assessment Initial Prompt: “What is genetic engineering?” Refined Prompt: “What is genetic engineering, and how is CRISPR-Cas9 used in modifying gene expression in eukaryotic organisms? Include mechanisms of double- stranded breaks, non-homologous end joining, and off-target effects in clinical gene therapy contexts.” Subcomponent Scores: Syntactic Complexity Gain (ΔSC) = 17.32. Semantic Elaboration Gain (ΔSE) = 0.38. Raw PRD Score = 14.62. Final PRD Score = 100.00. This prompt sequence reflects deliberate expansion and cognitive elaboration. The refined version introduces specific biological mechanisms, clarifies organismal context, and expands conceptual scope—evidencing metacognitive control over inquiry framing and information extraction. The structural and semantic gains suggest high-quality prompt engineering and self-regulated learning. Low PRD Example. PRD Score: 17.74. B.5.2.",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,24,"refined version introduces specific biological mechanisms, clarifies organismal context, and expands conceptual scope—evidencing metacognitive control over inquiry framing and information extraction. The structural and semantic gains suggest high-quality prompt engineering and self-regulated learning. Low PRD Example. PRD Score: 17.74. B.5.2. Student B – environmental impact assessment Initial Prompt: “What is genetic engineering?” Refined Prompt: “Could you tell me more about genetic engineering?” Subcomponent Scores: Syntactic Complexity Gain (ΔSC) = 2.95. Semantic Elaboration Gain (ΔSE) = 0.12. Raw PRD Score = 2.59. Final PRD Score = 17.74. While the refined prompt is more polite and conversational, it introduces minimal new information and no structural complexity. There is little evidence of elaboration, specificity, or effort to scaffold AI-generated output. This low PRD score suggests shallow engagement and limited iterative strategy during the prompting process. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 24",146
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,25,"These examples illustrate how PRD captures depth of refinement and cognitive effort, distinguishing between surface-level edits and meaningful prompt evolution. B.6. AI response utility score (RU) The AI Response Utility Score (RU) evaluates the extent to which students meaningfully incorporate AI-generated content into their writing through semantic novelty, conceptual transformation, and contribution to the final assignment. The following examples are drawn from a fourth-year Environmental Engineering tutorial on Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS), where students received AI explanations and were then asked to reflect on their usefulness and implications. The examples illustrate how RU distinguishes passive reproduction from critical engagement and independent synthesis. High RU Example. RU Score: 72.64. AI Response: “Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) is a climate mitigation strategy involving the capture of CO₂ emissions from sources like power plants and storing it underground to prevent atmospheric release. Though technically viable, CCS faces challenges such as long-term monitoring, leakage risks, and high economic costs. Critics argue it delays structural energy transitions, while proponents see it as essential for decarbonizing hard-to-abate sectors like cement and steel. Public acceptance and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,25,"Though technically viable, CCS faces challenges such as long-term monitoring, leakage risks, and high economic costs. Critics argue it delays structural energy transitions, while proponents see it as essential for decarbonizing hard-to-abate sectors like cement and steel. Public acceptance and regulatory clarity remain critical barriers to adoption.” Student Writing: “Carbon capture seems like a practical bridge, but I'm not convinced it should be central to long-term climate policy. It's effective in theory, but implementation costs and safety risks make me skeptical. What stood out to me during the tutorial was how industries like steel and cement might genuinely need it — not because they're lazy, but because no other options scale well yet. Still, delaying renewable investment by banking on CCS feels short-sighted. It's a backup, not a strategy.” Subcomponent Scores: Semantic Novelty (SN): 85.34. Conceptual Transformation (CT): 57.76. Contribution to Final Assignment (CFA): 54.88. Final RU Score: 72.64. This student recontextualized the AI response with a critical and original perspective. By paraphrasing central ideas and integrating personal reflections from the tutorial, the student advanced the conceptual space beyond AI",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,25,"to Final Assignment (CFA): 54.88. Final RU Score: 72.64. This student recontextualized the AI response with a critical and original perspective. By paraphrasing central ideas and integrating personal reflections from the tutorial, the student advanced the conceptual space beyond AI content. The writing demonstrates thoughtful skepticism, domain reasoning, and selective integration, aligning with the goals of knowledge construction and interpretive agency. Low RU Example. RU Score: 46.01. AI Response: “Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) captures CO₂ from power plants and stores it underground. This process helps reduce greenhouse gas emis­ sions. CCS is used in industries where emissions are hard to eliminate. The system involves three steps: capture, transport, and storage. It is supported by some governments but also faces concerns about cost and safety.” Student Writing: “Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) is a method used to lower emissions by capturing CO₂ and storing it underground. It works in places like power plants and helps reduce pollution. There are three parts: capturing the gas, moving it, and putting it underground. Some people support it, but others think it costs too",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,25,"capturing CO₂ and storing it underground. It works in places like power plants and helps reduce pollution. There are three parts: capturing the gas, moving it, and putting it underground. Some people support it, but others think it costs too much or might leak. It helps in industries where reducing emissions is difficult.” Subcomponent Scores: Semantic Novelty (SN): 36.69. Conceptual Transformation (CT): 50.52. Contribution to Final Assignment (CFA): 81.51. Final RU Score: 46.01. Despite high surface-level alignment, this student writing exhibits minimal modification of the AI content. Sentence structure, phrasing, and conceptual framing remain close to the original AI response, indicating low interpretive contribution. The high CFA score reflects basic relevance and completeness, but the limited semantic and conceptual novelty indicate low epistemic engagement. These examples demonstrate how RU differentiates between passive adoption and active transformation of AI content, offering a nuanced lens for assessing AI-assisted learning. B.7. AI-driven problem-solving score (PS) The AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score (PS) evaluates how effectively students integrate AI-generated content into their responses to support structured reasoning, conceptual application, and independent elaboration. The following examples are",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,25,"a nuanced lens for assessing AI-assisted learning. B.7. AI-driven problem-solving score (PS) The AI-Driven Problem-Solving Score (PS) evaluates how effectively students integrate AI-generated content into their responses to support structured reasoning, conceptual application, and independent elaboration. The following examples are drawn from a Process Optimization Labo­ ratory assignment, where students used ChatGPT-4o to analyze heat conduction in composite materials. These examples illustrate how PS captures both alignment with AI reasoning and student-driven extension of ideas. High PS Example. Final PS Score: 71.74. Subcomponent Scores: Stepwise Alignment (SA): 58.7. Conceptual Application (CA): 91.31. Independent Expansion (IE): 58.69. AI Response: “The process of thermal conduction in composite materials depends on the interface resistance and the material's intrinsic thermal conductivity. To compute effective thermal resistance, Fourier's Law is applied to each segment, summing inverse conductances in series. Assumptions include steady- state flow, one-dimensional transfer, and perfect bonding.” L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 25",158
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"Student Writing: “In our analysis, we treated thermal resistance as a sum of inverse conductivities following Fourier's Law. The series model assumes a steady-state, one-dimensional conduction across bonded materials, where interface resistance becomes significant.” This student retained key technical insights from the AI response while rephrasing them in a more applied and personalized form. The writing demonstrates a clear understanding of the underlying model (SA), accurately transfers domain-specific concepts (CA), and adds framing relevant to the experimental context (IE). This balance between reuse and reinterpretation resulted in a high PS score. Low PS Example. Final PS Score: 36.4. Subcomponent Scores: Stepwise Alignment (SA): 31.45. Conceptual Application (CA): 19.1. Independent Expansion (IE): 80.9. AI Response: “In composite materials, how heat moves depends on the properties of the materials and how well the layers connect. To find out the total thermal resistance, Fourier's Law can be used, adding the resistances in each section. The situation is usually steady, with one-directional flow and good contact between materials.” Student Writing: “The experiment looked at how heat moved through different parts of a material. Each",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"Fourier's Law can be used, adding the resistances in each section. The situation is usually steady, with one-directional flow and good contact between materials.” Student Writing: “The experiment looked at how heat moved through different parts of a material. Each part slowed the heat by a different amount. The results were written down and compared to what was expected. Some errors happened that may have changed the numbers.” While this student demonstrates some independence in narrative construction (IE), the technical depth is limited. The conceptual application of Fourier's Law and interface resistance is missing, and the description lacks alignment with the AI's reasoning process. As a result, the CA and SA components are low, yielding a substantially lower PS score. These examples highlight how PS distinguishes between mere paraphrasing, surface-level reporting, and deeper, structured application of AI- derived knowledge within domain-specific problem-solving contexts. Appendix C. Data aggregation pipeline To compute the complete set of AI interaction and writing metrics used in this study, a multistep data processing pipeline was developed to align each student's prompts, AI responses, written assignments, and",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"domain-specific problem-solving contexts. Appendix C. Data aggregation pipeline To compute the complete set of AI interaction and writing metrics used in this study, a multistep data processing pipeline was developed to align each student's prompts, AI responses, written assignments, and grades on a per-session basis. During the semester, each AI access session was logged as a raw TXT file labeled with the student's anonymized ID number, session number, and course name. Each file contained a timestamped sequence of prompt–response pairs. These logs were parsed to separate individual prompts from their corresponding responses, then cleaned to remove duplicate or near-duplicate prompts—defined as those with ≥95% textual similarity—using Lev­ enshtein distance thresholds implemented in Python. The remaining prompts and responses were concatenated into two separate chronological text blocks (one for prompts and one for responses), with a delimiter inserted between each entry to preserve separation for later parsing. The compiled AI output was then matched to the corresponding written assignment (in DOCX format) using the student's anonymized ID and converted to plain text. Assignment scores were retrieved from a structured grading spreadsheet",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"to preserve separation for later parsing. The compiled AI output was then matched to the corresponding written assignment (in DOCX format) using the student's anonymized ID and converted to plain text. Assignment scores were retrieved from a structured grading spreadsheet and merged by student ID. All files were combined into a single CSV dataset, producing one unified record per student per session. Each row included the written assignment text, cleaned prompt block, compiled AI response block, assignment score, and contextual metadata (e.g., course, week, AI access status). From this merged dataset: • Prompt-based metrics were calculated using only the prompt logs. • Response-to-writing metrics were computed by comparing the full AI output block to the student submission. • Prompt–response relationship metrics were derived from their respective paired text blocks. • Writing quality metrics were extracted directly from the final assignments. This unified structure enabled systematic computation of all AI interaction and writing metrics across 936 AI access logs and 1866 written as­ signments. After metric calculation, data from both AI-access and non-AI sessions were compiled into a single dataset. Each",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"This unified structure enabled systematic computation of all AI interaction and writing metrics across 936 AI access logs and 1866 written as­ signments. After metric calculation, data from both AI-access and non-AI sessions were compiled into a single dataset. Each row represented a unique combination of student ID, course, and week. Columns included anonymized ID, course type, week, AI access status, activity type, attendance, final assignment score, and all computed metrics. For AI-access sessions, metric fields contained calculated values; for non-AI sessions, AI-related fields were set to zero. The final dataset was exported as an .xlsx file and deposited in the public data repository available via OSF (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a). Appendix D. Software environment and reproducibility All analyses were performed in Python 3.10.13 on Windows 11 Pro (x64). Table D1 lists the main libraries and versions used throughout the analytical workflow. All scripts, datasets, and code modules required for full replication are archived in the corresponding Zenodo repository (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b) and include randomization, preprocessing, metric computation, and statistical modeling files. Table D.1. Python libraries used and purpose. Package Version",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,26,"workflow. All scripts, datasets, and code modules required for full replication are archived in the corresponding Zenodo repository (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b) and include randomization, preprocessing, metric computation, and statistical modeling files. Table D.1. Python libraries used and purpose. Package Version Purpose spaCy 3.7.2 Text lemmatization and linguistic preprocessing NLTK 3.8.1 Tokenization and stop-word filtering scikit-learn 1.5.0 Machine-learning models (Random Forest, PCA, cross-validation) Pandas 2.2.2 Data handling, merging, and normalization (continued on next page) L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 26",87
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,27,"(continued) Package Version Purpose NumPy 1.26.4 Numerical operations and randomization Statsmodels 0.14 GLM and linear mixed-effects modeling sentence-transformers 2.7.0 Semantic-similarity calculations (auxiliary validation) matplotlib / seaborn 3.8.4 / 0.13.2 Visualization and figure generation All computations used random_state = 42 for reproducibility. Hardware configuration: Intel Core i7-12700H CPU (2.3 GHz), 32 GB RAM. D.1. Randomization and data integrity Stratified randomization was implemented via the script randomization.py (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b) using NumPy's pseudo-random generator (np. random.seed = 42). Each student was assigned to eight AI and eight non-AI sessions across 16 weeks. Attendance filtering excluded students below 80% participation to maintain balanced exposure. The final randomized schedule was stored as AI_randomization_schedule.csv and verified for 8 AI: 8 non-AI symmetry per student. D.2. Preprocessing pipeline All AI interaction logs were preprocessed before metric computation: 1. Metadata removal: timestamps and IDs stripped. 2. Normalization: text converted to lowercase and lemmatized. 3. Duplicate handling: identical or empty prompts removed. 4. Non-ASCII filtering: characters outside the 7-bit ASCII range (e.g., accented letters, emojis, non-Latin symbols) deleted to ensure UTF-8 compatibility. 5. Tokenization: processed via NLTK 3.8.1",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,27,"text converted to lowercase and lemmatized. 3. Duplicate handling: identical or empty prompts removed. 4. Non-ASCII filtering: characters outside the 7-bit ASCII range (e.g., accented letters, emojis, non-Latin symbols) deleted to ensure UTF-8 compatibility. 5. Tokenization: processed via NLTK 3.8.1 and spaCy 3.7.2. 6. Numeric scaling: continuous metric outputs normalized to a 0–100 range using min–max scaling prior to aggregation. This procedure ensured cross-student comparability and compatibility with the machine-learning pipeline. For extended descriptions and pseu­ docode examples, see Appendix C (Data Aggregation and Matching Process). D.3. Machine-learning and statistical model parameters Random Forest Regressor (scikit-learn 1.5.0): n_estimators = 500, random_state = 42, max_depth = None, min_samples_split = 2, min_samples_leaf = 1. Evaluation metrics included R2, MAE, and RMSE, averaged across 10-fold cross-validation (80 / 20 train–test). Feature importances were estimated via permutation importance to avoid bias toward high-cardinality predictors. PCA: Applied on z-standardized values of PRD, RC, RU, and PS metrics. Components with eigenvalues >1 and cumulative variance ≥70% were retained (Kaiser criterion). Bootstrapped stability (1000 resamples) yielded SD < 0.02 for all loadings. GLM: Estimated via statsmodels.OLS under",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,27,"PCA: Applied on z-standardized values of PRD, RC, RU, and PS metrics. Components with eigenvalues >1 and cumulative variance ≥70% were retained (Kaiser criterion). Bootstrapped stability (1000 resamples) yielded SD < 0.02 for all loadings. GLM: Estimated via statsmodels.OLS under Gaussian distribution and identity link. Predictors included CGPA and the four most predictive metrics. Reported statistics: β, 95% CIs, p, R2, and RMSE. Mixed-Effects Model: Fitted using statsmodels.mixedlm with restricted maximum likelihood (REML). Fixed effects: CGPA + four AI metrics. Random intercept: Student ID (1866 assignments nested within 128 students). Significance tests used Wald Z; parameter stability confirmed via 1000 Monte Carlo iterations (SD < 0.02). D.4. Reproducibility resources All materials required for replication are openly available: • Zenodo (Isaza Domínguez, 2025b) – full Python codebase (randomization, preprocessing, modeling, figures). • Open Science Framework (Isaza Domínguez, 2025a) – anonymized dataset (AI logs, assignment scores, computed metrics). Appendix E. Statistical power and robustness diagnostics To evaluate the robustness, stability, and adequacy of the statistical and machine-learning procedures applied in this study, a series of diagnostic and sensitivity analyses were conducted before",180
NEW10,NEW10.pdf,10.1016/j.iheduc.2026.101105,27,"(AI logs, assignment scores, computed metrics). Appendix E. Statistical power and robustness diagnostics To evaluate the robustness, stability, and adequacy of the statistical and machine-learning procedures applied in this study, a series of diagnostic and sensitivity analyses were conducted before and after modeling. These procedures examined sample adequacy, parameter stability, predictor independence, repeated-measures balance, and confidence-interval robustness across the primary analytical workflows. The diagnostics supported the suitability of the dataset and modeling framework for the inferential, predictive, and multilevel analyses performed throughout the study. Table E1 summarizes the statistical power analyses, robustness procedures, and diagnostic evaluations conducted across the comparative, correlational, machine-learning, and mixed-effects analyses. L.G. Isaza Dominguez et al. The Internet and Higher Education 71 (2026) 101105 27",119
